if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-122"]="PREFACE BOOK FIRST—THE THREE WOMEN I. A Face on Which Time Makes but Little Impression II. Humanity Appears upon the Scene, Hand in Hand with Trouble III. The Custom of the Country IV. The Halt on the Turnpike Road V. Perplexity among Honest People VI. The Figure against the Sky VII. Queen of Night VIII. Those Who Are Found Where There Is Said to Be Nobody IX. Love Leads a Shrewd Man into Strategy X. A Desperate Attempt at Persuasion XI. The Dishonesty of an Honest Woman\n\nBOOK SECOND—THE ARRIVAL I. Tidings of the Comer II. The People at Blooms-End Make Ready III. How a Little Sound Produced a Great Dream IV. Eustacia Is Led on to an Adventure V. Through the Moonlight VI. The Two Stand Face to Face VII. A Coalition between Beauty and Oddness VIII. Firmness Is Discovered in a Gentle Heart\n\nBOOK THIRD—THE FASCINATION I. “My Mind to Me a Kingdom Is” II. The New Course Causes Disappointment III. The First Act in a Timeworn Drama IV. An Hour of Bliss and Many Hours of Sadness V. Sharp Words Are Spoken, and a Crisis Ensues VI. Yeobright Goes, and the Breach Is Complete VII. The Morning and the Evening of a Day VIII. A New Force Disturbs the Current\n\nBOOK FOURTH—THE CLOSED DOOR I. The Rencounter by the Pool II. He Is Set upon by Adversities but He Sings a Song III. She Goes Out to Battle against Depression IV. Rough Coercion Is Employed V. The Journey across the Heath VI. A Conjuncture, and Its Result upon the Pedestrian VII. The Tragic Meeting of Two Old Friends VIII. Eustacia Hears of Good Fortune, and Beholds Evil\nBOOK FIFTH—THE DISCOVERY I. “Wherefore Is Light Given to Him That Is in Misery” II. A Lurid Light Breaks in upon a Darkened Understanding III. Eustacia Dresses Herself on a Black Morning IV. The Ministrations of a Half-forgotten One V. An Old Move Inadvertently Repeated VI. Thomasin Argues with Her Cousin, and He Writes a Letter VII. The Night of the Sixth of November VIII. Rain, Darkness, and Anxious Wanderers IX. Sights and Sounds Draw the Wanderers Together\n\nBOOK SIXTH—AFTERCOURSES I. The Inevitable Movement Onward II. Thomasin Walks in a Green Place by the Roman Road III. The Serious Discourse of Clym with His Cousin IV. Cheerfulness Again Asserts Itself at Blooms-End, and Clym Finds His Vocation\n“To sorrow I bade good morrow, And thought to leave her far away behind; But cheerly, cheerly, She loves me dearly; She is so constant to me, and so kind. I would deceive her, And so leave her, But ah! she is so constant and so kind.”\n\nThe date at which the following events are assumed to have occurred may be set down as between 1840 and 1850, when the old watering place herein called “Budmouth” still retained sufficient afterglow from its Georgian gaiety and prestige to lend it an absorbing attractiveness to the romantic and imaginative soul of a lonely dweller inland.\nUnder the general name of “Egdon Heath,” which has been given to the sombre scene of the story, are united or typified heaths of various real names, to the number of at least a dozen; these being virtually one in character and aspect, though their original unity, or partial unity, is now somewhat disguised by intrusive strips and slices brought under the plough with varying degrees of success, or planted to woodland.\n\nIt is pleasant to dream that some spot in the extensive tract whose southwestern quarter is here described, may be the heath of that traditionary King of Wessex—Lear.\n_July_, 1895.\n\nI. A Face on Which Time Makes but Little Impression\nA Saturday afternoon in November was approaching the time of twilight, and the vast tract of unenclosed wild known as Egdon Heath embrowned itself moment by moment. Overhead the hollow stretch of whitish cloud shutting out the sky was as a tent which had the whole heath for its floor.\n\nThe heaven being spread with this pallid screen and the earth with the darkest vegetation, their meeting-line at the horizon was clearly marked. In such contrast the heath wore the appearance of an instalment of night which had taken up its place before its astronomical hour was come: darkness had to a great extent arrived hereon, while day stood distinct in the sky. Looking upwards, a furze-cutter would have been inclined to continue work; looking down, he would have decided to finish his faggot and go home. The distant rims of the world and of the firmament seemed to be a division in time no less than a division in matter. The face of the heath by its mere complexion added half an hour to evening; it could in like manner retard the dawn, sadden noon, anticipate the frowning of storms scarcely generated, and intensify the opacity of a moonless midnight to a cause of shaking and dread.\n\nIn fact, precisely at this transitional point of its nightly roll into darkness the great and particular glory of the Egdon waste began, and nobody could be said to understand the heath who had not been there at such a time. It could best be felt when it could not clearly be seen, its complete effect and explanation lying in this and the succeeding hours before the next dawn; then, and only then, did it tell its true tale. The spot was, indeed, a near relation of night, and when night showed itself an apparent tendency to gravitate together could be perceived in its shades and the scene. The sombre stretch of rounds and hollows seemed to rise and meet the evening gloom in pure sympathy, the heath exhaling darkness as rapidly as the heavens precipitated it. And so the obscurity in the air and the obscurity in the land closed together in a black fraternization towards which each advanced halfway.\n\nThe place became full of a watchful intentness now; for when other things sank brooding to sleep the heath appeared slowly to awake and listen. Every night its Titanic form seemed to await something; but it had waited thus, unmoved, during so many centuries, through the crises of so many things, that it could only be imagined to await one last crisis—the final overthrow.\n\nIt was a spot which returned upon the memory of those who loved it with an aspect of peculiar and kindly congruity. Smiling champaigns of flowers and fruit hardly do this, for they are permanently harmonious only with an existence of better reputation as to its issues than the present. Twilight combined with the scenery of Egdon Heath to evolve a thing majestic without severity, impressive without showiness, emphatic in its admonitions, grand in its simplicity. The qualifications which frequently invest the façade of a prison with far more dignity than is found in the façade of a palace double its size lent to this heath a sublimity in which spots renowned for beauty of the accepted kind are utterly wanting. Fair prospects wed happily with fair times; but alas, if times be not fair! Men have oftener suffered from, the mockery of a place too smiling for their reason than from the oppression of surroundings oversadly tinged. Haggard Egdon appealed to a subtler and scarcer instinct, to a more recently learnt emotion, than that which responds to the sort of beauty called charming and fair.\n\nIndeed, it is a question if the exclusive reign of this orthodox beauty is not approaching its last quarter. The new Vale of Tempe may be a gaunt waste in Thule; human souls may find themselves in closer and closer harmony with external things wearing a sombreness distasteful to our race when it was young. The time seems near, if it has not actually arrived, when the chastened sublimity of a moor, a sea, or a mountain will be all of nature that is absolutely in keeping with the moods of the more thinking among mankind. And ultimately, to the commonest tourist, spots like Iceland may become what the vineyards and myrtle gardens of South Europe are to him now; and Heidelberg and Baden be passed unheeded as he hastens from the Alps to the sand dunes of Scheveningen.\n\nThe most thoroughgoing ascetic could feel that he had a natural right to wander on Egdon—he was keeping within the line of legitimate indulgence when he laid himself open to influences such as these. Colours and beauties so far subdued were, at least, the birthright of all. Only in summer days of highest feather did its mood touch the level of gaiety. Intensity was more usually reached by way of the solemn than by way of the brilliant, and such a sort of intensity was often arrived at during winter darkness, tempests, and mists. Then Egdon was aroused to reciprocity; for the storm was its lover, and the wind its friend. Then it became the home of strange phantoms; and it was found to be the hitherto unrecognized original of those wild regions of obscurity which are vaguely felt to be compassing us about in midnight dreams of flight and disaster, and are never thought of after the dream till revived by scenes like this.\n\nIt was at present a place perfectly accordant with man’s nature—neither ghastly, hateful, nor ugly; neither commonplace, unmeaning, nor tame; but, like man, slighted and enduring; and withal singularly colossal and mysterious in its swarthy monotony. As with some persons who have long lived apart, solitude seemed to look out of its countenance. It had a lonely face, suggesting tragical possibilities.\n\nThis obscure, obsolete, superseded country figures in Domesday. Its condition is recorded therein as that of heathy, furzy, briary wilderness—“Bruaria.” Then follows the length and breadth in leagues; and, though some uncertainty exists as to the exact extent of this ancient lineal measure, it appears from the figures that the area of Egdon down to the present day has but little diminished. “Turbaria Bruaria”—the right of cutting heath-turf—occurs in charters relating to the district. “Overgrown with heth and mosse,” says Leland of the same dark sweep of country.\n\nHere at least were intelligible facts regarding landscape—far-reaching proofs productive of genuine satisfaction. The untameable, Ishmaelitish thing that Egdon now was it always had been. Civilization was its enemy; and ever since the beginning of vegetation its soil had worn the same antique brown dress, the natural and invariable garment of the particular formation. In its venerable one coat lay a certain vein of satire on human vanity in clothes. A person on a heath in raiment of modern cut and colours has more or less an anomalous look. We seem to want the oldest and simplest human clothing where the clothing of the earth is so primitive.\n\nTo recline on a stump of thorn in the central valley of Egdon, between afternoon and night, as now, where the eye could reach nothing of the world outside the summits and shoulders of heathland which filled the whole circumference of its glance, and to know that everything around and underneath had been from prehistoric times as unaltered as the stars overhead, gave ballast to the mind adrift on change, and harassed by the irrepressible New. The great inviolate place had an ancient permanence which the sea cannot claim. Who can say of a particular sea that it is old? Distilled by the sun, kneaded by the moon, it is renewed in a year, in a day, or in an hour. The sea changed, the fields changed, the rivers, the villages, and the people changed, yet Egdon remained. Those surfaces were neither so steep as to be destructible by weather, nor so flat as to be the victims of floods and deposits. With the exception of an aged highway, and a still more aged barrow presently to be referred to—themselves almost crystallized to natural products by long continuance—even the trifling irregularities were not caused by pickaxe, plough, or spade, but remained as the very finger-touches of the last geological change.\n\nThe above-mentioned highway traversed the lower levels of the heath, from one horizon to another. In many portions of its course it overlaid an old vicinal way, which branched from the great Western road of the Romans, the Via Iceniana, or Ikenild Street, hard by. On the evening under consideration it would have been noticed that, though the gloom had increased sufficiently to confuse the minor features of the heath, the white surface of the road remained almost as clear as ever.\n\nII. Humanity Appears upon the Scene, Hand in Hand with Trouble\nAlong the road walked an old man. He was white-headed as a mountain, bowed in the shoulders, and faded in general aspect. He wore a glazed hat, an ancient boat-cloak, and shoes; his brass buttons bearing an anchor upon their face. In his hand was a silver-headed walking stick, which he used as a veritable third leg, perseveringly dotting the ground with its point at every few inches’ interval. One would have said that he had been, in his day, a naval officer of some sort or other.\n\nBefore him stretched the long, laborious road, dry, empty, and white. It was quite open to the heath on each side, and bisected that vast dark surface like the parting-line on a head of black hair, diminishing and bending away on the furthest horizon.\n\nThe old man frequently stretched his eyes ahead to gaze over the tract that he had yet to traverse. At length he discerned, a long distance in front of him, a moving spot, which appeared to be a vehicle, and it proved to be going the same way as that in which he himself was journeying. It was the single atom of life that the scene contained, and it only served to render the general loneliness more evident. Its rate of advance was slow, and the old man gained upon it sensibly.\n\nWhen he drew nearer he perceived it to be a spring van, ordinary in shape, but singular in colour, this being a lurid red. The driver walked beside it; and, like his van, he was completely red. One dye of that tincture covered his clothes, the cap upon his head, his boots, his face, and his hands. He was not temporarily overlaid with the colour; it permeated him.\n\nThe old man knew the meaning of this. The traveller with the cart was a reddleman—a person whose vocation it was to supply farmers with redding for their sheep. He was one of a class rapidly becoming extinct in Wessex, filling at present in the rural world the place which, during the last century, the dodo occupied in the world of animals. He is a curious, interesting, and nearly perished link between obsolete forms of life and those which generally prevail.\n\nThe decayed officer, by degrees, came up alongside his fellow-wayfarer, and wished him good evening. The reddleman turned his head, and replied in sad and occupied tones. He was young, and his face, if not exactly handsome, approached so near to handsome that nobody would have contradicted an assertion that it really was so in its natural colour. His eye, which glared so strangely through his stain, was in itself attractive—keen as that of a bird of prey, and blue as autumn mist. He had neither whisker nor moustache, which allowed the soft curves of the lower part of his face to be apparent. His lips were thin, and though, as it seemed, compressed by thought, there was a pleasant twitch at their corners now and then. He was clothed throughout in a tight-fitting suit of corduroy, excellent in quality, not much worn, and well-chosen for its purpose, but deprived of its original colour by his trade. It showed to advantage the good shape of his figure. A certain well-to-do air about the man suggested that he was not poor for his degree. The natural query of an observer would have been, Why should such a promising being as this have hidden his prepossessing exterior by adopting that singular occupation?\n\nAfter replying to the old man’s greeting he showed no inclination to continue in talk, although they still walked side by side, for the elder traveller seemed to desire company. There were no sounds but that of the booming wind upon the stretch of tawny herbage around them, the crackling wheels, the tread of the men, and the footsteps of the two shaggy ponies which drew the van. They were small, hardy animals, of a breed between Galloway and Exmoor, and were known as “heath-croppers” here.\n\nNow, as they thus pursued their way, the reddleman occasionally left his companion’s side, and, stepping behind the van, looked into its interior through a small window. The look was always anxious. He would then return to the old man, who made another remark about the state of the country and so on, to which the reddleman again abstractedly replied, and then again they would lapse into silence. The silence conveyed to neither any sense of awkwardness; in these lonely places wayfarers, after a first greeting, frequently plod on for miles without speech; contiguity amounts to a tacit conversation where, otherwise than in cities, such contiguity can be put an end to on the merest inclination, and where not to put an end to it is intercourse in itself.\n\nPossibly these two might not have spoken again till their parting, had it not been for the reddleman’s visits to his van. When he returned from his fifth time of looking in the old man said, “You have something inside there besides your load?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Somebody who wants looking after?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nNot long after this a faint cry sounded from the interior. The reddleman hastened to the back, looked in, and came away again.\n\n“You have a child there, my man?”\n\n“No, sir, I have a woman.”\n\n“The deuce you have! Why did she cry out?”\n\n“Oh, she has fallen asleep, and not being used to traveling, she’s uneasy, and keeps dreaming.”\n\n“A young woman?”\n\n“Yes, a young woman.”\n\n“That would have interested me forty years ago. Perhaps she’s your wife?”\n\n“My wife!” said the other bitterly. “She’s above mating with such as I. But there’s no reason why I should tell you about that.”\n\n“That’s true. And there’s no reason why you should not. What harm can I do to you or to her?”\n\nThe reddleman looked in the old man’s face. “Well, sir,” he said at last, “I knew her before today, though perhaps it would have been better if I had not. But she’s nothing to me, and I am nothing to her; and she wouldn’t have been in my van if any better carriage had been there to take her.”\n\n“Where, may I ask?”\n\n“At Anglebury.”\n\n“I know the town well. What was she doing there?”\n\n“Oh, not much—to gossip about. However, she’s tired to death now, and not at all well, and that’s what makes her so restless. She dropped off into a nap about an hour ago, and ’twill do her good.”\n\n“A nice-looking girl, no doubt?”\n\n“You would say so.”\n\nThe other traveller turned his eyes with interest towards the van window, and, without withdrawing them, said, “I presume I might look in upon her?”\n\n“No,” said the reddleman abruptly. “It is getting too dark for you to see much of her; and, more than that, I have no right to allow you. Thank God she sleeps so well, I hope she won’t wake till she’s home.”\n\n“Who is she? One of the neighbourhood?”\n\n“’Tis no matter who, excuse me.”\n\n“It is not that girl of Blooms-End, who has been talked about more or less lately? If so, I know her; and I can guess what has happened.”\n\n“’Tis no matter.... Now, sir, I am sorry to say that we shall soon have to part company. My ponies are tired, and I have further to go, and I am going to rest them under this bank for an hour.”\n\nThe elder traveller nodded his head indifferently, and the reddleman turned his horses and van in upon the turf, saying, “Good night.” The old man replied, and proceeded on his way as before.\n\nThe reddleman watched his form as it diminished to a speck on the road and became absorbed in the thickening films of night. He then took some hay from a truss which was slung up under the van, and, throwing a portion of it in front of the horses, made a pad of the rest, which he laid on the ground beside his vehicle. Upon this he sat down, leaning his back against the wheel. From the interior a low soft breathing came to his ear. It appeared to satisfy him, and he musingly surveyed the scene, as if considering the next step that he should take.\n\nTo do things musingly, and by small degrees, seemed, indeed, to be a duty in the Egdon valleys at this transitional hour, for there was that in the condition of the heath itself which resembled protracted and halting dubiousness. It was the quality of the repose appertaining to the scene. This was not the repose of actual stagnation, but the apparent repose of incredible slowness. A condition of healthy life so nearly resembling the torpor of death is a noticeable thing of its sort; to exhibit the inertness of the desert, and at the same time to be exercising powers akin to those of the meadow, and even of the forest, awakened in those who thought of it the attentiveness usually engendered by understatement and reserve.\n\nThe scene before the reddleman’s eyes was a gradual series of ascents from the level of the road backward into the heart of the heath. It embraced hillocks, pits, ridges, acclivities, one behind the other, till all was finished by a high hill cutting against the still light sky. The traveller’s eye hovered about these things for a time, and finally settled upon one noteworthy object up there. It was a barrow. This bossy projection of earth above its natural level occupied the loftiest ground of the loneliest height that the heath contained. Although from the vale it appeared but as a wart on an Atlantean brow, its actual bulk was great. It formed the pole and axis of this heathery world.\n\nAs the resting man looked at the barrow he became aware that its summit, hitherto the highest object in the whole prospect round, was surmounted by something higher. It rose from the semiglobular mound like a spike from a helmet. The first instinct of an imaginative stranger might have been to suppose it the person of one of the Celts who built the barrow, so far had all of modern date withdrawn from the scene. It seemed a sort of last man among them, musing for a moment before dropping into eternal night with the rest of his race.\n\nThere the form stood, motionless as the hill beneath. Above the plain rose the hill, above the hill rose the barrow, and above the barrow rose the figure. Above the figure was nothing that could be mapped elsewhere than on a celestial globe.\n\nSuch a perfect, delicate, and necessary finish did the figure give to the dark pile of hills that it seemed to be the only obvious justification of their outline. Without it, there was the dome without the lantern; with it the architectural demands of the mass were satisfied. The scene was strangely homogeneous, in that the vale, the upland, the barrow, and the figure above it amounted only to unity. Looking at this or that member of the group was not observing a complete thing, but a fraction of a thing.\n\nThe form was so much like an organic part of the entire motionless structure that to see it move would have impressed the mind as a strange phenomenon. Immobility being the chief characteristic of that whole which the person formed portion of, the discontinuance of immobility in any quarter suggested confusion.\n\nYet that is what happened. The figure perceptibly gave up its fixity, shifted a step or two, and turned round. As if alarmed, it descended on the right side of the barrow, with the glide of a water-drop down a bud, and then vanished. The movement had been sufficient to show more clearly the characteristics of the figure, and that it was a woman’s.\nThe reason of her sudden displacement now appeared. With her dropping out of sight on the right side, a newcomer, bearing a burden, protruded into the sky on the left side, ascended the tumulus, and deposited the burden on the top. A second followed, then a third, a fourth, a fifth, and ultimately the whole barrow was peopled with burdened figures.\n\nThe only intelligible meaning in this sky-backed pantomime of silhouettes was that the woman had no relation to the forms who had taken her place, was sedulously avoiding these, and had come thither for another object than theirs. The imagination of the observer clung by preference to that vanished, solitary figure, as to something more interesting, more important, more likely to have a history worth knowing than these newcomers, and unconsciously regarded them as intruders. But they remained, and established themselves; and the lonely person who hitherto had been queen of the solitude did not at present seem likely to return.\nIII. The Custom of the Country\n\nHad a looker-on been posted in the immediate vicinity of the barrow, he would have learned that these persons were boys and men of the neighbouring hamlets. Each, as he ascended the barrow, had been heavily laden with furze faggots, carried upon the shoulder by means of a long stake sharpened at each end for impaling them easily—two in front and two behind. They came from a part of the heath a quarter of a mile to the rear, where furze almost exclusively prevailed as a product.\n\nEvery individual was so involved in furze by his method of carrying the faggots that he appeared like a bush on legs till he had thrown them down. The party had marched in trail, like a travelling flock of sheep; that is to say, the strongest first, the weak and young behind.\n\nThe loads were all laid together, and a pyramid of furze thirty feet in circumference now occupied the crown of the tumulus, which was known as Rainbarrow for many miles round. Some made themselves busy with matches, and in selecting the driest tufts of furze, others in loosening the bramble bonds which held the faggots together. Others, again, while this was in progress, lifted their eyes and swept the vast expanse of country commanded by their position, now lying nearly obliterated by shade. In the valleys of the heath nothing save its own wild face was visible at any time of day; but this spot commanded a horizon enclosing a tract of far extent, and in many cases lying beyond the heath country. None of its features could be seen now, but the whole made itself felt as a vague stretch of remoteness.\n\nWhile the men and lads were building the pile, a change took place in the mass of shade which denoted the distant landscape. Red suns and tufts of fire one by one began to arise, flecking the whole country round. They were the bonfires of other parishes and hamlets that were engaged in the same sort of commemoration. Some were distant, and stood in a dense atmosphere, so that bundles of pale straw-like beams radiated around them in the shape of a fan. Some were large and near, glowing scarlet-red from the shade, like wounds in a black hide. Some were Mænades, with winy faces and blown hair. These tinctured the silent bosom of the clouds above them and lit up their ephemeral caves, which seemed thenceforth to become scalding caldrons. Perhaps as many as thirty bonfires could be counted within the whole bounds of the district; and as the hour may be told on a clock-face when the figures themselves are invisible, so did the men recognize the locality of each fire by its angle and direction, though nothing of the scenery could be viewed.\n\nThe first tall flame from Rainbarrow sprang into the sky, attracting all eyes that had been fixed on the distant conflagrations back to their own attempt in the same kind. The cheerful blaze streaked the inner surface of the human circle—now increased by other stragglers, male and female—with its own gold livery, and even overlaid the dark turf around with a lively luminousness, which softened off into obscurity where the barrow rounded downwards out of sight. It showed the barrow to be the segment of a globe, as perfect as on the day when it was thrown up, even the little ditch remaining from which the earth was dug. Not a plough had ever disturbed a grain of that stubborn soil. In the heath’s barrenness to the farmer lay its fertility to the historian. There had been no obliteration, because there had been no tending.\n\nIt seemed as if the bonfire-makers were standing in some radiant upper story of the world, detached from and independent of the dark stretches below. The heath down there was now a vast abyss, and no longer a continuation of what they stood on; for their eyes, adapted to the blaze, could see nothing of the deeps beyond its influence. Occasionally, it is true, a more vigorous flare than usual from their faggots sent darting lights like aides-de-camp down the inclines to some distant bush, pool, or patch of white sand, kindling these to replies of the same colour, till all was lost in darkness again. Then the whole black phenomenon beneath represented Limbo as viewed from the brink by the sublime Florentine in his vision, and the muttered articulations of the wind in the hollows were as complaints and petitions from the “souls of mighty worth” suspended therein.\n\nIt was as if these men and boys had suddenly dived into past ages, and fetched therefrom an hour and deed which had before been familiar with this spot. The ashes of the original British pyre which blazed from that summit lay fresh and undisturbed in the barrow beneath their tread. The flames from funeral piles long ago kindled there had shone down upon the lowlands as these were shining now. Festival fires to Thor and Woden had followed on the same ground and duly had their day. Indeed, it is pretty well known that such blazes as this the heathmen were now enjoying are rather the lineal descendants from jumbled Druidical rites and Saxon ceremonies than the invention of popular feeling about Gunpowder Plot.\n\nMoreover to light a fire is the instinctive and resistant act of man when, at the winter ingress, the curfew is sounded throughout Nature. It indicates a spontaneous, Promethean rebelliousness against that fiat that this recurrent season shall bring foul times, cold darkness, misery and death. Black chaos comes, and the fettered gods of the earth say, Let there be light.\n\nThe brilliant lights and sooty shades which struggled upon the skin and clothes of the persons standing round caused their lineaments and general contours to be drawn with Dureresque vigour and dash. Yet the permanent moral expression of each face it was impossible to discover, for as the nimble flames towered, nodded, and swooped through the surrounding air, the blots of shade and flakes of light upon the countenances of the group changed shape and position endlessly. All was unstable; quivering as leaves, evanescent as lightning. Shadowy eye-sockets, deep as those of a death’s head, suddenly turned into pits of lustre: a lantern-jaw was cavernous, then it was shining; wrinkles were emphasized to ravines, or obliterated entirely by a changed ray. Nostrils were dark wells; sinews in old necks were gilt mouldings; things with no particular polish on them were glazed; bright objects, such as the tip of a furze-hook one of the men carried, were as glass; eyeballs glowed like little lanterns. Those whom Nature had depicted as merely quaint became grotesque, the grotesque became preternatural; for all was in extremity.\n\nHence it may be that the face of an old man, who had like others been called to the heights by the rising flames, was not really the mere nose and chin that it appeared to be, but an appreciable quantity of human countenance. He stood complacently sunning himself in the heat. With a speaker, or stake, he tossed the outlying scraps of fuel into the conflagration, looking at the midst of the pile, occasionally lifting his eyes to measure the height of the flame, or to follow the great sparks which rose with it and sailed away into darkness. The beaming sight, and the penetrating warmth, seemed to breed in him a cumulative cheerfulness, which soon amounted to delight. With his stick in his hand he began to jig a private minuet, a bunch of copper seals shining and swinging like a pendulum from under his waistcoat: he also began to sing, in the voice of a bee up a flue—\n\n“The king′ call’d down′ his no-bles all′, By one′, by two′, by three′; Earl Mar′-shal, I’ll′ go shrive′-the queen′, And thou′ shalt wend′ with me′.\n\n“A boon′, a boon′, quoth Earl′ Mar-shal′, And fell′ on his bend′-ded knee′, That what′-so-e’er′ the queen′ shall say′, No harm′ there-of′ may be′.”\n\nWant of breath prevented a continuance of the song; and the breakdown attracted the attention of a firm-standing man of middle age, who kept each corner of his crescent-shaped mouth rigorously drawn back into his cheek, as if to do away with any suspicion of mirthfulness which might erroneously have attached to him.\n\n“A fair stave, Grandfer Cantle; but I am afeard ’tis too much for the mouldy weasand of such a old man as you,” he said to the wrinkled reveller. “Dostn’t wish th’ wast three sixes again, Grandfer, as you was when you first learnt to sing it?”\n\n“Hey?” said Grandfer Cantle, stopping in his dance.\n\n“Dostn’t wish wast young again, I say? There’s a hole in thy poor bellows nowadays seemingly.”\n\n“But there’s good art in me? If I couldn’t make a little wind go a long ways I should seem no younger than the most aged man, should I, Timothy?”\n\n“And how about the new-married folks down there at the Quiet Woman Inn?” the other inquired, pointing towards a dim light in the direction of the distant highway, but considerably apart from where the reddleman was at that moment resting. “What’s the rights of the matter about ’em? You ought to know, being an understanding man.”\n\n“But a little rakish, hey? I own to it. Master Cantle is that, or he’s nothing. Yet ’tis a gay fault, neighbour Fairway, that age will cure.”\n\n“I heard that they were coming home tonight. By this time they must have come. What besides?”\n\n“The next thing is for us to go and wish ’em joy, I suppose?”\n\n“Well, no.”\n\n“No? Now, I thought we must. I must, or ’twould be very unlike me—the first in every spree that’s going!\n\n“Do thou′ put on′ a fri′-ar’s coat′, And I’ll′ put on′ a-no′-ther, And we′ will to′ Queen Ele′anor go′, Like Fri′ar and′ his bro′ther.\n\nI met Mis’ess Yeobright, the young bride’s aunt, last night, and she told me that her son Clym was coming home a’ Christmas. Wonderful clever, ’a believe—ah, I should like to have all that’s under that young man’s hair. Well, then, I spoke to her in my well-known merry way, and she said, ‘O that what’s shaped so venerable should talk like a fool!’—that’s what she said to me. I don’t care for her, be jowned if I do, and so I told her. ‘Be jowned if I care for ’ee,’ I said. I had her there—hey?”\n\n“I rather think she had you,” said Fairway.\n\n“No,” said Grandfer Cantle, his countenance slightly flagging. “’Tisn’t so bad as that with me?”\n\n“Seemingly ’tis, however, is it because of the wedding that Clym is coming home a’ Christmas—to make a new arrangement because his mother is now left in the house alone?”\n\n“Yes, yes—that’s it. But, Timothy, hearken to me,” said the Grandfer earnestly. “Though known as such a joker, I be an understanding man if you catch me serious, and I am serious now. I can tell ’ee lots about the married couple. Yes, this morning at six o’clock they went up the country to do the job, and neither vell nor mark have been seen of ’em since, though I reckon that this afternoon has brought ’em home again man and woman—wife, that is. Isn’t it spoke like a man, Timothy, and wasn’t Mis’ess Yeobright wrong about me?”\n\n“Yes, it will do. I didn’t know the two had walked together since last fall, when her aunt forbad the banns. How long has this new set-to been in mangling then? Do you know, Humphrey?”\n\n“Yes, how long?” said Grandfer Cantle smartly, likewise turning to Humphrey. “I ask that question.”\n\n“Ever since her aunt altered her mind, and said she might have the man after all,” replied Humphrey, without removing his eyes from the fire. He was a somewhat solemn young fellow, and carried the hook and leather gloves of a furze-cutter, his legs, by reason of that occupation, being sheathed in bulging leggings as stiff as the Philistine’s greaves of brass. “That’s why they went away to be married, I count. You see, after kicking up such a nunny-watch and forbidding the banns ’twould have made Mis’ess Yeobright seem foolish-like to have a banging wedding in the same parish all as if she’d never gainsaid it.”\n\n“Exactly—seem foolish-like; and that’s very bad for the poor things that be so, though I only guess as much, to be sure,” said Grandfer Cantle, still strenuously preserving a sensible bearing and mien.\n\n“Ah, well, I was at church that day,” said Fairway, “which was a very curious thing to happen.”\n\n“If ’twasn’t my name’s Simple,” said the Grandfer emphatically. “I ha’n’t been there to-year; and now the winter is a-coming on I won’t say I shall.”\n\n“I ha’n’t been these three years,” said Humphrey; “for I’m so dead sleepy of a Sunday; and ’tis so terrible far to get there; and when you do get there ’tis such a mortal poor chance that you’ll be chose for up above, when so many bain’t, that I bide at home and don’t go at all.”\n\n“I not only happened to be there,” said Fairway, with a fresh collection of emphasis, “but I was sitting in the same pew as Mis’ess Yeobright. And though you may not see it as such, it fairly made my blood run cold to hear her. Yes, it is a curious thing; but it made my blood run cold, for I was close at her elbow.” The speaker looked round upon the bystanders, now drawing closer to hear him, with his lips gathered tighter than ever in the rigorousness of his descriptive moderation.\n\n“’Tis a serious job to have things happen to ’ee there,” said a woman behind.\n\n“‘Ye are to declare it,’ was the parson’s words,” Fairway continued. “And then up stood a woman at my side—a-touching of me. ‘Well, be damned if there isn’t Mis’ess Yeobright a-standing up,’ I said to myself. Yes, neighbours, though I was in the temple of prayer that’s what I said. ’Tis against my conscience to curse and swear in company, and I hope any woman here will overlook it. Still what I did say I did say, and ’twould be a lie if I didn’t own it.”\n\n“So ’twould, neighbour Fairway.”\n\n“‘Be damned if there isn’t Mis’ess Yeobright a-standing up,’ I said,” the narrator repeated, giving out the bad word with the same passionless severity of face as before, which proved how entirely necessity and not gusto had to do with the iteration. “And the next thing I heard was, ‘I forbid the banns,’ from her. ‘I’ll speak to you after the service,’ said the parson, in quite a homely way—yes, turning all at once into a common man no holier than you or I. Ah, her face was pale! Maybe you can call to mind that monument in Weatherbury church—the cross-legged soldier that have had his arm knocked away by the schoolchildren? Well, he would about have matched that woman’s face, when she said, ‘I forbid the banns.’”\n\nThe audience cleared their throats and tossed a few stalks into the fire, not because these deeds were urgent, but to give themselves time to weigh the moral of the story.\n\n“I’m sure when I heard they’d been forbid I felt as glad as if anybody had gied me sixpence,” said an earnest voice—that of Olly Dowden, a woman who lived by making heath brooms, or besoms. Her nature was to be civil to enemies as well as to friends, and grateful to all the world for letting her remain alive.\n\n“And now the maid have married him just the same,” said Humphrey.\n\n“After that Mis’ess Yeobright came round and was quite agreeable,” Fairway resumed, with an unheeding air, to show that his words were no appendage to Humphrey’s, but the result of independent reflection.\n\n“Supposing they were ashamed, I don’t see why they shouldn’t have done it here-right,” said a wide-spread woman whose stays creaked like shoes whenever she stooped or turned. “’Tis well to call the neighbours together and to hae a good racket once now and then; and it may as well be when there’s a wedding as at tide-times. I don’t care for close ways.”\n\n“Ah, now, you’d hardly believe it, but I don’t care for gay weddings,” said Timothy Fairway, his eyes again travelling round. “I hardly blame Thomasin Yeobright and neighbour Wildeve for doing it quiet, if I must own it. A wedding at home means five and six-handed reels by the hour; and they do a man’s legs no good when he’s over forty.”\n\n“True. Once at the woman’s house you can hardly say nay to being one in a jig, knowing all the time that you be expected to make yourself worth your victuals.”\n\n“You be bound to dance at Christmas because ’tis the time o’ year; you must dance at weddings because ’tis the time o’ life. At christenings folk will even smuggle in a reel or two, if ’tis no further on than the first or second chiel. And this is not naming the songs you’ve got to sing.... For my part I like a good hearty funeral as well as anything. You’ve as splendid victuals and drink as at other parties, and even better. And it don’t wear your legs to stumps in talking over a poor fellow’s ways as it do to stand up in hornpipes.”\n\n“Nine folks out of ten would own ’twas going too far to dance then, I suppose?” suggested Grandfer Cantle.\n\n“’Tis the only sort of party a staid man can feel safe at after the mug have been round a few times.”\n\n“Well, I can’t understand a quiet ladylike little body like Tamsin Yeobright caring to be married in such a mean way,” said Susan Nunsuch, the wide woman, who preferred the original subject. “’Tis worse than the poorest do. And I shouldn’t have cared about the man, though some may say he’s good-looking.”\n\n“To give him his due he’s a clever, learned fellow in his way—a’most as clever as Clym Yeobright used to be. He was brought up to better things than keeping the Quiet Woman. An engineer—that’s what the man was, as we know; but he threw away his chance, and so ’a took a public house to live. His learning was no use to him at all.”\n\n“Very often the case,” said Olly, the besom-maker. “And yet how people do strive after it and get it! The class of folk that couldn’t use to make a round O to save their bones from the pit can write their names now without a sputter of the pen, oftentimes without a single blot—what do I say?—why, almost without a desk to lean their stomachs and elbows upon.”\n\n“True—’tis amazing what a polish the world have been brought to,” said Humphrey.\n\n“Why, afore I went a soldier in the Bang-up Locals (as we was called), in the year four,” chimed in Grandfer Cantle brightly, “I didn’t know no more what the world was like than the commonest man among ye. And now, jown it all, I won’t say what I bain’t fit for, hey?”\n\n“Couldst sign the book, no doubt,” said Fairway, “if wast young enough to join hands with a woman again, like Wildeve and Mis’ess Tamsin, which is more than Humph there could do, for he follows his father in learning. Ah, Humph, well I can mind when I was married how I zid thy father’s mark staring me in the face as I went to put down my name. He and your mother were the couple married just afore we were and there stood they father’s cross with arms stretched out like a great banging scarecrow. What a terrible black cross that was—thy father’s very likeness in en! To save my soul I couldn’t help laughing when I zid en, though all the time I was as hot as dog-days, what with the marrying, and what with the woman a-hanging to me, and what with Jack Changley and a lot more chaps grinning at me through church window. But the next moment a strawmote would have knocked me down, for I called to mind that if thy father and mother had had high words once, they’d been at it twenty times since they’d been man and wife, and I zid myself as the next poor stunpoll to get into the same mess.... Ah—well, what a day ’twas!”\n\n“Wildeve is older than Tamsin Yeobright by a good-few summers. A pretty maid too she is. A young woman with a home must be a fool to tear her smock for a man like that.”\n\nThe speaker, a peat- or turf-cutter, who had newly joined the group, carried across his shoulder the singular heart-shaped spade of large dimensions used in that species of labour, and its well-whetted edge gleamed like a silver bow in the beams of the fire.\n\n“A hundred maidens would have had him if he’d asked ’em,” said the wide woman.\n\n“Didst ever know a man, neighbour, that no woman at all would marry?” inquired Humphrey.\n\n“I never did,” said the turf-cutter.\n\n“Nor I,” said another.\n\n“Nor I,” said Grandfer Cantle.\n\n“Well, now, I did once,” said Timothy Fairway, adding more firmness to one of his legs. “I did know of such a man. But only once, mind.” He gave his throat a thorough rake round, as if it were the duty of every person not to be mistaken through thickness of voice. “Yes, I knew of such a man,” he said.\n\n“And what ghastly gallicrow might the poor fellow have been like, Master Fairway?” asked the turf-cutter.\n\n“Well, ’a was neither a deaf man, nor a dumb man, nor a blind man. What ’a was I don’t say.”\n\n“Is he known in these parts?” said Olly Dowden.\n\n“Hardly,” said Timothy; “but I name no name.... Come, keep the fire up there, youngsters.”\n\n“Whatever is Christian Cantle’s teeth a-chattering for?” said a boy from amid the smoke and shades on the other side of the blaze. “Be ye a-cold, Christian?”\n\nA thin jibbering voice was heard to reply, “No, not at all.”\n\n“Come forward, Christian, and show yourself. I didn’t know you were here,” said Fairway, with a humane look across towards that quarter.\n\nThus requested, a faltering man, with reedy hair, no shoulders, and a great quantity of wrist and ankle beyond his clothes, advanced a step or two by his own will, and was pushed by the will of others half a dozen steps more. He was Grandfer Cantle’s youngest son.\n\n“What be ye quaking for, Christian?” said the turf-cutter kindly.\n\n“I’m the man.”\n\n“What man?”\n\n“The man no woman will marry.”\n\n“The deuce you be!” said Timothy Fairway, enlarging his gaze to cover Christian’s whole surface and a great deal more, Grandfer Cantle meanwhile staring as a hen stares at the duck she has hatched.\n\n“Yes, I be he; and it makes me afeard,” said Christian. “D’ye think ’twill hurt me? I shall always say I don’t care, and swear to it, though I do care all the while.”\n\n“Well, be damned if this isn’t the queerest start ever I know’d,” said Mr. Fairway. “I didn’t mean you at all. There’s another in the country, then! Why did ye reveal yer misfortune, Christian?”\n\n“’Twas to be if ’twas, I suppose. I can’t help it, can I?” He turned upon them his painfully circular eyes, surrounded by concentric lines like targets.\n\n“No, that’s true. But ’tis a melancholy thing, and my blood ran cold when you spoke, for I felt there were two poor fellows where I had thought only one. ’Tis a sad thing for ye, Christian. How’st know the women won’t hae thee?”\n\n“I’ve asked ’em.”\n\n“Sure I should never have thought you had the face. Well, and what did the last one say to ye? Nothing that can’t be got over, perhaps, after all?”\n\n“‘Get out of my sight, you slack-twisted, slim-looking maphrotight fool,’ was the woman’s words to me.”\n\n“Not encouraging, I own,” said Fairway. “‘Get out of my sight, you slack-twisted, slim-looking maphrotight fool,’ is rather a hard way of saying No. But even that might be overcome by time and patience, so as to let a few grey hairs show themselves in the hussy’s head. How old be you, Christian?”\n\n“Thirty-one last tatie-digging, Mister Fairway.”\n\n“Not a boy—not a boy. Still there’s hope yet.”\n\n“That’s my age by baptism, because that’s put down in the great book of the Judgment that they keep in church vestry; but Mother told me I was born some time afore I was christened.”\n\n“Ah!”\n\n“But she couldn’t tell when, to save her life, except that there was no moon.”\n\n“No moon—that’s bad. Hey, neighbours, that’s bad for him!”\n\n“Yes, ’tis bad,” said Grandfer Cantle, shaking his head.\n\n“Mother know’d ’twas no moon, for she asked another woman that had an almanac, as she did whenever a boy was born to her, because of the saying, ‘No moon, no man,’ which made her afeard every man-child she had. Do ye really think it serious, Mister Fairway, that there was no moon?”\n\n“Yes. ‘No moon, no man.’ ’Tis one of the truest sayings ever spit out. The boy never comes to anything that’s born at new moon. A bad job for thee, Christian, that you should have showed your nose then of all days in the month.”\n\n“I suppose the moon was terrible full when you were born?” said Christian, with a look of hopeless admiration at Fairway.\n\n“Well, ’a was not new,” Mr. Fairway replied, with a disinterested gaze.\n\n“I’d sooner go without drink at Lammas-tide than be a man of no moon,” continued Christian, in the same shattered recitative. “’Tis said I be only the rames of a man, and no good for my race at all; and I suppose that’s the cause o’t.”\n\n“Ay,” said Grandfer Cantle, somewhat subdued in spirit; “and yet his mother cried for scores of hours when ’a was a boy, for fear he should outgrow hisself and go for a soldier.”\n\n“Well, there’s many just as bad as he.” said Fairway.\n\n“Wethers must live their time as well as other sheep, poor soul.”\n\n“So perhaps I shall rub on? Ought I to be afeared o’ nights, Master Fairway?”\n\n“You’ll have to lie alone all your life; and ’tis not to married couples but to single sleepers that a ghost shows himself when ’a do come. One has been seen lately, too. A very strange one.”\n\n“No—don’t talk about it if ’tis agreeable of ye not to! ’Twill make my skin crawl when I think of it in bed alone. But you will—ah, you will, I know, Timothy; and I shall dream all night o’t! A very strange one? What sort of a spirit did ye mean when ye said, a very strange one, Timothy?—no, no—don’t tell me.”\n\n“I don’t half believe in spirits myself. But I think it ghostly enough—what I was told. ’Twas a little boy that zid it.”\n\n“What was it like?—no, don’t—”\n\n“A red one. Yes, most ghosts be white; but this is as if it had been dipped in blood.”\n\nChristian drew a deep breath without letting it expand his body, and Humphrey said, “Where has it been seen?”\n\n“Not exactly here; but in this same heth. But ’tisn’t a thing to talk about. What do ye say,” continued Fairway in brisker tones, and turning upon them as if the idea had not been Grandfer Cantle’s—“what do you say to giving the new man and wife a bit of a song tonight afore we go to bed—being their wedding-day? When folks are just married ’tis as well to look glad o’t, since looking sorry won’t unjoin ’em. I am no drinker, as we know, but when the womenfolk and youngsters have gone home we can drop down across to the Quiet Woman, and strike up a ballet in front of the married folks’ door. ’Twill please the young wife, and that’s what I should like to do, for many’s the skinful I’ve had at her hands when she lived with her aunt at Blooms-End.”\n\n“Hey? And so we will!” said Grandfer Cantle, turning so briskly that his copper seals swung extravagantly. “I’m as dry as a kex with biding up here in the wind, and I haven’t seen the colour of drink since nammet-time today. ’Tis said that the last brew at the Woman is very pretty drinking. And, neighbours, if we should be a little late in the finishing, why, tomorrow’s Sunday, and we can sleep it off?”\n\n“Grandfer Cantle! you take things very careless for an old man,” said the wide woman.\n\n“I take things careless; I do—too careless to please the women! Klk! I’ll sing the ‘Jovial Crew,’ or any other song, when a weak old man would cry his eyes out. Jown it; I am up for anything.\n\n“The king′ look’d o′-ver his left′ shoul-der′, And a grim′ look look′-ed hee′, Earl Mar′-shal, he said′, but for′ my oath′ Or hang′-ed thou′ shouldst bee′.”\n\n“Well, that’s what we’ll do,” said Fairway. “We’ll give ’em a song, an’ it please the Lord. What’s the good of Thomasin’s cousin Clym a-coming home after the deed’s done? He should have come afore, if so be he wanted to stop it, and marry her himself.”\n\n“Perhaps he’s coming to bide with his mother a little time, as she must feel lonely now the maid’s gone.”\n\n“Now, ’tis very odd, but I never feel lonely—no, not at all,” said Grandfer Cantle. “I am as brave in the nighttime as a’ admiral!”\n\nThe bonfire was by this time beginning to sink low, for the fuel had not been of that substantial sort which can support a blaze long. Most of the other fires within the wide horizon were also dwindling weak. Attentive observation of their brightness, colour, and length of existence would have revealed the quality of the material burnt, and through that, to some extent the natural produce of the district in which each bonfire was situate. The clear, kingly effulgence that had characterized the majority expressed a heath and furze country like their own, which in one direction extended an unlimited number of miles; the rapid flares and extinctions at other points of the compass showed the lightest of fuel—straw, beanstalks, and the usual waste from arable land. The most enduring of all—steady unaltering eyes like Planets—signified wood, such as hazel-branches, thorn-faggots, and stout billets. Fires of the last-mentioned materials were rare, and though comparatively small in magnitude beside the transient blazes, now began to get the best of them by mere long continuance. The great ones had perished, but these remained. They occupied the remotest visible positions—sky-backed summits rising out of rich coppice and plantation districts to the north, where the soil was different, and heath foreign and strange.\n\nSave one; and this was the nearest of any, the moon of the whole shining throng. It lay in a direction precisely opposite to that of the little window in the vale below. Its nearness was such that, notwithstanding its actual smallness, its glow infinitely transcended theirs.\n\nThis quiet eye had attracted attention from time to time; and when their own fire had become sunken and dim it attracted more; some even of the wood fires more recently lighted had reached their decline, but no change was perceptible here.\n\n“To be sure, how near that fire is!” said Fairway. “Seemingly. I can see a fellow of some sort walking round it. Little and good must be said of that fire, surely.”\n\n“I can throw a stone there,” said the boy.\n\n“And so can I!” said Grandfer Cantle.\n\n“No, no, you can’t, my sonnies. That fire is not much less than a mile off, for all that ’a seems so near.”\n\n“’Tis in the heath, but no furze,” said the turf-cutter.\n\n“’Tis cleft-wood, that’s what ’tis,” said Timothy Fairway. “Nothing would burn like that except clean timber. And ’tis on the knap afore the old captain’s house at Mistover. Such a queer mortal as that man is! To have a little fire inside your own bank and ditch, that nobody else may enjoy it or come anigh it! And what a zany an old chap must be, to light a bonfire when there’s no youngsters to please.”\n\n“Cap’n Vye has been for a long walk today, and is quite tired out,” said Grandfer Cantle, “so ’tisn’t likely to be he.”\n\n“And he would hardly afford good fuel like that,” said the wide woman.\n\n“Then it must be his granddaughter,” said Fairway. “Not that a body of her age can want a fire much.”\n\n“She is very strange in her ways, living up there by herself, and such things please her,” said Susan.\n\n“She’s a well-favoured maid enough,” said Humphrey the furze-cutter, “especially when she’s got one of her dandy gowns on.”\n\n“That’s true,” said Fairway. “Well, let her bonfire burn an’t will. Ours is well-nigh out by the look o’t.”\n\n“How dark ’tis now the fire’s gone down!” said Christian Cantle, looking behind him with his hare eyes. “Don’t ye think we’d better get home-along, neighbours? The heth isn’t haunted, I know; but we’d better get home.... Ah, what was that?”\n\n“Only the wind,” said the turf-cutter.\n\n“I don’t think Fifth-of-Novembers ought to be kept up by night except in towns. It should be by day in outstep, ill-accounted places like this!”\n\n“Nonsense, Christian. Lift up your spirits like a man! Susy, dear, you and I will have a jig—hey, my honey?—before ’tis quite too dark to see how well-favoured you be still, though so many summers have passed since your husband, a son of a witch, snapped you up from me.”\n\nThis was addressed to Susan Nunsuch; and the next circumstance of which the beholders were conscious was a vision of the matron’s broad form whisking off towards the space whereon the fire had been kindled. She was lifted bodily by Mr. Fairway’s arm, which had been flung round her waist before she had become aware of his intention. The site of the fire was now merely a circle of ashes flecked with red embers and sparks, the furze having burnt completely away. Once within the circle he whirled her round and round in a dance. She was a woman noisily constructed; in addition to her enclosing framework of whalebone and lath, she wore pattens summer and winter, in wet weather and in dry, to preserve her boots from wear; and when Fairway began to jump about with her, the clicking of the pattens, the creaking of the stays, and her screams of surprise, formed a very audible concert.\n\n“I’ll crack thy numskull for thee, you mandy chap!” said Mrs. Nunsuch, as she helplessly danced round with him, her feet playing like drumsticks among the sparks. “My ankles were all in a fever before, from walking through that prickly furze, and now you must make ’em worse with these vlankers!”\n\nThe vagary of Timothy Fairway was infectious. The turf-cutter seized old Olly Dowden, and, somewhat more gently, poussetted with her likewise. The young men were not slow to imitate the example of their elders, and seized the maids; Grandfer Cantle and his stick jigged in the form of a three-legged object among the rest; and in half a minute all that could be seen on Rainbarrow was a whirling of dark shapes amid a boiling confusion of sparks, which leapt around the dancers as high as their waists. The chief noises were women’s shrill cries, men’s laughter, Susan’s stays and pattens, Olly Dowden’s “heu-heu-heu!” and the strumming of the wind upon the furze-bushes, which formed a kind of tune to the demoniac measure they trod. Christian alone stood aloof, uneasily rocking himself as he murmured, “They ought not to do it—how the vlankers do fly! ’tis tempting the Wicked one, ’tis.”\n\n“What was that?” said one of the lads, stopping.\n\n“Ah—where?” said Christian, hastily closing up to the rest.\n\nThe dancers all lessened their speed.\n\n“’Twas behind you, Christian, that I heard it—down here.”\n\n“Yes—’tis behind me!” Christian said. “Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John, bless the bed that I lie on; four angels guard—”\n\n“Hold your tongue. What is it?” said Fairway.\n\n“Hoi-i-i-i!” cried a voice from the darkness.\n\n“Halloo-o-o-o!” said Fairway.\n\n“Is there any cart track up across here to Mis’ess Yeobright’s, of Blooms-End?” came to them in the same voice, as a long, slim indistinct figure approached the barrow.\n\n“Ought we not to run home as hard as we can, neighbours, as ’tis getting late?” said Christian. “Not run away from one another, you know; run close together, I mean.”\n\n“Scrape up a few stray locks of furze, and make a blaze, so that we can see who the man is,” said Fairway.\n\nWhen the flame arose it revealed a young man in tight raiment, and red from top to toe. “Is there a track across here to Mis’ess Yeobright’s house?” he repeated.\n\n“Ay—keep along the path down there.”\n\n“I mean a way two horses and a van can travel over?”\n\n“Well, yes; you can get up the vale below here with time. The track is rough, but if you’ve got a light your horses may pick along wi’ care. Have ye brought your cart far up, neighbour reddleman?”\n\n“I’ve left it in the bottom, about half a mile back, I stepped on in front to make sure of the way, as ’tis night-time, and I han’t been here for so long.”\n\n“Oh, well you can get up,” said Fairway. “What a turn it did give me when I saw him!” he added to the whole group, the reddleman included. “Lord’s sake, I thought, whatever fiery mommet is this come to trouble us? No slight to your looks, reddleman, for ye bain’t bad-looking in the groundwork, though the finish is queer. My meaning is just to say how curious I felt. I half thought it ’twas the devil or the red ghost the boy told of.”\n\n“It gied me a turn likewise,” said Susan Nunsuch, “for I had a dream last night of a death’s head.”\n\n“Don’t ye talk o’t no more,” said Christian. “If he had a handkerchief over his head he’d look for all the world like the Devil in the picture of the Temptation.”\n\n“Well, thank you for telling me,” said the young reddleman, smiling faintly. “And good night t’ye all.”\n\nHe withdrew from their sight down the barrow.\n\n“I fancy I’ve seen that young man’s face before,” said Humphrey. “But where, or how, or what his name is, I don’t know.”\n\nThe reddleman had not been gone more than a few minutes when another person approached the partially revived bonfire. It proved to be a well-known and respected widow of the neighbourhood, of a standing which can only be expressed by the word genteel. Her face, encompassed by the blackness of the receding heath, showed whitely, and without half-lights, like a cameo.\n\nShe was a woman of middle-age, with well-formed features of the type usually found where perspicacity is the chief quality enthroned within. At moments she seemed to be regarding issues from a Nebo denied to others around. She had something of an estranged mien; the solitude exhaled from the heath was concentrated in this face that had risen from it. The air with which she looked at the heathmen betokened a certain unconcern at their presence, or at what might be their opinions of her for walking in that lonely spot at such an hour, thus indirectly implying that in some respect or other they were not up to her level. The explanation lay in the fact that though her husband had been a small farmer she herself was a curate’s daughter, who had once dreamt of doing better things.\n\nPersons with any weight of character carry, like planets, their atmospheres along with them in their orbits; and the matron who entered now upon the scene could, and usually did, bring her own tone into a company. Her normal manner among the heathfolk had that reticence which results from the consciousness of superior communicative power. But the effect of coming into society and light after lonely wandering in darkness is a sociability in the comer above its usual pitch, expressed in the features even more than in words.\n\n“Why, ’tis Mis’ess Yeobright,” said Fairway. “Mis’ess Yeobright, not ten minutes ago a man was here asking for you—a reddleman.”\n\n“What did he want?” said she.\n\n“He didn’t tell us.”\n\n“Something to sell, I suppose; what it can be I am at a loss to understand.”\n\n“I am glad to hear that your son Mr. Clym is coming home at Christmas, ma’am,” said Sam, the turf-cutter. “What a dog he used to be for bonfires!”\n\n“Yes. I believe he is coming,” she said.\n\n“He must be a fine fellow by this time,” said Fairway.\n\n“He is a man now,” she replied quietly.\n\n“’Tis very lonesome for ’ee in the heth tonight, mis’ess,” said Christian, coming from the seclusion he had hitherto maintained. “Mind you don’t get lost. Egdon Heth is a bad place to get lost in, and the winds do huffle queerer tonight than ever I heard ’em afore. Them that know Egdon best have been pixy-led here at times.”\n\n“Is that you, Christian?” said Mrs. Yeobright. “What made you hide away from me?”\n\n“’Twas that I didn’t know you in this light, mis’ess; and being a man of the mournfullest make, I was scared a little, that’s all. Oftentimes if you could see how terrible down I get in my mind, ’twould make ’ee quite nervous for fear I should die by my hand.”\n\n“You don’t take after your father,” said Mrs. Yeobright, looking towards the fire, where Grandfer Cantle, with some want of originality, was dancing by himself among the sparks, as the others had done before.\n\n“Now, Grandfer,” said Timothy Fairway, “we are ashamed of ye. A reverent old patriarch man as you be—seventy if a day—to go hornpiping like that by yourself!”\n\n“A harrowing old man, Mis’ess Yeobright,” said Christian despondingly. “I wouldn’t live with him a week, so playward as he is, if I could get away.”\n\n“’Twould be more seemly in ye to stand still and welcome Mis’ess Yeobright, and you the venerablest here, Grandfer Cantle,” said the besom-woman.\n\n“Faith, and so it would,” said the reveller checking himself repentantly. “I’ve such a bad memory, Mis’ess Yeobright, that I forget how I’m looked up to by the rest of ’em. My spirits must be wonderful good, you’ll say? But not always. ’Tis a weight upon a man to be looked up to as commander, and I often feel it.”\n\n“I am sorry to stop the talk,” said Mrs. Yeobright. “But I must be leaving you now. I was passing down the Anglebury Road, towards my niece’s new home, who is returning tonight with her husband; and seeing the bonfire and hearing Olly’s voice among the rest I came up here to learn what was going on. I should like her to walk with me, as her way is mine.”\n\n“Ay, sure, ma’am, I’m just thinking of moving,” said Olly.\n\n“Why, you’ll be safe to meet the reddleman that I told ye of,” said Fairway. “He’s only gone back to get his van. We heard that your niece and her husband were coming straight home as soon as they were married, and we are going down there shortly, to give ’em a song o’ welcome.”\n\n“Thank you indeed,” said Mrs. Yeobright.\n\n“But we shall take a shorter cut through the furze than you can go with long clothes; so we won’t trouble you to wait.”\n\n“Very well—are you ready, Olly?”\n\n“Yes, ma’am. And there’s a light shining from your niece’s window, see. It will help to keep us in the path.”\n\nShe indicated the faint light at the bottom of the valley which Fairway had pointed out; and the two women descended the tumulus.\n\nIV. The Halt on the Turnpike Road\nDown, downward they went, and yet further down—their descent at each step seeming to outmeasure their advance. Their skirts were scratched noisily by the furze, their shoulders brushed by the ferns, which, though dead and dry, stood erect as when alive, no sufficient winter weather having as yet arrived to beat them down. Their Tartarean situation might by some have been called an imprudent one for two unattended women. But these shaggy recesses were at all seasons a familiar surrounding to Olly and Mrs. Yeobright; and the addition of darkness lends no frightfulness to the face of a friend.\n\n“And so Tamsin has married him at last,” said Olly, when the incline had become so much less steep that their foot-steps no longer required undivided attention.\n\nMrs. Yeobright answered slowly, “Yes; at last.”\n\n“How you will miss her—living with ’ee as a daughter, as she always have.”\n\n“I do miss her.”\n\nOlly, though without the tact to perceive when remarks were untimely, was saved by her very simplicity from rendering them offensive. Questions that would have been resented in others she could ask with impunity. This accounted for Mrs. Yeobright’s acquiescence in the revival of an evidently sore subject.\n\n“I was quite strook to hear you’d agreed to it, ma’am, that I was,” continued the besom-maker.\n\n“You were not more struck by it than I should have been last year this time, Olly. There are a good many sides to that wedding. I could not tell you all of them, even if I tried.”\n\n“I felt myself that he was hardly solid-going enough to mate with your family. Keeping an inn—what is it? But ’a’s clever, that’s true, and they say he was an engineering gentleman once, but has come down by being too outwardly given.”\n\n“I saw that, upon the whole, it would be better she should marry where she wished.”\n\n“Poor little thing, her feelings got the better of her, no doubt. ’Tis nature. Well, they may call him what they will—he’ve several acres of heth-ground broke up here, besides the public house, and the heth-croppers, and his manners be quite like a gentleman’s. And what’s done cannot be undone.”\n\n“It cannot,” said Mrs. Yeobright. “See, here’s the wagon-track at last. Now we shall get along better.”\n\nThe wedding subject was no further dwelt upon; and soon a faint diverging path was reached, where they parted company, Olly first begging her companion to remind Mr. Wildeve that he had not sent her sick husband the bottle of wine promised on the occasion of his marriage. The besom-maker turned to the left towards her own house, behind a spur of the hill, and Mrs. Yeobright followed the straight track, which further on joined the highway by the Quiet Woman Inn, whither she supposed her niece to have returned with Wildeve from their wedding at Anglebury that day.\n\nShe first reached Wildeve’s Patch, as it was called, a plot of land redeemed from the heath, and after long and laborious years brought into cultivation. The man who had discovered that it could be tilled died of the labour; the man who succeeded him in possession ruined himself in fertilizing it. Wildeve came like Amerigo Vespucci, and received the honours due to those who had gone before.\n\nWhen Mrs. Yeobright had drawn near to the inn, and was about to enter, she saw a horse and vehicle some two hundred yards beyond it, coming towards her, a man walking alongside with a lantern in his hand. It was soon evident that this was the reddleman who had inquired for her. Instead of entering the inn at once, she walked by it and towards the van.\n\nThe conveyance came close, and the man was about to pass her with little notice, when she turned to him and said, “I think you have been inquiring for me? I am Mrs. Yeobright of Blooms-End.”\n\nThe reddleman started, and held up his finger. He stopped the horses, and beckoned to her to withdraw with him a few yards aside, which she did, wondering.\n\n“You don’t know me, ma’am, I suppose?” he said.\n\n“I do not,” said she. “Why, yes, I do! You are young Venn—your father was a dairyman somewhere here?”\n\n“Yes; and I knew your niece, Miss Tamsin, a little. I have something bad to tell you.”\n\n“About her—no! She has just come home, I believe, with her husband. They arranged to return this afternoon—to the inn beyond here.”\n\n“She’s not there.”\n\n“How do you know?”\n\n“Because she’s here. She’s in my van,” he added slowly.\n\n“What new trouble has come?” murmured Mrs. Yeobright, putting her hand over her eyes.\n\n“I can’t explain much, ma’am. All I know is that, as I was going along the road this morning, about a mile out of Anglebury, I heard something trotting after me like a doe, and looking round there she was, white as death itself. ‘Oh, Diggory Venn!’ she said, ‘I thought ’twas you—will you help me? I am in trouble.’”\n\n“How did she know your Christian name?” said Mrs. Yeobright doubtingly.\n\n“I had met her as a lad before I went away in this trade. She asked then if she might ride, and then down she fell in a faint. I picked her up and put her in, and there she has been ever since. She has cried a good deal, but she has hardly spoke; all she has told me being that she was to have been married this morning. I tried to get her to eat something, but she couldn’t; and at last she fell asleep.”\n\n“Let me see her at once,” said Mrs. Yeobright, hastening towards the van.\n\nThe reddleman followed with the lantern, and, stepping up first, assisted Mrs. Yeobright to mount beside him. On the door being opened she perceived at the end of the van an extemporized couch, around which was hung apparently all the drapery that the reddleman possessed, to keep the occupant of the little couch from contact with the red materials of his trade. A young girl lay thereon, covered with a cloak. She was asleep, and the light of the lantern fell upon her features.\n\nA fair, sweet, and honest country face was revealed, reposing in a nest of wavy chestnut hair. It was between pretty and beautiful. Though her eyes were closed, one could easily imagine the light necessarily shining in them as the culmination of the luminous workmanship around. The groundwork of the face was hopefulness; but over it now lay like a foreign substance a film of anxiety and grief. The grief had been there so shortly as to have abstracted nothing of the bloom, and had as yet but given a dignity to what it might eventually undermine. The scarlet of her lips had not had time to abate, and just now it appeared still more intense by the absence of the neighbouring and more transient colour of her cheek. The lips frequently parted, with a murmur of words. She seemed to belong rightly to a madrigal—to require viewing through rhyme and harmony.\n\nOne thing at least was obvious: she was not made to be looked at thus. The reddleman had appeared conscious of as much, and, while Mrs. Yeobright looked in upon her, he cast his eyes aside with a delicacy which well became him. The sleeper apparently thought so too, for the next moment she opened her own.\n\nThe lips then parted with something of anticipation, something more of doubt; and her several thoughts and fractions of thoughts, as signalled by the changes on her face, were exhibited by the light to the utmost nicety. An ingenuous, transparent life was disclosed, as if the flow of her existence could be seen passing within her. She understood the scene in a moment.\n\n“O yes, it is I, Aunt,” she cried. “I know how frightened you are, and how you cannot believe it; but all the same, it is I who have come home like this!”\n\n“Tamsin, Tamsin!” said Mrs. Yeobright, stooping over the young woman and kissing her. “O my dear girl!”\n\nThomasin was now on the verge of a sob, but by an unexpected self-command she uttered no sound. With a gentle panting breath she sat upright.\n\n“I did not expect to see you in this state, any more than you me,” she went on quickly. “Where am I, Aunt?”\n\n“Nearly home, my dear. In Egdon Bottom. What dreadful thing is it?”\n\n“I’ll tell you in a moment. So near, are we? Then I will get out and walk. I want to go home by the path.”\n\n“But this kind man who has done so much will, I am sure, take you right on to my house?” said the aunt, turning to the reddleman, who had withdrawn from the front of the van on the awakening of the girl, and stood in the road.\n\n“Why should you think it necessary to ask me? I will, of course,” said he.\n\n“He is indeed kind,” murmured Thomasin. “I was once acquainted with him, Aunt, and when I saw him today I thought I should prefer his van to any conveyance of a stranger. But I’ll walk now. Reddleman, stop the horses, please.”\n\nThe man regarded her with tender reluctance, but stopped them\n\nAunt and niece then descended from the van, Mrs. Yeobright saying to its owner, “I quite recognize you now. What made you change from the nice business your father left you?”\n\n“Well, I did,” he said, and looked at Thomasin, who blushed a little. “Then you’ll not be wanting me any more tonight, ma’am?”\n\nMrs. Yeobright glanced around at the dark sky, at the hills, at the perishing bonfires, and at the lighted window of the inn they had neared. “I think not,” she said, “since Thomasin wishes to walk. We can soon run up the path and reach home—we know it well.”\n\nAnd after a few further words they parted, the reddleman moving onwards with his van, and the two women remaining standing in the road. As soon as the vehicle and its driver had withdrawn so far as to be beyond all possible reach of her voice, Mrs. Yeobright turned to her niece.\n\n“Now, Thomasin,” she said sternly, “what’s the meaning of this disgraceful performance?”\n\nV. Perplexity among Honest People\nThomasin looked as if quite overcome by her aunt’s change of manner. “It means just what it seems to mean: I am—not married,” she replied faintly. “Excuse me—for humiliating you, Aunt, by this mishap—I am sorry for it. But I cannot help it.”\n\n“Me? Think of yourself first.”\n\n“It was nobody’s fault. When we got there the parson wouldn’t marry us because of some trifling irregularity in the license.”\n\n“What irregularity?”\n\n“I don’t know. Mr. Wildeve can explain. I did not think when I went away this morning that I should come back like this.” It being dark, Thomasin allowed her emotion to escape her by the silent way of tears, which could roll down her cheek unseen.\n\n“I could almost say that it serves you right—if I did not feel that you don’t deserve it,” continued Mrs. Yeobright, who, possessing two distinct moods in close contiguity, a gentle mood and an angry, flew from one to the other without the least warning. “Remember, Thomasin, this business was none of my seeking; from the very first, when you began to feel foolish about that man, I warned you he would not make you happy. I felt it so strongly that I did what I would never have believed myself capable of doing—stood up in the church, and made myself the public talk for weeks. But having once consented, I don’t submit to these fancies without good reason. Marry him you must after this.”\n\n“Do you think I wish to do otherwise for one moment?” said Thomasin, with a heavy sigh. “I know how wrong it was of me to love him, but don’t pain me by talking like that, Aunt! You would not have had me stay there with him, would you?—and your house is the only home I have to return to. He says we can be married in a day or two.”\n\n“I wish he had never seen you.”\n\n“Very well; then I will be the miserablest woman in the world, and not let him see me again. No, I won’t have him!”\n\n“It is too late to speak so. Come with me. I am going to the inn to see if he has returned. Of course I shall get to the bottom of this story at once. Mr. Wildeve must not suppose he can play tricks upon me, or any belonging to me.”\n\n“It was not that. The license was wrong, and he couldn’t get another the same day. He will tell you in a moment how it was, if he comes.”\n\n“Why didn’t he bring you back?”\n\n“That was me!” again sobbed Thomasin. “When I found we could not be married I didn’t like to come back with him, and I was very ill. Then I saw Diggory Venn, and was glad to get him to take me home. I cannot explain it any better, and you must be angry with me if you will.”\n\n“I shall see about that,” said Mrs. Yeobright; and they turned towards the inn, known in the neighbourhood as the Quiet Woman, the sign of which represented the figure of a matron carrying her head under her arm. The front of the house was towards the heath and Rainbarrow, whose dark shape seemed to threaten it from the sky. Upon the door was a neglected brass plate, bearing the unexpected inscription, “Mr. Wildeve, Engineer”—a useless yet cherished relic from the time when he had been started in that profession in an office at Budmouth by those who had hoped much from him, and had been disappointed. The garden was at the back, and behind this ran a still deep stream, forming the margin of the heath in that direction, meadow-land appearing beyond the stream.\n\nBut the thick obscurity permitted only skylines to be visible of any scene at present. The water at the back of the house could be heard, idly spinning whirpools in its creep between the rows of dry feather-headed reeds which formed a stockade along each bank. Their presence was denoted by sounds as of a congregation praying humbly, produced by their rubbing against each other in the slow wind.\n\nThe window, whence the candlelight had shone up the vale to the eyes of the bonfire group, was uncurtained, but the sill lay too high for a pedestrian on the outside to look over it into the room. A vast shadow, in which could be dimly traced portions of a masculine contour, blotted half the ceiling.\n\n“He seems to be at home,” said Mrs. Yeobright.\n\n“Must I come in, too, Aunt?” asked Thomasin faintly. “I suppose not; it would be wrong.”\n\n“You must come, certainly—to confront him, so that he may make no false representations to me. We shall not be five minutes in the house, and then we’ll walk home.”\n\nEntering the open passage, she tapped at the door of the private parlour, unfastened it, and looked in.\n\nThe back and shoulders of a man came between Mrs. Yeobright’s eyes and the fire. Wildeve, whose form it was, immediately turned, arose, and advanced to meet his visitors.\n\nHe was quite a young man, and of the two properties, form and motion, the latter first attracted the eye in him. The grace of his movement was singular—it was the pantomimic expression of a lady-killing career. Next came into notice the more material qualities, among which was a profuse crop of hair impending over the top of his face, lending to his forehead the high-cornered outline of an early Gothic shield; and a neck which was smooth and round as a cylinder. The lower half of his figure was of light build. Altogether he was one in whom no man would have seen anything to admire, and in whom no woman would have seen anything to dislike.\n\nHe discerned the young girl’s form in the passage, and said, “Thomasin, then, has reached home. How could you leave me in that way, darling?” And turning to Mrs. Yeobright—“It was useless to argue with her. She would go, and go alone.”\n\n“But what’s the meaning of it all?” demanded Mrs. Yeobright haughtily.\n\n“Take a seat,” said Wildeve, placing chairs for the two women. “Well, it was a very stupid mistake, but such mistakes will happen. The license was useless at Anglebury. It was made out for Budmouth, but as I didn’t read it I wasn’t aware of that.”\n\n“But you had been staying at Anglebury?”\n\n“No. I had been at Budmouth—till two days ago—and that was where I had intended to take her; but when I came to fetch her we decided upon Anglebury, forgetting that a new license would be necessary. There was not time to get to Budmouth afterwards.”\n\n“I think you are very much to blame,” said Mrs. Yeobright.\n\n“It was quite my fault we chose Anglebury,” Thomasin pleaded. “I proposed it because I was not known there.”\n\n“I know so well that I am to blame that you need not remind me of it,” replied Wildeve shortly.\n\n“Such things don’t happen for nothing,” said the aunt. “It is a great slight to me and my family; and when it gets known there will be a very unpleasant time for us. How can she look her friends in the face tomorrow? It is a very great injury, and one I cannot easily forgive. It may even reflect on her character.”\n\n“Nonsense,” said Wildeve.\n\nThomasin’s large eyes had flown from the face of one to the face of the other during this discussion, and she now said anxiously, “Will you allow me, Aunt, to talk it over alone with Damon for five minutes? Will you, Damon?”\n\n“Certainly, dear,” said Wildeve, “if your aunt will excuse us.” He led her into an adjoining room, leaving Mrs. Yeobright by the fire.\n\nAs soon as they were alone, and the door closed, Thomasin said, turning up her pale, tearful face to him, “It is killing me, this, Damon! I did not mean to part from you in anger at Anglebury this morning; but I was frightened and hardly knew what I said. I’ve not let Aunt know how much I suffered today; and it is so hard to command my face and voice, and to smile as if it were a slight thing to me; but I try to do so, that she may not be still more indignant with you. I know you could not help it, dear, whatever Aunt may think.”\n\n“She is very unpleasant.”\n\n“Yes,” Thomasin murmured, “and I suppose I seem so now.... Damon, what do you mean to do about me?”\n\n“Do about you?”\n\n“Yes. Those who don’t like you whisper things which at moments make me doubt you. We mean to marry, I suppose, don’t we?”\n\n“Of course we do. We have only to go to Budmouth on Monday, and we marry at once.”\n\n“Then do let us go!—O Damon, what you make me say!” She hid her face in her handkerchief. “Here am I asking you to marry me, when by rights you ought to be on your knees imploring me, your cruel mistress, not to refuse you, and saying it would break your heart if I did. I used to think it would be pretty and sweet like that; but how different!”\n\n“Yes, real life is never at all like that.”\n\n“But I don’t care personally if it never takes place,” she added with a little dignity; “no, I can live without you. It is Aunt I think of. She is so proud, and thinks so much of her family respectability, that she will be cut down with mortification if this story should get abroad before—it is done. My cousin Clym, too, will be much wounded.”\n\n“Then he will be very unreasonable. In fact, you are all rather unreasonable.”\n\nThomasin coloured a little, and not with love. But whatever the momentary feeling which caused that flush in her, it went as it came, and she humbly said, “I never mean to be, if I can help it. I merely feel that you have my aunt to some extent in your power at last.”\n\n“As a matter of justice it is almost due to me,” said Wildeve. “Think what I have gone through to win her consent; the insult that it is to any man to have the banns forbidden—the double insult to a man unlucky enough to be cursed with sensitiveness, and blue demons, and Heaven knows what, as I am. I can never forget those banns. A harsher man would rejoice now in the power I have of turning upon your aunt by going no further in the business.”\n\nShe looked wistfully at him with her sorrowful eyes as he said those words, and her aspect showed that more than one person in the room could deplore the possession of sensitiveness. Seeing that she was really suffering he seemed disturbed and added, “This is merely a reflection you know. I have not the least intention to refuse to complete the marriage, Tamsie mine—I could not bear it.”\n\n“You could not, I know!” said the fair girl, brightening. “You, who cannot bear the sight of pain in even an insect, or any disagreeable sound, or unpleasant smell even, will not long cause pain to me and mine.”\n\n“I will not, if I can help it.”\n\n“Your hand upon it, Damon.”\n\nHe carelessly gave her his hand.\n\n“Ah, by my crown, what’s that?” he said suddenly.\n\nThere fell upon their ears the sound of numerous voices singing in front of the house. Among these, two made themselves prominent by their peculiarity: one was a very strong bass, the other a wheezy thin piping. Thomasin recognized them as belonging to Timothy Fairway and Grandfer Cantle respectively.\n\n“What does it mean—it is not skimmity-riding, I hope?” she said, with a frightened gaze at Wildeve.\n\n“Of course not; no, it is that the heath-folk have come to sing to us a welcome. This is intolerable!” He began pacing about, the men outside singing cheerily—\n\n“He told′ her that she′ was the joy′ of his life′, And if′ she’d con-sent′ he would make her his wife′; She could′ not refuse′ him; to church′ so they went′, Young Will was forgot′, and young Sue′ was content′; And then′ was she kiss’d′ and set down′ on his knee′, No man′ in the world′ was so lov′-ing as he′!”\n\nMrs. Yeobright burst in from the outer room. “Thomasin, Thomasin!” she said, looking indignantly at Wildeve; “here’s a pretty exposure! Let us escape at once. Come!”\n\nIt was, however, too late to get away by the passage. A rugged knocking had begun upon the door of the front room. Wildeve, who had gone to the window, came back.\n\n“Stop!” he said imperiously, putting his hand upon Mrs. Yeobright’s arm. “We are regularly besieged. There are fifty of them out there if there’s one. You stay in this room with Thomasin; I’ll go out and face them. You must stay now, for my sake, till they are gone, so that it may seem as if all was right. Come, Tamsie dear, don’t go making a scene—we must marry after this; that you can see as well as I. Sit still, that’s all—and don’t speak much. I’ll manage them. Blundering fools!”\n\nHe pressed the agitated girl into a seat, returned to the outer room and opened the door. Immediately outside, in the passage, appeared Grandfer Cantle singing in concert with those still standing in front of the house. He came into the room and nodded abstractedly to Wildeve, his lips still parted, and his features excruciatingly strained in the emission of the chorus. This being ended, he said heartily, “Here’s welcome to the new-made couple, and God bless ’em!”\n\n“Thank you,” said Wildeve, with dry resentment, his face as gloomy as a thunderstorm.\n\nAt the Grandfer’s heels now came the rest of the group, which included Fairway, Christian, Sam the turf-cutter, Humphrey, and a dozen others. All smiled upon Wildeve, and upon his tables and chairs likewise, from a general sense of friendliness towards the articles as well as towards their owner.\n\n“We be not here afore Mrs. Yeobright after all,” said Fairway, recognizing the matron’s bonnet through the glass partition which divided the public apartment they had entered from the room where the women sat. “We struck down across, d’ye see, Mr. Wildeve, and she went round by the path.”\n\n“And I see the young bride’s little head!” said Grandfer, peeping in the same direction, and discerning Thomasin, who was waiting beside her aunt in a miserable and awkward way. “Not quite settled in yet—well, well, there’s plenty of time.”\n\nWildeve made no reply; and probably feeling that the sooner he treated them the sooner they would go, he produced a stone jar, which threw a warm halo over matters at once.\n\n“That’s a drop of the right sort, I can see,” said Grandfer Cantle, with the air of a man too well-mannered to show any hurry to taste it.\n\n“Yes,” said Wildeve, “’tis some old mead. I hope you will like it.”\n\n“O ay!” replied the guests, in the hearty tones natural when the words demanded by politeness coincide with those of deepest feeling. “There isn’t a prettier drink under the sun.”\n\n“I’ll take my oath there isn’t,” added Grandfer Cantle. “All that can be said against mead is that ’tis rather heady, and apt to lie about a man a good while. But tomorrow’s Sunday, thank God.”\n\n“I feel’d for all the world like some bold soldier after I had had some once,” said Christian.\n\n“You shall feel so again,” said Wildeve, with condescension, “Cups or glasses, gentlemen?”\n\n“Well, if you don’t mind, we’ll have the beaker, and pass ’en round; ’tis better than heling it out in dribbles.”\n\n“Jown the slippery glasses,” said Grandfer Cantle. “What’s the good of a thing that you can’t put down in the ashes to warm, hey, neighbours; that’s what I ask?”\n\n“Right, Grandfer,” said Sam; and the mead then circulated.\n\n“Well,” said Timothy Fairway, feeling demands upon his praise in some form or other, “’tis a worthy thing to be married, Mr. Wildeve; and the woman you’ve got is a dimant, so says I. Yes,” he continued, to Grandfer Cantle, raising his voice so as to be heard through the partition, “her father (inclining his head towards the inner room) was as good a feller as ever lived. He always had his great indignation ready against anything underhand.”\n\n“Is that very dangerous?” said Christian.\n\n“And there were few in these parts that were upsides with him,” said Sam. “Whenever a club walked he’d play the clarinet in the band that marched before ’em as if he’d never touched anything but a clarinet all his life. And then, when they got to church door he’d throw down the clarinet, mount the gallery, snatch up the bass viol, and rozum away as if he’d never played anything but a bass viol. Folk would say—folk that knowed what a true stave was—‘Surely, surely that’s never the same man that I saw handling the clarinet so masterly by now!’”\n\n“I can mind it,” said the furze-cutter. “’Twas a wonderful thing that one body could hold it all and never mix the fingering.”\n\n“There was Kingsbere church likewise,” Fairway recommenced, as one opening a new vein of the same mine of interest.\n\nWildeve breathed the breath of one intolerably bored, and glanced through the partition at the prisoners.\n\n“He used to walk over there of a Sunday afternoon to visit his old acquaintance Andrew Brown, the first clarinet there; a good man enough, but rather screechy in his music, if you can mind?”\n\n“’A was.”\n\n“And neighbour Yeobright would take Andrey’s place for some part of the service, to let Andrey have a bit of a nap, as any friend would naturally do.”\n\n“As any friend would,” said Grandfer Cantle, the other listeners expressing the same accord by the shorter way of nodding their heads.\n\n“No sooner was Andrey asleep and the first whiff of neighbour Yeobright’s wind had got inside Andrey’s clarinet than everyone in church feeled in a moment there was a great soul among ’em. All heads would turn, and they’d say, ‘Ah, I thought ’twas he!’ One Sunday I can well mind—a bass viol day that time, and Yeobright had brought his own. ’Twas the Hundred-and-thirty-third to ‘Lydia’; and when they’d come to ‘Ran down his beard and o’er his robes its costly moisture shed,’ neighbour Yeobright, who had just warmed to his work, drove his bow into them strings that glorious grand that he e’en a’most sawed the bass viol into two pieces. Every winder in church rattled as if ’twere a thunderstorm. Old Pa’son Williams lifted his hands in his great holy surplice as natural as if he’d been in common clothes, and seemed to say hisself, ‘Oh for such a man in our parish!’ But not a soul in Kingsbere could hold a candle to Yeobright.”\n\n“Was it quite safe when the winder shook?” Christian inquired.\n\nHe received no answer, all for the moment sitting rapt in admiration of the performance described. As with Farinelli’s singing before the princesses, Sheridan’s renowned Begum Speech, and other such examples, the fortunate condition of its being for ever lost to the world invested the deceased Mr. Yeobright’s tour de force on that memorable afternoon with a cumulative glory which comparative criticism, had that been possible, might considerably have shorn down.\n\n“He was the last you’d have expected to drop off in the prime of life,” said Humphrey.\n\n“Ah, well; he was looking for the earth some months afore he went. At that time women used to run for smocks and gown-pieces at Greenhill Fair, and my wife that is now, being a long-legged slittering maid, hardly husband-high, went with the rest of the maidens, for ’a was a good runner afore she got so heavy. When she came home I said—we were then just beginning to walk together—‘What have ye got, my honey?’ ‘I’ve won—well, I’ve won—a gown-piece,’ says she, her colours coming up in a moment. ’Tis a smock for a crown, I thought; and so it turned out. Ay, when I think what she’ll say to me now without a mossel of red in her face, it do seem strange that ’a wouldn’t say such a little thing then.... However, then she went on, and that’s what made me bring up the story, ‘Well, whatever clothes I’ve won, white or figured, for eyes to see or for eyes not to see’ (’a could do a pretty stroke of modesty in those days), ‘I’d sooner have lost it than have seen what I have. Poor Mr. Yeobright was took bad directly he reached the fair ground, and was forced to go home again.’ That was the last time he ever went out of the parish.”\n\n“’A faltered on from one day to another, and then we heard he was gone.”\n\n“D’ye think he had great pain when ’a died?” said Christian.\n\n“O no—quite different. Nor any pain of mind. He was lucky enough to be God A’mighty’s own man.”\n\n“And other folk—d’ye think ’twill be much pain to ’em, Mister Fairway?”\n\n“That depends on whether they be afeard.”\n\n“I bain’t afeard at all, I thank God!” said Christian strenuously. “I’m glad I bain’t, for then ’twon’t pain me.... I don’t think I be afeard—or if I be I can’t help it, and I don’t deserve to suffer. I wish I was not afeard at all!”\n\nThere was a solemn silence, and looking from the window, which was unshuttered and unblinded, Timothy said, “Well, what a fess little bonfire that one is, out by Cap’n Vye’s! ’Tis burning just the same now as ever, upon my life.”\n\nAll glances went through the window, and nobody noticed that Wildeve disguised a brief, telltale look. Far away up the sombre valley of heath, and to the right of Rainbarrow, could indeed be seen the light, small, but steady and persistent as before.\n\n“It was lighted before ours was,” Fairway continued; “and yet every one in the country round is out afore ’n.”\n\n“Perhaps there’s meaning in it!” murmured Christian.\n\n“How meaning?” said Wildeve sharply.\n\nChristian was too scattered to reply, and Timothy helped him.\n\n“He means, sir, that the lonesome dark-eyed creature up there that some say is a witch—ever I should call a fine young woman such a name—is always up to some odd conceit or other; and so perhaps ’tis she.”\n\n“I’d be very glad to ask her in wedlock, if she’d hae me and take the risk of her wild dark eyes ill-wishing me,” said Grandfer Cantle staunchly.\n\n“Don’t ye say it, Father!” implored Christian.\n\n“Well, be dazed if he who do marry the maid won’t hae an uncommon picture for his best parlour,” said Fairway in a liquid tone, placing down the cup of mead at the end of a good pull.\n\n“And a partner as deep as the North Star,” said Sam, taking up the cup and finishing the little that remained. “Well, really, now I think we must be moving,” said Humphrey, observing the emptiness of the vessel.\n\n“But we’ll gie ’em another song?” said Grandfer Cantle. “I’m as full of notes as a bird!”\n\n“Thank you, Grandfer,” said Wildeve. “But we will not trouble you now. Some other day must do for that—when I have a party.”\n\n“Be jown’d if I don’t learn ten new songs for’t, or I won’t learn a line!” said Grandfer Cantle. “And you may be sure I won’t disappoint ye by biding away, Mr. Wildeve.”\n\n“I quite believe you,” said that gentleman.\n\nAll then took their leave, wishing their entertainer long life and happiness as a married man, with recapitulations which occupied some time. Wildeve attended them to the door, beyond which the deep-dyed upward stretch of heath stood awaiting them, an amplitude of darkness reigning from their feet almost to the zenith, where a definite form first became visible in the lowering forehead of Rainbarrow. Diving into the dense obscurity in a line headed by Sam the turf-cutter, they pursued their trackless way home.\n\nWhen the scratching of the furze against their leggings had fainted upon the ear, Wildeve returned to the room where he had left Thomasin and her aunt. The women were gone.\n\nThey could only have left the house in one way, by the back window; and this was open.\n\nWildeve laughed to himself, remained a moment thinking, and idly returned to the front room. Here his glance fell upon a bottle of wine which stood on the mantelpiece. “Ah—old Dowden!” he murmured; and going to the kitchen door shouted, “Is anybody here who can take something to old Dowden?”\n\nThere was no reply. The room was empty, the lad who acted as his factotum having gone to bed. Wildeve came back, put on his hat, took the bottle, and left the house, turning the key in the door, for there was no guest at the inn tonight. As soon as he was on the road the little bonfire on Mistover Knap again met his eye.\n\n“Still waiting, are you, my lady?” he murmured.\n\nHowever, he did not proceed that way just then; but leaving the hill to the left of him, he stumbled over a rutted road that brought him to a cottage which, like all other habitations on the heath at this hour, was only saved from being visible by a faint shine from its bedroom window. This house was the home of Olly Dowden, the besom-maker, and he entered.\n\nThe lower room was in darkness; but by feeling his way he found a table, whereon he placed the bottle, and a minute later emerged again upon the heath. He stood and looked northeast at the undying little fire—high up above him, though not so high as Rainbarrow.\n\nWe have been told what happens when a woman deliberates; and the epigram is not always terminable with woman, provided that one be in the case, and that a fair one. Wildeve stood, and stood longer, and breathed perplexedly, and then said to himself with resignation, “Yes—by Heaven, I must go to her, I suppose!”\n\nInstead of turning in the direction of home he pressed on rapidly by a path under Rainbarrow towards what was evidently a signal light.\n\nVI. The Figure against the Sky\nWhen the whole Egdon concourse had left the site of the bonfire to its accustomed loneliness, a closely wrapped female figure approached the barrow from that quarter of the heath in which the little fire lay. Had the reddleman been watching he might have recognized her as the woman who had first stood there so singularly, and vanished at the approach of strangers. She ascended to her old position at the top, where the red coals of the perishing fire greeted her like living eyes in the corpse of day. There she stood still around her stretching the vast night atmosphere, whose incomplete darkness in comparison with the total darkness of the heath below it might have represented a venial beside a mortal sin.\n\nThat she was tall and straight in build, that she was lady-like in her movements, was all that could be learnt of her just now, her form being wrapped in a shawl folded in the old cornerwise fashion, and her head in a large kerchief, a protection not superfluous at this hour and place. Her back was towards the wind, which blew from the northwest; but whether she had avoided that aspect because of the chilly gusts which played about her exceptional position, or because her interest lay in the southeast, did not at first appear.\n\nHer reason for standing so dead still as the pivot of this circle of heath-country was just as obscure. Her extraordinary fixity, her conspicuous loneliness, her heedlessness of night, betokened among other things an utter absence of fear. A tract of country unaltered from that sinister condition which made Cæsar anxious every year to get clear of its glooms before the autumnal equinox, a kind of landscape and weather which leads travellers from the South to describe our island as Homer’s Cimmerian land, was not, on the face of it, friendly to women.\n\nIt might reasonably have been supposed that she was listening to the wind, which rose somewhat as the night advanced, and laid hold of the attention. The wind, indeed, seemed made for the scene, as the scene seemed made for the hour. Part of its tone was quite special; what was heard there could be heard nowhere else. Gusts in innumerable series followed each other from the northwest, and when each one of them raced past the sound of its progress resolved into three. Treble, tenor, and bass notes were to be found therein. The general ricochet of the whole over pits and prominences had the gravest pitch of the chime. Next there could be heard the baritone buzz of a holly tree. Below these in force, above them in pitch, a dwindled voice strove hard at a husky tune, which was the peculiar local sound alluded to. Thinner and less immediately traceable than the other two, it was far more impressive than either. In it lay what may be called the linguistic peculiarity of the heath; and being audible nowhere on earth off a heath, it afforded a shadow of reason for the woman’s tenseness, which continued as unbroken as ever.\n\nThroughout the blowing of these plaintive November winds that note bore a great resemblance to the ruins of human song which remain to the throat of fourscore and ten. It was a worn whisper, dry and papery, and it brushed so distinctly across the ear that, by the accustomed, the material minutiæ in which it originated could be realized as by touch. It was the united products of infinitesimal vegetable causes, and these were neither stems, leaves, fruit, blades, prickles, lichen, nor moss.\nThey were the mummied heathbells of the past summer, originally tender and purple, now washed colourless by Michaelmas rains, and dried to dead skins by October suns. So low was an individual sound from these that a combination of hundreds only just emerged from silence, and the myriads of the whole declivity reached the woman’s ear but as a shrivelled and intermittent recitative. Yet scarcely a single accent among the many afloat tonight could have such power to impress a listener with thoughts of its origin. One inwardly saw the infinity of those combined multitudes; and perceived that each of the tiny trumpets was seized on entered, scoured and emerged from by the wind as thoroughly as if it were as vast as a crater.\n\n“The spirit moved them.” A meaning of the phrase forced itself upon the attention; and an emotional listener’s fetichistic mood might have ended in one of more advanced quality. It was not, after all, that the left-hand expanse of old blooms spoke, or the right-hand, or those of the slope in front; but it was the single person of something else speaking through each at once.\n\nSuddenly, on the barrow, there mingled with all this wild rhetoric of night a sound which modulated so naturally into the rest that its beginning and ending were hardly to be distinguished. The bluffs, and the bushes, and the heather-bells had broken silence; at last, so did the woman; and her articulation was but as another phrase of the same discourse as theirs. Thrown out on the winds it became twined in with them, and with them it flew away.\n\nWhat she uttered was a lengthened sighing, apparently at something in her mind which had led to her presence here. There was a spasmodic abandonment about it as if, in allowing herself to utter the sound the woman’s brain had authorized what it could not regulate. One point was evident in this; that she had been existing in a suppressed state, and not in one of languor, or stagnation.\n\nFar away down the valley the faint shine from the window of the inn still lasted on; and a few additional moments proved that the window, or what was within it, had more to do with the woman’s sigh than had either her own actions or the scene immediately around. She lifted her left hand, which held a closed telescope. This she rapidly extended, as if she were well accustomed to the operation, and raising it to her eye directed it towards the light beaming from the inn.\n\nThe handkerchief which had hooded her head was now a little thrown back, her face being somewhat elevated. A profile was visible against the dull monochrome of cloud around her; and it was as though side shadows from the features of Sappho and Mrs. Siddons had converged upwards from the tomb to form an image like neither but suggesting both. This, however, was mere superficiality. In respect of character a face may make certain admissions by its outline; but it fully confesses only in its changes. So much is this the case that what is called the play of the features often helps more in understanding a man or woman than the earnest labours of all the other members together. Thus the night revealed little of her whose form it was embracing, for the mobile parts of her countenance could not be seen.\n\nAt last she gave up her spying attitude, closed the telescope, and turned to the decaying embers. From these no appreciable beams now radiated, except when a more than usually smart gust brushed over their faces and raised a fitful glow which came and went like the blush of a girl. She stooped over the silent circle, and selecting from the brands a piece of stick which bore the largest live coal at its end, brought it to where she had been standing before.\n\nShe held the brand to the ground, blowing the red coal with her mouth at the same time; till it faintly illuminated the sod, and revealed a small object, which turned out to be an hourglass, though she wore a watch. She blew long enough to show that the sand had all slipped through.\n\n“Ah!” she said, as if surprised.\n\nThe light raised by her breath had been very fitful, and a momentary irradiation of flesh was all that it had disclosed of her face. That consisted of two matchless lips and a cheek only, her head being still enveloped. She threw away the stick, took the glass in her hand, the telescope under her arm, and moved on.\n\nAlong the ridge ran a faint foot-track, which the lady followed. Those who knew it well called it a path; and, while a mere visitor would have passed it unnoticed even by day, the regular haunters of the heath were at no loss for it at midnight. The whole secret of following these incipient paths, when there was not light enough in the atmosphere to show a turnpike road, lay in the development of the sense of touch in the feet, which comes with years of night-rambling in little-trodden spots. To a walker practised in such places a difference between impact on maiden herbage, and on the crippled stalks of a slight footway, is perceptible through the thickest boot or shoe.\n\nThe solitary figure who walked this beat took no notice of the windy tune still played on the dead heathbells. She did not turn her head to look at a group of dark creatures further on, who fled from her presence as she skirted a ravine where they fed. They were about a score of the small wild ponies known as heath-croppers. They roamed at large on the undulations of Egdon, but in numbers too few to detract much from the solitude.\n\nThe pedestrian noticed nothing just now, and a clue to her abstraction was afforded by a trivial incident. A bramble caught hold of her skirt, and checked her progress. Instead of putting it off and hastening along, she yielded herself up to the pull, and stood passively still. When she began to extricate herself it was by turning round and round, and so unwinding the prickly switch. She was in a desponding reverie.\n\nHer course was in the direction of the small undying fire which had drawn the attention of the men on Rainbarrow and of Wildeve in the valley below. A faint illumination from its rays began to glow upon her face, and the fire soon revealed itself to be lit, not on the level ground, but on a salient corner or redan of earth, at the junction of two converging bank fences. Outside was a ditch, dry except immediately under the fire, where there was a large pool, bearded all round by heather and rushes. In the smooth water of the pool the fire appeared upside down.\n\nThe banks meeting behind were bare of a hedge, save such as was formed by disconnected tufts of furze, standing upon stems along the top, like impaled heads above a city wall. A white mast, fitted up with spars and other nautical tackle, could be seen rising against the dark clouds whenever the flames played brightly enough to reach it. Altogether the scene had much the appearance of a fortification upon which had been kindled a beacon fire.\n\nNobody was visible; but ever and anon a whitish something moved above the bank from behind, and vanished again. This was a small human hand, in the act of lifting pieces of fuel into the fire, but for all that could be seen the hand, like that which troubled Belshazzar, was there alone. Occasionally an ember rolled off the bank, and dropped with a hiss into the pool.\n\nAt one side of the pool rough steps built of clods enabled everyone who wished to do so to mount the bank; which the woman did. Within was a paddock in an uncultivated state, though bearing evidence of having once been tilled; but the heath and fern had insidiously crept in, and were reasserting their old supremacy. Further ahead were dimly visible an irregular dwelling-house, garden, and outbuildings, backed by a clump of firs.\n\nThe young lady—for youth had revealed its presence in her buoyant bound up the bank—walked along the top instead of descending inside, and came to the corner where the fire was burning. One reason for the permanence of the blaze was now manifest: the fuel consisted of hard pieces of wood, cleft and sawn—the knotty boles of old thorn trees which grew in twos and threes about the hillsides. A yet unconsumed pile of these lay in the inner angle of the bank; and from this corner the upturned face of a little boy greeted her eyes. He was dilatorily throwing up a piece of wood into the fire every now and then, a business which seemed to have engaged him a considerable part of the evening, for his face was somewhat weary.\n\n“I am glad you have come, Miss Eustacia,” he said, with a sigh of relief. “I don’t like biding by myself.”\n\n“Nonsense. I have only been a little way for a walk. I have been gone only twenty minutes.”\n\n“It seemed long,” murmured the sad boy. “And you have been so many times.”\n\n“Why, I thought you would be pleased to have a bonfire. Are you not much obliged to me for making you one?”\n\n“Yes; but there’s nobody here to play wi’ me.”\n\n“I suppose nobody has come while I’ve been away?”\n\n“Nobody except your grandfather—he looked out of doors once for ’ee. I told him you were walking round upon the hill to look at the other bonfires.”\n\n“A good boy.”\n\n“I think I hear him coming again, miss.”\n\nAn old man came into the remoter light of the fire from the direction of the homestead. He was the same who had overtaken the reddleman on the road that afternoon. He looked wistfully to the top of the bank at the woman who stood there, and his teeth, which were quite unimpaired, showed like parian from his parted lips.\n\n“When are you coming indoors, Eustacia?” he asked. “’Tis almost bedtime. I’ve been home these two hours, and am tired out. Surely ’tis somewhat childish of you to stay out playing at bonfires so long, and wasting such fuel. My precious thorn roots, the rarest of all firing, that I laid by on purpose for Christmas—you have burnt ’em nearly all!”\n\n“I promised Johnny a bonfire, and it pleases him not to let it go out just yet,” said Eustacia, in a way which told at once that she was absolute queen here. “Grandfather, you go in to bed. I shall follow you soon. You like the fire, don’t you, Johnny?”\n\nThe boy looked up doubtfully at her and murmured, “I don’t think I want it any longer.”\n\nHer grandfather had turned back again, and did not hear the boy’s reply. As soon as the white-haired man had vanished she said in a tone of pique to the child, “Ungrateful little boy, how can you contradict me? Never shall you have a bonfire again unless you keep it up now. Come, tell me you like to do things for me, and don’t deny it.”\n\nThe repressed child said, “Yes, I do, miss,” and continued to stir the fire perfunctorily.\n\n“Stay a little longer and I will give you a crooked six-pence,” said Eustacia, more gently. “Put in one piece of wood every two or three minutes, but not too much at once. I am going to walk along the ridge a little longer, but I shall keep on coming to you. And if you hear a frog jump into the pond with a flounce like a stone thrown in, be sure you run and tell me, because it is a sign of rain.”\n\n“Yes, Eustacia.”\n\n“Miss Vye, sir.”\n\n“Miss Vy—stacia.”\n\n“That will do. Now put in one stick more.”\n\nThe little slave went on feeding the fire as before. He seemed a mere automaton, galvanized into moving and speaking by the wayward Eustacia’s will. He might have been the brass statue which Albertus Magnus is said to have animated just so far as to make it chatter, and move, and be his servant.\n\nBefore going on her walk again the young girl stood still on the bank for a few instants and listened. It was to the full as lonely a place as Rainbarrow, though at rather a lower level; and it was more sheltered from wind and weather on account of the few firs to the north. The bank which enclosed the homestead, and protected it from the lawless state of the world without, was formed of thick square clods, dug from the ditch on the outside, and built up with a slight batter or incline, which forms no slight defense where hedges will not grow because of the wind and the wilderness, and where wall materials are unattainable. Otherwise the situation was quite open, commanding the whole length of the valley which reached to the river behind Wildeve’s house. High above this to the right, and much nearer thitherward than the Quiet Woman Inn, the blurred contour of Rainbarrow obstructed the sky.\n\nAfter her attentive survey of the wild slopes and hollow ravines a gesture of impatience escaped Eustacia. She vented petulant words every now and then, but there were sighs between her words, and sudden listenings between her sighs. Descending from her perch she again sauntered off towards Rainbarrow, though this time she did not go the whole way.\n\nTwice she reappeared at intervals of a few minutes and each time she said—\n\n“Not any flounce into the pond yet, little man?”\n\n“No, Miss Eustacia,” the child replied.\n\n“Well,” she said at last, “I shall soon be going in, and then I will give you the crooked sixpence, and let you go home.”\n\n“Thank’ee, Miss Eustacia,” said the tired stoker, breathing more easily. And Eustacia again strolled away from the fire, but this time not towards Rainbarrow. She skirted the bank and went round to the wicket before the house, where she stood motionless, looking at the scene.\n\nFifty yards off rose the corner of the two converging banks, with the fire upon it; within the bank, lifting up to the fire one stick at a time, just as before, the figure of the little child. She idly watched him as he occasionally climbed up in the nook of the bank and stood beside the brands. The wind blew the smoke, and the child’s hair, and the corner of his pinafore, all in the same direction; the breeze died, and the pinafore and hair lay still, and the smoke went up straight.\nWhile Eustacia looked on from this distance the boy’s form visibly started—he slid down the bank and ran across towards the white gate.\n\n“Well?” said Eustacia.\n\n“A hopfrog have jumped into the pond. Yes, I heard ’en!”\n\n“Then it is going to rain, and you had better go home. You will not be afraid?” She spoke hurriedly, as if her heart had leapt into her throat at the boy’s words.\n\n“No, because I shall hae the crooked sixpence.”\n\n“Yes, here it is. Now run as fast as you can—not that way—through the garden here. No other boy in the heath has had such a bonfire as yours.”\n\nThe boy, who clearly had had too much of a good thing, marched away into the shadows with alacrity. When he was gone Eustacia, leaving her telescope and hourglass by the gate, brushed forward from the wicket towards the angle of the bank, under the fire.\n\nHere, screened by the outwork, she waited. In a few moments a splash was audible from the pond outside. Had the child been there he would have said that a second frog had jumped in; but by most people the sound would have been likened to the fall of a stone into the water. Eustacia stepped upon the bank.\n\n“Yes?” she said, and held her breath.\n\nThereupon the contour of a man became dimly visible against the low-reaching sky over the valley, beyond the outer margin of the pool. He came round it and leapt upon the bank beside her. A low laugh escaped her—the third utterance which the girl had indulged in tonight. The first, when she stood upon Rainbarrow, had expressed anxiety; the second, on the ridge, had expressed impatience; the present was one of triumphant pleasure. She let her joyous eyes rest upon him without speaking, as upon some wondrous thing she had created out of chaos.\n\n“I have come,” said the man, who was Wildeve. “You give me no peace. Why do you not leave me alone? I have seen your bonfire all the evening.” The words were not without emotion, and retained their level tone as if by a careful equipoise between imminent extremes.\n\nAt this unexpectedly repressing manner in her lover the girl seemed to repress herself also. “Of course you have seen my fire,” she answered with languid calmness, artificially maintained. “Why shouldn’t I have a bonfire on the Fifth of November, like other denizens of the heath?”\n\n“I knew it was meant for me.”\n\n“How did you know it? I have had no word with you since you—you chose her, and walked about with her, and deserted me entirely, as if I had never been yours life and soul so irretrievably!”\n\n“Eustacia! could I forget that last autumn at this same day of the month and at this same place you lighted exactly such a fire as a signal for me to come and see you? Why should there have been a bonfire again by Captain Vye’s house if not for the same purpose?”\n\n“Yes, yes—I own it,” she cried under her breath, with a drowsy fervour of manner and tone which was quite peculiar to her. “Don’t begin speaking to me as you did, Damon; you will drive me to say words I would not wish to say to you. I had given you up, and resolved not to think of you any more; and then I heard the news, and I came out and got the fire ready because I thought that you had been faithful to me.”\n\n“What have you heard to make you think that?” said Wildeve, astonished.\n\n“That you did not marry her!” she murmured exultingly. “And I knew it was because you loved me best, and couldn’t do it.... Damon, you have been cruel to me to go away, and I have said I would never forgive you. I do not think I can forgive you entirely, even now—it is too much for a woman of any spirit to quite overlook.”\n\n“If I had known you wished to call me up here only to reproach me, I wouldn’t have come.”\n\n“But I don’t mind it, and I do forgive you now that you have not married her, and have come back to me!”\n\n“Who told you that I had not married her?”\n\n“My grandfather. He took a long walk today, and as he was coming home he overtook some person who told him of a broken-off wedding—he thought it might be yours, and I knew it was.”\n\n“Does anybody else know?”\n\n“I suppose not. Now Damon, do you see why I lit my signal fire? You did not think I would have lit it if I had imagined you to have become the husband of this woman. It is insulting my pride to suppose that.”\n\nWildeve was silent; it was evident that he had supposed as much.\n\n“Did you indeed think I believed you were married?” she again demanded earnestly. “Then you wronged me; and upon my life and heart I can hardly bear to recognize that you have such ill thoughts of me! Damon, you are not worthy of me—I see it, and yet I love you. Never mind, let it go—I must bear your mean opinion as best I may.... It is true, is it not,” she added with ill-concealed anxiety, on his making no demonstration, “that you could not bring yourself to give me up, and are still going to love me best of all?”\n\n“Yes; or why should I have come?” he said touchily. “Not that fidelity will be any great merit in me after your kind speech about my unworthiness, which should have been said by myself if by anybody, and comes with an ill grace from you. However, the curse of inflammability is upon me, and I must live under it, and take any snub from a woman. It has brought me down from engineering to innkeeping—what lower stage it has in store for me I have yet to learn.” He continued to look upon her gloomily.\n\nShe seized the moment, and throwing back the shawl so that the firelight shone full upon her face and throat, said with a smile, “Have you seen anything better than that in your travels?”\n\nEustacia was not one to commit herself to such a position without good ground. He said quietly, “No.”\n\n“Not even on the shoulders of Thomasin?”\n\n“Thomasin is a pleasing and innocent woman.”\n\n“That’s nothing to do with it,” she cried with quick passionateness. “We will leave her out; there are only you and me now to think of.” After a long look at him she resumed with the old quiescent warmth, “Must I go on weakly confessing to you things a woman ought to conceal; and own that no words can express how gloomy I have been because of that dreadful belief I held till two hours ago—that you had quite deserted me?”\n\n“I am sorry I caused you that pain.”\n\n“But perhaps it is not wholly because of you that I get gloomy,” she archly added. “It is in my nature to feel like that. It was born in my blood, I suppose.”\n\n“Hypochondriasis.”\n\n“Or else it was coming into this wild heath. I was happy enough at Budmouth. O the times, O the days at Budmouth! But Egdon will be brighter again now.”\n\n“I hope it will,” said Wildeve moodily. “Do you know the consequence of this recall to me, my old darling? I shall come to see you again as before, at Rainbarrow.”\n\n“Of course you will.”\n\n“And yet I declare that until I got here tonight I intended, after this one good-bye, never to meet you again.”\n\n“I don’t thank you for that,” she said, turning away, while indignation spread through her like subterranean heat. “You may come again to Rainbarrow if you like, but you won’t see me; and you may call, but I shall not listen; and you may tempt me, but I won’t give myself to you any more.”\n\n“You have said as much before, sweet; but such natures as yours don’t so easily adhere to their words. Neither, for the matter of that, do such natures as mine.”\n\n“This is the pleasure I have won by my trouble,” she whispered bitterly. “Why did I try to recall you? Damon, a strange warring takes place in my mind occasionally. I think when I become calm after you woundings, ‘Do I embrace a cloud of common fog after all?’ You are a chameleon, and now you are at your worst colour. Go home, or I shall hate you!”\n\nHe looked absently towards Rainbarrow while one might have counted twenty, and said, as if he did not much mind all this, “Yes, I will go home. Do you mean to see me again?”\n\n“If you own to me that the wedding is broken off because you love me best.”\n\n“I don’t think it would be good policy,” said Wildeve, smiling. “You would get to know the extent of your power too clearly.”\n\n“But tell me!”\n\n“You know.”\n“Where is she now?”\n\n“I don’t know. I prefer not to speak of her to you. I have not yet married her; I have come in obedience to your call. That is enough.”\n\n“I merely lit that fire because I was dull, and thought I would get a little excitement by calling you up and triumphing over you as the Witch of Endor called up Samuel. I determined you should come; and you have come! I have shown my power. A mile and half hither, and a mile and half back again to your home—three miles in the dark for me. Have I not shown my power?”\n\nHe shook his head at her. “I know you too well, my Eustacia; I know you too well. There isn’t a note in you which I don’t know; and that hot little bosom couldn’t play such a cold-blooded trick to save its life. I saw a woman on Rainbarrow at dusk looking down towards my house. I think I drew out you before you drew out me.”\n\nThe revived embers of an old passion glowed clearly in Wildeve now; and he leant forward as if about to put his face towards her cheek.\n\n“O no,” she said, intractably moving to the other side of the decayed fire. “What did you mean by that?”\n\n“Perhaps I may kiss your hand?”\n\n“No, you may not.”\n\n“Then I may shake your hand?”\n\n“No.”\n“Then I wish you good night without caring for either. Good-bye, good-bye.”\n\nShe returned no answer, and with the bow of a dancing-master he vanished on the other side of the pool as he had come.\n\nEustacia sighed—it was no fragile maiden sigh, but a sigh which shook her like a shiver. Whenever a flash of reason darted like an electric light upon her lover—as it sometimes would—and showed his imperfections, she shivered thus. But it was over in a second, and she loved on. She knew that he trifled with her; but she loved on. She scattered the half-burnt brands, went indoors immediately, and up to her bedroom without a light. Amid the rustles which denoted her to be undressing in the darkness other heavy breaths frequently came; and the same kind of shudder occasionally moved through her when, ten minutes later, she lay on her bed asleep.\n\nVII. Queen of Night\n\nEustacia Vye was the raw material of a divinity. On Olympus she would have done well with a little preparation. She had the passions and instincts which make a model goddess, that is, those which make not quite a model woman. Had it been possible for the earth and mankind to be entirely in her grasp for a while, she had handled the distaff, the spindle, and the shears at her own free will, few in the world would have noticed the change of government. There would have been the same inequality of lot, the same heaping up of favours here, of contumely there, the same generosity before justice, the same perpetual dilemmas, the same captious alteration of caresses and blows that we endure now.\n\nShe was in person full-limbed and somewhat heavy; without ruddiness, as without pallor; and soft to the touch as a cloud. To see her hair was to fancy that a whole winter did not contain darkness enough to form its shadow—it closed over her forehead like nightfall extinguishing the western glow.\n\nHer nerves extended into those tresses, and her temper could always be softened by stroking them down. When her hair was brushed she would instantly sink into stillness and look like the Sphinx. If, in passing under one of the Egdon banks, any of its thick skeins were caught, as they sometimes were, by a prickly tuft of the large _Ulex Europæus_—which will act as a sort of hairbrush—she would go back a few steps, and pass against it a second time.\n\nShe had pagan eyes, full of nocturnal mysteries, and their light, as it came and went, and came again, was partially hampered by their oppressive lids and lashes; and of these the under lid was much fuller than it usually is with English women. This enabled her to indulge in reverie without seeming to do so—she might have been believed capable of sleeping without closing them up. Assuming that the souls of men and women were visible essences, you could fancy the colour of Eustacia’s soul to be flamelike. The sparks from it that rose into her dark pupils gave the same impression.\n\nThe mouth seemed formed less to speak than to quiver, less to quiver than to kiss. Some might have added, less to kiss than to curl. Viewed sideways, the closing-line of her lips formed, with almost geometric precision, the curve so well known in the arts of design as the cima-recta, or ogee. The sight of such a flexible bend as that on grim Egdon was quite an apparition. It was felt at once that the mouth did not come over from Sleswig with a band of Saxon pirates whose lips met like the two halves of a muffin. One had fancied that such lip-curves were mostly lurking underground in the South as fragments of forgotten marbles. So fine were the lines of her lips that, though full, each corner of her mouth was as clearly cut as the point of a spear. This keenness of corner was only blunted when she was given over to sudden fits of gloom, one of the phases of the night-side of sentiment which she knew too well for her years.\n\nHer presence brought memories of such things as Bourbon roses, rubies, and tropical midnight; her moods recalled lotus-eaters and the march in Athalie; her motions, the ebb and flow of the sea; her voice, the viola. In a dim light, and with a slight rearrangement of her hair, her general figure might have stood for that of either of the higher female deities. The new moon behind her head, an old helmet upon it, a diadem of accidental dewdrops round her brow, would have been adjuncts sufficient to strike the note of Artemis, Athena, or Hera respectively, with as close an approximation to the antique as that which passes muster on many respected canvases.\n\nBut celestial imperiousness, love, wrath, and fervour had proved to be somewhat thrown away on netherward Egdon. Her power was limited, and the consciousness of this limitation had biassed her development. Egdon was her Hades, and since coming there she had imbibed much of what was dark in its tone, though inwardly and eternally unreconciled thereto. Her appearance accorded well with this smouldering rebelliousness, and the shady splendour of her beauty was the real surface of the sad and stifled warmth within her. A true Tartarean dignity sat upon her brow, and not factitiously or with marks of constraint, for it had grown in her with years.\n\nAcross the upper part of her head she wore a thin fillet of black velvet, restraining the luxuriance of her shady hair, in a way which added much to this class of majesty by irregularly clouding her forehead. “Nothing can embellish a beautiful face more than a narrow band drawn over the brow,” says Richter. Some of the neighbouring girls wore coloured ribbon for the same purpose, and sported metallic ornaments elsewhere; but if anyone suggested coloured ribbon and metallic ornaments to Eustacia Vye she laughed and went on.\n\nWhy did a woman of this sort live on Egdon Heath? Budmouth was her native place, a fashionable seaside resort at that date. She was the daughter of the bandmaster of a regiment which had been quartered there—a Corfiote by birth, and a fine musician—who met his future wife during her trip thither with her father the captain, a man of good family. The marriage was scarcely in accord with the old man’s wishes, for the bandmaster’s pockets were as light as his occupation. But the musician did his best; adopted his wife’s name, made England permanently his home, took great trouble with his child’s education, the expenses of which were defrayed by the grandfather, and throve as the chief local musician till her mother’s death, when he left off thriving, drank, and died also. The girl was left to the care of her grandfather, who, since three of his ribs became broken in a shipwreck, had lived in this airy perch on Egdon, a spot which had taken his fancy because the house was to be had for next to nothing, and because a remote blue tinge on the horizon between the hills, visible from the cottage door, was traditionally believed to be the English Channel. She hated the change; she felt like one banished; but here she was forced to abide.\n\nThus it happened that in Eustacia’s brain were juxtaposed the strangest assortment of ideas, from old time and from new. There was no middle distance in her perspective—romantic recollections of sunny afternoons on an esplanade, with military bands, officers, and gallants around, stood like gilded letters upon the dark tablet of surrounding Egdon. Every bizarre effect that could result from the random intertwining of watering-place glitter with the grand solemnity of a heath, was to be found in her. Seeing nothing of human life now, she imagined all the more of what she had seen.\n\nWhere did her dignity come from? By a latent vein from Alcinous’ line, her father hailing from Phæacia’s isle?—or from Fitzalan and De Vere, her maternal grandfather having had a cousin in the peerage? Perhaps it was the gift of Heaven—a happy convergence of natural laws. Among other things opportunity had of late years been denied her of learning to be undignified, for she lived lonely. Isolation on a heath renders vulgarity well-nigh impossible. It would have been as easy for the heath-ponies, bats, and snakes to be vulgar as for her. A narrow life in Budmouth might have completely demeaned her.\n\nThe only way to look queenly without realms or hearts to queen it over is to look as if you had lost them; and Eustacia did that to a triumph. In the captain’s cottage she could suggest mansions she had never seen. Perhaps that was because she frequented a vaster mansion than any of them, the open hills. Like the summer condition of the place around her, she was an embodiment of the phrase “a populous solitude”—apparently so listless, void, and quiet, she was really busy and full.\n\nTo be loved to madness—such was her great desire. Love was to her the one cordial which could drive away the eating loneliness of her days. And she seemed to long for the abstraction called passionate love more than for any particular lover.\n\nShe could show a most reproachful look at times, but it was directed less against human beings than against certain creatures of her mind, the chief of these being Destiny, through whose interference she dimly fancied it arose that love alighted only on gliding youth—that any love she might win would sink simultaneously with the sand in the glass. She thought of it with an ever-growing consciousness of cruelty, which tended to breed actions of reckless unconventionality, framed to snatch a year’s, a week’s, even an hour’s passion from anywhere while it could be won. Through want of it she had sung without being merry, possessed without enjoying, outshone without triumphing. Her loneliness deepened her desire. On Egdon, coldest and meanest kisses were at famine prices, and where was a mouth matching hers to be found?\n\nFidelity in love for fidelity’s sake had less attraction for her than for most women; fidelity because of love’s grip had much. A blaze of love, and extinction, was better than a lantern glimmer of the same which should last long years. On this head she knew by prevision what most women learn only by experience—she had mentally walked round love, told the towers thereof, considered its palaces, and concluded that love was but a doleful joy. Yet she desired it, as one in a desert would be thankful for brackish water.\n\nShe often repeated her prayers; not at particular times, but, like the unaffectedly devout, when she desired to pray. Her prayer was always spontaneous, and often ran thus, “O deliver my heart from this fearful gloom and loneliness; send me great love from somewhere, else I shall die.”\n\nHer high gods were William the Conqueror, Strafford, and Napoleon Buonaparte, as they had appeared in the Lady’s History used at the establishment in which she was educated. Had she been a mother she would have christened her boys such names as Saul or Sisera in preference to Jacob or David, neither of whom she admired. At school she had used to side with the Philistines in several battles, and had wondered if Pontius Pilate were as handsome as he was frank and fair.\n\nThus she was a girl of some forwardness of mind, indeed, weighed in relation to her situation among the very rearward of thinkers, very original. Her instincts towards social non-comformity were at the root of this. In the matter of holidays, her mood was that of horses who, when turned out to grass, enjoy looking upon their kind at work on the highway. She only valued rest to herself when it came in the midst of other people’s labour. Hence she hated Sundays when all was at rest, and often said they would be the death of her. To see the heathmen in their Sunday condition, that is, with their hands in their pockets, their boots newly oiled, and not laced up (a particularly Sunday sign), walking leisurely among the turves and furze-faggots they had cut during the week, and kicking them critically as if their use were unknown, was a fearful heaviness to her. To relieve the tedium of this untimely day she would overhaul the cupboards containing her grandfather’s old charts and other rubbish, humming Saturday-night ballads of the country people the while. But on Saturday nights she would frequently sing a psalm, and it was always on a weekday that she read the Bible, that she might be unoppressed with a sense of doing her duty.\n\nSuch views of life were to some extent the natural begettings of her situation upon her nature. To dwell on a heath without studying its meanings was like wedding a foreigner without learning his tongue. The subtle beauties of the heath were lost to Eustacia; she only caught its vapours. An environment which would have made a contented woman a poet, a suffering woman a devotee, a pious woman a psalmist, even a giddy woman thoughtful, made a rebellious woman saturnine.\n\nEustacia had got beyond the vision of some marriage of inexpressible glory; yet, though her emotions were in full vigour, she cared for no meaner union. Thus we see her in a strange state of isolation. To have lost the godlike conceit that we may do what we will, and not to have acquired a homely zest for doing what we can, shows a grandeur of temper which cannot be objected to in the abstract, for it denotes a mind that, though disappointed, forswears compromise. But, if congenial to philosophy, it is apt to be dangerous to the commonwealth. In a world where doing means marrying, and the commonwealth is one of hearts and hands, the same peril attends the condition.\n\nAnd so we see our Eustacia—for at times she was not altogether unlovable—arriving at that stage of enlightenment which feels that nothing is worth while, and filling up the spare hours of her existence by idealizing Wildeve for want of a better object. This was the sole reason of his ascendency: she knew it herself. At moments her pride rebelled against her passion for him, and she even had longed to be free. But there was only one circumstance which could dislodge him, and that was the advent of a greater man.\n\nFor the rest, she suffered much from depression of spirits, and took slow walks to recover them, in which she carried her grandfather’s telescope and her grandmother’s hourglass—the latter because of a peculiar pleasure she derived from watching a material representation of time’s gradual glide away. She seldom schemed, but when she did scheme, her plans showed rather the comprehensive strategy of a general than the small arts called womanish, though she could utter oracles of Delphian ambiguity when she did not choose to be direct. In heaven she will probably sit between the Héloïses and the Cleopatras.\n\nVIII. Those Who Are Found Where There Is Said to Be Nobody\n\nAs soon as the sad little boy had withdrawn from the fire he clasped the money tight in the palm of his hand, as if thereby to fortify his courage, and began to run. There was really little danger in allowing a child to go home alone on this part of Egdon Heath. The distance to the boy’s house was not more than three-eighths of a mile, his father’s cottage, and one other a few yards further on, forming part of the small hamlet of Mistover Knap: the third and only remaining house was that of Captain Vye and Eustacia, which stood quite away from the small cottages and was the loneliest of lonely houses on these thinly populated slopes.\n\nHe ran until he was out of breath, and then, becoming more courageous, walked leisurely along, singing in an old voice a little song about a sailor-boy and a fair one, and bright gold in store. In the middle of this the child stopped—from a pit under the hill ahead of him shone a light, whence proceeded a cloud of floating dust and a smacking noise.\n\nOnly unusual sights and sounds frightened the boy. The shrivelled voice of the heath did not alarm him, for that was familiar. The thornbushes which arose in his path from time to time were less satisfactory, for they whistled gloomily, and had a ghastly habit after dark of putting on the shapes of jumping madmen, sprawling giants, and hideous cripples. Lights were not uncommon this evening, but the nature of all of them was different from this. Discretion rather than terror prompted the boy to turn back instead of passing the light, with a view of asking Miss Eustacia Vye to let her servant accompany him home.\n\nWhen the boy had reascended to the top of the valley he found the fire to be still burning on the bank, though lower than before. Beside it, instead of Eustacia’s solitary form, he saw two persons, the second being a man. The boy crept along under the bank to ascertain from the nature of the proceedings if it would be prudent to interrupt so splendid a creature as Miss Eustacia on his poor trivial account.\n\nAfter listening under the bank for some minutes to the talk he turned in a perplexed and doubting manner and began to withdraw as silently as he had come. That he did not, upon the whole, think it advisable to interrupt her conversation with Wildeve, without being prepared to bear the whole weight of her displeasure, was obvious.\n\nHere was a Scyllæo-Charybdean position for a poor boy. Pausing when again safe from discovery, he finally decided to face the pit phenomenon as the lesser evil. With a heavy sigh he retraced the slope, and followed the path he had followed before.\n\nThe light had gone, the rising dust had disappeared—he hoped for ever. He marched resolutely along, and found nothing to alarm him till, coming within a few yards of the sandpit, he heard a slight noise in front, which led him to halt. The halt was but momentary, for the noise resolved itself into the steady bites of two animals grazing.\n\n“Two he’th-croppers down here,” he said aloud. “I have never known ’em come down so far afore.”\n\nThe animals were in the direct line of his path, but that the child thought little of; he had played round the fetlocks of horses from his infancy. On coming nearer, however, the boy was somewhat surprised to find that the little creatures did not run off, and that each wore a clog, to prevent his going astray; this signified that they had been broken in. He could now see the interior of the pit, which, being in the side of the hill, had a level entrance. In the innermost corner the square outline of a van appeared, with its back towards him. A light came from the interior, and threw a moving shadow upon the vertical face of gravel at the further side of the pit into which the vehicle faced.\n\nThe child assumed that this was the cart of a gipsy, and his dread of those wanderers reached but to that mild pitch which titillates rather than pains. Only a few inches of mud wall kept him and his family from being gipsies themselves. He skirted the gravel pit at a respectful distance, ascended the slope, and came forward upon the brow, in order to look into the open door of the van and see the original of the shadow.\n\nThe picture alarmed the boy. By a little stove inside the van sat a figure red from head to heels—the man who had been Thomasin’s friend. He was darning a stocking, which was red like the rest of him. Moreover, as he darned he smoked a pipe, the stem and bowl of which were red also.\n\nAt this moment one of the heath-croppers feeding in the outer shadows was audibly shaking off the clog attached to its foot. Aroused by the sound, the reddleman laid down his stocking, lit a lantern which hung beside him, and came out from the van. In sticking up the candle he lifted the lantern to his face, and the light shone into the whites of his eyes and upon his ivory teeth, which, in contrast with the red surrounding, lent him a startling aspect enough to the gaze of a juvenile. The boy knew too well for his peace of mind upon whose lair he had lighted. Uglier persons than gipsies were known to cross Egdon at times, and a reddleman was one of them.\n\n“How I wish ’twas only a gipsy!” he murmured.\n\nThe man was by this time coming back from the horses. In his fear of being seen the boy rendered detection certain by nervous motion. The heather and peat stratum overhung the brow of the pit in mats, hiding the actual verge. The boy had stepped beyond the solid ground; the heather now gave way, and down he rolled over the scarp of grey sand to the very foot of the man.\n\nThe red man opened the lantern and turned it upon the figure of the prostrate boy.\n\n“Who be ye?” he said.\n\n“Johnny Nunsuch, master!”\n\n“What were you doing up there?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“Watching me, I suppose?”\n\n“Yes, master.”\n\n“What did you watch me for?”\n\n“Because I was coming home from Miss Vye’s bonfire.”\n\n“Beest hurt?”\n\n“Why, yes, you be—your hand is bleeding. Come under my tilt and let me tie it up.”\n\n“Please let me look for my sixpence.”\n\n“How did you come by that?”\n\n“Miss Vye gied it to me for keeping up her bonfire.”\n\nThe sixpence was found, and the man went to the van, the boy behind, almost holding his breath.\n\nThe man took a piece of rag from a satchel containing sewing materials, tore off a strip, which, like everything else, was tinged red, and proceeded to bind up the wound.\n\n“My eyes have got foggy-like—please may I sit down, master?” said the boy.\n\n“To be sure, poor chap. ’Tis enough to make you feel fainty. Sit on that bundle.”\n\nThe man finished tying up the gash, and the boy said, “I think I’ll go home now, master.”\n\n“You are rather afraid of me. Do you know what I be?”\n\nThe child surveyed his vermilion figure up and down with much misgiving and finally said, “Yes.”\n\n“Well, what?”\n\n“The reddleman!” he faltered.\n\n“Yes, that’s what I be. Though there’s more than one. You little children think there’s only one cuckoo, one fox, one giant, one devil, and one reddleman, when there’s lots of us all.”\n\n“Is there? You won’t carry me off in your bags, will ye, master? ’Tis said that the reddleman will sometimes.”\n\n“Nonsense. All that reddlemen do is sell reddle. You see all these bags at the back of my cart? They are not full of little boys—only full of red stuff.”\n\n“Was you born a reddleman?”\n\n“No, I took to it. I should be as white as you if I were to give up the trade—that is, I should be white in time—perhaps six months; not at first, because ’tis grow’d into my skin and won’t wash out. Now, you’ll never be afraid of a reddleman again, will ye?”\n\n“No, never. Willy Orchard said he seed a red ghost here t’other day—perhaps that was you?”\n\n“I was here t’other day.”\n\n“Were you making that dusty light I saw by now?”\n\n“Oh yes, I was beating out some bags. And have you had a good bonfire up there? I saw the light. Why did Miss Vye want a bonfire so bad that she should give you sixpence to keep it up?”\n\n“I don’t know. I was tired, but she made me bide and keep up the fire just the same, while she kept going up across Rainbarrow way.”\n\n“And how long did that last?”\n\n“Until a hopfrog jumped into the pond.”\n\nThe reddleman suddenly ceased to talk idly. “A hopfrog?” he inquired. “Hopfrogs don’t jump into ponds this time of year.”\n\n“They do, for I heard one.”\n\n“Certain-sure?”\n\n“Yes. She told me afore that I should hear’n; and so I did. They say she’s clever and deep, and perhaps she charmed ’en to come.”\n\n“And what then?”\n\n“Then I came down here, and I was afeard, and I went back; but I didn’t like to speak to her, because of the gentleman, and I came on here again.”\n\n“A gentleman—ah! What did she say to him, my man?”\n\n“Told him she supposed he had not married the other woman because he liked his old sweetheart best; and things like that.”\n\n“What did the gentleman say to her, my sonny?”\n\n“He only said he did like her best, and how he was coming to see her again under Rainbarrow o’ nights.”\n\n“Ha!” cried the reddleman, slapping his hand against the side of his van so that the whole fabric shook under the blow. “That’s the secret o’t!”\n\nThe little boy jumped clean from the stool.\n\n“My man, don’t you be afraid,” said the dealer in red, suddenly becoming gentle. “I forgot you were here. That’s only a curious way reddlemen have of going mad for a moment; but they don’t hurt anybody. And what did the lady say then?”\n\n“I can’t mind. Please, Master Reddleman, may I go home-along now?”\n\n“Ay, to be sure you may. I’ll go a bit of ways with you.”\n\nHe conducted the boy out of the gravel pit and into the path leading to his mother’s cottage. When the little figure had vanished in the darkness the reddleman returned, resumed his seat by the fire, and proceeded to darn again.\n\nIX. Love Leads a Shrewd Man into Strategy\n\nReddlemen of the old school are now but seldom seen. Since the introduction of railways Wessex farmers have managed to do without these Mephistophelian visitants, and the bright pigment so largely used by shepherds in preparing sheep for the fair is obtained by other routes. Even those who yet survive are losing the poetry of existence which characterized them when the pursuit of the trade meant periodical journeys to the pit whence the material was dug, a regular camping out from month to month, except in the depth of winter, a peregrination among farms which could be counted by the hundred, and in spite of this Arab existence the preservation of that respectability which is insured by the never-failing production of a well-lined purse.\n\nReddle spreads its lively hues over everything it lights on, and stamps unmistakably, as with the mark of Cain, any person who has handled it half an hour.\n\nA child’s first sight of a reddleman was an epoch in his life. That blood-coloured figure was a sublimation of all the horrid dreams which had afflicted the juvenile spirit since imagination began. “The reddleman is coming for you!” had been the formulated threat of Wessex mothers for many generations. He was successfully supplanted for a while, at the beginning of the present century, by Buonaparte; but as process of time rendered the latter personage stale and ineffective the older phrase resumed its early prominence. And now the reddleman has in his turn followed Buonaparte to the land of worn-out bogeys, and his place is filled by modern inventions.\n\nThe reddleman lived like a gipsy; but gipsies he scorned. He was about as thriving as travelling basket and mat makers; but he had nothing to do with them. He was more decently born and brought up than the cattledrovers who passed and repassed him in his wanderings; but they merely nodded to him. His stock was more valuable than that of pedlars; but they did not think so, and passed his cart with eyes straight ahead. He was such an unnatural colour to look at that the men of roundabouts and waxwork shows seemed gentlemen beside him; but he considered them low company, and remained aloof. Among all these squatters and folks of the road the reddleman continually found himself; yet he was not of them. His occupation tended to isolate him, and isolated he was mostly seen to be.\n\nIt was sometimes suggested that reddlemen were criminals for whose misdeeds other men wrongfully suffered—that in escaping the law they had not escaped their own consciences, and had taken to the trade as a lifelong penance. Else why should they have chosen it? In the present case such a question would have been particularly apposite. The reddleman who had entered Egdon that afternoon was an instance of the pleasing being wasted to form the ground-work of the singular, when an ugly foundation would have done just as well for that purpose. The one point that was forbidding about this reddleman was his colour. Freed from that he would have been as agreeable a specimen of rustic manhood as one would often see. A keen observer might have been inclined to think—which was, indeed, partly the truth—that he had relinquished his proper station in life for want of interest in it. Moreover, after looking at him one would have hazarded the guess that good nature, and an acuteness as extreme as it could be without verging on craft, formed the framework of his character.\n\nWhile he darned the stocking his face became rigid with thought. Softer expressions followed this, and then again recurred the tender sadness which had sat upon him during his drive along the highway that afternoon. Presently his needle stopped. He laid down the stocking, arose from his seat, and took a leathern pouch from a hook in the corner of the van. This contained among other articles a brown-paper packet, which, to judge from the hinge-like character of its worn folds, seemed to have been carefully opened and closed a good many times. He sat down on a three-legged milking stool that formed the only seat in the van, and, examining his packet by the light of a candle, took thence an old letter and spread it open. The writing had originally been traced on white paper, but the letter had now assumed a pale red tinge from the accident of its situation; and the black strokes of writing thereon looked like the twigs of a winter hedge against a vermilion sunset. The letter bore a date some two years previous to that time, and was signed “Thomasin Yeobright.” It ran as follows:—\n\nDEAR DIGGORY VENN,—The question you put when you overtook me coming home from Pond-close gave me such a surprise that I am afraid I did not make you exactly understand what I meant. Of course, if my aunt had not met me I could have explained all then at once, but as it was there was no chance. I have been quite uneasy since, as you know I do not wish to pain you, yet I fear I shall be doing so now in contradicting what I seemed to say then. I cannot, Diggory, marry you, or think of letting you call me your sweetheart. I could not, indeed, Diggory. I hope you will not much mind my saying this, and feel in a great pain. It makes me very sad when I think it may, for I like you very much, and I always put you next to my cousin Clym in my mind. There are so many reasons why we cannot be married that I can hardly name them all in a letter. I did not in the least expect that you were going to speak on such a thing when you followed me, because I had never thought of you in the sense of a lover at all. You must not becall me for laughing when you spoke; you mistook when you thought I laughed at you as a foolish man. I laughed because the idea was so odd, and not at you at all. The great reason with my own personal self for not letting you court me is, that I do not feel the things a woman ought to feel who consents to walk with you with the meaning of being your wife. It is not as you think, that I have another in my mind, for I do not encourage anybody, and never have in my life. Another reason is my aunt. She would not, I know, agree to it, even if I wished to have you. She likes you very well, but she will want me to look a little higher than a small dairy-farmer, and marry a professional man. I hope you will not set your heart against me for writing plainly, but I felt you might try to see me again, and it is better that we should not meet. I shall always think of you as a good man, and be anxious for your well-doing. I send this by Jane Orchard’s little maid,—And remain Diggory, your faithful friend,\n\nTo Mr. VENN, Dairy-farmer.\n\nSince the arrival of that letter, on a certain autumn morning long ago, the reddleman and Thomasin had not met till today. During the interval he had shifted his position even further from hers than it had originally been, by adopting the reddle trade; though he was really in very good circumstances still. Indeed, seeing that his expenditure was only one-fourth of his income, he might have been called a prosperous man.\n\nRejected suitors take to roaming as naturally as unhived bees; and the business to which he had cynically devoted himself was in many ways congenial to Venn. But his wanderings, by mere stress of old emotions, had frequently taken an Egdon direction, though he never intruded upon her who attracted him thither. To be in Thomasin’s heath, and near her, yet unseen, was the one ewe-lamb of pleasure left to him.\n\nThen came the incident of that day, and the reddleman, still loving her well, was excited by this accidental service to her at a critical juncture to vow an active devotion to her cause, instead of, as hitherto, sighing and holding aloof. After what had happened it was impossible that he should not doubt the honesty of Wildeve’s intentions. But her hope was apparently centred upon him; and dismissing his regrets Venn determined to aid her to be happy in her own chosen way. That this way was, of all others, the most distressing to himself, was awkward enough; but the reddleman’s love was generous.\n\nHis first active step in watching over Thomasin’s interests was taken about seven o’clock the next evening and was dictated by the news which he had learnt from the sad boy. That Eustacia was somehow the cause of Wildeve’s carelessness in relation to the marriage had at once been Venn’s conclusion on hearing of the secret meeting between them. It did not occur to his mind that Eustacia’s love signal to Wildeve was the tender effect upon the deserted beauty of the intelligence which her grandfather had brought home. His instinct was to regard her as a conspirator against rather than as an antecedent obstacle to Thomasin’s happiness.\n\nDuring the day he had been exceedingly anxious to learn the condition of Thomasin, but he did not venture to intrude upon a threshold to which he was a stranger, particularly at such an unpleasant moment as this. He had occupied his time in moving with his ponies and load to a new point in the heath, eastward to his previous station; and here he selected a nook with a careful eye to shelter from wind and rain, which seemed to mean that his stay there was to be a comparatively extended one. After this he returned on foot some part of the way that he had come; and, it being now dark, he diverged to the left till he stood behind a holly bush on the edge of a pit not twenty yards from Rainbarrow.\n\nHe watched for a meeting there, but he watched in vain. Nobody except himself came near the spot that night.\n\nBut the loss of his labour produced little effect upon the reddleman. He had stood in the shoes of Tantalus, and seemed to look upon a certain mass of disappointment as the natural preface to all realizations, without which preface they would give cause for alarm.\nThe same hour the next evening found him again at the same place; but Eustacia and Wildeve, the expected trysters, did not appear.\n\nHe pursued precisely the same course yet four nights longer, and without success. But on the next, being the day-week of their previous meeting, he saw a female shape floating along the ridge and the outline of a young man ascending from the valley. They met in the little ditch encircling the barrow—the original excavation from which it had been thrown up by the ancient British people.\n\nThe reddleman, stung with suspicion of wrong to Thomasin, was aroused to strategy in a moment. He instantly left the bush and crept forward on his hands and knees. When he had got as close as he might safely venture without discovery he found that, owing to a cross-wind, the conversation of the trysting pair could not be overheard.\n\nNear him, as in divers places about the heath, were areas strewn with large turves, which lay edgeways and upside down awaiting removal by Timothy Fairway, previous to the winter weather. He took two of these as he lay, and dragged them over him till one covered his head and shoulders, the other his back and legs. The reddleman would now have been quite invisible, even by daylight; the turves, standing upon him with the heather upwards, looked precisely as if they were growing. He crept along again, and the turves upon his back crept with him. Had he approached without any covering the chances are that he would not have been perceived in the dusk; approaching thus, it was as though he burrowed underground. In this manner he came quite close to where the two were standing.\n\n“Wish to consult me on the matter?” reached his ears in the rich, impetuous accents of Eustacia Vye. “Consult me? It is an indignity to me to talk so—I won’t bear it any longer!” She began weeping. “I have loved you, and have shown you that I loved you, much to my regret; and yet you can come and say in that frigid way that you wish to consult with me whether it would not be better to marry Thomasin. Better—of course it would be. Marry her—she is nearer to your own position in life than I am!”\n\n“Yes, yes; that’s very well,” said Wildeve peremptorily. “But we must look at things as they are. Whatever blame may attach to me for having brought it about, Thomasin’s position is at present much worse than yours. I simply tell you that I am in a strait.”\n\n“But you shall not tell me! You must see that it is only harassing me. Damon, you have not acted well; you have sunk in my opinion. You have not valued my courtesy—the courtesy of a lady in loving you—who used to think of far more ambitious things. But it was Thomasin’s fault. She won you away from me, and she deserves to suffer for it. Where is she staying now? Not that I care, nor where I am myself. Ah, if I were dead and gone how glad she would be! Where is she, I ask?”\n\n“Thomasin is now staying at her aunt’s shut up in a bedroom, and keeping out of everybody’s sight,” he said indifferently.\n\n“I don’t think you care much about her even now,” said Eustacia with sudden joyousness, “for if you did you wouldn’t talk so coolly about her. Do you talk so coolly to her about me? Ah, I expect you do! Why did you originally go away from me? I don’t think I can ever forgive you, except on one condition, that whenever you desert me, you come back again, sorry that you served me so.”\n\n“I never wish to desert you.”\n\n“I do not thank you for that. I should hate it to be all smooth. Indeed, I think I like you to desert me a little once now and then. Love is the dismallest thing where the lover is quite honest. O, it is a shame to say so; but it is true!” She indulged in a little laugh. “My low spirits begin at the very idea. Don’t you offer me tame love, or away you go!”\n\n“I wish Tamsie were not such a confoundedly good little woman,” said Wildeve, “so that I could be faithful to you without injuring a worthy person. It is I who am the sinner after all; I am not worth the little finger of either of you.”\n\n“But you must not sacrifice yourself to her from any sense of justice,” replied Eustacia quickly. “If you do not love her it is the most merciful thing in the long run to leave her as she is. That’s always the best way. There, now I have been unwomanly, I suppose. When you have left me I am always angry with myself for things that I have said to you.”\n\nWildeve walked a pace or two among the heather without replying. The pause was filled up by the intonation of a pollard thorn a little way to windward, the breezes filtering through its unyielding twigs as through a strainer. It was as if the night sang dirges with clenched teeth.\n\nShe continued, half sorrowfully, “Since meeting you last, it has occurred to me once or twice that perhaps it was not for love of me you did not marry her. Tell me, Damon—I’ll try to bear it. Had I nothing whatever to do with the matter?”\n\n“Do you press me to tell?”\n\n“Yes, I must know. I see I have been too ready to believe in my own power.”\n\n“Well, the immediate reason was that the license would not do for the place, and before I could get another she ran away. Up to that point you had nothing to do with it. Since then her aunt has spoken to me in a tone which I don’t at all like.”\n\n“Yes, yes! I am nothing in it—I am nothing in it. You only trifle with me. Heaven, what can I, Eustacia Vye, be made of to think so much of you!”\n\n“Nonsense; do not be so passionate.... Eustacia, how we roved among these bushes last year, when the hot days had got cool, and the shades of the hills kept us almost invisible in the hollows!”\n\nShe remained in moody silence till she said, “Yes; and how I used to laugh at you for daring to look up to me! But you have well made me suffer for that since.”\n\n“Yes, you served me cruelly enough until I thought I had found someone fairer than you. A blessed find for me, Eustacia.”\n\n“Do you still think you found somebody fairer?”\n\n“Sometimes I do, sometimes I don’t. The scales are balanced so nicely that a feather would turn them.”\n\n“But don’t you really care whether I meet you or whether I don’t?” she said slowly.\n\n“I care a little, but not enough to break my rest,” replied the young man languidly. “No, all that’s past. I find there are two flowers where I thought there was only one. Perhaps there are three, or four, or any number as good as the first.... Mine is a curious fate. Who would have thought that all this could happen to me?”\n\nShe interrupted with a suppressed fire of which either love or anger seemed an equally possible issue, “Do you love me now?”\n\n“Who can say?”\n\n“Tell me; I will know it!”\n\n“I do, and I do not,” said he mischievously. “That is, I have my times and my seasons. One moment you are too tall, another moment you are too do-nothing, another too melancholy, another too dark, another I don’t know what, except—that you are not the whole world to me that you used to be, my dear. But you are a pleasant lady to know and nice to meet, and I dare say as sweet as ever—almost.”\n\nEustacia was silent, and she turned from him, till she said, in a voice of suspended mightiness, “I am for a walk, and this is my way.”\n\n“Well, I can do worse than follow you.”\n\n“You know you can’t do otherwise, for all your moods and changes!” she answered defiantly. “Say what you will; try as you may; keep away from me all that you can—you will never forget me. You will love me all your life long. You would jump to marry me!”\n\n“So I would!” said Wildeve. “Such strange thoughts as I’ve had from time to time, Eustacia; and they come to me this moment. You hate the heath as much as ever; that I know.”\n\n“I do,” she murmured deeply. “’Tis my cross, my shame, and will be my death!”\n\n“I abhor it too,” said he. “How mournfully the wind blows round us now!”\n\nShe did not answer. Its tone was indeed solemn and pervasive. Compound utterances addressed themselves to their senses, and it was possible to view by ear the features of the neighbourhood. Acoustic pictures were returned from the darkened scenery; they could hear where the tracts of heather began and ended; where the furze was growing stalky and tall; where it had been recently cut; in what direction the fir-clump lay, and how near was the pit in which the hollies grew; for these differing features had their voices no less than their shapes and colours.\n\n“God, how lonely it is!” resumed Wildeve. “What are picturesque ravines and mists to us who see nothing else? Why should we stay here? Will you go with me to America? I have kindred in Wisconsin.”\n\n“That wants consideration.”\n\n“It seems impossible to do well here, unless one were a wild bird or a landscape-painter. Well?”\n\n“Give me time,” she softly said, taking his hand. “America is so far away. Are you going to walk with me a little way?”\n\nAs Eustacia uttered the latter words she retired from the base of the barrow, and Wildeve followed her, so that the reddleman could hear no more.\n\nHe lifted the turves and arose. Their black figures sank and disappeared from against the sky. They were as two horns which the sluggish heath had put forth from its crown, like a mollusc, and had now again drawn in.\n\nThe reddleman’s walk across the vale, and over into the next where his cart lay, was not sprightly for a slim young fellow of twenty-four. His spirit was perturbed to aching. The breezes that blew around his mouth in that walk carried off upon them the accents of a commination.\n\nHe entered the van, where there was a fire in a stove. Without lighting his candle he sat down at once on the three-legged stool, and pondered on what he had seen and heard touching that still-loved one of his. He uttered a sound which was neither sigh nor sob, but was even more indicative than either of a troubled mind.\n\n“My Tamsie,” he whispered heavily. “What can be done? Yes, I will see that Eustacia Vye.”\n\nX. A Desperate Attempt at Persuasion\nThe next morning, at the time when the height of the sun appeared very insignificant from any part of the heath as compared with the altitude of Rainbarrow, and when all the little hills in the lower levels were like an archipelago in a fog-formed Ægean, the reddleman came from the brambled nook which he had adopted as his quarters and ascended the slopes of Mistover Knap.\n\nThough these shaggy hills were apparently so solitary, several keen round eyes were always ready on such a wintry morning as this to converge upon a passer-by. Feathered species sojourned here in hiding which would have created wonder if found elsewhere. A bustard haunted the spot, and not many years before this five and twenty might have been seen in Egdon at one time. Marsh-harriers looked up from the valley by Wildeve’s. A cream-coloured courser had used to visit this hill, a bird so rare that not more than a dozen have ever been seen in England; but a barbarian rested neither night nor day till he had shot the African truant, and after that event cream-coloured coursers thought fit to enter Egdon no more.\n\nA traveller who should walk and observe any of these visitants as Venn observed them now could feel himself to be in direct communication with regions unknown to man. Here in front of him was a wild mallard—just arrived from the home of the north wind. The creature brought within him an amplitude of Northern knowledge. Glacial catastrophes, snowstorm episodes, glittering auroral effects, Polaris in the zenith, Franklin underfoot—the category of his commonplaces was wonderful. But the bird, like many other philosophers, seemed as he looked at the reddleman to think that a present moment of comfortable reality was worth a decade of memories.\n\nVenn passed on through these towards the house of the isolated beauty who lived up among them and despised them. The day was Sunday; but as going to church, except to be married or buried, was exceptional at Egdon, this made little difference. He had determined upon the bold stroke of asking for an interview with Miss Vye—to attack her position as Thomasin’s rival either by art or by storm, showing therein, somewhat too conspicuously, the want of gallantry characteristic of a certain astute sort of men, from clowns to kings. The great Frederick making war on the beautiful Archduchess, Napoleon refusing terms to the beautiful Queen of Prussia, were not more dead to difference of sex than the reddleman was, in his peculiar way, in planning the displacement of Eustacia.\n\nTo call at the captain’s cottage was always more or less an undertaking for the inferior inhabitants. Though occasionally chatty, his moods were erratic, and nobody could be certain how he would behave at any particular moment. Eustacia was reserved, and lived very much to herself. Except the daughter of one of the cotters, who was their servant, and a lad who worked in the garden and stable, scarcely anyone but themselves ever entered the house. They were the only genteel people of the district except the Yeobrights, and though far from rich, they did not feel that necessity for preserving a friendly face towards every man, bird, and beast which influenced their poorer neighbours.\n\nWhen the reddleman entered the garden the old man was looking through his glass at the stain of blue sea in the distant landscape, the little anchors on his buttons twinkling in the sun. He recognized Venn as his companion on the highway, but made no remark on that circumstance, merely saying, “Ah, reddleman—you here? Have a glass of grog?”\n\nVenn declined, on the plea of it being too early, and stated that his business was with Miss Vye. The captain surveyed him from cap to waistcoat and from waistcoat to leggings for a few moments, and finally asked him to go indoors.\n\nMiss Vye was not to be seen by anybody just then; and the reddleman waited in the window-bench of the kitchen, his hands hanging across his divergent knees, and his cap hanging from his hands.\n\n“I suppose the young lady is not up yet?” he presently said to the servant.\n\n“Not quite yet. Folks never call upon ladies at this time of day.”\n\n“Then I’ll step outside,” said Venn. “If she is willing to see me, will she please send out word, and I’ll come in.”\n\nThe reddleman left the house and loitered on the hill adjoining. A considerable time elapsed, and no request for his presence was brought. He was beginning to think that his scheme had failed, when he beheld the form of Eustacia herself coming leisurely towards him. A sense of novelty in giving audience to that singular figure had been sufficient to draw her forth.\n\nShe seemed to feel, after a bare look at Diggory Venn, that the man had come on a strange errand, and that he was not so mean as she had thought him; for her close approach did not cause him to writhe uneasily, or shift his feet, or show any of those little signs which escape an ingenuous rustic at the advent of the uncommon in womankind. On his inquiring if he might have a conversation with her she replied, “Yes, walk beside me,” and continued to move on.\n\nBefore they had gone far it occurred to the perspicacious reddleman that he would have acted more wisely by appearing less unimpressionable, and he resolved to correct the error as soon as he could find opportunity.\n\n“I have made so bold, miss, as to step across and tell you some strange news which has come to my ears about that man.”\n\n“Ah! what man?”\n\nHe jerked his elbow to the southeast—the direction of the Quiet Woman.\nEustacia turned quickly to him. “Do you mean Mr. Wildeve?”\n\n“Yes, there is trouble in a household on account of him, and I have come to let you know of it, because I believe you might have power to drive it away.”\n\n“I? What is the trouble?”\n\n“It is quite a secret. It is that he may refuse to marry Thomasin Yeobright after all.”\n\nEustacia, though set inwardly pulsing by his words, was equal to her part in such a drama as this. She replied coldly, “I do not wish to listen to this, and you must not expect me to interfere.”\n\n“But, miss, you will hear one word?”\n\n“I cannot. I am not interested in the marriage, and even if I were I could not compel Mr. Wildeve to do my bidding.”\n\n“As the only lady on the heath I think you might,” said Venn with subtle indirectness. “This is how the case stands. Mr. Wildeve would marry Thomasin at once, and make all matters smooth, if so be there were not another woman in the case. This other woman is some person he has picked up with, and meets on the heath occasionally, I believe. He will never marry her, and yet through her he may never marry the woman who loves him dearly. Now, if you, miss, who have so much sway over us menfolk, were to insist that he should treat your young neighbour Tamsin with honourable kindness and give up the other woman, he would perhaps do it, and save her a good deal of misery.”\n\n“Ah, my life!” said Eustacia, with a laugh which unclosed her lips so that the sun shone into her mouth as into a tulip, and lent it a similar scarlet fire. “You think too much of my influence over menfolk indeed, reddleman. If I had such a power as you imagine I would go straight and use it for the good of anybody who has been kind to me—which Thomasin Yeobright has not particularly, to my knowledge.”\n\n“Can it be that you really don’t know of it—how much she had always thought of you?”\n\n“I have never heard a word of it. Although we live only two miles apart I have never been inside her aunt’s house in my life.”\n\nThe superciliousness that lurked in her manner told Venn that thus far he had utterly failed. He inwardly sighed and felt it necessary to unmask his second argument.\n\n“Well, leaving that out of the question, ’tis in your power, I assure you, Miss Vye, to do a great deal of good to another woman.”\n\nShe shook her head.\n\n“Your comeliness is law with Mr. Wildeve. It is law with all men who see ’ee. They say, ‘This well-favoured lady coming—what’s her name? How handsome!’ Handsomer than Thomasin Yeobright,” the reddleman persisted, saying to himself, “God forgive a rascal for lying!” And she was handsomer, but the reddleman was far from thinking so. There was a certain obscurity in Eustacia’s beauty, and Venn’s eye was not trained. In her winter dress, as now, she was like the tiger-beetle, which, when observed in dull situations, seems to be of the quietest neutral colour, but under a full illumination blazes with dazzling splendour.\n\nEustacia could not help replying, though conscious that she endangered her dignity thereby. “Many women are lovelier than Thomasin,” she said, “so not much attaches to that.”\n\nThe reddleman suffered the wound and went on: “He is a man who notices the looks of women, and you could twist him to your will like withywind, if you only had the mind.”\n\n“Surely what she cannot do who has been so much with him I cannot do living up here away from him.”\n\nThe reddleman wheeled and looked her in the face. “Miss Vye!” he said.\n\n“Why do you say that—as if you doubted me?” She spoke faintly, and her breathing was quick. “The idea of your speaking in that tone to me!” she added, with a forced smile of hauteur. “What could have been in your mind to lead you to speak like that?”\n\n“Miss Vye, why should you make believe that you don’t know this man?—I know why, certainly. He is beneath you, and you are ashamed.”\n\n“You are mistaken. What do you mean?”\n\nThe reddleman had decided to play the card of truth. “I was at the meeting by Rainbarrow last night and heard every word,” he said. “The woman that stands between Wildeve and Thomasin is yourself.”\n\nIt was a disconcerting lift of the curtain, and the mortification of Candaules’ wife glowed in her. The moment had arrived when her lip would tremble in spite of herself, and when the gasp could no longer be kept down.\n\n“I am unwell,” she said hurriedly. “No—it is not that—I am not in a humour to hear you further. Leave me, please.”\n\n“I must speak, Miss Vye, in spite of paining you. What I would put before you is this. However it may come about—whether she is to blame, or you—her case is without doubt worse than yours. Your giving up Mr. Wildeve will be a real advantage to you, for how could you marry him? Now she cannot get off so easily—everybody will blame her if she loses him. Then I ask you—not because her right is best, but because her situation is worst—to give him up to her.”\n\n“No—I won’t, I won’t!” she said impetuously, quite forgetful of her previous manner towards the reddleman as an underling. “Nobody has ever been served so! It was going on well—I will not be beaten down—by an inferior woman like her. It is very well for you to come and plead for her, but is she not herself the cause of all her own trouble? Am I not to show favour to any person I may choose without asking permission of a parcel of cottagers? She has come between me and my inclination, and now that she finds herself rightly punished she gets you to plead for her!”\n\n“Indeed,” said Venn earnestly, “she knows nothing whatever about it. It is only I who ask you to give him up. It will be better for her and you both. People will say bad things if they find out that a lady secretly meets a man who has ill-used another woman.”\n\n“I have _not_ injured her—he was mine before he was hers! He came back—because—because he liked me best!” she said wildly. “But I lose all self-respect in talking to you. What am I giving way to!”\n\n“I can keep secrets,” said Venn gently. “You need not fear. I am the only man who knows of your meetings with him. There is but one thing more to speak of, and then I will be gone. I heard you say to him that you hated living here—that Egdon Heath was a jail to you.”\n\n“I did say so. There is a sort of beauty in the scenery, I know; but it is a jail to me. The man you mention does not save me from that feeling, though he lives here. I should have cared nothing for him had there been a better person near.”\n\nThe reddleman looked hopeful; after these words from her his third attempt seemed promising. “As we have now opened our minds a bit, miss,” he said, “I’ll tell you what I have got to propose. Since I have taken to the reddle trade I travel a good deal, as you know.”\n\nShe inclined her head, and swept round so that her eyes rested in the misty vale beneath them.\n\n“And in my travels I go near Budmouth. Now Budmouth is a wonderful place—wonderful—a great salt sheening sea bending into the land like a bow—thousands of gentlepeople walking up and down—bands of music playing—officers by sea and officers by land walking among the rest—out of every ten folks you meet nine of ’em in love.”\n\n“I know it,” she said disdainfully. “I know Budmouth better than you. I was born there. My father came to be a military musician there from abroad. Ah, my soul, Budmouth! I wish I was there now.”\n\nThe reddleman was surprised to see how a slow fire could blaze on occasion. “If you were, miss,” he replied, “in a week’s time you would think no more of Wildeve than of one of those he’th-croppers that we see yond. Now, I could get you there.”\n\n“How?” said Eustacia, with intense curiosity in her heavy eyes.\n\n“My uncle has been for five and twenty years the trusty man of a rich widow-lady who has a beautiful house facing the sea. This lady has become old and lame, and she wants a young company-keeper to read and sing to her, but can’t get one to her mind to save her life, though she’ve advertised in the papers, and tried half a dozen. She would jump to get you, and Uncle would make it all easy.”\n\n“I should have to work, perhaps?”\n\n“No, not real work—you’d have a little to do, such as reading and that. You would not be wanted till New Year’s Day.”\n\n“I knew it meant work,” she said, drooping to languor again.\n\n“I confess there would be a trifle to do in the way of amusing her; but though idle people might call it work, working people would call it play. Think of the company and the life you’d lead, miss; the gaiety you’d see, and the gentleman you’d marry. My uncle is to inquire for a trustworthy young lady from the country, as she don’t like town girls.”\n\n“It is to wear myself out to please her! and I won’t go. O, if I could live in a gay town as a lady should, and go my own ways, and do my own doings, I’d give the wrinkled half of my life! Yes, reddleman, that would I.”\n\n“Help me to get Thomasin happy, miss, and the chance shall be yours,” urged her companion.\n\n“Chance—’tis no chance,” she said proudly. “What can a poor man like you offer me, indeed?—I am going indoors. I have nothing more to say. Don’t your horses want feeding, or your reddlebags want mending, or don’t you want to find buyers for your goods, that you stay idling here like this?”\n\nVenn spoke not another word. With his hands behind him he turned away, that she might not see the hopeless disappointment in his face. The mental clearness and power he had found in this lonely girl had indeed filled his manner with misgiving even from the first few minutes of close quarters with her. Her youth and situation had led him to expect a simplicity quite at the beck of his method. But a system of inducement which might have carried weaker country lasses along with it had merely repelled Eustacia. As a rule, the word Budmouth meant fascination on Egdon. That Royal port and watering place, if truly mirrored in the minds of the heathfolk, must have combined, in a charming and indescribable manner a Carthaginian bustle of building with Tarentine luxuriousness and Baian health and beauty. Eustacia felt little less extravagantly about the place; but she would not sink her independence to get there.\n\nWhen Diggory Venn had gone quite away, Eustacia walked to the bank and looked down the wild and picturesque vale towards the sun, which was also in the direction of Wildeve’s. The mist had now so far collapsed that the tips of the trees and bushes around his house could just be discerned, as if boring upwards through a vast white cobweb which cloaked them from the day. There was no doubt that her mind was inclined thitherward; indefinitely, fancifully—twining and untwining about him as the single object within her horizon on which dreams might crystallize. The man who had begun by being merely her amusement, and would never have been more than her hobby but for his skill in deserting her at the right moments, was now again her desire. Cessation in his love-making had revivified her love. Such feeling as Eustacia had idly given to Wildeve was dammed into a flood by Thomasin. She had used to tease Wildeve, but that was before another had favoured him. Often a drop of irony into an indifferent situation renders the whole piquant.\n\n“I will never give him up—never!” she said impetuously.\n\nThe reddleman’s hint that rumour might show her to disadvantage had no permanent terror for Eustacia. She was as unconcerned at that contingency as a goddess at a lack of linen. This did not originate in inherent shamelessness, but in her living too far from the world to feel the impact of public opinion. Zenobia in the desert could hardly have cared what was said about her at Rome. As far as social ethics were concerned Eustacia approached the savage state, though in emotion she was all the while an epicure. She had advanced to the secret recesses of sensuousness, yet had hardly crossed the threshold of conventionality.\nXI. The Dishonesty of an Honest Woman\n\nThe reddleman had left Eustacia’s presence with desponding views on Thomasin’s future happiness; but he was awakened to the fact that one other channel remained untried by seeing, as he followed the way to his van, the form of Mrs. Yeobright slowly walking towards the Quiet Woman. He went across to her; and could almost perceive in her anxious face that this journey of hers to Wildeve was undertaken with the same object as his own to Eustacia.\n\nShe did not conceal the fact. “Then,” said the reddleman, “you may as well leave it alone, Mrs. Yeobright.”\n\n“I half think so myself,” she said. “But nothing else remains to be done besides pressing the question upon him.”\n\n“I should like to say a word first,” said Venn firmly. “Mr. Wildeve is not the only man who has asked Thomasin to marry him; and why should not another have a chance? Mrs. Yeobright, I should be glad to marry your niece and would have done it any time these last two years. There, now it is out, and I have never told anybody before but herself.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright was not demonstrative, but her eyes involuntarily glanced towards his singular though shapely figure.\n\n“Looks are not everything,” said the reddleman, noticing the glance. “There’s many a calling that don’t bring in so much as mine, if it comes to money; and perhaps I am not so much worse off than Wildeve. There is nobody so poor as these professional fellows who have failed; and if you shouldn’t like my redness—well, I am not red by birth, you know; I only took to this business for a freak; and I might turn my hand to something else in good time.”\n\n“I am much obliged to you for your interest in my niece; but I fear there would be objections. More than that, she is devoted to this man.”\n\n“True; or I shouldn’t have done what I have this morning.”\n\n“Otherwise there would be no pain in the case, and you would not see me going to his house now. What was Thomasin’s answer when you told her of your feelings?”\n\n“She wrote that you would object to me; and other things.”\n\n“She was in a measure right. You must not take this unkindly—I merely state it as a truth. You have been good to her, and we do not forget it. But as she was unwilling on her own account to be your wife, that settles the point without my wishes being concerned.”\n\n“Yes. But there is a difference between then and now, ma’am. She is distressed now, and I have thought that if you were to talk to her about me, and think favourably of me yourself, there might be a chance of winning her round, and getting her quite independent of this Wildeve’s backward and forward play, and his not knowing whether he’ll have her or no.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright shook her head. “Thomasin thinks, and I think with her, that she ought to be Wildeve’s wife, if she means to appear before the world without a slur upon her name. If they marry soon, everybody will believe that an accident did really prevent the wedding. If not, it may cast a shade upon her character—at any rate make her ridiculous. In short, if it is anyhow possible they must marry now.”\n\n“I thought that till half an hour ago. But, after all, why should her going off with him to Anglebury for a few hours do her any harm? Anybody who knows how pure she is will feel any such thought to be quite unjust. I have been trying this morning to help on this marriage with Wildeve—yes, I, ma’am—in the belief that I ought to do it, because she was so wrapped up in him. But I much question if I was right, after all. However, nothing came of it. And now I offer myself.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright appeared disinclined to enter further into the question. “I fear I must go on,” she said. “I do not see that anything else can be done.”\n\nAnd she went on. But though this conversation did not divert Thomasin’s aunt from her purposed interview with Wildeve, it made a considerable difference in her mode of conducting that interview. She thanked God for the weapon which the reddleman had put into her hands.\n\nWildeve was at home when she reached the inn. He showed her silently into the parlour, and closed the door. Mrs. Yeobright began—\n\n“I have thought it my duty to call today. A new proposal has been made to me, which has rather astonished me. It will affect Thomasin greatly; and I have decided that it should at least be mentioned to you.”\n\n“Yes? What is it?” he said civilly.\n\n“It is, of course, in reference to her future. You may not be aware that another man has shown himself anxious to marry Thomasin. Now, though I have not encouraged him yet, I cannot conscientiously refuse him a chance any longer. I don’t wish to be short with you; but I must be fair to him and to her.”\n\n“Who is the man?” said Wildeve with surprise.\n\n“One who has been in love with her longer than she has with you. He proposed to her two years ago. At that time she refused him.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“He has seen her lately, and has asked me for permission to pay his addresses to her. She may not refuse him twice.”\n\n“What is his name?”\n\nMrs. Yeobright declined to say. “He is a man Thomasin likes,” she added, “and one whose constancy she respects at least. It seems to me that what she refused then she would be glad to get now. She is much annoyed at her awkward position.”\n\n“She never once told me of this old lover.”\n\n“The gentlest women are not such fools as to show _every_ card.”\n\n“Well, if she wants him I suppose she must have him.”\n\n“It is easy enough to say that; but you don’t see the difficulty. He wants her much more than she wants him; and before I can encourage anything of the sort I must have a clear understanding from you that you will not interfere to injure an arrangement which I promote in the belief that it is for the best. Suppose, when they are engaged, and everything is smoothly arranged for their marriage, that you should step between them and renew your suit? You might not win her back, but you might cause much unhappiness.”\n\n“Of course I should do no such thing,” said Wildeve “But they are not engaged yet. How do you know that Thomasin would accept him?”\n\n“That’s a question I have carefully put to myself; and upon the whole the probabilities are in favour of her accepting him in time. I flatter myself that I have some influence over her. She is pliable, and I can be strong in my recommendations of him.”\n\n“And in your disparagement of me at the same time.”\n\n“Well, you may depend upon my not praising you,” she said drily. “And if this seems like manœuvring, you must remember that her position is peculiar, and that she has been hardly used. I shall also be helped in making the match by her own desire to escape from the humiliation of her present state; and a woman’s pride in these cases will lead her a very great way. A little managing may be required to bring her round; but I am equal to that, provided that you agree to the one thing indispensable; that is, to make a distinct declaration that she is to think no more of you as a possible husband. That will pique her into accepting him.”\n\n“I can hardly say that just now, Mrs. Yeobright. It is so sudden.”\n\n“And so my whole plan is interfered with! It is very inconvenient that you refuse to help my family even to the small extent of saying distinctly you will have nothing to do with us.”\n\nWildeve reflected uncomfortably. “I confess I was not prepared for this,” he said. “Of course I’ll give her up if you wish, if it is necessary. But I thought I might be her husband.”\n\n“We have heard that before.”\n\n“Now, Mrs. Yeobright, don’t let us disagree. Give me a fair time. I don’t want to stand in the way of any better chance she may have; only I wish you had let me know earlier. I will write to you or call in a day or two. Will that suffice?”\n\n“Yes,” she replied, “provided you promise not to communicate with Thomasin without my knowledge.”\n\n“I promise that,” he said. And the interview then terminated, Mrs. Yeobright returning homeward as she had come.\n\nBy far the greatest effect of her simple strategy on that day was, as often happens, in a quarter quite outside her view when arranging it. In the first place, her visit sent Wildeve the same evening after dark to Eustacia’s house at Mistover.\n\nAt this hour the lonely dwelling was closely blinded and shuttered from the chill and darkness without. Wildeve’s clandestine plan with her was to take a little gravel in his hand and hold it to the crevice at the top of the window shutter, which was on the outside, so that it should fall with a gentle rustle, resembling that of a mouse, between shutter and glass. This precaution in attracting her attention was to avoid arousing the suspicions of her grandfather.\n\nThe soft words, “I hear; wait for me,” in Eustacia’s voice from within told him that she was alone.\n\nHe waited in his customary manner by walking round the enclosure and idling by the pool, for Wildeve was never asked into the house by his proud though condescending mistress. She showed no sign of coming out in a hurry. The time wore on, and he began to grow impatient. In the course of twenty minutes she appeared from round the corner, and advanced as if merely taking an airing.\n\n“You would not have kept me so long had you known what I come about,” he said with bitterness. “Still, you are worth waiting for.”\n\n“What has happened?” said Eustacia. “I did not know you were in trouble. I too am gloomy enough.”\n\n“I am not in trouble,” said he. “It is merely that affairs have come to a head, and I must take a clear course.”\n\n“What course is that?” she asked with attentive interest.\n\n“And can you forget so soon what I proposed to you the other night? Why, take you from this place, and carry you away with me abroad.”\n\n“I have not forgotten. But why have you come so unexpectedly to repeat the question, when you only promised to come next Saturday? I thought I was to have plenty of time to consider.”\n\n“Yes, but the situation is different now.”\n\n“Explain to me.”\n\n“I don’t want to explain, for I may pain you.”\n\n“But I must know the reason of this hurry.”\n\n“It is simply my ardour, dear Eustacia. Everything is smooth now.”\n\n“Then why are you so ruffled?”\n\n“I am not aware of it. All is as it should be. Mrs. Yeobright—but she is nothing to us.”\n\n“Ah, I knew she had something to do with it! Come, I don’t like reserve.”\n\n“No—she has nothing. She only says she wishes me to give up Thomasin because another man is anxious to marry her. The woman, now she no longer needs me, actually shows off!” Wildeve’s vexation has escaped him in spite of himself.\n\nEustacia was silent a long while. “You are in the awkward position of an official who is no longer wanted,” she said in a changed tone.\n\n“It seems so. But I have not yet seen Thomasin.”\n\n“And that irritates you. Don’t deny it, Damon. You are actually nettled by this slight from an unexpected quarter.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“And you come to get me because you cannot get her. This is certainly a new position altogether. I am to be a stop-gap.”\n\n“Please remember that I proposed the same thing the other day.”\n\nEustacia again remained in a sort of stupefied silence. What curious feeling was this coming over her? Was it really possible that her interest in Wildeve had been so entirely the result of antagonism that the glory and the dream departed from the man with the first sound that he was no longer coveted by her rival? She was, then, secure of him at last. Thomasin no longer required him. What a humiliating victory! He loved her best, she thought; and yet—dared she to murmur such treacherous criticism ever so softly?—what was the man worth whom a woman inferior to herself did not value? The sentiment which lurks more or less in all animate nature—that of not desiring the undesired of others—was lively as a passion in the supersubtle, epicurean heart of Eustacia. Her social superiority over him, which hitherto had scarcely ever impressed her, became unpleasantly insistent, and for the first time she felt that she had stooped in loving him.\n\n“Well, darling, you agree?” said Wildeve.\n\n“If it could be London, or even Budmouth, instead of America,” she murmured languidly. “Well, I will think. It is too great a thing for me to decide offhand. I wish I hated the heath less—or loved you more.”\n\n“You can be painfully frank. You loved me a month ago warmly enough to go anywhere with me.”\n\n“And you loved Thomasin.”\n\n“Yes, perhaps that was where the reason lay,” he returned, with almost a sneer. “I don’t hate her now.”\n\n“Exactly. The only thing is that you can no longer get her.”\n\n“Come—no taunts, Eustacia, or we shall quarrel. If you don’t agree to go with me, and agree shortly, I shall go by myself.”\n\n“Or try Thomasin again. Damon, how strange it seems that you could have married her or me indifferently, and only have come to me because I am—cheapest! Yes, yes—it is true. There was a time when I should have exclaimed against a man of that sort, and been quite wild; but it is all past now.”\n\n“Will you go, dearest? Come secretly with me to Bristol, marry me, and turn our backs upon this dog-hole of England for ever? Say Yes.”\n\n“I want to get away from here at almost any cost,” she said with weariness, “but I don’t like to go with you. Give me more time to decide.”\n\n“I have already,” said Wildeve. “Well, I give you one more week.”\n\n“A little longer, so that I may tell you decisively. I have to consider so many things. Fancy Thomasin being anxious to get rid of you! I cannot forget it.”\n\n“Never mind that. Say Monday week. I will be here precisely at this time.”\n\n“Let it be at Rainbarrow,” said she. “This is too near home; my grandfather may be walking out.”\n\n“Thank you, dear. On Monday week at this time I will be at the Barrow. Till then good-bye.”\n\n“Good-bye. No, no, you must not touch me now. Shaking hands is enough till I have made up my mind.”\n\nEustacia watched his shadowy form till it had disappeared. She placed her hand to her forehead and breathed heavily; and then her rich, romantic lips parted under that homely impulse—a yawn. She was immediately angry at having betrayed even to herself the possible evanescence of her passion for him. She could not admit at once that she might have overestimated Wildeve, for to perceive his mediocrity now was to admit her own great folly heretofore. And the discovery that she was the owner of a disposition so purely that of the dog in the manger had something in it which at first made her ashamed.\n\nThe fruit of Mrs. Yeobright’s diplomacy was indeed remarkable, though not as yet of the kind she had anticipated. It had appreciably influenced Wildeve, but it was influencing Eustacia far more. Her lover was no longer to her an exciting man whom many women strove for, and herself could only retain by striving with them. He was a superfluity.\n\nShe went indoors in that peculiar state of misery which is not exactly grief, and which especially attends the dawnings of reason in the latter days of an ill-judged, transient love. To be conscious that the end of the dream is approaching, and yet has not absolutely come, is one of the most wearisome as well as the most curious stages along the course between the beginning of a passion and its end.\n\nHer grandfather had returned, and was busily engaged in pouring some gallons of newly arrived rum into the square bottles of his square cellaret. Whenever these home supplies were exhausted he would go to the Quiet Woman, and, standing with his back to the fire, grog in hand, tell remarkable stories of how he had lived seven years under the waterline of his ship, and other naval wonders, to the natives, who hoped too earnestly for a treat of ale from the teller to exhibit any doubts of his truth.\n\nHe had been there this evening. “I suppose you have heard the Egdon news, Eustacia?” he said, without looking up from the bottles. “The men have been talking about it at the Woman as if it were of national importance.”\n\n“I have heard none,” she said.\n\n“Young Clym Yeobright, as they call him, is coming home next week to spend Christmas with his mother. He is a fine fellow by this time, it seems. I suppose you remember him?”\n\n“I never saw him in my life.”\n\n“Ah, true; he left before you came here. I well remember him as a promising boy.”\n\n“Where has he been living all these years?”\n\n“In that rookery of pomp and vanity, Paris, I believe.”\n\nI. Tidings of the Comer\nOn the fine days at this time of the year, and earlier, certain ephemeral operations were apt to disturb, in their trifling way, the majestic calm of Egdon Heath. They were activities which, beside those of a town, a village, or even a farm, would have appeared as the ferment of stagnation merely, a creeping of the flesh of somnolence. But here, away from comparisons, shut in by the stable hills, among which mere walking had the novelty of pageantry, and where any man could imagine himself to be Adam without the least difficulty, they attracted the attention of every bird within eyeshot, every reptile not yet asleep, and set the surrounding rabbits curiously watching from hillocks at a safe distance.\n\nThe performance was that of bringing together and building into a stack the furze faggots which Humphrey had been cutting for the captain’s use during the foregoing fine days. The stack was at the end of the dwelling, and the men engaged in building it were Humphrey and Sam, the old man looking on.\n\nIt was a fine and quiet afternoon, about three o’clock; but the winter solstice having stealthily come on, the lowness of the sun caused the hour to seem later than it actually was, there being little here to remind an inhabitant that he must unlearn his summer experience of the sky as a dial. In the course of many days and weeks sunrise had advanced its quarters from northeast to southeast, sunset had receded from northwest to southwest; but Egdon had hardly heeded the change.\n\nEustacia was indoors in the dining-room, which was really more like a kitchen, having a stone floor and a gaping chimney-corner. The air was still, and while she lingered a moment here alone sounds of voices in conversation came to her ears directly down the chimney. She entered the recess, and, listening, looked up the old irregular shaft, with its cavernous hollows, where the smoke blundered about on its way to the square bit of sky at the top, from which the daylight struck down with a pallid glare upon the tatters of soot draping the flue as seaweed drapes a rocky fissure.\n\nShe remembered: the furze-stack was not far from the chimney, and the voices were those of the workers.\n\nHer grandfather joined in the conversation. “That lad ought never to have left home. His father’s occupation would have suited him best, and the boy should have followed on. I don’t believe in these new moves in families. My father was a sailor, so was I, and so should my son have been if I had had one.”\n\n“The place he’s been living at is Paris,” said Humphrey, “and they tell me ’tis where the king’s head was cut off years ago. My poor mother used to tell me about that business. ‘Hummy,’ she used to say, ‘I was a young maid then, and as I was at home ironing Mother’s caps one afternoon the parson came in and said, “They’ve cut the king’s head off, Jane; and what ’twill be next God knows.’”\n\n“A good many of us knew as well as He before long,” said the captain, chuckling. “I lived seven years under water on account of it in my boyhood—in that damned surgery of the Triumph, seeing men brought down to the cockpit with their legs and arms blown to Jericho.... And so the young man has settled in Paris. Manager to a diamond merchant, or some such thing, is he not?”\n\n“Yes, sir, that’s it. ’Tis a blazing great business that he belongs to, so I’ve heard his mother say—like a king’s palace, as far as diments go.”\n\n“I can well mind when he left home,” said Sam.\n\n“’Tis a good thing for the feller,” said Humphrey. “A sight of times better to be selling diments than nobbling about here.”\n\n“It must cost a good few shillings to deal at such a place.”\n\n“A good few indeed, my man,” replied the captain. “Yes, you may make away with a deal of money and be neither drunkard nor glutton.”\n\n“They say, too, that Clym Yeobright is become a real perusing man, with the strangest notions about things. There, that’s because he went to school early, such as the school was.”\n\n“Strange notions, has he?” said the old man. “Ah, there’s too much of that sending to school in these days! It only does harm. Every gatepost and barn’s door you come to is sure to have some bad word or other chalked upon it by the young rascals—a woman can hardly pass for shame sometimes. If they’d never been taught how to write they wouldn’t have been able to scribble such villainy. Their fathers couldn’t do it, and the country was all the better for it.”\n\n“Now, I should think, Cap’n, that Miss Eustacia had about as much in her head that comes from books as anybody about here?”\n\n“Perhaps if Miss Eustacia, too, had less romantic nonsense in her head it would be better for her,” said the captain shortly; after which he walked away.\n\n“I say, Sam,” observed Humphrey when the old man was gone, “she and Clym Yeobright would make a very pretty pigeon-pair—hey? If they wouldn’t I’ll be dazed! Both of one mind about niceties for certain, and learned in print, and always thinking about high doctrine—there couldn’t be a better couple if they were made o’ purpose. Clym’s family is as good as hers. His father was a farmer, that’s true; but his mother was a sort of lady, as we know. Nothing would please me better than to see them two man and wife.”\n\n“They’d look very natty, arm-in-crook together, and their best clothes on, whether or no, if he’s at all the well-favoured fellow he used to be.”\n\n“They would, Humphrey. Well, I should like to see the chap terrible much after so many years. If I knew for certain when he was coming I’d stroll out three or four miles to meet him and help carry anything for’n; though I suppose he’s altered from the boy he was. They say he can talk French as fast as a maid can eat blackberries; and if so, depend upon it we who have stayed at home shall seem no more than scroff in his eyes.”\n\n“Coming across the water to Budmouth by steamer, isn’t he?”\n\n“Yes; but how he’s coming from Budmouth I don’t know.”\n\n“That’s a bad trouble about his cousin Thomasin. I wonder such a nice-notioned fellow as Clym likes to come home into it. What a nunnywatch we were in, to be sure, when we heard they weren’t married at all, after singing to ’em as man and wife that night! Be dazed if I should like a relation of mine to have been made such a fool of by a man. It makes the family look small.”\n\n“Yes. Poor maid, her heart has ached enough about it. Her health is suffering from it, I hear, for she will bide entirely indoors. We never see her out now, scampering over the furze with a face as red as a rose, as she used to do.”\n\n“I’ve heard she wouldn’t have Wildeve now if he asked her.”\n\n“You have? ’Tis news to me.”\n\nWhile the furze-gatherers had desultorily conversed thus Eustacia’s face gradually bent to the hearth in a profound reverie, her toe unconsciously tapping the dry turf which lay burning at her feet.\n\nThe subject of their discourse had been keenly interesting to her. A young and clever man was coming into that lonely heath from, of all contrasting places in the world, Paris. It was like a man coming from heaven. More singular still, the heathmen had instinctively coupled her and this man together in their minds as a pair born for each other.\n\nThat five minutes of overhearing furnished Eustacia with visions enough to fill the whole blank afternoon. Such sudden alternations from mental vacuity do sometimes occur thus quietly. She could never have believed in the morning that her colourless inner world would before night become as animated as water under a microscope, and that without the arrival of a single visitor. The words of Sam and Humphrey on the harmony between the unknown and herself had on her mind the effect of the invading Bard’s prelude in the Castle of Indolence, at which myriads of imprisoned shapes arose where had previously appeared the stillness of a void.\n\nInvolved in these imaginings she knew nothing of time. When she became conscious of externals it was dusk. The furze-rick was finished; the men had gone home. Eustacia went upstairs, thinking that she would take a walk at this her usual time; and she determined that her walk should be in the direction of Blooms-End, the birthplace of young Yeobright and the present home of his mother. She had no reason for walking elsewhere, and why should she not go that way? The scene of the daydream is sufficient for a pilgrimage at nineteen. To look at the palings before the Yeobrights’ house had the dignity of a necessary performance. Strange that such a piece of idling should have seemed an important errand.\n\nShe put on her bonnet, and, leaving the house, descended the hill on the side towards Blooms-End, where she walked slowly along the valley for a distance of a mile and a half. This brought her to a spot in which the green bottom of the dale began to widen, the furze bushes to recede yet further from the path on each side, till they were diminished to an isolated one here and there by the increasing fertility of the soil. Beyond the irregular carpet of grass was a row of white palings, which marked the verge of the heath in this latitude. They showed upon the dusky scene that they bordered as distinctly as white lace on velvet. Behind the white palings was a little garden; behind the garden an old, irregular, thatched house, facing the heath, and commanding a full view of the valley. This was the obscure, removed spot to which was about to return a man whose latter life had been passed in the French capital—the centre and vortex of the fashionable world.\nII. The People at Blooms-End Make Ready\n\nAll that afternoon the expected arrival of the subject of Eustacia’s ruminations created a bustle of preparation at Blooms-End. Thomasin had been persuaded by her aunt, and by an instinctive impulse of loyalty towards her cousin Clym, to bestir herself on his account with an alacrity unusual in her during these most sorrowful days of her life. At the time that Eustacia was listening to the rick-makers’ conversation on Clym’s return, Thomasin was climbing into a loft over her aunt’s fuelhouse, where the store-apples were kept, to search out the best and largest of them for the coming holiday-time.\n\nThe loft was lighted by a semicircular hole, through which the pigeons crept to their lodgings in the same high quarters of the premises; and from this hole the sun shone in a bright yellow patch upon the figure of the maiden as she knelt and plunged her naked arms into the soft brown fern, which, from its abundance, was used on Egdon in packing away stores of all kinds. The pigeons were flying about her head with the greatest unconcern, and the face of her aunt was just visible above the floor of the loft, lit by a few stray motes of light, as she stood halfway up the ladder, looking at a spot into which she was not climber enough to venture.\n\n“Now a few russets, Tamsin. He used to like them almost as well as ribstones.”\n\nThomasin turned and rolled aside the fern from another nook, where more mellow fruit greeted her with its ripe smell. Before picking them out she stopped a moment.\n\n“Dear Clym, I wonder how your face looks now?” she said, gazing abstractedly at the pigeon-hole, which admitted the sunlight so directly upon her brown hair and transparent tissues that it almost seemed to shine through her.\n\n“If he could have been dear to you in another way,” said Mrs. Yeobright from the ladder, “this might have been a happy meeting.”\n\n“Is there any use in saying what can do no good, Aunt?”\n\n“Yes,” said her aunt, with some warmth. “To thoroughly fill the air with the past misfortune, so that other girls may take warning and keep clear of it.”\n\nThomasin lowered her face to the apples again. “I am a warning to others, just as thieves and drunkards and gamblers are,” she said in a low voice. “What a class to belong to! Do I really belong to them? ’Tis absurd! Yet why, Aunt, does everybody keep on making me think that I do, by the way they behave towards me? Why don’t people judge me by my acts? Now, look at me as I kneel here, picking up these apples—do I look like a lost woman?... I wish all good women were as good as I!” she added vehemently.\n\n“Strangers don’t see you as I do,” said Mrs. Yeobright; “they judge from false report. Well, it is a silly job, and I am partly to blame.”\n\n“How quickly a rash thing can be done!” replied the girl. Her lips were quivering, and tears so crowded themselves into her eyes that she could hardly distinguish apples from fern as she continued industriously searching to hide her weakness.\n\n“As soon as you have finished getting the apples,” her aunt said, descending the ladder, “come down, and we’ll go for the holly. There is nobody on the heath this afternoon, and you need not fear being stared at. We must get some berries, or Clym will never believe in our preparations.”\n\nThomasin came down when the apples were collected, and together they went through the white palings to the heath beyond. The open hills were airy and clear, and the remote atmosphere appeared, as it often appears on a fine winter day, in distinct planes of illumination independently toned, the rays which lit the nearer tracts of landscape streaming visibly across those further off; a stratum of ensaffroned light was imposed on a stratum of deep blue, and behind these lay still remoter scenes wrapped in frigid grey.\n\nThey reached the place where the hollies grew, which was in a conical pit, so that the tops of the trees were not much above the general level of the ground. Thomasin stepped up into a fork of one of the bushes, as she had done under happier circumstances on many similar occasions, and with a small chopper that they had brought she began to lop off the heavily berried boughs.\n\n“Don’t scratch your face,” said her aunt, who stood at the edge of the pit, regarding the girl as she held on amid the glistening green and scarlet masses of the tree. “Will you walk with me to meet him this evening?”\n\n“I should like to. Else it would seem as if I had forgotten him,” said Thomasin, tossing out a bough. “Not that that would matter much; I belong to one man; nothing can alter that. And that man I must marry, for my pride’s sake.”\n\n“I am afraid—” began Mrs. Yeobright.\n\n“Ah, you think, ‘That weak girl—how is she going to get a man to marry her when she chooses?’ But let me tell you one thing, Aunt: Mr. Wildeve is not a profligate man, any more than I am an improper woman. He has an unfortunate manner, and doesn’t try to make people like him if they don’t wish to do it of their own accord.”\n\n“Thomasin,” said Mrs. Yeobright quietly, fixing her eye upon her niece, “do you think you deceive me in your defence of Mr. Wildeve?”\n\n“How do you mean?”\n\n“I have long had a suspicion that your love for him has changed its colour since you have found him not to be the saint you thought him, and that you act a part to me.”\n\n“He wished to marry me, and I wish to marry him.”\n\n“Now, I put it to you: would you at this present moment agree to be his wife if that had not happened to entangle you with him?”\n\nThomasin looked into the tree and appeared much disturbed. “Aunt,” she said presently, “I have, I think, a right to refuse to answer that question.”\n\n“Yes, you have.”\n\n“You may think what you choose. I have never implied to you by word or deed that I have grown to think otherwise of him, and I never will. And I shall marry him.”\n\n“Well, wait till he repeats his offer. I think he may do it, now that he knows—something I told him. I don’t for a moment dispute that it is the most proper thing for you to marry him. Much as I have objected to him in bygone days, I agree with you now, you may be sure. It is the only way out of a false position, and a very galling one.”\n\n“What did you tell him?”\n\n“That he was standing in the way of another lover of yours.”\n\n“Aunt,” said Thomasin, with round eyes, “what _do_ you mean?”\n\n“Don’t be alarmed; it was my duty. I can say no more about it now, but when it is over I will tell you exactly what I said, and why I said it.”\n\nThomasin was perforce content.\n\n“And you will keep the secret of my would-be marriage from Clym for the present?” she next asked.\n\n“I have given my word to. But what is the use of it? He must soon know what has happened. A mere look at your face will show him that something is wrong.”\n\nThomasin turned and regarded her aunt from the tree. “Now, hearken to me,” she said, her delicate voice expanding into firmness by a force which was other than physical. “Tell him nothing. If he finds out that I am not worthy to be his cousin, let him. But, since he loved me once, we will not pain him by telling him my trouble too soon. The air is full of the story, I know; but gossips will not dare to speak of it to him for the first few days. His closeness to me is the very thing that will hinder the tale from reaching him early. If I am not made safe from sneers in a week or two I will tell him myself.”\n\nThe earnestness with which Thomasin spoke prevented further objections. Her aunt simply said, “Very well. He should by rights have been told at the time that the wedding was going to be. He will never forgive you for your secrecy.”\n\n“Yes, he will, when he knows it was because I wished to spare him, and that I did not expect him home so soon. And you must not let me stand in the way of your Christmas party. Putting it off would only make matters worse.”\n\n“Of course I shall not. I do not wish to show myself beaten before all Egdon, and the sport of a man like Wildeve. We have enough berries now, I think, and we had better take them home. By the time we have decked the house with this and hung up the mistletoe, we must think of starting to meet him.”\n\nThomasin came out of the tree, shook from her hair and dress the loose berries which had fallen thereon, and went down the hill with her aunt, each woman bearing half the gathered boughs. It was now nearly four o’clock, and the sunlight was leaving the vales. When the west grew red the two relatives came again from the house and plunged into the heath in a different direction from the first, towards a point in the distant highway along which the expected man was to return.\nIII. How a Little Sound Produced a Great Dream\n\nEustacia stood just within the heath, straining her eyes in the direction of Mrs. Yeobright’s house and premises. No light, sound, or movement was perceptible there. The evening was chilly; the spot was dark and lonely. She inferred that the guest had not yet come; and after lingering ten or fifteen minutes she turned again towards home.\n\nShe had not far retraced her steps when sounds in front of her betokened the approach of persons in conversation along the same path. Soon their heads became visible against the sky. They were walking slowly; and though it was too dark for much discovery of character from aspect, the gait of them showed that they were not workers on the heath. Eustacia stepped a little out of the foot-track to let them pass. They were two women and a man; and the voices of the women were those of Mrs. Yeobright and Thomasin.\n\nThey went by her, and at the moment of passing appeared to discern her dusky form. There came to her ears in a masculine voice, “Good night!”\nShe murmured a reply, glided by them, and turned round. She could not, for a moment, believe that chance, unrequested, had brought into her presence the soul of the house she had gone to inspect, the man without whom her inspection would not have been thought of.\n\nShe strained her eyes to see them, but was unable. Such was her intentness, however, that it seemed as if her ears were performing the functions of seeing as well as hearing. This extension of power can almost be believed in at such moments. The deaf Dr. Kitto was probably under the influence of a parallel fancy when he described his body as having become, by long endeavour, so sensitive to vibrations that he had gained the power of perceiving by it as by ears.\n\nShe could follow every word that the ramblers uttered. They were talking no secrets. They were merely indulging in the ordinary vivacious chat of relatives who have long been parted in person though not in soul. But it was not to the words that Eustacia listened; she could not even have recalled, a few minutes later, what the words were. It was to the alternating voice that gave out about one-tenth of them—the voice that had wished her good night. Sometimes this throat uttered Yes, sometimes it uttered No; sometimes it made inquiries about a time-worn denizen of the place. Once it surprised her notions by remarking upon the friendliness and geniality written in the faces of the hills around.\n\nThe three voices passed on, and decayed and died out upon her ear. Thus much had been granted her; and all besides withheld. No event could have been more exciting. During the greater part of the afternoon she had been entrancing herself by imagining the fascination which must attend a man come direct from beautiful Paris—laden with its atmosphere, familiar with its charms. And this man had greeted her.\nWith the departure of the figures the profuse articulations of the women wasted away from her memory; but the accents of the other stayed on. Was there anything in the voice of Mrs. Yeobright’s son—for Clym it was—startling as a sound? No; it was simply comprehensive. All emotional things were possible to the speaker of that “good night.” Eustacia’s imagination supplied the rest—except the solution to one riddle. What _could_ the tastes of that man be who saw friendliness and geniality in these shaggy hills?\n\nOn such occasions as this a thousand ideas pass through a highly charged woman’s head; and they indicate themselves on her face; but the changes, though actual, are minute. Eustacia’s features went through a rhythmical succession of them. She glowed; remembering the mendacity of the imagination, she flagged; then she freshened; then she fired; then she cooled again. It was a cycle of aspects, produced by a cycle of visions.\n\nEustacia entered her own house; she was excited. Her grandfather was enjoying himself over the fire, raking about the ashes and exposing the red-hot surface of the turves, so that their lurid glare irradiated the chimney-corner with the hues of a furnace.\n\n“Why is it that we are never friendly with the Yeobrights?” she said, coming forward and stretching her soft hands over the warmth. “I wish we were. They seem to be very nice people.”\n\n“Be hanged if I know why,” said the captain. “I liked the old man well enough, though he was as rough as a hedge. But you would never have cared to go there, even if you might have, I am well sure.”\n\n“Why shouldn’t I?”\n\n“Your town tastes would find them far too countrified. They sit in the kitchen, drink mead and elder-wine, and sand the floor to keep it clean. A sensible way of life; but how would you like it?”\n\n“I thought Mrs. Yeobright was a ladylike woman? A curate’s daughter, was she not?”\n\n“Yes; but she was obliged to live as her husband did; and I suppose she has taken kindly to it by this time. Ah, I recollect that I once accidentally offended her, and I have never seen her since.”\n\nThat night was an eventful one to Eustacia’s brain, and one which she hardly ever forgot. She dreamt a dream; and few human beings, from Nebuchadnezzar to the Swaffham tinker, ever dreamt a more remarkable one. Such an elaborately developed, perplexing, exciting dream was certainly never dreamed by a girl in Eustacia’s situation before. It had as many ramifications as the Cretan labyrinth, as many fluctuations as the northern lights, as much colour as a parterre in June, and was as crowded with figures as a coronation. To Queen Scheherazade the dream might have seemed not far removed from commonplace; and to a girl just returned from all the courts of Europe it might have seemed not more than interesting. But amid the circumstances of Eustacia’s life it was as wonderful as a dream could be.\n\nThere was, however, gradually evolved from its transformation scenes a less extravagant episode, in which the heath dimly appeared behind the general brilliancy of the action. She was dancing to wondrous music, and her partner was the man in silver armour who had accompanied her through the previous fantastic changes, the visor of his helmet being closed. The mazes of the dance were ecstatic. Soft whispering came into her ear from under the radiant helmet, and she felt like a woman in Paradise. Suddenly these two wheeled out from the mass of dancers, dived into one of the pools of the heath, and came out somewhere into an iridescent hollow, arched with rainbows. “It must be here,” said the voice by her side, and blushingly looking up she saw him removing his casque to kiss her. At that moment there was a cracking noise, and his figure fell into fragments like a pack of cards.\n\nShe cried aloud. “O that I had seen his face!”\n\nEustacia awoke. The cracking had been that of the window shutter downstairs, which the maid-servant was opening to let in the day, now slowly increasing to Nature’s meagre allowance at this sickly time of the year. “O that I had seen his face!” she said again. “’Twas meant for Mr. Yeobright!”\n\nWhen she became cooler she perceived that many of the phases of the dream had naturally arisen out of the images and fancies of the day before. But this detracted little from its interest, which lay in the excellent fuel it provided for newly kindled fervour. She was at the modulating point between indifference and love, at the stage called “having a fancy for.” It occurs once in the history of the most gigantic passions, and it is a period when they are in the hands of the weakest will.\n\nThe perfervid woman was by this time half in love with a vision. The fantastic nature of her passion, which lowered her as an intellect, raised her as a soul. If she had had a little more self-control she would have attenuated the emotion to nothing by sheer reasoning, and so have killed it off. If she had had a little less pride she might have gone and circumambulated the Yeobrights’ premises at Blooms-End at any maidenly sacrifice until she had seen him. But Eustacia did neither of these things. She acted as the most exemplary might have acted, being so influenced; she took an airing twice or thrice a day upon the Egdon hills, and kept her eyes employed.\n\nThe first occasion passed, and he did not come that way.\n\nShe promenaded a second time, and was again the sole wanderer there.\n\nThe third time there was a dense fog; she looked around, but without much hope. Even if he had been walking within twenty yards of her she could not have seen him.\n\nAt the fourth attempt to encounter him it began to rain in torrents, and she turned back.\n\nThe fifth sally was in the afternoon; it was fine, and she remained out long, walking to the very top of the valley in which Blooms-End lay. She saw the white paling about half a mile off; but he did not appear. It was almost with heart-sickness that she came home and with a sense of shame at her weakness. She resolved to look for the man from Paris no more.\n\nBut Providence is nothing if not coquettish; and no sooner had Eustacia formed this resolve than the opportunity came which, while sought, had been entirely withholden.\n\nIV. Eustacia Is Led on to an Adventure\nIn the evening of this last day of expectation, which was the twenty-third of December, Eustacia was at home alone. She had passed the recent hour in lamenting over a rumour newly come to her ears—that Yeobright’s visit to his mother was to be of short duration, and would end some time the next week. “Naturally,” she said to herself. A man in the full swing of his activities in a gay city could not afford to linger long on Egdon Heath. That she would behold face to face the owner of the awakening voice within the limits of such a holiday was most unlikely, unless she were to haunt the environs of his mother’s house like a robin, to do which was difficult and unseemly.\n\nThe customary expedient of provincial girls and men in such circumstances is churchgoing. In an ordinary village or country town one can safely calculate that, either on Christmas day or the Sunday contiguous, any native home for the holidays, who has not through age or ennui lost the appetite for seeing and being seen, will turn up in some pew or other, shining with hope, self-consciousness, and new clothes. Thus the congregation on Christmas morning is mostly a Tussaud collection of celebrities who have been born in the neighbourhood. Hither the mistress, left neglected at home all the year, can steal and observe the development of the returned lover who has forgotten her, and think as she watches him over her prayer book that he may throb with a renewed fidelity when novelties have lost their charm. And hither a comparatively recent settler like Eustacia may betake herself to scrutinize the person of a native son who left home before her advent upon the scene, and consider if the friendship of his parents be worth cultivating during his next absence in order to secure a knowledge of him on his next return.\n\nBut these tender schemes were not feasible among the scattered inhabitants of Egdon Heath. In name they were parishioners, but virtually they belonged to no parish at all. People who came to these few isolated houses to keep Christmas with their friends remained in their friends’ chimney-corners drinking mead and other comforting liquors till they left again for good and all. Rain, snow, ice, mud everywhere around, they did not care to trudge two or three miles to sit wet-footed and splashed to the nape of their necks among those who, though in some measure neighbours, lived close to the church, and entered it clean and dry. Eustacia knew it was ten to one that Clym Yeobright would go to no church at all during his few days of leave, and that it would be a waste of labour for her to go driving the pony and gig over a bad road in hope to see him there.\n\nIt was dusk, and she was sitting by the fire in the dining-room or hall, which they occupied at this time of the year in preference to the parlour, because of its large hearth, constructed for turf-fires, a fuel the captain was partial to in the winter season. The only visible articles in the room were those on the window-sill, which showed their shapes against the low sky, the middle article being the old hourglass, and the other two a pair of ancient British urns which had been dug from a barrow near, and were used as flowerpots for two razor-leaved cactuses. Somebody knocked at the door. The servant was out; so was her grandfather. The person, after waiting a minute, came in and tapped at the door of the room.\n\n“Who’s there?” said Eustacia.\n\n“Please, Cap’n Vye, will you let us——”\n\nEustacia arose and went to the door. “I cannot allow you to come in so boldly. You should have waited.”\n\n“The cap’n said I might come in without any fuss,” was answered in a lad’s pleasant voice.\n\n“Oh, did he?” said Eustacia more gently. “What do you want, Charley?”\n\n“Please will your grandfather lend us his fuelhouse to try over our parts in, tonight at seven o’clock?”\n\n“What, are you one of the Egdon mummers for this year?”\n\n“Yes, miss. The cap’n used to let the old mummers practise here.”\n\n“I know it. Yes, you may use the fuelhouse if you like,” said Eustacia languidly.\n\nThe choice of Captain Vye’s fuelhouse as the scene of rehearsal was dictated by the fact that his dwelling was nearly in the centre of the heath. The fuelhouse was as roomy as a barn, and was a most desirable place for such a purpose. The lads who formed the company of players lived at different scattered points around, and by meeting in this spot the distances to be traversed by all the comers would be about equally proportioned.\n\nFor mummers and mumming Eustacia had the greatest contempt. The mummers themselves were not afflicted with any such feeling for their art, though at the same time they were not enthusiastic. A traditional pastime is to be distinguished from a mere revival in no more striking feature than in this, that while in the revival all is excitement and fervour, the survival is carried on with a stolidity and absence of stir which sets one wondering why a thing that is done so perfunctorily should be kept up at all. Like Balaam and other unwilling prophets, the agents seem moved by an inner compulsion to say and do their allotted parts whether they will or no. This unweeting manner of performance is the true ring by which, in this refurbishing age, a fossilized survival may be known from a spurious reproduction.\n\nThe piece was the well-known play of Saint George, and all who were behind the scenes assisted in the preparations, including the women of each household. Without the co-operation of sisters and sweethearts the dresses were likely to be a failure; but on the other hand, this class of assistance was not without its drawbacks. The girls could never be brought to respect tradition in designing and decorating the armour; they insisted on attaching loops and bows of silk and velvet in any situation pleasing to their taste. Gorget, gusset, basinet, cuirass, gauntlet, sleeve, all alike in the view of these feminine eyes were practicable spaces whereon to sew scraps of fluttering colour.\n\nIt might be that Joe, who fought on the side of Christendom, had a sweetheart, and that Jim, who fought on the side of the Moslem, had one likewise. During the making of the costumes it would come to the knowledge of Joe’s sweetheart that Jim’s was putting brilliant silk scallops at the bottom of her lover’s surcoat, in addition to the ribbons of the visor, the bars of which, being invariably formed of coloured strips about half an inch wide hanging before the face, were mostly of that material. Joe’s sweetheart straight-way placed brilliant silk on the scallops of the hem in question, and, going a little further, added ribbon tufts to the shoulder pieces. Jim’s, not to be outdone, would affix bows and rosettes everywhere.\n\nThe result was that in the end the Valiant Soldier, of the Christian army, was distinguished by no peculiarity of accoutrement from the Turkish Knight; and what was worse, on a casual view Saint George himself might be mistaken for his deadly enemy, the Saracen. The guisers themselves, though inwardly regretting this confusion of persons, could not afford to offend those by whose assistance they so largely profited, and the innovations were allowed to stand.\n\nThere was, it is true, a limit to this tendency to uniformity. The Leech or Doctor preserved his character intact—his darker habiliments, peculiar hat, and the bottle of physic slung under his arm, could never be mistaken. And the same might be said of the conventional figure of Father Christmas, with his gigantic club, an older man, who accompanied the band as general protector in long night journeys from parish to parish, and was bearer of the purse.\n\nSeven o’clock, the hour of the rehearsal, came round, and in a short time Eustacia could hear voices in the fuelhouse. To dissipate in some trifling measure her abiding sense of the murkiness of human life she went to the “linhay” or lean-to shed, which formed the root-store of their dwelling and abutted on the fuelhouse. Here was a small rough hole in the mud wall, originally made for pigeons, through which the interior of the next shed could be viewed. A light came from it now; and Eustacia stepped upon a stool to look in upon the scene.\n\nOn a ledge in the fuelhouse stood three tall rushlights and by the light of them seven or eight lads were marching about, haranguing, and confusing each other, in endeavours to perfect themselves in the play. Humphrey and Sam, the furze- and turf-cutters, were there looking on, so also was Timothy Fairway, who leant against the wall and prompted the boys from memory, interspersing among the set words remarks and anecdotes of the superior days when he and others were the Egdon mummers-elect that these lads were now.\n\n“Well, ye be as well up to it as ever ye will be,” he said. “Not that such mumming would have passed in our time. Harry as the Saracen should strut a bit more, and John needn’t holler his inside out. Beyond that perhaps you’ll do. Have you got all your clothes ready?”\n\n“We shall by Monday.”\n\n“Your first outing will be Monday night, I suppose?”\n\n“Yes. At Mrs. Yeobright’s.”\n\n“Oh, Mrs. Yeobright’s. What makes her want to see ye? I should think a middle-aged woman was tired of mumming.”\n\n“She’s got up a bit of a party, because ’tis the first Christmas that her son Clym has been home for a long time.”\n\n“To be sure, to be sure—her party! I am going myself. I almost forgot it, upon my life.”\n\nEustacia’s face flagged. There was to be a party at the Yeobrights’; she, naturally, had nothing to do with it. She was a stranger to all such local gatherings, and had always held them as scarcely appertaining to her sphere. But had she been going, what an opportunity would have been afforded her of seeing the man whose influence was penetrating her like summer sun! To increase that influence was coveted excitement; to cast it off might be to regain serenity; to leave it as it stood was tantalizing.\n\nThe lads and men prepared to leave the premises, and Eustacia returned to her fireside. She was immersed in thought, but not for long. In a few minutes the lad Charley, who had come to ask permission to use the place, returned with the key to the kitchen. Eustacia heard him, and opening the door into the passage said, “Charley, come here.”\n\nThe lad was surprised. He entered the front room not without blushing; for he, like many, had felt the power of this girl’s face and form.\n\nShe pointed to a seat by the fire, and entered the other side of the chimney-corner herself. It could be seen in her face that whatever motive she might have had in asking the youth indoors would soon appear.\n\n“Which part do you play, Charley—the Turkish Knight, do you not?” inquired the beauty, looking across the smoke of the fire to him on the other side.\n\n“Yes, miss, the Turkish Knight,” he replied diffidently.\n\n“Is yours a long part?”\n\n“Nine speeches, about.”\n\n“Can you repeat them to me? If so I should like to hear them.”\n\nThe lad smiled into the glowing turf and began—\n\n“Here come I, a Turkish Knight, Who learnt in Turkish land to fight,”\n\ncontinuing the discourse throughout the scenes to the concluding catastrophe of his fall by the hand of Saint George.\n\nEustacia had occasionally heard the part recited before. When the lad ended she began, precisely in the same words, and ranted on without hitch or divergence till she too reached the end. It was the same thing, yet how different. Like in form, it had the added softness and finish of a Raffaelle after Perugino, which, while faithfully reproducing the original subject, entirely distances the original art.\n\nCharley’s eyes rounded with surprise. “Well, you be a clever lady!” he said, in admiration. “I’ve been three weeks learning mine.”\n\n“I have heard it before,” she quietly observed. “Now, would you do anything to please me, Charley?”\n\n“I’d do a good deal, miss.”\n\n“Would you let me play your part for one night?”\n\n“Oh, miss! But your woman’s gown—you couldn’t.”\n\n“I can get boy’s clothes—at least all that would be wanted besides the mumming dress. What should I have to give you to lend me your things, to let me take your place for an hour or two on Monday night, and on no account to say a word about who or what I am? You would, of course, have to excuse yourself from playing that night, and to say that somebody—a cousin of Miss Vye’s—would act for you. The other mummers have never spoken to me in their lives so that it would be safe enough; and if it were not, I should not mind. Now, what must I give you to agree to this? Half a crown?”\n\nThe youth shook his head\n\n“Five shillings?”\n\nHe shook his head again. “Money won’t do it,” he said, brushing the iron head of the firedog with the hollow of his hand.\n\n“What will, then, Charley?” said Eustacia in a disappointed tone.\n\n“You know what you forbade me at the Maypoling, miss,” murmured the lad, without looking at her, and still stroking the firedog’s head.\n\n“Yes,” said Eustacia, with a little more hauteur. “You wanted to join hands with me in the ring, if I recollect?”\n\n“Half an hour of that, and I’ll agree, miss.”\n\nEustacia regarded the youth steadfastly. He was three years younger than herself, but apparently not backward for his age. “Half an hour of what?” she said, though she guessed what.\n\n“Holding your hand in mine.”\n\nShe was silent. “Make it a quarter of an hour,” she said\n\n“Yes, Miss Eustacia—I will, if I may kiss it too. A quarter of an hour. And I’ll swear to do the best I can to let you take my place without anybody knowing. Don’t you think somebody might know your tongue, miss?”\n\n“It is possible. But I will put a pebble in my mouth to make is less likely. Very well; you shall be allowed to have my hand as soon as you bring the dress and your sword and staff. I don’t want you any longer now.”\n\nCharley departed, and Eustacia felt more and more interest in life. Here was something to do: here was some one to see, and a charmingly adventurous way to see him. “Ah,” she said to herself, “want of an object to live for—that’s all is the matter with me!”\n\nEustacia’s manner was as a rule of a slumberous sort, her passions being of the massive rather than the vivacious kind. But when aroused she would make a dash which, just for the time, was not unlike the move of a naturally lively person.\n\nOn the question of recognition she was somewhat indifferent. By the acting lads themselves she was not likely to be known. With the guests who might be assembled she was hardly so secure. Yet detection, after all, would be no such dreadful thing. The fact only could be detected, her true motive never. It would be instantly set down as the passing freak of a girl whose ways were already considered singular. That she was doing for an earnest reason what would most naturally be done in jest was at any rate a safe secret.\n\nThe next evening Eustacia stood punctually at the fuelhouse door, waiting for the dusk which was to bring Charley with the trappings. Her grandfather was at home tonight, and she would be unable to ask her confederate indoors.\n\nHe appeared on the dark ridge of heathland, like a fly on a Negro, bearing the articles with him, and came up breathless with his walk.\n\n“Here are the things,” he whispered, placing them upon the threshold. “And now, Miss Eustacia—”\n\n“The payment. It is quite ready. I am as good as my word.”\n\nShe leant against the door-post, and gave him her hand. Charley took it in both his own with a tenderness beyond description, unless it was like that of a child holding a captured sparrow.\n\n“Why, there’s a glove on it!” he said in a deprecating way.\n\n“I have been walking,” she observed.\n\n“But, miss!”\n\n“Well—it is hardly fair.” She pulled off the glove, and gave him her bare hand.\n\nThey stood together minute after minute, without further speech, each looking at the blackening scene, and each thinking his and her own thoughts.\n\n“I think I won’t use it all up tonight,” said Charley devotedly, when six or eight minutes had been passed by him caressing her hand. “May I have the other few minutes another time?”\n\n“As you like,” said she without the least emotion. “But it must be over in a week. Now, there is only one thing I want you to do—to wait while I put on the dress, and then to see if I do my part properly. But let me look first indoors.”\n\nShe vanished for a minute or two, and went in. Her grandfather was safely asleep in his chair. “Now, then,” she said, on returning, “walk down the garden a little way, and when I am ready I’ll call you.”\n\nCharley walked and waited, and presently heard a soft whistle. He returned to the fuelhouse door.\n\n“Did you whistle, Miss Vye?”\n\n“Yes; come in,” reached him in Eustacia’s voice from a back quarter. “I must not strike a light till the door is shut, or it may be seen shining. Push your hat into the hole through to the wash-house, if you can feel your way across.”\n\nCharley did as commanded, and she struck the light revealing herself to be changed in sex, brilliant in colours, and armed from top to toe. Perhaps she quailed a little under Charley’s vigorous gaze, but whether any shyness at her male attire appeared upon her countenance could not be seen by reason of the strips of ribbon which used to cover the face in mumming costumes, representing the barred visor of the mediæval helmet.\n\n“It fits pretty well,” she said, looking down at the white overalls, “except that the tunic, or whatever you call it, is long in the sleeve. The bottom of the overalls I can turn up inside. Now pay attention.”\n\nEustacia then proceeded in her delivery, striking the sword against the staff or lance at the minatory phrases, in the orthodox mumming manner, and strutting up and down. Charley seasoned his admiration with criticism of the gentlest kind, for the touch of Eustacia’s hand yet remained with him.\n\n“And now for your excuse to the others,” she said. “Where do you meet before you go to Mrs. Yeobright’s?”\n\n“We thought of meeting here, miss, if you have nothing to say against it. At eight o’clock, so as to get there by nine.”\n\n“Yes. Well, you of course must not appear. I will march in about five minutes late, ready-dressed, and tell them that you can’t come. I have decided that the best plan will be for you to be sent somewhere by me, to make a real thing of the excuse. Our two heath-croppers are in the habit of straying into the meads, and tomorrow evening you can go and see if they are gone there. I’ll manage the rest. Now you may leave me.”\n\n“Yes, miss. But I think I’ll have one minute more of what I am owed, if you don’t mind.”\n\nEustacia gave him her hand as before.\n\n“One minute,” she said, and counted on till she reached seven or eight minutes. Hand and person she then withdrew to a distance of several feet, and recovered some of her old dignity. The contract completed, she raised between them a barrier impenetrable as a wall.\n\n“There, ’tis all gone; and I didn’t mean quite all,” he said, with a sigh.\n\n“You had good measure,” said she, turning away.\n\n“Yes, miss. Well, ’tis over, and now I’ll get home-along.”\n\nV. Through the Moonlight\nThe next evening the mummers were assembled in the same spot, awaiting the entrance of the Turkish Knight.\n\n“Twenty minutes after eight by the Quiet Woman, and Charley not come.”\n\n“Ten minutes past by Blooms-End.”\n\n“It wants ten minutes to, by Grandfer Cantle’s watch.”\n\n“And ’tis five minutes past by the captain’s clock.”\n\nOn Egdon there was no absolute hour of the day. The time at any moment was a number of varying doctrines professed by the different hamlets, some of them having originally grown up from a common root, and then become divided by secession, some having been alien from the beginning. West Egdon believed in Blooms-End time, East Egdon in the time of the Quiet Woman Inn. Grandfer Cantle’s watch had numbered many followers in years gone by, but since he had grown older faiths were shaken. Thus, the mummers having gathered hither from scattered points each came with his own tenets on early and late; and they waited a little longer as a compromise.\n\nEustacia had watched the assemblage through the hole; and seeing that now was the proper moment to enter, she went from the “linhay” and boldly pulled the bobbin of the fuelhouse door. Her grandfather was safe at the Quiet Woman.\n\n“Here’s Charley at last! How late you be, Charley.”\n\n“’Tis not Charley,” said the Turkish Knight from within his visor. “’Tis a cousin of Miss Vye’s, come to take Charley’s place from curiosity. He was obliged to go and look for the heath-croppers that have got into the meads, and I agreed to take his place, as he knew he couldn’t come back here again tonight. I know the part as well as he.”\n\nHer graceful gait, elegant figure, and dignified manner in general won the mummers to the opinion that they had gained by the exchange, if the newcomer were perfect in his part.\n\n“It don’t matter—if you be not too young,” said Saint George. Eustacia’s voice had sounded somewhat more juvenile and fluty than Charley’s.\n\n“I know every word of it, I tell you,” said Eustacia decisively. Dash being all that was required to carry her triumphantly through, she adopted as much as was necessary. “Go ahead, lads, with the try-over. I’ll challenge any of you to find a mistake in me.”\n\nThe play was hastily rehearsed, whereupon the other mummers were delighted with the new knight. They extinguished the candles at half-past eight, and set out upon the heath in the direction of Mrs. Yeobright’s house at Bloom’s-End.\n\nThere was a slight hoarfrost that night, and the moon, though not more than half full, threw a spirited and enticing brightness upon the fantastic figures of the mumming band, whose plumes and ribbons rustled in their walk like autumn leaves. Their path was not over Rainbarrow now, but down a valley which left that ancient elevation a little to the east. The bottom of the vale was green to a width of ten yards or thereabouts, and the shining facets of frost upon the blades of grass seemed to move on with the shadows of those they surrounded. The masses of furze and heath to the right and left were dark as ever; a mere half-moon was powerless to silver such sable features as theirs.\n\nHalf-an-hour of walking and talking brought them to the spot in the valley where the grass riband widened and led down to the front of the house. At sight of the place Eustacia who had felt a few passing doubts during her walk with the youths, again was glad that the adventure had been undertaken. She had come out to see a man who might possibly have the power to deliver her soul from a most deadly oppression. What was Wildeve? Interesting, but inadequate. Perhaps she would see a sufficient hero tonight.\n\nAs they drew nearer to the front of the house the mummers became aware that music and dancing were briskly flourishing within. Every now and then a long low note from the serpent, which was the chief wind instrument played at these times, advanced further into the heath than the thin treble part, and reached their ears alone; and next a more than usual loud tread from a dancer would come the same way. With nearer approach these fragmentary sounds became pieced together, and were found to be the salient points of the tune called “Nancy’s Fancy.”\n\nHe was there, of course. Who was she that he danced with? Perhaps some unknown woman, far beneath herself in culture, was by the most subtle of lures sealing his fate this very instant. To dance with a man is to concentrate a twelvemonth’s regulation fire upon him in the fragment of an hour. To pass to courtship without acquaintance, to pass to marriage without courtship, is a skipping of terms reserved for those alone who tread this royal road. She would see how his heart lay by keen observation of them all.\n\nThe enterprising lady followed the mumming company through the gate in the white paling, and stood before the open porch. The house was encrusted with heavy thatchings, which dropped between the upper windows; the front, upon which the moonbeams directly played, had originally been white; but a huge pyracanth now darkened the greater portion.\n\nIt became at once evident that the dance was proceeding immediately within the surface of the door, no apartment intervening. The brushing of skirts and elbows, sometimes the bumping of shoulders, could be heard against the very panels. Eustacia, though living within two miles of the place, had never seen the interior of this quaint old habitation. Between Captain Vye and the Yeobrights there had never existed much acquaintance, the former having come as a stranger and purchased the long-empty house at Mistover Knap not long before the death of Mrs. Yeobright’s husband; and with that event and the departure of her son such friendship as had grown up became quite broken off.\n\n“Is there no passage inside the door, then?” asked Eustacia as they stood within the porch.\n\n“No,” said the lad who played the Saracen. “The door opens right upon the front sitting-room, where the spree’s going on.”\n\n“So that we cannot open the door without stopping the dance.”\n\n“That’s it. Here we must bide till they have done, for they always bolt the back door after dark.”\n\n“They won’t be much longer,” said Father Christmas.\n\nThis assertion, however, was hardly borne out by the event. Again the instruments ended the tune; again they recommenced with as much fire and pathos as if it were the first strain. The air was now that one without any particular beginning, middle, or end, which perhaps, among all the dances which throng an inspired fiddler’s fancy, best conveys the idea of the interminable—the celebrated “Devil’s Dream.” The fury of personal movement that was kindled by the fury of the notes could be approximately imagined by these outsiders under the moon, from the occasional kicks of toes and heels against the door, whenever the whirl round had been of more than customary velocity.\n\nThe first five minutes of listening was interesting enough to the mummers. The five minutes extended to ten minutes, and these to a quarter of an hour; but no signs of ceasing were audible in the lively “Dream.” The bumping against the door, the laughter, the stamping, were all as vigorous as ever, and the pleasure in being outside lessened considerably.\n\n“Why does Mrs. Yeobright give parties of this sort?” Eustacia asked, a little surprised to hear merriment so pronounced.\n\n“It is not one of her bettermost parlour-parties. She’s asked the plain neighbours and workpeople without drawing any lines, just to give ’em a good supper and such like. Her son and she wait upon the folks.”\n\n“I see,” said Eustacia.\n\n“’Tis the last strain, I think,” said Saint George, with his ear to the panel. “A young man and woman have just swung into this corner, and he’s saying to her, ‘Ah, the pity; ’tis over for us this time, my own.’”\n\n“Thank God,” said the Turkish Knight, stamping, and taking from the wall the conventional lance that each of the mummers carried. Her boots being thinner than those of the young men, the hoar had damped her feet and made them cold.\n\n“Upon my song ’tis another ten minutes for us,” said the Valiant Soldier, looking through the keyhole as the tune modulated into another without stopping. “Grandfer Cantle is standing in this corner, waiting his turn.”\n\n“’Twon’t be long; ’tis a six-handed reel,” said the Doctor.\n\n“Why not go in, dancing or no? They sent for us,” said the Saracen.\n\n“Certainly not,” said Eustacia authoritatively, as she paced smartly up and down from door to gate to warm herself. “We should burst into the middle of them and stop the dance, and that would be unmannerly.”\n\n“He thinks himself somebody because he has had a bit more schooling than we,” said the Doctor.\n\n“You may go to the deuce!” said Eustacia.\n\nThere was a whispered conversation between three or four of them, and one turned to her.\n\n“Will you tell us one thing?” he said, not without gentleness. “Be you Miss Vye? We think you must be.”\n\n“You may think what you like,” said Eustacia slowly. “But honourable lads will not tell tales upon a lady.”\n\n“We’ll say nothing, miss. That’s upon our honour.”\n\n“Thank you,” she replied.\n\nAt this moment the fiddles finished off with a screech, and the serpent emitted a last note that nearly lifted the roof. When, from the comparative quiet within, the mummers judged that the dancers had taken their seats, Father Christmas advanced, lifted the latch, and put his head inside the door.\n\n“Ah, the mummers, the mummers!” cried several guests at once. “Clear a space for the mummers.”\n\nHumpbacked Father Christmas then made a complete entry, swinging his huge club, and in a general way clearing the stage for the actors proper, while he informed the company in smart verse that he was come, welcome or welcome not; concluding his speech with\n\n“Make room, make room, my gallant boys, And give us space to rhyme; We’ve come to show Saint George’s play, Upon this Christmas time.”\n\nThe guests were now arranging themselves at one end of the room, the fiddler was mending a string, the serpent-player was emptying his mouthpiece, and the play began. First of those outside the Valiant Soldier entered, in the interest of Saint George—\n\n“Here come I, the Valiant Soldier; Slasher is my name”;\n\nand so on. This speech concluded with a challenge to the infidel, at the end of which it was Eustacia’s duty to enter as the Turkish Knight. She, with the rest who were not yet on, had hitherto remained in the moonlight which streamed under the porch. With no apparent effort or backwardness she came in, beginning—\n\n“Here come I, a Turkish Knight, Who learnt in Turkish land to fight; I’ll fight this man with courage bold: If his blood’s hot I’ll make it cold!”\n\nDuring her declamation Eustacia held her head erect, and spoke as roughly as she could, feeling pretty secure from observation. But the concentration upon her part necessary to prevent discovery, the newness of the scene, the shine of the candles, and the confusing effect upon her vision of the ribboned visor which hid her features, left her absolutely unable to perceive who were present as spectators. On the further side of a table bearing candles she could faintly discern faces, and that was all.\n\nMeanwhile Jim Starks as the Valiant Soldier had come forward, and, with a glare upon the Turk, replied—\n\n“If, then, thou art that Turkish Knight, Draw out thy sword, and let us fight!”\n\nAnd fight they did; the issue of the combat being that the Valiant Soldier was slain by a preternaturally inadequate thrust from Eustacia, Jim, in his ardour for genuine histrionic art, coming down like a log upon the stone floor with force enough to dislocate his shoulder. Then, after more words from the Turkish Knight, rather too faintly delivered, and statements that he’d fight Saint George and all his crew, Saint George himself magnificently entered with the well-known flourish—\n\n“Here come I, Saint George, the valiant man, With naked sword and spear in hand, Who fought the dragon and brought him to the slaughter, And by this won fair Sabra, the King of Egypt’s daughter; What mortal man would dare to stand Before me with my sword in hand?”\n\nThis was the lad who had first recognized Eustacia; and when she now, as the Turk, replied with suitable defiance, and at once began the combat, the young fellow took especial care to use his sword as gently as possible. Being wounded, the Knight fell upon one knee, according to the direction. The Doctor now entered, restored the Knight by giving him a draught from the bottle which he carried, and the fight was again resumed, the Turk sinking by degrees until quite overcome—dying as hard in this venerable drama as he is said to do at the present day.\n\nThis gradual sinking to the earth was, in fact, one reason why Eustacia had thought that the part of the Turkish Knight, though not the shortest, would suit her best. A direct fall from upright to horizontal, which was the end of the other fighting characters, was not an elegant or decorous part for a girl. But it was easy to die like a Turk, by a dogged decline.\n\nEustacia was now among the number of the slain, though not on the floor, for she had managed to sink into a sloping position against the clock-case, so that her head was well elevated. The play proceeded between Saint George, the Saracen, the Doctor, and Father Christmas; and Eustacia, having no more to do, for the first time found leisure to observe the scene round, and to search for the form that had drawn her hither.\n\nVI. The Two Stand Face to Face\nThe room had been arranged with a view to the dancing, the large oak table having been moved back till it stood as a breastwork to the fireplace. At each end, behind, and in the chimney-corner were grouped the guests, many of them being warm-faced and panting, among whom Eustacia cursorily recognized some well-to-do persons from beyond the heath. Thomasin, as she had expected, was not visible, and Eustacia recollected that a light had shone from an upper window when they were outside—the window, probably, of Thomasin’s room. A nose, chin, hands, knees, and toes projected from the seat within the chimney opening, which members she found to unite in the person of Grandfer Cantle, Mrs. Yeobright’s occasional assistant in the garden, and therefore one of the invited. The smoke went up from an Etna of peat in front of him, played round the notches of the chimney-crook, struck against the salt-box, and got lost among the flitches.\n\nAnother part of the room soon riveted her gaze. At the other side of the chimney stood the settle, which is the necessary supplement to a fire so open that nothing less than a strong breeze will carry up the smoke. It is, to the hearths of old-fashioned cavernous fireplaces, what the east belt of trees is to the exposed country estate, or the north wall to the garden. Outside the settle candles gutter, locks of hair wave, young women shiver, and old men sneeze. Inside is Paradise. Not a symptom of a draught disturbs the air; the sitters’ backs are as warm as their faces, and songs and old tales are drawn from the occupants by the comfortable heat, like fruit from melon plants in a frame.\n\nIt was, however, not with those who sat in the settle that Eustacia was concerned. A face showed itself with marked distinctness against the dark-tanned wood of the upper part. The owner, who was leaning against the settle’s outer end, was Clement Yeobright, or Clym, as he was called here; she knew it could be nobody else. The spectacle constituted an area of two feet in Rembrandt’s intensest manner. A strange power in the lounger’s appearance lay in the fact that, though his whole figure was visible, the observer’s eye was only aware of his face.\n\nTo one of middle age the countenance was that of a young man, though a youth might hardly have seen any necessity for the term of immaturity. But it was really one of those faces which convey less the idea of so many years as its age than of so much experience as its store. The number of their years may have adequately summed up Jared, Mahalaleel, and the rest of the antediluvians, but the age of a modern man is to be measured by the intensity of his history.\n\nThe face was well shaped, even excellently. But the mind within was beginning to use it as a mere waste tablet whereon to trace its idiosyncrasies as they developed themselves. The beauty here visible would in no long time be ruthlessly over-run by its parasite, thought, which might just as well have fed upon a plainer exterior where there was nothing it could harm. Had Heaven preserved Yeobright from a wearing habit of meditation, people would have said, “A handsome man.” Had his brain unfolded under sharper contours they would have said, “A thoughtful man.” But an inner strenuousness was preying upon an outer symmetry, and they rated his look as singular.\n\nHence people who began by beholding him ended by perusing him. His countenance was overlaid with legible meanings. Without being thought-worn he yet had certain marks derived from a perception of his surroundings, such as are not unfrequently found on men at the end of the four or five years of endeavour which follow the close of placid pupilage. He already showed that thought is a disease of flesh, and indirectly bore evidence that ideal physical beauty is incompatible with emotional development and a full recognition of the coil of things. Mental luminousness must be fed with the oil of life, even though there is already a physical need for it; and the pitiful sight of two demands on one supply was just showing itself here.\n\nWhen standing before certain men the philosopher regrets that thinkers are but perishable tissue, the artist that perishable tissue has to think. Thus to deplore, each from his point of view, the mutually destructive interdependence of spirit and flesh would have been instinctive with these in critically observing Yeobright.\n\nAs for his look, it was a natural cheerfulness striving against depression from without, and not quite succeeding. The look suggested isolation, but it revealed something more. As is usual with bright natures, the deity that lies ignominiously chained within an ephemeral human carcase shone out of him like a ray.\n\nThe effect upon Eustacia was palpable. The extraordinary pitch of excitement that she had reached beforehand would, indeed, have caused her to be influenced by the most commonplace man. She was troubled at Yeobright’s presence.\n\nThe remainder of the play ended—the Saracen’s head was cut off, and Saint George stood as victor. Nobody commented, any more than they would have commented on the fact of mushrooms coming in autumn or snowdrops in spring. They took the piece as phlegmatically as did the actors themselves. It was a phase of cheerfulness which was, as a matter of course, to be passed through every Christmas; and there was no more to be said.\n\nThey sang the plaintive chant which follows the play, during which all the dead men rise to their feet in a silent and awful manner, like the ghosts of Napoleon’s soldiers in the Midnight Review. Afterwards the door opened, and Fairway appeared on the threshold, accompanied by Christian and another. They had been waiting outside for the conclusion of the play, as the players had waited for the conclusion of the dance.\n\n“Come in, come in,” said Mrs. Yeobright; and Clym went forward to welcome them. “How is it you are so late? Grandfer Cantle has been here ever so long, and we thought you’d have come with him, as you live so near one another.”\n\n“Well, I should have come earlier,” Mr. Fairway said and paused to look along the beam of the ceiling for a nail to hang his hat on; but, finding his accustomed one to be occupied by the mistletoe, and all the nails in the walls to be burdened with bunches of holly, he at last relieved himself of the hat by ticklishly balancing it between the candle-box and the head of the clock-case. “I should have come earlier, ma’am,” he resumed, with a more composed air, “but I know what parties be, and how there’s none too much room in folks’ houses at such times, so I thought I wouldn’t come till you’d got settled a bit.”\n\n“And I thought so too, Mrs. Yeobright,” said Christian earnestly, “but Father there was so eager that he had no manners at all, and left home almost afore ’twas dark. I told him ’twas barely decent in a’ old man to come so oversoon; but words be wind.”\n\n“Klk! I wasn’t going to bide waiting about, till half the game was over! I’m as light as a kite when anything’s going on!” crowed Grandfer Cantle from the chimneyseat.\n\nFairway had meanwhile concluded a critical gaze at Yeobright. “Now, you may not believe it,” he said to the rest of the room, “but I should never have knowed this gentleman if I had met him anywhere off his own he’th—he’s altered so much.”\n\n“You too have altered, and for the better, I think Timothy,” said Yeobright, surveying the firm figure of Fairway.\n\n“Master Yeobright, look me over too. I have altered for the better, haven’t I, hey?” said Grandfer Cantle, rising and placing himself something above half a foot from Clym’s eye, to induce the most searching criticism.\n\n“To be sure we will,” said Fairway, taking the candle and moving it over the surface of the Grandfer’s countenance, the subject of his scrutiny irradiating himself with light and pleasant smiles, and giving himself jerks of juvenility.\n\n“You haven’t changed much,” said Yeobright.\n\n“If there’s any difference, Grandfer is younger,” appended Fairway decisively.\n\n“And yet not my own doing, and I feel no pride in it,” said the pleased ancient. “But I can’t be cured of my vagaries; them I plead guilty to. Yes, Master Cantle always was that, as we know. But I am nothing by the side of you, Mister Clym.”\n\n“Nor any o’ us,” said Humphrey, in a low rich tone of admiration, not intended to reach anybody’s ears.\n\n“Really, there would have been nobody here who could have stood as decent second to him, or even third, if I hadn’t been a soldier in the Bang-up Locals (as we was called for our smartness),” said Grandfer Cantle. “And even as ’tis we all look a little scammish beside him. But in the year four ’twas said there wasn’t a finer figure in the whole South Wessex than I, as I looked when dashing past the shop-winders with the rest of our company on the day we ran out o’ Budmouth because it was thoughted that Boney had landed round the point. There was I, straight as a young poplar, wi’ my firelock, and my bagnet, and my spatterdashes, and my stock sawing my jaws off, and my accoutrements sheening like the seven stars! Yes, neighbours, I was a pretty sight in my soldiering days. You ought to have seen me in four!”\n\n“’Tis his mother’s side where Master Clym’s figure comes from, bless ye,” said Timothy. “I know’d her brothers well. Longer coffins were never made in the whole country of South Wessex, and ’tis said that poor George’s knees were crumpled up a little e’en as ’twas.”\n\n“Coffins, where?” inquired Christian, drawing nearer. “Have the ghost of one appeared to anybody, Master Fairway?”\n\n“No, no. Don’t let your mind so mislead your ears, Christian; and be a man,” said Timothy reproachfully.\n\n“I will.” said Christian. “But now I think o’t my shadder last night seemed just the shape of a coffin. What is it a sign of when your shade’s like a coffin, neighbours? It can’t be nothing to be afeared of, I suppose?”\n\n“Afeared, no!” said the Grandfer. “Faith, I was never afeard of nothing except Boney, or I shouldn’t ha’ been the soldier I was. Yes, ’tis a thousand pities you didn’t see me in four!”\n\nBy this time the mummers were preparing to leave; but Mrs. Yeobright stopped them by asking them to sit down and have a little supper. To this invitation Father Christmas, in the name of them all, readily agreed.\n\nEustacia was happy in the opportunity of staying a little longer. The cold and frosty night without was doubly frigid to her. But the lingering was not without its difficulties. Mrs. Yeobright, for want of room in the larger apartment, placed a bench for the mummers halfway through the pantry door, which opened from the sitting-room. Here they seated themselves in a row, the door being left open—thus they were still virtually in the same apartment. Mrs. Yeobright now murmured a few words to her son, who crossed the room to the pantry door, striking his head against the mistletoe as he passed, and brought the mummers beef and bread, cake pastry, mead, and elder-wine, the waiting being done by him and his mother, that the little maid-servant might sit as guest. The mummers doffed their helmets, and began to eat and drink.\n\n“But you will surely have some?” said Clym to the Turkish Knight, as he stood before that warrior, tray in hand. She had refused, and still sat covered, only the sparkle of her eyes being visible between the ribbons which covered her face.\n\n“None, thank you,” replied Eustacia.\n\n“He’s quite a youngster,” said the Saracen apologetically, “and you must excuse him. He’s not one of the old set, but have jined us because t’other couldn’t come.”\n\n“But he will take something?” persisted Yeobright. “Try a glass of mead or elder-wine.”\n\n“Yes, you had better try that,” said the Saracen. “It will keep the cold out going home-along.”\n\nThough Eustacia could not eat without uncovering her face she could drink easily enough beneath her disguise. The elder-wine was accordingly accepted, and the glass vanished inside the ribbons.\n\nAt moments during this performance Eustacia was half in doubt about the security of her position; yet it had a fearful joy. A series of attentions paid to her, and yet not to her but to some imaginary person, by the first man she had ever been inclined to adore, complicated her emotions indescribably. She had loved him partly because he was exceptional in this scene, partly because she had determined to love him, chiefly because she was in desperate need of loving somebody after wearying of Wildeve. Believing that she must love him in spite of herself, she had been influenced after the fashion of the second Lord Lyttleton and other persons, who have dreamed that they were to die on a certain day, and by stress of a morbid imagination have actually brought about that event. Once let a maiden admit the possibility of her being stricken with love for someone at a certain hour and place, and the thing is as good as done.\n\nDid anything at this moment suggest to Yeobright the sex of the creature whom that fantastic guise inclosed, how extended was her scope both in feeling and in making others feel, and how far her compass transcended that of her companions in the band? When the disguised Queen of Love appeared before Æneas a preternatural perfume accompanied her presence and betrayed her quality. If such a mysterious emanation ever was projected by the emotions of an earthly woman upon their object, it must have signified Eustacia’s presence to Yeobright now. He looked at her wistfully, then seemed to fall into a reverie, as if he were forgetting what he observed. The momentary situation ended, he passed on, and Eustacia sipped her wine without knowing what she drank. The man for whom she had pre-determined to nourish a passion went into the small room, and across it to the further extremity.\n\nThe mummers, as has been stated, were seated on a bench, one end of which extended into the small apartment, or pantry, for want of space in the outer room. Eustacia, partly from shyness, had chosen the midmost seat, which thus commanded a view of the interior of the pantry as well as the room containing the guests. When Clym passed down the pantry her eyes followed him in the gloom which prevailed there. At the remote end was a door which, just as he was about to open it for himself, was opened by somebody within; and light streamed forth.\n\nThe person was Thomasin, with a candle, looking anxious, pale, and interesting. Yeobright appeared glad to see her, and pressed her hand. “That’s right, Tamsie,” he said heartily, as though recalled to himself by the sight of her, “you have decided to come down. I am glad of it.”\n\n“Hush—no, no,” she said quickly. “I only came to speak to you.”\n\n“But why not join us?”\n\n“I cannot. At least I would rather not. I am not well enough, and we shall have plenty of time together now you are going to be home a good long holiday.”\n\n“It isn’t nearly so pleasant without you. Are you really ill?”\n\n“Just a little, my old cousin—here,” she said, playfully sweeping her hand across her heart.\n\n“Ah, Mother should have asked somebody else to be present tonight, perhaps?”\n\n“O no, indeed. I merely stepped down, Clym, to ask you—” Here he followed her through the doorway into the private room beyond, and, the door closing, Eustacia and the mummer who sat next to her, the only other witness of the performance, saw and heard no more.\n\nThe heat flew to Eustacia’s head and cheeks. She instantly guessed that Clym, having been home only these two or three days, had not as yet been made acquainted with Thomasin’s painful situation with regard to Wildeve; and seeing her living there just as she had been living before he left home, he naturally suspected nothing. Eustacia felt a wild jealousy of Thomasin on the instant. Though Thomasin might possibly have tender sentiments towards another man as yet, how long could they be expected to last when she was shut up here with this interesting and travelled cousin of hers? There was no knowing what affection might not soon break out between the two, so constantly in each other’s society, and not a distracting object near. Clym’s boyish love for her might have languished, but it might easily be revived again.\n\nEustacia was nettled by her own contrivances. What a sheer waste of herself to be dressed thus while another was shining to advantage! Had she known the full effect of the encounter she would have moved heaven and earth to get here in a natural manner. The power of her face all lost, the charm of her emotions all disguised, the fascinations of her coquetry denied existence, nothing but a voice left to her; she had a sense of the doom of Echo. “Nobody here respects me,” she said. She had overlooked the fact that, in coming as a boy among other boys, she would be treated as a boy. The slight, though of her own causing, and self-explanatory, she was unable to dismiss as unwittingly shown, so sensitive had the situation made her.\n\nWomen have done much for themselves in histrionic dress. To look far below those who, like a certain fair personator of Polly Peachum early in the last century, and another of Lydia Languish early in this,[1] have won not only love but ducal coronets into the bargain, whole shoals of them have reached to the initial satisfaction of getting love almost whence they would. But the Turkish Knight was denied even the chance of achieving this by the fluttering ribbons which she dared not brush aside.\n\n[1] Written in 1877.\n\nYeobright returned to the room without his cousin. When within two or three feet of Eustacia he stopped, as if again arrested by a thought. He was gazing at her. She looked another way, disconcerted, and wondered how long this purgatory was to last. After lingering a few seconds he passed on again.\n\nTo court their own discomfiture by love is a common instinct with certain perfervid women. Conflicting sensations of love, fear, and shame reduced Eustacia to a state of the utmost uneasiness. To escape was her great and immediate desire. The other mummers appeared to be in no hurry to leave; and murmuring to the lad who sat next to her that she preferred waiting for them outside the house, she moved to the door as imperceptibly as possible, opened it, and slipped out.\n\nThe calm, lone scene reassured her. She went forward to the palings and leant over them, looking at the moon. She had stood thus but a little time when the door again opened. Expecting to see the remainder of the band Eustacia turned; but no—Clym Yeobright came out as softly as she had done, and closed the door behind him.\n\nHe advanced and stood beside her. “I have an odd opinion,” he said, “and should like to ask you a question. Are you a woman—or am I wrong?”\n\n“I am a woman.”\n\nHis eyes lingered on her with great interest. “Do girls often play as mummers now? They never used to.”\n\n“They don’t now.”\n\n“Why did you?”\n\n“To get excitement and shake off depression,” she said in low tones.\n\n“What depressed you?”\n“Life.”\n\n“That’s a cause of depression a good many have to put up with.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nA long silence. “And do you find excitement?” asked Clym at last.\n\n“At this moment, perhaps.”\n\n“Then you are vexed at being discovered?”\n\n“Yes; though I thought I might be.”\n\n“I would gladly have asked you to our party had I known you wished to come. Have I ever been acquainted with you in my youth?”\n\n“Never.”\n\n“Won’t you come in again, and stay as long as you like?”\n\n“No. I wish not to be further recognized.”\n\n“Well, you are safe with me.” After remaining in thought a minute he added gently, “I will not intrude upon you longer. It is a strange way of meeting, and I will not ask why I find a cultivated woman playing such a part as this.”\n\nShe did not volunteer the reason which he seemed to hope for, and he wished her good night, going thence round to the back of the house, where he walked up and down by himself for some time before re-entering.\n\nEustacia, warmed with an inner fire, could not wait for her companions after this. She flung back the ribbons from her face, opened the gate, and at once struck into the heath. She did not hasten along. Her grandfather was in bed at this hour, for she so frequently walked upon the hills on moonlight nights that he took no notice of her comings and goings, and, enjoying himself in his own way, left her to do likewise. A more important subject than that of getting indoors now engrossed her. Yeobright, if he had the least curiosity, would infallibly discover her name. What then? She first felt a sort of exultation at the way in which the adventure had terminated, even though at moments between her exultations she was abashed and blushful. Then this consideration recurred to chill her: What was the use of her exploit? She was at present a total stranger to the Yeobright family. The unreasonable nimbus of romance with which she had encircled that man might be her misery. How could she allow herself to become so infatuated with a stranger? And to fill the cup of her sorrow there would be Thomasin, living day after day in inflammable proximity to him; for she had just learnt that, contrary to her first belief, he was going to stay at home some considerable time.\n\nShe reached the wicket at Mistover Knap, but before opening it she turned and faced the heath once more. The form of Rainbarrow stood above the hills, and the moon stood above Rainbarrow. The air was charged with silence and frost. The scene reminded Eustacia of a circumstance which till that moment she had totally forgotten. She had promised to meet Wildeve by the Barrow this very night at eight, to give a final answer to his pleading for an elopement.\n\nShe herself had fixed the evening and the hour. He had probably come to the spot, waited there in the cold, and been greatly disappointed.\n“Well, so much the better—it did not hurt him,” she said serenely. Wildeve had at present the rayless outline of the sun through smoked glass, and she could say such things as that with the greatest facility.\n\nShe remained deeply pondering; and Thomasin’s winning manner towards her cousin arose again upon Eustacia’s mind.\n\n“O that she had been married to Damon before this!” she said. “And she would if it hadn’t been for me! If I had only known—if I had only known!”\n\nEustacia once more lifted her deep stormy eyes to the moonlight, and, sighing that tragic sigh of hers which was so much like a shudder, entered the shadow of the roof. She threw off her trappings in the outhouse, rolled them up, and went indoors to her chamber.\nVII. A Coalition between Beauty and Oddness\n\nThe old captain’s prevailing indifference to his granddaughter’s movements left her free as a bird to follow her own courses; but it so happened that he did take upon himself the next morning to ask her why she had walked out so late.\n\n“Only in search of events, Grandfather,” she said, looking out of the window with that drowsy latency of manner which discovered so much force behind it whenever the trigger was pressed.\n\n“Search of events—one would think you were one of the bucks I knew at one-and-twenty.”\n\n“It is lonely here.”\n\n“So much the better. If I were living in a town my whole time would be taken up in looking after you. I fully expected you would have been home when I returned from the Woman.”\n\n“I won’t conceal what I did. I wanted an adventure, and I went with the mummers. I played the part of the Turkish Knight.”\n\n“No, never? Ha, ha! Good gad! I didn’t expect it of you, Eustacia.”\n\n“It was my first performance, and it certainly will be my last. Now I have told you—and remember it is a secret.”\n\n“Of course. But, Eustacia, you never did—ha! ha! Dammy, how ’twould have pleased me forty years ago! But remember, no more of it, my girl. You may walk on the heath night or day, as you choose, so that you don’t bother me; but no figuring in breeches again.”\n\n“You need have no fear for me, Grandpapa.”\n\nHere the conversation ceased, Eustacia’s moral training never exceeding in severity a dialogue of this sort, which, if it ever became profitable to good works, would be a result not dear at the price. But her thoughts soon strayed far from her own personality; and, full of a passionate and indescribable solicitude for one to whom she was not even a name, she went forth into the amplitude of tanned wild around her, restless as Ahasuerus the Jew. She was about half a mile from her residence when she beheld a sinister redness arising from a ravine a little way in advance—dull and lurid like a flame in sunlight and she guessed it to signify Diggory Venn.\n\nWhen the farmers who had wished to buy in a new stock of reddle during the last month had inquired where Venn was to be found, people replied, “On Egdon Heath.” Day after day the answer was the same. Now, since Egdon was populated with heath-croppers and furze-cutters rather than with sheep and shepherds, and the downs where most of the latter were to be found lay some to the north, some to the west of Egdon, his reason for camping about there like Israel in Zin was not apparent. The position was central and occasionally desirable. But the sale of reddle was not Diggory’s primary object in remaining on the heath, particularly at so late a period of the year, when most travellers of his class had gone into winter quarters.\n\nEustacia looked at the lonely man. Wildeve had told her at their last meeting that Venn had been thrust forward by Mrs. Yeobright as one ready and anxious to take his place as Thomasin’s betrothed. His figure was perfect, his face young and well outlined, his eye bright, his intelligence keen, and his position one which he could readily better if he chose. But in spite of possibilities it was not likely that Thomasin would accept this Ishmaelitish creature while she had a cousin like Yeobright at her elbow, and Wildeve at the same time not absolutely indifferent. Eustacia was not long in guessing that poor Mrs. Yeobright, in her anxiety for her niece’s future, had mentioned this lover to stimulate the zeal of the other. Eustacia was on the side of the Yeobrights now, and entered into the spirit of the aunt’s desire.\n\n“Good morning, miss,” said the reddleman, taking off his cap of hareskin, and apparently bearing her no ill-will from recollection of their last meeting.\n\n“Good morning, reddleman,” she said, hardly troubling to lift her heavily shaded eyes to his. “I did not know you were so near. Is your van here too?”\n\nVenn moved his elbow towards a hollow in which a dense brake of purple-stemmed brambles had grown to such vast dimensions as almost to form a dell. Brambles, though churlish when handled, are kindly shelter in early winter, being the latest of the deciduous bushes to lose their leaves.\n\nThe roof and chimney of Venn’s caravan showed behind the tracery and tangles of the brake.\n\n“You remain near this part?” she asked with more interest.\n\n“Yes, I have business here.”\n\n“Not altogether the selling of reddle?”\n\n“It has nothing to do with that.”\n\n“It has to do with Miss Yeobright?”\n\nHer face seemed to ask for an armed peace, and he therefore said frankly, “Yes, miss; it is on account of her.”\n\n“On account of your approaching marriage with her?”\n\nVenn flushed through his stain. “Don’t make sport of me, Miss Vye,” he said.\n\n“It isn’t true?”\n\n“Certainly not.”\n\nShe was thus convinced that the reddleman was a mere _pis aller_ in Mrs. Yeobright’s mind; one, moreover, who had not even been informed of his promotion to that lowly standing. “It was a mere notion of mine,” she said quietly; and was about to pass by without further speech, when, looking round to the right, she saw a painfully well-known figure serpentining upwards by one of the little paths which led to the top where she stood. Owing to the necessary windings of his course his back was at present towards them. She glanced quickly round; to escape that man there was only one way. Turning to Venn, she said, “Would you allow me to rest a few minutes in your van? The banks are damp for sitting on.”\n\n“Certainly, miss; I’ll make a place for you.”\n\nShe followed him behind the dell of brambles to his wheeled dwelling into which Venn mounted, placing the three-legged stool just within the door.\n\n“That is the best I can do for you,” he said, stepping down and retiring to the path, where he resumed the smoking of his pipe as he walked up and down.\n\nEustacia bounded into the vehicle and sat on the stool, ensconced from view on the side towards the trackway. Soon she heard the brushing of other feet than the reddleman’s, a not very friendly “Good day” uttered by two men in passing each other, and then the dwindling of the foot-fall of one of them in a direction onwards. Eustacia stretched her neck forward till she caught a glimpse of a receding back and shoulders; and she felt a wretched twinge of misery, she knew not why. It was the sickening feeling which, if the changed heart has any generosity at all in its composition, accompanies the sudden sight of a once-loved one who is beloved no more.\n\nWhen Eustacia descended to proceed on her way the reddleman came near. “That was Mr. Wildeve who passed, miss,” he said slowly, and expressed by his face that he expected her to feel vexed at having been sitting unseen.\n\n“Yes, I saw him coming up the hill,” replied Eustacia. “Why should you tell me that?” It was a bold question, considering the reddleman’s knowledge of her past love; but her undemonstrative manner had power to repress the opinions of those she treated as remote from her.\n\n“I am glad to hear that you can ask it,” said the reddleman bluntly. “And, now I think of it, it agrees with what I saw last night.”\n\n“Ah—what was that?” Eustacia wished to leave him, but wished to know.\n\n“Mr. Wildeve stayed at Rainbarrow a long time waiting for a lady who didn’t come.”\n\n“You waited too, it seems?”\n\n“Yes, I always do. I was glad to see him disappointed. He will be there again tonight.”\n\n“To be again disappointed. The truth is, reddleman, that that lady, so far from wishing to stand in the way of Thomasin’s marriage with Mr. Wildeve, would be very glad to promote it.”\n\nVenn felt much astonishment at this avowal, though he did not show it clearly; that exhibition may greet remarks which are one remove from expectation, but it is usually withheld in complicated cases of two removes and upwards. “Indeed, miss,” he replied.\n\n“How do you know that Mr. Wildeve will come to Rainbarrow again tonight?” she asked.\n\n“I heard him say to himself that he would. He’s in a regular temper.”\n\nEustacia looked for a moment what she felt, and she murmured, lifting her deep dark eyes anxiously to his, “I wish I knew what to do. I don’t want to be uncivil to him; but I don’t wish to see him again; and I have some few little things to return to him.”\n\n“If you choose to send ’em by me, miss, and a note to tell him that you wish to say no more to him, I’ll take it for you quite privately. That would be the most straightforward way of letting him know your mind.”\n\n“Very well,” said Eustacia. “Come towards my house, and I will bring it out to you.”\n\nShe went on, and as the path was an infinitely small parting in the shaggy locks of the heath, the reddleman followed exactly in her trail. She saw from a distance that the captain was on the bank sweeping the horizon with his telescope; and bidding Venn to wait where he stood she entered the house alone.\n\nIn ten minutes she returned with a parcel and a note, and said, in placing them in his hand, “Why are you so ready to take these for me?”\n\n“Can you ask that?”\n\n“I suppose you think to serve Thomasin in some way by it. Are you as anxious as ever to help on her marriage?”\n\nVenn was a little moved. “I would sooner have married her myself,” he said in a low voice. “But what I feel is that if she cannot be happy without him I will do my duty in helping her to get him, as a man ought.”\n\nEustacia looked curiously at the singular man who spoke thus. What a strange sort of love, to be entirely free from that quality of selfishness which is frequently the chief constituent of the passion, and sometimes its only one! The reddleman’s disinterestedness was so well deserving of respect that it overshot respect by being barely comprehended; and she almost thought it absurd.\n\n“Then we are both of one mind at last,” she said.\n\n“Yes,” replied Venn gloomily. “But if you would tell me, miss, why you take such an interest in her, I should be easier. It is so sudden and strange.”\n\nEustacia appeared at a loss. “I cannot tell you that, reddleman,” she said coldly.\n\nVenn said no more. He pocketed the letter, and, bowing to Eustacia, went away.\n\nRainbarrow had again become blended with night when Wildeve ascended the long acclivity at its base. On his reaching the top a shape grew up from the earth immediately behind him. It was that of Eustacia’s emissary. He slapped Wildeve on the shoulder. The feverish young inn-keeper and ex-engineer started like Satan at the touch of Ithuriel’s spear.\n\n“The meeting is always at eight o’clock, at this place,” said Venn, “and here we are—we three.”\n\n“We three?” said Wildeve, looking quickly round.\n\n“Yes; you, and I, and she. This is she.” He held up the letter and parcel.\n\nWildeve took them wonderingly. “I don’t quite see what this means,” he said. “How do you come here? There must be some mistake.”\n\n“It will be cleared from your mind when you have read the letter. Lanterns for one.” The reddleman struck a light, kindled an inch of tallow-candle which he had brought, and sheltered it with his cap.\n\n“Who are you?” said Wildeve, discerning by the candle-light an obscure rubicundity of person in his companion. “You are the reddleman I saw on the hill this morning—why, you are the man who——”\n\n“Please read the letter.”\n\n“If you had come from the other one I shouldn’t have been surprised,” murmured Wildeve as he opened the letter and read. His face grew serious.\n\n“After some thought I have decided once and for all that we must hold no further communication. The more I consider the matter the more I am convinced that there must be an end to our acquaintance. Had you been uniformly faithful to me throughout these two years you might now have some ground for accusing me of heartlessness; but if you calmly consider what I bore during the period of your desertion, and how I passively put up with your courtship of another without once interfering, you will, I think, own that I have a right to consult my own feelings when you come back to me again. That these are not what they were towards you may, perhaps, be a fault in me, but it is one which you can scarcely reproach me for when you remember how you left me for Thomasin. The little articles you gave me in the early part of our friendship are returned by the bearer of this letter. They should rightly have been sent back when I first heard of your engagement to her.\n\nBy the time that Wildeve reached her name the blankness with which he had read the first half of the letter intensified to mortification. “I am made a great fool of, one way and another,” he said pettishly. “Do you know what is in this letter?”\n\nThe reddleman hummed a tune.\n\n“Can’t you answer me?” asked Wildeve warmly.\n\n“Ru-um-tum-tum,” sang the reddleman.\n\nWildeve stood looking on the ground beside Venn’s feet, till he allowed his eyes to travel upwards over Diggory’s form, as illuminated by the candle, to his head and face. “Ha-ha! Well, I suppose I deserve it, considering how I have played with them both,” he said at last, as much to himself as to Venn. “But of all the odd things that ever I knew, the oddest is that you should so run counter to your own interests as to bring this to me.”\n\n“My interests?”\n\n“Certainly. ’Twas your interest not to do anything which would send me courting Thomasin again, now she has accepted you—or something like it. Mrs. Yeobright says you are to marry her. ’Tisn’t true, then?”\n\n“Good Lord! I heard of this before, but didn’t believe it. When did she say so?”\n\nWildeve began humming as the reddleman had done.\n\n“I don’t believe it now,” cried Venn.\n\n“Ru-um-tum-tum,” sang Wildeve.\n\n“O Lord—how we can imitate!” said Venn contemptuously. “I’ll have this out. I’ll go straight to her.”\n\nDiggory withdrew with an emphatic step, Wildeve’s eye passing over his form in withering derision, as if he were no more than a heath-cropper. When the reddleman’s figure could no longer be seen, Wildeve himself descended and plunged into the rayless hollow of the vale.\n\nTo lose the two women—he who had been the well-beloved of both—was too ironical an issue to be endured. He could only decently save himself by Thomasin; and once he became her husband, Eustacia’s repentance, he thought, would set in for a long and bitter term. It was no wonder that Wildeve, ignorant of the new man at the back of the scene, should have supposed Eustacia to be playing a part. To believe that the letter was not the result of some momentary pique, to infer that she really gave him up to Thomasin, would have required previous knowledge of her transfiguration by that man’s influence. Who was to know that she had grown generous in the greediness of a new passion, that in coveting one cousin she was dealing liberally with another, that in her eagerness to appropriate she gave way?\n\nFull of this resolve to marry in haste, and wring the heart of the proud girl, Wildeve went his way.\n\nMeanwhile Diggory Venn had returned to his van, where he stood looking thoughtfully into the stove. A new vista was opened up to him. But, however promising Mrs. Yeobright’s views of him might be as a candidate for her niece’s hand, one condition was indispensable to the favour of Thomasin herself, and that was a renunciation of his present wild mode of life. In this he saw little difficulty.\n\nHe could not afford to wait till the next day before seeing Thomasin and detailing his plan. He speedily plunged himself into toilet operations, pulled a suit of cloth clothes from a box, and in about twenty minutes stood before the van-lantern as a reddleman in nothing but his face, the vermilion shades of which were not to be removed in a day. Closing the door and fastening it with a padlock, Venn set off towards Blooms-End.\n\nHe had reached the white palings and laid his hand upon the gate when the door of the house opened, and quickly closed again. A female form had glided in. At the same time a man, who had seemingly been standing with the woman in the porch, came forward from the house till he was face to face with Venn. It was Wildeve again.\n\n“Man alive, you’ve been quick at it,” said Diggory sarcastically.\n\n“And you slow, as you will find,” said Wildeve. “And,” lowering his voice, “you may as well go back again now. I’ve claimed her, and got her. Good night, reddleman!” Thereupon Wildeve walked away.\n\nVenn’s heart sank within him, though it had not risen unduly high. He stood leaning over the palings in an indecisive mood for nearly a quarter of an hour. Then he went up the garden path, knocked, and asked for Mrs. Yeobright.\n\nInstead of requesting him to enter she came to the porch. A discourse was carried on between them in low measured tones for the space of ten minutes or more. At the end of the time Mrs. Yeobright went in, and Venn sadly retraced his steps into the heath. When he had again regained his van he lit the lantern, and with an apathetic face at once began to pull off his best clothes, till in the course of a few minutes he reappeared as the confirmed and irretrievable reddleman that he had seemed before.\nVIII. Firmness Is Discovered in a Gentle Heart\n\nOn that evening the interior of Blooms-End, though cosy and comfortable, had been rather silent. Clym Yeobright was not at home. Since the Christmas party he had gone on a few days’ visit to a friend about ten miles off.\n\nThe shadowy form seen by Venn to part from Wildeve in the porch, and quickly withdraw into the house, was Thomasin’s. On entering she threw down a cloak which had been carelessly wrapped round her, and came forward to the light, where Mrs. Yeobright sat at her work-table, drawn up within the settle, so that part of it projected into the chimney-corner.\n\n“I don’t like your going out after dark alone, Tamsin,” said her aunt quietly, without looking up from her work.\n\n“I have only been just outside the door.”\n\n“Well?” inquired Mrs. Yeobright, struck by a change in the tone of Thomasin’s voice, and observing her. Thomasin’s cheek was flushed to a pitch far beyond that which it had reached before her troubles, and her eyes glittered.\n\n“It was _he_ who knocked,” she said.\n\n“I thought as much.”\n\n“He wishes the marriage to be at once.”\n\n“Indeed! What—is he anxious?” Mrs. Yeobright directed a searching look upon her niece. “Why did not Mr. Wildeve come in?”\n\n“He did not wish to. You are not friends with him, he says. He would like the wedding to be the day after tomorrow, quite privately; at the church of his parish—not at ours.”\n\n“Oh! And what did you say?”\n\n“I agreed to it,” Thomasin answered firmly. “I am a practical woman now. I don’t believe in hearts at all. I would marry him under any circumstances since—since Clym’s letter.”\n\nA letter was lying on Mrs. Yeobright’s work-basket, and at Thomasin’s words her aunt reopened it, and silently read for the tenth time that day:—\n\n“What is the meaning of this silly story that people are circulating about Thomasin and Mr. Wildeve? I should call such a scandal humiliating if there was the least chance of its being true. How could such a gross falsehood have arisen? It is said that one should go abroad to hear news of home, and I appear to have done it. Of course I contradict the tale everywhere; but it is very vexing, and I wonder how it could have originated. It is too ridiculous that such a girl as Thomasin could so mortify us as to get jilted on the wedding day. What has she done?”\n\n“Yes,” Mrs. Yeobright said sadly, putting down the letter. “If you think you can marry him, do so. And since Mr. Wildeve wishes it to be unceremonious, let it be that too. I can do nothing. It is all in your own hands now. My power over your welfare came to an end when you left this house to go with him to Anglebury.” She continued, half in bitterness, “I may almost ask, why do you consult me in the matter at all? If you had gone and married him without saying a word to me, I could hardly have been angry—simply because, poor girl, you can’t do a better thing.”\n\n“Don’t say that and dishearten me.”\n\n“You are right—I will not.”\n\n“I do not plead for him, Aunt. Human nature is weak, and I am not a blind woman to insist that he is perfect. I did think so, but I don’t now. But I know my course, and you know that I know it. I hope for the best.”\n\n“And so do I, and we will both continue to,” said Mrs. Yeobright, rising and kissing her. “Then the wedding, if it comes off, will be on the morning of the very day Clym comes home?”\n\n“Yes. I decided that it ought to be over before he came. After that you can look him in the face, and so can I. Our concealments will matter nothing.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright moved her head in thoughtful assent, and presently said, “Do you wish me to give you away? I am willing to undertake that, you know, if you wish, as I was last time. After once forbidding the banns I think I can do no less.”\n\n“I don’t think I will ask you to come,” said Thomasin reluctantly, but with decision. “It would be unpleasant, I am almost sure. Better let there be only strangers present, and none of my relations at all. I would rather have it so. I do not wish to do anything which may touch your credit, and I feel that I should be uncomfortable if you were there, after what has passed. I am only your niece, and there is no necessity why you should concern yourself more about me.”\n\n“Well, he has beaten us,” her aunt said. “It really seems as if he had been playing with you in this way in revenge for my humbling him as I did by standing up against him at first.”\n\n“O no, Aunt,” murmured Thomasin.\n\nThey said no more on the subject then. Diggory Venn’s knock came soon after; and Mrs. Yeobright, on returning from her interview with him in the porch, carelessly observed, “Another lover has come to ask for you.”\n\n“No?”\n“Yes, that queer young man Venn.”\n\n“Asks to pay his addresses to me?”\n\n“Yes; and I told him he was too late.”\n\nThomasin looked silently into the candle-flame. “Poor Diggory!” she said, and then aroused herself to other things.\n\nThe next day was passed in mere mechanical deeds of preparation, both the women being anxious to immerse themselves in these to escape the emotional aspect of the situation. Some wearing apparel and other articles were collected anew for Thomasin, and remarks on domestic details were frequently made, so as to obscure any inner misgivings about her future as Wildeve’s wife.\n\nThe appointed morning came. The arrangement with Wildeve was that he should meet her at the church to guard against any unpleasant curiosity which might have affected them had they been seen walking off together in the usual country way.\n\nAunt and niece stood together in the bedroom where the bride was dressing. The sun, where it could catch it, made a mirror of Thomasin’s hair, which she always wore braided. It was braided according to a calendar system—the more important the day the more numerous the strands in the braid. On ordinary working-days she braided it in threes; on ordinary Sundays in fours; at Maypolings, gipsyings, and the like, she braided it in fives. Years ago she had said that when she married she would braid it in sevens. She had braided it in sevens today.\n\n“I have been thinking that I will wear my blue silk after all,” she said. “It is my wedding day, even though there may be something sad about the time. I mean,” she added, anxious to correct any wrong impression, “not sad in itself, but in its having had great disappointment and trouble before it.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright breathed in a way which might have been called a sigh. “I almost wish Clym had been at home,” she said. “Of course you chose the time because of his absence.”\n\n“Partly. I have felt that I acted unfairly to him in not telling him all; but, as it was done not to grieve him, I thought I would carry out the plan to its end, and tell the whole story when the sky was clear.”\n\n“You are a practical little woman,” said Mrs. Yeobright, smiling. “I wish you and he—no, I don’t wish anything. There, it is nine o’clock,” she interrupted, hearing a whizz and a dinging downstairs.\n\n“I told Damon I would leave at nine,” said Thomasin, hastening out of the room.\n\nHer aunt followed. When Thomasin was going up the little walk from the door to the wicket-gate, Mrs. Yeobright looked reluctantly at her, and said, “It is a shame to let you go alone.”\n\n“It is necessary,” said Thomasin.\n\n“At any rate,” added her aunt with forced cheerfulness, “I shall call upon you this afternoon, and bring the cake with me. If Clym has returned by that time he will perhaps come too. I wish to show Mr. Wildeve that I bear him no ill-will. Let the past be forgotten. Well, God bless you! There, I don’t believe in old superstitions, but I’ll do it.” She threw a slipper at the retreating figure of the girl, who turned, smiled, and went on again.\n\nA few steps further, and she looked back. “Did you call me, Aunt?” she tremulously inquired. “Good-bye!”\n\nMoved by an uncontrollable feeling as she looked upon Mrs. Yeobright’s worn, wet face, she ran back, when her aunt came forward, and they met again. “O—Tamsie,” said the elder, weeping, “I don’t like to let you go.”\n\n“I—I am—” Thomasin began, giving way likewise. But, quelling her grief, she said “Good-bye!” again and went on.\n\nThen Mrs. Yeobright saw a little figure wending its way between the scratching furze-bushes, and diminishing far up the valley—a pale-blue spot in a vast field of neutral brown, solitary and undefended except by the power of her own hope.\n\nBut the worst feature in the case was one which did not appear in the landscape; it was the man.\n\nThe hour chosen for the ceremony by Thomasin and Wildeve had been so timed as to enable her to escape the awkwardness of meeting her cousin Clym, who was returning the same morning. To own to the partial truth of what he had heard would be distressing as long as the humiliating position resulting from the event was unimproved. It was only after a second and successful journey to the altar that she could lift up her head and prove the failure of the first attempt a pure accident.\n\nShe had not been gone from Blooms-End more than half an hour when Yeobright came by the meads from the other direction and entered the house.\n\n“I had an early breakfast,” he said to his mother after greeting her. “Now I could eat a little more.”\n\nThey sat down to the repeated meal, and he went on in a low, anxious voice, apparently imagining that Thomasin had not yet come downstairs, “What’s this I have heard about Thomasin and Mr. Wildeve?”\n\n“It is true in many points,” said Mrs. Yeobright quietly; “but it is all right now, I hope.” She looked at the clock.\n\n“True?”\n“Thomasin is gone to him today.”\n\nClym pushed away his breakfast. “Then there is a scandal of some sort, and that’s what’s the matter with Thomasin. Was it this that made her ill?”\n\n“Yes. Not a scandal—a misfortune. I will tell you all about it, Clym. You must not be angry, but you must listen, and you’ll find that what we have done has been done for the best.”\n\nShe then told him the circumstances. All that he had known of the affair before he returned from Paris was that there had existed an attachment between Thomasin and Wildeve, which his mother had at first discountenanced, but had since, owing to the arguments of Thomasin, looked upon in a little more favourable light. When she, therefore, proceeded to explain all he was greatly surprised and troubled.\n\n“And she determined that the wedding should be over before you came back,” said Mrs. Yeobright, “that there might be no chance of her meeting you, and having a very painful time of it. That’s why she has gone to him; they have arranged to be married this morning.”\n\n“But I can’t understand it,” said Yeobright, rising. “’Tis so unlike her. I can see why you did not write to me after her unfortunate return home. But why didn’t you let me know when the wedding was going to be—the first time?”\n\n“Well, I felt vexed with her just then. She seemed to me to be obstinate; and when I found that you were nothing in her mind I vowed that she should be nothing in yours. I felt that she was only my niece after all; I told her she might marry, but that I should take no interest in it, and should not bother you about it either.”\n\n“It wouldn’t have been bothering me. Mother, you did wrong.”\n\n“I thought it might disturb you in your business, and that you might throw up your situation, or injure your prospects in some way because of it, so I said nothing. Of course, if they had married at that time in a proper manner, I should have told you at once.”\n\n“Tamsin actually being married while we are sitting here!”\n\n“Yes. Unless some accident happens again, as it did the first time. It may, considering he’s the same man.”\n\n“Yes, and I believe it will. Was it right to let her go? Suppose Wildeve is really a bad fellow?”\n\n“Then he won’t come, and she’ll come home again.”\n\n“You should have looked more into it.”\n\n“It is useless to say that,” his mother answered with an impatient look of sorrow. “You don’t know how bad it has been here with us all these weeks, Clym. You don’t know what a mortification anything of that sort is to a woman. You don’t know the sleepless nights we’ve had in this house, and the almost bitter words that have passed between us since that Fifth of November. I hope never to pass seven such weeks again. Tamsin has not gone outside the door, and I have been ashamed to look anybody in the face; and now you blame me for letting her do the only thing that can be done to set that trouble straight.”\n\n“No,” he said slowly. “Upon the whole I don’t blame you. But just consider how sudden it seems to me. Here was I, knowing nothing; and then I am told all at once that Tamsie is gone to be married. Well, I suppose there was nothing better to do. Do you know, Mother,” he continued after a moment or two, looking suddenly interested in his own past history, “I once thought of Tamsin as a sweetheart? Yes, I did. How odd boys are! And when I came home and saw her this time she seemed so much more affectionate than usual, that I was quite reminded of those days, particularly on the night of the party, when she was unwell. We had the party just the same—was not that rather cruel to her?”\n\n“It made no difference. I had arranged to give one, and it was not worth while to make more gloom than necessary. To begin by shutting ourselves up and telling you of Tamsin’s misfortunes would have been a poor sort of welcome.”\n\nClym remained thinking. “I almost wish you had not had that party,” he said; “and for other reasons. But I will tell you in a day or two. We must think of Tamsin now.”\n\nThey lapsed into silence. “I’ll tell you what,” said Yeobright again, in a tone which showed some slumbering feeling still. “I don’t think it kind to Tamsin to let her be married like this, and neither of us there to keep up her spirits or care a bit about her. She hasn’t disgraced herself, or done anything to deserve that. It is bad enough that the wedding should be so hurried and unceremonious, without our keeping away from it in addition. Upon my soul, ’tis almost a shame. I’ll go.”\n\n“It is over by this time,” said his mother with a sigh; “unless they were late, or he—”\n\n“Then I shall be soon enough to see them come out. I don’t quite like your keeping me in ignorance, Mother, after all. Really, I half hope he has failed to meet her!”\n\n“And ruined her character?”\n\n“Nonsense—that wouldn’t ruin Thomasin.”\n\nHe took up his hat and hastily left the house. Mrs. Yeobright looked rather unhappy, and sat still, deep in thought. But she was not long left alone. A few minutes later Clym came back again, and in his company came Diggory Venn.\n\n“I find there isn’t time for me to get there,” said Clym.\n\n“Is she married?” Mrs. Yeobright inquired, turning to the reddleman a face in which a strange strife of wishes, for and against, was apparent.\n\nVenn bowed. “She is, ma’am.”\n\n“How strange it sounds,” murmured Clym.\n\n“And he didn’t disappoint her this time?” said Mrs. Yeobright.\n\n“He did not. And there is now no slight on her name. I was hastening ath’art to tell you at once, as I saw you were not there.”\n\n“How came you to be there? How did you know it?” she asked.\n\n“I have been in that neighbourhood for some time, and I saw them go in,” said the reddleman. “Wildeve came up to the door, punctual as the clock. I didn’t expect it of him.” He did not add, as he might have added, that how he came to be in that neighbourhood was not by accident; that, since Wildeve’s resumption of his right to Thomasin, Venn, with the thoroughness which was part of his character, had determined to see the end of the episode.\n\n“Who was there?” said Mrs. Yeobright.\n\n“Nobody hardly. I stood right out of the way, and she did not see me.” The reddleman spoke huskily, and looked into the garden.\n\n“Who gave her away?”\n“Miss Vye.”\n\n“How very remarkable! Miss Vye! It is to be considered an honour, I suppose?”\n\n“Who’s Miss Vye?” said Clym.\n\n“Captain Vye’s granddaughter, of Mistover Knap.”\n\n“A proud girl from Budmouth,” said Mrs. Yeobright. “One not much to my liking. People say she’s a witch, but of course that’s absurd.”\n\nThe reddleman kept to himself his acquaintance with that fair personage, and also that Eustacia was there because he went to fetch her, in accordance with a promise he had given as soon as he learnt that the marriage was to take place. He merely said, in continuation of the story——\n\n“I was sitting on the churchyard wall when they came up, one from one way, the other from the other; and Miss Vye was walking thereabouts, looking at the headstones. As soon as they had gone in I went to the door, feeling I should like to see it, as I knew her so well. I pulled off my boots because they were so noisy, and went up into the gallery. I saw then that the parson and clerk were already there.”\n\n“How came Miss Vye to have anything to do with it, if she was only on a walk that way?”\n\n“Because there was nobody else. She had gone into the church just before me, not into the gallery. The parson looked round before beginning, and as she was the only one near he beckoned to her, and she went up to the rails. After that, when it came to signing the book, she pushed up her veil and signed; and Tamsin seemed to thank her for her kindness.” The reddleman told the tale thoughtfully for there lingered upon his vision the changing colour of Wildeve, when Eustacia lifted the thick veil which had concealed her from recognition and looked calmly into his face. “And then,” said Diggory sadly, “I came away, for her history as Tamsin Yeobright was over.”\n\n“I offered to go,” said Mrs. Yeobright regretfully. “But she said it was not necessary.”\n\n“Well, it is no matter,” said the reddleman. “The thing is done at last as it was meant to be at first, and God send her happiness. Now I’ll wish you good morning.”\n\nHe placed his cap on his head and went out.\n\nFrom that instant of leaving Mrs. Yeobright’s door, the reddleman was seen no more in or about Egdon Heath for a space of many months. He vanished entirely. The nook among the brambles where his van had been standing was as vacant as ever the next morning, and scarcely a sign remained to show that he had been there, excepting a few straws, and a little redness on the turf, which was washed away by the next storm of rain.\n\nThe report that Diggory had brought of the wedding, correct as far as it went, was deficient in one significant particular, which had escaped him through his being at some distance back in the church. When Thomasin was tremblingly engaged in signing her name Wildeve had flung towards Eustacia a glance that said plainly, “I have punished you now.” She had replied in a low tone—and he little thought how truly—“You mistake; it gives me sincerest pleasure to see her your wife today.”\n\nI. “My Mind to Me a Kingdom Is”\n\nIn Clym Yeobright’s face could be dimly seen the typical countenance of the future. Should there be a classic period to art hereafter, its Pheidias may produce such faces. The view of life as a thing to be put up with, replacing that zest for existence which was so intense in early civilizations, must ultimately enter so thoroughly into the constitution of the advanced races that its facial expression will become accepted as a new artistic departure. People already feel that a man who lives without disturbing a curve of feature, or setting a mark of mental concern anywhere upon himself, is too far removed from modern perceptiveness to be a modern type. Physically beautiful men—the glory of the race when it was young—are almost an anachronism now; and we may wonder whether, at some time or other, physically beautiful women may not be an anachronism likewise.\n\nThe truth seems to be that a long line of disillusive centuries has permanently displaced the Hellenic idea of life, or whatever it may be called. What the Greeks only suspected we know well; what their Æschylus imagined our nursery children feel. That old-fashioned revelling in the general situation grows less and less possible as we uncover the defects of natural laws, and see the quandary that man is in by their operation.\n\nThe lineaments which will get embodied in ideals based upon this new recognition will probably be akin to those of Yeobright. The observer’s eye was arrested, not by his face as a picture, but by his face as a page; not by what it was, but by what it recorded. His features were attractive in the light of symbols, as sounds intrinsically common become attractive in language, and as shapes intrinsically simple become interesting in writing.\n\nHe had been a lad of whom something was expected. Beyond this all had been chaos. That he would be successful in an original way, or that he would go to the dogs in an original way, seemed equally probable. The only absolute certainty about him was that he would not stand still in the circumstances amid which he was born.\n\nHence, when his name was casually mentioned by neighbouring yeomen, the listener said, “Ah, Clym Yeobright—what is he doing now?” When the instinctive question about a person is, What is he doing? it is felt that he will be found to be, like most of us, doing nothing in particular. There is an indefinite sense that he must be invading some region of singularity, good or bad. The devout hope is that he is doing well. The secret faith is that he is making a mess of it. Half a dozen comfortable market-men, who were habitual callers at the Quiet Woman as they passed by in their carts, were partial to the topic. In fact, though they were not Egdon men, they could hardly avoid it while they sucked their long clay tubes and regarded the heath through the window. Clym had been so inwoven with the heath in his boyhood that hardly anybody could look upon it without thinking of him. So the subject recurred: if he were making a fortune and a name, so much the better for him; if he were making a tragical figure in the world, so much the better for a narrative.\n\nThe fact was that Yeobright’s fame had spread to an awkward extent before he left home. “It is bad when your fame outruns your means,” said the Spanish Jesuit Gracian. At the age of six he had asked a Scripture riddle: “Who was the first man known to wear breeches?” and applause had resounded from the very verge of the heath. At seven he painted the Battle of Waterloo with tiger-lily pollen and black-currant juice, in the absence of water-colours. By the time he reached twelve he had in this manner been heard of as artist and scholar for at least two miles round. An individual whose fame spreads three or four thousand yards in the time taken by the fame of others similarly situated to travel six or eight hundred, must of necessity have something in him. Possibly Clym’s fame, like Homer’s, owed something to the accidents of his situation; nevertheless famous he was.\n\nHe grew up and was helped out in life. That waggery of fate which started Clive as a writing clerk, Gay as a linen-draper, Keats as a surgeon, and a thousand others in a thousand other odd ways, banished the wild and ascetic heath lad to a trade whose sole concern was with the especial symbols of self-indulgence and vainglory.\n\nThe details of this choice of a business for him it is not necessary to give. At the death of his father a neighbouring gentleman had kindly undertaken to give the boy a start, and this assumed the form of sending him to Budmouth. Yeobright did not wish to go there, but it was the only feasible opening. Thence he went to London; and thence, shortly after, to Paris, where he had remained till now.\n\nSomething being expected of him, he had not been at home many days before a great curiosity as to why he stayed on so long began to arise in the heath. The natural term of a holiday had passed, yet he still remained. On the Sunday morning following the week of Thomasin’s marriage a discussion on this subject was in progress at a hair-cutting before Fairway’s house. Here the local barbering was always done at this hour on this day, to be followed by the great Sunday wash of the inhabitants at noon, which in its turn was followed by the great Sunday dressing an hour later. On Egdon Heath Sunday proper did not begin till dinner-time, and even then it was a somewhat battered specimen of the day.\n\nThese Sunday-morning hair-cuttings were performed by Fairway; the victim sitting on a chopping-block in front of the house, without a coat, and the neighbours gossiping around, idly observing the locks of hair as they rose upon the wind after the snip, and flew away out of sight to the four quarters of the heavens. Summer and winter the scene was the same, unless the wind were more than usually blusterous, when the stool was shifted a few feet round the corner. To complain of cold in sitting out of doors, hatless and coatless, while Fairway told true stories between the cuts of the scissors, would have been to pronounce yourself no man at once. To flinch, exclaim, or move a muscle of the face at the small stabs under the ear received from those instruments, or at scarifications of the neck by the comb, would have been thought a gross breach of good manners, considering that Fairway did it all for nothing. A bleeding about the poll on Sunday afternoons was amply accounted for by the explanation. “I have had my hair cut, you know.”\n\nThe conversation on Yeobright had been started by a distant view of the young man rambling leisurely across the heath before them.\n\n“A man who is doing well elsewhere wouldn’t bide here two or three weeks for nothing,” said Fairway. “He’s got some project in ’s head—depend upon that.”\n\n“Well, ’a can’t keep a diment shop here,” said Sam.\n\n“I don’t see why he should have had them two heavy boxes home if he had not been going to bide; and what there is for him to do here the Lord in heaven knows.”\n\nBefore many more surmises could be indulged in Yeobright had come near; and seeing the hair-cutting group he turned aside to join them. Marching up, and looking critically at their faces for a moment, he said, without introduction, “Now, folks, let me guess what you have been talking about.”\n\n“Ay, sure, if you will,” said Sam.\n\n“About me.”\n\n“Now, it is a thing I shouldn’t have dreamed of doing, otherwise,” said Fairway in a tone of integrity; “but since you have named it, Master Yeobright, I’ll own that we was talking about ’ee. We were wondering what could keep you home here mollyhorning about when you have made such a world-wide name for yourself in the nick-nack trade—now, that’s the truth o’t.”\n\n“I’ll tell you,” said Yeobright with unexpected earnestness. “I am not sorry to have the opportunity. I’ve come home because, all things considered, I can be a trifle less useless here than anywhere else. But I have only lately found this out. When I first got away from home I thought this place was not worth troubling about. I thought our life here was contemptible. To oil your boots instead of blacking them, to dust your coat with a switch instead of a brush—was there ever anything more ridiculous? I said.”\n\n“So ’tis; so ’tis!”\n\n“No, no—you are wrong; it isn’t.”\n\n“Beg your pardon, we thought that was your maning?”\n\n“Well, as my views changed my course became very depressing. I found that I was trying to be like people who had hardly anything in common with myself. I was endeavouring to put off one sort of life for another sort of life, which was not better than the life I had known before. It was simply different.”\n\n“True; a sight different,” said Fairway.\n\n“Yes, Paris must be a taking place,” said Humphrey. “Grand shop-winders, trumpets, and drums; and here be we out of doors in all winds and weathers—”\n\n“But you mistake me,” pleaded Clym. “All this was very depressing. But not so depressing as something I next perceived—that my business was the idlest, vainest, most effeminate business that ever a man could be put to. That decided me—I would give it up and try to follow some rational occupation among the people I knew best, and to whom I could be of most use. I have come home; and this is how I mean to carry out my plan. I shall keep a school as near to Egdon as possible, so as to be able to walk over here and have a night-school in my mother’s house. But I must study a little at first, to get properly qualified. Now, neighbours, I must go.”\n\nAnd Clym resumed his walk across the heath.\n\n“He’ll never carry it out in the world,” said Fairway. “In a few weeks he’ll learn to see things otherwise.”\n\n“’Tis good-hearted of the young man,” said another. “But, for my part, I think he had better mind his business.”\n\nII. The New Course Causes Disappointment\n\nYeobright loved his kind. He had a conviction that the want of most men was knowledge of a sort which brings wisdom rather than affluence. He wished to raise the class at the expense of individuals rather than individuals at the expense of the class. What was more, he was ready at once to be the first unit sacrificed.\n\nIn passing from the bucolic to the intellectual life the intermediate stages are usually two at least, frequently many more; and one of those stages is almost sure to be worldly advanced. We can hardly imagine bucolic placidity quickening to intellectual aims without imagining social aims as the transitional phase. Yeobright’s local peculiarity was that in striving at high thinking he still cleaved to plain living—nay, wild and meagre living in many respects, and brotherliness with clowns.\n\nHe was a John the Baptist who took ennoblement rather than repentance for his text. Mentally he was in a provincial future, that is, he was in many points abreast with the central town thinkers of his date. Much of this development he may have owed to his studious life in Paris, where he had become acquainted with ethical systems popular at the time.\n\nIn consequence of this relatively advanced position, Yeobright might have been called unfortunate. The rural world was not ripe for him. A man should be only partially before his time—to be completely to the vanward in aspirations is fatal to fame. Had Philip’s warlike son been intellectually so far ahead as to have attempted civilization without bloodshed, he would have been twice the godlike hero that he seemed, but nobody would have heard of an Alexander.\n\nIn the interests of renown the forwardness should lie chiefly in the capacity to handle things. Successful propagandists have succeeded because the doctrine they bring into form is that which their listeners have for some time felt without being able to shape. A man who advocates æsthetic effort and deprecates social effort is only likely to be understood by a class to which social effort has become a stale matter. To argue upon the possibility of culture before luxury to the bucolic world may be to argue truly, but it is an attempt to disturb a sequence to which humanity has been long accustomed. Yeobright preaching to the Egdon eremites that they might rise to a serene comprehensiveness without going through the process of enriching themselves was not unlike arguing to ancient Chaldeans that in ascending from earth to the pure empyrean it was not necessary to pass first into the intervening heaven of ether.\n\nWas Yeobright’s mind well-proportioned? No. A well proportioned mind is one which shows no particular bias; one of which we may safely say that it will never cause its owner to be confined as a madman, tortured as a heretic, or crucified as a blasphemer. Also, on the other hand, that it will never cause him to be applauded as a prophet, revered as a priest, or exalted as a king. Its usual blessings are happiness and mediocrity. It produces the poetry of Rogers, the paintings of West, the statecraft of North, the spiritual guidance of Tomline; enabling its possessors to find their way to wealth, to wind up well, to step with dignity off the stage, to die comfortably in their beds, and to get the decent monument which, in many cases, they deserve. It never would have allowed Yeobright to do such a ridiculous thing as throw up his business to benefit his fellow-creatures.\n\nHe walked along towards home without attending to paths. If anyone knew the heath well it was Clym. He was permeated with its scenes, with its substance, and with its odours. He might be said to be its product. His eyes had first opened thereon; with its appearance all the first images of his memory were mingled, his estimate of life had been coloured by it: his toys had been the flint knives and arrow-heads which he found there, wondering why stones should “grow” to such odd shapes; his flowers, the purple bells and yellow furze: his animal kingdom, the snakes and croppers; his society, its human haunters. Take all the varying hates felt by Eustacia Vye towards the heath, and translate them into loves, and you have the heart of Clym. He gazed upon the wide prospect as he walked, and was glad.\n\nTo many persons this Egdon was a place which had slipped out of its century generations ago, to intrude as an uncouth object into this. It was an obsolete thing, and few cared to study it. How could this be otherwise in the days of square fields, plashed hedges, and meadows watered on a plan so rectangular that on a fine day they looked like silver gridirons? The farmer, in his ride, who could smile at artificial grasses, look with solicitude at the coming corn, and sigh with sadness at the fly-eaten turnips, bestowed upon the distant upland of heath nothing better than a frown. But as for Yeobright, when he looked from the heights on his way he could not help indulging in a barbarous satisfaction at observing that, in some of the attempts at reclamation from the waste, tillage, after holding on for a year or two, had receded again in despair, the ferns and furze-tufts stubbornly reasserting themselves.\n\nHe descended into the valley, and soon reached his home at Blooms-End. His mother was snipping dead leaves from the window-plants. She looked up at him as if she did not understand the meaning of his long stay with her; her face had worn that look for several days. He could perceive that the curiosity which had been shown by the hair-cutting group amounted in his mother to concern. But she had asked no question with her lips, even when the arrival of his trunk suggested that he was not going to leave her soon. Her silence besought an explanation of him more loudly than words.\n\n“I am not going back to Paris again, Mother,” he said. “At least, in my old capacity. I have given up the business.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright turned in pained surprise. “I thought something was amiss, because of the boxes. I wonder you did not tell me sooner.”\n\n“I ought to have done it. But I have been in doubt whether you would be pleased with my plan. I was not quite clear on a few points myself. I am going to take an entirely new course.”\n\n“I am astonished, Clym. How can you want to do better than you’ve been doing?”\n\n“Very easily. But I shall not do better in the way you mean; I suppose it will be called doing worse. But I hate that business of mine, and I want to do some worthy thing before I die. As a schoolmaster I think to do it—a school-master to the poor and ignorant, to teach them what nobody else will.”\n\n“After all the trouble that has been taken to give you a start, and when there is nothing to do but to keep straight on towards affluence, you say you will be a poor man’s schoolmaster. Your fancies will be your ruin, Clym.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright spoke calmly, but the force of feeling behind the words was but too apparent to one who knew her as well as her son did. He did not answer. There was in his face that hopelessness of being understood which comes when the objector is constitutionally beyond the reach of a logic that, even under favouring conditions, is almost too coarse a vehicle for the subtlety of the argument.\n\nNo more was said on the subject till the end of dinner. His mother then began, as if there had been no interval since the morning. “It disturbs me, Clym, to find that you have come home with such thoughts as those. I hadn’t the least idea that you meant to go backward in the world by your own free choice. Of course, I have always supposed you were going to push straight on, as other men do—all who deserve the name—when they have been put in a good way of doing well.”\n\n“I cannot help it,” said Clym, in a troubled tone. “Mother, I hate the flashy business. Talk about men who deserve the name, can any man deserving the name waste his time in that effeminate way, when he sees half the world going to ruin for want of somebody to buckle to and teach them how to breast the misery they are born to? I get up every morning and see the whole creation groaning and travailing in pain, as St. Paul says, and yet there am I, trafficking in glittering splendours with wealthy women and titled libertines, and pandering to the meanest vanities—I, who have health and strength enough for anything. I have been troubled in my mind about it all the year, and the end is that I cannot do it any more.”\n\n“Why can’t you do it as well as others?”\n\n“I don’t know, except that there are many things other people care for which I don’t; and that’s partly why I think I ought to do this. For one thing, my body does not require much of me. I cannot enjoy delicacies; good things are wasted upon me. Well, I ought to turn that defect to advantage, and by being able to do without what other people require I can spend what such things cost upon anybody else.”\n\nNow, Yeobright, having inherited some of these very instincts from the woman before him, could not fail to awaken a reciprocity in her through her feelings, if not by arguments, disguise it as she might for his good. She spoke with less assurance. “And yet you might have been a wealthy man if you had only persevered. Manager to that large diamond establishment—what better can a man wish for? What a post of trust and respect! I suppose you will be like your father; like him, you are getting weary of doing well.”\n\n“No,” said her son, “I am not weary of that, though I am weary of what you mean by it. Mother, what is doing well?”\n\nMrs. Yeobright was far too thoughtful a woman to be content with ready definitions, and, like the “What is wisdom?” of Plato’s Socrates, and the “What is truth?” of Pontius Pilate, Yeobright’s burning question received no answer.\n\nThe silence was broken by the clash of the garden gate, a tap at the door, and its opening. Christian Cantle appeared in the room in his Sunday clothes.\n\nIt was the custom on Egdon to begin the preface to a story before absolutely entering the house, so as to be well in for the body of the narrative by the time visitor and visited stood face to face. Christian had been saying to them while the door was leaving its latch, “To think that I, who go from home but once in a while, and hardly then, should have been there this morning!”\n\n“’Tis news you have brought us, then, Christian?” said Mrs. Yeobright.\n\n“Ay, sure, about a witch, and ye must overlook my time o’ day; for, says I, ‘I must go and tell ’em, though they won’t have half done dinner.’ I assure ye it made me shake like a driven leaf. Do ye think any harm will come o’t?”\n\n“Well—what?”\n\n“This morning at church we was all standing up, and the pa’son said, ‘Let us pray.’ ‘Well,’ thinks I, ‘one may as well kneel as stand’; so down I went; and, more than that, all the rest were as willing to oblige the man as I. We hadn’t been hard at it for more than a minute when a most terrible screech sounded through church, as if somebody had just gied up their heart’s blood. All the folk jumped up and then we found that Susan Nunsuch had pricked Miss Vye with a long stocking-needle, as she had threatened to do as soon as ever she could get the young lady to church, where she don’t come very often. She’ve waited for this chance for weeks, so as to draw her blood and put an end to the bewitching of Susan’s children that has been carried on so long. Sue followed her into church, sat next to her, and as soon as she could find a chance in went the stocking-needle into my lady’s arm.”\n\n“Good heaven, how horrid!” said Mrs. Yeobright.\n\n“Sue pricked her that deep that the maid fainted away; and as I was afeard there might be some tumult among us, I got behind the bass viol and didn’t see no more. But they carried her out into the air, ’tis said; but when they looked round for Sue she was gone. What a scream that girl gied, poor thing! There were the pa’son in his surplice holding up his hand and saying, ‘Sit down, my good people, sit down!’ But the deuce a bit would they sit down. O, and what d’ye think I found out, Mrs. Yeobright? The pa’son wears a suit of clothes under his surplice!—I could see his black sleeves when he held up his arm.”\n\n“’Tis a cruel thing,” said Yeobright.\n\n“Yes,” said his mother.\n\n“The nation ought to look into it,” said Christian. “Here’s Humphrey coming, I think.”\n\nIn came Humphrey. “Well, have ye heard the news? But I see you have. ’Tis a very strange thing that whenever one of Egdon folk goes to church some rum job or other is sure to be doing. The last time one of us was there was when neighbour Fairway went in the fall; and that was the day you forbad the banns, Mrs. Yeobright.”\n\n“Has this cruelly treated girl been able to walk home?” said Clym.\n\n“They say she got better, and went home very well. And now I’ve told it I must be moving homeward myself.”\n\n“And I,” said Humphrey. “Truly now we shall see if there’s anything in what folks say about her.”\n\nWhen they were gone into the heath again Yeobright said quietly to his mother, “Do you think I have turned teacher too soon?”\n\n“It is right that there should be schoolmasters, and missionaries, and all such men,” she replied. “But it is right, too, that I should try to lift you out of this life into something richer, and that you should not come back again, and be as if I had not tried at all.”\n\nLater in the day Sam, the turf-cutter, entered. “I’ve come a-borrowing, Mrs. Yeobright. I suppose you have heard what’s been happening to the beauty on the hill?”\n\n“Yes, Sam: half a dozen have been telling us.”\n\n“Beauty?” said Clym.\n\n“Yes, tolerably well-favoured,” Sam replied. “Lord! all the country owns that ’tis one of the strangest things in the world that such a woman should have come to live up there.”\n“Dark or fair?”\n\n“Now, though I’ve seen her twenty times, that’s a thing I cannot call to mind.”\n\n“Darker than Tamsin,” murmured Mrs. Yeobright.\n\n“A woman who seems to care for nothing at all, as you may say.”\n\n“She is melancholy, then?” inquired Clym.\n\n“She mopes about by herself, and don’t mix in with the people.”\n\n“Is she a young lady inclined for adventures?”\n\n“Not to my knowledge.”\n\n“Doesn’t join in with the lads in their games, to get some sort of excitement in this lonely place?”\n\n“No.”\n“Mumming, for instance?”\n\n“No. Her notions be different. I should rather say her thoughts were far away from here, with lords and ladies she’ll never know, and mansions she’ll never see again.”\n\nObserving that Clym appeared singularly interested Mrs. Yeobright said rather uneasily to Sam, “You see more in her than most of us do. Miss Vye is to my mind too idle to be charming. I have never heard that she is of any use to herself or to other people. Good girls don’t get treated as witches even on Egdon.”\n\n“Nonsense—that proves nothing either way,” said Yeobright.\n\n“Well, of course I don’t understand such niceties,” said Sam, withdrawing from a possibly unpleasant argument; “and what she is we must wait for time to tell us. The business that I have really called about is this, to borrow the longest and strongest rope you have. The captain’s bucket has dropped into the well, and they are in want of water; and as all the chaps are at home today we think we can get it out for him. We have three cart-ropes already, but they won’t reach to the bottom.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright told him that he might have whatever ropes he could find in the outhouse, and Sam went out to search. When he passed by the door Clym joined him, and accompanied him to the gate.\n\n“Is this young witch-lady going to stay long at Mistover?” he asked.\n\n“I should say so.”\n\n“What a cruel shame to ill-use her, She must have suffered greatly—more in mind than in body.”\n\n“’Twas a graceless trick—such a handsome girl, too. You ought to see her, Mr. Yeobright, being a young man come from far, and with a little more to show for your years than most of us.”\n\n“Do you think she would like to teach children?” said Clym.\n\nSam shook his head. “Quite a different sort of body from that, I reckon.”\n\n“O, it was merely something which occurred to me. It would of course be necessary to see her and talk it over—not an easy thing, by the way, for my family and hers are not very friendly.”\n\n“I’ll tell you how you mid see her, Mr. Yeobright,” said Sam. “We are going to grapple for the bucket at six o’clock tonight at her house, and you could lend a hand. There’s five or six coming, but the well is deep, and another might be useful, if you don’t mind appearing in that shape. She’s sure to be walking round.”\n\n“I’ll think of it,” said Yeobright; and they parted.\n\nHe thought of it a good deal; but nothing more was said about Eustacia inside the house at that time. Whether this romantic martyr to superstition and the melancholy mummer he had conversed with under the full moon were one and the same person remained as yet a problem.\n\nIII. The First Act in a Timeworn Drama\n\nThe afternoon was fine, and Yeobright walked on the heath for an hour with his mother. When they reached the lofty ridge which divided the valley of Blooms-End from the adjoining valley they stood still and looked round. The Quiet Woman Inn was visible on the low margin of the heath in one direction, and afar on the other hand rose Mistover Knap.\n\n“You mean to call on Thomasin?” he inquired.\n\n“Yes. But you need not come this time,” said his mother.\n\n“In that case I’ll branch off here, Mother. I am going to Mistover.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright turned to him inquiringly.\n\n“I am going to help them get the bucket out of the captain’s well,” he continued. “As it is so very deep I may be useful. And I should like to see this Miss Vye—not so much for her good looks as for another reason.”\n\n“Must you go?” his mother asked.\n\n“I thought to.”\n\nAnd they parted. “There is no help for it,” murmured Clym’s mother gloomily as he withdrew. “They are sure to see each other. I wish Sam would carry his news to other houses than mine.”\n\nClym’s retreating figure got smaller and smaller as it rose and fell over the hillocks on his way. “He is tender-hearted,” said Mrs. Yeobright to herself while she watched him; “otherwise it would matter little. How he’s going on!”\n\nHe was, indeed, walking with a will over the furze, as straight as a line, as if his life depended upon it. His mother drew a long breath, and, abandoning the visit to Thomasin, turned back. The evening films began to make nebulous pictures of the valleys, but the high lands still were raked by the declining rays of the winter sun, which glanced on Clym as he walked forward, eyed by every rabbit and field-fare around, a long shadow advancing in front of him.\n\nOn drawing near to the furze-covered bank and ditch which fortified the captain’s dwelling he could hear voices within, signifying that operations had been already begun. At the side-entrance gate he stopped and looked over.\n\nHalf a dozen able-bodied men were standing in a line from the well-mouth, holding a rope which passed over the well-roller into the depths below. Fairway, with a piece of smaller rope round his body, made fast to one of the standards, to guard against accidents, was leaning over the opening, his right hand clasping the vertical rope that descended into the well.\n\n“Now, silence, folks,” said Fairway.\n\nThe talking ceased, and Fairway gave a circular motion to the rope, as if he were stirring batter. At the end of a minute a dull splashing reverberated from the bottom of the well; the helical twist he had imparted to the rope had reached the grapnel below.\n\n“Haul!” said Fairway; and the men who held the rope began to gather it over the wheel.\n\n“I think we’ve got sommat,” said one of the haulers-in.\n\n“Then pull steady,” said Fairway.\n\nThey gathered up more and more, till a regular dripping into the well could be heard below. It grew smarter with the increasing height of the bucket, and presently a hundred and fifty feet of rope had been pulled in.\n\nFairway then lit a lantern, tied it to another cord, and began lowering it into the well beside the first: Clym came forward and looked down. Strange humid leaves, which knew nothing of the seasons of the year, and quaint-natured mosses were revealed on the wellside as the lantern descended; till its rays fell upon a confused mass of rope and bucket dangling in the dank, dark air.\n\n“We’ve only got en by the edge of the hoop—steady, for God’s sake!” said Fairway.\n\nThey pulled with the greatest gentleness, till the wet bucket appeared about two yards below them, like a dead friend come to earth again. Three or four hands were stretched out, then jerk went the rope, whizz went the wheel, the two foremost haulers fell backward, the beating of a falling body was heard, receding down the sides of the well, and a thunderous uproar arose at the bottom. The bucket was gone again.\n\n“Damn the bucket!” said Fairway.\n\n“Lower again,” said Sam.\n\n“I’m as stiff as a ram’s horn stooping so long,” said Fairway, standing up and stretching himself till his joints creaked.\n\n“Rest a few minutes, Timothy,” said Yeobright. “I’ll take your place.”\n\nThe grapnel was again lowered. Its smart impact upon the distant water reached their ears like a kiss, whereupon Yeobright knelt down, and leaning over the well began dragging the grapnel round and round as Fairway had done.\n\n“Tie a rope round him—it is dangerous!” cried a soft and anxious voice somewhere above them.\n\nEverybody turned. The speaker was a woman, gazing down upon the group from an upper window, whose panes blazed in the ruddy glare from the west. Her lips were parted and she appeared for the moment to forget where she was.\n\nThe rope was accordingly tied round his waist, and the work proceeded. At the next haul the weight was not heavy, and it was discovered that they had only secured a coil of the rope detached from the bucket. The tangled mass was thrown into the background. Humphrey took Yeobright’s place, and the grapnel was lowered again.\n\nYeobright retired to the heap of recovered rope in a meditative mood. Of the identity between the lady’s voice and that of the melancholy mummer he had not a moment’s doubt. “How thoughtful of her!” he said to himself.\n\nEustacia, who had reddened when she perceived the effect of her exclamation upon the group below, was no longer to be seen at the window, though Yeobright scanned it wistfully. While he stood there the men at the well succeeded in getting up the bucket without a mishap. One of them went to inquire for the captain, to learn what orders he wished to give for mending the well-tackle. The captain proved to be away from home, and Eustacia appeared at the door and came out. She had lapsed into an easy and dignified calm, far removed from the intensity of life in her words of solicitude for Clym’s safety.\n\n“Will it be possible to draw water here tonight?” she inquired.\n\n“No, miss; the bottom of the bucket is clean knocked out. And as we can do no more now we’ll leave off, and come again tomorrow morning.”\n\n“No water,” she murmured, turning away.\n\n“I can send you up some from Blooms-End,” said Clym, coming forward and raising his hat as the men retired.\n\nYeobright and Eustacia looked at each other for one instant, as if each had in mind those few moments during which a certain moonlight scene was common to both. With the glance the calm fixity of her features sublimed itself to an expression of refinement and warmth; it was like garish noon rising to the dignity of sunset in a couple of seconds.\n\n“Thank you; it will hardly be necessary,” she replied.\n\n“But if you have no water?”\n\n“Well, it is what I call no water,” she said, blushing, and lifting her long-lashed eyelids as if to lift them were a work requiring consideration. “But my grandfather calls it water enough. I’ll show you what I mean.”\n\nShe moved away a few yards, and Clym followed. When she reached the corner of the enclosure, where the steps were formed for mounting the boundary bank, she sprang up with a lightness which seemed strange after her listless movement towards the well. It incidentally showed that her apparent languor did not arise from lack of force.\n\nClym ascended behind her, and noticed a circular burnt patch at the top of the bank. “Ashes?” he said.\n\n“Yes,” said Eustacia. “We had a little bonfire here last Fifth of November, and those are the marks of it.”\n\nOn that spot had stood the fire she had kindled to attract Wildeve.\n\n“That’s the only kind of water we have,” she continued, tossing a stone into the pool, which lay on the outside of the bank like the white of an eye without its pupil. The stone fell with a flounce, but no Wildeve appeared on the other side, as on a previous occasion there. “My grandfather says he lived for more than twenty years at sea on water twice as bad as that,” she went on, “and considers it quite good enough for us here on an emergency.”\n\n“Well, as a matter of fact there are no impurities in the water of these pools at this time of the year. It has only just rained into them.”\n\nShe shook her head. “I am managing to exist in a wilderness, but I cannot drink from a pond,” she said.\n\nClym looked towards the well, which was now deserted, the men having gone home. “It is a long way to send for spring-water,” he said, after a silence. “But since you don’t like this in the pond, I’ll try to get you some myself.” He went back to the well. “Yes, I think I could do it by tying on this pail.”\n\n“But, since I would not trouble the men to get it, I cannot in conscience let you.”\n\n“I don’t mind the trouble at all.”\n\nHe made fast the pail to the long coil of rope, put it over the wheel, and allowed it to descend by letting the rope slip through his hands. Before it had gone far, however, he checked it.\n\n“I must make fast the end first, or we may lose the whole,” he said to Eustacia, who had drawn near. “Could you hold this a moment, while I do it—or shall I call your servant?”\n\n“I can hold it,” said Eustacia; and he placed the rope in her hands, going then to search for the end.\n\n“I suppose I may let it slip down?” she inquired.\n\n“I would advise you not to let it go far,” said Clym. “It will get much heavier, you will find.”\n\nHowever, Eustacia had begun to pay out. While he was tying she cried, “I cannot stop it!”\n\nClym ran to her side, and found he could only check the rope by twisting the loose part round the upright post, when it stopped with a jerk. “Has it hurt you?”\n\n“Yes,” she replied.\n\n“Very much?”\n\n“No; I think not.” She opened her hands. One of them was bleeding; the rope had dragged off the skin. Eustacia wrapped it in her handkerchief.\n\n“You should have let go,” said Yeobright. “Why didn’t you?”\n\n“You said I was to hold on.... This is the second time I have been wounded today.”\n\n“Ah, yes; I have heard of it. I blush for my native Egdon. Was it a serious injury you received in church, Miss Vye?”\n\nThere was such an abundance of sympathy in Clym’s tone that Eustacia slowly drew up her sleeve and disclosed her round white arm. A bright red spot appeared on its smooth surface, like a ruby on Parian marble.\n\n“There it is,” she said, putting her finger against the spot.\n\n“It was dastardly of the woman,” said Clym. “Will not Captain Vye get her punished?”\n\n“He is gone from home on that very business. I did not know that I had such a magic reputation.”\n\n“And you fainted?” said Clym, looking at the scarlet little puncture as if he would like to kiss it and make it well.\n\n“Yes, it frightened me. I had not been to church for a long time. And now I shall not go again for ever so long—perhaps never. I cannot face their eyes after this. Don’t you think it dreadfully humiliating? I wished I was dead for hours after, but I don’t mind now.”\n\n“I have come to clean away these cobwebs,” said Yeobright. “Would you like to help me—by high-class teaching? We might benefit them much.”\n\n“I don’t quite feel anxious to. I have not much love for my fellow-creatures. Sometimes I quite hate them.”\n\n“Still I think that if you were to hear my scheme you might take an interest in it. There is no use in hating people—if you hate anything, you should hate what produced them.”\n\n“Do you mean Nature? I hate her already. But I shall be glad to hear your scheme at any time.”\n\nThe situation had now worked itself out, and the next natural thing was for them to part. Clym knew this well enough, and Eustacia made a move of conclusion; yet he looked at her as if he had one word more to say. Perhaps if he had not lived in Paris it would never have been uttered.\n\n“We have met before,” he said, regarding her with rather more interest than was necessary.\n\n“I do not own it,” said Eustacia, with a repressed, still look.\n\n“But I may think what I like.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You are lonely here.”\n\n“I cannot endure the heath, except in its purple season. The heath is a cruel taskmaster to me.”\n\n“Can you say so?” he asked. “To my mind it is most exhilarating, and strengthening, and soothing. I would rather live on these hills than anywhere else in the world.”\n\n“It is well enough for artists; but I never would learn to draw.”\n\n“And there is a very curious druidical stone just out there.” He threw a pebble in the direction signified. “Do you often go to see it?”\n\n“I was not even aware there existed any such curious druidical stone. I am aware that there are boulevards in Paris.”\n\nYeobright looked thoughtfully on the ground. “That means much,” he said.\n\n“It does indeed,” said Eustacia.\n\n“I remember when I had the same longing for town bustle. Five years of a great city would be a perfect cure for that.”\n\n“Heaven send me such a cure! Now, Mr. Yeobright, I will go indoors and plaster my wounded hand.”\n\nThey separated, and Eustacia vanished in the increasing shade. She seemed full of many things. Her past was a blank, her life had begun. The effect upon Clym of this meeting he did not fully discover till some time after. During his walk home his most intelligible sensation was that his scheme had somehow become glorified. A beautiful woman had been intertwined with it.\n\nOn reaching the house he went up to the room which was to be made his study, and occupied himself during the evening in unpacking his books from the boxes and arranging them on shelves. From another box he drew a lamp and a can of oil. He trimmed the lamp, arranged his table, and said, “Now, I am ready to begin.”\n\nHe rose early the next morning, read two hours before breakfast by the light of his lamp—read all the morning, all the afternoon. Just when the sun was going down his eyes felt weary, and he leant back in his chair.\n\nHis room overlooked the front of the premises and the valley of the heath beyond. The lowest beams of the winter sun threw the shadow of the house over the palings, across the grass margin of the heath, and far up the vale, where the chimney outlines and those of the surrounding tree-tops stretched forth in long dark prongs. Having been seated at work all day, he decided to take a turn upon the hills before it got dark; and, going out forthwith, he struck across the heath towards Mistover.\n\nIt was an hour and a half later when he again appeared at the garden gate. The shutters of the house were closed, and Christian Cantle, who had been wheeling manure about the garden all day, had gone home. On entering he found that his mother, after waiting a long time for him, had finished her meal.\n\n“Where have you been, Clym?” she immediately said. “Why didn’t you tell me that you were going away at this time?”\n\n“I have been on the heath.”\n\n“You’ll meet Eustacia Vye if you go up there.”\n\nClym paused a minute. “Yes, I met her this evening,” he said, as though it were spoken under the sheer necessity of preserving honesty.\n\n“I wondered if you had.”\n\n“It was no appointment.”\n\n“No; such meetings never are.”\n\n“But you are not angry, Mother?”\n\n“I can hardly say that I am not. Angry? No. But when I consider the usual nature of the drag which causes men of promise to disappoint the world I feel uneasy.”\n\n“You deserve credit for the feeling, Mother. But I can assure you that you need not be disturbed by it on my account.”\n\n“When I think of you and your new crotchets,” said Mrs. Yeobright, with some emphasis, “I naturally don’t feel so comfortable as I did a twelvemonth ago. It is incredible to me that a man accustomed to the attractive women of Paris and elsewhere should be so easily worked upon by a girl in a heath. You could just as well have walked another way.”\n\n“I had been studying all day.”\n\n“Well, yes,” she added more hopefully, “I have been thinking that you might get on as a schoolmaster, and rise that way, since you really are determined to hate the course you were pursuing.”\n\nYeobright was unwilling to disturb this idea, though his scheme was far enough removed from one wherein the education of youth should be made a mere channel of social ascent. He had no desires of that sort. He had reached the stage in a young man’s life when the grimness of the general human situation first becomes clear; and the realization of this causes ambition to halt awhile. In France it is not uncustomary to commit suicide at this stage; in England we do much better, or much worse, as the case may be.\n\nThe love between the young man and his mother was strangely invisible now. Of love it may be said, the less earthly the less demonstrative. In its absolutely indestructible form it reaches a profundity in which all exhibition of itself is painful. It was so with these. Had conversations between them been overheard, people would have said, “How cold they are to each other!”\n\nHis theory and his wishes about devoting his future to teaching had made an impression on Mrs. Yeobright. Indeed, how could it be otherwise when he was a part of her—when their discourses were as if carried on between the right and the left hands of the same body? He had despaired of reaching her by argument; and it was almost as a discovery to him that he could reach her by a magnetism which was as superior to words as words are to yells.\n\nStrangely enough he began to feel now that it would not be so hard to persuade her who was his best friend that comparative poverty was essentially the higher course for him, as to reconcile to his feelings the act of persuading her. From every provident point of view his mother was so undoubtedly right, that he was not without a sickness of heart in finding he could shake her.\n\nShe had a singular insight into life, considering that she had never mixed with it. There are instances of persons who, without clear ideas of the things they criticize have yet had clear ideas of the relations of those things. Blacklock, a poet blind from his birth, could describe visual objects with accuracy; Professor Sanderson, who was also blind, gave excellent lectures on colour, and taught others the theory of ideas which they had and he had not. In the social sphere these gifted ones are mostly women; they can watch a world which they never saw, and estimate forces of which they have only heard. We call it intuition.\n\nWhat was the great world to Mrs. Yeobright? A multitude whose tendencies could be perceived, though not its essences. Communities were seen by her as from a distance; she saw them as we see the throngs which cover the canvases of Sallaert, Van Alsloot, and others of that school—vast masses of beings, jostling, zigzagging, and processioning in definite directions, but whose features are indistinguishable by the very comprehensiveness of the view.\n\nOne could see that, as far as it had gone, her life was very complete on its reflective side. The philosophy of her nature, and its limitation by circumstances, was almost written in her movements. They had a majestic foundation, though they were far from being majestic; and they had a ground-work of assurance, but they were not assured. As her once elastic walk had become deadened by time, so had her natural pride of life been hindered in its blooming by her necessities.\n\nThe next slight touch in the shaping of Clym’s destiny occurred a few days after. A barrow was opened on the heath, and Yeobright attended the operation, remaining away from his study during several hours. In the afternoon Christian returned from a journey in the same direction, and Mrs. Yeobright questioned him.\n\n“They have dug a hole, and they have found things like flowerpots upside down, Mis’ess Yeobright; and inside these be real charnel bones. They have carried ’em off to men’s houses; but I shouldn’t like to sleep where they will bide. Dead folks have been known to come and claim their own. Mr. Yeobright had got one pot of the bones, and was going to bring ’em home—real skellington bones—but ’twas ordered otherwise. You’ll be relieved to hear that he gave away his pot and all, on second thoughts; and a blessed thing for ye, Mis’ess Yeobright, considering the wind o’ nights.”\n\n“Gave it away?”\n\n“Yes. To Miss Vye. She has a cannibal taste for such churchyard furniture seemingly.”\n\n“Miss Vye was there too?”\n\n“Ay, ’a b’lieve she was.”\n\nWhen Clym came home, which was shortly after, his mother said, in a curious tone, “The urn you had meant for me you gave away.”\n\nYeobright made no reply; the current of her feeling was too pronounced to admit it.\n\nThe early weeks of the year passed on. Yeobright certainly studied at home, but he also walked much abroad, and the direction of his walk was always towards some point of a line between Mistover and Rainbarrow.\n\nThe month of March arrived, and the heath showed its first signs of awakening from winter trance. The awakening was almost feline in its stealthiness. The pool outside the bank by Eustacia’s dwelling, which seemed as dead and desolate as ever to an observer who moved and made noises in his observation, would gradually disclose a state of great animation when silently watched awhile. A timid animal world had come to life for the season. Little tadpoles and efts began to bubble up through the water, and to race along beneath it; toads made noises like very young ducks, and advanced to the margin in twos and threes; overhead, bumblebees flew hither and thither in the thickening light, their drone coming and going like the sound of a gong.\n\nOn an evening such as this Yeobright descended into the Blooms-End valley from beside that very pool, where he had been standing with another person quite silently and quite long enough to hear all this puny stir of resurrection in nature; yet he had not heard it. His walk was rapid as he came down, and he went with a springy trend. Before entering upon his mother’s premises he stopped and breathed. The light which shone forth on him from the window revealed that his face was flushed and his eye bright. What it did not show was something which lingered upon his lips like a seal set there. The abiding presence of this impress was so real that he hardly dared to enter the house, for it seemed as if his mother might say, “What red spot is that glowing upon your mouth so vividly?”\n\nBut he entered soon after. The tea was ready, and he sat down opposite his mother. She did not speak many words; and as for him, something had been just done and some words had been just said on the hill which prevented him from beginning a desultory chat. His mother’s taciturnity was not without ominousness, but he appeared not to care. He knew why she said so little, but he could not remove the cause of her bearing towards him. These half-silent sittings were far from uncommon with them now. At last Yeobright made a beginning of what was intended to strike at the whole root of the matter.\n\n“Five days have we sat like this at meals with scarcely a word. What’s the use of it, Mother?”\n\n“None,” said she, in a heart-swollen tone. “But there is only too good a reason.”\n\n“Not when you know all. I have been wanting to speak about this, and I am glad the subject is begun. The reason, of course, is Eustacia Vye. Well, I confess I have seen her lately, and have seen her a good many times.”\n\n“Yes, yes; and I know what that amounts to. It troubles me, Clym. You are wasting your life here; and it is solely on account of her. If it had not been for that woman you would never have entertained this teaching scheme at all.”\n\nClym looked hard at his mother. “You know that is not it,” he said.\n\n“Well, I know you had decided to attempt it before you saw her; but that would have ended in intentions. It was very well to talk of, but ridiculous to put in practice. I fully expected that in the course of a month or two you would have seen the folly of such self-sacrifice, and would have been by this time back again to Paris in some business or other. I can understand objections to the diamond trade—I really was thinking that it might be inadequate to the life of a man like you even though it might have made you a millionaire. But now I see how mistaken you are about this girl I doubt if you could be correct about other things.”\n\n“How am I mistaken in her?”\n\n“She is lazy and dissatisfied. But that is not all of it. Supposing her to be as good a woman as any you can find, which she certainly is not, why do you wish to connect yourself with anybody at present?”\n\n“Well, there are practical reasons,” Clym began, and then almost broke off under an overpowering sense of the weight of argument which could be brought against his statement. “If I take a school an educated woman would be invaluable as a help to me.”\n\n“What! you really mean to marry her?”\n\n“It would be premature to state that plainly. But consider what obvious advantages there would be in doing it. She——”\n\n“Don’t suppose she has any money. She hasn’t a farthing.”\n\n“She is excellently educated, and would make a good matron in a boarding-school. I candidly own that I have modified my views a little, in deference to you; and it should satisfy you. I no longer adhere to my intention of giving with my own mouth rudimentary education to the lowest class. I can do better. I can establish a good private school for farmers’ sons, and without stopping the school I can manage to pass examinations. By this means, and by the assistance of a wife like her——”\n\n“Oh, Clym!”\n\n“I shall ultimately, I hope, be at the head of one of the best schools in the county.”\n\nYeobright had enunciated the word “her” with a fervour which, in conversation with a mother, was absurdly indiscreet. Hardly a maternal heart within the four seas could in such circumstances, have helped being irritated at that ill-timed betrayal of feeling for a new woman.\n\n“You are blinded, Clym,” she said warmly. “It was a bad day for you when you first set eyes on her. And your scheme is merely a castle in the air built on purpose to justify this folly which has seized you, and to salve your conscience on the irrational situation you are in.”\n\n“Mother, that’s not true,” he firmly answered.\n\n“Can you maintain that I sit and tell untruths, when all I wish to do is to save you from sorrow? For shame, Clym! But it is all through that woman—a hussy!”\n\nClym reddened like fire and rose. He placed his hand upon his mother’s shoulder and said, in a tone which hung strangely between entreaty and command, “I won’t hear it. I may be led to answer you in a way which we shall both regret.”\n\nHis mother parted her lips to begin some other vehement truth, but on looking at him she saw that in his face which led her to leave the words unsaid. Yeobright walked once or twice across the room, and then suddenly went out of the house. It was eleven o’clock when he came in, though he had not been further than the precincts of the garden. His mother was gone to bed. A light was left burning on the table, and supper was spread. Without stopping for any food he secured the doors and went upstairs.\n\nIV. An Hour of Bliss and Many Hours of Sadness\n\nThe next day was gloomy enough at Blooms-End. Yeobright remained in his study, sitting over the open books; but the work of those hours was miserably scant. Determined that there should be nothing in his conduct towards his mother resembling sullenness, he had occasionally spoken to her on passing matters, and would take no notice of the brevity of her replies. With the same resolve to keep up a show of conversation he said, about seven o’clock in the evening, “There’s an eclipse of the moon tonight. I am going out to see it.” And, putting on his overcoat, he left her.\n\nThe low moon was not as yet visible from the front of the house, and Yeobright climbed out of the valley until he stood in the full flood of her light. But even now he walked on, and his steps were in the direction of Rainbarrow.\n\nIn half an hour he stood at the top. The sky was clear from verge to verge, and the moon flung her rays over the whole heath, but without sensibly lighting it, except where paths and water-courses had laid bare the white flints and glistening quartz sand, which made streaks upon the general shade. After standing awhile he stooped and felt the heather. It was dry, and he flung himself down upon the barrow, his face towards the moon, which depicted a small image of herself in each of his eyes.\n\nHe had often come up here without stating his purpose to his mother; but this was the first time that he had been ostensibly frank as to his purpose while really concealing it. It was a moral situation which, three months earlier, he could hardly have credited of himself. In returning to labour in this sequestered spot he had anticipated an escape from the chafing of social necessities; yet behold they were here also. More than ever he longed to be in some world where personal ambition was not the only recognized form of progress—such, perhaps, as might have been the case at some time or other in the silvery globe then shining upon him. His eye travelled over the length and breadth of that distant country—over the Bay of Rainbows, the sombre Sea of Crises, the Ocean of Storms, the Lake of Dreams, the vast Walled Plains, and the wondrous Ring Mountains—till he almost felt himself to be voyaging bodily through its wild scenes, standing on its hollow hills, traversing its deserts, descending its vales and old sea bottoms, or mounting to the edges of its craters.\n\nWhile he watched the far-removed landscape a tawny stain grew into being on the lower verge—the eclipse had begun. This marked a preconcerted moment—for the remote celestial phenomenon had been pressed into sublunary service as a lover’s signal. Yeobright’s mind flew back to earth at the sight; he arose, shook himself and listened. Minute after minute passed by, perhaps ten minutes passed, and the shadow on the moon perceptibly widened. He heard a rustling on his left hand, a cloaked figure with an upturned face appeared at the base of the Barrow, and Clym descended. In a moment the figure was in his arms, and his lips upon hers.\n\n“My Eustacia!”\n\n“Clym, dearest!”\n\nSuch a situation had less than three months brought forth.\n\nThey remained long without a single utterance, for no language could reach the level of their condition—words were as the rusty implements of a by-gone barbarous epoch, and only to be occasionally tolerated.\n\n“I began to wonder why you did not come,” said Yeobright, when she had withdrawn a little from his embrace.\n\n“You said ten minutes after the first mark of shade on the edge of the moon, and that’s what it is now.”\n\n“Well, let us only think that here we are.”\n\nThen, holding each other’s hand, they were again silent, and the shadow on the moon’s disc grew a little larger.\n\n“Has it seemed long since you last saw me?” she asked.\n\n“It has seemed sad.”\n\n“And not long? That’s because you occupy yourself, and so blind yourself to my absence. To me, who can do nothing, it has been like living under stagnant water.”\n\n“I would rather bear tediousness, dear, than have time made short by such means as have shortened mine.”\n\n“In what way is that? You have been thinking you wished you did not love me.”\n\n“How can a man wish that, and yet love on? No, Eustacia.”\n“Men can, women cannot.”\n\n“Well, whatever I may have thought, one thing is certain—I do love you—past all compass and description. I love you to oppressiveness—I, who have never before felt more than a pleasant passing fancy for any woman I have ever seen. Let me look right into your moonlit face and dwell on every line and curve in it! Only a few hairbreadths make the difference between this face and faces I have seen many times before I knew you; yet what a difference—the difference between everything and nothing at all. One touch on that mouth again! there, and there, and there. Your eyes seem heavy, Eustacia.”\n\n“No, it is my general way of looking. I think it arises from my feeling sometimes an agonizing pity for myself that I ever was born.”\n\n“You don’t feel it now?”\n\n“No. Yet I know that we shall not love like this always. Nothing can ensure the continuance of love. It will evaporate like a spirit, and so I feel full of fears.”\n\n“You need not.”\n\n“Ah, you don’t know. You have seen more than I, and have been into cities and among people that I have only heard of, and have lived more years than I; but yet I am older at this than you. I loved another man once, and now I love you.”\n\n“In God’s mercy don’t talk so, Eustacia!”\n\n“But I do not think I shall be the one who wearies first. It will, I fear, end in this way: your mother will find out that you meet me, and she will influence you against me!”\n\n“That can never be. She knows of these meetings already.”\n\n“And she speaks against me?”\n\n“I will not say.”\n\n“There, go away! Obey her. I shall ruin you. It is foolish of you to meet me like this. Kiss me, and go away forever. Forever—do you hear?—forever!”\n\n“Not I.”\n“It is your only chance. Many a man’s love has been a curse to him.”\n\n“You are desperate, full of fancies, and wilful; and you misunderstand. I have an additional reason for seeing you tonight besides love of you. For though, unlike you, I feel our affection may be eternal. I feel with you in this, that our present mode of existence cannot last.”\n\n“Oh! ’tis your mother. Yes, that’s it! I knew it.”\n\n“Never mind what it is. Believe this, I cannot let myself lose you. I must have you always with me. This very evening I do not like to let you go. There is only one cure for this anxiety, dearest—you must be my wife.”\n\nShe started—then endeavoured to say calmly, “Cynics say that cures the anxiety by curing the love.”\n\n“But you must answer me. Shall I claim you some day—I don’t mean at once?”\n\n“I must think,” Eustacia murmured. “At present speak of Paris to me. Is there any place like it on earth?”\n\n“It is very beautiful. But will you be mine?”\n\n“I will be nobody else’s in the world—does that satisfy you?”\n\n“Yes, for the present.”\n\n“Now tell me of the Tuileries, and the Louvre,” she continued evasively.\n\n“I hate talking of Paris! Well, I remember one sunny room in the Louvre which would make a fitting place for you to live in—the Galerie d’Apollon. Its windows are mainly east; and in the early morning, when the sun is bright, the whole apartment is in a perfect blaze of splendour. The rays bristle and dart from the encrustations of gilding to the magnificent inlaid coffers, from the coffers to the gold and silver plate, from the plate to the jewels and precious stones, from these to the enamels, till there is a perfect network of light which quite dazzles the eye. But now, about our marriage——”\n\n“And Versailles—the King’s Gallery is some such gorgeous room, is it not?”\n\n“Yes. But what’s the use of talking of gorgeous rooms? By the way, the Little Trianon would suit us beautifully to live in, and you might walk in the gardens in the moonlight and think you were in some English shrubbery; it is laid out in English fashion.”\n\n“I should hate to think that!”\n\n“Then you could keep to the lawn in front of the Grand Palace. All about there you would doubtless feel in a world of historical romance.”\nHe went on, since it was all new to her, and described Fontainebleau, St. Cloud, the Bois, and many other familiar haunts of the Parisians; till she said—\n\n“When used you to go to these places?”\n\n“On Sundays.”\n\n“Ah, yes. I dislike English Sundays. How I should chime in with their manners over there! Dear Clym, you’ll go back again?”\n\nClym shook his head, and looked at the eclipse.\n\n“If you’ll go back again I’ll—be something,” she said tenderly, putting her head near his breast. “If you’ll agree I’ll give my promise, without making you wait a minute longer.”\n\n“How extraordinary that you and my mother should be of one mind about this!” said Yeobright. “I have vowed not to go back, Eustacia. It is not the place I dislike; it is the occupation.”\n\n“But you can go in some other capacity.”\n\n“No. Besides, it would interfere with my scheme. Don’t press that, Eustacia. Will you marry me?”\n\n“I cannot tell.”\n\n“Now—never mind Paris; it is no better than other spots. Promise, sweet!”\n\n“You will never adhere to your education plan, I am quite sure; and then it will be all right for me; and so I promise to be yours for ever and ever.”\n\nClym brought her face towards his by a gentle pressure of the hand, and kissed her.\n\n“Ah! but you don’t know what you have got in me,” she said. “Sometimes I think there is not that in Eustacia Vye which will make a good homespun wife. Well, let it go—see how our time is slipping, slipping, slipping!” She pointed towards the half-eclipsed moon.\n\n“You are too mournful.”\n\n“No. Only I dread to think of anything beyond the present. What is, we know. We are together now, and it is unknown how long we shall be so; the unknown always fills my mind with terrible possibilities, even when I may reasonably expect it to be cheerful.... Clym, the eclipsed moonlight shines upon your face with a strange foreign colour, and shows its shape as if it were cut out in gold. That means that you should be doing better things than this.”\n\n“You are ambitious, Eustacia—no, not exactly ambitious, luxurious. I ought to be of the same vein, to make you happy, I suppose. And yet, far from that, I could live and die in a hermitage here, with proper work to do.”\n\nThere was that in his tone which implied distrust of his position as a solicitous lover, a doubt if he were acting fairly towards one whose tastes touched his own only at rare and infrequent points. She saw his meaning, and whispered, in a low, full accent of eager assurance, “Don’t mistake me, Clym—though I should like Paris, I love you for yourself alone. To be your wife and live in Paris would be heaven to me; but I would rather live with you in a hermitage here than not be yours at all. It is gain to me either way, and very great gain. There’s my too candid confession.”\n\n“Spoken like a woman. And now I must soon leave you. I’ll walk with you towards your house.”\n\n“But must you go home yet?” she asked. “Yes, the sand has nearly slipped away, I see, and the eclipse is creeping on more and more. Don’t go yet! Stop till the hour has run itself out; then I will not press you any more. You will go home and sleep well; I keep sighing in my sleep! Do you ever dream of me?”\n\n“I cannot recollect a clear dream of you.”\n\n“I see your face in every scene of my dreams, and hear your voice in every sound. I wish I did not. It is too much what I feel. They say such love never lasts. But it must! And yet once, I remember, I saw an officer of the Hussars ride down the street at Budmouth, and though he was a total stranger and never spoke to me, I loved him till I thought I should really die of love—but I didn’t die, and at last I left off caring for him. How terrible it would be if a time should come when I could not love you, my Clym!”\n\n“Please don’t say such reckless things. When we see such a time at hand we will say, ‘I have outlived my faith and purpose,’ and die. There, the hour has expired—now let us walk on.”\n\nHand in hand they went along the path towards Mistover. When they were near the house he said, “It is too late for me to see your grandfather tonight. Do you think he will object to it?”\n\n“I will speak to him. I am so accustomed to be my own mistress that it did not occur to me that we should have to ask him.”\n\nThen they lingeringly separated, and Clym descended towards Blooms-End.\nAnd as he walked further and further from the charmed atmosphere of his Olympian girl his face grew sad with a new sort of sadness. A perception of the dilemma in which his love had placed him came back in full force. In spite of Eustacia’s apparent willingness to wait through the period of an unpromising engagement, till he should be established in his new pursuit, he could not but perceive at moments that she loved him rather as a visitant from a gay world to which she rightly belonged than as a man with a purpose opposed to that recent past of his which so interested her. It meant that, though she made no conditions as to his return to the French capital, this was what she secretly longed for in the event of marriage; and it robbed him of many an otherwise pleasant hour. Along with that came the widening breach between himself and his mother. Whenever any little occurrence had brought into more prominence than usual the disappointment that he was causing her it had sent him on lone and moody walks; or he was kept awake a great part of the night by the turmoil of spirit which such a recognition created. If Mrs. Yeobright could only have been led to see what a sound and worthy purpose this purpose of his was and how little it was being affected by his devotions to Eustacia, how differently would she regard him!\n\nThus as his sight grew accustomed to the first blinding halo kindled about him by love and beauty, Yeobright began to perceive what a strait he was in. Sometimes he wished that he had never known Eustacia, immediately to retract the wish as brutal. Three antagonistic growths had to be kept alive: his mother’s trust in him, his plan for becoming a teacher, and Eustacia’s happiness. His fervid nature could not afford to relinquish one of these, though two of the three were as many as he could hope to preserve. Though his love was as chaste as that of Petrarch for his Laura, it had made fetters of what previously was only a difficulty. A position which was not too simple when he stood whole-hearted had become indescribably complicated by the addition of Eustacia. Just when his mother was beginning to tolerate one scheme he had introduced another still bitterer than the first, and the combination was more than she could bear.\n\nV. Sharp Words Are Spoken, and a Crisis Ensues\n\nWhen Yeobright was not with Eustacia he was sitting slavishly over his books; when he was not reading he was meeting her. These meetings were carried on with the greatest secrecy.\n\nOne afternoon his mother came home from a morning visit to Thomasin. He could see from a disturbance in the lines of her face that something had happened.\n\n“I have been told an incomprehensible thing,” she said mournfully. “The captain has let out at the Woman that you and Eustacia Vye are engaged to be married.”\n\n“We are,” said Yeobright. “But it may not be yet for a very long time.”\n\n“I should hardly think it _would_ be yet for a very long time! You will take her to Paris, I suppose?” She spoke with weary hopelessness.\n\n“I am not going back to Paris.”\n\n“What will you do with a wife, then?”\n\n“Keep a school in Budmouth, as I have told you.”\n\n“That’s incredible! The place is overrun with schoolmasters. You have no special qualifications. What possible chance is there for such as you?”\n\n“There is no chance of getting rich. But with my system of education, which is as new as it is true, I shall do a great deal of good to my fellow-creatures.”\n\n“Dreams, dreams! If there had been any system left to be invented they would have found it out at the universities long before this time.”\n\n“Never, Mother. They cannot find it out, because their teachers don’t come in contact with the class which demands such a system—that is, those who have had no preliminary training. My plan is one for instilling high knowledge into empty minds without first cramming them with what has to be uncrammed again before true study begins.”\n\n“I might have believed you if you had kept yourself free from entanglements; but this woman—if she had been a good girl it would have been bad enough; but being——”\n\n“She is a good girl.”\n\n“So you think. A Corfu bandmaster’s daughter! What has her life been? Her surname even is not her true one.”\n\n“She is Captain Vye’s granddaughter, and her father merely took her mother’s name. And she is a lady by instinct.”\n\n“They call him ‘captain,’ but anybody is captain.”\n\n“He was in the Royal Navy!”\n\n“No doubt he has been to sea in some tub or other. Why doesn’t he look after her? No lady would rove about the heath at all hours of the day and night as she does. But that’s not all of it. There was something queer between her and Thomasin’s husband at one time—I am as sure of it as that I stand here.”\n\n“Eustacia has told me. He did pay her a little attention a year ago; but there’s no harm in that. I like her all the better.”\n\n“Clym,” said his mother with firmness, “I have no proofs against her, unfortunately. But if she makes you a good wife, there has never been a bad one.”\n\n“Believe me, you are almost exasperating,” said Yeobright vehemently. “And this very day I had intended to arrange a meeting between you. But you give me no peace; you try to thwart my wishes in everything.”\n\n“I hate the thought of any son of mine marrying badly! I wish I had never lived to see this; it is too much for me—it is more than I dreamt!” She turned to the window. Her breath was coming quickly, and her lips were pale, parted, and trembling.\n\n“Mother,” said Clym, “whatever you do, you will always be dear to me—that you know. But one thing I have a right to say, which is, that at my age I am old enough to know what is best for me.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright remained for some time silent and shaken, as if she could say no more. Then she replied, “Best? Is it best for you to injure your prospects for such a voluptuous, idle woman as that? Don’t you see that by the very fact of your choosing her you prove that you do not know what is best for you? You give up your whole thought—you set your whole soul—to please a woman.”\n\n“I do. And that woman is you.”\n\n“How can you treat me so flippantly!” said his mother, turning again to him with a tearful look. “You are unnatural, Clym, and I did not expect it.”\n\n“Very likely,” said he cheerlessly. “You did not know the measure you were going to mete me, and therefore did not know the measure that would be returned to you again.”\n\n“You answer me; you think only of her. You stick to her in all things.”\n\n“That proves her to be worthy. I have never yet supported what is bad. And I do not care only for her. I care for you and for myself, and for anything that is good. When a woman once dislikes another she is merciless!”\n\n“O Clym! please don’t go setting down as my fault what is your obstinate wrongheadedness. If you wished to connect yourself with an unworthy person why did you come home here to do it? Why didn’t you do it in Paris?—it is more the fashion there. You have come only to distress me, a lonely woman, and shorten my days! I wish that you would bestow your presence where you bestow your love!”\n\nClym said huskily, “You are my mother. I will say no more—beyond this, that I beg your pardon for having thought this my home. I will no longer inflict myself upon you; I’ll go.” And he went out with tears in his eyes.\n\nIt was a sunny afternoon at the beginning of summer, and the moist hollows of the heath had passed from their brown to their green stage. Yeobright walked to the edge of the basin which extended down from Mistover and Rainbarrow.\n\nBy this time he was calm, and he looked over the landscape. In the minor valleys, between the hillocks which diversified the contour of the vale, the fresh young ferns were luxuriantly growing up, ultimately to reach a height of five or six feet. He descended a little way, flung himself down in a spot where a path emerged from one of the small hollows, and waited. Hither it was that he had promised Eustacia to bring his mother this afternoon, that they might meet and be friends. His attempt had utterly failed.\n\nHe was in a nest of vivid green. The ferny vegetation round him, though so abundant, was quite uniform—it was a grove of machine-made foliage, a world of green triangles with saw-edges, and not a single flower. The air was warm with a vaporous warmth, and the stillness was unbroken. Lizards, grasshoppers, and ants were the only living things to be beheld. The scene seemed to belong to the ancient world of the carboniferous period, when the forms of plants were few, and of the fern kind; when there was neither bud nor blossom, nothing but a monotonous extent of leafage, amid which no bird sang.\n\nWhen he had reclined for some considerable time, gloomily pondering, he discerned above the ferns a drawn bonnet of white silk approaching from the left, and Yeobright knew directly that it covered the head of her he loved. His heart awoke from its apathy to a warm excitement, and, jumping to his feet, he said aloud, “I knew she was sure to come.”\n\nShe vanished in a hollow for a few moments, and then her whole form unfolded itself from the brake.\n\n“Only you here?” she exclaimed, with a disappointed air, whose hollowness was proved by her rising redness and her half-guilty low laugh. “Where is Mrs. Yeobright?”\n\n“She has not come,” he replied in a subdued tone.\n\n“I wish I had known that you would be here alone,” she said seriously, “and that we were going to have such an idle, pleasant time as this. Pleasure not known beforehand is half wasted; to anticipate it is to double it. I have not thought once today of having you all to myself this afternoon, and the actual moment of a thing is so soon gone.”\n\n“It is indeed.”\n\n“Poor Clym!” she continued, looking tenderly into his face. “You are sad. Something has happened at your home. Never mind what is—let us only look at what seems.”\n\n“But, darling, what shall we do?” said he.\n\n“Still go on as we do now—just live on from meeting to meeting, never minding about another day. You, I know, are always thinking of that—I can see you are. But you must not—will you, dear Clym?”\n\n“You are just like all women. They are ever content to build their lives on any incidental position that offers itself; whilst men would fain make a globe to suit them. Listen to this, Eustacia. There is a subject I have determined to put off no longer. Your sentiment on the wisdom of _Carpe diem_ does not impress me today. Our present mode of life must shortly be brought to an end.”\n\n“It is your mother!”\n\n“It is. I love you none the less in telling you; it is only right you should know.”\n\n“I have feared my bliss,” she said, with the merest motion of her lips. “It has been too intense and consuming.”\n\n“There is hope yet. There are forty years of work in me yet, and why should you despair? I am only at an awkward turning. I wish people wouldn’t be so ready to think that there is no progress without uniformity.”\n\n“Ah—your mind runs off to the philosophical side of it. Well, these sad and hopeless obstacles are welcome in one sense, for they enable us to look with indifference upon the cruel satires that Fate loves to indulge in. I have heard of people, who, upon coming suddenly into happiness, have died from anxiety lest they should not live to enjoy it. I felt myself in that whimsical state of uneasiness lately; but I shall be spared it now. Let us walk on.”\n\nClym took the hand which was already bared for him—it was a favourite way with them to walk bare hand in bare hand—and led her through the ferns. They formed a very comely picture of love at full flush, as they walked along the valley that late afternoon, the sun sloping down on their right, and throwing their thin spectral shadows, tall as poplar trees, far out across the furze and fern. Eustacia went with her head thrown back fancifully, a certain glad and voluptuous air of triumph pervading her eyes at having won by her own unaided self a man who was her perfect complement in attainment, appearance, and age. On the young man’s part, the paleness of face which he had brought with him from Paris, and the incipient marks of time and thought, were less perceptible than when he returned, the healthful and energetic sturdiness which was his by nature having partially recovered its original proportions. They wandered onward till they reached the nether margin of the heath, where it became marshy and merged in moorland.\n\n“I must part from you here, Clym,” said Eustacia.\n\nThey stood still and prepared to bid each other farewell. Everything before them was on a perfect level. The sun, resting on the horizon line, streamed across the ground from between copper-coloured and lilac clouds, stretched out in flats beneath a sky of pale soft green. All dark objects on the earth that lay towards the sun were overspread by a purple haze, against which groups of wailing gnats shone out, rising upwards and dancing about like sparks of fire.\n\n“O! this leaving you is too hard to bear!” exclaimed Eustacia in a sudden whisper of anguish. “Your mother will influence you too much; I shall not be judged fairly, it will get afloat that I am not a good girl, and the witch story will be added to make me blacker!”\n\n“They cannot. Nobody dares to speak disrespectfully of you or of me.”\n\n“Oh how I wish I was sure of never losing you—that you could not be able to desert me anyhow!”\n\nClym stood silent a moment. His feelings were high, the moment was passionate, and he cut the knot.\n\n“You shall be sure of me, darling,” he said, folding her in his arms. “We will be married at once.”\n\n“O Clym!”\n\n“Do you agree to it?”\n“If—if we can.”\n\n“We certainly can, both being of full age. And I have not followed my occupation all these years without having accumulated money; and if you will agree to live in a tiny cottage somewhere on the heath, until I take a house in Budmouth for the school, we can do it at a very little expense.”\n\n“How long shall we have to live in the tiny cottage, Clym?”\n\n“About six months. At the end of that time I shall have finished my reading—yes, we will do it, and this heart-aching will be over. We shall, of course, live in absolute seclusion, and our married life will only begin to outward view when we take the house in Budmouth, where I have already addressed a letter on the matter. Would your grandfather allow you?”\n\n“I think he would—on the understanding that it should not last longer than six months.”\n\n“I will guarantee that, if no misfortune happens.”\n\n“If no misfortune happens,” she repeated slowly.\n\n“Which is not likely. Dearest, fix the exact day.”\n\nAnd then they consulted on the question, and the day was chosen. It was to be a fortnight from that time.\n\nThis was the end of their talk, and Eustacia left him. Clym watched her as she retired towards the sun. The luminous rays wrapped her up with her increasing distance, and the rustle of her dress over the sprouting sedge and grass died away. As he watched, the dead flat of the scenery overpowered him, though he was fully alive to the beauty of that untarnished early summer green which was worn for the nonce by the poorest blade. There was something in its oppressive horizontality which too much reminded him of the arena of life; it gave him a sense of bare equality with, and no superiority to, a single living thing under the sun.\n\nEustacia was now no longer the goddess but the woman to him, a being to fight for, support, help, be maligned for. Now that he had reached a cooler moment he would have preferred a less hasty marriage; but the card was laid, and he determined to abide by the game. Whether Eustacia was to add one other to the list of those who love too hotly to love long and well, the forthcoming event was certainly a ready way of proving.\n\nVI. Yeobright Goes, and the Breach Is Complete\n\nAll that evening smart sounds denoting an active packing up came from Yeobright’s room to the ears of his mother downstairs.\n\nNext morning he departed from the house and again proceeded across the heath. A long day’s march was before him, his object being to secure a dwelling to which he might take Eustacia when she became his wife. Such a house, small, secluded, and with its windows boarded up, he had casually observed a month earlier, about two miles beyond the village of East Egdon, and six miles distant altogether; and thither he directed his steps today.\n\nThe weather was far different from that of the evening before. The yellow and vapoury sunset which had wrapped up Eustacia from his parting gaze had presaged change. It was one of those not infrequent days of an English June which are as wet and boisterous as November. The cold clouds hastened on in a body, as if painted on a moving slide. Vapours from other continents arrived upon the wind, which curled and parted round him as he walked on.\n\nAt length Clym reached the margin of a fir and beech plantation that had been enclosed from heath-land in the year of his birth. Here the trees, laden heavily with their new and humid leaves, were now suffering more damage than during the highest winds of winter, when the boughs are especially disencumbered to do battle with the storm. The wet young beeches were undergoing amputations, bruises, cripplings, and harsh lacerations, from which the wasting sap would bleed for many a day to come, and which would leave scars visible till the day of their burning. Each stem was wrenched at the root, where it moved like a bone in its socket, and at every onset of the gale convulsive sounds came from the branches, as if pain were felt. In a neighbouring brake a finch was trying to sing; but the wind blew under his feathers till they stood on end, twisted round his little tail, and made him give up his song.\n\nYet a few yards to Yeobright’s left, on the open heath, how ineffectively gnashed the storm! Those gusts which tore the trees merely waved the furze and heather in a light caress. Egdon was made for such times as these.\n\nYeobright reached the empty house about midday. It was almost as lonely as that of Eustacia’s grandfather, but the fact that it stood near a heath was disguised by a belt of firs which almost enclosed the premises. He journeyed on about a mile further to the village in which the owner lived, and, returning with him to the house, arrangements were completed, and the man undertook that one room at least should be ready for occupation the next day. Clym’s intention was to live there alone until Eustacia should join him on their wedding-day.\n\nThen he turned to pursue his way homeward through the drizzle that had so greatly transformed the scene. The ferns, among which he had lain in comfort yesterday, were dripping moisture from every frond, wetting his legs through as he brushed past; and the fur of the rabbits leaping before him was clotted into dark locks by the same watery surrounding.\n\nHe reached home damp and weary enough after his ten-mile walk. It had hardly been a propitious beginning, but he had chosen his course, and would show no swerving. The evening and the following morning were spent in concluding arrangements for his departure. To stay at home a minute longer than necessary after having once come to his determination would be, he felt, only to give new pain to his mother by some word, look, or deed.\n\nHe had hired a conveyance and sent off his goods by two o’clock that day. The next step was to get some furniture, which, after serving for temporary use in the cottage, would be available for the house at Budmouth when increased by goods of a better description. A mart extensive enough for the purpose existed at Anglebury, some miles beyond the spot chosen for his residence, and there he resolved to pass the coming night.\n\nIt now only remained to wish his mother good-bye. She was sitting by the window as usual when he came downstairs.\n\n“Mother, I am going to leave you,” he said, holding out his hand.\n\n“I thought you were, by your packing,” replied Mrs. Yeobright in a voice from which every particle of emotion was painfully excluded.\n\n“And you will part friends with me?”\n\n“Certainly, Clym.”\n\n“I am going to be married on the twenty-fifth.”\n\n“I thought you were going to be married.”\n\n“And then—and then you must come and see us. You will understand me better after that, and our situation will not be so wretched as it is now.”\n\n“I do not think it likely I shall come to see you.”\n\n“Then it will not be my fault or Eustacia’s, Mother. Good-bye!”\n\nHe kissed her cheek, and departed in great misery, which was several hours in lessening itself to a controllable level. The position had been such that nothing more could be said without, in the first place, breaking down a barrier; and that was not to be done.\n\nNo sooner had Yeobright gone from his mother’s house than her face changed its rigid aspect for one of blank despair. After a while she wept, and her tears brought some relief. During the rest of the day she did nothing but walk up and down the garden path in a state bordering on stupefaction. Night came, and with it but little rest. The next day, with an instinct to do something which should reduce prostration to mournfulness, she went to her son’s room, and with her own hands arranged it in order, for an imaginary time when he should return again. She gave some attention to her flowers, but it was perfunctorily bestowed, for they no longer charmed her.\n\nIt was a great relief when, early in the afternoon, Thomasin paid her an unexpected visit. This was not the first meeting between the relatives since Thomasin’s marriage; and past blunders having been in a rough way rectified, they could always greet each other with pleasure and ease.\n\nThe oblique band of sunlight which followed her through the door became the young wife well. It illuminated her as her presence illuminated the heath. In her movements, in her gaze, she reminded the beholder of the feathered creatures who lived around her home. All similes and allegories concerning her began and ended with birds. There was as much variety in her motions as in their flight. When she was musing she was a kestrel, which hangs in the air by an invisible motion of its wings. When she was in a high wind her light body was blown against trees and banks like a heron’s. When she was frightened she darted noiselessly like a kingfisher. When she was serene she skimmed like a swallow, and that is how she was moving now.\n\n“You are looking very blithe, upon my word, Tamsie,” said Mrs. Yeobright, with a sad smile. “How is Damon?”\n\n“He is very well.”\n\n“Is he kind to you, Thomasin?” And Mrs. Yeobright observed her narrowly.\n\n“Pretty fairly.”\n\n“Is that honestly said?”\n\n“Yes, Aunt. I would tell you if he were unkind.” She added, blushing, and with hesitation, “He—I don’t know if I ought to complain to you about this, but I am not quite sure what to do. I want some money, you know, Aunt—some to buy little things for myself—and he doesn’t give me any. I don’t like to ask him; and yet, perhaps, he doesn’t give it me because he doesn’t know. Ought I to mention it to him, Aunt?”\n\n“Of course you ought. Have you never said a word on the matter?”\n\n“You see, I had some of my own,” said Thomasin evasively, “and I have not wanted any of his until lately. I did just say something about it last week; but he seems—not to remember.”\n\n“He must be made to remember. You are aware that I have a little box full of spade-guineas, which your uncle put into my hands to divide between yourself and Clym whenever I chose. Perhaps the time has come when it should be done. They can be turned into sovereigns at any moment.”\n\n“I think I should like to have my share—that is, if you don’t mind.”\n\n“You shall, if necessary. But it is only proper that you should first tell your husband distinctly that you are without any, and see what he will do.”\n\n“Very well, I will.... Aunt, I have heard about Clym. I know you are in trouble about him, and that’s why I have come.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright turned away, and her features worked in her attempt to conceal her feelings. Then she ceased to make any attempt, and said, weeping, “O Thomasin, do you think he hates me? How can he bear to grieve me so, when I have lived only for him through all these years?”\n\n“Hate you—no,” said Thomasin soothingly. “It is only that he loves her too well. Look at it quietly—do. It is not so very bad of him. Do you know, I thought it not the worst match he could have made. Miss Vye’s family is a good one on her mother’s side; and her father was a romantic wanderer—a sort of Greek Ulysses.”\n\n“It is no use, Thomasin; it is no use. Your intention is good; but I will not trouble you to argue. I have gone through the whole that can be said on either side times, and many times. Clym and I have not parted in anger; we have parted in a worse way. It is not a passionate quarrel that would have broken my heart; it is the steady opposition and persistence in going wrong that he has shown. O Thomasin, he was so good as a little boy—so tender and kind!”\n\n“He was, I know.”\n\n“I did not think one whom I called mine would grow up to treat me like this. He spoke to me as if I opposed him to injure him. As though I could wish him ill!”\n\n“There are worse women in the world than Eustacia Vye.”\n\n“There are too many better that’s the agony of it. It was she, Thomasin, and she only, who led your husband to act as he did—I would swear it!”\n\n“No,” said Thomasin eagerly. “It was before he knew me that he thought of her, and it was nothing but a mere flirtation.”\n\n“Very well; we will let it be so. There is little use in unravelling that now. Sons must be blind if they will. Why is it that a woman can see from a distance what a man cannot see close? Clym must do as he will—he is nothing more to me. And this is maternity—to give one’s best years and best love to ensure the fate of being despised!”\n\n“You are too unyielding. Think how many mothers there are whose sons have brought them to public shame by real crimes before you feel so deeply a case like this.”\n\n“Thomasin, don’t lecture me—I can’t have it. It is the excess above what we expect that makes the force of the blow, and that may not be greater in their case than in mine—they may have foreseen the worst.... I am wrongly made, Thomasin,” she added, with a mournful smile. “Some widows can guard against the wounds their children give them by turning their hearts to another husband and beginning life again. But I always was a poor, weak, one-idea’d creature—I had not the compass of heart nor the enterprise for that. Just as forlorn and stupefied as I was when my husband’s spirit flew away I have sat ever since—never attempting to mend matters at all. I was comparatively a young woman then, and I might have had another family by this time, and have been comforted by them for the failure of this one son.”\n\n“It is more noble in you that you did not.”\n\n“The more noble, the less wise.”\n\n“Forget it, and be soothed, dear Aunt. And I shall not leave you alone for long. I shall come and see you every day.”\n\nAnd for one week Thomasin literally fulfilled her word. She endeavoured to make light of the wedding; and brought news of the preparations, and that she was invited to be present. The next week she was rather unwell, and did not appear. Nothing had as yet been done about the guineas, for Thomasin feared to address her husband again on the subject, and Mrs. Yeobright had insisted upon this.\n\nOne day just before this time Wildeve was standing at the door of the Quiet Woman. In addition to the upward path through the heath to Rainbarrow and Mistover, there was a road which branched from the highway a short distance below the inn, and ascended to Mistover by a circuitous and easy incline. This was the only route on that side for vehicles to the captain’s retreat. A light cart from the nearest town descended the road, and the lad who was driving pulled up in front of the inn for something to drink.\n\n“You come from Mistover?” said Wildeve.\n\n“Yes. They are taking in good things up there. Going to be a wedding.” And the driver buried his face in his mug.\n\nWildeve had not received an inkling of the fact before, and a sudden expression of pain overspread his face. He turned for a moment into the passage to hide it. Then he came back again.\n\n“Do you mean Miss Vye?” he said. “How is it—that she can be married so soon?”\n\n“By the will of God and a ready young man, I suppose.”\n\n“You don’t mean Mr. Yeobright?”\n\n“Yes. He has been creeping about with her all the spring.”\n\n“I suppose—she was immensely taken with him?”\n\n“She is crazy about him, so their general servant of all work tells me. And that lad Charley that looks after the horse is all in a daze about it. The stun-poll has got fond-like of her.”\n\n“Is she lively—is she glad? Going to be married so soon—well!”\n\n“It isn’t so very soon.”\n\n“No; not so very soon.”\n\nWildeve went indoors to the empty room, a curious heartache within him. He rested his elbow upon the mantelpiece and his face upon his hand. When Thomasin entered the room he did not tell her of what he had heard. The old longing for Eustacia had reappeared in his soul—and it was mainly because he had discovered that it was another man’s intention to possess her.\n\nTo be yearning for the difficult, to be weary of that offered; to care for the remote, to dislike the near; it was Wildeve’s nature always. This is the true mark of the man of sentiment. Though Wildeve’s fevered feeling had not been elaborated to real poetical compass, it was of the standard sort. His might have been called the Rousseau of Egdon.\n\nVII. The Morning and the Evening of a Day\n\nThe wedding morning came. Nobody would have imagined from appearances that Blooms-End had any interest in Mistover that day. A solemn stillness prevailed around the house of Clym’s mother, and there was no more animation indoors. Mrs. Yeobright, who had declined to attend the ceremony, sat by the breakfast table in the old room which communicated immediately with the porch, her eyes listlessly directed towards the open door. It was the room in which, six months earlier, the merry Christmas party had met, to which Eustacia came secretly and as a stranger. The only living thing that entered now was a sparrow; and seeing no movements to cause alarm, he hopped boldly round the room, endeavoured to go out by the window, and fluttered among the pot-flowers. This roused the lonely sitter, who got up, released the bird, and went to the door. She was expecting Thomasin, who had written the night before to state that the time had come when she would wish to have the money and that she would if possible call this day.\n\nYet Thomasin occupied Mrs. Yeobright’s thoughts but slightly as she looked up the valley of the heath, alive with butterflies, and with grasshoppers whose husky noises on every side formed a whispered chorus. A domestic drama, for which the preparations were now being made a mile or two off, was but little less vividly present to her eyes than if enacted before her. She tried to dismiss the vision, and walked about the garden plot; but her eyes ever and anon sought out the direction of the parish church to which Mistover belonged, and her excited fancy clove the hills which divided the building from her eyes. The morning wore away. Eleven o’clock struck—could it be that the wedding was then in progress? It must be so. She went on imagining the scene at the church, which he had by this time approached with his bride. She pictured the little group of children by the gate as the pony carriage drove up in which, as Thomasin had learnt, they were going to perform the short journey. Then she saw them enter and proceed to the chancel and kneel; and the service seemed to go on.\n\nShe covered her face with her hands. “O, it is a mistake!” she groaned. “And he will rue it some day, and think of me!”\n\nWhile she remained thus, overcome by her forebodings, the old clock indoors whizzed forth twelve strokes. Soon after, faint sounds floated to her ear from afar over the hills. The breeze came from that quarter, and it had brought with it the notes of distant bells, gaily starting off in a peal: one, two, three, four, five. The ringers at East Egdon were announcing the nuptials of Eustacia and her son.\n\n“Then it is over,” she murmured. “Well, well! and life too will be over soon. And why should I go on scalding my face like this? Cry about one thing in life, cry about all; one thread runs through the whole piece. And yet we say, ‘a time to laugh!’”\n\nTowards evening Wildeve came. Since Thomasin’s marriage Mrs. Yeobright had shown him that grim friendliness which at last arises in all such cases of undesired affinity. The vision of what ought to have been is thrown aside in sheer weariness, and browbeaten human endeavour listlessly makes the best of the fact that is. Wildeve, to do him justice, had behaved very courteously to his wife’s aunt; and it was with no surprise that she saw him enter now.\n\n“Thomasin has not been able to come, as she promised to do,” he replied to her inquiry, which had been anxious, for she knew that her niece was badly in want of money. “The captain came down last night and personally pressed her to join them today. So, not to be unpleasant, she determined to go. They fetched her in the pony-chaise, and are going to bring her back.”\n\n“Then it is done,” said Mrs. Yeobright. “Have they gone to their new home?”\n\n“I don’t know. I have had no news from Mistover since Thomasin left to go.”\n\n“You did not go with her?” said she, as if there might be good reasons why.\n\n“I could not,” said Wildeve, reddening slightly. “We could not both leave the house; it was rather a busy morning, on account of Anglebury Great Market. I believe you have something to give to Thomasin? If you like, I will take it.”\n\nMrs. Yeobright hesitated, and wondered if Wildeve knew what the something was. “Did she tell you of this?” she inquired.\n\n“Not particularly. She casually dropped a remark about having arranged to fetch some article or other.”\n\n“It is hardly necessary to send it. She can have it whenever she chooses to come.”\n\n“That won’t be yet. In the present state of her health she must not go on walking so much as she has done.” He added, with a faint twang of sarcasm, “What wonderful thing is it that I cannot be trusted to take?”\n\n“Nothing worth troubling you with.”\n\n“One would think you doubted my honesty,” he said, with a laugh, though his colour rose in a quick resentfulness frequent with him.\n\n“You need think no such thing,” said she drily. “It is simply that I, in common with the rest of the world, feel that there are certain things which had better be done by certain people than by others.”\n\n“As you like, as you like,” said Wildeve laconically. “It is not worth arguing about. Well, I think I must turn homeward again, as the inn must not be left long in charge of the lad and the maid only.”\n\nHe went his way, his farewell being scarcely so courteous as his greeting. But Mrs. Yeobright knew him thoroughly by this time, and took little notice of his manner, good or bad.\n\nWhen Wildeve was gone Mrs. Yeobright stood and considered what would be the best course to adopt with regard to the guineas, which she had not liked to entrust to Wildeve. It was hardly credible that Thomasin had told him to ask for them, when the necessity for them had arisen from the difficulty of obtaining money at his hands. At the same time Thomasin really wanted them, and might be unable to come to Blooms-End for another week at least. To take or send the money to her at the inn would be impolite, since Wildeve would pretty surely be present, or would discover the transaction; and if, as her aunt suspected, he treated her less kindly than she deserved to be treated, he might then get the whole sum out of her gentle hands. But on this particular evening Thomasin was at Mistover, and anything might be conveyed to her there without the knowledge of her husband. Upon the whole the opportunity was worth taking advantage of.\n\nHer son, too, was there, and was now married. There could be no more proper moment to render him his share of the money than the present. And the chance that would be afforded her, by sending him this gift, of showing how far she was from bearing him ill-will, cheered the sad mother’s heart.\n\nShe went upstairs and took from a locked drawer a little box, out of which she poured a hoard of broad unworn guineas that had lain there many a year. There were a hundred in all, and she divided them into two heaps, fifty in each. Tying up these in small canvas bags, she went down to the garden and called to Christian Cantle, who was loitering about in hope of a supper which was not really owed him. Mrs. Yeobright gave him the moneybags, charged him to go to Mistover, and on no account to deliver them into any one’s hands save her son’s and Thomasin’s. On further thought she deemed it advisable to tell Christian precisely what the two bags contained, that he might be fully impressed with their importance. Christian pocketed the moneybags, promised the greatest carefulness, and set out on his way.\n\n“You need not hurry,” said Mrs. Yeobright. “It will be better not to get there till after dusk, and then nobody will notice you. Come back here to supper, if it is not too late.”\n\nIt was nearly nine o’clock when he began to ascend the vale towards Mistover; but the long days of summer being at their climax, the first obscurity of evening had only just begun to tan the landscape. At this point of his journey Christian heard voices, and found that they proceeded from a company of men and women who were traversing a hollow ahead of him, the tops only of their heads being visible.\n\nHe paused and thought of the money he carried. It was almost too early even for Christian seriously to fear robbery; nevertheless he took a precaution which ever since his boyhood he had adopted whenever he carried more than two or three shillings upon his person—a precaution somewhat like that of the owner of the Pitt Diamond when filled with similar misgivings. He took off his boots, untied the guineas, and emptied the contents of one little bag into the right boot, and of the other into the left, spreading them as flatly as possible over the bottom of each, which was really a spacious coffer by no means limited to the size of the foot. Pulling them on again and lacing them to the very top, he proceeded on his way, more easy in his head than under his soles.\n\nHis path converged towards that of the noisy company, and on coming nearer he found to his relief that they were several Egdon people whom he knew very well, while with them walked Fairway, of Blooms-End.\n\n“What! Christian going too?” said Fairway as soon as he recognized the newcomer. “You’ve got no young woman nor wife to your name to gie a gown-piece to, I’m sure.”\n\n“What d’ye mean?” said Christian.\n\n“Why, the raffle. The one we go to every year. Going to the raffle as well as ourselves?”\n\n“Never knew a word o’t. Is it like cudgel playing or other sportful forms of bloodshed? I don’t want to go, thank you, Mister Fairway, and no offence.”\n\n“Christian don’t know the fun o’t, and ’twould be a fine sight for him,” said a buxom woman. “There’s no danger at all, Christian. Every man puts in a shilling apiece, and one wins a gown-piece for his wife or sweetheart if he’s got one.”\n\n“Well, as that’s not my fortune there’s no meaning in it to me. But I should like to see the fun, if there’s nothing of the black art in it, and if a man may look on without cost or getting into any dangerous wrangle?”\n\n“There will be no uproar at all,” said Timothy. “Sure, Christian, if you’d like to come we’ll see there’s no harm done.”\n\n“And no ba’dy gaieties, I suppose? You see, neighbours, if so, it would be setting father a bad example, as he is so light moral’d. But a gown-piece for a shilling, and no black art—’tis worth looking in to see, and it wouldn’t hinder me half an hour. Yes, I’ll come, if you’ll step a little way towards Mistover with me afterwards, supposing night should have closed in, and nobody else is going that way?”\nOne or two promised; and Christian, diverging from his direct path, turned round to the right with his companions towards the Quiet Woman.\n\nWhen they entered the large common room of the inn they found assembled there about ten men from among the neighbouring population, and the group was increased by the new contingent to double that number. Most of them were sitting round the room in seats divided by wooden elbows like those of crude cathedral stalls, which were carved with the initials of many an illustrious drunkard of former times who had passed his days and his nights between them, and now lay as an alcoholic cinder in the nearest churchyard. Among the cups on the long table before the sitters lay an open parcel of light drapery—the gown-piece, as it was called—which was to be raffled for. Wildeve was standing with his back to the fireplace smoking a cigar; and the promoter of the raffle, a packman from a distant town, was expatiating upon the value of the fabric as material for a summer dress.\n\n“Now, gentlemen,” he continued, as the newcomers drew up to the table, “there’s five have entered, and we want four more to make up the number. I think, by the faces of those gentlemen who have just come in, that they are shrewd enough to take advantage of this rare opportunity of beautifying their ladies at a very trifling expense.”\n\nFairway, Sam, and another placed their shillings on the table, and the man turned to Christian.\n\n“No, sir,” said Christian, drawing back, with a quick gaze of misgiving. “I am only a poor chap come to look on, an it please ye, sir. I don’t so much as know how you do it. If so be I was sure of getting it I would put down the shilling; but I couldn’t otherwise.”\n\n“I think you might almost be sure,” said the pedlar. “In fact, now I look into your face, even if I can’t say you are sure to win, I can say that I never saw anything look more like winning in my life.”\n\n“You’ll anyhow have the same chance as the rest of us,” said Sam.\n\n“And the extra luck of being the last comer,” said another.\n\n“And I was born wi’ a caul, and perhaps can be no more ruined than drowned?” Christian added, beginning to give way.\n\nUltimately Christian laid down his shilling, the raffle began, and the dice went round. When it came to Christian’s turn he took the box with a trembling hand, shook it fearfully, and threw a pair-royal. Three of the others had thrown common low pairs, and all the rest mere points.\n\n“The gentleman looked like winning, as I said,” observed the chapman blandly. “Take it, sir; the article is yours.”\n\n“Haw-haw-haw!” said Fairway. “I’m damned if this isn’t the quarest start that ever I knowed!”\n\n“Mine?” asked Christian, with a vacant stare from his target eyes. “I—I haven’t got neither maid, wife, nor widder belonging to me at all, and I’m afeard it will make me laughed at to ha’e it, Master Traveller. What with being curious to join in I never thought of that! What shall I do wi’ a woman’s clothes in _my_ bedroom, and not lose my decency!”\n\n“Keep ’em, to be sure,” said Fairway, “if it is only for luck. Perhaps ’twill tempt some woman that thy poor carcase had no power over when standing empty-handed.”\n\n“Keep it, certainly,” said Wildeve, who had idly watched the scene from a distance.\n\nThe table was then cleared of the articles, and the men began to drink.\n\n“Well, to be sure!” said Christian, half to himself. “To think I should have been born so lucky as this, and not have found it out until now! What curious creatures these dice be—powerful rulers of us all, and yet at my command! I am sure I never need be afeared of anything after this.” He handled the dice fondly one by one. “Why, sir,” he said in a confidential whisper to Wildeve, who was near his left hand, “if I could only use this power that’s in me of multiplying money I might do some good to a near relation of yours, seeing what I’ve got about me of hers—eh?” He tapped one of his money-laden boots upon the floor.\n\n“What do you mean?” said Wildeve.\n\n“That’s a secret. Well, I must be going now.” He looked anxiously towards Fairway.\n\n“Where are you going?” Wildeve asked.\n\n“To Mistover Knap. I have to see Mrs. Thomasin there—that’s all.”\n\n“I am going there, too, to fetch Mrs. Wildeve. We can walk together.”\n\nWildeve became lost in thought, and a look of inward illumination came into his eyes. It was money for his wife that Mrs. Yeobright could not trust him with. “Yet she could trust this fellow,” he said to himself. “Why doesn’t that which belongs to the wife belong to the husband too?”\nHe called to the pot-boy to bring him his hat, and said, “Now, Christian, I am ready.”\n\n“Mr. Wildeve,” said Christian timidly, as he turned to leave the room, “would you mind lending me them wonderful little things that carry my luck inside ’em, that I might practise a bit by myself, you know?” He looked wistfully at the dice and box lying on the mantlepiece.\n\n“Certainly,” said Wildeve carelessly. “They were only cut out by some lad with his knife, and are worth nothing.” And Christian went back and privately pocketed them.\n\nWildeve opened the door and looked out. The night was warm and cloudy. “By Gad! ’tis dark,” he continued. “But I suppose we shall find our way.”\n\n“If we should lose the path it might be awkward,” said Christian. “A lantern is the only shield that will make it safe for us.”\n\n“Let’s have a lantern by all means.” The stable lantern was fetched and lighted. Christian took up his gownpiece, and the two set out to ascend the hill.\n\nWithin the room the men fell into chat till their attention was for a moment drawn to the chimney-corner. This was large, and, in addition to its proper recess, contained within its jambs, like many on Egdon, a receding seat, so that a person might sit there absolutely unobserved, provided there was no fire to light him up, as was the case now and throughout the summer. From the niche a single object protruded into the light from the candles on the table. It was a clay pipe, and its colour was reddish. The men had been attracted to this object by a voice behind the pipe asking for a light.\n\n“Upon my life, it fairly startled me when the man spoke!” said Fairway, handing a candle. “Oh—’tis the reddleman! You’ve kept a quiet tongue, young man.”\n\n“Yes, I had nothing to say,” observed Venn. In a few minutes he arose and wished the company good night.\n\nMeanwhile Wildeve and Christian had plunged into the heath.\n\nIt was a stagnant, warm, and misty night, full of all the heavy perfumes of new vegetation not yet dried by hot sun, and among these particularly the scent of the fern. The lantern, dangling from Christian’s hand, brushed the feathery fronds in passing by, disturbing moths and other winged insects, which flew out and alighted upon its horny panes.\n\n“So you have money to carry to Mrs. Wildeve?” said Christian’s companion, after a silence. “Don’t you think it very odd that it shouldn’t be given to me?”\n\n“As man and wife be one flesh, ’twould have been all the same, I should think,” said Christian. “But my strict documents was, to give the money into Mrs. Wildeve’s hand—and ’tis well to do things right.”\n\n“No doubt,” said Wildeve. Any person who had known the circumstances might have perceived that Wildeve was mortified by the discovery that the matter in transit was money, and not, as he had supposed when at Blooms-End, some fancy nick-nack which only interested the two women themselves. Mrs. Yeobright’s refusal implied that his honour was not considered to be of sufficiently good quality to make him a safer bearer of his wife’s property.\n\n“How very warm it is tonight, Christian!” he said, panting, when they were nearly under Rainbarrow. “Let us sit down for a few minutes, for Heaven’s sake.”\n\nWildeve flung himself down on the soft ferns; and Christian, placing the lantern and parcel on the ground, perched himself in a cramped position hard by, his knees almost touching his chin. He presently thrust one hand into his coat-pocket and began shaking it about.\n\n“What are you rattling in there?” said Wildeve.\n\n“Only the dice, sir,” said Christian, quickly withdrawing his hand. “What magical machines these little things be, Mr. Wildeve! ’Tis a game I should never get tired of. Would you mind my taking ’em out and looking at ’em for a minute, to see how they are made? I didn’t like to look close before the other men, for fear they should think it bad manners in me.” Christian took them out and examined them in the hollow of his hand by the lantern light. “That these little things should carry such luck, and such charm, and such a spell, and such power in ’em, passes all I ever heard or zeed,” he went on, with a fascinated gaze at the dice, which, as is frequently the case in country places, were made of wood, the points being burnt upon each face with the end of a wire.\n\n“They are a great deal in a small compass, You think?”\n\n“Yes. Do ye suppose they really be the devil’s playthings, Mr. Wildeve? If so, ’tis no good sign that I be such a lucky man.”\n\n“You ought to win some money, now that you’ve got them. Any woman would marry you then. Now is your time, Christian, and I would recommend you not to let it slip. Some men are born to luck, some are not. I belong to the latter class.”\n\n“Did you ever know anybody who was born to it besides myself?”\n\n“O yes. I once heard of an Italian, who sat down at a gaming table with only a louis, (that’s a foreign sovereign), in his pocket. He played on for twenty-four hours, and won ten thousand pounds, stripping the bank he had played against. Then there was another man who had lost a thousand pounds, and went to the broker’s next day to sell stock, that he might pay the debt. The man to whom he owed the money went with him in a hackney-coach; and to pass the time they tossed who should pay the fare. The ruined man won, and the other was tempted to continue the game, and they played all the way. When the coachman stopped he was told to drive home again: the whole thousand pounds had been won back by the man who was going to sell.”\n\n“Ha—ha—splendid!” exclaimed Christian. “Go on—go on!”\n\n“Then there was a man of London, who was only a waiter at White’s clubhouse. He began playing first half-crown stakes, and then higher and higher, till he became very rich, got an appointment in India, and rose to be Governor of Madras. His daughter married a member of Parliament, and the Bishop of Carlisle stood godfather to one of the children.”\n\n“Wonderful! wonderful!”\n\n“And once there was a young man in America who gambled till he had lost his last dollar. He staked his watch and chain, and lost as before; staked his umbrella, lost again; staked his hat, lost again; staked his coat and stood in his shirt-sleeves, lost again. Began taking off his breeches, and then a looker-on gave him a trifle for his pluck. With this he won. Won back his coat, won back his hat, won back his umbrella, his watch, his money, and went out of the door a rich man.”\n\n“Oh, ’tis too good—it takes away my breath! Mr. Wildeve, I think I will try another shilling with you, as I am one of that sort; no danger can come o’t, and you can afford to lose.”\n\n“Very well,” said Wildeve, rising. Searching about with the lantern, he found a large flat stone, which he placed between himself and Christian, and sat down again. The lantern was opened to give more light, and its rays directed upon the stone. Christian put down a shilling, Wildeve another, and each threw. Christian won. They played for two, Christian won again.\n\n“Let us try four,” said Wildeve. They played for four. This time the stakes were won by Wildeve.\n\n“Ah, those little accidents will, of course, sometimes happen, to the luckiest man,” he observed.\n\n“And now I have no more money!” explained Christian excitedly. “And yet, if I could go on, I should get it back again, and more. I wish this was mine.” He struck his boot upon the ground, so that the guineas chinked within.\n\n“What! you have not put Mrs. Wildeve’s money there?”\n\n“Yes. ’Tis for safety. Is it any harm to raffle with a married lady’s money when, if I win, I shall only keep my winnings, and give her her own all the same; and if t’other man wins, her money will go to the lawful owner?”\n\n“None at all.”\n\nWildeve had been brooding ever since they started on the mean estimation in which he was held by his wife’s friends; and it cut his heart severely. As the minutes passed he had gradually drifted into a revengeful intention without knowing the precise moment of forming it. This was to teach Mrs. Yeobright a lesson, as he considered it to be; in other words, to show her if he could that her niece’s husband was the proper guardian of her niece’s money.\n\n“Well, here goes!” said Christian, beginning to unlace one boot. “I shall dream of it nights and nights, I suppose; but I shall always swear my flesh don’t crawl when I think o’t!”\n\nHe thrust his hand into the boot and withdrew one of poor Thomasin’s precious guineas, piping hot. Wildeve had already placed a sovereign on the stone. The game was then resumed. Wildeve won first, and Christian ventured another, winning himself this time. The game fluctuated, but the average was in Wildeve’s favour. Both men became so absorbed in the game that they took no heed of anything but the pigmy objects immediately beneath their eyes, the flat stone, the open lantern, the dice, and the few illuminated fern-leaves which lay under the light, were the whole world to them.\n\nAt length Christian lost rapidly; and presently, to his horror, the whole fifty guineas belonging to Thomasin had been handed over to his adversary.\n\n“I don’t care—I don’t care!” he moaned, and desperately set about untying his left boot to get at the other fifty. “The devil will toss me into the flames on his three-pronged fork for this night’s work, I know! But perhaps I shall win yet, and then I’ll get a wife to sit up with me o’ nights and I won’t be afeard, I won’t! Here’s another for’ee, my man!” He slapped another guinea down upon the stone, and the dice-box was rattled again.\n\nTime passed on. Wildeve began to be as excited as Christian himself. When commencing the game his intention had been nothing further than a bitter practical joke on Mrs. Yeobright. To win the money, fairly or otherwise, and to hand it contemptuously to Thomasin in her aunt’s presence, had been the dim outline of his purpose. But men are drawn from their intentions even in the course of carrying them out, and it was extremely doubtful, by the time the twentieth guinea had been reached, whether Wildeve was conscious of any other intention than that of winning for his own personal benefit. Moreover, he was now no longer gambling for his wife’s money, but for Yeobright’s; though of this fact Christian, in his apprehensiveness, did not inform him till afterwards.\n\nIt was nearly eleven o’clock, when, with almost a shriek, Christian placed Yeobright’s last gleaming guinea upon the stone. In thirty seconds it had gone the way of its companions.\n\nChristian turned and flung himself on the ferns in a convulsion of remorse, “O, what shall I do with my wretched self?” he groaned. “What shall I do? Will any good Heaven hae mercy upon my wicked soul?”\n\n“Do? Live on just the same.”\n\n“I won’t live on just the same! I’ll die! I say you are a—a——”\n\n“A man sharper than my neighbour.”\n\n“Yes, a man sharper than my neighbour; a regular sharper!”\n\n“Poor chips-in-porridge, you are very unmannerly.”\n\n“I don’t know about that! And I say you be unmannerly! You’ve got money that isn’t your own. Half the guineas are poor Mr. Clym’s.”\n\n“How’s that?”\n\n“Because I had to gie fifty of ’em to him. Mrs. Yeobright said so.”\n\n“Oh?... Well, ’twould have been more graceful of her to have given them to his wife Eustacia. But they are in my hands now.”\n\nChristian pulled on his boots, and with heavy breathings, which could be heard to some distance, dragged his limbs together, arose, and tottered away out of sight. Wildeve set about shutting the lantern to return to the house, for he deemed it too late to go to Mistover to meet his wife, who was to be driven home in the captain’s four-wheel. While he was closing the little horn door a figure rose from behind a neighbouring bush and came forward into the lantern light. It was the reddleman approaching.\nVIII. A New Force Disturbs the Current\n\nWildeve stared. Venn looked coolly towards Wildeve, and, without a word being spoken, he deliberately sat himself down where Christian had been seated, thrust his hand into his pocket, drew out a sovereign, and laid it on the stone.\n\n“You have been watching us from behind that bush?” said Wildeve.\n\nThe reddleman nodded. “Down with your stake,” he said. “Or haven’t you pluck enough to go on?”\n\nNow, gambling is a species of amusement which is much more easily begun with full pockets than left off with the same; and though Wildeve in a cooler temper might have prudently declined this invitation, the excitement of his recent success carried him completely away. He placed one of the guineas on a slab beside the reddleman’s sovereign. “Mine is a guinea,” he said.\n\n“A guinea that’s not your own,” said Venn sarcastically.\n\n“It is my own,” answered Wildeve haughtily. “It is my wife’s, and what is hers is mine.”\n\n“Very well; let’s make a beginning.” He shook the box, and threw eight, ten, and nine; the three casts amounted to twenty-seven.\n\nThis encouraged Wildeve. He took the box; and his three casts amounted to forty-five.\n\nDown went another of the reddleman’s sovereigns against his first one which Wildeve laid. This time Wildeve threw fifty-one points, but no pair. The reddleman looked grim, threw a raffle of aces, and pocketed the stakes.\n\n“Here you are again,” said Wildeve contemptuously. “Double the stakes.” He laid two of Thomasin’s guineas, and the reddleman his two pounds. Venn won again. New stakes were laid on the stone, and the gamblers proceeded as before.\n\nWildeve was a nervous and excitable man, and the game was beginning to tell upon his temper. He writhed, fumed, shifted his seat, and the beating of his heart was almost audible. Venn sat with lips impassively closed and eyes reduced to a pair of unimportant twinkles; he scarcely appeared to breathe. He might have been an Arab, or an automaton; he would have been like a red sandstone statue but for the motion of his arm with the dice-box.\n\nThe game fluctuated, now in favour of one, now in favour of the other, without any great advantage on the side of either. Nearly twenty minutes were passed thus. The light of the candle had by this time attracted heath-flies, moths, and other winged creatures of night, which floated round the lantern, flew into the flame, or beat about the faces of the two players.\n\nBut neither of the men paid much attention to these things, their eyes being concentrated upon the little flat stone, which to them was an arena vast and important as a battlefield. By this time a change had come over the game; the reddleman won continually. At length sixty guineas—Thomasin’s fifty, and ten of Clym’s—had passed into his hands. Wildeve was reckless, frantic, exasperated.\n\n“‘Won back his coat,’” said Venn slily.\n\nAnother throw, and the money went the same way.\n\n“‘Won back his hat,’” continued Venn.\n\n“Oh, oh!” said Wildeve.\n\n“‘Won back his watch, won back his money, and went out of the door a rich man,’” added Venn sentence by sentence, as stake after stake passed over to him.\n\n“Five more!” shouted Wildeve, dashing down the money. “And three casts be hanged—one shall decide.”\n\nThe red automaton opposite lapsed into silence, nodded, and followed his example. Wildeve rattled the box, and threw a pair of sixes and five points. He clapped his hands; “I have done it this time—hurrah!”\n\n“There are two playing, and only one has thrown,” said the reddleman, quietly bringing down the box. The eyes of each were then so intently converged upon the stone that one could fancy their beams were visible, like rays in a fog.\nVenn lifted the box, and behold a triplet of sixes was disclosed.\n\nWildeve was full of fury. While the reddleman was grasping the stakes Wildeve seized the dice and hurled them, box and all, into the darkness, uttering a fearful imprecation. Then he arose and began stamping up and down like a madman.\n\n“It is all over, then?” said Venn.\n\n“No, no!” cried Wildeve. “I mean to have another chance yet. I must!”\n\n“But, my good man, what have you done with the dice?”\n\n“I threw them away—it was a momentary irritation. What a fool I am! Here—come and help me to look for them—we must find them again.”\n\nWildeve snatched up the lantern and began anxiously prowling among the furze and fern.\n\n“You are not likely to find them there,” said Venn, following. “What did you do such a crazy thing as that for? Here’s the box. The dice can’t be far off.”\n\nWildeve turned the light eagerly upon the spot where Venn had found the box, and mauled the herbage right and left. In the course of a few minutes one of the dice was found. They searched on for some time, but no other was to be seen.\n\n“Never mind,” said Wildeve; “let’s play with one.”\n\n“Agreed,” said Venn.\n\nDown they sat again, and recommenced with single guinea stakes; and the play went on smartly. But Fortune had unmistakably fallen in love with the reddleman tonight. He won steadily, till he was the owner of fourteen more of the gold pieces. Seventy-nine of the hundred guineas were his, Wildeve possessing only twenty-one. The aspect of the two opponents was now singular. Apart from motions, a complete diorama of the fluctuations of the game went on in their eyes. A diminutive candle-flame was mirrored in each pupil, and it would have been possible to distinguish therein between the moods of hope and the moods of abandonment, even as regards the reddleman, though his facial muscles betrayed nothing at all. Wildeve played on with the recklessness of despair.\n\n“What’s that?” he suddenly exclaimed, hearing a rustle; and they both looked up.\n\nThey were surrounded by dusky forms between four and five feet high, standing a few paces beyond the rays of the lantern. A moment’s inspection revealed that the encircling figures were heath-croppers, their heads being all towards the players, at whom they gazed intently.\n\n“Hoosh!” said Wildeve, and the whole forty or fifty animals at once turned and galloped away. Play was again resumed.\n\nTen minutes passed away. Then a large death’s head moth advanced from the obscure outer air, wheeled twice round the lantern, flew straight at the candle, and extinguished it by the force of the blow. Wildeve had just thrown, but had not lifted the box to see what he had cast; and now it was impossible.\n\n“What the infernal!” he shrieked. “Now, what shall we do? Perhaps I have thrown six—have you any matches?”\n\n“None,” said Venn.\n\n“Christian had some—I wonder where he is. Christian!”\n\nBut there was no reply to Wildeve’s shout, save a mournful whining from the herons which were nesting lower down the vale. Both men looked blankly round without rising. As their eyes grew accustomed to the darkness they perceived faint greenish points of light among the grass and fern. These lights dotted the hillside like stars of a low magnitude.\n\n“Ah—glowworms,” said Wildeve. “Wait a minute. We can continue the game.”\n\nVenn sat still, and his companion went hither and thither till he had gathered thirteen glowworms—as many as he could find in a space of four or five minutes—upon a fox-glove leaf which he pulled for the purpose. The reddleman vented a low humorous laugh when he saw his adversary return with these. “Determined to go on, then?” he said drily.\n\n“I always am!” said Wildeve angrily. And shaking the glowworms from the leaf he ranged them with a trembling hand in a circle on the stone, leaving a space in the middle for the descent of the dice-box, over which the thirteen tiny lamps threw a pale phosphoric shine. The game was again renewed. It happened to be that season of the year at which glowworms put forth their greatest brilliancy, and the light they yielded was more than ample for the purpose, since it is possible on such nights to read the handwriting of a letter by the light of two or three.\n\nThe incongruity between the men’s deeds and their environment was great. Amid the soft juicy vegetation of the hollow in which they sat, the motionless and the uninhabited solitude, intruded the chink of guineas, the rattle of dice, the exclamations of the reckless players.\nWildeve had lifted the box as soon as the lights were obtained, and the solitary die proclaimed that the game was still against him.\n\n“I won’t play any more—you’ve been tampering with the dice,” he shouted.\n\n“How—when they were your own?” said the reddleman.\n\n“We’ll change the game: the lowest point shall win the stake—it may cut off my ill luck. Do you refuse?”\n\n“No—go on,” said Venn.\n\n“O, there they are again—damn them!” cried Wildeve, looking up. The heath-croppers had returned noiselessly, and were looking on with erect heads just as before, their timid eyes fixed upon the scene, as if they were wondering what mankind and candlelight could have to do in these haunts at this untoward hour.\n\n“What a plague those creatures are—staring at me so!” he said, and flung a stone, which scattered them; when the game was continued as before.\n\nWildeve had now ten guineas left; and each laid five. Wildeve threw three points; Venn two, and raked in the coins. The other seized the die, and clenched his teeth upon it in sheer rage, as if he would bite it in pieces. “Never give in—here are my last five!” he cried, throwing them down. “Hang the glowworms—they are going out. Why don’t you burn, you little fools? Stir them up with a thorn.”\n\nHe probed the glowworms with a bit of stick, and rolled them over, till the bright side of their tails was upwards.\n\n“There’s light enough. Throw on,” said Venn.\n\nWildeve brought down the box within the shining circle and looked eagerly. He had thrown ace. “Well done!—I said it would turn, and it has turned.” Venn said nothing; but his hand shook slightly.\n\nHe threw ace also.\n\n“O!” said Wildeve. “Curse me!”\n\nThe die smacked the stone a second time. It was ace again. Venn looked gloomy, threw—the die was seen to be lying in two pieces, the cleft sides uppermost.\n\n“I’ve thrown nothing at all,” he said.\n\n“Serves me right—I split the die with my teeth. Here—take your money. Blank is less than one.”\n\n“I don’t wish it.”\n\n“Take it, I say—you’ve won it!” And Wildeve threw the stakes against the reddleman’s chest. Venn gathered them up, arose, and withdrew from the hollow, Wildeve sitting stupefied.\n\nWhen he had come to himself he also arose, and, with the extinguished lantern in his hand, went towards the highroad. On reaching it he stood still. The silence of night pervaded the whole heath except in one direction; and that was towards Mistover. There he could hear the noise of light wheels, and presently saw two carriagelamps descending the hill. Wildeve screened himself under a bush and waited.\n\nThe vehicle came on and passed before him. It was a hired carriage, and behind the coachman were two persons whom he knew well. There sat Eustacia and Yeobright, the arm of the latter being round her waist. They turned the sharp corner at the bottom towards the temporary home which Clym had hired and furnished, about five miles to the eastward.\nWildeve forgot the loss of the money at the sight of his lost love, whose preciousness in his eyes was increasing in geometrical progression with each new incident that reminded him of their hopeless division. Brimming with the subtilized misery that he was capable of feeling, he followed the opposite way towards the inn.\n\nAbout the same moment that Wildeve stepped into the highway Venn also had reached it at a point a hundred yards further on; and he, hearing the same wheels, likewise waited till the carriage should come up. When he saw who sat therein he seemed to be disappointed. Reflecting a minute or two, during which interval the carriage rolled on, he crossed the road, and took a short cut through the furze and heath to a point where the turnpike road bent round in ascending a hill. He was now again in front of the carriage, which presently came up at a walking pace. Venn stepped forward and showed himself.\n\nEustacia started when the lamp shone upon him, and Clym’s arm was involuntarily withdrawn from her waist. He said, “What, Diggory? You are having a lonely walk.”\n\n“Yes—I beg your pardon for stopping you,” said Venn. “But I am waiting about for Mrs. Wildeve: I have something to give her from Mrs. Yeobright. Can you tell me if she’s gone home from the party yet?”\n\n“No. But she will be leaving soon. You may possibly meet her at the corner.”\n\nVenn made a farewell obeisance, and walked back to his former position, where the byroad from Mistover joined the highway. Here he remained fixed for nearly half an hour, and then another pair of lights came down the hill. It was the old-fashioned wheeled nondescript belonging to the captain, and Thomasin sat in it alone, driven by Charley.\n\nThe reddleman came up as they slowly turned the corner. “I beg pardon for stopping you, Mrs. Wildeve,” he said. “But I have something to give you privately from Mrs. Yeobright.” He handed a small parcel; it consisted of the hundred guineas he had just won, roughly twisted up in a piece of paper.\n\nThomasin recovered from her surprise, and took the packet. “That’s all, ma’am—I wish you good night,” he said, and vanished from her view.\n\nThus Venn, in his anxiety to rectify matters, had placed in Thomasin’s hands not only the fifty guineas which rightly belonged to her, but also the fifty intended for her cousin Clym. His mistake had been based upon Wildeve’s words at the opening of the game, when he indignantly denied that the guinea was not his own. It had not been comprehended by the reddleman that at halfway through the performance the game was continued with the money of another person; and it was an error which afterwards helped to cause more misfortune than treble the loss in money value could have done.\n\nThe night was now somewhat advanced; and Venn plunged deeper into the heath, till he came to a ravine where his van was standing—a spot not more than two hundred yards from the site of the gambling bout. He entered this movable home of his, lit his lantern, and, before closing his door for the night, stood reflecting on the circumstances of the preceding hours. While he stood the dawn grew visible in the northeast quarter of the heavens, which, the clouds having cleared off, was bright with a soft sheen at this midsummer time, though it was only between one and two o’clock. Venn, thoroughly weary, then shut his door and flung himself down to sleep.\n\nI. The Rencounter by the Pool\n\nThe July sun shone over Egdon and fired its crimson heather to scarlet. It was the one season of the year, and the one weather of the season, in which the heath was gorgeous. This flowering period represented the second or noontide division in the cycle of those superficial changes which alone were possible here; it followed the green or young-fern period, representing the morn, and preceded the brown period, when the heathbells and ferns would wear the russet tinges of evening; to be in turn displaced by the dark hue of the winter period, representing night.\n\nClym and Eustacia, in their little house at Alderworth, beyond East Egdon, were living on with a monotony which was delightful to them. The heath and changes of weather were quite blotted out from their eyes for the present. They were enclosed in a sort of luminous mist, which hid from them surroundings of any inharmonious colour, and gave to all things the character of light. When it rained they were charmed, because they could remain indoors together all day with such a show of reason; when it was fine they were charmed, because they could sit together on the hills. They were like those double stars which revolve round and round each other, and from a distance appear to be one. The absolute solitude in which they lived intensified their reciprocal thoughts; yet some might have said that it had the disadvantage of consuming their mutual affections at a fearfully prodigal rate. Yeobright did not fear for his own part; but recollection of Eustacia’s old speech about the evanescence of love, now apparently forgotten by her, sometimes caused him to ask himself a question; and he recoiled at the thought that the quality of finiteness was not foreign to Eden.\n\nWhen three or four weeks had been passed thus, Yeobright resumed his reading in earnest. To make up for lost time he studied indefatigably, for he wished to enter his new profession with the least possible delay.\n\nNow, Eustacia’s dream had always been that, once married to Clym, she would have the power of inducing him to return to Paris. He had carefully withheld all promise to do so; but would he be proof against her coaxing and argument? She had calculated to such a degree on the probability of success that she had represented Paris, and not Budmouth, to her grandfather as in all likelihood their future home. Her hopes were bound up in this dream. In the quiet days since their marriage, when Yeobright had been poring over her lips, her eyes, and the lines of her face, she had mused and mused on the subject, even while in the act of returning his gaze; and now the sight of the books, indicating a future which was antagonistic to her dream, struck her with a positively painful jar. She was hoping for the time when, as the mistress of some pretty establishment, however small, near a Parisian Boulevard, she would be passing her days on the skirts at least of the gay world, and catching stray wafts from those town pleasures she was so well fitted to enjoy. Yet Yeobright was as firm in the contrary intention as if the tendency of marriage were rather to develop the fantasies of young philanthropy than to sweep them away.\n\nHer anxiety reached a high pitch; but there was something in Clym’s undeviating manner which made her hesitate before sounding him on the subject. At this point in their experience, however, an incident helped her. It occurred one evening about six weeks after their union, and arose entirely out of the unconscious misapplication of Venn of the fifty guineas intended for Yeobright.\n\nA day or two after the receipt of the money Thomasin had sent a note to her aunt to thank her. She had been surprised at the largeness of the amount; but as no sum had ever been mentioned she set that down to her late uncle’s generosity. She had been strictly charged by her aunt to say nothing to her husband of this gift; and Wildeve, as was natural enough, had not brought himself to mention to his wife a single particular of the midnight scene in the heath. Christian’s terror, in like manner, had tied his tongue on the share he took in that proceeding; and hoping that by some means or other the money had gone to its proper destination, he simply asserted as much, without giving details.\n\nTherefore, when a week or two had passed away, Mrs. Yeobright began to wonder why she never heard from her son of the receipt of the present; and to add gloom to her perplexity came the possibility that resentment might be the cause of his silence. She could hardly believe as much, but why did he not write? She questioned Christian, and the confusion in his answers would at once have led her to believe that something was wrong, had not one-half of his story been corroborated by Thomasin’s note.\n\nMrs. Yeobright was in this state of uncertainty when she was informed one morning that her son’s wife was visiting her grandfather at Mistover. She determined to walk up the hill, see Eustacia, and ascertain from her daughter-in-law’s lips whether the family guineas, which were to Mrs. Yeobright what family jewels are to wealthier dowagers, had miscarried or not.\n\nWhen Christian learnt where she was going his concern reached its height. At the moment of her departure he could prevaricate no longer, and, confessing to the gambling, told her the truth as far as he knew it—that the guineas had been won by Wildeve.\n\n“What, is he going to keep them?” Mrs. Yeobright cried.\n\n“I hope and trust not!” moaned Christian. “He’s a good man, and perhaps will do right things. He said you ought to have gied Mr. Clym’s share to Eustacia, and that’s perhaps what he’ll do himself.”\n\nTo Mrs. Yeobright, as soon as she could calmly reflect, there was much likelihood in this, for she could hardly believe that Wildeve would really appropriate money belonging to her son. The intermediate course of giving it to Eustacia was the sort of thing to please Wildeve’s fancy. But it filled the mother with anger none the less. That Wildeve should have got command of the guineas after all, and should rearrange the disposal of them, placing Clym’s share in Clym’s wife’s hands, because she had been his own sweetheart, and might be so still, was as irritating a pain as any that Mrs. Yeobright had ever borne.\n\nShe instantly dismissed the wretched Christian from her employ for his conduct in the affair; but, feeling quite helpless and unable to do without him, told him afterwards that he might stay a little longer if he chose. Then she hastened off to Eustacia, moved by a much less promising emotion towards her daughter-in-law than she had felt half an hour earlier, when planning her journey. At that time it was to inquire in a friendly spirit if there had been any accidental loss; now it was to ask plainly if Wildeve had privately given her money which had been intended as a sacred gift to Clym.\n\nShe started at two o’clock, and her meeting with Eustacia was hastened by the appearance of the young lady beside the pool and bank which bordered her grandfather’s premises, where she stood surveying the scene, and perhaps thinking of the romantic enactments it had witnessed in past days. When Mrs. Yeobright approached, Eustacia surveyed her with the calm stare of a stranger.\n\nThe mother-in-law was the first to speak. “I was coming to see you,” she said.\n\n“Indeed!” said Eustacia with surprise, for Mrs. Yeobright, much to the girl’s mortification, had refused to be present at the wedding. “I did not at all expect you.”\n\n“I was coming on business only,” said the visitor, more coldly than at first. “Will you excuse my asking this—Have you received a gift from Thomasin’s husband?”\n\n“A gift?”\n\n“I mean money!”\n\n“What—I myself?”\n\n“Well, I meant yourself, privately—though I was not going to put it in that way.”\n\n“Money from Mr. Wildeve? No—never! Madam, what do you mean by that?” Eustacia fired up all too quickly, for her own consciousness of the old attachment between herself and Wildeve led her to jump to the conclusion that Mrs. Yeobright also knew of it, and might have come to accuse her of receiving dishonourable presents from him now.\n\n“I simply ask the question,” said Mrs. Yeobright. “I have been——”\n\n“You ought to have better opinions of me—I feared you were against me from the first!” exclaimed Eustacia.\n\n“No. I was simply for Clym,” replied Mrs. Yeobright, with too much emphasis in her earnestness. “It is the instinct of everyone to look after their own.”\n\n“How can you imply that he required guarding against me?” cried Eustacia, passionate tears in her eyes. “I have not injured him by marrying him! What sin have I done that you should think so ill of me? You had no right to speak against me to him when I have never wronged you.”\n\n“I only did what was fair under the circumstances,” said Mrs. Yeobright more softly. “I would rather not have gone into this question at present, but you compel me. I am not ashamed to tell you the honest truth. I was firmly convinced that he ought not to marry you—therefore I tried to dissuade him by all the means in my power. But it is done now, and I have no idea of complaining any more. I am ready to welcome you.”\n\n“Ah, yes, it is very well to see things in that business point of view,” murmured Eustacia with a smothered fire of feeling. “But why should you think there is anything between me and Mr. Wildeve? I have a spirit as well as you. I am indignant; and so would any woman be. It was a condescension in me to be Clym’s wife, and not a manœuvre, let me remind you; and therefore I will not be treated as a schemer whom it becomes necessary to bear with because she has crept into the family.”\n\n“Oh!” said Mrs. Yeobright, vainly endeavouring to control her anger. “I have never heard anything to show that my son’s lineage is not as good as the Vyes’—perhaps better. It is amusing to hear you talk of condescension.”\n\n“It was condescension, nevertheless,” said Eustacia vehemently. “And if I had known then what I know now, that I should be living in this wild heath a month after my marriage, I—I should have thought twice before agreeing.”\n\n“It would be better not to say that; it might not sound truthful. I am not aware that any deception was used on his part—I know there was not—whatever might have been the case on the other side.”\n\n“This is too exasperating!” answered the younger woman huskily, her face crimsoning, and her eyes darting light. “How can you dare to speak to me like that? I insist upon repeating to you that had I known that my life would from my marriage up to this time have been as it is, I should have said _No_. I don’t complain. I have never uttered a sound of such a thing to him; but it is true. I hope therefore that in the future you will be silent on my eagerness. If you injure me now you injure yourself.”\n\n“Injure you? Do you think I am an evil-disposed person?”\n\n“You injured me before my marriage, and you have now suspected me of secretly favouring another man for money!”\n\n“I could not help what I thought. But I have never spoken of you outside my house.”\n\n“You spoke of me within it, to Clym, and you could not do worse.”\n\n“I did my duty.”\n“And I’ll do mine.”\n\n“A part of which will possibly be to set him against his mother. It is always so. But why should I not bear it as others have borne it before me!”\n\n“I understand you,” said Eustacia, breathless with emotion. “You think me capable of every bad thing. Who can be worse than a wife who encourages a lover, and poisons her husband’s mind against his relative? Yet that is now the character given to me. Will you not come and drag him out of my hands?”\n\nMrs. Yeobright gave back heat for heat.\n\n“Don’t rage at me, madam! It ill becomes your beauty, and I am not worth the injury you may do it on my account, I assure you. I am only a poor old woman who has lost a son.”\n\n“If you had treated me honourably you would have had him still.” Eustacia said, while scalding tears trickled from her eyes. “You have brought yourself to folly; you have caused a division which can never be healed!”\n\n“I have done nothing. This audacity from a young woman is more than I can bear.”\n\n“It was asked for; you have suspected me, and you have made me speak of my husband in a way I would not have done. You will let him know that I have spoken thus, and it will cause misery between us. Will you go away from me? You are no friend!”\n\n“I will go when I have spoken a word. If anyone says I have come here to question you without good grounds for it, that person speaks untruly. If anyone says that I attempted to stop your marriage by any but honest means, that person, too, does not speak the truth. I have fallen on an evil time; God has been unjust to me in letting you insult me! Probably my son’s happiness does not lie on this side of the grave, for he is a foolish man who neglects the advice of his parent. You, Eustacia, stand on the edge of a precipice without knowing it. Only show my son one-half the temper you have shown me today—and you may before long—and you will find that though he is as gentle as a child with you now, he can be as hard as steel!”\n\nThe excited mother then withdrew, and Eustacia, panting, stood looking into the pool.\n\nII. He Is Set upon by Adversities but He Sings a Song\n\nThe result of that unpropitious interview was that Eustacia, instead of passing the afternoon with her grandfather, hastily returned home to Clym, where she arrived three hours earlier than she had been expected.\nShe came indoors with her face flushed, and her eyes still showing traces of her recent excitement. Yeobright looked up astonished; he had never seen her in any way approaching to that state before. She passed him by, and would have gone upstairs unnoticed, but Clym was so concerned that he immediately followed her.\n\n“What is the matter, Eustacia?” he said. She was standing on the hearthrug in the bedroom, looking upon the floor, her hands clasped in front of her, her bonnet yet unremoved. For a moment she did not answer; and then she replied in a low voice—\n\n“I have seen your mother; and I will never see her again!”\n\nA weight fell like a stone upon Clym. That same morning, when Eustacia had arranged to go and see her grandfather, Clym had expressed a wish that she would drive down to Blooms-End and inquire for her mother-in-law, or adopt any other means she might think fit to bring about a reconciliation. She had set out gaily; and he had hoped for much.\n\n“Why is this?” he asked.\n\n“I cannot tell—I cannot remember. I met your mother. And I will never meet her again.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“What do I know about Mr. Wildeve now? I won’t have wicked opinions passed on me by anybody. O! it was too humiliating to be asked if I had received any money from him, or encouraged him, or something of the sort—I don’t exactly know what!”\n\n“How could she have asked you that?”\n\n“She did.”\n\n“Then there must have been some meaning in it. What did my mother say besides?”\n\n“I don’t know what she said, except in so far as this, that we both said words which can never be forgiven!”\n\n“Oh, there must be some misapprehension. Whose fault was it that her meaning was not made clear?”\n\n“I would rather not say. It may have been the fault of the circumstances, which were awkward at the very least. O Clym—I cannot help expressing it—this is an unpleasant position that you have placed me in. But you must improve it—yes, say you will—for I hate it all now! Yes, take me to Paris, and go on with your old occupation, Clym! I don’t mind how humbly we live there at first, if it can only be Paris, and not Egdon Heath.”\n\n“But I have quite given up that idea,” said Yeobright, with surprise. “Surely I never led you to expect such a thing?”\n\n“I own it. Yet there are thoughts which cannot be kept out of mind, and that one was mine. Must I not have a voice in the matter, now I am your wife and the sharer of your doom?”\n\n“Well, there are things which are placed beyond the pale of discussion; and I thought this was specially so, and by mutual agreement.”\n\n“Clym, I am unhappy at what I hear,” she said in a low voice; and her eyes drooped, and she turned away.\n\nThis indication of an unexpected mine of hope in Eustacia’s bosom disconcerted her husband. It was the first time that he had confronted the fact of the indirectness of a woman’s movement towards her desire. But his intention was unshaken, though he loved Eustacia well. All the effect that her remark had upon him was a resolve to chain himself more closely than ever to his books, so as to be the sooner enabled to appeal to substantial results from another course in arguing against her whim.\n\nNext day the mystery of the guineas was explained. Thomasin paid them a hurried visit, and Clym’s share was delivered up to him by her own hands. Eustacia was not present at the time.\n\n“Then this is what my mother meant,” exclaimed Clym. “Thomasin, do you know that they have had a bitter quarrel?”\n\nThere was a little more reticence now than formerly in Thomasin’s manner towards her cousin. It is the effect of marriage to engender in several directions some of the reserve it annihilates in one. “Your mother told me,” she said quietly. “She came back to my house after seeing Eustacia.”\n\n“The worst thing I dreaded has come to pass. Was Mother much disturbed when she came to you, Thomasin?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Very much indeed?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nClym leant his elbow upon the post of the garden gate, and covered his eyes with his hand.\n\n“Don’t trouble about it, Clym. They may get to be friends.”\n\nHe shook his head. “Not two people with inflammable natures like theirs. Well, what must be will be.”\n\n“One thing is cheerful in it—the guineas are not lost.”\n\n“I would rather have lost them twice over than have had this happen.”\n\nAmid these jarring events Yeobright felt one thing to be indispensable—that he should speedily make some show of progress in his scholastic plans. With this view he read far into the small hours during many nights.\n\nOne morning, after a severer strain than usual, he awoke with a strange sensation in his eyes. The sun was shining directly upon the window-blind, and at his first glance thitherward a sharp pain obliged him to close his eyelids quickly. At every new attempt to look about him the same morbid sensibility to light was manifested, and excoriating tears ran down his cheeks. He was obliged to tie a bandage over his brow while dressing; and during the day it could not be abandoned. Eustacia was thoroughly alarmed. On finding that the case was no better the next morning they decided to send to Anglebury for a surgeon.\n\nTowards evening he arrived, and pronounced the disease to be acute inflammation induced by Clym’s night studies, continued in spite of a cold previously caught, which had weakened his eyes for the time.\nFretting with impatience at this interruption to a task he was so anxious to hasten, Clym was transformed into an invalid. He was shut up in a room from which all light was excluded, and his condition would have been one of absolute misery had not Eustacia read to him by the glimmer of a shaded lamp. He hoped that the worst would soon be over; but at the surgeon’s third visit he learnt to his dismay that although he might venture out of doors with shaded eyes in the course of a month, all thought of pursuing his work, or of reading print of any description, would have to be given up for a long time to come.\n\nOne week and another week wore on, and nothing seemed to lighten the gloom of the young couple. Dreadful imaginings occurred to Eustacia, but she carefully refrained from uttering them to her husband. Suppose he should become blind, or, at all events, never recover sufficient strength of sight to engage in an occupation which would be congenial to her feelings, and conduce to her removal from this lonely dwelling among the hills? That dream of beautiful Paris was not likely to cohere into substance in the presence of this misfortune. As day after day passed by, and he got no better, her mind ran more and more in this mournful groove, and she would go away from him into the garden and weep despairing tears.\n\nYeobright thought he would send for his mother; and then he thought he would not. Knowledge of his state could only make her the more unhappy; and the seclusion of their life was such that she would hardly be likely to learn the news except through a special messenger. Endeavouring to take the trouble as philosophically as possible, he waited on till the third week had arrived, when he went into the open air for the first time since the attack. The surgeon visited him again at this stage, and Clym urged him to express a distinct opinion. The young man learnt with added surprise that the date at which he might expect to resume his labours was as uncertain as ever, his eyes being in that peculiar state which, though affording him sight enough for walking about, would not admit of their being strained upon any definite object without incurring the risk of reproducing ophthalmia in its acute form.\n\nClym was very grave at the intelligence, but not despairing. A quiet firmness, and even cheerfulness, took possession of him. He was not to be blind; that was enough. To be doomed to behold the world through smoked glass for an indefinite period was bad enough, and fatal to any kind of advance; but Yeobright was an absolute stoic in the face of mishaps which only affected his social standing; and, apart from Eustacia, the humblest walk of life would satisfy him if it could be made to work in with some form of his culture scheme. To keep a cottage night-school was one such form; and his affliction did not master his spirit as it might otherwise have done.\n\nHe walked through the warm sun westward into those tracts of Egdon with which he was best acquainted, being those lying nearer to his old home. He saw before him in one of the valleys the gleaming of whetted iron, and advancing, dimly perceived that the shine came from the tool of a man who was cutting furze. The worker recognized Clym, and Yeobright learnt from the voice that the speaker was Humphrey.\n\nHumphrey expressed his sorrow at Clym’s condition, and added, “Now, if yours was low-class work like mine, you could go on with it just the same.”\n\n“Yes, I could,” said Yeobright musingly. “How much do you get for cutting these faggots?”\n\n“Half-a-crown a hundred, and in these long days I can live very well on the wages.”\n\nDuring the whole of Yeobright’s walk home to Alderworth he was lost in reflections which were not of an unpleasant kind. On his coming up to the house Eustacia spoke to him from the open window, and he went across to her.\n\n“Darling,” he said, “I am much happier. And if my mother were reconciled to me and to you I should, I think, be happy quite.”\n\n“I fear that will never be,” she said, looking afar with her beautiful stormy eyes. “How _can_ you say ‘I am happier,’ and nothing changed?”\n\n“It arises from my having at last discovered something I can do, and get a living at, in this time of misfortune.”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“I am going to be a furze- and turf-cutter.”\n\n“No, Clym!” she said, the slight hopefulness previously apparent in her face going off again, and leaving her worse than before.\n\n“Surely I shall. Is it not very unwise in us to go on spending the little money we’ve got when I can keep down expenditures by an honest occupation? The outdoor exercise will do me good, and who knows but that in a few months I shall be able to go on with my reading again?”\n\n“But my grandfather offers to assist us, if we require assistance.”\n\n“We don’t require it. If I go furze-cutting we shall be fairly well off.”\n\n“In comparison with slaves, and the Israelites in Egypt, and such people!” A bitter tear rolled down Eustacia’s face, which he did not see. There had been nonchalance in his tone, showing her that he felt no absolute grief at a consummation which to her was a positive horror.\n\nThe very next day Yeobright went to Humphrey’s cottage, and borrowed of him leggings, gloves, a whetstone, and a hook, to use till he should be able to purchase some for himself. Then he sallied forth with his new fellow-labourer and old acquaintance, and selecting a spot where the furze grew thickest he struck the first blow in his adopted calling. His sight, like the wings in Rasselas, though useless to him for his grand purpose, sufficed for this strait, and he found that when a little practice should have hardened his palms against blistering he would be able to work with ease.\n\nDay after day he rose with the sun, buckled on his leggings, and went off to the rendezvous with Humphrey. His custom was to work from four o’clock in the morning till noon; then, when the heat of the day was at its highest, to go home and sleep for an hour or two; afterwards coming out again and working till dusk at nine.\n\nThis man from Paris was now so disguised by his leather accoutrements, and by the goggles he was obliged to wear over his eyes, that his closest friend might have passed by without recognizing him. He was a brown spot in the midst of an expanse of olive-green gorse, and nothing more. Though frequently depressed in spirit when not actually at work, owing to thoughts of Eustacia’s position and his mother’s estrangement, when in the full swing of labour he was cheerfully disposed and calm.\nHis daily life was of a curious microscopic sort, his whole world being limited to a circuit of a few feet from his person. His familiars were creeping and winged things, and they seemed to enroll him in their band. Bees hummed around his ears with an intimate air, and tugged at the heath and furze-flowers at his side in such numbers as to weigh them down to the sod. The strange amber-coloured butterflies which Egdon produced, and which were never seen elsewhere, quivered in the breath of his lips, alighted upon his bowed back, and sported with the glittering point of his hook as he flourished it up and down. Tribes of emerald-green grasshoppers leaped over his feet, falling awkwardly on their backs, heads, or hips, like unskilful acrobats, as chance might rule; or engaged themselves in noisy flirtations under the fern-fronds with silent ones of homely hue. Huge flies, ignorant of larders and wire-netting, and quite in a savage state, buzzed about him without knowing that he was a man. In and out of the fern-dells snakes glided in their most brilliant blue and yellow guise, it being the season immediately following the shedding of their old skins, when their colours are brightest. Litters of young rabbits came out from their forms to sun themselves upon hillocks, the hot beams blazing through the delicate tissue of each thin-fleshed ear, and firing it to a blood-red transparency in which the veins could be seen. None of them feared him.\n\nThe monotony of his occupation soothed him, and was in itself a pleasure. A forced limitation of effort offered a justification of homely courses to an unambitious man, whose conscience would hardly have allowed him to remain in such obscurity while his powers were unimpeded. Hence Yeobright sometimes sang to himself, and when obliged to accompany Humphrey in search of brambles for faggot-bonds he would amuse his companion with sketches of Parisian life and character, and so while away the time.\n\nOn one of these warm afternoons Eustacia walked out alone in the direction of Yeobright’s place of work. He was busily chopping away at the furze, a long row of faggots which stretched downward from his position representing the labour of the day. He did not observe her approach, and she stood close to him, and heard his undercurrent of song. It shocked her. To see him there, a poor afflicted man, earning money by the sweat of his brow, had at first moved her to tears; but to hear him sing and not at all rebel against an occupation which, however satisfactory to himself, was degrading to her, as an educated lady-wife, wounded her through. Unconscious of her presence, he still went on singing:—\n\n“Le point du jour A nos bosquets rend toute leur parure; Flore est plus belle à son retour; L’oiseau reprend doux chant d’amour; Tout célèbre dans la nature Le point du jour.\n\n“Le point du jour Cause parfois, cause douleur extrême; Que l’espace des nuits est court Pour le berger brûlant d’amour, Forcé de quitter ce qu’il aime Au point du jour.”\n\nIt was bitterly plain to Eustacia that he did not care much about social failure; and the proud fair woman bowed her head and wept in sick despair at thought of the blasting effect upon her own life of that mood and condition in him. Then she came forward.\n\n“I would starve rather than do it!” she exclaimed vehemently. “And you can sing! I will go and live with my grandfather again!”\n\n“Eustacia! I did not see you, though I noticed something moving,” he said gently. He came forward, pulled off his huge leather glove, and took her hand. “Why do you speak in such a strange way? It is only a little old song which struck my fancy when I was in Paris, and now just applies to my life with you. Has your love for me all died, then, because my appearance is no longer that of a fine gentleman?”\n\n“Dearest, you must not question me unpleasantly, or it may make me not love you.”\n\n“Do you believe it possible that I would run the risk of doing that?”\n\n“Well, you follow out your own ideas, and won’t give in to mine when I wish you to leave off this shameful labour. Is there anything you dislike in me that you act so contrarily to my wishes? I am your wife, and why will you not listen? Yes, I am your wife indeed!”\n\n“I know what that tone means.”\n\n“What tone?”\n\n“The tone in which you said, ‘Your wife indeed.’ It meant, ‘Your wife, worse luck.’”\n\n“It is hard in you to probe me with that remark. A woman may have reason, though she is not without heart, and if I felt ‘worse luck,’ it was no ignoble feeling—it was only too natural. There, you see that at any rate I do not attempt untruths. Do you remember how, before we were married, I warned you that I had not good wifely qualities?”\n\n“You mock me to say that now. On that point at least the only noble course would be to hold your tongue, for you are still queen of me, Eustacia, though I may no longer be king of you.”\n\n“You are my husband. Does not that content you?”\n\n“Not unless you are my wife without regret.”\n\n“I cannot answer you. I remember saying that I should be a serious matter on your hands.”\n\n“Yes, I saw that.”\n\n“Then you were too quick to see! No true lover would have seen any such thing; you are too severe upon me, Clym—I won’t like your speaking so at all.”\n\n“Well, I married you in spite of it, and don’t regret doing so. How cold you seem this afternoon! and yet I used to think there never was a warmer heart than yours.”\n\n“Yes, I fear we are cooling—I see it as well as you,” she sighed mournfully. “And how madly we loved two months ago! You were never tired of contemplating me, nor I of contemplating you. Who could have thought then that by this time my eyes would not seem so very bright to yours, nor your lips so very sweet to mine? Two months—is it possible? Yes, ’tis too true!”\n\n“You sigh, dear, as if you were sorry for it; and that’s a hopeful sign.”\n\n“No. I don’t sigh for that. There are other things for me to sigh for, or any other woman in my place.”\n\n“That your chances in life are ruined by marrying in haste an unfortunate man?”\n\n“Why will you force me, Clym, to say bitter things? I deserve pity as much as you. As much?—I think I deserve it more. For you can sing! It would be a strange hour which should catch me singing under such a cloud as this! Believe me, sweet, I could weep to a degree that would astonish and confound such an elastic mind as yours. Even had you felt careless about your own affliction, you might have refrained from singing out of sheer pity for mine. God! if I were a man in such a position I would curse rather than sing.”\n\nYeobright placed his hand upon her arm. “Now, don’t you suppose, my inexperienced girl, that I cannot rebel, in high Promethean fashion, against the gods and fate as well as you. I have felt more steam and smoke of that sort than you have ever heard of. But the more I see of life the more do I perceive that there is nothing particularly great in its greatest walks, and therefore nothing particularly small in mine of furze-cutting. If I feel that the greatest blessings vouchsafed to us are not very valuable, how can I feel it to be any great hardship when they are taken away? So I sing to pass the time. Have you indeed lost all tenderness for me, that you begrudge me a few cheerful moments?”\n\n“I have still some tenderness left for you.”\n\n“Your words have no longer their old flavour. And so love dies with good fortune!”\n\n“I cannot listen to this, Clym—it will end bitterly,” she said in a broken voice. “I will go home.”\n\nIII. She Goes Out to Battle against Depression\n\nA few days later, before the month of August had expired, Eustacia and Yeobright sat together at their early dinner.\n\nEustacia’s manner had become of late almost apathetic. There was a forlorn look about her beautiful eyes which, whether she deserved it or not, would have excited pity in the breast of anyone who had known her during the full flush of her love for Clym. The feelings of husband and wife varied, in some measure, inversely with their positions. Clym, the afflicted man, was cheerful; and he even tried to comfort her, who had never felt a moment of physical suffering in her whole life.\n\n“Come, brighten up, dearest; we shall be all right again. Some day perhaps I shall see as well as ever. And I solemnly promise that I’ll leave off cutting furze as soon as I have the power to do anything better. You cannot seriously wish me to stay idling at home all day?”\n\n“But it is so dreadful—a furze-cutter! and you a man who have lived about the world, and speak French, and German, and who are fit for what is so much better than this.”\n\n“I suppose when you first saw me and heard about me I was wrapped in a sort of golden halo to your eyes—a man who knew glorious things, and had mixed in brilliant scenes—in short, an adorable, delightful, distracting hero?”\n\n“Yes,” she said, sobbing.\n\n“And now I am a poor fellow in brown leather.”\n\n“Don’t taunt me. But enough of this. I will not be depressed any more. I am going from home this afternoon, unless you greatly object. There is to be a village picnic—a gipsying, they call it—at East Egdon, and I shall go.”\n\n“To dance?”\n\n“Why not? You can sing.”\n\n“Well, well, as you will. Must I come to fetch you?”\n\n“If you return soon enough from your work. But do not inconvenience yourself about it. I know the way home, and the heath has no terror for me.”\n\n“And can you cling to gaiety so eagerly as to walk all the way to a village festival in search of it?”\n\n“Now, you don’t like my going alone! Clym, you are not jealous?”\n\n“No. But I would come with you if it could give you any pleasure; though, as things stand, perhaps you have too much of me already. Still, I somehow wish that you did not want to go. Yes, perhaps I am jealous; and who could be jealous with more reason than I, a half-blind man, over such a woman as you?”\n\n“Don’t think like it. Let me go, and don’t take all my spirits away!”\n\n“I would rather lose all my own, my sweet wife. Go and do whatever you like. Who can forbid your indulgence in any whim? You have all my heart yet, I believe; and because you bear with me, who am in truth a drag upon you, I owe you thanks. Yes, go alone and shine. As for me, I will stick to my doom. At that kind of meeting people would shun me. My hook and gloves are like the St. Lazarus rattle of the leper, warning the world to get out of the way of a sight that would sadden them.” He kissed her, put on his leggings, and went out.\n\nWhen he was gone she rested her head upon her hands and said to herself, “Two wasted lives—his and mine. And I am come to this! Will it drive me out of my mind?”\n\nShe cast about for any possible course which offered the least improvement on the existing state of things, and could find none. She imagined how all those Budmouth ones who should learn what had become of her would say, “Look at the girl for whom nobody was good enough!” To Eustacia the situation seemed such a mockery of her hopes that death appeared the only door of relief if the satire of Heaven should go much further.\n\nSuddenly she aroused herself and exclaimed, “But I’ll shake it off. Yes, I _will_ shake it off! No one shall know my suffering. I’ll be bitterly merry, and ironically gay, and I’ll laugh in derision. And I’ll begin by going to this dance on the green.”\n\nShe ascended to her bedroom and dressed herself with scrupulous care. To an onlooker her beauty would have made her feelings almost seem reasonable. The gloomy corner into which accident as much as indiscretion had brought this woman might have led even a moderate partisan to feel that she had cogent reasons for asking the Supreme Power by what right a being of such exquisite finish had been placed in circumstances calculated to make of her charms a curse rather than a blessing.\n\nIt was five in the afternoon when she came out from the house ready for her walk. There was material enough in the picture for twenty new conquests. The rebellious sadness that was rather too apparent when she sat indoors without a bonnet was cloaked and softened by her outdoor attire, which always had a sort of nebulousness about it, devoid of harsh edges anywhere; so that her face looked from its environment as from a cloud, with no noticeable lines of demarcation between flesh and clothes. The heat of the day had scarcely declined as yet, and she went along the sunny hills at a leisurely pace, there being ample time for her idle expedition. Tall ferns buried her in their leafage whenever her path lay through them, which now formed miniature forests, though not one stem of them would remain to bud the next year.\n\nThe site chosen for the village festivity was one of the lawnlike oases which were occasionally, yet not often, met with on the plateaux of the heath district. The brakes of furze and fern terminated abruptly round the margin, and the grass was unbroken. A green cattletrack skirted the spot, without, however, emerging from the screen of fern, and this path Eustacia followed, in order to reconnoitre the group before joining it. The lusty notes of the East Egdon band had directed her unerringly, and she now beheld the musicians themselves, sitting in a blue wagon with red wheels scrubbed as bright as new, and arched with sticks, to which boughs and flowers were tied. In front of this was the grand central dance of fifteen or twenty couples, flanked by minor dances of inferior individuals whose gyrations were not always in strict keeping with the tune.\n\nThe young men wore blue and white rosettes, and with a flush on their faces footed it to the girls, who, with the excitement and the exercise, blushed deeper than the pink of their numerous ribbons. Fair ones with long curls, fair ones with short curls, fair ones with lovelocks, fair ones with braids, flew round and round; and a beholder might well have wondered how such a prepossessing set of young women of like size, age, and disposition, could have been collected together where there were only one or two villages to choose from. In the background was one happy man dancing by himself, with closed eyes, totally oblivious of all the rest. A fire was burning under a pollard thorn a few paces off, over which three kettles hung in a row. Hard by was a table where elderly dames prepared tea, but Eustacia looked among them in vain for the cattle-dealer’s wife who had suggested that she should come, and had promised to obtain a courteous welcome for her.\nThis unexpected absence of the only local resident whom Eustacia knew considerably damaged her scheme for an afternoon of reckless gaiety. Joining in became a matter of difficulty, notwithstanding that, were she to advance, cheerful dames would come forward with cups of tea and make much of her as a stranger of superior grace and knowledge to themselves. Having watched the company through the figures of two dances, she decided to walk a little further, to a cottage where she might get some refreshment, and then return homeward in the shady time of evening.\n\nThis she did, and by the time that she retraced her steps towards the scene of the gipsying, which it was necessary to repass on her way to Alderworth, the sun was going down. The air was now so still that she could hear the band afar off, and it seemed to be playing with more spirit, if that were possible, than when she had come away. On reaching the hill the sun had quite disappeared; but this made little difference either to Eustacia or to the revellers, for a round yellow moon was rising before her, though its rays had not yet outmastered those from the west. The dance was going on just the same, but strangers had arrived and formed a ring around the figure, so that Eustacia could stand among these without a chance of being recognized.\n\nA whole village-full of sensuous emotion, scattered abroad all the year long, surged here in a focus for an hour. The forty hearts of those waving couples were beating as they had not done since, twelve months before, they had come together in similar jollity. For the time paganism was revived in their hearts, the pride of life was all in all, and they adored none other than themselves.\n\nHow many of those impassioned but temporary embraces were destined to become perpetual was possibly the wonder of some of those who indulged in them, as well as of Eustacia who looked on. She began to envy those pirouetters, to hunger for the hope and happiness which the fascination of the dance seemed to engender within them. Desperately fond of dancing herself, one of Eustacia’s expectations of Paris had been the opportunity it might afford her of indulgence in this favourite pastime. Unhappily, that expectation was now extinct within her for ever.\n\nWhilst she abstractedly watched them spinning and fluctuating in the increasing moonlight she suddenly heard her name whispered by a voice over her shoulder. Turning in surprise, she beheld at her elbow one whose presence instantly caused her to flush to the temples.\n\nIt was Wildeve. Till this moment he had not met her eye since the morning of his marriage, when she had been loitering in the church, and had startled him by lifting her veil and coming forward to sign the register as witness. Yet why the sight of him should have instigated that sudden rush of blood she could not tell.\n\nBefore she could speak he whispered, “Do you like dancing as much as ever?”\n\n“I think I do,” she replied in a low voice.\n\n“Will you dance with me?”\n\n“It would be a great change for me; but will it not seem strange?”\n\n“What strangeness can there be in relations dancing together?”\n\n“Ah—yes, relations. Perhaps none.”\n\n“Still, if you don’t like to be seen, pull down your veil; though there is not much risk of being known by this light. Lots of strangers are here.”\n\nShe did as he suggested; and the act was a tacit acknowledgment that she accepted his offer.\n\nWildeve gave her his arm and took her down on the outside of the ring to the bottom of the dance, which they entered. In two minutes more they were involved in the figure and began working their way upwards to the top. Till they had advanced halfway thither Eustacia wished more than once that she had not yielded to his request; from the middle to the top she felt that, since she had come out to seek pleasure, she was only doing a natural thing to obtain it. Fairly launched into the ceaseless glides and whirls which their new position as top couple opened up to them, Eustacia’s pulses began to move too quickly for long rumination of any kind.\n\nThrough the length of five-and-twenty couples they threaded their giddy way, and a new vitality entered her form. The pale ray of evening lent a fascination to the experience. There is a certain degree and tone of light which tends to disturb the equilibrium of the senses, and to promote dangerously the tenderer moods; added to movement, it drives the emotions to rankness, the reason becoming sleepy and unperceiving in inverse proportion; and this light fell now upon these two from the disc of the moon. All the dancing girls felt the symptoms, but Eustacia most of all. The grass under their feet became trodden away, and the hard, beaten surface of the sod, when viewed aslant towards the moonlight, shone like a polished table. The air became quite still, the flag above the wagon which held the musicians clung to the pole, and the players appeared only in outline against the sky; except when the circular mouths of the trombone, ophicleide, and French horn gleamed out like huge eyes from the shade of their figures. The pretty dresses of the maids lost their subtler day colours and showed more or less of a misty white. Eustacia floated round and round on Wildeve’s arm, her face rapt and statuesque; her soul had passed away from and forgotten her features, which were left empty and quiescent, as they always are when feeling goes beyond their register.\n\nHow near she was to Wildeve! it was terrible to think of. She could feel his breathing, and he, of course, could feel hers. How badly she had treated him! yet, here they were treading one measure. The enchantment of the dance surprised her. A clear line of difference divided like a tangible fence her experience within this maze of motion from her experience without it. Her beginning to dance had been like a change of atmosphere; outside, she had been steeped in arctic frigidity by comparison with the tropical sensations here. She had entered the dance from the troubled hours of her late life as one might enter a brilliant chamber after a night walk in a wood. Wildeve by himself would have been merely an agitation; Wildeve added to the dance, and the moonlight, and the secrecy, began to be a delight. Whether his personality supplied the greater part of this sweetly compounded feeling, or whether the dance and the scene weighed the more therein, was a nice point upon which Eustacia herself was entirely in a cloud.\n\nPeople began to say “Who are they?” but no invidious inquiries were made. Had Eustacia mingled with the other girls in their ordinary daily walks the case would have been different: here she was not inconvenienced by excessive inspection, for all were wrought to their brightest grace by the occasion. Like the planet Mercury surrounded by the lustre of sunset, her permanent brilliancy passed without much notice in the temporary glory of the situation.\n\nAs for Wildeve, his feelings are easy to guess. Obstacles were a ripening sun to his love, and he was at this moment in a delirium of exquisite misery. To clasp as his for five minutes what was another man’s through all the rest of the year was a kind of thing he of all men could appreciate. He had long since begun to sigh again for Eustacia; indeed, it may be asserted that signing the marriage register with Thomasin was the natural signal to his heart to return to its first quarters, and that the extra complication of Eustacia’s marriage was the one addition required to make that return compulsory.\n\nThus, for different reasons, what was to the rest an exhilarating movement was to these two a riding upon the whirlwind. The dance had come like an irresistible attack upon whatever sense of social order there was in their minds, to drive them back into old paths which were now doubly irregular. Through three dances in succession they spun their way; and then, fatigued with the incessant motion, Eustacia turned to quit the circle in which she had already remained too long. Wildeve led her to a grassy mound a few yards distant, where she sat down, her partner standing beside her. From the time that he addressed her at the beginning of the dance till now they had not exchanged a word.\n\n“The dance and the walking have tired you?” he said tenderly.\n\n“No; not greatly.”\n\n“It is strange that we should have met here of all places, after missing each other so long.”\n\n“We have missed because we tried to miss, I suppose.”\n\n“Yes. But you began that proceeding—by breaking a promise.”\n\n“It is scarcely worth while to talk of that now. We have formed other ties since then—you no less than I.”\n\n“I am sorry to hear that your husband is ill.”\n\n“He is not ill—only incapacitated.”\n\n“Yes—that is what I mean. I sincerely sympathize with you in your trouble. Fate has treated you cruelly.”\n\nShe was silent awhile. “Have you heard that he has chosen to work as a furze-cutter?” she said in a low, mournful voice.\n\n“It has been mentioned to me,” answered Wildeve hesitatingly. “But I hardly believed it.”\n\n“It is true. What do you think of me as a furze-cutter’s wife?”\n\n“I think the same as ever of you, Eustacia. Nothing of that sort can degrade you—you ennoble the occupation of your husband.”\n\n“I wish I could feel it.”\n\n“Is there any chance of Mr. Yeobright getting better?”\n\n“He thinks so. I doubt it.”\n\n“I was quite surprised to hear that he had taken a cottage. I thought, in common with other people, that he would have taken you off to a home in Paris immediately after you had married him. ‘What a gay, bright future she has before her!’ I thought. He will, I suppose, return there with you, if his sight gets strong again?”\n\nObserving that she did not reply he regarded her more closely. She was almost weeping. Images of a future never to be enjoyed, the revived sense of her bitter disappointment, the picture of the neighbour’s suspended ridicule which was raised by Wildeve’s words, had been too much for proud Eustacia’s equanimity.\n\nWildeve could hardly control his own too forward feelings when he saw her silent perturbation. But he affected not to notice this, and she soon recovered her calmness.\n\n“You do not intend to walk home by yourself?” he asked.\n\n“O yes,” said Eustacia. “What could hurt me on this heath, who have nothing?”\n\n“By diverging a little I can make my way home the same as yours. I shall be glad to keep you company as far as Throope Corner.” Seeing that Eustacia sat on in hesitation he added, “Perhaps you think it unwise to be seen in the same road with me after the events of last summer?”\n\n“Indeed I think no such thing,” she said haughtily. “I shall accept whose company I choose, for all that may be said by the miserable inhabitants of Egdon.”\n\n“Then let us walk on—if you are ready. Our nearest way is towards that holly bush with the dark shadow that you see down there.”\n\nEustacia arose, and walked beside him in the direction signified, brushing her way over the damping heath and fern, and followed by the strains of the merrymakers, who still kept up the dance. The moon had now waxed bright and silvery, but the heath was proof against such illumination, and there was to be observed the striking scene of a dark, rayless tract of country under an atmosphere charged from its zenith to its extremities with whitest light. To an eye above them their two faces would have appeared amid the expanse like two pearls on a table of ebony.\n\nOn this account the irregularities of the path were not visible, and Wildeve occasionally stumbled; whilst Eustacia found it necessary to perform some graceful feats of balancing whenever a small tuft of heather or root of furze protruded itself through the grass of the narrow track and entangled her feet. At these junctures in her progress a hand was invariably stretched forward to steady her, holding her firmly until smooth ground was again reached, when the hand was again withdrawn to a respectful distance.\n\nThey performed the journey for the most part in silence, and drew near to Throope Corner, a few hundred yards from which a short path branched away to Eustacia’s house. By degrees they discerned coming towards them a pair of human figures, apparently of the male sex.\n\nWhen they came a little nearer Eustacia broke the silence by saying, “One of those men is my husband. He promised to come to meet me.”\n\n“And the other is my greatest enemy,” said Wildeve.\n\n“It looks like Diggory Venn.”\n\n“That is the man.”\n\n“It is an awkward meeting,” said she; “but such is my fortune. He knows too much about me, unless he could know more, and so prove to himself that what he now knows counts for nothing. Well, let it be—you must deliver me up to them.”\n\n“You will think twice before you direct me to do that. Here is a man who has not forgotten an item in our meetings at Rainbarrow—he is in company with your husband. Which of them, seeing us together here, will believe that our meeting and dancing at the gipsy party was by chance?”\n\n“Very well,” she whispered gloomily. “Leave me before they come up.”\n\nWildeve bade her a tender farewell, and plunged across the fern and furze, Eustacia slowly walking on. In two or three minutes she met her husband and his companion.\n\n“My journey ends here for tonight, reddleman,” said Yeobright as soon as he perceived her. “I turn back with this lady. Good night.”\n\n“Good night, Mr. Yeobright,” said Venn. “I hope to see you better soon.”\n\nThe moonlight shone directly upon Venn’s face as he spoke, and revealed all its lines to Eustacia. He was looking suspiciously at her. That Venn’s keen eye had discerned what Yeobright’s feeble vision had not—a man in the act of withdrawing from Eustacia’s side—was within the limits of the probable.\n\nIf Eustacia had been able to follow the reddleman she would soon have found striking confirmation of her thought. No sooner had Clym given her his arm and led her off the scene than the reddleman turned back from the beaten track towards East Egdon, whither he had been strolling merely to accompany Clym in his walk, Diggory’s van being again in the neighbourhood. Stretching out his long legs, he crossed the pathless portion of the heath somewhat in the direction which Wildeve had taken. Only a man accustomed to nocturnal rambles could at this hour have descended those shaggy slopes with Venn’s velocity without falling headlong into a pit, or snapping off his leg by jamming his foot into some rabbit burrow. But Venn went on without much inconvenience to himself, and the course of his scamper was towards the Quiet Woman Inn. This place he reached in about half an hour, and he was well aware that no person who had been near Throope Corner when he started could have got down here before him.\n\nThe lonely inn was not yet closed, though scarcely an individual was there, the business done being chiefly with travellers who passed the inn on long journeys, and these had now gone on their way. Venn went to the public room, called for a mug of ale, and inquired of the maid in an indifferent tone if Mr. Wildeve was at home.\n\nThomasin sat in an inner room and heard Venn’s voice. When customers were present she seldom showed herself, owing to her inherent dislike for the business; but perceiving that no one else was there tonight she came out.\n\n“He is not at home yet, Diggory,” she said pleasantly. “But I expected him sooner. He has been to East Egdon to buy a horse.”\n\n“Did he wear a light wideawake?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then I saw him at Throope Corner, leading one home,” said Venn drily. “A beauty, with a white face and a mane as black as night. He will soon be here, no doubt.” Rising and looking for a moment at the pure, sweet face of Thomasin, over which a shadow of sadness had passed since the time when he had last seen her, he ventured to add, “Mr. Wildeve seems to be often away at this time.”\n\n“O yes,” cried Thomasin in what was intended to be a tone of gaiety. “Husbands will play the truant, you know. I wish you could tell me of some secret plan that would help me to keep him home at my will in the evenings.”\n\n“I will consider if I know of one,” replied Venn in that same light tone which meant no lightness. And then he bowed in a manner of his own invention and moved to go. Thomasin offered him her hand; and without a sigh, though with food for many, the reddleman went out.\n\nWhen Wildeve returned, a quarter of an hour later Thomasin said simply, and in the abashed manner usual with her now, “Where is the horse, Damon?”\n\n“O, I have not bought it, after all. The man asks too much.”\n\n“But somebody saw you at Throope Corner leading it home—a beauty, with a white face and a mane as black as night.”\n\n“Ah!” said Wildeve, fixing his eyes upon her; “who told you that?”\n\n“Venn the reddleman.”\n\nThe expression of Wildeve’s face became curiously condensed. “That is a mistake—it must have been someone else,” he said slowly and testily, for he perceived that Venn’s countermoves had begun again.\n\nIV. Rough Coercion Is Employed\n\nThose words of Thomasin, which seemed so little, but meant so much, remained in the ears of Diggory Venn: “Help me to keep him home in the evenings.”\n\nOn this occasion Venn had arrived on Egdon Heath only to cross to the other side—he had no further connection with the interests of the Yeobright family, and he had a business of his own to attend to. Yet he suddenly began to feel himself drifting into the old track of manœuvring on Thomasin’s account.\n\nHe sat in his van and considered. From Thomasin’s words and manner he had plainly gathered that Wildeve neglected her. For whom could he neglect her if not for Eustacia? Yet it was scarcely credible that things had come to such a head as to indicate that Eustacia systematically encouraged him. Venn resolved to reconnoitre somewhat carefully the lonely road which led along the vale from Wildeve’s dwelling to Clym’s house at Alderworth.\n\nAt this time, as has been seen, Wildeve was quite innocent of any predetermined act of intrigue, and except at the dance on the green he had not once met Eustacia since her marriage. But that the spirit of intrigue was in him had been shown by a recent romantic habit of his—a habit of going out after dark and strolling towards Alderworth, there looking at the moon and stars, looking at Eustacia’s house, and walking back at leisure.\n\nAccordingly, when watching on the night after the festival, the reddleman saw him ascend by the little path, lean over the front gate of Clym’s garden, sigh, and turn to go back again. It was plain that Wildeve’s intrigue was rather ideal than real. Venn retreated before him down the hill to a place where the path was merely a deep groove between the heather; here he mysteriously bent over the ground for a few minutes, and retired. When Wildeve came on to that spot his ankle was caught by something, and he fell headlong.\n\nAs soon as he had recovered the power of respiration he sat up and listened. There was not a sound in the gloom beyond the spiritless stir of the summer wind. Feeling about for the obstacle which had flung him down, he discovered that two tufts of heath had been tied together across the path, forming a loop, which to a traveller was certain overthrow. Wildeve pulled off the string that bound them, and went on with tolerable quickness. On reaching home he found the cord to be of a reddish colour. It was just what he had expected.\n\nAlthough his weaknesses were not specially those akin to physical fear, this species of coup-de-Jarnac from one he knew too well troubled the mind of Wildeve. But his movements were unaltered thereby. A night or two later he again went along the vale to Alderworth, taking the precaution of keeping out of any path. The sense that he was watched, that craft was employed to circumvent his errant tastes, added piquancy to a journey so entirely sentimental, so long as the danger was of no fearful sort. He imagined that Venn and Mrs. Yeobright were in league, and felt that there was a certain legitimacy in combating such a coalition.\n\nThe heath tonight appeared to be totally deserted; and Wildeve, after looking over Eustacia’s garden gate for some little time, with a cigar in his mouth, was tempted by the fascination that emotional smuggling had for his nature to advance towards the window, which was not quite closed, the blind being only partly drawn down. He could see into the room, and Eustacia was sitting there alone. Wildeve contemplated her for a minute, and then retreating into the heath beat the ferns lightly, whereupon moths flew out alarmed. Securing one, he returned to the window, and holding the moth to the chink, opened his hand. The moth made towards the candle upon Eustacia’s table, hovered round it two or three times, and flew into the flame.\n\nEustacia started up. This had been a well-known signal in old times when Wildeve had used to come secretly wooing to Mistover. She at once knew that Wildeve was outside, but before she could consider what to do her husband came in from upstairs. Eustacia’s face burnt crimson at the unexpected collision of incidents, and filled it with an animation that it too frequently lacked.\n\n“You have a very high colour, dearest,” said Yeobright, when he came close enough to see it. “Your appearance would be no worse if it were always so.”\n\n“I am warm,” said Eustacia. “I think I will go into the air for a few minutes.”\n\n“Shall I go with you?”\n\n“O no. I am only going to the gate.”\n\nShe arose, but before she had time to get out of the room a loud rapping began upon the front door.\n\n“I’ll go—I’ll go,” said Eustacia in an unusually quick tone for her; and she glanced eagerly towards the window whence the moth had flown; but nothing appeared there.\n\n“You had better not at this time of the evening,” he said. Clym stepped before her into the passage, and Eustacia waited, her somnolent manner covering her inner heat and agitation.\n\nShe listened, and Clym opened the door. No words were uttered outside, and presently he closed it and came back, saying, “Nobody was there. I wonder what that could have meant?”\n\nHe was left to wonder during the rest of the evening, for no explanation offered itself, and Eustacia said nothing, the additional fact that she knew of only adding more mystery to the performance.\n\nMeanwhile a little drama had been acted outside which saved Eustacia from all possibility of compromising herself that evening at least. Whilst Wildeve had been preparing his moth-signal another person had come behind him up to the gate. This man, who carried a gun in his hand, looked on for a moment at the other’s operation by the window, walked up to the house, knocked at the door, and then vanished round the corner and over the hedge.\n\n“Damn him!” said Wildeve. “He has been watching me again.”\n\nAs his signal had been rendered futile by this uproarious rapping Wildeve withdrew, passed out at the gate, and walked quickly down the path without thinking of anything except getting away unnoticed. Halfway down the hill the path ran near a knot of stunted hollies, which in the general darkness of the scene stood as the pupil in a black eye. When Wildeve reached this point a report startled his ear, and a few spent gunshots fell among the leaves around him.\n\nThere was no doubt that he himself was the cause of that gun’s discharge; and he rushed into the clump of hollies, beating the bushes furiously with his stick; but nobody was there. This attack was a more serious matter than the last, and it was some time before Wildeve recovered his equanimity. A new and most unpleasant system of menace had begun, and the intent appeared to be to do him grievous bodily harm. Wildeve had looked upon Venn’s first attempt as a species of horseplay, which the reddleman had indulged in for want of knowing better; but now the boundary line was passed which divides the annoying from the perilous.\n\nHad Wildeve known how thoroughly in earnest Venn had become he might have been still more alarmed. The reddleman had been almost exasperated by the sight of Wildeve outside Clym’s house, and he was prepared to go to any lengths short of absolutely shooting him, to terrify the young innkeeper out of his recalcitrant impulses. The doubtful legitimacy of such rough coercion did not disturb the mind of Venn. It troubles few such minds in such cases, and sometimes this is not to be regretted. From the impeachment of Strafford to Farmer Lynch’s short way with the scamps of Virginia there have been many triumphs of justice which are mockeries of law.\n\nAbout half a mile below Clym’s secluded dwelling lay a hamlet where lived one of the two constables who preserved the peace in the parish of Alderworth, and Wildeve went straight to the constable’s cottage. Almost the first thing that he saw on opening the door was the constable’s truncheon hanging to a nail, as if to assure him that here were the means to his purpose. On inquiry, however, of the constable’s wife he learnt that the constable was not at home. Wildeve said he would wait.\n\nThe minutes ticked on, and the constable did not arrive. Wildeve cooled down from his state of high indignation to a restless dissatisfaction with himself, the scene, the constable’s wife, and the whole set of circumstances. He arose and left the house. Altogether, the experience of that evening had had a cooling, not to say a chilling, effect on misdirected tenderness, and Wildeve was in no mood to ramble again to Alderworth after nightfall in hope of a stray glance from Eustacia.\n\nThus far the reddleman had been tolerably successful in his rude contrivances for keeping down Wildeve’s inclination to rove in the evening. He had nipped in the bud the possible meeting between Eustacia and her old lover this very night. But he had not anticipated that the tendency of his action would be to divert Wildeve’s movement rather than to stop it. The gambling with the guineas had not conduced to make him a welcome guest to Clym; but to call upon his wife’s relative was natural, and he was determined to see Eustacia. It was necessary to choose some less untoward hour than ten o’clock at night. “Since it is unsafe to go in the evening,” he said, “I’ll go by day.”\n\nMeanwhile Venn had left the heath and gone to call upon Mrs. Yeobright, with whom he had been on friendly terms since she had learnt what a providential countermove he had made towards the restitution of the family guineas. She wondered at the lateness of his call, but had no objection to see him.\n\nHe gave her a full account of Clym’s affliction, and of the state in which he was living; then, referring to Thomasin, touched gently upon the apparent sadness of her days. “Now, ma’am, depend upon it,” he said, “you couldn’t do a better thing for either of ’em than to make yourself at home in their houses, even if there should be a little rebuff at first.”\n\n“Both she and my son disobeyed me in marrying; therefore I have no interest in their households. Their troubles are of their own making.” Mrs. Yeobright tried to speak severely; but the account of her son’s state had moved her more than she cared to show.\n\n“Your visits would make Wildeve walk straighter than he is inclined to do, and might prevent unhappiness down the heath.”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“I saw something tonight out there which I didn’t like at all. I wish your son’s house and Mr. Wildeve’s were a hundred miles apart instead of four or five.”\n\n“Then there _was_ an understanding between him and Clym’s wife when he made a fool of Thomasin!”\n\n“We’ll hope there’s no understanding now.”\n\n“And our hope will probably be very vain. O Clym! O Thomasin!”\n\n“There’s no harm done yet. In fact, I’ve persuaded Wildeve to mind his own business.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“O, not by talking—by a plan of mine called the silent system.”\n\n“I hope you’ll succeed.”\n\n“I shall if you help me by calling and making friends with your son. You’ll have a chance then of using your eyes.”\n\n“Well, since it has come to this,” said Mrs. Yeobright sadly, “I will own to you, reddleman, that I thought of going. I should be much happier if we were reconciled. The marriage is unalterable, my life may be cut short, and I should wish to die in peace. He is my only son; and since sons are made of such stuff I am not sorry I have no other. As for Thomasin, I never expected much from her; and she has not disappointed me. But I forgave her long ago; and I forgive him now. I’ll go.”\n\nAt this very time of the reddleman’s conversation with Mrs. Yeobright at Blooms-End another conversation on the same subject was languidly proceeding at Alderworth.\n\nAll the day Clym had borne himself as if his mind were too full of its own matter to allow him to care about outward things, and his words now showed what had occupied his thoughts. It was just after the mysterious knocking that he began the theme. “Since I have been away today, Eustacia, I have considered that something must be done to heal up this ghastly breach between my dear mother and myself. It troubles me.”\n\n“What do you propose to do?” said Eustacia abstractedly, for she could not clear away from her the excitement caused by Wildeve’s recent manœuvre for an interview.\n\n“You seem to take a very mild interest in what I propose, little or much,” said Clym, with tolerable warmth.\n\n“You mistake me,” she answered, reviving at his reproach. “I am only thinking.”\n\n“What of?”\n\n“Partly of that moth whose skeleton is getting burnt up in the wick of the candle,” she said slowly. “But you know I always take an interest in what you say.”\n\n“Very well, dear. Then I think I must go and call upon her.” ...He went on with tender feeling: “It is a thing I am not at all too proud to do, and only a fear that I might irritate her has kept me away so long. But I must do something. It is wrong in me to allow this sort of thing to go on.”\n\n“What have you to blame yourself about?”\n\n“She is getting old, and her life is lonely, and I am her only son.”\n\n“She has Thomasin.”\n\n“Thomasin is not her daughter; and if she were that would not excuse me. But this is beside the point. I have made up my mind to go to her, and all I wish to ask you is whether you will do your best to help me—that is, forget the past; and if she shows her willingness to be reconciled, meet her halfway by welcoming her to our house, or by accepting a welcome to hers?”\n\nAt first Eustacia closed her lips as if she would rather do anything on the whole globe than what he suggested. But the lines of her mouth softened with thought, though not so far as they might have softened, and she said, “I will put nothing in your way; but after what has passed it is asking too much that I go and make advances.”\n\n“You never distinctly told me what did pass between you.”\n\n“I could not do it then, nor can I now. Sometimes more bitterness is sown in five minutes than can be got rid of in a whole life; and that may be the case here.” She paused a few moments, and added, “If you had never returned to your native place, Clym, what a blessing it would have been for you!... It has altered the destinies of——”\n\n“Three people.”\n\n“Five,” Eustacia thought; but she kept that in.\n\nV. The Journey across the Heath\n\nThursday, the thirty-first of August, was one of a series of days during which snug houses were stifling, and when cool draughts were treats; when cracks appeared in clayey gardens, and were called “earthquakes” by apprehensive children; when loose spokes were discovered in the wheels of carts and carriages; and when stinging insects haunted the air, the earth, and every drop of water that was to be found.\n\nIn Mrs. Yeobright’s garden large-leaved plants of a tender kind flagged by ten o’clock in the morning; rhubarb bent downward at eleven; and even stiff cabbages were limp by noon.\n\nIt was about eleven o’clock on this day that Mrs. Yeobright started across the heath towards her son’s house, to do her best in getting reconciled with him and Eustacia, in conformity with her words to the reddleman. She had hoped to be well advanced in her walk before the heat of the day was at its highest, but after setting out she found that this was not to be done. The sun had branded the whole heath with its mark, even the purple heath-flowers having put on a brownness under the dry blazes of the few preceding days. Every valley was filled with air like that of a kiln, and the clean quartz sand of the winter water-courses, which formed summer paths, had undergone a species of incineration since the drought had set in.\n\nIn cool, fresh weather Mrs. Yeobright would have found no inconvenience in walking to Alderworth, but the present torrid attack made the journey a heavy undertaking for a woman past middle age; and at the end of the third mile she wished that she had hired Fairway to drive her a portion at least of the distance. But from the point at which she had arrived it was as easy to reach Clym’s house as to get home again. So she went on, the air around her pulsating silently, and oppressing the earth with lassitude. She looked at the sky overhead, and saw that the sapphirine hue of the zenith in spring and early summer had been replaced by a metallic violet.\n\nOccasionally she came to a spot where independent worlds of ephemerons were passing their time in mad carousal, some in the air, some on the hot ground and vegetation, some in the tepid and stringy water of a nearly dried pool. All the shallower ponds had decreased to a vaporous mud amid which the maggoty shapes of innumerable obscure creatures could be indistinctly seen, heaving and wallowing with enjoyment. Being a woman not disinclined to philosophize she sometimes sat down under her umbrella to rest and to watch their happiness, for a certain hopefulness as to the result of her visit gave ease to her mind, and between important thoughts left it free to dwell on any infinitesimal matter which caught her eyes.\n\nMrs. Yeobright had never before been to her son’s house, and its exact position was unknown to her. She tried one ascending path and another, and found that they led her astray. Retracing her steps, she came again to an open level, where she perceived at a distance a man at work. She went towards him and inquired the way.\n\nThe labourer pointed out the direction, and added, “Do you see that furze-cutter, ma’am, going up that footpath yond?”\n\nMrs. Yeobright strained her eyes, and at last said that she did perceive him.\n\n“Well, if you follow him you can make no mistake. He’s going to the same place, ma’am.”\n\nShe followed the figure indicated. He appeared of a russet hue, not more distinguishable from the scene around him than the green caterpillar from the leaf it feeds on. His progress when actually walking was more rapid than Mrs. Yeobright’s; but she was enabled to keep at an equable distance from him by his habit of stopping whenever he came to a brake of brambles, where he paused awhile. On coming in her turn to each of these spots she found half a dozen long limp brambles which he had cut from the bush during his halt and laid out straight beside the path. They were evidently intended for furze-faggot bonds which he meant to collect on his return.\n\nThe silent being who thus occupied himself seemed to be of no more account in life than an insect. He appeared as a mere parasite of the heath, fretting its surface in his daily labour as a moth frets a garment, entirely engrossed with its products, having no knowledge of anything in the world but fern, furze, heath, lichens, and moss.\n\nThe furze-cutter was so absorbed in the business of his journey that he never turned his head; and his leather-legged and gauntleted form at length became to her as nothing more than a moving handpost to show her the way. Suddenly she was attracted to his individuality by observing peculiarities in his walk. It was a gait she had seen somewhere before; and the gait revealed the man to her, as the gait of Ahimaaz in the distant plain made him known to the watchman of the king. “His walk is exactly as my husband’s used to be,” she said; and then the thought burst upon her that the furze-cutter was her son.\n\nShe was scarcely able to familiarize herself with this strange reality. She had been told that Clym was in the habit of cutting furze, but she had supposed that he occupied himself with the labour only at odd times, by way of useful pastime; yet she now beheld him as a furze-cutter and nothing more—wearing the regulation dress of the craft, and thinking the regulation thoughts, to judge by his motions. Planning a dozen hasty schemes for at once preserving him and Eustacia from this mode of life, she throbbingly followed the way, and saw him enter his own door.\n\nAt one side of Clym’s house was a knoll, and on the top of the knoll a clump of fir trees so highly thrust up into the sky that their foliage from a distance appeared as a black spot in the air above the crown of the hill. On reaching this place Mrs. Yeobright felt distressingly agitated, weary, and unwell. She ascended, and sat down under their shade to recover herself, and to consider how best to break the ground with Eustacia, so as not to irritate a woman underneath whose apparent indolence lurked passions even stronger and more active than her own.\n\nThe trees beneath which she sat were singularly battered, rude, and wild, and for a few minutes Mrs. Yeobright dismissed thoughts of her own storm-broken and exhausted state to contemplate theirs. Not a bough in the nine trees which composed the group but was splintered, lopped, and distorted by the fierce weather that there held them at its mercy whenever it prevailed. Some were blasted and split as if by lightning, black stains as from fire marking their sides, while the ground at their feet was strewn with dead fir-needles and heaps of cones blown down in the gales of past years. The place was called the Devil’s Bellows, and it was only necessary to come there on a March or November night to discover the forcible reasons for that name. On the present heated afternoon, when no perceptible wind was blowing, the trees kept up a perpetual moan which one could hardly believe to be caused by the air.\n\nHere she sat for twenty minutes or more ere she could summon resolution to go down to the door, her courage being lowered to zero by her physical lassitude. To any other person than a mother it might have seemed a little humiliating that she, the elder of the two women, should be the first to make advances. But Mrs. Yeobright had well considered all that, and she only thought how best to make her visit appear to Eustacia not abject but wise.\n\nFrom her elevated position the exhausted woman could perceive the roof of the house below, and the garden and the whole enclosure of the little domicile. And now, at the moment of rising, she saw a second man approaching the gate. His manner was peculiar, hesitating, and not that of a person come on business or by invitation. He surveyed the house with interest, and then walked round and scanned the outer boundary of the garden, as one might have done had it been the birthplace of Shakespeare, the prison of Mary Stuart, or the Château of Hougomont. After passing round and again reaching the gate he went in. Mrs. Yeobright was vexed at this, having reckoned on finding her son and his wife by themselves; but a moment’s thought showed her that the presence of an acquaintance would take off the awkwardness of her first appearance in the house, by confining the talk to general matters until she had begun to feel comfortable with them. She came down the hill to the gate, and looked into the hot garden.\n\nThere lay the cat asleep on the bare gravel of the path, as if beds, rugs, and carpets were unendurable. The leaves of the hollyhocks hung like half-closed umbrellas, the sap almost simmered in the stems, and foliage with a smooth surface glared like metallic mirrors. A small apple tree, of the sort called Ratheripe, grew just inside the gate, the only one which throve in the garden, by reason of the lightness of the soil; and among the fallen apples on the ground beneath were wasps rolling drunk with the juice, or creeping about the little caves in each fruit which they had eaten out before stupefied by its sweetness. By the door lay Clym’s furze-hook and the last handful of faggot-bonds she had seen him gather; they had plainly been thrown down there as he entered the house.\n\nVI. A Conjuncture, and Its Result upon the Pedestrian\n\nWildeve, as has been stated, was determined to visit Eustacia boldly, by day, and on the easy terms of a relation, since the reddleman had spied out and spoilt his walks to her by night. The spell that she had thrown over him in the moonlight dance made it impossible for a man having no strong puritanic force within him to keep away altogether. He merely calculated on meeting her and her husband in an ordinary manner, chatting a little while, and leaving again. Every outward sign was to be conventional; but the one great fact would be there to satisfy him—he would see her. He did not even desire Clym’s absence, since it was just possible that Eustacia might resent any situation which could compromise her dignity as a wife, whatever the state of her heart towards him. Women were often so.\n\nHe went accordingly; and it happened that the time of his arrival coincided with that of Mrs. Yeobright’s pause on the hill near the house. When he had looked round the premises in the manner she had noticed he went and knocked at the door. There was a few minutes’ interval, and then the key turned in the lock, the door opened, and Eustacia herself confronted him.\n\nNobody could have imagined from her bearing now that here stood the woman who had joined with him in the impassioned dance of the week before, unless indeed he could have penetrated below the surface and gauged the real depth of that still stream.\n\n“I hope you reached home safely?” said Wildeve.\n\n“O yes,” she carelessly returned.\n\n“And were you not tired the next day? I feared you might be.”\n\n“I was rather. You need not speak low—nobody will over-hear us. My small servant is gone on an errand to the village.”\n\n“Then Clym is not at home?”\n\n“Yes, he is.”\n\n“O! I thought that perhaps you had locked the door because you were alone and were afraid of tramps.”\n\n“No—here is my husband.”\n\nThey had been standing in the entry. Closing the front door and turning the key, as before, she threw open the door of the adjoining room and asked him to walk in. Wildeve entered, the room appearing to be empty; but as soon as he had advanced a few steps he started. On the hearthrug lay Clym asleep. Beside him were the leggings, thick boots, leather gloves, and sleeve-waistcoat in which he worked.\n\n“You may go in; you will not disturb him,” she said, following behind. “My reason for fastening the door is that he may not be intruded upon by any chance comer while lying here, if I should be in the garden or upstairs.”\n\n“Why is he sleeping there?” said Wildeve in low tones.\n\n“He is very weary. He went out at half-past four this morning, and has been working ever since. He cuts furze because it is the only thing he can do that does not put any strain upon his poor eyes.” The contrast between the sleeper’s appearance and Wildeve’s at this moment was painfully apparent to Eustacia, Wildeve being elegantly dressed in a new summer suit and light hat; and she continued: “Ah! you don’t know how differently he appeared when I first met him, though it is such a little while ago. His hands were as white and soft as mine; and look at them now, how rough and brown they are! His complexion is by nature fair, and that rusty look he has now, all of a colour with his leather clothes, is caused by the burning of the sun.”\n\n“Why does he go out at all!” Wildeve whispered.\n\n“Because he hates to be idle; though what he earns doesn’t add much to our exchequer. However, he says that when people are living upon their capital they must keep down current expenses by turning a penny where they can.”\n\n“The fates have not been kind to you, Eustacia Yeobright.”\n\n“I have nothing to thank them for.”\n\n“Nor has he—except for their one great gift to him.”\n\n“What’s that?”\n\nWildeve looked her in the eyes.\n\nEustacia blushed for the first time that day. “Well, I am a questionable gift,” she said quietly. “I thought you meant the gift of content—which he has, and I have not.”\n\n“I can understand content in such a case—though how the outward situation can attract him puzzles me.”\n\n“That’s because you don’t know him. He’s an enthusiast about ideas, and careless about outward things. He often reminds me of the Apostle Paul.”\n\n“I am glad to hear that he’s so grand in character as that.”\n\n“Yes; but the worst of it is that though Paul was excellent as a man in the Bible he would hardly have done in real life.”\n\nTheir voices had instinctively dropped lower, though at first they had taken no particular care to avoid awakening Clym. “Well, if that means that your marriage is a misfortune to you, you know who is to blame,” said Wildeve.\n\n“The marriage is no misfortune in itself,” she retorted with some little petulance. “It is simply the accident which has happened since that has been the cause of my ruin. I have certainly got thistles for figs in a worldly sense, but how could I tell what time would bring forth?”\n\n“Sometimes, Eustacia, I think it is a judgment upon you. You rightly belonged to me, you know; and I had no idea of losing you.”\n\n“No, it was not my fault! Two could not belong to you; and remember that, before I was aware, you turned aside to another woman. It was cruel levity in you to do that. I never dreamt of playing such a game on my side till you began it on yours.”\n\n“I meant nothing by it,” replied Wildeve. “It was a mere interlude. Men are given to the trick of having a passing fancy for somebody else in the midst of a permanent love, which reasserts itself afterwards just as before. On account of your rebellious manner to me I was tempted to go further than I should have done; and when you still would keep playing the same tantalizing part I went further still, and married her.” Turning and looking again at the unconscious form of Clym, he murmured, “I am afraid that you don’t value your prize, Clym.... He ought to be happier than I in one thing at least. He may know what it is to come down in the world, and to be afflicted with a great personal calamity; but he probably doesn’t know what it is to lose the woman he loved.”\n\n“He is not ungrateful for winning her,” whispered Eustacia, “and in that respect he is a good man. Many women would go far for such a husband. But do I desire unreasonably much in wanting what is called life—music, poetry, passion, war, and all the beating and pulsing that are going on in the great arteries of the world? That was the shape of my youthful dream; but I did not get it. Yet I thought I saw the way to it in my Clym.”\n\n“And you only married him on that account?”\n\n“There you mistake me. I married him because I loved him, but I won’t say that I didn’t love him partly because I thought I saw a promise of that life in him.”\n\n“You have dropped into your old mournful key.”\n\n“But I am not going to be depressed,” she cried perversely. “I began a new system by going to that dance, and I mean to stick to it. Clym can sing merrily; why should not I?”\n\nWildeve looked thoughtfully at her. “It is easier to say you will sing than to do it; though if I could I would encourage you in your attempt. But as life means nothing to me, without one thing which is now impossible, you will forgive me for not being able to encourage you.”\n\n“Damon, what is the matter with you, that you speak like that?” she asked, raising her deep shady eyes to his.\n\n“That’s a thing I shall never tell plainly; and perhaps if I try to tell you in riddles you will not care to guess them.”\n\nEustacia remained silent for a minute, and she said, “We are in a strange relationship today. You mince matters to an uncommon nicety. You mean, Damon, that you still love me. Well, that gives me sorrow, for I am not made so entirely happy by my marriage that I am willing to spurn you for the information, as I ought to do. But we have said too much about this. Do you mean to wait until my husband is awake?”\n\n“I thought to speak to him; but it is unnecessary, Eustacia, if I offend you by not forgetting you, you are right to mention it; but do not talk of spurning.”\n\nShe did not reply, and they stood looking musingly at Clym as he slept on in that profound sleep which is the result of physical labour carried on in circumstances that wake no nervous fear.\n\n“God, how I envy him that sweet sleep!” said Wildeve. “I have not slept like that since I was a boy—years and years ago.”\n\nWhile they thus watched him a click at the gate was audible, and a knock came to the door. Eustacia went to a window and looked out.\n\nHer countenance changed. First she became crimson, and then the red subsided till it even partially left her lips.\n\n“Shall I go away?” said Wildeve, standing up.\n\n“I hardly know.”\n\n“Who is it?”\n\n“Mrs. Yeobright. O, what she said to me that day! I cannot understand this visit—what does she mean? And she suspects that past time of ours.”\n\n“I am in your hands. If you think she had better not see me here I’ll go into the next room.”\n\n“Well, yes—go.”\n\nWildeve at once withdrew; but before he had been half a minute in the adjoining apartment Eustacia came after him.\n\n“No,” she said, “we won’t have any of this. If she comes in she must see you—and think if she likes there’s something wrong! But how can I open the door to her, when she dislikes me—wishes to see not me, but her son? I won’t open the door!”\n\nMrs. Yeobright knocked again more loudly.\n\n“Her knocking will, in all likelihood, awaken him,” continued Eustacia, “and then he will let her in himself. Ah—listen.”\n\nThey could hear Clym moving in the other room, as if disturbed by the knocking, and he uttered the word “Mother.”\n\n“Yes—he is awake—he will go to the door,” she said, with a breath of relief. “Come this way. I have a bad name with her, and you must not be seen. Thus I am obliged to act by stealth, not because I do ill, but because others are pleased to say so.”\n\nBy this time she had taken him to the back door, which was open, disclosing a path leading down the garden. “Now, one word, Damon,” she remarked as he stepped forth. “This is your first visit here; let it be your last. We have been hot lovers in our time, but it won’t do now. Good-bye.”\n\n“Good-bye,” said Wildeve. “I have had all I came for, and I am satisfied.”\n\n“What was it?”\n\n“A sight of you. Upon my eternal honour I came for no more.”\n\nWildeve kissed his hand to the beautiful girl he addressed, and passed into the garden, where she watched him down the path, over the stile at the end, and into the ferns outside, which brushed his hips as he went along till he became lost in their thickets. When he had quite gone she slowly turned, and directed her attention to the interior of the house.\n\nBut it was possible that her presence might not be desired by Clym and his mother at this moment of their first meeting, or that it would be superfluous. At all events, she was in no hurry to meet Mrs. Yeobright. She resolved to wait till Clym came to look for her, and glided back into the garden. Here she idly occupied herself for a few minutes, till finding no notice was taken of her she retraced her steps through the house to the front, where she listened for voices in the parlour. But hearing none she opened the door and went in. To her astonishment Clym lay precisely as Wildeve and herself had left him, his sleep apparently unbroken. He had been disturbed and made to dream and murmur by the knocking, but he had not awakened. Eustacia hastened to the door, and in spite of her reluctance to open it to a woman who had spoken of her so bitterly, she unfastened it and looked out. Nobody was to be seen. There, by the scraper, lay Clym’s hook and the handful of faggot-bonds he had brought home; in front of her were the empty path, the garden gate standing slightly ajar; and, beyond, the great valley of purple heath thrilling silently in the sun. Mrs. Yeobright was gone.\n\nClym’s mother was at this time following a path which lay hidden from Eustacia by a shoulder of the hill. Her walk thither from the garden gate had been hasty and determined, as of a woman who was now no less anxious to escape from the scene than she had previously been to enter it. Her eyes were fixed on the ground; within her two sights were graven—that of Clym’s hook and brambles at the door, and that of a woman’s face at a window. Her lips trembled, becoming unnaturally thin as she murmured, “’Tis too much—Clym, how can he bear to do it! He is at home; and yet he lets her shut the door against me!”\n\nIn her anxiety to get out of the direct view of the house she had diverged from the straightest path homeward, and while looking about to regain it she came upon a little boy gathering whortleberries in a hollow. The boy was Johnny Nunsuch, who had been Eustacia’s stoker at the bonfire, and, with the tendency of a minute body to gravitate towards a greater, he began hovering round Mrs. Yeobright as soon as she appeared, and trotted on beside her without perceptible consciousness of his act.\n\nMrs. Yeobright spoke to him as one in a mesmeric sleep. “’Tis a long way home, my child, and we shall not get there till evening.”\n\n“I shall,” said her small companion. “I am going to play marnels afore supper, and we go to supper at six o’clock, because Father comes home. Does your father come home at six too?”\n\n“No, he never comes; nor my son either, nor anybody.”\n\n“What have made you so down? Have you seen a ooser?”\n\n“I have seen what’s worse—a woman’s face looking at me through a windowpane.”\n\n“Is that a bad sight?”\n\n“Yes. It is always a bad sight to see a woman looking out at a weary wayfarer and not letting her in.”\n\n“Once when I went to Throope Great Pond to catch effets I seed myself looking up at myself, and I was frightened and jumped back like anything.”\n\n...“If they had only shown signs of meeting my advances halfway how well it might have been done! But there is no chance. Shut out! She must have set him against me. Can there be beautiful bodies without hearts inside? I think so. I would not have done it against a neighbour’s cat on such a fiery day as this!”\n\n“What is it you say?”\n\n“Never again—never! Not even if they send for me!”\n\n“You must be a very curious woman to talk like that.”\n\n“O no, not at all,” she said, returning to the boy’s prattle. “Most people who grow up and have children talk as I do. When you grow up your mother will talk as I do too.”\n\n“I hope she won’t; because ’tis very bad to talk nonsense.”\n\n“Yes, child; it is nonsense, I suppose. Are you not nearly spent with the heat?”\n\n“Yes. But not so much as you be.”\n\n“How do you know?”\n\n“Your face is white and wet, and your head is hanging-down-like.”\n\n“Ah, I am exhausted from inside.”\n\n“Why do you, every time you take a step, go like this?” The child in speaking gave to his motion the jerk and limp of an invalid.\n\n“Because I have a burden which is more than I can bear.”\n\nThe little boy remained silently pondering, and they tottered on side by side until more than a quarter of an hour had elapsed, when Mrs. Yeobright, whose weakness plainly increased, said to him, “I must sit down here to rest.”\n\nWhen she had seated herself he looked long in her face and said, “How funny you draw your breath—like a lamb when you drive him till he’s nearly done for. Do you always draw your breath like that?”\n\n“Not always.” Her voice was now so low as to be scarcely above a whisper.\n\n“You will go to sleep there, I suppose, won’t you? You have shut your eyes already.”\n\n“No. I shall not sleep much till—another day, and then I hope to have a long, long one—very long. Now can you tell me if Rimsmoor Pond is dry this summer?”\n\n“Rimsmoor Pond is, but Oker’s Pool isn’t, because he is deep, and is never dry—’tis just over there.”\n\n“Is the water clear?”\n\n“Yes, middling—except where the heath-croppers walk into it.”\n\n“Then, take this, and go as fast as you can, and dip me up the clearest you can find. I am very faint.”\n\nShe drew from the small willow reticule that she carried in her hand an old-fashioned china teacup without a handle; it was one of half a dozen of the same sort lying in the reticule, which she had preserved ever since her childhood, and had brought with her today as a small present for Clym and Eustacia.\n\nThe boy started on his errand, and soon came back with the water, such as it was. Mrs. Yeobright attempted to drink, but it was so warm as to give her nausea, and she threw it away. Afterwards she still remained sitting, with her eyes closed.\n\nThe boy waited, played near her, caught several of the little brown butterflies which abounded, and then said as he waited again, “I like going on better than biding still. Will you soon start again?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“I wish I might go on by myself,” he resumed, fearing, apparently, that he was to be pressed into some unpleasant service. “Do you want me any more, please?”\n\nMrs. Yeobright made no reply.\n\n“What shall I tell Mother?” the boy continued.\n\n“Tell her you have seen a broken-hearted woman cast off by her son.”\n\nBefore quite leaving her he threw upon her face a wistful glance, as if he had misgivings on the generosity of forsaking her thus. He gazed into her face in a vague, wondering manner, like that of one examining some strange old manuscript the key to whose characters is undiscoverable. He was not so young as to be absolutely without a sense that sympathy was demanded, he was not old enough to be free from the terror felt in childhood at beholding misery in adult quarters hitherto deemed impregnable; and whether she were in a position to cause trouble or to suffer from it, whether she and her affliction were something to pity or something to fear, it was beyond him to decide. He lowered his eyes and went on without another word. Before he had gone half a mile he had forgotten all about her, except that she was a woman who had sat down to rest.\n\nMrs. Yeobright’s exertions, physical and emotional, had well-nigh prostrated her; but she continued to creep along in short stages with long breaks between. The sun had now got far to the west of south and stood directly in her face, like some merciless incendiary, brand in hand, waiting to consume her. With the departure of the boy all visible animation disappeared from the landscape, though the intermittent husky notes of the male grasshoppers from every tuft of furze were enough to show that amid the prostration of the larger animal species an unseen insect world was busy in all the fullness of life.\n\nIn two hours she reached a slope about three-fourths the whole distance from Alderworth to her own home, where a little patch of shepherd’s-thyme intruded upon the path; and she sat down upon the perfumed mat it formed there. In front of her a colony of ants had established a thoroughfare across the way, where they toiled a never-ending and heavy-laden throng. To look down upon them was like observing a city street from the top of a tower. She remembered that this bustle of ants had been in progress for years at the same spot—doubtless those of the old times were the ancestors of these which walked there now. She leant back to obtain more thorough rest, and the soft eastern portion of the sky was as great a relief to her eyes as the thyme was to her head. While she looked a heron arose on that side of the sky and flew on with his face towards the sun. He had come dripping wet from some pool in the valleys, and as he flew the edges and lining of his wings, his thighs and his breast were so caught by the bright sunbeams that he appeared as if formed of burnished silver. Up in the zenith where he was seemed a free and happy place, away from all contact with the earthly ball to which she was pinioned; and she wished that she could arise uncrushed from its surface and fly as he flew then.\n\nBut, being a mother, it was inevitable that she should soon cease to ruminate upon her own condition. Had the track of her next thought been marked by a streak in the air, like the path of a meteor, it would have shown a direction contrary to the heron’s, and have descended to the eastward upon the roof of Clym’s house.\n\nVII. The Tragic Meeting of Two Old Friends\n\nHe in the meantime had aroused himself from sleep, sat up, and looked around. Eustacia was sitting in a chair hard by him, and though she held a book in her hand she had not looked into it for some time.\n\n“Well, indeed!” said Clym, brushing his eyes with his hands. “How soundly I have slept! I have had such a tremendous dream, too—one I shall never forget.”\n\n“I thought you had been dreaming,” said she.\n\n“Yes. It was about my mother. I dreamt that I took you to her house to make up differences, and when we got there we couldn’t get in, though she kept on crying to us for help. However, dreams are dreams. What o’clock is it, Eustacia?”\n\n“Half-past two.”\n\n“So late, is it? I didn’t mean to stay so long. By the time I have had something to eat it will be after three.”\n\n“Ann is not come back from the village, and I thought I would let you sleep on till she returned.”\n\nClym went to the window and looked out. Presently he said, musingly, “Week after week passes, and yet Mother does not come. I thought I should have heard something from her long before this.”\n\nMisgiving, regret, fear, resolution, ran their swift course of expression in Eustacia’s dark eyes. She was face to face with a monstrous difficulty, and she resolved to get free of it by postponement.\n\n“I must certainly go to Blooms-End soon,” he continued, “and I think I had better go alone.” He picked up his leggings and gloves, threw them down again, and added, “As dinner will be so late today I will not go back to the heath, but work in the garden till the evening, and then, when it will be cooler, I will walk to Blooms-End. I am quite sure that if I make a little advance Mother will be willing to forget all. It will be rather late before I can get home, as I shall not be able to do the distance either way in less than an hour and a half. But you will not mind for one evening, dear? What are you thinking of to make you look so abstracted?”\n\n“I cannot tell you,” she said heavily. “I wish we didn’t live here, Clym. The world seems all wrong in this place.”\n\n“Well—if we make it so. I wonder if Thomasin has been to Blooms-End lately. I hope so. But probably not, as she is, I believe, expecting to be confined in a month or so. I wish I had thought of that before. Poor Mother must indeed be very lonely.”\n\n“I don’t like you going tonight.”\n\n“Why not tonight?”\n\n“Something may be said which will terribly injure me.”\n\n“My mother is not vindictive,” said Clym, his colour faintly rising.\n\n“But I wish you would not go,” Eustacia repeated in a low tone. “If you agree not to go tonight I promise to go by myself to her house tomorrow, and make it up with her, and wait till you fetch me.”\n\n“Why do you want to do that at this particular time, when at every previous time that I have proposed it you have refused?”\n\n“I cannot explain further than that I should like to see her alone before you go,” she answered, with an impatient move of her head, and looking at him with an anxiety more frequently seen upon those of a sanguine temperament than upon such as herself.\n\n“Well, it is very odd that just when I had decided to go myself you should want to do what I proposed long ago. If I wait for you to go tomorrow another day will be lost; and I know I shall be unable to rest another night without having been. I want to get this settled, and will. You must visit her afterwards—it will be all the same.”\n\n“I could even go with you now?”\n\n“You could scarcely walk there and back without a longer rest than I shall take. No, not tonight, Eustacia.”\n\n“Let it be as you say, then,” she replied in the quiet way of one who, though willing to ward off evil consequences by a mild effort, would let events fall out as they might sooner than wrestle hard to direct them.\n\nClym then went into the garden; and a thoughtful languor stole over Eustacia for the remainder of the afternoon, which her husband attributed to the heat of the weather.\n\nIn the evening he set out on the journey. Although the heat of summer was yet intense the days had considerably shortened, and before he had advanced a mile on his way all the heath purples, browns, and greens had merged in a uniform dress without airiness or graduation, and broken only by touches of white where the little heaps of clean quartz sand showed the entrance to a rabbit burrow, or where the white flints of a footpath lay like a thread over the slopes. In almost every one of the isolated and stunted thorns which grew here and there a nighthawk revealed his presence by whirring like the clack of a mill as long as he could hold his breath, then stopping, flapping his wings, wheeling round the bush, alighting, and after a silent interval of listening beginning to whirr again. At each brushing of Clym’s feet white millermoths flew into the air just high enough to catch upon their dusty wings the mellowed light from the west, which now shone across the depressions and levels of the ground without falling thereon to light them up.\n\nYeobright walked on amid this quiet scene with a hope that all would soon be well. Three miles on he came to a spot where a soft perfume was wafted across his path, and he stood still for a moment to inhale the familiar scent. It was the place at which, four hours earlier, his mother had sat down exhausted on the knoll covered with shepherd’s-thyme. While he stood a sound between a breathing and a moan suddenly reached his ears.\n\nHe looked to where the sound came from; but nothing appeared there save the verge of the hillock stretching against the sky in an unbroken line. He moved a few steps in that direction, and now he perceived a recumbent figure almost close to his feet.\n\nAmong the different possibilities as to the person’s individuality there did not for a moment occur to Yeobright that it might be one of his own family. Sometimes furze-cutters had been known to sleep out of doors at these times, to save a long journey homeward and back again; but Clym remembered the moan and looked closer, and saw that the form was feminine; and a distress came over him like cold air from a cave. But he was not absolutely certain that the woman was his mother till he stooped and beheld her face, pallid, and with closed eyes.\n\nHis breath went, as it were, out of his body and the cry of anguish which would have escaped him died upon his lips. During the momentary interval that elapsed before he became conscious that something must be done all sense of time and place left him, and it seemed as if he and his mother were as when he was a child with her many years ago on this heath at hours similar to the present. Then he awoke to activity; and bending yet lower he found that she still breathed, and that her breath though feeble was regular, except when disturbed by an occasional gasp.\n\n“O, what is it! Mother, are you very ill—you are not dying?” he cried, pressing his lips to her face. “I am your Clym. How did you come here? What does it all mean?”\n\nAt that moment the chasm in their lives which his love for Eustacia had caused was not remembered by Yeobright, and to him the present joined continuously with that friendly past that had been their experience before the division.\n\nShe moved her lips, appeared to know him, but could not speak; and then Clym strove to consider how best to move her, as it would be necessary to get her away from the spot before the dews were intense. He was able-bodied, and his mother was thin. He clasped his arms round her, lifted her a little, and said, “Does that hurt you?”\n\nShe shook her head, and he lifted her up; then, at a slow pace, went onward with his load. The air was now completely cool; but whenever he passed over a sandy patch of ground uncarpeted with vegetation there was reflected from its surface into his face the heat which it had imbibed during the day. At the beginning of his undertaking he had thought but little of the distance which yet would have to be traversed before Blooms-End could be reached; but though he had slept that afternoon he soon began to feel the weight of his burden. Thus he proceeded, like Æneas with his father; the bats circling round his head, nightjars flapping their wings within a yard of his face, and not a human being within call.\n\nWhile he was yet nearly a mile from the house his mother exhibited signs of restlessness under the constraint of being borne along, as if his arms were irksome to her. He lowered her upon his knees and looked around. The point they had now reached, though far from any road, was not more than a mile from the Blooms-End cottages occupied by Fairway, Sam, Humphrey, and the Cantles. Moreover, fifty yards off stood a hut, built of clods and covered with thin turves, but now entirely disused. The simple outline of the lonely shed was visible, and thither he determined to direct his steps. As soon as he arrived he laid her down carefully by the entrance, and then ran and cut with his pocketknife an armful of the dryest fern. Spreading this within the shed, which was entirely open on one side, he placed his mother thereon; then he ran with all his might towards the dwelling of Fairway.\n\nNearly a quarter of an hour had passed, disturbed only by the broken breathing of the sufferer, when moving figures began to animate the line between heath and sky. In a few moments Clym arrived with Fairway, Humphrey, and Susan Nunsuch; Olly Dowden, who had chanced to be at Fairway’s, Christian and Grandfer Cantle following helter-skelter behind. They had brought a lantern and matches, water, a pillow, and a few other articles which had occurred to their minds in the hurry of the moment. Sam had been despatched back again for brandy, and a boy brought Fairway’s pony, upon which he rode off to the nearest medical man, with directions to call at Wildeve’s on his way, and inform Thomasin that her aunt was unwell.\n\nSam and the brandy soon arrived, and it was administered by the light of the lantern; after which she became sufficiently conscious to signify by signs that something was wrong with her foot. Olly Dowden at length understood her meaning, and examined the foot indicated. It was swollen and red. Even as they watched the red began to assume a more livid colour, in the midst of which appeared a scarlet speck, smaller than a pea, and it was found to consist of a drop of blood, which rose above the smooth flesh of her ankle in a hemisphere.\n\n“I know what it is,” cried Sam. “She has been stung by an adder!”\n\n“Yes,” said Clym instantly. “I remember when I was a child seeing just such a bite. O, my poor mother!”\n\n“It was my father who was bit,” said Sam. “And there’s only one way to cure it. You must rub the place with the fat of other adders, and the only way to get that is by frying them. That’s what they did for him.”\n\n“’Tis an old remedy,” said Clym distrustfully, “and I have doubts about it. But we can do nothing else till the doctor comes.”\n\n“’Tis a sure cure,” said Olly Dowden, with emphasis. “I’ve used it when I used to go out nursing.”\n\n“Then we must pray for daylight, to catch them,” said Clym gloomily.\n\n“I will see what I can do,” said Sam.\n\nHe took a green hazel which he had used as a walking stick, split it at the end, inserted a small pebble, and with the lantern in his hand went out into the heath. Clym had by this time lit a small fire, and despatched Susan Nunsuch for a frying pan. Before she had returned Sam came in with three adders, one briskly coiling and uncoiling in the cleft of the stick, and the other two hanging dead across it.\n\n“I have only been able to get one alive and fresh as he ought to be,” said Sam. “These limp ones are two I killed today at work; but as they don’t die till the sun goes down they can’t be very stale meat.”\n\nThe live adder regarded the assembled group with a sinister look in its small black eye, and the beautiful brown and jet pattern on its back seemed to intensify with indignation. Mrs. Yeobright saw the creature, and the creature saw her—she quivered throughout, and averted her eyes.\n\n“Look at that,” murmured Christian Cantle. “Neighbours, how do we know but that something of the old serpent in God’s garden, that gied the apple to the young woman with no clothes, lives on in adders and snakes still? Look at his eye—for all the world like a villainous sort of black currant. ’Tis to be hoped he can’t ill-wish us! There’s folks in heath who’ve been overlooked already. I will never kill another adder as long as I live.”\n\n“Well, ’tis right to be afeard of things, if folks can’t help it,” said Grandfer Cantle. “’Twould have saved me many a brave danger in my time.”\n\n“I fancy I heard something outside the shed,” said Christian. “I wish troubles would come in the daytime, for then a man could show his courage, and hardly beg for mercy of the most broomstick old woman he should see, if he was a brave man, and able to run out of her sight!”\n\n“Even such an ignorant fellow as I should know better than do that,” said Sam.\n\n“Well, there’s calamities where we least expect it, whether or no. Neighbours, if Mrs. Yeobright were to die, d’ye think we should be took up and tried for the manslaughter of a woman?”\n\n“No, they couldn’t bring it in as that,” said Sam, “unless they could prove we had been poachers at some time of our lives. But she’ll fetch round.”\n\n“Now, if I had been stung by ten adders I should hardly have lost a day’s work for’t,” said Grandfer Cantle. “Such is my spirit when I am on my mettle. But perhaps ’tis natural in a man trained for war. Yes, I’ve gone through a good deal; but nothing ever came amiss to me after I joined the Locals in four.” He shook his head and smiled at a mental picture of himself in uniform. “I was always first in the most galliantest scrapes in my younger days!”\n\n“I suppose that was because they always used to put the biggest fool afore,” said Fairway from the fire, beside which he knelt, blowing it with his breath.\n\n“D’ye think so, Timothy?” said Grandfer Cantle, coming forward to Fairway’s side with sudden depression in his face. “Then a man may feel for years that he is good solid company, and be wrong about himself after all?”\n\n“Never mind that question, Grandfer. Stir your stumps and get some more sticks. ’Tis very nonsense of an old man to prattle so when life and death’s in mangling.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Grandfer Cantle, with melancholy conviction. “Well, this is a bad night altogether for them that have done well in their time; and if I were ever such a dab at the hautboy or tenor viol, I shouldn’t have the heart to play tunes upon ’em now.”\n\nSusan now arrived with the frying pan, when the live adder was killed and the heads of the three taken off. The remainders, being cut into lengths and split open, were tossed into the pan, which began hissing and crackling over the fire. Soon a rill of clear oil trickled from the carcases, whereupon Clym dipped the corner of his handkerchief into the liquid and anointed the wound.\n\nVIII. Eustacia Hears of Good Fortune, and Beholds Evil\n\nIn the meantime Eustacia, left alone in her cottage at Alderworth, had become considerably depressed by the posture of affairs. The consequences which might result from Clym’s discovery that his mother had been turned from his door that day were likely to be disagreeable, and this was a quality in events which she hated as much as the dreadful.\n\nTo be left to pass the evening by herself was irksome to her at any time, and this evening it was more irksome than usual by reason of the excitements of the past hours. The two visits had stirred her into restlessness. She was not wrought to any great pitch of uneasiness by the probability of appearing in an ill light in the discussion between Clym and his mother, but she was wrought to vexation, and her slumbering activities were quickened to the extent of wishing that she had opened the door. She had certainly believed that Clym was awake, and the excuse would be an honest one as far as it went; but nothing could save her from censure in refusing to answer at the first knock. Yet, instead of blaming herself for the issue she laid the fault upon the shoulders of some indistinct, colossal Prince of the World, who had framed her situation and ruled her lot.\n\nAt this time of the year it was pleasanter to walk by night than by day, and when Clym had been absent about an hour she suddenly resolved to go out in the direction of Blooms-End, on the chance of meeting him on his return. When she reached the garden gate she heard wheels approaching, and looking round beheld her grandfather coming up in his car.\n\n“I can’t stay a minute, thank ye,” he answered to her greeting. “I am driving to East Egdon; but I came round here just to tell you the news. Perhaps you have heard—about Mr. Wildeve’s fortune?”\n\n“No,” said Eustacia blankly.\n\n“Well, he has come into a fortune of eleven thousand pounds—uncle died in Canada, just after hearing that all his family, whom he was sending home, had gone to the bottom in the Cassiopeia; so Wildeve has come into everything, without in the least expecting it.”\n\nEustacia stood motionless awhile. “How long has he known of this?” she asked.\n\n“Well, it was known to him this morning early, for I knew it at ten o’clock, when Charley came back. Now, he is what I call a lucky man. What a fool you were, Eustacia!”\n\n“In what way?” she said, lifting her eyes in apparent calmness.\n\n“Why, in not sticking to him when you had him.”\n\n“Had him, indeed!”\n\n“I did not know there had ever been anything between you till lately; and, faith, I should have been hot and strong against it if I had known; but since it seems that there was some sniffing between ye, why the deuce didn’t you stick to him?”\n\nEustacia made no reply, but she looked as if she could say as much upon that subject as he if she chose.\n\n“And how is your poor purblind husband?” continued the old man. “Not a bad fellow either, as far as he goes.”\n\n“He is quite well.”\n\n“It is a good thing for his cousin what-d’ye-call-her? By George, you ought to have been in that galley, my girl! Now I must drive on. Do you want any assistance? What’s mine is yours, you know.”\n\n“Thank you, Grandfather, we are not in want at present,” she said coldly. “Clym cuts furze, but he does it mostly as a useful pastime, because he can do nothing else.”\n\n“He is paid for his pastime, isn’t he? Three shillings a hundred, I heard.”\n\n“Clym has money,” she said, colouring, “but he likes to earn a little.”\n\n“Very well; good night.” And the captain drove on.\n\nWhen her grandfather was gone Eustacia went on her way mechanically; but her thoughts were no longer concerning her mother-in-law and Clym. Wildeve, notwithstanding his complaints against his fate, had been seized upon by destiny and placed in the sunshine once more. Eleven thousand pounds! From every Egdon point of view he was a rich man. In Eustacia’s eyes, too, it was an ample sum—one sufficient to supply those wants of hers which had been stigmatized by Clym in his more austere moods as vain and luxurious. Though she was no lover of money she loved what money could bring; and the new accessories she imagined around him clothed Wildeve with a great deal of interest. She recollected now how quietly well-dressed he had been that morning—he had probably put on his newest suit, regardless of damage by briars and thorns. And then she thought of his manner towards herself.\n\n“O I see it, I see it,” she said. “How much he wishes he had me now, that he might give me all I desire!”\n\nIn recalling the details of his glances and words—at the time scarcely regarded—it became plain to her how greatly they had been dictated by his knowledge of this new event. “Had he been a man to bear a jilt ill-will he would have told me of his good fortune in crowing tones; instead of doing that he mentioned not a word, in deference to my misfortunes, and merely implied that he loved me still, as one superior to him.”\n\nWildeve’s silence that day on what had happened to him was just the kind of behaviour calculated to make an impression on such a woman. Those delicate touches of good taste were, in fact, one of the strong points in his demeanour towards the other sex. The peculiarity of Wildeve was that, while at one time passionate, upbraiding, and resentful towards a woman, at another he would treat her with such unparalleled grace as to make previous neglect appear as no discourtesy, injury as no insult, interference as a delicate attention, and the ruin of her honour as excess of chivalry. This man, whose admiration today Eustacia had disregarded, whose good wishes she had scarcely taken the trouble to accept, whom she had shown out of the house by the back door, was the possessor of eleven thousand pounds—a man of fair professional education, and one who had served his articles with a civil engineer.\n\nSo intent was Eustacia upon Wildeve’s fortunes that she forgot how much closer to her own course were those of Clym; and instead of walking on to meet him at once she sat down upon a stone. She was disturbed in her reverie by a voice behind, and turning her head beheld the old lover and fortunate inheritor of wealth immediately beside her.\n\nShe remained sitting, though the fluctuation in her look might have told any man who knew her so well as Wildeve that she was thinking of him.\n\n“How did you come here?” she said in her clear low tone. “I thought you were at home.”\n\n“I went on to the village after leaving your garden; and now I have come back again—that’s all. Which way are you walking, may I ask?”\n\nShe waved her hand in the direction of Blooms-End. “I am going to meet my husband. I think I may possibly have got into trouble whilst you were with me today.”\n\n“How could that be?”\n\n“By not letting in Mrs. Yeobright.”\n\n“I hope that visit of mine did you no harm.”\n\n“None. It was not your fault,” she said quietly.\n\nBy this time she had risen; and they involuntarily sauntered on together, without speaking, for two or three minutes; when Eustacia broke silence by saying, “I assume I must congratulate you.”\n\n“On what? O yes; on my eleven thousand pounds, you mean. Well, since I didn’t get something else, I must be content with getting that.”\n\n“You seem very indifferent about it. Why didn’t you tell me today when you came?” she said in the tone of a neglected person. “I heard of it quite by accident.”\n\n“I did mean to tell you,” said Wildeve. “But I—well, I will speak frankly—I did not like to mention it when I saw, Eustacia, that your star was not high. The sight of a man lying wearied out with hard work, as your husband lay, made me feel that to brag of my own fortune to you would be greatly out of place. Yet, as you stood there beside him, I could not help feeling too that in many respects he was a richer man than I.”\n\nAt this Eustacia said, with slumbering mischievousness, “What, would you exchange with him—your fortune for me?”\n\n“I certainly would,” said Wildeve.\n\n“As we are imagining what is impossible and absurd, suppose we change the subject?”\n\n“Very well; and I will tell you of my plans for the future, if you care to hear them. I shall permanently invest nine thousand pounds, keep one thousand as ready money, and with the remaining thousand travel for a year or so.”\n\n“Travel? What a bright idea! Where will you go to?”\n\n“From here to Paris, where I shall pass the winter and spring. Then I shall go to Italy, Greece, Egypt, and Palestine, before the hot weather comes on. In the summer I shall go to America; and then, by a plan not yet settled, I shall go to Australia and round to India. By that time I shall have begun to have had enough of it. Then I shall probably come back to Paris again, and there I shall stay as long as I can afford to.”\n\n“Back to Paris again,” she murmured in a voice that was nearly a sigh. She had never once told Wildeve of the Parisian desires which Clym’s description had sown in her; yet here was he involuntarily in a position to gratify them. “You think a good deal of Paris?” she added.\n\n“Yes. In my opinion it is the central beauty-spot of the world.”\n\n“And in mine! And Thomasin will go with you?”\n\n“Yes, if she cares to. She may prefer to stay at home.”\n\n“So you will be going about, and I shall be staying here!”\n\n“I suppose you will. But we know whose fault that is.”\n\n“I am not blaming you,” she said quickly.\n\n“Oh, I thought you were. If ever you _should_ be inclined to blame me, think of a certain evening by Rainbarrow, when you promised to meet me and did not. You sent me a letter; and my heart ached to read that as I hope yours never will. That was one point of divergence. I then did something in haste.... But she is a good woman, and I will say no more.”\n\n“I know that the blame was on my side that time,” said Eustacia. “But it had not always been so. However, it is my misfortune to be too sudden in feeling. O, Damon, don’t reproach me any more—I can’t bear that.”\n\nThey went on silently for a distance of two or three miles, when Eustacia said suddenly, “Haven’t you come out of your way, Mr. Wildeve?”\n\n“My way is anywhere tonight. I will go with you as far as the hill on which we can see Blooms-End, as it is getting late for you to be alone.”\n\n“Don’t trouble. I am not obliged to be out at all. I think I would rather you did not accompany me further. This sort of thing would have an odd look if known.”\n\n“Very well, I will leave you.” He took her hand unexpectedly, and kissed it—for the first time since her marriage. “What light is that on the hill?” he added, as it were to hide the caress.\n\nShe looked, and saw a flickering firelight proceeding from the open side of a hovel a little way before them. The hovel, which she had hitherto always found empty, seemed to be inhabited now.\n\n“Since you have come so far,” said Eustacia, “will you see me safely past that hut? I thought I should have met Clym somewhere about here, but as he doesn’t appear I will hasten on and get to Blooms-End before he leaves.”\n\nThey advanced to the turf-shed, and when they got near it the firelight and the lantern inside showed distinctly enough the form of a woman reclining on a bed of fern, a group of heath men and women standing around her. Eustacia did not recognize Mrs. Yeobright in the reclining figure, nor Clym as one of the standers-by till she came close. Then she quickly pressed her hand up on Wildeve’s arm and signified to him to come back from the open side of the shed into the shadow.\n\n“It is my husband and his mother,” she whispered in an agitated voice. “What can it mean? Will you step forward and tell me?”\n\nWildeve left her side and went to the back wall of the hut. Presently Eustacia perceived that he was beckoning to her, and she advanced and joined him.\n\n“It is a serious case,” said Wildeve.\n\nFrom their position they could hear what was proceeding inside.\n\n“I cannot think where she could have been going,” said Clym to someone. “She had evidently walked a long way, but even when she was able to speak just now she would not tell me where. What do you really think of her?”\n\n“There is a great deal to fear,” was gravely answered, in a voice which Eustacia recognized as that of the only surgeon in the district. “She has suffered somewhat from the bite of the adder; but it is exhaustion which has overpowered her. My impression is that her walk must have been exceptionally long.”\n\n“I used to tell her not to overwalk herself this weather,” said Clym, with distress. “Do you think we did well in using the adder’s fat?”\n\n“Well, it is a very ancient remedy—the old remedy of the viper-catchers, I believe,” replied the doctor. “It is mentioned as an infallible ointment by Hoffman, Mead, and I think the Abbé Fontana. Undoubtedly it was as good a thing as you could do; though I question if some other oils would not have been equally efficacious.”\n\n“Come here, come here!” was then rapidly said in anxious female tones, and Clym and the doctor could be heard rushing forward from the back part of the shed to where Mrs. Yeobright lay.\n\n“Oh, what is it?” whispered Eustacia.\n\n“’Twas Thomasin who spoke,” said Wildeve. “Then they have fetched her. I wonder if I had better go in—yet it might do harm.”\n\nFor a long time there was utter silence among the group within; and it was broken at last by Clym saying, in an agonized voice, “O Doctor, what does it mean?”\n\nThe doctor did not reply at once; ultimately he said, “She is sinking fast. Her heart was previously affected, and physical exhaustion has dealt the finishing blow.”\n\nThen there was a weeping of women, then waiting, then hushed exclamations, then a strange gasping sound, then a painful stillness.\n\n“It is all over,” said the doctor.\n\nFurther back in the hut the cotters whispered, “Mrs. Yeobright is dead.”\n\nAlmost at the same moment the two watchers observed the form of a small old-fashioned child entering at the open side of the shed. Susan Nunsuch, whose boy it was, went forward to the opening and silently beckoned to him to go back.\n\n“I’ve got something to tell ’ee, Mother,” he cried in a shrill tone. “That woman asleep there walked along with me today; and she said I was to say that I had seed her, and she was a broken-hearted woman and cast off by her son, and then I came on home.”\n\nA confused sob as from a man was heard within, upon which Eustacia gasped faintly, “That’s Clym—I must go to him—yet dare I do it? No—come away!”\n\nWhen they had withdrawn from the neighbourhood of the shed she said huskily, “I am to blame for this. There is evil in store for me.”\n\n“Was she not admitted to your house after all?” Wildeve inquired.\n\n“No, and that’s where it all lies! Oh, what shall I do! I shall not intrude upon them—I shall go straight home. Damon, good-bye! I cannot speak to you any more now.”\n\nThey parted company; and when Eustacia had reached the next hill she looked back. A melancholy procession was wending its way by the light of the lantern from the hut towards Blooms-End. Wildeve was nowhere to be seen.\n\nI. “Wherefore Is Light Given to Him That Is in Misery”\nOne evening, about three weeks after the funeral of Mrs. Yeobright, when the silver face of the moon sent a bundle of beams directly upon the floor of Clym’s house at Alderworth, a woman came forth from within. She reclined over the garden gate as if to refresh herself awhile. The pale lunar touches which make beauties of hags lent divinity to this face, already beautiful.\n\nShe had not long been there when a man came up the road and with some hesitation said to her, “How is he tonight, ma’am, if you please?”\n\n“He is better, though still very unwell, Humphrey,” replied Eustacia.\n\n“Is he light-headed, ma’am?”\n\n“No. He is quite sensible now.”\n\n“Do he rave about his mother just the same, poor fellow?” continued Humphrey.\n\n“Just as much, though not quite so wildly,” she said in a low voice.\n\n“It was very unfortunate, ma’am, that the boy Johnny should ever ha’ told him his mother’s dying words, about her being broken-hearted and cast off by her son. ’Twas enough to upset any man alive.”\n\nEustacia made no reply beyond that of a slight catch in her breath, as of one who fain would speak but could not; and Humphrey, declining her invitation to come in, went away.\n\nEustacia turned, entered the house, and ascended to the front bedroom, where a shaded light was burning. In the bed lay Clym, pale, haggard, wide awake, tossing to one side and to the other, his eyes lit by a hot light, as if the fire in their pupils were burning up their substance.\n\n“Is it you, Eustacia?” he said as she sat down.\n\n“Yes, Clym. I have been down to the gate. The moon is shining beautifully, and there is not a leaf stirring.”\n\n“Shining, is it? What’s the moon to a man like me? Let it shine—let anything be, so that I never see another day!... Eustacia, I don’t know where to look—my thoughts go through me like swords. O, if any man wants to make himself immortal by painting a picture of wretchedness, let him come here!”\n\n“Why do you say so?”\n\n“I cannot help feeling that I did my best to kill her.”\n\n“No, Clym.”\n\n“Yes, it was so; it is useless to excuse me! My conduct to her was too hideous—I made no advances; and she could not bring herself to forgive me. Now she is dead! If I had only shown myself willing to make it up with her sooner, and we had been friends, and then she had died, it wouldn’t be so hard to bear. But I never went near her house, so she never came near mine, and didn’t know how welcome she would have been—that’s what troubles me. She did not know I was going to her house that very night, for she was too insensible to understand me. If she had only come to see me! I longed that she would. But it was not to be.”\n\nThere escaped from Eustacia one of those shivering sighs which used to shake her like a pestilent blast. She had not yet told.\n\nBut Yeobright was too deeply absorbed in the ramblings incidental to his remorseful state to notice her. During his illness he had been continually talking thus. Despair had been added to his original grief by the unfortunate disclosure of the boy who had received the last words of Mrs. Yeobright—words too bitterly uttered in an hour of misapprehension. Then his distress had overwhelmed him, and he longed for death as a field labourer longs for the shade. It was the pitiful sight of a man standing in the very focus of sorrow. He continually bewailed his tardy journey to his mother’s house, because it was an error which could never be rectified, and insisted that he must have been horribly perverted by some fiend not to have thought before that it was his duty to go to her, since she did not come to him. He would ask Eustacia to agree with him in his self-condemnation; and when she, seared inwardly by a secret she dared not tell, declared that she could not give an opinion, he would say, “That’s because you didn’t know my mother’s nature. She was always ready to forgive if asked to do so; but I seemed to her to be as an obstinate child, and that made her unyielding. Yet not unyielding—she was proud and reserved, no more.... Yes, I can understand why she held out against me so long. She was waiting for me. I dare say she said a hundred times in her sorrow, ‘What a return he makes for all the sacrifices I have made for him!’ I never went to her! When I set out to visit her it was too late. To think of that is nearly intolerable!”\n\nSometimes his condition had been one of utter remorse, unsoftened by a single tear of pure sorrow: and then he writhed as he lay, fevered far more by thought than by physical ills. “If I could only get one assurance that she did not die in a belief that I was resentful,” he said one day when in this mood, “it would be better to think of than a hope of heaven. But that I cannot do.”\n\n“You give yourself up too much to this wearying despair,” said Eustacia. “Other men’s mothers have died.”\n\n“That doesn’t make the loss of mine less. Yet it is less the loss than the circumstances of the loss. I sinned against her, and on that account there is no light for me.”\n\n“She sinned against you, I think.”\n\n“No, she did not. I committed the guilt; and may the whole burden be upon my head!”\n\n“I think you might consider twice before you say that,” Eustacia replied. “Single men have, no doubt, a right to curse themselves as much as they please; but men with wives involve two in the doom they pray down.”\n\n“I am in too sorry a state to understand what you are refining on,” said the wretched man. “Day and night shout at me, ‘You have helped to kill her.’ But in loathing myself I may, I own, be unjust to you, my poor wife. Forgive me for it, Eustacia, for I scarcely know what I do.”\n\nEustacia was always anxious to avoid the sight of her husband in such a state as this, which had become as dreadful to her as the trial scene was to Judas Iscariot. It brought before her eyes the spectre of a worn-out woman knocking at a door which she would not open; and she shrank from contemplating it. Yet it was better for Yeobright himself when he spoke openly of his sharp regret, for in silence he endured infinitely more, and would sometimes remain so long in a tense, brooding mood, consuming himself by the gnawing of his thought, that it was imperatively necessary to make him talk aloud, that his grief might in some degree expend itself in the effort.\n\nEustacia had not been long indoors after her look at the moonlight when a soft footstep came up to the house, and Thomasin was announced by the woman downstairs.\n\n“Ah, Thomasin! Thank you for coming tonight,” said Clym when she entered the room. “Here am I, you see. Such a wretched spectacle am I, that I shrink from being seen by a single friend, and almost from you.”\n\n“You must not shrink from me, dear Clym,” said Thomasin earnestly, in that sweet voice of hers which came to a sufferer like fresh air into a Black Hole. “Nothing in you can ever shock me or drive me away. I have been here before, but you don’t remember it.”\n\n“Yes, I do; I am not delirious, Thomasin, nor have I been so at all. Don’t you believe that if they say so. I am only in great misery at what I have done, and that, with the weakness, makes me seem mad. But it has not upset my reason. Do you think I should remember all about my mother’s death if I were out of my mind? No such good luck. Two months and a half, Thomasin, the last of her life, did my poor mother live alone, distracted and mourning because of me; yet she was unvisited by me, though I was living only six miles off. Two months and a half—seventy-five days did the sun rise and set upon her in that deserted state which a dog didn’t deserve! Poor people who had nothing in common with her would have cared for her, and visited her had they known her sickness and loneliness; but I, who should have been all to her, stayed away like a cur. If there is any justice in God let Him kill me now. He has nearly blinded me, but that is not enough. If He would only strike me with more pain I would believe in Him forever!”\n\n“Hush, hush! O, pray, Clym, don’t, don’t say it!” implored Thomasin, affrighted into sobs and tears; while Eustacia, at the other side of the room, though her pale face remained calm, writhed in her chair. Clym went on without heeding his cousin.\n\n“But I am not worth receiving further proof even of Heaven’s reprobation. Do you think, Thomasin, that she knew me—that she did not die in that horrid mistaken notion about my not forgiving her, which I can’t tell you how she acquired? If you could only assure me of that! Do you think so, Eustacia? Do speak to me.”\n\n“I think I can assure you that she knew better at last,” said Thomasin. The pallid Eustacia said nothing.\n\n“Why didn’t she come to my house? I would have taken her in and showed her how I loved her in spite of all. But she never came; and I didn’t go to her, and she died on the heath like an animal kicked out, nobody to help her till it was too late. If you could have seen her, Thomasin, as I saw her—a poor dying woman, lying in the dark upon the bare ground, moaning, nobody near, believing she was utterly deserted by all the world, it would have moved you to anguish, it would have moved a brute. And this poor woman my mother! No wonder she said to the child, ‘You have seen a broken-hearted woman.’ What a state she must have been brought to, to say that! and who can have done it but I? It is too dreadful to think of, and I wish I could be punished more heavily than I am. How long was I what they called out of my senses?”\n\n“A week, I think.”\n\n“And then I became calm.”\n\n“Yes, for four days.”\n\n“And now I have left off being calm.”\n\n“But try to be quiet—please do, and you will soon be strong. If you could remove that impression from your mind—”\n\n“Yes, yes,” he said impatiently. “But I don’t want to get strong. What’s the use of my getting well? It would be better for me if I die, and it would certainly be better for Eustacia. Is Eustacia there?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“It would be better for you, Eustacia, if I were to die?”\n\n“Don’t press such a question, dear Clym.”\n\n“Well, it really is but a shadowy supposition; for unfortunately I am going to live. I feel myself getting better. Thomasin, how long are you going to stay at the inn, now that all this money has come to your husband?”\n\n“Another month or two, probably; until my illness is over. We cannot get off till then. I think it will be a month or more.”\n\n“Yes, yes. Of course. Ah, Cousin Tamsie, you will get over your trouble—one little month will take you through it, and bring something to console you; but I shall never get over mine, and no consolation will come!”\n\n“Clym, you are unjust to yourself. Depend upon it, Aunt thought kindly of you. I know that, if she had lived, you would have been reconciled with her.”\n\n“But she didn’t come to see me, though I asked her, before I married, if she would come. Had she come, or had I gone there, she would never have died saying, ‘I am a broken-hearted woman, cast off by my son.’ My door has always been open to her—a welcome here has always awaited her. But that she never came to see.”\n\n“You had better not talk any more now, Clym,” said Eustacia faintly from the other part of the room, for the scene was growing intolerable to her.\n\n“Let me talk to you instead for the little time I shall be here,” Thomasin said soothingly. “Consider what a one-sided way you have of looking at the matter, Clym. When she said that to the little boy you had not found her and taken her into your arms; and it might have been uttered in a moment of bitterness. It was rather like Aunt to say things in haste. She sometimes used to speak so to me. Though she did not come I am convinced that she thought of coming to see you. Do you suppose a man’s mother could live two or three months without one forgiving thought? She forgave me; and why should she not have forgiven you?”\n\n“You laboured to win her round; I did nothing. I, who was going to teach people the higher secrets of happiness, did not know how to keep out of that gross misery which the most untaught are wise enough to avoid.”\n\n“How did you get here tonight, Thomasin?” said Eustacia.\n\n“Damon set me down at the end of the lane. He has driven into East Egdon on business, and he will come and pick me up by-and-by.”\n\nAccordingly they soon after heard the noise of wheels. Wildeve had come, and was waiting outside with his horse and gig.\n\n“Send out and tell him I will be down in two minutes,” said Thomasin.\n\n“I will run down myself,” said Eustacia.\n\nShe went down. Wildeve had alighted, and was standing before the horse’s head when Eustacia opened the door. He did not turn for a moment, thinking the comer Thomasin. Then he looked, startled ever so little, and said one word: “Well?”\n\n“I have not yet told him,” she replied in a whisper.\n\n“Then don’t do so till he is well—it will be fatal. You are ill yourself.”\n\n“I am wretched.... O Damon,” she said, bursting into tears, “I—I can’t tell you how unhappy I am! I can hardly bear this. I can tell nobody of my trouble—nobody knows of it but you.”\n\n“Poor girl!” said Wildeve, visibly affected at her distress, and at last led on so far as to take her hand. “It is hard, when you have done nothing to deserve it, that you should have got involved in such a web as this. You were not made for these sad scenes. I am to blame most. If I could only have saved you from it all!”\n\n“But, Damon, please pray tell me what I must do? To sit by him hour after hour, and hear him reproach himself as being the cause of her death, and to know that I am the sinner, if any human being is at all, drives me into cold despair. I don’t know what to do. Should I tell him or should I not tell him? I always am asking myself that. O, I want to tell him; and yet I am afraid. If he finds it out he must surely kill me, for nothing else will be in proportion to his feelings now. ‘Beware the fury of a patient man’ sounds day by day in my ears as I watch him.”\n\n“Well, wait till he is better, and trust to chance. And when you tell, you must only tell part—for his own sake.”\n\n“Which part should I keep back?”\n\nWildeve paused. “That I was in the house at the time,” he said in a low tone.\n\n“Yes; it must be concealed, seeing what has been whispered. How much easier are hasty actions than speeches that will excuse them!”\n\n“If he were only to die—” Wildeve murmured.\n\n“Do not think of it! I would not buy hope of immunity by so cowardly a desire even if I hated him. Now I am going up to him again. Thomasin bade me tell you she would be down in a few minutes. Good-bye.”\n\nShe returned, and Thomasin soon appeared. When she was seated in the gig with her husband, and the horse was turning to go off, Wildeve lifted his eyes to the bedroom windows. Looking from one of them he could discern a pale, tragic face watching him drive away. It was Eustacia’s.\n\nII. A Lurid Light Breaks in upon a Darkened Understanding\nClym’s grief became mitigated by wearing itself out. His strength returned, and a month after the visit of Thomasin he might have been seen walking about the garden. Endurance and despair, equanimity and gloom, the tints of health and the pallor of death, mingled weirdly in his face. He was now unnaturally silent upon all of the past that related to his mother; and though Eustacia knew that he was thinking of it none the less, she was only too glad to escape the topic ever to bring it up anew. When his mind had been weaker his heart had led him to speak out; but reason having now somewhat recovered itself he sank into taciturnity.\n\nOne evening when he was thus standing in the garden, abstractedly spudding up a weed with his stick, a bony figure turned the corner of the house and came up to him.\n\n“Christian, isn’t it?” said Clym. “I am glad you have found me out. I shall soon want you to go to Blooms-End and assist me in putting the house in order. I suppose it is all locked up as I left it?”\n\n“Yes, Mister Clym.”\n\n“Have you dug up the potatoes and other roots?”\n\n“Yes, without a drop o’ rain, thank God. But I was coming to tell ’ee of something else which is quite different from what we have lately had in the family. I am sent by the rich gentleman at the Woman, that we used to call the landlord, to tell ’ee that Mrs. Wildeve is doing well of a girl, which was born punctually at one o’clock at noon, or a few minutes more or less; and ’tis said that expecting of this increase is what have kept ’em there since they came into their money.”\n\n“And she is getting on well, you say?”\n\n“Yes, sir. Only Mr. Wildeve is twanky because ’tisn’t a boy—that’s what they say in the kitchen, but I was not supposed to notice that.”\n\n“Christian, now listen to me.”\n\n“Yes, sure, Mr. Yeobright.”\n\n“Did you see my mother the day before she died?”\n\n“No, I did not.”\n\nYeobright’s face expressed disappointment.\n\n“But I zeed her the morning of the same day she died.”\n\nClym’s look lighted up. “That’s nearer still to my meaning,” he said.\n\n“Yes, I know ’twas the same day; for she said, ‘I be going to see him, Christian; so I shall not want any vegetables brought in for dinner.’”\n\n“See whom?”\n\n“See you. She was going to your house, you understand.”\n\nYeobright regarded Christian with intense surprise. “Why did you never mention this?” he said. “Are you sure it was my house she was coming to?”\n\n“O yes. I didn’t mention it because I’ve never zeed you lately. And as she didn’t get there it was all nought, and nothing to tell.”\n\n“And I have been wondering why she should have walked in the heath on that hot day! Well, did she say what she was coming for? It is a thing, Christian, I am very anxious to know.”\n\n“Yes, Mister Clym. She didn’t say it to me, though I think she did to one here and there.”\n\n“Do you know one person to whom she spoke of it?”\n\n“There is one man, please, sir, but I hope you won’t mention my name to him, as I have seen him in strange places, particular in dreams. One night last summer he glared at me like Famine and Sword, and it made me feel so low that I didn’t comb out my few hairs for two days. He was standing, as it might be, Mister Yeobright, in the middle of the path to Mistover, and your mother came up, looking as pale—”\n\n“Yes, when was that?”\n\n“Last summer, in my dream.”\n\n“Pooh! Who’s the man?”\n\n“Diggory, the reddleman. He called upon her and sat with her the evening before she set out to see you. I hadn’t gone home from work when he came up to the gate.”\n\n“I must see Venn—I wish I had known it before,” said Clym anxiously. “I wonder why he has not come to tell me?”\n\n“He went out of Egdon Heath the next day, so would not be likely to know you wanted him.”\n\n“Christian,” said Clym, “you must go and find Venn. I am otherwise engaged, or I would go myself. Find him at once, and tell him I want to speak to him.”\n\n“I am a good hand at hunting up folk by day,” said Christian, looking dubiously round at the declining light; “but as to night-time, never is such a bad hand as I, Mister Yeobright.”\n\n“Search the heath when you will, so that you bring him soon. Bring him tomorrow, if you can.”\n\nChristian then departed. The morrow came, but no Venn. In the evening Christian arrived, looking very weary. He had been searching all day, and had heard nothing of the reddleman.\n\n“Inquire as much as you can tomorrow without neglecting your work,” said Yeobright. “Don’t come again till you have found him.”\n\nThe next day Yeobright set out for the old house at Blooms-End, which, with the garden, was now his own. His severe illness had hindered all preparations for his removal thither; but it had become necessary that he should go and overlook its contents, as administrator to his mother’s little property; for which purpose he decided to pass the next night on the premises.\n\nHe journeyed onward, not quickly or decisively, but in the slow walk of one who has been awakened from a stupefying sleep. It was early afternoon when he reached the valley. The expression of the place, the tone of the hour, were precisely those of many such occasions in days gone by; and these antecedent similarities fostered the illusion that she, who was there no longer, would come out to welcome him. The garden gate was locked and the shutters were closed, just as he himself had left them on the evening after the funeral. He unlocked the gate, and found that a spider had already constructed a large web, tying the door to the lintel, on the supposition that it was never to be opened again. When he had entered the house and flung back the shutters he set about his task of overhauling the cupboards and closets, burning papers, and considering how best to arrange the place for Eustacia’s reception, until such time as he might be in a position to carry out his long-delayed scheme, should that time ever arrive.\n\nAs he surveyed the rooms he felt strongly disinclined for the alterations which would have to be made in the time-honoured furnishing of his parents and grandparents, to suit Eustacia’s modern ideas. The gaunt oak-cased clock, with the picture of the Ascension on the door panel and the Miraculous Draught of Fishes on the base; his grandmother’s corner cupboard with the glass door, through which the spotted china was visible; the dumb-waiter; the wooden tea trays; the hanging fountain with the brass tap—whither would these venerable articles have to be banished?\n\nHe noticed that the flowers in the window had died for want of water, and he placed them out upon the ledge, that they might be taken away. While thus engaged he heard footsteps on the gravel without, and somebody knocked at the door.\n\nYeobright opened it, and Venn was standing before him.\n\n“Good morning,” said the reddleman. “Is Mrs. Yeobright at home?”\n\nYeobright looked upon the ground. “Then you have not seen Christian or any of the Egdon folks?” he said.\n\n“No. I have only just returned after a long stay away. I called here the day before I left.”\n\n“And you have heard nothing?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“My mother is—dead.”\n\n“Dead!” said Venn mechanically.\n\n“Her home now is where I shouldn’t mind having mine.”\n\nVenn regarded him, and then said, “If I didn’t see your face I could never believe your words. Have you been ill?”\n\n“I had an illness.”\n\n“Well, the change! When I parted from her a month ago everything seemed to say that she was going to begin a new life.”\n\n“And what seemed came true.”\n\n“You say right, no doubt. Trouble has taught you a deeper vein of talk than mine. All I meant was regarding her life here. She has died too soon.”\n\n“Perhaps through my living too long. I have had a bitter experience on that score this last month, Diggory. But come in; I have been wanting to see you.”\n\nHe conducted the reddleman into the large room where the dancing had taken place the previous Christmas, and they sat down in the settle together. “There’s the cold fireplace, you see,” said Clym. “When that half-burnt log and those cinders were alight she was alive! Little has been changed here yet. I can do nothing. My life creeps like a snail.”\n\n“How came she to die?” said Venn.\n\nYeobright gave him some particulars of her illness and death, and continued: “After this no kind of pain will ever seem more than an indisposition to me. I began saying that I wanted to ask you something, but I stray from subjects like a drunken man. I am anxious to know what my mother said to you when she last saw you. You talked with her a long time, I think?”\n\n“I talked with her more than half an hour.”\n\n“About me?”\n\n“Yes. And it must have been on account of what we said that she was on the heath. Without question she was coming to see you.”\n\n“But why should she come to see me if she felt so bitterly against me? There’s the mystery.”\n\n“Yet I know she quite forgave ’ee.”\n\n“But, Diggory—would a woman, who had quite forgiven her son, say, when she felt herself ill on the way to his house, that she was broken-hearted because of his ill-usage? Never!”\n\n“What I know is that she didn’t blame you at all. She blamed herself for what had happened, and only herself. I had it from her own lips.”\n\n“You had it from her lips that I had _not_ ill-treated her; and at the same time another had it from her lips that I _had_ ill-treated her? My mother was no impulsive woman who changed her opinion every hour without reason. How can it be, Venn, that she should have told such different stories in close succession?”\n\n“I cannot say. It is certainly odd, when she had forgiven you, and had forgiven your wife, and was going to see ye on purpose to make friends.”\n\n“If there was one thing wanting to bewilder me it was this incomprehensible thing!... Diggory, if we, who remain alive, were only allowed to hold conversation with the dead—just once, a bare minute, even through a screen of iron bars, as with persons in prison—what we might learn! How many who now ride smiling would hide their heads! And this mystery—I should then be at the bottom of it at once. But the grave has forever shut her in; and how shall it be found out now?”\n\nNo reply was returned by his companion, since none could be given; and when Venn left, a few minutes later, Clym had passed from the dullness of sorrow to the fluctuation of carking incertitude.\n\nHe continued in the same state all the afternoon. A bed was made up for him in the same house by a neighbour, that he might not have to return again the next day; and when he retired to rest in the deserted place it was only to remain awake hour after hour thinking the same thoughts. How to discover a solution to this riddle of death seemed a query of more importance than highest problems of the living. There was housed in his memory a vivid picture of the face of a little boy as he entered the hovel where Clym’s mother lay. The round eyes, eager gaze, the piping voice which enunciated the words, had operated like stilettos on his brain.\n\nA visit to the boy suggested itself as a means of gleaning new particulars; though it might be quite unproductive. To probe a child’s mind after the lapse of six weeks, not for facts which the child had seen and understood, but to get at those which were in their nature beyond him, did not promise much; yet when every obvious channel is blocked we grope towards the small and obscure. There was nothing else left to do; after that he would allow the enigma to drop into the abyss of undiscoverable things.\n\nIt was about daybreak when he had reached this decision, and he at once arose. He locked up the house and went out into the green patch which merged in heather further on. In front of the white garden-palings the path branched into three like a broad arrow. The road to the right led to the Quiet Woman and its neighbourhood; the middle track led to Mistover Knap; the left-hand track led over the hill to another part of Mistover, where the child lived. On inclining into the latter path Yeobright felt a creeping chilliness, familiar enough to most people, and probably caused by the unsunned morning air. In after days he thought of it as a thing of singular significance.\n\nWhen Yeobright reached the cottage of Susan Nunsuch, the mother of the boy he sought, he found that the inmates were not yet astir. But in upland hamlets the transition from a-bed to abroad is surprisingly swift and easy. There no dense partition of yawns and toilets divides humanity by night from humanity by day. Yeobright tapped at the upper windowsill, which he could reach with his walking stick; and in three or four minutes the woman came down.\n\nIt was not till this moment that Clym recollected her to be the person who had behaved so barbarously to Eustacia. It partly explained the insuavity with which the woman greeted him. Moreover, the boy had been ailing again; and Susan now, as ever since the night when he had been pressed into Eustacia’s service at the bonfire, attributed his indispositions to Eustacia’s influence as a witch. It was one of those sentiments which lurk like moles underneath the visible surface of manners, and may have been kept alive by Eustacia’s entreaty to the captain, at the time that he had intended to prosecute Susan for the pricking in church, to let the matter drop; which he accordingly had done.\n\nYeobright overcame his repugnance, for Susan had at least borne his mother no ill-will. He asked kindly for the boy; but her manner did not improve.\n\n“I wish to see him,” continued Yeobright, with some hesitation, “to ask him if he remembers anything more of his walk with my mother than what he has previously told.”\n\nShe regarded him in a peculiar and criticizing manner. To anybody but a half-blind man it would have said, “You want another of the knocks which have already laid you so low.”\n\nShe called the boy downstairs, asked Clym to sit down on a stool, and continued, “Now, Johnny, tell Mr. Yeobright anything you can call to mind.”\n\n“You have not forgotten how you walked with the poor lady on that hot day?” said Clym.\n\n“No,” said the boy.\n\n“And what she said to you?”\n\nThe boy repeated the exact words he had used on entering the hut. Yeobright rested his elbow on the table and shaded his face with his hand; and the mother looked as if she wondered how a man could want more of what had stung him so deeply.\n\n“She was going to Alderworth when you first met her?”\n\n“No; she was coming away.”\n\n“That can’t be.”\n\n“Yes; she walked along with me. I was coming away, too.”\n\n“Then where did you first see her?”\n\n“At your house.”\n\n“Attend, and speak the truth!” said Clym sternly.\n\n“Yes, sir; at your house was where I seed her first.”\n\nClym started up, and Susan smiled in an expectant way which did not embellish her face; it seemed to mean, “Something sinister is coming!”\n\n“What did she do at my house?”\n\n“She went and sat under the trees at the Devil’s Bellows.”\n\n“Good God! this is all news to me!”\n\n“You never told me this before?” said Susan.\n\n“No, Mother; because I didn’t like to tell ’ee I had been so far. I was picking blackhearts, and went further than I meant.”\n\n“What did she do then?” said Yeobright.\n\n“Looked at a man who came up and went into your house.”\n\n“That was myself—a furze-cutter, with brambles in his hand.”\n\n“No; ’twas not you. ’Twas a gentleman. You had gone in afore.”\n\n“Who was he?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“Now tell me what happened next.”\n\n“The poor lady went and knocked at your door, and the lady with black hair looked out of the side window at her.”\n\nThe boy’s mother turned to Clym and said, “This is something you didn’t expect?”\n\nYeobright took no more notice of her than if he had been of stone. “Go on, go on,” he said hoarsely to the boy.\n\n“And when she saw the young lady look out of the window the old lady knocked again; and when nobody came she took up the furze-hook and looked at it, and put it down again, and then she looked at the faggot-bonds; and then she went away, and walked across to me, and blowed her breath very hard, like this. We walked on together, she and I, and I talked to her and she talked to me a bit, but not much, because she couldn’t blow her breath.”\n\n“O!” murmured Clym, in a low tone, and bowed his head. “Let’s have more,” he said.\n\n“She couldn’t talk much, and she couldn’t walk; and her face was, O so queer!”\n\n“How was her face?”\n\n“Like yours is now.”\n\nThe woman looked at Yeobright, and beheld him colourless, in a cold sweat. “Isn’t there meaning in it?” she said stealthily. “What do you think of her now?”\n\n“Silence!” said Clym fiercely. And, turning to the boy, “And then you left her to die?”\n\n“No,” said the woman, quickly and angrily. “He did not leave her to die! She sent him away. Whoever says he forsook her says what’s not true.”\n\n“Trouble no more about that,” answered Clym, with a quivering mouth. “What he did is a trifle in comparison with what he saw. Door kept shut, did you say? Kept shut, she looking out of window? Good heart of God!—what does it mean?”\n\nThe child shrank away from the gaze of his questioner.\n\n“He said so,” answered the mother, “and Johnny’s a God-fearing boy and tells no lies.”\n\n“‘Cast off by my son!’ No, by my best life, dear mother, it is not so! But by your son’s, your son’s—May all murderesses get the torment they deserve!”\n\nWith these words Yeobright went forth from the little dwelling. The pupils of his eyes, fixed steadfastly on blankness, were vaguely lit with an icy shine; his mouth had passed into the phase more or less imaginatively rendered in studies of Oedipus. The strangest deeds were possible to his mood. But they were not possible to his situation. Instead of there being before him the pale face of Eustacia, and a masculine shape unknown, there was only the imperturbable countenance of the heath, which, having defied the cataclysmal onsets of centuries, reduced to insignificance by its seamed and antique features the wildest turmoil of a single man.\nIII. Eustacia Dresses Herself on a Black Morning\n\nA consciousness of a vast impassivity in all which lay around him took possession even of Yeobright in his wild walk towards Alderworth. He had once before felt in his own person this overpowering of the fervid by the inanimate; but then it had tended to enervate a passion far sweeter than that which at present pervaded him. It was once when he stood parting from Eustacia in the moist still levels beyond the hills.\nBut dismissing all this he went onward home, and came to the front of his house. The blinds of Eustacia’s bedroom were still closely drawn, for she was no early riser. All the life visible was in the shape of a solitary thrush cracking a small snail upon the door-stone for his breakfast, and his tapping seemed a loud noise in the general silence which prevailed; but on going to the door Clym found it unfastened, the young girl who attended upon Eustacia being astir in the back part of the premises. Yeobright entered and went straight to his wife’s room.\n\nThe noise of his arrival must have aroused her, for when he opened the door she was standing before the looking glass in her nightdress, the ends of her hair gathered into one hand, with which she was coiling the whole mass round her head, previous to beginning toilette operations. She was not a woman given to speaking first at a meeting, and she allowed Clym to walk across in silence, without turning her head. He came behind her, and she saw his face in the glass. It was ashy, haggard, and terrible. Instead of starting towards him in sorrowful surprise, as even Eustacia, undemonstrative wife as she was, would have done in days before she burdened herself with a secret, she remained motionless, looking at him in the glass. And while she looked the carmine flush with which warmth and sound sleep had suffused her cheeks and neck dissolved from view, and the deathlike pallor in his face flew across into hers. He was close enough to see this, and the sight instigated his tongue.\n\n“You know what is the matter,” he said huskily. “I see it in your face.”\n\nHer hand relinquished the rope of hair and dropped to her side, and the pile of tresses, no longer supported, fell from the crown of her head about her shoulders and over the white nightgown. She made no reply.\n\n“Speak to me,” said Yeobright peremptorily.\n\nThe blanching process did not cease in her, and her lips now became as white as her face. She turned to him and said, “Yes, Clym, I’ll speak to you. Why do you return so early? Can I do anything for you?”\n\n“Yes, you can listen to me. It seems that my wife is not very well?”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Your face, my dear; your face. Or perhaps it is the pale morning light which takes your colour away? Now I am going to reveal a secret to you. Ha-ha!”\n\n“O, that is ghastly!”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Your laugh.”\n\n“There’s reason for ghastliness. Eustacia, you have held my happiness in the hollow of your hand, and like a devil you have dashed it down!”\n\nShe started back from the dressing-table, retreated a few steps from him, and looked him in the face. “Ah! you think to frighten me,” she said, with a slight laugh. “Is it worth while? I am undefended, and alone.”\n\n“How extraordinary!”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“As there is ample time I will tell you, though you know well enough. I mean that it is extraordinary that you should be alone in my absence. Tell me, now, where is he who was with you on the afternoon of the thirty-first of August? Under the bed? Up the chimney?”\n\nA shudder overcame her and shook the light fabric of her nightdress throughout. “I do not remember dates so exactly,” she said. “I cannot recollect that anybody was with me besides yourself.”\n\n“The day I mean,” said Yeobright, his voice growing louder and harsher, “was the day you shut the door against my mother and killed her. O, it is too much—too bad!” He leant over the footpiece of the bedstead for a few moments, with his back towards her; then rising again—“Tell me, tell me! tell me—do you hear?” he cried, rushing up to her and seizing her by the loose folds of her sleeve.\n\nThe superstratum of timidity which often overlies those who are daring and defiant at heart had been passed through, and the mettlesome substance of the woman was reached. The red blood inundated her face, previously so pale.\n\n“What are you going to do?” she said in a low voice, regarding him with a proud smile. “You will not alarm me by holding on so; but it would be a pity to tear my sleeve.”\n\nInstead of letting go he drew her closer to him. “Tell me the particulars of—my mother’s death,” he said in a hard, panting whisper; “or—I’ll—I’ll—”\n\n“Clym,” she answered slowly, “do you think you dare do anything to me that I dare not bear? But before you strike me listen. You will get nothing from me by a blow, even though it should kill me, as it probably will. But perhaps you do not wish me to speak—killing may be all you mean?”\n\n“Kill you! Do you expect it?”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“No less degree of rage against me will match your previous grief for her.”\n\n“Phew—I shall not kill you,” he said contemptuously, as if under a sudden change of purpose. “I did think of it; but—I shall not. That would be making a martyr of you, and sending you to where she is; and I would keep you away from her till the universe come to an end, if I could.”\n\n“I almost wish you would kill me,” said she with gloomy bitterness. “It is with no strong desire, I assure you, that I play the part I have lately played on earth. You are no blessing, my husband.”\n\n“You shut the door—you looked out of the window upon her—you had a man in the house with you—you sent her away to die. The inhumanity—the treachery—I will not touch you—stand away from me—and confess every word!”\n\n“Never! I’ll hold my tongue like the very death that I don’t mind meeting, even though I can clear myself of half you believe by speaking. Yes. I will! Who of any dignity would take the trouble to clear cobwebs from a wild man’s mind after such language as this? No; let him go on, and think his narrow thoughts, and run his head into the mire. I have other cares.”\n\n“’Tis too much—but I must spare you.”\n\n“Poor charity.”\n\n“By my wretched soul you sting me, Eustacia! I can keep it up, and hotly too. Now, then, madam, tell me his name!”\n\n“Never, I am resolved.”\n\n“How often does he write to you? Where does he put his letters—when does he meet you? Ah, his letters! Do you tell me his name?”\n\n“I do not.”\n\n“Then I’ll find it myself.” His eyes had fallen upon a small desk that stood near, on which she was accustomed to write her letters. He went to it. It was locked.\n\n“Unlock this!”\n\n“You have no right to say it. That’s mine.”\n\nWithout another word he seized the desk and dashed it to the floor. The hinge burst open, and a number of letters tumbled out.\n\n“Stay!” said Eustacia, stepping before him with more excitement than she had hitherto shown.\n\n“Come, come! stand away! I must see them.”\n\nShe looked at the letters as they lay, checked her feeling and moved indifferently aside; when he gathered them up, and examined them.\n\nBy no stretch of meaning could any but a harmless construction be placed upon a single one of the letters themselves. The solitary exception was an empty envelope directed to her, and the handwriting was Wildeve’s. Yeobright held it up. Eustacia was doggedly silent.\n\n“Can you read, madam? Look at this envelope. Doubtless we shall find more soon, and what was inside them. I shall no doubt be gratified by learning in good time what a well-finished and full-blown adept in a certain trade my lady is.”\n\n“Do you say it to me—do you?” she gasped.\n\nHe searched further, but found nothing more. “What was in this letter?” he said.\n\n“Ask the writer. Am I your hound that you should talk to me in this way?”\n\n“Do you brave me? do you stand me out, mistress? Answer. Don’t look at me with those eyes if you would bewitch me again! Sooner than that I die. You refuse to answer?”\n\n“I wouldn’t tell you after this, if I were as innocent as the sweetest babe in heaven!”\n\n“Which you are not.”\n\n“Certainly I am not absolutely,” she replied. “I have not done what you suppose; but if to have done no harm at all is the only innocence recognized, I am beyond forgiveness. But I require no help from your conscience.”\n\n“You can resist, and resist again! Instead of hating you I could, I think, mourn for and pity you, if you were contrite, and would confess all. Forgive you I never can. I don’t speak of your lover—I will give you the benefit of the doubt in that matter, for it only affects me personally. But the other—had you half-killed _me_, had it been that you wilfully took the sight away from these feeble eyes of mine, I could have forgiven you. But _that’s_ too much for nature!”\n\n“Say no more. I will do without your pity. But I would have saved you from uttering what you will regret.”\n\n“I am going away now. I shall leave you.”\n\n“You need not go, as I am going myself. You will keep just as far away from me by staying here.”\n\n“Call her to mind—think of her—what goodness there was in her—it showed in every line of her face! Most women, even when but slightly annoyed, show a flicker of evil in some curl of the mouth or some corner of the cheek; but as for her, never in her angriest moments was there anything malicious in her look. She was angered quickly, but she forgave just as readily, and underneath her pride there was the meekness of a child. What came of it?—what cared you? You hated her just as she was learning to love you. O! couldn’t you see what was best for you, but must bring a curse upon me, and agony and death upon her, by doing that cruel deed! What was the fellow’s name who was keeping you company and causing you to add cruelty to her to your wrong to me? Was it Wildeve? Was it poor Thomasin’s husband? Heaven, what wickedness! Lost your voice, have you? It is natural after detection of that most noble trick.... Eustacia, didn’t any tender thought of your own mother lead you to think of being gentle to mine at such a time of weariness? Did not one grain of pity enter your heart as she turned away? Think what a vast opportunity was then lost of beginning a forgiving and honest course. Why did not you kick him out, and let her in, and say I’ll be an honest wife and a noble woman from this hour? Had I told you to go and quench eternally our last flickering chance of happiness here you could have done no worse. Well, she’s asleep now; and have you a hundred gallants, neither they nor you can insult her any more.”\n\n“You exaggerate fearfully,” she said in a faint, weary voice; “but I cannot enter into my defence—it is not worth doing. You are nothing to me in future, and the past side of the story may as well remain untold. I have lost all through you, but I have not complained. Your blunders and misfortunes may have been a sorrow to you, but they have been a wrong to me. All persons of refinement have been scared away from me since I sank into the mire of marriage. Is this your cherishing—to put me into a hut like this, and keep me like the wife of a hind? You deceived me—not by words, but by appearances, which are less seen through than words. But the place will serve as well as any other—as somewhere to pass from—into my grave.” Her words were smothered in her throat, and her head drooped down.\n\n“I don’t know what you mean by that. Am I the cause of your sin?” (Eustacia made a trembling motion towards him.) “What, you can begin to shed tears and offer me your hand? Good God! can you? No, not I. I’ll not commit the fault of taking that.” (The hand she had offered dropped nervelessly, but the tears continued flowing.) “Well, yes, I’ll take it, if only for the sake of my own foolish kisses that were wasted there before I knew what I cherished. How bewitched I was! How could there be any good in a woman that everybody spoke ill of?”\n\n“O, O, O!” she cried, breaking down at last; and, shaking with sobs which choked her, she sank upon her knees. “O, will you have done! O, you are too relentless—there’s a limit to the cruelty of savages! I have held out long—but you crush me down. I beg for mercy—I cannot bear this any longer—it is inhuman to go further with this! If I had—killed your—mother with my own hand—I should not deserve such a scourging to the bone as this. O, O! God have mercy upon a miserable woman!... You have beaten me in this game—I beg you to stay your hand in pity!... I confess that I—wilfully did not undo the door the first time she knocked—but—I should have unfastened it the second—if I had not thought you had gone to do it yourself. When I found you had not I opened it, but she was gone. That’s the extent of my crime—towards _her_. Best natures commit bad faults sometimes, don’t they?—I think they do. Now I will leave you—for ever and ever!”\n\n“Tell all, and I _will_ pity you. Was the man in the house with you Wildeve?”\n\n“I cannot tell,” she said desperately through her sobbing. “Don’t insist further—I cannot tell. I am going from this house. We cannot both stay here.”\n\n“You need not go—I will go. You can stay here.”\n\n“No, I will dress, and then I will go.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“Where I came from, or _else_where.”\n\nShe hastily dressed herself, Yeobright moodily walking up and down the room the whole of the time. At last all her things were on. Her little hands quivered so violently as she held them to her chin to fasten her bonnet that she could not tie the strings, and after a few moments she relinquished the attempt. Seeing this he moved forward and said, “Let me tie them.”\n\nShe assented in silence, and lifted her chin. For once at least in her life she was totally oblivious of the charm of her attitude. But he was not, and he turned his eyes aside, that he might not be tempted to softness.\n\nThe strings were tied; she turned from him. “Do you still prefer going away yourself to my leaving you?” he inquired again.\n\n“I do.”\n\n“Very well—let it be. And when you will confess to the man I may pity you.”\n\nShe flung her shawl about her and went downstairs, leaving him standing in the room.\n\nEustacia had not long been gone when there came a knock at the door of the bedroom; and Yeobright said, “Well?”\n\nIt was the servant; and she replied, “Somebody from Mrs. Wildeve’s have called to tell ’ee that the mis’ess and the baby are getting on wonderful well, and the baby’s name is to be Eustacia Clementine.” And the girl retired.\n\n“What a mockery!” said Clym. “This unhappy marriage of mine to be perpetuated in that child’s name!”\n\nIV. The Ministrations of a Half-forgotten One\nEustacia’s journey was at first as vague in direction as that of thistledown on the wind. She did not know what to do. She wished it had been night instead of morning, that she might at least have borne her misery without the possibility of being seen. Tracing mile after mile along between the dying ferns and the wet white spiders’ webs, she at length turned her steps towards her grandfather’s house. She found the front door closed and locked. Mechanically she went round to the end where the stable was, and on looking in at the stable door she saw Charley standing within.\n\n“Captain Vye is not at home?” she said.\n\n“No, ma’am,” said the lad in a flutter of feeling; “he’s gone to Weatherbury, and won’t be home till night. And the servant is gone home for a holiday. So the house is locked up.”\n\nEustacia’s face was not visible to Charley as she stood at the doorway, her back being to the sky, and the stable but indifferently lighted; but the wildness of her manner arrested his attention. She turned and walked away across the enclosure to the gate, and was hidden by the bank.\n\nWhen she had disappeared Charley, with misgiving in his eyes, slowly came from the stable door, and going to another point in the bank he looked over. Eustacia was leaning against it on the outside, her face covered with her hands, and her head pressing the dewy heather which bearded the bank’s outer side. She appeared to be utterly indifferent to the circumstance that her bonnet, hair, and garments were becoming wet and disarranged by the moisture of her cold, harsh pillow. Clearly something was wrong.\n\nCharley had always regarded Eustacia as Eustacia had regarded Clym when she first beheld him—as a romantic and sweet vision, scarcely incarnate. He had been so shut off from her by the dignity of her look and the pride of her speech, except at that one blissful interval when he was allowed to hold her hand, that he had hardly deemed her a woman, wingless and earthly, subject to household conditions and domestic jars. The inner details of her life he had only conjectured. She had been a lovely wonder, predestined to an orbit in which the whole of his own was but a point; and this sight of her leaning like a helpless, despairing creature against a wild wet bank filled him with an amazed horror. He could no longer remain where he was. Leaping over, he came up, touched her with his finger, and said tenderly, “You are poorly, ma’am. What can I do?”\n\nEustacia started up, and said, “Ah, Charley—you have followed me. You did not think when I left home in the summer that I should come back like this!”\n\n“I did not, dear ma’am. Can I help you now?”\n\n“I am afraid not. I wish I could get into the house. I feel giddy—that’s all.”\n\n“Lean on my arm, ma’am, till we get to the porch, and I will try to open the door.”\n\nHe supported her to the porch, and there depositing her on a seat hastened to the back, climbed to a window by the help of a ladder, and descending inside opened the door. Next he assisted her into the room, where there was an old-fashioned horsehair settee as large as a donkey wagon. She lay down here, and Charley covered her with a cloak he found in the hall.\n\n“Shall I get you something to eat and drink?” he said.\n\n“If you please, Charley. But I suppose there is no fire?”\n\n“I can light it, ma’am.”\n\nHe vanished, and she heard a splitting of wood and a blowing of bellows; and presently he returned, saying, “I have lighted a fire in the kitchen, and now I’ll light one here.”\n\nHe lit the fire, Eustacia dreamily observing him from her couch. When it was blazing up he said, “Shall I wheel you round in front of it, ma’am, as the morning is chilly?”\n\n“Yes, if you like.”\n\n“Shall I go and bring the victuals now?”\n\n“Yes, do,” she murmured languidly.\n\nWhen he had gone, and the dull sounds occasionally reached her ears of his movements in the kitchen, she forgot where she was, and had for a moment to consider by an effort what the sounds meant. After an interval which seemed short to her whose thoughts were elsewhere, he came in with a tray on which steamed tea and toast, though it was nearly lunch-time.\n\n“Place it on the table,” she said. “I shall be ready soon.”\n\nHe did so, and retired to the door; when, however, he perceived that she did not move he came back a few steps.\n\n“Let me hold it to you, if you don’t wish to get up,” said Charley. He brought the tray to the front of the couch, where he knelt down, adding, “I will hold it for you.”\n\nEustacia sat up and poured out a cup of tea. “You are very kind to me, Charley,” she murmured as she sipped.\n\n“Well, I ought to be,” said he diffidently, taking great trouble not to rest his eyes upon her, though this was their only natural position, Eustacia being immediately before him. “You have been kind to me.”\n\n“How have I?” said Eustacia.\n\n“You let me hold your hand when you were a maiden at home.”\n\n“Ah, so I did. Why did I do that? My mind is lost—it had to do with the mumming, had it not?”\n\n“Yes, you wanted to go in my place.”\n\n“I remember. I do indeed remember—too well!”\n\nShe again became utterly downcast; and Charley, seeing that she was not going to eat or drink any more, took away the tray.\n\nAfterwards he occasionally came in to see if the fire was burning, to ask her if she wanted anything, to tell her that the wind had shifted from south to west, to ask her if she would like him to gather her some blackberries; to all which inquiries she replied in the negative or with indifference.\n\nShe remained on the settee some time longer, when she aroused herself and went upstairs. The room in which she had formerly slept still remained much as she had left it, and the recollection that this forced upon her of her own greatly changed and infinitely worsened situation again set on her face the undetermined and formless misery which it had worn on her first arrival. She peeped into her grandfather’s room, through which the fresh autumn air was blowing from the open window. Her eye was arrested by what was a familiar sight enough, though it broke upon her now with a new significance.\n\nIt was a brace of pistols, hanging near the head of her grandfather’s bed, which he always kept there loaded, as a precaution against possible burglars, the house being very lonely. Eustacia regarded them long, as if they were the page of a book in which she read a new and a strange matter. Quickly, like one afraid of herself, she returned downstairs and stood in deep thought.\n\n“If I could only do it!” she said. “It would be doing much good to myself and all connected with me, and no harm to a single one.”\n\nThe idea seemed to gather force within her, and she remained in a fixed attitude nearly ten minutes, when a certain finality was expressed in her gaze, and no longer the blankness of indecision.\n\nShe turned and went up the second time—softly and stealthily now—and entered her grandfather’s room, her eyes at once seeking the head of the bed. The pistols were gone.\n\nThe instant quashing of her purpose by their absence affected her brain as a sudden vacuum affects the body—she nearly fainted. Who had done this? There was only one person on the premises besides herself. Eustacia involuntarily turned to the open window which overlooked the garden as far as the bank that bounded it. On the summit of the latter stood Charley, sufficiently elevated by its height to see into the room. His gaze was directed eagerly and solicitously upon her.\n\nShe went downstairs to the door and beckoned to him.\n\n“You have taken them away?”\n\n“Yes, ma’am.”\n\n“Why did you do it?”\n\n“I saw you looking at them too long.”\n\n“What has that to do with it?”\n\n“You have been heart-broken all the morning, as if you did not want to live.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“And I could not bear to leave them in your way. There was meaning in your look at them.”\n\n“Where are they now?”\n\n“Locked up.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“In the stable.”\n\n“Give them to me.”\n\n“No, ma’am.”\n\n“You refuse?”\n\n“I do. I care too much for you to give ’em up.”\n\nShe turned aside, her face for the first time softening from the stony immobility of the earlier day, and the corners of her mouth resuming something of that delicacy of cut which was always lost in her moments of despair. At last she confronted him again.\n\n“Why should I not die if I wish?” she said tremulously. “I have made a bad bargain with life, and I am weary of it—weary. And now you have hindered my escape. O, why did you, Charley! What makes death painful except the thought of others’ grief?—and that is absent in my case, for not a sigh would follow me!”\n\n“Ah, it is trouble that has done this! I wish in my very soul that he who brought it about might die and rot, even if ’tis transportation to say it!”\n\n“Charley, no more of that. What do you mean to do about this you have seen?”\n\n“Keep it close as night, if you promise not to think of it again.”\n\n“You need not fear. The moment has passed. I promise.” She then went away, entered the house, and lay down.\n\nLater in the afternoon her grandfather returned. He was about to question her categorically, but on looking at her he withheld his words.\n\n“Yes, it is too bad to talk of,” she slowly returned in answer to his glance. “Can my old room be got ready for me tonight, Grandfather? I shall want to occupy it again.”\n\nHe did not ask what it all meant, or why she had left her husband, but ordered the room to be prepared.\n\nV. An Old Move Inadvertently Repeated\n\nCharley’s attentions to his former mistress were unbounded. The only solace to his own trouble lay in his attempts to relieve hers. Hour after hour he considered her wants; he thought of her presence there with a sort of gratitude, and, while uttering imprecations on the cause of her unhappiness, in some measure blessed the result. Perhaps she would always remain there, he thought, and then he would be as happy as he had been before. His dread was lest she should think fit to return to Alderworth, and in that dread his eyes, with all the inquisitiveness of affection, frequently sought her face when she was not observing him, as he would have watched the head of a stockdove to learn if it contemplated flight. Having once really succoured her, and possibly preserved her from the rashest of acts, he mentally assumed in addition a guardian’s responsibility for her welfare.\n\nFor this reason he busily endeavoured to provide her with pleasant distractions, bringing home curious objects which he found in the heath, such as white trumpet-shaped mosses, redheaded lichens, stone arrowheads used by the old tribes on Egdon, and faceted crystals from the hollows of flints. These he deposited on the premises in such positions that she should see them as if by accident.\n\nA week passed, Eustacia never going out of the house. Then she walked into the enclosed plot and looked through her grandfather’s spyglass, as she had been in the habit of doing before her marriage. One day she saw, at a place where the highroad crossed the distant valley, a heavily laden wagon passing along. It was piled with household furniture. She looked again and again, and recognized it to be her own. In the evening her grandfather came indoors with a rumour that Yeobright had removed that day from Alderworth to the old house at Blooms-End.\n\nOn another occasion when reconnoitring thus she beheld two female figures walking in the vale. The day was fine and clear; and the persons not being more than half a mile off she could see their every detail with the telescope. The woman walking in front carried a white bundle in her arms, from one end of which hung a long appendage of drapery; and when the walkers turned, so that the sun fell more directly upon them, Eustacia could see that the object was a baby. She called Charley, and asked him if he knew who they were, though she well guessed.\n\n“Mrs. Wildeve and the nurse-girl,” said Charley.\n\n“The nurse is carrying the baby?” said Eustacia.\n\n“No, ’tis Mrs. Wildeve carrying that,” he answered, “and the nurse walks behind carrying nothing.”\n\nThe lad was in good spirits that day, for the Fifth of November had again come round, and he was planning yet another scheme to divert her from her too absorbing thoughts. For two successive years his mistress had seemed to take pleasure in lighting a bonfire on the bank overlooking the valley; but this year she had apparently quite forgotten the day and the customary deed. He was careful not to remind her, and went on with his secret preparations for a cheerful surprise, the more zealously that he had been absent last time and unable to assist. At every vacant minute he hastened to gather furze-stumps, thorn-tree roots, and other solid materials from the adjacent slopes, hiding them from cursory view.\n\nThe evening came, and Eustacia was still seemingly unconscious of the anniversary. She had gone indoors after her survey through the glass, and had not been visible since. As soon as it was quite dark Charley began to build the bonfire, choosing precisely that spot on the bank which Eustacia had chosen at previous times.\n\nWhen all the surrounding bonfires had burst into existence Charley kindled his, and arranged its fuel so that it should not require tending for some time. He then went back to the house, and lingered round the door and windows till she should by some means or other learn of his achievement and come out to witness it. But the shutters were closed, the door remained shut, and no heed whatever seemed to be taken of his performance. Not liking to call her he went back and replenished the fire, continuing to do this for more than half an hour. It was not till his stock of fuel had greatly diminished that he went to the back door and sent in to beg that Mrs. Yeobright would open the window-shutters and see the sight outside.\n\nEustacia, who had been sitting listlessly in the parlour, started up at the intelligence and flung open the shutters. Facing her on the bank blazed the fire, which at once sent a ruddy glare into the room where she was, and overpowered the candles.\n\n“Well done, Charley!” said Captain Vye from the chimney-corner. “But I hope it is not my wood that he’s burning.... Ah, it was this time last year that I met with that man Venn, bringing home Thomasin Yeobright—to be sure it was! Well, who would have thought that girl’s troubles would have ended so well? What a snipe you were in that matter, Eustacia! Has your husband written to you yet?”\n\n“No,” said Eustacia, looking vaguely through the window at the fire, which just then so much engaged her mind that she did not resent her grandfather’s blunt opinion. She could see Charley’s form on the bank, shovelling and stirring the fire; and there flashed upon her imagination some other form which that fire might call up.\n\nShe left the room, put on her garden bonnet and cloak, and went out. Reaching the bank, she looked over with a wild curiosity and misgiving, when Charley said to her, with a pleased sense of himself, “I made it o’ purpose for you, ma’am.”\n\n“Thank you,” she said hastily. “But I wish you to put it out now.”\n\n“It will soon burn down,” said Charley, rather disappointed. “Is it not a pity to knock it out?”\n\n“I don’t know,” she musingly answered.\n\nThey stood in silence, broken only by the crackling of the flames, till Charley, perceiving that she did not want to talk to him, moved reluctantly away.\n\nEustacia remained within the bank looking at the fire, intending to go indoors, yet lingering still. Had she not by her situation been inclined to hold in indifference all things honoured of the gods and of men she would probably have come away. But her state was so hopeless that she could play with it. To have lost is less disturbing than to wonder if we may possibly have won; and Eustacia could now, like other people at such a stage, take a standing-point outside herself, observe herself as a disinterested spectator, and think what a sport for Heaven this woman Eustacia was.\n\nWhile she stood she heard a sound. It was the splash of a stone in the pond.\n\nHad Eustacia received the stone full in the bosom her heart could not have given a more decided thump. She had thought of the possibility of such a signal in answer to that which had been unwittingly given by Charley; but she had not expected it yet. How prompt Wildeve was! Yet how could he think her capable of deliberately wishing to renew their assignations now? An impulse to leave the spot, a desire to stay, struggled within her; and the desire held its own. More than that it did not do, for she refrained even from ascending the bank and looking over. She remained motionless, not disturbing a muscle of her face or raising her eyes; for were she to turn up her face the fire on the bank would shine upon it, and Wildeve might be looking down.\n\nThere was a second splash into the pond.\n\nWhy did he stay so long without advancing and looking over? Curiosity had its way—she ascended one or two of the earth-steps in the bank and glanced out.\n\nWildeve was before her. He had come forward after throwing the last pebble, and the fire now shone into each of their faces from the bank stretching breast-high between them.\n\n“I did not light it!” cried Eustacia quickly. “It was lit without my knowledge. Don’t, don’t come over to me!”\n\n“Why have you been living here all these days without telling me? You have left your home. I fear I am something to blame in this?”\n\n“I did not let in his mother; that’s how it is!”\n\n“You do not deserve what you have got, Eustacia; you are in great misery; I see it in your eyes, your mouth, and all over you. My poor, poor girl!” He stepped over the bank. “You are beyond everything unhappy!”\n\n“No, no; not exactly—”\n\n“It has been pushed too far—it is killing you—I do think it!”\n\nHer usually quiet breathing had grown quicker with his words. “I—I—” she began, and then burst into quivering sobs, shaken to the very heart by the unexpected voice of pity—a sentiment whose existence in relation to herself she had almost forgotten.\n\nThis outbreak of weeping took Eustacia herself so much by surprise that she could not leave off, and she turned aside from him in some shame, though turning hid nothing from him. She sobbed on desperately; then the outpour lessened, and she became quieter. Wildeve had resisted the impulse to clasp her, and stood without speaking.\n\n“Are you not ashamed of me, who used never to be a crying animal?” she asked in a weak whisper as she wiped her eyes. “Why didn’t you go away? I wish you had not seen quite all that; it reveals too much by half.”\n\n“You might have wished it, because it makes me as sad as you,” he said with emotion and deference. “As for revealing—the word is impossible between us two.”\n\n“I did not send for you—don’t forget it, Damon; I am in pain, but I did not send for you! As a wife, at least, I’ve been straight.”\n\n“Never mind—I came. O, Eustacia, forgive me for the harm I have done you in these two past years! I see more and more that I have been your ruin.”\n\n“Not you. This place I live in.”\n\n“Ah, your generosity may naturally make you say that. But I am the culprit. I should either have done more or nothing at all.”\n\n“In what way?”\n\n“I ought never to have hunted you out, or, having done it, I ought to have persisted in retaining you. But of course I have no right to talk of that now. I will only ask this—can I do anything for you? Is there anything on the face of the earth that a man can do to make you happier than you are at present? If there is, I will do it. You may command me, Eustacia, to the limit of my influence; and don’t forget that I am richer now. Surely something can be done to save you from this! Such a rare plant in such a wild place it grieves me to see. Do you want anything bought? Do you want to go anywhere? Do you want to escape the place altogether? Only say it, and I’ll do anything to put an end to those tears, which but for me would never have been at all.”\n\n“We are each married to another person,” she said faintly; “and assistance from you would have an evil sound—after—after—”\n\n“Well, there’s no preventing slanderers from having their fill at any time; but you need not be afraid. Whatever I may feel I promise you on my word of honour never to speak to you about—or act upon—until you say I may. I know my duty to Thomasin quite as well as I know my duty to you as a woman unfairly treated. What shall I assist you in?”\n\n“In getting away from here.”\n\n“Where do you wish to go to?”\n\n“I have a place in my mind. If you could help me as far as Budmouth I can do all the rest. Steamers sail from there across the Channel, and so I can get to Paris, where I want to be. Yes,” she pleaded earnestly, “help me to get to Budmouth harbour without my grandfather’s or my husband’s knowledge, and I can do all the rest.”\n\n“Will it be safe to leave you there alone?”\n\n“Yes, yes. I know Budmouth well.”\n\n“Shall I go with you? I am rich now.”\n\nShe was silent.\n\n“Say yes, sweet!”\n\nShe was silent still.\n\n“Well, let me know when you wish to go. We shall be at our present house till December; after that we remove to Casterbridge. Command me in anything till that time.”\n\n“I will think of this,” she said hurriedly. “Whether I can honestly make use of you as a friend, or must close with you as a lover—that is what I must ask myself. If I wish to go and decide to accept your company I will signal to you some evening at eight o’clock punctually, and this will mean that you are to be ready with a horse and trap at twelve o’clock the same night to drive me to Budmouth harbour in time for the morning boat.”\n\n“I will look out every night at eight, and no signal shall escape me.”\n\n“Now please go away. If I decide on this escape I can only meet you once more unless—I cannot go without you. Go—I cannot bear it longer. Go—go!”\n\nWildeve slowly went up the steps and descended into the darkness on the other side; and as he walked he glanced back, till the bank blotted out her form from his further view.\n\nVI. Thomasin Argues with Her Cousin, and He Writes a Letter\n\nYeobright was at this time at Blooms-End, hoping that Eustacia would return to him. The removal of furniture had been accomplished only that day, though Clym had lived in the old house for more than a week. He had spent the time in working about the premises, sweeping leaves from the garden paths, cutting dead stalks from the flower beds, and nailing up creepers which had been displaced by the autumn winds. He took no particular pleasure in these deeds, but they formed a screen between himself and despair. Moreover, it had become a religion with him to preserve in good condition all that had lapsed from his mother’s hands to his own.\n\nDuring these operations he was constantly on the watch for Eustacia. That there should be no mistake about her knowing where to find him he had ordered a notice board to be affixed to the garden gate at Alderworth, signifying in white letters whither he had removed. When a leaf floated to the earth he turned his head, thinking it might be her foot-fall. A bird searching for worms in the mould of the flower-beds sounded like her hand on the latch of the gate; and at dusk, when soft, strange ventriloquisms came from holes in the ground, hollow stalks, curled dead leaves, and other crannies wherein breezes, worms, and insects can work their will, he fancied that they were Eustacia, standing without and breathing wishes of reconciliation.\n\nUp to this hour he had persevered in his resolve not to invite her back. At the same time the severity with which he had treated her lulled the sharpness of his regret for his mother, and awoke some of his old solicitude for his mother’s supplanter. Harsh feelings produce harsh usage, and this by reaction quenches the sentiments that gave it birth. The more he reflected the more he softened. But to look upon his wife as innocence in distress was impossible, though he could ask himself whether he had given her quite time enough—if he had not come a little too suddenly upon her on that sombre morning.\n\nNow that the first flush of his anger had paled he was disinclined to ascribe to her more than an indiscreet friendship with Wildeve, for there had not appeared in her manner the signs of dishonour. And this once admitted, an absolutely dark interpretation of her act towards his mother was no longer forced upon him.\n\nOn the evening of the fifth November his thoughts of Eustacia were intense. Echoes from those past times when they had exchanged tender words all the day long came like the diffused murmur of a seashore left miles behind. “Surely,” he said, “she might have brought herself to communicate with me before now, and confess honestly what Wildeve was to her.”\n\nInstead of remaining at home that night he determined to go and see Thomasin and her husband. If he found opportunity he would allude to the cause of the separation between Eustacia and himself, keeping silence, however, on the fact that there was a third person in his house when his mother was turned away. If it proved that Wildeve was innocently there he would doubtless openly mention it. If he were there with unjust intentions Wildeve, being a man of quick feeling, might possibly say something to reveal the extent to which Eustacia was compromised.\n\nBut on reaching his cousin’s house he found that only Thomasin was at home, Wildeve being at that time on his way towards the bonfire innocently lit by Charley at Mistover. Thomasin then, as always, was glad to see Clym, and took him to inspect the sleeping baby, carefully screening the candlelight from the infant’s eyes with her hand.\n\n“Tamsin, have you heard that Eustacia is not with me now?” he said when they had sat down again.\n\n“No,” said Thomasin, alarmed.\n\n“And not that I have left Alderworth?”\n\n“No. I never hear tidings from Alderworth unless you bring them. What is the matter?”\n\nClym in a disturbed voice related to her his visit to Susan Nunsuch’s boy, the revelation he had made, and what had resulted from his charging Eustacia with having wilfully and heartlessly done the deed. He suppressed all mention of Wildeve’s presence with her.\n\n“All this, and I not knowing it!” murmured Thomasin in an awestruck tone, “Terrible! What could have made her—O, Eustacia! And when you found it out you went in hot haste to her? Were you too cruel?—or is she really so wicked as she seems?”\n\n“Can a man be too cruel to his mother’s enemy?”\n\n“I can fancy so.”\n\n“Very well, then—I’ll admit that he can. But now what is to be done?”\n\n“Make it up again—if a quarrel so deadly can ever be made up. I almost wish you had not told me. But do try to be reconciled. There are ways, after all, if you both wish to.”\n\n“I don’t know that we do both wish to make it up,” said Clym. “If she had wished it, would she not have sent to me by this time?”\n\n“You seem to wish to, and yet you have not sent to her.”\n\n“True; but I have been tossed to and fro in doubt if I ought, after such strong provocation. To see me now, Thomasin, gives you no idea of what I have been; of what depths I have descended to in these few last days. O, it was a bitter shame to shut out my mother like that! Can I ever forget it, or even agree to see her again?”\n\n“She might not have known that anything serious would come of it, and perhaps she did not mean to keep Aunt out altogether.”\n\n“She says herself that she did not. But the fact remains that keep her out she did.”\n\n“Believe her sorry, and send for her.”\n\n“How if she will not come?”\n\n“It will prove her guilty, by showing that it is her habit to nourish enmity. But I do not think that for a moment.”\n\n“I will do this. I will wait for a day or two longer—not longer than two days certainly; and if she does not send to me in that time I will indeed send to her. I thought to have seen Wildeve here tonight. Is he from home?”\n\nThomasin blushed a little. “No,” she said. “He is merely gone out for a walk.”\n\n“Why didn’t he take you with him? The evening is fine. You want fresh air as well as he.”\n\n“Oh, I don’t care for going anywhere; besides, there is baby.”\n\n“Yes, yes. Well, I have been thinking whether I should not consult your husband about this as well as you,” said Clym steadily.\n\n“I fancy I would not,” she quickly answered. “It can do no good.”\n\nHer cousin looked her in the face. No doubt Thomasin was ignorant that her husband had any share in the events of that tragic afternoon; but her countenance seemed to signify that she concealed some suspicion or thought of the reputed tender relations between Wildeve and Eustacia in days gone by.\n\nClym, however, could make nothing of it, and he rose to depart, more in doubt than when he came.\n\n“You will write to her in a day or two?” said the young woman earnestly. “I do so hope the wretched separation may come to an end.”\n\n“I will,” said Clym; “I don’t rejoice in my present state at all.”\n\nAnd he left her and climbed over the hill to Blooms-End. Before going to bed he sat down and wrote the following letter:—\n\nMY DEAR EUSTACIA,—I must obey my heart without consulting my reason too closely. Will you come back to me? Do so, and the past shall never be mentioned. I was too severe; but O, Eustacia, the provocation! You don’t know, you never will know, what those words of anger cost me which you drew down upon yourself. All that an honest man can promise you I promise now, which is that from me you shall never suffer anything on this score again. After all the vows we have made, Eustacia, I think we had better pass the remainder of our lives in trying to keep them. Come to me, then, even if you reproach me. I have thought of your sufferings that morning on which I parted from you; I know they were genuine, and they are as much as you ought to bear. Our love must still continue. Such hearts as ours would never have been given us but to be concerned with each other. I could not ask you back at first, Eustacia, for I was unable to persuade myself that he who was with you was not there as a lover. But if you will come and explain distracting appearances I do not question that you can show your honesty to me. Why have you not come before? Do you think I will not listen to you? Surely not, when you remember the kisses and vows we exchanged under the summer moon. Return then, and you shall be warmly welcomed. I can no longer think of you to your prejudice—I am but too much absorbed in justifying you.—Your husband as ever,\n\n“There,” he said, as he laid it in his desk, “that’s a good thing done. If she does not come before tomorrow night I will send it to her.”\n\nMeanwhile, at the house he had just left Thomasin sat sighing uneasily. Fidelity to her husband had that evening induced her to conceal all suspicion that Wildeve’s interest in Eustacia had not ended with his marriage. But she knew nothing positive; and though Clym was her well-beloved cousin there was one nearer to her still.\n\nWhen, a little later, Wildeve returned from his walk to Mistover, Thomasin said, “Damon, where have you been? I was getting quite frightened, and thought you had fallen into the river. I dislike being in the house by myself.”\n\n“Frightened?” he said, touching her cheek as if she were some domestic animal. “Why, I thought nothing could frighten you. It is that you are getting proud, I am sure, and don’t like living here since we have risen above our business. Well, it is a tedious matter, this getting a new house; but I couldn’t have set about it sooner, unless our ten thousand pounds had been a hundred thousand, when we could have afforded to despise caution.”\n\n“No—I don’t mind waiting—I would rather stay here twelve months longer than run any risk with baby. But I don’t like your vanishing so in the evenings. There’s something on your mind—I know there is, Damon. You go about so gloomily, and look at the heath as if it were somebody’s gaol instead of a nice wild place to walk in.”\n\nHe looked towards her with pitying surprise. “What, do you like Egdon Heath?” he said.\n\n“I like what I was born near to; I admire its grim old face.”\n\n“Pooh, my dear. You don’t know what you like.”\n\n“I am sure I do. There’s only one thing unpleasant about Egdon.”\n\n“What’s that?”\n\n“You never take me with you when you walk there. Why do you wander so much in it yourself if you so dislike it?”\n\nThe inquiry, though a simple one, was plainly disconcerting, and he sat down before replying. “I don’t think you often see me there. Give an instance.”\n\n“I will,” she answered triumphantly. “When you went out this evening I thought that as baby was asleep I would see where you were going to so mysteriously without telling me. So I ran out and followed behind you. You stopped at the place where the road forks, looked round at the bonfires, and then said, ‘Damn it, I’ll go!’ And you went quickly up the left-hand road. Then I stood and watched you.”\n\nWildeve frowned, afterwards saying, with a forced smile, “Well, what wonderful discovery did you make?”\n\n“There—now you are angry, and we won’t talk of this any more.” She went across to him, sat on a footstool, and looked up in his face.\n\n“Nonsense!” he said, “that’s how you always back out. We will go on with it now we have begun. What did you next see? I particularly want to know.”\n\n“Don’t be like that, Damon!” she murmured. “I didn’t see anything. You vanished out of sight, and then I looked round at the bonfires and came in.”\n\n“Perhaps this is not the only time you have dogged my steps. Are you trying to find out something bad about me?”\n\n“Not at all! I have never done such a thing before, and I shouldn’t have done it now if words had not sometimes been dropped about you.”\n\n“What _do_ you mean?” he impatiently asked.\n\n“They say—they say you used to go to Alderworth in the evenings, and it puts into my mind what I have heard about—”\n\nWildeve turned angrily and stood up in front of her. “Now,” he said, flourishing his hand in the air, “just out with it, madam! I demand to know what remarks you have heard.”\n\n“Well, I heard that you used to be very fond of Eustacia—nothing more than that, though dropped in a bit-by-bit way. You ought not to be angry!”\n\nHe observed that her eyes were brimming with tears. “Well,” he said, “there is nothing new in that, and of course I don’t mean to be rough towards you, so you need not cry. Now, don’t let us speak of the subject any more.”\n\nAnd no more was said, Thomasin being glad enough of a reason for not mentioning Clym’s visit to her that evening, and his story.\n\nVII. The Night of the Sixth of November\n\nHaving resolved on flight Eustacia at times seemed anxious that something should happen to thwart her own intention. The only event that could really change her position was the appearance of Clym. The glory which had encircled him as her lover was departed now; yet some good simple quality of his would occasionally return to her memory and stir a momentary throb of hope that he would again present himself before her. But calmly considered it was not likely that such a severance as now existed would ever close up—she would have to live on as a painful object, isolated, and out of place. She had used to think of the heath alone as an uncongenial spot to be in; she felt it now of the whole world.\n\nTowards evening on the sixth her determination to go away again revived. About four o’clock she packed up anew the few small articles she had brought in her flight from Alderworth, and also some belonging to her which had been left here; the whole formed a bundle not too large to be carried in her hand for a distance of a mile or two. The scene without grew darker; mud-coloured clouds bellied downwards from the sky like vast hammocks slung across it, and with the increase of night a stormy wind arose; but as yet there was no rain.\n\nEustacia could not rest indoors, having nothing more to do, and she wandered to and fro on the hill, not far from the house she was soon to leave. In these desultory ramblings she passed the cottage of Susan Nunsuch, a little lower down than her grandfather’s. The door was ajar, and a riband of bright firelight fell over the ground without. As Eustacia crossed the firebeams she appeared for an instant as distinct as a figure in a phantasmagoria—a creature of light surrounded by an area of darkness; the moment passed, and she was absorbed in night again.\n\nA woman who was sitting inside the cottage had seen and recognized her in that momentary irradiation. This was Susan herself, occupied in preparing a posset for her little boy, who, often ailing, was now seriously unwell. Susan dropped the spoon, shook her fist at the vanished figure, and then proceeded with her work in a musing, absent way.\n\nAt eight o’clock, the hour at which Eustacia had promised to signal Wildeve if ever she signalled at all, she looked around the premises to learn if the coast was clear, went to the furze-rick, and pulled thence a long-stemmed bough of that fuel. This she carried to the corner of the bank, and, glancing behind to see if the shutters were all closed, she struck a light, and kindled the furze. When it was thoroughly ablaze Eustacia took it by the stem and waved it in the air above her head till it had burned itself out.\n\nShe was gratified, if gratification were possible to such a mood, by seeing a similar light in the vicinity of Wildeve’s residence a minute or two later. Having agreed to keep watch at this hour every night, in case she should require assistance, this promptness proved how strictly he had held to his word. Four hours after the present time, that is, at midnight, he was to be ready to drive her to Budmouth, as prearranged.\n\nEustacia returned to the house. Supper having been got over she retired early, and sat in her bedroom waiting for the time to go by. The night being dark and threatening, Captain Vye had not strolled out to gossip in any cottage or to call at the inn, as was sometimes his custom on these long autumn nights; and he sat sipping grog alone downstairs. About ten o’clock there was a knock at the door. When the servant opened it the rays of the candle fell upon the form of Fairway.\n\n“I was a-forced to go to Lower Mistover tonight,” he said, “and Mr. Yeobright asked me to leave this here on my way; but, faith, I put it in the lining of my hat, and thought no more about it till I got back and was hasping my gate before going to bed. So I have run back with it at once.”\n\nHe handed in a letter and went his way. The girl brought it to the captain, who found that it was directed to Eustacia. He turned it over and over, and fancied that the writing was her husband’s, though he could not be sure. However, he decided to let her have it at once if possible, and took it upstairs for that purpose; but on reaching the door of her room and looking in at the keyhole he found there was no light within, the fact being that Eustacia, without undressing, had flung herself upon the bed, to rest and gather a little strength for her coming journey. Her grandfather concluded from what he saw that he ought not to disturb her; and descending again to the parlour he placed the letter on the mantelpiece to give it to her in the morning.\n\nAt eleven o’clock he went to bed himself, smoked for some time in his bedroom, put out his light at half-past eleven, and then, as was his invariable custom, pulled up the blind before getting into bed, that he might see which way the wind blew on opening his eyes in the morning, his bedroom window commanding a view of the flagstaff and vane. Just as he had lain down he was surprised to observe the white pole of the staff flash into existence like a streak of phosphorus drawn downwards across the shade of night without. Only one explanation met this—a light had been suddenly thrown upon the pole from the direction of the house. As everybody had retired to rest the old man felt it necessary to get out of bed, open the window softly, and look to the right and left. Eustacia’s bedroom was lighted up, and it was the shine from her window which had lighted the pole. Wondering what had aroused her, he remained undecided at the window, and was thinking of fetching the letter to slip it under her door, when he heard a slight brushing of garments on the partition dividing his room from the passage.\n\nThe captain concluded that Eustacia, feeling wakeful, had gone for a book, and would have dismissed the matter as unimportant if he had not also heard her distinctly weeping as she passed.\n\n“She is thinking of that husband of hers,” he said to himself. “Ah, the silly goose! she had no business to marry him. I wonder if that letter is really his?”\n\nHe arose, threw his boat-cloak round him, opened the door, and said, “Eustacia!” There was no answer. “Eustacia!” he repeated louder, “there is a letter on the mantelpiece for you.”\n\nBut no response was made to this statement save an imaginary one from the wind, which seemed to gnaw at the corners of the house, and the stroke of a few drops of rain upon the windows.\n\nHe went on to the landing, and stood waiting nearly five minutes. Still she did not return. He went back for a light, and prepared to follow her; but first he looked into her bedroom. There, on the outside of the quilt, was the impression of her form, showing that the bed had not been opened; and, what was more significant, she had not taken her candlestick downstairs. He was now thoroughly alarmed; and hastily putting on his clothes he descended to the front door, which he himself had bolted and locked. It was now unfastened. There was no longer any doubt that Eustacia had left the house at this midnight hour; and whither could she have gone? To follow her was almost impossible. Had the dwelling stood in an ordinary road, two persons setting out, one in each direction, might have made sure of overtaking her; but it was a hopeless task to seek for anybody on a heath in the dark, the practicable directions for flight across it from any point being as numerous as the meridians radiating from the pole. Perplexed what to do, he looked into the parlour, and was vexed to find that the letter still lay there untouched.\n\nAt half-past eleven, finding that the house was silent, Eustacia had lighted her candle, put on some warm outer wrappings, taken her bag in her hand, and, extinguishing the light again, descended the staircase. When she got into the outer air she found that it had begun to rain, and as she stood pausing at the door it increased, threatening to come on heavily. But having committed herself to this line of action there was no retreating for bad weather. Even the receipt of Clym’s letter would not have stopped her now. The gloom of the night was funereal; all nature seemed clothed in crape. The spiky points of the fir trees behind the house rose into the sky like the turrets and pinnacles of an abbey. Nothing below the horizon was visible save a light which was still burning in the cottage of Susan Nunsuch.\n\nEustacia opened her umbrella and went out from the enclosure by the steps over the bank, after which she was beyond all danger of being perceived. Skirting the pool, she followed the path towards Rainbarrow, occasionally stumbling over twisted furze roots, tufts of rushes, or oozing lumps of fleshy fungi, which at this season lay scattered about the heath like the rotten liver and lungs of some colossal animal. The moon and stars were closed up by cloud and rain to the degree of extinction. It was a night which led the traveller’s thoughts instinctively to dwell on nocturnal scenes of disaster in the chronicles of the world, on all that is terrible and dark in history and legend—the last plague of Egypt, the destruction of Sennacherib’s host, the agony in Gethsemane.\n\nEustacia at length reached Rainbarrow, and stood still there to think. Never was harmony more perfect than that between the chaos of her mind and the chaos of the world without. A sudden recollection had flashed on her this moment—she had not money enough for undertaking a long journey. Amid the fluctuating sentiments of the day her unpractical mind had not dwelt on the necessity of being well-provided, and now that she thoroughly realized the conditions she sighed bitterly and ceased to stand erect, gradually crouching down under the umbrella as if she were drawn into the Barrow by a hand from beneath. Could it be that she was to remain a captive still? Money—she had never felt its value before. Even to efface herself from the country means were required. To ask Wildeve for pecuniary aid without allowing him to accompany her was impossible to a woman with a shadow of pride left in her; to fly as his mistress—and she knew that he loved her—was of the nature of humiliation.\n\nAnyone who had stood by now would have pitied her, not so much on account of her exposure to weather, and isolation from all of humanity except the mouldered remains inside the tumulus; but for that other form of misery which was denoted by the slightly rocking movement that her feelings imparted to her person. Extreme unhappiness weighed visibly upon her. Between the drippings of the rain from her umbrella to her mantle, from her mantle to the heather, from the heather to the earth, very similar sounds could be heard coming from her lips; and the tearfulness of the outer scene was repeated upon her face. The wings of her soul were broken by the cruel obstructiveness of all about her; and even had she seen herself in a promising way of getting to Budmouth, entering a steamer, and sailing to some opposite port, she would have been but little more buoyant, so fearfully malignant were other things. She uttered words aloud. When a woman in such a situation, neither old, deaf, crazed, nor whimsical, takes upon herself to sob and soliloquize aloud there is something grievous the matter.\n\n“Can I go, can I go?” she moaned. “He’s not _great_ enough for me to give myself to—he does not suffice for my desire!... If he had been a Saul or a Bonaparte—ah! But to break my marriage vow for him—it is too poor a luxury!... And I have no money to go alone! And if I could, what comfort to me? I must drag on next year, as I have dragged on this year, and the year after that as before. How I have tried and tried to be a splendid woman, and how destiny has been against me!... I do not deserve my lot!” she cried in a frenzy of bitter revolt. “O, the cruelty of putting me into this ill-conceived world! I was capable of much; but I have been injured and blighted and crushed by things beyond my control! O, how hard it is of Heaven to devise such tortures for me, who have done no harm to Heaven at all!”\n\nThe distant light which Eustacia had cursorily observed in leaving the house came, as she had divined, from the cottage window of Susan Nunsuch. What Eustacia did not divine was the occupation of the woman within at that moment. Susan’s sight of her passing figure earlier in the evening, not five minutes after the sick boy’s exclamation, “Mother, I do feel so bad!” persuaded the matron that an evil influence was certainly exercised by Eustacia’s propinquity.\n\nOn this account Susan did not go to bed as soon as the evening’s work was over, as she would have done at ordinary times. To counteract the malign spell which she imagined poor Eustacia to be working, the boy’s mother busied herself with a ghastly invention of superstition, calculated to bring powerlessness, atrophy, and annihilation on any human being against whom it was directed. It was a practice well known on Egdon at that date, and one that is not quite extinct at the present day.\n\nShe passed with her candle into an inner room, where, among other utensils, were two large brown pans, containing together perhaps a hundredweight of liquid honey, the produce of the bees during the foregoing summer. On a shelf over the pans was a smooth and solid yellow mass of a hemispherical form, consisting of beeswax from the same take of honey. Susan took down the lump, and cutting off several thin slices, heaped them in an iron ladle, with which she returned to the living-room, and placed the vessel in the hot ashes of the fireplace. As soon as the wax had softened to the plasticity of dough she kneaded the pieces together. And now her face became more intent. She began moulding the wax; and it was evident from her manner of manipulation that she was endeavouring to give it some preconceived form. The form was human.\n\nBy warming and kneading, cutting and twisting, dismembering and re-joining the incipient image she had in about a quarter of an hour produced a shape which tolerably well resembled a woman, and was about six inches high. She laid it on the table to get cold and hard. Meanwhile she took the candle and went upstairs to where the little boy was lying.\n\n“Did you notice, my dear, what Mrs. Eustacia wore this afternoon besides the dark dress?”\n\n“A red ribbon round her neck.”\n\n“Anything else?”\n\n“No—except sandal-shoes.”\n\n“A red ribbon and sandal-shoes,” she said to herself.\n\nMrs. Nunsuch went and searched till she found a fragment of the narrowest red ribbon, which she took downstairs and tied round the neck of the image. Then fetching ink and a quilt from the rickety bureau by the window, she blackened the feet of the image to the extent presumably covered by shoes; and on the instep of each foot marked cross-lines in the shape taken by the sandalstrings of those days. Finally she tied a bit of black thread round the upper part of the head, in faint resemblance to a snood worn for confining the hair.\n\nSusan held the object at arm’s length and contemplated it with a satisfaction in which there was no smile. To anybody acquainted with the inhabitants of Egdon Heath the image would have suggested Eustacia Yeobright.\n\nFrom her workbasket in the window-seat the woman took a paper of pins, of the old long and yellow sort, whose heads were disposed to come off at their first usage. These she began to thrust into the image in all directions, with apparently excruciating energy. Probably as many as fifty were thus inserted, some into the head of the wax model, some into the shoulders, some into the trunk, some upwards through the soles of the feet, till the figure was completely permeated with pins.\n\nShe turned to the fire. It had been of turf; and though the high heap of ashes which turf fires produce was somewhat dark and dead on the outside, upon raking it abroad with the shovel the inside of the mass showed a glow of red heat. She took a few pieces of fresh turf from the chimney-corner and built them together over the glow, upon which the fire brightened. Seizing with the tongs the image that she had made of Eustacia, she held it in the heat, and watched it as it began to waste slowly away. And while she stood thus engaged there came from between her lips a murmur of words.\n\nIt was a strange jargon—the Lord’s Prayer repeated backwards—the incantation usual in proceedings for obtaining unhallowed assistance against an enemy. Susan uttered the lugubrious discourse three times slowly, and when it was completed the image had considerably diminished. As the wax dropped into the fire a long flame arose from the spot, and curling its tongue round the figure ate still further into its substance. A pin occasionally dropped with the wax, and the embers heated it red as it lay.\n\nVIII. Rain, Darkness, and Anxious Wanderers\n\nWhile the effigy of Eustacia was melting to nothing, and the fair woman herself was standing on Rainbarrow, her soul in an abyss of desolation seldom plumbed by one so young, Yeobright sat lonely at Blooms-End. He had fulfilled his word to Thomasin by sending off Fairway with the letter to his wife, and now waited with increased impatience for some sound or signal of her return. Were Eustacia still at Mistover the very least he expected was that she would send him back a reply tonight by the same hand; though, to leave all to her inclination, he had cautioned Fairway not to ask for an answer. If one were handed to him he was to bring it immediately; if not, he was to go straight home without troubling to come round to Blooms-End again that night.\n\nBut secretly Clym had a more pleasing hope. Eustacia might possibly decline to use her pen—it was rather her way to work silently—and surprise him by appearing at his door. How fully her mind was made up to do otherwise he did not know.\n\nTo Clym’s regret it began to rain and blow hard as the evening advanced. The wind rasped and scraped at the corners of the house, and filliped the eavesdroppings like peas against the panes. He walked restlessly about the untenanted rooms, stopping strange noises in windows and doors by jamming splinters of wood into the casements and crevices, and pressing together the leadwork of the quarries where it had become loosened from the glass. It was one of those nights when cracks in the walls of old churches widen, when ancient stains on the ceilings of decayed manor houses are renewed and enlarged from the size of a man’s hand to an area of many feet. The little gate in the palings before his dwelling continually opened and clicked together again, but when he looked out eagerly nobody was there; it was as if invisible shapes of the dead were passing in on their way to visit him.\n\nBetween ten and eleven o’clock, finding that neither Fairway nor anybody else came to him, he retired to rest, and despite his anxieties soon fell asleep. His sleep, however, was not very sound, by reason of the expectancy he had given way to, and he was easily awakened by a knocking which began at the door about an hour after. Clym arose and looked out of the window. Rain was still falling heavily, the whole expanse of heath before him emitting a subdued hiss under the downpour. It was too dark to see anything at all.\n\n“Who’s there?” he cried.\n\nLight footsteps shifted their position in the porch, and he could just distinguish in a plaintive female voice the words, “O Clym, come down and let me in!”\n\nHe flushed hot with agitation. “Surely it is Eustacia!” he murmured. If so, she had indeed come to him unawares.\n\nHe hastily got a light, dressed himself, and went down. On his flinging open the door the rays of the candle fell upon a woman closely wrapped up, who at once came forward.\n\n“Thomasin!” he exclaimed in an indescribable tone of disappointment. “It is Thomasin, and on such a night as this! O, where is Eustacia?”\n\nThomasin it was, wet, frightened, and panting.\n\n“Eustacia? I don’t know, Clym; but I can think,” she said with much perturbation. “Let me come in and rest—I will explain this. There is a great trouble brewing—my husband and Eustacia!”\n\n“What, what?”\n\n“I think my husband is going to leave me or do something dreadful—I don’t know what—Clym, will you go and see? I have nobody to help me but you; Eustacia has not yet come home?”\n“No.”\n\nShe went on breathlessly: “Then they are going to run off together! He came indoors tonight about eight o’clock and said in an off-hand way, ‘Tamsie, I have just found that I must go a journey.’ ‘When?’ I said. ‘Tonight,’ he said. ‘Where?’ I asked him. ‘I cannot tell you at present,’ he said; ‘I shall be back again tomorrow.’ He then went and busied himself in looking up his things, and took no notice of me at all. I expected to see him start, but he did not, and then it came to be ten o’clock, when he said, ‘You had better go to bed.’ I didn’t know what to do, and I went to bed. I believe he thought I fell asleep, for half an hour after that he came up and unlocked the oak chest we keep money in when we have much in the house and took out a roll of something which I believe was banknotes, though I was not aware that he had ’em there. These he must have got from the bank when he went there the other day. What does he want banknotes for, if he is only going off for a day? When he had gone down I thought of Eustacia, and how he had met her the night before—I know he did meet her, Clym, for I followed him part of the way; but I did not like to tell you when you called, and so make you think ill of him, as I did not think it was so serious. Then I could not stay in bed; I got up and dressed myself, and when I heard him out in the stable I thought I would come and tell you. So I came downstairs without any noise and slipped out.”\n\n“Then he was not absolutely gone when you left?”\n\n“No. Will you, dear Cousin Clym, go and try to persuade him not to go? He takes no notice of what I say, and puts me off with the story of his going on a journey, and will be home tomorrow, and all that; but I don’t believe it. I think you could influence him.”\n\n“I’ll go,” said Clym. “O, Eustacia!”\n\nThomasin carried in her arms a large bundle; and having by this time seated herself she began to unroll it, when a baby appeared as the kernel to the husks—dry, warm, and unconscious of travel or rough weather. Thomasin briefly kissed the baby, and then found time to begin crying as she said, “I brought baby, for I was afraid what might happen to her. I suppose it will be her death, but I couldn’t leave her with Rachel!”\n\nClym hastily put together the logs on the hearth, raked abroad the embers, which were scarcely yet extinct, and blew up a flame with the bellows.\n\n“Dry yourself,” he said. “I’ll go and get some more wood.”\n\n“No, no—don’t stay for that. I’ll make up the fire. Will you go at once—please will you?”\n\nYeobright ran upstairs to finish dressing himself. While he was gone another rapping came to the door. This time there was no delusion that it might be Eustacia’s—the footsteps just preceding it had been heavy and slow. Yeobright thinking it might possibly be Fairway with a note in answer, descended again and opened the door.\n\n“Captain Vye?” he said to a dripping figure.\n\n“Is my granddaughter here?” said the captain.\n\n“No.”\n“Then where is she?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“But you ought to know—you are her husband.”\n\n“Only in name apparently,” said Clym with rising excitement. “I believe she means to elope tonight with Wildeve. I am just going to look to it.”\n\n“Well, she has left my house; she left about half an hour ago. Who’s sitting there?”\n\n“My cousin Thomasin.”\n\nThe captain bowed in a preoccupied way to her. “I only hope it is no worse than an elopement,” he said.\n\n“Worse? What’s worse than the worst a wife can do?”\n\n“Well, I have been told a strange tale. Before starting in search of her I called up Charley, my stable lad. I missed my pistols the other day.”\n\n“Pistols?”\n\n“He said at the time that he took them down to clean. He has now owned that he took them because he saw Eustacia looking curiously at them; and she afterwards owned to him that she was thinking of taking her life, but bound him to secrecy, and promised never to think of such a thing again. I hardly suppose she will ever have bravado enough to use one of them; but it shows what has been lurking in her mind; and people who think of that sort of thing once think of it again.”\n\n“Where are the pistols?”\n\n“Safely locked up. O no, she won’t touch them again. But there are more ways of letting out life than through a bullet-hole. What did you quarrel about so bitterly with her to drive her to all this? You must have treated her badly indeed. Well, I was always against the marriage, and I was right.”\n\n“Are you going with me?” said Yeobright, paying no attention to the captain’s latter remark. “If so I can tell you what we quarrelled about as we walk along.”\n\n“Where to?”\n\n“To Wildeve’s—that was her destination, depend upon it.”\n\nThomasin here broke in, still weeping: “He said he was only going on a sudden short journey; but if so why did he want so much money? O, Clym, what do you think will happen? I am afraid that you, my poor baby, will soon have no father left to you!”\n\n“I am off now,” said Yeobright, stepping into the porch.\n\n“I would fain go with ’ee,” said the old man doubtfully. “But I begin to be afraid that my legs will hardly carry me there such a night as this. I am not so young as I was. If they are interrupted in their flight she will be sure to come back to me, and I ought to be at the house to receive her. But be it as ’twill I can’t walk to the Quiet Woman, and that’s an end on’t. I’ll go straight home.”\n\n“It will perhaps be best,” said Clym. “Thomasin, dry yourself, and be as comfortable as you can.”\n\nWith this he closed the door upon her, and left the house in company with Captain Vye, who parted from him outside the gate, taking the middle path, which led to Mistover. Clym crossed by the right-hand track towards the inn.\n\nThomasin, being left alone, took off some of her wet garments, carried the baby upstairs to Clym’s bed, and then came down to the sitting-room again, where she made a larger fire, and began drying herself. The fire soon flared up the chimney, giving the room an appearance of comfort that was doubled by contrast with the drumming of the storm without, which snapped at the windowpanes and breathed into the chimney strange low utterances that seemed to be the prologue to some tragedy.\n\nBut the least part of Thomasin was in the house, for her heart being at ease about the little girl upstairs she was mentally following Clym on his journey. Having indulged in this imaginary peregrination for some considerable interval, she became impressed with a sense of the intolerable slowness of time. But she sat on. The moment then came when she could scarcely sit longer, and it was like a satire on her patience to remember that Clym could hardly have reached the inn as yet. At last she went to the baby’s bedside. The child was sleeping soundly; but her imagination of possibly disastrous events at her home, the predominance within her of the unseen over the seen, agitated her beyond endurance. She could not refrain from going down and opening the door. The rain still continued, the candlelight falling upon the nearest drops and making glistening darts of them as they descended across the throng of invisible ones behind. To plunge into that medium was to plunge into water slightly diluted with air. But the difficulty of returning to her house at this moment made her all the more desirous of doing so—anything was better than suspense. “I have come here well enough,” she said, “and why shouldn’t I go back again? It is a mistake for me to be away.”\n\nShe hastily fetched the infant, wrapped it up, cloaked herself as before, and shoveling the ashes over the fire, to prevent accidents, went into the open air. Pausing first to put the door key in its old place behind the shutter, she resolutely turned her face to the confronting pile of firmamental darkness beyond the palings, and stepped into its midst. But Thomasin’s imagination being so actively engaged elsewhere, the night and the weather had for her no terror beyond that of their actual discomfort and difficulty.\n\nShe was soon ascending Blooms-End valley and traversing the undulations on the side of the hill. The noise of the wind over the heath was shrill, and as if it whistled for joy at finding a night so congenial as this. Sometimes the path led her to hollows between thickets of tall and dripping bracken, dead, though not yet prostrate, which enclosed her like a pool. When they were more than usually tall she lifted the baby to the top of her head, that it might be out of the reach of their drenching fronds. On higher ground, where the wind was brisk and sustained, the rain flew in a level flight without sensible descent, so that it was beyond all power to imagine the remoteness of the point at which it left the bosoms of the clouds. Here self-defence was impossible, and individual drops stuck into her like the arrows into Saint Sebastian. She was enabled to avoid puddles by the nebulous paleness which signified their presence, though beside anything less dark than the heath they themselves would have appeared as blackness.\n\nYet in spite of all this Thomasin was not sorry that she had started. To her there were not, as to Eustacia, demons in the air, and malice in every bush and bough. The drops which lashed her face were not scorpions, but prosy rain; Egdon in the mass was no monster whatever, but impersonal open ground. Her fears of the place were rational, her dislikes of its worst moods reasonable. At this time it was in her view a windy, wet place, in which a person might experience much discomfort, lose the path without care, and possibly catch cold.\n\nIf the path is well known the difficulty at such times of keeping therein is not altogether great, from its familiar feel to the feet; but once lost it is irrecoverable. Owing to her baby, who somewhat impeded Thomasin’s view forward and distracted her mind, she did at last lose the track. This mishap occurred when she was descending an open slope about two-thirds home. Instead of attempting, by wandering hither and thither, the hopeless task of finding such a mere thread, she went straight on, trusting for guidance to her general knowledge of the contours, which was scarcely surpassed by Clym’s or by that of the heath-croppers themselves.\n\nAt length Thomasin reached a hollow and began to discern through the rain a faint blotted radiance, which presently assumed the oblong form of an open door. She knew that no house stood hereabouts, and was soon aware of the nature of the door by its height above the ground.\n\n“Why, it is Diggory Venn’s van, surely!” she said.\n\nA certain secluded spot near Rainbarrow was, she knew, often Venn’s chosen centre when staying in this neighbourhood; and she guessed at once that she had stumbled upon this mysterious retreat. The question arose in her mind whether or not she should ask him to guide her into the path. In her anxiety to reach home she decided that she would appeal to him, notwithstanding the strangeness of appearing before his eyes at this place and season. But when, in pursuance of this resolve, Thomasin reached the van and looked in she found it to be untenanted; though there was no doubt that it was the reddleman’s. The fire was burning in the stove, the lantern hung from the nail. Round the doorway the floor was merely sprinkled with rain, and not saturated, which told her that the door had not long been opened.\n\nWhile she stood uncertainly looking in Thomasin heard a footstep advancing from the darkness behind her, and turning, beheld the well-known form in corduroy, lurid from head to foot, the lantern beams falling upon him through an intervening gauze of raindrops.\n\n“I thought you went down the slope,” he said, without noticing her face. “How do you come back here again?”\n\n“Diggory?” said Thomasin faintly.\n\n“Who are you?” said Venn, still unperceiving. “And why were you crying so just now?”\n\n“O, Diggory! don’t you know me?” said she. “But of course you don’t, wrapped up like this. What do you mean? I have not been crying here, and I have not been here before.”\n\nVenn then came nearer till he could see the illuminated side of her form.\n\n“Mrs. Wildeve!” he exclaimed, starting. “What a time for us to meet! And the baby too! What dreadful thing can have brought you out on such a night as this?”\n\nShe could not immediately answer; and without asking her permission he hopped into his van, took her by the arm, and drew her up after him.\n\n“What is it?” he continued when they stood within.\n\n“I have lost my way coming from Blooms-End, and I am in a great hurry to get home. Please show me as quickly as you can! It is so silly of me not to know Egdon better, and I cannot think how I came to lose the path. Show me quickly, Diggory, please.”\n\n“Yes, of course. I will go with ’ee. But you came to me before this, Mrs. Wildeve?”\n\n“I only came this minute.”\n\n“That’s strange. I was lying down here asleep about five minutes ago, with the door shut to keep out the weather, when the brushing of a woman’s clothes over the heath-bushes just outside woke me up, for I don’t sleep heavy, and at the same time I heard a sobbing or crying from the same woman. I opened my door and held out my lantern, and just as far as the light would reach I saw a woman; she turned her head when the light sheened on her, and then hurried on downhill. I hung up the lantern, and was curious enough to pull on my things and dog her a few steps, but I could see nothing of her any more. That was where I had been when you came up; and when I saw you I thought you were the same one.”\n\n“Perhaps it was one of the heathfolk going home?”\n\n“No, it couldn’t be. ’Tis too late. The noise of her gown over the he’th was of a whistling sort that nothing but silk will make.”\n\n“It wasn’t I, then. My dress is not silk, you see.... Are we anywhere in a line between Mistover and the inn?”\n\n“Well, yes; not far out.”\n\n“Ah, I wonder if it was she! Diggory, I must go at once!”\n\nShe jumped down from the van before he was aware, when Venn unhooked the lantern and leaped down after her. “I’ll take the baby, ma’am,” he said. “You must be tired out by the weight.”\n\nThomasin hesitated a moment, and then delivered the baby into Venn’s hands. “Don’t squeeze her, Diggory,” she said, “or hurt her little arm; and keep the cloak close over her like this, so that the rain may not drop in her face.”\n\n“I will,” said Venn earnestly. “As if I could hurt anything belonging to you!”\n\n“I only meant accidentally,” said Thomasin.\n\n“The baby is dry enough, but you are pretty wet,” said the reddleman when, in closing the door of his cart to padlock it, he noticed on the floor a ring of water drops where her cloak had hung from her.\n\nThomasin followed him as he wound right and left to avoid the larger bushes, stopping occasionally and covering the lantern, while he looked over his shoulder to gain some idea of the position of Rainbarrow above them, which it was necessary to keep directly behind their backs to preserve a proper course.\n\n“You are sure the rain does not fall upon baby?”\n\n“Quite sure. May I ask how old he is, ma’am?”\n\n“He!” said Thomasin reproachfully. “Anybody can see better than that in a moment. She is nearly two months old. How far is it now to the inn?”\n\n“A little over a quarter of a mile.”\n\n“Will you walk a little faster?”\n\n“I was afraid you could not keep up.”\n\n“I am very anxious to get there. Ah, there is a light from the window!”\n\n“’Tis not from the window. That’s a gig-lamp, to the best of my belief.”\n\n“O!” said Thomasin in despair. “I wish I had been there sooner—give me the baby, Diggory—you can go back now.”\n\n“I must go all the way,” said Venn. “There is a quag between us and that light, and you will walk into it up to your neck unless I take you round.”\n\n“But the light is at the inn, and there is no quag in front of that.”\n\n“No, the light is below the inn some two or three hundred yards.”\n\n“Never mind,” said Thomasin hurriedly. “Go towards the light, and not towards the inn.”\n\n“Yes,” answered Venn, swerving round in obedience; and, after a pause, “I wish you would tell me what this great trouble is. I think you have proved that I can be trusted.”\n\n“There are some things that cannot be—cannot be told to—” And then her heart rose into her throat, and she could say no more.\n\nIX. Sights and Sounds Draw the Wanderers Together\n\nHaving seen Eustacia’s signal from the hill at eight o’clock, Wildeve immediately prepared to assist her in her flight, and, as he hoped, accompany her. He was somewhat perturbed, and his manner of informing Thomasin that he was going on a journey was in itself sufficient to rouse her suspicions. When she had gone to bed he collected the few articles he would require, and went upstairs to the money-chest, whence he took a tolerably bountiful sum in notes, which had been advanced to him on the property he was so soon to have in possession, to defray expenses incidental to the removal.\n\nHe then went to the stable and coach-house to assure himself that the horse, gig, and harness were in a fit condition for a long drive. Nearly half an hour was spent thus, and on returning to the house Wildeve had no thought of Thomasin being anywhere but in bed. He had told the stable lad not to stay up, leading the boy to understand that his departure would be at three or four in the morning; for this, though an exceptional hour, was less strange than midnight, the time actually agreed on, the packet from Budmouth sailing between one and two.\n\nAt last all was quiet, and he had nothing to do but to wait. By no effort could he shake off the oppression of spirits which he had experienced ever since his last meeting with Eustacia, but he hoped there was that in his situation which money could cure. He had persuaded himself that to act not ungenerously towards his gentle wife by settling on her the half of his property, and with chivalrous devotion towards another and greater woman by sharing her fate, was possible. And though he meant to adhere to Eustacia’s instructions to the letter, to deposit her where she wished and to leave her, should that be her will, the spell that she had cast over him intensified, and his heart was beating fast in the anticipated futility of such commands in the face of a mutual wish that they should throw in their lot together.\n\nHe would not allow himself to dwell long upon these conjectures, maxims, and hopes, and at twenty minutes to twelve he again went softly to the stable, harnessed the horse, and lit the lamps; whence, taking the horse by the head, he led him with the covered car out of the yard to a spot by the roadside some quarter of a mile below the inn.\n\nHere Wildeve waited, slightly sheltered from the driving rain by a high bank that had been cast up at this place. Along the surface of the road where lit by the lamps the loosened gravel and small stones scudded and clicked together before the wind, which, leaving them in heaps, plunged into the heath and boomed across the bushes into darkness. Only one sound rose above this din of weather, and that was the roaring of a ten-hatch weir to the southward, from a river in the meads which formed the boundary of the heath in this direction.\n\nHe lingered on in perfect stillness till he began to fancy that the midnight hour must have struck. A very strong doubt had arisen in his mind if Eustacia would venture down the hill in such weather; yet knowing her nature he felt that she might. “Poor thing! ’tis like her ill-luck,” he murmured.\n\nAt length he turned to the lamp and looked at his watch. To his surprise it was nearly a quarter past midnight. He now wished that he had driven up the circuitous road to Mistover, a plan not adopted because of the enormous length of the route in proportion to that of the pedestrian’s path down the open hillside, and the consequent increase of labour for the horse.\n\nAt this moment a footstep approached; but the light of the lamps being in a different direction the comer was not visible. The step paused, then came on again.\n\n“Eustacia?” said Wildeve.\n\nThe person came forward, and the light fell upon the form of Clym, glistening with wet, whom Wildeve immediately recognized; but Wildeve, who stood behind the lamp, was not at once recognized by Yeobright.\n\nHe stopped as if in doubt whether this waiting vehicle could have anything to do with the flight of his wife or not. The sight of Yeobright at once banished Wildeve’s sober feelings, who saw him again as the deadly rival from whom Eustacia was to be kept at all hazards. Hence Wildeve did not speak, in the hope that Clym would pass by without particular inquiry.\n\nWhile they both hung thus in hesitation a dull sound became audible above the storm and wind. Its origin was unmistakable—it was the fall of a body into the stream in the adjoining mead, apparently at a point near the weir.\n\nBoth started. “Good God! can it be she?” said Clym.\n\n“Why should it be she?” said Wildeve, in his alarm forgetting that he had hitherto screened himself.\n\n“Ah!—that’s you, you traitor, is it?” cried Yeobright. “Why should it be she? Because last week she would have put an end to her life if she had been able. She ought to have been watched! Take one of the lamps and come with me.”\n\nYeobright seized the one on his side and hastened on; Wildeve did not wait to unfasten the other, but followed at once along the meadow track to the weir, a little in the rear of Clym.\n\nShadwater Weir had at its foot a large circular pool, fifty feet in diameter, into which the water flowed through ten huge hatches, raised and lowered by a winch and cogs in the ordinary manner. The sides of the pool were of masonry, to prevent the water from washing away the bank; but the force of the stream in winter was sometimes such as to undermine the retaining wall and precipitate it into the hole. Clym reached the hatches, the framework of which was shaken to its foundations by the velocity of the current. Nothing but the froth of the waves could be discerned in the pool below. He got upon the plank bridge over the race, and holding to the rail, that the wind might not blow him off, crossed to the other side of the river. There he leant over the wall and lowered the lamp, only to behold the vortex formed at the curl of the returning current.\n\nWildeve meanwhile had arrived on the former side, and the light from Yeobright’s lamp shed a flecked and agitated radiance across the weir pool, revealing to the ex-engineer the tumbling courses of the currents from the hatches above. Across this gashed and puckered mirror a dark body was slowly borne by one of the backward currents.\n\n“O, my darling!” exclaimed Wildeve in an agonized voice; and, without showing sufficient presence of mind even to throw off his greatcoat, he leaped into the boiling caldron.\n\nYeobright could now also discern the floating body, though but indistinctly; and imagining from Wildeve’s plunge that there was life to be saved he was about to leap after. Bethinking himself of a wiser plan, he placed the lamp against a post to make it stand upright, and running round to the lower part of the pool, where there was no wall, he sprang in and boldly waded upwards towards the deeper portion. Here he was taken off his legs, and in swimming was carried round into the centre of the basin, where he perceived Wildeve struggling.\n\nWhile these hasty actions were in progress here, Venn and Thomasin had been toiling through the lower corner of the heath in the direction of the light. They had not been near enough to the river to hear the plunge, but they saw the removal of the carriage lamp, and watched its motion into the mead. As soon as they reached the car and horse Venn guessed that something new was amiss, and hastened to follow in the course of the moving light. Venn walked faster than Thomasin, and came to the weir alone.\n\nThe lamp placed against the post by Clym still shone across the water, and the reddleman observed something floating motionless. Being encumbered with the infant, he ran back to meet Thomasin.\n\n“Take the baby, please, Mrs. Wildeve,” he said hastily. “Run home with her, call the stable lad, and make him send down to me any men who may be living near. Somebody has fallen into the weir.”\n\nThomasin took the child and ran. When she came to the covered car the horse, though fresh from the stable, was standing perfectly still, as if conscious of misfortune. She saw for the first time whose it was. She nearly fainted, and would have been unable to proceed another step but that the necessity of preserving the little girl from harm nerved her to an amazing self-control. In this agony of suspense she entered the house, put the baby in a place of safety, woke the lad and the female domestic, and ran out to give the alarm at the nearest cottage.\n\nDiggory, having returned to the brink of the pool, observed that the small upper hatches or floats were withdrawn. He found one of these lying upon the grass, and taking it under one arm, and with his lantern in his hand, entered at the bottom of the pool as Clym had done. As soon as he began to be in deep water he flung himself across the hatch; thus supported he was able to keep afloat as long as he chose, holding the lantern aloft with his disengaged hand. Propelled by his feet, he steered round and round the pool, ascending each time by one of the back streams and descending in the middle of the current.\n\nAt first he could see nothing. Then amidst the glistening of the whirlpools and the white clots of foam he distinguished a woman’s bonnet floating alone. His search was now under the left wall, when something came to the surface almost close beside him. It was not, as he had expected, a woman, but a man. The reddleman put the ring of the lantern between his teeth, seized the floating man by the collar, and, holding on to the hatch with his remaining arm, struck out into the strongest race, by which the unconscious man, the hatch, and himself were carried down the stream. As soon as Venn found his feet dragging over the pebbles of the shallower part below he secured his footing and waded towards the brink. There, where the water stood at about the height of his waist, he flung away the hatch, and attempted to drag forth the man. This was a matter of great difficulty, and he found as the reason that the legs of the unfortunate stranger were tightly embraced by the arms of another man, who had hitherto been entirely beneath the surface.\n\nAt this moment his heart bounded to hear footsteps running towards him, and two men, roused by Thomasin, appeared at the brink above. They ran to where Venn was, and helped him in lifting out the apparently drowned persons, separating them, and laying them out upon the grass. Venn turned the light upon their faces. The one who had been uppermost was Yeobright; he who had been completely submerged was Wildeve.\n\n“Now we must search the hole again,” said Venn. “A woman is in there somewhere. Get a pole.”\n\nOne of the men went to the footbridge and tore off the handrail. The reddleman and the two others then entered the water together from below as before, and with their united force probed the pool forwards to where it sloped down to its central depth. Venn was not mistaken in supposing that any person who had sunk for the last time would be washed down to this point, for when they had examined to about halfway across something impeded their thrust.\n\n“Pull it forward,” said Venn, and they raked it in with the pole till it was close to their feet.\n\nVenn vanished under the stream, and came up with an armful of wet drapery enclosing a woman’s cold form, which was all that remained of the desperate Eustacia.\n\nWhen they reached the bank there stood Thomasin, in a stress of grief, bending over the two unconscious ones who already lay there. The horse and cart were brought to the nearest point in the road, and it was the work of a few minutes only to place the three in the vehicle. Venn led on the horse, supporting Thomasin upon his arm, and the two men followed, till they reached the inn.\n\nThe woman who had been shaken out of her sleep by Thomasin had hastily dressed herself and lighted a fire, the other servant being left to snore on in peace at the back of the house. The insensible forms of Eustacia, Clym, and Wildeve were then brought in and laid on the carpet, with their feet to the fire, when such restorative processes as could be thought of were adopted at once, the stableman being in the meantime sent for a doctor. But there seemed to be not a whiff of life in either of the bodies. Then Thomasin, whose stupor of grief had been thrust off awhile by frantic action, applied a bottle of hartshorn to Clym’s nostrils, having tried it in vain upon the other two. He sighed.\n\n“Clym’s alive!” she exclaimed.\n\nHe soon breathed distinctly, and again and again did she attempt to revive her husband by the same means; but Wildeve gave no sign. There was too much reason to think that he and Eustacia both were for ever beyond the reach of stimulating perfumes. Their exertions did not relax till the doctor arrived, when one by one, the senseless three were taken upstairs and put into warm beds.\n\nVenn soon felt himself relieved from further attendance, and went to the door, scarcely able yet to realize the strange catastrophe that had befallen the family in which he took so great an interest. Thomasin surely would be broken down by the sudden and overwhelming nature of this event. No firm and sensible Mrs. Yeobright lived now to support the gentle girl through the ordeal; and, whatever an unimpassioned spectator might think of her loss of such a husband as Wildeve, there could be no doubt that for the moment she was distracted and horrified by the blow. As for himself, not being privileged to go to her and comfort her, he saw no reason for waiting longer in a house where he remained only as a stranger.\n\nHe returned across the heath to his van. The fire was not yet out, and everything remained as he had left it. Venn now bethought himself of his clothes, which were saturated with water to the weight of lead. He changed them, spread them before the fire, and lay down to sleep. But it was more than he could do to rest here while excited by a vivid imagination of the turmoil they were in at the house he had quitted, and, blaming himself for coming away, he dressed in another suit, locked up the door, and again hastened across to the inn. Rain was still falling heavily when he entered the kitchen. A bright fire was shining from the hearth, and two women were bustling about, one of whom was Olly Dowden.\n\n“Well, how is it going on now?” said Venn in a whisper.\n\n“Mr. Yeobright is better; but Mrs. Yeobright and Mr. Wildeve are dead and cold. The doctor says they were quite gone before they were out of the water.”\n\n“Ah! I thought as much when I hauled ’em up. And Mrs. Wildeve?”\n\n“She is as well as can be expected. The doctor had her put between blankets, for she was almost as wet as they that had been in the river, poor young thing. You don’t seem very dry, reddleman.”\n\n“Oh, ’tis not much. I have changed my things. This is only a little dampness I’ve got coming through the rain again.”\n\n“Stand by the fire. Mis’ess says you be to have whatever you want, and she was sorry when she was told that you’d gone away.”\n\nVenn drew near to the fireplace, and looked into the flames in an absent mood. The steam came from his leggings and ascended the chimney with the smoke, while he thought of those who were upstairs. Two were corpses, one had barely escaped the jaws of death, another was sick and a widow. The last occasion on which he had lingered by that fireplace was when the raffle was in progress; when Wildeve was alive and well; Thomasin active and smiling in the next room; Yeobright and Eustacia just made husband and wife, and Mrs. Yeobright living at Blooms-End. It had seemed at that time that the then position of affairs was good for at least twenty years to come. Yet, of all the circle, he himself was the only one whose situation had not materially changed.\n\nWhile he ruminated a footstep descended the stairs. It was the nurse, who brought in her hand a rolled mass of wet paper. The woman was so engrossed with her occupation that she hardly saw Venn. She took from a cupboard some pieces of twine, which she strained across the fireplace, tying the end of each piece to the firedog, previously pulled forward for the purpose, and, unrolling the wet papers, she began pinning them one by one to the strings in a manner of clothes on a line.\n\n“What be they?” said Venn.\n\n“Poor master’s banknotes,” she answered. “They were found in his pocket when they undressed him.”\n\n“Then he was not coming back again for some time?” said Venn.\n\n“That we shall never know,” said she.\n\nVenn was loth to depart, for all on earth that interested him lay under this roof. As nobody in the house had any more sleep that night, except the two who slept for ever, there was no reason why he should not remain. So he retired into the niche of the fireplace where he had used to sit, and there he continued, watching the steam from the double row of banknotes as they waved backwards and forwards in the draught of the chimney till their flaccidity was changed to dry crispness throughout. Then the woman came and unpinned them, and, folding them together, carried the handful upstairs. Presently the doctor appeared from above with the look of a man who could do no more, and, pulling on his gloves, went out of the house, the trotting of his horse soon dying away upon the road.\n\nAt four o’clock there was a gentle knock at the door. It was from Charley, who had been sent by Captain Vye to inquire if anything had been heard of Eustacia. The girl who admitted him looked in his face as if she did not know what answer to return, and showed him in to where Venn was seated, saying to the reddleman, “Will you tell him, please?”\n\nVenn told. Charley’s only utterance was a feeble, indistinct sound. He stood quite still; then he burst out spasmodically, “I shall see her once more?”\n\n“I dare say you may see her,” said Diggory gravely. “But hadn’t you better run and tell Captain Vye?”\n\n“Yes, yes. Only I do hope I shall see her just once again.”\n\n“You shall,” said a low voice behind; and starting round they beheld by the dim light, a thin, pallid, almost spectral form, wrapped in a blanket, and looking like Lazarus coming from the tomb.\n\nIt was Yeobright. Neither Venn nor Charley spoke, and Clym continued, “You shall see her. There will be time enough to tell the captain when it gets daylight. You would like to see her too—would you not, Diggory? She looks very beautiful now.”\n\nVenn assented by rising to his feet, and with Charley he followed Clym to the foot of the staircase, where he took off his boots; Charley did the same. They followed Yeobright upstairs to the landing, where there was a candle burning, which Yeobright took in his hand, and with it led the way into an adjoining room. Here he went to the bedside and folded back the sheet.\n\nThey stood silently looking upon Eustacia, who, as she lay there still in death, eclipsed all her living phases. Pallor did not include all the quality of her complexion, which seemed more than whiteness; it was almost light. The expression of her finely carved mouth was pleasant, as if a sense of dignity had just compelled her to leave off speaking. Eternal rigidity had seized upon it in a momentary transition between fervour and resignation. Her black hair was looser now than either of them had ever seen it before, and surrounded her brow like a forest. The stateliness of look which had been almost too marked for a dweller in a country domicile had at last found an artistically happy background.\n\nNobody spoke, till at length Clym covered her and turned aside. “Now come here,” he said.\n\nThey went to a recess in the same room, and there, on a smaller bed, lay another figure—Wildeve. Less repose was visible in his face than in Eustacia’s, but the same luminous youthfulness overspread it, and the least sympathetic observer would have felt at sight of him now that he was born for a higher destiny than this. The only sign upon him of his recent struggle for life was in his fingertips, which were worn and sacrificed in his dying endeavours to obtain a hold on the face of the weir-wall.\n\nYeobright’s manner had been so quiet, he had uttered so few syllables since his reappearance, that Venn imagined him resigned. It was only when they had left the room and stood upon the landing that the true state of his mind was apparent. Here he said, with a wild smile, inclining his head towards the chamber in which Eustacia lay, “She is the second woman I have killed this year. I was a great cause of my mother’s death, and I am the chief cause of hers.”\n\n“How?” said Venn.\n\n“I spoke cruel words to her, and she left my house. I did not invite her back till it was too late. It is I who ought to have drowned myself. It would have been a charity to the living had the river overwhelmed me and borne her up. But I cannot die. Those who ought to have lived lie dead; and here am I alive!”\n\n“But you can’t charge yourself with crimes in that way,” said Venn. “You may as well say that the parents be the cause of a murder by the child, for without the parents the child would never have been begot.”\n\n“Yes, Venn, that is very true; but you don’t know all the circumstances. If it had pleased God to put an end to me it would have been a good thing for all. But I am getting used to the horror of my existence. They say that a time comes when men laugh at misery through long acquaintance with it. Surely that time will soon come to me!”\n\n“Your aim has always been good,” said Venn. “Why should you say such desperate things?”\n\n“No, they are not desperate. They are only hopeless; and my great regret is that for what I have done no man or law can punish me!”\nI. The Inevitable Movement Onward\n\nThe story of the deaths of Eustacia and Wildeve was told throughout Egdon, and far beyond, for many weeks and months. All the known incidents of their love were enlarged, distorted, touched up, and modified, till the original reality bore but a slight resemblance to the counterfeit presentation by surrounding tongues. Yet, upon the whole, neither the man nor the woman lost dignity by sudden death. Misfortune had struck them gracefully, cutting off their erratic histories with a catastrophic dash, instead of, as with many, attenuating each life to an uninteresting meagreness, through long years of wrinkles, neglect, and decay.\n\nOn those most nearly concerned the effect was somewhat different. Strangers who had heard of many such cases now merely heard of one more; but immediately where a blow falls no previous imaginings amount to appreciable preparation for it. The very suddenness of her bereavement dulled, to some extent, Thomasin’s feelings; yet irrationally enough, a consciousness that the husband she had lost ought to have been a better man did not lessen her mourning at all. On the contrary, this fact seemed at first to set off the dead husband in his young wife’s eyes, and to be the necessary cloud to the rainbow.\nBut the horrors of the unknown had passed. Vague misgivings about her future as a deserted wife were at an end. The worst had once been matter of trembling conjecture; it was now matter of reason only, a limited badness. Her chief interest, the little Eustacia, still remained. There was humility in her grief, no defiance in her attitude; and when this is the case a shaken spirit is apt to be stilled.\n\nCould Thomasin’s mournfulness now and Eustacia’s serenity during life have been reduced to common measure, they would have touched the same mark nearly. But Thomasin’s former brightness made shadow of that which in a sombre atmosphere was light itself.\n\nThe spring came and calmed her; the summer came and soothed her; the autumn arrived, and she began to be comforted, for her little girl was strong and happy, growing in size and knowledge every day. Outward events flattered Thomasin not a little. Wildeve had died intestate, and she and the child were his only relatives. When administration had been granted, all the debts paid, and the residue of her husband’s uncle’s property had come into her hands, it was found that the sum waiting to be invested for her own and the child’s benefit was little less than ten thousand pounds.\n\nWhere should she live? The obvious place was Blooms-End. The old rooms, it is true, were not much higher than the between-decks of a frigate, necessitating a sinking in the floor under the new clock-case she brought from the inn, and the removal of the handsome brass knobs on its head, before there was height for it to stand; but, such as the rooms were, there were plenty of them, and the place was endeared to her by every early recollection. Clym very gladly admitted her as a tenant, confining his own existence to two rooms at the top of the back staircase, where he lived on quietly, shut off from Thomasin and the three servants she had thought fit to indulge in now that she was a mistress of money, going his own ways, and thinking his own thoughts.\n\nHis sorrows had made some change in his outward appearance; and yet the alteration was chiefly within. It might have been said that he had a wrinkled mind. He had no enemies, and he could get nobody to reproach him, which was why he so bitterly reproached himself.\n\nHe did sometimes think he had been ill-used by fortune, so far as to say that to be born is a palpable dilemma, and that instead of men aiming to advance in life with glory they should calculate how to retreat out of it without shame. But that he and his had been sarcastically and pitilessly handled in having such irons thrust into their souls he did not maintain long. It is usually so, except with the sternest of men. Human beings, in their generous endeavour to construct a hypothesis that shall not degrade a First Cause, have always hesitated to conceive a dominant power of lower moral quality than their own; and, even while they sit down and weep by the waters of Babylon, invent excuses for the oppression which prompts their tears.\nThus, though words of solace were vainly uttered in his presence, he found relief in a direction of his own choosing when left to himself. For a man of his habits the house and the hundred and twenty pounds a year which he had inherited from his mother were enough to supply all worldly needs. Resources do not depend upon gross amounts, but upon the proportion of spendings to takings.\n\nHe frequently walked the heath alone, when the past seized upon him with its shadowy hand, and held him there to listen to its tale. His imagination would then people the spot with its ancient inhabitants—forgotten Celtic tribes trod their tracks about him, and he could almost live among them, look in their faces, and see them standing beside the barrows which swelled around, untouched and perfect as at the time of their erection. Those of the dyed barbarians who had chosen the cultivable tracts were, in comparison with those who had left their marks here, as writers on paper beside writers on parchment. Their records had perished long ago by the plough, while the works of these remained. Yet they all had lived and died unconscious of the different fates awaiting their relics. It reminded him that unforeseen factors operate in the evolution of immortality.\n\nWinter again came round, with its winds, frosts, tame robins, and sparkling starlight. The year previous Thomasin had hardly been conscious of the season’s advance; this year she laid her heart open to external influences of every kind. The life of this sweet cousin, her baby, and her servants, came to Clym’s senses only in the form of sounds through a wood partition as he sat over books of exceptionally large type; but his ear became at last so accustomed to these slight noises from the other part of the house that he almost could witness the scenes they signified. A faint beat of half-seconds conjured up Thomasin rocking the cradle, a wavering hum meant that she was singing the baby to sleep, a crunching of sand as between millstones raised the picture of Humphrey’s, Fairway’s, or Sam’s heavy feet crossing the stone floor of the kitchen; a light boyish step, and a gay tune in a high key, betokened a visit from Grandfer Cantle; a sudden break-off in the Grandfer’s utterances implied the application to his lips of a mug of small beer, a bustling and slamming of doors meant starting to go to market; for Thomasin, in spite of her added scope of gentility, led a ludicrously narrow life, to the end that she might save every possible pound for her little daughter.\n\nOne summer day Clym was in the garden, immediately outside the parlour window, which was as usual open. He was looking at the pot-flowers on the sill; they had been revived and restored by Thomasin to the state in which his mother had left them. He heard a slight scream from Thomasin, who was sitting inside the room.\n\n“O, how you frightened me!” she said to someone who had entered. “I thought you were the ghost of yourself.”\n\nClym was curious enough to advance a little further and look in at the window. To his astonishment there stood within the room Diggory Venn, no longer a reddleman, but exhibiting the strangely altered hues of an ordinary Christian countenance, white shirt-front, light flowered waistcoat, blue-spotted neckerchief, and bottle-green coat. Nothing in this appearance was at all singular but the fact of its great difference from what he had formerly been. Red, and all approach to red, was carefully excluded from every article of clothes upon him; for what is there that persons just out of harness dread so much as reminders of the trade which has enriched them?\n\nYeobright went round to the door and entered.\n\n“I was so alarmed!” said Thomasin, smiling from one to the other. “I couldn’t believe that he had got white of his own accord! It seemed supernatural.”\n\n“I gave up dealing in reddle last Christmas,” said Venn. “It was a profitable trade, and I found that by that time I had made enough to take the dairy of fifty cows that my father had in his lifetime. I always thought of getting to that place again if I changed at all, and now I am there.”\n\n“How did you manage to become white, Diggory?” Thomasin asked.\n\n“I turned so by degrees, ma’am.”\n\n“You look much better than ever you did before.”\n\nVenn appeared confused; and Thomasin, seeing how inadvertently she had spoken to a man who might possibly have tender feelings for her still, blushed a little. Clym saw nothing of this, and added good-humouredly—\n\n“What shall we have to frighten Thomasin’s baby with, now you have become a human being again?”\n\n“Sit down, Diggory,” said Thomasin, “and stay to tea.”\n\nVenn moved as if he would retire to the kitchen, when Thomasin said with pleasant pertness as she went on with some sewing, “Of course you must sit down here. And where does your fifty-cow dairy lie, Mr. Venn?”\n\n“At Stickleford—about two miles to the right of Alderworth, ma’am, where the meads begin. I have thought that if Mr. Yeobright would like to pay me a visit sometimes he shouldn’t stay away for want of asking. I’ll not bide to tea this afternoon, thank’ee, for I’ve got something on hand that must be settled. ’Tis Maypole-day tomorrow, and the Shadwater folk have clubbed with a few of your neighbours here to have a pole just outside your palings in the heath, as it is a nice green place.” Venn waved his elbow towards the patch in front of the house. “I have been talking to Fairway about it,” he continued, “and I said to him that before we put up the pole it would be as well to ask Mrs. Wildeve.”\n\n“I can say nothing against it,” she answered. “Our property does not reach an inch further than the white palings.”\n\n“But you might not like to see a lot of folk going crazy round a stick, under your very nose?”\n\n“I shall have no objection at all.”\n\nVenn soon after went away, and in the evening Yeobright strolled as far as Fairway’s cottage. It was a lovely May sunset, and the birch trees which grew on this margin of the vast Egdon wilderness had put on their new leaves, delicate as butterflies’ wings, and diaphanous as amber. Beside Fairway’s dwelling was an open space recessed from the road, and here were now collected all the young people from within a radius of a couple of miles. The pole lay with one end supported on a trestle, and women were engaged in wreathing it from the top downwards with wild-flowers. The instincts of merry England lingered on here with exceptional vitality, and the symbolic customs which tradition has attached to each season of the year were yet a reality on Egdon. Indeed, the impulses of all such outlandish hamlets are pagan still—in these spots homage to nature, self-adoration, frantic gaieties, fragments of Teutonic rites to divinities whose names are forgotten, seem in some way or other to have survived mediæval doctrine.\n\nYeobright did not interrupt the preparations, and went home again. The next morning, when Thomasin withdrew the curtains of her bedroom window, there stood the Maypole in the middle of the green, its top cutting into the sky. It had sprung up in the night, or rather early morning, like Jack’s bean-stalk. She opened the casement to get a better view of the garlands and posies that adorned it. The sweet perfume of the flowers had already spread into the surrounding air, which, being free from every taint, conducted to her lips a full measure of the fragrance received from the spire of blossom in its midst. At the top of the pole were crossed hoops decked with small flowers; beneath these came a milk-white zone of Maybloom; then a zone of bluebells, then of cowslips, then of lilacs, then of ragged-robins, daffodils, and so on, till the lowest stage was reached. Thomasin noticed all these, and was delighted that the May revel was to be so near.\n\nWhen afternoon came people began to gather on the green, and Yeobright was interested enough to look out upon them from the open window of his room. Soon after this Thomasin walked out from the door immediately below and turned her eyes up to her cousin’s face. She was dressed more gaily than Yeobright had ever seen her dressed since the time of Wildeve’s death, eighteen months before; since the day of her marriage even she had not exhibited herself to such advantage.\n\n“How pretty you look today, Thomasin!” he said. “Is it because of the Maypole?”\n\n“Not altogether.” And then she blushed and dropped her eyes, which he did not specially observe, though her manner seemed to him to be rather peculiar, considering that she was only addressing himself. Could it be possible that she had put on her summer clothes to please him?\n\nHe recalled her conduct towards him throughout the last few weeks, when they had often been working together in the garden, just as they had formerly done when they were boy and girl under his mother’s eye. What if her interest in him were not so entirely that of a relative as it had formerly been? To Yeobright any possibility of this sort was a serious matter; and he almost felt troubled at the thought of it. Every pulse of loverlike feeling which had not been stilled during Eustacia’s lifetime had gone into the grave with her. His passion for her had occurred too far on in his manhood to leave fuel enough on hand for another fire of that sort, as may happen with more boyish loves. Even supposing him capable of loving again, that love would be a plant of slow and laboured growth, and in the end only small and sickly, like an autumn-hatched bird.\n\nHe was so distressed by this new complexity that when the enthusiastic brass band arrived and struck up, which it did about five o’clock, with apparently wind enough among its members to blow down his house, he withdrew from his rooms by the back door, went down the garden, through the gate in the hedge, and away out of sight. He could not bear to remain in the presence of enjoyment today, though he had tried hard.\nNothing was seen of him for four hours. When he came back by the same path it was dusk, and the dews were coating every green thing. The boisterous music had ceased; but, entering the premises as he did from behind, he could not see if the May party had all gone till he had passed through Thomasin’s division of the house to the front door. Thomasin was standing within the porch alone.\n\nShe looked at him reproachfully. “You went away just when it began, Clym,” she said.\n\n“Yes. I felt I could not join in. You went out with them, of course?”\n\n“No, I did not.”\n\n“You appeared to be dressed on purpose.”\n\n“Yes, but I could not go out alone; so many people were there. One is there now.”\n\nYeobright strained his eyes across the dark-green patch beyond the paling, and near the black form of the Maypole he discerned a shadowy figure, sauntering idly up and down. “Who is it?” he said.\n\n“Mr. Venn,” said Thomasin.\n\n“You might have asked him to come in, I think, Tamsie. He has been very kind to you first and last.”\n\n“I will now,” she said; and, acting on the impulse, went through the wicket to where Venn stood under the Maypole.\n\n“It is Mr. Venn, I think?” she inquired.\n\nVenn started as if he had not seen her—artful man that he was—and said, “Yes.”\n\n“Will you come in?”\n\n“I am afraid that I—”\n\n“I have seen you dancing this evening, and you had the very best of the girls for your partners. Is it that you won’t come in because you wish to stand here, and think over the past hours of enjoyment?”\n\n“Well, that’s partly it,” said Mr. Venn, with ostentatious sentiment. “But the main reason why I am biding here like this is that I want to wait till the moon rises.”\n\n“To see how pretty the Maypole looks in the moonlight?”\n\n“No. To look for a glove that was dropped by one of the maidens.”\n\nThomasin was speechless with surprise. That a man who had to walk some four or five miles to his home should wait here for such a reason pointed to only one conclusion—the man must be amazingly interested in that glove’s owner.\n\n“Were you dancing with her, Diggory?” she asked, in a voice which revealed that he had made himself considerably more interesting to her by this disclosure.\n\n“No,” he sighed.\n\n“And you will not come in, then?”\n\n“Not tonight, thank you, ma’am.”\n\n“Shall I lend you a lantern to look for the young person’s glove, Mr. Venn?”\n\n“O no; it is not necessary, Mrs. Wildeve, thank you. The moon will rise in a few minutes.”\n\nThomasin went back to the porch. “Is he coming in?” said Clym, who had been waiting where she had left him.\n\n“He would rather not tonight,” she said, and then passed by him into the house; whereupon Clym too retired to his own rooms.\n\nWhen Clym was gone Thomasin crept upstairs in the dark, and, just listening by the cot, to assure herself that the child was asleep, she went to the window, gently lifted the corner of the white curtain, and looked out. Venn was still there. She watched the growth of the faint radiance appearing in the sky by the eastern hill, till presently the edge of the moon burst upwards and flooded the valley with light. Diggory’s form was now distinct on the green; he was moving about in a bowed attitude, evidently scanning the grass for the precious missing article, walking in zigzags right and left till he should have passed over every foot of the ground.\n\n“How very ridiculous!” Thomasin murmured to herself, in a tone which was intended to be satirical. “To think that a man should be so silly as to go mooning about like that for a girl’s glove! A respectable dairyman, too, and a man of money as he is now. What a pity!”\n\nAt last Venn appeared to find it; whereupon he stood up and raised it to his lips. Then placing it in his breastpocket—the nearest receptacle to a man’s heart permitted by modern raiment—he ascended the valley in a mathematically direct line towards his distant home in the meadows.\nII. Thomasin Walks in a Green Place by the Roman Road\n\nClym saw little of Thomasin for several days after this; and when they met she was more silent than usual. At length he asked her what she was thinking of so intently.\n\n“I am thoroughly perplexed,” she said candidly. “I cannot for my life think who it is that Diggory Venn is so much in love with. None of the girls at the Maypole were good enough for him, and yet she must have been there.”\n\nClym tried to imagine Venn’s choice for a moment; but ceasing to be interested in the question he went on again with his gardening.\n\nNo clearing up of the mystery was granted her for some time. But one afternoon Thomasin was upstairs getting ready for a walk, when she had occasion to come to the landing and call “Rachel.” Rachel was a girl about thirteen, who carried the baby out for airings; and she came upstairs at the call.\n\n“Have you seen one of my last new gloves about the house, Rachel?” inquired Thomasin. “It is the fellow to this one.”\n\nRachel did not reply.\n\n“Why don’t you answer?” said her mistress.\n\n“I think it is lost, ma’am.”\n\n“Lost? Who lost it? I have never worn them but once.”\n\nRachel appeared as one dreadfully troubled, and at last began to cry. “Please, ma’am, on the day of the Maypole I had none to wear, and I seed yours on the table, and I thought I would borrow ’em. I did not mean to hurt ’em at all, but one of them got lost. Somebody gave me some money to buy another pair for you, but I have not been able to go anywhere to get ’em.”\n\n“Who’s somebody?”\n\n“Mr. Venn.”\n\n“Did he know it was my glove?”\n\n“Yes. I told him.”\n\nThomasin was so surprised by the explanation that she quite forgot to lecture the girl, who glided silently away. Thomasin did not move further than to turn her eyes upon the grass-plat where the Maypole had stood. She remained thinking, then said to herself that she would not go out that afternoon, but would work hard at the baby’s unfinished lovely plaid frock, cut on the cross in the newest fashion. How she managed to work hard, and yet do no more than she had done at the end of two hours, would have been a mystery to anyone not aware that the recent incident was of a kind likely to divert her industry from a manual to a mental channel.\n\nNext day she went her ways as usual, and continued her custom of walking in the heath with no other companion than little Eustacia, now of the age when it is a matter of doubt with such characters whether they are intended to walk through the world on their hands or on their feet; so that they get into painful complications by trying both. It was very pleasant to Thomasin, when she had carried the child to some lonely place, to give her a little private practice on the green turf and shepherd’s-thyme, which formed a soft mat to fall headlong upon them when equilibrium was lost.\n\nOnce, when engaged in this system of training, and stooping to remove bits of stick, fern-stalks, and other such fragments from the child’s path, that the journey might not be brought to an untimely end by some insuperable barrier a quarter of an inch high, she was alarmed by discovering that a man on horseback was almost close beside her, the soft natural carpet having muffled the horse’s tread. The rider, who was Venn, waved his hat in the air and bowed gallantly.\n\n“Diggory, give me my glove,” said Thomasin, whose manner it was under any circumstances to plunge into the midst of a subject which engrossed her.\n\nVenn immediately dismounted, put his hand in his breastpocket, and handed the glove.\n\n“Thank you. It was very good of you to take care of it.”\n\n“It is very good of you to say so.”\n\n“O no. I was quite glad to find you had it. Everybody gets so indifferent that I was surprised to know you thought of me.”\n\n“If you had remembered what I was once you wouldn’t have been surprised.”\n\n“Ah, no,” she said quickly. “But men of your character are mostly so independent.”\n\n“What is my character?” he asked.\n\n“I don’t exactly know,” said Thomasin simply, “except it is to cover up your feelings under a practical manner, and only to show them when you are alone.”\n\n“Ah, how do you know that?” said Venn strategically.\n\n“Because,” said she, stopping to put the little girl, who had managed to get herself upside down, right end up again, “because I do.”\n\n“You mustn’t judge by folks in general,” said Venn. “Still I don’t know much what feelings are nowadays. I have got so mixed up with business of one sort and t’other that my soft sentiments are gone off in vapour like. Yes, I am given up body and soul to the making of money. Money is all my dream.”\n\n“O Diggory, how wicked!” said Thomasin reproachfully, and looking at him in exact balance between taking his words seriously and judging them as said to tease her.\n\n“Yes, ’tis rather a rum course,” said Venn, in the bland tone of one comfortably resigned to sins he could no longer overcome.\n\n“You, who used to be so nice!”\n\n“Well, that’s an argument I rather like, because what a man has once been he may be again.” Thomasin blushed. “Except that it is rather harder now,” Venn continued.\n\n“Why?” she asked.\n\n“Because you be richer than you were at that time.”\n\n“O no—not much. I have made it nearly all over to the baby, as it was my duty to do, except just enough to live on.”\n\n“I am rather glad of that,” said Venn softly, and regarding her from the corner of his eye, “for it makes it easier for us to be friendly.”\n\nThomasin blushed again, and, when a few more words had been said of a not unpleasing kind, Venn mounted his horse and rode on.\n\nThis conversation had passed in a hollow of the heath near the old Roman road, a place much frequented by Thomasin. And it might have been observed that she did not in future walk that way less often from having met Venn there now. Whether or not Venn abstained from riding thither because he had met Thomasin in the same place might easily have been guessed from her proceedings about two months later in the same year.\n\nIII. The Serious Discourse of Clym with His Cousin\nThroughout this period Yeobright had more or less pondered on his duty to his cousin Thomasin. He could not help feeling that it would be a pitiful waste of sweet material if the tender-natured thing should be doomed from this early stage of her life onwards to dribble away her winsome qualities on lonely gorse and fern. But he felt this as an economist merely, and not as a lover. His passion for Eustacia had been a sort of conserve of his whole life, and he had nothing more of that supreme quality left to bestow. So far the obvious thing was not to entertain any idea of marriage with Thomasin, even to oblige her.\n\nBut this was not all. Years ago there had been in his mother’s mind a great fancy about Thomasin and himself. It had not positively amounted to a desire, but it had always been a favourite dream. That they should be man and wife in good time, if the happiness of neither were endangered thereby, was the fancy in question. So that what course save one was there now left for any son who reverenced his mother’s memory as Yeobright did? It is an unfortunate fact that any particular whim of parents, which might have been dispersed by half an hour’s conversation during their lives, becomes sublimated by their deaths into a fiat the most absolute, with such results to conscientious children as those parents, had they lived, would have been the first to decry.\n\nHad only Yeobright’s own future been involved he would have proposed to Thomasin with a ready heart. He had nothing to lose by carrying out a dead mother’s hope. But he dreaded to contemplate Thomasin wedded to the mere corpse of a lover that he now felt himself to be. He had but three activities alive in him. One was his almost daily walk to the little graveyard wherein his mother lay; another, his just as frequent visits by night to the more distant enclosure which numbered his Eustacia among its dead; the third was self-preparation for a vocation which alone seemed likely to satisfy his cravings—that of an itinerant preacher of the eleventh commandment. It was difficult to believe that Thomasin would be cheered by a husband with such tendencies as these.\n\nYet he resolved to ask her, and let her decide for herself. It was even with a pleasant sense of doing his duty that he went downstairs to her one evening for this purpose, when the sun was printing on the valley the same long shadow of the housetop that he had seen lying there times out of number while his mother lived.\n\nThomasin was not in her room, and he found her in the front garden. “I have long been wanting, Thomasin,” he began, “to say something about a matter that concerns both our futures.”\n\n“And you are going to say it now?” she remarked quickly, colouring as she met his gaze. “Do stop a minute, Clym, and let me speak first, for oddly enough, I have been wanting to say something to you.”\n\n“By all means say on, Tamsie.”\n\n“I suppose nobody can overhear us?” she went on, casting her eyes around and lowering her voice. “Well, first you will promise me this—that you won’t be angry and call me anything harsh if you disagree with what I propose?”\n\nYeobright promised, and she continued: “What I want is your advice, for you are my relation—I mean, a sort of guardian to me—aren’t you, Clym?”\n\n“Well, yes, I suppose I am; a sort of guardian. In fact, I am, of course,” he said, altogether perplexed as to her drift.\n\n“I am thinking of marrying,” she then observed blandly. “But I shall not marry unless you assure me that you approve of such a step. Why don’t you speak?”\n\n“I was taken rather by surprise. But, nevertheless, I am very glad to hear such news. I shall approve, of course, dear Tamsie. Who can it be? I am quite at a loss to guess. No I am not—’tis the old doctor!—not that I mean to call him old, for he is not very old after all. Ah—I noticed when he attended you last time!”\n\n“No, no,” she said hastily. “’Tis Mr. Venn.”\n\nClym’s face suddenly became grave.\n\n“There, now, you don’t like him, and I wish I hadn’t mentioned him!” she exclaimed almost petulantly. “And I shouldn’t have done it, either, only he keeps on bothering me so till I don’t know what to do!”\n\nClym looked at the heath. “I like Venn well enough,” he answered at last. “He is a very honest and at the same time astute man. He is clever too, as is proved by his having got you to favour him. But really, Thomasin, he is not quite—”\n\n“Gentleman enough for me? That is just what I feel. I am sorry now that I asked you, and I won’t think any more of him. At the same time I must marry him if I marry anybody—that I _will_ say!”\n\n“I don’t see that,” said Clym, carefully concealing every clue to his own interrupted intention, which she plainly had not guessed. “You might marry a professional man, or somebody of that sort, by going into the town to live and forming acquaintances there.”\n\n“I am not fit for town life—so very rural and silly as I always have been. Do not you yourself notice my countrified ways?”\n\n“Well, when I came home from Paris I did, a little; but I don’t now.”\n\n“That’s because you have got countrified too. O, I couldn’t live in a street for the world! Egdon is a ridiculous old place; but I have got used to it, and I couldn’t be happy anywhere else at all.”\n\n“Neither could I,” said Clym.\n\n“Then how could you say that I should marry some town man? I am sure, say what you will, that I must marry Diggory, if I marry at all. He has been kinder to me than anybody else, and has helped me in many ways that I don’t know of!” Thomasin almost pouted now.\n\n“Yes, he has,” said Clym in a neutral tone. “Well, I wish with all my heart that I could say, marry him. But I cannot forget what my mother thought on that matter, and it goes rather against me not to respect her opinion. There is too much reason why we should do the little we can to respect it now.”\n\n“Very well, then,” sighed Thomasin. “I will say no more.”\n\n“But you are not bound to obey my wishes. I merely say what I think.”\n\n“O no—I don’t want to be rebellious in that way,” she said sadly. “I had no business to think of him—I ought to have thought of my family. What dreadfully bad impulses there are in me!” Her lips trembled, and she turned away to hide a tear.\n\nClym, though vexed at what seemed her unaccountable taste, was in a measure relieved to find that at any rate the marriage question in relation to himself was shelved. Through several succeeding days he saw her at different times from the window of his room moping disconsolately about the garden. He was half angry with her for choosing Venn; then he was grieved at having put himself in the way of Venn’s happiness, who was, after all, as honest and persevering a young fellow as any on Egdon, since he had turned over a new leaf. In short, Clym did not know what to do.\n\nWhen next they met she said abruptly, “He is much more respectable now than he was then!”\n\n“Who? O yes—Diggory Venn.”\n\n“Aunt only objected because he was a reddleman.”\n\n“Well, Thomasin, perhaps I don’t know all the particulars of my mother’s wish. So you had better use your own discretion.”\n\n“You will always feel that I slighted your mother’s memory.”\n\n“No, I will not. I shall think you are convinced that, had she seen Diggory in his present position, she would have considered him a fitting husband for you. Now, that’s my real feeling. Don’t consult me any more, but do as you like, Thomasin. I shall be content.”\n\nIt is to be supposed that Thomasin was convinced; for a few days after this, when Clym strayed into a part of the heath that he had not lately visited, Humphrey, who was at work there, said to him, “I am glad to see that Mrs. Wildeve and Venn have made it up again, seemingly.”\n\n“Have they?” said Clym abstractedly.\n\n“Yes; and he do contrive to stumble upon her whenever she walks out on fine days with the chiel. But, Mr. Yeobright, I can’t help feeling that your cousin ought to have married you. ’Tis a pity to make two chimleycorners where there need be only one. You could get her away from him now, ’tis my belief, if you were only to set about it.”\n\n“How can I have the conscience to marry after having driven two women to their deaths? Don’t think such a thing, Humphrey. After my experience I should consider it too much of a burlesque to go to church and take a wife. In the words of Job, ‘I have made a covenant with mine eyes; when then should I think upon a maid?’”\n\n“No, Mr. Clym, don’t fancy that about driving two women to their deaths. You shouldn’t say it.”\n\n“Well, we’ll leave that out,” said Yeobright. “But anyhow God has set a mark upon me which wouldn’t look well in a love-making scene. I have two ideas in my head, and no others. I am going to keep a night-school; and I am going to turn preacher. What have you got to say to that, Humphrey?”\n\n“I’ll come and hear ’ee with all my heart.”\n\n“Thanks. ’Tis all I wish.”\n\nAs Clym descended into the valley Thomasin came down by the other path, and met him at the gate. “What do you think I have to tell you, Clym?” she said, looking archly over her shoulder at him.\n\n“I can guess,” he replied.\n\nShe scrutinized his face. “Yes, you guess right. It is going to be after all. He thinks I may as well make up my mind, and I have got to think so too. It is to be on the twenty-fifth of next month, if you don’t object.”\n\n“Do what you think right, dear. I am only too glad that you see your way clear to happiness again. My sex owes you every amends for the treatment you received in days gone by.”*\n\n[*] The writer may state here that the original conception of the story did not design a marriage between Thomasin and Venn. He was to have retained his isolated and weird character to the last, and to have disappeared mysteriously from the heath, nobody knowing whither—Thomasin remaining a widow. But certain circumstances of serial publication led to a change of intent. Readers can therefore choose between the endings, and those with an austere artistic code can assume the more consistent conclusion to be the true one.\n\nIV. Cheerfulness Again Asserts Itself at Blooms-End, and Clym Finds His Vocation\n\nAnybody who had passed through Blooms-End about eleven o’clock on the morning fixed for the wedding would have found that, while Yeobright’s house was comparatively quiet, sounds denoting great activity came from the dwelling of his nearest neighbour, Timothy Fairway. It was chiefly a noise of feet, briskly crunching hither and thither over the sanded floor within. One man only was visible outside, and he seemed to be later at an appointment than he had intended to be, for he hastened up to the door, lifted the latch, and walked in without ceremony.\n\nThe scene within was not quite the customary one. Standing about the room was the little knot of men who formed the chief part of the Egdon coterie, there being present Fairway himself, Grandfer Cantle, Humphrey, Christian, and one or two turf-cutters. It was a warm day, and the men were as a matter of course in their shirtsleeves, except Christian, who had always a nervous fear of parting with a scrap of his clothing when in anybody’s house but his own. Across the stout oak table in the middle of the room was thrown a mass of striped linen, which Grandfer Cantle held down on one side, and Humphrey on the other, while Fairway rubbed its surface with a yellow lump, his face being damp and creased with the effort of the labour.\n\n“Waxing a bed-tick, souls?” said the newcomer.\n\n“Yes, Sam,” said Grandfer Cantle, as a man too busy to waste words. “Shall I stretch this corner a shade tighter, Timothy?”\n\nFairway replied, and the waxing went on with unabated vigour. “’Tis going to be a good bed, by the look o’t,” continued Sam, after an interval of silence. “Who may it be for?”\n\n“’Tis a present for the new folks that’s going to set up housekeeping,” said Christian, who stood helpless and overcome by the majesty of the proceedings.\n\n“Ah, to be sure; and a valuable one, ’a b’lieve.”\n\n“Beds be dear to fokes that don’t keep geese, bain’t they, Mister Fairway?” said Christian, as to an omniscient being.\n\n“Yes,” said the furze-dealer, standing up, giving his forehead a thorough mopping, and handing the beeswax to Humphrey, who succeeded at the rubbing forthwith. “Not that this couple be in want of one, but ’twas well to show ’em a bit of friendliness at this great racketing vagary of their lives. I set up both my own daughters in one when they was married, and there have been feathers enough for another in the house the last twelve months. Now then, neighbours, I think we have laid on enough wax. Grandfer Cantle, you turn the tick the right way outwards, and then I’ll begin to shake in the feathers.”\n\nWhen the bed was in proper trim Fairway and Christian brought forward vast paper bags, stuffed to the full, but light as balloons, and began to turn the contents of each into the receptacle just prepared. As bag after bag was emptied, airy tufts of down and feathers floated about the room in increasing quantity till, through a mishap of Christian’s, who shook the contents of one bag outside the tick, the atmosphere of the room became dense with gigantic flakes, which descended upon the workers like a windless snowstorm.\n\n“I never saw such a clumsy chap as you, Christian,” said Grandfer Cantle severely. “You might have been the son of a man that’s never been outside Blooms-End in his life for all the wit you have. Really all the soldiering and smartness in the world in the father seems to count for nothing in forming the nater of the son. As far as that chief Christian is concerned I might as well have stayed at home and seed nothing, like all the rest of ye here. Though, as far as myself is concerned, a dashing spirit has counted for sommat, to be sure!”\n\n“Don’t ye let me down so, Father; I feel no bigger than a ninepin after it. I’ve made but a bruckle hit, I’m afeard.”\n\n“Come, come. Never pitch yerself in such a low key as that, Christian; you should try more,” said Fairway.\n\n“Yes, you should try more,” echoed the Grandfer with insistence, as if he had been the first to make the suggestion. “In common conscience every man ought either to marry or go for a soldier. ’Tis a scandal to the nation to do neither one nor t’other. I did both, thank God! Neither to raise men nor to lay ’em low—that shows a poor do-nothing spirit indeed.”\n\n“I never had the nerve to stand fire,” faltered Christian. “But as to marrying, I own I’ve asked here and there, though without much fruit from it. Yes, there’s some house or other that might have had a man for a master—such as he is—that’s now ruled by a woman alone. Still it might have been awkward if I had found her; for, d’ye see, neighbours, there’d have been nobody left at home to keep down Father’s spirits to the decent pitch that becomes a old man.”\n\n“And you’ve your work cut out to do that, my son,” said Grandfer Cantle smartly. “I wish that the dread of infirmities was not so strong in me!—I’d start the very first thing tomorrow to see the world over again! But seventy-one, though nothing at home, is a high figure for a rover.... Ay, seventy-one, last Candlemasday. Gad, I’d sooner have it in guineas than in years!” And the old man sighed.\n\n“Don’t you be mournful, Grandfer,” said Fairway. “Empt some more feathers into the bed-tick, and keep up yer heart. Though rather lean in the stalks you be a green-leaved old man still. There’s time enough left to ye yet to fill whole chronicles.”\n\n“Begad, I’ll go to ’em, Timothy—to the married pair!” said Granfer Cantle in an encouraged voice, and starting round briskly. “I’ll go to ’em tonight and sing a wedding song, hey? ’Tis like me to do so, you know; and they’d see it as such. My ‘Down in Cupid’s Gardens’ was well liked in four; still, I’ve got others as good, and even better. What do you say to my\n\nShe cal′-led to′ her love′ From the lat′-tice a-bove, ′O come in′ from the fog-gy fog′-gy dew′.\n\n’Twould please ’em well at such a time! Really, now I come to think of it, I haven’t turned my tongue in my head to the shape of a real good song since Old Midsummer night, when we had the ‘Barley Mow’ at the Woman; and ’tis a pity to neglect your strong point where there’s few that have the compass for such things!”\n\n“So ’tis, so ’tis,” said Fairway. “Now gie the bed a shake down. We’ve put in seventy pounds of best feathers, and I think that’s as many as the tick will fairly hold. A bit and a drap wouldn’t be amiss now, I reckon. Christian, maul down the victuals from corner-cupboard if canst reach, man, and I’ll draw a drap o’ sommat to wet it with.”\n\nThey sat down to a lunch in the midst of their work, feathers around, above, and below them; the original owners of which occasionally came to the open door and cackled begrudgingly at sight of such a quantity of their old clothes.\n\n“Upon my soul I shall be chokt,” said Fairway when, having extracted a feather from his mouth, he found several others floating on the mug as it was handed round.\n\n“I’ve swallered several; and one had a tolerable quill,” said Sam placidly from the corner.\n\n“Hullo—what’s that—wheels I hear coming?” Grandfer Cantle exclaimed, jumping up and hastening to the door. “Why, ’tis they back again—I didn’t expect ’em yet this half-hour. To be sure, how quick marrying can be done when you are in the mind for’t!”\n\n“O yes, it can soon be _done_,” said Fairway, as if something should be added to make the statement complete.\n\nHe arose and followed the Grandfer, and the rest also went to the door. In a moment an open fly was driven past, in which sat Venn and Mrs. Venn, Yeobright, and a grand relative of Venn’s who had come from Budmouth for the occasion. The fly had been hired at the nearest town, regardless of distance and cost, there being nothing on Egdon Heath, in Venn’s opinion, dignified enough for such an event when such a woman as Thomasin was the bride; and the church was too remote for a walking bridal-party.\n\nAs the fly passed the group which had run out from the homestead they shouted “Hurrah!” and waved their hands; feathers and down floating from their hair, their sleeves, and the folds of their garments at every motion, and Grandfer Cantle’s seals dancing merrily in the sunlight as he twirled himself about. The driver of the fly turned a supercilious gaze upon them; he even treated the wedded pair themselves with something like condescension; for in what other state than heathen could people, rich or poor, exist who were doomed to abide in such a world’s end as Egdon? Thomasin showed no such superiority to the group at the door, fluttering her hand as quickly as a bird’s wing towards them, and asking Diggory, with tears in her eyes, if they ought not to alight and speak to these kind neighbours. Venn, however, suggested that, as they were all coming to the house in the evening, this was hardly necessary.\n\nAfter this excitement the saluting party returned to their occupation, and the stuffing and sewing were soon afterwards finished, when Fairway harnessed a horse, wrapped up the cumbrous present, and drove off with it in the cart to Venn’s house at Stickleford.\n\nYeobright, having filled the office at the wedding service which naturally fell to his hands, and afterwards returned to the house with the husband and wife, was indisposed to take part in the feasting and dancing that wound up the evening. Thomasin was disappointed.\n\n“I wish I could be there without dashing your spirits,” he said. “But I might be too much like the skull at the banquet.”\n\n“No, no.”\n“Well, dear, apart from that, if you would excuse me, I should be glad. I know it seems unkind; but, dear Thomasin, I fear I should not be happy in the company—there, that’s the truth of it. I shall always be coming to see you at your new home, you know, so that my absence now will not matter.”\n\n“Then I give in. Do whatever will be most comfortable to yourself.”\n\nClym retired to his lodging at the housetop much relieved, and occupied himself during the afternoon in noting down the heads of a sermon, with which he intended to initiate all that really seemed practicable of the scheme that had originally brought him hither, and that he had so long kept in view under various modifications, and through evil and good report. He had tested and weighed his convictions again and again, and saw no reason to alter them, though he had considerably lessened his plan. His eyesight, by long humouring in his native air, had grown stronger, but not sufficiently strong to warrant his attempting his extensive educational project. Yet he did not repine—there was still more than enough of an unambitious sort to tax all his energies and occupy all his hours.\n\nEvening drew on, and sounds of life and movement in the lower part of the domicile became more pronounced, the gate in the palings clicking incessantly. The party was to be an early one, and all the guests were assembled long before it was dark. Yeobright went down the back staircase and into the heath by another path than that in front, intending to walk in the open air till the party was over, when he would return to wish Thomasin and her husband good-bye as they departed. His steps were insensibly bent towards Mistover by the path that he had followed on that terrible morning when he learnt the strange news from Susan’s boy.\n\nHe did not turn aside to the cottage, but pushed on to an eminence, whence he could see over the whole quarter that had once been Eustacia’s home. While he stood observing the darkening scene somebody came up. Clym, seeing him but dimly, would have let him pass silently, had not the pedestrian, who was Charley, recognized the young man and spoken to him.\n\n“Charley, I have not seen you for a length of time,” said Yeobright. “Do you often walk this way?”\n\n“No,” the lad replied. “I don’t often come outside the bank.”\n\n“You were not at the Maypole.”\n\n“No,” said Charley, in the same listless tone. “I don’t care for that sort of thing now.”\n\n“You rather liked Miss Eustacia, didn’t you?” Yeobright gently asked. Eustacia had frequently told him of Charley’s romantic attachment.\n\n“Yes, very much. Ah, I wish—”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“I wish, Mr. Yeobright, you could give me something to keep that once belonged to her—if you don’t mind.”\n\n“I shall be very happy to. It will give me very great pleasure, Charley. Let me think what I have of hers that you would like. But come with me to the house, and I’ll see.”\n\nThey walked towards Blooms-End together. When they reached the front it was dark, and the shutters were closed, so that nothing of the interior could be seen.\n\n“Come round this way,” said Clym. “My entrance is at the back for the present.”\n\nThe two went round and ascended the crooked stair in darkness till Clym’s sitting-room on the upper floor was reached, where he lit a candle, Charley entering gently behind. Yeobright searched his desk, and taking out a sheet of tissue-paper unfolded from it two or three undulating locks of raven hair, which fell over the paper like black streams. From these he selected one, wrapped it up, and gave it to the lad, whose eyes had filled with tears. He kissed the packet, put it in his pocket, and said in a voice of emotion, “O, Mr. Clym, how good you are to me!”\n\n“I will go a little way with you,” said Clym. And amid the noise of merriment from below they descended. Their path to the front led them close to a little side window, whence the rays of candles streamed across the shrubs. The window, being screened from general observation by the bushes, had been left unblinded, so that a person in this private nook could see all that was going on within the room which contained the wedding guests, except in so far as vision was hindered by the green antiquity of the panes.\n\n“Charley, what are they doing?” said Clym. “My sight is weaker again tonight, and the glass of this window is not good.”\n\nCharley wiped his own eyes, which were rather blurred with moisture, and stepped closer to the casement. “Mr. Venn is asking Christian Cantle to sing,” he replied, “and Christian is moving about in his chair as if he were much frightened at the question, and his father has struck up a stave instead of him.”\n\n“Yes, I can hear the old man’s voice,” said Clym. “So there’s to be no dancing, I suppose. And is Thomasin in the room? I see something moving in front of the candles that resembles her shape, I think.”\n\n“Yes. She do seem happy. She is red in the face, and laughing at something Fairway has said to her. O my!”\n\n“What noise was that?” said Clym.\n\n“Mr. Venn is so tall that he knocked his head against the beam in gieing a skip as he passed under. Mrs. Venn has run up quite frightened and now she’s put her hand to his head to feel if there’s a lump. And now they be all laughing again as if nothing had happened.”\n\n“Do any of them seem to care about my not being there?” Clym asked.\n\n“No, not a bit in the world. Now they are all holding up their glasses and drinking somebody’s health.”\n\n“I wonder if it is mine?”\n\n“No, ’tis Mr. and Mrs. Venn’s, because he is making a hearty sort of speech. There—now Mrs. Venn has got up, and is going away to put on her things, I think.”\n\n“Well, they haven’t concerned themselves about me, and it is quite right they should not. It is all as it should be, and Thomasin at least is happy. We will not stay any longer now, as they will soon be coming out to go home.”\n\nHe accompanied the lad into the heath on his way home, and, returning alone to the house a quarter of an hour later, found Venn and Thomasin ready to start, all the guests having departed in his absence. The wedded pair took their seats in the four-wheeled dogcart which Venn’s head milker and handy man had driven from Stickleford to fetch them in; little Eustacia and the nurse were packed securely upon the open flap behind; and the milker, on an ancient overstepping pony, whose shoes clashed like cymbals at every tread, rode in the rear, in the manner of a body-servant of the last century.\n\n“Now we leave you in absolute possession of your own house again,” said Thomasin as she bent down to wish her cousin good night. “It will be rather lonely for you, Clym, after the hubbub we have been making.”\n\n“O, that’s no inconvenience,” said Clym, smiling rather sadly. And then the party drove off and vanished in the night shades, and Yeobright entered the house. The ticking of the clock was the only sound that greeted him, for not a soul remained; Christian, who acted as cook, valet, and gardener to Clym, sleeping at his father’s house. Yeobright sat down in one of the vacant chairs, and remained in thought a long time. His mother’s old chair was opposite; it had been sat in that evening by those who had scarcely remembered that it ever was hers. But to Clym she was almost a presence there, now as always. Whatever she was in other people’s memories, in his she was the sublime saint whose radiance even his tenderness for Eustacia could not obscure. But his heart was heavy, that Mother had _not_ crowned him in the day of his espousals and in the day of the gladness of his heart. And events had borne out the accuracy of her judgment, and proved the devotedness of her care. He should have heeded her for Eustacia’s sake even more than for his own. “It was all my fault,” he whispered. “O, my mother, my mother! would to God that I could live my life again, and endure for you what you endured for me!”\n\nOn the Sunday after this wedding an unusual sight was to be seen on Rainbarrow. From a distance there simply appeared to be a motionless figure standing on the top of the tumulus, just as Eustacia had stood on that lonely summit some two years and a half before. But now it was fine warm weather, with only a summer breeze blowing, and early afternoon instead of dull twilight. Those who ascended to the immediate neighbourhood of the Barrow perceived that the erect form in the centre, piercing the sky, was not really alone. Round him upon the slopes of the Barrow a number of heathmen and women were reclining or sitting at their ease. They listened to the words of the man in their midst, who was preaching, while they abstractedly pulled heather, stripped ferns, or tossed pebbles down the slope. This was the first of a series of moral lectures or Sermons on the Mount, which were to be delivered from the same place every Sunday afternoon as long as the fine weather lasted.\n\nThe commanding elevation of Rainbarrow had been chosen for two reasons: first, that it occupied a central position among the remote cottages around; secondly, that the preacher thereon could be seen from all adjacent points as soon as he arrived at his post, the view of him being thus a convenient signal to those stragglers who wished to draw near. The speaker was bareheaded, and the breeze at each waft gently lifted and lowered his hair, somewhat too thin for a man of his years, these still numbering less than thirty-three. He wore a shade over his eyes, and his face was pensive and lined; but, though these bodily features were marked with decay there was no defect in the tones of his voice, which were rich, musical, and stirring. He stated that his discourses to people were to be sometimes secular, and sometimes religious, but never dogmatic; and that his texts would be taken from all kinds of books. This afternoon the words were as follows:—\n\n“‘And the king rose up to meet her, and bowed himself unto her, and sat down on his throne, and caused a seat to be set for the king’s mother; and she sat on his right hand. Then she said, I desire one small petition of thee; I pray thee say me not nay. And the king said unto her, Ask, on, my mother: for I will not say thee nay.’”\n\nYeobright had, in fact, found his vocation in the career of an itinerant open-air preacher and lecturer on morally unimpeachable subjects; and from this day he laboured incessantly in that office, speaking not only in simple language on Rainbarrow and in the hamlets round, but in a more cultivated strain elsewhere—from the steps and porticoes of town halls, from market-crosses, from conduits, on esplanades and on wharves, from the parapets of bridges, in barns and outhouses, and all other such places in the neighbouring Wessex towns and villages. He left alone creeds and systems of philosophy, finding enough and more than enough to occupy his tongue in the opinions and actions common to all good men. Some believed him, and some believed not; some said that his words were commonplace, others complained of his want of theological doctrine; while others again remarked that it was well enough for a man to take to preaching who could not see to do anything else. But everywhere he was kindly received, for the story of his life had become generally known.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-122"]="\"슬픔에게 나는 안녕을 고했네, 그녀를 멀리 뒤에 두고 떠나려 했건만, 그러나 즐거이, 즐거이, 그녀는 나를 살뜰히 사랑하니, 내게 그토록 한결같고, 그토록 다정하다네. 나는 그녀를 속이려, 그리하여 떠나려 했건만, 아! 그녀는 그토록 한결같고 그토록 다정하다네.\"\n\n뒤이어 펼쳐지는 사건들이 일어났다고 상정되는 시기는 1840년에서 1850년 사이로 잡을 수 있는데, 이 무렵까지만 해도 여기서 \"버드머스\"라 불리는 옛 온천 휴양지는 조지 왕조 시대의 활기와 명성에서 비롯한 잔광을 아직 충분히 간직하고 있어, 내륙에 홀로 사는 이의 낭만적이고 상상력 풍부한 영혼에게 흠뻑 빠져들 만한 매력을 풍기고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이 이야기의 음울한 무대에 붙여진 \"에그던 황야\"라는 총칭 아래에는 실제 이름이 제각기 다른 황야들이, 적어도 열두 곳에 이르는 황야들이 하나로 묶이거나 그 전형으로 대표되어 있는데, 이들은 성격과 양상에서 사실상 하나였으되, 그 본래의 통일성, 혹은 부분적 통일성은 이제 다소 가려져 있으니, 갖가지 성패의 정도로 쟁기질에 부쳐져 경작지로 편입되거나 삼림으로 조성된 침입성 띠와 조각들 때문이다.\n\n남서쪽 한 귀퉁이가 여기 묘사된 그 광대한 지대의 어느 한 지점이, 전설로 전해지는 웨식스의 왕—리어—의 황야일지도 모른다고 꿈꾸어 보는 것은 즐거운 일이다.\n\nT.H.\n\n1895년 7월.\n\nI need to translate starting from first paragraph, no headings.\n\nLet me translate.11월의 어느 토요일 오후가 황혼 무렵에 다가서고 있었고, 에그던 히스라 불리는 울타리 없는 광막한 야생의 대지는 시시각각 검갈색으로 물들어갔다. 머리 위로는 하늘을 가리며 펼쳐진 희끄무레한 구름의 텅 빈 자락이 마치 히스 전체를 바닥으로 삼은 천막처럼 드리워 있었다.\n\n하늘은 이 창백한 장막으로, 땅은 가장 어두운 초목으로 뒤덮여 있었으니, 지평선에서 그 둘이 맞닿는 경계선이 또렷하게 그어졌다. 그런 대조 속에서 히스는 천문학적 시각이 닥치기도 전에 미리 자리를 잡은 밤의 한 부분 같은 모습을 띠었으니, 이곳에는 어둠이 이미 상당히 도래해 있는 반면 하늘에는 낮이 또렷이 남아 있었던 것이다. 위를 올려다보면 가시금작화를 베던 사람은 일을 더 계속하고 싶어졌을 것이요, 아래를 내려다보면 다발을 마무리하고 집으로 돌아가기로 마음먹었을 것이다. 멀리 세계의 가장자리와 창공의 가장자리는 물질의 경계인 동시에 시간의 경계인 듯했다. 히스의 얼굴은 그 안색만으로 저녁에 반 시간을 더 보탰으니, 그와 마찬가지로 새벽을 늦추고, 정오를 시름겹게 하고, 미처 생겨나지도 않은 폭풍의 찌푸림을 앞당기고, 달 없는 한밤의 칠흑 같은 어둠을 더욱 짙게 하여 전율과 공포의 빌미로 만들 수도 있었다.\n\n실로, 어둠을 향한 밤의 회전이 바로 이 전환의 지점에 이르렀을 때 에그던 황야의 거대하고도 독특한 영광이 시작되었으니, 그런 시각에 그곳에 있어보지 않은 자라면 누구도 이 히스를 이해한다고 말할 수 없었다. 그것은 또렷이 보이지 않을 때 가장 잘 느껴졌으니, 그 온전한 효과와 해명이 바로 이 시각과 다음 새벽까지 이어지는 시간 속에 깃들어 있었던 것이요, 그때, 오직 그때에야 비로소 히스는 자신의 참된 이야기를 들려주었다. 이곳은 실로 밤의 가까운 친족이었으니, 밤이 모습을 드러낼 때면 그 음영과 풍경 속에서 서로 한데 끌려 모이려는 뚜렷한 경향이 감지되었다. 둥근 언덕과 움푹한 골짜기가 어슴푸레 뻗은 자락은 순전한 공감으로 솟아올라 저녁의 어스름과 맞닿는 듯했으니, 하늘이 어둠을 쏟아내리는 만큼이나 빠르게 히스는 어둠을 내뿜었다. 그리하여 공중의 어둠과 대지의 어둠은 서로 절반씩 다가서며 검은 동포애 속에 한데 합쳐졌다.\n\n이제 그곳은 깨어 지켜보는 긴장으로 가득 찼으니, 다른 것들이 잠 속으로 가라앉아 골똘히 잠겨들 때 히스는 서서히 깨어나 귀 기울이는 듯했던 것이다. 밤마다 그 거대한 형상은 무언가를 기다리는 듯했으나, 워낙 여러 세기 동안, 그토록 많은 것들의 위기를 거치면서도 미동 없이 그렇게 기다려왔던 터라, 그것이 기다릴 수 있는 것이라곤 단 하나의 마지막 위기—최후의 전복뿐이라 상상될 따름이었다.\n\n이곳은 이곳을 사랑한 이들의 기억 속에 독특하고도 정겨운 조화의 모습으로 되살아나는 장소였다. 꽃과 열매가 미소 짓는 들판은 좀처럼 그러지 못하니, 그것들은 현재보다 결말이 더 평판 좋은 존재와만 영구히 어우러지기 때문이다. 황혼은 에그던 히스의 풍경과 어우러져 준엄함 없이 장엄하고, 화려함 없이 인상적이며, 그 훈계가 단호하고, 그 소박함이 웅대한 무언가를 빚어냈다. 어느 감옥의 정면을 그 두 배 크기의 궁전 정면보다 훨씬 더 위엄 있게 만들곤 하는 그 자질들이 이 히스에 숭고함을 부여했으니, 통념상 아름답다고 일컬어지는 장소들에는 도무지 결여된 숭고함이었다. 아름다운 경치는 아름다운 시절과 행복히 맺어지지만, 아 슬프게도, 시절이 아름답지 못하다면! 사람들은 지나치게 음울한 빛으로 물든 환경의 압박보다, 분별로는 견디기 힘들 만큼 지나치게 미소 짓는 장소의 조롱에 더 자주 시달려왔다. 초췌한 에그던은, 매력적이고 곱다고 일컬어지는 그런 종류의 아름다움에 반응하는 본능보다, 더 미묘하고 더 희귀한 본능에, 더 최근에야 익혀진 감정에 호소했다.\n\n실로, 이 정통적 아름다움의 독점적 지배가 어쩌면 그 마지막 사분기에 다가서고 있는 것은 아닌가 하는 의문이 든다. 새로운 템페 계곡은 툴레의 황량한 황야가 될지도 모르며, 인간의 영혼은 우리 종족이 어렸던 시절에는 못마땅해했던 음울함을 두른 외부의 사물과 점점 더 가까운 조화를 이루게 될지도 모른다. 황무지나 바다나 산의 절제된 숭고함만이 더 사색적인 인류의 기분과 절대적으로 부합하는 유일한 자연이 될 그런 때가, 실제로 오지 않았다면, 가까워 보인다. 그리고 끝내는, 가장 평범한 관광객에게도 아이슬란드 같은 곳이 지금 그에게 남유럽의 포도밭과 도금양 정원이 지니는 의미를 띠게 될지도 모르며, 알프스에서 스헤베닝언의 모래언덕으로 서둘러 가는 그에게 하이델베르크와 바덴은 눈길 한번 받지 못한 채 지나쳐질지도 모른다.\n\n가장 철저한 금욕주의자조차 에그던을 거닐 자연스러운 권리가 자기에게 있다고 느낄 수 있었으니—이런 종류의 영향에 자신을 내맡길 때 그는 정당한 탐닉의 선 안에 머무는 셈이었다. 그토록 절제된 빛깔과 아름다움은 적어도 만인의 타고난 권리였다. 가장 화창한 한여름 날에만 그 기분은 흥겨움의 수준에 닿았다. 강렬함은 찬란함을 통해서보다 엄숙함을 통해서 더 흔히 도달되었으니, 그런 종류의 강렬함은 겨울의 어둠과 폭풍과 안개 속에서 종종 이루어졌다. 그때면 에그던은 화답하도록 깨어났으니, 폭풍이 그 연인이요 바람이 그 벗이었다. 그때면 그곳은 기이한 환영들의 보금자리가 되었으니, 도주와 재난을 그리는 한밤의 꿈속에서 우리를 어렴풋이 에워싸고 있다 느껴지는, 그러나 꿈을 깨고 나면 이런 풍경에 의해 되살아나기 전까지는 결코 떠올리지 못하는 그 음울하고 야생적인 영역들의, 지금껏 알아채지 못했던 원형이 바로 이곳임이 드러났다.\n\n이곳은 현재 인간의 본성과 완벽히 부합하는 장소였으니—섬뜩하지도, 가증스럽지도, 추하지도 않았고, 진부하지도, 무의미하지도, 시시하지도 않았으며, 다만 인간처럼 푸대접받으면서도 견뎌내는 곳이었고, 게다가 그 검은 단조로움 속에 유난히 거대하고 신비로운 곳이었다. 오래도록 홀로 떨어져 살아온 어떤 사람들이 그러하듯, 고독이 그 얼굴에서 내다보는 듯했다. 그것은 외로운 얼굴이었으니, 비극적 가능성을 암시하는 얼굴이었다.\n\n이 어둡고, 낡고, 밀려난 고장은 둠즈데이 토지대장에 등장한다. 그 안에 그 상태가 히스와 가시금작화와 가시덤불의 황무지, 곧 \"브루아리아Bruaria\"로 기록되어 있다. 이어서 길이와 너비가 리그 단위로 적혀 있는데, 이 옛 거리 단위의 정확한 크기에 다소 불확실함이 있긴 하나, 그 수치로 보아 에그던의 면적은 오늘날에 이르기까지 거의 줄어들지 않은 듯하다. \"투르바리아 브루아리아Turbaria Bruaria\"—히스의 뗏장을 베어낼 권리—라는 표현이 이 지역에 관한 특허장들에 나온다. \"히스와 이끼로 뒤덮였다\"고 릴런드는 같은 어두운 일대의 고장을 두고 말한다.\n\n여기에는 적어도 풍경에 관한 이해할 만한 사실들이—진정한 만족을 낳는, 멀리까지 뻗는 증거들이 있었다. 길들일 수 없는, 이스마엘 같은 존재로서 지금 에그던이 그러하듯, 그것은 늘 그러했다. 문명은 그 적이었으니, 초목이 생겨난 이래로 그 흙은 줄곧 똑같은 고풍의 갈색 옷을 걸쳐왔으니, 그 특정 지층에 자연스럽고도 한결같은 의복이었다. 그 유서 깊은 단벌 외투 속에는 옷에 대한 인간의 허영심을 향한 어떤 풍자의 가닥이 깃들어 있었다. 현대식 재단과 빛깔의 옷을 입고 히스에 선 사람은 어딘가 어색해 보인다. 대지의 의복이 이토록 원시적인 곳에서는, 우리에게도 가장 오래되고 가장 단순한 인간의 옷이 필요한 듯하다.\n\n지금처럼 오후와 밤 사이에, 시선이 닿는 한 그 둘레를 온통 채운 히스랜드의 봉우리와 어깨 너머로는 바깥 세계의 어떤 것도 눈에 들어오지 않는 에그던 한복판 골짜기에서, 가시나무 그루터기에 기대어 비스듬히 누운 채, 주위와 발밑의 모든 것이 선사 시대 이래로 머리 위의 별들만큼이나 변함없었음을 안다는 것은, 변화에 떠밀려 표류하고 억누를 수 없는 새로움에 시달리던 마음에 안정의 무게추를 달아주었다. 이 위대하고 침범당하지 않은 장소는 바다도 주장할 수 없는 태곳적의 영속성을 지녔다. 어느 특정한 바다를 두고 누가 그것이 늙었다 말할 수 있겠는가? 태양에 증류되고 달에 반죽되어, 그것은 한 해 만에, 하루 만에, 한 시간 만에 새로워진다. 바다가 변하고, 들판이 변하고, 강과 마을과 사람들이 변해도 에그던은 그대로 남았다. 그 표면들은 비바람에 깎여나갈 만큼 가파르지도, 홍수와 퇴적의 제물이 될 만큼 평평하지도 않았다. 머잖아 언급될, 오랜 세월의 지속으로 거의 자연의 산물처럼 결정화된 늙은 큰길 하나와, 그보다 더 늙은 봉분 하나를 제외하면, 그 사소한 굴곡들조차 곡괭이나 쟁기나 삽이 만든 것이 아니라, 마지막 지질 변동이 직접 손끝으로 남긴 자취 그대로 남아 있었다.\n\n앞서 언급한 큰길은 히스의 낮은 지대를 가로질러 한 지평선에서 다른 지평선까지 뻗어 있었다. 그 노정의 많은 구간에서 그것은 옛 마을 길 위에 겹쳐져 있었으니, 그 길은 바로 가까이에서 로마인들의 거대한 서부 가도, 곧 비아 이케니아나, 일명 아이크닐드 거리로부터 갈라져 나온 것이었다. 지금 살펴보는 그 저녁, 어스름이 히스의 자잘한 윤곽들을 흐려놓을 만큼 짙어졌는데도 길의 흰 표면만큼은 여전히 거의 또렷이 남아 있음을 사람들은 알아챘을 것이다.\n\n길을 따라 한 노인이 걸어가고 있었다. 그는 산처럼 머리가 희었고, 어깨가 굽었으며, 전체적인 행색이 빛바래 있었다. 그는 광택 나는 모자와 낡은 선원 망토와 구두를 걸쳤는데, 그 놋쇠 단추에는 닻이 새겨져 있었다. 손에는 은제 손잡이가 달린 지팡이가 들려 있었으니, 그는 그것을 진짜 세 번째 다리처럼 부려, 몇 인치 간격마다 그 끝으로 끈질기게 땅을 콕콕 찍어댔다. 한때 그가 이런저런 종류의 해군 장교였으리라고 누구나 말했을 법했다.\n\n그의 앞에는 메마르고 텅 비고 하얀, 길고 고된 길이 뻗어 있었다. 그것은 양옆으로 히스를 향해 완전히 트여 있었고, 마치 검은 머리채의 가르마처럼 그 광막한 어두운 표면을 둘로 갈라놓으며, 가장 먼 지평선에서 가늘어지다 굽어 사라졌다.\n\n노인은 자주 눈길을 앞으로 뻗어 자기가 아직 지나가야 할 구간을 바라보았다. 마침내 그는 자기 앞 한참 먼 곳에서 움직이는 점 하나를 알아보았으니, 그것은 탈것인 듯했고, 알고 보니 그 자신이 가는 방향과 똑같은 쪽으로 가고 있었다. 그것은 이 풍경이 품은 단 하나의 생명의 입자였으니, 다만 전체의 외로움을 더 두드러지게 할 따름이었다. 그 전진 속도는 느렸고, 노인은 눈에 띄게 거리를 좁혀갔다.\n\n가까이 다가서자 그는 그것이 모양은 평범하나 빛깔이 기이한, 곧 음산한 붉은빛을 띤 용수철 짐마차임을 알아보았다. 마부가 그 옆을 걸었는데, 그 마차처럼 그 또한 온통 붉었다. 그 물감의 한 빛깔이 그의 옷과 머리에 쓴 모자와 장화와 얼굴과 손을 뒤덮고 있었다. 그는 일시적으로 그 색을 뒤집어쓴 것이 아니라, 그 색이 그에게 배어든 것이었다.\n\n노인은 그것의 의미를 알고 있었다. 마차를 끄는 그 나그네는 레들맨—양에 바를 붉은 염료를 농부들에게 대주는 일을 생업으로 삼는 자였다. 그는 웨식스에서 급속히 사라져가는 부류의 하나로, 지난 세기에 도도새가 동물 세계에서 차지했던 자리를 지금 시골 세계에서 메우고 있었다. 그는 사라진 옛 생활 양식과 두루 만연한 현재의 양식 사이를 잇는, 기이하고 흥미로우며 거의 절멸 직전에 이른 고리였다.\n\n영락한 그 장교는 차츰차츰 길동무 곁으로 다가서서는 좋은 저녁이라 인사를 건넸다. 레들맨은 고개를 돌려 침울하고 골똘한 어조로 답했다. 그는 젊었으니, 그 얼굴은 정확히 잘생겼다고는 못 해도 잘생긴 데 워낙 가까워, 본래의 빛깔로 치면 정말로 잘생겼다는 주장을 누구도 반박하지 못했을 것이다. 그 물감을 뚫고 그토록 야릇하게 번득이는 그의 눈은 그 자체로 매력적이었으니—맹금의 눈처럼 날카롭고, 가을 안개처럼 푸르렀다. 그는 구레나룻도 콧수염도 없어, 얼굴 아래쪽의 부드러운 곡선이 그대로 드러났다. 입술은 얇았고, 보아하니 생각에 잠겨 다물려 있는 듯했으나, 이따금 그 양 끝에 유쾌한 씰룩임이 있었다. 그는 온몸을 몸에 꼭 맞는 코듀로이 한 벌로 감쌌으니, 품질이 뛰어나고 그다지 닳지 않았으며 그 용도에 잘 골라진 옷이었으나, 그 생업 탓에 본래의 빛깔을 빼앗긴 것이었다. 그 옷은 그의 좋은 체격을 돋보이게 했다. 그 사내에게서 풍기는 어떤 넉넉한 분위기는 그가 그 신분치고는 가난하지 않음을 일러주었다. 이를 본 사람이라면 으레 이런 의문을 품었을 것이다, 어찌하여 이토록 장래가 촉망되는 존재가 저 별난 직업을 택하여 자신의 호감 가는 외양을 가려버렸단 말인가?\n\n노인의 인사에 답한 뒤 그는 대화를 이어갈 기색을 보이지 않았으니, 그래도 둘은 여전히 나란히 걸었던바, 연장자인 나그네가 길동무를 바라는 듯했기 때문이다. 그들을 에워싼 적갈색 풀밭 위로 윙윙대는 바람 소리와, 삐걱대는 바퀴 소리와, 두 사람의 발소리와, 마차를 끄는 털북숭이 조랑말 두 마리의 발굽 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 없었다. 그것들은 작고 억센 짐승으로, 갤러웨이와 엑스무어의 교배종이었으며, 이곳에서 \"히스 뜯는 놈들\"이라 불렸다.\n\n이제 그렇게 길을 가는 동안, 레들맨은 이따금 길동무의 곁을 떠나, 마차 뒤로 돌아가서는 작은 창을 통해 그 안을 들여다보았다. 그 눈길은 언제나 근심스러웠다. 그러고는 노인에게로 돌아왔고, 노인은 다시 고장의 형편이며 그런 따위에 관해 한마디 했으며, 레들맨은 또 멍하니 거기에 답했고, 그러고 나면 둘은 다시 침묵에 잠겨들었다. 그 침묵은 어느 쪽에도 어색함을 안기지 않았으니, 이런 외진 곳에서는 나그네들이 첫인사를 나눈 뒤 몇 마일이고 말없이 터덜터덜 걷곤 했으며, 도시에서와 달리 그저 마음 내키는 대로 끝낼 수 있고 끝내지 않는 것 자체가 곧 교제인 그런 곳에서는, 곁에 함께 있다는 것이 무언의 대화나 다름없었던 것이다.\n\n레들맨이 자기 마차를 들여다보러 가지만 않았던들, 어쩌면 이 둘은 헤어질 때까지 다시 입을 열지 않았을지도 모른다. 다섯 번째로 들여다보고 돌아왔을 때 노인이 말했다, \"거기 안에 짐 말고도 뭔가 있는 모양이오?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"보살펴줘야 할 누가 있는 거요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n이러고 얼마 지나지 않아 안에서 희미한 울음소리가 들려왔다. 레들맨은 서둘러 뒤로 가서 안을 들여다보고는 다시 나왔다.\n\n\"거기 아이가 있는 거요, 여보게?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 어르신, 여자입니다.\"\n\n\"세상에 별일이군! 그런데 왜 울음을 내질렀소?\"\n\n\"아, 잠이 들었는데, 여행에 익숙지 않아 불안해서 자꾸 꿈을 꾸는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"젊은 여자요?\"\n\n\"네, 젊은 여자입니다.\"\n\n\"마흔 해 전이라면 내 흥미를 끌었을 일이구먼. 어쩌면 자네 아내인가?\"\n\n\"제 아내요!\" 사내가 쓰디쓰게 말했다. \"저 같은 놈과 짝지을 분이 아닙니다. 하지만 어르신께 그 얘길 할 까닭은 없지요.\"\n\n\"그건 그렇지. 그렇다고 안 할 까닭도 없잖나. 내가 자네한테나 저 여자한테 무슨 해를 끼치겠나?\"\n\n레들맨은 노인의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. \"글쎄요, 어르신,\" 그가 마침내 말했다, \"전 오늘 전부터 저 사람을 알고 있었습니다, 어쩌면 모르는 편이 나았을지도 모르지만요. 하지만 저 사람은 제게 아무것도 아니고, 저도 저 사람에게 아무것도 아닙니다. 저 사람을 태울 더 나은 마차가 거기 있었더라면 제 마차에 타지도 않았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"실례지만, 어디서?\"\n\n\"앵글베리에서요.\"\n\n\"그 읍이라면 잘 알지. 거기서 뭘 하고 있었나?\"\n\n\"아, 별것 아닙니다—입에 올릴 만한 건요. 어쨌든 지금은 죽도록 지쳐 있고 도무지 몸도 성치 않아서, 그래서 저렇게 안절부절못하는 겁니다. 한 시간쯤 전에 깜박 잠이 들었는데, 그래야 몸이 좀 나아질 겁니다.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 어여쁜 처자겠지?\"\n\n\"어르신도 그리 말씀하실 겁니다.\"\n\n또 한 사람의 나그네는 관심 어린 눈길을 마차 창 쪽으로 돌리고는, 그 눈길을 거두지 않은 채 말했다, \"내가 안을 한번 들여다봐도 되겠지?\"\n\n\"안 됩니다,\" 레들맨이 퉁명스레 말했다. \"어두워져서 어르신이 저 사람을 제대로 보기도 어렵고, 게다가 제겐 그걸 허락할 권리가 없습니다. 다행히 곤히 잠들어 있으니, 집에 닿을 때까지 깨지 않았으면 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n\"저 여자가 누군가? 이 동네 사람인가?\"\n\n\"누구든 무슨 상관입니까, 죄송합니다만.\"\n\n\"요즘 들어 이러쿵저러쿵 입에 오르내리던 그 블룸스엔드의 처자는 아니겠지? 만일 그렇다면 나는 저 여자를 알고, 무슨 일이 있었는지도 짐작할 수 있네만.\"\n\n\"누구든 무슨 상관입니까…… 자, 어르신, 죄송한 말씀입니다만 이제 곧 헤어져야겠습니다. 제 조랑말들이 지쳤고, 저는 갈 길이 멉니다. 이 둑 아래에서 한 시간쯤 쉬게 할 참입니다.\"\n\n연장자인 나그네는 무심히 고개를 끄덕였고, 레들맨은 말과 마차를 풀밭 안쪽으로 돌리며 말했다, \"안녕히 가십시오.\" 노인이 답하고는 아까처럼 길을 갔다.\n\n레들맨은 노인의 형체가 길 위에서 한 점으로 작아져 짙어가는 밤의 막 속으로 빨려드는 것을 지켜보았다. 그러고는 마차 밑에 매달아둔 건초 단에서 건초를 좀 꺼내, 그 일부를 말들 앞에 던져주고, 나머지로는 받침을 만들어 마차 옆 땅바닥에 깔았다. 그 위에 그는 바퀴에 등을 기대고 앉았다. 안에서 낮고 부드러운 숨소리가 그의 귀에 들려왔다. 그것이 그를 안심시키는 듯했고, 그는 다음에 어떤 걸음을 내디뎌야 할지 가늠하듯, 골똘히 풍경을 둘러보았다.\n\n골똘히, 그리고 조금씩 천천히 일을 행한다는 것은, 실로 이 전환의 시각 에그던의 골짜기에서 마땅히 지켜야 할 도리인 듯했으니, 길게 늘어진 머뭇거리는 망설임을 닮은 무언가가 히스 자체의 형편에 깃들어 있었기 때문이다. 그것은 이 풍경에 어린 정적의 성질이었다. 이는 실제 정체의 정적이 아니라, 믿기지 않을 만큼 느린 움직임의 겉보기 정적이었다. 죽음의 마비와 이토록 흡사한 건강한 생명의 상태란 그 나름으로 주목할 만한 것이니, 사막의 무기력을 드러내면서도 동시에 초원의, 나아가 숲의 힘에 가까운 힘을 발휘한다는 것은, 그것을 곱씹는 이들에게 으레 절제와 함축이 자아내는 그런 주의력을 불러일으켰다.\n\n레들맨의 눈앞에 펼쳐진 풍경은 길의 높이에서 뒤로 물러나 히스의 심장부로 이어지는 점진적인 오르막의 연속이었다. 그것은 작은 언덕과 구덩이와 능선과 비탈을 차례로 품으며, 마침내 아직 환한 하늘을 배경으로 또렷이 도드라진 높은 언덕에서 모든 것이 마무리되었다. 나그네의 눈은 한동안 이것들 위를 맴돌다가, 마침내 저 위의 주목할 만한 한 사물에 가닿았다. 그것은 봉분이었다. 본래의 지면 위로 불룩 솟은 이 흙더미는 히스가 품은 가장 외로운 높은 곳의 가장 드높은 자리를 차지하고 있었다. 골짜기에서 보면 그것은 아틀라스 같은 이마에 돋은 사마귀에 지나지 않아 보였으나, 실제 부피는 거대했다. 그것은 이 히스 세계의 극이자 축을 이루었다.\n\n쉬고 있던 사내가 그 봉분을 바라보던 중, 그는 지금껏 사방 풍경에서 가장 높은 사물이던 그 꼭대기 위로 더 높은 무언가가 솟아 있음을 알아챘다. 그것은 반구형 흙더미에서 마치 투구에 박힌 창끝처럼 솟아올라 있었다. 상상력 있는 이방인이라면 그것을 봉분을 세운 켈트족 중 한 사람의 형상이라 짐작하는 게 첫 본능이었을 법하니, 현대에 속한 모든 것이 그토록 멀리 이 풍경에서 물러나 있었던 것이다. 그것은 마치 그들 가운데 마지막으로 남은 한 사람이, 제 종족의 나머지와 더불어 영원한 밤 속으로 떨어지기 전 잠시 생각에 잠긴 듯 보였다.\n\n거기 그 형체는, 발밑의 언덕만큼이나 미동도 없이 서 있었다. 평지 위로 언덕이 솟았고, 언덕 위로 봉분이 솟았으며, 봉분 위로 그 형체가 솟아 있었다. 그 형체 위로는 천구의天球儀가 아니고서는 어디에도 그릴 수 없을 것밖에 없었다.\n\n그 형체는 어두운 언덕 더미에 그토록 완벽하고 섬세하며 필연적인 마무리를 부여하여, 그 윤곽을 정당화하는 유일하고도 명백한 근거인 듯했다. 그것이 없으면 채광탑 없는 둥근 지붕이었고, 그것이 있으면 그 덩어리의 건축적 요구가 충족되었다. 이 풍경은 기묘하게도 균질했으니, 골짜기와 고지와 봉분과 그 위의 형체가 오직 하나의 통일체를 이룰 뿐이었기 때문이다. 그 무리의 이런저런 일원을 바라보는 것은 온전한 하나를 보는 것이 아니라 어떤 것의 한 조각을 보는 셈이었다.\n\n그 형체는 이 미동 없는 전체 구조의 유기적인 한 부분과 너무도 흡사하여, 그것이 움직이는 것을 본다면 마음에 기이한 현상으로 각인되었을 것이다. 그 사람이 일부를 이룬 전체의 주된 특징이 부동不動이었으니, 어느 한구석에서 그 부동이 그쳐진다는 것은 곧 혼란을 암시했다.\n\n그런데 바로 그 일이 일어났다. 그 형체는 눈에 띄게 그 고정됨을 풀고, 한두 걸음 옮기더니, 몸을 돌렸다. 마치 놀란 듯, 그것은 봉오리를 타고 흐르는 물방울처럼 미끄러져 봉분의 오른편으로 내려가더니, 곧 사라졌다. 그 움직임은 그 형체의 특징을, 그것이 여자의 것임을 더 또렷이 드러내기에 충분했다. 그녀가 그토록 돌연 자리를 옮긴 까닭이 이제 드러났다. 그녀가 오른편으로 사라지자, 짐을 진 새로운 인물 하나가 왼편 하늘로 불쑥 솟아올라 무덤을 오르더니, 그 짐을 꼭대기에 내려놓았다. 두 번째가 뒤따랐고, 이어 세 번째, 네 번째, 다섯 번째가 따르더니, 마침내 봉분 전체가 짐 진 형체들로 가득 찼다.\n\n하늘을 배경으로 펼쳐진 이 실루엣들의 무언극에서 알아낼 수 있는 단 하나의 뜻은, 그 여자가 자기 자리를 대신 차지한 형체들과 아무런 관련이 없으며, 그들을 부지런히 피하고 있었고, 그들과는 다른 목적으로 그곳에 왔다는 것이었다. 관찰자의 상상은 새로 온 이들보다, 사라진 그 외로운 형체에—더 흥미롭고, 더 중요하며, 알아둘 만한 사연을 지녔을 법한 무언가로서—기꺼이 매달렸으니, 무의식중에 그들을 침입자로 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 그들은 남아서 자리를 잡았고, 지금껏 이 고독의 여왕이었던 그 외로운 사람은 당장은 돌아올 성싶지 않았다.\n\n봉분 바로 곁에 누군가 지켜보는 이가 배치되어 있었더라면, 그는 이 사람들이 인근 마을의 소년과 사내들임을 알아냈을 것이다. 저마다 봉분을 오를 때면 가시금작화 다발을 잔뜩 짊어지고 있었으니, 그것들을 손쉽게 꿰찌르려고 양 끝을 뾰족하게 깎은 긴 막대를 어깨에 메어—앞에 둘, 뒤에 둘—나르는 것이었다. 그들은 뒤쪽으로 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 히스의 한 구역에서 왔으니, 그곳은 거의 가시금작화만이 산물로 자라는 곳이었다.\n\n저마다 다발을 나르는 방식 탓에 가시금작화에 온통 파묻혀, 그것을 내던지기 전까지는 다리 달린 덤불처럼 보였다. 일행은 길 떠난 양 떼처럼 한 줄로 행진해왔으니, 다시 말해 가장 힘센 자가 앞장서고, 약하고 어린 자가 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n짐들이 모두 한데 쌓이자, 둘레가 서른 자에 이르는 가시금작화 피라미드가 이제 그 무덤의 꼭대기를 차지했으니, 그곳은 사방 여러 마일에 걸쳐 레인배로라 알려진 곳이었다. 어떤 이들은 성냥을 만지작거리며 가장 마른 가시금작화 더미를 골랐고, 다른 이들은 다발을 묶고 있던 가시덤불 끈을 풀었다. 또 다른 이들은, 이 일이 진행되는 동안, 눈을 들어 그들의 위치가 굽어보는 광막한 고장을 둘러보았는데, 그곳은 이제 어스름에 거의 지워져 있었다. 히스의 골짜기에서는 하루 어느 때든 제 자신의 야생적 얼굴 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았으나, 이 자리는 멀리까지 뻗은 일대를, 더러는 히스 고장 너머에까지 이르는 지평선을 굽어보았다. 그 어떤 지형도 이제는 분간할 수 없었으나, 그 전체가 아득한 먼 거리의 어렴풋한 자락으로 느껴졌다.\n\n사내들과 소년들이 더미를 쌓는 동안, 먼 풍경을 나타내던 그 어둠의 덩어리 속에 변화가 일었다. 붉은 태양과 불꽃 다발이 하나둘 솟아오르기 시작하여, 사방 온 고장에 점점이 무늬를 놓았다. 그것들은 같은 종류의 기념 행사를 벌이는 다른 교구와 마을의 모닥불이었다. 어떤 것들은 멀리, 짙은 대기 속에 서 있어, 창백한 짚 빛깔의 빛줄기 다발이 부챗살 모양으로 그 둘레에 뻗어 나왔다. 어떤 것들은 크고 가까워, 마치 검은 가죽에 난 상처처럼 어둠 속에서 진홍빛으로 이글거렸다. 어떤 것들은 매내드 같았으니, 술 취한 얼굴에 머리칼을 흩날렸다. 이것들은 머리 위 구름의 고요한 가슴을 물들이며 그 덧없는 동굴을 밝혔으니, 그 동굴은 그때부터 펄펄 끓는 가마솥이 되는 듯했다. 어쩌면 이 지역의 경계 안에서 서른 개나 되는 모닥불을 헤아릴 수 있었을 것이며, 시계판의 숫자가 보이지 않아도 시각을 읽어낼 수 있듯이, 사내들은 비록 풍경이라곤 아무것도 볼 수 없는데도 그 각도와 방향으로 저마다의 불이 어느 곳에 있는지 알아냈다.\n\n레인배로에서 첫 키 큰 불길이 하늘로 솟구치며, 멀리 타오르는 불길들에 박혀 있던 모든 눈길을 같은 종류의 제 시도로 다시 끌어당겼다. 그 명랑한 불꽃은 이제 다른 어슬렁대던 이들이—남자도 여자도—합류해 불어난 인간 동그라미의 안쪽 면을 제 황금빛 제복으로 줄무늬 지웠고, 둘레의 어두운 풀밭마저 발랄한 빛으로 뒤덮었으니, 그 빛은 봉분이 둥글게 휘어 시야에서 사라지는 곳에서 어둠 속으로 부드러이 잦아들었다. 그 빛은 봉분이 흙을 파낸 작은 도랑까지 그대로 남아 있는, 처음 쌓아 올린 그날만큼이나 완벽한 구球의 한 조각임을 드러냈다. 쟁기 한 자루도 그 완강한 흙의 한 알갱이조차 건드린 적이 없었다. 농부에게는 척박함인 이 히스가 역사가에게는 비옥함이었다. 손길을 댄 적이 없었기에, 지워진 것도 없었던 것이다.\n\n마치 모닥불을 피우는 이들이 아래로 뻗은 어두운 자락에서 떨어져 나와 그것과 무관하게, 세계의 어느 빛나는 위층에 서 있는 듯했다. 저 아래 히스는 이제 광막한 심연이었지, 더는 그들이 선 곳의 연장이 아니었으니, 불빛에 적응한 그들의 눈은 그 불빛이 미치는 범위 너머의 깊은 곳을 아무것도 볼 수 없었기 때문이다. 물론 이따금, 그들의 다발에서 평소보다 한층 거센 불길이 솟아 부관副官처럼 빛살을 비탈 아래로 쏘아 보내 멀리 어느 덤불이나 웅덩이나 흰 모래밭에 가닿게 하면, 그것들이 같은 빛깔로 화답하며 타올랐다가, 다시 모든 것이 어둠 속으로 사라졌다. 그러면 발밑의 그 온통 검은 광경은, 환상 속에서 그 가장자리에 서서 굽어보던 숭고한 피렌체 사람이 바라본 림보를 나타냈으니, 움푹한 골짜기에서 웅얼대는 바람의 또렷찮은 말소리는 거기 매달린 \"위대한 가치를 지닌 영혼들\"의 하소연이자 탄원 같았다.\n\n마치 이 사내들과 소년들이 돌연 지난 시대 속으로 뛰어들어, 일찍이 이 자리와 친숙했던 어느 한 시각과 행위를 거기서 길어 올린 듯했다. 저 꼭대기에서 타올랐던 원래의 브리튼인 화장단火葬壇의 재가 그들의 발밑 봉분 속에 생생히 흐트러짐 없이 묻혀 있었다. 오래전 거기 지펴진 장례 화장단의 불길이 지금처럼 저지대를 비춰 내렸었다. 토르와 보덴에게 바치는 축제의 불이 같은 땅 위에 뒤따라 마땅히 한 시절을 누렸다. 실로, 지금 이 히스 사람들이 즐기는 이런 불길은, 화약 음모 사건을 둘러싼 민중 정서의 발명이라기보다는, 뒤죽박죽 섞인 드루이드교 의식과 색슨족 의례의 직계 후손에 가깝다는 것이 꽤나 잘 알려진 사실이다.\n\n게다가 불을 피우는 것은, 겨울이 들어서는 길목에 자연 전체에 통금의 종이 울릴 때, 인간이 본능적으로 저항하는 행위이다. 그것은 이 되풀이되는 계절이 사나운 시절과 차가운 어둠과 비참과 죽음을 가져오리라는 그 율령에 맞서는 자발적이고 프로메테우스적인 반항을 나타낸다. 검은 혼돈이 닥쳐오면, 사슬에 묶인 대지의 신들이 말한다, 빛이 있으라.\n\n둘러선 사람들의 살갗과 옷 위에서 다투던 눈부신 빛과 검댕 같은 그늘은 그들의 이목구비와 전체 윤곽을 뒤러풍의 힘과 기세로 그려냈다. 그러나 저마다의 얼굴에 항구히 새겨진 도덕적 표정만큼은 도무지 알아낼 길이 없었으니, 날렵한 불길이 솟구치고 까딱이고 주위 대기를 휩쓸며 곤두박질칠 때마다, 그 무리의 얼굴들 위에 어린 그늘의 얼룩과 빛의 조각이 끝없이 모양과 자리를 바꾸었기 때문이다. 모든 것이 불안정했으니, 잎새처럼 떨리고 번개처럼 덧없었다. 해골만큼 깊던 어둑한 눈구멍이 돌연 광채의 구덩이로 바뀌었고, 길쭉한 턱이 동굴 같다가 이내 번쩍였으며, 주름이 협곡으로 도드라지거나 빛살이 바뀌며 아예 지워지기도 했다. 콧구멍은 어두운 우물이었고, 늙은 목의 힘줄은 금박 입힌 쇠시리였으며, 별달리 윤기 없던 것들이 반들거렸고, 한 사내가 든 가시금작화 낫 끝 같은 밝은 사물은 유리 같았으며, 눈알은 작은 등불처럼 이글거렸다. 자연이 그저 별나게 빚어놓은 이들이 기괴해졌고, 기괴한 것은 초자연적인 것이 되었으니, 모든 것이 극단에 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그러기에, 다른 이들처럼 솟구치는 불길에 끌려 이 높은 곳으로 불려온 한 노인의 얼굴이, 보이는 대로 그저 코와 턱뿐인 것이 아니라, 제법 가늠할 만한 양의 인간다운 얼굴이었음도 그럴 법한 일이다. 그는 흐뭇하게 그 열기에 몸을 쬐며 서 있었다. 막대인지 말뚝인지를 들고 그는 한 켠에 흩어진 땔감 부스러기를 불 속으로 던져 넣으며, 더미의 한복판을 들여다보다가, 이따금 눈을 들어 불길의 높이를 가늠하거나, 그와 함께 솟아올라 어둠 속으로 흘러가는 큰 불티를 좇았다. 빛나는 광경과 파고드는 온기가 그의 안에 점점 쌓여가는 쾌활함을 길러내는 듯했고, 그것은 이내 환희에 이르렀다. 손에 막대를 든 채 그는 혼자만의 미뉴에트를 추기 시작했고, 조끼 밑에서 한 묶음의 구리 인장이 추처럼 반짝이며 흔들렸다. 그는 또한, 굴뚝을 타고 오르는 벌의 목소리로 노래하기 시작했다—\n\n\"임금님께서 신하들을 죄다 불러들이셨네, 하나둘 셋 차례로 불러들이셨네, 마셜 백작이여, 내 가서 왕비의 죄를 사하리니, 그대도 나와 함께 길을 나서야 하리.\"\n\n\"은총을, 은총을, 마셜 백작이 여쭈오며, 무릎을 꺾어 꿇어앉고는, 왕비께서 무슨 말씀을 하시든, 거기서 화가 미치지 않게 하소서.\"\n\n숨이 차서 노래를 더 이어가지 못했고, 그 끊김은 중년의 한 사내, 자기에게 혹여 잘못 들러붙었을지 모를 흥겨움의 기미를 죄다 떨쳐버리려는 듯 초승달 모양 입가의 양 끝을 두 볼 속으로 단단히 끌어당겨 다물고 굳건히 선 사내의 주의를 끌었다.\n\n\"멋진 가락이오, 캔틀 영감님. 하지만 영감님 같은 노친네의 곰팡 핀 목구멍엔 너무 벅찰까 걱정이구려,\" 그가 그 주름투성이 흥취꾼에게 말했다. \"처음 그 노래를 배웠을 때처럼 다시 열여덟 살이 되고 싶진 않소, 영감님?\"\n\n\"어?\" 캔틀 영감이 춤을 멈추며 말했다.\n\n\"다시 젊어지고 싶지 않냐고 했소. 요즘 영감님 그 가엾은 풀무엔 구멍이 난 모양인데.\"\n\n\"하지만 내겐 그래도 솜씨가 있잖나? 약간의 바람으로 멀리까지 가지 못한다면야, 나도 세상 제일 늙은 노인네보다 젊어 보일 게 없지 않겠나, 그렇잖나, 티머시?\"\n\n\"그건 그렇고 저 아래 콰이엇 우먼 주막에 새로 혼인한 사람들은 어찌 됐답니까?\" 상대가 물으며, 멀리 큰길 쪽으로, 그러나 그 순간 레들맨이 쉬고 있던 자리와는 한참 떨어진 곳의 어슴푸레한 불빛 쪽을 가리켰다. \"그 사람들 일이 어찌 된 게 옳은 거요? 영감님은 알아야 마땅하지, 이치를 아는 분이시니.\"\n\n\"좀 난봉꾼이긴 하지, 어? 인정하네. 캔틀 어른이 바로 그런 사람이거나, 아니면 아무것도 아니거나지. 하지만 그건 흥겨운 흠이니, 페어웨이 이웃, 나이가 고쳐줄 걸세.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤 집에 온다고 들었소. 이맘때면 벌써 왔겠지. 그밖에는?\"\n\n\"다음 차례는 우리가 가서 그들에게 기쁨을 빌어주는 거겠지, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"음, 아니오.\"\n\n\"아니라고? 난 우리가 마땅히 그래야 하는 줄 알았네. 적어도 난 그래야지, 안 그러면 나답지 않을 테니—무릇 벌어지는 잔치라면 첫째로 끼어드는 게 나 아닌가!\n\n\"그대는 수도사의 옷을 걸치게, 나는 또 다른 옷을 걸칠 테니, 우리는 엘리노어 왕비께로 가세, 수도사와 그 형제처럼.\n\n지난밤에 요브라이트 부인을, 그 젊은 신부의 고모를 만났는데, 아들 클림이 크리스마스에 집에 온다고 하더군. 어찌나 똑똑한지, 정말로—아, 저 젊은이 머릿속에 든 걸 죄다 나도 가졌으면 좋겠어. 그래, 그래서 내가 그 사람한테 내 그 유명한 익살스러운 투로 말을 건넸더니, 그 사람이 그러더군, '오, 점잖아 뵈는 양반이 어찌 바보처럼 말한담!'—그게 그 사람이 내게 한 말이야. 난 그 사람한테 신경 안 써, 진짜로 안 써, 그래서 그렇게 말해줬지. '나야말로 당신한테 신경 안 쓴다네,' 라고 말이야. 내가 거기서 한 방 먹였지—어?\"\n\n\"오히려 그분이 영감님을 먹인 것 같소만,\" 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n\"아니,\" 캔틀 영감이 말했는데, 그 안색이 다소 풀이 죽었다. \"나도 그 정도로 형편없진 않지?\"\n\n\"보아하니 그런 모양인데, 어쨌든, 클림이 크리스마스에 집에 오는 게 그 혼사 때문이오—이제 어머니가 집에 혼자 남게 됐으니 새로 살림을 꾸리려고?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래—바로 그거야. 하지만, 티머시, 내 말 좀 들어보게,\" 영감이 진지하게 말했다. \"내가 그런 익살꾼으로 알려졌어도, 진지해진 나를 붙잡고 보면 난 이치를 아는 사람이고, 지금은 진지하다네. 그 부부에 관해서라면 내가 자네한테 많이 일러줄 수 있어. 그래, 오늘 아침 여섯 시에 그 일을 치르러 시골 위쪽으로 갔는데, 그 뒤로는 코빼기도 자취도 안 보였어, 그래도 내 짐작에 오늘 오후엔 남자와 여자로—아니, 부부로 다시 집에 돌아왔을 게야. 사내답게 말한 거 아닌가, 티머시, 요브라이트 부인이 나를 두고 한 말이 틀린 거 아닌가?\"\n\n\"그래, 됐소. 지난가을 그분이 결혼 예고를 막은 뒤로 그 둘이 다시 함께 다닌 줄은 몰랐구려. 그럼 이 새 일이 갈려나간 지는 얼마나 된 거요? 자네 아나, 험프리?\"\n\n\"그래, 얼마나 됐나?\" 캔틀 영감이 잽싸게 험프리를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"내가 그걸 묻는 거야.\"\n\n\"그분이 마음을 바꿔서, 그래도 그 남자한테 시집가도 좋다고 한 뒤로 줄곧이지요,\" 험프리가 불에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 답했다. 그는 어딘가 엄숙한 젊은이로, 가시금작화 베는 사람의 낫과 가죽 장갑을 지니고 있었으며, 그 일 때문에 두 다리는 블레셋 사람의 놋쇠 정강이받이만큼 뻣뻣하게 불룩한 각반에 싸여 있었다. \"그래서 둘이 결혼하러 떠난 거라고 봅니다. 보세요, 그렇게 한바탕 소동을 피우고 결혼 예고를 막아놓고 나서, 마치 한 번도 반대한 적 없던 것처럼 같은 교구에서 떠들썩한 잔치를 벌였다면 요브라이트 부인이 바보처럼 보였을 거 아닙니까.\"\n\n\"바로 그거야—바보처럼 보일 테지. 그리고 그건 그런 처지의 가엾은 사람들한테 아주 안된 일이지, 물론 나야 그저 짐작만 할 뿐이지만 말일세,\" 캔틀 영감이 여전히 분별 있는 태도와 풍모를 애써 지키며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그런데, 난 그날 교회에 있었다오,\" 페어웨이가 말했다, \"참 묘한 일이 벌어졌지.\"\n\n\"내가 거기 있었다면야 내 이름은 심플이지,\" 영감이 힘주어 말했다. \"난 올해 거긴 안 갔어. 그리고 이제 겨울이 다가오니 갈 거라고도 못 하겠네.\"\n\n\"난 삼 년째 안 갔소,\" 험프리가 말했다. \"일요일이면 어찌나 죽도록 졸린지, 게다가 거기까지 가기가 끔찍이도 멀어요. 또 막상 가본들 그토록 많은 이들이 못 뽑히는 마당에 내가 저 위로 뽑힐 가망이 워낙 지독히도 적으니, 그냥 집에 들어앉아 아예 안 가는 거요.\"\n\n\"난 거기 있었을 뿐 아니라,\" 페어웨이가 새삼 힘을 그러모아 말했다, \"요브라이트 부인과 같은 신도석에 앉아 있었지. 그러니 자네들이야 그렇게 안 볼지 몰라도, 그 사람 말을 듣자니 정말이지 피가 얼어붙더군. 그래, 묘한 일이야. 하지만 내 피를 얼어붙게 하더라니까, 내가 바로 그 사람 팔꿈치 옆에 붙어 있었으니.\" 말하는 이는 둘러선 이들을 죽 둘러보았는데, 그들은 이제 그의 말을 들으려 바싹 다가들었고, 그 입술은 묘사의 절제를 엄히 지키느라 어느 때보다도 더 꼭 다물려 있었다.\n\n\"거기서 그런 일을 당하는 건 심각한 노릇이지요,\" 뒤편의 한 여자가 말했다.\n\n\"'이의를 밝히시오,' 그게 목사님 말씀이었지,\" 페어웨이가 이어갔다. \"그러자 내 곁의 한 여자가—내게 닿을 만큼 가까이서 벌떡 일어서더군. '아니, 빌어먹을, 저거 요브라이트 부인이 일어선 거 아닌가,' 하고 난 속으로 말했지. 그래, 이웃들, 내가 비록 기도의 성전에 있었어도 그렇게 말했다네. 사람들 앞에서 욕하고 악담하는 건 내 양심에 거슬리는 일이고, 여기 어느 여자분이든 너그러이 봐주길 바라네. 그래도 내가 한 말은 한 말이니, 그걸 인정 안 하면 거짓말이 될 게야.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요, 페어웨이 이웃.\"\n\n\"'빌어먹을, 저거 요브라이트 부인이 일어선 거 아닌가,' 하고 난 말했지,\" 이야기꾼이 되풀이하며, 그 나쁜 말을 아까와 똑같이 무덤덤하게 굳은 얼굴로 내뱉었으니, 그 되풀이가 흥에 겨워서가 아니라 전적으로 필요에 따른 것임을 입증했다. \"그리고 다음으로 들린 말은, 그 사람한테서 나온 '나는 이 결혼 예고를 막습니다'였어. '예식 끝나고 얘기합시다,' 목사님이 아주 소탈하게 말하더군—그래, 단번에 자네나 나보다 조금도 거룩할 것 없는 보통 사람으로 변해버린 거야. 아, 그 사람 얼굴이 어찌나 창백하던지! 자네들 혹시 웨더베리 교회에 있는 그 기념상 기억하나—학교 아이들이 팔을 떨어뜨려 버린 그 다리 꼰 병사 말이야? 음, '나는 이 결혼 예고를 막습니다,' 하고 말할 때 그 사람 얼굴이 그 상이랑 거의 똑같았다네.\"\n\n청중은 목청을 가다듬고 줄기 몇 개를 불 속에 던져 넣었으니, 그것이 급한 일이어서가 아니라 그 이야기의 교훈을 곱씹을 시간을 자기들에게 주려는 것이었다.\n\n\"그 결혼 예고가 막혔다는 소릴 들었을 땐, 정말이지 누가 나한테 6펜스라도 쥐여준 것마냥 기뻤어요,\" 진지한 목소리가—히스 빗자루, 곧 비를 만들어 살아가는 여자 올리 다우든의 목소리가 말했다. 그녀는 천성이 원수에게도 벗에게나 다름없이 공손했고, 자기를 살아 있게 해준다는 이유로 온 세상에 감사했다.\n\n\"그런데 이제 그 처자가 끝내 그 남자한테 똑같이 시집을 갔구먼,\" 험프리가 말했다.\n\n\"그 일 뒤에 요브라이트 부인이 마음을 돌려서 아주 흔쾌해졌지,\" 페어웨이가 자기 말이 험프리의 말에 덧붙은 것이 아니라 독자적인 사색의 결과임을 보이려는 듯 아랑곳없는 투로 다시 말했다.\n\n\"부끄러웠다 치더라도, 어째서 바로 여기서 못 했는지 난 모르겠어요,\" 몸을 굽히거나 돌릴 때마다 코르셋이 구두처럼 삐걱대는 펑퍼짐한 여자가 말했다. \"이웃들을 한데 불러 모아 가끔 한바탕 떠들썩하게 노는 것도 좋은 일이고, 그게 명절 때든 혼사 때든 매한가지지요. 난 쉬쉬하는 방식은 질색이에요.\"\n\n\"아, 그런데, 믿기 어렵겠지만, 난 떠들썩한 결혼식은 좋아하질 않아,\" 티머시 페어웨이가 다시 눈길을 죽 돌리며 말했다. \"솔직히 말하자면, 토머신 요브라이트와 와일디브 이웃이 조용히 치른 걸 난 별로 탓하지 않네. 집에서 하는 결혼식이란 다섯 손 여섯 손 잡고 몇 시간이고 릴 춤을 추는 건데, 마흔 넘은 사내한테 그건 다리에 영 안 좋거든.\"\n\n\"맞아요. 일단 여자 집에 발을 들이면, 춤판에 한몫 끼는 걸 마다하기가 어렵지요, 내내 제 밥값은 해야 한다는 걸 알면서 말이오.\"\n\n\"크리스마스에 춤춰야 하는 건 그게 그 철이기 때문이고, 결혼식에 춤춰야 하는 건 그게 인생의 그 시절이기 때문이지. 세례식에서도 사람들은 첫째나 둘째 아이까지는 릴 춤 한두 판을 슬쩍 끼워 넣곤 한다네. 게다가 이건 자네가 불러야 할 노래들은 아직 입에도 안 올린 거야…… 난 말이야, 무엇 못잖게 푸짐한 장례식이 좋더라고. 다른 잔치만큼, 아니 그보다 더 훌륭한 음식과 술이 있고, 가엾은 친구의 살아온 이야기를 두고 떠드는 건 혼파이프 춤이랍시고 벌떡벌떡 일어서는 것처럼 다리를 닳아 없애지도 않거든.\"\n\n\"열에 아홉은 그럴 때 춤을 추는 건 도가 지나친 일이라고 인정할 거요, 아마?\" 캔틀 영감이 넌지시 말했다.\n\n\"그건 점잖은 사내가 술잔이 몇 순배 돈 뒤에도 마음 놓을 수 있는 유일한 잔치지.\"\n\n\"음, 토머신 요브라이트처럼 조용하고 숙녀다운 자그마한 처자가 그런 초라한 식으로 결혼하고 싶어 했다는 게 난 도무지 이해가 안 돼요,\" 본래의 화제를 더 좋아하는 펑퍼짐한 여자 수전 넌서치가 말했다. \"그건 제일 가난한 사람들이 하는 것보다도 못해요. 그리고 난 그 남자라면 신경도 안 썼을 거예요, 더러는 잘생겼다고들 하지만.\"\n\n\"공평하게 말하자면 그는 제 나름으로 영리하고 학식 있는 친구지—거의 옛날 클림 요브라이트만큼 영리했어. 콰이엇 우먼 주막이나 지키기보다 더 나은 일을 하도록 자라났지. 기술자—우리가 알다시피 그 남자가 그런 사람이었어. 그런데 제 기회를 내던지고 그래서 먹고살려고 주막을 차린 거야. 그 학식이 그에겐 아무짝에도 쓸모없게 됐지.\"\n\n\"흔한 일이지요,\" 비 만드는 올리가 말했다. \"그런데도 사람들이 그걸 얼마나 애써 손에 넣으려 하는지! 한때는 제 목숨이 구덩이에 빠지는 걸 막으려 해도 둥근 오자 하나 못 긋던 부류의 사람들이, 이제는 펜을 더듬거리지도 않고, 곧잘 얼룩 한 점 없이 제 이름을 쓴다니까요—무슨 말이냐 하면—아니, 배와 팔꿈치를 기댈 책상조차 거의 없이도 말이에요.\"\n\n\"맞아—세상이 얼마나 윤이 났는지 놀라울 따름이지,\" 험프리가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 내가 사년도에 (우리가 그렇게 불렸지) 뱅업 의용대에 군인으로 들어가기 전엔,\" 캔틀 영감이 명랑하게 끼어들었다, \"세상이 어떻게 생겨먹은 건지 자네들 중 제일 평범한 사람만큼도 몰랐다네. 그런데 이제는, 젠장, 내가 못 할 게 뭐라고는 말 안 하겠어, 어?\"\n\n\"명부에 서명이야 하실 수 있겠지,\" 페어웨이가 말했다, \"와일디브와 토머신 색시처럼 다시 어떤 여자랑 손잡을 만큼 젊기만 하다면야. 그건 저기 험프 녀석은 못 할 일이지, 글 배우는 건 제 아비를 닮았으니. 아, 험프, 난 내가 결혼할 때 내 이름을 적으러 가다가 자네 아비의 표시가 떡하니 나를 노려보던 게 똑똑히 기억나네. 그 양반하고 자네 어미가 우리 바로 앞에 결혼한 부부였는데, 거기 자네 아비의 십자 표시가 떡 버틴 허수아비처럼 두 팔을 쩍 벌리고 서 있었지. 그게 어찌나 끔찍하게 시커먼 십자던지—그 안에 자네 아비를 빼다 박았더라고! 그걸 보고 난 죽어도 웃음을 못 참겠더군, 그러는 내내 난 한증막처럼 후끈 달아 있었는데, 결혼한답시고 그렇고, 여자가 내게 달라붙어 그렇고, 잭 체인글리며 다른 녀석들이 교회 창문 너머로 날 보고 히죽대서 그랬지. 그런데 다음 순간엔 지푸라기 하나에도 쓰러질 지경이었어, 자네 아비랑 어미가 한 번이라도 험한 말을 주고받았다면 부부 된 뒤로 스무 번은 더 그랬을 거라는 데 생각이 미쳤거든, 그리고 똑같은 수렁에 빠질 다음번 가엾은 멍청이가 바로 나라는 게 보이더라고…… 아—참, 그 무슨 날이었던지!\"\n\n\"와일디브는 토머신 요브라이트보다 여러 여름은 더 나이를 먹었지. 그 색시도 어여쁜 처자야. 제 집이 있는 젊은 여자가 그런 남자 때문에 제 속치마를 찢는다면 바보지.\"\n\n이 말을 한 사람은 토탄, 곧 뗏장 베는 일꾼으로, 새로 일행에 끼어든 자였는데, 그 일에 쓰이는 커다란 하트 모양의 별난 삽을 어깨에 가로질러 메고 있었으니, 잘 갈린 그 날은 불빛을 받아 은빛 활처럼 번득였다.\n\n\"청하기만 했어도 백 명의 처녀가 그한테 갔을걸요,\" 펑퍼짐한 여자가 말했다.\n\n\"이웃, 그 어떤 여자도 도무지 시집가려 하지 않을 남자를 본 적 있소?\" 험프리가 물었다.\n\n\"난 본 적 없네,\" 뗏장 일꾼이 말했다.\n\n\"나도,\" 또 한 사람이 말했다.\n\n\"나도,\" 캔틀 영감이 말했다.\n\n\"음, 그런데, 난 한 번 있었지,\" 티머시 페어웨이가 한쪽 다리에 더욱 힘을 실으며 말했다. \"그런 남자를 하나 알았어. 하지만 딱 한 번뿐이야, 알겠나.\" 그는 마치 사람마다 목소리가 탁해서 오해를 사선 안 될 의무라도 있는 듯, 목구멍을 철저히 한 바퀴 긁어냈다. \"그래, 난 그런 남자를 알았다네,\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그 가엾은 친구가 대체 어떤 끔찍한 허수아비 꼴이었답니까, 페어웨이 어른?\" 뗏장 일꾼이 물었다.\n\n\"음, 그는 귀머거리도 아니고, 벙어리도 아니고, 장님도 아니었어. 그가 무엇이었는지는 말 안 하겠네.\"\n\n\"이 근방에 알려진 사람이오?\" 올리 다우든이 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄,\" 티머시가 말했다. \"하지만 이름은 안 대겠어…… 자, 거기 불 좀 살려놓게, 젊은이들.\"\n\n\"크리스천 캔틀은 대체 이가 왜 덜덜 떨리는 거야?\" 불길 건너편 연기와 어둠 속에서 한 소년이 말했다. \"추워, 크리스천?\"\n\n가늘게 더듬대는 목소리가 답하는 게 들렸다, \"아니, 전혀.\"\n\n\"이리 나와봐, 크리스천, 모습을 좀 보이게. 자네가 여기 있는 줄 몰랐네,\" 페어웨이가 그 방향으로 인정 어린 눈길을 던지며 말했다.\n\n이리 청을 받자, 갈대 같은 머리칼에 어깨라곤 없고, 옷 밖으로 손목과 발목이 잔뜩 삐져나온 머뭇거리는 사내 하나가 제 뜻으로 한두 걸음 나섰고, 다른 이들의 뜻에 떠밀려 대여섯 걸음을 더 나아갔다. 그는 캔틀 영감의 막내아들이었다.\n\n\"뭐가 그리 떨리는 거야, 크리스천?\" 뗏장 일꾼이 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"내가 바로 그 남자예요.\"\n\n\"무슨 남자?\"\n\n\"어떤 여자도 시집 안 가려는 남자요.\"\n\n\"세상에 자네가!\" 티머시 페어웨이가, 크리스천의 온 표면과 그보다 훨씬 더한 데까지 덮을 만큼 눈길을 넓히며 말했고, 그러는 동안 캔틀 영감은 마치 제가 품어 깐 오리 새끼를 멍하니 바라보는 암탉처럼 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"네, 제가 바로 그자예요. 그게 무서워요,\" 크리스천이 말했다. \"그게 절 해칠 것 같아요? 전 늘 신경 안 쓴다고 말하고 그렇게 맹세도 하겠지만, 사실은 내내 신경이 쓰여요.\"\n\n\"음, 빌어먹을, 이런 희한한 일은 내 생전 처음일세,\" 페어웨이 씨가 말했다. \"난 자네를 두고 한 말이 전혀 아니었어. 그럼 이 고장에 또 하나가 있는 거로군! 어쩌자고 제 불운을 까발리는 거야, 크리스천?\"\n\n\"그럴 일이면 그런 거겠지요, 뭐. 저도 어쩔 수 없잖아요, 그렇죠?\" 그는 동심원 무늬가 과녁처럼 둘러친, 가련하리만치 동그란 눈을 그들에게로 돌렸다.\n\n\"그래, 그건 맞아. 하지만 그건 우울한 일이고, 자네가 말할 때 내 피가 얼어붙더라고, 하나뿐인 줄 알았던 자리에 가엾은 친구가 둘이나 있다는 게 느껴졌으니. 자네한텐 안된 일이야, 크리스천. 여자들이 자네를 안 받아주리란 걸 어찌 아나?\"\n\n\"제가 물어봤어요.\"\n\n\"원, 자네한테 그럴 낯이 있을 줄은 몰랐네. 그래, 마지막 여자는 자네한테 뭐랬나? 어쩌면, 따지고 보면 도무지 넘을 수 없는 말은 아니었을지도?\"\n\n\"'내 눈앞에서 꺼져, 이 흐물흐물 비실비실한 반편이 바보야,' 그게 그 여자가 내게 한 말이에요.\"\n\n\"썩 고무적이진 않군, 인정하네,\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"'내 눈앞에서 꺼져, 이 흐물흐물 비실비실한 반편이 바보야'라는 건 싫다는 말치고는 꽤 모진 투지. 하지만 그것조차 세월과 인내로, 그 계집애 머리에 흰머리 몇 가닥 돋게 할 만큼만 견디면 넘을 수 있을지도 몰라. 자네 나이가 몇인가, 크리스천?\"\n\n\"지난 감자 캘 무렵에 서른하나였어요, 페어웨이 어른.\"\n\n\"애송이는 아니군—애송이는 아니야. 그래도 아직 희망은 있어.\"\n\n\"그게 세례로 친 제 나이예요, 교회 제의실에 보관하는 그 심판의 대장에 그렇게 적혀 있으니까요. 그런데 어머니가 그러는데 전 세례받기 얼마 전에 태어났대요.\"\n\n\"아!\"\n\n\"하지만 달이 없었다는 것 말곤, 어머니도 죽어라 해도 언제였는지 못 댔어요.\"\n\n\"달이 없었다—그건 나쁜데. 어이, 이웃들, 저 친구한텐 그게 나쁘구먼!\"\n\n\"그래, 나쁘지,\" 캔틀 영감이 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"어머니는 달이 없었다는 걸 알았어요, 달력 가진 다른 여자한테 물어봤거든요, 사내아이를 낳을 때마다 그랬대요, '달이 없으면 사내도 없다'는 속담 때문에 사내아이를 낳을 때마다 겁이 났던 거죠. 정말로 심각하다고 보세요, 페어웨이 어른, 달이 없었다는 게?\"\n\n\"그럼. '달이 없으면 사내도 없다.' 그건 여태 내뱉은 말 중에 가장 참된 속담 중 하나야. 초승달에 태어난 아이는 결코 뭐가 못 되거든. 자네한테 안된 일이지, 크리스천, 하필 한 달 중에 그런 날에 코빼기를 내밀었으니.\"\n\n\"어른은 태어났을 때 달이 끔찍이도 꽉 찼었나 봐요?\" 크리스천이 가망 없는 흠모의 눈빛으로 페어웨이를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"음, 초승달은 아니었지,\" 페어웨이 씨가 무심한 눈길로 답했다.\n\n\"전 달 없는 사내가 되느니 라마스 절기에 술을 끊는 편을 택하겠어요,\" 크리스천이 똑같이 부서진 읊조림으로 이어갔다. \"전 사내의 허깨비일 뿐이라, 제 핏줄엔 아무짝에도 쓸모없다는 말이 있던데, 그게 그 때문인가 봐요.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 캔틀 영감이 다소 풀 죽은 마음으로 말했다, \"그래도 저 녀석이 사내아이일 적엔 제 어미가 몇십 시간이고 울었지, 저놈이 너무 자라서 군인으로 가버릴까 봐 무서워서 말이야.\"\n\n\"음, 저 친구만큼 딱한 사람이야 많지,\" 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n\"불깐 양도 다른 양들처럼 제 명을 다 살아야지, 가엾은 것.\"\n\n\"그럼 저도 그럭저럭 버텨낼까요? 밤이면 무서워해야 할까요, 페어웨이 어른?\"\n\n\"자넨 평생 혼자 자게 될 거야. 그리고 귀신은 나타날 때 부부한테가 아니라 혼자 자는 이한테 모습을 보이는 법이지. 게다가 요즘 하나가 보였다더군. 아주 이상한 놈이.\"\n\n\"안 돼요—괜찮으시다면 그 얘긴 마세요! 침대에 혼자 누워 그걸 떠올리면 소름이 끼친다고요. 하지만 하실 거죠—아, 하실 거예요, 알아요, 티머시. 그럼 밤새도록 그 꿈을 꿀 거라고요! 아주 이상한 놈이라뇨? 아주 이상한 놈이라고 했을 때 무슨 영을 두고 한 말이에요, 티머시?—아뇨, 아뇨—말하지 마세요.\"\n\n\"나도 귀신을 절반도 안 믿어. 하지만 내가 들은 얘긴—제법 으스스하다고 보네. 그걸 본 건 어린 사내아이였어.\"\n\n\"어떻게 생겼대요?—아뇨, 말하지—\"\n\n\"빨간 놈이야. 그래, 귀신은 대개 흰데, 이건 마치 피에 푹 담갔던 것 같다는 거지.\"\n\n크리스천은 몸을 부풀리지도 못한 채 깊이 숨을 들이켰고, 험프리가 말했다, \"그게 어디서 보였답니까?\"\n\n\"바로 여긴 아니고, 같은 이 히스에서지. 하지만 입에 올릴 만한 일은 아니야. 자네들 생각은 어떤가,\" 페어웨이가 한층 활기찬 어조로, 마치 그 생각이 캔틀 영감의 것이 아니었던 양 그들을 향해 돌아서며 이어갔다—\"잠자리에 들기 전에 오늘 밤 새 부부에게 노래나 한 곡 불러주는 게—그들의 혼인날이니 말이야. 사람들이 막 결혼했을 땐 기뻐해주는 게 좋지, 슬픈 척한들 그들이 갈라지진 않을 테니. 우리가 알다시피 난 술꾼은 아니지만, 아낙네와 젊은것들이 집으로 돌아가고 나면, 콰이엇 우먼으로 슬쩍 건너가, 그 부부 집 문 앞에서 노래나 한 자락 뽑을 수 있잖나. 젊은 색시가 기뻐할 게고, 나도 그러고 싶어, 그 색시가 블룸스엔드에서 고모랑 살 적에 그 손에 술 한 잔 얻어 마신 게 한두 번이 아니거든.\"\n\n\"어? 그럼 그렇게 하세!\" 캔틀 영감이 어찌나 잽싸게 몸을 돌렸는지 구리 인장들이 요란하게 흔들렸다. \"여기 바람 속에 죽치고 있었더니 마른 줄기처럼 바싹 말랐어, 오늘 점심나절 이후로 술 빛깔이라곤 구경도 못 했고. 듣자니 콰이엇 우먼의 마지막 양조주가 아주 맛난 술이라더군. 게다가, 이웃들, 마무리가 좀 늦어진들 어떤가, 뭐, 내일이 일요일이니 자고 풀면 되지?\"\n\n\"캔틀 영감님! 노인네치고 일을 너무 무심히 받아들이시네,\" 펑퍼짐한 여자가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 일을 무심히 받아들이지, 그래—여자들 비위 맞추기엔 너무 무심하지! 클크! 난 약한 노인네 같으면 눈이 빠지게 울 판에도 '명랑한 패거리'든 다른 어떤 노래든 부를 거야. 젠장, 난 뭐든 할 준비가 됐다고.\n\n\"임금님께서 왼쪽 어깨 너머로 돌아보시니, 그 눈초리가 험상궂게 보였네, 마셜 백작이여, 그가 말하길, 내 맹세만 아니었던들 그대는 교수형에 처해졌으리라.\"\n\n\"음, 그럼 그렇게 하지,\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"주님 뜻이라면 그들에게 노래를 한 곡 불러주세. 일이 다 끝난 마당에 토머신의 사촌 클림이 집에 온들 무슨 소용인가? 막고 싶었다면, 제가 그 색시랑 결혼할 셈이었다면, 진작 왔어야지.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 어머니 곁에 한동안 머물러 있으려는 걸지도 모르지, 색시가 떠났으니 이제 외로우실 테니.\"\n\n\"그런데, 참 묘하게도, 난 도무지 외롭다는 느낌을 모르겠어—그래, 전혀,\" 캔틀 영감이 말했다. \"난 밤에도 제독만큼이나 용감하다네!\"\n\n이맘때쯤 모닥불은 사그라들기 시작했으니, 땔감이 불길을 오래 지탱할 만큼 실한 종류가 아니었기 때문이다. 드넓은 지평선 안의 다른 불들 대부분도 시들시들 약해지고 있었다. 그것들의 밝기와 빛깔과 지속 시간을 주의 깊게 살피면 태운 재료의 성질이, 그리고 그를 통해 저마다의 모닥불이 자리한 지역의 자연 산물이 어느 정도 드러났을 것이다. 대다수의 특징이던 그 맑고 제왕다운 광채는 그들의 고장처럼 히스와 가시금작화의 고장을 나타냈으니, 그것은 한 방향으로 끝없이 여러 마일에 걸쳐 뻗어 있었다. 나침반의 다른 지점들에서 보이는 재빠른 화염과 소멸은 가장 가벼운 땔감을—짚과 콩대와 경작지에서 으레 나오는 부스러기를 보여주었다. 그 가운데 가장 오래가는 것은—행성처럼 한결같이 변함없는 눈은—개암나무 가지나 가시나무 다발이나 굵은 장작 같은 목재를 뜻했다. 마지막에 든 재료로 피운 불은 드물었으니, 덧없이 타오르던 불길 곁에서는 규모가 비교적 작았지만, 이제는 그저 오래 지속됨으로써 그것들을 능가하기 시작했다. 거대한 불들은 스러졌으나, 이것들은 남았다. 그것들은 보이는 가장 먼 자리를—북쪽으로, 흙이 다르고 히스가 낯설고 생경한, 풍요로운 잡목림과 조림지 지대에서 하늘을 등지고 솟은 봉우리들을 차지하고 있었다.\n\n하나만 빼고. 그리고 이것은 모든 것 중 가장 가까웠으니, 빛나는 무리 전체의 달이었다. 그것은 저 아래 골짜기의 그 작은 창과는 정확히 반대 방향에 놓여 있었다. 그 가까움이 어찌나 대단했던지, 실제로는 자그마한데도 그 빛이 다른 불들의 빛을 한없이 능가했다.\n\n이 고요한 눈은 이따금 주의를 끌었고, 그들 자신의 불이 사그라들어 어둑해지자 더욱 주의를 끌었으니, 더 최근에 지핀 목재 불들조차 더러 쇠퇴기에 접어들었건만, 여기서는 아무 변화도 감지되지 않았다.\n\n\"참말이지, 저 불이 어찌나 가까운지!\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"보아하니 그래. 누군가 그 둘레를 도는 게 보여. 저 불을 두곤 작지만 좋다고 해야 마땅하지, 분명히.\"\n\n\"제가 거기 돌멩이를 던질 수 있어요,\" 소년이 말했다.\n\n\"나도 던질 수 있지!\" 캔틀 영감이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 못 던져, 얘들아. 저 불은 저렇게 가까워 보여도 거의 일 마일이나 떨어져 있어.\"\n\n\"히스에 난 불인데, 가시금작화는 아니구먼,\" 뗏장 일꾼이 말했다.\n\n\"쪼갠 장작이야, 그게 바로 그거지,\" 티머시 페어웨이가 말했다. \"깨끗한 목재 말곤 저렇게 타는 건 없어. 그리고 저건 미스토버의 노대위 집 앞 언덕마루에 있는 거야. 그 양반은 어찌나 별난 인간인지! 제 둑과 도랑 안에 작은 불을 피워놓고는, 아무도 그걸 즐기거나 가까이 못 오게 한다니까! 게다가 기쁘게 해줄 젊은것들도 없는데 모닥불을 피우다니, 늙은이가 얼마나 멍청한 거야.\"\n\n\"바이 대위는 오늘 오래 산책을 다녀와서 아주 녹초가 됐어,\" 캔틀 영감이 말했다, \"그러니 그 양반일 리는 없겠지.\"\n\n\"게다가 그 양반이 저런 좋은 땔감을 댈 리도 없고,\" 펑퍼짐한 여자가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 손녀가 한 게 분명하지,\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"그 나이 또래가 불을 그리 원할 일이야 없겠지만.\"\n\n\"그 애는 행실이 아주 별나요, 저 위에서 혼자 살면서 그런 걸 좋아하지요,\" 수전이 말했다.\n\n\"제법 잘생긴 처녀지,\" 가시금작화 베는 험프리가 말했다, \"특히 멋들어진 드레스 중 하날 걸쳤을 땐 말이야.\"\n\n\"맞아,\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"음, 그 애 모닥불이야 타고 싶은 대로 타게 두지. 우리 건 보아하니 거의 다 꺼졌구먼.\"\n\n\"불이 사그라드니 이제 어찌나 어두운지!\" 크리스천 캔틀이 토끼 같은 눈으로 뒤를 돌아보며 말했다. \"이만 집으로 가는 게 낫지 않을까요, 이웃들? 이 히스에 귀신이 안 나온다는 건 나도 알지만, 그래도 집에 가는 게 좋겠어요…… 아, 저게 뭐야?\"\n\n\"그냥 바람이야,\" 뗏장 일꾼이 말했다.\n\n\"십일월 오일 행사는 읍내가 아닌 한 밤엔 안 지키는 게 좋아요. 이런 외지고 후미진 데선 낮에 해야 한다고요!\"\n\n\"허튼소리, 크리스천. 사내답게 기운 좀 차려! 수지, 자기, 자네하고 내가 한바탕 춤을 추세—어때, 내 꿀단지?—아직 자네가 얼마나 잘생겼는지 보이지 않을 만큼 깜깜해지기 전에 말이야, 마녀의 아들 같은 자네 남편이 내게서 자네를 낚아채간 뒤로 여러 여름이 지났어도 말이지.\"\n\n이 말은 수전 넌서치에게 건네진 것이었고, 지켜보던 이들이 다음으로 의식한 정황이란 그 아낙의 펑퍼짐한 형체가 불이 지펴졌던 자리를 향해 휙 끌려가는 광경이었다. 그녀는 페어웨이 씨의 팔에 통째로 들려갔으니, 그녀가 그 속셈을 알아채기도 전에 이미 그 팔이 그녀의 허리를 휘감았던 것이다. 불자리는 이제 가시금작화가 죄다 타버려, 붉은 잉걸불과 불티가 점점이 박힌 잿더미의 동그라미일 뿐이었다. 동그라미 안에 들어서자 그는 그녀를 빙글빙글 돌리며 춤을 추었다. 그녀는 요란하게 만들어진 여자였으니, 고래뼈와 나무오리로 둘러친 골조에 더해, 여름이고 겨울이고 비가 오나 마나 장화가 닳는 걸 막으려고 나막신을 신고 다녔던 터라, 페어웨이가 그녀와 함께 펄쩍거리기 시작하자 나막신 딸깍대는 소리와 코르셋 삐걱대는 소리와 그녀의 놀란 비명 소리가 자못 똑똑히 들리는 합주를 이루었다.\n\n\"네 멍청한 대가리를 쪼개버릴 테다, 이 건방진 놈아!\" 넌서치 부인이 말했다.\n그녀는 그와 함께 어쩔 수 없이 빙글빙글 돌며 춤을 추었고, 발은 불티 사이에서 북채처럼 놀았다. \"가시 돋친 가시금작화를 헤치며 걷느라 발목이 가뜩이나 후끈거렸는데, 이제 이 불티로 더 못살게 굴려고 그래요!\"\n\n티머시 페어웨이의 변덕은 전염성이 있었다. 잔디 베는 일꾼이 늙은 올리 다우든을 붙잡고는 좀 더 부드럽게, 그녀와 마찬가지로 푸세트를 추었다. 젊은 사내들도 어른들의 본을 따르는 데 굼뜨지 않아 처녀들을 붙들었으며, 캔틀 영감은 지팡이와 함께 다리 셋 달린 물체 모양으로 나머지 무리 사이에서 깡충거렸고, 채 반 분도 지나지 않아 레인배로 위에 보이는 것이라곤 들끓듯 어지러운 불티 한가운데서 빙빙 도는 검은 형상들뿐이었으니, 불티는 춤추는 이들의 허리께까지 솟구쳐 그들 주위로 뛰어올랐다. 가장 큰 소리는 여인들의 날카로운 비명, 사내들의 웃음, 수전의 코르셋과 나막신 소리, 올리 다우든의 \"헤헤헤!\", 그리고 가시금작화 덤불 위를 두드리는 바람의 가락이었으니, 그것은 그들이 밟는 악마적인 박자에 일종의 곡조를 이루었다. 오직 크리스천만이 떨어져 서서, 불안하게 몸을 흔들며 중얼거리길, \"저러면 안 되는데—저 불티가 어찌나 날아다니는지! 악마를 부르는 짓이야, 저게.\"\n\n\"저게 뭐였지?\" 청년들 중 하나가 멈춰 서며 말했다.\n\n\"어—어디?\" 크리스천이 황급히 나머지 무리 곁으로 바싹 붙으며 말했다.\n\n춤추던 이들이 모두 속도를 늦췄다.\n\n\"네 뒤에서 들렸어, 크리스천—여기 아래에서.\"\n\n\"그래—내 뒤야!\" 크리스천이 말했다. \"마태, 마가, 누가, 요한, 내가 누운 침상에 복을 내리소서, 네 천사가 지키사—\"\n\n\"입 좀 다물어. 저게 뭐야?\" 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n\"호이이이이!\" 어둠 속에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"할로오오오!\" 페어웨이가 응했다.\n\n\"이 위로 블룸스엔드의 요브라이트 부인 댁으로 가는 수레길이 있소?\" 똑같은 목소리가 그들에게 들려왔으니, 길쭉하고 호리호리한 흐릿한 형체가 봉분 쪽으로 다가왔다.\n\n\"이웃들, 시간도 늦었으니 우리 집까지 힘껏 달려가야 하지 않을까?\" 크리스천이 말했다. \"서로한테서 달아나자는 게 아니라, 알다시피, 바싹 붙어서 같이 달리자는 거야.\"\n\n\"흩어진 가시금작화나 몇 줌 긁어모아 불을 피워서 저자가 누군지 보자고.\" 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n불길이 일어나자 그것은 몸에 딱 붙는 옷을 입고 머리끝부터 발끝까지 붉은 젊은이를 드러냈다. \"이 위로 요브라이트 부인 댁으로 가는 길이 있소?\" 그가 되물었다.\n\n\"있지—저 아래 길을 따라 쭉 가시오.\"\n\n\"말 두 마리와 짐마차가 지나갈 길 말이오?\"\n\n\"음, 그래요, 시간을 들이면 여기 아래 골짜기로 올라갈 수 있소. 길은 험하지만 등불이 있으면 말들이 조심조심 더듬어 갈 수 있을 거요. 수레는 멀리까지 끌고 왔소, 레들맨* 양반?\"\n\n\"바닥에 두고 왔소, 한 반 마일쯤 뒤에. 밤인 데다 오랫동안 와 본 적이 없어서 길을 확인하려고 앞서 걸어왔소.\"\n\n\"오, 음, 올라갈 수 있을 거요.\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"저자를 봤을 때 어찌나 놀랐던지!\" 그가 레들맨까지 포함한 무리 전체에게 덧붙였다. \"주여, 도대체 이 무슨 불 같은 허수아비가 우리를 괴롭히려고 왔나 했지. 당신 생김새를 깎아내리려는 건 아니오, 레들맨, 바탕은 못나지 않았으니까, 마무리가 좀 괴상하긴 해도. 그저 내가 얼마나 야릇한 기분이 들었는지 말하려는 거요. 악마이거나 아니면 저 아이가 말하던 붉은 유령이 아닌가 반쯤 생각했소.\"\n\n\"나도 놀랐어요.\" 수전 넌석이 말했다. \"어젯밤에 해골 꿈을 꿨거든요.\"\n\n\"그 얘긴 더 하지 마.\" 크리스천이 말했다. \"머리에 손수건을 둘렀다면 영락없이 「유혹」 그림 속 악마처럼 보일 거야.\"\n\n\"음, 말해 줘서 고맙소.\" 젊은 레들맨이 희미하게 웃으며 말했다. \"그럼 다들 안녕히.\"\n\n그는 봉분 아래로 내려가 그들의 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n\"저 젊은이 얼굴을 전에 본 적이 있는 것 같은데.\" 험프리가 말했다. \"하지만 어디서, 어떻게, 이름이 뭔지는 모르겠어.\"\n\n레들맨이 떠난 지 몇 분도 채 지나지 않아 또 다른 사람이 어느 정도 되살아난 모닥불 쪽으로 다가왔다. 그 사람은 이 인근에서 잘 알려지고 존경받는 과부로 드러났으니, 그 지위는 오직 '품위 있는'이라는 말로밖에는 표현할 수 없는 것이었다. 그 얼굴은 물러나는 황야의 어둠에 둘러싸여, 반쯤의 그늘도 없이 카메오처럼 하얗게 떠올랐다.\n\n그녀는 중년의 여인으로, 통찰력이 안에 군림하는 으뜸가는 자질일 때 흔히 보이는 유형의 단정한 이목구비를 지녔다. 이따금 그녀는 주위의 다른 이들에게는 허락되지 않은 느보산에서 사안들을 굽어보는 듯했다. 그녀에게는 어딘가 동떨어진 기색이 있었으니, 황야에서 뿜어 나오는 고독이 그 황야로부터 솟아오른 이 얼굴 안에 응축되어 있었다. 그녀가 황야 사람들을 바라보는 태도는 그들의 존재에 대한, 또는 이런 시각에 그토록 외진 곳을 거니는 자신에 대한 그들의 의견이 무엇이든 그것에 대한 모종의 무관심을 드러냈으니, 그리하여 그녀가 어떤 면에서든 그들의 수준에 못 미친다는 것을 간접적으로 시사했다. 그 까닭은 비록 그녀의 남편은 영세한 농부였으나 그녀 자신은 보좌신부의 딸로서 한때 더 나은 것을 이루기를 꿈꾸었던 사실에 있었다.\n\n성품에 무게가 있는 사람은 행성처럼 자신의 궤도를 따라 제 대기를 함께 끌고 다니거니와, 이제 무대에 등장한 이 부인은 자신의 분위기를 좌중에 들여올 수 있었고, 또 으레 그렇게 했다. 황야 사람들 사이에서 그녀가 보이는 평소의 태도에는 우월한 화술의 자각에서 비롯되는 그 과묵함이 있었다. 그러나 어둠 속을 외로이 헤맨 끝에 사람들과 빛 속으로 들어왔을 때의 효과는, 들어온 이에게 평소보다 한층 높아진 사교성으로 나타나, 말보다도 오히려 표정에 더욱 드러났다.\n\n\"아니, 요브라이트 부인이시군.\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"요브라이트 부인, 십 분도 채 안 됐는데 한 사내가 여기 와서 부인을 찾았소—레들맨이오.\"\n\n\"그자가 뭘 원했나요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"우리한테는 말 안 했소.\"\n\n\"뭔가 팔 게 있나 보지요. 그게 뭘 수 있을지는 도무지 모르겠네요.\"\n\n\"댁의 아드님 클림 도련님이 크리스마스에 집에 온다니 반갑습니다, 부인.\" 잔디 베는 일꾼 샘이 말했다. \"모닥불이라면 사족을 못 쓰던 녀석이었지요!\"\n\n\"그래요. 오는 모양이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"지금쯤이면 훌륭한 청년이 됐겠지요.\" 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n\"이제 어른이지요.\" 그녀가 조용히 대답했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 황야에서 부인 혼자라니 무척 적적하시겠소, 부인.\" 크리스천이 그때껏 지키던 은둔에서 나오며 말했다. \"길 잃지 않게 조심하시오. 에그던 황야는 길 잃기 나쁜 곳이고, 오늘 밤 바람은 내가 여태 들어 본 어느 때보다 괴상하게 윙윙거리오. 에그던을 가장 잘 아는 이들도 더러 여기서 도깨비에 홀려 길을 헤맸소.\"\n\n\"자네 크리스천인가?\" 요브라이트 부인이 말했다. \"왜 내게서 숨어 있었나?\"\n\n\"이 불빛에 부인인 줄 몰라서요, 부인. 게다가 워낙 우울하게 생겨먹은 사람이라 조금 겁이 났을 뿐입니다. 제가 마음속으로 얼마나 끔찍하게 가라앉는지 보신다면, 제가 제 손으로 죽지나 않을까 부인도 무척 마음 졸이실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"자네는 아비를 닮지 않았군.\" 요브라이트 부인이 불 쪽을 바라보며 말했으니, 그곳에서는 캔틀 영감이 독창성이라곤 없이 앞서 다른 이들이 그랬듯 불티 사이에서 혼자 춤추고 있었다.\n\n\"이봐요, 영감,\" 티머시 페어웨이가 말했다. \"우리가 다 부끄럽소. 영감처럼 점잖은 늙은 가장이—하루를 더 잡아도 일흔인 양반이—혼자서 그렇게 혼파이프나 추다니!\"\n\n\"진을 빼는 영감이지요, 요브라이트 부인.\" 크리스천이 풀 죽어 말했다. \"도망칠 수만 있다면 저렇게 장난기 많은 양반하고는 일주일도 같이 못 살겠어요.\"\n\n\"가만히 서서 요브라이트 부인을 맞이하는 게 더 점잖은 처신이지요, 게다가 여기서 제일 어른이신 캔틀 영감 아니세요.\" 빗자루 만드는 여인이 말했다.\n\n\"암, 그렇고말고.\" 흥에 겨운 영감이 뉘우치며 자제하면서 말했다. \"내가 워낙 기억력이 나빠서요, 요브라이트 부인, 나머지 사람들이 나를 얼마나 우러러보는지 깜빡한다오. 내 기운이 굉장히 좋다고 하실 테지요? 하지만 늘 그런 건 아니오. 우두머리로 우러름받는다는 건 사내에게 짐이 되는 거라, 나도 종종 그걸 느낀다오.\"\n\n\"이야기를 끊어서 미안하네요.\" 요브라이트 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 이제 가 봐야겠어요. 앵글베리 큰길을 따라 내려가던 참이었는데, 오늘 밤 남편과 함께 돌아오는 조카딸의 새집으로 가는 길이었지요. 모닥불이 보이고 다른 이들 틈에서 올리의 목소리가 들리기에 무슨 일인가 알아보려고 여기 올라왔어요. 조카가 가는 길이 내 길과 같으니 같이 걷고 싶어서요.\"\n\n\"네, 그럼요, 부인, 저도 막 움직일 참이었어요.\" 올리가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 부인은 내가 말한 그 레들맨을 틀림없이 만나겠소.\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"수레를 가지러 막 돌아갔을 뿐이오. 부인의 조카딸과 그 남편이 혼인하는 대로 곧장 집으로 온다는 얘길 들었는데, 우리도 곧 그리로 내려가서 환영의 노래를 한 곡 불러 줄 참이오.\"\n\n\"정말 고마워요.\" 요브라이트 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 우리는 부인이 긴 옷자락으로 가는 것보다 가시금작화 사이로 지름길을 질러갈 테니, 기다리시게 폐를 끼치진 않겠소.\"\n\n\"좋아요—준비됐나, 올리?\"\n\n\"네, 부인. 그리고 봐요, 댁의 조카딸 창문에서 불빛이 비치네요. 우리가 길을 잃지 않게 도와줄 거예요.\"\n\n그녀는 페어웨이가 가리켰던, 골짜기 바닥의 희미한 불빛을 가리켰고, 두 여인은 봉분을 내려갔다.\n\n아래로, 아래로 그들은 내려갔고, 더욱 아래로 내려갔으니—한 걸음 한 걸음 내려갈 때마다 하강이 전진보다 더 큰 듯했다. 그들의 치맛자락은 가시금작화에 요란하게 긁혔고, 어깨는 고사리에 스쳤으니, 고사리는 죽어 말랐어도 살아 있을 때처럼 꼿꼿이 서 있었던바, 아직 그것들을 쓰러뜨릴 만한 겨울 날씨가 충분히 닥치지 않은 까닭이었다. 어떤 이들은 시중드는 이 없는 두 여인에게 이 지옥 같은 처지가 무모한 것이라 했을 법도 하다. 그러나 이 텁수룩한 후미진 곳은 사철 올리와 요브라이트 부인에게 익숙한 둘레였고, 어둠이 더해진들 벗의 얼굴에 무서움을 보태지는 않는 법이다.\n\n\"그래서 태신이 결국 그자와 결혼했네요.\" 비탈이 한결 완만해져 발걸음에 온 신경을 쏟지 않아도 되자 올리가 말했다.\n\n요브라이트 부인이 천천히 대답했다. \"그래, 결국.\"\n\n\"얼마나 허전하시겠어요—늘 그래 왔듯 딸처럼 함께 살았으니.\"\n\n\"허전하지.\"\n\n올리는 발언이 때에 맞지 않을 때를 알아챌 만한 눈치는 없었으나, 바로 그 순박함 덕분에 그것이 불쾌하게 들리는 일은 면했다. 다른 이들에게서라면 노여움을 살 만한 물음도 그녀는 탈 없이 던질 수 있었다. 명백히 아픈 화제를 다시 들추는데도 요브라이트 부인이 묵인한 것은 그 때문이었다.\n\n\"부인이 거기 동의하셨단 얘길 듣고 정말 깜짝 놀랐어요, 부인, 정말이지.\" 빗자루 만드는 여인이 이어 말했다.\n\n\"작년 이맘때였다면 나도 자네 못지않게 놀랐을 거야, 올리. 그 결혼에는 여러 면이 있어. 다 말하려 해도 자네한테 전부 말할 순 없네.\"\n\n\"저도 그자가 부인 집안과 짝이 되기엔 어딘가 미덥지 못하다 싶었어요. 여관을 친다는 게—그게 뭐예요? 하지만 영리한 건 사실이고, 한때는 공학 신사였다는데 너무 겉치레에 빠져서 영락했다더군요.\"\n\n\"전체로 보아, 그 애가 바라는 곳으로 시집가는 게 낫겠다고 봤지.\"\n\n\"가엾은 것, 분명 감정에 휘둘렸겠지요. 그게 인지상정이에요. 음, 사람들이 그자를 뭐라 부르든—여기 황야 땅 몇 에이커를 일궈 놓은 데다 여관도 있고, 황야 망아지들도 있고, 처신도 영락없는 신사 같던걸요. 그리고 이미 한 일은 돌이킬 수 없는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"돌이킬 수 없지.\" 요브라이트 부인이 말했다. \"봐, 드디어 수레길이네. 이제 좀 수월히 가겠어.\"\n\n결혼 화제는 더 이상 거론되지 않았고, 이내 희미하게 갈라지는 샛길에 이르러 그들은 헤어졌으니, 올리는 먼저 자기 동행에게, 와일디브가 혼인을 기념해 약속한 포도주 한 병을 자기 병든 남편에게 보내지 않았다는 걸 일러 달라고 청했다. 빗자루 만드는 여인은 언덕 한 자락 뒤편 자기 집을 향해 왼쪽으로 꺾었고, 요브라이트 부인은 곧은 길을 따라갔으니, 그 길은 더 가서 콰이어트 우먼 여관 곁의 큰길과 합쳐졌는바, 그녀의 조카딸이 그날 앵글베리에서의 혼례를 마치고 와일디브와 함께 그리로 돌아왔으리라 짐작한 곳이었다.\n\n그녀는 먼저, 흔히 일컫는 대로 와일디브의 땅뙈기에 이르렀으니, 황야에서 일궈 낸 한 뙈기로, 길고 고된 세월 끝에 경작지로 만들어진 곳이었다. 그것이 갈아 일굴 수 있음을 발견한 사내는 그 노동으로 죽었고, 그 뒤를 이어 그 땅을 소유한 사내는 거기 거름을 대다가 망했다. 와일디브는 아메리고 베스푸치처럼 와서, 앞서 간 이들에게 돌아갔어야 할 영예를 받았다.\n\n요브라이트 부인이 여관에 가까이 다가가 막 들어서려 할 때, 그녀는 여관에서 이백 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에서 말과 수레가 자기 쪽으로 다가오고, 한 사내가 손에 등불을 들고 그 곁을 걷는 것을 보았다. 이내 그 사내가 자기를 찾던 그 레들맨임이 분명해졌다. 그녀는 여관으로 곧장 들어가는 대신 그것을 지나쳐 수레 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n수레가 가까이 다가왔고, 사내가 그녀를 거의 알아보지 못한 채 지나치려는데, 그녀가 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"나를 찾으셨다지요? 나는 블룸스엔드의 요브라이트 부인이에요.\"\n\n레들맨이 흠칫하더니 손가락을 들어 보였다. 그는 말을 세우고 그녀에게 몇 야드 옆으로 함께 물러나자고 손짓했으며, 그녀는 의아해하면서도 그렇게 했다.\n\n\"저를 모르시겠지요, 부인?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"몰라요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아니, 알겠어요! 젊은 베너로군요—자네 아버지가 여기 어디서 낙농을 했지요?\"\n\n\"네, 그리고 댁의 조카딸 태신 아가씨도 좀 알았습니다. 안 좋은 소식을 전해 드려야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그 애에 대해—설마! 그 애는 방금 남편과 함께 집에 온 줄 아는데. 오늘 오후에 돌아오기로 했어요—여기 너머 여관으로.\"\n\n\"거기 없습니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게 아나요?\"\n\n\"여기 있으니까요. 제 수레 안에 있습니다.\" 그가 천천히 덧붙였다.\n\n\"무슨 새 화가 닥쳤단 말인가?\" 요브라이트 부인이 손으로 눈을 가리며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"자세히는 설명 못 드립니다, 부인. 제가 아는 건 그저, 오늘 아침 길을 따라가는데, 앵글베리에서 한 일 마일쯤 벗어났을 때, 뭔가 암사슴처럼 제 뒤를 종종걸음으로 따라오는 소리가 들려서 돌아보니 그 아가씨가 거기 있었습니다, 죽음 그 자체처럼 새하얗게요. '오, 디고리 베너!' 하더니, '당신인 줄 알았어요—저를 도와주시겠어요? 곤경에 처했어요.' 하더군요.\"\n\n\"그 애가 자네 세례명을 어떻게 알았지?\" 요브라이트 부인이 미심쩍어하며 말했다.\n\n\"이 장사를 하러 떠나기 전, 소년 시절에 그 아가씨를 만난 적이 있습니다. 그때 태워 줄 수 있겠느냐 묻더니, 그러고는 까무러쳐 쓰러졌습니다. 제가 안아 올려 안에 태웠고, 그때부터 줄곧 거기 있었습니다. 많이 울었지만 말은 거의 안 했고, 한 얘기라곤 오늘 아침 결혼하기로 되어 있었다는 것뿐이었습니다. 뭐라도 먹이려 했지만 못 먹더니, 끝내 잠이 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"당장 그 애를 봐야겠어요.\" 요브라이트 부인이 수레 쪽으로 서둘러 가며 말했다.\n\n레들맨이 등불을 들고 뒤따랐고, 먼저 올라서서 요브라이트 부인이 자기 곁으로 오르도록 거들었다. 문이 열리자 그녀는 수레 끝에 임시로 마련한 침상을 보았으니, 그 둘레에는 레들맨이 가진 천이란 천은 다 걸어 놓은 듯했는데, 그 작은 침상에 누운 이가 그의 장사 밑천인 붉은 재료에 닿지 않게 하려는 것이었다. 한 젊은 처녀가 거기 외투를 덮고 누워 있었다. 그녀는 잠들어 있었고, 등불의 빛이 그 이목구비에 떨어졌다.\n\n물결치는 밤색 머리칼의 둥지 속에 누인, 곱고 사랑스럽고 정직한 시골 얼굴이 드러났다. 그것은 예쁨과 아름다움 사이쯤이었다. 두 눈은 감겨 있었으나, 그 둘레의 빛나는 솜씨가 절정에 이른 양 그 눈에 응당 빛이 어렸으리라는 것은 쉬이 상상할 수 있었다. 그 얼굴의 바탕은 희망이었으나, 이제 그 위에는 마치 이물질처럼 불안과 슬픔의 막이 덮여 있었다. 슬픔이 거기 깃든 지 얼마 되지 않아 발그레함은 조금도 앗아 가지 못했고, 다만 끝내 무너뜨릴지도 모를 것에 아직은 위엄을 더해 주었을 뿐이었다. 그 입술의 진홍빛은 옅어질 새가 없었으니, 곁에 있던, 더 덧없는 뺨의 빛깔이 사라진 지금은 한층 짙어 보였다. 입술은 자주 벌어지며 말을 중얼거렸다. 그녀는 마드리갈에 어울리는 사람 같았으니—운율과 화음을 통해 바라보아야 할 듯했다.\n\n적어도 한 가지는 분명했다. 그녀는 이렇게 바라보이도록 만들어진 사람이 아니었다. 레들맨도 그만큼은 의식한 듯했으니, 요브라이트 부인이 그녀를 들여다보는 동안 그는 그에게 썩 어울리는 섬세함으로 눈길을 돌렸다. 잠든 이도 그리 여긴 듯했으니, 다음 순간 그녀가 제 눈을 떴기 때문이다.\n\n그러자 입술이 어딘가 기대를 머금고, 더욱이는 의혹을 머금고 벌어졌으며, 그녀의 여러 생각과 생각의 조각들은, 얼굴 위 변화로 신호되듯, 빛에 의해 더없이 정교하게 드러났다. 마치 그녀 존재의 흐름이 그 안을 지나가는 것이 보이기라도 하듯, 꾸밈없고 투명한 생명이 드러났다. 그녀는 단번에 상황을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"오 그래요, 저예요, 이모.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"이모가 얼마나 놀라셨는지, 얼마나 믿기지 않으실지 알아요. 하지만 그래도 이렇게 집에 돌아온 건 저예요!\"\n\n\"태신, 태신!\" 요브라이트 부인이 젊은 여인 위로 몸을 굽혀 입맞추며 말했다. \"오 가엾은 것!\"\n\n토머신은 이제 막 흐느낌이 터질 듯했으나, 뜻밖의 자제력으로 아무 소리도 내지 않았다. 부드럽게 가쁜 숨을 쉬며 그녀는 똑바로 일어나 앉았다.\n\n\"제가 이런 꼴인 줄 몰랐던 만큼이나 이모도 모르셨겠지요.\" 그녀가 빠르게 말을 이었다. \"여기가 어디예요, 이모?\"\n\n\"거의 다 왔다, 얘야. 에그던 골짜기야. 도대체 무슨 끔찍한 일이니?\"\n\n\"곧 말씀드릴게요. 이렇게 가깝나요? 그럼 내려서 걸을래요. 오솔길로 집에 가고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 이렇게 애써 준 이 친절한 분이 우리 집까지 곧장 데려다주실 거다, 분명히.\" 이모가 레들맨을 향해 돌아서며 말했으니, 그는 처녀가 깨어나자 수레 앞쪽에서 물러나 길에 서 있었다.\n\n\"어찌 제게 그걸 물으셔야 한다고 여기십니까? 물론 그래야지요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 친절한 분이에요.\" 토머신이 중얼거렸다. \"한때 알던 분이에요, 이모, 오늘 이분을 보고 낯선 이의 어떤 탈것보다 이분 수레가 낫겠다 싶었어요. 하지만 이제 걸을래요. 레들맨, 말 좀 세워 주세요.\"\n\n사내는 다정한 망설임으로 그녀를 바라보았으나 말을 세웠다.\n\n그러자 이모와 조카딸이 수레에서 내렸고, 요브라이트 부인이 그 주인에게 말했다. \"이제 자네를 완전히 알아보겠네. 무엇 때문에 아버지가 물려준 그 좋은 장사를 그만두고 바꿨나?\"\n\n\"음, 그냥 그랬습니다.\" 그가 말하고는 토머신을 바라보았으니, 그녀는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"그럼 오늘 밤은 더 제가 필요치 않으시겠지요, 부인?\"\n\n요브라이트 부인이 어두운 하늘과, 언덕들과, 사그라드는 모닥불들과, 그들이 다가온 여관의 불 켜진 창을 둘러보았다. \"필요치 않겠네.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"토머신이 걷고 싶다 하니. 우리는 곧 오솔길을 올라 집에 닿을 수 있어—길을 잘 아니까.\"\n\n그러고는 몇 마디를 더 나눈 뒤 헤어졌으니, 레들맨은 수레를 끌고 앞으로 나아갔고, 두 여인은 길에 선 채로 남았다. 그 탈것과 모는 이가 그녀의 목소리가 닿을 수 있는 거리에서 멀찍이 물러나자마자, 요브라이트 부인은 조카딸을 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"자, 토머신,\" 그녀가 엄하게 말했다. \"이 망신스러운 사달이 도대체 무슨 영문이니?\"\n\n토머신은 이모의 달라진 태도에 완전히 압도된 듯 보였다. \"보이는 그대로예요. 저는—혼인하지 못했어요.\" 그녀가 힘없이 대답했다. \"용서하세요—이런 불상사로 이모를 욕보여서—죄송해요. 하지만 어쩔 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"나라니? 네 생각부터 먼저 해라.\"\n\n\"누구의 잘못도 아니었어요. 거기 도착했더니 목사님이 혼인 허가증의 사소한 하자 때문에 우리를 혼인시켜 주지 않으려 했어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 하자?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요. 와일디브 씨가 설명할 수 있어요. 오늘 아침 떠날 때는 이렇게 돌아오리라곤 생각도 못 했어요.\" 어두웠기에 토머신은 소리 없는 눈물로 감정을 흘려보낼 수 있었으니, 눈물이 남몰래 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"꼴좋다고 하고 싶을 지경이다—네가 이런 꼴을 당할 만하지 않다고 느끼지만 않는다면.\" 요브라이트 부인이 이어 말했으니, 그녀는 서로 바싹 맞붙은 두 가지 뚜렷한 기분, 곧 부드러운 기분과 노여운 기분을 지녀, 아무런 예고도 없이 하나에서 다른 하나로 옮겨 다녔다. \"기억해라, 토머신, 이 일은 내가 원해서 된 게 아니야. 네가 그 사내에게 어리석은 마음을 품기 시작한 맨 처음부터, 나는 그자가 너를 행복하게 해 주지 못하리라 경고했어. 어찌나 강하게 그리 느꼈던지, 나로서는 도저히 할 수 있으리라 믿지 못했을 일을 했지—교회에서 일어서서 몇 주씩이나 사람들 입길에 오르내렸어. 하지만 일단 동의한 이상, 충분한 이유 없이는 이런 변덕에 굴하지 않는다. 이렇게 된 마당에 너는 그자와 혼인해야 해.\"\n\n\"제가 단 한순간이라도 달리하고 싶어 한다고 생각하세요?\" 토머신이 무거운 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"그이를 사랑한 게 얼마나 잘못이었는지 알아요, 하지만 그렇게 말씀하셔서 저를 아프게 하지 마세요, 이모! 제가 거기서 그이와 함께 머물기를 바라진 않으셨을 거잖아요?—그리고 이모 집이 제가 돌아갈 유일한 보금자리예요. 그이는 하루 이틀이면 혼인할 수 있다고 했어요.\"\n\n\"그자가 너를 만난 적이 없었더라면.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 그럼 저는 세상에서 가장 비참한 여자가 되어, 그이가 다시는 저를 못 보게 할래요. 아니, 저 그이 싫어요!\"\n\n\"그렇게 말하기엔 너무 늦었다. 나랑 같이 가자. 여관에 가서 그자가 돌아왔는지 보겠어. 물론 이 이야기의 진상을 당장 캐낼 거야. 와일디브 씨가 나나 내 식구에게 농간을 부릴 수 있다고 여겨선 안 되지.\"\n\n\"그게 아니었어요. 허가증이 잘못됐고, 그이는 같은 날 다른 걸 구할 수 없었어요. 오면, 어찌 된 일인지 그이가 당장 말씀드릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"왜 너를 다시 데려오지 않았지?\"\n\n\"그건 저 때문이었어요!\" 토머신이 다시 흐느꼈다. \"혼인할 수 없다는 걸 알고 나니 그이와 함께 돌아오기가 싫었고, 저는 몹시 아팠어요. 그때 디고리 베너를 만났고, 그를 만나 집에 데려다 달라 할 수 있어 다행이었어요. 더는 잘 설명 못 하겠어요, 화내고 싶으시면 화내셔도 돼요.\"\n\n\"그건 두고 봐야지.\" 요브라이트 부인이 말했고, 그들은 인근에서 콰이어트 우먼이라 알려진 여관 쪽으로 돌아섰으니, 그 간판은 제 머리를 팔에 끼고 있는 부인의 모습을 그린 것이었다. 그 집 정면은 황야와 레인배로를 향해 있었고, 레인배로의 어두운 형상은 하늘에서 그 집을 위협하는 듯했다. 문에는 손길이 닿지 않은 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있었으니, \"공학자 와일디브 씨\"라는 뜻밖의 글귀가 새겨져 있었던바—그것은 그에게 큰 기대를 걸었다가 실망한 이들이 버드머스의 한 사무소에서 그를 그 직업에 입문시켰던 시절로부터 남은, 쓸모는 없으나 소중히 간직된 유물이었다. 정원은 뒤편에 있었고, 그 뒤로는 잔잔하고 깊은 시내가 흘러, 그 방향으로 황야의 가장자리를 이루었으며, 시내 너머로는 목초지가 나타났다.\n\n그러나 짙은 어둠은 지금 어떤 풍경이든 능선만을 보이게 허락할 뿐이었다. 집 뒤편의 물소리가 들렸으니, 양쪽 둑을 따라 울타리를 이룬, 깃털 같은 머리를 인 마른 갈대 줄기 사이를 기어가며 한가로이 소용돌이를 자아내는 소리였다. 그것들의 존재는 마치 회중이 겸손히 기도하는 듯한 소리로 알려졌다.\n\n모닥불 무리의 눈에 골짜기 위로 촛불빛을 비추던 그 창은 커튼이 쳐 있지 않았으나, 창턱이 너무 높아 바깥의 보행자가 그 너머로 방 안을 들여다볼 수는 없었다. 사내의 윤곽 일부를 어렴풋이 더듬을 수 있는 거대한 그림자가 천장의 절반을 뒤덮고 있었다.\n\n\"집에 있는 모양이다.\" 요브라이트 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"저도 들어가야 하나요, 이모?\" 토머신이 힘없이 물었다. \"안 그래야겠지요. 그건 옳지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"넌 꼭 들어와야 해—그자와 맞서서, 그자가 내게 거짓을 둘러대지 못하게 말이다. 집 안에 오 분도 머물지 않을 테니, 그러고 나서 집으로 걸어가자.\"\n\n열린 통로로 들어서서, 그녀는 사실私室의 문을 두드리고, 빗장을 풀고, 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n한 사내의 등과 어깨가 요브라이트 부인의 눈과 불 사이를 가로막았다. 그 형체의 주인인 와일디브가 곧장 돌아서서 일어나 방문객들을 맞으러 나아갔다.\n\n그는 아주 젊은 사내였고, 형태와 동작이라는 두 속성 중에 그에게서 먼저 눈길을 끄는 것은 후자였다. 그 동작의 우아함은 남달랐으니—그것은 여인을 후리는 이력의 무언극적 표현이었다. 다음으로 눈에 띈 것은 더 물질적인 자질들이었으니, 그중에는 얼굴 윗부분 위로 드리운 무성한 머리털이 있어, 초기 고딕 방패의 높은 모서리 윤곽을 그의 이마에 빌려주었고, 또 원통처럼 매끄럽고 둥근 목이 있었다. 그 몸의 아랫부분은 가냘픈 골격이었다. 통틀어 그는 어떤 사내도 감탄할 만한 것을 찾지 못할 사람이었으나, 어떤 여인도 싫어할 만한 것을 찾지 못할 사람이었다.\n\n그는 통로에 선 젊은 처녀의 형체를 알아보고 말했다. \"그래, 토머신은 집에 닿았군. 어쩌면 그렇게 나를 두고 가 버렸소, 여보?\" 그러고는 요브라이트 부인을 향해 돌아서며—\"저 사람과 입씨름해 봐야 소용없었습니다. 가겠다고, 그것도 혼자 가겠다고 하니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 이게 다 무슨 영문이지요?\" 요브라이트 부인이 도도하게 따져 물었다.\n\n\"앉으시지요.\" 와일디브가 두 여인에게 의자를 놓아 주며 말했다. \"음, 아주 멍청한 착오였습니다만, 그런 착오야 일어나기 마련이지요. 앵글베리에서는 그 허가증이 쓸모없었습니다. 버드머스용으로 발급된 것이었는데, 제가 읽어 보질 않아 그걸 몰랐던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 자네는 앵글베리에 머물고 있지 않았나?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 저는 버드머스에 있었지요—이틀 전까지요—그리고 거기서 저 사람과 혼인할 작정이었습니다. 그런데 데리러 갔을 때 우리는 앵글베리로 정했지요, 새 허가증이 필요하리라는 걸 잊고서요. 그 뒤에 버드머스까지 갈 시간이 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"내 보기에 자네 잘못이 아주 크네.\" 요브라이트 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"앵글베리를 고른 건 전적으로 제 잘못이에요.\" 토머신이 변호했다. \"거기선 제가 알려져 있지 않아서 제가 그러자고 했어요.\"\n\n\"제 잘못이라는 걸 너무나 잘 알고 있으니 일깨워 주실 필요 없습니다.\" 와일디브가 퉁명스레 대답했다.\n\n\"이런 일은 까닭 없이 일어나지 않아.\" 이모가 말했다. \"이건 나와 내 집안에 대한 큰 모욕이고, 이게 알려지면 우리에게 아주 거북한 시간이 닥칠 거야. 그 애가 내일 어떻게 벗들의 얼굴을 마주하겠나? 이건 아주 큰 해이고, 내가 쉬이 용서할 수 없는 거야. 그 애의 평판에까지 누가 될지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"당치 않습니다.\" 와일디브가 말했다.\n\n이 논쟁 내내 토머신의 큰 눈은 한 사람의 얼굴에서 다른 사람의 얼굴로 옮겨 다녔고, 이제 그녀가 불안하게 말했다. \"이모, 데이먼하고 단둘이 오 분만 이야기하게 해 주시겠어요? 그래 주겠어요, 데이먼?\"\n\n\"물론이지, 여보.\" 와일디브가 말했다. \"이모님께서 양해해 주신다면.\" 그는 그녀를 옆방으로 데려가고, 요브라이트 부인을 불가에 남겨 두었다.\n\n둘만 남고 문이 닫히자마자, 토머신은 창백하고 눈물 어린 얼굴을 그에게 들어 올리며 말했다. \"이게 저를 죽이고 있어요, 데이먼! 오늘 아침 앵글베리에서 화내며 당신과 헤어지려던 게 아니었어요. 하지만 겁이 나서 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 몰랐어요. 오늘 제가 얼마나 괴로웠는지 이모께는 알리지 않았어요. 얼굴과 목소리를 다잡고, 제게 별일 아닌 양 미소 짓기가 너무 힘들지만, 그래도 그러려 애써요, 이모가 당신에게 더 분개하지 않으시도록요. 당신이 어쩔 수 없었다는 걸 알아요, 여보, 이모가 무슨 생각을 하시든.\"\n\n\"이모님은 아주 불쾌한 분이군.\"\n\n\"네.\" 토머신이 중얼거렸다. \"그리고 이제 저도 그렇게 보이겠지요…… 데이먼, 저를 어떻게 하실 작정이에요?\"\n\n\"당신을 어떻게 하느냐고?\"\n\n\"네. 당신을 좋아하지 않는 이들이 수군대는 말들이 때때로 당신을 의심하게 만들어요. 우리 혼인할 거지요, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"물론 그래야지. 월요일에 버드머스로 가기만 하면, 당장 혼인하는 거야.\"\n\n\"그럼 가요!—오 데이먼, 당신이 저더러 이런 말을 하게 만드네요!\" 그녀는 손수건에 얼굴을 묻었다. \"여기 제가 당신에게 혼인해 달라 청하고 있어요, 본래대로라면 당신이 무릎 꿇고 잔인한 연인인 제게 거절하지 말아 달라 애원하며, 제가 거절하면 가슴이 미어질 거라 말해야 마땅한데. 전엔 그런 게 예쁘고 달콤하리라 생각했어요. 한데 어쩜 이리 다른지!\"\n\n\"그래, 진짜 삶은 결코 그런 것과는 다르지.\"\n\n\"하지만 저 개인으로는 그 일이 영영 이루어지지 않아도 상관없어요.\" 그녀가 약간의 위엄을 담아 덧붙였다. \"그래요, 저는 당신 없이도 살 수 있어요. 제가 생각하는 건 이모예요. 이모는 워낙 자존심이 강하고 집안의 체면을 무척 중히 여기셔서, 이 이야기가—마무리되기 전에—세상에 퍼지면 굴욕으로 무너지실 거예요. 제 사촌 클림도 몹시 상처받을 거고요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그는 아주 비합리적인 사람이군. 사실, 당신들은 다들 좀 비합리적이야.\"\n\n토머신이 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔으나, 사랑 때문은 아니었다. 그러나 그녀에게 그 홍조를 일으킨 순간의 감정이 무엇이었든, 그것은 온 것처럼 가 버렸고, 그녀는 겸허하게 말했다. \"어쩔 수만 있다면 결코 그러려는 게 아니에요. 그저 당신이 마침내 어느 정도 제 이모를 손아귀에 쥐었다는 걸 느낄 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"공정함으로 따지자면 그건 거의 제게 주어질 만한 거지.\" 와일디브가 말했다. \"제가 이모님 승낙을 얻으려고 무얼 겪었는지 생각해 봐요. 결혼 예고가 금지당하는 게 어떤 사내에게든 얼마나 모욕인지—나처럼 불운하게도 예민함과, 우울한 마귀와, 하늘만이 아는 온갖 것에 시달리는 사내에게는 갑절의 모욕이지. 나는 그 예고를 결코 잊을 수 없어. 더 모진 사내라면 이 일을 더 진척시키지 않음으로써 당신 이모를 되받아칠 수 있는 이 권력을 지금 기뻐할 텐데.\"\n\n그가 그 말을 하는 동안 그녀는 슬픈 눈으로 그를 애틋하게 바라보았으니, 그 모습은 이 방 안에 예민함을 지닌 것을 한탄할 만한 이가 한 사람만이 아님을 보여 주었다. 그녀가 정말로 괴로워하는 것을 보고 그는 마음이 어지러운 듯하더니 덧붙였다. \"이건 그저 하나의 상념일 뿐이야, 알잖아. 나는 이 혼인을 매듭짓길 거절할 마음이 추호도 없어, 내 태미—그건 견딜 수 없을 거야.\"\n\n\"못 견디실 거예요, 알아요!\" 고운 처녀가 환해지며 말했다. \"벌레 한 마리가 아파하는 꼴조차, 어떤 듣기 싫은 소리조차, 심지어 고약한 냄새조차 못 견디는 당신이, 저와 제 식구를 오래 아프게 하지는 않으실 거예요.\"\n\n\"안 그럴게, 어쩔 수만 있다면.\"\n\n\"손을 주세요, 데이먼.\"\n\n그는 무심히 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"아, 맙소사, 저게 뭐지?\" 그가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n집 앞에서 여러 목소리가 노래하는 소리가 그들의 귀에 떨어졌다. 그중 둘이 그 독특함으로 두드러졌으니, 하나는 아주 강한 베이스였고, 다른 하나는 쌕쌕거리는 가느다란 피리 소리였다. 토머신은 그것들이 각각 티머시 페어웨이와 캔틀 영감의 것임을 알아챘다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 영문이지요—설마 망신 행렬은 아니겠지요?\" 그녀가 겁먹은 눈길로 와일디브를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"물론 아니지. 아니, 황야 사람들이 우리한테 환영의 노래를 부르러 온 거야. 이건 참을 수 없군!\" 그가 서성이기 시작하자, 바깥의 사내들이 흥겹게 노래했다—\n\n\"그가 그녀에게 일렀네, 그녀가 그의 삶의 기쁨이라고, 그녀만 응한다면 그녀를 아내로 삼겠노라고, 그녀는 그를 거절할 수 없었네, 그리하여 교회로 갔다네, 젊은 윌은 잊히고, 젊은 수는 만족했다네, 그러고는 입맞춤받고 그의 무릎에 앉혀졌으니, 세상 어느 사내도 그처럼 다정하진 않았다네!\"\n\n요브라이트 부인이 바깥방에서 뛰어들어왔다. \"토머신, 토머신!\" 그녀가 와일디브를 분개한 눈으로 보며 말했다. \"이거 참 볼만한 망신이로구나! 당장 빠져나가자. 가자!\"\n\n그러나 통로로 빠져나가기엔 이미 늦었다. 앞방 문에 거친 노크 소리가 시작됐던 것이다. 창가로 갔던 와일디브가 돌아왔다.\n\n\"멈추세요!\" 그가 요브라이트 부인의 팔에 손을 얹으며 위엄 있게 말했다. \"우리는 완전히 포위됐습니다. 바깥에 한 명이라도 있으면 쉰 명은 있을 겁니다. 부인은 토머신과 이 방에 계세요. 제가 나가서 그들과 맞서지요. 저들이 갈 때까지 이제 부인도 머무셔야 합니다, 저를 봐서라도요, 모든 게 멀쩡한 것처럼 보이도록요. 자, 태미 여보, 소동 일으키지 마—이렇게 된 마당에 우리는 혼인해야 하고, 그건 나만큼 당신도 잘 알잖아. 가만히 앉아 있어, 그게 다야—그리고 말은 많이 하지 말고. 내가 저들을 처리할 테니. 멍청한 얼간이들!\"\n\n그는 동요한 처녀를 의자에 눌러 앉히고는 바깥방으로 돌아가 문을 열었다. 바로 바깥, 통로에 캔틀 영감이 나타나 집 앞에 아직 서 있는 이들과 어우러져 노래하고 있었다. 그는 방으로 들어와 와일디브에게 멍하니 고개를 끄덕였으니, 입술은 여전히 벌어진 채였고, 이목구비는 후렴을 토해 내느라 괴로울 만치 긴장돼 있었다. 그것이 끝나자 그가 진심으로 말했다. \"갓 맺어진 부부에게 환영을, 하느님의 복이 있기를!\"\n\n\"고맙소.\" 와일디브가 메마른 분기를 담아 말했으니, 그 얼굴은 뇌우雷雨처럼 음울했다.\n\n영감의 뒤를 이어 이제 나머지 무리가 들어왔으니, 페어웨이, 크리스천, 잔디 베는 샘, 험프리, 그 밖에 열두엇이 끼어 있었다. 모두가 와일디브를 향해, 또한 그의 탁자와 의자들을 향해 웃음 지었으니, 물건들에 대해서도 그 주인에 대해서와 마찬가지로 다정한 마음이 두루 일었던 까닭이다.\n\n\"우리가 결국 요브라이트 부인보다 먼저 온 건 아니로군.\" 페어웨이가 말했으니, 그들이 들어선 술청과 여인들이 앉은 방을 나누는 유리 칸막이 너머로 부인의 보닛을 알아본 것이었다. \"우리는 가로질러 내려왔잖소, 알겠소, 와일디브 씨, 부인은 오솔길로 빙 돌아갔고.\"\n\n\"젊은 신부의 작은 머리도 보이는군!\" 영감이 같은 방향을 들여다보며, 비참하고 어색한 모양새로 이모 곁에서 기다리는 토머신을 알아보고 말했다. \"아직 자리를 다 못 잡았구먼—뭐, 뭐, 시간이야 넉넉하지.\"\n\n와일디브는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으며, 아마도 빨리 대접할수록 빨리 가리라 여겼던지, 돌 항아리 하나를 내놓으니 그것이 단번에 사정에 따스한 후광을 드리웠다.\n\n\"저게 바로 제대로 된 술 한 방울이군, 딱 보면 알지.\" 캔틀 영감이 그것을 맛보려 서두르는 기색을 보이기엔 너무 예의 바른 사내의 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 와일디브가 말했다. \"묵은 벌꿀술이오. 마음에 드시길 바라오.\"\n\n\"오 암!\" 손님들이 응했으니, 예의가 요구하는 말이 가장 깊은 속내의 말과 일치할 때 자연히 우러나는 진정 어린 어조였다. \"하늘 아래 이보다 더 멋진 술은 없지.\"\n\n\"맹세코 없고말고.\" 캔틀 영감이 덧붙였다. \"벌꿀술에 대해 할 수 있는 흉이라곤 좀 독해서 사내한테 한참 들러붙기 쉽다는 것뿐이지. 하지만 다행히도 내일은 일요일 아닌가.\"\n\n\"한번 마셨더니 영락없이 무슨 용맹한 군인이라도 된 것 같더라고요.\" 크리스천이 말했다.\n\n\"또 그리 느끼게 해 드리지요.\" 와일디브가 너그러이 말했다. \"잔으로 드릴까요, 컵으로 드릴까요, 여러분?\"\n\n\"음, 괜찮으시다면 큰 잔으로 해서 돌려 마시지요. 찔끔찔끔 따라 마시는 것보다 그게 낫소.\"\n\n\"미끄러운 유리잔은 집어치워.\" 캔틀 영감이 말했다. \"데우려고 잿불에 내려놓을 수도 없는 물건이 무슨 소용이람, 안 그렇소, 이웃들? 내가 묻고 싶은 게 그거요.\"\n\n\"옳소, 영감.\" 샘이 말했고, 이윽고 벌꿀술이 돌았다.\n\n\"자,\" 티머시 페어웨이가, 어떤 형태로든 자기 칭찬이 요구된다 느끼며 말했다. \"혼인이란 갸륵한 일이지요, 와일디브 씨. 그리고 얻으신 여인은 보석이오, 내 그리 말하리다. 그렇소,\" 그가 캔틀 영감을 향해, 칸막이 너머로 들리도록 목청을 높여 이어 말했다. \"그 아가씨 아버지는 (안쪽 방을 향해 고개를 기울이며) 둘도 없이 훌륭한 양반이었소. 무슨 음흉한 짓이든 그 앞에서는 큰 분노를 늘 채비하고 있었지.\"\n\n\"그게 그리 위험한 거예요?\" 크리스천이 말했다.\n\n\"이 근방에 그 양반에 견줄 만한 이는 몇 안 됐소.\" 샘이 말했다. \"어느 조합이 행진할 때마다 그 앞장서는 악대에서 클라리넷을 불었는데, 평생 클라리넷밖에 안 만져 본 사람처럼 불었지. 그러고는 교회 문에 이르면 클라리넷을 내려놓고 회랑으로 올라가 베이스 비올을 낚아채 켜는데, 평생 베이스 비올밖에 안 켜 본 사람처럼 송진을 먹여 그어 댔소. 사람들이—진짜 가락이 뭔지 아는 사람들이—이러더군, '아니, 아니, 저 양반이 방금 클라리넷을 그리 능란하게 다루던 그 사람이 정말 맞나!'\"\n\n\"내 그걸 기억하오.\" 가시금작화 베는 일꾼이 말했다. \"한 몸이 그걸 다 해내면서 운지를 한 번도 안 헷갈렸다니 놀라운 일이었지.\"\n\n\"킹스비어 교회도 있었지.\" 페어웨이가, 같은 흥미의 광맥에서 새 줄기를 캐는 사람처럼 다시 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n와일디브는 견딜 수 없이 지루한 사람의 숨을 내쉬며, 칸막이 너머로 갇힌 이들을 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"그 양반은 일요일 오후면 거기 거기 첫 클라리넷인 옛 지인 앤드루 브라운을 만나러 걸어가곤 했소. 사람은 꽤 괜찮았지만, 음악은 좀 째지는 편이었지, 기억나는지 모르겠소만?\"\n\n\"그랬지.\"\n\n\"그러면 이웃 요브라이트는 예배 어느 대목에서 앤드리 자리를 대신 맡아, 앤드리가 잠깐 눈이라도 붙이게 해 줬소, 어느 벗인들 그러는 게 인지상정이듯이.\"\n\n\"어느 벗인들 그러지.\" 캔틀 영감이 말했고, 다른 듣는 이들도 더 짧게 고개를 끄덕여 같은 동의를 나타냈다.\n\n\"앤드리가 잠들고 이웃 요브라이트의 첫 입김이 앤드리의 클라리넷 속으로 들어가기가 무섭게, 교회 안의 모두가 단번에 그들 가운데 위대한 영혼이 있음을 느꼈소. 머리란 머리는 다 돌아가며 이러더군, '아, 그 양반인 줄 알았어!' 한 일요일이 똑똑히 기억나오—그땐 베이스 비올 날이었고, 요브라이트가 제 것을 가져왔지. 「리디아」 가락에 맞춘 시편 백서른셋이었는데, '그의 수염을 타고 흘러 그의 옷자락에 값진 기름이 떨어졌도다' 대목에 이르렀을 때, 막 일에 흥이 오른 이웃 요브라이트가 그 영광스럽고 장대하게 현에 활을 박아 넣는 통에 베이스 비올을 거의 두 동강 낼 뻔했소. 교회의 창문이란 창문이 죄다 뇌우라도 친 듯 덜그럭거렸지. 늙은 윌리엄스 목사님은 거룩한 큰 제의를 입은 채로, 마치 평상복이라도 입은 양 자연스레 손을 들어 올리고는, 혼잣말로 이러는 듯했소, '오 우리 교구에도 저런 사람이 있었으면!' 하지만 킹스비어에서는 그 누구도 요브라이트의 발끝에도 못 미쳤소.\"\n\n\"창문이 흔들릴 때 정말 안전했어요?\" 크리스천이 물었다.\n\n그는 대답을 듣지 못했으니, 모두가 그 순간 묘사된 연주에 대한 감탄에 넋을 빼앗겨 있었던 까닭이다. 공주들 앞에서의 파리넬리의 노래, 셰리든의 그 유명한 베굼 연설, 그 밖의 그런 본보기들이 그렇듯, 그것이 세상에서 영영 사라져 버렸다는 다행스러운 처지가, 고故 요브라이트 씨의 그 잊지 못할 오후의 역작에 누적되는 영광을 입혀 주었으니, 만일 비교 비평이라는 것이 가능했더라면 그 영광을 상당히 깎아냈을 법도 했다.\n\n\"한창나이에 세상 뜰 줄은 정말 아무도 생각 못 했지.\" 험프리가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 글쎄. 가기 몇 달 전부터 그 양반은 땅을 바라보고 있었소. 그 무렵엔 여인들이 그린힐 장에서 속옷이며 옷감을 타려고 달음박질을 하곤 했는데, 지금 내 안사람이 된 여자가, 그땐 다리만 긴 덜렁대는 처녀로 시집갈 키도 채 못 됐던 게, 다른 처녀들 틈에 끼어 갔소, 몸이 그리 무거워지기 전엔 잘 달렸거든. 그 여자가 집에 왔을 때 내가 말했지—그때 우리는 막 함께 걷기 시작한 참이었소—'뭘 탔나, 내 꿀단지?' 하니까 '탔어요—저, 옷감을 탔어요' 하는데, 단번에 얼굴이 빨개지더군. 한 닢짜리 속옷이로구나, 했더니 과연 그렇더이다. 아이고, 이젠 얼굴에 빨간 기 한 점 없이 내게 무슨 말이든 할 그 여자가, 그때는 그런 사소한 걸 말 못 하던 게 참 이상해 보이오…… 어쨌든, 그 여자가 말을 이었는데, 그게 내가 이 이야길 꺼낸 까닭이오, '음, 무슨 옷을 탔든, 흰 거든 무늬진 거든, 남 보일 거든 못 보일 거든' (그땐 제법 예쁘장하게 수줍은 척을 할 줄 알았지), '그걸 잃었으면 잃었지 내가 본 걸 보지나 말 걸 그랬어요. 가엾은 요브라이트 씨가 장터에 닿자마자 탈이 나서, 도로 집에 가야 했어요.' 그게 그 양반이 교구 밖으로 나간 마지막이었소.\"\n\n\"하루하루 비틀비틀 버티더니, 이윽고 갔다는 소릴 들었지.\"\n\n\"죽을 때 많이 아팠을까요?\" 크리스천이 말했다.\n\n\"오 아니—전혀 달랐소. 마음의 고통도 없었지. 운 좋게도 전능하신 하느님 당신의 사람이었으니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 다른 사람들은—그들에겐 많이 아플까요, 페어웨이 씨?\"\n\n\"그야 두려워하느냐 안 하느냐에 달렸지.\"\n\n\"난 하나도 안 두려워요, 하느님 덕분에!\" 크리스천이 힘주어 말했다. \"안 두려워서 다행이에요, 그럼 아프지 않을 테니까…… 내가 두려워하는 것 같진 않아—설사 두렵다 해도 어쩔 수 없는 거고, 난 고통받을 만하지 않아. 아예 안 두려웠으면 좋겠어요!\"\n\n엄숙한 침묵이 흘렀고, 덧창도 차양도 없는 창밖을 내다보며 티머시가 말했다. \"거참, 바이 선장 댁 밖에 있는 저 불은 어찌 저리 작고 잘난 모닥불인지! 맹세코, 여태 그랬듯이 지금도 똑같이 타고 있구먼.\"\n\n모든 눈길이 창밖을 향했고, 와일디브가 짧고 속내를 드러내는 눈빛을 감추는 것은 아무도 눈치채지 못했다. 저 멀리 음울한 황야 골짜기 위로, 레인배로의 오른편에, 과연 그 불빛이 보였으니, 작지만 전과 다름없이 한결같고 끈질겼다.\n\n\"우리 것보다 먼저 붙은 거요.\" 페어웨이가 이어 말했다. \"그런데도 온 동네가 다 그것보다 먼저 꺼지는구려.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 거기 무슨 뜻이 있는지도 몰라요!\" 크리스천이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"무슨 뜻?\" 와일디브가 날카롭게 말했다.\n\n크리스천은 너무 산만해 대답하지 못했고, 티머시가 거들었다.\n\n\"이 사람 말은, 나리, 저 위에 사는 외롭고 검은 눈동자의 여자, 더러 마녀라 하는 그—참한 젊은 여자를 두고 내가 그런 이름으로 부를 일이야 결코 없겠소만—그 여자가 늘 이상한 변덕을 부린다는 거요, 그러니 어쩌면 그게 그 여자일지도 모른다는 거지요.\"\n\n\"그 처녀가 나를 받아 주고, 그 거친 검은 눈으로 내게 저주를 거는 위험을 감수해 준다면, 기꺼이 청혼이라도 하겠소.\" 캔틀 영감이 꿋꿋이 말했다.\n\n\"그런 말 마세요, 아버지!\" 크리스천이 애원했다.\n\n\"허, 저 처녀와 혼인하는 자는 제일 좋은 응접실에 둘 보기 드문 그림을 얻는 셈이니, 정신이 다 아찔할 거요.\" 페어웨이가, 한 모금 흠뻑 들이켠 끝에 벌꿀술 잔을 내려놓으며 유들유들한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 북극성처럼 속이 깊은 짝이지.\" 샘이 잔을 집어 들어 남은 조금을 비우며 말했다. \"음, 정말이지, 이제 우리 그만 움직여야 할 것 같소.\" 그릇이 빈 것을 보고 험프리가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 한 곡 더 불러 줘야지요?\" 캔틀 영감이 말했다. \"나는 새처럼 가락이 가득하다오!\"\n\n\"고맙소, 영감.\" 와일디브가 말했다. \"하지만 지금은 폐를 끼치지 않으리다. 그건 다른 날—내가 잔치를 벌일 때 하기로 합시다.\"\n\n\"그러려면 새 노래를 열 곡 안 배우면 내가 사람도 아니지, 한 소절도 안 배울 테면 안 배울지언정!\" 캔틀 영감이 말했다. \"그리고 와일디브 씨, 내 안 와서 실망시키는 일은 결코 없을 테니 안심하시오.\"\n\n\"믿어 의심치 않소.\" 그 신사가 말했다.\n\n그러고는 모두 작별을 고하며, 혼인한 사내로서 만수무강과 행복을 빌었으니, 그 되풀이가 한참을 잡아먹었다. 와일디브는 문까지 그들을 배웅했고, 그 너머로는 짙게 물든 황야가 위로 뻗어 그들을 기다리고 있었으니, 그들의 발치에서 거의 천정天頂까지 광막한 어둠이 군림했고, 그곳 레인배로의 찌푸린 이마에서 비로소 하나의 또렷한 형체가 처음으로 눈에 들어왔다. 잔디 베는 샘을 선두로 빽빽한 어둠 속으로 일렬로 뛰어들어, 그들은 길도 없는 귀갓길을 더듬어 갔다.\n\n가시금작화가 그들의 각반을 긁는 소리가 귀에서 잦아들자, 와일디브는 토머신과 그 이모를 두고 나왔던 방으로 돌아왔다. 여인들은 사라지고 없었다.\n\n그들이 집을 떠날 수 있는 길은 단 하나, 뒤쪽 창문뿐이었으니, 그 창이 열려 있었다.\n\n와일디브는 혼자 웃으며 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 무심코 앞방으로 돌아왔다. 거기서 그의 눈길이 벽난로 선반에 놓인 포도주 한 병에 떨어졌다. \"아—다우든 노인네!\" 그가 중얼거리고는 부엌문으로 가 외쳤다. \"다우든 노인네한테 뭐 좀 갖다줄 사람 여기 없나?\"\n\n대답이 없었다. 방은 비어 있었으니, 그의 잡일을 맡은 머슴 아이가 잠자리에 든 까닭이었다. 와일디브는 돌아와 모자를 쓰고, 병을 집어 들고는, 문을 잠그고 집을 나섰으니, 오늘 밤 여관에는 손님이 없었기 때문이다. 길에 들어서자마자 미스토버 놉의 작은 모닥불이 다시 그의 눈에 들어왔다.\n\n\"아직도 기다리시오, 아가씨?\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n그러나 그는 당장 그쪽으로 가지는 않았으니, 언덕을 왼편에 두고, 바퀴 자국 팬 길을 비틀비틀 넘어 한 오두막에 이르렀는데, 이 시각 황야의 다른 모든 거처와 마찬가지로 침실 창에서 새어 나오는 희미한 빛 덕분에 겨우 보이는 신세를 면한 곳이었다. 이 집은 빗자루 만드는 올리 다우든의 보금자리였고, 그는 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n아래층 방은 어둠에 잠겨 있었으나, 손으로 더듬어 탁자 하나를 찾아 그 위에 병을 놓고는, 잠시 뒤 다시 황야로 나왔다. 그는 서서, 사그라들지 않는 작은 불을 북동쪽으로 바라보았으니—그의 머리 위로 높이 있었으나, 레인배로만큼 높지는 않았다.\n\n여인이 망설일 때 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 우리는 익히 들어 왔거니와, 사정에 한 사람이 끼어 있고 그것이 어여쁜 이라면, 그 경구가 늘 여인에게서 끝나는 것은 아니다. 와일디브는 서 있다가, 더 오래 서 있다가, 어리둥절한 듯 숨을 쉬다가, 이윽고 체념하여 혼잣말했다. \"그래—맙소사, 그녀에게 가야겠지, 가는 수밖에!\"\n\n집 쪽으로 돌아서는 대신 그는 레인배로 아래 오솔길을 따라, 분명 신호인 듯한 불빛을 향해 빠르게 나아갔다.\n\n에그던의 온 무리가 모닥불 자리를 그 익숙한 고독에 내맡기고 떠나자, 단단히 몸을 감싼 한 여인의 형체가 그 작은 불이 놓인 황야 쪽에서 봉분으로 다가왔다. 레들맨이 지켜보고 있었다면 그녀가, 처음에 그토록 기이하게 그곳에 서 있다가 낯선 이들이 다가오자 사라졌던 그 여인임을 알아보았을지도 모른다. 그녀는 꼭대기의 옛 자리로 올라갔으니, 그곳에서는 사그라드는 불의 붉은 잉걸이 한낮의 주검 속 살아 있는 눈처럼 그녀를 맞았다. 거기 그녀는 가만히 서 있었고, 둘레로는 광막한 밤의 대기가 펼쳐졌으니, 그 아래 황야의 온전한 어둠에 견주면 그 불완전한 어둠은 죽을죄 곁의 가벼운 죄에 견줄 만했다.\n\n그녀가 키 크고 곧은 몸매라는 것, 그 거동이 귀부인답다는 것, 그것만이 지금 그녀에 대해 알 수 있는 전부였으니, 그 몸은 옛날식 대각선으로 접은 숄에 감싸여 있었고, 머리는 큰 머릿수건으로 싸여 있었던바, 이 시각 이 자리에서는 군더더기가 아닌 보호였다. 그녀의 등은 바람을 향하고 있었으니, 바람은 북서쪽에서 불어왔다. 그러나 그녀가 그쪽을 피한 것이 그 남다른 자리에 휘도는 차가운 돌풍 때문이었는지, 아니면 그녀의 관심이 남동쪽에 있었기 때문이었는지는 처음엔 드러나지 않았다.\n\n이 황야 고장 둘레의 축처럼 그토록 죽은 듯이 가만히 서 있는 그녀의 까닭 또한 그만큼 모호했다. 그 유별난 부동不動, 그 두드러진 고독, 밤에 대한 무심함은, 다른 무엇보다도 두려움이 전혀 없음을 드러냈다. 카이사르가 해마다 추분 전에 그 음울함에서 벗어나기를 애태웠던 그 불길한 상태에서 조금도 변하지 않은 땅, 남쪽에서 온 나그네들로 하여금 우리 섬을 호메로스의 키메르 땅이라 일컫게 하는 그런 풍광과 날씨는, 겉보기에 여인에게 살가운 곳이 아니었다.\n\n그녀가 바람에 귀 기울이고 있다고 여겼음 직도 했으니, 밤이 깊어 갈수록 바람은 다소 거세져 주의를 사로잡았다. 과연 바람은 그 풍경을 위해 빚어진 듯했으니, 그 풍경이 그 시각을 위해 빚어진 듯했던 것과 같았다. 그 음색의 일부는 자못 특별했으니, 거기서 들리는 것은 다른 어디서도 들을 수 없는 것이었다. 헤아릴 수 없이 잇따른 돌풍이 북서쪽에서 꼬리를 물고 따라왔고, 그 하나하나가 내달아 지나갈 때마다 그 진행의 소리는 세 갈래로 갈라졌다. 거기엔 고음, 중음, 저음이 들어 있었다. 구덩이와 두두룩한 곳을 넘는 그 전체의 두루뭉술한 반향은 그 화음에서 가장 묵직한 가락이었다. 다음으로 호랑가시나무 한 그루의 바리톤 윙윙거림이 들렸다. 이것들보다 세기로는 아래고 음높이로는 위인, 잦아든 한 목소리가 쉰 가락을 힘겹게 자아내고 있었으니, 그것이 앞서 일컬은 그 독특한 고장의 소리였다. 다른 두 소리보다 가늘고 곧장 짚어 내기 어려웠으나, 그 어느 것보다도 훨씬 인상적이었다. 거기엔 이른바 황야의 언어적 독특함이 깃들어 있었고, 황야를 벗어난 지상 어디서도 들을 수 없는 것이었으니, 여인의 긴장에 한 가닥 까닭의 그림자를 드리웠던바, 그 긴장은 여전히 깨어지지 않은 채였다.\n\n이 구슬픈 십일월 바람이 불어 대는 내내 그 소리는 아흔 노인의 목구멍에 남은 인간 노래의 잔해와 사뭇 닮아 있었다. 그것은 닳아빠진 속삭임으로, 메마르고 종잇장 같았으며, 어찌나 또렷이 귀를 스치는지, 익숙한 이라면 그 소리가 비롯된 물질적 미세함을 손으로 만지듯 실감할 수 있었다. 그것은 무한히 작은 식물적 원인들이 한데 빚어낸 산물이었으니, 그것들은 줄기도, 잎도, 열매도, 풀잎도, 가시도, 지의류도, 이끼도 아니었다. 그것들은 지난여름의 미라가 된 헤더 종꽃이었으니, 본디 여리고 자줏빛이었던 것이 이제 미카엘 축일의 비에 빛이 다 씻겨 나가고, 시월의 햇볕에 죽은 가죽처럼 말라붙은 것이었다. 그 하나하나에서 나는 소리가 어찌나 낮은지 수백 개가 어우러져야 겨우 침묵에서 떠올랐고, 비탈 전체의 무수한 것들이 여인의 귀에 닿는 것은 쪼그라들고 끊겼다 이어지는 영창詠唱일 따름이었다. 그러나 오늘 밤 떠도는 그 많은 가락 가운데 그것만큼 듣는 이에게 그 비롯됨을 떠올리게 할 힘을 지닌 것은 거의 없었다. 마음의 눈으로 그 어우러진 무리의 무한함이 보였고, 그 작디작은 나팔 하나하나가, 마치 분화구만큼이나 거대하기라도 한 양 바람에 붙들리고, 들어가지고, 훑어지고, 빠져나가지는 것이 느껴졌다.\n\n\"성령이 그들을 움직였다.\" 그 구절의 한 뜻이 절로 마음에 떠올랐고, 감정에 휘둘린 듣는 이의 물신적 기분은 한층 진전된 무엇으로 끝맺었을지도 모른다. 결국 말하는 것은 왼편으로 펼쳐진 묵은 꽃밭도, 오른편의 것도, 앞쪽 비탈의 것도 아니었으니, 그것은 그 모두를 통해 한꺼번에 말하는 어떤 다른 것의 단일한 인격이었다.\n\n문득, 봉분 위에서, 이 모든 밤의 거친 수사修辭에 어떤 소리가 섞여 들었으니, 어찌나 자연스레 나머지에 녹아드는지 그 시작과 끝을 가려내기 어려울 정도였다. 벼랑도, 덤불도, 헤더 종꽃도 침묵을 깼던바, 마침내 여인도 그러했으니, 그녀의 발음은 그들의 것과 같은 담화의 또 다른 한 구절일 따름이었다. 바람에 내던져져 그것은 바람과 뒤얽혔고, 바람과 함께 날아가 버렸다.\n\n그녀가 낸 것은 길게 끄는 한숨이었으니, 분명 그녀를 이곳에 오게 한 마음속 무언가에서 비롯된 듯했다. 거기엔 발작적인 자포자기가 깃들어 있었으니, 마치 그 소리를 내도록 스스로를 내버려 둠으로써 여인의 머리가 다스릴 수 없는 것을 인가認可하기라도 한 듯했다. 한 가지는 이로써 분명했으니, 그녀가 노곤함이나 침체가 아니라 억눌린 상태로 지내 왔다는 것이었다.\n\n저 멀리 골짜기 아래로 여관 창의 희미한 빛이 여전히 이어지고 있었으니, 몇 순간이 더 지나자 그 창이, 혹은 그 안에 있는 것이, 여인의 한숨과 더 관련이 있음이 드러났으니, 그녀 자신의 행동이나 바로 둘레의 풍경보다도 그러했다. 그녀가 왼손을 들어 올렸으니, 접힌 망원경을 쥐고 있었다. 이것을 그녀는 익숙한 동작인 양 잽싸게 폈고, 눈에 갖다 대어 여관에서 비쳐 나오는 불빛 쪽으로 겨누었다.\n\n머리를 덮었던 손수건이 이제 조금 뒤로 젖혀졌고, 얼굴이 다소 들려 있었다. 둘레의 흐릿한 단색 구름을 배경으로 옆얼굴이 드러났으니, 그것은 마치 사포와 시던스 부인의 이목구비에서 비롯된 옆그림자들이 무덤에서 위로 모여들어, 그 어느 쪽과도 닮지 않았으나 둘 다를 떠올리게 하는 형상을 이룬 듯했다. 그러나 이는 한낱 겉모습일 뿐이었다. 성품에 관한 한, 얼굴은 그 윤곽으로 어떤 것을 시인할 수는 있으나, 온전히 고백하는 것은 오직 그 변화에서뿐이다. 그러한즉 이른바 이목구비의 움직임이야말로, 흔히 다른 모든 부위의 진지한 수고를 합친 것보다도 한 사람을 이해하는 데 더 도움이 된다. 하여 밤은, 그 형체를 품에 안고 있으면서도, 그 임자에 대해 거의 드러내지 못했으니, 그녀 얼굴의 움직이는 부분들은 보이지 않았던 까닭이다.\n\n마침내 그녀는 엿보는 자세를 거두고, 망원경을 접고, 사그라드는 잉걸불 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 거기서 이제 이렇다 할 빛줄기는 뻗어 나오지 않았으니, 다만 여느 때보다 매서운 돌풍이 그 낯을 쓸고 지나가며, 소녀의 홍조처럼 왔다 가는 변덕스러운 불빛을 일으킬 때만은 예외였다. 그녀는 고요한 원圓 위로 몸을 굽혀, 타다 남은 가지들 중에서 끝에 가장 큰 잉걸을 단 막대기 하나를 골라, 앞서 서 있던 자리로 가져갔다.\n\n그녀는 막대기를 땅에 대고, 동시에 입으로 그 붉은 잉걸을 불었으니, 마침내 그것이 잔디를 희미하게 비추어 작은 물체 하나를 드러냈는데, 알고 보니 모래시계였다, 그녀가 손목시계를 차고 있었는데도. 그녀는 모래가 다 흘러내렸음을 보일 만큼 오래 불었다.\n\n\"아!\" 그녀가 놀란 듯이 말했다.\n\n그녀의 입김으로 일어난 빛은 무척 변덕스러웠고, 그것이 그녀 얼굴에서 드러낸 것이라곤 한순간 살갗의 반짝임이 전부였다. 그것은 둘도 없는 두 입술과 한쪽 뺨뿐이었으니, 머리는 여전히 싸여 있었다. 그녀는 막대기를 던져 버리고, 모래시계를 손에 들고, 망원경을 겨드랑이에 끼고, 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n능선을 따라 희미한 발자취가 나 있었고, 부인은 그것을 따라갔다. 그것을 잘 아는 이들은 그것을 길이라 불렀거니와, 한낱 방문객이라면 대낮에도 알아채지 못하고 지나쳤을 그것을, 황야를 늘 드나드는 이들은 한밤중에도 어렵잖게 찾았다. 대기에 큰길을 비출 만한 빛조차 없을 때 이 갓 생긴 길들을 따라가는 비결은 온통, 발의 촉각을 발달시키는 데 있었으니, 그것은 사람 발길이 드문 곳에서 여러 해 밤을 쏘다녀야 생기는 것이었다. 그런 곳에 단련된 보행자에게는, 처녀지의 풀밭을 디딜 때와 자그마한 오솔길의 짓밟힌 줄기를 디딜 때의 차이가, 아무리 두꺼운 장화나 신발을 통해서도 느껴졌다.\n\n이 길을 걷는 외로운 형체는 죽은 헤더 종꽃 위에서 여전히 연주되는 바람의 가락에 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그녀는 더 앞쪽의 검은 짐승 무리를 보려고 고개를 돌리지도 않았으니, 그것들은 그녀가 풀을 뜯는 그것들 곁의 협곡을 에둘러 갈 때 그녀의 존재 앞에서 달아났다. 그것들은 황야 망아지라 불리는 작은 야생 조랑말 스무 마리쯤이었다. 그것들은 에그던의 굽이진 곳을 멋대로 누볐으나, 그 수가 너무 적어 고독을 크게 덜지는 못했다.\n\n보행자는 지금 아무것도 알아채지 못했고, 그녀의 골똘함에 대한 한 가닥 실마리는 사소한 사건으로 드러났다. 가시나무가 그녀의 치맛자락을 붙들어 걸음을 막았다. 그것을 떼어 내고 서둘러 가는 대신, 그녀는 그 당김에 몸을 내맡기고 가만히 멈춰 섰다. 그녀가 빠져나오기 시작한 것은 빙빙 돌아 그 가시 돋친 회초리를 풀어내는 식이었다. 그녀는 풀 죽은 몽상에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n그녀의 길은, 레인배로의 사내들과 아래 골짜기의 와일디브의 주의를 끌었던 그 작고 사그라들지 않는 불을 향한 것이었다. 그 빛줄기에서 나온 희미한 빛이 그녀 얼굴에 어리기 시작했고, 이내 그 불은 평지가 아니라, 두 둑이 모여드는 어귀의 흙으로 된 돌출한 모서리, 곧 능보稜堡 위에 피워졌음이 드러났다. 바깥에는 도랑이 있었으니, 불 바로 아래만 빼고는 말라 있었는데, 그곳엔 둘레가 온통 헤더와 골풀로 수염처럼 둘린 큰 웅덩이가 있었다. 웅덩이의 매끄러운 물에는 불이 거꾸로 비쳤다.\n\n뒤에서 만나는 둑들은 산울타리가 없었으니, 다만 꼭대기를 따라 줄기 위에 선 띄엄띄엄한 가시금작화 덤불이, 도성 벽 위에 꽂힌 머리들처럼 이룬 것을 빼면 그러했다. 활대며 그 밖의 항해 장비를 갖춘 흰 돛대 하나가, 불길이 거기 닿을 만큼 환히 일렁일 때마다 어두운 구름을 배경으로 솟아 있는 것이 보였다. 통틀어 그 광경은 봉화를 피워 올린 요새와 사뭇 닮은 모습이었다.\n\n아무도 보이지 않았으나, 이따금 희끄무레한 무언가가 뒤편 둑 위로 움직였다가 다시 사라졌다. 그것은 불 속에 땔감을 들어 올려 넣는 작은 사람 손이었으나, 보이는 것만으로는 그 손은, 벨사살을 괴롭히던 그 손처럼, 홀로 거기 있었다. 때때로 잉걸 하나가 둑에서 굴러떨어져 쉭 소리를 내며 웅덩이로 떨어졌다.\n\n웅덩이 한쪽에는 흙덩이로 쌓은 거친 계단이 있어, 둑에 오르고 싶은 누구든 오를 수 있게 했으니, 여인이 그렇게 했다. 안쪽에는\n언젠가 경작된 적이 있음을 보여주는 흔적은 남아 있으나 지금은 묵정밭으로 방치된 작은 목초지였는데, 히스와 고사리가 슬그머니 기어들어와 옛 패권을 다시금 주장하고 있었다. 더 앞쪽으로는 불규칙한 모양의 주택과 정원, 부속 건물이 전나무 덤불을 배경으로 어렴풋이 보였다.\n\n그 젊은 여인은—둑 위로 가뿐히 뛰어오르는 그 몸짓에서 젊음이 제 존재를 드러냈으니—둑 안쪽으로 내려가는 대신 꼭대기를 따라 걸어 불이 타오르는 모퉁이에 이르렀다. 불길이 좀처럼 사그라들지 않는 이유 하나가 이제 분명해졌으니, 땔감이 쪼개고 톱질한 단단한 나무토막—언덕 비탈 여기저기에 둘씩 셋씩 자라는 늙은 가시나무의 옹이진 밑동—으로 이루어져 있었던 것이다. 아직 다 타지 않은 이런 나무들이 둑 안쪽 모서리에 무더기로 쌓여 있었고, 그 모퉁이에서 한 어린 소년이 얼굴을 들어 그녀를 맞이했다. 소년은 이따금 나무토막 하나를 굼뜨게 불 속에 던져 넣고 있었는데, 이 일이 저녁 내내 그를 붙들어두었던 모양인지 얼굴에는 다소 지친 기색이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"와 주셔서 다행이에요, 유스테이샤 아씨.\" 소년이 안도의 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"혼자 있는 건 싫어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리. 그저 잠깐 산책을 다녀온 것뿐인데. 떠난 지 이십 분밖에 안 됐어.\"\n\n\"길게 느껴졌는걸요.\" 슬픈 소년이 중얼거렸다. \"게다가 여러 번 가셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"아니, 나는 네가 모닥불을 좋아할 줄 알았는데. 모닥불을 만들어줬는데도 고맙지 않니?\"\n\n\"고맙죠. 그런데 같이 놀 사람이 아무도 없는걸요.\"\n\n\"내가 없는 동안 아무도 안 온 모양이지?\"\n\n\"할아버지 말고는 아무도 안 왔어요—할아버지가 한 번 문밖을 내다보셨어요, 아씨 찾으시려고. 아씨가 언덕을 돌아다니면서 다른 모닥불들을 보고 계시다고 말씀드렸어요.\"\n\n\"착한 아이로구나.\"\n\n\"또 오시는 소리가 들리는 것 같아요, 아씨.\"\n\n한 노인이 농가 쪽에서 불빛이 닿는 먼 가장자리로 들어섰다. 그날 오후 길에서 레들맨을 따라잡았던 바로 그 사람이었다. 그는 둑 꼭대기에 선 여인을 그리운 눈빛으로 올려다보았고, 조금도 상하지 않은 그의 이가 벌어진 입술 사이로 파리언 대리석처럼 드러났다.\n\n\"언제 들어올 거냐, 유스테이샤?\" 그가 물었다. \"벌써 잘 시간이 다 됐다. 나는 집에 온 지 두 시간이나 됐고, 녹초가 됐구나. 이렇게 오래 모닥불 놀이를 하며 밖에 머무르고 그런 땔감을 낭비하다니, 참으로 어린애 같지 않으냐. 그 귀한 가시나무 뿌리, 온갖 땔감 가운데서도 가장 진귀한 것을 일부러 크리스마스용으로 따로 챙겨두었는데—그걸 거의 다 태워버렸구나!\"\n\n\"조니에게 모닥불을 약속했고, 이 아이는 아직 꺼뜨리지 않는 걸 좋아하는걸요.\" 유스테이샤가 자신이 여기서 절대적인 여왕임을 단번에 드러내는 어조로 말했다. \"할아버지, 들어가서 주무세요. 곧 따라 들어갈게요. 너는 불이 좋지, 조니?\"\n\n소년이 미심쩍은 표정으로 그녀를 올려다보며 중얼거렸다. \"이제 별로 안 좋은 것 같은데요.\"\n\n할아버지는 이미 돌아서 있어서 소년의 대답을 듣지 못했다. 백발의 노인이 사라지자마자 그녀는 약이 오른 어조로 아이에게 말했다. \"은혜도 모르는 녀석, 어떻게 나한테 말대꾸를 하니? 지금 불을 계속 지피지 않으면 다시는 모닥불을 못 가질 줄 알아라. 자, 나를 위해 뭔가 하는 게 좋다고 말해, 부인하지 말고.\"\n\n기가 죽은 아이가 말했다. \"네, 좋아요, 아씨.\" 그러고는 건성으로 불을 계속 휘저었다.\n\n\"조금만 더 있으면 휜 육펜스를 줄게.\" 유스테이샤가 한결 부드럽게 말했다. \"이삼 분마다 나무토막 하나씩 넣어, 그렇지만 한꺼번에 너무 많이는 말고. 나는 능선을 따라 좀 더 걸을 거지만 계속 너한테 올 거야. 그리고 돌을 던진 것처럼 첨벙 소리를 내며 개구리가 못으로 뛰어드는 소리가 들리거든, 꼭 달려와서 나한테 말해, 그건 비가 올 징조니까.\"\n\n\"네, 유스테이샤.\"\n\n\"바이 아씨라고 해야지.\"\n\n\"바이—스테이샤 아씨.\"\n\n\"그럼 됐어. 자, 이제 막대기 하나 더 넣어.\"\n\n어린 노예는 전과 같이 계속해서 불에 땔감을 먹였다. 그는 그저 자동인형 같았으니, 변덕스러운 유스테이샤의 의지에 의해 움직이고 말하도록 전기로 작동되는 듯했다. 그는 알베르투스 마그누스가 단지 재잘거리고 움직이며 자기 종노릇을 할 만큼만 생명을 불어넣었다는 그 청동상이었을지도 모른다.\n\n다시 산책을 나서기 전에 젊은 처녀는 잠시 둑 위에 가만히 서서 귀를 기울였다. 이곳은 레인배로보다 다소 낮은 지대이긴 했어도 그에 못지않게 외진 곳이었으며, 북쪽의 몇 그루 전나무 덕분에 바람과 궂은 날씨로부터 더 잘 가려져 있었다. 농가를 에워싸 바깥세상의 무법 상태로부터 그곳을 지켜주는 둑은 바깥쪽 도랑에서 파낸 두툼한 네모꼴 흙덩이로 만들어졌으며, 약간 비탈지게 경사를 두어 쌓아올린 것이었으니, 이는 바람과 황야 탓에 산울타리가 자라지 못하고 담을 쌓을 재료도 구할 수 없는 곳에서 결코 가볍지 않은 방어가 되어주었다. 그 밖으로는 사방이 탁 트여, 와일디브의 집 뒤 강에 이르는 계곡 전체를 한눈에 굽어보았다. 그 위쪽으로 오른편 높이, 콰이어트 우먼 여관보다 그쪽으로 훨씬 가까이, 레인배로의 흐릿한 윤곽이 하늘을 가로막고 있었다.\n\n거친 비탈과 움푹한 협곡을 주의 깊게 살펴본 뒤 유스테이샤의 입에서 조바심의 몸짓이 새어 나왔다. 그녀는 이따금 짜증 섞인 말을 내뱉었으나, 그 말들 사이사이에는 한숨이 있었고, 한숨들 사이사이에는 불쑥 귀를 기울이는 순간들이 있었다. 자기 자리에서 내려와 그녀는 다시 레인배로 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어갔으나, 이번에는 끝까지 가지 않았다.\n\n몇 분 간격을 두고 그녀는 두 차례 다시 나타났고, 그때마다 이렇게 말했다—\n\n\"아직 못에 첨벙 소리는 안 났니, 꼬마야?\"\n\n\"네, 유스테이샤 아씨.\" 아이가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 마침내 그녀가 말했다. \"이제 곧 들어갈 거니까, 그때 휜 육펜스를 주고 집에 보내줄게.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 유스테이샤 아씨.\" 지친 화부가 한결 편히 숨을 쉬며 말했다. 그리고 유스테이샤는 다시 불에서 멀어졌으나, 이번에는 레인배로 쪽이 아니었다. 그녀는 둑을 따라 돌아 집 앞의 쪽문에 이르렀고, 거기에 꼼짝 않고 서서 풍경을 바라보았다.\n\n오십 야드 떨어진 곳에 두 둑이 모여 이루는 모퉁이가 솟아 있었고, 그 위에 불이 타올랐으며, 둑 안쪽에서는 어린아이의 형체가 전과 똑같이 한 번에 막대기 하나씩 불에 들어올리고 있었다. 그녀는 아이가 이따금 둑 구석으로 기어올라 타다 남은 장작 옆에 서는 것을 무심히 지켜보았다. 바람이 연기와 아이의 머리카락과 앞치마 자락을 한 방향으로 똑같이 불었고, 미풍이 잦아들자 앞치마와 머리카락이 가만히 멈췄으며 연기는 곧장 위로 올라갔다. 유스테이샤가 이 거리에서 지켜보는 동안 소년의 몸이 눈에 띄게 움찔하더니—그는 둑을 미끄러져 내려와 흰 대문 쪽으로 달려왔다.\n\n\"무슨 일이니?\" 유스테이샤가 말했다.\n\n\"개구리가 못으로 뛰어들었어요. 네, 들었어요!\"\n\n\"그럼 비가 오려나 보다, 너는 이제 집에 가는 게 좋겠어. 무섭지 않겠니?\" 그녀는 아이의 말에 심장이 목구멍까지 튀어오르기라도 한 듯 황급히 말했다.\n\n\"안 무서워요, 휜 육펜스가 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그래, 여기 있다. 자, 최대한 빨리 달려가—그쪽 말고—이쪽 정원을 통해서. 이 황야에서 너만큼 좋은 모닥불을 가져본 아이는 없을 거야.\"\n\n좋은 것이라면 이제 넌더리가 난 게 분명한 소년은 기꺼운 마음으로 어둠 속으로 성큼성큼 걸어갔다. 그가 가버리자 유스테이샤는 망원경과 모래시계를 대문 옆에 놓아둔 채 쪽문에서 불 아래쪽 둑 모퉁이를 향해 쓸 듯이 나아갔다.\n\n여기, 바깥 방벽에 가려진 채 그녀는 기다렸다. 몇 순간 지나자 바깥 못에서 첨벙 소리가 들렸다. 아이가 거기 있었다면 두 번째 개구리가 뛰어들었다고 했겠지만, 대부분의 사람들에게 그 소리는 물에 돌이 떨어지는 소리에 가깝게 들렸을 것이다. 유스테이샤가 둑 위로 발을 디뎠다.\n\n\"왔어요?\" 그녀가 말하며 숨을 죽였다.\n\n그러자 못 바깥 가장자리 너머, 계곡 위로 낮게 깔린 하늘을 배경으로 한 남자의 윤곽이 어렴풋이 드러났다. 그는 못을 돌아와 그녀 옆 둑 위로 뛰어올랐다. 그녀의 입에서 낮은 웃음이 새어 나왔으니—그날 밤 처녀가 허락한 세 번째 발성이었다. 첫 번째, 레인배로 위에 섰을 때의 것은 불안을 드러냈고, 두 번째, 능선에서의 것은 조바심을 드러냈으며, 지금의 것은 의기양양한 기쁨의 것이었다. 그녀는 혼돈에서 자신이 창조해낸 어떤 경이로운 존재를 보듯, 말없이 기쁨에 찬 눈길을 그에게 머물게 했다.\n\n\"왔소.\" 와일디브인 그 남자가 말했다. \"당신은 나를 가만 놔두질 않는군. 왜 나를 좀 내버려두지 않는 거요? 저녁 내내 당신 모닥불을 보았소.\" 그 말에는 감정이 없지 않았으나, 임박한 양극단 사이에서 조심스럽게 균형을 잡기라도 한 듯 평탄한 어조를 유지하고 있었다.\n\n연인의 이 뜻밖에도 억제된 태도에 처녀도 자신을 억누르는 듯했다. \"당신이 내 불을 본 건 당연하죠.\" 그녀가 인위적으로 유지한 나른한 침착함으로 대답했다. \"내가 황야의 다른 사람들처럼 십일월 오일에 모닥불을 피우면 안 될 이유라도 있나요?\"\n\n\"그게 나를 위한 거라는 걸 알았소.\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 알았어요? 당신이—당신이 그 여자를 택해서, 그 여자와 함께 돌아다니고, 마치 내가 한 번도 돌이킬 수 없이 당신의 목숨이자 영혼이었던 적이 없었다는 듯 나를 완전히 버린 뒤로 당신과는 한마디도 나눈 적이 없는데!\"\n\n\"유스테이샤! 작년 이맘때, 같은 달 같은 날, 같은 장소에서 당신이 나더러 와서 만나자는 신호로 바로 이런 불을 피웠던 걸 내가 잊을 수 있겠소? 같은 목적이 아니라면 어째서 바이 선장의 집 옆에 또다시 모닥불이 피워졌겠소?\"\n\n\"네, 네—인정해요.\" 그녀가 그녀만의 독특한 나른한 열정이 깃든 태도와 어조로 숨죽여 외쳤다. \"예전처럼 나한테 말하기 시작하지 마요, 데이먼. 당신은 내가 당신에게 하고 싶지 않은 말들을 하게 만들 거예요. 나는 당신을 포기했고, 더는 당신을 생각하지 않기로 결심했었어요. 그런데 그 소식을 듣고, 당신이 나에게 변함없었다는 생각이 들어서 밖으로 나와 불을 준비한 거예요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말을 들었길래 그렇게 생각한 거요?\" 와일디브가 놀라서 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 그 여자와 결혼하지 않았다는 거요!\" 그녀가 환희에 차서 중얼거렸다. \"그리고 그건 당신이 나를 가장 사랑해서, 차마 결혼할 수 없었기 때문임을 알았어요…… 데이먼, 당신은 떠나가서 나에게 잔인하게 굴었고, 나는 결코 용서하지 않겠다고 했어요. 지금도 당신을 온전히 용서할 수 있을 것 같진 않아요—기개 있는 여자라면 그걸 완전히 눈감아주기란 너무 벅찬 일이니까요.\"\n\n\"당신이 나를 여기로 부른 게 그저 나를 책망하기 위해서인 줄 알았다면, 나는 오지 않았을 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 그래도 상관없고, 당신이 그 여자와 결혼하지 않고 나에게 돌아왔으니 이제 당신을 용서해요!\"\n\n\"내가 그 여자와 결혼하지 않았다고 누가 말했소?\"\n\n\"할아버지요. 오늘 오래 산책을 하셨는데, 집에 돌아오는 길에 어떤 사람을 따라잡았고 그 사람이 깨진 혼사 이야기를 했대요—할아버지는 그게 당신 것일지도 모른다고 생각하셨고, 나는 그게 당신 것임을 알았죠.\"\n\n\"다른 사람도 아오?\"\n\n\"아닐걸요. 자 데이먼, 이제 내가 왜 신호 불을 피웠는지 알겠어요? 당신이 이 여자의 남편이 됐다고 생각했다면 내가 불을 피웠으리라고는 당신도 생각하지 않겠죠. 그렇게 짐작하는 건 내 자존심을 모욕하는 거예요.\"\n\n와일디브는 잠자코 있었으니, 그가 그렇게 짐작했음이 분명했다.\n\n\"정말로 내가 당신이 결혼했다고 믿었으리라 생각했어요?\" 그녀가 다시 간절히 물었다. \"그렇다면 당신은 나를 욕보인 거예요. 목숨을 걸고 진심으로 말하건대, 당신이 나를 그렇게 나쁘게 생각한다는 걸 인정하기가 견디기 힘들 정도예요! 데이먼, 당신은 나에게 어울리는 사람이 못 돼요—나도 알아요, 그런데도 나는 당신을 사랑해요. 괜찮아요, 그냥 둬요—나는 나에 대한 당신의 비열한 생각을 어떻게든 견뎌야겠죠…… 정말이죠, 그렇죠.\" 그가 아무 반응도 보이지 않자 그녀가 잘 감추지 못한 불안을 담아 덧붙였다. \"당신은 차마 나를 포기할 수 없었고, 지금도 나를 누구보다 사랑할 거라는 거?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 아니면 내가 왜 왔겠소?\" 그가 발끈하며 말했다. \"내가 어울리지 않는다는 당신의 그 친절한 말 뒤에는 충실함이 나에게 그리 대단한 미덕이 되진 못하겠지만 말이오, 그건 누구든 입에 올린다면 내 입으로 했어야 할 말인데 당신이 하니 영 어색하게 들리는군. 어쨌든 불붙기 쉬운 천성이라는 저주가 내게 씌었으니, 나는 그걸 안고 살며 여자에게 어떤 냉대든 받아야 하나 보오. 그 때문에 나는 기술자에서 여관 주인으로 전락했소—그게 나를 위해 어떤 더 낮은 단계를 예비해두고 있는지는 아직 알 수 없지만.\" 그는 침울하게 그녀를 계속 바라보았다.\n\n그녀는 그 순간을 놓치지 않고, 불빛이 얼굴과 목에 가득 비치도록 숄을 젖히며 미소 띤 채 말했다. \"여행을 다니면서 이보다 나은 걸 본 적 있어요?\"\n\n유스테이샤는 충분한 근거 없이 그런 자세를 취할 사람이 아니었다. 그가 조용히 말했다. \"없소.\"\n\n\"토머신의 어깨 위에서도요?\"\n\n\"토머신은 사랑스럽고 순진한 여자요.\"\n\n\"그건 상관없는 얘기예요.\" 그녀가 격정에 차 재빨리 외쳤다. \"그 여자는 빼고 말하죠. 이제 생각할 사람은 당신과 나뿐이에요.\" 그를 한참 바라본 뒤 그녀는 예의 잔잔한 온기를 되찾으며 말을 이었다. \"여자라면 마땅히 감추어야 할 것들을 나약하게 계속 당신에게 고백해야 하나요. 그리고 두 시간 전까지 품고 있던 그 끔찍한 믿음—당신이 나를 완전히 버렸다는 믿음 때문에 내가 얼마나 우울했는지 말로는 다 표현할 수 없다고 털어놓아야 하나요?\"\n\n\"당신에게 그런 고통을 준 것은 미안하오.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 우울해지는 게 다 당신 때문은 아닐지도 몰라요.\" 그녀가 짓궂게 덧붙였다. \"그렇게 느끼는 게 내 천성이에요. 핏속에 타고난 거겠죠, 아마.\"\n\n\"우울증이로군.\"\n\n\"아니면 이 거친 황야로 들어온 탓이겠죠. 버드머스에서는 충분히 행복했어요. 아, 버드머스에서의 그 시절, 그 나날들! 하지만 이제 에그던도 다시 환해지겠죠.\"\n\n\"그러길 바라오.\" 와일디브가 시무룩하게 말했다. \"당신이 나를 이렇게 다시 불러낸 결과가 어떨지 아오, 내 옛 연인이여? 나는 예전처럼 다시 당신을 보러 오게 될 거요, 레인배로에서.\"\n\n\"당연히 그렇겠죠.\"\n\n\"그런데도 오늘 밤 여기 오기 전까지 나는 이 마지막 작별 인사를 끝으로 다시는 당신을 만나지 않으려 했다고 분명히 말해두겠소.\"\n\n\"그래 줘서 고맙진 않네요.\" 그녀가 몸을 돌리며 말했고, 그동안 분노가 지하의 열기처럼 그녀를 타고 번졌다. \"원한다면 다시 레인배로에 와도 좋지만, 나를 보진 못할 거예요. 불러도 좋지만 나는 귀 기울이지 않을 거고요. 유혹해도 좋지만 다시는 당신에게 나를 내주지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"전에도 그렇게 말했었지, 내 사랑. 하지만 당신 같은 천성은 자기 말을 그리 쉽게 지키지 못하오. 그 점에 관해서라면 나 같은 천성도 마찬가지요.\"\n\n\"이게 내가 고생해서 얻은 즐거움이로군요.\" 그녀가 씁쓸하게 속삭였다. \"내가 왜 당신을 다시 불러냈을까? 데이먼, 이따금 내 마음속에 이상한 싸움이 벌어져요. 당신이 나에게 상처를 준 뒤 마음이 가라앉으면 이런 생각이 들어요, '결국 나는 한 조각 흔한 안개를 끌어안고 있는 건가?' 당신은 카멜레온이에요, 그리고 지금 당신은 가장 흉한 빛깔을 띠고 있어요. 집으로 가요, 안 그러면 당신을 미워하게 될 테니!\"\n\n그는 스물을 셀 만큼 동안 멍하니 레인배로 쪽을 바라보다가, 이 모든 게 별로 마음에 두이지 않는다는 듯 말했다. \"그래, 집에 가겠소. 나를 다시 볼 생각이 있소?\"\n\n\"혼사가 깨진 게 당신이 나를 가장 사랑하기 때문이라고 인정한다면요.\"\n\n\"그건 좋은 책략이 못 될 것 같은데.\" 와일디브가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"당신이 자기 힘의 크기를 너무 분명히 알게 될 테니.\"\n\"하지만 말해줘요!\"\n\n\"하지만 말해줘요!\"\n\n\"당신도 알잖소.\" \"그 여자는 지금 어디 있어요?\"\n\n\"모르오. 그 여자 얘기는 당신에게 하고 싶지 않소. 나는 아직 그 여자와 결혼하지 않았고, 당신의 부름에 따라 왔소. 그걸로 됐소.\"\n\n\"나는 그저 따분해서 그 불을 피웠을 뿐이에요, 엔도르의 마녀가 사무엘을 불러냈듯 당신을 불러내 당신을 이겨먹으면 조금은 신이 날까 싶어서요. 당신이 오도록 하겠다고 마음먹었고, 당신은 왔어요! 내 힘을 보여준 거죠. 여기까지 일 마일 반, 다시 당신 집까지 일 마일 반—나로서는 어둠 속에서 삼 마일이에요. 내가 힘을 보여준 게 아닌가요?\"\n\n그가 그녀를 향해 고개를 저었다. \"나는 당신을 너무 잘 아오, 내 유스테이샤. 너무 잘 알아. 내가 모르는 음色이 당신 안엔 하나도 없소. 그 뜨거운 작은 가슴은 죽었다 깨도 그런 냉혹한 술수는 부리지 못하오. 나는 해질 녘 레인배로에서 내 집 쪽을 내려다보는 여자를 보았소. 당신이 나를 끌어내기 전에 내가 당신을 끌어낸 것 같은데.\"\n\n이제 와일디브의 안에서 옛 열정의 되살아난 잉걸불이 또렷이 타올랐고, 그는 그녀의 뺨에 얼굴을 가져다 대려는 듯 몸을 앞으로 기울였다.\n\n\"아, 안 돼요.\" 그녀가 고집스럽게 사그라든 불 반대편으로 옮겨가며 말했다. \"그게 무슨 뜻이었어요?\"\n\n\"당신 손에 입 맞춰도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"안 돼요.\"\n\n\"그럼 악수라도 할까?\"\n\"안 돼요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 둘 다 아쉬워 말고 잘 자라는 인사나 하겠소. 잘 가요, 잘 가요.\"\n\n그녀는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았고, 그는 무도 교사처럼 인사를 하고는 왔던 대로 못 반대편으로 사라졌다.\n\n유스테이샤는 한숨을 쉬었으니—그것은 가냘픈 처녀의 한숨이 아니라 전율처럼 그녀를 뒤흔드는 한숨이었다. 이성의 섬광이 전등불처럼 연인을 비추어—이따금 그러하듯—그의 결점을 드러낼 때마다 그녀는 이렇게 몸을 떨었다. 하지만 그것은 일순간에 지나갔고, 그녀는 변함없이 사랑했다. 그가 자기를 가지고 논다는 것을 알면서도 그녀는 변함없이 사랑했다. 그녀는 반쯤 탄 장작을 흩어버리고는 곧장 안으로 들어가, 불도 없이 침실로 올라갔다. 어둠 속에서 옷을 벗고 있음을 알려주는 옷자락 스치는 소리 사이로 무거운 숨이 자주 섞여 들었고, 십 분 뒤 침대에 잠들어 누웠을 때에도 똑같은 종류의 전율이 이따금 그녀를 훑고 지나갔다.\n\nVII.\n밤의 여왕\n\n유스테이샤 바이는 신성神性의 원료였다. 약간의 다듬질만 거쳤다면 올림포스에서도 잘 해냈을 것이다. 그녀에게는 본보기가 될 만한 여신을 만드는 열정과 본능이 있었으니, 다시 말해 본보기가 될 만한 여자는 못 만드는 그런 것들이었다. 잠시나마 대지와 인류가 온전히 그녀의 손아귀에 들어와 그녀가 마음대로 실패와 물레와 가위를 다룰 수 있었다 한들, 세상에서 그 통치의 교체를 알아챌 사람은 거의 없었을 것이다. 똑같은 운명의 불평등, 한쪽에는 은총이 쌓이고 다른 쪽에는 모욕이 쌓이는 똑같은 적체, 정의에 앞서는 똑같은 관대함, 똑같은 영원한 진퇴양난, 우리가 지금 견디고 있는 것과 똑같이 변덕스럽게 교차하는 애무와 매질이 있었을 것이다.\n\n몸은 사지가 풍만하고 다소 무거웠으며, 불그스름하지도 창백하지도 않았고, 손에 닿는 느낌은 구름처럼 부드러웠다. 그녀의 머리카락을 보면 겨우내 모인 어둠을 다 합쳐도 그 그림자를 이룰 만큼 충분하지 못하리라는 상상이 들었으니—그 머리카락은 서녘의 노을을 지우는 땅거미처럼 그녀의 이마 위로 덮여 내렸다.\n\n그녀의 신경은 그 머릿단 속까지 뻗어 있어서, 그것을 쓸어내리면 언제든 성미를 누그러뜨릴 수 있었다. 머리를 빗질하면 그녀는 즉시 고요 속으로 가라앉아 스핑크스처럼 보였다. 에그던의 둑 하나를 지나다가 그 두꺼운 머릿단이 이따금 그러하듯 일종의 머리빗 노릇을 하는 커다란 가시금작화—울렉스 유로페우스—의 가시 돋친 덤불에 걸리면, 그녀는 몇 걸음 되돌아가 다시 한번 거기에 머리를 스쳤다.\n\n그녀는 밤의 신비로 가득한 이교도의 눈을 지녔으며, 오고 가고 다시 오는 그 눈빛은 그녀의 묵직한 눈꺼풀과 속눈썹에 어느 정도 가려져 있었고, 그중 아래 눈꺼풀은 영국 여자들에게서 흔히 보이는 것보다 훨씬 도톰했다. 이 덕분에 그녀는 공상에 잠겨 있어도 그래 보이지 않을 수 있었으니—눈을 감지 않고도 잠들 수 있는 사람이라 믿어졌을 법했다. 남녀의 영혼이 눈에 보이는 정수精髓라고 가정한다면, 유스테이샤의 영혼 빛깔은 불꽃 같으리라 상상할 수 있었다. 거기서 솟아올라 그녀의 검은 눈동자로 들어가는 불꽃들도 똑같은 인상을 주었다.\n\n그 입은 말하기보다는 떨기 위해, 떨기보다는 입 맞추기 위해 빚어진 듯했다. 누군가는 입 맞추기보다는 비웃듯 휘어지기 위해 빚어졌다고 덧붙였을지도 모른다. 옆에서 보면 그녀 입술이 맞물리는 선은 거의 기하학적 정밀함으로, 디자인 예술에서 키마 렉타cima-recta, 곧 곡선 몰딩이라 불리는 그 잘 알려진 곡선을 이루었다. 음울한 에그던에서 그런 유연한 곡선을 본다는 것은 그야말로 유령의 출현 같은 일이었다. 그 입이, 입술이 머핀의 두 쪽처럼 맞붙던 색슨 해적 무리와 함께 슐레스비히에서 건너온 것이 아님을 누구나 단번에 느꼈다. 그런 입술 곡선은 대개 남쪽 지방에서 잊힌 대리석 조각의 파편으로 땅속에 숨어 있으리라 여겨져왔다. 그녀의 입술 선은 어찌나 고운지, 도톰하면서도 입가의 양 모서리는 창끝처럼 또렷하게 새겨져 있었다. 이 모서리의 날카로움이 무뎌지는 것은 그녀가 별안간 우울에 사로잡힐 때뿐이었으니, 이는 그녀가 제 나이답지 않게 너무도 잘 알고 있던 감정의 밤 같은 면, 그 한 양상이었다.\n\n그녀의 존재는 부르봉 장미와 루비와 열대의 한밤 같은 것들을 떠올리게 했고, 그녀의 기분은 연꽃을 먹는 사람들과 「아탈리」의 행진곡을 떠올리게 했으며, 그녀의 동작은 바다의 썰물과 밀물을, 그녀의 목소리는 비올라를 떠올리게 했다. 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 머리카락을 조금만 고쳐 매면 그녀의 전체적인 자태는 더 높은 여신들 가운데 어느 누구로도 통할 만했다. 머리 뒤의 초승달, 그 위에 얹은 낡은 투구, 이마를 두른 우연한 이슬방울의 왕관이라면 각각 아르테미스나 아테나, 혹은 헤라의 인상을 자아내기에 충분한 부속물이었을 것이며, 여러 존경받는 화폭에서 통용되는 것 못지않게 고대에 근접했을 것이다.\n\n하지만 천상의 위엄과 사랑과 분노와 열정은 저지대 에그던에서는 다소 헛되이 쓰이는 것으로 드러났다. 그녀의 힘은 한정되어 있었고, 이 한계에 대한 의식이 그녀의 성장을 한쪽으로 기울게 했다. 에그던은 그녀의 하데스였으니, 그곳에 온 뒤로 그녀는 그곳의 어두운 기조를 많이 빨아들였으되 속으로는 영원히 그것과 화해하지 못했다. 그녀의 외양은 이 속에서 타들어가는 반항심과 잘 어울렸으며, 그녀 아름다움의 그늘진 광채는 그 안의 슬프고 억눌린 온기의 진짜 표면이었다. 진정한 타르타로스의 위엄이 그녀의 이마에 깃들어 있었으니, 꾸며낸 것도 억지스러운 흔적이 있는 것도 아니었으며, 세월과 함께 그녀 안에서 자라난 것이었다.\n\n머리 윗부분을 가로질러 그녀는 검은 벨벳의 가느다란 머리띠를 두르고 있었는데, 이는 그늘진 머리카락의 무성함을 제어하면서 이마를 불규칙하게 흐림으로써 이런 종류의 위엄을 한층 더해주었다. \"좁은 띠를 이마 위로 두르는 것보다 아름다운 얼굴을 더 돋보이게 하는 것은 없다\"라고 리히터는 말한다. 이웃의 처녀들 중 몇몇은 같은 목적으로 색 리본을 두르고 다른 곳에는 금속 장신구를 자랑삼아 달았지만, 누군가 유스테이샤 바이에게 색 리본과 금속 장신구를 권하면 그녀는 웃으며 가던 길을 갔다.\n\n어째서 이런 부류의 여인이 에그던 황야에 살았던가? 버드머스가 그녀의 고향이었으니, 당시에는 유행하던 해변 휴양지였다. 그녀는 그곳에 주둔했던 한 연대 군악대장의 딸이었으며—코르푸 태생에 빼어난 음악가였던 그는—명문가 출신인 선장 아버지와 함께 그곳으로 여행 온 장차 자기 아내가 될 여인을 만났다. 그 결혼은 군악대장의 호주머니가 그의 직업만큼이나 가벼웠던 탓에 노인의 바람에는 거의 부합하지 않았다. 하지만 그 음악가는 최선을 다했으니, 아내의 성을 따랐고, 영국을 영구히 자기 집으로 삼았으며, 비용은 외할아버지가 댔으나 자식의 교육에 큰 공을 들였고, 아내가 죽기 전까지는 그 지방의 으뜸가는 음악가로 번성하다가, 아내가 죽자 번성하기를 그치고 술을 마시다가 역시 죽었다. 소녀는 외할아버지의 보살핌에 맡겨졌는데, 그는 난파 사고로 갈비뼈 세 대가 부러진 뒤로 이 바람 잘 드는 에그던의 높은 자리에서 살아왔으니, 집을 거저나 다름없이 얻을 수 있었던 데다, 오두막 문에서 보이는 언덕들 사이 지평선의 아련한 푸른빛이 예로부터 영국 해협이라 믿어진 까닭에 그곳이 마음에 들었던 것이다. 그녀는 그 변화를 싫어했고, 추방당한 사람 같은 심정이었으나, 여기서 머물러 살도록 강요받았다.\n\n그리하여 유스테이샤의 머릿속에는 옛 시절과 새 시절에서 온, 가장 기이한 갖가지 관념들이 나란히 놓이게 되었다. 그녀의 시야에는 중경中景이 없었으니—군악대와 장교들과 멋쟁이들에 둘러싸여 해변 산책로에서 보낸 화창한 오후들의 낭만적인 추억이, 그녀를 에워싼 에그던의 어두운 서판 위에 금박 글자처럼 놓여 있었다. 휴양지의 화려함이 황야의 장엄한 엄숙함과 무작위로 뒤얽힘으로써 빚어질 수 있는 온갖 기괴한 효과가 그녀 안에서 발견되었다. 이제는 인간의 삶을 전혀 보지 못하게 되자, 그녀는 자기가 보았던 것을 그만큼 더 상상했다.\n\n그녀의 위엄은 어디서 왔던가? 아버지가 파이아키아 섬 출신이니 알키노오스 가문에서 내려온 잠재된 핏줄에서?—아니면 외할아버지에게 귀족 사촌이 있었으니 피츠앨런과 드 비어 가문에서? 어쩌면 그것은 하늘의 선물—자연 법칙의 다행스러운 수렴이었는지도 모른다. 무엇보다 근래 들어 그녀는 위엄 없이 구는 법을 배울 기회를 박탈당했으니, 외롭게 살았기 때문이다. 황야에서의 고립은 천박함을 거의 불가능하게 만든다. 그녀가 천박해지기란 황야의 조랑말과 박쥐와 뱀이 천박해지는 것만큼이나 어려웠을 것이다. 버드머스에서의 옹졸한 삶이었다면 그녀를 완전히 격하시켰을지도 모른다.\n\n다스릴 영토도 마음도 없이 여왕다워 보이는 유일한 길은 그것을 잃어버린 듯 보이는 것인데, 유스테이샤는 그것을 멋들어지게 해냈다. 선장의 오두막에서 그녀는 한 번도 본 적 없는 대저택을 떠올리게 할 수 있었다. 어쩌면 그것은 그녀가 그 어떤 대저택보다 더 광대한 저택, 곧 탁 트인 언덕을 드나들었기 때문이리라. 그녀를 둘러싼 그곳의 여름 풍경처럼, 그녀는 \"사람으로 붐비는 고독\"이라는 문구의 화신이었으니—겉으로는 그토록 나른하고 공허하고 고요해 보였으나, 실은 분주하고 충만했다.\n\n미칠 듯이 사랑받는 것—이것이 그녀의 커다란 바람이었다. 그녀에게 사랑이란 나날을 갉아먹는 외로움을 몰아내줄 수 있는 단 하나의 강심제였다. 그리고 그녀는 어느 특정한 연인보다도 격렬한 사랑이라 불리는 그 추상적 관념을 갈망하는 듯했다.\n\n그녀는 때때로 더없이 책망하는 눈빛을 지을 수 있었으나, 그것은 인간을 향한 것이라기보다 자기 마음이 빚어낸 어떤 존재들을 향한 것이었으니, 그중 으뜸은 운명이었으며, 사랑이 미끄러져 가는 젊음에만 내려앉는다는 것—자기가 얻을 수 있는 어떤 사랑이든 모래시계의 모래와 함께 동시에 가라앉으리라는 것은 운명의 간섭에서 비롯된 일이라고 그녀는 어렴풋이 여겼다. 그녀는 점점 자라나는 잔혹함의 의식과 함께 그것을 생각했으며, 이 의식은 얻을 수 있는 한 어디서든 일 년의, 일주일의, 단 한 시간의 열정이라도 낚아채려는 무모하게 인습을 벗어난 행동을 낳는 경향이 있었다. 그것이 없었던 탓에 그녀는 즐겁지 않으면서도 노래했고, 누리지 못하면서도 소유했으며, 의기양양하지 못하면서도 남들을 압도했다. 그녀의 외로움은 욕망을 깊게 했다. 에그던에서는 가장 차갑고 가장 보잘것없는 입맞춤조차 기근의 시세였으니, 그녀의 입에 맞먹는 입을 어디서 찾을 수 있었겠는가?\n\n충실함 그 자체를 위한 사랑의 충실함은 대다수 여자들에게보다 그녀에게 덜 매력적이었고, 사랑의 손아귀에 붙들린 까닭의 충실함은 큰 매력이 있었다. 사랑의 불길이 활활 타올랐다가 꺼지는 것이, 오랜 세월 지속되는 똑같은 사랑의 등불 깜빡임보다 나았다. 이 점에 관해서는 대다수 여자들이 경험으로만 배우는 것을 그녀는 예지로써 알았으니—마음속으로 사랑의 둘레를 돌아보고, 그 탑들을 헤아리고, 그 궁전들을 살핀 뒤, 사랑이란 애처로운 기쁨에 지나지 않는다는 결론에 이르렀다. 그러나 사막에 있는 사람이 짠물에라도 감사하듯, 그녀는 그것을 갈망했다.\n\n그녀는 자주 기도를 되풀이했으니, 정해진 시간에 하는 것이 아니라, 꾸밈없이 독실한 사람들처럼 기도하고 싶을 때 했다. 그녀의 기도는 늘 자발적인 것이었고, 흔히 이러했다. \"오, 이 무서운 우울과 외로움에서 제 마음을 구해주소서. 어디서든 제게 위대한 사랑을 보내주소서, 그러지 않으면 저는 죽고 말 것입니다.\"\n\n그녀의 드높은 신들은 정복왕 윌리엄, 스트래퍼드, 나폴레옹 보나파르트였으니, 그녀가 교육받은 학교에서 쓰던 「숙녀를 위한 역사」에 나오는 모습 그대로였다. 만약 그녀가 어머니가 되었다면 야곱이나 다윗 같은 이름보다는—둘 다 그녀가 흠모하지 않았으니—사울이나 시스라 같은 이름을 아들들에게 붙였을 것이다. 학교에서 그녀는 여러 전투에서 블레셋 사람들 편을 들곤 했고, 본디오 빌라도가 솔직하고 공정한 만큼 잘생겼을까 궁금해하기도 했다.\n\n이처럼 그녀는 다소 앞선 사고를 지닌 처녀였으니, 실로 자기 처지에 비추어 따져보면 사상가들 중에서도 가장 뒤떨어진 축에 속하면서도 무척 독창적이었다. 사회적 비순응을 향한 그녀의 본능이 이것의 뿌리였다. 휴일에 관한 한 그녀의 기분은, 풀밭에 풀어놓아져도 큰길에서 제 동족이 일하는 모습을 바라보기 좋아하는 말들의 그것과 같았다. 그녀는 오직 남들이 노동하는 한가운데서 찾아올 때만 자기에게 주어지는 휴식을 귀하게 여겼다. 그래서 그녀는 만물이 쉬는 일요일을 싫어했으며, 일요일이 자기를 죽음으로 몰아넣을 거라고 곧잘 말했다. 황야의 사내들이 일요일 차림으로, 다시 말해 호주머니에 손을 찔러 넣고, 새로 기름칠한 부츠 끈을 매지 않은 채로(이는 유난히 일요일다운 표시였다), 한 주 동안 베어낸 뗏장과 가시금작화 단 사이를 한가로이 거닐며 마치 그 쓰임새를 모르는 양 비판하듯 발로 툭툭 차는 모습을 보는 것은 그녀에게 끔찍한 짐이었다. 이 때아닌 날의 지루함을 달래려고 그녀는 시골 사람들의 토요일 밤 발라드를 흥얼거리며 외할아버지의 낡은 해도海圖와 다른 잡동사니가 든 찬장을 뒤지곤 했다. 하지만 토요일 밤이면 그녀는 곧잘 찬송가를 불렀고, 성경을 읽는 것은 늘 평일이었으니, 의무를 다하고 있다는 부담감에 짓눌리지 않기 위함이었다.\n\n이런 인생관은 어느 정도는 그녀의 처지가 그녀의 천성에 자연스럽게 낳은 산물이었다. 황야에 살면서 그 의미를 헤아리지 않는 것은 외국인과 결혼하면서 그의 언어를 배우지 않는 것과 같았다. 황야의 미묘한 아름다움은 유스테이샤에게는 사라져버렸고, 그녀는 그 안개만을 붙잡았다. 만족한 여자라면 시인으로, 고통받는 여자라면 신봉자로, 경건한 여자라면 시편 작가로, 심지어 들뜬 여자라면 사색가로 만들었을 환경이, 반항하는 여자는 침울한 사람으로 만들었다.\n\n유스테이샤는 표현할 수 없는 영광의 결혼이라는 환상을 이미 넘어선 상태였으나, 그녀의 정념은 충만했음에도 그보다 못한 결합에는 마음을 두지 않았다. 그리하여 우리는 그녀가 기이한 고립 상태에 처한 것을 본다. 우리가 원하는 것을 할 수 있다는 신과 같은 자만을 잃어버리고도 우리가 할 수 있는 것을 하는 소박한 열의를 얻지 못했다는 것은, 추상적으로는 흠잡을 수 없는 기개의 위대함을 보여주는 것이니, 실망했을지언정 타협을 거부하는 정신을 가리키기 때문이다. 하지만 철학에는 어울릴지언정 그것은 공동체에는 위험하기 십상이다. 무언가를 한다는 것이 결혼함을 뜻하고 공동체가 마음과 손의 공동체인 세상에서는, 그런 상태에 똑같은 위험이 따른다.\n\n그리하여 우리는 우리의 유스테이샤가—이따금 그녀는 아주 사랑스럽지 않은 것도 아니었으니—무엇 하나 할 가치가 없다고 느끼는 그런 깨달음의 단계에 이르러, 더 나은 대상이 없는 탓에 와일디브를 이상화하며 자기 존재의 남는 시간을 채우는 것을 본다. 이것이 그가 우위를 점한 유일한 이유였으니, 그녀 자신도 그것을 알았다. 어떤 순간이면 그녀의 자존심이 그를 향한 정념에 맞서 반항했고, 그녀는 심지어 자유로워지기를 갈망하기도 했다. 하지만 그를 밀어낼 수 있는 상황은 단 하나뿐이었으니, 그것은 더 위대한 남자의 등장이었다.\n\n그 밖에 그녀는 심한 우울에 시달렸고, 그것을 회복하려고 느릿한 산책을 다녔으니, 그럴 때면 외할아버지의 망원경과 외할머니의 모래시계를 들고 다녔는데—후자는 시간이 점차 미끄러져 가는 것을 물질로 나타낸 모습을 지켜보는 데서 얻는 독특한 즐거움 때문이었다. 그녀는 좀처럼 책략을 꾸미지 않았으나, 꾸밀 때면 그녀의 계획은 여자다운 잔재주라기보다 장군의 포괄적인 전략을 드러냈으니, 다만 직설적이고 싶지 않을 때면 델포이 신탁처럼 모호한 말을 내뱉을 수 있었다. 천국에서 그녀는 아마 엘로이즈들과 클레오파트라들 사이에 앉을 것이다.\n\nVIII.\n아무도 없다는 곳에서 발견되는 이들\n\n슬픈 어린 소년은 불에서 물러나자마자 마치 그것으로 용기를 북돋우려는 듯 손바닥 안에 동전을 꼭 쥐고 달리기 시작했다. 에그던 황야의 이 지역에서 아이가 혼자 집에 가도록 두는 것은 실로 위험이 거의 없는 일이었다. 소년의 집까지 거리는 팔분의 삼 마일이 채 안 되었으니, 그의 아버지의 오두막과 거기서 몇 야드 더 떨어진 또 다른 오두막 한 채가 미스토버 냅이라는 작은 마을의 일부를 이루었고, 셋째이자 유일하게 남은 집은 바이 선장과 유스테이샤의 집이었는데, 이 집은 작은 오두막들에서 완전히 떨어진 채 이 인구 드문 비탈에서 가장 외로운 집들 가운데서도 가장 외로운 집이었다.\n\n소년은 숨이 찰 때까지 달리다가, 한결 용감해지자 한가로이 걸으며 뱃사람 소년과 어여쁜 아가씨, 그리고 쟁여둔 빛나는 금에 관한 짧은 노래를 늙은이 같은 목소리로 불렀다. 그 노래 도중에 아이는 멈춰 섰으니—앞쪽 언덕 아래 구덩이에서 불빛이 비쳐 나왔고, 거기서 떠도는 먼지구름과 철썩이는 소리가 흘러나왔던 것이다.\n\n흔치 않은 광경과 소리만이 소년을 겁먹게 했다. 황야의 쪼그라든 목소리는 익숙한 것이었기에 그를 놀라게 하지 않았다. 이따금 가는 길에 나타나는 가시덤불은 좀 덜 만족스러웠으니, 음울하게 휘파람 소리를 냈고, 어두워진 뒤에는 펄쩍 뛰는 미치광이나 사지를 뻗은 거인, 흉측한 절름발이의 형상을 띠는 섬뜩한 버릇이 있었기 때문이다. 이날 저녁 불빛은 드물지 않았으나, 그 모든 불빛의 성질은 이것과 달랐다. 공포라기보다 신중함에 이끌려 소년은 그 불빛을 지나치는 대신 되돌아가기로 했으니, 유스테이샤 바이 아씨에게 하인을 딸려 집까지 바래다주게 해달라고 청할 셈이었다.\n\n소년이 다시 계곡 꼭대기로 올라와 보니 불은 전보다 낮아지긴 했으나 여전히 둑 위에서 타오르고 있었다. 그 곁에는 유스테이샤의 외로운 형체 대신 두 사람이 있었으니, 둘째는 한 남자였다. 소년은 자신의 보잘것없고 하찮은 용건 때문에 유스테이샤 아씨처럼 훌륭한 존재를 방해하는 것이 분별 있는 일일지를 그 일의 성질로부터 가늠해보려고 둑 아래로 살금살금 기어갔다.\n\n둑 아래에서 몇 분 동안 대화를 엿들은 뒤 그는 어리둥절하고 망설이는 태도로 돌아서서 왔던 것처럼 소리 없이 물러나기 시작했다. 그가 대체로 보아, 그녀의 노여움 전체를 감당할 각오 없이 와일디브와의 대화를 방해하는 것이 현명하다고 여기지 않았음은 분명했다.\n\n가난한 소년에게는 이것이 스킬라와 카리브디스 사이의 처지였다. 다시 들킬 염려 없는 곳에서 멈춰 선 그는 마침내 더 작은 악으로서 구덩이의 현상과 마주하기로 작정했다. 무거운 한숨과 함께 그는 비탈을 되짚어 올라가, 전에 따랐던 길을 따라갔다.\n\n불빛은 사라졌고, 피어오르던 먼지도 사라졌으니—그가 바라기로는 영영. 그는 단호히 행진해 갔고, 모래 구덩이에서 몇 야드 안에 이르렀을 때 앞쪽에서 희미한 소리가 들려 멈춰 서기 전까지는 그를 놀라게 할 만한 것을 아무것도 찾지 못했다. 멈춤은 잠깐뿐이었으니, 그 소리가 풀을 뜯는 두 짐승의 꾸준한 씹는 소리로 판명되었기 때문이다.\n\n\"황야 조랑말 두 마리가 여기 내려와 있네.\" 그가 소리 내어 말했다. \"이렇게 멀리까지 내려온 건 처음 봐.\"\n\n짐승들은 그의 길 정면에 있었으나 아이는 그것을 대수롭지 않게 여겼으니, 어려서부터 말들의 발목 둘레에서 놀았던 것이다. 하지만 가까이 다가가자 소년은 그 작은 짐승들이 달아나지 않는 것과, 길을 잘못 들지 못하도록 저마다 나무 차꼬를 차고 있는 것을 보고 다소 놀랐으니, 이는 그것들이 길들여졌음을 뜻했다. 이제 그는 구덩이 내부를 볼 수 있었는데, 언덕 옆구리에 자리 잡은 까닭에 입구가 평평했다. 가장 안쪽 모서리에는 짐마차의 네모난 윤곽이 그를 향해 뒤를 둔 채 나타났다. 내부에서 불빛이 새어 나와, 마차가 마주한 구덩이 저편의 수직 자갈 면 위로 움직이는 그림자를 던졌다.\n\n아이는 이것이 집시의 마차라고 짐작했고, 그 떠돌이들에 대한 그의 두려움은 고통이라기보다 오히려 짜릿함을 주는 정도의 가벼운 수준에 머물렀다. 단지 몇 인치의 진흙 벽이 그와 그의 가족을 집시 신세에서 떼어놓고 있을 뿐이었다. 그는 적당한 거리를 두고 자갈 구덩이를 빙 돌아 비탈을 올라, 마차의 열린 문 안을 들여다보고 그림자의 원본을 보려고 꼭대기로 나아갔다.\n\n그 광경에 소년은 겁을 먹었다. 마차 안 작은 난로 옆에 머리끝부터 발끝까지 붉은 형체가 앉아 있었으니—토머신의 친구였던 그 남자였다. 그는 양말을 깁고 있었는데, 양말도 그의 나머지처럼 붉었다. 게다가 양말을 기우며 파이프를 피우고 있었는데, 파이프의 대와 통도 붉었다.\n\n이 순간 바깥 그림자 속에서 풀을 뜯던 황야 조랑말 하나가 발에 채운 차꼬를 떨어내려고 흔드는 소리가 또렷이 들렸다. 그 소리에 깨어난 레들맨은 양말을 내려놓고 곁에 걸린 등을 켜고 마차에서 나왔다. 양초를 꽂아 세우면서 그가 등을 얼굴로 들어올리자, 불빛이 그의 흰자위와 상아 같은 이를 비췄고, 둘러싼 붉은빛과 대비되어 그것은 어린아이의 눈에는 충분히 섬뜩한 모습을 그에게 입혔다. 소년은 자기가 누구의 소굴에 발을 들였는지 마음이 편치 않을 만큼 너무 잘 알았다. 집시보다 더 흉한 사람들이 이따금 에그던을 건너는 것으로 알려져 있었는데, 레들맨도 그중 하나였다.\n\n\"그냥 집시였으면 좋으련만!\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n남자는 이때쯤 말들에게서 돌아오고 있었다. 들킬까 봐 두려운 나머지 소년은 초조한 동작으로 도리어 들킴을 자초하고 말았다. 히스와 토탄 층이 구덩이 꼭대기 위로 매트처럼 드리워져 실제 가장자리를 가리고 있었다. 소년은 단단한 땅 너머로 발을 디뎠고, 이제 히스가 무너지면서 그는 회색 모래 비탈을 굴러 떨어져 바로 그 남자의 발치에 이르렀다.\n\n붉은 남자는 등을 열어 엎어진 소년의 형체에 그것을 비췄다.\n\n\"너는 누구냐?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"조니 넌서치예요, 나리!\"\n\n\"거기서 뭘 하고 있었느냐?\"\n\n\"몰라요.\"\n\n\"나를 지켜보고 있었던 게로구나?\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"왜 나를 지켜봤느냐?\"\n\n\"바이 아씨네 모닥불에서 집에 오는 길이었거든요.\"\n\n\"다쳤느냐?\"\n\n\"아니, 다쳤구나—손에서 피가 나는걸. 내 차양 아래로 와라, 싸매주마.\"\n\n\"제 육펜스 좀 찾게 해주세요.\"\n\n\"그건 어떻게 생긴 거냐?\"\n\n\"바이 아씨가 모닥불을 계속 지펴준 값으로 주셨어요.\"\n\n육펜스를 찾았고, 남자는 마차로 갔으며, 소년은 거의 숨을 죽인 채 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n남자는 바느질 도구가 든 가방에서 헝겊 조각을 꺼내, 다른 모든 것처럼 붉게 물든 한 가닥을 찢어내 상처를 싸매기 시작했다.\n\n\"눈앞이 안개 낀 것 같아요—앉아도 될까요, 나리?\" 소년이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼, 가엾은 녀석. 어지러울 만도 하지. 저 보따리에 앉아라.\"\n\n남자는 베인 상처를 다 싸맸고, 소년이 말했다. \"이제 집에 가야겠어요, 나리.\"\n\n\"네가 나를 좀 무서워하는구나. 내가 뭔지 아느냐?\"\n\n아이는 그의 주홍빛 형체를 위아래로 미심쩍게 훑어보다가 마침내 말했다. \"네.\"\n\n\"그래, 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"레들맨이요!\" 그가 떠듬떠듬 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그게 나다. 그렇지만 레들맨은 한 명이 아니다. 너희 어린것들은 뻐꾸기도 한 마리, 여우도 한 마리, 거인도 하나, 악마도 하나, 레들맨도 하나뿐인 줄 알지만, 우리는 잔뜩 있단다.\"\n\n\"그래요? 나리, 나를 자루에 넣어 데려가진 않을 거죠? 레들맨이 가끔 그런다던데요.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리. 레들맨이 하는 일이라곤 레들을 파는 것뿐이다. 내 마차 뒤에 있는 이 자루들이 다 보이지? 그건 어린 소년들로 차 있는 게 아니라—그냥 붉은 가루로만 차 있어.\"\n\n\"레들맨으로 태어났어요?\"\n\n\"아니, 이 일을 하게 된 거지. 이 장사를 그만두면 나도 너처럼 하얘질 거다—그러니까 시간이 지나면 하얘질 거야—아마 여섯 달쯤. 처음에는 아니지, 이게 살갗에 배어들어 씻어도 안 빠지니까. 자, 이제 다시는 레들맨을 무서워하지 않겠지?\"\n\n\"네, 절대로요. 윌리 오처드가 일전에 여기서 붉은 귀신을 봤다고 했는데—그게 나리였나 봐요?\"\n\n\"내가 일전에 여기 있었지.\"\n\n\"방금 제가 본 그 먼지 같은 불빛을 만들고 있었어요?\"\n\n\"오 그래, 자루를 좀 두드려 털고 있었지. 그래, 저 위에서 모닥불은 잘 즐겼느냐? 불빛을 봤다. 바이 아씨가 왜 그렇게 모닥불을 간절히 원해서 너한테 육펜스를 주며 계속 지피게 한 거냐?\"\n\n\"몰라요. 저는 피곤했는데, 아씨가 계속 레인배로 쪽으로 오르락내리락하는 동안 저더러 그대로 남아서 불을 지키라고 시켰어요.\"\n\n\"그게 얼마나 계속됐느냐?\"\n\n\"개구리가 못으로 뛰어들 때까지요.\"\n\n레들맨은 갑자기 한가로이 떠들기를 멈췄다. \"개구리?\" 그가 물었다. \"개구리는 이맘때 못으로 뛰어들지 않는데.\"\n\n\"뛰어들어요, 제가 한 마리 들었거든요.\"\n\n\"틀림없느냐?\"\n\n\"네. 아씨가 미리 들릴 거라고 했고, 정말 그랬어요. 사람들이 그러는데 아씨는 영리하고 속이 깊대요, 어쩌면 아씨가 그걸 홀려서 오게 했나 봐요.\"\n\n\"그래서 어떻게 됐느냐?\"\n\n\"그래서 저는 여기로 내려왔는데, 무서워서 되돌아갔어요. 그렇지만 그 신사분 때문에 아씨한테 말 걸기가 싫어서 다시 이리로 온 거예요.\"\n\n\"신사라—아! 아씨가 그 사람한테 뭐라고 하더냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"신사분이 다른 여자랑 결혼하지 않은 건 옛 연인을 가장 좋아하기 때문인 것 같다고 했어요, 그런 비슷한 얘기요.\"\n\n\"신사가 아씨한테 뭐라고 하더냐, 우리 아가?\"\n\n\"그저 아씨를 가장 좋아한다고만 했어요, 그리고 밤마다 레인배로 아래로 아씨를 다시 보러 오겠다고요.\"\n\n\"하!\" 레들맨이 마차 옆구리를 손으로 철썩 쳐서 한 방에 그 구조물 전체가 흔들렸다. \"그게 비밀이로구나!\"\n\n어린 소년이 걸상에서 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다.\n\n\"얘야, 무서워 마라.\" 붉은 것을 파는 사람이 갑자기 부드러워지며 말했다. \"네가 여기 있는 걸 잊었구나. 이건 그저 레들맨들이 잠깐 미쳐 날뛰는 별난 버릇일 뿐이다, 그렇지만 아무도 해치진 않아. 그래서 아씨가 그다음에 뭐라고 하더냐?\"\n\n\"기억이 안 나요. 레들맨 나리, 이제 집에 가도 될까요?\"\n\n\"그럼, 물론 가도 되지. 내가 좀 바래다주마.\"\n\n그는 소년을 자갈 구덩이에서 데리고 나와 그의 어머니의 오두막으로 이어지는 길에 들여놓았다. 그 작은 형체가 어둠 속으로 사라지자 레들맨은 돌아와 다시 불가의 자리에 앉아 또다시 양말을 깁기 시작했다.\n\nIX.\n사랑은 약삭빠른 사내를 책략으로 이끌고\n\n옛 부류의 레들맨은 이제 좀처럼 볼 수 없다. 철도가 도입된 이후로 웨식스의 농부들은 이 메피스토펠레스 같은 방문객들 없이도 그럭저럭 지내게 되었고, 양치기들이 양을 장에 내보내려고 손질할 때 그토록 많이 쓰던 그 선명한 안료도 다른 경로로 구하게 되었다. 아직 살아남은 이들조차 그들의 특징이었던 존재의 시정詩情을 잃어가고 있으니, 그 시절 이 장사를 한다는 것은 재료를 캐내는 구덩이로 주기적으로 여행을 떠나고, 한겨울만 빼고는 달마다 규칙적으로 야영을 하며, 수백으로 헤아릴 만한 농장들 사이를 두루 떠도는 일을 뜻했으되, 이런 아랍인 같은 생활에도 불구하고 결코 비는 일이 없는 두둑한 지갑이 보증하는 그 체면을 지켜내는 일을 뜻했다.\n\n레들은 닿는 모든 것 위에 제 생기 넘치는 빛깔을 퍼뜨리며, 그것을 반 시간만 다룬 사람이라면 누구든 카인의 표식처럼 틀림없는 낙인을 찍는다.\n\n아이가 레들맨을 처음 본 것은 그의 인생에서 한 시대였다. 그 핏빛 형체는 상상이 시작된 이래 어린 영혼을 괴롭혀온 온갖 끔찍한 꿈들의 승화였다. \"레들맨이 너를 잡으러 온다!\"는 여러 세대에 걸쳐 웨식스 어머니들의 공식적인 위협이었다. 그는 금세기 초 한동안 보나파르트에게 성공적으로 자리를 빼앗겼으나, 세월의 흐름이 후자의 인물을 진부하고 무력하게 만들자 더 오래된 그 문구가 본래의 두드러짐을 되찾았다. 그리고 이제 레들맨은 그 차례가 되어 보나파르트의 뒤를 따라 닳아빠진 도깨비들의 땅으로 갔으며, 그의 자리는 근대의 발명품들로 채워진다.\n\n레들맨은 집시처럼 살았으나, 집시는 경멸했다. 그는 떠돌이 바구니 장수나 돗자리 장수만큼이나 그럭저럭 형편이 괜찮았으나, 그들과는 아무 상관이 없었다. 그는 떠돌이 중에 그를 지나치고 또 지나치던 가축 몰이꾼들보다 더 점잖게 태어나 자랐으나, 그들은 그에게 그저 고갯짓이나 했을 뿐이다. 그의 물건은 행상꾼들의 것보다 더 값졌으나, 그들은 그렇게 여기지 않고 정면만 바라본 채 그의 마차를 지나쳤다. 그는 보기에 어찌나 부자연스러운 빛깔이었던지 회전목마와 밀랍 인형 가게의 사내들조차 그의 곁에서는 신사처럼 보였으나, 그는 그들을 천한 무리로 여겨 거리를 두었다. 이 모든 무단 거주자들과 길 위의 사람들 사이에서 레들맨은 끊임없이 자신을 발견했으되, 그는 그들에 속하지 않았다. 그의 직업은 그를 고립시키는 경향이 있었고, 고립된 채로 그는 대개 눈에 띄었다.\n\n레들맨이 범죄자들이며, 다른 사람들이 그들의 악행 때문에 부당하게 고초를 겪는 거라는—법은 피했어도 양심은 피하지 못하여 평생의 속죄로서 이 장사에 들어섰다는 말이 때때로 나돌았다. 안 그러면 어째서 그것을 택했겠는가? 지금의 경우에 그런 의문은 특히나 적절했을 것이다. 그날 오후 에그던에 들어선 레들맨은, 흉한 토대로도 충분했을 그 목적을 위해 기이함의 바탕을 이루는 데 호감 가는 것이 허비된 사례였다. 이 레들맨에게서 거부감을 일으키는 단 한 가지는 그의 빛깔이었다. 그것에서 벗어났다면 그는 좀처럼 보기 힘들 만큼 호감 가는 시골 사내의 본보기였을 것이다. 예리한 관찰자라면—이는 실로 부분적으로 진실이었으니—그가 흥미를 잃어 인생에서 제 마땅한 자리를 내놓았다고 생각하기 쉬웠을 것이다. 게다가 그를 보고 나면 누구든, 선한 천성과, 교활함으로 치우치기 직전까지 갈 만큼 극도로 예리한 면이 그의 성격의 골격을 이룬다고 짐작했을 것이다.\n\n양말을 깁는 동안 그의 얼굴은 생각으로 굳어졌다. 그 뒤로 한결 부드러운 표정이 따랐고, 다시금 그날 오후 큰길을 달리는 동안 그에게 깃들어 있던 애틋한 슬픔이 되살아났다. 이윽고 그의 바늘이 멈췄다. 그는 양말을 내려놓고 자리에서 일어나, 마차 모퉁이의 갈고리에서 가죽 주머니를 꺼냈다. 거기에는 다른 물건들과 함께 갈색 종이 꾸러미가 들어 있었는데, 닳아빠진 접힌 자국이 경첩 같은 성질을 띤 것으로 보아 여러 차례 조심스럽게 펼쳐졌다 닫혔던 모양이었다. 그는 마차 안의 유일한 자리인 세 발 달린 젖 짜는 의자에 앉아, 양초 불빛으로 꾸러미를 살피다가 거기서 낡은 편지 한 통을 꺼내 펼쳤다. 글씨는 본디 흰 종이 위에 쓰였으나, 편지는 이제 처해 있던 곳의 사정으로 연한 붉은빛을 띠게 되었고, 그 위의 검은 필획은 주홍빛 노을을 배경으로 한 겨울 산울타리의 잔가지처럼 보였다. 편지에는 그때로부터 두 해쯤 전의 날짜가 적혀 있었고, \"토머신 요브라이트\"라고 서명되어 있었다. 내용은 다음과 같았다—\n\n친애하는 디고리 벤에게,—폰드클로즈에서 집으로 오는 저를 따라잡으셨을 때 던지신 그 질문에 저는 너무 놀란 나머지 제가 무슨 뜻이었는지 정확히 이해시켜드리지 못한 것 같아 두렵습니다. 물론 숙모님이 저를 만나지만 않으셨다면 그 자리에서 곧장 다 설명드릴 수 있었겠지만, 형편이 그렇지 못했지요. 저는 그 후로 줄곧 마음이 편치 않았으니, 아시다시피 당신을 아프게 하고 싶지 않은데도, 그때 제가 한 듯한 말을 뒤집음으로써 지금 그러고 있을까 봐 두렵기 때문입니다. 디고리, 저는 당신과 결혼할 수도, 당신이 저를 연인이라 부르게 할 생각을 할 수도 없습니다. 정말로 그럴 수 없어요, 디고리. 제가 이런 말을 한다고 너무 마음 쓰거나 크게 아파하지 않으시기를 바랍니다. 그러실까 생각하면 무척 슬퍼지니, 저는 당신을 무척 좋아하고, 마음속으로 늘 당신을 사촌 클림 다음에 두었기 때문입니다. 우리가 결혼할 수 없는 이유가 너무 많아서 편지에 다 적기조차 어려울 정도예요. 당신이 저를 따라오셨을 때 그런 이야기를 하시리라고는 조금도 예상하지 못했으니, 저는 당신을 연인이라는 의미로는 전혀 생각해본 적이 없었기 때문입니다. 당신이 말씀하셨을 때 제가 웃었다고 저를 욕하지 말아주세요. 제가 당신을 어리석은 사람이라며 비웃었다고 생각하셨다면 오해하신 거예요. 저는 그 생각이 너무 이상해서 웃은 것이지, 당신을 비웃은 게 결코 아닙니다. 제 개인적인 본심에서 당신이 저에게 구애하도록 두지 못하는 가장 큰 이유는, 당신의 아내가 될 뜻으로 당신과 함께 걸음을 같이하기로 승낙하는 여자가 마땅히 느껴야 할 감정을 제가 느끼지 못한다는 것입니다. 당신이 생각하시듯 제 마음에 다른 사람이 있어서가 아니니, 저는 아무도 부추기지 않으며 평생 그런 적도 없었습니다. 또 다른 이유는 제 숙모님입니다. 제가 당신을 원한다 해도 숙모님은 동의하지 않으실 거예요. 숙모님은 당신을 무척 좋아하시지만, 제가 소규모 낙농가보다는 조금 더 높이 보아 전문직 사내와 결혼하기를 바라실 거예요. 솔직하게 적었다고 저에게 마음을 닫지 않으시기를 바라지만, 당신이 저를 다시 보려 하실지도 모른다는 생각이 들었고, 우리는 만나지 않는 편이 낫겠어요. 저는 늘 당신을 선한 사람으로 생각하고 당신이 잘되기를 간절히 바라겠습니다. 이 편지는 제인 오처드의 어린 하녀 편에 보냅니다,—그럼 이만 줄이며, 디고리, 당신의 충실한 벗,\n\n토머신 요브라이트.\n낙농가 벤 씨께.\n\n오래전 어느 가을 아침에 그 편지가 도착한 이래로 레들맨과 토머신은 오늘까지 만나지 못했다. 그 사이에 그는 레들 장사를 택함으로써 자신의 처지를 본디보다 그녀에게서 한층 더 멀리 옮겨놓았으나, 실은 여전히 형편이 무척 넉넉했다. 실로 지출이 수입의 사분의 일에 불과한 것을 보면 그는 번창한 사내라 불릴 만했다.\n\n거절당한 구혼자는 벌집에서 쫓겨난 벌처럼 자연스레 떠돌게 되며, 그가 냉소적으로 몸 바친 그 일은 여러모로 벤에게 잘 맞았다. 하지만 그의 떠돎은 단지 옛 감정의 압박만으로도 곧잘 에그던 방향을 향했으니, 비록 그곳으로 그를 끌어들인 그녀에게 결코 끼어들지는 않았지만 말이다. 토머신의 황야에 머무는 것, 그녀 가까이에 있되 눈에 띄지 않는 것이 그에게 남은 단 하나의 귀한 즐거움이었다.\n\n그러다 그날의 사건이 일어났고, 여전히 그녀를 깊이 사랑하던 레들맨은 중대한 고비에 그녀에게 우연히 봉사하게 된 데에 자극받아, 지금껏처럼 한숨이나 쉬며 멀찍이 떨어져 있는 대신, 그녀의 대의에 적극적인 헌신을 바치기로 맹세하게 되었다. 그날 일어난 일을 보고서 그가 와일디브의 의도가 정직한지 의심하지 않을 수는 없었다. 하지만 그녀의 희망은 분명 그에게 쏠려 있었고, 벤은 자기 회한을 떨쳐버리고 그녀가 스스로 택한 방식으로 행복해지도록 돕기로 작정했다. 이 방식이 다른 무엇보다도 그 자신에게 가장 괴로운 것이라는 점은 충분히 거북한 일이었으나, 레들맨의 사랑은 너그러웠다.\n\n토머신의 이익을 보살피려는 그의 첫 적극적 발걸음은 이튿날 저녁 일곱 시 무렵에 내디뎌졌으며, 슬픈 소년에게서 알아낸 소식이 시킨 것이었다. 유스테이샤가 어떻게든 결혼에 관한 와일디브의 무관심의 원인이라는 것이, 그 둘 사이의 은밀한 만남을 듣자마자 벤이 내린 결론이었다. 유스테이샤가 와일디브에게 보낸 사랑의 신호가 외할아버지가 가져온 소식이 버림받은 미인에게 미친 애틋한 영향이라는 데에는 그의 생각이 미치지 못했다. 그의 본능은 그녀를 토머신의 행복에 앞선 장애물이라기보다는 그것을 거스르는 음모자로 여기는 쪽이었다.\n\n낮 동안 그는 토머신의 형편을 알고 싶어 몹시 안달했으나, 자신이 낯선 이로 머무는 문턱에, 특히 지금처럼 이토록 거북한 순간에 끼어들 엄두를 내지 못했다. 그는 조랑말들과 짐을 끌고 황야의 새 지점으로, 이전 자리에서 동쪽으로 옮기며 시간을 보냈고, 여기서 바람과 비를 피할 자리를 신중한 눈으로 골랐는데, 이는 그곳에서의 체류가 비교적 길어지리라는 뜻인 듯했다. 그 후 그는 왔던 길의 일부를 걸어서 되돌아갔고, 이제 어두워졌으므로 왼쪽으로 방향을 틀어 레인배로에서 스무 야드도 안 떨어진 구덩이 가장자리의 호랑가시나무 덤불 뒤에 섰다.\n\n그는 거기서 만남을 기다렸으나, 헛되이 기다렸다. 그날 밤 그 자신 말고는 아무도 그곳에 가까이 오지 않았다.\n\n하지만 헛수고는 레들맨에게 별다른 영향을 주지 않았다. 그는 탄탈로스의 처지에 서본 적이 있었고, 어느 정도의 실망은 모든 실현에 앞선 자연스러운 서막이며, 그 서막이 없다면 도리어 불안을 일으킬 거라고 여기는 듯했다. 이튿날 저녁 같은 시각에 그는 다시 같은 자리에 있었으나, 기다리던 밀회의 두 사람, 유스테이샤와 와일디브는 나타나지 않았다.\n\n그는 나흘 밤을 더 똑같은 방식으로 밀고 나갔으되 성과가 없었다. 하지만 그다음 날, 곧 그들의 앞선 만남에서 꼭 일주일 되는 날, 그는 능선을 따라 떠가는 여자의 형체와 계곡에서 올라오는 젊은 남자의 윤곽을 보았다. 그들은 봉분을 에워싼 작은 도랑—고대 브리튼 사람들이 봉분을 쌓아올리려고 본디 파낸 자리—에서 만났다.\n\n토머신에게 부당한 일이 벌어진다는 의심에 찔린 레들맨은 한순간에 책략에 눈을 떴다. 그는 즉시 덤불을 떠나 손과 무릎으로 기어 앞으로 나아갔다. 들키지 않고 안전하게 갈 수 있는 만큼 가까이 다가가 보니, 맞바람 탓에 밀회하는 두 사람의 대화를 엿들을 수 없었다.\n\n그의 가까이에는, 황야 곳곳의 여러 곳에서처럼, 겨울 날씨에 앞서 티머시 페어웨이가 옮겨가기를 기다리며 모로 세워져 뒤집힌 큰 뗏장이 흩어진 곳들이 있었다. 그는 누운 채로 그중 둘을 집어, 하나는 머리와 어깨를, 다른 하나는 등과 다리를 덮을 때까지 제 위로 끌어왔다. 이제 레들맨은 대낮에도 거의 보이지 않게 되었을 것이니, 히스가 위로 향한 채 그 위에 선 뗏장들은 영락없이 자라고 있는 것처럼 보였다. 그는 다시 기어갔고, 등에 얹힌 뗏장들도 그와 함께 기어갔다. 그가 아무것도 덮지 않고 다가갔더라면 십중팔구 어스름 속에서 눈에 띄지 않았을 테지만, 이렇게 다가가니 마치 땅속으로 파고드는 듯했다. 이런 식으로 그는 둘이 서 있는 곳에 아주 가까이 이르렀다.\n\n\"이 일을 두고 나랑 의논하고 싶다고요?\" 유스테이샤 바이의 풍성하고 격정적인 어조가 그의 귀에 닿았다. \"나랑 의논해요? 그렇게 말하다니 나에 대한 모욕이에요—더는 못 참겠어요!\" 그녀가 울기 시작했다. \"나는 당신을 사랑했고, 후회막급일 만큼 당신을 사랑한다는 걸 당신에게 보여주었는데, 그런데도 당신은 와서 토머신과 결혼하는 게 낫지 않겠는지 나랑 의논하고 싶다고 그 차디찬 투로 말하다니. 낫다—당연히 낫겠죠. 그 여자랑 결혼해요—그 여자는 나보다 당신 처지에 더 가까우니까!\"\n\n\"그래, 그래. 그건 다 좋은 말이오.\" 와일디브가 단호하게 말했다. \"하지만 우리는 일을 있는 그대로 봐야 하오. 이 일을 빚어낸 데 어떤 비난이 나에게 따르든, 토머신의 처지는 지금 당신 것보다 훨씬 나쁘오. 나는 그저 내가 진퇴양난에 빠졌다고 말하는 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 나한테 말하지 말아요! 그건 그저 나를 괴롭히는 짓일 뿐이라는 걸 알아야죠. 데이먼, 당신은 처신을 잘하지 못했어요. 내 안에서 당신은 추락했어요. 당신은 내 호의를—훨씬 더 야심 찬 것들을 생각하곤 했던 한 숙녀가 당신을 사랑하는 그 호의를—귀하게 여기지 않았어요. 하지만 그건 토머신 탓이에요. 그 여자가 당신을 나에게서 빼앗아갔으니, 그 때문에 고초를 겪어 마땅해요. 그 여자는 지금 어디 머물러요? 내가 신경 쓴다는 게 아니라, 내가 어디 있는지도 모르겠어요. 아, 내가 죽어 없어진다면 그 여자가 얼마나 기뻐할까! 그 여자가 어디 있느냐고요?\"\n\n\"토머신은 지금 숙모 댁에 머물면서 침실에 틀어박혀 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않으려 하고 있소.\" 그가 무심히 말했다.\n\n\"지금도 당신이 그 여자한테 별로 마음을 쓰는 것 같진 않네요.\" 유스테이샤가 갑작스러운 환희에 차서 말했다. \"마음을 쓴다면 그렇게 냉담하게 그 여자 얘기를 하진 않을 테니까요. 그 여자한테도 나 얘기를 그렇게 냉담하게 해요? 아, 그러겠죠! 애초에 왜 나에게서 떠나갔어요? 한 가지 조건이 아니면 나는 당신을 영영 용서할 수 없을 것 같아요, 그 조건이란 당신이 언제 나를 버리든 나에게 그렇게 한 걸 뉘우치며 다시 돌아오는 거예요.\"\n\n\"나는 결코 당신을 버리고 싶지 않소.\"\n\n\"그건 고맙지 않네요. 모든 게 매끄럽기만 한 건 싫어요. 사실 나는 당신이 가끔 한 번씩 나를 조금 버려주는 게 좋아요. 사랑은 연인이 너무 정직하면 더없이 음울한 거예요. 오, 이런 말 하다니 부끄럽지만, 그래도 사실인걸요!\" 그녀가 가벼운 웃음을 지었다. \"그런 생각만으로도 내 기운이 가라앉기 시작해요. 나에게 길든 사랑 따위 내밀지 말아요, 안 그러면 당장 꺼져버려요!\"\n\n와일드브가 입을 열어 말하길, \"탐신이 그토록 지독하게 착한 여자만 아니었더라면, 그래서 누구 멀쩡한 사람에게 상처 주지 않으면서도 당신한테 충실할 수 있었더라면 좋았을 텐데, 결국 죄인은 나요, 나는 당신네 둘 가운데 어느 쪽의 새끼손가락만도 못한 인간이니.\"\n\n\"하지만 무슨 정의감 때문에 자신을 그 여자한테 희생할 건 없어요.\" 유스테시아가 재빨리 받아쳤다. \"그 여자를 사랑하지 않는다면 길게 보아서는 지금 그대로 놓아두는 게 가장 자비로운 일이에요. 그게 언제나 가장 좋은 방법이고요. 자, 이제 보니 내가 또 여자답지 못한 소리를 했나 봐요. 당신이 떠나고 나면 나는 늘 당신한테 한 말들 때문에 나 자신에게 화가 나니.\"\n\n와일드브는 대꾸 없이 히스 사이를 한두 걸음 거닐었다. 그 침묵을 바람 불어오는 쪽 멀지 않은 곳에 선 가지치기한 가시나무의 가락이 채웠는데, 미풍이 체를 통과하듯 그 굽힘 없는 잔가지들 사이로 걸러져 나왔다. 마치 밤이 이를 악문 채 만가를 부르는 것 같았다.\n\n그녀는 반쯤 서글픈 목소리로 이어 말했다. \"지난번 당신을 만난 뒤로, 어쩌면 당신이 그 여자와 결혼하지 않은 게 나를 사랑해서가 아니었을지도 모른다는 생각이 한두 번 들었어요. 말해줘요, 데이먼―견뎌볼 테니. 이 일에 나는 정말 아무 상관도 없었던 건가요?\"\n\n\"기어이 말하라고 닦달하는 거요?\"\n\n\"네, 알아야겠어요. 보아하니 내가 내 힘을 너무 쉽게 믿어버렸나 봐요.\"\n\n\"음, 당장의 이유라면 혼인 허가증이 그 장소에 맞지 않았던 거요, 그래서 내가 다른 걸 구하기도 전에 그 여자가 달아나버린 거고. 거기까지는 당신과 아무 상관이 없소. 그 뒤로 그 여자 이모가 내가 도무지 마음에 들지 않는 말투로 내게 말을 해왔지만.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래! 나는 거기서 아무것도 아니에요―나는 거기서 아무것도 아니라고요. 당신은 그저 나를 가지고 노는 거예요. 맙소사, 나 유스테시아 바이가 당신을 이토록 마음에 두다니, 대체 무엇으로 빚어진 인간인지!\"\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리, 그렇게 격해지지 마시오…… 유스테시아, 작년에 더운 날들이 서늘해지고 언덕 그늘이 우묵한 데서 우리를 거의 보이지 않게 해주었을 때, 우리가 이 덤불 사이를 얼마나 쏘다녔던지!\"\n\n그녀는 침울한 침묵에 잠겨 있다가 입을 열었다. \"그래요, 그리고 감히 나를 우러러본다고 내가 당신을 얼마나 비웃었던지! 하지만 그 뒤로 당신은 그 일로 나를 톡톡히 괴롭혔죠.\"\n\n\"그래, 당신은 나를 충분히 모질게 다뤘소, 당신보다 더 고운 사람을 찾았다고 여길 때까지는. 내게는 복된 발견이었지, 유스테시아.\"\n\n\"아직도 더 고운 사람을 찾았다고 여기나요?\"\n\n\"어떤 때는 그렇고, 어떤 때는 아니오. 저울이 얼마나 절묘하게 맞아 있는지 깃털 하나로도 기울 정도지.\"\n\n\"하지만 정말로 내가 당신을 만나든 안 만나든 아무 상관 없는 건가요?\" 그녀가 천천히 물었다.\n\n\"조금은 상관있소, 그렇다고 잠을 설칠 정도는 아니지만.\" 젊은이가 나른하게 대꾸했다. \"아니, 그건 다 지나간 일이오. 하나뿐인 줄 알았던 자리에 꽃이 둘 있더란 걸 깨달았지. 어쩌면 셋, 아니면 넷, 아니 첫 번째 못지않게 좋은 꽃이 몇 송이든 있을지도…… 내 팔자는 묘하단 말이오. 이 모든 일이 내게 닥칠 줄 누가 알았겠소?\"\n\n그녀는 사랑이든 분노든 어느 쪽으로도 똑같이 터져 나올 법한 억눌린 불길로 그의 말을 끊었다. \"지금 나를 사랑하나요?\"\n\n\"누가 알겠소?\"\n\n\"말해요, 기어이 알아낼 테니!\"\n\n\"사랑하기도 하고, 사랑하지 않기도 하오.\" 그가 짓궂게 말했다. \"그러니까, 내게도 때가 있고 철이 있단 말이오. 어떤 순간엔 당신이 너무 크고, 어떤 순간엔 너무 아무것도 안 하고, 어떤 순간엔 너무 우울하고, 어떤 순간엔 너무 어둡고, 또 어떤 순간엔 뭔지 모르겠지만―다만, 예전처럼 당신이 내게 온 세상은 아니라는 거요, 내 사랑. 그래도 당신은 알고 지내기 즐거운 여인이고 만나기 좋은 사람이고, 감히 말하건대 여전히 그 어느 때 못지않게 사랑스럽지―거의.\"\n\n유스테시아는 말이 없었고, 그에게서 몸을 돌렸다가 억눌린 위엄이 어린 목소리로 말했다. \"나는 산책을 하려는데, 이쪽이 내 길이에요.\"\n\n\"음, 당신을 따라가는 것보다 못한 짓이야 얼마든지 할 수 있겠지.\"\n\n\"당신은 별짓을 다 해도 그럴 수밖에 없다는 걸 알잖아요, 그 변덕과 변심을 다 부려봐도 말이에요!\" 그녀가 도전하듯 대꾸했다. \"뭐라고 하든, 아무리 애를 써도, 할 수 있는 한 내게서 멀어져 봐도―당신은 결코 나를 잊지 못해요. 당신은 평생토록 나를 사랑할 거예요. 당신은 나와 결혼하려고 뛰어들 거라고요!\"\n\n\"그렇게 할 거요!\" 와일드브가 말했다. \"유스테시아, 때때로 나는 참 묘한 생각들을 해왔는데, 지금 이 순간에도 그게 떠오르는구려. 당신은 여전히 이 히스를 끔찍이 싫어하지, 그건 나도 아오.\"\n\n\"싫어해요.\" 그녀가 깊은 목소리로 중얼거렸다. \"이건 내 십자가요, 내 수치요, 끝내 내 죽음이 될 거예요!\"\n\n\"나도 이곳이 진저리나오.\" 그가 말했다. \"지금 우리 둘레로 바람이 얼마나 구슬프게 부는지!\"\n\n그녀는 대답하지 않았다. 그 소리는 과연 엄숙하고도 사방에 스며 있었다. 복합된 발성이 그들의 감각에 호소해왔고, 귀로써 인근의 생김새를 살펴볼 수도 있었다. 어두워진 풍경에서 음향의 그림들이 되돌아왔으니, 그들은 히스의 너른 자락이 어디서 시작하고 끝나는지, 가시금작화가 어디서 줄기를 뻗어 키 크게 자라는지, 어디가 최근에 베인 곳인지, 전나무 무리가 어느 방향에 있는지, 호랑가시나무가 자라는 구덩이가 얼마나 가까운지 들을 수 있었으니, 이 서로 다른 생김새들은 그 형상과 빛깔 못지않게 저마다의 목소리를 지녔던 것이다.\n\n\"하느님, 어쩌면 이리도 적막한지!\" 와일드브가 다시 입을 열었다. \"이것 말고는 아무것도 보지 못하는 우리에게 그림 같은 협곡과 안개가 다 무슨 소용이오? 우리가 왜 여기 머물러야 하지? 나와 함께 미국으로 가지 않겠소? 위스콘신에 친척이 있소.\"\n\n\"그건 생각해봐야 할 일이에요.\"\n\n\"여기서는 야생의 새나 풍경화가가 아니고서는 잘 살아낼 도리가 없는 것 같소. 어떻소?\"\n\n\"시간을 줘요.\" 그녀가 그의 손을 잡으며 부드럽게 말했다. \"미국은 너무 멀어요. 나하고 조금만 같이 걸어줄래요?\"\n\n유스테시아는 뒤의 말을 내뱉으며 무덤 기슭에서 물러났고, 와일드브가 그녀를 뒤따랐으므로, 레들맨은 더는 아무것도 들을 수 없었다.\n\n그는 뗏장을 들어올리고 몸을 일으켰다. 그들의 검은 형상이 하늘을 배경으로 가라앉으며 사라졌다. 그들은 마치 굼뜬 히스가 연체동물처럼 제 머리에서 내밀었다가 이제 다시 거두어들인 두 개의 더듬이 같았다.\n\n레들맨이 골짜기를 가로질러, 다시 수레가 놓인 다음 골짜기로 넘어가는 그 걸음걸이는, 스물네 살 호리호리한 젊은이치고는 활기차지 못했다. 그의 정신은 아릴 만큼 흔들려 있었다. 그 걸음에서 그의 입가를 맴돌던 미풍이 저주의 가락을 실어갔다.\n\n그는 난로에 불이 지펴진 짐마차 안으로 들어갔다. 초에 불을 붙이지도 않은 채 그는 곧장 세발 걸상에 앉아, 아직도 사랑하는 그 사람에 관하여 보고 들은 것을 곰곰이 헤아렸다. 그는 한숨도 흐느낌도 아니지만, 그 어느 쪽보다도 심란한 마음을 더욱 잘 드러내는 소리를 냈다.\n\n\"내 탐시.\" 그가 무겁게 속삭였다. \"어찌하면 좋단 말인가? 그래, 그 유스테시아 바이를 만나봐야겠다.\"\n\n이튿날 아침, 해의 높이가 히스 어느 곳에서 보더라도 레인배로의 높이에 비하면 참으로 보잘것없어 보이는 시각, 낮은 지대의 자그마한 언덕들이 죄다 안개로 빚어진 에게해의 군도처럼 보이는 시각에, 레들맨은 제 거처로 삼았던 가시덤불 우거진 구석에서 나와 미스토버 냅의 비탈을 올랐다.\n\n이 텁수룩한 언덕들이 겉보기에는 그토록 호젓했으나, 이런 겨울 아침이면 날카롭고 동그란 눈 여럿이 언제나 지나가는 이에게로 모여들 채비를 갖추고 있었다. 다른 데서 발견되었다면 경이를 자아냈을 깃털 달린 종들이 여기 숨어 머물렀다. 능에 한 마리가 이곳을 즐겨 찾았고, 이보다 그리 멀지 않은 옛적에는 에그던에서 스물다섯 마리를 한꺼번에 볼 수도 있었으리라. 개구리매들이 와일드브의 집 옆 골짜기에서 올려다보았다. 크림빛 달리기새 한 마리가 이 언덕을 찾곤 했는데, 영국에서 통틀어 열두 마리도 채 본 적이 없을 만큼 희귀한 새였으나, 어느 야만인이 그 아프리카에서 온 길손을 쏘아 죽일 때까지 밤낮으로 쉬지 않았고, 그 일이 있은 뒤로 크림빛 달리기새들은 다시는 에그던에 발을 들이지 않는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼다.\n\n벤이 지금 이 길손들을 관찰하듯 그것들을 거닐며 살펴볼 나그네라면, 자신이 인간에게 알려지지 않은 영역과 직접 통하고 있다고 느낄 수 있었으리라. 여기 그의 앞에 야생 청둥오리 한 마리가―북풍의 고향에서 막 당도한 채로―있었다. 그 짐승은 북방의 앎을 한아름 그에게 품고 들여왔다. 빙하의 재앙, 눈보라의 사건, 반짝이는 오로라의 효과, 천정의 북극성, 발밑의 프랭클린―그 평범한 지식의 목록은 놀라웠다. 그러나 그 새는, 다른 숱한 철학자들처럼, 레들맨을 바라보며 안락한 현실의 지금 이 순간이 십 년치의 추억만큼 값지다고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n벤은 이것들 사이를 지나, 이것들 가운데 외따로 살며 이것들을 업신여기는 그 고립된 미인의 집을 향해 나아갔다. 그날은 일요일이었으나, 에그던에서는 혼례나 장례를 치르려는 게 아니고서는 교회에 가는 일이 드물었으니, 이 점은 별 차이가 없었다. 그는 바이 양에게 면담을 청한다는 대담한 한 수를 두기로 마음먹었던 터―기교로든 강습으로든 토머신의 연적이라는 그녀의 입지를 공략하려는 것이었으니, 거기서 그는, 다소 지나치게 두드러지게, 어릿광대에서 임금에 이르기까지 어떤 빈틈없는 부류의 사내들에게 특유한 그 신사다움의 결여를 드러내고 있었다. 위대한 프리드리히가 아름다운 여대공에게 전쟁을 벌인 것이나, 나폴레옹이 아름다운 프로이센 왕비에게 강화 조건을 거절한 것이나, 그 어느 것도 레들맨이 제 나름의 방식으로 유스테시아를 밀어내려 꾀하면서 성별의 차이에 무감한 것보다 더 무감하지는 않았다.\n\n선장의 오두막에 들르는 일은 미천한 주민들에게는 언제나 어지간한 큰일이었다. 더러 수다스럽기도 했으나 그의 기분은 종잡을 수가 없었고, 어느 한순간 그가 어떻게 굴지는 아무도 장담할 수 없었다. 유스테시아는 말수가 적었고 거의 혼자 틀어박혀 지냈다. 그들의 하인 노릇을 하는 어느 소작농의 딸과 정원과 마구간에서 일하는 사내아이 말고는, 그들 자신을 빼면 이 집에 발을 들이는 이가 거의 없었다. 그들은 이 고장에서 요브라이트 일가를 빼면 유일하게 점잖은 사람들이었고, 부유함과는 거리가 멀었으나, 더 가난한 이웃들을 움직이는 그것―사람과 새와 짐승 하나하나에게 우호적인 낯을 지켜야 한다는 그 절박함을 느끼지 않았다.\n\n레들맨이 정원에 들어섰을 때 노인은 망원경으로 멀리 풍경 속에 묻은 푸른 바다의 얼룩을 들여다보고 있었고, 단추에 달린 자그마한 닻들이 햇빛에 반짝였다. 그는 벤을 큰길에서 만난 길동무로 알아보았으나, 그 일에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않고 그저 이렇게 말했을 뿐이다. \"아, 레들맨―자네 여기 웬일인가? 그로그 한 잔 들겠나?\"\n\n벤은 너무 이르다는 핑계로 사양하고, 자기 볼일은 바이 양에게 있다고 밝혔다. 선장은 잠시 그를 모자에서 조끼까지, 조끼에서 각반까지 훑어보더니, 마침내 안으로 들어가라고 청했다.\n\n바이 양은 마침 그때 누구도 만날 수가 없었고, 레들맨은 부엌 창가 의자에서 두 손을 벌어진 무릎에 걸친 채, 그 손에 모자를 걸친 채 기다렸다.\n\n\"아가씨가 아직 안 일어나신 모양이지요?\" 그가 이윽고 하인에게 물었다.\n\n\"아직은요. 이런 시각에 숙녀를 찾아오는 사람은 없는 법이에요.\"\n\n\"그럼 밖에 나가 있겠소.\" 벤이 말했다. \"나를 만나줄 마음이 있거든 부디 전갈을 보내주시오, 그러면 들어오리다.\"\n\n레들맨은 집을 나와 붙어 있는 언덕에서 어슬렁거렸다. 적잖은 시간이 흘렀으나 그를 들라는 청은 전해지지 않았다. 그가 자기 계략이 실패했나 보다 생각하기 시작할 무렵, 유스테시아 본인의 모습이 한가로이 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보았다. 그 별난 인물에게 면담을 허락한다는 데서 느낀 새삼스러운 흥미만으로도 그녀를 끌어내기에 충분했던 것이다.\n\n디고리 벤을 슬쩍 한 번 보고 나서, 그녀는 이 사내가 묘한 용건으로 왔으며, 자신이 여겼던 만큼 천한 인물은 아니라는 것을 느끼는 듯했다. 그녀가 바싹 다가서도 그가 불편하게 몸을 비틀거나 발을 옮기거나, 여자들 가운데 흔치 않은 부류를 마주했을 때 순박한 시골뜨기에게서 새어나오는 그런 자잘한 기색을 조금도 내보이지 않았기 때문이다. 자기와 대화를 나눌 수 있겠느냐는 그의 물음에 그녀는 \"그래요, 옆에서 걸어요\"라고 답하고는 계속 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n그들이 멀리 가기도 전에, 통찰력 있는 레들맨은 자신이 좀 덜 무덤덤하게 비쳤더라면 더 현명하게 처신했으리라는 데 생각이 미쳤고, 틈이 나는 대로 그 잘못을 바로잡기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"아가씨, 제가 외람되이 이렇게 건너와서, 그 사내에 관하여 제 귀에 들어온 묘한 소식을 알려드리려는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"아! 무슨 사내요?\"\n\n그는 팔꿈치를 남동쪽으로―콰이엇 우먼 쪽으로 홱 내질렀다. 유스테시아가 재빨리 그를 향해 돌아섰다. \"와일드브 씨 말이에요?\"\n\n\"네, 그분 때문에 어느 집안에 풍파가 일었는데, 아가씨께 그걸 알려드리려 온 것입니다, 아가씨께서는 그걸 몰아낼 힘을 지니셨을지도 모른다고 믿기 때문이지요.\"\n\n\"내가요? 무슨 풍파인데요?\"\n\n\"이건 아주 비밀스러운 일입니다. 그분이 끝내 토머신 요브라이트와 결혼하기를 마다할지도 모른다는 것입니다.\"\n\n유스테시아는 그 말에 속이 두근거렸으나, 이런 극에서 제가 맡은 역할쯤은 감당할 줄 알았다. 그녀는 싸늘하게 대꾸했다. \"나는 이런 이야기를 듣고 싶지 않고, 당신은 내가 끼어들기를 바라서는 안 돼요.\"\n\n\"하지만 아가씨, 한마디만 들어주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"못 들어요. 나는 그 결혼에 관심이 없고, 설령 관심이 있다 한들 와일드브 씨더러 내 뜻대로 하라고 강요할 수도 없어요.\"\n\n\"이 히스에서 유일한 숙녀이시니 하실 수 있으리라 봅니다.\" 벤이 교묘하게 에둘러 말했다. \"사정은 이렇습니다. 와일드브 씨는, 이 일에 다른 여자만 끼어 있지 않다면 당장이라도 토머신과 결혼해 만사를 매끈하게 정리하실 분입니다. 그 다른 여자란 그분이 어쩌다 사귀게 되어 가끔 이 히스에서 만나는 누군가라고 합니다. 그분은 결코 그 여자와 결혼하지 않을 텐데, 그러면서도 그 여자 때문에 자신을 끔찍이 사랑하는 여자와는 영영 결혼하지 못하게 될지도 모릅니다. 자, 우리 사내들을 그토록 쥐락펴락하시는 아가씨께서, 그분이 이웃 아가씨 탐신을 명예롭고 다정하게 대하고 그 다른 여자를 포기하라고 다그치신다면, 어쩌면 그분이 그리할 것이고, 그러면 탐신을 숱한 비참함에서 구하시는 게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n\"아이고, 내 팔자야!\" 유스테시아가 웃음을 터뜨렸는데, 그 웃음에 입술이 벌어지면서 튤립 속으로 들듯 햇빛이 입안으로 비쳐들어 거기에 똑같은 진홍빛 불꽃을 입혔다. \"레들맨, 정말이지 당신은 내가 사내들에게 미치는 영향을 너무 대단하게 여기는군요. 만약 내게 당신이 상상하는 그런 힘이 있다면 나는 곧장 가서 내게 친절했던 누구든 그 사람을 위해 그 힘을 쓸 텐데―내가 아는 한 토머신 요브라이트는 딱히 그러지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"정말로 모르신단 말입니까―그 아가씨가 늘 아가씨를 얼마나 높이 여겼는지를요?\"\n\n\"나는 그런 말 한마디도 들어본 적 없어요. 우리가 겨우 이 마일 떨어져 살아도, 나는 평생 그 이모 집 안에 들어가 본 적이 없어요.\"\n\n그녀의 태도에 도사린 거만함이 벤에게, 여기까지는 자신이 완전히 실패했음을 일러주었다. 그는 속으로 한숨을 내쉬며 두 번째 논거의 가면을 벗길 필요를 느꼈다.\n\n\"음, 그건 차치하더라도, 장담컨대 다른 여자에게 큰 선행을 베푸는 일은 아가씨의 힘 안에 있습니다, 바이 양.\"\n\n그녀는 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"아가씨의 고우심은 와일드브 씨에게 곧 법입니다. 아가씨를 보는 사내라면 누구에게나 법이지요. 다들 이럽니다. '저기 오는 저 잘난 숙녀가―이름이 뭐지? 어쩜 저리 어여쁠까!' 토머신 요브라이트보다 더 어여쁘다고요.\" 레들맨은 속으로 '거짓말하는 이 악당을 하느님은 용서하소서!' 하고 되뇌면서 끈질기게 우겼다. 실제로 그녀가 더 어여뻤으나, 레들맨은 결코 그렇게 여기지 않았다. 유스테시아의 아름다움에는 어떤 가려진 데가 있었고, 벤의 눈은 단련되어 있지 않았다. 지금처럼 겨울옷을 입으면 그녀는 길앞잡이 같았으니, 칙칙한 데서 보면 더없이 수수한 중간색을 띤 듯하지만 환한 빛 아래서는 눈부신 광채로 타오르는 그 벌레 말이다.\n\n유스테시아는 그렇게 함으로써 제 위엄을 위태롭게 한다는 걸 의식하면서도 대꾸하지 않을 수 없었다. \"토머신보다 더 사랑스러운 여자는 많아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그러니 거기에 대단한 의미를 둘 건 없어요.\"\n\n레들맨은 그 상처를 감내하고 말을 이었다. \"그분은 여자들의 용모를 눈여겨보는 사내인지라, 아가씨께서 마음만 먹으신다면 메꽃 줄기 비틀듯 그분을 아가씨 뜻대로 휘감으실 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그토록 오래 그와 함께 지낸 여자도 못 하는 일을, 그에게서 멀리 떨어진 이 위에 사는 내가 할 수 있을 리 없잖아요.\"\n\n레들맨은 홱 돌아서서 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다. \"바이 양!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"어째서 그런 말을 하나요―나를 의심하기라도 하는 듯이?\" 그녀는 힘없이 말했고, 숨결이 가빴다. \"당신이 내게 그런 말투로 말하다니!\" 그녀는 거만함을 꾸며낸 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다. \"대체 무슨 속셈으로 그런 말을 하게 됐나요?\"\n\n\"바이 양, 어찌하여 이 사내를 모르는 척하십니까?―물론 저는 그 까닭을 압니다. 그가 아가씨께 미치지 못하니, 아가씨께서 부끄러우신 게지요.\"\n\n\"잘못 짚었어요. 무슨 뜻이에요?\"\n\n레들맨은 진실의 패를 던지기로 작정했다. \"저는 어젯밤 레인배로 옆 그 만남 자리에 있었고, 한마디도 빠짐없이 들었습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"와일드브와 토머신 사이를 가로막은 그 여자는 바로 아가씨입니다.\"\n\n그것은 사람을 당황케 하는 휘장의 들춤이었고, 칸다울레스 왕비의 굴욕이 그녀의 내면에서 달아올랐다. 본인의 의지와 무관하게 입술이 떨려오고, 더는 헐떡임을 억누를 수 없게 되는 순간이 닥쳐온 것이다.\n\n\"몸이 안 좋아요.\" 그녀가 허둥지둥 말했다. \"아니―그게 아니라―당신 말을 더 들을 기분이 아니에요. 제발 나를 두고 가요.\"\n\n\"아가씨를 괴롭히더라도 말씀드려야겠습니다, 바이 양. 제가 아가씨 앞에 펼쳐놓으려는 것은 이것입니다. 어찌하여 그리되었든―잘못이 그 아가씨에게 있든 아가씨께 있든―그 아가씨의 처지가 아가씨 처지보다 더 나쁜 것은 의심할 여지가 없습니다. 와일드브 씨를 포기하시는 것은 아가씨께 진짜로 이득일 것이니, 어차피 아가씨께서 어찌 그와 결혼하실 수 있겠습니까? 한데 그 아가씨는 그리 쉽게 벗어날 수가 없습니다―그를 놓치면 모두가 그 아가씨를 탓할 테니까요. 그러니 저는 청합니다―그 아가씨의 권리가 더 옳아서가 아니라 그 아가씨의 처지가 가장 나쁘기에―그를 그 아가씨에게 넘겨주십사고요.\"\n\n\"안 돼―안 할 거예요, 안 할 거예요!\" 그녀는 충동적으로, 레들맨을 아랫것으로 대하던 앞서의 태도는 까맣게 잊은 채 외쳤다. \"이런 취급을 당한 사람은 여태 아무도 없었어! 잘 풀려가고 있었는데―나는 짓밟히지 않을 거예요―그 여자 같은 못한 여자한테는요. 당신이 와서 그 여자를 두둔하는 건 좋아요, 하지만 그 여자의 고생은 죄다 그 여자 스스로 빚은 게 아닌가요? 나는 한 무리의 소작농들에게 허락을 구하지 않고서는 내가 고른 누구에게도 호의를 베풀 수 없단 말인가요? 그 여자가 나와 내 마음 사이에 끼어든 거고, 이제 제가 마땅한 벌을 받게 되니까 당신을 시켜 자기를 두둔하게 하는 거잖아요!\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 벤이 진지하게 말했다. \"그 아가씨는 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모릅니다. 그를 포기하시라 청하는 건 오직 저뿐입니다. 그게 그 아가씨에게도 아가씨에게도 더 나을 겁니다. 다른 여자를 험하게 다룬 사내를 어느 숙녀가 몰래 만난다는 게 사람들에게 알려지면, 입방아에 오를 겁니다.\"\n\n\"나는 그 여자를 해치지 않았어요―그는 그 여자 것이기 전에 내 것이었다고요! 그가 돌아온 건―나를―나를 가장 좋아했기 때문이에요!\" 그녀가 거칠게 말했다. \"하지만 당신과 이런 이야기를 하다 보니 자존심을 다 잃고 마는군요. 내가 무엇에 휘말리고 있는 거람!\"\n\n\"저는 비밀을 지킬 수 있습니다.\" 벤이 부드럽게 말했다. \"두려워하실 것 없습니다. 아가씨가 그와 만나는 걸 아는 사내는 저 하나뿐입니다. 한 가지만 더 말씀드리고 물러가겠습니다. 아가씨께서 그에게, 여기 사는 게 싫다고―에그던 히스가 아가씨께는 감옥이라고 하시는 걸 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말했죠. 이 풍경에 일종의 아름다움이 있다는 건 알아요. 하지만 내게는 감옥이에요. 당신이 말한 그 사내가 여기 산다고 해서 내가 그 느낌에서 벗어나는 건 아니에요. 가까이에 더 나은 사람이 있었더라면 나는 그에게 아무런 마음도 두지 않았을 거예요.\"\n\n레들맨은 희망에 차 보였으니, 그녀의 이 말 뒤로는 세 번째 시도가 가망 있어 보였다. \"이제 우리가 속을 좀 터놓았으니, 아가씨,\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 제안하려는 바를 말씀드리겠습니다. 제가 레들 장사에 나선 뒤로, 아시다시피 저는 두루 많이 돌아다닙니다.\"\n\n그녀는 고개를 살짝 숙이고는 몸을 휙 돌려, 그 시선이 발아래 안개 낀 골짜기에 머물게 했다.\n\n\"제 돌아다니는 길에 버드머스 가까이를 지납니다. 한데 버드머스는 멋진 곳입니다―멋지지요―크나큰 소금기 어린 번쩍이는 바다가 활처럼 뭍으로 휘어들고―수천의 점잖은 분들이 오르락내리락 거닐고―악단이 음악을 연주하고―바다의 장교와 뭍의 장교가 그 틈에서 함께 거닐고―만나는 사람 열에 아홉은 사랑에 빠져 있지요.\"\n\n\"알아요.\" 그녀가 깔보듯 말했다. \"버드머스라면 내가 당신보다 더 잘 알아요. 나는 거기서 태어났어요. 내 아버지는 외국에서 그곳으로 와 군악대 악사가 됐죠. 아아, 내 영혼이여, 버드머스! 지금 거기 있다면 좋으련만.\"\n\n레들맨은 굼뜬 불길이 때에 따라 어떻게 활활 타오를 수 있는지를 보고 놀랐다. \"거기 계신다면, 아가씨,\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"일주일이면 아가씨는 저기 보이는 저 히스 망아지들만큼도 와일드브를 생각하지 않으실 겁니다. 자, 제가 아가씨를 거기로 보내드릴 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게요?\" 유스테시아가 그 무거운 눈에 강렬한 호기심을 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"제 숙부가 스물다섯 해 동안 어느 부유한 미망인 마님의 믿음직한 일꾼으로 지내왔는데, 그 마님이 바다를 마주한 아름다운 저택을 가지고 계십니다. 이 마님은 늙고 다리를 절게 되어, 책을 읽어주고 노래를 불러줄 젊은 말동무를 구하시는데, 신문에 광고까지 내고 대여섯이나 써봤어도 도통 마음에 드는 사람을 못 구해 애를 태우십니다. 그분은 아가씨를 모실 수만 있다면 펄쩍 뛰며 좋아하실 테고, 숙부가 모든 걸 수월하게 해줄 겁니다.\"\n\n\"내가 일을 해야 하나요, 아마도?\"\n\n\"아니요, 진짜 일은 아닙니다―읽어드리는 일이라든가 그런 자잘한 게 좀 있을 뿐입니다. 새해 첫날까지는 부르지도 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"일이라는 뜻인 줄 알았어요.\" 그녀가 다시 나른함 속으로 시들며 말했다.\n\n\"솔직히 그분을 즐겁게 해드리는 일이 약간은 있을 겁니다. 하지만 한가한 사람들이야 그걸 일이라 부르겠지만, 일하는 사람들은 그걸 놀이라 부를 겁니다. 아가씨가 누리실 교제와 삶을 생각해 보십시오, 아가씨가 보실 화려함과, 아가씨가 맞으실 신사 말입니다. 제 숙부가 시골 출신의 믿음직한 젊은 숙녀를 수소문하기로 되어 있습니다, 그분이 도회지 아가씨들을 안 좋아하시니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 그 사람을 즐겁게 해주느라 나를 닳아 없애는 거예요! 나는 안 갈 거예요. 아아, 내가 숙녀답게 화려한 도회지에서 살면서, 내 갈 길을 가고, 내 하고 싶은 대로 할 수만 있다면, 내 인생의 주름진 절반이라도 내놓겠어요! 그래요, 레들맨, 그렇게 하겠어요.\"\n\n\"제가 토머신을 행복하게 해주도록 도와주십시오, 아가씨, 그러면 그 기회는 아가씨 것이 됩니다.\" 길동무가 다그쳤다.\n\n\"기회라니―그건 기회가 아니에요.\" 그녀가 도도하게 말했다. \"당신 같은 가난뱅이가 내게 무얼 줄 수 있다는 거예요, 정말?―나는 들어가겠어요. 더는 할 말이 없어요. 당신네 말들은 여물을 안 줘도 되나요, 레들 자루는 안 기워도 되나요, 아니면 물건 사 줄 사람을 안 찾아도 되기에 이렇게 여기서 빈둥거리고 있는 건가요?\"\n\n벤은 더는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그는 그녀가 자기 얼굴의 가망 없는 실망을 보지 못하도록, 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌리고 발길을 돌렸다. 이 외로운 처녀에게서 발견한 정신의 명료함과 힘이, 그녀와 바싹 마주한 처음 몇 분부터 이미 그의 태도를 불안으로 가득 채웠던 것이다. 그녀의 젊음과 처지를 보고 그는 제 수법에 고스란히 휘둘릴 만큼의 단순함을 기대했었다. 그러나 더 마음 약한 시골 처녀들이라면 함께 휩쓸려갔을 법한 그 유혹의 방략이, 유스테시아에게는 그저 거부감만 일으켰다. 보통 에그던에서 버드머스라는 말은 매혹을 뜻했다. 그 왕실의 항구이자 휴양지가 히스 사람들의 마음에 참되게 비쳤다면, 그것은 카르타고적인 건설의 분주함과 타렌툼적인 호사로움과 바이아이적인 건강과 아름다움을 매혹적이고도 형언키 어려운 방식으로 한데 어우른 것이었으리라. 유스테시아도 그곳을 두고 그에 못지않게 분에 넘치게 마음을 품었으나, 거기 가려고 제 독립을 굽히려 하지는 않았다.\n\n디고리 벤이 완전히 떠나가자, 유스테시아는 둑으로 걸어가 해 쪽으로, 또한 와일드브의 집 방향이기도 한 그쪽으로, 거칠고 그림 같은 골짜기를 내려다보았다. 안개가 이제 어지간히 가라앉아, 그의 집 둘레 나무와 덤불의 우듬지가 가까스로 분간될 정도였으니, 마치 그것들을 햇빛으로부터 가린 거대한 흰 거미줄을 뚫고 위로 솟아오르는 듯했다. 그녀의 마음이 그쪽으로 기울어 있음은 의심할 여지가 없었으니, 막연하게, 공상에 잠겨―제 시야 안에서 꿈이 응결될 수 있는 유일한 대상인 그를 휘감았다 풀었다 했다. 한낱 그녀의 심심풀이로 시작했던 사내, 적절한 순간마다 그녀를 버려두는 솜씨가 아니었던들 결코 그녀의 노리개 이상은 되지 못했을 사내가, 이제 다시금 그녀의 갈망이 되었다. 그의 구애가 그친 것이 그녀의 사랑을 되살린 것이다. 유스테시아가 와일드브에게 무심히 내주었던 정도의 감정이 토머신에 의해 둑이 막혀 큰물이 되었다. 예전에 그녀는 와일드브를 곧잘 놀려댔으나, 그것은 다른 여자가 그를 어여삐 여기기 전의 일이었다. 무관심한 상황에 아이러니 한 방울이 떨어지면 흔히 그 전부가 짜릿해지는 법이다.\n\n\"나는 결코 그를 포기하지 않아―결코!\" 그녀가 충동적으로 말했다.\n\n소문이 그녀를 불리하게 비출지도 모른다는 레들맨의 암시는 유스테시아에게 지속적인 두려움이 되지 못했다. 그녀는 그런 가능성을 여신이 아마포가 모자란 일을 대하듯 아랑곳하지 않았다. 이는 타고난 파렴치함에서 나온 게 아니라, 세상에서 너무 멀리 떨어져 살아 여론의 충격을 느끼지 못하는 데서 비롯된 것이었다. 사막의 제노비아라면 로마에서 자기를 두고 무슨 말을 하든 거의 개의치 않았으리라. 사회 윤리에 관한 한 유스테시아는 야만의 상태에 가까웠으나, 정서에 있어서는 그러는 내내 향락가였다. 그녀는 관능의 은밀한 깊은 곳까지 나아가 있었으나, 인습의 문턱은 거의 넘어선 적이 없었다.\n\n레들맨은 토머신의 앞날의 행복에 대해 낙담스러운 전망을 품은 채 유스테시아 앞에서 물러났으나, 제 짐마차로 가는 길을 따라가다가 요브라이트 부인의 모습이 콰이엇 우먼을 향해 천천히 걸어가는 것을 보고는, 아직 시도해보지 않은 길이 하나 더 남았다는 사실에 눈을 떴다. 그는 그녀에게로 건너갔고, 그녀의 근심 어린 얼굴에서 와일드브를 향한 이 행차가 자신이 유스테시아를 찾아간 것과 똑같은 목적으로 이루어졌음을 거의 알아챌 수 있었다.\n\n그녀는 그 사실을 숨기지 않았다. \"그렇다면,\" 레들맨이 말했다. \"그냥 내버려두시는 편이 낫겠습니다, 요브라이트 부인.\"\n\n\"나도 반쯤은 그렇게 생각해요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 그에게 이 문제를 다그치는 것 말고는 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없네요.\"\n\n\"먼저 한마디 드리고 싶습니다.\" 벤이 단호하게 말했다. \"토머신에게 결혼하자고 청한 사내가 와일드브 씨 하나만은 아닙니다. 그러니 다른 사람이라고 기회를 못 가질 까닭이 있겠습니까? 요브라이트 부인, 저는 부인의 조카와 기꺼이 결혼하고 싶고, 지난 이 년 동안 언제든 그리했을 것입니다. 자, 이제 다 털어놓았는데, 이건 그 아가씨 본인 말고는 아무에게도 한 적 없는 말입니다.\"\n\n요브라이트 부인은 감정을 잘 드러내는 편이 아니었으나, 그 눈길이 저도 모르게 그의 별나면서도 잘 빠진 풍채로 갔다.\n\n\"겉모습이 다는 아니지요.\" 그 눈길을 알아챈 레들맨이 말했다. \"돈으로 따지면 제 일만큼도 못 버는 직업이 수두룩합니다. 어쩌면 저는 와일드브보다 그리 못한 처지도 아닐지 모릅니다. 실패한 그런 직업인들만큼 가난한 사람도 없으니까요. 그리고 제 붉은 물이 마음에 안 드신다면―뭐, 저는 날 때부터 붉었던 게 아닙니다, 아시다시피 그저 변덕으로 이 장사에 들어선 것뿐이니, 때가 되면 다른 일에 손을 댈 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n\"내 조카를 마음에 두어주니 정말 고마워요. 하지만 걸림돌이 있을까 두렵네요. 게다가 그 아이는 이 사내에게 마음을 다 바쳤어요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 아니라면 제가 오늘 아침에 한 일을 하지도 않았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지 않다면 이 일에 아무런 괴로움도 없을 테고, 지금 내가 그의 집으로 가는 것도 보지 못했겠지요. 당신이 마음을 털어놓았을 때 토머신은 뭐라고 답하던가요?\"\n\n\"부인께서 저를 반대하시리라고, 그리고 다른 이런저런 사정도 적어 보냈습니다.\"\n\n\"그 아이 말이 어느 정도는 옳았어요. 이걸 야박하게 받아들이지는 말아요―나는 그저 사실을 말하는 것뿐이니. 당신은 그 아이에게 잘해주었고, 우리도 그걸 잊지 않아요. 하지만 그 아이가 제 마음으로 당신의 아내가 되기를 꺼린 이상, 내 바람이 끼어들 것도 없이 그것으로 결판난 거예요.\"\n\n\"네. 하지만 그때와 지금은 다릅니다, 마님. 그 아가씨는 지금 곤경에 빠졌고, 만약 부인께서 그 아가씨에게 저에 대해 말씀해주시고, 부인 스스로도 저를 좋게 여겨주신다면, 그 아가씨의 마음을 돌려, 이 와일드브가 가질지 말지도 모른 채 갔다 왔다 하는 그 수작에서 완전히 벗어나게 할 가망이 있지 않을까 생각했습니다.\"\n\n요브라이트 부인은 고개를 가로저었다. \"토머신은, 그리고 나도 그 아이와 같은 생각인데, 그 아이가 이름에 오점 없이 세상 앞에 나서려면 마땅히 와일드브의 아내가 되어야 한다고 봐요. 그들이 곧 결혼하면, 정말로 사고가 있어서 혼례를 못 치른 거라고 다들 믿을 거예요. 그렇지 않으면 그 아이 인품에 그늘을 드리울지도 모르고―어쨌든 웃음거리로 만들겠지요. 한마디로, 어떻게든 가능하다면 그들은 지금 결혼해야 해요.\"\n\n\"저도 반 시간 전까지는 그렇게 생각했습니다. 하지만 따지고 보면, 그 아가씨가 몇 시간 그와 함께 앵글베리로 다녀온 게 무슨 해가 된다는 겁니까? 그 아가씨가 얼마나 순결한지 아는 사람이라면 누구든 그런 생각을 영 부당하게 여길 겁니다. 저는 오늘 아침 이 와일드브와의 혼사를 거들려고 애썼습니다―네, 저 말입니다, 마님―그 아가씨가 그에게 그토록 푹 빠져 있으니 마땅히 그래야 한다고 믿어서요. 하지만 따지고 보면 제가 옳았는지 무척 의심스럽습니다. 어쨌든 아무 성과도 없었지요. 그래서 이제 제 자신을 내놓는 것입니다.\"\n\n요브라이트 부인은 이 문제를 더 파고들 마음이 내키지 않는 듯했다. \"아무래도 가봐야겠어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"달리 어찌할 도리가 있을 것 같지 않으니.\"\n\n그러고는 그녀는 계속 갔다. 그러나 이 대화가 토머신의 이모를 와일드브와의 작정한 면담에서 돌려세우지는 못했어도, 그 면담을 이끌어가는 방식에는 상당한 차이를 가져왔다. 그녀는 레들맨이 제 손에 쥐여준 무기를 두고 하느님께 감사했다.\n\n그녀가 여관에 다다랐을 때 와일드브는 집에 있었다. 그는 말없이 그녀를 응접실로 안내하고 문을 닫았다. 요브라이트 부인이 입을 열었다―\n\n\"오늘 들르는 게 내 도리라고 생각했어요. 내게 새로운 제안이 하나 들어왔는데, 적잖이 놀랐어요. 그게 토머신에게 크게 영향을 줄 테고, 그래서 적어도 자네에게는 알려야겠다고 마음먹었어요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까? 그게 무엇입니까?\" 그가 정중하게 말했다.\n\n\"물론 그 아이의 앞날에 관한 거예요. 자네는 모를지도 모르지만, 다른 사내가 토머신과 결혼하고 싶어 안달하는 기색을 보였어요. 자, 내가 아직 그를 부추기지는 않았지만, 양심상 더는 그에게 기회를 거절할 수가 없어요. 자네에게 매정하게 굴고 싶지는 않지만, 그와 그 아이에게 공정해야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"그 사내가 누구입니까?\" 와일드브가 놀라서 물었다.\n\n\"그 아이가 자네에게 마음을 둔 것보다 더 오래 그 아이에게 마음을 둔 사람이에요. 그는 이 년 전에 그 아이에게 청혼했어요. 그때는 그 아이가 거절했지요.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\"\n\n\"그가 요즘 그 아이를 보았고, 내게 그 아이에게 구애할 허락을 청했어요. 그 아이가 두 번씩 거절하지는 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"이름이 뭡니까?\"\n\n요브라이트 부인은 말하기를 거절했다. \"토머신이 좋아하는 사내예요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"적어도 그 한결같음을 그 아이가 존중하는 사람이지요. 내가 보기엔 그때 거절한 것을 지금은 기꺼이 받아들이려 할 거예요. 그 아이는 제 어색한 처지를 무척 괴로워하고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그 아가씨는 그런 옛 연인 이야기를 단 한 번도 내게 한 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"아무리 온순한 여자라도 패를 다 까 보일 만큼 바보는 아니지요.\"\n\n\"음, 그 아가씨가 그를 원한다면 그를 가져야겠지요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말하기야 쉽지요. 하지만 자네는 그 어려움을 보지 못해요. 그가 그 아이를 원하는 것이 그 아이가 그를 원하는 것보다 훨씬 더하니, 내가 그런 일을 부추기기 전에 자네에게서 분명한 다짐을 받아두어야겠어요, 내가 최선이라 믿고 추진하는 그 일을 자네가 끼어들어 그르치지 않겠다는 다짐을요. 그들이 약혼하고 만사가 매끄럽게 정해져 혼례를 앞두었을 때, 자네가 그들 사이에 끼어들어 다시 구애를 한다고 쳐봐요. 그 아이를 도로 차지하지는 못할지언정 숱한 불행을 일으킬 수는 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"물론 그런 짓은 하지 않을 것입니다.\" 와일드브가 말했다. \"하지만 그들은 아직 약혼한 게 아닙니다. 토머신이 그를 받아들일지 부인께서 어찌 아십니까?\"\n\n\"그건 나도 신중히 자문해본 문제예요. 대체로 따져보면 그 아이가 머잖아 그를 받아들일 공산이 높아요. 내가 그 아이에게 어느 정도 영향력이 있다고 자부하고 있고요. 그 아이는 마음이 무르고, 나는 그를 강하게 천거할 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"그러면서 동시에 저를 깎아내리시겠지요.\"\n\n\"음, 내가 자네를 칭찬하지 않으리란 건 믿어도 좋아요.\" 그녀가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"그리고 이게 술책처럼 보인다면, 그 아이의 처지가 유별나고 그 아이가 험한 일을 당했다는 걸 기억해야 해요. 게다가 그 아이 스스로 지금의 굴욕에서 벗어나고 싶어 하는 마음이 이 혼사를 돕는 데 보탬이 될 테고, 이런 경우 여자의 자존심은 그 아이를 아주 멀리까지 이끌어가지요. 마음을 돌리게 하려면 약간의 손씀이 필요할지도 모르나, 나는 그 정도는 해낼 수 있어요, 자네가 그 한 가지 없어서는 안 될 일에 동의한다면 말이에요. 그건, 그 아이가 자네를 두고 더는 남편감으로 여기지 말아야 한다고 분명히 선언하는 거예요. 그래야 그 아이가 발끈해서 그를 받아들일 테니까요.\"\n\n\"지금 당장 그렇게 말씀드리기는 어렵습니다, 요브라이트 부인. 너무 갑작스러워서요.\"\n\n\"그래서 내 계획이 통째로 어그러지는군요! 자네가 우리와는 아무런 상관이 없겠다고 분명히 말해주는 그 작은 일조차 거절해서 우리 가족을 돕지 않다니 참으로 곤란하네요.\"\n\n와일드브는 거북하게 곰곰 생각했다. \"솔직히 저는 이런 일에 대비가 안 되어 있었습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"물론 부인께서 원하신다면, 필요하다면 그 아가씨를 포기하겠습니다. 하지만 저는 제가 그 아가씨의 남편이 될 수도 있다고 생각했습니다.\"\n\n\"그 말은 전에도 들었어요.\"\n\n\"자, 요브라이트 부인, 우리 다투지는 맙시다. 제게 적당한 시간을 주십시오. 저는 그 아가씨에게 더 나은 어떤 기회가 있다면 그것을 가로막고 싶지 않습니다. 다만 좀 더 일찍 알려주셨더라면 좋았겠다 싶을 뿐입니다. 하루 이틀 안에 편지를 드리거나 찾아뵙겠습니다. 그러면 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"내가 모르게 토머신과 연락하지 않겠다고 약속한다면요.\"\n\n\"약속하겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다. 그리고 면담은 그것으로 끝났고, 요브라이트 부인은 왔던 대로 집을 향해 돌아갔다.\n\n흔히 그렇듯, 그날 그녀의 단순한 책략이 가져온 가장 큰 효과는, 그것을 꾸밀 때 그녀의 시야에서 한참 벗어나 있던 데서 나타났다. 우선, 그녀의 방문은 그날 저녁 어둠이 내린 뒤 와일드브를 미스토버의 유스테시아 집으로 보냈다.\n\n이 시각, 그 외딴집은 바깥의 한기와 어둠으로부터 빈틈없이 가림막을 치고 덧창을 닫아걸고 있었다. 와일드브가 그녀와 짠 은밀한 방법은, 자갈을 조금 손에 쥐고 바깥에 달린 덧창 윗부분의 틈에 갖다 대어, 덧창과 유리 사이로 생쥐가 내는 듯한 가벼운 바스락 소리를 내며 떨어지게 하는 것이었다. 이렇게 그녀의 주의를 끄는 데 조심을 기한 것은 그 할아버지의 의심을 사지 않기 위해서였다.\n\n안에서 유스테시아의 목소리로 들려온 \"들려요, 기다려요\"라는 부드러운 말이, 그녀가 혼자임을 그에게 일러주었다.\n\n그는 늘 하던 식으로 울타리 둘레를 돌고 못가에서 어슬렁거리며 기다렸으니, 와일드브는 거만하면서도 짐짓 너그러운 그 연인에게 결코 집 안으로 청해진 적이 없었던 것이다. 그녀는 서둘러 나올 기색이 전혀 없었다. 시간이 흘렀고, 그는 초조해지기 시작했다. 이십 분쯤 지나자 그녀가 모퉁이를 돌아 나타나, 마치 그저 바람을 쐬러 나온 양 다가왔다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 일로 왔는지 알았더라면 이렇게 오래 기다리게 하지는 않았을 텐데.\" 그가 씁쓸하게 말했다. \"그래도 당신은 기다릴 만한 사람이오.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 있었나요?\" 유스테시아가 말했다. \"당신이 곤경에 처한 줄은 몰랐어요. 나도 충분히 우울한걸요.\"\n\n\"나는 곤경에 처한 게 아니오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그저 일이 어떤 고비에 이르렀고, 내가 분명한 길을 택해야 한다는 것뿐이오.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 길인데요?\" 그녀가 주의 깊은 관심을 보이며 물었다.\n\n\"지난밤에 내가 당신에게 제안한 걸 그새 잊을 수 있단 말이오? 아니, 당신을 이곳에서 데리고 나가, 함께 외국으로 떠나자고 한 것 말이오.\"\n\n\"잊지 않았어요. 한데 다음 토요일에야 오겠다고 약속해놓고, 어째서 이리도 뜻밖에 그 물음을 되풀이하러 온 거예요? 나는 곰곰 따져볼 시간이 넉넉히 있는 줄 알았는데요.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 이제 사정이 달라졌소.\"\n\n\"내게 설명해줘요.\"\n\n\"설명하고 싶지 않소, 당신을 괴롭히게 될지도 모르니.\"\n\n\"하지만 이 서두름의 까닭을 알아야겠어요.\"\n\n\"그저 내 열정 때문이오, 사랑하는 유스테시아. 이제 모든 게 매끄럽소.\"\n\n\"그럼 어째서 그리도 심기가 헝클어져 있나요?\"\n\n\"나는 그런 줄 모르겠소. 모든 게 마땅한 대로요. 요브라이트 부인이―아니, 그 여자는 우리에게 아무것도 아니오.\"\n\n\"아, 그 여자가 무언가 관련이 있는 줄 알았어요! 자, 나는 숨기는 거 싫어요.\"\n\n\"아니―그 여자는 아무것도 아니오. 그저 다른 사내가 토머신과 결혼하고 싶어 안달이니 내가 그 여자를 포기하기를 바란다고 할 뿐이오. 그 여자가, 이제 내가 더는 필요 없으니까, 정말 거드름을 피우는구려!\" 와일드브의 분함이 본인도 모르게 새어나왔다.\n\n유스테시아는 한참 동안 말이 없었다. \"당신은 더는 쓸모없어진 관리 같은 어색한 처지에 놓였군요.\" 그녀가 달라진 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"그런 것 같소. 하지만 나는 아직 토머신을 보지 못했소.\"\n\n\"그게 당신을 약 오르게 하는 거예요. 부인하지 말아요, 데이먼. 당신은 정말이지 뜻밖의 데서 날아온 이 무시에 발끈해 있어요.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\"\n\n\"그러니까 당신은 그 여자를 못 가지니까 나를 가지러 온 거예요. 이건 확실히 완전히 새로운 처지네요. 나더러 땜빵이 되라는 거잖아요.\"\n\n\"내가 며칠 전에도 똑같은 걸 제안했다는 걸 부디 잊지 마시오.\"\n\n유스테시아는 다시금 일종의 멍한 침묵에 잠겼다. 그녀에게 밀려오는 이 묘한 감정은 무엇이었을까? 와일드브를 향한 그녀의 관심이 그토록 전적으로 적대심의 산물이어서, 자기 연적이 더는 그를 탐내지 않는다는 첫 소리에 그 사내에게서 영광과 꿈이 떠나버렸다는 게 정말로 있을 법한 일일까? 그렇다면 이제 그녀는 마침내 그를 확실히 차지한 셈이었다. 토머신은 더는 그를 필요로 하지 않았다. 이 얼마나 굴욕적인 승리인가! 그가 자기를 가장 사랑한다고 그녀는 생각했다. 그런데도―이토록 배신스러운 비판을 아무리 나직이라도 감히 중얼거려도 될까?―제 자신보다 못한 여자조차 값쳐주지 않는 사내가 대체 무슨 값어치란 말인가? 살아 있는 온갖 자연에 다소간 도사린 그 정서―남들이 탐내지 않는 것은 탐하지 않는다는 그 정서가, 지나치게 섬세하고 향락적인 유스테시아의 마음속에서 정념처럼 생생했다. 그를 향한 그녀의 사회적 우월함은, 여태껏 거의 그녀에게 와닿은 적이 없었으나, 이제 불쾌하리만치 끈질기게 들이밀어졌고, 그녀는 난생처음 자기가 그를 사랑하느라 몸을 굽혔다는 걸 느꼈다.\n\n\"자, 그대여, 동의하는 거요?\" 와일드브가 말했다.\n\n\"미국 말고 런던, 아니 버드머스라도 된다면,\" 그녀가 나른하게 중얼거렸다. \"음, 생각해볼게요. 이건 내가 즉석에서 정하기엔 너무 큰 일이에요. 내가 이 히스를 덜 미워하든가―아니면 당신을 더 사랑하든가 했으면 좋으련만.\"\n\n\"당신은 사람을 아프도록 솔직할 줄 아는군. 한 달 전만 해도 당신은 나와 함께라면 어디든 갈 만큼 나를 뜨겁게 사랑했잖소.\"\n\n\"그리고 당신은 토머신을 사랑했죠.\"\n\n\"그래, 어쩌면 거기에 까닭이 있었는지도 모르지.\" 그가 거의 비웃음 섞인 투로 받아쳤다. \"나는 이제 그 여자를 미워하지 않소.\"\n\n\"바로 그거예요. 다만 당신이 더는 그 여자를 가질 수 없다는 게 문제죠.\"\n\n\"이봐―빈정대지 마시오, 유스테시아, 안 그러면 우리는 다투게 될 거요. 당신이 나와 함께 가기로, 그것도 곧 가기로 동의하지 않는다면 나는 혼자 떠나겠소.\"\n\n\"아니면 토머신에게 다시 매달려 보든가. 데이먼, 당신이 그 여자든 나든 아무래도 상관없이 결혼할 수 있었다는 게, 그러고는 내가 제일―값싸니까 나에게 왔다는 게 어쩜 이리도 묘한지! 그래, 그래―사실이에요. 한때 같으면 나는 그런 부류의 사내를 두고 호되게 따져들며 펄펄 뛰었을 테지만, 이제 다 지나간 일이에요.\"\n\n\"가겠소, 사랑하는 사람? 나와 몰래 브리스틀로 가서 결혼하고, 이 영국의 개굴 같은 곳에 영영 등을 돌립시다. 좋다고 말해줘요.\"\n\n\"나는 거의 무슨 대가를 치르더라도 여기서 벗어나고 싶어요.\" 그녀가 지친 듯이 말했다. \"하지만 당신과 함께 가기는 싫어요. 정할 시간을 더 줘요.\"\n\n\"이미 줬잖소.\" 와일드브가 말했다. \"음, 일주일 더 주겠소.\"\n\n\"조금만 더요, 내가 분명하게 답해줄 수 있도록요. 따져봐야 할 일이 너무 많아요. 토머신이 당신을 떼어내고 싶어 안달이라니, 상상이나 돼요? 나는 그걸 잊을 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"그건 신경 쓰지 마시오. 다음 주 월요일이라고 합시다. 바로 이 시각에 여기 와 있겠소.\"\n\n\"레인배로에서 만나요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"여기는 집과 너무 가까워요. 할아버지가 산책 나와 계실지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"고맙소, 그대여. 다음 주 월요일 이 시각에 무덤에 가 있겠소. 그때까지 안녕히.\"\n\n\"안녕히. 아니, 안 돼요, 지금은 나를 만지면 안 돼요. 내가 마음을 정할 때까지는 악수로 족해요.\"\n\n유스테시아는 그의 어슴푸레한 형상이 사라질 때까지 지켜보았다. 그녀는 손을 이마에 대고 무겁게 숨을 내쉬었고, 그러더니 그 풍성하고 낭만적인 입술이 그 수수한 충동에 못 이겨 벌어졌다―하품이었다. 그녀는 자신을 향한 정념이 어쩌면 스러질지도 모른다는 것을 제 자신에게라도 들켜버린 데에 이내 화가 났다. 그녀는 자신이 와일드브를 과대평가했을지도 모른다는 것을 단번에 인정할 수가 없었으니, 지금 그의 범상함을 알아본다는 것은 곧 여태껏 제가 저지른 크나큰 어리석음을 인정하는 것이었기 때문이다. 그리고 자신이 그토록 순전히 여물통 속의 개 같은 심보의 소유자라는 발견에는, 처음엔 그녀를 부끄럽게 만드는 무언가가 있었다.\n\n요브라이트 부인의 외교술이 맺은 열매는 과연 놀라웠으나, 아직은 그녀가 예상한 종류의 것은 아니었다. 그것은 와일드브에게 어느 정도 영향을 미쳤으나, 유스테시아에게는 훨씬 더 크게 영향을 미치고 있었다. 그녀의 연인은 이제 그녀에게 더는 숱한 여자들이 차지하려 다투고, 그녀 자신은 그들과 다투어야만 붙들 수 있는 그런 가슴 설레는 사내가 아니었다. 그는 군더더기였다.\n\n그녀는 정확히 슬픔이라고는 할 수 없는, 그리고 특히 그릇되게 판단한 덧없는 사랑이 끝나갈 무렵 이성이 동터올 때 따르곤 하는 그 묘한 비참함의 상태에 잠겨 집 안으로 들어갔다. 꿈의 끝이 다가오고 있음을, 그러면서도 아직 완전히 당도하지는 않았음을 의식하는 것은, 정념의 시작에서 그 끝에 이르는 길에서 가장 진을 빼는 동시에 가장 기이한 단계의 하나다.\n\n그녀의 할아버지가 돌아와, 새로 들어온 럼주 몇 갤런을 자신의 네모난 술궤 속 네모난 병들에 따라 넣느라 분주했다. 이 집안 비축분이 떨어질 때면 그는 콰이엇 우먼으로 가, 난로를 등지고 서서 그로그 잔을 손에 든 채, 자기가 어떻게 배의 흘수선 아래에서 칠 년을 살았는지며 그 밖의 해군의 경이들을 토박이들에게 들려주곤 했으니, 그들은 이야기꾼에게서 에일 한턱을 너무도 간절히 바란 나머지 그의 진실성에 조금의 의심도 내비치지 못했다.\n\n그는 이날 저녁에도 거기 다녀온 터였다. \"에그던 소식 들었겠지, 유스테시아?\" 그가 병들에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다. \"사내들이 그 술집에서 무슨 나라의 큰일이라도 되는 양 그 이야기를 하고 있더구나.\"\n\n\"아무 소식도 못 들었어요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"다들 클림 요브라이트라고 부르는 그 젊은이가, 다음 주에 어머니와 크리스마스를 보내러 집에 온단다. 이젠 어지간한 사내가 됐다더구나. 너도 그를 기억하겠지?\"\n\n\"평생 그를 본 적이 없는걸요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지. 네가 여기 오기 전에 그가 떠났으니. 나는 그를 장래가 촉망되는 소년으로 잘 기억하고 있단다.\"\n\n\"그동안 줄곧 어디서 살았는데요?\"\n\n\"그 호화와 허영의 소굴, 파리에서 살았다지, 아마.\"\n\n이맘때, 그리고 그 전의 화창한 날이면 어떤 덧없는 작업들이 사소한 방식으로나마 에그던 황야의 장엄한 정적을 어지럽히곤 했다. 도시나 마을, 심지어 농장의 활동과 견주면 그것은 그저 정체停滯의 발효, 졸음의 살갗이 스멀거리는 것쯤으로 보였을 테지만, 그러나 이곳에서는 비교 대상이 멀리 사라지고 변치 않는 언덕에 갇힌 채 그 가운데서는 그저 걷는 것조차 행렬의 새로움을 띠고 누구든지 자신을 아담이라고 상상하기에 조금도 어려움이 없는바, 시야에 든 모든 새와 아직 잠들지 않은 모든 파충류의 주의를 끌어, 둘레의 토끼들이 안전한 거리의 작은 둔덕에서 호기심 어린 눈으로 지켜보게 만들었다.\n\n그 작업이란 지난 화창한 날들 동안 험프리가 선장이 쓰도록 베어둔 가시금작화 다발을 한데 모아 더미로 쌓는 일이었다. 더미는 거처의 끝자락에 있었고, 그것을 쌓는 일에 매달린 이들은 험프리와 샘이었으며, 노인은 그것을 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n화창하고 고요한 오후, 세 시쯤이었으나, 동지冬至가 슬그머니 다가온 탓에 해가 낮게 걸려 시각은 실제보다 늦게 느껴졌으니, 이곳에는 거주민으로 하여금 하늘을 해시계로 여기던 여름의 경험을 버려야 함을 일깨워줄 만한 것이 거의 없었기 때문이다. 여러 날과 여러 주가 흐르는 동안 일출은 그 자리를 북동쪽에서 남동쪽으로 옮겨왔고, 일몰은 북서쪽에서 남서쪽으로 물러났으나, 에그던은 그 변화에 거의 마음을 두지 않았다.\n\n유스테이샤는 실내에, 사실은 부엌에 더 가까운 식당에 있었으니, 돌바닥에 입을 떡 벌린 굴뚝 모퉁이가 있는 방이었다. 공기는 잔잔했고, 그녀가 잠시 이곳에 홀로 머무는 동안 대화하는 목소리들이 굴뚝을 타고 곧장 그녀의 귀에 와 닿았다. 그녀는 우묵한 곳으로 들어가 귀를 기울이며, 동굴처럼 우묵한 자리들이 패인 그 오래되고 일그러진 굴뚝 통로를 올려다보았으니, 거기서는 연기가 꼭대기의 네모난 하늘 조각을 향해 가는 길에 갈팡질팡 헤매고 있었고, 그 하늘로부터 낮빛이 창백한 광채로 내리쏟아져, 바위 틈을 해초가 덮듯 굴뚝 통로를 늘어진 검댕 누더기들 위로 떨어지고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 떠올렸다. 가시금작화 더미는 굴뚝에서 멀지 않았고, 그 목소리들은 일꾼들의 것이었다.\n\n그녀의 할아버지가 대화에 끼어들었다. \"저 녀석은 결코 집을 떠나선 안 됐어. 제 아비의 일이 그 애한테 제일 맞았을 테고, 아들은 그 뒤를 이었어야지. 난 집안에서 이런 새로운 변화를 믿지 않아. 내 아비는 뱃사람이었고, 나도 그랬으니, 내게 아들이 있었다면 그 애도 그랬어야 했어.\"\n\n\"그 애가 살고 있는 곳은 파리예요.\" 험프리가 말했다. \"거기가 몇 해 전에 왕의 머리를 잘라낸 데라고들 하더군요. 가엾은 우리 어머니가 그 일을 곧잘 들려주셨지요. '허미야,' 어머니는 말씀하시곤 했어요. '그때 난 어린 처녀였는데, 어느 오후에 집에서 어머니 모자를 다림질하고 있는데 목사님이 들어오시더니, \"왕의 머리를 잘라냈다네, 제인. 다음엔 또 무슨 일이 벌어질지 하느님만 아시겠지.\"'\"\n\n\"우리 가운데 많은 사람도 머잖아 그분 못지않게 잘 알게 됐지.\" 선장이 껄껄 웃으며 말했다. \"난 그 일 때문에 소년 시절에 일곱 해를 물 밑에서 살았어—저 빌어먹을 트라이엄프 호의 수술실에서, 사내들이 팔다리가 예리코까지 날아간 채 갑판 밑 치료실로 실려 내려오는 걸 보면서 말이야…… 그래서 그 젊은 친구가 파리에 자리를 잡았다는 거지. 다이아몬드 상인의 지배인이라든가 뭐 그런 거 아닌가?\"\n\n\"네, 선장님, 바로 그겁니다. 그 애가 몸담은 데가 어마어마하게 큰 사업이라고, 그 애 어머니가 말하는 걸 들었지요—다이아몬드로 치면 임금님 궁전 같은 데라고요.\"\n\n\"그 애가 집을 떠날 때를 똑똑히 기억해.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"그 친구한텐 잘된 일이지.\" 험프리가 말했다. \"여기서 빈둥거리느니 다이아몬드를 파는 게 백번 낫고말고.\"\n\n\"그런 데서 거래하려면 실링께나 들겠군.\"\n\n\"실링께나 정도가 아니지, 이 사람아.\" 선장이 대답했다. \"그래, 술꾼도 대식가도 아니면서 큰돈을 날려버릴 수 있는 법이야.\"\n\n\"게다가 클림 예오브라이트가 진짜로 책을 파고드는 사람이 됐다고들 해요, 세상사에 관해 아주 별난 생각들을 품고서 말입니다. 거 봐요, 그게 다 일찍 학교에 다녔기 때문이지요, 그게 학교라고 할 만한 거였든 아니든.\"\n\n\"별난 생각이라, 그 애가?\" 노인이 말했다. \"아, 요즘은 학교에 보내는 일이 너무 지나쳐! 해만 끼칠 뿐이야. 어디 가도 문기둥이며 헛간 문짝마다 어린 망나니들이 분필로 갈겨놓은 못된 말이 꼭 적혀 있어서—여자는 더러 부끄러워서 지나가지도 못한다니까. 글 쓰는 법을 배운 적이 없었다면 그런 악담을 끄적이지도 못했을 게야. 그 애들 아비는 그런 걸 못했고, 그래서 시골은 그만큼 더 나았던 게지.\"\n\n\"그런데 제 생각엔, 선장님, 유스테이샤 아가씨도 여기 누구 못지않게 책에서 얻은 게 머릿속에 가득한 듯합니다만?\"\n\n\"아마 유스테이샤도 머릿속에 낭만적인 헛소리가 좀 덜 들었다면 그 애한테 더 나았을 거야.\" 선장이 짤막하게 말하고는 자리를 떴다.\n\n\"이봐, 샘.\" 노인이 사라지자 험프리가 말했다. \"그 애랑 클림 예오브라이트는 아주 그럴듯한 한 쌍이 될 거야—응? 안 그렇다면 내가 얼이 빠진 거지! 둘 다 고상한 것들에 대해 분명 한마음일 테고, 책에 통달했고, 늘 높은 학설을 생각하니—일부러 만들어내도 그보다 나은 짝은 없을 거야. 클림네 집안도 그 애 집안 못지않게 좋아. 그 애 아비가 농부였던 건 사실이지만, 그 애 어미는 우리도 알다시피 귀부인 비슷한 사람이었으니까. 그 둘이 부부가 되는 걸 보는 것보다 더 기쁜 일은 없겠어.\"\n\n\"그 둘이 팔짱을 끼고 제일 좋은 옷을 차려입으면 어쨌든 아주 말쑥해 보일 거야, 그 애가 예전처럼 잘생긴 친구이기만 하다면 말이지.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 험프리. 글쎄, 난 이렇게 여러 해가 지났으니 그 친구가 끔찍이 보고 싶어. 그가 언제 오는지 확실히 알면 한 서너 마일 마중을 나가서 짐을 들어주고 싶다니까—하기야 그가 예전의 소년에서 변했을 테지. 그가 처녀가 검은딸기 먹어 치우듯 빠르게 프랑스어를 한다고들 하니, 그렇다면 집에 남아 있던 우리 같은 자들은 그의 눈에 찌꺼기로밖에 안 보일 게 뻔해.\"\n\n\"증기선으로 물을 건너 버드머스로 온다면서, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"그래. 하지만 버드머스에서 어떻게 오는지는 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"그 애 사촌 토머신 일은 곤란한 골칫거리지. 클림처럼 그렇게 생각이 고상한 친구가 그런 일 속으로 집에 돌아오고 싶어 하다니 이상한 노릇이야. 그날 밤 둘을 부부라고 노래까지 불러줬는데, 사실은 아예 결혼도 안 했다는 걸 듣고서, 우리가 정말이지 얼마나 당황했던가! 내 친척이 사내한테 그렇게 바보 취급을 당했다면 난 얼이 다 빠졌을 거야. 그러면 집안이 작아 보이거든.\"\n\n\"그래. 가엾은 처녀, 그 일로 가슴이 충분히 아팠지. 건강도 그 때문에 상하고 있다더군, 통 밖에 안 나오고 안에만 틀어박혀 있다니까. 예전처럼 장미처럼 붉은 얼굴로 가시금작화 위를 뛰어다니는 모습을 이제는 도무지 못 보게 됐어.\"\n\n\"이젠 와일디브가 청한다 해도 그 처녀가 그를 받아들이지 않을 거라고 들었어.\"\n\n\"그랬어? 나한텐 처음 듣는 소린데.\"\n\n가시금작화를 모으던 이들이 이렇게 두서없이 대화를 나누는 동안 유스테이샤의 얼굴은 깊은 몽상에 잠겨 차츰 난로 쪽으로 기울어졌고, 발끝은 무심결에 발치에서 타고 있는 마른 잔디를 톡톡 두드리고 있었다.\n\n그들 이야기의 주제는 그녀의 흥미를 강하게 끌었다. 젊고 영리한 사내가, 세상에서 가장 대조적인 곳을 다 제쳐두고 하필이면 파리에서, 저 외로운 황야로 들어오고 있다는 것이었다. 그것은 마치 한 사내가 천국에서 오는 것 같았다. 더욱 기이하게도, 황야 사람들은 본능적으로 그와 그녀를 서로를 위해 태어난 한 쌍으로 마음속에 묶어두고 있었던 것이다.\n\n그 오 분간의 엿들음은 유스테이샤에게 텅 빈 오후 전체를 채우고도 남을 만큼의 환영을 안겨주었다. 정신적 공허에서 이렇게 돌연한 전환이 이따금 이토록 조용히 일어나곤 한다. 그녀는 아침나절만 해도 자신의 빛바랜 내면세계가 밤이 오기 전에 현미경 아래의 물처럼 활기를 띠리라고는, 그것도 단 한 명의 방문객도 오지 않은 채 그러리라고는 결코 믿지 못했을 것이다. 그 미지의 인물과 그녀 사이의 조화에 관한 샘과 험프리의 말은 그녀의 마음에 「나태의 성」에서 침입하는 음유시인의 전주곡과도 같은 효과를 미쳤으니, 그 전주곡에 앞서 텅 빈 정적만 있던 자리에서 갇혀 있던 무수한 형상들이 솟아올랐던 것이다.\n\n이런 상상에 잠겨 그녀는 시간 가는 줄을 몰랐다. 바깥세상이 다시 의식에 들어왔을 때는 어스름이었다. 가시금작화 더미는 완성되었고, 사내들은 집으로 돌아간 뒤였다. 유스테이샤는 늘 산책을 나가던 이 시간에 산책을 하리라 생각하며 위층으로 올라갔고, 그 산책의 방향을 젊은 예오브라이트의 출생지이자 지금은 그의 어머니가 살고 있는 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 정했다. 그녀에게는 다른 데로 걸어갈 이유가 없었으니, 어찌 그쪽으로 가지 못하겠는가? 백일몽의 무대는 열아홉 살에게 순례지로 삼기에 충분했다. 예오브라이트네 집 앞 말뚝 울타리를 바라보는 일은 꼭 해야 할 일의 위엄을 띠었다. 그런 한가한 짓거리가 중대한 볼일처럼 여겨졌다니 이상한 노릇이었다.\n\n그녀는 보닛을 쓰고 집을 나서, 블룸스엔드 쪽 비탈을 내려가, 골짜기를 따라 일 마일 반쯤 천천히 걸었다. 그러자 골짜기의 푸른 바닥이 넓어지기 시작하는 지점에 이르렀으니, 가시금작화 덤불이 길 양편에서 더욱 물러나 토양이 점점 비옥해지면서 여기저기 외따로 한 그루씩 남을 만큼 줄어들고 있었다. 고르지 못한 풀밭 카펫 너머로는 흰 말뚝 울타리 한 줄이 있어, 이 위도에서 황야의 가장자리를 표시하고 있었다. 그것들은 자기네가 경계 짓고 있는 어스름한 풍경 위로, 벨벳 위의 흰 레이스만큼이나 또렷이 드러나 있었다. 흰 말뚝 울타리 뒤에는 작은 정원이 있었고, 그 정원 뒤에는 황야를 마주한, 골짜기 전체를 한눈에 굽어보는 오래되고 일그러진 초가집 한 채가 있었다. 이곳이 바로, 만년의 삶을 유행 세계의 중심이자 소용돌이인 프랑스 수도에서 보낸 한 사내가 막 돌아오려 하는, 그 후미지고 외딴 자리였다.\n\n그날 오후 내내 유스테이샤가 골똘히 생각하던 그 인물이 도착하리라는 예상은 블룸스엔드에서 준비의 부산함을 자아냈다. 토머신은 고모의 설득과, 사촌 클림에 대한 본능적인 충정의 충동에 이끌려, 생애 가장 슬픈 이 나날에는 좀처럼 보기 드문 민첩함으로 그를 위해 분주히 움직였다. 유스테이샤가 클림의 귀향에 관한 더미 쌓는 이들의 대화에 귀를 기울이고 있을 무렵, 토머신은 고모네 땔감 창고 위 다락으로 기어 올라가고 있었으니, 저장 사과를 두는 곳이라, 다가오는 명절을 위해 그 가운데 가장 좋고 큰 것들을 골라내려는 참이었다.\n\n다락은 반원형 구멍으로 빛이 들었으니, 비둘기들이 그 구멍을 통해 같은 높은 자리에 있는 자기네 보금자리로 기어들곤 했다. 이 구멍에서 햇빛이 환한 노란 조각을 이루며, 처녀가 무릎을 꿇고 맨팔을 부드러운 갈색 고사리 속으로 파묻는 모습 위로 비쳐 들었으니, 그 고사리는 풍부하기에 에그던에서는 온갖 저장물을 싸두는 데 쓰였다. 비둘기들은 더할 나위 없이 무심하게 그녀의 머리 둘레를 날아다녔고, 고모의 얼굴은 다락 바닥 위로 간신히 보일 만큼 떠올라 있었으니, 떠도는 몇 가닥 빛 티끌에 비추인 채 사다리 중간쯤에 서서, 자신은 올라갈 만큼 솜씨가 좋지 못해 감히 들어서지 못하는 곳을 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"이제 러셋 사과를 몇 개, 탬신. 그 애는 그걸 립스톤 사과만큼이나 좋아했지.\"\n\n토머신은 몸을 돌려 또 다른 구석의 고사리를 헤쳐냈으니, 거기서는 더 무르익은 열매가 농익은 냄새로 그녀를 맞이했다. 그것들을 골라내기 전에 그녀는 잠시 멈추었다.\n\n\"사랑하는 클림, 지금 네 얼굴은 어떤 모습일까?\" 그녀는 비둘기 구멍을 멍하니 바라보며 말했으니, 그 구멍으로 햇빛이 그녀의 갈색 머리카락과 투명한 살결 위로 어찌나 곧장 비쳐 들던지 거의 그녀를 꿰뚫고 빛나는 듯했다.\n\n\"그 애가 너에게 다른 방식으로 소중한 사이였더라면,\" 사다리에서 예오브라이트 부인이 말했다. \"이번 만남은 행복한 자리가 됐을 텐데.\"\n\n\"아무 소용도 없는 말을 해서 무슨 쓸모가 있어요, 고모?\"\n\n\"있지.\" 고모가 다소 열을 띠며 말했다. \"지난 불행으로 공기를 속속들이 채워서, 다른 처녀들이 경고로 삼아 그런 일을 멀리하게 하는 거야.\"\n\n토머신은 다시 사과 쪽으로 얼굴을 숙였다. \"전 다른 이들에게 경고가 되는 거예요, 도둑이며 술꾼이며 노름꾼이 그러듯이요.\" 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"그런 부류에 끼다니! 제가 정말 그들 부류인가요? 어처구니없어요! 그런데 고모, 왜 다들 저를 대하는 태도로 제가 그렇다고 자꾸 생각하게 만드는 걸까요? 왜 사람들은 제 행실로 저를 판단하지 않는 거죠? 자, 제가 여기 무릎 꿇고 이 사과들을 줍는 모습을 보세요—제가 타락한 여자처럼 보이나요?…… 모든 선량한 여자들이 저만큼만 선량하면 좋으련만!\" 그녀가 격하게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"낯선 사람들은 나처럼 너를 보지 않아.\" 예오브라이트 부인이 말했다. \"그들은 거짓 소문으로 판단하지. 그래, 이건 어리석은 일이고, 나한테도 얼마간 잘못이 있어.\"\n\n\"경솔한 일은 어찌 그리 순식간에 저질러지는지!\" 처녀가 대꾸했다. 그녀의 입술이 떨리고 있었고, 눈물이 어찌나 눈에 그득 차오르던지, 약한 모습을 감추려 부지런히 사과를 골라내면서도 사과와 고사리를 거의 분간하지 못할 지경이었다.\n\n\"사과를 다 챙기는 대로,\" 고모가 사다리를 내려오며 말했다. \"내려오너라, 우리 호랑가시나무를 구하러 가자. 오늘 오후엔 황야에 아무도 없으니, 사람들 눈에 띌까 두려워할 것 없다. 빨간 열매를 좀 구해야지, 안 그러면 클림은 우리가 준비했다는 걸 믿지 않을 게야.\"\n\n사과를 다 모으자 토머신은 내려왔고, 둘은 함께 흰 말뚝 울타리를 지나 그 너머 황야로 갔다. 탁 트인 언덕은 공기가 상쾌하고 맑았으며, 먼 대기는 화창한 겨울날에 흔히 그러하듯 저마다 독립적으로 색조를 띤 또렷한 빛의 층들로 나타났으니, 풍경의 가까운 자락을 밝히는 빛살은 더 먼 자락을 가로질러 눈에 보이게 흘러갔고, 사프란빛으로 물든 빛의 한 층이 짙은 푸른빛 한 층 위에 얹혔으며, 이것들 뒤로는 더욱 먼 풍경들이 싸늘한 잿빛에 싸여 누워 있었다.\n\n그들은 호랑가시나무가 자라는 곳에 이르렀으니, 원뿔형으로 파인 구덩이 안이라 나무 꼭대기조차 지면의 일반 높이보다 그리 높지 않았다. 토머신은 비슷한 자리에서 더 행복한 처지에 여러 차례 그러했듯이 덤불 하나의 갈라진 가지 위로 올라섰고, 그들이 가져온 작은 도끼로 빨간 열매가 묵직하게 달린 가지들을 쳐내기 시작했다.\n\n\"얼굴을 긁히지 마라.\" 구덩이 가장자리에 서서, 반짝이는 초록과 진홍 더미 속에 매달린 처녀를 바라보며 고모가 말했다. \"오늘 저녁 그를 마중하러 나랑 같이 가겠니?\"\n\n\"그러고 싶어요. 안 그러면 제가 그를 잊은 것처럼 보일 테니까요.\" 토머신이 가지 하나를 던지며 말했다. \"그게 그리 대수로운 일은 아니지만요. 전 한 사내한테 속한 몸이고, 그건 무엇으로도 바꿀 수 없으니까요. 그리고 그 사내와 전 결혼해야 해요, 제 자존심을 위해서라도요.\"\n\n\"내 걱정은—\" 예오브라이트 부인이 입을 열었다.\n\n\"아, 고모는 이렇게 생각하시는 거죠. '저 약해빠진 애가—마음만 먹으면 어떻게 자기랑 결혼할 사내를 얻겠다는 거야?' 하지만 한 가지만 말씀드릴게요, 고모. 와일디브 씨는 방탕한 사내가 아니에요, 제가 부정한 여자가 아닌 것과 마찬가지로요. 그분은 태도가 좀 불운한 데가 있고, 사람들이 제 마음으로 좋아하려 들지 않으면 굳이 자기를 좋아하게 만들려고 애쓰지 않을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"토머신.\" 예오브라이트 부인이 조용히 조카에게 눈길을 박으며 말했다. \"와일디브 씨를 그렇게 두둔하면서 네가 나를 속이고 있다고 생각하니?\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말씀이세요?\"\n\n\"난 오래전부터 의심해왔다, 네가 그를 네가 생각하던 성인聖人이 아님을 알게 된 뒤로 그에 대한 네 사랑의 빛깔이 변했으며, 네가 내 앞에서 연기를 하고 있다고 말이다.\"\n\n\"그분은 저와 결혼하고 싶어 했고, 저도 그분과 결혼하고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"자, 그럼 내가 너한테 묻겠다. 그 일이 너를 그와 얽어매지 않았더라면, 너는 지금 이 순간에도 그의 아내가 되겠다고 하겠느냐?\"\n\n토머신은 나무 안쪽을 들여다보았고 몹시 동요한 듯했다. \"고모.\" 그녀가 이윽고 말했다. \"전 그 물음에 답하기를 거부할 권리가 있다고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"그래, 있고말고.\"\n\n\"고모 좋으실 대로 생각하세요. 전 말로든 행동으로든 제가 그분을 달리 생각하게 됐다는 기색을 고모께 내비친 적이 없고, 앞으로도 결코 그러지 않을 거예요. 그리고 전 그분과 결혼할 거예요.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그가 청혼을 다시 할 때까지 기다려보렴. 이제 그가—내가 그에게 말해준 어떤 걸—알았으니, 그가 그럴지도 모르겠구나. 네가 그와 결혼하는 게 가장 온당한 일이라는 건 나도 한순간도 의심하지 않아. 지난날 내가 그를 그토록 못마땅해했지만, 이제는 너와 생각이 같단다, 그건 분명히 알아두렴. 그것만이 거짓된 처지에서, 그것도 몹시 속 쓰린 처지에서 벗어나는 유일한 길이니까.\"\n\n\"그분께 뭐라고 하셨는데요?\"\n\n\"그가 네 다른 구애자의 앞길을 막고 서 있다고 했지.\"\n\n\"고모.\" 토머신이 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 말했다. \"대체 무슨 말씀이세요?\"\n\n\"놀라지 마라. 그건 내 도리였어. 지금은 그 일에 대해 더 말할 수 없지만, 다 끝나면 내가 정확히 뭐라고 했는지, 또 왜 그렇게 말했는지 네게 일러주마.\"\n\n토머신은 어쩔 수 없이 잠자코 따랐다.\n\n\"그럼 제가 결혼하려 한다는 비밀은 당분간 클림에게 숨겨주실 거죠?\" 그녀가 이어 물었다.\n\n\"그러마고 약속했지. 하지만 그게 무슨 소용이겠니? 무슨 일이 있었는지 그가 곧 알게 될 텐데. 네 얼굴만 한 번 봐도 뭔가 잘못됐다는 걸 알아챌 게야.\"\n\n토머신은 나무에서 몸을 돌려 고모를 바라보았다. \"자, 제 말 좀 들어보세요.\" 그녀가 말했으니, 그 가냘픈 목소리는 육체적인 것과는 다른 어떤 힘으로 단호하게 부풀어 올랐다. \"그분께 아무 말도 하지 마세요. 제가 그분의 사촌 될 자격이 없다는 걸 그분이 알아낸다면, 그러라고 하세요. 하지만 그분이 한때 저를 사랑했으니, 제 곤경을 너무 일찍 알려서 그분을 아프게 하진 않을 거예요. 이 이야기가 사방에 퍼져 있다는 건 저도 알아요. 하지만 험담꾼들도 처음 며칠은 감히 그분께 그 말을 꺼내지 못할 거예요. 제가 그분과 가까운 사이라는 바로 그 점이 이야기가 일찍 그분 귀에 들어가는 걸 막아줄 테니까요. 한두 주 안에 제가 비웃음에서 안전해지지 못한다면, 제가 직접 그분께 말씀드릴게요.\"\n\n토머신이 말하는 그 진지함이 더 이상의 반대를 가로막았다. 고모는 그저 이렇게만 말했다. \"좋아. 결혼식을 올릴 거라는 걸 마땅히 그때 그에게 알렸어야 했어. 그는 네가 숨긴 걸 결코 용서하지 않을 게다.\"\n\n\"용서하실 거예요, 제가 그분을 아끼고 싶어서 그랬다는 걸, 그리고 제가 그분이 이렇게 빨리 집에 오시리라고는 예상하지 못했다는 걸 아시면요. 그리고 저 때문에 고모의 크리스마스 모임을 그르치게 하면 안 돼요. 미루는 건 일을 더 나쁘게 만들 뿐이니까요.\"\n\n\"물론 그러지 않을 게다. 난 온 에그던 앞에서, 또 와일디브 같은 사내의 놀림감이 되어 패배한 꼴을 보이고 싶지 않아. 이제 빨간 열매는 넉넉히 모은 듯하니, 집으로 가져가는 게 좋겠구나. 이걸로 집을 꾸미고 겨우살이를 매달 즈음이면, 그를 마중하러 나설 채비를 해야 할 게야.\"\n\n토머신은 나무에서 내려와, 머리와 옷에 떨어진 흩어진 열매들을 털어내고, 고모와 함께 비탈을 내려갔으니, 두 여자는 저마다 모은 가지의 절반씩을 이고 있었다. 이제 거의 네 시였고, 햇빛은 골짜기를 떠나고 있었다. 서쪽이 붉어지자 두 친척은 다시 집에서 나와, 처음과는 다른 방향으로, 기다리던 사내가 돌아올 저 먼 큰길의 한 지점을 향해 황야 속으로 들어섰다.\n\n유스테이샤는 황야 어귀에 막 들어선 채, 예오브라이트 부인의 집과 땅 쪽으로 눈을 부릅뜨고 있었다. 그곳에는 불빛도, 소리도, 움직임도 분간되지 않았다. 저녁은 쌀쌀했고, 그 자리는 어둡고 외로웠다. 그녀는 손님이 아직 오지 않았다고 짐작했고, 십 분이나 십오 분쯤 머물다가 다시 집 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n왔던 길을 그리 멀리 되짚어 가지 않았을 때, 앞쪽에서 들려오는 소리가 같은 길을 따라 대화하며 다가오는 사람들의 접근을 알려주었다. 이윽고 그들의 머리가 하늘을 배경으로 드러났다. 그들은 천천히 걷고 있었고, 생김새로 인물됨을 알아보기엔 너무 어두웠으나, 그 걸음걸이로 보아 황야의 일꾼들은 아니었다. 유스테이샤는 그들이 지나가도록 발길에서 조금 비켜섰다. 그들은 두 여자와 한 사내였고, 여자들의 목소리는 예오브라이트 부인과 토머신의 것이었다.\n\n그들은 그녀 곁을 지나쳤고, 스쳐 가는 순간 그녀의 어스름한 형체를 알아본 듯했다. 한 사내의 목소리로 \"안녕히 주무세요!\" 하는 소리가 그녀의 귀에 와 닿았다. 그녀는 우물거리며 답하고는, 그들 곁을 미끄러져 지나, 몸을 돌렸다. 그녀는 한순간 믿을 수가 없었으니, 우연이 청하지도 않았는데 그녀 앞으로, 그녀가 살펴보러 갔던 그 집의 영혼을, 그가 없었다면 그 살핌도 애초에 생각조차 못 했을 그 사내를 데려다 놓았다는 것을.\n\n그녀는 그들을 보려고 눈을 부릅떴으나 그럴 수 없었다. 그러나 그 골똘함이 어찌나 깊던지, 마치 그녀의 귀가 듣는 기능뿐 아니라 보는 기능까지 수행하는 듯했다. 그런 순간에는 이런 능력의 확장이 거의 믿어질 만하다. 귀가 먼 키토 박사도 오랜 노력 끝에 자신의 몸이 진동에 어찌나 민감해졌던지 귀로 하듯 그 몸으로 지각하는 능력을 얻었다고 묘사했을 때, 아마 이와 비슷한 환상에 사로잡혀 있었을 것이다.\n\n그녀는 거니는 이들이 입에 올리는 말을 한마디 한마디 좇을 수 있었다. 그들은 비밀을 이야기하고 있지 않았다. 그들은 그저, 몸은 오래 떨어져 있었으나 마음은 그렇지 않았던 친척들이 으레 나누는 활기찬 잡담에 빠져 있을 뿐이었다. 그러나 유스테이샤가 귀 기울인 것은 그 말들이 아니었으니, 그녀는 몇 분 뒤에는 그 말들이 무엇이었는지조차 떠올리지 못했을 것이다. 그것은 그 말 가운데 십분의 일쯤을 내놓던 번갈아 들려오는 목소리—그녀에게 안녕히 주무시라 했던 그 목소리였다. 그 목구멍은 어떤 때는 예, 라고 하고, 어떤 때는 아니오, 라고 했으며, 어떤 때는 그 고장의 한 늙은 거주민에 관해 물었다. 한번은 둘레 언덕들의 얼굴에 어린 다정함과 정겨움을 두고 한마디 함으로써 그녀의 관념을 놀라게 했다.\n\n세 목소리는 지나가 버렸고, 그녀의 귓전에서 잦아들다가 사라졌다. 이만큼이 그녀에게 허락되었고, 그 밖의 모든 것은 보류되었다. 이보다 더 흥미진진한 일은 있을 수 없었다. 오후의 대부분을 그녀는 아름다운 파리에서—그곳의 분위기를 가득 싣고, 그곳의 매혹에 익숙한 채—곧장 온 사내에게 따르기 마련인 매력을 상상하며 황홀경에 빠져 있었다. 그런데 그 사내가 그녀에게 인사를 건넨 것이다. 형체들이 멀어지자 여자들의 흘러넘치던 또렷한 말소리는 그녀의 기억에서 스러져갔으나, 그 다른 목소리의 억양은 머물렀다. 예오브라이트 부인의 아들—그가 바로 클림이었으니—그 목소리에 소리로서 놀랄 만한 무언가가 있었던가? 아니었다. 그것은 그저 모든 것을 아우르는 목소리였다. 그 \"안녕히 주무세요\"를 말한 이에게는 온갖 감정의 일이 가능했다. 유스테이샤의 상상이 나머지를 채워주었다—단 하나의 수수께끼에 대한 해답만 빼고. 이 텁수룩한 언덕들에서 다정함과 정겨움을 본 그 사내의 취향은 대체 어떤 것일 수 있단 말인가?\n\n이런 때에는 잔뜩 충전된 여인의 머릿속을 천 가지 생각이 스쳐 지나가고, 그것들은 그녀의 얼굴에 드러나기 마련이지만, 그 변화는 실제이긴 해도 미세하다. 유스테이샤의 이목구비는 그런 변화의 율동적인 연속을 거쳤다. 그녀는 발갛게 달아올랐다가, 상상의 거짓됨을 떠올리며 시들해졌다가, 다시 생기를 띠었다가, 다시 불타올랐다가, 다시 식어버렸다. 그것은 환영의 순환이 빚어낸 양상樣相의 순환이었다.\n\n유스테이샤는 자기 집에 들어섰으니, 흥분해 있었다. 그녀의 할아버지는 난롯가에서 흥겨워하며, 재를 헤집어 잔디의 시뻘겋게 단 표면을 드러내고 있었으니, 그 음산한 빛이 굴뚝 모퉁이를 용광로 같은 빛깔로 환히 밝혔다.\n\n\"우리는 어째서 예오브라이트네 사람들과 통 가깝게 지내지 않는 거예요?\" 그녀가 앞으로 나와 그 온기 위로 부드러운 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"우리가 가깝게 지내면 좋겠어요. 아주 좋은 사람들 같던데요.\"\n\n\"내가 그 이유를 알면 목을 매달지.\" 선장이 말했다. \"그 노인은 산울타리처럼 거칠긴 했어도 난 그런대로 좋아했어. 하지만 넌 거기 갈 수 있었다 해도 결코 가고 싶어 하지 않았을 게다, 그건 내가 잘 알지.\"\n\n\"제가 왜 안 가고 싶어 했겠어요?\"\n\n\"네 도시 취향에는 그들이 너무 촌스러워 보였을 거다. 그들은 부엌에 앉아 벌꿀술과 엘더베리주를 마시고, 바닥을 깨끗이 하려고 모래를 뿌린단다. 분별 있는 삶의 방식이지. 하지만 네가 그걸 좋아하겠니?\"\n\n\"예오브라이트 부인은 귀부인다운 여자인 줄 알았는데요? 부목사의 딸이지 않았나요?\"\n\n\"그래. 하지만 남편이 사는 대로 살 수밖에 없었고, 이제는 그 삶에 정이 들었겠지. 아, 생각해보니 내가 언젠가 무심코 그 사람 기분을 상하게 한 적이 있어서, 그 뒤로는 통 못 봤구나.\"\n\n그날 밤은 유스테이샤의 머릿속에 사건으로 가득한 밤이었고, 그녀가 좀처럼 잊지 못할 밤이었다. 그녀는 꿈을 꾸었으니, 네부카드네자르에서 스와팜의 땜장이에 이르기까지, 그보다 더 놀라운 꿈을 꾼 인간은 거의 없었다. 그토록 정교하게 펼쳐지고, 어리둥절하고, 흥분되는 꿈을 유스테이샤 같은 처지의 처녀가 꾼 적은 분명 일찍이 없었다. 그것은 크레타의 미궁만큼이나 갈래가 많았고, 북극광만큼이나 변화무쌍했으며, 유월의 화단만큼이나 색채가 풍부했고, 대관식만큼이나 형상들로 북적였다. 셰에라자드 여왕에게라면 그 꿈은 평범한 것에서 그리 멀지 않게 여겨졌을지도 모르고, 유럽의 온갖 궁정을 막 돌아온 처녀에게라면 흥미로운 정도로밖에 여겨지지 않았을지도 모른다. 그러나 유스테이샤의 삶의 정황 속에서 그것은 꿈이 그럴 수 있는 한 가장 경이로운 것이었다.\n\n그러나 그 변환의 장면들로부터 차츰 덜 터무니없는 한 삽화가 펼쳐져 나왔으니, 그 속에서는 전체적인 화려함을 거느린 행위 뒤로 황야가 어렴풋이 나타났다. 그녀는 경이로운 음악에 맞추어 춤추고 있었고, 그녀의 상대는 앞서의 환상적인 변화들을 그녀와 함께 거쳐온 은빛 갑옷의 사내였으니, 투구의 면갑은 닫혀 있었다. 춤의 미로는 황홀했다. 빛나는 투구 아래에서 부드러운 속삭임이 그녀의 귓속으로 흘러들었고, 그녀는 낙원에 있는 여인 같은 기분이었다. 별안간 이 둘은 춤추는 무리에서 빙글 돌아 빠져나와, 황야의 못 하나로 뛰어들더니, 무지개로 아치를 이룬 어느 영롱한 우묵한 곳으로 나왔다. \"여기인 게 분명해.\" 곁의 목소리가 말했고, 얼굴을 붉히며 올려다보니 그가 입 맞추려고 투구를 벗고 있었다. 바로 그 순간 우지끈하는 소리가 나더니, 그의 형체가 카드 한 벌처럼 산산조각으로 무너져 내렸다.\n\n그녀는 소리 내어 울부짖었다. \"아, 그의 얼굴을 보았더라면!\"\n\n유스테이샤는 깨어났다. 그 우지끈하는 소리는 아래층 덧창 소리였으니, 하녀가 그것을 열어 한 해의 이 병약한 시절에 자연이 인색하게 베푸는, 이제 더디게 늘어나는 낮빛을 들이는 참이었다. \"아, 그의 얼굴을 보았더라면!\" 그녀가 다시 말했다. \"그건 예오브라이트 씨를 뜻한 거였어!\"\n\n좀 더 마음이 식자 그녀는 그 꿈의 여러 국면이 으레 전날의 형상과 공상에서 비롯되었음을 알아차렸다. 그러나 이것은 꿈의 흥미를 거의 깎아내리지 못했으니, 그 흥미란 새로이 지펴진 열정에 더없이 좋은 땔감을 대주는 데 있었기 때문이다. 그녀는 무관심과 사랑 사이의 변조점變調點에, 이른바 \"마음이 끌리는\" 단계에 있었다. 그것은 가장 거대한 정념의 역사에서도 단 한 번 찾아오며, 그 정념이 가장 약한 의지의 손아귀에 놓이는 시기이다.\n\n이 열정에 들뜬 여인은 이제 환영과 반쯤 사랑에 빠져 있었다. 그녀의 정념이 띤 환상적 성질은 그녀를 지성으로서는 낮추었으나 영혼으로서는 높여주었다. 그녀에게 자제력이 조금만 더 있었더라면 순전한 추론으로 그 감정을 무無로 잦아들게 하여 죽여버렸을 것이다. 자존심이 조금만 덜했더라면, 그를 볼 때까지 처녀로서 어떤 희생을 치르더라도 블룸스엔드의 예오브라이트네 땅을 빙빙 돌며 서성였을 것이다. 그러나 유스테이샤는 이 가운데 어느 것도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 그토록 영향을 받았을 때 가장 모범적인 처녀가 했을 법한 대로 행동했으니, 하루에 두세 번 에그던 언덕으로 바람을 쐬러 나가, 두 눈을 부지런히 놀렸다.\n\n첫 번째 나들이가 지나갔으나, 그는 그쪽으로 오지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 두 번째로 거닐었으나, 거기서 또다시 홀로 헤매는 이였다.\n\n세 번째에는 짙은 안개가 끼었으니, 그녀는 둘레를 둘러보았으나 큰 기대는 없었다. 설령 그가 그녀에게서 스무 야드 안에서 걷고 있었다 한들 그녀는 그를 보지 못했을 것이다.\n\n그를 만나려는 네 번째 시도에서는 비가 억수같이 퍼붓기 시작했고, 그녀는 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n다섯 번째 출격은 오후였으니, 날이 화창하여 그녀는 오래도록 밖에 머물며, 블룸스엔드가 자리한 골짜기의 맨 꼭대기까지 걸어 올라갔다. 그녀는 반 마일쯤 떨어진 흰 말뚝 울타리를 보았으나, 그는 나타나지 않았다. 그녀가 집으로 돌아온 것은 거의 가슴앓이를 하면서였고, 자신의 나약함에 부끄러움을 느끼면서였다. 그녀는 더는 파리에서 온 사내를 찾지 않기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그러나 섭리란 요염하지 않으면 아무것도 아닌 법이니, 유스테이샤가 이 결심을 굳히자마자, 찾을 때는 온전히 보류되었던 그 기회가 찾아왔다.\n\n기다림의 마지막 날인 이 십이월 이십삼일 저녁, 유스테이샤는 집에 홀로 있었다. 그녀는 새로이 귀에 들어온 한 소문—예오브라이트가 어머니를 찾아온 일정이 짧게 끝나, 다음 주 어느 때쯤 마무리되리라는 소문—을 두고 한탄하며 근래의 한 시간을 보냈다. \"당연하지.\" 그녀는 속으로 말했다. 화려한 도시에서 활동에 한창 박차를 가하던 사내가 에그던 황야에서 오래 머물 여유는 없을 터였다. 그 깨어나게 하는 목소리의 임자를 그토록 짧은 명절 기간 안에 얼굴을 맞대고 보게 될 가능성은, 그녀가 울새처럼 그의 어머니 집 언저리를 맴돌지 않는 한 거의 없었으니, 그렇게 하는 것은 어렵고도 꼴사나운 일이었다.\n\n이런 처지의 시골 처녀와 사내가 흔히 쓰는 방편은 교회에 가는 일이다. 여느 마을이나 시골 읍에서는, 크리스마스 당일이든 그 인접한 일요일이든, 명절을 쇠러 고향에 와 있는 토박이로서 나이나 권태로 보고 보이려는 욕구를 잃지 않은 자라면 누구나 어느 신도석엔가 나타나, 희망과 자의식과 새 옷으로 빛나리라고 안심하고 셈할 수 있다. 그리하여 크리스마스 아침의 회중會衆은 대개 그 인근에서 태어난 명사들의 투소 밀랍 인형 진열장 같은 것이 된다. 한 해 내내 집에 버려진 채 방치된 정인情人은 이곳으로 몰래 와서, 자기를 잊어버린 돌아온 연인의 변모를 지켜보며, 기도서 너머로 그를 살피며 이런 생각을 할 수 있으니, 새로운 것들이 매력을 잃고 나면 그가 되살아난 정절情節로 두근거릴지도 모른다는 것이다. 또한 유스테이샤처럼 비교적 최근에 정착한 이도 이곳으로 와서, 그녀가 이 무대에 등장하기 전에 고향을 떠난 한 토박이 아들의 인물됨을 면밀히 살피고, 그가 다음번에 돌아왔을 때 그를 알아둘 발판을 마련하고자 그가 다음번에 떠나 있는 동안 그 부모와의 친분을 다질 만한 가치가 있을지 따져볼 수 있는 것이다.\n\n그러나 이런 다정한 책략들은 에그던 황야의 흩어진 주민들 사이에서는 실현될 수 없었다. 명목상으로 그들은 교구민이었으나, 실질적으로는 어느 교구에도 속해 있지 않았다. 친구들과 크리스마스를 쇠러 이 몇 채 안 되는 외딴집들로 온 사람들은 영영 떠날 때까지 친구의 굴뚝 모퉁이에 눌러앉아 벌꿀술이며 그 밖의 속을 데워주는 술을 마셨다. 둘레 사방이 비며 눈이며 얼음이며 진흙이니, 어느 정도 이웃이긴 해도 교회 가까이 살면서 깨끗하고 보송보송하게 교회에 드는 사람들 틈에, 발이 젖고 목덜미까지 흙탕물이 튄 채 앉아 있으려고 이삼 마일을 터덜터덜 걸어갈 마음은 없었던 것이다. 유스테이샤는 클림 예오브라이트가 며칠 안 되는 휴가 동안 어느 교회에도 가지 않을 공산이 십중팔구라는 것을, 그리고 그곳에서 그를 보겠다는 희망으로 험한 길에 조랑말과 이륜마차를 몰고 가는 것이 헛수고이리라는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n어스름이었고, 그녀는 식당 또는 홀의 난롯가에 앉아 있었으니, 한 해의 이 시절에는 응접실보다 이 방을 즐겨 쓰는 까닭은 큰 난로가 있기 때문이었고, 그것은 잔디 불을 때려고 만든 것으로, 선장이 겨울철에 즐기는 땔감이었다. 방 안에 눈에 띄는 물건이라곤 창턱 위에 놓인 것들뿐이었으니, 낮게 깔린 하늘을 배경으로 제 모양을 드러내고 있었고, 가운데 것은 오래된 모래시계였으며, 다른 두 개는 근처 봉분에서 파낸 고대 브리튼의 항아리 한 쌍으로, 면도날 같은 잎의 선인장 두 그루를 심은 화분으로 쓰이고 있었다. 누군가 문을 두드렸다. 하녀는 외출 중이었고, 할아버지도 그러했다. 그 사람은 잠깐 기다린 뒤 안으로 들어와 방문을 두드렸다.\n\n\"거기 누구세요?\" 유스테이샤가 말했다.\n\n\"저, 바이 선장님, 저희가 좀—\"\n\n유스테이샤가 일어나 문으로 갔다. \"그렇게 막무가내로 들어오게 둘 수는 없어. 기다렸어야지.\"\n\n\"선장님이 별 격식 차리지 말고 들어와도 된다고 하셨어요.\" 소년의 쾌활한 목소리로 답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"아, 그러셨어?\" 유스테이샤가 좀 더 부드럽게 말했다. \"무슨 일이니, 찰리?\"\n\n\"저, 오늘 밤 일곱 시에 저희가 배역을 연습해보게 할아버님께서 땔감 창고를 좀 빌려주실 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"뭐, 너 올해 에그던 가면극 패의 한 사람이냐?\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨. 선장님께서 예전 가면극 패도 여기서 연습하게 해주시곤 했어요.\"\n\n\"알겠다. 그래, 원하면 땔감 창고를 써도 좋아.\" 유스테이샤가 나른하게 말했다.\n\n연습 장소로 바이 선장의 땔감 창고를 고른 것은 그의 거처가 황야의 거의 한가운데에 있다는 사실에서 비롯된 것이었다. 땔감 창고는 헛간만큼이나 널찍하여 그런 목적에는 더없이 알맞은 곳이었다. 그 연희패를 이룬 소년들은 둘레의 여기저기 흩어진 곳에 살았으니, 이 자리에서 만나면 오는 이들 모두가 가로질러야 할 거리가 대체로 엇비슷하게 나뉘었다.\n\n가면극과 가면극 놀이에 대해 유스테이샤는 더없는 경멸을 품고 있었다. 가면극 배우들 자신은 자기네 예술에 대해 그런 감정에 시달리지는 않았으나, 그렇다고 열성적이지도 않았다. 전통적 유희가 한낱 부활과 구별되는 가장 두드러진 특징이라면, 부활에서는 모든 것이 흥분과 열기인 데 반해, 존속하는 것은 무덤덤함과 동요의 부재 속에 이어진다는 점이니, 그토록 건성으로 행해지는 일을 대체 왜 계속 이어가는지 의아하게 만든다. 발람과 그 밖의 마지못한 예언자들처럼, 그 행위자들은 원하든 원치 않든 자기에게 맡겨진 배역을 말하고 행하도록 어떤 내적 강제에 떠밀리는 듯하다. 이 무자각한 연희 방식이야말로, 이 개수改修의 시대에 화석이 된 존속물을 가짜 재현물과 구별해주는 참된 표지標識이다.\n\n그 작품은 널리 알려진 「성聖 게오르기우스」 극이었고, 무대 뒤에 있는 이들은 누구나 준비를 거들었으니, 각 집안의 여자들도 그러했다. 누이들과 연인들의 협력 없이는 의상이 실패하기 십상이었으나, 다른 한편 이런 부류의 도움에도 결점이 없지는 않았다. 처녀들은 갑옷을 도안하고 꾸미는 데 도무지 전통을 존중하도록 만들 수가 없었으니, 그들은 제 취향에 드는 곳이면 어디든 비단과 벨벳 고리며 매듭을 다는 것을 고집했다. 목가리개, 겨드랑이 받이, 투구, 흉갑, 손등 보호대, 소매—이 여인네들의 눈에는 죄다 한가지로, 펄럭이는 색색 조각을 꿰매 붙일 만한 쓸 만한 자리였다.\n\n기독교 진영에서 싸우는 조에게 연인이 있고, 이슬람 진영에서 싸우는 짐에게도 마찬가지로 연인이 있을 수 있었다. 의상을 만드는 동안 조의 연인은 짐의 연인이 면갑의 리본—얼굴 앞에 늘어뜨리는, 반 인치쯤 너비의 색색 띠로 으레 만들어지는 그 막대들도 대개 그 비단으로 되어 있었으니—에 더하여, 제 연인의 겉옷 자락에 화려한 비단 가리비 무늬를 달고 있다는 것을 알게 되곤 했다. 그러면 조의 연인은 곧장 문제의 그 가장자리 가리비 무늬에 화려한 비단을 붙이고, 한술 더 떠 어깨받이에 리본 술까지 달았다. 그러면 짐의 연인도 지지 않으려고 사방에 매듭과 장미꽃 매듭을 달아댔다.\n\n그 결과 끝내 기독교 군대의 용감한 병사는 차림새의 어떤 특징으로도 터키 기사와 구별되지 않았고, 더 고약하게는, 무심히 보면 성 게오르기우스 자신마저도 그의 철천지원수인 사라센으로 오인될 지경이었다. 가면극 배우들 자신은 이런 인물의 혼동을 속으로는 안타까워하면서도, 자기네가 그토록 크게 덕을 보는 그 도움 주는 이들의 비위를 거스를 형편이 못 되었으니, 그 변형들은 그대로 두어졌다.\n\n물론 이런 획일화 경향에도 한계는 있었다. 의원醫員 곧 의사는 그 인물됨을 고스란히 지켰으니—그의 더 짙은 의복, 독특한 모자, 겨드랑이에 둘러맨 약병은 결코 오인될 수 없었다. 또 관습적인 인물인 산타클로스도 마찬가지였으니, 거대한 곤봉을 든 더 나이 든 사내로, 교구에서 교구로 이어지는 긴 밤길에 전체의 보호자 노릇을 하며 그 패를 따라다녔고, 돈주머니를 지니는 이였다.\n\n연습 시각인 일곱 시가 돌아왔고, 얼마 안 가 유스테이샤는 땔감 창고에서 목소리들을 들을 수 있었다. 인간 삶의 음울함에 대한 변치 않는 자신의 감각을 조금이나마 흩어버리려고, 그녀는 \"린헤이\" 곧 비스듬히 기댄 헛간으로 갔으니, 그것은 그들 거처의 뿌리채소 저장고를 이루며 땔감 창고에 붙어 있었다. 여기에는 흙벽에 난 작고 거친 구멍이 하나 있었으니, 본디 비둘기를 위해 낸 것이라, 그것을 통해 옆 헛간의 안이 들여다보였다. 지금 거기서 불빛이 새어 나오고 있었고, 유스테이샤는 그 장면을 들여다보려고 걸상 위에 올라섰다.\n\n땔감 창고 안 선반 위에는 키 큰 골풀 양초 세 개가 서 있었고, 그 불빛으로 일고여덟 명의 소년들이 극을 완벽하게 익히려 애쓰며 이리저리 행진하고, 큰소리로 떠들고, 서로를 헷갈리게 하고 있었다. 가시금작화와 잔디를 베는 험프리와 샘이 거기서 구경하고 있었고, 티머시 페어웨이도 그러했으니, 그는 벽에 기대어 기억을 더듬어 소년들에게 대사를 일러주며, 정해진 대사들 사이에, 자기와 다른 이들이 지금 이 소년들이 그러하듯 에그던 가면극 배우로 뽑혀 있던 더 나았던 시절에 관한 논평과 일화를 끼워 넣었다.\n\n\"음, 너희는 앞으로 더 잘할 것도 없게 다 익혔구나.\" 그가 말했다. \"하기야 그런 가면극이 우리 시절엔 통하지도 않았겠지만 말이다. 사라센 역의 해리는 좀 더 으스대며 걸어야 하고, 존은 속이 다 빠지도록 고함칠 것까지는 없어. 그 정도만 빼면 그럭저럭 괜찮겠다. 옷은 다 준비됐냐?\"\n\n\"월요일까진 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"첫 나들이가 월요일 밤이겠지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"네. 예오브라이트 부인 댁에서요.\"\n\n\"오, 예오브라이트 부인 댁이라. 그 사람이 무엇 때문에 너희를 보고 싶어 한다더냐? 중년 여자라면 가면극에 물렸을 법한데.\"\n\n\"좀 모임을 마련하셨대요, 아드님 클림이 오랜만에 처음 집에 와서 쇠는 크리스마스라서요.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 그렇고말고—그 사람 모임! 나도 갈 거다. 정말이지 깜빡 잊을 뻔했구나.\"\n\n유스테이샤의 얼굴이 시들해졌다. 예오브라이트네에서 모임이 있을 예정이었으니, 그녀는 당연히 그것과 아무 상관이 없었다. 그녀는 그런 온갖 지역 모임에 낯선 이였고, 늘 그런 것들을 자기 영역에 거의 속하지 않는 것으로 여겨왔다. 그러나 만약 그녀가 가게 된다면, 그 영향이 여름 햇살처럼 그녀에게 스며들고 있는 그 사내를 볼 기회가 얼마나 주어질 것인가! 그 영향을 키우는 것은 탐나는 흥분이었고, 그것을 떨쳐내는 것은 평온을 되찾는 길일지도 모르며, 그것을 있는 그대로 두는 것은 애간장을 태우는 일이었다.\n\n소년들과 사내들이 그곳을 떠날 채비를 했고, 유스테이샤는 자기 난롯가로 돌아갔다. 그녀는 생각에 잠겨 있었으나 오래가지는 않았다. 몇 분 뒤, 그곳을 쓰게 해달라고 청하러 왔던 소년 찰리가 부엌 열쇠를 가지고 돌아왔다. 유스테이샤는 그 소리를 듣고, 복도로 난 문을 열며 말했다. \"찰리, 이리 오너라.\"\n\n소년은 놀랐다. 그는 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수 없이 앞방으로 들어섰으니, 그도 많은 이들처럼 이 처녀의 얼굴과 자태가 지닌 힘을 느껴왔던 것이다.\n\n그녀는 난롯가의 한 자리를 가리켰고, 자신은 굴뚝 모퉁이의 다른 편으로 들어가 앉았다. 소년을 안으로 부른 데에 무슨 동기가 있었든 그것이 곧 드러나리라는 것을 그녀의 얼굴에서 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"넌 어느 역을 맡았니, 찰리—터키 기사 아니냐?\" 미녀가 난로의 연기 너머 맞은편의 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"네, 아가씨, 터키 기사예요.\" 그가 머뭇거리며 대답했다.\n\n\"네 배역은 기냐?\"\n\n\"대사가 아홉 개쯤 돼요.\"\n\n\"나한테 외워줄 수 있겠니? 그렇다면 들어보고 싶구나.\"\n\n소년은 시뻘겋게 단 잔디 불을 보고 미소 지으며 시작했다—\n\n\"여기 내가 오노라, 터키 기사,\n터키 땅에서 싸움을 배운 몸이로다,\"\n\n그렇게 모든 장면을 거쳐 성 게오르기우스의 손에 쓰러지는 그의 마지막 파국에 이르기까지 그 대사를 죽 이어갔다.\n\n유스테이샤는 전에도 이따금 그 배역을 읊는 것을 들은 적이 있었다. 소년이 끝내자 그녀는 토씨 하나 다르지 않은 똑같은 말로 시작하여, 멈춤도 어긋남도 없이 줄줄 읊어, 그녀 역시 끝에 다다랐다. 같은 것이었으나, 어찌 그리 달랐던가. 형식은 같았으되, 그것은 페루지노를 본뜬 라파엘로처럼 더해진 부드러움과 완성도를 지녔으니, 원래의 주제를 충실히 재현하면서도 원작의 기예를 온전히 멀찍이 따돌리는 것이었다.\n\n찰리의 두 눈이 놀라움에 동그래졌다. \"와, 영리한 아가씨시네요!\" 그가 감탄하며 말했다. \"전 제 것 외우는 데 세 주가 걸렸는걸요.\"\n\n\"전에 들은 적이 있단다.\" 그녀가 조용히 말했다. \"자, 찰리, 날 기쁘게 해주려고 무슨 일이든 해주겠니?\"\n\n\"꽤 많은 걸 해드릴 수 있어요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"하룻밤 내가 네 역을 맡게 해주겠니?\"\n\n\"오, 아가씨! 하지만 아가씨의 여자 옷으로는—그럴 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"난 사내아이 옷을 구할 수 있어—적어도 가면극 의상 말고 더 필요한 건 다 말이지. 네 물건을 빌려주고, 월요일 밤 한두 시간 내가 네 자리를 대신하게 해주고, 내가 누구이며 무엇인지 절대 한마디도 발설하지 않게 하려면, 내가 너한테 뭘 주면 되겠니? 너야 물론 그날 밤 연희에서 빠지겠다고 둘러대고, 누군가—바이 양의 사촌이—너 대신 해줄 거라고 말해야 하겠지. 다른 가면극 배우들은 평생 나와 말을 섞은 적이 없으니 충분히 안전할 게다. 그리고 설령 안전하지 않다 해도 난 개의치 않아. 자, 네가 이걸 받아들이게 하려면 내가 뭘 줘야 하지? 반 크라운?\"\n\n소년은 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"오 실링?\"\n\n그는 다시 고개를 저었다. \"돈으론 안 돼요.\" 그가 장작 받침쇠의 쇠머리를 손바닥 오목한 곳으로 쓸며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 뭐가 되겠니, 찰리?\" 유스테이샤가 실망한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"오월제 기둥 춤 때 아가씨가 저한테 못 하게 하신 거 아시잖아요, 아가씨.\" 소년이 그녀를 보지 않은 채, 여전히 장작 받침쇠의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 우물거렸다.\n\n\"그래.\" 유스테이샤가 조금 더 도도하게 말했다. \"내 기억이 맞다면, 넌 원 안에서 나랑 손을 맞잡고 싶어 했지?\"\n\n\"그걸 반 시간만요, 그럼 받아들일게요, 아가씨.\"\n\n유스테이샤는 소년을 물끄러미 바라보았다. 그는 그녀보다 세 살 어렸으나, 보아하니 나이에 비해 숙된 데가 없지는 않았다. \"반 시간 동안 뭘?\" 그녀가 말했으니, 무엇인지 짐작은 하면서였다.\n\n\"아가씨 손을 제 손에 쥐는 걸요.\"\n\n그녀는 잠자코 있었다. \"십오 분으로 하자.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 유스테이샤 아가씨—그럴게요, 거기다 입 맞춰도 된다면요. 십오 분. 그리고 아무도 모르게 아가씨가 제 자리를 대신하게 하도록 제가 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다하겠다고 맹세할게요. 누가 아가씨 말투를 알아챌 수도 있지 않을까요, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"그럴 수 있지. 하지만 그럴 가능성을 줄이려고 입에 조약돌을 물고 있을 거야. 좋아. 네가 옷과 칼과 지팡이를 가져오는 대로 내 손을 잡게 해주마. 이제 더는 네가 필요 없구나.\"\n\n찰리는 떠났고, 유스테이샤는 삶에 점점 더 흥미를 느꼈다. 여기 할 일이 생겼고, 여기 만날 사람이 생겼으며, 그를 만날 매혹적으로 모험적인 방도가 생긴 것이다. \"아,\" 그녀는 속으로 말했다. \"살아갈 목적이 없는 것—그게 내 문제의 전부야!\"\n\n유스테이샤의 태도는 대개 졸음에 잠긴 듯한 것이었으니, 그녀의 정념은 활발하다기보다 묵직한 부류였다. 그러나 일단 일깨워지면 그녀는 그 순간만큼은 천성이 활달한 사람의 움직임과 다르지 않은 돌진을 감행하곤 했다.\n\n정체를 들킬 문제에 대해서는 그녀는 다소 무관심했다. 연희하는 소년들 자신에게는 알아보일 공산이 없었다. 모일 법한 손님들에 대해서는 그만큼 안전하지는 못했다. 그러나 따지고 보면 들킨다 해도 그리 끔찍한 일은 아니었다. 사실만 들킬 뿐, 그녀의 진짜 동기는 결코 들키지 않을 터였다. 그것은 즉각, 이미 그 방식이 별나다고 여겨지는 한 처녀의 한때의 변덕으로 치부될 것이었다. 더없이 진지한 이유로 하는 일을 가장 자연스럽게는 장난삼아 할 법한 그 일을 한다는 것은, 어쨌든 안전한 비밀이었다.\n\n이튿날 저녁 유스테이샤는 약속대로 땔감 창고 문 앞에 서서, 찰리가 의상을 가지고 올 어스름을 기다렸다. 할아버지가 오늘 밤은 집에 있었으니, 그녀는 공모자를 안으로 청할 수가 없었다.\n\n그는 흑인 위의 파리처럼 어둑한 황야의 능선에 나타나, 그 물건들을 지니고 와서는, 걸어오느라 숨이 차서 다가왔다.\n\n\"여기 물건들이요.\" 그가 그것들을 문지방에 내려놓으며 속삭였다. \"자, 이제, 유스테이샤 아가씨—\"\n\n\"값을 치러야지. 다 준비됐다. 난 약속은 꼭 지키는 사람이야.\"\n\n그녀는 문설주에 기대어 그에게 손을 내밀었다. 찰리는 형언할 수 없는 다정함으로 두 손에 그것을 받아 쥐었으니, 사로잡은 참새를 쥔 아이의 다정함이라고나 할까.\n\n\"아니, 장갑을 끼셨네요!\" 그가 못마땅한 투로 말했다.\n\n\"걸어 다녔거든.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만, 아가씨!\"\n\n\"음—공평치 못하긴 하지.\" 그녀는 장갑을 벗어 그에게 맨손을 내밀었다.\n\n그들은 더 이상의 말없이 분이 지나고 또 지나도록 함께 서서, 저마다 검어져가는 풍경을 바라보며, 저마다 제 생각에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 다 써버리지는 않을래요.\" 찰리가 그녀의 손을 어루만지며 육칠 분을 보낸 뒤 정성스럽게 말했다. \"남은 몇 분은 다음에 가져도 될까요?\"\n\n\"좋을 대로.\" 그녀가 조금도 감정 없이 말했다. \"하지만 한 주 안에 끝내야 해. 자, 이제 네게 바라는 건 한 가지뿐이야—내가 옷을 입는 동안 기다렸다가, 내가 배역을 제대로 하는지 봐주는 거야. 하지만 먼저 안을 좀 봐야겠다.\"\n\n그녀는 잠깐 사라졌다가 안으로 들어갔다. 할아버지는 의자에서 무사히 잠들어 있었다. \"자, 그럼.\" 그녀가 돌아와서 말했다. \"정원을 따라 조금 내려가 있어. 준비되면 부를게.\"\n\n찰리는 걸어가 기다렸고, 이윽고 나직한 휘파람 소리를 들었다. 그는 땔감 창고 문으로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"휘파람 부셨어요, 바이 양?\"\n\n\"그래, 들어와.\" 유스테이샤의 목소리가 안쪽 어딘가에서 그에게 닿았다. \"문을 닫기 전엔 불을 켜선 안 돼, 안 그러면 새어 나가는 게 보일지도 몰라. 더듬어서 가로질러 갈 수 있겠거든, 모자를 세탁장으로 통하는 구멍에 밀어 넣어둬.\"\n\n찰리는 시키는 대로 했고, 그녀가 불을 켜자 그 모습이 드러났으니, 성별이 바뀌고, 색채로 눈부시고, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 무장한 모습이었다. 어쩌면 그녀는 찰리의 힘찬 눈길 아래 조금 움츠러들었을지도 모르나, 남장에 대한 부끄러움이 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐는지 어떤지는, 가면극 의상에서 으레 얼굴을 가리던, 중세 투구의 막대 면갑을 본뜬 리본 띠들 때문에 알아볼 수 없었다.\n\n\"꽤 잘 맞네.\" 그녀가 흰 작업바지를 내려다보며 말했다. \"튜닉인지 뭔지 하는 이것 소매가 긴 것만 빼면 말이야. 작업바지 아랫단은 안으로 접어 올리면 되고. 자, 잘 봐.\"\n\n이어 유스테이샤는 대사를 읊어나갔으니, 위협하는 구절에서는 정통 가면극 방식대로 칼을 지팡이나 창에 부딪치고, 이리저리 으스대며 걸었다. 찰리는 가장 부드러운 종류의 비평으로 자신의 감탄에 양념을 더했으니, 유스테이샤의 손길이 아직 그에게 남아 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"자, 이제 다른 애들한테 둘러댈 핑계 말인데.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"예오브라이트 부인 댁에 가기 전에 너희는 어디서 모이니?\"\n\n\"여기서 모일까 했어요, 아가씨, 아가씨가 반대하지 않으신다면요. 아홉 시까지 거기 가려고 여덟 시에요.\"\n\n\"그래. 음, 넌 물론 나타나선 안 돼. 내가 채비를 다 갖추고 오 분쯤 늦게 들어가서, 네가 못 온다고 말할게. 핑계를 진짜처럼 보이게 하려면, 내가 너를 어딘가로 심부름 보내는 게 가장 좋은 수일 것 같다고 정했어. 우리 황야 조랑말 두 마리가 풀밭으로 곧잘 흘러 들어가니, 내일 저녁 네가 가서 그놈들이 거기로 갔는지 보면 돼. 나머지는 내가 처리하마. 자, 이제 가도 좋아.\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨. 하지만 괜찮으시다면 받기로 한 걸 일 분만 더 받을까 해요.\"\n\n유스테이샤는 아까처럼 그에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"일 분이야.\" 그녀가 말하고는, 칠팔 분에 이를 때까지 세어 나갔다. 그러고는 손과 몸을 몇 피트 떨어진 거리로 물리고, 예전의 위엄을 어느 정도 되찾았다. 계약이 완료되자, 그녀는 둘 사이에 벽처럼 뚫고 들 수 없는 장벽을 세웠다.\n\n\"자, 다 가버렸네요. 다는 가질 생각이 아니었는데.\" 그가 한숨지으며 말했다.\n\n\"넉넉히 받았잖니.\" 그녀가 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 아가씨. 그럼, 다 끝났으니, 이제 집으로 가야겠어요.\"\n\n이튿날 저녁 가면극 배우들은 같은 자리에 모여, 터키 기사의 등장을 기다리고 있었다.\n\n\"콰이어트 우먼 시계로 여덟 시 이십 분인데, 찰리가 안 왔네.\"\n\n\"블룸스엔드 기준으론 여덟 시 십 분이야.\"\n\n\"그랜퍼 캔틀 시계로는 십 분 전이고.\"\n\n\"그리고 선장님 시계로는 오 분 지났어.\"\n\n에그던에는 하루의 절대적인 시각이라는 것이 없었다. 어느 순간의 시간이란 여러 마을이 저마다 내세우는 다양한 교리의 집합이었으니, 그중 어떤 것은 본래 한 뿌리에서 자라났다가 분파로 갈라진 것이었고, 어떤 것은 처음부터 이질적인 것이었다. 서쪽 에그던은 블룸스엔드 시간을 믿었고, 동쪽 에그던은 콰이어트 우먼 여인숙의 시간을 믿었다. 그랜퍼 캔틀의 시계는 지난 세월 추종자를 많이 거느렸으나, 그가 늙어가면서 그 신앙도 흔들렸다. 그리하여 흩어진 곳에서 이리로 모인 가면극 배우들은 저마다 이르고 늦음에 관한 제 나름의 교의를 지니고 왔으니, 그들은 타협 삼아 좀 더 기다렸다.\n\n유스테이샤는 구멍을 통해 그 모임을 지켜보고 있었고, 이제 들어갈 적기適期임을 알아채고는, \"린헤이\"에서 나와 대담하게 땔감 창고 문의 빗장 끈을 잡아당겼다. 할아버지는 콰이어트 우먼에 안전하게 가 있었다.\n\n\"드디어 찰리 왔다! 어찌 이리 늦었니, 찰리.\"\n\n\"찰리가 아니에요.\" 면갑 안에서 터키 기사가 말했다. \"바이 양의 사촌인데, 호기심에 찰리 자리를 대신하러 왔어요. 그 애는 풀밭으로 들어간 황야 조랑말들을 찾으러 가야 해서, 오늘 밤 다시 여기 못 온다는 걸 알기에 제가 그 애 자리를 대신하기로 했지요. 저도 그 애만큼 배역을 잘 알아요.\"\n\n그녀의 우아한 걸음걸이, 단아한 자태, 그리고 전반적으로 위엄 있는 태도는, 새로 온 이가 제 배역에 완벽하기만 하다면 이 교체로 득을 보았다는 쪽으로 가면극 배우들의 견해를 이끌었다.\n\n\"상관없어—네가 너무 어리지만 않다면 말이야.\" 성 게오르기우스가 말했다. 유스테이샤의 목소리가 찰리보다 다소 더 앳되고 피리 소리 같았던 것이다.\n\n\"한마디도 빠짐없이 다 안다니까.\" 유스테이샤가 단호하게 말했다. 그녀를 의기양양하게 헤쳐 나가게 해줄 데 필요한 것이라곤 기세뿐이었으니, 그녀는 필요한 만큼 그것을 취했다. \"어서들 한번 맞춰보자. 너희 중 누구든 내게서 틀린 데를 찾아내봐.\"\n\n극은 서둘러 연습되었고, 그리하여 다른 가면극 배우들은 새 기사에게 만족했다. 그들은 여덟 시 반에 양초들을 끄고, 블룸스엔드의 예오브라이트 부인 댁을 향해 황야로 나섰다.\n\n그날 밤은 무서리가 살짝 내렸고, 달은 반 넘게 차지는 않았으나, 가면극 패의 환상적인 형상들 위로 생기 있고 매혹적인 밝음을 던졌으니, 그들의 깃털과 리본은 걸을 때 가을 낙엽처럼 바스락거렸다. 그들의 길은 이제 레인배로 너머가 아니라, 그 오래된 봉우리를 동쪽으로 조금 비켜둔 골짜기를 따라 내려가는 길이었다. 골짜기 바닥은 십 야드쯤 너비로 푸르렀고, 풀잎 위에 맺힌 서리의 빛나는 면들은 자기들이 둘러싼 이들의 그림자와 함께 따라 움직이는 듯했다. 좌우의 가시금작화와 히스 덤불 더미는 여느 때처럼 어두웠으니, 한낱 반달은 그토록 새까만 생김새를 은빛으로 물들일 힘이 없었다.\n\n반 시간을 걷고 이야기한 끝에 그들은 골짜기에서 풀 띠가 넓어져 집 앞쪽으로 이어지는 지점에 다다랐다. 그곳을 보자, 소년들과 걷는 동안 몇 차례 스쳐가는 의구심을 느꼈던 유스테이샤는, 이 모험에 나선 것을 다시금 기뻐했다. 그녀는 어쩌면 가장 끔찍한 억압에서 자신의 영혼을 건져낼 힘을 지녔을지도 모르는 한 사내를 보러 나온 것이었다. 와일디브가 뭐란 말인가? 흥미롭긴 했으나, 모자랐다. 어쩌면 오늘 밤 그녀는 흡족한 영웅을 보게 될지도 몰랐다.\n\n집 앞쪽으로 다가가면서 가면극 배우들은 안에서 음악과 춤이 활기차게 한창임을 알아차렸다. 이따금 이런 때 연주되는 주된 관악기인 세르팡에서 나오는 길고 낮은 한 음이, 가느다란 고음부보다 더 멀리 황야 속으로 나아가 그들의 귀에만 닿았고, 그다음에는 여느 때보다 큰 춤꾼의 발 구르는 소리가 같은 식으로 다가왔다. 더 가까이 다가가자 이 조각난 소리들은 한데 이어 맞춰져, 「낸시의 변덕」이라는 곡의 두드러진 대목들임이 드러났다.\n\n물론 그가 거기 있었다. 그가 함께 춤추는 여자는 누구일까? 어쩌면 어떤 알지 못할 여자, 교양에서 자기보다 훨씬 못한 여자가, 바로 이 순간 가장 교묘한 유혹으로 그의 운명을 봉인하고 있는지도 몰랐다. 한 사내와 춤춘다는 것은 일 년치 절제된 불길을 한 시간의 조각에다 그에게 집중시키는 일이다. 친분도 없이 구애로 건너뛰고, 구애도 없이 결혼으로 건너뛰는 것은, 오직 이 왕도王道를 밟는 자들에게만 허락된 단계의 생략이다. 그녀는 그들 모두를 예리하게 관찰하여 그의 마음이 어디에 놓여 있는지 알아낼 셈이었다.\n\n이 진취적인 숙녀는 흰 말뚝 울타리의 문을 지나 가면극 패를 따라 들어가, 열린 현관 앞에 섰다. 집은 묵직한 이엉으로 뒤덮여 위쪽 창들 사이로 그것이 늘어져 있었고, 달빛이 곧장 어리는 정면은 본디 흰색이었으나, 이제는 거대한 피라칸타가 그 대부분을 어둑하게 가리고 있었다.\n\n춤이 문 표면 바로 안쪽에서, 사이에 끼인 방 하나 없이 벌어지고 있다는 것이 단박에 분명해졌다. 치맛자락과 팔꿈치가 스치는 소리, 이따금 어깨가 부딪치는 소리가 바로 그 문짝에 와 닿는 것이 들렸다. 유스테이샤는 그곳에서 이 마일 안에 살면서도 이 별스럽게 오래된 거처의 내부를 본 적이 없었다. 바이 선장과 예오브라이트네 사이에는 그리 깊은 친분이 있었던 적이 없었으니, 전자는 낯선 이로 와서 예오브라이트 부인 남편이 죽기 얼마 전에 미스토버 냅의 오래 비어 있던 집을 사들였고, 그 일과 그녀 아들의 떠남으로, 싹터 있던 친분이라 할 만한 것마저 아주 끊겨버린 까닭이었다.\n\n\"그럼 문 안쪽에 통로가 없는 거니?\" 현관 안에 서서 유스테이샤가 물었다.\n\n\"없어요.\" 사라센 역을 맡은 소년이 말했다. \"문은 곧장 앞쪽 거실로 열려요, 흥겨운 자리가 벌어지고 있는 데로요.\"\n\n\"그러니까 춤을 멈추게 하지 않고는 문을 열 수 없다는 거지.\"\n\n\"바로 그거예요. 우린 저들이 끝낼 때까지 여기서 기다려야 해요, 어두워지면 늘 뒷문에 빗장을 지르거든요.\"\n\n\"오래 안 걸릴 거다.\" 산타클로스가 말했다.\n\n그러나 이 단언은 실제 사태로 거의 뒷받침되지 못했다. 다시금 악기들이 곡을 끝냈고, 다시금 마치 첫 가락인 양 그만큼의 열기와 애상으로 다시 시작했다. 이제 그 곡은 특별한 처음도, 중간도, 끝도 없는 것이었으니, 어쩌면 영감 받은 바이올린 연주자의 상상에 떼 지어 몰려드는 온갖 춤곡 가운데 끝없음의 관념을 가장 잘 전하는 그 유명한 「악마의 꿈」이었다. 음표들의 광란으로 지펴진 인간 동작의 광란은, 빙글 도는 회전이 여느 때보다 빠를 때마다 발끝과 발꿈치가 문에 부딪치는 이따금의 소리로, 달빛 아래 이 바깥의 사람들에게 어림짐작될 수 있었다.\n\n처음 오 분간의 엿듣기는 가면극 배우들에게 충분히 흥미로웠다. 그 오 분이 십 분으로 늘어났고, 그것이 다시 십오 분으로 늘어났으나, 그 흥겨운\n「꿈이지.」 문에 부딪치는 소리, 웃음소리, 발 구르는 소리는 여전히 기운찼고, 바깥에 있는 즐거움은 상당히 줄어들었다.\n\n「요브라이트 부인은 왜 이런 식의 잔치를 여는 거예요?」 유스테이샤가 그렇게 떠들썩한 흥겨움을 듣고 다소 놀라며 물었다.\n\n「부인의 제일가는 응접실 잔치는 아니야. 선을 긋지 않고 수수한 이웃들과 일꾼들을 불러서 그저 든든한 저녁이나 그런 걸 대접하는 거지. 아들이랑 부인이 직접 사람들 시중을 들고.」\n\n「알겠어요.」 유스테이샤가 말했다.\n\n「내 생각엔 이게 마지막 가락인 것 같아.」 성 게오르기우스가 문짝에 귀를 대고 말했다. 「젊은 남녀 한 쌍이 막 이쪽 모퉁이로 빙글 돌아왔는데, 사내가 여자한테 이러는군, ‘아, 안타깝구나, 우리한텐 이번이 끝이야, 내 사랑.’」\n\n「하느님 감사합니다.」 터키 기사가 발을 구르며, 가면극 배우들이 저마다 들고 다니는 관습적인 창을 벽에서 집어 들며 말했다. 그녀의 장화는 젊은 사내들 것보다 얇아서, 서리에 발이 젖어 차가워졌다.\n\n「맙소사, 우리한테는 십 분이 더 남았어.」 용맹한 병사가 가락이 멈추지 않고 다른 곡으로 옮겨 가는 동안 열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보며 말했다. 「캔틀 영감이 이쪽 모퉁이에 서서 자기 차례를 기다리고 있군.」\n\n「오래 걸리진 않아, 여섯 명이 추는 릴 춤이니까.」 의사가 말했다.\n\n「춤이고 뭐고 그냥 들어가면 어때? 사람들이 우릴 부른 거잖아.」 사라센이 말했다.\n\n「절대 안 돼.」 유스테이샤가 몸을 덥히려고 문에서 대문까지 활기차게 오가며 권위 있게 말했다. 「우리가 한복판으로 뛰어들어 춤을 멈추게 할 거고, 그건 예의에 어긋나는 일이야.」\n\n「저 친구는 우리보다 학교를 좀 더 다녔다고 자기가 대단한 줄 알아.」 의사가 말했다.\n\n「뒈져 버려라!」 유스테이샤가 말했다.\n\n서너 명이 속닥속닥 이야기를 나누더니, 한 사람이 그녀를 돌아보았다.\n\n「한 가지만 말해 줄래?」 그가 무뚝뚝하지 않게 말했다. 「너 바이 양 아니야? 우린 그런 것 같아.」\n\n「네 맘대로 생각해.」 유스테이샤가 천천히 말했다. 「하지만 명예로운 사내들은 숙녀 일로 고자질을 하진 않지.」\n\n「아무 말도 안 할게, 아가씨. 명예를 걸고.」\n\n「고마워.」 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n이 순간 바이올린들이 끽 소리와 함께 마무리되었고, 뱀나팔이 지붕을 들어 올릴 뻔한 마지막 음을 토해 냈다. 안쪽이 비교적 조용해지자 가면극 배우들은 춤추던 이들이 자리에 앉았다고 판단했고, 크리스마스 영감이 앞으로 나아가 빗장을 들어 올리고 문 안으로 머리를 들이밀었다.\n\n「아, 가면극이다, 가면극!」 여러 손님이 동시에 외쳤다. 「가면극 배우들을 위해 자리를 비워라.」\n\n곱사등이 크리스마스 영감은 그러고 나서 자신의 거대한 곤봉을 휘두르며 완전히 들어와, 본격적인 배우들을 위해 무대를 대강 비우는 한편, 자기가 환영받든 안 받든 왔노라고 좌중에게 경쾌한 운문으로 알리며 다음과 같은 말로 연설을 맺었다.\n\n「자리를 내라, 자리를 내라, 용감한 친구들이여, 우리에게 운율 읊을 공간을 다오. 우리는 성 게오르기우스의 극을 보여 주러 왔노라, 바로 이 크리스마스 때에.」\n\n이제 손님들이 방 한쪽 끝에 자리를 잡았고, 바이올린 연주자는 줄을 손보고 있었으며, 뱀나팔 연주자는 마우스피스를 비우고 있었고, 극이 시작되었다. 바깥에 있던 이들 가운데 첫째로 용맹한 병사가 성 게오르기우스를 위하여 등장했으니—\n\n「여기 내가 왔노라, 용맹한 병사라. 슬래셔가 내 이름이니라.」\n\n이렇게 이어졌다. 이 대사는 이교도를 향한 도전으로 끝났고, 그 끝에서 유스테이샤가 터키 기사로 등장하는 것이 그녀의 임무였다. 그녀는 아직 무대에 나오지 않은 나머지 이들과 함께 그때까지 현관 아래로 흘러드는 달빛 속에 머물러 있었다. 별다른 노력이나 망설임 없이 그녀가 들어오며 시작했다—\n\n「여기 내가 왔노라, 터키 기사라, 터키 땅에서 싸움을 배운 자이니라. 나는 이 사내와 용감하고 대담하게 싸우리니, 그자의 피가 뜨겁다면 차갑게 식혀 주리라!」\n\n대사를 읊는 동안 유스테이샤는 머리를 꼿꼿이 세우고 최대한 거칠게 말했으며, 눈에 띄지 않으리라는 상당한 확신을 느꼈다. 그러나 정체를 들키지 않으려면 자기 역할에 집중해야 했던 점, 무대의 생소함, 촛불의 광채, 그리고 얼굴을 가린 리본 달린 면갑이 시야에 일으킨 혼란스러운 효과 때문에, 그녀는 구경꾼으로 누가 와 있는지 도무지 알아볼 수가 없었다. 촛불을 받친 탁자 저편으로 그녀는 얼굴들을 희미하게 분간할 수 있었으나, 그게 전부였다.\n\n한편 용맹한 병사 역의 짐 스타크스가 앞으로 나와, 터키인을 노려보며 대답했다—\n\n「그렇다면 네가 그 터키 기사라면, 칼을 뽑아라, 그리고 싸우자!」\n\n그리고 그들은 싸웠으니, 결투의 결말은 용맹한 병사가 유스테이샤의 터무니없이 부실한 일격에 쓰러져 죽는 것이었는데, 짐은 진정한 연기 예술에 대한 열의로 어깨가 빠질 만큼 세게 통나무처럼 돌바닥에 쾅 넘어졌다. 그러고 나서 터키 기사가 다소 너무 희미하게 전달한 말을 좀 더 늘어놓고, 성 게오르기우스와 그 무리 전부와도 싸우겠노라 외치고 나자, 성 게오르기우스 본인이 잘 알려진 화려한 어구와 함께 당당하게 등장했다—\n\n「여기 내가 왔노라, 성 게오르기우스, 용맹한 사내라, 맨 칼을 들고 손에 창을 쥐었노라, 용을 무찔러 도살하였고, 그로써 이집트 왕의 딸 어여쁜 사브라를 얻었노라. 어느 인간이 감히 맞설 수 있겠는가, 손에 칼을 쥔 내 앞에?」\n\n이자가 바로 유스테이샤를 처음으로 알아본 젊은이였으니, 이제 그녀가 터키인으로서 적절한 반항으로 응수하고 곧장 결투를 시작하자, 그 젊은 친구는 가능한 한 부드럽게 칼을 쓰려고 각별히 신경을 썼다. 상처를 입은 기사는 지시에 따라 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 쓰러졌다. 이제 의사가 등장해, 자기가 들고 다니던 병에서 한 모금을 먹여 기사를 회복시켰고, 다시 싸움이 재개되었으니, 터키인은 차츰차츰 가라앉아 마침내 완전히 제압당했고—이 유서 깊은 극에서도 오늘날 그렇다고들 하는 것처럼 좀처럼 쉽게 죽지 않았다.\n\n이렇게 땅으로 차츰 가라앉는 것이, 사실 유스테이샤가 터키 기사 역이 가장 짧지는 않으나 자기에게 가장 잘 맞으리라 여겼던 한 가지 이유였다. 다른 싸우는 인물들의 최후처럼 똑바로 선 자세에서 수평으로 곧장 넘어지는 것은 처녀에게 우아하거나 점잖은 역할이 아니었다. 그러나 터키인처럼 끈질기게 기울어 가며 죽는 것은 쉬웠다.\n\n이제 유스테이샤는 쓰러진 자들 축에 끼었으나, 바닥에 누운 것은 아니었으니, 시계 케이스에 기댄 비스듬한 자세로 가라앉는 데 용케 성공해서 머리를 충분히 높이 들고 있었다. 극은 성 게오르기우스, 사라센, 의사, 크리스마스 영감 사이에서 진행되었고, 더 이상 할 일이 없어진 유스테이샤는 처음으로 주변 광경을 살피고 자신을 이곳으로 이끈 그 형상을 찾을 여유를 얻었다.\n\n방은 춤추기에 알맞도록 배치되어, 커다란 떡갈나무 탁자가 벽난로 앞 흉벽처럼 설 때까지 뒤로 밀려나 있었다. 양 끝과 뒤편과 굴뚝 모퉁이에 손님들이 모여 있었는데, 그중 다수는 얼굴이 달아오른 채 헐떡이고 있었으며, 유스테이샤는 그들 가운데 황야 너머에서 온 잘사는 사람 몇몇을 대충 알아보았다. 예상했던 대로 토머신은 보이지 않았고, 유스테이샤는 그들이 바깥에 있을 때 위층 창문에서 불빛이 새어 나왔던 일을 떠올렸으니—아마 토머신 방의 창문이었으리라. 굴뚝 입구 안쪽 자리에서 코와 턱과 손과 무릎과 발끝이 튀어나와 있었는데, 그 부위들이 모여 캔틀 영감이라는 한 인물을 이루고 있음을 그녀는 알아챘으니, 그는 요브라이트 부인의 정원 일을 이따금 거드는 사람이었고 그래서 초대받은 이들 중 하나였다. 그 앞에서 토탄 화산에서 연기가 솟아올라 굴뚝 고리의 홈을 휘감고 소금 통에 부딪쳤다가 베이컨 덩어리들 사이로 사라졌다.\n\n방의 다른 부분이 곧 그녀의 시선을 사로잡았다. 굴뚝 반대편에는 긴 등받이 의자가 서 있었으니, 이는 강한 바람이 아니고서는 연기를 위로 끌어올리지 못할 만큼 입을 크게 벌린 불에 반드시 딸려야 하는 보완물이었다. 그것은 옛날식 동굴 같은 벽난로의 화덕에게, 노출된 시골 영지에게 동쪽 나무 띠가 그러하고 정원에게 북쪽 담이 그러한 것과 같은 존재였다. 등받이 의자 바깥에서는 촛불이 흘러내리고 머리카락 다발이 흔들리며 젊은 여자들이 떨고 노인들이 재채기를 한다. 안쪽은 낙원이다. 바람의 기미 하나 공기를 어지럽히지 않고, 앉은 이들의 등은 그들의 얼굴만큼이나 따뜻하며, 안락한 온기에 이끌려 그 자리에 앉은 이들에게서 노래와 옛이야기가 흘러나오니, 마치 온실 틀 속 멜론 줄기에서 열매가 맺히듯 한다.\n\n그러나 유스테이샤가 관심을 둔 것은 등받이 의자에 앉은 이들이 아니었다. 윗부분의 짙게 그을린 나무를 배경으로 한 얼굴 하나가 유독 또렷이 드러났다. 등받이 의자 바깥쪽 끝에 기대선 그 임자는 클레멘트 요브라이트, 이곳에서 불리는 이름으로는 클림이었으니, 그녀는 다른 누구일 리 없음을 알았다. 그 광경은 렘브란트의 가장 강렬한 화법으로 그려진 가로세로 두 자 남짓한 면적을 이루었다. 빈둥거리는 자의 모습에 깃든 기묘한 힘은, 그의 온몸이 보이는데도 보는 이의 눈에는 오직 그의 얼굴만이 인식된다는 사실에 있었다.\n\n중년에게 그 얼굴은 젊은이의 것이었으나, 청년이라면 미숙함이라는 말을 굳이 붙일 필요를 거의 못 느꼈을 것이다. 그러나 그것은 실로, 나이로 따진 세월의 길이보다 축적된 경험의 양을 더 많이 전하는 그런 얼굴 가운데 하나였다. 세월의 수가 야렛이며 마할랄렐이며 그 밖의 대홍수 이전 사람들을 적절히 요약했을지는 모르나, 현대인의 나이는 그 이력의 강렬함으로 재야 한다.\n\n그 얼굴은 잘 빚어진, 심지어 빼어나게 잘 빚어진 얼굴이었다. 그러나 그 안의 정신은 그것을 한낱 빈 서판으로 쓰기 시작해, 자신의 특질이 발현되는 대로 거기에 새기고 있었다. 여기 보이는 아름다움은 머지않아 그 기생충인 사고思考에게 무자비하게 잠식당할 터였으니, 그 사고는 해칠 것이 아무것도 없는 더 평범한 외모에 깃들어도 무방했으리라. 하늘이 요브라이트를 명상이라는 닳게 하는 습관에서 지켜 주었더라면 사람들은 「잘생긴 사내」라고 했을 것이다. 그의 뇌가 더 날카로운 윤곽 아래 펼쳐졌더라면 사람들은 「사색하는 사내」라고 했을 것이다. 그러나 내면의 치열함이 외면의 균형을 갉아먹고 있었기에, 사람들은 그의 용모를 별나다고 평했다.\n\n그리하여 그를 바라보기 시작한 사람들은 끝내 그를 정독하게 되었다. 그의 용모에는 읽어 낼 수 있는 의미들이 겹겹이 덧씌워져 있었다. 사고에 시달린 것은 아니었으되 그에게는 자기 주변에 대한 지각에서 비롯된 어떤 흔적들이 있었으니, 평온한 학창 시절이 끝나고 이어지는 사오 년간의 분투가 끝날 무렵의 사내들에게서 드물지 않게 발견되는 그런 것이었다. 그는 사고가 육신의 병임을 이미 보여 주고 있었고, 이상적인 육체의 아름다움은 정서의 발달이며 세상사 얽힘에 대한 온전한 인식과 양립할 수 없음을 간접으로 입증하고 있었다. 정신의 광휘는 생명의 기름을 먹여야만 하는 법이니, 이미 육체에 그 기름이 필요한데도 그러하며, 하나의 공급원에 두 가지 요구가 걸린 가련한 광경이 바로 여기서 막 드러나고 있었다.\n\n어떤 사내들 앞에 섰을 때 철학자는 사색가들이 한낱 썩어 없어질 조직에 불과함을 안타까워하고, 예술가는 썩어 없어질 조직이 사색을 해야만 함을 안타까워한다. 그리하여 정신과 육체의 서로를 파괴하는 상호 의존을 저마다의 관점에서 개탄하는 것이, 요브라이트를 비판적으로 살피는 이들에게는 본능적이었으리라.\n\n그의 표정으로 말하자면, 그것은 바깥에서 내리누르는 우울에 맞서 싸우면서도 완전히 이겨 내지는 못하는 타고난 쾌활함이었다. 그 표정은 고립을 암시했으나, 그 이상의 무언가를 드러내기도 했다. 밝은 천성을 지닌 이들에게 흔히 그러하듯, 하루살이 같은 인간의 시신屍身 안에 치욕스럽게 사슬로 묶인 신성이 광선처럼 그에게서 뿜어 나왔다.\n\n유스테이샤에게 미친 효과는 뚜렷했다. 그녀가 미리 다다라 있던 그 비상한 흥분의 정도라면, 사실 더없이 평범한 사내에게도 영향을 받았을 것이다. 그녀는 요브라이트의 존재에 마음이 어지러웠다.\n\n극의 나머지가 끝났다—사라센의 머리가 잘렸고, 성 게오르기우스가 승자로 섰다. 누구도 거기에 대해 논평하지 않았으니, 가을에 버섯이 나오거나 봄에 스노드롭이 피는 사실에 논평하지 않는 것과 다를 바 없었다. 그들은 배우들 자신이 그러하듯 그 극을 무덤덤하게 받아들였다. 그것은 으레 크리스마스마다 거쳐야 하는 한 가지 쾌활함의 국면이었으니, 더 할 말이 없었다.\n\n그들은 극에 뒤따르는 구슬픈 노래를 불렀는데, 그동안 죽었던 사내들이 모두 말없이 무시무시하게 일어섰으니, 마치 〈한밤의 열병閱兵〉에 나오는 나폴레옹 병사들의 유령 같았다. 그 후 문이 열리고, 페어웨이가 크리스천과 또 한 사람을 데리고 문턱에 나타났다. 그들은 배우들이 춤이 끝나기를 기다렸던 것처럼 극이 끝나기를 바깥에서 기다리고 있었다.\n\n「들어와요, 들어와.」 요브라이트 부인이 말했고, 클림이 그들을 맞으러 앞으로 나아갔다. 「어쩌다 이리 늦었어요? 캔틀 영감은 진작 와 있었는데, 서로 이리 가까이 사니 같이 올 줄 알았지요.」\n\n「그게, 더 일찍 왔어야 하는데요.」 페어웨이 씨가 말하다가 모자를 걸 못을 찾으려 천장 들보를 따라 시선을 보내며 멈췄으나, 늘 쓰던 못은 겨우살이가 차지하고 벽의 못들은 죄다 호랑가시나무 다발에 점령된 것을 보고는, 마침내 촛불 통과 시계 케이스 머리 사이에 모자를 조마조마하게 균형 잡아 얹어 떼어 놓았다. 「더 일찍 왔어야 하는데요, 부인.」 그가 한결 차분해진 태도로 다시 말을 이었다. 「하지만 잔치라는 게 어떤 건지, 그런 때 사람들 집에 자리가 넉넉지 않다는 걸 잘 아니까, 좀 자리가 잡힐 때까지 안 오는 게 낫겠다 싶었지요.」\n\n「저도 그렇게 생각했어요, 요브라이트 부인.」 크리스천이 진지하게 말했다. 「그런데 우리 아버지가 어찌나 안달이 나셨는지 예의고 뭐고 없이, 거의 어두워지기도 전에 집을 나서셨지 뭐예요. 제가 늙은 양반이 이렇게 너무 일찍 가는 건 점잖지 못하다고 말씀드렸지만, 말은 다 바람이지요.」\n\n「클! 게임이 반이나 끝날 때까지 우두커니 기다리고 있을 순 없지! 무슨 일이 벌어진다 하면 난 연만큼 가뿐하다고!」 캔틀 영감이 굴뚝 자리에서 의기양양하게 외쳤다.\n\n그동안 페어웨이는 요브라이트를 비판적으로 응시하기를 마쳤다. 「자, 안 믿을지도 모르겠지만.」 그가 방 안의 나머지 사람들에게 말했다. 「이 양반을 제 황야 밖 어디서 마주쳤더라면 난 절대 못 알아봤을 거요—어찌나 많이 변했는지.」\n\n「당신도 변했어요, 그것도 더 나은 쪽으로요, 티머시.」 요브라이트가 페어웨이의 단단한 풍채를 살피며 말했다.\n\n「요브라이트 도련님, 나도 좀 봐 주시오. 난 더 나은 쪽으로 변했지요, 안 그렇소, 응?」 캔틀 영감이 일어서며, 가장 샅샅이 뜯어보게 하려고 클림의 눈에서 반 자 남짓 떨어진 곳에 자기 몸을 들이대며 말했다.\n\n「물론 봐 드리지요.」 페어웨이가 촛불을 들어 영감의 얼굴 표면 위로 옮겨 가며 말했고, 살펴봄의 대상이 된 당사자는 환한 빛을 받으며 흐뭇한 미소를 짓고 젊은이처럼 몸을 들썩여 보였다.\n\n「별로 안 변하셨네요.」 요브라이트가 말했다.\n\n「차이가 있다면, 영감님이 더 젊어지셨군.」 페어웨이가 단호하게 덧붙였다.\n\n「그런데도 내가 한 일은 아니니, 거기엔 자랑이 없어.」 흐뭇해진 노인이 말했다. 「하지만 내 변덕은 못 고쳐, 그건 인정하지. 그래, 캔틀 어른은 늘 그러셨지, 우리도 알다시피. 하지만 난 자네 옆에 서면 아무것도 아니야, 클림 씨.」\n\n「우리 중 누구도 그렇지.」 험프리가 누구의 귀에도 닿지 않게 할 작정으로 낮고 깊은 감탄의 어조로 말했다.\n\n「정말이지, 내가 뱅업 향토 의용군(우리가 어찌나 멋졌던지 그렇게 불렸지) 병사가 아니었더라면, 여기 그 사람 옆에 그럴듯한 둘째로, 아니 셋째로라도 설 수 있는 자가 아무도 없었을 거야.」 캔틀 영감이 말했다. 「그런데도 우리 다 그 사람 옆에 서면 좀 어설퍼 보여. 하지만 4년에는, 보니가 곶 근처에 상륙했다 싶어서 우리 중대 나머지랑 같이 버드머스에서 뛰쳐나오던 날, 가게 진열창 옆을 휙휙 지나칠 적 내 모습이 남웨식스 통틀어 그보다 더 멋진 풍채는 없었다고들 했지. 거기 내가 있었으니, 어린 포플러처럼 꼿꼿하게, 화승총에, 총검에, 각반에, 턱이 쓸릴 만한 옷깃에, 일곱 별처럼 번쩍이는 장구들까지! 그래, 이웃들, 내 군 복무 시절엔 내가 볼만한 광경이었지. 4년에 날 봤어야 하는데!」\n\n「클림 도련님 풍채는 어머니 쪽에서 온 거요, 정말이지.」 티머시가 말했다. 「난 그 외삼촌들을 잘 알았소. 남웨식스 온 고장을 통틀어 그보다 긴 관은 만든 적이 없었고, 가엾은 조지는 그러고도 무릎이 좀 구부러졌었다는 얘기까지 있지.」\n\n「관이라니, 어디?」 크리스천이 더 가까이 다가오며 물었다. 「누구한테 관 귀신이라도 나타났소, 페어웨이 어른?」\n\n「아니, 아니야. 마음이 그렇게 네 귀를 잘못 이끌게 두지 말고, 사내답게 굴어, 크리스천.」 티머시가 나무라듯 말했다.\n\n「그러지요.」 크리스천이 말했다. 「하지만 지금 생각하니 어젯밤 내 그림자가 꼭 관 모양 같았어요. 그림자가 관 모양이면 무슨 징조요, 이웃들? 무서워할 건 아니겠지요, 설마?」\n\n「무서워? 아니지!」 영감이 말했다. 「참말이지, 난 보니 말고는 아무것도 무서워한 적이 없어, 안 그랬으면 그런 병사가 못 됐겠지. 그래, 4년에 자네가 날 못 본 게 천추의 한이로다!」\n\n이쯤 되자 가면극 배우들은 떠날 채비를 하고 있었으나, 요브라이트 부인이 앉아서 간단히 저녁이라도 들고 가라고 청하며 그들을 붙들었다. 이 청에 크리스마스 영감이 모두를 대표해 흔쾌히 응했다.\n\n유스테이샤는 조금 더 머무를 기회가 생겨 기뻤다. 바깥의 춥고 서리 내린 밤은 그녀에게 곱절로 차가웠다. 그러나 이 지체에는 곤란이 따랐다. 요브라이트 부인은 큰 방에 자리가 없어, 거실에서 통하는 식료품 저장실 문에 반쯤 걸쳐서 가면극 배우들을 위한 긴 의자를 놓았다. 여기에 그들이 한 줄로 앉았으니, 문은 열어 둔 채여서—그리하여 그들은 사실상 여전히 같은 방에 있는 셈이었다. 이제 요브라이트 부인이 아들에게 몇 마디 속삭였고, 아들은 방을 가로질러 식료품 저장실 문으로 가다가 지나면서 겨우살이에 머리를 부딪쳤으며, 배우들에게 쇠고기와 빵, 케이크 페이스트리, 벌꿀술, 엘더베리주를 가져다주었으니, 시중은 그와 그의 어머니가 들어서 어린 하녀가 손님으로 앉을 수 있게 했다. 배우들은 투구를 벗고 먹고 마시기 시작했다.\n\n「하지만 자네도 분명 좀 들겠지?」 클림이 쟁반을 손에 들고 그 전사 앞에 서서 터키 기사에게 말했다. 그녀는 거절했고 여전히 가린 채 앉아 있어, 얼굴을 덮은 리본들 사이로 두 눈의 반짝임만 보였다.\n\n「됐어요, 고맙지만.」 유스테이샤가 대답했다.\n\n「얘는 아주 풋내기라.」 사라센이 변명하듯 말했다. 「봐주셔야 해요. 옛 무리는 아니고, 다른 애가 못 와서 우리한테 낀 거예요.」\n\n「그래도 뭔가 들겠지?」 요브라이트가 끈질기게 말했다. 「벌꿀술이나 엘더베리주 한 잔 해 봐.」\n\n「그래, 그게 낫겠다.」 사라센이 말했다. 「집에 가는 길에 추위를 막아 줄 테니.」\n\n유스테이샤는 얼굴을 드러내지 않고서는 먹을 수 없었으나 변장을 한 채로도 충분히 쉽게 마실 수는 있었다. 그리하여 엘더베리주는 받아들여졌고, 잔은 리본들 안쪽으로 사라졌다.\n\n이 일을 하는 동안 순간순간 유스테이샤는 자기 처지의 안전에 대해 반쯤 의심에 빠졌으나, 거기엔 두려운 기쁨이 있었다. 그녀가 흠모하고 싶다고 마음이 기운 첫 사내가 그녀에게, 하지만 그녀에게가 아니라 어떤 가상의 인물에게 베푸는 일련의 친절이 그녀의 감정을 형언할 수 없이 복잡하게 만들었다. 그녀가 그를 사랑하게 된 것은 부분적으로는 그가 이 무대에서 예외적인 존재였기 때문이고, 부분적으로는 그를 사랑하기로 작정했기 때문이며, 무엇보다 와일디브에게 싫증이 난 끝에 누군가를 사랑하는 일이 절박하게 필요했기 때문이었다. 자신이 어쩔 수 없이 그를 사랑해야 한다고 믿으면서, 그녀는 둘째 리틀턴 경이며, 어느 날 죽으리라 꿈꾸고는 병적인 상상의 압박으로 실제 그 일을 불러온 다른 이들과 같은 방식으로 영향을 받았다. 처녀가 어느 시각 어느 장소에서 누군가에게 사랑에 빠질 가능성을 일단 인정하기만 하면, 그 일은 이미 이루어진 것이나 다름없다.\n\n이 순간 그 환상적인 차림 안에 갇힌 존재의 성별이며, 그녀의 영역이 느끼는 데서나 남을 느끼게 하는 데서나 얼마나 넓은지, 그리고 그녀의 그릇이 무리 속 동료들의 것을 얼마나 멀리 능가하는지를 요브라이트에게 무언가 일러 주는 것이 있었을까? 변장한 사랑의 여왕이 아이네이아스 앞에 나타났을 때 초자연적인 향기가 그녀의 임재에 따라붙어 그 신분을 드러냈다. 만약 그러한 신비로운 발산이 지상의 여인의 감정에서 그 대상을 향해 투사된 적이 있다면, 그것은 지금 요브라이트에게 유스테이샤의 임재를 알렸을 것이다. 그는 그녀를 애틋하게 바라보더니, 자기가 본 것을 잊어 가는 듯 몽상에 잠긴 것 같았다. 잠깐의 상황이 끝나자 그는 지나쳐 갔고, 유스테이샤는 자기가 무엇을 마시는지도 모른 채 술을 홀짝였다. 그녀가 미리 정해 두고 정념을 키우려던 사내는 작은 방으로 들어가, 그곳을 가로질러 먼 끝으로 갔다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이 가면극 배우들은 긴 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 바깥방에 자리가 없어 그 한쪽 끝이 작은 방, 곧 식료품 저장실로 뻗어 있었다. 유스테이샤는 일부는 수줍음 때문에 한가운데 자리를 골랐는데, 그리하여 그 자리에서는 식료품 저장실 안쪽뿐 아니라 손님들이 있는 방까지 내다볼 수 있었다. 클림이 식료품 저장실로 내려갈 때 그녀의 눈은 그곳에 깔린 어둠 속에서 그를 좇았다. 먼 끝에 문 하나가 있었는데, 그가 막 그 문을 직접 열려는 순간 안에 있던 누군가가 문을 열었고, 빛이 흘러나왔다.\n\n그 사람은 토머신으로, 촛불을 들고 있었으며, 근심스럽고 창백하고 흥미로워 보였다. 요브라이트는 그녀를 보고 반가워하며 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n「잘됐네, 탬지.」 그녀를 보고 제정신으로 돌아온 듯 그가 다정하게 말했다. 「내려오기로 했구나. 기뻐.」\n\n「쉿—아니, 아니에요.」 그녀가 재빨리 말했다. 「그냥 오빠한테 말하러 온 거예요.」\n\n「그래도 같이 끼지 그래?」\n\n「못 해요. 적어도 안 하는 편이 낫겠어요. 몸이 그리 좋지 않고, 오빠가 이제 꽤 긴 휴가로 집에 있을 테니 함께할 시간이야 충분할 거예요.」\n\n「네가 없으면 영 즐겁지가 않은데. 정말 아픈 거야?」\n\n「조금요, 내 오랜 사촌 오빠—여기가요.」 그녀가 장난스럽게 손으로 가슴을 쓸며 말했다.\n\n「아, 어머니가 오늘 밤엔 다른 사람을 불렀어야 했나 보네?」\n\n「오, 아니에요, 정말로. 그냥 잠깐 내려온 거예요, 클림 오빠, 오빠한테 부탁하려고요—」 여기서 그가 그녀를 따라 문간을 지나 그 너머 사적인 방으로 들어갔고, 문이 닫히자, 그 일을 지켜본 유일한 또 다른 증인인 유스테이샤와 그 옆에 앉은 가면극 배우는 더는 보지도 듣지도 못했다.\n\n열기가 유스테이샤의 머리와 두 뺨으로 솟구쳤다. 그녀는 클림이 집에 온 지 겨우 이삼 일밖에 안 되었으니 와일디브와 관련된 토머신의 괴로운 처지를 아직 모르고 있으며, 그가 집을 떠나기 전과 똑같이 토머신이 거기 사는 것을 보고는 당연히 아무것도 의심하지 않으리라고 단박에 짐작했다. 유스테이샤는 그 순간 토머신에 대한 격렬한 질투를 느꼈다. 토머신이 아직은 다른 사내에게 다정한 마음을 품고 있을지 몰라도, 이렇게 흥미롭고 견문 넓은 사촌 오빠와 한 집에 갇혀 있는데 그 마음이 얼마나 오래가리라 기대할 수 있겠는가? 이렇게 끊임없이 서로의 곁에 있고 마음을 흩뜨릴 대상이 가까이 하나도 없으니, 둘 사이에 머잖아 어떤 애정이 싹틀지 알 수 없는 노릇이었다. 클림의 그녀를 향한 소년 같은 사랑은 시들었을지 몰라도, 그것은 손쉽게 다시 되살아날 수 있었다.\n\n유스테이샤는 제 꾀에 제가 약이 올랐다. 다른 여자가 돋보이게 빛나는 동안 자기는 이런 차림을 하고 있다니 이 얼마나 순전한 자기 낭비인가! 이 만남의 온전한 효과를 알았더라면 그녀는 자연스러운 방식으로 여기 오려고 하늘과 땅을 움직였을 것이다. 얼굴의 힘은 죄다 사라지고, 감정의 매력은 죄다 가려지고, 교태의 매혹은 존재 자체가 부정되고, 그녀에게 남은 것이라곤 목소리뿐이니, 그녀는 에코의 운명을 느꼈다. 「여기선 아무도 날 존중하지 않아.」 그녀가 말했다. 다른 소년들 사이에 소년으로 끼어들었으니 소년 대접을 받으리라는 사실을 그녀는 간과하고 있었다. 그 무시는, 비록 자기가 자초한 것이고 저절로 설명되는 것이었으나, 무심코 드러난 것이라 치고 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없었으니, 그만큼 그 처지가 그녀를 예민하게 만들었던 것이다.\n\n여자들은 무대 의상을 입고 스스로를 위해 많은 것을 이루었다. 지난 세기 초의 어느 어여쁜 폴리 피첨 연기자며 금세기 초의 또 다른 리디아 랭귀시 연기자처럼, 사랑뿐 아니라 덤으로 공작 부인의 관까지 얻은 이들보다 한참 아래를 보더라도, 그 무리 전체가 거의 자기들이 원하는 데서 사랑을 얻는다는 초보적 만족에는 이르렀다. 그러나 터키 기사는 감히 옆으로 쓸어낼 수 없는 펄럭이는 리본들 때문에 이것을 이룰 기회조차 거부당했다.\n\n[1] 1877년에 쓰임.\n\n요브라이트가 사촌 누이 없이 방으로 돌아왔다. 유스테이샤로부터 두세 걸음 거리에 이르렀을 때 그는 다시 어떤 생각에 붙들린 듯 멈춰 섰다. 그가 그녀를 응시하고 있었다. 그녀는 어쩔 줄 몰라 다른 데로 시선을 돌리며, 이 연옥이 얼마나 오래갈지 궁금해했다. 몇 초간 머뭇거린 뒤 그는 다시 지나쳐 갔다.\n\n사랑으로 제 불편을 자초하는 것은 어떤 격정적인 여자들에게 흔한 본능이다. 사랑과 두려움과 수치의 상충하는 감각이 유스테이샤를 더없이 불안한 상태로 몰아넣었다. 달아나는 것이 그녀의 크고 즉각적인 욕망이었다. 다른 가면극 배우들은 떠날 기색이 전혀 없어 보였고, 그녀는 자기 옆에 앉은 젊은이에게 집 밖에서 그들을 기다리는 편이 낫겠다고 속삭이고는, 되도록 눈에 띄지 않게 문으로 다가가 문을 열고 빠져나왔다.\n\n고요하고 외딴 풍경이 그녀를 안심시켰다. 그녀는 울타리 말뚝으로 나아가 거기 기대어 달을 바라보았다. 그렇게 선 지 얼마 안 되어 문이 다시 열렸다. 무리의 나머지가 보이리라 기대하며 유스테이샤가 돌아섰으나, 아니었으니—클림 요브라이트가 그녀가 그랬던 만큼이나 살그머니 나와 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다.\n\n그가 다가와 그녀 곁에 섰다. 「내겐 기묘한 생각이 있어요.」 그가 말했다. 「그래서 한 가지 물어보고 싶군요. 당신은 여자입니까—아니면 내가 틀렸나요?」\n\n「난 여자예요.」\n\n그의 눈이 큰 흥미를 띠고 그녀에게 머물렀다. 「요즘은 처녀들이 가면극을 자주 합니까? 예전엔 안 그랬는데요.」\n\n「요즘도 안 해요.」\n\n「그런데 왜 했나요?」\n\n「흥분을 얻고 우울을 떨치려고요.」 그녀가 낮은 어조로 말했다.\n\n「무엇이 당신을 우울하게 했지요?」\n\n「삶이요.」\n「그건 많은 이가 견뎌야 하는 우울의 원인이지요.」\n\n「그렇죠.」\n\n긴 침묵. 「그래서 흥분은 얻나요?」 마침내 클림이 물었다.\n\n「이 순간엔, 아마도요.」\n\n「그럼 들킨 게 분한가요?」\n\n「네, 그럴지도 모른다고 생각은 했지만요.」\n\n「오고 싶어 한 줄 알았더라면 기꺼이 우리 잔치에 청했을 텐데. 내 어린 시절에 당신과 알고 지낸 적이 있던가요?」\n\n「전혀요.」\n\n「다시 안으로 들어와 원하는 만큼 있지 않겠어요?」\n\n「아니요. 더는 알아보는 사람이 없었으면 해요.」\n\n「그렇군요, 내겐 안심해도 됩니다.」 잠시 생각에 잠겨 있다가 그가 부드럽게 덧붙였다. 「더는 당신을 방해하지 않겠어요. 참 기묘한 만남이군요, 그리고 교양 있는 여자가 어째서 이런 역할을 하는지는 묻지 않겠습니다.」\n\n그녀는 그가 바라는 듯한 이유를 자청해 내놓지 않았고, 그는 그녀에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하고는 거기서 집 뒤쪽으로 돌아가, 다시 안으로 들어가기 전에 한동안 혼자 거닐었다.\n\n내면의 불로 달아오른 유스테이샤는 이런 일이 있고 난 뒤로 동료들을 기다릴 수가 없었다. 그녀는 얼굴에서 리본을 젖히고 대문을 열어 곧장 황야로 들어섰다. 서둘러 걷지는 않았다. 이 시각이면 할아버지는 잠자리에 들어 있었으니, 그녀가 달 밝은 밤이면 워낙 자주 언덕을 거닐었기에 할아버지는 그녀가 오고 가는 데 신경 쓰지 않았고, 자기 식대로 즐기면서 그녀도 그러도록 내버려 두었다. 집 안으로 들어가는 일보다 더 중요한 주제가 이제 그녀를 사로잡고 있었다. 요브라이트가 조금이라도 호기심이 있다면 틀림없이 그녀의 이름을 알아낼 터였다. 그러면 어떻게 되는가? 그녀는 이 모험이 끝난 방식에 대해 처음엔 일종의 환희를 느꼈으니, 환희 사이사이 순간순간 무안하고 얼굴이 붉어지긴 했지만 말이다. 그러다 이런 생각이 되돌아와 그녀를 서늘하게 했다. 자기 활약이 무슨 소용인가? 현재 그녀는 요브라이트 가家에 완전히 낯선 사람이었다. 자기가 그 사내를 에워싼 그 터무니없는 낭만의 후광이 도리어 그녀의 비참함이 될지도 몰랐다. 어떻게 자기가 낯선 사람에게 이토록 빠져드는 것을 허락할 수 있단 말인가? 게다가 그녀의 슬픔의 잔을 가득 채우려는 듯 토머신이 있어, 날이면 날마다 그와 인화성 있게 가까이 살고 있을 터였으니, 그녀는 처음 믿었던 것과 달리 그가 상당히 오랜 기간 집에 머무를 것임을 방금 알게 되었기 때문이다.\n\n그녀는 미스토버 냅의 쪽문에 이르렀으나, 문을 열기 전에 돌아서서 다시 한번 황야를 마주했다. 레인배로의 형상이 언덕들 위로 솟아 있었고, 달이 레인배로 위에 떠 있었다. 공기는 정적과 서리로 가득했다. 그 풍경은 유스테이샤에게 그 순간까지 까맣게 잊고 있던 한 가지 사정을 일깨웠다. 그녀는 바로 오늘 밤 여덟 시에 레인배로 옆에서 와일디브를 만나, 달아나자는 그의 간청에 마지막 대답을 주기로 약속해 두었던 것이다.\n\n그 저녁과 시각을 정한 것은 그녀 자신이었다. 그는 아마 그 자리에 와서 추위 속에 거기 기다리다가 크게 실망했을 것이다. 「뭐, 차라리 잘됐어—그가 다칠 일은 없었으니까.」 그녀가 태연히 말했다. 와일디브는 지금 그을린 유리를 통해 본 태양의 광선 없는 윤곽 같은 처지였고, 그녀는 그런 말을 더없이 수월하게 할 수 있었다.\n\n그녀는 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 머물렀고, 사촌 오빠를 향한 토머신의 사람을 끄는 태도가 다시 유스테이샤의 마음에 떠올랐다.\n\n「아, 그 애가 이 일 전에 데이먼과 결혼했더라면!」 그녀가 말했다. 「나만 아니었으면 그랬을 텐데! 내가 알기만 했더라면—내가 알기만 했더라면!」\n\n유스테이샤는 다시 한번 깊고 폭풍 같은 두 눈을 달빛으로 들어 올리고는, 몸서리에 그토록 가까운 그 비극적인 한숨을 내쉬며 지붕의 그늘 안으로 들어섰다. 헛간에서 의상을 벗어 둘둘 말고는, 안으로 들어가 제 방으로 갔다.\n\n손녀의 행동거지에 대한 노 선장의 한결같은 무관심은 그녀를 새처럼 자유롭게 제 갈 길을 가도록 내버려 두었으나, 마침 이튿날 아침 그가 직접 나서서 그녀에게 왜 그렇게 늦게까지 밖에 나가 있었느냐고 물었다.\n\n「그냥 사건거리를 찾으려고요, 할아버지.」 방아쇠가 당겨질 때마다 그 뒤에 그토록 큰 힘이 도사리고 있음을 드러내는 그 졸린 듯한 태도로 창밖을 내다보며 그녀가 말했다.\n\n「사건거리를 찾는다—꼭 내가 스물한 살에 알던 한량들 중 하나 같구나.」\n\n「여긴 외로워요.」\n\n「그게 더 낫지. 내가 읍내에 산다면 내 모든 시간을 너 챙기는 데 다 써야 할 거다. 내가 ‘우먼’에서 돌아왔을 때 네가 집에 있으리라 잔뜩 기대했는데.」\n\n「제가 한 일을 숨기진 않을게요. 모험을 원했고, 가면극 배우들과 갔어요. 터키 기사 역을 했지요.」\n\n「설마, 그럴 리가? 하하! 이런 세상에! 너한테 그런 걸 기대하진 않았다, 유스테이샤.」\n\n「제 첫 공연이었고, 분명 마지막일 거예요. 자, 말씀드렸으니—기억하세요, 이건 비밀이에요.」\n\n「물론이지. 하지만, 유스테이샤, 네가 설마—하! 하! 빌어먹을, 사십 년 전이었으면 얼마나 기뻤을꼬! 하지만 명심해라, 다신 안 된다, 얘야. 황야는 밤이든 낮이든 네 맘대로 거닐어도 좋아, 날 귀찮게만 안 하면. 하지만 다신 바지 차림으로 나서는 건 안 돼.」\n\n「저 때문에 걱정하실 일 없어요, 할아버지.」\n\n여기서 대화가 그쳤으니, 유스테이샤의 도덕 교육은 이런 식의 대화보다 더 엄격한 적이 없었고, 이것이 혹여 선행에 보탬이 되는 것으로 드러난다면 그 값에 비해 결코 비싸지 않은 결과이리라. 그러나 그녀의 생각은 곧 제 자신에 관한 것에서 멀리 벗어났으니, 자기는 이름조차 아닌 한 사람을 향한 격정적이고 형언할 수 없는 염려로 가득한 채, 그녀는 떠도는 유대인 아하스에로처럼 안절부절못하며 자기를 둘러싼 그을린 황야의 광활함 속으로 나아갔다. 거처에서 반 마일쯤 떨어졌을 때 그녀는 조금 앞쪽 협곡에서 솟아오르는 불길한 붉은빛을 보았으니—햇빛 속 불꽃처럼 흐릿하고 음산한 그것을 그녀는 디고리 벤을 뜻한다고 짐작했다.\n\n지난 한 달 사이에 황토 재고를 새로 사들이고 싶었던 농부들이 벤을 어디서 찾을 수 있느냐고 물으면 사람들은 「에그던 황야에서.」라고 대답했다. 날이면 날마다 대답은 똑같았다. 그런데 에그던에는 양과 양치기보다는 황야 풀을 뜯는 망아지와 가시금작화를 베는 이들이 살고 있었으며, 후자가 대부분 모여 있는 구릉지는 더러는 에그던 북쪽에, 더러는 서쪽에 놓여 있었으니, 그가 이스라엘이 신 광야에서 그러했듯 이 부근에서 야영하는 이유는 분명치 않았다. 그 위치는 중심에 있어 이따금 탐날 만했다. 그러나 황토 판매가 디고리가 황야에 남아 있는 일차적 목적은 아니었으니, 특히 그와 같은 부류의 떠돌이 대부분이 겨울 거처로 들어간 이렇게 늦은 철에는 더욱 그러했다.\n\n유스테이샤는 그 외로운 사내를 바라보았다. 와일디브는 지난번 만남에서, 토머신의 약혼자로서 자기 자리를 기꺼이 간절히 차지할 사람으로 요브라이트 부인이 벤을 내세웠다고 그녀에게 말했었다. 그의 풍채는 완벽했고, 얼굴은 젊고 윤곽이 또렷했으며, 눈은 빛났고, 머리는 예리했으며, 처지는 마음만 먹으면 쉽사리 나아질 수 있는 것이었다. 그러나 가능성에도 불구하고, 요브라이트 같은 사촌 오빠가 곁에 있고 동시에 와일디브도 전혀 무관심하지는 않은 마당에, 토머신이 이 이스마엘 같은 존재를 받아들일 성싶지는 않았다. 유스테이샤는 가엾은 요브라이트 부인이 조카딸의 장래를 염려한 나머지 다른 쪽의 열성을 자극하려고 이 구혼자를 언급한 것임을 짐작하는 데 오래 걸리지 않았다. 유스테이샤는 이제 요브라이트 편이었고, 그 이모의 바람의 정신에 공감했다.\n\n「안녕하시오, 아가씨.」 황토장이가 토끼 가죽 모자를 벗으며 말했는데, 지난번 만남을 떠올리고도 그녀에게 별 악감정을 품은 것 같지는 않았다.\n\n「안녕하세요, 황토장이.」 그녀가 짙게 그늘진 눈을 그에게 들어 올리는 수고조차 거의 하지 않으며 말했다. 「이렇게 가까이 있는 줄 몰랐네요. 마차도 여기 있나요?」\n\n벤이 팔꿈치를 한 움푹한 곳 쪽으로 움직였는데, 거기엔 자줏빛 줄기의 가시덤불이 빽빽이 우거져 어찌나 엄청난 규모로 자랐는지 거의 작은 골짜기를 이룰 정도였다. 가시덤불은 손을 대면 심술궂으나, 초겨울에는 다정한 피난처가 되어 주니, 잎을 떨구는 관목들 가운데 가장 늦게 잎을 잃기 때문이다.\n\n덤불의 얽힌 가지와 엉킨 가지 뒤로 벤의 포장마차 지붕과 굴뚝이 보였다.\n\n「이 근처에 머무르나요?」 그녀가 한층 흥미를 띠고 물었다.\n\n「예, 여기 볼일이 있어서요.」\n\n「황토 파는 일이 다는 아니고요?」\n\n「그것하곤 상관없는 일이오.」\n\n「요브라이트 양과 관련된 일인가요?」\n\n그녀의 얼굴이 무장한 평화를 청하는 듯 보였기에, 그는 솔직하게 말했다. 「예, 아가씨. 그 사람 때문이오.」\n\n「그 사람과 곧 있을 결혼 때문이에요?」\n\n벤은 칠한 얼굴 너머로 붉어졌다. 「날 놀리지 마시오, 바이 양.」 그가 말했다.\n\n「사실이 아니에요?」\n\n「물론 아니오.」\n\n그리하여 그녀는 황토장이가 요브라이트 부인의 마음속에서 한낱 차선책일 뿐이며, 게다가 그 보잘것없는 지위로 승격되었다는 사실조차 통지받지 못한 사람임을 확신하게 되었다. 「그냥 제 생각이었어요.」 그녀가 조용히 말하고는, 더 말을 잇지 않고 지나치려던 참에, 오른쪽을 돌아보다가 그녀가 선 꼭대기로 이어지는 작은 오솔길 중 하나를 따라 구불구불 올라오는, 괴롭도록 낯익은 형상을 보았다. 길이 어쩔 수 없이 굽이지는 탓에 그의 등이 지금은 그들 쪽을 향해 있었다. 그녀는 재빨리 둘러보았으니, 저 사내를 피할 길은 단 하나뿐이었다. 벤을 돌아보며 그녀가 말했다. 「마차에서 몇 분 쉬게 해 주시겠어요? 둑은 앉기엔 축축해서요.」\n\n「그럼요, 아가씨. 자리를 마련해 드리지요.」\n\n그녀는 가시덤불 골짜기 뒤로 그를 따라 그의 바퀴 달린 거처로 갔고, 벤이 올라가 세 발 의자를 문 바로 안쪽에 놓았다.\n\n「이게 내가 해 드릴 수 있는 최선이오.」 그가 내려와 오솔길로 물러나며 말했고, 거기서 그는 오가며 다시 파이프를 피웠다.\n\n유스테이샤는 마차 안으로 뛰어올라 의자에 앉았으니, 길 쪽에서는 보이지 않게 가려져 있었다. 곧 그녀는 황토장이 것이 아닌 다른 발소리가 스치는 것을, 두 사내가 서로를 지나치며 내뱉는 그리 다정하지 않은 「안녕하시오.」를, 그러고는 그중 하나의 발소리가 앞쪽 방향으로 차츰 잦아드는 것을 들었다. 유스테이샤는 멀어져 가는 등과 어깨를 언뜻 볼 수 있을 때까지 목을 앞으로 길게 뺐고, 까닭 모를 비참한 아픔의 욱신거림을 느꼈다. 그것은 변해 버린 마음에 조금이라도 너그러움이 깃들어 있다면, 한때 사랑했으나 더는 사랑하지 않는 이를 문득 보게 될 때 따라오는 그 메스꺼운 느낌이었다.\n\n유스테이샤가 가던 길을 계속 가려고 내려왔을 때 황토장이가 가까이 왔다. 「방금 지나간 게 와일디브 씨였소, 아가씨.」 그가 천천히 말했고, 그녀가 들키지 않고 앉아 있었던 데 분해할 것이라 예상한다는 표정을 얼굴로 드러냈다.\n\n「네, 그가 언덕을 올라오는 걸 봤어요.」 유스테이샤가 대답했다. 「그걸 왜 나한테 말하죠?」 황토장이가 그녀의 지난 사랑을 알고 있음을 생각하면 대담한 질문이었으나, 그녀의 감정을 드러내지 않는 태도에는 자기가 멀게 대하는 이들의 견해를 억누르는 힘이 있었다.\n\n「그걸 물을 수 있다니 들으니 기쁘오.」 황토장이가 퉁명스럽게 말했다. 「그리고 이제 생각해 보니, 그게 어젯밤 내가 본 거랑 들어맞는군.」\n\n「아—그게 뭔데요?」 유스테이샤는 그를 떠나고 싶었으나, 알고도 싶었다.\n\n「와일디브 씨가 레인배로에서 오지 않는 숙녀를 한참 기다렸소.」\n\n「당신도 기다렸나 보네요?」\n\n「예, 난 늘 그러오. 그가 실망하는 걸 보니 기뻤소. 그는 오늘 밤 또 거기 올 거요.」\n\n「또 실망하려고요. 사실은요, 황토장이, 그 숙녀는 토머신과 와일디브 씨의 결혼을 가로막기는커녕 오히려 그걸 아주 기꺼이 거들고 싶어 한답니다.」\n\n벤은 이 고백에 적잖이 놀랐으나, 그것을 분명히 드러내지는 않았으니, 그런 표현은 예상에서 한 발짝 벗어난 말에는 따라올 수 있어도, 두 발짝 이상 벗어난 복잡한 경우엔 대개 억제되는 법이다. 「그렇군요, 아가씨.」 그가 대답했다.\n\n「와일디브 씨가 오늘 밤 또 레인배로에 오리라는 걸 어떻게 알죠?」 그녀가 물었다.\n\n「혼잣말로 그러겠다고 하는 걸 들었소. 단단히 성이 나 있더군.」\n\n유스테이샤는 한순간 자기가 느끼는 바를 그대로 내비치고는, 깊고 검은 눈을 근심스레 그에게 들어 올리며 중얼거렸다. 「어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요. 그에게 무례하게 굴고 싶진 않지만, 그를 다시 보고 싶지도 않고, 또 그에게 돌려줄 자잘한 물건이 몇 개 있어요.」\n\n「원하시면 그걸 나한테 맡기시오, 아가씨, 그리고 더는 그에게 할 말이 없다고 알리는 쪽지도요. 아주 은밀하게 가져다드리리다. 그게 그에게 아가씨 마음을 알리는 가장 솔직한 방법일 거요.」\n\n「좋아요.」 유스테이샤가 말했다. 「우리 집 쪽으로 와요, 가지고 나올 테니.」\n\n그녀가 앞장섰고, 오솔길은 황야의 텁수룩한 더부룩함 사이로 난 한없이 작은 가르마였기에, 황토장이는 그녀의 자취를 정확히 따라갔다. 그녀는 멀리서 선장이 둑 위에서 망원경으로 지평선을 훑고 있는 것을 보았고, 벤에게 선 자리에서 기다리라 이르고는 혼자 집으로 들어갔다.\n\n십 분 뒤 그녀는 꾸러미와 쪽지를 들고 돌아와, 그것들을 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 말했다. 「왜 이걸 나 대신 이렇게 선뜻 맡아 주는 거죠?」\n\n「그걸 물을 수 있소?」\n\n「당신은 이걸로 어떻게든 토머신을 도울 생각인가 봐요. 여전히 그녀의 결혼을 돕고 싶어 그렇게 안달인가요?」\n\n벤은 조금 마음이 움직였다. 「차라리 내가 그 사람과 결혼했으면 했소.」 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 「하지만 내가 느끼는 건, 그 사람이 그자 없이는 행복할 수 없다면 사내가 마땅히 그래야 하듯 그 사람이 그자를 얻도록 돕는 내 도리를 다하겠다는 거요.」\n\n유스테이샤는 이렇게 말하는 그 별난 사내를 신기한 듯 바라보았다. 정념의 주된 구성 요소이자 때로는 그 유일한 요소이기도 한 그 이기심이라는 성질에서 완전히 자유로운 사랑이라니, 이 얼마나 기묘한 종류의 사랑인가! 황토장이의 사심 없음은 존중받을 만한 것이 어찌나 충분한지 도무지 이해되지 않을 만큼 존중을 넘어섰고, 그녀는 그것을 거의 어처구니없다고까지 여겼다.\n\n「그럼 우리 둘은 마침내 한마음이군요.」 그녀가 말했다.\n\n「그렇소.」 벤이 침울하게 대답했다. 「하지만 아가씨, 어째서 그 사람한테 그런 관심을 두는지 말해 주면 마음이 좀 놓이겠소. 너무 갑작스럽고 이상해서요.」\n\n유스테이샤는 어찌할 바를 모르는 듯했다. 「그건 말해 줄 수 없어요, 황토장이.」 그녀가 차갑게 말했다.\n\n벤은 더 말하지 않았다. 그는 편지를 주머니에 넣고, 유스테이샤에게 절하고는 떠나갔다.\n\n와일디브가 레인배로 기슭의 긴 비탈을 올랐을 때 그곳은 다시 밤과 한데 섞여 있었다. 그가 꼭대기에 이르자 그의 바로 등 뒤 땅에서 한 형상이 솟아올랐다. 그것은 유스테이샤의 사자使者의 것이었다. 그가 와일디브의 어깨를 탁 쳤다. 열병에 들뜬 젊은 여관 주인이자 전직 기사技士는 이투리엘의 창에 닿은 사탄처럼 흠칫했다.\n\n「약속은 늘 여덟 시, 이 자리지요.」 벤이 말했다. 「그리고 우리가 여기 있군요—우리 셋이.」\n\n「우리 셋?」 와일디브가 재빨리 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n「예, 당신과 나, 그리고 그 여자요. 이게 그 여자요.」 그가 편지와 꾸러미를 들어 보였다.\n\n와일디브가 의아해하며 그것들을 받았다. 「이게 무슨 뜻인지 통 모르겠군.」 그가 말했다. 「어떻게 여기 온 거요? 뭔가 착오가 있는 게 분명해.」\n\n「편지를 읽으면 마음에서 풀릴 거요. 등불 하나 켭시다.」 황토장이가 불을 켜, 자기가 가져온 우지 양초 한 토막에 불을 붙이고 모자로 그것을 가렸다.\n\n「당신 누구요?」 와일디브가 촛불 빛으로 동행의 흐릿하게 불그스름한 몰골을 분간하며 말했다. 「당신은 오늘 아침 언덕에서 본 그 황토장이로군—아니, 당신은 바로 그 사내가——」\n\n「부디 편지나 읽으시오.」\n\n「다른 여자한테서 왔다면 놀라지도 않았을 텐데.」 와일디브가 편지를 펼쳐 읽으며 중얼거렸다. 그의 얼굴이 심각해졌다.\n「와일디브 씨께.\n\n여러 번 생각한 끝에, 우리가 더는 어떤 연락도 주고받아선 안 된다고 단번에 결심했습니다. 이 문제를 생각하면 할수록 우리 교제가 끝나야 한다는 확신이 더 굳어집니다. 이 두 해 동안 당신이 한결같이 내게 충실했더라면 이제 나를 무정하다 비난할 근거가 좀 있을지도 모르겠습니다만, 당신이 나를 버린 동안 내가 무엇을 견뎠는지, 그리고 당신이 다른 여자에게 구애하는 것을 내가 한 번도 끼어들지 않고 묵묵히 참아 냈는지를 차분히 헤아려 본다면, 당신이 내게 다시 돌아왔을 때 내가 내 감정을 따를 권리가 있음을 인정하시리라 생각합니다. 그 감정이 예전 당신을 향하던 것과 다른 것은, 어쩌면 내 잘못일지 모르나, 당신이 토머신을 택해 나를 떠난 일을 떠올린다면 도무지 나를 책망할 수 없는 잘못입니다.\n우리 우정의 초기에 당신이 내게 준 자잘한 물건들은 이 편지를 전하는 이 편에 돌려보냅니다. 마땅히 당신이 그녀와 약혼했다는 소식을 내가 처음 들었을 때 돌려보냈어야 할 것들입니다.\n\n유스테이샤.」\n와일디브가 그녀의 이름에 다다랐을 즈음, 편지 전반부를 읽으며 떠올랐던 그 막막함이 굴욕으로 짙어졌다. 「이래저래 내가 단단히 바보가 됐군.」 그가 부루퉁하게 말했다. 「이 편지에 뭐가 들었는지 아시오?」\n\n황토장이가 곡조를 흥얼거렸다.\n\n「대답을 못 하겠소?」 와일디브가 발끈하며 물었다.\n\n「루-움-툼-툼.」 황토장이가 노래했다.\n\n와일디브는 벤의 발치 땅바닥을 바라보며 서 있다가, 촛불에 비친 디고리의 몸을 따라 머리와 얼굴까지 시선을 위로 옮겼다. 「하-하! 뭐, 두 사람을 다 가지고 놀았으니 이 정도는 받아 마땅하겠지.」 그가 마침내, 벤에게라기보다 자기 자신에게 하듯 말했다. 「하지만 내가 여태 알던 온갖 기이한 일 중에서도 가장 기이한 건, 당신이 제 잇속에 이토록 어긋나게 이걸 나한테 가져왔다는 거요.」\n\n「내 잇속?」\n\n「그렇소. 그 여자가 당신을 받아들였으니—혹은 그 비슷하게 됐으니, 내가 토머신한테 다시 구애하게 만들 짓은 안 하는 게 당신 잇속이었지. 요브라이트 부인은 당신이 그녀와 결혼할 거라던데. 그럼 그게 사실이 아니오?」\n\n「세상에! 이 얘긴 전에도 들었지만 안 믿었소. 그 사람이 언제 그랬소?」\n\n와일디브가 황토장이가 그랬듯이 흥얼거리기 시작했다.\n\n「이젠 안 믿소.」 벤이 외쳤다.\n\n「루-움-툼-툼.」 와일디브가 노래했다.\n\n「오 이런—우리도 흉내는 잘 내는군!」 벤이 경멸하듯 말했다. 「이건 끝장을 봐야겠소. 곧장 그 사람한테 가겠소.」\n\n디고리가 단호한 발걸음으로 물러갔고, 와일디브의 눈은 그를 마치 황야 풀을 뜯는 망아지보다 나을 게 없는 양 매섭게 조롱하며 그의 몸을 훑었다. 황토장이의 형상이 더는 보이지 않게 되자 와일디브 자신도 내려가 골짜기의 빛 없는 움푹한 곳으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n두 여자를 다 잃다니—양쪽 모두에게 사랑받던 자가—견디기엔 너무도 얄궂은 결말이었다. 그가 그나마 체면을 차려 자신을 구할 길은 토머신뿐이었고, 일단 그가 그녀의 남편이 되고 나면 유스테이샤의 후회가 길고 쓰라린 기간 동안 시작되리라고 그는 생각했다. 무대 뒤의 새 인물을 모르는 와일디브가 유스테이샤를 연기를 하고 있다고 여긴 것은 무리가 아니었다. 그 편지가 순간의 토라짐의 산물이 아니라고 믿고, 그녀가 정말로 그를 토머신에게 넘긴다고 추론하려면, 그 사내의 영향으로 그녀가 변모했다는 사전 지식이 필요했을 것이다. 그녀가 새로운 정념의 탐욕 속에서 너그러워졌음을, 한 사촌을 탐내면서 다른 한쪽에 후하게 굴고 있음을, 차지하려는 열망 속에서 양보하고 있음을 누가 알 수 있었겠는가?\n\n서둘러 결혼해 그 도도한 처녀의 가슴을 비틀어 놓겠다는 이 결심으로 가득 차서 와일디브는 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n한편 디고리 벤은 마차로 돌아와, 거기 서서 난로 안을 골똘히 들여다보았다. 새로운 전망이 그에게 열린 셈이었다. 그러나 조카딸의 손을 두고 요브라이트 부인이 그를 후보로 보는 견해가 아무리 유망하다 한들, 토머신 본인의 호의를 얻으려면 한 가지 조건이 반드시 따라야 했으니, 그것은 그가 지금의 거친 생활 방식을 버리는 것이었다. 여기에 그는 별 어려움을 느끼지 않았다.\n\n토머신을 만나 자기 계획을 자세히 들려주는 일을 이튿날까지 미룰 형편이 못 되었다. 그는 잽싸게 몸단장에 들어가, 궤짝에서 천으로 된 양복 한 벌을 꺼냈고, 이십 분쯤 지나자 얼굴만 빼고는 황토장이가 아닌 모습으로 마차 등불 앞에 섰으니, 얼굴의 주홍빛 색조는 하루 만에 지워질 것이 아니었다. 문을 닫고 자물쇠로 잠근 뒤, 벤은 블룸스엔드를 향해 길을 나섰다.\n\n그가 흰 울타리 말뚝에 이르러 대문에 손을 얹었을 때 집 문이 열렸다가 이내 다시 닫혔다. 한 여자의 형상이 미끄러지듯 안으로 들어갔던 것이다. 동시에, 보아하니 그 여자와 현관에 함께 서 있던 듯한 한 사내가 벤과 얼굴을 맞댈 때까지 집에서 앞으로 나왔다. 또다시 와일디브였다.\n\n「이런, 빠르기도 하군.」 디고리가 비꼬듯 말했다.\n\n「당신은 느리지, 곧 알게 될 거요.」 와일디브가 말했다. 그러고는 목소리를 낮춰 「이제 그만 돌아가는 게 좋을 거요. 난 그녀를 차지하겠다 했고, 차지했으니. 잘 가시오, 황토장이!」 그러고 나서 와일디브가 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n벤의 가슴은 안에서 내려앉았으니, 부당하게 높이 솟아오른 적도 없었건만 그러했다. 그는 거의 십오 분간 울타리 말뚝에 기댄 채 갈피를 못 잡고 서 있었다. 그러다 정원 길을 올라가 문을 두드리고 요브라이트 부인을 청했다.\n\n부인은 그에게 들어오라 하는 대신 현관으로 나왔다. 둘 사이에 낮고 절제된 어조로 십 분 남짓 이야기가 오갔다. 그 시간이 끝나자 요브라이트 부인은 안으로 들어갔고, 벤은 슬프게 발길을 되돌려 황야로 들어섰다. 그가 다시 마차에 이르자 등불을 켜고, 무심한 얼굴로 곧장 제일 좋은 옷을 벗기 시작해, 몇 분 사이에 앞서 보였던 그대로 굳어진, 돌이킬 수 없는 황토장이로 다시 나타났다.\n\n그날 저녁 블룸스엔드의 내부는 아늑하고 안락했으나 다소 적막했다. 클림 요브라이트가 집에 없었던 것이다. 크리스마스 잔치 이후로 그는 십 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 사는 친구를 며칠간 방문하러 가 있었다.\n\n벤이 현관에서 와일디브와 헤어져 재빨리 집 안으로 물러나는 것을 본 그 어렴풋한 형상은 토머신의 것이었다. 안으로 들어서며 그녀는 아무렇게나 몸에 둘렀던 망토를 벗어 던지고, 불빛 쪽으로 나아갔으니, 거기에는 요브라이트 부인이 일감 탁자 앞에 앉아 있었고, 그 탁자는 긴 등받이 의자 안쪽으로 당겨져 일부가 굴뚝 모퉁이로 비어져 나와 있었다.\n\n「어두워진 뒤에 혼자 밖에 나가는 거 마음에 안 든다, 탬신.」 이모가 일감에서 눈도 들지 않은 채 조용히 말했다.\n\n「그냥 문 바로 밖에 있었어요.」\n\n「그래?」 토머신의 목소리 어조가 달라진 데 짚이는 바가 있어 요브라이트 부인이 그녀를 살피며 물었다. 토머신의 뺨은 그녀의 괴로운 일들이 닥치기 전에 이른 적 있던 정도를 한참 넘어 붉게 달아올라 있었고, 두 눈은 반짝였다.\n\n「문을 두드린 건 그 사람이었어요.」 그녀가 말했다.\n\n「그럴 거라 짐작했다.」\n\n「그 사람은 결혼을 당장 하고 싶어 해요.」\n\n「그래! 뭐—그가 안달이라도 났니?」 요브라이트 부인이 조카딸에게 살피는 눈길을 보냈다. 「왜 와일디브 씨는 들어오지 않았지?」\n\n「들어오고 싶어 하지 않았어요. 이모가 자기랑 사이가 좋지 않다고 하더라고요. 모레 결혼식을 하고 싶대요, 아주 은밀하게, 자기 교구 교회에서요—우리 교회가 아니라요.」\n\n「오! 그래서 넌 뭐라고 했니?」\n\n「그렇게 하기로 했어요.」 토머신이 단호하게 대답했다. 「난 이제 현실적인 여자예요. 마음 같은 건 전혀 믿지 않아요. 어떤 사정이든 그와 결혼하겠어요, 그—그 클림 오빠 편지 이후로는요.」\n\n요브라이트 부인의 일감 바구니 위에 편지 한 통이 놓여 있었는데, 토머신의 말에 이모는 그것을 다시 펼쳐, 그날 열 번째로 말없이 읽었다—\n\n「토머신과 와일디브 씨에 대해 사람들이 퍼뜨리는 이 어리석은 이야기는 대체 무슨 뜻인가요? 조금이라도 사실일 가능성이 있다면 그런 추문은 모욕적이라 부르겠어요. 어떻게 그런 터무니없는 거짓이 생겨났을까요? 고향 소식을 들으려면 외국에 나가 봐야 한다더니, 제가 꼭 그 짝이 난 것 같네요. 물론 가는 곳마다 그 이야기를 부인하고 있습니다만, 몹시 짜증스럽고, 대체 어디서 비롯됐는지 의아할 따름입니다. 토머신 같은 처녀가 결혼식 날 버림받아 우리를 그토록 망신시킨다는 건 너무도 우스꽝스러운 일이에요. 대체 그 애가 무슨 짓을 했단 말입니까?」\n\n「그래.」 요브라이트 부인이 편지를 내려놓으며 슬프게 말했다. 「네가 그와 결혼할 수 있다 여긴다면, 그렇게 하렴. 그리고 와일디브 씨가 격식 없이 하길 바란다니, 그것도 그렇게 하고. 난 아무것도 할 수 없다. 이제 다 네 손에 달렸어. 네가 그와 함께 앵글베리로 가겠다고 이 집을 떠난 그때 네 안위에 대한 내 힘은 끝이 났으니.」 그녀가 반쯤 쓰라림에 잠겨 말을 이었다. 「내가 이 일로 너한테 의논을 받기는 하는 건지 거의 묻고 싶구나. 네가 나한테 한마디 없이 가서 그와 결혼했더라도, 난 좀처럼 화내지 못했을 거다—그저, 가엾은 것, 네가 그보다 나은 일을 할 수 없으니 말이야.」\n\n「그렇게 말씀하셔서 절 낙담시키지 마세요.」\n\n「네 말이 맞다—그러지 않으마.」\n\n「전 그를 두둔하는 게 아니에요, 이모. 사람의 본성은 약하고, 전 그가 완벽하다고 우길 만큼 눈먼 여자가 아니에요. 한땐 그렇게 여겼지만, 지금은 아니에요. 하지만 제 갈 길을 알고, 제가 그걸 안다는 걸 이모도 아시잖아요. 전 잘되기를 바라요.」\n\n「나도 그렇단다, 우리 둘 다 계속 그러자꾸나.」 요브라이트 부인이 일어서서 그녀에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. 「그럼 결혼식이, 만약 치러진다면, 클림이 집에 오는 바로 그날 아침이 되겠구나?」\n\n「네. 오빠가 오기 전에 끝나야 한다고 결정했어요. 그러고 나면 이모도 오빠 얼굴을 떳떳이 볼 수 있고, 저도 그럴 수 있어요. 우리가 숨긴 일들이 아무 문제도 안 될 거예요.」\n\n요브라이트 부인이 생각에 잠겨 고개를 끄덕여 동의하더니, 이윽고 말했다. 「내가 너를 인도해 주길 바라니? 네가 원한다면 기꺼이 그 일을 맡으마, 알다시피, 지난번처럼. 한 번 결혼 예고를 막은 마당이니 그 정도는 해야지 싶구나.」\n\n「오시라고 청하진 않을게요.」 토머신이 마지못해, 그러나 단호하게 말했다. 「불편할 거예요, 거의 분명히요. 차라리 낯선 사람들만 참석하고, 제 친척은 아무도 없는 편이 나아요. 전 그러는 게 더 좋아요. 이모의 체면에 누가 될 일은 하고 싶지 않고, 지난 일이 있고 나서 이모가 거기 계시면 제가 불편할 것 같아요. 전 그저 이모의 조카일 뿐이고, 이모가 저 때문에 더 마음 쓰셔야 할 이유는 없어요.」\n\n「그래, 그가 우릴 이겼구나.」 이모가 말했다. 「처음에 내가 맞서서 그를 굽힌 것에 대한 앙갚음으로, 그가 이런 식으로 너를 가지고 논 것만 같아 정말이지.」\n\n「오 아니에요, 이모.」 토머신이 중얼거렸다.\n\n그들은 그때 그 일에 대해 더 말하지 않았다. 곧이어 디고리 벤의 노크 소리가 들렸고, 요브라이트 부인은 현관에서 그와 만난 뒤 돌아와 무심하게 말했다. 「또 다른 구혼자가 너를 청하러 왔더구나.」\n「설마요?」\n\n「그래, 그 별난 젊은이 벤 말이다.」\n\n「제게 구애를 청한다고요?」\n\n「그래, 그래서 너무 늦었다고 했다.」\n\n토머신이 말없이 촛불 불꽃을 들여다보았다. 「가엾은 디고리!」 그녀가 말하고는, 다른 일들로 마음을 추슬렀다.\n\n이튿날은 그저 기계적인 준비 행위들로 지나갔으니, 두 여자 모두 그 일에 몰두함으로써 상황의 감정적인 측면에서 벗어나고 싶어 했다. 토머신을 위해 입을 옷가지며 다른 물건들이 새로 모아졌고, 와일디브의 아내로서의 그녀의 장래에 대한 어떤 내심의 불안도 가리려는 듯 집안의 자질구레한 일들에 관한 말들이 자주 오갔다.\n\n정해진 아침이 왔다. 와일디브와의 약속은, 흔한 시골 방식대로 둘이 함께 걸어가는 것이 누군가의 눈에 띄어 불쾌한 호기심을 살 수도 있는 일을 막기 위해, 그가 교회에서 그녀를 만나기로 한 것이었다.\n\n이모와 조카딸은 신부가 단장을 하는 침실에 함께 서 있었다. 햇빛은 닿을 수 있는 곳에서 토머신의 머리카락을 거울로 만들었으니, 그녀는 늘 머리를 땋아 늘이고 다녔다. 그것은 달력 방식에 따라 땋은 것이었다—날이 중요할수록 땋은 가닥의 수가 더 많아지는 식이었다. 평범한 일하는 날엔 세 가닥으로, 평범한 일요일엔 네 가닥으로, 메이폴 축제며 집시 놀이며 그 비슷한 때엔 다섯 가닥으로 땋았다. 몇 해 전 그녀는 결혼할 때면 일곱 가닥으로 땋겠다고 말한 적이 있었다. 오늘 그녀는 일곱 가닥으로 땋았다.\n\n「아무래도 파란 비단옷을 입는 게 좋겠다고 생각했어요.」 그녀가 말했다. 「제 결혼식 날이잖아요, 비록 그 시기에 뭔가 슬픈 데가 있을지라도요. 제 말은,」 그녀가 어떤 그릇된 인상이라도 바로잡으려 애쓰며 덧붙였다. 「그 자체가 슬프다는 게 아니라, 그 앞에 큰 실망과 괴로움이 있었다는 점에서요.」\n\n요브라이트 부인이 한숨이라 할 만한 식으로 숨을 내쉬었다. 「클림이 집에 있었으면 싶을 지경이구나.」 그녀가 말했다. 「물론 넌 그가 없는 때를 골랐지.」\n\n「얼마쯤은요. 오빠한테 다 말하지 않은 게 그에게 부당하게 굴었다는 생각이 들긴 했어요. 하지만 그를 슬프게 하지 않으려고 한 일이니, 계획을 끝까지 밀고 나가서 하늘이 맑아졌을 때 모든 이야기를 털어놓는 게 낫겠다 싶었어요.」\n\n「넌 실속 있는 작은 여인이로구나.」 요브라이트 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다. 「너와 그가—아니, 난 아무것도 바라지 않으련다. 자, 아홉 시구나.」 그녀가 아래층에서 윙 하는 소리와 댕 하는 소리를 듣고 말을 끊었다.\n\n「데이먼한테 아홉 시에 나가겠다고 했어요.」 토머신이 방에서 서둘러 나가며 말했다.\n\n이모가 뒤따랐다. 토머신이 문에서 쪽문까지 작은 길을 올라갈 때, 요브라이트 부인이 마지못한 듯 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. 「너를 혼자 보내다니 부끄러운 일이구나.」\n\n「그래야만 해요.」 토머신이 말했다.\n\n「어쨌든,」 이모가 애써 밝게 덧붙였다. 「오늘 오후에 너를 찾아가마, 케이크도 가지고 가고. 클림이\n그때까지 그 애가 돌아온다면 어쩌면 그 사람도 같이 올지 모르지. 나는 와일디브 씨에게 아무 악감정도 없다는 걸 보여주고 싶구나. 지난 일은 잊자꾸나. 자, 신의 가호가 있기를! 이런, 나는 옛 미신을 믿지 않지만 그래도 해보마.\" 그녀는 멀어져가는 처녀의 모습을 향해 슬리퍼 한 짝을 던졌고, 처녀는 돌아서서 미소 짓고는 다시 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n몇 걸음 더 가더니 그녀는 뒤를 돌아보았다. \"저를 부르셨어요, 이모?\" 그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 물었다. \"안녕히 계세요!\"\n\n미세스 예오브라이트의 지치고 젖은 얼굴을 바라보다가 걷잡을 수 없는 감정에 사로잡힌 그녀는 다시 뛰어왔고, 이모도 마주 나아가 두 사람은 다시 만났다. \"오—탬지,\" 손위 여인이 울며 말했다. \"널 보내고 싶지 않구나.\"\n\n\"저—저도—\" 토머신도 마찬가지로 무너지며 말을 꺼냈다. 그러나 슬픔을 억누르고는 다시 \"안녕히 계세요!\" 하고 말한 뒤 걸음을 이어갔다.\n\n이윽고 미세스 예오브라이트는 자그마한 형체 하나가 할퀴어대는 가시금작화 덤불 사이로 길을 헤치며 골짜기 저 위로 점점 작아져가는 것을 보았으니—무미한 갈색의 광대한 들판 한가운데 옅은 푸른빛 점 하나, 외롭고 무방비하며 오직 자신의 희망이라는 힘만으로 지켜질 뿐이었다.\n\n그러나 이 일에서 가장 나쁜 점은 풍경 속에 드러나지 않은 것이었으니, 그것은 바로 그 남자였다.\n\n토머신과 와일디브가 식을 올리기로 택한 시각은, 그녀가 같은 날 아침에 돌아오는 사촌 클림과 마주치는 어색함을 피할 수 있도록 맞춰진 것이었다. 그가 들은 이야기가 부분적으로나마 사실임을 인정하는 것은, 그 사건으로 빚어진 굴욕적인 처지가 나아지지 않는 한 괴로운 일일 터였다. 두 번째로 제단을 향해 가서 그것이 성공한 뒤에야 비로소 그녀는 고개를 들고 첫 번째 시도의 실패가 순전한 우연이었음을 입증할 수 있을 것이었다.\n\n그녀가 블룸스엔드를 떠난 지 반 시간이 채 못 되어, 예오브라이트가 반대편에서 풀밭을 가로질러 와 집 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"아침을 일찍 먹었어요.\" 그가 어머니에게 인사를 건넨 뒤 말했다. \"이제 좀 더 먹을 수 있겠어요.\"\n\n두 사람은 다시 차린 식사 앞에 마주 앉았고, 그는 토머신이 아직 내려오지 않았다고 여기는 듯, 낮고 근심 어린 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"토머신하고 와일디브 씨 일에 대해 제가 들은 게 대체 뭐죠?\"\n\n\"여러 가지로 사실이란다.\" 미세스 예오브라이트가 조용히 말했다. \"하지만 이제는 다 잘되었으면 좋겠구나.\" 그녀는 시계를 바라보았다.\n\"사실이라고요?\"\n\n\"토머신은 오늘 그 사람에게 갔단다.\"\n\n클림은 식사를 옆으로 밀쳐냈다. \"그렇다면 무슨 추문이 있었던 거고, 그게 바로 토머신을 괴롭힌 거군요. 그것 때문에 그 애가 앓아누운 거예요?\"\n\n\"그래. 추문은 아니고—불운이지. 다 이야기해주마, 클림. 화내선 안 된다, 다만 들어다오, 그러면 우리가 한 일이 다 좋자고 한 것임을 알게 될 게다.\"\n\n그러고서 그녀는 그에게 사정을 들려주었다. 그가 파리에서 돌아오기 전에 그 일에 대해 알고 있던 것이라곤, 토머신과 와일디브 사이에 정분이 있었고, 어머니가 처음에는 그것을 못마땅해했으나 이후 토머신의 항변 때문에 조금은 호의적으로 보게 되었다는 정도였다. 그러므로 그녀가 자초지종을 설명해나가자 그는 몹시 놀라고 괴로워했다.\n\n\"그래서 그 애는 네가 돌아오기 전에 혼례를 끝내야겠다고 마음먹은 게다.\" 미세스 예오브라이트가 말했다. \"너와 마주쳐 아주 괴로운 시간을 겪을 일이 없도록 말이지. 그래서 그 사람한테 간 거란다. 오늘 아침에 식을 올리기로 둘이 정했어.\"\n\n\"하지만 이해가 안 돼요.\" 예오브라이트가 일어서며 말했다. \"그 애답지 않아요. 그 애가 불운하게 돌아온 뒤 어머니가 제게 편지를 안 쓰신 건 이해가 가요. 그런데 처음에 혼례가 언제 있을 예정인지는 왜 제게 알리지 않으셨어요?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그때는 그 애한테 화가 나 있었단다. 내겐 그 애가 고집스러워 보였어. 게다가 네가 그 애 마음속에 아무것도 아니라는 걸 알고 나니, 그 애도 네 마음속에 아무것도 아니어야 한다고 다짐했지. 따지고 보면 그 애는 그저 내 조카일 뿐이라는 생각이 들더구나. 결혼해도 좋다고는 했지만, 나는 거기에 관심도 두지 않을 거고 그 일로 너를 성가시게 하지도 않겠다고 그 애한테 말했단다.\"\n\n\"성가시지 않았을 거예요. 어머니, 잘못하셨어요.\"\n\n\"네 일에 방해가 될까, 그것 때문에 네가 자리를 내던지거나 어떻게든 앞날을 망칠까 싶어서 아무 말도 안 했지. 물론 그때 둘이 제대로 격식을 갖춰 결혼했더라면 곧장 네게 알렸을 게다.\"\n\n\"탬신이 지금 우리가 여기 앉아 있는 동안에 정말로 결혼을 하고 있다니!\"\n\n\"그래. 처음처럼 또 무슨 차질이 생기지만 않는다면 말이다. 같은 남자인 걸 생각하면, 그럴 수도 있지.\"\n\n\"네, 그리고 저는 그렇게 되리라 믿어요. 그 애를 보낸 게 옳은 일이었을까요? 와일디브가 정말로 못된 작자라면요?\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그자가 안 올 테고, 그 애는 다시 집으로 돌아오겠지.\"\n\n\"좀 더 알아보셨어야죠.\"\n\n\"그런 말은 부질없다.\" 그의 어머니가 슬픔에 찬 초조한 눈빛으로 대답했다. \"지난 몇 주 동안 우리가 여기서 얼마나 힘들었는지 너는 모른다, 클림. 그런 일이 여자에게 얼마나 큰 치욕인지 너는 몰라. 이 집에서 우리가 보낸 잠 못 이루는 밤들을, 그 십일월 오일 이후로 우리 사이에 오간 모진 말들을 너는 모른다. 그런 일곱 주를 다시는 겪지 않았으면 싶구나. 탬신은 문밖에도 나가지 못했고, 나는 누구의 얼굴도 떳떳이 볼 수 없어 부끄러웠다. 그런데 이제 와서 너는, 그 곤경을 바로잡을 수 있는 유일한 일을 그 애가 하도록 둔 것을 두고 나를 탓하는구나.\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 그가 천천히 말했다. \"대체로 어머니를 탓하지는 않아요. 하지만 제겐 이게 얼마나 갑작스럽게 느껴지는지 헤아려보세요. 저는 여기서 아무것도 모르고 있다가, 느닷없이 탬지가 결혼하러 갔다는 말을 듣잖아요. 뭐, 달리 더 나은 방도가 없었나 보죠. 어머니, 그거 아세요,\" 그가 잠시 후 자신의 지난 내력에 갑자기 흥미가 동한 듯 말을 이었다. \"저는 한때 탬신을 연인으로 생각한 적이 있어요. 네, 그랬죠. 사내애들이란 참 이상하다니까요! 이번에 집에 와서 그 애를 봤을 때, 그 애가 여느 때보다 훨씬 다정해 보여서 그 시절이 떠오를 정도였어요. 특히 그 애가 몸이 안 좋았던 잔치가 있던 밤에요. 우리는 잔치를 그대로 열었잖아요—그건 그 애한테 좀 잔인한 일이 아니었나요?\"\n\n\"달라질 건 없었다. 잔치를 열기로 정해두었으니, 굳이 필요 이상으로 음울하게 만들 까닭이 없었지. 처음부터 우리끼리 틀어박혀 네게 탬신의 불운을 늘어놓는 걸로 시작했다면 형편없는 환영이 되었을 게다.\"\n\n클림은 생각에 잠긴 채 있었다. \"그 잔치를 안 여셨더라면 좋았을 뻔했어요.\" 그가 말했다. \"다른 이유들 때문에도요. 하지만 그건 하루 이틀 안에 말씀드릴게요. 지금은 탬신을 생각해야죠.\"\n\n두 사람은 침묵에 빠졌다. \"제 생각은 이래요.\" 예오브라이트가 아직 잠들지 않은 감정이 묻어나는 어조로 다시 말했다. \"이렇게 결혼시키는 건, 우리 둘 다 그 자리에 없어 그 애 기운을 북돋아주지도, 조금도 마음 써주지도 못하는 건, 탬신한테 다정한 처사가 아니에요. 그 애는 스스로를 욕되게 하지도, 그런 대접을 받을 짓을 하지도 않았어요. 혼례가 이토록 서둘러 격식 없이 치러지는 것만 해도 충분히 안된 일인데, 거기다 우리까지 멀리 떨어져 있다니요. 정말이지, 이건 거의 부끄러운 일이에요. 제가 갈게요.\"\n\n\"이맘때면 다 끝났을 게다.\" 그의 어머니가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"그들이 늦지 않았다면, 아니면 그자가—\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그들이 나오는 걸 보기엔 충분히 이르겠네요. 어쨌든 어머니가 저를 모르는 채로 두신 게 영 마음에 들지 않아요. 정말이지, 절반쯤은 그자가 그 애를 만나러 오지 못했기를 바란다니까요!\"\n\n\"그래서 그 애 평판을 망치라고?\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼요—그런다고 토머신이 망쳐지진 않아요.\"\n\n그는 모자를 집어 들고 황급히 집을 나섰다. 미세스 예오브라이트는 자못 불행한 기색으로, 깊은 생각에 잠겨 가만히 앉아 있었다. 그러나 홀로 남겨진 것은 오래가지 않았다. 몇 분 뒤 클림이 다시 돌아왔는데, 그와 함께 디고리 벤이 들어왔다.\n\n\"제가 거기 갈 시간이 안 되더라고요.\" 클림이 말했다.\n\n\"그 애는 결혼했나요?\" 미세스 예오브라이트가 레들맨 쪽으로 돌아서며 물었는데, 그 얼굴에는 바라는 마음과 바라지 않는 마음이 기묘하게 다투는 기색이 역력했다.\n\n벤이 고개를 숙였다. \"했습니다, 마님.\"\n\n\"참 이상하게 들리는군요.\" 클림이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그래서 이번에는 그자가 그 애를 실망시키지 않았나요?\" 미세스 예오브라이트가 말했다.\n\n\"그러지 않았습니다. 이제 그분 이름에는 아무 흠도 없습니다. 마님께서 거기 안 계신 걸 보고, 곧장 알려드리려고 들판을 가로질러 서둘러 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"어쩌다 거기 가 있었나요? 그건 또 어떻게 알았고요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"얼마간 그 동네에 있었는데, 그들이 들어가는 걸 봤습니다.\" 레들맨이 말했다. \"와일디브는 시계처럼 정확하게 문 앞으로 올라오더군요. 그자에게서 그런 건 기대하지 않았는데 말입니다.\" 그는 덧붙일 수도 있었으나 덧붙이지 않았으니, 자신이 그 동네에 있게 된 것이 우연이 아니라는 것, 와일디브가 토머신에 대한 권리를 되찾은 이후로 벤은 천성의 일부인 철저함으로써 이 사건의 결말을 끝까지 지켜보기로 작정했다는 것이었다.\n\n\"누가 거기 있었나요?\" 미세스 예오브라이트가 말했다.\n\n\"거의 아무도 없었습니다. 저는 멀찍이 비켜서 있어서, 그분은 저를 보지 못했습니다.\" 레들맨은 쉰 목소리로 말하며 정원 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n\"누가 그 애를 신랑에게 인도했나요?\"\n\n\"바이 양입니다.\"\n\"참으로 별일이군요! 바이 양이라니! 그건 영광으로 여겨야 할 일이겠죠, 아마도?\"\n\n\"바이 양이 누구예요?\" 클림이 말했다.\n\n\"미스토버 냅의 바이 선장 손녀란다.\"\n\n\"버드머스에서 온 오만한 처자지.\" 미세스 예오브라이트가 말했다. \"내 마음에 그리 드는 아이는 아니야. 사람들은 그 애가 마녀라고들 하지만, 물론 터무니없는 소리지.\"\n\n레들맨은 그 어여쁜 인물과 자신이 아는 사이라는 것을, 또 유스테이시아가 거기 있었던 것이 결혼이 있을 거라는 걸 알자마자 자신이 했던 약속에 따라 그녀를 데리러 갔기 때문이라는 것을 혼자만 알고 있었다. 그는 이야기를 이어가며 그저 이렇게만 말했다—\n\n\"제가 묘지 담장에 앉아 있는데 그들이 올라왔습니다, 한 사람은 이쪽에서 다른 한 사람은 저쪽에서요. 그리고 바이 양이 그 근처를 거닐며 묘비들을 들여다보고 있었습니다. 그들이 안으로 들어가자마자, 저는 그분을 워낙 잘 아는지라 그 광경을 보고 싶은 마음에 문간으로 갔습니다. 장화가 너무 시끄러워서 벗어버리고, 회랑으로 올라갔지요. 그때 보니 목사와 서기는 이미 와 있더군요.\"\n\n\"그저 그 길로 산책 나온 것뿐이었다면, 바이 양이 어쩌다 그 일에 관여하게 됐나요?\"\n\n\"달리 아무도 없었으니까요. 그분은 저보다 조금 앞서 교회 안으로 들어와 있었는데, 회랑은 아니었습니다. 목사가 시작하기 전에 둘러보았고, 가까이 있는 사람이 그분뿐이어서 손짓해 부르자 그분이 난간으로 올라갔습니다. 그 뒤에, 서명할 차례가 되자 그분은 베일을 걷어 올리고 서명했고, 탬신은 그 친절에 고마워하는 듯했습니다.\" 레들맨은 그 이야기를 생각에 잠긴 채 들려주었으니, 유스테이시아가 자신을 알아보지 못하도록 가려주던 두꺼운 베일을 걷어 올리고 차분히 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았을 때 와일디브의 변하던 안색이 그의 눈앞에 어른거렸던 것이다. \"그러고서,\" 디고리가 쓸쓸하게 말했다. \"저는 자리를 떴습니다, 탬신 예오브라이트로서의 그분 내력은 끝났으니까요.\"\n\n\"내가 가겠다고 했단다.\" 미세스 예오브라이트가 후회하듯 말했다. \"하지만 그 애는 그럴 필요 없다고 하더구나.\"\n\n\"뭐, 상관없습니다.\" 레들맨이 말했다. \"처음에 그러기로 했던 대로 마침내 일이 이루어졌으니, 신이 그분께 행복을 내려주시길 빕니다. 그럼 이만 안녕히 계십시오.\"\n\n그는 모자를 머리에 얹고 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n미세스 예오브라이트의 집 문을 나선 그 순간부터, 레들맨은 여러 달 동안 에그던 황야 안에서도 그 언저리에서도 다시는 보이지 않았다. 그는 완전히 자취를 감추었다. 그의 마차가 서 있던 가시덤불 사이의 후미진 구석은 이튿날 아침이면 여느 때처럼 텅 비어 있었고, 그가 거기 있었음을 보여줄 흔적이라곤 지푸라기 몇 가닥과 잔디 위의 약간 붉은 자국밖에 거의 남지 않았는데, 그마저도 다음번 빗줄기에 씻겨나가고 말았다.\n\n디고리가 전한 혼례 소식은, 전한 데까지는 정확했으나 한 가지 의미심장한 대목이 빠져 있었으니, 그가 교회 안에서 다소 뒤쪽 멀찍이 있었던 탓에 놓친 것이었다. 토머신이 떨리는 손으로 자기 이름을 서명하고 있을 때, 와일디브는 유스테이시아를 향해 \"이제 너에게 복수했다\"라고 분명히 말하는 듯한 눈길을 던졌다. 그녀는 낮은 목소리로 응답했는데—그것이 얼마나 진실한 말인지 그는 거의 헤아리지 못했으니—\"잘못 아셨어요. 오늘 그 애가 당신의 아내가 되는 걸 보니 더없이 진심으로 기쁘답니다.\"\n\nI.\n“내 마음이 곧 나의 왕국이라”\n\n클림 요브라이트의 얼굴에서는 미래의 전형적 용모가 희미하게 엿보였다. 앞으로 예술에 고전기古典期가 도래한다면 그 시대의 페이디아스는 이런 얼굴들을 빚어낼 것이다. 초기 문명에서 그토록 강렬했던 삶을 향한 열망을 대신하여, 인생을 견뎌내야 할 무엇으로 보는 관점이 결국에는 진보한 종족의 기질 속으로 너무도 철저히 스며들어, 그 얼굴 표정이 새로운 예술적 출발점으로 받아들여지게 될 것이다. 사람들은 이미 느끼고 있는바, 이목구비의 윤곽 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않고 정신적 근심의 자취를 제 몸 어디에도 새기지 않은 채 살아가는 사람은 현대적 감수성으로부터 너무 멀리 떨어져 있어 현대적 전형이 될 수 없다는 것이다. 육체적으로 아름다운 남자들―종족이 젊었을 적의 영광―은 이제 거의 시대착오에 가깝거니와, 어느 때고 육체적으로 아름다운 여자들 역시 마찬가지로 시대착오가 되지 않으리란 법이 있을까 의문을 품어봄직하다.\n\n진실은 이러한 듯하다. 환멸로 가득한 기나긴 세기의 행렬이 헬레니즘적 인생관을―뭐라 부르든 간에―영원히 밀어내버렸다는 것. 그리스인이 그저 어렴풋이 짐작했던 것을 우리는 잘 알고 있고, 그들의 아이스킬로스가 상상했던 것을 우리 집 아이들은 느끼고 있다. 우리가 자연법칙의 결함을 들춰내고 그 작용으로 인간이 처한 진퇴양난을 목도할수록, 전체 상황을 두고 떠들썩하게 즐기던 그 구식 흥취는 점점 더 불가능해진다.\n\n이 새로운 인식에 기초한 이상理想 속에 구현될 윤곽이라면 아마도 요브라이트의 그것과 닮았으리라. 관찰자의 눈을 멈춰 세운 것은 한 폭의 그림으로서의 그의 얼굴이 아니라 한 페이지로서의 그의 얼굴이었으니, 그것이 무엇인가가 아니라 무엇을 기록하고 있는가였다. 그의 이목구비는 상징의 관점에서 매력적이었는데, 이는 본디 흔하디흔한 소리가 언어 속에서 매력을 띠게 되고, 본디 단순하기 그지없는 형태가 글씨 속에서 흥미로워지는 것과 같았다.\n\n그는 무언가 기대를 모으던 소년이었다. 그 너머는 온통 혼돈이었다. 그가 독창적인 방식으로 성공하리라는 것이나 독창적인 방식으로 파멸하리라는 것이나 똑같이 가능성 있어 보였다. 그에 관해 단 하나 확실한 것은 자신이 태어난 환경 속에 가만히 머물러 있지는 않으리라는 점이었다.\n\n그래서 이웃 자작농들이 무심코 그의 이름을 입에 올리면 듣는 이는 이렇게 말했다. “아, 클림 요브라이트―그 사람 지금 뭘 하고 있다지?” 한 사람에 관해 본능적으로 던지는 물음이 ‘그가 무엇을 하고 있느냐’일 때면, 알고 보니 그도 우리 대다수처럼 딱히 하는 일이 없으리라는 느낌이 든다. 그가 좋은 쪽이든 나쁜 쪽이든 어떤 독특한 영역을 침범하고 있음이 틀림없다는 막연한 감각이 있다. 경건한 바람은 그가 잘 지내고 있으리란 것이요, 속으로 품은 믿음은 그가 일을 망치고 있으리란 것이다. 안락한 장사꾼 대여섯이 수레를 몰고 지나는 길에 ‘과묵한 여인’ 주막에 으레 들르곤 했는데 이 화제를 즐겨 입에 올렸다. 사실 그들은 에그던 사람은 아니었으나 긴 점토 담뱃대를 빨며 창 너머로 황야를 바라보는 동안 그 이야기를 피하기란 어려웠다. 클림은 소년 시절 황야와 너무도 깊이 얽혀 있어, 그 누구든 황야를 바라보면서 그를 떠올리지 않기란 거의 불가능했다. 그리하여 화제는 되풀이되었으니, 그가 재산과 명성을 쌓고 있다면 그에게 더없이 좋은 일이요, 그가 세상에서 비극적 인물이 되어가고 있다면 이야깃거리로 더없이 좋은 일이었다.\n\n사실인즉 요브라이트가 고향을 떠나기도 전에 그의 명성은 거북할 만큼 널리 퍼져 있었다. “명성이 능력을 앞지르면 좋지 못하다”라고 스페인의 예수회 수사 그라시안은 말했다. 여섯 살 때 그는 성경 수수께끼를 냈다. “바지를 입은 것으로 알려진 최초의 사람은 누구인가?” 그러자 황야의 맨 끄트머리에서부터 박수갈채가 울려 퍼졌다. 일곱 살 때는 수채물감이 없어 참나리 꽃가루와 까막까치밥나무 즙으로 워털루 전투를 그렸다. 열두 살이 될 무렵에는 이런 식으로 적어도 사방 2마일에 걸쳐 화가요 학자로 소문이 났다. 비슷한 처지에 놓인 다른 이들의 명성이 6,700야드를 가는 동안 자기 명성을 3,4천 야드나 퍼뜨리는 자라면 필시 무언가 됨됨이가 있는 법이다. 어쩌면 클림의 명성도 호메로스의 명성처럼 처지의 우연에 얼마간 빚지고 있었는지 모르나, 어쨌든 그는 유명했다.\n\n그는 자라났고 살아가는 데 도움을 받았다. 클라이브를 필경사로, 게이를 포목상으로, 키츠를 외과의로, 그밖에 수천 명을 수천 가지 기이한 길로 들여보낸 그 운명의 익살이, 거칠고 금욕적인 황야의 소년을 자기탐닉과 헛된 영광의 특별한 상징만을 다루는 장사로 추방해버렸다.\n\n그를 위해 이 장사를 택하게 된 자초지종은 굳이 밝힐 필요가 없다. 아버지가 돌아가셨을 때 이웃의 한 신사가 친절하게도 그 소년에게 출발의 발판을 마련해주겠노라 나섰는데, 그것이 그를 버드머스로 보내는 형태를 띠었다. 요브라이트는 그곳에 가고 싶지 않았으나 그것이 유일하게 실현 가능한 길이었다. 거기서 그는 런던으로 갔고, 거기서 얼마 안 가 파리로 가서 지금까지 머물러 있었던 것이다.\n\n무언가 기대를 모으던 인물인지라, 그가 고향에 돌아온 지 며칠 되지도 않아 그가 어째서 그토록 오래 눌러앉아 있는가에 대한 큰 호기심이 황야에 일기 시작했다. 휴가의 자연스러운 기한이 지났는데도 그는 여전히 머물러 있었다. 토머신이 결혼한 그 주가 지난 뒤 일요일 아침, 페어웨이의 집 앞에서 이발이 이루어지던 중에 이 화제를 두고 한바탕 토론이 벌어졌다. 이 시각 이날이면 그곳에서 늘 동네 이발이 행해졌고, 그다음 정오에는 주민들의 거창한 일요 목욕이 뒤따랐으며, 그것은 다시 한 시간 뒤 거창한 일요 단장으로 이어졌다. 에그던 황야에서 본격적인 일요일은 점심때나 되어야 시작되었고, 그때조차 다소 닳고 망가진 표본 같은 하루였다.\n\n이 일요일 아침의 이발은 페어웨이가 맡아 했는데, 희생자는 웃옷을 벗은 채 집 앞 장작 패는 받침대에 걸터앉고, 이웃들은 그 둘레에 둘러서서 잡담을 나누며, 가위질 뒤에 바람을 타고 솟아올라 하늘 사방으로 날아가 시야에서 사라지는 머리카락을 무심히 바라보았다. 여름이든 겨울이든 풍경은 한결같았으니, 다만 바람이 평소보다 더 사납게 몰아칠 때면 의자를 모퉁이 돌아 몇 자쯤 옮길 따름이었다. 페어웨이가 가위질 사이사이 진짜 있었던 이야기를 들려주는 동안 모자도 웃옷도 없이 바깥에 앉아 있으면서 춥다고 불평하는 것은, 곧장 사내답지 못함을 자처하는 격이었다. 그 도구가 귀 밑을 콕콕 찌르거나 빗이 목덜미를 긁어낼 때 움찔하거나 소리치거나 얼굴 근육 하나라도 움직이는 것은, 페어웨이가 이 모든 일을 거저 해준다는 점을 생각하면 예의에 심히 어긋나는 짓으로 여겨졌다. 일요일 오후에 정수리께가 피로 물든 것은 “나 머리 깎았잖아” 하는 설명으로 충분히 해명되었다.\n\n요브라이트에 관한 이야기는 그 젊은이가 그들 앞에서 황야를 가로질러 한가로이 거니는 것이 멀리 보이는 데서 비롯되었다.\n\n“딴 데서 잘나가는 사람이라면 까닭 없이 이런 데서 두세 주씩 눌러앉지 않을 거여”라고 페어웨이가 말했다. “머릿속에 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있는 거지―틀림없어.”\n\n“글쎄, 여기서 다이아몬드 가게를 차릴 수도 없는 노릇이고”라고 샘이 말했다.\n\n“여기 눌러앉을 작정이 아니었담 그 무거운 상자 둘을 왜 집에 들였겠어. 게다가 여기서 뭘 할 게 있는지는 하늘에 계신 주님이나 아실 일이지.”\n\n더 많은 추측이 오가기도 전에 요브라이트가 가까이 다가왔다. 이발하는 무리를 보고는 그쪽으로 길을 틀어 합류했다. 성큼성큼 다가와 잠시 그들의 얼굴을 유심히 살피더니 인사도 없이 말했다. “자, 여러분, 무슨 얘기를 하고 있었는지 내가 한번 맞혀보지요.”\n\n“암, 그러시구려, 정 그러고 싶으시다면”이라고 샘이 말했다.\n\n“내 얘기였죠.”\n\n“원, 안 그랬더라면야 꿈에도 그럴 생각이 없었지요”라고 페어웨이가 정직한 어조로 말했다. “허나 도련님이 먼저 꺼내셨으니 인정하리다, 요브라이트 도련님, 우리가 도련님 얘기를 하던 참이오. 자질구레한 장신구 장사로 세상이 다 아는 이름을 얻으신 양반이 어째 집구석에 처박혀 빈둥대고 계시나 하고 궁금해하던 참이지요―자, 그게 사실이오.”\n\n“말씀드리지요”라고 요브라이트가 뜻밖의 진지함을 띠고 말했다. “그 기회를 얻은 게 싫지 않습니다. 제가 고향에 돌아온 건, 이것저것 따져볼 때 다른 어디서보다 여기서 조금이나마 덜 쓸모없을 수 있기 때문입니다. 그런데 이걸 깨달은 건 얼마 안 됐어요. 처음 고향을 떠났을 땐 이곳이 신경 쓸 가치도 없는 데라고 여겼지요. 우리네 여기 삶이 경멸스럽다고 생각했어요. 장화에 구두약을 칠하는 대신 기름을 먹이고, 솔질로 웃옷 먼지를 터는 대신 회초리로 터는―이보다 더 우스꽝스러운 게 어디 있겠나, 했지요.”\n\n“그렇고말고요, 그렇고말고요!”\n\n“아니, 아니―틀렸어요, 그렇지가 않아요.”\n\n“실례지만, 그게 도련님 뜻인 줄 알았는뎁쇼?”\n\n“음, 제 생각이 바뀌면서 제 행로가 무척 우울해졌어요. 알고 보니 저는 저와 거의 아무 공통점도 없는 사람들을 닮으려 애쓰고 있더군요. 한 종류의 삶을 벗어던지고 다른 종류의 삶을 입으려 안간힘을 쓰고 있었는데, 그것은 전에 알던 삶보다 나을 것이 없었어요. 그저 다를 뿐이었지요.”\n\n“그렇소, 한참 다르고말고요”라고 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n“하긴, 파리는 사람을 사로잡는 데일 거요”라고 험프리가 말했다. “으리으리한 진열창, 나팔이며 북소리―헌데 우리는 온갖 바람과 날씨에 바깥에서 지내니―”\n\n“하지만 제 말을 오해하셨군요”라고 클림이 호소하듯 말했다. “이 모든 게 무척 우울했어요. 그런데 그다음에 깨달은 것만큼 우울하진 않았지요―제 장사가 사내가 떠맡을 수 있는 일 가운데 가장 한가롭고 가장 헛되고 가장 계집애 같은 장사라는 걸 말입니다. 그게 저를 결심하게 했어요―그걸 그만두고 내가 가장 잘 아는 사람들, 내가 가장 쓸모 있을 사람들 틈에서 이성적인 일을 좇아보자고요. 그래서 고향에 돌아온 겁니다. 제 계획을 이렇게 실행할 작정이에요. 에그던에서 되도록 가까운 곳에 학교를 열어, 이리로 걸어와 어머니 댁에서 야학을 열 수 있게 할 겁니다. 다만 처음엔 제대로 자격을 갖추려고 공부를 좀 해야겠지요. 자, 여러분, 저는 이만 가봐야겠습니다.”\n\n그러고는 클림은 황야를 가로지르던 걸음을 다시 옮겼다.\n\n“저 사람 절대 그걸 끝까지 못 해낼 거여”라고 페어웨이가 말했다. “몇 주만 지나면 세상을 달리 보는 법을 배우겠지.”\n\n“마음씨가 고운 젊은이야”라고 다른 이가 말했다. “허나 내 생각엔 제 본업이나 신경 쓰는 게 나을 거여.”\n\nII.\n새로운 행로가 실망을 부르다\n\n요브라이트는 동족을 사랑했다. 그는 대다수 사람에게 부족한 것이 부유함이 아니라 지혜를 가져다주는 종류의 지식이라고 확신했다. 그는 개인을 희생시켜 계층을 높이기보다 계층을 높이는 데 개인을 희생시키고자 했다. 더구나 그는 당장에라도 가장 먼저 희생되는 한 단위가 될 각오가 되어 있었다.\n\n전원의 삶에서 지성의 삶으로 넘어갈 때 중간 단계는 보통 적어도 둘, 흔히는 그보다 훨씬 많거니와, 그 단계들 가운데 하나는 거의 틀림없이 세속적으로 출세하는 단계다. 사회적 야심을 과도기적 국면으로 떠올리지 않고서 전원의 평온이 지적 목표로 빠르게 나아간다고 상상하기란 좀처럼 어렵다. 요브라이트의 지역적 특이함은, 그가 고매한 사유에 힘쓰면서도 여전히 검소한 생활―아니, 여러 면에서 거칠고 빈한한 생활, 그리고 시골뜨기들과의 형제애에 매달려 있었다는 점이다.\n\n그는 회개가 아니라 고양高揚을 설교의 주제로 삼은 세례 요한이었다. 정신적으로 그는 지방의 미래에 살고 있었으니, 곧 여러 면에서 당대 중심 도시 사상가들과 어깨를 나란히 하고 있었다. 이러한 발전의 상당 부분은 어쩌면 파리에서의 학구적 생활에 빚진 것일 수 있는데, 그곳에서 그는 당대에 유행하던 윤리 체계들을 접하게 되었다.\n\n이렇듯 비교적 앞선 위치 탓에 요브라이트는 불운하다 일컬어졌을 법하다. 시골 세계는 그를 받아들일 만큼 무르익지 않았던 것이다. 사람은 제 시대를 부분적으로만 앞서야 하거니와―포부에 있어 완전히 선두에 서는 것은 명성에 치명적이다. 필리포스의 호전적인 아들이 지성에서 너무도 앞서 있어 유혈 없이 문명화를 시도했더라면 그는 보이는 것의 두 배는 신과 같은 영웅이 되었으련만, 알렉산드로스라는 이름은 아무도 들어보지 못했으리라.\n\n명성을 위해서라면 그 앞섬은 주로 사물을 다루는 능력에 있어야 한다. 성공한 선전가들이 성공한 까닭은 그들이 형태로 빚어낸 교리가 곧 그들의 청중이 한동안 느껴왔으되 모양을 잡지 못하던 바로 그것이었기 때문이다. 미적 노력을 옹호하고 사회적 노력을 폄하하는 사람은, 사회적 노력이 이미 진부해진 계층에게나 이해받을 법하다. 사치에 앞서 교양이 가능한가를 두고 전원 세계에 논변을 펴는 것은 옳은 논변일지 몰라도, 인류가 오래도록 익숙해진 순서를 흐트러뜨리려는 시도다. 에그던의 은자들에게 스스로 부유해지는 과정을 거치지 않고도 평온한 포괄성에 이를 수 있다고 설교하는 요브라이트는, 고대 칼데아인들에게 대지에서 순수한 최고천最高天으로 오를 때 굳이 그 사이에 놓인 에테르의 하늘을 먼저 통과할 필요가 없다고 논하는 것과 다를 바 없었다.\n\n요브라이트의 정신은 균형이 잘 잡혀 있었던가? 아니다. 균형 잡힌 정신이란 특별한 편향을 드러내지 않는 정신이요, 그 소유자를 결코 광인으로 감금되게 하거나 이단자로 고문받게 하거나 신성모독자로 십자가에 못 박히게 하지 않으리라고 안심하고 말할 수 있는 정신이다. 또한 반대편으로는, 그 소유자가 결코 예언자로 갈채받거나 사제로 추앙받거나 왕으로 떠받들어지게 하지도 않을 정신이다. 그것이 주는 통상적 축복은 행복과 평범함이다. 그것은 로저스의 시, 웨스트의 그림, 노스의 치세, 톰라인의 영적 인도를 낳으니, 그 소유자들로 하여금 부에 이르는 길을 찾고, 잘 마무리짓고, 위엄 있게 무대에서 내려서고, 자기 침상에서 편안히 죽고, 많은 경우 마땅히 받을 만한 점잖은 기념비를 얻게 해준다. 그것은 요브라이트가 동족을 이롭게 하려고 제 장사를 내던지는 따위의 우스꽝스러운 짓을 하도록 결코 내버려두지 않았으리라.\n\n그는 길도 살피지 않은 채 집을 향해 걸었다. 황야를 잘 아는 이가 있다면 그건 클림이었다. 그는 황야의 풍경에, 그 본질에, 그 냄새에 흠뻑 배어 있었다. 그는 황야의 산물이라 해도 좋았다. 그의 눈은 그 위에서 처음 떠졌고, 기억 속 최초의 영상들이 모두 그 모습과 뒤섞여 있었으며, 그의 인생관은 그것에 물들어 있었으니, 그의 장난감은 그곳에서 주운 부싯돌 칼과 화살촉이었고―어째서 돌이 저토록 기이한 모양으로 “자라나는지” 의아해하면서―그의 꽃은 자줏빛 종 모양 꽃과 노란 가시금작화였으며, 그의 동물 왕국은 뱀과 망아지였고, 그의 사교계는 그곳을 드나드는 인간들이었다. 유스테이시아 바이가 황야를 향해 품은 갖가지 증오를 모두 취해 그것을 사랑으로 옮겨놓으면 곧 클림의 마음이 된다. 그는 걸으면서 광활한 전망을 바라보았고, 기뻤다.\n\n많은 이에게 이 에그던은 여러 세대 전에 제 세기에서 미끄러져 나와 이 세기에 투박한 물체로 끼어든 곳이었다. 그것은 한물간 것이었고, 그것을 굳이 살피려는 이는 드물었다. 네모반듯한 밭, 엮어 다듬은 산울타리, 그리고 맑은 날이면 은빛 석쇠처럼 보일 만큼 직각으로 반듯하게 물을 댄 목초지의 시대에 어찌 그렇지 않을 수 있었으랴? 말을 타고 가던 농부는 인공 목초를 보고 미소 짓고, 자라나는 곡식을 근심스레 바라보고, 벌레 먹은 순무를 보며 서글피 한숨짓다가도, 저 멀리 황야의 고지에는 찌푸린 얼굴이나 던질 뿐이었다. 그러나 요브라이트로 말하자면, 가는 길에 높은 곳에서 내려다볼 때면, 황무지를 개간하려던 시도들 가운데 더러는 경작이 한두 해 버티다 다시 절망하여 물러나고 양치류와 가시금작화 덤불이 끈질기게 제자리를 되찾은 것을 보며 야만적인 만족감에 빠지지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그는 골짜기로 내려갔고, 이내 블룸스엔드의 집에 다다랐다. 어머니는 창가 화초에서 시든 잎을 따내고 있었다. 어머니는 그가 자기 곁에 오래 머무는 의미를 이해하지 못하겠다는 듯 그를 올려다보았으니, 며칠째 그 표정을 짓고 있었다. 이발하던 무리가 내비친 호기심이 어머니에게서는 근심에 이르렀음을 그는 알아챌 수 있었다. 그러나 그의 트렁크가 도착해 그가 곧 떠나지 않으리라는 것을 암시했을 때조차 어머니는 입으로는 아무 질문도 하지 않았다. 어머니의 침묵은 말보다 더 큰 소리로 그에게 해명을 청했다.\n\n“다시는 파리로 돌아가지 않을 거예요, 어머니”라고 그가 말했다. “적어도 예전 일을 하러는요. 그 장사를 그만뒀어요.”\n\n요브라이트 부인은 고통스러운 놀라움에 몸을 돌렸다. “상자 때문에 뭔가 잘못됐구나 했지. 진작 말하지 그랬니.”\n\n“그랬어야 했죠. 하지만 제 계획을 어머니가 좋아하실지 미심쩍었어요. 저도 몇몇 점에서는 아주 분명하지가 않았고요. 저는 완전히 새로운 길을 갈 작정이에요.”\n\n“놀랍구나, 클림. 지금까지 해온 것보다 어떻게 더 잘하길 바랄 수 있단 말이냐?”\n\n“아주 쉬워요. 다만 어머니가 말씀하시는 식으로 더 잘하진 않을 겁니다. 아마 더 못한다고들 하겠지요. 하지만 저는 그 장사가 싫고, 죽기 전에 무언가 보람된 일을 하고 싶어요. 교사가 되어 그걸 하려 합니다―가난하고 무지한 이들에게, 아무도 가르쳐주지 않을 것을 가르치는 교사 말이에요.”\n\n“네 앞길을 열어주려고 그토록 애를 썼는데, 그저 부富를 향해 곧장 나아가기만 하면 되는 마당에, 가난뱅이의 교사가 되겠다고 하는구나. 네 그 공상이 너를 망칠 거다, 클림.”\n\n요브라이트 부인은 차분히 말했으나, 그 말 뒤에 깔린 감정의 힘은 그녀를 아들만큼 잘 아는 이에게는 너무도 분명했다. 그는 대답하지 않았다. 그의 얼굴에는, 반박하는 이가 기질상 논리의 손길이 닿지 못하는 곳에 있을 때―유리한 조건 아래서조차 논리란 논변의 미묘함을 담기에 너무 거친 그릇인데―찾아오는, 이해받기를 단념한 빛이 어려 있었다.\n\n저녁 식사가 끝날 때까지 이 화제에 관해 더는 말이 없었다. 그러다 어머니가, 아침 이래로 시간 간격이 전혀 없었다는 듯이 입을 열었다. “클림, 네가 그런 생각을 품고 집에 돌아온 걸 알고 나니 마음이 어지럽구나. 네가 제 발로 세상에서 뒷걸음치려 한다곤 꿈에도 생각지 못했다. 물론 나는 네가 다른 남자들처럼―그 이름값을 하는 모든 이들처럼―잘될 좋은 길에 들어섰으면 곧장 밀고 나가려니 늘 여겼지.”\n\n“어쩔 수 없어요”라고 클림이 괴로운 어조로 말했다. “어머니, 저는 그 번쩍거리는 장사가 싫어요. 이름값 하는 남자 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 세상의 절반이 타고난 비참함을 어떻게 헤쳐나갈지 떨쳐 일어나 가르쳐줄 사람이 없어 파멸해가는 걸 빤히 보면서, 그 이름값 하는 사내가 그토록 계집애 같은 식으로 시간을 허비할 수 있겠어요? 저는 아침마다 일어나 사도 바울의 말마따나 온 피조물이 고통 속에 신음하며 진통을 겪는 걸 보면서도, 부유한 여인들과 작위 있는 난봉꾼들을 상대로 번쩍거리는 사치품을 거래하고 가장 비천한 허영심에 영합하고 있어요―무엇이든 해낼 만큼 건강과 힘을 갖춘 제가 말이에요. 일 년 내내 그 일로 마음이 괴로웠고, 결국은 더는 그걸 할 수 없게 됐어요.”\n\n“어째서 너는 남들만큼 잘 해내지 못한단 말이냐?”\n\n“모르겠어요, 다만 남들이 중히 여기는 많은 것을 제가 중히 여기지 않는다는 것뿐이죠. 그게 제가 이 일을 해야 한다고 생각하는 까닭의 일부고요. 우선 제 몸은 제게 많은 걸 요구하지 않아요. 저는 진미珍味를 즐기지 못해요. 좋은 것들이 제게는 헛되이 버려지지요. 그렇다면 저는 그 결함을 이로움으로 돌려, 남들이 필요로 하는 것 없이도 지낼 수 있는 만큼 그런 것에 드는 비용을 다른 누군가에게 쓸 수 있는 거예요.”\n\n한데 요브라이트는 바로 그 본능들 가운데 얼마간을 눈앞의 여인에게서 물려받았으니, 그녀가 그를 위한답시고 아무리 감추려 한들, 논변으로는 아니더라도 감정을 통해 그녀 안에 공명을 일깨우지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀는 한결 자신 없는 투로 말했다. “그래도 네가 꾸준히 버티기만 했더라면 부유한 사람이 될 수도 있었어. 그 큰 다이아몬드 상회의 지배인이라니―사내가 그보다 더 바랄 게 뭐가 있겠니? 얼마나 신임받고 존경받는 자리냐! 너도 네 아버지를 닮아가는 모양이다. 그이처럼 너도 잘 사는 일에 싫증이 나는 게지.”\n\n“아니에요”라고 아들이 말했다. “저는 그 일에 싫증 난 게 아니라, 어머니가 그렇게 부르시는 것에 싫증이 난 거예요. 어머니, 잘 산다는 게 뭐죠?”\n\n요브라이트 부인은 손쉬운 정의에 만족하기엔 너무도 사려 깊은 여인이었으니, 플라톤이 그린 소크라테스의 “지혜란 무엇인가?”와 본디오 빌라도의 “진리란 무엇인가?”처럼, 요브라이트의 불타는 물음도 답을 얻지 못했다.\n\n그 침묵을 깬 것은 정원 문이 철컹 부딪치는 소리, 문을 똑똑 두드리는 소리, 그리고 문이 열리는 소리였다. 크리스천 캔틀이 일요일 나들이옷 차림으로 방에 나타났다.\n\n에그던에서는 손님과 주인이 마주 서게 될 즈음에는 이미 이야기의 본론에 깊숙이 들어가 있도록, 집 안에 완전히 들어서기도 전에 이야기의 서두를 시작하는 것이 관례였다. 크리스천은 문이 빗장에서 막 떨어지는 사이에 그들에게 말하고 있었다. “이렇게 어쩌다 한 번, 그것도 좀처럼 집을 안 나서는 제가, 글쎄 오늘 아침에 마침 거기 있었지 뭐예요!”\n\n“그래, 무슨 소식을 가져왔구먼, 크리스천?”이라고 요브라이트 부인이 말했다.\n\n“암요, 마녀 얘기랍니다. 식사하실 때라 들이닥쳐 죄송해유, 글쎄, ‘가서 알려드려야지, 식사가 반도 안 끝나셨겠지만’ 하고 마음먹었거든유. 정말이지 바람에 흩날리는 잎사귀처럼 부들부들 떨렸다니까유. 무슨 화라도 닥칠까유?”\n\n“글쎄―뭔데?”\n\n“오늘 아침 교회에서 다들 일어서 있었는데, 목사님이 ‘기도합시다’ 하시더라고유. ‘그럼,’ 하고 생각했지유, ‘서 있느니 무릎 꿇는 게 낫겠다.’ 그래 무릎을 꿇었는데, 게다가 다른 사람들도 다 저처럼 그분 말씀 따를 마음이 있었던가 봐유. 한 일 분도 열심히 안 했는데 글쎄 교회를 가로질러 가장 끔찍한 비명이 울리는 거예유, 누가 막 심장의 피라도 쏟은 것처럼유. 다들 벌떡 일어났는데 알고 보니 수전 넌서치가 긴 양말 바늘로 바이 아가씨를 콕 찔렀더라고유. 그 아가씨를 교회로 끌어들이기만 하면―아가씨가 교회엔 잘 안 오거든유―당장 그러겠다고 별러온 대로유. 수전은 몇 주를 이 기회만 기다렸대유, 아가씨 피를 뽑아서 그토록 오래 이어진 제 아이들 홀림을 끝장내려고유. 수는 아가씨를 따라 교회에 들어가 그 옆에 앉았다가, 기회가 나자마자 양말 바늘을 아가씨 팔에 푹 찔러 넣은 거지유.”\n\n“맙소사, 끔찍해라!”라고 요브라이트 부인이 말했다.\n\n“수가 어찌나 깊이 찔렀던지 아가씨가 까무러쳤어유. 저는 우리 사이에 무슨 소동이 날까 겁이 나서 콘트라베이스 뒤에 숨어 더는 못 봤어유. 하지만 사람들이 아가씨를 바깥바람 쐬게 들고 나갔다고들 하더라고유. 그런데 수를 찾으려고 둘러보니 사라지고 없었대유. 그 아가씨가 어찌나 비명을 질렀던지, 가엾어라! 목사님은 제의祭衣 차림으로 손을 들고 ‘앉으세요, 선량한 여러분, 앉으세요!’ 하셨지만, 도무지 누구 하나 앉질 않았어유. 아, 그리고 제가 뭘 알아냈게유, 요브라이트 부인? 목사님은 제의 밑에 양복을 받쳐 입으셨더라고유!―팔을 들 때 검은 소맷자락이 보였거든유.”\n\n“잔인한 짓이야”라고 요브라이트가 말했다.\n\n“그래”라고 어머니가 말했다.\n\n“나라에서 들여다봐야 할 일이에유”라고 크리스천이 말했다. “저기 험프리가 오는 모양인데유.”\n\n험프리가 들어왔다. “자, 소식 들으셨소? 아니, 들으신 모양이네. 참 묘한 일이지, 에그던 사람 하나가 교회에 갈 때마다 무슨 괴상한 일이 꼭 벌어진다니까. 우리 중 누가 마지막으로 거기 갔던 게 이웃 페어웨이가 가을에 갔을 때인데, 그날이 바로 부인이 결혼 공시를 막으신 날이었잖수, 요브라이트 부인.”\n\n“그 잔인하게 당한 아가씨는 집까지 걸어갈 수 있었답니까?”라고 클림이 물었다.\n\n“나아져서 멀쩡히 집에 갔다고들 합디다. 자, 이 얘길 했으니 나도 집으로 가봐야겠소.”\n\n“나도요”라고 험프리가 말했다. “이제야 사람들이 그 아가씨를 두고 하는 말에 뭔가가 있는지 알게 되겠구려.”\n\n그들이 다시 황야로 나가버리자 요브라이트는 어머니에게 조용히 말했다. “제가 너무 일찍 교사가 되겠다고 한 걸까요?”\n\n“교사도, 선교사도, 그런 사람들도 다 있어야 마땅하지”라고 어머니가 대답했다. “하지만 내가 너를 이런 삶에서 끌어내 더 풍요로운 무언가로 올려놓으려 애쓰는 것도 마땅하고, 네가 다시 돌아와 마치 내가 도무지 애쓰지 않았던 것처럼 되지 않는 것도 마땅하단다.”\n\n그날 늦게 잔디 베는 일꾼 샘이 들어왔다. “뭐 좀 빌리러 왔어유, 요브라이트 부인. 언덕 위 미인한테 무슨 일이 있었는지 들으셨지유?”\n\n“그래, 샘. 대여섯이 우리한테 얘기해줬어.”\n\n“미인?”이라고 클림이 말했다.\n\n“예, 꽤 잘생긴 편이지유”라고 샘이 대답했다. “원! 그런 여자가 저 위에 와서 산다는 게 세상에서 가장 묘한 일 중 하나라고 온 동네가 입을 모은다니까유.”\n\n“검은 머리던가, 금발이던가?”\n“원, 스무 번은 봤는데도 그건 통 떠오르질 않네유.”\n\n“탬신보다 가무잡잡하지”라고 요브라이트 부인이 중얼거렸다.\n\n“말하자면 아무것도 통 마음에 두지 않는 여자라고 할까유.”\n\n“그럼 우울한 사람인가?”라고 클림이 물었다.\n\n“혼자 시무룩하게 돌아다니고, 사람들과 어울리지 않아유.”\n\n“모험을 즐기는 아가씬가?”\n\n“제가 알기론 아니에유.”\n\n“이 외진 곳에서 무슨 자극이라도 얻으려고 젊은 사내들 놀음에 끼지도 않고?”\n“안 끼어유.”\n\n“이를테면 가면극 같은 거 말이야?”\n\n“안 해유. 그 아가씬 생각하는 게 달라유. 차라리 그 아가씨 마음은 여기서 멀리 떨어진 데, 결코 알지 못할 귀족 나리들과 부인들, 다시는 보지 못할 저택들에 가 있다고 해야겠지유.”\n\n클림이 유달리 흥미로워하는 것을 알아챈 요브라이트 부인이 다소 불안한 듯 샘에게 말했다. “자네는 우리 대부분보다 그 아가씨한테서 더 많은 걸 보는구먼. 바이 아가씨는 내 보기에 매력을 부리기엔 너무 게을러. 그 애가 제게든 남에게든 무슨 쓸모가 있다는 말은 들어본 적이 없어. 착한 아가씨라면 에그던에서조차 마녀 취급은 안 받지.”\n\n“말도 안 돼요―그건 어느 쪽으로도 아무것도 증명하지 못해요”라고 요브라이트가 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 물론 저야 그런 미묘한 건 모르지유”라고 샘이 자칫 불쾌해질 수 있는 논쟁에서 물러나며 말했다. “그 아가씨가 어떤 사람인지는 세월이 일러줄 때까지 기다려봐야겠지유. 제가 정말로 들른 용건은 이거예유, 댁에 있는 가장 길고 튼튼한 밧줄을 좀 빌리려고유. 선장님 두레박이 우물에 빠져서 물이 없어 곤란하대유. 오늘은 사내들이 다 집에 있어서 두레박을 꺼내드릴 수 있을 것 같아유. 수레용 밧줄이 벌써 셋 있긴 한데, 바닥까지 닿질 않아유.”\n\n요브라이트 부인은 헛간에서 찾을 수 있는 밧줄은 무엇이든 가져가도 좋다고 일러주었고, 샘은 찾으러 나갔다. 그가 문을 지나칠 때 클림이 합류해 문간까지 그와 동행했다.\n\n“그 젊은 마녀 아가씨는 미스트오버에 오래 머물 작정인가?”라고 그가 물었다.\n\n“그럴 것 같은데유.”\n\n“그 아가씨를 그렇게 못살게 굴다니 어쩌면 그리 잔인할까. 몹시 괴로웠겠지―몸보다 마음이 더.”\n\n“참 못된 짓이었지유―게다가 그렇게 잘생긴 아가씨를. 한번 보셔야 해유, 요브라이트 도련님, 멀리서 오신 젊은 분이고, 게다가 우리 대부분보다 나이값에 보여줄 것도 좀 더 있으신데.”\n\n“그 아가씨가 아이들을 가르치고 싶어 할 것 같은가?”라고 클림이 말했다.\n\n샘은 고개를 가로저었다. “그것과는 영 다른 부류의 사람이지 싶은데유.”\n\n“아, 그냥 문득 떠오른 생각이야. 물론 만나서 의논해봐야 알 일이지―한데 그게 쉬운 일은 아니야, 우리 집안과 그 집안이 그리 사이가 좋지 않으니.”\n\n“어떻게 하면 그 아가씨를 만나실 수 있는지 일러드릴게유, 요브라이트 도련님”이라고 샘이 말했다. “우리가 오늘 밤 여섯 시에 그 댁에서 두레박을 갈고리로 건져 올릴 참인데, 도련님이 거들어주실 수 있잖아유. 대여섯이 오긴 하는데 우물이 깊어서, 그런 차림으로 나서는 게 꺼림칙하지 않으시다면 한 사람 더 있으면 쓸모가 있을 거예유. 그 아가씨도 틀림없이 근처를 거닐 테고요.”\n\n“생각해보지”라고 요브라이트가 말했다. 그렇게 그들은 헤어졌다.\n\n그는 그 일을 꽤 곰곰이 생각했으나, 그때 집 안에서는 유스테이시아에 관해 더는 아무 말도 오가지 않았다. 미신에 바쳐진 이 낭만적 순교자와, 보름달 아래서 그가 이야기를 나눈 그 우울한 가면극 배우가 한 사람이며 같은 사람인지는 아직 풀리지 않은 수수께끼로 남아 있었다.\n\nIII.\n세월에 닳은 한 편의 극, 그 첫 막\n\n오후는 화창했고, 요브라이트는 어머니와 함께 한 시간 동안 황야를 거닐었다. 블룸스엔드 골짜기를 이웃 골짜기와 가르는 높은 능선에 이르자 두 사람은 걸음을 멈추고 둘러보았다. 한쪽으로는 ‘과묵한 여인’ 주막이 황야의 나지막한 가장자리에 보였고, 저 멀리 다른 쪽으로는 미스트오버 냅이 솟아 있었다.\n\n“토머신을 찾아가실 거죠?”라고 그가 물었다.\n\n“그래. 하지만 이번엔 네가 안 와도 된다”라고 어머니가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 저는 여기서 갈라설게요, 어머니. 미스트오버에 갈 거예요.”\n\n요브라이트 부인은 캐묻는 눈길로 그를 돌아보았다.\n\n“선장님 우물에서 두레박을 꺼내는 걸 도우러 가는 거예요”라고 그가 이어 말했다. “워낙 깊은 우물이라 제가 쓸모가 있을지 모르죠. 그리고 그 바이 아가씨를 보고 싶기도 하고요―미모 때문이라기보다는 다른 이유로요.”\n\n“꼭 가야겠니?”라고 어머니가 물었다.\n\n“그러려고요.”\n\n그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다. “어쩔 도리가 없지”라고 클림의 어머니는 그가 멀어지는 동안 침울하게 중얼거렸다. “저 둘은 결국 서로 보고 말 거야. 샘이 제 소식을 우리 집 말고 다른 집들에 가지고 갔더라면 좋았으련만.”\n\n클림의 멀어지는 형상은 가는 길에 작은 언덕들을 오르내리며 점점 더 작아졌다. “저 애는 마음이 여려”라고 요브라이트 부인은 그를 지켜보며 혼잣말을 했다. “안 그랬다면야 별일 아니었을 텐데. 저렇게 가는 꼴이라니!”\n\n과연 그는 가시금작화 위를 마치 목숨이라도 걸린 듯 한 일자로 곧장, 의지를 다잡고 걸어가고 있었다. 그의 어머니는 길게 숨을 내쉬고는 토머신을 찾아가려던 것을 단념하고 발길을 돌렸다. 저녁의 안개막이 골짜기들에 어렴풋한 그림을 그리기 시작했으나, 높은 땅은 여전히 기우는 겨울 햇살에 빗질당하듯 비추어졌으니, 그 햇살은 앞으로 나아가는 클림을 비추었고, 둘레의 토끼며 들지빠귀가 모두 그를 지켜보는 가운데, 길게 늘어진 그림자 하나가 그의 앞에서 나아가고 있었다.\n\n선장의 거처를 요새처럼 두른 가시금작화 덮인 둑과 도랑에 가까이 다가가자 안에서 말소리가 들렸으니, 작업이 이미 시작되었음을 알리는 소리였다. 옆문 출입구에서 그는 걸음을 멈추고 안을 넘겨다보았다.\n\n건장한 사내 대여섯이 우물 입구로부터 한 줄로 늘어서서, 우물의 도르래를 넘어 저 아래 깊은 곳으로 드리워진 밧줄을 붙들고 있었다. 페어웨이는 사고에 대비해 몸에 가는 밧줄 한 가닥을 둘러 기둥 하나에 단단히 묶고서, 우물 입구 위로 몸을 기울인 채, 오른손으로는 우물 속으로 내려간 수직 밧줄을 움켜쥐고 있었다.\n\n“자, 조용히 하시오, 여러분”이라고 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n이야기가 그치자 페어웨이는 마치 반죽을 휘젓듯 밧줄을 빙글빙글 돌렸다. 일 분쯤 지나자 둔탁하게 물 첨벙이는 소리가 우물 바닥에서 울려 올라왔다. 그가 밧줄에 먹인 나선형 비틀림이 저 아래 갈고리에까지 닿은 것이었다.\n\n“당겨!”라고 페어웨이가 말했다. 밧줄을 붙들고 있던 사내들이 그것을 바퀴 위로 거두어들이기 시작했다.\n\n“뭔가 걸린 것 같소”라고 당기던 이들 중 하나가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 꾸준히 당기게”라고 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n그들은 점점 더 거두어들였고, 마침내 우물 아래로 규칙적으로 물방울 떨어지는 소리가 들렸다. 두레박이 높이 올라올수록 그 소리는 더 또렷해졌고, 이윽고 150피트의 밧줄이 거두어졌다.\n\n그러자 페어웨이는 등불을 켜 다른 끈에 매달아, 먼저 내린 밧줄 옆으로 우물 속에 내리기 시작했다. 클림이 앞으로 나아가 아래를 내려다보았다. 등불이 내려갈수록 우물 벽에는 한 해의 계절 따위는 알지 못하는 기묘하게 축축한 잎사귀와 별난 성질의 이끼들이 드러났고, 마침내 그 빛이 눅눅하고 어두운 공기 속에 매달려 흔들리는 밧줄과 두레박의 뒤엉킨 덩어리 위로 떨어졌다.\n\n“테두리 가장자리만 겨우 걸었으니―제발, 조심해!”라고 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n그들은 더없이 조심스럽게 당겼고, 마침내 젖은 두레박이 그들 두 자쯤 아래에 모습을 드러냈으니, 마치 죽은 벗이 다시 땅 위로 돌아온 듯했다. 서너 개의 손이 뻗어 나왔다. 그 순간 밧줄이 홱 풀리고, 바퀴가 윙 돌고, 맨 앞의 두 사람이 뒤로 나뒹굴고, 떨어지는 물체가 우물 벽을 따라 멀어지며 부딪치는 소리가 들리더니, 바닥에서 우레 같은 소동이 일었다. 두레박이 다시 사라진 것이다.\n\n“빌어먹을 두레박!”이라고 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n“다시 내려”라고 샘이 말했다.\n\n“이렇게 오래 구부리고 있었더니 숫양 뿔처럼 뻣뻣하구먼”이라고 페어웨이가 몸을 일으켜 관절이 우두둑 소리 나도록 기지개를 켜며 말했다.\n\n“몇 분 쉬세요, 티머시”라고 요브라이트가 말했다. “제가 자리를 대신하지요.”\n\n갈고리를 다시 내렸다. 저 멀리 물 위에 갈고리가 가볍게 부딪치는 소리가 마치 입맞춤처럼 그들의 귀에 닿았고, 이에 요브라이트는 무릎을 꿇고 우물 위로 몸을 기울여 페어웨이가 하던 대로 갈고리를 빙빙 돌리며 끌기 시작했다.\n\n“저 사람한테 밧줄을 둘러요―위험해요!”라고 그들 위 어딘가에서 부드럽고 근심 어린 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n모두가 돌아보았다. 말한 이는 한 여인으로, 위층 창에서 아래 무리를 내려다보고 있었으니, 창유리는 서쪽에서 비쳐드는 불그스름한 노을에 타오르고 있었다. 그녀의 입술은 벌어져 있었고, 잠시 자기가 어디 있는지조차 잊은 듯했다.\n\n그리하여 그의 허리에 밧줄이 둘러졌고, 작업은 계속되었다. 다음번 당길 때는 무게가 무겁지 않았으니, 알고 보니 두레박에서 떨어져나온 밧줄 한 사리만 건졌을 따름이었다. 그 뒤엉킨 덩어리를 뒤편으로 던져버렸다. 험프리가 요브라이트의 자리를 넘겨받았고, 갈고리는 다시 내려졌다.\n\n요브라이트는 건져 올린 밧줄 더미께로 물러나 사색에 잠겼다. 그 여인의 목소리와 그 우울한 가면극 배우의 목소리가 동일하다는 데는 한순간의 의심도 없었다. “참으로 사려 깊은 사람이로군!”이라고 그는 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n유스테이시아는 자기 외침이 아래 무리에게 미친 효과를 깨닫고 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 요브라이트가 아쉬운 듯 창을 살폈으나 더는 그곳에 보이지 않았다. 그가 거기 서 있는 동안 우물가의 사내들은 별 탈 없이 두레박을 건져 올리는 데 성공했다. 그들 중 하나가 선장에게 우물 도구를 고치는 데 어떤 지시를 내리고 싶은지 물으러 갔다. 선장은 집을 비운 것으로 드러났고, 유스테이시아가 문간에 나타나더니 밖으로 나왔다. 그녀는 클림의 안전을 염려하던 말 속의 생기 넘치던 강렬함과는 사뭇 동떨어진, 편안하고 위엄 있는 차분함에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n“오늘 밤 여기서 물을 길을 수 있을까요?”라고 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“안 됩니다, 아가씨. 두레박 밑바닥이 완전히 떨어져나갔어유. 이제 더는 어쩔 수 없으니 그만두고 내일 아침에 다시 오겠어유.”\n\n“물이 없네”라고 그녀가 돌아서며 중얼거렸다.\n\n“블룸스엔드에서 좀 보내드릴게요”라고 클림이, 사내들이 물러가는 가운데 앞으로 나서며 모자를 들어 올리며 말했다.\n\n요브라이트와 유스테이시아는 한순간 서로를 바라보았으니, 마치 둘 다 어떤 달빛 어린 정경이 둘 모두에게 공유되었던 그 짧은 순간들을 마음에 떠올린 듯했다. 그 눈길과 더불어 그녀의 차분하게 굳어 있던 이목구비가 우아함과 따스함의 표정으로 승화되었으니, 그것은 마치 눈부신 한낮이 한두 초 만에 석양의 위엄으로 떠오르는 듯했다.\n\n“고마워요, 하지만 그러실 것까진 없을 거예요”라고 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“그래도 물이 없으시잖아요?”\n\n“글쎄요, 제가 물이 없다고 한 거예요”라고 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며, 마치 그 일이 숙고를 요하는 일이기라도 한 듯 긴 속눈썹의 눈꺼풀을 들어 올리며 말했다. “하지만 할아버지는 그걸 충분한 물이라 부르신답니다. 제 말이 무슨 뜻인지 보여드리죠.”\n\n그녀가 몇 자쯤 자리를 옮기자 클림이 뒤따랐다. 경계 둑을 오르도록 계단이 만들어진 울타리 모퉁이에 이르자, 그녀는 우물 쪽으로 나른하게 움직이던 모습 뒤끝이라 기이하게 느껴질 만큼 가뿐히 뛰어올랐다. 이는 우연찮게도 그녀의 겉보기 나른함이 힘이 부족해서가 아님을 드러내 보였다.\n\n클림이 그녀 뒤를 따라 올라가다 둑 꼭대기에 둥글게 탄 자국이 있는 것을 보았다. “재인가요?”라고 그가 말했다.\n\n“네”라고 유스테이시아가 말했다. “지난 십일월 오일에 여기서 작은 모닥불을 피웠는데, 그 흔적이에요.”\n\n바로 그 자리에 그녀가 와일디브를 끌어내려고 지폈던 불이 타올랐던 것이다.\n\n“우리한테 물이라곤 저런 종류뿐이에요”라고 그녀가 둑 바깥쪽에, 동공 없는 흰자위처럼 놓인 못에 돌멩이 하나를 던지며 이어 말했다. 돌은 첨벙 소리를 내며 떨어졌으나, 지난번 그곳에서처럼 못 건너편에 와일디브가 나타나지는 않았다. “할아버지 말씀이 바다에서 이십 년 넘게 저것보다 두 배는 나쁜 물로 사셨대요”라고 그녀가 말을 이었다. “그래서 비상시에 여기 우리한테는 그 정도면 더없이 충분하다고 여기시지요.”\n\n“음, 사실 이맘때면 이런 못의 물에는 불순물이 없어요. 방금 빗물이 고인 것뿐이거든요.”\n\n그녀는 고개를 가로저었다. “저는 어떻게든 황무지에서 버티고 있긴 하지만, 못물을 마실 수는 없어요”라고 그녀가 말했다.\n\n클림은 이제 사내들이 집으로 돌아가 텅 빈 우물 쪽을 바라보았다. “샘물을 길어 오라고 하기엔 길이 멀군요”라고 그가 한참 침묵 끝에 말했다. “하지만 이 못물이 싫으시다니, 제가 직접 좀 길어드려보지요.” 그는 우물로 돌아갔다. “그래요, 이 들통을 매달면 될 것 같아요.”\n\n“하지만 사내들한테 길어달라고 폐를 끼치지 않으려는 마당에, 양심상 당신께 그러시게 둘 수는 없어요.”\n\n“번거로움 따위는 조금도 개의치 않아요.”\n\n그는 들통을 기다란 밧줄 사리에 단단히 매어 바퀴 위에 걸치고는, 밧줄이 손가락 사이로 미끄러져 나가게 하며 아래로 내렸다. 그러나 멀리 내려가기 전에 그는 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n“먼저 끝을 단단히 매어둬야겠어요, 안 그러면 통째로 잃을 수도 있으니”라고 그가, 가까이 다가와 있던 유스테이시아에게 말했다. “제가 매는 동안 이걸 잠깐 잡아주시겠어요―아니면 하녀를 부를까요?”\n\n“제가 잡을게요”라고 유스테이시아가 말했다. 그가 밧줄을 그녀 손에 쥐여주고는 끝을 찾으러 갔다.\n\n“이걸 미끄러뜨려 내려도 되겠죠?”라고 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“멀리까지 내려가게 두지 않으시는 게 좋아요”라고 클림이 말했다. “보시면 알겠지만 훨씬 무거워질 테니까요.”\n\n그러나 유스테이시아는 밧줄을 풀어 내리기 시작했다. 그가 매고 있는 동안 그녀가 외쳤다. “멈출 수가 없어요!”\n\n클림이 그녀 곁으로 달려가, 풀린 부분을 곧추선 기둥에 둘러 감아야만 밧줄을 멈춰 세울 수 있음을 알았으니, 그러자 밧줄이 홱 멈췄다. “다치셨어요?”\n\n“네”라고 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“많이요?”\n\n“아니요, 그렇진 않은 것 같아요.” 그녀가 두 손을 펴 보였다. 한 손에서 피가 나고 있었으니, 밧줄이 살갗을 벗겨낸 것이었다. 유스테이시아는 손을 손수건으로 감쌌다.\n\n“놓으셨어야죠”라고 요브라이트가 말했다. “왜 안 놓으셨어요?”\n\n“당신이 붙들고 있으라 하셨잖아요…… 오늘 다친 게 이번이 두 번째예요.”\n\n“아, 그렇지요. 그 일은 저도 들었어요. 제 고향 에그던이 부끄럽습니다. 교회에서 입으신 상처가 심한가요, 바이 아가씨?”\n\n클림의 어조에 동정이 어찌나 넘쳐흘렀던지 유스테이시아는 천천히 소매를 걷어 올려 둥글고 흰 팔을 드러냈다. 그 매끄러운 살결 위에 밝은 붉은 점 하나가 돋아 있었으니, 마치 파로스 대리석 위의 루비 같았다.\n\n“여기예요”라고 그녀가 그 점에 손가락을 갖다 대며 말했다.\n\n“그 여자가 비열하게 굴었군요”라고 클림이 말했다. “바이 선장님이 그 여자를 벌받게 하지 않으실까요?”\n\n“바로 그 일로 집을 비우셨어요. 제게 그런 마법적인 명성이 있는 줄은 몰랐네요.”\n\n“그리고 까무러치셨다죠?”라고 클림이, 그 진홍빛 작은 구멍을 마치 입 맞춰 낫게 해주고 싶다는 듯 바라보며 말했다.\n\n“네, 무서웠거든요. 교회에 안 간 지 오래됐었어요. 그리고 이제 아주 오랫동안 다시는 안 갈 거예요―어쩌면 영영. 이 일이 있고 나선 사람들 눈을 마주할 수가 없어요. 끔찍하게 모욕적이라고 생각지 않으세요? 그 뒤로 몇 시간이나 죽어버렸으면 했지만, 지금은 괜찮아요.”\n\n“저는 이런 거미줄을 걷어내러 왔어요”라고 요브라이트가 말했다. “저를 도와주시겠어요―수준 높은 가르침으로요? 우리가 저들에게 큰 도움이 될 수 있을 거예요.”\n\n“그러고 싶은 마음이 썩 들지 않네요. 저는 제 동족을 그리 사랑하지 않아요. 때로는 아주 미워하기까지 해요.”\n\n“그래도 제 계획을 들으시면 흥미를 느끼실지 모른다고 생각해요. 사람을 미워하는 건 아무 소용 없어요―무언가를 미워하려거든, 그들을 만들어낸 것을 미워하셔야죠.”\n\n“자연을 말씀하시는 건가요? 저는 이미 자연을 미워해요. 하지만 당신 계획은 언제든 기꺼이 듣겠어요.”\n\n이제 상황은 마무리되었고, 다음에 자연스러운 일은 두 사람이 헤어지는 것이었다. 클림도 이를 잘 알았고 유스테이시아도 마무리하는 몸짓을 했으나, 그는 한마디 더 할 말이 있다는 듯 그녀를 바라보았다. 어쩌면 그가 파리에서 살아본 적이 없었더라면 그 말은 결코 나오지 않았으리라.\n\n“우리 전에 만난 적이 있죠”라고 그가 필요 이상으로 흥미를 띠고 그녀를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n“저는 인정 못 해요”라고 유스테이시아가 억누른, 고요한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n“하지만 제가 생각하고 싶은 대로 생각할 수야 있죠.”\n\n“네.”\n\n“여기서 외로우시죠.”\n\n“저는 황야를 견딜 수가 없어요, 자줏빛으로 물드는 철만 빼고는요. 황야는 제게 잔인한 폭군이에요.”\n\n“그렇게 말씀하실 수 있나요?”라고 그가 물었다. “제게는 더없이 기운을 북돋우고, 힘을 주고, 마음을 달래주는 곳인데요. 저는 세상 어디보다 이 언덕에서 살고 싶어요.”\n\n“화가들한테야 충분히 좋겠죠. 하지만 저는 그림 그리는 법은 결코 배우지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“그리고 바로 저기 아주 진기한 드루이드 돌이 하나 있어요.” 그가 가리키는 방향으로 조약돌 하나를 던졌다. “자주 보러 가시나요?”\n\n“그런 진기한 드루이드 돌이 있는 줄도 몰랐어요. 제가 아는 건 파리에 큰길들이 있다는 거예요.”\n\n요브라이트는 생각에 잠겨 땅을 내려다보았다. “그건 많은 걸 뜻하죠”라고 그가 말했다.\n\n“정말 그래요”라고 유스테이시아가 말했다.\n\n“저도 도시의 북적임을 똑같이 갈망하던 때가 기억나요. 대도시에서 오 년만 지내면 그건 완벽하게 치유될 거예요.”\n\n“하늘이 제게 그런 치유를 내려주시길! 자, 요브라이트 씨, 저는 안으로 들어가 다친 손에 약이나 발라야겠어요.”\n\n두 사람은 헤어졌고, 유스테이시아는 짙어가는 그늘 속으로 사라졌다. 그녀는 온갖 것으로 가득 찬 듯했다. 그녀의 과거는 백지였고, 그녀의 삶은 이제 막 시작된 것이었다.\n이 만남이 클림에게 미친 영향을 그는 한참 뒤에야 온전히 깨달았다. 집으로 걸어 돌아오는 동안 그가 느낀 가장 또렷한 감각은, 자기 계획이 어쩐지 영광스러워졌다는 것이었다. 한 아름다운 여인이 그 계획에 얽혀든 것이었다.\n\n집에 도착하자 그는 서재로 쓸 방으로 올라가, 저녁 내내 상자에서 책을 꺼내 선반에 정리하는 데 몰두했다. 다른 상자에서는 등잔과 기름통을 꺼냈다. 등잔 심지를 다듬고 탁자를 정돈하고는 말했다. “자, 이제 시작할 준비가 됐다.”\n\n이튿날 아침 그는 일찍 일어나, 아침 식사 전에 두 시간을 등잔불에 의지해 읽었고―오전 내내, 오후 내내 읽었다. 마침 해가 지려 할 즈음 눈이 피로해져 그는 의자에 등을 기댔다.\n\n그의 방은 집 앞마당과 그 너머 황야의 골짜기를 굽어보았다. 겨울 햇살의 가장 낮은 빛줄기가 집의 그림자를 말뚝 울타리 너머로, 황야의 풀밭 가장자리를 가로질러, 골짜기 저 위까지 드리웠으니, 그곳에서는 굴뚝의 윤곽과 둘레 나무 우듬지들의 윤곽이 길고 어두운 갈래로 뻗어 있었다. 온종일 앉아 일했던 터라 그는 어두워지기 전에 언덕을 한 바퀴 거닐기로 마음먹고, 곧장 밖으로 나가 황야를 가로질러 미스트오버 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n그가 다시 정원 문에 나타난 것은 한 시간 반 뒤였다. 집의 덧창은 닫혀 있었고, 온종일 정원에 거름을 수레로 나르던 크리스천 캔틀은 집으로 가버린 뒤였다. 안으로 들어서니 어머니가 그를 오래 기다리다 식사를 끝낸 것을 알았다.\n\n“어디 갔었니, 클림?”이라고 어머니가 대뜸 물었다. “이런 시간에 나간다고 왜 말하지 않았어?”\n\n“황야에 다녀왔어요.”\n\n“거기 올라가면 유스테이시아 바이를 만날 텐데.”\n\n클림은 잠시 멈칫했다. “네, 오늘 저녁에 그녀를 만났어요”라고 그가, 마치 정직을 지켜야 한다는 절대적 필요에 떠밀려 내뱉듯이 말했다.\n\n“그랬을까 했다.”\n\n“약속한 건 아니었어요.”\n\n“그래, 그런 만남이란 결코 약속이 아니지.”\n\n“하지만 화나신 건 아니죠, 어머니?”\n\n“화가 안 났다곤 차마 말 못 하겠다. 화가 났냐고? 아니다. 하지만 장래가 촉망되는 사람들이 세상을 실망시키게 만드는 그 끌어내리는 힘이 흔히 어떤 성질의 것인지 생각하면, 마음이 불안하구나.”\n\n“그렇게 느끼시는 건 칭찬받을 만한 일이에요, 어머니. 하지만 그 일로 저 때문에 불안해하실 필요는 없다고 분명히 말씀드릴 수 있어요.”\n\n“네 생각이며 네 새 변덕을 떠올리면 말이다”라고 요브라이트 부인이 다소 힘주어 말했다. “일 년 전만큼 마음이 편하지가 않은 게 당연하지. 파리며 여기저기서 매력적인 여인들을 익히 봐온 사내가 황야의 처녀 하나한테 그토록 쉽사리 휘둘린다는 게 내겐 도무지 믿기지 않는다. 다른 길로 가도 되었을 텐데.”\n\n“온종일 공부하던 참이었어요.”\n\n“음, 그래”라고 어머니가 한결 기대를 담아 덧붙였다. “네가 정말로 걷던 길을 미워하기로 작정했다니, 교사로 잘 풀려 그렇게 출세할 수도 있겠다고 생각하던 참이다.”\n\n요브라이트는 이 생각을 흩뜨리고 싶지 않았으나, 그의 계획은 청소년 교육을 그저 사회적 출세의 통로로 삼는 것과는 한참 동떨어진 것이었다. 그에게는 그런 욕망이 없었다. 그는 인간 전반의 처지가 지닌 냉혹함이 처음으로 분명해지는, 젊은이 인생의 한 단계에 이르러 있었으니, 이를 깨달으면 야심이 잠시 멈춰 서는 법이다. 프랑스에서는 이 단계에서 스스로 목숨을 끊는 일이 드물지 않거니와, 영국에서는 우리가 훨씬 더 잘 처신하든가, 경우에 따라서는 훨씬 더 못 처신한다.\n\n이 젊은이와 그 어머니 사이의 사랑은 이제 기이하게도 눈에 보이지 않았다. 사랑이란, 세속적일수록 덜 드러내는 법이라 할 만하다. 절대적으로 파괴할 수 없는 형태에 이르면, 그것은 스스로를 드러내는 일체의 표현이 고통스러운 깊이에 다다른다. 이 두 사람이 그러했다. 그들 사이의 대화를 누가 엿들었더라면 이렇게 말했으리라. “저 둘은 서로 어쩌면 저리 냉랭할까!”\n\n미래를 교직에 바치겠다는 그의 이론과 바람은 요브라이트 부인에게 인상을 남겼다. 하긴 어찌 안 그렇겠는가, 그가 그녀의 일부인 것을―두 사람의 담론이 마치 같은 몸의 오른손과 왼손 사이에서 오가는 듯한 것을 생각하면. 그는 논변으로 그녀에게 가닿기를 단념했었거니와, 말이 고함보다 우월한 만큼이나 말보다 우월한 어떤 자력磁力으로 그녀에게 가닿을 수 있음은 그에게 거의 하나의 발견과도 같았다.\n\n기이하게도 그는 이제, 자기에게 비교적 가난한 길이 본질적으로 더 높은 행로임을 가장 좋은 벗인 그녀에게 설득하는 일이, 그녀를 설득한다는 행위를 제 감정과 화해시키는 일만큼 어렵지는 않으리라 느끼기 시작했다. 앞을 내다보는 온갖 관점에서 그의 어머니가 너무도 의심할 바 없이 옳았기에, 그는 자신이 그녀를 흔들 수 있음을 알고는 가슴이 아려오지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n세상과 한 번도 섞여본 적이 없는 것을 생각하면, 그녀에게는 삶에 대한 남다른 통찰이 있었다. 자기가 논평하는 것들에 대한 분명한 관념은 없으되 그것들 사이의 관계에 대해서는 분명한 관념을 지닌 사람들의 예가 있다. 날 때부터 눈먼 시인 블랙록은 시각적 대상을 정확히 묘사할 수 있었고, 역시 눈먼 샌더슨 교수는 색채에 관해 탁월한 강의를 펼치며, 자기에게는 없으되 남들에게는 있는 관념의 이론을 그들에게 가르쳤다. 사회적 영역에서 이러한 재능을 지닌 이들은 대개 여자다. 그들은 한 번도 본 적 없는 세상을 살필 수 있고, 그저 소문으로만 들은 힘들을 가늠할 수 있다. 우리는 그것을 직관이라 부른다.\n\n요브라이트 부인에게 저 큰 세상이란 무엇이었던가? 그 본질은 알 수 없으되 그 경향만은 감지할 수 있는 군중이었다. 공동체들은 그녀에게 마치 멀리서 보이듯 했으니, 그녀는 그것들을, 살라르트며 반 알슬로트며 그 화파의 화가들의 화폭을 뒤덮은 무리―서로 떠밀고, 지그재그로 움직이고, 일정한 방향으로 행렬을 이루되, 바로 그 시야의 광대함 탓에 이목구비가 분간되지 않는 방대한 존재의 덩어리―를 보듯 보았다.\n\n여기까지 흘러온 한, 그녀의 삶이 그 성찰적 측면에서 매우 완성되어 있음을 알 수 있었다. 그녀 본성의 철학과 환경에 의한 그 한계가 그녀의 거동에 거의 쓰여 있다시피 했다. 그 거동에는 위엄 있는 토대가 있었으되 거동 자체는 위엄과는 거리가 멀었고, 확신의 기반이 깔려 있었으되 확신에 차 있지는 않았다. 한때 탄력 있던 그녀의 걸음걸이가 세월에 무뎌졌듯이, 그녀의 타고난 삶의 자긍심도 궁핍에 가로막혀 활짝 피지 못했던 것이다.\n\n클림의 운명을 빚어내는 다음번 미미한 손길은 며칠 뒤에 일어났다. 황야에서 고분古墳 하나가 발굴되었고, 요브라이트가 그 작업에 참석하느라 몇 시간 동안 서재를 비웠다. 오후에 크리스천이 같은 방향으로 나갔다 돌아오자 요브라이트 부인이 그에게 캐물었다.\n\n“구덩이를 팠는데유, 거꾸로 엎어놓은 화분 같은 게 나왔어유, 요브라이트 부인. 그 안에 진짜 송장 뼈가 들었더라고유. 사람들 집으로 그걸 가져갔는데, 저라면 그게 놓인 데서는 자고 싶지 않을 거예유. 죽은 이들이 와서 제 것을 찾아간다는 말이 있거든유. 요브라이트 도련님도 뼈가 든 단지를 하나 얻어서 집에 가져오시려고 했는데―진짜 해골 뼈를유―허나 달리 정해졌나 봐유. 도련님이 다시 생각하셔서 단지째 다 줘버리셨다니 마음 놓으셔도 되겠어유, 요브라이트 부인. 밤바람을 생각하면야 부인한테 천만다행이지유.”\n\n“줘버렸다고?”\n\n“네. 바이 아가씨한테유. 그 아가씬 그런 묘지 가구를 식인종처럼 좋아하는 취향인가 봐유.”\n\n“바이 아가씨도 거기 있었어?”\n\n“네, 있었던 것 같아유.”\n\n클림이 집에 돌아오자―곧 돌아왔는데―어머니가 묘한 어조로 말했다. “나한테 주려던 단지를 줘버렸더구나.”\n\n요브라이트는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으니, 그녀 감정의 물살이 너무도 또렷하여 대답을 허락지 않았다.\n\n새해의 초기 몇 주가 지나갔다. 요브라이트는 분명 집에서 공부했으나, 또한 밖으로 많이 거닐었으니, 그 걸음의 방향은 늘 미스트오버와 레인배로를 잇는 선의 어느 지점을 향했다.\n\n삼월이 찾아왔고, 황야는 겨울잠에서 깨어나는 첫 기미를 보였다. 그 깨어남은 거의 고양이처럼 은밀했다. 유스테이시아의 거처 옆 둑 바깥의 못은, 움직이고 소리 내며 관찰하는 이의 눈에는 여전히 죽은 듯 황량해 보였으나, 잠자코 한동안 지켜보면 차츰 크나큰 생기로 가득 찬 상태를 드러내곤 했다. 소심한 동물의 세계가 그 철을 맞아 되살아난 것이었다. 작은 올챙이와 영원이 물을 뚫고 거품처럼 솟아올라 그 아래를 내달리기 시작했고, 두꺼비들은 아주 어린 오리 같은 소리를 내며 둘씩 셋씩 물가로 다가왔으며, 머리 위로는 호박벌들이 짙어가는 빛 속을 이리저리 날았으니, 그 윙윙거림이 마치 징 소리처럼 다가왔다 멀어졌다 했다.\n\n바로 이런 어느 저녁에 요브라이트는 다름 아닌 그 못가에서 블룸스엔드 골짜기로 내려왔으니, 그곳에서 그는 다른 한 사람과 더불어 자연의 이 가냘픈 부활의 술렁임을 죄다 들을 수 있을 만큼 충분히 오래, 또 아무 말 없이 서 있었건만, 그것을 듣지 못했다. 내려오는 그의 걸음은 빨랐고, 발걸음에 탄력이 실려 있었다. 어머니의 집에 들어서기 전에 그는 멈춰 서서 숨을 골랐다. 창에서 그에게 비쳐 나온 빛은 그의 얼굴이 상기되어 있고 눈이 빛나고 있음을 드러냈다. 그 빛이 드러내지 못한 것은, 그의 입술에 봉인처럼 찍혀 머무는 무엇이었다. 이 흔적이 머무는 기색이 어찌나 생생했던지 그는 집에 들어서기조차 거의 두려웠으니, 마치 어머니가 “네 입에 그토록 선명하게 타오르는 그 붉은 점은 무엇이냐?”라고 물을 것만 같았기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 그는 곧 들어섰다. 차가 준비되어 있었고, 그는 어머니 맞은편에 앉았다. 어머니는 많은 말을 하지 않았고, 그로 말하자면, 언덕에서 방금 어떤 일이 행해지고 어떤 말이 막 오갔던 터라 이런저런 한담을 꺼낼 수가 없었다. 어머니의 과묵함에는 불길함이 없지 않았으나 그는 개의치 않는 듯했다. 그는 어머니가 어째서 그토록 말이 없는지 알았으나, 자신을 향한 어머니의 태도의 원인을 없앨 수는 없었다. 이제 이런 반쯤 침묵 속의 마주 앉음은 두 사람에게 결코 드문 일이 아니었다. 마침내 요브라이트는 문제의 뿌리 전체를 치고 들려는 시도의 첫발을 떼었다.\n\n“우리 닷새째 이렇게 식사 자리에 앉아 거의 말 한마디 안 하고 있어요. 이게 무슨 소용이에요, 어머니?”\n\n“소용없지”라고 어머니가 가슴이 미어지는 어조로 말했다. “하지만 너무도 그럴 만한 이유가 있단다.”\n\n“다 아시면 안 그러실 거예요. 이 얘기를 꺼내고 싶던 참이라, 화제가 시작돼서 다행이에요. 이유는 물론 유스테이시아 바이지요. 음, 솔직히 저는 요즘 그녀를 만났고, 꽤 여러 번 만났어요.”\n\n“그래, 그래. 그게 무슨 뜻인지 나도 안다. 그게 나를 괴롭히는구나, 클림. 너는 여기서 네 인생을 허비하고 있어. 그것도 오로지 그 여자 때문에. 그 여자만 아니었다면 너는 이 교사 계획 따위는 애초에 품지도 않았을 거다.”\n\n클림은 어머니를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. “그게 아니란 거 아시잖아요”라고 그가 말했다.\n\n“음, 네가 그 애를 보기 전에 그걸 해보겠다고 마음먹은 건 안다. 하지만 그건 작정으로만 끝났을 거야. 말하기야 그럴듯했지만, 실행에 옮기기엔 우스꽝스러운 일이지. 나는 한두 달 안에 네가 그런 자기희생의 어리석음을 깨닫고, 지금쯤이면 무슨 일자리든 얻어 파리로 돌아가 있으리라고 철석같이 기대했어. 다이아몬드 장사에 대한 거부감이야 나도 이해한다―솔직히 그게 너 같은 사내의 인생에는 미흡할 수도 있겠다고, 설령 그게 너를 백만장자로 만들어줄지언정, 생각하던 참이었지. 하지만 이제 네가 그 처녀에 대해 얼마나 잘못 알고 있는지 알고 나니, 네가 다른 일들에 대해선들 제대로 알고 있을지 의심스럽구나.”\n\n“제가 그녀를 어떻게 잘못 알고 있다는 거죠?”\n\n“그 애는 게으르고 불만투성이야. 하지만 그게 다가 아니다. 그 애가 네가 찾을 수 있는 어느 여자 못지않게 좋은 여자라고 친다 한들―분명코 그렇지 않다만―어째서 지금 네가 누군가와 인연을 맺으려 한단 말이냐?”\n\n“음, 현실적인 이유들이 있어요”라고 클림이 입을 떼었으나, 자기 주장에 맞설 수 있는 논변의 무게를 압도적으로 실감하고는 거의 말끝을 흐렸다. “제가 학교를 연다면 교육받은 여자가 제게 더없이 값진 조력자가 될 거예요.”\n\n“뭐라고! 정말로 그 애와 결혼하겠다는 거냐?”\n\n“그걸 분명히 말하기엔 너무 이르죠. 하지만 그렇게 했을 때의 명백한 이점들을 생각해보세요. 그녀는――”\n\n“그 애한테 돈이 있을 거란 생각은 하지도 마라. 그 앤 한 푼도 없어.”\n\n“그녀는 더없이 잘 교육받았고, 기숙학교에서 훌륭한 사감이 될 거예요. 솔직히 인정하건대, 어머니를 존중해 제 생각을 조금 바꿨어요. 그러니 그걸로 어머니도 만족하셔야 해요. 저는 더 이상 제 입으로 직접 최하층 계급에 기초 교육을 베풀겠다는 뜻을 고집하지 않아요. 더 나은 걸 할 수 있어요. 농부의 아들들을 위한 좋은 사립학교를 세우고, 학교를 멈추지 않고도 시험을 칠 수 있게 해나갈 수 있어요. 이런 방법으로, 그리고 그녀 같은 아내의 도움으로――”\n\n“오, 클림!”\n\n“저는 결국, 바라건대, 이 주州에서 가장 좋은 학교 중 하나의 교장이 될 거예요.”\n\n요브라이트는 “그녀”라는 말을, 어머니와의 대화에서는 우스꽝스러우리만치 분별없을 만큼의 열정을 담아 발음했다. 이런 상황에서, 새로운 여자를 향한 그 때 이른 감정의 누설에 짜증나지 않을 어머니의 마음이란 사해四海 안에 거의 없으리라.\n\n“너는 눈이 멀었다, 클림”이라고 그녀가 발끈하여 말했다. “그 애를 처음 눈에 담은 날이 네게 흉한 날이었어. 그리고 네 그 계획이란 건, 너를 사로잡은 이 어리석음을 정당화하고, 네가 처한 이 비이성적인 상황에 대한 양심을 달래려고 일부러 지어 올린 한낱 공중누각일 뿐이야.”\n\n“어머니, 그건 사실이 아니에요”라고 그가 단호히 대답했다.\n\n“그저 너를 슬픔에서 구하고 싶을 뿐인 내가, 앉아서 거짓말이나 하고 있다고 우길 셈이냐? 부끄러운 줄 알아라, 클림! 하지만 이게 다 그 여자 때문이야―화냥년 같으니!”\n\n클림은 불처럼 얼굴이 붉어지며 일어섰다. 그는 어머니의 어깨에 손을 얹고, 애원과 명령 사이에 기이하게 걸친 어조로 말했다. “그 말은 듣지 않겠어요. 우리 둘 다 후회할 식으로 어머니께 대꾸하게 될지도 몰라요.”\n\n어머니는 또 다른 격렬한 진실을 입에 올리려 입술을 떼었으나, 그를 바라보니 그 얼굴에서, 그 말을 입 밖에 내지 않게 하는 무언가를 보았다. 요브라이트는 방을 한두 번 가로질러 거닐다가, 별안간 집 밖으로 나가버렸다. 그가 들어온 것은 열한 시였으나, 정원 경내보다 더 멀리는 가지 않았던 터였다. 어머니는 잠자리에 든 뒤였다. 탁자 위에는 등불 하나가 켜진 채 남겨져 있었고,\n저녁상이 차려져 있었다. 그는 음식에는 입도 대지 않고 문단속을 한 뒤 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\nIV. 한 시간의 환희와 여러 시간의 슬픔\n\n이튿날 블룸스엔드는 충분히 음울했다. 예오브라이트는 서재에 머무르며 펼쳐진 책들 앞에 앉아 있었으나, 그 시간에 이룬 일이라곤 한심할 만큼 보잘것없었다. 어머니를 대하는 자신의 태도에 시무룩함 비슷한 것이 조금도 배어들지 않도록 하리라 마음먹은 그는 이따금 사소한 일들에 관해 어머니에게 말을 건넸고, 어머니의 대답이 짧은 것에 대해서는 모른 척했다. 대화하는 시늉을 유지하려는 똑같은 결심으로 그는 저녁 일곱 시쯤에 \"오늘 밤 월식이 있어요. 보러 나갔다 오겠어요\"라고 말하고는 외투를 걸친 뒤 어머니 곁을 떠났다.\n\n나지막이 뜬 달은 아직 집 정면에서는 보이지 않았으므로, 예오브라이트는 골짜기를 기어올라 달빛이 가득 쏟아지는 곳에 이를 때까지 걸었다. 그러나 그는 거기서도 멈추지 않고 계속 걸었으니, 발걸음은 레인배로 쪽을 향하고 있었다.\n\n반 시간 만에 그는 봉분 꼭대기에 섰다. 하늘은 끝에서 끝까지 맑았고, 달은 광야 전체에 빛을 흩뿌렸으나, 길과 물길이 흰 부싯돌과 반짝이는 석영 모래를 드러내어 전체적인 그늘 위에 줄무늬를 그어놓은 곳을 제외하고는 눈에 띄게 밝혀주지는 않았다. 한동안 서 있던 그는 몸을 숙여 히스를 만져보았다. 마른 것을 확인한 그는 봉분 위에 몸을 던지듯 누웠고, 얼굴을 달 쪽으로 향했으니, 달은 그의 두 눈 속에 각각 자그마한 제 모습을 그려 넣었다.\n\n그는 어머니에게 목적을 밝히지 않은 채 여기 올라온 적이 많았으나, 실제로는 목적을 감추면서도 겉으로는 솔직한 척한 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 그것은 석 달 전이었다면 자기 자신에게서 좀처럼 믿기 어려웠을 도덕적 상황이었다. 이 외딴곳으로 돌아와 일하면서 그는 사회적 필요가 가하는 마찰에서 벗어나리라 기대했었는데, 보라, 그것들은 여기에도 있었다. 그는 그 어느 때보다도, 개인적 야망이 진보의 유일하게 인정받는 형태가 아닌 어떤 세계에 있고 싶다는 갈망에 사로잡혔으니—이를테면 그때 그를 비추던 저 은빛 천체에서 언젠가 한 번쯤은 그러했을지도 모를 그런 세계였다. 그의 시선은 그 머나먼 나라의 길이와 너비를 두루 훑었으니—무지개 만灣을, 음울한 위기의 바다를, 폭풍의 대양을, 꿈의 호수를, 광대한 벽으로 둘러싸인 평원들을, 그리고 경이로운 고리 모양 산맥을 거치며—마침내 그는 거의 자신이 몸소 그 거친 풍경 속을 항해하며, 그 텅 빈 언덕 위에 서고, 그 사막을 가로지르고, 그 골짜기와 옛 바다 밑바닥으로 내려가고, 혹은 그 분화구의 가장자리로 기어오르는 듯한 느낌에 사로잡혔다.\n\n그가 그토록 멀리 떨어진 풍경을 바라보고 있을 때 아래쪽 가장자리에 황갈색 얼룩이 생겨났으니—월식이 시작된 것이었다. 이것이 미리 약속해둔 순간을 표시했으니—저 머나먼 천체의 현상이 연인의 신호로서 이 달 아래 세계의 일에 동원된 것이었다. 그 광경을 보자 예오브라이트의 마음은 다시 땅으로 날아 돌아왔고, 그는 일어나 몸을 추스르며 귀를 기울였다. 분초가 흘러갔으니, 아마 십 분쯤 지났을 테고, 달 위의 그림자는 눈에 띄게 넓어졌다. 그는 왼편에서 부스럭거리는 소리를 들었고, 망토를 두르고 얼굴을 위로 향한 형상이 봉분 아래에 나타나더니, 클림은 내려갔다. 한순간에 그 형상은 그의 품에 안겼고, 그의 입술은 그녀의 입술에 닿았다.\n\n\"내 유스테이시아!\"\n\n\"클림, 사랑하는 이여!\"\n\n이런 상황을 석 달도 채 안 되는 시간이 만들어낸 것이었다.\n\n그들은 오래도록 한마디도 하지 않고 머물러 있었으니, 어떤 언어도 그들의 상태에 미칠 수 없었기 때문이요—말이란 지나간 야만의 시대에 쓰이던 녹슨 연장과 같아서, 그저 이따금 용인될 따름이었다.\n\n\"왜 안 오나 궁금해지기 시작했어요.\" 그녀가 그의 포옹에서 조금 물러났을 때 예오브라이트가 말했다.\n\n\"달 가장자리에 그림자가 처음 드리운 후 십 분이라고 하셨고, 지금이 바로 그때예요.\"\n\n\"음, 우리가 여기 함께 있다는 것만 생각합시다.\"\n\n그러고는 서로의 손을 잡은 채 그들은 다시 침묵했고, 달 원반 위의 그림자는 조금 더 커졌다.\n\n\"마지막으로 절 본 뒤로 길게 느껴졌나요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"슬프게 느껴졌소.\"\n\n\"길지는 않고요? 그건 당신이 일에 몰두해서, 그래서 제 부재에 눈을 감기 때문이에요. 아무것도 할 수 없는 저에게는, 그게 고인 물 밑에서 사는 것 같았어요.\"\n\n\"여보, 나는 내 시간을 짧게 만든 그런 수단으로 시간이 짧아지는 것보다는 차라리 지루함을 견디겠소.\"\n\n\"그게 어떤 식으로요? 당신은 나를 사랑하지 않았으면 하고 바랐던 거로군요.\"\n\n\"어찌 사내가 그러기를 바라면서 동시에 사랑할 수 있겠소? 아니오, 유스테이시아.\"\n\n\"사내는 그럴 수 있지만, 여인은 못 해요.\"\n\"음, 내가 무슨 생각을 했든, 한 가지만은 분명하오—나는 당신을 사랑하오—그 어떤 척도나 묘사로도 다 담을 수 없을 만큼. 나는 당신을 짓눌릴 만큼 사랑하오—그동안 어떤 여인을 보아도 그저 스쳐 지나가는 즐거운 변덕 이상은 느껴본 적 없던 내가 말이오. 달빛에 비친 당신 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 그 모든 선과 곡선 하나하나에 머물게 해주오! 이 얼굴과, 당신을 알기 전에 여러 번 보았던 얼굴들 사이의 차이라곤 머리카락 몇 올 굵기에 지나지 않는데, 그런데도 얼마나 큰 차이인지—전부와 아무것도 아닌 것 사이의 차이라오. 저 입에 다시 한번 입맞춤을! 자, 여기, 여기, 그리고 여기. 당신 눈이 무거워 보이는구려, 유스테이시아.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 제가 늘 보는 모습이 그래요. 제가 태어났다는 것에 대해 이따금 저 자신을 향한 괴로운 연민을 느끼는 데서 비롯되는 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"지금은 그걸 느끼지 않소?\"\n\n\"네. 그래도 우리가 늘 이렇게 사랑하지는 못하리라는 걸 알아요. 그 무엇도 사랑의 지속을 보장할 수 없어요. 사랑은 정령처럼 증발해버릴 테고, 그래서 저는 두려움으로 가득 차 있어요.\"\n\n\"그럴 필요 없소.\"\n\n\"아, 당신은 몰라요. 당신은 저보다 더 많은 걸 보았고, 제가 이야기로만 들어본 도시들과 사람들 사이에 있어봤고, 저보다 더 많은 세월을 살았지만, 그래도 이 점에서는 제가 당신보다 더 늙었어요. 저는 한때 다른 남자를 사랑했고, 이제는 당신을 사랑해요.\"\n\n\"하느님의 자비를 봐서라도 그런 말 마오, 유스테이시아!\"\n\n\"하지만 먼저 싫증 내는 쪽이 저는 아닐 거예요. 두렵건대 이런 식으로 끝날 거예요. 당신 어머니가 당신이 저를 만난다는 걸 알아내실 테고, 저에게 등 돌리도록 당신을 움직이실 거예요!\"\n\n\"그런 일은 결코 없을 거요. 어머니는 이 만남에 대해 이미 알고 계시오.\"\n\n\"그러면서 저를 헐뜯으시고요?\"\n\n\"말하지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"자, 가요! 어머니께 순종하세요. 저는 당신을 망칠 거예요. 이렇게 저를 만나는 건 어리석은 일이에요. 입맞춰주고, 영원히 떠나세요. 영원히—들리세요?—영원히요!\"\n\n\"난 안 떠나오.\" \"그게 당신의 유일한 기회예요. 많은 사내의 사랑이 그에게 저주가 되었어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 절망에 빠져 망상으로 가득하고 고집스럽소. 그리고 오해하고 있소. 오늘 밤 당신을 만나는 데에는 당신을 향한 사랑 말고도 또 다른 이유가 있소. 비록 나는 당신과 달리 우리의 애정이 영원할 수 있다고 느끼지만, 우리의 현재 존재 방식이 지속될 수 없다는 점에서는 당신과 같은 마음이오.\"\n\n\"오! 당신 어머니로군요. 그래요, 바로 그거예요! 알았어요.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇이든 신경 쓰지 마오. 이것만은 믿어주오, 나는 당신을 잃도록 나 자신을 내버려둘 수 없소. 나는 늘 당신을 곁에 두어야만 하오. 바로 오늘 저녁만 해도 당신을 보내고 싶지 않소. 이 불안에는 단 하나의 치유책이 있을 뿐이오, 가장 사랑하는 이여—당신이 내 아내가 되어야만 하오.\"\n\n그녀는 흠칫하더니—이내 차분하게 말하려 애쓰며, \"냉소가들은 그게 사랑을 치유함으로써 불안을 치유하는 거라고 말하던데요\" 하고 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 당신은 내게 답해야 하오. 언젠가 내가 당신을 내 사람이라 주장해도 되겠소—당장이라는 뜻은 아니오만?\"\n\n\"생각해봐야 해요.\" 유스테이시아가 중얼거렸다. \"지금은 제게 파리 이야기를 해주세요. 이 땅에 그런 곳이 또 있을까요?\"\n\n\"무척 아름답소. 하지만 당신은 내 사람이 되겠소?\"\n\n\"이 세상 누구의 것도 되지 않을 거예요—그러면 만족하시겠어요?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 지금으로서는.\"\n\n\"이제 튈르리와 루브르 이야기를 해주세요.\" 그녀가 둘러대듯 말을 이었다.\n\n\"파리 얘기는 하기 싫소! 음, 루브르에서 본 어느 햇살 가득한 방이 기억나는데, 당신이 살기에 알맞은 곳일 거요—아폴론 회랑이라오. 그 창들은 대체로 동쪽을 향해 있어서, 이른 아침 해가 밝을 때면 방 전체가 완벽한 화려함의 불길에 휩싸인다오. 빛줄기가 도금된 장식 더께에서 솟구쳐 화려하게 상감된 함으로 쏘아가고, 함에서 금은 식기로, 식기에서 보석과 귀한 돌로, 거기서 다시 칠보로 옮아가, 마침내 눈을 완전히 부시게 하는 완벽한 빛의 그물이 짜인다오. 그건 그렇고, 이제 우리 혼인 얘기인데—\"\n\n\"그리고 베르사유는—국왕의 회랑도 그런 호화로운 방이지요, 그렇지 않아요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 하지만 호화로운 방 얘기를 해서 무슨 소용이오? 그건 그렇고, 프티 트리아농이 우리가 살기에 더없이 좋을 거요, 거기서 당신은 달빛 속 정원을 거닐며 마치 어느 영국식 관목 숲에 있는 듯 여길 수 있을 거요. 영국식으로 꾸며져 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇게 여기는 건 정말 싫어요!\"\n\n\"그러면 대궁전 앞 잔디밭에 머물 수도 있소. 그 일대라면 분명 역사적 로맨스의 세계에 있는 듯한 기분이 들 거요.\" 그녀에게는 모든 것이 새로웠으므로 그는 계속 이야기하며 퐁텐블로, 생클루, 부아, 그 밖에 파리 사람들이 즐겨 찾는 여러 명소를 묘사했고, 마침내 그녀가 말했다—\n\n\"언제 그런 곳들에 가곤 했어요?\"\n\n\"일요일에.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇군요. 저는 영국식 일요일이 싫어요. 거기서라면 그들의 풍습에 얼마나 잘 어울릴까요! 사랑하는 클림, 다시 돌아갈 거죠?\"\n\n클림은 고개를 가로젓고는 월식을 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신이 돌아간다면 저는—뭔가가 되겠어요.\" 그녀가 그의 가슴께에 머리를 기대며 다정하게 말했다. \"당신이 동의한다면, 단 일 분도 더 기다리게 하지 않고 약속할게요.\"\n\n\"당신과 우리 어머니가 이 문제에 한마음이라니 어찌 이리도 기이한지!\" 예오브라이트가 말했다. \"나는 돌아가지 않겠다고 맹세했소, 유스테이시아. 내가 싫은 것은 그곳이 아니라 그 일이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 다른 자격으로 갈 수도 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"안 되오. 게다가 그건 내 계획을 방해할 거요. 그걸 강요하지 마오, 유스테이시아. 나와 혼인하겠소?\"\n\n\"말씀드릴 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"자—파리는 신경 쓰지 마오, 다른 어떤 곳보다 나을 것 없소. 약속해주오, 어여쁜 이여!\"\n\n\"당신은 결코 교육 계획을 고수하지 못할 거예요, 저는 확신해요. 그러면 저로서는 다 괜찮은 셈이고, 그러니 영원토록 당신의 것이 되겠다고 약속할게요.\"\n\n클림은 손을 부드럽게 눌러 그녀의 얼굴을 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기고는 입맞췄다.\n\n\"아! 하지만 당신은 나에게서 무엇을 얻게 되는지 몰라요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"때때로 저는 이 유스테이시아 바이에게는 살뜰한 살림꾼 아내가 될 만한 무언가가 없다고 생각해요. 음, 내버려둬요—보세요, 우리 시간이 어떻게 흘러가는지, 흘러가고, 흘러가고, 흘러가는지!\" 그녀가 절반쯤 가려진 달을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 너무 애처롭소.\"\n\n\"아니에요. 다만 현재 너머의 무언가를 생각하는 게 두려울 뿐이에요. 지금 있는 것은 우리가 알지요. 우리는 지금 함께 있고, 얼마나 오래 그럴지는 알 수 없어요. 알 수 없는 것은 언제나 제 마음을 끔찍한 가능성들로 채워요, 마땅히 즐거우리라 기대해도 좋을 때조차요…… 클림, 월식에 든 달빛이 당신 얼굴 위로 묘하게 이국적인 빛깔로 비치고, 마치 황금으로 깎아낸 듯이 그 윤곽을 드러내고 있어요. 그건 당신이 이런 것보다 더 나은 일을 해야 한다는 뜻이에요.\"\n\n\"당신은 야심이 있구려, 유스테이시아—아니, 정확히 야심이라기보다는 호사를 즐기는 거요. 당신을 행복하게 해주려면 나도 같은 기질이어야겠지요, 아마. 그런데도 그와는 거리가 멀어서, 나는 마땅한 일거리만 있다면 여기 은둔처에서 살다 죽을 수도 있소.\"\n\n그의 어조에는 애를 태우는 연인으로서의 자기 입지에 대한 불신이, 자신의 취향과 어쩌다 드문 지점에서만 맞닿는 사람을 향해 자신이 공정하게 행동하고 있는지에 대한 의구심이 배어 있었다. 그녀는 그 뜻을 알아채고는, 나직하면서도 절절하게 안심시키려는 어조로 속삭였다, \"오해하지 마요, 클림—제가 파리를 좋아하기는 해도, 저는 당신 그 자체만을 사랑해요. 당신의 아내가 되어 파리에서 사는 것은 제게 천국이겠지만, 아예 당신의 것이 되지 못하느니 차라리 여기 은둔처에서라도 당신과 함께 살겠어요. 어느 쪽이든 제게는 얻는 것이요, 아주 큰 얻음이에요. 이게 너무 솔직한 제 고백이에요.\"\n\n\"여인답게 말하는구려. 그리고 이제 곧 당신과 헤어져야 하오. 당신 집 쪽으로 함께 걷겠소.\"\n\n\"하지만 벌써 집에 가야 하나요?\" 그녀가 물었다. \"그래요, 모래가 거의 다 빠져나간 게 보이네요, 그리고 월식이 점점 더 번지고 있어요. 아직 가지 마요! 한 시간이 다 지날 때까지 머물러요. 그러면 더는 당신을 붙잡지 않을게요. 당신은 집에 가서 잘 자겠지요. 저는 자면서도 자꾸 한숨을 쉬어요! 제 꿈을 꾸기는 하나요?\"\n\n\"당신에 관한 또렷한 꿈은 기억나지 않소.\"\n\n\"저는 제 꿈의 모든 장면에서 당신 얼굴을 보고, 모든 소리에서 당신 목소리를 들어요. 그러지 않았으면 싶어요. 제가 느끼는 게 너무 지나쳐요. 사람들은 그런 사랑은 오래가지 않는다고 해요. 하지만 그래야만 해요! 그런데 한 번, 기억나는데, 부드머스 거리에서 후사르 기병 장교 하나가 말을 타고 내려가는 걸 본 적이 있어요, 그는 전혀 모르는 사람이었고 제게 말 한마디 건넨 적도 없는데, 저는 정말 사랑 때문에 죽겠다 싶을 만큼 그를 사랑했어요—하지만 죽지 않았고, 결국에는 그에게 마음 쓰는 걸 그만뒀어요. 만약 당신을 사랑할 수 없는 때가 온다면 얼마나 끔찍할까요, 내 클림!\"\n\n\"제발 그런 무모한 말 마오. 그런 때가 가까이 다가오는 걸 보게 되면 우리는 '내 신념과 목적보다 더 오래 살아남았구나' 하고 말하며 죽읍시다. 자, 한 시간이 다 됐소—이제 걸읍시다.\"\n\n손을 맞잡고 그들은 미스토버 쪽 오솔길을 따라 걸었다. 집 가까이 이르렀을 때 그가 말했다, \"오늘 밤은 당신 할아버지를 뵙기에 너무 늦었소. 그분이 반대하실 것 같소?\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드릴게요. 제 일은 제가 알아서 하는 데 너무 익숙해져서, 우리가 그분께 여쭈어야 한다는 생각조차 들지 않았어요.\"\n\n그러고는 그들은 머뭇거리며 헤어졌고, 클림은 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 내려갔다. 그리고 자신의 올림포스 같은 여인의 매혹적인 분위기에서 점점 더 멀어져 갈수록 그의 얼굴은 새로운 종류의 슬픔으로 어두워졌다. 자신의 사랑이 그를 빠뜨린 진퇴양난을 깨닫는 마음이 온전한 힘으로 되돌아왔다. 그가 새로운 일에 자리 잡을 때까지 전망 없는 약혼 기간을 견디며 기다리겠다는 유스테이시아의 겉보기 의향에도 불구하고, 그는 순간순간 그녀가 자신을 사랑하는 것이 마땅히 그녀가 속한 화려한 세계에서 온 방문객으로서이지, 그녀의 흥미를 그토록 끌었던 그 최근의 과거에 맞서는 목적을 지닌 한 사내로서가 아님을 알아챌 수밖에 없었다. 그것은, 그녀가 그의 프랑스 수도 복귀에 관해 아무런 조건을 달지 않았음에도, 혼인하게 될 경우 그녀가 남몰래 바라는 바가 바로 그것임을 뜻했고, 그것은 본디 즐거웠을 많은 시간을 그에게서 앗아갔다. 그와 더불어 자신과 어머니 사이에 벌어지는 균열도 다가왔다. 무언가 자그마한 사건이 그가 어머니에게 안기고 있는 실망을 평소보다 더 두드러지게 할 때마다 그것은 그를 외롭고 침울한 산책길로 내몰았으며, 혹은 그런 인식이 빚어내는 마음의 소요로 밤의 태반을 깬 채로 보내게 했다. 만약 예오브라이트 부인이 그의 이 목적이 얼마나 건전하고 가치 있는 것인지, 그리고 그것이 유스테이시아를 향한 헌신에 얼마나 적게 영향받고 있는지를 보도록 이끌릴 수만 있었다면, 그녀는 그를 얼마나 다르게 여겼을 것인가!\n\n이렇게 사랑과 아름다움이 처음에 자기 둘레에 피워 올린 눈멀게 하는 후광에 그의 시야가 차츰 익숙해지면서, 예오브라이트는 자신이 얼마나 곤경에 처했는지를 깨닫기 시작했다. 때때로 그는 유스테이시아를 결코 알지 않았더라면 하고 바랐다가, 곧바로 그 소원이 잔인하다며 거두어들이곤 했다. 서로 적대하는 세 가지 성장이 모두 살아 있어야만 했으니—자신을 향한 어머니의 믿음, 교사가 되려는 자신의 계획, 그리고 유스테이시아의 행복이었다. 그의 격렬한 천성으로는 이 가운데 하나도 포기할 수 없었으나, 셋 중 둘조차도 그가 지켜내기를 바랄 수 있는 최대치였다. 그의 사랑은 페트라르카가 라우라를 향해 품었던 것만큼이나 정결했음에도, 그것은 이전에는 그저 하나의 어려움에 지나지 않았던 것을 족쇄로 만들어버렸다. 그가 한마음으로 서 있을 때조차 그리 단순하지만은 않았던 처지가, 유스테이시아가 더해지면서 형언할 수 없이 복잡해졌다. 어머니가 막 한 가지 계획을 용인하기 시작했을 때 그는 처음 것보다 더 쓰디쓴 또 다른 계획을 끄집어냈으니, 그 조합은 그녀가 감당할 수 있는 것 이상이었다.\n\nV. 날카로운 말이 오가고, 위기가 닥치다\n\n예오브라이트는 유스테이시아와 함께 있지 않을 때면 노예처럼 책에 매달려 앉아 있었고, 책을 읽지 않을 때면 그녀를 만나고 있었다. 이 만남들은 극도의 비밀 속에서 이루어졌다.\n\n어느 날 오후 그의 어머니가 토머신을 오전에 방문하고 돌아왔다. 그녀의 얼굴 주름이 흐트러진 것을 보고 그는 무슨 일인가가 일어났음을 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"도무지 이해할 수 없는 이야기를 들었구나.\" 그녀가 애통하게 말했다. \"선장이 콰이엇 우먼에서 너와 유스테이시아 바이가 혼인하기로 약혼했다고 떠벌렸다더라.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 예오브라이트가 말했다. \"하지만 아주 오랫동안 그렇게 되지 않을 수도 있어요.\"\n\n\"아주 오랫동안 그렇게 되지 않으리라고는 좀처럼 생각되지 않는구나! 그 애를 파리로 데려갈 테지, 그렇지?\" 그녀가 지친 절망감으로 말했다.\n\n\"파리로 돌아가지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 아내를 데리고 무얼 하려고?\"\n\n\"말씀드렸듯이 부드머스에서 학교를 운영할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그건 도무지 믿을 수 없는 일이다! 그곳은 교사들로 넘쳐난다. 너는 특별한 자격도 없잖니. 너 같은 사람에게 무슨 가망이 있겠어?\"\n\n\"부자가 될 가망은 없지요. 하지만 제 교육 방식은 참된 만큼이나 새로운 것이라, 저는 동포들에게 크나큰 이로움을 주게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"꿈이다, 꿈이야! 발명될 만한 방식이 조금이라도 남아 있었다면 진작에 대학에서 찾아냈을 게다.\"\n\n\"결코 그렇지 않아요, 어머니. 그들은 찾아낼 수 없습니다, 그들의 교사들이 그런 방식을 요구하는 계층과—다시 말해 아무런 사전 훈련도 받지 못한 사람들과 접촉하지 않으니까요. 제 계획은 빈 마음에 높은 지식을 불어넣되, 참된 공부가 시작되기 전에 다시 비워내야 할 것들로 그 마음을 먼저 채워 넣지 않는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"네가 얽매임에서 자유로운 채로 지냈더라면 너를 믿었을지도 모른다. 하지만 이 여자는—그 애가 착한 처녀였더라도 충분히 나빴을 텐데, 하물며—\"\n\n\"착한 처녀예요.\"\n\n\"네 생각엔 그렇겠지. 코르푸 군악대장의 딸이라니! 그 애가 살아온 인생이 어땠겠니? 성姓조차 진짜가 아니잖아.\"\n\n\"그분은 바이 선장의 손녀이고, 그녀의 아버지가 그저 어머니 쪽 성을 따랐을 뿐입니다. 그리고 그녀는 천성이 숙녀예요.\"\n\n\"사람들이 그자를 '선장'이라 부르지만, 누구든 선장이 될 수 있지.\"\n\n\"그분은 영국 해군에 계셨어요!\"\n\n\"틀림없이 무슨 통짝배인지 뭔지를 타고 바다에 나갔겠지. 그자는 어째서 손녀를 돌보지 않는 게냐? 숙녀라면 그 애처럼 밤낮 가리지 않고 광야를 쏘다니지 않는다. 그러나 그게 전부가 아니다. 한때 그 애와 토머신의 남편 사이에 묘한 일이 있었어—내가 여기 서 있는 것만큼이나 확실하다.\"\n\n\"유스테이시아가 제게 말해줬어요. 그가 일 년 전에 그녀에게 약간의 관심을 보이긴 했지만, 거기에 해로울 건 없습니다. 저는 그녀가 더욱 좋아질 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"클림,\" 그의 어머니가 단호하게 말했다, \"안타깝게도 그 애를 거스를 증거는 내게 없다. 하지만 그 애가 네게 좋은 아내가 된다면, 나쁜 아내란 세상에 한 번도 없었던 셈이 될 거다.\"\n\n\"정말이지 어머니는 사람을 거의 격분시키시는군요.\" 예오브라이트가 격하게 말했다. \"바로 오늘 두 분이 만나도록 자리를 마련하려 했어요. 그런데 어머니는 제게 한 점의 평안도 주지 않으시고, 매사에 제 바람을 가로막으려 하시는군요.\"\n\n\"내 자식이 잘못된 혼인을 한다는 생각이 너무나 싫구나! 이런 꼴을 보려고 이렇게 살아오지는 않았다. 내겐 너무 벅차—내가 꿈꾼 것 이상이야!\" 그녀가 창문 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 숨이 가빠지고, 입술은 창백하게 벌어진 채 떨리고 있었다.\n\n\"어머니,\" 클림이 말했다, \"어머니가 무얼 하시든, 제게는 늘 소중한 분이실 거예요—그건 아시잖아요. 하지만 한 가지 제가 말할 권리가 있는 게 있으니, 제 나이쯤 되면 무엇이 제게 최선인지 알 만큼은 충분히 나이를 먹었다는 거예요.\"\n\n예오브라이트 부인은 한동안 침묵한 채 흔들렸으니, 마치 더는 할 말이 없는 듯했다. 그러더니 그녀가 대답했다, \"최선이라고? 저런 음탕하고 게으른 여자 때문에 네 앞날을 망치는 게 네게 최선이란 말이냐? 바로 그 애를 택했다는 사실 자체로 네가 무엇이 네게 최선인지 모른다는 걸 입증하는 게 보이지 않니? 너는 한 여자를 기쁘게 하려고 네 모든 생각을 내던지고—네 온 영혼을 바치고 있다.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 그리고 그 여자가 바로 어머니예요.\"\n\n\"어찌 나를 그리도 경박하게 대할 수 있단 말이냐!\" 그의 어머니가 눈물 어린 표정으로 다시 그를 향하며 말했다. \"넌 비정상이다, 클림, 나는 그러리라곤 예상하지 못했어.\"\n\n\"그러시겠지요.\" 그가 풀죽은 목소리로 말했다. \"어머니는 제게 베푸시려던 잣대를 모르셨고, 그러니 그 잣대가 어머니께 되돌아오리라는 것도 모르셨던 거예요.\"\n\n\"넌 내게 말대답을 하는구나. 넌 그 애 생각뿐이야. 매사에 그 애 편을 들고.\"\n\n\"그건 그녀가 그럴 만한 사람임을 입증하는 거예요. 저는 여태껏 나쁜 것을 편든 적이 없습니다. 그리고 저는 그녀만 위하는 게 아니에요. 저는 어머니를, 그리고 저 자신을, 또 좋은 것이라면 무엇이든 위합니다. 여자가 일단 다른 여자를 싫어하면 그토록 무자비한 법이지요!\"\n\n\"오 클림! 네 고집스러운 외고집을 부디 내 잘못으로 돌리지 말아다오. 가치 없는 사람과 맺어지고 싶었다면 어째서 굳이 그러려고 이 집에 돌아온 게냐? 어째서 파리에서 그러지 않았어?—거기서는 그게 더 흔한 풍습이잖니. 너는 그저 나를, 외로운 여자를 괴롭히고 내 수명을 줄이려고 돌아온 게다! 네 사랑을 바치는 곳에 네 존재도 바쳤으면 좋겠구나!\"\n\n클림이 쉰 목소리로 말했다, \"어머니는 제 어머니십니다. 더는 말하지 않겠어요—다만 이것만은, 이곳을 제 집이라 여겼던 것을 용서해주십사 빌 뿐입니다. 더는 어머니께 저를 떠안기지 않겠어요. 떠나겠습니다.\" 그러고는 눈에 눈물을 머금은 채 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n초여름이 시작될 무렵의 햇살 가득한 오후였고, 광야의 축축한 우묵한 곳들은 갈색에서 초록 단계로 옮아가 있었다. 예오브라이트는 미스토버와 레인배로에서부터 뻗어 내려온 분지의 가장자리까지 걸어갔다.\n\n이 무렵 그는 차분해져서 풍경을 둘러보았다. 골짜기의 윤곽을 다채롭게 하는 자그마한 둔덕들 사이의 작은 골짜기들에는, 싱싱한 어린 고사리들이 무성하게 자라 올라 마침내 대여섯 자 높이에 이를 참이었다. 그는 조금 내려가, 작은 우묵한 곳 하나에서 오솔길이 나오는 자리에 몸을 던지듯 누워 기다렸다. 바로 이곳으로 그는 오늘 오후 어머니를 모셔 와 유스테이시아와 만나게 하여 두 사람이 화해하도록 하겠노라고 약속했었다. 그의 시도는 완전히 실패하고 말았다.\n\n그는 선명한 초록의 둥지 속에 있었다. 그를 둘러싼 고사리 식생은 그토록 무성하면서도 아주 한결같았으니—기계로 찍어낸 듯한 잎사귀들의 숲이요, 톱니 모양의 가장자리를 지닌 초록 삼각형들의 세계였고, 단 한 송이의 꽃도 없었다. 공기는 수증기 같은 온기로 따스했고, 정적은 깨지지 않았다. 도마뱀과 메뚜기와 개미만이 눈에 띄는 유일한 생물이었다. 그 광경은 식물의 형태가 몇 안 되고 고사리류뿐이던, 봉오리도 꽃도 없이 그저 단조롭게 펼쳐진 잎사귀밖에 없어 그 가운데서 새 한 마리 노래하지 않던 석탄기의 태고 세계에 속한 듯이 보였다.\n\n상당한 시간 동안 몸을 기댄 채 침울하게 생각에 잠겨 있던 그는, 고사리 위로 흰 비단 보닛이 왼편에서 다가오는 것을 알아보았고, 예오브라이트는 그것이 자신이 사랑하는 이의 머리를 감싸고 있음을 곧장 알았다. 그의 마음은 무기력에서 깨어나 따스한 흥분으로 들떴고, 벌떡 일어서며 그는 소리 내어 말했다, \"그녀가 틀림없이 오리라는 걸 알고 있었어.\"\n\n그녀는 잠시 우묵한 곳으로 사라졌다가, 이윽고 덤불에서 온몸을 펼치듯 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"여기 당신뿐이에요?\" 그녀가 실망한 듯한 기색으로 외쳤으나, 그 기색의 공허함은 점점 붉어지는 얼굴과 절반쯤 죄지은 듯한 나직한 웃음으로 드러났다. \"예오브라이트 부인은 어디 계세요?\"\n\n\"안 오셨소.\" 그가 가라앉은 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"당신이 여기 혼자 있으리라는 걸, 그리고 우리가 이렇게 한가롭고 즐거운 시간을 갖게 되리라는 걸 미리 알았더라면 좋았을 텐데.\" 그녀가 진지하게 말했다. \"미리 알지 못한 즐거움은 절반은 허비되는 거예요. 즐거움을 기대하는 것은 그걸 두 배로 만드는 거니까요. 오늘 저는 이 오후에 당신을 온전히 제 차지로 두게 되리라곤 한 번도 생각하지 못했고, 어떤 일이든 실제 그 순간은 너무도 빨리 지나가버려요.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇소.\"\n\n\"가엾은 클림!\" 그녀가 그의 얼굴을 다정하게 들여다보며 말을 이었다. \"당신은 슬프군요. 댁에서 무슨 일이 있었어요. 무엇이 있는지는 신경 쓰지 마요—그저 무엇이 있어 보이는지만 봐요.\"\n\n\"하지만 여보, 우리 어떻게 해야 하오?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그냥 지금처럼 계속해요—그저 만남에서 만남으로 살아가며, 다른 날에 대해서는 마음 쓰지 마요. 당신이 늘 그걸 생각하고 있다는 걸 저는 알아요—보면 알아요. 하지만 그래선 안 돼요—그러지 않을 거죠, 사랑하는 클림?\"\n\n\"당신은 꼭 모든 여인 같구려. 그들은 어쩌다 주어지는 처지 위에 자기 삶을 세우는 것에 늘 만족하지만, 사내들은 자기에게 맞는 세계를 기꺼이 만들어내려 하지요. 이 말을 들어보오, 유스테이시아. 더는 미루지 않기로 작정한 문제가 하나 있소. '카르페 디엠'의 지혜에 관한 당신의 정서는 오늘 내게 와닿지 않소. 우리의 현재 삶의 방식은 머지않아 끝나야만 하오.\"\n\n\"당신 어머니 때문이군요!\"\n\n\"그렇소. 이걸 말한다고 해서 당신을 덜 사랑하는 게 아니오. 다만 당신이 마땅히 알아야 하기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"제 환희가 두려웠어요.\" 그녀가 입술을 거의 움직이지 않은 채 말했다. \"그것은 너무 강렬하고 소진시키는 것이었어요.\"\n\n\"아직 희망은 있소. 내게는 아직 사십 년의 일이 남아 있는데, 어찌 절망해야 하겠소? 나는 그저 어색한 길목에 서 있을 뿐이오. 한결같음 없이는 진보도 없다고 사람들이 그토록 쉽게 단정하지 않았으면 좋겠소.\"\n\n\"아—당신 마음은 그 일의 철학적인 측면으로 달아나는군요. 음, 이 슬프고 가망 없는 장애물들은 어떤 의미에서는 반가운 것이에요, 운명이 즐겨 부리는 잔인한 풍자들을 무심하게 바라볼 수 있게 해주니까요. 들은 적이 있어요, 갑자기 행복에 들어선 사람들이 그걸 누릴 만큼 오래 살지 못할까 봐 불안에 못 이겨 죽었다는 이야기를요. 저도 최근에 그런 변덕스러운 불안의 상태에 빠져 있었는데, 이제는 그걸 면하게 되겠네요. 자, 걸어요.\"\n\n클림은 이미 그를 위해 드러나 있던 손을 잡았으니—맨손과 맨손을 맞잡고 걷는 것이 그들이 좋아하던 방식이었다—그리고 고사리 사이로 그녀를 이끌었다. 그날 저물녘 골짜기를 따라 걸어가는 그들은 한껏 만개한 사랑의 더없이 어여쁜 그림을 이루었으니, 해는 그들의 오른편으로 비스듬히 기울며 미루나무처럼 큰 그들의 가냘프고 유령 같은 그림자를 가시금작화와 고사리 너머 멀리까지 드리웠다. 유스테이시아는 짐짓 머리를 뒤로 젖힌 채 걸었으니, 성취와 외모와 나이에서 자신을 완벽하게 보완해주는 사내를 오로지 자기 혼자 힘으로 얻어냈다는, 어떤 기쁘고도 관능적인 승리의 기운이 그 눈에 두루 어려 있었다. 젊은 사내 쪽으로 말하자면, 파리에서 가져왔던 얼굴의 창백함과 세월과 사색이 남긴 초기의 흔적들은, 본디 그의 것이었던 건강하고 활기찬 다부짐이 원래의 비율을 어느 정도 되찾으면서, 돌아왔을 때보다는 덜 두드러졌다. 그들은 광야의 가장 낮은 가장자리에 이를 때까지 거닐었으니, 그곳에서 땅은 질척해지며 황무지로 녹아들었다.\n\n\"여기서 당신과 헤어져야 해요, 클림.\" 유스테이시아가 말했다.\n\n그들은 멈춰 서서 작별 인사를 나눌 채비를 했다. 그들 앞의 모든 것은 완벽하게 평평했다. 지평선에 걸린 해는 구릿빛과 연보랏빛 구름 사이로 땅을 가로질러 빛을 흘려보냈고, 그 구름들은 옅고 부드러운 초록빛 하늘 아래 평평하게 펼쳐져 있었다. 해를 향해 누운 땅 위의 온갖 어두운 것들은 자줏빛 아지랑이에 뒤덮였고, 그것을 배경으로 구슬피 우는 각다귀 떼가 빛을 내며 위로 솟아올라 불티처럼 춤추듯 맴돌았다.\n\n\"오! 당신과 헤어지는 건 견디기에 너무 힘겨워요!\" 유스테이시아가 별안간 고뇌에 찬 속삭임으로 외쳤다. \"당신 어머니가 당신을 너무 많이 움직이실 거예요. 저는 공정하게 판단받지 못할 거고, 제가 착한 처녀가 아니라는 소문이 퍼질 거고, 거기에 마녀 이야기까지 더해져 저를 더 새카맣게 만들 거예요!\"\n\n\"그럴 수 없소. 누구도 감히 당신이나 나에 대해 함부로 말하지 못하오.\"\n\n\"오, 제가 당신을 결코 잃지 않으리라고—당신이 어떻게도 저를 저버릴 수 없으리라고 확신할 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까요!\"\n\n클림은 잠시 말없이 서 있었다. 그의 감정은 고조되어 있었고, 그 순간은 격정적이었으며, 그는 매듭을 끊어버렸다.\n\n\"당신은 나를 확신해도 좋소, 여보.\" 그가 그녀를 품에 안으며 말했다. \"우리 당장 혼인합시다.\"\n\n\"오, 클림!\"\n\n\"동의하오?\" \"만약—만약 할 수 있다면요.\"\n\n\"우리는 분명 할 수 있소, 둘 다 성년이니. 그리고 나는 이 여러 해 동안 일을 하면서 돈을 모아두지 않은 것도 아니오. 학교를 차리려고 부드머스에 집을 얻을 때까지 당신이 광야 어딘가 자그마한 오두막에서 살기로 동의해준다면, 우리는 아주 적은 비용으로 해낼 수 있소.\"\n\n\"그 자그마한 오두막에서 우리는 얼마나 오래 살아야 해요, 클림?\"\n\n\"여섯 달쯤이오. 그쯤 되면 나는 공부를 마칠 거요—그래요, 우리 그렇게 합시다, 그러면 이 마음앓이도 끝날 거요. 물론 우리는 완전한 은둔 속에 살 테고, 우리 부부생활은 부드머스에 집을 얻고 나서야 비로소 겉보기로도 시작될 텐데, 그 일에 관해서는 이미 편지를 부쳐두었소. 당신 할아버지가 허락하실까?\"\n\n\"허락하실 것 같아요—여섯 달보다 더 길어지지 않는다는 조건이라면요.\"\n\n\"불행한 일만 일어나지 않는다면 그건 내가 보장하리다.\"\n\n\"불행한 일만 일어나지 않는다면요.\" 그녀가 천천히 되뇌었다.\n\n\"그럴 리야 없소. 가장 사랑하는 이여, 정확한 날을 정합시다.\"\n\n그러고는 그들은 그 문제를 의논했고, 날이 정해졌다. 그날로부터 두 주 뒤로 하기로 했다.\n\n이것으로 그들의 이야기는 끝났고, 유스테이시아는 그를 떠났다. 클림은 그녀가 해를 향해 물러가는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그녀가 멀어질수록 빛나는 햇살이 그녀를 감싸 안았고, 돋아나는 사초와 풀 위로 스치는 그녀의 옷자락 소리가 잦아들었다. 그가 지켜보는 동안, 그 풍경의 죽은 듯한 평평함이 그를 압도했으니, 비록 그가 그 흠 없는 초여름 초록—가장 보잘것없는 풀잎조차 잠시나마 걸치고 있는 그 초록의 아름다움에는 온전히 깨어 있었음에도 그러했다. 그 짓누르는 듯한 수평성에는 너무도 삶의 무대를 떠올리게 하는 무언가가 있었으니, 그것은 그에게 해 아래의 그 어떤 살아 있는 것보다도 우월하지 않고 그저 헐벗은 채 대등할 뿐이라는 느낌을 안겨주었다.\n\n이제 유스테이시아는 그에게 더는 여신이 아니라 한 여인이었으니, 그를 위해 싸우고, 부양하고, 돕고, 그로 인해 욕먹어야 할 존재였다. 더 냉정해진 순간에 이르고 보니 그는 좀 덜 성급한 혼인을 택했으면 했으나, 패가 던져졌으니 그는 이 승부를 따르기로 마음먹었다. 유스테이시아가 오래도록 잘 사랑하기에는 너무 뜨겁게 사랑하는 이들의 명단에 또 하나의 이름을 보태게 될지, 다가올 사건이야말로 분명 그것을 가려낼 손쉬운 방법이었다.\n\nVI. 예오브라이트가 떠나고, 균열이 완성되다\n\n그날 저녁 내내 부지런히 짐을 꾸리는 것을 알리는 분주한 소리들이 예오브라이트의 방에서 아래층의 어머니 귀로 흘러 내려왔다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그는 집을 떠나 다시 광야를 가로질러 나아갔다. 긴 하루의 행군이 그 앞에 놓여 있었으니, 그의 목적은 유스테이시아가 아내가 되었을 때 데려갈 거처를 마련하는 것이었다. 그런 집—작고 외지며 창에 널빤지가 덧대어진 집을 그는 한 달쯤 전에 이스트 에그던 마을 너머 두 마일쯤 떨어진, 통틀어 여섯 마일 거리의 곳에서 우연히 봐두었고, 오늘 그곳으로 발걸음을 향했다.\n\n날씨는 전날 저녁과는 사뭇 달랐다. 작별의 시선에서 유스테이시아를 감싸 안았던 노랗고 수증기 어린 일몰은 변화를 예고했었다. 십일월처럼 비 내리고 거센, 영국 유월의 드물지 않은 그런 날들 가운데 하루였다. 차가운 구름들이 마치 움직이는 슬라이드에 그려진 듯 한 덩어리로 서둘러 지나갔다. 다른 대륙에서 온 수증기들이 바람을 타고 닿아, 그가 걷는 동안 그를 둘러싸고 휘돌다 갈라졌다.\n\n마침내 클림은 그가 태어난 해에 광야에서 떼어내 둘러친 전나무와 너도밤나무 농원의 가장자리에 이르렀다. 이곳의 나무들은 새로 돋은 축축한 잎을 무겁게 인 채, 가지들이 폭풍과 싸우려 한껏 헐벗는 한겨울의 거센 바람 때보다도 지금 더 큰 피해를 입고 있었다. 비에 젖은 어린 너도밤나무들은 절단과 타박상과 불구가 됨과 모진 찢김을 겪고 있었으니, 그로부터 흘러나오는 수액은 앞으로 여러 날 피처럼 흐를 테고, 불태워지는 날까지도 눈에 보일 흉터를 남길 터였다. 줄기마다 뿌리께가 비틀려, 마치 관절 속의 뼈처럼 움직였고, 돌풍이 들이칠 때마다 가지들에서 경련하는 듯한 소리가 났으니, 마치 고통을 느끼는 듯했다. 이웃한 덤불에서 되새 한 마리가 노래하려 애쓰고 있었으나, 바람이 깃털 밑으로 불어 들어 깃이 곤두서고 작은 꽁지가 비틀리게 하더니 끝내 그 노래를 포기하게 만들었다.\n\n그러나 예오브라이트의 왼편으로 몇 자 떨어진, 탁 트인 광야에서는 폭풍이 얼마나 무력하게 이를 갈았던가! 나무들을 찢어발기던 그 돌풍은 가시금작화와 히스를 그저 가벼운 애무로 흔들 뿐이었다. 에그던은 바로 이런 시절을 위해 지어진 곳이었다.\n\n예오브라이트는 정오쯤 빈집에 이르렀다. 그 집은 유스테이시아의 할아버지 집만큼이나 외로웠으나, 광야 가까이 서 있다는 사실은 그 부지를 거의 에워싼 전나무 띠에 가려져 있었다. 그는 주인이 사는 마을까지 일 마일쯤 더 가서, 그와 함께 집으로 돌아와 합의를 매듭지었고, 그 사내는 적어도 방 하나는 이튿날 들어가 살 수 있도록 마련해두겠노라 약속했다. 클림의 뜻은 혼인날 유스테이시아가 합류할 때까지 그곳에서 혼자 사는 것이었다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 풍경을 그토록 크게 바꿔놓은 이슬비를 뚫고 집으로 돌아가는 길에 올랐다. 어제 편히 누워 있던 고사리들은 잎사귀마다 물기를 떨구고 있어, 그가 스쳐 지날 때 다리를 흠뻑 적셨고, 그 앞에서 깡충거리며 달아나는 토끼들의 털은 똑같은 물기 어린 둘레로 인해 거뭇한 가닥으로 엉겨 있었다.\n\n십 마일을 걸은 끝에 그는 충분히 젖고 지친 채로 집에 이르렀다. 그것은 결코 길조의 시작이라 할 수 없었으나, 그는 자기 길을 택했으니 한 치의 흔들림도 보이지 않으리라 다짐했다. 저녁과 이튿날 아침은 떠날 채비를 마무리하는 데 쓰였다. 일단 결심에 이른 마당에 필요 이상으로 단 일 분이라도 집에 더 머무는 것은, 그가 느끼기에 어떤 말이나 표정이나 행동으로 어머니에게 새로운 고통을 안기는 일에 지나지 않을 터였다.\n\n그는 운송 수단을 빌려 그날 두 시까지 짐을 부쳐 보냈다. 다음 차례는 가구 몇 점을 마련하는 일이었는데, 그것들은 오두막에서 임시로 쓰인 뒤 더 나은 종류의 물건들이 더해지면 부드머스의 집에서도 쓸 수 있을 터였다. 그 목적에 충분히 넉넉한 시장이 그가 거처로 정한 곳에서 몇 마일 너머인 앵글베리에 있었으니, 그는 다가오는 밤을 그곳에서 보내기로 작정했다.\n\n이제 남은 것은 어머니에게 작별을 고하는 일뿐이었다. 그가 아래층으로 내려왔을 때 그녀는 여느 때처럼 창가에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"어머니, 떠나려고 합니다.\" 그가 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"네 짐 꾸리는 걸 보고 그러려는 줄 알았다.\" 예오브라이트 부인이, 감정의 입자가 하나도 남지 않도록 고통스럽게 배제된 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"그래도 저와는 벗으로서 헤어져주시겠지요?\"\n\n\"물론이지, 클림.\"\n\n\"이십오 일에 혼인할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"혼인하려는 줄 알고 있었다.\"\n\n\"그러고 나서—그러고 나서 어머니가 오셔서 저희를 봐주셔야 해요. 그러고 나면 저를 더 잘 이해하시게 될 거고, 우리 사정도 지금처럼 비참하지는 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 너를 보러 갈 것 같지는 않구나.\"\n\n\"그러면 그건 제 잘못도, 유스테이시아의 잘못도 아닐 거예요, 어머니. 안녕히 계세요!\"\n\n그는 어머니의 뺨에 입맞추고는 큰 비참함을 안은 채 떠났으니, 그 비참함이 감당할 만한 수준으로 누그러지기까지는 여러 시간이 걸렸다. 처지가 그러하여, 우선 하나의 장벽을 허물지 않고서는 더는 아무 말도 할 수 없었고, 그것은 할 수 없는 일이었다.\n\n예오브라이트가 어머니의 집을 떠나기가 무섭게 그녀의 얼굴은 굳어 있던 기색을 거두고 막막한 절망의 얼굴로 바뀌었다. 한참 뒤 그녀는 울었고, 눈물이 얼마간 위안을 가져다주었다. 그날의 나머지 동안 그녀는 멍한 상태에 가까운 채로 정원 길을 오르내리기만 했다. 밤이 왔으나, 함께 온 안식은 거의 없었다. 이튿날, 무너진 기력을 슬픔 정도로나마 추슬러보려는 본능에 이끌려 그녀는 아들의 방으로 가서, 그가 다시 돌아올 어떤 가상의 때를 위해 제 손으로 방을 정돈했다. 그녀는 꽃들에 약간의 손길을 주었으나, 그것은 건성으로 베푼 것이었으니, 꽃들이 더는 그녀를 매혹하지 못했기 때문이다.\n\n그날 오후 이른 시각에 토머신이 뜻밖에 그녀를 찾아왔을 때 그것은 큰 위안이었다. 이것은 토머신의 혼인 이후 두 친척 사이의 첫 만남은 아니었으니, 지난날의 실수들이 거칠게나마 바로잡혔던 터라 그들은 언제나 기쁨과 편안함으로 서로를 맞이할 수 있었다.\n\n그녀를 따라 문으로 들어온 비스듬한 햇살 띠는 그 젊은 아내에게 잘 어울렸다. 그것은 그녀의 존재가 광야를 밝히듯 그녀를 환히 비추었다. 그녀의 움직임에서, 그녀의 눈길에서, 그녀는 보는 이로 하여금 제 집 둘레에 사는 깃털 달린 생물들을 떠올리게 했다. 그녀에 관한 모든 비유와 우의는 새로 시작해 새로 끝났다. 그녀의 움직임에는 새들의 비행만큼이나 다채로움이 있었다. 생각에 잠겼을 때 그녀는 보이지 않는 날갯짓으로 공중에 멈춰 있는 황조롱이였다. 거센 바람 속에 있을 때면 그녀의 가벼운 몸은 왜가리처럼 나무와 둑에 떠밀렸다. 겁먹었을 때면 물총새처럼 소리 없이 쏜살같이 내달았다. 평온할 때면 제비처럼 미끄러지듯 날았으니, 지금 그녀가 움직이는 모습이 바로 그러했다.\n\n\"정말이지 무척 명랑해 보이는구나, 탬지.\" 예오브라이트 부인이 슬픈 미소를 띠고 말했다. \"데이먼은 어떻게 지내니?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지내요.\"\n\n\"네게 다정하게 대하니, 토머신?\" 그러면서 예오브라이트 부인은 그녀를 유심히 살폈다.\n\n\"제법 괜찮게요.\"\n\n\"그게 솔직한 대답이냐?\"\n\n\"네, 이모. 그이가 다정하지 않다면 이모께 말씀드릴 거예요.\" 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며, 머뭇거리는 어조로 덧붙였다, \"그이가—이걸 이모께 하소연해도 되는 건지 모르겠지만, 어찌해야 할지 잘 모르겠어요. 저는 돈이 좀 필요한데, 그러니까, 이모, 제 자잘한 것들을 사려고요—그런데 그이가 한 푼도 주질 않아요. 달라고 하기는 싫고, 그래도 어쩌면 그이가 안 주는 건 몰라서일지도 몰라요. 제가 그이에게 말을 꺼내야 할까요, 이모?\"\n\n\"당연히 그래야지. 그 일에 대해 한마디도 한 적이 없니?\"\n\n\"있잖아요, 제게 제 돈이 좀 있었거든요.\" 토머신이 둘러대듯 말했다, \"그래서 최근까지는 그이의 돈이 필요하지 않았어요. 지난주에 그 얘기를 살짝 꺼내기는 했는데, 그이는—기억하지 못하는 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"기억하게 만들어야 한다. 너도 알다시피 내게는 스페이드 기니가 가득 든 작은 상자가 하나 있는데, 네 큰아버지가 내 손에 맡기면서 내가 원하는 때에 너와 클림에게 나눠주라고 하셨다. 어쩌면 그렇게 할 때가 온 모양이구나. 그 돈은 언제든 소브린으로 바꿀 수 있다.\"\n\n\"제 몫을 받고 싶어요—이모만 괜찮으시다면요.\"\n\n\"필요하다면 받아야지. 하지만 우선 네 남편에게 네게 한 푼도 없다고 분명히 말하고 그가 어찌하는지 보는 것이 옳은 일이다.\"\n\n\"알겠어요, 그럴게요…… 이모, 저 클림 일을 들었어요. 이모가 그 일로 괴로워하시는 걸 알아요, 그래서 온 거예요.\"\n\n예오브라이트 부인은 얼굴을 돌렸고, 감정을 감추려는 시도 속에 그녀의 이목구비가 일그러졌다. 그러더니 그녀는 더는 어떤 시도도 하지 않은 채, 울면서 말했다, \"오 토머신, 그 애가 나를 미워하는 걸까? 이 모든 세월 동안 오로지 그 애만을 위해 살았는데, 어찌 그 애가 나를 이토록 슬프게 할 수 있단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"미워하다니요—아니에요.\" 토머신이 달래듯 말했다. \"그저 그 사람을 너무 깊이 사랑하는 것뿐이에요. 차분히 보세요—그러세요. 그가 그렇게 아주 나쁜 건 아니에요. 아세요, 저는 그가 맺을 수 있는 최악의 인연은 아니라고 생각했어요. 바이 양의 집안은 어머니 쪽으로는 좋은 가문이고, 그녀의 아버지는 낭만적인 방랑자였어요—일종의 그리스의 오디세우스 같은 사람이었지요.\"\n\n\"소용없다, 토머신, 소용없어. 네 뜻은 좋다만, 너를 수고롭게 따지게 하지는 않으마. 양쪽에서 할 수 있는 말은 전부 몇 번이고, 또 몇 번이고 곱씹어보았다. 클림과 나는 화가 나서 헤어진 게 아니다. 우리는 그보다 더 나쁜 식으로 헤어졌어. 내 마음을 무너뜨렸을 건 격정적인 다툼이 아니라, 그 애가 보인 그 한결같은 반항과, 그릇된 길로 끝까지 밀고 나가는 고집이다. 오 토머신, 그 애는 어린 사내아이일 때 그토록 착했단다—그토록 다정하고 상냥했어!\"\n\n\"그랬지요, 알아요.\"\n\n\"내가 내 자식이라 부르던 아이가 자라서 나를 이렇게 대하게 될 줄은 생각도 못 했다. 마치 내가 자기를 해치려고 반대하는 것처럼 내게 말하더구나. 내가 그 애가 잘못되기를 바라기라도 한다는 듯이!\"\n\n\"세상에는 유스테이시아 바이보다 더 나쁜 여자들도 있어요.\"\n\n\"더 나은 여자들이 너무 많지—그게 바로 고통이다. 네 남편이 그렇게 행동하도록 이끈 건 그 애였다, 토머신, 오직 그 애뿐이었어—나는 맹세할 수 있다!\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 토머신이 다급하게 말했다. \"그가 그녀를 마음에 둔 건 저를 알기도 전이었고, 그건 그저 한낱 가벼운 희롱에 지나지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"좋다, 그렇다고 해두자. 이제 와서 그걸 풀어헤쳐봐야 별 소용이 없지. 아들들이란 자기가 그러고자 하면 눈멀기 마련이니까. 어찌하여 여자는 멀리서도 사내가 코앞에서도 못 보는 걸 보는 걸까? 클림은 제멋대로 하라지—그 애는 내게 더는 아무것도 아니다. 그리고 이것이 모성이라는 거다—최고의 세월과 최고의 사랑을 다 바쳐 멸시당할 운명을 보장받는 일 말이다!\"\n\n\"이모는 너무 완고하세요. 이 정도 일을 그토록 깊이 느끼시기 전에, 진짜 범죄로 아들이 어머니를 세상의 수치 속에 빠뜨린 어머니들이 얼마나 많은지 생각해보세요.\"\n\n\"토머신, 내게 설교하지 마라—그건 견딜 수 없구나. 타격의 힘을 만드는 건 우리가 기대한 것을 넘어서는 그 초과분인데, 그 초과분이 그들의 경우라고 해서 내 경우보다 더 크지는 않을 게다—그들은 최악을 미리 내다보았을지도 모르니까…… 나는 잘못 만들어진 사람이다, 토머신.\" 그녀가 애통한 미소를 띠고 덧붙였다. \"어떤 과부들은 마음을 다른 남편에게로 돌려 삶을 다시 시작함으로써 자식이 안기는 상처를 막아낼 수 있지. 하지만 나는 늘 가엾고 나약한, 한 가지 생각밖에 모르는 사람이었어—그럴 만한 마음의 그릇도, 그럴 만한 진취성도 없었다. 남편의 혼이 날아가버렸을 때처럼 처량하고 멍한 채로 나는 그 후로 줄곧 주저앉아 있었지—사정을 고쳐보려는 시도조차 한 번 하지 않은 채로 말이다. 그때 나는 비교적 젊은 여자였으니, 지금쯤이면 또 다른 가족을 두었을 수도 있었고, 이 한 아들의 실패에 대해 그들에게서 위로를 받았을 수도 있었을 거다.\"\n\n\"그러지 않으신 게 더 고결한 거예요.\"\n\n\"고결할수록, 어리석은 거지.\"\n\n\"잊어버리시고 마음을 가라앉히세요, 이모. 그리고 저는 이모를 오래 혼자 두지 않을 거예요. 매일 와서 뵐게요.\"\n\n그리고 한 주 동안 토머신은 말 그대로 약속을 지켰다. 그녀는 혼사를 대수롭지 않게 넘기려 애썼고, 준비 상황에 관한 소식과, 자신이 참석하도록 초대받았다는 소식을 전해주었다. 그다음 주에 그녀는 다소 몸이 안 좋아 모습을 보이지 않았다. 기니에 관해서는 아직 아무것도 진행된 것이 없었으니, 토머신이 그 문제로 다시 남편에게 말 꺼내기를 두려워했고, 예오브라이트 부인이 그렇게 하도록 고집했기 때문이었다.\n\n이 무렵 직전의 어느 날 와일디브가 콰이엇 우먼의 문가에 서 있었다. 광야를 가로질러 레인배로와 미스토버로 올라가는 길 말고도, 여관에서 조금 아래쪽 큰길에서 갈라져 나와 빙 둘러 완만하게 경사를 이루며 미스토버로 오르는 길이 하나 있었다. 그쪽에서 선장의 은거지로 마차가 다닐 수 있는 길은 이것뿐이었다. 가장 가까운 읍에서 온 가벼운 짐수레가 그 길을 내려왔고, 수레를 몰던 젊은이가 마실 것을 청하려 여관 앞에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"미스토버에서 오는 길인가?\" 와일디브가 말했다.\n\n\"네. 저 위에 좋은 물건들을 들이고 있던데요. 혼사가 있을 모양이에요.\" 그러고는 마부가 머그잔에 얼굴을 파묻었다.\n\n와일디브는 그전까지 그 사실에 대해 한 톨의 낌새도 받지 못했던 터라, 별안간 고통의 표정이 그의 얼굴에 번졌다. 그는 그것을 감추려고 잠시 복도 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 그러고는 다시 돌아왔다.\n\n\"바이 양 말인가?\" 그가 말했다. \"어찌하여—그녀가 그렇게 빨리 혼인할 수 있단 말이지?\"\n\n\"하느님의 뜻이고 채비된 젊은 사내가 있어서겠지요.\"\n\n\"예오브라이트 씨를 말하는 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"맞아요. 그 사람이 봄내 그녀와 슬금슬금 만나고 다녔잖아요.\"\n\n\"그러면—그녀가 그에게 단단히 빠졌단 말인가?\"\n\n\"그녀가 그 사람한테 푹 빠졌다고, 그 댁 막일하는 하녀가 그러더라고요. 그리고 말을 돌보는 그 찰리라는 녀석도 그 일로 얼이 다 빠져 있어요. 그 멍청이가 그녀한테 마음을 둔 거지요.\"\n\n\"그녀가 들떠 있던가—기뻐하던가? 그렇게 빨리 혼인하다니—원!\"\n\n\"그렇게 아주 빠른 건 아니에요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇게 아주 빠른 건 아니지.\"\n\n와일디브는 묘한 가슴앓이를 품은 채 빈방으로 들어갔다. 그는 팔꿈치를 벽난로 선반에 괴고 얼굴을 손에 묻었다. 토머신이 방에 들어왔을 때 그는 자신이 들은 것을 그녀에게 말하지 않았다. 유스테이시아를 향한 옛 갈망이 그의 영혼 속에 되살아났으니—그것은 주로 다른 사내가 그녀를 차지하려 한다는 것을 알아챘기 때문이었다.\n\n어려운 것을 갈망하고, 손에 주어진 것에는 싫증 내며, 멀리 있는 것에 마음 쓰고, 가까이 있는 것을 싫어하는 것—그것이 언제나 와일디브의 천성이었다. 이것이야말로 감상가의 참된 표지다. 비록 와일디브의 들뜬 감정이 진정한 시적 경지로까지 다듬어진 것은 아니었으나, 그것은 그 표준적인 부류에 속하는 것이었다. 그를 두고 에그던의 루소라 불러도 좋았으리라.\n\nVII. 어느 하루의 아침과 저녁\n\n혼인날 아침이 밝았다. 그날 블룸스엔드가 미스토버에 무슨 관심이라도 두고 있으리라고는 겉모습으로는 누구도 상상하지 못했을 것이다. 클림의 어머니 집 둘레에는 엄숙한 정적이 감돌았고, 집 안에도 더 이상의 생기는 없었다. 예식 참석을 마다했던 예오브라이트 부인은, 현관과 곧장 통하는 옛 방의 아침상 곁에 앉아, 눈을 멍하니 열린 문 쪽으로 향하고 있었다. 그것은 여섯 달 전 흥겨운 크리스마스 모임이 열렸던 방이요, 유스테이시아가 남몰래 낯선 이로 가장하고 찾아왔던 그 방이었다. 지금 그 방에 들어온 유일한 생물은 참새 한 마리였는데, 놀랄 만한 움직임이 없는 것을 보고는 대담하게 방 안을 통통 뛰어다니다가 창문으로 나가려 애쓰며 화분 꽃들 사이에서 파닥거렸다. 이것이 외로이 앉아 있던 이를 일깨웠으니, 그녀는 일어나 새를 놓아주고는 문가로 갔다. 그녀는 토머신을 기다리고 있었는데, 토머신이 전날 밤 편지를 보내, 이제 그 돈을 받고 싶은 때가 왔으며 될 수 있으면 오늘 들르겠다고 알려왔던 것이다.\n\n그러나 토머신은, 나비들이 가득하고 사방에서 메뚜기들이 내는 쉰 듯한 소리가 속삭이는 합창을 이루는 광야의 골짜기를 올려다보는 동안, 예오브라이트 부인의 마음을 조금밖에 차지하지 못했다. 일이 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에서 지금 준비가 진행되고 있는 한 편의 가정사가, 마치 그녀의 눈앞에서 펼쳐지는 것이나 다름없을 만큼 생생하게 그녀의 눈에 어렸다. 그녀는 그 환영을 떨쳐내려 애쓰며 정원 뙈기를 거닐었으나, 그 눈은 이따금 미스토버가 속한 교구 교회 쪽을 자꾸만 찾았고, 흥분한 그녀의 상상은 그 건물을 그녀의 눈에서 갈라놓고 있는 언덕들을 가르고 들어갔다.\n아침이 흘러갔다. 열한 시를 알리는 종이 울렸다—그렇다면 혼례가 그때 진행되고 있다는 것인가? 분명 그러할 터였다. 그녀는 이 무렵이면 그가 신부를 데리고 다다랐을 교회의 광경을 계속 떠올렸다. 토머신에게서 들었듯이 그 짧은 여정을 치르기 위해 타고 갈 조랑말 마차가 다가올 때 문가에 모여 있을 자그마한 아이들 무리를 그녀는 그려보았다. 그러고는 그들이 들어와 성단소로 나아가 무릎 꿇는 것을 보았고, 예식이 진행되는 듯했다.\n\n그녀는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. \"오, 이건 잘못된 거야!\" 그녀가 신음했다. \"그리고 그 애는 언젠가 이를 뉘우치며 나를 떠올릴 거야!\"\n\n그녀가 그렇게 불길한 예감에 사로잡힌 채 머물러 있는 동안, 집 안의 낡은 시계가 윙 소리를 내며 열두 번을 쳤다. 곧이어 언덕 너머 먼 곳에서 희미한 소리들이 그녀의 귀로 흘러왔다. 산들바람이 그쪽에서 불어와, 멀리서 한바탕 흥겹게 울려 퍼지기 시작한 종소리를 실어 왔다. 하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯. 이스트 에그던의 종지기들이 유스테이시아와 그녀의 아들의 혼례를 알리고 있었다.\n\n\"그러면 끝난 거구나.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"그래, 그래! 삶도 곧 끝나겠지. 그런데 어찌하여 나는 이렇게 계속 얼굴이 따갑도록 울고 있는 걸까? 삶의 한 가지 일로 울면, 모든 일로 우는 셈이지. 한 가닥 실이 천 전체를 꿰뚫고 흐르니까. 그런데도 우리는 '웃을 때가 있다'고 말하지!\"\n\n저녁 무렵 와일디브가 왔다. 토머신의 혼인 이후 예오브라이트 부인은 그에게, 원치 않는 인척 관계의 이런 모든 경우에 끝내 생겨나는 그 무뚝뚝한 우호를 보여왔다. 마땅히 그러했어야 할 것에 대한 환상은 순전한 지침 속에 내던져지고, 기죽은 인간의 노력은 무기력하게 있는 그대로의 사실을 최선으로 받아들인다. 와일디브는, 공평하게 말하자면, 아내의 이모에게 매우 정중하게 처신해왔으니, 그녀가 지금 그가 들어서는 것을 보고도 놀라지 않은 것은 그래서였다.\n\n\"토머신이 약속한 대로 올 수 없게 됐습니다.\" 그녀의 물음에 그가 대답했는데, 그 물음은 근심에 찬 것이었으니, 그녀는 조카가 돈이 몹시 궁하다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다. \"선장이 어젯밤 내려와서 직접 오늘 함께하자고 그녀를 졸랐어요. 그래서 모나게 굴지 않으려고 그녀가 가기로 작정했지요. 조랑말 마차로 그녀를 데려갔고, 다시 데려다줄 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 일이 다 끝났군요.\" 예오브라이트 부인이 말했다. \"그들은 새 보금자리로 갔나요?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다. 토머신이 떠난 뒤로 미스토버에서는 아무 소식도 받지 못했어요.\"\n\n\"자네는 그 애와 함께 가지 않았나요?\" 그녀가, 마치 그럴 만한 까닭이라도 있다는 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"갈 수 없었어요.\" 와일디브가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"우리 둘 다 집을 비울 수는 없었지요. 앵글베리 큰 장날이라 꽤 분주한 아침이었거든요. 토머신에게 주실 게 있다고 들었는데요? 괜찮으시면 제가 가져가겠습니다.\"\n\n예오브라이트 부인은 망설이며, 와일디브가 그 무언가가 무엇인지 알고 있는지 의아해했다. \"그 애가 자네에게 이 얘길 했나요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"딱히 그런 건 아닙니다. 무슨 물건인가를 가져오기로 했다고 지나가는 말로 한마디 흘리더군요.\"\n\n\"굳이 보낼 필요는 없어요. 그 애가 오고 싶을 때 언제든 가져갈 수 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 당분간 안 될 겁니다. 지금 그 사람 건강 상태로는 그동안 해온 것처럼 그렇게 많이 걸어 다녀선 안 되거든요.\" 그가, 그에게 흔한 희미한 빈정거림의 가락을 띠고 덧붙였다. \"제가 미덥지 못해서 맡길 수 없는 그 대단한 물건이란 게 뭡니까?\"\n\n\"자네를 수고롭게 할 만한 건 아니에요.\"\n\n\"제 정직함을 의심하시는 것 같군요.\" 그가 웃으며 말했으나, 그에게 잦은 빠른 분개로 얼굴빛이 달아올랐다.\n\n\"그런 생각은 할 필요 없어요.\" 그녀가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"그저 나도 세상 사람들과 마찬가지로, 어떤 일들은 다른 이보다 특정한 이가 하는 편이 낫다고 느끼는 것뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"좋으실 대로, 좋으실 대로 하세요.\" 와일디브가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"다툴 만한 일도 아니지요. 음, 이제 그만 집으로 돌아가야겠습니다, 여관을 머슴애와 하녀에게만 오래 맡겨둘 수는 없으니까요.\"\n\n그는 제 갈 길을 갔으니, 그 작별은 인사 때만큼 정중하지도 않았다. 그러나 예오브라이트 부인은 이 무렵이면 그를 속속들이 알고 있었으므로, 좋든 나쁘든 그의 태도에 거의 개의치 않았다.\n\n와일디브가 떠나자 예오브라이트 부인은 서서, 와일디브에게 맡기고 싶지 않았던 그 기니에 관해 어떤 길을 택하는 것이 가장 좋을지 곰곰이 생각했다. 토머신이 그에게 그것을 청해 오라고 시켰으리라고는 좀처럼 믿기 어려웠으니, 애초에 그것이 필요해진 까닭이 바로 그의 손에서 돈을 얻어내기 어려웠기 때문이었다. 동시에 토머신은 정말로 그것이 필요했고, 적어도 또 한 주는 블룸스엔드에 오지 못할지도 몰랐다. 여관으로 돈을 가져가거나 보내는 것은 사려 깊지 못한 일일 터였으니, 와일디브가 거의 틀림없이 자리에 있거나 그 거래를 알아챌 것이고, 만약 그녀의 이모가 의심하는 대로 그가 그녀를 받아 마땅한 만큼 다정하게 대하지 않는다면, 그는 그녀의 온순한 손에서 그 전액을 빼앗아 갈지도 몰랐다. 그러나 바로 이날 저녁 토머신은 미스토버에 있었으니, 그곳에서라면 남편 모르게 무엇이든 그녀에게 전할 수 있었다. 대체로 이 기회는 활용할 만한 가치가 있었다.\n\n게다가 그녀의 아들도 거기 있었고, 이제 혼인한 몸이었다. 그에게 그의 몫의 돈을 건넬 만한 때로 지금보다 더 적절한 순간은 없을 터였다. 그리고 그에게 이 선물을 보냄으로써 자신이 그에게 악감정을 품기는커녕 얼마나 그러지 않는지를 보여줄 기회가 주어진다는 것이 그 슬픈 어머니의 마음에 위안을 주었다.\n\n그녀는 위층으로 올라가 잠긴 서랍에서 작은 상자를 꺼냈고, 거기서 여러 해 동안 거기 놓여 있던, 넓적하고 한 번도 닳지 않은 기니 무더기를 쏟아냈다. 모두 백 개였고, 그녀는 그것을 두 무더기로, 각각 쉰 개씩 나누었다. 이것들을 작은 캔버스 자루에 묶고 나서 그녀는 정원으로 내려가, 정작 받을 자격도 없는 저녁밥을 바라며 어슬렁거리고 있던 크리스천 캔틀을 불렀다. 예오브라이트 부인은 그에게 돈 자루를 건네며, 미스토버로 가서 어떤 경우에도 그것을 자기 아들과 토머신 말고는 누구의 손에도 넘기지 말라고 단단히 일렀다. 더 생각한 끝에 그녀는, 그가 그 중요성을 충분히 새기도록 두 자루에 무엇이 들었는지를 크리스천에게 정확히 일러두는 것이 좋겠다고 판단했다. 크리스천은 돈 자루를 주머니에 넣고 더없이 조심하겠노라 약속한 뒤 길을 나섰다.\n\n\"서두를 것 없다.\" 예오브라이트 부인이 말했다. \"해가 진 뒤에 도착하는 편이 나을 게다, 그러면 아무도 너를 눈여겨보지 않을 테니. 너무 늦지 않거든 여기로 돌아와 저녁을 먹으렴.\"\n\n그가 미스토버 쪽 골짜기를 오르기 시작한 것은 거의 아홉 시 무렵이었으나, 여름의 긴 낮이 절정에 이른 터라 저녁의 첫 어스름이 이제 막 풍경을 거뭇하게 그을리기 시작했을 뿐이었다. 여정의 이 지점에서 크리스천은 사람들 목소리를 들었고, 그것이 자기 앞쪽의 우묵한 곳을 가로질러 가는 한 무리의 남녀에게서 나는 것임을 알았으니, 그들의 머리 꼭대기만이 보일 뿐이었다.\n\n그는 멈춰 서서 자기가 지닌 돈을 떠올렸다. 크리스천이라 해도 강도질을 진지하게 두려워하기에는 거의 너무 이른 때였으나, 그럼에도 그는 소년 시절 이래로 두세 실링 이상을 몸에 지닐 때마다 채택해온 한 가지 예방책을 취했으니—그것은 똑같은 우려에 사로잡힌 피트 다이아몬드 주인의 것과 다소 비슷한 예방책이었다. 그는 부츠를 벗고 기니 자루를 풀어, 한쪽 작은 자루의 내용물을 오른쪽 부츠에, 다른 쪽 것을 왼쪽 부츠에 쏟아붓고는, 결코 발 크기에 한정되지 않은 실로 널찍한 함이라 할 만한 각 부츠의 바닥에 될 수 있는 한 평평하게 펼쳤다. 다시 부츠를 신고 맨 위까지 끈을 졸라맨 뒤, 그는 발바닥 밑보다는 머릿속이 더 편안해진 채로 제 갈 길을 나아갔다.\n\n그의 길은 그 시끌벅적한 무리의 길과 차츰 가까워졌고, 더 가까이 다가가면서 그는 안도하게도 그들이 자신이 아주 잘 아는 에그던 사람들 여럿이며, 그들과 함께 블룸스엔드의 페어웨이가 걷고 있음을 알았다.\n\n\"아니! 크리스천도 가는 길인가?\" 페어웨이가 새로 온 이를 알아보자마자 말했다. \"자네야 가운 감을 사줄 처녀도, 마누라도 없을 게 분명한데.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이오?\" 크리스천이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 추첨 말이야. 우리가 해마다 가는 그거. 우리처럼 추첨하러 가는 거 아닌가?\"\n\n\"그런 건 한 마디도 들은 적 없소. 그게 몽둥이 시합이나, 뭐 피 보는 다른 놀이 같은 거요? 가고 싶지 않소, 고맙소만, 페어웨이 씨, 기분 상하진 마시오.\"\n\n\"크리스천은 그게 무슨 재미인지 모르니, 저 친구한테는 좋은 구경거리겠네.\" 한 통통한 여인이 말했다. \"위험이라곤 전혀 없어, 크리스천. 다들 한 사람당 일 실링씩 내고, 마누라나 정인이 있으면 그중 하나가 그이한테 줄 가운 감을 따는 거지.\"\n\n\"음, 그건 내 팔자가 아니니 내겐 아무 의미도 없소. 하지만 거기에 흑마술 같은 건 없고, 돈 안 들이고 위험한 시비에 휘말리지 않고 구경만 할 수 있다면, 그 재미는 보고 싶긴 하오.\"\n\n\"소란이라곤 전혀 없을 거야.\" 티머시가 말했다. \"정말이지, 크리스천, 자네가 오고 싶다면 우리가 아무 일도 안 생기게 봐줌세.\"\n\n\"그리고 음탕한 흥청거림 같은 것도 없겠지요? 보시오, 이웃들, 만약 그런 게 있다면 아버지께 나쁜 본을 보이는 셈이 될 거요, 그 어른이 워낙 마음이 가벼우신 분이니 말이오. 하지만 일 실링에 가운 감이고, 흑마술도 없다면—들여다볼 만한 가치가 있고, 반시간도 안 걸릴 테지. 그래, 가겠소, 끝나고 밤이 깊었는데 그쪽으로 가는 사람이 달리 없다면, 미스토버 쪽으로 조금만 데려다준다면 말이오.\"\n\n한둘이 약속했고, 크리스천은 곧장 가던 길에서 벗어나 일행과 함께 오른쪽으로 돌아 콰이엇 우먼 쪽으로 향했다. 그들이 여관의 널따란 공동방에 들어섰을 때 그곳에는 인근 주민들 가운데 열 명쯤이 모여 있었고, 새로 합류한 무리로 그 수는 두 배로 불어났다. 그들 대부분은, 거친 성당 좌석 같은 나무 팔걸이로 칸막이가 된 자리에 빙 둘러앉아 있었는데, 그 자리에는 지난 시절 한자리에서 낮과 밤을 보내다가 이제는 알코올에 절은 한 줌의 재가 되어 가장 가까운 묘지에 누워 있는 수많은 이름난 술꾼들의 이름 머리글자가 새겨져 있었다. 긴 탁자 위 술잔들 사이에는\n도박에 참가한 이들 앞에는 얇은 천이 든 꾸러미가 펼쳐져 있었으니—이른바 옷감이라 불리는 것으로—제비뽑기의 상품이었다. 와일디브는 벽난로를 등진 채 서서 시가를 피우고 있었고, 먼 고장에서 온 행상인이자 이 제비뽑기를 주관하는 사내는 여름 드레스 옷감으로 이만한 게 없다며 그 천의 값어치를 늘어놓고 있었다.\n\n\"자, 신사 여러분.\" 새로 들어온 이들이 탁자로 다가서자 그가 말을 이었다. \"다섯 분이 들어오셨고, 수를 채우려면 네 분이 더 있어야 합니다. 방금 들어오신 신사분들의 얼굴을 보아하니, 푼돈으로 부인을 단장시킬 이 흔치 않은 기회를 놓치지 않으실 만큼 영리한 분들이로군요.\"\n\n페어웨이와 샘, 그리고 또 한 사람이 탁자 위에 1실링씩을 올려놓았고, 사내는 크리스천을 향했다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 나리.\" 크리스천이 의심스러운 눈빛으로 얼른 훑어보며 뒤로 물러섰다. \"저는 그저 구경이나 하러 온 가난뱅이일 뿐입니다요, 괜찮으시다면요, 나리. 어떻게 하는 건지조차 모릅니다요. 만일 따게 될 게 확실하다면야 1실링을 내겠지만, 그렇지 않고서야 못합니다요.\"\n\n\"거의 확실하다고 봐도 될 겁니다.\" 행상인이 말했다. \"실은, 이렇게 얼굴을 들여다보니, 따리라고 장담할 순 없어도, 평생 이만큼 딸 것처럼 생긴 얼굴은 본 적이 없다고는 말할 수 있겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 우리 나머지하고 똑같은 가망은 있는 거잖나.\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 맨 마지막에 온 운까지 더해지고 말이지.\" 또 한 사람이 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 난 양막을 쓰고 태어났으니, 물에 빠져 죽는 것 말고는 망할 일이 없는지도 모르지?\" 크리스천이 마음이 흔들리기 시작하며 덧붙였다.\n\n마침내 크리스천은 1실링을 내려놓았고, 제비뽑기가 시작되어 주사위가 차례로 돌았다. 크리스천의 차례가 오자 그는 떨리는 손으로 주사위 통을 들어 겁먹은 듯 흔들고는 트리플을 던졌다. 다른 셋은 그저 그런 낮은 페어를 던졌고, 나머지는 죄다 끗수뿐이었다.\n\n\"제가 말씀드린 대로 이 신사분이 딸 상이었지요.\" 행상인이 부드럽게 말했다. \"가져가십시오, 나리. 그 물건은 당신 것입니다.\"\n\n\"하―하―하!\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"이렇게 별난 일은 내 살다 살다 처음 보겠구먼!\"\n\n\"제 거라고요?\" 크리스천이 과녁 같은 눈으로 멍하니 바라보며 물었다. \"저―저는 처녀든 아내든 과부든 제게 딸린 여자라곤 하나도 없는데요, 이걸 가지면 사람들한테 웃음거리가 될까 봐 무섭습니다요, 행상인 나리. 끼고 싶은 마음에 그만 그 생각을 못 했습니다요! 제 침실에 여자 옷을 두고서 어떻게 체면을 잃지 않겠습니까요!\"\n\n\"가지고 있게, 그럼.\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"운이라 치고서라도 말이지. 자네 그 가련한 몰골이 맨손으로 서 있을 땐 꼬드기지 못했던 여자라도, 어쩌면 이걸로 꾀어낼지 누가 알겠나.\"\n\n\"가지게, 당연히.\" 멀찍이서 그 장면을 무심히 지켜보던 와일디브가 말했다.\n\n이윽고 탁자 위의 물건들이 치워지고, 사내들은 술을 마시기 시작했다.\n\n\"이런, 정말이지!\" 크리스천이 반쯤 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"내가 이렇게 운 좋게 태어났는데 이제껏 그걸 몰랐다니! 이 주사위라는 게 어찌나 묘한 물건인지―우리 모두를 쥐고 흔드는 막강한 지배자인데도, 내 명령을 따르다니! 이젠 정말이지 무서울 게 하나도 없겠다.\" 그는 주사위를 하나씩 애지중지 만지작거렸다. \"그런데, 나리.\" 그가 왼쪽 가까이에 있는 와일디브에게 은밀히 속삭였다. \"내 안에 깃든 이 돈 불리는 능력을 쓸 수만 있다면, 나리하고 가까운 친척분께 좋은 일을 해드릴 수도 있을 텐데요, 제가 그분 것을 몸에 지니고 있는 걸 생각하면 말입니다요―예?\" 그는 돈이 가득 든 부츠 한 짝으로 바닥을 톡톡 쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 소린가?\" 와일디브가 물었다.\n\n\"그건 비밀입죠. 자, 이제 가봐야겠습니다요.\" 그는 불안한 듯 페어웨이 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n\"어디로 가는가?\" 와일디브가 물었다.\n\n\"미스토버 냅으로요. 거기서 토머신 부인을 뵈어야 합니다요―그게 다입니다요.\"\n\n\"나도 그리로 가는 길이네, 와일디브 부인을 데리러. 같이 걸어가지.\"\n\n와일디브는 깊은 생각에 잠겼고, 그의 눈에 안에서 무언가 밝아지는 빛이 어렸다. 요브라이트 부인이 그에게 맡기지 못한 것이 아내에게 줄 돈이었던 것이다. \"그런데도 이 작자한테는 맡길 수 있단 말이지.\" 그가 속으로 중얼거렸다. \"아내 것이 어찌하여 남편 것이기도 하지 않단 말인가?\" 그는 술 따르는 소년에게 모자를 가져오라 부른 뒤 말했다. \"자, 크리스천, 준비됐네.\"\n\n\"와일디브 나리.\" 크리스천이 방을 나서려 돌아서며 머뭇머뭇 말했다. \"제 운을 그 안에 품고 다니는 그 신통한 작은 물건들을 제게 빌려주시지 않겠습니까요, 혼자서 좀 연습해보게 말입니다요, 아시잖습니까요?\" 그는 벽난로 선반 위에 놓인 주사위와 통을 못내 그리운 듯 바라보았다.\n\n\"물론.\" 와일디브가 무심히 말했다. \"어차피 어느 녀석이 칼로 깎은 것일 뿐, 값나갈 것도 없네.\" 그러자 크리스천은 되돌아가 그것을 슬쩍 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n와일디브는 문을 열고 밖을 내다보았다. 밤은 따뜻하고 흐렸다. \"이런! 캄캄하군.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그래도 길은 찾겠지.\"\n\n\"길을 잃기라도 하면 곤란할 텐데요.\" 크리스천이 말했다. \"등불만이 우리를 안전하게 지켜줄 유일한 방패입니다요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 있어도 등불은 챙기세.\" 마구간 등불을 가져와 불을 붙였다. 크리스천은 옷감을 집어 들었고, 두 사람은 언덕을 오르러 길을 나섰다.\n\n방 안에서는 사내들이 한담을 나누다, 잠시 그들의 주의가 굴뚝 모퉁이로 쏠렸다. 그 모퉁이는 넓었고, 본래의 우묵한 공간 외에도, 에그던의 많은 집들이 그렇듯 양옆 기둥 안쪽으로 뒤로 물러난 자리가 있어서, 불이 켜져 사람을 비추지만 않으면 누구든 거기 앉아 전혀 눈에 띄지 않을 수 있었으니, 지금처럼, 또 여름 내내 그러했다. 그 우묵한 자리에서 단 하나의 물건이 탁자 위 촛불의 빛 속으로 비죽 나와 있었다. 그것은 진흙 파이프였고, 그 빛깔은 불그스름했다. 사내들이 이 물건에 주의를 끌린 것은 파이프 뒤에서 불을 청하는 목소리 때문이었다.\n\n\"맹세코, 그자가 말을 하니 정말 깜짝 놀랐네!\" 페어웨이가 양초를 건네며 말했다. \"오―레들맨이로군! 어지간히도 조용히 입 다물고 있었구먼, 젊은이.\"\n\n\"예, 할 말이 없었으니까요.\" 벤이 말했다. 그는 몇 분 뒤 일어나 일행에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸다.\n\n그러는 사이 와일디브와 크리스천은 황야 속으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n답답하고, 따뜻하고, 안개 자욱한 밤이었으니, 뜨거운 햇볕에 아직 마르지 않은 새 초목의 온갖 짙은 향기가 가득했고, 그 가운데서도 특히 고사리 내음이 그러했다. 크리스천의 손에 매달려 흔들리는 등불은 지나가며 깃털 같은 고사리 잎을 스쳐, 나방과 그 밖의 날벌레들을 휘저어 날아오르게 했고, 그것들은 날아올라 등불의 뿔로 만든 유리판에 내려앉았다.\n\n\"그러니까 자네가 와일디브 부인에게 돈을 가져다주는 거로군?\" 침묵이 흐른 뒤 크리스천의 동행이 말했다. \"그게 내게 주어지지 않은 게 참 이상하다고 생각하지 않나?\"\n\n\"남편과 아내는 한 몸이니, 어느 쪽이든 매한가지였을 거라고 봅니다요.\" 크리스천이 말했다. \"하지만 제가 받은 엄한 지시는, 그 돈을 와일디브 부인 손에 직접 쥐여드리라는 거였습니다요―일은 제대로 하는 게 좋으니까요.\"\n\n\"하긴.\" 와일디브가 말했다. 사정을 아는 사람이라면 누구든, 운반 중인 것이 돈이라는 사실에―블룸스엔드에서 그가 짐작했던 것처럼 두 여인끼리만 관심을 둘 어떤 자질구레한 장신구가 아니라는 사실에―와일디브가 굴욕감을 느끼고 있음을 알아챘으리라. 요브라이트 부인의 거절은 그의 명예가 아내의 재산을 더 안전히 맡길 만큼 충분히 좋은 품질로 여겨지지 않는다는 뜻이었다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 어찌 이리 더운가, 크리스천!\" 그들이 레인배로 거의 아래에 이르렀을 때 그가 헐떡이며 말했다. \"잠깐만 앉아 쉬세, 제발 좀.\"\n\n와일디브는 부드러운 고사리 위에 몸을 던졌고, 크리스천은 등불과 꾸러미를 바닥에 내려놓은 뒤 바로 곁에 무릎이 거의 턱에 닿도록 옹송그린 자세로 자리를 잡았다. 그는 곧 한 손을 외투 주머니에 찔러 넣고 안에서 무언가를 달그락거리기 시작했다.\n\n\"거기서 뭘 그리 달그락거리나?\" 와일디브가 말했다.\n\n\"그저 주사위입니다요, 나리.\" 크리스천이 얼른 손을 빼며 말했다. \"이 작은 물건들은 어찌나 신통한 기계인지요, 와일디브 나리! 아무리 해도 질리지 않을 놀이입니다요. 잠깐 꺼내서 어떻게 만들어졌는지 들여다봐도 괜찮을까요? 아까는 다른 사람들 앞에서 가까이 들여다보기가 꺼려졌습니다요, 무례하다 여길까 봐서요.\" 크리스천은 그것을 꺼내 등불 빛에 손바닥을 오목하게 모아 살펴보았다. \"이 작은 것들이 이런 운과, 이런 마력과, 이런 주술과, 이런 힘을 품고 있다니, 제가 여태 듣거나 본 그 무엇보다도 놀랍습니다요.\" 그는 주사위를 홀린 듯 바라보며 말을 이었으니, 그 주사위는 시골에서 흔히 그렇듯 나무로 만든 것이어서, 면마다 끗수가 철사 끝으로 지져 새겨져 있었다.\n\n\"작은 데에 많은 걸 담고 있다고, 그런 뜻인가?\"\n\n\"예. 정말로 이게 악마의 노리개라고 보십니까요, 와일디브 나리? 만일 그렇다면, 제가 이렇게 운 좋은 사내라는 건 좋은 징조가 아니겠지요.\"\n\n\"이걸 손에 넣었으니 이제 돈을 좀 따야지. 그러면 어느 여자든 자네와 혼인할 걸세. 지금이 자네 때야, 크리스천, 놓치지 말라고 권하고 싶네. 어떤 사내는 운을 타고나고, 어떤 사내는 못 타고나지. 나는 후자에 속하고.\"\n\n\"저 말고 그런 운을 타고난 사람을 아십니까요?\"\n\n\"오, 알지. 언젠가 어느 이탈리아 사람 이야기를 들었네, 주머니에 루이(그게 외국 소브린이지) 한 닢만 들고 도박판에 앉았다더군. 스물네 시간을 내리 노름해서 만 파운드를 따고는, 상대한 물주를 알거지로 만들었다네. 그리고 또 어떤 사내는 천 파운드를 잃고서 빚을 갚으려고 이튿날 증권 중개인에게 주식을 팔러 갔다네. 돈을 빌려준 사람이 그와 함께 삯마차에 탔는데, 시간이나 때울 겸 누가 삯을 낼지 던지기로 정했지. 망한 사내가 이겼고, 다른 쪽이 노름을 계속하자고 꾀어서 가는 내내 던졌다네. 마부가 멈추자 다시 집으로 가자고 했다지. 주식을 팔러 가던 사내가 천 파운드를 통째로 도로 따냈으니 말이야.\"\n\n\"하―하―멋지군요!\" 크리스천이 외쳤다. \"더―더 해주십쇼!\"\n\n\"그리고 런던에 한 사내가 있었으니, 화이트 클럽하우스의 한낱 웨이터였지. 처음엔 반 크라운씩 걸고 시작해서 점점 더 높이, 더 높이 걸다가, 아주 부자가 되어 인도에 자리를 얻고, 마드라스 총독까지 올랐다네. 그 딸은 국회의원과 혼인했고, 칼라일 주교가 그 자식 중 하나의 대부가 되어주었지.\"\n\n\"굉장합니다요! 굉장해요!\"\n\n\"또 한번은 미국에 한 젊은이가 있었는데, 노름을 하다가 마지막 한 달러까지 잃었다네. 회중시계와 줄을 걸고 또 잃고, 우산을 걸고 또 잃고, 모자를 걸고 또 잃고, 외투를 걸고 셔츠 바람으로 남고도 또 잃었지. 바지를 벗기 시작하니, 구경하던 자가 그 배짱이 가상하다며 푼돈을 쥐여주었다네. 그걸로 그가 이겼지. 외투를 도로 따고, 모자를 도로 따고, 우산을, 시계를, 돈을 도로 따서, 부자가 되어 문을 나섰다네.\"\n\n\"오, 너무 좋아서―숨이 막힙니다요! 와일디브 나리, 저도 그런 부류이니, 나리하고 1실링 더 해봐야겠습니다요. 위험할 것도 없고, 나리는 잃어도 괜찮으시잖습니까요.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 와일디브가 일어서며 말했다. 등불을 들고 이리저리 더듬어 그는 크고 평평한 돌을 찾아내 자신과 크리스천 사이에 놓고 다시 앉았다. 더 밝게 하려고 등불을 열어 그 빛을 돌 위로 향하게 했다. 크리스천이 1실링을 내려놓고, 와일디브가 또 1실링을 내려놓고, 각자 던졌다. 크리스천이 이겼다. 둘을 걸고 했더니, 크리스천이 또 이겼다.\n\n\"넷으로 해보세.\" 와일디브가 말했다. 그들은 넷을 걸고 했다. 이번엔 와일디브가 판돈을 땄다.\n\n\"아, 저런 사소한 사고야 물론 더러 일어나는 법이지, 아무리 운 좋은 사내한테도 말이야.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"이제 제겐 돈이 더 없습니다요!\" 크리스천이 들떠서 설명했다. \"그런데도, 계속할 수만 있다면 도로 따고 또 더 딸 텐데요. 이게 내 것이라면.\" 그는 부츠로 바닥을 쳤고, 그러자 안에서 기니가 짤랑거렸다.\n\n\"뭐라! 자네 와일디브 부인의 돈을 거기 넣어둔 건가?\"\n\n\"예. 안전하게 두려고요. 결혼한 부인의 돈으로 제비뽑기를 하는 게 무슨 해가 되겠습니까요, 제가 따더라도 딴 것만 챙기고 그분 몫은 그대로 돌려드릴 거고, 다른 분이 따더라도 그분 돈은 정당한 임자에게 돌아갈 텐데 말입니다요?\"\n\n\"전혀 안 되지.\"\n\n와일디브는 길을 나선 뒤로 줄곧, 아내의 친지들이 자신을 하찮게 여긴다는 사실을 곱씹어왔으니, 그것이 그의 심장을 모질게 도려냈다. 시간이 흐를수록 그는 정확히 어느 순간에 그런 생각이 들었는지도 모른 채 차츰 복수심에 빠져들어 갔다. 그것은 요브라이트 부인에게―그가 보기엔―따끔한 교훈을 주려는 것이었으니, 다시 말해, 할 수만 있다면 그녀의 조카딸 남편이야말로 조카딸 돈의 마땅한 보호자임을 보여주려는 것이었다.\n\n\"자, 간다!\" 크리스천이 부츠 한 짝의 끈을 풀기 시작하며 말했다. \"밤이고 밤이고 이 일을 꿈에 볼 테지만, 생각해도 살갗이 오싹하지 않노라고 늘 맹세하겠습니다요!\"\n\n그는 부츠 안에 손을 넣어 가련한 토머신의 귀한 기니 한 닢을, 뜨끈뜨끈한 것을 꺼냈다. 와일디브는 이미 소브린 한 닢을 돌 위에 올려놓은 터였다. 그리하여 노름이 다시 시작되었다. 와일디브가 먼저 이겼고, 크리스천이 또 한 닢을 걸어 이번엔 자신이 이겼다. 승부는 엎치락뒤치락했으나, 평균적으로는 와일디브에게 유리했다. 두 사내 모두 노름에 어찌나 빠져들었는지, 바로 눈앞의 자그마한 것들 외엔 아무것에도 신경 쓰지 않았으니, 평평한 돌, 열린 등불, 주사위, 그리고 그 빛 아래 깔린 환히 비치는 몇 잎의 고사리가 그들에겐 온 세상이었다.\n\n마침내 크리스천은 빠르게 잃었고, 이내, 소스라치게도, 토머신의 것인 오십 기니가 통째로 그의 상대에게 넘어가버렸다.\n\n\"상관없어―상관없어!\" 그가 신음하며, 나머지 오십 기니를 꺼내려 절박하게 왼쪽 부츠 끈을 풀기 시작했다. \"이 밤에 벌인 일로 악마가 그 세 갈래 갈퀴로 나를 불길 속에 처넣을 테지, 알고말고! 하지만 어쩌면 그래도 딸지도 몰라, 그러면 밤마다 나하고 같이 앉아 지새워줄 아내를 얻을 거고, 그럼 무섭지 않을 거야, 안 무서워! 자, 또 한 닢 가시오, 나리!\" 그는 또 한 닢의 기니를 돌 위에 탁 내려놓았고, 주사위 통이 다시 달그락거렸다.\n\n시간이 흘러갔다. 와일디브도 크리스천 못지않게 흥분하기 시작했다. 노름을 시작할 때만 해도 그의 의도는 요브라이트 부인을 향한 쓰디쓴 짓궂은 장난, 그 이상이 아니었다. 정당하게든 아니든 돈을 따서, 그 이모 앞에서 토머신에게 경멸하듯 건네주는 것이 그가 품은 목적의 흐릿한 윤곽이었다. 그러나 사람은 의도를 실행에 옮기는 와중에도 그 의도에서 멀어지는 법이어서, 스무 번째 기니에 이르렀을 무렵엔 와일디브가 그저 자기 잇속을 챙기려 딴다는 의도 외에 다른 의도를 의식하고 있는지조차 몹시 의심스러웠다. 게다가 그가 이제 거는 것은 아내의 돈이 아니라 요브라이트의 돈이었으나, 이 사실을 크리스천은 겁에 질린 나머지 나중에야 그에게 알렸다.\n\n거의 열한 시가 되었을 때, 크리스천은 비명에 가까운 소리를 내며 요브라이트의 마지막 번득이는 기니를 돌 위에 올려놓았다. 삼십 초 만에 그것은 동무들이 간 길을 따라가버렸다.\n\n크리스천은 몸을 돌려 후회의 발작에 휩싸여 고사리 위로 몸을 던졌다. \"오, 이 비참한 놈을 어찌해야 한단 말인가?\" 그가 신음했다. \"어찌해야 하나? 자비로우신 하늘이 이 죄 많은 영혼에 자비를 베풀어주실까?\"\n\n\"어쩌긴? 그냥 똑같이 살아가는 거지.\"\n\n\"똑같이 살지 않을 테요! 죽어버릴 테요! 당신은―당신은―\"\n\n\"이웃보다 약빠른 사내지.\"\n\n\"그래요, 이웃보다 약빠른 사내, 영락없는 사기꾼이로구먼!\"\n\n\"가련한 죽 속의 부스러기 같으니, 자네 참 무례하군.\"\n\n\"그건 모르겠소! 그리고 당신이야말로 무례하다고 하겠소! 당신은 당신 것도 아닌 돈을 가졌어. 그 기니의 절반은 가련한 클림 도련님 거란 말이오.\"\n\n\"그건 또 무슨 소린가?\"\n\n\"그분께 오십 닢을 드려야 했으니까요. 요브라이트 부인이 그렇게 말씀하셨소.\"\n\n\"오?… 글쎄, 그렇다면 그의 아내 유스테이샤에게 주는 편이 더 점잖았을 텐데. 어쨌든 이젠 내 손에 있네.\"\n\n크리스천은 부츠를 신고서, 멀찍이까지 들릴 만큼 거친 숨을 몰아쉬며 사지를 끌어모아 일어나, 비척비척 시야 밖으로 사라졌다. 와일디브는 집으로 돌아가려 등불을 닫기 시작했으니, 미스토버로 가서 아내를 맞기엔 너무 늦었다고 여겼던 것이고, 그녀는 선장의 네 바퀴 마차로 집까지 실려 갈 참이었다. 그가 작은 뿔판 문을 닫고 있을 때, 가까운 덤불 뒤에서 한 형상이 일어나 등불 빛 속으로 나섰다. 다가오는 것은 레들맨이었다.\n\n와일디브가 빤히 쳐다보았다. 벤은 와일디브 쪽을 차갑게 바라보더니, 한마디도 없이 크리스천이 앉았던 자리에 천천히 앉아, 주머니에 손을 넣어 소브린 한 닢을 꺼내 돌 위에 올려놓았다.\n\n\"저 덤불 뒤에서 우리를 지켜보고 있었나?\" 와일디브가 말했다.\n\n레들맨은 고개를 끄덕였다. \"판돈을 거시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"아니면 계속할 배짱이 없는 거요?\"\n\n그런데 도박이란 주머니가 두둑할 때 시작하기는 쉬워도 그 두둑한 채로 손 떼기는 훨씬 어려운 종류의 소일거리여서, 와일디브가 좀 더 냉정한 마음이었다면 신중히 이 권유를 물렸을지 몰라도, 방금 거둔 성공의 흥분이 그를 완전히 휩쓸어갔다. 그는 레들맨의 소브린 곁에, 평평한 돌 위에 기니 한 닢을 올려놓았다. \"내 건 기니일세.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"당신 것도 아닌 기니지.\" 벤이 빈정대며 말했다.\n\n\"내 것이네.\" 와일디브가 거만하게 답했다. \"아내 것이고, 아내 것은 곧 내 것이니까.\"\n\n\"좋소. 그럼 시작해봅시다.\" 그는 통을 흔들어 여덟, 열, 아홉을 던졌고, 세 번 던진 합이 스물일곱이었다.\n\n이에 와일디브가 고무되었다. 그가 통을 들었고, 세 번 던진 합이 마흔다섯이었다.\n\n레들맨의 또 다른 소브린 한 닢이, 와일디브가 올려둔 그의 첫 닢을 마주하여 놓였다. 이번에 와일디브는 끗수로 쉰하나를 던졌으나 페어는 없었다. 레들맨은 무뚝뚝한 얼굴로 에이스만 가득한 패를 던지고는 판돈을 챙겼다.\n\n\"또 시작이로군.\" 와일디브가 경멸하듯 말했다. \"판돈을 두 배로 하지.\" 그는 토머신의 기니 두 닢을 올려놓았고, 레들맨은 2파운드를 올려놓았다. 또 벤이 이겼다. 새 판돈이 돌 위에 놓였고, 노름꾼들은 전과 같이 계속했다.\n\n와일디브는 신경이 곤두서고 쉬이 흥분하는 사내였으니, 노름이 그의 성미를 건드리기 시작했다. 그는 몸을 뒤틀고, 씩씩거리고, 자리를 옮겼으며, 심장 뛰는 소리가 거의 들릴 지경이었다. 벤은 입술을 무심히 다물고 눈을 하찮은 한 쌍의 반짝임으로 좁힌 채 앉아 있었으니, 숨을 쉬는 기색조차 거의 없었다. 그는 아랍인이거나 자동인형 같았으니, 주사위 통을 든 팔의 움직임만 아니었다면 붉은 사암으로 만든 조상 같았으리라.\n\n승부는 엎치락뒤치락하며, 어느 한쪽이 크게 유리하다 할 것도 없이 이쪽으로 갔다 저쪽으로 갔다 했다. 그렇게 거의 이십 분이 지났다. 그새 촛불의 빛이 황야의 파리며 나방이며 그 밖의 밤 날벌레들을 끌어모았으니, 그것들은 등불 둘레를 떠다니다, 불꽃 속으로 날아들거나, 두 노름꾼의 얼굴 언저리에서 푸덕거렸다.\n\n그러나 두 사내는 그런 것엔 별 주의를 두지 않았으니, 그들의 눈은 그 자그마한 평평한 돌에 쏠려 있었고, 그것은 그들에게 전장만큼이나 광대하고 중대한 격투장이었다. 이 무렵 노름에 변화가 찾아왔으니, 레들맨이 내리 이겼다. 마침내 육십 기니―토머신의 오십, 그리고 클림의 십―가 그의 손으로 넘어갔다. 와일디브는 무모하고, 광란하고, 격분했다.\n\n\"'외투를 도로 따고,'\" 벤이 능청맞게 말했다.\n\n또 한 번 던졌고, 돈은 같은 길을 갔다.\n\n\"'모자를 도로 따고,'\" 벤이 이어 말했다.\n\n\"오, 오!\" 와일디브가 말했다.\n\n\"'시계를 도로 따고, 돈을 도로 따서, 부자가 되어 문을 나섰다,'\" 벤이 판돈이 자기 쪽으로 넘어올 때마다 한 구절씩 덧붙였다.\n\n\"다섯 닢 더!\" 와일디브가 돈을 내동댕이치며 외쳤다. \"세 번 던지기는 집어치워―한 번으로 결판내자고.\"\n\n맞은편의 붉은 자동인형은 침묵에 잠겨 고개를 끄덕이고는 그를 따랐다. 와일디브가 통을 달그락거려 육 한 쌍에 끗수 다섯을 던졌다. 그는 손뼉을 쳤다. \"이번엔 해냈군―만세!\"\n\n\"노름하는 사람이 둘인데, 던진 건 하나뿐이오.\" 레들맨이 조용히 통을 내려놓으며 말했다. 그러자 둘의 눈이 어찌나 골똘히 돌 위로 모였던지, 그 시선의 빛줄기가 안개 속의 광선처럼 눈에 보이는 듯했다.\n\n벤이 통을 들어올리자, 보라, 육 세 개가 드러났다. 와일디브는 분노로 가득 찼다. 레들맨이 판돈을 그러쥐는 사이, 와일디브는 주사위를 움켜쥐고 통째로 어둠 속으로 내던지며 끔찍한 저주를 내뱉었다. 그러고는 일어나 미친 사람처럼 위아래로 쿵쿵거리며 걷기 시작했다.\n\n\"그럼 다 끝난 거요?\" 벤이 말했다.\n\n\"아냐, 아냐!\" 와일디브가 외쳤다. \"난 한 번 더 기회를 가질 작정이오. 그래야 해!\"\n\n\"한데, 이보쇼, 주사위를 어찌해버린 거요?\"\n\n\"내던졌소―한순간의 화풀이였소. 내가 바보 천치지! 자―와서 찾는 걸 도와주시오―다시 찾아야 하오.\"\n\n와일디브는 등불을 낚아채 가시금작화와 고사리 사이를 안달하며 헤집기 시작했다.\n\n\"거기선 찾기 어려울 거요.\" 벤이 뒤따르며 말했다. \"대체 무슨 미친 짓을 한 거요? 여기 통은 있소. 주사위도 멀리 가진 않았을 거요.\"\n\n와일디브는 벤이 통을 찾은 자리에 등불을 들이대고는 풀숲을 이리저리 마구 헤집었다. 몇 분이 지나 주사위 하나를 찾았다. 그들은 한동안 더 뒤졌으나, 다른 하나는 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"괜찮소.\" 와일디브가 말했다. \"하나로 합시다.\"\n\n\"좋소.\" 벤이 말했다.\n\n그들은 다시 앉아 기니 한 닢씩을 걸고 다시 시작했고, 노름은 재빠르게 진행되었다. 그러나 운명은 오늘 밤 분명코 레들맨과 사랑에 빠진 터였다. 그는 꾸준히 이겨 금화를 열네 닢 더 손에 넣었다. 백 기니 중 일흔아홉이 그의 것이었고, 와일디브에겐 고작 스물하나가 남았다. 두 적수의 모습은 이제 기묘했다. 몸짓을 제쳐두면, 노름의 부침을 고스란히 담은 디오라마가 그들의 눈 속에서 펼쳐지고 있었다. 자그마한 촛불의 불꽃이 저마다의 눈동자에 비쳤으니, 그 안에서 희망의 기색과 체념의 기색을 분간하는 것이 가능했고, 얼굴 근육은 아무것도 드러내지 않았으나 레들맨조차 그러했다. 와일디브는 절망의 무모함으로 노름을 이어갔다.\n\n\"저게 뭐야?\" 그가 부스럭 소리를 듣고 갑자기 외쳤고, 둘 다 고개를 들었다.\n\n그들은 키 4피트에서 5피트쯤 되는 거무스름한 형상들에 둘러싸여 있었으니, 등불 빛이 닿는 곳에서 몇 걸음 떨어진 데 서 있었다. 잠시 살펴보니 둘레를 에워싼 형상들은 황야의 조랑말들로, 머리를 죄다 노름꾼들 쪽으로 향한 채 그들을 골똘히 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"훠이!\" 와일디브가 외치자, 마흔이나 쉰 마리쯤 되는 짐승 떼가 일제히 돌아서서 내달려 갔다. 노름이 다시 재개되었다.\n\n십 분이 지났다. 그때 커다란 박각시나방 한 마리가 흐릿한 바깥 공기 속에서 나아와 등불 둘레를 두 바퀴 돌더니, 촛불을 향해 곧장 날아가, 부딪치는 힘으로 그것을 꺼뜨렸다. 와일디브는 막 던진 참이었으나, 통을 들어 자신이 던진 것을 보지 못한 터였으니, 이제 그건 불가능했다.\n\n\"이런 빌어먹을!\" 그가 비명을 질렀다. \"이제 어쩌지? 어쩌면 내가 육을 던졌을지도 몰라―성냥 있소?\"\n\n\"없소.\" 벤이 말했다.\n\n\"크리스천이 좀 가지고 있었는데―그 작자 어디 있나 몰라. 크리스천!\"\n\n그러나 와일디브의 외침에 대한 답이라곤, 골짜기 아래쪽에 둥지를 튼 왜가리들의 구슬픈 울음소리뿐이었다. 두 사내는 일어서지 않은 채 멍하니 둘레를 둘러보았다. 눈이 어둠에 익숙해지자, 풀과 고사리 사이로 희미한 푸르스름한 빛점들이 보였다. 그 빛들은 작은 등급의 별들처럼 언덕 비탈에 점점이 박혀 있었다.\n\n\"아―반딧불이로군.\" 와일디브가 말했다. \"잠깐만. 노름을 계속할 수 있겠어.\"\n\n벤은 가만히 앉아 있었고, 그의 동행은 이리저리 다니며, 사오 분 사이에 찾을 수 있는 만큼―열세 마리의 반딧불이를―그 일을 위해 뜯어온 디기탈리스 잎 위에 모았다. 레들맨은 적수가 이것들을 들고 돌아오는 것을 보고 나직이 익살스러운 웃음을 흘렸다. \"그래도 계속하기로 마음먹은 거요?\" 그가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다.\n\n\"난 늘 그렇소!\" 와일디브가 성을 내며 말했다. 그러고는 잎에서 반딧불이를 털어내, 떨리는 손으로 돌 위에 둥글게 늘어놓고, 가운데엔 주사위 통이 내려올 자리를 남겨두었으니, 그 위로 열세 개의 작은 등불이 창백한 인광을 던졌다. 노름이 다시 새로 시작되었다. 마침 그 무렵은 반딧불이가 가장 찬란히 빛을 내는 철이어서, 그것들이 내는 빛은 이 일에 차고도 넘쳤으니, 그런 밤이면 두세 마리의 빛으로도 편지의 글씨를 읽을 수 있을 정도였다.\n\n두 사내의 행위와 그들이 처한 환경 사이의 부조화는 컸다. 그들이 앉은 오목한 자리의 보드랍고 물기 머금은 초목 가운데, 움직임 없고 인적 없는 적막 속으로, 기니의 짤랑임, 주사위의 달그락거림, 무모한 노름꾼들의 외침이 끼어들었다. 와일디브는 빛이 갖춰지자마자 통을 들어올렸고, 외톨이 주사위는 노름이 여전히 그에게 불리함을 알렸다.\n\n\"더는 안 하겠소―당신이 주사위에 손을 댄 거야.\" 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"어떻게―당신 것이었는데?\" 레들맨이 말했다.\n\n\"방식을 바꿉시다. 가장 낮은 끗수가 판돈을 가져가는 걸로―그러면 내 불운이 끊길지도 모르지. 거절하겠소?\"\n\n\"아니―계속합시다.\" 벤이 말했다.\n\n\"오, 저것들 또 왔군―빌어먹을 것들!\" 와일디브가 위를 올려다보며 외쳤다. 황야의 조랑말들이 소리 없이 돌아와, 전과 똑같이 머리를 곧추세우고 지켜보고 있었으니, 그 겁먹은 눈을 그 장면에 고정한 채, 이 어울리지 않는 시각에 인간과 촛불이 이런 후미진 곳에 무슨 볼일이 있는지 의아해하는 듯했다.\n\n\"저 짐승들 참 골칫거리야―날 저렇게 빤히 쳐다보다니!\" 그가 말하며 돌을 던지자 그것들이 흩어졌고, 노름은 전과 같이 이어졌다.\n\n이제 와일디브에겐 열 기니가 남았고, 각자 다섯 닢을 걸었다. 와일디브는 끗수 셋을 던졌고, 벤은 둘을 던져 동전을 긁어모았다. 다른 쪽은 주사위를 움켜쥐고 이로 깨물 듯 격분하여, 산산조각으로 깨물어버릴 것처럼 어금니를 악물었다. \"절대 안 굽혀―여기 내 마지막 다섯 닢이오!\" 그가 그것들을 내던지며 외쳤다. \"빌어먹을 반딧불이―꺼져가는군. 왜 안 빛나는 거야, 이 멍청한 것들아? 가시로 좀 쑤셔서 깨워.\"\n\n그는 막대기 조각으로 반딧불이를 쑤셔, 꽁무니의 밝은 쪽이 위로 가도록 굴렸다.\n\n\"빛은 충분하오. 던지시오.\" 벤이 말했다.\n\n와일디브는 빛나는 원 안으로 통을 내려놓고 간절히 들여다보았다. 그는 에이스를 던졌다. \"잘됐다!―운이 돌 거라 했더니, 돌았어.\" 벤은 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 그의 손이 살짝 떨렸다.\n\n그도 에이스를 던졌다.\n\n\"오!\" 와일디브가 말했다. \"젠장!\"\n\n주사위가 돌을 두 번째로 탁 쳤다. 또 에이스였다. 벤은 침울한 얼굴로 던졌고―주사위는 두 조각으로 갈라져, 갈라진 면이 위로 향한 채 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"난 아무것도 던지지 못한 거요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 당해도 싸지―이로 주사위를 쪼개버렸으니. 자―당신 돈 가져가시오. 무는 하나보다 적으니까.\"\n\n\"난 그걸 바라지 않소.\"\n\n\"가져가라니까―당신이 딴 거요!\" 그러고는 와일디브가 판돈을 레들맨의 가슴팍에 던졌다. 벤은 그것을 그러모아 일어나 오목한 자리에서 물러났고, 와일디브는 망연자실하여 앉아 있었다.\n\n정신이 들자 그도 일어나, 꺼진 등불을 손에 든 채 큰길 쪽으로 갔다. 큰길에 이르자 그는 가만히 멈춰 섰다. 밤의 정적이 황야 전체에 깔려 있었으니, 한 방향만이 예외였고, 그것은 미스토버 쪽이었다. 거기서 그는 가벼운 바퀴 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 이내 마차 등불 두 개가 언덕을 내려오는 것을 보았다. 와일디브는 덤불 아래 몸을 숨기고 기다렸다.\n\n마차가 다가와 그의 앞을 지나갔다. 그것은 삯마차였고, 마부 뒤에는 그가 잘 아는 두 사람이 앉아 있었다. 거기 유스테이샤와 요브라이트가 앉아 있었으니, 후자의 팔이 그녀의 허리를 두르고 있었다. 그들은 밑자락의 급커브를 돌아, 클림이 빌려 가구를 들여놓은 임시 거처 쪽으로, 동쪽으로 5마일쯤 되는 곳으로 향했다. 와일디브는 잃어버린 연인의 모습에 돈 잃은 일을 잊었으니, 그녀의 귀함은 그의 눈에, 그들의 가망 없는 갈라섬을 일깨우는 새로운 일이 생길 때마다 기하급수적으로 더해갔다. 그가 느낄 수 있는 정련된 비참함으로 가득 차서, 그는 반대 방향으로, 여인숙 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n와일디브가 큰길로 들어선 것과 거의 같은 순간에, 벤도 백 야드쯤 더 떨어진 지점에서 큰길에 다다랐고, 그 역시 같은 바퀴 소리를 듣고 마차가 다가올 때까지 마찬가지로 기다렸다. 그 안에 누가 앉아 있는지 보고는 실망한 듯했다. 일이 분 곰곰이 생각하는 사이 마차가 굴러갔고, 그는 길을 건너 가시금작화와 황야를 가로질러 지름길로, 통행세 길이 언덕을 오르며 굽이도는 지점으로 갔다. 이제 그는 다시 마차 앞에 있었으니, 마차는 이내 걷는 걸음으로 다가왔다. 벤은 앞으로 나서서 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n등불이 그를 비추자 유스테이샤가 흠칫했고, 클림의 팔이 저도 모르게 그녀의 허리에서 거두어졌다. 그가 말했다. \"아니, 디고리? 외로이 걷고 있군.\"\n\n\"예―멈춰 세워 죄송합니다.\" 벤이 말했다. \"하지만 저는 와일디브 부인을 기다리고 있습니다. 요브라이트 부인이 전하라는 게 있어서요. 부인이 잔치에서 벌써 집으로 가셨는지 아십니까?\"\n\n\"아니. 하지만 곧 떠날 걸세. 모퉁이에서 만날 수도 있겠군.\"\n\n벤은 작별 인사로 고개를 숙이고, 미스토버에서 오는 샛길이 큰길과 만나는 본래의 자리로 되돌아갔다. 여기서 그는 거의 반 시간을 붙박여 있었고, 그러자 또 한 쌍의 등불이 언덕을 내려왔다. 그것은 선장 소유의 구식 바퀴 달린 정체 모를 탈것이었고, 토머신이 홀로 그 안에 앉아 찰리가 모는 대로 있었다.\n\n레들맨은 그들이 천천히 모퉁이를 돌 때 다가갔다. \"멈춰 세워 죄송합니다, 와일디브 부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 요브라이트 부인이 부인께 따로 전하라는 게 있습니다.\" 그는 작은 꾸러미를 건넸으니, 방금 그가 딴 백 기니가 종이 한 장에 대충 비틀어 싸인 것이었다.\n\n토머신은 놀라움에서 정신을 차리고 그 꾸러미를 받았다. \"그게 다입니다, 마님―안녕히 가십시오.\" 그가 말하고는 그녀의 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n이리하여 벤은 일을 바로잡으려는 안달 속에, 토머신의 손에 정당히 그녀의 것인 오십 기니뿐만 아니라 그녀의 사촌 클림에게 갈 오십 기니까지 쥐여준 것이었다. 그의 착오는 노름 첫머리에 와일디브가 그 기니가 자기 것이 아니라는 말을 분개하며 부인했던 데에서 비롯한 것이었다. 노름이 절반쯤 진행되었을 무렵부터는 다른 사람의 돈으로 이어졌다는 사실을 레들맨이 헤아리지 못했으니, 그것은 훗날 돈의 값어치로 친 손실의 세 곱절보다도 더 큰 불행을 불러오는 데 한몫한 잘못이었다.\n\n밤은 이제 어지간히 깊었고, 벤은 황야 속으로 더 깊이 파고들어, 그의 짐마차가 서 있는 협곡에 다다랐으니―노름판이 벌어진 자리에서 이백 야드도 채 안 떨어진 곳이었다. 그는 이 움직이는 보금자리로 들어가 등불을 켰고, 밤을 위해 문을 닫기 전에 서서 지난 몇 시간의 정황을 곱씹었다. 그가 서 있는 동안 하늘 북동쪽 모퉁이로 새벽이 보이기 시작했으니, 구름이 걷힌 그곳은 이 한여름철에 부드러운 광채로 환했고, 그래도 이제 겨우 한 시에서 두 시 사이였다. 그러자 벤은 몹시 지쳐서 문을 닫고 몸을 던져 잠을 청했다.\n\nI.\n연못가의 우연한 만남\n\n칠월의 태양이 에그던 위로 내리쬐어 진홍빛 히스를 새빨갛게 불태웠다. 한 해 가운데 단 한 철, 그 한 철 가운데 단 한 가지 날씨, 그때라야 황야는 화려했다. 이 개화기는 이곳에서만 가능한 표면적 변화들의 순환 가운데 두 번째, 곧 정오에 해당하는 구간을 나타냈으니, 그것은 아침을 나타내는 푸른 어린 고사리 시기에 뒤이어 왔으며, 히스꽃과 고사리가 저녁의 적갈색 빛깔을 두르게 되는 갈색 시기에 앞섰고, 그 갈색 시기는 다시 밤을 나타내는 겨울 시기의 짙은 색조에 자리를 내주게 될 것이었다.\n\n이스트 에그던 너머 올더워스의 작은 집에서 클림과 유스테이샤는 단조로운 나날을 이어가고 있었는데, 그 단조로움이 두 사람에게는 즐거웠다. 황야도 날씨의 변화도 지금만큼은 그들의 눈에서 완전히 지워져 있었다. 그들은 일종의 환한 안개에 둘러싸여 있었으니, 그 안개는 조화롭지 못한 색채의 주변 풍경을 그들에게서 가려주고 모든 사물에 빛의 성격을 부여했다. 비가 내리면 그들은 매료되었는데, 그토록 그럴듯한 명분을 내세워 온종일 둘이서 집 안에 머물 수 있었기 때문이요, 날이 맑으면 또 매료되었는데, 둘이서 언덕에 앉아 있을 수 있었기 때문이다. 그들은 서로의 둘레를 돌고 또 도는 이중성과 같았으니, 멀리서 보면 하나로 보이는 별들이었다. 그들이 살아가는 절대적인 고독은 서로를 향한 생각을 한층 짙게 했다. 그러나 어떤 이는 그 고독이 서로를 향한 애정을 무서울 만큼 헤프게 소진시킨다는 약점을 지녔다고 말했을지도 모른다. 요브라이트 자신은 두려워하지 않았으나, 사랑의 덧없음에 관해 유스테이샤가 예전에 했던 말—이제는 그녀가 잊은 듯한 그 말—을 떠올릴 때면 이따금 스스로에게 한 가지 물음을 던졌으며, 유한함이라는 성질이 에덴에도 낯선 것이 아니라는 생각에 그는 움찔하여 물러섰다.\n\n이렇게 서너 주가 지나자 요브라이트는 다시 본격적으로 책 읽기에 몰두했다. 잃어버린 시간을 만회하려고 그는 지칠 줄 모르고 공부했으니, 새로운 직업에 가능한 한 지체 없이 발을 들이고 싶었기 때문이다.\n\n그런데 유스테이샤의 꿈은 언제나 이러했으니, 일단 클림과 결혼하기만 하면 그를 구슬려 파리로 돌아가게 할 힘이 자기에게 생기리라는 것이었다. 그는 그렇게 하겠다는 약속이라면 무엇이든 조심스럽게 보류해왔으나, 그녀의 어르고 달램과 설득에 끝내 버틸 수 있을 것인가? 그녀는 성공 가능성을 어찌나 굳게 헤아렸던지, 할아버지에게 장차 살 곳을 알릴 때 버드머스가 아니라 파리라고, 그것이 십중팔구 그들의 보금자리가 되리라고 말해두었던 터였다. 그녀의 희망은 이 꿈에 송두리째 매여 있었다. 결혼 이후 조용한 나날 동안, 요브라이트가 그녀의 입술과 눈과 얼굴의 선을 들여다보며 골몰해 있을 때, 그녀는 그의 시선을 마주 되돌려주는 바로 그 순간에도 이 문제를 두고 거듭거듭 골똘히 생각에 잠겼으며, 이제 자기 꿈과 적대되는 미래를 가리키는 책들을 보자 그것은 그녀에게 그야말로 고통스러운 충격으로 다가왔다. 그녀는 파리의 어느 큰길 가까이에, 아무리 작더라도 어여쁜 살림집의 안주인이 되어, 적어도 화려한 사교계의 언저리에서나마 나날을 보내며, 자기가 그토록 누리기에 알맞은 그 도회의 즐거움에서 흘러나오는 향기를 이따금이라도 붙잡을 그 시절을 고대하고 있었다. 그러나 요브라이트는 정반대의 결심을 굳건히 지켰으니, 마치 결혼이라는 것이 젊은 박애의 환상을 쓸어내기보다 도리어 그것을 키워내는 쪽으로 기우는 것이기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n그녀의 불안은 한껏 높아졌으나, 클림의 한결같은 태도에는 그 문제를 두고 그를 떠보기 전에 망설이게 만드는 무언가가 있었다. 그렇지만 두 사람이 겪는 일 가운데 이 대목에서 한 가지 사건이 그녀를 거들었다. 그 일은 두 사람이 맺어진 지 여섯 주쯤 지난 어느 저녁에 일어났으며, 요브라이트에게 가야 할 오십 기니를 벤이 자기도 모르게 엉뚱한 데로 돌린 데서 전적으로 비롯되었다.\n\n돈을 받은 지 하루 이틀 지나 토머신은 고모에게 고마움을 전하는 쪽지를 보냈다. 그녀는 액수가 큰 데 놀랐으나, 정해진 금액이 언급된 적은 한 번도 없었던 터라 그것을 돌아가신 큰아버지의 너그러움 덕분으로 돌렸다. 고모는 그녀에게 이 선물에 관해 남편에게 한마디도 하지 말라고 단단히 일러두었으며, 와일디브는 또 와일디브대로—지극히 자연스러운 일이지만—황야에서 벌어진 한밤의 장면을 단 한 가지 시시콜콜한 것조차 아내에게 입에 올릴 마음이 들지 않았다. 마찬가지로 크리스천도 그 일에서 자기가 맡은 몫에 관해서는 두려움 때문에 혀가 묶였으며, 어떻게든 돈이 제 갈 곳으로 갔으리라 바라면서 자세한 내막은 밝히지 않은 채 그저 그렇게 되었다고만 우겨댔다.\n\n그리하여 한두 주가 지나자 요브라이트 부인은 어째서 아들에게서 선물을 받았다는 기별이 도무지 없는지 의아해하기 시작했으며, 그 당혹스러움에 더욱 어두운 그늘을 드리운 것은 그의 침묵의 원인이 어쩌면 원망일지도 모른다는 가능성이었다. 그녀는 차마 그렇게는 믿기 어려웠으나, 그렇다면 어찌하여 그는 편지를 쓰지 않는가? 그녀가 크리스천에게 캐묻자, 그의 대답에 깃든 혼란은 곧장 무언가 잘못되었다고 그녀가 믿게 만들 만했으나, 그의 이야기 절반이 토머신의 쪽지로 뒷받침된 까닭에 그렇게까지는 가지 않았다.\n\n요브라이트 부인이 이런 미심쩍은 상태에 있던 어느 아침, 그녀는 아들의 아내가 미스토버에서 할아버지를 찾아뵙고 있다는 소식을 들었다. 그녀는 언덕을 걸어 올라가 유스테이샤를 만나, 며느리의 입을 통해 집안의 기니가—그 기니는 요브라이트 부인에게 더 부유한 미망인들에게 집안의 보석이 갖는 것과 같은 의미였다—잘못 전해졌는지 아닌지를 알아내기로 마음먹었다.\n\n크리스천은 그녀가 어디로 가는지 알게 되자 걱정이 극에 달했다. 그녀가 떠나려는 순간 그는 더는 둘러댈 수 없어, 노름에 손댄 일을 실토하고는 자기가 아는 한도 내에서 사실을 털어놓았으니—그 기니는 와일디브가 따 갔다는 것이었다.\n\n\"뭐라고, 그자가 그걸 가지려 한다는 말이냐?\" 요브라이트 부인이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"부디 그러지 않기를 바랍니다요!\" 크리스천이 끙끙거렸다. \"그분은 좋은 분이니, 어쩌면 옳은 일을 하실 거예요. 클림 도련님 몫은 마님이 유스테이샤 아씨에게 주셨어야 했다고 그러시더니, 어쩌면 자기가 직접 그렇게 하실지도 모르지요.\"\n\n요브라이트 부인은 차분히 곱씹어볼 수 있게 되자, 이 말에 그럴듯한 구석이 많다고 느꼈으니, 와일디브가 아들의 돈을 정말로 제 것으로 가로채리라고는 차마 믿기 어려웠기 때문이다. 그것을 유스테이샤에게 넘겨준다는 그 중간의 행로야말로 와일디브의 변덕을 흡족하게 할 만한 종류의 일이었다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서 어머니의 가슴이 분노로 가득 차지 않은 것은 아니었다. 결국 와일디브가 그 기니를 손에 넣고는, 클림의 몫을 클림의 아내 손에—그녀가 한때 자기 연인이었고 어쩌면 지금도 그러할지 모른다는 이유로—쥐여주며 그 처분을 제멋대로 다시 정하려 한다는 것은, 요브라이트 부인이 여태껏 견뎌낸 그 어떤 것 못지않게 속을 긁는 고통이었다.\n\n그녀는 그 일에서 보인 처신을 두고 가련한 크리스천을 당장 일자리에서 내쫓았으나, 그가 없으면 도무지 어찌할 도리가 없겠다 싶어 속수무책인 심정이 들자, 나중에는 그가 원한다면 조금 더 머물러도 좋다고 일렀다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 유스테이샤를 향해 서둘러 떠났는데, 며느리를 향한 마음은 반 시간 전 길 떠날 채비를 하던 때 품었던 것보다 훨씬 덜 너그러운 감정에 떠밀려 있었다. 그때만 해도 우연히 잃어버린 것은 없는지 다정한 마음으로 물어보려는 것이었으나, 이제는 클림에게 바칠 신성한 선물로 정해졌던 돈을 와일디브가 그녀에게 몰래 건넨 것이 아닌지 대놓고 따져 물으려는 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 두 시에 길을 나섰고, 유스테이샤와의 만남은 그 젊은 아가씨가 할아버지 집터를 두른 연못과 둑가에 나타나 앞당겨졌으니, 그녀는 거기 서서 풍경을 바라보며 어쩌면 지난날 그곳이 지켜본 낭만적인 장면들을 떠올리고 있었는지도 모른다. 요브라이트 부인이 다가가자 유스테이샤는 낯선 사람을 보듯 차분히 빤히 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n먼저 입을 연 것은 시어머니였다. \"너를 보러 오는 길이었다.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"어머나!\" 유스테이샤가 놀라며 말했는데, 요브라이트 부인이 결혼식에 참석하기를 거절했던 일이 그 아가씨에게는 여간 모욕스러운 것이 아니었던 까닭이다. \"전혀 뜻밖이네요.\"\n\n\"긴히 볼일이 있어 왔을 뿐이다.\" 손님이 처음보다 더 차갑게 말했다. \"이걸 묻는 걸 양해해다오—토머신의 남편한테서 선물을 받은 일이 있느냐?\"\n\n\"선물요?\"\n\n\"돈 말이다!\"\n\n\"뭐라고요—제가 말씀이세요?\"\n\n\"그래, 너 말이다, 몰래 받은 것 말이야—이런 식으로 말할 생각은 아니었다만.\"\n\n\"와일디브 씨한테서 돈을요? 아뇨—천만에요! 부인, 그게 무슨 말씀이세요?\" 유스테이샤는 너무도 성급히 발끈했으니, 자기와 와일디브 사이의 옛 정을 스스로 의식하고 있던 탓에 요브라이트 부인 역시 그것을 알고 있어, 이제 그에게서 떳떳지 못한 선물을 받았다고 자기를 나무라러 온 것이라고 지레 결론으로 치달았기 때문이다.\n\n\"그저 물어보는 것뿐이다.\" 요브라이트 부인이 말했다. \"나는 그동안——\"\n\n\"저를 좀 더 좋게 봐주셔야 했어요—처음부터 저를 못마땅하게 여기시는 게 아닐까 두려웠어요!\" 유스테이샤가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니다. 나는 그저 클림 편이었을 뿐이야.\" 요브라이트 부인이 대답했는데, 그 진지함에는 힘이 지나치게 실려 있었다. \"제 자식을 챙기는 건 누구에게나 있는 본능이지.\"\n\n\"제게서 그 애를 지켜야 했다는 듯이 어찌 그런 암시를 하실 수 있으세요?\" 유스테이샤가 눈에 격정의 눈물을 띤 채 외쳤다. \"저는 그 사람과 결혼해서 그 사람을 해친 일이 없어요! 제가 무슨 죄를 지었기에 저를 그토록 나쁘게 여기시는 거예요? 저는 부인께 잘못한 적이 없는데, 부인께서는 그 사람한테 저를 헐뜯어 말할 권리가 없으셨어요.\"\n\n\"나는 그저 상황에 비추어 마땅한 일을 했을 뿐이다.\" 요브라이트 부인이 한결 부드럽게 말했다. \"지금 이 문제를 끄집어내고 싶지는 않았다만, 네가 나를 떠미는구나. 나는 정직한 진실을 네게 말하기가 부끄럽지 않다. 나는 그 애가 너와 결혼해서는 안 된다고 굳게 믿었어—그래서 내 힘이 닿는 온갖 방법으로 그 애를 말리려 했지. 하지만 이제 다 된 일이고, 더는 불평할 생각이 없어. 나는 너를 기꺼이 맞아들일 준비가 되어 있다.\"\n\n\"아, 그래요, 일을 그렇게 거래의 관점에서 보시면야 더할 나위 없겠죠.\" 유스테이샤가 짓눌린 감정의 불길을 품고 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 저와 와일디브 씨 사이에 무슨 일이 있다고 어째서 생각하시는 거예요? 저도 부인 못지않게 자존심이 있어요. 저는 분개하고 있고, 어떤 여자라도 그럴 거예요. 클림의 아내가 된 건 제 쪽에서 몸을 낮춘 거지 술책이 아니었다는 걸 일러두겠어요. 그러니 저는 집안에 슬그머니 기어들었으니 어쩔 수 없이 참아주어야 하는 모사꾼 취급을 받지는 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"오!\" 요브라이트 부인이 부질없이 분노를 억누르려 애쓰며 말했다. \"내 아들의 혈통이 바이 집안만 못하다는 말은 여태 들어본 적이 없다—어쩌면 더 나을 수도 있지. 네가 몸을 낮췄다고 말하는 걸 들으니 우습구나.\"\n\n\"그래도 몸을 낮춘 거예요.\" 유스테이샤가 격하게 말했다. \"그리고 지금 아는 걸 그때 알았더라면, 곧 결혼한 지 한 달 만에 이런 황량한 황야에서 살게 되리라는 걸 알았더라면, 저는—저는 승낙하기 전에 두 번은 생각했을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런 말은 안 하는 편이 좋겠구나. 진실하게 들리지 않을 수도 있으니. 그 애 쪽에서 무슨 속임수가 쓰였다는 건 나는 알지 못한다—없었던 걸 알아—다른 쪽에서야 어떠했든 간에 말이다.\"\n\n\"이건 너무 화가 나는 일이에요!\" 젊은 여자가 목이 잠긴 채 대답했는데, 얼굴은 새빨개지고 두 눈에서는 빛이 쏟아졌다. \"어떻게 감히 제게 그런 식으로 말씀하실 수 있어요? 다시 한번 분명히 말씀드릴게요, 결혼한 뒤로 지금까지 제 삶이 이러하리라는 걸 알았더라면 저는 싫다고 했을 거예요. 저는 불평하는 게 아니에요. 그 사람한테는 그런 말을 한마디도 입 밖에 낸 적이 없어요. 하지만 사실이 그래요. 그러니 앞으로는 제가 결혼을 간절히 바랐던 일에 대해 입을 다물어주시길 바라요. 지금 저를 해치시면 부인 자신을 해치시는 거예요.\"\n\n\"너를 해친다고? 내가 못된 마음을 품은 사람이라고 생각하느냐?\"\n\n\"결혼 전에도 저를 해치셨고, 이제는 제가 돈 때문에 다른 남자한테 몰래 마음을 두었다고 의심하셨잖아요!\"\n\n\"내가 생각한 걸 어쩔 수는 없었다. 하지만 내 집 밖에서는 네 이야기를 입에 올린 적이 없어.\"\n\n\"집 안에서는 클림한테 제 이야기를 하셨고, 그보다 더한 짓은 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"나는 내 도리를 다했을 뿐이다.\"\n\n\"그럼 저도 제 도리를 다하겠어요.\"\n\"그 도리의 일부는 아마 그 애를 제 어미한테 등 돌리게 하는 일이겠지. 늘 그런 법이니까. 하지만 나보다 앞서 다른 이들이 견뎌온 것을 내가 어찌 못 견디겠느냐!\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻인지 알겠어요.\" 유스테이샤가 감정에 겨워 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"부인께서는 제가 온갖 못된 짓을 다 할 수 있는 사람이라고 여기시는군요. 정부情夫를 부추기고 남편의 마음에 그 친척을 두고 독을 풀어 넣는 아내보다 더 못된 사람이 어디 있겠어요? 그런데 그게 지금 제게 씌워진 인물됨이로군요. 그 사람을 제 손아귀에서 끌어내가지 않으시겠어요?\"\n\n요브라이트 부인은 열기에 열기로 되받았다.\n\n\"내게 화를 내지 마라, 얘야! 그건 네 아름다움에 어울리지 않거니와, 나 때문에 네가 그 아름다움을 해칠 만한 값어치가 내게는 없으니 말이다, 정말이지. 나는 그저 아들을 잃은 가련한 늙은이일 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"저를 떳떳하게 대해주셨더라면 그 사람을 여전히 곁에 두셨을 거예요.\" 유스테이샤가 말하는 동안 데일 듯이 뜨거운 눈물이 두 눈에서 주르륵 흘러내렸다. \"부인 스스로 어리석은 짓에 빠지신 거예요. 결코 아물 수 없는 균열을 일으키신 거라고요!\"\n\n\"나는 아무것도 한 게 없다. 젊은 여자가 이리 뻔뻔하게 구는 걸 나는 견딜 수가 없구나.\"\n\n\"자초하신 일이에요. 저를 의심하셨고, 제가 하지 않았을 방식으로 제 남편 얘기를 하게 만드셨잖아요. 제가 이렇게 말했다는 걸 그 사람한테 알리실 테고, 그러면 우리 사이에 불행이 생길 거예요. 제게서 물러가시겠어요? 부인은 제 편이 아니에요!\"\n\n\"한마디만 하고 가마. 누구든 내가 정당한 까닭 없이 너를 따지러 여기 왔다고 말한다면, 그 사람은 거짓을 말하는 게다. 누구든 내가 정직하지 못한 수단으로 네 결혼을 막으려 했다고 말한다면, 그 사람 역시 진실을 말하는 게 아니야. 나는 사나운 때를 만난 게다. 네가 나를 모욕하도록 내버려두시다니 하느님이 내게 부당하셨어! 아마 내 아들의 행복은 무덤 이쪽에는 없는가 보다. 부모의 충고를 저버리는 자는 어리석은 자이니 말이다. 유스테이샤, 너는 그런 줄도 모르고 벼랑 끝에 서 있는 게야. 오늘 내게 보인 성미의 절반만이라도 내 아들에게 보여보아라—머지않아 그럴지도 모르지—그러면 알게 될 게다, 지금은 그 애가 네게 어린아이처럼 순하다 해도 강철처럼 단단해질 수 있다는 걸!\"\n\n흥분한 어머니는 그러고 나서 물러갔고, 유스테이샤는 숨을 헐떡이며 서서 연못을 들여다보았다.\n\nII.\n그는 역경에 시달리나 노래를 부른다\n\n그 불길한 만남의 결과로, 유스테이샤는 오후를 할아버지와 보내는 대신 황급히 클림에게로 돌아왔으니, 예상보다 세 시간이나 일찍 집에 닿았다. 그녀는 얼굴이 상기되고 두 눈에는 방금 전 격앙의 흔적이 아직 남은 채 집 안으로 들어섰다. 요브라이트는 놀라 올려다보았는데, 그녀가 그 비슷한 상태에 이른 모습은 여태껏 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 그녀는 그를 지나쳐 눈에 띄지 않게 위층으로 올라가려 했으나, 클림이 어찌나 마음이 쓰였던지 곧장 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오, 유스테이샤?\" 그가 말했다. 그녀는 침실의 난롯가 깔개 위에 서서 바닥을 내려다보며, 두 손을 앞으로 맞잡은 채, 모자도 아직 벗지 않은 채로 있었다. 한순간 그녀는 대답하지 않았고, 그러고 나서 낮은 목소리로 답했다—\n\n\"당신 어머니를 만났어요. 그리고 다시는 그분을 만나지 않겠어요!\"\n\n돌덩이 같은 무게가 클림을 짓눌렀다. 바로 그날 아침, 유스테이샤가 할아버지를 뵈러 가기로 했을 때, 클림은 그녀가 블룸스엔드로 마차를 몰고 내려가 시어머니의 안부를 묻거나, 화해를 이끌어낼 만하다고 여겨지는 다른 어떤 방법이든 취해주었으면 하는 바람을 내비쳤던 터였다. 그녀는 즐거이 길을 나섰고, 그는 많은 것을 기대했었다.\n\n\"왜 이러는 거요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"말 못 하겠어요—기억도 안 나요. 당신 어머니를 만났어요. 그리고 다시는 그분을 만나지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"제가 이제 와일디브 씨에 대해 뭘 안다고요? 누구한테도 저를 두고 못된 험담을 듣지 않겠어요. 오! 그한테서 돈을 받았느냐, 그를 부추겼느냐, 뭐 그런 비슷한 걸 묻다니 너무 굴욕적이었어요—정확히 뭔지도 모르겠어요!\"\n\n\"어떻게 어머니가 당신한테 그런 걸 물을 수 있었지?\"\n\n\"그러셨어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 거기엔 무슨 까닭이 있었을 거요. 어머니가 그 밖에 또 뭐라고 하셨소?\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀을 하셨는지 모르겠어요, 다만 이것만은요, 우리 둘 다 결코 용서받을 수 없는 말들을 했다는 것!\"\n\n\"오, 거기엔 무슨 오해가 있었던 게 분명하오. 어머니 말씀의 뜻이 분명히 전해지지 않은 게 누구 잘못이었소?\"\n\n\"말하고 싶지 않아요. 상황 탓이었을지도 모르죠, 적어도 어색하기 짝이 없었으니까요. 오 클림—말하지 않을 수가 없네요—이건 당신이 저를 몰아넣은 달갑잖은 처지예요. 하지만 당신은 이걸 나아지게 해야 해요—그래요, 그러겠다고 말해줘요—이제 이게 다 싫으니까요! 그래요, 저를 파리로 데려가서 예전 일을 다시 하세요, 클림! 처음엔 거기서 아무리 초라하게 살아도 상관없어요, 그게 에그던 황야가 아니라 파리이기만 하다면요.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 그 생각을 아주 접었소.\" 요브라이트가 놀라며 말했다. \"내가 당신한테 그런 걸 기대하게 만든 적은 결코 없을 텐데?\"\n\n\"인정해요. 그래도 마음에서 떨쳐낼 수 없는 생각이라는 게 있고, 그게 제 생각이었어요. 이제 제가 당신 아내이고 당신 운명을 함께 짊어진 사람인데, 이 문제에 목소리를 낼 수 없는 건가요?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 논의의 울타리 밖에 놓인 일들이라는 게 있는 법이오. 나는 이것이 특히 그러하다고, 그것도 서로 합의한 일이라고 여겼소.\"\n\n\"클림, 그 말을 들으니 비참해요.\" 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했고, 두 눈을 떨구더니 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n유스테이샤의 가슴속에 뜻밖의 희망의 광맥이 묻혀 있었다는 이 징후는 그녀의 남편을 당황하게 했다. 여자가 욕망을 향해 에둘러 움직인다는 사실과 마주한 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 그러나 그는 유스테이샤를 깊이 사랑했음에도 그 결심만큼은 흔들리지 않았다. 그녀의 말이 그에게 미친 영향이라고는, 그녀의 변덕에 맞서 다른 길에서 거둔 실질적인 성과에 호소할 수 있도록 한시바삐 자신을 책에 더욱 단단히 옭아매겠다는 결심뿐이었다.\n\n이튿날 기니의 수수께끼가 풀렸다. 토머신이 황급히 다녀갔고, 클림의 몫은 그녀가 직접 그의 손에 건네주었다. 그때 유스테이샤는 자리에 없었다.\n\n\"그러니까 이게 어머니가 뜻하신 거였군.\" 클림이 외쳤다. \"토머신, 그분들이 모진 다툼을 벌인 걸 알아?\"\n\n토머신이 사촌을 대하는 태도에는 이제 예전보다 다소 조심스러움이 더해져 있었다. 결혼이란 한 방향에서 없애버린 신중함을 여러 방향에서 새로 자아내는 효과가 있는 법이다. \"고모님이 제게 말씀해주셨어요.\" 그녀가 조용히 말했다. \"유스테이샤를 만나신 뒤에 우리 집으로 다시 오셨거든요.\"\n\n\"내가 가장 두려워하던 일이 벌어지고 말았군. 너한테 오셨을 때 어머니가 많이 심란해하셨니, 토머신?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"아주 많이?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n클림은 정원 문기둥에 팔꿈치를 기대고 손으로 두 눈을 가렸다.\n\n\"너무 마음 쓰지 마세요, 클림. 두 분이 친구가 되실 수도 있어요.\"\n\n그는 고개를 저었다. \"저 두 분처럼 불같은 성정을 지닌 사람들끼리는 안 돼. 뭐, 될 일은 되겠지.\"\n\n\"그래도 한 가지 다행스러운 게 있어요—기니는 잃지 않았잖아요.\"\n\n\"이런 일이 벌어지느니 차라리 그 돈을 두 번 잃는 편이 나았을 텐데.\"\n\n이렇게 어긋나는 일들 가운데서 요브라이트는 한 가지가 꼭 필요하다고 느꼈으니—자신의 학업 계획에서 어떤 식으로든 어서 진전을 보여야 한다는 것이었다. 그런 생각으로 그는 여러 밤 동안 새벽녘까지 책에 깊이 파고들었다.\n\n여느 때보다 더 혹독하게 무리한 어느 아침, 그는 두 눈에 야릇한 느낌이 들며 잠에서 깨었다. 햇빛이 창문 가리개에 곧장 내리쬐고 있었고, 그쪽으로 첫 눈길을 던지자 날카로운 통증에 그는 어쩔 수 없이 얼른 눈꺼풀을 감았다. 주위를 둘러보려고 새로 시도할 때마다 빛에 대한 똑같은 병적인 예민함이 드러났고, 살이 벗겨질 듯 따가운 눈물이 두 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다. 그는 옷을 입는 동안 이마에 붕대를 매어야 했으며, 낮 동안에도 그것을 떼어낼 수 없었다. 유스테이샤는 몹시 놀랐다. 이튿날 아침에도 차도가 없자 두 사람은 앵글베리로 사람을 보내 의사를 부르기로 했다.\n\n저녁 무렵 그가 도착하여, 이 병은 클림의 밤샘 공부로 인해 생긴 급성 염증이라고 진단했으니, 그 공부가 앞서 걸린 감기에도 아랑곳없이 이어진 탓에 한동안 그의 눈이 약해져 있었던 것이다. 그토록 서두르고 싶던 일이 중단된 데 안달하며 애를 태우던 클림은 환자 신세로 바뀌었다. 그는 빛이 일절 들지 않는 방에 갇혀 지냈고, 만일 유스테이샤가 갓 씌운 등불의 희미한 빛에 의지해 책을 읽어주지 않았더라면 그의 처지는 그야말로 비참했을 것이다. 그는 최악의 고비가 곧 지나가기를 바랐으나, 의사의 세 번째 방문 때 낙담스럽게도 알게 된바, 한 달쯤 지나면 눈을 가린 채 바깥출입은 해볼 수 있겠으나, 자기 일을 계속하거나 어떤 종류의 인쇄물이든 읽으려는 생각은 앞으로 오랫동안 단념해야 하리라는 것이었다.\n\n한 주 또 한 주가 흘러갔으나, 그 무엇도 젊은 부부의 우울을 덜어주는 것 같지 않았다. 끔찍한 상상들이 유스테이샤에게 떠올랐으나, 그녀는 그것을 남편에게 입에 올리지 않으려 조심스레 삼갔다. 만일 그가 눈이 멀게 된다면, 아니 적어도 그녀의 마음에 맞고 이 언덕 사이 외딴집에서 그녀를 데리고 나가는 데 도움이 될 직업에 종사할 만큼 시력을 다시 회복하지 못한다면? 아름다운 파리의 그 꿈은 이 불행 앞에서 실체로 엉겨붙을 가망이 없었다. 하루하루 지나도 그가 나아지지 않자, 그녀의 마음은 점점 더 이 구슬픈 홈을 따라 굴러갔고, 그녀는 그에게서 벗어나 정원으로 나가 절망의 눈물을 흘리곤 했다.\n\n요브라이트는 어머니를 부를까 생각했다가, 이내 부르지 않기로 생각을 고쳐먹었다. 자기 처지를 안다 한들 어머니를 더 불행하게 만들 뿐이요, 두 사람의 삶이 어찌나 외진지라 특별히 심부름꾼을 보내지 않고서는 어머니가 그 소식을 알 일도 좀처럼 없을 터였다. 이 고난을 되도록 의연하게 받아들이려 애쓰며, 그는 셋째 주가 올 때까지 기다렸고, 그제야 발병 이후 처음으로 바깥바람을 쐬러 나갔다. 의사는 이 무렵 그를 다시 찾아왔고, 클림은 분명한 소견을 말해달라고 그를 다그쳤다. 젊은이는 한층 더 놀라며 알게 되었으니, 그가 다시 일을 시작하리라 기대할 수 있는 시점은 여전히 막연하기만 했는데, 그의 두 눈이 그 묘한 상태에 있었던 까닭이라, 걸어 다닐 만큼의 시력은 주어졌으나 어떤 일정한 대상에 눈을 무리하게 쓰면 급성 형태의 안염이 재발할 위험을 무릅쓰지 않고서는 안 되는 것이었다.\n\n클림은 그 소식에 매우 침울했으나 절망하지는 않았다. 조용한 결연함이, 심지어 명랑함마저 그를 사로잡았다. 그는 눈멀지는 않을 터였으니, 그것으로 충분했다. 기약 없는 세월 동안 그을린 유리를 통해 세상을 바라보아야 할 운명이란 어지간히 고약한 일이요, 어떤 종류의 진척에도 치명적이었으나, 요브라이트는 그저 자신의 사회적 지위에만 영향을 미치는 불운 앞에서는 완전한 금욕주의자였으며, 유스테이샤만 아니라면 아무리 미천한 삶의 길이라도 어떤 형태로든 자신의 교화 계획과 맞물리게만 할 수 있다면 그를 만족시킬 터였다. 오두막 야학을 여는 것도 그런 형태 가운데 하나였으니, 그의 고난은 본디 그러했을 만큼 그의 기개를 짓누르지는 못했다.\n\n그는 따스한 햇볕을 가르며 서쪽으로, 자기에게 가장 익숙한 에그던의 그 일대로 걸어갔으니, 옛 집에서 더 가까운 곳에 자리한 지역이었다. 그는 골짜기 가운데 한 곳에서 날 선 쇠붙이가 번쩍이는 것을 앞에 두고 보았고, 다가가서야 그 번쩍임이 가시금작화를 베고 있는 한 남자의 연장에서 나온 것임을 어렴풋이 알아차렸다. 일하던 사람은 클림을 알아보았고, 요브라이트는 그 목소리로 말하는 이가 험프리임을 알았다.\n\n험프리는 클림의 처지에 안타까움을 표하고는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그런데 도련님 일이 저 같은 막일이라면, 그냥 똑같이 계속하실 수 있을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그럴 수 있겠지.\" 요브라이트가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"이 단을 베면 얼마나 받나?\"\n\n\"백 단에 반 크라운이요, 이렇게 해가 긴 날엔 그 삯으로도 아주 잘 살 수 있지요.\"\n\n요브라이트가 올더워스로 걸어 돌아오는 내내 그는 결코 언짢지 않은 상념에 잠겨 있었다. 집에 다다르자 유스테이샤가 열린 창에서 그에게 말을 건넸고, 그는 그녀에게로 건너갔다.\n\n\"여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 한결 행복하오. 그리고 어머니가 나와 당신과 화해하신다면, 나는 아마 더없이 행복할 것 같소.\"\n\n\"그건 결코 이루어지지 않을 거예요.\" 그녀가 아름다운 폭풍 같은 눈으로 먼 곳을 바라보며 말했다. \"어떻게 아무것도 달라진 게 없는데 '나는 한결 행복하오' 하고 말할 수 있어요?\"\n\n\"이 불운한 때에 내가 할 수 있는, 그리고 그것으로 먹고살 수 있는 무언가를 마침내 찾아낸 데서 비롯된 거요.\"\n\n\"뭔데요?\"\n\n\"나는 가시금작화와 뗏장을 베는 사람이 될 거요.\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 클림!\" 그녀가 말했고, 조금 전 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 희미한 희망의 빛은 다시 사라져 이전보다 더 나쁜 상태로 그녀를 남겨놓았다.\n\n\"분명 그렇게 할 거요. 정직한 일로 지출을 줄일 수 있는데도 얼마 안 되는 우리 돈을 계속 써대는 게 매우 어리석은 짓이 아니겠소? 바깥일을 하면 내게도 좋을 테고, 몇 달 뒤엔 어쩌면 다시 책 읽기를 계속할 수 있게 될지 누가 알겠소?\"\n\n\"하지만 할아버지가 우리를 도와주겠다고 하시잖아요, 도움이 필요하다면요.\"\n\n\"우린 그게 필요 없소. 내가 가시금작화를 베면 우린 그런대로 넉넉하게 지낼 거요.\"\n\n\"노예들이나, 이집트의 이스라엘 사람들이나, 그런 사람들에 비하면 말이죠!\" 쓰디쓴 눈물 한 방울이 유스테이샤의 얼굴을 타고 흘렀으나, 그는 그것을 보지 못했다. 그의 어조에는 무심함이 있었으니, 그녀에게는 그야말로 끔찍한 그 귀결에 그가 절대적인 슬픔을 느끼지 않는다는 것을 그녀에게 일러주는 것이었다.\n\n바로 그 이튿날 요브라이트는 험프리의 오두막으로 가서, 자기 것을 살 수 있게 될 때까지 쓰려고 각반과 장갑과 숫돌과 낫을 빌렸다. 그러고는 새 동료 일꾼이자 오랜 지인과 함께 길을 나서, 가시금작화가 가장 빽빽이 자란 자리를 골라, 자기가 택한 생업에서 첫 일격을 내리쳤다. 그의 시력은, 「라셀라스」에 나오는 날개처럼 그 원대한 목적에는 쓸모가 없었으나 이 고비에는 충분했으니, 그는 알게 되었다, 손바닥이 물집에 단련되어 좀 더 단단해지면 수월하게 일할 수 있으리라는 것을.\n\n날이면 날마다 그는 해와 함께 일어나 각반을 졸라매고 험프리와 만나기로 한 자리로 나갔다. 그의 일과는 새벽 네 시부터 정오까지 일하고, 그런 다음 하루 중 가장 더운 때가 되면 집으로 가 한두 시간 잠을 자고, 그 뒤에 다시 나와 저물녘 아홉 시까지 일하는 것이었다.\n\n파리에서 온 이 남자는 이제 가죽 차림새와 눈 위에 어쩔 수 없이 써야 하는 보안경으로 어찌나 변장한 꼴이 되었던지, 가장 가까운 벗이라도 알아보지 못한 채 곁을 지나칠 만했다. 그는 올리브빛 도는 초록색 가시금작화가 펼쳐진 가운데 자리한 갈색 점 하나일 뿐, 그 이상도 아니었다. 실제로 일하고 있지 않을 때면 유스테이샤의 처지와 어머니와의 불화를 생각하느라 종종 기분이 가라앉았으나, 일이 한창 신명나게 돌아갈 때면 그는 명랑하고 차분했다. 그의 일상은 기이하게도 미시적인 종류였으니, 그의 온 세계가 제 몸에서 몇 자 떨어진 둘레에 한정되어 있었다. 그와 친숙한 것들이라곤 기어다니고 날아다니는 것들이었고, 그것들은 그를 제 무리에 끼워 넣은 듯했다. 벌들이 다정한 기색으로 그의 귓가에서 윙윙거리며 옆에서 히스꽃과 가시금작화꽃을 어찌나 떼 지어 잡아당기던지 꽃들을 흙바닥까지 짓누를 지경이었다. 에그던이 길러냈고 다른 어디서도 본 적 없는 그 기이한 호박빛 나비들은 그의 입김에 파르르 떨다가 그의 굽은 등에 내려앉기도 하고, 그가 낫을 위아래로 휘두를 때 그 번쩍이는 끝과 어울려 장난을 쳤다. 에메랄드빛 초록 메뚜기 무리가 그의 발 위로 뛰어넘다가, 서투른 곡예사처럼 운수에 따라 등이며 머리며 엉덩이로 어설피 나자빠지기도 하고, 혹은 고사리 잎사귀 아래서 수수한 빛깔의 말 없는 메뚜기들과 요란한 시시덕거림에 빠져들기도 했다. 식료품 저장실도 철망도 모르는, 그야말로 야생 상태 그대로인 커다란 파리들이 그가 사람인 줄도 모른 채 그의 둘레에서 윙윙거렸다. 뱀들은 고사리 골짜기를 들고나며 가장 찬란한 파랑과 노랑의 자태로 미끄러져 다녔으니, 묵은 허물을 갓 벗어 그 빛깔이 가장 선명한 철에 바로 뒤이은 때였기 때문이다. 어린 토끼 무리가 보금자리에서 나와 둔덕에서 햇볕을 쬐었는데, 뜨거운 햇살이 저마다 얇은 살의 귀에 깃든 섬세한 조직을 꿰뚫어, 핏줄이 들여다보일 만큼 핏빛으로 투명하게 그것을 불태웠다. 그것들 가운데 어느 하나도 그를 두려워하지 않았다.\n\n그의 일이 지닌 단조로움은 그를 달래주었고, 그 자체로 하나의 즐거움이었다. 노력에 강제로 한계가 지워진 덕에, 야심 없는 한 남자에게 수수한 길을 가는 일이 정당화되었으니, 그렇지 않았다면 그의 양심은 자기 능력이 막힘없는데도 그토록 무명 속에 머물러 있도록 좀처럼 허락하지 않았을 것이다. 그리하여 요브라이트는 이따금 혼자 노래를 불렀고, 단을 묶을 끈으로 쓸 가시덤불을 찾으러 험프리를 따라가야 할 때면 파리의 생활과 인물상을 그려 보이며 동료를 즐겁게 했으니, 그렇게 시간을 보냈다.\n\n이렇게 따스한 어느 오후, 유스테이샤는 요브라이트가 일하는 곳을 향해 홀로 걸어 나갔다. 그는 가시금작화를 부지런히 베어내는 중이었고, 그가 있는 자리에서 아래로 길게 늘어선 단의 행렬이 그날 하루의 노동을 나타내고 있었다. 그는 그녀가 다가오는 것을 알아채지 못했고, 그녀는 그의 바로 곁에 서서 그가 낮게 흥얼거리는 노랫가락을 들었다. 그것이 그녀에게 충격을 주었다. 그가 거기서, 가련하게도 병든 남자가 이마에 땀을 흘려 돈벌이를 하는 것을 보았을 때 처음에는 눈물이 솟았으나, 그가 노래를 부르며, 그 자신에게야 흡족할지 몰라도 교육받은 숙녀 아내인 그녀에게는 품위를 떨어뜨리는 일에 조금도 반항하지 않는 것을 듣자, 그것이 그녀의 가슴을 후벼 팠다. 그녀가 곁에 있음을 알아채지 못한 채 그는 계속 노래를 불렀다—\n\n\"날이 밝아오니 우리 숲에 온갖 단장을 되돌려주네. 플로라는 돌아와 더욱 어여쁘고, 새는 다시 사랑의 다정한 노래를 부르네. 자연 만물이 기리어 노래하네 밝아오는 날을.\n\n날이 밝아오니 때로는 시름을, 때로는 모진 아픔을 자아내네. 밤의 자락이 어찌 이리 짧은가, 사랑에 불타는 양치기에게는, 날이 밝아오면 사랑하는 이를 떠나야만 하는 그에게는.\"\n\n유스테이샤에게는 그가 사회적 실패 따위는 그다지 개의치 않는다는 것이 쓰라리도록 분명했고, 자존심 강한 그 아름다운 여인은 고개를 숙이고 병든 절망 속에서 울었으니, 그의 그러한 기분과 처지가 제 삶에 미칠 황폐한 영향을 생각한 까닭이었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n\"나라면 그런 짓을 하느니 차라리 굶어 죽겠어요!\" 그녀가 격하게 외쳤다. \"그런데 당신은 노래를 부르다니! 저는 다시 할아버지한테 가서 살겠어요!\"\n\n\"유스테이샤! 무언가 움직이는 건 알아챘지만 당신인 줄은 몰랐소.\" 그가 부드럽게 말했다. 그는 앞으로 나서더니 커다란 가죽 장갑을 벗고 그녀의 손을 잡았다. \"어째서 그런 야릇한 식으로 말하는 거요? 그저 파리에 있을 때 내 마음을 사로잡았던 자그마한 옛 노래일 뿐이고, 지금 마침 당신과 함께하는 내 삶에 들어맞아서 그렇소. 그럼 내 모습이 더는 훌륭한 신사 같지 않다고 해서 나를 향한 당신의 사랑이 죄다 죽어버린 거요?\"\n\n\"여보, 저를 언짢게 캐묻지 마세요, 안 그러면 당신을 사랑하지 않게 될지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"내가 그렇게 만들 위험을 무릅쓸 거라고 정말 믿는 거요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 당신은 당신 생각만 따르고, 제가 이 부끄러운 막일을 그만두기를 바랄 때조차 제 생각엔 굽혀주지 않잖아요. 제게 무슨 못마땅한 점이라도 있어서 제 바람과 그토록 어긋나게 구는 건가요? 저는 당신 아내인데, 어째서 귀 기울이지 않으세요? 그래요, 저는 정말이지 당신 아내라고요!\"\n\n\"그 말투가 무슨 뜻인지 알겠소.\"\n\n\"무슨 말투요?\"\n\n\"당신이 '정말이지 당신 아내'라고 말한 그 말투 말이오. 그건 '당신 아내라니, 더 박복한 일이지'라는 뜻이었소.\"\n\n\"그런 말로 저를 들쑤시다니 당신도 모질군요. 여자에게도 가슴이 없지 않으면서 나름의 이유가 있을 수 있는 거예요. 그리고 제가 '더 박복하다'고 느꼈다면, 그건 비천한 감정이 아니었어요—너무도 자연스러운 것이었을 뿐이죠. 자, 보세요, 어쨌든 저는 거짓을 꾸미려 들지 않잖아요. 우리가 결혼하기 전에, 제게 좋은 아내 될 자질이 없다고 미리 일러둔 걸 기억하세요?\"\n\n\"이제 와 그 말을 하다니 나를 조롱하는 거요. 적어도 그 점에 있어서는 입을 다무는 게 유일하게 고결한 길일 거요, 유스테이샤, 당신은 여전히 내 여왕이니 말이오, 내가 더는 당신의 왕이 아닐지라도.\"\n\n\"당신은 제 남편이에요. 그것으로 만족하지 못하세요?\"\n\n\"당신이 후회 없는 내 아내가 아니라면 만족 못 하오.\"\n\n\"당신께 대답할 수 없어요. 제가 당신 손에 짐스러운 존재가 되리라고 말했던 건 기억해요.\"\n\n\"그래, 나도 그걸 알아챘소.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 너무 빨리 알아챈 거예요! 참된 연인이라면 그런 걸 알아채지 않았을 거예요. 당신은 제게 너무 가혹해요, 클림—당신이 그렇게 말하는 게 전혀 마음에 안 들어요.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 나는 그런데도 당신과 결혼했고, 그렇게 한 걸 후회하지 않소. 오늘 오후엔 당신이 어쩜 이리 차가워 보이는지! 게다가 나는 당신 가슴보다 더 따뜻한 가슴은 없으리라 여겼었는데.\"\n\n\"그래요, 우리가 식어가는 게 아닌가 싶어요—당신만큼 저도 알겠어요.\" 그녀가 구슬프게 한숨지었다. \"두 달 전엔 우리가 얼마나 미친 듯이 사랑했는지! 당신은 저를 바라보는 데 결코 싫증 내지 않았고, 저도 당신을 바라보는 데 그랬어요. 그때 누가 생각이나 했겠어요, 이맘때면 제 눈이 당신 눈에 그리 밝아 보이지 않고, 당신 입술도 제 입술에 그리 달콤하지 않으리라는 걸요? 두 달—그게 있을 수 있는 일인가요? 그래요, 너무도 사실이에요!\"\n\n\"당신은 한숨을 쉬는구려, 여보, 마치 그걸 애석해하듯이. 그건 희망적인 징조요.\"\n\n\"아뇨. 그것 때문에 한숨짓는 게 아니에요. 제게는, 아니 제 처지에 놓인 어떤 여자에게라도, 한숨지을 다른 것들이 있어요.\"\n\n\"불운한 남자와 서둘러 결혼하는 바람에 인생의 기회를 망쳐버린 것 말이오?\"\n\n\"어째서 저더러 쓰라린 말을 하게 만드시나요, 클림? 저도 당신만큼 동정받을 만해요. 당신만큼이라고요?—제가 더 동정받을 만하다고 생각해요. 당신은 노래라도 부를 수 있으니까요! 이런 먹구름 아래서 제가 노래 부르는 걸 들킨다면 참 별난 일일 거예요! 정말이지, 여보, 저는 당신처럼 탄력 있는 마음이라면 놀라 어쩔 줄 모를 만큼 울 수도 있어요. 설령 당신이 자기 자신의 고난에는 무심했다 하더라도, 순전히 제 고난을 가엾이 여겨서라도 노래만은 삼갈 수 있었을 거예요. 맙소사! 제가 이런 처지에 놓인 남자라면 노래를 부르느니 차라리 저주를 퍼붓겠어요.\"\n\n요브라이트는 그녀의 팔에 손을 얹었다. \"자, 내 풋내기 아가씨, 나라고 당신만큼 프로메테우스답게 드높이 신들과 운명에 맞서 반항할 수 없으리라고는 생각지 마시오. 나는 당신이 들어본 그 어떤 것보다 더 많은 그런 종류의 김과 연기를 느껴왔소. 하지만 삶을 보면 볼수록, 그 가장 높은 길에도 딱히 위대할 게 없으며, 따라서 가시금작화를 베는 내 길에도 딱히 하찮을 게 없다는 걸 알게 되오. 우리에게 허락된 가장 큰 축복조차 그리 값진 게 아니라고 느낀다면, 그것이 거두어진들 어찌 그게 대단한 고난이라 느낄 수 있겠소? 그래서 나는 시간을 보내려고 노래를 부르는 거요. 당신은 정말로 나를 향한 다정함을 죄다 잃어버린 거요, 그래서 내게 명랑한 순간 몇 가지를 누리는 것조차 못마땅해하는 거요?\"\n\n\"당신을 향한 다정함이 아직 좀 남아 있어요.\"\n\n\"당신 말엔 더는 옛날의 향취가 없소. 그러니 사랑은 행운과 더불어 죽는구려!\"\n\n\"이런 말 못 듣겠어요, 클림—쓰라리게 끝날 거예요.\" 그녀가 갈라진 목소리로 말했다. \"집으로 가겠어요.\"\n\nIII.\n그녀는 우울에 맞서 싸우러 나간다\n\n며칠 뒤, 팔월이 채 다 가기 전에, 유스테이샤와 요브라이트는 이른 저녁식사를 함께하며 마주 앉아 있었다.\n\n유스테이샤의 태도는 근래 거의 무기력해져 있었다. 그녀의 아름다운 두 눈에는 처량한 빛이 어려 있었으니, 그녀가 그럴 만한 까닭이 있든 없든, 클림을 향한 사랑이 한껏 무르익었던 시절의 그녀를 알았던 이라면 누구든 가슴에 연민이 일었을 것이다. 남편과 아내의 심정은 어느 정도 그들의 처지와는 반대로 엇갈렸다. 병든 남자인 클림은 명랑했고, 평생 단 한 순간도 육체의 고통을 느껴본 적 없는 그녀를 도리어 위로하려 애쓰기까지 했다.\n\n\"자, 기운 내요, 여보. 우린 다시 다 괜찮아질 거요. 언젠가 어쩌면 나도 예전처럼 잘 보게 될 거요. 그리고 나는 엄숙히 약속하오, 무언가 더 나은 일을 할 힘이 생기는 대로 가시금작화 베는 일은 그만두겠다고. 설마 내가 하루 종일 집에서 빈둥거리며 지내기를 진심으로 바라는 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"하지만 너무 끔찍하잖아요—가시금작화 베는 사람이라니! 게다가 당신은 세상을 두루 살아왔고, 프랑스어도 독일어도 할 줄 알며, 이것보다 훨씬 더 나은 일에 어울리는 사람인데요.\"\n\n\"당신이 나를 처음 보고 내 이야기를 들었을 때, 내가 당신 눈에는 일종의 금빛 후광에 감싸인 것처럼 보였나 보군—영광스러운 것들을 알고, 화려한 무대에 섞여본 남자—요컨대 흠모할 만하고, 황홀하고, 정신을 쏙 빼놓는 영웅으로 말이오?\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 그녀가 흐느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 이제 나는 갈색 가죽을 걸친 가련한 사내가 되었군.\"\n\n\"저를 빈정대지 마세요. 하지만 이런 얘긴 그만하죠. 저는 더는 우울해하지 않겠어요. 당신이 크게 반대하지 않는다면, 오늘 오후 집을 나가 있겠어요. 이스트 에그던에서 마을 소풍이—집시 놀이라고들 하는 게—열린대요, 저는 거기 갈래요.\"\n\n\"춤추러?\"\n\n\"왜 안 되나요? 당신은 노래를 부르잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 당신 좋을 대로 하오. 내가 데리러 가야겠소?\"\n\n\"당신 일에서 충분히 일찍 돌아온다면요. 하지만 그것 때문에 무리하진 마세요. 저는 집에 오는 길을 알고, 황야가 제겐 조금도 무섭지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"그래, 당신은 그 흥겨움에 그토록 간절히 매달려, 그걸 찾아 마을 잔치까지 줄곧 걸어갈 만하단 말이오?\"\n\n\"이런, 제가 혼자 가는 게 싫으신 거군요! 클림, 질투하시는 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"아니오. 하지만 당신에게 조금이라도 즐거움이 된다면 함께 가겠소. 비록 지금 형편상 당신은 이미 나를 너무 많이 곁에 두고 있는지도 모르지만 말이오. 그래도 어쩐지 나는 당신이 가고 싶어 하지 않았으면 싶소. 그래, 어쩌면 나는 질투하는 거요. 그런데 반쯤 눈먼 남자가 당신 같은 여자를 두고 질투하는 것보다 더 그럴 만한 이유를 가진 사람이 어디 있겠소?\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각지 마세요. 저를 보내주시고, 제 기운을 죄다 앗아가지 마세요!\"\n\n\"차라리 내 기운을 죄다 잃는 편이 낫겠소, 내 사랑하는 아내여. 가서 당신 하고 싶은 대로 하오. 누가 당신이 어떤 변덕에 빠지는 것을 막을 수 있겠소? 당신은 아직 내 마음을 온통 가지고 있다고 나는 믿소. 그리고 사실 당신에게 짐이 되는 나를 당신이 견뎌주니, 나는 당신에게 고마운 빚을 지고 있소. 그래, 혼자 가서 빛나구려. 나로 말하면, 나는 내 운명을 붙들고 있겠소. 그런 모임에서 사람들은 나를 피할 테니. 내 낫과 장갑은 나병 환자의 성 나사로 딸랑이와 같아서, 보는 이를 슬프게 할 광경을 비키라고 세상에 경고하는 거요.\" 그는 그녀에게 입을 맞추고 각반을 차고는 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n그가 떠나자 그녀는 두 손에 머리를 기대고 혼잣말을 했다. \"허비된 두 인생—그의 것과 내 것. 그런데 내가 이 지경에 이르다니! 이러다 내가 미쳐버리는 건 아닐까?\"\n\n그녀는 지금의 상태에서 조금이라도 나아질 만한 길이 있을까 두루 궁리했으나, 아무것도 찾을 수 없었다. 자기가 어찌 되었는지 알게 될 버드머스의 그 사람들이 \"아무도 성에 차지 않던 그 계집애 좀 봐!\" 하고 말하는 광경을 그녀는 그려보았다. 유스테이샤에게는 이 처지가 자기 희망에 대한 너무도 지독한 조롱처럼 여겨져, 만일 하늘의 풍자가 더 멀리까지 나아간다면 죽음만이 유일한 구원의 문으로 보였다.\n\n문득 그녀는 정신을 차리고 외쳤다. \"하지만 난 이걸 떨쳐내겠어. 그래, 난 기어이 떨쳐낼 거야! 아무도 내 고통을 알지 못하게 하겠어. 쓰라리도록 즐거워하고, 비꼬듯 명랑하게 굴며, 비웃듯 깔깔거리겠어. 그리고 우선 풀밭의 이 춤판에 가는 것부터 시작하겠어.\"\n\n그녀는 침실로 올라가 더없이 세심하게 차림새를 갖췄다. 곁에서 보는 이라면 그녀의 아름다움 덕에 그녀의 심정이 거의 당연한 것처럼 여겨졌을 것이다. 무분별 못지않게 우연이 이 여인을 몰아넣은 그 음울한 구석은, 어지간히 미온적인 편들기를 하는 사람조차도 이렇게 느끼게 했을 법하니, 곧 그녀가 지고至高한 권능에 대고 도대체 무슨 권리로 이토록 정교하게 빚어진 존재를, 제 매력이 축복이 아니라 도리어 저주가 되도록 꾸며진 상황 속에 놓았느냐고 따져 물을 만한 절절한 이유가 그녀에게 있다고 말이다.\n\n그녀가 외출 채비를 마치고 집에서 나선 것은 오후 다섯 시였다. 그 그림 속에는 새로운 정복을 스무 번은 더 이뤄낼 만한 밑감이 담겨 있었다. 모자도 쓰지 않고 집 안에 앉아 있을 때면 다소 지나치게 도드라지던 반항기 어린 슬픔은, 그녀의 나들이옷에 가려 누그러져 있었으니, 그 옷차림에는 늘 어딘가 흐릿한 데가 있어 어디에도 모진 윤곽이 없었다. 그리하여 그녀의 얼굴은 마치 구름에서 내다보듯 그 옷자락 속에서 내다보였고, 살과 옷 사이에 이렇다 할 경계선이 보이지 않았다. 한낮의 열기는 아직 거의 누그러지지 않았고, 그녀는 한가로이 빈둥거릴 그 나들이에 시간이 넉넉했던 터라, 햇볕 드는 언덕을 따라 느릿한 걸음으로 걸어갔다. 길이 키 큰 고사리 사이로 날 때면 그것들의 잎사귀가 그녀를 파묻었으니, 그것들은 이제 자그마한 숲을 이루고 있었으나, 그 줄기 가운데 단 하나도 이듬해에 새싹을 틔우려 남아 있지는 않을 터였다.\n\n마을 잔치를 위해 고른 자리는 잔디밭 같은 오아시스 가운데 하나였으니, 황야 지대의 고원에서 이따금, 그러나 흔치는 않게 마주치는 곳이었다. 가시금작화와 고사리 덤불이 그 가장자리를 빙 둘러 뚝 끊겼고, 풀밭은 끊긴 데 없이 이어져 있었다. 푸른 가축길 하나가 그곳을 따라 났으나, 고사리 장막에서 벗어나지는 않았는데, 유스테이샤는 무리에 끼어들기 전에 정찰해보려고 이 길을 따라갔다. 이스트 에그던 악단의 우렁찬 가락이 그녀를 한 치도 어긋남 없이 인도했고, 이제 그녀는 악사들의 모습을 마주 보게 되었으니, 그들은 새것처럼 반짝반짝 닦은 붉은 바퀴가 달린 파란 짐마차에 앉아 있었고, 그 마차는 나뭇가지와 꽃을 동여맨 막대들로 아치를 이루고 있었다. 그 앞에서는 열다섯에서 스무 쌍이 이루는 웅장한 한가운데 춤판이 벌어졌고, 그 가락에 늘 엄격히 맞지는 않게 빙글빙글 도는, 한층 못한 이들의 자잘한 춤판들이 그 양옆에 늘어서 있었다.\n\n젊은 남자들은 파란색과 흰색 장식꽃을 달고, 얼굴에 홍조를 띤 채 처녀들에게 발맞춰 춤추었으니, 처녀들은 흥분과 운동으로 저들이 단 무수한 리본의 분홍빛보다 더 짙게 볼을 붉혔다. 긴 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨린 고운 처녀들, 짧은 곱슬머리의 고운 처녀들, 애교머리를 늘어뜨린 고운 처녀들, 땋은 머리를 한 고운 처녀들이 빙글빙글 돌고 또 돌았고, 보는 이는 이처럼 비슷한 키와 나이와 기질을 지닌 호감 가는 젊은 여인들이, 고를 만한 마을이라곤 한둘밖에 없는 곳에서 어찌 이리 한데 모일 수 있었는지 자못 의아해할 만했다. 뒤편에서는 행복한 사내 하나가 눈을 감고 나머지 모든 것은 까맣게 잊은 채 혼자 춤추고 있었다. 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에서는 잘라낸 가시나무 아래 불이 타고 있었고, 그 위로 주전자 세 개가 한 줄로 걸려 있었다. 바로 곁에는 나이 든 부인들이 차를 마련하는 탁자가 있었으나, 유스테이샤가 그들 사이에서 아무리 찾아보아도, 자기더러 오라고 권하며 정중한 환대를 받게 해주겠다고 약속했던 그 가축상의 아내는 보이지 않았다. 유스테이샤가 아는 단 한 사람인 그 고장 주민이 뜻밖에 자리에 없자, 마음껏 흥겨운 오후를 보내려던 그녀의 계획은 적잖이 어그러졌다. 무리에 끼어드는 일이 곤란해졌으니, 그녀가 나서기만 한다면 명랑한 부인들이 찻잔을 들고 다가와, 자기들보다 우아함과 식견이 빼어난 낯선 손님이라며 떠받들 터인데도 그러했다. 두 차례 춤이 펼쳐지는 동안 무리를 지켜본 그녀는, 조금 더 멀리 어느 오두막까지 걸어가 요기를 좀 하고, 그늘이 지는 저녁때에 집으로 돌아가기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그녀는 그렇게 했고, 올더워스로 가는 길에 다시 지나쳐야 하는 집시 놀이판 쪽으로 발길을 되돌릴 무렵에는 해가 기울고 있었다. 공기가 이제 어찌나 고요했던지 그녀는 멀리서 들려오는 악단 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 그 소리는 그녀가 자리를 떴을 때보다, 그럴 수 있다면, 한층 신명나게 연주되는 듯했다. 언덕에 다다랐을 때 해는 완전히 자취를 감췄으나, 그것은 유스테이샤에게도 흥청거리는 이들에게도 별다른 차이가 없었으니, 둥근 노란 달이 그녀 앞에서 떠오르고 있었기 때문이다, 비록 그 빛살이 아직 서녘에서 오는 빛살을 압도하지는 못했으나. 춤은 여전히 똑같이 펼쳐지고 있었으나, 낯선 이들이 와서 춤판 둘레에 고리를 이룬 터라, 유스테이샤는 알아볼 만한 일 없이 그들 사이에 끼어 설 수 있었다.\n\n한 해 내내 사방으로 흩어져 있던 한 마을 가득한 관능의 정념이, 여기 한 시간 동안 한 초점으로 솟구쳐 올랐다. 물결치듯 춤추는 그 쌍들의 마흔 개 심장은, 열두 달 전 비슷한 흥겨움 속에 함께 모인 이래 뛰어보지 못한 만큼 세차게 뛰고 있었다. 한동안 그들의 가슴속에 이교의 정신이 되살아났고, 삶의 자긍심이 전부였으며, 그들은 자기 자신 말고는 아무것도 떠받들지 않았다.\n\n그 격정에 사로잡혔으되 한순간일 뿐인 포옹들 가운데 얼마만큼이 영원한 것이 될 운명인지는, 어쩌면 그것에 빠져든 이들 가운데 몇몇뿐 아니라 곁에서 지켜보는 유스테이샤에게도 궁금한 일이었으리라. 그녀는 그 빙글빙글 도는 이들을 부러워하기 시작했고, 춤의 매혹이 그들 안에 자아내는 듯한 그 희망과 행복에 굶주리기 시작했다. 본디 춤이라면 사족을 못 쓸 만큼 좋아했던 유스테이샤가 파리에 걸었던 기대 가운데 하나는, 이 가장 좋아하는 소일거리에 마음껏 빠져들 기회를 거기서 얻으리라는 것이었다. 불행히도 이제 그 기대는 그녀 안에서 영영 꺼져버린 터였다.\n\n그녀가 짙어가는 달빛 속에서 빙글빙글 돌며 물결치는 그들을 넋 놓고 바라보던 중에, 문득 어깨 너머에서 어떤 목소리가 자기 이름을 속삭이는 것을 들었다. 놀라 돌아서니, 그녀는 팔꿈치께에서 한 사람을 보았는데, 그가 거기 있다는 것만으로도 그녀는 대번에 관자놀이까지 붉어졌다.\n\n와일디브였다. 이 순간까지 그는 자기 결혼식 날 아침 이래 그녀와 눈이 마주친 적이 없었으니, 그날 그녀는 교회 안을 서성이다가 면사포를 들어 올리고 증인으로서 혼인 명부에 서명하러 앞으로 나서서 그를 깜짝 놀라게 했었다. 그러나 어째서 그를 보자 그토록 갑작스레 피가 솟구쳤는지 그녀로서는 알 수 없었다.\n\n그녀가 입을 열기도 전에 그가 속삭였다. \"여전히 그렇게나 춤을 좋아하시오?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같아요.\" 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"나와 춤추시겠소?\"\n\n\"제겐 큰 변화가 되겠죠. 하지만 이상해 보이지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"친척끼리 함께 춤추는 데 이상할 게 뭐가 있겠소?\"\n\n\"아—그래요, 친척이죠. 어쩌면 아무것도 없겠네요.\"\n\n\"그래도 남의 눈에 띄는 게 싫다면 면사포를 내리시오. 이런 빛 속에서야 알아볼 위험이 크지 않겠지만 말이오. 낯선 이들이 잔뜩 와 있소.\"\n\n그녀는 그가 일러준 대로 했고, 그 행동은 그의 청을 받아들였다는 무언의 인정이었다.\n\n와일디브는 그녀에게 팔을 내주고 고리 바깥쪽을 따라 그녀를 춤판 맨 아래로 데려갔으며, 두 사람은 거기서 춤판에 들어섰다. 다시 이 분쯤 지나자 그들은 춤의 대형에 휘말려 위쪽 맨 윗자리를 향해 차근차근 나아가기 시작했다. 그리로 절반쯤 나아가는 동안 유스테이샤는 그의 청에 굴복하지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을 하고 한 번 이상 바랐으나, 중간에서 맨 위에 이르는 동안에는 즐거움을 찾아 나선 이상 그것을 얻으려 하는 것은 그저 자연스러운 일을 하는 것일 뿐이라고 느꼈다. 맨 윗자리 짝이라는 새로운 위치가 그들에게 열어준 끊임없는 미끄러짐과 휘돎 속으로 거침없이 뛰어드니, 유스테이샤의 맥박은 어떤 종류의 긴 사색에 잠기기에는 너무 빨리 뛰기 시작했다.\n\n스물다섯 쌍의 줄을 누비며 그들은 어지러운 길을 헤쳐갔고, 새로운 생기가 그녀의 몸속으로 들어왔다. 저물녘의 창백한 빛살이 그 경험에 매혹을 더했다. 감각의 균형을 흐트러뜨리고 한결 부드러운 기분을 위태로울 만큼 부추기는, 어떤 정도와 가락의 빛이라는 것이 있으니, 거기에 움직임이 더해지면 그것은 감정을 무성하게 내몰고, 이성은 그에 반비례하여 졸음에 겨워 분별하지 못하게 되는데, 지금 이 빛이 달의 원반에서 이 두 사람 위로 내려앉았다. 춤추는 처녀들 모두가 그 증상을 느꼈으나, 그 누구보다 유스테이샤가 가장 강하게 느꼈다. 그들의 발밑에서 풀은 짓밟혀 사라졌고, 단단히 다져진 흙바닥은 달빛 쪽으로 비스듬히 바라보면 잘 닦인 식탁처럼 빛났다. 공기는 아주 고요해졌고, 악사들을 태운 짐마차 위의 깃발은 깃대에 달라붙었으며, 연주자들은 하늘을 배경으로 윤곽으로만 보였으니, 다만 트롬본과 오피클라이드와 프렌치 호른의 둥근 입이 그들 형체의 그늘에서 거대한 눈처럼 번쩍일 때만은 예외였다. 처녀들의 어여쁜 옷들은 한낮의 그 미묘한 빛깔을 잃고 어느 정도 뿌연 흰빛을 띠었다. 유스테이샤는 와일디브의 팔에 의지해 빙글빙글 돌고 또 돌았고, 그녀의 얼굴은 넋을 잃은 듯 조각처럼 굳어 있었으니, 그녀의 영혼이 그 이목구비에서 빠져나가 그것을 잊어버려, 감정이 그것들의 표현 범위를 넘어설 때면 늘 그러하듯 그것들은 텅 빈 채 잠잠히 남겨졌다.\n\n그녀가 와일디브와 얼마나 가까이 있었던가! 생각만 해도 끔찍한 일이었다. 그녀는 그의 숨결을 느낄 수 있었고, 그 또한 물론 그녀의 숨결을 느낄 수 있었다. 그녀가 그를 얼마나 모질게 대했던가! 그런데도 여기서 그들은 하나의 박자를 밟고 있었다. 춤의 마법이 그녀를 놀라게 했다. 또렷한 차이의 선 하나가 손에 잡힐 듯한 울타리처럼, 이 움직임의 미로 안에서의 경험과 그 바깥에서의 경험을 갈라놓았다. 그녀가 춤추기 시작한 것은 마치 대기가 바뀌는 것과 같았으니, 바깥에서 그녀는 여기 이 열대의 감각에 견주면 북극의 매서운 추위에 푹 잠겨 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 근래의 그 시름겨운 시간들로부터 이 춤 속으로 들어왔으니, 마치 숲속에서 밤길을 걷다가 휘황한 방으로 들어서는 것과 같았다. 와일디브 혼자였다면 그저 마음의 동요에 지나지 않았을 것이나, 춤과 달빛과 은밀함에 더해진 와일디브는 하나의 기쁨이 되기 시작했다. 이 달콤하게 뒤섞인 감정의 더 큰 몫을 그의 사람됨이 빚어냈는지, 아니면 춤과 그 정경이 거기서 더 큰 무게를 차지했는지는, 유스테이샤 자신도 도무지 알 수 없는 미묘한 문제였다.\n\n사람들이 \"저들이 누구지?\" 하고 말하기 시작했으나, 시샘 어린 캐물음은 일지 않았다. 유스테이샤가 다른 처녀들과 어울려 평소의 나날을 걸었더라면 사정은 달랐을 테지만, 여기서는 지나친 눈길에 시달리지 않았으니, 모두가 이 흥겨운 자리에 한껏 우아하게 매만져진 까닭이었다. 해 질 녘의 광채에 둘러싸인 수성水星이라는 행성처럼, 그녀의 변함없는 광휘는 그 순간의 일시적인 영광 속에서 그다지 눈에 띄지 않은 채 지나갔다.\n\n와일디브로 말하면, 그의 심정은 어렵잖게 짐작할 수 있다. 장애물은 그의 사랑에 무르익게 하는 햇볕이었고, 그는 이 순간 더없이 절묘한 비참함의 광란 속에 있었다. 한 해 내내 다른 남자의 것인 그 여자를 단 오 분이나마 제 것으로 끌어안는다는 것은, 다른 누구보다도 그가 진가를 알아볼 만한 종류의 일이었다. 그는 이미 오래전부터 유스테이샤를 향해 다시 한숨짓기 시작한 터였으니, 실로 토머신과 함께 혼인 명부에 서명한 것이야말로 그의 마음이 첫 거처로 되돌아가게 만든 자연스러운 신호였으며, 유스테이샤의 결혼이라는 부가된 복잡함이야말로 그 되돌아감을 거역할 수 없게 만드는 데 필요한 단 하나의 덤이었다고 단언할 수 있다.\n\n이렇게, 서로 다른 까닭에서, 나머지 사람들에게는 신명나는 율동이던 것이 이 두 사람에게는 회오리바람을 올라타는 일이었다. 춤은 그들 마음속에 남아 있던 사회적 질서 감각이 무엇이든 그것을 향한 거역할 수 없는 공격처럼 들이닥쳐, 이제는 갑절로 어긋난 옛길로 그들을 도로 몰아넣었다. 잇따라 세 차례 춤을 추며 그들은 길을 빙빙 돌았고, 그런 다음 쉼 없는 움직임에 지친 유스테이샤는 이미 너무 오래 머물러 있던 그 고리를 떠나려고 몸을 돌렸다. 와일디브는 그녀를 몇 자 떨어진 풀 둔덕으로 이끌었고, 거기 그녀가 앉자 그녀의 짝은 그 곁에 섰다. 춤이 시작될 무렵 그가 그녀에게 말을 건 때부터 지금까지 그들은 한마디도 주고받지 않았다.\n\n\"춤추고 걷느라 지치셨소?\" 그가 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"아뇨, 그리 많이는요.\"\n\n\"하필 모든 곳 가운데 여기서, 그토록 오래 서로 못 본 끝에 우리가 만났다니 이상한 일이오.\"\n\n\"우리가 서로 못 본 건 안 보려고 애썼기 때문이겠죠, 아마도.\"\n\n\"그렇소. 하지만 그 일을 시작한 건 당신이오—약속을 깨뜨려서 말이오.\"\n\n\"이제 와 그 얘긴 할 만한 가치도 없어요. 우리는 그 뒤로 다른 인연을 맺었으니까요—저 못지않게 당신도요.\"\n\n\"당신 남편이 편찮다니 안되었소.\"\n\n\"편찮은 게 아니에요—그저 일을 못 하게 됐을 뿐이죠.\"\n\n\"그래요—내 말이 그 말이오. 당신이 겪는 곤경에 진심으로 마음 아파하오. 운명이 당신을 모질게 대했구려.\"\n\n그녀는 한동안 말이 없었다. \"그가 가시금작화 베는 일을 하기로 했다는 걸 들으셨어요?\" 그녀가 낮고 구슬픈 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그런 얘기가 제게 전해지긴 했소.\" 와일디브가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"하지만 도무지 믿기지 않았소.\"\n\n\"사실이에요. 가시금작화 베는 사내의 아내가 된 저를 어떻게 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"나는 당신을 한결같이 생각하오, 유스테이샤. 그런 종류의 일이 당신의 품위를 떨어뜨릴 수는 없소—당신은 남편의 일을 도리어 고귀하게 만드는 거요.\"\n\n\"저도 그렇게 느낄 수 있다면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"요브라이트 씨가 나아질 가망은 좀 있소?\"\n\n\"그는 그렇게 생각해요. 저는 미심쩍지만요.\"\n\n\"그가 오두막을 얻었다는 말을 듣고 나는 자못 놀랐소. 나는 다른 사람들처럼, 그가 당신과 결혼하자마자 곧장 당신을 파리의 보금자리로 데려가리라 여겼었소. '그녀 앞에는 얼마나 흥겹고 빛나는 미래가 펼쳐져 있는가!' 하고 나는 생각했소. 그의 시력이 다시 튼튼해지면, 그는 아마 당신과 함께 그리로 돌아가겠지요?\"\n\n그녀가 대답하지 않는 것을 보고 그는 그녀를 더 가까이 살폈다. 그녀는 거의 울고 있었다. 결코 누리지 못할 미래의 영상들, 쓰라린 환멸이 되살아난 느낌, 와일디브의 말이 불러일으킨 이웃들의 잠시 미뤄둔 비웃음의 그림—이 모든 것이 자존심 강한 유스테이샤의 평정에는 너무 벅찼던 것이다.\n\n와일디브는 그녀의 말없는 동요를 보자 너무 앞서 나가는 제 감정을 좀처럼 다스리기 어려웠다. 그러나 그는 이를 알아채지 못한 체했고, 그녀는 이내 평정을 되찾았다.\n\n\"혼자 걸어서 집에 갈 생각은 아니겠지요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"아뇨, 그럴 거예요.\" 유스테이샤가 말했다. \"가진 것 하나 없는 제가 이 황야에서 무엇에 해를 입겠어요?\"\n\n\"조금만 길을 돌면 나도 당신과 같은 길로 집에 갈 수 있소. 스루프 모퉁이까지라도 기꺼이 동무가 되어드리겠소.\" 유스테이샤가 망설이며 그대로 앉아 있는 것을 보고 그가 덧붙였다. \"지난여름의 일들이 있고 나서 나와 같은 길에 있는 걸 남에게 보이는 게 현명치 못하다고 여기는 거요?\"\n\n\"정말이지 그런 건 전혀 생각지 않아요.\" 그녀가 도도하게 말했다. \"에그던의 그 가련한 주민들이 무어라 떠들든, 저는 제가 고른 사람의 동무를 받아들이겠어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 걸어갑시다—채비가 되었다면 말이오. 우리의 가장 가까운 길은 저 아래 보이는, 그늘이 짙게 드리운 저 호랑가시나무 덤불 쪽이오.\"\n\n유스테이샤는 일어나 그가 가리킨 방향으로 그의 곁에서 걸었으니, 이슬에 젖어가는 히스와 고사리를 헤치고 길을 내며, 여전히 춤을 이어가는 흥겨운 이들의 가락을 등 뒤로 들으며 걸었다. 달은 이제 환하고 은빛으로 차올라 있었으나, 황야는 그러한 빛 비춤에도 끄떡없었으니, 그리하여 진기한 정경이 펼쳐졌다, 천정에서 끝자락까지 더없이 흰 빛으로 가득 찬 대기 아래, 어둡고 빛살 하나 들지 않는 한 자락 들판이라는 정경이. 그들 위에서 내려다보는 눈이 있었다면, 그 두 사람의 얼굴은 그 너른 들판 가운데 흑단黑檀 탁자 위에 놓인 두 알의 진주처럼 보였을 것이다.\n\n이러한 까닭에 길의 울퉁불퉁함은 눈에 보이지 않았고, 와일디브는 이따금 발이 걸려 비틀거렸으며, 한편 유스테이샤는 작은 히스 덤불이나 가시금작화 뿌리가 그 좁은 길의 풀 사이로 불쑥 솟아나 발을 휘감을 때마다 우아하게 균형을 잡는 묘기를 부려야 했다. 그렇게 길을 가는 고비고비마다 그녀를 붙들어주려 한 손이 어김없이 앞으로 내밀어져, 평탄한 땅에 다시 이를 때까지 그녀를 단단히 붙잡았고, 그러고 나면 그 손은 다시 정중한 거리로 거두어졌다.\n\n그들은 길의 대부분을 말없이 갔고, 스루프 모퉁이에 가까워졌으니, 거기서 몇백 야드 떨어진 곳에서 짧은 샛길 하나가 유스테이샤의 집으로 갈라져 나갔다. 차츰 그들은 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 한 쌍의 사람 형상을 알아보았는데, 보아하니 남자들인 듯했다.\n\n그들이 조금 더 가까이 왔을 때 유스테이샤가 침묵을 깨고 말했다. \"저 사람들 중 하나는 제 남편이에요. 저를 마중 나오겠다고 약속했거든요.\"\n\n\"그리고 다른 하나는 내 가장 큰 원수요.\" 와일디브가 말했다.\n\n\"디고리 벤 같네요.\"\n\n\"바로 그 사람이오.\"\n\n\"거북한 만남이네요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 그게 제 팔자죠. 그는 저에 대해 너무 많이 알아요, 더 많이 알아서 지금 자기가 아는 게 아무것도 아니라는 걸 스스로 증명해 보일 수 없는 한 말이죠. 뭐, 될 대로 되라죠—당신은 저를 저들에게 넘겨주셔야 해요.\"\n\n\"내게 그렇게 하라고 시키기 전에 두 번은 생각하실 거요. 저기 우리가 레인배로에서 만났던 일을 단 한 가지도 잊지 않은 사내가 있소—그가 당신 남편과 함께 있단 말이오. 여기서 우리가 함께 있는 걸 보고서, 우리가 집시 잔치에서 만나 춤춘 게 우연이었다고 저들 중 누가 믿겠소?\"\n\n\"좋아요.\" 그녀가 음울하게 속삭였다. \"저들이 올라오기 전에 저를 떠나세요.\"\n\n와일디브는 그녀에게 다정한 작별을 고하고 고사리와 가시금작화를 가로질러 뛰어들었고, 유스테이샤는 천천히 계속 걸었다. 이삼 분 뒤 그녀는 남편과 그 동행을 만났다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 내 여정은 여기서 끝나오, 레들맨.\" 요브라이트가 그녀를 알아보자마자 말했다. \"나는 이 사람과 함께 돌아가겠소. 잘 가시오.\"\n\n\"안녕히 가세요, 요브라이트 씨.\" 벤이 말했다. \"곧 더 나아지신 모습을 뵙기 바랍니다.\"\n\n벤이 말할 때 달빛이 그의 얼굴에 곧장 비쳐 그 모든 주름살을 유스테이샤에게 드러냈다. 그는 미심쩍은 눈으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다. 그\n역시 빛에 익숙한 눈으로 베인이 알아본 것을 요브라이트의 약한 시력은 보지 못했던 것이니 — 유스테이시아 곁에서 막 물러나는 한 남자의 모습이었다 — 그것은 충분히 있을 법한 일의 범위 안에 있었다.\n\n만약 유스테이시아가 레들맨을 뒤따라갈 수 있었다면 자신의 생각을 뒷받침하는 놀라운 증거를 곧 발견했으리라. 클림이 그녀에게 팔을 내주어 그 자리에서 데리고 나가자마자 레들맨은 이스트 에그던으로 향하던 다져진 길에서 발길을 돌렸는데, 그가 그쪽으로 어슬렁거리며 걸었던 것은 단지 클림의 산책에 동행하기 위해서였을 뿐, 디고리의 마차가 다시 그 근방에 와 있었기 때문이다. 그는 긴 다리를 쭉 뻗어 와일드브가 갔던 방향과 어느 정도 일치하는 쪽으로 황야의 길 없는 구간을 가로질렀다. 이 시각에 베인만한 속도로 그 텁수룩한 비탈을 내려가면서도 구덩이에 곤두박질치거나 토끼 굴에 발이 끼어 다리가 부러지지 않을 수 있는 사람은 밤마다 떠돌아다니는 데 익숙한 자뿐이었으리라. 그러나 베인은 별다른 불편 없이 나아갔고, 그가 내달리는 진로는 콰이엇 우먼 여관을 향하고 있었다. 그는 반 시간쯤 만에 그곳에 다다랐으며, 자신이 출발할 때 스루프 코너 부근에 있던 어떤 사람도 자기보다 먼저 여기 내려올 수는 없었으리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n외딴 여관은 아직 문을 닫지 않았으나 안에 있는 사람이라곤 거의 없었으니, 거기서 이루어지는 영업이란 주로 먼 여정에 그 여관을 지나가는 나그네들을 상대로 하는 것인데, 그들은 이제 제 갈 길을 떠난 뒤였다. 베인은 공동 객실로 들어가 에일 한 잔을 청하고는 무심한 어조로 하녀에게 와일드브 씨가 집에 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n토머신은 안쪽 방에 앉아 있다가 베인의 목소리를 들었다. 손님이 있을 때면 그녀는 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않았으니, 이 일에 대한 타고난 거부감 때문이었는데, 오늘 밤은 거기에 다른 사람이 아무도 없음을 알아채고는 나왔다.\n\n\"그이는 아직 안 들어왔어요, 디고리.\" 그녀가 상냥하게 말했다. \"하지만 더 일찍 올 줄 알았는데. 이스트 에그던에 말을 사러 갔거든요.\"\n\n\"밝은 색 챙 넓은 모자를 쓰고 있었나요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 스루프 코너에서 말 한 마리를 집으로 끌고 가는 그를 봤어요.\" 베인이 무덤덤하게 말했다. \"얼굴은 희고 갈기는 밤처럼 까만 멋진 놈이더군요. 곧 여기 오겠지요, 틀림없이.\" 그는 자리에서 일어나 토머신의 맑고 다정한 얼굴을 잠시 바라보았는데, 마지막으로 본 이래로 그 얼굴 위에는 슬픔의 그림자가 드리워 있었으니, 그는 한마디 덧붙이는 모험을 감행했다. \"와일드브 씨가 요즘 자주 집을 비우는 것 같네요.\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요.\" 토머신이 명랑한 어조로 들리도록 한 목소리로 외쳤다. \"남편이란 게 다 잘 빠져나가잖아요. 저녁마다 그이를 제 마음대로 집에 붙잡아둘 수 있는 무슨 비책이라도 일러주시면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"아는 게 있는지 생각해보지요.\" 베인은 가벼움이 전혀 담기지 않은 그 똑같이 가벼운 어조로 대답했다. 그러고는 자기 나름으로 고안한 식으로 절을 하고 가려고 몸을 움직였다. 토머신이 그에게 손을 내밀었고, 레들맨은 한숨 한 번 내쉬지 않았으나, 한숨 여러 번 쉴 거리를 안은 채 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n십오 분쯤 뒤에 와일드브가 돌아오자 토머신은 요즘 그녀에게 버릇이 된 그 머뭇거리는 태도로 그저 간단하게 말했다. \"말은 어디 있어요, 데이먼?\"\n\n\"아, 결국 안 샀어. 그자가 너무 비싸게 부르더라고.\"\n\n\"하지만 누가 당신이 스루프 코너에서 그걸 끌고 오는 걸 봤다던데요 — 얼굴은 희고 갈기는 밤처럼 까만 멋진 놈을요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 와일드브가 그녀에게 눈길을 고정하며 말했다. \"누가 그런 소릴 하던가?\"\n\n\"레들맨 베인이요.\"\n\n와일드브의 얼굴 표정이 묘하게 응축되었다. \"그건 잘못 본 거야 — 다른 누군가였겠지.\" 그는 느리고 신경질적으로 말했으니, 베인의 반격이 다시 시작되었음을 알아챘기 때문이다.\n\n거친 강압이 동원되다\n\n토머신의 그 말은 별것 아닌 듯하면서도 많은 것을 담고 있었으니, 디고리 베인의 귀에 그대로 남았다. \"저녁마다 그이를 집에 붙잡아두게 도와주세요.\"\n\n이번에 베인이 에그던 황야에 온 것은 그저 반대편으로 건너가기 위해서였을 뿐 — 그는 요브라이트 가문의 이해관계와 더 이상 아무 연관이 없었고, 따로 처리해야 할 자기 일이 있었다. 그러나 그는 문득 토머신을 위해 책략을 부리던 옛 궤도로 자신이 떠밀려 들어가고 있음을 느끼기 시작했다.\n\n그는 마차 안에 앉아 곰곰이 생각했다. 토머신의 말과 태도로 보아 와일드브가 그녀를 등한시하고 있음을 그는 분명히 짐작할 수 있었다. 유스테이시아가 아니라면 그가 대체 누구 때문에 그녀를 등한시하겠는가? 하지만 유스테이시아가 조직적으로 그를 부추기고 있음을 가리킬 만큼 일이 그렇게까지 무르익었다는 것은 좀처럼 믿기 어려웠다. 베인은 와일드브의 거처에서 골짜기를 따라 올더워스의 클림 집으로 이어지는 그 외딴 길을 어느 정도 조심스럽게 정찰하기로 마음먹었다.\n\n이 무렵, 앞서 보았듯이 와일드브는 미리 작정한 어떤 음모적 행위와도 전혀 무관했고, 잔디밭 춤판에서를 제외하면 그녀가 결혼한 이래 유스테이시아를 한 번도 만난 적이 없었다. 그러나 음모의 기운이 그의 안에 있었다는 것은 그가 최근에 들인 낭만적인 버릇 — 날이 어두워진 뒤에 나가서 올더워스 쪽으로 어슬렁거리며 거기서 달과 별을 바라보고, 유스테이시아의 집을 바라보고, 느긋하게 걸어 돌아오는 버릇으로 드러난 바 있다.\n\n따라서 축제 다음 날 밤 망을 보던 레들맨은 그가 작은 오솔길을 따라 올라가 클림네 정원 앞 대문에 기대어 한숨을 쉬고는 다시 돌아가려고 몸을 돌리는 것을 보았다. 와일드브의 음모란 실제적이라기보다는 관념적인 것임이 분명했다. 베인은 그보다 앞서 언덕을 내려가 오솔길이 그저 히스 사이로 깊이 파인 고랑에 지나지 않는 곳까지 물러났는데, 여기서 그는 몇 분간 알 수 없는 모양새로 땅 위로 몸을 굽혔다가 자리를 떴다. 와일드브가 그 지점에 이르렀을 때 그의 발목이 무언가에 걸려 그는 곤두박질쳤다.\n\n숨 쉴 힘을 되찾자마자 그는 일어나 앉아 귀를 기울였다. 어둠 속에는 여름 바람의 맥없는 일렁임 말고는 아무 소리도 없었다. 자신을 넘어뜨린 장애물을 손으로 더듬어 찾던 그는 히스 두 다발이 길을 가로질러 서로 묶여 고리를 이루고 있음을 발견했으니, 그것은 나그네에게 영락없는 넘어짐을 안기는 것이었다. 와일드브는 그것들을 묶은 끈을 잡아 뜯어내고는 제법 빠르게 길을 갔다. 집에 다다라 보니 그 끈은 불그스름한 색이었다. 그가 예상했던 바로 그것이었다.\n\n그의 약점이 특별히 육체적 공포에 가까운 부류는 아니었으나, 너무도 잘 아는 자에게서 받은 이런 종류의 기습은 와일드브의 마음을 어지럽혔다. 그러나 그의 행보는 그로 인해 달라지지 않았다. 하루 이틀 뒤에 그는 다시 골짜기를 따라 올더워스로 갔으니, 어떤 오솔길에도 들어서지 않는 조심성을 발휘했다. 누가 자신을 감시하고 있다는 것, 자신의 일탈적 취향을 가로막으려고 책략이 동원되고 있다는 느낌은 — 위험이 무서운 종류가 아닌 한 — 이토록 전적으로 감상적인 여정에 짜릿함을 더해주었다. 그는 베인과 요브라이트 부인이 한패라고 짐작했고, 그런 연합에 맞서 싸우는 데에는 어떤 정당성이 있다고 느꼈다.\n\n오늘 밤 황야는 완전히 인적이 끊긴 듯했고, 와일드브는 입에 시가를 문 채 잠시 유스테이시아의 정원 대문 너머를 들여다본 뒤, 감정의 밀수가 그의 천성에 안기는 매혹에 이끌려 창문 쪽으로 다가갔는데, 그 창문은 완전히 닫혀 있지 않았고 블라인드도 일부만 내려져 있었다. 그는 방 안을 들여다볼 수 있었고, 유스테이시아는 거기 혼자 앉아 있었다. 와일드브는 잠시 그녀를 바라보다가 히스 속으로 물러나 양치류를 가볍게 두드렸더니, 그러자 나방들이 놀라 날아올랐다. 한 마리를 붙잡은 그는 창문으로 돌아와 나방을 틈새에 대고 손을 폈다. 나방은 유스테이시아의 탁자 위 촛불을 향해 가더니 두세 번 그 둘레를 맴돌다가 불꽃 속으로 날아들었다.\n\n유스테이시아가 벌떡 일어섰다. 이것은 옛 시절 와일드브가 미스토버로 몰래 구애하러 오곤 하던 때의 잘 알려진 신호였다. 그녀는 와일드브가 밖에 있음을 즉시 알아챘으나, 어떻게 해야 할지 미처 생각하기도 전에 남편이 위층에서 내려왔다. 뜻하지 않은 사건들의 충돌에 유스테이시아의 얼굴이 진홍빛으로 타올랐으니, 너무도 자주 결여되어 있던 생기로 그 얼굴이 채워졌다.\n\n\"안색이 아주 좋구려, 여보.\" 요브라이트가 그것을 볼 수 있을 만큼 가까이 다가오며 말했다. \"늘 그렇다면 모습이 더 나빠지진 않을 텐데.\"\n\n\"더워서요.\" 유스테이시아가 말했다. \"잠깐 바람 좀 쐬고 와야겠어요.\"\n\n\"같이 갈까?\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요. 대문까지만 갈 거예요.\"\n\n그녀는 일어섰으나 방을 나설 새도 없이 현관문에 요란한 두드림이 시작되었다.\n\n\"제가 갈게요 — 제가 갈게요.\" 유스테이시아가 그녀답지 않게 빠른 어조로 말하고는 나방이 날아든 창문 쪽을 간절히 흘끗 보았으나, 거기엔 아무것도 나타나지 않았다.\n\n\"이 저녁 시간엔 자네가 안 나가는 게 낫겠어.\" 그가 말했다. 클림이 그녀보다 앞서 복도로 나섰고, 유스테이시아는 기다렸는데, 그 졸린 듯한 태도가 속의 열기와 동요를 가리고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 귀를 기울였고, 클림이 문을 열었다. 밖에서는 아무 말도 들리지 않았고, 이윽고 그가 문을 닫고 돌아오며 말했다. \"아무도 없는데. 대체 무슨 영문이었을까?\"\n\n그는 그날 저녁 내내 영문을 모른 채 남겨졌으니, 아무런 해명도 떠오르지 않았고, 유스테이시아는 아무 말도 하지 않았던바, 자신만이 아는 추가적인 사실이 그 일에 더 큰 수수께끼만 보탤 뿐이었다.\n\n한편 바깥에서는 작은 한 막의 연극이 펼쳐졌으니, 적어도 그날 저녁만큼은 유스테이시아가 자신의 입장을 위태롭게 할 모든 가능성에서 벗어나게 해준 것이었다. 와일드브가 나방 신호를 준비하고 있는 동안 또 다른 사람이 그의 뒤를 따라 대문까지 올라와 있었다. 손에 총을 든 이 남자는 창가에서 벌어지는 상대방의 작업을 잠시 지켜보다가 집 쪽으로 걸어가 문을 두드리고는 모퉁이를 돌아 산울타리를 넘어 사라졌다.\n\n\"빌어먹을 놈!\" 와일드브가 말했다. \"또 나를 감시하고 있었구나.\"\n\n이 요란한 두드림 때문에 자신의 신호가 헛것이 되자 와일드브는 물러나 대문 밖으로 나갔고, 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 빠져나가는 것 말고는 아무 생각 없이 오솔길을 따라 빠르게 걸어 내려갔다. 언덕을 반쯤 내려갔을 때 오솔길은 키 작은 호랑가시나무 한 무더기 가까이로 지나갔는데, 그것은 전체적으로 어두운 풍경 속에서 검은 눈동자 속의 동공처럼 서 있었다. 와일드브가 이 지점에 이르렀을 때 한 발의 총성이 그의 귀를 놀라게 했고, 다 타버린 산탄 몇 알이 그의 주위 나뭇잎들 사이로 떨어졌다.\n\n그 총이 발사된 원인이 바로 자기 자신이라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었으니, 그는 호랑가시나무 무더기 속으로 뛰어들어 지팡이로 미친 듯이 덤불을 후려쳤으나 거기엔 아무도 없었다. 이 공격은 지난번보다 더 심각한 일이었고, 와일드브가 평정을 되찾기까지는 얼마간 시간이 걸렸다. 새롭고 더없이 불쾌한 위협의 방식이 시작되었으며, 그 의도는 그에게 중대한 신체적 위해를 가하려는 것으로 보였다. 와일드브는 베인의 첫 시도를 일종의 거친 장난, 곧 레들맨이 더 나은 방법을 몰라 부린 짓쯤으로 여겼으나, 이제는 성가신 것과 위태로운 것을 가르는 경계선이 넘어선 것이었다.\n\n베인이 얼마나 철저히 진지해졌는지를 와일드브가 알았더라면 그는 더욱 경악했으리라. 레들맨은 클림의 집 밖에 있는 와일드브의 모습을 보고 거의 격분한 상태였으며, 그 젊은 여관 주인의 반항적인 충동을 겁주어 몰아내기 위해서라면 실제로 그를 쏘아 죽이는 것을 제외하고는 어떤 극단까지라도 갈 각오가 되어 있었다. 그런 거친 강압의 미심쩍은 정당성은 베인의 마음을 어지럽히지 않았다. 그런 경우에 그런 마음을 어지럽히는 일은 드물며, 때로는 그것이 유감스러운 일도 아니다. 스트래퍼드의 탄핵에서부터 버지니아의 무뢰배들을 다룬 린치 농부의 간단명료한 방식에 이르기까지, 법을 조롱하는 정의의 승리는 수없이 많았다.\n\n클림의 외딴 거처에서 반 마일쯤 아래에 한 작은 마을이 있었는데, 올더워스 교구의 치안을 지키는 두 치안관 중 하나가 거기 살았으니, 와일드브는 곧장 그 치안관의 오두막으로 갔다. 문을 열자마자 그가 거의 처음으로 본 것은 못에 걸려 있는 치안관의 곤봉이었는데, 마치 여기에 자기 목적을 이룰 수단이 있노라고 그를 안심시키려는 듯했다. 그러나 치안관의 아내에게 물어보니 치안관은 집에 없다는 것이었다. 와일드브는 기다리겠다고 말했다.\n\n시간이 째깍째깍 흘러갔으나 치안관은 오지 않았다. 와일드브는 격렬한 분노의 상태에서 가라앉아 자기 자신과 그 상황과 치안관의 아내와 모든 정황에 대한 안절부절못하는 불만으로 식어갔다. 그는 일어나 그 집을 떠났다. 무릇 그날 저녁의 경험은 잘못 향한 애정에 식히는, 아니 얼리는 효과를 미쳤다고 해도 좋을 것이었고, 와일드브는 유스테이시아의 우연한 눈길 한 번을 바라며 날 저문 뒤에 다시 올더워스로 어슬렁거릴 기분이 전혀 아니었다.\n\n여기까지 레들맨은 와일드브가 저녁에 쏘다니려는 성향을 억누르려는 자신의 투박한 책략에서 제법 성공을 거두었다. 그는 바로 이날 밤 유스테이시아와 그녀의 옛 연인 사이에 있을 수 있는 만남을 싹부터 잘라버렸다. 그러나 그는 자신의 행동이 가져올 경향이 와일드브의 움직임을 멈추기보다는 오히려 다른 데로 돌려놓는 것이리라고는 예상하지 못했다. 기니 금화를 걸고 한 노름이 그를 클림에게 반가운 손님으로 만들어주진 못했으나, 아내의 친척을 찾아가는 것은 자연스러운 일이었고, 그는 유스테이시아를 보기로 작정했다. 밤 열 시 같은 험상궂은 시각보다는 좀 덜 거북한 시간을 골라야 했다. \"저녁에 가는 게 안전하지 않으니,\" 그가 말했다. \"낮에 가야겠다.\"\n\n한편 베인은 황야를 떠나 요브라이트 부인을 찾아갔으니, 그가 집안의 기니 금화를 되찾는 쪽으로 얼마나 섭리적인 반격을 가했는지를 부인이 알게 된 이래로 둘은 우호적인 사이였다. 부인은 그가 늦은 시각에 찾아온 것을 의아해했으나 그를 만나는 데에는 이의가 없었다.\n\n그는 부인에게 클림의 고통과 그가 살아가는 형편에 대해 소상히 이야기했고, 이어 토머신을 언급하며 그 나날의 역력한 슬픔을 부드럽게 건드렸다. \"자, 마님, 제 말 믿으세요.\" 그가 말했다. \"두 사람 누구에게든 마님이 그들 집에 스스럼없이 드나드는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없습니다, 처음엔 좀 퇴짜를 맞는다 해도 말이죠.\"\n\n\"그 애도 내 아들도 결혼하면서 나를 거역했네. 그러니 나는 그들의 살림에 아무 관심이 없어. 그들의 곤경은 제 손으로 만든 거야.\" 요브라이트 부인은 매정하게 말하려 했으나, 아들의 형편에 관한 이야기는 그녀가 내색하고 싶은 것 이상으로 마음을 움직였다.\n\n\"마님이 찾아가시면 와일드브가 지금 마음먹은 것보다 더 똑바로 처신하게 될 거고, 저 아래 황야에서 불행이 생기는 걸 막을 수도 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소린가?\"\n\n\"오늘 밤 저기서 영 마음에 들지 않는 무언가를 봤습니다. 댁의 아드님 집과 와일드브 씨 집이 사오 마일이 아니라 백 마일쯤 떨어져 있으면 좋겠다는 생각이 듭니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그자가 토머신을 우롱하던 그때에 그와 클림의 아내 사이에 정말로 무슨 내통이 있었구나!\"\n\n\"지금은 내통이 없기를 바라야지요.\"\n\n\"그 바람도 아마 한낱 헛것이겠지. 아, 클림! 아, 토머신!\"\n\n\"아직 잘못된 일은 없습니다. 사실 제가 와일드브더러 제 일에나 신경 쓰라고 설득해두었어요.\"\n\n\"어떻게?\"\n\n\"아, 말로 한 게 아니라 — 침묵의 방식이라는 제 나름의 계책으로요.\"\n\n\"부디 성공하길 바라네.\"\n\n\"마님이 아드님을 찾아가 가까워지는 걸로 저를 도와주신다면 성공할 겁니다. 그러면 마님 눈으로 직접 살펴보실 기회도 생기고요.\"\n\n\"그래, 일이 이쯤 되었으니,\" 요브라이트 부인이 슬프게 말했다. \"자네에게 털어놓겠네, 레들맨, 나도 갈 생각을 하고 있었어. 화해할 수 있다면 훨씬 행복할 게야. 그 결혼은 돌이킬 수 없고, 내 목숨은 길지 않을지도 모르니, 평안히 죽고 싶구나. 그 애는 내 하나뿐인 아들이고, 아들이란 게 그런 재목으로 빚어지는 것이라면 다른 아들이 없는 게 안타깝지도 않아. 토머신으로 말하자면 애초에 그 애에게 큰 기대를 하지 않았으니, 그 애도 나를 실망시키지 않았지. 하지만 그 애는 오래전에 용서했고, 이제 그자도 용서하겠네. 가겠어.\"\n\n레들맨이 블룸스엔드에서 요브라이트 부인과 이야기를 나누던 바로 그 시각에, 올더워스에서는 같은 주제에 관한 또 하나의 대화가 나른하게 이어지고 있었다.\n\n온종일 클림은 마치 마음이 제 일로 가득 차서 바깥 것에는 신경 쓸 겨를이 없는 사람처럼 처신했고, 이제 그의 말은 무엇이 그의 생각을 차지하고 있었는지를 드러냈다. 그가 그 화제를 꺼낸 것은 바로 그 알 수 없는 두드림이 있은 직후였다. \"유스테이시아, 오늘 나가 있는 동안 생각해봤는데, 나와 어머니 사이의 이 끔찍한 균열을 메우기 위해 뭔가 해야겠어. 그게 나를 괴롭혀.\"\n\n\"어떻게 하실 작정인데요?\" 유스테이시아가 멍하니 말했으니, 와일드브가 최근에 면담을 노려 부린 책략이 일으킨 흥분을 떨쳐낼 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 제안을 하든 당신은 거기에 영 시들한 관심밖에 없어 보이는군.\" 클림이 제법 격해진 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"오해하시는 거예요.\" 그녀가 그의 책망에 정신을 차리며 대답했다. \"그냥 생각에 잠겨 있었을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"무슨 생각?\"\n\n\"한편으론 저 나방 생각요, 골격이 촛불 심지에서 타들어가고 있는 저 나방요.\" 그녀가 느릿느릿 말했다. \"하지만 당신이 하는 말엔 늘 관심이 있다는 거 아시잖아요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 여보. 그럼 내가 가서 어머니를 찾아뵈어야겠어.\" …그가 다정한 심정으로 말을 이었다. \"그건 내가 자존심 때문에 못 할 일은 전혀 아니고, 다만 어머니를 자극할까 두려운 마음에 이렇게 오래 멀리해온 것뿐이야. 하지만 뭔가는 해야겠어. 이런 식의 일을 계속 내버려두는 건 내 잘못이지.\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에 자책하시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"어머니는 늙어가고, 그 삶은 외로우며, 나는 어머니의 하나뿐인 아들이잖아.\"\n\n\"토머신이 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"토머신은 어머니의 딸이 아니야. 설령 딸이라 해도 그것이 나를 면죄해주진 못하지. 하지만 그건 요점에서 벗어난 얘기야. 나는 어머니를 찾아뵙기로 마음을 굳혔고, 당신에게 부탁하고 싶은 건 오직 하나, 나를 도우려고 최선을 다해주겠느냐는 거야 — 그러니까 과거를 잊고, 만약 어머니가 화해할 뜻을 보이거든 우리 집에 어머니를 맞아들이든 어머니 집의 환대를 받아들이든 해서 중간에서 손을 마주잡아주겠어?\"\n\n처음에 유스테이시아는 마치 그가 제안한 일보다는 차라리 이 세상 무슨 일이라도 하겠다는 듯이 입술을 꾹 다물었다. 그러나 입가의 선이 생각에 잠겨 누그러졌는데, 누그러질 수 있을 만큼까지는 아니었으나, 그녀는 말했다. \"당신 가는 길을 막지는 않겠어요. 하지만 그간 일어난 일을 생각하면 제가 가서 먼저 손을 내밀라는 건 지나친 요구예요.\"\n\n\"당신과 어머니 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지 분명하게 말해준 적이 한 번도 없잖아.\"\n\n\"그때도 그럴 수 없었고, 지금도 그럴 수 없어요. 때로는 단 오 분 동안에 한평생을 다 써도 떨쳐낼 수 없는 쓰라림이 뿌려지기도 하는데, 여기가 그런 경우일지도 모르죠.\" 그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. \"당신이 고향에 돌아오지만 않았더라면, 클림, 당신에게 얼마나 큰 복이었을까요!… 그 일이 운명을 바꿔놓았어요, 사람들의——\"\n\n\"세 사람의 운명을.\"\n\n'다섯 사람의.' 유스테이시아는 그렇게 생각했으나 그것은 속에 묻어두었다.\n\n황야를 가로지르는 여정\n\n팔월 삼십일일 목요일은, 아늑한 집들이 숨막히고 시원한 바람결이 진미가 되는, 점토질 정원에 갈라진 틈이 생겨나 겁 많은 아이들이 그것을 \"지진\"이라 부르는, 짐수레와 마차 바퀴에서 헐거워진 살이 발견되는, 쏘아대는 벌레들이 공중과 땅과 찾을 수 있는 물 한 방울 한 방울마다 들끓는 그런 날들 가운데 하나였다.\n\n요브라이트 부인의 정원에서는 잎이 큰 연약한 식물들이 아침 열 시면 시들었고, 대황은 열한 시면 아래로 휘었으며, 뻣뻣한 양배추마저도 정오면 축 늘어졌다.\n\n요브라이트 부인이 레들맨에게 한 말에 따라 아들과 유스테이시아와 화해해보려고 최선을 다하고자 황야를 가로질러 아들의 집을 향해 출발한 것은 이날 열한 시쯤이었다. 부인은 하루의 더위가 가장 심해지기 전에 걸음을 상당히 진척시키기를 바랐으나, 막상 나서고 보니 그렇게 될 수 없음을 알았다. 해는 황야 전체에 자신의 낙인을 찍어놓았으니, 보랏빛 히스 꽃마저도 앞서 며칠간의 메마른 불볕 아래 갈색을 띠고 있었다. 골짜기마다 가마 속 같은 공기가 들어차 있었고, 여름철 오솔길을 이루던 겨울 물길의 깨끗한 석영 모래는 가뭄이 시작된 이래 일종의 소성燒成을 겪은 터였다.\n\n서늘하고 상쾌한 날씨였다면 요브라이트 부인은 올더워스까지 걷는 데 아무런 불편이 없었으련만, 지금의 이 작열하는 공격은 중년을 넘긴 여인에게 그 여정을 힘겨운 과업으로 만들었으니, 삼 마일째가 끝날 무렵 그녀는 적어도 거리의 일부라도 페어웨이를 시켜 마차로 데려다 달라고 할 것을 그랬다고 후회했다. 그러나 그녀가 다다른 지점에서는 다시 집으로 돌아가는 것이나 클림의 집에 이르는 것이나 매한가지로 수월했다. 그래서 그녀는 계속 나아갔으니, 주위의 공기는 소리 없이 고동치며 땅을 나른함으로 짓누르고 있었다. 그녀는 머리 위 하늘을 올려다보았고, 봄과 초여름이면 천정天頂을 물들이던 사파이어빛 색조가 금속성 보랏빛으로 대체되어 있음을 보았다.\n\n이따금 그녀는 하루살이들의 독립된 세계가 미친 듯한 잔치로 시간을 보내고 있는 곳에 다다랐으니, 어떤 것들은 공중에서, 어떤 것들은 뜨거운 땅과 초목 위에서, 또 어떤 것들은 거의 말라붙은 웅덩이의 미적지근하고 끈적한 물속에서 그러했다. 더 얕은 못들은 모두 김 서린 진흙으로 졸아들어 있었고, 그 속에서는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 이름 모를 생물들의 구더기 같은 형체들이 즐거이 들썩이며 뒹구는 것이 어렴풋이 보였다. 사색하기를 싫어하지 않는 여인이었던지라 그녀는 이따금 우산 아래 앉아 쉬면서 그들의 행복을 지켜보았으니, 방문의 결과에 대한 어떤 희망이 그녀의 마음에 편안함을 주어, 중대한 생각들 사이에서 마음이 눈에 들어오는 자질구레한 일이라면 무엇에든 자유로이 머물 수 있게 한 것이었다.\n\n요브라이트 부인은 전에 아들의 집에 가본 적이 한 번도 없었던지라 그 정확한 위치를 알지 못했다. 그녀는 오르막길을 이리저리 시험해보았으나 그것들이 자신을 엉뚱한 데로 이끈다는 것을 알았다. 발걸음을 되짚어 그녀는 다시 탁 트인 평지에 이르렀는데, 거기서 멀찍이 한 남자가 일하고 있는 것을 알아보았다. 그녀는 그에게로 가서 길을 물었다.\n\n그 일꾼은 방향을 가리켜주고는 덧붙였다. \"마님, 저기 저 오솔길을 올라가는 가시금작화 베는 사람이 보이십니까?\"\n\n요브라이트 부인은 눈을 잔뜩 부릅뜨고 보다가 마침내 그가 보인다고 말했다.\n\n\"그럼, 그 사람을 따라가시면 틀림없을 겁니다. 같은 데로 가는 길이거든요, 마님.\"\n\n그녀는 가리켜진 그 형체를 따라갔다. 그는 적갈색을 띠어, 그 둘레의 풍경과 구별되는 정도가 잎을 갉아먹는 초록 애벌레가 그 잎에서 구별되는 정도보다 더하지 않았다. 실제로 걸을 때 그의 속도는 요브라이트 부인보다 빨랐으나, 그가 가시덤불 수풀에 이를 때마다 멈춰 서서 한동안 머무는 버릇 덕분에 그녀는 그와 일정한 거리를 유지할 수 있었다. 그런 곳에 차례로 다다를 때마다 그녀는 그가 멈춘 동안 덤불에서 베어내 오솔길 옆에 곧게 펴놓은 길고 늘어진 가시덩굴 대여섯 가닥을 발견했다. 그것들은 그가 돌아오는 길에 거두어가려는 가시금작화 단 묶음 끈으로 쓸 작정인 것이 분명했다.\n\n이렇게 일에 몰두한 그 말 없는 존재는 한 마리 곤충보다 더 대단할 것이 없어 보였다. 그는 황야의 한낱 기생충처럼 보였으니, 나방이 옷가지를 갉듯 날마다의 노동으로 그 표면을 갉아대며, 그 산물에 온통 정신이 팔려, 양치류와 가시금작화와 히스와 지의류와 이끼 말고는 이 세상 어떤 것도 알지 못했다.\n\n가시금작화 베는 이는 제 여정의 일에 너무도 몰두해 한 번도 고개를 돌리지 않았고, 가죽 각반을 차고 토시를 낀 그의 형체는 마침내 그녀에게는 길을 일러주는 움직이는 이정표 외에 아무것도 아닌 것이 되었다. 그러다 문득 그녀는 그의 걸음걸이에서 어떤 특이한 점을 알아채고는 그 사람됨에 마음이 끌렸다. 그것은 어디선가 전에 본 적 있는 걸음걸이였으니, 그 걸음걸이가 마치 먼 들판에서의 아히마아스의 걸음걸이가 그를 임금의 파수꾼에게 알려주었듯 그 사람을 그녀에게 알려주었다. \"저 걸음걸이는 내 남편이 걷던 것과 똑같구나.\" 그녀가 말했고, 그러자 저 가시금작화 베는 이가 제 아들이라는 생각이 그녀를 덮쳐왔다.\n\n그녀는 이 기이한 현실에 좀처럼 자신을 익숙하게 만들 수 없었다. 클림이 가시금작화를 베는 습관이 있다는 말은 들었으나, 그녀는 그가 그 노동에 매달리는 것이 어쩌다 한 번씩, 유익한 소일거리 삼아 하는 것이려니 짐작했었다. 그런데 이제 그녀는 그가 가시금작화 베는 이, 그 이상도 이하도 아닌 모습으로 — 그 일의 정해진 복장을 걸치고, 그 몸짓으로 미루어 보건대 정해진 생각을 하면서 있는 것을 보고 있었다. 그와 유스테이시아를 이런 삶의 방식에서 당장 구해낼 열두 가지 황급한 방책을 궁리하며 그녀는 가슴을 두근거리며 길을 따라갔고, 그가 제 집 문으로 들어가는 것을 보았다.\n\n클림의 집 한쪽에는 작은 둔덕이 있었고, 그 둔덕 꼭대기에는 전나무 한 무더기가 하늘 높이 솟구쳐 있어서 멀리서 보면 그 잎이 언덕 마루 위 공중의 검은 점처럼 보였다. 이곳에 다다랐을 때 요브라이트 부인은 괴로울 만큼 동요하고 지치고 몸이 좋지 않음을 느꼈다. 그녀는 둔덕을 올라가 그 그늘 아래 앉아 기운을 차리고, 유스테이시아에게 어떻게 말문을 떼는 것이 가장 좋을지를 — 겉으로 드러난 나태함 아래 자기 못지않게, 아니 더 강하고 활발한 격정이 도사리고 있는 그 여인을 자극하지 않도록 — 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n그녀가 그 아래 앉은 나무들은 유난히 두들겨 맞고 거칠고 사나운 모습이었으니, 잠시 요브라이트 부인은 폭풍에 꺾이고 기진한 제 자신의 상태에 대한 생각을 떨치고 그것들의 상태를 응시했다. 그 무리를 이루는 아홉 그루 나무에서 그 어느 가지 하나도 사나운 날씨에 쪼개지고 잘리고 뒤틀리지 않은 것이 없었으니, 그 날씨는 기승을 부릴 때마다 거기서 그것들을 제멋대로 다루었다. 어떤 것들은 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 시들고 갈라졌으며, 불에 그은 듯한 검은 자국이 옆구리에 나 있었고, 그 발치 땅바닥에는 죽은 솔잎과 지난 세월의 강풍에 떨어진 솔방울 더미가 흩어져 있었다. 그곳은 악마의 풀무라 불렸는데, 그 이름의 강력한 까닭을 알아내려면 삼월이나 십일월의 어느 밤에 거기 와보기만 하면 되었다. 눈에 띄는 바람이라곤 불지 않는 이 무더운 오후에도 나무들은 끊임없는 신음을 이어갔으니, 그것이 공기로 인한 것이라고는 좀처럼 믿기 어려웠다.\n\n그녀는 문으로 내려갈 결심을 그러모을 수 있기까지 여기서 이십 분 남짓 앉아 있었으니, 육체적 나른함에 그 용기가 영점까지 떨어진 것이었다. 어머니가 아닌 다른 어떤 사람에게는, 두 여인 중 손위인 자신이 먼저 손을 내미는 것이 다소 굴욕적으로 여겨졌을지도 모른다. 그러나 요브라이트 부인은 그 모든 것을 충분히 헤아린 터였고, 그녀는 다만 자신의 방문이 유스테이시아에게 비굴한 것이 아니라 현명한 것으로 비치게 할 가장 좋은 방법만을 생각했다.\n\n그 높은 자리에서 지친 여인은 아래쪽 집의 지붕과 정원, 그리고 그 작은 거처의 울안 전체를 살펴볼 수 있었다. 그런데 막 일어서려는 순간, 그녀는 또 한 남자가 대문으로 다가오는 것을 보았다. 그의 태도는 묘하고 머뭇거리는 것이었으며, 볼일이 있어서나 초대를 받아 온 사람의 태도가 아니었다. 그는 흥미로운 듯 집을 둘러보고는 빙 돌아가 정원의 바깥 경계를 살폈는데, 마치 그곳이 셰익스피어의 출생지나 메리 스튜어트의 감옥이나 우구몽의 성관城館이라도 되는 양 했다. 빙 돌고 나서 다시 대문에 이른 그는 안으로 들어갔다. 요브라이트 부인은 이에 언짢았으니, 아들과 며느리만 있으리라고 셈해두었기 때문이다. 그러나 한순간 생각해보니, 아는 사람이 있으면 자신이 익숙해질 때까지 대화가 일반적인 사안에 한정됨으로써 그 집에 처음 나타나는 어색함이 가시리라는 것을 알았다. 그녀는 언덕을 내려가 대문에 이르렀고, 뜨거운 정원 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n거기 오솔길의 맨 자갈 위에 고양이가 잠들어 있었으니, 마치 잠자리며 깔개며 양탄자가 견딜 수 없다는 듯했다. 접시꽃 잎은 반쯤 접힌 우산처럼 늘어졌고, 줄기 속에서는 수액이 거의 끓어오를 지경이었으며, 매끄러운 표면의 잎사귀는 금속 거울처럼 번쩍였다. 대문 바로 안쪽에는 래더라이프라 불리는 종류의 작은 사과나무 한 그루가 자라고 있었는데, 흙이 가벼운 덕분에 그 정원에서 잘 자란 유일한 나무였다. 그 아래 땅에 떨어진 사과들 사이로는 과즙에 취해 뒹구는 말벌들이 있었고, 그 단맛에 마취되기 전에 열매마다 파먹어놓은 작은 굴 속을 기어 다니는 것들도 있었다. 문 옆에는 클림의 가시금작화 낫과, 그가 거두는 것을 그녀가 본 마지막 한 줌의 단 묶음 끈이 놓여 있었으니, 그가 집에 들어가면서 거기에 던져놓은 것이 분명했다.\n\n우연한 마주침, 그리고 그것이 보행자에게 미친 결과\n\n앞서 말했듯이 와일드브는, 레들맨이 자신이 밤에 그녀에게 가는 길을 염탐해내어 망쳐놓았으므로, 대놓고 낮에, 친척이라는 편한 명분으로 유스테이시아를 찾아가기로 작정한 터였다. 달빛 춤판에서 그녀가 그에게 걸어놓은 마법은, 내면에 강한 청교도적 힘을 갖추지 못한 남자로서는 도저히 아주 멀리할 수 없게 만들었다. 그는 그저 그녀와 그녀의 남편을 평범하게 만나 잠시 담소를 나누고 다시 떠나는 것만을 계산했다. 겉으로 드러나는 모든 표시는 관습에 맞을 것이로되, 그를 만족시킬 단 하나의 큰 사실이 거기 있을 터였으니 — 그는 그녀를 보게 되리라는 것이었다. 그는 클림이 없기를 바라지조차 않았으니, 그녀의 마음이 그에게 어떠하든 간에 아내로서의 위엄을 위태롭게 할 수 있는 어떤 상황이라도 유스테이시아가 못마땅해할 가능성이 충분히 있었기 때문이다. 여자들은 흔히 그러했다.\n\n그리하여 그는 갔고, 그가 도착한 시각이 요브라이트 부인이 집 근처 언덕에서 멈춰 있던 시각과 우연히 겹쳤다. 그가 부인이 본 그런 식으로 집 안팎을 둘러본 뒤 그는 가서 문을 두드렸다. 몇 분의 간격이 있은 다음 자물쇠에서 열쇠가 돌아가고 문이 열렸으며, 유스테이시아가 직접 그를 마주했다.\n\n지금 그녀의 태도만 보아서는, 만약 누군가 그 표면 아래를 꿰뚫어보고 그 고요한 물줄기의 진짜 깊이를 가늠할 수 있는 사람이 아니고서야, 여기 서 있는 이가 지난주에 그와 더불어 그 격정적인 춤을 추었던 바로 그 여인이라고는 도저히 상상할 수 없었으리라.\n\n\"무사히 집에 도착하셨기를 바랍니다.\" 와일드브가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그럼요.\" 그녀가 무심하게 대답했다.\n\n\"그리고 이튿날 피곤하지 않으셨나요? 그러셨을까 봐 걱정했습니다.\"\n\n\"좀 피곤하긴 했어요. 목소리를 낮추실 필요 없어요 — 우리 말을 엿들을 사람이 아무도 없으니까요. 제 어린 하녀는 마을에 심부름 갔거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼 클림은 집에 없나요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 있어요.\"\n\n\"아! 혹시 혼자 계셔서 떠돌이들이 무서워 문을 잠그신 줄 알았습니다.\"\n\n\"아니에요 — 여기 제 남편이 있어요.\"\n\n그들은 현관에 서 있던 참이었다. 그녀는 현관문을 닫고 아까처럼 열쇠를 돌린 뒤, 이웃한 방의 문을 열어젖히고는 그에게 들어오라고 청했다. 와일드브가 들어서니 방은 비어 있는 듯했다. 그러나 몇 걸음 나아가자마자 그는 흠칫했다. 난로 앞 깔개 위에 클림이 잠들어 있었던 것이다. 그 곁에는 그가 일할 때 차는 각반과 두꺼운 장화, 가죽 장갑, 소매 달린 조끼가 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"들어오셔도 돼요. 그이를 깨우진 않을 거예요.\" 그녀가 뒤따라오며 말했다. \"제가 문을 잠근 까닭은, 제가 정원에 있거나 위층에 있을 때 그이가 여기 누워 있는 동안 어쩌다 들른 사람에게 방해받지 않게 하려는 거예요.\"\n\n\"왜 저기서 자고 있죠?\" 와일드브가 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"몹시 지쳤거든요. 오늘 아침 네 시 반에 나가서 그때부터 줄곧 일했어요. 가시금작화를 베는 건 그 가엾은 눈에 아무 부담도 주지 않으면서 할 수 있는 유일한 일이라서요.\" 잠든 이의 모습과 이 순간 와일드브의 모습 사이의 대조가 유스테이시아에게는 아프도록 또렷했으니, 와일드브는 새 여름 양복에 가벼운 모자를 쓰고 우아하게 차려입고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"아! 처음 만났을 때 그이가 얼마나 다른 모습이었는지 당신은 모를 거예요, 그리 오래전 일도 아닌데 말이죠. 그이 손은 제 손처럼 희고 부드러웠어요. 그런데 지금 저것 좀 보세요, 얼마나 거칠고 갈색이 되었는지! 그이 안색은 본래 희멀건데, 지금 저 가죽옷과 한가지 색으로 녹슨 듯한 저 빛깔은 햇볕에 타서 생긴 거예요.\"\n\n\"대체 왜 나가는 겁니까!\" 와일드브가 속삭였다.\n\n\"빈둥거리는 걸 질색하니까요. 벌어들이는 게 우리 살림에 보태지는 것도 별로 없지만요. 그래도 그이 말로는, 가진 밑천을 까먹고 사는 사람들은 벌 수 있는 데서 한 푼이라도 벌어 당장의 지출을 줄여야 한다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"운명의 여신들이 당신에게 친절하지 못했군요, 유스테이시아 요브라이트.\"\n\n\"그들에게 고마워할 게 아무것도 없어요.\"\n\n\"그이도 마찬가지죠 — 그들이 그이에게 준 단 하나의 큰 선물만 빼면요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데요?\"\n\n와일드브가 그녀의 눈을 들여다보았다.\n\n유스테이시아가 그날 처음으로 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"글쎄요, 저야 미심쩍은 선물이죠.\" 그녀가 조용히 말했다. \"저는 만족이라는 선물을 말씀하시는 줄 알았어요 — 그이는 가졌고 저는 갖지 못한 그것 말이에요.\"\n\n\"그런 경우라면 만족도 이해할 수 있죠 — 다만 어떻게 저 겉으로 드러난 처지가 그이를 끌어당기는지는 저로선 알 수 없는 노릇이지만요.\"\n\n\"그건 당신이 그이를 몰라서 그래요. 그이는 관념에 열광하고 바깥 것에는 무심하거든요. 종종 저는 그이를 보면 사도 바울이 떠올라요.\"\n\n\"그이가 그토록 위대한 인품을 지녔다니 반가운 말이로군요.\"\n\n\"그래요. 하지만 곤란한 건, 바울이 성경 속 인물로는 더할 나위 없었을지언정 실제 삶에서는 어림도 없었으리라는 거예요.\"\n\n그들의 목소리는 본능적으로 한층 낮아졌는데, 처음에는 클림을 깨우지 않으려고 딱히 조심하지도 않았던 터였다. \"그게, 당신의 결혼이 당신에게 불행이라는 뜻이라면, 누구 탓인지는 당신도 알겠지요.\" 와일드브가 말했다.\n\n\"결혼 자체는 불행이 아니에요.\" 그녀가 약간 발끈하며 받아쳤다. \"그저 그 뒤에 일어난 우연이 제 파멸의 원인이 된 거죠. 세속적인 의미에서 무화과 대신 엉겅퀴를 얻은 건 분명하지만, 세월이 무엇을 가져올지 제가 어떻게 알 수 있었겠어요?\"\n\n\"가끔은, 유스테이시아, 그게 당신에게 내린 심판이라는 생각이 들어요. 당신은 응당 내 사람이었잖아요, 알다시피. 그리고 나는 당신을 잃을 생각이 전혀 없었고요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그건 제 잘못이 아니었어요! 두 사람이 당신 사람일 수는 없었고, 게다가 기억하세요, 제가 미처 알기도 전에 당신이 먼저 다른 여자에게로 돌아섰다는 걸요. 그렇게 한 건 당신의 잔인한 변덕이었어요. 당신이 당신 쪽에서 그걸 시작하기 전에는 저도 제 쪽에서 그런 장난을 칠 생각은 꿈에도 없었어요.\"\n\n\"별 뜻 없이 한 거였어요.\" 와일드브가 대답했다. \"그저 막간극이었을 뿐이죠. 남자들이란 변치 않는 사랑 한가운데서 누군가에게 잠깐 마음이 끌리는 버릇이 있는데, 그 사랑은 나중에 다시 예전 그대로 고개를 드는 거예요. 당신이 내게 반항하는 태도를 보이니 나는 그러지 말았어야 할 정도로 더 나아가고 싶은 유혹을 받았고, 당신이 여전히 그 애타게 하는 역할을 계속하니 나는 더더욱 나아가서 그녀와 결혼한 거예요.\" 그는 몸을 돌려 의식 없는 클림의 형체를 다시 바라보며 중얼거렸다. \"당신은 자기가 얻은 보물의 가치를 모르는 것 같소, 클림.… 적어도 한 가지 점에서는 그가 나보다 행복할 거요. 그는 세상에서 영락하는 것이 어떤 것인지, 큰 개인적 재앙에 시달리는 것이 어떤 것인지는 알지 모르나, 사랑하던 여자를 잃는 것이 어떤 것인지는 아마 모를 테니.\"\n\n\"그이는 그녀를 얻은 것에 고마움을 모르는 사람이 아니에요.\" 유스테이시아가 속삭였다. \"그 점에서 그이는 좋은 사람이죠. 많은 여자들이 그런 남편을 위해서라면 멀리까지 갈 거예요. 하지만 제가 이른바 생⏤음악과 시와 열정과 전쟁, 그리고 이 세상의 큰 동맥 속을 흐르며 고동치고 약동하는 그 모든 것⏤을 바란다고 해서 분에 넘치게 너무 많은 걸 바라는 걸까요? 그게 제 젊은 날 꿈의 모습이었는데, 저는 그걸 얻지 못했어요. 그래도 저는 제 클림에게서 거기로 가는 길을 본 것 같았어요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그것 때문에만 그이와 결혼한 거요?\"\n\n\"거기서 당신은 저를 오해하고 있어요. 저는 그이를 사랑해서 결혼했어요. 다만 그이에게서 그런 삶의 약속을 본 것 같았기 때문에 부분적으로 그이를 사랑한 게 아니라고는 말하지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"당신, 옛날의 그 구슬픈 가락으로 빠져들었군요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 가라앉지 않을 거예요.\" 그녀가 짐짓 외쳤다. \"저는 그 춤판에 가는 걸로 새로운 방식을 시작했고, 그걸 고수할 작정이에요. 클림도 즐겁게 노래할 수 있는데, 저라고 왜 못 하겠어요?\"\n\n와일드브가 생각에 잠긴 듯 그녀를 바라보았다. \"노래하겠다고 말하기는 노래하기보다 쉽죠. 그래도 할 수만 있다면 저는 당신의 시도를 북돋아 드리고 싶어요. 하지만 이제는 불가능해진 단 한 가지가 없으면 제게 삶이란 아무 의미도 없으니, 당신을 북돋아 드릴 수 없는 저를 용서해주세요.\"\n\n\"데이먼, 무슨 일이 있길래 그렇게 말씀하시는 거예요?\" 그녀가 그늘진 깊은 눈을 들어 그를 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"그건 제가 결코 대놓고 말하지 않을 거예요. 어쩌면 수수께끼로 말해보려 해도 당신은 그걸 풀어보려 하지 않으실 테죠.\"\n\n유스테이시아는 잠시 잠자코 있다가 말했다. \"오늘 우리는 묘한 관계에 있네요. 당신은 유난스레 까다롭게 말을 에두르고 계세요. 데이먼, 당신은 여전히 저를 사랑한다는 뜻이군요. 글쎄요, 그건 제게 슬픔을 안겨주는데, 저는 그 말을 빌미로 응당 그래야 하듯 당신을 매몰차게 내칠 만큼 결혼으로 그렇게 온전히 행복하지가 못하니까요. 하지만 우리는 이 얘기를 너무 많이 했어요. 제 남편이 깰 때까지 기다리실 작정이세요?\"\n\n\"그이와 이야기할 생각이었는데, 그럴 필요는 없겠어요, 유스테이시아. 당신을 잊지 못하는 것으로 제가 당신을 언짢게 했다면, 그걸 입에 올리는 건 당신이 옳아요. 하지만 매몰차게 내친다는 말은 하지 마세요.\"\n\n그녀는 대답하지 않았고, 두 사람은 클림이 그 깊은 잠 속에서 계속 자고 있는 것을 생각에 잠긴 듯 바라보며 서 있었으니, 그것은 어떤 신경질적인 두려움도 일으키지 않는 환경에서 행해진 육체노동의 결과인 그런 잠이었다.\n\n\"하느님, 저 달콤한 잠이 얼마나 부러운지!\" 와일드브가 말했다. \"나는 소년이던 시절 이후로 저렇게 자본 적이 없어요⏤아주 오래전, 까마득한 옛날 이후로요.\"\n\n그들이 이렇게 그를 지켜보고 있을 때 대문에서 찰칵하는 소리가 들리더니 문에 두드림이 왔다. 유스테이시아가 창가로 가서 밖을 내다보았다.\n\n그녀의 안색이 변했다. 처음에는 진홍빛이 되었다가, 이윽고 그 붉은 기가 가라앉으며 입술에서마저 부분적으로 빠져나갔다.\n\n\"제가 가는 게 좋겠어요?\" 와일드브가 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"잘 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"누군데요?\"\n\n\"요브라이트 부인이에요. 아, 그날 그분이 제게 한 말이라니! 이 방문을 도무지 이해할 수 없네요⏤대체 무슨 속셈일까요? 게다가 그분은 우리의 그 지난 시절을 의심하고 있어요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신 손에 달렸어요. 그분이 저를 여기서 보지 않는 게 낫겠다 싶으면 옆방으로 들어가지요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 네⏤들어가세요.\"\n\n와일드브는 즉시 물러갔다. 그러나 그가 옆방에 들어간 지 반 분도 채 되지 않아 유스테이시아가 그를 뒤따라왔다.\n\n\"아니에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이런 짓은 하지 않겠어요. 그분이 들어오면 당신을 봐야 할 거고⏤그러고 싶다면 뭔가 잘못됐다고 생각하라죠! 하지만 그분이 저를 싫어하는데⏤보고 싶은 건 제가 아니라 아들인데, 제가 어떻게 그분에게 문을 열어드리겠어요? 문을 열지 않겠어요!\"\n\n요브라이트 부인이 더 크게 다시 두드렸다.\n\n\"저 두드림이 십중팔구 그이를 깨울 거예요.\" 유스테이시아가 말을 이었다. \"그러면 그이가 직접 어머니를 들이겠죠. 아⏤들어보세요.\"\n\n그들은 클림이 두드림에 잠을 설친 듯 다른 방에서 뒤척이는 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 그가 \"어머니\"라는 말을 내뱉었다.\n\n\"네⏤그이가 깼어요⏤문으로 갈 거예요.\" 그녀가 안도의 숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"이쪽으로 오세요. 그분에게 저는 평판이 나쁘니, 당신이 들켜서는 안 돼요. 이렇게 저는 몰래 행동해야만 하는데, 제가 못된 짓을 해서가 아니라 남들이 그렇다고 말하기를 즐기기 때문이에요.\"\n\n이때쯤 그녀는 그를 뒷문으로 데려갔는데, 그 문은 열려 있어 정원으로 내려가는 오솔길이 드러나 있었다. \"자, 한마디만 해요, 데이먼.\" 그가 밖으로 나서자 그녀가 말했다. \"이게 당신의 이곳 첫 방문이니, 마지막 방문이 되게 하세요. 우리도 한때는 뜨거운 연인이었지만, 이제는 그래선 안 돼요. 잘 가요.\"\n\n\"잘 있어요.\" 와일드브가 말했다. \"여기 온 목적은 다 이뤘으니, 저는 만족합니다.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐였는데요?\"\n\n\"당신을 한 번 보는 것. 제 영원한 명예를 걸고, 그 이상을 바라고 온 게 아니에요.\"\n\n와일드브는 자기가 말을 건넨 그 아름다운 여인에게 손에 입맞춤을 보내고는 정원으로 들어섰고, 그녀는 그가 오솔길을 내려가 그 끝의 디딤단을 넘어 바깥의 양치류 속으로 들어가는 것을 지켜보았는데, 그 양치류는 그가 걸어가는 동안 그의 허리께를 스치다가 마침내 그가 그 덤불 속으로 사라져 보이지 않게 되었다. 그가 완전히 가버리자 그녀는 천천히 몸을 돌려 집 안쪽으로 주의를 돌렸다.\n\n그러나 클림과 그 어머니가 처음 만나는 이 순간에 자신이 거기 있는 것을 두 사람이 바라지 않을 수도, 아니면 그것이 군더더기일 수도 있었다. 어쨌든 그녀는 요브라이트 부인을 만나는 데 서두를 마음이 전혀 없었다. 그녀는 클림이 자기를 찾으러 올 때까지 기다리기로 작정하고 정원으로 슬그머니 돌아갔다. 여기서 그녀는 몇 분간 하릴없이 시간을 보냈는데, 자신에게 아무 기별이 없는 것을 알고는 집을 가로질러 앞쪽으로 발걸음을 되짚어가서 거실의 목소리에 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 아무 소리도 들리지 않자 그녀는 문을 열고 들어갔다. 놀랍게도 클림은 와일드브와 그녀가 두고 떠났던 바로 그 모습 그대로 누워 있었으니, 그 잠은 깨지지 않은 듯했다. 그는 두드림에 잠을 설치고 꿈을 꾸며 중얼거리긴 했으나 잠에서 깨지는 않았던 것이다. 유스테이시아는 서둘러 문으로 갔고, 자신을 그토록 모질게 말한 여인에게 문을 열어주기가 꺼려졌음에도 빗장을 풀고 밖을 내다보았다. 아무도 보이지 않았다. 거기 흙털개 옆에는 클림의 낫과 그가 집으로 가져온 한 줌의 단 묶음 끈이 놓여 있었고, 그녀 앞에는 텅 빈 오솔길과 살짝 열린 채 서 있는 정원 대문이 있었으며, 그 너머로는 보랏빛 히스의 큰 골짜기가 햇볕 속에서 소리 없이 전율하고 있었다. 요브라이트 부인은 가버린 것이었다.\n\n클림의 어머니는 이때 언덕의 한 자락에 가려 유스테이시아에게는 보이지 않는 오솔길을 따라가고 있었다. 정원 대문에서부터 거기까지의 그녀의 걸음은 황급하고 단호했으니, 앞서 그 장면 안으로 들어가려 했던 것 못지않게 이제는 거기서 벗어나려고 애타는 여인의 걸음이었다. 그녀의 눈은 땅에 박혀 있었고, 그녀 안에는 두 가지 광경이 새겨져 있었으니 — 문 옆에 놓인 클림의 낫과 가시덩굴, 그리고 창가에 비친 한 여인의 얼굴이었다. 그녀의 입술은 떨렸고, 부자연스레 얇아지면서 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"너무하구나⏤클림, 그 애가 어떻게 저럴 수가! 집에 있으면서도 그 애가 그 여자더러 내게 문을 닫게 내버려두다니!\"\n\n집을 정면으로 보는 시야에서 벗어나려는 조바심에 그녀는 집으로 가는 가장 곧은 길에서 벗어났고, 그 길을 다시 찾으려고 둘러보다가 움푹한 곳에서 월귤을 따고 있는 어린 사내아이와 마주쳤다. 그 아이는 모닥불 때 유스테이시아의 불 때는 일꾼 노릇을 했던 조니 넌서치였는데, 작은 몸뚱이가 더 큰 것에 끌려가는 경향대로, 그는 요브라이트 부인이 나타나자마자 그녀 둘레를 맴돌기 시작하더니 제 행동을 뚜렷이 의식하지도 못한 채 그녀 곁에서 종종걸음을 쳤다.\n\n요브라이트 부인은 최면에 걸린 잠 속에 있는 사람처럼 그에게 말했다. \"집까지는 먼 길이란다, 얘야, 저녁이 되어야 거기 닿겠구나.\"\n\n\"저는 닿을 거예요.\" 그녀의 작은 동행이 말했다. \"저는 저녁 먹기 전에 구슬치기를 할 거고, 우리는 여섯 시에 저녁을 먹어요, 아버지가 집에 오시니까요. 아주머니 아버지도 여섯 시에 오세요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그이는 절대 안 오신단다. 내 아들도 안 오고, 아무도 안 와.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 시무룩하세요? 우서를 보셨어요?\"\n\n\"그보다 더한 걸 봤단다⏤창유리 너머로 나를 바라보는 한 여자의 얼굴을.\"\n\n\"그게 나쁜 광경이에요?\"\n\n\"그래. 지친 나그네를 바라보면서도 들여보내 주지 않는 여자를 보는 건 언제나 나쁜 광경이지.\"\n\n\"한번은 영원을 잡으러 스루프 큰 못에 갔을 때, 저 자신이 저를 올려다보는 걸 봤는데, 깜짝 놀라서 막 뒤로 펄쩍 뛰었어요.\"\n\n…\"그들이 내 손길에 중간에서라도 응할 기미만 보였더라면 얼마나 잘됐을까! 하지만 가망이 없구나. 문전박대라니! 그 여자가 그 애를 나에게 등 돌리게 한 게 분명해. 속에 마음 없는 아름다운 몸뚱이가 있을 수 있을까? 그럴 수 있을 것 같구나. 이렇게 불볕 같은 날에는 이웃집 고양이한테도 그런 짓은 안 했을 텐데!\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요?\"\n\n\"두 번 다시⏤절대로! 그들이 나를 부르러 보낸다 해도 안 가!\"\n\n\"그렇게 말씀하시는 걸 보니 아주 별난 아주머니인가 봐요.\"\n\n\"아, 아니야, 전혀 그렇지 않단다.\" 그녀가 아이의 재잘거림으로 돌아오며 말했다. \"자라서 자식을 둔 사람들은 대개 나처럼 말한단다. 너도 자라면 네 어머니가 나처럼 말할 거야.\"\n\n\"안 그러셨으면 좋겠어요, 헛소리하는 건 아주 나쁜 거니까요.\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야, 헛소리지, 아마. 너도 더위에 거의 다 지치지 않았니?\"\n\n\"네. 하지만 아주머니만큼은 아니에요.\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 아니?\"\n\n\"아주머니 얼굴이 하얗고 축축하고, 머리가 축 늘어진 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"아, 나는 속에서부터 기진했단다.\"\n\n\"왜 한 걸음 뗄 때마다 이렇게 하세요?\" 아이가 말하면서 제 몸짓에 병자의 절뚝거림과 휘청거림을 흉내 냈다.\n\n\"내가 감당할 수 없을 만큼 무거운 짐을 졌기 때문이란다.\"\n\n어린 사내아이는 잠자코 골똘히 생각에 잠겼고, 두 사람은 십오 분 남짓이 지나도록 나란히 비틀거리며 나아갔는데, 그때 기운이 눈에 띄게 더 빠진 요브라이트 부인이 그에게 말했다. \"여기 좀 앉아서 쉬어야겠다.\"\n\n그녀가 자리에 앉자 아이는 그녀의 얼굴을 오래 들여다보고는 말했다. \"숨을 참 우습게 쉬세요⏤다 죽게 될 때까지 몰아대는 새끼 양처럼요. 늘 그렇게 숨을 쉬세요?\"\n\n\"늘 그렇진 않단다.\" 그녀의 목소리는 이제 너무 낮아 속삭임보다 겨우 조금 높을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"거기서 주무실 거죠, 그렇죠? 벌써 눈을 감으셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"아니. 나는 잠을 많이 못 잘 거야, 다른 어느 날까지는⏤그러면 그때는 길고 긴, 아주 긴 잠을 자게 되길 바란단다. 그런데 너 림스무어 못이 올여름에 말랐는지 말해줄 수 있겠니?\"\n\n\"림스무어 못은 말랐는데, 오커스 풀은 안 말랐어요, 거긴 깊어서 절대 안 마르거든요⏤바로 저기예요.\"\n\n\"물은 맑니?\"\n\n\"네, 그럭저럭요⏤히스 뜯어먹는 망아지들이 들어가는 데만 빼면요.\"\n\n\"그럼 이걸 받아서 되도록 빨리 가서, 네가 찾을 수 있는 가장 맑은 물을 떠다 주렴. 나는 몹시 기운이 없구나.\"\n\n그녀는 손에 들고 있던 작은 버들고리 손가방에서 손잡이 없는 구식 도자기 찻잔을 꺼냈다. 그것은 손가방 안에 들어 있는 같은 종류의 잔 대여섯 개 중 하나로, 그녀가 어린 시절부터 줄곧 간직해온 것이었으며, 오늘 클림과 유스테이시아에게 줄 자그마한 선물로 가져온 것이었다.\n\n아이는 심부름을 떠났고, 곧 물을 떠서 돌아왔는데, 그저 그런 물이었다. 요브라이트 부인은 마셔보려 했으나 물이 너무 미지근해 구역질이 나서 그것을 쏟아버렸다. 그 뒤로도 그녀는 눈을 감은 채 계속 앉아 있었다.\n\n아이는 기다리며 그녀 곁에서 놀았고, 지천으로 널린 작은 갈색 나비를 몇 마리 잡고는 다시 기다리다가 말했다. \"저는 가만히 있는 것보다 계속 가는 게 더 좋아요. 곧 다시 출발하실 거예요?\"\n\n\"모르겠다.\"\n\n\"저 혼자 가도 됐으면 좋겠어요.\" 그가 무언가 달갑잖은 일에 끌려 들어갈까 봐 두려운 듯 말을 이었다. \"제가 더 필요하세요, 아주머니?\"\n\n요브라이트 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"엄마한테 뭐라고 말할까요?\" 아이가 계속 말했다.\n\n\"아들에게 버림받은 가슴 찢어진 여자를 봤다고 말하렴.\"\n\n그녀를 아주 떠나기 전에 아이는 그녀의 얼굴에 그리운 듯한 눈길을 던졌으니, 마치 그녀를 이렇게 버려두는 것이 인심 좋은 일인지 마음에 걸리는 듯했다. 그는 막연하고 의아한 태도로 그녀의 얼굴을 빤히 들여다보았는데, 마치 그 글자를 풀 열쇠를 도무지 찾을 수 없는 어떤 기이한 옛 사본을 살피는 사람의 태도 같았다. 그는 동정이 요구된다는 느낌이 전혀 없을 만큼 어리지는 않았고, 그때껏 난공불락으로 여겨온 어른들 영역에서 비참함을 목격할 때 어린 시절에 느끼는 그 공포에서 벗어날 만큼 나이가 들지도 않았다. 그리고 그녀가 곤경을 일으킬 처지인지 그것에 시달리는 처지인지, 그녀와 그녀의 고통이 가엾어해야 할 무엇인지 두려워해야 할 무엇인지를 가려내는 것은 그의 능력 밖이었다. 그는 눈을 내리깔고 더는 한마디도 없이 가던 길을 갔다. 반 마일도 채 가기 전에 그는 그녀가 쉬려고 주저앉은 한 여자였다는 것 말고는 그녀에 관한 모든 것을 잊어버렸다.\n\n요브라이트 부인의 육체적, 정서적 노고는 그녀를 거의 쓰러뜨릴 지경이었으나, 그녀는 짧게짧게 나아가며 그 사이사이 길게 쉬어가는 식으로 계속 기어가듯 갔다. 해는 이제 남쪽에서 서쪽으로 멀찍이 기울어 그녀의 얼굴을 정면으로 비추고 있었으니, 마치 손에 횃불을 든 어떤 무자비한 방화범이 그녀를 태워버리려고 기다리는 듯했다. 아이가 떠나자 풍경에서 눈에 보이는 모든 생기가 사라졌으나, 가시금작화 더미마다에서 띄엄띄엄 들려오는 수컷 메뚜기들의 쉰 듯한 울음소리만으로도, 더 큰 동물 종들이 기진해 쓰러진 가운데 눈에 보이지 않는 곤충의 세계가 생명의 충만함 속에서 분주하다는 것을 충분히 알 수 있었다.\n\n두 시간 만에 그녀는 올더워스에서 자기 집까지 전체 거리의 사분의 삼쯤 되는 한 비탈에 다다랐는데, 거기에는 자그마한 백리향 무더기가 오솔길로 비집고 들어와 있었으니, 그녀는 그것이 이룬 향기로운 깔개 위에 앉았다. 그녀 앞에는 개미 떼가 길을 가로질러 한길을 닦아놓고는 거기서 끝없이 무거운 짐을 진 무리를 이루어 고된 일을 하고 있었다. 그것들을 내려다보는 것은 탑 꼭대기에서 도시의 거리를 굽어보는 것과 같았다. 그녀는 이 개미들의 부산함이 바로 이 자리에서 여러 해 동안 이어져왔음을 떠올렸으니⏤필시 옛 시절의 그것들이 지금 거기 걸어 다니는 이것들의 조상이리라. 그녀는 더 온전히 쉬려고 몸을 뒤로 기댔는데, 하늘의 부드러운 동쪽 부분은 백리향이 그녀의 머리에 그러했던 것만큼이나 그녀의 눈에 큰 안식이었다. 그녀가 바라보고 있자니 그쪽 하늘에서 왜가리 한 마리가 솟아올라 해를 향해 얼굴을 두고 날아갔다. 그것은 골짜기 어느 못에서 흠뻑 젖은 채로 날아온 것이었는데, 날아가는 동안 그 날개의 가장자리와 안쪽, 그 넓적다리와 가슴이 밝은 햇살에 어찌나 사로잡혔는지, 마치 윤이 나는 은으로 빚어진 것처럼 보였다. 그가 떠 있는 저 높은 천정은, 그녀가 결박당해 있는 이 지상의 공에서 멀리 떨어진, 자유롭고 행복한 곳으로 보였다. 그녀는 자기도 짓눌리지 않고 그 표면에서 일어나 그가 지금 나는 것처럼 날아오를 수 있기를 바랐다.\n\n그러나 어머니인지라, 그녀가 곧 제 자신의 처지에 대한 생각을 멈추는 것은 불가피한 일이었다. 만약 그녀의 다음 생각의 자취가 유성의 길처럼 공중에 한 줄기 선으로 그어졌더라면, 그것은 왜가리와는 반대되는 방향을 가리키며 동쪽으로 내려가 클림의 집 지붕 위에 내려앉았으리라.\n\n두 옛 벗의 비극적인 만남\n\n그동안 그는 잠에서 깨어나 일어나 앉아 주위를 둘러보았다. 유스테이시아가 그의 바로 곁 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 손에 책을 들고 있었으나 한동안 그것을 들여다보지 않은 채였다.\n\n\"이것 참!\" 클림이 손으로 눈을 비비며 말했다. \"내가 정말 깊이 잤군! 게다가 어찌나 엄청난 꿈을 꾸었는지⏤결코 못 잊을 그런 꿈을 말이야.\"\n\n\"꿈을 꾸시는 것 같더라고요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그래. 어머니에 관한 꿈이었어. 꿈에서 내가 당신을 어머니 집에 데려가 불화를 풀려고 했는데, 막상 거기 닿으니 들어갈 수가 없는 거야, 어머니가 우리한테 도와달라고 계속 울부짖는데도 말이지. 하지만 꿈은 꿈일 뿐이지. 몇 시야, 유스테이시아?\"\n\n\"두 시 반이에요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 늦었어? 이렇게 오래 있을 생각은 아니었는데. 뭐 좀 먹고 나면 세 시가 넘겠구나.\"\n\n\"앤이 마을에서 아직 안 돌아와서, 그 애가 올 때까지 당신을 자게 두는 게 좋겠다 싶었어요.\"\n\n클림은 창가로 가서 밖을 내다보았다. 이윽고 그가 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다. \"주가 가고 또 주가 가는데도 어머니는 오시지 않는구나. 진작에 어머니한테서 무슨 기별이 있을 줄 알았는데.\"\n\n불안, 회한, 두려움, 결심이 유스테이시아의 검은 눈 속에서 잇따라 빠르게 표정으로 스쳐 지나갔다. 그녀는 끔찍한 난관과 정면으로 마주하고 있었고, 미루기로 거기서 벗어나기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"머잖아 블룸스엔드에 꼭 가봐야겠어.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 혼자 가는 게 낫겠다 싶어.\" 그는 각반과 장갑을 집어 들었다가 도로 내던지고는 덧붙였다. \"오늘은 저녁이 이렇게 늦어질 테니 황야로 다시 나가진 않고 저녁까지 정원에서 일하다가, 좀 더 선선해지면 블룸스엔드까지 걸어가야겠어. 내가 조금만 먼저 다가가면 어머니는 기꺼이 모든 걸 잊어주실 거라고 확신해. 집에 돌아오기까지는 좀 늦어지겠지, 어느 쪽으로든 한 시간 반 안에는 그 거리를 갈 수 없을 테니까. 하지만 당신, 하룻저녁쯤은 괜찮지, 여보? 그렇게 넋이 나간 표정을 짓는 걸 보니 무슨 생각을 하는 거야?\"\n\n\"말씀드릴 수 없어요.\" 그녀가 무겁게 말했다. \"우리가 여기 살지 않았으면 좋겠어요, 클림. 이곳에서는 세상이 온통 잘못된 것만 같아요.\"\n\n\"글쎄⏤우리가 그렇게 만든다면 그렇겠지. 토머신이 요즘 블룸스엔드에 다녀갔는지 모르겠네. 다녀갔으면 좋으련만. 하지만 아마 아닐 거야, 내가 알기로 그 애는 한 달쯤 뒤에 해산할 예정이니까. 진작 그 생각을 했어야 했는데. 가엾은 어머니는 정말 무척 외로우실 거야.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤 당신이 가는 게 싫어요.\"\n\n\"왜 오늘 밤은 안 되지?\"\n\n\"저를 끔찍하게 해칠 무슨 말이 나올지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"우리 어머니는 앙심을 품는 분이 아니야.\" 클림이 얼굴빛이 희미하게 붉어지며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 당신이 안 갔으면 좋겠어요.\" 유스테이시아가 낮은 목소리로 거듭 말했다. \"오늘 밤은 안 가기로 해주시면, 제가 내일 혼자 그분 댁에 가서 화해하고, 당신이 데리러 올 때까지 기다리겠다고 약속할게요.\"\n\n\"전에 내가 그러자고 할 때마다 매번 마다하더니, 어째서 하필 이 시점에 그러고 싶어하는 거야?\"\n\n\"당신이 가기 전에 제가 그분을 혼자 뵙고 싶다는 것 말고는 더 설명할 수가 없어요.\" 그녀가 머리를 초조하게 한 번 움직이며 대답했는데, 그녀 같은 사람보다는 낙천적인 기질의 사람들에게서 더 자주 보이는 그런 불안을 띠고 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"참 이상하군, 막 내가 직접 가기로 정한 바로 그때에 당신은 내가 오래전에 제안했던 걸 하고 싶어하다니. 내일 당신이 가기를 기다리면 하루를 또 잃게 되고, 나는 다녀오지 않고서는 또 하룻밤을 도무지 편히 쉴 수 없을 게 분명해. 나는 이 일을 매듭짓고 싶고, 그럴 거야. 당신은 나중에 그분을 찾아뵈면 돼⏤매한가지일 테니.\"\n\n\"지금 당신과 같이 가도 될까요?\"\n\n\"내가 쉴 것보다 더 오래 쉬지 않고서는 당신은 거기까지 갔다 오기 힘들 거야. 아니, 오늘 밤은 안 돼, 유스테이시아.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신 말씀대로 하죠.\" 그녀는, 비록 온건한 노력으로 나쁜 결과를 막으려는 마음은 있으나, 그것들을 기어이 비틀어 이끌려고 안간힘을 쓰느니 차라리 일이 되어가는 대로 내버려두려는 사람의 그 조용한 투로 대답했다.\n\n이윽고 클림은 정원으로 나갔고, 오후의 남은 시간 내내 생각에 잠긴 나른함이 유스테이시아에게 스며들었는데, 남편은 그것을 날씨의 더위 탓으로 돌렸다.\n\n저녁이 되자 그는 그 여정에 나섰다. 여름의 더위는 아직 맹렬했으나 낮은 상당히 짧아져 있었고, 그가 길을 일 마일도 채 나아가기 전에 히스의 모든 보랏빛, 갈색, 초록빛은 산뜻함도 단계도 없는 한결같은 옷으로 녹아들어, 깨끗한 석영 모래의 작은 무더기들이 토끼 굴 입구를 드러내는 곳이나 오솔길의 흰 부싯돌들이 비탈 위로 실처럼 놓인 곳에서 흰빛으로 점점이 끊길 뿐이었다. 여기저기 자라난 외따로 떨어진 키 작은 가시나무들 거의 하나하나마다에서 쏙독새 한 마리가 숨을 참을 수 있는 한 물방앗간 덜컹이는 소리처럼 윙윙거리다가 멈추고, 날개를 퍼덕이고, 덤불 둘레를 빙 돌고, 내려앉고, 잠시 말없이 귀 기울인 뒤 다시 윙윙거리기 시작하는 것으로 제 존재를 드러냈다. 클림의 발이 스칠 때마다 흰 가루나방들이 공중으로, 서쪽에서 비쳐오는 누그러진 빛을 그 가루투성이 날개로 받을 만큼만 딱 그만큼 높이 날아올랐는데, 그 빛은 이제 땅의 움푹한 곳과 평평한 곳을 가로질러 비치되 그 위에 내려앉아 그것들을 밝히지는 않았다.\n\n요브라이트는 모든 일이 곧 잘되리라는 희망을 안고 이 고요한 풍경 속을 걸어갔다. 삼 마일을 가서 그는 부드러운 향기가 길을 가로질러 풍겨오는 곳에 다다랐고, 그 낯익은 냄새를 들이마시려고 잠시 멈춰 섰다. 그곳은 네 시간 전에 그의 어머니가 백리향으로 뒤덮인 둔덕에 기진해 주저앉았던 자리였다. 그가 서 있는 동안 숨소리와 신음 사이쯤 되는 어떤 소리가 문득 그의 귀에 닿았다.\n\n그는 그 소리가 나는 쪽을 보았으나, 거기에는 끊어지지 않은 선으로 하늘을 향해 뻗어 있는 둔덕의 가장자리 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 그가 그쪽으로 몇 걸음 옮기자, 이제 그는 거의 제 발치에 누워 있는 한 형체를 알아보았다.\n\n그 사람이 누구일지에 관한 갖가지 가능성 가운데, 그것이 제 가족 중 하나일지 모른다는 생각은 요브라이트에게 한순간도 떠오르지 않았다. 이따금 가시금작화 베는 이들이 집까지 멀리 갔다가 다시 오는 수고를 덜려고 이런 때 한데서 자기도 한다는 것은 알려진 바였다. 그러나 클림은 그 신음을 떠올리고는 더 가까이 들여다보았고, 그 형체가 여자임을 알았다. 그러자 동굴에서 새어 나오는 찬 공기 같은 비통함이 그를 덮쳐왔다. 그러나 그는 몸을 굽혀 창백하고 눈을 감은 그 얼굴을 보고 나서야 비로소 그 여인이 제 어머니임을 완전히 확신했다.\n\n숨이, 말하자면, 그의 몸에서 빠져나갔고, 그에게서 터져 나왔을 고뇌의 외침은 입술에서 사그라졌다. 무언가 조처를 취해야 한다는 것을 의식하기까지의 그 찰나의 간격 동안 시간과 장소에 대한 모든 감각이 그에게서 떠났으니, 마치 그와\n그의 어머니는 여러 해 전 이 황야에서, 지금과 비슷한 시각에 그가 아이로서 함께 있을 때와 똑같았다. 그러자 그는 정신이 번쩍 들어 활동에 나섰고, 몸을 더 낮게 굽혀보니 어머니가 아직 숨을 쉬고 있으며, 그 숨은 약하긴 해도 이따금 헐떡임에 흐트러질 뿐 규칙적이라는 것을 알았다.\n\n\"아, 이게 어찌 된 일이에요! 어머니, 많이 편찮으세요—돌아가시는 건 아니죠?\" 그가 입술을 어머니의 얼굴에 대며 외쳤다. \"저 클림이에요. 어떻게 여기 오셨어요? 이게 다 무슨 영문이에요?\"\n\n그 순간 유스테이샤에 대한 그의 사랑이 빚어낸 두 사람 삶 사이의 균열은 요브라이트의 기억에 떠오르지 않았으니, 그에게 현재는 갈라서기 전 두 사람이 함께 겪었던 그 다정한 과거와 끊김 없이 이어져 있었다.\n\n어머니가 입술을 움직였고, 그를 알아보는 듯했으나 말은 하지 못했고, 그러자 클림은 어머니를 어떻게 옮기는 것이 가장 좋을지 헤아리려 애썼으니, 이슬이 짙어지기 전에 어머니를 이 자리에서 데려가야 할 것이었기 때문이다. 그는 몸이 튼튼했고, 어머니는 야위었다. 그는 두 팔로 어머니를 감싸 안고 조금 들어올리며 말했다. \"이러면 아프세요?\"\n\n어머니가 고개를 저었고, 그는 어머니를 들어올렸으며, 그러고는 느린 걸음으로 짐을 안고 나아갔다. 이제 공기는 완전히 서늘했으나, 풀이 깔리지 않은 모래밭을 지날 때마다 그 표면에서 낮 동안 빨아들인 열기가 그의 얼굴로 반사되었다. 일을 시작할 때만 해도 블룸스엔드에 닿기까지 가로질러야 할 거리에 대해서는 거의 생각하지 않았으나, 그날 오후에 잠을 자긴 했어도 곧 그는 짐의 무게를 느끼기 시작했다. 그렇게 그는 아버지를 업은 아이네이아스처럼 나아갔으니, 박쥐들이 머리 주위를 맴돌고, 쏙독새들이 그의 얼굴에서 한 자도 안 되는 거리에서 날개를 퍼덕였으며, 부를 만한 사람이라곤 하나도 없었다.\n\n집까지 아직 일 마일 가까이 남았을 즈음 어머니가 실려 가는 답답함에 안절부절못하는 기색을 보였으니, 마치 그의 두 팔이 거추장스럽다는 듯했다. 그는 어머니를 무릎 위로 내려놓고 주위를 둘러보았다. 이제 두 사람이 다다른 지점은 어떤 길에서도 멀리 떨어져 있긴 했으나, 페어웨이와 샘, 험프리, 캔틀 일가가 사는 블룸스엔드의 오두막들에서 일 마일이 채 안 되는 곳이었다. 게다가 오십 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에 흙덩이로 짓고 얇은 뗏장으로 덮은 헛간이 하나 서 있었는데, 지금은 아주 버려진 채였다. 외딴 헛간의 단순한 윤곽이 보였고, 그는 그쪽으로 발걸음을 향하기로 마음먹었다. 도착하자마자 그는 어머니를 입구 옆에 조심스레 눕히고는, 달려가 주머니칼로 가장 마른 고사리를 한 아름 베었다. 한쪽이 완전히 트인 헛간 안에 그것을 깔고 어머니를 그 위에 눕히고는, 페어웨이의 집을 향해 있는 힘껏 달렸다.\n\n병자의 끊긴 숨소리만이 정적을 깨뜨리는 가운데 십오 분 가까이 흘렀을 즈음, 움직이는 형체들이 황야와 하늘 사이의 경계선을 살아 움직이게 하기 시작했다. 잠시 후 클림이 페어웨이, 험프리, 수전 넌서치와 함께 도착했고, 마침 페어웨이의 집에 와 있던 올리 다우든, 그리고 크리스천과 캔틀 영감이 뒤에서 허둥지둥 따라왔다. 그들은 등불과 성냥, 물, 베개, 그밖에 그 황급한 순간에 머리에 떠오른 몇 가지 물건을 가져왔다. 샘은 브랜디를 가지러 다시 보내졌고, 한 소년이 페어웨이의 조랑말을 끌고 와서 그는 그것을 타고 가장 가까운 의원에게 달려갔는데, 가는 길에 와일디브의 집에 들러 토머신에게 숙모가 편찮다는 것을 알리라는 당부를 받았다.\n\n샘과 브랜디가 곧 도착해 등불 빛 아래서 브랜디를 먹이니, 그 후 어머니는 발에 무언가 잘못되었다는 것을 손짓으로 알릴 만큼 의식을 차렸다. 올리 다우든이 마침내 그 뜻을 알아채고 가리킨 발을 살펴보았다. 발은 부어올라 붉었다. 그들이 지켜보는 사이에도 붉은빛은 더 검푸른 색을 띠기 시작했고, 그 한가운데 완두콩보다 작은 진홍색 점 하나가 나타났는데, 살펴보니 그것은 핏방울 한 점으로, 발목의 매끄러운 살 위로 반구처럼 돋아 있었다.\n\n\"뭔지 알겠어요,\" 샘이 외쳤다. \"독사에게 물리셨어요!\"\n\n\"그래,\" 클림이 곧바로 말했다. \"어릴 적에 꼭 이런 물린 자국을 봤던 게 기억나. 아, 가엾은 어머니!\"\n\n\"우리 아버지가 물리셨었지,\" 샘이 말했다. \"그리고 그걸 낫게 하는 길은 하나뿐이에요. 그 자리를 다른 독사들의 기름으로 문질러야 하는데, 그걸 얻는 유일한 방법은 그것들을 튀기는 거예요. 우리 아버지한테도 그렇게 했죠.\"\n\n\"그건 옛날 처방이라,\" 클림이 미심쩍게 말했다. \"나는 그게 미덥지가 않아. 하지만 의사가 올 때까지는 달리 할 수 있는 게 없지.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 듣는 처방이에요,\" 올리 다우든이 힘주어 말했다. \"내가 병구완하러 다니던 시절에 써봤어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 날이 밝기를 빌어야겠군, 그것들을 잡으려면 말이야,\" 클림이 침울하게 말했다.\n\n\"내가 어떻게든 해보지요,\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n그는 지팡이로 쓰던 푸른 개암나무 가지를 가져다 끝을 쪼개고 작은 조약돌을 끼워 넣은 다음, 손에 등불을 들고 황야로 나갔다. 이때쯤 클림은 작은 불을 피워놓고 수전 넌서치를 보내 프라이팬을 가져오게 했다. 그녀가 돌아오기도 전에 샘이 독사 세 마리를 들고 들어왔는데, 한 마리는 막대기 갈라진 틈에서 활발하게 똬리를 틀었다 풀었다 했고, 나머지 두 마리는 죽은 채 그 위에 걸쳐져 있었다.\n\n\"마땅히 그래야 하는 만큼 산 채로 싱싱하게 잡은 건 한 마리뿐이에요,\" 샘이 말했다. \"이 흐물흐물한 두 마리는 오늘 일하다가 잡은 건데, 해가 질 때까지는 죽지 않으니 그렇게 상한 고기는 아닐 거예요.\"\n\n산 독사는 작고 검은 눈에 음흉한 빛을 띤 채 모여 선 무리를 바라보았고, 등에 새겨진 아름다운 갈색과 검정 무늬는 분노로 한층 짙어지는 듯했다. 요브라이트 부인이 그 짐승을 보았고, 짐승도 그녀를 보았으니—그녀는 온몸을 떨며 눈길을 돌렸다.\n\n\"저것 좀 봐,\" 크리스천 캔틀이 중얼거렸다. \"여보게들, 옷도 안 걸친 젊은 여자한테 사과를 건넨, 하느님의 동산에 있던 그 옛 뱀의 무언가가 아직도 독사와 뱀들 속에 살아 있지 않다고 어찌 장담하겠나? 그 눈을 좀 봐—영락없이 무슨 사악한 검은 까치밥나무 열매 같잖아. 우리한테 악담을 못 걸기를 바라야지! 황야엔 벌써 흘린 눈길을 받은 사람들도 있다고. 나는 살아 있는 한 다시는 독사를 죽이지 않을 테야.\"\n\n\"하긴, 어쩔 수 없다면 이런저런 걸 무서워하는 것도 옳지,\" 캔틀 영감이 말했다. \"그랬으면 내 평생 숱한 용감한 위험을 피할 수 있었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"헛간 밖에서 무슨 소리가 들린 것 같아,\" 크리스천이 말했다. \"재난이 낮에 닥쳐주면 좋겠어, 그래야 사내가 용기를 보일 수도 있고, 용감한 사내라서 그 여편네 눈에 안 띄게 달아날 수만 있다면, 아무리 빗자루 같은 늙은 마녀를 만나도 자비를 구걸하지 않아도 될 테니!\"\n\n\"나처럼 무지한 작자도 그것보단 잘 알겠다,\" 샘이 말했다.\n\n\"하긴, 어찌 됐든 가장 안 그럴 것 같은 데서 재앙이 닥치는 법이지. 여보게들, 요브라이트 부인이 죽기라도 하면 우리가 한 여자를 죽인 과실치사로 붙들려 재판받게 될까?\"\n\n\"아니, 그렇게 몰아갈 수는 없어,\" 샘이 말했다. \"우리가 살면서 언젠가 밀렵꾼이었다고 입증할 수 있다면 모를까. 하지만 부인은 회복하실 거야.\"\n\n\"이봐, 내가 독사 열 마리한테 물렸어도 그 때문에 하루치 일을 잃지는 않았을 거야,\" 캔틀 영감이 말했다. \"내가 기운이 펄펄할 때면 그런 사람이지. 하긴 전쟁을 위해 훈련받은 사내한테는 자연스러운 건지도 모르지. 그래, 나는 별의별 일을 다 겪었어. 하지만 사 년에 의용군에 들어간 뒤로는 무엇 하나 나한테 거슬리는 게 없었지.\" 그는 고개를 저으며 제복을 입은 자신의 모습을 마음속에 그리고는 미소 지었다. \"젊은 시절엔 가장 늠름한 곤경에서 늘 내가 앞장섰단 말이야!\"\n\n\"그건 늘 가장 멍청한 놈을 앞세웠기 때문이겠지,\" 불가에 무릎 꿇고 입김으로 불을 살리던 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각하나, 티머시?\" 캔틀 영감이 얼굴에 갑작스러운 침울함을 띠고 페어웨이 곁으로 다가오며 말했다. \"그렇다면 사내가 여러 해 동안 자기가 든든하고 알찬 동무라고 느껴왔는데, 결국 제 자신에 대해 틀렸던 걸 수도 있는 건가?\"\n\n\"그딴 문제는 신경 쓰지 마쇼, 영감. 다리나 놀려서 장작이나 좀 더 가져오쇼. 생사가 갈리는 판에 늙은이가 그렇게 떠벌리는 건 영 같잖은 짓이오.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래,\" 캔틀 영감이 우울한 확신을 담아 말했다. \"하긴, 한창때 잘나가던 이들한테는 오늘 밤이 통째로 고약한 밤이지. 내가 아무리 오보에나 테너 비올을 잘 다뤘던들, 지금 그것들로 가락을 켤 마음은 안 났을 테야.\"\n\n이제 수전이 프라이팬을 들고 도착하자, 산 독사를 죽이고 세 마리의 머리를 모두 떼어냈다. 나머지 몸통은 토막 내어 갈라 펼친 채 팬에 던져 넣으니, 팬이 불 위에서 지글지글 타닥타닥 소리를 내기 시작했다. 곧 사체에서 맑은 기름이 가느다란 줄기로 흘러나왔고, 그러자 클림은 손수건 모서리를 그 액체에 적셔 상처에 발랐다.\n\nVIII.\n유스테이샤가 행운의 소식을 듣고, 악을 목도하다\n\n그동안 올더워스의 오두막에 홀로 남겨진 유스테이샤는 사태의 형국에 적잖이 침울해져 있었다. 어머니가 그날 자기 집 문 앞에서 쫓겨났다는 사실을 클림이 알게 됨으로써 빚어질 수 있는 결과들은 불쾌할 공산이 컸으니, 이는 그녀가 끔찍한 것 못지않게 싫어하는, 사건이 지닌 성질이었다.\n\n홀로 저녁 시간을 보내도록 남겨지는 것은 언제든 그녀에게 거추장스러운 일이었으나, 이날 저녁은 지난 몇 시간의 흥분 때문에 여느 때보다 더 거추장스러웠다. 두 차례의 방문이 그녀를 안절부절못하게 휘저어 놓았던 것이다. 클림과 그 어머니 사이의 언쟁에서 자기가 나쁜 처지에 놓이게 될 개연성에 대해 그녀가 크게 불안에 휩싸인 것은 아니었으나, 짜증이 일도록 휘저어진 것은 사실이었으니, 잠들어 있던 활동성이 되살아나 차라리 문을 열어줄걸 하고 바라는 지경에 이르렀다. 그녀는 분명 클림이 깨어 있다고 믿었었고, 그 변명은 그 한도 안에서는 정직한 것이었으나, 첫 노크에 응답하기를 거부한 일에 대해서는 그 무엇도 그녀를 비난에서 구해줄 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 그 결과에 대해 자신을 탓하는 대신, 자기 처지를 짜고 자기 운명을 지배해온, 어떤 흐릿하고 거대한 이 세상의 군주의 어깨에 그 잘못을 떠넘겼다.\n\n해마다 이맘때면 낮보다 밤에 걷는 것이 더 쾌적했고, 클림이 떠난 지 한 시간쯤 되었을 무렵 그녀는 돌아오는 그를 만날 수 있을까 하여 불현듯 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 나가보기로 마음먹었다. 정원 문에 다다랐을 때 바퀴 소리가 다가오는 것이 들렸고, 둘러보니 할아버지가 마차를 몰고 올라오고 있었다.\n\n\"고맙다만 잠시도 못 머문다,\" 그가 그녀의 인사에 답했다. \"이스트에그던으로 가는 길이야. 다만 소식이나 전하려고 이리 둘러 왔지. 어쩌면 들었을지도 모르겠다—와일디브 씨의 재산에 대해서 말이다.\"\n\n\"아뇨,\" 유스테이샤가 멍하니 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 그 사람이 만천 파운드의 재산을 물려받게 됐단다—캐나다에 있던 삼촌이 죽었는데, 본국으로 보내려던 식구들이 죄다 카시오페이아호와 함께 가라앉았다는 소식을 듣자마자 죽었다지. 그래서 와일디브가 전혀 기대도 안 했는데 모든 걸 다 물려받게 된 거야.\"\n\n유스테이샤는 잠시 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다. \"그가 이 일을 안 지 얼마나 됐어요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 오늘 아침 일찍 알았을 게다, 내가 열 시에 알았으니 말이다, 찰리가 돌아왔을 때였지. 자, 그가 바로 내가 말하는 운 좋은 사내야. 너야말로 어리석었지, 유스테이샤!\"\n\n\"어떤 점에서요?\" 그녀가 짐짓 침착하게 눈을 들며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 잡았을 때 붙들어두지 않았잖니.\"\n\n\"잡긴 뭘 잡아요!\"\n\n\"나는 최근까지도 너희 사이에 뭔가 있었던 줄 몰랐다. 그래, 알았더라면 펄펄 뛰며 격하게 반대했겠지. 하지만 너희 사이에 얼마간 냄새가 났던 듯하니, 그렇다면 도대체 왜 그를 붙들어두지 않았느냐?\"\n\n유스테이샤는 아무런 대꾸도 하지 않았으나, 마음만 먹으면 그 일에 대해 자기도 그만큼 할 말이 있다는 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n\"그래, 네 가엾은 반쯤 눈먼 남편은 좀 어떠냐?\" 노인이 말을 이었다. \"그 친구도 그 나름으로는 나쁜 사람은 아니지.\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지내요.\"\n\n\"그 사촌, 그 뭐라더라? 그 여자한테는 잘된 일이지. 이런, 네가 그 배에 탔어야 하는 건데, 얘야! 자, 이제 가봐야겠다. 도움이 필요하냐? 내 것이 곧 네 것이라는 거, 알지.\"\n\n\"고맙지만 할아버지, 지금은 부족한 게 없어요,\" 그녀가 차갑게 말했다. \"클림은 가시금작화를 베고 있지만, 거의 유익한 소일거리 삼아 하는 거예요, 달리 할 수 있는 게 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"그 소일거리로 삯도 받지 않느냐? 백 단에 삼 실링이라고 들었다만.\"\n\n\"클림은 돈이 있어요,\" 그녀가 낯을 붉히며 말했다. \"다만 조금이라도 벌고 싶어 하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 잘 자거라.\" 그러고는 선장은 마차를 몰고 떠났다.\n\n할아버지가 가버리자 유스테이샤는 기계적으로 가던 길을 걸었으나, 그녀의 생각은 더 이상 시어머니와 클림에 관한 것이 아니었다. 와일디브는 제 운명에 대한 온갖 불평에도 불구하고 운명의 손에 붙들려 다시금 햇살 속에 놓인 것이었다. 만천 파운드! 모든 에그던식 관점에서 그는 부자였다. 유스테이샤의 눈에도 그것은 넉넉한 액수였으니—클림이 더 엄격한 기분일 때면 헛되고 사치스럽다고 낙인찍던 그녀의 그 욕구들을 채워주기에 충분한 금액이었다. 그녀가 돈을 사랑하는 사람은 아니었으나 돈이 가져다줄 수 있는 것은 사랑했고, 이제 그를 둘러싸고 있으리라 상상되는 그 새로운 부속물들이 와일디브를 더없는 흥미로 휘감았다. 그녀는 이제 그가 그날 아침 얼마나 조용히 잘 차려입고 있었던가를 떠올렸으니—그는 아마 찔레와 가시에 상하든 말든 아랑곳 않고 가장 새 옷을 차려입었던 것이다. 그러고는 그가 자기를 대하던 태도를 생각했다.\n\n\"아, 알겠어, 알겠어,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그가 지금 나를 가졌으면 하고 얼마나 바라겠어, 내가 원하는 걸 죄다 줄 수 있을 테니까!\"\n\n당시에는 거의 눈여겨보지 않았던 그의 눈길과 말의 세세한 점들을 되짚어보니, 그것들이 얼마나 크게 이 새로운 사건에 대한 그의 앎에 좌우되었던가가 그녀에게 분명해졌다. \"버림받은 데 앙심을 품을 사내였다면 의기양양한 어조로 제 행운을 내게 떠벌렸겠지. 그러기는커녕 그는 내 불운을 헤아려 한 마디도 입에 올리지 않았고, 그저 자기보다 우월한 사람을 대하듯 여전히 나를 사랑한다고만 넌지시 비쳤을 뿐이야.\"\n\n자기에게 일어난 일에 대해 그날 와일디브가 보인 침묵은 바로 그런 여자에게 깊은 인상을 남기도록 계산된 종류의 처신이었다. 그 섬세한 고상함의 손길이야말로 실은 이성을 대하는 그의 태도에서 강점 가운데 하나였다. 와일디브의 특이한 점은, 어떤 때는 한 여자에게 열정적이고 비난조이며 원망에 차 있다가도, 또 어떤 때는 전에 없이 우아하게 대함으로써, 앞서의 무관심을 무례가 아닌 것처럼, 해를 끼친 것을 모욕이 아닌 것처럼, 간섭을 섬세한 배려처럼, 그녀의 명예를 망친 것을 지나친 기사도처럼 보이게 만든다는 데 있었다. 그날 유스테이샤가 그 흠모를 무시했고, 그 호의를 애써 받아들이려 들지도 않았으며, 뒷문으로 집에서 내보냈던 이 사내가 바로 만천 파운드의 소유자였으니—제법 전문적인 교육을 받았고, 토목 기사 밑에서 견습 기간을 마친 사람이었다.\n\n와일디브의 처지에 너무나 몰두한 나머지 유스테이샤는 클림의 처지가 자기 행로에 얼마나 더 가까이 닿아 있는가를 잊어버렸고, 곧장 걸어가 그를 만나는 대신 돌 위에 주저앉았다. 등 뒤의 목소리에 몽상이 흐트러져 고개를 돌리니, 그 옛 연인이자 행운으로 부를 물려받은 이가 바로 그녀 곁에 와 있었다.\n\n그녀는 앉은 채로 있었으나, 그 표정의 일렁임은 와일디브만큼 그녀를 잘 아는 사내라면 누구든 그녀가 그를 생각하고 있었음을 알아챌 만한 것이었다.\n\n\"어떻게 여기 왔어요?\" 그녀가 맑고 낮은 어조로 말했다. \"집에 있는 줄 알았는데.\"\n\n\"당신 정원을 떠난 뒤 마을로 갔다가, 이제 다시 돌아오는 길이오—그게 다요. 어느 쪽으로 걷고 있는지 물어봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n그녀는 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 손을 저어 보였다. \"남편을 만나러 가는 길이에요. 오늘 당신이 나와 함께 있는 동안 아마 곤란한 일에 빠졌을지도 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"어쩌다 그럴 수가 있소?\"\n\n\"요브라이트 부인을 들이지 않았으니까요.\"\n\n\"그 방문이 당신에게 해를 끼치지 않았기를 바라오.\"\n\n\"전혀요. 당신 탓은 아니에요,\" 그녀가 조용히 말했다.\n\n이때쯤 그녀는 일어서 있었고, 두 사람은 저도 모르게 아무 말 없이 이삼 분 동안 함께 거닐었는데, 유스테이샤가 침묵을 깨고 말했다. \"축하 인사를 드려야 할 것 같네요.\"\n\n\"무엇에 대해? 아 그렇지, 내 만천 파운드 말이군. 글쎄, 다른 무언가를 얻지 못했으니, 그거라도 얻은 걸로 만족해야겠지.\"\n\n\"그 일에 대해 무척 무심해 보이네요. 오늘 왔을 때 왜 내게 말해주지 않았어요?\" 그녀가 무시당한 사람의 어조로 말했다. \"나는 순전히 우연히 들었다고요.\"\n\n\"말해줄 생각이긴 했소,\" 와일디브가 말했다. \"하지만 나는—그래, 솔직히 말하리다—유스테이샤, 당신의 별이 높이 떠 있지 않은 걸 보고는 그 얘길 꺼내고 싶지 않았소. 당신 남편이 그러고 누워 있던 것처럼, 한 사내가 고된 일에 지쳐 쓰러져 누워 있는 모습을 보니, 당신에게 내 행운을 자랑한다는 게 영 격에 맞지 않게 느껴졌소. 그러면서도, 당신이 그 곁에 서 있는 모습을 보니, 여러모로 그가 나보다 더 부유한 사내라는 느낌이 들지 않을 수 없었소.\"\n\n이에 유스테이샤가 잠들어 있던 짓궂음을 담아 말했다. \"어머, 그럼 그와 바꾸겠어요—당신 재산을 주고 나를 가지겠어요?\"\n\n\"물론 그러겠소,\" 와일디브가 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 불가능하고 터무니없는 걸 상상하고 있으니, 이쯤에서 화제를 바꾸는 게 어때요?\"\n\n\"좋소. 듣고 싶다면 앞날에 대한 내 계획을 말해주겠소. 구천 파운드는 영구히 투자하고, 천 파운드는 당장 쓸 돈으로 두고, 남은 천 파운드로 일 년 남짓 여행을 다닐 작정이오.\"\n\n\"여행이요? 정말 멋진 생각이네요! 어디로 갈 건데요?\"\n\n\"여기서 파리로 가서 거기서 겨울과 봄을 보낼 거요. 그런 다음 더위가 닥치기 전에 이탈리아, 그리스, 이집트, 팔레스타인으로 갈 거요. 여름엔 아메리카로 가고, 그다음엔 아직 정해지지 않은 계획대로 오스트레일리아로 갔다가 인도로 돌아올 거요. 그쯤이면 슬슬 진력이 나기 시작하겠지. 그러면 아마 다시 파리로 돌아와서, 형편이 닿는 한 거기 오래 머물 거요.\"\n\n\"다시 파리로,\" 그녀가 거의 한숨에 가까운 목소리로 중얼거렸다. 클림의 묘사가 그녀 안에 심어놓은 그 파리에 대한 갈망을 그녀는 와일디브에게 단 한 번도 말한 적이 없었으나, 여기 그가 저도 모르게 그 갈망을 채워줄 수 있는 처지에 있었던 것이다. \"파리를 무척 높이 치는군요?\" 그녀가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그렇소. 내 생각에 그곳은 세상의 중심이 되는 아름다운 곳이오.\"\n\n\"내 생각에도요! 그럼 토머신도 같이 가나요?\"\n\n\"그럴 마음이 있다면 그렇겠지. 집에 남는 편을 택할지도 모르고.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 여기저기 돌아다니고, 나는 여기 머물러 있게 되겠네요!\"\n\n\"그렇게 되겠지. 하지만 그게 누구 탓인지는 우리가 알잖소.\"\n\n\"당신을 탓하는 건 아니에요,\" 그녀가 얼른 말했다.\n\n\"오, 탓하는 줄 알았소. 혹여라도 나를 탓하고 싶어지거든, 레인배로 곁의 어느 저녁을 떠올려보시오, 나를 만나기로 약속해놓고 나오지 않았던 그날을. 당신은 내게 편지를 보냈고, 나는 그걸 읽으며 가슴이 아렸으니, 당신은 부디 그런 일이 없기를 바라오. 그게 한 갈림길이었지. 그러고 나서 나는 성급하게 무언가를 했고…… 하지만 그 여자는 착한 여인이니, 더는 말하지 않으리다.\"\n\n\"그때는 잘못이 내 쪽에 있었다는 거 알아요,\" 유스테이샤가 말했다. \"하지만 늘 그랬던 건 아니에요. 하지만 너무 갑작스레 감정에 휩쓸리는 게 내 불운이지요. 아, 데이먼, 더 이상 나를 책망하지 말아요—나는 그걸 견딜 수가 없어요.\"\n\n두 사람은 이삼 마일 거리를 말없이 걸었는데, 유스테이샤가 불쑥 말했다. \"당신 가던 길에서 벗어난 거 아니에요, 와일디브 씨?\"\n\n\"오늘 밤 내 길은 어디든 다 길이오. 당신 혼자 다니기엔 늦은 시각이니, 블룸스엔드가 보이는 언덕까지 함께 가리다.\"\n\n\"수고하지 말아요. 나는 꼭 나와 있어야 할 까닭도 없어요. 더는 동행하지 않는 편이 나을 것 같아요. 이런 일은 알려지면 묘하게 보일 테니까요.\"\n\n\"좋소, 그럼 당신을 두고 가리다.\" 그가 뜻밖에 그녀의 손을 잡더니 입을 맞추었다—그녀가 결혼한 이래 처음이었다. \"저 언덕 위의 저 불빛은 뭐요?\" 그가 마치 그 입맞춤을 감추려는 듯 덧붙였다.\n\n그녀가 바라보니, 조금 앞쪽 오두막의 트인 한쪽에서 깜빡이는 불빛이 새어 나오고 있었다. 여태껏 늘 비어 있던 그 오두막에 이제 누군가 들어와 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"기왕 이렇게 멀리까지 왔으니,\" 유스테이샤가 말했다. \"저 헛간을 무사히 지날 때까지만 데려다줄래요? 이 근처 어디선가 클림을 만날 줄 알았는데, 나타나질 않으니 서둘러 가서 그가 떠나기 전에 블룸스엔드에 닿아야겠어요.\"\n\n두 사람이 뗏장 헛간으로 다가가니, 가까이 갔을 때 안쪽의 불빛과 등불이 고사리 자리에 비스듬히 누운 한 여인의 모습과 그 주위에 둘러선 황야의 남녀 무리를 또렷이 비춰 보였다. 유스테이샤는 가까이 다가가서야 비로소 누운 형체가 요브라이트 부인임을, 그리고 둘러선 이들 가운데 한 사람이 클림임을 알아보았다. 그러자 그녀는 황급히 와일디브의 팔을 손으로 눌러, 헛간의 트인 쪽에서 그늘 속으로 물러나자고 신호했다.\n\n\"내 남편하고 그 어머니예요,\" 그녀가 동요한 목소리로 속삭였다. \"이게 무슨 일이지? 앞으로 나가서 알아봐 줄래요?\"\n\n와일디브가 그녀 곁을 떠나 오두막 뒷벽으로 갔다. 이윽고 유스테이샤는 그가 자기를 손짓해 부르는 것을 알아채고는 다가가 그에게 합류했다.\n\n\"심각한 상황이오,\" 와일디브가 말했다.\n\n그들이 선 자리에서는 안에서 오가는 말이 들렸다.\n\n\"어머니가 어디로 가시던 길이었는지 도무지 짐작이 안 갑니다,\" 클림이 누군가에게 말했다. \"분명 먼 길을 걸어오셨는데, 조금 전 말을 할 수 있었을 때조차 어디였는지 말씀해주지 않으셨어요. 어머니를 정말 어떻게 보십니까?\"\n\n\"두려워해야 할 점이 많습니다,\" 진중하게 답하는 목소리가 들렸는데, 유스테이샤는 그것이 이 지역의 유일한 외과의의 목소리임을 알아챘다. \"독사에게 물린 것으로도 다소 고통을 겪으셨습니다만, 그분을 쓰러뜨린 것은 탈진입니다. 제 인상으로는 그분의 걸음이 유달리 길었던 게 틀림없습니다.\"\n\n\"이런 날씨엔 무리하게 걷지 마시라고 늘 말씀드렸는데,\" 클림이 비통하게 말했다. \"독사 기름을 쓴 게 잘한 일이었을까요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 그건 아주 오래된 처방입니다—제가 알기로 독사잡이들의 옛 처방이지요,\" 의사가 답했다. \"호프만과 미드, 그리고 제 생각엔 폰타나 신부도 틀림없이 듣는 연고라고 언급했습니다. 분명 할 수 있는 것 가운데 그만한 게 없었지요. 다른 기름들도 똑같이 효험이 있지 않았을까 싶긴 합니다만.\"\n\n\"이리 와요, 이리 와!\" 그때 다급한 여자의 어조로 빠르게 외치는 소리가 났고, 클림과 의사가 헛간 뒤편에서 요브라이트 부인이 누운 곳으로 달려가는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"아, 무슨 일이지?\" 유스테이샤가 속삭였다.\n\n\"토머신이 말한 거요,\" 와일디브가 말했다. \"그럼 저들이 그녀를 데려왔군. 내가 들어가는 게 나을지—그래도 해가 될지도 모르겠소.\"\n\n오랫동안 안의 무리 사이에는 완전한 정적이 감돌았고, 마침내 클림이 고통에 찬 목소리로 말함으로써 그 정적이 깨졌다. \"오 선생님, 이게 무슨 뜻입니까?\"\n\n의사는 곧바로 대답하지 않았으나, 끝내 이렇게 말했다. \"급격히 가라앉고 계십니다. 심장이 전부터 약했던 데다, 육체의 탈진이 마지막 일격을 가한 겁니다.\"\n\n그러자 여자들의 울음소리가 나더니, 기다림이 이어지고, 이어 숨죽인 탄식, 이어 기이한 헐떡임 소리, 이어 괴로운 정적이 따랐다.\n\n\"다 끝났습니다,\" 의사가 말했다.\n\n오두막 더 안쪽에서 농막 사람들이 속삭였다. \"요브라이트 부인이 돌아가셨어.\"\n\n거의 같은 순간 두 사람은 자그마하고 구식 차림을 한 아이 하나가 헛간의 트인 쪽으로 들어서는 모습을 보았다. 그 아이의 어미인 수전 넌서치가 입구로 나아가 말없이 손짓하여 돌아가라고 일렀다.\n\n\"엄마한테 할 말이 있어요,\" 아이가 새된 소리로 외쳤다. \"저기 잠든 저 아주머니가 오늘 나하고 같이 걸었는데, 자기를 봤다고 말하랬어요, 자기는 가슴이 찢어진 여자고 아들한테 버림받았다고요, 그러고 나서 나는 집으로 왔어요.\"\n\n안에서 어떤 사내가 흐느끼는 듯한 어수선한 오열 소리가 들렸고, 그 소리에 유스테이샤가 희미하게 숨을 들이켜며 말했다. \"저건 클림이야—그에게 가야 해—그래도 감히 그럴 수 있을까? 안 돼—가요, 어서!\"\n\n헛간 근처에서 물러난 뒤 그녀가 잠긴 목소리로 말했다. \"이건 내 탓이에요. 나에게 닥칠 악이 도사리고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그분이 결국 당신 집에 들지 못했단 말이오?\" 와일디브가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 바로 거기에 모든 게 걸려 있어요! 오, 나는 어쩌면 좋아! 저들에게 끼어들지 않겠어요—곧장 집으로 갈래요. 데이먼, 잘 가요! 이제 더는 당신과 말할 수가 없어요.\"\n\n두 사람은 헤어졌고, 유스테이샤가 다음 언덕에 다다라 뒤돌아보았다. 등불 빛에 의지한 한 가닥 애처로운 행렬이 오두막에서 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 길을 더듬어 가고 있었다. 와일디브의 모습은 어디에도 보이지 않았다.\n\n어느 날 저녁, 요브라이트 부인의 장례식이 끝나고 삼 주쯤 지났을 때, 은빛 달의 얼굴이 한 다발의 빛줄기를 올더워스에 있는 클림의 집 마룻바닥으로 곧장 쏘아 보낼 무렵, 한 여인이 집 안에서 밖으로 나왔다. 그녀는 잠시나마 숨을 돌리려는 듯 정원 문에 기대었다. 노파마저 미인으로 만들어버리는 창백한 달빛의 손길이, 이미 아름다운 이 얼굴에 신성을 더했다.\n\n그곳에 나온 지 얼마 되지 않아 한 사내가 길을 올라오더니 다소 머뭇거리며 그녀에게 말했다. \"오늘 밤은 좀 어떠신지요, 마님, 괜찮으시다면 여쭙고 싶습니다만?\"\n\n\"좀 나아졌어요, 험프리, 그래도 여전히 많이 편찮으시고요.\" 유스테이샤가 대답했다.\n\n\"정신이 오락가락하시나요, 마님?\"\n\n\"아니요. 지금은 아주 또렷하세요.\"\n\n\"여전히 어머님 얘기로 헛소리를 하시나요, 가엾으셔라?\" 험프리가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"여전히 그러시는데, 그래도 그렇게 심하지는 않아요.\" 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"정말 안된 일이지요, 마님, 그 어린 조니 녀석이 그 양반한테 어머님 임종 때 하신 말씀을 다 일러바쳤다니 말입니다, 아드님한테 버림받아 가슴이 찢어지셨다는 그 말씀을요. 살아 있는 사람이라면 누구라도 무너뜨릴 만한 얘기지요.\"\n\n유스테이샤는 무어라 말하고 싶지만 그러지 못하는 사람처럼 살짝 숨이 막히는 소리를 낼 뿐 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으며, 들어오라는 그녀의 권유를 마다하고 험프리는 자리를 떴다.\n\n유스테이샤는 몸을 돌려 집 안으로 들어가 갓을 씌운 등불이 타고 있는 앞쪽 침실로 올라갔다. 침대에는 창백하고 초췌한 클림이 말똥말똥 깨어 이리저리 몸을 뒤척이고 있었으며, 그의 두 눈은 마치 동공 속의 불꽃이 그 실체를 다 태워버리기라도 하는 듯 뜨거운 빛으로 번득였다.\n\n\"당신이오, 유스테이샤?\" 그녀가 앉자 그가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 클림. 문간에 내려갔다 왔어요. 달이 아름답게 비치고 있고 나뭇잎 하나 흔들리지 않아요.\"\n\n\"비친다고? 나 같은 사람한테 달이 다 무슨 소용이오? 비추든 말든—무엇이든 다 있으라지, 내가 또 하루를 보지 않게만 된다면!… 유스테이샤, 어디를 봐야 할지 모르겠소—생각들이 칼처럼 나를 꿰뚫고 지나가오. 오, 비참함을 그린 그림으로 불멸을 얻고 싶은 자가 있거든 여기로 오라지!\"\n\n\"왜 그런 말을 하세요?\"\n\n\"내가 어머니를 죽이려고 최선을 다했다는 느낌을 떨칠 수가 없소.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 클림.\"\n\n\"아니, 그랬소. 나를 변명해봐야 소용없소! 어머니께 한 내 행동은 너무도 끔찍했소—나는 먼저 다가가지 않았고, 어머니는 차마 나를 용서하지 못하셨소. 그런데 이제 돌아가셨소! 좀 더 일찍 화해할 뜻을 내비치기만 했더라도, 우리가 친구처럼 지냈더라면, 그러고 나서 어머니가 돌아가셨더라면, 이토록 견디기 힘들지는 않았을 텐데. 그런데 나는 한 번도 어머니 집 근처에 가지 않았고, 그래서 어머니도 내 집 근처에 오지 않으셨고, 와도 얼마나 반겼을지 모르신 채 돌아가셨소—그게 나를 괴롭히는 거요. 바로 그날 밤 내가 어머니 집으로 가려 했다는 걸 어머니는 모르셨소, 너무 정신을 잃으셔서 내 말을 알아듣지 못하셨으니까. 어머니가 나를 보러 오기만 하셨더라면! 나는 그러시기를 간절히 바랐소. 하지만 그렇게 될 운명이 아니었소.\"\n\n유스테이샤에게서 예전에 그녀를 역병 같은 돌풍처럼 뒤흔들곤 하던 그 떨리는 한숨이 새어 나왔다. 그녀는 아직 말하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n하지만 요브라이트는 회한에 사로잡힌 자신의 상태에서 비롯된 두서없는 넋두리에 너무도 깊이 빠져 있어 그녀를 알아채지 못했다. 앓는 동안 그는 줄곧 이렇게 떠들어댔다. 오해의 시간에 너무도 쓰라리게 내뱉은, 요브라이트 부인의 마지막 말을 전해 들은 소년의 불운한 폭로 때문에 그의 본래의 슬픔에 절망이 더해졌던 것이다. 그때 비탄이 그를 압도했고, 그는 들일하는 일꾼이 그늘을 갈망하듯 죽음을 갈망했다. 슬픔의 한가운데 초점에 서 있는 한 사내의 가련한 광경이었다. 그는 어머니의 집으로 너무 늦게 떠난 것을 끊임없이 한탄했으니, 그것은 결코 바로잡을 수 없는 잘못이었기 때문이요, 어머니가 자기에게 오지 않으니 자기가 어머니에게 가는 것이 도리임을 진작 생각하지 못하다니 어떤 악마에게 무섭게 홀린 게 틀림없다고 우겼다. 그는 유스테이샤에게 자신을 향한 자책에 동의해달라고 청하곤 했으며, 차마 말할 수 없는 비밀에 속이 타들어가던 그녀가 자기는 의견을 말할 수 없다고 하면, 그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"그건 당신이 우리 어머니의 성품을 몰라서 그러오. 어머니는 부탁만 하면 언제든 용서할 분이셨소. 하지만 어머니가 보시기에 나는 고집 센 아이 같았고, 그래서 어머니가 굽히지 않으셨던 거요. 그렇다고 완고한 분은 아니셨소—자존심이 강하고 말수가 적으셨을 뿐, 그 이상은 아니었소…. 그래, 어머니가 왜 그토록 오래 나를 거부하셨는지 이해할 수 있소. 어머니는 나를 기다리고 계셨던 거요. 슬픔 속에서 백 번도 더 이렇게 말씀하셨을 거요. '내가 그 애를 위해 바친 그 모든 희생에 그 애는 이렇게 보답하는구나!' 나는 어머니께 한 번도 가지 않았소! 어머니를 뵈러 나섰을 때는 이미 너무 늦었소. 그걸 생각하면 거의 견딜 수가 없소!\"\n\n때로 그의 상태는, 순수한 슬픔의 눈물 한 방울로도 누그러지지 않는 완전한 회한에 빠진 것이었으니, 그럴 때면 그는 누운 채 몸부림쳤으며, 육체의 병보다 생각으로 인해 훨씬 더 심하게 달아올랐다. \"어머니가 내가 원망한다는 믿음 속에서 돌아가신 게 아니라는 확신 하나만 얻을 수 있다면,\" 어느 날 이런 기분에 잠겨 그가 말했다. \"그건 천국의 희망보다도 더 생각하기 좋은 일일 거요. 하지만 나는 그걸 할 수가 없소.\"\n\n\"당신은 이 지치게 하는 절망에 너무 빠져들고 있어요.\" 유스테이샤가 말했다. \"다른 사람들의 어머니도 돌아가셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 내 어머니를 잃은 게 덜한 일이 되는 건 아니오. 하지만 이건 잃음 자체보다도 그 잃음의 정황이 문제요. 나는 어머니께 죄를 지었고, 그래서 내게는 빛이 없소.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 어머님이 당신께 죄를 지으신 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그렇지 않소. 죄를 지은 건 나요, 그 모든 짐을 내 머리 위에 지울지어다!\"\n\n\"그 말씀 하시기 전에 두 번은 생각해보셔야 할 것 같아요.\" 유스테이샤가 대답했다. \"독신 남자야 마음껏 자기 자신을 저주할 권리가 있겠지요, 의심할 바 없이. 하지만 아내가 있는 남자는 자기가 빌어 내리는 그 천벌에 둘을 끌어들이는 거예요.\"\n\n\"당신이 무슨 궤변을 늘어놓는지 알아들을 만큼 내 처지가 한가하지가 않소.\" 그 가련한 사내가 말했다. \"낮이고 밤이고 내게 소리치오, '너는 어머니를 죽이는 데 한몫했다.' 하지만 나 자신을 혐오하다 보면, 인정하건대, 가엾은 내 아내인 당신에게 부당하게 굴 수도 있겠지. 그건 용서해주오, 유스테이샤, 나는 내가 무얼 하는지도 거의 모르오.\"\n\n유스테이샤는 이런 상태에 빠진 남편을 보는 것을 늘 피하고 싶어했으니, 그것은 가룟 유다에게 심판의 장면이 그러했던 것만큼이나 그녀에게 끔찍한 일이 되어 있었다. 그것은 열어주지 않을 문을 두드리는, 기진맥진한 여인의 유령을 그녀의 눈앞에 불러왔으며, 그녀는 그것을 떠올리기만 해도 몸을 움츠렸다. 그렇지만 요브라이트 자신에게는 그가 자신의 날카로운 회한을 거리낌없이 말하는 편이 더 나았으니, 침묵 속에서는 그가 한없이 더 많은 것을 견뎌야 했기 때문이요, 때로는 너무도 오랫동안 팽팽하고 침울한 기분에 잠겨 자기 생각을 갉아먹으며 자신을 소진시켰던 까닭에, 그의 슬픔이 그 노력 속에서 어느 정도라도 발산되도록 그를 소리 내어 말하게 만드는 것이 반드시 필요했던 것이다.\n\n달빛을 바라본 뒤 유스테이샤가 집 안에 들어온 지 얼마 되지 않아 부드러운 발소리가 집으로 다가왔고, 아래층의 여자가 토머신이 왔음을 알렸다.\n\n\"아, 토머신! 오늘 밤 와줘서 고마워.\" 토머신이 방에 들어서자 클림이 말했다. \"보다시피 내 꼴이 이렇소. 워낙 비참한 몰골이라 친구 하나에게도 보이기가 움츠러들고, 당신한테조차 거의 그렇소.\"\n\n\"나한테 움츠러들면 안 돼요, 클림.\" 토머신이 진심을 담아, 흑굴 속으로 흘러드는 신선한 공기처럼 고통받는 이에게 다가오는 그 다정한 목소리로 말했다. \"당신의 그 무엇도 결코 나를 놀라게 하거나 내쫓을 수 없어요. 전에도 여기 왔었는데 기억 못 하시는 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니, 기억하오. 나는 정신 착란이 아니오, 토머신, 한 번도 그런 적 없었소. 누가 그렇게 말하더라도 믿지 마시오. 나는 그저 내가 저지른 일 때문에 큰 비탄에 잠겨 있을 뿐인데, 그게 이 쇠약함과 더해져서 내가 미친 사람처럼 보이게 하는 거요. 하지만 그게 내 이성을 흩뜨려놓지는 않았소. 내가 정신이 나갔다면 어머니의 죽음에 관한 모든 걸 다 기억할 것 같소? 그런 다행은 없소. 두 달 반 동안, 토머신, 생애 마지막 두 달 반을 우리 가엾은 어머니는 홀로, 나 때문에 미칠 듯이 슬퍼하며 사셨소. 그런데도 겨우 육 마일 떨어진 곳에 살던 나는 한 번도 찾아가지 않았소. 두 달 반—이레레 닷새 동안 해가 떴다 졌는데, 그 버림받은 처지는 개도 받을 만한 게 아니었소! 어머니와 아무런 인연도 없는 가난한 사람들이라도 어머니의 병과 외로움을 알았더라면 보살피고 찾아갔을 거요. 그런데 어머니께 모든 것이 되어드려야 했던 나는 똥개처럼 멀찍이 떨어져 있었소. 하느님께 정의가 있다면 지금 나를 죽이소서. 그분은 내 눈을 거의 멀게 하셨소, 하지만 그것으로는 부족하오. 그분이 내게 더 큰 고통을 내리시기만 한다면 나는 영원히 그분을 믿으리다!\"\n\n\"쉿, 쉿! 오, 제발, 클림, 그러지, 그런 말 하지 마요!\" 토머신이 흐느낌과 눈물에 사로잡혀 애원했으며, 방 저편의 유스테이샤는 창백한 얼굴은 평온함을 유지했으나 의자에 앉은 채 몸부림쳤다.\n클림은 사촌은 아랑곳하지 않고 말을 이어갔다.\n\n\"하지만 나는 하늘의 단죄를 더 입증받을 자격조차 없소. 토머신, 당신 생각엔 어머니가 나를 알아보셨을 것 같소—내가 어머니를 용서하지 않는다는 그 끔찍한 잘못된 생각, 어떻게 그런 생각을 품으셨는지 도무지 말할 수 없는 그 생각 속에서 돌아가신 게 아닐 것 같소? 그것만이라도 확신시켜준다면! 그렇게 생각하오, 유스테이샤? 어디 내게 말 좀 해보오.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 어머님이 마지막엔 더 잘 아셨다고 장담할 수 있어요.\" 토머신이 말했다.\n창백한 유스테이샤는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"어머니는 왜 내 집에 오지 않으셨을까? 나는 어머니를 받아들이고, 그 모든 일에도 불구하고 내가 얼마나 어머니를 사랑하는지 보여드렸을 텐데. 그런데 어머니는 끝내 오지 않으셨고, 나도 어머니께 가지 않았고, 어머니는 발길질당해 쫓겨난 짐승처럼 황야에서 돌아가셨소, 너무 늦을 때까지 도와줄 이 하나 없이. 토머신, 당신이 내가 본 대로 어머니를 보았더라면—맨땅 위 어둠 속에 누워 신음하는, 곁에 아무도 없이, 온 세상에게서 완전히 버림받았다고 믿는, 죽어가는 가엾은 여인을 보았더라면, 당신은 비통함에 사로잡혔을 거요, 짐승이라도 마음이 움직였을 거요. 그런데 이 가엾은 여인이 내 어머니라니! 어머니가 그 아이에게 '너는 가슴이 찢어진 여인을 보았다'라고 하신 것도 무리가 아니지. 그런 말을 하기까지 어머니가 어떤 지경에 이르셨겠소! 그렇게 만든 게 나 말고 누구겠소? 너무 끔찍해서 생각조차 할 수 없으니, 지금보다 더 무겁게 벌받을 수 있다면 좋겠소. 내가 소위 정신이 나가 있었다는 게 얼마나 됐소?\"\n\n\"일주일쯤이었던 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그러고 나서 내가 차분해졌고.\"\n\n\"네, 나흘 동안요.\"\n\n\"그러더니 이젠 더는 차분하지 못하게 됐군.\"\n\n\"하지만 조용히 있으려고 애써봐요—제발 그래요, 그러면 곧 기운을 차릴 거예요. 그 생각만 마음에서 떨쳐낼 수 있다면—\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 그가 조바심을 내며 말했다. \"하지만 나는 기운 차리고 싶지 않소. 내가 낫는 게 다 무슨 소용이오? 죽는 편이 내겐 더 나을 거고, 유스테이샤한테도 분명 더 나을 거요. 유스테이샤 거기 있소?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"내가 죽으면 당신한테 더 나을 거요, 유스테이샤?\"\n\n\"그런 걸 캐묻지 말아요, 클림.\"\n\n\"뭐, 사실 그저 허망한 가정일 뿐이지, 안타깝게도 나는 살아갈 테니까. 내가 차도가 있는 게 느껴지오. 토머신, 이번에 그 많은 돈이 당신 남편한테 들어왔으니, 여관에는 얼마나 더 머물 셈이오?\"\n\n\"아마 한두 달 더요, 제 병이 나을 때까지요. 그전엔 떠날 수가 없어요. 한 달이나 그 이상 걸릴 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래. 그렇겠지. 아, 사촌 탬지, 당신은 슬픔을 이겨낼 거요—한 달이면 그걸 헤쳐나가 위안이 될 무언가를 얻게 되겠지. 하지만 나는 결코 내 슬픔을 이겨내지 못할 거고, 어떤 위안도 오지 않을 거요!\"\n\n\"클림, 당신은 스스로에게 부당해요. 정말이지 숙모님은 당신을 다정하게 생각하셨어요. 살아 계셨더라면 당신과 화해하셨을 거예요, 저는 알아요.\"\n\n\"하지만 어머니는 나를 보러 오지 않으셨소, 내가 결혼하기 전에 와주시겠냐고 청했는데도 말이오. 어머니가 오셨거나 내가 거기 갔더라면, 어머니가 '나는 가슴이 찢어진 여인이다, 아들에게 버림받았다'라고 하시며 돌아가시지는 않았을 거요. 어머니께 내 문은 늘 열려 있었소—여기서는 늘 환대가 어머니를 기다리고 있었소. 그런데 어머니는 끝내 그것을 보러 오지 않으셨소.\"\n\n\"이제 그만 말씀하시는 게 좋겠어요, 클림.\" 방 저편에서 유스테이샤가 희미하게 말했으니, 그 장면이 그녀로서는 견딜 수 없어지고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"내가 여기 머무는 짧은 동안엔 대신 제가 얘기할게요.\" 토머신이 달래듯 말했다. \"당신이 이 일을 얼마나 한쪽으로 치우치게 보고 있는지 생각해봐요, 클림. 숙모님이 그 어린 소년에게 그 말을 하셨을 때 당신은 아직 숙모님을 찾아내 품에 안아드리지 못했어요. 그건 비통함의 한순간에 내뱉은 말일지도 몰라요. 숙모님은 좀 다급하게 말씀을 하시는 편이었어요. 제게도 가끔 그렇게 말씀하시곤 했어요. 비록 오시지는 않았어도, 저는 숙모님이 당신을 보러 올 생각을 하셨다고 확신해요. 어머니가 두세 달을 단 한 번의 용서하는 마음도 없이 사실 수 있다고 생각하세요? 숙모님은 저를 용서하셨어요, 그러니 당신을 용서하지 않으셨을 리가요?\"\n\n\"당신은 어머니의 마음을 돌리려고 애썼지만, 나는 아무것도 하지 않았소. 사람들에게 행복의 더 높은 비밀을 가르치려던 내가, 가장 못 배운 자들도 피할 만큼은 지혜로운 그 천박한 비참함에서 벗어나는 법조차 몰랐던 거요.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤은 어떻게 여기 왔어요, 토머신?\" 유스테이샤가 말했다.\n\n\"데이먼이 길 끝에서 내려줬어요. 일이 있어 이스트 에그던으로 마차를 몰고 갔는데, 이따가 와서 저를 태워 갈 거예요.\"\n\n과연 그 직후에 그들은 바퀴 소리를 들었다. 와일디브가 와서 말과 이륜마차를 데리고 밖에서 기다리고 있었다.\n\n\"제가 이 분 안에 내려가겠다고 사람을 내보내 전해주세요.\" 토머신이 말했다.\n\n\"제가 직접 내려갈게요.\" 유스테이샤가 말했다.\n\n그녀가 내려갔다. 와일디브는 마차에서 내려 말 머리 앞에 서 있었는데, 유스테이샤가 문을 열었다. 그는 잠시 돌아보지 않았으니, 온 사람이 토머신이라고 여겼던 것이다. 그러더니 그가 돌아보며 아주 조금 놀란 기색으로 한마디를 했다. \"어떻게 됐소?\"\n\n\"아직 그이한테 말 안 했어요.\" 그녀가 속삭이듯 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 그가 나을 때까지는 하지 마시오—치명적일 거요. 당신도 편치 않아 보이오.\"\n\n\"비참해요…. 오, 데이먼.\" 그녀가 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"내가—내가 얼마나 불행한지 당신한테 말로 다 할 수가 없어요! 이걸 도저히 견딜 수가 없어요. 내 괴로움을 아무한테도 말할 수 없어요—그걸 아는 사람은 당신밖에 없어요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 사람!\" 와일디브가 그녀의 비탄에 눈에 띄게 마음이 움직여 말했으며, 마침내 그녀의 손을 잡기에 이를 만큼 이끌려 들어갔다. \"당신이 그럴 만한 짓을 아무것도 하지 않았는데 이런 거미줄 같은 일에 얽혀들다니 가혹한 일이오. 당신은 이런 슬픈 장면에 어울리게 태어난 사람이 아니오. 누구보다 내 잘못이 크오. 이 모든 일에서 당신을 구해낼 수만 있었더라면!\"\n\n\"하지만, 데이먼, 제발 제가 어떻게 해야 할지 말해줘요. 그이 곁에 몇 시간이고 앉아 그이가 어머니의 죽음의 원인이 자기라고 자책하는 걸 듣고 있노라면, 그리고 사람이 조금이라도 죄인이라면 그게 바로 나라는 걸 알고 있노라면, 차디찬 절망 속으로 내몰려요. 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요. 그이한테 말해야 할까요, 말하지 말아야 할까요? 늘 스스로에게 그걸 묻고 있어요. 오, 말하고 싶어요, 그런데도 두려워요. 그이가 알게 되면 분명 나를 죽일 거예요, 지금 그이의 감정에 걸맞은 건 다른 그 무엇도 아닐 테니까요. '참는 사람의 분노를 조심하라'라는 말이, 그이를 지켜보는 내 귀에 날마다 울려요.\"\n\n\"음, 그가 나아질 때까지 기다렸다가 운에 맡기시오. 그리고 말할 때는 일부만 말해야 하오—그를 위해서라도.\"\n\n\"어느 부분을 숨겨야 하죠?\"\n\n와일디브가 잠시 머뭇거렸다. \"그때 내가 그 집 안에 있었다는 것 말이오.\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그건 숨겨야겠지요, 사람들이 수군거린 걸 생각하면. 성급한 행동이 그것을 변명할 말보다 얼마나 더 쉬운지!\"\n\n\"그가 죽기만 한다면—\" 와일디브가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그런 생각 마요! 설령 내가 그이를 미워한다 해도, 그토록 비겁한 바람으로 화를 면할 희망을 사들이지는 않을 거예요. 이제 그이한테 다시 올라갈게요. 토머신이 몇 분 안에 내려간다고 전해달라고 했어요. 안녕히.\"\n\n그녀가 돌아갔고, 곧 토머신이 나타났다. 그녀가 남편과 함께 이륜마차에 앉고 말이 돌아서 떠나려 할 때, 와일디브가 침실 창문 쪽으로 눈을 들었다. 그중 한 창에서 자기가 마차를 몰고 떠나는 것을 지켜보는 창백하고 비극적인 얼굴을 알아볼 수 있었다. 유스테이샤의 얼굴이었다.\n\n클림의 슬픔은 스스로 소진되어가며 누그러졌다. 기운이 돌아왔고, 토머신이 다녀간 지 한 달 후에는 그가 정원을 거니는 모습을 볼 수 있게 되었다. 인내와 절망, 평정과 우수, 건강의 빛깔과 죽음의 창백함이 그의 얼굴 위에 기이하게 뒤섞여 있었다. 그는 이제 어머니와 관련된 과거의 모든 일에 대해 부자연스러우리만치 입을 다물었으며, 유스테이샤는 그가 여전히 그것을 생각하고 있음을 알면서도 그 화제에서 벗어날 수 있어 너무도 다행스러운 나머지 그것을 새삼 다시 꺼내지 않았다. 정신이 더 약했을 때는 마음이 그를 털어놓게 했으나, 이제 이성이 다소 회복되자 그는 과묵 속으로 가라앉았다.\n\n어느 날 저녁 그가 이렇게 정원에 서서, 지팡이로 잡초를 멍하니 파헤치고 있을 때, 뼈만 앙상한 한 형체가 집 모퉁이를 돌아 그에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"크리스천, 자넨가?\" 클림이 말했다. \"나를 찾아와줘서 반갑네. 곧 자네한테 블룸스엔드에 가서 집 정리를 도와달라고 부탁할 참이었네. 내가 떠날 때 그대로 다 잠가둔 채겠지?\"\n\n\"네, 클림 도련님.\"\n\n\"감자랑 다른 뿌리들은 캐냈고?\"\n\n\"네, 비 한 방울 안 맞히고요, 다행스럽게도요. 그런데 그것 말고 다른 걸 말씀드리러 온 거예요, 요새 우리 집안에 있었던 일하고는 영 다른 일이지요. 우리가 여관 주인이라고 부르던 우먼의 그 부자 양반이 절 보내서 전하라고 했는데요, 와일디브 부인이 딸을 무사히 낳으셨대요, 정오 한 시 정각에, 아니면 몇 분 더 되거나 덜 되거나 해서 태어났다는데요. 그분들이 돈이 들어온 뒤로 거기 머물렀던 게 바로 이 식구 늘 일을 기다려서라는 얘기예요.\"\n\n\"그래서 부인은 잘 회복하고 계신다는 거지?\"\n\n\"네, 도련님. 다만 와일디브 씨가 아들이 아니라서 좀 시무룩하시다지요—부엌에서들 하는 말이긴 한데, 제가 그걸 알아챘다고는 안 하기로 했어요.\"\n\n\"크리스천, 이제 내 말 들어보게.\"\n\n\"네, 그럼요, 요브라이트 도련님.\"\n\n\"우리 어머니 돌아가시기 전날 우리 어머니를 뵈었나?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 못 뵈었어요.\"\n\n요브라이트의 얼굴에 실망이 드러났다.\n\n\"하지만 돌아가시던 바로 그날 아침엔 뵈었어요.\"\n\n클림의 표정이 환해졌다. \"그게 내 말뜻에 더 가깝군.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 같은 날이었던 게 분명해요, 마님이 '나는 그 애를 보러 갈 참이다, 크리스천, 그러니 저녁 찬거리는 들여올 것 없다'라고 하셨거든요.\"\n\n\"누구를 본단 거지?\"\n\n\"도련님을요. 도련님 댁으로 가시는 길이었던 거예요, 아시겠지요.\"\n\n요브라이트는 강렬한 놀라움으로 크리스천을 바라보았다. \"왜 이걸 한 번도 얘기하지 않았나?\" 그가 말했다. \"어머니가 오시려던 곳이 내 집이었다는 게 확실해?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요. 요새 도련님을 못 뵈어서 말씀을 못 드린 거예요. 그리고 마님이 거기 닿지도 못하셨으니 다 헛일이 됐고, 말씀드릴 것도 없었던 거고요.\"\n\n\"그래서 나는 어머니가 왜 그 더운 날 황야를 걸으셨을까 의아해하고 있었는데! 그래, 어머니가 무엇 때문에 오신다고 하셨나? 크리스천, 그건 내가 꼭 알고 싶은 일이네.\"\n\n\"네, 클림 도련님. 저한텐 말씀 안 하셨는데, 여기저기 한두 사람한텐 하신 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"어머니가 그 얘기를 하신 사람을 한 명이라도 아나?\"\n\n\"한 사람 있어요, 도련님, 그런데 제 이름은 그 사람한테 말하지 말아주셨으면 해요, 제가 그 사람을 이상한 곳에서, 특히 꿈속에서 봤거든요. 지난여름 어느 날 밤엔 그가 기근과 칼처럼 저를 노려봐서 어찌나 기가 죽었던지 이틀이나 제 몇 가닥 안 되는 머리도 안 빗었지 뭐예요. 그가, 말하자면, 요브라이트 도련님, 미스토버로 가는 길 한복판에 서 있었는데, 마님이 다가오셨고, 어찌나 창백해 보이시던지—\"\n\n\"그래, 그게 언제였나?\"\n\n\"지난여름요, 제 꿈속에서요.\"\n\n\"쳇! 그 사람이 누군가?\"\n\n\"디고리요, 그 레들맨이요. 마님이 도련님을 보러 떠나기 전날 저녁에 그가 찾아와 마님과 함께 앉아 있었어요. 그가 문간으로 올라올 때 저는 아직 일에서 돌아오지 않았었고요.\"\n\n\"벤을 만나봐야겠군—진작 알았더라면 좋았을 것을.\" 클림이 초조하게 말했다. \"그 사람은 왜 나한테 말하러 오지 않았을까?\"\n\n\"그 사람이 이튿날 에그던 황야를 떠났거든요, 그러니 도련님이 자길 찾는다는 걸 알 리가 없었지요.\"\n\n\"크리스천,\" 클림이 말했다. \"자네가 가서 벤을 찾아주게. 나는 달리 일이 있으니, 아니면 내가 직접 가겠네만. 당장 찾아내서 내가 얘기하고 싶어한다고 전해주게.\"\n\n\"낮에 사람 찾아내는 일이라면 제가 솜씨가 좋지요.\" 크리스천이 기우는 햇빛을 미심쩍게 둘러보며 말했다. \"그런데 밤이 되면, 저만큼 서툰 사람도 없답니다, 요브라이트 도련님.\"\n\n\"언제든 황야를 뒤지게나, 그를 곧 데려오기만 한다면. 할 수 있거든 내일 데려오게.\"\n\n그리하여 크리스천은 떠났다. 이튿날이 왔지만 벤은 오지 않았다. 저녁에 크리스천이 몹시 지친 모습으로 돌아왔다. 그는 온종일 찾아다녔으나 레들맨에 관해서는 아무것도 듣지 못했다.\n\n\"일을 소홀히 하지 않는 선에서 내일 되도록 많이 수소문해보게.\" 요브라이트가 말했다. \"그를 찾기 전엔 다시 오지 말게.\"\n\n이튿날 요브라이트는 블룸스엔드의 옛집으로 떠났으니, 그 집은 정원과 함께 이제 그의 소유가 되어 있었다. 그의 중병이 그곳으로 이사할 모든 준비를 가로막았으나, 어머니의 작은 재산의 관리인으로서 그가 가서 그 안의 물건들을 살펴보아야 할 필요가 생겼던 것이며, 그 목적을 위해 그는 다음 날 밤을 그 집에서 보내기로 했다.\n\n그는 빠르거나 단호한 걸음이 아니라, 정신이 멍한 잠에서 깨어난 사람의 느린 걸음으로 길을 나아갔다. 그가 골짜기에 다다른 것은 이른 오후였다. 그곳의 표정도, 그 시각의 색조도, 지나간 날들의 그러한 수많은 순간들과 정확히 똑같았으며, 이 앞선 유사함들이, 이제 그곳에 더는 없는 그녀가 밖으로 나와 그를 맞아줄 것 같은 환상을 키웠다. 정원 문은 잠겨 있었고 덧창들은 닫혀 있었으니, 장례식 다음 날 저녁 그 자신이 떠날 때 그대로였다. 그가 문을 따 보니, 거미 한 마리가 다시는 그 문이 열리지 않으리라 짐작하고서 문을 문틀에 묶어두는 커다란 거미줄을 벌써 쳐놓았다. 집 안에 들어가 덧창을 다시 젖힌 그는, 찬장과 벽장을 샅샅이 뒤지고 서류를 태우며, 그가 오래 미뤄둔 계획을 실행할 처지가 될 때까지—그런 때가 과연 온다면—유스테이샤를 맞이하기에 가장 좋도록 어떻게 집을 꾸밀지 궁리하는 일에 착수했다.\n\n여러 방을 둘러보면서 그는, 유스테이샤의 현대적인 취향에 맞추려고 부모와 조부모의 유서 깊은 가구들에 가해야 할 변경에 강한 거부감을 느꼈다. 문짝 판에 승천 그림이, 받침대에 기적의 고기잡이 그림이 그려진 그 앙상한 오크 상자 시계며, 점박이 자기가 들여다보이는 유리문이 달린 할머니의 모퉁이 찬장이며, 덤웨이터며, 나무 찻쟁반들이며, 놋쇠 꼭지가 달린 매달린 물통—이 유서 깊은 물건들을 대체 어디로 추방해야 한단 말인가?\n\n그는 창가의 꽃들이 물이 없어 시들어버린 것을 알아챘고, 가져가버릴 수 있도록 그것들을 선반 위로 내놓았다. 이렇게 분주히 일하는 동안 그는 밖의 자갈길에서 발소리를 들었고, 누군가 문을 두드렸다.\n\n요브라이트가 문을 열자 벤이 그 앞에 서 있었다.\n\n\"안녕하세요.\" 레들맨이 말했다. \"요브라이트 부인 계신가요?\"\n\n요브라이트는 땅바닥을 내려다보았다. \"그럼 크리스천이나 에그던 사람 누구도 못 만났소?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"못 만났습니다. 오래 떠나 있다가 막 돌아온 참이라서요. 떠나기 전날 여기 들렀었지요.\"\n\n\"그러면 아무것도 못 들었소?\"\n\n\"아무것도요.\"\n\n\"우리 어머니가—돌아가셨소.\"\n\n\"돌아가셨다고요!\" 벤이 기계적으로 말했다.\n\n\"이제 어머니의 집은, 나도 내 집을 거기 두어도 좋겠다 싶은 그곳이오.\"\n\n벤이 그를 바라보더니 말했다. \"도련님 얼굴을 보지 않았다면 그 말씀을 도무지 믿을 수 없었을 겁니다. 앓으셨나요?\"\n\n\"한 차례 앓았소.\"\n\n\"그런데 이런 변고가! 제가 한 달 전 마님과 헤어질 땐 모든 게 마님이 새 삶을 시작하시려는 것처럼 보였는데요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그렇게 보이던 것이 사실이 되었소.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀이십니다, 틀림없이요. 슬픔이 도련님께 저보다 더 깊은 말솜씨를 가르쳤군요. 제가 말씀드린 건 그저 마님이 이곳에서 사실 삶에 관한 거였습니다. 마님은 너무 일찍 돌아가셨어요.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 내가 너무 오래 살아서일지도 모르지. 지난 한 달 동안 나는 그 점에 관해 쓰라린 경험을 했소, 디고리. 하지만 들어오시오. 당신을 만나고 싶었소.\"\n\n그는 레들맨을 데리고, 지난 크리스마스에 춤판이 벌어졌던 그 큰 방으로 들어가, 함께 긴 의자에 앉았다. \"저 차가운 벽난로가 보이오.\" 클림이 말했다. \"저 반쯤 탄 장작과 저 잿더미에 불이 붙어 있었을 때 어머니는 살아 계셨소! 여긴 아직 바뀐 게 거의 없소. 나는 아무것도 할 수가 없소. 내 삶은 달팽이처럼 기어가오.\"\n\n\"마님이 어쩌다 돌아가셨습니까?\" 벤이 말했다.\n\n요브라이트는 그에게 어머니의 병과 죽음에 관한 몇 가지 사정을 들려준 뒤 말을 이었다. \"이 일을 겪고 나니 이제 그 어떤 고통도 내겐 가벼운 병치레 이상으로 여겨지지 않을 거요. 내가 뭔가 물어보고 싶다고 말을 꺼내놓고는 술 취한 사람처럼 화제를 벗어나고 있군. 어머니가 당신을 마지막으로 보셨을 때 무슨 말씀을 하셨는지 꼭 알고 싶소. 어머니와 한참 이야기를 나눴겠지?\"\n\n\"반 시간도 더 이야기를 나눴습니다.\"\n\n\"나에 관해서?\"\n\n\"네. 그리고 마님이 황야에 나와 계셨던 건 우리가 나눈 그 얘기 때문이었던 게 분명합니다. 의심할 바 없이 마님은 도련님을 보러 오시던 길이었어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 나를 그토록 모질게 여기셨다면 왜 나를 보러 오셨겠소? 거기에 수수께끼가 있소.\"\n\n\"그래도 마님이 도련님을 완전히 용서하셨다는 건 제가 압니다.\"\n\n\"하지만, 디고리—아들을 완전히 용서한 여인이, 그 아들의 집으로 가는 길에 몸이 아파지자, 아들의 학대 때문에 가슴이 찢어졌다고 말하겠소? 결코 아니지!\"\n\n\"제가 아는 건 마님이 도련님을 전혀 탓하지 않으셨다는 겁니다. 마님은 일어난 일을 두고 자기 자신을, 오직 자기 자신만을 탓하셨어요. 마님 입으로 직접 들은 얘기예요.\"\n\n\"내가 어머니를 학대하지 않았다는 걸 당신은 어머니 입으로 들었고, 동시에 다른 사람은 내가 어머니를 학대했다는 걸 어머니 입으로 들었다? 우리 어머니는 까닭 없이 매시간 의견을 바꾸는 충동적인 분이 아니셨소. 어떻게, 벤, 어머니가 그렇게 짧은 간격을 두고 그토록 다른 얘기를 하셨을 수 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"저도 모르겠습니다. 도련님을 용서하시고, 도련님 부인까지 용서하시고, 화해하려고 일부러 도련님을 보러 가시던 길이었으니, 분명 이상한 일이긴 합니다.\"\n\n\"나를 어리둥절하게 만들 한 가지가 더 필요했다면 바로 이 도무지 알 수 없는 일이었소!… 디고리, 만약 우리, 살아남은 자들이 죽은 자들과 대화를 나누는 것이 허락된다면—단 한 번, 단 일 분만이라도, 감옥에 있는 사람과 그러하듯 쇠창살 너머로라도—우리가 무엇을 알아낼 수 있겠소! 지금 웃으며 활보하는 자들 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 고개를 숨기겠소! 그리고 이 수수께끼—나는 단번에 그 밑바닥까지 닿을 텐데. 하지만 무덤이 어머니를 영원히 가두어버렸으니, 이제 어찌 그것을 밝혀낸단 말이오?\"\n\n그의 동행은 아무런 대답도 하지 않았으니, 아무런 대답도 할 수 없었기 때문이며, 몇 분 후 벤이 떠났을 때 클림은 슬픔의 무딘 상태에서 마음을 갉아먹는 불확실함의 동요로 넘어가 있었다.\n\n그는 오후 내내 그 상태로 머물렀다. 이웃 사람이 그가 다음 날 다시 돌아오지 않아도 되도록 같은 집에 잠자리를 마련해주었으며, 그 적막한 곳에서 그가 잠자리에 들었을 때, 그는 똑같은 생각을 곱씹으며 시간이 가도록 깨어 있을 뿐이었다. 이 죽음의 수수께끼에 대한 해답을 어떻게 찾아낼 것인가가, 살아 있는 자들의 가장 높은 문제들보다 더 중대한 물음으로 여겨졌다. 그의 기억 속에는, 클림의 어머니가 누워 있던 오두막으로 들어설 때의 어린 소년의 얼굴이 또렷한 그림으로 깃들어 있었다. 그 둥근 눈과 간절한 눈빛과, 그 말들을 또박또박 내뱉던 가느다란 목소리가, 그의 뇌리에 단검처럼 박혀들었다.\n\n그 소년을 찾아가는 것이, 비록 아무 소득이 없을지라도, 새로운 사정을 얻어낼 방편으로 떠올랐다. 여섯 주가 지난 뒤에 아이의 마음을, 아이가 보고 이해한 사실들을 캐내려는 게 아니라 본질상 아이의 능력 너머에 있는 것들을 얻어내려고 더듬는 일이 큰 성과를 기약하지는 못했으나, 뻔한 길목이 모두 막혀 있을 때 우리는 작고 희미한 것을 향해 더듬어 나아가는 법이다. 달리 할 일은 아무것도 남아 있지 않았으니, 그러고 나면 그는 이 수수께끼가 알아낼 수 없는 것들의 심연 속으로 떨어지도록 내버려두려 했다.\n\n그가 이 결심에 이른 것은 동틀 무렵이었고, 그는 곧바로 일어났다. 집을 잠그고 나서 그는, 좀 더 가면 히스 들판으로 이어지는 푸른 풀밭으로 나섰다. 흰 정원 울타리 앞에서 길은 넓은 화살촉처럼 세 갈래로 갈라졌다. 오른쪽 길은 콰이어트 우먼과 그 인근으로 이어졌고, 가운데 길은 미스토버 냅으로 이어졌으며, 왼쪽 길은 언덕을 넘어 그 아이가 사는 미스토버의 다른 지역으로 이어졌다. 마지막 길로 접어들면서 요브라이트는 대부분의 사람들에게 익히 친숙한, 그리고 아마도 햇볕이 들지 않은 아침 공기 탓이었을, 스멀스멀 기어드는 한기를 느꼈다. 훗날 그는 그것을 유난히 의미심장한 일로 떠올렸다.\n\n요브라이트가 그가 찾던 소년의 어머니인 수전 넌서치의 오두막에 다다랐을 때, 안의 식구들은 아직 일어나지 않은 모양이었다. 하지만 고지대의 작은 마을에서는 잠자리에서 바깥으로의 전환이 놀라우리만치 빠르고도 수월하다. 거기서는 하품과 몸단장의 빽빽한 칸막이가 밤의 인류와 낮의 인류를 갈라놓지 않는다. 요브라이트는 지팡이로 닿을 수 있는 위층 창턱을 두드렸고, 서너 분 만에 여자가 내려왔다.\n\n클림이 그녀가 유스테이샤에게 그토록 야만적으로 군 바로 그 사람임을 떠올린 것은 이 순간에 이르러서였다. 그 사실이 여자가 그를 맞이하는 무뚝뚝함을 어느 정도 설명해주었다. 게다가 소년이 또 앓고 있었으며, 수전은 모닥불 자리에서 그가 유스테이샤의 시중에 떠밀려 들었던 그날 밤 이후로 늘 그래왔듯이, 아이의 병치레를 마녀로서의 유스테이샤의 영향 탓으로 돌렸다. 그것은 예의범절의 보이는 표면 아래 두더지처럼 숨어 있는 그런 감정 중 하나였으며, 대장이 교회에서의 바늘 찌르기 일로 수전을 고발하려던 무렵 유스테이샤가 그 일을 그냥 덮어달라고 간청해 그가 그에 따라 그렇게 해주었던 까닭에 명맥을 유지해온 것일 수도 있었다.\n\n요브라이트는 거부감을 누그러뜨렸으니, 수전은 적어도 그의 어머니에게는 아무런 악감정도 품지 않았기 때문이다. 그는 다정하게 소년의 안부를 물었으나, 그녀의 태도는 나아지지 않았다.\n\n\"그 애를 보고 싶소.\" 요브라이트가 다소 머뭇거리며 말을 이었다. \"그 애가 우리 어머니와 걸었던 일에 대해, 전에 얘기한 것 말고 더 기억하는 게 있는지 물어보려고요.\"\n\n그녀가 묘하고도 따지는 듯한 태도로 그를 바라보았다. 반쯤 눈먼 사람이 아니고서야 누구라도 그것이 이렇게 말하는 것임을 알았으리라. \"당신은 이미 당신을 그토록 쓰러뜨린 매질을 한 번 더 맞고 싶은 게로군.\"\n\n그녀가 소년을 아래층으로 불러 클림에게 걸상에 앉으라 하고는 말을 이었다. \"자, 조니, 요브라이트 씨한테 네가 떠올릴 수 있는 건 뭐든 다 말씀드려라.\"\n\n\"네가 그 더운 날 그 가엾은 부인과 함께 걸었던 거 잊지 않았지?\" 클림이 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 소년이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그분이 너한테 뭐라고 하셨는지도?\"\n\n소년은 오두막에 들어설 때 자기가 했던 바로 그 말들을 되풀이했다. 요브라이트는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 얼굴을 가렸으며, 어머니는 한 사내가 그토록 깊이 자기를 찔러놓은 일을 어떻게 더 듣고 싶어할 수 있는지 의아해하는 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n\"네가 그분을 처음 만났을 때 그분은 올더워스로 가시던 길이었니?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 떠나오시던 길이었어요.\"\n\n\"그럴 리가 없어.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 그분이 저랑 같이 걸으셨어요. 저도 떠나오던 길이었거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그분을 어디서 처음 봤니?\"\n\n\"도련님 댁에서요.\"\n\n\"잘 듣고, 사실대로 말해라!\" 클림이 엄하게 말했다.\n\n\"네, 도련님, 도련님 댁에서 그분을 처음 봤어요.\"\n\n클림이 벌떡 일어났고, 수전은 그 얼굴을 돋보이게 하지 못하는, 무언가를 기대하는 듯한 미소를 지었으니, 그것은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"불길한 일이 다가오고 있어!\"\n\n\"그분이 내 집에서 무얼 하셨니?\"\n\n\"악마의 풀무 옆 나무들 아래로 가서 앉으셨어요.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 이건 죄다 처음 듣는 얘기로군!\"\n\n\"넌 나한테 이걸 한 번도 얘기 안 했잖니?\" 수전이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 엄마, 제가 그렇게 멀리 갔었다고 말하기가 싫어서요. 검은딸기를 따다가 생각보다 멀리 갔던 거예요.\"\n\n\"그러고 나서 그분이 무얼 하셨니?\" 요브라이트가 말했다.\n\n\"한 남자가 올라와서 도련님 댁으로 들어가는 걸 보셨어요.\"\n\n\"그게 나였다—가시덤불을 손에 든 가시금작화 베는 사람이었지.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 도련님이 아니었어요. 신사였어요. 도련님은 앞서 들어가셨고요.\"\n\n\"그게 누구였지?\"\n\n\"몰라요.\"\n\n\"이제 그다음에 무슨 일이 있었는지 말해보렴.\"\n\n\"그 가엾은 부인이 가서 도련님 댁 문을 두드리셨는데, 검은 머리의 부인이 옆 창문으로 그분을 내다봤어요.\"\n\n소년의 어머니가 클림을 돌아보며 말했다. \"이건 당신이 예상 못 한 일이지요?\"\n\n요브라이트는 돌멩이라도 된 듯 그녀를 더는 거들떠보지 않았다. \"계속해라, 계속해.\" 그가 소년에게 쉰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그 젊은 부인이 창문으로 내다보는 걸 보고서 그 나이 든 부인이 또 문을 두드리셨어요. 그래도 아무도 안 나오니까 가시금작화 낫을 집어 들어 들여다보고는 도로 내려놓으시고, 그러고는 나뭇단 묶음을 보시더니, 가버리셨고, 저한테 걸어오셔서, 이렇게, 숨을 아주 가쁘게 내쉬셨어요. 우리는, 그분이랑 저랑, 같이 걸어갔고, 제가 그분한테 얘기하고 그분도 저한테 얘기를 좀 하셨는데, 많이는 못 하셨어요, 숨을 쉴 수가 없으셔서요.\"\n\n\"오!\" 클림이 낮은 목소리로 중얼거리며 고개를 떨구었다. \"더 들어보자.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"많이 얘기도 못 하시고, 걷지도 못하셨어요. 그리고 얼굴이, 오, 어찌나 이상하던지요!\"\n\n\"얼굴이 어땠는데?\"\n\n\"지금 도련님 얼굴 같았어요.\"\n\n여자가 요브라이트를 바라보니, 그는 핏기 하나 없이 식은땀에 젖어 있었다. \"여기에 뜻이 있지 않겠어요?\" 그녀가 슬그머니 말했다. \"이제 그 여자를 어떻게 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"조용히 하시오!\" 클림이 사납게 말했다. 그러고는 소년을 돌아보며 \"그러고 나서 너는 그분을 죽도록 내버려두었니?\"\n\n\"아뇨.\" 여자가 재빠르고 성난 목소리로 말했다. \"이 애는 그분을 죽도록 내버려두지 않았어요! 그분이 이 애를 돌려보내신 거예요. 이 애가 그분을 버렸다고 하는 자는 사실이 아닌 말을 하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그건 더 신경 쓰지 마시오.\" 클림이 떨리는 입으로 대답했다. \"이 애가 한 일은 이 애가 본 것에 비하면 사소하오. 문이 닫혀 있었다고 했지? 닫혀 있었고, 그 여자가 창문으로 내다보고 있었다고? 자비로우신 하느님!—이게 무슨 뜻이란 말이오?\"\n\n아이가 자기를 캐묻는 사람의 시선에서 움츠러들었다.\n\n\"이 애가 그렇게 말했어요.\" 어머니가 대답했다. \"그리고 조니는 하느님을 두려워하는 아이라 거짓말은 안 해요.\"\n\n\"'아들에게 버림받았다!' 아니요, 내 가장 소중한 목숨을 걸고, 어머니, 그렇지 않습니다! 하지만 당신의 아들에게는, 당신의 아들에게는—모든 살인하는 여자들이 마땅히 받아야 할 형벌을 받을지어다!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 요브라이트는 그 작은 거처에서 밖으로 나왔다. 텅 빈 허공에 한결같이 못 박힌 그의 두 눈동자는 얼음 같은 광채로 어렴풋이 빛났으며, 그의 입은 오이디푸스 연구에서 다소간 상상적으로 표현된 그 모습으로 옮아가 있었다. 그의 기분이라면 가장 기이한 행위들도 가능했다. 하지만 그것들은 그의 처지에서는 불가능했다. 그의 앞에는 유스테이샤의 창백한 얼굴과 알 수 없는 남자의 형체가 있는 것이 아니라, 그저 황야의 흔들림 없는 표정만이 있었으니, 그 황야는 수백 년에 걸친 격변의 습격을 막아내며, 주름지고 태고와도 같은 그 모습으로 한 인간의 그 가장 사나운 혼란을 하찮은 것으로 깎아내렸다.\n\n자기를 에워싼 모든 것 속에 깃든 광대한 무감각함에 대한 자각이, 올더워스를 향한 그 미친 듯한 걸음 속에서 요브라이트마저 사로잡았다. 그는 전에 한 번 무생물이 열정적인 것을 짓누르는 이 느낌을 제 몸으로 느낀 적이 있었으나, 그때 그것은 지금 그를 가득 채운 것보다 훨씬 더 달콤한 열정을 무디게 하는 쪽으로 작용했다. 그것은 언젠가 그가 언덕 너머 축축하고 고요한 평지에서 유스테이샤와 헤어지며 서 있을 때였다. 하지만 이 모든 것을 떨치고 그는 집으로 나아가, 자기 집 앞에 다다랐다. 유스테이샤의 침실 블라인드는 여전히 꼭 닫혀 있었으니, 그녀는 일찍 일어나는 사람이 아니었던 것이다. 눈에 보이는 살아 있는 것이라곤, 문간 돌 위에서 작은 달팽이를 깨뜨려 아침으로 삼고 있는 외로운 개똥지빠귀 한 마리뿐이었고, 사방에 감도는 적막 속에서 그 톡톡거리는 소리가 큰 소음처럼 들렸다. 하지만 문으로 가보니 클림은 그것이 잠겨 있지 않음을 알았으니, 유스테이샤의 시중을 드는 어린 소녀가 집 뒤편에서 부산을 떨고 있었던 것이다. 요브라이트는 안으로 들어가 곧장 아내의 방으로 향했다.\n\n그가 도착한 소리에 그녀가 깨어났던 게 분명하니, 그가 문을 열었을 때 그녀는 잠옷 차림으로 거울 앞에 서서, 한 손에 머리끝을 모아 쥐고는 단장을 시작하기에 앞서 그 머리채 전부를 머리 둘레로 둘둘 감고 있었다. 그녀는 마주칠 때 먼저 말을 거는 여자가 아니었기에, 고개를 돌리지 않은 채 클림이 말없이 걸어 들어오게 두었다. 그가 그녀의 뒤로 다가왔고, 그녀는 거울 속에서 그의 얼굴을 보았다. 그것은 잿빛에 초췌하고 끔찍했다. 비밀로 자신을 옭아매기 전이었다면, 감정을 잘 드러내지 않는 아내인 유스테이샤조차 그러했을 법한, 슬픈 놀라움에 차서 그를 향해 몸을 돌리는 대신, 그녀는 미동도 없이 거울 속의 그를 바라보았다. 그리고 그녀가 바라보는 동안, 온기와 깊은 잠으로 그녀의 두 뺨과 목에 번져 있던 진홍빛 홍조가 시야에서 사라지고, 그의 얼굴에 어린 죽음 같은 창백함이 그녀의 얼굴로 옮아 날아들었다. 그는 이것을 볼 만큼 가까이 있었고, 그 광경이 그의 혀를 부추겼다.\n\n\"당신은 무슨 일인지 알고 있군.\" 그가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"당신 얼굴에서 그게 보이오.\"\n\n그녀의 손이 머리채를 놓아 옆구리로 떨어졌고, 더는 받쳐지지 않은 머리타래 더미가 머리 꼭대기에서 흘러내려 그녀의 어깨와 흰 잠옷 위로 쏟아졌다. 그녀는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"내게 말해.\" 요브라이트가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n그녀에게서 핏기 가시는 과정이 멈추지 않았고, 이제 그녀의 입술도 얼굴만큼이나 하얘졌다. 그녀가 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"그래요, 클림, 당신한테 말할게요. 왜 이렇게 일찍 돌아왔어요? 제가 뭐 해드릴 게 있나요?\"\n\n\"그래, 당신은 내 말을 들을 수 있지. 보아하니 내 아내가 별로 편치 않은가 보군?\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"당신 얼굴 말이오, 여보, 당신 얼굴. 아니면 어쩌면 창백한 아침 빛이 당신의 핏기를 앗아가는 건가? 자, 이제 당신한테 비밀을 하나 밝히려 하오. 하하!\"\n\n\"오, 그건 소름 끼쳐요!\"\n\n\"뭐가?\"\n\n\"당신 웃음이요.\"\n\n\"소름 끼칠 만한 까닭이 있지. 유스테이샤, 당신은 내 행복을 손바닥에 쥐고 있었으면서, 악마처럼 그것을 내동댕이쳐버렸어!\"\n\n그녀가 화장대에서 뒷걸음질 치며 그에게서 몇 걸음 물러나 그의 얼굴을 정면으로 바라보았다. \"아! 당신은 나를 겁주려는 거군요.\" 그녀가 살짝 웃으며 말했다. \"그게 그럴 만한 가치가 있어요? 나는 무방비에, 혼자예요.\"\n\n\"참으로 별일이군!\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이에요?\"\n\n\"시간이 넉넉하니 말해주지, 당신도 충분히 알고 있겠지만. 내 말은, 내가 없는 동안 당신이 혼자였다는 게 별일이라는 거요. 자, 어디 말해보지, 팔월 삼십일일 오후에 당신과 함께 있던 자는 어디 있소? 침대 밑에? 굴뚝 위에?\"\n\n전율이 그녀를 덮쳐 그녀의 잠옷의 얇은 천을 온통 흔들었다. \"저는 날짜를 그렇게 정확히 기억하지 못해요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신 말고 누가 저와 함께 있었던 건 기억나지 않아요.\"\n\n\"내가 말하는 그날은,\" 요브라이트의 목소리가 점점 커지고 거칠어지며 말했다. \"당신이 우리 어머니를 문전박대해 죽인 날이오. 오, 너무하군—너무 끔찍해!\" 그는 잠시 그녀에게 등을 돌린 채 침대 발치에 기댔다가, 다시 몸을 일으키며—\"말해, 말해! 말하란 말이야—듣고 있어?\" 그가 외치며 그녀에게 달려들어 헐렁한 소매 자락을 움켜쥐었다.\n\n마음속으로는 대담하고 반항적인 이들을 흔히 덮고 있는 그 소심함의 표층이 뚫리고, 이 여자의 기개 넘치는 본바탕에 가닿았다. 앞서 그토록 창백했던 그녀의 얼굴로 붉은 피가 밀려들었다.\n\n\"뭘 하려는 거예요?\" 그녀가 낮은 목소리로, 당당한 미소를 띠고 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"그렇게 붙잡는다고 나를 겁줄 수는 없어요. 그래도 내 소매를 찢는 건 아까운 일이겠네요.\"\n\n그는 놓아주기는커녕 그녀를 자기 쪽으로 더 바싹 끌어당겼다. \"우리 어머니의—죽음의 자초지종을 말해.\" 그가 모질고도 헐떡이는 속삭임으로 말했다. \"안 그러면—내가—내가—\"\n\n\"클림,\" 그녀가 천천히 대답했다. \"당신은 내가 견디지 못할 짓을 나한테 감히 할 수 있을 것 같아요? 하지만 나를 치기 전에 들어요. 나를 친다고, 설령 그게 나를 죽인다고 해도—아마 그렇게 되겠지만—당신은 나한테서 아무것도 얻지 못할 거예요. 하지만 어쩌면 당신은 내가 말하길 바라지 않는지도 모르죠—죽이는 게 당신이 바라는 전부일지도 모르니까요?\"\n\n\"당신을 죽인다고! 그걸 기대하나?\"\n\n\"기대해요.\"\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"나를 향한 어떤 격분도, 그만큼 덜한 정도로는 어머니를 잃은 당신의 앞선 슬픔에 걸맞지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"흥—나는 당신을 죽이지 않겠어.\" 그가 갑자기 마음을 바꾼 듯 경멸하듯 말했다. \"그러려고 생각은 했지만—하지 않겠어. 그건 당신을 순교자로 만들어 어머니가 계신 곳으로 보내는 일일 테고, 나는 할 수만 있다면 우주가 끝장날 때까지 당신을 어머니에게서 떼어놓겠어.\"\n\n\"차라리 당신이 나를 죽여주면 좋겠어요.\" 그녀가 침울한 비통함으로 말했다. \"장담하건대, 제가 요사이 이 땅에서 맡아온 역할을 무슨 강한 욕망에서 하고 있는 게 아니에요. 당신은 내게 축복이 아니에요, 내 남편.\"\n\n\"당신은 문을 닫았고—창문으로 어머니를 내다봤고—집 안에 한 남자를 들이고 있었고—어머니를 돌려보내 죽게 했어. 그 비인간성—그 배신—나는 당신한테 손대지 않겠어—내게서 떨어져—그러고는 한마디도 빠짐없이 자백해!\"\n\n\"절대로! 나는 그 죽음처럼 입을 다물겠어요, 비록 말함으로써 당신이 믿는 것의 절반은 결백을 밝힐 수 있다 해도, 나는 그 죽음을 만나는 것조차 두렵지 않아요. 그래요, 그러겠어요! 이런 말을 듣고서, 자존심 있는 사람이라면 누가 거친 사내의 마음에서 거미줄을 걷어내는 수고를 하겠어요? 아니요, 그가 계속하라죠, 자기 옹졸한 생각이나 하고, 진창에 머리나 처박으라죠. 나한텐 다른 근심이 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"너무하군—하지만 당신을 봐줘야겠어.\"\n\n\"가엾은 자비로군요.\"\n\n\"이 비참한 내 영혼을 걸고, 당신은 나를 찌르는군, 유스테이샤! 나는 계속할 수 있어, 그것도 격렬하게. 자, 그럼, 부인, 그자의 이름을 말해!\"\n\n\"절대로, 나는 결심했어요.\"\n\n\"그자가 당신한테 얼마나 자주 편지를 쓰지? 어디다 그 편지를 두지—언제 당신을 만나지? 아, 그자의 편지들! 그자의 이름을 말해줄 텐가?\"\n\n\"안 해요.\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 직접 찾아내지.\" 그의 눈이 그녀가 편지를 쓰곤 하던, 근처에 놓인 작은 책상에 가닿았다. 그가 그리로 갔다. 책상은 잠겨 있었다.\n\n\"이걸 열어!\"\n\n\"당신한텐 그렇게 말할 권리가 없어요. 그건 내 거예요.\"\n\n그는 더 말하지 않고 책상을 움켜쥐어 바닥에 내동댕이쳤다. 경첩이 떨어져 나가며 여러 통의 편지가 쏟아져 나왔다.\n\n\"멈춰요!\" 유스테이샤가 지금까지 보였던 것보다 더한 흥분에 차서 그의 앞을 가로막으며 말했다.\n\n\"이리, 이리! 비켜! 내가 봐야겠어.\"\n\n그녀는 놓여 있는 편지들을 바라보다가 감정을 억누르고 무심한 듯 옆으로 비켜섰으며, 그러자 그가 편지들을 그러모아 살펴보았다.\n\n편지 그 자체로는, 어떻게 의미를 늘여 잡아도 단 한 통에조차 해 될 것 없는 해석 말고는 달리 붙일 수가 없었다. 단 하나의 예외는 그녀 앞으로 부쳐진 빈 봉투였는데, 필적은 와일디브의 것이었다. 요브라이트가 그것을 들어 올렸다. 유스테이샤는 완강하게 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"글을 읽을 줄 아오, 부인? 이 봉투를 보시오. 분명 우리는 곧 더 많은 걸, 그 안에 들어 있던 걸 찾아내게 되겠지. 나는 우리 마님께서 어떤 장사에서 얼마나 완성도 높고 만개한 명수名手이신지를 머지않아 알게 되어 틀림없이 흡족하겠군.\"\n\n\"나한테 그런 말을 해요—그러는 거예요?\" 그녀가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다.\n\n그는 더 뒤졌으나 더는 아무것도 찾지 못했다. \"이 편지엔 뭐가 들어 있었지?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"쓴 사람한테 물어봐요. 내가 당신 사냥개라서 나한테 이런 식으로 말하는 거예요?\"\n\n\"나한테 맞서는 거요? 나한테 버티는 거요, 마님? 대답해. 또다시 나를 홀리려거든 그 눈으로 나를 보지 마! 그러느니 차라리 내가 죽겠어. 대답하기를 거부하나?\"\n\n\"이런 일이 있고서는, 내가 천국에서 가장 사랑스러운 갓난아기만큼 결백하다 해도, 당신한테는 말하지 않겠어요!\"\n\n\"당신은 그렇지 않은데도 말이지.\"\n\n\"전적으로 결백한 건 아니죠, 분명히.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"당신이 짐작하는 짓은 하지 않았어요. 하지만 아무런 해도 끼치지 않은 것만이 인정받는 결백이라면, 나는 용서받을 길이 없겠죠. 하지만 나는 당신 양심의 도움 따윈 필요 없어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 버티고, 또 버틸 수 있군! 당신이 뉘우치고 모든 걸 자백하기만 한다면, 나는 당신을 미워하기는커녕 슬퍼하고 가엾이 여길 수도 있을 것 같소. 하지만 용서는 결코 할 수 없어. 당신의 정부情夫 얘기를 하는 게 아니오—그 문제는 나 개인에게만 관계되는 일이니 의심의 이득은 당신에게 주리다. 하지만 다른 문제는—당신이 나를 반쯤 죽였다 해도, 당신이 일부러 이 약한 두 눈에서 시력을 앗아갔다 해도, 나는 당신을 용서할 수 있었을 거요. 하지만 그건 사람의 본성으론 너무하단 말이오!\"\n\n\"더 말하지 마요. 나는 당신의 동정 없이도 살아갈게요. 하지만 나는 당신이 후회할 말을 내뱉지 않도록 막아주고 싶었어요.\"\n\n\"나는 이제 떠나겠어. 당신을 두고 가겠어.\"\n\n\"당신이 갈 필요 없어요, 내가 직접 갈 테니까요. 여기 머물든 떠나든 당신은 나한테서 꼭 그만큼 멀리 떨어져 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"어머니를 떠올려봐—생각해봐—어머니 안에 얼마나 많은 선함이 있었는지를—그게 얼굴 한 줄 한 줄마다 드러났었지! 대부분의 여자는 살짝만 짜증이 나도 입가의 어떤 비틀림이나 뺨의 어떤 구석에서 악의의 깜박임을 드러내는데, 어머니로 말하자면, 가장 화나신 순간에도 그 눈빛엔 악의라곤 결코 없었어. 어머니는 금세 화를 내셨지만 그만큼 선뜻 용서하셨고, 그 자존심 밑에는 아이 같은 온순함이 있었지. 그런데 결과가 어찌 되었소?—당신이 무얼 마음에 두었소? 어머니가 막 당신을 사랑하려던 참에 당신은 어머니를 미워했어. 오! 당신은 무엇이 당신한테 최선인지를 보지 못하고, 기어이 그 잔인한 짓을 저질러 나한테는 저주를, 어머니한테는 고통과 죽음을 안겨주어야 했단 말이오! 당신과 한패가 되어 당신이 나한테 한 잘못에 어머니한테까지 잔인하게 굴도록 부추긴 그 작자의 이름이 뭐였소? 와일디브였소? 가엾은 토머신의 남편이었소? 맙소사, 이 무슨 사악함이오! 목소리를 잃었소? 그 더없이 고결한 농간이 들통난 뒤이니 당연하겠지…. 유스테이샤, 당신 자신의 어머니에 대한 다정한 생각이 그토록 지친 때에 우리 어머니한테 부드럽게 대하도록 당신을 이끌지 못했소? 어머니가 돌아서실 때 한 톨의 연민도 당신 마음에 들어오지 않았소? 그때 용서하고 정직한 길로 들어설 그 얼마나 큰 기회를 놓쳤는지 생각해보시오. 왜 그자를 걷어차 내쫓고 어머니를 들이며, 이 순간부터 정직한 아내요 고결한 여자가 되겠다고 말하지 않았소? 내가 당신한테 우리의 마지막 깜박이는 행복의 기회를 여기서 영원히 꺼버리라고 시켰다 해도 당신은 이보다 더 나쁘게 할 수 없었을 거요. 자, 어머니는 이제 잠들어 계시니, 당신한테 백 명의 정부가 있다 한들 그들도 당신도 더는 어머니를 모욕할 수 없소.\"\n\n\"당신은 끔찍하게 과장하고 있어요.\" 그녀가 희미하고 지친 목소리로 말했다. \"하지만 나는 변명에 나설 수가 없어요—그럴 가치가 없으니까요. 당신은 앞으로 내게 아무것도 아니고, 그 이야기의 지나간 쪽은 끝내 말하지 않은 채로 두는 게 차라리 낫겠어요. 나는 당신 때문에 모든 걸 잃었지만 불평하지 않았어요. 당신의 실책과 불운은 당신한테는 슬픔이었을지 몰라도 나한테는 부당함이었어요. 내가 결혼이라는 진창에 빠진 뒤로 교양 있는 사람들은 죄다 나한테서 겁먹고 달아나버렸어요. 이게 당신의 보살핌인가요—나를 이런 오두막에 처박아 농사꾼의 아내처럼 가두는 게? 당신은 나를 속였어요—말로가 아니라 겉모습으로, 말보다 덜 꿰뚫어 보이는 겉모습으로요. 하지만 이곳도 다른 어떤 곳 못지않게 쓸모가 있겠죠—거쳐 가는 어딘가로—내 무덤으로 가기 위한.\" 그녀의 말이 목구멍에서 짓눌리고, 그녀의 고개가 떨구어졌다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠군. 내가 당신 죄의 원인이란 말이오?\" (유스테이샤가 그를 향해 떨리는 동작으로 다가섰다.) \"뭐, 당신이 눈물을 흘리며 나한테 손을 내밀려는 거요? 맙소사! 당신이? 안 돼, 나는 안 받아. 그 손을 잡는 잘못은 저지르지 않겠어.\" (그녀가 내밀었던 손이 맥없이 떨어졌으나, 눈물은 계속 흘렀다.) \"음, 그래, 잡지, 단지 내가 무얼 소중히 여기는지도 모른 채 저기서 허비해버린 그 어리석은 입맞춤들을 봐서라도. 내가 얼마나 홀려 있었던가! 모두가 험담하던 여자한테 어찌 선함이 있을 수 있었겠소?\"\n\n\"오, 오, 오!\" 그녀가 마침내 무너지며 부르짖었으며, 목이 메도록 흐느낌에 떨면서 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉았다. \"오, 이제 그만해줘요! 오, 당신은 너무 무자비해요—야만인의 잔인함에도 한계가 있어요! 나는 오래 버텼어요—그런데 당신이 나를 짓밟아 눕히는군요. 자비를 빌어요—더는 이걸 견딜 수가 없어요—이걸 더 끌고 가는 건 사람의 짓이 아니에요! 내가 당신의—어머니를—내 손으로—죽였다 해도—이렇게 뼛속까지 후려치는 매질을 받을 만하진 않을 거예요. 오, 오! 하느님, 이 비참한 여자에게 자비를 베푸소서!… 당신은 이 시합에서 나를 이겼어요—제발 가엾이 여겨 손을 멈춰줘요!… 고백할게요, 내가—그분이 처음 문을 두드렸을 때 일부러 문을 열지 않았다는 걸—하지만—두 번째엔 열었을 거예요—당신이 직접 열러 가신 줄 알지 않았더라면요. 당신이 안 그랬다는 걸 알았을 때 내가 문을 열었지만, 그분은 이미 가버리셨어요. 그게 내 죄의 전부예요—그분한테 지은. 더없이 선한 본성도 때로는 나쁜 잘못을 저지르잖아요, 안 그래요?—그렇다고 생각해요. 이제 나는 당신을 떠나겠어요—영원히, 영원히!\"\n\n\"전부 말하시오, 그러면 내가 당신을 가엾이 여기리다. 당신과 함께 집 안에 있던 남자가 와일디브였소?\"\n\n\"말 못 해요.\" 그녀가 흐느낌 사이로 절박하게 말했다. \"더 다그치지 마요—말 못 해요. 나는 이 집을 떠날 거예요. 우리 둘 다 여기 머물 수는 없어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 갈 필요 없소—내가 가겠어. 당신은 여기 있어도 돼.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 옷을 차려입고서 내가 가겠어요.\"\n\n\"어디로?\"\n\n\"내가 온 곳으로, 아니면 다른 어딘가로요.\"\n\n그녀는 서둘러 옷을 차려입었고, 요브라이트는 그동안 내내 침울하게 방안을 서성였다. 마침내 그녀의 차림이 다 갖춰졌다. 보닛을 매려고 턱에 갖다 댄 그녀의 작은 두 손이 너무도 격렬하게 떨려 끈을 묶을 수가 없었고, 몇 순간이 지나자 그녀는 그 시도를 단념했다. 이를 본 그가 다가서며 말했다. \"내가 묶어주지.\"\n\n그녀가 말없이 응하고는 턱을 들어올렸다. 그녀의 일생에서 적어도 한 번은, 그녀가 자기 자세의 매력을 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 하지만 그는 그렇지 않았기에, 부드러움에 빠져들 유혹을 받지 않으려고 눈을 옆으로 돌렸다.\n\n끈이 묶였고, 그녀가 그에게서 돌아섰다. \"내가 당신을 떠나기보다는 여전히 당신이 직접 떠나기를 바라오?\" 그가 다시 물었다.\n\n\"바라요.\"\n\n\"좋소—그렇게 하지. 그리고 당신이 그 남자에게 자백하거든 그때는 당신을 가엾이 여길지도 모르오.\"\n\n그녀가 숄을 두르고 아래층으로 내려갔으며, 그는 방안에 선 채로 남겨졌다.\n\n유스테이샤가 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않아 침실 문에서 노크 소리가 났고, 요브라이트가 말했다. \"무슨 일인가?\"\n\n하녀였고, 그녀가 대답했다. \"와일디브 부인 댁에서 누가 와서 전하라고 했는데요, 마님과 아기가 놀라우리만치 잘 지내고 있고, 아기 이름은 유스테이샤 클레먼타인이 될 거래요.\" 그러고는 소녀가 물러갔다.\n\n\"이런 조롱이 다 있나!\" 클림이 말했다. \"내 이 불행한 결혼이 그 아이의 이름으로 영원히 이어지다니!\"\n\n유스테이샤의 여정은 처음엔 바람에 날리는 엉겅퀴 솜털만큼이나 방향이 모호했다. 그녀는 무엇을 해야 할지 몰랐다. 그녀는 차라리 아침이 아니라 밤이었더라면 좋았으리라 바랐으니, 그러면 적어도 남에게 보일 가능성 없이 자기 비참함을 견딜 수 있었을 것이다. 죽어가는 양치류와 젖은 흰 거미줄 사이로 한 마일 또 한 마일을 더듬어 가며, 그녀는 마침내 할아버지의 집으로 발길을 돌렸다. 앞문이 닫혀 잠겨 있는 것을 그녀는 발견했다. 기계적으로 그녀는 마구간이 있는 끝 쪽으로 돌아갔고, 마구간 문 안을 들여다보니 찰리가 안에 서 있는 것이 보였다.\n\n\"바이 대장님은 댁에 안 계시니?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 마님.\" 소년이 마음의 동요에 휩싸여 말했다. \"웨더베리에 가셨는데, 밤이 되어야 돌아오실 거예요. 그리고 하인은 휴가로 집에 갔고요. 그래서 집은 잠겨 있어요.\"\n\n유스테이샤가 문간에 서 있어 등 뒤로 하늘을 두고 있었고 마구간도 어슴푸레하게밖에 밝지 않았던 까닭에, 그녀의 얼굴은 찰리에게 보이지 않았으나, 그녀의 거친 태도가 그의 주의를 사로잡았다. 그녀는 돌아서서 울안을 가로질러 문 쪽으로 걸어가더니 둑에 가려 보이지 않게 되었다.\n\n그녀가 사라지자 찰리는 눈에 불안을 담고서 천천히 마구간 문에서 나와, 둑의 다른 지점으로 가서 너머를 살펴보았다. 유스테이샤가 바깥쪽에서 둑에 기대어 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채, 둑의 바깥쪽 면에 수염처럼 돋은 이슬 젖은 히스에 머리를 짓누르고 있었다. 그녀는 자기 보닛과 머리와 옷가지가 그 차갑고 거친 베개의 습기에 젖어 헝클어지고 있다는 사정에 완전히 무심한 듯 보였다. 분명 무언가 잘못된 것이었다.\n\n찰리는 늘 유스테이샤를, 유스테이샤가 클림을 처음 보았을 때 그를 여겼던 것과 똑같이 여겨왔으니—거의 육신을 입지 않은, 낭만적이고 감미로운 환영으로 여겼던 것이다. 그녀의 손을 잡도록 허락받았던 그 단 한 번의 더없이 행복한 시간을 빼고는, 그녀의 눈빛이 지닌 위엄과 말투의 자존심에 가로막혀 그녀와 너무도 단절되어 있었던 까닭에, 그는 그녀를 살림살이의 조건과 가정의 불화에 매인, 날개도 없고 지상의 것인 여자라고는 거의 여기지 않았다. 그녀의 삶의 내밀한 속사정은 그저 짐작만 했을 뿐이었다. 그녀는 사랑스러운 경이驚異였으며, 그 자신의 삶 전부가 한낱 점에 지나지 않는 어떤 궤도를 돌도록 예정된 존재였으니, 거칠고 젖은 둑에 무력하고 절망한 피조물처럼 기대어 있는 그녀의 이 모습이 그를 아연한 공포로 가득 채웠다. 그는 더는 있던 자리에 머물러 있을 수 없었다. 뛰어넘어 다가가서, 그가 손가락으로 그녀를 건드리며 부드럽게 말했다. \"편찮으신가 봐요, 마님. 제가 뭘 해드릴까요?\"\n\n유스테이샤가 흠칫 일어나며 말했다. \"아, 찰리—네가 나를 따라왔구나. 내가 여름에 집을 떠날 때 이런 꼴로 돌아오리라고는 너도 생각 못 했겠지!\"\n\n\"못 했어요, 마님. 지금 제가 도와드릴 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"안 될 것 같구나. 집 안으로 들어갈 수 있으면 좋겠는데. 어지러워서—그뿐이야.\"\n\n\"제 팔을 잡으세요, 마님, 현관까지 가게요, 그러면 제가 문을 열어보겠어요.\"\n\n그는 그녀를 부축해 현관까지 데려가, 그곳 자리에 그녀를 앉히고는 서둘러 뒤편으로 가서 사다리를 디뎌 창문으로 올라가, 안으로 내려가 문을 열었다. 다음으로 그는 그녀를 부축해 방으로 들여보냈는데, 그곳에는 당나귀 짐수레만큼이나 큼직한 옛날식 말총 긴 의자가 있었다. 그녀는 거기 누웠고, 찰리는 현관에서 찾아낸 외투로 그녀를 덮어주었다.\n\n\"뭐 먹고 마실 걸 가져다드릴까요?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그래주렴, 찰리. 그런데 불이 없겠지?\"\n\n\"제가 피울 수 있어요, 마님.\"\n\n그가 사라지자 그녀는 장작 쪼개는 소리와 풀무 부는 소리를 들었고, 이윽고 그가 돌아와 말했다. \"부엌에 불을 피웠고, 이제 여기도 피울게요.\"\n\n그가 불을 피웠고, 유스테이샤는 자기 의자에서 몽롱하게 그를 바라보았다. 불이 활활 타오르자 그가 말했다. \"아침이 쌀쌀하니, 제가 마님을 불 앞으로 돌려 모실까요?\"\n\n\"그래, 네가 그러고 싶으면.\"\n\n\"이제 가서 음식을 가져올까요?\"\n\n\"그래, 그러렴.\" 그녀가 나른하게 중얼거렸다.\n\n그가 가고 난 뒤, 부엌에서 그가 움직이는 둔탁한 소리가 이따금 그녀의 귀에 닿았으나, 그녀는 자기가 어디에 있는지 잊어, 잠시 동안 애써 그 소리가 무얼 뜻하는지 헤아려야 했다. 마음이 딴 데 가 있는 그녀에게는 짧게 느껴진 한참 후에, 그가 김이 모락모락 나는 차와 토스트를 얹은 쟁반을 들고 들어왔으니, 점심때가 다 되어 있었다.\n\n\"탁자에 놓아두렴.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"곧 준비할게.\"\n\n그가 그렇게 하고는 문 쪽으로 물러났으나, 그녀가 움직이지 않는 것을 알아채고는 몇 걸음 되돌아왔다.\n\n\"일어나기 싫으시면, 제가 들고 있을게요.\" 찰리가 말했다. 그는 쟁반을 긴 의자 앞으로 가져와 거기 무릎을 꿇으며 덧붙였다. \"제가 마님 대신 들고 있을게요.\"\n\n유스테이샤가 일어나 앉아 찻잔에 차를 따랐다. \"넌 나한테 참 친절하구나, 찰리.\" 그녀가 차를 홀짝이며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"음, 마땅히 그래야지요.\" 그가 수줍게, 유스테이샤가 바로 앞에 있었으니 그게 두 눈의 유일하게 자연스러운 자리였는데도, 그녀에게 눈길을 두지 않으려고 무진 애를 쓰며 말했다. \"마님은 저한테 친절하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게 그랬는데?\" 유스테이샤가 말했다.\n\n\"마님이 처녀로 집에 계실 때 제가 마님 손을 잡게 해주셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"아, 그랬지. 내가 왜 그랬을까? 정신이 다 나가버렸네—그 가면극하고 관련된 거였지, 아니었니?\"\n\n\"네, 마님이 제 대신 들어가고 싶어 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"기억나. 정말이지 기억나—너무도 또렷하게!\"\n\n그녀는 다시 완전히 풀이 죽어버렸고, 찰리는 그녀가 더는 먹지도 마시지도 않으리라는 것을 보고서 쟁반을 치웠다.\n\n그 후 그는 이따금 들어와서 불이 잘 타고 있는지 살피고, 그녀에게 무언가 필요한 게 있는지 묻고, 바람이 남쪽에서 서쪽으로 바뀌었다고 알려주고, 검은딸기를 좀 따다 드릴까 물었으나, 이 모든 물음에 그녀는 아니라고 하거나 무심하게 대꾸했다.\n\n그녀는 그 긴 의자에 한참을 더 머물러 있다가, 정신을 차려 위층으로 올라갔다. 예전에 그녀가 자던 방은 그녀가 떠날 때와 거의 그대로였으며, 이것이 그녀에게 강제로 떠올리게 한, 자신이 처한 크게 달라지고 한없이 나빠진 상황에 대한 회상이, 처음 도착했을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 어렸던 그 막연하고 형체 없는 비참함을 그녀의 얼굴에 다시 새겨놓았다. 그녀는 할아버지의 방을 들여다보았는데, 열린 창문으로 신선한 가을 공기가 그 방을 가로질러 불어들고 있었다. 그녀의 눈길이, 익히 친숙한 광경이긴 했으나\n\n이제 그녀에게 새로운 의미를 띠고 다가왔다.\n그것은 한 쌍의 권총으로, 할아버지의 침대 머리맡에 걸려 있었으며, 집이 워낙 외진 탓에 침입자가 들지도 모른다는 대비책으로 늘 장전해둔 채 그곳에 두던 것이었다. 유스테이샤는 마치 새롭고 낯선 사연이 적힌 책장이라도 들여다보듯 그것을 오랫동안 바라보았다. 이윽고 제 자신이 두렵기라도 한 사람처럼 황급히 아래층으로 내려와 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 섰다.\n\n\"내가 그걸 할 수만 있다면!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나 자신에게도, 나와 얽힌 모든 사람에게도 크게 이로울 것이요, 어느 누구에게도 해가 되지 않을 텐데.\"\n\n그 생각은 그녀 안에서 힘을 키워가는 듯했고, 그녀는 거의 십 분 가까이 한자리에 붙박인 채 서 있었는데, 마침내 그녀의 눈빛에는 더 이상 결단을 못 내리는 멍한 기색이 아니라 어떤 확고한 결말이 어려 있었다.\n\n그녀는 돌아서서 두 번째로 위층에 올라갔다—이번에는 살그머니 몰래—할아버지의 방으로 들어서며 즉시 침대 머리맡으로 눈길을 보냈다. 권총은 사라지고 없었다.\n\n그것이 없어 자신의 의도가 단숨에 좌절되자 마치 갑작스러운 진공이 몸에 미치듯 그녀의 머릿속도 영향을 받아—그녀는 거의 정신을 잃을 뻔했다. 누가 이런 짓을 했단 말인가? 이 집 안에 그녀 말고 사람은 단 한 명뿐이었다. 유스테이샤는 자기도 모르게 정원을, 정원을 두른 둑까지 내려다보이는 열린 창문 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 그 둑 꼭대기에 찰리가 서 있었는데, 둑의 높이 덕분에 방 안이 들여다보일 만큼 충분히 올라가 있었다. 그의 시선은 간절하고 근심 어린 빛으로 그녀에게 쏠려 있었다.\n\n그녀는 아래층 문가로 내려가 그에게 오라고 손짓했다.\n\n\"네가 그것들을 치웠지?\"\n\n\"예, 아씨.\"\n\n\"왜 그랬느냐?\"\n\n\"아씨가 그걸 너무 오래 들여다보시는 걸 봤어요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 상관이란 말이냐?\"\n\n\"아침 내내 아씨는 가슴이 미어지신 듯, 살고 싶지 않으신 것처럼 보였어요.\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"그래서 그걸 아씨 손 닿는 곳에 두고 차마 떠날 수가 없었어요. 아씨가 그걸 보시는 눈빛에 어떤 뜻이 담겨 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"지금 그것들은 어디 있느냐?\"\n\n\"잠가뒀어요.\"\n\n\"어디에?\"\n\n\"마구간에요.\"\n\n\"내게 다오.\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 아씨.\"\n\n\"거절하는 게냐?\"\n\n\"그래요. 아씨를 너무 아끼니까 내드릴 수 없어요.\"\n\n그녀는 고개를 돌렸고, 그녀의 얼굴은 그날 아침의 돌처럼 굳은 무표정에서 처음으로 누그러졌으며, 절망의 순간이면 늘 사라지고 말던 그 섬세한 윤곽이 입꼬리에 다시금 어렸다. 마침내 그녀는 다시 그를 마주했다.\n\n\"내가 죽고 싶다면 죽지 못할 게 뭐란 말이냐?\" 그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"나는 삶과 잘못된 거래를 맺었고, 이제 그것에 지쳤어—지쳤다고. 그런데 네가 내 탈출을 막은 거야. 오, 찰리, 너는 어쩌자고! 남들이 슬퍼하리라는 생각 말고 죽음을 고통스럽게 하는 게 무엇이겠느냐?—그런데 내 경우엔 그것조차 없으니, 내 뒤를 따라올 한숨 하나 없을 테니까!\"\n\n\"아아, 이 모든 게 그 고통 탓이에요! 이걸 입에 담는 게 유형감에 처할 일이라 해도, 이 일을 꾸민 그자가 죽어 썩어버리기를 영혼 깊이 바라요!\"\n\n\"찰리, 그런 말은 그만하거라. 네가 본 이 일을 어찌할 셈이냐?\"\n\n\"아씨가 다시는 그런 생각 안 하겠다 약속하시면, 밤처럼 비밀로 묻어두겠어요.\"\n\n\"걱정할 것 없다. 그 순간은 지나갔으니. 약속하마.\" 그러고는 그녀는 자리를 떠나 집 안으로 들어가 자리에 누웠다.\n\n오후 늦게 그녀의 할아버지가 돌아왔다. 그는 그녀에게 조목조목 캐물으려 했으나, 그녀를 바라보고는 말을 거두었다.\n\n\"네, 입에 담기엔 너무 끔찍한 일이에요.\" 그녀는 그의 눈길에 대답하듯 천천히 받았다. \"할아버지, 오늘 밤 제 옛 방을 다시 쓸 수 있게 준비해주실 수 있어요? 다시 그 방을 쓰고 싶어요.\"\n\n그는 이 모든 게 무슨 영문인지, 그녀가 왜 남편을 떠났는지 묻지 않고, 방을 준비하도록 일렀다.\n\nV.\n무심코 되풀이된 옛 수\n\n옛 주인을 향한 찰리의 정성은 끝이 없었다. 그 자신의 괴로움을 달랠 유일한 위안은 그녀의 괴로움을 덜어주려는 노력에 있었다. 시간이 가고 또 가도 그는 그녀에게 필요한 것을 헤아렸으며, 그녀가 그곳에 있다는 사실을 일종의 감사하는 마음으로 떠올렸고, 그녀의 불행을 일으킨 원인을 저주하면서도 그 결과만큼은 어느 정도 축복했다. 어쩌면 그녀가 늘 이곳에 머물지도 모른다고, 그러면 자기도 예전처럼 행복해지리라고 그는 생각했다. 그가 두려워한 것은 그녀가 올더워스로 돌아가는 게 마땅하다고 여기는 일이었으며, 그 두려움 속에서 그의 눈은, 애정 특유의 온갖 살핌을 담은 채, 그녀가 자기를 보지 않을 때면 자주 그녀의 얼굴을 좇았으니, 마치 산비둘기가 날아오를 궁리를 하는지 알아보려 그 머리를 지켜보듯 했다. 한 번 정말로 그녀를 구해냈고, 어쩌면 가장 무모한 짓에서 그녀를 지켜냈기에, 그는 마음속으로 그녀의 안녕에 대한 보호자의 책임까지 덧붙여 떠맡았다.\n\n이런 까닭에 그는 그녀에게 즐거운 소일거리를 마련해주려 부지런히 애썼으니, 황야에서 찾아낸 진귀한 물건들—나팔 모양의 흰 이끼며, 머리가 붉은 지의류며, 옛적 에그던의 부족들이 쓰던 돌화살촉이며, 부싯돌 구멍에서 나온 결정면이 진 수정 따위를 집으로 가져왔다. 이것들을 그는 그녀가 우연인 듯 보게끔 집 안 곳곳 적당한 자리에 놓아두었다.\n\n한 주가 지나도록 유스테이샤는 한 번도 집 밖으로 나가지 않았다. 그러던 어느 날 그녀는 울타리를 두른 땅으로 걸어 나가 할아버지의 망원경으로 내다보았으니, 결혼 전에 늘 하던 버릇 그대로였다. 어느 날 그녀는 큰길이 멀리 골짜기를 가로지르는 지점에서 짐을 잔뜩 실은 마차가 지나가는 것을 보았다. 마차에는 살림살이가 수북이 쌓여 있었다. 그녀는 보고 또 보다가, 그것이 자기 것임을 알아보았다. 저녁이 되자 할아버지가 집 안으로 들어오며, 요브라이트가 그날 올더워스에서 블룸스엔드의 옛집으로 이사했다는 소문을 전했다.\n\n또 한 번은 그렇게 망원경으로 살피다가 골짜기를 걸어가는 두 여인의 모습을 보았다. 날은 맑고 화창했으며, 그들이 반 마일도 채 떨어지지 않은 터라 그녀는 망원경으로 그들의 세세한 모습까지 다 볼 수 있었다. 앞서 걷는 여자는 흰 보따리를 두 팔에 안고 있었는데, 그 한쪽 끝에서는 길게 늘어진 천자락이 드리워져 있었으며, 두 사람이 몸을 돌려 햇빛이 더 곧장 그들 위로 떨어지자 유스테이샤는 그 물건이 아기임을 알아볼 수 있었다. 그녀는 이미 짐작은 했으면서도 찰리를 불러, 저들이 누구인지 아느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"와일디브 부인하고 보모 아이예요.\" 찰리가 말했다.\n\n\"보모가 아기를 안고 있는 게냐?\" 유스테이샤가 말했다.\n\n\"아뇨, 그건 와일디브 부인이 안고 있는 거고요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"보모는 뒤에서 아무것도 안 들고 걷고 있어요.\"\n\n그날 그 소년은 기분이 좋았으니, 십일월 오일이 다시 돌아온 까닭에, 그녀를 너무 골똘한 생각에서 돌려놓을 또 다른 계획을 짜고 있던 것이었다. 두 해 잇따라 그의 주인 아씨는 골짜기가 내려다보이는 둑에 모닥불을 피우는 것을 즐기는 듯했으나, 올해는 그 날도 그 으레 하던 일도 까맣게 잊은 모양이었다. 그는 그녀에게 일깨우지 않으려 조심하면서, 즐거운 깜짝 선물을 마련하려는 은밀한 채비를 이어갔으니, 지난번에는 자기가 자리를 비워 거들지 못했던 터라 더욱 열심이었다. 짬이 날 때마다 그는 부지런히 가까운 비탈에서 가시금작화 그루터기며, 가시나무 뿌리며, 그 밖의 단단한 땔감을 그러모아, 얼핏 봐서는 보이지 않게 감추어 두었다.\n\n저녁이 되었으나 유스테이샤는 여전히 그 기일을 까맣게 잊은 듯했다. 그녀는 망원경으로 둘러본 뒤 집 안으로 들어갔고, 그 후로는 모습을 보이지 않았다. 사방이 완전히 어두워지자마자 찰리는 모닥불을 쌓기 시작했으니, 바로 유스테이샤가 예전에 골랐던 그 둑 위의 자리를 똑같이 골랐다.\n\n주위의 모닥불이 죄다 살아나 타오르자 찰리도 제 불을 지폈으며, 한동안 손볼 일이 없도록 땔감을 잘 쌓아두었다. 그러고는 집으로 돌아가, 그녀가 어떻게든 자기가 해낸 일을 알아채고 나와서 보아주기를 바라며 문과 창문 언저리를 서성였다. 그러나 덧창은 닫혀 있었고, 문도 닫힌 채였으며, 그가 한 일에는 아무런 관심도 두지 않는 듯했다. 그녀를 부르기는 내키지 않아 그는 다시 가서 불을 돋우었으며, 반 시간 넘게 그 일을 거듭했다. 땔감이 거의 다 떨어지고 나서야 그는 뒷문으로 가서, 요브라이트 부인이 창의 덧창을 열고 바깥 광경을 보아주십사 청하는 말을 안으로 들여보냈다.\n\n응접실에서 맥없이 앉아 있던 유스테이샤는 그 기별에 벌떡 일어나 덧창을 활짝 열어젖혔다. 둑 위에서 그녀를 마주 보며 불이 타오르고 있었으니, 그것은 즉시 그녀가 있는 방 안으로 불그레한 불빛을 던져 넣어 촛불을 압도했다.\n\n\"잘했다, 찰리!\" 굴뚝 모퉁이에서 바이 선장이 말했다. \"허나 그놈이 태우는 게 내 장작이 아니길 바라네…… 아, 그러고 보니 작년 이맘때였지, 그 베너라는 사내가 토머신 요브라이트를 집으로 데려오는 걸 만난 게—정말 그랬지! 그래, 그 처녀의 풍파가 그리 잘 끝날 줄 누가 알았겠나? 그 일에서 너는 어찌 그리 멍청했던지, 유스테이샤! 네 남편한테서 이제 편지는 왔느냐?\"\n\n\"아뇨.\" 유스테이샤가 창 너머로 불을 멍하니 바라보며 말했으니, 그 불이 마침 그녀의 마음을 너무도 사로잡은 터라 그녀는 할아버지의 무뚝뚝한 평에도 발끈하지 않았다. 그녀는 둑 위에서 삽질을 하며 불을 쑤석이는 찰리의 모습을 볼 수 있었으며, 그 불이 불러낼지도 모를 또 다른 어떤 모습이 문득 그녀의 상상 속에 번뜩였다.\n\n그녀는 방을 나와 정원용 보닛과 망토를 걸치고 밖으로 나갔다. 둑에 이르러 그녀가 격한 호기심과 불안을 안고 너머를 살피자, 찰리가 자기 자신이 흐뭇한 듯 그녀에게 말했다. \"아씨를 위해 일부러 피운 거예요.\"\n\n\"고맙구나.\" 그녀가 황급히 말했다. \"허나 이제 그걸 꺼주었으면 한다.\"\n\n\"곧 사그라들 거예요.\" 찰리가 다소 실망한 듯 말했다. \"꺼버리기엔 아깝지 않아요?\"\n\n\"모르겠다.\" 그녀가 골똘히 대답했다.\n\n두 사람은 불꽃이 타닥거리는 소리 말고는 아무것도 깨뜨리지 않는 침묵 속에 서 있었으나, 이윽고 그녀가 자기와 이야기하고 싶어하지 않음을 알아챈 찰리가 마지못해 자리를 떴다.\n\n유스테이샤는 집 안으로 들어가려 마음먹으면서도 여전히 머뭇거리며, 둑 안쪽에 남아 불을 바라보았다. 그녀가 처한 처지로 말미암아 신들과 인간들이 떠받드는 온갖 것을 무심히 여기는 마음이 들지 않았더라면, 아마 그녀는 그 자리를 떠났으리라. 그러나 그녀의 처지는 너무도 가망 없어 그것을 가지고 놀 수도 있을 정도였다. 잃어버린 것은, 어쩌면 이겼을지도 모른다고 의아해하는 것보다 덜 어지러운 법이니, 이제 유스테이샤도 그런 처지에 놓인 다른 이들처럼 자기 바깥에 발판을 두고, 짐짓 무관심한 구경꾼처럼 자기를 바라보며, 이 유스테이샤라는 여인이 하늘에게는 얼마나 좋은 노리개인가 생각할 수 있었다.\n\n그렇게 서 있는데 어떤 소리가 들렸다. 연못에 돌이 떨어지며 첨벙이는 소리였다.\n\n유스테이샤가 그 돌을 가슴 한복판에 정통으로 맞았더라도 그녀의 심장이 그보다 더 확연히 쿵 뛰지는 못했으리라. 그녀는 찰리가 무심코 보낸 신호에 대한 응답으로 그런 신호가 올 가능성을 생각해두기는 했으나, 아직은 오리라 예상하지 못했다. 와일디브는 어찌 그리 재빠른가! 허나 이제 와서 자기가 작정하고 밀회를 다시 잇기를 바랄 수 있는 여자라고 그가 어찌 여길 수 있단 말인가? 그 자리를 떠나려는 충동과 머무르려는 욕망이 그녀 안에서 다투었으며, 욕망이 우세를 지켰다. 그 이상은 하지 않았으니, 둑에 올라 너머를 살피는 것조차 삼간 것이었다. 그녀는 미동도 없이, 얼굴 근육 하나 까딱하지 않고 눈도 들지 않은 채 머물렀으니, 얼굴을 들었다가는 둑의 불빛이 그 위로 비칠 것이요, 와일디브가 내려다보고 있을지도 몰랐기 때문이다.\n\n연못으로 두 번째 첨벙이는 소리가 났다.\n\n그는 어찌하여 앞으로 나와 너머를 살피지도 않고 그리 오래 머물러 있는 걸까? 호기심이 끝내 이겨—그녀는 둑의 흙계단을 한두 칸 올라가 밖을 흘끗 내다보았다.\n\n와일디브가 그녀 앞에 있었다. 마지막 조약돌을 던진 뒤 그가 앞으로 나온 것이었으며, 이제 불빛이, 두 사람 사이에 가슴 높이까지 뻗어 있는 둑 너머로, 둘 각자의 얼굴을 비추었다.\n\n\"내가 피운 게 아니에요!\" 유스테이샤가 황급히 외쳤다. \"내가 모르는 새 피워진 거예요. 그러지, 그러지 말고 내게로 넘어오지 말아요!\"\n\n\"왜 이 여러 날을 내게 알리지도 않고 여기서 지냈소? 당신은 집을 떠났구려. 이 일에 내 탓도 좀 있는 게 아닐까 두렵소만?\"\n\n\"내가 그이의 어머니를 들이지 않았어요. 그게 사연이에요!\"\n\n\"당신은 지금 받고 있는 일을 받을 사람이 아니오, 유스테이샤. 당신은 크나큰 고통 속에 있소. 당신 눈에서, 입에서, 온몸에서 그게 보이오. 가엾고 가엾은 사람!\" 그가 둑을 넘었다. \"당신은 무엇보다도 불행하구려!\"\n\n\"아뇨, 아뇨, 꼭 그런 건—\"\n\n\"너무 멀리 밀어붙여졌어—그게 당신을 죽이고 있소—정말로 그런 것 같소!\"\n\n평소엔 잔잔하던 그녀의 숨결이 그의 말에 가빠졌다. \"나는—나는—\" 그녀가 입을 떼더니, 이내 떨리는 흐느낌을 터뜨렸으니, 뜻밖의 연민의 목소리에—자기 자신과 관련해서는 그런 감정이 존재한다는 것조차 거의 잊고 있던 그 감정에—가슴 깊은 데까지 흔들린 것이었다.\n\n이 울음의 터짐은 유스테이샤 자신마저 너무도 놀라게 한 터라 그녀는 그칠 수가 없었고, 다소 부끄러워 그에게서 옆으로 고개를 돌렸으나, 돌린다고 그에게 가려지는 것은 없었다. 그녀는 절망적으로 흐느껴 울었으며, 이윽고 쏟아짐이 잦아들더니 차츰 잠잠해졌다. 와일디브는 그녀를 끌어안고 싶은 충동을 억누른 채, 아무 말 없이 서 있었다.\n\n\"한 번도 울보였던 적이 없는 나 같은 게, 부끄럽지 않아요?\" 그녀가 눈을 닦으며 가냘픈 속삭임으로 물었다. \"왜 가지 않았어요? 이 모든 걸 다 보지 않았으면 좋았을 것을. 너무 많은 걸 드러내고 마니까요.\"\n\n\"그리 바랐을지도 모르겠소, 그게 나를 당신만큼이나 슬프게 하니까.\" 그가 감정을 담아 조심스럽게 말했다. \"드러낸다는 것으로 말하자면—우리 둘 사이에 그런 말은 가당치 않소.\"\n\n\"내가 당신을 부른 게 아니에요—그걸 잊지 말아요, 데이먼. 나는 괴롭지만, 당신을 부른 게 아니에요! 적어도 아내로서, 나는 떳떳했어요.\"\n\n\"괘념치 마오—내가 온 거니까. 오, 유스테이샤, 지난 두 해 동안 내가 당신에게 끼친 해를 용서해주오! 내가 당신을 망쳐온 거라는 걸 점점 더 알겠소.\"\n\n\"당신이 아니에요. 내가 사는 이곳이에요.\"\n\n\"아아, 당신의 너그러움이 그리 말하게 하는 것도 당연하겠지. 허나 죄인은 나요. 나는 더 했어야 했거나, 아니면 아예 아무것도 하지 말았어야 했소.\"\n\n\"어떻게요?\"\n\n\"나는 결코 당신을 찾아 나서지 말았어야 했거나, 아니면 그렇게 한 이상 끝까지 당신을 붙들었어야 했소. 허나 물론 이제 와 그런 말을 할 자격이 내겐 없지. 이것만 묻겠소—내가 당신을 위해 무어라도 할 수 있겠소? 지금보다 당신을 더 행복하게 해주려 사내가 이 땅 위에서 할 수 있는 일이 무어라도 있다면? 있다면, 내가 하리다. 명하시오, 유스테이샤, 내 힘이 미치는 한껏. 그리고 내가 이제 더 부유해졌다는 걸 잊지 마오. 분명 당신을 이 처지에서 구할 무언가가 있을 거요! 이런 거친 곳에 이런 진귀한 화초라니, 보는 것만으로도 가슴이 아프오. 사주었으면 하는 게 있소? 가고 싶은 데가 있소? 이곳을 아주 벗어나고 싶소? 말만 하오, 그러면 나 때문이 아니었다면 애초에 흐르지도 않았을 그 눈물을 그치게 하기 위해 무엇이든 하리다.\"\n\n\"우리는 각자 다른 사람과 혼인한 몸이에요.\" 그녀가 힘없이 말했다. \"그리고 당신에게서 도움을 받으면 좋지 않은 소문이 날 거예요—나중에—나중에—\"\n\n\"음, 험담꾼들이 언제든 실컷 떠드는 걸 막을 길이야 없지만, 당신은 두려워할 것 없소. 내가 무엇을 느끼든, 당신이 좋다고 할 때까지는 결코 그것을 당신에게 입에 올리지도—행동에 옮기지도—않겠다고 명예를 걸고 약속하리다. 부당한 대접을 받은 한 여인으로서 당신에 대한 내 도리를 아는 만큼이나, 토머신에 대한 내 도리도 잘 알고 있소. 무엇을 도와드리면 되겠소?\"\n\n\"여기서 벗어나는 거예요.\"\n\n\"어디로 가고 싶소?\"\n\n\"마음에 둔 곳이 있어요. 버드머스까지만 도와주면 나머지는 다 내가 할 수 있어요. 거기서 증기선이 해협을 건너 떠나니, 그걸 타면 내가 가고 싶은 파리로 갈 수 있어요. 그래요.\" 그녀가 간곡히 청했다. \"할아버지나 남편이 모르게 버드머스 항구까지만 가게 도와줘요, 그러면 나머지는 다 내가 할게요.\"\n\n\"거기 당신 혼자 두는 게 안전하겠소?\"\n\n\"네, 네. 나는 버드머스를 잘 알아요.\"\n\n\"내가 함께 가리까? 나는 이제 부유하오.\"\n\n그녀는 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"좋다고 하오, 내 사랑!\"\n\n그녀는 여전히 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"그럼, 떠나고 싶을 때 알려주오. 우리는 십이월까지 지금 집에 있을 거요. 그 후에는 캐스터브리지로 옮기오. 그때까지는 무엇이든 내게 명하시오.\"\n\n\"이 일은 생각해볼게요.\" 그녀가 황급히 말했다. \"당신을 떳떳이 친구로 쓸 수 있을지, 아니면 연인으로 받아들여야 할지—그게 내가 나 자신에게 물어야 할 거예요. 떠나기로 마음먹고 당신의 동행을 받아들이기로 하면, 어느 날 저녁 정각 여덟 시에 신호를 보낼게요, 그러면 그건 당신이 같은 날 밤 열두 시에 말과 이륜마차를 채비하고 나를 버드머스 항구로 데려가 아침 배 시간에 대게 하라는 뜻이에요.\"\n\n\"매일 밤 여덟 시에 살피겠소, 어떤 신호도 놓치지 않으리다.\"\n\n\"이제 제발 가요. 이 탈출을 결심한다면 당신을 다시 만날 수 있는 건 한 번뿐이에요, 그렇지 않으면—나는 당신 없이는 갈 수 없으니. 가요—더는 견딜 수가 없어요. 가요—가요!\"\n\n와일디브는 천천히 계단을 올라 반대편 어둠 속으로 내려갔으며, 걸으면서 자꾸 뒤를 돌아보았으나, 이윽고 둑이 그의 시야에서 그녀의 모습을 지워버렸다.\n\nVI.\n토머신이 사촌과 다투고, 그는 편지를 쓰다\n\n이 무렵 요브라이트는 블룸스엔드에 있으면서, 유스테이샤가 자기에게 돌아오기를 바라고 있었다. 클림은 그 옛집에서 한 주가 넘게 지내왔으나, 가구를 옮기는 일은 바로 그날에야 끝마쳤다. 그는 그 시간을 집 안팎을 손보는 일로 보냈으니, 정원길의 낙엽을 쓸고, 화단의 시든 줄기를 자르고, 가을바람에 떨어진 덩굴식물을 못질해 붙였다. 이런 일들에서 그가 별다른 즐거움을 느낀 것은 아니었으나, 그것들은 그 자신과 절망 사이의 가림막을 이루어주었다. 게다가 어머니의 손에서 자기 손으로 넘어온 모든 것을 좋은 상태로 간수하는 일은 그에게 일종의 신앙이 되어 있었다.\n\n이런 일들을 하는 내내 그는 끊임없이 유스테이샤를 살피고 있었다. 그녀가 자기를 어디서 찾아야 할지 헷갈리지 않도록, 그는 올더워스의 정원 대문에 안내판을 붙여 자기가 어디로 옮겨 갔는지 흰 글씨로 알려두게 했다. 나뭇잎 하나가 땅으로 떠내려가면 그는 그녀의 발소리인가 싶어 고개를 돌렸다. 화단의 부드러운 흙에서 벌레를 찾는 새 한 마리가 그녀가 대문 빗장에 손을 대는 소리처럼 들렸으며, 땅거미가 질 무렵 땅의 구멍이며, 속이 빈 줄기며, 말려 죽은 잎이며, 산들바람과 벌레와 곤충이 제멋대로 굴 수 있는 그 밖의 틈새에서 부드럽고 기묘한 복화술 같은 소리가 들려오면, 그는 그것이 밖에 서서 화해의 소망을 내쉬는 유스테이샤라고 상상했다.\n\n이 순간까지 그는 그녀를 돌아오라 청하지 않으리라는 결심을 굳건히 지켜왔다. 한편으로 그가 그녀를 다룬 그 매정함은 어머니를 향한 회한의 날카로움을 누그러뜨렸고, 어머니의 자리를 빼앗은 여인을 향한 옛 애틋함을 얼마간 되살려놓았다. 모진 감정은 모진 처사를 낳고, 그것은 다시 반작용으로 그것을 낳은 정서를 꺼뜨리는 법이다. 곰곰이 헤아릴수록 그는 더 누그러졌다. 그러나 아내를 곤경에 빠진 결백으로 여기는 것은 불가능했으니, 다만 자기가 그녀에게 충분한 시간을 준 것이었는지—그 음울한 아침에 너무 갑작스레 그녀에게 들이닥친 것은 아니었는지 스스로 물어볼 수는 있었다.\n\n처음의 끓어오르던 분노가 가신 지금, 그는 그녀에게 와일디브와의 경솔한 우정 이상의 것을 돌리고 싶지 않았으니, 그녀의 태도에 부정의 기색은 나타나지 않았던 까닭이다. 그리고 이것을 일단 인정하고 나니, 어머니를 향한 그녀의 행동에 대한 더없이 어두운 해석이 더는 억지로 들이밀어지지 않았다.\n\n십일월 오일 저녁, 유스테이샤를 향한 그의 생각은 격렬했다. 온종일 다정한 말을 주고받던 그 지난 시절의 메아리가 몇 마일이나 뒤에 두고 온 바닷가의 어렴풋한 웅성거림처럼 밀려왔다. \"분명,\" 그가 말했다. \"그녀도 지금쯤은 나에게 연락을 해와, 와일디브가 자기에게 어떤 사람이었는지 솔직히 털어놓을 만했건만.\"\n\n그날 밤 집에 머무는 대신 그는 토머신과 그 남편을 보러 가기로 마음먹었다. 기회가 닿으면 유스테이샤와 자기 사이의 결별 원인을 넌지시 언급하되, 어머니가 쫓겨났을 때 집 안에 제삼자가 있었다는 사실에 대해서는 입을 다물 작정이었다. 와일디브가 결백하게 거기 있었던 것으로 드러난다면 그는 분명 그 사실을 거리낌 없이 말할 것이요, 만일 부당한 속셈을 품고 거기 있었던 거라면, 감정이 빨리 동하는 사내인 와일디브가 유스테이샤가 얼마나 곤란한 처지에 놓였는지 드러내는 무언가를 어쩌면 발설할지도 몰랐다.\n\n그러나 사촌의 집에 이르고 보니 토머신만 집에 있었으니, 와일디브는 그때 찰리가 미스토버에서 무심코 피운 모닥불 쪽으로 가는 길이었다. 그때나 늘 그러하듯 토머신은 클림을 보고 반가워하며, 그를 데려가 잠든 아기를 들여다보게 했는데, 손으로 촛불을 가려 아기의 눈에 빛이 들지 않도록 조심스레 막았다.\n\n\"탐신, 유스테이샤가 지금 나와 함께 있지 않다는 걸 들었느냐?\" 두 사람이 다시 자리에 앉았을 때 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아뇨.\" 토머신이 놀라 말했다.\n\n\"내가 올더워스를 떠났다는 것도?\"\n\n\"아뇨. 오라버니가 가져오지 않는 한 올더워스 소식은 도통 듣지를 못해요. 무슨 일이에요?\"\n\n클림은 불안한 목소리로 그녀에게 자기가 수전 넌서치의 아들을 찾아갔던 일과, 그 아이가 털어놓은 사실과, 유스테이샤가 고의로 매정하게 그 짓을 했다고 자기가 그녀를 추궁하여 빚어진 결과를 들려주었다. 그녀와 함께 와일디브가 있었다는 이야기는 일절 입에 담지 않았다.\n\n\"이 모든 일을, 나는 까맣게 모르고 있었다니!\" 토머신이 두려움에 질린 어조로 중얼거렸다. \"끔찍해요! 무엇이 그녀를 그리—오, 유스테이샤! 그리고 그걸 알아내고는 오라버니가 한달음에 그녀에게 달려갔다고요? 너무 모질지는 않으셨어요?—아니면 그녀가 정말 보이는 그대로 그리 사악한가요?\"\n\n\"제 어머니의 원수에게 사내가 너무 모질 수가 있겠느냐?\"\n\n\"그럴 수 있을 것도 같아요.\"\n\n\"좋다, 그럼—그럴 수 있다고 인정하마. 헌데 이제 어찌해야 하느냐?\"\n\n\"다시 화해하세요—이리 끔찍한 다툼도 화해될 수 있다면요. 차라리 이 얘기를 안 들었으면 싶을 정도예요. 허나 부디 화해하려 애써보세요. 두 분 다 원하기만 한다면, 어쨌든 길은 있는 법이니까요.\"\n\n\"우리 둘 다 화해를 원하는지 모르겠구나.\" 클림이 말했다. \"그녀가 원했다면, 지금쯤 나에게 기별을 보내지 않았겠느냐?\"\n\n\"오라버니는 원하는 것 같으면서도, 그녀에게 기별을 보내지 않으셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"그렇지. 허나 나는 그만한 도발을 당하고도 마땅히 그래야 하는지, 의심 속에서 이리저리 마음이 흔들려왔다. 지금의 나를 보고는, 탐신, 너는 내가 어떤 꼴이었는지, 이 며칠 사이 내가 어떤 나락까지 떨어졌는지 짐작도 못 할 게다. 오, 그런 식으로 내 어머니를 문전박대한 것은 쓰라린 치욕이었어! 내가 그걸 잊을 수 있겠느냐, 아니 그녀를 다시 보는 데 응할 수나 있겠느냐?\"\n\n\"그녀는 그 일로 심각한 무슨 일이 생기리라곤 몰랐을지도 몰라요, 어쩌면 큰이모님을 아주 막아버릴 생각은 아니었을지도 모르고요.\"\n\n\"그녀 스스로도 그럴 생각은 아니었다고 하더구나. 허나 막아버린 건 사실로 남아 있지 않으냐.\"\n\n\"그녀가 뉘우치고 있다 믿고, 사람을 보내 불러오세요.\"\n\n\"그녀가 오지 않으려 하면 어쩌고?\"\n\n\"그건 그녀가 적의를 품는 게 버릇임을 보여주는 셈이니, 죄가 있음을 증명하는 거겠죠. 허나 저는 한순간도 그렇게는 생각지 않아요.\"\n\n\"이렇게 하마. 하루나 이틀쯤 더 기다리겠다—분명 이틀을 넘기지는 않으마. 그래도 그 안에 그녀가 나에게 기별을 보내지 않으면, 그땐 정말 내가 그녀에게 기별을 보내마. 오늘 밤 여기서 와일디브를 볼 줄 알았는데. 그는 집을 비웠느냐?\"\n\n토머신이 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"아뇨.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그냥 산책하러 나갔어요.\"\n\n\"왜 너를 데려가지 않았지? 저녁 날씨가 좋은데. 너도 그 사람만큼이나 바람을 쐬어야지.\"\n\n\"오, 저는 어디 가는 거 별로 안 좋아해요, 게다가 아기도 있고요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래. 그런데 나는 이 일을 너만이 아니라 네 남편한테도 의논해야 하는 게 아닐까 생각해왔다.\" 클림이 차분히 말했다.\n\n\"안 하시는 게 좋을 것 같아요.\" 그녀가 얼른 대답했다. \"도움이 안 될 거예요.\"\n\n사촌은 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 토머신이 그 비극적인 오후의 일에 제 남편이 한몫 끼어 있었다는 것을 모르고 있는 것은 틀림없었으나, 그녀의 표정은 지난날 와일디브와 유스테이샤 사이에 다정한 관계가 있었다는 소문에 대한 어떤 의심이나 생각을 감추고 있음을 드러내는 듯했다.\n\n그러나 클림은 그것에서 아무것도 알아낼 수 없었고, 그는 올 때보다 더 큰 의심을 안은 채 떠나려 일어섰다.\n\n\"하루 이틀 안에 그녀에게 편지 쓰실 거죠?\" 젊은 여인이 간곡히 말했다. \"이 비참한 결별이 끝나기를 정말이지 간절히 바라요.\"\n\n\"그러마.\" 클림이 말했다. \"나도 지금의 처지가 조금도 기쁘지 않으니까.\"\n\n그러고는 그녀와 헤어져 언덕을 넘어 블룸스엔드로 갔다. 잠자리에 들기 전 그는 자리에 앉아 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다.\n\n사랑하는 유스테이샤,—나는 내 이성에 너무 가까이 묻지 않고 내 마음을 따라야겠소. 내게 돌아와주겠소? 그래주오, 그러면 지난 일은 결코 입에 올리지 않으리다. 내가 너무 모질었소. 허나 오, 유스테이샤, 그 도발이라니! 당신이 스스로 불러들인 그 분노의 말들이 내게 얼마만 한 대가를 치르게 했는지 당신은 모르오, 결코 알지 못하리다. 정직한 사내가 약속할 수 있는 모든 것을 나는 이제 약속하니, 곧 이 일로 당신이 나로 인해 다시는 어떤 고통도 겪지 않으리라는 것이오. 우리가 맺은 그 모든 맹세를 두고 보건대, 유스테이샤, 우리는 남은 생을 그것을 지키려 애쓰며 보내는 편이 낫겠다 싶소. 그러니 내게 오시오, 당신이 나를 책망한다 해도. 내가 당신과 헤어진 그 아침의 당신의 고통을 나는 떠올려왔소. 그것이 진심이었음을 나는 알며, 그것만으로도 당신이 감당해야 할 몫은 다한 것이오. 우리의 사랑은 여전히 이어져야 하오. 우리 같은 마음은 서로에게 매이라고가 아니고서야 우리에게 주어졌을 리 없소. 처음에는 당신을 돌아오라 청할 수 없었소, 유스테이샤, 당신과 함께 있던 그 사내가 연인으로 거기 있던 게 아니라고 내 자신을 납득시킬 수 없었던 까닭이오. 허나 당신이 와서 그 어지러운 정황을 설명해준다면, 당신이 내게 자신의 결백을 보일 수 있으리라는 걸 나는 의심치 않소. 어찌하여 진작 오지 않았소? 내가 당신 말을 듣지 않으리라 여기오? 여름 달빛 아래 우리가 주고받은 입맞춤과 맹세를 떠올린다면, 분명 그럴 리 없을 텐데. 그러니 돌아오시오, 그러면 따뜻이 맞으리다. 더는 당신을 해롭게 생각할 수 없소—나는 당신을 두둔하는 데에 너무도 골몰해 있으니.—언제나 당신의 남편,\n\n클림.\n\"됐다.\" 그것을 책상에 넣으며 그가 말했다. \"잘한 일이지. 내일 밤까지 그녀가 오지 않으면 이걸 그녀에게 보내야겠다.\"\n\n한편, 그가 막 떠나온 집에서 토머신은 마음이 편치 않아 한숨을 짓고 앉아 있었다. 그날 저녁 남편에 대한 정절로 인해, 그녀는 와일디브의 유스테이샤를 향한 관심이 결혼과 더불어 끝나지 않았으리라는 모든 의심을 감추었던 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 확실히 아는 것이 없었고, 클림이 그녀가 끔찍이 아끼는 사촌이긴 했으나, 그녀에게는 그보다 더 가까운 이가 있었다.\n\n조금 뒤 와일디브가 미스토버 산책에서 돌아오자, 토머신이 말했다. \"데이먼, 어디 갔었어요? 무척 겁이 나서, 당신이 강에 빠진 줄 알았다고요. 나는 혼자 집에 있는 게 싫어요.\"\n\n\"겁이 났다고?\" 그가 마치 그녀를 무슨 집짐승이라도 되는 양 그 뺨을 어루만지며 말했다. \"원, 나는 무엇도 당신을 겁먹게 하지 못하는 줄 알았는데. 분명 당신이 콧대가 높아져서, 우리가 장사 밑천에서 벗어나 형편이 나아진 뒤로 이런 데서 사는 게 마음에 안 드는 게지. 음, 새집을 구하는 건 따분한 일이오. 허나 우리 만 파운드가 십만 파운드였다면 모를까, 신중을 무시할 만큼 여유가 있는 게 아니고서야 그보다 더 일찍 손댈 수는 없었소.\"\n\n\"아뇨—나는 기다리는 거 상관없어요—아기에게 무슨 위험이라도 무릅쓰느니 차라리 여기서 열두 달을 더 머무는 게 나아요. 허나 당신이 저녁마다 그렇게 사라지는 게 싫어요. 무언가 마음에 걸리는 게 있죠—분명 있어요, 데이먼. 당신은 그리 음울하게 돌아다니고, 황야를 마치 거닐기 좋은 멋진 들판이 아니라 누군가의 감옥인 양 바라보잖아요.\"\n\n그가 가엾다는 듯 놀란 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보았다. \"뭐라, 당신은 에그던 황야가 좋소?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 내가 가까이서 나고 자란 것을 좋아해요. 그 험상궂은 옛 얼굴이 좋아요.\"\n\n\"쳇, 여보. 당신은 자기가 뭘 좋아하는지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"분명히 알아요. 에그던에 대해 마음에 안 드는 건 딱 하나뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데?\"\n\n\"당신이 거길 거닐 때 한 번도 나를 데려가지 않는 거요. 그토록 싫다면서 어째서 당신은 거길 그리 혼자 헤매고 다니는 거예요?\"\n\n그 물음은 단순한 것이었으나 분명 당황스러운 데가 있어, 그는 대답하기 전에 자리에 앉았다. \"당신이 거기서 나를 자주 보는 것 같진 않은데. 한 번 예를 들어보오.\"\n\n\"그러죠.\" 그녀가 의기양양하게 대답했다. \"오늘 저녁 당신이 나갔을 때, 아기가 잠들었으니 당신이 나한테 말도 없이 그렇게 알 수 없는 데로 어딜 가는지 봐야겠다 싶었어요. 그래서 뛰어나가 당신 뒤를 따라갔죠. 당신은 길이 갈라지는 곳에 멈춰 서서 모닥불들을 둘러보더니, '제기랄, 가야겠다!' 하고는 왼쪽 길로 빠르게 올라갔어요. 그래서 나는 서서 당신을 지켜봤죠.\"\n\n와일디브가 얼굴을 찌푸렸다가, 이내 억지웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"음, 그래서 무슨 놀라운 발견을 했소?\"\n\n\"거봐요—이제 당신은 화를 내잖아요, 그러니 이 얘기는 더 안 할래요.\" 그녀는 그에게로 건너가, 발받침에 앉아 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다.\n\n\"실없는 소리!\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 늘 그런 식으로 발을 빼지. 일단 시작했으니 이제 계속합시다. 그다음에 뭘 봤소? 나는 그게 특히 알고 싶군.\"\n\n\"그러지 말아요, 데이먼!\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"아무것도 못 봤어요. 당신이 시야에서 사라져서, 나는 모닥불들을 둘러보고 들어왔어요.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 당신이 내 뒤를 밟은 게 이번이 처음이 아닐지도 모르지. 나에 대해 뭔가 나쁜 걸 캐내려는 거요?\"\n\n\"전혀 아니에요! 전엔 한 번도 그런 적 없고, 당신에 대해 이런저런 말이 가끔 흘러나오지 않았더라면 지금도 안 했을 거예요.\"\n\n\"대체 무슨 소리요?\" 그가 참지 못하고 물었다.\n\n\"사람들이 그러는데—당신이 저녁마다 올더워스에 가곤 했다더라고요, 그래서 내가 들은 어떤 얘기가 떠올라서—\"\n\n와일디브가 성을 내며 돌아서 그녀 앞에 우뚝 섰다. \"자,\" 그가 손을 허공에 휘저으며 말했다. \"어디 다 털어놔보시오, 부인! 당신이 무슨 말을 들었는지 내 알아야겠소.\"\n\n\"음, 당신이 예전에 유스테이샤를 무척 좋아했다는 얘기를 들었어요—그게 다예요, 그것도 한 토막씩 흘리는 식으로요. 화내지 마세요!\"\n\n그는 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 것을 보았다. \"음,\" 그가 말했다. \"그거야 새삼스러울 게 없는 얘기고, 물론 나는 당신에게 거칠게 굴 생각은 없으니, 울 것 없소. 자, 이 얘기는 더 하지 맙시다.\"\n\n그리고 더는 말이 없었으니, 토머신으로서는 그날 저녁 클림이 자기를 찾아온 일과 그가 한 이야기를 입에 올리지 않을 구실이 생긴 것이 적이 다행이었다.\n\nVII.\n십일월 엿샛날 밤\n\n탈출을 결심하고 나자 유스테이샤는 때때로 무슨 일인가 일어나 제 의도가 어그러지기를 바라는 듯했다. 그녀의 처지를 정녕 바꿔놓을 수 있는 단 하나의 사건은 클림이 나타나는 것이었다. 연인으로서 그를 감싸던 그 영광은 이제 가셨으나, 그래도 그의 어떤 선량하고 소박한 자질이 이따금 그녀의 기억으로 되돌아와, 그가 다시 제 앞에 나타나주리라는 한순간의 희망의 떨림을 자아내곤 했다. 그러나 차분히 헤아려보면, 지금처럼 벌어진 단절이 다시 메워지리라고는 도무지 보이지 않았으니—그녀는 고립되고 어울리지 않는, 고통스러운 존재로 살아가야 할 터였다. 예전에는 황야만을 머물기에 마뜩잖은 곳으로 여겼으나, 이제는 온 세상을 그렇게 느꼈다.\n\n엿샛날 저녁 무렵, 다시 떠나리라는 그녀의 결심이 되살아났다. 네 시쯤 그녀는 올더워스에서 도망쳐 나올 때 가져왔던 몇 안 되는 자잘한 물건들과, 이곳에 두고 갔던 자기 소지품 몇 가지를 새로 꾸렸으니, 모두 합쳐 한두 마일쯤은 손에 들고 갈 만한 그리 크지 않은 보따리가 되었다. 바깥 풍경은 점점 어두워졌으며, 진흙빛 구름이 마치 하늘을 가로질러 매단 거대한 그물침대처럼 하늘에서 아래로 불룩이 처져 내렸고, 밤이 깊어감에 따라 사나운 바람이 일었으나, 아직 비는 내리지 않았다.\n\n유스테이샤는 더 할 일도 없는 터라 집 안에 가만히 있을 수가 없어, 곧 떠나게 될 그 집에서 멀지 않은 언덕을 이리저리 거닐었다. 이렇게 정처 없이 헤매던 중 그녀는 할아버지의 집보다 조금 더 아래쪽에 있는 수전 넌서치의 오두막을 지나게 되었다. 문이 빠끔히 열려 있어, 밝은 불빛의 띠가 바깥 땅 위로 드리워져 있었다. 유스테이샤가 그 불빛 줄기를 가로지르자 그녀는 한순간 환등 속의 형상처럼 또렷이—어둠의 영역에 둘러싸인 빛의 피조물처럼—드러났으나, 그 순간이 지나자 그녀는 다시 밤 속으로 빨려들었다.\n\n오두막 안에 앉아 있던 한 여인이 그 한순간의 빛 속에서 그녀를 보고 알아보았다. 그것은 수전 자신이었으니, 자주 골골거리다가 이제는 심히 앓아누운 어린 아들에게 줄 따뜻한 우유죽을 만들고 있던 참이었다. 수전은 숟가락을 떨어뜨리고, 사라진 형상을 향해 주먹을 흔들고는, 골똘히 넋이 나간 듯한 태도로 다시 제 일을 이어갔다.\n\n여덟 시, 곧 유스테이샤가 신호를 보낼 일이 있다면 와일디브에게 신호를 보내기로 약속한 그 시각에, 그녀는 집 안팎을 둘러보아 인적이 없는지 살핀 뒤, 가시금작화 더미로 가서 그 땔감의 긴 줄기가 달린 가지 하나를 뽑아냈다. 이것을 그녀는 둑 모퉁이로 가져가, 덧창이 다 닫혀 있는지 뒤를 흘끗 돌아본 다음, 불을 켜 가시금작화에 불을 붙였다. 그것이 완전히 활활 타오르자 유스테이샤는 줄기를 잡고 그것이 다 타버릴 때까지 머리 위 허공에 휘저었다.\n\n이런 심정에 만족이라는 게 가능하다면, 그녀는 일이 분쯤 뒤 와일디브의 거처 근처에서 비슷한 불빛이 보이는 것에 만족했다. 도움이 필요할 경우를 대비해 이 시각에 매일 밤 망을 보기로 약속해두었던바, 이 신속함은 그가 얼마나 철저히 제 약속을 지켰는지를 증명해주었다. 지금부터 네 시간 뒤, 곧 자정에 그는 미리 정해둔 대로 그녀를 버드머스로 데려갈 채비를 하고 있어야 했다.\n\n유스테이샤는 집으로 돌아왔다. 저녁 식사가 끝나자 그녀는 일찌감치 물러나, 시간이 가기를 기다리며 침실에 앉아 있었다. 밤이 어둡고 험상궂은 터라 바이 선장은 이 긴 가을밤이면 더러 그러하듯 어느 오두막에 어슬렁어슬렁 잡담을 하러 나가거나 주막에 들르지도 않고, 아래층에서 홀로 그로그주를 홀짝이며 앉아 있었다. 열 시쯤 문 두드리는 소리가 났다. 하인이 문을 열자 촛불이 페어웨이의 모습을 비추었다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 어쩔 수 없이 로어 미스토버에 가야 했는데,\" 그가 말했다. \"요브라이트 씨가 가는 길에 이걸 여기 두고 가달라더군요. 헌데, 원, 그걸 모자 안감에 끼워놓고는, 돌아와서 자기 전에 대문을 걸려다가 다시 떠올릴 때까지 까맣게 잊고 있었지 뭐요. 그래서 곧장 들고 되돌아온 거요.\"\n\n그는 편지 한 통을 건네고는 제 갈 길을 갔다. 하녀가 그것을 선장에게 가져갔는데, 선장은 그것이 유스테이샤에게 보내진 것임을 알았다. 그는 그것을 이리저리 뒤집어보며, 확신할 수는 없었으나 글씨가 그녀 남편의 것이리라 짐작했다. 어쨌든 그는 되도록 빨리 그녀에게 전해주기로 마음먹고 그러려고 그것을 위층으로 가져갔으나, 그녀의 방문에 이르러 열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보니 안에 불빛이 없었으니, 실은 유스테이샤가 옷도 벗지 않은 채 다가올 여정에 대비해 잠깐 쉬며 기운을 좀 차리려고 침대에 몸을 던진 것이었다. 할아버지는 본 것으로 미루어 그녀를 깨우지 않는 편이 낫겠다 판단하고, 다시 응접실로 내려와 아침에 그녀에게 주려고 편지를 벽난로 선반 위에 놓아두었다.\n\n열한 시에 그는 자신도 잠자리에 들어, 침실에서 한동안 담배를 피우다가 열한 시 반에 불을 끄고는, 늘 변함없이 하는 버릇대로, 침대에 들기 전에 차양을 걷어 올렸으니, 아침에 눈을 뜰 때 바람이 어느 쪽으로 부는지 볼 수 있도록 함이었고, 그의 침실 창은 깃대와 풍향계가 내다보이는 자리였다. 막 자리에 누웠을 때, 그는 깃대의 흰 기둥이 마치 바깥 밤의 그늘을 가로질러 아래로 그은 인광의 줄무늬처럼 번뜩 나타나는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 이를 설명할 길은 단 하나뿐이었으니—집 쪽에서 그 기둥에 갑자기 불빛이 던져진 것이었다. 모두가 잠자리에 든 터라 노인은 침대에서 나와 살그머니 창을 열고 좌우를 살펴볼 필요를 느꼈다. 유스테이샤의 침실에 불이 켜져 있었고, 기둥을 비춘 것은 그녀의 창에서 나온 빛이었다. 무엇이 그녀를 깨웠을까 의아해하며 그는 창가에서 망설이고 있었고, 편지를 가져다 그녀의 방문 밑으로 밀어 넣을까 생각하던 차에, 그의 방과 복도를 가르는 칸막이벽 너머에서 옷자락이 가볍게 스치는 소리를 들었다.\n\n선장은 유스테이샤가 잠이 깨어 책을 가지러 갔으려니 짐작했고, 그녀가 지나가며 또렷이 우는 소리를 듣지 않았더라면 대수롭지 않은 일로 여기고 넘겼을 것이다.\n\n\"그 남편 생각을 하고 있구나.\" 그가 혼잣말을 했다. \"아아, 어리석은 것! 그자와 혼인할 일이 아니었거늘. 저 편지가 정말 그자 것일까?\"\n\n그는 일어나 보트클로크를 둘러 걸치고 문을 열며 말했다. \"유스테이샤!\" 대답이 없었다. \"유스테이샤!\" 그가 더 크게 되풀이했다. \"벽난로 선반 위에 네게 온 편지가 있다.\"\n\n그러나 이 말에 돌아온 대답이라곤, 집 모퉁이를 갉아대는 듯한 바람의 가상의 응답과, 창문에 떨어지는 몇 방울 빗방울의 두드림뿐이었다.\n\n그는 층계참으로 나가, 거의 오 분을 기다리며 서 있었다. 그래도 그녀는 돌아오지 않았다. 그는 불을 가지러 되돌아갔다가 그녀를 따라가려 채비했으나, 우선 그녀의 침실을 들여다보았다. 거기, 누비이불 위에 그녀의 몸이 누웠던 자국이 남아 있어, 침대를 펴지 않았음을 보여주었으며, 더욱 의미심장하게도 그녀는 촛대를 아래층으로 들고 가지 않았다. 이제 그는 완전히 질겁하여, 황급히 옷을 챙겨 입고 자기가 손수 빗장을 지르고 잠가둔 현관문으로 내려갔다. 그것은 이제 잠겨 있지 않았다. 유스테이샤가 이 한밤중에 집을 나섰다는 데에는 더 이상 의심의 여지가 없었으니, 대체 그녀는 어디로 갔단 말인가? 그녀를 뒤쫓기란 거의 불가능했다. 만일 집이 평범한 길가에 있었다면, 두 사람이 각기 한 방향씩 나서서 그녀를 따라잡을 수 있었으련만, 어둠 속 황야에서 누군가를 찾는다는 것은 가망 없는 일이었으니, 어느 지점에서든 황야를 가로질러 도망칠 수 있는 길은 극점에서 뻗어 나가는 자오선만큼이나 수두룩한 까닭이었다. 어찌할 바를 몰라 그는 응접실을 들여다보았다가, 편지가 여전히 손도 대지 않은 채 거기 놓여 있는 것을 보고 부아가 치밀었다.\n\n열한 시 반, 집 안이 조용해진 것을 알고 유스테이샤는 촛불을 켜고, 따뜻한 겉옷을 두르고, 손에 가방을 든 다음, 다시 불을 끄고 계단을 내려왔다. 바깥 공기 속으로 나서자 그녀는 비가 내리기 시작한 것을 알았고, 문가에 멈춰 서 있는 동안 비는 점점 거세져, 세차게 쏟아질 기세였다. 그러나 이 행로에 몸을 맡긴 이상 궂은 날씨 때문에 물러설 수는 없었다. 클림의 편지를 받았다 해도 이제는 그녀를 멈추게 하지 못했을 것이다. 밤의 음울함은 장례식 같았으니, 온 자연이 상장을 두른 듯했다. 집 뒤편 전나무들의 뾰족한 꼭대기는 마치 수도원의 작은 탑과 첨탑처럼 하늘로 솟아 있었다. 지평선 아래로는 아무것도 보이지 않았으니, 다만 수전 넌서치의 오두막에서 여전히 타고 있는 불빛 하나뿐이었다.\n\n유스테이샤는 우산을 펴고 둑 위 계단을 통해 울안에서 나갔으며, 그 후로는 누군가에게 들킬 위험에서 완전히 벗어났다. 연못을 끼고 돌아, 그녀는 레인배로 쪽 오솔길을 따라갔으니, 이따금 뒤틀린 가시금작화 뿌리며, 골풀 다발이며, 진물이 흐르는 물컹한 균류 덩어리에 걸려 비틀거렸는데, 그 균류는 이 철이면 마치 어떤 거대한 짐승의 썩은 간과 허파처럼 황야 곳곳에 흩어져 있었다. 달과 별은 구름과 비에 막혀 아주 꺼져버린 지경이었다. 그것은 나그네의 생각을 절로 세상의 연대기에 적힌 밤의 재앙 장면들로, 역사와 전설 속의 온갖 끔찍하고 어두운 것들로—이집트의 마지막 재앙으로, 산헤립 군대의 멸망으로, 겟세마네의 고뇌로—이끄는 밤이었다.\n\n유스테이샤는 마침내 레인배로에 이르러, 생각에 잠기려 그곳에 멈춰 섰다. 그녀의 마음의 혼돈과 바깥 세상의 혼돈 사이만큼 완벽한 조화는 일찍이 없었다. 한 가지 기억이 이 순간 그녀에게 번뜩 떠올랐으니—긴 여정에 나서기에는 돈이 충분하지 않다는 것이었다. 그날의 변덕스러운 감정들 한가운데서 그녀의 비현실적인 머리는 채비를 잘 갖춰야 한다는 필요를 헤아리지 못했고, 이제 그 사정을 절절히 깨닫자 그녀는 쓰라리게 한숨지으며 더는 똑바로 서 있지 못하고, 마치 아래에서 뻗어 나온 손에 봉분 속으로 끌려들기라도 하듯 우산 아래 차츰 몸을 웅크렸다. 자기가 여전히 포로로 남아야 한단 말인가? 돈—그녀는 한 번도 그 값어치를 느껴본 적이 없었다. 이 고장에서 자취를 지우는 데조차 수단이 필요했다. 자기와 동행하는 것을 허락하지 않으면서 와일디브에게 금전적 도움을 청하기란, 자존심의 그림자라도 남은 여인에게는 불가능한 일이었으며, 그의 정부로 도망친다는 것은—그가 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 그녀는 알았으나—굴욕에 다름 아니었다.\n\n지금 곁에 서 있던 누군가가 있었다면 그녀를 가엾이 여겼으리라, 비록 그것이 그녀가 비바람에 내몰린 탓이나, 봉분 속 삭아버린 유해 말고는 온 인류에서 고립된 탓이라기보다는, 그녀의 감정이 그 몸에 실어주는 그 살짝 흔들리는 움직임으로 드러나던 그 다른 형태의 비참함 탓이었다. 극도의 불행이 그녀를 눈에 띄게 짓누르고 있었다. 우산에서 망토로, 망토에서 히스로, 히스에서 땅으로 비가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 사이사이, 그것과 아주 흡사한 소리가 그녀의 입술에서도 흘러나왔으며, 바깥 풍경의 눈물겨움이 그녀의 얼굴 위에서 되풀이되었다. 그녀의 영혼의 날개는 주위 온갖 것들의 모진 가로막음에 부러져버렸으며, 설령 자기가 버드머스로 가서 증기선에 올라 어느 맞은편 항구로 떠나는 가망 있는 길에 들어선 모습을 보았다 한들, 다른 것들이 너무도 무섭도록 악의에 차 있던 터라 그녀는 그다지 더 들떴을 리 없었다.\n그녀는 소리 내어 말을 내뱉었다. 늙지도, 귀먹지도, 미치지도, 변덕스럽지도 않은 여인이 이런 처지에서 소리 내어 흐느끼고 혼잣말을 하기에 이른다면, 무언가 비통한 일이 있는 것이다.\n\n\"갈 수 있을까, 갈 수 있을까?\" 그녀가 신음했다. \"그이는 내가 나를 바칠 만큼 위대하지 못해—그이는 내 욕망을 채워주지 못해!…… 그이가 사울이나 보나파르트 같은 사람이었더라면—아아! 허나 그런 사람을 위해 내 혼인 서약을 깨다니—그건 너무도 초라한 호사야!…… 게다가 나는 혼자 떠날 돈도 없어! 또 설령 떠난들, 그게 나에게 무슨 위안이 될까? 나는 올해를 끌어왔듯 내년에도 끌어가야 할 테고, 그다음 해도 그전과 같겠지. 내가 얼마나 찬란한 여인이 되려 애쓰고 또 애썼는데, 운명은 또 얼마나 나를 거슬러왔는지!…… 나는 이런 신세를 당할 까닭이 없어!\" 그녀가 쓰라린 반항의 광기에 휩싸여 외쳤다. \"오, 나를 이 잘못 빚어진 세상에 들여놓다니 이 무슨 잔혹함인가! 나는 많은 것을 할 수 있는 사람이었건만, 내 힘으로 어쩔 수 없는 것들에 다치고 시들고 짓밟혀버렸어! 오, 하늘에는 아무 해도 끼친 적 없는 나에게 이런 고문을 궁리해내다니, 하늘은 어찌 이리도 모진가!\"\n\n유스테이샤가 집을 나설 때 얼핏 보았던 그 멀리의 불빛은, 그녀가 짐작한 대로 수전 넌서치의 오두막 창에서 나온 것이었다. 그러나 유스테이샤가 짐작하지 못한 것은 그 순간 안에 있는 여인이 하고 있던 일이었다. 그날 저녁 좀 더 일찍, 앓는 아이가 \"엄마, 나 정말 너무 괴로워!\" 하고 외친 지 채 오 분도 안 되어 그녀가 지나가는 모습을 본 것이, 그 부인으로 하여금 유스테이샤가 가까이 있음으로써 분명 어떤 악한 기운이 미치고 있다고 확신케 했다.\n\n이런 까닭에 수전은 평소 같으면 저녁 일이 끝나자마자 곧장 잠자리에 들었을 것을 그러지 않았다. 가엾은 유스테이샤가 부린다고 그녀가 상상한 그 악한 주술을 막으려고, 아이의 어머니는 어떤 섬뜩한 미신의 술수에 몰두했으니, 그것은 그 대상이 된 어떤 인간에게든 무력함과 쇠약과 파멸을 가져온다고 여겨지는 것이었다. 그것은 그 시절 에그던에서 익히 알려진 풍습이었으며, 오늘날까지도 아주 사라지지는 않은 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 촛불을 들고 안쪽 방으로 들어갔으니, 그곳에는 다른 살림 도구들 가운데 큼직한 갈색 항아리 두 개가 있었는데, 둘을 합치면 어쩌면 백웨이트쯤 되는 묽은 꿀이 담겨 있었으며, 지난여름 벌들이 거둔 산물이었다. 항아리 위 선반에는 매끈하고 단단한 노란 덩어리가 반구 모양으로 놓여 있었으니, 그 꿀을 거둘 때 함께 나온 밀랍이었다. 수전은 그 덩어리를 내려, 얇게 몇 조각을 잘라 쇠국자에 수북이 담아 거실로 돌아와서는, 그 그릇을 벽난로의 뜨거운 잿더미 속에 놓았다. 밀랍이 반죽처럼 말랑말랑해지자 그녀는 그 조각들을 한데 이겨 붙였다. 그러자 그녀의 얼굴은 한층 골똘해졌다. 그녀는 밀랍을 빚기 시작했으며, 그 다루는 손놀림으로 미루어 거기에 미리 마음에 둔 어떤 형상을 부여하려 애쓰고 있음이 분명했다. 그 형상은 사람이었다.\n\n데우고 이기고, 자르고 비틀고, 떼었다 다시 붙이고 하며 만들다 만 그 형상을 가지고 그녀는 십오 분쯤 만에 제법 여인과 비슷한, 높이 육 인치쯤 되는 모양을 빚어냈다. 그녀는 그것을 식어 단단해지도록 탁자 위에 놓았다. 그러는 사이 그녀는 촛불을 들고 어린 아들이 누워 있는 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n\"얘야, 오늘 오후에 유스테이샤 부인이 그 검은 옷 말고 또 무얼 걸치고 있었는지 봤느냐?\"\n\n\"목에 빨간 리본을 둘렀어요.\"\n\n\"또 다른 건?\"\n\n\"아뇨—샌들 신발 말고는요.\"\n\n\"빨간 리본에 샌들 신발이라.\" 그녀가 혼자 중얼거렸다.\n\n넌서치 부인은 가서 뒤지다가 가장 가느다란 빨간 리본 한 조각을 찾아내, 그것을 아래층으로 가져가 형상의 목에 둘러 묶었다. 그런 다음 창가의 삐걱대는 책상에서 잉크와 깃펜을 가져와, 신발에 가려졌으리라 짐작되는 만큼 형상의 발을 검게 칠했으며, 양 발등에는 그 시절 샌들 끈이 이루던 모양대로 가로지른 선들을 그어 표시했다. 마지막으로 그녀는 머리 윗부분에 검은 실 한 가닥을 둘러 묶었으니, 머리카락을 여미려 두르던 머리띠를 어렴풋이 본뜬 것이었다.\n\n수전은 그것을 팔 끝까지 뻗어 들고서, 웃음기라곤 없는 만족스러운 눈으로 그것을 바라보았다. 에그던 황야의 주민들을 아는 이라면 누구든 그 형상에서 유스테이샤 요브라이트를 떠올렸을 것이다.\n\n여인은 창가 자리의 반짇고리에서 핀 한 쌈을 꺼냈으니, 옛날식의 길고 누런 종류로, 처음 쓸 때부터 머리가 잘 빠지는 것들이었다. 이것들을 그녀는 형상에 사방으로 찔러 넣기 시작했으니, 보아하니 살을 에는 듯한 기세였다. 아마도 쉰 개쯤을 그렇게 찔러 넣었을 터인데, 더러는 밀랍 모형의 머리에, 더러는 어깨에, 더러는 몸통에, 더러는 발바닥을 뚫고 위쪽으로 박아, 마침내 그 형상은 온통 핀으로 빈틈없이 뒤덮였다.\n\n그녀는 불 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 그것은 토탄 불이었는데, 토탄 불이 만들어내는 그 높이 쌓인 잿더미가 겉은 다소 거뭇하고 사위어 있었으나, 부삽으로 그것을 헤쳐놓자 그 속살은 시뻘건 열기로 이글거리고 있었다. 그녀는 굴뚝 모퉁이에서 새 토탄 몇 덩이를 가져와 그 불기 위에 쌓아 올렸고, 그러자 불이 환해졌다. 부젓가락으로 자기가 만든 유스테이샤의 형상을 집어, 그녀는 그것을 그 열기 속에 들이대고, 그것이 서서히 사위어가기 시작하는 것을 지켜보았다. 그리고 그렇게 골몰해 서 있는 동안 그녀의 입술 사이로 나직이 웅얼거리는 말이 새어 나왔다.\n\n그것은 기이한 주문이었으니—거꾸로 외는 주기도문이었으며—원수를 거스를 부정한 도움을 얻으려는 일에서 으레 쓰이는 주술이었다. 수전은 그 음울한 사설을 세 번 천천히 읊조렸고, 그것이 끝났을 때 형상은 상당히 줄어들어 있었다. 밀랍이 불 속으로 떨어지자 그 자리에서 긴 불꽃이 일어, 혀를 형상에 감으며 그 몸을 한층 더 파먹어 들어갔다. 이따금 핀 하나가 밀랍과 함께 떨어지면, 잉걸불이 그것이 놓인 채로 시뻘겋게 달구었다.\n\nVIII.\n비와, 어둠과, 애타게 헤매는 이들\n\n유스테이샤의 형상이 녹아 사라져가고, 그 아름다운 여인 자신은 레인배로에 서서, 그토록 젊은 사람으로서는 좀처럼 가늠하기 힘든 황폐의 심연에 영혼을 담그고 있을 무렵, 요브라이트는 블룸스엔드에 홀로 앉아 있었다. 그는 페어웨이를 시켜 아내에게 편지를 보냄으로써 토머신에게 한 약속을 이행했으며, 이제는 그녀가 돌아오리라는 어떤 기척이나 신호를 더한층 초조하게 기다리고 있었다. 유스테이샤가 아직 미스토버에 있다면, 그가 바란 최소한의 것은 그녀가 오늘 밤 같은 사람 편에 답장을 보내주리라는 것이었으나, 모든 것을 그녀의 마음에 맡기고자 그는 페어웨이에게 답장을 청하지 말라고 일러두었다. 답장이 그에게 건네지면 즉시 가져오되, 그렇지 않으면 그날 밤 다시 블룸스엔드로 돌아오는 수고를 할 것 없이 곧장 집으로 가라고 했다.\n\n그러나 클림은 속으로 더 흐뭇한 희망을 품고 있었다. 유스테이샤가 어쩌면 펜을 들기를 마다할지도 모르고—말없이 일을 처리하는 것이 차라리 그녀다운 방식이었으니—제 집 문간에 나타나 그를 놀라게 할지도 몰랐다. 그녀의 마음이 얼마나 굳게 그와는 다른 쪽으로 정해져 있는지 그는 알지 못했다.\n\n클림으로서는 안타깝게도 저녁이 깊어가면서 비가 내리고 바람이 거세지기 시작했다. 바람이 집 모퉁이를 긁고 할퀴며, 처마에서 떨어지는 물방울을 콩알처럼 유리창에 튀겼다. 그는 빈방들을 안절부절못하고 거닐며, 나뭇조각을 창틀과 틈새에 끼워 넣고, 유리에서 헐거워진 격자창의 납틀을 서로 눌러 붙임으로써 창과 문에서 나는 기이한 소리들을 멎게 했다. 오래된 교회 벽의 갈라진 틈이 더 벌어지고, 쇠락한 장원 저택 천장의 해묵은 얼룩이 사람 손바닥만 한 크기에서 여러 피트 넓이로 새로 번지고 커지는, 그런 밤들 중 하나였다. 집 앞 말뚝 울타리의 작은 대문이 끊임없이 열렸다가 다시 딸깍 닫혔으나, 그가 애타게 내다보면 거기엔 아무도 없었으니, 마치 죽은 이들의 보이지 않는 형상들이 그를 찾아오는 길에 들어서고 있는 듯했다.\n\n열 시와 열한 시 사이, 페어웨이도 그 누구도 자기에게 오지 않는 것을 알고 그는 잠자리에 들었으며, 온갖 근심에도 이내 잠이 들었다. 그러나 그가 빠져들었던 그 기대 탓에 그의 잠은 그리 깊지 못했고, 한 시간쯤 뒤 문에서 시작된 두드림에 그는 쉽게 잠이 깼다. 클림은 일어나 창밖을 내다보았다. 비는 여전히 세차게 내리고 있었고, 그 앞에 펼쳐진 황야 전체가 쏟아지는 빗줄기 아래 나직이 쉭쉭거리는 소리를 내고 있었다. 너무 어두워 아무것도 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"거기 누구요?\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n가벼운 발소리가 현관에서 자리를 옮겼고, 그는 애처로운 여인의 목소리로 \"오, 클림, 내려와 나를 들여보내줘요!\" 하는 말을 겨우 알아들을 수 있었다.\n\n그는 동요로 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐다. \"분명 유스테이샤야!\" 그가 중얼거렸다. 그렇다면 그녀가 정말 뜻밖에 그에게 온 것이었다.\n\n그는 급히 불을 켜고 옷을 챙겨 입고는 내려갔다. 문을 활짝 열어젖히자 촛불이 몸을 꽁꽁 싸맨 한 여인을 비추었으니, 그녀가 즉시 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n\"토머신!\" 그가 형용할 수 없는 실망의 어조로 외쳤다. \"토머신이로구나, 그것도 이런 밤에! 오, 유스테이샤는 어디 있는 거지?\"\n\n토머신이었으니, 흠뻑 젖고 겁에 질려 헐떡이고 있었다.\n\n\"유스테이샤요? 나도 몰라요, 클림. 하지만 짐작은 가요.\" 그녀가 몹시 동요하며 말했다. \"안에 들어가 좀 쉬게 해줘요—내가 설명할게요. 큰일이 벌어지고 있어요—내 남편하고 유스테이샤가!\"\n\n\"뭐라, 뭐라고?\"\n\n\"내 남편이 나를 떠나려는 것 같아요, 아니면 무슨 끔찍한 짓을 하려는 건지—뭔지 모르겠어요—클림, 가서 좀 봐주겠어요? 나를 도와줄 사람은 오라버니밖에 없어요. 유스테이샤는 아직 집에 안 왔죠?\"\n\n\"안 왔다.\"\n그녀가 숨가쁘게 이어갔다. \"그럼 둘이서 함께 달아나려는 거예요! 그이가 오늘 밤 여덟 시쯤 집에 들어와서는 아무렇지 않은 듯 말하더라고요, '탐시, 방금 알았는데 나 어디 좀 다녀와야겠소.' '언제요?' 내가 물었죠. '오늘 밤이오.' 그가 말했어요. '어디로요?' 내가 물었죠. '지금은 말해줄 수 없소.' 그가 말했어요. '내일 다시 돌아올 거요.' 그러고는 가서 제 물건들을 챙기느라 부산을 떨며, 나는 거들떠보지도 않았어요. 나는 그이가 떠나는 걸 보려니 했는데, 떠나질 않더니, 열 시가 되자 '당신은 이만 자는 게 좋겠소' 하더군요. 나는 어찌할 바를 몰라 잠자리에 들었어요. 그이는 내가 잠든 줄 알았나봐요, 그러고 반 시간쯤 뒤에 올라와서는 집에 돈이 많을 때 넣어두는 떡갈나무 궤짝을 열고 무언가 둘둘 만 것을 꺼냈는데, 지폐 뭉치였던 것 같아요, 거기 그런 게 있는 줄도 몰랐는데 말이에요. 그건 분명 며칠 전 은행에 갔을 때 거기서 찾아온 거겠죠. 하루만 다녀올 거라면서 지폐는 뭣 하러 필요한 걸까요? 그이가 내려간 뒤 나는 유스테이샤 생각이 났어요, 그이가 어젯밤 그녀를 만난 것도요—분명히 만났어요, 클림, 내가 가는 길 일부를 따라가봤거든요. 하지만 오라버니가 찾아왔을 때는 차마 말할 수가 없었어요, 그래서 오라버니가 그이를 나쁘게 여기게 하고 싶지 않았던 거예요, 그렇게 심각한 일인 줄은 몰랐으니까요. 그러고 나니 도저히 침대에 있을 수가 없어서, 일어나 옷을 챙겨 입고, 그이가 마구간에 나가 있는 소리가 들리기에 와서 오라버니한테 알려야겠다 싶었어요. 그래서 소리 없이 아래층으로 내려와 빠져나온 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼 네가 떠나올 때 그는 아직 완전히 가버린 건 아니었구나?\"\n\n\"네. 사랑하는 클림 오라버니, 가서 그이가 떠나지 않도록 설득해보겠어요? 그이는 내가 하는 말은 들은 척도 안 하고, 여행을 다녀온다, 내일 돌아온다, 하는 따위의 얘기로 나를 따돌리는데, 나는 그 말을 믿지 않아요. 오라버니라면 그이를 움직일 수 있을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"가마.\" 클림이 말했다. \"오, 유스테이샤!\"\n\n토머신은 두 팔에 커다란 보따리를 안고 있었는데, 이때쯤 자리에 앉아 그것을 풀기 시작하자, 겉껍질 속의 알맹이처럼 아기가 나타났으니—보송보송하고 따뜻하며, 여정도 궂은 날씨도 모르는 채였다. 토머신은 아기에게 가볍게 입을 맞추고는, 그제야 짬을 내어 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"이 애한테 무슨 일이 생길까 두려워 데려왔어요. 아마 이 애 목숨을 앗는 일이 되겠지만, 레이철한테 맡기고 올 수가 없었어요!\"\n\n클림은 급히 화덕에 장작을 모아 쌓고, 아직 채 꺼지지 않은 잉걸불을 헤쳐 놓고는, 풀무로 불을 일으켰다.\n\n\"몸을 말리거라.\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 가서 장작을 좀 더 가져오마.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 아뇨—그것 때문에 지체하지 말아요. 불은 내가 지필게요. 곧장 가주겠어요—제발 가주겠어요?\"\n\n요브라이트는 옷차림을 마무리하려 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다. 그가 간 사이 또 한 번 문 두드리는 소리가 났다. 이번에는 그것이 유스테이샤의 것일지 모른다는 헛된 기대는 없었으니—그에 앞서 들린 발소리가 묵직하고 느렸던 까닭이다. 요브라이트는 어쩌면 페어웨이가 답장을 가지고 온 것일지도 모른다 생각하며 다시 내려가 문을 열었다.\n\n\"바이 선장님?\" 그가 물에 흠뻑 젖은 형상에게 말했다.\n\n\"내 손녀가 여기 있는가?\" 선장이 말했다.\n\"아뇨.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 애는 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 자네가 알아야지—자네가 그 애 남편이 아닌가.\"\n\n\"이름만 그런 모양이지요.\" 클림이 점점 격해지며 말했다. \"그녀는 오늘 밤 와일디브와 달아날 작정인 것 같습니다. 저는 막 그걸 살피러 가려던 참이고요.\"\n\n\"음, 그 애는 내 집을 떠났네. 반 시간쯤 전에 나갔어. 거기 앉아 있는 건 누군가?\"\n\n\"제 사촌 토머신입니다.\"\n\n선장이 정신이 딴 데 팔린 듯 그녀에게 인사를 했다. \"그저 사랑의 도피보다 더 나쁜 일이나 아니길 바랄 뿐이네.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"더 나쁘다니요? 아내가 저지를 수 있는 가장 나쁜 일보다 더 나쁜 게 무어란 말입니까?\"\n\n\"음, 내 기이한 이야기를 하나 들었네. 그 애를 찾아 나서기 전에 내 마구간지기 소년 찰리를 불러 세웠지. 며칠 전 내 권총들이 없어진 걸 알았거든.\"\n\n\"권총이요?\"\n\n\"그때 그 애는 닦으려고 그걸 내렸다고 하더군. 헌데 이제 와 털어놓기를, 유스테이샤가 그걸 묘하게 바라보는 걸 봐서 가져갔다는 거야. 그리고 그 애는 나중에 그에게 제 목숨을 끊을 생각을 하고 있었다고 실토했다지, 헌데 그에게 비밀을 지키라 다짐을 두고, 다시는 그런 생각을 않겠다 약속했다는군. 그 애가 그중 하나를 쓸 만큼 객기를 부리리라고는 도무지 생각지 않네만, 그건 그 애 마음속에 무엇이 도사려왔는지를 보여주지. 그리고 한 번 그런 걸 생각한 사람은 또 생각하는 법이거든.\"\n\n\"권총들은 어디 있습니까?\"\n\n\"안전하게 잠가뒀네. 오 아니, 그 애는 다시는 거기 손대지 않을 거야. 하지만 총알구멍 말고도 목숨을 내보낼 길은 더 있지. 대체 무엇 때문에 그 애와 그리 모질게 다투어 그 애를 이 지경까지 몰아넣은 건가? 자네가 그 애를 정말 못되게 다룬 게 틀림없어. 음, 나는 늘 이 혼인을 반대했고, 내가 옳았던 거야.\"\n\n\"저와 함께 가시겠습니까?\" 요브라이트가 선장의 뒷말에는 아랑곳하지 않고 말했다. \"그러시면 가는 길에 우리가 무엇 때문에 다투었는지 말씀드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어디로 가는가?\"\n\n\"와일드브 집으로—거기가 그 사람 목적지요, 틀림없이.\"\n\n토마신이 여기서 여전히 흐느끼며 끼어들었다. \"그이는 그저 갑작스럽게 잠깐 다녀올 데가 있다고 했어요. 하지만 정말 그렇다면 왜 그렇게 많은 돈이 필요했겠어요? 아, 클림, 무슨 일이 벌어질 것 같아요? 가엾은 우리 아기, 너는 곧 아버지마저 잃게 될까 봐 무서워요!\"\n\n\"이제 가야겠어요.\" 요브라이트가 현관으로 들어서며 말했다.\n\n\"나도 자네와 함께 가고 싶네만.\" 노인이 미심쩍게 말했다. \"이런 밤에 내 다리가 거기까지 나를 데려다줄 수 있을지 슬슬 두렵네. 예전만큼 젊지가 못하니. 만약 도망치다가 누가 가로막으면 그 애는 분명 내게로 돌아올 테고, 그러면 나는 집에서 그 애를 맞이해야 하지. 하지만 어찌 됐든 나는 콰이엇 우먼까지 걸어갈 수 없으니, 그걸로 끝일세. 곧장 집으로 가겠네.\"\n\n\"그게 아마 가장 나을 겁니다.\" 클림이 말했다. \"토마신, 몸을 좀 말리고 되도록 편히 있어요.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 그녀 앞에서 문을 닫고는, 바이 선장과 함께 집을 나섰는데, 선장은 대문 밖에서 그와 갈라져 미스토버로 이어지는 가운뎃길로 접어들었다. 클림은 오른쪽 샛길을 가로질러 여관 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n홀로 남겨진 토마신은 젖은 옷가지를 몇 벌 벗고, 아기를 위층 클림의 침대로 안고 올라갔다가, 다시 거실로 내려와 불을 더 크게 지피고는 몸을 말리기 시작했다. 불은 곧 굴뚝 위로 활활 타올라 방 안에 안락한 기운을 자아냈는데, 그것은 바깥에서 두드려대는 폭풍과의 대비로 곱절이 되었으니, 폭풍은 창유리를 물어뜯고 굴뚝 속으로 기이한 낮은 소리를 불어넣었으며, 그 소리는 어떤 비극의 서막인 듯했다.\n\n하지만 토마신의 마음은 거의 집 안에 있지 않았으니, 위층 어린 딸에 대한 걱정이 가라앉자 그녀는 마음속으로 길 떠난 클림을 뒤따르고 있었다. 이 상상의 여정에 상당한 시간 동안 빠져 있다가, 그녀는 시간이 견딜 수 없을 만큼 더디 흐른다는 느낌에 사로잡혔다. 그래도 그녀는 그대로 앉아 있었다. 그러다 더는 앉아 있을 수 없는 순간이 닥쳤고, 클림이 아직 여관에 닿았을 리 만무하다는 것을 떠올리니 그것은 마치 그녀의 인내심을 비웃는 풍자 같았다. 마침내 그녀는 아기의 머리맡으로 갔다. 아이는 곤히 잠들어 있었다. 하지만 집에서 어쩌면 끔찍한 일이 벌어졌을지 모른다는 상상, 보이는 것보다 보이지 않는 것이 그녀 안에서 우세를 점하는 형국이 견딜 수 없을 만큼 그녀를 흔들어놓았다.\n그녀는 아래층으로 내려가 문을 여는 것을 참을 수 없었다. 비는 여전히 쏟아지고 있었고, 촛불은 가장 가까운 빗방울들 위로 떨어져 그것들이 뒤편에 무리 지은 보이지 않는 빗방울들을 가로질러 내려올 때 반짝이는 화살처럼 만들어냈다. 그 매질 속으로 뛰어든다는 것은 공기로 살짝 묽어진 물속으로 뛰어드는 것이나 다름없었다. 하지만 지금 이 순간에 집으로 돌아가기가 어렵다는 점이 도리어 그녀로 하여금 더욱 그렇게 하고 싶게 만들었으니—그 무엇이든 마음 졸이는 것보다는 나았다. \"여기까지 그럭저럭 잘 왔잖아.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그러니 다시 못 돌아갈 게 뭐람? 내가 집을 비운 건 잘못이야.\"\n\n그녀는 서둘러 아기를 데려와 감싸고, 전처럼 망토를 두른 다음, 사고를 막으려고 불 위에 재를 끼얹고는 바깥으로 나섰다. 우선 멈춰 서서 문 열쇠를 덧문 뒤 본래 자리에 둔 뒤, 그녀는 단호히 울타리 너머에 마주 선 창공의 어둠 덩어리를 향해 얼굴을 돌리고 그 한복판으로 발을 내디뎠다. 하지만 토마신의 상상은 다른 데 그토록 활발히 매여 있었던 터라, 밤과 날씨는 그녀에게 실제의 불편함과 어려움 이상의 두려움은 주지 못했다.\n\n이윽고 그녀는 블룸스엔드 골짜기를 오르며 언덕 비탈의 굴곡을 가로지르고 있었다. 황야 위로 부는 바람 소리는 날카로웠고, 마치 이토록 마음에 드는 밤을 만난 기쁨에 휘파람을 부는 듯했다. 이따금 길은 그녀를 키 큰 고사리 덤불 사이 우묵한 곳으로 이끌었는데, 물방울이 뚝뚝 듣는 그 고사리들은 죽었으나 아직 쓰러지지 않은 채 그녀를 못처럼 에워쌌다. 고사리가 평소보다 더 키 큰 곳에서는 그녀가 아기를 머리끝까지 들어 올려 흠뻑 젖은 잎사귀가 닿지 않게 했다. 바람이 거세고 끊임없는 더 높은 지대에서는 비가 느낄 만큼 떨어지는 기색도 없이 수평으로 날아들어, 그것이 구름의 품을 떠난 지점이 얼마나 먼지 가늠하기란 도무지 불가능했다. 여기서는 방어란 불가능했고, 빗방울 하나하나가 마치 성 세바스티아누스에게 박힌 화살처럼 그녀에게 박혔다. 그녀는 웅덩이의 존재를 알려주는 흐릿한 창백함 덕에 웅덩이를 피할 수 있었는데, 황야보다 덜 어두운 무언가 곁에 있었다면 그 웅덩이들 자체가 검은 빛깔로 보였을 것이었다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 것에도 토마신은 길을 나선 것을 후회하지 않았다. 그녀에게는, 유스테이샤에게와 달리, 대기 속에 악령이 있다거나 모든 덤불과 가지마다 악의가 깃들어 있다거나 하지 않았다. 그녀의 얼굴을 후려치는 빗방울은 전갈이 아니라 그저 무미건조한 비였고, 에그던 전체도 결코 괴물 따위가 아니라 비인격적인 트인 땅이었다. 이 장소에 대한 그녀의 두려움은 이치에 맞았고, 그 최악의 변덕에 대한 그녀의 혐오는 합당했다. 지금 그녀가 보기에 이곳은 사람이 적잖이 불편을 겪고, 조심하지 않으면 길을 잃고, 어쩌면 감기에 걸릴 수도 있는, 바람 불고 비 내리는 장소였다.\n\n길을 잘 알면 이런 때에도 그 길을 벗어나지 않기란 그리 어렵지 않으니, 발에 닿는 익숙한 감촉 덕분이다. 하지만 일단 길을 잃으면 되찾을 길이 없다. 토마신의 앞쪽 시야를 다소 가리고 정신을 흩뜨리는 아기 탓에, 그녀는 끝내 길을 잃고 말았다. 이 낭패는 그녀가 집까지 삼분의 이쯤 와서 트인 비탈을 내려가던 중에 일어났다. 이리저리 헤매며 한낱 실오라기 같은 길을 찾으려는 가망 없는 일을 시도하는 대신, 그녀는 곧장 앞으로 나아가며 지형의 윤곽에 관한 전반적인 지식에 의지해 길을 가늠했는데, 그 지식은 클림이나 황야의 풀 뜯는 사람들의 것에도 거의 뒤지지 않았다.\n\n마침내 토마신은 우묵한 곳에 이르러 빗속으로 희미하게 번진 빛을 분간하기 시작했는데, 그 빛은 이내 열린 문의 길쭉한 형태를 띠었다. 그녀는 이 근방에 집이 없다는 것을 알았고, 그 문이 땅 위로 솟은 높이를 보고 곧 그것의 정체를 알아챘다.\n\n\"아니, 이건 분명 디고리 벤의 마차잖아!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n레인배로 인근의 어느 외진 곳이, 그녀가 알기로, 벤이 이 일대에 머물 때 곧잘 택하는 거점이었다. 그래서 그녀는 자기가 이 수수께끼 같은 은신처에 우연히 닿았음을 단번에 짐작했다. 그를 불러 길로 안내해달라고 청할 것인가 말 것인가 하는 물음이 그녀 마음에 일었다. 집에 닿고 싶은 조바심에, 그녀는 이런 장소와 계절에 그의 눈앞에 나타나는 일이 아무리 기이하다 한들 그에게 도움을 청하기로 마음을 정했다. 하지만 이 결심을 좇아 토마신이 마차에 이르러 안을 들여다보니, 그것은 비어 있었다. 그것이 레들먼의 것임에는 의심의 여지가 없었지만 말이다. 난로에는 불이 타고 있었고, 등불은 못에 걸려 있었다. 문간 둘레의 바닥에는 비가 흩뿌려졌을 뿐 흠뻑 젖지는 않았는데, 이는 문이 열린 지 오래되지 않았음을 그녀에게 일러주었다.\n\n머뭇거리며 안을 들여다보고 서 있는데 토마신은 뒤편 어둠 속에서 다가오는 발소리를 들었고, 돌아보니 머리부터 발끝까지 시뻘건, 코듀로이 차림의 낯익은 형상이 보였으며, 등불 빛이 가로놓인 빗방울의 망사를 뚫고 그에게 떨어지고 있었다.\n\n\"비탈 아래로 내려간 줄 알았는데.\" 그가 그녀의 얼굴은 알아보지 못한 채 말했다. \"어쩌다 다시 여기로 돌아왔소?\"\n\n\"디고리?\" 토마신이 가냘프게 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 누구요?\" 벤이 여전히 알아채지 못하고 말했다. \"그리고 방금은 왜 그리 울었소?\"\n\n\"아, 디고리! 나를 못 알아보겠어요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하긴 이렇게 둘둘 감싸고 있으니 그럴 만도 하죠. 무슨 말이에요? 나는 여기서 운 적도 없고, 전에 여기 온 적도 없는걸요.\"\n\n그러자 벤이 더 가까이 다가와 그녀 형상의 불빛 비치는 쪽을 볼 수 있게 되었다.\n\n\"와일드브 부인!\" 그가 흠칫 놀라며 외쳤다. \"우리가 만날 때가 따로 있지! 게다가 아기까지! 도대체 무슨 끔찍한 일이 있었기에 이런 밤에 밖으로 나오셨소?\"\n\n그녀는 곧바로 대답할 수 없었다. 그러자 그는 허락을 구하지도 않고 자기 마차로 훌쩍 올라타 그녀의 팔을 잡고 뒤따라 끌어 올렸다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 두 사람이 안에 서자 그가 이어 물었다.\n\n\"블룸스엔드에서 오는 길에 길을 잃었어요. 어서 집에 가야 해요. 되도록 빨리 길을 가르쳐주세요! 에그던을 더 잘 알지 못하는 게 어찌나 어리석은지, 어쩌다 길을 잃었는지 도무지 모르겠어요. 어서 가르쳐주세요, 디고리, 제발요.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 물론이죠. 함께 가드리리다. 그런데 부인, 이보다 먼저 제게 오시지 않았소?\"\n\n\"방금 막 왔는걸요.\"\n\n\"이상한 일이군요. 한 오 분 전쯤에 나는 날씨를 막으려고 문을 닫아두고 여기 누워 잠들어 있었는데, 바로 바깥 황야 덤불 위를 스치는 여인의 옷자락 소리에 잠이 깼소. 나는 잠귀가 밝아서 말이오. 그와 동시에 같은 여인이 흐느끼거나 우는 소리를 들었지요. 문을 열고 등불을 내미니, 빛이 닿는 데까지 어떤 여인이 보였소. 빛이 비치자 그 여인은 고개를 돌리더니, 비탈 아래로 서둘러 가버렸지. 나는 등불을 다시 걸고는, 호기심이 일어 옷을 챙겨 입고 몇 걸음 뒤쫓았지만, 그 여인은 더 이상 어디서도 보이지 않았소. 부인이 올라왔을 때 내가 가 있던 데가 바로 거기였고, 부인을 보고 나는 같은 여인인 줄 알았던 거요.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 집으로 가던 황야 사람 가운데 하나였을지도 모르죠?\"\n\n\"아니, 그럴 리 없소. 너무 늦은 시각이오. 황야 위를 스치던 그 가운 소리는 비단이 아니고서는 낼 수 없는 휘파람 같은 소리였소.\"\n\n\"그럼 나는 아니었네요. 보다시피 내 옷은 비단이 아니니까요…… 우리가 지금 미스토버와 여관 사이 어디쯤에 있는 건가요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 그렇소. 그리 벗어나지는 않았소.\"\n\n\"아, 그게 그 여자였을까! 디고리, 당장 가야겠어요!\"\n\n그가 미처 알아채기도 전에 그녀가 마차에서 뛰어내리자, 벤은 등불을 떼어 들고 그녀를 따라 뛰어내렸다. \"아기는 제가 안겠소, 부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"무게에 지쳐 녹초가 되셨겠소.\"\n\n토마신은 잠시 망설이다가 아기를 벤의 손에 건넸다. \"꽉 쥐지 마세요, 디고리.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"작은 팔도 다치게 하지 말고요. 망토는 이렇게 바짝 덮어서 빗물이 얼굴에 떨어지지 않게 해주세요.\"\n\n\"그러리다.\" 벤이 진지하게 말했다. \"내가 어찌 부인에게 딸린 무엇 하나라도 다치게 하겠소!\"\n\n\"그저 실수로 그럴까 봐 그런 거예요.\" 토마신이 말했다.\n\n\"아기는 충분히 말라 있지만, 부인은 흠뻑 젖으셨소.\" 레들먼은 자기 수레의 문을 닫아 자물쇠를 채우다가, 바닥에 그녀의 망토가 드리워졌던 자리로 동그랗게 떨어진 물방울 자국을 보고 말했다.\n\n토마신은 그가 더 큰 덤불을 피해 오른쪽 왼쪽으로 굽이굽이 나아가는 것을 뒤따랐는데, 그는 이따금 멈춰 서서 등불을 가리고는, 어깨너머로 머리 위 레인배로의 위치를 가늠하려 돌아보았으니, 제대로 된 방향을 유지하려면 그것을 정확히 등 뒤에 두어야 했다.\n\n\"비가 아기한테 안 떨어지는 거 확실해요?\"\n\n\"아주 확실하오. 한데 부인, 이 녀석은 몇 살이오?\"\n\n\"이 녀석이라뇨!\" 토마신이 나무라듯 말했다. \"누구든 보면 한눈에 알 텐데. 얘는 태어난 지 두 달이 다 됐어요. 여관까지는 이제 얼마나 남았죠?\"\n\n\"사분의 일 마일 좀 넘게 남았소.\"\n\n\"좀 더 빨리 걸어줄래요?\"\n\n\"부인이 못 따라오실까 봐 그랬소.\"\n\n\"거기 닿는 게 너무도 조급해요. 아, 저기 창문에서 불빛이 비치네요!\"\n\n\"창문에서 나는 게 아니오. 내가 보기엔 마차 등불이오.\"\n\n\"아!\" 토마신이 절망하여 말했다. \"더 일찍 거기 닿았더라면—아기를 주세요, 디고리—이제 돌아가셔도 돼요.\"\n\n\"끝까지 가야 하오.\" 벤이 말했다. \"우리와 저 불빛 사이에 늪이 있어서, 내가 부인을 돌아가게 하지 않으면 목까지 빠지게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 불빛은 여관에 있고, 여관 앞에는 늪이 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"아니오, 그 불빛은 여관에서 이삼백 야드쯤 아래에 있소.\"\n\n\"상관없어요.\" 토마신이 서둘러 말했다. \"여관 쪽이 아니라 불빛 쪽으로 가요.\"\n\n\"알겠소.\" 벤이 그 말에 따라 방향을 틀며 답했고, 잠시 사이를 두었다가 말했다. \"이 큰 걱정거리가 무엇인지 제게 말씀해주시면 좋겠소. 부인은 내가 믿을 만한 사람이라는 걸 증명받으신 셈이잖소.\"\n\n\"세상엔 말할 수 없는—말할 수 없는 일이 있어요—\" 그러고는 가슴이 목구멍까지 치밀어 올라 그녀는 더 말을 잇지 못했다.\n\nIX.\n광경과 소리가 방랑자들을 한데로 이끌고\n\n여덟 시에 언덕에서 유스테이샤의 신호를 보았던 와일드브는 곧바로 그녀의 도주를 돕고, 또 바라건대 그녀와 동행할 채비를 했다. 그는 다소 마음이 어지러웠고, 길을 떠난다고 토마신에게 알린 그 태도만으로도 그녀의 의심을 사기에 충분했다. 그녀가 잠자리에 들자 그는 필요한 몇 가지 물건을 챙긴 뒤, 위층의 돈궤로 가서 적잖이 넉넉한 금액의 지폐를 꺼냈는데, 그 돈은 그가 곧 손에 넣게 될 재산을 담보로 그에게 미리 내어준 것으로, 이주에 따르는 비용을 치르기 위한 것이었다.\n\n그런 다음 그는 마구간과 마차 보관소로 가서 말과 이륜마차와 마구가 먼 길을 가기에 적당한 상태인지 확인했다. 거의 반 시간이 그렇게 흘렀고, 집으로 돌아온 와일드브는 토마신이 잠자리 말고 다른 어디에 있으리라고는 생각조차 하지 않았다. 그는 마구간 소년에게 깨어 있지 말라고 일러두었는데, 자기 출발이 새벽 서너 시쯤이리라고 소년이 알아듣게끔 했으니, 그 시각이 비록 예사롭지는 않으나, 실제로 약속한 시각인 자정보다는 덜 이상했기 때문이요, 버드머스에서 오는 배는 한 시와 두 시 사이에 출항하기로 되어 있었다.\n\n마침내 사방이 조용해졌고, 그는 기다리는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었다. 유스테이샤와 마지막으로 만난 이래로 줄곧 겪어온 마음의 짓눌림을 그는 아무리 애써도 떨쳐낼 수 없었으나, 자기 처지에는 돈으로 고칠 수 있는 무언가가 있으리라 바랐다. 그는 자기 재산의 절반을 온화한 아내에게 떼어줌으로써 그녀에게 야박하지 않게 굴고, 또 다른 더 위대한 여인과 운명을 함께함으로써 그녀에게 기사도적 헌신을 다하는 것이 가능하다고 스스로를 설득해두었다. 그리고 비록 그는 유스테이샤가 원하는 곳에 그녀를 내려놓고, 그녀의 뜻이 그렇다면 그녀를 떠나라는 그녀의 지시를 한 자도 어김없이 따르려 했으나, 그녀가 그에게 건 마력은 점점 강해졌고, 둘이 운명을 함께하기를 바라는 서로의 마음 앞에서 그런 지시가 부질없으리라는 예감에 그의 심장은 빠르게 뛰고 있었다.\n\n그는 이런 추측과 격언과 희망에 오래 머무는 것을 스스로 허락하지 않았으니, 열두 시 이십 분 전에 그는 다시 살그머니 마구간으로 가서 말에 마구를 채우고 등을 켰으며, 그런 다음 말의 머리를 잡고 덮개 씌운 수레와 함께 마당을 빠져나가 여관에서 사분의 일 마일쯤 아래 길가의 한 지점으로 끌고 갔다.\n\n여기서 와일드브는, 이곳에 쌓아 올려진 높은 둑에 몰아치는 비를 조금 피하며 기다렸다. 등불에 비친 길 위에서는 풀린 자갈과 작은 돌멩이들이 바람 앞에서 후드득 굴러가며 서로 부딪쳐 딸각거렸고, 바람은 그것들을 무더기로 남겨둔 채 황야 속으로 뛰어들어 덤불을 가로질러 어둠 속으로 우르릉거렸다. 이 날씨의 소란 위로 솟아오르는 소리는 단 하나뿐이었으니, 그것은 남쪽으로 열 개의 수문을 둔 둑에서 나는 굉음이었고, 그 둑은 이 방향에서 황야의 경계를 이루는 풀밭의 강에 있었다.\n\n그는 완벽한 정적 속에서 머뭇거리며 머물다가, 어느덧 자정이 쳤을 게 틀림없다는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 유스테이샤가 이런 날씨에 과연 언덕을 내려올까 하는 의구심이 그의 마음에 강하게 일었으나, 그녀의 성정을 아는 터라 그는 그녀가 그럴 수도 있겠다고 느꼈다. \"가엾은 것! 그 애의 불운다운 일이지.\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n마침내 그는 등불 쪽으로 돌아서서 시계를 보았다. 놀랍게도 자정에서 십오 분쯤 지나 있었다. 이제 그는 미스토버로 빙 도는 길로 마차를 몰고 올라갔더라면 좋았을 것이라 여겼는데, 그 길을 택하지 않은 까닭은 트인 비탈을 걸어 내려오는 보행로에 비해 그 노선이 엄청나게 긴 데다, 그만큼 말의 수고가 늘어나기 때문이었다.\n\n이 순간 발소리가 다가왔다. 하지만 등불은 다른 방향을 비추고 있던 터라 오는 사람은 보이지 않았다. 발소리가 멈추었다가 다시 다가왔다.\n\n\"유스테이샤?\" 와일드브가 말했다.\n\n그 사람이 앞으로 나오자 빛이 클림의 형상 위로 떨어졌는데, 그는 흠뻑 젖어 번들거리고 있었고, 와일드브는 그를 즉시 알아보았다. 하지만 등불 뒤에 서 있던 와일드브를 요브라이트는 곧바로 알아보지 못했다.\n\n그는 이 기다리는 마차가 자기 아내의 도주와 무슨 관계가 있는지 없는지 의심하듯 멈춰 섰다. 요브라이트의 모습은 와일드브의 차분한 감정을 단숨에 몰아냈으니, 그는 클림을 또다시 그 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 유스테이샤에게서 떼어놓아야 할 철천지원수로 보았다. 그리하여 와일드브는, 클림이 별다른 캐물음 없이 지나쳐 가기를 바라며 입을 열지 않았다.\n\n둘이 그렇게 머뭇거리며 멈춰 있는 동안 폭풍과 바람 위로 둔탁한 소리가 들려왔다. 그 소리의 정체는 틀림없었으니—이웃한 풀밭의 개울로 무언가가 떨어진 소리였고, 둑 가까운 지점인 듯했다.\n\n둘 다 흠칫했다. \"맙소사! 그 애일 수가 있나?\" 클림이 말했다.\n\n\"왜 그게 그 애겠소?\" 와일드브가 당황한 나머지, 자신이 여태 몸을 숨기고 있었다는 것을 잊고 말했다.\n\n\"아!—당신이군, 이 배신자, 그렇지?\" 요브라이트가 외쳤다. \"왜 그게 그 애냐고? 지난주에 그 사람은 할 수만 있었다면 제 목숨을 끊으려 했으니까. 누군가 지켜봤어야 했어! 등불 하나 들고 나를 따라와.\"\n\n요브라이트는 자기 쪽에 있던 등불을 움켜쥐고 서둘러 나아갔다. 와일드브는 다른 등불을 풀러 떼낼 새도 없이 곧장 클림보다 조금 뒤처져 풀밭 길을 따라 둑으로 뒤따랐다.\n\n섀드워터 둑은 그 아래에 지름 오십 피트의 커다란 원형 못을 두고 있었는데, 그 못으로 물이 열 개의 거대한 수문을 통해 흘러들었고, 수문은 여느 방식대로 권양기와 톱니바퀴로 올리고 내렸다. 못의 가장자리는 물이 둑을 씻어내지 못하도록 석조로 되어 있었으나, 겨울철 물살의 힘이 때로 워낙 거세어 그 옹벽 밑을 파고들어 구멍 속으로 무너뜨리기도 했다. 클림은 수문에 이르렀는데, 그 수문틀은 물살의 빠르기에 기초까지 흔들리고 있었다. 아래 못에서는 물결의 거품 말고는 아무것도 분간할 수 없었다. 그는 방수로 위에 놓인 널다리에 올라서서, 바람에 떠밀려 떨어지지 않도록 난간을 붙잡고 강 건너편으로 건너갔다. 거기서 그는 벽 너머로 몸을 기울여 등불을 내렸으나, 되돌아 흐르는 물살이 휘말리는 곳에 생긴 소용돌이만 보일 뿐이었다.\n\n그사이 와일드브는 먼젓번 쪽에 다다랐고, 요브라이트의 등불에서 나온 빛이 얼룩지고 일렁이는 광채를 둑 못 위로 던지며, 위쪽 수문에서 쏟아져 내려 뒤엉키는 물줄기를 그 옛 기사技士에게 드러내 보였다. 이 베이고 주름진 거울 위로 검은 물체 하나가 뒤로 흐르는 물살 가운데 하나에 실려 천천히 떠밀려 왔다.\n\n\"오, 내 사랑!\" 와일드브가 고통에 찬 목소리로 외쳤다. 그러고는 외투를 벗어 던질 분별력조차 보이지 못한 채, 부글거리는 가마솥 속으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n이제 요브라이트도 떠 있는 물체를 분간할 수 있었으나, 흐릿하게나마였다. 와일드브가 뛰어든 것을 보고 구할 목숨이 있는 줄로 여겨 그도 막 뒤따라 뛰어들려 했다. 더 현명한 방도를 떠올린 그는 등불이 똑바로 서 있도록 기둥에 기대 세워놓고, 벽이 없는 못의 아래쪽으로 돌아 뛰어가, 그 안으로 뛰어들어 대담하게 더 깊은 쪽을 향해 거슬러 헤쳐 나아갔다. 여기서 그는 발이 땅에서 떨어졌고, 헤엄을 치다 보니 못의 한복판으로 휩쓸려 들어갔는데, 거기서 그는 와일드브가 허우적거리는 것을 알아보았다.\n\n이곳에서 이 다급한 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 벤과 토마신은 그 불빛 방향으로 황야의 아래쪽 모퉁이를 헤치며 애써 나아가고 있었다. 그들은 첨벙 소리를 들을 만큼 강에 가까이 있지는 못했으나, 마차 등불이 옮겨지는 것을 보았고, 그것이 풀밭으로 움직여 가는 것을 지켜보았다. 수레와 말에 다다르자마자 벤은 무언가 새로운 일이 어긋났음을 짐작하고, 움직이는 불빛의 행로를 서둘러 뒤따랐다. 벤은 토마신보다 빨리 걸었던 터라, 혼자서 둑에 다다랐다.\n\n클림이 기둥에 기대 세워둔 등불은 여전히 물 위로 빛을 비추고 있었고, 레들먼은 무언가가 미동도 없이 떠 있는 것을 보았다. 아기를 안고 있던 터라 그는 토마신을 맞으러 도로 뛰어갔다.\n\n\"아기를 받으세요, 와일드브 부인.\" 그가 다급히 말했다. \"아기를 데리고 집으로 뛰어가, 마구간 소년을 불러서, 근처에 사는 남자들을 누구든 내게로 내려보내게 하시오. 누군가 둑에 빠졌소.\"\n\n토마신은 아이를 받아 안고 뛰었다. 그녀가 덮개 씌운 수레에 이르렀을 때, 말은 마구간에서 갓 나온 참인데도, 마치 불행을 의식하기라도 한 듯 완벽히 가만히 서 있었다. 그녀는 그것이 누구의 것인지 그제야 처음으로 알아보았다. 그녀는 거의 졸도할 뻔했고, 어린 딸을 화에서 지켜야 한다는 절박함이 그녀에게 놀라운 자제력을 불어넣지 않았더라면 한 발짝도 더 나아갈 수 없었을 것이다. 이 마음 졸이는 고통 속에서 그녀는 집으로 들어가 아기를 안전한 곳에 누이고, 소년과 하녀를 깨운 다음, 가장 가까운 오두막으로 달려가 알렸다.\n\n못 가장자리로 돌아온 디고리는, 위쪽의 작은 수문, 즉 부표들이 빠져 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 그중 하나가 풀밭에 놓여 있는 것을 발견하고, 그것을 한쪽 팔에 끼고 다른 손에 등불을 든 채, 클림이 그랬던 것처럼 못의 아래쪽으로 들어갔다. 깊은 물에 잠기기 시작하자마자 그는 그 수문 위로 몸을 던졌다. 그리하여 받쳐진 그는 빈 손으로 등불을 높이 치켜든 채 원하는 만큼 떠 있을 수 있었다. 발로 밀어내며 그는 못을 빙빙 돌았는데, 매번 뒤로 흐르는 물줄기 가운데 하나를 타고 올라갔다가 물살 한복판으로 내려왔다.\n\n처음에 그는 아무것도 볼 수 없었다. 그러다 소용돌이의 번득임과 하얀 거품 덩어리들 가운데서 그는 여인의 보닛이 홀로 떠 있는 것을 분간했다. 이제 그의 수색은 왼쪽 벽 아래에서 이루어지고 있었는데, 무언가가 거의 그의 바로 곁에서 수면으로 떠올랐다. 그것은 그가 예상했던 것과 달리 여인이 아니라 남자였다. 레들먼은 등불의 고리를 이로 문 채, 떠 있는 남자의 멱살을 움켜쥐고, 남은 한 팔로 수문을 붙들면서, 가장 거센 물줄기 속으로 헤쳐 나아갔는데, 그 물줄기에 의식 잃은 남자와 수문과 그 자신이 함께 하류로 떠밀려 갔다. 발이 아래쪽 더 얕은 곳의 자갈에 끌리는 것을 벤이 느끼자마자 그는 발 디딜 자리를 단단히 잡고 가장자리를 향해 헤쳐 나아갔다. 거기, 물이 그의 허리 높이쯤 차오르는 곳에서, 그는 수문을 던져버리고 남자를 끌어내려 했다. 이것은 무척 어려운 일이었는데, 그 까닭은 그 불운한 낯선 남자의 다리를, 여태 완전히 수면 아래 잠겨 있던 또 다른 남자의 팔이 단단히 끌어안고 있기 때문임을 그는 알게 되었다.\n\n이 순간 그를 향해 달려오는 발소리에 그의 심장이 뛰었으니, 토마신에게 깨워진 두 남자가 위쪽 가장자리에 나타났다. 그들은 벤이 있는 곳으로 달려와, 익사한 듯한 사람들을 들어 올려 떼어놓고 풀밭에 눕히는 것을 도왔다. 벤은 그들의 얼굴에 불빛을 비추었다. 위에 있던 사람은 요브라이트였고, 완전히 물에 잠겨 있던 사람은 와일드브였다.\n\n\"이제 구멍을 다시 뒤져야 하오.\" 벤이 말했다. \"그 안 어딘가에 여자가 있소. 장대를 가져오시오.\"\n\n남자들 중 하나가 널다리로 가서 난간을 뜯어냈다. 그러자 레들먼과 다른 두 사람은 다 함께 아래쪽에서 전처럼 물속으로 들어가, 합친 힘으로 못이 그 한복판의 깊은 곳으로 비스듬히 떨어지는 데까지 앞으로 더듬어 나아갔다. 마지막으로 가라앉은 사람이라면 누구든 이 지점으로 떠밀려 내려가리라는 벤의 짐작은 틀리지 않았으니, 그들이 절반쯤 가로질러 살폈을 때 무언가가 그들의 찔러대는 장대를 가로막았다.\n\n\"앞으로 끌어당기시오.\" 벤이 말했고, 그들은 그것이 발치에 가까이 올 때까지 장대로 긁어당겼다.\n\n벤이 물속으로 사라졌다가, 여인의 차가운 형체를 감싼 젖은 옷자락을 한 아름 안고 떠올랐는데, 그것이 절망에 빠졌던 유스테이샤에게 남은 전부였다.\n\n그들이 둑에 다다랐을 때 거기에는 토마신이, 슬픔에 짓눌린 채, 이미 그곳에 누인 의식 없는 두 사람 위로 몸을 숙이고 서 있었다. 말과 수레가 길의 가장 가까운 지점으로 끌려왔고, 셋을 마차에 싣는 데는 몇 분밖에 걸리지 않았다. 벤은 토마신을 자기 팔로 부축하며 말을 끌고 앞장섰고, 두 남자가 뒤따라 여관에 다다랐다.\n\n토마신에게 잠을 깨운 여인은 황급히 옷을 챙겨 입고 불을 지폈는데, 다른 하인은 집 뒤편에서 편히 코를 골도록 그대로 두었다. 그런 다음 유스테이샤와 클림과 와일드브의 정신 잃은 몸이 안으로 옮겨져, 발을 불 쪽으로 두고 양탄자 위에 눕혀졌고, 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 소생술이 즉시 시도되었으며, 그동안 마구간지기는 의사를 부르러 보내졌다. 하지만 그 어느 몸에도 한 가닥 생기조차 없어 보였다. 그때 토마신이, 슬픔에 멍한 상태가 미친 듯한 행동에 잠시 밀려나 있던 터라, 사슴뿔 정精 한 병을 클림의 콧구멍에 갖다 댔는데, 다른 둘에게는 헛되이 써본 뒤였다. 그가 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"클림이 살아 있어요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다.\n\n그는 곧 또렷이 숨을 쉬었고, 그녀는 같은 방법으로 남편을 되살려보려 거듭거듭 애썼으나, 와일드브는 아무런 기척이 없었다. 그와 유스테이샤 둘 다 자극적인 향기가 미칠 수 없는 곳으로 영영 떠났다고 여길 근거가 너무도 많았다. 그들의 노력은 의사가 도착하고서야 누그러졌고, 그제야 정신 잃은 셋이 하나씩 위층으로 옮겨져 따뜻한 침대에 뉘어졌다.\n\n벤은 곧 더 거들 일이 없게 되었음을 느끼고 문가로 갔는데, 자기가 그토록 깊은 관심을 두던 가족에게 닥친 이 기이한 참변을 아직 좀처럼 실감하지 못했다. 토마신은 분명 이 갑작스럽고 압도적인 사건의 본질에 무너지고 말 것이었다. 이제 그 온화한 처녀를 이 시련 속에서 지탱해줄 굳세고 분별 있는 요브라이트 부인은 살아 있지 않았다. 그리고 와일드브 같은 남편을 잃은 것을 두고 무심한 구경꾼이야 어떻게 생각하든, 그녀가 지금 이 순간 그 충격에 넋이 나가고 소름이 끼쳐 있다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그 자신으로 말하자면, 그녀에게로 가서 위로할 처지가 못 되는 터라, 그저 낯선 사람으로 머물 뿐인 집에서 더 기다릴 까닭이 없다고 여겼다.\n\n그는 황야를 가로질러 자기 마차로 돌아왔다. 불은 아직 꺼지지 않았고, 모든 것이 그가 두고 간 그대로였다. 이제 벤은 납만큼이나 무겁게 물에 절은 자기 옷이 떠올랐다. 그는 옷을 갈아입고 그것을 불 앞에 펼쳐 놓은 다음, 잠을 자려 누웠다. 하지만 떠나온 그 집의 소란을 생생히 떠올리며 마음이 들떠 있는 동안 여기서 쉬기란 그가 할 수 있는 일이 못 되었으니, 떠나온 것을 자책하며 그는 다른 옷 한 벌로 갈아입고 문을 잠근 뒤, 다시 황야를 가로질러 여관으로 서둘러 갔다. 그가 부엌에 들어섰을 때 비는 여전히 세차게 내리고 있었다. 화덕에서는 환한 불이 빛나고 있었고, 두 여인이 부산히 오가고 있었는데, 그중 하나는 올리 다우든이었다.\n\n\"그래, 지금은 어찌 돼가오?\" 벤이 속삭이듯 물었다.\n\n\"요브라이트 씨는 좋아지셨어요. 하지만 요브라이트 부인과 와일드브 씨는 차갑게 죽었답니다. 의사 말로는 물에서 나오기도 전에 완전히 숨이 끊겼다네요.\"\n\n\"아! 끌어 올릴 때 나도 그런 줄 짐작했소. 그럼 와일드브 부인은?\"\n\n\"기대할 만큼은 괜찮으세요. 의사가 담요 사이에 누이게 했는데, 그분도 강에 빠졌던 이들 못지않게 흠뻑 젖어 있었거든요, 가엾은 젊은 분. 레들먼, 당신도 그리 말라 보이지 않네요.\"\n\n\"오, 별것 아니오. 나는 옷을 갈아입었소. 이건 다시 빗속을 뚫고 오느라 생긴 약간의 축축함일 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"불가에 서세요. 마님께서 당신이 원하는 건 무엇이든 드리라 하셨고, 당신이 가버렸다는 말을 듣고는 안타까워하셨답니다.\"\n\n벤은 화덕 가까이 다가가, 멍한 기분으로 불꽃 속을 들여다보았다. 그의 각반에서 김이 피어올라 연기와 함께 굴뚝으로 솟아오르는 동안, 그는 위층에 있는 이들을 생각했다. 둘은 주검이었고, 하나는 가까스로 죽음의 아가리를 벗어났으며, 또 하나는 병들고 과부가 된 여인이었다. 그가 그 화덕가에 마지막으로 머물렀던 때는 제비뽑기가 한창이던 무렵으로, 그때 와일드브는 살아서 멀쩡했고, 토마신은 옆방에서 활기차게 미소 짓고 있었으며, 요브라이트와 유스테이샤는 갓 부부가 된 참이었고, 요브라이트 부인은 블룸스엔드에 살아 있었다. 그때만 해도 당시의 형편은 적어도 앞으로 이십 년은 거뜬할 것처럼 보였다. 그러나 그 무리 가운데, 처지가 본질적으로 달라지지 않은 이는 그 자신뿐이었다.\n\n그가 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨 있는데 발소리가 계단을 내려왔다. 그것은 간병하는 여인이었는데, 손에 둘둘 만 젖은 종이 뭉치를 들고 있었다. 그 여인은 자기 일에 어찌나 몰두해 있던지 벤을 거의 보지도 못했다. 그녀는 찬장에서 노끈 몇 가닥을 꺼내 화덕 위로 팽팽히 걸쳤는데, 그 일을 위해 미리 앞으로 끌어낸 장작 받침쇠에 각 가닥의 끝을 묶고는, 젖은 종이를 펼쳐 빨랫줄에 빨래를 널듯 한 장씩 그 줄에 핀으로 꽂기 시작했다.\n\n\"그게 뭐요?\" 벤이 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 주인님 지폐예요.\" 그녀가 답했다. \"옷을 벗길 때 주머니에서 나왔답니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그분은 한동안 다시 돌아오지 않을 작정이었나 보군?\" 벤이 말했다.\n\n\"그건 우리가 영영 알 수 없을 거예요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n벤은 차마 떠나기가 싫었으니, 세상에서 그의 관심을 끄는 모든 것이 이 지붕 아래 있었기 때문이다. 그 집에서는 그날 밤 더 이상 아무도 잠을 자지 못했으니, 영영 잠든 두 사람만 빼고 말이며, 그러니 그가 머물지 못할 까닭이 없었다. 그리하여 그는 늘 앉곤 하던 화덕가 우묵한 자리로 물러나, 거기서 두 줄로 늘어선 지폐가 굴뚝의 외풍에 앞뒤로 나부끼다가 그 흐물흐물함이 속속들이 바싹한 빳빳함으로 바뀔 때까지 그것들에서 피어오르는 김을 지켜보며 계속 있었다. 그러자 여인이 와서 핀을 빼고는, 그것들을 한데 접어 한 움큼 위층으로 가져갔다. 이윽고 의사가 위층에서 더는 손쓸 도리가 없는 사람의 표정으로 나타나, 장갑을 끼며 집을 나섰고, 그의 말이 종종걸음 치는 소리는 이내 길 위에서 잦아들었다.\n\n네 시에 문에서 가벼운 노크 소리가 났다. 그것은 찰리였는데, 유스테이샤에 관해 무슨 소식이라도 들었는지 알아보라고 바이 선장이 보낸 것이었다. 그를 들인 처녀는 무슨 대답을 해야 할지 모르겠다는 듯 그의 얼굴을 바라보다가, 벤이 앉아 있는 데로 안내하며 레들먼에게 말했다. \"당신이 말해줄래요?\"\n\n벤이 말했다. 찰리의 입에서 나온 것이라고는 힘없고 또렷잖은 소리뿐이었다. 그는 아주 가만히 서 있다가, 발작하듯 터뜨렸다. \"한 번만 더 그분을 볼 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"아마 볼 수 있을 거다.\" 디고리가 무겁게 말했다. \"하지만 어서 달려가 바이 선장께 알리는 게 낫지 않겠니?\"\n\n\"네, 네. 다만 한 번만, 단 한 번만 더 그분을 뵙고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"그러게 될 거다.\" 등 뒤에서 낮은 목소리가 말했고, 흠칫 돌아보니 그들은 희미한 빛 속에서, 담요에 둘둘 감싸인 채 무덤에서 나온 나사로 같은, 야위고 창백한, 거의 유령 같은 형상을 보았다.\n\n그것은 요브라이트였다. 벤도 찰리도 입을 열지 않았고, 클림이 말을 이었다. \"그 사람을 보게 될 거다. 날이 밝으면 선장께 알릴 시간은 충분해. 자네도 그 사람을 보고 싶겠지—그렇지 않은가, 디고리? 지금 그 사람은 무척 아름다워 보인다네.\"\n\n벤은 자리에서 일어나는 것으로 응했고, 찰리와 함께 클림을 따라 계단 아래로 갔는데, 거기서 그는 장화를 벗었고 찰리도 그렇게 했다. 그들은 요브라이트를 따라 위층 층계참으로 올라갔으며, 거기에는 촛불 하나가 타고 있어 요브라이트가 그것을 손에 들고 앞장서 옆방으로 이끌었다. 거기서 그는 침대맡으로 가서 시트를 젖혔다.\n\n그들은 유스테이샤를 말없이 내려다보며 서 있었는데, 죽음 속에 그렇게 누운 그녀는 살아생전의 그 모든 모습을 무색하게 했다. 창백함이라는 말로는 그녀 안색의 온갖 결을 다 담을 수 없었으니, 그것은 하양 이상으로 보였고, 거의 빛에 가까웠다. 정교하게 새겨진 그녀의 입가에 어린 표정은 흐뭇해 보였는데, 마치 위엄의 감각이 방금 그녀에게 말을 멈추게 한 듯했다. 영원한 굳음이 열정과 체념 사이의 순간적인 전환의 와중에 그것을 사로잡았던 것이다. 그녀의 검은 머리카락은 이제 그 둘 중 누구도 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 풀어헤쳐져, 숲처럼 그녀의 이마를 에워싸고 있었다. 시골 거처에 사는 이에게는 거의 지나치다 싶었던 그 위풍당당한 모습이 마침내 예술적으로 흡족한 배경을 찾은 것이었다.\n\n아무도 입을 열지 않다가, 마침내 클림이 그녀를 덮고 돌아섰다. \"이제 이리 와.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그들은 같은 방의 한쪽 후미진 곳으로 갔는데, 거기 더 작은 침대 위에 또 다른 형상이 누워 있었으니—와일드브였다. 그의 얼굴에는 유스테이샤보다 평온함이 덜 보였으나, 똑같은 환한 젊음이 그 위에 어려 있어, 아무리 무덤덤한 관찰자라도 지금 그를 보면 그가 이런 것보다 더 높은 운명을 타고났음을 느꼈을 것이다. 그가 최근에 목숨을 부지하려 사투를 벌인 흔적이라고는 그의 손끝에 남은 것뿐이었으니, 그 손끝은 둑 벽면을 붙잡으려 죽어가며 안간힘을 쓰는 통에 닳고 망가져 있었다.\n\n요브라이트의 태도가 어찌나 차분했던지, 다시 나타난 이래 어찌나 몇 마디 하지 않았던지, 벤은 그가 체념했으려니 여겼다. 그의 마음의 참된 상태가 드러난 것은 그들이 방을 나와 층계참에 섰을 때뿐이었다. 여기서 그는 유스테이샤가 누운 방 쪽으로 고개를 기울이며 광기 어린 미소를 띤 채 말했다. \"저 사람이 올해 내가 죽인 두 번째 여자야. 나는 어머니의 죽음에 큰 원인이 되었고, 저 사람의 죽음에는 으뜸가는 원인이지.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\" 벤이 말했다.\n\n\"나는 어머니에게 모진 말을 했고, 어머니는 내 집을 떠났어. 너무 늦어서야 나는 어머니더러 돌아오라고 청했지. 물에 빠져 죽었어야 할 사람은 바로 나야. 강이 나를 집어삼키고 저 사람을 떠받쳐주었더라면 산 사람들에게는 자비였을 텐데. 그러나 나는 죽을 수가 없어. 살았어야 할 이들은 죽어 누웠는데, 여기 나는 살아 있으니!\"\n\n\"하지만 그런 식으로 스스로에게 죄를 씌울 수는 없소.\" 벤이 말했다. \"그건 마치 부모가 없었다면 자식이 결코 잉태되지 않았으리라는 이유로, 자식이 저지른 살인의 원인이 부모라고 말하는 것이나 마찬가지요.\"\n\n\"그래, 벤, 그건 아주 옳은 말이야. 하지만 자네는 모든 정황을 알지 못해. 하느님께서 나를 끝내주시는 게 마땅하셨다면 모두에게 좋은 일이었을 거야. 그러나 나는 내 존재의 끔찍함에 차차 익숙해지고 있어. 사람이 비참함과 오래 함께 지내다 보면 그것을 비웃게 되는 때가 온다고들 하지. 분명 내게도 곧 그런 때가 오겠지!\"\n\n\"자네의 뜻은 늘 선했네.\" 벤이 말했다. \"어찌하여 그토록 자포자기한 말을 하는가?\"\n\n\"아니, 자포자기한 게 아니야. 그저 가망 없을 뿐이지. 그리고 내 가장 큰 한은, 내가 저지른 일을 두고 그 어떤 사람도, 그 어떤 법도 나를 벌할 수 없다는 거야!\"\n\n유스테이시아와 와일디브의 죽음에 관한 이야기는 여러 주, 여러 달에 걸쳐 에그던 전역으로, 그 너머 먼 곳까지 퍼져나갔다. 두 사람의 사랑에 얽힌 알려진 일화들은 모조리 부풀려지고, 일그러지고, 덧칠되고, 변형되어, 마침내 원래의 실상은 주변 입들이 빚어낸 위조된 형상과 좀처럼 닮은 구석이 없게 되었다. 그럼에도 전체적으로 보면 그 사내도 그 여인도 급작스러운 죽음으로 위엄을 잃지는 않았으니, 불행이 그들을 우아하게 후려쳐, 많은 이들처럼 오랜 세월의 주름과 무관심과 쇠락을 거치며 한 생애를 시들하고 보잘것없는 것으로 깎아내리는 대신, 그들의 종잡을 수 없던 내력을 파국의 일격으로 단숨에 끊어버렸기 때문이다.\n\n가장 가까이 얽힌 이들에게는 그 영향이 다소 달랐다. 그런 사건을 숱하게 들어온 외인들에게는 이제 한 건이 더 추가되었을 뿐이나, 타격이 떨어지는 바로 그 자리에서는 어떤 사전의 상상도 그것에 대한 이렇다 할 대비가 되지 못하는 법이니, 사별의 그 급작스러움 자체가 토머신의 감정을 어느 정도 무디게 했는데, 그러면서도 어이없게도, 잃어버린 남편이 더 나은 사내였어야 마땅하다는 의식이 그녀의 애도를 조금도 덜어주지 못했다. 오히려 이 사실은 처음에는 젊은 아내의 눈에 죽은 남편을 한층 돋보이게 하는 듯했고, 무지개에 꼭 필요한 구름처럼 보였다. 그러나 미지의 것이 안기던 공포는 지나갔다. 버림받은 아내로서 자신의 앞날에 대한 막연한 불안도 끝이 났다. 한때는 떨리는 추측의 대상이던 최악의 일이 이제는 한낱 이성의 문제, 한정된 나쁨에 불과했다. 그녀의 으뜸가는 관심사인 어린 유스테이시아는 여전히 곁에 남아 있었다. 그녀의 슬픔에는 겸허함이 있었고, 그 태도에는 반항이 없었으니—이럴 때 흔들린 정신은 가라앉기 마련이다.\n\n지금의 토머신의 비탄과 살아 있을 적 유스테이시아의 평온을 공통된 척도로 환산할 수 있었다면, 둘은 거의 같은 지점에 가닿았으리라. 그러나 토머신의 예전의 밝음은, 음울한 분위기에서라면 그 자체로 빛이었을 것을 그림자로 만들어버렸다.\n\n봄이 와서 그녀를 진정시켰고, 여름이 와서 그녀를 어루만졌으며, 가을이 닥쳐오자 그녀는 위안을 받기 시작했으니, 어린 딸이 튼튼하고 행복하게, 날마다 몸집도 지식도 자라고 있었기 때문이다. 바깥의 사정도 토머신을 적잖이 다독였다. 와일디브는 유언 없이 죽었고, 그녀와 아이가 그의 유일한 혈육이었다. 유산 관리가 인가되고, 모든 빚이 청산되고, 남편의 숙부가 남긴 재산의 잔여분이 그녀의 손에 들어오자, 그녀 자신과 아이의 이익을 위해 투자될 수 있는 금액이 일만 파운드에 조금 못 미친다는 사실이 드러났다.\n\n그녀는 어디서 살아야 할 것인가? 두말할 것 없이 블룸스엔드였다. 옛 방들이 프리깃함의 갑판 사이만큼이나 천장이 낮은 것은 사실이라, 여관에서 가져온 새 괘종시계를 세우려면 그 아래 바닥을 파내고 시계 머리에 달린 멋진 놋쇠 손잡이들을 떼어내야 비로소 세울 만한 높이가 나왔지만, 방들이야 어떻든 수가 넉넉했고, 그곳은 어린 시절의 온갖 추억으로 그녀에게 정겨운 곳이었다. 클림은 기꺼이 그녀를 세입자로 받아들였고, 자신의 거처는 뒤쪽 계단 꼭대기의 두 방으로 한정하여, 이제 돈을 쥔 안주인이 되어 거느려도 좋겠다 여긴 토머신과 세 하인으로부터 떨어져, 자기만의 길을 걷고 자기만의 생각을 하며 조용히 지냈다.\n\n슬픔은 그의 겉모습에도 얼마간 변화를 가져왔으나, 그 달라짐은 주로 속에 있었다. 그의 정신에 주름이 졌다고 말할 수도 있었으리라. 그에게는 적이 없었고, 그를 책망해줄 자를 아무도 찾을 수 없었으니, 그래서 그토록 모질게 자신을 책망했다.\n\n그는 자신이 운명에게 부당하게 다루어졌다고 이따금 생각하기는 했으니, 태어난다는 것이 명백한 진퇴양난이며, 인간은 영광과 함께 인생에서 전진하려 할 것이 아니라 수치 없이 그곳에서 후퇴할 방도를 궁리해야 한다고까지 말할 정도였다. 그러나 자신과 자신의 사람들이 영혼에 그런 쇠꼬챙이를 박히는 일을 당함으로써 빈정거림과 무자비함의 대상이 되었다는 주장은 오래 고수하지 못했다. 가장 냉혹한 부류의 인간을 제외하면 대개 그러한데, 인간이란 존재는 첫째 원인을 격하하지 않는 가설을 세우려는 그 후한 노력 속에서, 자신들보다 도덕적 질이 낮은 지배적 권능을 상상하기를 늘 주저해왔으며, 바빌론 강가에 주저앉아 울면서도 자신들의 눈물을 자아내는 그 억압에 변명을 지어내는 것이다. 그리하여 그의 면전에서 위로의 말이 부질없이 발화되긴 했어도, 그는 혼자 남겨졌을 때 스스로 택한 방향에서 위안을 찾았다. 그 같은 습성을 지닌 사내에게는 어머니에게서 물려받은 집과 연 백이십 파운드면 세속의 온갖 필요를 채우기에 충분했다. 살림이란 총액에 달린 것이 아니라 수입에 대한 지출의 비율에 달린 것이다.\n\n그는 자주 혼자 황야를 걸었으니, 그럴 때면 과거가 그림자 같은 손으로 그를 붙들어 거기 세워두고 제 이야기를 듣게 했다. 그러면 그의 상상은 그 자리를 그 옛 거주자들로 채웠으니—잊힌 켈트 부족들이 그를 둘러싸고 제 자취를 밟고 다녔고, 그는 거의 그들 사이에서 살며, 그들의 얼굴을 들여다보고, 사방으로 부풀어 오른 봉분 곁에 서 있는 그들을, 세워졌던 그 시절처럼 손상되지 않고 온전한 모습으로 볼 수 있었다. 물들인 야만인들 가운데 경작지를 택했던 자들은, 여기 자취를 남긴 자들에 견주면, 양피지에 글을 쓴 자들 옆에 선 종이에 글을 쓴 자들과 같았다. 전자의 기록은 오래전 쟁기에 스러졌으나 후자의 작업은 남아 있었다. 그럼에도 그들은 모두 제 유물을 기다리는 상이한 운명을 알지 못한 채 살다 죽었다. 그것은 그에게 불멸의 진화에는 예측 못 할 요인들이 작용한다는 사실을 일깨워주었다.\n\n다시 겨울이 돌아왔으니, 그 바람과 서리와 길든 울새와 반짝이는 별빛과 함께였다. 지난해에 토머신은 계절의 흐름을 거의 의식하지 못했으나, 올해는 온갖 종류의 외부 영향에 마음을 활짝 열었다. 이 다정한 사촌과 그녀의 갓난아기와 하인들의 삶은, 유달리 큰 활자의 책을 들여다보며 앉은 클림의 감각에는 나무 칸막이 너머로 들려오는 소리의 형태로만 가닿았지만, 그의 귀는 마침내 집의 다른 편에서 나는 이 사소한 소음들에 어찌나 익숙해졌던지, 그 소리가 가리키는 장면들을 거의 목격할 수 있을 지경이었다. 반초의 희미한 박자는 토머신이 요람을 흔드는 모습을 떠올리게 했고, 일렁이는 흥얼거림은 그녀가 아기를 재우며 노래하고 있다는 뜻이었으며, 맷돌 사이에서 나듯 모래가 바스러지는 소리는 험프리나 페어웨이나 샘의 무거운 발이 부엌의 돌바닥을 가로지르는 그림을 불러일으켰고, 소년 같은 가벼운 발걸음과 높은 음역의 흥겨운 곡조는 캔틀 영감이 찾아온 것을 알렸으며, 영감의 말이 별안간 뚝 끊기는 것은 약한 맥주 잔이 그의 입술에 갖다 대어졌다는 뜻이었고, 부산하게 문이 여닫히는 소리는 장에 나서려는 채비를 뜻했으니—토머신은 점잖음의 여지가 늘어났음에도, 어린 딸을 위해 가능한 모든 파운드를 아끼겠다는 일념으로, 우스꽝스러우리만치 빠듯한 삶을 살았다.\n\n어느 여름날 클림은 거실 창문 바로 바깥의 정원에 있었는데, 창문은 여느 때처럼 열려 있었다. 그는 창턱의 화분 꽃들을 바라보고 있었으니, 토머신이 그것들을 되살리고 손질하여 그의 어머니가 남겨두었던 상태로 돌려놓은 터였다. 방 안에 앉아 있던 토머신의 작은 비명 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"어머, 사람 놀라게 하긴!\" 그녀가 들어온 누군가에게 말했다. \"당신 유령인 줄 알았잖아요.\"\n\n클림은 호기심이 일어 조금 더 다가가 창문 안을 들여다보았다. 놀랍게도 방 안에는 디고리 벤이 서 있었는데, 이제는 더 이상 레들맨이 아니라, 평범한 기독교인의 이상하게 달라진 안색—새하얀 셔츠 앞섶, 꽃무늬가 든 밝은 조끼, 푸른 점박이 목수건, 병녹색 외투—을 드러내고 있었다. 이 행색에 조금이라도 기이한 데가 있다면 그것은 그가 예전의 모습과 엄청나게 달라졌다는 사실뿐이었다. 붉은색은, 붉은색에 가까운 그 무엇도, 그의 몸에 걸친 모든 의복에서 세심하게 배제되어 있었으니, 막 일에서 손을 뗀 사람들이 자신을 부유하게 만들어준 그 생업을 상기시키는 것만큼 질색하는 것이 또 있겠는가?\n\n여브라이트는 문 쪽으로 돌아가 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"어찌나 놀랐는지!\" 토머신이 두 사람을 번갈아 보며 미소 지었다. \"이분이 제 힘으로 하얘졌다는 게 도무지 믿기지 않았어요! 무슨 초자연적인 일 같았다니까요.\"\n\n\"지난 크리스마스에 레들 장사를 접었지요.\" 벤이 말했다. \"수지맞는 일이긴 했고, 그때쯤 되니 아버지가 생전에 치던 젖소 쉰 마리짜리 낙농장을 차릴 만큼은 벌었더군요. 무슨 변화를 줄 작정이라면 그 자리로 다시 돌아가고 싶다고 늘 생각해왔는데, 이제 거기 가 있는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게 하얘진 거예요, 디고리?\" 토머신이 물었다.\n\n\"차차 그리됐습죠, 마님.\"\n\n\"전보다 훨씬 보기 좋아요.\"\n\n벤은 당황한 기색이었고, 토머신은 어쩌면 아직도 자신에게 애틋한 마음을 품고 있을지 모를 사내에게 자기가 얼마나 경솔하게 말했는지 깨닫고는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다. 클림은 이런 낌새를 전혀 알아채지 못한 채 유쾌하게 덧붙였다—\n\n\"이제 자네가 다시 사람이 되었으니, 토머신네 아기를 무엇으로 겁줘서 달랜담?\"\n\n\"앉으세요, 디고리.\" 토머신이 말했다. \"차나 한잔 들고 가요.\"\n\n벤이 부엌으로 물러가려는 듯 몸을 움직이자, 토머신은 바느질을 계속하며 정답고도 야무진 투로 말했다. \"당연히 여기 앉으셔야죠. 그런데 그 젖소 쉰 마리짜리 낙농장은 어디 있어요, 벤 씨?\"\n\n\"스티클포드입니다—올더워스에서 오른쪽으로 한 이 마일 가서, 풀밭이 시작되는 데지요, 마님. 여브라이트 씨가 가끔 저를 찾아주고 싶으시다면, 청하지 않았다고 못 오시는 일은 없도록 했으면 합니다. 오늘 오후엔 차를 들고 갈 수가 없겠습니다, 고맙습니다만, 매듭지어야 할 일이 하나 있어서요. 내일이 메이폴 날인데, 섀드워터 사람들이 여기 이웃 몇 분과 한패가 되어, 댁의 울타리 바로 바깥 황야에다 기둥을 세우기로 했답니다, 거기가 푸르고 좋은 자리라서요.\" 벤은 팔꿈치를 집 앞의 빈터 쪽으로 휘둘렀다. \"페어웨이하고 그 얘길 나눴는데,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"기둥을 세우기 전에 와일디브 부인께 여쭤보는 게 좋겠다고 그에게 일러두었지요.\"\n\n\"반대할 이유가 없네요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"우리 땅은 저 흰 울타리에서 한 치도 더 나가지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 코앞에서 한 무리의 사람들이 막대기를 둘러싸고 미쳐 날뛰는 걸 보는 게 달갑지 않으실 수도 있잖습니까?\"\n\n\"전혀 거리낄 것 없어요.\"\n\n벤은 곧 자리를 떴고, 저녁에 여브라이트는 페어웨이의 오두막까지 산책 삼아 걸어갔다. 사랑스러운 5월의 일몰이었고, 광막한 에그던 황무지의 이 가장자리에 자란 자작나무들은 나비 날개처럼 섬세하고 호박처럼 투명한 새잎을 내밀고 있었다. 페어웨이의 거처 옆에는 길에서 움푹 들어간 빈터가 있었고, 거기에는 이제 반경 이 마일 안의 젊은이들이 죄다 모여 있었다. 기둥은 한쪽 끝을 받침대에 괴어 누인 채였고, 여인들은 그것을 꼭대기에서부터 들꽃으로 휘감는 일에 매달려 있었다. 즐거운 잉글랜드의 본능이 유달리 왕성한 생기로 이곳에 깃들어 있었고, 한 해의 각 계절에 전통이 결부시켜온 상징적 풍습들이 에그던에서는 아직 현실이었다. 사실 이런 외진 촌락들의 충동은 여전히 이교적인 것이니—이런 곳에서는 자연에 대한 경배, 자기 숭배, 광란의 흥겨움, 이름조차 잊힌 신들에게 바치던 게르만 제례의 단편들이, 어떤 식으로든 중세의 교리를 뚫고 살아남은 듯했다.\n\n여브라이트는 준비에 끼어들지 않고 다시 집으로 돌아갔다. 이튿날 아침 토머신이 침실 창문의 커튼을 걷자, 빈터 한가운데에 메이폴이 서서 그 꼭대기가 하늘을 가르고 있었다. 그것은 잭의 콩나무처럼 밤사이에, 아니 이른 아침에 불쑥 솟아난 것이었다. 그녀는 그것을 장식한 화환과 꽃다발을 더 잘 보려고 여닫이창을 열었다. 꽃들의 달콤한 향기가 이미 둘레의 대기로 퍼져 있었는데, 그 대기는 어떤 오염도 없이 깨끗하여, 그 한가운데 솟은 꽃의 첨탑에서 받아낸 향기를 가득 그녀의 입술로 실어다 주었다. 기둥 꼭대기에는 작은 꽃들로 장식된 십자 모양의 테가 얹혀 있었고, 그 아래로는 우윳빛 산사꽃 띠가, 그다음에는 블루벨 띠가, 그다음에는 카우슬립이, 그다음에는 라일락이, 그다음에는 동자꽃과 수선화 따위가, 가장 아래 단에 이르기까지 차례로 둘러져 있었다. 토머신은 이 모든 것을 알아보았고, 5월의 흥겨운 잔치가 이토록 가까이서 벌어진다는 데 기뻤다.\n\n오후가 되자 사람들이 빈터에 모여들기 시작했고, 여브라이트도 자기 방의 열린 창문으로 그들을 내다볼 만큼은 흥미를 느꼈다. 그러고 나서 곧 토머신이 바로 아래 문에서 걸어 나와 사촌의 얼굴 쪽으로 눈을 들었다. 그녀는 십팔 개월 전 와일디브가 죽은 이래로 여브라이트가 본 그 어느 때보다도 화사하게 차려입었으니, 결혼하던 날조차 그녀는 이만큼 자신을 돋보이게 드러낸 적이 없었다.\n\n\"오늘 참 예뻐 보이는구나, 토머신!\" 그가 말했다. \"메이폴 때문이니?\"\n\n\"꼭 그것만은 아니에요.\" 그러고 나서 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 눈을 내리떴는데, 그는 이를 특별히 눈여겨보지 않았으나, 그녀가 자기에게만 말을 건네고 있을 뿐인 것치고는 그 태도가 어딘지 묘하게 여겨졌다. 그녀가 자기를 기쁘게 하려고 여름옷을 차려입은 것이 가당키나 한 일일까?\n\n그는 지난 몇 주 동안 그녀가 그를 대한 태도를 떠올렸으니, 그 무렵 두 사람은 어머니의 눈길 아래 소년 소녀였던 옛날처럼 종종 함께 정원에서 일하곤 했다. 그녀가 그에게 품은 관심이 예전처럼 그저 친족의 그것만은 아니라면 어쩐단 말인가? 여브라이트에게 이런 종류의 가능성은 무엇이든 심각한 문제였고, 그는 그 생각만으로도 거의 괴로움을 느꼈다. 유스테이시아가 살아 있는 동안 잠재워지지 않았던 연인다운 감정의 고동은 죄다 그녀와 함께 무덤으로 들어가버렸다. 그녀를 향한 그의 정열은 사내로서 그의 생애에서 너무 늦은 지점에 닥쳐온 탓에, 더 소년다운 사랑에서라면 그럴 수도 있겠지만, 그런 종류의 또 다른 불길을 지필 만한 땔감을 손에 남겨두지 못했다. 설령 그가 다시 사랑할 수 있다 한들, 그 사랑은 더디고 힘겹게 자라는 식물일 것이고, 끝내는 가을에 부화한 새처럼 자그맣고 병약한 것에 그칠 터였다.\n\n이 새로운 복잡함에 어찌나 괴로웠던지, 그 열성적인 금관악대가 다섯 시쯤 도착하여 연주를 시작했을 때, 단원들 사이에 그의 집을 불어 날려버릴 만큼 바람이 그득한 듯한 그 소리가 울려 퍼지자, 그는 뒷문으로 자기 방을 빠져나가, 정원을 내려가, 산울타리의 쪽문을 지나, 보이지 않는 곳으로 멀리 가버렸다. 오늘만큼은 흥겨움의 한복판에 머물러 있는 것을 견딜 수 없었으니, 무던히 애를 썼는데도 그러했다. 네 시간 동안 그의 모습은 보이지 않았다. 그가 같은 길로 돌아왔을 때는 땅거미가 졌고, 이슬이 모든 푸른 것을 적시고 있었다. 떠들썩한 음악은 그쳐 있었으나, 그가 그렇게 뒤편으로 부지에 들어선 탓에, 토머신 쪽 집을 가로질러 앞문에 이를 때까지는 메이폴 잔치꾼들이 다 갔는지 알 수 없었다. 토머신은 현관 안에 혼자 서 있었다.\n\n그녀가 그를 나무라듯 바라보았다. \"막 시작하려니까 가버리셨잖아요, 클림.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그래. 끼어들 수가 없겠더구나. 너야 물론 그들과 함께 나갔겠지?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 안 나갔어요.\"\n\n\"일부러 차려입은 것 같던데.\"\n\n\"그렇긴 한데, 혼자서는 나갈 수가 없었어요, 사람이 그리 많은데. 지금도 한 사람 나와 있네요.\"\n\n여브라이트는 울타리 너머 짙푸른 빈터로 눈을 부릅떴고, 메이폴의 검은 형체 가까이에서 그림자 같은 형상 하나가 하릴없이 오르내리며 거니는 것을 분간해냈다. \"누구냐?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"벤 씨예요.\" 토머신이 말했다.\n\n\"안으로 들어오라고 청하지 그랬니, 탬시. 그 사람은 처음부터 끝까지 너한테 무척 친절했잖니.\"\n\n\"지금 할게요.\" 그녀가 말하고는, 충동에 이끌려, 쪽문을 지나 메이폴 아래 벤이 서 있는 곳으로 갔다.\n\n\"벤 씨, 맞죠?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n벤은 그녀를 못 본 척—교활한 사내답게—흠칫하더니 말했다. \"네.\"\n\n\"들어오시겠어요?\"\n\n\"아무래도 저는—\"\n\n\"오늘 저녁 춤추시는 걸 봤는데, 처녀들 중에 제일 좋은 짝들을 골라 추시던걸요. 들어오지 않으시려는 게, 여기 서서 지나간 즐거운 시간들을 곱씹어보고 싶어서인가요?\"\n\n\"뭐, 그것도 한몫합죠.\" 벤이 짐짓 감상에 젖은 투로 말했다. \"하지만 제가 이렇게 여기 머무는 주된 까닭은, 달이 떠오를 때까지 기다리고 싶어서랍니다.\"\n\n\"달빛 속에서 메이폴이 얼마나 예쁜지 보려고요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 처녀들 중 하나가 떨어뜨린 장갑을 찾으려고요.\"\n\n토머신은 놀라 말문이 막혔다. 집까지 사오 마일은 걸어야 하는 사내가 그런 이유로 여기서 기다린다는 것은 단 하나의 결론을 가리켰으니—그 사내는 그 장갑 임자에게 놀랍도록 마음이 가 있는 게 틀림없었다.\n\n\"그 처녀와 춤추셨어요, 디고리?\" 이 고백으로 그가 자기에게 훨씬 더 흥미로운 존재가 되었음을 드러내는 목소리로 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 그가 한숨지었다.\n\n\"그럼 들어오지 않으시겠다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"오늘 밤은 안 되겠습니다, 고맙습니다, 마님.\"\n\n\"그 젊은 처녀의 장갑을 찾으시게 등불을 빌려드릴까요, 벤 씨?\"\n\n\"아, 아닙니다, 그럴 것 없습니다, 와일디브 부인, 고맙습니다만. 몇 분이면 달이 뜰 테니까요.\"\n\n토머신은 현관으로 돌아갔다. \"들어온다더냐?\" 그녀가 두고 간 자리에서 기다리던 클림이 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 안 들어오는 게 낫겠대요.\" 그녀는 그렇게 말하고는 그를 지나쳐 집 안으로 들어갔고, 그러자 클림도 자기 방으로 물러갔다.\n\n클림이 가버리자 토머신은 어둠 속에서 살금살금 위층으로 올라가, 요람 곁에서 잠깐 귀를 기울여 아이가 잠든 것을 확인하고는, 창가로 가서 흰 커튼 자락을 살며시 들어 올려 밖을 내다보았다. 벤은 아직도 거기 있었다. 그녀는 동쪽 언덕 옆 하늘에 나타난 희미한 빛이 자라나는 것을 지켜보았고, 이윽고 달의 가장자리가 위로 솟구쳐 골짜기를 빛으로 가득 채웠다. 디고리의 형체가 이제 빈터에 또렷이 드러났으니, 그는 허리를 굽힌 자세로 움직이며, 분명 그 귀한 잃어버린 물건을 찾으려 풀밭을 살피고 있었고, 땅의 한 자 한 자를 빠짐없이 훑을 때까지 좌우로 갈지자를 그리며 걷고 있었다.\n\n\"참 우스꽝스럽기도 하지!\" 토머신이 빈정거리려는 어조로 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"사내라는 사람이 계집애 장갑 하나 때문에 저렇게 얼빠진 모양으로 어슬렁거리다니, 어쩜 저리 미련할까! 더구나 어엿한 낙농가에다, 이제는 돈도 있는 사람이! 안됐기도 하지!\"\n\n마침내 벤이 그것을 찾은 듯했으니, 그러자 그는 몸을 일으켜 그것을 입술로 가져갔다. 그러고는 그것을 가슴 주머니—현대의 의복이 허락하는, 사람의 심장에 가장 가까운 보관처—에 넣고서, 풀밭 너머 멀리 있는 자기 집을 향해 수학적으로 곧은 일직선을 그리며 골짜기를 거슬러 올라갔다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤 며칠 동안 클림은 토머신을 거의 보지 못했고, 마주칠 때면 그녀는 여느 때보다 말이 적었다. 마침내 그는 그녀에게 무엇을 그리 골똘히 생각하느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"정말 갈피를 못 잡겠어요.\" 그녀가 솔직하게 말했다. \"디고리 벤이 그토록 사랑에 빠진 상대가 누군지, 아무리 해도 짐작이 안 가요. 메이폴에 있던 처녀들 중에 그 사람한테 어울릴 만한 애는 하나도 없었는데, 그래도 그 자리에 분명 있었을 거 아녜요.\"\n\n클림은 잠깐 벤이 골랐을 상대를 떠올려보려 했으나, 그 문제에 흥미가 시들해지자 다시 정원 일을 계속했다.\n\n한동안 그녀에게 그 수수께끼가 풀리지 않았다. 그러나 어느 오후 토머신은 위층에서 산책 채비를 하다가, 마침 층계참으로 나와 \"레이철\" 하고 부를 일이 생겼다. 레이철은 열세 살쯤 된 계집아이로, 아기를 데리고 나가 바람을 쐬어주는 일을 했는데, 그 부름에 위층으로 올라왔다.\n\n\"내 새 장갑 한 짝을 집 안에서 본 적 있니, 레이철?\" 토머신이 물었다. \"이거랑 짝이 되는 거 말이야.\"\n\n레이철은 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"왜 대답을 안 해?\" 안주인이 말했다.\n\n\"잃어버린 것 같아요, 마님.\"\n\n\"잃어버려? 누가 잃어버렸는데? 난 그걸 딱 한 번밖에 안 꼈는데.\"\n\n레이철은 몹시 곤란해하는 사람처럼 굴더니, 끝내 울음을 터뜨렸다. \"저, 마님, 메이폴 날에 제가 낄 장갑이 없었는데, 탁자 위에서 마님 것을 보고는 빌려 끼면 되겠다 싶었어요. 상하게 할 마음은 조금도 없었는데, 그중 한 짝을 잃어버리고 말았어요. 어떤 분이 마님께 새 한 켤레를 사드리라고 저한테 돈을 주셨는데, 어디 가서 살 짬을 못 냈어요.\"\n\n\"어떤 분이라니?\"\n\n\"벤 씨요.\"\n\n\"그가 그게 내 장갑인 줄 알았어?\"\n\n\"네. 제가 말씀드렸거든요.\"\n\n토머신은 그 설명에 어찌나 놀랐던지 계집아이를 나무라는 것마저 깜빡 잊었고, 아이는 소리 없이 슬그머니 물러갔다. 토머신은 메이폴이 서 있던 풀밭 쪽으로 눈을 돌리는 것 말고는 더 움직이지 않았다. 그녀는 생각에 잠긴 채 있다가, 그날 오후엔 나가지 않고 아기의 미완성 격자무늬 고운 원피스—최신 유행대로 비스듬히 재단한 것—를 부지런히 짓겠노라고 혼잣말을 했다. 그녀가 어떻게 부지런히 일하면서도 두 시간이 다 가도록 한 일이 그것뿐이었는지는, 방금의 그 사건이 그녀의 부지런함을 손의 일에서 마음의 일로 돌려놓기 십상인 종류였음을 모르는 이에게는 수수께끼였으리라.\n\n이튿날 그녀는 여느 때처럼 제 일을 보았고, 어린 유스테이시아 말고는 동행 없이 황야를 거니는 버릇을 이어갔으니, 아이는 이제 그런 부류의 존재가 손으로 세상을 헤쳐나가게 되어 있는지 발로 헤쳐나가게 되어 있는지가 의심스러운 나이라, 양쪽을 다 시도하다가 고통스러운 곤경에 빠지곤 했다. 토머신에게는, 아이를 외딴 곳으로 데려가, 균형을 잃었을 때 곤두박질해도 부드러운 깔개가 되어주는 푸른 잔디와 백리향 위에서 살짝 남몰래 연습을 시켜주는 것이 무척이나 즐거운 일이었다.\n\n한번은 이런 식의 훈련에 골몰하여, 사 분의 일 인치 높이의 넘을 수 없는 장벽 따위에 아이의 여정이 때 이르게 끝나버리는 일이 없도록, 아이가 갈 길에서 나뭇가지 토막이며 고사리 줄기며 그 밖의 그런 부스러기를 치우려 허리를 굽히고 있을 때, 말을 탄 사내 하나가 자기 바로 곁에 거의 다 와 있는 것을 발견하고 그녀는 깜짝 놀랐으니, 부드러운 천연의 양탄자가 말발굽 소리를 죽여버린 탓이었다. 벤이었던 그 기수는 공중에 모자를 흔들고 정중하게 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"디고리, 내 장갑 줘요.\" 어떤 처지에서든 자기 마음을 사로잡은 주제의 한복판으로 곧장 뛰어드는 것이 토머신의 방식이었다.\n\n벤은 즉시 말에서 내려, 가슴 주머니에 손을 넣더니, 장갑을 건넸다.\n\n\"고마워요. 잘 간직해주시다니 참 친절도 하셔라.\"\n\n\"그리 말씀해주시니 부인이야말로 참 친절하십니다.\"\n\n\"아, 아녜요. 그걸 당신이 가지고 있어서 정말 기뻤어요. 다들 어찌나 무심해지는지, 당신이 나를 떠올려줬다는 게 놀라웠는걸요.\"\n\n\"제가 한때 어떤 사람이었는지 기억하셨다면 놀라지 않으셨을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지요.\" 그녀가 얼른 말했다. \"하지만 당신 같은 성품의 남자들은 대개 너무나 자립적이잖아요.\"\n\n\"제 성품이 어떤데요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"정확히는 모르겠어요.\" 토머신이 솔직하게 말했다. \"다만 자기 감정을 실속 있는 태도 아래 감춰두고, 혼자 있을 때만 그걸 드러낸다는 것뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"아, 그건 또 어찌 아십니까?\" 벤이 슬쩍 떠보듯 말했다.\n\n\"왜냐면,\" 그녀가 거꾸로 뒤집힌 꼴이 되어버린 어린 딸을 바로 세워주려고 멈추며 말했다. \"그냥 아니까요.\"\n\n\"세상 사람들 일반으로 헤아리시면 안 됩니다.\" 벤이 말했다. \"하긴 저도 요새는 감정이라는 게 뭔지 잘 모르겠습니다. 이런저런 사업에 어찌나 뒤엉켜 지냈던지, 제 무른 마음씨는 죄다 김처럼 날아가버렸어요. 그래요, 저는 몸도 마음도 돈벌이에 내맡겨버렸습니다. 돈이 제 꿈의 전부랍니다.\"\n\n\"어머 디고리, 어쩜 그리 못됐어요!\" 토머신이 나무라듯 말하면서, 그 말을 진지하게 받아들일지 자기를 놀리려는 말로 여길지 딱 중간에서 저울질하며 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"네, 좀 괴상한 노릇이긴 합죠.\" 더는 이겨낼 수 없는 죄에 편안히 체념한 사람의 그 무던한 어조로 벤이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게나 착하던 분이!\"\n\n\"뭐, 그건 제가 꽤나 좋아하는 논리인데요, 사람이 한때 어떠했다면 다시 그리 될 수도 있는 법이니까요.\" 토머신이 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"다만 지금은 좀 더 어렵게 됐다는 게 문제지만요.\" 벤이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"왜요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"부인이 그때보다 더 부자가 되셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"아, 아녜요—그리 대단치 않아요. 마땅히 해야 할 도리라, 딱 먹고살 만큼만 빼고는 거의 다 아기 앞으로 돌려놨거든요.\"\n\n\"그 말씀 들으니 좀 기쁘군요.\" 벤이 부드럽게 말하며, 곁눈으로 그녀를 보았다. \"그래야 우리가 가깝게 지내기 더 수월하니 말입니다.\"\n\n토머신은 다시 얼굴을 붉혔고, 싫지 않은 종류의 말이 몇 마디 더 오간 뒤, 벤은 말에 올라 떠나갔다.\n\n이 대화는 옛 로마 가도 가까운 황야의 우묵한 곳에서 오갔는데, 토머신이 자주 다니던 곳이었다. 그리고 그곳에서 이제 벤을 만났다 하여 그녀가 이후로 그 길을 덜 다니지는 않았음을 누구든 눈여겨보았을 법하다. 벤이 같은 곳에서 토머신을 만났다 하여 그곳으로 말을 달리는 것을 삼갔는지 아닌지는, 그해 두어 달쯤 뒤 같은 자리에서 보인 그녀의 행적으로 능히 짐작할 수 있었으리라.\n\n이 기간 내내 여브라이트는 사촌 토머신에 대한 자신의 도리를 다소간 곱씹어왔다. 이 마음씨 고운 사람이 생애의 이 이른 단계에서부터 줄곧 외로운 가시금작화와 고사리에다 제 사랑스러운 자질을 한 방울씩 흘려보내도록 운명 지어진다면, 그것은 달콤한 재료의 가련한 낭비이리라는 느낌을 그는 떨칠 수 없었다. 그러나 그가 이를 느낀 것은 한낱 경제가經濟家로서일 뿐, 연인으로서는 아니었다. 유스테이시아를 향한 그의 정열은 말하자면 그의 온 생애가 졸여진 것이었기에, 그에게는 그 지고한 것을 다시 내어줄 만큼이 더는 남아 있지 않았다. 거기까지만 보면 두말할 것 없이 토머신과의 결혼이라는 생각은, 설령 그녀의 청을 들어주기 위해서라도, 품지 않는 것이 도리였다.\n\n그러나 그것이 전부가 아니었다. 여러 해 전 그의 어머니의 마음속에는 토머신과 그 자신에 관한 큰 공상이 있었다. 그것이 단호히 소망에까지 이른 것은 아니었으나, 늘 마음에 드는 꿈이긴 했다. 어느 쪽의 행복도 그로 인해 위태로워지지 않는다면, 때가 무르익었을 때 둘이 부부가 되어야 한다는 것, 그것이 문제의 그 공상이었다. 그러니 여브라이트만큼 어머니의 기억을 경외하는 아들에게 이제 하나 말고 무슨 길이 남아 있단 말인가? 부모의 어떤 특정한 변덕이란, 그들이 살아 있을 적 반 시간의 대화로 흩어버릴 수도 있었을 것이건만, 그들의 죽음으로 더없이 절대적인 명령으로 승화되어, 양심적인 자식들에게는 그런 결과를 안기니, 정작 그 부모가 살아 있었다면 누구보다 먼저 그것을 못마땅해했을 일이라는 것은 불행한 사실이다.\n\n여브라이트 자신의 앞날만 걸린 일이었다면 그는 기꺼운 마음으로 토머신에게 청혼했으리라. 죽은 어머니의 바람을 이룬다 하여 그가 잃을 것은 없었다. 그러나 토머신이 이제 그 자신이 느끼기에 한낱 연인의 시신에 불과한 사내에게 시집가는 것을 떠올리는 것은 두려웠다. 그의 안에 살아 있는 활동은 셋뿐이었다. 하나는 어머니가 누워 있는 작은 묘지로 거의 날마다 걷는 것이었고, 또 하나는 유스테이시아를 그 망자들 가운데 헤아리는 더 멀리 떨어진 묘역으로 밤마다 그만큼 잦게 찾아가는 것이었으며, 셋째는 자신의 갈망을 채워줄 성싶은 유일한 천직—열한 번째 계명을 전하는 떠돌이 설교자—을 위해 스스로를 채비시키는 것이었다. 토머신이 이런 성향을 지닌 남편에게서 기쁨을 얻으리라고는 믿기 어려웠다.\n\n그래도 그는 그녀에게 청하여, 스스로 결정하게 하기로 마음먹었다. 도리를 다한다는 흐뭇한 느낌마저 안고 어느 저녁 그는 이 일을 위해 그녀에게로 아래층으로 내려갔으니, 그때 해가 골짜기에 지붕마루의 그 긴 그림자를, 어머니가 살아 있을 적 셀 수 없이 여러 번 거기 드리워져 있던 것을 보았던 바로 그 그림자를 찍어내고 있었다.\n\n토머신은 자기 방에 없었고, 그는 앞뜰에서 그녀를 찾아냈다. \"오래전부터 말하고 싶었던 게 있었다, 토머신.\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"우리 둘의 앞날에 다 관련된 일이야.\"\n\n\"그걸 지금 말씀하시려는 거예요?\" 그의 시선과 마주치며 얼굴을 붉힌 채 그녀가 얼른 말했다. \"잠깐만요, 클림, 저부터 말하게 해주세요, 이상하게도 저 역시 오라버니께 드릴 말씀이 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"얼마든지 말하렴, 탬시.\"\n\n\"아무도 우리 얘길 못 엿듣겠죠?\" 그녀가 사방으로 눈길을 돌리고 목소리를 낮추며 말을 이었다. \"자, 우선 이걸 약속해주세요—제가 드리는 제안이 마음에 안 들더라도 화내거나 모진 말로 저를 나무라지 않겠다고요.\"\n\n여브라이트가 약속하자 그녀가 계속했다. \"제가 바라는 건 오라버니의 조언이에요, 오라버니는 제 친족이니까요—그러니까, 일종의 후견인이잖아요—그렇죠, 클림?\"\n\n\"음, 그래, 그렇지, 일종의 후견인이지. 사실, 물론 그렇고말고.\" 그녀의 속내가 무언지 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채 그가 말했다.\n\n\"저 결혼할까 생각 중이에요.\" 그러고서 그녀가 무던하게 말했다. \"하지만 오라버니가 그런 발걸음을 찬성한다고 다짐해주지 않으면 안 할 거예요. 왜 말씀이 없으세요?\"\n\n\"좀 놀라서 그랬다. 하지만 그래도 그런 소식 들으니 무척 기쁘구나. 물론 찬성하지, 우리 탬시. 누굴까? 도무지 짐작이 안 가는걸. 아니다—그 늙은 의사 선생이구나!—늙었다고 부를 생각은 아니다, 따지고 보면 그리 늙은 양반도 아니니까. 아—지난번에 그가 너를 돌볼 때 내가 눈치챘지!\"\n\n\"아녜요, 아녜요.\" 그녀가 황급히 말했다. \"벤 씨예요.\"\n\n클림의 얼굴이 별안간 굳어졌다.\n\n\"거 봐요, 오라버닌 그분을 싫어하잖아요, 괜히 그 사람 얘길 꺼냈나 봐요!\" 그녀가 거의 토라진 투로 소리쳤다. \"사실 꺼내지 말았어야 했는데, 그분이 어찌나 자꾸 졸라대는지 어쩔 줄을 모르겠어서요!\"\n\n클림은 황야를 바라보았다. \"벤은 나도 어지간히 좋아한다.\" 마침내 그가 대답했다. \"정직하면서도 동시에 빈틈없는 사람이지. 영리하기도 하고, 너로 하여금 자기에게 마음 두게 만든 것만 봐도 그게 증명되잖니. 하지만 정말로, 토머신, 그는 영 그게—\"\n\n\"제게 어울릴 만한 신사가 못 된다고요? 저도 꼭 그게 마음에 걸려요. 이제 여쭤본 게 후회되고, 더는 그분 생각 안 할래요. 하지만 그래도 누구하고든 결혼한다면 저는 그분과 결혼해야 해요—이건 꼭 말하겠어요!\"\n\n\"그건 잘 모르겠구나.\" 그녀가 전혀 눈치채지 못한, 가로막혀버린 자기 본래의 의중에 대한 단서를 일일이 조심스레 감추며 클림이 말했다. \"읍내에 나가 살면서 거기서 사람들과 사귀면, 전문직에 종사하는 사람이나 그런 부류의 누군가와 결혼할 수도 있을 텐데.\"\n\n\"전 읍내 생활엔 안 맞아요—제가 늘 이렇게나 촌스럽고 어리석은데. 오라버니도 제 촌티 나는 태도를 알아채지 않으셨어요?\"\n\n\"음, 파리에서 돌아왔을 땐 좀 그랬지, 하지만 지금은 아니야.\"\n\n\"그건 오라버니도 촌사람이 다 되어서 그래요. 아, 저는 죽었다 깨어나도 거리에선 못 살아요! 에그던은 우스꽝스러운 낡은 곳이지만, 저는 거기 익숙해졌고, 다른 어디서도 도무지 행복할 수가 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"나도 그래.\" 클림이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 어떻게 제가 어디 읍내 사람과 결혼해야 한다고 말씀하실 수 있어요? 분명히 말하지만, 뭐라 하시든, 제가 결혼한다면 디고리와 해야 해요. 그분은 누구보다 제게 친절했고, 제가 알지도 못하는 여러 방식으로 저를 도와주셨다고요!\" 토머신은 이제 거의 입을 삐죽였다.\n\n\"그래, 그랬지.\" 클림이 덤덤한 어조로 말했다. \"음, 진심으로 마음 같아선 그와 결혼하라고 말하고 싶다. 하지만 어머니가 그 문제에 대해 어떻게 생각하셨는지 잊을 수가 없고, 그 뜻을 존중하지 않는다는 게 영 마음에 걸리는구나. 이제 와 우리가 할 수 있는 작은 일이나마 해서 그 뜻을 존중해야 할 이유가 너무도 많지 않니.\"\n\n\"그럼 좋아요.\" 토머신이 한숨지었다. \"더는 아무 말 안 할게요.\"\n\n\"하지만 네가 내 뜻에 따라야 할 의무는 없어. 난 그저 내 생각을 말할 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"아, 아녜요—그런 식으로 거역하고 싶진 않아요.\" 그녀가 서글프게 말했다. \"그분을 생각해선 안 되는 거였어요—제 집안을 생각했어야지요. 제 안에 얼마나 끔찍하게 못된 충동이 있는지!\" 그녀의 입술이 떨렸고, 그녀는 눈물을 감추려 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n클림은 도무지 알 수 없는 그녀의 취향에 언짢기는 했어도, 어쨌든 자기와 관련된 결혼 문제만은 보류되었음을 알고 어느 정도 안도했다. 그 뒤로 며칠에 걸쳐 그는 여러 차례 자기 방 창문으로, 그녀가 풀이 죽어 정원을 침울하게 서성이는 것을 보았다. 그는 그녀가 벤을 택한 것에 반쯤은 화가 났고, 그러다가는 결국 마음을 고쳐먹은 뒤로는 에그던의 어느 젊은이 못지않게 정직하고 끈기 있는 그 사내의 행복에 자기가 끼어든 꼴이 된 것에 마음이 쓰였다. 요컨대 클림은 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었다.\n\n다음번에 마주쳤을 때 그녀가 불쑥 말했다. \"그분은 그때보다 지금 훨씬 더 어엿하다고요!\"\n\n\"누가? 아 그래—디고리 벤 말이구나.\"\n\n\"고모님은 그분이 레들맨이라서 반대하셨을 뿐이잖아요.\"\n\n\"음, 토머신, 어쩌면 내가 어머니 바람의 자초지종을 다 아는 게 아닐지도 모르지. 그러니 네 판단대로 하는 게 좋겠다.\"\n\n\"오라버닌 제가 어머니의 기억을 업신여겼다고 두고두고 여기실 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그러지 않아. 어머니가 지금 처지의 디고리를 보셨다면 그를 네게 어울리는 남편으로 여기셨으리라고 네가 확신한 거라고 생각할 거야. 자, 그게 내 솔직한 마음이다. 더는 내게 묻지 말고, 네 뜻대로 하렴, 토머신. 난 만족할 테니까.\"\n\n토머신이 확신을 얻었던 모양이니, 이 일이 있은 며칠 뒤 클림이 근래 가보지 않았던 황야의 한 구역으로 발길이 닿았을 때, 거기서 일하던 험프리가 그에게 말했다. \"와일디브 부인하고 벤이 보아하니 다시 좋게 된 것 같아서 다행입니다.\"\n\n\"그런가?\" 클림이 멍하니 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그리고 부인이 좋은 날 아이를 데리고 산책 나올 때마다 그가 용케 마주치곤 합죠. 하지만, 여브라이트 씨, 저는 자꾸만, 사촌 누이께서 댁과 결혼했어야 했다는 느낌이 들어 어쩔 수가 없어요. 굴뚝가가 하나면 될 것을 둘로 만드는 건 애석한 노릇이지요. 댁이 마음만 먹으신다면 지금이라도 그 여자를 그자한테서 떼어올 수 있다는 게 제 생각입니다.\"\n\n\"여자를 둘이나 죽음으로 몰아넣은 사람이 무슨 염치로 결혼을 한단 말인가? 그런 생각일랑 말게, 험프리. 내가 겪은 일을 생각하면, 교회에 가서 아내를 맞는다는 건 너무도 우스꽝스러운 익살이라 여겨질 걸세. 욥의 말마따나, '내가 내 눈과 약속하였나니, 어찌 처녀에게 주목하랴.'\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 클림 도련님, 여자 둘을 죽음으로 몰았다느니 그런 생각은 하지 마십쇼. 그런 말씀 하시면 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"음, 그건 빼고 얘기하세.\" 여브라이트가 말했다. \"하지만 어쨌든 하느님이 내게 표를 찍어두셨는데, 그게 구애의 장면에선 영 보기 좋지 않을 걸세. 내 머릿속엔 두 가지 생각뿐이고 다른 건 없네. 야학을 열 작정이고, 또 설교자가 될 작정이야. 거기에 대해 자넨 뭐라 하겠나, 험프리?\"\n\n\"진심으로 나가서 도련님 말씀을 듣겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"고맙네. 내가 바라는 건 그게 전부야.\"\n\n클림이 골짜기로 내려가는데 토머신이 다른 길로 내려와 문에서 그와 마주쳤다. \"제가 무슨 말씀을 드리려는지 아세요, 클림?\" 어깨 너머로 그를 짓궂게 바라보며 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"짐작할 수 있지.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n그녀가 그의 얼굴을 찬찬히 살폈다. \"네, 옳게 짐작하셨어요. 결국 그렇게 되려나 봐요. 그분 말이 저도 이제 마음을 정하는 게 좋겠다고 하시고, 저도 그래야겠다는 생각이 들었거든요. 오라버니만 반대 안 하시면, 다음 달 이십오일에 하기로 했어요.\"\n\n\"옳다고 여기는 대로 하렴, 얘야. 네가 다시 행복으로 가는 길을 환히 본다니 더없이 기쁠 뿐이다. 지난날 네가 받은 대접에 대해 내 성性은 네게 온갖 보상을 해야 마땅하지.\"*\n\n[*] 글쓴이는 여기서, 이 이야기의 본래 구상이 토머신과 벤의 결혼을 의도하지 않았음을 밝혀둘 수 있겠다. 그는 끝까지 고립되고 기괴한 성격을 간직한 채, 누구도 어디로 갔는지 모르게 황야에서 신비롭게 사라질 것이었고—토머신은 과부로 남을 것이었다. 그러나 연재 출판의 어떤 사정으로 의도가 바뀌게 되었다.\n\n따라서 독자는 두 가지 결말 사이에서 고를 수 있으며, 엄정한 예술적 규율을 지닌 이들은 더 일관된 결론을 참된 것으로 여겨도 좋다.\n\n결혼식이 정해진 날 오전 열한 시쯤 블룸스엔드를 지나간 사람이라면, 여브라이트의 집은 비교적 조용한 반면, 그의 가장 가까운 이웃인 티머시 페어웨이의 거처에서는 굉장한 부산함을 알리는 소리가 흘러나오는 것을 알아챘으리라. 그것은 주로 발소리였으니, 안쪽 모래 깔린 바닥을 이리저리 활기차게 밟아대는 소리였다. 바깥에 보이는 사람은 단 하나였는데, 그는 작정했던 것보다 약속에 늦은 모양이었으니, 서둘러 문으로 다가가, 빗장을 들어 올리고, 인사도 없이 들어섰기 때문이다.\n\n안의 광경은 그리 흔한 것은 아니었다. 방 여기저기에는 에그던 패거리의 주축을 이루는 사내들이 옹기종기 모여 서 있었으니, 페어웨이 본인과 캔틀 영감과 험프리와 크리스천과 한둘쯤 되는 잔디꾼이 자리해 있었다. 따뜻한 날이라 사내들은 으레 그렇듯 셔츠 바람이었는데, 다만 크리스천만은 예외였으니, 그는 제 집이 아닌 남의 집에서는 옷가지 한 조각이라도 벗는 것에 늘 신경질적인 두려움을 느꼈다. 방 한가운데 튼튼한 떡갈나무 탁자 위에는 줄무늬 아마포 한 무더기가 던져져 있었고, 캔틀 영감이 한쪽을, 험프리가 다른 쪽을 눌러 잡은 가운데, 페어웨이가 노란 덩어리로 그 표면을 문지르고 있었으니, 그 일의 고됨에 얼굴이 땀에 젖고 잔뜩 찌푸려져 있었다.\n\n\"이불 천에 밀랍 먹이는 건가, 양반들?\" 새로 온 이가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 샘.\" 말을 낭비하기엔 너무 바쁜 사람처럼 캔틀 영감이 말했다. \"이 귀퉁이를 좀 더 팽팽히 당길까, 티머시?\"\n\n페어웨이가 대답했고, 밀랍 먹이기는 줄지 않는 기세로 이어졌다. \"보아하니 좋은 이불이 되겠는걸.\" 한동안 잠잠하다가 샘이 말을 이었다. \"누구 줄 건데?\"\n\n\"이제 새살림 차릴 새 식구들한테 줄 선물이지.\" 이 절차의 위엄에 짓눌려 어쩔 줄 모르고 서 있던 크리스천이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇고말고, 값나가는 선물이렷다.\"\n\n\"이불은 거위를 안 치는 사람들한테는 비싼 거 아닌가요, 페어웨이 양반?\" 전지전능한 존재에게 묻듯 크리스천이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지.\" 가시금작화 장수가 몸을 일으켜, 이마를 말끔히 훔치고는, 벌집 밀랍을 험프리에게 건네자, 그가 곧장 문지르는 일을 이어받았다. \"이 두 사람이 이불이 없어서 그러는 건 아니지만, 한평생에 이런 큰 법석이자 야단인 때에 정을 좀 보이는 게 좋지. 내 두 딸도 시집갈 때 하나씩 장만해 살림을 차려줬고, 지난 열두 달 동안 또 하나 만들 만큼 깃털이 집에 쌓였단 말이야. 자, 이웃들, 밀랍은 이만하면 충분히 먹인 것 같네. 캔틀 영감, 천을 바로 뒤집어 겉이 밖으로 나오게 하시게, 그럼 내가 깃털을 털어 넣기 시작하겠네.\"\n\n이불이 제대로 채비되자 페어웨이와 크리스천이 가득 채워졌으되 풍선처럼 가벼운 커다란 종이 봉지들을 들고 와, 방금 마련한 자루에다 봉지마다의 내용물을 쏟아붓기 시작했다. 봉지가 하나씩 비워질 때마다 솜털과 깃털이 점점 더 많이 방 안을 둥둥 떠다녔고, 마침내 크리스천이 봉지 하나의 내용물을 천 바깥에 쏟아버린 실수로 방 안 공기가 거대한 송이들로 자욱해져, 바람 없는 눈보라처럼 일꾼들 위로 내려앉았다.\n\n\"자네처럼 굼뜬 녀석은 본 적이 없네, 크리스천.\" 캔틀 영감이 매섭게 말했다. \"지닌 재간으로 치면 자넨 평생 블룸스엔드 밖으로 한 발짝도 나가본 적 없는 사내의 아들 같다니까. 정말이지 애비가 군대 다니며 닦은 그 온갖 영민함이 아들놈 천성 만드는 데는 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없는 모양이야. 저 잘난 크리스천을 두고 보면, 나도 여기 자네들 나머지 모두처럼 집에 들어앉아 아무것도 못 본 거나 다름없을 뻔했어. 하긴 나로 말하자면, 패기 넘치는 기상이 한몫했고말고, 암!\"\n\n\"그렇게 깎아내리진 마세요, 아버지, 그러시면 제가 볼링 핀만큼도 못한 것 같단 말예요. 변변찮게 어설픈 솜씨밖에 못 부린 게 아닌가 싶어 겁이 나요.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런. 그렇게까지 풀죽은 가락은 잡지 말게나, 크리스천, 좀 더 애를 써봐.\" 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 좀 더 애를 써야지.\" 마치 자기가 먼저 그 말을 꺼낸 양 우기듯이 영감이 따라 말했다. \"양심상 사내라면 마땅히 장가를 들든지 군인이 되든지 해야 하는 법이야. 둘 다 안 하는 건 나라에 망신이지. 난 둘 다 했어, 하느님 덕분에! 사람을 일으키지도 눕히지도 못한다는 건 정말이지 보잘것없는 무위無爲의 기상을 드러내는 것이렷다.\"\n\n\"전 한 번도 포화 앞에 설 배짱이 없었어요.\" 크리스천이 더듬거렸다. \"하지만 장가에 관해서라면, 여기저기 청해본 건 사실이에요, 별 소득은 없었지만요. 그래요, 어딘가엔 사내를 주인으로—이 꼴이나마—맞을 수도 있었을 집이 한둘 있었는데, 지금은 여자 혼자 다스리고 있지요. 그래도 제가 그 여자를 찾아냈다면 곤란했을지도 몰라요, 왜냐면, 보세요, 이웃들, 그랬으면 집에 남아서 늙은이한테 어울리는 점잖은 가락으로 아버지 기운을 눌러둘 사람이 아무도 없었을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 일이라면 자네가 할 일이 태산이지, 이 아들놈아.\" 캔틀 영감이 잽싸게 말했다. \"노쇠가 두렵다는 게 내 안에 이리 강하지만 않다면!—내일 당장 제일 먼저 길을 떠나 세상을 다시 한 바퀴 돌아볼 텐데! 하지만 일흔하나면, 집에선 아무것도 아니라도, 떠돌이로는 높은 나이지…… 그래, 일흔하나, 지난 성촉절에 말이야. 제기랄, 이게 나이가 아니라 기니였으면 좋겠구먼!\" 그러고서 노인이 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"서글퍼하지 마세요, 영감님.\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"깃털이나 좀 더 이불 천에 부어 넣고, 기운 차리세요. 줄기야 좀 빼빼하셔도 영감님은 아직 푸른 잎 단 노인이에요. 온 연대기를 채울 만한 시간이 아직 영감님께 충분히 남았답니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 가야지, 티머시—신혼부부한테로 말이야!\" 캔틀 영감이 기운을 차린 목소리로 말하며, 기운차게 빙그르르 돌았다. \"오늘 밤 그들한테 가서 혼례 노래를 한 곡 불러야겠어, 어떤가? 그게 나다운 일이지, 알겠나, 그들도 그리 여길 거야. 내가 부르던 '큐피드 동산에서'는 네 군데서 인기가 좋았지, 그래도 그만큼 좋은, 아니 더 나은 노래도 있어. 이건 어떤가,\n\n윗창에서 그녀 임을 부르네\n'오 들어오소 안개 자욱 이슬 속에서'\n\n그런 때엔 그들도 무척 좋아할 거야! 정말이지, 가만 생각해보니, 우리가 여인숙에서 '보릿단' 부르던 그 한여름 밤 이후로는 입을 놀려 제대로 좋은 노래 가락을 빚어본 적이 없구먼, 그런 노래 부를 만한 음역을 갖춘 이가 드문데 자기 장기를 묵히는 건 애석한 노릇이지!\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 그렇고말고.\" 페어웨이가 말했다. \"자, 이불을 한 번 흔들어 다지세. 제일 좋은 깃털을 일흔 파운드나 넣었으니, 천이 너끈히 담을 만큼은 다 됐을 거야. 이제 요기 좀 하고 한 모금 적시는 것도 나쁘지 않겠는걸. 크리스천, 손이 닿거든 모퉁이 찬장에서 먹을 것 좀 끄집어 내리게, 이 사람아, 그동안 난 목 축일 걸 한 잔 따라보겠네.\"\n\n그들은 일하던 한복판에서 점심을 들려고 자리에 앉았으니, 깃털이 그들 둘레와 위와 아래에 가득했고, 그 본래 임자들은 이따금 열린 문간에 와서 제 묵은 옷가지가 그렇게 잔뜩 쌓인 것을 보고는 아까운 듯 꽥꽥 울어댔다.\n\n\"내 영혼을 걸고 말하건대 이러다 목이 막히겠네.\" 입에서 깃털 하나를 빼낸 페어웨이가, 잔이 돌려질 때 거기 또 몇 개가 둥둥 떠 있는 것을 보고 말했다.\n\n\"난 벌써 몇 개나 삼켰는걸, 하난 깃대가 제법 굵더라고.\" 구석에서 샘이 태평하게 말했다.\n\n\"이런—저게 뭐야—바퀴 소리가 들리는데?\" 캔틀 영감이 벌떡 일어나 문으로 달려가며 소리쳤다. \"아니, 저들이 벌써 돌아오네—이렇게 빨리 올 줄은 몰랐는데, 반 시간은 더 걸릴 줄 알았건만. 하긴, 마음만 먹으면 혼례라는 게 어찌나 빨리 끝나는지!\"\n\n\"오 그래, 끝내는 거야 금세 되지.\" 그 말을 온전히 완성하려면 무언가 덧붙여야 한다는 듯이 페어웨이가 말했다.\n\n그가 일어나 영감을 뒤따랐고, 나머지도 문으로 갔다. 곧이어 덮개 없는 마차 한 대가 지나갔는데, 거기에는 벤과 벤 부인과 여브라이트와, 이 일을 위해 버드머스에서 온 벤의 한 점잖은 친척이 타고 있었다. 마차는 거리도 비용도 아랑곳없이 가장 가까운 읍내에서 빌려온 것이었으니, 벤이 보기에 에그던 황야에는 토머신 같은 여인이 신부일 때 그런 행사에 걸맞을 만큼 위엄 있는 것이 아무것도 없었고, 교회는 걸어서 가는 혼례 행렬을 꾸리기엔 너무 멀었기 때문이다.\n\n마차가 농가에서 뛰쳐나온 무리 곁을 지날 때 그들은 \"만세!\" 하고 외치며 손을 흔들었으니, 움직일 때마다 깃털과 솜털이 그들의 머리에서, 소매에서, 옷자락에서 떠다녔고, 캔틀 영감이 몸을 빙빙 돌리는 동안 그의 인장들이 햇빛 속에서 흥겹게 춤추었다. 마차 마부는 그들에게 거만한 시선을 던졌으니, 신혼부부 본인들마저도 다소 깔보는 듯이 대했는데, 에그던 같은 세상의 끝에 머물도록 운명 지어진 사람들이라면, 부자든 가난뱅이든, 이교도 말고 다른 무슨 상태로 존재할 수 있단 말인가? 토머신은 문간의 무리에게 그런 우월감 따위 전혀 내비치지 않은 채, 새 날개처럼 잽싸게 그들을 향해 손을 펄럭이며, 눈에 눈물이 어린 채로, 내려서 이 친절한 이웃들에게 인사를 건네야 하지 않겠느냐고 디고리에게 물었다. 그러나 벤은, 저녁에 다들 집으로 올 터이니 그럴 것까진 없겠다고 일러주었다.\n\n이 소동이 지난 뒤 인사를 건넨 무리는 다시 제 일로 돌아갔고, 곧이어 속 채우기와 바느질이 마무리되자, 페어웨이가 말 한 마리에 마구를 채워, 그 거추장스러운 선물을 싸서, 수레에 싣고 스티클포드의 벤네 집으로 몰고 갔다.\n\n여브라이트는 자연히 자기 손에 떨어진 혼례 예식에서의 소임을 다하고, 그 뒤 부부와 함께 집으로 돌아왔으나, 저녁을 마무리 짓는 잔치와 춤판에 끼어들 마음은 없었다. 토머신은 실망했다.\n\n\"네 흥을 깨지 않고 거기 함께할 수 있다면 좋으련만.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 난 잔치판의 해골 꼴이 되기 십상이겠지.\"\n\"아녜요, 아녜요.\"\n\n\"음, 얘야, 그건 그렇다 치고, 양해해준다면, 빠지는 게 좋겠다. 매정하게 보이는 줄은 안다, 하지만 사랑하는 토머신, 그 자리에서 내가 행복하지 못할까 봐 두렵구나—그래, 그게 솔직한 마음이야. 알다시피 앞으로도 네 새집으로 너를 보러 늘 갈 테니, 지금 내가 빠진다 해도 대수롭지 않을 거다.\"\n\n\"그럼 제가 졌어요. 오라버니 마음 가장 편한 대로 하세요.\"\n\n클림은 한결 마음이 놓여 지붕마루의 제 거처로 물러갔고, 오후 동안 설교 한 편의 요지를 적어 내려가는 일에 매달렸으니, 그 설교로써 그는 애초에 자기를 이리로 데려왔고 그토록 오랫동안 여러 변형을 거치며 좋은 평판과 나쁜 평판을 두루 겪으면서도 줄곧 마음에 품어온 그 계획 가운데 정말로 실행할 만해 보이는 모든 것을 시작할 작정이었다. 그는 자기 신념을 거듭거듭 시험하고 가늠해보았으나 그것을 바꿀 까닭을 찾지 못했으니, 다만 계획은 상당히 줄여놓은 터였다. 그의 시력은 고향의 공기 속에서 오래 다독여진 덕에 더 강해지긴 했으나, 그 방대한 교육 사업을 시도해도 좋을 만큼 충분히 강하지는 않았다. 그래도 그는 한탄하지 않았으니—그의 온 정력을 쏟고 온 시간을 채울 만한, 야심 없는 종류의 일은 여전히 차고 넘쳤다.\n\n저녁이 깊어갔고, 집 아래편에서 들려오는 생기와 움직임의 소리가 더 또렷해졌으니, 울타리의 쪽문이 쉴 새 없이 찰칵거렸다. 잔치는 일찍 여는 것으로 되어 있어, 손님들은 어두워지기 한참 전에 죄다 모였다. 여브라이트는 뒤쪽 계단으로 내려가, 앞쪽이 아닌 다른 길로 황야에 들어섰으니, 잔치가 끝날 때까지 바깥 공기 속을 거닐다가, 토머신과 그 남편이 떠날 때 돌아와 작별 인사를 건넬 작정이었다. 그의 발걸음은 어느새 미스토버 쪽으로, 수전의 아들에게서 그 기이한 소식을 들었던 그 끔찍한 아침에 따라갔던 그 길로 향했다.\n\n그는 오두막 쪽으로 빠지지 않고, 한때 유스테이시아의 집이었던 그 일대 전체를 굽어볼 수 있는 높직한 데로 밀고 올라갔다. 어두워져가는 풍경을 바라보며 서 있는데 누군가 다가왔다. 그를 어렴풋이밖에 보지 못한 클림은 그냥 말없이 지나치게 두었을 터인데, 그 보행자가, 곧 찰리였는데, 청년을 알아보고 그에게 말을 걸어왔다.\n\n\"찰리, 오랫동안 못 봤구나.\" 여브라이트가 말했다. \"이 길로 자주 다니니?\"\n\n\"아뇨.\" 청년이 대답했다. \"둑 바깥으로는 잘 안 나옵니다.\"\n\n\"메이폴에는 안 왔더구나.\"\n\n\"네.\" 찰리가 같은 맥없는 어조로 말했다. \"이젠 그런 거에 마음이 안 가요.\"\n\n\"넌 유스테이시아 아씨를 꽤 좋아했지, 그렇지?\" 여브라이트가 부드럽게 물었다. 유스테이시아는 찰리의 그 낭만적인 연모를 그에게 자주 이야기했었다.\n\n\"네, 무척이나요. 아, 저는 바라기를—\"\n\n\"뭘?\"\n\n\"저는 바랍니다, 여브라이트 씨, 한때 아씨 것이었던 걸 간직하게 뭐든 제게 주실 수 있다면—괜찮으시다면요.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러마. 그러면 나도 무척 기쁘겠다, 찰리. 네가 가지고 싶어할 만한 아씨 물건이 뭐가 있는지 어디 생각해보자. 그러니 나와 함께 집으로 가자, 가서 찾아보마.\"\n\n두 사람은 함께 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 걸었다. 앞쪽에 이르렀을 때는 어두웠고, 덧창이 닫혀 있어 안의 모습은 전혀 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"이리 돌아가자.\" 클림이 말했다. \"당분간 내 출입구는 뒤편이거든.\"\n\n둘은 빙 돌아가, 어둠 속에서 구불구불한 계단을 올라 위층의 클림의 거실에 이르렀고, 거기서 그가 초에 불을 붙이는 동안 찰리가 살며시 뒤따라 들어왔다. 여브라이트는 책상을 뒤져, 박엽지 한 장을 꺼내, 거기서 칠흑같이 검은 머리채 두세 가닥을 펼쳐냈으니, 그것들이 검은 물줄기처럼 종이 위로 흘러내렸다. 그중 하나를 골라, 싸서, 청년에게 건넸으니, 청년의 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁했다. 그는 그 꾸러미에 입을 맞추고, 주머니에 넣고는, 북받치는 목소리로 말했다. \"오, 클림 도련님, 어쩜 제게 이리 잘해주십니까!\"\n\n\"가는 길을 조금 함께 가주마.\" 클림이 말했다. 그리고 아래층에서 들려오는 흥겨운 소란 속에 둘은 내려갔다. 앞쪽으로 가는 길은 그들을 작은 옆창 가까이로 데려갔으니, 거기서 촛불의 빛줄기가 관목들을 가로질러 새어 나오고 있었다. 그 창문은 덤불에 가려 사람들의 눈에 띄지 않았기에 가리개를 치지 않은 채였고, 그래서 이 은밀한 구석에 있는 사람은, 유리창의 푸르스름한 고풍스러움에 시야가 가로막히는 만큼을 빼고는, 혼례 손님들이 든 방 안에서 벌어지는 일을 죄다 볼 수 있었다.\n\n\"찰리, 저들이 뭘 하고 있느냐?\" 클림이 말했다. \"오늘 밤은 내 눈이 다시 약해진 데다, 이 창유리도 좋질 않구나.\"\n\n찰리는 습기로 다소 흐려진 제 눈을 닦고, 여닫이창에 더 바싹 다가섰다. \"벤 씨가 크리스천 캔틀더러 노래를 하라고 청하는데요,\" 그가 대답했다. \"크리스천은 그 청에 잔뜩 겁먹은 듯이 의자에서 들썩들썩하고, 그래서 그 아버지가 대신 한 가락 뽑기 시작했어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 노인 목소리가 들리는구나.\" 클림이 말했다. \"그럼 춤은 없는 모양이지. 토머신은 방에 있느냐? 촛불 앞에서 그 애 모습 같은 게 움직이는 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"네. 행복해 보이는걸요. 얼굴이 붉고, 페어웨이가 한 말에 웃고 계세요. 어이쿠!\"\n\n\"무슨 소리였느냐?\" 클림이 말했다.\n\n\"벤 씨가 어찌나 키가 큰지, 들보 밑을 지나며 깡충 뛰다가 머리를 들보에 쾅 부딪쳤어요. 벤 부인이 잔뜩 놀라 달려가서, 지금 혹이 났나 만져보려고 그 머리에 손을 대고 있고요. 이제 다들 아무 일 없었던 듯 다시 웃네요.\"\n\n\"내가 거기 없는 걸 누가 마음 쓰는 것 같으냐?\" 클림이 물었다.\n\n\"아뇨, 눈곱만큼도요. 이제 다들 잔을 치켜들고 누군가의 건강을 위해 마시고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그게 내 건강일까?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 벤 씨 부부 거예요, 그분이 흥겨운 투로 한 자락 연설을 하고 계시거든요. 저것 봐요—이제 벤 부인이 일어나서, 채비를 하러 자리를 뜨시는 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그래, 저들이 나에 대해 마음 쓰지 않았구나, 그게 마땅하지. 다 마땅한 대로 되었고, 적어도 토머신은 행복하니. 이제 더는 머물지 말자, 곧 다들 집에 가려고 나올 테니까.\"\n\n그는 청년을 황야까지 바래다 돌아가는 길에 함께해주고, 십오 분 뒤 혼자 집으로 돌아오니, 그가 없는 사이 손님들은 죄다 떠났고 벤과 토머신이 출발 채비를 마치고 있었다. 신혼부부는 벤네 우두머리 젖꾼이자 잡일꾼이 그들을 데리러 스티클포드에서 몰고 온 네 바퀴 이륜마차에 자리를 잡았고, 어린 유스테이시아와 유모는 뒤쪽 열린 받침판에 단단히 실렸으며, 발굽이 디딜 때마다 심벌즈처럼 부딪치는 편자를 단 늙고 발 헛디디는 조랑말에 올라탄 젖꾼이, 지난 세기의 시종처럼 뒤를 따라 달렸다.\n\n\"이제 다시 오라버니 집을 오롯이 오라버니께 맡기고 갈게요.\" 사촌에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하려고 몸을 숙이며 토머신이 말했다. \"우리가 떨던 이 법석 끝이라 오라버닌 좀 쓸쓸하시겠어요, 클림.\"\n\n\"아, 그런 거야 거리낄 게 못 되지.\" 다소 서글프게 미소 지으며 클림이 말했다. 그러고 나서 일행은 마차를 몰아 떠나 밤 그늘 속으로 사라졌고, 여브라이트는 집으로 들어섰다. 그를 맞아준 것은 시계의 똑딱거림뿐이었으니, 한 사람의 혼도 남지 않았기 때문이라, 클림의 요리사이자 시중꾼이자 정원사 노릇을 하는 크리스천은 제 아버지 집에서 잤던 것이다. 여브라이트는 빈 의자 가운데 하나에 앉아, 오래도록 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 어머니의 낡은 의자가 맞은편에 있었으니, 그날 저녁 그것이 한때 어머니의 것이었음을 거의 기억하지 못하는 이들이 거기 앉아 있었다. 그러나 클림에게 어머니는 거기, 지금도 늘 그렇듯, 거의 하나의 현존이었다. 다른 이들의 기억 속에서 어머니가 어떤 존재였든, 그의 기억 속에서 어머니는, 유스테이시아를 향한 그의 애틋함조차 흐리지 못한 광휘를 지닌 숭고한 성인聖人이었다. 그러나 그의 마음은 무거웠으니, 어머니가 그의 혼례날에, 그의 마음이 기쁘던 날에 그에게 면류관을 씌워주지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 사건들은 어머니 판단의 정확함을 입증했고, 그 보살핌의 헌신을 증명했다. 그는 자기 자신을 위해서보다도 유스테이시아를 위해서 더더욱 어머니의 말씀을 새겼어야 했다. \"다 내 탓이었어.\" 그가 속삭였다. \"오, 어머니, 어머니! 제발 다시 한번 제 삶을 살아, 어머니가 저를 위해 견디신 것을 제가 어머니를 위해 견딜 수 있다면!\"\n\n이 혼례가 있은 다음 일요일, 레인배로에서 진귀한 광경을 볼 수 있었다. 멀리서 보면 그저 봉분 꼭대기에 미동도 않는 형상 하나가 서 있는 듯했으니, 한 이 년 반 전 유스테이시아가 그 외로운 정상에 서 있던 모습 그대로였다. 그러나 이번에는 화창하고 따뜻한 날씨에, 여름 산들바람만 불고 있었고, 칙칙한 황혼이 아니라 이른 오후였다. 봉분 바로 가까이까지 올라간 이들은, 하늘을 찌르는 그 한가운데의 꼿꼿한 형상이 실은 혼자가 아님을 알아챘다. 그를 둘러싸고 봉분 비탈에는 여러 황야 사내와 여인이 편히 기대거나 앉아 있었다. 그들은 가운데 선 사내의 말에 귀를 기울이고 있었으니, 그는 설교를 하고 있었고, 그들은 그러는 동안 멍하니 히스를 뜯거나, 고사리를 훑어 벗기거나, 조약돌을 비탈 아래로 던지고 있었다. 이것이 일련의 도덕 강론, 곧 산상수훈의 첫 번째였으니, 화창한 날씨가 이어지는 한 매주 일요일 오후 같은 자리에서 베풀어질 것이었다.\n\n레인배로의 위풍당당한 높이가 택해진 데에는 두 가지 까닭이 있었으니, 첫째는 그것이 둘레의 외딴 오두막들 가운데 중심을 차지하고 있다는 것이었고, 둘째는 그 위의 설교자가 제 자리에 이르자마자 인접한 모든 지점에서 보일 수 있어, 그가 보이는 것 자체가 가까이 다가오고자 하는 떠돌이들에게 편리한 신호가 된다는 것이었다. 말하는 이는 맨머리였고, 산들바람이 한 줄기씩 불 때마다 그의 머리칼을, 그 나이의 사내치고는 다소 지나치게 성긴 머리칼을 부드럽게 들어 올렸다 내렸으니, 그 나이는 아직 서른셋이 못 되었다. 그는 눈에 차양을 쓰고 있었고, 얼굴은 수심에 잠겨 주름져 있었으나, 이런 육신의 특징들에 쇠락의 흔적이 어려 있긴 해도, 그 목소리의 가락에는 아무런 결함이 없었으니, 풍부하고, 음악적이고, 마음을 울리는 것이었다. 그는 사람들에게 베푸는 자신의 강론이 때로는 세속적인 것이고, 때로는\n종교적이되 결코 독단적이지 않았으며, 그의 설교 본문은 온갖 종류의 책에서 따왔다는 것이다. 이날 오후의 말씀은 다음과 같았다.—\n\n\"'왕이 일어나 어머니를 맞이하여 몸을 굽혀 절하고는 자기 보좌에 앉으며 왕의 어머니를 위하여 자리를 베풀게 하니, 어머니가 그의 오른편에 앉으니라. 그러고는 어머니가 이르되, 내가 그대에게 한 가지 작은 청이 있으니 청컨대 거절하지 마소서. 왕이 어머니에게 이르되, 청하소서, 어머니여, 내가 거절하지 아니하리이다.'\"\n\n요브라이트는 사실 도덕적으로 흠잡을 데 없는 주제를 다루는 떠돌이 야외 설교자이자 강연자라는 직업에서 자신의 소명을 찾았으니, 이날부터 그는 그 직무에 쉼 없이 힘써 레인배로와 그 주변 촌락에서 평이한 언어로 설교하였을 뿐 아니라 다른 곳에서는 한층 세련된 어조로—읍사무소의 계단과 주랑에서, 시장 십자가에서, 수도전에서, 해안 산책로와 부두에서, 다리의 난간에서, 헛간과 곳간에서, 그 밖에 인근 웨섹스 읍과 마을의 온갖 그러한 장소에서 말하였다. 그는 교리와 철학 체계는 내버려두었으니, 모든 선한 사람에게 공통된 견해와 행동만으로도 그의 혀를 충분히, 아니 차고 넘치도록 바쁘게 할 거리를 찾은 까닭이다. 어떤 이는 그를 믿었고 어떤 이는 믿지 않았으며, 어떤 이는 그의 말이 평범하다 하였고 또 다른 이는 신학적 교리의 결여를 탓하였으며, 한편 또 어떤 이들은 달리 할 수 있는 일을 찾지 못하는 사람이 설교에 나서는 것도 그런대로 괜찮은 일이라고 평하였다. 그러나 어디서나 그는 따뜻이 맞아들여졌으니, 그의 생애에 얽힌 이야기가 두루 알려진 까닭이었다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-122"]=[[["PREFACE BOOK FIRST—THE THREE WOMEN I. A Face on Which Time Makes but Little Impression II. Humanity Appears upon the Scene, Hand in Hand with Trouble III. The Custom of the Country IV. The Halt on the Turnpike Road V. Perplexity among Honest People VI. The Figure against the Sky VII. Queen of Night VIII. Those Who Are Found Where There Is Said to Be Nobody IX. Love Leads a Shrewd Man into Strategy X. A Desperate Attempt at Persuasion XI. The Dishonesty of an Honest Woman", "\"슬픔에게 나는 안녕을 고했네, 그녀를 멀리 뒤에 두고 떠나려 했건만, 그러나 즐거이, 즐거이, 그녀는 나를 살뜰히 사랑하니, 내게 그토록 한결같고, 그토록 다정하다네. 나는 그녀를 속이려, 그리하여 떠나려 했건만, 아! 그녀는 그토록 한결같고 그토록 다정하다네.\""]], [["BOOK SECOND—THE ARRIVAL I. Tidings of the Comer II. The People at Blooms-End Make Ready III. How a Little Sound Produced a Great Dream IV. Eustacia Is Led on to an Adventure V. Through the Moonlight VI. The Two Stand Face to Face VII. A Coalition between Beauty and Oddness VIII. Firmness Is Discovered in a Gentle Heart", "뒤이어 펼쳐지는 사건들이 일어났다고 상정되는 시기는 1840년에서 1850년 사이로 잡을 수 있는데, 이 무렵까지만 해도 여기서 \"버드머스\"라 불리는 옛 온천 휴양지는 조지 왕조 시대의 활기와 명성에서 비롯한 잔광을 아직 충분히 간직하고 있어, 내륙에 홀로 사는 이의 낭만적이고 상상력 풍부한 영혼에게 흠뻑 빠져들 만한 매력을 풍기고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["BOOK THIRD—THE FASCINATION I. “My Mind to Me a Kingdom Is” II. The New Course Causes Disappointment III. The First Act in a Timeworn Drama IV. An Hour of Bliss and Many Hours of Sadness V. Sharp Words Are Spoken, and a Crisis Ensues VI. Yeobright Goes, and the Breach Is Complete VII. The Morning and the Evening of a Day VIII. A New Force Disturbs the Current", "이 이야기의 음울한 무대에 붙여진 \"에그던 황야\"라는 총칭 아래에는 실제 이름이 제각기 다른 황야들이, 적어도 열두 곳에 이르는 황야들이 하나로 묶이거나 그 전형으로 대표되어 있는데, 이들은 성격과 양상에서 사실상 하나였으되, 그 본래의 통일성, 혹은 부분적 통일성은 이제 다소 가려져 있으니, 갖가지 성패의 정도로 쟁기질에 부쳐져 경작지로 편입되거나 삼림으로 조성된 침입성 띠와 조각들 때문이다."]], [["BOOK FOURTH—THE CLOSED DOOR I. The Rencounter by the Pool II. He Is Set upon by Adversities but He Sings a Song III. She Goes Out to Battle against Depression IV. Rough Coercion Is Employed V. The Journey across the Heath VI. A Conjuncture, and Its Result upon the Pedestrian VII. The Tragic Meeting of Two Old Friends VIII. Eustacia Hears of Good Fortune, and Beholds Evil\nBOOK FIFTH—THE DISCOVERY I. “Wherefore Is Light Given to Him That Is in Misery” II. A Lurid Light Breaks in upon a Darkened Understanding III. Eustacia Dresses Herself on a Black Morning IV. The Ministrations of a Half-forgotten One V. An Old Move Inadvertently Repeated VI. Thomasin Argues with Her Cousin, and He Writes a Letter VII. The Night of the Sixth of November VIII. Rain, Darkness, and Anxious Wanderers IX. Sights and Sounds Draw the Wanderers Together", "남서쪽 한 귀퉁이가 여기 묘사된 그 광대한 지대의 어느 한 지점이, 전설로 전해지는 웨식스의 왕—리어—의 황야일지도 모른다고 꿈꾸어 보는 것은 즐거운 일이다."]], [["BOOK SIXTH—AFTERCOURSES I. The Inevitable Movement Onward II. Thomasin Walks in a Green Place by the Roman Road III. The Serious Discourse of Clym with His Cousin IV. Cheerfulness Again Asserts Itself at Blooms-End, and Clym Finds His Vocation\n“To sorrow I bade good morrow, And thought to leave her far away behind; But cheerly, cheerly, She loves me dearly; She is so constant to me, and so kind. I would deceive her, And so leave her, But ah! she is so constant and so kind.”", "T.H."]], [["The date at which the following events are assumed to have occurred may be set down as between 1840 and 1850, when the old watering place herein called “Budmouth” still retained sufficient afterglow from its Georgian gaiety and prestige to lend it an absorbing attractiveness to the romantic and imaginative soul of a lonely dweller inland. Under the general name of “Egdon Heath,” which has been given to the sombre scene of the story, are united or typified heaths of various real names, to the number of at least a dozen; these being virtually one in character and aspect, though their original unity, or partial unity, is now somewhat disguised by intrusive strips and slices brought under the plough with varying degrees of success, or planted to woodland.", "1895년 7월."]], [["It is pleasant to dream that some spot in the extensive tract whose southwestern quarter is here described, may be the heath of that traditionary King of Wessex—Lear. _July_, 1895.", "I need to translate starting from first paragraph, no headings."]], [["I. A Face on Which Time Makes but Little Impression\nA Saturday afternoon in November was approaching the time of twilight, and the vast tract of unenclosed wild known as Egdon Heath embrowned itself moment by moment.", "Let me translate.11월의 어느 토요일 오후가 황혼 무렵에 다가서고 있었고, 에그던 히스라 불리는 울타리 없는 광막한 야생의 대지는 시시각각 검갈색으로 물들어갔다."], ["Overhead the hollow stretch of whitish cloud shutting out the sky was as a tent which had the whole heath for its floor.", "머리 위로는 하늘을 가리며 펼쳐진 희끄무레한 구름의 텅 빈 자락이 마치 히스 전체를 바닥으로 삼은 천막처럼 드리워 있었다."]], [["The heaven being spread with this pallid screen and the earth with the darkest vegetation, their meeting-line at the horizon was clearly marked.", "하늘은 이 창백한 장막으로, 땅은 가장 어두운 초목으로 뒤덮여 있었으니, 지평선에서 그 둘이 맞닿는 경계선이 또렷하게 그어졌다."], ["In such contrast the heath wore the appearance of an instalment of night which had taken up its place before its astronomical hour was come: darkness had to a great extent arrived hereon, while day stood distinct in the sky.", "그런 대조 속에서 히스는 천문학적 시각이 닥치기도 전에 미리 자리를 잡은 밤의 한 부분 같은 모습을 띠었으니, 이곳에는 어둠이 이미 상당히 도래해 있는 반면 하늘에는 낮이 또렷이 남아 있었던 것이다."], ["Looking upwards, a furze-cutter would have been inclined to continue work; looking down, he would have decided to finish his faggot and go home.", "위를 올려다보면 가시금작화를 베던 사람은 일을 더 계속하고 싶어졌을 것이요, 아래를 내려다보면 다발을 마무리하고 집으로 돌아가기로 마음먹었을 것이다."], ["The distant rims of the world and of the firmament seemed to be a division in time no less than a division in matter.", "멀리 세계의 가장자리와 창공의 가장자리는 물질의 경계인 동시에 시간의 경계인 듯했다."], ["The face of the heath by its mere complexion added half an hour to evening; it could in like manner retard the dawn, sadden noon, anticipate the frowning of storms scarcely generated, and intensify the opacity of a moonless midnight to a cause of shaking and dread.", "히스의 얼굴은 그 안색만으로 저녁에 반 시간을 더 보탰으니, 그와 마찬가지로 새벽을 늦추고, 정오를 시름겹게 하고, 미처 생겨나지도 않은 폭풍의 찌푸림을 앞당기고, 달 없는 한밤의 칠흑 같은 어둠을 더욱 짙게 하여 전율과 공포의 빌미로 만들 수도 있었다."]], [["In fact, precisely at this transitional point of its nightly roll into darkness the great and particular glory of the Egdon waste began, and nobody could be said to understand the heath who had not been there at such a time.", "실로, 어둠을 향한 밤의 회전이 바로 이 전환의 지점에 이르렀을 때 에그던 황야의 거대하고도 독특한 영광이 시작되었으니, 그런 시각에 그곳에 있어보지 않은 자라면 누구도 이 히스를 이해한다고 말할 수 없었다."], ["It could best be felt when it could not clearly be seen, its complete effect and explanation lying in this and the succeeding hours before the next dawn; then, and only then, did it tell its true tale.", "그것은 또렷이 보이지 않을 때 가장 잘 느껴졌으니, 그 온전한 효과와 해명이 바로 이 시각과 다음 새벽까지 이어지는 시간 속에 깃들어 있었던 것이요, 그때, 오직 그때에야 비로소 히스는 자신의 참된 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["The spot was, indeed, a near relation of night, and when night showed itself an apparent tendency to gravitate together could be perceived in its shades and the scene.", "이곳은 실로 밤의 가까운 친족이었으니, 밤이 모습을 드러낼 때면 그 음영과 풍경 속에서 서로 한데 끌려 모이려는 뚜렷한 경향이 감지되었다."], ["The sombre stretch of rounds and hollows seemed to rise and meet the evening gloom in pure sympathy, the heath exhaling darkness as rapidly as the heavens precipitated it.", "둥근 언덕과 움푹한 골짜기가 어슴푸레 뻗은 자락은 순전한 공감으로 솟아올라 저녁의 어스름과 맞닿는 듯했으니, 하늘이 어둠을 쏟아내리는 만큼이나 빠르게 히스는 어둠을 내뿜었다."], ["And so the obscurity in the air and the obscurity in the land closed together in a black fraternization towards which each advanced halfway.", "그리하여 공중의 어둠과 대지의 어둠은 서로 절반씩 다가서며 검은 동포애 속에 한데 합쳐졌다."]], [["The place became full of a watchful intentness now; for when other things sank brooding to sleep the heath appeared slowly to awake and listen.", "이제 그곳은 깨어 지켜보는 긴장으로 가득 찼으니, 다른 것들이 잠 속으로 가라앉아 골똘히 잠겨들 때 히스는 서서히 깨어나 귀 기울이는 듯했던 것이다."], ["Every night its Titanic form seemed to await something; but it had waited thus, unmoved, during so many centuries, through the crises of so many things, that it could only be imagined to await one last crisis—the final overthrow.", "밤마다 그 거대한 형상은 무언가를 기다리는 듯했으나, 워낙 여러 세기 동안, 그토록 많은 것들의 위기를 거치면서도 미동 없이 그렇게 기다려왔던 터라, 그것이 기다릴 수 있는 것이라곤 단 하나의 마지막 위기—최후의 전복뿐이라 상상될 따름이었다."]], [["It was a spot which returned upon the memory of those who loved it with an aspect of peculiar and kindly congruity.", "이곳은 이곳을 사랑한 이들의 기억 속에 독특하고도 정겨운 조화의 모습으로 되살아나는 장소였다."], ["Smiling champaigns of flowers and fruit hardly do this, for they are permanently harmonious only with an existence of better reputation as to its issues than the present.", "꽃과 열매가 미소 짓는 들판은 좀처럼 그러지 못하니, 그것들은 현재보다 결말이 더 평판 좋은 존재와만 영구히 어우러지기 때문이다."], ["Twilight combined with the scenery of Egdon Heath to evolve a thing majestic without severity, impressive without showiness, emphatic in its admonitions, grand in its simplicity.", "황혼은 에그던 히스의 풍경과 어우러져 준엄함 없이 장엄하고, 화려함 없이 인상적이며, 그 훈계가 단호하고, 그 소박함이 웅대한 무언가를 빚어냈다."], ["The qualifications which frequently invest the façade of a prison with far more dignity than is found in the façade of a palace double its size lent to this heath a sublimity in which spots renowned for beauty of the accepted kind are utterly wanting.", "어느 감옥의 정면을 그 두 배 크기의 궁전 정면보다 훨씬 더 위엄 있게 만들곤 하는 그 자질들이 이 히스에 숭고함을 부여했으니, 통념상 아름답다고 일컬어지는 장소들에는 도무지 결여된 숭고함이었다."], ["Fair prospects wed happily with fair times; but alas, if times be not fair!", "아름다운 경치는 아름다운 시절과 행복히 맺어지지만, 아 슬프게도, 시절이 아름답지 못하다면!"], ["Men have oftener suffered from, the mockery of a place too smiling for their reason than from the oppression of surroundings oversadly tinged.", "사람들은 지나치게 음울한 빛으로 물든 환경의 압박보다, 분별로는 견디기 힘들 만큼 지나치게 미소 짓는 장소의 조롱에 더 자주 시달려왔다."], ["Haggard Egdon appealed to a subtler and scarcer instinct, to a more recently learnt emotion, than that which responds to the sort of beauty called charming and fair.", "초췌한 에그던은, 매력적이고 곱다고 일컬어지는 그런 종류의 아름다움에 반응하는 본능보다, 더 미묘하고 더 희귀한 본능에, 더 최근에야 익혀진 감정에 호소했다."]], [["Indeed, it is a question if the exclusive reign of this orthodox beauty is not approaching its last quarter.", "실로, 이 정통적 아름다움의 독점적 지배가 어쩌면 그 마지막 사분기에 다가서고 있는 것은 아닌가 하는 의문이 든다."], ["The new Vale of Tempe may be a gaunt waste in Thule; human souls may find themselves in closer and closer harmony with external things wearing a sombreness distasteful to our race when it was young.", "새로운 템페 계곡은 툴레의 황량한 황야가 될지도 모르며, 인간의 영혼은 우리 종족이 어렸던 시절에는 못마땅해했던 음울함을 두른 외부의 사물과 점점 더 가까운 조화를 이루게 될지도 모른다."], ["The time seems near, if it has not actually arrived, when the chastened sublimity of a moor, a sea, or a mountain will be all of nature that is absolutely in keeping with the moods of the more thinking among mankind.", "황무지나 바다나 산의 절제된 숭고함만이 더 사색적인 인류의 기분과 절대적으로 부합하는 유일한 자연이 될 그런 때가, 실제로 오지 않았다면, 가까워 보인다."], ["And ultimately, to the commonest tourist, spots like Iceland may become what the vineyards and myrtle gardens of South Europe are to him now; and Heidelberg and Baden be passed unheeded as he hastens from the Alps to the sand dunes of Scheveningen.", "그리고 끝내는, 가장 평범한 관광객에게도 아이슬란드 같은 곳이 지금 그에게 남유럽의 포도밭과 도금양 정원이 지니는 의미를 띠게 될지도 모르며, 알프스에서 스헤베닝언의 모래언덕으로 서둘러 가는 그에게 하이델베르크와 바덴은 눈길 한번 받지 못한 채 지나쳐질지도 모른다."]], [["The most thoroughgoing ascetic could feel that he had a natural right to wander on Egdon—he was keeping within the line of legitimate indulgence when he laid himself open to influences such as these.", "가장 철저한 금욕주의자조차 에그던을 거닐 자연스러운 권리가 자기에게 있다고 느낄 수 있었으니—이런 종류의 영향에 자신을 내맡길 때 그는 정당한 탐닉의 선 안에 머무는 셈이었다."], ["Colours and beauties so far subdued were, at least, the birthright of all.", "그토록 절제된 빛깔과 아름다움은 적어도 만인의 타고난 권리였다."], ["Only in summer days of highest feather did its mood touch the level of gaiety.", "가장 화창한 한여름 날에만 그 기분은 흥겨움의 수준에 닿았다."], ["Intensity was more usually reached by way of the solemn than by way of the brilliant, and such a sort of intensity was often arrived at during winter darkness, tempests, and mists.", "강렬함은 찬란함을 통해서보다 엄숙함을 통해서 더 흔히 도달되었으니, 그런 종류의 강렬함은 겨울의 어둠과 폭풍과 안개 속에서 종종 이루어졌다."], ["Then Egdon was aroused to reciprocity; for the storm was its lover, and the wind its friend.", "그때면 에그던은 화답하도록 깨어났으니, 폭풍이 그 연인이요 바람이 그 벗이었다."], ["Then it became the home of strange phantoms; and it was found to be the hitherto unrecognized original of those wild regions of obscurity which are vaguely felt to be compassing us about in midnight dreams of flight and disaster, and are never thought of after the dream till revived by scenes like this.", "그때면 그곳은 기이한 환영들의 보금자리가 되었으니, 도주와 재난을 그리는 한밤의 꿈속에서 우리를 어렴풋이 에워싸고 있다 느껴지는, 그러나 꿈을 깨고 나면 이런 풍경에 의해 되살아나기 전까지는 결코 떠올리지 못하는 그 음울하고 야생적인 영역들의, 지금껏 알아채지 못했던 원형이 바로 이곳임이 드러났다."]], [["It was at present a place perfectly accordant with man’s nature—neither ghastly, hateful, nor ugly; neither commonplace, unmeaning, nor tame; but, like man, slighted and enduring; and withal singularly colossal and mysterious in its swarthy monotony.", "이곳은 현재 인간의 본성과 완벽히 부합하는 장소였으니—섬뜩하지도, 가증스럽지도, 추하지도 않았고, 진부하지도, 무의미하지도, 시시하지도 않았으며, 다만 인간처럼 푸대접받으면서도 견뎌내는 곳이었고, 게다가 그 검은 단조로움 속에 유난히 거대하고 신비로운 곳이었다."], ["As with some persons who have long lived apart, solitude seemed to look out of its countenance.", "오래도록 홀로 떨어져 살아온 어떤 사람들이 그러하듯, 고독이 그 얼굴에서 내다보는 듯했다."], ["It had a lonely face, suggesting tragical possibilities.", "그것은 외로운 얼굴이었으니, 비극적 가능성을 암시하는 얼굴이었다."]], [["This obscure, obsolete, superseded country figures in Domesday.", "이 어둡고, 낡고, 밀려난 고장은 둠즈데이 토지대장에 등장한다."], ["Its condition is recorded therein as that of heathy, furzy, briary wilderness—“Bruaria.”", "그 안에 그 상태가 히스와 가시금작화와 가시덤불의 황무지, 곧 \"브루아리아Bruaria\"로 기록되어 있다."], ["Then follows the length and breadth in leagues; and, though some uncertainty exists as to the exact extent of this ancient lineal measure, it appears from the figures that the area of Egdon down to the present day has but little diminished.", "이어서 길이와 너비가 리그 단위로 적혀 있는데, 이 옛 거리 단위의 정확한 크기에 다소 불확실함이 있긴 하나, 그 수치로 보아 에그던의 면적은 오늘날에 이르기까지 거의 줄어들지 않은 듯하다."], ["“Turbaria Bruaria”—the right of cutting heath-turf—occurs in charters relating to the district.", "\"투르바리아 브루아리아Turbaria Bruaria\"—히스의 뗏장을 베어낼 권리—라는 표현이 이 지역에 관한 특허장들에 나온다."], ["“Overgrown with heth and mosse,” says Leland of the same dark sweep of country.", "\"히스와 이끼로 뒤덮였다\"고 릴런드는 같은 어두운 일대의 고장을 두고 말한다."]], [["Here at least were intelligible facts regarding landscape—far-reaching proofs productive of genuine satisfaction.", "여기에는 적어도 풍경에 관한 이해할 만한 사실들이—진정한 만족을 낳는, 멀리까지 뻗는 증거들이 있었다."], ["The untameable, Ishmaelitish thing that Egdon now was it always had been.", "길들일 수 없는, 이스마엘 같은 존재로서 지금 에그던이 그러하듯, 그것은 늘 그러했다."], ["Civilization was its enemy; and ever since the beginning of vegetation its soil had worn the same antique brown dress, the natural and invariable garment of the particular formation.", "문명은 그 적이었으니, 초목이 생겨난 이래로 그 흙은 줄곧 똑같은 고풍의 갈색 옷을 걸쳐왔으니, 그 특정 지층에 자연스럽고도 한결같은 의복이었다."], ["In its venerable one coat lay a certain vein of satire on human vanity in clothes.", "그 유서 깊은 단벌 외투 속에는 옷에 대한 인간의 허영심을 향한 어떤 풍자의 가닥이 깃들어 있었다."], ["A person on a heath in raiment of modern cut and colours has more or less an anomalous look.", "현대식 재단과 빛깔의 옷을 입고 히스에 선 사람은 어딘가 어색해 보인다."], ["We seem to want the oldest and simplest human clothing where the clothing of the earth is so primitive.", "대지의 의복이 이토록 원시적인 곳에서는, 우리에게도 가장 오래되고 가장 단순한 인간의 옷이 필요한 듯하다."]], [["To recline on a stump of thorn in the central valley of Egdon, between afternoon and night, as now, where the eye could reach nothing of the world outside the summits and shoulders of heathland which filled the whole circumference of its glance, and to know that everything around and underneath had been from prehistoric times as unaltered as the stars overhead, gave ballast to the mind adrift on change, and harassed by the irrepressible New.", "지금처럼 오후와 밤 사이에, 시선이 닿는 한 그 둘레를 온통 채운 히스랜드의 봉우리와 어깨 너머로는 바깥 세계의 어떤 것도 눈에 들어오지 않는 에그던 한복판 골짜기에서, 가시나무 그루터기에 기대어 비스듬히 누운 채, 주위와 발밑의 모든 것이 선사 시대 이래로 머리 위의 별들만큼이나 변함없었음을 안다는 것은, 변화에 떠밀려 표류하고 억누를 수 없는 새로움에 시달리던 마음에 안정의 무게추를 달아주었다."], ["The great inviolate place had an ancient permanence which the sea cannot claim.", "이 위대하고 침범당하지 않은 장소는 바다도 주장할 수 없는 태곳적의 영속성을 지녔다."], ["Who can say of a particular sea that it is old?", "어느 특정한 바다를 두고 누가 그것이 늙었다 말할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Distilled by the sun, kneaded by the moon, it is renewed in a year, in a day, or in an hour.", "태양에 증류되고 달에 반죽되어, 그것은 한 해 만에, 하루 만에, 한 시간 만에 새로워진다."], ["The sea changed, the fields changed, the rivers, the villages, and the people changed, yet Egdon remained.", "바다가 변하고, 들판이 변하고, 강과 마을과 사람들이 변해도 에그던은 그대로 남았다."], ["Those surfaces were neither so steep as to be destructible by weather, nor so flat as to be the victims of floods and deposits.", "그 표면들은 비바람에 깎여나갈 만큼 가파르지도, 홍수와 퇴적의 제물이 될 만큼 평평하지도 않았다."], ["With the exception of an aged highway, and a still more aged barrow presently to be referred to—themselves almost crystallized to natural products by long continuance—even the trifling irregularities were not caused by pickaxe, plough, or spade, but remained as the very finger-touches of the last geological change.", "머잖아 언급될, 오랜 세월의 지속으로 거의 자연의 산물처럼 결정화된 늙은 큰길 하나와, 그보다 더 늙은 봉분 하나를 제외하면, 그 사소한 굴곡들조차 곡괭이나 쟁기나 삽이 만든 것이 아니라, 마지막 지질 변동이 직접 손끝으로 남긴 자취 그대로 남아 있었다."]], [["The above-mentioned highway traversed the lower levels of the heath, from one horizon to another.", "앞서 언급한 큰길은 히스의 낮은 지대를 가로질러 한 지평선에서 다른 지평선까지 뻗어 있었다."], ["In many portions of its course it overlaid an old vicinal way, which branched from the great Western road of the Romans, the Via Iceniana, or Ikenild Street, hard by.", "그 노정의 많은 구간에서 그것은 옛 마을 길 위에 겹쳐져 있었으니, 그 길은 바로 가까이에서 로마인들의 거대한 서부 가도, 곧 비아 이케니아나, 일명 아이크닐드 거리로부터 갈라져 나온 것이었다."], ["On the evening under consideration it would have been noticed that, though the gloom had increased sufficiently to confuse the minor features of the heath, the white surface of the road remained almost as clear as ever.", "지금 살펴보는 그 저녁, 어스름이 히스의 자잘한 윤곽들을 흐려놓을 만큼 짙어졌는데도 길의 흰 표면만큼은 여전히 거의 또렷이 남아 있음을 사람들은 알아챘을 것이다."]], [["II. Humanity Appears upon the Scene, Hand in Hand with Trouble\nAlong the road walked an old man.", "길을 따라 한 노인이 걸어가고 있었다."], ["He was white-headed as a mountain, bowed in the shoulders, and faded in general aspect.", "그는 산처럼 머리가 희었고, 어깨가 굽었으며, 전체적인 행색이 빛바래 있었다."], ["He wore a glazed hat, an ancient boat-cloak, and shoes; his brass buttons bearing an anchor upon their face.", "그는 광택 나는 모자와 낡은 선원 망토와 구두를 걸쳤는데, 그 놋쇠 단추에는 닻이 새겨져 있었다."], ["In his hand was a silver-headed walking stick, which he used as a veritable third leg, perseveringly dotting the ground with its point at every few inches’ interval.", "손에는 은제 손잡이가 달린 지팡이가 들려 있었으니, 그는 그것을 진짜 세 번째 다리처럼 부려, 몇 인치 간격마다 그 끝으로 끈질기게 땅을 콕콕 찍어댔다."], ["One would have said that he had been, in his day, a naval officer of some sort or other.", "한때 그가 이런저런 종류의 해군 장교였으리라고 누구나 말했을 법했다."]], [["Before him stretched the long, laborious road, dry, empty, and white.", "그의 앞에는 메마르고 텅 비고 하얀, 길고 고된 길이 뻗어 있었다."], ["It was quite open to the heath on each side, and bisected that vast dark surface like the parting-line on a head of black hair, diminishing and bending away on the furthest horizon.", "그것은 양옆으로 히스를 향해 완전히 트여 있었고, 마치 검은 머리채의 가르마처럼 그 광막한 어두운 표면을 둘로 갈라놓으며, 가장 먼 지평선에서 가늘어지다 굽어 사라졌다."]], [["The old man frequently stretched his eyes ahead to gaze over the tract that he had yet to traverse.", "노인은 자주 눈길을 앞으로 뻗어 자기가 아직 지나가야 할 구간을 바라보았다."], ["At length he discerned, a long distance in front of him, a moving spot, which appeared to be a vehicle, and it proved to be going the same way as that in which he himself was journeying.", "마침내 그는 자기 앞 한참 먼 곳에서 움직이는 점 하나를 알아보았으니, 그것은 탈것인 듯했고, 알고 보니 그 자신이 가는 방향과 똑같은 쪽으로 가고 있었다."], ["It was the single atom of life that the scene contained, and it only served to render the general loneliness more evident.", "그것은 이 풍경이 품은 단 하나의 생명의 입자였으니, 다만 전체의 외로움을 더 두드러지게 할 따름이었다."], ["Its rate of advance was slow, and the old man gained upon it sensibly.", "그 전진 속도는 느렸고, 노인은 눈에 띄게 거리를 좁혀갔다."]], [["When he drew nearer he perceived it to be a spring van, ordinary in shape, but singular in colour, this being a lurid red.", "가까이 다가서자 그는 그것이 모양은 평범하나 빛깔이 기이한, 곧 음산한 붉은빛을 띤 용수철 짐마차임을 알아보았다."], ["The driver walked beside it; and, like his van, he was completely red.", "마부가 그 옆을 걸었는데, 그 마차처럼 그 또한 온통 붉었다."], ["One dye of that tincture covered his clothes, the cap upon his head, his boots, his face, and his hands.", "그 물감의 한 빛깔이 그의 옷과 머리에 쓴 모자와 장화와 얼굴과 손을 뒤덮고 있었다."], ["He was not temporarily overlaid with the colour; it permeated him.", "그는 일시적으로 그 색을 뒤집어쓴 것이 아니라, 그 색이 그에게 배어든 것이었다."]], [["The old man knew the meaning of this.", "노인은 그것의 의미를 알고 있었다."], ["The traveller with the cart was a reddleman—a person whose vocation it was to supply farmers with redding for their sheep.", "마차를 끄는 그 나그네는 레들맨—양에 바를 붉은 염료를 농부들에게 대주는 일을 생업으로 삼는 자였다."], ["He was one of a class rapidly becoming extinct in Wessex, filling at present in the rural world the place which, during the last century, the dodo occupied in the world of animals.", "그는 웨식스에서 급속히 사라져가는 부류의 하나로, 지난 세기에 도도새가 동물 세계에서 차지했던 자리를 지금 시골 세계에서 메우고 있었다."], ["He is a curious, interesting, and nearly perished link between obsolete forms of life and those which generally prevail.", "그는 사라진 옛 생활 양식과 두루 만연한 현재의 양식 사이를 잇는, 기이하고 흥미로우며 거의 절멸 직전에 이른 고리였다."]], [["The decayed officer, by degrees, came up alongside his fellow-wayfarer, and wished him good evening.", "영락한 그 장교는 차츰차츰 길동무 곁으로 다가서서는 좋은 저녁이라 인사를 건넸다."], ["The reddleman turned his head, and replied in sad and occupied tones.", "레들맨은 고개를 돌려 침울하고 골똘한 어조로 답했다."], ["He was young, and his face, if not exactly handsome, approached so near to handsome that nobody would have contradicted an assertion that it really was so in its natural colour.", "그는 젊었으니, 그 얼굴은 정확히 잘생겼다고는 못 해도 잘생긴 데 워낙 가까워, 본래의 빛깔로 치면 정말로 잘생겼다는 주장을 누구도 반박하지 못했을 것이다."], ["His eye, which glared so strangely through his stain, was in itself attractive—keen as that of a bird of prey, and blue as autumn mist.", "그 물감을 뚫고 그토록 야릇하게 번득이는 그의 눈은 그 자체로 매력적이었으니—맹금의 눈처럼 날카롭고, 가을 안개처럼 푸르렀다."], ["He had neither whisker nor moustache, which allowed the soft curves of the lower part of his face to be apparent.", "그는 구레나룻도 콧수염도 없어, 얼굴 아래쪽의 부드러운 곡선이 그대로 드러났다."], ["His lips were thin, and though, as it seemed, compressed by thought, there was a pleasant twitch at their corners now and then.", "입술은 얇았고, 보아하니 생각에 잠겨 다물려 있는 듯했으나, 이따금 그 양 끝에 유쾌한 씰룩임이 있었다."], ["He was clothed throughout in a tight-fitting suit of corduroy, excellent in quality, not much worn, and well-chosen for its purpose, but deprived of its original colour by his trade.", "그는 온몸을 몸에 꼭 맞는 코듀로이 한 벌로 감쌌으니, 품질이 뛰어나고 그다지 닳지 않았으며 그 용도에 잘 골라진 옷이었으나, 그 생업 탓에 본래의 빛깔을 빼앗긴 것이었다."], ["It showed to advantage the good shape of his figure.", "그 옷은 그의 좋은 체격을 돋보이게 했다."], ["A certain well-to-do air about the man suggested that he was not poor for his degree.", "그 사내에게서 풍기는 어떤 넉넉한 분위기는 그가 그 신분치고는 가난하지 않음을 일러주었다."], ["The natural query of an observer would have been, Why should such a promising being as this have hidden his prepossessing exterior by adopting that singular occupation?", "이를 본 사람이라면 으레 이런 의문을 품었을 것이다, 어찌하여 이토록 장래가 촉망되는 존재가 저 별난 직업을 택하여 자신의 호감 가는 외양을 가려버렸단 말인가?"]], [["After replying to the old man’s greeting he showed no inclination to continue in talk, although they still walked side by side, for the elder traveller seemed to desire company.", "노인의 인사에 답한 뒤 그는 대화를 이어갈 기색을 보이지 않았으니, 그래도 둘은 여전히 나란히 걸었던바, 연장자인 나그네가 길동무를 바라는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["There were no sounds but that of the booming wind upon the stretch of tawny herbage around them, the crackling wheels, the tread of the men, and the footsteps of the two shaggy ponies which drew the van.", "그들을 에워싼 적갈색 풀밭 위로 윙윙대는 바람 소리와, 삐걱대는 바퀴 소리와, 두 사람의 발소리와, 마차를 끄는 털북숭이 조랑말 두 마리의 발굽 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 없었다."], ["They were small, hardy animals, of a breed between Galloway and Exmoor, and were known as “heath-croppers” here.", "그것들은 작고 억센 짐승으로, 갤러웨이와 엑스무어의 교배종이었으며, 이곳에서 \"히스 뜯는 놈들\"이라 불렸다."]], [["Now, as they thus pursued their way, the reddleman occasionally left his companion’s side, and, stepping behind the van, looked into its interior through a small window.", "이제 그렇게 길을 가는 동안, 레들맨은 이따금 길동무의 곁을 떠나, 마차 뒤로 돌아가서는 작은 창을 통해 그 안을 들여다보았다."], ["The look was always anxious.", "그 눈길은 언제나 근심스러웠다."], ["He would then return to the old man, who made another remark about the state of the country and so on, to which the reddleman again abstractedly replied, and then again they would lapse into silence.", "그러고는 노인에게로 돌아왔고, 노인은 다시 고장의 형편이며 그런 따위에 관해 한마디 했으며, 레들맨은 또 멍하니 거기에 답했고, 그러고 나면 둘은 다시 침묵에 잠겨들었다."], ["The silence conveyed to neither any sense of awkwardness; in these lonely places wayfarers, after a first greeting, frequently plod on for miles without speech; contiguity amounts to a tacit conversation where, otherwise than in cities, such contiguity can be put an end to on the merest inclination, and where not to put an end to it is intercourse in itself.", "그 침묵은 어느 쪽에도 어색함을 안기지 않았으니, 이런 외진 곳에서는 나그네들이 첫인사를 나눈 뒤 몇 마일이고 말없이 터덜터덜 걷곤 했으며, 도시에서와 달리 그저 마음 내키는 대로 끝낼 수 있고 끝내지 않는 것 자체가 곧 교제인 그런 곳에서는, 곁에 함께 있다는 것이 무언의 대화나 다름없었던 것이다."]], [["Possibly these two might not have spoken again till their parting, had it not been for the reddleman’s visits to his van.", "레들맨이 자기 마차를 들여다보러 가지만 않았던들, 어쩌면 이 둘은 헤어질 때까지 다시 입을 열지 않았을지도 모른다."], ["When he returned from his fifth time of looking in the old man said, “You have something inside there besides your load?”", "다섯 번째로 들여다보고 돌아왔을 때 노인이 말했다, \"거기 안에 짐 말고도 뭔가 있는 모양이오?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Somebody who wants looking after?”", "\"보살펴줘야 할 누가 있는 거요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["Not long after this a faint cry sounded from the interior.", "이러고 얼마 지나지 않아 안에서 희미한 울음소리가 들려왔다."], ["The reddleman hastened to the back, looked in, and came away again.", "레들맨은 서둘러 뒤로 가서 안을 들여다보고는 다시 나왔다."]], [["“You have a child there, my man?”", "\"거기 아이가 있는 거요, 여보게?\""]], [["“No, sir, I have a woman.”", "\"아닙니다, 어르신, 여자입니다.\""]], [["“The deuce you have!", "\"세상에 별일이군!"], ["Why did she cry out?”", "그런데 왜 울음을 내질렀소?\""]], [["“Oh, she has fallen asleep, and not being used to traveling, she’s uneasy, and keeps dreaming.”", "\"아, 잠이 들었는데, 여행에 익숙지 않아 불안해서 자꾸 꿈을 꾸는 겁니다.\""]], [["“A young woman?”", "\"젊은 여자요?\""]], [["“Yes, a young woman.”", "\"네, 젊은 여자입니다.\""]], [["“That would have interested me forty years ago.", "\"마흔 해 전이라면 내 흥미를 끌었을 일이구먼."], ["Perhaps she’s your wife?”", "어쩌면 자네 아내인가?\""]], [["“My wife!”", "\"제 아내요!\""], ["said the other bitterly.", "사내가 쓰디쓰게 말했다."], ["“She’s above mating with such as I.", "\"저 같은 놈과 짝지을 분이 아닙니다."], ["But there’s no reason why I should tell you about that.”", "하지만 어르신께 그 얘길 할 까닭은 없지요.\""]], [["“That’s true.", "\"그건 그렇지."], ["And there’s no reason why you should not.", "그렇다고 안 할 까닭도 없잖나."], ["What harm can I do to you or to her?”", "내가 자네한테나 저 여자한테 무슨 해를 끼치겠나?\""]], [["The reddleman looked in the old man’s face.", "레들맨은 노인의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."], ["“Well, sir,”", "\"글쎄요, 어르신,\""], ["he said at last, “I knew her before today, though perhaps it would have been better if I had not.", "그가 마침내 말했다, \"전 오늘 전부터 저 사람을 알고 있었습니다, 어쩌면 모르는 편이 나았을지도 모르지만요."], ["But she’s nothing to me, and I am nothing to her; and she wouldn’t have been in my van if any better carriage had been there to take her.”", "하지만 저 사람은 제게 아무것도 아니고, 저도 저 사람에게 아무것도 아닙니다. 저 사람을 태울 더 나은 마차가 거기 있었더라면 제 마차에 타지도 않았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Where, may I ask?”", "\"실례지만, 어디서?\""]], [["“At Anglebury.”", "\"앵글베리에서요.\""]], [["“I know the town well.", "\"그 읍이라면 잘 알지."], ["What was she doing there?”", "거기서 뭘 하고 있었나?\""]], [["“Oh, not much—to gossip about.", "\"아, 별것 아닙니다—입에 올릴 만한 건요."], ["However, she’s tired to death now, and not at all well, and that’s what makes her so restless.", "어쨌든 지금은 죽도록 지쳐 있고 도무지 몸도 성치 않아서, 그래서 저렇게 안절부절못하는 겁니다."], ["She dropped off into a nap about an hour ago, and ’twill do her good.”", "한 시간쯤 전에 깜박 잠이 들었는데, 그래야 몸이 좀 나아질 겁니다.\""]], [["“A nice-looking girl, no doubt?”", "\"틀림없이 어여쁜 처자겠지?\""]], [["“You would say so.”", "\"어르신도 그리 말씀하실 겁니다.\""]], [["The other traveller turned his eyes with interest towards the van window, and, without withdrawing them, said, “I presume I might look in upon her?”", "또 한 사람의 나그네는 관심 어린 눈길을 마차 창 쪽으로 돌리고는, 그 눈길을 거두지 않은 채 말했다, \"내가 안을 한번 들여다봐도 되겠지?\""]], [["“No,” said the reddleman abruptly.", "\"안 됩니다,\""], ["“It is getting too dark for you to see much of her; and, more than that, I have no right to allow you.", "레들맨이 퉁명스레 말했다. \"어두워져서 어르신이 저 사람을 제대로 보기도 어렵고, 게다가 제겐 그걸 허락할 권리가 없습니다."], ["Thank God she sleeps so well, I hope she won’t wake till she’s home.”", "다행히 곤히 잠들어 있으니, 집에 닿을 때까지 깨지 않았으면 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["“Who is she?", "\"저 여자가 누군가?"], ["One of the neighbourhood?”", "이 동네 사람인가?\""]], [["“’Tis no matter who, excuse me.”", "\"누구든 무슨 상관입니까, 죄송합니다만.\""]], [["“It is not that girl of Blooms-End, who has been talked about more or less lately?", "\"요즘 들어 이러쿵저러쿵 입에 오르내리던 그 블룸스엔드의 처자는 아니겠지?"], ["If so, I know her; and I can guess what has happened.”", "만일 그렇다면 나는 저 여자를 알고, 무슨 일이 있었는지도 짐작할 수 있네만.\""]], [["“’Tis no matter....", "\"누구든 무슨 상관입니까……"], ["Now, sir, I am sorry to say that we shall soon have to part company.", "자, 어르신, 죄송한 말씀입니다만 이제 곧 헤어져야겠습니다."], ["My ponies are tired, and I have further to go, and I am going to rest them under this bank for an hour.”", "제 조랑말들이 지쳤고, 저는 갈 길이 멉니다. 이 둑 아래에서 한 시간쯤 쉬게 할 참입니다.\""]], [["The elder traveller nodded his head indifferently, and the reddleman turned his horses and van in upon the turf, saying, “Good night.”", "연장자인 나그네는 무심히 고개를 끄덕였고, 레들맨은 말과 마차를 풀밭 안쪽으로 돌리며 말했다, \"안녕히 가십시오.\""], ["The old man replied, and proceeded on his way as before.", "노인이 답하고는 아까처럼 길을 갔다."]], [["The reddleman watched his form as it diminished to a speck on the road and became absorbed in the thickening films of night.", "레들맨은 노인의 형체가 길 위에서 한 점으로 작아져 짙어가는 밤의 막 속으로 빨려드는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["He then took some hay from a truss which was slung up under the van, and, throwing a portion of it in front of the horses, made a pad of the rest, which he laid on the ground beside his vehicle.", "그러고는 마차 밑에 매달아둔 건초 단에서 건초를 좀 꺼내, 그 일부를 말들 앞에 던져주고, 나머지로는 받침을 만들어 마차 옆 땅바닥에 깔았다."], ["Upon this he sat down, leaning his back against the wheel.", "그 위에 그는 바퀴에 등을 기대고 앉았다."], ["From the interior a low soft breathing came to his ear.", "안에서 낮고 부드러운 숨소리가 그의 귀에 들려왔다."], ["It appeared to satisfy him, and he musingly surveyed the scene, as if considering the next step that he should take.", "그것이 그를 안심시키는 듯했고, 그는 다음에 어떤 걸음을 내디뎌야 할지 가늠하듯, 골똘히 풍경을 둘러보았다."]], [["To do things musingly, and by small degrees, seemed, indeed, to be a duty in the Egdon valleys at this transitional hour, for there was that in the condition of the heath itself which resembled protracted and halting dubiousness.", "골똘히, 그리고 조금씩 천천히 일을 행한다는 것은, 실로 이 전환의 시각 에그던의 골짜기에서 마땅히 지켜야 할 도리인 듯했으니, 길게 늘어진 머뭇거리는 망설임을 닮은 무언가가 히스 자체의 형편에 깃들어 있었기 때문이다."], ["It was the quality of the repose appertaining to the scene.", "그것은 이 풍경에 어린 정적의 성질이었다."], ["This was not the repose of actual stagnation, but the apparent repose of incredible slowness.", "이는 실제 정체의 정적이 아니라, 믿기지 않을 만큼 느린 움직임의 겉보기 정적이었다."], ["A condition of healthy life so nearly resembling the torpor of death is a noticeable thing of its sort; to exhibit the inertness of the desert, and at the same time to be exercising powers akin to those of the meadow, and even of the forest, awakened in those who thought of it the attentiveness usually engendered by understatement and reserve.", "죽음의 마비와 이토록 흡사한 건강한 생명의 상태란 그 나름으로 주목할 만한 것이니, 사막의 무기력을 드러내면서도 동시에 초원의, 나아가 숲의 힘에 가까운 힘을 발휘한다는 것은, 그것을 곱씹는 이들에게 으레 절제와 함축이 자아내는 그런 주의력을 불러일으켰다."]], [["The scene before the reddleman’s eyes was a gradual series of ascents from the level of the road backward into the heart of the heath.", "레들맨의 눈앞에 펼쳐진 풍경은 길의 높이에서 뒤로 물러나 히스의 심장부로 이어지는 점진적인 오르막의 연속이었다."], ["It embraced hillocks, pits, ridges, acclivities, one behind the other, till all was finished by a high hill cutting against the still light sky.", "그것은 작은 언덕과 구덩이와 능선과 비탈을 차례로 품으며, 마침내 아직 환한 하늘을 배경으로 또렷이 도드라진 높은 언덕에서 모든 것이 마무리되었다."], ["The traveller’s eye hovered about these things for a time, and finally settled upon one noteworthy object up there.", "나그네의 눈은 한동안 이것들 위를 맴돌다가, 마침내 저 위의 주목할 만한 한 사물에 가닿았다."], ["It was a barrow.", "그것은 봉분이었다."], ["This bossy projection of earth above its natural level occupied the loftiest ground of the loneliest height that the heath contained.", "본래의 지면 위로 불룩 솟은 이 흙더미는 히스가 품은 가장 외로운 높은 곳의 가장 드높은 자리를 차지하고 있었다."], ["Although from the vale it appeared but as a wart on an Atlantean brow, its actual bulk was great.", "골짜기에서 보면 그것은 아틀라스 같은 이마에 돋은 사마귀에 지나지 않아 보였으나, 실제 부피는 거대했다."], ["It formed the pole and axis of this heathery world.", "그것은 이 히스 세계의 극이자 축을 이루었다."]], [["As the resting man looked at the barrow he became aware that its summit, hitherto the highest object in the whole prospect round, was surmounted by something higher.", "쉬고 있던 사내가 그 봉분을 바라보던 중, 그는 지금껏 사방 풍경에서 가장 높은 사물이던 그 꼭대기 위로 더 높은 무언가가 솟아 있음을 알아챘다."], ["It rose from the semiglobular mound like a spike from a helmet.", "그것은 반구형 흙더미에서 마치 투구에 박힌 창끝처럼 솟아올라 있었다."], ["The first instinct of an imaginative stranger might have been to suppose it the person of one of the Celts who built the barrow, so far had all of modern date withdrawn from the scene.", "상상력 있는 이방인이라면 그것을 봉분을 세운 켈트족 중 한 사람의 형상이라 짐작하는 게 첫 본능이었을 법하니, 현대에 속한 모든 것이 그토록 멀리 이 풍경에서 물러나 있었던 것이다."], ["It seemed a sort of last man among them, musing for a moment before dropping into eternal night with the rest of his race.", "그것은 마치 그들 가운데 마지막으로 남은 한 사람이, 제 종족의 나머지와 더불어 영원한 밤 속으로 떨어지기 전 잠시 생각에 잠긴 듯 보였다."]], [["There the form stood, motionless as the hill beneath.", "거기 그 형체는, 발밑의 언덕만큼이나 미동도 없이 서 있었다."], ["Above the plain rose the hill, above the hill rose the barrow, and above the barrow rose the figure.", "평지 위로 언덕이 솟았고, 언덕 위로 봉분이 솟았으며, 봉분 위로 그 형체가 솟아 있었다."], ["Above the figure was nothing that could be mapped elsewhere than on a celestial globe.", "그 형체 위로는 천구의天球儀가 아니고서는 어디에도 그릴 수 없을 것밖에 없었다."]], [["Such a perfect, delicate, and necessary finish did the figure give to the dark pile of hills that it seemed to be the only obvious justification of their outline.", "그 형체는 어두운 언덕 더미에 그토록 완벽하고 섬세하며 필연적인 마무리를 부여하여, 그 윤곽을 정당화하는 유일하고도 명백한 근거인 듯했다."], ["Without it, there was the dome without the lantern; with it the architectural demands of the mass were satisfied.", "그것이 없으면 채광탑 없는 둥근 지붕이었고, 그것이 있으면 그 덩어리의 건축적 요구가 충족되었다."], ["The scene was strangely homogeneous, in that the vale, the upland, the barrow, and the figure above it amounted only to unity.", "이 풍경은 기묘하게도 균질했으니, 골짜기와 고지와 봉분과 그 위의 형체가 오직 하나의 통일체를 이룰 뿐이었기 때문이다."], ["Looking at this or that member of the group was not observing a complete thing, but a fraction of a thing.", "그 무리의 이런저런 일원을 바라보는 것은 온전한 하나를 보는 것이 아니라 어떤 것의 한 조각을 보는 셈이었다."]], [["The form was so much like an organic part of the entire motionless structure that to see it move would have impressed the mind as a strange phenomenon.", "그 형체는 이 미동 없는 전체 구조의 유기적인 한 부분과 너무도 흡사하여, 그것이 움직이는 것을 본다면 마음에 기이한 현상으로 각인되었을 것이다."], ["Immobility being the chief characteristic of that whole which the person formed portion of, the discontinuance of immobility in any quarter suggested confusion.", "그 사람이 일부를 이룬 전체의 주된 특징이 부동不動이었으니, 어느 한구석에서 그 부동이 그쳐진다는 것은 곧 혼란을 암시했다."]], [["Yet that is what happened.", "그런데 바로 그 일이 일어났다."], ["The figure perceptibly gave up its fixity, shifted a step or two, and turned round.", "그 형체는 눈에 띄게 그 고정됨을 풀고, 한두 걸음 옮기더니, 몸을 돌렸다."], ["As if alarmed, it descended on the right side of the barrow, with the glide of a water-drop down a bud, and then vanished.", "마치 놀란 듯, 그것은 봉오리를 타고 흐르는 물방울처럼 미끄러져 봉분의 오른편으로 내려가더니, 곧 사라졌다."], ["The movement had been sufficient to show more clearly the characteristics of the figure, and that it was a woman’s.", "그 움직임은 그 형체의 특징을, 그것이 여자의 것임을 더 또렷이 드러내기에 충분했다."], ["The reason of her sudden displacement now appeared.", "그녀가 그토록 돌연 자리를 옮긴 까닭이 이제 드러났다."], ["With her dropping out of sight on the right side, a newcomer, bearing a burden, protruded into the sky on the left side, ascended the tumulus, and deposited the burden on the top.", "그녀가 오른편으로 사라지자, 짐을 진 새로운 인물 하나가 왼편 하늘로 불쑥 솟아올라 무덤을 오르더니, 그 짐을 꼭대기에 내려놓았다."], ["A second followed, then a third, a fourth, a fifth, and ultimately the whole barrow was peopled with burdened figures.", "두 번째가 뒤따랐고, 이어 세 번째, 네 번째, 다섯 번째가 따르더니, 마침내 봉분 전체가 짐 진 형체들로 가득 찼다."]], [["The only intelligible meaning in this sky-backed pantomime of silhouettes was that the woman had no relation to the forms who had taken her place, was sedulously avoiding these, and had come thither for another object than theirs.", "하늘을 배경으로 펼쳐진 이 실루엣들의 무언극에서 알아낼 수 있는 단 하나의 뜻은, 그 여자가 자기 자리를 대신 차지한 형체들과 아무런 관련이 없으며, 그들을 부지런히 피하고 있었고, 그들과는 다른 목적으로 그곳에 왔다는 것이었다."], ["The imagination of the observer clung by preference to that vanished, solitary figure, as to something more interesting, more important, more likely to have a history worth knowing than these newcomers, and unconsciously regarded them as intruders. But they remained, and established themselves; and the lonely person who hitherto had been queen of the solitude did not at present seem likely to return.", "관찰자의 상상은 새로 온 이들보다, 사라진 그 외로운 형체에—더 흥미롭고, 더 중요하며, 알아둘 만한 사연을 지녔을 법한 무언가로서—기꺼이 매달렸으니, 무의식중에 그들을 침입자로 여겼던 것이다."], ["III. The Custom of the Country", "그러나 그들은 남아서 자리를 잡았고, 지금껏 이 고독의 여왕이었던 그 외로운 사람은 당장은 돌아올 성싶지 않았다."]], [["Had a looker-on been posted in the immediate vicinity of the barrow, he would have learned that these persons were boys and men of the neighbouring hamlets.", "봉분 바로 곁에 누군가 지켜보는 이가 배치되어 있었더라면, 그는 이 사람들이 인근 마을의 소년과 사내들임을 알아냈을 것이다."], ["Each, as he ascended the barrow, had been heavily laden with furze faggots, carried upon the shoulder by means of a long stake sharpened at each end for impaling them easily—two in front and two behind.", "저마다 봉분을 오를 때면 가시금작화 다발을 잔뜩 짊어지고 있었으니, 그것들을 손쉽게 꿰찌르려고 양 끝을 뾰족하게 깎은 긴 막대를 어깨에 메어—앞에 둘, 뒤에 둘—나르는 것이었다."], ["They came from a part of the heath a quarter of a mile to the rear, where furze almost exclusively prevailed as a product.", "그들은 뒤쪽으로 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 히스의 한 구역에서 왔으니, 그곳은 거의 가시금작화만이 산물로 자라는 곳이었다."]], [["Every individual was so involved in furze by his method of carrying the faggots that he appeared like a bush on legs till he had thrown them down.", "저마다 다발을 나르는 방식 탓에 가시금작화에 온통 파묻혀, 그것을 내던지기 전까지는 다리 달린 덤불처럼 보였다."], ["The party had marched in trail, like a travelling flock of sheep; that is to say, the strongest first, the weak and young behind.", "일행은 길 떠난 양 떼처럼 한 줄로 행진해왔으니, 다시 말해 가장 힘센 자가 앞장서고, 약하고 어린 자가 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["The loads were all laid together, and a pyramid of furze thirty feet in circumference now occupied the crown of the tumulus, which was known as Rainbarrow for many miles round.", "짐들이 모두 한데 쌓이자, 둘레가 서른 자에 이르는 가시금작화 피라미드가 이제 그 무덤의 꼭대기를 차지했으니, 그곳은 사방 여러 마일에 걸쳐 레인배로라 알려진 곳이었다."], ["Some made themselves busy with matches, and in selecting the driest tufts of furze, others in loosening the bramble bonds which held the faggots together.", "어떤 이들은 성냥을 만지작거리며 가장 마른 가시금작화 더미를 골랐고, 다른 이들은 다발을 묶고 있던 가시덤불 끈을 풀었다."], ["Others, again, while this was in progress, lifted their eyes and swept the vast expanse of country commanded by their position, now lying nearly obliterated by shade.", "또 다른 이들은, 이 일이 진행되는 동안, 눈을 들어 그들의 위치가 굽어보는 광막한 고장을 둘러보았는데, 그곳은 이제 어스름에 거의 지워져 있었다."], ["In the valleys of the heath nothing save its own wild face was visible at any time of day; but this spot commanded a horizon enclosing a tract of far extent, and in many cases lying beyond the heath country.", "히스의 골짜기에서는 하루 어느 때든 제 자신의 야생적 얼굴 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았으나, 이 자리는 멀리까지 뻗은 일대를, 더러는 히스 고장 너머에까지 이르는 지평선을 굽어보았다."], ["None of its features could be seen now, but the whole made itself felt as a vague stretch of remoteness.", "그 어떤 지형도 이제는 분간할 수 없었으나, 그 전체가 아득한 먼 거리의 어렴풋한 자락으로 느껴졌다."]], [["While the men and lads were building the pile, a change took place in the mass of shade which denoted the distant landscape.", "사내들과 소년들이 더미를 쌓는 동안, 먼 풍경을 나타내던 그 어둠의 덩어리 속에 변화가 일었다."], ["Red suns and tufts of fire one by one began to arise, flecking the whole country round.", "붉은 태양과 불꽃 다발이 하나둘 솟아오르기 시작하여, 사방 온 고장에 점점이 무늬를 놓았다."], ["They were the bonfires of other parishes and hamlets that were engaged in the same sort of commemoration.", "그것들은 같은 종류의 기념 행사를 벌이는 다른 교구와 마을의 모닥불이었다."], ["Some were distant, and stood in a dense atmosphere, so that bundles of pale straw-like beams radiated around them in the shape of a fan.", "어떤 것들은 멀리, 짙은 대기 속에 서 있어, 창백한 짚 빛깔의 빛줄기 다발이 부챗살 모양으로 그 둘레에 뻗어 나왔다."], ["Some were large and near, glowing scarlet-red from the shade, like wounds in a black hide.", "어떤 것들은 크고 가까워, 마치 검은 가죽에 난 상처처럼 어둠 속에서 진홍빛으로 이글거렸다."], ["Some were Mænades, with winy faces and blown hair.", "어떤 것들은 매내드 같았으니, 술 취한 얼굴에 머리칼을 흩날렸다."], ["These tinctured the silent bosom of the clouds above them and lit up their ephemeral caves, which seemed thenceforth to become scalding caldrons.", "이것들은 머리 위 구름의 고요한 가슴을 물들이며 그 덧없는 동굴을 밝혔으니, 그 동굴은 그때부터 펄펄 끓는 가마솥이 되는 듯했다."], ["Perhaps as many as thirty bonfires could be counted within the whole bounds of the district; and as the hour may be told on a clock-face when the figures themselves are invisible, so did the men recognize the locality of each fire by its angle and direction, though nothing of the scenery could be viewed.", "어쩌면 이 지역의 경계 안에서 서른 개나 되는 모닥불을 헤아릴 수 있었을 것이며, 시계판의 숫자가 보이지 않아도 시각을 읽어낼 수 있듯이, 사내들은 비록 풍경이라곤 아무것도 볼 수 없는데도 그 각도와 방향으로 저마다의 불이 어느 곳에 있는지 알아냈다."]], [["The first tall flame from Rainbarrow sprang into the sky, attracting all eyes that had been fixed on the distant conflagrations back to their own attempt in the same kind.", "레인배로에서 첫 키 큰 불길이 하늘로 솟구치며, 멀리 타오르는 불길들에 박혀 있던 모든 눈길을 같은 종류의 제 시도로 다시 끌어당겼다."], ["The cheerful blaze streaked the inner surface of the human circle—now increased by other stragglers, male and female—with its own gold livery, and even overlaid the dark turf around with a lively luminousness, which softened off into obscurity where the barrow rounded downwards out of sight.", "그 명랑한 불꽃은 이제 다른 어슬렁대던 이들이—남자도 여자도—합류해 불어난 인간 동그라미의 안쪽 면을 제 황금빛 제복으로 줄무늬 지웠고, 둘레의 어두운 풀밭마저 발랄한 빛으로 뒤덮었으니, 그 빛은 봉분이 둥글게 휘어 시야에서 사라지는 곳에서 어둠 속으로 부드러이 잦아들었다."], ["It showed the barrow to be the segment of a globe, as perfect as on the day when it was thrown up, even the little ditch remaining from which the earth was dug.", "그 빛은 봉분이 흙을 파낸 작은 도랑까지 그대로 남아 있는, 처음 쌓아 올린 그날만큼이나 완벽한 구球의 한 조각임을 드러냈다."], ["Not a plough had ever disturbed a grain of that stubborn soil.", "쟁기 한 자루도 그 완강한 흙의 한 알갱이조차 건드린 적이 없었다."], ["In the heath’s barrenness to the farmer lay its fertility to the historian.", "농부에게는 척박함인 이 히스가 역사가에게는 비옥함이었다."], ["There had been no obliteration, because there had been no tending.", "손길을 댄 적이 없었기에, 지워진 것도 없었던 것이다."]], [["It seemed as if the bonfire-makers were standing in some radiant upper story of the world, detached from and independent of the dark stretches below.", "마치 모닥불을 피우는 이들이 아래로 뻗은 어두운 자락에서 떨어져 나와 그것과 무관하게, 세계의 어느 빛나는 위층에 서 있는 듯했다."], ["The heath down there was now a vast abyss, and no longer a continuation of what they stood on; for their eyes, adapted to the blaze, could see nothing of the deeps beyond its influence.", "저 아래 히스는 이제 광막한 심연이었지, 더는 그들이 선 곳의 연장이 아니었으니, 불빛에 적응한 그들의 눈은 그 불빛이 미치는 범위 너머의 깊은 곳을 아무것도 볼 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["Occasionally, it is true, a more vigorous flare than usual from their faggots sent darting lights like aides-de-camp down the inclines to some distant bush, pool, or patch of white sand, kindling these to replies of the same colour, till all was lost in darkness again.", "물론 이따금, 그들의 다발에서 평소보다 한층 거센 불길이 솟아 부관副官처럼 빛살을 비탈 아래로 쏘아 보내 멀리 어느 덤불이나 웅덩이나 흰 모래밭에 가닿게 하면, 그것들이 같은 빛깔로 화답하며 타올랐다가, 다시 모든 것이 어둠 속으로 사라졌다."], ["Then the whole black phenomenon beneath represented Limbo as viewed from the brink by the sublime Florentine in his vision, and the muttered articulations of the wind in the hollows were as complaints and petitions from the “souls of mighty worth” suspended therein.", "그러면 발밑의 그 온통 검은 광경은, 환상 속에서 그 가장자리에 서서 굽어보던 숭고한 피렌체 사람이 바라본 림보를 나타냈으니, 움푹한 골짜기에서 웅얼대는 바람의 또렷찮은 말소리는 거기 매달린 \"위대한 가치를 지닌 영혼들\"의 하소연이자 탄원 같았다."]], [["It was as if these men and boys had suddenly dived into past ages, and fetched therefrom an hour and deed which had before been familiar with this spot.", "마치 이 사내들과 소년들이 돌연 지난 시대 속으로 뛰어들어, 일찍이 이 자리와 친숙했던 어느 한 시각과 행위를 거기서 길어 올린 듯했다."], ["The ashes of the original British pyre which blazed from that summit lay fresh and undisturbed in the barrow beneath their tread.", "저 꼭대기에서 타올랐던 원래의 브리튼인 화장단火葬壇의 재가 그들의 발밑 봉분 속에 생생히 흐트러짐 없이 묻혀 있었다."], ["The flames from funeral piles long ago kindled there had shone down upon the lowlands as these were shining now.", "오래전 거기 지펴진 장례 화장단의 불길이 지금처럼 저지대를 비춰 내렸었다."], ["Festival fires to Thor and Woden had followed on the same ground and duly had their day.", "토르와 보덴에게 바치는 축제의 불이 같은 땅 위에 뒤따라 마땅히 한 시절을 누렸다."], ["Indeed, it is pretty well known that such blazes as this the heathmen were now enjoying are rather the lineal descendants from jumbled Druidical rites and Saxon ceremonies than the invention of popular feeling about Gunpowder Plot.", "실로, 지금 이 히스 사람들이 즐기는 이런 불길은, 화약 음모 사건을 둘러싼 민중 정서의 발명이라기보다는, 뒤죽박죽 섞인 드루이드교 의식과 색슨족 의례의 직계 후손에 가깝다는 것이 꽤나 잘 알려진 사실이다."]], [["Moreover to light a fire is the instinctive and resistant act of man when, at the winter ingress, the curfew is sounded throughout Nature.", "게다가 불을 피우는 것은, 겨울이 들어서는 길목에 자연 전체에 통금의 종이 울릴 때, 인간이 본능적으로 저항하는 행위이다."], ["It indicates a spontaneous, Promethean rebelliousness against that fiat that this recurrent season shall bring foul times, cold darkness, misery and death.", "그것은 이 되풀이되는 계절이 사나운 시절과 차가운 어둠과 비참과 죽음을 가져오리라는 그 율령에 맞서는 자발적이고 프로메테우스적인 반항을 나타낸다."], ["Black chaos comes, and the fettered gods of the earth say, Let there be light.", "검은 혼돈이 닥쳐오면, 사슬에 묶인 대지의 신들이 말한다, 빛이 있으라."]], [["The brilliant lights and sooty shades which struggled upon the skin and clothes of the persons standing round caused their lineaments and general contours to be drawn with Dureresque vigour and dash.", "둘러선 사람들의 살갗과 옷 위에서 다투던 눈부신 빛과 검댕 같은 그늘은 그들의 이목구비와 전체 윤곽을 뒤러풍의 힘과 기세로 그려냈다."], ["Yet the permanent moral expression of each face it was impossible to discover, for as the nimble flames towered, nodded, and swooped through the surrounding air, the blots of shade and flakes of light upon the countenances of the group changed shape and position endlessly.", "그러나 저마다의 얼굴에 항구히 새겨진 도덕적 표정만큼은 도무지 알아낼 길이 없었으니, 날렵한 불길이 솟구치고 까딱이고 주위 대기를 휩쓸며 곤두박질칠 때마다, 그 무리의 얼굴들 위에 어린 그늘의 얼룩과 빛의 조각이 끝없이 모양과 자리를 바꾸었기 때문이다."], ["All was unstable; quivering as leaves, evanescent as lightning.", "모든 것이 불안정했으니, 잎새처럼 떨리고 번개처럼 덧없었다."], ["Shadowy eye-sockets, deep as those of a death’s head, suddenly turned into pits of lustre: a lantern-jaw was cavernous, then it was shining; wrinkles were emphasized to ravines, or obliterated entirely by a changed ray.", "해골만큼 깊던 어둑한 눈구멍이 돌연 광채의 구덩이로 바뀌었고, 길쭉한 턱이 동굴 같다가 이내 번쩍였으며, 주름이 협곡으로 도드라지거나 빛살이 바뀌며 아예 지워지기도 했다."], ["Nostrils were dark wells; sinews in old necks were gilt mouldings; things with no particular polish on them were glazed; bright objects, such as the tip of a furze-hook one of the men carried, were as glass; eyeballs glowed like little lanterns.", "콧구멍은 어두운 우물이었고, 늙은 목의 힘줄은 금박 입힌 쇠시리였으며, 별달리 윤기 없던 것들이 반들거렸고, 한 사내가 든 가시금작화 낫 끝 같은 밝은 사물은 유리 같았으며, 눈알은 작은 등불처럼 이글거렸다."], ["Those whom Nature had depicted as merely quaint became grotesque, the grotesque became preternatural; for all was in extremity.", "자연이 그저 별나게 빚어놓은 이들이 기괴해졌고, 기괴한 것은 초자연적인 것이 되었으니, 모든 것이 극단에 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Hence it may be that the face of an old man, who had like others been called to the heights by the rising flames, was not really the mere nose and chin that it appeared to be, but an appreciable quantity of human countenance.", "그러기에, 다른 이들처럼 솟구치는 불길에 끌려 이 높은 곳으로 불려온 한 노인의 얼굴이, 보이는 대로 그저 코와 턱뿐인 것이 아니라, 제법 가늠할 만한 양의 인간다운 얼굴이었음도 그럴 법한 일이다."], ["He stood complacently sunning himself in the heat.", "그는 흐뭇하게 그 열기에 몸을 쬐며 서 있었다."], ["With a speaker, or stake, he tossed the outlying scraps of fuel into the conflagration, looking at the midst of the pile, occasionally lifting his eyes to measure the height of the flame, or to follow the great sparks which rose with it and sailed away into darkness.", "막대인지 말뚝인지를 들고 그는 한 켠에 흩어진 땔감 부스러기를 불 속으로 던져 넣으며, 더미의 한복판을 들여다보다가, 이따금 눈을 들어 불길의 높이를 가늠하거나, 그와 함께 솟아올라 어둠 속으로 흘러가는 큰 불티를 좇았다."], ["The beaming sight, and the penetrating warmth, seemed to breed in him a cumulative cheerfulness, which soon amounted to delight.", "빛나는 광경과 파고드는 온기가 그의 안에 점점 쌓여가는 쾌활함을 길러내는 듯했고, 그것은 이내 환희에 이르렀다."], ["With his stick in his hand he began to jig a private minuet, a bunch of copper seals shining and swinging like a pendulum from under his waistcoat: he also began to sing, in the voice of a bee up a flue—", "손에 막대를 든 채 그는 혼자만의 미뉴에트를 추기 시작했고, 조끼 밑에서 한 묶음의 구리 인장이 추처럼 반짝이며 흔들렸다. 그는 또한, 굴뚝을 타고 오르는 벌의 목소리로 노래하기 시작했다—"]], [["“The king′ call’d down′ his no-bles all′, By one′, by two′, by three′; Earl Mar′-shal, I’ll′ go shrive′-the queen′, And thou′ shalt wend′ with me′.", "\"임금님께서 신하들을 죄다 불러들이셨네, 하나둘 셋 차례로 불러들이셨네, 마셜 백작이여, 내 가서 왕비의 죄를 사하리니, 그대도 나와 함께 길을 나서야 하리.\""]], [["“A boon′, a boon′, quoth Earl′ Mar-shal′, And fell′ on his bend′-ded knee′, That what′-so-e’er′ the queen′ shall say′, No harm′ there-of′ may be′.”", "\"은총을, 은총을, 마셜 백작이 여쭈오며, 무릎을 꺾어 꿇어앉고는, 왕비께서 무슨 말씀을 하시든, 거기서 화가 미치지 않게 하소서.\""]], [["Want of breath prevented a continuance of the song; and the breakdown attracted the attention of a firm-standing man of middle age, who kept each corner of his crescent-shaped mouth rigorously drawn back into his cheek, as if to do away with any suspicion of mirthfulness which might erroneously have attached to him.", "숨이 차서 노래를 더 이어가지 못했고, 그 끊김은 중년의 한 사내, 자기에게 혹여 잘못 들러붙었을지 모를 흥겨움의 기미를 죄다 떨쳐버리려는 듯 초승달 모양 입가의 양 끝을 두 볼 속으로 단단히 끌어당겨 다물고 굳건히 선 사내의 주의를 끌었다."]], [["“A fair stave, Grandfer Cantle; but I am afeard ’tis too much for the mouldy weasand of such a old man as you,”", "\"멋진 가락이오, 캔틀 영감님. 하지만 영감님 같은 노친네의 곰팡 핀 목구멍엔 너무 벅찰까 걱정이구려,\""], ["he said to the wrinkled reveller.", "그가 그 주름투성이 흥취꾼에게 말했다."], ["“Dostn’t wish th’ wast three sixes again, Grandfer, as you was when you first learnt to sing it?”", "\"처음 그 노래를 배웠을 때처럼 다시 열여덟 살이 되고 싶진 않소, 영감님?\""]], [["“Hey?”", "\"어?\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, stopping in his dance.", "캔틀 영감이 춤을 멈추며 말했다."]], [["“Dostn’t wish wast young again, I say?", "\"다시 젊어지고 싶지 않냐고 했소."], ["There’s a hole in thy poor bellows nowadays seemingly.”", "요즘 영감님 그 가엾은 풀무엔 구멍이 난 모양인데.\""]], [["“But there’s good art in me?", "\"하지만 내겐 그래도 솜씨가 있잖나?"], ["If I couldn’t make a little wind go a long ways I should seem no younger than the most aged man, should I, Timothy?”", "약간의 바람으로 멀리까지 가지 못한다면야, 나도 세상 제일 늙은 노인네보다 젊어 보일 게 없지 않겠나, 그렇잖나, 티머시?\""]], [["“And how about the new-married folks down there at the Quiet Woman Inn?”", "\"그건 그렇고 저 아래 콰이엇 우먼 주막에 새로 혼인한 사람들은 어찌 됐답니까?\""], ["the other inquired, pointing towards a dim light in the direction of the distant highway, but considerably apart from where the reddleman was at that moment resting.", "상대가 물으며, 멀리 큰길 쪽으로, 그러나 그 순간 레들맨이 쉬고 있던 자리와는 한참 떨어진 곳의 어슴푸레한 불빛 쪽을 가리켰다."], ["“What’s the rights of the matter about ’em?", "\"그 사람들 일이 어찌 된 게 옳은 거요?"], ["You ought to know, being an understanding man.”", "영감님은 알아야 마땅하지, 이치를 아는 분이시니.\""]], [["“But a little rakish, hey?", "\"좀 난봉꾼이긴 하지, 어? 인정하네."], ["I own to it. Master Cantle is that, or he’s nothing.", "캔틀 어른이 바로 그런 사람이거나, 아니면 아무것도 아니거나지."], ["Yet ’tis a gay fault, neighbour Fairway, that age will cure.”", "하지만 그건 흥겨운 흠이니, 페어웨이 이웃, 나이가 고쳐줄 걸세.\""]], [["“I heard that they were coming home tonight.", "\"오늘 밤 집에 온다고 들었소."], ["By this time they must have come.", "이맘때면 벌써 왔겠지."], ["What besides?”", "그밖에는?\""]], [["“The next thing is for us to go and wish ’em joy, I suppose?”", "\"다음 차례는 우리가 가서 그들에게 기쁨을 빌어주는 거겠지, 안 그런가?\""]], [["“Well, no.”", "\"음, 아니오.\""]], [["“No?", "\"아니라고?"], ["Now, I thought we must.", "난 우리가 마땅히 그래야 하는 줄 알았네."], ["I must, or ’twould be very unlike me—the first in every spree that’s going!", "적어도 난 그래야지, 안 그러면 나답지 않을 테니—무릇 벌어지는 잔치라면 첫째로 끼어드는 게 나 아닌가!"]], [["“Do thou′ put on′ a fri′-ar’s coat′, And I’ll′ put on′ a-no′-ther, And we′ will to′ Queen Ele′anor go′, Like Fri′ar and′ his bro′ther.", "\"그대는 수도사의 옷을 걸치게, 나는 또 다른 옷을 걸칠 테니, 우리는 엘리노어 왕비께로 가세, 수도사와 그 형제처럼."]], [["I met Mis’ess Yeobright, the young bride’s aunt, last night, and she told me that her son Clym was coming home a’ Christmas.", "지난밤에 요브라이트 부인을, 그 젊은 신부의 고모를 만났는데, 아들 클림이 크리스마스에 집에 온다고 하더군."], ["Wonderful clever, ’a believe—ah, I should like to have all that’s under that young man’s hair.", "어찌나 똑똑한지, 정말로—아, 저 젊은이 머릿속에 든 걸 죄다 나도 가졌으면 좋겠어."], ["Well, then, I spoke to her in my well-known merry way, and she said, ‘O that what’s shaped so venerable should talk like a fool!’—that’s what she said to me.", "그래, 그래서 내가 그 사람한테 내 그 유명한 익살스러운 투로 말을 건넸더니, 그 사람이 그러더군, '오, 점잖아 뵈는 양반이 어찌 바보처럼 말한담!'—그게 그 사람이 내게 한 말이야."], ["I don’t care for her, be jowned if I do, and so I told her.", "난 그 사람한테 신경 안 써, 진짜로 안 써, 그래서 그렇게 말해줬지."], ["‘Be jowned if I care for ’ee,’ I said.", "'나야말로 당신한테 신경 안 쓴다네,' 라고 말이야."], ["I had her there—hey?”", "내가 거기서 한 방 먹였지—어?\""]], [["“I rather think she had you,”", "\"오히려 그분이 영감님을 먹인 것 같소만,\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, his countenance slightly flagging.", "캔틀 영감이 말했는데, 그 안색이 다소 풀이 죽었다."], ["“’Tisn’t so bad as that with me?”", "\"나도 그 정도로 형편없진 않지?\""]], [["“Seemingly ’tis, however, is it because of the wedding that Clym is coming home a’ Christmas—to make a new arrangement because his mother is now left in the house alone?”", "\"보아하니 그런 모양인데, 어쨌든, 클림이 크리스마스에 집에 오는 게 그 혼사 때문이오—이제 어머니가 집에 혼자 남게 됐으니 새로 살림을 꾸리려고?\""]], [["“Yes, yes—that’s it.", "\"그래, 그래—바로 그거야."], ["But, Timothy, hearken to me,”", "하지만, 티머시, 내 말 좀 들어보게,\""], ["said the Grandfer earnestly.", "영감이 진지하게 말했다."], ["“Though known as such a joker, I be an understanding man if you catch me serious, and I am serious now.", "\"내가 그런 익살꾼으로 알려졌어도, 진지해진 나를 붙잡고 보면 난 이치를 아는 사람이고, 지금은 진지하다네."], ["I can tell ’ee lots about the married couple.", "그 부부에 관해서라면 내가 자네한테 많이 일러줄 수 있어."], ["Yes, this morning at six o’clock they went up the country to do the job, and neither vell nor mark have been seen of ’em since, though I reckon that this afternoon has brought ’em home again man and woman—wife, that is.", "그래, 오늘 아침 여섯 시에 그 일을 치르러 시골 위쪽으로 갔는데, 그 뒤로는 코빼기도 자취도 안 보였어, 그래도 내 짐작에 오늘 오후엔 남자와 여자로—아니, 부부로 다시 집에 돌아왔을 게야."], ["Isn’t it spoke like a man, Timothy, and wasn’t Mis’ess Yeobright wrong about me?”", "사내답게 말한 거 아닌가, 티머시, 요브라이트 부인이 나를 두고 한 말이 틀린 거 아닌가?\""]], [["“Yes, it will do.", "\"그래, 됐소."], ["I didn’t know the two had walked together since last fall, when her aunt forbad the banns.", "지난가을 그분이 결혼 예고를 막은 뒤로 그 둘이 다시 함께 다닌 줄은 몰랐구려."], ["How long has this new set-to been in mangling then?", "그럼 이 새 일이 갈려나간 지는 얼마나 된 거요?"], ["Do you know, Humphrey?”", "자네 아나, 험프리?\""]], [["“Yes, how long?”", "\"그래, 얼마나 됐나?\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle smartly, likewise turning to Humphrey.", "캔틀 영감이 잽싸게 험프리를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."], ["“I ask that question.”", "\"내가 그걸 묻는 거야.\""]], [["“Ever since her aunt altered her mind, and said she might have the man after all,”", "\"그분이 마음을 바꿔서, 그래도 그 남자한테 시집가도 좋다고 한 뒤로 줄곧이지요,\""], ["replied Humphrey, without removing his eyes from the fire.", "험프리가 불에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 답했다."], ["He was a somewhat solemn young fellow, and carried the hook and leather gloves of a furze-cutter, his legs, by reason of that occupation, being sheathed in bulging leggings as stiff as the Philistine’s greaves of brass.", "그는 어딘가 엄숙한 젊은이로, 가시금작화 베는 사람의 낫과 가죽 장갑을 지니고 있었으며, 그 일 때문에 두 다리는 블레셋 사람의 놋쇠 정강이받이만큼 뻣뻣하게 불룩한 각반에 싸여 있었다."], ["“That’s why they went away to be married, I count.", "\"그래서 둘이 결혼하러 떠난 거라고 봅니다."], ["You see, after kicking up such a nunny-watch and forbidding the banns ’twould have made Mis’ess Yeobright seem foolish-like to have a banging wedding in the same parish all as if she’d never gainsaid it.”", "보세요, 그렇게 한바탕 소동을 피우고 결혼 예고를 막아놓고 나서, 마치 한 번도 반대한 적 없던 것처럼 같은 교구에서 떠들썩한 잔치를 벌였다면 요브라이트 부인이 바보처럼 보였을 거 아닙니까.\""]], [["“Exactly—seem foolish-like; and that’s very bad for the poor things that be so, though I only guess as much, to be sure,”", "\"바로 그거야—바보처럼 보일 테지. 그리고 그건 그런 처지의 가엾은 사람들한테 아주 안된 일이지, 물론 나야 그저 짐작만 할 뿐이지만 말일세,\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, still strenuously preserving a sensible bearing and mien.", "캔틀 영감이 여전히 분별 있는 태도와 풍모를 애써 지키며 말했다."]], [["“Ah, well, I was at church that day,”", "\"아, 그런데, 난 그날 교회에 있었다오,\""], ["said Fairway, “which was a very curious thing to happen.”", "페어웨이가 말했다, \"참 묘한 일이 벌어졌지.\""]], [["“If ’twasn’t my name’s Simple,”", "\"내가 거기 있었다면야 내 이름은 심플이지,\""], ["said the Grandfer emphatically.", "영감이 힘주어 말했다."], ["“I ha’n’t been there to-year; and now the winter is a-coming on I won’t say I shall.”", "\"난 올해 거긴 안 갔어. 그리고 이제 겨울이 다가오니 갈 거라고도 못 하겠네.\""]], [["“I ha’n’t been these three years,”", "\"난 삼 년째 안 갔소,\" 험프리가 말했다."], ["said Humphrey; “for I’m so dead sleepy of a Sunday; and ’tis so terrible far to get there; and when you do get there ’tis such a mortal poor chance that you’ll be chose for up above, when so many bain’t, that I bide at home and don’t go at all.”", "\"일요일이면 어찌나 죽도록 졸린지, 게다가 거기까지 가기가 끔찍이도 멀어요. 또 막상 가본들 그토록 많은 이들이 못 뽑히는 마당에 내가 저 위로 뽑힐 가망이 워낙 지독히도 적으니, 그냥 집에 들어앉아 아예 안 가는 거요.\""]], [["“I not only happened to be there,”", "\"난 거기 있었을 뿐 아니라,\""], ["said Fairway, with a fresh collection of emphasis, “but I was sitting in the same pew as Mis’ess Yeobright.", "페어웨이가 새삼 힘을 그러모아 말했다, \"요브라이트 부인과 같은 신도석에 앉아 있었지."], ["And though you may not see it as such, it fairly made my blood run cold to hear her.", "그러니 자네들이야 그렇게 안 볼지 몰라도, 그 사람 말을 듣자니 정말이지 피가 얼어붙더군."], ["Yes, it is a curious thing; but it made my blood run cold, for I was close at her elbow.”", "그래, 묘한 일이야. 하지만 내 피를 얼어붙게 하더라니까, 내가 바로 그 사람 팔꿈치 옆에 붙어 있었으니.\""], ["The speaker looked round upon the bystanders, now drawing closer to hear him, with his lips gathered tighter than ever in the rigorousness of his descriptive moderation.", "말하는 이는 둘러선 이들을 죽 둘러보았는데, 그들은 이제 그의 말을 들으려 바싹 다가들었고, 그 입술은 묘사의 절제를 엄히 지키느라 어느 때보다도 더 꼭 다물려 있었다."]], [["“’Tis a serious job to have things happen to ’ee there,”", "\"거기서 그런 일을 당하는 건 심각한 노릇이지요,\""], ["said a woman behind.", "뒤편의 한 여자가 말했다."]], [["“‘Ye are to declare it,’ was the parson’s words,”", "\"'이의를 밝히시오,' 그게 목사님 말씀이었지,\""], ["Fairway continued.", "페어웨이가 이어갔다."], ["“And then up stood a woman at my side—a-touching of me.", "\"그러자 내 곁의 한 여자가—내게 닿을 만큼 가까이서 벌떡 일어서더군."], ["‘Well, be damned if there isn’t Mis’ess Yeobright a-standing up,’ I said to myself.", "'아니, 빌어먹을, 저거 요브라이트 부인이 일어선 거 아닌가,' 하고 난 속으로 말했지."], ["Yes, neighbours, though I was in the temple of prayer that’s what I said.", "그래, 이웃들, 내가 비록 기도의 성전에 있었어도 그렇게 말했다네."], ["’Tis against my conscience to curse and swear in company, and I hope any woman here will overlook it.", "사람들 앞에서 욕하고 악담하는 건 내 양심에 거슬리는 일이고, 여기 어느 여자분이든 너그러이 봐주길 바라네."], ["Still what I did say I did say, and ’twould be a lie if I didn’t own it.”", "그래도 내가 한 말은 한 말이니, 그걸 인정 안 하면 거짓말이 될 게야.\""]], [["“So ’twould, neighbour Fairway.”", "\"그렇고말고요, 페어웨이 이웃.\""]], [["“‘Be damned if there isn’t Mis’ess Yeobright a-standing up,’ I said,”", "\"'빌어먹을, 저거 요브라이트 부인이 일어선 거 아닌가,'"], ["the narrator repeated, giving out the bad word with the same passionless severity of face as before, which proved how entirely necessity and not gusto had to do with the iteration.", "하고 난 말했지,\" 이야기꾼이 되풀이하며, 그 나쁜 말을 아까와 똑같이 무덤덤하게 굳은 얼굴로 내뱉었으니, 그 되풀이가 흥에 겨워서가 아니라 전적으로 필요에 따른 것임을 입증했다."], ["“And the next thing I heard was, ‘I forbid the banns,’ from her.", "\"그리고 다음으로 들린 말은, 그 사람한테서 나온 '나는 이 결혼 예고를 막습니다'였어."], ["‘I’ll speak to you after the service,’ said the parson, in quite a homely way—yes, turning all at once into a common man no holier than you or I.", "'예식 끝나고 얘기합시다,' 목사님이 아주 소탈하게 말하더군—그래, 단번에 자네나 나보다 조금도 거룩할 것 없는 보통 사람으로 변해버린 거야."], ["Ah, her face was pale!", "아, 그 사람 얼굴이 어찌나 창백하던지!"], ["Maybe you can call to mind that monument in Weatherbury church—the cross-legged soldier that have had his arm knocked away by the schoolchildren?", "자네들 혹시 웨더베리 교회에 있는 그 기념상 기억하나—학교 아이들이 팔을 떨어뜨려 버린 그 다리 꼰 병사 말이야?"], ["Well, he would about have matched that woman’s face, when she said, ‘I forbid the banns.’”", "음, '나는 이 결혼 예고를 막습니다,' 하고 말할 때 그 사람 얼굴이 그 상이랑 거의 똑같았다네.\""]], [["The audience cleared their throats and tossed a few stalks into the fire, not because these deeds were urgent, but to give themselves time to weigh the moral of the story.", "청중은 목청을 가다듬고 줄기 몇 개를 불 속에 던져 넣었으니, 그것이 급한 일이어서가 아니라 그 이야기의 교훈을 곱씹을 시간을 자기들에게 주려는 것이었다."]], [["“I’m sure when I heard they’d been forbid I felt as glad as if anybody had gied me sixpence,”", "\"그 결혼 예고가 막혔다는 소릴 들었을 땐, 정말이지 누가 나한테 6펜스라도 쥐여준 것마냥 기뻤어요,\""], ["said an earnest voice—that of Olly Dowden, a woman who lived by making heath brooms, or besoms.", "진지한 목소리가—히스 빗자루, 곧 비를 만들어 살아가는 여자 올리 다우든의 목소리가 말했다."], ["Her nature was to be civil to enemies as well as to friends, and grateful to all the world for letting her remain alive.", "그녀는 천성이 원수에게도 벗에게나 다름없이 공손했고, 자기를 살아 있게 해준다는 이유로 온 세상에 감사했다."]], [["“And now the maid have married him just the same,”", "\"그런데 이제 그 처자가 끝내 그 남자한테 똑같이 시집을 갔구먼,\""], ["said Humphrey.", "험프리가 말했다."]], [["“After that Mis’ess Yeobright came round and was quite agreeable,”", "\"그 일 뒤에 요브라이트 부인이 마음을 돌려서 아주 흔쾌해졌지,\""], ["Fairway resumed, with an unheeding air, to show that his words were no appendage to Humphrey’s, but the result of independent reflection.", "페어웨이가 자기 말이 험프리의 말에 덧붙은 것이 아니라 독자적인 사색의 결과임을 보이려는 듯 아랑곳없는 투로 다시 말했다."]], [["“Supposing they were ashamed, I don’t see why they shouldn’t have done it here-right,”", "\"부끄러웠다 치더라도, 어째서 바로 여기서 못 했는지 난 모르겠어요,\""], ["said a wide-spread woman whose stays creaked like shoes whenever she stooped or turned.", "몸을 굽히거나 돌릴 때마다 코르셋이 구두처럼 삐걱대는 펑퍼짐한 여자가 말했다."], ["“’Tis well to call the neighbours together and to hae a good racket once now and then; and it may as well be when there’s a wedding as at tide-times.", "\"이웃들을 한데 불러 모아 가끔 한바탕 떠들썩하게 노는 것도 좋은 일이고, 그게 명절 때든 혼사 때든 매한가지지요."], ["I don’t care for close ways.”", "난 쉬쉬하는 방식은 질색이에요.\""]], [["“Ah, now, you’d hardly believe it, but I don’t care for gay weddings,”", "\"아, 그런데, 믿기 어렵겠지만, 난 떠들썩한 결혼식은 좋아하질 않아,\""], ["said Timothy Fairway, his eyes again travelling round.", "티머시 페어웨이가 다시 눈길을 죽 돌리며 말했다."], ["“I hardly blame Thomasin Yeobright and neighbour Wildeve for doing it quiet, if I must own it.", "\"솔직히 말하자면, 토머신 요브라이트와 와일디브 이웃이 조용히 치른 걸 난 별로 탓하지 않네."], ["A wedding at home means five and six-handed reels by the hour; and they do a man’s legs no good when he’s over forty.”", "집에서 하는 결혼식이란 다섯 손 여섯 손 잡고 몇 시간이고 릴 춤을 추는 건데, 마흔 넘은 사내한테 그건 다리에 영 안 좋거든.\""]], [["“True.", "\"맞아요."], ["Once at the woman’s house you can hardly say nay to being one in a jig, knowing all the time that you be expected to make yourself worth your victuals.”", "일단 여자 집에 발을 들이면, 춤판에 한몫 끼는 걸 마다하기가 어렵지요, 내내 제 밥값은 해야 한다는 걸 알면서 말이오.\""]], [["“You be bound to dance at Christmas because ’tis the time o’ year; you must dance at weddings because ’tis the time o’ life.", "\"크리스마스에 춤춰야 하는 건 그게 그 철이기 때문이고, 결혼식에 춤춰야 하는 건 그게 인생의 그 시절이기 때문이지."], ["At christenings folk will even smuggle in a reel or two, if ’tis no further on than the first or second chiel.", "세례식에서도 사람들은 첫째나 둘째 아이까지는 릴 춤 한두 판을 슬쩍 끼워 넣곤 한다네."], ["And this is not naming the songs you’ve got to sing....", "게다가 이건 자네가 불러야 할 노래들은 아직 입에도 안 올린 거야……"], ["For my part I like a good hearty funeral as well as anything.", "난 말이야, 무엇 못잖게 푸짐한 장례식이 좋더라고."], ["You’ve as splendid victuals and drink as at other parties, and even better. And it don’t wear your legs to stumps in talking over a poor fellow’s ways as it do to stand up in hornpipes.”", "다른 잔치만큼, 아니 그보다 더 훌륭한 음식과 술이 있고, 가엾은 친구의 살아온 이야기를 두고 떠드는 건 혼파이프 춤이랍시고 벌떡벌떡 일어서는 것처럼 다리를 닳아 없애지도 않거든.\""]], [["“Nine folks out of ten would own ’twas going too far to dance then, I suppose?”", "\"열에 아홉은 그럴 때 춤을 추는 건 도가 지나친 일이라고 인정할 거요, 아마?\""], ["suggested Grandfer Cantle.", "캔틀 영감이 넌지시 말했다."]], [["“’Tis the only sort of party a staid man can feel safe at after the mug have been round a few times.”", "\"그건 점잖은 사내가 술잔이 몇 순배 돈 뒤에도 마음 놓을 수 있는 유일한 잔치지.\""]], [["“Well, I can’t understand a quiet ladylike little body like Tamsin Yeobright caring to be married in such a mean way,”", "\"음, 토머신 요브라이트처럼 조용하고 숙녀다운 자그마한 처자가 그런 초라한 식으로 결혼하고 싶어 했다는 게 난 도무지 이해가 안 돼요,\""], ["said Susan Nunsuch, the wide woman, who preferred the original subject.", "본래의 화제를 더 좋아하는 펑퍼짐한 여자 수전 넌서치가 말했다."], ["“’Tis worse than the poorest do.", "\"그건 제일 가난한 사람들이 하는 것보다도 못해요."], ["And I shouldn’t have cared about the man, though some may say he’s good-looking.”", "그리고 난 그 남자라면 신경도 안 썼을 거예요, 더러는 잘생겼다고들 하지만.\""]], [["“To give him his due he’s a clever, learned fellow in his way—a’most as clever as Clym Yeobright used to be.", "\"공평하게 말하자면 그는 제 나름으로 영리하고 학식 있는 친구지—거의 옛날 클림 요브라이트만큼 영리했어."], ["He was brought up to better things than keeping the Quiet Woman.", "콰이엇 우먼 주막이나 지키기보다 더 나은 일을 하도록 자라났지."], ["An engineer—that’s what the man was, as we know; but he threw away his chance, and so ’a took a public house to live.", "기술자—우리가 알다시피 그 남자가 그런 사람이었어. 그런데 제 기회를 내던지고 그래서 먹고살려고 주막을 차린 거야."], ["His learning was no use to him at all.”", "그 학식이 그에겐 아무짝에도 쓸모없게 됐지.\""]], [["“Very often the case,”", "\"흔한 일이지요,\""], ["said Olly, the besom-maker.", "비 만드는 올리가 말했다."], ["“And yet how people do strive after it and get it!", "\"그런데도 사람들이 그걸 얼마나 애써 손에 넣으려 하는지!"], ["The class of folk that couldn’t use to make a round O to save their bones from the pit can write their names now without a sputter of the pen, oftentimes without a single blot—what do I say?—why, almost without a desk to lean their stomachs and elbows upon.”", "한때는 제 목숨이 구덩이에 빠지는 걸 막으려 해도 둥근 오자 하나 못 긋던 부류의 사람들이, 이제는 펜을 더듬거리지도 않고, 곧잘 얼룩 한 점 없이 제 이름을 쓴다니까요—무슨 말이냐 하면—아니, 배와 팔꿈치를 기댈 책상조차 거의 없이도 말이에요.\""]], [["“True—’tis amazing what a polish the world have been brought to,”", "\"맞아—세상이 얼마나 윤이 났는지 놀라울 따름이지,\""], ["said Humphrey.", "험프리가 말했다."]], [["“Why, afore I went a soldier in the Bang-up Locals (as we was called), in the year four,”", "\"아, 내가 사년도에 (우리가 그렇게 불렸지) 뱅업 의용대에 군인으로 들어가기 전엔,\""], ["chimed in Grandfer Cantle brightly, “I didn’t know no more what the world was like than the commonest man among ye.", "캔틀 영감이 명랑하게 끼어들었다, \"세상이 어떻게 생겨먹은 건지 자네들 중 제일 평범한 사람만큼도 몰랐다네."], ["And now, jown it all, I won’t say what I bain’t fit for, hey?”", "그런데 이제는, 젠장, 내가 못 할 게 뭐라고는 말 안 하겠어, 어?\""]], [["“Couldst sign the book, no doubt,”", "\"명부에 서명이야 하실 수 있겠지,\""], ["said Fairway, “if wast young enough to join hands with a woman again, like Wildeve and Mis’ess Tamsin, which is more than Humph there could do, for he follows his father in learning.", "페어웨이가 말했다, \"와일디브와 토머신 색시처럼 다시 어떤 여자랑 손잡을 만큼 젊기만 하다면야. 그건 저기 험프 녀석은 못 할 일이지, 글 배우는 건 제 아비를 닮았으니."], ["Ah, Humph, well I can mind when I was married how I zid thy father’s mark staring me in the face as I went to put down my name.", "아, 험프, 난 내가 결혼할 때 내 이름을 적으러 가다가 자네 아비의 표시가 떡하니 나를 노려보던 게 똑똑히 기억나네."], ["He and your mother were the couple married just afore we were and there stood they father’s cross with arms stretched out like a great banging scarecrow.", "그 양반하고 자네 어미가 우리 바로 앞에 결혼한 부부였는데, 거기 자네 아비의 십자 표시가 떡 버틴 허수아비처럼 두 팔을 쩍 벌리고 서 있었지."], ["What a terrible black cross that was—thy father’s very likeness in en!", "그게 어찌나 끔찍하게 시커먼 십자던지—그 안에 자네 아비를 빼다 박았더라고!"], ["To save my soul I couldn’t help laughing when I zid en, though all the time I was as hot as dog-days, what with the marrying, and what with the woman a-hanging to me, and what with Jack Changley and a lot more chaps grinning at me through church window.", "그걸 보고 난 죽어도 웃음을 못 참겠더군, 그러는 내내 난 한증막처럼 후끈 달아 있었는데, 결혼한답시고 그렇고, 여자가 내게 달라붙어 그렇고, 잭 체인글리며 다른 녀석들이 교회 창문 너머로 날 보고 히죽대서 그랬지."], ["But the next moment a strawmote would have knocked me down, for I called to mind that if thy father and mother had had high words once, they’d been at it twenty times since they’d been man and wife, and I zid myself as the next poor stunpoll to get into the same mess....", "그런데 다음 순간엔 지푸라기 하나에도 쓰러질 지경이었어, 자네 아비랑 어미가 한 번이라도 험한 말을 주고받았다면 부부 된 뒤로 스무 번은 더 그랬을 거라는 데 생각이 미쳤거든, 그리고 똑같은 수렁에 빠질 다음번 가엾은 멍청이가 바로 나라는 게 보이더라고……"], ["Ah—well, what a day ’twas!”", "아—참, 그 무슨 날이었던지!\""]], [["“Wildeve is older than Tamsin Yeobright by a good-few summers.", "\"와일디브는 토머신 요브라이트보다 여러 여름은 더 나이를 먹었지."], ["A pretty maid too she is.", "그 색시도 어여쁜 처자야."], ["A young woman with a home must be a fool to tear her smock for a man like that.”", "제 집이 있는 젊은 여자가 그런 남자 때문에 제 속치마를 찢는다면 바보지.\""]], [["The speaker, a peat- or turf-cutter, who had newly joined the group, carried across his shoulder the singular heart-shaped spade of large dimensions used in that species of labour, and its well-whetted edge gleamed like a silver bow in the beams of the fire.", "이 말을 한 사람은 토탄, 곧 뗏장 베는 일꾼으로, 새로 일행에 끼어든 자였는데, 그 일에 쓰이는 커다란 하트 모양의 별난 삽을 어깨에 가로질러 메고 있었으니, 잘 갈린 그 날은 불빛을 받아 은빛 활처럼 번득였다."]], [["“A hundred maidens would have had him if he’d asked ’em,”", "\"청하기만 했어도 백 명의 처녀가 그한테 갔을걸요,\""], ["said the wide woman.", "펑퍼짐한 여자가 말했다."]], [["“Didst ever know a man, neighbour, that no woman at all would marry?”", "\"이웃, 그 어떤 여자도 도무지 시집가려 하지 않을 남자를 본 적 있소?\""], ["inquired Humphrey.", "험프리가 물었다."]], [["“I never did,”", "\"난 본 적 없네,\""], ["said the turf-cutter.", "뗏장 일꾼이 말했다."]], [["“Nor I,”", "\"나도,\""], ["said another.", "또 한 사람이 말했다."]], [["“Nor I,”", "\"나도,\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle.", "캔틀 영감이 말했다."]], [["“Well, now, I did once,”", "\"음, 그런데, 난 한 번 있었지,\""], ["said Timothy Fairway, adding more firmness to one of his legs.", "티머시 페어웨이가 한쪽 다리에 더욱 힘을 실으며 말했다."], ["“I did know of such a man.", "\"그런 남자를 하나 알았어."], ["But only once, mind.”", "하지만 딱 한 번뿐이야, 알겠나.\""], ["He gave his throat a thorough rake round, as if it were the duty of every person not to be mistaken through thickness of voice.", "그는 마치 사람마다 목소리가 탁해서 오해를 사선 안 될 의무라도 있는 듯, 목구멍을 철저히 한 바퀴 긁어냈다."], ["“Yes, I knew of such a man,”", "\"그래, 난 그런 남자를 알았다네,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“And what ghastly gallicrow might the poor fellow have been like, Master Fairway?”", "\"그 가엾은 친구가 대체 어떤 끔찍한 허수아비 꼴이었답니까, 페어웨이 어른?\""], ["asked the turf-cutter.", "뗏장 일꾼이 물었다."]], [["“Well, ’a was neither a deaf man, nor a dumb man, nor a blind man.", "\"음, 그는 귀머거리도 아니고, 벙어리도 아니고, 장님도 아니었어."], ["What ’a was I don’t say.”", "그가 무엇이었는지는 말 안 하겠네.\""]], [["“Is he known in these parts?”", "\"이 근방에 알려진 사람이오?\""], ["said Olly Dowden.", "올리 다우든이 말했다."]], [["“Hardly,”", "\"글쎄,\""], ["said Timothy; “but I name no name....", "티머시가 말했다. \"하지만 이름은 안 대겠어……"], ["Come, keep the fire up there, youngsters.”", "자, 거기 불 좀 살려놓게, 젊은이들.\""]], [["“Whatever is Christian Cantle’s teeth a-chattering for?”", "\"크리스천 캔틀은 대체 이가 왜 덜덜 떨리는 거야?\""], ["said a boy from amid the smoke and shades on the other side of the blaze.", "불길 건너편 연기와 어둠 속에서 한 소년이 말했다."], ["“Be ye a-cold, Christian?”", "\"추워, 크리스천?\""]], [["A thin jibbering voice was heard to reply, “No, not at all.”", "가늘게 더듬대는 목소리가 답하는 게 들렸다, \"아니, 전혀.\""]], [["“Come forward, Christian, and show yourself.", "\"이리 나와봐, 크리스천, 모습을 좀 보이게."], ["I didn’t know you were here,”", "자네가 여기 있는 줄 몰랐네,\""], ["said Fairway, with a humane look across towards that quarter.", "페어웨이가 그 방향으로 인정 어린 눈길을 던지며 말했다."]], [["Thus requested, a faltering man, with reedy hair, no shoulders, and a great quantity of wrist and ankle beyond his clothes, advanced a step or two by his own will, and was pushed by the will of others half a dozen steps more.", "이리 청을 받자, 갈대 같은 머리칼에 어깨라곤 없고, 옷 밖으로 손목과 발목이 잔뜩 삐져나온 머뭇거리는 사내 하나가 제 뜻으로 한두 걸음 나섰고, 다른 이들의 뜻에 떠밀려 대여섯 걸음을 더 나아갔다."], ["He was Grandfer Cantle’s youngest son.", "그는 캔틀 영감의 막내아들이었다."]], [["“What be ye quaking for, Christian?”", "\"뭐가 그리 떨리는 거야, 크리스천?\""], ["said the turf-cutter kindly.", "뗏장 일꾼이 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“I’m the man.”", "\"내가 바로 그 남자예요.\""]], [["“What man?”", "\"무슨 남자?\""]], [["“The man no woman will marry.”", "\"어떤 여자도 시집 안 가려는 남자요.\""]], [["“The deuce you be!”", "\"세상에 자네가!\""], ["said Timothy Fairway, enlarging his gaze to cover Christian’s whole surface and a great deal more, Grandfer Cantle meanwhile staring as a hen stares at the duck she has hatched.", "티머시 페어웨이가, 크리스천의 온 표면과 그보다 훨씬 더한 데까지 덮을 만큼 눈길을 넓히며 말했고, 그러는 동안 캔틀 영감은 마치 제가 품어 깐 오리 새끼를 멍하니 바라보는 암탉처럼 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Yes, I be he; and it makes me afeard,”", "\"네, 제가 바로 그자예요. 그게 무서워요,\""], ["said Christian.", "크리스천이 말했다."], ["“D’ye think ’twill hurt me?", "\"그게 절 해칠 것 같아요?"], ["I shall always say I don’t care, and swear to it, though I do care all the while.”", "전 늘 신경 안 쓴다고 말하고 그렇게 맹세도 하겠지만, 사실은 내내 신경이 쓰여요.\""]], [["“Well, be damned if this isn’t the queerest start ever I know’d,”", "\"음, 빌어먹을, 이런 희한한 일은 내 생전 처음일세,\""], ["said Mr. Fairway.", "페어웨이 씨가 말했다."], ["“I didn’t mean you at all.", "\"난 자네를 두고 한 말이 전혀 아니었어."], ["There’s another in the country, then!", "그럼 이 고장에 또 하나가 있는 거로군!"], ["Why did ye reveal yer misfortune, Christian?”", "어쩌자고 제 불운을 까발리는 거야, 크리스천?\""]], [["“’Twas to be if ’twas, I suppose.", "\"그럴 일이면 그런 거겠지요, 뭐."], ["I can’t help it, can I?”", "저도 어쩔 수 없잖아요, 그렇죠?\""], ["He turned upon them his painfully circular eyes, surrounded by concentric lines like targets.", "그는 동심원 무늬가 과녁처럼 둘러친, 가련하리만치 동그란 눈을 그들에게로 돌렸다."]], [["“No, that’s true.", "\"그래, 그건 맞아."], ["But ’tis a melancholy thing, and my blood ran cold when you spoke, for I felt there were two poor fellows where I had thought only one.", "하지만 그건 우울한 일이고, 자네가 말할 때 내 피가 얼어붙더라고, 하나뿐인 줄 알았던 자리에 가엾은 친구가 둘이나 있다는 게 느껴졌으니."], ["’Tis a sad thing for ye, Christian.", "자네한텐 안된 일이야, 크리스천."], ["How’st know the women won’t hae thee?”", "여자들이 자네를 안 받아주리란 걸 어찌 아나?\""]], [["“I’ve asked ’em.”", "\"제가 물어봤어요.\""]], [["“Sure I should never have thought you had the face.", "\"원, 자네한테 그럴 낯이 있을 줄은 몰랐네."], ["Well, and what did the last one say to ye?", "그래, 마지막 여자는 자네한테 뭐랬나?"], ["Nothing that can’t be got over, perhaps, after all?”", "어쩌면, 따지고 보면 도무지 넘을 수 없는 말은 아니었을지도?\""]], [["“‘Get out of my sight, you slack-twisted, slim-looking maphrotight fool,’ was the woman’s words to me.”", "\"'내 눈앞에서 꺼져, 이 흐물흐물 비실비실한 반편이 바보야,' 그게 그 여자가 내게 한 말이에요.\""]], [["“Not encouraging, I own,”", "\"썩 고무적이진 않군, 인정하네,\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“‘Get out of my sight, you slack-twisted, slim-looking maphrotight fool,’ is rather a hard way of saying No.", "\"'내 눈앞에서 꺼져, 이 흐물흐물 비실비실한 반편이 바보야'라는 건 싫다는 말치고는 꽤 모진 투지."], ["But even that might be overcome by time and patience, so as to let a few grey hairs show themselves in the hussy’s head.", "하지만 그것조차 세월과 인내로, 그 계집애 머리에 흰머리 몇 가닥 돋게 할 만큼만 견디면 넘을 수 있을지도 몰라."], ["How old be you, Christian?”", "자네 나이가 몇인가, 크리스천?\""]], [["“Thirty-one last tatie-digging, Mister Fairway.”", "\"지난 감자 캘 무렵에 서른하나였어요, 페어웨이 어른.\""]], [["“Not a boy—not a boy.", "\"애송이는 아니군—애송이는 아니야."], ["Still there’s hope yet.”", "그래도 아직 희망은 있어.\""]], [["“That’s my age by baptism, because that’s put down in the great book of the Judgment that they keep in church vestry; but Mother told me I was born some time afore I was christened.”", "\"그게 세례로 친 제 나이예요, 교회 제의실에 보관하는 그 심판의 대장에 그렇게 적혀 있으니까요. 그런데 어머니가 그러는데 전 세례받기 얼마 전에 태어났대요.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""]], [["“But she couldn’t tell when, to save her life, except that there was no moon.”", "\"하지만 달이 없었다는 것 말곤, 어머니도 죽어라 해도 언제였는지 못 댔어요.\""]], [["“No moon—that’s bad.", "\"달이 없었다—그건 나쁜데."], ["Hey, neighbours, that’s bad for him!”", "어이, 이웃들, 저 친구한텐 그게 나쁘구먼!\""]], [["“Yes, ’tis bad,”", "\"그래, 나쁘지,\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, shaking his head.", "캔틀 영감이 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["“Mother know’d ’twas no moon, for she asked another woman that had an almanac, as she did whenever a boy was born to her, because of the saying, ‘No moon, no man,’ which made her afeard every man-child she had.", "\"어머니는 달이 없었다는 걸 알았어요, 달력 가진 다른 여자한테 물어봤거든요, 사내아이를 낳을 때마다 그랬대요, '달이 없으면 사내도 없다'는 속담 때문에 사내아이를 낳을 때마다 겁이 났던 거죠."], ["Do ye really think it serious, Mister Fairway, that there was no moon?”", "정말로 심각하다고 보세요, 페어웨이 어른, 달이 없었다는 게?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그럼."], ["‘No moon, no man.’ ’Tis one of the truest sayings ever spit out.", "'달이 없으면 사내도 없다.' 그건 여태 내뱉은 말 중에 가장 참된 속담 중 하나야."], ["The boy never comes to anything that’s born at new moon.", "초승달에 태어난 아이는 결코 뭐가 못 되거든."], ["A bad job for thee, Christian, that you should have showed your nose then of all days in the month.”", "자네한테 안된 일이지, 크리스천, 하필 한 달 중에 그런 날에 코빼기를 내밀었으니.\""]], [["“I suppose the moon was terrible full when you were born?”", "\"어른은 태어났을 때 달이 끔찍이도 꽉 찼었나 봐요?\""], ["said Christian, with a look of hopeless admiration at Fairway.", "크리스천이 가망 없는 흠모의 눈빛으로 페어웨이를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Well, ’a was not new,” Mr.", "\"음, 초승달은 아니었지,\""], ["Fairway replied, with a disinterested gaze.", "페어웨이 씨가 무심한 눈길로 답했다."]], [["“I’d sooner go without drink at Lammas-tide than be a man of no moon,”", "\"전 달 없는 사내가 되느니 라마스 절기에 술을 끊는 편을 택하겠어요,\""], ["continued Christian, in the same shattered recitative.", "크리스천이 똑같이 부서진 읊조림으로 이어갔다."], ["“’Tis said I be only the rames of a man, and no good for my race at all; and I suppose that’s the cause o’t.”", "\"전 사내의 허깨비일 뿐이라, 제 핏줄엔 아무짝에도 쓸모없다는 말이 있던데, 그게 그 때문인가 봐요.\""]], [["“Ay,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, somewhat subdued in spirit; “and yet his mother cried for scores of hours when ’a was a boy, for fear he should outgrow hisself and go for a soldier.”", "캔틀 영감이 다소 풀 죽은 마음으로 말했다, \"그래도 저 녀석이 사내아이일 적엔 제 어미가 몇십 시간이고 울었지, 저놈이 너무 자라서 군인으로 가버릴까 봐 무서워서 말이야.\""]], [["“Well, there’s many just as bad as he.”", "\"음, 저 친구만큼 딱한 사람이야 많지,\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["“Wethers must live their time as well as other sheep, poor soul.”", "\"불깐 양도 다른 양들처럼 제 명을 다 살아야지, 가엾은 것.\""]], [["“So perhaps I shall rub on?", "\"그럼 저도 그럭저럭 버텨낼까요?"], ["Ought I to be afeared o’ nights, Master Fairway?”", "밤이면 무서워해야 할까요, 페어웨이 어른?\""]], [["“You’ll have to lie alone all your life; and ’tis not to married couples but to single sleepers that a ghost shows himself when ’a do come.", "\"자넨 평생 혼자 자게 될 거야. 그리고 귀신은 나타날 때 부부한테가 아니라 혼자 자는 이한테 모습을 보이는 법이지."], ["One has been seen lately, too.", "게다가 요즘 하나가 보였다더군."], ["A very strange one.”", "아주 이상한 놈이.\""]], [["“No—don’t talk about it if ’tis agreeable of ye not to!", "\"안 돼요—괜찮으시다면 그 얘긴 마세요!"], ["’Twill make my skin crawl when I think of it in bed alone.", "침대에 혼자 누워 그걸 떠올리면 소름이 끼친다고요."], ["But you will—ah, you will, I know, Timothy; and I shall dream all night o’t!", "하지만 하실 거죠—아, 하실 거예요, 알아요, 티머시. 그럼 밤새도록 그 꿈을 꿀 거라고요!"], ["A very strange one?", "아주 이상한 놈이라뇨?"], ["What sort of a spirit did ye mean when ye said, a very strange one, Timothy?—no, no—don’t tell me.”", "아주 이상한 놈이라고 했을 때 무슨 영을 두고 한 말이에요, 티머시?—아뇨, 아뇨—말하지 마세요.\""]], [["“I don’t half believe in spirits myself.", "\"나도 귀신을 절반도 안 믿어."], ["But I think it ghostly enough—what I was told.", "하지만 내가 들은 얘긴—제법 으스스하다고 보네."], ["’Twas a little boy that zid it.”", "그걸 본 건 어린 사내아이였어.\""]], [["“What was it like?—no, don’t—”", "\"어떻게 생겼대요?—아뇨, 말하지—\""]], [["“A red one.", "\"빨간 놈이야."], ["Yes, most ghosts be white; but this is as if it had been dipped in blood.”", "그래, 귀신은 대개 흰데, 이건 마치 피에 푹 담갔던 것 같다는 거지.\""]], [["Christian drew a deep breath without letting it expand his body, and Humphrey said, “Where has it been seen?”", "크리스천은 몸을 부풀리지도 못한 채 깊이 숨을 들이켰고, 험프리가 말했다, \"그게 어디서 보였답니까?\""]], [["“Not exactly here; but in this same heth.", "\"바로 여긴 아니고, 같은 이 히스에서지."], ["But ’tisn’t a thing to talk about.", "하지만 입에 올릴 만한 일은 아니야."], ["What do ye say,”", "자네들 생각은 어떤가,\""], ["continued Fairway in brisker tones, and turning upon them as if the idea had not been Grandfer Cantle’s—“what do you say to giving the new man and wife a bit of a song tonight afore we go to bed—being their wedding-day?", "페어웨이가 한층 활기찬 어조로, 마치 그 생각이 캔틀 영감의 것이 아니었던 양 그들을 향해 돌아서며 이어갔다—\"잠자리에 들기 전에 오늘 밤 새 부부에게 노래나 한 곡 불러주는 게—그들의 혼인날이니 말이야."], ["When folks are just married ’tis as well to look glad o’t, since looking sorry won’t unjoin ’em.", "사람들이 막 결혼했을 땐 기뻐해주는 게 좋지, 슬픈 척한들 그들이 갈라지진 않을 테니."], ["I am no drinker, as we know, but when the womenfolk and youngsters have gone home we can drop down across to the Quiet Woman, and strike up a ballet in front of the married folks’ door.", "우리가 알다시피 난 술꾼은 아니지만, 아낙네와 젊은것들이 집으로 돌아가고 나면, 콰이엇 우먼으로 슬쩍 건너가, 그 부부 집 문 앞에서 노래나 한 자락 뽑을 수 있잖나."], ["’Twill please the young wife, and that’s what I should like to do, for many’s the skinful I’ve had at her hands when she lived with her aunt at Blooms-End.”", "젊은 색시가 기뻐할 게고, 나도 그러고 싶어, 그 색시가 블룸스엔드에서 고모랑 살 적에 그 손에 술 한 잔 얻어 마신 게 한두 번이 아니거든.\""]], [["“Hey?", "\"어?"], ["And so we will!”", "그럼 그렇게 하세!\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, turning so briskly that his copper seals swung extravagantly.", "캔틀 영감이 어찌나 잽싸게 몸을 돌렸는지 구리 인장들이 요란하게 흔들렸다."], ["“I’m as dry as a kex with biding up here in the wind, and I haven’t seen the colour of drink since nammet-time today.", "\"여기 바람 속에 죽치고 있었더니 마른 줄기처럼 바싹 말랐어, 오늘 점심나절 이후로 술 빛깔이라곤 구경도 못 했고."], ["’Tis said that the last brew at the Woman is very pretty drinking.", "듣자니 콰이엇 우먼의 마지막 양조주가 아주 맛난 술이라더군."], ["And, neighbours, if we should be a little late in the finishing, why, tomorrow’s Sunday, and we can sleep it off?”", "게다가, 이웃들, 마무리가 좀 늦어진들 어떤가, 뭐, 내일이 일요일이니 자고 풀면 되지?\""]], [["“Grandfer Cantle!", "\"캔틀 영감님!"], ["you take things very careless for an old man,”", "노인네치고 일을 너무 무심히 받아들이시네,\""], ["said the wide woman.", "펑퍼짐한 여자가 말했다."]], [["“I take things careless; I do—too careless to please the women!", "\"내가 일을 무심히 받아들이지, 그래—여자들 비위 맞추기엔 너무 무심하지!"], ["Klk!", "클크!"], ["I’ll sing the ‘Jovial Crew,’ or any other song, when a weak old man would cry his eyes out.", "난 약한 노인네 같으면 눈이 빠지게 울 판에도 '명랑한 패거리'든 다른 어떤 노래든 부를 거야."], ["Jown it; I am up for anything.", "젠장, 난 뭐든 할 준비가 됐다고."]], [["“The king′ look’d o′-ver his left′ shoul-der′, And a grim′ look look′-ed hee′, Earl Mar′-shal, he said′, but for′ my oath′ Or hang′-ed thou′ shouldst bee′.”", "\"임금님께서 왼쪽 어깨 너머로 돌아보시니, 그 눈초리가 험상궂게 보였네, 마셜 백작이여, 그가 말하길, 내 맹세만 아니었던들 그대는 교수형에 처해졌으리라.\""]], [["“Well, that’s what we’ll do,”", "\"음, 그럼 그렇게 하지,\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“We’ll give ’em a song, an’ it please the Lord.", "\"주님 뜻이라면 그들에게 노래를 한 곡 불러주세."], ["What’s the good of Thomasin’s cousin Clym a-coming home after the deed’s done?", "일이 다 끝난 마당에 토머신의 사촌 클림이 집에 온들 무슨 소용인가?"], ["He should have come afore, if so be he wanted to stop it, and marry her himself.”", "막고 싶었다면, 제가 그 색시랑 결혼할 셈이었다면, 진작 왔어야지.\""]], [["“Perhaps he’s coming to bide with his mother a little time, as she must feel lonely now the maid’s gone.”", "\"어쩌면 어머니 곁에 한동안 머물러 있으려는 걸지도 모르지, 색시가 떠났으니 이제 외로우실 테니.\""]], [["“Now, ’tis very odd, but I never feel lonely—no, not at all,”", "\"그런데, 참 묘하게도, 난 도무지 외롭다는 느낌을 모르겠어—그래, 전혀,\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle.", "캔틀 영감이 말했다."], ["“I am as brave in the nighttime as a’ admiral!”", "\"난 밤에도 제독만큼이나 용감하다네!\""]], [["The bonfire was by this time beginning to sink low, for the fuel had not been of that substantial sort which can support a blaze long.", "이맘때쯤 모닥불은 사그라들기 시작했으니, 땔감이 불길을 오래 지탱할 만큼 실한 종류가 아니었기 때문이다."], ["Most of the other fires within the wide horizon were also dwindling weak.", "드넓은 지평선 안의 다른 불들 대부분도 시들시들 약해지고 있었다."], ["Attentive observation of their brightness, colour, and length of existence would have revealed the quality of the material burnt, and through that, to some extent the natural produce of the district in which each bonfire was situate.", "그것들의 밝기와 빛깔과 지속 시간을 주의 깊게 살피면 태운 재료의 성질이, 그리고 그를 통해 저마다의 모닥불이 자리한 지역의 자연 산물이 어느 정도 드러났을 것이다."], ["The clear, kingly effulgence that had characterized the majority expressed a heath and furze country like their own, which in one direction extended an unlimited number of miles; the rapid flares and extinctions at other points of the compass showed the lightest of fuel—straw, beanstalks, and the usual waste from arable land.", "대다수의 특징이던 그 맑고 제왕다운 광채는 그들의 고장처럼 히스와 가시금작화의 고장을 나타냈으니, 그것은 한 방향으로 끝없이 여러 마일에 걸쳐 뻗어 있었다. 나침반의 다른 지점들에서 보이는 재빠른 화염과 소멸은 가장 가벼운 땔감을—짚과 콩대와 경작지에서 으레 나오는 부스러기를 보여주었다."], ["The most enduring of all—steady unaltering eyes like Planets—signified wood, such as hazel-branches, thorn-faggots, and stout billets.", "그 가운데 가장 오래가는 것은—행성처럼 한결같이 변함없는 눈은—개암나무 가지나 가시나무 다발이나 굵은 장작 같은 목재를 뜻했다."], ["Fires of the last-mentioned materials were rare, and though comparatively small in magnitude beside the transient blazes, now began to get the best of them by mere long continuance.", "마지막에 든 재료로 피운 불은 드물었으니, 덧없이 타오르던 불길 곁에서는 규모가 비교적 작았지만, 이제는 그저 오래 지속됨으로써 그것들을 능가하기 시작했다."], ["The great ones had perished, but these remained.", "거대한 불들은 스러졌으나, 이것들은 남았다."], ["They occupied the remotest visible positions—sky-backed summits rising out of rich coppice and plantation districts to the north, where the soil was different, and heath foreign and strange.", "그것들은 보이는 가장 먼 자리를—북쪽으로, 흙이 다르고 히스가 낯설고 생경한, 풍요로운 잡목림과 조림지 지대에서 하늘을 등지고 솟은 봉우리들을 차지하고 있었다."]], [["Save one; and this was the nearest of any, the moon of the whole shining throng.", "하나만 빼고. 그리고 이것은 모든 것 중 가장 가까웠으니, 빛나는 무리 전체의 달이었다."], ["It lay in a direction precisely opposite to that of the little window in the vale below.", "그것은 저 아래 골짜기의 그 작은 창과는 정확히 반대 방향에 놓여 있었다."], ["Its nearness was such that, notwithstanding its actual smallness, its glow infinitely transcended theirs.", "그 가까움이 어찌나 대단했던지, 실제로는 자그마한데도 그 빛이 다른 불들의 빛을 한없이 능가했다."]], [["This quiet eye had attracted attention from time to time; and when their own fire had become sunken and dim it attracted more; some even of the wood fires more recently lighted had reached their decline, but no change was perceptible here.", "이 고요한 눈은 이따금 주의를 끌었고, 그들 자신의 불이 사그라들어 어둑해지자 더욱 주의를 끌었으니, 더 최근에 지핀 목재 불들조차 더러 쇠퇴기에 접어들었건만, 여기서는 아무 변화도 감지되지 않았다."]], [["“To be sure, how near that fire is!”", "\"참말이지, 저 불이 어찌나 가까운지!\""], ["said Fairway. “Seemingly.", "페어웨이가 말했다."], ["I can see a fellow of some sort walking round it.", "\"보아하니 그래. 누군가 그 둘레를 도는 게 보여."], ["Little and good must be said of that fire, surely.”", "저 불을 두곤 작지만 좋다고 해야 마땅하지, 분명히.\""]], [["“I can throw a stone there,”", "\"제가 거기 돌멩이를 던질 수 있어요,\""], ["said the boy.", "소년이 말했다."]], [["“And so can I!”", "\"나도 던질 수 있지!\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle.", "캔틀 영감이 말했다."]], [["“No, no, you can’t, my sonnies.", "\"아니, 아니, 못 던져, 얘들아."], ["That fire is not much less than a mile off, for all that ’a seems so near.”", "저 불은 저렇게 가까워 보여도 거의 일 마일이나 떨어져 있어.\""]], [["“’Tis in the heath, but no furze,”", "\"히스에 난 불인데, 가시금작화는 아니구먼,\""], ["said the turf-cutter.", "뗏장 일꾼이 말했다."]], [["“’Tis cleft-wood, that’s what ’tis,”", "\"쪼갠 장작이야, 그게 바로 그거지,\""], ["said Timothy Fairway.", "티머시 페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“Nothing would burn like that except clean timber.", "\"깨끗한 목재 말곤 저렇게 타는 건 없어."], ["And ’tis on the knap afore the old captain’s house at Mistover.", "그리고 저건 미스토버의 노대위 집 앞 언덕마루에 있는 거야."], ["Such a queer mortal as that man is!", "그 양반은 어찌나 별난 인간인지!"], ["To have a little fire inside your own bank and ditch, that nobody else may enjoy it or come anigh it!", "제 둑과 도랑 안에 작은 불을 피워놓고는, 아무도 그걸 즐기거나 가까이 못 오게 한다니까!"], ["And what a zany an old chap must be, to light a bonfire when there’s no youngsters to please.”", "게다가 기쁘게 해줄 젊은것들도 없는데 모닥불을 피우다니, 늙은이가 얼마나 멍청한 거야.\""]], [["“Cap’n Vye has been for a long walk today, and is quite tired out,”", "\"바이 대위는 오늘 오래 산책을 다녀와서 아주 녹초가 됐어,\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, “so ’tisn’t likely to be he.”", "캔틀 영감이 말했다, \"그러니 그 양반일 리는 없겠지.\""]], [["“And he would hardly afford good fuel like that,”", "\"게다가 그 양반이 저런 좋은 땔감을 댈 리도 없고,\""], ["said the wide woman.", "펑퍼짐한 여자가 말했다."]], [["“Then it must be his granddaughter,”", "\"그럼 손녀가 한 게 분명하지,\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“Not that a body of her age can want a fire much.”", "\"그 나이 또래가 불을 그리 원할 일이야 없겠지만.\""]], [["“She is very strange in her ways, living up there by herself, and such things please her,”", "\"그 애는 행실이 아주 별나요, 저 위에서 혼자 살면서 그런 걸 좋아하지요,\""], ["said Susan.", "수전이 말했다."]], [["“She’s a well-favoured maid enough,”", "\"제법 잘생긴 처녀지,\""], ["said Humphrey the furze-cutter, “especially when she’s got one of her dandy gowns on.”", "가시금작화 베는 험프리가 말했다, \"특히 멋들어진 드레스 중 하날 걸쳤을 땐 말이야.\""]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"맞아,\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“Well, let her bonfire burn an’t will.", "\"음, 그 애 모닥불이야 타고 싶은 대로 타게 두지."], ["Ours is well-nigh out by the look o’t.”", "우리 건 보아하니 거의 다 꺼졌구먼.\""]], [["“How dark ’tis now the fire’s gone down!”", "\"불이 사그라드니 이제 어찌나 어두운지!\""], ["said Christian Cantle, looking behind him with his hare eyes.", "크리스천 캔틀이 토끼 같은 눈으로 뒤를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["“Don’t ye think we’d better get home-along, neighbours?", "\"이만 집으로 가는 게 낫지 않을까요, 이웃들?"], ["The heth isn’t haunted, I know; but we’d better get home....", "이 히스에 귀신이 안 나온다는 건 나도 알지만, 그래도 집에 가는 게 좋겠어요……"], ["Ah, what was that?”", "아, 저게 뭐야?\""]], [["“Only the wind,”", "\"그냥 바람이야,\""], ["said the turf-cutter.", "뗏장 일꾼이 말했다."]], [["“I don’t think Fifth-of-Novembers ought to be kept up by night except in towns.", "\"십일월 오일 행사는 읍내가 아닌 한 밤엔 안 지키는 게 좋아요."], ["It should be by day in outstep, ill-accounted places like this!”", "이런 외지고 후미진 데선 낮에 해야 한다고요!\""]], [["“Nonsense, Christian.", "\"허튼소리, 크리스천."], ["Lift up your spirits like a man!", "사내답게 기운 좀 차려!"], ["Susy, dear, you and I will have a jig—hey, my honey?—before ’tis quite too dark to see how well-favoured you be still, though so many summers have passed since your husband, a son of a witch, snapped you up from me.”", "수지, 자기, 자네하고 내가 한바탕 춤을 추세—어때, 내 꿀단지?—아직 자네가 얼마나 잘생겼는지 보이지 않을 만큼 깜깜해지기 전에 말이야, 마녀의 아들 같은 자네 남편이 내게서 자네를 낚아채간 뒤로 여러 여름이 지났어도 말이지.\""]], [["This was addressed to Susan Nunsuch; and the next circumstance of which the beholders were conscious was a vision of the matron’s broad form whisking off towards the space whereon the fire had been kindled.", "이 말은 수전 넌서치에게 건네진 것이었고, 지켜보던 이들이 다음으로 의식한 정황이란 그 아낙의 펑퍼짐한 형체가 불이 지펴졌던 자리를 향해 휙 끌려가는 광경이었다."], ["She was lifted bodily by Mr. Fairway’s arm, which had been flung round her waist before she had become aware of his intention.", "그녀는 페어웨이 씨의 팔에 통째로 들려갔으니, 그녀가 그 속셈을 알아채기도 전에 이미 그 팔이 그녀의 허리를 휘감았던 것이다."], ["The site of the fire was now merely a circle of ashes flecked with red embers and sparks, the furze having burnt completely away.", "불자리는 이제 가시금작화가 죄다 타버려, 붉은 잉걸불과 불티가 점점이 박힌 잿더미의 동그라미일 뿐이었다."], ["Once within the circle he whirled her round and round in a dance.", "동그라미 안에 들어서자 그는 그녀를 빙글빙글 돌리며 춤을 추었다."], ["She was a woman noisily constructed; in addition to her enclosing framework of whalebone and lath, she wore pattens summer and winter, in wet weather and in dry, to preserve her boots from wear; and when Fairway began to jump about with her, the clicking of the pattens, the creaking of the stays, and her screams of surprise, formed a very audible concert.", "그녀는 요란하게 만들어진 여자였으니, 고래뼈와 나무오리로 둘러친 골조에 더해, 여름이고 겨울이고 비가 오나 마나 장화가 닳는 걸 막으려고 나막신을 신고 다녔던 터라, 페어웨이가 그녀와 함께 펄쩍거리기 시작하자 나막신 딸깍대는 소리와 코르셋 삐걱대는 소리와 그녀의 놀란 비명 소리가 자못 똑똑히 들리는 합주를 이루었다."]], [["“I’ll crack thy numskull for thee, you mandy chap!” said Mrs.", "\"네 멍청한 대가리를 쪼개버릴 테다, 이 건방진 놈아!\""], ["Nunsuch, as she helplessly danced round with him, her feet playing like drumsticks among the sparks.", "넌서치 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 그와 함께 어쩔 수 없이 빙글빙글 돌며 춤을 추었고, 발은 불티 사이에서 북채처럼 놀았다."], ["“My ankles were all in a fever before, from walking through that prickly furze, and now you must make ’em worse with these vlankers!”", "\"가시 돋친 가시금작화를 헤치며 걷느라 발목이 가뜩이나 후끈거렸는데, 이제 이 불티로 더 못살게 굴려고 그래요!\""]], [["The vagary of Timothy Fairway was infectious.", "티머시 페어웨이의 변덕은 전염성이 있었다."], ["The turf-cutter seized old Olly Dowden, and, somewhat more gently, poussetted with her likewise.", "잔디 베는 일꾼이 늙은 올리 다우든을 붙잡고는 좀 더 부드럽게, 그녀와 마찬가지로 푸세트를 추었다."], ["The young men were not slow to imitate the example of their elders, and seized the maids; Grandfer Cantle and his stick jigged in the form of a three-legged object among the rest; and in half a minute all that could be seen on Rainbarrow was a whirling of dark shapes amid a boiling confusion of sparks, which leapt around the dancers as high as their waists.", "젊은 사내들도 어른들의 본을 따르는 데 굼뜨지 않아 처녀들을 붙들었으며, 캔틀 영감은 지팡이와 함께 다리 셋 달린 물체 모양으로 나머지 무리 사이에서 깡충거렸고, 채 반 분도 지나지 않아 레인배로 위에 보이는 것이라곤 들끓듯 어지러운 불티 한가운데서 빙빙 도는 검은 형상들뿐이었으니, 불티는 춤추는 이들의 허리께까지 솟구쳐 그들 주위로 뛰어올랐다."], ["The chief noises were women’s shrill cries, men’s laughter, Susan’s stays and pattens, Olly Dowden’s “heu-heu-heu!” and the strumming of the wind upon the furze-bushes, which formed a kind of tune to the demoniac measure they trod.", "가장 큰 소리는 여인들의 날카로운 비명, 사내들의 웃음, 수전의 코르셋과 나막신 소리, 올리 다우든의 \"헤헤헤!\", 그리고 가시금작화 덤불 위를 두드리는 바람의 가락이었으니, 그것은 그들이 밟는 악마적인 박자에 일종의 곡조를 이루었다."], ["Christian alone stood aloof, uneasily rocking himself as he murmured, “They ought not to do it—how the vlankers do fly!", "오직 크리스천만이 떨어져 서서, 불안하게 몸을 흔들며 중얼거리길, \"저러면 안 되는데—저 불티가 어찌나 날아다니는지!"], ["’tis tempting the Wicked one, ’tis.”", "악마를 부르는 짓이야, 저게.\""]], [["“What was that?”", "\"저게 뭐였지?\""], ["said one of the lads, stopping.", "청년들 중 하나가 멈춰 서며 말했다."]], [["“Ah—where?”", "\"어—어디?\""], ["said Christian, hastily closing up to the rest.", "크리스천이 황급히 나머지 무리 곁으로 바싹 붙으며 말했다."]], [["The dancers all lessened their speed.", "춤추던 이들이 모두 속도를 늦췄다."]], [["“’Twas behind you, Christian, that I heard it—down here.”", "\"네 뒤에서 들렸어, 크리스천—여기 아래에서.\""]], [["“Yes—’tis behind me!”", "\"그래—내 뒤야!\""], ["Christian said.", "크리스천이 말했다."], ["“Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John, bless the bed that I lie on; four angels guard—”", "\"마태, 마가, 누가, 요한, 내가 누운 침상에 복을 내리소서, 네 천사가 지키사—\""]], [["“Hold your tongue.", "\"입 좀 다물어."], ["What is it?”", "저게 뭐야?\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["“Hoi-i-i-i!”", "\"호이이이이!\""], ["cried a voice from the darkness.", "어둠 속에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["“Halloo-o-o-o!”", "\"할로오오오!\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 응했다."]], [["“Is there any cart track up across here to Mis’ess Yeobright’s, of Blooms-End?”", "\"이 위로 블룸스엔드의 요브라이트 부인 댁으로 가는 수레길이 있소?\""], ["came to them in the same voice, as a long, slim indistinct figure approached the barrow.", "똑같은 목소리가 그들에게 들려왔으니, 길쭉하고 호리호리한 흐릿한 형체가 봉분 쪽으로 다가왔다."]], [["“Ought we not to run home as hard as we can, neighbours, as ’tis getting late?”", "\"이웃들, 시간도 늦었으니 우리 집까지 힘껏 달려가야 하지 않을까?\""], ["said Christian.", "크리스천이 말했다."], ["“Not run away from one another, you know; run close together, I mean.”", "\"서로한테서 달아나자는 게 아니라, 알다시피, 바싹 붙어서 같이 달리자는 거야.\""]], [["“Scrape up a few stray locks of furze, and make a blaze, so that we can see who the man is,”", "\"흩어진 가시금작화나 몇 줌 긁어모아 불을 피워서 저자가 누군지 보자고.\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["When the flame arose it revealed a young man in tight raiment, and red from top to toe.", "불길이 일어나자 그것은 몸에 딱 붙는 옷을 입고 머리끝부터 발끝까지 붉은 젊은이를 드러냈다."], ["“Is there a track across here to Mis’ess Yeobright’s house?”", "\"이 위로 요브라이트 부인 댁으로 가는 길이 있소?\""], ["he repeated.", "그가 되물었다."]], [["“Ay—keep along the path down there.”", "\"있지—저 아래 길을 따라 쭉 가시오.\""]], [["“I mean a way two horses and a van can travel over?”", "\"말 두 마리와 짐마차가 지나갈 길 말이오?\""]], [["“Well, yes; you can get up the vale below here with time.", "\"음, 그래요, 시간을 들이면 여기 아래 골짜기로 올라갈 수 있소."], ["The track is rough, but if you’ve got a light your horses may pick along wi’ care.", "길은 험하지만 등불이 있으면 말들이 조심조심 더듬어 갈 수 있을 거요."], ["Have ye brought your cart far up, neighbour reddleman?”", "수레는 멀리까지 끌고 왔소, 레들맨* 양반?\""]], [["“I’ve left it in the bottom, about half a mile back, I stepped on in front to make sure of the way, as ’tis night-time, and I han’t been here for so long.”", "\"바닥에 두고 왔소, 한 반 마일쯤 뒤에. 밤인 데다 오랫동안 와 본 적이 없어서 길을 확인하려고 앞서 걸어왔소.\""]], [["“Oh, well you can get up,” said Fairway.", "\"오, 음, 올라갈 수 있을 거요.\""], ["“What a turn it did give me when I saw him!”", "페어웨이가 말했다. \"저자를 봤을 때 어찌나 놀랐던지!\""], ["he added to the whole group, the reddleman included.", "그가 레들맨까지 포함한 무리 전체에게 덧붙였다."], ["“Lord’s sake, I thought, whatever fiery mommet is this come to trouble us?", "\"주여, 도대체 이 무슨 불 같은 허수아비가 우리를 괴롭히려고 왔나 했지."], ["No slight to your looks, reddleman, for ye bain’t bad-looking in the groundwork, though the finish is queer.", "당신 생김새를 깎아내리려는 건 아니오, 레들맨, 바탕은 못나지 않았으니까, 마무리가 좀 괴상하긴 해도."], ["My meaning is just to say how curious I felt.", "그저 내가 얼마나 야릇한 기분이 들었는지 말하려는 거요."], ["I half thought it ’twas the devil or the red ghost the boy told of.”", "악마이거나 아니면 저 아이가 말하던 붉은 유령이 아닌가 반쯤 생각했소.\""]], [["“It gied me a turn likewise,”", "\"나도 놀랐어요.\""], ["said Susan Nunsuch, “for I had a dream last night of a death’s head.”", "수전 넌석이 말했다. \"어젯밤에 해골 꿈을 꿨거든요.\""]], [["“Don’t ye talk o’t no more,”", "\"그 얘긴 더 하지 마.\""], ["said Christian.", "크리스천이 말했다."], ["“If he had a handkerchief over his head he’d look for all the world like the Devil in the picture of the Temptation.”", "\"머리에 손수건을 둘렀다면 영락없이 「유혹」 그림 속 악마처럼 보일 거야.\""]], [["“Well, thank you for telling me,”", "\"음, 말해 줘서 고맙소.\""], ["said the young reddleman, smiling faintly.", "젊은 레들맨이 희미하게 웃으며 말했다."], ["“And good night t’ye all.”", "\"그럼 다들 안녕히.\""]], [["He withdrew from their sight down the barrow.", "그는 봉분 아래로 내려가 그들의 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["“I fancy I’ve seen that young man’s face before,”", "\"저 젊은이 얼굴을 전에 본 적이 있는 것 같은데.\""], ["said Humphrey.", "험프리가 말했다."], ["“But where, or how, or what his name is, I don’t know.”", "\"하지만 어디서, 어떻게, 이름이 뭔지는 모르겠어.\""]], [["The reddleman had not been gone more than a few minutes when another person approached the partially revived bonfire.", "레들맨이 떠난 지 몇 분도 채 지나지 않아 또 다른 사람이 어느 정도 되살아난 모닥불 쪽으로 다가왔다."], ["It proved to be a well-known and respected widow of the neighbourhood, of a standing which can only be expressed by the word genteel.", "그 사람은 이 인근에서 잘 알려지고 존경받는 과부로 드러났으니, 그 지위는 오직 '품위 있는'이라는 말로밖에는 표현할 수 없는 것이었다."], ["Her face, encompassed by the blackness of the receding heath, showed whitely, and without half-lights, like a cameo.", "그 얼굴은 물러나는 황야의 어둠에 둘러싸여, 반쯤의 그늘도 없이 카메오처럼 하얗게 떠올랐다."]], [["She was a woman of middle-age, with well-formed features of the type usually found where perspicacity is the chief quality enthroned within.", "그녀는 중년의 여인으로, 통찰력이 안에 군림하는 으뜸가는 자질일 때 흔히 보이는 유형의 단정한 이목구비를 지녔다."], ["At moments she seemed to be regarding issues from a Nebo denied to others around.", "이따금 그녀는 주위의 다른 이들에게는 허락되지 않은 느보산에서 사안들을 굽어보는 듯했다."], ["She had something of an estranged mien; the solitude exhaled from the heath was concentrated in this face that had risen from it.", "그녀에게는 어딘가 동떨어진 기색이 있었으니, 황야에서 뿜어 나오는 고독이 그 황야로부터 솟아오른 이 얼굴 안에 응축되어 있었다."], ["The air with which she looked at the heathmen betokened a certain unconcern at their presence, or at what might be their opinions of her for walking in that lonely spot at such an hour, thus indirectly implying that in some respect or other they were not up to her level.", "그녀가 황야 사람들을 바라보는 태도는 그들의 존재에 대한, 또는 이런 시각에 그토록 외진 곳을 거니는 자신에 대한 그들의 의견이 무엇이든 그것에 대한 모종의 무관심을 드러냈으니, 그리하여 그녀가 어떤 면에서든 그들의 수준에 못 미친다는 것을 간접적으로 시사했다."], ["The explanation lay in the fact that though her husband had been a small farmer she herself was a curate’s daughter, who had once dreamt of doing better things.", "그 까닭은 비록 그녀의 남편은 영세한 농부였으나 그녀 자신은 보좌신부의 딸로서 한때 더 나은 것을 이루기를 꿈꾸었던 사실에 있었다."]], [["Persons with any weight of character carry, like planets, their atmospheres along with them in their orbits; and the matron who entered now upon the scene could, and usually did, bring her own tone into a company.", "성품에 무게가 있는 사람은 행성처럼 자신의 궤도를 따라 제 대기를 함께 끌고 다니거니와, 이제 무대에 등장한 이 부인은 자신의 분위기를 좌중에 들여올 수 있었고, 또 으레 그렇게 했다."], ["Her normal manner among the heathfolk had that reticence which results from the consciousness of superior communicative power.", "황야 사람들 사이에서 그녀가 보이는 평소의 태도에는 우월한 화술의 자각에서 비롯되는 그 과묵함이 있었다."], ["But the effect of coming into society and light after lonely wandering in darkness is a sociability in the comer above its usual pitch, expressed in the features even more than in words.", "그러나 어둠 속을 외로이 헤맨 끝에 사람들과 빛 속으로 들어왔을 때의 효과는, 들어온 이에게 평소보다 한층 높아진 사교성으로 나타나, 말보다도 오히려 표정에 더욱 드러났다."]], [["“Why, ’tis Mis’ess Yeobright,”", "\"아니, 요브라이트 부인이시군.\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“Mis’ess Yeobright, not ten minutes ago a man was here asking for you—a reddleman.”", "\"요브라이트 부인, 십 분도 채 안 됐는데 한 사내가 여기 와서 부인을 찾았소—레들맨이오.\""]], [["“What did he want?”", "\"그자가 뭘 원했나요?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“He didn’t tell us.”", "\"우리한테는 말 안 했소.\""]], [["“Something to sell, I suppose; what it can be I am at a loss to understand.”", "\"뭔가 팔 게 있나 보지요. 그게 뭘 수 있을지는 도무지 모르겠네요.\""]], [["“I am glad to hear that your son Mr. Clym is coming home at Christmas, ma’am,”", "\"댁의 아드님 클림 도련님이 크리스마스에 집에 온다니 반갑습니다, 부인.\""], ["said Sam, the turf-cutter.", "잔디 베는 일꾼 샘이 말했다."], ["“What a dog he used to be for bonfires!”", "\"모닥불이라면 사족을 못 쓰던 녀석이었지요!\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래요."], ["I believe he is coming,”", "오는 모양이에요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“He must be a fine fellow by this time,”", "\"지금쯤이면 훌륭한 청년이 됐겠지요.\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["“He is a man now,”", "\"이제 어른이지요.\""], ["she replied quietly.", "그녀가 조용히 대답했다."]], [["“’Tis very lonesome for ’ee in the heth tonight, mis’ess,”", "\"오늘 밤 황야에서 부인 혼자라니 무척 적적하시겠소, 부인.\""], ["said Christian, coming from the seclusion he had hitherto maintained.", "크리스천이 그때껏 지키던 은둔에서 나오며 말했다."], ["“Mind you don’t get lost.", "\"길 잃지 않게 조심하시오."], ["Egdon Heth is a bad place to get lost in, and the winds do huffle queerer tonight than ever I heard ’em afore.", "에그던 황야는 길 잃기 나쁜 곳이고, 오늘 밤 바람은 내가 여태 들어 본 어느 때보다 괴상하게 윙윙거리오."], ["Them that know Egdon best have been pixy-led here at times.”", "에그던을 가장 잘 아는 이들도 더러 여기서 도깨비에 홀려 길을 헤맸소.\""]], [["“Is that you, Christian?”", "\"자네 크리스천인가?\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "요브라이트 부인이 말했다."], ["“What made you hide away from me?”", "\"왜 내게서 숨어 있었나?\""]], [["“’Twas that I didn’t know you in this light, mis’ess; and being a man of the mournfullest make, I was scared a little, that’s all.", "\"이 불빛에 부인인 줄 몰라서요, 부인. 게다가 워낙 우울하게 생겨먹은 사람이라 조금 겁이 났을 뿐입니다."], ["Oftentimes if you could see how terrible down I get in my mind, ’twould make ’ee quite nervous for fear I should die by my hand.”", "제가 마음속으로 얼마나 끔찍하게 가라앉는지 보신다면, 제가 제 손으로 죽지나 않을까 부인도 무척 마음 졸이실 겁니다.\""]], [["“You don’t take after your father,”", "\"자네는 아비를 닮지 않았군.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright, looking towards the fire, where Grandfer Cantle, with some want of originality, was dancing by himself among the sparks, as the others had done before.", "요브라이트 부인이 불 쪽을 바라보며 말했으니, 그곳에서는 캔틀 영감이 독창성이라곤 없이 앞서 다른 이들이 그랬듯 불티 사이에서 혼자 춤추고 있었다."]], [["“Now, Grandfer,”", "\"이봐요, 영감,\""], ["said Timothy Fairway, “we are ashamed of ye.", "티머시 페어웨이가 말했다. \"우리가 다 부끄럽소."], ["A reverent old patriarch man as you be—seventy if a day—to go hornpiping like that by yourself!”", "영감처럼 점잖은 늙은 가장이—하루를 더 잡아도 일흔인 양반이—혼자서 그렇게 혼파이프나 추다니!\""]], [["“A harrowing old man, Mis’ess Yeobright,”", "\"진을 빼는 영감이지요, 요브라이트 부인.\""], ["said Christian despondingly.", "크리스천이 풀 죽어 말했다."], ["“I wouldn’t live with him a week, so playward as he is, if I could get away.”", "\"도망칠 수만 있다면 저렇게 장난기 많은 양반하고는 일주일도 같이 못 살겠어요.\""]], [["“’Twould be more seemly in ye to stand still and welcome Mis’ess Yeobright, and you the venerablest here, Grandfer Cantle,”", "\"가만히 서서 요브라이트 부인을 맞이하는 게 더 점잖은 처신이지요, 게다가 여기서 제일 어른이신 캔틀 영감 아니세요.\""], ["said the besom-woman.", "빗자루 만드는 여인이 말했다."]], [["“Faith, and so it would,”", "\"암, 그렇고말고.\""], ["said the reveller checking himself repentantly.", "흥에 겨운 영감이 뉘우치며 자제하면서 말했다."], ["“I’ve such a bad memory, Mis’ess Yeobright, that I forget how I’m looked up to by the rest of ’em.", "\"내가 워낙 기억력이 나빠서요, 요브라이트 부인, 나머지 사람들이 나를 얼마나 우러러보는지 깜빡한다오."], ["My spirits must be wonderful good, you’ll say?", "내 기운이 굉장히 좋다고 하실 테지요?"], ["But not always.", "하지만 늘 그런 건 아니오."], ["’Tis a weight upon a man to be looked up to as commander, and I often feel it.”", "우두머리로 우러름받는다는 건 사내에게 짐이 되는 거라, 나도 종종 그걸 느낀다오.\""]], [["“I am sorry to stop the talk,”", "\"이야기를 끊어서 미안하네요.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "요브라이트 부인이 말했다."], ["“But I must be leaving you now.", "\"하지만 이제 가 봐야겠어요."], ["I was passing down the Anglebury Road, towards my niece’s new home, who is returning tonight with her husband; and seeing the bonfire and hearing Olly’s voice among the rest I came up here to learn what was going on.", "앵글베리 큰길을 따라 내려가던 참이었는데, 오늘 밤 남편과 함께 돌아오는 조카딸의 새집으로 가는 길이었지요. 모닥불이 보이고 다른 이들 틈에서 올리의 목소리가 들리기에 무슨 일인가 알아보려고 여기 올라왔어요."], ["I should like her to walk with me, as her way is mine.”", "조카가 가는 길이 내 길과 같으니 같이 걷고 싶어서요.\""]], [["“Ay, sure, ma’am, I’m just thinking of moving,”", "\"네, 그럼요, 부인, 저도 막 움직일 참이었어요.\""], ["said Olly.", "올리가 말했다."]], [["“Why, you’ll be safe to meet the reddleman that I told ye of,”", "\"아니, 부인은 내가 말한 그 레들맨을 틀림없이 만나겠소.\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“He’s only gone back to get his van.", "\"수레를 가지러 막 돌아갔을 뿐이오."], ["We heard that your niece and her husband were coming straight home as soon as they were married, and we are going down there shortly, to give ’em a song o’ welcome.”", "부인의 조카딸과 그 남편이 혼인하는 대로 곧장 집으로 온다는 얘길 들었는데, 우리도 곧 그리로 내려가서 환영의 노래를 한 곡 불러 줄 참이오.\""]], [["“Thank you indeed,”", "\"정말 고마워요.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "요브라이트 부인이 말했다."]], [["“But we shall take a shorter cut through the furze than you can go with long clothes; so we won’t trouble you to wait.”", "\"하지만 우리는 부인이 긴 옷자락으로 가는 것보다 가시금작화 사이로 지름길을 질러갈 테니, 기다리시게 폐를 끼치진 않겠소.\""]], [["“Very well—are you ready, Olly?”", "\"좋아요—준비됐나, 올리?\""]], [["“Yes, ma’am.", "\"네, 부인."], ["And there’s a light shining from your niece’s window, see.", "그리고 봐요, 댁의 조카딸 창문에서 불빛이 비치네요."], ["It will help to keep us in the path.”", "우리가 길을 잃지 않게 도와줄 거예요.\""]], [["She indicated the faint light at the bottom of the valley which Fairway had pointed out; and the two women descended the tumulus.", "그녀는 페어웨이가 가리켰던, 골짜기 바닥의 희미한 불빛을 가리켰고, 두 여인은 봉분을 내려갔다."]], [["IV. The Halt on the Turnpike Road\nDown, downward they went, and yet further down—their descent at each step seeming to outmeasure their advance.", "아래로, 아래로 그들은 내려갔고, 더욱 아래로 내려갔으니—한 걸음 한 걸음 내려갈 때마다 하강이 전진보다 더 큰 듯했다."], ["Their skirts were scratched noisily by the furze, their shoulders brushed by the ferns, which, though dead and dry, stood erect as when alive, no sufficient winter weather having as yet arrived to beat them down.", "그들의 치맛자락은 가시금작화에 요란하게 긁혔고, 어깨는 고사리에 스쳤으니, 고사리는 죽어 말랐어도 살아 있을 때처럼 꼿꼿이 서 있었던바, 아직 그것들을 쓰러뜨릴 만한 겨울 날씨가 충분히 닥치지 않은 까닭이었다."], ["Their Tartarean situation might by some have been called an imprudent one for two unattended women.", "어떤 이들은 시중드는 이 없는 두 여인에게 이 지옥 같은 처지가 무모한 것이라 했을 법도 하다."], ["But these shaggy recesses were at all seasons a familiar surrounding to Olly and Mrs. Yeobright; and the addition of darkness lends no frightfulness to the face of a friend.", "그러나 이 텁수룩한 후미진 곳은 사철 올리와 요브라이트 부인에게 익숙한 둘레였고, 어둠이 더해진들 벗의 얼굴에 무서움을 보태지는 않는 법이다."]], [["“And so Tamsin has married him at last,”", "\"그래서 태신이 결국 그자와 결혼했네요.\""], ["said Olly, when the incline had become so much less steep that their foot-steps no longer required undivided attention.", "비탈이 한결 완만해져 발걸음에 온 신경을 쏟지 않아도 되자 올리가 말했다."]], [["Mrs.", "요브라이트 부인이 천천히 대답했다."], ["Yeobright answered slowly, “Yes; at last.”", "\"그래, 결국.\""]], [["“How you will miss her—living with ’ee as a daughter, as she always have.”", "\"얼마나 허전하시겠어요—늘 그래 왔듯 딸처럼 함께 살았으니.\""]], [["“I do miss her.”", "\"허전하지.\""]], [["Olly, though without the tact to perceive when remarks were untimely, was saved by her very simplicity from rendering them offensive.", "올리는 발언이 때에 맞지 않을 때를 알아챌 만한 눈치는 없었으나, 바로 그 순박함 덕분에 그것이 불쾌하게 들리는 일은 면했다."], ["Questions that would have been resented in others she could ask with impunity.", "다른 이들에게서라면 노여움을 살 만한 물음도 그녀는 탈 없이 던질 수 있었다."], ["This accounted for Mrs. Yeobright’s acquiescence in the revival of an evidently sore subject.", "명백히 아픈 화제를 다시 들추는데도 요브라이트 부인이 묵인한 것은 그 때문이었다."]], [["“I was quite strook to hear you’d agreed to it, ma’am, that I was,”", "\"부인이 거기 동의하셨단 얘길 듣고 정말 깜짝 놀랐어요, 부인, 정말이지.\""], ["continued the besom-maker.", "빗자루 만드는 여인이 이어 말했다."]], [["“You were not more struck by it than I should have been last year this time, Olly.", "\"작년 이맘때였다면 나도 자네 못지않게 놀랐을 거야, 올리."], ["There are a good many sides to that wedding.", "그 결혼에는 여러 면이 있어."], ["I could not tell you all of them, even if I tried.”", "다 말하려 해도 자네한테 전부 말할 순 없네.\""]], [["“I felt myself that he was hardly solid-going enough to mate with your family.", "\"저도 그자가 부인 집안과 짝이 되기엔 어딘가 미덥지 못하다 싶었어요."], ["Keeping an inn—what is it?", "여관을 친다는 게—그게 뭐예요?"], ["But ’a’s clever, that’s true, and they say he was an engineering gentleman once, but has come down by being too outwardly given.”", "하지만 영리한 건 사실이고, 한때는 공학 신사였다는데 너무 겉치레에 빠져서 영락했다더군요.\""]], [["“I saw that, upon the whole, it would be better she should marry where she wished.”", "\"전체로 보아, 그 애가 바라는 곳으로 시집가는 게 낫겠다고 봤지.\""]], [["“Poor little thing, her feelings got the better of her, no doubt.", "\"가엾은 것, 분명 감정에 휘둘렸겠지요. 그게 인지상정이에요."], ["’Tis nature. Well, they may call him what they will—he’ve several acres of heth-ground broke up here, besides the public house, and the heth-croppers, and his manners be quite like a gentleman’s.", "음, 사람들이 그자를 뭐라 부르든—여기 황야 땅 몇 에이커를 일궈 놓은 데다 여관도 있고, 황야 망아지들도 있고, 처신도 영락없는 신사 같던걸요."], ["And what’s done cannot be undone.”", "그리고 이미 한 일은 돌이킬 수 없는 법이지요.\""]], [["“It cannot,”", "\"돌이킬 수 없지.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "요브라이트 부인이 말했다."], ["“See, here’s the wagon-track at last.", "\"봐, 드디어 수레길이네."], ["Now we shall get along better.”", "이제 좀 수월히 가겠어.\""]], [["The wedding subject was no further dwelt upon; and soon a faint diverging path was reached, where they parted company, Olly first begging her companion to remind Mr. Wildeve that he had not sent her sick husband the bottle of wine promised on the occasion of his marriage.", "결혼 화제는 더 이상 거론되지 않았고, 이내 희미하게 갈라지는 샛길에 이르러 그들은 헤어졌으니, 올리는 먼저 자기 동행에게, 와일디브가 혼인을 기념해 약속한 포도주 한 병을 자기 병든 남편에게 보내지 않았다는 걸 일러 달라고 청했다."], ["The besom-maker turned to the left towards her own house, behind a spur of the hill, and Mrs. Yeobright followed the straight track, which further on joined the highway by the Quiet Woman Inn, whither she supposed her niece to have returned with Wildeve from their wedding at Anglebury that day.", "빗자루 만드는 여인은 언덕 한 자락 뒤편 자기 집을 향해 왼쪽으로 꺾었고, 요브라이트 부인은 곧은 길을 따라갔으니, 그 길은 더 가서 콰이어트 우먼 여관 곁의 큰길과 합쳐졌는바, 그녀의 조카딸이 그날 앵글베리에서의 혼례를 마치고 와일디브와 함께 그리로 돌아왔으리라 짐작한 곳이었다."]], [["She first reached Wildeve’s Patch, as it was called, a plot of land redeemed from the heath, and after long and laborious years brought into cultivation.", "그녀는 먼저, 흔히 일컫는 대로 와일디브의 땅뙈기에 이르렀으니, 황야에서 일궈 낸 한 뙈기로, 길고 고된 세월 끝에 경작지로 만들어진 곳이었다."], ["The man who had discovered that it could be tilled died of the labour; the man who succeeded him in possession ruined himself in fertilizing it.", "그것이 갈아 일굴 수 있음을 발견한 사내는 그 노동으로 죽었고, 그 뒤를 이어 그 땅을 소유한 사내는 거기 거름을 대다가 망했다."], ["Wildeve came like Amerigo Vespucci, and received the honours due to those who had gone before.", "와일디브는 아메리고 베스푸치처럼 와서, 앞서 간 이들에게 돌아갔어야 할 영예를 받았다."]], [["When Mrs. Yeobright had drawn near to the inn, and was about to enter, she saw a horse and vehicle some two hundred yards beyond it, coming towards her, a man walking alongside with a lantern in his hand.", "요브라이트 부인이 여관에 가까이 다가가 막 들어서려 할 때, 그녀는 여관에서 이백 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에서 말과 수레가 자기 쪽으로 다가오고, 한 사내가 손에 등불을 들고 그 곁을 걷는 것을 보았다."], ["It was soon evident that this was the reddleman who had inquired for her.", "이내 그 사내가 자기를 찾던 그 레들맨임이 분명해졌다."], ["Instead of entering the inn at once, she walked by it and towards the van.", "그녀는 여관으로 곧장 들어가는 대신 그것을 지나쳐 수레 쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["The conveyance came close, and the man was about to pass her with little notice, when she turned to him and said, “I think you have been inquiring for me?", "수레가 가까이 다가왔고, 사내가 그녀를 거의 알아보지 못한 채 지나치려는데, 그녀가 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"나를 찾으셨다지요?"], ["I am Mrs. Yeobright of Blooms-End.”", "나는 블룸스엔드의 요브라이트 부인이에요.\""]], [["The reddleman started, and held up his finger.", "레들맨이 흠칫하더니 손가락을 들어 보였다."], ["He stopped the horses, and beckoned to her to withdraw with him a few yards aside, which she did, wondering.", "그는 말을 세우고 그녀에게 몇 야드 옆으로 함께 물러나자고 손짓했으며, 그녀는 의아해하면서도 그렇게 했다."]], [["“You don’t know me, ma’am, I suppose?”", "\"저를 모르시겠지요, 부인?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I do not,”", "\"몰라요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Why, yes, I do!", "\"아니, 알겠어요!"], ["You are young Venn—your father was a dairyman somewhere here?”", "젊은 베너로군요—자네 아버지가 여기 어디서 낙농을 했지요?\""]], [["“Yes; and I knew your niece, Miss Tamsin, a little.", "\"네, 그리고 댁의 조카딸 태신 아가씨도 좀 알았습니다."], ["I have something bad to tell you.”", "안 좋은 소식을 전해 드려야겠습니다.\""]], [["“About her—no!", "\"그 애에 대해—설마!"], ["She has just come home, I believe, with her husband.", "그 애는 방금 남편과 함께 집에 온 줄 아는데."], ["They arranged to return this afternoon—to the inn beyond here.”", "오늘 오후에 돌아오기로 했어요—여기 너머 여관으로.\""]], [["“She’s not there.”", "\"거기 없습니다.\""]], [["“How do you know?”", "\"어떻게 아나요?\""]], [["“Because she’s here.", "\"여기 있으니까요."], ["She’s in my van,”", "제 수레 안에 있습니다.\""], ["he added slowly.", "그가 천천히 덧붙였다."]], [["“What new trouble has come?”", "\"무슨 새 화가 닥쳤단 말인가?\""], ["murmured Mrs. Yeobright, putting her hand over her eyes.", "요브라이트 부인이 손으로 눈을 가리며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“I can’t explain much, ma’am.", "\"자세히는 설명 못 드립니다, 부인."], ["All I know is that, as I was going along the road this morning, about a mile out of Anglebury, I heard something trotting after me like a doe, and looking round there she was, white as death itself.", "제가 아는 건 그저, 오늘 아침 길을 따라가는데, 앵글베리에서 한 일 마일쯤 벗어났을 때, 뭔가 암사슴처럼 제 뒤를 종종걸음으로 따라오는 소리가 들려서 돌아보니 그 아가씨가 거기 있었습니다, 죽음 그 자체처럼 새하얗게요."], ["‘Oh, Diggory Venn!’ she said, ‘I thought ’twas you—will you help me?", "'오, 디고리 베너!' 하더니, '당신인 줄 알았어요—저를 도와주시겠어요?"], ["I am in trouble.’”", "곤경에 처했어요.' 하더군요.\""]], [["“How did she know your Christian name?”", "\"그 애가 자네 세례명을 어떻게 알았지?\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright doubtingly.", "요브라이트 부인이 미심쩍어하며 말했다."]], [["“I had met her as a lad before I went away in this trade.", "\"이 장사를 하러 떠나기 전, 소년 시절에 그 아가씨를 만난 적이 있습니다."], ["She asked then if she might ride, and then down she fell in a faint.", "그때 태워 줄 수 있겠느냐 묻더니, 그러고는 까무러쳐 쓰러졌습니다."], ["I picked her up and put her in, and there she has been ever since.", "제가 안아 올려 안에 태웠고, 그때부터 줄곧 거기 있었습니다."], ["She has cried a good deal, but she has hardly spoke; all she has told me being that she was to have been married this morning.", "많이 울었지만 말은 거의 안 했고, 한 얘기라곤 오늘 아침 결혼하기로 되어 있었다는 것뿐이었습니다."], ["I tried to get her to eat something, but she couldn’t; and at last she fell asleep.”", "뭐라도 먹이려 했지만 못 먹더니, 끝내 잠이 들었습니다.\""]], [["“Let me see her at once,”", "\"당장 그 애를 봐야겠어요.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright, hastening towards the van.", "요브라이트 부인이 수레 쪽으로 서둘러 가며 말했다."]], [["The reddleman followed with the lantern, and, stepping up first, assisted Mrs. Yeobright to mount beside him.", "레들맨이 등불을 들고 뒤따랐고, 먼저 올라서서 요브라이트 부인이 자기 곁으로 오르도록 거들었다."], ["On the door being opened she perceived at the end of the van an extemporized couch, around which was hung apparently all the drapery that the reddleman possessed, to keep the occupant of the little couch from contact with the red materials of his trade.", "문이 열리자 그녀는 수레 끝에 임시로 마련한 침상을 보았으니, 그 둘레에는 레들맨이 가진 천이란 천은 다 걸어 놓은 듯했는데, 그 작은 침상에 누운 이가 그의 장사 밑천인 붉은 재료에 닿지 않게 하려는 것이었다."], ["A young girl lay thereon, covered with a cloak.", "한 젊은 처녀가 거기 외투를 덮고 누워 있었다."], ["She was asleep, and the light of the lantern fell upon her features.", "그녀는 잠들어 있었고, 등불의 빛이 그 이목구비에 떨어졌다."]], [["A fair, sweet, and honest country face was revealed, reposing in a nest of wavy chestnut hair.", "물결치는 밤색 머리칼의 둥지 속에 누인, 곱고 사랑스럽고 정직한 시골 얼굴이 드러났다."], ["It was between pretty and beautiful.", "그것은 예쁨과 아름다움 사이쯤이었다."], ["Though her eyes were closed, one could easily imagine the light necessarily shining in them as the culmination of the luminous workmanship around.", "두 눈은 감겨 있었으나, 그 둘레의 빛나는 솜씨가 절정에 이른 양 그 눈에 응당 빛이 어렸으리라는 것은 쉬이 상상할 수 있었다."], ["The groundwork of the face was hopefulness; but over it now lay like a foreign substance a film of anxiety and grief.", "그 얼굴의 바탕은 희망이었으나, 이제 그 위에는 마치 이물질처럼 불안과 슬픔의 막이 덮여 있었다."], ["The grief had been there so shortly as to have abstracted nothing of the bloom, and had as yet but given a dignity to what it might eventually undermine.", "슬픔이 거기 깃든 지 얼마 되지 않아 발그레함은 조금도 앗아 가지 못했고, 다만 끝내 무너뜨릴지도 모를 것에 아직은 위엄을 더해 주었을 뿐이었다."], ["The scarlet of her lips had not had time to abate, and just now it appeared still more intense by the absence of the neighbouring and more transient colour of her cheek.", "그 입술의 진홍빛은 옅어질 새가 없었으니, 곁에 있던, 더 덧없는 뺨의 빛깔이 사라진 지금은 한층 짙어 보였다."], ["The lips frequently parted, with a murmur of words.", "입술은 자주 벌어지며 말을 중얼거렸다."], ["She seemed to belong rightly to a madrigal—to require viewing through rhyme and harmony.", "그녀는 마드리갈에 어울리는 사람 같았으니—운율과 화음을 통해 바라보아야 할 듯했다."]], [["One thing at least was obvious: she was not made to be looked at thus.", "적어도 한 가지는 분명했다. 그녀는 이렇게 바라보이도록 만들어진 사람이 아니었다."], ["The reddleman had appeared conscious of as much, and, while Mrs. Yeobright looked in upon her, he cast his eyes aside with a delicacy which well became him.", "레들맨도 그만큼은 의식한 듯했으니, 요브라이트 부인이 그녀를 들여다보는 동안 그는 그에게 썩 어울리는 섬세함으로 눈길을 돌렸다."], ["The sleeper apparently thought so too, for the next moment she opened her own.", "잠든 이도 그리 여긴 듯했으니, 다음 순간 그녀가 제 눈을 떴기 때문이다."]], [["The lips then parted with something of anticipation, something more of doubt; and her several thoughts and fractions of thoughts, as signalled by the changes on her face, were exhibited by the light to the utmost nicety.", "그러자 입술이 어딘가 기대를 머금고, 더욱이는 의혹을 머금고 벌어졌으며, 그녀의 여러 생각과 생각의 조각들은, 얼굴 위 변화로 신호되듯, 빛에 의해 더없이 정교하게 드러났다."], ["An ingenuous, transparent life was disclosed, as if the flow of her existence could be seen passing within her.", "마치 그녀 존재의 흐름이 그 안을 지나가는 것이 보이기라도 하듯, 꾸밈없고 투명한 생명이 드러났다."], ["She understood the scene in a moment.", "그녀는 단번에 상황을 알아차렸다."]], [["“O yes, it is I, Aunt,”", "\"오 그래요, 저예요, 이모.\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["“I know how frightened you are, and how you cannot believe it; but all the same, it is I who have come home like this!”", "\"이모가 얼마나 놀라셨는지, 얼마나 믿기지 않으실지 알아요. 하지만 그래도 이렇게 집에 돌아온 건 저예요!\""]], [["“Tamsin, Tamsin!”", "\"태신, 태신!\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright, stooping over the young woman and kissing her.", "요브라이트 부인이 젊은 여인 위로 몸을 굽혀 입맞추며 말했다."], ["“O my dear girl!”", "\"오 가엾은 것!\""]], [["Thomasin was now on the verge of a sob, but by an unexpected self-command she uttered no sound.", "토머신은 이제 막 흐느낌이 터질 듯했으나, 뜻밖의 자제력으로 아무 소리도 내지 않았다."], ["With a gentle panting breath she sat upright.", "부드럽게 가쁜 숨을 쉬며 그녀는 똑바로 일어나 앉았다."]], [["“I did not expect to see you in this state, any more than you me,”", "\"제가 이런 꼴인 줄 몰랐던 만큼이나 이모도 모르셨겠지요.\""], ["she went on quickly.", "그녀가 빠르게 말을 이었다."], ["“Where am I, Aunt?”", "\"여기가 어디예요, 이모?\""]], [["“Nearly home, my dear.", "\"거의 다 왔다, 얘야."], ["In Egdon Bottom.", "에그던 골짜기야."], ["What dreadful thing is it?”", "도대체 무슨 끔찍한 일이니?\""]], [["“I’ll tell you in a moment.", "\"곧 말씀드릴게요."], ["So near, are we?", "이렇게 가깝나요?"], ["Then I will get out and walk.", "그럼 내려서 걸을래요."], ["I want to go home by the path.”", "오솔길로 집에 가고 싶어요.\""]], [["“But this kind man who has done so much will, I am sure, take you right on to my house?”", "\"하지만 이렇게 애써 준 이 친절한 분이 우리 집까지 곧장 데려다주실 거다, 분명히.\""], ["said the aunt, turning to the reddleman, who had withdrawn from the front of the van on the awakening of the girl, and stood in the road.", "이모가 레들맨을 향해 돌아서며 말했으니, 그는 처녀가 깨어나자 수레 앞쪽에서 물러나 길에 서 있었다."]], [["“Why should you think it necessary to ask me?", "\"어찌 제게 그걸 물으셔야 한다고 여기십니까?"], ["I will, of course,”", "물론 그래야지요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“He is indeed kind,”", "\"정말 친절한 분이에요.\""], ["murmured Thomasin.", "토머신이 중얼거렸다."], ["“I was once acquainted with him, Aunt, and when I saw him today I thought I should prefer his van to any conveyance of a stranger.", "\"한때 알던 분이에요, 이모, 오늘 이분을 보고 낯선 이의 어떤 탈것보다 이분 수레가 낫겠다 싶었어요."], ["But I’ll walk now.", "하지만 이제 걸을래요."], ["Reddleman, stop the horses, please.”", "레들맨, 말 좀 세워 주세요.\""]], [["The man regarded her with tender reluctance, but stopped them", "사내는 다정한 망설임으로 그녀를 바라보았으나 말을 세웠다."]], [["Aunt and niece then descended from the van, Mrs.", "그러자 이모와 조카딸이 수레에서 내렸고, 요브라이트 부인이 그 주인에게 말했다."], ["Yeobright saying to its owner, “I quite recognize you now.", "\"이제 자네를 완전히 알아보겠네."], ["What made you change from the nice business your father left you?”", "무엇 때문에 아버지가 물려준 그 좋은 장사를 그만두고 바꿨나?\""]], [["“Well, I did,”", "\"음, 그냥 그랬습니다.\""], ["he said, and looked at Thomasin, who blushed a little.", "그가 말하고는 토머신을 바라보았으니, 그녀는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["“Then you’ll not be wanting me any more tonight, ma’am?”", "\"그럼 오늘 밤은 더 제가 필요치 않으시겠지요, 부인?\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright glanced around at the dark sky, at the hills, at the perishing bonfires, and at the lighted window of the inn they had neared.", "요브라이트 부인이 어두운 하늘과, 언덕들과, 사그라드는 모닥불들과, 그들이 다가온 여관의 불 켜진 창을 둘러보았다."], ["“I think not,”", "\"필요치 않겠네.\""], ["she said, “since Thomasin wishes to walk.", "그녀가 말했다. \"토머신이 걷고 싶다 하니."], ["We can soon run up the path and reach home—we know it well.”", "우리는 곧 오솔길을 올라 집에 닿을 수 있어—길을 잘 아니까.\""]], [["And after a few further words they parted, the reddleman moving onwards with his van, and the two women remaining standing in the road.", "그러고는 몇 마디를 더 나눈 뒤 헤어졌으니, 레들맨은 수레를 끌고 앞으로 나아갔고, 두 여인은 길에 선 채로 남았다."], ["As soon as the vehicle and its driver had withdrawn so far as to be beyond all possible reach of her voice, Mrs. Yeobright turned to her niece.", "그 탈것과 모는 이가 그녀의 목소리가 닿을 수 있는 거리에서 멀찍이 물러나자마자, 요브라이트 부인은 조카딸을 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“Now, Thomasin,”", "\"자, 토머신,\""], ["she said sternly, “what’s the meaning of this disgraceful performance?”", "그녀가 엄하게 말했다. \"이 망신스러운 사달이 도대체 무슨 영문이니?\""]], [["V. Perplexity among Honest People\nThomasin looked as if quite overcome by her aunt’s change of manner.", "토머신은 이모의 달라진 태도에 완전히 압도된 듯 보였다."], ["“It means just what it seems to mean: I am—not married,”", "\"보이는 그대로예요. 저는—혼인하지 못했어요.\""], ["she replied faintly.", "그녀가 힘없이 대답했다."], ["“Excuse me—for humiliating you, Aunt, by this mishap—I am sorry for it.", "\"용서하세요—이런 불상사로 이모를 욕보여서—죄송해요."], ["But I cannot help it.”", "하지만 어쩔 수 없어요.\""]], [["“Me?", "\"나라니?"], ["Think of yourself first.”", "네 생각부터 먼저 해라.\""]], [["“It was nobody’s fault.", "\"누구의 잘못도 아니었어요."], ["When we got there the parson wouldn’t marry us because of some trifling irregularity in the license.”", "거기 도착했더니 목사님이 혼인 허가증의 사소한 하자 때문에 우리를 혼인시켜 주지 않으려 했어요.\""]], [["“What irregularity?”", "\"무슨 하자?\""]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"모르겠어요."], ["Mr. Wildeve can explain.", "와일디브 씨가 설명할 수 있어요."], ["I did not think when I went away this morning that I should come back like this.”", "오늘 아침 떠날 때는 이렇게 돌아오리라곤 생각도 못 했어요.\""], ["It being dark, Thomasin allowed her emotion to escape her by the silent way of tears, which could roll down her cheek unseen.", "어두웠기에 토머신은 소리 없는 눈물로 감정을 흘려보낼 수 있었으니, 눈물이 남몰래 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."]], [["“I could almost say that it serves you right—if I did not feel that you don’t deserve it,”", "\"꼴좋다고 하고 싶을 지경이다—네가 이런 꼴을 당할 만하지 않다고 느끼지만 않는다면.\""], ["continued Mrs. Yeobright, who, possessing two distinct moods in close contiguity, a gentle mood and an angry, flew from one to the other without the least warning.", "요브라이트 부인이 이어 말했으니, 그녀는 서로 바싹 맞붙은 두 가지 뚜렷한 기분, 곧 부드러운 기분과 노여운 기분을 지녀, 아무런 예고도 없이 하나에서 다른 하나로 옮겨 다녔다."], ["“Remember, Thomasin, this business was none of my seeking; from the very first, when you began to feel foolish about that man, I warned you he would not make you happy.", "\"기억해라, 토머신, 이 일은 내가 원해서 된 게 아니야. 네가 그 사내에게 어리석은 마음을 품기 시작한 맨 처음부터, 나는 그자가 너를 행복하게 해 주지 못하리라 경고했어."], ["I felt it so strongly that I did what I would never have believed myself capable of doing—stood up in the church, and made myself the public talk for weeks.", "어찌나 강하게 그리 느꼈던지, 나로서는 도저히 할 수 있으리라 믿지 못했을 일을 했지—교회에서 일어서서 몇 주씩이나 사람들 입길에 오르내렸어."], ["But having once consented, I don’t submit to these fancies without good reason.", "하지만 일단 동의한 이상, 충분한 이유 없이는 이런 변덕에 굴하지 않는다."], ["Marry him you must after this.”", "이렇게 된 마당에 너는 그자와 혼인해야 해.\""]], [["“Do you think I wish to do otherwise for one moment?”", "\"제가 단 한순간이라도 달리하고 싶어 한다고 생각하세요?\""], ["said Thomasin, with a heavy sigh.", "토머신이 무거운 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“I know how wrong it was of me to love him, but don’t pain me by talking like that, Aunt!", "\"그이를 사랑한 게 얼마나 잘못이었는지 알아요, 하지만 그렇게 말씀하셔서 저를 아프게 하지 마세요, 이모!"], ["You would not have had me stay there with him, would you?—and your house is the only home I have to return to.", "제가 거기서 그이와 함께 머물기를 바라진 않으셨을 거잖아요?—그리고 이모 집이 제가 돌아갈 유일한 보금자리예요."], ["He says we can be married in a day or two.”", "그이는 하루 이틀이면 혼인할 수 있다고 했어요.\""]], [["“I wish he had never seen you.”", "\"그자가 너를 만난 적이 없었더라면.\""]], [["“Very well; then I will be the miserablest woman in the world, and not let him see me again.", "\"좋아요, 그럼 저는 세상에서 가장 비참한 여자가 되어, 그이가 다시는 저를 못 보게 할래요."], ["No, I won’t have him!”", "아니, 저 그이 싫어요!\""]], [["“It is too late to speak so.", "\"그렇게 말하기엔 너무 늦었다."], ["Come with me.", "나랑 같이 가자."], ["I am going to the inn to see if he has returned.", "여관에 가서 그자가 돌아왔는지 보겠어."], ["Of course I shall get to the bottom of this story at once.", "물론 이 이야기의 진상을 당장 캐낼 거야."], ["Mr. Wildeve must not suppose he can play tricks upon me, or any belonging to me.”", "와일디브 씨가 나나 내 식구에게 농간을 부릴 수 있다고 여겨선 안 되지.\""]], [["“It was not that.", "\"그게 아니었어요."], ["The license was wrong, and he couldn’t get another the same day.", "허가증이 잘못됐고, 그이는 같은 날 다른 걸 구할 수 없었어요."], ["He will tell you in a moment how it was, if he comes.”", "오면, 어찌 된 일인지 그이가 당장 말씀드릴 거예요.\""]], [["“Why didn’t he bring you back?”", "\"왜 너를 다시 데려오지 않았지?\""]], [["“That was me!”", "\"그건 저 때문이었어요!\""], ["again sobbed Thomasin.", "토머신이 다시 흐느꼈다."], ["“When I found we could not be married I didn’t like to come back with him, and I was very ill.", "\"혼인할 수 없다는 걸 알고 나니 그이와 함께 돌아오기가 싫었고, 저는 몹시 아팠어요."], ["Then I saw Diggory Venn, and was glad to get him to take me home.", "그때 디고리 베너를 만났고, 그를 만나 집에 데려다 달라 할 수 있어 다행이었어요."], ["I cannot explain it any better, and you must be angry with me if you will.”", "더는 잘 설명 못 하겠어요, 화내고 싶으시면 화내셔도 돼요.\""]], [["“I shall see about that,”", "\"그건 두고 봐야지.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright; and they turned towards the inn, known in the neighbourhood as the Quiet Woman, the sign of which represented the figure of a matron carrying her head under her arm.", "요브라이트 부인이 말했고, 그들은 인근에서 콰이어트 우먼이라 알려진 여관 쪽으로 돌아섰으니, 그 간판은 제 머리를 팔에 끼고 있는 부인의 모습을 그린 것이었다."], ["The front of the house was towards the heath and Rainbarrow, whose dark shape seemed to threaten it from the sky.", "그 집 정면은 황야와 레인배로를 향해 있었고, 레인배로의 어두운 형상은 하늘에서 그 집을 위협하는 듯했다."], ["Upon the door was a neglected brass plate, bearing the unexpected inscription, “Mr. Wildeve, Engineer”—a useless yet cherished relic from the time when he had been started in that profession in an office at Budmouth by those who had hoped much from him, and had been disappointed.", "문에는 손길이 닿지 않은 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있었으니, \"공학자 와일디브 씨\"라는 뜻밖의 글귀가 새겨져 있었던바—그것은 그에게 큰 기대를 걸었다가 실망한 이들이 버드머스의 한 사무소에서 그를 그 직업에 입문시켰던 시절로부터 남은, 쓸모는 없으나 소중히 간직된 유물이었다."], ["The garden was at the back, and behind this ran a still deep stream, forming the margin of the heath in that direction, meadow-land appearing beyond the stream.", "정원은 뒤편에 있었고, 그 뒤로는 잔잔하고 깊은 시내가 흘러, 그 방향으로 황야의 가장자리를 이루었으며, 시내 너머로는 목초지가 나타났다."]], [["But the thick obscurity permitted only skylines to be visible of any scene at present.", "그러나 짙은 어둠은 지금 어떤 풍경이든 능선만을 보이게 허락할 뿐이었다."], ["The water at the back of the house could be heard, idly spinning whirpools in its creep between the rows of dry feather-headed reeds which formed a stockade along each bank.", "집 뒤편의 물소리가 들렸으니, 양쪽 둑을 따라 울타리를 이룬, 깃털 같은 머리를 인 마른 갈대 줄기 사이를 기어가며 한가로이 소용돌이를 자아내는 소리였다."], ["Their presence was denoted by sounds as of a congregation praying humbly, produced by their rubbing against each other in the slow wind.", "그것들의 존재는 마치 회중이 겸손히 기도하는 듯한 소리로 알려졌다."]], [["The window, whence the candlelight had shone up the vale to the eyes of the bonfire group, was uncurtained, but the sill lay too high for a pedestrian on the outside to look over it into the room.", "모닥불 무리의 눈에 골짜기 위로 촛불빛을 비추던 그 창은 커튼이 쳐 있지 않았으나, 창턱이 너무 높아 바깥의 보행자가 그 너머로 방 안을 들여다볼 수는 없었다."], ["A vast shadow, in which could be dimly traced portions of a masculine contour, blotted half the ceiling.", "사내의 윤곽 일부를 어렴풋이 더듬을 수 있는 거대한 그림자가 천장의 절반을 뒤덮고 있었다."]], [["“He seems to be at home,”", "\"집에 있는 모양이다.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "요브라이트 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Must I come in, too, Aunt?”", "\"저도 들어가야 하나요, 이모?\""], ["asked Thomasin faintly.", "토머신이 힘없이 물었다."], ["“I suppose not; it would be wrong.”", "\"안 그래야겠지요. 그건 옳지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“You must come, certainly—to confront him, so that he may make no false representations to me.", "\"넌 꼭 들어와야 해—그자와 맞서서, 그자가 내게 거짓을 둘러대지 못하게 말이다."], ["We shall not be five minutes in the house, and then we’ll walk home.”", "집 안에 오 분도 머물지 않을 테니, 그러고 나서 집으로 걸어가자.\""]], [["Entering the open passage, she tapped at the door of the private parlour, unfastened it, and looked in.", "열린 통로로 들어서서, 그녀는 사실私室의 문을 두드리고, 빗장을 풀고, 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["The back and shoulders of a man came between Mrs. Yeobright’s eyes and the fire.", "한 사내의 등과 어깨가 요브라이트 부인의 눈과 불 사이를 가로막았다."], ["Wildeve, whose form it was, immediately turned, arose, and advanced to meet his visitors.", "그 형체의 주인인 와일디브가 곧장 돌아서서 일어나 방문객들을 맞으러 나아갔다."]], [["He was quite a young man, and of the two properties, form and motion, the latter first attracted the eye in him.", "그는 아주 젊은 사내였고, 형태와 동작이라는 두 속성 중에 그에게서 먼저 눈길을 끄는 것은 후자였다."], ["The grace of his movement was singular—it was the pantomimic expression of a lady-killing career.", "그 동작의 우아함은 남달랐으니—그것은 여인을 후리는 이력의 무언극적 표현이었다."], ["Next came into notice the more material qualities, among which was a profuse crop of hair impending over the top of his face, lending to his forehead the high-cornered outline of an early Gothic shield; and a neck which was smooth and round as a cylinder.", "다음으로 눈에 띈 것은 더 물질적인 자질들이었으니, 그중에는 얼굴 윗부분 위로 드리운 무성한 머리털이 있어, 초기 고딕 방패의 높은 모서리 윤곽을 그의 이마에 빌려주었고, 또 원통처럼 매끄럽고 둥근 목이 있었다."], ["The lower half of his figure was of light build.", "그 몸의 아랫부분은 가냘픈 골격이었다."], ["Altogether he was one in whom no man would have seen anything to admire, and in whom no woman would have seen anything to dislike.", "통틀어 그는 어떤 사내도 감탄할 만한 것을 찾지 못할 사람이었으나, 어떤 여인도 싫어할 만한 것을 찾지 못할 사람이었다."]], [["He discerned the young girl’s form in the passage, and said, “Thomasin, then, has reached home.", "그는 통로에 선 젊은 처녀의 형체를 알아보고 말했다. \"그래, 토머신은 집에 닿았군."], ["How could you leave me in that way, darling?”", "어쩌면 그렇게 나를 두고 가 버렸소, 여보?\""], ["And turning to Mrs. Yeobright—“It was useless to argue with her.", "그러고는 요브라이트 부인을 향해 돌아서며—\"저 사람과 입씨름해 봐야 소용없었습니다."], ["She would go, and go alone.”", "가겠다고, 그것도 혼자 가겠다고 하니까요.\""]], [["“But what’s the meaning of it all?”", "\"하지만 이게 다 무슨 영문이지요?\""], ["demanded Mrs. Yeobright haughtily.", "요브라이트 부인이 도도하게 따져 물었다."]], [["“Take a seat,”", "\"앉으시지요.\""], ["said Wildeve, placing chairs for the two women.", "와일디브가 두 여인에게 의자를 놓아 주며 말했다."], ["“Well, it was a very stupid mistake, but such mistakes will happen.", "\"음, 아주 멍청한 착오였습니다만, 그런 착오야 일어나기 마련이지요."], ["The license was useless at Anglebury.", "앵글베리에서는 그 허가증이 쓸모없었습니다."], ["It was made out for Budmouth, but as I didn’t read it I wasn’t aware of that.”", "버드머스용으로 발급된 것이었는데, 제가 읽어 보질 않아 그걸 몰랐던 겁니다.\""]], [["“But you had been staying at Anglebury?”", "\"하지만 자네는 앵글베리에 머물고 있지 않았나?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아닙니다."], ["I had been at Budmouth—till two days ago—and that was where I had intended to take her; but when I came to fetch her we decided upon Anglebury, forgetting that a new license would be necessary.", "저는 버드머스에 있었지요—이틀 전까지요—그리고 거기서 저 사람과 혼인할 작정이었습니다. 그런데 데리러 갔을 때 우리는 앵글베리로 정했지요, 새 허가증이 필요하리라는 걸 잊고서요."], ["There was not time to get to Budmouth afterwards.”", "그 뒤에 버드머스까지 갈 시간이 없었습니다.\""]], [["“I think you are very much to blame,”", "\"내 보기에 자네 잘못이 아주 크네.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "요브라이트 부인이 말했다."]], [["“It was quite my fault we chose Anglebury,”", "\"앵글베리를 고른 건 전적으로 제 잘못이에요.\""], ["Thomasin pleaded.", "토머신이 변호했다."], ["“I proposed it because I was not known there.”", "\"거기선 제가 알려져 있지 않아서 제가 그러자고 했어요.\""]], [["“I know so well that I am to blame that you need not remind me of it,”", "\"제 잘못이라는 걸 너무나 잘 알고 있으니 일깨워 주실 필요 없습니다.\""], ["replied Wildeve shortly.", "와일디브가 퉁명스레 대답했다."]], [["“Such things don’t happen for nothing,”", "\"이런 일은 까닭 없이 일어나지 않아.\""], ["said the aunt.", "이모가 말했다."], ["“It is a great slight to me and my family; and when it gets known there will be a very unpleasant time for us.", "\"이건 나와 내 집안에 대한 큰 모욕이고, 이게 알려지면 우리에게 아주 거북한 시간이 닥칠 거야."], ["How can she look her friends in the face tomorrow?", "그 애가 내일 어떻게 벗들의 얼굴을 마주하겠나?"], ["It is a very great injury, and one I cannot easily forgive.", "이건 아주 큰 해이고, 내가 쉬이 용서할 수 없는 거야."], ["It may even reflect on her character.”", "그 애의 평판에까지 누가 될지도 몰라.\""]], [["“Nonsense,”", "\"당치 않습니다.\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."]], [["Thomasin’s large eyes had flown from the face of one to the face of the other during this discussion, and she now said anxiously, “Will you allow me, Aunt, to talk it over alone with Damon for five minutes?", "이 논쟁 내내 토머신의 큰 눈은 한 사람의 얼굴에서 다른 사람의 얼굴로 옮겨 다녔고, 이제 그녀가 불안하게 말했다. \"이모, 데이먼하고 단둘이 오 분만 이야기하게 해 주시겠어요?"], ["Will you, Damon?”", "그래 주겠어요, 데이먼?\""]], [["“Certainly, dear,”", "\"물론이지, 여보.\""], ["said Wildeve, “if your aunt will excuse us.”", "와일디브가 말했다. \"이모님께서 양해해 주신다면.\""], ["He led her into an adjoining room, leaving Mrs. Yeobright by the fire.", "그는 그녀를 옆방으로 데려가고, 요브라이트 부인을 불가에 남겨 두었다."]], [["As soon as they were alone, and the door closed, Thomasin said, turning up her pale, tearful face to him, “It is killing me, this, Damon!", "둘만 남고 문이 닫히자마자, 토머신은 창백하고 눈물 어린 얼굴을 그에게 들어 올리며 말했다. \"이게 저를 죽이고 있어요, 데이먼!"], ["I did not mean to part from you in anger at Anglebury this morning; but I was frightened and hardly knew what I said.", "오늘 아침 앵글베리에서 화내며 당신과 헤어지려던 게 아니었어요. 하지만 겁이 나서 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 몰랐어요."], ["I’ve not let Aunt know how much I suffered today; and it is so hard to command my face and voice, and to smile as if it were a slight thing to me; but I try to do so, that she may not be still more indignant with you.", "오늘 제가 얼마나 괴로웠는지 이모께는 알리지 않았어요. 얼굴과 목소리를 다잡고, 제게 별일 아닌 양 미소 짓기가 너무 힘들지만, 그래도 그러려 애써요, 이모가 당신에게 더 분개하지 않으시도록요."], ["I know you could not help it, dear, whatever Aunt may think.”", "당신이 어쩔 수 없었다는 걸 알아요, 여보, 이모가 무슨 생각을 하시든.\""]], [["“She is very unpleasant.”", "\"이모님은 아주 불쾌한 분이군.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["Thomasin murmured, “and I suppose I seem so now....", "토머신이 중얼거렸다. \"그리고 이제 저도 그렇게 보이겠지요……"], ["Damon, what do you mean to do about me?”", "데이먼, 저를 어떻게 하실 작정이에요?\""]], [["“Do about you?”", "\"당신을 어떻게 하느냐고?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Those who don’t like you whisper things which at moments make me doubt you.", "당신을 좋아하지 않는 이들이 수군대는 말들이 때때로 당신을 의심하게 만들어요."], ["We mean to marry, I suppose, don’t we?”", "우리 혼인할 거지요, 그렇지요?\""]], [["“Of course we do.", "\"물론 그래야지."], ["We have only to go to Budmouth on Monday, and we marry at once.”", "월요일에 버드머스로 가기만 하면, 당장 혼인하는 거야.\""]], [["“Then do let us go!—O Damon, what you make me say!”", "\"그럼 가요!—오 데이먼, 당신이 저더러 이런 말을 하게 만드네요!\""], ["She hid her face in her handkerchief.", "그녀는 손수건에 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["“Here am I asking you to marry me, when by rights you ought to be on your knees imploring me, your cruel mistress, not to refuse you, and saying it would break your heart if I did.", "\"여기 제가 당신에게 혼인해 달라 청하고 있어요, 본래대로라면 당신이 무릎 꿇고 잔인한 연인인 제게 거절하지 말아 달라 애원하며, 제가 거절하면 가슴이 미어질 거라 말해야 마땅한데."], ["I used to think it would be pretty and sweet like that; but how different!”", "전엔 그런 게 예쁘고 달콤하리라 생각했어요. 한데 어쩜 이리 다른지!\""]], [["“Yes, real life is never at all like that.”", "\"그래, 진짜 삶은 결코 그런 것과는 다르지.\""]], [["“But I don’t care personally if it never takes place,”", "\"하지만 저 개인으로는 그 일이 영영 이루어지지 않아도 상관없어요.\""], ["she added with a little dignity; “no, I can live without you.", "그녀가 약간의 위엄을 담아 덧붙였다. \"그래요, 저는 당신 없이도 살 수 있어요."], ["It is Aunt I think of.", "제가 생각하는 건 이모예요."], ["She is so proud, and thinks so much of her family respectability, that she will be cut down with mortification if this story should get abroad before—it is done.", "이모는 워낙 자존심이 강하고 집안의 체면을 무척 중히 여기셔서, 이 이야기가—마무리되기 전에—세상에 퍼지면 굴욕으로 무너지실 거예요."], ["My cousin Clym, too, will be much wounded.”", "제 사촌 클림도 몹시 상처받을 거고요.\""]], [["“Then he will be very unreasonable.", "\"그렇다면 그는 아주 비합리적인 사람이군."], ["In fact, you are all rather unreasonable.”", "사실, 당신들은 다들 좀 비합리적이야.\""]], [["Thomasin coloured a little, and not with love.", "토머신이 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔으나, 사랑 때문은 아니었다."], ["But whatever the momentary feeling which caused that flush in her, it went as it came, and she humbly said, “I never mean to be, if I can help it.", "그러나 그녀에게 그 홍조를 일으킨 순간의 감정이 무엇이었든, 그것은 온 것처럼 가 버렸고, 그녀는 겸허하게 말했다. \"어쩔 수만 있다면 결코 그러려는 게 아니에요."], ["I merely feel that you have my aunt to some extent in your power at last.”", "그저 당신이 마침내 어느 정도 제 이모를 손아귀에 쥐었다는 걸 느낄 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“As a matter of justice it is almost due to me,”", "\"공정함으로 따지자면 그건 거의 제게 주어질 만한 거지.\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."], ["“Think what I have gone through to win her consent; the insult that it is to any man to have the banns forbidden—the double insult to a man unlucky enough to be cursed with sensitiveness, and blue demons, and Heaven knows what, as I am.", "\"제가 이모님 승낙을 얻으려고 무얼 겪었는지 생각해 봐요. 결혼 예고가 금지당하는 게 어떤 사내에게든 얼마나 모욕인지—나처럼 불운하게도 예민함과, 우울한 마귀와, 하늘만이 아는 온갖 것에 시달리는 사내에게는 갑절의 모욕이지."], ["I can never forget those banns.", "나는 그 예고를 결코 잊을 수 없어."], ["A harsher man would rejoice now in the power I have of turning upon your aunt by going no further in the business.”", "더 모진 사내라면 이 일을 더 진척시키지 않음으로써 당신 이모를 되받아칠 수 있는 이 권력을 지금 기뻐할 텐데.\""]], [["She looked wistfully at him with her sorrowful eyes as he said those words, and her aspect showed that more than one person in the room could deplore the possession of sensitiveness.", "그가 그 말을 하는 동안 그녀는 슬픈 눈으로 그를 애틋하게 바라보았으니, 그 모습은 이 방 안에 예민함을 지닌 것을 한탄할 만한 이가 한 사람만이 아님을 보여 주었다."], ["Seeing that she was really suffering he seemed disturbed and added, “This is merely a reflection you know.", "그녀가 정말로 괴로워하는 것을 보고 그는 마음이 어지러운 듯하더니 덧붙였다. \"이건 그저 하나의 상념일 뿐이야, 알잖아."], ["I have not the least intention to refuse to complete the marriage, Tamsie mine—I could not bear it.”", "나는 이 혼인을 매듭짓길 거절할 마음이 추호도 없어, 내 태미—그건 견딜 수 없을 거야.\""]], [["“You could not, I know!”", "\"못 견디실 거예요, 알아요!\""], ["said the fair girl, brightening.", "고운 처녀가 환해지며 말했다."], ["“You, who cannot bear the sight of pain in even an insect, or any disagreeable sound, or unpleasant smell even, will not long cause pain to me and mine.”", "\"벌레 한 마리가 아파하는 꼴조차, 어떤 듣기 싫은 소리조차, 심지어 고약한 냄새조차 못 견디는 당신이, 저와 제 식구를 오래 아프게 하지는 않으실 거예요.\""]], [["“I will not, if I can help it.”", "\"안 그럴게, 어쩔 수만 있다면.\""]], [["“Your hand upon it, Damon.”", "\"손을 주세요, 데이먼.\""]], [["He carelessly gave her his hand.", "그는 무심히 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Ah, by my crown, what’s that?”", "\"아, 맙소사, 저게 뭐지?\""], ["he said suddenly.", "그가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["There fell upon their ears the sound of numerous voices singing in front of the house.", "집 앞에서 여러 목소리가 노래하는 소리가 그들의 귀에 떨어졌다."], ["Among these, two made themselves prominent by their peculiarity: one was a very strong bass, the other a wheezy thin piping.", "그중 둘이 그 독특함으로 두드러졌으니, 하나는 아주 강한 베이스였고, 다른 하나는 쌕쌕거리는 가느다란 피리 소리였다."], ["Thomasin recognized them as belonging to Timothy Fairway and Grandfer Cantle respectively.", "토머신은 그것들이 각각 티머시 페어웨이와 캔틀 영감의 것임을 알아챘다."]], [["“What does it mean—it is not skimmity-riding, I hope?”", "\"이게 무슨 영문이지요—설마 망신 행렬은 아니겠지요?\""], ["she said, with a frightened gaze at Wildeve.", "그녀가 겁먹은 눈길로 와일디브를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Of course not; no, it is that the heath-folk have come to sing to us a welcome.", "\"물론 아니지. 아니, 황야 사람들이 우리한테 환영의 노래를 부르러 온 거야."], ["This is intolerable!”", "이건 참을 수 없군!\""], ["He began pacing about, the men outside singing cheerily—", "그가 서성이기 시작하자, 바깥의 사내들이 흥겹게 노래했다—"]], [["“He told′ her that she′ was the joy′ of his life′, And if′ she’d con-sent′ he would make her his wife′; She could′ not refuse′ him; to church′ so they went′, Young Will was forgot′, and young Sue′ was content′; And then′ was she kiss’d′ and set down′ on his knee′, No man′ in the world′ was so lov′-ing as he′!”", "\"그가 그녀에게 일렀네, 그녀가 그의 삶의 기쁨이라고, 그녀만 응한다면 그녀를 아내로 삼겠노라고, 그녀는 그를 거절할 수 없었네, 그리하여 교회로 갔다네, 젊은 윌은 잊히고, 젊은 수는 만족했다네, 그러고는 입맞춤받고 그의 무릎에 앉혀졌으니, 세상 어느 사내도 그처럼 다정하진 않았다네!\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright burst in from the outer room.", "요브라이트 부인이 바깥방에서 뛰어들어왔다."], ["“Thomasin, Thomasin!”", "\"토머신, 토머신!\""], ["she said, looking indignantly at Wildeve; “here’s a pretty exposure!", "그녀가 와일디브를 분개한 눈으로 보며 말했다. \"이거 참 볼만한 망신이로구나!"], ["Let us escape at once.", "당장 빠져나가자."], ["Come!”", "가자!\""]], [["It was, however, too late to get away by the passage.", "그러나 통로로 빠져나가기엔 이미 늦었다."], ["A rugged knocking had begun upon the door of the front room.", "앞방 문에 거친 노크 소리가 시작됐던 것이다."], ["Wildeve, who had gone to the window, came back.", "창가로 갔던 와일디브가 돌아왔다."]], [["“Stop!”", "\"멈추세요!\""], ["he said imperiously, putting his hand upon Mrs. Yeobright’s arm.", "그가 요브라이트 부인의 팔에 손을 얹으며 위엄 있게 말했다."], ["“We are regularly besieged.", "\"우리는 완전히 포위됐습니다."], ["There are fifty of them out there if there’s one.", "바깥에 한 명이라도 있으면 쉰 명은 있을 겁니다."], ["You stay in this room with Thomasin; I’ll go out and face them.", "부인은 토머신과 이 방에 계세요. 제가 나가서 그들과 맞서지요."], ["You must stay now, for my sake, till they are gone, so that it may seem as if all was right.", "저들이 갈 때까지 이제 부인도 머무셔야 합니다, 저를 봐서라도요, 모든 게 멀쩡한 것처럼 보이도록요."], ["Come, Tamsie dear, don’t go making a scene—we must marry after this; that you can see as well as I.", "자, 태미 여보, 소동 일으키지 마—이렇게 된 마당에 우리는 혼인해야 하고, 그건 나만큼 당신도 잘 알잖아."], ["Sit still, that’s all—and don’t speak much.", "가만히 앉아 있어, 그게 다야—그리고 말은 많이 하지 말고."], ["I’ll manage them.", "내가 저들을 처리할 테니."], ["Blundering fools!”", "멍청한 얼간이들!\""]], [["He pressed the agitated girl into a seat, returned to the outer room and opened the door.", "그는 동요한 처녀를 의자에 눌러 앉히고는 바깥방으로 돌아가 문을 열었다."], ["Immediately outside, in the passage, appeared Grandfer Cantle singing in concert with those still standing in front of the house.", "바로 바깥, 통로에 캔틀 영감이 나타나 집 앞에 아직 서 있는 이들과 어우러져 노래하고 있었다."], ["He came into the room and nodded abstractedly to Wildeve, his lips still parted, and his features excruciatingly strained in the emission of the chorus.", "그는 방으로 들어와 와일디브에게 멍하니 고개를 끄덕였으니, 입술은 여전히 벌어진 채였고, 이목구비는 후렴을 토해 내느라 괴로울 만치 긴장돼 있었다."], ["This being ended, he said heartily, “Here’s welcome to the new-made couple, and God bless ’em!”", "그것이 끝나자 그가 진심으로 말했다. \"갓 맺어진 부부에게 환영을, 하느님의 복이 있기를!\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙소.\""], ["said Wildeve, with dry resentment, his face as gloomy as a thunderstorm.", "와일디브가 메마른 분기를 담아 말했으니, 그 얼굴은 뇌우雷雨처럼 음울했다."]], [["At the Grandfer’s heels now came the rest of the group, which included Fairway, Christian, Sam the turf-cutter, Humphrey, and a dozen others.", "영감의 뒤를 이어 이제 나머지 무리가 들어왔으니, 페어웨이, 크리스천, 잔디 베는 샘, 험프리, 그 밖에 열두엇이 끼어 있었다."], ["All smiled upon Wildeve, and upon his tables and chairs likewise, from a general sense of friendliness towards the articles as well as towards their owner.", "모두가 와일디브를 향해, 또한 그의 탁자와 의자들을 향해 웃음 지었으니, 물건들에 대해서도 그 주인에 대해서와 마찬가지로 다정한 마음이 두루 일었던 까닭이다."]], [["“We be not here afore Mrs. Yeobright after all,”", "\"우리가 결국 요브라이트 부인보다 먼저 온 건 아니로군.\""], ["said Fairway, recognizing the matron’s bonnet through the glass partition which divided the public apartment they had entered from the room where the women sat.", "페어웨이가 말했으니, 그들이 들어선 술청과 여인들이 앉은 방을 나누는 유리 칸막이 너머로 부인의 보닛을 알아본 것이었다."], ["“We struck down across, d’ye see, Mr. Wildeve, and she went round by the path.”", "\"우리는 가로질러 내려왔잖소, 알겠소, 와일디브 씨, 부인은 오솔길로 빙 돌아갔고.\""]], [["“And I see the young bride’s little head!”", "\"젊은 신부의 작은 머리도 보이는군!\""], ["said Grandfer, peeping in the same direction, and discerning Thomasin, who was waiting beside her aunt in a miserable and awkward way.", "영감이 같은 방향을 들여다보며, 비참하고 어색한 모양새로 이모 곁에서 기다리는 토머신을 알아보고 말했다."], ["“Not quite settled in yet—well, well, there’s plenty of time.”", "\"아직 자리를 다 못 잡았구먼—뭐, 뭐, 시간이야 넉넉하지.\""]], [["Wildeve made no reply; and probably feeling that the sooner he treated them the sooner they would go, he produced a stone jar, which threw a warm halo over matters at once.", "와일디브는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으며, 아마도 빨리 대접할수록 빨리 가리라 여겼던지, 돌 항아리 하나를 내놓으니 그것이 단번에 사정에 따스한 후광을 드리웠다."]], [["“That’s a drop of the right sort, I can see,”", "\"저게 바로 제대로 된 술 한 방울이군, 딱 보면 알지.\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, with the air of a man too well-mannered to show any hurry to taste it.", "캔틀 영감이 그것을 맛보려 서두르는 기색을 보이기엔 너무 예의 바른 사내의 태도로 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["said Wildeve, “’tis some old mead.", "와일디브가 말했다. \"묵은 벌꿀술이오."], ["I hope you will like it.”", "마음에 드시길 바라오.\""]], [["“O ay!”", "\"오 암!\""], ["replied the guests, in the hearty tones natural when the words demanded by politeness coincide with those of deepest feeling.", "손님들이 응했으니, 예의가 요구하는 말이 가장 깊은 속내의 말과 일치할 때 자연히 우러나는 진정 어린 어조였다."], ["“There isn’t a prettier drink under the sun.”", "\"하늘 아래 이보다 더 멋진 술은 없지.\""]], [["“I’ll take my oath there isn’t,”", "\"맹세코 없고말고.\""], ["added Grandfer Cantle.", "캔틀 영감이 덧붙였다."], ["“All that can be said against mead is that ’tis rather heady, and apt to lie about a man a good while.", "\"벌꿀술에 대해 할 수 있는 흉이라곤 좀 독해서 사내한테 한참 들러붙기 쉽다는 것뿐이지."], ["But tomorrow’s Sunday, thank God.”", "하지만 다행히도 내일은 일요일 아닌가.\""]], [["“I feel’d for all the world like some bold soldier after I had had some once,”", "\"한번 마셨더니 영락없이 무슨 용맹한 군인이라도 된 것 같더라고요.\""], ["said Christian.", "크리스천이 말했다."]], [["“You shall feel so again,”", "\"또 그리 느끼게 해 드리지요.\""], ["said Wildeve, with condescension, “Cups or glasses, gentlemen?”", "와일디브가 너그러이 말했다. \"잔으로 드릴까요, 컵으로 드릴까요, 여러분?\""]], [["“Well, if you don’t mind, we’ll have the beaker, and pass ’en round; ’tis better than heling it out in dribbles.”", "\"음, 괜찮으시다면 큰 잔으로 해서 돌려 마시지요. 찔끔찔끔 따라 마시는 것보다 그게 낫소.\""]], [["“Jown the slippery glasses,”", "\"미끄러운 유리잔은 집어치워.\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle.", "캔틀 영감이 말했다."], ["“What’s the good of a thing that you can’t put down in the ashes to warm, hey, neighbours; that’s what I ask?”", "\"데우려고 잿불에 내려놓을 수도 없는 물건이 무슨 소용이람, 안 그렇소, 이웃들? 내가 묻고 싶은 게 그거요.\""]], [["“Right, Grandfer,”", "\"옳소, 영감.\""], ["said Sam; and the mead then circulated.", "샘이 말했고, 이윽고 벌꿀술이 돌았다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Timothy Fairway, feeling demands upon his praise in some form or other, “’tis a worthy thing to be married, Mr.", "티머시 페어웨이가, 어떤 형태로든 자기 칭찬이 요구된다 느끼며 말했다. \"혼인이란 갸륵한 일이지요, 와일디브 씨."], ["Wildeve; and the woman you’ve got is a dimant, so says I.", "그리고 얻으신 여인은 보석이오, 내 그리 말하리다."], ["Yes,”", "그렇소,\""], ["he continued, to Grandfer Cantle, raising his voice so as to be heard through the partition, “her father (inclining his head towards the inner room) was as good a feller as ever lived.", "그가 캔틀 영감을 향해, 칸막이 너머로 들리도록 목청을 높여 이어 말했다. \"그 아가씨 아버지는 (안쪽 방을 향해 고개를 기울이며) 둘도 없이 훌륭한 양반이었소."], ["He always had his great indignation ready against anything underhand.”", "무슨 음흉한 짓이든 그 앞에서는 큰 분노를 늘 채비하고 있었지.\""]], [["“Is that very dangerous?”", "\"그게 그리 위험한 거예요?\""], ["said Christian.", "크리스천이 말했다."]], [["“And there were few in these parts that were upsides with him,”", "\"이 근방에 그 양반에 견줄 만한 이는 몇 안 됐소.\""], ["said Sam.", "샘이 말했다."], ["“Whenever a club walked he’d play the clarinet in the band that marched before ’em as if he’d never touched anything but a clarinet all his life.", "\"어느 조합이 행진할 때마다 그 앞장서는 악대에서 클라리넷을 불었는데, 평생 클라리넷밖에 안 만져 본 사람처럼 불었지."], ["And then, when they got to church door he’d throw down the clarinet, mount the gallery, snatch up the bass viol, and rozum away as if he’d never played anything but a bass viol.", "그러고는 교회 문에 이르면 클라리넷을 내려놓고 회랑으로 올라가 베이스 비올을 낚아채 켜는데, 평생 베이스 비올밖에 안 켜 본 사람처럼 송진을 먹여 그어 댔소."], ["Folk would say—folk that knowed what a true stave was—‘Surely, surely that’s never the same man that I saw handling the clarinet so masterly by now!’”", "사람들이—진짜 가락이 뭔지 아는 사람들이—이러더군, '아니, 아니, 저 양반이 방금 클라리넷을 그리 능란하게 다루던 그 사람이 정말 맞나!'\""]], [["“I can mind it,”", "\"내 그걸 기억하오.\""], ["said the furze-cutter.", "가시금작화 베는 일꾼이 말했다."], ["“’Twas a wonderful thing that one body could hold it all and never mix the fingering.”", "\"한 몸이 그걸 다 해내면서 운지를 한 번도 안 헷갈렸다니 놀라운 일이었지.\""]], [["“There was Kingsbere church likewise,”", "\"킹스비어 교회도 있었지.\""], ["Fairway recommenced, as one opening a new vein of the same mine of interest.", "페어웨이가, 같은 흥미의 광맥에서 새 줄기를 캐는 사람처럼 다시 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["Wildeve breathed the breath of one intolerably bored, and glanced through the partition at the prisoners.", "와일디브는 견딜 수 없이 지루한 사람의 숨을 내쉬며, 칸막이 너머로 갇힌 이들을 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“He used to walk over there of a Sunday afternoon to visit his old acquaintance Andrew Brown, the first clarinet there; a good man enough, but rather screechy in his music, if you can mind?”", "\"그 양반은 일요일 오후면 거기 거기 첫 클라리넷인 옛 지인 앤드루 브라운을 만나러 걸어가곤 했소. 사람은 꽤 괜찮았지만, 음악은 좀 째지는 편이었지, 기억나는지 모르겠소만?\""]], [["“’A was.”", "\"그랬지.\""]], [["“And neighbour Yeobright would take Andrey’s place for some part of the service, to let Andrey have a bit of a nap, as any friend would naturally do.”", "\"그러면 이웃 요브라이트는 예배 어느 대목에서 앤드리 자리를 대신 맡아, 앤드리가 잠깐 눈이라도 붙이게 해 줬소, 어느 벗인들 그러는 게 인지상정이듯이.\""]], [["“As any friend would,”", "\"어느 벗인들 그러지.\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, the other listeners expressing the same accord by the shorter way of nodding their heads.", "캔틀 영감이 말했고, 다른 듣는 이들도 더 짧게 고개를 끄덕여 같은 동의를 나타냈다."]], [["“No sooner was Andrey asleep and the first whiff of neighbour Yeobright’s wind had got inside Andrey’s clarinet than everyone in church feeled in a moment there was a great soul among ’em.", "\"앤드리가 잠들고 이웃 요브라이트의 첫 입김이 앤드리의 클라리넷 속으로 들어가기가 무섭게, 교회 안의 모두가 단번에 그들 가운데 위대한 영혼이 있음을 느꼈소. 머리란 머리는 다 돌아가며 이러더군, '아, 그 양반인 줄 알았어!'"], ["All heads would turn, and they’d say, ‘Ah, I thought ’twas he!’ One Sunday I can well mind—a bass viol day that time, and Yeobright had brought his own.", "한 일요일이 똑똑히 기억나오—그땐 베이스 비올 날이었고, 요브라이트가 제 것을 가져왔지. 「리디아」"], ["’Twas the Hundred-and-thirty-third to ‘Lydia’; and when they’d come to ‘Ran down his beard and o’er his robes its costly moisture shed,’ neighbour Yeobright, who had just warmed to his work, drove his bow into them strings that glorious grand that he e’en a’most sawed the bass viol into two pieces.", "가락에 맞춘 시편 백서른셋이었는데, '그의 수염을 타고 흘러 그의 옷자락에 값진 기름이 떨어졌도다' 대목에 이르렀을 때, 막 일에 흥이 오른 이웃 요브라이트가 그 영광스럽고 장대하게 현에 활을 박아 넣는 통에 베이스 비올을 거의 두 동강 낼 뻔했소."], ["Every winder in church rattled as if ’twere a thunderstorm.", "교회의 창문이란 창문이 죄다 뇌우라도 친 듯 덜그럭거렸지."], ["Old Pa’son Williams lifted his hands in his great holy surplice as natural as if he’d been in common clothes, and seemed to say hisself, ‘Oh for such a man in our parish!’ But not a soul in Kingsbere could hold a candle to Yeobright.”", "늙은 윌리엄스 목사님은 거룩한 큰 제의를 입은 채로, 마치 평상복이라도 입은 양 자연스레 손을 들어 올리고는, 혼잣말로 이러는 듯했소, '오 우리 교구에도 저런 사람이 있었으면!' 하지만 킹스비어에서는 그 누구도 요브라이트의 발끝에도 못 미쳤소.\""]], [["“Was it quite safe when the winder shook?”", "\"창문이 흔들릴 때 정말 안전했어요?\""], ["Christian inquired.", "크리스천이 물었다."]], [["He received no answer, all for the moment sitting rapt in admiration of the performance described.", "그는 대답을 듣지 못했으니, 모두가 그 순간 묘사된 연주에 대한 감탄에 넋을 빼앗겨 있었던 까닭이다."], ["As with Farinelli’s singing before the princesses, Sheridan’s renowned Begum Speech, and other such examples, the fortunate condition of its being for ever lost to the world invested the deceased Mr. Yeobright’s tour de force on that memorable afternoon with a cumulative glory which comparative criticism, had that been possible, might considerably have shorn down.", "공주들 앞에서의 파리넬리의 노래, 셰리든의 그 유명한 베굼 연설, 그 밖의 그런 본보기들이 그렇듯, 그것이 세상에서 영영 사라져 버렸다는 다행스러운 처지가, 고故 요브라이트 씨의 그 잊지 못할 오후의 역작에 누적되는 영광을 입혀 주었으니, 만일 비교 비평이라는 것이 가능했더라면 그 영광을 상당히 깎아냈을 법도 했다."]], [["“He was the last you’d have expected to drop off in the prime of life,”", "\"한창나이에 세상 뜰 줄은 정말 아무도 생각 못 했지.\""], ["said Humphrey.", "험프리가 말했다."]], [["“Ah, well; he was looking for the earth some months afore he went.", "\"아, 글쎄. 가기 몇 달 전부터 그 양반은 땅을 바라보고 있었소."], ["At that time women used to run for smocks and gown-pieces at Greenhill Fair, and my wife that is now, being a long-legged slittering maid, hardly husband-high, went with the rest of the maidens, for ’a was a good runner afore she got so heavy.", "그 무렵엔 여인들이 그린힐 장에서 속옷이며 옷감을 타려고 달음박질을 하곤 했는데, 지금 내 안사람이 된 여자가, 그땐 다리만 긴 덜렁대는 처녀로 시집갈 키도 채 못 됐던 게, 다른 처녀들 틈에 끼어 갔소, 몸이 그리 무거워지기 전엔 잘 달렸거든."], ["When she came home I said—we were then just beginning to walk together—‘What have ye got, my honey?’ ‘I’ve won—well, I’ve won—a gown-piece,’ says she, her colours coming up in a moment.", "그 여자가 집에 왔을 때 내가 말했지—그때 우리는 막 함께 걷기 시작한 참이었소—'뭘 탔나, 내 꿀단지?' 하니까 '탔어요—저, 옷감을 탔어요'"], ["’Tis a smock for a crown, I thought; and so it turned out.", "하는데, 단번에 얼굴이 빨개지더군. 한 닢짜리 속옷이로구나, 했더니 과연 그렇더이다."], ["Ay, when I think what she’ll say to me now without a mossel of red in her face, it do seem strange that ’a wouldn’t say such a little thing then....", "아이고, 이젠 얼굴에 빨간 기 한 점 없이 내게 무슨 말이든 할 그 여자가, 그때는 그런 사소한 걸 말 못 하던 게 참 이상해 보이오…… 어쨌든, 그 여자가 말을 이었는데, 그게 내가 이 이야길 꺼낸 까닭이오, '음, 무슨 옷을 탔든, 흰 거든 무늬진 거든, 남 보일 거든 못 보일 거든'"], ["However, then she went on, and that’s what made me bring up the story, ‘Well, whatever clothes I’ve won, white or figured, for eyes to see or for eyes not to see’ (’a could do a pretty stroke of modesty in those days), ‘I’d sooner have lost it than have seen what I have. Poor Mr.", "(그땐 제법 예쁘장하게 수줍은 척을 할 줄 알았지), '그걸 잃었으면 잃었지 내가 본 걸 보지나 말 걸 그랬어요."], ["Yeobright was took bad directly he reached the fair ground, and was forced to go home again.’ That was the last time he ever went out of the parish.”", "가엾은 요브라이트 씨가 장터에 닿자마자 탈이 나서, 도로 집에 가야 했어요.' 그게 그 양반이 교구 밖으로 나간 마지막이었소.\""]], [["“’A faltered on from one day to another, and then we heard he was gone.”", "\"하루하루 비틀비틀 버티더니, 이윽고 갔다는 소릴 들었지.\""]], [["“D’ye think he had great pain when ’a died?”", "\"죽을 때 많이 아팠을까요?\""], ["said Christian.", "크리스천이 말했다."]], [["“O no—quite different.", "\"오 아니—전혀 달랐소."], ["Nor any pain of mind.", "마음의 고통도 없었지."], ["He was lucky enough to be God A’mighty’s own man.”", "운 좋게도 전능하신 하느님 당신의 사람이었으니까.\""]], [["“And other folk—d’ye think ’twill be much pain to ’em, Mister Fairway?”", "\"그럼 다른 사람들은—그들에겐 많이 아플까요, 페어웨이 씨?\""]], [["“That depends on whether they be afeard.”", "\"그야 두려워하느냐 안 하느냐에 달렸지.\""]], [["“I bain’t afeard at all, I thank God!”", "\"난 하나도 안 두려워요, 하느님 덕분에!\""], ["said Christian strenuously.", "크리스천이 힘주어 말했다."], ["“I’m glad I bain’t, for then ’twon’t pain me....", "\"안 두려워서 다행이에요, 그럼 아프지 않을 테니까……"], ["I don’t think I be afeard—or if I be I can’t help it, and I don’t deserve to suffer.", "내가 두려워하는 것 같진 않아—설사 두렵다 해도 어쩔 수 없는 거고, 난 고통받을 만하지 않아."], ["I wish I was not afeard at all!”", "아예 안 두려웠으면 좋겠어요!\""]], [["There was a solemn silence, and looking from the window, which was unshuttered and unblinded, Timothy said, “Well, what a fess little bonfire that one is, out by Cap’n Vye’s!", "엄숙한 침묵이 흘렀고, 덧창도 차양도 없는 창밖을 내다보며 티머시가 말했다. \"거참, 바이 선장 댁 밖에 있는 저 불은 어찌 저리 작고 잘난 모닥불인지!"], ["’Tis burning just the same now as ever, upon my life.”", "맹세코, 여태 그랬듯이 지금도 똑같이 타고 있구먼.\""]], [["All glances went through the window, and nobody noticed that Wildeve disguised a brief, telltale look.", "모든 눈길이 창밖을 향했고, 와일디브가 짧고 속내를 드러내는 눈빛을 감추는 것은 아무도 눈치채지 못했다."], ["Far away up the sombre valley of heath, and to the right of Rainbarrow, could indeed be seen the light, small, but steady and persistent as before.", "저 멀리 음울한 황야 골짜기 위로, 레인배로의 오른편에, 과연 그 불빛이 보였으니, 작지만 전과 다름없이 한결같고 끈질겼다."]], [["“It was lighted before ours was,”", "\"우리 것보다 먼저 붙은 거요.\""], ["Fairway continued; “and yet every one in the country round is out afore ’n.”", "페어웨이가 이어 말했다. \"그런데도 온 동네가 다 그것보다 먼저 꺼지는구려.\""]], [["“Perhaps there’s meaning in it!”", "\"어쩌면 거기 무슨 뜻이 있는지도 몰라요!\""], ["murmured Christian.", "크리스천이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“How meaning?”", "\"무슨 뜻?\""], ["said Wildeve sharply.", "와일디브가 날카롭게 말했다."]], [["Christian was too scattered to reply, and Timothy helped him.", "크리스천은 너무 산만해 대답하지 못했고, 티머시가 거들었다."]], [["“He means, sir, that the lonesome dark-eyed creature up there that some say is a witch—ever I should call a fine young woman such a name—is always up to some odd conceit or other; and so perhaps ’tis she.”", "\"이 사람 말은, 나리, 저 위에 사는 외롭고 검은 눈동자의 여자, 더러 마녀라 하는 그—참한 젊은 여자를 두고 내가 그런 이름으로 부를 일이야 결코 없겠소만—그 여자가 늘 이상한 변덕을 부린다는 거요, 그러니 어쩌면 그게 그 여자일지도 모른다는 거지요.\""]], [["“I’d be very glad to ask her in wedlock, if she’d hae me and take the risk of her wild dark eyes ill-wishing me,”", "\"그 처녀가 나를 받아 주고, 그 거친 검은 눈으로 내게 저주를 거는 위험을 감수해 준다면, 기꺼이 청혼이라도 하겠소.\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle staunchly.", "캔틀 영감이 꿋꿋이 말했다."]], [["“Don’t ye say it, Father!”", "\"그런 말 마세요, 아버지!\""], ["implored Christian.", "크리스천이 애원했다."]], [["“Well, be dazed if he who do marry the maid won’t hae an uncommon picture for his best parlour,”", "\"허, 저 처녀와 혼인하는 자는 제일 좋은 응접실에 둘 보기 드문 그림을 얻는 셈이니, 정신이 다 아찔할 거요.\""], ["said Fairway in a liquid tone, placing down the cup of mead at the end of a good pull.", "페어웨이가, 한 모금 흠뻑 들이켠 끝에 벌꿀술 잔을 내려놓으며 유들유들한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“And a partner as deep as the North Star,”", "\"게다가 북극성처럼 속이 깊은 짝이지.\""], ["said Sam, taking up the cup and finishing the little that remained.", "샘이 잔을 집어 들어 남은 조금을 비우며 말했다."], ["“Well, really, now I think we must be moving,”", "\"음, 정말이지, 이제 우리 그만 움직여야 할 것 같소.\""], ["said Humphrey, observing the emptiness of the vessel.", "그릇이 빈 것을 보고 험프리가 말했다."]], [["“But we’ll gie ’em another song?”", "\"하지만 한 곡 더 불러 줘야지요?\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle.", "캔틀 영감이 말했다."], ["“I’m as full of notes as a bird!”", "\"나는 새처럼 가락이 가득하다오!\""]], [["“Thank you, Grandfer,”", "\"고맙소, 영감.\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."], ["“But we will not trouble you now.", "\"하지만 지금은 폐를 끼치지 않으리다."], ["Some other day must do for that—when I have a party.”", "그건 다른 날—내가 잔치를 벌일 때 하기로 합시다.\""]], [["“Be jown’d if I don’t learn ten new songs for’t, or I won’t learn a line!”", "\"그러려면 새 노래를 열 곡 안 배우면 내가 사람도 아니지, 한 소절도 안 배울 테면 안 배울지언정!\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle.", "캔틀 영감이 말했다."], ["“And you may be sure I won’t disappoint ye by biding away, Mr. Wildeve.”", "\"그리고 와일디브 씨, 내 안 와서 실망시키는 일은 결코 없을 테니 안심하시오.\""]], [["“I quite believe you,”", "\"믿어 의심치 않소.\""], ["said that gentleman.", "그 신사가 말했다."]], [["All then took their leave, wishing their entertainer long life and happiness as a married man, with recapitulations which occupied some time.", "그러고는 모두 작별을 고하며, 혼인한 사내로서 만수무강과 행복을 빌었으니, 그 되풀이가 한참을 잡아먹었다."], ["Wildeve attended them to the door, beyond which the deep-dyed upward stretch of heath stood awaiting them, an amplitude of darkness reigning from their feet almost to the zenith, where a definite form first became visible in the lowering forehead of Rainbarrow.", "와일디브는 문까지 그들을 배웅했고, 그 너머로는 짙게 물든 황야가 위로 뻗어 그들을 기다리고 있었으니, 그들의 발치에서 거의 천정天頂까지 광막한 어둠이 군림했고, 그곳 레인배로의 찌푸린 이마에서 비로소 하나의 또렷한 형체가 처음으로 눈에 들어왔다."], ["Diving into the dense obscurity in a line headed by Sam the turf-cutter, they pursued their trackless way home.", "잔디 베는 샘을 선두로 빽빽한 어둠 속으로 일렬로 뛰어들어, 그들은 길도 없는 귀갓길을 더듬어 갔다."]], [["When the scratching of the furze against their leggings had fainted upon the ear, Wildeve returned to the room where he had left Thomasin and her aunt.", "가시금작화가 그들의 각반을 긁는 소리가 귀에서 잦아들자, 와일디브는 토머신과 그 이모를 두고 나왔던 방으로 돌아왔다."], ["The women were gone.", "여인들은 사라지고 없었다."]], [["They could only have left the house in one way, by the back window; and this was open.", "그들이 집을 떠날 수 있는 길은 단 하나, 뒤쪽 창문뿐이었으니, 그 창이 열려 있었다."]], [["Wildeve laughed to himself, remained a moment thinking, and idly returned to the front room.", "와일디브는 혼자 웃으며 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 무심코 앞방으로 돌아왔다."], ["Here his glance fell upon a bottle of wine which stood on the mantelpiece.", "거기서 그의 눈길이 벽난로 선반에 놓인 포도주 한 병에 떨어졌다."], ["“Ah—old Dowden!”", "\"아—다우든 노인네!\""], ["he murmured; and going to the kitchen door shouted, “Is anybody here who can take something to old Dowden?”", "그가 중얼거리고는 부엌문으로 가 외쳤다. \"다우든 노인네한테 뭐 좀 갖다줄 사람 여기 없나?\""]], [["There was no reply.", "대답이 없었다."], ["The room was empty, the lad who acted as his factotum having gone to bed.", "방은 비어 있었으니, 그의 잡일을 맡은 머슴 아이가 잠자리에 든 까닭이었다."], ["Wildeve came back, put on his hat, took the bottle, and left the house, turning the key in the door, for there was no guest at the inn tonight.", "와일디브는 돌아와 모자를 쓰고, 병을 집어 들고는, 문을 잠그고 집을 나섰으니, 오늘 밤 여관에는 손님이 없었기 때문이다."], ["As soon as he was on the road the little bonfire on Mistover Knap again met his eye.", "길에 들어서자마자 미스토버 놉의 작은 모닥불이 다시 그의 눈에 들어왔다."]], [["“Still waiting, are you, my lady?”", "\"아직도 기다리시오, 아가씨?\""], ["he murmured.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["However, he did not proceed that way just then; but leaving the hill to the left of him, he stumbled over a rutted road that brought him to a cottage which, like all other habitations on the heath at this hour, was only saved from being visible by a faint shine from its bedroom window.", "그러나 그는 당장 그쪽으로 가지는 않았으니, 언덕을 왼편에 두고, 바퀴 자국 팬 길을 비틀비틀 넘어 한 오두막에 이르렀는데, 이 시각 황야의 다른 모든 거처와 마찬가지로 침실 창에서 새어 나오는 희미한 빛 덕분에 겨우 보이는 신세를 면한 곳이었다."], ["This house was the home of Olly Dowden, the besom-maker, and he entered.", "이 집은 빗자루 만드는 올리 다우든의 보금자리였고, 그는 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["The lower room was in darkness; but by feeling his way he found a table, whereon he placed the bottle, and a minute later emerged again upon the heath.", "아래층 방은 어둠에 잠겨 있었으나, 손으로 더듬어 탁자 하나를 찾아 그 위에 병을 놓고는, 잠시 뒤 다시 황야로 나왔다."], ["He stood and looked northeast at the undying little fire—high up above him, though not so high as Rainbarrow.", "그는 서서, 사그라들지 않는 작은 불을 북동쪽으로 바라보았으니—그의 머리 위로 높이 있었으나, 레인배로만큼 높지는 않았다."]], [["We have been told what happens when a woman deliberates; and the epigram is not always terminable with woman, provided that one be in the case, and that a fair one.", "여인이 망설일 때 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 우리는 익히 들어 왔거니와, 사정에 한 사람이 끼어 있고 그것이 어여쁜 이라면, 그 경구가 늘 여인에게서 끝나는 것은 아니다."], ["Wildeve stood, and stood longer, and breathed perplexedly, and then said to himself with resignation, “Yes—by Heaven, I must go to her, I suppose!”", "와일디브는 서 있다가, 더 오래 서 있다가, 어리둥절한 듯 숨을 쉬다가, 이윽고 체념하여 혼잣말했다. \"그래—맙소사, 그녀에게 가야겠지, 가는 수밖에!\""]], [["Instead of turning in the direction of home he pressed on rapidly by a path under Rainbarrow towards what was evidently a signal light.", "집 쪽으로 돌아서는 대신 그는 레인배로 아래 오솔길을 따라, 분명 신호인 듯한 불빛을 향해 빠르게 나아갔다."]], [["VI. The Figure against the Sky\nWhen the whole Egdon concourse had left the site of the bonfire to its accustomed loneliness, a closely wrapped female figure approached the barrow from that quarter of the heath in which the little fire lay.", "에그던의 온 무리가 모닥불 자리를 그 익숙한 고독에 내맡기고 떠나자, 단단히 몸을 감싼 한 여인의 형체가 그 작은 불이 놓인 황야 쪽에서 봉분으로 다가왔다."], ["Had the reddleman been watching he might have recognized her as the woman who had first stood there so singularly, and vanished at the approach of strangers.", "레들맨이 지켜보고 있었다면 그녀가, 처음에 그토록 기이하게 그곳에 서 있다가 낯선 이들이 다가오자 사라졌던 그 여인임을 알아보았을지도 모른다."], ["She ascended to her old position at the top, where the red coals of the perishing fire greeted her like living eyes in the corpse of day.", "그녀는 꼭대기의 옛 자리로 올라갔으니, 그곳에서는 사그라드는 불의 붉은 잉걸이 한낮의 주검 속 살아 있는 눈처럼 그녀를 맞았다."], ["There she stood still around her stretching the vast night atmosphere, whose incomplete darkness in comparison with the total darkness of the heath below it might have represented a venial beside a mortal sin.", "거기 그녀는 가만히 서 있었고, 둘레로는 광막한 밤의 대기가 펼쳐졌으니, 그 아래 황야의 온전한 어둠에 견주면 그 불완전한 어둠은 죽을죄 곁의 가벼운 죄에 견줄 만했다."]], [["That she was tall and straight in build, that she was lady-like in her movements, was all that could be learnt of her just now, her form being wrapped in a shawl folded in the old cornerwise fashion, and her head in a large kerchief, a protection not superfluous at this hour and place.", "그녀가 키 크고 곧은 몸매라는 것, 그 거동이 귀부인답다는 것, 그것만이 지금 그녀에 대해 알 수 있는 전부였으니, 그 몸은 옛날식 대각선으로 접은 숄에 감싸여 있었고, 머리는 큰 머릿수건으로 싸여 있었던바, 이 시각 이 자리에서는 군더더기가 아닌 보호였다."], ["Her back was towards the wind, which blew from the northwest; but whether she had avoided that aspect because of the chilly gusts which played about her exceptional position, or because her interest lay in the southeast, did not at first appear.", "그녀의 등은 바람을 향하고 있었으니, 바람은 북서쪽에서 불어왔다. 그러나 그녀가 그쪽을 피한 것이 그 남다른 자리에 휘도는 차가운 돌풍 때문이었는지, 아니면 그녀의 관심이 남동쪽에 있었기 때문이었는지는 처음엔 드러나지 않았다."]], [["Her reason for standing so dead still as the pivot of this circle of heath-country was just as obscure.", "이 황야 고장 둘레의 축처럼 그토록 죽은 듯이 가만히 서 있는 그녀의 까닭 또한 그만큼 모호했다."], ["Her extraordinary fixity, her conspicuous loneliness, her heedlessness of night, betokened among other things an utter absence of fear.", "그 유별난 부동不動, 그 두드러진 고독, 밤에 대한 무심함은, 다른 무엇보다도 두려움이 전혀 없음을 드러냈다."], ["A tract of country unaltered from that sinister condition which made Cæsar anxious every year to get clear of its glooms before the autumnal equinox, a kind of landscape and weather which leads travellers from the South to describe our island as Homer’s Cimmerian land, was not, on the face of it, friendly to women.", "카이사르가 해마다 추분 전에 그 음울함에서 벗어나기를 애태웠던 그 불길한 상태에서 조금도 변하지 않은 땅, 남쪽에서 온 나그네들로 하여금 우리 섬을 호메로스의 키메르 땅이라 일컫게 하는 그런 풍광과 날씨는, 겉보기에 여인에게 살가운 곳이 아니었다."]], [["It might reasonably have been supposed that she was listening to the wind, which rose somewhat as the night advanced, and laid hold of the attention.", "그녀가 바람에 귀 기울이고 있다고 여겼음 직도 했으니, 밤이 깊어 갈수록 바람은 다소 거세져 주의를 사로잡았다."], ["The wind, indeed, seemed made for the scene, as the scene seemed made for the hour.", "과연 바람은 그 풍경을 위해 빚어진 듯했으니, 그 풍경이 그 시각을 위해 빚어진 듯했던 것과 같았다."], ["Part of its tone was quite special; what was heard there could be heard nowhere else.", "그 음색의 일부는 자못 특별했으니, 거기서 들리는 것은 다른 어디서도 들을 수 없는 것이었다."], ["Gusts in innumerable series followed each other from the northwest, and when each one of them raced past the sound of its progress resolved into three.", "헤아릴 수 없이 잇따른 돌풍이 북서쪽에서 꼬리를 물고 따라왔고, 그 하나하나가 내달아 지나갈 때마다 그 진행의 소리는 세 갈래로 갈라졌다."], ["Treble, tenor, and bass notes were to be found therein.", "거기엔 고음, 중음, 저음이 들어 있었다."], ["The general ricochet of the whole over pits and prominences had the gravest pitch of the chime.", "구덩이와 두두룩한 곳을 넘는 그 전체의 두루뭉술한 반향은 그 화음에서 가장 묵직한 가락이었다."], ["Next there could be heard the baritone buzz of a holly tree.", "다음으로 호랑가시나무 한 그루의 바리톤 윙윙거림이 들렸다."], ["Below these in force, above them in pitch, a dwindled voice strove hard at a husky tune, which was the peculiar local sound alluded to.", "이것들보다 세기로는 아래고 음높이로는 위인, 잦아든 한 목소리가 쉰 가락을 힘겹게 자아내고 있었으니, 그것이 앞서 일컬은 그 독특한 고장의 소리였다."], ["Thinner and less immediately traceable than the other two, it was far more impressive than either.", "다른 두 소리보다 가늘고 곧장 짚어 내기 어려웠으나, 그 어느 것보다도 훨씬 인상적이었다."], ["In it lay what may be called the linguistic peculiarity of the heath; and being audible nowhere on earth off a heath, it afforded a shadow of reason for the woman’s tenseness, which continued as unbroken as ever.", "거기엔 이른바 황야의 언어적 독특함이 깃들어 있었고, 황야를 벗어난 지상 어디서도 들을 수 없는 것이었으니, 여인의 긴장에 한 가닥 까닭의 그림자를 드리웠던바, 그 긴장은 여전히 깨어지지 않은 채였다."]], [["Throughout the blowing of these plaintive November winds that note bore a great resemblance to the ruins of human song which remain to the throat of fourscore and ten.", "이 구슬픈 십일월 바람이 불어 대는 내내 그 소리는 아흔 노인의 목구멍에 남은 인간 노래의 잔해와 사뭇 닮아 있었다."], ["It was a worn whisper, dry and papery, and it brushed so distinctly across the ear that, by the accustomed, the material minutiæ in which it originated could be realized as by touch.", "그것은 닳아빠진 속삭임으로, 메마르고 종잇장 같았으며, 어찌나 또렷이 귀를 스치는지, 익숙한 이라면 그 소리가 비롯된 물질적 미세함을 손으로 만지듯 실감할 수 있었다."], ["It was the united products of infinitesimal vegetable causes, and these were neither stems, leaves, fruit, blades, prickles, lichen, nor moss.", "그것은 무한히 작은 식물적 원인들이 한데 빚어낸 산물이었으니, 그것들은 줄기도, 잎도, 열매도, 풀잎도, 가시도, 지의류도, 이끼도 아니었다."], ["They were the mummied heathbells of the past summer, originally tender and purple, now washed colourless by Michaelmas rains, and dried to dead skins by October suns.", "그것들은 지난여름의 미라가 된 헤더 종꽃이었으니, 본디 여리고 자줏빛이었던 것이 이제 미카엘 축일의 비에 빛이 다 씻겨 나가고, 시월의 햇볕에 죽은 가죽처럼 말라붙은 것이었다."], ["So low was an individual sound from these that a combination of hundreds only just emerged from silence, and the myriads of the whole declivity reached the woman’s ear but as a shrivelled and intermittent recitative.", "그 하나하나에서 나는 소리가 어찌나 낮은지 수백 개가 어우러져야 겨우 침묵에서 떠올랐고, 비탈 전체의 무수한 것들이 여인의 귀에 닿는 것은 쪼그라들고 끊겼다 이어지는 영창詠唱일 따름이었다."], ["Yet scarcely a single accent among the many afloat tonight could have such power to impress a listener with thoughts of its origin.", "그러나 오늘 밤 떠도는 그 많은 가락 가운데 그것만큼 듣는 이에게 그 비롯됨을 떠올리게 할 힘을 지닌 것은 거의 없었다."], ["One inwardly saw the infinity of those combined multitudes; and perceived that each of the tiny trumpets was seized on entered, scoured and emerged from by the wind as thoroughly as if it were as vast as a crater.", "마음의 눈으로 그 어우러진 무리의 무한함이 보였고, 그 작디작은 나팔 하나하나가, 마치 분화구만큼이나 거대하기라도 한 양 바람에 붙들리고, 들어가지고, 훑어지고, 빠져나가지는 것이 느껴졌다."]], [["“The spirit moved them.”", "\"성령이 그들을 움직였다.\""], ["A meaning of the phrase forced itself upon the attention; and an emotional listener’s fetichistic mood might have ended in one of more advanced quality.", "그 구절의 한 뜻이 절로 마음에 떠올랐고, 감정에 휘둘린 듣는 이의 물신적 기분은 한층 진전된 무엇으로 끝맺었을지도 모른다."], ["It was not, after all, that the left-hand expanse of old blooms spoke, or the right-hand, or those of the slope in front; but it was the single person of something else speaking through each at once.", "결국 말하는 것은 왼편으로 펼쳐진 묵은 꽃밭도, 오른편의 것도, 앞쪽 비탈의 것도 아니었으니, 그것은 그 모두를 통해 한꺼번에 말하는 어떤 다른 것의 단일한 인격이었다."]], [["Suddenly, on the barrow, there mingled with all this wild rhetoric of night a sound which modulated so naturally into the rest that its beginning and ending were hardly to be distinguished.", "문득, 봉분 위에서, 이 모든 밤의 거친 수사修辭에 어떤 소리가 섞여 들었으니, 어찌나 자연스레 나머지에 녹아드는지 그 시작과 끝을 가려내기 어려울 정도였다."], ["The bluffs, and the bushes, and the heather-bells had broken silence; at last, so did the woman; and her articulation was but as another phrase of the same discourse as theirs.", "벼랑도, 덤불도, 헤더 종꽃도 침묵을 깼던바, 마침내 여인도 그러했으니, 그녀의 발음은 그들의 것과 같은 담화의 또 다른 한 구절일 따름이었다."], ["Thrown out on the winds it became twined in with them, and with them it flew away.", "바람에 내던져져 그것은 바람과 뒤얽혔고, 바람과 함께 날아가 버렸다."]], [["What she uttered was a lengthened sighing, apparently at something in her mind which had led to her presence here.", "그녀가 낸 것은 길게 끄는 한숨이었으니, 분명 그녀를 이곳에 오게 한 마음속 무언가에서 비롯된 듯했다."], ["There was a spasmodic abandonment about it as if, in allowing herself to utter the sound the woman’s brain had authorized what it could not regulate.", "거기엔 발작적인 자포자기가 깃들어 있었으니, 마치 그 소리를 내도록 스스로를 내버려 둠으로써 여인의 머리가 다스릴 수 없는 것을 인가認可하기라도 한 듯했다."], ["One point was evident in this; that she had been existing in a suppressed state, and not in one of languor, or stagnation.", "한 가지는 이로써 분명했으니, 그녀가 노곤함이나 침체가 아니라 억눌린 상태로 지내 왔다는 것이었다."]], [["Far away down the valley the faint shine from the window of the inn still lasted on; and a few additional moments proved that the window, or what was within it, had more to do with the woman’s sigh than had either her own actions or the scene immediately around.", "저 멀리 골짜기 아래로 여관 창의 희미한 빛이 여전히 이어지고 있었으니, 몇 순간이 더 지나자 그 창이, 혹은 그 안에 있는 것이, 여인의 한숨과 더 관련이 있음이 드러났으니, 그녀 자신의 행동이나 바로 둘레의 풍경보다도 그러했다."], ["She lifted her left hand, which held a closed telescope.", "그녀가 왼손을 들어 올렸으니, 접힌 망원경을 쥐고 있었다."], ["This she rapidly extended, as if she were well accustomed to the operation, and raising it to her eye directed it towards the light beaming from the inn.", "이것을 그녀는 익숙한 동작인 양 잽싸게 폈고, 눈에 갖다 대어 여관에서 비쳐 나오는 불빛 쪽으로 겨누었다."]], [["The handkerchief which had hooded her head was now a little thrown back, her face being somewhat elevated.", "머리를 덮었던 손수건이 이제 조금 뒤로 젖혀졌고, 얼굴이 다소 들려 있었다."], ["A profile was visible against the dull monochrome of cloud around her; and it was as though side shadows from the features of Sappho and Mrs. Siddons had converged upwards from the tomb to form an image like neither but suggesting both.", "둘레의 흐릿한 단색 구름을 배경으로 옆얼굴이 드러났으니, 그것은 마치 사포와 시던스 부인의 이목구비에서 비롯된 옆그림자들이 무덤에서 위로 모여들어, 그 어느 쪽과도 닮지 않았으나 둘 다를 떠올리게 하는 형상을 이룬 듯했다."], ["This, however, was mere superficiality.", "그러나 이는 한낱 겉모습일 뿐이었다."], ["In respect of character a face may make certain admissions by its outline; but it fully confesses only in its changes.", "성품에 관한 한, 얼굴은 그 윤곽으로 어떤 것을 시인할 수는 있으나, 온전히 고백하는 것은 오직 그 변화에서뿐이다."], ["So much is this the case that what is called the play of the features often helps more in understanding a man or woman than the earnest labours of all the other members together.", "그러한즉 이른바 이목구비의 움직임이야말로, 흔히 다른 모든 부위의 진지한 수고를 합친 것보다도 한 사람을 이해하는 데 더 도움이 된다."], ["Thus the night revealed little of her whose form it was embracing, for the mobile parts of her countenance could not be seen.", "하여 밤은, 그 형체를 품에 안고 있으면서도, 그 임자에 대해 거의 드러내지 못했으니, 그녀 얼굴의 움직이는 부분들은 보이지 않았던 까닭이다."]], [["At last she gave up her spying attitude, closed the telescope, and turned to the decaying embers.", "마침내 그녀는 엿보는 자세를 거두고, 망원경을 접고, 사그라드는 잉걸불 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["From these no appreciable beams now radiated, except when a more than usually smart gust brushed over their faces and raised a fitful glow which came and went like the blush of a girl.", "거기서 이제 이렇다 할 빛줄기는 뻗어 나오지 않았으니, 다만 여느 때보다 매서운 돌풍이 그 낯을 쓸고 지나가며, 소녀의 홍조처럼 왔다 가는 변덕스러운 불빛을 일으킬 때만은 예외였다."], ["She stooped over the silent circle, and selecting from the brands a piece of stick which bore the largest live coal at its end, brought it to where she had been standing before.", "그녀는 고요한 원圓 위로 몸을 굽혀, 타다 남은 가지들 중에서 끝에 가장 큰 잉걸을 단 막대기 하나를 골라, 앞서 서 있던 자리로 가져갔다."]], [["She held the brand to the ground, blowing the red coal with her mouth at the same time; till it faintly illuminated the sod, and revealed a small object, which turned out to be an hourglass, though she wore a watch.", "그녀는 막대기를 땅에 대고, 동시에 입으로 그 붉은 잉걸을 불었으니, 마침내 그것이 잔디를 희미하게 비추어 작은 물체 하나를 드러냈는데, 알고 보니 모래시계였다, 그녀가 손목시계를 차고 있었는데도."], ["She blew long enough to show that the sand had all slipped through.", "그녀는 모래가 다 흘러내렸음을 보일 만큼 오래 불었다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["she said, as if surprised.", "그녀가 놀란 듯이 말했다."]], [["The light raised by her breath had been very fitful, and a momentary irradiation of flesh was all that it had disclosed of her face.", "그녀의 입김으로 일어난 빛은 무척 변덕스러웠고, 그것이 그녀 얼굴에서 드러낸 것이라곤 한순간 살갗의 반짝임이 전부였다."], ["That consisted of two matchless lips and a cheek only, her head being still enveloped.", "그것은 둘도 없는 두 입술과 한쪽 뺨뿐이었으니, 머리는 여전히 싸여 있었다."], ["She threw away the stick, took the glass in her hand, the telescope under her arm, and moved on.", "그녀는 막대기를 던져 버리고, 모래시계를 손에 들고, 망원경을 겨드랑이에 끼고, 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["Along the ridge ran a faint foot-track, which the lady followed.", "능선을 따라 희미한 발자취가 나 있었고, 부인은 그것을 따라갔다."], ["Those who knew it well called it a path; and, while a mere visitor would have passed it unnoticed even by day, the regular haunters of the heath were at no loss for it at midnight.", "그것을 잘 아는 이들은 그것을 길이라 불렀거니와, 한낱 방문객이라면 대낮에도 알아채지 못하고 지나쳤을 그것을, 황야를 늘 드나드는 이들은 한밤중에도 어렵잖게 찾았다."], ["The whole secret of following these incipient paths, when there was not light enough in the atmosphere to show a turnpike road, lay in the development of the sense of touch in the feet, which comes with years of night-rambling in little-trodden spots.", "대기에 큰길을 비출 만한 빛조차 없을 때 이 갓 생긴 길들을 따라가는 비결은 온통, 발의 촉각을 발달시키는 데 있었으니, 그것은 사람 발길이 드문 곳에서 여러 해 밤을 쏘다녀야 생기는 것이었다."], ["To a walker practised in such places a difference between impact on maiden herbage, and on the crippled stalks of a slight footway, is perceptible through the thickest boot or shoe.", "그런 곳에 단련된 보행자에게는, 처녀지의 풀밭을 디딜 때와 자그마한 오솔길의 짓밟힌 줄기를 디딜 때의 차이가, 아무리 두꺼운 장화나 신발을 통해서도 느껴졌다."]], [["The solitary figure who walked this beat took no notice of the windy tune still played on the dead heathbells.", "이 길을 걷는 외로운 형체는 죽은 헤더 종꽃 위에서 여전히 연주되는 바람의 가락에 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["She did not turn her head to look at a group of dark creatures further on, who fled from her presence as she skirted a ravine where they fed.", "그녀는 더 앞쪽의 검은 짐승 무리를 보려고 고개를 돌리지도 않았으니, 그것들은 그녀가 풀을 뜯는 그것들 곁의 협곡을 에둘러 갈 때 그녀의 존재 앞에서 달아났다."], ["They were about a score of the small wild ponies known as heath-croppers.", "그것들은 황야 망아지라 불리는 작은 야생 조랑말 스무 마리쯤이었다."], ["They roamed at large on the undulations of Egdon, but in numbers too few to detract much from the solitude.", "그것들은 에그던의 굽이진 곳을 멋대로 누볐으나, 그 수가 너무 적어 고독을 크게 덜지는 못했다."]], [["The pedestrian noticed nothing just now, and a clue to her abstraction was afforded by a trivial incident.", "보행자는 지금 아무것도 알아채지 못했고, 그녀의 골똘함에 대한 한 가닥 실마리는 사소한 사건으로 드러났다."], ["A bramble caught hold of her skirt, and checked her progress.", "가시나무가 그녀의 치맛자락을 붙들어 걸음을 막았다."], ["Instead of putting it off and hastening along, she yielded herself up to the pull, and stood passively still.", "그것을 떼어 내고 서둘러 가는 대신, 그녀는 그 당김에 몸을 내맡기고 가만히 멈춰 섰다."], ["When she began to extricate herself it was by turning round and round, and so unwinding the prickly switch.", "그녀가 빠져나오기 시작한 것은 빙빙 돌아 그 가시 돋친 회초리를 풀어내는 식이었다."], ["She was in a desponding reverie.", "그녀는 풀 죽은 몽상에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["Her course was in the direction of the small undying fire which had drawn the attention of the men on Rainbarrow and of Wildeve in the valley below.", "그녀의 길은, 레인배로의 사내들과 아래 골짜기의 와일디브의 주의를 끌었던 그 작고 사그라들지 않는 불을 향한 것이었다."], ["A faint illumination from its rays began to glow upon her face, and the fire soon revealed itself to be lit, not on the level ground, but on a salient corner or redan of earth, at the junction of two converging bank fences.", "그 빛줄기에서 나온 희미한 빛이 그녀 얼굴에 어리기 시작했고, 이내 그 불은 평지가 아니라, 두 둑이 모여드는 어귀의 흙으로 된 돌출한 모서리, 곧 능보稜堡 위에 피워졌음이 드러났다."], ["Outside was a ditch, dry except immediately under the fire, where there was a large pool, bearded all round by heather and rushes.", "바깥에는 도랑이 있었으니, 불 바로 아래만 빼고는 말라 있었는데, 그곳엔 둘레가 온통 헤더와 골풀로 수염처럼 둘린 큰 웅덩이가 있었다."], ["In the smooth water of the pool the fire appeared upside down.", "웅덩이의 매끄러운 물에는 불이 거꾸로 비쳤다."]], [["The banks meeting behind were bare of a hedge, save such as was formed by disconnected tufts of furze, standing upon stems along the top, like impaled heads above a city wall.", "뒤에서 만나는 둑들은 산울타리가 없었으니, 다만 꼭대기를 따라 줄기 위에 선 띄엄띄엄한 가시금작화 덤불이, 도성 벽 위에 꽂힌 머리들처럼 이룬 것을 빼면 그러했다."], ["A white mast, fitted up with spars and other nautical tackle, could be seen rising against the dark clouds whenever the flames played brightly enough to reach it.", "활대며 그 밖의 항해 장비를 갖춘 흰 돛대 하나가, 불길이 거기 닿을 만큼 환히 일렁일 때마다 어두운 구름을 배경으로 솟아 있는 것이 보였다."], ["Altogether the scene had much the appearance of a fortification upon which had been kindled a beacon fire.", "통틀어 그 광경은 봉화를 피워 올린 요새와 사뭇 닮은 모습이었다."]], [["Nobody was visible; but ever and anon a whitish something moved above the bank from behind, and vanished again.", "아무도 보이지 않았으나, 이따금 희끄무레한 무언가가 뒤편 둑 위로 움직였다가 다시 사라졌다."], ["This was a small human hand, in the act of lifting pieces of fuel into the fire, but for all that could be seen the hand, like that which troubled Belshazzar, was there alone.", "그것은 불 속에 땔감을 들어 올려 넣는 작은 사람 손이었으나, 보이는 것만으로는 그 손은, 벨사살을 괴롭히던 그 손처럼, 홀로 거기 있었다."], ["Occasionally an ember rolled off the bank, and dropped with a hiss into the pool.", "때때로 잉걸 하나가 둑에서 굴러떨어져 쉭 소리를 내며 웅덩이로 떨어졌다."]], [["At one side of the pool rough steps built of clods enabled everyone who wished to do so to mount the bank; which the woman did.", "웅덩이 한쪽에는 흙덩이로 쌓은 거친 계단이 있어, 둑에 오르고 싶은 누구든 오를 수 있게 했으니, 여인이 그렇게 했다."], ["Within was a paddock in an uncultivated state, though bearing evidence of having once been tilled; but the heath and fern had insidiously crept in, and were reasserting their old supremacy.", "안쪽에는\n언젠가 경작된 적이 있음을 보여주는 흔적은 남아 있으나 지금은 묵정밭으로 방치된 작은 목초지였는데, 히스와 고사리가 슬그머니 기어들어와 옛 패권을 다시금 주장하고 있었다."], ["Further ahead were dimly visible an irregular dwelling-house, garden, and outbuildings, backed by a clump of firs.", "더 앞쪽으로는 불규칙한 모양의 주택과 정원, 부속 건물이 전나무 덤불을 배경으로 어렴풋이 보였다."]], [["The young lady—for youth had revealed its presence in her buoyant bound up the bank—walked along the top instead of descending inside, and came to the corner where the fire was burning.", "그 젊은 여인은—둑 위로 가뿐히 뛰어오르는 그 몸짓에서 젊음이 제 존재를 드러냈으니—둑 안쪽으로 내려가는 대신 꼭대기를 따라 걸어 불이 타오르는 모퉁이에 이르렀다."], ["One reason for the permanence of the blaze was now manifest: the fuel consisted of hard pieces of wood, cleft and sawn—the knotty boles of old thorn trees which grew in twos and threes about the hillsides.", "불길이 좀처럼 사그라들지 않는 이유 하나가 이제 분명해졌으니, 땔감이 쪼개고 톱질한 단단한 나무토막—언덕 비탈 여기저기에 둘씩 셋씩 자라는 늙은 가시나무의 옹이진 밑동—으로 이루어져 있었던 것이다."], ["A yet unconsumed pile of these lay in the inner angle of the bank; and from this corner the upturned face of a little boy greeted her eyes.", "아직 다 타지 않은 이런 나무들이 둑 안쪽 모서리에 무더기로 쌓여 있었고, 그 모퉁이에서 한 어린 소년이 얼굴을 들어 그녀를 맞이했다."], ["He was dilatorily throwing up a piece of wood into the fire every now and then, a business which seemed to have engaged him a considerable part of the evening, for his face was somewhat weary.", "소년은 이따금 나무토막 하나를 굼뜨게 불 속에 던져 넣고 있었는데, 이 일이 저녁 내내 그를 붙들어두었던 모양인지 얼굴에는 다소 지친 기색이 어려 있었다."]], [["“I am glad you have come, Miss Eustacia,”", "\"와 주셔서 다행이에요, 유스테이샤 아씨.\""], ["he said, with a sigh of relief.", "소년이 안도의 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“I don’t like biding by myself.”", "\"혼자 있는 건 싫어요.\""]], [["“Nonsense.", "\"무슨 소리."], ["I have only been a little way for a walk.", "그저 잠깐 산책을 다녀온 것뿐인데."], ["I have been gone only twenty minutes.”", "떠난 지 이십 분밖에 안 됐어.\""]], [["“It seemed long,”", "\"길게 느껴졌는걸요.\""], ["murmured the sad boy.", "슬픈 소년이 중얼거렸다."], ["“And you have been so many times.”", "\"게다가 여러 번 가셨잖아요.\""]], [["“Why, I thought you would be pleased to have a bonfire.", "\"아니, 나는 네가 모닥불을 좋아할 줄 알았는데."], ["Are you not much obliged to me for making you one?”", "모닥불을 만들어줬는데도 고맙지 않니?\""]], [["“Yes; but there’s nobody here to play wi’ me.”", "\"고맙죠. 그런데 같이 놀 사람이 아무도 없는걸요.\""]], [["“I suppose nobody has come while I’ve been away?”", "\"내가 없는 동안 아무도 안 온 모양이지?\""]], [["“Nobody except your grandfather—he looked out of doors once for ’ee.", "\"할아버지 말고는 아무도 안 왔어요—할아버지가 한 번 문밖을 내다보셨어요, 아씨 찾으시려고."], ["I told him you were walking round upon the hill to look at the other bonfires.”", "아씨가 언덕을 돌아다니면서 다른 모닥불들을 보고 계시다고 말씀드렸어요.\""]], [["“A good boy.”", "\"착한 아이로구나.\""]], [["“I think I hear him coming again, miss.”", "\"또 오시는 소리가 들리는 것 같아요, 아씨.\""]], [["An old man came into the remoter light of the fire from the direction of the homestead.", "한 노인이 농가 쪽에서 불빛이 닿는 먼 가장자리로 들어섰다."], ["He was the same who had overtaken the reddleman on the road that afternoon.", "그날 오후 길에서 레들맨을 따라잡았던 바로 그 사람이었다."], ["He looked wistfully to the top of the bank at the woman who stood there, and his teeth, which were quite unimpaired, showed like parian from his parted lips.", "그는 둑 꼭대기에 선 여인을 그리운 눈빛으로 올려다보았고, 조금도 상하지 않은 그의 이가 벌어진 입술 사이로 파리언 대리석처럼 드러났다."]], [["“When are you coming indoors, Eustacia?”", "\"언제 들어올 거냐, 유스테이샤?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."], ["“’Tis almost bedtime.", "\"벌써 잘 시간이 다 됐다."], ["I’ve been home these two hours, and am tired out.", "나는 집에 온 지 두 시간이나 됐고, 녹초가 됐구나."], ["Surely ’tis somewhat childish of you to stay out playing at bonfires so long, and wasting such fuel.", "이렇게 오래 모닥불 놀이를 하며 밖에 머무르고 그런 땔감을 낭비하다니, 참으로 어린애 같지 않으냐."], ["My precious thorn roots, the rarest of all firing, that I laid by on purpose for Christmas—you have burnt ’em nearly all!”", "그 귀한 가시나무 뿌리, 온갖 땔감 가운데서도 가장 진귀한 것을 일부러 크리스마스용으로 따로 챙겨두었는데—그걸 거의 다 태워버렸구나!\""]], [["“I promised Johnny a bonfire, and it pleases him not to let it go out just yet,”", "\"조니에게 모닥불을 약속했고, 이 아이는 아직 꺼뜨리지 않는 걸 좋아하는걸요.\""], ["said Eustacia, in a way which told at once that she was absolute queen here.", "유스테이샤가 자신이 여기서 절대적인 여왕임을 단번에 드러내는 어조로 말했다."], ["“Grandfather, you go in to bed.", "\"할아버지, 들어가서 주무세요."], ["I shall follow you soon.", "곧 따라 들어갈게요."], ["You like the fire, don’t you, Johnny?”", "너는 불이 좋지, 조니?\""]], [["The boy looked up doubtfully at her and murmured, “I don’t think I want it any longer.”", "소년이 미심쩍은 표정으로 그녀를 올려다보며 중얼거렸다. \"이제 별로 안 좋은 것 같은데요.\""]], [["Her grandfather had turned back again, and did not hear the boy’s reply.", "할아버지는 이미 돌아서 있어서 소년의 대답을 듣지 못했다."], ["As soon as the white-haired man had vanished she said in a tone of pique to the child, “Ungrateful little boy, how can you contradict me?", "백발의 노인이 사라지자마자 그녀는 약이 오른 어조로 아이에게 말했다. \"은혜도 모르는 녀석, 어떻게 나한테 말대꾸를 하니?"], ["Never shall you have a bonfire again unless you keep it up now.", "지금 불을 계속 지피지 않으면 다시는 모닥불을 못 가질 줄 알아라."], ["Come, tell me you like to do things for me, and don’t deny it.”", "자, 나를 위해 뭔가 하는 게 좋다고 말해, 부인하지 말고.\""]], [["The repressed child said, “Yes, I do, miss,”", "기가 죽은 아이가 말했다. \"네, 좋아요, 아씨.\""], ["and continued to stir the fire perfunctorily.", "그러고는 건성으로 불을 계속 휘저었다."]], [["“Stay a little longer and I will give you a crooked six-pence,”", "\"조금만 더 있으면 휜 육펜스를 줄게.\""], ["said Eustacia, more gently.", "유스테이샤가 한결 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“Put in one piece of wood every two or three minutes, but not too much at once.", "\"이삼 분마다 나무토막 하나씩 넣어, 그렇지만 한꺼번에 너무 많이는 말고."], ["I am going to walk along the ridge a little longer, but I shall keep on coming to you.", "나는 능선을 따라 좀 더 걸을 거지만 계속 너한테 올 거야."], ["And if you hear a frog jump into the pond with a flounce like a stone thrown in, be sure you run and tell me, because it is a sign of rain.”", "그리고 돌을 던진 것처럼 첨벙 소리를 내며 개구리가 못으로 뛰어드는 소리가 들리거든, 꼭 달려와서 나한테 말해, 그건 비가 올 징조니까.\""]], [["“Yes, Eustacia.”", "\"네, 유스테이샤.\""]], [["“Miss Vye, sir.”", "\"바이 아씨라고 해야지.\""]], [["“Miss Vy—stacia.”", "\"바이—스테이샤 아씨.\""]], [["“That will do.", "\"그럼 됐어."], ["Now put in one stick more.”", "자, 이제 막대기 하나 더 넣어.\""]], [["The little slave went on feeding the fire as before.", "어린 노예는 전과 같이 계속해서 불에 땔감을 먹였다."], ["He seemed a mere automaton, galvanized into moving and speaking by the wayward Eustacia’s will.", "그는 그저 자동인형 같았으니, 변덕스러운 유스테이샤의 의지에 의해 움직이고 말하도록 전기로 작동되는 듯했다."], ["He might have been the brass statue which Albertus Magnus is said to have animated just so far as to make it chatter, and move, and be his servant.", "그는 알베르투스 마그누스가 단지 재잘거리고 움직이며 자기 종노릇을 할 만큼만 생명을 불어넣었다는 그 청동상이었을지도 모른다."]], [["Before going on her walk again the young girl stood still on the bank for a few instants and listened.", "다시 산책을 나서기 전에 젊은 처녀는 잠시 둑 위에 가만히 서서 귀를 기울였다."], ["It was to the full as lonely a place as Rainbarrow, though at rather a lower level; and it was more sheltered from wind and weather on account of the few firs to the north.", "이곳은 레인배로보다 다소 낮은 지대이긴 했어도 그에 못지않게 외진 곳이었으며, 북쪽의 몇 그루 전나무 덕분에 바람과 궂은 날씨로부터 더 잘 가려져 있었다."], ["The bank which enclosed the homestead, and protected it from the lawless state of the world without, was formed of thick square clods, dug from the ditch on the outside, and built up with a slight batter or incline, which forms no slight defense where hedges will not grow because of the wind and the wilderness, and where wall materials are unattainable.", "농가를 에워싸 바깥세상의 무법 상태로부터 그곳을 지켜주는 둑은 바깥쪽 도랑에서 파낸 두툼한 네모꼴 흙덩이로 만들어졌으며, 약간 비탈지게 경사를 두어 쌓아올린 것이었으니, 이는 바람과 황야 탓에 산울타리가 자라지 못하고 담을 쌓을 재료도 구할 수 없는 곳에서 결코 가볍지 않은 방어가 되어주었다."], ["Otherwise the situation was quite open, commanding the whole length of the valley which reached to the river behind Wildeve’s house.", "그 밖으로는 사방이 탁 트여, 와일디브의 집 뒤 강에 이르는 계곡 전체를 한눈에 굽어보았다."], ["High above this to the right, and much nearer thitherward than the Quiet Woman Inn, the blurred contour of Rainbarrow obstructed the sky.", "그 위쪽으로 오른편 높이, 콰이어트 우먼 여관보다 그쪽으로 훨씬 가까이, 레인배로의 흐릿한 윤곽이 하늘을 가로막고 있었다."]], [["After her attentive survey of the wild slopes and hollow ravines a gesture of impatience escaped Eustacia.", "거친 비탈과 움푹한 협곡을 주의 깊게 살펴본 뒤 유스테이샤의 입에서 조바심의 몸짓이 새어 나왔다."], ["She vented petulant words every now and then, but there were sighs between her words, and sudden listenings between her sighs.", "그녀는 이따금 짜증 섞인 말을 내뱉었으나, 그 말들 사이사이에는 한숨이 있었고, 한숨들 사이사이에는 불쑥 귀를 기울이는 순간들이 있었다."], ["Descending from her perch she again sauntered off towards Rainbarrow, though this time she did not go the whole way.", "자기 자리에서 내려와 그녀는 다시 레인배로 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어갔으나, 이번에는 끝까지 가지 않았다."]], [["Twice she reappeared at intervals of a few minutes and each time she said—", "몇 분 간격을 두고 그녀는 두 차례 다시 나타났고, 그때마다 이렇게 말했다—"]], [["“Not any flounce into the pond yet, little man?”", "\"아직 못에 첨벙 소리는 안 났니, 꼬마야?\""]], [["“No, Miss Eustacia,”", "\"네, 유스테이샤 아씨.\""], ["the child replied.", "아이가 대답했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["she said at last, “I shall soon be going in, and then I will give you the crooked sixpence, and let you go home.”", "마침내 그녀가 말했다. \"이제 곧 들어갈 거니까, 그때 휜 육펜스를 주고 집에 보내줄게.\""]], [["“Thank’ee, Miss Eustacia,”", "\"고맙습니다, 유스테이샤 아씨.\""], ["said the tired stoker, breathing more easily.", "지친 화부가 한결 편히 숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["And Eustacia again strolled away from the fire, but this time not towards Rainbarrow.", "그리고 유스테이샤는 다시 불에서 멀어졌으나, 이번에는 레인배로 쪽이 아니었다."], ["She skirted the bank and went round to the wicket before the house, where she stood motionless, looking at the scene.", "그녀는 둑을 따라 돌아 집 앞의 쪽문에 이르렀고, 거기에 꼼짝 않고 서서 풍경을 바라보았다."]], [["Fifty yards off rose the corner of the two converging banks, with the fire upon it; within the bank, lifting up to the fire one stick at a time, just as before, the figure of the little child.", "오십 야드 떨어진 곳에 두 둑이 모여 이루는 모퉁이가 솟아 있었고, 그 위에 불이 타올랐으며, 둑 안쪽에서는 어린아이의 형체가 전과 똑같이 한 번에 막대기 하나씩 불에 들어올리고 있었다."], ["She idly watched him as he occasionally climbed up in the nook of the bank and stood beside the brands.", "그녀는 아이가 이따금 둑 구석으로 기어올라 타다 남은 장작 옆에 서는 것을 무심히 지켜보았다."], ["The wind blew the smoke, and the child’s hair, and the corner of his pinafore, all in the same direction; the breeze died, and the pinafore and hair lay still, and the smoke went up straight.", "바람이 연기와 아이의 머리카락과 앞치마 자락을 한 방향으로 똑같이 불었고, 미풍이 잦아들자 앞치마와 머리카락이 가만히 멈췄으며 연기는 곧장 위로 올라갔다."], ["While Eustacia looked on from this distance the boy’s form visibly started—he slid down the bank and ran across towards the white gate.", "유스테이샤가 이 거리에서 지켜보는 동안 소년의 몸이 눈에 띄게 움찔하더니—그는 둑을 미끄러져 내려와 흰 대문 쪽으로 달려왔다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"무슨 일이니?\""], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 말했다."]], [["“A hopfrog have jumped into the pond.", "\"개구리가 못으로 뛰어들었어요."], ["Yes, I heard ’en!”", "네, 들었어요!\""]], [["“Then it is going to rain, and you had better go home.", "\"그럼 비가 오려나 보다, 너는 이제 집에 가는 게 좋겠어."], ["You will not be afraid?”", "무섭지 않겠니?\""], ["She spoke hurriedly, as if her heart had leapt into her throat at the boy’s words.", "그녀는 아이의 말에 심장이 목구멍까지 튀어오르기라도 한 듯 황급히 말했다."]], [["“No, because I shall hae the crooked sixpence.”", "\"안 무서워요, 휜 육펜스가 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Yes, here it is.", "\"그래, 여기 있다."], ["Now run as fast as you can—not that way—through the garden here.", "자, 최대한 빨리 달려가—그쪽 말고—이쪽 정원을 통해서."], ["No other boy in the heath has had such a bonfire as yours.”", "이 황야에서 너만큼 좋은 모닥불을 가져본 아이는 없을 거야.\""]], [["The boy, who clearly had had too much of a good thing, marched away into the shadows with alacrity.", "좋은 것이라면 이제 넌더리가 난 게 분명한 소년은 기꺼운 마음으로 어둠 속으로 성큼성큼 걸어갔다."], ["When he was gone Eustacia, leaving her telescope and hourglass by the gate, brushed forward from the wicket towards the angle of the bank, under the fire.", "그가 가버리자 유스테이샤는 망원경과 모래시계를 대문 옆에 놓아둔 채 쪽문에서 불 아래쪽 둑 모퉁이를 향해 쓸 듯이 나아갔다."]], [["Here, screened by the outwork, she waited.", "여기, 바깥 방벽에 가려진 채 그녀는 기다렸다."], ["In a few moments a splash was audible from the pond outside.", "몇 순간 지나자 바깥 못에서 첨벙 소리가 들렸다."], ["Had the child been there he would have said that a second frog had jumped in; but by most people the sound would have been likened to the fall of a stone into the water.", "아이가 거기 있었다면 두 번째 개구리가 뛰어들었다고 했겠지만, 대부분의 사람들에게 그 소리는 물에 돌이 떨어지는 소리에 가깝게 들렸을 것이다."], ["Eustacia stepped upon the bank.", "유스테이샤가 둑 위로 발을 디뎠다."]], [["“Yes?”", "\"왔어요?\""], ["she said, and held her breath.", "그녀가 말하며 숨을 죽였다."]], [["Thereupon the contour of a man became dimly visible against the low-reaching sky over the valley, beyond the outer margin of the pool.", "그러자 못 바깥 가장자리 너머, 계곡 위로 낮게 깔린 하늘을 배경으로 한 남자의 윤곽이 어렴풋이 드러났다."], ["He came round it and leapt upon the bank beside her.", "그는 못을 돌아와 그녀 옆 둑 위로 뛰어올랐다."], ["A low laugh escaped her—the third utterance which the girl had indulged in tonight.", "그녀의 입에서 낮은 웃음이 새어 나왔으니—그날 밤 처녀가 허락한 세 번째 발성이었다."], ["The first, when she stood upon Rainbarrow, had expressed anxiety; the second, on the ridge, had expressed impatience; the present was one of triumphant pleasure.", "첫 번째, 레인배로 위에 섰을 때의 것은 불안을 드러냈고, 두 번째, 능선에서의 것은 조바심을 드러냈으며, 지금의 것은 의기양양한 기쁨의 것이었다."], ["She let her joyous eyes rest upon him without speaking, as upon some wondrous thing she had created out of chaos.", "그녀는 혼돈에서 자신이 창조해낸 어떤 경이로운 존재를 보듯, 말없이 기쁨에 찬 눈길을 그에게 머물게 했다."]], [["“I have come,”", "\"왔소.\""], ["said the man, who was Wildeve.", "와일디브인 그 남자가 말했다."], ["“You give me no peace.", "\"당신은 나를 가만 놔두질 않는군."], ["Why do you not leave me alone?", "왜 나를 좀 내버려두지 않는 거요?"], ["I have seen your bonfire all the evening.”", "저녁 내내 당신 모닥불을 보았소.\""], ["The words were not without emotion, and retained their level tone as if by a careful equipoise between imminent extremes.", "그 말에는 감정이 없지 않았으나, 임박한 양극단 사이에서 조심스럽게 균형을 잡기라도 한 듯 평탄한 어조를 유지하고 있었다."]], [["At this unexpectedly repressing manner in her lover the girl seemed to repress herself also.", "연인의 이 뜻밖에도 억제된 태도에 처녀도 자신을 억누르는 듯했다."], ["“Of course you have seen my fire,”", "\"당신이 내 불을 본 건 당연하죠.\""], ["she answered with languid calmness, artificially maintained.", "그녀가 인위적으로 유지한 나른한 침착함으로 대답했다."], ["“Why shouldn’t I have a bonfire on the Fifth of November, like other denizens of the heath?”", "\"내가 황야의 다른 사람들처럼 십일월 오일에 모닥불을 피우면 안 될 이유라도 있나요?\""]], [["“I knew it was meant for me.”", "\"그게 나를 위한 거라는 걸 알았소.\""]], [["“How did you know it?", "\"그걸 어떻게 알았어요?"], ["I have had no word with you since you—you chose her, and walked about with her, and deserted me entirely, as if I had never been yours life and soul so irretrievably!”", "당신이—당신이 그 여자를 택해서, 그 여자와 함께 돌아다니고, 마치 내가 한 번도 돌이킬 수 없이 당신의 목숨이자 영혼이었던 적이 없었다는 듯 나를 완전히 버린 뒤로 당신과는 한마디도 나눈 적이 없는데!\""]], [["“Eustacia!", "\"유스테이샤!"], ["could I forget that last autumn at this same day of the month and at this same place you lighted exactly such a fire as a signal for me to come and see you?", "작년 이맘때, 같은 달 같은 날, 같은 장소에서 당신이 나더러 와서 만나자는 신호로 바로 이런 불을 피웠던 걸 내가 잊을 수 있겠소?"], ["Why should there have been a bonfire again by Captain Vye’s house if not for the same purpose?”", "같은 목적이 아니라면 어째서 바이 선장의 집 옆에 또다시 모닥불이 피워졌겠소?\""]], [["“Yes, yes—I own it,”", "\"네, 네—인정해요.\""], ["she cried under her breath, with a drowsy fervour of manner and tone which was quite peculiar to her.", "그녀가 그녀만의 독특한 나른한 열정이 깃든 태도와 어조로 숨죽여 외쳤다."], ["“Don’t begin speaking to me as you did, Damon; you will drive me to say words I would not wish to say to you.", "\"예전처럼 나한테 말하기 시작하지 마요, 데이먼. 당신은 내가 당신에게 하고 싶지 않은 말들을 하게 만들 거예요."], ["I had given you up, and resolved not to think of you any more; and then I heard the news, and I came out and got the fire ready because I thought that you had been faithful to me.”", "나는 당신을 포기했고, 더는 당신을 생각하지 않기로 결심했었어요. 그런데 그 소식을 듣고, 당신이 나에게 변함없었다는 생각이 들어서 밖으로 나와 불을 준비한 거예요.\""]], [["“What have you heard to make you think that?”", "\"무슨 말을 들었길래 그렇게 생각한 거요?\""], ["said Wildeve, astonished.", "와일디브가 놀라서 말했다."]], [["“That you did not marry her!”", "\"당신이 그 여자와 결혼하지 않았다는 거요!\""], ["she murmured exultingly.", "그녀가 환희에 차서 중얼거렸다."], ["“And I knew it was because you loved me best, and couldn’t do it....", "\"그리고 그건 당신이 나를 가장 사랑해서, 차마 결혼할 수 없었기 때문임을 알았어요……"], ["Damon, you have been cruel to me to go away, and I have said I would never forgive you.", "데이먼, 당신은 떠나가서 나에게 잔인하게 굴었고, 나는 결코 용서하지 않겠다고 했어요."], ["I do not think I can forgive you entirely, even now—it is too much for a woman of any spirit to quite overlook.”", "지금도 당신을 온전히 용서할 수 있을 것 같진 않아요—기개 있는 여자라면 그걸 완전히 눈감아주기란 너무 벅찬 일이니까요.\""]], [["“If I had known you wished to call me up here only to reproach me, I wouldn’t have come.”", "\"당신이 나를 여기로 부른 게 그저 나를 책망하기 위해서인 줄 알았다면, 나는 오지 않았을 거요.\""]], [["“But I don’t mind it, and I do forgive you now that you have not married her, and have come back to me!”", "\"하지만 나는 그래도 상관없고, 당신이 그 여자와 결혼하지 않고 나에게 돌아왔으니 이제 당신을 용서해요!\""]], [["“Who told you that I had not married her?”", "\"내가 그 여자와 결혼하지 않았다고 누가 말했소?\""]], [["“My grandfather.", "\"할아버지요."], ["He took a long walk today, and as he was coming home he overtook some person who told him of a broken-off wedding—he thought it might be yours, and I knew it was.”", "오늘 오래 산책을 하셨는데, 집에 돌아오는 길에 어떤 사람을 따라잡았고 그 사람이 깨진 혼사 이야기를 했대요—할아버지는 그게 당신 것일지도 모른다고 생각하셨고, 나는 그게 당신 것임을 알았죠.\""]], [["“Does anybody else know?”", "\"다른 사람도 아오?\""]], [["“I suppose not.", "\"아닐걸요."], ["Now Damon, do you see why I lit my signal fire?", "자 데이먼, 이제 내가 왜 신호 불을 피웠는지 알겠어요?"], ["You did not think I would have lit it if I had imagined you to have become the husband of this woman.", "당신이 이 여자의 남편이 됐다고 생각했다면 내가 불을 피웠으리라고는 당신도 생각하지 않겠죠."], ["It is insulting my pride to suppose that.”", "그렇게 짐작하는 건 내 자존심을 모욕하는 거예요.\""]], [["Wildeve was silent; it was evident that he had supposed as much.", "와일디브는 잠자코 있었으니, 그가 그렇게 짐작했음이 분명했다."]], [["“Did you indeed think I believed you were married?”", "\"정말로 내가 당신이 결혼했다고 믿었으리라 생각했어요?\""], ["she again demanded earnestly.", "그녀가 다시 간절히 물었다."], ["“Then you wronged me; and upon my life and heart I can hardly bear to recognize that you have such ill thoughts of me!", "\"그렇다면 당신은 나를 욕보인 거예요. 목숨을 걸고 진심으로 말하건대, 당신이 나를 그렇게 나쁘게 생각한다는 걸 인정하기가 견디기 힘들 정도예요!"], ["Damon, you are not worthy of me—I see it, and yet I love you.", "데이먼, 당신은 나에게 어울리는 사람이 못 돼요—나도 알아요, 그런데도 나는 당신을 사랑해요."], ["Never mind, let it go—I must bear your mean opinion as best I may....", "괜찮아요, 그냥 둬요—나는 나에 대한 당신의 비열한 생각을 어떻게든 견뎌야겠죠……"], ["It is true, is it not,”", "정말이죠, 그렇죠.\""], ["she added with ill-concealed anxiety, on his making no demonstration, “that you could not bring yourself to give me up, and are still going to love me best of all?”", "그가 아무 반응도 보이지 않자 그녀가 잘 감추지 못한 불안을 담아 덧붙였다. \"당신은 차마 나를 포기할 수 없었고, 지금도 나를 누구보다 사랑할 거라는 거?\""]], [["“Yes; or why should I have come?”", "\"그렇소. 아니면 내가 왜 왔겠소?\""], ["he said touchily.", "그가 발끈하며 말했다."], ["“Not that fidelity will be any great merit in me after your kind speech about my unworthiness, which should have been said by myself if by anybody, and comes with an ill grace from you.", "\"내가 어울리지 않는다는 당신의 그 친절한 말 뒤에는 충실함이 나에게 그리 대단한 미덕이 되진 못하겠지만 말이오, 그건 누구든 입에 올린다면 내 입으로 했어야 할 말인데 당신이 하니 영 어색하게 들리는군."], ["However, the curse of inflammability is upon me, and I must live under it, and take any snub from a woman.", "어쨌든 불붙기 쉬운 천성이라는 저주가 내게 씌었으니, 나는 그걸 안고 살며 여자에게 어떤 냉대든 받아야 하나 보오."], ["It has brought me down from engineering to innkeeping—what lower stage it has in store for me I have yet to learn.”", "그 때문에 나는 기술자에서 여관 주인으로 전락했소—그게 나를 위해 어떤 더 낮은 단계를 예비해두고 있는지는 아직 알 수 없지만.\""], ["He continued to look upon her gloomily.", "그는 침울하게 그녀를 계속 바라보았다."]], [["She seized the moment, and throwing back the shawl so that the firelight shone full upon her face and throat, said with a smile, “Have you seen anything better than that in your travels?”", "그녀는 그 순간을 놓치지 않고, 불빛이 얼굴과 목에 가득 비치도록 숄을 젖히며 미소 띤 채 말했다. \"여행을 다니면서 이보다 나은 걸 본 적 있어요?\""]], [["Eustacia was not one to commit herself to such a position without good ground.", "유스테이샤는 충분한 근거 없이 그런 자세를 취할 사람이 아니었다."], ["He said quietly, “No.”", "그가 조용히 말했다. \"없소.\""]], [["“Not even on the shoulders of Thomasin?”", "\"토머신의 어깨 위에서도요?\""]], [["“Thomasin is a pleasing and innocent woman.”", "\"토머신은 사랑스럽고 순진한 여자요.\""]], [["“That’s nothing to do with it,”", "\"그건 상관없는 얘기예요.\""], ["she cried with quick passionateness.", "그녀가 격정에 차 재빨리 외쳤다. \"그 여자는 빼고 말하죠."], ["“We will leave her out; there are only you and me now to think of.”", "이제 생각할 사람은 당신과 나뿐이에요.\" 그를 한참 바라본 뒤 그녀는 예의 잔잔한 온기를 되찾으며 말을 이었다."], ["After a long look at him she resumed with the old quiescent warmth, “Must I go on weakly confessing to you things a woman ought to conceal; and own that no words can express how gloomy I have been because of that dreadful belief I held till two hours ago—that you had quite deserted me?”", "\"여자라면 마땅히 감추어야 할 것들을 나약하게 계속 당신에게 고백해야 하나요. 그리고 두 시간 전까지 품고 있던 그 끔찍한 믿음—당신이 나를 완전히 버렸다는 믿음 때문에 내가 얼마나 우울했는지 말로는 다 표현할 수 없다고 털어놓아야 하나요?\""]], [["“I am sorry I caused you that pain.”", "\"당신에게 그런 고통을 준 것은 미안하오.\""]], [["“But perhaps it is not wholly because of you that I get gloomy,”", "\"하지만 내가 우울해지는 게 다 당신 때문은 아닐지도 몰라요.\""], ["she archly added.", "그녀가 짓궂게 덧붙였다."], ["“It is in my nature to feel like that.", "\"그렇게 느끼는 게 내 천성이에요."], ["It was born in my blood, I suppose.”", "핏속에 타고난 거겠죠, 아마.\""]], [["“Hypochondriasis.”", "\"우울증이로군.\""]], [["“Or else it was coming into this wild heath.", "\"아니면 이 거친 황야로 들어온 탓이겠죠."], ["I was happy enough at Budmouth.", "버드머스에서는 충분히 행복했어요."], ["O the times, O the days at Budmouth!", "아, 버드머스에서의 그 시절, 그 나날들!"], ["But Egdon will be brighter again now.”", "하지만 이제 에그던도 다시 환해지겠죠.\""]], [["“I hope it will,”", "\"그러길 바라오.\""], ["said Wildeve moodily.", "와일디브가 시무룩하게 말했다."], ["“Do you know the consequence of this recall to me, my old darling?", "\"당신이 나를 이렇게 다시 불러낸 결과가 어떨지 아오, 내 옛 연인이여?"], ["I shall come to see you again as before, at Rainbarrow.”", "나는 예전처럼 다시 당신을 보러 오게 될 거요, 레인배로에서.\""]], [["“Of course you will.”", "\"당연히 그렇겠죠.\""]], [["“And yet I declare that until I got here tonight I intended, after this one good-bye, never to meet you again.”", "\"그런데도 오늘 밤 여기 오기 전까지 나는 이 마지막 작별 인사를 끝으로 다시는 당신을 만나지 않으려 했다고 분명히 말해두겠소.\""]], [["“I don’t thank you for that,”", "\"그래 줘서 고맙진 않네요.\""], ["she said, turning away, while indignation spread through her like subterranean heat.", "그녀가 몸을 돌리며 말했고, 그동안 분노가 지하의 열기처럼 그녀를 타고 번졌다. \"원한다면 다시 레인배로에 와도 좋지만, 나를 보진 못할 거예요."], ["“You may come again to Rainbarrow if you like, but you won’t see me; and you may call, but I shall not listen; and you may tempt me, but I won’t give myself to you any more.”", "불러도 좋지만 나는 귀 기울이지 않을 거고요. 유혹해도 좋지만 다시는 당신에게 나를 내주지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“You have said as much before, sweet; but such natures as yours don’t so easily adhere to their words.", "\"전에도 그렇게 말했었지, 내 사랑. 하지만 당신 같은 천성은 자기 말을 그리 쉽게 지키지 못하오."], ["Neither, for the matter of that, do such natures as mine.”", "그 점에 관해서라면 나 같은 천성도 마찬가지요.\""]], [["“This is the pleasure I have won by my trouble,”", "\"이게 내가 고생해서 얻은 즐거움이로군요.\""], ["she whispered bitterly.", "그녀가 씁쓸하게 속삭였다."], ["“Why did I try to recall you?", "\"내가 왜 당신을 다시 불러냈을까?"], ["Damon, a strange warring takes place in my mind occasionally.", "데이먼, 이따금 내 마음속에 이상한 싸움이 벌어져요."], ["I think when I become calm after you woundings, ‘Do I embrace a cloud of common fog after all?’ You are a chameleon, and now you are at your worst colour.", "당신이 나에게 상처를 준 뒤 마음이 가라앉으면 이런 생각이 들어요, '결국 나는 한 조각 흔한 안개를 끌어안고 있는 건가?' 당신은 카멜레온이에요, 그리고 지금 당신은 가장 흉한 빛깔을 띠고 있어요."], ["Go home, or I shall hate you!”", "집으로 가요, 안 그러면 당신을 미워하게 될 테니!\""]], [["He looked absently towards Rainbarrow while one might have counted twenty, and said, as if he did not much mind all this, “Yes, I will go home.", "그는 스물을 셀 만큼 동안 멍하니 레인배로 쪽을 바라보다가, 이 모든 게 별로 마음에 두이지 않는다는 듯 말했다. \"그래, 집에 가겠소."], ["Do you mean to see me again?”", "나를 다시 볼 생각이 있소?\""]], [["“If you own to me that the wedding is broken off because you love me best.”", "\"혼사가 깨진 게 당신이 나를 가장 사랑하기 때문이라고 인정한다면요.\""]], [["“I don’t think it would be good policy,”", "\"그건 좋은 책략이 못 될 것 같은데.\""], ["said Wildeve, smiling.", "와일디브가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“You would get to know the extent of your power too clearly.”", "\"당신이 자기 힘의 크기를 너무 분명히 알게 될 테니.\" \"하지만 말해줘요!\""]], [["“But tell me!”", "\"하지만 말해줘요!\""]], [["“You know.”", "\"당신도 알잖소.\""], ["“Where is she now?”", "\"그 여자는 지금 어디 있어요?\""]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"모르오."], ["I prefer not to speak of her to you.", "그 여자 얘기는 당신에게 하고 싶지 않소."], ["I have not yet married her; I have come in obedience to your call.", "나는 아직 그 여자와 결혼하지 않았고, 당신의 부름에 따라 왔소."], ["That is enough.”", "그걸로 됐소.\""]], [["“I merely lit that fire because I was dull, and thought I would get a little excitement by calling you up and triumphing over you as the Witch of Endor called up Samuel.", "\"나는 그저 따분해서 그 불을 피웠을 뿐이에요, 엔도르의 마녀가 사무엘을 불러냈듯 당신을 불러내 당신을 이겨먹으면 조금은 신이 날까 싶어서요."], ["I determined you should come; and you have come!", "당신이 오도록 하겠다고 마음먹었고, 당신은 왔어요!"], ["I have shown my power.", "내 힘을 보여준 거죠."], ["A mile and half hither, and a mile and half back again to your home—three miles in the dark for me.", "여기까지 일 마일 반, 다시 당신 집까지 일 마일 반—나로서는 어둠 속에서 삼 마일이에요."], ["Have I not shown my power?”", "내가 힘을 보여준 게 아닌가요?\""]], [["He shook his head at her.", "그가 그녀를 향해 고개를 저었다."], ["“I know you too well, my Eustacia; I know you too well.", "\"나는 당신을 너무 잘 아오, 내 유스테이샤. 너무 잘 알아."], ["There isn’t a note in you which I don’t know; and that hot little bosom couldn’t play such a cold-blooded trick to save its life.", "내가 모르는 음色이 당신 안엔 하나도 없소. 그 뜨거운 작은 가슴은 죽었다 깨도 그런 냉혹한 술수는 부리지 못하오."], ["I saw a woman on Rainbarrow at dusk looking down towards my house.", "나는 해질 녘 레인배로에서 내 집 쪽을 내려다보는 여자를 보았소."], ["I think I drew out you before you drew out me.”", "당신이 나를 끌어내기 전에 내가 당신을 끌어낸 것 같은데.\""]], [["The revived embers of an old passion glowed clearly in Wildeve now; and he leant forward as if about to put his face towards her cheek.", "이제 와일디브의 안에서 옛 열정의 되살아난 잉걸불이 또렷이 타올랐고, 그는 그녀의 뺨에 얼굴을 가져다 대려는 듯 몸을 앞으로 기울였다."]], [["“O no,”", "\"아, 안 돼요.\""], ["she said, intractably moving to the other side of the decayed fire.", "그녀가 고집스럽게 사그라든 불 반대편으로 옮겨가며 말했다."], ["“What did you mean by that?”", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이었어요?\""]], [["“Perhaps I may kiss your hand?”", "\"당신 손에 입 맞춰도 되겠소?\""]], [["“No, you may not.”", "\"안 돼요.\""]], [["“Then I may shake your hand?”", "\"그럼 악수라도 할까?\" \"안 돼요.\""]], [["“No.” “Then I wish you good night without caring for either.", "\"그렇다면 둘 다 아쉬워 말고 잘 자라는 인사나 하겠소."], ["Good-bye, good-bye.”", "잘 가요, 잘 가요.\""]], [["She returned no answer, and with the bow of a dancing-master he vanished on the other side of the pool as he had come.", "그녀는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았고, 그는 무도 교사처럼 인사를 하고는 왔던 대로 못 반대편으로 사라졌다."]], [["Eustacia sighed—it was no fragile maiden sigh, but a sigh which shook her like a shiver.", "유스테이샤는 한숨을 쉬었으니—그것은 가냘픈 처녀의 한숨이 아니라 전율처럼 그녀를 뒤흔드는 한숨이었다."], ["Whenever a flash of reason darted like an electric light upon her lover—as it sometimes would—and showed his imperfections, she shivered thus.", "이성의 섬광이 전등불처럼 연인을 비추어—이따금 그러하듯—그의 결점을 드러낼 때마다 그녀는 이렇게 몸을 떨었다."], ["But it was over in a second, and she loved on.", "하지만 그것은 일순간에 지나갔고, 그녀는 변함없이 사랑했다."], ["She knew that he trifled with her; but she loved on.", "그가 자기를 가지고 논다는 것을 알면서도 그녀는 변함없이 사랑했다."], ["She scattered the half-burnt brands, went indoors immediately, and up to her bedroom without a light.", "그녀는 반쯤 탄 장작을 흩어버리고는 곧장 안으로 들어가, 불도 없이 침실로 올라갔다."], ["Amid the rustles which denoted her to be undressing in the darkness other heavy breaths frequently came; and the same kind of shudder occasionally moved through her when, ten minutes later, she lay on her bed asleep.", "어둠 속에서 옷을 벗고 있음을 알려주는 옷자락 스치는 소리 사이로 무거운 숨이 자주 섞여 들었고, 십 분 뒤 침대에 잠들어 누웠을 때에도 똑같은 종류의 전율이 이따금 그녀를 훑고 지나갔다."]], [["VII.", "VII."], ["Queen of Night", "밤의 여왕"]], [["Eustacia Vye was the raw material of a divinity.", "유스테이샤 바이는 신성神性의 원료였다."], ["On Olympus she would have done well with a little preparation.", "약간의 다듬질만 거쳤다면 올림포스에서도 잘 해냈을 것이다."], ["She had the passions and instincts which make a model goddess, that is, those which make not quite a model woman.", "그녀에게는 본보기가 될 만한 여신을 만드는 열정과 본능이 있었으니, 다시 말해 본보기가 될 만한 여자는 못 만드는 그런 것들이었다."], ["Had it been possible for the earth and mankind to be entirely in her grasp for a while, she had handled the distaff, the spindle, and the shears at her own free will, few in the world would have noticed the change of government.", "잠시나마 대지와 인류가 온전히 그녀의 손아귀에 들어와 그녀가 마음대로 실패와 물레와 가위를 다룰 수 있었다 한들, 세상에서 그 통치의 교체를 알아챌 사람은 거의 없었을 것이다."], ["There would have been the same inequality of lot, the same heaping up of favours here, of contumely there, the same generosity before justice, the same perpetual dilemmas, the same captious alteration of caresses and blows that we endure now.", "똑같은 운명의 불평등, 한쪽에는 은총이 쌓이고 다른 쪽에는 모욕이 쌓이는 똑같은 적체, 정의에 앞서는 똑같은 관대함, 똑같은 영원한 진퇴양난, 우리가 지금 견디고 있는 것과 똑같이 변덕스럽게 교차하는 애무와 매질이 있었을 것이다."]], [["She was in person full-limbed and somewhat heavy; without ruddiness, as without pallor; and soft to the touch as a cloud.", "몸은 사지가 풍만하고 다소 무거웠으며, 불그스름하지도 창백하지도 않았고, 손에 닿는 느낌은 구름처럼 부드러웠다."], ["To see her hair was to fancy that a whole winter did not contain darkness enough to form its shadow—it closed over her forehead like nightfall extinguishing the western glow.", "그녀의 머리카락을 보면 겨우내 모인 어둠을 다 합쳐도 그 그림자를 이룰 만큼 충분하지 못하리라는 상상이 들었으니—그 머리카락은 서녘의 노을을 지우는 땅거미처럼 그녀의 이마 위로 덮여 내렸다."]], [["Her nerves extended into those tresses, and her temper could always be softened by stroking them down.", "그녀의 신경은 그 머릿단 속까지 뻗어 있어서, 그것을 쓸어내리면 언제든 성미를 누그러뜨릴 수 있었다."], ["When her hair was brushed she would instantly sink into stillness and look like the Sphinx.", "머리를 빗질하면 그녀는 즉시 고요 속으로 가라앉아 스핑크스처럼 보였다."], ["If, in passing under one of the Egdon banks, any of its thick skeins were caught, as they sometimes were, by a prickly tuft of the large _Ulex Europæus_—which will act as a sort of hairbrush—she would go back a few steps, and pass against it a second time.", "에그던의 둑 하나를 지나다가 그 두꺼운 머릿단이 이따금 그러하듯 일종의 머리빗 노릇을 하는 커다란 가시금작화—울렉스 유로페우스—의 가시 돋친 덤불에 걸리면, 그녀는 몇 걸음 되돌아가 다시 한번 거기에 머리를 스쳤다."]], [["She had pagan eyes, full of nocturnal mysteries, and their light, as it came and went, and came again, was partially hampered by their oppressive lids and lashes; and of these the under lid was much fuller than it usually is with English women.", "그녀는 밤의 신비로 가득한 이교도의 눈을 지녔으며, 오고 가고 다시 오는 그 눈빛은 그녀의 묵직한 눈꺼풀과 속눈썹에 어느 정도 가려져 있었고, 그중 아래 눈꺼풀은 영국 여자들에게서 흔히 보이는 것보다 훨씬 도톰했다."], ["This enabled her to indulge in reverie without seeming to do so—she might have been believed capable of sleeping without closing them up.", "이 덕분에 그녀는 공상에 잠겨 있어도 그래 보이지 않을 수 있었으니—눈을 감지 않고도 잠들 수 있는 사람이라 믿어졌을 법했다."], ["Assuming that the souls of men and women were visible essences, you could fancy the colour of Eustacia’s soul to be flamelike.", "남녀의 영혼이 눈에 보이는 정수精髓라고 가정한다면, 유스테이샤의 영혼 빛깔은 불꽃 같으리라 상상할 수 있었다."], ["The sparks from it that rose into her dark pupils gave the same impression.", "거기서 솟아올라 그녀의 검은 눈동자로 들어가는 불꽃들도 똑같은 인상을 주었다."]], [["The mouth seemed formed less to speak than to quiver, less to quiver than to kiss.", "그 입은 말하기보다는 떨기 위해, 떨기보다는 입 맞추기 위해 빚어진 듯했다."], ["Some might have added, less to kiss than to curl.", "누군가는 입 맞추기보다는 비웃듯 휘어지기 위해 빚어졌다고 덧붙였을지도 모른다."], ["Viewed sideways, the closing-line of her lips formed, with almost geometric precision, the curve so well known in the arts of design as the cima-recta, or ogee.", "옆에서 보면 그녀 입술이 맞물리는 선은 거의 기하학적 정밀함으로, 디자인 예술에서 키마 렉타cima-recta, 곧 곡선 몰딩이라 불리는 그 잘 알려진 곡선을 이루었다."], ["The sight of such a flexible bend as that on grim Egdon was quite an apparition.", "음울한 에그던에서 그런 유연한 곡선을 본다는 것은 그야말로 유령의 출현 같은 일이었다."], ["It was felt at once that the mouth did not come over from Sleswig with a band of Saxon pirates whose lips met like the two halves of a muffin.", "그 입이, 입술이 머핀의 두 쪽처럼 맞붙던 색슨 해적 무리와 함께 슐레스비히에서 건너온 것이 아님을 누구나 단번에 느꼈다."], ["One had fancied that such lip-curves were mostly lurking underground in the South as fragments of forgotten marbles.", "그런 입술 곡선은 대개 남쪽 지방에서 잊힌 대리석 조각의 파편으로 땅속에 숨어 있으리라 여겨져왔다."], ["So fine were the lines of her lips that, though full, each corner of her mouth was as clearly cut as the point of a spear.", "그녀의 입술 선은 어찌나 고운지, 도톰하면서도 입가의 양 모서리는 창끝처럼 또렷하게 새겨져 있었다."], ["This keenness of corner was only blunted when she was given over to sudden fits of gloom, one of the phases of the night-side of sentiment which she knew too well for her years.", "이 모서리의 날카로움이 무뎌지는 것은 그녀가 별안간 우울에 사로잡힐 때뿐이었으니, 이는 그녀가 제 나이답지 않게 너무도 잘 알고 있던 감정의 밤 같은 면, 그 한 양상이었다."]], [["Her presence brought memories of such things as Bourbon roses, rubies, and tropical midnight; her moods recalled lotus-eaters and the march in Athalie; her motions, the ebb and flow of the sea; her voice, the viola.", "그녀의 존재는 부르봉 장미와 루비와 열대의 한밤 같은 것들을 떠올리게 했고, 그녀의 기분은 연꽃을 먹는 사람들과 「아탈리」의 행진곡을 떠올리게 했으며, 그녀의 동작은 바다의 썰물과 밀물을, 그녀의 목소리는 비올라를 떠올리게 했다."], ["In a dim light, and with a slight rearrangement of her hair, her general figure might have stood for that of either of the higher female deities.", "어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 머리카락을 조금만 고쳐 매면 그녀의 전체적인 자태는 더 높은 여신들 가운데 어느 누구로도 통할 만했다."], ["The new moon behind her head, an old helmet upon it, a diadem of accidental dewdrops round her brow, would have been adjuncts sufficient to strike the note of Artemis, Athena, or Hera respectively, with as close an approximation to the antique as that which passes muster on many respected canvases.", "머리 뒤의 초승달, 그 위에 얹은 낡은 투구, 이마를 두른 우연한 이슬방울의 왕관이라면 각각 아르테미스나 아테나, 혹은 헤라의 인상을 자아내기에 충분한 부속물이었을 것이며, 여러 존경받는 화폭에서 통용되는 것 못지않게 고대에 근접했을 것이다."]], [["But celestial imperiousness, love, wrath, and fervour had proved to be somewhat thrown away on netherward Egdon.", "하지만 천상의 위엄과 사랑과 분노와 열정은 저지대 에그던에서는 다소 헛되이 쓰이는 것으로 드러났다."], ["Her power was limited, and the consciousness of this limitation had biassed her development.", "그녀의 힘은 한정되어 있었고, 이 한계에 대한 의식이 그녀의 성장을 한쪽으로 기울게 했다."], ["Egdon was her Hades, and since coming there she had imbibed much of what was dark in its tone, though inwardly and eternally unreconciled thereto.", "에그던은 그녀의 하데스였으니, 그곳에 온 뒤로 그녀는 그곳의 어두운 기조를 많이 빨아들였으되 속으로는 영원히 그것과 화해하지 못했다."], ["Her appearance accorded well with this smouldering rebelliousness, and the shady splendour of her beauty was the real surface of the sad and stifled warmth within her.", "그녀의 외양은 이 속에서 타들어가는 반항심과 잘 어울렸으며, 그녀 아름다움의 그늘진 광채는 그 안의 슬프고 억눌린 온기의 진짜 표면이었다."], ["A true Tartarean dignity sat upon her brow, and not factitiously or with marks of constraint, for it had grown in her with years.", "진정한 타르타로스의 위엄이 그녀의 이마에 깃들어 있었으니, 꾸며낸 것도 억지스러운 흔적이 있는 것도 아니었으며, 세월과 함께 그녀 안에서 자라난 것이었다."]], [["Across the upper part of her head she wore a thin fillet of black velvet, restraining the luxuriance of her shady hair, in a way which added much to this class of majesty by irregularly clouding her forehead.", "머리 윗부분을 가로질러 그녀는 검은 벨벳의 가느다란 머리띠를 두르고 있었는데, 이는 그늘진 머리카락의 무성함을 제어하면서 이마를 불규칙하게 흐림으로써 이런 종류의 위엄을 한층 더해주었다."], ["“Nothing can embellish a beautiful face more than a narrow band drawn over the brow,” says Richter.", "\"좁은 띠를 이마 위로 두르는 것보다 아름다운 얼굴을 더 돋보이게 하는 것은 없다\"라고 리히터는 말한다."], ["Some of the neighbouring girls wore coloured ribbon for the same purpose, and sported metallic ornaments elsewhere; but if anyone suggested coloured ribbon and metallic ornaments to Eustacia Vye she laughed and went on.", "이웃의 처녀들 중 몇몇은 같은 목적으로 색 리본을 두르고 다른 곳에는 금속 장신구를 자랑삼아 달았지만, 누군가 유스테이샤 바이에게 색 리본과 금속 장신구를 권하면 그녀는 웃으며 가던 길을 갔다."]], [["Why did a woman of this sort live on Egdon Heath?", "어째서 이런 부류의 여인이 에그던 황야에 살았던가?"], ["Budmouth was her native place, a fashionable seaside resort at that date.", "버드머스가 그녀의 고향이었으니, 당시에는 유행하던 해변 휴양지였다."], ["She was the daughter of the bandmaster of a regiment which had been quartered there—a Corfiote by birth, and a fine musician—who met his future wife during her trip thither with her father the captain, a man of good family.", "그녀는 그곳에 주둔했던 한 연대 군악대장의 딸이었으며—코르푸 태생에 빼어난 음악가였던 그는—명문가 출신인 선장 아버지와 함께 그곳으로 여행 온 장차 자기 아내가 될 여인을 만났다."], ["The marriage was scarcely in accord with the old man’s wishes, for the bandmaster’s pockets were as light as his occupation.", "그 결혼은 군악대장의 호주머니가 그의 직업만큼이나 가벼웠던 탓에 노인의 바람에는 거의 부합하지 않았다."], ["But the musician did his best; adopted his wife’s name, made England permanently his home, took great trouble with his child’s education, the expenses of which were defrayed by the grandfather, and throve as the chief local musician till her mother’s death, when he left off thriving, drank, and died also.", "하지만 그 음악가는 최선을 다했으니, 아내의 성을 따랐고, 영국을 영구히 자기 집으로 삼았으며, 비용은 외할아버지가 댔으나 자식의 교육에 큰 공을 들였고, 아내가 죽기 전까지는 그 지방의 으뜸가는 음악가로 번성하다가, 아내가 죽자 번성하기를 그치고 술을 마시다가 역시 죽었다."], ["The girl was left to the care of her grandfather, who, since three of his ribs became broken in a shipwreck, had lived in this airy perch on Egdon, a spot which had taken his fancy because the house was to be had for next to nothing, and because a remote blue tinge on the horizon between the hills, visible from the cottage door, was traditionally believed to be the English Channel.", "소녀는 외할아버지의 보살핌에 맡겨졌는데, 그는 난파 사고로 갈비뼈 세 대가 부러진 뒤로 이 바람 잘 드는 에그던의 높은 자리에서 살아왔으니, 집을 거저나 다름없이 얻을 수 있었던 데다, 오두막 문에서 보이는 언덕들 사이 지평선의 아련한 푸른빛이 예로부터 영국 해협이라 믿어진 까닭에 그곳이 마음에 들었던 것이다."], ["She hated the change; she felt like one banished; but here she was forced to abide.", "그녀는 그 변화를 싫어했고, 추방당한 사람 같은 심정이었으나, 여기서 머물러 살도록 강요받았다."]], [["Thus it happened that in Eustacia’s brain were juxtaposed the strangest assortment of ideas, from old time and from new.", "그리하여 유스테이샤의 머릿속에는 옛 시절과 새 시절에서 온, 가장 기이한 갖가지 관념들이 나란히 놓이게 되었다."], ["There was no middle distance in her perspective—romantic recollections of sunny afternoons on an esplanade, with military bands, officers, and gallants around, stood like gilded letters upon the dark tablet of surrounding Egdon.", "그녀의 시야에는 중경中景이 없었으니—군악대와 장교들과 멋쟁이들에 둘러싸여 해변 산책로에서 보낸 화창한 오후들의 낭만적인 추억이, 그녀를 에워싼 에그던의 어두운 서판 위에 금박 글자처럼 놓여 있었다."], ["Every bizarre effect that could result from the random intertwining of watering-place glitter with the grand solemnity of a heath, was to be found in her.", "휴양지의 화려함이 황야의 장엄한 엄숙함과 무작위로 뒤얽힘으로써 빚어질 수 있는 온갖 기괴한 효과가 그녀 안에서 발견되었다."], ["Seeing nothing of human life now, she imagined all the more of what she had seen.", "이제는 인간의 삶을 전혀 보지 못하게 되자, 그녀는 자기가 보았던 것을 그만큼 더 상상했다."]], [["Where did her dignity come from?", "그녀의 위엄은 어디서 왔던가?"], ["By a latent vein from Alcinous’ line, her father hailing from Phæacia’s isle?—or from Fitzalan and De Vere, her maternal grandfather having had a cousin in the peerage?", "아버지가 파이아키아 섬 출신이니 알키노오스 가문에서 내려온 잠재된 핏줄에서?—아니면 외할아버지에게 귀족 사촌이 있었으니 피츠앨런과 드 비어 가문에서?"], ["Perhaps it was the gift of Heaven—a happy convergence of natural laws.", "어쩌면 그것은 하늘의 선물—자연 법칙의 다행스러운 수렴이었는지도 모른다."], ["Among other things opportunity had of late years been denied her of learning to be undignified, for she lived lonely.", "무엇보다 근래 들어 그녀는 위엄 없이 구는 법을 배울 기회를 박탈당했으니, 외롭게 살았기 때문이다."], ["Isolation on a heath renders vulgarity well-nigh impossible.", "황야에서의 고립은 천박함을 거의 불가능하게 만든다."], ["It would have been as easy for the heath-ponies, bats, and snakes to be vulgar as for her.", "그녀가 천박해지기란 황야의 조랑말과 박쥐와 뱀이 천박해지는 것만큼이나 어려웠을 것이다."], ["A narrow life in Budmouth might have completely demeaned her.", "버드머스에서의 옹졸한 삶이었다면 그녀를 완전히 격하시켰을지도 모른다."]], [["The only way to look queenly without realms or hearts to queen it over is to look as if you had lost them; and Eustacia did that to a triumph.", "다스릴 영토도 마음도 없이 여왕다워 보이는 유일한 길은 그것을 잃어버린 듯 보이는 것인데, 유스테이샤는 그것을 멋들어지게 해냈다."], ["In the captain’s cottage she could suggest mansions she had never seen.", "선장의 오두막에서 그녀는 한 번도 본 적 없는 대저택을 떠올리게 할 수 있었다."], ["Perhaps that was because she frequented a vaster mansion than any of them, the open hills.", "어쩌면 그것은 그녀가 그 어떤 대저택보다 더 광대한 저택, 곧 탁 트인 언덕을 드나들었기 때문이리라."], ["Like the summer condition of the place around her, she was an embodiment of the phrase “a populous solitude”—apparently so listless, void, and quiet, she was really busy and full.", "그녀를 둘러싼 그곳의 여름 풍경처럼, 그녀는 \"사람으로 붐비는 고독\"이라는 문구의 화신이었으니—겉으로는 그토록 나른하고 공허하고 고요해 보였으나, 실은 분주하고 충만했다."]], [["To be loved to madness—such was her great desire.", "미칠 듯이 사랑받는 것—이것이 그녀의 커다란 바람이었다."], ["Love was to her the one cordial which could drive away the eating loneliness of her days.", "그녀에게 사랑이란 나날을 갉아먹는 외로움을 몰아내줄 수 있는 단 하나의 강심제였다."], ["And she seemed to long for the abstraction called passionate love more than for any particular lover.", "그리고 그녀는 어느 특정한 연인보다도 격렬한 사랑이라 불리는 그 추상적 관념을 갈망하는 듯했다."]], [["She could show a most reproachful look at times, but it was directed less against human beings than against certain creatures of her mind, the chief of these being Destiny, through whose interference she dimly fancied it arose that love alighted only on gliding youth—that any love she might win would sink simultaneously with the sand in the glass.", "그녀는 때때로 더없이 책망하는 눈빛을 지을 수 있었으나, 그것은 인간을 향한 것이라기보다 자기 마음이 빚어낸 어떤 존재들을 향한 것이었으니, 그중 으뜸은 운명이었으며, 사랑이 미끄러져 가는 젊음에만 내려앉는다는 것—자기가 얻을 수 있는 어떤 사랑이든 모래시계의 모래와 함께 동시에 가라앉으리라는 것은 운명의 간섭에서 비롯된 일이라고 그녀는 어렴풋이 여겼다."], ["She thought of it with an ever-growing consciousness of cruelty, which tended to breed actions of reckless unconventionality, framed to snatch a year’s, a week’s, even an hour’s passion from anywhere while it could be won.", "그녀는 점점 자라나는 잔혹함의 의식과 함께 그것을 생각했으며, 이 의식은 얻을 수 있는 한 어디서든 일 년의, 일주일의, 단 한 시간의 열정이라도 낚아채려는 무모하게 인습을 벗어난 행동을 낳는 경향이 있었다."], ["Through want of it she had sung without being merry, possessed without enjoying, outshone without triumphing.", "그것이 없었던 탓에 그녀는 즐겁지 않으면서도 노래했고, 누리지 못하면서도 소유했으며, 의기양양하지 못하면서도 남들을 압도했다."], ["Her loneliness deepened her desire.", "그녀의 외로움은 욕망을 깊게 했다."], ["On Egdon, coldest and meanest kisses were at famine prices, and where was a mouth matching hers to be found?", "에그던에서는 가장 차갑고 가장 보잘것없는 입맞춤조차 기근의 시세였으니, 그녀의 입에 맞먹는 입을 어디서 찾을 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["Fidelity in love for fidelity’s sake had less attraction for her than for most women; fidelity because of love’s grip had much.", "충실함 그 자체를 위한 사랑의 충실함은 대다수 여자들에게보다 그녀에게 덜 매력적이었고, 사랑의 손아귀에 붙들린 까닭의 충실함은 큰 매력이 있었다."], ["A blaze of love, and extinction, was better than a lantern glimmer of the same which should last long years.", "사랑의 불길이 활활 타올랐다가 꺼지는 것이, 오랜 세월 지속되는 똑같은 사랑의 등불 깜빡임보다 나았다."], ["On this head she knew by prevision what most women learn only by experience—she had mentally walked round love, told the towers thereof, considered its palaces, and concluded that love was but a doleful joy.", "이 점에 관해서는 대다수 여자들이 경험으로만 배우는 것을 그녀는 예지로써 알았으니—마음속으로 사랑의 둘레를 돌아보고, 그 탑들을 헤아리고, 그 궁전들을 살핀 뒤, 사랑이란 애처로운 기쁨에 지나지 않는다는 결론에 이르렀다."], ["Yet she desired it, as one in a desert would be thankful for brackish water.", "그러나 사막에 있는 사람이 짠물에라도 감사하듯, 그녀는 그것을 갈망했다."]], [["She often repeated her prayers; not at particular times, but, like the unaffectedly devout, when she desired to pray.", "그녀는 자주 기도를 되풀이했으니, 정해진 시간에 하는 것이 아니라, 꾸밈없이 독실한 사람들처럼 기도하고 싶을 때 했다. 그녀의 기도는 늘 자발적인 것이었고, 흔히 이러했다."], ["Her prayer was always spontaneous, and often ran thus, “O deliver my heart from this fearful gloom and loneliness; send me great love from somewhere, else I shall die.”", "\"오, 이 무서운 우울과 외로움에서 제 마음을 구해주소서. 어디서든 제게 위대한 사랑을 보내주소서, 그러지 않으면 저는 죽고 말 것입니다.\""]], [["Her high gods were William the Conqueror, Strafford, and Napoleon Buonaparte, as they had appeared in the Lady’s History used at the establishment in which she was educated.", "그녀의 드높은 신들은 정복왕 윌리엄, 스트래퍼드, 나폴레옹 보나파르트였으니, 그녀가 교육받은 학교에서 쓰던 「숙녀를 위한 역사」에 나오는 모습 그대로였다."], ["Had she been a mother she would have christened her boys such names as Saul or Sisera in preference to Jacob or David, neither of whom she admired.", "만약 그녀가 어머니가 되었다면 야곱이나 다윗 같은 이름보다는—둘 다 그녀가 흠모하지 않았으니—사울이나 시스라 같은 이름을 아들들에게 붙였을 것이다."], ["At school she had used to side with the Philistines in several battles, and had wondered if Pontius Pilate were as handsome as he was frank and fair.", "학교에서 그녀는 여러 전투에서 블레셋 사람들 편을 들곤 했고, 본디오 빌라도가 솔직하고 공정한 만큼 잘생겼을까 궁금해하기도 했다."]], [["Thus she was a girl of some forwardness of mind, indeed, weighed in relation to her situation among the very rearward of thinkers, very original.", "이처럼 그녀는 다소 앞선 사고를 지닌 처녀였으니, 실로 자기 처지에 비추어 따져보면 사상가들 중에서도 가장 뒤떨어진 축에 속하면서도 무척 독창적이었다."], ["Her instincts towards social non-comformity were at the root of this.", "사회적 비순응을 향한 그녀의 본능이 이것의 뿌리였다."], ["In the matter of holidays, her mood was that of horses who, when turned out to grass, enjoy looking upon their kind at work on the highway.", "휴일에 관한 한 그녀의 기분은, 풀밭에 풀어놓아져도 큰길에서 제 동족이 일하는 모습을 바라보기 좋아하는 말들의 그것과 같았다."], ["She only valued rest to herself when it came in the midst of other people’s labour.", "그녀는 오직 남들이 노동하는 한가운데서 찾아올 때만 자기에게 주어지는 휴식을 귀하게 여겼다."], ["Hence she hated Sundays when all was at rest, and often said they would be the death of her.", "그래서 그녀는 만물이 쉬는 일요일을 싫어했으며, 일요일이 자기를 죽음으로 몰아넣을 거라고 곧잘 말했다."], ["To see the heathmen in their Sunday condition, that is, with their hands in their pockets, their boots newly oiled, and not laced up (a particularly Sunday sign), walking leisurely among the turves and furze-faggots they had cut during the week, and kicking them critically as if their use were unknown, was a fearful heaviness to her.", "황야의 사내들이 일요일 차림으로, 다시 말해 호주머니에 손을 찔러 넣고, 새로 기름칠한 부츠 끈을 매지 않은 채로(이는 유난히 일요일다운 표시였다), 한 주 동안 베어낸 뗏장과 가시금작화 단 사이를 한가로이 거닐며 마치 그 쓰임새를 모르는 양 비판하듯 발로 툭툭 차는 모습을 보는 것은 그녀에게 끔찍한 짐이었다."], ["To relieve the tedium of this untimely day she would overhaul the cupboards containing her grandfather’s old charts and other rubbish, humming Saturday-night ballads of the country people the while.", "이 때아닌 날의 지루함을 달래려고 그녀는 시골 사람들의 토요일 밤 발라드를 흥얼거리며 외할아버지의 낡은 해도海圖와 다른 잡동사니가 든 찬장을 뒤지곤 했다."], ["But on Saturday nights she would frequently sing a psalm, and it was always on a weekday that she read the Bible, that she might be unoppressed with a sense of doing her duty.", "하지만 토요일 밤이면 그녀는 곧잘 찬송가를 불렀고, 성경을 읽는 것은 늘 평일이었으니, 의무를 다하고 있다는 부담감에 짓눌리지 않기 위함이었다."]], [["Such views of life were to some extent the natural begettings of her situation upon her nature.", "이런 인생관은 어느 정도는 그녀의 처지가 그녀의 천성에 자연스럽게 낳은 산물이었다."], ["To dwell on a heath without studying its meanings was like wedding a foreigner without learning his tongue.", "황야에 살면서 그 의미를 헤아리지 않는 것은 외국인과 결혼하면서 그의 언어를 배우지 않는 것과 같았다."], ["The subtle beauties of the heath were lost to Eustacia; she only caught its vapours.", "황야의 미묘한 아름다움은 유스테이샤에게는 사라져버렸고, 그녀는 그 안개만을 붙잡았다."], ["An environment which would have made a contented woman a poet, a suffering woman a devotee, a pious woman a psalmist, even a giddy woman thoughtful, made a rebellious woman saturnine.", "만족한 여자라면 시인으로, 고통받는 여자라면 신봉자로, 경건한 여자라면 시편 작가로, 심지어 들뜬 여자라면 사색가로 만들었을 환경이, 반항하는 여자는 침울한 사람으로 만들었다."]], [["Eustacia had got beyond the vision of some marriage of inexpressible glory; yet, though her emotions were in full vigour, she cared for no meaner union.", "유스테이샤는 표현할 수 없는 영광의 결혼이라는 환상을 이미 넘어선 상태였으나, 그녀의 정념은 충만했음에도 그보다 못한 결합에는 마음을 두지 않았다."], ["Thus we see her in a strange state of isolation.", "그리하여 우리는 그녀가 기이한 고립 상태에 처한 것을 본다."], ["To have lost the godlike conceit that we may do what we will, and not to have acquired a homely zest for doing what we can, shows a grandeur of temper which cannot be objected to in the abstract, for it denotes a mind that, though disappointed, forswears compromise.", "우리가 원하는 것을 할 수 있다는 신과 같은 자만을 잃어버리고도 우리가 할 수 있는 것을 하는 소박한 열의를 얻지 못했다는 것은, 추상적으로는 흠잡을 수 없는 기개의 위대함을 보여주는 것이니, 실망했을지언정 타협을 거부하는 정신을 가리키기 때문이다."], ["But, if congenial to philosophy, it is apt to be dangerous to the commonwealth.", "하지만 철학에는 어울릴지언정 그것은 공동체에는 위험하기 십상이다."], ["In a world where doing means marrying, and the commonwealth is one of hearts and hands, the same peril attends the condition.", "무언가를 한다는 것이 결혼함을 뜻하고 공동체가 마음과 손의 공동체인 세상에서는, 그런 상태에 똑같은 위험이 따른다."]], [["And so we see our Eustacia—for at times she was not altogether unlovable—arriving at that stage of enlightenment which feels that nothing is worth while, and filling up the spare hours of her existence by idealizing Wildeve for want of a better object.", "그리하여 우리는 우리의 유스테이샤가—이따금 그녀는 아주 사랑스럽지 않은 것도 아니었으니—무엇 하나 할 가치가 없다고 느끼는 그런 깨달음의 단계에 이르러, 더 나은 대상이 없는 탓에 와일디브를 이상화하며 자기 존재의 남는 시간을 채우는 것을 본다."], ["This was the sole reason of his ascendency: she knew it herself.", "이것이 그가 우위를 점한 유일한 이유였으니, 그녀 자신도 그것을 알았다."], ["At moments her pride rebelled against her passion for him, and she even had longed to be free.", "어떤 순간이면 그녀의 자존심이 그를 향한 정념에 맞서 반항했고, 그녀는 심지어 자유로워지기를 갈망하기도 했다."], ["But there was only one circumstance which could dislodge him, and that was the advent of a greater man.", "하지만 그를 밀어낼 수 있는 상황은 단 하나뿐이었으니, 그것은 더 위대한 남자의 등장이었다."]], [["For the rest, she suffered much from depression of spirits, and took slow walks to recover them, in which she carried her grandfather’s telescope and her grandmother’s hourglass—the latter because of a peculiar pleasure she derived from watching a material representation of time’s gradual glide away.", "그 밖에 그녀는 심한 우울에 시달렸고, 그것을 회복하려고 느릿한 산책을 다녔으니, 그럴 때면 외할아버지의 망원경과 외할머니의 모래시계를 들고 다녔는데—후자는 시간이 점차 미끄러져 가는 것을 물질로 나타낸 모습을 지켜보는 데서 얻는 독특한 즐거움 때문이었다."], ["She seldom schemed, but when she did scheme, her plans showed rather the comprehensive strategy of a general than the small arts called womanish, though she could utter oracles of Delphian ambiguity when she did not choose to be direct.", "그녀는 좀처럼 책략을 꾸미지 않았으나, 꾸밀 때면 그녀의 계획은 여자다운 잔재주라기보다 장군의 포괄적인 전략을 드러냈으니, 다만 직설적이고 싶지 않을 때면 델포이 신탁처럼 모호한 말을 내뱉을 수 있었다."], ["In heaven she will probably sit between the Héloïses and the Cleopatras.", "천국에서 그녀는 아마 엘로이즈들과 클레오파트라들 사이에 앉을 것이다."]], [["VIII.", "VIII."], ["Those Who Are Found Where There Is Said to Be Nobody", "아무도 없다는 곳에서 발견되는 이들"]], [["As soon as the sad little boy had withdrawn from the fire he clasped the money tight in the palm of his hand, as if thereby to fortify his courage, and began to run.", "슬픈 어린 소년은 불에서 물러나자마자 마치 그것으로 용기를 북돋우려는 듯 손바닥 안에 동전을 꼭 쥐고 달리기 시작했다."], ["There was really little danger in allowing a child to go home alone on this part of Egdon Heath.", "에그던 황야의 이 지역에서 아이가 혼자 집에 가도록 두는 것은 실로 위험이 거의 없는 일이었다."], ["The distance to the boy’s house was not more than three-eighths of a mile, his father’s cottage, and one other a few yards further on, forming part of the small hamlet of Mistover Knap: the third and only remaining house was that of Captain Vye and Eustacia, which stood quite away from the small cottages and was the loneliest of lonely houses on these thinly populated slopes.", "소년의 집까지 거리는 팔분의 삼 마일이 채 안 되었으니, 그의 아버지의 오두막과 거기서 몇 야드 더 떨어진 또 다른 오두막 한 채가 미스토버 냅이라는 작은 마을의 일부를 이루었고, 셋째이자 유일하게 남은 집은 바이 선장과 유스테이샤의 집이었는데, 이 집은 작은 오두막들에서 완전히 떨어진 채 이 인구 드문 비탈에서 가장 외로운 집들 가운데서도 가장 외로운 집이었다."]], [["He ran until he was out of breath, and then, becoming more courageous, walked leisurely along, singing in an old voice a little song about a sailor-boy and a fair one, and bright gold in store.", "소년은 숨이 찰 때까지 달리다가, 한결 용감해지자 한가로이 걸으며 뱃사람 소년과 어여쁜 아가씨, 그리고 쟁여둔 빛나는 금에 관한 짧은 노래를 늙은이 같은 목소리로 불렀다."], ["In the middle of this the child stopped—from a pit under the hill ahead of him shone a light, whence proceeded a cloud of floating dust and a smacking noise.", "그 노래 도중에 아이는 멈춰 섰으니—앞쪽 언덕 아래 구덩이에서 불빛이 비쳐 나왔고, 거기서 떠도는 먼지구름과 철썩이는 소리가 흘러나왔던 것이다."]], [["Only unusual sights and sounds frightened the boy.", "흔치 않은 광경과 소리만이 소년을 겁먹게 했다."], ["The shrivelled voice of the heath did not alarm him, for that was familiar.", "황야의 쪼그라든 목소리는 익숙한 것이었기에 그를 놀라게 하지 않았다."], ["The thornbushes which arose in his path from time to time were less satisfactory, for they whistled gloomily, and had a ghastly habit after dark of putting on the shapes of jumping madmen, sprawling giants, and hideous cripples.", "이따금 가는 길에 나타나는 가시덤불은 좀 덜 만족스러웠으니, 음울하게 휘파람 소리를 냈고, 어두워진 뒤에는 펄쩍 뛰는 미치광이나 사지를 뻗은 거인, 흉측한 절름발이의 형상을 띠는 섬뜩한 버릇이 있었기 때문이다."], ["Lights were not uncommon this evening, but the nature of all of them was different from this.", "이날 저녁 불빛은 드물지 않았으나, 그 모든 불빛의 성질은 이것과 달랐다."], ["Discretion rather than terror prompted the boy to turn back instead of passing the light, with a view of asking Miss Eustacia Vye to let her servant accompany him home.", "공포라기보다 신중함에 이끌려 소년은 그 불빛을 지나치는 대신 되돌아가기로 했으니, 유스테이샤 바이 아씨에게 하인을 딸려 집까지 바래다주게 해달라고 청할 셈이었다."]], [["When the boy had reascended to the top of the valley he found the fire to be still burning on the bank, though lower than before.", "소년이 다시 계곡 꼭대기로 올라와 보니 불은 전보다 낮아지긴 했으나 여전히 둑 위에서 타오르고 있었다."], ["Beside it, instead of Eustacia’s solitary form, he saw two persons, the second being a man.", "그 곁에는 유스테이샤의 외로운 형체 대신 두 사람이 있었으니, 둘째는 한 남자였다."], ["The boy crept along under the bank to ascertain from the nature of the proceedings if it would be prudent to interrupt so splendid a creature as Miss Eustacia on his poor trivial account.", "소년은 자신의 보잘것없고 하찮은 용건 때문에 유스테이샤 아씨처럼 훌륭한 존재를 방해하는 것이 분별 있는 일일지를 그 일의 성질로부터 가늠해보려고 둑 아래로 살금살금 기어갔다."]], [["After listening under the bank for some minutes to the talk he turned in a perplexed and doubting manner and began to withdraw as silently as he had come.", "둑 아래에서 몇 분 동안 대화를 엿들은 뒤 그는 어리둥절하고 망설이는 태도로 돌아서서 왔던 것처럼 소리 없이 물러나기 시작했다."], ["That he did not, upon the whole, think it advisable to interrupt her conversation with Wildeve, without being prepared to bear the whole weight of her displeasure, was obvious.", "그가 대체로 보아, 그녀의 노여움 전체를 감당할 각오 없이 와일디브와의 대화를 방해하는 것이 현명하다고 여기지 않았음은 분명했다."]], [["Here was a Scyllæo-Charybdean position for a poor boy.", "가난한 소년에게는 이것이 스킬라와 카리브디스 사이의 처지였다."], ["Pausing when again safe from discovery, he finally decided to face the pit phenomenon as the lesser evil.", "다시 들킬 염려 없는 곳에서 멈춰 선 그는 마침내 더 작은 악으로서 구덩이의 현상과 마주하기로 작정했다."], ["With a heavy sigh he retraced the slope, and followed the path he had followed before.", "무거운 한숨과 함께 그는 비탈을 되짚어 올라가, 전에 따랐던 길을 따라갔다."]], [["The light had gone, the rising dust had disappeared—he hoped for ever.", "불빛은 사라졌고, 피어오르던 먼지도 사라졌으니—그가 바라기로는 영영."], ["He marched resolutely along, and found nothing to alarm him till, coming within a few yards of the sandpit, he heard a slight noise in front, which led him to halt.", "그는 단호히 행진해 갔고, 모래 구덩이에서 몇 야드 안에 이르렀을 때 앞쪽에서 희미한 소리가 들려 멈춰 서기 전까지는 그를 놀라게 할 만한 것을 아무것도 찾지 못했다."], ["The halt was but momentary, for the noise resolved itself into the steady bites of two animals grazing.", "멈춤은 잠깐뿐이었으니, 그 소리가 풀을 뜯는 두 짐승의 꾸준한 씹는 소리로 판명되었기 때문이다."]], [["“Two he’th-croppers down here,” he said aloud.", "\"황야 조랑말 두 마리가 여기 내려와 있네.\""], ["“I have never known ’em come down so far afore.”", "그가 소리 내어 말했다. \"이렇게 멀리까지 내려온 건 처음 봐.\""]], [["The animals were in the direct line of his path, but that the child thought little of; he had played round the fetlocks of horses from his infancy.", "짐승들은 그의 길 정면에 있었으나 아이는 그것을 대수롭지 않게 여겼으니, 어려서부터 말들의 발목 둘레에서 놀았던 것이다."], ["On coming nearer, however, the boy was somewhat surprised to find that the little creatures did not run off, and that each wore a clog, to prevent his going astray; this signified that they had been broken in.", "하지만 가까이 다가가자 소년은 그 작은 짐승들이 달아나지 않는 것과, 길을 잘못 들지 못하도록 저마다 나무 차꼬를 차고 있는 것을 보고 다소 놀랐으니, 이는 그것들이 길들여졌음을 뜻했다."], ["He could now see the interior of the pit, which, being in the side of the hill, had a level entrance.", "이제 그는 구덩이 내부를 볼 수 있었는데, 언덕 옆구리에 자리 잡은 까닭에 입구가 평평했다."], ["In the innermost corner the square outline of a van appeared, with its back towards him.", "가장 안쪽 모서리에는 짐마차의 네모난 윤곽이 그를 향해 뒤를 둔 채 나타났다."], ["A light came from the interior, and threw a moving shadow upon the vertical face of gravel at the further side of the pit into which the vehicle faced.", "내부에서 불빛이 새어 나와, 마차가 마주한 구덩이 저편의 수직 자갈 면 위로 움직이는 그림자를 던졌다."]], [["The child assumed that this was the cart of a gipsy, and his dread of those wanderers reached but to that mild pitch which titillates rather than pains.", "아이는 이것이 집시의 마차라고 짐작했고, 그 떠돌이들에 대한 그의 두려움은 고통이라기보다 오히려 짜릿함을 주는 정도의 가벼운 수준에 머물렀다."], ["Only a few inches of mud wall kept him and his family from being gipsies themselves.", "단지 몇 인치의 진흙 벽이 그와 그의 가족을 집시 신세에서 떼어놓고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["He skirted the gravel pit at a respectful distance, ascended the slope, and came forward upon the brow, in order to look into the open door of the van and see the original of the shadow.", "그는 적당한 거리를 두고 자갈 구덩이를 빙 돌아 비탈을 올라, 마차의 열린 문 안을 들여다보고 그림자의 원본을 보려고 꼭대기로 나아갔다."]], [["The picture alarmed the boy.", "그 광경에 소년은 겁을 먹었다."], ["By a little stove inside the van sat a figure red from head to heels—the man who had been Thomasin’s friend.", "마차 안 작은 난로 옆에 머리끝부터 발끝까지 붉은 형체가 앉아 있었으니—토머신의 친구였던 그 남자였다."], ["He was darning a stocking, which was red like the rest of him.", "그는 양말을 깁고 있었는데, 양말도 그의 나머지처럼 붉었다."], ["Moreover, as he darned he smoked a pipe, the stem and bowl of which were red also.", "게다가 양말을 기우며 파이프를 피우고 있었는데, 파이프의 대와 통도 붉었다."]], [["At this moment one of the heath-croppers feeding in the outer shadows was audibly shaking off the clog attached to its foot.", "이 순간 바깥 그림자 속에서 풀을 뜯던 황야 조랑말 하나가 발에 채운 차꼬를 떨어내려고 흔드는 소리가 또렷이 들렸다."], ["Aroused by the sound, the reddleman laid down his stocking, lit a lantern which hung beside him, and came out from the van.", "그 소리에 깨어난 레들맨은 양말을 내려놓고 곁에 걸린 등을 켜고 마차에서 나왔다."], ["In sticking up the candle he lifted the lantern to his face, and the light shone into the whites of his eyes and upon his ivory teeth, which, in contrast with the red surrounding, lent him a startling aspect enough to the gaze of a juvenile.", "양초를 꽂아 세우면서 그가 등을 얼굴로 들어올리자, 불빛이 그의 흰자위와 상아 같은 이를 비췄고, 둘러싼 붉은빛과 대비되어 그것은 어린아이의 눈에는 충분히 섬뜩한 모습을 그에게 입혔다."], ["The boy knew too well for his peace of mind upon whose lair he had lighted.", "소년은 자기가 누구의 소굴에 발을 들였는지 마음이 편치 않을 만큼 너무 잘 알았다."], ["Uglier persons than gipsies were known to cross Egdon at times, and a reddleman was one of them.", "집시보다 더 흉한 사람들이 이따금 에그던을 건너는 것으로 알려져 있었는데, 레들맨도 그중 하나였다."]], [["“How I wish ’twas only a gipsy!”", "\"그냥 집시였으면 좋으련만!\""], ["he murmured.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["The man was by this time coming back from the horses.", "남자는 이때쯤 말들에게서 돌아오고 있었다."], ["In his fear of being seen the boy rendered detection certain by nervous motion.", "들킬까 봐 두려운 나머지 소년은 초조한 동작으로 도리어 들킴을 자초하고 말았다."], ["The heather and peat stratum overhung the brow of the pit in mats, hiding the actual verge.", "히스와 토탄 층이 구덩이 꼭대기 위로 매트처럼 드리워져 실제 가장자리를 가리고 있었다."], ["The boy had stepped beyond the solid ground; the heather now gave way, and down he rolled over the scarp of grey sand to the very foot of the man.", "소년은 단단한 땅 너머로 발을 디뎠고, 이제 히스가 무너지면서 그는 회색 모래 비탈을 굴러 떨어져 바로 그 남자의 발치에 이르렀다."]], [["The red man opened the lantern and turned it upon the figure of the prostrate boy.", "붉은 남자는 등을 열어 엎어진 소년의 형체에 그것을 비췄다."]], [["“Who be ye?”", "\"너는 누구냐?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Johnny Nunsuch, master!”", "\"조니 넌서치예요, 나리!\""]], [["“What were you doing up there?”", "\"거기서 뭘 하고 있었느냐?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"몰라요.\""]], [["“Watching me, I suppose?”", "\"나를 지켜보고 있었던 게로구나?\""]], [["“Yes, master.”", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["“What did you watch me for?”", "\"왜 나를 지켜봤느냐?\""]], [["“Because I was coming home from Miss Vye’s bonfire.”", "\"바이 아씨네 모닥불에서 집에 오는 길이었거든요.\""]], [["“Beest hurt?”", "\"다쳤느냐?\""]], [["“Why, yes, you be—your hand is bleeding.", "\"아니, 다쳤구나—손에서 피가 나는걸."], ["Come under my tilt and let me tie it up.”", "내 차양 아래로 와라, 싸매주마.\""]], [["“Please let me look for my sixpence.”", "\"제 육펜스 좀 찾게 해주세요.\""]], [["“How did you come by that?”", "\"그건 어떻게 생긴 거냐?\""]], [["“Miss Vye gied it to me for keeping up her bonfire.”", "\"바이 아씨가 모닥불을 계속 지펴준 값으로 주셨어요.\""]], [["The sixpence was found, and the man went to the van, the boy behind, almost holding his breath.", "육펜스를 찾았고, 남자는 마차로 갔으며, 소년은 거의 숨을 죽인 채 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["The man took a piece of rag from a satchel containing sewing materials, tore off a strip, which, like everything else, was tinged red, and proceeded to bind up the wound.", "남자는 바느질 도구가 든 가방에서 헝겊 조각을 꺼내, 다른 모든 것처럼 붉게 물든 한 가닥을 찢어내 상처를 싸매기 시작했다."]], [["“My eyes have got foggy-like—please may I sit down, master?”", "\"눈앞이 안개 낀 것 같아요—앉아도 될까요, 나리?\""], ["said the boy.", "소년이 말했다."]], [["“To be sure, poor chap.", "\"그럼, 가엾은 녀석."], ["’Tis enough to make you feel fainty.", "어지러울 만도 하지."], ["Sit on that bundle.”", "저 보따리에 앉아라.\""]], [["The man finished tying up the gash, and the boy said, “I think I’ll go home now, master.”", "남자는 베인 상처를 다 싸맸고, 소년이 말했다. \"이제 집에 가야겠어요, 나리.\""]], [["“You are rather afraid of me.", "\"네가 나를 좀 무서워하는구나."], ["Do you know what I be?”", "내가 뭔지 아느냐?\""]], [["The child surveyed his vermilion figure up and down with much misgiving and finally said, “Yes.”", "아이는 그의 주홍빛 형체를 위아래로 미심쩍게 훑어보다가 마침내 말했다. \"네.\""]], [["“Well, what?”", "\"그래, 뭐냐?\""]], [["“The reddleman!”", "\"레들맨이요!\""], ["he faltered.", "그가 떠듬떠듬 말했다."]], [["“Yes, that’s what I be.", "\"그래, 그게 나다."], ["Though there’s more than one.", "그렇지만 레들맨은 한 명이 아니다."], ["You little children think there’s only one cuckoo, one fox, one giant, one devil, and one reddleman, when there’s lots of us all.”", "너희 어린것들은 뻐꾸기도 한 마리, 여우도 한 마리, 거인도 하나, 악마도 하나, 레들맨도 하나뿐인 줄 알지만, 우리는 잔뜩 있단다.\""]], [["“Is there?", "\"그래요?"], ["You won’t carry me off in your bags, will ye, master?", "나리, 나를 자루에 넣어 데려가진 않을 거죠?"], ["’Tis said that the reddleman will sometimes.”", "레들맨이 가끔 그런다던데요.\""]], [["“Nonsense.", "\"무슨 소리."], ["All that reddlemen do is sell reddle.", "레들맨이 하는 일이라곤 레들을 파는 것뿐이다."], ["You see all these bags at the back of my cart?", "내 마차 뒤에 있는 이 자루들이 다 보이지?"], ["They are not full of little boys—only full of red stuff.”", "그건 어린 소년들로 차 있는 게 아니라—그냥 붉은 가루로만 차 있어.\""]], [["“Was you born a reddleman?”", "\"레들맨으로 태어났어요?\""]], [["“No, I took to it.", "\"아니, 이 일을 하게 된 거지."], ["I should be as white as you if I were to give up the trade—that is, I should be white in time—perhaps six months; not at first, because ’tis grow’d into my skin and won’t wash out.", "이 장사를 그만두면 나도 너처럼 하얘질 거다—그러니까 시간이 지나면 하얘질 거야—아마 여섯 달쯤. 처음에는 아니지, 이게 살갗에 배어들어 씻어도 안 빠지니까."], ["Now, you’ll never be afraid of a reddleman again, will ye?”", "자, 이제 다시는 레들맨을 무서워하지 않겠지?\""]], [["“No, never.", "\"네, 절대로요."], ["Willy Orchard said he seed a red ghost here t’other day—perhaps that was you?”", "윌리 오처드가 일전에 여기서 붉은 귀신을 봤다고 했는데—그게 나리였나 봐요?\""]], [["“I was here t’other day.”", "\"내가 일전에 여기 있었지.\""]], [["“Were you making that dusty light I saw by now?”", "\"방금 제가 본 그 먼지 같은 불빛을 만들고 있었어요?\""]], [["“Oh yes, I was beating out some bags.", "\"오 그래, 자루를 좀 두드려 털고 있었지."], ["And have you had a good bonfire up there?", "그래, 저 위에서 모닥불은 잘 즐겼느냐?"], ["I saw the light.", "불빛을 봤다."], ["Why did Miss Vye want a bonfire so bad that she should give you sixpence to keep it up?”", "바이 아씨가 왜 그렇게 모닥불을 간절히 원해서 너한테 육펜스를 주며 계속 지피게 한 거냐?\""]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"몰라요."], ["I was tired, but she made me bide and keep up the fire just the same, while she kept going up across Rainbarrow way.”", "저는 피곤했는데, 아씨가 계속 레인배로 쪽으로 오르락내리락하는 동안 저더러 그대로 남아서 불을 지키라고 시켰어요.\""]], [["“And how long did that last?”", "\"그게 얼마나 계속됐느냐?\""]], [["“Until a hopfrog jumped into the pond.”", "\"개구리가 못으로 뛰어들 때까지요.\""]], [["The reddleman suddenly ceased to talk idly.", "레들맨은 갑자기 한가로이 떠들기를 멈췄다."], ["“A hopfrog?”", "\"개구리?\""], ["he inquired.", "그가 물었다."], ["“Hopfrogs don’t jump into ponds this time of year.”", "\"개구리는 이맘때 못으로 뛰어들지 않는데.\""]], [["“They do, for I heard one.”", "\"뛰어들어요, 제가 한 마리 들었거든요.\""]], [["“Certain-sure?”", "\"틀림없느냐?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["She told me afore that I should hear’n; and so I did.", "아씨가 미리 들릴 거라고 했고, 정말 그랬어요."], ["They say she’s clever and deep, and perhaps she charmed ’en to come.”", "사람들이 그러는데 아씨는 영리하고 속이 깊대요, 어쩌면 아씨가 그걸 홀려서 오게 했나 봐요.\""]], [["“And what then?”", "\"그래서 어떻게 됐느냐?\""]], [["“Then I came down here, and I was afeard, and I went back; but I didn’t like to speak to her, because of the gentleman, and I came on here again.”", "\"그래서 저는 여기로 내려왔는데, 무서워서 되돌아갔어요. 그렇지만 그 신사분 때문에 아씨한테 말 걸기가 싫어서 다시 이리로 온 거예요.\""]], [["“A gentleman—ah!", "\"신사라—아!"], ["What did she say to him, my man?”", "아씨가 그 사람한테 뭐라고 하더냐, 얘야?\""]], [["“Told him she supposed he had not married the other woman because he liked his old sweetheart best; and things like that.”", "\"신사분이 다른 여자랑 결혼하지 않은 건 옛 연인을 가장 좋아하기 때문인 것 같다고 했어요, 그런 비슷한 얘기요.\""]], [["“What did the gentleman say to her, my sonny?”", "\"신사가 아씨한테 뭐라고 하더냐, 우리 아가?\""]], [["“He only said he did like her best, and how he was coming to see her again under Rainbarrow o’ nights.”", "\"그저 아씨를 가장 좋아한다고만 했어요, 그리고 밤마다 레인배로 아래로 아씨를 다시 보러 오겠다고요.\""]], [["“Ha!”", "\"하!\""], ["cried the reddleman, slapping his hand against the side of his van so that the whole fabric shook under the blow.", "레들맨이 마차 옆구리를 손으로 철썩 쳐서 한 방에 그 구조물 전체가 흔들렸다."], ["“That’s the secret o’t!”", "\"그게 비밀이로구나!\""]], [["The little boy jumped clean from the stool.", "어린 소년이 걸상에서 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다."]], [["“My man, don’t you be afraid,”", "\"얘야, 무서워 마라.\""], ["said the dealer in red, suddenly becoming gentle.", "붉은 것을 파는 사람이 갑자기 부드러워지며 말했다."], ["“I forgot you were here.", "\"네가 여기 있는 걸 잊었구나."], ["That’s only a curious way reddlemen have of going mad for a moment; but they don’t hurt anybody.", "이건 그저 레들맨들이 잠깐 미쳐 날뛰는 별난 버릇일 뿐이다, 그렇지만 아무도 해치진 않아."], ["And what did the lady say then?”", "그래서 아씨가 그다음에 뭐라고 하더냐?\""]], [["“I can’t mind.", "\"기억이 안 나요."], ["Please, Master Reddleman, may I go home-along now?”", "레들맨 나리, 이제 집에 가도 될까요?\""]], [["“Ay, to be sure you may.", "\"그럼, 물론 가도 되지."], ["I’ll go a bit of ways with you.”", "내가 좀 바래다주마.\""]], [["He conducted the boy out of the gravel pit and into the path leading to his mother’s cottage.", "그는 소년을 자갈 구덩이에서 데리고 나와 그의 어머니의 오두막으로 이어지는 길에 들여놓았다."], ["When the little figure had vanished in the darkness the reddleman returned, resumed his seat by the fire, and proceeded to darn again.", "그 작은 형체가 어둠 속으로 사라지자 레들맨은 돌아와 다시 불가의 자리에 앉아 또다시 양말을 깁기 시작했다."]], [["IX.", "IX."], ["Love Leads a Shrewd Man into Strategy", "사랑은 약삭빠른 사내를 책략으로 이끌고"]], [["Reddlemen of the old school are now but seldom seen.", "옛 부류의 레들맨은 이제 좀처럼 볼 수 없다."], ["Since the introduction of railways Wessex farmers have managed to do without these Mephistophelian visitants, and the bright pigment so largely used by shepherds in preparing sheep for the fair is obtained by other routes.", "철도가 도입된 이후로 웨식스의 농부들은 이 메피스토펠레스 같은 방문객들 없이도 그럭저럭 지내게 되었고, 양치기들이 양을 장에 내보내려고 손질할 때 그토록 많이 쓰던 그 선명한 안료도 다른 경로로 구하게 되었다."], ["Even those who yet survive are losing the poetry of existence which characterized them when the pursuit of the trade meant periodical journeys to the pit whence the material was dug, a regular camping out from month to month, except in the depth of winter, a peregrination among farms which could be counted by the hundred, and in spite of this Arab existence the preservation of that respectability which is insured by the never-failing production of a well-lined purse.", "아직 살아남은 이들조차 그들의 특징이었던 존재의 시정詩情을 잃어가고 있으니, 그 시절 이 장사를 한다는 것은 재료를 캐내는 구덩이로 주기적으로 여행을 떠나고, 한겨울만 빼고는 달마다 규칙적으로 야영을 하며, 수백으로 헤아릴 만한 농장들 사이를 두루 떠도는 일을 뜻했으되, 이런 아랍인 같은 생활에도 불구하고 결코 비는 일이 없는 두둑한 지갑이 보증하는 그 체면을 지켜내는 일을 뜻했다."]], [["Reddle spreads its lively hues over everything it lights on, and stamps unmistakably, as with the mark of Cain, any person who has handled it half an hour.", "레들은 닿는 모든 것 위에 제 생기 넘치는 빛깔을 퍼뜨리며, 그것을 반 시간만 다룬 사람이라면 누구든 카인의 표식처럼 틀림없는 낙인을 찍는다."]], [["A child’s first sight of a reddleman was an epoch in his life.", "아이가 레들맨을 처음 본 것은 그의 인생에서 한 시대였다."], ["That blood-coloured figure was a sublimation of all the horrid dreams which had afflicted the juvenile spirit since imagination began.", "그 핏빛 형체는 상상이 시작된 이래 어린 영혼을 괴롭혀온 온갖 끔찍한 꿈들의 승화였다."], ["“The reddleman is coming for you!” had been the formulated threat of Wessex mothers for many generations.", "\"레들맨이 너를 잡으러 온다!\"는 여러 세대에 걸쳐 웨식스 어머니들의 공식적인 위협이었다."], ["He was successfully supplanted for a while, at the beginning of the present century, by Buonaparte; but as process of time rendered the latter personage stale and ineffective the older phrase resumed its early prominence.", "그는 금세기 초 한동안 보나파르트에게 성공적으로 자리를 빼앗겼으나, 세월의 흐름이 후자의 인물을 진부하고 무력하게 만들자 더 오래된 그 문구가 본래의 두드러짐을 되찾았다."], ["And now the reddleman has in his turn followed Buonaparte to the land of worn-out bogeys, and his place is filled by modern inventions.", "그리고 이제 레들맨은 그 차례가 되어 보나파르트의 뒤를 따라 닳아빠진 도깨비들의 땅으로 갔으며, 그의 자리는 근대의 발명품들로 채워진다."]], [["The reddleman lived like a gipsy; but gipsies he scorned.", "레들맨은 집시처럼 살았으나, 집시는 경멸했다."], ["He was about as thriving as travelling basket and mat makers; but he had nothing to do with them.", "그는 떠돌이 바구니 장수나 돗자리 장수만큼이나 그럭저럭 형편이 괜찮았으나, 그들과는 아무 상관이 없었다."], ["He was more decently born and brought up than the cattledrovers who passed and repassed him in his wanderings; but they merely nodded to him.", "그는 떠돌이 중에 그를 지나치고 또 지나치던 가축 몰이꾼들보다 더 점잖게 태어나 자랐으나, 그들은 그에게 그저 고갯짓이나 했을 뿐이다."], ["His stock was more valuable than that of pedlars; but they did not think so, and passed his cart with eyes straight ahead.", "그의 물건은 행상꾼들의 것보다 더 값졌으나, 그들은 그렇게 여기지 않고 정면만 바라본 채 그의 마차를 지나쳤다."], ["He was such an unnatural colour to look at that the men of roundabouts and waxwork shows seemed gentlemen beside him; but he considered them low company, and remained aloof.", "그는 보기에 어찌나 부자연스러운 빛깔이었던지 회전목마와 밀랍 인형 가게의 사내들조차 그의 곁에서는 신사처럼 보였으나, 그는 그들을 천한 무리로 여겨 거리를 두었다."], ["Among all these squatters and folks of the road the reddleman continually found himself; yet he was not of them.", "이 모든 무단 거주자들과 길 위의 사람들 사이에서 레들맨은 끊임없이 자신을 발견했으되, 그는 그들에 속하지 않았다."], ["His occupation tended to isolate him, and isolated he was mostly seen to be.", "그의 직업은 그를 고립시키는 경향이 있었고, 고립된 채로 그는 대개 눈에 띄었다."]], [["It was sometimes suggested that reddlemen were criminals for whose misdeeds other men wrongfully suffered—that in escaping the law they had not escaped their own consciences, and had taken to the trade as a lifelong penance.", "레들맨이 범죄자들이며, 다른 사람들이 그들의 악행 때문에 부당하게 고초를 겪는 거라는—법은 피했어도 양심은 피하지 못하여 평생의 속죄로서 이 장사에 들어섰다는 말이 때때로 나돌았다."], ["Else why should they have chosen it?", "안 그러면 어째서 그것을 택했겠는가?"], ["In the present case such a question would have been particularly apposite.", "지금의 경우에 그런 의문은 특히나 적절했을 것이다."], ["The reddleman who had entered Egdon that afternoon was an instance of the pleasing being wasted to form the ground-work of the singular, when an ugly foundation would have done just as well for that purpose.", "그날 오후 에그던에 들어선 레들맨은, 흉한 토대로도 충분했을 그 목적을 위해 기이함의 바탕을 이루는 데 호감 가는 것이 허비된 사례였다."], ["The one point that was forbidding about this reddleman was his colour.", "이 레들맨에게서 거부감을 일으키는 단 한 가지는 그의 빛깔이었다."], ["Freed from that he would have been as agreeable a specimen of rustic manhood as one would often see.", "그것에서 벗어났다면 그는 좀처럼 보기 힘들 만큼 호감 가는 시골 사내의 본보기였을 것이다."], ["A keen observer might have been inclined to think—which was, indeed, partly the truth—that he had relinquished his proper station in life for want of interest in it.", "예리한 관찰자라면—이는 실로 부분적으로 진실이었으니—그가 흥미를 잃어 인생에서 제 마땅한 자리를 내놓았다고 생각하기 쉬웠을 것이다."], ["Moreover, after looking at him one would have hazarded the guess that good nature, and an acuteness as extreme as it could be without verging on craft, formed the framework of his character.", "게다가 그를 보고 나면 누구든, 선한 천성과, 교활함으로 치우치기 직전까지 갈 만큼 극도로 예리한 면이 그의 성격의 골격을 이룬다고 짐작했을 것이다."]], [["While he darned the stocking his face became rigid with thought.", "양말을 깁는 동안 그의 얼굴은 생각으로 굳어졌다."], ["Softer expressions followed this, and then again recurred the tender sadness which had sat upon him during his drive along the highway that afternoon.", "그 뒤로 한결 부드러운 표정이 따랐고, 다시금 그날 오후 큰길을 달리는 동안 그에게 깃들어 있던 애틋한 슬픔이 되살아났다."], ["Presently his needle stopped.", "이윽고 그의 바늘이 멈췄다."], ["He laid down the stocking, arose from his seat, and took a leathern pouch from a hook in the corner of the van.", "그는 양말을 내려놓고 자리에서 일어나, 마차 모퉁이의 갈고리에서 가죽 주머니를 꺼냈다."], ["This contained among other articles a brown-paper packet, which, to judge from the hinge-like character of its worn folds, seemed to have been carefully opened and closed a good many times.", "거기에는 다른 물건들과 함께 갈색 종이 꾸러미가 들어 있었는데, 닳아빠진 접힌 자국이 경첩 같은 성질을 띤 것으로 보아 여러 차례 조심스럽게 펼쳐졌다 닫혔던 모양이었다."], ["He sat down on a three-legged milking stool that formed the only seat in the van, and, examining his packet by the light of a candle, took thence an old letter and spread it open.", "그는 마차 안의 유일한 자리인 세 발 달린 젖 짜는 의자에 앉아, 양초 불빛으로 꾸러미를 살피다가 거기서 낡은 편지 한 통을 꺼내 펼쳤다."], ["The writing had originally been traced on white paper, but the letter had now assumed a pale red tinge from the accident of its situation; and the black strokes of writing thereon looked like the twigs of a winter hedge against a vermilion sunset.", "글씨는 본디 흰 종이 위에 쓰였으나, 편지는 이제 처해 있던 곳의 사정으로 연한 붉은빛을 띠게 되었고, 그 위의 검은 필획은 주홍빛 노을을 배경으로 한 겨울 산울타리의 잔가지처럼 보였다."], ["The letter bore a date some two years previous to that time, and was signed “Thomasin Yeobright.”", "편지에는 그때로부터 두 해쯤 전의 날짜가 적혀 있었고, \"토머신 요브라이트\"라고 서명되어 있었다."], ["It ran as follows:—", "내용은 다음과 같았다—"]], [["DEAR DIGGORY VENN,—The question you put when you overtook me coming home from Pond-close gave me such a surprise that I am afraid I did not make you exactly understand what I meant.", "친애하는 디고리 벤에게,—폰드클로즈에서 집으로 오는 저를 따라잡으셨을 때 던지신 그 질문에 저는 너무 놀란 나머지 제가 무슨 뜻이었는지 정확히 이해시켜드리지 못한 것 같아 두렵습니다."], ["Of course, if my aunt had not met me I could have explained all then at once, but as it was there was no chance.", "물론 숙모님이 저를 만나지만 않으셨다면 그 자리에서 곧장 다 설명드릴 수 있었겠지만, 형편이 그렇지 못했지요."], ["I have been quite uneasy since, as you know I do not wish to pain you, yet I fear I shall be doing so now in contradicting what I seemed to say then.", "저는 그 후로 줄곧 마음이 편치 않았으니, 아시다시피 당신을 아프게 하고 싶지 않은데도, 그때 제가 한 듯한 말을 뒤집음으로써 지금 그러고 있을까 봐 두렵기 때문입니다."], ["I cannot, Diggory, marry you, or think of letting you call me your sweetheart.", "디고리, 저는 당신과 결혼할 수도, 당신이 저를 연인이라 부르게 할 생각을 할 수도 없습니다."], ["I could not, indeed, Diggory.", "정말로 그럴 수 없어요, 디고리."], ["I hope you will not much mind my saying this, and feel in a great pain.", "제가 이런 말을 한다고 너무 마음 쓰거나 크게 아파하지 않으시기를 바랍니다."], ["It makes me very sad when I think it may, for I like you very much, and I always put you next to my cousin Clym in my mind.", "그러실까 생각하면 무척 슬퍼지니, 저는 당신을 무척 좋아하고, 마음속으로 늘 당신을 사촌 클림 다음에 두었기 때문입니다."], ["There are so many reasons why we cannot be married that I can hardly name them all in a letter.", "우리가 결혼할 수 없는 이유가 너무 많아서 편지에 다 적기조차 어려울 정도예요."], ["I did not in the least expect that you were going to speak on such a thing when you followed me, because I had never thought of you in the sense of a lover at all.", "당신이 저를 따라오셨을 때 그런 이야기를 하시리라고는 조금도 예상하지 못했으니, 저는 당신을 연인이라는 의미로는 전혀 생각해본 적이 없었기 때문입니다."], ["You must not becall me for laughing when you spoke; you mistook when you thought I laughed at you as a foolish man.", "당신이 말씀하셨을 때 제가 웃었다고 저를 욕하지 말아주세요. 제가 당신을 어리석은 사람이라며 비웃었다고 생각하셨다면 오해하신 거예요."], ["I laughed because the idea was so odd, and not at you at all.", "저는 그 생각이 너무 이상해서 웃은 것이지, 당신을 비웃은 게 결코 아닙니다."], ["The great reason with my own personal self for not letting you court me is, that I do not feel the things a woman ought to feel who consents to walk with you with the meaning of being your wife.", "제 개인적인 본심에서 당신이 저에게 구애하도록 두지 못하는 가장 큰 이유는, 당신의 아내가 될 뜻으로 당신과 함께 걸음을 같이하기로 승낙하는 여자가 마땅히 느껴야 할 감정을 제가 느끼지 못한다는 것입니다."], ["It is not as you think, that I have another in my mind, for I do not encourage anybody, and never have in my life.", "당신이 생각하시듯 제 마음에 다른 사람이 있어서가 아니니, 저는 아무도 부추기지 않으며 평생 그런 적도 없었습니다."], ["Another reason is my aunt.", "또 다른 이유는 제 숙모님입니다."], ["She would not, I know, agree to it, even if I wished to have you.", "제가 당신을 원한다 해도 숙모님은 동의하지 않으실 거예요."], ["She likes you very well, but she will want me to look a little higher than a small dairy-farmer, and marry a professional man.", "숙모님은 당신을 무척 좋아하시지만, 제가 소규모 낙농가보다는 조금 더 높이 보아 전문직 사내와 결혼하기를 바라실 거예요."], ["I hope you will not set your heart against me for writing plainly, but I felt you might try to see me again, and it is better that we should not meet.", "솔직하게 적었다고 저에게 마음을 닫지 않으시기를 바라지만, 당신이 저를 다시 보려 하실지도 모른다는 생각이 들었고, 우리는 만나지 않는 편이 낫겠어요."], ["I shall always think of you as a good man, and be anxious for your well-doing.", "저는 늘 당신을 선한 사람으로 생각하고 당신이 잘되기를 간절히 바라겠습니다."], ["I send this by Jane Orchard’s little maid,—And remain Diggory, your faithful friend,", "이 편지는 제인 오처드의 어린 하녀 편에 보냅니다,—그럼 이만 줄이며, 디고리, 당신의 충실한 벗,"]], [["To Mr.", "토머신 요브라이트."], ["VENN, Dairy-farmer.", "낙농가 벤 씨께."]], [["Since the arrival of that letter, on a certain autumn morning long ago, the reddleman and Thomasin had not met till today.", "오래전 어느 가을 아침에 그 편지가 도착한 이래로 레들맨과 토머신은 오늘까지 만나지 못했다."], ["During the interval he had shifted his position even further from hers than it had originally been, by adopting the reddle trade; though he was really in very good circumstances still.", "그 사이에 그는 레들 장사를 택함으로써 자신의 처지를 본디보다 그녀에게서 한층 더 멀리 옮겨놓았으나, 실은 여전히 형편이 무척 넉넉했다."], ["Indeed, seeing that his expenditure was only one-fourth of his income, he might have been called a prosperous man.", "실로 지출이 수입의 사분의 일에 불과한 것을 보면 그는 번창한 사내라 불릴 만했다."]], [["Rejected suitors take to roaming as naturally as unhived bees; and the business to which he had cynically devoted himself was in many ways congenial to Venn.", "거절당한 구혼자는 벌집에서 쫓겨난 벌처럼 자연스레 떠돌게 되며, 그가 냉소적으로 몸 바친 그 일은 여러모로 벤에게 잘 맞았다."], ["But his wanderings, by mere stress of old emotions, had frequently taken an Egdon direction, though he never intruded upon her who attracted him thither.", "하지만 그의 떠돎은 단지 옛 감정의 압박만으로도 곧잘 에그던 방향을 향했으니, 비록 그곳으로 그를 끌어들인 그녀에게 결코 끼어들지는 않았지만 말이다."], ["To be in Thomasin’s heath, and near her, yet unseen, was the one ewe-lamb of pleasure left to him.", "토머신의 황야에 머무는 것, 그녀 가까이에 있되 눈에 띄지 않는 것이 그에게 남은 단 하나의 귀한 즐거움이었다."]], [["Then came the incident of that day, and the reddleman, still loving her well, was excited by this accidental service to her at a critical juncture to vow an active devotion to her cause, instead of, as hitherto, sighing and holding aloof.", "그러다 그날의 사건이 일어났고, 여전히 그녀를 깊이 사랑하던 레들맨은 중대한 고비에 그녀에게 우연히 봉사하게 된 데에 자극받아, 지금껏처럼 한숨이나 쉬며 멀찍이 떨어져 있는 대신, 그녀의 대의에 적극적인 헌신을 바치기로 맹세하게 되었다."], ["After what had happened it was impossible that he should not doubt the honesty of Wildeve’s intentions.", "그날 일어난 일을 보고서 그가 와일디브의 의도가 정직한지 의심하지 않을 수는 없었다."], ["But her hope was apparently centred upon him; and dismissing his regrets Venn determined to aid her to be happy in her own chosen way.", "하지만 그녀의 희망은 분명 그에게 쏠려 있었고, 벤은 자기 회한을 떨쳐버리고 그녀가 스스로 택한 방식으로 행복해지도록 돕기로 작정했다."], ["That this way was, of all others, the most distressing to himself, was awkward enough; but the reddleman’s love was generous.", "이 방식이 다른 무엇보다도 그 자신에게 가장 괴로운 것이라는 점은 충분히 거북한 일이었으나, 레들맨의 사랑은 너그러웠다."]], [["His first active step in watching over Thomasin’s interests was taken about seven o’clock the next evening and was dictated by the news which he had learnt from the sad boy.", "토머신의 이익을 보살피려는 그의 첫 적극적 발걸음은 이튿날 저녁 일곱 시 무렵에 내디뎌졌으며, 슬픈 소년에게서 알아낸 소식이 시킨 것이었다."], ["That Eustacia was somehow the cause of Wildeve’s carelessness in relation to the marriage had at once been Venn’s conclusion on hearing of the secret meeting between them.", "유스테이샤가 어떻게든 결혼에 관한 와일디브의 무관심의 원인이라는 것이, 그 둘 사이의 은밀한 만남을 듣자마자 벤이 내린 결론이었다."], ["It did not occur to his mind that Eustacia’s love signal to Wildeve was the tender effect upon the deserted beauty of the intelligence which her grandfather had brought home.", "유스테이샤가 와일디브에게 보낸 사랑의 신호가 외할아버지가 가져온 소식이 버림받은 미인에게 미친 애틋한 영향이라는 데에는 그의 생각이 미치지 못했다."], ["His instinct was to regard her as a conspirator against rather than as an antecedent obstacle to Thomasin’s happiness.", "그의 본능은 그녀를 토머신의 행복에 앞선 장애물이라기보다는 그것을 거스르는 음모자로 여기는 쪽이었다."]], [["During the day he had been exceedingly anxious to learn the condition of Thomasin, but he did not venture to intrude upon a threshold to which he was a stranger, particularly at such an unpleasant moment as this.", "낮 동안 그는 토머신의 형편을 알고 싶어 몹시 안달했으나, 자신이 낯선 이로 머무는 문턱에, 특히 지금처럼 이토록 거북한 순간에 끼어들 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["He had occupied his time in moving with his ponies and load to a new point in the heath, eastward to his previous station; and here he selected a nook with a careful eye to shelter from wind and rain, which seemed to mean that his stay there was to be a comparatively extended one.", "그는 조랑말들과 짐을 끌고 황야의 새 지점으로, 이전 자리에서 동쪽으로 옮기며 시간을 보냈고, 여기서 바람과 비를 피할 자리를 신중한 눈으로 골랐는데, 이는 그곳에서의 체류가 비교적 길어지리라는 뜻인 듯했다."], ["After this he returned on foot some part of the way that he had come; and, it being now dark, he diverged to the left till he stood behind a holly bush on the edge of a pit not twenty yards from Rainbarrow.", "그 후 그는 왔던 길의 일부를 걸어서 되돌아갔고, 이제 어두워졌으므로 왼쪽으로 방향을 틀어 레인배로에서 스무 야드도 안 떨어진 구덩이 가장자리의 호랑가시나무 덤불 뒤에 섰다."]], [["He watched for a meeting there, but he watched in vain.", "그는 거기서 만남을 기다렸으나, 헛되이 기다렸다."], ["Nobody except himself came near the spot that night.", "그날 밤 그 자신 말고는 아무도 그곳에 가까이 오지 않았다."]], [["But the loss of his labour produced little effect upon the reddleman.", "하지만 헛수고는 레들맨에게 별다른 영향을 주지 않았다."], ["He had stood in the shoes of Tantalus, and seemed to look upon a certain mass of disappointment as the natural preface to all realizations, without which preface they would give cause for alarm.", "그는 탄탈로스의 처지에 서본 적이 있었고, 어느 정도의 실망은 모든 실현에 앞선 자연스러운 서막이며, 그 서막이 없다면 도리어 불안을 일으킬 거라고 여기는 듯했다."], ["The same hour the next evening found him again at the same place; but Eustacia and Wildeve, the expected trysters, did not appear.", "이튿날 저녁 같은 시각에 그는 다시 같은 자리에 있었으나, 기다리던 밀회의 두 사람, 유스테이샤와 와일디브는 나타나지 않았다."]], [["He pursued precisely the same course yet four nights longer, and without success.", "그는 나흘 밤을 더 똑같은 방식으로 밀고 나갔으되 성과가 없었다."], ["But on the next, being the day-week of their previous meeting, he saw a female shape floating along the ridge and the outline of a young man ascending from the valley.", "하지만 그다음 날, 곧 그들의 앞선 만남에서 꼭 일주일 되는 날, 그는 능선을 따라 떠가는 여자의 형체와 계곡에서 올라오는 젊은 남자의 윤곽을 보았다."], ["They met in the little ditch encircling the barrow—the original excavation from which it had been thrown up by the ancient British people.", "그들은 봉분을 에워싼 작은 도랑—고대 브리튼 사람들이 봉분을 쌓아올리려고 본디 파낸 자리—에서 만났다."]], [["The reddleman, stung with suspicion of wrong to Thomasin, was aroused to strategy in a moment.", "토머신에게 부당한 일이 벌어진다는 의심에 찔린 레들맨은 한순간에 책략에 눈을 떴다."], ["He instantly left the bush and crept forward on his hands and knees.", "그는 즉시 덤불을 떠나 손과 무릎으로 기어 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["When he had got as close as he might safely venture without discovery he found that, owing to a cross-wind, the conversation of the trysting pair could not be overheard.", "들키지 않고 안전하게 갈 수 있는 만큼 가까이 다가가 보니, 맞바람 탓에 밀회하는 두 사람의 대화를 엿들을 수 없었다."]], [["Near him, as in divers places about the heath, were areas strewn with large turves, which lay edgeways and upside down awaiting removal by Timothy Fairway, previous to the winter weather.", "그의 가까이에는, 황야 곳곳의 여러 곳에서처럼, 겨울 날씨에 앞서 티머시 페어웨이가 옮겨가기를 기다리며 모로 세워져 뒤집힌 큰 뗏장이 흩어진 곳들이 있었다."], ["He took two of these as he lay, and dragged them over him till one covered his head and shoulders, the other his back and legs.", "그는 누운 채로 그중 둘을 집어, 하나는 머리와 어깨를, 다른 하나는 등과 다리를 덮을 때까지 제 위로 끌어왔다."], ["The reddleman would now have been quite invisible, even by daylight; the turves, standing upon him with the heather upwards, looked precisely as if they were growing.", "이제 레들맨은 대낮에도 거의 보이지 않게 되었을 것이니, 히스가 위로 향한 채 그 위에 선 뗏장들은 영락없이 자라고 있는 것처럼 보였다."], ["He crept along again, and the turves upon his back crept with him.", "그는 다시 기어갔고, 등에 얹힌 뗏장들도 그와 함께 기어갔다."], ["Had he approached without any covering the chances are that he would not have been perceived in the dusk; approaching thus, it was as though he burrowed underground.", "그가 아무것도 덮지 않고 다가갔더라면 십중팔구 어스름 속에서 눈에 띄지 않았을 테지만, 이렇게 다가가니 마치 땅속으로 파고드는 듯했다."], ["In this manner he came quite close to where the two were standing.", "이런 식으로 그는 둘이 서 있는 곳에 아주 가까이 이르렀다."]], [["“Wish to consult me on the matter?”", "\"이 일을 두고 나랑 의논하고 싶다고요?\""], ["reached his ears in the rich, impetuous accents of Eustacia Vye.", "유스테이샤 바이의 풍성하고 격정적인 어조가 그의 귀에 닿았다."], ["“Consult me?", "\"나랑 의논해요?"], ["It is an indignity to me to talk so—I won’t bear it any longer!”", "그렇게 말하다니 나에 대한 모욕이에요—더는 못 참겠어요!\""], ["She began weeping.", "그녀가 울기 시작했다."], ["“I have loved you, and have shown you that I loved you, much to my regret; and yet you can come and say in that frigid way that you wish to consult with me whether it would not be better to marry Thomasin.", "\"나는 당신을 사랑했고, 후회막급일 만큼 당신을 사랑한다는 걸 당신에게 보여주었는데, 그런데도 당신은 와서 토머신과 결혼하는 게 낫지 않겠는지 나랑 의논하고 싶다고 그 차디찬 투로 말하다니."], ["Better—of course it would be.", "낫다—당연히 낫겠죠."], ["Marry her—she is nearer to your own position in life than I am!”", "그 여자랑 결혼해요—그 여자는 나보다 당신 처지에 더 가까우니까!\""]], [["“Yes, yes; that’s very well,”", "\"그래, 그래. 그건 다 좋은 말이오.\""], ["said Wildeve peremptorily.", "와일디브가 단호하게 말했다."], ["“But we must look at things as they are.", "\"하지만 우리는 일을 있는 그대로 봐야 하오."], ["Whatever blame may attach to me for having brought it about, Thomasin’s position is at present much worse than yours.", "이 일을 빚어낸 데 어떤 비난이 나에게 따르든, 토머신의 처지는 지금 당신 것보다 훨씬 나쁘오."], ["I simply tell you that I am in a strait.”", "나는 그저 내가 진퇴양난에 빠졌다고 말하는 거요.\""]], [["“But you shall not tell me!", "\"하지만 나한테 말하지 말아요!"], ["You must see that it is only harassing me.", "그건 그저 나를 괴롭히는 짓일 뿐이라는 걸 알아야죠."], ["Damon, you have not acted well; you have sunk in my opinion.", "데이먼, 당신은 처신을 잘하지 못했어요. 내 안에서 당신은 추락했어요."], ["You have not valued my courtesy—the courtesy of a lady in loving you—who used to think of far more ambitious things.", "당신은 내 호의를—훨씬 더 야심 찬 것들을 생각하곤 했던 한 숙녀가 당신을 사랑하는 그 호의를—귀하게 여기지 않았어요."], ["But it was Thomasin’s fault.", "하지만 그건 토머신 탓이에요."], ["She won you away from me, and she deserves to suffer for it.", "그 여자가 당신을 나에게서 빼앗아갔으니, 그 때문에 고초를 겪어 마땅해요."], ["Where is she staying now?", "그 여자는 지금 어디 머물러요?"], ["Not that I care, nor where I am myself.", "내가 신경 쓴다는 게 아니라, 내가 어디 있는지도 모르겠어요."], ["Ah, if I were dead and gone how glad she would be!", "아, 내가 죽어 없어진다면 그 여자가 얼마나 기뻐할까!"], ["Where is she, I ask?”", "그 여자가 어디 있느냐고요?\""]], [["“Thomasin is now staying at her aunt’s shut up in a bedroom, and keeping out of everybody’s sight,”", "\"토머신은 지금 숙모 댁에 머물면서 침실에 틀어박혀 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않으려 하고 있소.\""], ["he said indifferently.", "그가 무심히 말했다."]], [["“I don’t think you care much about her even now,”", "\"지금도 당신이 그 여자한테 별로 마음을 쓰는 것 같진 않네요.\""], ["said Eustacia with sudden joyousness, “for if you did you wouldn’t talk so coolly about her.", "유스테이샤가 갑작스러운 환희에 차서 말했다. \"마음을 쓴다면 그렇게 냉담하게 그 여자 얘기를 하진 않을 테니까요."], ["Do you talk so coolly to her about me?", "그 여자한테도 나 얘기를 그렇게 냉담하게 해요?"], ["Ah, I expect you do!", "아, 그러겠죠!"], ["Why did you originally go away from me?", "애초에 왜 나에게서 떠나갔어요?"], ["I don’t think I can ever forgive you, except on one condition, that whenever you desert me, you come back again, sorry that you served me so.”", "한 가지 조건이 아니면 나는 당신을 영영 용서할 수 없을 것 같아요, 그 조건이란 당신이 언제 나를 버리든 나에게 그렇게 한 걸 뉘우치며 다시 돌아오는 거예요.\""]], [["“I never wish to desert you.”", "\"나는 결코 당신을 버리고 싶지 않소.\""]], [["“I do not thank you for that.", "\"그건 고맙지 않네요."], ["I should hate it to be all smooth.", "모든 게 매끄럽기만 한 건 싫어요."], ["Indeed, I think I like you to desert me a little once now and then.", "사실 나는 당신이 가끔 한 번씩 나를 조금 버려주는 게 좋아요."], ["Love is the dismallest thing where the lover is quite honest.", "사랑은 연인이 너무 정직하면 더없이 음울한 거예요."], ["O, it is a shame to say so; but it is true!”", "오, 이런 말 하다니 부끄럽지만, 그래도 사실인걸요!\""], ["She indulged in a little laugh.", "그녀가 가벼운 웃음을 지었다."], ["“My low spirits begin at the very idea.", "\"그런 생각만으로도 내 기운이 가라앉기 시작해요."], ["Don’t you offer me tame love, or away you go!”", "나에게 길든 사랑 따위 내밀지 말아요, 안 그러면 당장 꺼져버려요!\""]], [["“I wish Tamsie were not such a confoundedly good little woman,” said Wildeve, “so that I could be faithful to you without injuring a worthy person. It is I who am the sinner after all; I am not worth the little finger of either of you.”", "와일드브가 입을 열어 말하길, \"탐신이 그토록 지독하게 착한 여자만 아니었더라면, 그래서 누구 멀쩡한 사람에게 상처 주지 않으면서도 당신한테 충실할 수 있었더라면 좋았을 텐데, 결국 죄인은 나요, 나는 당신네 둘 가운데 어느 쪽의 새끼손가락만도 못한 인간이니.\""]], [["“But you must not sacrifice yourself to her from any sense of justice,”", "\"하지만 무슨 정의감 때문에 자신을 그 여자한테 희생할 건 없어요.\""], ["replied Eustacia quickly.", "유스테시아가 재빨리 받아쳤다."], ["“If you do not love her it is the most merciful thing in the long run to leave her as she is.", "\"그 여자를 사랑하지 않는다면 길게 보아서는 지금 그대로 놓아두는 게 가장 자비로운 일이에요."], ["That’s always the best way.", "그게 언제나 가장 좋은 방법이고요."], ["There, now I have been unwomanly, I suppose.", "자, 이제 보니 내가 또 여자답지 못한 소리를 했나 봐요."], ["When you have left me I am always angry with myself for things that I have said to you.”", "당신이 떠나고 나면 나는 늘 당신한테 한 말들 때문에 나 자신에게 화가 나니.\""]], [["Wildeve walked a pace or two among the heather without replying.", "와일드브는 대꾸 없이 히스 사이를 한두 걸음 거닐었다."], ["The pause was filled up by the intonation of a pollard thorn a little way to windward, the breezes filtering through its unyielding twigs as through a strainer.", "그 침묵을 바람 불어오는 쪽 멀지 않은 곳에 선 가지치기한 가시나무의 가락이 채웠는데, 미풍이 체를 통과하듯 그 굽힘 없는 잔가지들 사이로 걸러져 나왔다."], ["It was as if the night sang dirges with clenched teeth.", "마치 밤이 이를 악문 채 만가를 부르는 것 같았다."]], [["She continued, half sorrowfully, “Since meeting you last, it has occurred to me once or twice that perhaps it was not for love of me you did not marry her.", "그녀는 반쯤 서글픈 목소리로 이어 말했다. \"지난번 당신을 만난 뒤로, 어쩌면 당신이 그 여자와 결혼하지 않은 게 나를 사랑해서가 아니었을지도 모른다는 생각이 한두 번 들었어요."], ["Tell me, Damon—I’ll try to bear it.", "말해줘요, 데이먼―견뎌볼 테니."], ["Had I nothing whatever to do with the matter?”", "이 일에 나는 정말 아무 상관도 없었던 건가요?\""]], [["“Do you press me to tell?”", "\"기어이 말하라고 닦달하는 거요?\""]], [["“Yes, I must know.", "\"네, 알아야겠어요."], ["I see I have been too ready to believe in my own power.”", "보아하니 내가 내 힘을 너무 쉽게 믿어버렸나 봐요.\""]], [["“Well, the immediate reason was that the license would not do for the place, and before I could get another she ran away.", "\"음, 당장의 이유라면 혼인 허가증이 그 장소에 맞지 않았던 거요, 그래서 내가 다른 걸 구하기도 전에 그 여자가 달아나버린 거고."], ["Up to that point you had nothing to do with it.", "거기까지는 당신과 아무 상관이 없소."], ["Since then her aunt has spoken to me in a tone which I don’t at all like.”", "그 뒤로 그 여자 이모가 내가 도무지 마음에 들지 않는 말투로 내게 말을 해왔지만.\""]], [["“Yes, yes!", "\"그래요, 그래!"], ["I am nothing in it—I am nothing in it.", "나는 거기서 아무것도 아니에요―나는 거기서 아무것도 아니라고요."], ["You only trifle with me.", "당신은 그저 나를 가지고 노는 거예요."], ["Heaven, what can I, Eustacia Vye, be made of to think so much of you!”", "맙소사, 나 유스테시아 바이가 당신을 이토록 마음에 두다니, 대체 무엇으로 빚어진 인간인지!\""]], [["“Nonsense; do not be so passionate....", "\"말도 안 되는 소리, 그렇게 격해지지 마시오……"], ["Eustacia, how we roved among these bushes last year, when the hot days had got cool, and the shades of the hills kept us almost invisible in the hollows!”", "유스테시아, 작년에 더운 날들이 서늘해지고 언덕 그늘이 우묵한 데서 우리를 거의 보이지 않게 해주었을 때, 우리가 이 덤불 사이를 얼마나 쏘다녔던지!\""]], [["She remained in moody silence till she said, “Yes; and how I used to laugh at you for daring to look up to me!", "그녀는 침울한 침묵에 잠겨 있다가 입을 열었다. \"그래요, 그리고 감히 나를 우러러본다고 내가 당신을 얼마나 비웃었던지!"], ["But you have well made me suffer for that since.”", "하지만 그 뒤로 당신은 그 일로 나를 톡톡히 괴롭혔죠.\""]], [["“Yes, you served me cruelly enough until I thought I had found someone fairer than you.", "\"그래, 당신은 나를 충분히 모질게 다뤘소, 당신보다 더 고운 사람을 찾았다고 여길 때까지는."], ["A blessed find for me, Eustacia.”", "내게는 복된 발견이었지, 유스테시아.\""]], [["“Do you still think you found somebody fairer?”", "\"아직도 더 고운 사람을 찾았다고 여기나요?\""]], [["“Sometimes I do, sometimes I don’t.", "\"어떤 때는 그렇고, 어떤 때는 아니오."], ["The scales are balanced so nicely that a feather would turn them.”", "저울이 얼마나 절묘하게 맞아 있는지 깃털 하나로도 기울 정도지.\""]], [["“But don’t you really care whether I meet you or whether I don’t?”", "\"하지만 정말로 내가 당신을 만나든 안 만나든 아무 상관 없는 건가요?\""], ["she said slowly.", "그녀가 천천히 물었다."]], [["“I care a little, but not enough to break my rest,”", "\"조금은 상관있소, 그렇다고 잠을 설칠 정도는 아니지만.\""], ["replied the young man languidly.", "젊은이가 나른하게 대꾸했다."], ["“No, all that’s past.", "\"아니, 그건 다 지나간 일이오."], ["I find there are two flowers where I thought there was only one.", "하나뿐인 줄 알았던 자리에 꽃이 둘 있더란 걸 깨달았지."], ["Perhaps there are three, or four, or any number as good as the first....", "어쩌면 셋, 아니면 넷, 아니 첫 번째 못지않게 좋은 꽃이 몇 송이든 있을지도……"], ["Mine is a curious fate.", "내 팔자는 묘하단 말이오."], ["Who would have thought that all this could happen to me?”", "이 모든 일이 내게 닥칠 줄 누가 알았겠소?\""]], [["She interrupted with a suppressed fire of which either love or anger seemed an equally possible issue, “Do you love me now?”", "그녀는 사랑이든 분노든 어느 쪽으로도 똑같이 터져 나올 법한 억눌린 불길로 그의 말을 끊었다. \"지금 나를 사랑하나요?\""]], [["“Who can say?”", "\"누가 알겠소?\""]], [["“Tell me; I will know it!”", "\"말해요, 기어이 알아낼 테니!\""]], [["“I do, and I do not,”", "\"사랑하기도 하고, 사랑하지 않기도 하오.\""], ["said he mischievously.", "그가 짓궂게 말했다."], ["“That is, I have my times and my seasons.", "\"그러니까, 내게도 때가 있고 철이 있단 말이오."], ["One moment you are too tall, another moment you are too do-nothing, another too melancholy, another too dark, another I don’t know what, except—that you are not the whole world to me that you used to be, my dear.", "어떤 순간엔 당신이 너무 크고, 어떤 순간엔 너무 아무것도 안 하고, 어떤 순간엔 너무 우울하고, 어떤 순간엔 너무 어둡고, 또 어떤 순간엔 뭔지 모르겠지만―다만, 예전처럼 당신이 내게 온 세상은 아니라는 거요, 내 사랑."], ["But you are a pleasant lady to know and nice to meet, and I dare say as sweet as ever—almost.”", "그래도 당신은 알고 지내기 즐거운 여인이고 만나기 좋은 사람이고, 감히 말하건대 여전히 그 어느 때 못지않게 사랑스럽지―거의.\""]], [["Eustacia was silent, and she turned from him, till she said, in a voice of suspended mightiness, “I am for a walk, and this is my way.”", "유스테시아는 말이 없었고, 그에게서 몸을 돌렸다가 억눌린 위엄이 어린 목소리로 말했다. \"나는 산책을 하려는데, 이쪽이 내 길이에요.\""]], [["“Well, I can do worse than follow you.”", "\"음, 당신을 따라가는 것보다 못한 짓이야 얼마든지 할 수 있겠지.\""]], [["“You know you can’t do otherwise, for all your moods and changes!”", "\"당신은 별짓을 다 해도 그럴 수밖에 없다는 걸 알잖아요, 그 변덕과 변심을 다 부려봐도 말이에요!\""], ["she answered defiantly.", "그녀가 도전하듯 대꾸했다."], ["“Say what you will; try as you may; keep away from me all that you can—you will never forget me.", "\"뭐라고 하든, 아무리 애를 써도, 할 수 있는 한 내게서 멀어져 봐도―당신은 결코 나를 잊지 못해요."], ["You will love me all your life long.", "당신은 평생토록 나를 사랑할 거예요."], ["You would jump to marry me!”", "당신은 나와 결혼하려고 뛰어들 거라고요!\""]], [["“So I would!”", "\"그렇게 할 거요!\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일드브가 말했다."], ["“Such strange thoughts as I’ve had from time to time, Eustacia; and they come to me this moment.", "\"유스테시아, 때때로 나는 참 묘한 생각들을 해왔는데, 지금 이 순간에도 그게 떠오르는구려."], ["You hate the heath as much as ever; that I know.”", "당신은 여전히 이 히스를 끔찍이 싫어하지, 그건 나도 아오.\""]], [["“I do,”", "\"싫어해요.\""], ["she murmured deeply.", "그녀가 깊은 목소리로 중얼거렸다."], ["“’Tis my cross, my shame, and will be my death!”", "\"이건 내 십자가요, 내 수치요, 끝내 내 죽음이 될 거예요!\""]], [["“I abhor it too,”", "\"나도 이곳이 진저리나오.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“How mournfully the wind blows round us now!”", "\"지금 우리 둘레로 바람이 얼마나 구슬프게 부는지!\""]], [["She did not answer.", "그녀는 대답하지 않았다."], ["Its tone was indeed solemn and pervasive.", "그 소리는 과연 엄숙하고도 사방에 스며 있었다."], ["Compound utterances addressed themselves to their senses, and it was possible to view by ear the features of the neighbourhood.", "복합된 발성이 그들의 감각에 호소해왔고, 귀로써 인근의 생김새를 살펴볼 수도 있었다."], ["Acoustic pictures were returned from the darkened scenery; they could hear where the tracts of heather began and ended; where the furze was growing stalky and tall; where it had been recently cut; in what direction the fir-clump lay, and how near was the pit in which the hollies grew; for these differing features had their voices no less than their shapes and colours.", "어두워진 풍경에서 음향의 그림들이 되돌아왔으니, 그들은 히스의 너른 자락이 어디서 시작하고 끝나는지, 가시금작화가 어디서 줄기를 뻗어 키 크게 자라는지, 어디가 최근에 베인 곳인지, 전나무 무리가 어느 방향에 있는지, 호랑가시나무가 자라는 구덩이가 얼마나 가까운지 들을 수 있었으니, 이 서로 다른 생김새들은 그 형상과 빛깔 못지않게 저마다의 목소리를 지녔던 것이다."]], [["“God, how lonely it is!”", "\"하느님, 어쩌면 이리도 적막한지!\""], ["resumed Wildeve.", "와일드브가 다시 입을 열었다."], ["“What are picturesque ravines and mists to us who see nothing else?", "\"이것 말고는 아무것도 보지 못하는 우리에게 그림 같은 협곡과 안개가 다 무슨 소용이오?"], ["Why should we stay here?", "우리가 왜 여기 머물러야 하지?"], ["Will you go with me to America?", "나와 함께 미국으로 가지 않겠소?"], ["I have kindred in Wisconsin.”", "위스콘신에 친척이 있소.\""]], [["“That wants consideration.”", "\"그건 생각해봐야 할 일이에요.\""]], [["“It seems impossible to do well here, unless one were a wild bird or a landscape-painter.", "\"여기서는 야생의 새나 풍경화가가 아니고서는 잘 살아낼 도리가 없는 것 같소."], ["Well?”", "어떻소?\""]], [["“Give me time,”", "\"시간을 줘요.\""], ["she softly said, taking his hand.", "그녀가 그의 손을 잡으며 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“America is so far away.", "\"미국은 너무 멀어요."], ["Are you going to walk with me a little way?”", "나하고 조금만 같이 걸어줄래요?\""]], [["As Eustacia uttered the latter words she retired from the base of the barrow, and Wildeve followed her, so that the reddleman could hear no more.", "유스테시아는 뒤의 말을 내뱉으며 무덤 기슭에서 물러났고, 와일드브가 그녀를 뒤따랐으므로, 레들맨은 더는 아무것도 들을 수 없었다."]], [["He lifted the turves and arose.", "그는 뗏장을 들어올리고 몸을 일으켰다."], ["Their black figures sank and disappeared from against the sky.", "그들의 검은 형상이 하늘을 배경으로 가라앉으며 사라졌다."], ["They were as two horns which the sluggish heath had put forth from its crown, like a mollusc, and had now again drawn in.", "그들은 마치 굼뜬 히스가 연체동물처럼 제 머리에서 내밀었다가 이제 다시 거두어들인 두 개의 더듬이 같았다."]], [["The reddleman’s walk across the vale, and over into the next where his cart lay, was not sprightly for a slim young fellow of twenty-four.", "레들맨이 골짜기를 가로질러, 다시 수레가 놓인 다음 골짜기로 넘어가는 그 걸음걸이는, 스물네 살 호리호리한 젊은이치고는 활기차지 못했다."], ["His spirit was perturbed to aching.", "그의 정신은 아릴 만큼 흔들려 있었다."], ["The breezes that blew around his mouth in that walk carried off upon them the accents of a commination.", "그 걸음에서 그의 입가를 맴돌던 미풍이 저주의 가락을 실어갔다."]], [["He entered the van, where there was a fire in a stove.", "그는 난로에 불이 지펴진 짐마차 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Without lighting his candle he sat down at once on the three-legged stool, and pondered on what he had seen and heard touching that still-loved one of his.", "초에 불을 붙이지도 않은 채 그는 곧장 세발 걸상에 앉아, 아직도 사랑하는 그 사람에 관하여 보고 들은 것을 곰곰이 헤아렸다."], ["He uttered a sound which was neither sigh nor sob, but was even more indicative than either of a troubled mind.", "그는 한숨도 흐느낌도 아니지만, 그 어느 쪽보다도 심란한 마음을 더욱 잘 드러내는 소리를 냈다."]], [["“My Tamsie,”", "\"내 탐시.\""], ["he whispered heavily.", "그가 무겁게 속삭였다."], ["“What can be done?", "\"어찌하면 좋단 말인가?"], ["Yes, I will see that Eustacia Vye.”", "그래, 그 유스테시아 바이를 만나봐야겠다.\""]], [["X. A Desperate Attempt at Persuasion\nThe next morning, at the time when the height of the sun appeared very insignificant from any part of the heath as compared with the altitude of Rainbarrow, and when all the little hills in the lower levels were like an archipelago in a fog-formed Ægean, the reddleman came from the brambled nook which he had adopted as his quarters and ascended the slopes of Mistover Knap.", "이튿날 아침, 해의 높이가 히스 어느 곳에서 보더라도 레인배로의 높이에 비하면 참으로 보잘것없어 보이는 시각, 낮은 지대의 자그마한 언덕들이 죄다 안개로 빚어진 에게해의 군도처럼 보이는 시각에, 레들맨은 제 거처로 삼았던 가시덤불 우거진 구석에서 나와 미스토버 냅의 비탈을 올랐다."]], [["Though these shaggy hills were apparently so solitary, several keen round eyes were always ready on such a wintry morning as this to converge upon a passer-by.", "이 텁수룩한 언덕들이 겉보기에는 그토록 호젓했으나, 이런 겨울 아침이면 날카롭고 동그란 눈 여럿이 언제나 지나가는 이에게로 모여들 채비를 갖추고 있었다."], ["Feathered species sojourned here in hiding which would have created wonder if found elsewhere.", "다른 데서 발견되었다면 경이를 자아냈을 깃털 달린 종들이 여기 숨어 머물렀다."], ["A bustard haunted the spot, and not many years before this five and twenty might have been seen in Egdon at one time.", "능에 한 마리가 이곳을 즐겨 찾았고, 이보다 그리 멀지 않은 옛적에는 에그던에서 스물다섯 마리를 한꺼번에 볼 수도 있었으리라."], ["Marsh-harriers looked up from the valley by Wildeve’s.", "개구리매들이 와일드브의 집 옆 골짜기에서 올려다보았다."], ["A cream-coloured courser had used to visit this hill, a bird so rare that not more than a dozen have ever been seen in England; but a barbarian rested neither night nor day till he had shot the African truant, and after that event cream-coloured coursers thought fit to enter Egdon no more.", "크림빛 달리기새 한 마리가 이 언덕을 찾곤 했는데, 영국에서 통틀어 열두 마리도 채 본 적이 없을 만큼 희귀한 새였으나, 어느 야만인이 그 아프리카에서 온 길손을 쏘아 죽일 때까지 밤낮으로 쉬지 않았고, 그 일이 있은 뒤로 크림빛 달리기새들은 다시는 에그던에 발을 들이지 않는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼다."]], [["A traveller who should walk and observe any of these visitants as Venn observed them now could feel himself to be in direct communication with regions unknown to man.", "벤이 지금 이 길손들을 관찰하듯 그것들을 거닐며 살펴볼 나그네라면, 자신이 인간에게 알려지지 않은 영역과 직접 통하고 있다고 느낄 수 있었으리라."], ["Here in front of him was a wild mallard—just arrived from the home of the north wind.", "여기 그의 앞에 야생 청둥오리 한 마리가―북풍의 고향에서 막 당도한 채로―있었다."], ["The creature brought within him an amplitude of Northern knowledge.", "그 짐승은 북방의 앎을 한아름 그에게 품고 들여왔다."], ["Glacial catastrophes, snowstorm episodes, glittering auroral effects, Polaris in the zenith, Franklin underfoot—the category of his commonplaces was wonderful.", "빙하의 재앙, 눈보라의 사건, 반짝이는 오로라의 효과, 천정의 북극성, 발밑의 프랭클린―그 평범한 지식의 목록은 놀라웠다."], ["But the bird, like many other philosophers, seemed as he looked at the reddleman to think that a present moment of comfortable reality was worth a decade of memories.", "그러나 그 새는, 다른 숱한 철학자들처럼, 레들맨을 바라보며 안락한 현실의 지금 이 순간이 십 년치의 추억만큼 값지다고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["Venn passed on through these towards the house of the isolated beauty who lived up among them and despised them.", "벤은 이것들 사이를 지나, 이것들 가운데 외따로 살며 이것들을 업신여기는 그 고립된 미인의 집을 향해 나아갔다."], ["The day was Sunday; but as going to church, except to be married or buried, was exceptional at Egdon, this made little difference.", "그날은 일요일이었으나, 에그던에서는 혼례나 장례를 치르려는 게 아니고서는 교회에 가는 일이 드물었으니, 이 점은 별 차이가 없었다."], ["He had determined upon the bold stroke of asking for an interview with Miss Vye—to attack her position as Thomasin’s rival either by art or by storm, showing therein, somewhat too conspicuously, the want of gallantry characteristic of a certain astute sort of men, from clowns to kings.", "그는 바이 양에게 면담을 청한다는 대담한 한 수를 두기로 마음먹었던 터―기교로든 강습으로든 토머신의 연적이라는 그녀의 입지를 공략하려는 것이었으니, 거기서 그는, 다소 지나치게 두드러지게, 어릿광대에서 임금에 이르기까지 어떤 빈틈없는 부류의 사내들에게 특유한 그 신사다움의 결여를 드러내고 있었다."], ["The great Frederick making war on the beautiful Archduchess, Napoleon refusing terms to the beautiful Queen of Prussia, were not more dead to difference of sex than the reddleman was, in his peculiar way, in planning the displacement of Eustacia.", "위대한 프리드리히가 아름다운 여대공에게 전쟁을 벌인 것이나, 나폴레옹이 아름다운 프로이센 왕비에게 강화 조건을 거절한 것이나, 그 어느 것도 레들맨이 제 나름의 방식으로 유스테시아를 밀어내려 꾀하면서 성별의 차이에 무감한 것보다 더 무감하지는 않았다."]], [["To call at the captain’s cottage was always more or less an undertaking for the inferior inhabitants.", "선장의 오두막에 들르는 일은 미천한 주민들에게는 언제나 어지간한 큰일이었다."], ["Though occasionally chatty, his moods were erratic, and nobody could be certain how he would behave at any particular moment.", "더러 수다스럽기도 했으나 그의 기분은 종잡을 수가 없었고, 어느 한순간 그가 어떻게 굴지는 아무도 장담할 수 없었다."], ["Eustacia was reserved, and lived very much to herself.", "유스테시아는 말수가 적었고 거의 혼자 틀어박혀 지냈다."], ["Except the daughter of one of the cotters, who was their servant, and a lad who worked in the garden and stable, scarcely anyone but themselves ever entered the house.", "그들의 하인 노릇을 하는 어느 소작농의 딸과 정원과 마구간에서 일하는 사내아이 말고는, 그들 자신을 빼면 이 집에 발을 들이는 이가 거의 없었다."], ["They were the only genteel people of the district except the Yeobrights, and though far from rich, they did not feel that necessity for preserving a friendly face towards every man, bird, and beast which influenced their poorer neighbours.", "그들은 이 고장에서 요브라이트 일가를 빼면 유일하게 점잖은 사람들이었고, 부유함과는 거리가 멀었으나, 더 가난한 이웃들을 움직이는 그것―사람과 새와 짐승 하나하나에게 우호적인 낯을 지켜야 한다는 그 절박함을 느끼지 않았다."]], [["When the reddleman entered the garden the old man was looking through his glass at the stain of blue sea in the distant landscape, the little anchors on his buttons twinkling in the sun.", "레들맨이 정원에 들어섰을 때 노인은 망원경으로 멀리 풍경 속에 묻은 푸른 바다의 얼룩을 들여다보고 있었고, 단추에 달린 자그마한 닻들이 햇빛에 반짝였다."], ["He recognized Venn as his companion on the highway, but made no remark on that circumstance, merely saying, “Ah, reddleman—you here?", "그는 벤을 큰길에서 만난 길동무로 알아보았으나, 그 일에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않고 그저 이렇게 말했을 뿐이다. \"아, 레들맨―자네 여기 웬일인가?"], ["Have a glass of grog?”", "그로그 한 잔 들겠나?\""]], [["Venn declined, on the plea of it being too early, and stated that his business was with Miss Vye.", "벤은 너무 이르다는 핑계로 사양하고, 자기 볼일은 바이 양에게 있다고 밝혔다."], ["The captain surveyed him from cap to waistcoat and from waistcoat to leggings for a few moments, and finally asked him to go indoors.", "선장은 잠시 그를 모자에서 조끼까지, 조끼에서 각반까지 훑어보더니, 마침내 안으로 들어가라고 청했다."]], [["Miss Vye was not to be seen by anybody just then; and the reddleman waited in the window-bench of the kitchen, his hands hanging across his divergent knees, and his cap hanging from his hands.", "바이 양은 마침 그때 누구도 만날 수가 없었고, 레들맨은 부엌 창가 의자에서 두 손을 벌어진 무릎에 걸친 채, 그 손에 모자를 걸친 채 기다렸다."]], [["“I suppose the young lady is not up yet?”", "\"아가씨가 아직 안 일어나신 모양이지요?\""], ["he presently said to the servant.", "그가 이윽고 하인에게 물었다."]], [["“Not quite yet.", "\"아직은요."], ["Folks never call upon ladies at this time of day.”", "이런 시각에 숙녀를 찾아오는 사람은 없는 법이에요.\""]], [["“Then I’ll step outside,”", "\"그럼 밖에 나가 있겠소.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."], ["“If she is willing to see me, will she please send out word, and I’ll come in.”", "\"나를 만나줄 마음이 있거든 부디 전갈을 보내주시오, 그러면 들어오리다.\""]], [["The reddleman left the house and loitered on the hill adjoining.", "레들맨은 집을 나와 붙어 있는 언덕에서 어슬렁거렸다."], ["A considerable time elapsed, and no request for his presence was brought.", "적잖은 시간이 흘렀으나 그를 들라는 청은 전해지지 않았다."], ["He was beginning to think that his scheme had failed, when he beheld the form of Eustacia herself coming leisurely towards him.", "그가 자기 계략이 실패했나 보다 생각하기 시작할 무렵, 유스테시아 본인의 모습이 한가로이 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보았다."], ["A sense of novelty in giving audience to that singular figure had been sufficient to draw her forth.", "그 별난 인물에게 면담을 허락한다는 데서 느낀 새삼스러운 흥미만으로도 그녀를 끌어내기에 충분했던 것이다."]], [["She seemed to feel, after a bare look at Diggory Venn, that the man had come on a strange errand, and that he was not so mean as she had thought him; for her close approach did not cause him to writhe uneasily, or shift his feet, or show any of those little signs which escape an ingenuous rustic at the advent of the uncommon in womankind.", "디고리 벤을 슬쩍 한 번 보고 나서, 그녀는 이 사내가 묘한 용건으로 왔으며, 자신이 여겼던 만큼 천한 인물은 아니라는 것을 느끼는 듯했다. 그녀가 바싹 다가서도 그가 불편하게 몸을 비틀거나 발을 옮기거나, 여자들 가운데 흔치 않은 부류를 마주했을 때 순박한 시골뜨기에게서 새어나오는 그런 자잘한 기색을 조금도 내보이지 않았기 때문이다."], ["On his inquiring if he might have a conversation with her she replied, “Yes, walk beside me,” and continued to move on.", "자기와 대화를 나눌 수 있겠느냐는 그의 물음에 그녀는 \"그래요, 옆에서 걸어요\"라고 답하고는 계속 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["Before they had gone far it occurred to the perspicacious reddleman that he would have acted more wisely by appearing less unimpressionable, and he resolved to correct the error as soon as he could find opportunity.", "그들이 멀리 가기도 전에, 통찰력 있는 레들맨은 자신이 좀 덜 무덤덤하게 비쳤더라면 더 현명하게 처신했으리라는 데 생각이 미쳤고, 틈이 나는 대로 그 잘못을 바로잡기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“I have made so bold, miss, as to step across and tell you some strange news which has come to my ears about that man.”", "\"아가씨, 제가 외람되이 이렇게 건너와서, 그 사내에 관하여 제 귀에 들어온 묘한 소식을 알려드리려는 것입니다.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["what man?”", "무슨 사내요?\""]], [["He jerked his elbow to the southeast—the direction of the Quiet Woman.", "그는 팔꿈치를 남동쪽으로―콰이엇 우먼 쪽으로 홱 내질렀다."], ["Eustacia turned quickly to him.", "유스테시아가 재빨리 그를 향해 돌아섰다."], ["“Do you mean Mr. Wildeve?”", "\"와일드브 씨 말이에요?\""]], [["“Yes, there is trouble in a household on account of him, and I have come to let you know of it, because I believe you might have power to drive it away.”", "\"네, 그분 때문에 어느 집안에 풍파가 일었는데, 아가씨께 그걸 알려드리려 온 것입니다, 아가씨께서는 그걸 몰아낼 힘을 지니셨을지도 모른다고 믿기 때문이지요.\""]], [["“I?", "\"내가요?"], ["What is the trouble?”", "무슨 풍파인데요?\""]], [["“It is quite a secret.", "\"이건 아주 비밀스러운 일입니다."], ["It is that he may refuse to marry Thomasin Yeobright after all.”", "그분이 끝내 토머신 요브라이트와 결혼하기를 마다할지도 모른다는 것입니다.\""]], [["Eustacia, though set inwardly pulsing by his words, was equal to her part in such a drama as this.", "유스테시아는 그 말에 속이 두근거렸으나, 이런 극에서 제가 맡은 역할쯤은 감당할 줄 알았다."], ["She replied coldly, “I do not wish to listen to this, and you must not expect me to interfere.”", "그녀는 싸늘하게 대꾸했다. \"나는 이런 이야기를 듣고 싶지 않고, 당신은 내가 끼어들기를 바라서는 안 돼요.\""]], [["“But, miss, you will hear one word?”", "\"하지만 아가씨, 한마디만 들어주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I cannot.", "\"못 들어요."], ["I am not interested in the marriage, and even if I were I could not compel Mr. Wildeve to do my bidding.”", "나는 그 결혼에 관심이 없고, 설령 관심이 있다 한들 와일드브 씨더러 내 뜻대로 하라고 강요할 수도 없어요.\""]], [["“As the only lady on the heath I think you might,”", "\"이 히스에서 유일한 숙녀이시니 하실 수 있으리라 봅니다.\""], ["said Venn with subtle indirectness.", "벤이 교묘하게 에둘러 말했다."], ["“This is how the case stands.", "\"사정은 이렇습니다."], ["Mr. Wildeve would marry Thomasin at once, and make all matters smooth, if so be there were not another woman in the case.", "와일드브 씨는, 이 일에 다른 여자만 끼어 있지 않다면 당장이라도 토머신과 결혼해 만사를 매끈하게 정리하실 분입니다."], ["This other woman is some person he has picked up with, and meets on the heath occasionally, I believe.", "그 다른 여자란 그분이 어쩌다 사귀게 되어 가끔 이 히스에서 만나는 누군가라고 합니다."], ["He will never marry her, and yet through her he may never marry the woman who loves him dearly.", "그분은 결코 그 여자와 결혼하지 않을 텐데, 그러면서도 그 여자 때문에 자신을 끔찍이 사랑하는 여자와는 영영 결혼하지 못하게 될지도 모릅니다."], ["Now, if you, miss, who have so much sway over us menfolk, were to insist that he should treat your young neighbour Tamsin with honourable kindness and give up the other woman, he would perhaps do it, and save her a good deal of misery.”", "자, 우리 사내들을 그토록 쥐락펴락하시는 아가씨께서, 그분이 이웃 아가씨 탐신을 명예롭고 다정하게 대하고 그 다른 여자를 포기하라고 다그치신다면, 어쩌면 그분이 그리할 것이고, 그러면 탐신을 숱한 비참함에서 구하시는 게 될 것입니다.\""]], [["“Ah, my life!”", "\"아이고, 내 팔자야!\""], ["said Eustacia, with a laugh which unclosed her lips so that the sun shone into her mouth as into a tulip, and lent it a similar scarlet fire.", "유스테시아가 웃음을 터뜨렸는데, 그 웃음에 입술이 벌어지면서 튤립 속으로 들듯 햇빛이 입안으로 비쳐들어 거기에 똑같은 진홍빛 불꽃을 입혔다."], ["“You think too much of my influence over menfolk indeed, reddleman.", "\"레들맨, 정말이지 당신은 내가 사내들에게 미치는 영향을 너무 대단하게 여기는군요."], ["If I had such a power as you imagine I would go straight and use it for the good of anybody who has been kind to me—which Thomasin Yeobright has not particularly, to my knowledge.”", "만약 내게 당신이 상상하는 그런 힘이 있다면 나는 곧장 가서 내게 친절했던 누구든 그 사람을 위해 그 힘을 쓸 텐데―내가 아는 한 토머신 요브라이트는 딱히 그러지 않았어요.\""]], [["“Can it be that you really don’t know of it—how much she had always thought of you?”", "\"정말로 모르신단 말입니까―그 아가씨가 늘 아가씨를 얼마나 높이 여겼는지를요?\""]], [["“I have never heard a word of it.", "\"나는 그런 말 한마디도 들어본 적 없어요."], ["Although we live only two miles apart I have never been inside her aunt’s house in my life.”", "우리가 겨우 이 마일 떨어져 살아도, 나는 평생 그 이모 집 안에 들어가 본 적이 없어요.\""]], [["The superciliousness that lurked in her manner told Venn that thus far he had utterly failed.", "그녀의 태도에 도사린 거만함이 벤에게, 여기까지는 자신이 완전히 실패했음을 일러주었다."], ["He inwardly sighed and felt it necessary to unmask his second argument.", "그는 속으로 한숨을 내쉬며 두 번째 논거의 가면을 벗길 필요를 느꼈다."]], [["“Well, leaving that out of the question, ’tis in your power, I assure you, Miss Vye, to do a great deal of good to another woman.”", "\"음, 그건 차치하더라도, 장담컨대 다른 여자에게 큰 선행을 베푸는 일은 아가씨의 힘 안에 있습니다, 바이 양.\""]], [["She shook her head.", "그녀는 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“Your comeliness is law with Mr. Wildeve.", "\"아가씨의 고우심은 와일드브 씨에게 곧 법입니다."], ["It is law with all men who see ’ee.", "아가씨를 보는 사내라면 누구에게나 법이지요."], ["They say, ‘This well-favoured lady coming—what’s her name?", "다들 이럽니다. '저기 오는 저 잘난 숙녀가―이름이 뭐지?"], ["How handsome!’ Handsomer than Thomasin Yeobright,”", "어쩜 저리 어여쁠까!' 토머신 요브라이트보다 더 어여쁘다고요.\""], ["the reddleman persisted, saying to himself, “God forgive a rascal for lying!”", "레들맨은 속으로 '거짓말하는 이 악당을 하느님은 용서하소서!' 하고 되뇌면서 끈질기게 우겼다."], ["And she was handsomer, but the reddleman was far from thinking so.", "실제로 그녀가 더 어여뻤으나, 레들맨은 결코 그렇게 여기지 않았다."], ["There was a certain obscurity in Eustacia’s beauty, and Venn’s eye was not trained.", "유스테시아의 아름다움에는 어떤 가려진 데가 있었고, 벤의 눈은 단련되어 있지 않았다."], ["In her winter dress, as now, she was like the tiger-beetle, which, when observed in dull situations, seems to be of the quietest neutral colour, but under a full illumination blazes with dazzling splendour.", "지금처럼 겨울옷을 입으면 그녀는 길앞잡이 같았으니, 칙칙한 데서 보면 더없이 수수한 중간색을 띤 듯하지만 환한 빛 아래서는 눈부신 광채로 타오르는 그 벌레 말이다."]], [["Eustacia could not help replying, though conscious that she endangered her dignity thereby.", "유스테시아는 그렇게 함으로써 제 위엄을 위태롭게 한다는 걸 의식하면서도 대꾸하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["“Many women are lovelier than Thomasin,”", "\"토머신보다 더 사랑스러운 여자는 많아요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “so not much attaches to that.”", "\"그러니 거기에 대단한 의미를 둘 건 없어요.\""]], [["The reddleman suffered the wound and went on: “He is a man who notices the looks of women, and you could twist him to your will like withywind, if you only had the mind.”", "레들맨은 그 상처를 감내하고 말을 이었다. \"그분은 여자들의 용모를 눈여겨보는 사내인지라, 아가씨께서 마음만 먹으신다면 메꽃 줄기 비틀듯 그분을 아가씨 뜻대로 휘감으실 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Surely what she cannot do who has been so much with him I cannot do living up here away from him.”", "\"그토록 오래 그와 함께 지낸 여자도 못 하는 일을, 그에게서 멀리 떨어진 이 위에 사는 내가 할 수 있을 리 없잖아요.\""]], [["The reddleman wheeled and looked her in the face.", "레들맨은 홱 돌아서서 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다."], ["“Miss Vye!”", "\"바이 양!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Why do you say that—as if you doubted me?”", "\"어째서 그런 말을 하나요―나를 의심하기라도 하는 듯이?\""], ["She spoke faintly, and her breathing was quick.", "그녀는 힘없이 말했고, 숨결이 가빴다."], ["“The idea of your speaking in that tone to me!”", "\"당신이 내게 그런 말투로 말하다니!\""], ["she added, with a forced smile of hauteur.", "그녀는 거만함을 꾸며낸 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."], ["“What could have been in your mind to lead you to speak like that?”", "\"대체 무슨 속셈으로 그런 말을 하게 됐나요?\""]], [["“Miss Vye, why should you make believe that you don’t know this man?—I know why, certainly.", "\"바이 양, 어찌하여 이 사내를 모르는 척하십니까?―물론 저는 그 까닭을 압니다."], ["He is beneath you, and you are ashamed.”", "그가 아가씨께 미치지 못하니, 아가씨께서 부끄러우신 게지요.\""]], [["“You are mistaken.", "\"잘못 짚었어요."], ["What do you mean?”", "무슨 뜻이에요?\""]], [["The reddleman had decided to play the card of truth.", "레들맨은 진실의 패를 던지기로 작정했다."], ["“I was at the meeting by Rainbarrow last night and heard every word,”", "\"저는 어젯밤 레인배로 옆 그 만남 자리에 있었고, 한마디도 빠짐없이 들었습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“The woman that stands between Wildeve and Thomasin is yourself.”", "\"와일드브와 토머신 사이를 가로막은 그 여자는 바로 아가씨입니다.\""]], [["It was a disconcerting lift of the curtain, and the mortification of Candaules’ wife glowed in her.", "그것은 사람을 당황케 하는 휘장의 들춤이었고, 칸다울레스 왕비의 굴욕이 그녀의 내면에서 달아올랐다."], ["The moment had arrived when her lip would tremble in spite of herself, and when the gasp could no longer be kept down.", "본인의 의지와 무관하게 입술이 떨려오고, 더는 헐떡임을 억누를 수 없게 되는 순간이 닥쳐온 것이다."]], [["“I am unwell,”", "\"몸이 안 좋아요.\""], ["she said hurriedly.", "그녀가 허둥지둥 말했다."], ["“No—it is not that—I am not in a humour to hear you further.", "\"아니―그게 아니라―당신 말을 더 들을 기분이 아니에요."], ["Leave me, please.”", "제발 나를 두고 가요.\""]], [["“I must speak, Miss Vye, in spite of paining you.", "\"아가씨를 괴롭히더라도 말씀드려야겠습니다, 바이 양."], ["What I would put before you is this.", "제가 아가씨 앞에 펼쳐놓으려는 것은 이것입니다."], ["However it may come about—whether she is to blame, or you—her case is without doubt worse than yours.", "어찌하여 그리되었든―잘못이 그 아가씨에게 있든 아가씨께 있든―그 아가씨의 처지가 아가씨 처지보다 더 나쁜 것은 의심할 여지가 없습니다."], ["Your giving up Mr. Wildeve will be a real advantage to you, for how could you marry him?", "와일드브 씨를 포기하시는 것은 아가씨께 진짜로 이득일 것이니, 어차피 아가씨께서 어찌 그와 결혼하실 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Now she cannot get off so easily—everybody will blame her if she loses him.", "한데 그 아가씨는 그리 쉽게 벗어날 수가 없습니다―그를 놓치면 모두가 그 아가씨를 탓할 테니까요."], ["Then I ask you—not because her right is best, but because her situation is worst—to give him up to her.”", "그러니 저는 청합니다―그 아가씨의 권리가 더 옳아서가 아니라 그 아가씨의 처지가 가장 나쁘기에―그를 그 아가씨에게 넘겨주십사고요.\""]], [["“No—I won’t, I won’t!”", "\"안 돼―안 할 거예요, 안 할 거예요!\""], ["she said impetuously, quite forgetful of her previous manner towards the reddleman as an underling.", "그녀는 충동적으로, 레들맨을 아랫것으로 대하던 앞서의 태도는 까맣게 잊은 채 외쳤다."], ["“Nobody has ever been served so!", "\"이런 취급을 당한 사람은 여태 아무도 없었어!"], ["It was going on well—I will not be beaten down—by an inferior woman like her.", "잘 풀려가고 있었는데―나는 짓밟히지 않을 거예요―그 여자 같은 못한 여자한테는요."], ["It is very well for you to come and plead for her, but is she not herself the cause of all her own trouble?", "당신이 와서 그 여자를 두둔하는 건 좋아요, 하지만 그 여자의 고생은 죄다 그 여자 스스로 빚은 게 아닌가요?"], ["Am I not to show favour to any person I may choose without asking permission of a parcel of cottagers?", "나는 한 무리의 소작농들에게 허락을 구하지 않고서는 내가 고른 누구에게도 호의를 베풀 수 없단 말인가요?"], ["She has come between me and my inclination, and now that she finds herself rightly punished she gets you to plead for her!”", "그 여자가 나와 내 마음 사이에 끼어든 거고, 이제 제가 마땅한 벌을 받게 되니까 당신을 시켜 자기를 두둔하게 하는 거잖아요!\""]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["said Venn earnestly, “she knows nothing whatever about it.", "벤이 진지하게 말했다. \"그 아가씨는 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모릅니다."], ["It is only I who ask you to give him up.", "그를 포기하시라 청하는 건 오직 저뿐입니다."], ["It will be better for her and you both.", "그게 그 아가씨에게도 아가씨에게도 더 나을 겁니다."], ["People will say bad things if they find out that a lady secretly meets a man who has ill-used another woman.”", "다른 여자를 험하게 다룬 사내를 어느 숙녀가 몰래 만난다는 게 사람들에게 알려지면, 입방아에 오를 겁니다.\""]], [["“I have _not_ injured her—he was mine before he was hers!", "\"나는 그 여자를 해치지 않았어요―그는 그 여자 것이기 전에 내 것이었다고요!"], ["He came back—because—because he liked me best!”", "그가 돌아온 건―나를―나를 가장 좋아했기 때문이에요!\""], ["she said wildly.", "그녀가 거칠게 말했다."], ["“But I lose all self-respect in talking to you.", "\"하지만 당신과 이런 이야기를 하다 보니 자존심을 다 잃고 마는군요."], ["What am I giving way to!”", "내가 무엇에 휘말리고 있는 거람!\""]], [["“I can keep secrets,”", "\"저는 비밀을 지킬 수 있습니다.\""], ["said Venn gently.", "벤이 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“You need not fear.", "\"두려워하실 것 없습니다."], ["I am the only man who knows of your meetings with him.", "아가씨가 그와 만나는 걸 아는 사내는 저 하나뿐입니다."], ["There is but one thing more to speak of, and then I will be gone.", "한 가지만 더 말씀드리고 물러가겠습니다."], ["I heard you say to him that you hated living here—that Egdon Heath was a jail to you.”", "아가씨께서 그에게, 여기 사는 게 싫다고―에그던 히스가 아가씨께는 감옥이라고 하시는 걸 들었습니다.\""]], [["“I did say so.", "\"그렇게 말했죠."], ["There is a sort of beauty in the scenery, I know; but it is a jail to me.", "이 풍경에 일종의 아름다움이 있다는 건 알아요. 하지만 내게는 감옥이에요."], ["The man you mention does not save me from that feeling, though he lives here.", "당신이 말한 그 사내가 여기 산다고 해서 내가 그 느낌에서 벗어나는 건 아니에요."], ["I should have cared nothing for him had there been a better person near.”", "가까이에 더 나은 사람이 있었더라면 나는 그에게 아무런 마음도 두지 않았을 거예요.\""]], [["The reddleman looked hopeful; after these words from her his third attempt seemed promising.", "레들맨은 희망에 차 보였으니, 그녀의 이 말 뒤로는 세 번째 시도가 가망 있어 보였다."], ["“As we have now opened our minds a bit, miss,”", "\"이제 우리가 속을 좀 터놓았으니, 아가씨,\""], ["he said, “I’ll tell you what I have got to propose.", "그가 말했다. \"제가 제안하려는 바를 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Since I have taken to the reddle trade I travel a good deal, as you know.”", "제가 레들 장사에 나선 뒤로, 아시다시피 저는 두루 많이 돌아다닙니다.\""]], [["She inclined her head, and swept round so that her eyes rested in the misty vale beneath them.", "그녀는 고개를 살짝 숙이고는 몸을 휙 돌려, 그 시선이 발아래 안개 낀 골짜기에 머물게 했다."]], [["“And in my travels I go near Budmouth.", "\"제 돌아다니는 길에 버드머스 가까이를 지납니다."], ["Now Budmouth is a wonderful place—wonderful—a great salt sheening sea bending into the land like a bow—thousands of gentlepeople walking up and down—bands of music playing—officers by sea and officers by land walking among the rest—out of every ten folks you meet nine of ’em in love.”", "한데 버드머스는 멋진 곳입니다―멋지지요―크나큰 소금기 어린 번쩍이는 바다가 활처럼 뭍으로 휘어들고―수천의 점잖은 분들이 오르락내리락 거닐고―악단이 음악을 연주하고―바다의 장교와 뭍의 장교가 그 틈에서 함께 거닐고―만나는 사람 열에 아홉은 사랑에 빠져 있지요.\""]], [["“I know it,”", "\"알아요.\""], ["she said disdainfully.", "그녀가 깔보듯 말했다."], ["“I know Budmouth better than you.", "\"버드머스라면 내가 당신보다 더 잘 알아요."], ["I was born there.", "나는 거기서 태어났어요."], ["My father came to be a military musician there from abroad.", "내 아버지는 외국에서 그곳으로 와 군악대 악사가 됐죠."], ["Ah, my soul, Budmouth!", "아아, 내 영혼이여, 버드머스!"], ["I wish I was there now.”", "지금 거기 있다면 좋으련만.\""]], [["The reddleman was surprised to see how a slow fire could blaze on occasion.", "레들맨은 굼뜬 불길이 때에 따라 어떻게 활활 타오를 수 있는지를 보고 놀랐다."], ["“If you were, miss,”", "\"거기 계신다면, 아가씨,\""], ["he replied, “in a week’s time you would think no more of Wildeve than of one of those he’th-croppers that we see yond.", "그가 대꾸했다. \"일주일이면 아가씨는 저기 보이는 저 히스 망아지들만큼도 와일드브를 생각하지 않으실 겁니다."], ["Now, I could get you there.”", "자, 제가 아가씨를 거기로 보내드릴 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“How?”", "\"어떻게요?\""], ["said Eustacia, with intense curiosity in her heavy eyes.", "유스테시아가 그 무거운 눈에 강렬한 호기심을 띠고 말했다."]], [["“My uncle has been for five and twenty years the trusty man of a rich widow-lady who has a beautiful house facing the sea.", "\"제 숙부가 스물다섯 해 동안 어느 부유한 미망인 마님의 믿음직한 일꾼으로 지내왔는데, 그 마님이 바다를 마주한 아름다운 저택을 가지고 계십니다."], ["This lady has become old and lame, and she wants a young company-keeper to read and sing to her, but can’t get one to her mind to save her life, though she’ve advertised in the papers, and tried half a dozen.", "이 마님은 늙고 다리를 절게 되어, 책을 읽어주고 노래를 불러줄 젊은 말동무를 구하시는데, 신문에 광고까지 내고 대여섯이나 써봤어도 도통 마음에 드는 사람을 못 구해 애를 태우십니다."], ["She would jump to get you, and Uncle would make it all easy.”", "그분은 아가씨를 모실 수만 있다면 펄쩍 뛰며 좋아하실 테고, 숙부가 모든 걸 수월하게 해줄 겁니다.\""]], [["“I should have to work, perhaps?”", "\"내가 일을 해야 하나요, 아마도?\""]], [["“No, not real work—you’d have a little to do, such as reading and that.", "\"아니요, 진짜 일은 아닙니다―읽어드리는 일이라든가 그런 자잘한 게 좀 있을 뿐입니다."], ["You would not be wanted till New Year’s Day.”", "새해 첫날까지는 부르지도 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“I knew it meant work,”", "\"일이라는 뜻인 줄 알았어요.\""], ["she said, drooping to languor again.", "그녀가 다시 나른함 속으로 시들며 말했다."]], [["“I confess there would be a trifle to do in the way of amusing her; but though idle people might call it work, working people would call it play.", "\"솔직히 그분을 즐겁게 해드리는 일이 약간은 있을 겁니다. 하지만 한가한 사람들이야 그걸 일이라 부르겠지만, 일하는 사람들은 그걸 놀이라 부를 겁니다."], ["Think of the company and the life you’d lead, miss; the gaiety you’d see, and the gentleman you’d marry.", "아가씨가 누리실 교제와 삶을 생각해 보십시오, 아가씨가 보실 화려함과, 아가씨가 맞으실 신사 말입니다."], ["My uncle is to inquire for a trustworthy young lady from the country, as she don’t like town girls.”", "제 숙부가 시골 출신의 믿음직한 젊은 숙녀를 수소문하기로 되어 있습니다, 그분이 도회지 아가씨들을 안 좋아하시니까요.\""]], [["“It is to wear myself out to please her!", "\"그건 그 사람을 즐겁게 해주느라 나를 닳아 없애는 거예요!"], ["and I won’t go.", "나는 안 갈 거예요."], ["O, if I could live in a gay town as a lady should, and go my own ways, and do my own doings, I’d give the wrinkled half of my life!", "아아, 내가 숙녀답게 화려한 도회지에서 살면서, 내 갈 길을 가고, 내 하고 싶은 대로 할 수만 있다면, 내 인생의 주름진 절반이라도 내놓겠어요!"], ["Yes, reddleman, that would I.”", "그래요, 레들맨, 그렇게 하겠어요.\""]], [["“Help me to get Thomasin happy, miss, and the chance shall be yours,”", "\"제가 토머신을 행복하게 해주도록 도와주십시오, 아가씨, 그러면 그 기회는 아가씨 것이 됩니다.\""], ["urged her companion.", "길동무가 다그쳤다."]], [["“Chance—’tis no chance,”", "\"기회라니―그건 기회가 아니에요.\""], ["she said proudly.", "그녀가 도도하게 말했다."], ["“What can a poor man like you offer me, indeed?—I am going indoors.", "\"당신 같은 가난뱅이가 내게 무얼 줄 수 있다는 거예요, 정말?―나는 들어가겠어요."], ["I have nothing more to say.", "더는 할 말이 없어요."], ["Don’t your horses want feeding, or your reddlebags want mending, or don’t you want to find buyers for your goods, that you stay idling here like this?”", "당신네 말들은 여물을 안 줘도 되나요, 레들 자루는 안 기워도 되나요, 아니면 물건 사 줄 사람을 안 찾아도 되기에 이렇게 여기서 빈둥거리고 있는 건가요?\""]], [["Venn spoke not another word.", "벤은 더는 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["With his hands behind him he turned away, that she might not see the hopeless disappointment in his face.", "그는 그녀가 자기 얼굴의 가망 없는 실망을 보지 못하도록, 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌리고 발길을 돌렸다."], ["The mental clearness and power he had found in this lonely girl had indeed filled his manner with misgiving even from the first few minutes of close quarters with her.", "이 외로운 처녀에게서 발견한 정신의 명료함과 힘이, 그녀와 바싹 마주한 처음 몇 분부터 이미 그의 태도를 불안으로 가득 채웠던 것이다."], ["Her youth and situation had led him to expect a simplicity quite at the beck of his method.", "그녀의 젊음과 처지를 보고 그는 제 수법에 고스란히 휘둘릴 만큼의 단순함을 기대했었다."], ["But a system of inducement which might have carried weaker country lasses along with it had merely repelled Eustacia.", "그러나 더 마음 약한 시골 처녀들이라면 함께 휩쓸려갔을 법한 그 유혹의 방략이, 유스테시아에게는 그저 거부감만 일으켰다."], ["As a rule, the word Budmouth meant fascination on Egdon.", "보통 에그던에서 버드머스라는 말은 매혹을 뜻했다."], ["That Royal port and watering place, if truly mirrored in the minds of the heathfolk, must have combined, in a charming and indescribable manner a Carthaginian bustle of building with Tarentine luxuriousness and Baian health and beauty.", "그 왕실의 항구이자 휴양지가 히스 사람들의 마음에 참되게 비쳤다면, 그것은 카르타고적인 건설의 분주함과 타렌툼적인 호사로움과 바이아이적인 건강과 아름다움을 매혹적이고도 형언키 어려운 방식으로 한데 어우른 것이었으리라."], ["Eustacia felt little less extravagantly about the place; but she would not sink her independence to get there.", "유스테시아도 그곳을 두고 그에 못지않게 분에 넘치게 마음을 품었으나, 거기 가려고 제 독립을 굽히려 하지는 않았다."]], [["When Diggory Venn had gone quite away, Eustacia walked to the bank and looked down the wild and picturesque vale towards the sun, which was also in the direction of Wildeve’s.", "디고리 벤이 완전히 떠나가자, 유스테시아는 둑으로 걸어가 해 쪽으로, 또한 와일드브의 집 방향이기도 한 그쪽으로, 거칠고 그림 같은 골짜기를 내려다보았다."], ["The mist had now so far collapsed that the tips of the trees and bushes around his house could just be discerned, as if boring upwards through a vast white cobweb which cloaked them from the day.", "안개가 이제 어지간히 가라앉아, 그의 집 둘레 나무와 덤불의 우듬지가 가까스로 분간될 정도였으니, 마치 그것들을 햇빛으로부터 가린 거대한 흰 거미줄을 뚫고 위로 솟아오르는 듯했다."], ["There was no doubt that her mind was inclined thitherward; indefinitely, fancifully—twining and untwining about him as the single object within her horizon on which dreams might crystallize.", "그녀의 마음이 그쪽으로 기울어 있음은 의심할 여지가 없었으니, 막연하게, 공상에 잠겨―제 시야 안에서 꿈이 응결될 수 있는 유일한 대상인 그를 휘감았다 풀었다 했다."], ["The man who had begun by being merely her amusement, and would never have been more than her hobby but for his skill in deserting her at the right moments, was now again her desire.", "한낱 그녀의 심심풀이로 시작했던 사내, 적절한 순간마다 그녀를 버려두는 솜씨가 아니었던들 결코 그녀의 노리개 이상은 되지 못했을 사내가, 이제 다시금 그녀의 갈망이 되었다."], ["Cessation in his love-making had revivified her love.", "그의 구애가 그친 것이 그녀의 사랑을 되살린 것이다."], ["Such feeling as Eustacia had idly given to Wildeve was dammed into a flood by Thomasin.", "유스테시아가 와일드브에게 무심히 내주었던 정도의 감정이 토머신에 의해 둑이 막혀 큰물이 되었다."], ["She had used to tease Wildeve, but that was before another had favoured him.", "예전에 그녀는 와일드브를 곧잘 놀려댔으나, 그것은 다른 여자가 그를 어여삐 여기기 전의 일이었다."], ["Often a drop of irony into an indifferent situation renders the whole piquant.", "무관심한 상황에 아이러니 한 방울이 떨어지면 흔히 그 전부가 짜릿해지는 법이다."]], [["“I will never give him up—never!”", "\"나는 결코 그를 포기하지 않아―결코!\""], ["she said impetuously.", "그녀가 충동적으로 말했다."]], [["The reddleman’s hint that rumour might show her to disadvantage had no permanent terror for Eustacia.", "소문이 그녀를 불리하게 비출지도 모른다는 레들맨의 암시는 유스테시아에게 지속적인 두려움이 되지 못했다."], ["She was as unconcerned at that contingency as a goddess at a lack of linen.", "그녀는 그런 가능성을 여신이 아마포가 모자란 일을 대하듯 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["This did not originate in inherent shamelessness, but in her living too far from the world to feel the impact of public opinion. Zenobia in the desert could hardly have cared what was said about her at Rome.", "이는 타고난 파렴치함에서 나온 게 아니라, 세상에서 너무 멀리 떨어져 살아 여론의 충격을 느끼지 못하는 데서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["As far as social ethics were concerned Eustacia approached the savage state, though in emotion she was all the while an epicure.", "사막의 제노비아라면 로마에서 자기를 두고 무슨 말을 하든 거의 개의치 않았으리라."], ["She had advanced to the secret recesses of sensuousness, yet had hardly crossed the threshold of conventionality.", "사회 윤리에 관한 한 유스테시아는 야만의 상태에 가까웠으나, 정서에 있어서는 그러는 내내 향락가였다."], ["XI. The Dishonesty of an Honest Woman", "그녀는 관능의 은밀한 깊은 곳까지 나아가 있었으나, 인습의 문턱은 거의 넘어선 적이 없었다."]], [["The reddleman had left Eustacia’s presence with desponding views on Thomasin’s future happiness; but he was awakened to the fact that one other channel remained untried by seeing, as he followed the way to his van, the form of Mrs. Yeobright slowly walking towards the Quiet Woman.", "레들맨은 토머신의 앞날의 행복에 대해 낙담스러운 전망을 품은 채 유스테시아 앞에서 물러났으나, 제 짐마차로 가는 길을 따라가다가 요브라이트 부인의 모습이 콰이엇 우먼을 향해 천천히 걸어가는 것을 보고는, 아직 시도해보지 않은 길이 하나 더 남았다는 사실에 눈을 떴다."], ["He went across to her; and could almost perceive in her anxious face that this journey of hers to Wildeve was undertaken with the same object as his own to Eustacia.", "그는 그녀에게로 건너갔고, 그녀의 근심 어린 얼굴에서 와일드브를 향한 이 행차가 자신이 유스테시아를 찾아간 것과 똑같은 목적으로 이루어졌음을 거의 알아챌 수 있었다."]], [["She did not conceal the fact.", "그녀는 그 사실을 숨기지 않았다. \"그렇다면,\""], ["“Then,”", "레들맨이 말했다."], ["said the reddleman, “you may as well leave it alone, Mrs. Yeobright.”", "\"그냥 내버려두시는 편이 낫겠습니다, 요브라이트 부인.\""]], [["“I half think so myself,”", "\"나도 반쯤은 그렇게 생각해요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“But nothing else remains to be done besides pressing the question upon him.”", "\"하지만 그에게 이 문제를 다그치는 것 말고는 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없네요.\""]], [["“I should like to say a word first,”", "\"먼저 한마디 드리고 싶습니다.\""], ["said Venn firmly. “Mr.", "벤이 단호하게 말했다."], ["Wildeve is not the only man who has asked Thomasin to marry him; and why should not another have a chance?", "\"토머신에게 결혼하자고 청한 사내가 와일드브 씨 하나만은 아닙니다. 그러니 다른 사람이라고 기회를 못 가질 까닭이 있겠습니까?"], ["Mrs. Yeobright, I should be glad to marry your niece and would have done it any time these last two years.", "요브라이트 부인, 저는 부인의 조카와 기꺼이 결혼하고 싶고, 지난 이 년 동안 언제든 그리했을 것입니다."], ["There, now it is out, and I have never told anybody before but herself.”", "자, 이제 다 털어놓았는데, 이건 그 아가씨 본인 말고는 아무에게도 한 적 없는 말입니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright was not demonstrative, but her eyes involuntarily glanced towards his singular though shapely figure.", "요브라이트 부인은 감정을 잘 드러내는 편이 아니었으나, 그 눈길이 저도 모르게 그의 별나면서도 잘 빠진 풍채로 갔다."]], [["“Looks are not everything,”", "\"겉모습이 다는 아니지요.\""], ["said the reddleman, noticing the glance.", "그 눈길을 알아챈 레들맨이 말했다."], ["“There’s many a calling that don’t bring in so much as mine, if it comes to money; and perhaps I am not so much worse off than Wildeve.", "\"돈으로 따지면 제 일만큼도 못 버는 직업이 수두룩합니다. 어쩌면 저는 와일드브보다 그리 못한 처지도 아닐지 모릅니다."], ["There is nobody so poor as these professional fellows who have failed; and if you shouldn’t like my redness—well, I am not red by birth, you know; I only took to this business for a freak; and I might turn my hand to something else in good time.”", "실패한 그런 직업인들만큼 가난한 사람도 없으니까요. 그리고 제 붉은 물이 마음에 안 드신다면―뭐, 저는 날 때부터 붉었던 게 아닙니다, 아시다시피 그저 변덕으로 이 장사에 들어선 것뿐이니, 때가 되면 다른 일에 손을 댈 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["“I am much obliged to you for your interest in my niece; but I fear there would be objections.", "\"내 조카를 마음에 두어주니 정말 고마워요. 하지만 걸림돌이 있을까 두렵네요."], ["More than that, she is devoted to this man.”", "게다가 그 아이는 이 사내에게 마음을 다 바쳤어요.\""]], [["“True; or I shouldn’t have done what I have this morning.”", "\"그렇지요, 아니라면 제가 오늘 아침에 한 일을 하지도 않았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Otherwise there would be no pain in the case, and you would not see me going to his house now.", "\"그렇지 않다면 이 일에 아무런 괴로움도 없을 테고, 지금 내가 그의 집으로 가는 것도 보지 못했겠지요."], ["What was Thomasin’s answer when you told her of your feelings?”", "당신이 마음을 털어놓았을 때 토머신은 뭐라고 답하던가요?\""]], [["“She wrote that you would object to me; and other things.”", "\"부인께서 저를 반대하시리라고, 그리고 다른 이런저런 사정도 적어 보냈습니다.\""]], [["“She was in a measure right.", "\"그 아이 말이 어느 정도는 옳았어요."], ["You must not take this unkindly—I merely state it as a truth.", "이걸 야박하게 받아들이지는 말아요―나는 그저 사실을 말하는 것뿐이니."], ["You have been good to her, and we do not forget it.", "당신은 그 아이에게 잘해주었고, 우리도 그걸 잊지 않아요."], ["But as she was unwilling on her own account to be your wife, that settles the point without my wishes being concerned.”", "하지만 그 아이가 제 마음으로 당신의 아내가 되기를 꺼린 이상, 내 바람이 끼어들 것도 없이 그것으로 결판난 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["But there is a difference between then and now, ma’am.", "하지만 그때와 지금은 다릅니다, 마님."], ["She is distressed now, and I have thought that if you were to talk to her about me, and think favourably of me yourself, there might be a chance of winning her round, and getting her quite independent of this Wildeve’s backward and forward play, and his not knowing whether he’ll have her or no.”", "그 아가씨는 지금 곤경에 빠졌고, 만약 부인께서 그 아가씨에게 저에 대해 말씀해주시고, 부인 스스로도 저를 좋게 여겨주신다면, 그 아가씨의 마음을 돌려, 이 와일드브가 가질지 말지도 모른 채 갔다 왔다 하는 그 수작에서 완전히 벗어나게 할 가망이 있지 않을까 생각했습니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright shook her head.", "요브라이트 부인은 고개를 가로저었다."], ["“Thomasin thinks, and I think with her, that she ought to be Wildeve’s wife, if she means to appear before the world without a slur upon her name.", "\"토머신은, 그리고 나도 그 아이와 같은 생각인데, 그 아이가 이름에 오점 없이 세상 앞에 나서려면 마땅히 와일드브의 아내가 되어야 한다고 봐요."], ["If they marry soon, everybody will believe that an accident did really prevent the wedding.", "그들이 곧 결혼하면, 정말로 사고가 있어서 혼례를 못 치른 거라고 다들 믿을 거예요."], ["If not, it may cast a shade upon her character—at any rate make her ridiculous.", "그렇지 않으면 그 아이 인품에 그늘을 드리울지도 모르고―어쨌든 웃음거리로 만들겠지요."], ["In short, if it is anyhow possible they must marry now.”", "한마디로, 어떻게든 가능하다면 그들은 지금 결혼해야 해요.\""]], [["“I thought that till half an hour ago.", "\"저도 반 시간 전까지는 그렇게 생각했습니다."], ["But, after all, why should her going off with him to Anglebury for a few hours do her any harm?", "하지만 따지고 보면, 그 아가씨가 몇 시간 그와 함께 앵글베리로 다녀온 게 무슨 해가 된다는 겁니까?"], ["Anybody who knows how pure she is will feel any such thought to be quite unjust.", "그 아가씨가 얼마나 순결한지 아는 사람이라면 누구든 그런 생각을 영 부당하게 여길 겁니다."], ["I have been trying this morning to help on this marriage with Wildeve—yes, I, ma’am—in the belief that I ought to do it, because she was so wrapped up in him.", "저는 오늘 아침 이 와일드브와의 혼사를 거들려고 애썼습니다―네, 저 말입니다, 마님―그 아가씨가 그에게 그토록 푹 빠져 있으니 마땅히 그래야 한다고 믿어서요."], ["But I much question if I was right, after all.", "하지만 따지고 보면 제가 옳았는지 무척 의심스럽습니다."], ["However, nothing came of it.", "어쨌든 아무 성과도 없었지요."], ["And now I offer myself.”", "그래서 이제 제 자신을 내놓는 것입니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright appeared disinclined to enter further into the question.", "요브라이트 부인은 이 문제를 더 파고들 마음이 내키지 않는 듯했다."], ["“I fear I must go on,”", "\"아무래도 가봐야겠어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I do not see that anything else can be done.”", "\"달리 어찌할 도리가 있을 것 같지 않으니.\""]], [["And she went on.", "그러고는 그녀는 계속 갔다."], ["But though this conversation did not divert Thomasin’s aunt from her purposed interview with Wildeve, it made a considerable difference in her mode of conducting that interview.", "그러나 이 대화가 토머신의 이모를 와일드브와의 작정한 면담에서 돌려세우지는 못했어도, 그 면담을 이끌어가는 방식에는 상당한 차이를 가져왔다."], ["She thanked God for the weapon which the reddleman had put into her hands.", "그녀는 레들맨이 제 손에 쥐여준 무기를 두고 하느님께 감사했다."]], [["Wildeve was at home when she reached the inn.", "그녀가 여관에 다다랐을 때 와일드브는 집에 있었다."], ["He showed her silently into the parlour, and closed the door.", "그는 말없이 그녀를 응접실로 안내하고 문을 닫았다."], ["Mrs. Yeobright began—", "요브라이트 부인이 입을 열었다―"]], [["“I have thought it my duty to call today.", "\"오늘 들르는 게 내 도리라고 생각했어요."], ["A new proposal has been made to me, which has rather astonished me.", "내게 새로운 제안이 하나 들어왔는데, 적잖이 놀랐어요."], ["It will affect Thomasin greatly; and I have decided that it should at least be mentioned to you.”", "그게 토머신에게 크게 영향을 줄 테고, 그래서 적어도 자네에게는 알려야겠다고 마음먹었어요.\""]], [["“Yes?", "\"그렇습니까?"], ["What is it?”", "그게 무엇입니까?\""], ["he said civilly.", "그가 정중하게 말했다."]], [["“It is, of course, in reference to her future.", "\"물론 그 아이의 앞날에 관한 거예요."], ["You may not be aware that another man has shown himself anxious to marry Thomasin.", "자네는 모를지도 모르지만, 다른 사내가 토머신과 결혼하고 싶어 안달하는 기색을 보였어요."], ["Now, though I have not encouraged him yet, I cannot conscientiously refuse him a chance any longer.", "자, 내가 아직 그를 부추기지는 않았지만, 양심상 더는 그에게 기회를 거절할 수가 없어요."], ["I don’t wish to be short with you; but I must be fair to him and to her.”", "자네에게 매정하게 굴고 싶지는 않지만, 그와 그 아이에게 공정해야 하니까요.\""]], [["“Who is the man?”", "\"그 사내가 누구입니까?\""], ["said Wildeve with surprise.", "와일드브가 놀라서 물었다."]], [["“One who has been in love with her longer than she has with you.", "\"그 아이가 자네에게 마음을 둔 것보다 더 오래 그 아이에게 마음을 둔 사람이에요."], ["He proposed to her two years ago.", "그는 이 년 전에 그 아이에게 청혼했어요."], ["At that time she refused him.”", "그때는 그 아이가 거절했지요.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["“He has seen her lately, and has asked me for permission to pay his addresses to her.", "\"그가 요즘 그 아이를 보았고, 내게 그 아이에게 구애할 허락을 청했어요."], ["She may not refuse him twice.”", "그 아이가 두 번씩 거절하지는 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“What is his name?”", "\"이름이 뭡니까?\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright declined to say.", "요브라이트 부인은 말하기를 거절했다."], ["“He is a man Thomasin likes,”", "\"토머신이 좋아하는 사내예요.\""], ["she added, “and one whose constancy she respects at least.", "그녀가 덧붙였다. \"적어도 그 한결같음을 그 아이가 존중하는 사람이지요."], ["It seems to me that what she refused then she would be glad to get now.", "내가 보기엔 그때 거절한 것을 지금은 기꺼이 받아들이려 할 거예요."], ["She is much annoyed at her awkward position.”", "그 아이는 제 어색한 처지를 무척 괴로워하고 있어요.\""]], [["“She never once told me of this old lover.”", "\"그 아가씨는 그런 옛 연인 이야기를 단 한 번도 내게 한 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“The gentlest women are not such fools as to show _every_ card.”", "\"아무리 온순한 여자라도 패를 다 까 보일 만큼 바보는 아니지요.\""]], [["“Well, if she wants him I suppose she must have him.”", "\"음, 그 아가씨가 그를 원한다면 그를 가져야겠지요.\""]], [["“It is easy enough to say that; but you don’t see the difficulty.", "\"그렇게 말하기야 쉽지요. 하지만 자네는 그 어려움을 보지 못해요."], ["He wants her much more than she wants him; and before I can encourage anything of the sort I must have a clear understanding from you that you will not interfere to injure an arrangement which I promote in the belief that it is for the best.", "그가 그 아이를 원하는 것이 그 아이가 그를 원하는 것보다 훨씬 더하니, 내가 그런 일을 부추기기 전에 자네에게서 분명한 다짐을 받아두어야겠어요, 내가 최선이라 믿고 추진하는 그 일을 자네가 끼어들어 그르치지 않겠다는 다짐을요."], ["Suppose, when they are engaged, and everything is smoothly arranged for their marriage, that you should step between them and renew your suit?", "그들이 약혼하고 만사가 매끄럽게 정해져 혼례를 앞두었을 때, 자네가 그들 사이에 끼어들어 다시 구애를 한다고 쳐봐요."], ["You might not win her back, but you might cause much unhappiness.”", "그 아이를 도로 차지하지는 못할지언정 숱한 불행을 일으킬 수는 있겠지요.\""]], [["“Of course I should do no such thing,”", "\"물론 그런 짓은 하지 않을 것입니다.\""], ["said Wildeve “But they are not engaged yet.", "와일드브가 말했다. \"하지만 그들은 아직 약혼한 게 아닙니다."], ["How do you know that Thomasin would accept him?”", "토머신이 그를 받아들일지 부인께서 어찌 아십니까?\""]], [["“That’s a question I have carefully put to myself; and upon the whole the probabilities are in favour of her accepting him in time.", "\"그건 나도 신중히 자문해본 문제예요. 대체로 따져보면 그 아이가 머잖아 그를 받아들일 공산이 높아요."], ["I flatter myself that I have some influence over her.", "내가 그 아이에게 어느 정도 영향력이 있다고 자부하고 있고요."], ["She is pliable, and I can be strong in my recommendations of him.”", "그 아이는 마음이 무르고, 나는 그를 강하게 천거할 수 있어요.\""]], [["“And in your disparagement of me at the same time.”", "\"그러면서 동시에 저를 깎아내리시겠지요.\""]], [["“Well, you may depend upon my not praising you,” she said drily.", "\"음, 내가 자네를 칭찬하지 않으리란 건 믿어도 좋아요.\""], ["“And if this seems like manœuvring, you must remember that her position is peculiar, and that she has been hardly used.", "그녀가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"그리고 이게 술책처럼 보인다면, 그 아이의 처지가 유별나고 그 아이가 험한 일을 당했다는 걸 기억해야 해요."], ["I shall also be helped in making the match by her own desire to escape from the humiliation of her present state; and a woman’s pride in these cases will lead her a very great way.", "게다가 그 아이 스스로 지금의 굴욕에서 벗어나고 싶어 하는 마음이 이 혼사를 돕는 데 보탬이 될 테고, 이런 경우 여자의 자존심은 그 아이를 아주 멀리까지 이끌어가지요."], ["A little managing may be required to bring her round; but I am equal to that, provided that you agree to the one thing indispensable; that is, to make a distinct declaration that she is to think no more of you as a possible husband.", "마음을 돌리게 하려면 약간의 손씀이 필요할지도 모르나, 나는 그 정도는 해낼 수 있어요, 자네가 그 한 가지 없어서는 안 될 일에 동의한다면 말이에요. 그건, 그 아이가 자네를 두고 더는 남편감으로 여기지 말아야 한다고 분명히 선언하는 거예요."], ["That will pique her into accepting him.”", "그래야 그 아이가 발끈해서 그를 받아들일 테니까요.\""]], [["“I can hardly say that just now, Mrs. Yeobright.", "\"지금 당장 그렇게 말씀드리기는 어렵습니다, 요브라이트 부인."], ["It is so sudden.”", "너무 갑작스러워서요.\""]], [["“And so my whole plan is interfered with!", "\"그래서 내 계획이 통째로 어그러지는군요!"], ["It is very inconvenient that you refuse to help my family even to the small extent of saying distinctly you will have nothing to do with us.”", "자네가 우리와는 아무런 상관이 없겠다고 분명히 말해주는 그 작은 일조차 거절해서 우리 가족을 돕지 않다니 참으로 곤란하네요.\""]], [["Wildeve reflected uncomfortably.", "와일드브는 거북하게 곰곰 생각했다."], ["“I confess I was not prepared for this,”", "\"솔직히 저는 이런 일에 대비가 안 되어 있었습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Of course I’ll give her up if you wish, if it is necessary.", "\"물론 부인께서 원하신다면, 필요하다면 그 아가씨를 포기하겠습니다."], ["But I thought I might be her husband.”", "하지만 저는 제가 그 아가씨의 남편이 될 수도 있다고 생각했습니다.\""]], [["“We have heard that before.”", "\"그 말은 전에도 들었어요.\""]], [["“Now, Mrs. Yeobright, don’t let us disagree.", "\"자, 요브라이트 부인, 우리 다투지는 맙시다."], ["Give me a fair time.", "제게 적당한 시간을 주십시오."], ["I don’t want to stand in the way of any better chance she may have; only I wish you had let me know earlier.", "저는 그 아가씨에게 더 나은 어떤 기회가 있다면 그것을 가로막고 싶지 않습니다. 다만 좀 더 일찍 알려주셨더라면 좋았겠다 싶을 뿐입니다."], ["I will write to you or call in a day or two.", "하루 이틀 안에 편지를 드리거나 찾아뵙겠습니다."], ["Will that suffice?”", "그러면 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요.\""], ["she replied, “provided you promise not to communicate with Thomasin without my knowledge.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"내가 모르게 토머신과 연락하지 않겠다고 약속한다면요.\""]], [["“I promise that,”", "\"약속하겠습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["And the interview then terminated, Mrs. Yeobright returning homeward as she had come.", "그리고 면담은 그것으로 끝났고, 요브라이트 부인은 왔던 대로 집을 향해 돌아갔다."]], [["By far the greatest effect of her simple strategy on that day was, as often happens, in a quarter quite outside her view when arranging it.", "흔히 그렇듯, 그날 그녀의 단순한 책략이 가져온 가장 큰 효과는, 그것을 꾸밀 때 그녀의 시야에서 한참 벗어나 있던 데서 나타났다."], ["In the first place, her visit sent Wildeve the same evening after dark to Eustacia’s house at Mistover.", "우선, 그녀의 방문은 그날 저녁 어둠이 내린 뒤 와일드브를 미스토버의 유스테시아 집으로 보냈다."]], [["At this hour the lonely dwelling was closely blinded and shuttered from the chill and darkness without.", "이 시각, 그 외딴집은 바깥의 한기와 어둠으로부터 빈틈없이 가림막을 치고 덧창을 닫아걸고 있었다."], ["Wildeve’s clandestine plan with her was to take a little gravel in his hand and hold it to the crevice at the top of the window shutter, which was on the outside, so that it should fall with a gentle rustle, resembling that of a mouse, between shutter and glass.", "와일드브가 그녀와 짠 은밀한 방법은, 자갈을 조금 손에 쥐고 바깥에 달린 덧창 윗부분의 틈에 갖다 대어, 덧창과 유리 사이로 생쥐가 내는 듯한 가벼운 바스락 소리를 내며 떨어지게 하는 것이었다."], ["This precaution in attracting her attention was to avoid arousing the suspicions of her grandfather.", "이렇게 그녀의 주의를 끄는 데 조심을 기한 것은 그 할아버지의 의심을 사지 않기 위해서였다."]], [["The soft words, “I hear; wait for me,” in Eustacia’s voice from within told him that she was alone.", "안에서 유스테시아의 목소리로 들려온 \"들려요, 기다려요\"라는 부드러운 말이, 그녀가 혼자임을 그에게 일러주었다."]], [["He waited in his customary manner by walking round the enclosure and idling by the pool, for Wildeve was never asked into the house by his proud though condescending mistress.", "그는 늘 하던 식으로 울타리 둘레를 돌고 못가에서 어슬렁거리며 기다렸으니, 와일드브는 거만하면서도 짐짓 너그러운 그 연인에게 결코 집 안으로 청해진 적이 없었던 것이다."], ["She showed no sign of coming out in a hurry.", "그녀는 서둘러 나올 기색이 전혀 없었다."], ["The time wore on, and he began to grow impatient.", "시간이 흘렀고, 그는 초조해지기 시작했다."], ["In the course of twenty minutes she appeared from round the corner, and advanced as if merely taking an airing.", "이십 분쯤 지나자 그녀가 모퉁이를 돌아 나타나, 마치 그저 바람을 쐬러 나온 양 다가왔다."]], [["“You would not have kept me so long had you known what I come about,”", "\"내가 무슨 일로 왔는지 알았더라면 이렇게 오래 기다리게 하지는 않았을 텐데.\""], ["he said with bitterness.", "그가 씁쓸하게 말했다."], ["“Still, you are worth waiting for.”", "\"그래도 당신은 기다릴 만한 사람이오.\""]], [["“What has happened?”", "\"무슨 일이 있었나요?\""], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테시아가 말했다."], ["“I did not know you were in trouble.", "\"당신이 곤경에 처한 줄은 몰랐어요."], ["I too am gloomy enough.”", "나도 충분히 우울한걸요.\""]], [["“I am not in trouble,” said he.", "\"나는 곤경에 처한 게 아니오.\""], ["“It is merely that affairs have come to a head, and I must take a clear course.”", "그가 말했다. \"그저 일이 어떤 고비에 이르렀고, 내가 분명한 길을 택해야 한다는 것뿐이오.\""]], [["“What course is that?”", "\"그게 무슨 길인데요?\""], ["she asked with attentive interest.", "그녀가 주의 깊은 관심을 보이며 물었다."]], [["“And can you forget so soon what I proposed to you the other night?", "\"지난밤에 내가 당신에게 제안한 걸 그새 잊을 수 있단 말이오?"], ["Why, take you from this place, and carry you away with me abroad.”", "아니, 당신을 이곳에서 데리고 나가, 함께 외국으로 떠나자고 한 것 말이오.\""]], [["“I have not forgotten.", "\"잊지 않았어요."], ["But why have you come so unexpectedly to repeat the question, when you only promised to come next Saturday?", "한데 다음 토요일에야 오겠다고 약속해놓고, 어째서 이리도 뜻밖에 그 물음을 되풀이하러 온 거예요?"], ["I thought I was to have plenty of time to consider.”", "나는 곰곰 따져볼 시간이 넉넉히 있는 줄 알았는데요.\""]], [["“Yes, but the situation is different now.”", "\"그래, 하지만 이제 사정이 달라졌소.\""]], [["“Explain to me.”", "\"내게 설명해줘요.\""]], [["“I don’t want to explain, for I may pain you.”", "\"설명하고 싶지 않소, 당신을 괴롭히게 될지도 모르니.\""]], [["“But I must know the reason of this hurry.”", "\"하지만 이 서두름의 까닭을 알아야겠어요.\""]], [["“It is simply my ardour, dear Eustacia.", "\"그저 내 열정 때문이오, 사랑하는 유스테시아."], ["Everything is smooth now.”", "이제 모든 게 매끄럽소.\""]], [["“Then why are you so ruffled?”", "\"그럼 어째서 그리도 심기가 헝클어져 있나요?\""]], [["“I am not aware of it.", "\"나는 그런 줄 모르겠소."], ["All is as it should be.", "모든 게 마땅한 대로요."], ["Mrs. Yeobright—but she is nothing to us.”", "요브라이트 부인이―아니, 그 여자는 우리에게 아무것도 아니오.\""]], [["“Ah, I knew she had something to do with it!", "\"아, 그 여자가 무언가 관련이 있는 줄 알았어요!"], ["Come, I don’t like reserve.”", "자, 나는 숨기는 거 싫어요.\""]], [["“No—she has nothing.", "\"아니―그 여자는 아무것도 아니오."], ["She only says she wishes me to give up Thomasin because another man is anxious to marry her.", "그저 다른 사내가 토머신과 결혼하고 싶어 안달이니 내가 그 여자를 포기하기를 바란다고 할 뿐이오."], ["The woman, now she no longer needs me, actually shows off!”", "그 여자가, 이제 내가 더는 필요 없으니까, 정말 거드름을 피우는구려!\""], ["Wildeve’s vexation has escaped him in spite of himself.", "와일드브의 분함이 본인도 모르게 새어나왔다."]], [["Eustacia was silent a long while.", "유스테시아는 한참 동안 말이 없었다."], ["“You are in the awkward position of an official who is no longer wanted,”", "\"당신은 더는 쓸모없어진 관리 같은 어색한 처지에 놓였군요.\""], ["she said in a changed tone.", "그녀가 달라진 어조로 말했다."]], [["“It seems so.", "\"그런 것 같소."], ["But I have not yet seen Thomasin.”", "하지만 나는 아직 토머신을 보지 못했소.\""]], [["“And that irritates you.", "\"그게 당신을 약 오르게 하는 거예요."], ["Don’t deny it, Damon.", "부인하지 말아요, 데이먼."], ["You are actually nettled by this slight from an unexpected quarter.”", "당신은 정말이지 뜻밖의 데서 날아온 이 무시에 발끈해 있어요.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["“And you come to get me because you cannot get her.", "\"그러니까 당신은 그 여자를 못 가지니까 나를 가지러 온 거예요."], ["This is certainly a new position altogether.", "이건 확실히 완전히 새로운 처지네요."], ["I am to be a stop-gap.”", "나더러 땜빵이 되라는 거잖아요.\""]], [["“Please remember that I proposed the same thing the other day.”", "\"내가 며칠 전에도 똑같은 걸 제안했다는 걸 부디 잊지 마시오.\""]], [["Eustacia again remained in a sort of stupefied silence.", "유스테시아는 다시금 일종의 멍한 침묵에 잠겼다."], ["What curious feeling was this coming over her?", "그녀에게 밀려오는 이 묘한 감정은 무엇이었을까?"], ["Was it really possible that her interest in Wildeve had been so entirely the result of antagonism that the glory and the dream departed from the man with the first sound that he was no longer coveted by her rival?", "와일드브를 향한 그녀의 관심이 그토록 전적으로 적대심의 산물이어서, 자기 연적이 더는 그를 탐내지 않는다는 첫 소리에 그 사내에게서 영광과 꿈이 떠나버렸다는 게 정말로 있을 법한 일일까?"], ["She was, then, secure of him at last.", "그렇다면 이제 그녀는 마침내 그를 확실히 차지한 셈이었다."], ["Thomasin no longer required him.", "토머신은 더는 그를 필요로 하지 않았다."], ["What a humiliating victory!", "이 얼마나 굴욕적인 승리인가!"], ["He loved her best, she thought; and yet—dared she to murmur such treacherous criticism ever so softly?—what was the man worth whom a woman inferior to herself did not value?", "그가 자기를 가장 사랑한다고 그녀는 생각했다. 그런데도―이토록 배신스러운 비판을 아무리 나직이라도 감히 중얼거려도 될까?―제 자신보다 못한 여자조차 값쳐주지 않는 사내가 대체 무슨 값어치란 말인가?"], ["The sentiment which lurks more or less in all animate nature—that of not desiring the undesired of others—was lively as a passion in the supersubtle, epicurean heart of Eustacia.", "살아 있는 온갖 자연에 다소간 도사린 그 정서―남들이 탐내지 않는 것은 탐하지 않는다는 그 정서가, 지나치게 섬세하고 향락적인 유스테시아의 마음속에서 정념처럼 생생했다."], ["Her social superiority over him, which hitherto had scarcely ever impressed her, became unpleasantly insistent, and for the first time she felt that she had stooped in loving him.", "그를 향한 그녀의 사회적 우월함은, 여태껏 거의 그녀에게 와닿은 적이 없었으나, 이제 불쾌하리만치 끈질기게 들이밀어졌고, 그녀는 난생처음 자기가 그를 사랑하느라 몸을 굽혔다는 걸 느꼈다."]], [["“Well, darling, you agree?”", "\"자, 그대여, 동의하는 거요?\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일드브가 말했다."]], [["“If it could be London, or even Budmouth, instead of America,”", "\"미국 말고 런던, 아니 버드머스라도 된다면,\""], ["she murmured languidly.", "그녀가 나른하게 중얼거렸다."], ["“Well, I will think.", "\"음, 생각해볼게요."], ["It is too great a thing for me to decide offhand.", "이건 내가 즉석에서 정하기엔 너무 큰 일이에요."], ["I wish I hated the heath less—or loved you more.”", "내가 이 히스를 덜 미워하든가―아니면 당신을 더 사랑하든가 했으면 좋으련만.\""]], [["“You can be painfully frank.", "\"당신은 사람을 아프도록 솔직할 줄 아는군."], ["You loved me a month ago warmly enough to go anywhere with me.”", "한 달 전만 해도 당신은 나와 함께라면 어디든 갈 만큼 나를 뜨겁게 사랑했잖소.\""]], [["“And you loved Thomasin.”", "\"그리고 당신은 토머신을 사랑했죠.\""]], [["“Yes, perhaps that was where the reason lay,”", "\"그래, 어쩌면 거기에 까닭이 있었는지도 모르지.\""], ["he returned, with almost a sneer.", "그가 거의 비웃음 섞인 투로 받아쳤다."], ["“I don’t hate her now.”", "\"나는 이제 그 여자를 미워하지 않소.\""]], [["“Exactly.", "\"바로 그거예요."], ["The only thing is that you can no longer get her.”", "다만 당신이 더는 그 여자를 가질 수 없다는 게 문제죠.\""]], [["“Come—no taunts, Eustacia, or we shall quarrel.", "\"이봐―빈정대지 마시오, 유스테시아, 안 그러면 우리는 다투게 될 거요."], ["If you don’t agree to go with me, and agree shortly, I shall go by myself.”", "당신이 나와 함께 가기로, 그것도 곧 가기로 동의하지 않는다면 나는 혼자 떠나겠소.\""]], [["“Or try Thomasin again.", "\"아니면 토머신에게 다시 매달려 보든가."], ["Damon, how strange it seems that you could have married her or me indifferently, and only have come to me because I am—cheapest!", "데이먼, 당신이 그 여자든 나든 아무래도 상관없이 결혼할 수 있었다는 게, 그러고는 내가 제일―값싸니까 나에게 왔다는 게 어쩜 이리도 묘한지!"], ["Yes, yes—it is true.", "그래, 그래―사실이에요."], ["There was a time when I should have exclaimed against a man of that sort, and been quite wild; but it is all past now.”", "한때 같으면 나는 그런 부류의 사내를 두고 호되게 따져들며 펄펄 뛰었을 테지만, 이제 다 지나간 일이에요.\""]], [["“Will you go, dearest?", "\"가겠소, 사랑하는 사람?"], ["Come secretly with me to Bristol, marry me, and turn our backs upon this dog-hole of England for ever?", "나와 몰래 브리스틀로 가서 결혼하고, 이 영국의 개굴 같은 곳에 영영 등을 돌립시다."], ["Say Yes.”", "좋다고 말해줘요.\""]], [["“I want to get away from here at almost any cost,”", "\"나는 거의 무슨 대가를 치르더라도 여기서 벗어나고 싶어요.\""], ["she said with weariness, “but I don’t like to go with you.", "그녀가 지친 듯이 말했다. \"하지만 당신과 함께 가기는 싫어요."], ["Give me more time to decide.”", "정할 시간을 더 줘요.\""]], [["“I have already,”", "\"이미 줬잖소.\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일드브가 말했다."], ["“Well, I give you one more week.”", "\"음, 일주일 더 주겠소.\""]], [["“A little longer, so that I may tell you decisively.", "\"조금만 더요, 내가 분명하게 답해줄 수 있도록요."], ["I have to consider so many things.", "따져봐야 할 일이 너무 많아요."], ["Fancy Thomasin being anxious to get rid of you!", "토머신이 당신을 떼어내고 싶어 안달이라니, 상상이나 돼요?"], ["I cannot forget it.”", "나는 그걸 잊을 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“Never mind that.", "\"그건 신경 쓰지 마시오."], ["Say Monday week.", "다음 주 월요일이라고 합시다."], ["I will be here precisely at this time.”", "바로 이 시각에 여기 와 있겠소.\""]], [["“Let it be at Rainbarrow,”", "\"레인배로에서 만나요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“This is too near home; my grandfather may be walking out.”", "\"여기는 집과 너무 가까워요. 할아버지가 산책 나와 계실지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“Thank you, dear.", "\"고맙소, 그대여."], ["On Monday week at this time I will be at the Barrow.", "다음 주 월요일 이 시각에 무덤에 가 있겠소."], ["Till then good-bye.”", "그때까지 안녕히.\""]], [["“Good-bye.", "\"안녕히."], ["No, no, you must not touch me now.", "아니, 안 돼요, 지금은 나를 만지면 안 돼요."], ["Shaking hands is enough till I have made up my mind.”", "내가 마음을 정할 때까지는 악수로 족해요.\""]], [["Eustacia watched his shadowy form till it had disappeared.", "유스테시아는 그의 어슴푸레한 형상이 사라질 때까지 지켜보았다."], ["She placed her hand to her forehead and breathed heavily; and then her rich, romantic lips parted under that homely impulse—a yawn.", "그녀는 손을 이마에 대고 무겁게 숨을 내쉬었고, 그러더니 그 풍성하고 낭만적인 입술이 그 수수한 충동에 못 이겨 벌어졌다―하품이었다."], ["She was immediately angry at having betrayed even to herself the possible evanescence of her passion for him.", "그녀는 자신을 향한 정념이 어쩌면 스러질지도 모른다는 것을 제 자신에게라도 들켜버린 데에 이내 화가 났다."], ["She could not admit at once that she might have overestimated Wildeve, for to perceive his mediocrity now was to admit her own great folly heretofore.", "그녀는 자신이 와일드브를 과대평가했을지도 모른다는 것을 단번에 인정할 수가 없었으니, 지금 그의 범상함을 알아본다는 것은 곧 여태껏 제가 저지른 크나큰 어리석음을 인정하는 것이었기 때문이다."], ["And the discovery that she was the owner of a disposition so purely that of the dog in the manger had something in it which at first made her ashamed.", "그리고 자신이 그토록 순전히 여물통 속의 개 같은 심보의 소유자라는 발견에는, 처음엔 그녀를 부끄럽게 만드는 무언가가 있었다."]], [["The fruit of Mrs. Yeobright’s diplomacy was indeed remarkable, though not as yet of the kind she had anticipated.", "요브라이트 부인의 외교술이 맺은 열매는 과연 놀라웠으나, 아직은 그녀가 예상한 종류의 것은 아니었다."], ["It had appreciably influenced Wildeve, but it was influencing Eustacia far more.", "그것은 와일드브에게 어느 정도 영향을 미쳤으나, 유스테시아에게는 훨씬 더 크게 영향을 미치고 있었다."], ["Her lover was no longer to her an exciting man whom many women strove for, and herself could only retain by striving with them.", "그녀의 연인은 이제 그녀에게 더는 숱한 여자들이 차지하려 다투고, 그녀 자신은 그들과 다투어야만 붙들 수 있는 그런 가슴 설레는 사내가 아니었다."], ["He was a superfluity.", "그는 군더더기였다."]], [["She went indoors in that peculiar state of misery which is not exactly grief, and which especially attends the dawnings of reason in the latter days of an ill-judged, transient love.", "그녀는 정확히 슬픔이라고는 할 수 없는, 그리고 특히 그릇되게 판단한 덧없는 사랑이 끝나갈 무렵 이성이 동터올 때 따르곤 하는 그 묘한 비참함의 상태에 잠겨 집 안으로 들어갔다."], ["To be conscious that the end of the dream is approaching, and yet has not absolutely come, is one of the most wearisome as well as the most curious stages along the course between the beginning of a passion and its end.", "꿈의 끝이 다가오고 있음을, 그러면서도 아직 완전히 당도하지는 않았음을 의식하는 것은, 정념의 시작에서 그 끝에 이르는 길에서 가장 진을 빼는 동시에 가장 기이한 단계의 하나다."]], [["Her grandfather had returned, and was busily engaged in pouring some gallons of newly arrived rum into the square bottles of his square cellaret.", "그녀의 할아버지가 돌아와, 새로 들어온 럼주 몇 갤런을 자신의 네모난 술궤 속 네모난 병들에 따라 넣느라 분주했다."], ["Whenever these home supplies were exhausted he would go to the Quiet Woman, and, standing with his back to the fire, grog in hand, tell remarkable stories of how he had lived seven years under the waterline of his ship, and other naval wonders, to the natives, who hoped too earnestly for a treat of ale from the teller to exhibit any doubts of his truth.", "이 집안 비축분이 떨어질 때면 그는 콰이엇 우먼으로 가, 난로를 등지고 서서 그로그 잔을 손에 든 채, 자기가 어떻게 배의 흘수선 아래에서 칠 년을 살았는지며 그 밖의 해군의 경이들을 토박이들에게 들려주곤 했으니, 그들은 이야기꾼에게서 에일 한턱을 너무도 간절히 바란 나머지 그의 진실성에 조금의 의심도 내비치지 못했다."]], [["He had been there this evening.", "그는 이날 저녁에도 거기 다녀온 터였다."], ["“I suppose you have heard the Egdon news, Eustacia?”", "\"에그던 소식 들었겠지, 유스테시아?\""], ["he said, without looking up from the bottles.", "그가 병들에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다."], ["“The men have been talking about it at the Woman as if it were of national importance.”", "\"사내들이 그 술집에서 무슨 나라의 큰일이라도 되는 양 그 이야기를 하고 있더구나.\""]], [["“I have heard none,”", "\"아무 소식도 못 들었어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Young Clym Yeobright, as they call him, is coming home next week to spend Christmas with his mother.", "\"다들 클림 요브라이트라고 부르는 그 젊은이가, 다음 주에 어머니와 크리스마스를 보내러 집에 온단다."], ["He is a fine fellow by this time, it seems.", "이젠 어지간한 사내가 됐다더구나."], ["I suppose you remember him?”", "너도 그를 기억하겠지?\""]], [["“I never saw him in my life.”", "\"평생 그를 본 적이 없는걸요.\""]], [["“Ah, true; he left before you came here.", "\"아, 그렇지. 네가 여기 오기 전에 그가 떠났으니."], ["I well remember him as a promising boy.”", "나는 그를 장래가 촉망되는 소년으로 잘 기억하고 있단다.\""]], [["“Where has he been living all these years?”", "\"그동안 줄곧 어디서 살았는데요?\""]], [["“In that rookery of pomp and vanity, Paris, I believe.”", "\"그 호화와 허영의 소굴, 파리에서 살았다지, 아마.\""]], [["I. Tidings of the Comer\nOn the fine days at this time of the year, and earlier, certain ephemeral operations were apt to disturb, in their trifling way, the majestic calm of Egdon Heath.", "이맘때, 그리고 그 전의 화창한 날이면 어떤 덧없는 작업들이 사소한 방식으로나마 에그던 황야의 장엄한 정적을 어지럽히곤 했다."], ["They were activities which, beside those of a town, a village, or even a farm, would have appeared as the ferment of stagnation merely, a creeping of the flesh of somnolence. But here, away from comparisons, shut in by the stable hills, among which mere walking had the novelty of pageantry, and where any man could imagine himself to be Adam without the least difficulty, they attracted the attention of every bird within eyeshot, every reptile not yet asleep, and set the surrounding rabbits curiously watching from hillocks at a safe distance.", "도시나 마을, 심지어 농장의 활동과 견주면 그것은 그저 정체停滯의 발효, 졸음의 살갗이 스멀거리는 것쯤으로 보였을 테지만, 그러나 이곳에서는 비교 대상이 멀리 사라지고 변치 않는 언덕에 갇힌 채 그 가운데서는 그저 걷는 것조차 행렬의 새로움을 띠고 누구든지 자신을 아담이라고 상상하기에 조금도 어려움이 없는바, 시야에 든 모든 새와 아직 잠들지 않은 모든 파충류의 주의를 끌어, 둘레의 토끼들이 안전한 거리의 작은 둔덕에서 호기심 어린 눈으로 지켜보게 만들었다."]], [["The performance was that of bringing together and building into a stack the furze faggots which Humphrey had been cutting for the captain’s use during the foregoing fine days.", "그 작업이란 지난 화창한 날들 동안 험프리가 선장이 쓰도록 베어둔 가시금작화 다발을 한데 모아 더미로 쌓는 일이었다."], ["The stack was at the end of the dwelling, and the men engaged in building it were Humphrey and Sam, the old man looking on.", "더미는 거처의 끝자락에 있었고, 그것을 쌓는 일에 매달린 이들은 험프리와 샘이었으며, 노인은 그것을 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["It was a fine and quiet afternoon, about three o’clock; but the winter solstice having stealthily come on, the lowness of the sun caused the hour to seem later than it actually was, there being little here to remind an inhabitant that he must unlearn his summer experience of the sky as a dial.", "화창하고 고요한 오후, 세 시쯤이었으나, 동지冬至가 슬그머니 다가온 탓에 해가 낮게 걸려 시각은 실제보다 늦게 느껴졌으니, 이곳에는 거주민으로 하여금 하늘을 해시계로 여기던 여름의 경험을 버려야 함을 일깨워줄 만한 것이 거의 없었기 때문이다."], ["In the course of many days and weeks sunrise had advanced its quarters from northeast to southeast, sunset had receded from northwest to southwest; but Egdon had hardly heeded the change.", "여러 날과 여러 주가 흐르는 동안 일출은 그 자리를 북동쪽에서 남동쪽으로 옮겨왔고, 일몰은 북서쪽에서 남서쪽으로 물러났으나, 에그던은 그 변화에 거의 마음을 두지 않았다."]], [["Eustacia was indoors in the dining-room, which was really more like a kitchen, having a stone floor and a gaping chimney-corner.", "유스테이샤는 실내에, 사실은 부엌에 더 가까운 식당에 있었으니, 돌바닥에 입을 떡 벌린 굴뚝 모퉁이가 있는 방이었다."], ["The air was still, and while she lingered a moment here alone sounds of voices in conversation came to her ears directly down the chimney.", "공기는 잔잔했고, 그녀가 잠시 이곳에 홀로 머무는 동안 대화하는 목소리들이 굴뚝을 타고 곧장 그녀의 귀에 와 닿았다."], ["She entered the recess, and, listening, looked up the old irregular shaft, with its cavernous hollows, where the smoke blundered about on its way to the square bit of sky at the top, from which the daylight struck down with a pallid glare upon the tatters of soot draping the flue as seaweed drapes a rocky fissure.", "그녀는 우묵한 곳으로 들어가 귀를 기울이며, 동굴처럼 우묵한 자리들이 패인 그 오래되고 일그러진 굴뚝 통로를 올려다보았으니, 거기서는 연기가 꼭대기의 네모난 하늘 조각을 향해 가는 길에 갈팡질팡 헤매고 있었고, 그 하늘로부터 낮빛이 창백한 광채로 내리쏟아져, 바위 틈을 해초가 덮듯 굴뚝 통로를 늘어진 검댕 누더기들 위로 떨어지고 있었다."]], [["She remembered: the furze-stack was not far from the chimney, and the voices were those of the workers.", "그녀는 떠올렸다. 가시금작화 더미는 굴뚝에서 멀지 않았고, 그 목소리들은 일꾼들의 것이었다."]], [["Her grandfather joined in the conversation.", "그녀의 할아버지가 대화에 끼어들었다."], ["“That lad ought never to have left home.", "\"저 녀석은 결코 집을 떠나선 안 됐어."], ["His father’s occupation would have suited him best, and the boy should have followed on.", "제 아비의 일이 그 애한테 제일 맞았을 테고, 아들은 그 뒤를 이었어야지."], ["I don’t believe in these new moves in families.", "난 집안에서 이런 새로운 변화를 믿지 않아."], ["My father was a sailor, so was I, and so should my son have been if I had had one.”", "내 아비는 뱃사람이었고, 나도 그랬으니, 내게 아들이 있었다면 그 애도 그랬어야 했어.\""]], [["“The place he’s been living at is Paris,”", "\"그 애가 살고 있는 곳은 파리예요.\" 험프리가 말했다."], ["said Humphrey, “and they tell me ’tis where the king’s head was cut off years ago.", "\"거기가 몇 해 전에 왕의 머리를 잘라낸 데라고들 하더군요. 가엾은 우리 어머니가 그 일을 곧잘 들려주셨지요."], ["My poor mother used to tell me about that business.", "'허미야,' 어머니는 말씀하시곤 했어요."], ["‘Hummy,’ she used to say, ‘I was a young maid then, and as I was at home ironing Mother’s caps one afternoon the parson came in and said, “They’ve cut the king’s head off, Jane; and what ’twill be next God knows.’”", "'그때 난 어린 처녀였는데, 어느 오후에 집에서 어머니 모자를 다림질하고 있는데 목사님이 들어오시더니, \"왕의 머리를 잘라냈다네, 제인. 다음엔 또 무슨 일이 벌어질지 하느님만 아시겠지.\"'\""]], [["“A good many of us knew as well as He before long,”", "\"우리 가운데 많은 사람도 머잖아 그분 못지않게 잘 알게 됐지.\""], ["said the captain, chuckling.", "선장이 껄껄 웃으며 말했다."], ["“I lived seven years under water on account of it in my boyhood—in that damned surgery of the Triumph, seeing men brought down to the cockpit with their legs and arms blown to Jericho....", "\"난 그 일 때문에 소년 시절에 일곱 해를 물 밑에서 살았어—저 빌어먹을 트라이엄프 호의 수술실에서, 사내들이 팔다리가 예리코까지 날아간 채 갑판 밑 치료실로 실려 내려오는 걸 보면서 말이야……"], ["And so the young man has settled in Paris.", "그래서 그 젊은 친구가 파리에 자리를 잡았다는 거지."], ["Manager to a diamond merchant, or some such thing, is he not?”", "다이아몬드 상인의 지배인이라든가 뭐 그런 거 아닌가?\""]], [["“Yes, sir, that’s it.", "\"네, 선장님, 바로 그겁니다."], ["’Tis a blazing great business that he belongs to, so I’ve heard his mother say—like a king’s palace, as far as diments go.”", "그 애가 몸담은 데가 어마어마하게 큰 사업이라고, 그 애 어머니가 말하는 걸 들었지요—다이아몬드로 치면 임금님 궁전 같은 데라고요.\""]], [["“I can well mind when he left home,”", "\"그 애가 집을 떠날 때를 똑똑히 기억해.\""], ["said Sam.", "샘이 말했다."]], [["“’Tis a good thing for the feller,”", "\"그 친구한텐 잘된 일이지.\""], ["said Humphrey.", "험프리가 말했다."], ["“A sight of times better to be selling diments than nobbling about here.”", "\"여기서 빈둥거리느니 다이아몬드를 파는 게 백번 낫고말고.\""]], [["“It must cost a good few shillings to deal at such a place.”", "\"그런 데서 거래하려면 실링께나 들겠군.\""]], [["“A good few indeed, my man,”", "\"실링께나 정도가 아니지, 이 사람아.\""], ["replied the captain.", "선장이 대답했다."], ["“Yes, you may make away with a deal of money and be neither drunkard nor glutton.”", "\"그래, 술꾼도 대식가도 아니면서 큰돈을 날려버릴 수 있는 법이야.\""]], [["“They say, too, that Clym Yeobright is become a real perusing man, with the strangest notions about things.", "\"게다가 클림 예오브라이트가 진짜로 책을 파고드는 사람이 됐다고들 해요, 세상사에 관해 아주 별난 생각들을 품고서 말입니다."], ["There, that’s because he went to school early, such as the school was.”", "거 봐요, 그게 다 일찍 학교에 다녔기 때문이지요, 그게 학교라고 할 만한 거였든 아니든.\""]], [["“Strange notions, has he?”", "\"별난 생각이라, 그 애가?\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."], ["“Ah, there’s too much of that sending to school in these days!", "\"아, 요즘은 학교에 보내는 일이 너무 지나쳐!"], ["It only does harm.", "해만 끼칠 뿐이야."], ["Every gatepost and barn’s door you come to is sure to have some bad word or other chalked upon it by the young rascals—a woman can hardly pass for shame sometimes.", "어디 가도 문기둥이며 헛간 문짝마다 어린 망나니들이 분필로 갈겨놓은 못된 말이 꼭 적혀 있어서—여자는 더러 부끄러워서 지나가지도 못한다니까."], ["If they’d never been taught how to write they wouldn’t have been able to scribble such villainy.", "글 쓰는 법을 배운 적이 없었다면 그런 악담을 끄적이지도 못했을 게야."], ["Their fathers couldn’t do it, and the country was all the better for it.”", "그 애들 아비는 그런 걸 못했고, 그래서 시골은 그만큼 더 나았던 게지.\""]], [["“Now, I should think, Cap’n, that Miss Eustacia had about as much in her head that comes from books as anybody about here?”", "\"그런데 제 생각엔, 선장님, 유스테이샤 아가씨도 여기 누구 못지않게 책에서 얻은 게 머릿속에 가득한 듯합니다만?\""]], [["“Perhaps if Miss Eustacia, too, had less romantic nonsense in her head it would be better for her,”", "\"아마 유스테이샤도 머릿속에 낭만적인 헛소리가 좀 덜 들었다면 그 애한테 더 나았을 거야.\""], ["said the captain shortly; after which he walked away.", "선장이 짤막하게 말하고는 자리를 떴다."]], [["“I say, Sam,”", "\"이봐, 샘.\""], ["observed Humphrey when the old man was gone, “she and Clym Yeobright would make a very pretty pigeon-pair—hey?", "노인이 사라지자 험프리가 말했다. \"그 애랑 클림 예오브라이트는 아주 그럴듯한 한 쌍이 될 거야—응?"], ["If they wouldn’t I’ll be dazed!", "안 그렇다면 내가 얼이 빠진 거지!"], ["Both of one mind about niceties for certain, and learned in print, and always thinking about high doctrine—there couldn’t be a better couple if they were made o’ purpose.", "둘 다 고상한 것들에 대해 분명 한마음일 테고, 책에 통달했고, 늘 높은 학설을 생각하니—일부러 만들어내도 그보다 나은 짝은 없을 거야."], ["Clym’s family is as good as hers.", "클림네 집안도 그 애 집안 못지않게 좋아."], ["His father was a farmer, that’s true; but his mother was a sort of lady, as we know.", "그 애 아비가 농부였던 건 사실이지만, 그 애 어미는 우리도 알다시피 귀부인 비슷한 사람이었으니까."], ["Nothing would please me better than to see them two man and wife.”", "그 둘이 부부가 되는 걸 보는 것보다 더 기쁜 일은 없겠어.\""]], [["“They’d look very natty, arm-in-crook together, and their best clothes on, whether or no, if he’s at all the well-favoured fellow he used to be.”", "\"그 둘이 팔짱을 끼고 제일 좋은 옷을 차려입으면 어쨌든 아주 말쑥해 보일 거야, 그 애가 예전처럼 잘생긴 친구이기만 하다면 말이지.\""]], [["“They would, Humphrey.", "\"그렇고말고, 험프리."], ["Well, I should like to see the chap terrible much after so many years.", "글쎄, 난 이렇게 여러 해가 지났으니 그 친구가 끔찍이 보고 싶어."], ["If I knew for certain when he was coming I’d stroll out three or four miles to meet him and help carry anything for’n; though I suppose he’s altered from the boy he was.", "그가 언제 오는지 확실히 알면 한 서너 마일 마중을 나가서 짐을 들어주고 싶다니까—하기야 그가 예전의 소년에서 변했을 테지."], ["They say he can talk French as fast as a maid can eat blackberries; and if so, depend upon it we who have stayed at home shall seem no more than scroff in his eyes.”", "그가 처녀가 검은딸기 먹어 치우듯 빠르게 프랑스어를 한다고들 하니, 그렇다면 집에 남아 있던 우리 같은 자들은 그의 눈에 찌꺼기로밖에 안 보일 게 뻔해.\""]], [["“Coming across the water to Budmouth by steamer, isn’t he?”", "\"증기선으로 물을 건너 버드머스로 온다면서, 안 그런가?\""]], [["“Yes; but how he’s coming from Budmouth I don’t know.”", "\"그래. 하지만 버드머스에서 어떻게 오는지는 모르겠어.\""]], [["“That’s a bad trouble about his cousin Thomasin.", "\"그 애 사촌 토머신 일은 곤란한 골칫거리지."], ["I wonder such a nice-notioned fellow as Clym likes to come home into it.", "클림처럼 그렇게 생각이 고상한 친구가 그런 일 속으로 집에 돌아오고 싶어 하다니 이상한 노릇이야."], ["What a nunnywatch we were in, to be sure, when we heard they weren’t married at all, after singing to ’em as man and wife that night!", "그날 밤 둘을 부부라고 노래까지 불러줬는데, 사실은 아예 결혼도 안 했다는 걸 듣고서, 우리가 정말이지 얼마나 당황했던가!"], ["Be dazed if I should like a relation of mine to have been made such a fool of by a man.", "내 친척이 사내한테 그렇게 바보 취급을 당했다면 난 얼이 다 빠졌을 거야."], ["It makes the family look small.”", "그러면 집안이 작아 보이거든.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Poor maid, her heart has ached enough about it.", "가엾은 처녀, 그 일로 가슴이 충분히 아팠지."], ["Her health is suffering from it, I hear, for she will bide entirely indoors.", "건강도 그 때문에 상하고 있다더군, 통 밖에 안 나오고 안에만 틀어박혀 있다니까."], ["We never see her out now, scampering over the furze with a face as red as a rose, as she used to do.”", "예전처럼 장미처럼 붉은 얼굴로 가시금작화 위를 뛰어다니는 모습을 이제는 도무지 못 보게 됐어.\""]], [["“I’ve heard she wouldn’t have Wildeve now if he asked her.”", "\"이젠 와일디브가 청한다 해도 그 처녀가 그를 받아들이지 않을 거라고 들었어.\""]], [["“You have?", "\"그랬어?"], ["’Tis news to me.”", "나한텐 처음 듣는 소린데.\""]], [["While the furze-gatherers had desultorily conversed thus Eustacia’s face gradually bent to the hearth in a profound reverie, her toe unconsciously tapping the dry turf which lay burning at her feet.", "가시금작화를 모으던 이들이 이렇게 두서없이 대화를 나누는 동안 유스테이샤의 얼굴은 깊은 몽상에 잠겨 차츰 난로 쪽으로 기울어졌고, 발끝은 무심결에 발치에서 타고 있는 마른 잔디를 톡톡 두드리고 있었다."]], [["The subject of their discourse had been keenly interesting to her.", "그들 이야기의 주제는 그녀의 흥미를 강하게 끌었다."], ["A young and clever man was coming into that lonely heath from, of all contrasting places in the world, Paris.", "젊고 영리한 사내가, 세상에서 가장 대조적인 곳을 다 제쳐두고 하필이면 파리에서, 저 외로운 황야로 들어오고 있다는 것이었다."], ["It was like a man coming from heaven.", "그것은 마치 한 사내가 천국에서 오는 것 같았다."], ["More singular still, the heathmen had instinctively coupled her and this man together in their minds as a pair born for each other.", "더욱 기이하게도, 황야 사람들은 본능적으로 그와 그녀를 서로를 위해 태어난 한 쌍으로 마음속에 묶어두고 있었던 것이다."]], [["That five minutes of overhearing furnished Eustacia with visions enough to fill the whole blank afternoon.", "그 오 분간의 엿들음은 유스테이샤에게 텅 빈 오후 전체를 채우고도 남을 만큼의 환영을 안겨주었다."], ["Such sudden alternations from mental vacuity do sometimes occur thus quietly.", "정신적 공허에서 이렇게 돌연한 전환이 이따금 이토록 조용히 일어나곤 한다."], ["She could never have believed in the morning that her colourless inner world would before night become as animated as water under a microscope, and that without the arrival of a single visitor.", "그녀는 아침나절만 해도 자신의 빛바랜 내면세계가 밤이 오기 전에 현미경 아래의 물처럼 활기를 띠리라고는, 그것도 단 한 명의 방문객도 오지 않은 채 그러리라고는 결코 믿지 못했을 것이다."], ["The words of Sam and Humphrey on the harmony between the unknown and herself had on her mind the effect of the invading Bard’s prelude in the Castle of Indolence, at which myriads of imprisoned shapes arose where had previously appeared the stillness of a void.", "그 미지의 인물과 그녀 사이의 조화에 관한 샘과 험프리의 말은 그녀의 마음에 「나태의 성」에서 침입하는 음유시인의 전주곡과도 같은 효과를 미쳤으니, 그 전주곡에 앞서 텅 빈 정적만 있던 자리에서 갇혀 있던 무수한 형상들이 솟아올랐던 것이다."]], [["Involved in these imaginings she knew nothing of time.", "이런 상상에 잠겨 그녀는 시간 가는 줄을 몰랐다."], ["When she became conscious of externals it was dusk.", "바깥세상이 다시 의식에 들어왔을 때는 어스름이었다."], ["The furze-rick was finished; the men had gone home.", "가시금작화 더미는 완성되었고, 사내들은 집으로 돌아간 뒤였다."], ["Eustacia went upstairs, thinking that she would take a walk at this her usual time; and she determined that her walk should be in the direction of Blooms-End, the birthplace of young Yeobright and the present home of his mother.", "유스테이샤는 늘 산책을 나가던 이 시간에 산책을 하리라 생각하며 위층으로 올라갔고, 그 산책의 방향을 젊은 예오브라이트의 출생지이자 지금은 그의 어머니가 살고 있는 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 정했다."], ["She had no reason for walking elsewhere, and why should she not go that way?", "그녀에게는 다른 데로 걸어갈 이유가 없었으니, 어찌 그쪽으로 가지 못하겠는가?"], ["The scene of the daydream is sufficient for a pilgrimage at nineteen.", "백일몽의 무대는 열아홉 살에게 순례지로 삼기에 충분했다."], ["To look at the palings before the Yeobrights’ house had the dignity of a necessary performance.", "예오브라이트네 집 앞 말뚝 울타리를 바라보는 일은 꼭 해야 할 일의 위엄을 띠었다."], ["Strange that such a piece of idling should have seemed an important errand.", "그런 한가한 짓거리가 중대한 볼일처럼 여겨졌다니 이상한 노릇이었다."]], [["She put on her bonnet, and, leaving the house, descended the hill on the side towards Blooms-End, where she walked slowly along the valley for a distance of a mile and a half.", "그녀는 보닛을 쓰고 집을 나서, 블룸스엔드 쪽 비탈을 내려가, 골짜기를 따라 일 마일 반쯤 천천히 걸었다."], ["This brought her to a spot in which the green bottom of the dale began to widen, the furze bushes to recede yet further from the path on each side, till they were diminished to an isolated one here and there by the increasing fertility of the soil.", "그러자 골짜기의 푸른 바닥이 넓어지기 시작하는 지점에 이르렀으니, 가시금작화 덤불이 길 양편에서 더욱 물러나 토양이 점점 비옥해지면서 여기저기 외따로 한 그루씩 남을 만큼 줄어들고 있었다."], ["Beyond the irregular carpet of grass was a row of white palings, which marked the verge of the heath in this latitude. They showed upon the dusky scene that they bordered as distinctly as white lace on velvet.", "고르지 못한 풀밭 카펫 너머로는 흰 말뚝 울타리 한 줄이 있어, 이 위도에서 황야의 가장자리를 표시하고 있었다."], ["Behind the white palings was a little garden; behind the garden an old, irregular, thatched house, facing the heath, and commanding a full view of the valley.", "그것들은 자기네가 경계 짓고 있는 어스름한 풍경 위로, 벨벳 위의 흰 레이스만큼이나 또렷이 드러나 있었다."], ["This was the obscure, removed spot to which was about to return a man whose latter life had been passed in the French capital—the centre and vortex of the fashionable world.", "흰 말뚝 울타리 뒤에는 작은 정원이 있었고, 그 정원 뒤에는 황야를 마주한, 골짜기 전체를 한눈에 굽어보는 오래되고 일그러진 초가집 한 채가 있었다."], ["II. The People at Blooms-End Make Ready", "이곳이 바로, 만년의 삶을 유행 세계의 중심이자 소용돌이인 프랑스 수도에서 보낸 한 사내가 막 돌아오려 하는, 그 후미지고 외딴 자리였다."]], [["All that afternoon the expected arrival of the subject of Eustacia’s ruminations created a bustle of preparation at Blooms-End.", "그날 오후 내내 유스테이샤가 골똘히 생각하던 그 인물이 도착하리라는 예상은 블룸스엔드에서 준비의 부산함을 자아냈다."], ["Thomasin had been persuaded by her aunt, and by an instinctive impulse of loyalty towards her cousin Clym, to bestir herself on his account with an alacrity unusual in her during these most sorrowful days of her life.", "토머신은 고모의 설득과, 사촌 클림에 대한 본능적인 충정의 충동에 이끌려, 생애 가장 슬픈 이 나날에는 좀처럼 보기 드문 민첩함으로 그를 위해 분주히 움직였다."], ["At the time that Eustacia was listening to the rick-makers’ conversation on Clym’s return, Thomasin was climbing into a loft over her aunt’s fuelhouse, where the store-apples were kept, to search out the best and largest of them for the coming holiday-time.", "유스테이샤가 클림의 귀향에 관한 더미 쌓는 이들의 대화에 귀를 기울이고 있을 무렵, 토머신은 고모네 땔감 창고 위 다락으로 기어 올라가고 있었으니, 저장 사과를 두는 곳이라, 다가오는 명절을 위해 그 가운데 가장 좋고 큰 것들을 골라내려는 참이었다."]], [["The loft was lighted by a semicircular hole, through which the pigeons crept to their lodgings in the same high quarters of the premises; and from this hole the sun shone in a bright yellow patch upon the figure of the maiden as she knelt and plunged her naked arms into the soft brown fern, which, from its abundance, was used on Egdon in packing away stores of all kinds.", "다락은 반원형 구멍으로 빛이 들었으니, 비둘기들이 그 구멍을 통해 같은 높은 자리에 있는 자기네 보금자리로 기어들곤 했다. 이 구멍에서 햇빛이 환한 노란 조각을 이루며, 처녀가 무릎을 꿇고 맨팔을 부드러운 갈색 고사리 속으로 파묻는 모습 위로 비쳐 들었으니, 그 고사리는 풍부하기에 에그던에서는 온갖 저장물을 싸두는 데 쓰였다."], ["The pigeons were flying about her head with the greatest unconcern, and the face of her aunt was just visible above the floor of the loft, lit by a few stray motes of light, as she stood halfway up the ladder, looking at a spot into which she was not climber enough to venture.", "비둘기들은 더할 나위 없이 무심하게 그녀의 머리 둘레를 날아다녔고, 고모의 얼굴은 다락 바닥 위로 간신히 보일 만큼 떠올라 있었으니, 떠도는 몇 가닥 빛 티끌에 비추인 채 사다리 중간쯤에 서서, 자신은 올라갈 만큼 솜씨가 좋지 못해 감히 들어서지 못하는 곳을 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“Now a few russets, Tamsin.", "\"이제 러셋 사과를 몇 개, 탬신."], ["He used to like them almost as well as ribstones.”", "그 애는 그걸 립스톤 사과만큼이나 좋아했지.\""]], [["Thomasin turned and rolled aside the fern from another nook, where more mellow fruit greeted her with its ripe smell.", "토머신은 몸을 돌려 또 다른 구석의 고사리를 헤쳐냈으니, 거기서는 더 무르익은 열매가 농익은 냄새로 그녀를 맞이했다."], ["Before picking them out she stopped a moment.", "그것들을 골라내기 전에 그녀는 잠시 멈추었다."]], [["“Dear Clym, I wonder how your face looks now?”", "\"사랑하는 클림, 지금 네 얼굴은 어떤 모습일까?\""], ["she said, gazing abstractedly at the pigeon-hole, which admitted the sunlight so directly upon her brown hair and transparent tissues that it almost seemed to shine through her.", "그녀는 비둘기 구멍을 멍하니 바라보며 말했으니, 그 구멍으로 햇빛이 그녀의 갈색 머리카락과 투명한 살결 위로 어찌나 곧장 비쳐 들던지 거의 그녀를 꿰뚫고 빛나는 듯했다."]], [["“If he could have been dear to you in another way,” said Mrs.", "\"그 애가 너에게 다른 방식으로 소중한 사이였더라면,\""], ["Yeobright from the ladder, “this might have been a happy meeting.”", "사다리에서 예오브라이트 부인이 말했다. \"이번 만남은 행복한 자리가 됐을 텐데.\""]], [["“Is there any use in saying what can do no good, Aunt?”", "\"아무 소용도 없는 말을 해서 무슨 쓸모가 있어요, 고모?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"있지.\""], ["said her aunt, with some warmth.", "고모가 다소 열을 띠며 말했다."], ["“To thoroughly fill the air with the past misfortune, so that other girls may take warning and keep clear of it.”", "\"지난 불행으로 공기를 속속들이 채워서, 다른 처녀들이 경고로 삼아 그런 일을 멀리하게 하는 거야.\""]], [["Thomasin lowered her face to the apples again.", "토머신은 다시 사과 쪽으로 얼굴을 숙였다."], ["“I am a warning to others, just as thieves and drunkards and gamblers are,”", "\"전 다른 이들에게 경고가 되는 거예요, 도둑이며 술꾼이며 노름꾼이 그러듯이요.\""], ["she said in a low voice.", "그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“What a class to belong to!", "\"그런 부류에 끼다니!"], ["Do I really belong to them?", "제가 정말 그들 부류인가요?"], ["’Tis absurd!", "어처구니없어요!"], ["Yet why, Aunt, does everybody keep on making me think that I do, by the way they behave towards me?", "그런데 고모, 왜 다들 저를 대하는 태도로 제가 그렇다고 자꾸 생각하게 만드는 걸까요?"], ["Why don’t people judge me by my acts?", "왜 사람들은 제 행실로 저를 판단하지 않는 거죠?"], ["Now, look at me as I kneel here, picking up these apples—do I look like a lost woman?...", "자, 제가 여기 무릎 꿇고 이 사과들을 줍는 모습을 보세요—제가 타락한 여자처럼 보이나요?……"], ["I wish all good women were as good as I!”", "모든 선량한 여자들이 저만큼만 선량하면 좋으련만!\""], ["she added vehemently.", "그녀가 격하게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Strangers don’t see you as I do,” said Mrs.", "\"낯선 사람들은 나처럼 너를 보지 않아.\""], ["Yeobright; “they judge from false report.", "예오브라이트 부인이 말했다. \"그들은 거짓 소문으로 판단하지."], ["Well, it is a silly job, and I am partly to blame.”", "그래, 이건 어리석은 일이고, 나한테도 얼마간 잘못이 있어.\""]], [["“How quickly a rash thing can be done!”", "\"경솔한 일은 어찌 그리 순식간에 저질러지는지!\""], ["replied the girl.", "처녀가 대꾸했다."], ["Her lips were quivering, and tears so crowded themselves into her eyes that she could hardly distinguish apples from fern as she continued industriously searching to hide her weakness.", "그녀의 입술이 떨리고 있었고, 눈물이 어찌나 눈에 그득 차오르던지, 약한 모습을 감추려 부지런히 사과를 골라내면서도 사과와 고사리를 거의 분간하지 못할 지경이었다."]], [["“As soon as you have finished getting the apples,”", "\"사과를 다 챙기는 대로,\""], ["her aunt said, descending the ladder, “come down, and we’ll go for the holly.", "고모가 사다리를 내려오며 말했다. \"내려오너라, 우리 호랑가시나무를 구하러 가자."], ["There is nobody on the heath this afternoon, and you need not fear being stared at.", "오늘 오후엔 황야에 아무도 없으니, 사람들 눈에 띌까 두려워할 것 없다."], ["We must get some berries, or Clym will never believe in our preparations.”", "빨간 열매를 좀 구해야지, 안 그러면 클림은 우리가 준비했다는 걸 믿지 않을 게야.\""]], [["Thomasin came down when the apples were collected, and together they went through the white palings to the heath beyond.", "사과를 다 모으자 토머신은 내려왔고, 둘은 함께 흰 말뚝 울타리를 지나 그 너머 황야로 갔다."], ["The open hills were airy and clear, and the remote atmosphere appeared, as it often appears on a fine winter day, in distinct planes of illumination independently toned, the rays which lit the nearer tracts of landscape streaming visibly across those further off; a stratum of ensaffroned light was imposed on a stratum of deep blue, and behind these lay still remoter scenes wrapped in frigid grey.", "탁 트인 언덕은 공기가 상쾌하고 맑았으며, 먼 대기는 화창한 겨울날에 흔히 그러하듯 저마다 독립적으로 색조를 띤 또렷한 빛의 층들로 나타났으니, 풍경의 가까운 자락을 밝히는 빛살은 더 먼 자락을 가로질러 눈에 보이게 흘러갔고, 사프란빛으로 물든 빛의 한 층이 짙은 푸른빛 한 층 위에 얹혔으며, 이것들 뒤로는 더욱 먼 풍경들이 싸늘한 잿빛에 싸여 누워 있었다."]], [["They reached the place where the hollies grew, which was in a conical pit, so that the tops of the trees were not much above the general level of the ground.", "그들은 호랑가시나무가 자라는 곳에 이르렀으니, 원뿔형으로 파인 구덩이 안이라 나무 꼭대기조차 지면의 일반 높이보다 그리 높지 않았다."], ["Thomasin stepped up into a fork of one of the bushes, as she had done under happier circumstances on many similar occasions, and with a small chopper that they had brought she began to lop off the heavily berried boughs.", "토머신은 비슷한 자리에서 더 행복한 처지에 여러 차례 그러했듯이 덤불 하나의 갈라진 가지 위로 올라섰고, 그들이 가져온 작은 도끼로 빨간 열매가 묵직하게 달린 가지들을 쳐내기 시작했다."]], [["“Don’t scratch your face,”", "\"얼굴을 긁히지 마라.\""], ["said her aunt, who stood at the edge of the pit, regarding the girl as she held on amid the glistening green and scarlet masses of the tree.", "구덩이 가장자리에 서서, 반짝이는 초록과 진홍 더미 속에 매달린 처녀를 바라보며 고모가 말했다."], ["“Will you walk with me to meet him this evening?”", "\"오늘 저녁 그를 마중하러 나랑 같이 가겠니?\""]], [["“I should like to.", "\"그러고 싶어요."], ["Else it would seem as if I had forgotten him,”", "안 그러면 제가 그를 잊은 것처럼 보일 테니까요.\""], ["said Thomasin, tossing out a bough.", "토머신이 가지 하나를 던지며 말했다."], ["“Not that that would matter much; I belong to one man; nothing can alter that.", "\"그게 그리 대수로운 일은 아니지만요. 전 한 사내한테 속한 몸이고, 그건 무엇으로도 바꿀 수 없으니까요."], ["And that man I must marry, for my pride’s sake.”", "그리고 그 사내와 전 결혼해야 해요, 제 자존심을 위해서라도요.\""]], [["“I am afraid—”", "\"내 걱정은—\""], ["began Mrs. Yeobright.", "예오브라이트 부인이 입을 열었다."]], [["“Ah, you think, ‘That weak girl—how is she going to get a man to marry her when she chooses?’ But let me tell you one thing, Aunt: Mr.", "\"아, 고모는 이렇게 생각하시는 거죠. '저 약해빠진 애가—마음만 먹으면 어떻게 자기랑 결혼할 사내를 얻겠다는 거야?'"], ["Wildeve is not a profligate man, any more than I am an improper woman.", "하지만 한 가지만 말씀드릴게요, 고모. 와일디브 씨는 방탕한 사내가 아니에요, 제가 부정한 여자가 아닌 것과 마찬가지로요."], ["He has an unfortunate manner, and doesn’t try to make people like him if they don’t wish to do it of their own accord.”", "그분은 태도가 좀 불운한 데가 있고, 사람들이 제 마음으로 좋아하려 들지 않으면 굳이 자기를 좋아하게 만들려고 애쓰지 않을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Thomasin,”", "\"토머신.\""], ["said Mrs.", "예오브라이트 부인이 조용히 조카에게 눈길을 박으며 말했다."], ["Yeobright quietly, fixing her eye upon her niece, “do you think you deceive me in your defence of Mr. Wildeve?”", "\"와일디브 씨를 그렇게 두둔하면서 네가 나를 속이고 있다고 생각하니?\""]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 말씀이세요?\""]], [["“I have long had a suspicion that your love for him has changed its colour since you have found him not to be the saint you thought him, and that you act a part to me.”", "\"난 오래전부터 의심해왔다, 네가 그를 네가 생각하던 성인聖人이 아님을 알게 된 뒤로 그에 대한 네 사랑의 빛깔이 변했으며, 네가 내 앞에서 연기를 하고 있다고 말이다.\""]], [["“He wished to marry me, and I wish to marry him.”", "\"그분은 저와 결혼하고 싶어 했고, 저도 그분과 결혼하고 싶어요.\""]], [["“Now, I put it to you: would you at this present moment agree to be his wife if that had not happened to entangle you with him?”", "\"자, 그럼 내가 너한테 묻겠다. 그 일이 너를 그와 얽어매지 않았더라면, 너는 지금 이 순간에도 그의 아내가 되겠다고 하겠느냐?\""]], [["Thomasin looked into the tree and appeared much disturbed.", "토머신은 나무 안쪽을 들여다보았고 몹시 동요한 듯했다."], ["“Aunt,”", "\"고모.\""], ["she said presently, “I have, I think, a right to refuse to answer that question.”", "그녀가 이윽고 말했다. \"전 그 물음에 답하기를 거부할 권리가 있다고 생각해요.\""]], [["“Yes, you have.”", "\"그래, 있고말고.\""]], [["“You may think what you choose.", "\"고모 좋으실 대로 생각하세요."], ["I have never implied to you by word or deed that I have grown to think otherwise of him, and I never will.", "전 말로든 행동으로든 제가 그분을 달리 생각하게 됐다는 기색을 고모께 내비친 적이 없고, 앞으로도 결코 그러지 않을 거예요."], ["And I shall marry him.”", "그리고 전 그분과 결혼할 거예요.\""]], [["“Well, wait till he repeats his offer.", "\"글쎄, 그가 청혼을 다시 할 때까지 기다려보렴."], ["I think he may do it, now that he knows—something I told him.", "이제 그가—내가 그에게 말해준 어떤 걸—알았으니, 그가 그럴지도 모르겠구나."], ["I don’t for a moment dispute that it is the most proper thing for you to marry him.", "네가 그와 결혼하는 게 가장 온당한 일이라는 건 나도 한순간도 의심하지 않아."], ["Much as I have objected to him in bygone days, I agree with you now, you may be sure.", "지난날 내가 그를 그토록 못마땅해했지만, 이제는 너와 생각이 같단다, 그건 분명히 알아두렴."], ["It is the only way out of a false position, and a very galling one.”", "그것만이 거짓된 처지에서, 그것도 몹시 속 쓰린 처지에서 벗어나는 유일한 길이니까.\""]], [["“What did you tell him?”", "\"그분께 뭐라고 하셨는데요?\""]], [["“That he was standing in the way of another lover of yours.”", "\"그가 네 다른 구애자의 앞길을 막고 서 있다고 했지.\""]], [["“Aunt,”", "\"고모.\""], ["said Thomasin, with round eyes, “what _do_ you mean?”", "토머신이 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 말했다. \"대체 무슨 말씀이세요?\""]], [["“Don’t be alarmed; it was my duty.", "\"놀라지 마라. 그건 내 도리였어."], ["I can say no more about it now, but when it is over I will tell you exactly what I said, and why I said it.”", "지금은 그 일에 대해 더 말할 수 없지만, 다 끝나면 내가 정확히 뭐라고 했는지, 또 왜 그렇게 말했는지 네게 일러주마.\""]], [["Thomasin was perforce content.", "토머신은 어쩔 수 없이 잠자코 따랐다."]], [["“And you will keep the secret of my would-be marriage from Clym for the present?”", "\"그럼 제가 결혼하려 한다는 비밀은 당분간 클림에게 숨겨주실 거죠?\""], ["she next asked.", "그녀가 이어 물었다."]], [["“I have given my word to.", "\"그러마고 약속했지."], ["But what is the use of it?", "하지만 그게 무슨 소용이겠니?"], ["He must soon know what has happened.", "무슨 일이 있었는지 그가 곧 알게 될 텐데."], ["A mere look at your face will show him that something is wrong.”", "네 얼굴만 한 번 봐도 뭔가 잘못됐다는 걸 알아챌 게야.\""]], [["Thomasin turned and regarded her aunt from the tree.", "토머신은 나무에서 몸을 돌려 고모를 바라보았다."], ["“Now, hearken to me,”", "\"자, 제 말 좀 들어보세요.\""], ["she said, her delicate voice expanding into firmness by a force which was other than physical.", "그녀가 말했으니, 그 가냘픈 목소리는 육체적인 것과는 다른 어떤 힘으로 단호하게 부풀어 올랐다."], ["“Tell him nothing.", "\"그분께 아무 말도 하지 마세요."], ["If he finds out that I am not worthy to be his cousin, let him.", "제가 그분의 사촌 될 자격이 없다는 걸 그분이 알아낸다면, 그러라고 하세요."], ["But, since he loved me once, we will not pain him by telling him my trouble too soon.", "하지만 그분이 한때 저를 사랑했으니, 제 곤경을 너무 일찍 알려서 그분을 아프게 하진 않을 거예요."], ["The air is full of the story, I know; but gossips will not dare to speak of it to him for the first few days.", "이 이야기가 사방에 퍼져 있다는 건 저도 알아요. 하지만 험담꾼들도 처음 며칠은 감히 그분께 그 말을 꺼내지 못할 거예요."], ["His closeness to me is the very thing that will hinder the tale from reaching him early.", "제가 그분과 가까운 사이라는 바로 그 점이 이야기가 일찍 그분 귀에 들어가는 걸 막아줄 테니까요."], ["If I am not made safe from sneers in a week or two I will tell him myself.”", "한두 주 안에 제가 비웃음에서 안전해지지 못한다면, 제가 직접 그분께 말씀드릴게요.\""]], [["The earnestness with which Thomasin spoke prevented further objections.", "토머신이 말하는 그 진지함이 더 이상의 반대를 가로막았다."], ["Her aunt simply said, “Very well.", "고모는 그저 이렇게만 말했다."], ["He should by rights have been told at the time that the wedding was going to be.", "\"좋아. 결혼식을 올릴 거라는 걸 마땅히 그때 그에게 알렸어야 했어."], ["He will never forgive you for your secrecy.”", "그는 네가 숨긴 걸 결코 용서하지 않을 게다.\""]], [["“Yes, he will, when he knows it was because I wished to spare him, and that I did not expect him home so soon.", "\"용서하실 거예요, 제가 그분을 아끼고 싶어서 그랬다는 걸, 그리고 제가 그분이 이렇게 빨리 집에 오시리라고는 예상하지 못했다는 걸 아시면요."], ["And you must not let me stand in the way of your Christmas party.", "그리고 저 때문에 고모의 크리스마스 모임을 그르치게 하면 안 돼요."], ["Putting it off would only make matters worse.”", "미루는 건 일을 더 나쁘게 만들 뿐이니까요.\""]], [["“Of course I shall not.", "\"물론 그러지 않을 게다."], ["I do not wish to show myself beaten before all Egdon, and the sport of a man like Wildeve.", "난 온 에그던 앞에서, 또 와일디브 같은 사내의 놀림감이 되어 패배한 꼴을 보이고 싶지 않아."], ["We have enough berries now, I think, and we had better take them home.", "이제 빨간 열매는 넉넉히 모은 듯하니, 집으로 가져가는 게 좋겠구나."], ["By the time we have decked the house with this and hung up the mistletoe, we must think of starting to meet him.”", "이걸로 집을 꾸미고 겨우살이를 매달 즈음이면, 그를 마중하러 나설 채비를 해야 할 게야.\""]], [["Thomasin came out of the tree, shook from her hair and dress the loose berries which had fallen thereon, and went down the hill with her aunt, each woman bearing half the gathered boughs. It was now nearly four o’clock, and the sunlight was leaving the vales.", "토머신은 나무에서 내려와, 머리와 옷에 떨어진 흩어진 열매들을 털어내고, 고모와 함께 비탈을 내려갔으니, 두 여자는 저마다 모은 가지의 절반씩을 이고 있었다."], ["When the west grew red the two relatives came again from the house and plunged into the heath in a different direction from the first, towards a point in the distant highway along which the expected man was to return.", "이제 거의 네 시였고, 햇빛은 골짜기를 떠나고 있었다."], ["III. How a Little Sound Produced a Great Dream", "서쪽이 붉어지자 두 친척은 다시 집에서 나와, 처음과는 다른 방향으로, 기다리던 사내가 돌아올 저 먼 큰길의 한 지점을 향해 황야 속으로 들어섰다."]], [["Eustacia stood just within the heath, straining her eyes in the direction of Mrs. Yeobright’s house and premises.", "유스테이샤는 황야 어귀에 막 들어선 채, 예오브라이트 부인의 집과 땅 쪽으로 눈을 부릅뜨고 있었다."], ["No light, sound, or movement was perceptible there.", "그곳에는 불빛도, 소리도, 움직임도 분간되지 않았다."], ["The evening was chilly; the spot was dark and lonely.", "저녁은 쌀쌀했고, 그 자리는 어둡고 외로웠다."], ["She inferred that the guest had not yet come; and after lingering ten or fifteen minutes she turned again towards home.", "그녀는 손님이 아직 오지 않았다고 짐작했고, 십 분이나 십오 분쯤 머물다가 다시 집 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["She had not far retraced her steps when sounds in front of her betokened the approach of persons in conversation along the same path.", "왔던 길을 그리 멀리 되짚어 가지 않았을 때, 앞쪽에서 들려오는 소리가 같은 길을 따라 대화하며 다가오는 사람들의 접근을 알려주었다."], ["Soon their heads became visible against the sky.", "이윽고 그들의 머리가 하늘을 배경으로 드러났다."], ["They were walking slowly; and though it was too dark for much discovery of character from aspect, the gait of them showed that they were not workers on the heath.", "그들은 천천히 걷고 있었고, 생김새로 인물됨을 알아보기엔 너무 어두웠으나, 그 걸음걸이로 보아 황야의 일꾼들은 아니었다."], ["Eustacia stepped a little out of the foot-track to let them pass.", "유스테이샤는 그들이 지나가도록 발길에서 조금 비켜섰다."], ["They were two women and a man; and the voices of the women were those of Mrs. Yeobright and Thomasin.", "그들은 두 여자와 한 사내였고, 여자들의 목소리는 예오브라이트 부인과 토머신의 것이었다."]], [["They went by her, and at the moment of passing appeared to discern her dusky form.", "그들은 그녀 곁을 지나쳤고, 스쳐 가는 순간 그녀의 어스름한 형체를 알아본 듯했다."], ["There came to her ears in a masculine voice, “Good night!”", "한 사내의 목소리로 \"안녕히 주무세요!\" 하는 소리가 그녀의 귀에 와 닿았다."], ["She murmured a reply, glided by them, and turned round.", "그녀는 우물거리며 답하고는, 그들 곁을 미끄러져 지나, 몸을 돌렸다."], ["She could not, for a moment, believe that chance, unrequested, had brought into her presence the soul of the house she had gone to inspect, the man without whom her inspection would not have been thought of.", "그녀는 한순간 믿을 수가 없었으니, 우연이 청하지도 않았는데 그녀 앞으로, 그녀가 살펴보러 갔던 그 집의 영혼을, 그가 없었다면 그 살핌도 애초에 생각조차 못 했을 그 사내를 데려다 놓았다는 것을."]], [["She strained her eyes to see them, but was unable.", "그녀는 그들을 보려고 눈을 부릅떴으나 그럴 수 없었다."], ["Such was her intentness, however, that it seemed as if her ears were performing the functions of seeing as well as hearing.", "그러나 그 골똘함이 어찌나 깊던지, 마치 그녀의 귀가 듣는 기능뿐 아니라 보는 기능까지 수행하는 듯했다."], ["This extension of power can almost be believed in at such moments.", "그런 순간에는 이런 능력의 확장이 거의 믿어질 만하다."], ["The deaf Dr. Kitto was probably under the influence of a parallel fancy when he described his body as having become, by long endeavour, so sensitive to vibrations that he had gained the power of perceiving by it as by ears.", "귀가 먼 키토 박사도 오랜 노력 끝에 자신의 몸이 진동에 어찌나 민감해졌던지 귀로 하듯 그 몸으로 지각하는 능력을 얻었다고 묘사했을 때, 아마 이와 비슷한 환상에 사로잡혀 있었을 것이다."]], [["She could follow every word that the ramblers uttered.", "그녀는 거니는 이들이 입에 올리는 말을 한마디 한마디 좇을 수 있었다."], ["They were talking no secrets.", "그들은 비밀을 이야기하고 있지 않았다."], ["They were merely indulging in the ordinary vivacious chat of relatives who have long been parted in person though not in soul.", "그들은 그저, 몸은 오래 떨어져 있었으나 마음은 그렇지 않았던 친척들이 으레 나누는 활기찬 잡담에 빠져 있을 뿐이었다."], ["But it was not to the words that Eustacia listened; she could not even have recalled, a few minutes later, what the words were.", "그러나 유스테이샤가 귀 기울인 것은 그 말들이 아니었으니, 그녀는 몇 분 뒤에는 그 말들이 무엇이었는지조차 떠올리지 못했을 것이다."], ["It was to the alternating voice that gave out about one-tenth of them—the voice that had wished her good night.", "그것은 그 말 가운데 십분의 일쯤을 내놓던 번갈아 들려오는 목소리—그녀에게 안녕히 주무시라 했던 그 목소리였다."], ["Sometimes this throat uttered Yes, sometimes it uttered No; sometimes it made inquiries about a time-worn denizen of the place.", "그 목구멍은 어떤 때는 예, 라고 하고, 어떤 때는 아니오, 라고 했으며, 어떤 때는 그 고장의 한 늙은 거주민에 관해 물었다."], ["Once it surprised her notions by remarking upon the friendliness and geniality written in the faces of the hills around.", "한번은 둘레 언덕들의 얼굴에 어린 다정함과 정겨움을 두고 한마디 함으로써 그녀의 관념을 놀라게 했다."]], [["The three voices passed on, and decayed and died out upon her ear.", "세 목소리는 지나가 버렸고, 그녀의 귓전에서 잦아들다가 사라졌다."], ["Thus much had been granted her; and all besides withheld.", "이만큼이 그녀에게 허락되었고, 그 밖의 모든 것은 보류되었다."], ["No event could have been more exciting.", "이보다 더 흥미진진한 일은 있을 수 없었다."], ["During the greater part of the afternoon she had been entrancing herself by imagining the fascination which must attend a man come direct from beautiful Paris—laden with its atmosphere, familiar with its charms.", "오후의 대부분을 그녀는 아름다운 파리에서—그곳의 분위기를 가득 싣고, 그곳의 매혹에 익숙한 채—곧장 온 사내에게 따르기 마련인 매력을 상상하며 황홀경에 빠져 있었다. 그런데 그 사내가 그녀에게 인사를 건넨 것이다."], ["And this man had greeted her. With the departure of the figures the profuse articulations of the women wasted away from her memory; but the accents of the other stayed on.", "형체들이 멀어지자 여자들의 흘러넘치던 또렷한 말소리는 그녀의 기억에서 스러져갔으나, 그 다른 목소리의 억양은 머물렀다."], ["Was there anything in the voice of Mrs. Yeobright’s son—for Clym it was—startling as a sound?", "예오브라이트 부인의 아들—그가 바로 클림이었으니—그 목소리에 소리로서 놀랄 만한 무언가가 있었던가?"], ["No; it was simply comprehensive.", "아니었다. 그것은 그저 모든 것을 아우르는 목소리였다."], ["All emotional things were possible to the speaker of that “good night.”", "그 \"안녕히 주무세요\"를 말한 이에게는 온갖 감정의 일이 가능했다."], ["Eustacia’s imagination supplied the rest—except the solution to one riddle.", "유스테이샤의 상상이 나머지를 채워주었다—단 하나의 수수께끼에 대한 해답만 빼고."], ["What _could_ the tastes of that man be who saw friendliness and geniality in these shaggy hills?", "이 텁수룩한 언덕들에서 다정함과 정겨움을 본 그 사내의 취향은 대체 어떤 것일 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["On such occasions as this a thousand ideas pass through a highly charged woman’s head; and they indicate themselves on her face; but the changes, though actual, are minute.", "이런 때에는 잔뜩 충전된 여인의 머릿속을 천 가지 생각이 스쳐 지나가고, 그것들은 그녀의 얼굴에 드러나기 마련이지만, 그 변화는 실제이긴 해도 미세하다."], ["Eustacia’s features went through a rhythmical succession of them.", "유스테이샤의 이목구비는 그런 변화의 율동적인 연속을 거쳤다."], ["She glowed; remembering the mendacity of the imagination, she flagged; then she freshened; then she fired; then she cooled again.", "그녀는 발갛게 달아올랐다가, 상상의 거짓됨을 떠올리며 시들해졌다가, 다시 생기를 띠었다가, 다시 불타올랐다가, 다시 식어버렸다."], ["It was a cycle of aspects, produced by a cycle of visions.", "그것은 환영의 순환이 빚어낸 양상樣相의 순환이었다."]], [["Eustacia entered her own house; she was excited.", "유스테이샤는 자기 집에 들어섰으니, 흥분해 있었다."], ["Her grandfather was enjoying himself over the fire, raking about the ashes and exposing the red-hot surface of the turves, so that their lurid glare irradiated the chimney-corner with the hues of a furnace.", "그녀의 할아버지는 난롯가에서 흥겨워하며, 재를 헤집어 잔디의 시뻘겋게 단 표면을 드러내고 있었으니, 그 음산한 빛이 굴뚝 모퉁이를 용광로 같은 빛깔로 환히 밝혔다."]], [["“Why is it that we are never friendly with the Yeobrights?”", "\"우리는 어째서 예오브라이트네 사람들과 통 가깝게 지내지 않는 거예요?\""], ["she said, coming forward and stretching her soft hands over the warmth.", "그녀가 앞으로 나와 그 온기 위로 부드러운 손을 뻗으며 말했다."], ["“I wish we were.", "\"우리가 가깝게 지내면 좋겠어요."], ["They seem to be very nice people.”", "아주 좋은 사람들 같던데요.\""]], [["“Be hanged if I know why,”", "\"내가 그 이유를 알면 목을 매달지.\""], ["said the captain.", "선장이 말했다."], ["“I liked the old man well enough, though he was as rough as a hedge.", "\"그 노인은 산울타리처럼 거칠긴 했어도 난 그런대로 좋아했어."], ["But you would never have cared to go there, even if you might have, I am well sure.”", "하지만 넌 거기 갈 수 있었다 해도 결코 가고 싶어 하지 않았을 게다, 그건 내가 잘 알지.\""]], [["“Why shouldn’t I?”", "\"제가 왜 안 가고 싶어 했겠어요?\""]], [["“Your town tastes would find them far too countrified.", "\"네 도시 취향에는 그들이 너무 촌스러워 보였을 거다."], ["They sit in the kitchen, drink mead and elder-wine, and sand the floor to keep it clean.", "그들은 부엌에 앉아 벌꿀술과 엘더베리주를 마시고, 바닥을 깨끗이 하려고 모래를 뿌린단다."], ["A sensible way of life; but how would you like it?”", "분별 있는 삶의 방식이지. 하지만 네가 그걸 좋아하겠니?\""]], [["“I thought Mrs. Yeobright was a ladylike woman?", "\"예오브라이트 부인은 귀부인다운 여자인 줄 알았는데요?"], ["A curate’s daughter, was she not?”", "부목사의 딸이지 않았나요?\""]], [["“Yes; but she was obliged to live as her husband did; and I suppose she has taken kindly to it by this time.", "\"그래. 하지만 남편이 사는 대로 살 수밖에 없었고, 이제는 그 삶에 정이 들었겠지."], ["Ah, I recollect that I once accidentally offended her, and I have never seen her since.”", "아, 생각해보니 내가 언젠가 무심코 그 사람 기분을 상하게 한 적이 있어서, 그 뒤로는 통 못 봤구나.\""]], [["That night was an eventful one to Eustacia’s brain, and one which she hardly ever forgot.", "그날 밤은 유스테이샤의 머릿속에 사건으로 가득한 밤이었고, 그녀가 좀처럼 잊지 못할 밤이었다."], ["She dreamt a dream; and few human beings, from Nebuchadnezzar to the Swaffham tinker, ever dreamt a more remarkable one.", "그녀는 꿈을 꾸었으니, 네부카드네자르에서 스와팜의 땜장이에 이르기까지, 그보다 더 놀라운 꿈을 꾼 인간은 거의 없었다."], ["Such an elaborately developed, perplexing, exciting dream was certainly never dreamed by a girl in Eustacia’s situation before.", "그토록 정교하게 펼쳐지고, 어리둥절하고, 흥분되는 꿈을 유스테이샤 같은 처지의 처녀가 꾼 적은 분명 일찍이 없었다."], ["It had as many ramifications as the Cretan labyrinth, as many fluctuations as the northern lights, as much colour as a parterre in June, and was as crowded with figures as a coronation.", "그것은 크레타의 미궁만큼이나 갈래가 많았고, 북극광만큼이나 변화무쌍했으며, 유월의 화단만큼이나 색채가 풍부했고, 대관식만큼이나 형상들로 북적였다."], ["To Queen Scheherazade the dream might have seemed not far removed from commonplace; and to a girl just returned from all the courts of Europe it might have seemed not more than interesting.", "셰에라자드 여왕에게라면 그 꿈은 평범한 것에서 그리 멀지 않게 여겨졌을지도 모르고, 유럽의 온갖 궁정을 막 돌아온 처녀에게라면 흥미로운 정도로밖에 여겨지지 않았을지도 모른다."], ["But amid the circumstances of Eustacia’s life it was as wonderful as a dream could be.", "그러나 유스테이샤의 삶의 정황 속에서 그것은 꿈이 그럴 수 있는 한 가장 경이로운 것이었다."]], [["There was, however, gradually evolved from its transformation scenes a less extravagant episode, in which the heath dimly appeared behind the general brilliancy of the action.", "그러나 그 변환의 장면들로부터 차츰 덜 터무니없는 한 삽화가 펼쳐져 나왔으니, 그 속에서는 전체적인 화려함을 거느린 행위 뒤로 황야가 어렴풋이 나타났다."], ["She was dancing to wondrous music, and her partner was the man in silver armour who had accompanied her through the previous fantastic changes, the visor of his helmet being closed.", "그녀는 경이로운 음악에 맞추어 춤추고 있었고, 그녀의 상대는 앞서의 환상적인 변화들을 그녀와 함께 거쳐온 은빛 갑옷의 사내였으니, 투구의 면갑은 닫혀 있었다."], ["The mazes of the dance were ecstatic.", "춤의 미로는 황홀했다."], ["Soft whispering came into her ear from under the radiant helmet, and she felt like a woman in Paradise.", "빛나는 투구 아래에서 부드러운 속삭임이 그녀의 귓속으로 흘러들었고, 그녀는 낙원에 있는 여인 같은 기분이었다."], ["Suddenly these two wheeled out from the mass of dancers, dived into one of the pools of the heath, and came out somewhere into an iridescent hollow, arched with rainbows.", "별안간 이 둘은 춤추는 무리에서 빙글 돌아 빠져나와, 황야의 못 하나로 뛰어들더니, 무지개로 아치를 이룬 어느 영롱한 우묵한 곳으로 나왔다."], ["“It must be here,”", "\"여기인 게 분명해.\""], ["said the voice by her side, and blushingly looking up she saw him removing his casque to kiss her.", "곁의 목소리가 말했고, 얼굴을 붉히며 올려다보니 그가 입 맞추려고 투구를 벗고 있었다."], ["At that moment there was a cracking noise, and his figure fell into fragments like a pack of cards.", "바로 그 순간 우지끈하는 소리가 나더니, 그의 형체가 카드 한 벌처럼 산산조각으로 무너져 내렸다."]], [["She cried aloud.", "그녀는 소리 내어 울부짖었다."], ["“O that I had seen his face!”", "\"아, 그의 얼굴을 보았더라면!\""]], [["Eustacia awoke.", "유스테이샤는 깨어났다."], ["The cracking had been that of the window shutter downstairs, which the maid-servant was opening to let in the day, now slowly increasing to Nature’s meagre allowance at this sickly time of the year.", "그 우지끈하는 소리는 아래층 덧창 소리였으니, 하녀가 그것을 열어 한 해의 이 병약한 시절에 자연이 인색하게 베푸는, 이제 더디게 늘어나는 낮빛을 들이는 참이었다."], ["“O that I had seen his face!”", "\"아, 그의 얼굴을 보았더라면!\""], ["she said again.", "그녀가 다시 말했다."], ["“’Twas meant for Mr. Yeobright!”", "\"그건 예오브라이트 씨를 뜻한 거였어!\""]], [["When she became cooler she perceived that many of the phases of the dream had naturally arisen out of the images and fancies of the day before.", "좀 더 마음이 식자 그녀는 그 꿈의 여러 국면이 으레 전날의 형상과 공상에서 비롯되었음을 알아차렸다."], ["But this detracted little from its interest, which lay in the excellent fuel it provided for newly kindled fervour.", "그러나 이것은 꿈의 흥미를 거의 깎아내리지 못했으니, 그 흥미란 새로이 지펴진 열정에 더없이 좋은 땔감을 대주는 데 있었기 때문이다."], ["She was at the modulating point between indifference and love, at the stage called “having a fancy for.”", "그녀는 무관심과 사랑 사이의 변조점變調點에, 이른바 \"마음이 끌리는\" 단계에 있었다."], ["It occurs once in the history of the most gigantic passions, and it is a period when they are in the hands of the weakest will.", "그것은 가장 거대한 정념의 역사에서도 단 한 번 찾아오며, 그 정념이 가장 약한 의지의 손아귀에 놓이는 시기이다."]], [["The perfervid woman was by this time half in love with a vision.", "이 열정에 들뜬 여인은 이제 환영과 반쯤 사랑에 빠져 있었다."], ["The fantastic nature of her passion, which lowered her as an intellect, raised her as a soul.", "그녀의 정념이 띤 환상적 성질은 그녀를 지성으로서는 낮추었으나 영혼으로서는 높여주었다."], ["If she had had a little more self-control she would have attenuated the emotion to nothing by sheer reasoning, and so have killed it off.", "그녀에게 자제력이 조금만 더 있었더라면 순전한 추론으로 그 감정을 무無로 잦아들게 하여 죽여버렸을 것이다."], ["If she had had a little less pride she might have gone and circumambulated the Yeobrights’ premises at Blooms-End at any maidenly sacrifice until she had seen him.", "자존심이 조금만 덜했더라면, 그를 볼 때까지 처녀로서 어떤 희생을 치르더라도 블룸스엔드의 예오브라이트네 땅을 빙빙 돌며 서성였을 것이다."], ["But Eustacia did neither of these things.", "그러나 유스테이샤는 이 가운데 어느 것도 하지 않았다."], ["She acted as the most exemplary might have acted, being so influenced; she took an airing twice or thrice a day upon the Egdon hills, and kept her eyes employed.", "그녀는 그토록 영향을 받았을 때 가장 모범적인 처녀가 했을 법한 대로 행동했으니, 하루에 두세 번 에그던 언덕으로 바람을 쐬러 나가, 두 눈을 부지런히 놀렸다."]], [["The first occasion passed, and he did not come that way.", "첫 번째 나들이가 지나갔으나, 그는 그쪽으로 오지 않았다."]], [["She promenaded a second time, and was again the sole wanderer there.", "그녀는 두 번째로 거닐었으나, 거기서 또다시 홀로 헤매는 이였다."]], [["The third time there was a dense fog; she looked around, but without much hope.", "세 번째에는 짙은 안개가 끼었으니, 그녀는 둘레를 둘러보았으나 큰 기대는 없었다."], ["Even if he had been walking within twenty yards of her she could not have seen him.", "설령 그가 그녀에게서 스무 야드 안에서 걷고 있었다 한들 그녀는 그를 보지 못했을 것이다."]], [["At the fourth attempt to encounter him it began to rain in torrents, and she turned back.", "그를 만나려는 네 번째 시도에서는 비가 억수같이 퍼붓기 시작했고, 그녀는 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["The fifth sally was in the afternoon; it was fine, and she remained out long, walking to the very top of the valley in which Blooms-End lay.", "다섯 번째 출격은 오후였으니, 날이 화창하여 그녀는 오래도록 밖에 머물며, 블룸스엔드가 자리한 골짜기의 맨 꼭대기까지 걸어 올라갔다."], ["She saw the white paling about half a mile off; but he did not appear.", "그녀는 반 마일쯤 떨어진 흰 말뚝 울타리를 보았으나, 그는 나타나지 않았다."], ["It was almost with heart-sickness that she came home and with a sense of shame at her weakness.", "그녀가 집으로 돌아온 것은 거의 가슴앓이를 하면서였고, 자신의 나약함에 부끄러움을 느끼면서였다."], ["She resolved to look for the man from Paris no more.", "그녀는 더는 파리에서 온 사내를 찾지 않기로 마음먹었다."]], [["But Providence is nothing if not coquettish; and no sooner had Eustacia formed this resolve than the opportunity came which, while sought, had been entirely withholden.", "그러나 섭리란 요염하지 않으면 아무것도 아닌 법이니, 유스테이샤가 이 결심을 굳히자마자, 찾을 때는 온전히 보류되었던 그 기회가 찾아왔다."]], [["IV. Eustacia Is Led on to an Adventure\nIn the evening of this last day of expectation, which was the twenty-third of December, Eustacia was at home alone.", "기다림의 마지막 날인 이 십이월 이십삼일 저녁, 유스테이샤는 집에 홀로 있었다."], ["She had passed the recent hour in lamenting over a rumour newly come to her ears—that Yeobright’s visit to his mother was to be of short duration, and would end some time the next week.", "그녀는 새로이 귀에 들어온 한 소문—예오브라이트가 어머니를 찾아온 일정이 짧게 끝나, 다음 주 어느 때쯤 마무리되리라는 소문—을 두고 한탄하며 근래의 한 시간을 보냈다."], ["“Naturally,”", "\"당연하지.\""], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 속으로 말했다."], ["A man in the full swing of his activities in a gay city could not afford to linger long on Egdon Heath.", "화려한 도시에서 활동에 한창 박차를 가하던 사내가 에그던 황야에서 오래 머물 여유는 없을 터였다."], ["That she would behold face to face the owner of the awakening voice within the limits of such a holiday was most unlikely, unless she were to haunt the environs of his mother’s house like a robin, to do which was difficult and unseemly.", "그 깨어나게 하는 목소리의 임자를 그토록 짧은 명절 기간 안에 얼굴을 맞대고 보게 될 가능성은, 그녀가 울새처럼 그의 어머니 집 언저리를 맴돌지 않는 한 거의 없었으니, 그렇게 하는 것은 어렵고도 꼴사나운 일이었다."]], [["The customary expedient of provincial girls and men in such circumstances is churchgoing.", "이런 처지의 시골 처녀와 사내가 흔히 쓰는 방편은 교회에 가는 일이다."], ["In an ordinary village or country town one can safely calculate that, either on Christmas day or the Sunday contiguous, any native home for the holidays, who has not through age or ennui lost the appetite for seeing and being seen, will turn up in some pew or other, shining with hope, self-consciousness, and new clothes.", "여느 마을이나 시골 읍에서는, 크리스마스 당일이든 그 인접한 일요일이든, 명절을 쇠러 고향에 와 있는 토박이로서 나이나 권태로 보고 보이려는 욕구를 잃지 않은 자라면 누구나 어느 신도석엔가 나타나, 희망과 자의식과 새 옷으로 빛나리라고 안심하고 셈할 수 있다."], ["Thus the congregation on Christmas morning is mostly a Tussaud collection of celebrities who have been born in the neighbourhood.", "그리하여 크리스마스 아침의 회중會衆은 대개 그 인근에서 태어난 명사들의 투소 밀랍 인형 진열장 같은 것이 된다."], ["Hither the mistress, left neglected at home all the year, can steal and observe the development of the returned lover who has forgotten her, and think as she watches him over her prayer book that he may throb with a renewed fidelity when novelties have lost their charm.", "한 해 내내 집에 버려진 채 방치된 정인情人은 이곳으로 몰래 와서, 자기를 잊어버린 돌아온 연인의 변모를 지켜보며, 기도서 너머로 그를 살피며 이런 생각을 할 수 있으니, 새로운 것들이 매력을 잃고 나면 그가 되살아난 정절情節로 두근거릴지도 모른다는 것이다."], ["And hither a comparatively recent settler like Eustacia may betake herself to scrutinize the person of a native son who left home before her advent upon the scene, and consider if the friendship of his parents be worth cultivating during his next absence in order to secure a knowledge of him on his next return.", "또한 유스테이샤처럼 비교적 최근에 정착한 이도 이곳으로 와서, 그녀가 이 무대에 등장하기 전에 고향을 떠난 한 토박이 아들의 인물됨을 면밀히 살피고, 그가 다음번에 돌아왔을 때 그를 알아둘 발판을 마련하고자 그가 다음번에 떠나 있는 동안 그 부모와의 친분을 다질 만한 가치가 있을지 따져볼 수 있는 것이다."]], [["But these tender schemes were not feasible among the scattered inhabitants of Egdon Heath.", "그러나 이런 다정한 책략들은 에그던 황야의 흩어진 주민들 사이에서는 실현될 수 없었다."], ["In name they were parishioners, but virtually they belonged to no parish at all.", "명목상으로 그들은 교구민이었으나, 실질적으로는 어느 교구에도 속해 있지 않았다."], ["People who came to these few isolated houses to keep Christmas with their friends remained in their friends’ chimney-corners drinking mead and other comforting liquors till they left again for good and all.", "친구들과 크리스마스를 쇠러 이 몇 채 안 되는 외딴집들로 온 사람들은 영영 떠날 때까지 친구의 굴뚝 모퉁이에 눌러앉아 벌꿀술이며 그 밖의 속을 데워주는 술을 마셨다."], ["Rain, snow, ice, mud everywhere around, they did not care to trudge two or three miles to sit wet-footed and splashed to the nape of their necks among those who, though in some measure neighbours, lived close to the church, and entered it clean and dry.", "둘레 사방이 비며 눈이며 얼음이며 진흙이니, 어느 정도 이웃이긴 해도 교회 가까이 살면서 깨끗하고 보송보송하게 교회에 드는 사람들 틈에, 발이 젖고 목덜미까지 흙탕물이 튄 채 앉아 있으려고 이삼 마일을 터덜터덜 걸어갈 마음은 없었던 것이다."], ["Eustacia knew it was ten to one that Clym Yeobright would go to no church at all during his few days of leave, and that it would be a waste of labour for her to go driving the pony and gig over a bad road in hope to see him there.", "유스테이샤는 클림 예오브라이트가 며칠 안 되는 휴가 동안 어느 교회에도 가지 않을 공산이 십중팔구라는 것을, 그리고 그곳에서 그를 보겠다는 희망으로 험한 길에 조랑말과 이륜마차를 몰고 가는 것이 헛수고이리라는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["It was dusk, and she was sitting by the fire in the dining-room or hall, which they occupied at this time of the year in preference to the parlour, because of its large hearth, constructed for turf-fires, a fuel the captain was partial to in the winter season.", "어스름이었고, 그녀는 식당 또는 홀의 난롯가에 앉아 있었으니, 한 해의 이 시절에는 응접실보다 이 방을 즐겨 쓰는 까닭은 큰 난로가 있기 때문이었고, 그것은 잔디 불을 때려고 만든 것으로, 선장이 겨울철에 즐기는 땔감이었다."], ["The only visible articles in the room were those on the window-sill, which showed their shapes against the low sky, the middle article being the old hourglass, and the other two a pair of ancient British urns which had been dug from a barrow near, and were used as flowerpots for two razor-leaved cactuses.", "방 안에 눈에 띄는 물건이라곤 창턱 위에 놓인 것들뿐이었으니, 낮게 깔린 하늘을 배경으로 제 모양을 드러내고 있었고, 가운데 것은 오래된 모래시계였으며, 다른 두 개는 근처 봉분에서 파낸 고대 브리튼의 항아리 한 쌍으로, 면도날 같은 잎의 선인장 두 그루를 심은 화분으로 쓰이고 있었다."], ["Somebody knocked at the door.", "누군가 문을 두드렸다."], ["The servant was out; so was her grandfather.", "하녀는 외출 중이었고, 할아버지도 그러했다."], ["The person, after waiting a minute, came in and tapped at the door of the room.", "그 사람은 잠깐 기다린 뒤 안으로 들어와 방문을 두드렸다."]], [["“Who’s there?”", "\"거기 누구세요?\""], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 말했다."]], [["“Please, Cap’n Vye, will you let us——”", "\"저, 바이 선장님, 저희가 좀—\""]], [["Eustacia arose and went to the door.", "유스테이샤가 일어나 문으로 갔다."], ["“I cannot allow you to come in so boldly.", "\"그렇게 막무가내로 들어오게 둘 수는 없어."], ["You should have waited.”", "기다렸어야지.\""]], [["“The cap’n said I might come in without any fuss,”", "\"선장님이 별 격식 차리지 말고 들어와도 된다고 하셨어요.\""], ["was answered in a lad’s pleasant voice.", "소년의 쾌활한 목소리로 답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“Oh, did he?”", "\"아, 그러셨어?\""], ["said Eustacia more gently.", "유스테이샤가 좀 더 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“What do you want, Charley?”", "\"무슨 일이니, 찰리?\""]], [["“Please will your grandfather lend us his fuelhouse to try over our parts in, tonight at seven o’clock?”", "\"저, 오늘 밤 일곱 시에 저희가 배역을 연습해보게 할아버님께서 땔감 창고를 좀 빌려주실 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“What, are you one of the Egdon mummers for this year?”", "\"뭐, 너 올해 에그던 가면극 패의 한 사람이냐?\""]], [["“Yes, miss.", "\"네, 아가씨."], ["The cap’n used to let the old mummers practise here.”", "선장님께서 예전 가면극 패도 여기서 연습하게 해주시곤 했어요.\""]], [["“I know it.", "\"알겠다."], ["Yes, you may use the fuelhouse if you like,”", "그래, 원하면 땔감 창고를 써도 좋아.\""], ["said Eustacia languidly.", "유스테이샤가 나른하게 말했다."]], [["The choice of Captain Vye’s fuelhouse as the scene of rehearsal was dictated by the fact that his dwelling was nearly in the centre of the heath.", "연습 장소로 바이 선장의 땔감 창고를 고른 것은 그의 거처가 황야의 거의 한가운데에 있다는 사실에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["The fuelhouse was as roomy as a barn, and was a most desirable place for such a purpose.", "땔감 창고는 헛간만큼이나 널찍하여 그런 목적에는 더없이 알맞은 곳이었다."], ["The lads who formed the company of players lived at different scattered points around, and by meeting in this spot the distances to be traversed by all the comers would be about equally proportioned.", "그 연희패를 이룬 소년들은 둘레의 여기저기 흩어진 곳에 살았으니, 이 자리에서 만나면 오는 이들 모두가 가로질러야 할 거리가 대체로 엇비슷하게 나뉘었다."]], [["For mummers and mumming Eustacia had the greatest contempt.", "가면극과 가면극 놀이에 대해 유스테이샤는 더없는 경멸을 품고 있었다."], ["The mummers themselves were not afflicted with any such feeling for their art, though at the same time they were not enthusiastic.", "가면극 배우들 자신은 자기네 예술에 대해 그런 감정에 시달리지는 않았으나, 그렇다고 열성적이지도 않았다."], ["A traditional pastime is to be distinguished from a mere revival in no more striking feature than in this, that while in the revival all is excitement and fervour, the survival is carried on with a stolidity and absence of stir which sets one wondering why a thing that is done so perfunctorily should be kept up at all.", "전통적 유희가 한낱 부활과 구별되는 가장 두드러진 특징이라면, 부활에서는 모든 것이 흥분과 열기인 데 반해, 존속하는 것은 무덤덤함과 동요의 부재 속에 이어진다는 점이니, 그토록 건성으로 행해지는 일을 대체 왜 계속 이어가는지 의아하게 만든다."], ["Like Balaam and other unwilling prophets, the agents seem moved by an inner compulsion to say and do their allotted parts whether they will or no.", "발람과 그 밖의 마지못한 예언자들처럼, 그 행위자들은 원하든 원치 않든 자기에게 맡겨진 배역을 말하고 행하도록 어떤 내적 강제에 떠밀리는 듯하다."], ["This unweeting manner of performance is the true ring by which, in this refurbishing age, a fossilized survival may be known from a spurious reproduction.", "이 무자각한 연희 방식이야말로, 이 개수改修의 시대에 화석이 된 존속물을 가짜 재현물과 구별해주는 참된 표지標識이다."]], [["The piece was the well-known play of Saint George, and all who were behind the scenes assisted in the preparations, including the women of each household.", "그 작품은 널리 알려진 「성聖 게오르기우스」 극이었고, 무대 뒤에 있는 이들은 누구나 준비를 거들었으니, 각 집안의 여자들도 그러했다."], ["Without the co-operation of sisters and sweethearts the dresses were likely to be a failure; but on the other hand, this class of assistance was not without its drawbacks.", "누이들과 연인들의 협력 없이는 의상이 실패하기 십상이었으나, 다른 한편 이런 부류의 도움에도 결점이 없지는 않았다."], ["The girls could never be brought to respect tradition in designing and decorating the armour; they insisted on attaching loops and bows of silk and velvet in any situation pleasing to their taste.", "처녀들은 갑옷을 도안하고 꾸미는 데 도무지 전통을 존중하도록 만들 수가 없었으니, 그들은 제 취향에 드는 곳이면 어디든 비단과 벨벳 고리며 매듭을 다는 것을 고집했다."], ["Gorget, gusset, basinet, cuirass, gauntlet, sleeve, all alike in the view of these feminine eyes were practicable spaces whereon to sew scraps of fluttering colour.", "목가리개, 겨드랑이 받이, 투구, 흉갑, 손등 보호대, 소매—이 여인네들의 눈에는 죄다 한가지로, 펄럭이는 색색 조각을 꿰매 붙일 만한 쓸 만한 자리였다."]], [["It might be that Joe, who fought on the side of Christendom, had a sweetheart, and that Jim, who fought on the side of the Moslem, had one likewise.", "기독교 진영에서 싸우는 조에게 연인이 있고, 이슬람 진영에서 싸우는 짐에게도 마찬가지로 연인이 있을 수 있었다."], ["During the making of the costumes it would come to the knowledge of Joe’s sweetheart that Jim’s was putting brilliant silk scallops at the bottom of her lover’s surcoat, in addition to the ribbons of the visor, the bars of which, being invariably formed of coloured strips about half an inch wide hanging before the face, were mostly of that material.", "의상을 만드는 동안 조의 연인은 짐의 연인이 면갑의 리본—얼굴 앞에 늘어뜨리는, 반 인치쯤 너비의 색색 띠로 으레 만들어지는 그 막대들도 대개 그 비단으로 되어 있었으니—에 더하여, 제 연인의 겉옷 자락에 화려한 비단 가리비 무늬를 달고 있다는 것을 알게 되곤 했다."], ["Joe’s sweetheart straight-way placed brilliant silk on the scallops of the hem in question, and, going a little further, added ribbon tufts to the shoulder pieces.", "그러면 조의 연인은 곧장 문제의 그 가장자리 가리비 무늬에 화려한 비단을 붙이고, 한술 더 떠 어깨받이에 리본 술까지 달았다."], ["Jim’s, not to be outdone, would affix bows and rosettes everywhere.", "그러면 짐의 연인도 지지 않으려고 사방에 매듭과 장미꽃 매듭을 달아댔다."]], [["The result was that in the end the Valiant Soldier, of the Christian army, was distinguished by no peculiarity of accoutrement from the Turkish Knight; and what was worse, on a casual view Saint George himself might be mistaken for his deadly enemy, the Saracen.", "그 결과 끝내 기독교 군대의 용감한 병사는 차림새의 어떤 특징으로도 터키 기사와 구별되지 않았고, 더 고약하게는, 무심히 보면 성 게오르기우스 자신마저도 그의 철천지원수인 사라센으로 오인될 지경이었다."], ["The guisers themselves, though inwardly regretting this confusion of persons, could not afford to offend those by whose assistance they so largely profited, and the innovations were allowed to stand.", "가면극 배우들 자신은 이런 인물의 혼동을 속으로는 안타까워하면서도, 자기네가 그토록 크게 덕을 보는 그 도움 주는 이들의 비위를 거스를 형편이 못 되었으니, 그 변형들은 그대로 두어졌다."]], [["There was, it is true, a limit to this tendency to uniformity.", "물론 이런 획일화 경향에도 한계는 있었다."], ["The Leech or Doctor preserved his character intact—his darker habiliments, peculiar hat, and the bottle of physic slung under his arm, could never be mistaken.", "의원醫員 곧 의사는 그 인물됨을 고스란히 지켰으니—그의 더 짙은 의복, 독특한 모자, 겨드랑이에 둘러맨 약병은 결코 오인될 수 없었다."], ["And the same might be said of the conventional figure of Father Christmas, with his gigantic club, an older man, who accompanied the band as general protector in long night journeys from parish to parish, and was bearer of the purse.", "또 관습적인 인물인 산타클로스도 마찬가지였으니, 거대한 곤봉을 든 더 나이 든 사내로, 교구에서 교구로 이어지는 긴 밤길에 전체의 보호자 노릇을 하며 그 패를 따라다녔고, 돈주머니를 지니는 이였다."]], [["Seven o’clock, the hour of the rehearsal, came round, and in a short time Eustacia could hear voices in the fuelhouse.", "연습 시각인 일곱 시가 돌아왔고, 얼마 안 가 유스테이샤는 땔감 창고에서 목소리들을 들을 수 있었다."], ["To dissipate in some trifling measure her abiding sense of the murkiness of human life she went to the “linhay”", "인간 삶의 음울함에 대한 변치 않는 자신의 감각을 조금이나마 흩어버리려고, 그녀는 \"린헤이\""], ["or lean-to shed, which formed the root-store of their dwelling and abutted on the fuelhouse.", "곧 비스듬히 기댄 헛간으로 갔으니, 그것은 그들 거처의 뿌리채소 저장고를 이루며 땔감 창고에 붙어 있었다."], ["Here was a small rough hole in the mud wall, originally made for pigeons, through which the interior of the next shed could be viewed.", "여기에는 흙벽에 난 작고 거친 구멍이 하나 있었으니, 본디 비둘기를 위해 낸 것이라, 그것을 통해 옆 헛간의 안이 들여다보였다."], ["A light came from it now; and Eustacia stepped upon a stool to look in upon the scene.", "지금 거기서 불빛이 새어 나오고 있었고, 유스테이샤는 그 장면을 들여다보려고 걸상 위에 올라섰다."]], [["On a ledge in the fuelhouse stood three tall rushlights and by the light of them seven or eight lads were marching about, haranguing, and confusing each other, in endeavours to perfect themselves in the play.", "땔감 창고 안 선반 위에는 키 큰 골풀 양초 세 개가 서 있었고, 그 불빛으로 일고여덟 명의 소년들이 극을 완벽하게 익히려 애쓰며 이리저리 행진하고, 큰소리로 떠들고, 서로를 헷갈리게 하고 있었다."], ["Humphrey and Sam, the furze- and turf-cutters, were there looking on, so also was Timothy Fairway, who leant against the wall and prompted the boys from memory, interspersing among the set words remarks and anecdotes of the superior days when he and others were the Egdon mummers-elect that these lads were now.", "가시금작화와 잔디를 베는 험프리와 샘이 거기서 구경하고 있었고, 티머시 페어웨이도 그러했으니, 그는 벽에 기대어 기억을 더듬어 소년들에게 대사를 일러주며, 정해진 대사들 사이에, 자기와 다른 이들이 지금 이 소년들이 그러하듯 에그던 가면극 배우로 뽑혀 있던 더 나았던 시절에 관한 논평과 일화를 끼워 넣었다."]], [["“Well, ye be as well up to it as ever ye will be,”", "\"음, 너희는 앞으로 더 잘할 것도 없게 다 익혔구나.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Not that such mumming would have passed in our time.", "\"하기야 그런 가면극이 우리 시절엔 통하지도 않았겠지만 말이다."], ["Harry as the Saracen should strut a bit more, and John needn’t holler his inside out.", "사라센 역의 해리는 좀 더 으스대며 걸어야 하고, 존은 속이 다 빠지도록 고함칠 것까지는 없어."], ["Beyond that perhaps you’ll do.", "그 정도만 빼면 그럭저럭 괜찮겠다."], ["Have you got all your clothes ready?”", "옷은 다 준비됐냐?\""]], [["“We shall by Monday.”", "\"월요일까진 될 거예요.\""]], [["“Your first outing will be Monday night, I suppose?”", "\"첫 나들이가 월요일 밤이겠지, 그렇지?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["At Mrs. Yeobright’s.”", "예오브라이트 부인 댁에서요.\""]], [["“Oh, Mrs. Yeobright’s.", "\"오, 예오브라이트 부인 댁이라."], ["What makes her want to see ye?", "그 사람이 무엇 때문에 너희를 보고 싶어 한다더냐?"], ["I should think a middle-aged woman was tired of mumming.”", "중년 여자라면 가면극에 물렸을 법한데.\""]], [["“She’s got up a bit of a party, because ’tis the first Christmas that her son Clym has been home for a long time.”", "\"좀 모임을 마련하셨대요, 아드님 클림이 오랜만에 처음 집에 와서 쇠는 크리스마스라서요.\""]], [["“To be sure, to be sure—her party!", "\"그렇고말고, 그렇고말고—그 사람 모임!"], ["I am going myself.", "나도 갈 거다."], ["I almost forgot it, upon my life.”", "정말이지 깜빡 잊을 뻔했구나.\""]], [["Eustacia’s face flagged.", "유스테이샤의 얼굴이 시들해졌다."], ["There was to be a party at the Yeobrights’; she, naturally, had nothing to do with it.", "예오브라이트네에서 모임이 있을 예정이었으니, 그녀는 당연히 그것과 아무 상관이 없었다."], ["She was a stranger to all such local gatherings, and had always held them as scarcely appertaining to her sphere.", "그녀는 그런 온갖 지역 모임에 낯선 이였고, 늘 그런 것들을 자기 영역에 거의 속하지 않는 것으로 여겨왔다."], ["But had she been going, what an opportunity would have been afforded her of seeing the man whose influence was penetrating her like summer sun!", "그러나 만약 그녀가 가게 된다면, 그 영향이 여름 햇살처럼 그녀에게 스며들고 있는 그 사내를 볼 기회가 얼마나 주어질 것인가!"], ["To increase that influence was coveted excitement; to cast it off might be to regain serenity; to leave it as it stood was tantalizing.", "그 영향을 키우는 것은 탐나는 흥분이었고, 그것을 떨쳐내는 것은 평온을 되찾는 길일지도 모르며, 그것을 있는 그대로 두는 것은 애간장을 태우는 일이었다."]], [["The lads and men prepared to leave the premises, and Eustacia returned to her fireside.", "소년들과 사내들이 그곳을 떠날 채비를 했고, 유스테이샤는 자기 난롯가로 돌아갔다."], ["She was immersed in thought, but not for long.", "그녀는 생각에 잠겨 있었으나 오래가지는 않았다."], ["In a few minutes the lad Charley, who had come to ask permission to use the place, returned with the key to the kitchen.", "몇 분 뒤, 그곳을 쓰게 해달라고 청하러 왔던 소년 찰리가 부엌 열쇠를 가지고 돌아왔다."], ["Eustacia heard him, and opening the door into the passage said, “Charley, come here.”", "유스테이샤는 그 소리를 듣고, 복도로 난 문을 열며 말했다. \"찰리, 이리 오너라.\""]], [["The lad was surprised.", "소년은 놀랐다."], ["He entered the front room not without blushing; for he, like many, had felt the power of this girl’s face and form.", "그는 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수 없이 앞방으로 들어섰으니, 그도 많은 이들처럼 이 처녀의 얼굴과 자태가 지닌 힘을 느껴왔던 것이다."]], [["She pointed to a seat by the fire, and entered the other side of the chimney-corner herself.", "그녀는 난롯가의 한 자리를 가리켰고, 자신은 굴뚝 모퉁이의 다른 편으로 들어가 앉았다."], ["It could be seen in her face that whatever motive she might have had in asking the youth indoors would soon appear.", "소년을 안으로 부른 데에 무슨 동기가 있었든 그것이 곧 드러나리라는 것을 그녀의 얼굴에서 알 수 있었다."]], [["“Which part do you play, Charley—the Turkish Knight, do you not?”", "\"넌 어느 역을 맡았니, 찰리—터키 기사 아니냐?\""], ["inquired the beauty, looking across the smoke of the fire to him on the other side.", "미녀가 난로의 연기 너머 맞은편의 그에게 물었다."]], [["“Yes, miss, the Turkish Knight,”", "\"네, 아가씨, 터키 기사예요.\""], ["he replied diffidently.", "그가 머뭇거리며 대답했다."]], [["“Is yours a long part?”", "\"네 배역은 기냐?\""]], [["“Nine speeches, about.”", "\"대사가 아홉 개쯤 돼요.\""]], [["“Can you repeat them to me?", "\"나한테 외워줄 수 있겠니?"], ["If so I should like to hear them.”", "그렇다면 들어보고 싶구나.\""]], [["The lad smiled into the glowing turf and began—", "소년은 시뻘겋게 단 잔디 불을 보고 미소 지으며 시작했다—"]], [["“Here come I, a Turkish Knight, Who learnt in Turkish land to fight,”", "\"여기 내가 오노라, 터키 기사,\n터키 땅에서 싸움을 배운 몸이로다,\""]], [["continuing the discourse throughout the scenes to the concluding catastrophe of his fall by the hand of Saint George.", "그렇게 모든 장면을 거쳐 성 게오르기우스의 손에 쓰러지는 그의 마지막 파국에 이르기까지 그 대사를 죽 이어갔다."]], [["Eustacia had occasionally heard the part recited before.", "유스테이샤는 전에도 이따금 그 배역을 읊는 것을 들은 적이 있었다."], ["When the lad ended she began, precisely in the same words, and ranted on without hitch or divergence till she too reached the end.", "소년이 끝내자 그녀는 토씨 하나 다르지 않은 똑같은 말로 시작하여, 멈춤도 어긋남도 없이 줄줄 읊어, 그녀 역시 끝에 다다랐다."], ["It was the same thing, yet how different.", "같은 것이었으나, 어찌 그리 달랐던가."], ["Like in form, it had the added softness and finish of a Raffaelle after Perugino, which, while faithfully reproducing the original subject, entirely distances the original art.", "형식은 같았으되, 그것은 페루지노를 본뜬 라파엘로처럼 더해진 부드러움과 완성도를 지녔으니, 원래의 주제를 충실히 재현하면서도 원작의 기예를 온전히 멀찍이 따돌리는 것이었다."]], [["Charley’s eyes rounded with surprise.", "찰리의 두 눈이 놀라움에 동그래졌다."], ["“Well, you be a clever lady!”", "\"와, 영리한 아가씨시네요!\""], ["he said, in admiration.", "그가 감탄하며 말했다."], ["“I’ve been three weeks learning mine.”", "\"전 제 것 외우는 데 세 주가 걸렸는걸요.\""]], [["“I have heard it before,”", "\"전에 들은 적이 있단다.\""], ["she quietly observed.", "그녀가 조용히 말했다."], ["“Now, would you do anything to please me, Charley?”", "\"자, 찰리, 날 기쁘게 해주려고 무슨 일이든 해주겠니?\""]], [["“I’d do a good deal, miss.”", "\"꽤 많은 걸 해드릴 수 있어요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“Would you let me play your part for one night?”", "\"하룻밤 내가 네 역을 맡게 해주겠니?\""]], [["“Oh, miss!", "\"오, 아가씨!"], ["But your woman’s gown—you couldn’t.”", "하지만 아가씨의 여자 옷으로는—그럴 수 없어요.\""]], [["“I can get boy’s clothes—at least all that would be wanted besides the mumming dress.", "\"난 사내아이 옷을 구할 수 있어—적어도 가면극 의상 말고 더 필요한 건 다 말이지."], ["What should I have to give you to lend me your things, to let me take your place for an hour or two on Monday night, and on no account to say a word about who or what I am?", "네 물건을 빌려주고, 월요일 밤 한두 시간 내가 네 자리를 대신하게 해주고, 내가 누구이며 무엇인지 절대 한마디도 발설하지 않게 하려면, 내가 너한테 뭘 주면 되겠니?"], ["You would, of course, have to excuse yourself from playing that night, and to say that somebody—a cousin of Miss Vye’s—would act for you.", "너야 물론 그날 밤 연희에서 빠지겠다고 둘러대고, 누군가—바이 양의 사촌이—너 대신 해줄 거라고 말해야 하겠지."], ["The other mummers have never spoken to me in their lives so that it would be safe enough; and if it were not, I should not mind.", "다른 가면극 배우들은 평생 나와 말을 섞은 적이 없으니 충분히 안전할 게다. 그리고 설령 안전하지 않다 해도 난 개의치 않아."], ["Now, what must I give you to agree to this?", "자, 네가 이걸 받아들이게 하려면 내가 뭘 줘야 하지?"], ["Half a crown?”", "반 크라운?\""]], [["The youth shook his head", "소년은 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Five shillings?”", "\"오 실링?\""]], [["He shook his head again.", "그는 다시 고개를 저었다."], ["“Money won’t do it,”", "\"돈으론 안 돼요.\""], ["he said, brushing the iron head of the firedog with the hollow of his hand.", "그가 장작 받침쇠의 쇠머리를 손바닥 오목한 곳으로 쓸며 말했다."]], [["“What will, then, Charley?”", "\"그럼 뭐가 되겠니, 찰리?\""], ["said Eustacia in a disappointed tone.", "유스테이샤가 실망한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“You know what you forbade me at the Maypoling, miss,”", "\"오월제 기둥 춤 때 아가씨가 저한테 못 하게 하신 거 아시잖아요, 아가씨.\""], ["murmured the lad, without looking at her, and still stroking the firedog’s head.", "소년이 그녀를 보지 않은 채, 여전히 장작 받침쇠의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 우물거렸다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Eustacia, with a little more hauteur.", "유스테이샤가 조금 더 도도하게 말했다."], ["“You wanted to join hands with me in the ring, if I recollect?”", "\"내 기억이 맞다면, 넌 원 안에서 나랑 손을 맞잡고 싶어 했지?\""]], [["“Half an hour of that, and I’ll agree, miss.”", "\"그걸 반 시간만요, 그럼 받아들일게요, 아가씨.\""]], [["Eustacia regarded the youth steadfastly.", "유스테이샤는 소년을 물끄러미 바라보았다."], ["He was three years younger than herself, but apparently not backward for his age.", "그는 그녀보다 세 살 어렸으나, 보아하니 나이에 비해 숙된 데가 없지는 않았다."], ["“Half an hour of what?”", "\"반 시간 동안 뭘?\""], ["she said, though she guessed what.", "그녀가 말했으니, 무엇인지 짐작은 하면서였다."]], [["“Holding your hand in mine.”", "\"아가씨 손을 제 손에 쥐는 걸요.\""]], [["She was silent.", "그녀는 잠자코 있었다."], ["“Make it a quarter of an hour,”", "\"십오 분으로 하자.\""], ["she said", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Miss Eustacia—I will, if I may kiss it too.", "\"네, 유스테이샤 아가씨—그럴게요, 거기다 입 맞춰도 된다면요."], ["A quarter of an hour.", "십오 분."], ["And I’ll swear to do the best I can to let you take my place without anybody knowing.", "그리고 아무도 모르게 아가씨가 제 자리를 대신하게 하도록 제가 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다하겠다고 맹세할게요."], ["Don’t you think somebody might know your tongue, miss?”", "누가 아가씨 말투를 알아챌 수도 있지 않을까요, 아가씨?\""]], [["“It is possible.", "\"그럴 수 있지."], ["But I will put a pebble in my mouth to make is less likely.", "하지만 그럴 가능성을 줄이려고 입에 조약돌을 물고 있을 거야."], ["Very well; you shall be allowed to have my hand as soon as you bring the dress and your sword and staff.", "좋아. 네가 옷과 칼과 지팡이를 가져오는 대로 내 손을 잡게 해주마."], ["I don’t want you any longer now.”", "이제 더는 네가 필요 없구나.\""]], [["Charley departed, and Eustacia felt more and more interest in life.", "찰리는 떠났고, 유스테이샤는 삶에 점점 더 흥미를 느꼈다."], ["Here was something to do: here was some one to see, and a charmingly adventurous way to see him.", "여기 할 일이 생겼고, 여기 만날 사람이 생겼으며, 그를 만날 매혹적으로 모험적인 방도가 생긴 것이다."], ["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["she said to herself, “want of an object to live for—that’s all is the matter with me!”", "그녀는 속으로 말했다. \"살아갈 목적이 없는 것—그게 내 문제의 전부야!\""]], [["Eustacia’s manner was as a rule of a slumberous sort, her passions being of the massive rather than the vivacious kind.", "유스테이샤의 태도는 대개 졸음에 잠긴 듯한 것이었으니, 그녀의 정념은 활발하다기보다 묵직한 부류였다."], ["But when aroused she would make a dash which, just for the time, was not unlike the move of a naturally lively person.", "그러나 일단 일깨워지면 그녀는 그 순간만큼은 천성이 활달한 사람의 움직임과 다르지 않은 돌진을 감행하곤 했다."]], [["On the question of recognition she was somewhat indifferent.", "정체를 들킬 문제에 대해서는 그녀는 다소 무관심했다."], ["By the acting lads themselves she was not likely to be known.", "연희하는 소년들 자신에게는 알아보일 공산이 없었다."], ["With the guests who might be assembled she was hardly so secure.", "모일 법한 손님들에 대해서는 그만큼 안전하지는 못했다."], ["Yet detection, after all, would be no such dreadful thing.", "그러나 따지고 보면 들킨다 해도 그리 끔찍한 일은 아니었다."], ["The fact only could be detected, her true motive never.", "사실만 들킬 뿐, 그녀의 진짜 동기는 결코 들키지 않을 터였다."], ["It would be instantly set down as the passing freak of a girl whose ways were already considered singular.", "그것은 즉각, 이미 그 방식이 별나다고 여겨지는 한 처녀의 한때의 변덕으로 치부될 것이었다."], ["That she was doing for an earnest reason what would most naturally be done in jest was at any rate a safe secret.", "더없이 진지한 이유로 하는 일을 가장 자연스럽게는 장난삼아 할 법한 그 일을 한다는 것은, 어쨌든 안전한 비밀이었다."]], [["The next evening Eustacia stood punctually at the fuelhouse door, waiting for the dusk which was to bring Charley with the trappings.", "이튿날 저녁 유스테이샤는 약속대로 땔감 창고 문 앞에 서서, 찰리가 의상을 가지고 올 어스름을 기다렸다."], ["Her grandfather was at home tonight, and she would be unable to ask her confederate indoors.", "할아버지가 오늘 밤은 집에 있었으니, 그녀는 공모자를 안으로 청할 수가 없었다."]], [["He appeared on the dark ridge of heathland, like a fly on a Negro, bearing the articles with him, and came up breathless with his walk.", "그는 흑인 위의 파리처럼 어둑한 황야의 능선에 나타나, 그 물건들을 지니고 와서는, 걸어오느라 숨이 차서 다가왔다."]], [["“Here are the things,”", "\"여기 물건들이요.\""], ["he whispered, placing them upon the threshold.", "그가 그것들을 문지방에 내려놓으며 속삭였다."], ["“And now, Miss Eustacia—”", "\"자, 이제, 유스테이샤 아가씨—\""]], [["“The payment.", "\"값을 치러야지."], ["It is quite ready.", "다 준비됐다."], ["I am as good as my word.”", "난 약속은 꼭 지키는 사람이야.\""]], [["She leant against the door-post, and gave him her hand.", "그녀는 문설주에 기대어 그에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["Charley took it in both his own with a tenderness beyond description, unless it was like that of a child holding a captured sparrow.", "찰리는 형언할 수 없는 다정함으로 두 손에 그것을 받아 쥐었으니, 사로잡은 참새를 쥔 아이의 다정함이라고나 할까."]], [["“Why, there’s a glove on it!”", "\"아니, 장갑을 끼셨네요!\""], ["he said in a deprecating way.", "그가 못마땅한 투로 말했다."]], [["“I have been walking,”", "\"걸어 다녔거든.\""], ["she observed.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“But, miss!”", "\"하지만, 아가씨!\""]], [["“Well—it is hardly fair.”", "\"음—공평치 못하긴 하지.\""], ["She pulled off the glove, and gave him her bare hand.", "그녀는 장갑을 벗어 그에게 맨손을 내밀었다."]], [["They stood together minute after minute, without further speech, each looking at the blackening scene, and each thinking his and her own thoughts.", "그들은 더 이상의 말없이 분이 지나고 또 지나도록 함께 서서, 저마다 검어져가는 풍경을 바라보며, 저마다 제 생각에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["“I think I won’t use it all up tonight,”", "\"오늘 밤 다 써버리지는 않을래요.\""], ["said Charley devotedly, when six or eight minutes had been passed by him caressing her hand.", "찰리가 그녀의 손을 어루만지며 육칠 분을 보낸 뒤 정성스럽게 말했다."], ["“May I have the other few minutes another time?”", "\"남은 몇 분은 다음에 가져도 될까요?\""]], [["“As you like,”", "\"좋을 대로.\""], ["said she without the least emotion.", "그녀가 조금도 감정 없이 말했다."], ["“But it must be over in a week.", "\"하지만 한 주 안에 끝내야 해."], ["Now, there is only one thing I want you to do—to wait while I put on the dress, and then to see if I do my part properly.", "자, 이제 네게 바라는 건 한 가지뿐이야—내가 옷을 입는 동안 기다렸다가, 내가 배역을 제대로 하는지 봐주는 거야."], ["But let me look first indoors.”", "하지만 먼저 안을 좀 봐야겠다.\""]], [["She vanished for a minute or two, and went in.", "그녀는 잠깐 사라졌다가 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Her grandfather was safely asleep in his chair.", "할아버지는 의자에서 무사히 잠들어 있었다. \"자, 그럼.\""], ["“Now, then,”", "그녀가 돌아와서 말했다."], ["she said, on returning, “walk down the garden a little way, and when I am ready I’ll call you.”", "\"정원을 따라 조금 내려가 있어. 준비되면 부를게.\""]], [["Charley walked and waited, and presently heard a soft whistle.", "찰리는 걸어가 기다렸고, 이윽고 나직한 휘파람 소리를 들었다."], ["He returned to the fuelhouse door.", "그는 땔감 창고 문으로 돌아왔다."]], [["“Did you whistle, Miss Vye?”", "\"휘파람 부셨어요, 바이 양?\""]], [["“Yes; come in,”", "\"그래, 들어와.\""], ["reached him in Eustacia’s voice from a back quarter.", "유스테이샤의 목소리가 안쪽 어딘가에서 그에게 닿았다."], ["“I must not strike a light till the door is shut, or it may be seen shining.", "\"문을 닫기 전엔 불을 켜선 안 돼, 안 그러면 새어 나가는 게 보일지도 몰라."], ["Push your hat into the hole through to the wash-house, if you can feel your way across.”", "더듬어서 가로질러 갈 수 있겠거든, 모자를 세탁장으로 통하는 구멍에 밀어 넣어둬.\""]], [["Charley did as commanded, and she struck the light revealing herself to be changed in sex, brilliant in colours, and armed from top to toe.", "찰리는 시키는 대로 했고, 그녀가 불을 켜자 그 모습이 드러났으니, 성별이 바뀌고, 색채로 눈부시고, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 무장한 모습이었다."], ["Perhaps she quailed a little under Charley’s vigorous gaze, but whether any shyness at her male attire appeared upon her countenance could not be seen by reason of the strips of ribbon which used to cover the face in mumming costumes, representing the barred visor of the mediæval helmet.", "어쩌면 그녀는 찰리의 힘찬 눈길 아래 조금 움츠러들었을지도 모르나, 남장에 대한 부끄러움이 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐는지 어떤지는, 가면극 의상에서 으레 얼굴을 가리던, 중세 투구의 막대 면갑을 본뜬 리본 띠들 때문에 알아볼 수 없었다."]], [["“It fits pretty well,”", "\"꽤 잘 맞네.\""], ["she said, looking down at the white overalls, “except that the tunic, or whatever you call it, is long in the sleeve.", "그녀가 흰 작업바지를 내려다보며 말했다. \"튜닉인지 뭔지 하는 이것 소매가 긴 것만 빼면 말이야."], ["The bottom of the overalls I can turn up inside.", "작업바지 아랫단은 안으로 접어 올리면 되고."], ["Now pay attention.”", "자, 잘 봐.\""]], [["Eustacia then proceeded in her delivery, striking the sword against the staff or lance at the minatory phrases, in the orthodox mumming manner, and strutting up and down.", "이어 유스테이샤는 대사를 읊어나갔으니, 위협하는 구절에서는 정통 가면극 방식대로 칼을 지팡이나 창에 부딪치고, 이리저리 으스대며 걸었다."], ["Charley seasoned his admiration with criticism of the gentlest kind, for the touch of Eustacia’s hand yet remained with him.", "찰리는 가장 부드러운 종류의 비평으로 자신의 감탄에 양념을 더했으니, 유스테이샤의 손길이 아직 그에게 남아 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“And now for your excuse to the others,”", "\"자, 이제 다른 애들한테 둘러댈 핑계 말인데.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Where do you meet before you go to Mrs. Yeobright’s?”", "\"예오브라이트 부인 댁에 가기 전에 너희는 어디서 모이니?\""]], [["“We thought of meeting here, miss, if you have nothing to say against it.", "\"여기서 모일까 했어요, 아가씨, 아가씨가 반대하지 않으신다면요."], ["At eight o’clock, so as to get there by nine.”", "아홉 시까지 거기 가려고 여덟 시에요.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Well, you of course must not appear.", "음, 넌 물론 나타나선 안 돼."], ["I will march in about five minutes late, ready-dressed, and tell them that you can’t come.", "내가 채비를 다 갖추고 오 분쯤 늦게 들어가서, 네가 못 온다고 말할게."], ["I have decided that the best plan will be for you to be sent somewhere by me, to make a real thing of the excuse.", "핑계를 진짜처럼 보이게 하려면, 내가 너를 어딘가로 심부름 보내는 게 가장 좋은 수일 것 같다고 정했어."], ["Our two heath-croppers are in the habit of straying into the meads, and tomorrow evening you can go and see if they are gone there.", "우리 황야 조랑말 두 마리가 풀밭으로 곧잘 흘러 들어가니, 내일 저녁 네가 가서 그놈들이 거기로 갔는지 보면 돼."], ["I’ll manage the rest.", "나머지는 내가 처리하마."], ["Now you may leave me.”", "자, 이제 가도 좋아.\""]], [["“Yes, miss.", "\"네, 아가씨."], ["But I think I’ll have one minute more of what I am owed, if you don’t mind.”", "하지만 괜찮으시다면 받기로 한 걸 일 분만 더 받을까 해요.\""]], [["Eustacia gave him her hand as before.", "유스테이샤는 아까처럼 그에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“One minute,”", "\"일 분이야.\""], ["she said, and counted on till she reached seven or eight minutes.", "그녀가 말하고는, 칠팔 분에 이를 때까지 세어 나갔다."], ["Hand and person she then withdrew to a distance of several feet, and recovered some of her old dignity.", "그러고는 손과 몸을 몇 피트 떨어진 거리로 물리고, 예전의 위엄을 어느 정도 되찾았다."], ["The contract completed, she raised between them a barrier impenetrable as a wall.", "계약이 완료되자, 그녀는 둘 사이에 벽처럼 뚫고 들 수 없는 장벽을 세웠다."]], [["“There, ’tis all gone; and I didn’t mean quite all,”", "\"자, 다 가버렸네요. 다는 가질 생각이 아니었는데.\""], ["he said, with a sigh.", "그가 한숨지으며 말했다."]], [["“You had good measure,”", "\"넉넉히 받았잖니.\""], ["said she, turning away.", "그녀가 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, miss.", "\"네, 아가씨."], ["Well, ’tis over, and now I’ll get home-along.”", "그럼, 다 끝났으니, 이제 집으로 가야겠어요.\""]], [["V. Through the Moonlight\nThe next evening the mummers were assembled in the same spot, awaiting the entrance of the Turkish Knight.", "이튿날 저녁 가면극 배우들은 같은 자리에 모여, 터키 기사의 등장을 기다리고 있었다."]], [["“Twenty minutes after eight by the Quiet Woman, and Charley not come.”", "\"콰이어트 우먼 시계로 여덟 시 이십 분인데, 찰리가 안 왔네.\""]], [["“Ten minutes past by Blooms-End.”", "\"블룸스엔드 기준으론 여덟 시 십 분이야.\""]], [["“It wants ten minutes to, by Grandfer Cantle’s watch.”", "\"그랜퍼 캔틀 시계로는 십 분 전이고.\""]], [["“And ’tis five minutes past by the captain’s clock.”", "\"그리고 선장님 시계로는 오 분 지났어.\""]], [["On Egdon there was no absolute hour of the day.", "에그던에는 하루의 절대적인 시각이라는 것이 없었다."], ["The time at any moment was a number of varying doctrines professed by the different hamlets, some of them having originally grown up from a common root, and then become divided by secession, some having been alien from the beginning.", "어느 순간의 시간이란 여러 마을이 저마다 내세우는 다양한 교리의 집합이었으니, 그중 어떤 것은 본래 한 뿌리에서 자라났다가 분파로 갈라진 것이었고, 어떤 것은 처음부터 이질적인 것이었다."], ["West Egdon believed in Blooms-End time, East Egdon in the time of the Quiet Woman Inn.", "서쪽 에그던은 블룸스엔드 시간을 믿었고, 동쪽 에그던은 콰이어트 우먼 여인숙의 시간을 믿었다."], ["Grandfer Cantle’s watch had numbered many followers in years gone by, but since he had grown older faiths were shaken.", "그랜퍼 캔틀의 시계는 지난 세월 추종자를 많이 거느렸으나, 그가 늙어가면서 그 신앙도 흔들렸다."], ["Thus, the mummers having gathered hither from scattered points each came with his own tenets on early and late; and they waited a little longer as a compromise.", "그리하여 흩어진 곳에서 이리로 모인 가면극 배우들은 저마다 이르고 늦음에 관한 제 나름의 교의를 지니고 왔으니, 그들은 타협 삼아 좀 더 기다렸다."]], [["Eustacia had watched the assemblage through the hole; and seeing that now was the proper moment to enter, she went from the “linhay” and boldly pulled the bobbin of the fuelhouse door.", "유스테이샤는 구멍을 통해 그 모임을 지켜보고 있었고, 이제 들어갈 적기適期임을 알아채고는, \"린헤이\"에서 나와 대담하게 땔감 창고 문의 빗장 끈을 잡아당겼다."], ["Her grandfather was safe at the Quiet Woman.", "할아버지는 콰이어트 우먼에 안전하게 가 있었다."]], [["“Here’s Charley at last!", "\"드디어 찰리 왔다!"], ["How late you be, Charley.”", "어찌 이리 늦었니, 찰리.\""]], [["“’Tis not Charley,”", "\"찰리가 아니에요.\""], ["said the Turkish Knight from within his visor.", "면갑 안에서 터키 기사가 말했다."], ["“’Tis a cousin of Miss Vye’s, come to take Charley’s place from curiosity.", "\"바이 양의 사촌인데, 호기심에 찰리 자리를 대신하러 왔어요."], ["He was obliged to go and look for the heath-croppers that have got into the meads, and I agreed to take his place, as he knew he couldn’t come back here again tonight.", "그 애는 풀밭으로 들어간 황야 조랑말들을 찾으러 가야 해서, 오늘 밤 다시 여기 못 온다는 걸 알기에 제가 그 애 자리를 대신하기로 했지요."], ["I know the part as well as he.”", "저도 그 애만큼 배역을 잘 알아요.\""]], [["Her graceful gait, elegant figure, and dignified manner in general won the mummers to the opinion that they had gained by the exchange, if the newcomer were perfect in his part.", "그녀의 우아한 걸음걸이, 단아한 자태, 그리고 전반적으로 위엄 있는 태도는, 새로 온 이가 제 배역에 완벽하기만 하다면 이 교체로 득을 보았다는 쪽으로 가면극 배우들의 견해를 이끌었다."]], [["“It don’t matter—if you be not too young,”", "\"상관없어—네가 너무 어리지만 않다면 말이야.\""], ["said Saint George.", "성 게오르기우스가 말했다."], ["Eustacia’s voice had sounded somewhat more juvenile and fluty than Charley’s.", "유스테이샤의 목소리가 찰리보다 다소 더 앳되고 피리 소리 같았던 것이다."]], [["“I know every word of it, I tell you,”", "\"한마디도 빠짐없이 다 안다니까.\""], ["said Eustacia decisively.", "유스테이샤가 단호하게 말했다."], ["Dash being all that was required to carry her triumphantly through, she adopted as much as was necessary.", "그녀를 의기양양하게 헤쳐 나가게 해줄 데 필요한 것이라곤 기세뿐이었으니, 그녀는 필요한 만큼 그것을 취했다."], ["“Go ahead, lads, with the try-over.", "\"어서들 한번 맞춰보자."], ["I’ll challenge any of you to find a mistake in me.”", "너희 중 누구든 내게서 틀린 데를 찾아내봐.\""]], [["The play was hastily rehearsed, whereupon the other mummers were delighted with the new knight.", "극은 서둘러 연습되었고, 그리하여 다른 가면극 배우들은 새 기사에게 만족했다."], ["They extinguished the candles at half-past eight, and set out upon the heath in the direction of Mrs. Yeobright’s house at Bloom’s-End.", "그들은 여덟 시 반에 양초들을 끄고, 블룸스엔드의 예오브라이트 부인 댁을 향해 황야로 나섰다."]], [["There was a slight hoarfrost that night, and the moon, though not more than half full, threw a spirited and enticing brightness upon the fantastic figures of the mumming band, whose plumes and ribbons rustled in their walk like autumn leaves.", "그날 밤은 무서리가 살짝 내렸고, 달은 반 넘게 차지는 않았으나, 가면극 패의 환상적인 형상들 위로 생기 있고 매혹적인 밝음을 던졌으니, 그들의 깃털과 리본은 걸을 때 가을 낙엽처럼 바스락거렸다."], ["Their path was not over Rainbarrow now, but down a valley which left that ancient elevation a little to the east.", "그들의 길은 이제 레인배로 너머가 아니라, 그 오래된 봉우리를 동쪽으로 조금 비켜둔 골짜기를 따라 내려가는 길이었다."], ["The bottom of the vale was green to a width of ten yards or thereabouts, and the shining facets of frost upon the blades of grass seemed to move on with the shadows of those they surrounded.", "골짜기 바닥은 십 야드쯤 너비로 푸르렀고, 풀잎 위에 맺힌 서리의 빛나는 면들은 자기들이 둘러싼 이들의 그림자와 함께 따라 움직이는 듯했다."], ["The masses of furze and heath to the right and left were dark as ever; a mere half-moon was powerless to silver such sable features as theirs.", "좌우의 가시금작화와 히스 덤불 더미는 여느 때처럼 어두웠으니, 한낱 반달은 그토록 새까만 생김새를 은빛으로 물들일 힘이 없었다."]], [["Half-an-hour of walking and talking brought them to the spot in the valley where the grass riband widened and led down to the front of the house.", "반 시간을 걷고 이야기한 끝에 그들은 골짜기에서 풀 띠가 넓어져 집 앞쪽으로 이어지는 지점에 다다랐다."], ["At sight of the place Eustacia who had felt a few passing doubts during her walk with the youths, again was glad that the adventure had been undertaken.", "그곳을 보자, 소년들과 걷는 동안 몇 차례 스쳐가는 의구심을 느꼈던 유스테이샤는, 이 모험에 나선 것을 다시금 기뻐했다."], ["She had come out to see a man who might possibly have the power to deliver her soul from a most deadly oppression.", "그녀는 어쩌면 가장 끔찍한 억압에서 자신의 영혼을 건져낼 힘을 지녔을지도 모르는 한 사내를 보러 나온 것이었다."], ["What was Wildeve?", "와일디브가 뭐란 말인가?"], ["Interesting, but inadequate.", "흥미롭긴 했으나, 모자랐다."], ["Perhaps she would see a sufficient hero tonight.", "어쩌면 오늘 밤 그녀는 흡족한 영웅을 보게 될지도 몰랐다."]], [["As they drew nearer to the front of the house the mummers became aware that music and dancing were briskly flourishing within.", "집 앞쪽으로 다가가면서 가면극 배우들은 안에서 음악과 춤이 활기차게 한창임을 알아차렸다."], ["Every now and then a long low note from the serpent, which was the chief wind instrument played at these times, advanced further into the heath than the thin treble part, and reached their ears alone; and next a more than usual loud tread from a dancer would come the same way.", "이따금 이런 때 연주되는 주된 관악기인 세르팡에서 나오는 길고 낮은 한 음이, 가느다란 고음부보다 더 멀리 황야 속으로 나아가 그들의 귀에만 닿았고, 그다음에는 여느 때보다 큰 춤꾼의 발 구르는 소리가 같은 식으로 다가왔다."], ["With nearer approach these fragmentary sounds became pieced together, and were found to be the salient points of the tune called “Nancy’s Fancy.”", "더 가까이 다가가자 이 조각난 소리들은 한데 이어 맞춰져, 「낸시의 변덕」이라는 곡의 두드러진 대목들임이 드러났다."]], [["He was there, of course.", "물론 그가 거기 있었다."], ["Who was she that he danced with?", "그가 함께 춤추는 여자는 누구일까?"], ["Perhaps some unknown woman, far beneath herself in culture, was by the most subtle of lures sealing his fate this very instant.", "어쩌면 어떤 알지 못할 여자, 교양에서 자기보다 훨씬 못한 여자가, 바로 이 순간 가장 교묘한 유혹으로 그의 운명을 봉인하고 있는지도 몰랐다."], ["To dance with a man is to concentrate a twelvemonth’s regulation fire upon him in the fragment of an hour.", "한 사내와 춤춘다는 것은 일 년치 절제된 불길을 한 시간의 조각에다 그에게 집중시키는 일이다."], ["To pass to courtship without acquaintance, to pass to marriage without courtship, is a skipping of terms reserved for those alone who tread this royal road.", "친분도 없이 구애로 건너뛰고, 구애도 없이 결혼으로 건너뛰는 것은, 오직 이 왕도王道를 밟는 자들에게만 허락된 단계의 생략이다."], ["She would see how his heart lay by keen observation of them all.", "그녀는 그들 모두를 예리하게 관찰하여 그의 마음이 어디에 놓여 있는지 알아낼 셈이었다."]], [["The enterprising lady followed the mumming company through the gate in the white paling, and stood before the open porch.", "이 진취적인 숙녀는 흰 말뚝 울타리의 문을 지나 가면극 패를 따라 들어가, 열린 현관 앞에 섰다."], ["The house was encrusted with heavy thatchings, which dropped between the upper windows; the front, upon which the moonbeams directly played, had originally been white; but a huge pyracanth now darkened the greater portion.", "집은 묵직한 이엉으로 뒤덮여 위쪽 창들 사이로 그것이 늘어져 있었고, 달빛이 곧장 어리는 정면은 본디 흰색이었으나, 이제는 거대한 피라칸타가 그 대부분을 어둑하게 가리고 있었다."]], [["It became at once evident that the dance was proceeding immediately within the surface of the door, no apartment intervening.", "춤이 문 표면 바로 안쪽에서, 사이에 끼인 방 하나 없이 벌어지고 있다는 것이 단박에 분명해졌다."], ["The brushing of skirts and elbows, sometimes the bumping of shoulders, could be heard against the very panels.", "치맛자락과 팔꿈치가 스치는 소리, 이따금 어깨가 부딪치는 소리가 바로 그 문짝에 와 닿는 것이 들렸다."], ["Eustacia, though living within two miles of the place, had never seen the interior of this quaint old habitation.", "유스테이샤는 그곳에서 이 마일 안에 살면서도 이 별스럽게 오래된 거처의 내부를 본 적이 없었다."], ["Between Captain Vye and the Yeobrights there had never existed much acquaintance, the former having come as a stranger and purchased the long-empty house at Mistover Knap not long before the death of Mrs. Yeobright’s husband; and with that event and the departure of her son such friendship as had grown up became quite broken off.", "바이 선장과 예오브라이트네 사이에는 그리 깊은 친분이 있었던 적이 없었으니, 전자는 낯선 이로 와서 예오브라이트 부인 남편이 죽기 얼마 전에 미스토버 냅의 오래 비어 있던 집을 사들였고, 그 일과 그녀 아들의 떠남으로, 싹터 있던 친분이라 할 만한 것마저 아주 끊겨버린 까닭이었다."]], [["“Is there no passage inside the door, then?”", "\"그럼 문 안쪽에 통로가 없는 거니?\""], ["asked Eustacia as they stood within the porch.", "현관 안에 서서 유스테이샤가 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"없어요.\""], ["said the lad who played the Saracen.", "사라센 역을 맡은 소년이 말했다."], ["“The door opens right upon the front sitting-room, where the spree’s going on.”", "\"문은 곧장 앞쪽 거실로 열려요, 흥겨운 자리가 벌어지고 있는 데로요.\""]], [["“So that we cannot open the door without stopping the dance.”", "\"그러니까 춤을 멈추게 하지 않고는 문을 열 수 없다는 거지.\""]], [["“That’s it.", "\"바로 그거예요."], ["Here we must bide till they have done, for they always bolt the back door after dark.”", "우린 저들이 끝낼 때까지 여기서 기다려야 해요, 어두워지면 늘 뒷문에 빗장을 지르거든요.\""]], [["“They won’t be much longer,”", "\"오래 안 걸릴 거다.\""], ["said Father Christmas.", "산타클로스가 말했다."]], [["This assertion, however, was hardly borne out by the event.", "그러나 이 단언은 실제 사태로 거의 뒷받침되지 못했다."], ["Again the instruments ended the tune; again they recommenced with as much fire and pathos as if it were the first strain.", "다시금 악기들이 곡을 끝냈고, 다시금 마치 첫 가락인 양 그만큼의 열기와 애상으로 다시 시작했다."], ["The air was now that one without any particular beginning, middle, or end, which perhaps, among all the dances which throng an inspired fiddler’s fancy, best conveys the idea of the interminable—the celebrated “Devil’s Dream.”", "이제 그 곡은 특별한 처음도, 중간도, 끝도 없는 것이었으니, 어쩌면 영감 받은 바이올린 연주자의 상상에 떼 지어 몰려드는 온갖 춤곡 가운데 끝없음의 관념을 가장 잘 전하는 그 유명한 「악마의 꿈」이었다."], ["The fury of personal movement that was kindled by the fury of the notes could be approximately imagined by these outsiders under the moon, from the occasional kicks of toes and heels against the door, whenever the whirl round had been of more than customary velocity.", "음표들의 광란으로 지펴진 인간 동작의 광란은, 빙글 도는 회전이 여느 때보다 빠를 때마다 발끝과 발꿈치가 문에 부딪치는 이따금의 소리로, 달빛 아래 이 바깥의 사람들에게 어림짐작될 수 있었다."]], [["The first five minutes of listening was interesting enough to the mummers.", "처음 오 분간의 엿듣기는 가면극 배우들에게 충분히 흥미로웠다."], ["The five minutes extended to ten minutes, and these to a quarter of an hour; but no signs of ceasing were audible in the lively “Dream.”", "그 오 분이 십 분으로 늘어났고, 그것이 다시 십오 분으로 늘어났으나, 그 흥겨운\n「꿈이지.」"], ["The bumping against the door, the laughter, the stamping, were all as vigorous as ever, and the pleasure in being outside lessened considerably.", "문에 부딪치는 소리, 웃음소리, 발 구르는 소리는 여전히 기운찼고, 바깥에 있는 즐거움은 상당히 줄어들었다."]], [["“Why does Mrs. Yeobright give parties of this sort?”", "「요브라이트 부인은 왜 이런 식의 잔치를 여는 거예요?」"], ["Eustacia asked, a little surprised to hear merriment so pronounced.", "유스테이샤가 그렇게 떠들썩한 흥겨움을 듣고 다소 놀라며 물었다."]], [["“It is not one of her bettermost parlour-parties.", "「부인의 제일가는 응접실 잔치는 아니야."], ["She’s asked the plain neighbours and workpeople without drawing any lines, just to give ’em a good supper and such like.", "선을 긋지 않고 수수한 이웃들과 일꾼들을 불러서 그저 든든한 저녁이나 그런 걸 대접하는 거지."], ["Her son and she wait upon the folks.”", "아들이랑 부인이 직접 사람들 시중을 들고.」"]], [["“I see,”", "「알겠어요.」"], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 말했다."]], [["“’Tis the last strain, I think,”", "「내 생각엔 이게 마지막 가락인 것 같아.」"], ["said Saint George, with his ear to the panel.", "성 게오르기우스가 문짝에 귀를 대고 말했다."], ["“A young man and woman have just swung into this corner, and he’s saying to her, ‘Ah, the pity; ’tis over for us this time, my own.’”", "「젊은 남녀 한 쌍이 막 이쪽 모퉁이로 빙글 돌아왔는데, 사내가 여자한테 이러는군, ‘아, 안타깝구나, 우리한텐 이번이 끝이야, 내 사랑.’」"]], [["“Thank God,”", "「하느님 감사합니다.」"], ["said the Turkish Knight, stamping, and taking from the wall the conventional lance that each of the mummers carried.", "터키 기사가 발을 구르며, 가면극 배우들이 저마다 들고 다니는 관습적인 창을 벽에서 집어 들며 말했다."], ["Her boots being thinner than those of the young men, the hoar had damped her feet and made them cold.", "그녀의 장화는 젊은 사내들 것보다 얇아서, 서리에 발이 젖어 차가워졌다."]], [["“Upon my song ’tis another ten minutes for us,”", "「맙소사, 우리한테는 십 분이 더 남았어.」"], ["said the Valiant Soldier, looking through the keyhole as the tune modulated into another without stopping.", "용맹한 병사가 가락이 멈추지 않고 다른 곡으로 옮겨 가는 동안 열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“Grandfer Cantle is standing in this corner, waiting his turn.”", "「캔틀 영감이 이쪽 모퉁이에 서서 자기 차례를 기다리고 있군.」"]], [["“’Twon’t be long; ’tis a six-handed reel,”", "「오래 걸리진 않아, 여섯 명이 추는 릴 춤이니까.」"], ["said the Doctor.", "의사가 말했다."]], [["“Why not go in, dancing or no?", "「춤이고 뭐고 그냥 들어가면 어때?"], ["They sent for us,”", "사람들이 우릴 부른 거잖아.」"], ["said the Saracen.", "사라센이 말했다."]], [["“Certainly not,”", "「절대 안 돼.」"], ["said Eustacia authoritatively, as she paced smartly up and down from door to gate to warm herself.", "유스테이샤가 몸을 덥히려고 문에서 대문까지 활기차게 오가며 권위 있게 말했다."], ["“We should burst into the middle of them and stop the dance, and that would be unmannerly.”", "「우리가 한복판으로 뛰어들어 춤을 멈추게 할 거고, 그건 예의에 어긋나는 일이야.」"]], [["“He thinks himself somebody because he has had a bit more schooling than we,”", "「저 친구는 우리보다 학교를 좀 더 다녔다고 자기가 대단한 줄 알아.」"], ["said the Doctor.", "의사가 말했다."]], [["“You may go to the deuce!”", "「뒈져 버려라!」"], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 말했다."]], [["There was a whispered conversation between three or four of them, and one turned to her.", "서너 명이 속닥속닥 이야기를 나누더니, 한 사람이 그녀를 돌아보았다."]], [["“Will you tell us one thing?”", "「한 가지만 말해 줄래?」"], ["he said, not without gentleness.", "그가 무뚝뚝하지 않게 말했다."], ["“Be you Miss Vye?", "「너 바이 양 아니야?"], ["We think you must be.”", "우린 그런 것 같아.」"]], [["“You may think what you like,”", "「네 맘대로 생각해.」"], ["said Eustacia slowly.", "유스테이샤가 천천히 말했다."], ["“But honourable lads will not tell tales upon a lady.”", "「하지만 명예로운 사내들은 숙녀 일로 고자질을 하진 않지.」"]], [["“We’ll say nothing, miss.", "「아무 말도 안 할게, 아가씨."], ["That’s upon our honour.”", "명예를 걸고.」"]], [["“Thank you,”", "「고마워.」"], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["At this moment the fiddles finished off with a screech, and the serpent emitted a last note that nearly lifted the roof.", "이 순간 바이올린들이 끽 소리와 함께 마무리되었고, 뱀나팔이 지붕을 들어 올릴 뻔한 마지막 음을 토해 냈다."], ["When, from the comparative quiet within, the mummers judged that the dancers had taken their seats, Father Christmas advanced, lifted the latch, and put his head inside the door.", "안쪽이 비교적 조용해지자 가면극 배우들은 춤추던 이들이 자리에 앉았다고 판단했고, 크리스마스 영감이 앞으로 나아가 빗장을 들어 올리고 문 안으로 머리를 들이밀었다."]], [["“Ah, the mummers, the mummers!”", "「아, 가면극이다, 가면극!」"], ["cried several guests at once.", "여러 손님이 동시에 외쳤다."], ["“Clear a space for the mummers.”", "「가면극 배우들을 위해 자리를 비워라.」"]], [["Humpbacked Father Christmas then made a complete entry, swinging his huge club, and in a general way clearing the stage for the actors proper, while he informed the company in smart verse that he was come, welcome or welcome not; concluding his speech with", "곱사등이 크리스마스 영감은 그러고 나서 자신의 거대한 곤봉을 휘두르며 완전히 들어와, 본격적인 배우들을 위해 무대를 대강 비우는 한편, 자기가 환영받든 안 받든 왔노라고 좌중에게 경쾌한 운문으로 알리며 다음과 같은 말로 연설을 맺었다."]], [["“Make room, make room, my gallant boys, And give us space to rhyme; We’ve come to show Saint George’s play, Upon this Christmas time.”", "「자리를 내라, 자리를 내라, 용감한 친구들이여, 우리에게 운율 읊을 공간을 다오. 우리는 성 게오르기우스의 극을 보여 주러 왔노라, 바로 이 크리스마스 때에.」"]], [["The guests were now arranging themselves at one end of the room, the fiddler was mending a string, the serpent-player was emptying his mouthpiece, and the play began.", "이제 손님들이 방 한쪽 끝에 자리를 잡았고, 바이올린 연주자는 줄을 손보고 있었으며, 뱀나팔 연주자는 마우스피스를 비우고 있었고, 극이 시작되었다."], ["First of those outside the Valiant Soldier entered, in the interest of Saint George—", "바깥에 있던 이들 가운데 첫째로 용맹한 병사가 성 게오르기우스를 위하여 등장했으니—"]], [["“Here come I, the Valiant Soldier; Slasher is my name”;", "「여기 내가 왔노라, 용맹한 병사라. 슬래셔가 내 이름이니라.」"]], [["and so on.", "이렇게 이어졌다."], ["This speech concluded with a challenge to the infidel, at the end of which it was Eustacia’s duty to enter as the Turkish Knight.", "이 대사는 이교도를 향한 도전으로 끝났고, 그 끝에서 유스테이샤가 터키 기사로 등장하는 것이 그녀의 임무였다."], ["She, with the rest who were not yet on, had hitherto remained in the moonlight which streamed under the porch.", "그녀는 아직 무대에 나오지 않은 나머지 이들과 함께 그때까지 현관 아래로 흘러드는 달빛 속에 머물러 있었다."], ["With no apparent effort or backwardness she came in, beginning—", "별다른 노력이나 망설임 없이 그녀가 들어오며 시작했다—"]], [["“Here come I, a Turkish Knight, Who learnt in Turkish land to fight; I’ll fight this man with courage bold: If his blood’s hot I’ll make it cold!”", "「여기 내가 왔노라, 터키 기사라, 터키 땅에서 싸움을 배운 자이니라. 나는 이 사내와 용감하고 대담하게 싸우리니, 그자의 피가 뜨겁다면 차갑게 식혀 주리라!」"]], [["During her declamation Eustacia held her head erect, and spoke as roughly as she could, feeling pretty secure from observation.", "대사를 읊는 동안 유스테이샤는 머리를 꼿꼿이 세우고 최대한 거칠게 말했으며, 눈에 띄지 않으리라는 상당한 확신을 느꼈다."], ["But the concentration upon her part necessary to prevent discovery, the newness of the scene, the shine of the candles, and the confusing effect upon her vision of the ribboned visor which hid her features, left her absolutely unable to perceive who were present as spectators.", "그러나 정체를 들키지 않으려면 자기 역할에 집중해야 했던 점, 무대의 생소함, 촛불의 광채, 그리고 얼굴을 가린 리본 달린 면갑이 시야에 일으킨 혼란스러운 효과 때문에, 그녀는 구경꾼으로 누가 와 있는지 도무지 알아볼 수가 없었다."], ["On the further side of a table bearing candles she could faintly discern faces, and that was all.", "촛불을 받친 탁자 저편으로 그녀는 얼굴들을 희미하게 분간할 수 있었으나, 그게 전부였다."]], [["Meanwhile Jim Starks as the Valiant Soldier had come forward, and, with a glare upon the Turk, replied—", "한편 용맹한 병사 역의 짐 스타크스가 앞으로 나와, 터키인을 노려보며 대답했다—"]], [["“If, then, thou art that Turkish Knight, Draw out thy sword, and let us fight!”", "「그렇다면 네가 그 터키 기사라면, 칼을 뽑아라, 그리고 싸우자!」"]], [["And fight they did; the issue of the combat being that the Valiant Soldier was slain by a preternaturally inadequate thrust from Eustacia, Jim, in his ardour for genuine histrionic art, coming down like a log upon the stone floor with force enough to dislocate his shoulder.", "그리고 그들은 싸웠으니, 결투의 결말은 용맹한 병사가 유스테이샤의 터무니없이 부실한 일격에 쓰러져 죽는 것이었는데, 짐은 진정한 연기 예술에 대한 열의로 어깨가 빠질 만큼 세게 통나무처럼 돌바닥에 쾅 넘어졌다."], ["Then, after more words from the Turkish Knight, rather too faintly delivered, and statements that he’d fight Saint George and all his crew, Saint George himself magnificently entered with the well-known flourish—", "그러고 나서 터키 기사가 다소 너무 희미하게 전달한 말을 좀 더 늘어놓고, 성 게오르기우스와 그 무리 전부와도 싸우겠노라 외치고 나자, 성 게오르기우스 본인이 잘 알려진 화려한 어구와 함께 당당하게 등장했다—"]], [["“Here come I, Saint George, the valiant man, With naked sword and spear in hand, Who fought the dragon and brought him to the slaughter, And by this won fair Sabra, the King of Egypt’s daughter; What mortal man would dare to stand Before me with my sword in hand?”", "「여기 내가 왔노라, 성 게오르기우스, 용맹한 사내라, 맨 칼을 들고 손에 창을 쥐었노라, 용을 무찔러 도살하였고, 그로써 이집트 왕의 딸 어여쁜 사브라를 얻었노라. 어느 인간이 감히 맞설 수 있겠는가, 손에 칼을 쥔 내 앞에?」"]], [["This was the lad who had first recognized Eustacia; and when she now, as the Turk, replied with suitable defiance, and at once began the combat, the young fellow took especial care to use his sword as gently as possible.", "이자가 바로 유스테이샤를 처음으로 알아본 젊은이였으니, 이제 그녀가 터키인으로서 적절한 반항으로 응수하고 곧장 결투를 시작하자, 그 젊은 친구는 가능한 한 부드럽게 칼을 쓰려고 각별히 신경을 썼다."], ["Being wounded, the Knight fell upon one knee, according to the direction.", "상처를 입은 기사는 지시에 따라 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 쓰러졌다."], ["The Doctor now entered, restored the Knight by giving him a draught from the bottle which he carried, and the fight was again resumed, the Turk sinking by degrees until quite overcome—dying as hard in this venerable drama as he is said to do at the present day.", "이제 의사가 등장해, 자기가 들고 다니던 병에서 한 모금을 먹여 기사를 회복시켰고, 다시 싸움이 재개되었으니, 터키인은 차츰차츰 가라앉아 마침내 완전히 제압당했고—이 유서 깊은 극에서도 오늘날 그렇다고들 하는 것처럼 좀처럼 쉽게 죽지 않았다."]], [["This gradual sinking to the earth was, in fact, one reason why Eustacia had thought that the part of the Turkish Knight, though not the shortest, would suit her best.", "이렇게 땅으로 차츰 가라앉는 것이, 사실 유스테이샤가 터키 기사 역이 가장 짧지는 않으나 자기에게 가장 잘 맞으리라 여겼던 한 가지 이유였다."], ["A direct fall from upright to horizontal, which was the end of the other fighting characters, was not an elegant or decorous part for a girl.", "다른 싸우는 인물들의 최후처럼 똑바로 선 자세에서 수평으로 곧장 넘어지는 것은 처녀에게 우아하거나 점잖은 역할이 아니었다."], ["But it was easy to die like a Turk, by a dogged decline.", "그러나 터키인처럼 끈질기게 기울어 가며 죽는 것은 쉬웠다."]], [["Eustacia was now among the number of the slain, though not on the floor, for she had managed to sink into a sloping position against the clock-case, so that her head was well elevated.", "이제 유스테이샤는 쓰러진 자들 축에 끼었으나, 바닥에 누운 것은 아니었으니, 시계 케이스에 기댄 비스듬한 자세로 가라앉는 데 용케 성공해서 머리를 충분히 높이 들고 있었다."], ["The play proceeded between Saint George, the Saracen, the Doctor, and Father Christmas; and Eustacia, having no more to do, for the first time found leisure to observe the scene round, and to search for the form that had drawn her hither.", "극은 성 게오르기우스, 사라센, 의사, 크리스마스 영감 사이에서 진행되었고, 더 이상 할 일이 없어진 유스테이샤는 처음으로 주변 광경을 살피고 자신을 이곳으로 이끈 그 형상을 찾을 여유를 얻었다."]], [["VI. The Two Stand Face to Face\nThe room had been arranged with a view to the dancing, the large oak table having been moved back till it stood as a breastwork to the fireplace.", "방은 춤추기에 알맞도록 배치되어, 커다란 떡갈나무 탁자가 벽난로 앞 흉벽처럼 설 때까지 뒤로 밀려나 있었다."], ["At each end, behind, and in the chimney-corner were grouped the guests, many of them being warm-faced and panting, among whom Eustacia cursorily recognized some well-to-do persons from beyond the heath.", "양 끝과 뒤편과 굴뚝 모퉁이에 손님들이 모여 있었는데, 그중 다수는 얼굴이 달아오른 채 헐떡이고 있었으며, 유스테이샤는 그들 가운데 황야 너머에서 온 잘사는 사람 몇몇을 대충 알아보았다."], ["Thomasin, as she had expected, was not visible, and Eustacia recollected that a light had shone from an upper window when they were outside—the window, probably, of Thomasin’s room.", "예상했던 대로 토머신은 보이지 않았고, 유스테이샤는 그들이 바깥에 있을 때 위층 창문에서 불빛이 새어 나왔던 일을 떠올렸으니—아마 토머신 방의 창문이었으리라."], ["A nose, chin, hands, knees, and toes projected from the seat within the chimney opening, which members she found to unite in the person of Grandfer Cantle, Mrs. Yeobright’s occasional assistant in the garden, and therefore one of the invited.", "굴뚝 입구 안쪽 자리에서 코와 턱과 손과 무릎과 발끝이 튀어나와 있었는데, 그 부위들이 모여 캔틀 영감이라는 한 인물을 이루고 있음을 그녀는 알아챘으니, 그는 요브라이트 부인의 정원 일을 이따금 거드는 사람이었고 그래서 초대받은 이들 중 하나였다."], ["The smoke went up from an Etna of peat in front of him, played round the notches of the chimney-crook, struck against the salt-box, and got lost among the flitches.", "그 앞에서 토탄 화산에서 연기가 솟아올라 굴뚝 고리의 홈을 휘감고 소금 통에 부딪쳤다가 베이컨 덩어리들 사이로 사라졌다."]], [["Another part of the room soon riveted her gaze.", "방의 다른 부분이 곧 그녀의 시선을 사로잡았다."], ["At the other side of the chimney stood the settle, which is the necessary supplement to a fire so open that nothing less than a strong breeze will carry up the smoke.", "굴뚝 반대편에는 긴 등받이 의자가 서 있었으니, 이는 강한 바람이 아니고서는 연기를 위로 끌어올리지 못할 만큼 입을 크게 벌린 불에 반드시 딸려야 하는 보완물이었다."], ["It is, to the hearths of old-fashioned cavernous fireplaces, what the east belt of trees is to the exposed country estate, or the north wall to the garden.", "그것은 옛날식 동굴 같은 벽난로의 화덕에게, 노출된 시골 영지에게 동쪽 나무 띠가 그러하고 정원에게 북쪽 담이 그러한 것과 같은 존재였다."], ["Outside the settle candles gutter, locks of hair wave, young women shiver, and old men sneeze.", "등받이 의자 바깥에서는 촛불이 흘러내리고 머리카락 다발이 흔들리며 젊은 여자들이 떨고 노인들이 재채기를 한다."], ["Inside is Paradise.", "안쪽은 낙원이다."], ["Not a symptom of a draught disturbs the air; the sitters’ backs are as warm as their faces, and songs and old tales are drawn from the occupants by the comfortable heat, like fruit from melon plants in a frame.", "바람의 기미 하나 공기를 어지럽히지 않고, 앉은 이들의 등은 그들의 얼굴만큼이나 따뜻하며, 안락한 온기에 이끌려 그 자리에 앉은 이들에게서 노래와 옛이야기가 흘러나오니, 마치 온실 틀 속 멜론 줄기에서 열매가 맺히듯 한다."]], [["It was, however, not with those who sat in the settle that Eustacia was concerned.", "그러나 유스테이샤가 관심을 둔 것은 등받이 의자에 앉은 이들이 아니었다."], ["A face showed itself with marked distinctness against the dark-tanned wood of the upper part.", "윗부분의 짙게 그을린 나무를 배경으로 한 얼굴 하나가 유독 또렷이 드러났다."], ["The owner, who was leaning against the settle’s outer end, was Clement Yeobright, or Clym, as he was called here; she knew it could be nobody else.", "등받이 의자 바깥쪽 끝에 기대선 그 임자는 클레멘트 요브라이트, 이곳에서 불리는 이름으로는 클림이었으니, 그녀는 다른 누구일 리 없음을 알았다."], ["The spectacle constituted an area of two feet in Rembrandt’s intensest manner.", "그 광경은 렘브란트의 가장 강렬한 화법으로 그려진 가로세로 두 자 남짓한 면적을 이루었다."], ["A strange power in the lounger’s appearance lay in the fact that, though his whole figure was visible, the observer’s eye was only aware of his face.", "빈둥거리는 자의 모습에 깃든 기묘한 힘은, 그의 온몸이 보이는데도 보는 이의 눈에는 오직 그의 얼굴만이 인식된다는 사실에 있었다."]], [["To one of middle age the countenance was that of a young man, though a youth might hardly have seen any necessity for the term of immaturity.", "중년에게 그 얼굴은 젊은이의 것이었으나, 청년이라면 미숙함이라는 말을 굳이 붙일 필요를 거의 못 느꼈을 것이다."], ["But it was really one of those faces which convey less the idea of so many years as its age than of so much experience as its store.", "그러나 그것은 실로, 나이로 따진 세월의 길이보다 축적된 경험의 양을 더 많이 전하는 그런 얼굴 가운데 하나였다."], ["The number of their years may have adequately summed up Jared, Mahalaleel, and the rest of the antediluvians, but the age of a modern man is to be measured by the intensity of his history.", "세월의 수가 야렛이며 마할랄렐이며 그 밖의 대홍수 이전 사람들을 적절히 요약했을지는 모르나, 현대인의 나이는 그 이력의 강렬함으로 재야 한다."]], [["The face was well shaped, even excellently.", "그 얼굴은 잘 빚어진, 심지어 빼어나게 잘 빚어진 얼굴이었다."], ["But the mind within was beginning to use it as a mere waste tablet whereon to trace its idiosyncrasies as they developed themselves.", "그러나 그 안의 정신은 그것을 한낱 빈 서판으로 쓰기 시작해, 자신의 특질이 발현되는 대로 거기에 새기고 있었다."], ["The beauty here visible would in no long time be ruthlessly over-run by its parasite, thought, which might just as well have fed upon a plainer exterior where there was nothing it could harm.", "여기 보이는 아름다움은 머지않아 그 기생충인 사고思考에게 무자비하게 잠식당할 터였으니, 그 사고는 해칠 것이 아무것도 없는 더 평범한 외모에 깃들어도 무방했으리라."], ["Had Heaven preserved Yeobright from a wearing habit of meditation, people would have said, “A handsome man.”", "하늘이 요브라이트를 명상이라는 닳게 하는 습관에서 지켜 주었더라면 사람들은 「잘생긴 사내」라고 했을 것이다."], ["Had his brain unfolded under sharper contours they would have said, “A thoughtful man.”", "그의 뇌가 더 날카로운 윤곽 아래 펼쳐졌더라면 사람들은 「사색하는 사내」라고 했을 것이다."], ["But an inner strenuousness was preying upon an outer symmetry, and they rated his look as singular.", "그러나 내면의 치열함이 외면의 균형을 갉아먹고 있었기에, 사람들은 그의 용모를 별나다고 평했다."]], [["Hence people who began by beholding him ended by perusing him.", "그리하여 그를 바라보기 시작한 사람들은 끝내 그를 정독하게 되었다."], ["His countenance was overlaid with legible meanings.", "그의 용모에는 읽어 낼 수 있는 의미들이 겹겹이 덧씌워져 있었다."], ["Without being thought-worn he yet had certain marks derived from a perception of his surroundings, such as are not unfrequently found on men at the end of the four or five years of endeavour which follow the close of placid pupilage.", "사고에 시달린 것은 아니었으되 그에게는 자기 주변에 대한 지각에서 비롯된 어떤 흔적들이 있었으니, 평온한 학창 시절이 끝나고 이어지는 사오 년간의 분투가 끝날 무렵의 사내들에게서 드물지 않게 발견되는 그런 것이었다."], ["He already showed that thought is a disease of flesh, and indirectly bore evidence that ideal physical beauty is incompatible with emotional development and a full recognition of the coil of things.", "그는 사고가 육신의 병임을 이미 보여 주고 있었고, 이상적인 육체의 아름다움은 정서의 발달이며 세상사 얽힘에 대한 온전한 인식과 양립할 수 없음을 간접으로 입증하고 있었다."], ["Mental luminousness must be fed with the oil of life, even though there is already a physical need for it; and the pitiful sight of two demands on one supply was just showing itself here.", "정신의 광휘는 생명의 기름을 먹여야만 하는 법이니, 이미 육체에 그 기름이 필요한데도 그러하며, 하나의 공급원에 두 가지 요구가 걸린 가련한 광경이 바로 여기서 막 드러나고 있었다."]], [["When standing before certain men the philosopher regrets that thinkers are but perishable tissue, the artist that perishable tissue has to think.", "어떤 사내들 앞에 섰을 때 철학자는 사색가들이 한낱 썩어 없어질 조직에 불과함을 안타까워하고, 예술가는 썩어 없어질 조직이 사색을 해야만 함을 안타까워한다."], ["Thus to deplore, each from his point of view, the mutually destructive interdependence of spirit and flesh would have been instinctive with these in critically observing Yeobright.", "그리하여 정신과 육체의 서로를 파괴하는 상호 의존을 저마다의 관점에서 개탄하는 것이, 요브라이트를 비판적으로 살피는 이들에게는 본능적이었으리라."]], [["As for his look, it was a natural cheerfulness striving against depression from without, and not quite succeeding.", "그의 표정으로 말하자면, 그것은 바깥에서 내리누르는 우울에 맞서 싸우면서도 완전히 이겨 내지는 못하는 타고난 쾌활함이었다."], ["The look suggested isolation, but it revealed something more.", "그 표정은 고립을 암시했으나, 그 이상의 무언가를 드러내기도 했다."], ["As is usual with bright natures, the deity that lies ignominiously chained within an ephemeral human carcase shone out of him like a ray.", "밝은 천성을 지닌 이들에게 흔히 그러하듯, 하루살이 같은 인간의 시신屍身 안에 치욕스럽게 사슬로 묶인 신성이 광선처럼 그에게서 뿜어 나왔다."]], [["The effect upon Eustacia was palpable.", "유스테이샤에게 미친 효과는 뚜렷했다."], ["The extraordinary pitch of excitement that she had reached beforehand would, indeed, have caused her to be influenced by the most commonplace man.", "그녀가 미리 다다라 있던 그 비상한 흥분의 정도라면, 사실 더없이 평범한 사내에게도 영향을 받았을 것이다."], ["She was troubled at Yeobright’s presence.", "그녀는 요브라이트의 존재에 마음이 어지러웠다."]], [["The remainder of the play ended—the Saracen’s head was cut off, and Saint George stood as victor.", "극의 나머지가 끝났다—사라센의 머리가 잘렸고, 성 게오르기우스가 승자로 섰다."], ["Nobody commented, any more than they would have commented on the fact of mushrooms coming in autumn or snowdrops in spring.", "누구도 거기에 대해 논평하지 않았으니, 가을에 버섯이 나오거나 봄에 스노드롭이 피는 사실에 논평하지 않는 것과 다를 바 없었다."], ["They took the piece as phlegmatically as did the actors themselves.", "그들은 배우들 자신이 그러하듯 그 극을 무덤덤하게 받아들였다."], ["It was a phase of cheerfulness which was, as a matter of course, to be passed through every Christmas; and there was no more to be said.", "그것은 으레 크리스마스마다 거쳐야 하는 한 가지 쾌활함의 국면이었으니, 더 할 말이 없었다."]], [["They sang the plaintive chant which follows the play, during which all the dead men rise to their feet in a silent and awful manner, like the ghosts of Napoleon’s soldiers in the Midnight Review.", "그들은 극에 뒤따르는 구슬픈 노래를 불렀는데, 그동안 죽었던 사내들이 모두 말없이 무시무시하게 일어섰으니, 마치 〈한밤의 열병閱兵〉에 나오는 나폴레옹 병사들의 유령 같았다."], ["Afterwards the door opened, and Fairway appeared on the threshold, accompanied by Christian and another.", "그 후 문이 열리고, 페어웨이가 크리스천과 또 한 사람을 데리고 문턱에 나타났다."], ["They had been waiting outside for the conclusion of the play, as the players had waited for the conclusion of the dance.", "그들은 배우들이 춤이 끝나기를 기다렸던 것처럼 극이 끝나기를 바깥에서 기다리고 있었다."]], [["“Come in, come in,” said Mrs.", "「들어와요, 들어와.」"], ["Yeobright; and Clym went forward to welcome them. “How is it you are so late?", "요브라이트 부인이 말했고, 클림이 그들을 맞으러 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["Grandfer Cantle has been here ever so long, and we thought you’d have come with him, as you live so near one another.”", "「어쩌다 이리 늦었어요? 캔틀 영감은 진작 와 있었는데, 서로 이리 가까이 사니 같이 올 줄 알았지요.」"]], [["“Well, I should have come earlier,” Mr.", "「그게, 더 일찍 왔어야 하는데요.」"], ["Fairway said and paused to look along the beam of the ceiling for a nail to hang his hat on; but, finding his accustomed one to be occupied by the mistletoe, and all the nails in the walls to be burdened with bunches of holly, he at last relieved himself of the hat by ticklishly balancing it between the candle-box and the head of the clock-case.", "페어웨이 씨가 말하다가 모자를 걸 못을 찾으려 천장 들보를 따라 시선을 보내며 멈췄으나, 늘 쓰던 못은 겨우살이가 차지하고 벽의 못들은 죄다 호랑가시나무 다발에 점령된 것을 보고는, 마침내 촛불 통과 시계 케이스 머리 사이에 모자를 조마조마하게 균형 잡아 얹어 떼어 놓았다."], ["“I should have come earlier, ma’am,”", "「더 일찍 왔어야 하는데요, 부인.」"], ["he resumed, with a more composed air, “but I know what parties be, and how there’s none too much room in folks’ houses at such times, so I thought I wouldn’t come till you’d got settled a bit.”", "그가 한결 차분해진 태도로 다시 말을 이었다. 「하지만 잔치라는 게 어떤 건지, 그런 때 사람들 집에 자리가 넉넉지 않다는 걸 잘 아니까, 좀 자리가 잡힐 때까지 안 오는 게 낫겠다 싶었지요.」"]], [["“And I thought so too, Mrs. Yeobright,”", "「저도 그렇게 생각했어요, 요브라이트 부인.」"], ["said Christian earnestly, “but Father there was so eager that he had no manners at all, and left home almost afore ’twas dark.", "크리스천이 진지하게 말했다. 「그런데 우리 아버지가 어찌나 안달이 나셨는지 예의고 뭐고 없이, 거의 어두워지기도 전에 집을 나서셨지 뭐예요."], ["I told him ’twas barely decent in a’ old man to come so oversoon; but words be wind.”", "제가 늙은 양반이 이렇게 너무 일찍 가는 건 점잖지 못하다고 말씀드렸지만, 말은 다 바람이지요.」"]], [["“Klk!", "「클!"], ["I wasn’t going to bide waiting about, till half the game was over!", "게임이 반이나 끝날 때까지 우두커니 기다리고 있을 순 없지!"], ["I’m as light as a kite when anything’s going on!”", "무슨 일이 벌어진다 하면 난 연만큼 가뿐하다고!」"], ["crowed Grandfer Cantle from the chimneyseat.", "캔틀 영감이 굴뚝 자리에서 의기양양하게 외쳤다."]], [["Fairway had meanwhile concluded a critical gaze at Yeobright.", "그동안 페어웨이는 요브라이트를 비판적으로 응시하기를 마쳤다."], ["“Now, you may not believe it,”", "「자, 안 믿을지도 모르겠지만.」"], ["he said to the rest of the room, “but I should never have knowed this gentleman if I had met him anywhere off his own he’th—he’s altered so much.”", "그가 방 안의 나머지 사람들에게 말했다. 「이 양반을 제 황야 밖 어디서 마주쳤더라면 난 절대 못 알아봤을 거요—어찌나 많이 변했는지.」"]], [["“You too have altered, and for the better, I think Timothy,”", "「당신도 변했어요, 그것도 더 나은 쪽으로요, 티머시.」"], ["said Yeobright, surveying the firm figure of Fairway.", "요브라이트가 페어웨이의 단단한 풍채를 살피며 말했다."]], [["“Master Yeobright, look me over too.", "「요브라이트 도련님, 나도 좀 봐 주시오."], ["I have altered for the better, haven’t I, hey?”", "난 더 나은 쪽으로 변했지요, 안 그렇소, 응?」"], ["said Grandfer Cantle, rising and placing himself something above half a foot from Clym’s eye, to induce the most searching criticism.", "캔틀 영감이 일어서며, 가장 샅샅이 뜯어보게 하려고 클림의 눈에서 반 자 남짓 떨어진 곳에 자기 몸을 들이대며 말했다."]], [["“To be sure we will,”", "「물론 봐 드리지요.」"], ["said Fairway, taking the candle and moving it over the surface of the Grandfer’s countenance, the subject of his scrutiny irradiating himself with light and pleasant smiles, and giving himself jerks of juvenility.", "페어웨이가 촛불을 들어 영감의 얼굴 표면 위로 옮겨 가며 말했고, 살펴봄의 대상이 된 당사자는 환한 빛을 받으며 흐뭇한 미소를 짓고 젊은이처럼 몸을 들썩여 보였다."]], [["“You haven’t changed much,”", "「별로 안 변하셨네요.」"], ["said Yeobright.", "요브라이트가 말했다."]], [["“If there’s any difference, Grandfer is younger,”", "「차이가 있다면, 영감님이 더 젊어지셨군.」"], ["appended Fairway decisively.", "페어웨이가 단호하게 덧붙였다."]], [["“And yet not my own doing, and I feel no pride in it,”", "「그런데도 내가 한 일은 아니니, 거기엔 자랑이 없어.」"], ["said the pleased ancient.", "흐뭇해진 노인이 말했다."], ["“But I can’t be cured of my vagaries; them I plead guilty to.", "「하지만 내 변덕은 못 고쳐, 그건 인정하지."], ["Yes, Master Cantle always was that, as we know.", "그래, 캔틀 어른은 늘 그러셨지, 우리도 알다시피."], ["But I am nothing by the side of you, Mister Clym.”", "하지만 난 자네 옆에 서면 아무것도 아니야, 클림 씨.」"]], [["“Nor any o’ us,”", "「우리 중 누구도 그렇지.」"], ["said Humphrey, in a low rich tone of admiration, not intended to reach anybody’s ears.", "험프리가 누구의 귀에도 닿지 않게 할 작정으로 낮고 깊은 감탄의 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Really, there would have been nobody here who could have stood as decent second to him, or even third, if I hadn’t been a soldier in the Bang-up Locals (as we was called for our smartness),”", "「정말이지, 내가 뱅업 향토 의용군(우리가 어찌나 멋졌던지 그렇게 불렸지) 병사가 아니었더라면, 여기 그 사람 옆에 그럴듯한 둘째로, 아니 셋째로라도 설 수 있는 자가 아무도 없었을 거야.」"], ["said Grandfer Cantle.", "캔틀 영감이 말했다."], ["“And even as ’tis we all look a little scammish beside him.", "「그런데도 우리 다 그 사람 옆에 서면 좀 어설퍼 보여."], ["But in the year four ’twas said there wasn’t a finer figure in the whole South Wessex than I, as I looked when dashing past the shop-winders with the rest of our company on the day we ran out o’ Budmouth because it was thoughted that Boney had landed round the point.", "하지만 4년에는, 보니가 곶 근처에 상륙했다 싶어서 우리 중대 나머지랑 같이 버드머스에서 뛰쳐나오던 날, 가게 진열창 옆을 휙휙 지나칠 적 내 모습이 남웨식스 통틀어 그보다 더 멋진 풍채는 없었다고들 했지."], ["There was I, straight as a young poplar, wi’ my firelock, and my bagnet, and my spatterdashes, and my stock sawing my jaws off, and my accoutrements sheening like the seven stars!", "거기 내가 있었으니, 어린 포플러처럼 꼿꼿하게, 화승총에, 총검에, 각반에, 턱이 쓸릴 만한 옷깃에, 일곱 별처럼 번쩍이는 장구들까지!"], ["Yes, neighbours, I was a pretty sight in my soldiering days.", "그래, 이웃들, 내 군 복무 시절엔 내가 볼만한 광경이었지."], ["You ought to have seen me in four!”", "4년에 날 봤어야 하는데!」"]], [["“’Tis his mother’s side where Master Clym’s figure comes from, bless ye,”", "「클림 도련님 풍채는 어머니 쪽에서 온 거요, 정말이지.」"], ["said Timothy.", "티머시가 말했다."], ["“I know’d her brothers well.", "「난 그 외삼촌들을 잘 알았소."], ["Longer coffins were never made in the whole country of South Wessex, and ’tis said that poor George’s knees were crumpled up a little e’en as ’twas.”", "남웨식스 온 고장을 통틀어 그보다 긴 관은 만든 적이 없었고, 가엾은 조지는 그러고도 무릎이 좀 구부러졌었다는 얘기까지 있지.」"]], [["“Coffins, where?”", "「관이라니, 어디?」"], ["inquired Christian, drawing nearer.", "크리스천이 더 가까이 다가오며 물었다."], ["“Have the ghost of one appeared to anybody, Master Fairway?”", "「누구한테 관 귀신이라도 나타났소, 페어웨이 어른?」"]], [["“No, no.", "「아니, 아니야."], ["Don’t let your mind so mislead your ears, Christian; and be a man,”", "마음이 그렇게 네 귀를 잘못 이끌게 두지 말고, 사내답게 굴어, 크리스천.」"], ["said Timothy reproachfully.", "티머시가 나무라듯 말했다."]], [["“I will.” said Christian.", "「그러지요.」"], ["“But now I think o’t my shadder last night seemed just the shape of a coffin.", "크리스천이 말했다. 「하지만 지금 생각하니 어젯밤 내 그림자가 꼭 관 모양 같았어요."], ["What is it a sign of when your shade’s like a coffin, neighbours?", "그림자가 관 모양이면 무슨 징조요, 이웃들?"], ["It can’t be nothing to be afeared of, I suppose?”", "무서워할 건 아니겠지요, 설마?」"]], [["“Afeared, no!”", "「무서워? 아니지!」"], ["said the Grandfer.", "영감이 말했다."], ["“Faith, I was never afeard of nothing except Boney, or I shouldn’t ha’ been the soldier I was.", "「참말이지, 난 보니 말고는 아무것도 무서워한 적이 없어, 안 그랬으면 그런 병사가 못 됐겠지."], ["Yes, ’tis a thousand pities you didn’t see me in four!”", "그래, 4년에 자네가 날 못 본 게 천추의 한이로다!」"]], [["By this time the mummers were preparing to leave; but Mrs. Yeobright stopped them by asking them to sit down and have a little supper.", "이쯤 되자 가면극 배우들은 떠날 채비를 하고 있었으나, 요브라이트 부인이 앉아서 간단히 저녁이라도 들고 가라고 청하며 그들을 붙들었다."], ["To this invitation Father Christmas, in the name of them all, readily agreed.", "이 청에 크리스마스 영감이 모두를 대표해 흔쾌히 응했다."]], [["Eustacia was happy in the opportunity of staying a little longer.", "유스테이샤는 조금 더 머무를 기회가 생겨 기뻤다."], ["The cold and frosty night without was doubly frigid to her.", "바깥의 춥고 서리 내린 밤은 그녀에게 곱절로 차가웠다."], ["But the lingering was not without its difficulties.", "그러나 이 지체에는 곤란이 따랐다."], ["Mrs. Yeobright, for want of room in the larger apartment, placed a bench for the mummers halfway through the pantry door, which opened from the sitting-room.", "요브라이트 부인은 큰 방에 자리가 없어, 거실에서 통하는 식료품 저장실 문에 반쯤 걸쳐서 가면극 배우들을 위한 긴 의자를 놓았다."], ["Here they seated themselves in a row, the door being left open—thus they were still virtually in the same apartment. Mrs.", "여기에 그들이 한 줄로 앉았으니, 문은 열어 둔 채여서—그리하여 그들은 사실상 여전히 같은 방에 있는 셈이었다."], ["Yeobright now murmured a few words to her son, who crossed the room to the pantry door, striking his head against the mistletoe as he passed, and brought the mummers beef and bread, cake pastry, mead, and elder-wine, the waiting being done by him and his mother, that the little maid-servant might sit as guest.", "이제 요브라이트 부인이 아들에게 몇 마디 속삭였고, 아들은 방을 가로질러 식료품 저장실 문으로 가다가 지나면서 겨우살이에 머리를 부딪쳤으며, 배우들에게 쇠고기와 빵, 케이크 페이스트리, 벌꿀술, 엘더베리주를 가져다주었으니, 시중은 그와 그의 어머니가 들어서 어린 하녀가 손님으로 앉을 수 있게 했다."], ["The mummers doffed their helmets, and began to eat and drink.", "배우들은 투구를 벗고 먹고 마시기 시작했다."]], [["“But you will surely have some?”", "「하지만 자네도 분명 좀 들겠지?」"], ["said Clym to the Turkish Knight, as he stood before that warrior, tray in hand.", "클림이 쟁반을 손에 들고 그 전사 앞에 서서 터키 기사에게 말했다."], ["She had refused, and still sat covered, only the sparkle of her eyes being visible between the ribbons which covered her face.", "그녀는 거절했고 여전히 가린 채 앉아 있어, 얼굴을 덮은 리본들 사이로 두 눈의 반짝임만 보였다."]], [["“None, thank you,”", "「됐어요, 고맙지만.」"], ["replied Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 대답했다."]], [["“He’s quite a youngster,”", "「얘는 아주 풋내기라.」"], ["said the Saracen apologetically, “and you must excuse him.", "사라센이 변명하듯 말했다. 「봐주셔야 해요."], ["He’s not one of the old set, but have jined us because t’other couldn’t come.”", "옛 무리는 아니고, 다른 애가 못 와서 우리한테 낀 거예요.」"]], [["“But he will take something?”", "「그래도 뭔가 들겠지?」"], ["persisted Yeobright.", "요브라이트가 끈질기게 말했다."], ["“Try a glass of mead or elder-wine.”", "「벌꿀술이나 엘더베리주 한 잔 해 봐.」"]], [["“Yes, you had better try that,”", "「그래, 그게 낫겠다.」"], ["said the Saracen.", "사라센이 말했다."], ["“It will keep the cold out going home-along.”", "「집에 가는 길에 추위를 막아 줄 테니.」"]], [["Though Eustacia could not eat without uncovering her face she could drink easily enough beneath her disguise.", "유스테이샤는 얼굴을 드러내지 않고서는 먹을 수 없었으나 변장을 한 채로도 충분히 쉽게 마실 수는 있었다."], ["The elder-wine was accordingly accepted, and the glass vanished inside the ribbons.", "그리하여 엘더베리주는 받아들여졌고, 잔은 리본들 안쪽으로 사라졌다."]], [["At moments during this performance Eustacia was half in doubt about the security of her position; yet it had a fearful joy.", "이 일을 하는 동안 순간순간 유스테이샤는 자기 처지의 안전에 대해 반쯤 의심에 빠졌으나, 거기엔 두려운 기쁨이 있었다."], ["A series of attentions paid to her, and yet not to her but to some imaginary person, by the first man she had ever been inclined to adore, complicated her emotions indescribably.", "그녀가 흠모하고 싶다고 마음이 기운 첫 사내가 그녀에게, 하지만 그녀에게가 아니라 어떤 가상의 인물에게 베푸는 일련의 친절이 그녀의 감정을 형언할 수 없이 복잡하게 만들었다."], ["She had loved him partly because he was exceptional in this scene, partly because she had determined to love him, chiefly because she was in desperate need of loving somebody after wearying of Wildeve.", "그녀가 그를 사랑하게 된 것은 부분적으로는 그가 이 무대에서 예외적인 존재였기 때문이고, 부분적으로는 그를 사랑하기로 작정했기 때문이며, 무엇보다 와일디브에게 싫증이 난 끝에 누군가를 사랑하는 일이 절박하게 필요했기 때문이었다."], ["Believing that she must love him in spite of herself, she had been influenced after the fashion of the second Lord Lyttleton and other persons, who have dreamed that they were to die on a certain day, and by stress of a morbid imagination have actually brought about that event.", "자신이 어쩔 수 없이 그를 사랑해야 한다고 믿으면서, 그녀는 둘째 리틀턴 경이며, 어느 날 죽으리라 꿈꾸고는 병적인 상상의 압박으로 실제 그 일을 불러온 다른 이들과 같은 방식으로 영향을 받았다."], ["Once let a maiden admit the possibility of her being stricken with love for someone at a certain hour and place, and the thing is as good as done.", "처녀가 어느 시각 어느 장소에서 누군가에게 사랑에 빠질 가능성을 일단 인정하기만 하면, 그 일은 이미 이루어진 것이나 다름없다."]], [["Did anything at this moment suggest to Yeobright the sex of the creature whom that fantastic guise inclosed, how extended was her scope both in feeling and in making others feel, and how far her compass transcended that of her companions in the band?", "이 순간 그 환상적인 차림 안에 갇힌 존재의 성별이며, 그녀의 영역이 느끼는 데서나 남을 느끼게 하는 데서나 얼마나 넓은지, 그리고 그녀의 그릇이 무리 속 동료들의 것을 얼마나 멀리 능가하는지를 요브라이트에게 무언가 일러 주는 것이 있었을까?"], ["When the disguised Queen of Love appeared before Æneas a preternatural perfume accompanied her presence and betrayed her quality.", "변장한 사랑의 여왕이 아이네이아스 앞에 나타났을 때 초자연적인 향기가 그녀의 임재에 따라붙어 그 신분을 드러냈다."], ["If such a mysterious emanation ever was projected by the emotions of an earthly woman upon their object, it must have signified Eustacia’s presence to Yeobright now.", "만약 그러한 신비로운 발산이 지상의 여인의 감정에서 그 대상을 향해 투사된 적이 있다면, 그것은 지금 요브라이트에게 유스테이샤의 임재를 알렸을 것이다."], ["He looked at her wistfully, then seemed to fall into a reverie, as if he were forgetting what he observed.", "그는 그녀를 애틋하게 바라보더니, 자기가 본 것을 잊어 가는 듯 몽상에 잠긴 것 같았다."], ["The momentary situation ended, he passed on, and Eustacia sipped her wine without knowing what she drank.", "잠깐의 상황이 끝나자 그는 지나쳐 갔고, 유스테이샤는 자기가 무엇을 마시는지도 모른 채 술을 홀짝였다."], ["The man for whom she had pre-determined to nourish a passion went into the small room, and across it to the further extremity.", "그녀가 미리 정해 두고 정념을 키우려던 사내는 작은 방으로 들어가, 그곳을 가로질러 먼 끝으로 갔다."]], [["The mummers, as has been stated, were seated on a bench, one end of which extended into the small apartment, or pantry, for want of space in the outer room.", "앞서 말했듯이 가면극 배우들은 긴 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 바깥방에 자리가 없어 그 한쪽 끝이 작은 방, 곧 식료품 저장실로 뻗어 있었다."], ["Eustacia, partly from shyness, had chosen the midmost seat, which thus commanded a view of the interior of the pantry as well as the room containing the guests.", "유스테이샤는 일부는 수줍음 때문에 한가운데 자리를 골랐는데, 그리하여 그 자리에서는 식료품 저장실 안쪽뿐 아니라 손님들이 있는 방까지 내다볼 수 있었다."], ["When Clym passed down the pantry her eyes followed him in the gloom which prevailed there.", "클림이 식료품 저장실로 내려갈 때 그녀의 눈은 그곳에 깔린 어둠 속에서 그를 좇았다."], ["At the remote end was a door which, just as he was about to open it for himself, was opened by somebody within; and light streamed forth.", "먼 끝에 문 하나가 있었는데, 그가 막 그 문을 직접 열려는 순간 안에 있던 누군가가 문을 열었고, 빛이 흘러나왔다."]], [["The person was Thomasin, with a candle, looking anxious, pale, and interesting.", "그 사람은 토머신으로, 촛불을 들고 있었으며, 근심스럽고 창백하고 흥미로워 보였다."], ["Yeobright appeared glad to see her, and pressed her hand.", "요브라이트는 그녀를 보고 반가워하며 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."], ["“That’s right, Tamsie,”", "「잘됐네, 탬지.」"], ["he said heartily, as though recalled to himself by the sight of her, “you have decided to come down.", "그녀를 보고 제정신으로 돌아온 듯 그가 다정하게 말했다. 「내려오기로 했구나."], ["I am glad of it.”", "기뻐.」"]], [["“Hush—no, no,”", "「쉿—아니, 아니에요.」"], ["she said quickly.", "그녀가 재빨리 말했다."], ["“I only came to speak to you.”", "「그냥 오빠한테 말하러 온 거예요.」"]], [["“But why not join us?”", "「그래도 같이 끼지 그래?」"]], [["“I cannot.", "「못 해요."], ["At least I would rather not.", "적어도 안 하는 편이 낫겠어요."], ["I am not well enough, and we shall have plenty of time together now you are going to be home a good long holiday.”", "몸이 그리 좋지 않고, 오빠가 이제 꽤 긴 휴가로 집에 있을 테니 함께할 시간이야 충분할 거예요.」"]], [["“It isn’t nearly so pleasant without you.", "「네가 없으면 영 즐겁지가 않은데."], ["Are you really ill?”", "정말 아픈 거야?」"]], [["“Just a little, my old cousin—here,”", "「조금요, 내 오랜 사촌 오빠—여기가요.」"], ["she said, playfully sweeping her hand across her heart.", "그녀가 장난스럽게 손으로 가슴을 쓸며 말했다."]], [["“Ah, Mother should have asked somebody else to be present tonight, perhaps?”", "「아, 어머니가 오늘 밤엔 다른 사람을 불렀어야 했나 보네?」"]], [["“O no, indeed.", "「오, 아니에요, 정말로."], ["I merely stepped down, Clym, to ask you—”", "그냥 잠깐 내려온 거예요, 클림 오빠, 오빠한테 부탁하려고요—」"], ["Here he followed her through the doorway into the private room beyond, and, the door closing, Eustacia and the mummer who sat next to her, the only other witness of the performance, saw and heard no more.", "여기서 그가 그녀를 따라 문간을 지나 그 너머 사적인 방으로 들어갔고, 문이 닫히자, 그 일을 지켜본 유일한 또 다른 증인인 유스테이샤와 그 옆에 앉은 가면극 배우는 더는 보지도 듣지도 못했다."]], [["The heat flew to Eustacia’s head and cheeks.", "열기가 유스테이샤의 머리와 두 뺨으로 솟구쳤다."], ["She instantly guessed that Clym, having been home only these two or three days, had not as yet been made acquainted with Thomasin’s painful situation with regard to Wildeve; and seeing her living there just as she had been living before he left home, he naturally suspected nothing.", "그녀는 클림이 집에 온 지 겨우 이삼 일밖에 안 되었으니 와일디브와 관련된 토머신의 괴로운 처지를 아직 모르고 있으며, 그가 집을 떠나기 전과 똑같이 토머신이 거기 사는 것을 보고는 당연히 아무것도 의심하지 않으리라고 단박에 짐작했다."], ["Eustacia felt a wild jealousy of Thomasin on the instant.", "유스테이샤는 그 순간 토머신에 대한 격렬한 질투를 느꼈다."], ["Though Thomasin might possibly have tender sentiments towards another man as yet, how long could they be expected to last when she was shut up here with this interesting and travelled cousin of hers?", "토머신이 아직은 다른 사내에게 다정한 마음을 품고 있을지 몰라도, 이렇게 흥미롭고 견문 넓은 사촌 오빠와 한 집에 갇혀 있는데 그 마음이 얼마나 오래가리라 기대할 수 있겠는가?"], ["There was no knowing what affection might not soon break out between the two, so constantly in each other’s society, and not a distracting object near.", "이렇게 끊임없이 서로의 곁에 있고 마음을 흩뜨릴 대상이 가까이 하나도 없으니, 둘 사이에 머잖아 어떤 애정이 싹틀지 알 수 없는 노릇이었다."], ["Clym’s boyish love for her might have languished, but it might easily be revived again.", "클림의 그녀를 향한 소년 같은 사랑은 시들었을지 몰라도, 그것은 손쉽게 다시 되살아날 수 있었다."]], [["Eustacia was nettled by her own contrivances.", "유스테이샤는 제 꾀에 제가 약이 올랐다."], ["What a sheer waste of herself to be dressed thus while another was shining to advantage!", "다른 여자가 돋보이게 빛나는 동안 자기는 이런 차림을 하고 있다니 이 얼마나 순전한 자기 낭비인가!"], ["Had she known the full effect of the encounter she would have moved heaven and earth to get here in a natural manner.", "이 만남의 온전한 효과를 알았더라면 그녀는 자연스러운 방식으로 여기 오려고 하늘과 땅을 움직였을 것이다."], ["The power of her face all lost, the charm of her emotions all disguised, the fascinations of her coquetry denied existence, nothing but a voice left to her; she had a sense of the doom of Echo.", "얼굴의 힘은 죄다 사라지고, 감정의 매력은 죄다 가려지고, 교태의 매혹은 존재 자체가 부정되고, 그녀에게 남은 것이라곤 목소리뿐이니, 그녀는 에코의 운명을 느꼈다."], ["“Nobody here respects me,” she said.", "「여기선 아무도 날 존중하지 않아.」"], ["She had overlooked the fact that, in coming as a boy among other boys, she would be treated as a boy.", "그녀가 말했다. 다른 소년들 사이에 소년으로 끼어들었으니 소년 대접을 받으리라는 사실을 그녀는 간과하고 있었다."], ["The slight, though of her own causing, and self-explanatory, she was unable to dismiss as unwittingly shown, so sensitive had the situation made her.", "그 무시는, 비록 자기가 자초한 것이고 저절로 설명되는 것이었으나, 무심코 드러난 것이라 치고 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없었으니, 그만큼 그 처지가 그녀를 예민하게 만들었던 것이다."]], [["Women have done much for themselves in histrionic dress.", "여자들은 무대 의상을 입고 스스로를 위해 많은 것을 이루었다."], ["To look far below those who, like a certain fair personator of Polly Peachum early in the last century, and another of Lydia Languish early in this,[1] have won not only love but ducal coronets into the bargain, whole shoals of them have reached to the initial satisfaction of getting love almost whence they would.", "지난 세기 초의 어느 어여쁜 폴리 피첨 연기자며 금세기 초의 또 다른 리디아 랭귀시 연기자처럼, 사랑뿐 아니라 덤으로 공작 부인의 관까지 얻은 이들보다 한참 아래를 보더라도, 그 무리 전체가 거의 자기들이 원하는 데서 사랑을 얻는다는 초보적 만족에는 이르렀다."], ["But the Turkish Knight was denied even the chance of achieving this by the fluttering ribbons which she dared not brush aside.", "그러나 터키 기사는 감히 옆으로 쓸어낼 수 없는 펄럭이는 리본들 때문에 이것을 이룰 기회조차 거부당했다."]], [["[1] Written in 1877.", "[1] 1877년에 쓰임."]], [["Yeobright returned to the room without his cousin.", "요브라이트가 사촌 누이 없이 방으로 돌아왔다."], ["When within two or three feet of Eustacia he stopped, as if again arrested by a thought.", "유스테이샤로부터 두세 걸음 거리에 이르렀을 때 그는 다시 어떤 생각에 붙들린 듯 멈춰 섰다."], ["He was gazing at her.", "그가 그녀를 응시하고 있었다."], ["She looked another way, disconcerted, and wondered how long this purgatory was to last.", "그녀는 어쩔 줄 몰라 다른 데로 시선을 돌리며, 이 연옥이 얼마나 오래갈지 궁금해했다."], ["After lingering a few seconds he passed on again.", "몇 초간 머뭇거린 뒤 그는 다시 지나쳐 갔다."]], [["To court their own discomfiture by love is a common instinct with certain perfervid women.", "사랑으로 제 불편을 자초하는 것은 어떤 격정적인 여자들에게 흔한 본능이다."], ["Conflicting sensations of love, fear, and shame reduced Eustacia to a state of the utmost uneasiness.", "사랑과 두려움과 수치의 상충하는 감각이 유스테이샤를 더없이 불안한 상태로 몰아넣었다."], ["To escape was her great and immediate desire.", "달아나는 것이 그녀의 크고 즉각적인 욕망이었다."], ["The other mummers appeared to be in no hurry to leave; and murmuring to the lad who sat next to her that she preferred waiting for them outside the house, she moved to the door as imperceptibly as possible, opened it, and slipped out.", "다른 가면극 배우들은 떠날 기색이 전혀 없어 보였고, 그녀는 자기 옆에 앉은 젊은이에게 집 밖에서 그들을 기다리는 편이 낫겠다고 속삭이고는, 되도록 눈에 띄지 않게 문으로 다가가 문을 열고 빠져나왔다."]], [["The calm, lone scene reassured her.", "고요하고 외딴 풍경이 그녀를 안심시켰다."], ["She went forward to the palings and leant over them, looking at the moon.", "그녀는 울타리 말뚝으로 나아가 거기 기대어 달을 바라보았다."], ["She had stood thus but a little time when the door again opened.", "그렇게 선 지 얼마 안 되어 문이 다시 열렸다."], ["Expecting to see the remainder of the band Eustacia turned; but no—Clym Yeobright came out as softly as she had done, and closed the door behind him.", "무리의 나머지가 보이리라 기대하며 유스테이샤가 돌아섰으나, 아니었으니—클림 요브라이트가 그녀가 그랬던 만큼이나 살그머니 나와 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다."]], [["He advanced and stood beside her.", "그가 다가와 그녀 곁에 섰다."], ["“I have an odd opinion,”", "「내겐 기묘한 생각이 있어요.」"], ["he said, “and should like to ask you a question.", "그가 말했다. 「그래서 한 가지 물어보고 싶군요."], ["Are you a woman—or am I wrong?”", "당신은 여자입니까—아니면 내가 틀렸나요?」"]], [["“I am a woman.”", "「난 여자예요.」"]], [["His eyes lingered on her with great interest.", "그의 눈이 큰 흥미를 띠고 그녀에게 머물렀다."], ["“Do girls often play as mummers now?", "「요즘은 처녀들이 가면극을 자주 합니까?"], ["They never used to.”", "예전엔 안 그랬는데요.」"]], [["“They don’t now.”", "「요즘도 안 해요.」"]], [["“Why did you?”", "「그런데 왜 했나요?」"]], [["“To get excitement and shake off depression,”", "「흥분을 얻고 우울을 떨치려고요.」"], ["she said in low tones.", "그녀가 낮은 어조로 말했다."]], [["“What depressed you?” “Life.”", "「무엇이 당신을 우울하게 했지요?」"]], [["“That’s a cause of depression a good many have to put up with.”", "「삶이요.」 「그건 많은 이가 견뎌야 하는 우울의 원인이지요.」"]], [["“Yes.”", "「그렇죠.」"]], [["A long silence.", "긴 침묵."], ["“And do you find excitement?”", "「그래서 흥분은 얻나요?」"], ["asked Clym at last.", "마침내 클림이 물었다."]], [["“At this moment, perhaps.”", "「이 순간엔, 아마도요.」"]], [["“Then you are vexed at being discovered?”", "「그럼 들킨 게 분한가요?」"]], [["“Yes; though I thought I might be.”", "「네, 그럴지도 모른다고 생각은 했지만요.」"]], [["“I would gladly have asked you to our party had I known you wished to come.", "「오고 싶어 한 줄 알았더라면 기꺼이 우리 잔치에 청했을 텐데."], ["Have I ever been acquainted with you in my youth?”", "내 어린 시절에 당신과 알고 지낸 적이 있던가요?」"]], [["“Never.”", "「전혀요.」"]], [["“Won’t you come in again, and stay as long as you like?”", "「다시 안으로 들어와 원하는 만큼 있지 않겠어요?」"]], [["“No.", "「아니요."], ["I wish not to be further recognized.”", "더는 알아보는 사람이 없었으면 해요.」"]], [["“Well, you are safe with me.”", "「그렇군요, 내겐 안심해도 됩니다.」"], ["After remaining in thought a minute he added gently, “I will not intrude upon you longer.", "잠시 생각에 잠겨 있다가 그가 부드럽게 덧붙였다. 「더는 당신을 방해하지 않겠어요."], ["It is a strange way of meeting, and I will not ask why I find a cultivated woman playing such a part as this.”", "참 기묘한 만남이군요, 그리고 교양 있는 여자가 어째서 이런 역할을 하는지는 묻지 않겠습니다.」"]], [["She did not volunteer the reason which he seemed to hope for, and he wished her good night, going thence round to the back of the house, where he walked up and down by himself for some time before re-entering.", "그녀는 그가 바라는 듯한 이유를 자청해 내놓지 않았고, 그는 그녀에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하고는 거기서 집 뒤쪽으로 돌아가, 다시 안으로 들어가기 전에 한동안 혼자 거닐었다."]], [["Eustacia, warmed with an inner fire, could not wait for her companions after this.", "내면의 불로 달아오른 유스테이샤는 이런 일이 있고 난 뒤로 동료들을 기다릴 수가 없었다."], ["She flung back the ribbons from her face, opened the gate, and at once struck into the heath.", "그녀는 얼굴에서 리본을 젖히고 대문을 열어 곧장 황야로 들어섰다."], ["She did not hasten along.", "서둘러 걷지는 않았다."], ["Her grandfather was in bed at this hour, for she so frequently walked upon the hills on moonlight nights that he took no notice of her comings and goings, and, enjoying himself in his own way, left her to do likewise.", "이 시각이면 할아버지는 잠자리에 들어 있었으니, 그녀가 달 밝은 밤이면 워낙 자주 언덕을 거닐었기에 할아버지는 그녀가 오고 가는 데 신경 쓰지 않았고, 자기 식대로 즐기면서 그녀도 그러도록 내버려 두었다."], ["A more important subject than that of getting indoors now engrossed her.", "집 안으로 들어가는 일보다 더 중요한 주제가 이제 그녀를 사로잡고 있었다."], ["Yeobright, if he had the least curiosity, would infallibly discover her name. What then?", "요브라이트가 조금이라도 호기심이 있다면 틀림없이 그녀의 이름을 알아낼 터였다."], ["She first felt a sort of exultation at the way in which the adventure had terminated, even though at moments between her exultations she was abashed and blushful.", "그러면 어떻게 되는가? 그녀는 이 모험이 끝난 방식에 대해 처음엔 일종의 환희를 느꼈으니, 환희 사이사이 순간순간 무안하고 얼굴이 붉어지긴 했지만 말이다."], ["Then this consideration recurred to chill her: What was the use of her exploit?", "그러다 이런 생각이 되돌아와 그녀를 서늘하게 했다. 자기 활약이 무슨 소용인가?"], ["She was at present a total stranger to the Yeobright family.", "현재 그녀는 요브라이트 가家에 완전히 낯선 사람이었다."], ["The unreasonable nimbus of romance with which she had encircled that man might be her misery.", "자기가 그 사내를 에워싼 그 터무니없는 낭만의 후광이 도리어 그녀의 비참함이 될지도 몰랐다."], ["How could she allow herself to become so infatuated with a stranger?", "어떻게 자기가 낯선 사람에게 이토록 빠져드는 것을 허락할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["And to fill the cup of her sorrow there would be Thomasin, living day after day in inflammable proximity to him; for she had just learnt that, contrary to her first belief, he was going to stay at home some considerable time.", "게다가 그녀의 슬픔의 잔을 가득 채우려는 듯 토머신이 있어, 날이면 날마다 그와 인화성 있게 가까이 살고 있을 터였으니, 그녀는 처음 믿었던 것과 달리 그가 상당히 오랜 기간 집에 머무를 것임을 방금 알게 되었기 때문이다."]], [["She reached the wicket at Mistover Knap, but before opening it she turned and faced the heath once more.", "그녀는 미스토버 냅의 쪽문에 이르렀으나, 문을 열기 전에 돌아서서 다시 한번 황야를 마주했다."], ["The form of Rainbarrow stood above the hills, and the moon stood above Rainbarrow.", "레인배로의 형상이 언덕들 위로 솟아 있었고, 달이 레인배로 위에 떠 있었다."], ["The air was charged with silence and frost.", "공기는 정적과 서리로 가득했다."], ["The scene reminded Eustacia of a circumstance which till that moment she had totally forgotten.", "그 풍경은 유스테이샤에게 그 순간까지 까맣게 잊고 있던 한 가지 사정을 일깨웠다."], ["She had promised to meet Wildeve by the Barrow this very night at eight, to give a final answer to his pleading for an elopement.", "그녀는 바로 오늘 밤 여덟 시에 레인배로 옆에서 와일디브를 만나, 달아나자는 그의 간청에 마지막 대답을 주기로 약속해 두었던 것이다."]], [["She herself had fixed the evening and the hour.", "그 저녁과 시각을 정한 것은 그녀 자신이었다."], ["He had probably come to the spot, waited there in the cold, and been greatly disappointed.", "그는 아마 그 자리에 와서 추위 속에 거기 기다리다가 크게 실망했을 것이다."], ["“Well, so much the better—it did not hurt him,”", "「뭐, 차라리 잘됐어—그가 다칠 일은 없었으니까.」"], ["she said serenely.", "그녀가 태연히 말했다."], ["Wildeve had at present the rayless outline of the sun through smoked glass, and she could say such things as that with the greatest facility.", "와일디브는 지금 그을린 유리를 통해 본 태양의 광선 없는 윤곽 같은 처지였고, 그녀는 그런 말을 더없이 수월하게 할 수 있었다."]], [["She remained deeply pondering; and Thomasin’s winning manner towards her cousin arose again upon Eustacia’s mind.", "그녀는 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 머물렀고, 사촌 오빠를 향한 토머신의 사람을 끄는 태도가 다시 유스테이샤의 마음에 떠올랐다."]], [["“O that she had been married to Damon before this!”", "「아, 그 애가 이 일 전에 데이먼과 결혼했더라면!」"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“And she would if it hadn’t been for me!", "「나만 아니었으면 그랬을 텐데!"], ["If I had only known—if I had only known!”", "내가 알기만 했더라면—내가 알기만 했더라면!」"]], [["Eustacia once more lifted her deep stormy eyes to the moonlight, and, sighing that tragic sigh of hers which was so much like a shudder, entered the shadow of the roof. She threw off her trappings in the outhouse, rolled them up, and went indoors to her chamber.", "유스테이샤는 다시 한번 깊고 폭풍 같은 두 눈을 달빛으로 들어 올리고는, 몸서리에 그토록 가까운 그 비극적인 한숨을 내쉬며 지붕의 그늘 안으로 들어섰다."], ["VII. A Coalition between Beauty and Oddness", "헛간에서 의상을 벗어 둘둘 말고는, 안으로 들어가 제 방으로 갔다."]], [["The old captain’s prevailing indifference to his granddaughter’s movements left her free as a bird to follow her own courses; but it so happened that he did take upon himself the next morning to ask her why she had walked out so late.", "손녀의 행동거지에 대한 노 선장의 한결같은 무관심은 그녀를 새처럼 자유롭게 제 갈 길을 가도록 내버려 두었으나, 마침 이튿날 아침 그가 직접 나서서 그녀에게 왜 그렇게 늦게까지 밖에 나가 있었느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Only in search of events, Grandfather,”", "「그냥 사건거리를 찾으려고요, 할아버지.」"], ["she said, looking out of the window with that drowsy latency of manner which discovered so much force behind it whenever the trigger was pressed.", "방아쇠가 당겨질 때마다 그 뒤에 그토록 큰 힘이 도사리고 있음을 드러내는 그 졸린 듯한 태도로 창밖을 내다보며 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Search of events—one would think you were one of the bucks I knew at one-and-twenty.”", "「사건거리를 찾는다—꼭 내가 스물한 살에 알던 한량들 중 하나 같구나.」"]], [["“It is lonely here.”", "「여긴 외로워요.」"]], [["“So much the better.", "「그게 더 낫지."], ["If I were living in a town my whole time would be taken up in looking after you.", "내가 읍내에 산다면 내 모든 시간을 너 챙기는 데 다 써야 할 거다."], ["I fully expected you would have been home when I returned from the Woman.”", "내가 ‘우먼’에서 돌아왔을 때 네가 집에 있으리라 잔뜩 기대했는데.」"]], [["“I won’t conceal what I did.", "「제가 한 일을 숨기진 않을게요."], ["I wanted an adventure, and I went with the mummers.", "모험을 원했고, 가면극 배우들과 갔어요."], ["I played the part of the Turkish Knight.”", "터키 기사 역을 했지요.」"]], [["“No, never?", "「설마, 그럴 리가?"], ["Ha, ha!", "하하!"], ["Good gad!", "이런 세상에!"], ["I didn’t expect it of you, Eustacia.”", "너한테 그런 걸 기대하진 않았다, 유스테이샤.」"]], [["“It was my first performance, and it certainly will be my last.", "「제 첫 공연이었고, 분명 마지막일 거예요."], ["Now I have told you—and remember it is a secret.”", "자, 말씀드렸으니—기억하세요, 이건 비밀이에요.」"]], [["“Of course.", "「물론이지."], ["But, Eustacia, you never did—ha!", "하지만, 유스테이샤, 네가 설마—하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["Dammy, how ’twould have pleased me forty years ago!", "빌어먹을, 사십 년 전이었으면 얼마나 기뻤을꼬!"], ["But remember, no more of it, my girl.", "하지만 명심해라, 다신 안 된다, 얘야."], ["You may walk on the heath night or day, as you choose, so that you don’t bother me; but no figuring in breeches again.”", "황야는 밤이든 낮이든 네 맘대로 거닐어도 좋아, 날 귀찮게만 안 하면. 하지만 다신 바지 차림으로 나서는 건 안 돼.」"]], [["“You need have no fear for me, Grandpapa.”", "「저 때문에 걱정하실 일 없어요, 할아버지.」"]], [["Here the conversation ceased, Eustacia’s moral training never exceeding in severity a dialogue of this sort, which, if it ever became profitable to good works, would be a result not dear at the price.", "여기서 대화가 그쳤으니, 유스테이샤의 도덕 교육은 이런 식의 대화보다 더 엄격한 적이 없었고, 이것이 혹여 선행에 보탬이 되는 것으로 드러난다면 그 값에 비해 결코 비싸지 않은 결과이리라."], ["But her thoughts soon strayed far from her own personality; and, full of a passionate and indescribable solicitude for one to whom she was not even a name, she went forth into the amplitude of tanned wild around her, restless as Ahasuerus the Jew.", "그러나 그녀의 생각은 곧 제 자신에 관한 것에서 멀리 벗어났으니, 자기는 이름조차 아닌 한 사람을 향한 격정적이고 형언할 수 없는 염려로 가득한 채, 그녀는 떠도는 유대인 아하스에로처럼 안절부절못하며 자기를 둘러싼 그을린 황야의 광활함 속으로 나아갔다."], ["She was about half a mile from her residence when she beheld a sinister redness arising from a ravine a little way in advance—dull and lurid like a flame in sunlight and she guessed it to signify Diggory Venn.", "거처에서 반 마일쯤 떨어졌을 때 그녀는 조금 앞쪽 협곡에서 솟아오르는 불길한 붉은빛을 보았으니—햇빛 속 불꽃처럼 흐릿하고 음산한 그것을 그녀는 디고리 벤을 뜻한다고 짐작했다."]], [["When the farmers who had wished to buy in a new stock of reddle during the last month had inquired where Venn was to be found, people replied, “On Egdon Heath.”", "지난 한 달 사이에 황토 재고를 새로 사들이고 싶었던 농부들이 벤을 어디서 찾을 수 있느냐고 물으면 사람들은 「에그던 황야에서.」라고 대답했다."], ["Day after day the answer was the same.", "날이면 날마다 대답은 똑같았다."], ["Now, since Egdon was populated with heath-croppers and furze-cutters rather than with sheep and shepherds, and the downs where most of the latter were to be found lay some to the north, some to the west of Egdon, his reason for camping about there like Israel in Zin was not apparent.", "그런데 에그던에는 양과 양치기보다는 황야 풀을 뜯는 망아지와 가시금작화를 베는 이들이 살고 있었으며, 후자가 대부분 모여 있는 구릉지는 더러는 에그던 북쪽에, 더러는 서쪽에 놓여 있었으니, 그가 이스라엘이 신 광야에서 그러했듯 이 부근에서 야영하는 이유는 분명치 않았다."], ["The position was central and occasionally desirable.", "그 위치는 중심에 있어 이따금 탐날 만했다."], ["But the sale of reddle was not Diggory’s primary object in remaining on the heath, particularly at so late a period of the year, when most travellers of his class had gone into winter quarters.", "그러나 황토 판매가 디고리가 황야에 남아 있는 일차적 목적은 아니었으니, 특히 그와 같은 부류의 떠돌이 대부분이 겨울 거처로 들어간 이렇게 늦은 철에는 더욱 그러했다."]], [["Eustacia looked at the lonely man.", "유스테이샤는 그 외로운 사내를 바라보았다."], ["Wildeve had told her at their last meeting that Venn had been thrust forward by Mrs. Yeobright as one ready and anxious to take his place as Thomasin’s betrothed.", "와일디브는 지난번 만남에서, 토머신의 약혼자로서 자기 자리를 기꺼이 간절히 차지할 사람으로 요브라이트 부인이 벤을 내세웠다고 그녀에게 말했었다."], ["His figure was perfect, his face young and well outlined, his eye bright, his intelligence keen, and his position one which he could readily better if he chose.", "그의 풍채는 완벽했고, 얼굴은 젊고 윤곽이 또렷했으며, 눈은 빛났고, 머리는 예리했으며, 처지는 마음만 먹으면 쉽사리 나아질 수 있는 것이었다."], ["But in spite of possibilities it was not likely that Thomasin would accept this Ishmaelitish creature while she had a cousin like Yeobright at her elbow, and Wildeve at the same time not absolutely indifferent.", "그러나 가능성에도 불구하고, 요브라이트 같은 사촌 오빠가 곁에 있고 동시에 와일디브도 전혀 무관심하지는 않은 마당에, 토머신이 이 이스마엘 같은 존재를 받아들일 성싶지는 않았다."], ["Eustacia was not long in guessing that poor Mrs. Yeobright, in her anxiety for her niece’s future, had mentioned this lover to stimulate the zeal of the other.", "유스테이샤는 가엾은 요브라이트 부인이 조카딸의 장래를 염려한 나머지 다른 쪽의 열성을 자극하려고 이 구혼자를 언급한 것임을 짐작하는 데 오래 걸리지 않았다."], ["Eustacia was on the side of the Yeobrights now, and entered into the spirit of the aunt’s desire.", "유스테이샤는 이제 요브라이트 편이었고, 그 이모의 바람의 정신에 공감했다."]], [["“Good morning, miss,”", "「안녕하시오, 아가씨.」"], ["said the reddleman, taking off his cap of hareskin, and apparently bearing her no ill-will from recollection of their last meeting.", "황토장이가 토끼 가죽 모자를 벗으며 말했는데, 지난번 만남을 떠올리고도 그녀에게 별 악감정을 품은 것 같지는 않았다."]], [["“Good morning, reddleman,”", "「안녕하세요, 황토장이.」"], ["she said, hardly troubling to lift her heavily shaded eyes to his.", "그녀가 짙게 그늘진 눈을 그에게 들어 올리는 수고조차 거의 하지 않으며 말했다."], ["“I did not know you were so near.", "「이렇게 가까이 있는 줄 몰랐네요."], ["Is your van here too?”", "마차도 여기 있나요?」"]], [["Venn moved his elbow towards a hollow in which a dense brake of purple-stemmed brambles had grown to such vast dimensions as almost to form a dell.", "벤이 팔꿈치를 한 움푹한 곳 쪽으로 움직였는데, 거기엔 자줏빛 줄기의 가시덤불이 빽빽이 우거져 어찌나 엄청난 규모로 자랐는지 거의 작은 골짜기를 이룰 정도였다."], ["Brambles, though churlish when handled, are kindly shelter in early winter, being the latest of the deciduous bushes to lose their leaves.", "가시덤불은 손을 대면 심술궂으나, 초겨울에는 다정한 피난처가 되어 주니, 잎을 떨구는 관목들 가운데 가장 늦게 잎을 잃기 때문이다."]], [["The roof and chimney of Venn’s caravan showed behind the tracery and tangles of the brake.", "덤불의 얽힌 가지와 엉킨 가지 뒤로 벤의 포장마차 지붕과 굴뚝이 보였다."]], [["“You remain near this part?”", "「이 근처에 머무르나요?」"], ["she asked with more interest.", "그녀가 한층 흥미를 띠고 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I have business here.”", "「예, 여기 볼일이 있어서요.」"]], [["“Not altogether the selling of reddle?”", "「황토 파는 일이 다는 아니고요?」"]], [["“It has nothing to do with that.”", "「그것하곤 상관없는 일이오.」"]], [["“It has to do with Miss Yeobright?”", "「요브라이트 양과 관련된 일인가요?」"]], [["Her face seemed to ask for an armed peace, and he therefore said frankly, “Yes, miss; it is on account of her.”", "그녀의 얼굴이 무장한 평화를 청하는 듯 보였기에, 그는 솔직하게 말했다. 「예, 아가씨. 그 사람 때문이오.」"]], [["“On account of your approaching marriage with her?”", "「그 사람과 곧 있을 결혼 때문이에요?」"]], [["Venn flushed through his stain.", "벤은 칠한 얼굴 너머로 붉어졌다."], ["“Don’t make sport of me, Miss Vye,”", "「날 놀리지 마시오, 바이 양.」"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“It isn’t true?”", "「사실이 아니에요?」"]], [["“Certainly not.”", "「물론 아니오.」"]], [["She was thus convinced that the reddleman was a mere _pis aller_ in Mrs. Yeobright’s mind; one, moreover, who had not even been informed of his promotion to that lowly standing.", "그리하여 그녀는 황토장이가 요브라이트 부인의 마음속에서 한낱 차선책일 뿐이며, 게다가 그 보잘것없는 지위로 승격되었다는 사실조차 통지받지 못한 사람임을 확신하게 되었다."], ["“It was a mere notion of mine,”", "「그냥 제 생각이었어요.」"], ["she said quietly; and was about to pass by without further speech, when, looking round to the right, she saw a painfully well-known figure serpentining upwards by one of the little paths which led to the top where she stood.", "그녀가 조용히 말하고는, 더 말을 잇지 않고 지나치려던 참에, 오른쪽을 돌아보다가 그녀가 선 꼭대기로 이어지는 작은 오솔길 중 하나를 따라 구불구불 올라오는, 괴롭도록 낯익은 형상을 보았다."], ["Owing to the necessary windings of his course his back was at present towards them.", "길이 어쩔 수 없이 굽이지는 탓에 그의 등이 지금은 그들 쪽을 향해 있었다."], ["She glanced quickly round; to escape that man there was only one way.", "그녀는 재빨리 둘러보았으니, 저 사내를 피할 길은 단 하나뿐이었다."], ["Turning to Venn, she said, “Would you allow me to rest a few minutes in your van?", "벤을 돌아보며 그녀가 말했다. 「마차에서 몇 분 쉬게 해 주시겠어요?"], ["The banks are damp for sitting on.”", "둑은 앉기엔 축축해서요.」"]], [["“Certainly, miss; I’ll make a place for you.”", "「그럼요, 아가씨. 자리를 마련해 드리지요.」"]], [["She followed him behind the dell of brambles to his wheeled dwelling into which Venn mounted, placing the three-legged stool just within the door.", "그녀는 가시덤불 골짜기 뒤로 그를 따라 그의 바퀴 달린 거처로 갔고, 벤이 올라가 세 발 의자를 문 바로 안쪽에 놓았다."]], [["“That is the best I can do for you,”", "「이게 내가 해 드릴 수 있는 최선이오.」"], ["he said, stepping down and retiring to the path, where he resumed the smoking of his pipe as he walked up and down.", "그가 내려와 오솔길로 물러나며 말했고, 거기서 그는 오가며 다시 파이프를 피웠다."]], [["Eustacia bounded into the vehicle and sat on the stool, ensconced from view on the side towards the trackway.", "유스테이샤는 마차 안으로 뛰어올라 의자에 앉았으니, 길 쪽에서는 보이지 않게 가려져 있었다."], ["Soon she heard the brushing of other feet than the reddleman’s, a not very friendly “Good day” uttered by two men in passing each other, and then the dwindling of the foot-fall of one of them in a direction onwards.", "곧 그녀는 황토장이 것이 아닌 다른 발소리가 스치는 것을, 두 사내가 서로를 지나치며 내뱉는 그리 다정하지 않은 「안녕하시오.」를, 그러고는 그중 하나의 발소리가 앞쪽 방향으로 차츰 잦아드는 것을 들었다."], ["Eustacia stretched her neck forward till she caught a glimpse of a receding back and shoulders; and she felt a wretched twinge of misery, she knew not why.", "유스테이샤는 멀어져 가는 등과 어깨를 언뜻 볼 수 있을 때까지 목을 앞으로 길게 뺐고, 까닭 모를 비참한 아픔의 욱신거림을 느꼈다."], ["It was the sickening feeling which, if the changed heart has any generosity at all in its composition, accompanies the sudden sight of a once-loved one who is beloved no more.", "그것은 변해 버린 마음에 조금이라도 너그러움이 깃들어 있다면, 한때 사랑했으나 더는 사랑하지 않는 이를 문득 보게 될 때 따라오는 그 메스꺼운 느낌이었다."]], [["When Eustacia descended to proceed on her way the reddleman came near.", "유스테이샤가 가던 길을 계속 가려고 내려왔을 때 황토장이가 가까이 왔다."], ["“That was Mr. Wildeve who passed, miss,”", "「방금 지나간 게 와일디브 씨였소, 아가씨.」"], ["he said slowly, and expressed by his face that he expected her to feel vexed at having been sitting unseen.", "그가 천천히 말했고, 그녀가 들키지 않고 앉아 있었던 데 분해할 것이라 예상한다는 표정을 얼굴로 드러냈다."]], [["“Yes, I saw him coming up the hill,”", "「네, 그가 언덕을 올라오는 걸 봤어요.」"], ["replied Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 대답했다."], ["“Why should you tell me that?”", "「그걸 왜 나한테 말하죠?」"], ["It was a bold question, considering the reddleman’s knowledge of her past love; but her undemonstrative manner had power to repress the opinions of those she treated as remote from her.", "황토장이가 그녀의 지난 사랑을 알고 있음을 생각하면 대담한 질문이었으나, 그녀의 감정을 드러내지 않는 태도에는 자기가 멀게 대하는 이들의 견해를 억누르는 힘이 있었다."]], [["“I am glad to hear that you can ask it,”", "「그걸 물을 수 있다니 들으니 기쁘오.」"], ["said the reddleman bluntly.", "황토장이가 퉁명스럽게 말했다."], ["“And, now I think of it, it agrees with what I saw last night.”", "「그리고 이제 생각해 보니, 그게 어젯밤 내가 본 거랑 들어맞는군.」"]], [["“Ah—what was that?”", "「아—그게 뭔데요?」"], ["Eustacia wished to leave him, but wished to know.", "유스테이샤는 그를 떠나고 싶었으나, 알고도 싶었다."]], [["“Mr. Wildeve stayed at Rainbarrow a long time waiting for a lady who didn’t come.”", "「와일디브 씨가 레인배로에서 오지 않는 숙녀를 한참 기다렸소.」"]], [["“You waited too, it seems?”", "「당신도 기다렸나 보네요?」"]], [["“Yes, I always do.", "「예, 난 늘 그러오."], ["I was glad to see him disappointed.", "그가 실망하는 걸 보니 기뻤소."], ["He will be there again tonight.”", "그는 오늘 밤 또 거기 올 거요.」"]], [["“To be again disappointed.", "「또 실망하려고요."], ["The truth is, reddleman, that that lady, so far from wishing to stand in the way of Thomasin’s marriage with Mr. Wildeve, would be very glad to promote it.”", "사실은요, 황토장이, 그 숙녀는 토머신과 와일디브 씨의 결혼을 가로막기는커녕 오히려 그걸 아주 기꺼이 거들고 싶어 한답니다.」"]], [["Venn felt much astonishment at this avowal, though he did not show it clearly; that exhibition may greet remarks which are one remove from expectation, but it is usually withheld in complicated cases of two removes and upwards.", "벤은 이 고백에 적잖이 놀랐으나, 그것을 분명히 드러내지는 않았으니, 그런 표현은 예상에서 한 발짝 벗어난 말에는 따라올 수 있어도, 두 발짝 이상 벗어난 복잡한 경우엔 대개 억제되는 법이다. 「그렇군요, 아가씨.」"], ["“Indeed, miss,” he replied.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["“How do you know that Mr. Wildeve will come to Rainbarrow again tonight?”", "「와일디브 씨가 오늘 밤 또 레인배로에 오리라는 걸 어떻게 알죠?」"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“I heard him say to himself that he would.", "「혼잣말로 그러겠다고 하는 걸 들었소."], ["He’s in a regular temper.”", "단단히 성이 나 있더군.」"]], [["Eustacia looked for a moment what she felt, and she murmured, lifting her deep dark eyes anxiously to his, “I wish I knew what to do.", "유스테이샤는 한순간 자기가 느끼는 바를 그대로 내비치고는, 깊고 검은 눈을 근심스레 그에게 들어 올리며 중얼거렸다. 「어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["I don’t want to be uncivil to him; but I don’t wish to see him again; and I have some few little things to return to him.”", "그에게 무례하게 굴고 싶진 않지만, 그를 다시 보고 싶지도 않고, 또 그에게 돌려줄 자잘한 물건이 몇 개 있어요.」"]], [["“If you choose to send ’em by me, miss, and a note to tell him that you wish to say no more to him, I’ll take it for you quite privately.", "「원하시면 그걸 나한테 맡기시오, 아가씨, 그리고 더는 그에게 할 말이 없다고 알리는 쪽지도요. 아주 은밀하게 가져다드리리다."], ["That would be the most straightforward way of letting him know your mind.”", "그게 그에게 아가씨 마음을 알리는 가장 솔직한 방법일 거요.」"]], [["“Very well,”", "「좋아요.」"], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 말했다."], ["“Come towards my house, and I will bring it out to you.”", "「우리 집 쪽으로 와요, 가지고 나올 테니.」"]], [["She went on, and as the path was an infinitely small parting in the shaggy locks of the heath, the reddleman followed exactly in her trail.", "그녀가 앞장섰고, 오솔길은 황야의 텁수룩한 더부룩함 사이로 난 한없이 작은 가르마였기에, 황토장이는 그녀의 자취를 정확히 따라갔다."], ["She saw from a distance that the captain was on the bank sweeping the horizon with his telescope; and bidding Venn to wait where he stood she entered the house alone.", "그녀는 멀리서 선장이 둑 위에서 망원경으로 지평선을 훑고 있는 것을 보았고, 벤에게 선 자리에서 기다리라 이르고는 혼자 집으로 들어갔다."]], [["In ten minutes she returned with a parcel and a note, and said, in placing them in his hand, “Why are you so ready to take these for me?”", "십 분 뒤 그녀는 꾸러미와 쪽지를 들고 돌아와, 그것들을 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 말했다. 「왜 이걸 나 대신 이렇게 선뜻 맡아 주는 거죠?」"]], [["“Can you ask that?”", "「그걸 물을 수 있소?」"]], [["“I suppose you think to serve Thomasin in some way by it.", "「당신은 이걸로 어떻게든 토머신을 도울 생각인가 봐요."], ["Are you as anxious as ever to help on her marriage?”", "여전히 그녀의 결혼을 돕고 싶어 그렇게 안달인가요?」"]], [["Venn was a little moved.", "벤은 조금 마음이 움직였다."], ["“I would sooner have married her myself,”", "「차라리 내가 그 사람과 결혼했으면 했소.」"], ["he said in a low voice.", "그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“But what I feel is that if she cannot be happy without him I will do my duty in helping her to get him, as a man ought.”", "「하지만 내가 느끼는 건, 그 사람이 그자 없이는 행복할 수 없다면 사내가 마땅히 그래야 하듯 그 사람이 그자를 얻도록 돕는 내 도리를 다하겠다는 거요.」"]], [["Eustacia looked curiously at the singular man who spoke thus.", "유스테이샤는 이렇게 말하는 그 별난 사내를 신기한 듯 바라보았다."], ["What a strange sort of love, to be entirely free from that quality of selfishness which is frequently the chief constituent of the passion, and sometimes its only one!", "정념의 주된 구성 요소이자 때로는 그 유일한 요소이기도 한 그 이기심이라는 성질에서 완전히 자유로운 사랑이라니, 이 얼마나 기묘한 종류의 사랑인가!"], ["The reddleman’s disinterestedness was so well deserving of respect that it overshot respect by being barely comprehended; and she almost thought it absurd.", "황토장이의 사심 없음은 존중받을 만한 것이 어찌나 충분한지 도무지 이해되지 않을 만큼 존중을 넘어섰고, 그녀는 그것을 거의 어처구니없다고까지 여겼다."]], [["“Then we are both of one mind at last,”", "「그럼 우리 둘은 마침내 한마음이군요.」"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "「그렇소.」"], ["replied Venn gloomily.", "벤이 침울하게 대답했다."], ["“But if you would tell me, miss, why you take such an interest in her, I should be easier.", "「하지만 아가씨, 어째서 그 사람한테 그런 관심을 두는지 말해 주면 마음이 좀 놓이겠소."], ["It is so sudden and strange.”", "너무 갑작스럽고 이상해서요.」"]], [["Eustacia appeared at a loss.", "유스테이샤는 어찌할 바를 모르는 듯했다."], ["“I cannot tell you that, reddleman,”", "「그건 말해 줄 수 없어요, 황토장이.」"], ["she said coldly.", "그녀가 차갑게 말했다."]], [["Venn said no more.", "벤은 더 말하지 않았다."], ["He pocketed the letter, and, bowing to Eustacia, went away.", "그는 편지를 주머니에 넣고, 유스테이샤에게 절하고는 떠나갔다."]], [["Rainbarrow had again become blended with night when Wildeve ascended the long acclivity at its base.", "와일디브가 레인배로 기슭의 긴 비탈을 올랐을 때 그곳은 다시 밤과 한데 섞여 있었다."], ["On his reaching the top a shape grew up from the earth immediately behind him.", "그가 꼭대기에 이르자 그의 바로 등 뒤 땅에서 한 형상이 솟아올랐다."], ["It was that of Eustacia’s emissary.", "그것은 유스테이샤의 사자使者의 것이었다."], ["He slapped Wildeve on the shoulder.", "그가 와일디브의 어깨를 탁 쳤다."], ["The feverish young inn-keeper and ex-engineer started like Satan at the touch of Ithuriel’s spear.", "열병에 들뜬 젊은 여관 주인이자 전직 기사技士는 이투리엘의 창에 닿은 사탄처럼 흠칫했다."]], [["“The meeting is always at eight o’clock, at this place,”", "「약속은 늘 여덟 시, 이 자리지요.」 벤이 말했다."], ["said Venn, “and here we are—we three.”", "「그리고 우리가 여기 있군요—우리 셋이.」"]], [["“We three?”", "「우리 셋?」"], ["said Wildeve, looking quickly round.", "와일디브가 재빨리 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["“Yes; you, and I, and she.", "「예, 당신과 나, 그리고 그 여자요."], ["This is she.”", "이게 그 여자요.」"], ["He held up the letter and parcel.", "그가 편지와 꾸러미를 들어 보였다."]], [["Wildeve took them wonderingly.", "와일디브가 의아해하며 그것들을 받았다."], ["“I don’t quite see what this means,”", "「이게 무슨 뜻인지 통 모르겠군.」"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“How do you come here?", "「어떻게 여기 온 거요?"], ["There must be some mistake.”", "뭔가 착오가 있는 게 분명해.」"]], [["“It will be cleared from your mind when you have read the letter.", "「편지를 읽으면 마음에서 풀릴 거요."], ["Lanterns for one.”", "등불 하나 켭시다.」"], ["The reddleman struck a light, kindled an inch of tallow-candle which he had brought, and sheltered it with his cap.", "황토장이가 불을 켜, 자기가 가져온 우지 양초 한 토막에 불을 붙이고 모자로 그것을 가렸다."]], [["“Who are you?”", "「당신 누구요?」"], ["said Wildeve, discerning by the candle-light an obscure rubicundity of person in his companion.", "와일디브가 촛불 빛으로 동행의 흐릿하게 불그스름한 몰골을 분간하며 말했다."], ["“You are the reddleman I saw on the hill this morning—why, you are the man who——”", "「당신은 오늘 아침 언덕에서 본 그 황토장이로군—아니, 당신은 바로 그 사내가——」"]], [["“Please read the letter.”", "「부디 편지나 읽으시오.」"]], [["“If you had come from the other one I shouldn’t have been surprised,”", "「다른 여자한테서 왔다면 놀라지도 않았을 텐데.」"], ["murmured Wildeve as he opened the letter and read.", "와일디브가 편지를 펼쳐 읽으며 중얼거렸다. 그의 얼굴이 심각해졌다."], ["His face grew serious.", "「와일디브 씨께."]], [["“After some thought I have decided once and for all that we must hold no further communication.", "여러 번 생각한 끝에, 우리가 더는 어떤 연락도 주고받아선 안 된다고 단번에 결심했습니다."], ["The more I consider the matter the more I am convinced that there must be an end to our acquaintance.", "이 문제를 생각하면 할수록 우리 교제가 끝나야 한다는 확신이 더 굳어집니다."], ["Had you been uniformly faithful to me throughout these two years you might now have some ground for accusing me of heartlessness; but if you calmly consider what I bore during the period of your desertion, and how I passively put up with your courtship of another without once interfering, you will, I think, own that I have a right to consult my own feelings when you come back to me again.", "이 두 해 동안 당신이 한결같이 내게 충실했더라면 이제 나를 무정하다 비난할 근거가 좀 있을지도 모르겠습니다만, 당신이 나를 버린 동안 내가 무엇을 견뎠는지, 그리고 당신이 다른 여자에게 구애하는 것을 내가 한 번도 끼어들지 않고 묵묵히 참아 냈는지를 차분히 헤아려 본다면, 당신이 내게 다시 돌아왔을 때 내가 내 감정을 따를 권리가 있음을 인정하시리라 생각합니다."], ["That these are not what they were towards you may, perhaps, be a fault in me, but it is one which you can scarcely reproach me for when you remember how you left me for Thomasin.", "그 감정이 예전 당신을 향하던 것과 다른 것은, 어쩌면 내 잘못일지 모르나, 당신이 토머신을 택해 나를 떠난 일을 떠올린다면 도무지 나를 책망할 수 없는 잘못입니다."], ["The little articles you gave me in the early part of our friendship are returned by the bearer of this letter.", "우리 우정의 초기에 당신이 내게 준 자잘한 물건들은 이 편지를 전하는 이 편에 돌려보냅니다."], ["They should rightly have been sent back when I first heard of your engagement to her.", "마땅히 당신이 그녀와 약혼했다는 소식을 내가 처음 들었을 때 돌려보냈어야 할 것들입니다."]], [["By the time that Wildeve reached her name the blankness with which he had read the first half of the letter intensified to mortification.", "유스테이샤.」 와일디브가 그녀의 이름에 다다랐을 즈음, 편지 전반부를 읽으며 떠올랐던 그 막막함이 굴욕으로 짙어졌다."], ["“I am made a great fool of, one way and another,”", "「이래저래 내가 단단히 바보가 됐군.」"], ["he said pettishly.", "그가 부루퉁하게 말했다."], ["“Do you know what is in this letter?”", "「이 편지에 뭐가 들었는지 아시오?」"]], [["The reddleman hummed a tune.", "황토장이가 곡조를 흥얼거렸다."]], [["“Can’t you answer me?”", "「대답을 못 하겠소?」"], ["asked Wildeve warmly.", "와일디브가 발끈하며 물었다."]], [["“Ru-um-tum-tum,”", "「루-움-툼-툼.」"], ["sang the reddleman.", "황토장이가 노래했다."]], [["Wildeve stood looking on the ground beside Venn’s feet, till he allowed his eyes to travel upwards over Diggory’s form, as illuminated by the candle, to his head and face.", "와일디브는 벤의 발치 땅바닥을 바라보며 서 있다가, 촛불에 비친 디고리의 몸을 따라 머리와 얼굴까지 시선을 위로 옮겼다."], ["“Ha-ha!", "「하-하!"], ["Well, I suppose I deserve it, considering how I have played with them both,”", "뭐, 두 사람을 다 가지고 놀았으니 이 정도는 받아 마땅하겠지.」"], ["he said at last, as much to himself as to Venn.", "그가 마침내, 벤에게라기보다 자기 자신에게 하듯 말했다."], ["“But of all the odd things that ever I knew, the oddest is that you should so run counter to your own interests as to bring this to me.”", "「하지만 내가 여태 알던 온갖 기이한 일 중에서도 가장 기이한 건, 당신이 제 잇속에 이토록 어긋나게 이걸 나한테 가져왔다는 거요.」"]], [["“My interests?”", "「내 잇속?」"]], [["“Certainly.", "「그렇소."], ["’Twas your interest not to do anything which would send me courting Thomasin again, now she has accepted you—or something like it.", "그 여자가 당신을 받아들였으니—혹은 그 비슷하게 됐으니, 내가 토머신한테 다시 구애하게 만들 짓은 안 하는 게 당신 잇속이었지."], ["Mrs. Yeobright says you are to marry her.", "요브라이트 부인은 당신이 그녀와 결혼할 거라던데."], ["’Tisn’t true, then?”", "그럼 그게 사실이 아니오?」"]], [["“Good Lord!", "「세상에!"], ["I heard of this before, but didn’t believe it.", "이 얘긴 전에도 들었지만 안 믿었소."], ["When did she say so?”", "그 사람이 언제 그랬소?」"]], [["Wildeve began humming as the reddleman had done.", "와일디브가 황토장이가 그랬듯이 흥얼거리기 시작했다."]], [["“I don’t believe it now,”", "「이젠 안 믿소.」"], ["cried Venn.", "벤이 외쳤다."]], [["“Ru-um-tum-tum,”", "「루-움-툼-툼.」"], ["sang Wildeve.", "와일디브가 노래했다."]], [["“O Lord—how we can imitate!”", "「오 이런—우리도 흉내는 잘 내는군!」"], ["said Venn contemptuously.", "벤이 경멸하듯 말했다."], ["“I’ll have this out.", "「이건 끝장을 봐야겠소."], ["I’ll go straight to her.”", "곧장 그 사람한테 가겠소.」"]], [["Diggory withdrew with an emphatic step, Wildeve’s eye passing over his form in withering derision, as if he were no more than a heath-cropper.", "디고리가 단호한 발걸음으로 물러갔고, 와일디브의 눈은 그를 마치 황야 풀을 뜯는 망아지보다 나을 게 없는 양 매섭게 조롱하며 그의 몸을 훑었다."], ["When the reddleman’s figure could no longer be seen, Wildeve himself descended and plunged into the rayless hollow of the vale.", "황토장이의 형상이 더는 보이지 않게 되자 와일디브 자신도 내려가 골짜기의 빛 없는 움푹한 곳으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["To lose the two women—he who had been the well-beloved of both—was too ironical an issue to be endured.", "두 여자를 다 잃다니—양쪽 모두에게 사랑받던 자가—견디기엔 너무도 얄궂은 결말이었다."], ["He could only decently save himself by Thomasin; and once he became her husband, Eustacia’s repentance, he thought, would set in for a long and bitter term.", "그가 그나마 체면을 차려 자신을 구할 길은 토머신뿐이었고, 일단 그가 그녀의 남편이 되고 나면 유스테이샤의 후회가 길고 쓰라린 기간 동안 시작되리라고 그는 생각했다."], ["It was no wonder that Wildeve, ignorant of the new man at the back of the scene, should have supposed Eustacia to be playing a part.", "무대 뒤의 새 인물을 모르는 와일디브가 유스테이샤를 연기를 하고 있다고 여긴 것은 무리가 아니었다."], ["To believe that the letter was not the result of some momentary pique, to infer that she really gave him up to Thomasin, would have required previous knowledge of her transfiguration by that man’s influence.", "그 편지가 순간의 토라짐의 산물이 아니라고 믿고, 그녀가 정말로 그를 토머신에게 넘긴다고 추론하려면, 그 사내의 영향으로 그녀가 변모했다는 사전 지식이 필요했을 것이다."], ["Who was to know that she had grown generous in the greediness of a new passion, that in coveting one cousin she was dealing liberally with another, that in her eagerness to appropriate she gave way?", "그녀가 새로운 정념의 탐욕 속에서 너그러워졌음을, 한 사촌을 탐내면서 다른 한쪽에 후하게 굴고 있음을, 차지하려는 열망 속에서 양보하고 있음을 누가 알 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["Full of this resolve to marry in haste, and wring the heart of the proud girl, Wildeve went his way.", "서둘러 결혼해 그 도도한 처녀의 가슴을 비틀어 놓겠다는 이 결심으로 가득 차서 와일디브는 제 갈 길을 갔다."]], [["Meanwhile Diggory Venn had returned to his van, where he stood looking thoughtfully into the stove.", "한편 디고리 벤은 마차로 돌아와, 거기 서서 난로 안을 골똘히 들여다보았다."], ["A new vista was opened up to him.", "새로운 전망이 그에게 열린 셈이었다."], ["But, however promising Mrs. Yeobright’s views of him might be as a candidate for her niece’s hand, one condition was indispensable to the favour of Thomasin herself, and that was a renunciation of his present wild mode of life.", "그러나 조카딸의 손을 두고 요브라이트 부인이 그를 후보로 보는 견해가 아무리 유망하다 한들, 토머신 본인의 호의를 얻으려면 한 가지 조건이 반드시 따라야 했으니, 그것은 그가 지금의 거친 생활 방식을 버리는 것이었다."], ["In this he saw little difficulty.", "여기에 그는 별 어려움을 느끼지 않았다."]], [["He could not afford to wait till the next day before seeing Thomasin and detailing his plan.", "토머신을 만나 자기 계획을 자세히 들려주는 일을 이튿날까지 미룰 형편이 못 되었다."], ["He speedily plunged himself into toilet operations, pulled a suit of cloth clothes from a box, and in about twenty minutes stood before the van-lantern as a reddleman in nothing but his face, the vermilion shades of which were not to be removed in a day.", "그는 잽싸게 몸단장에 들어가, 궤짝에서 천으로 된 양복 한 벌을 꺼냈고, 이십 분쯤 지나자 얼굴만 빼고는 황토장이가 아닌 모습으로 마차 등불 앞에 섰으니, 얼굴의 주홍빛 색조는 하루 만에 지워질 것이 아니었다."], ["Closing the door and fastening it with a padlock, Venn set off towards Blooms-End.", "문을 닫고 자물쇠로 잠근 뒤, 벤은 블룸스엔드를 향해 길을 나섰다."]], [["He had reached the white palings and laid his hand upon the gate when the door of the house opened, and quickly closed again.", "그가 흰 울타리 말뚝에 이르러 대문에 손을 얹었을 때 집 문이 열렸다가 이내 다시 닫혔다. 한 여자의 형상이 미끄러지듯 안으로 들어갔던 것이다."], ["A female form had glided in. At the same time a man, who had seemingly been standing with the woman in the porch, came forward from the house till he was face to face with Venn.", "동시에, 보아하니 그 여자와 현관에 함께 서 있던 듯한 한 사내가 벤과 얼굴을 맞댈 때까지 집에서 앞으로 나왔다."], ["It was Wildeve again.", "또다시 와일디브였다."]], [["“Man alive, you’ve been quick at it,”", "「이런, 빠르기도 하군.」"], ["said Diggory sarcastically.", "디고리가 비꼬듯 말했다."]], [["“And you slow, as you will find,”", "「당신은 느리지, 곧 알게 될 거요.」"], ["said Wildeve. “And,”", "와일디브가 말했다."], ["lowering his voice, “you may as well go back again now.", "그러고는 목소리를 낮춰 「이제 그만 돌아가는 게 좋을 거요."], ["I’ve claimed her, and got her.", "난 그녀를 차지하겠다 했고, 차지했으니."], ["Good night, reddleman!”", "잘 가시오, 황토장이!」"], ["Thereupon Wildeve walked away.", "그러고 나서 와일디브가 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["Venn’s heart sank within him, though it had not risen unduly high.", "벤의 가슴은 안에서 내려앉았으니, 부당하게 높이 솟아오른 적도 없었건만 그러했다."], ["He stood leaning over the palings in an indecisive mood for nearly a quarter of an hour.", "그는 거의 십오 분간 울타리 말뚝에 기댄 채 갈피를 못 잡고 서 있었다."], ["Then he went up the garden path, knocked, and asked for Mrs. Yeobright.", "그러다 정원 길을 올라가 문을 두드리고 요브라이트 부인을 청했다."]], [["Instead of requesting him to enter she came to the porch. A discourse was carried on between them in low measured tones for the space of ten minutes or more.", "부인은 그에게 들어오라 하는 대신 현관으로 나왔다."], ["At the end of the time Mrs. Yeobright went in, and Venn sadly retraced his steps into the heath.", "둘 사이에 낮고 절제된 어조로 십 분 남짓 이야기가 오갔다."], ["When he had again regained his van he lit the lantern, and with an apathetic face at once began to pull off his best clothes, till in the course of a few minutes he reappeared as the confirmed and irretrievable reddleman that he had seemed before.", "그 시간이 끝나자 요브라이트 부인은 안으로 들어갔고, 벤은 슬프게 발길을 되돌려 황야로 들어섰다."], ["VIII. Firmness Is Discovered in a Gentle Heart", "그가 다시 마차에 이르자 등불을 켜고, 무심한 얼굴로 곧장 제일 좋은 옷을 벗기 시작해, 몇 분 사이에 앞서 보였던 그대로 굳어진, 돌이킬 수 없는 황토장이로 다시 나타났다."]], [["On that evening the interior of Blooms-End, though cosy and comfortable, had been rather silent.", "그날 저녁 블룸스엔드의 내부는 아늑하고 안락했으나 다소 적막했다."], ["Clym Yeobright was not at home.", "클림 요브라이트가 집에 없었던 것이다."], ["Since the Christmas party he had gone on a few days’ visit to a friend about ten miles off.", "크리스마스 잔치 이후로 그는 십 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 사는 친구를 며칠간 방문하러 가 있었다."]], [["The shadowy form seen by Venn to part from Wildeve in the porch, and quickly withdraw into the house, was Thomasin’s.", "벤이 현관에서 와일디브와 헤어져 재빨리 집 안으로 물러나는 것을 본 그 어렴풋한 형상은 토머신의 것이었다."], ["On entering she threw down a cloak which had been carelessly wrapped round her, and came forward to the light, where Mrs. Yeobright sat at her work-table, drawn up within the settle, so that part of it projected into the chimney-corner.", "안으로 들어서며 그녀는 아무렇게나 몸에 둘렀던 망토를 벗어 던지고, 불빛 쪽으로 나아갔으니, 거기에는 요브라이트 부인이 일감 탁자 앞에 앉아 있었고, 그 탁자는 긴 등받이 의자 안쪽으로 당겨져 일부가 굴뚝 모퉁이로 비어져 나와 있었다."]], [["“I don’t like your going out after dark alone, Tamsin,”", "「어두워진 뒤에 혼자 밖에 나가는 거 마음에 안 든다, 탬신.」"], ["said her aunt quietly, without looking up from her work.", "이모가 일감에서 눈도 들지 않은 채 조용히 말했다."]], [["“I have only been just outside the door.”", "「그냥 문 바로 밖에 있었어요.」"]], [["“Well?”", "「그래?」"], ["inquired Mrs. Yeobright, struck by a change in the tone of Thomasin’s voice, and observing her.", "토머신의 목소리 어조가 달라진 데 짚이는 바가 있어 요브라이트 부인이 그녀를 살피며 물었다."], ["Thomasin’s cheek was flushed to a pitch far beyond that which it had reached before her troubles, and her eyes glittered.", "토머신의 뺨은 그녀의 괴로운 일들이 닥치기 전에 이른 적 있던 정도를 한참 넘어 붉게 달아올라 있었고, 두 눈은 반짝였다."]], [["“It was _he_ who knocked,”", "「문을 두드린 건 그 사람이었어요.」"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I thought as much.”", "「그럴 거라 짐작했다.」"]], [["“He wishes the marriage to be at once.”", "「그 사람은 결혼을 당장 하고 싶어 해요.」"]], [["“Indeed!", "「그래!"], ["What—is he anxious?” Mrs.", "뭐—그가 안달이라도 났니?」"], ["Yeobright directed a searching look upon her niece.", "요브라이트 부인이 조카딸에게 살피는 눈길을 보냈다."], ["“Why did not Mr. Wildeve come in?”", "「왜 와일디브 씨는 들어오지 않았지?」"]], [["“He did not wish to.", "「들어오고 싶어 하지 않았어요."], ["You are not friends with him, he says.", "이모가 자기랑 사이가 좋지 않다고 하더라고요."], ["He would like the wedding to be the day after tomorrow, quite privately; at the church of his parish—not at ours.”", "모레 결혼식을 하고 싶대요, 아주 은밀하게, 자기 교구 교회에서요—우리 교회가 아니라요.」"]], [["“Oh!", "「오!"], ["And what did you say?”", "그래서 넌 뭐라고 했니?」"]], [["“I agreed to it,”", "「그렇게 하기로 했어요.」"], ["Thomasin answered firmly.", "토머신이 단호하게 대답했다."], ["“I am a practical woman now.", "「난 이제 현실적인 여자예요."], ["I don’t believe in hearts at all.", "마음 같은 건 전혀 믿지 않아요."], ["I would marry him under any circumstances since—since Clym’s letter.”", "어떤 사정이든 그와 결혼하겠어요, 그—그 클림 오빠 편지 이후로는요.」"]], [["A letter was lying on Mrs. Yeobright’s work-basket, and at Thomasin’s words her aunt reopened it, and silently read for the tenth time that day:—", "요브라이트 부인의 일감 바구니 위에 편지 한 통이 놓여 있었는데, 토머신의 말에 이모는 그것을 다시 펼쳐, 그날 열 번째로 말없이 읽었다—"]], [["“What is the meaning of this silly story that people are circulating about Thomasin and Mr. Wildeve?", "「토머신과 와일디브 씨에 대해 사람들이 퍼뜨리는 이 어리석은 이야기는 대체 무슨 뜻인가요?"], ["I should call such a scandal humiliating if there was the least chance of its being true.", "조금이라도 사실일 가능성이 있다면 그런 추문은 모욕적이라 부르겠어요."], ["How could such a gross falsehood have arisen?", "어떻게 그런 터무니없는 거짓이 생겨났을까요?"], ["It is said that one should go abroad to hear news of home, and I appear to have done it.", "고향 소식을 들으려면 외국에 나가 봐야 한다더니, 제가 꼭 그 짝이 난 것 같네요."], ["Of course I contradict the tale everywhere; but it is very vexing, and I wonder how it could have originated.", "물론 가는 곳마다 그 이야기를 부인하고 있습니다만, 몹시 짜증스럽고, 대체 어디서 비롯됐는지 의아할 따름입니다."], ["It is too ridiculous that such a girl as Thomasin could so mortify us as to get jilted on the wedding day.", "토머신 같은 처녀가 결혼식 날 버림받아 우리를 그토록 망신시킨다는 건 너무도 우스꽝스러운 일이에요."], ["What has she done?”", "대체 그 애가 무슨 짓을 했단 말입니까?」"]], [["“Yes,” Mrs.", "「그래.」"], ["Yeobright said sadly, putting down the letter.", "요브라이트 부인이 편지를 내려놓으며 슬프게 말했다."], ["“If you think you can marry him, do so. And since Mr.", "「네가 그와 결혼할 수 있다 여긴다면, 그렇게 하렴."], ["Wildeve wishes it to be unceremonious, let it be that too.", "그리고 와일디브 씨가 격식 없이 하길 바란다니, 그것도 그렇게 하고."], ["I can do nothing.", "난 아무것도 할 수 없다."], ["It is all in your own hands now.", "이제 다 네 손에 달렸어."], ["My power over your welfare came to an end when you left this house to go with him to Anglebury.”", "네가 그와 함께 앵글베리로 가겠다고 이 집을 떠난 그때 네 안위에 대한 내 힘은 끝이 났으니.」"], ["She continued, half in bitterness, “I may almost ask, why do you consult me in the matter at all?", "그녀가 반쯤 쓰라림에 잠겨 말을 이었다. 「내가 이 일로 너한테 의논을 받기는 하는 건지 거의 묻고 싶구나."], ["If you had gone and married him without saying a word to me, I could hardly have been angry—simply because, poor girl, you can’t do a better thing.”", "네가 나한테 한마디 없이 가서 그와 결혼했더라도, 난 좀처럼 화내지 못했을 거다—그저, 가엾은 것, 네가 그보다 나은 일을 할 수 없으니 말이야.」"]], [["“Don’t say that and dishearten me.”", "「그렇게 말씀하셔서 절 낙담시키지 마세요.」"]], [["“You are right—I will not.”", "「네 말이 맞다—그러지 않으마.」"]], [["“I do not plead for him, Aunt.", "「전 그를 두둔하는 게 아니에요, 이모."], ["Human nature is weak, and I am not a blind woman to insist that he is perfect.", "사람의 본성은 약하고, 전 그가 완벽하다고 우길 만큼 눈먼 여자가 아니에요."], ["I did think so, but I don’t now.", "한땐 그렇게 여겼지만, 지금은 아니에요."], ["But I know my course, and you know that I know it.", "하지만 제 갈 길을 알고, 제가 그걸 안다는 걸 이모도 아시잖아요."], ["I hope for the best.”", "전 잘되기를 바라요.」"]], [["“And so do I, and we will both continue to,”", "「나도 그렇단다, 우리 둘 다 계속 그러자꾸나.」"], ["said Mrs. Yeobright, rising and kissing her.", "요브라이트 부인이 일어서서 그녀에게 입을 맞추며 말했다."], ["“Then the wedding, if it comes off, will be on the morning of the very day Clym comes home?”", "「그럼 결혼식이, 만약 치러진다면, 클림이 집에 오는 바로 그날 아침이 되겠구나?」"]], [["“Yes.", "「네."], ["I decided that it ought to be over before he came.", "오빠가 오기 전에 끝나야 한다고 결정했어요."], ["After that you can look him in the face, and so can I.", "그러고 나면 이모도 오빠 얼굴을 떳떳이 볼 수 있고, 저도 그럴 수 있어요."], ["Our concealments will matter nothing.”", "우리가 숨긴 일들이 아무 문제도 안 될 거예요.」"]], [["Mrs. Yeobright moved her head in thoughtful assent, and presently said, “Do you wish me to give you away?", "요브라이트 부인이 생각에 잠겨 고개를 끄덕여 동의하더니, 이윽고 말했다."], ["I am willing to undertake that, you know, if you wish, as I was last time.", "「내가 너를 인도해 주길 바라니? 네가 원한다면 기꺼이 그 일을 맡으마, 알다시피, 지난번처럼."], ["After once forbidding the banns I think I can do no less.”", "한 번 결혼 예고를 막은 마당이니 그 정도는 해야지 싶구나.」"]], [["“I don’t think I will ask you to come,”", "「오시라고 청하진 않을게요.」"], ["said Thomasin reluctantly, but with decision.", "토머신이 마지못해, 그러나 단호하게 말했다."], ["“It would be unpleasant, I am almost sure.", "「불편할 거예요, 거의 분명히요."], ["Better let there be only strangers present, and none of my relations at all.", "차라리 낯선 사람들만 참석하고, 제 친척은 아무도 없는 편이 나아요."], ["I would rather have it so.", "전 그러는 게 더 좋아요."], ["I do not wish to do anything which may touch your credit, and I feel that I should be uncomfortable if you were there, after what has passed.", "이모의 체면에 누가 될 일은 하고 싶지 않고, 지난 일이 있고 나서 이모가 거기 계시면 제가 불편할 것 같아요."], ["I am only your niece, and there is no necessity why you should concern yourself more about me.”", "전 그저 이모의 조카일 뿐이고, 이모가 저 때문에 더 마음 쓰셔야 할 이유는 없어요.」"]], [["“Well, he has beaten us,”", "「그래, 그가 우릴 이겼구나.」"], ["her aunt said.", "이모가 말했다."], ["“It really seems as if he had been playing with you in this way in revenge for my humbling him as I did by standing up against him at first.”", "「처음에 내가 맞서서 그를 굽힌 것에 대한 앙갚음으로, 그가 이런 식으로 너를 가지고 논 것만 같아 정말이지.」"]], [["“O no, Aunt,”", "「오 아니에요, 이모.」"], ["murmured Thomasin.", "토머신이 중얼거렸다."]], [["They said no more on the subject then.", "그들은 그때 그 일에 대해 더 말하지 않았다."], ["Diggory Venn’s knock came soon after; and Mrs.", "곧이어 디고리 벤의 노크 소리가 들렸고, 요브라이트 부인은 현관에서 그와 만난 뒤 돌아와 무심하게 말했다."], ["Yeobright, on returning from her interview with him in the porch, carelessly observed, “Another lover has come to ask for you.”", "「또 다른 구혼자가 너를 청하러 왔더구나.」 「설마요?」"]], [["“No?” “Yes, that queer young man Venn.”", "「그래, 그 별난 젊은이 벤 말이다.」"]], [["“Asks to pay his addresses to me?”", "「제게 구애를 청한다고요?」"]], [["“Yes; and I told him he was too late.”", "「그래, 그래서 너무 늦었다고 했다.」"]], [["Thomasin looked silently into the candle-flame.", "토머신이 말없이 촛불 불꽃을 들여다보았다."], ["“Poor Diggory!”", "「가엾은 디고리!」"], ["she said, and then aroused herself to other things.", "그녀가 말하고는, 다른 일들로 마음을 추슬렀다."]], [["The next day was passed in mere mechanical deeds of preparation, both the women being anxious to immerse themselves in these to escape the emotional aspect of the situation.", "이튿날은 그저 기계적인 준비 행위들로 지나갔으니, 두 여자 모두 그 일에 몰두함으로써 상황의 감정적인 측면에서 벗어나고 싶어 했다."], ["Some wearing apparel and other articles were collected anew for Thomasin, and remarks on domestic details were frequently made, so as to obscure any inner misgivings about her future as Wildeve’s wife.", "토머신을 위해 입을 옷가지며 다른 물건들이 새로 모아졌고, 와일디브의 아내로서의 그녀의 장래에 대한 어떤 내심의 불안도 가리려는 듯 집안의 자질구레한 일들에 관한 말들이 자주 오갔다."]], [["The appointed morning came.", "정해진 아침이 왔다."], ["The arrangement with Wildeve was that he should meet her at the church to guard against any unpleasant curiosity which might have affected them had they been seen walking off together in the usual country way.", "와일디브와의 약속은, 흔한 시골 방식대로 둘이 함께 걸어가는 것이 누군가의 눈에 띄어 불쾌한 호기심을 살 수도 있는 일을 막기 위해, 그가 교회에서 그녀를 만나기로 한 것이었다."]], [["Aunt and niece stood together in the bedroom where the bride was dressing.", "이모와 조카딸은 신부가 단장을 하는 침실에 함께 서 있었다."], ["The sun, where it could catch it, made a mirror of Thomasin’s hair, which she always wore braided.", "햇빛은 닿을 수 있는 곳에서 토머신의 머리카락을 거울로 만들었으니, 그녀는 늘 머리를 땋아 늘이고 다녔다."], ["It was braided according to a calendar system—the more important the day the more numerous the strands in the braid.", "그것은 달력 방식에 따라 땋은 것이었다—날이 중요할수록 땋은 가닥의 수가 더 많아지는 식이었다."], ["On ordinary working-days she braided it in threes; on ordinary Sundays in fours; at Maypolings, gipsyings, and the like, she braided it in fives.", "평범한 일하는 날엔 세 가닥으로, 평범한 일요일엔 네 가닥으로, 메이폴 축제며 집시 놀이며 그 비슷한 때엔 다섯 가닥으로 땋았다."], ["Years ago she had said that when she married she would braid it in sevens.", "몇 해 전 그녀는 결혼할 때면 일곱 가닥으로 땋겠다고 말한 적이 있었다."], ["She had braided it in sevens today.", "오늘 그녀는 일곱 가닥으로 땋았다."]], [["“I have been thinking that I will wear my blue silk after all,”", "「아무래도 파란 비단옷을 입는 게 좋겠다고 생각했어요.」"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“It is my wedding day, even though there may be something sad about the time.", "「제 결혼식 날이잖아요, 비록 그 시기에 뭔가 슬픈 데가 있을지라도요."], ["I mean,”", "제 말은,」"], ["she added, anxious to correct any wrong impression, “not sad in itself, but in its having had great disappointment and trouble before it.”", "그녀가 어떤 그릇된 인상이라도 바로잡으려 애쓰며 덧붙였다. 「그 자체가 슬프다는 게 아니라, 그 앞에 큰 실망과 괴로움이 있었다는 점에서요.」"]], [["Mrs. Yeobright breathed in a way which might have been called a sigh.", "요브라이트 부인이 한숨이라 할 만한 식으로 숨을 내쉬었다."], ["“I almost wish Clym had been at home,” she said.", "「클림이 집에 있었으면 싶을 지경이구나.」"], ["“Of course you chose the time because of his absence.”", "그녀가 말했다. 「물론 넌 그가 없는 때를 골랐지.」"]], [["“Partly.", "「얼마쯤은요."], ["I have felt that I acted unfairly to him in not telling him all; but, as it was done not to grieve him, I thought I would carry out the plan to its end, and tell the whole story when the sky was clear.”", "오빠한테 다 말하지 않은 게 그에게 부당하게 굴었다는 생각이 들긴 했어요. 하지만 그를 슬프게 하지 않으려고 한 일이니, 계획을 끝까지 밀고 나가서 하늘이 맑아졌을 때 모든 이야기를 털어놓는 게 낫겠다 싶었어요.」"]], [["“You are a practical little woman,”", "「넌 실속 있는 작은 여인이로구나.」"], ["said Mrs. Yeobright, smiling.", "요브라이트 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“I wish you and he—no, I don’t wish anything.", "「너와 그가—아니, 난 아무것도 바라지 않으련다."], ["There, it is nine o’clock,”", "자, 아홉 시구나.」"], ["she interrupted, hearing a whizz and a dinging downstairs.", "그녀가 아래층에서 윙 하는 소리와 댕 하는 소리를 듣고 말을 끊었다."]], [["“I told Damon I would leave at nine,”", "「데이먼한테 아홉 시에 나가겠다고 했어요.」"], ["said Thomasin, hastening out of the room.", "토머신이 방에서 서둘러 나가며 말했다."]], [["Her aunt followed.", "이모가 뒤따랐다."], ["When Thomasin was going up the little walk from the door to the wicket-gate, Mrs.", "토머신이 문에서 쪽문까지 작은 길을 올라갈 때, 요브라이트 부인이 마지못한 듯 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["Yeobright looked reluctantly at her, and said, “It is a shame to let you go alone.”", "「너를 혼자 보내다니 부끄러운 일이구나.」"]], [["“It is necessary,”", "「그래야만 해요.」"], ["said Thomasin.", "토머신이 말했다."]], [["“At any rate,”", "「어쨌든,」"], ["added her aunt with forced cheerfulness, “I shall call upon you this afternoon, and bring the cake with me.", "이모가 애써 밝게 덧붙였다. 「오늘 오후에 너를 찾아가마, 케이크도 가지고 가고."], ["If Clym has returned by that time he will perhaps come too. I wish to show Mr.", "클림이\n그때까지 그 애가 돌아온다면 어쩌면 그 사람도 같이 올지 모르지."], ["Wildeve that I bear him no ill-will. Let the past be forgotten.", "나는 와일디브 씨에게 아무 악감정도 없다는 걸 보여주고 싶구나."], ["Well, God bless you!", "지난 일은 잊자꾸나."], ["There, I don’t believe in old superstitions, but I’ll do it.”", "자, 신의 가호가 있기를! 이런, 나는 옛 미신을 믿지 않지만 그래도 해보마.\""], ["She threw a slipper at the retreating figure of the girl, who turned, smiled, and went on again.", "그녀는 멀어져가는 처녀의 모습을 향해 슬리퍼 한 짝을 던졌고, 처녀는 돌아서서 미소 짓고는 다시 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["A few steps further, and she looked back.", "몇 걸음 더 가더니 그녀는 뒤를 돌아보았다."], ["“Did you call me, Aunt?”", "\"저를 부르셨어요, 이모?\""], ["she tremulously inquired.", "그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 물었다."], ["“Good-bye!”", "\"안녕히 계세요!\""]], [["Moved by an uncontrollable feeling as she looked upon Mrs. Yeobright’s worn, wet face, she ran back, when her aunt came forward, and they met again.", "미세스 예오브라이트의 지치고 젖은 얼굴을 바라보다가 걷잡을 수 없는 감정에 사로잡힌 그녀는 다시 뛰어왔고, 이모도 마주 나아가 두 사람은 다시 만났다."], ["“O—Tamsie,”", "\"오—탬지,\""], ["said the elder, weeping, “I don’t like to let you go.”", "손위 여인이 울며 말했다. \"널 보내고 싶지 않구나.\""]], [["“I—I am—”", "\"저—저도—\""], ["Thomasin began, giving way likewise.", "토머신도 마찬가지로 무너지며 말을 꺼냈다."], ["But, quelling her grief, she said “Good-bye!”", "그러나 슬픔을 억누르고는 다시 \"안녕히 계세요!\""], ["again and went on.", "하고 말한 뒤 걸음을 이어갔다."]], [["Then Mrs. Yeobright saw a little figure wending its way between the scratching furze-bushes, and diminishing far up the valley—a pale-blue spot in a vast field of neutral brown, solitary and undefended except by the power of her own hope.", "이윽고 미세스 예오브라이트는 자그마한 형체 하나가 할퀴어대는 가시금작화 덤불 사이로 길을 헤치며 골짜기 저 위로 점점 작아져가는 것을 보았으니—무미한 갈색의 광대한 들판 한가운데 옅은 푸른빛 점 하나, 외롭고 무방비하며 오직 자신의 희망이라는 힘만으로 지켜질 뿐이었다."]], [["But the worst feature in the case was one which did not appear in the landscape; it was the man.", "그러나 이 일에서 가장 나쁜 점은 풍경 속에 드러나지 않은 것이었으니, 그것은 바로 그 남자였다."]], [["The hour chosen for the ceremony by Thomasin and Wildeve had been so timed as to enable her to escape the awkwardness of meeting her cousin Clym, who was returning the same morning.", "토머신과 와일디브가 식을 올리기로 택한 시각은, 그녀가 같은 날 아침에 돌아오는 사촌 클림과 마주치는 어색함을 피할 수 있도록 맞춰진 것이었다."], ["To own to the partial truth of what he had heard would be distressing as long as the humiliating position resulting from the event was unimproved.", "그가 들은 이야기가 부분적으로나마 사실임을 인정하는 것은, 그 사건으로 빚어진 굴욕적인 처지가 나아지지 않는 한 괴로운 일일 터였다."], ["It was only after a second and successful journey to the altar that she could lift up her head and prove the failure of the first attempt a pure accident.", "두 번째로 제단을 향해 가서 그것이 성공한 뒤에야 비로소 그녀는 고개를 들고 첫 번째 시도의 실패가 순전한 우연이었음을 입증할 수 있을 것이었다."]], [["She had not been gone from Blooms-End more than half an hour when Yeobright came by the meads from the other direction and entered the house.", "그녀가 블룸스엔드를 떠난 지 반 시간이 채 못 되어, 예오브라이트가 반대편에서 풀밭을 가로질러 와 집 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["“I had an early breakfast,”", "\"아침을 일찍 먹었어요.\""], ["he said to his mother after greeting her.", "그가 어머니에게 인사를 건넨 뒤 말했다."], ["“Now I could eat a little more.”", "\"이제 좀 더 먹을 수 있겠어요.\""]], [["They sat down to the repeated meal, and he went on in a low, anxious voice, apparently imagining that Thomasin had not yet come downstairs, “What’s this I have heard about Thomasin and Mr.", "두 사람은 다시 차린 식사 앞에 마주 앉았고, 그는 토머신이 아직 내려오지 않았다고 여기는 듯, 낮고 근심 어린 목소리로 말을 이었다."], ["Wildeve?”", "\"토머신하고 와일디브 씨 일에 대해 제가 들은 게 대체 뭐죠?\""]], [["“It is true in many points,” said Mrs.", "\"여러 가지로 사실이란다.\""], ["Yeobright quietly; “but it is all right now, I hope.”", "미세스 예오브라이트가 조용히 말했다. \"하지만 이제는 다 잘되었으면 좋겠구나.\""], ["She looked at the clock.", "그녀는 시계를 바라보았다. \"사실이라고요?\""]], [["“True?” “Thomasin is gone to him today.”", "\"토머신은 오늘 그 사람에게 갔단다.\""]], [["Clym pushed away his breakfast.", "클림은 식사를 옆으로 밀쳐냈다."], ["“Then there is a scandal of some sort, and that’s what’s the matter with Thomasin.", "\"그렇다면 무슨 추문이 있었던 거고, 그게 바로 토머신을 괴롭힌 거군요."], ["Was it this that made her ill?”", "그것 때문에 그 애가 앓아누운 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Not a scandal—a misfortune.", "추문은 아니고—불운이지."], ["I will tell you all about it, Clym.", "다 이야기해주마, 클림."], ["You must not be angry, but you must listen, and you’ll find that what we have done has been done for the best.”", "화내선 안 된다, 다만 들어다오, 그러면 우리가 한 일이 다 좋자고 한 것임을 알게 될 게다.\""]], [["She then told him the circumstances.", "그러고서 그녀는 그에게 사정을 들려주었다."], ["All that he had known of the affair before he returned from Paris was that there had existed an attachment between Thomasin and Wildeve, which his mother had at first discountenanced, but had since, owing to the arguments of Thomasin, looked upon in a little more favourable light.", "그가 파리에서 돌아오기 전에 그 일에 대해 알고 있던 것이라곤, 토머신과 와일디브 사이에 정분이 있었고, 어머니가 처음에는 그것을 못마땅해했으나 이후 토머신의 항변 때문에 조금은 호의적으로 보게 되었다는 정도였다."], ["When she, therefore, proceeded to explain all he was greatly surprised and troubled.", "그러므로 그녀가 자초지종을 설명해나가자 그는 몹시 놀라고 괴로워했다."]], [["“And she determined that the wedding should be over before you came back,” said Mrs.", "\"그래서 그 애는 네가 돌아오기 전에 혼례를 끝내야겠다고 마음먹은 게다.\""], ["Yeobright, “that there might be no chance of her meeting you, and having a very painful time of it.", "미세스 예오브라이트가 말했다. \"너와 마주쳐 아주 괴로운 시간을 겪을 일이 없도록 말이지."], ["That’s why she has gone to him; they have arranged to be married this morning.”", "그래서 그 사람한테 간 거란다. 오늘 아침에 식을 올리기로 둘이 정했어.\""]], [["“But I can’t understand it,”", "\"하지만 이해가 안 돼요.\""], ["said Yeobright, rising.", "예오브라이트가 일어서며 말했다."], ["“’Tis so unlike her.", "\"그 애답지 않아요."], ["I can see why you did not write to me after her unfortunate return home.", "그 애가 불운하게 돌아온 뒤 어머니가 제게 편지를 안 쓰신 건 이해가 가요."], ["But why didn’t you let me know when the wedding was going to be—the first time?”", "그런데 처음에 혼례가 언제 있을 예정인지는 왜 제게 알리지 않으셨어요?\""]], [["“Well, I felt vexed with her just then.", "\"글쎄, 그때는 그 애한테 화가 나 있었단다."], ["She seemed to me to be obstinate; and when I found that you were nothing in her mind I vowed that she should be nothing in yours.", "내겐 그 애가 고집스러워 보였어. 게다가 네가 그 애 마음속에 아무것도 아니라는 걸 알고 나니, 그 애도 네 마음속에 아무것도 아니어야 한다고 다짐했지."], ["I felt that she was only my niece after all; I told her she might marry, but that I should take no interest in it, and should not bother you about it either.”", "따지고 보면 그 애는 그저 내 조카일 뿐이라는 생각이 들더구나. 결혼해도 좋다고는 했지만, 나는 거기에 관심도 두지 않을 거고 그 일로 너를 성가시게 하지도 않겠다고 그 애한테 말했단다.\""]], [["“It wouldn’t have been bothering me.", "\"성가시지 않았을 거예요."], ["Mother, you did wrong.”", "어머니, 잘못하셨어요.\""]], [["“I thought it might disturb you in your business, and that you might throw up your situation, or injure your prospects in some way because of it, so I said nothing.", "\"네 일에 방해가 될까, 그것 때문에 네가 자리를 내던지거나 어떻게든 앞날을 망칠까 싶어서 아무 말도 안 했지."], ["Of course, if they had married at that time in a proper manner, I should have told you at once.”", "물론 그때 둘이 제대로 격식을 갖춰 결혼했더라면 곧장 네게 알렸을 게다.\""]], [["“Tamsin actually being married while we are sitting here!”", "\"탬신이 지금 우리가 여기 앉아 있는 동안에 정말로 결혼을 하고 있다니!\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Unless some accident happens again, as it did the first time.", "처음처럼 또 무슨 차질이 생기지만 않는다면 말이다."], ["It may, considering he’s the same man.”", "같은 남자인 걸 생각하면, 그럴 수도 있지.\""]], [["“Yes, and I believe it will.", "\"네, 그리고 저는 그렇게 되리라 믿어요."], ["Was it right to let her go?", "그 애를 보낸 게 옳은 일이었을까요?"], ["Suppose Wildeve is really a bad fellow?”", "와일디브가 정말로 못된 작자라면요?\""]], [["“Then he won’t come, and she’ll come home again.”", "\"그렇다면 그자가 안 올 테고, 그 애는 다시 집으로 돌아오겠지.\""]], [["“You should have looked more into it.”", "\"좀 더 알아보셨어야죠.\""]], [["“It is useless to say that,”", "\"그런 말은 부질없다.\""], ["his mother answered with an impatient look of sorrow.", "그의 어머니가 슬픔에 찬 초조한 눈빛으로 대답했다."], ["“You don’t know how bad it has been here with us all these weeks, Clym.", "\"지난 몇 주 동안 우리가 여기서 얼마나 힘들었는지 너는 모른다, 클림."], ["You don’t know what a mortification anything of that sort is to a woman.", "그런 일이 여자에게 얼마나 큰 치욕인지 너는 몰라."], ["You don’t know the sleepless nights we’ve had in this house, and the almost bitter words that have passed between us since that Fifth of November.", "이 집에서 우리가 보낸 잠 못 이루는 밤들을, 그 십일월 오일 이후로 우리 사이에 오간 모진 말들을 너는 모른다."], ["I hope never to pass seven such weeks again.", "그런 일곱 주를 다시는 겪지 않았으면 싶구나."], ["Tamsin has not gone outside the door, and I have been ashamed to look anybody in the face; and now you blame me for letting her do the only thing that can be done to set that trouble straight.”", "탬신은 문밖에도 나가지 못했고, 나는 누구의 얼굴도 떳떳이 볼 수 없어 부끄러웠다. 그런데 이제 와서 너는, 그 곤경을 바로잡을 수 있는 유일한 일을 그 애가 하도록 둔 것을 두고 나를 탓하는구나.\""]], [["“No,” he said slowly.", "\"아니에요.\""], ["“Upon the whole I don’t blame you.", "그가 천천히 말했다. \"대체로 어머니를 탓하지는 않아요."], ["But just consider how sudden it seems to me.", "하지만 제겐 이게 얼마나 갑작스럽게 느껴지는지 헤아려보세요."], ["Here was I, knowing nothing; and then I am told all at once that Tamsie is gone to be married.", "저는 여기서 아무것도 모르고 있다가, 느닷없이 탬지가 결혼하러 갔다는 말을 듣잖아요."], ["Well, I suppose there was nothing better to do.", "뭐, 달리 더 나은 방도가 없었나 보죠."], ["Do you know, Mother,”", "어머니, 그거 아세요,\""], ["he continued after a moment or two, looking suddenly interested in his own past history, “I once thought of Tamsin as a sweetheart?", "그가 잠시 후 자신의 지난 내력에 갑자기 흥미가 동한 듯 말을 이었다. \"저는 한때 탬신을 연인으로 생각한 적이 있어요."], ["Yes, I did.", "네, 그랬죠."], ["How odd boys are!", "사내애들이란 참 이상하다니까요!"], ["And when I came home and saw her this time she seemed so much more affectionate than usual, that I was quite reminded of those days, particularly on the night of the party, when she was unwell.", "이번에 집에 와서 그 애를 봤을 때, 그 애가 여느 때보다 훨씬 다정해 보여서 그 시절이 떠오를 정도였어요. 특히 그 애가 몸이 안 좋았던 잔치가 있던 밤에요."], ["We had the party just the same—was not that rather cruel to her?”", "우리는 잔치를 그대로 열었잖아요—그건 그 애한테 좀 잔인한 일이 아니었나요?\""]], [["“It made no difference.", "\"달라질 건 없었다."], ["I had arranged to give one, and it was not worth while to make more gloom than necessary.", "잔치를 열기로 정해두었으니, 굳이 필요 이상으로 음울하게 만들 까닭이 없었지."], ["To begin by shutting ourselves up and telling you of Tamsin’s misfortunes would have been a poor sort of welcome.”", "처음부터 우리끼리 틀어박혀 네게 탬신의 불운을 늘어놓는 걸로 시작했다면 형편없는 환영이 되었을 게다.\""]], [["Clym remained thinking.", "클림은 생각에 잠긴 채 있었다."], ["“I almost wish you had not had that party,”", "\"그 잔치를 안 여셨더라면 좋았을 뻔했어요.\""], ["he said; “and for other reasons.", "그가 말했다. \"다른 이유들 때문에도요."], ["But I will tell you in a day or two.", "하지만 그건 하루 이틀 안에 말씀드릴게요."], ["We must think of Tamsin now.”", "지금은 탬신을 생각해야죠.\""]], [["They lapsed into silence.", "두 사람은 침묵에 빠졌다."], ["“I’ll tell you what,”", "\"제 생각은 이래요.\""], ["said Yeobright again, in a tone which showed some slumbering feeling still.", "예오브라이트가 아직 잠들지 않은 감정이 묻어나는 어조로 다시 말했다."], ["“I don’t think it kind to Tamsin to let her be married like this, and neither of us there to keep up her spirits or care a bit about her.", "\"이렇게 결혼시키는 건, 우리 둘 다 그 자리에 없어 그 애 기운을 북돋아주지도, 조금도 마음 써주지도 못하는 건, 탬신한테 다정한 처사가 아니에요."], ["She hasn’t disgraced herself, or done anything to deserve that.", "그 애는 스스로를 욕되게 하지도, 그런 대접을 받을 짓을 하지도 않았어요."], ["It is bad enough that the wedding should be so hurried and unceremonious, without our keeping away from it in addition.", "혼례가 이토록 서둘러 격식 없이 치러지는 것만 해도 충분히 안된 일인데, 거기다 우리까지 멀리 떨어져 있다니요."], ["Upon my soul, ’tis almost a shame.", "정말이지, 이건 거의 부끄러운 일이에요."], ["I’ll go.”", "제가 갈게요.\""]], [["“It is over by this time,”", "\"이맘때면 다 끝났을 게다.\""], ["said his mother with a sigh; “unless they were late, or he—”", "그의 어머니가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"그들이 늦지 않았다면, 아니면 그자가—\""]], [["“Then I shall be soon enough to see them come out.", "\"그렇다면 그들이 나오는 걸 보기엔 충분히 이르겠네요."], ["I don’t quite like your keeping me in ignorance, Mother, after all.", "어쨌든 어머니가 저를 모르는 채로 두신 게 영 마음에 들지 않아요."], ["Really, I half hope he has failed to meet her!”", "정말이지, 절반쯤은 그자가 그 애를 만나러 오지 못했기를 바란다니까요!\""]], [["“And ruined her character?”", "\"그래서 그 애 평판을 망치라고?\""]], [["“Nonsense—that wouldn’t ruin Thomasin.”", "\"말도 안 돼요—그런다고 토머신이 망쳐지진 않아요.\""]], [["He took up his hat and hastily left the house.", "그는 모자를 집어 들고 황급히 집을 나섰다."], ["Mrs. Yeobright looked rather unhappy, and sat still, deep in thought.", "미세스 예오브라이트는 자못 불행한 기색으로, 깊은 생각에 잠겨 가만히 앉아 있었다."], ["But she was not long left alone.", "그러나 홀로 남겨진 것은 오래가지 않았다."], ["A few minutes later Clym came back again, and in his company came Diggory Venn.", "몇 분 뒤 클림이 다시 돌아왔는데, 그와 함께 디고리 벤이 들어왔다."]], [["“I find there isn’t time for me to get there,”", "\"제가 거기 갈 시간이 안 되더라고요.\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."]], [["“Is she married?” Mrs.", "\"그 애는 결혼했나요?\""], ["Yeobright inquired, turning to the reddleman a face in which a strange strife of wishes, for and against, was apparent.", "미세스 예오브라이트가 레들맨 쪽으로 돌아서며 물었는데, 그 얼굴에는 바라는 마음과 바라지 않는 마음이 기묘하게 다투는 기색이 역력했다."]], [["Venn bowed.", "벤이 고개를 숙였다."], ["“She is, ma’am.”", "\"했습니다, 마님.\""]], [["“How strange it sounds,”", "\"참 이상하게 들리는군요.\""], ["murmured Clym.", "클림이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“And he didn’t disappoint her this time?”", "\"그래서 이번에는 그자가 그 애를 실망시키지 않았나요?\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "미세스 예오브라이트가 말했다."]], [["“He did not.", "\"그러지 않았습니다."], ["And there is now no slight on her name.", "이제 그분 이름에는 아무 흠도 없습니다."], ["I was hastening ath’art to tell you at once, as I saw you were not there.”", "마님께서 거기 안 계신 걸 보고, 곧장 알려드리려고 들판을 가로질러 서둘러 왔습니다.\""]], [["“How came you to be there?", "\"어쩌다 거기 가 있었나요?"], ["How did you know it?”", "그건 또 어떻게 알았고요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“I have been in that neighbourhood for some time, and I saw them go in,”", "\"얼마간 그 동네에 있었는데, 그들이 들어가는 걸 봤습니다.\""], ["said the reddleman.", "레들맨이 말했다."], ["“Wildeve came up to the door, punctual as the clock. I didn’t expect it of him.”", "\"와일디브는 시계처럼 정확하게 문 앞으로 올라오더군요."], ["He did not add, as he might have added, that how he came to be in that neighbourhood was not by accident; that, since Wildeve’s resumption of his right to Thomasin, Venn, with the thoroughness which was part of his character, had determined to see the end of the episode.", "그자에게서 그런 건 기대하지 않았는데 말입니다.\" 그는 덧붙일 수도 있었으나 덧붙이지 않았으니, 자신이 그 동네에 있게 된 것이 우연이 아니라는 것, 와일디브가 토머신에 대한 권리를 되찾은 이후로 벤은 천성의 일부인 철저함으로써 이 사건의 결말을 끝까지 지켜보기로 작정했다는 것이었다."]], [["“Who was there?”", "\"누가 거기 있었나요?\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "미세스 예오브라이트가 말했다."]], [["“Nobody hardly.", "\"거의 아무도 없었습니다."], ["I stood right out of the way, and she did not see me.”", "저는 멀찍이 비켜서 있어서, 그분은 저를 보지 못했습니다.\""], ["The reddleman spoke huskily, and looked into the garden.", "레들맨은 쉰 목소리로 말하며 정원 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["“Who gave her away?” “Miss Vye.”", "\"누가 그 애를 신랑에게 인도했나요?\""]], [["“How very remarkable!", "\"바이 양입니다.\" \"참으로 별일이군요!"], ["Miss Vye!", "바이 양이라니!"], ["It is to be considered an honour, I suppose?”", "그건 영광으로 여겨야 할 일이겠죠, 아마도?\""]], [["“Who’s Miss Vye?”", "\"바이 양이 누구예요?\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."]], [["“Captain Vye’s granddaughter, of Mistover Knap.”", "\"미스토버 냅의 바이 선장 손녀란다.\""]], [["“A proud girl from Budmouth,”", "\"버드머스에서 온 오만한 처자지.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "미세스 예오브라이트가 말했다."], ["“One not much to my liking.", "\"내 마음에 그리 드는 아이는 아니야."], ["People say she’s a witch, but of course that’s absurd.”", "사람들은 그 애가 마녀라고들 하지만, 물론 터무니없는 소리지.\""]], [["The reddleman kept to himself his acquaintance with that fair personage, and also that Eustacia was there because he went to fetch her, in accordance with a promise he had given as soon as he learnt that the marriage was to take place.", "레들맨은 그 어여쁜 인물과 자신이 아는 사이라는 것을, 또 유스테이시아가 거기 있었던 것이 결혼이 있을 거라는 걸 알자마자 자신이 했던 약속에 따라 그녀를 데리러 갔기 때문이라는 것을 혼자만 알고 있었다."], ["He merely said, in continuation of the story——", "그는 이야기를 이어가며 그저 이렇게만 말했다—"]], [["“I was sitting on the churchyard wall when they came up, one from one way, the other from the other; and Miss Vye was walking thereabouts, looking at the headstones.", "\"제가 묘지 담장에 앉아 있는데 그들이 올라왔습니다, 한 사람은 이쪽에서 다른 한 사람은 저쪽에서요. 그리고 바이 양이 그 근처를 거닐며 묘비들을 들여다보고 있었습니다."], ["As soon as they had gone in I went to the door, feeling I should like to see it, as I knew her so well.", "그들이 안으로 들어가자마자, 저는 그분을 워낙 잘 아는지라 그 광경을 보고 싶은 마음에 문간으로 갔습니다."], ["I pulled off my boots because they were so noisy, and went up into the gallery.", "장화가 너무 시끄러워서 벗어버리고, 회랑으로 올라갔지요."], ["I saw then that the parson and clerk were already there.”", "그때 보니 목사와 서기는 이미 와 있더군요.\""]], [["“How came Miss Vye to have anything to do with it, if she was only on a walk that way?”", "\"그저 그 길로 산책 나온 것뿐이었다면, 바이 양이 어쩌다 그 일에 관여하게 됐나요?\""]], [["“Because there was nobody else.", "\"달리 아무도 없었으니까요."], ["She had gone into the church just before me, not into the gallery.", "그분은 저보다 조금 앞서 교회 안으로 들어와 있었는데, 회랑은 아니었습니다."], ["The parson looked round before beginning, and as she was the only one near he beckoned to her, and she went up to the rails.", "목사가 시작하기 전에 둘러보았고, 가까이 있는 사람이 그분뿐이어서 손짓해 부르자 그분이 난간으로 올라갔습니다."], ["After that, when it came to signing the book, she pushed up her veil and signed; and Tamsin seemed to thank her for her kindness.”", "그 뒤에, 서명할 차례가 되자 그분은 베일을 걷어 올리고 서명했고, 탬신은 그 친절에 고마워하는 듯했습니다.\""], ["The reddleman told the tale thoughtfully for there lingered upon his vision the changing colour of Wildeve, when Eustacia lifted the thick veil which had concealed her from recognition and looked calmly into his face.", "레들맨은 그 이야기를 생각에 잠긴 채 들려주었으니, 유스테이시아가 자신을 알아보지 못하도록 가려주던 두꺼운 베일을 걷어 올리고 차분히 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았을 때 와일디브의 변하던 안색이 그의 눈앞에 어른거렸던 것이다."], ["“And then,”", "\"그러고서,\""], ["said Diggory sadly, “I came away, for her history as Tamsin Yeobright was over.”", "디고리가 쓸쓸하게 말했다. \"저는 자리를 떴습니다, 탬신 예오브라이트로서의 그분 내력은 끝났으니까요.\""]], [["“I offered to go,”", "\"내가 가겠다고 했단다.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright regretfully.", "미세스 예오브라이트가 후회하듯 말했다."], ["“But she said it was not necessary.”", "\"하지만 그 애는 그럴 필요 없다고 하더구나.\""]], [["“Well, it is no matter,”", "\"뭐, 상관없습니다.\""], ["said the reddleman.", "레들맨이 말했다."], ["“The thing is done at last as it was meant to be at first, and God send her happiness.", "\"처음에 그러기로 했던 대로 마침내 일이 이루어졌으니, 신이 그분께 행복을 내려주시길 빕니다."], ["Now I’ll wish you good morning.”", "그럼 이만 안녕히 계십시오.\""]], [["He placed his cap on his head and went out.", "그는 모자를 머리에 얹고 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["From that instant of leaving Mrs. Yeobright’s door, the reddleman was seen no more in or about Egdon Heath for a space of many months.", "미세스 예오브라이트의 집 문을 나선 그 순간부터, 레들맨은 여러 달 동안 에그던 황야 안에서도 그 언저리에서도 다시는 보이지 않았다."], ["He vanished entirely.", "그는 완전히 자취를 감추었다."], ["The nook among the brambles where his van had been standing was as vacant as ever the next morning, and scarcely a sign remained to show that he had been there, excepting a few straws, and a little redness on the turf, which was washed away by the next storm of rain.", "그의 마차가 서 있던 가시덤불 사이의 후미진 구석은 이튿날 아침이면 여느 때처럼 텅 비어 있었고, 그가 거기 있었음을 보여줄 흔적이라곤 지푸라기 몇 가닥과 잔디 위의 약간 붉은 자국밖에 거의 남지 않았는데, 그마저도 다음번 빗줄기에 씻겨나가고 말았다."]], [["The report that Diggory had brought of the wedding, correct as far as it went, was deficient in one significant particular, which had escaped him through his being at some distance back in the church.", "디고리가 전한 혼례 소식은, 전한 데까지는 정확했으나 한 가지 의미심장한 대목이 빠져 있었으니, 그가 교회 안에서 다소 뒤쪽 멀찍이 있었던 탓에 놓친 것이었다."], ["When Thomasin was tremblingly engaged in signing her name Wildeve had flung towards Eustacia a glance that said plainly, “I have punished you now.”", "토머신이 떨리는 손으로 자기 이름을 서명하고 있을 때, 와일디브는 유스테이시아를 향해 \"이제 너에게 복수했다\"라고 분명히 말하는 듯한 눈길을 던졌다."], ["She had replied in a low tone—and he little thought how truly—“You mistake; it gives me sincerest pleasure to see her your wife today.”", "그녀는 낮은 목소리로 응답했는데—그것이 얼마나 진실한 말인지 그는 거의 헤아리지 못했으니—\"잘못 아셨어요. 오늘 그 애가 당신의 아내가 되는 걸 보니 더없이 진심으로 기쁘답니다.\""]], [["I.", "I."], ["“My Mind to Me a Kingdom Is”", "“내 마음이 곧 나의 왕국이라”"]], [["In Clym Yeobright’s face could be dimly seen the typical countenance of the future.", "클림 요브라이트의 얼굴에서는 미래의 전형적 용모가 희미하게 엿보였다."], ["Should there be a classic period to art hereafter, its Pheidias may produce such faces.", "앞으로 예술에 고전기古典期가 도래한다면 그 시대의 페이디아스는 이런 얼굴들을 빚어낼 것이다."], ["The view of life as a thing to be put up with, replacing that zest for existence which was so intense in early civilizations, must ultimately enter so thoroughly into the constitution of the advanced races that its facial expression will become accepted as a new artistic departure.", "초기 문명에서 그토록 강렬했던 삶을 향한 열망을 대신하여, 인생을 견뎌내야 할 무엇으로 보는 관점이 결국에는 진보한 종족의 기질 속으로 너무도 철저히 스며들어, 그 얼굴 표정이 새로운 예술적 출발점으로 받아들여지게 될 것이다."], ["People already feel that a man who lives without disturbing a curve of feature, or setting a mark of mental concern anywhere upon himself, is too far removed from modern perceptiveness to be a modern type.", "사람들은 이미 느끼고 있는바, 이목구비의 윤곽 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않고 정신적 근심의 자취를 제 몸 어디에도 새기지 않은 채 살아가는 사람은 현대적 감수성으로부터 너무 멀리 떨어져 있어 현대적 전형이 될 수 없다는 것이다."], ["Physically beautiful men—the glory of the race when it was young—are almost an anachronism now; and we may wonder whether, at some time or other, physically beautiful women may not be an anachronism likewise.", "육체적으로 아름다운 남자들―종족이 젊었을 적의 영광―은 이제 거의 시대착오에 가깝거니와, 어느 때고 육체적으로 아름다운 여자들 역시 마찬가지로 시대착오가 되지 않으리란 법이 있을까 의문을 품어봄직하다."]], [["The truth seems to be that a long line of disillusive centuries has permanently displaced the Hellenic idea of life, or whatever it may be called.", "진실은 이러한 듯하다. 환멸로 가득한 기나긴 세기의 행렬이 헬레니즘적 인생관을―뭐라 부르든 간에―영원히 밀어내버렸다는 것."], ["What the Greeks only suspected we know well; what their Æschylus imagined our nursery children feel.", "그리스인이 그저 어렴풋이 짐작했던 것을 우리는 잘 알고 있고, 그들의 아이스킬로스가 상상했던 것을 우리 집 아이들은 느끼고 있다."], ["That old-fashioned revelling in the general situation grows less and less possible as we uncover the defects of natural laws, and see the quandary that man is in by their operation.", "우리가 자연법칙의 결함을 들춰내고 그 작용으로 인간이 처한 진퇴양난을 목도할수록, 전체 상황을 두고 떠들썩하게 즐기던 그 구식 흥취는 점점 더 불가능해진다."]], [["The lineaments which will get embodied in ideals based upon this new recognition will probably be akin to those of Yeobright.", "이 새로운 인식에 기초한 이상理想 속에 구현될 윤곽이라면 아마도 요브라이트의 그것과 닮았으리라."], ["The observer’s eye was arrested, not by his face as a picture, but by his face as a page; not by what it was, but by what it recorded.", "관찰자의 눈을 멈춰 세운 것은 한 폭의 그림으로서의 그의 얼굴이 아니라 한 페이지로서의 그의 얼굴이었으니, 그것이 무엇인가가 아니라 무엇을 기록하고 있는가였다."], ["His features were attractive in the light of symbols, as sounds intrinsically common become attractive in language, and as shapes intrinsically simple become interesting in writing.", "그의 이목구비는 상징의 관점에서 매력적이었는데, 이는 본디 흔하디흔한 소리가 언어 속에서 매력을 띠게 되고, 본디 단순하기 그지없는 형태가 글씨 속에서 흥미로워지는 것과 같았다."]], [["He had been a lad of whom something was expected.", "그는 무언가 기대를 모으던 소년이었다."], ["Beyond this all had been chaos.", "그 너머는 온통 혼돈이었다."], ["That he would be successful in an original way, or that he would go to the dogs in an original way, seemed equally probable.", "그가 독창적인 방식으로 성공하리라는 것이나 독창적인 방식으로 파멸하리라는 것이나 똑같이 가능성 있어 보였다."], ["The only absolute certainty about him was that he would not stand still in the circumstances amid which he was born.", "그에 관해 단 하나 확실한 것은 자신이 태어난 환경 속에 가만히 머물러 있지는 않으리라는 점이었다."]], [["Hence, when his name was casually mentioned by neighbouring yeomen, the listener said, “Ah, Clym Yeobright—what is he doing now?”", "그래서 이웃 자작농들이 무심코 그의 이름을 입에 올리면 듣는 이는 이렇게 말했다. “아, 클림 요브라이트―그 사람 지금 뭘 하고 있다지?”"], ["When the instinctive question about a person is, What is he doing? it is felt that he will be found to be, like most of us, doing nothing in particular.", "한 사람에 관해 본능적으로 던지는 물음이 ‘그가 무엇을 하고 있느냐’일 때면, 알고 보니 그도 우리 대다수처럼 딱히 하는 일이 없으리라는 느낌이 든다."], ["There is an indefinite sense that he must be invading some region of singularity, good or bad.", "그가 좋은 쪽이든 나쁜 쪽이든 어떤 독특한 영역을 침범하고 있음이 틀림없다는 막연한 감각이 있다."], ["The devout hope is that he is doing well. The secret faith is that he is making a mess of it.", "경건한 바람은 그가 잘 지내고 있으리란 것이요, 속으로 품은 믿음은 그가 일을 망치고 있으리란 것이다."], ["Half a dozen comfortable market-men, who were habitual callers at the Quiet Woman as they passed by in their carts, were partial to the topic.", "안락한 장사꾼 대여섯이 수레를 몰고 지나는 길에 ‘과묵한 여인’ 주막에 으레 들르곤 했는데 이 화제를 즐겨 입에 올렸다."], ["In fact, though they were not Egdon men, they could hardly avoid it while they sucked their long clay tubes and regarded the heath through the window.", "사실 그들은 에그던 사람은 아니었으나 긴 점토 담뱃대를 빨며 창 너머로 황야를 바라보는 동안 그 이야기를 피하기란 어려웠다."], ["Clym had been so inwoven with the heath in his boyhood that hardly anybody could look upon it without thinking of him.", "클림은 소년 시절 황야와 너무도 깊이 얽혀 있어, 그 누구든 황야를 바라보면서 그를 떠올리지 않기란 거의 불가능했다."], ["So the subject recurred: if he were making a fortune and a name, so much the better for him; if he were making a tragical figure in the world, so much the better for a narrative.", "그리하여 화제는 되풀이되었으니, 그가 재산과 명성을 쌓고 있다면 그에게 더없이 좋은 일이요, 그가 세상에서 비극적 인물이 되어가고 있다면 이야깃거리로 더없이 좋은 일이었다."]], [["The fact was that Yeobright’s fame had spread to an awkward extent before he left home.", "사실인즉 요브라이트가 고향을 떠나기도 전에 그의 명성은 거북할 만큼 널리 퍼져 있었다."], ["“It is bad when your fame outruns your means,” said the Spanish Jesuit Gracian.", "“명성이 능력을 앞지르면 좋지 못하다”라고 스페인의 예수회 수사 그라시안은 말했다."], ["At the age of six he had asked a Scripture riddle: “Who was the first man known to wear breeches?”", "여섯 살 때 그는 성경 수수께끼를 냈다. “바지를 입은 것으로 알려진 최초의 사람은 누구인가?”"], ["and applause had resounded from the very verge of the heath.", "그러자 황야의 맨 끄트머리에서부터 박수갈채가 울려 퍼졌다."], ["At seven he painted the Battle of Waterloo with tiger-lily pollen and black-currant juice, in the absence of water-colours.", "일곱 살 때는 수채물감이 없어 참나리 꽃가루와 까막까치밥나무 즙으로 워털루 전투를 그렸다."], ["By the time he reached twelve he had in this manner been heard of as artist and scholar for at least two miles round.", "열두 살이 될 무렵에는 이런 식으로 적어도 사방 2마일에 걸쳐 화가요 학자로 소문이 났다."], ["An individual whose fame spreads three or four thousand yards in the time taken by the fame of others similarly situated to travel six or eight hundred, must of necessity have something in him.", "비슷한 처지에 놓인 다른 이들의 명성이 6,700야드를 가는 동안 자기 명성을 3,4천 야드나 퍼뜨리는 자라면 필시 무언가 됨됨이가 있는 법이다."], ["Possibly Clym’s fame, like Homer’s, owed something to the accidents of his situation; nevertheless famous he was.", "어쩌면 클림의 명성도 호메로스의 명성처럼 처지의 우연에 얼마간 빚지고 있었는지 모르나, 어쨌든 그는 유명했다."]], [["He grew up and was helped out in life.", "그는 자라났고 살아가는 데 도움을 받았다."], ["That waggery of fate which started Clive as a writing clerk, Gay as a linen-draper, Keats as a surgeon, and a thousand others in a thousand other odd ways, banished the wild and ascetic heath lad to a trade whose sole concern was with the especial symbols of self-indulgence and vainglory.", "클라이브를 필경사로, 게이를 포목상으로, 키츠를 외과의로, 그밖에 수천 명을 수천 가지 기이한 길로 들여보낸 그 운명의 익살이, 거칠고 금욕적인 황야의 소년을 자기탐닉과 헛된 영광의 특별한 상징만을 다루는 장사로 추방해버렸다."]], [["The details of this choice of a business for him it is not necessary to give.", "그를 위해 이 장사를 택하게 된 자초지종은 굳이 밝힐 필요가 없다."], ["At the death of his father a neighbouring gentleman had kindly undertaken to give the boy a start, and this assumed the form of sending him to Budmouth.", "아버지가 돌아가셨을 때 이웃의 한 신사가 친절하게도 그 소년에게 출발의 발판을 마련해주겠노라 나섰는데, 그것이 그를 버드머스로 보내는 형태를 띠었다."], ["Yeobright did not wish to go there, but it was the only feasible opening.", "요브라이트는 그곳에 가고 싶지 않았으나 그것이 유일하게 실현 가능한 길이었다."], ["Thence he went to London; and thence, shortly after, to Paris, where he had remained till now.", "거기서 그는 런던으로 갔고, 거기서 얼마 안 가 파리로 가서 지금까지 머물러 있었던 것이다."]], [["Something being expected of him, he had not been at home many days before a great curiosity as to why he stayed on so long began to arise in the heath.", "무언가 기대를 모으던 인물인지라, 그가 고향에 돌아온 지 며칠 되지도 않아 그가 어째서 그토록 오래 눌러앉아 있는가에 대한 큰 호기심이 황야에 일기 시작했다."], ["The natural term of a holiday had passed, yet he still remained.", "휴가의 자연스러운 기한이 지났는데도 그는 여전히 머물러 있었다."], ["On the Sunday morning following the week of Thomasin’s marriage a discussion on this subject was in progress at a hair-cutting before Fairway’s house.", "토머신이 결혼한 그 주가 지난 뒤 일요일 아침, 페어웨이의 집 앞에서 이발이 이루어지던 중에 이 화제를 두고 한바탕 토론이 벌어졌다."], ["Here the local barbering was always done at this hour on this day, to be followed by the great Sunday wash of the inhabitants at noon, which in its turn was followed by the great Sunday dressing an hour later.", "이 시각 이날이면 그곳에서 늘 동네 이발이 행해졌고, 그다음 정오에는 주민들의 거창한 일요 목욕이 뒤따랐으며, 그것은 다시 한 시간 뒤 거창한 일요 단장으로 이어졌다."], ["On Egdon Heath Sunday proper did not begin till dinner-time, and even then it was a somewhat battered specimen of the day.", "에그던 황야에서 본격적인 일요일은 점심때나 되어야 시작되었고, 그때조차 다소 닳고 망가진 표본 같은 하루였다."]], [["These Sunday-morning hair-cuttings were performed by Fairway; the victim sitting on a chopping-block in front of the house, without a coat, and the neighbours gossiping around, idly observing the locks of hair as they rose upon the wind after the snip, and flew away out of sight to the four quarters of the heavens.", "이 일요일 아침의 이발은 페어웨이가 맡아 했는데, 희생자는 웃옷을 벗은 채 집 앞 장작 패는 받침대에 걸터앉고, 이웃들은 그 둘레에 둘러서서 잡담을 나누며, 가위질 뒤에 바람을 타고 솟아올라 하늘 사방으로 날아가 시야에서 사라지는 머리카락을 무심히 바라보았다."], ["Summer and winter the scene was the same, unless the wind were more than usually blusterous, when the stool was shifted a few feet round the corner.", "여름이든 겨울이든 풍경은 한결같았으니, 다만 바람이 평소보다 더 사납게 몰아칠 때면 의자를 모퉁이 돌아 몇 자쯤 옮길 따름이었다."], ["To complain of cold in sitting out of doors, hatless and coatless, while Fairway told true stories between the cuts of the scissors, would have been to pronounce yourself no man at once.", "페어웨이가 가위질 사이사이 진짜 있었던 이야기를 들려주는 동안 모자도 웃옷도 없이 바깥에 앉아 있으면서 춥다고 불평하는 것은, 곧장 사내답지 못함을 자처하는 격이었다."], ["To flinch, exclaim, or move a muscle of the face at the small stabs under the ear received from those instruments, or at scarifications of the neck by the comb, would have been thought a gross breach of good manners, considering that Fairway did it all for nothing.", "그 도구가 귀 밑을 콕콕 찌르거나 빗이 목덜미를 긁어낼 때 움찔하거나 소리치거나 얼굴 근육 하나라도 움직이는 것은, 페어웨이가 이 모든 일을 거저 해준다는 점을 생각하면 예의에 심히 어긋나는 짓으로 여겨졌다."], ["A bleeding about the poll on Sunday afternoons was amply accounted for by the explanation.", "일요일 오후에 정수리께가 피로 물든 것은 “나 머리 깎았잖아”"], ["“I have had my hair cut, you know.”", "하는 설명으로 충분히 해명되었다."]], [["The conversation on Yeobright had been started by a distant view of the young man rambling leisurely across the heath before them.", "요브라이트에 관한 이야기는 그 젊은이가 그들 앞에서 황야를 가로질러 한가로이 거니는 것이 멀리 보이는 데서 비롯되었다."]], [["“A man who is doing well elsewhere wouldn’t bide here two or three weeks for nothing,” said Fairway.", "“딴 데서 잘나가는 사람이라면 까닭 없이 이런 데서 두세 주씩 눌러앉지 않을 거여”라고 페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“He’s got some project in ’s head—depend upon that.”", "“머릿속에 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있는 거지―틀림없어.”"]], [["“Well, ’a can’t keep a diment shop here,” said Sam.", "“글쎄, 여기서 다이아몬드 가게를 차릴 수도 없는 노릇이고”라고 샘이 말했다."]], [["“I don’t see why he should have had them two heavy boxes home if he had not been going to bide; and what there is for him to do here the Lord in heaven knows.”", "“여기 눌러앉을 작정이 아니었담 그 무거운 상자 둘을 왜 집에 들였겠어. 게다가 여기서 뭘 할 게 있는지는 하늘에 계신 주님이나 아실 일이지.”"]], [["Before many more surmises could be indulged in Yeobright had come near; and seeing the hair-cutting group he turned aside to join them.", "더 많은 추측이 오가기도 전에 요브라이트가 가까이 다가왔다. 이발하는 무리를 보고는 그쪽으로 길을 틀어 합류했다."], ["Marching up, and looking critically at their faces for a moment, he said, without introduction, “Now, folks, let me guess what you have been talking about.”", "성큼성큼 다가와 잠시 그들의 얼굴을 유심히 살피더니 인사도 없이 말했다. “자, 여러분, 무슨 얘기를 하고 있었는지 내가 한번 맞혀보지요.”"]], [["“Ay, sure, if you will,” said Sam.", "“암, 그러시구려, 정 그러고 싶으시다면”이라고 샘이 말했다."]], [["“About me.”", "“내 얘기였죠.”"]], [["“Now, it is a thing I shouldn’t have dreamed of doing, otherwise,”", "“원, 안 그랬더라면야 꿈에도 그럴 생각이 없었지요”라고 페어웨이가 정직한 어조로 말했다."], ["said Fairway in a tone of integrity; “but since you have named it, Master Yeobright, I’ll own that we was talking about ’ee.", "“허나 도련님이 먼저 꺼내셨으니 인정하리다, 요브라이트 도련님, 우리가 도련님 얘기를 하던 참이오."], ["We were wondering what could keep you home here mollyhorning about when you have made such a world-wide name for yourself in the nick-nack trade—now, that’s the truth o’t.”", "자질구레한 장신구 장사로 세상이 다 아는 이름을 얻으신 양반이 어째 집구석에 처박혀 빈둥대고 계시나 하고 궁금해하던 참이지요―자, 그게 사실이오.”"]], [["“I’ll tell you,” said Yeobright with unexpected earnestness.", "“말씀드리지요”라고 요브라이트가 뜻밖의 진지함을 띠고 말했다."], ["“I am not sorry to have the opportunity.", "“그 기회를 얻은 게 싫지 않습니다."], ["I’ve come home because, all things considered, I can be a trifle less useless here than anywhere else.", "제가 고향에 돌아온 건, 이것저것 따져볼 때 다른 어디서보다 여기서 조금이나마 덜 쓸모없을 수 있기 때문입니다."], ["But I have only lately found this out.", "그런데 이걸 깨달은 건 얼마 안 됐어요."], ["When I first got away from home I thought this place was not worth troubling about.", "처음 고향을 떠났을 땐 이곳이 신경 쓸 가치도 없는 데라고 여겼지요."], ["I thought our life here was contemptible.", "우리네 여기 삶이 경멸스럽다고 생각했어요."], ["To oil your boots instead of blacking them, to dust your coat with a switch instead of a brush—was there ever anything more ridiculous? I said.”", "장화에 구두약을 칠하는 대신 기름을 먹이고, 솔질로 웃옷 먼지를 터는 대신 회초리로 터는―이보다 더 우스꽝스러운 게 어디 있겠나, 했지요.”"]], [["“So ’tis; so ’tis!”", "“그렇고말고요, 그렇고말고요!”"]], [["“No, no—you are wrong; it isn’t.”", "“아니, 아니―틀렸어요, 그렇지가 않아요.”"]], [["“Beg your pardon, we thought that was your maning?”", "“실례지만, 그게 도련님 뜻인 줄 알았는뎁쇼?”"]], [["“Well, as my views changed my course became very depressing.", "“음, 제 생각이 바뀌면서 제 행로가 무척 우울해졌어요."], ["I found that I was trying to be like people who had hardly anything in common with myself.", "알고 보니 저는 저와 거의 아무 공통점도 없는 사람들을 닮으려 애쓰고 있더군요."], ["I was endeavouring to put off one sort of life for another sort of life, which was not better than the life I had known before.", "한 종류의 삶을 벗어던지고 다른 종류의 삶을 입으려 안간힘을 쓰고 있었는데, 그것은 전에 알던 삶보다 나을 것이 없었어요."], ["It was simply different.”", "그저 다를 뿐이었지요.”"]], [["“True; a sight different,” said Fairway.", "“그렇소, 한참 다르고말고요”라고 페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Paris must be a taking place,” said Humphrey.", "“하긴, 파리는 사람을 사로잡는 데일 거요”라고 험프리가 말했다."], ["“Grand shop-winders, trumpets, and drums; and here be we out of doors in all winds and weathers—”", "“으리으리한 진열창, 나팔이며 북소리―헌데 우리는 온갖 바람과 날씨에 바깥에서 지내니―”"]], [["“But you mistake me,” pleaded Clym.", "“하지만 제 말을 오해하셨군요”라고 클림이 호소하듯 말했다."], ["“All this was very depressing.", "“이 모든 게 무척 우울했어요."], ["But not so depressing as something I next perceived—that my business was the idlest, vainest, most effeminate business that ever a man could be put to.", "그런데 그다음에 깨달은 것만큼 우울하진 않았지요―제 장사가 사내가 떠맡을 수 있는 일 가운데 가장 한가롭고 가장 헛되고 가장 계집애 같은 장사라는 걸 말입니다."], ["That decided me—I would give it up and try to follow some rational occupation among the people I knew best, and to whom I could be of most use.", "그게 저를 결심하게 했어요―그걸 그만두고 내가 가장 잘 아는 사람들, 내가 가장 쓸모 있을 사람들 틈에서 이성적인 일을 좇아보자고요."], ["I have come home; and this is how I mean to carry out my plan.", "그래서 고향에 돌아온 겁니다. 제 계획을 이렇게 실행할 작정이에요."], ["I shall keep a school as near to Egdon as possible, so as to be able to walk over here and have a night-school in my mother’s house.", "에그던에서 되도록 가까운 곳에 학교를 열어, 이리로 걸어와 어머니 댁에서 야학을 열 수 있게 할 겁니다."], ["But I must study a little at first, to get properly qualified.", "다만 처음엔 제대로 자격을 갖추려고 공부를 좀 해야겠지요."], ["Now, neighbours, I must go.”", "자, 여러분, 저는 이만 가봐야겠습니다.”"]], [["And Clym resumed his walk across the heath.", "그러고는 클림은 황야를 가로지르던 걸음을 다시 옮겼다."]], [["“He’ll never carry it out in the world,” said Fairway.", "“저 사람 절대 그걸 끝까지 못 해낼 거여”라고 페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“In a few weeks he’ll learn to see things otherwise.”", "“몇 주만 지나면 세상을 달리 보는 법을 배우겠지.”"]], [["“’Tis good-hearted of the young man,” said another.", "“마음씨가 고운 젊은이야”라고 다른 이가 말했다."], ["“But, for my part, I think he had better mind his business.”", "“허나 내 생각엔 제 본업이나 신경 쓰는 게 나을 거여.”"]], [["II.", "II."], ["The New Course Causes Disappointment", "새로운 행로가 실망을 부르다"]], [["Yeobright loved his kind.", "요브라이트는 동족을 사랑했다."], ["He had a conviction that the want of most men was knowledge of a sort which brings wisdom rather than affluence.", "그는 대다수 사람에게 부족한 것이 부유함이 아니라 지혜를 가져다주는 종류의 지식이라고 확신했다."], ["He wished to raise the class at the expense of individuals rather than individuals at the expense of the class.", "그는 개인을 희생시켜 계층을 높이기보다 계층을 높이는 데 개인을 희생시키고자 했다."], ["What was more, he was ready at once to be the first unit sacrificed.", "더구나 그는 당장에라도 가장 먼저 희생되는 한 단위가 될 각오가 되어 있었다."]], [["In passing from the bucolic to the intellectual life the intermediate stages are usually two at least, frequently many more; and one of those stages is almost sure to be worldly advanced.", "전원의 삶에서 지성의 삶으로 넘어갈 때 중간 단계는 보통 적어도 둘, 흔히는 그보다 훨씬 많거니와, 그 단계들 가운데 하나는 거의 틀림없이 세속적으로 출세하는 단계다."], ["We can hardly imagine bucolic placidity quickening to intellectual aims without imagining social aims as the transitional phase.", "사회적 야심을 과도기적 국면으로 떠올리지 않고서 전원의 평온이 지적 목표로 빠르게 나아간다고 상상하기란 좀처럼 어렵다."], ["Yeobright’s local peculiarity was that in striving at high thinking he still cleaved to plain living—nay, wild and meagre living in many respects, and brotherliness with clowns.", "요브라이트의 지역적 특이함은, 그가 고매한 사유에 힘쓰면서도 여전히 검소한 생활―아니, 여러 면에서 거칠고 빈한한 생활, 그리고 시골뜨기들과의 형제애에 매달려 있었다는 점이다."]], [["He was a John the Baptist who took ennoblement rather than repentance for his text.", "그는 회개가 아니라 고양高揚을 설교의 주제로 삼은 세례 요한이었다."], ["Mentally he was in a provincial future, that is, he was in many points abreast with the central town thinkers of his date.", "정신적으로 그는 지방의 미래에 살고 있었으니, 곧 여러 면에서 당대 중심 도시 사상가들과 어깨를 나란히 하고 있었다."], ["Much of this development he may have owed to his studious life in Paris, where he had become acquainted with ethical systems popular at the time.", "이러한 발전의 상당 부분은 어쩌면 파리에서의 학구적 생활에 빚진 것일 수 있는데, 그곳에서 그는 당대에 유행하던 윤리 체계들을 접하게 되었다."]], [["In consequence of this relatively advanced position, Yeobright might have been called unfortunate.", "이렇듯 비교적 앞선 위치 탓에 요브라이트는 불운하다 일컬어졌을 법하다."], ["The rural world was not ripe for him.", "시골 세계는 그를 받아들일 만큼 무르익지 않았던 것이다."], ["A man should be only partially before his time—to be completely to the vanward in aspirations is fatal to fame.", "사람은 제 시대를 부분적으로만 앞서야 하거니와―포부에 있어 완전히 선두에 서는 것은 명성에 치명적이다."], ["Had Philip’s warlike son been intellectually so far ahead as to have attempted civilization without bloodshed, he would have been twice the godlike hero that he seemed, but nobody would have heard of an Alexander.", "필리포스의 호전적인 아들이 지성에서 너무도 앞서 있어 유혈 없이 문명화를 시도했더라면 그는 보이는 것의 두 배는 신과 같은 영웅이 되었으련만, 알렉산드로스라는 이름은 아무도 들어보지 못했으리라."]], [["In the interests of renown the forwardness should lie chiefly in the capacity to handle things.", "명성을 위해서라면 그 앞섬은 주로 사물을 다루는 능력에 있어야 한다."], ["Successful propagandists have succeeded because the doctrine they bring into form is that which their listeners have for some time felt without being able to shape.", "성공한 선전가들이 성공한 까닭은 그들이 형태로 빚어낸 교리가 곧 그들의 청중이 한동안 느껴왔으되 모양을 잡지 못하던 바로 그것이었기 때문이다."], ["A man who advocates æsthetic effort and deprecates social effort is only likely to be understood by a class to which social effort has become a stale matter.", "미적 노력을 옹호하고 사회적 노력을 폄하하는 사람은, 사회적 노력이 이미 진부해진 계층에게나 이해받을 법하다."], ["To argue upon the possibility of culture before luxury to the bucolic world may be to argue truly, but it is an attempt to disturb a sequence to which humanity has been long accustomed.", "사치에 앞서 교양이 가능한가를 두고 전원 세계에 논변을 펴는 것은 옳은 논변일지 몰라도, 인류가 오래도록 익숙해진 순서를 흐트러뜨리려는 시도다."], ["Yeobright preaching to the Egdon eremites that they might rise to a serene comprehensiveness without going through the process of enriching themselves was not unlike arguing to ancient Chaldeans that in ascending from earth to the pure empyrean it was not necessary to pass first into the intervening heaven of ether.", "에그던의 은자들에게 스스로 부유해지는 과정을 거치지 않고도 평온한 포괄성에 이를 수 있다고 설교하는 요브라이트는, 고대 칼데아인들에게 대지에서 순수한 최고천最高天으로 오를 때 굳이 그 사이에 놓인 에테르의 하늘을 먼저 통과할 필요가 없다고 논하는 것과 다를 바 없었다."]], [["Was Yeobright’s mind well-proportioned? No.", "요브라이트의 정신은 균형이 잘 잡혀 있었던가?"], ["A well proportioned mind is one which shows no particular bias; one of which we may safely say that it will never cause its owner to be confined as a madman, tortured as a heretic, or crucified as a blasphemer.", "아니다. 균형 잡힌 정신이란 특별한 편향을 드러내지 않는 정신이요, 그 소유자를 결코 광인으로 감금되게 하거나 이단자로 고문받게 하거나 신성모독자로 십자가에 못 박히게 하지 않으리라고 안심하고 말할 수 있는 정신이다."], ["Also, on the other hand, that it will never cause him to be applauded as a prophet, revered as a priest, or exalted as a king.", "또한 반대편으로는, 그 소유자가 결코 예언자로 갈채받거나 사제로 추앙받거나 왕으로 떠받들어지게 하지도 않을 정신이다."], ["Its usual blessings are happiness and mediocrity.", "그것이 주는 통상적 축복은 행복과 평범함이다."], ["It produces the poetry of Rogers, the paintings of West, the statecraft of North, the spiritual guidance of Tomline; enabling its possessors to find their way to wealth, to wind up well, to step with dignity off the stage, to die comfortably in their beds, and to get the decent monument which, in many cases, they deserve.", "그것은 로저스의 시, 웨스트의 그림, 노스의 치세, 톰라인의 영적 인도를 낳으니, 그 소유자들로 하여금 부에 이르는 길을 찾고, 잘 마무리짓고, 위엄 있게 무대에서 내려서고, 자기 침상에서 편안히 죽고, 많은 경우 마땅히 받을 만한 점잖은 기념비를 얻게 해준다."], ["It never would have allowed Yeobright to do such a ridiculous thing as throw up his business to benefit his fellow-creatures.", "그것은 요브라이트가 동족을 이롭게 하려고 제 장사를 내던지는 따위의 우스꽝스러운 짓을 하도록 결코 내버려두지 않았으리라."]], [["He walked along towards home without attending to paths.", "그는 길도 살피지 않은 채 집을 향해 걸었다."], ["If anyone knew the heath well it was Clym.", "황야를 잘 아는 이가 있다면 그건 클림이었다."], ["He was permeated with its scenes, with its substance, and with its odours.", "그는 황야의 풍경에, 그 본질에, 그 냄새에 흠뻑 배어 있었다."], ["He might be said to be its product.", "그는 황야의 산물이라 해도 좋았다."], ["His eyes had first opened thereon; with its appearance all the first images of his memory were mingled, his estimate of life had been coloured by it: his toys had been the flint knives and arrow-heads which he found there, wondering why stones should “grow”", "그의 눈은 그 위에서 처음 떠졌고, 기억 속 최초의 영상들이 모두 그 모습과 뒤섞여 있었으며, 그의 인생관은 그것에 물들어 있었으니, 그의 장난감은 그곳에서 주운 부싯돌 칼과 화살촉이었고―어째서 돌이 저토록 기이한 모양으로 “자라나는지”"], ["to such odd shapes; his flowers, the purple bells and yellow furze: his animal kingdom, the snakes and croppers; his society, its human haunters.", "의아해하면서―그의 꽃은 자줏빛 종 모양 꽃과 노란 가시금작화였으며, 그의 동물 왕국은 뱀과 망아지였고, 그의 사교계는 그곳을 드나드는 인간들이었다."], ["Take all the varying hates felt by Eustacia Vye towards the heath, and translate them into loves, and you have the heart of Clym.", "유스테이시아 바이가 황야를 향해 품은 갖가지 증오를 모두 취해 그것을 사랑으로 옮겨놓으면 곧 클림의 마음이 된다."], ["He gazed upon the wide prospect as he walked, and was glad.", "그는 걸으면서 광활한 전망을 바라보았고, 기뻤다."]], [["To many persons this Egdon was a place which had slipped out of its century generations ago, to intrude as an uncouth object into this.", "많은 이에게 이 에그던은 여러 세대 전에 제 세기에서 미끄러져 나와 이 세기에 투박한 물체로 끼어든 곳이었다."], ["It was an obsolete thing, and few cared to study it.", "그것은 한물간 것이었고, 그것을 굳이 살피려는 이는 드물었다."], ["How could this be otherwise in the days of square fields, plashed hedges, and meadows watered on a plan so rectangular that on a fine day they looked like silver gridirons?", "네모반듯한 밭, 엮어 다듬은 산울타리, 그리고 맑은 날이면 은빛 석쇠처럼 보일 만큼 직각으로 반듯하게 물을 댄 목초지의 시대에 어찌 그렇지 않을 수 있었으랴?"], ["The farmer, in his ride, who could smile at artificial grasses, look with solicitude at the coming corn, and sigh with sadness at the fly-eaten turnips, bestowed upon the distant upland of heath nothing better than a frown.", "말을 타고 가던 농부는 인공 목초를 보고 미소 짓고, 자라나는 곡식을 근심스레 바라보고, 벌레 먹은 순무를 보며 서글피 한숨짓다가도, 저 멀리 황야의 고지에는 찌푸린 얼굴이나 던질 뿐이었다."], ["But as for Yeobright, when he looked from the heights on his way he could not help indulging in a barbarous satisfaction at observing that, in some of the attempts at reclamation from the waste, tillage, after holding on for a year or two, had receded again in despair, the ferns and furze-tufts stubbornly reasserting themselves.", "그러나 요브라이트로 말하자면, 가는 길에 높은 곳에서 내려다볼 때면, 황무지를 개간하려던 시도들 가운데 더러는 경작이 한두 해 버티다 다시 절망하여 물러나고 양치류와 가시금작화 덤불이 끈질기게 제자리를 되찾은 것을 보며 야만적인 만족감에 빠지지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["He descended into the valley, and soon reached his home at Blooms-End.", "그는 골짜기로 내려갔고, 이내 블룸스엔드의 집에 다다랐다."], ["His mother was snipping dead leaves from the window-plants.", "어머니는 창가 화초에서 시든 잎을 따내고 있었다."], ["She looked up at him as if she did not understand the meaning of his long stay with her; her face had worn that look for several days.", "어머니는 그가 자기 곁에 오래 머무는 의미를 이해하지 못하겠다는 듯 그를 올려다보았으니, 며칠째 그 표정을 짓고 있었다."], ["He could perceive that the curiosity which had been shown by the hair-cutting group amounted in his mother to concern.", "이발하던 무리가 내비친 호기심이 어머니에게서는 근심에 이르렀음을 그는 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["But she had asked no question with her lips, even when the arrival of his trunk suggested that he was not going to leave her soon.", "그러나 그의 트렁크가 도착해 그가 곧 떠나지 않으리라는 것을 암시했을 때조차 어머니는 입으로는 아무 질문도 하지 않았다."], ["Her silence besought an explanation of him more loudly than words.", "어머니의 침묵은 말보다 더 큰 소리로 그에게 해명을 청했다."]], [["“I am not going back to Paris again, Mother,” he said.", "“다시는 파리로 돌아가지 않을 거예요, 어머니”라고 그가 말했다."], ["“At least, in my old capacity.", "“적어도 예전 일을 하러는요."], ["I have given up the business.”", "그 장사를 그만뒀어요.”"]], [["Mrs. Yeobright turned in pained surprise.", "요브라이트 부인은 고통스러운 놀라움에 몸을 돌렸다."], ["“I thought something was amiss, because of the boxes.", "“상자 때문에 뭔가 잘못됐구나 했지."], ["I wonder you did not tell me sooner.”", "진작 말하지 그랬니.”"]], [["“I ought to have done it.", "“그랬어야 했죠."], ["But I have been in doubt whether you would be pleased with my plan.", "하지만 제 계획을 어머니가 좋아하실지 미심쩍었어요."], ["I was not quite clear on a few points myself.", "저도 몇몇 점에서는 아주 분명하지가 않았고요."], ["I am going to take an entirely new course.”", "저는 완전히 새로운 길을 갈 작정이에요.”"]], [["“I am astonished, Clym.", "“놀랍구나, 클림."], ["How can you want to do better than you’ve been doing?”", "지금까지 해온 것보다 어떻게 더 잘하길 바랄 수 있단 말이냐?”"]], [["“Very easily.", "“아주 쉬워요."], ["But I shall not do better in the way you mean; I suppose it will be called doing worse.", "다만 어머니가 말씀하시는 식으로 더 잘하진 않을 겁니다. 아마 더 못한다고들 하겠지요."], ["But I hate that business of mine, and I want to do some worthy thing before I die.", "하지만 저는 그 장사가 싫고, 죽기 전에 무언가 보람된 일을 하고 싶어요."], ["As a schoolmaster I think to do it—a school-master to the poor and ignorant, to teach them what nobody else will.”", "교사가 되어 그걸 하려 합니다―가난하고 무지한 이들에게, 아무도 가르쳐주지 않을 것을 가르치는 교사 말이에요.”"]], [["“After all the trouble that has been taken to give you a start, and when there is nothing to do but to keep straight on towards affluence, you say you will be a poor man’s schoolmaster.", "“네 앞길을 열어주려고 그토록 애를 썼는데, 그저 부富를 향해 곧장 나아가기만 하면 되는 마당에, 가난뱅이의 교사가 되겠다고 하는구나."], ["Your fancies will be your ruin, Clym.”", "네 그 공상이 너를 망칠 거다, 클림.”"]], [["Mrs. Yeobright spoke calmly, but the force of feeling behind the words was but too apparent to one who knew her as well as her son did.", "요브라이트 부인은 차분히 말했으나, 그 말 뒤에 깔린 감정의 힘은 그녀를 아들만큼 잘 아는 이에게는 너무도 분명했다."], ["He did not answer.", "그는 대답하지 않았다."], ["There was in his face that hopelessness of being understood which comes when the objector is constitutionally beyond the reach of a logic that, even under favouring conditions, is almost too coarse a vehicle for the subtlety of the argument.", "그의 얼굴에는, 반박하는 이가 기질상 논리의 손길이 닿지 못하는 곳에 있을 때―유리한 조건 아래서조차 논리란 논변의 미묘함을 담기에 너무 거친 그릇인데―찾아오는, 이해받기를 단념한 빛이 어려 있었다."]], [["No more was said on the subject till the end of dinner.", "저녁 식사가 끝날 때까지 이 화제에 관해 더는 말이 없었다."], ["His mother then began, as if there had been no interval since the morning.", "그러다 어머니가, 아침 이래로 시간 간격이 전혀 없었다는 듯이 입을 열었다."], ["“It disturbs me, Clym, to find that you have come home with such thoughts as those.", "“클림, 네가 그런 생각을 품고 집에 돌아온 걸 알고 나니 마음이 어지럽구나."], ["I hadn’t the least idea that you meant to go backward in the world by your own free choice.", "네가 제 발로 세상에서 뒷걸음치려 한다곤 꿈에도 생각지 못했다."], ["Of course, I have always supposed you were going to push straight on, as other men do—all who deserve the name—when they have been put in a good way of doing well.”", "물론 나는 네가 다른 남자들처럼―그 이름값을 하는 모든 이들처럼―잘될 좋은 길에 들어섰으면 곧장 밀고 나가려니 늘 여겼지.”"]], [["“I cannot help it,” said Clym, in a troubled tone.", "“어쩔 수 없어요”라고 클림이 괴로운 어조로 말했다."], ["“Mother, I hate the flashy business.", "“어머니, 저는 그 번쩍거리는 장사가 싫어요."], ["Talk about men who deserve the name, can any man deserving the name waste his time in that effeminate way, when he sees half the world going to ruin for want of somebody to buckle to and teach them how to breast the misery they are born to?", "이름값 하는 남자 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 세상의 절반이 타고난 비참함을 어떻게 헤쳐나갈지 떨쳐 일어나 가르쳐줄 사람이 없어 파멸해가는 걸 빤히 보면서, 그 이름값 하는 사내가 그토록 계집애 같은 식으로 시간을 허비할 수 있겠어요?"], ["I get up every morning and see the whole creation groaning and travailing in pain, as St. Paul says, and yet there am I, trafficking in glittering splendours with wealthy women and titled libertines, and pandering to the meanest vanities—I, who have health and strength enough for anything.", "저는 아침마다 일어나 사도 바울의 말마따나 온 피조물이 고통 속에 신음하며 진통을 겪는 걸 보면서도, 부유한 여인들과 작위 있는 난봉꾼들을 상대로 번쩍거리는 사치품을 거래하고 가장 비천한 허영심에 영합하고 있어요―무엇이든 해낼 만큼 건강과 힘을 갖춘 제가 말이에요."], ["I have been troubled in my mind about it all the year, and the end is that I cannot do it any more.”", "일 년 내내 그 일로 마음이 괴로웠고, 결국은 더는 그걸 할 수 없게 됐어요.”"]], [["“Why can’t you do it as well as others?”", "“어째서 너는 남들만큼 잘 해내지 못한단 말이냐?”"]], [["“I don’t know, except that there are many things other people care for which I don’t; and that’s partly why I think I ought to do this.", "“모르겠어요, 다만 남들이 중히 여기는 많은 것을 제가 중히 여기지 않는다는 것뿐이죠. 그게 제가 이 일을 해야 한다고 생각하는 까닭의 일부고요."], ["For one thing, my body does not require much of me.", "우선 제 몸은 제게 많은 걸 요구하지 않아요."], ["I cannot enjoy delicacies; good things are wasted upon me.", "저는 진미珍味를 즐기지 못해요. 좋은 것들이 제게는 헛되이 버려지지요."], ["Well, I ought to turn that defect to advantage, and by being able to do without what other people require I can spend what such things cost upon anybody else.”", "그렇다면 저는 그 결함을 이로움으로 돌려, 남들이 필요로 하는 것 없이도 지낼 수 있는 만큼 그런 것에 드는 비용을 다른 누군가에게 쓸 수 있는 거예요.”"]], [["Now, Yeobright, having inherited some of these very instincts from the woman before him, could not fail to awaken a reciprocity in her through her feelings, if not by arguments, disguise it as she might for his good.", "한데 요브라이트는 바로 그 본능들 가운데 얼마간을 눈앞의 여인에게서 물려받았으니, 그녀가 그를 위한답시고 아무리 감추려 한들, 논변으로는 아니더라도 감정을 통해 그녀 안에 공명을 일깨우지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She spoke with less assurance.", "그녀는 한결 자신 없는 투로 말했다."], ["“And yet you might have been a wealthy man if you had only persevered.", "“그래도 네가 꾸준히 버티기만 했더라면 부유한 사람이 될 수도 있었어."], ["Manager to that large diamond establishment—what better can a man wish for?", "그 큰 다이아몬드 상회의 지배인이라니―사내가 그보다 더 바랄 게 뭐가 있겠니?"], ["What a post of trust and respect!", "얼마나 신임받고 존경받는 자리냐!"], ["I suppose you will be like your father; like him, you are getting weary of doing well.”", "너도 네 아버지를 닮아가는 모양이다. 그이처럼 너도 잘 사는 일에 싫증이 나는 게지.”"]], [["“No,” said her son, “I am not weary of that, though I am weary of what you mean by it.", "“아니에요”라고 아들이 말했다."], ["Mother, what is doing well?”", "“저는 그 일에 싫증 난 게 아니라, 어머니가 그렇게 부르시는 것에 싫증이 난 거예요. 어머니, 잘 산다는 게 뭐죠?”"]], [["Mrs. Yeobright was far too thoughtful a woman to be content with ready definitions, and, like the “What is wisdom?” of Plato’s Socrates, and the “What is truth?” of Pontius Pilate, Yeobright’s burning question received no answer.", "요브라이트 부인은 손쉬운 정의에 만족하기엔 너무도 사려 깊은 여인이었으니, 플라톤이 그린 소크라테스의 “지혜란 무엇인가?”와 본디오 빌라도의 “진리란 무엇인가?”처럼, 요브라이트의 불타는 물음도 답을 얻지 못했다."]], [["The silence was broken by the clash of the garden gate, a tap at the door, and its opening.", "그 침묵을 깬 것은 정원 문이 철컹 부딪치는 소리, 문을 똑똑 두드리는 소리, 그리고 문이 열리는 소리였다."], ["Christian Cantle appeared in the room in his Sunday clothes.", "크리스천 캔틀이 일요일 나들이옷 차림으로 방에 나타났다."]], [["It was the custom on Egdon to begin the preface to a story before absolutely entering the house, so as to be well in for the body of the narrative by the time visitor and visited stood face to face.", "에그던에서는 손님과 주인이 마주 서게 될 즈음에는 이미 이야기의 본론에 깊숙이 들어가 있도록, 집 안에 완전히 들어서기도 전에 이야기의 서두를 시작하는 것이 관례였다."], ["Christian had been saying to them while the door was leaving its latch, “To think that I, who go from home but once in a while, and hardly then, should have been there this morning!”", "크리스천은 문이 빗장에서 막 떨어지는 사이에 그들에게 말하고 있었다. “이렇게 어쩌다 한 번, 그것도 좀처럼 집을 안 나서는 제가, 글쎄 오늘 아침에 마침 거기 있었지 뭐예요!”"]], [["“’Tis news you have brought us, then, Christian?” said Mrs. Yeobright.", "“그래, 무슨 소식을 가져왔구먼, 크리스천?”이라고 요브라이트 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Ay, sure, about a witch, and ye must overlook my time o’ day; for, says I, ‘I must go and tell ’em, though they won’t have half done dinner.’ I assure ye it made me shake like a driven leaf.", "“암요, 마녀 얘기랍니다. 식사하실 때라 들이닥쳐 죄송해유, 글쎄, ‘가서 알려드려야지, 식사가 반도 안 끝나셨겠지만’ 하고 마음먹었거든유."], ["Do ye think any harm will come o’t?”", "정말이지 바람에 흩날리는 잎사귀처럼 부들부들 떨렸다니까유. 무슨 화라도 닥칠까유?”"]], [["“Well—what?”", "“글쎄―뭔데?”"]], [["“This morning at church we was all standing up, and the pa’son said, ‘Let us pray.’ ‘Well,’ thinks I, ‘one may as well kneel as stand’; so down I went; and, more than that, all the rest were as willing to oblige the man as I.", "“오늘 아침 교회에서 다들 일어서 있었는데, 목사님이 ‘기도합시다’ 하시더라고유. ‘그럼,’ 하고 생각했지유, ‘서 있느니 무릎 꿇는 게 낫겠다.’ 그래 무릎을 꿇었는데, 게다가 다른 사람들도 다 저처럼 그분 말씀 따를 마음이 있었던가 봐유."], ["We hadn’t been hard at it for more than a minute when a most terrible screech sounded through church, as if somebody had just gied up their heart’s blood.", "한 일 분도 열심히 안 했는데 글쎄 교회를 가로질러 가장 끔찍한 비명이 울리는 거예유, 누가 막 심장의 피라도 쏟은 것처럼유."], ["All the folk jumped up and then we found that Susan Nunsuch had pricked Miss Vye with a long stocking-needle, as she had threatened to do as soon as ever she could get the young lady to church, where she don’t come very often.", "다들 벌떡 일어났는데 알고 보니 수전 넌서치가 긴 양말 바늘로 바이 아가씨를 콕 찔렀더라고유. 그 아가씨를 교회로 끌어들이기만 하면―아가씨가 교회엔 잘 안 오거든유―당장 그러겠다고 별러온 대로유."], ["She’ve waited for this chance for weeks, so as to draw her blood and put an end to the bewitching of Susan’s children that has been carried on so long.", "수전은 몇 주를 이 기회만 기다렸대유, 아가씨 피를 뽑아서 그토록 오래 이어진 제 아이들 홀림을 끝장내려고유."], ["Sue followed her into church, sat next to her, and as soon as she could find a chance in went the stocking-needle into my lady’s arm.”", "수는 아가씨를 따라 교회에 들어가 그 옆에 앉았다가, 기회가 나자마자 양말 바늘을 아가씨 팔에 푹 찔러 넣은 거지유.”"]], [["“Good heaven, how horrid!” said Mrs. Yeobright.", "“맙소사, 끔찍해라!”라고 요브라이트 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Sue pricked her that deep that the maid fainted away; and as I was afeard there might be some tumult among us, I got behind the bass viol and didn’t see no more.", "“수가 어찌나 깊이 찔렀던지 아가씨가 까무러쳤어유. 저는 우리 사이에 무슨 소동이 날까 겁이 나서 콘트라베이스 뒤에 숨어 더는 못 봤어유."], ["But they carried her out into the air, ’tis said; but when they looked round for Sue she was gone.", "하지만 사람들이 아가씨를 바깥바람 쐬게 들고 나갔다고들 하더라고유. 그런데 수를 찾으려고 둘러보니 사라지고 없었대유."], ["What a scream that girl gied, poor thing!", "그 아가씨가 어찌나 비명을 질렀던지, 가엾어라!"], ["There were the pa’son in his surplice holding up his hand and saying, ‘Sit down, my good people, sit down!’ But the deuce a bit would they sit down.", "목사님은 제의祭衣 차림으로 손을 들고 ‘앉으세요, 선량한 여러분, 앉으세요!’ 하셨지만, 도무지 누구 하나 앉질 않았어유."], ["O, and what d’ye think I found out, Mrs. Yeobright?", "아, 그리고 제가 뭘 알아냈게유, 요브라이트 부인?"], ["The pa’son wears a suit of clothes under his surplice!—I could see his black sleeves when he held up his arm.”", "목사님은 제의 밑에 양복을 받쳐 입으셨더라고유!―팔을 들 때 검은 소맷자락이 보였거든유.”"]], [["“’Tis a cruel thing,” said Yeobright.", "“잔인한 짓이야”라고 요브라이트가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,” said his mother.", "“그래”라고 어머니가 말했다."]], [["“The nation ought to look into it,” said Christian.", "“나라에서 들여다봐야 할 일이에유”라고 크리스천이 말했다."], ["“Here’s Humphrey coming, I think.”", "“저기 험프리가 오는 모양인데유.”"]], [["In came Humphrey.", "험프리가 들어왔다."], ["“Well, have ye heard the news?", "“자, 소식 들으셨소?"], ["But I see you have.", "아니, 들으신 모양이네."], ["’Tis a very strange thing that whenever one of Egdon folk goes to church some rum job or other is sure to be doing.", "참 묘한 일이지, 에그던 사람 하나가 교회에 갈 때마다 무슨 괴상한 일이 꼭 벌어진다니까."], ["The last time one of us was there was when neighbour Fairway went in the fall; and that was the day you forbad the banns, Mrs. Yeobright.”", "우리 중 누가 마지막으로 거기 갔던 게 이웃 페어웨이가 가을에 갔을 때인데, 그날이 바로 부인이 결혼 공시를 막으신 날이었잖수, 요브라이트 부인.”"]], [["“Has this cruelly treated girl been able to walk home?” said Clym.", "“그 잔인하게 당한 아가씨는 집까지 걸어갈 수 있었답니까?”라고 클림이 물었다."]], [["“They say she got better, and went home very well.", "“나아져서 멀쩡히 집에 갔다고들 합디다."], ["And now I’ve told it I must be moving homeward myself.”", "자, 이 얘길 했으니 나도 집으로 가봐야겠소.”"]], [["“And I,” said Humphrey.", "“나도요”라고 험프리가 말했다."], ["“Truly now we shall see if there’s anything in what folks say about her.”", "“이제야 사람들이 그 아가씨를 두고 하는 말에 뭔가가 있는지 알게 되겠구려.”"]], [["When they were gone into the heath again Yeobright said quietly to his mother, “Do you think I have turned teacher too soon?”", "그들이 다시 황야로 나가버리자 요브라이트는 어머니에게 조용히 말했다. “제가 너무 일찍 교사가 되겠다고 한 걸까요?”"]], [["“It is right that there should be schoolmasters, and missionaries, and all such men,”", "“교사도, 선교사도, 그런 사람들도 다 있어야 마땅하지”라고 어머니가 대답했다."], ["she replied. “But it is right, too, that I should try to lift you out of this life into something richer, and that you should not come back again, and be as if I had not tried at all.”", "“하지만 내가 너를 이런 삶에서 끌어내 더 풍요로운 무언가로 올려놓으려 애쓰는 것도 마땅하고, 네가 다시 돌아와 마치 내가 도무지 애쓰지 않았던 것처럼 되지 않는 것도 마땅하단다.”"]], [["Later in the day Sam, the turf-cutter, entered.", "그날 늦게 잔디 베는 일꾼 샘이 들어왔다."], ["“I’ve come a-borrowing, Mrs. Yeobright.", "“뭐 좀 빌리러 왔어유, 요브라이트 부인."], ["I suppose you have heard what’s been happening to the beauty on the hill?”", "언덕 위 미인한테 무슨 일이 있었는지 들으셨지유?”"]], [["“Yes, Sam: half a dozen have been telling us.”", "“그래, 샘. 대여섯이 우리한테 얘기해줬어.”"]], [["“Beauty?” said Clym.", "“미인?”이라고 클림이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, tolerably well-favoured,” Sam replied.", "“예, 꽤 잘생긴 편이지유”라고 샘이 대답했다."], ["“Lord!", "“원!"], ["all the country owns that ’tis one of the strangest things in the world that such a woman should have come to live up there.” “Dark or fair?”", "그런 여자가 저 위에 와서 산다는 게 세상에서 가장 묘한 일 중 하나라고 온 동네가 입을 모은다니까유.”"]], [["“Now, though I’ve seen her twenty times, that’s a thing I cannot call to mind.”", "“검은 머리던가, 금발이던가?” “원, 스무 번은 봤는데도 그건 통 떠오르질 않네유.”"]], [["“Darker than Tamsin,” murmured Mrs. Yeobright.", "“탬신보다 가무잡잡하지”라고 요브라이트 부인이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“A woman who seems to care for nothing at all, as you may say.”", "“말하자면 아무것도 통 마음에 두지 않는 여자라고 할까유.”"]], [["“She is melancholy, then?” inquired Clym.", "“그럼 우울한 사람인가?”라고 클림이 물었다."]], [["“She mopes about by herself, and don’t mix in with the people.”", "“혼자 시무룩하게 돌아다니고, 사람들과 어울리지 않아유.”"]], [["“Is she a young lady inclined for adventures?”", "“모험을 즐기는 아가씬가?”"]], [["“Not to my knowledge.”", "“제가 알기론 아니에유.”"]], [["“Doesn’t join in with the lads in their games, to get some sort of excitement in this lonely place?”", "“이 외진 곳에서 무슨 자극이라도 얻으려고 젊은 사내들 놀음에 끼지도 않고?” “안 끼어유.”"]], [["“No.” “Mumming, for instance?”", "“이를테면 가면극 같은 거 말이야?”"]], [["“No. Her notions be different.", "“안 해유."], ["I should rather say her thoughts were far away from here, with lords and ladies she’ll never know, and mansions she’ll never see again.”", "그 아가씬 생각하는 게 달라유. 차라리 그 아가씨 마음은 여기서 멀리 떨어진 데, 결코 알지 못할 귀족 나리들과 부인들, 다시는 보지 못할 저택들에 가 있다고 해야겠지유.”"]], [["Observing that Clym appeared singularly interested Mrs.", "클림이 유달리 흥미로워하는 것을 알아챈 요브라이트 부인이 다소 불안한 듯 샘에게 말했다."], ["Yeobright said rather uneasily to Sam, “You see more in her than most of us do.", "“자네는 우리 대부분보다 그 아가씨한테서 더 많은 걸 보는구먼."], ["Miss Vye is to my mind too idle to be charming.", "바이 아가씨는 내 보기에 매력을 부리기엔 너무 게을러."], ["I have never heard that she is of any use to herself or to other people.", "그 애가 제게든 남에게든 무슨 쓸모가 있다는 말은 들어본 적이 없어."], ["Good girls don’t get treated as witches even on Egdon.”", "착한 아가씨라면 에그던에서조차 마녀 취급은 안 받지.”"]], [["“Nonsense—that proves nothing either way,” said Yeobright.", "“말도 안 돼요―그건 어느 쪽으로도 아무것도 증명하지 못해요”라고 요브라이트가 말했다."]], [["“Well, of course I don’t understand such niceties,” said Sam, withdrawing from a possibly unpleasant argument; “and what she is we must wait for time to tell us.", "“글쎄, 물론 저야 그런 미묘한 건 모르지유”라고 샘이 자칫 불쾌해질 수 있는 논쟁에서 물러나며 말했다."], ["The business that I have really called about is this, to borrow the longest and strongest rope you have.", "“그 아가씨가 어떤 사람인지는 세월이 일러줄 때까지 기다려봐야겠지유. 제가 정말로 들른 용건은 이거예유, 댁에 있는 가장 길고 튼튼한 밧줄을 좀 빌리려고유."], ["The captain’s bucket has dropped into the well, and they are in want of water; and as all the chaps are at home today we think we can get it out for him.", "선장님 두레박이 우물에 빠져서 물이 없어 곤란하대유. 오늘은 사내들이 다 집에 있어서 두레박을 꺼내드릴 수 있을 것 같아유."], ["We have three cart-ropes already, but they won’t reach to the bottom.”", "수레용 밧줄이 벌써 셋 있긴 한데, 바닥까지 닿질 않아유.”"]], [["Mrs. Yeobright told him that he might have whatever ropes he could find in the outhouse, and Sam went out to search.", "요브라이트 부인은 헛간에서 찾을 수 있는 밧줄은 무엇이든 가져가도 좋다고 일러주었고, 샘은 찾으러 나갔다."], ["When he passed by the door Clym joined him, and accompanied him to the gate.", "그가 문을 지나칠 때 클림이 합류해 문간까지 그와 동행했다."]], [["“Is this young witch-lady going to stay long at Mistover?” he asked.", "“그 젊은 마녀 아가씨는 미스트오버에 오래 머물 작정인가?”라고 그가 물었다."]], [["“I should say so.”", "“그럴 것 같은데유.”"]], [["“What a cruel shame to ill-use her, She must have suffered greatly—more in mind than in body.”", "“그 아가씨를 그렇게 못살게 굴다니 어쩌면 그리 잔인할까. 몹시 괴로웠겠지―몸보다 마음이 더.”"]], [["“’Twas a graceless trick—such a handsome girl, too.", "“참 못된 짓이었지유―게다가 그렇게 잘생긴 아가씨를."], ["You ought to see her, Mr. Yeobright, being a young man come from far, and with a little more to show for your years than most of us.”", "한번 보셔야 해유, 요브라이트 도련님, 멀리서 오신 젊은 분이고, 게다가 우리 대부분보다 나이값에 보여줄 것도 좀 더 있으신데.”"]], [["“Do you think she would like to teach children?” said Clym.", "“그 아가씨가 아이들을 가르치고 싶어 할 것 같은가?”라고 클림이 말했다."]], [["Sam shook his head.", "샘은 고개를 가로저었다."], ["“Quite a different sort of body from that, I reckon.”", "“그것과는 영 다른 부류의 사람이지 싶은데유.”"]], [["“O, it was merely something which occurred to me.", "“아, 그냥 문득 떠오른 생각이야."], ["It would of course be necessary to see her and talk it over—not an easy thing, by the way, for my family and hers are not very friendly.”", "물론 만나서 의논해봐야 알 일이지―한데 그게 쉬운 일은 아니야, 우리 집안과 그 집안이 그리 사이가 좋지 않으니.”"]], [["“I’ll tell you how you mid see her, Mr. Yeobright,”", "“어떻게 하면 그 아가씨를 만나실 수 있는지 일러드릴게유, 요브라이트 도련님”이라고 샘이 말했다."], ["said Sam. “We are going to grapple for the bucket at six o’clock tonight at her house, and you could lend a hand.", "“우리가 오늘 밤 여섯 시에 그 댁에서 두레박을 갈고리로 건져 올릴 참인데, 도련님이 거들어주실 수 있잖아유."], ["There’s five or six coming, but the well is deep, and another might be useful, if you don’t mind appearing in that shape.", "대여섯이 오긴 하는데 우물이 깊어서, 그런 차림으로 나서는 게 꺼림칙하지 않으시다면 한 사람 더 있으면 쓸모가 있을 거예유."], ["She’s sure to be walking round.”", "그 아가씨도 틀림없이 근처를 거닐 테고요.”"]], [["“I’ll think of it,”", "“생각해보지”라고 요브라이트가 말했다."], ["said Yeobright; and they parted.", "그렇게 그들은 헤어졌다."]], [["He thought of it a good deal; but nothing more was said about Eustacia inside the house at that time.", "그는 그 일을 꽤 곰곰이 생각했으나, 그때 집 안에서는 유스테이시아에 관해 더는 아무 말도 오가지 않았다."], ["Whether this romantic martyr to superstition and the melancholy mummer he had conversed with under the full moon were one and the same person remained as yet a problem.", "미신에 바쳐진 이 낭만적 순교자와, 보름달 아래서 그가 이야기를 나눈 그 우울한 가면극 배우가 한 사람이며 같은 사람인지는 아직 풀리지 않은 수수께끼로 남아 있었다."]], [["III.", "III."], ["The First Act in a Timeworn Drama", "세월에 닳은 한 편의 극, 그 첫 막"]], [["The afternoon was fine, and Yeobright walked on the heath for an hour with his mother.", "오후는 화창했고, 요브라이트는 어머니와 함께 한 시간 동안 황야를 거닐었다."], ["When they reached the lofty ridge which divided the valley of Blooms-End from the adjoining valley they stood still and looked round.", "블룸스엔드 골짜기를 이웃 골짜기와 가르는 높은 능선에 이르자 두 사람은 걸음을 멈추고 둘러보았다."], ["The Quiet Woman Inn was visible on the low margin of the heath in one direction, and afar on the other hand rose Mistover Knap.", "한쪽으로는 ‘과묵한 여인’ 주막이 황야의 나지막한 가장자리에 보였고, 저 멀리 다른 쪽으로는 미스트오버 냅이 솟아 있었다."]], [["“You mean to call on Thomasin?” he inquired.", "“토머신을 찾아가실 거죠?”라고 그가 물었다."]], [["“Yes.", "“그래."], ["But you need not come this time,” said his mother.", "하지만 이번엔 네가 안 와도 된다”라고 어머니가 말했다."]], [["“In that case I’ll branch off here, Mother.", "“그럼 저는 여기서 갈라설게요, 어머니."], ["I am going to Mistover.”", "미스트오버에 갈 거예요.”"]], [["Mrs. Yeobright turned to him inquiringly.", "요브라이트 부인은 캐묻는 눈길로 그를 돌아보았다."]], [["“I am going to help them get the bucket out of the captain’s well,” he continued.", "“선장님 우물에서 두레박을 꺼내는 걸 도우러 가는 거예요”라고 그가 이어 말했다."], ["“As it is so very deep I may be useful.", "“워낙 깊은 우물이라 제가 쓸모가 있을지 모르죠."], ["And I should like to see this Miss Vye—not so much for her good looks as for another reason.”", "그리고 그 바이 아가씨를 보고 싶기도 하고요―미모 때문이라기보다는 다른 이유로요.”"]], [["“Must you go?” his mother asked.", "“꼭 가야겠니?”라고 어머니가 물었다."]], [["“I thought to.”", "“그러려고요.”"]], [["And they parted.", "그렇게 두 사람은 헤어졌다."], ["“There is no help for it,” murmured Clym’s mother gloomily as he withdrew.", "“어쩔 도리가 없지”라고 클림의 어머니는 그가 멀어지는 동안 침울하게 중얼거렸다."], ["“They are sure to see each other.", "“저 둘은 결국 서로 보고 말 거야."], ["I wish Sam would carry his news to other houses than mine.”", "샘이 제 소식을 우리 집 말고 다른 집들에 가지고 갔더라면 좋았으련만.”"]], [["Clym’s retreating figure got smaller and smaller as it rose and fell over the hillocks on his way.", "클림의 멀어지는 형상은 가는 길에 작은 언덕들을 오르내리며 점점 더 작아졌다. “저 애는 마음이 여려”라고 요브라이트 부인은 그를 지켜보며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“He is tender-hearted,” said Mrs.", "“안 그랬다면야 별일 아니었을 텐데."], ["Yeobright to herself while she watched him; “otherwise it would matter little. How he’s going on!”", "저렇게 가는 꼴이라니!”"]], [["He was, indeed, walking with a will over the furze, as straight as a line, as if his life depended upon it.", "과연 그는 가시금작화 위를 마치 목숨이라도 걸린 듯 한 일자로 곧장, 의지를 다잡고 걸어가고 있었다."], ["His mother drew a long breath, and, abandoning the visit to Thomasin, turned back.", "그의 어머니는 길게 숨을 내쉬고는 토머신을 찾아가려던 것을 단념하고 발길을 돌렸다."], ["The evening films began to make nebulous pictures of the valleys, but the high lands still were raked by the declining rays of the winter sun, which glanced on Clym as he walked forward, eyed by every rabbit and field-fare around, a long shadow advancing in front of him.", "저녁의 안개막이 골짜기들에 어렴풋한 그림을 그리기 시작했으나, 높은 땅은 여전히 기우는 겨울 햇살에 빗질당하듯 비추어졌으니, 그 햇살은 앞으로 나아가는 클림을 비추었고, 둘레의 토끼며 들지빠귀가 모두 그를 지켜보는 가운데, 길게 늘어진 그림자 하나가 그의 앞에서 나아가고 있었다."]], [["On drawing near to the furze-covered bank and ditch which fortified the captain’s dwelling he could hear voices within, signifying that operations had been already begun.", "선장의 거처를 요새처럼 두른 가시금작화 덮인 둑과 도랑에 가까이 다가가자 안에서 말소리가 들렸으니, 작업이 이미 시작되었음을 알리는 소리였다."], ["At the side-entrance gate he stopped and looked over.", "옆문 출입구에서 그는 걸음을 멈추고 안을 넘겨다보았다."]], [["Half a dozen able-bodied men were standing in a line from the well-mouth, holding a rope which passed over the well-roller into the depths below.", "건장한 사내 대여섯이 우물 입구로부터 한 줄로 늘어서서, 우물의 도르래를 넘어 저 아래 깊은 곳으로 드리워진 밧줄을 붙들고 있었다."], ["Fairway, with a piece of smaller rope round his body, made fast to one of the standards, to guard against accidents, was leaning over the opening, his right hand clasping the vertical rope that descended into the well.", "페어웨이는 사고에 대비해 몸에 가는 밧줄 한 가닥을 둘러 기둥 하나에 단단히 묶고서, 우물 입구 위로 몸을 기울인 채, 오른손으로는 우물 속으로 내려간 수직 밧줄을 움켜쥐고 있었다."]], [["“Now, silence, folks,” said Fairway.", "“자, 조용히 하시오, 여러분”이라고 페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["The talking ceased, and Fairway gave a circular motion to the rope, as if he were stirring batter.", "이야기가 그치자 페어웨이는 마치 반죽을 휘젓듯 밧줄을 빙글빙글 돌렸다."], ["At the end of a minute a dull splashing reverberated from the bottom of the well; the helical twist he had imparted to the rope had reached the grapnel below.", "일 분쯤 지나자 둔탁하게 물 첨벙이는 소리가 우물 바닥에서 울려 올라왔다. 그가 밧줄에 먹인 나선형 비틀림이 저 아래 갈고리에까지 닿은 것이었다."]], [["“Haul!”", "“당겨!”라고 페어웨이가 말했다."], ["said Fairway; and the men who held the rope began to gather it over the wheel.", "밧줄을 붙들고 있던 사내들이 그것을 바퀴 위로 거두어들이기 시작했다."]], [["“I think we’ve got sommat,” said one of the haulers-in.", "“뭔가 걸린 것 같소”라고 당기던 이들 중 하나가 말했다."]], [["“Then pull steady,” said Fairway.", "“그럼 꾸준히 당기게”라고 페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["They gathered up more and more, till a regular dripping into the well could be heard below.", "그들은 점점 더 거두어들였고, 마침내 우물 아래로 규칙적으로 물방울 떨어지는 소리가 들렸다."], ["It grew smarter with the increasing height of the bucket, and presently a hundred and fifty feet of rope had been pulled in.", "두레박이 높이 올라올수록 그 소리는 더 또렷해졌고, 이윽고 150피트의 밧줄이 거두어졌다."]], [["Fairway then lit a lantern, tied it to another cord, and began lowering it into the well beside the first: Clym came forward and looked down.", "그러자 페어웨이는 등불을 켜 다른 끈에 매달아, 먼저 내린 밧줄 옆으로 우물 속에 내리기 시작했다. 클림이 앞으로 나아가 아래를 내려다보았다."], ["Strange humid leaves, which knew nothing of the seasons of the year, and quaint-natured mosses were revealed on the wellside as the lantern descended; till its rays fell upon a confused mass of rope and bucket dangling in the dank, dark air.", "등불이 내려갈수록 우물 벽에는 한 해의 계절 따위는 알지 못하는 기묘하게 축축한 잎사귀와 별난 성질의 이끼들이 드러났고, 마침내 그 빛이 눅눅하고 어두운 공기 속에 매달려 흔들리는 밧줄과 두레박의 뒤엉킨 덩어리 위로 떨어졌다."]], [["“We’ve only got en by the edge of the hoop—steady, for God’s sake!” said Fairway.", "“테두리 가장자리만 겨우 걸었으니―제발, 조심해!”라고 페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["They pulled with the greatest gentleness, till the wet bucket appeared about two yards below them, like a dead friend come to earth again.", "그들은 더없이 조심스럽게 당겼고, 마침내 젖은 두레박이 그들 두 자쯤 아래에 모습을 드러냈으니, 마치 죽은 벗이 다시 땅 위로 돌아온 듯했다."], ["Three or four hands were stretched out, then jerk went the rope, whizz went the wheel, the two foremost haulers fell backward, the beating of a falling body was heard, receding down the sides of the well, and a thunderous uproar arose at the bottom.", "서너 개의 손이 뻗어 나왔다. 그 순간 밧줄이 홱 풀리고, 바퀴가 윙 돌고, 맨 앞의 두 사람이 뒤로 나뒹굴고, 떨어지는 물체가 우물 벽을 따라 멀어지며 부딪치는 소리가 들리더니, 바닥에서 우레 같은 소동이 일었다."], ["The bucket was gone again.", "두레박이 다시 사라진 것이다."]], [["“Damn the bucket!” said Fairway.", "“빌어먹을 두레박!”이라고 페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["“Lower again,” said Sam.", "“다시 내려”라고 샘이 말했다."]], [["“I’m as stiff as a ram’s horn stooping so long,” said Fairway, standing up and stretching himself till his joints creaked.", "“이렇게 오래 구부리고 있었더니 숫양 뿔처럼 뻣뻣하구먼”이라고 페어웨이가 몸을 일으켜 관절이 우두둑 소리 나도록 기지개를 켜며 말했다."]], [["“Rest a few minutes, Timothy,” said Yeobright.", "“몇 분 쉬세요, 티머시”라고 요브라이트가 말했다."], ["“I’ll take your place.”", "“제가 자리를 대신하지요.”"]], [["The grapnel was again lowered.", "갈고리를 다시 내렸다."], ["Its smart impact upon the distant water reached their ears like a kiss, whereupon Yeobright knelt down, and leaning over the well began dragging the grapnel round and round as Fairway had done.", "저 멀리 물 위에 갈고리가 가볍게 부딪치는 소리가 마치 입맞춤처럼 그들의 귀에 닿았고, 이에 요브라이트는 무릎을 꿇고 우물 위로 몸을 기울여 페어웨이가 하던 대로 갈고리를 빙빙 돌리며 끌기 시작했다."]], [["“Tie a rope round him—it is dangerous!” cried a soft and anxious voice somewhere above them.", "“저 사람한테 밧줄을 둘러요―위험해요!”라고 그들 위 어딘가에서 부드럽고 근심 어린 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["Everybody turned.", "모두가 돌아보았다."], ["The speaker was a woman, gazing down upon the group from an upper window, whose panes blazed in the ruddy glare from the west.", "말한 이는 한 여인으로, 위층 창에서 아래 무리를 내려다보고 있었으니, 창유리는 서쪽에서 비쳐드는 불그스름한 노을에 타오르고 있었다."], ["Her lips were parted and she appeared for the moment to forget where she was.", "그녀의 입술은 벌어져 있었고, 잠시 자기가 어디 있는지조차 잊은 듯했다."]], [["The rope was accordingly tied round his waist, and the work proceeded.", "그리하여 그의 허리에 밧줄이 둘러졌고, 작업은 계속되었다."], ["At the next haul the weight was not heavy, and it was discovered that they had only secured a coil of the rope detached from the bucket.", "다음번 당길 때는 무게가 무겁지 않았으니, 알고 보니 두레박에서 떨어져나온 밧줄 한 사리만 건졌을 따름이었다."], ["The tangled mass was thrown into the background.", "그 뒤엉킨 덩어리를 뒤편으로 던져버렸다."], ["Humphrey took Yeobright’s place, and the grapnel was lowered again.", "험프리가 요브라이트의 자리를 넘겨받았고, 갈고리는 다시 내려졌다."]], [["Yeobright retired to the heap of recovered rope in a meditative mood.", "요브라이트는 건져 올린 밧줄 더미께로 물러나 사색에 잠겼다."], ["Of the identity between the lady’s voice and that of the melancholy mummer he had not a moment’s doubt.", "그 여인의 목소리와 그 우울한 가면극 배우의 목소리가 동일하다는 데는 한순간의 의심도 없었다."], ["“How thoughtful of her!” he said to himself.", "“참으로 사려 깊은 사람이로군!”이라고 그는 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["Eustacia, who had reddened when she perceived the effect of her exclamation upon the group below, was no longer to be seen at the window, though Yeobright scanned it wistfully.", "유스테이시아는 자기 외침이 아래 무리에게 미친 효과를 깨닫고 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 요브라이트가 아쉬운 듯 창을 살폈으나 더는 그곳에 보이지 않았다."], ["While he stood there the men at the well succeeded in getting up the bucket without a mishap.", "그가 거기 서 있는 동안 우물가의 사내들은 별 탈 없이 두레박을 건져 올리는 데 성공했다."], ["One of them went to inquire for the captain, to learn what orders he wished to give for mending the well-tackle.", "그들 중 하나가 선장에게 우물 도구를 고치는 데 어떤 지시를 내리고 싶은지 물으러 갔다."], ["The captain proved to be away from home, and Eustacia appeared at the door and came out.", "선장은 집을 비운 것으로 드러났고, 유스테이시아가 문간에 나타나더니 밖으로 나왔다."], ["She had lapsed into an easy and dignified calm, far removed from the intensity of life in her words of solicitude for Clym’s safety.", "그녀는 클림의 안전을 염려하던 말 속의 생기 넘치던 강렬함과는 사뭇 동떨어진, 편안하고 위엄 있는 차분함에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["“Will it be possible to draw water here tonight?” she inquired.", "“오늘 밤 여기서 물을 길을 수 있을까요?”라고 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“No, miss; the bottom of the bucket is clean knocked out.", "“안 됩니다, 아가씨. 두레박 밑바닥이 완전히 떨어져나갔어유."], ["And as we can do no more now we’ll leave off, and come again tomorrow morning.”", "이제 더는 어쩔 수 없으니 그만두고 내일 아침에 다시 오겠어유.”"]], [["“No water,” she murmured, turning away.", "“물이 없네”라고 그녀가 돌아서며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“I can send you up some from Blooms-End,” said Clym, coming forward and raising his hat as the men retired.", "“블룸스엔드에서 좀 보내드릴게요”라고 클림이, 사내들이 물러가는 가운데 앞으로 나서며 모자를 들어 올리며 말했다."]], [["Yeobright and Eustacia looked at each other for one instant, as if each had in mind those few moments during which a certain moonlight scene was common to both.", "요브라이트와 유스테이시아는 한순간 서로를 바라보았으니, 마치 둘 다 어떤 달빛 어린 정경이 둘 모두에게 공유되었던 그 짧은 순간들을 마음에 떠올린 듯했다."], ["With the glance the calm fixity of her features sublimed itself to an expression of refinement and warmth; it was like garish noon rising to the dignity of sunset in a couple of seconds.", "그 눈길과 더불어 그녀의 차분하게 굳어 있던 이목구비가 우아함과 따스함의 표정으로 승화되었으니, 그것은 마치 눈부신 한낮이 한두 초 만에 석양의 위엄으로 떠오르는 듯했다."]], [["“Thank you; it will hardly be necessary,” she replied.", "“고마워요, 하지만 그러실 것까진 없을 거예요”라고 그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“But if you have no water?”", "“그래도 물이 없으시잖아요?”"]], [["“Well, it is what I call no water,” she said, blushing, and lifting her long-lashed eyelids as if to lift them were a work requiring consideration.", "“글쎄요, 제가 물이 없다고 한 거예요”라고 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며, 마치 그 일이 숙고를 요하는 일이기라도 한 듯 긴 속눈썹의 눈꺼풀을 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["“But my grandfather calls it water enough.", "“하지만 할아버지는 그걸 충분한 물이라 부르신답니다."], ["I’ll show you what I mean.”", "제 말이 무슨 뜻인지 보여드리죠.”"]], [["She moved away a few yards, and Clym followed.", "그녀가 몇 자쯤 자리를 옮기자 클림이 뒤따랐다."], ["When she reached the corner of the enclosure, where the steps were formed for mounting the boundary bank, she sprang up with a lightness which seemed strange after her listless movement towards the well.", "경계 둑을 오르도록 계단이 만들어진 울타리 모퉁이에 이르자, 그녀는 우물 쪽으로 나른하게 움직이던 모습 뒤끝이라 기이하게 느껴질 만큼 가뿐히 뛰어올랐다."], ["It incidentally showed that her apparent languor did not arise from lack of force.", "이는 우연찮게도 그녀의 겉보기 나른함이 힘이 부족해서가 아님을 드러내 보였다."]], [["Clym ascended behind her, and noticed a circular burnt patch at the top of the bank.", "클림이 그녀 뒤를 따라 올라가다 둑 꼭대기에 둥글게 탄 자국이 있는 것을 보았다."], ["“Ashes?” he said.", "“재인가요?”라고 그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,” said Eustacia.", "“네”라고 유스테이시아가 말했다."], ["“We had a little bonfire here last Fifth of November, and those are the marks of it.”", "“지난 십일월 오일에 여기서 작은 모닥불을 피웠는데, 그 흔적이에요.”"]], [["On that spot had stood the fire she had kindled to attract Wildeve.", "바로 그 자리에 그녀가 와일디브를 끌어내려고 지폈던 불이 타올랐던 것이다."]], [["“That’s the only kind of water we have,” she continued, tossing a stone into the pool, which lay on the outside of the bank like the white of an eye without its pupil.", "“우리한테 물이라곤 저런 종류뿐이에요”라고 그녀가 둑 바깥쪽에, 동공 없는 흰자위처럼 놓인 못에 돌멩이 하나를 던지며 이어 말했다."], ["The stone fell with a flounce, but no Wildeve appeared on the other side, as on a previous occasion there.", "돌은 첨벙 소리를 내며 떨어졌으나, 지난번 그곳에서처럼 못 건너편에 와일디브가 나타나지는 않았다."], ["“My grandfather says he lived for more than twenty years at sea on water twice as bad as that,”", "“할아버지 말씀이 바다에서 이십 년 넘게 저것보다 두 배는 나쁜 물로 사셨대요”라고 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["she went on, “and considers it quite good enough for us here on an emergency.”", "“그래서 비상시에 여기 우리한테는 그 정도면 더없이 충분하다고 여기시지요.”"]], [["“Well, as a matter of fact there are no impurities in the water of these pools at this time of the year.", "“음, 사실 이맘때면 이런 못의 물에는 불순물이 없어요."], ["It has only just rained into them.”", "방금 빗물이 고인 것뿐이거든요.”"]], [["She shook her head.", "그녀는 고개를 가로저었다."], ["“I am managing to exist in a wilderness, but I cannot drink from a pond,” she said.", "“저는 어떻게든 황무지에서 버티고 있긴 하지만, 못물을 마실 수는 없어요”라고 그녀가 말했다."]], [["Clym looked towards the well, which was now deserted, the men having gone home.", "클림은 이제 사내들이 집으로 돌아가 텅 빈 우물 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["“It is a long way to send for spring-water,” he said, after a silence.", "“샘물을 길어 오라고 하기엔 길이 멀군요”라고 그가 한참 침묵 끝에 말했다."], ["“But since you don’t like this in the pond, I’ll try to get you some myself.”", "“하지만 이 못물이 싫으시다니, 제가 직접 좀 길어드려보지요.”"], ["He went back to the well.", "그는 우물로 돌아갔다."], ["“Yes, I think I could do it by tying on this pail.”", "“그래요, 이 들통을 매달면 될 것 같아요.”"]], [["“But, since I would not trouble the men to get it, I cannot in conscience let you.”", "“하지만 사내들한테 길어달라고 폐를 끼치지 않으려는 마당에, 양심상 당신께 그러시게 둘 수는 없어요.”"]], [["“I don’t mind the trouble at all.”", "“번거로움 따위는 조금도 개의치 않아요.”"]], [["He made fast the pail to the long coil of rope, put it over the wheel, and allowed it to descend by letting the rope slip through his hands.", "그는 들통을 기다란 밧줄 사리에 단단히 매어 바퀴 위에 걸치고는, 밧줄이 손가락 사이로 미끄러져 나가게 하며 아래로 내렸다."], ["Before it had gone far, however, he checked it.", "그러나 멀리 내려가기 전에 그는 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“I must make fast the end first, or we may lose the whole,” he said to Eustacia, who had drawn near.", "“먼저 끝을 단단히 매어둬야겠어요, 안 그러면 통째로 잃을 수도 있으니”라고 그가, 가까이 다가와 있던 유스테이시아에게 말했다."], ["“Could you hold this a moment, while I do it—or shall I call your servant?”", "“제가 매는 동안 이걸 잠깐 잡아주시겠어요―아니면 하녀를 부를까요?”"]], [["“I can hold it,”", "“제가 잡을게요”라고 유스테이시아가 말했다."], ["said Eustacia; and he placed the rope in her hands, going then to search for the end.", "그가 밧줄을 그녀 손에 쥐여주고는 끝을 찾으러 갔다."]], [["“I suppose I may let it slip down?” she inquired.", "“이걸 미끄러뜨려 내려도 되겠죠?”라고 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“I would advise you not to let it go far,” said Clym.", "“멀리까지 내려가게 두지 않으시는 게 좋아요”라고 클림이 말했다."], ["“It will get much heavier, you will find.”", "“보시면 알겠지만 훨씬 무거워질 테니까요.”"]], [["However, Eustacia had begun to pay out.", "그러나 유스테이시아는 밧줄을 풀어 내리기 시작했다."], ["While he was tying she cried, “I cannot stop it!”", "그가 매고 있는 동안 그녀가 외쳤다. “멈출 수가 없어요!”"]], [["Clym ran to her side, and found he could only check the rope by twisting the loose part round the upright post, when it stopped with a jerk.", "클림이 그녀 곁으로 달려가, 풀린 부분을 곧추선 기둥에 둘러 감아야만 밧줄을 멈춰 세울 수 있음을 알았으니, 그러자 밧줄이 홱 멈췄다."], ["“Has it hurt you?”", "“다치셨어요?”"]], [["“Yes,” she replied.", "“네”라고 그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“Very much?”", "“많이요?”"]], [["“No; I think not.”", "“아니요, 그렇진 않은 것 같아요.”"], ["She opened her hands.", "그녀가 두 손을 펴 보였다."], ["One of them was bleeding; the rope had dragged off the skin.", "한 손에서 피가 나고 있었으니, 밧줄이 살갗을 벗겨낸 것이었다."], ["Eustacia wrapped it in her handkerchief.", "유스테이시아는 손을 손수건으로 감쌌다."]], [["“You should have let go,” said Yeobright.", "“놓으셨어야죠”라고 요브라이트가 말했다."], ["“Why didn’t you?”", "“왜 안 놓으셨어요?”"]], [["“You said I was to hold on....", "“당신이 붙들고 있으라 하셨잖아요……"], ["This is the second time I have been wounded today.”", "오늘 다친 게 이번이 두 번째예요.”"]], [["“Ah, yes; I have heard of it.", "“아, 그렇지요. 그 일은 저도 들었어요."], ["I blush for my native Egdon.", "제 고향 에그던이 부끄럽습니다."], ["Was it a serious injury you received in church, Miss Vye?”", "교회에서 입으신 상처가 심한가요, 바이 아가씨?”"]], [["There was such an abundance of sympathy in Clym’s tone that Eustacia slowly drew up her sleeve and disclosed her round white arm.", "클림의 어조에 동정이 어찌나 넘쳐흘렀던지 유스테이시아는 천천히 소매를 걷어 올려 둥글고 흰 팔을 드러냈다."], ["A bright red spot appeared on its smooth surface, like a ruby on Parian marble.", "그 매끄러운 살결 위에 밝은 붉은 점 하나가 돋아 있었으니, 마치 파로스 대리석 위의 루비 같았다."]], [["“There it is,” she said, putting her finger against the spot.", "“여기예요”라고 그녀가 그 점에 손가락을 갖다 대며 말했다."]], [["“It was dastardly of the woman,” said Clym.", "“그 여자가 비열하게 굴었군요”라고 클림이 말했다."], ["“Will not Captain Vye get her punished?”", "“바이 선장님이 그 여자를 벌받게 하지 않으실까요?”"]], [["“He is gone from home on that very business.", "“바로 그 일로 집을 비우셨어요."], ["I did not know that I had such a magic reputation.”", "제게 그런 마법적인 명성이 있는 줄은 몰랐네요.”"]], [["“And you fainted?” said Clym, looking at the scarlet little puncture as if he would like to kiss it and make it well.", "“그리고 까무러치셨다죠?”라고 클림이, 그 진홍빛 작은 구멍을 마치 입 맞춰 낫게 해주고 싶다는 듯 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, it frightened me.", "“네, 무서웠거든요."], ["I had not been to church for a long time.", "교회에 안 간 지 오래됐었어요."], ["And now I shall not go again for ever so long—perhaps never.", "그리고 이제 아주 오랫동안 다시는 안 갈 거예요―어쩌면 영영."], ["I cannot face their eyes after this.", "이 일이 있고 나선 사람들 눈을 마주할 수가 없어요."], ["Don’t you think it dreadfully humiliating?", "끔찍하게 모욕적이라고 생각지 않으세요?"], ["I wished I was dead for hours after, but I don’t mind now.”", "그 뒤로 몇 시간이나 죽어버렸으면 했지만, 지금은 괜찮아요.”"]], [["“I have come to clean away these cobwebs,” said Yeobright.", "“저는 이런 거미줄을 걷어내러 왔어요”라고 요브라이트가 말했다."], ["“Would you like to help me—by high-class teaching?", "“저를 도와주시겠어요―수준 높은 가르침으로요?"], ["We might benefit them much.”", "우리가 저들에게 큰 도움이 될 수 있을 거예요.”"]], [["“I don’t quite feel anxious to.", "“그러고 싶은 마음이 썩 들지 않네요."], ["I have not much love for my fellow-creatures.", "저는 제 동족을 그리 사랑하지 않아요."], ["Sometimes I quite hate them.”", "때로는 아주 미워하기까지 해요.”"]], [["“Still I think that if you were to hear my scheme you might take an interest in it.", "“그래도 제 계획을 들으시면 흥미를 느끼실지 모른다고 생각해요."], ["There is no use in hating people—if you hate anything, you should hate what produced them.”", "사람을 미워하는 건 아무 소용 없어요―무언가를 미워하려거든, 그들을 만들어낸 것을 미워하셔야죠.”"]], [["“Do you mean Nature?", "“자연을 말씀하시는 건가요?"], ["I hate her already.", "저는 이미 자연을 미워해요."], ["But I shall be glad to hear your scheme at any time.”", "하지만 당신 계획은 언제든 기꺼이 듣겠어요.”"]], [["The situation had now worked itself out, and the next natural thing was for them to part.", "이제 상황은 마무리되었고, 다음에 자연스러운 일은 두 사람이 헤어지는 것이었다."], ["Clym knew this well enough, and Eustacia made a move of conclusion; yet he looked at her as if he had one word more to say.", "클림도 이를 잘 알았고 유스테이시아도 마무리하는 몸짓을 했으나, 그는 한마디 더 할 말이 있다는 듯 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Perhaps if he had not lived in Paris it would never have been uttered.", "어쩌면 그가 파리에서 살아본 적이 없었더라면 그 말은 결코 나오지 않았으리라."]], [["“We have met before,” he said, regarding her with rather more interest than was necessary.", "“우리 전에 만난 적이 있죠”라고 그가 필요 이상으로 흥미를 띠고 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“I do not own it,” said Eustacia, with a repressed, still look.", "“저는 인정 못 해요”라고 유스테이시아가 억누른, 고요한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“But I may think what I like.”", "“하지만 제가 생각하고 싶은 대로 생각할 수야 있죠.”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“네.”"]], [["“You are lonely here.”", "“여기서 외로우시죠.”"]], [["“I cannot endure the heath, except in its purple season.", "“저는 황야를 견딜 수가 없어요, 자줏빛으로 물드는 철만 빼고는요."], ["The heath is a cruel taskmaster to me.”", "황야는 제게 잔인한 폭군이에요.”"]], [["“Can you say so?” he asked.", "“그렇게 말씀하실 수 있나요?”라고 그가 물었다."], ["“To my mind it is most exhilarating, and strengthening, and soothing.", "“제게는 더없이 기운을 북돋우고, 힘을 주고, 마음을 달래주는 곳인데요."], ["I would rather live on these hills than anywhere else in the world.”", "저는 세상 어디보다 이 언덕에서 살고 싶어요.”"]], [["“It is well enough for artists; but I never would learn to draw.”", "“화가들한테야 충분히 좋겠죠. 하지만 저는 그림 그리는 법은 결코 배우지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["“And there is a very curious druidical stone just out there.”", "“그리고 바로 저기 아주 진기한 드루이드 돌이 하나 있어요.”"], ["He threw a pebble in the direction signified.", "그가 가리키는 방향으로 조약돌 하나를 던졌다."], ["“Do you often go to see it?”", "“자주 보러 가시나요?”"]], [["“I was not even aware there existed any such curious druidical stone.", "“그런 진기한 드루이드 돌이 있는 줄도 몰랐어요."], ["I am aware that there are boulevards in Paris.”", "제가 아는 건 파리에 큰길들이 있다는 거예요.”"]], [["Yeobright looked thoughtfully on the ground.", "요브라이트는 생각에 잠겨 땅을 내려다보았다."], ["“That means much,” he said.", "“그건 많은 걸 뜻하죠”라고 그가 말했다."]], [["“It does indeed,” said Eustacia.", "“정말 그래요”라고 유스테이시아가 말했다."]], [["“I remember when I had the same longing for town bustle.", "“저도 도시의 북적임을 똑같이 갈망하던 때가 기억나요."], ["Five years of a great city would be a perfect cure for that.”", "대도시에서 오 년만 지내면 그건 완벽하게 치유될 거예요.”"]], [["“Heaven send me such a cure! Now, Mr.", "“하늘이 제게 그런 치유를 내려주시길!"], ["Yeobright, I will go indoors and plaster my wounded hand.”", "자, 요브라이트 씨, 저는 안으로 들어가 다친 손에 약이나 발라야겠어요.”"]], [["They separated, and Eustacia vanished in the increasing shade.", "두 사람은 헤어졌고, 유스테이시아는 짙어가는 그늘 속으로 사라졌다."], ["She seemed full of many things.", "그녀는 온갖 것으로 가득 찬 듯했다."], ["Her past was a blank, her life had begun.", "그녀의 과거는 백지였고, 그녀의 삶은 이제 막 시작된 것이었다."], ["The effect upon Clym of this meeting he did not fully discover till some time after.", "이 만남이 클림에게 미친 영향을 그는 한참 뒤에야 온전히 깨달았다."], ["During his walk home his most intelligible sensation was that his scheme had somehow become glorified.", "집으로 걸어 돌아오는 동안 그가 느낀 가장 또렷한 감각은, 자기 계획이 어쩐지 영광스러워졌다는 것이었다."], ["A beautiful woman had been intertwined with it.", "한 아름다운 여인이 그 계획에 얽혀든 것이었다."]], [["On reaching the house he went up to the room which was to be made his study, and occupied himself during the evening in unpacking his books from the boxes and arranging them on shelves.", "집에 도착하자 그는 서재로 쓸 방으로 올라가, 저녁 내내 상자에서 책을 꺼내 선반에 정리하는 데 몰두했다."], ["From another box he drew a lamp and a can of oil.", "다른 상자에서는 등잔과 기름통을 꺼냈다."], ["He trimmed the lamp, arranged his table, and said, “Now, I am ready to begin.”", "등잔 심지를 다듬고 탁자를 정돈하고는 말했다. “자, 이제 시작할 준비가 됐다.”"]], [["He rose early the next morning, read two hours before breakfast by the light of his lamp—read all the morning, all the afternoon.", "이튿날 아침 그는 일찍 일어나, 아침 식사 전에 두 시간을 등잔불에 의지해 읽었고―오전 내내, 오후 내내 읽었다."], ["Just when the sun was going down his eyes felt weary, and he leant back in his chair.", "마침 해가 지려 할 즈음 눈이 피로해져 그는 의자에 등을 기댔다."]], [["His room overlooked the front of the premises and the valley of the heath beyond.", "그의 방은 집 앞마당과 그 너머 황야의 골짜기를 굽어보았다."], ["The lowest beams of the winter sun threw the shadow of the house over the palings, across the grass margin of the heath, and far up the vale, where the chimney outlines and those of the surrounding tree-tops stretched forth in long dark prongs.", "겨울 햇살의 가장 낮은 빛줄기가 집의 그림자를 말뚝 울타리 너머로, 황야의 풀밭 가장자리를 가로질러, 골짜기 저 위까지 드리웠으니, 그곳에서는 굴뚝의 윤곽과 둘레 나무 우듬지들의 윤곽이 길고 어두운 갈래로 뻗어 있었다."], ["Having been seated at work all day, he decided to take a turn upon the hills before it got dark; and, going out forthwith, he struck across the heath towards Mistover.", "온종일 앉아 일했던 터라 그는 어두워지기 전에 언덕을 한 바퀴 거닐기로 마음먹고, 곧장 밖으로 나가 황야를 가로질러 미스트오버 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["It was an hour and a half later when he again appeared at the garden gate.", "그가 다시 정원 문에 나타난 것은 한 시간 반 뒤였다."], ["The shutters of the house were closed, and Christian Cantle, who had been wheeling manure about the garden all day, had gone home.", "집의 덧창은 닫혀 있었고, 온종일 정원에 거름을 수레로 나르던 크리스천 캔틀은 집으로 가버린 뒤였다."], ["On entering he found that his mother, after waiting a long time for him, had finished her meal.", "안으로 들어서니 어머니가 그를 오래 기다리다 식사를 끝낸 것을 알았다."]], [["“Where have you been, Clym?” she immediately said.", "“어디 갔었니, 클림?”이라고 어머니가 대뜸 물었다."], ["“Why didn’t you tell me that you were going away at this time?”", "“이런 시간에 나간다고 왜 말하지 않았어?”"]], [["“I have been on the heath.”", "“황야에 다녀왔어요.”"]], [["“You’ll meet Eustacia Vye if you go up there.”", "“거기 올라가면 유스테이시아 바이를 만날 텐데.”"]], [["Clym paused a minute.", "클림은 잠시 멈칫했다."], ["“Yes, I met her this evening,” he said, as though it were spoken under the sheer necessity of preserving honesty.", "“네, 오늘 저녁에 그녀를 만났어요”라고 그가, 마치 정직을 지켜야 한다는 절대적 필요에 떠밀려 내뱉듯이 말했다."]], [["“I wondered if you had.”", "“그랬을까 했다.”"]], [["“It was no appointment.”", "“약속한 건 아니었어요.”"]], [["“No; such meetings never are.”", "“그래, 그런 만남이란 결코 약속이 아니지.”"]], [["“But you are not angry, Mother?”", "“하지만 화나신 건 아니죠, 어머니?”"]], [["“I can hardly say that I am not.", "“화가 안 났다곤 차마 말 못 하겠다."], ["Angry? No.", "화가 났냐고?"], ["But when I consider the usual nature of the drag which causes men of promise to disappoint the world I feel uneasy.”", "아니다. 하지만 장래가 촉망되는 사람들이 세상을 실망시키게 만드는 그 끌어내리는 힘이 흔히 어떤 성질의 것인지 생각하면, 마음이 불안하구나.”"]], [["“You deserve credit for the feeling, Mother.", "“그렇게 느끼시는 건 칭찬받을 만한 일이에요, 어머니."], ["But I can assure you that you need not be disturbed by it on my account.”", "하지만 그 일로 저 때문에 불안해하실 필요는 없다고 분명히 말씀드릴 수 있어요.”"]], [["“When I think of you and your new crotchets,” said Mrs.", "“네 생각이며 네 새 변덕을 떠올리면 말이다”라고 요브라이트 부인이 다소 힘주어 말했다."], ["Yeobright, with some emphasis, “I naturally don’t feel so comfortable as I did a twelvemonth ago.", "“일 년 전만큼 마음이 편하지가 않은 게 당연하지."], ["It is incredible to me that a man accustomed to the attractive women of Paris and elsewhere should be so easily worked upon by a girl in a heath.", "파리며 여기저기서 매력적인 여인들을 익히 봐온 사내가 황야의 처녀 하나한테 그토록 쉽사리 휘둘린다는 게 내겐 도무지 믿기지 않는다."], ["You could just as well have walked another way.”", "다른 길로 가도 되었을 텐데.”"]], [["“I had been studying all day.”", "“온종일 공부하던 참이었어요.”"]], [["“Well, yes,”", "“음, 그래”라고 어머니가 한결 기대를 담아 덧붙였다."], ["she added more hopefully, “I have been thinking that you might get on as a schoolmaster, and rise that way, since you really are determined to hate the course you were pursuing.”", "“네가 정말로 걷던 길을 미워하기로 작정했다니, 교사로 잘 풀려 그렇게 출세할 수도 있겠다고 생각하던 참이다.”"]], [["Yeobright was unwilling to disturb this idea, though his scheme was far enough removed from one wherein the education of youth should be made a mere channel of social ascent.", "요브라이트는 이 생각을 흩뜨리고 싶지 않았으나, 그의 계획은 청소년 교육을 그저 사회적 출세의 통로로 삼는 것과는 한참 동떨어진 것이었다."], ["He had no desires of that sort.", "그에게는 그런 욕망이 없었다."], ["He had reached the stage in a young man’s life when the grimness of the general human situation first becomes clear; and the realization of this causes ambition to halt awhile.", "그는 인간 전반의 처지가 지닌 냉혹함이 처음으로 분명해지는, 젊은이 인생의 한 단계에 이르러 있었으니, 이를 깨달으면 야심이 잠시 멈춰 서는 법이다."], ["In France it is not uncustomary to commit suicide at this stage; in England we do much better, or much worse, as the case may be.", "프랑스에서는 이 단계에서 스스로 목숨을 끊는 일이 드물지 않거니와, 영국에서는 우리가 훨씬 더 잘 처신하든가, 경우에 따라서는 훨씬 더 못 처신한다."]], [["The love between the young man and his mother was strangely invisible now.", "이 젊은이와 그 어머니 사이의 사랑은 이제 기이하게도 눈에 보이지 않았다."], ["Of love it may be said, the less earthly the less demonstrative.", "사랑이란, 세속적일수록 덜 드러내는 법이라 할 만하다."], ["In its absolutely indestructible form it reaches a profundity in which all exhibition of itself is painful.", "절대적으로 파괴할 수 없는 형태에 이르면, 그것은 스스로를 드러내는 일체의 표현이 고통스러운 깊이에 다다른다."], ["It was so with these.", "이 두 사람이 그러했다."], ["Had conversations between them been overheard, people would have said, “How cold they are to each other!”", "그들 사이의 대화를 누가 엿들었더라면 이렇게 말했으리라. “저 둘은 서로 어쩌면 저리 냉랭할까!”"]], [["His theory and his wishes about devoting his future to teaching had made an impression on Mrs.", "미래를 교직에 바치겠다는 그의 이론과 바람은 요브라이트 부인에게 인상을 남겼다."], ["Yeobright. Indeed, how could it be otherwise when he was a part of her—when their discourses were as if carried on between the right and the left hands of the same body?", "하긴 어찌 안 그렇겠는가, 그가 그녀의 일부인 것을―두 사람의 담론이 마치 같은 몸의 오른손과 왼손 사이에서 오가는 듯한 것을 생각하면."], ["He had despaired of reaching her by argument; and it was almost as a discovery to him that he could reach her by a magnetism which was as superior to words as words are to yells.", "그는 논변으로 그녀에게 가닿기를 단념했었거니와, 말이 고함보다 우월한 만큼이나 말보다 우월한 어떤 자력磁力으로 그녀에게 가닿을 수 있음은 그에게 거의 하나의 발견과도 같았다."]], [["Strangely enough he began to feel now that it would not be so hard to persuade her who was his best friend that comparative poverty was essentially the higher course for him, as to reconcile to his feelings the act of persuading her.", "기이하게도 그는 이제, 자기에게 비교적 가난한 길이 본질적으로 더 높은 행로임을 가장 좋은 벗인 그녀에게 설득하는 일이, 그녀를 설득한다는 행위를 제 감정과 화해시키는 일만큼 어렵지는 않으리라 느끼기 시작했다."], ["From every provident point of view his mother was so undoubtedly right, that he was not without a sickness of heart in finding he could shake her.", "앞을 내다보는 온갖 관점에서 그의 어머니가 너무도 의심할 바 없이 옳았기에, 그는 자신이 그녀를 흔들 수 있음을 알고는 가슴이 아려오지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["She had a singular insight into life, considering that she had never mixed with it.", "세상과 한 번도 섞여본 적이 없는 것을 생각하면, 그녀에게는 삶에 대한 남다른 통찰이 있었다."], ["There are instances of persons who, without clear ideas of the things they criticize have yet had clear ideas of the relations of those things.", "자기가 논평하는 것들에 대한 분명한 관념은 없으되 그것들 사이의 관계에 대해서는 분명한 관념을 지닌 사람들의 예가 있다."], ["Blacklock, a poet blind from his birth, could describe visual objects with accuracy; Professor Sanderson, who was also blind, gave excellent lectures on colour, and taught others the theory of ideas which they had and he had not.", "날 때부터 눈먼 시인 블랙록은 시각적 대상을 정확히 묘사할 수 있었고, 역시 눈먼 샌더슨 교수는 색채에 관해 탁월한 강의를 펼치며, 자기에게는 없으되 남들에게는 있는 관념의 이론을 그들에게 가르쳤다."], ["In the social sphere these gifted ones are mostly women; they can watch a world which they never saw, and estimate forces of which they have only heard.", "사회적 영역에서 이러한 재능을 지닌 이들은 대개 여자다. 그들은 한 번도 본 적 없는 세상을 살필 수 있고, 그저 소문으로만 들은 힘들을 가늠할 수 있다."], ["We call it intuition.", "우리는 그것을 직관이라 부른다."]], [["What was the great world to Mrs. Yeobright?", "요브라이트 부인에게 저 큰 세상이란 무엇이었던가?"], ["A multitude whose tendencies could be perceived, though not its essences.", "그 본질은 알 수 없으되 그 경향만은 감지할 수 있는 군중이었다."], ["Communities were seen by her as from a distance; she saw them as we see the throngs which cover the canvases of Sallaert, Van Alsloot, and others of that school—vast masses of beings, jostling, zigzagging, and processioning in definite directions, but whose features are indistinguishable by the very comprehensiveness of the view.", "공동체들은 그녀에게 마치 멀리서 보이듯 했으니, 그녀는 그것들을, 살라르트며 반 알슬로트며 그 화파의 화가들의 화폭을 뒤덮은 무리―서로 떠밀고, 지그재그로 움직이고, 일정한 방향으로 행렬을 이루되, 바로 그 시야의 광대함 탓에 이목구비가 분간되지 않는 방대한 존재의 덩어리―를 보듯 보았다."]], [["One could see that, as far as it had gone, her life was very complete on its reflective side.", "여기까지 흘러온 한, 그녀의 삶이 그 성찰적 측면에서 매우 완성되어 있음을 알 수 있었다."], ["The philosophy of her nature, and its limitation by circumstances, was almost written in her movements.", "그녀 본성의 철학과 환경에 의한 그 한계가 그녀의 거동에 거의 쓰여 있다시피 했다."], ["They had a majestic foundation, though they were far from being majestic; and they had a ground-work of assurance, but they were not assured.", "그 거동에는 위엄 있는 토대가 있었으되 거동 자체는 위엄과는 거리가 멀었고, 확신의 기반이 깔려 있었으되 확신에 차 있지는 않았다."], ["As her once elastic walk had become deadened by time, so had her natural pride of life been hindered in its blooming by her necessities.", "한때 탄력 있던 그녀의 걸음걸이가 세월에 무뎌졌듯이, 그녀의 타고난 삶의 자긍심도 궁핍에 가로막혀 활짝 피지 못했던 것이다."]], [["The next slight touch in the shaping of Clym’s destiny occurred a few days after.", "클림의 운명을 빚어내는 다음번 미미한 손길은 며칠 뒤에 일어났다."], ["A barrow was opened on the heath, and Yeobright attended the operation, remaining away from his study during several hours.", "황야에서 고분古墳 하나가 발굴되었고, 요브라이트가 그 작업에 참석하느라 몇 시간 동안 서재를 비웠다."], ["In the afternoon Christian returned from a journey in the same direction, and Mrs. Yeobright questioned him.", "오후에 크리스천이 같은 방향으로 나갔다 돌아오자 요브라이트 부인이 그에게 캐물었다."]], [["“They have dug a hole, and they have found things like flowerpots upside down, Mis’ess Yeobright; and inside these be real charnel bones.", "“구덩이를 팠는데유, 거꾸로 엎어놓은 화분 같은 게 나왔어유, 요브라이트 부인. 그 안에 진짜 송장 뼈가 들었더라고유."], ["They have carried ’em off to men’s houses; but I shouldn’t like to sleep where they will bide.", "사람들 집으로 그걸 가져갔는데, 저라면 그게 놓인 데서는 자고 싶지 않을 거예유."], ["Dead folks have been known to come and claim their own. Mr.", "죽은 이들이 와서 제 것을 찾아간다는 말이 있거든유."], ["Yeobright had got one pot of the bones, and was going to bring ’em home—real skellington bones—but ’twas ordered otherwise.", "요브라이트 도련님도 뼈가 든 단지를 하나 얻어서 집에 가져오시려고 했는데―진짜 해골 뼈를유―허나 달리 정해졌나 봐유."], ["You’ll be relieved to hear that he gave away his pot and all, on second thoughts; and a blessed thing for ye, Mis’ess Yeobright, considering the wind o’ nights.”", "도련님이 다시 생각하셔서 단지째 다 줘버리셨다니 마음 놓으셔도 되겠어유, 요브라이트 부인. 밤바람을 생각하면야 부인한테 천만다행이지유.”"]], [["“Gave it away?”", "“줘버렸다고?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“네."], ["To Miss Vye.", "바이 아가씨한테유."], ["She has a cannibal taste for such churchyard furniture seemingly.”", "그 아가씬 그런 묘지 가구를 식인종처럼 좋아하는 취향인가 봐유.”"]], [["“Miss Vye was there too?”", "“바이 아가씨도 거기 있었어?”"]], [["“Ay, ’a b’lieve she was.”", "“네, 있었던 것 같아유.”"]], [["When Clym came home, which was shortly after, his mother said, in a curious tone, “The urn you had meant for me you gave away.”", "클림이 집에 돌아오자―곧 돌아왔는데―어머니가 묘한 어조로 말했다. “나한테 주려던 단지를 줘버렸더구나.”"]], [["Yeobright made no reply; the current of her feeling was too pronounced to admit it.", "요브라이트는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으니, 그녀 감정의 물살이 너무도 또렷하여 대답을 허락지 않았다."]], [["The early weeks of the year passed on.", "새해의 초기 몇 주가 지나갔다."], ["Yeobright certainly studied at home, but he also walked much abroad, and the direction of his walk was always towards some point of a line between Mistover and Rainbarrow.", "요브라이트는 분명 집에서 공부했으나, 또한 밖으로 많이 거닐었으니, 그 걸음의 방향은 늘 미스트오버와 레인배로를 잇는 선의 어느 지점을 향했다."]], [["The month of March arrived, and the heath showed its first signs of awakening from winter trance.", "삼월이 찾아왔고, 황야는 겨울잠에서 깨어나는 첫 기미를 보였다."], ["The awakening was almost feline in its stealthiness.", "그 깨어남은 거의 고양이처럼 은밀했다."], ["The pool outside the bank by Eustacia’s dwelling, which seemed as dead and desolate as ever to an observer who moved and made noises in his observation, would gradually disclose a state of great animation when silently watched awhile.", "유스테이시아의 거처 옆 둑 바깥의 못은, 움직이고 소리 내며 관찰하는 이의 눈에는 여전히 죽은 듯 황량해 보였으나, 잠자코 한동안 지켜보면 차츰 크나큰 생기로 가득 찬 상태를 드러내곤 했다."], ["A timid animal world had come to life for the season.", "소심한 동물의 세계가 그 철을 맞아 되살아난 것이었다."], ["Little tadpoles and efts began to bubble up through the water, and to race along beneath it; toads made noises like very young ducks, and advanced to the margin in twos and threes; overhead, bumblebees flew hither and thither in the thickening light, their drone coming and going like the sound of a gong.", "작은 올챙이와 영원이 물을 뚫고 거품처럼 솟아올라 그 아래를 내달리기 시작했고, 두꺼비들은 아주 어린 오리 같은 소리를 내며 둘씩 셋씩 물가로 다가왔으며, 머리 위로는 호박벌들이 짙어가는 빛 속을 이리저리 날았으니, 그 윙윙거림이 마치 징 소리처럼 다가왔다 멀어졌다 했다."]], [["On an evening such as this Yeobright descended into the Blooms-End valley from beside that very pool, where he had been standing with another person quite silently and quite long enough to hear all this puny stir of resurrection in nature; yet he had not heard it.", "바로 이런 어느 저녁에 요브라이트는 다름 아닌 그 못가에서 블룸스엔드 골짜기로 내려왔으니, 그곳에서 그는 다른 한 사람과 더불어 자연의 이 가냘픈 부활의 술렁임을 죄다 들을 수 있을 만큼 충분히 오래, 또 아무 말 없이 서 있었건만, 그것을 듣지 못했다."], ["His walk was rapid as he came down, and he went with a springy trend.", "내려오는 그의 걸음은 빨랐고, 발걸음에 탄력이 실려 있었다."], ["Before entering upon his mother’s premises he stopped and breathed.", "어머니의 집에 들어서기 전에 그는 멈춰 서서 숨을 골랐다."], ["The light which shone forth on him from the window revealed that his face was flushed and his eye bright.", "창에서 그에게 비쳐 나온 빛은 그의 얼굴이 상기되어 있고 눈이 빛나고 있음을 드러냈다."], ["What it did not show was something which lingered upon his lips like a seal set there.", "그 빛이 드러내지 못한 것은, 그의 입술에 봉인처럼 찍혀 머무는 무엇이었다."], ["The abiding presence of this impress was so real that he hardly dared to enter the house, for it seemed as if his mother might say, “What red spot is that glowing upon your mouth so vividly?”", "이 흔적이 머무는 기색이 어찌나 생생했던지 그는 집에 들어서기조차 거의 두려웠으니, 마치 어머니가 “네 입에 그토록 선명하게 타오르는 그 붉은 점은 무엇이냐?”라고 물을 것만 같았기 때문이다."]], [["But he entered soon after.", "그러나 그는 곧 들어섰다."], ["The tea was ready, and he sat down opposite his mother.", "차가 준비되어 있었고, 그는 어머니 맞은편에 앉았다."], ["She did not speak many words; and as for him, something had been just done and some words had been just said on the hill which prevented him from beginning a desultory chat.", "어머니는 많은 말을 하지 않았고, 그로 말하자면, 언덕에서 방금 어떤 일이 행해지고 어떤 말이 막 오갔던 터라 이런저런 한담을 꺼낼 수가 없었다."], ["His mother’s taciturnity was not without ominousness, but he appeared not to care.", "어머니의 과묵함에는 불길함이 없지 않았으나 그는 개의치 않는 듯했다."], ["He knew why she said so little, but he could not remove the cause of her bearing towards him.", "그는 어머니가 어째서 그토록 말이 없는지 알았으나, 자신을 향한 어머니의 태도의 원인을 없앨 수는 없었다."], ["These half-silent sittings were far from uncommon with them now.", "이제 이런 반쯤 침묵 속의 마주 앉음은 두 사람에게 결코 드문 일이 아니었다."], ["At last Yeobright made a beginning of what was intended to strike at the whole root of the matter.", "마침내 요브라이트는 문제의 뿌리 전체를 치고 들려는 시도의 첫발을 떼었다."]], [["“Five days have we sat like this at meals with scarcely a word.", "“우리 닷새째 이렇게 식사 자리에 앉아 거의 말 한마디 안 하고 있어요."], ["What’s the use of it, Mother?”", "이게 무슨 소용이에요, 어머니?”"]], [["“None,” said she, in a heart-swollen tone.", "“소용없지”라고 어머니가 가슴이 미어지는 어조로 말했다."], ["“But there is only too good a reason.”", "“하지만 너무도 그럴 만한 이유가 있단다.”"]], [["“Not when you know all.", "“다 아시면 안 그러실 거예요."], ["I have been wanting to speak about this, and I am glad the subject is begun.", "이 얘기를 꺼내고 싶던 참이라, 화제가 시작돼서 다행이에요."], ["The reason, of course, is Eustacia Vye.", "이유는 물론 유스테이시아 바이지요."], ["Well, I confess I have seen her lately, and have seen her a good many times.”", "음, 솔직히 저는 요즘 그녀를 만났고, 꽤 여러 번 만났어요.”"]], [["“Yes, yes; and I know what that amounts to.", "“그래, 그래. 그게 무슨 뜻인지 나도 안다."], ["It troubles me, Clym.", "그게 나를 괴롭히는구나, 클림."], ["You are wasting your life here; and it is solely on account of her.", "너는 여기서 네 인생을 허비하고 있어. 그것도 오로지 그 여자 때문에."], ["If it had not been for that woman you would never have entertained this teaching scheme at all.”", "그 여자만 아니었다면 너는 이 교사 계획 따위는 애초에 품지도 않았을 거다.”"]], [["Clym looked hard at his mother.", "클림은 어머니를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["“You know that is not it,” he said.", "“그게 아니란 거 아시잖아요”라고 그가 말했다."]], [["“Well, I know you had decided to attempt it before you saw her; but that would have ended in intentions.", "“음, 네가 그 애를 보기 전에 그걸 해보겠다고 마음먹은 건 안다. 하지만 그건 작정으로만 끝났을 거야."], ["It was very well to talk of, but ridiculous to put in practice.", "말하기야 그럴듯했지만, 실행에 옮기기엔 우스꽝스러운 일이지."], ["I fully expected that in the course of a month or two you would have seen the folly of such self-sacrifice, and would have been by this time back again to Paris in some business or other.", "나는 한두 달 안에 네가 그런 자기희생의 어리석음을 깨닫고, 지금쯤이면 무슨 일자리든 얻어 파리로 돌아가 있으리라고 철석같이 기대했어."], ["I can understand objections to the diamond trade—I really was thinking that it might be inadequate to the life of a man like you even though it might have made you a millionaire.", "다이아몬드 장사에 대한 거부감이야 나도 이해한다―솔직히 그게 너 같은 사내의 인생에는 미흡할 수도 있겠다고, 설령 그게 너를 백만장자로 만들어줄지언정, 생각하던 참이었지."], ["But now I see how mistaken you are about this girl I doubt if you could be correct about other things.”", "하지만 이제 네가 그 처녀에 대해 얼마나 잘못 알고 있는지 알고 나니, 네가 다른 일들에 대해선들 제대로 알고 있을지 의심스럽구나.”"]], [["“How am I mistaken in her?”", "“제가 그녀를 어떻게 잘못 알고 있다는 거죠?”"]], [["“She is lazy and dissatisfied.", "“그 애는 게으르고 불만투성이야."], ["But that is not all of it.", "하지만 그게 다가 아니다."], ["Supposing her to be as good a woman as any you can find, which she certainly is not, why do you wish to connect yourself with anybody at present?”", "그 애가 네가 찾을 수 있는 어느 여자 못지않게 좋은 여자라고 친다 한들―분명코 그렇지 않다만―어째서 지금 네가 누군가와 인연을 맺으려 한단 말이냐?”"]], [["“Well, there are practical reasons,” Clym began, and then almost broke off under an overpowering sense of the weight of argument which could be brought against his statement.", "“음, 현실적인 이유들이 있어요”라고 클림이 입을 떼었으나, 자기 주장에 맞설 수 있는 논변의 무게를 압도적으로 실감하고는 거의 말끝을 흐렸다."], ["“If I take a school an educated woman would be invaluable as a help to me.”", "“제가 학교를 연다면 교육받은 여자가 제게 더없이 값진 조력자가 될 거예요.”"]], [["“What!", "“뭐라고!"], ["you really mean to marry her?”", "정말로 그 애와 결혼하겠다는 거냐?”"]], [["“It would be premature to state that plainly.", "“그걸 분명히 말하기엔 너무 이르죠."], ["But consider what obvious advantages there would be in doing it.", "하지만 그렇게 했을 때의 명백한 이점들을 생각해보세요."], ["She——”", "그녀는――”"]], [["“Don’t suppose she has any money.", "“그 애한테 돈이 있을 거란 생각은 하지도 마라."], ["She hasn’t a farthing.”", "그 앤 한 푼도 없어.”"]], [["“She is excellently educated, and would make a good matron in a boarding-school.", "“그녀는 더없이 잘 교육받았고, 기숙학교에서 훌륭한 사감이 될 거예요."], ["I candidly own that I have modified my views a little, in deference to you; and it should satisfy you.", "솔직히 인정하건대, 어머니를 존중해 제 생각을 조금 바꿨어요. 그러니 그걸로 어머니도 만족하셔야 해요."], ["I no longer adhere to my intention of giving with my own mouth rudimentary education to the lowest class.", "저는 더 이상 제 입으로 직접 최하층 계급에 기초 교육을 베풀겠다는 뜻을 고집하지 않아요."], ["I can do better.", "더 나은 걸 할 수 있어요."], ["I can establish a good private school for farmers’ sons, and without stopping the school I can manage to pass examinations.", "농부의 아들들을 위한 좋은 사립학교를 세우고, 학교를 멈추지 않고도 시험을 칠 수 있게 해나갈 수 있어요."], ["By this means, and by the assistance of a wife like her——”", "이런 방법으로, 그리고 그녀 같은 아내의 도움으로――”"]], [["“Oh, Clym!”", "“오, 클림!”"]], [["“I shall ultimately, I hope, be at the head of one of the best schools in the county.”", "“저는 결국, 바라건대, 이 주州에서 가장 좋은 학교 중 하나의 교장이 될 거예요.”"]], [["Yeobright had enunciated the word “her” with a fervour which, in conversation with a mother, was absurdly indiscreet.", "요브라이트는 “그녀”라는 말을, 어머니와의 대화에서는 우스꽝스러우리만치 분별없을 만큼의 열정을 담아 발음했다."], ["Hardly a maternal heart within the four seas could in such circumstances, have helped being irritated at that ill-timed betrayal of feeling for a new woman.", "이런 상황에서, 새로운 여자를 향한 그 때 이른 감정의 누설에 짜증나지 않을 어머니의 마음이란 사해四海 안에 거의 없으리라."]], [["“You are blinded, Clym,” she said warmly.", "“너는 눈이 멀었다, 클림”이라고 그녀가 발끈하여 말했다."], ["“It was a bad day for you when you first set eyes on her.", "“그 애를 처음 눈에 담은 날이 네게 흉한 날이었어."], ["And your scheme is merely a castle in the air built on purpose to justify this folly which has seized you, and to salve your conscience on the irrational situation you are in.”", "그리고 네 그 계획이란 건, 너를 사로잡은 이 어리석음을 정당화하고, 네가 처한 이 비이성적인 상황에 대한 양심을 달래려고 일부러 지어 올린 한낱 공중누각일 뿐이야.”"]], [["“Mother, that’s not true,” he firmly answered.", "“어머니, 그건 사실이 아니에요”라고 그가 단호히 대답했다."]], [["“Can you maintain that I sit and tell untruths, when all I wish to do is to save you from sorrow?", "“그저 너를 슬픔에서 구하고 싶을 뿐인 내가, 앉아서 거짓말이나 하고 있다고 우길 셈이냐?"], ["For shame, Clym!", "부끄러운 줄 알아라, 클림!"], ["But it is all through that woman—a hussy!”", "하지만 이게 다 그 여자 때문이야―화냥년 같으니!”"]], [["Clym reddened like fire and rose.", "클림은 불처럼 얼굴이 붉어지며 일어섰다."], ["He placed his hand upon his mother’s shoulder and said, in a tone which hung strangely between entreaty and command, “I won’t hear it.", "그는 어머니의 어깨에 손을 얹고, 애원과 명령 사이에 기이하게 걸친 어조로 말했다. “그 말은 듣지 않겠어요."], ["I may be led to answer you in a way which we shall both regret.”", "우리 둘 다 후회할 식으로 어머니께 대꾸하게 될지도 몰라요.”"]], [["His mother parted her lips to begin some other vehement truth, but on looking at him she saw that in his face which led her to leave the words unsaid.", "어머니는 또 다른 격렬한 진실을 입에 올리려 입술을 떼었으나, 그를 바라보니 그 얼굴에서, 그 말을 입 밖에 내지 않게 하는 무언가를 보았다."], ["Yeobright walked once or twice across the room, and then suddenly went out of the house.", "요브라이트는 방을 한두 번 가로질러 거닐다가, 별안간 집 밖으로 나가버렸다."], ["It was eleven o’clock when he came in, though he had not been further than the precincts of the garden.", "그가 들어온 것은 열한 시였으나, 정원 경내보다 더 멀리는 가지 않았던 터였다."], ["His mother was gone to bed.", "어머니는 잠자리에 든 뒤였다."], ["A light was left burning on the table, and supper was spread.", "탁자 위에는 등불 하나가 켜진 채 남겨져 있었고,\n저녁상이 차려져 있었다."], ["Without stopping for any food he secured the doors and went upstairs.", "그는 음식에는 입도 대지 않고 문단속을 한 뒤 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["IV.", "IV."], ["An Hour of Bliss and Many Hours of Sadness", "한 시간의 환희와 여러 시간의 슬픔"]], [["The next day was gloomy enough at Blooms-End.", "이튿날 블룸스엔드는 충분히 음울했다."], ["Yeobright remained in his study, sitting over the open books; but the work of those hours was miserably scant.", "예오브라이트는 서재에 머무르며 펼쳐진 책들 앞에 앉아 있었으나, 그 시간에 이룬 일이라곤 한심할 만큼 보잘것없었다."], ["Determined that there should be nothing in his conduct towards his mother resembling sullenness, he had occasionally spoken to her on passing matters, and would take no notice of the brevity of her replies.", "어머니를 대하는 자신의 태도에 시무룩함 비슷한 것이 조금도 배어들지 않도록 하리라 마음먹은 그는 이따금 사소한 일들에 관해 어머니에게 말을 건넸고, 어머니의 대답이 짧은 것에 대해서는 모른 척했다."], ["With the same resolve to keep up a show of conversation he said, about seven o’clock in the evening, “There’s an eclipse of the moon tonight.", "대화하는 시늉을 유지하려는 똑같은 결심으로 그는 저녁 일곱 시쯤에 \"오늘 밤 월식이 있어요."], ["I am going out to see it.” And, putting on his overcoat, he left her.", "보러 나갔다 오겠어요\"라고 말하고는 외투를 걸친 뒤 어머니 곁을 떠났다."]], [["The low moon was not as yet visible from the front of the house, and Yeobright climbed out of the valley until he stood in the full flood of her light.", "나지막이 뜬 달은 아직 집 정면에서는 보이지 않았으므로, 예오브라이트는 골짜기를 기어올라 달빛이 가득 쏟아지는 곳에 이를 때까지 걸었다."], ["But even now he walked on, and his steps were in the direction of Rainbarrow.", "그러나 그는 거기서도 멈추지 않고 계속 걸었으니, 발걸음은 레인배로 쪽을 향하고 있었다."]], [["In half an hour he stood at the top.", "반 시간 만에 그는 봉분 꼭대기에 섰다."], ["The sky was clear from verge to verge, and the moon flung her rays over the whole heath, but without sensibly lighting it, except where paths and water-courses had laid bare the white flints and glistening quartz sand, which made streaks upon the general shade.", "하늘은 끝에서 끝까지 맑았고, 달은 광야 전체에 빛을 흩뿌렸으나, 길과 물길이 흰 부싯돌과 반짝이는 석영 모래를 드러내어 전체적인 그늘 위에 줄무늬를 그어놓은 곳을 제외하고는 눈에 띄게 밝혀주지는 않았다."], ["After standing awhile he stooped and felt the heather.", "한동안 서 있던 그는 몸을 숙여 히스를 만져보았다."], ["It was dry, and he flung himself down upon the barrow, his face towards the moon, which depicted a small image of herself in each of his eyes.", "마른 것을 확인한 그는 봉분 위에 몸을 던지듯 누웠고, 얼굴을 달 쪽으로 향했으니, 달은 그의 두 눈 속에 각각 자그마한 제 모습을 그려 넣었다."]], [["He had often come up here without stating his purpose to his mother; but this was the first time that he had been ostensibly frank as to his purpose while really concealing it.", "그는 어머니에게 목적을 밝히지 않은 채 여기 올라온 적이 많았으나, 실제로는 목적을 감추면서도 겉으로는 솔직한 척한 것은 이번이 처음이었다."], ["It was a moral situation which, three months earlier, he could hardly have credited of himself.", "그것은 석 달 전이었다면 자기 자신에게서 좀처럼 믿기 어려웠을 도덕적 상황이었다."], ["In returning to labour in this sequestered spot he had anticipated an escape from the chafing of social necessities; yet behold they were here also.", "이 외딴곳으로 돌아와 일하면서 그는 사회적 필요가 가하는 마찰에서 벗어나리라 기대했었는데, 보라, 그것들은 여기에도 있었다."], ["More than ever he longed to be in some world where personal ambition was not the only recognized form of progress—such, perhaps, as might have been the case at some time or other in the silvery globe then shining upon him.", "그는 그 어느 때보다도, 개인적 야망이 진보의 유일하게 인정받는 형태가 아닌 어떤 세계에 있고 싶다는 갈망에 사로잡혔으니—이를테면 그때 그를 비추던 저 은빛 천체에서 언젠가 한 번쯤은 그러했을지도 모를 그런 세계였다."], ["His eye travelled over the length and breadth of that distant country—over the Bay of Rainbows, the sombre Sea of Crises, the Ocean of Storms, the Lake of Dreams, the vast Walled Plains, and the wondrous Ring Mountains—till he almost felt himself to be voyaging bodily through its wild scenes, standing on its hollow hills, traversing its deserts, descending its vales and old sea bottoms, or mounting to the edges of its craters.", "그의 시선은 그 머나먼 나라의 길이와 너비를 두루 훑었으니—무지개 만灣을, 음울한 위기의 바다를, 폭풍의 대양을, 꿈의 호수를, 광대한 벽으로 둘러싸인 평원들을, 그리고 경이로운 고리 모양 산맥을 거치며—마침내 그는 거의 자신이 몸소 그 거친 풍경 속을 항해하며, 그 텅 빈 언덕 위에 서고, 그 사막을 가로지르고, 그 골짜기와 옛 바다 밑바닥으로 내려가고, 혹은 그 분화구의 가장자리로 기어오르는 듯한 느낌에 사로잡혔다."]], [["While he watched the far-removed landscape a tawny stain grew into being on the lower verge—the eclipse had begun.", "그가 그토록 멀리 떨어진 풍경을 바라보고 있을 때 아래쪽 가장자리에 황갈색 얼룩이 생겨났으니—월식이 시작된 것이었다."], ["This marked a preconcerted moment—for the remote celestial phenomenon had been pressed into sublunary service as a lover’s signal.", "이것이 미리 약속해둔 순간을 표시했으니—저 머나먼 천체의 현상이 연인의 신호로서 이 달 아래 세계의 일에 동원된 것이었다."], ["Yeobright’s mind flew back to earth at the sight; he arose, shook himself and listened.", "그 광경을 보자 예오브라이트의 마음은 다시 땅으로 날아 돌아왔고, 그는 일어나 몸을 추스르며 귀를 기울였다."], ["Minute after minute passed by, perhaps ten minutes passed, and the shadow on the moon perceptibly widened.", "분초가 흘러갔으니, 아마 십 분쯤 지났을 테고, 달 위의 그림자는 눈에 띄게 넓어졌다."], ["He heard a rustling on his left hand, a cloaked figure with an upturned face appeared at the base of the Barrow, and Clym descended.", "그는 왼편에서 부스럭거리는 소리를 들었고, 망토를 두르고 얼굴을 위로 향한 형상이 봉분 아래에 나타나더니, 클림은 내려갔다."], ["In a moment the figure was in his arms, and his lips upon hers.", "한순간에 그 형상은 그의 품에 안겼고, 그의 입술은 그녀의 입술에 닿았다."]], [["“My Eustacia!”", "\"내 유스테이시아!\""]], [["“Clym, dearest!”", "\"클림, 사랑하는 이여!\""]], [["Such a situation had less than three months brought forth.", "이런 상황을 석 달도 채 안 되는 시간이 만들어낸 것이었다."]], [["They remained long without a single utterance, for no language could reach the level of their condition—words were as the rusty implements of a by-gone barbarous epoch, and only to be occasionally tolerated.", "그들은 오래도록 한마디도 하지 않고 머물러 있었으니, 어떤 언어도 그들의 상태에 미칠 수 없었기 때문이요—말이란 지나간 야만의 시대에 쓰이던 녹슨 연장과 같아서, 그저 이따금 용인될 따름이었다."]], [["“I began to wonder why you did not come,”", "\"왜 안 오나 궁금해지기 시작했어요.\""], ["said Yeobright, when she had withdrawn a little from his embrace.", "그녀가 그의 포옹에서 조금 물러났을 때 예오브라이트가 말했다."]], [["“You said ten minutes after the first mark of shade on the edge of the moon, and that’s what it is now.”", "\"달 가장자리에 그림자가 처음 드리운 후 십 분이라고 하셨고, 지금이 바로 그때예요.\""]], [["“Well, let us only think that here we are.”", "\"음, 우리가 여기 함께 있다는 것만 생각합시다.\""]], [["Then, holding each other’s hand, they were again silent, and the shadow on the moon’s disc grew a little larger.", "그러고는 서로의 손을 잡은 채 그들은 다시 침묵했고, 달 원반 위의 그림자는 조금 더 커졌다."]], [["“Has it seemed long since you last saw me?”", "\"마지막으로 절 본 뒤로 길게 느껴졌나요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“It has seemed sad.”", "\"슬프게 느껴졌소.\""]], [["“And not long?", "\"길지는 않고요?"], ["That’s because you occupy yourself, and so blind yourself to my absence.", "그건 당신이 일에 몰두해서, 그래서 제 부재에 눈을 감기 때문이에요."], ["To me, who can do nothing, it has been like living under stagnant water.”", "아무것도 할 수 없는 저에게는, 그게 고인 물 밑에서 사는 것 같았어요.\""]], [["“I would rather bear tediousness, dear, than have time made short by such means as have shortened mine.”", "\"여보, 나는 내 시간을 짧게 만든 그런 수단으로 시간이 짧아지는 것보다는 차라리 지루함을 견디겠소.\""]], [["“In what way is that?", "\"그게 어떤 식으로요?"], ["You have been thinking you wished you did not love me.”", "당신은 나를 사랑하지 않았으면 하고 바랐던 거로군요.\""]], [["“How can a man wish that, and yet love on? No, Eustacia.”", "\"어찌 사내가 그러기를 바라면서 동시에 사랑할 수 있겠소?"], ["“Men can, women cannot.”", "아니오, 유스테이시아.\""]], [["“Well, whatever I may have thought, one thing is certain—I do love you—past all compass and description.", "\"사내는 그럴 수 있지만, 여인은 못 해요.\" \"음, 내가 무슨 생각을 했든, 한 가지만은 분명하오—나는 당신을 사랑하오—그 어떤 척도나 묘사로도 다 담을 수 없을 만큼."], ["I love you to oppressiveness—I, who have never before felt more than a pleasant passing fancy for any woman I have ever seen.", "나는 당신을 짓눌릴 만큼 사랑하오—그동안 어떤 여인을 보아도 그저 스쳐 지나가는 즐거운 변덕 이상은 느껴본 적 없던 내가 말이오."], ["Let me look right into your moonlit face and dwell on every line and curve in it!", "달빛에 비친 당신 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 그 모든 선과 곡선 하나하나에 머물게 해주오!"], ["Only a few hairbreadths make the difference between this face and faces I have seen many times before I knew you; yet what a difference—the difference between everything and nothing at all.", "이 얼굴과, 당신을 알기 전에 여러 번 보았던 얼굴들 사이의 차이라곤 머리카락 몇 올 굵기에 지나지 않는데, 그런데도 얼마나 큰 차이인지—전부와 아무것도 아닌 것 사이의 차이라오."], ["One touch on that mouth again!", "저 입에 다시 한번 입맞춤을!"], ["there, and there, and there.", "자, 여기, 여기, 그리고 여기."], ["Your eyes seem heavy, Eustacia.”", "당신 눈이 무거워 보이는구려, 유스테이시아.\""]], [["“No, it is my general way of looking.", "\"아니에요, 제가 늘 보는 모습이 그래요."], ["I think it arises from my feeling sometimes an agonizing pity for myself that I ever was born.”", "제가 태어났다는 것에 대해 이따금 저 자신을 향한 괴로운 연민을 느끼는 데서 비롯되는 것 같아요.\""]], [["“You don’t feel it now?”", "\"지금은 그걸 느끼지 않소?\""]], [["“No.", "\"네."], ["Yet I know that we shall not love like this always.", "그래도 우리가 늘 이렇게 사랑하지는 못하리라는 걸 알아요."], ["Nothing can ensure the continuance of love.", "그 무엇도 사랑의 지속을 보장할 수 없어요."], ["It will evaporate like a spirit, and so I feel full of fears.”", "사랑은 정령처럼 증발해버릴 테고, 그래서 저는 두려움으로 가득 차 있어요.\""]], [["“You need not.”", "\"그럴 필요 없소.\""]], [["“Ah, you don’t know.", "\"아, 당신은 몰라요."], ["You have seen more than I, and have been into cities and among people that I have only heard of, and have lived more years than I; but yet I am older at this than you.", "당신은 저보다 더 많은 걸 보았고, 제가 이야기로만 들어본 도시들과 사람들 사이에 있어봤고, 저보다 더 많은 세월을 살았지만, 그래도 이 점에서는 제가 당신보다 더 늙었어요."], ["I loved another man once, and now I love you.”", "저는 한때 다른 남자를 사랑했고, 이제는 당신을 사랑해요.\""]], [["“In God’s mercy don’t talk so, Eustacia!”", "\"하느님의 자비를 봐서라도 그런 말 마오, 유스테이시아!\""]], [["“But I do not think I shall be the one who wearies first.", "\"하지만 먼저 싫증 내는 쪽이 저는 아닐 거예요."], ["It will, I fear, end in this way: your mother will find out that you meet me, and she will influence you against me!”", "두렵건대 이런 식으로 끝날 거예요. 당신 어머니가 당신이 저를 만난다는 걸 알아내실 테고, 저에게 등 돌리도록 당신을 움직이실 거예요!\""]], [["“That can never be.", "\"그런 일은 결코 없을 거요."], ["She knows of these meetings already.”", "어머니는 이 만남에 대해 이미 알고 계시오.\""]], [["“And she speaks against me?”", "\"그러면서 저를 헐뜯으시고요?\""]], [["“I will not say.”", "\"말하지 않겠소.\""]], [["“There, go away!", "\"자, 가요!"], ["Obey her. I shall ruin you.", "어머니께 순종하세요."], ["It is foolish of you to meet me like this.", "저는 당신을 망칠 거예요. 이렇게 저를 만나는 건 어리석은 일이에요."], ["Kiss me, and go away forever.", "입맞춰주고, 영원히 떠나세요."], ["Forever—do you hear?—forever!”", "영원히—들리세요?—영원히요!\""]], [["“Not I.”", "\"난 안 떠나오.\""], ["“It is your only chance.", "\"그게 당신의 유일한 기회예요."], ["Many a man’s love has been a curse to him.”", "많은 사내의 사랑이 그에게 저주가 되었어요.\""]], [["“You are desperate, full of fancies, and wilful; and you misunderstand.", "\"당신은 절망에 빠져 망상으로 가득하고 고집스럽소. 그리고 오해하고 있소."], ["I have an additional reason for seeing you tonight besides love of you.", "오늘 밤 당신을 만나는 데에는 당신을 향한 사랑 말고도 또 다른 이유가 있소."], ["For though, unlike you, I feel our affection may be eternal. I feel with you in this, that our present mode of existence cannot last.”", "비록 나는 당신과 달리 우리의 애정이 영원할 수 있다고 느끼지만, 우리의 현재 존재 방식이 지속될 수 없다는 점에서는 당신과 같은 마음이오.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["’tis your mother.", "당신 어머니로군요."], ["Yes, that’s it!", "그래요, 바로 그거예요!"], ["I knew it.”", "알았어요.\""]], [["“Never mind what it is.", "\"그게 무엇이든 신경 쓰지 마오."], ["Believe this, I cannot let myself lose you.", "이것만은 믿어주오, 나는 당신을 잃도록 나 자신을 내버려둘 수 없소."], ["I must have you always with me.", "나는 늘 당신을 곁에 두어야만 하오."], ["This very evening I do not like to let you go.", "바로 오늘 저녁만 해도 당신을 보내고 싶지 않소."], ["There is only one cure for this anxiety, dearest—you must be my wife.”", "이 불안에는 단 하나의 치유책이 있을 뿐이오, 가장 사랑하는 이여—당신이 내 아내가 되어야만 하오.\""]], [["She started—then endeavoured to say calmly, “Cynics say that cures the anxiety by curing the love.”", "그녀는 흠칫하더니—이내 차분하게 말하려 애쓰며, \"냉소가들은 그게 사랑을 치유함으로써 불안을 치유하는 거라고 말하던데요\" 하고 말했다."]], [["“But you must answer me.", "\"하지만 당신은 내게 답해야 하오."], ["Shall I claim you some day—I don’t mean at once?”", "언젠가 내가 당신을 내 사람이라 주장해도 되겠소—당장이라는 뜻은 아니오만?\""]], [["“I must think,”", "\"생각해봐야 해요.\""], ["Eustacia murmured.", "유스테이시아가 중얼거렸다."], ["“At present speak of Paris to me.", "\"지금은 제게 파리 이야기를 해주세요."], ["Is there any place like it on earth?”", "이 땅에 그런 곳이 또 있을까요?\""]], [["“It is very beautiful.", "\"무척 아름답소."], ["But will you be mine?”", "하지만 당신은 내 사람이 되겠소?\""]], [["“I will be nobody else’s in the world—does that satisfy you?”", "\"이 세상 누구의 것도 되지 않을 거예요—그러면 만족하시겠어요?\""]], [["“Yes, for the present.”", "\"그렇소, 지금으로서는.\""]], [["“Now tell me of the Tuileries, and the Louvre,”", "\"이제 튈르리와 루브르 이야기를 해주세요.\""], ["she continued evasively.", "그녀가 둘러대듯 말을 이었다."]], [["“I hate talking of Paris!", "\"파리 얘기는 하기 싫소!"], ["Well, I remember one sunny room in the Louvre which would make a fitting place for you to live in—the Galerie d’Apollon.", "음, 루브르에서 본 어느 햇살 가득한 방이 기억나는데, 당신이 살기에 알맞은 곳일 거요—아폴론 회랑이라오."], ["Its windows are mainly east; and in the early morning, when the sun is bright, the whole apartment is in a perfect blaze of splendour.", "그 창들은 대체로 동쪽을 향해 있어서, 이른 아침 해가 밝을 때면 방 전체가 완벽한 화려함의 불길에 휩싸인다오."], ["The rays bristle and dart from the encrustations of gilding to the magnificent inlaid coffers, from the coffers to the gold and silver plate, from the plate to the jewels and precious stones, from these to the enamels, till there is a perfect network of light which quite dazzles the eye.", "빛줄기가 도금된 장식 더께에서 솟구쳐 화려하게 상감된 함으로 쏘아가고, 함에서 금은 식기로, 식기에서 보석과 귀한 돌로, 거기서 다시 칠보로 옮아가, 마침내 눈을 완전히 부시게 하는 완벽한 빛의 그물이 짜인다오."], ["But now, about our marriage——”", "그건 그렇고, 이제 우리 혼인 얘기인데—\""]], [["“And Versailles—the King’s Gallery is some such gorgeous room, is it not?”", "\"그리고 베르사유는—국왕의 회랑도 그런 호화로운 방이지요, 그렇지 않아요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["But what’s the use of talking of gorgeous rooms?", "하지만 호화로운 방 얘기를 해서 무슨 소용이오?"], ["By the way, the Little Trianon would suit us beautifully to live in, and you might walk in the gardens in the moonlight and think you were in some English shrubbery; it is laid out in English fashion.”", "그건 그렇고, 프티 트리아농이 우리가 살기에 더없이 좋을 거요, 거기서 당신은 달빛 속 정원을 거닐며 마치 어느 영국식 관목 숲에 있는 듯 여길 수 있을 거요. 영국식으로 꾸며져 있으니 말이오.\""]], [["“I should hate to think that!”", "\"그렇게 여기는 건 정말 싫어요!\""]], [["“Then you could keep to the lawn in front of the Grand Palace.", "\"그러면 대궁전 앞 잔디밭에 머물 수도 있소."], ["All about there you would doubtless feel in a world of historical romance.”", "그 일대라면 분명 역사적 로맨스의 세계에 있는 듯한 기분이 들 거요.\""], ["He went on, since it was all new to her, and described Fontainebleau, St. Cloud, the Bois, and many other familiar haunts of the Parisians; till she said—", "그녀에게는 모든 것이 새로웠으므로 그는 계속 이야기하며 퐁텐블로, 생클루, 부아, 그 밖에 파리 사람들이 즐겨 찾는 여러 명소를 묘사했고, 마침내 그녀가 말했다—"]], [["“When used you to go to these places?”", "\"언제 그런 곳들에 가곤 했어요?\""]], [["“On Sundays.”", "\"일요일에.\""]], [["“Ah, yes.", "\"아, 그렇군요."], ["I dislike English Sundays.", "저는 영국식 일요일이 싫어요."], ["How I should chime in with their manners over there!", "거기서라면 그들의 풍습에 얼마나 잘 어울릴까요!"], ["Dear Clym, you’ll go back again?”", "사랑하는 클림, 다시 돌아갈 거죠?\""]], [["Clym shook his head, and looked at the eclipse.", "클림은 고개를 가로젓고는 월식을 바라보았다."]], [["“If you’ll go back again I’ll—be something,”", "\"당신이 돌아간다면 저는—뭔가가 되겠어요.\""], ["she said tenderly, putting her head near his breast.", "그녀가 그의 가슴께에 머리를 기대며 다정하게 말했다."], ["“If you’ll agree I’ll give my promise, without making you wait a minute longer.”", "\"당신이 동의한다면, 단 일 분도 더 기다리게 하지 않고 약속할게요.\""]], [["“How extraordinary that you and my mother should be of one mind about this!”", "\"당신과 우리 어머니가 이 문제에 한마음이라니 어찌 이리도 기이한지!\""], ["said Yeobright.", "예오브라이트가 말했다."], ["“I have vowed not to go back, Eustacia.", "\"나는 돌아가지 않겠다고 맹세했소, 유스테이시아."], ["It is not the place I dislike; it is the occupation.”", "내가 싫은 것은 그곳이 아니라 그 일이오.\""]], [["“But you can go in some other capacity.”", "\"하지만 다른 자격으로 갈 수도 있잖아요.\""]], [["“No.", "\"안 되오."], ["Besides, it would interfere with my scheme.", "게다가 그건 내 계획을 방해할 거요."], ["Don’t press that, Eustacia.", "그걸 강요하지 마오, 유스테이시아."], ["Will you marry me?”", "나와 혼인하겠소?\""]], [["“I cannot tell.”", "\"말씀드릴 수 없어요.\""]], [["“Now—never mind Paris; it is no better than other spots.", "\"자—파리는 신경 쓰지 마오, 다른 어떤 곳보다 나을 것 없소."], ["Promise, sweet!”", "약속해주오, 어여쁜 이여!\""]], [["“You will never adhere to your education plan, I am quite sure; and then it will be all right for me; and so I promise to be yours for ever and ever.”", "\"당신은 결코 교육 계획을 고수하지 못할 거예요, 저는 확신해요. 그러면 저로서는 다 괜찮은 셈이고, 그러니 영원토록 당신의 것이 되겠다고 약속할게요.\""]], [["Clym brought her face towards his by a gentle pressure of the hand, and kissed her.", "클림은 손을 부드럽게 눌러 그녀의 얼굴을 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기고는 입맞췄다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["but you don’t know what you have got in me,”", "하지만 당신은 나에게서 무엇을 얻게 되는지 몰라요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Sometimes I think there is not that in Eustacia Vye which will make a good homespun wife.", "\"때때로 저는 이 유스테이시아 바이에게는 살뜰한 살림꾼 아내가 될 만한 무언가가 없다고 생각해요."], ["Well, let it go—see how our time is slipping, slipping, slipping!”", "음, 내버려둬요—보세요, 우리 시간이 어떻게 흘러가는지, 흘러가고, 흘러가고, 흘러가는지!\""], ["She pointed towards the half-eclipsed moon.", "그녀가 절반쯤 가려진 달을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“You are too mournful.”", "\"당신은 너무 애처롭소.\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니에요."], ["Only I dread to think of anything beyond the present.", "다만 현재 너머의 무언가를 생각하는 게 두려울 뿐이에요."], ["What is, we know.", "지금 있는 것은 우리가 알지요."], ["We are together now, and it is unknown how long we shall be so; the unknown always fills my mind with terrible possibilities, even when I may reasonably expect it to be cheerful....", "우리는 지금 함께 있고, 얼마나 오래 그럴지는 알 수 없어요. 알 수 없는 것은 언제나 제 마음을 끔찍한 가능성들로 채워요, 마땅히 즐거우리라 기대해도 좋을 때조차요……"], ["Clym, the eclipsed moonlight shines upon your face with a strange foreign colour, and shows its shape as if it were cut out in gold.", "클림, 월식에 든 달빛이 당신 얼굴 위로 묘하게 이국적인 빛깔로 비치고, 마치 황금으로 깎아낸 듯이 그 윤곽을 드러내고 있어요."], ["That means that you should be doing better things than this.”", "그건 당신이 이런 것보다 더 나은 일을 해야 한다는 뜻이에요.\""]], [["“You are ambitious, Eustacia—no, not exactly ambitious, luxurious.", "\"당신은 야심이 있구려, 유스테이시아—아니, 정확히 야심이라기보다는 호사를 즐기는 거요."], ["I ought to be of the same vein, to make you happy, I suppose.", "당신을 행복하게 해주려면 나도 같은 기질이어야겠지요, 아마."], ["And yet, far from that, I could live and die in a hermitage here, with proper work to do.”", "그런데도 그와는 거리가 멀어서, 나는 마땅한 일거리만 있다면 여기 은둔처에서 살다 죽을 수도 있소.\""]], [["There was that in his tone which implied distrust of his position as a solicitous lover, a doubt if he were acting fairly towards one whose tastes touched his own only at rare and infrequent points.", "그의 어조에는 애를 태우는 연인으로서의 자기 입지에 대한 불신이, 자신의 취향과 어쩌다 드문 지점에서만 맞닿는 사람을 향해 자신이 공정하게 행동하고 있는지에 대한 의구심이 배어 있었다."], ["She saw his meaning, and whispered, in a low, full accent of eager assurance, “Don’t mistake me, Clym—though I should like Paris, I love you for yourself alone.", "그녀는 그 뜻을 알아채고는, 나직하면서도 절절하게 안심시키려는 어조로 속삭였다, \"오해하지 마요, 클림—제가 파리를 좋아하기는 해도, 저는 당신 그 자체만을 사랑해요."], ["To be your wife and live in Paris would be heaven to me; but I would rather live with you in a hermitage here than not be yours at all.", "당신의 아내가 되어 파리에서 사는 것은 제게 천국이겠지만, 아예 당신의 것이 되지 못하느니 차라리 여기 은둔처에서라도 당신과 함께 살겠어요."], ["It is gain to me either way, and very great gain.", "어느 쪽이든 제게는 얻는 것이요, 아주 큰 얻음이에요."], ["There’s my too candid confession.”", "이게 너무 솔직한 제 고백이에요.\""]], [["“Spoken like a woman.", "\"여인답게 말하는구려."], ["And now I must soon leave you.", "그리고 이제 곧 당신과 헤어져야 하오."], ["I’ll walk with you towards your house.”", "당신 집 쪽으로 함께 걷겠소.\""]], [["“But must you go home yet?”", "\"하지만 벌써 집에 가야 하나요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."], ["“Yes, the sand has nearly slipped away, I see, and the eclipse is creeping on more and more.", "\"그래요, 모래가 거의 다 빠져나간 게 보이네요, 그리고 월식이 점점 더 번지고 있어요."], ["Don’t go yet!", "아직 가지 마요!"], ["Stop till the hour has run itself out; then I will not press you any more.", "한 시간이 다 지날 때까지 머물러요. 그러면 더는 당신을 붙잡지 않을게요."], ["You will go home and sleep well; I keep sighing in my sleep!", "당신은 집에 가서 잘 자겠지요. 저는 자면서도 자꾸 한숨을 쉬어요!"], ["Do you ever dream of me?”", "제 꿈을 꾸기는 하나요?\""]], [["“I cannot recollect a clear dream of you.”", "\"당신에 관한 또렷한 꿈은 기억나지 않소.\""]], [["“I see your face in every scene of my dreams, and hear your voice in every sound.", "\"저는 제 꿈의 모든 장면에서 당신 얼굴을 보고, 모든 소리에서 당신 목소리를 들어요."], ["I wish I did not.", "그러지 않았으면 싶어요."], ["It is too much what I feel.", "제가 느끼는 게 너무 지나쳐요."], ["They say such love never lasts. But it must!", "사람들은 그런 사랑은 오래가지 않는다고 해요."], ["And yet once, I remember, I saw an officer of the Hussars ride down the street at Budmouth, and though he was a total stranger and never spoke to me, I loved him till I thought I should really die of love—but I didn’t die, and at last I left off caring for him.", "하지만 그래야만 해요! 그런데 한 번, 기억나는데, 부드머스 거리에서 후사르 기병 장교 하나가 말을 타고 내려가는 걸 본 적이 있어요, 그는 전혀 모르는 사람이었고 제게 말 한마디 건넨 적도 없는데, 저는 정말 사랑 때문에 죽겠다 싶을 만큼 그를 사랑했어요—하지만 죽지 않았고, 결국에는 그에게 마음 쓰는 걸 그만뒀어요."], ["How terrible it would be if a time should come when I could not love you, my Clym!”", "만약 당신을 사랑할 수 없는 때가 온다면 얼마나 끔찍할까요, 내 클림!\""]], [["“Please don’t say such reckless things.", "\"제발 그런 무모한 말 마오."], ["When we see such a time at hand we will say, ‘I have outlived my faith and purpose,’ and die.", "그런 때가 가까이 다가오는 걸 보게 되면 우리는 '내 신념과 목적보다 더 오래 살아남았구나'"], ["There, the hour has expired—now let us walk on.”", "하고 말하며 죽읍시다. 자, 한 시간이 다 됐소—이제 걸읍시다.\""]], [["Hand in hand they went along the path towards Mistover.", "손을 맞잡고 그들은 미스토버 쪽 오솔길을 따라 걸었다."], ["When they were near the house he said, “It is too late for me to see your grandfather tonight.", "집 가까이 이르렀을 때 그가 말했다, \"오늘 밤은 당신 할아버지를 뵙기에 너무 늦었소."], ["Do you think he will object to it?”", "그분이 반대하실 것 같소?\""]], [["“I will speak to him.", "\"제가 말씀드릴게요."], ["I am so accustomed to be my own mistress that it did not occur to me that we should have to ask him.”", "제 일은 제가 알아서 하는 데 너무 익숙해져서, 우리가 그분께 여쭈어야 한다는 생각조차 들지 않았어요.\""]], [["Then they lingeringly separated, and Clym descended towards Blooms-End.", "그러고는 그들은 머뭇거리며 헤어졌고, 클림은 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 내려갔다."], ["And as he walked further and further from the charmed atmosphere of his Olympian girl his face grew sad with a new sort of sadness.", "그리고 자신의 올림포스 같은 여인의 매혹적인 분위기에서 점점 더 멀어져 갈수록 그의 얼굴은 새로운 종류의 슬픔으로 어두워졌다."], ["A perception of the dilemma in which his love had placed him came back in full force.", "자신의 사랑이 그를 빠뜨린 진퇴양난을 깨닫는 마음이 온전한 힘으로 되돌아왔다."], ["In spite of Eustacia’s apparent willingness to wait through the period of an unpromising engagement, till he should be established in his new pursuit, he could not but perceive at moments that she loved him rather as a visitant from a gay world to which she rightly belonged than as a man with a purpose opposed to that recent past of his which so interested her.", "그가 새로운 일에 자리 잡을 때까지 전망 없는 약혼 기간을 견디며 기다리겠다는 유스테이시아의 겉보기 의향에도 불구하고, 그는 순간순간 그녀가 자신을 사랑하는 것이 마땅히 그녀가 속한 화려한 세계에서 온 방문객으로서이지, 그녀의 흥미를 그토록 끌었던 그 최근의 과거에 맞서는 목적을 지닌 한 사내로서가 아님을 알아챌 수밖에 없었다."], ["It meant that, though she made no conditions as to his return to the French capital, this was what she secretly longed for in the event of marriage; and it robbed him of many an otherwise pleasant hour.", "그것은, 그녀가 그의 프랑스 수도 복귀에 관해 아무런 조건을 달지 않았음에도, 혼인하게 될 경우 그녀가 남몰래 바라는 바가 바로 그것임을 뜻했고, 그것은 본디 즐거웠을 많은 시간을 그에게서 앗아갔다."], ["Along with that came the widening breach between himself and his mother.", "그와 더불어 자신과 어머니 사이에 벌어지는 균열도 다가왔다."], ["Whenever any little occurrence had brought into more prominence than usual the disappointment that he was causing her it had sent him on lone and moody walks; or he was kept awake a great part of the night by the turmoil of spirit which such a recognition created.", "무언가 자그마한 사건이 그가 어머니에게 안기고 있는 실망을 평소보다 더 두드러지게 할 때마다 그것은 그를 외롭고 침울한 산책길로 내몰았으며, 혹은 그런 인식이 빚어내는 마음의 소요로 밤의 태반을 깬 채로 보내게 했다."], ["If Mrs. Yeobright could only have been led to see what a sound and worthy purpose this purpose of his was and how little it was being affected by his devotions to Eustacia, how differently would she regard him!", "만약 예오브라이트 부인이 그의 이 목적이 얼마나 건전하고 가치 있는 것인지, 그리고 그것이 유스테이시아를 향한 헌신에 얼마나 적게 영향받고 있는지를 보도록 이끌릴 수만 있었다면, 그녀는 그를 얼마나 다르게 여겼을 것인가!"]], [["Thus as his sight grew accustomed to the first blinding halo kindled about him by love and beauty, Yeobright began to perceive what a strait he was in.", "이렇게 사랑과 아름다움이 처음에 자기 둘레에 피워 올린 눈멀게 하는 후광에 그의 시야가 차츰 익숙해지면서, 예오브라이트는 자신이 얼마나 곤경에 처했는지를 깨닫기 시작했다."], ["Sometimes he wished that he had never known Eustacia, immediately to retract the wish as brutal.", "때때로 그는 유스테이시아를 결코 알지 않았더라면 하고 바랐다가, 곧바로 그 소원이 잔인하다며 거두어들이곤 했다."], ["Three antagonistic growths had to be kept alive: his mother’s trust in him, his plan for becoming a teacher, and Eustacia’s happiness.", "서로 적대하는 세 가지 성장이 모두 살아 있어야만 했으니—자신을 향한 어머니의 믿음, 교사가 되려는 자신의 계획, 그리고 유스테이시아의 행복이었다."], ["His fervid nature could not afford to relinquish one of these, though two of the three were as many as he could hope to preserve.", "그의 격렬한 천성으로는 이 가운데 하나도 포기할 수 없었으나, 셋 중 둘조차도 그가 지켜내기를 바랄 수 있는 최대치였다."], ["Though his love was as chaste as that of Petrarch for his Laura, it had made fetters of what previously was only a difficulty.", "그의 사랑은 페트라르카가 라우라를 향해 품었던 것만큼이나 정결했음에도, 그것은 이전에는 그저 하나의 어려움에 지나지 않았던 것을 족쇄로 만들어버렸다."], ["A position which was not too simple when he stood whole-hearted had become indescribably complicated by the addition of Eustacia.", "그가 한마음으로 서 있을 때조차 그리 단순하지만은 않았던 처지가, 유스테이시아가 더해지면서 형언할 수 없이 복잡해졌다."], ["Just when his mother was beginning to tolerate one scheme he had introduced another still bitterer than the first, and the combination was more than she could bear.", "어머니가 막 한 가지 계획을 용인하기 시작했을 때 그는 처음 것보다 더 쓰디쓴 또 다른 계획을 끄집어냈으니, 그 조합은 그녀가 감당할 수 있는 것 이상이었다."]], [["V.", "V."], ["Sharp Words Are Spoken, and a Crisis Ensues", "날카로운 말이 오가고, 위기가 닥치다"]], [["When Yeobright was not with Eustacia he was sitting slavishly over his books; when he was not reading he was meeting her.", "예오브라이트는 유스테이시아와 함께 있지 않을 때면 노예처럼 책에 매달려 앉아 있었고, 책을 읽지 않을 때면 그녀를 만나고 있었다."], ["These meetings were carried on with the greatest secrecy.", "이 만남들은 극도의 비밀 속에서 이루어졌다."]], [["One afternoon his mother came home from a morning visit to Thomasin.", "어느 날 오후 그의 어머니가 토머신을 오전에 방문하고 돌아왔다."], ["He could see from a disturbance in the lines of her face that something had happened.", "그녀의 얼굴 주름이 흐트러진 것을 보고 그는 무슨 일인가가 일어났음을 알 수 있었다."]], [["“I have been told an incomprehensible thing,”", "\"도무지 이해할 수 없는 이야기를 들었구나.\""], ["she said mournfully.", "그녀가 애통하게 말했다."], ["“The captain has let out at the Woman that you and Eustacia Vye are engaged to be married.”", "\"선장이 콰이엇 우먼에서 너와 유스테이시아 바이가 혼인하기로 약혼했다고 떠벌렸다더라.\""]], [["“We are,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["said Yeobright.", "예오브라이트가 말했다."], ["“But it may not be yet for a very long time.”", "\"하지만 아주 오랫동안 그렇게 되지 않을 수도 있어요.\""]], [["“I should hardly think it _would_ be yet for a very long time!", "\"아주 오랫동안 그렇게 되지 않으리라고는 좀처럼 생각되지 않는구나!"], ["You will take her to Paris, I suppose?”", "그 애를 파리로 데려갈 테지, 그렇지?\""], ["She spoke with weary hopelessness.", "그녀가 지친 절망감으로 말했다."]], [["“I am not going back to Paris.”", "\"파리로 돌아가지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“What will you do with a wife, then?”", "\"그러면 아내를 데리고 무얼 하려고?\""]], [["“Keep a school in Budmouth, as I have told you.”", "\"말씀드렸듯이 부드머스에서 학교를 운영할 겁니다.\""]], [["“That’s incredible!", "\"그건 도무지 믿을 수 없는 일이다!"], ["The place is overrun with schoolmasters.", "그곳은 교사들로 넘쳐난다."], ["You have no special qualifications.", "너는 특별한 자격도 없잖니."], ["What possible chance is there for such as you?”", "너 같은 사람에게 무슨 가망이 있겠어?\""]], [["“There is no chance of getting rich.", "\"부자가 될 가망은 없지요."], ["But with my system of education, which is as new as it is true, I shall do a great deal of good to my fellow-creatures.”", "하지만 제 교육 방식은 참된 만큼이나 새로운 것이라, 저는 동포들에게 크나큰 이로움을 주게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“Dreams, dreams!", "\"꿈이다, 꿈이야!"], ["If there had been any system left to be invented they would have found it out at the universities long before this time.”", "발명될 만한 방식이 조금이라도 남아 있었다면 진작에 대학에서 찾아냈을 게다.\""]], [["“Never, Mother.", "\"결코 그렇지 않아요, 어머니."], ["They cannot find it out, because their teachers don’t come in contact with the class which demands such a system—that is, those who have had no preliminary training.", "그들은 찾아낼 수 없습니다, 그들의 교사들이 그런 방식을 요구하는 계층과—다시 말해 아무런 사전 훈련도 받지 못한 사람들과 접촉하지 않으니까요."], ["My plan is one for instilling high knowledge into empty minds without first cramming them with what has to be uncrammed again before true study begins.”", "제 계획은 빈 마음에 높은 지식을 불어넣되, 참된 공부가 시작되기 전에 다시 비워내야 할 것들로 그 마음을 먼저 채워 넣지 않는 것입니다.\""]], [["“I might have believed you if you had kept yourself free from entanglements; but this woman—if she had been a good girl it would have been bad enough; but being——”", "\"네가 얽매임에서 자유로운 채로 지냈더라면 너를 믿었을지도 모른다. 하지만 이 여자는—그 애가 착한 처녀였더라도 충분히 나빴을 텐데, 하물며—\""]], [["“She is a good girl.”", "\"착한 처녀예요.\""]], [["“So you think.", "\"네 생각엔 그렇겠지."], ["A Corfu bandmaster’s daughter!", "코르푸 군악대장의 딸이라니!"], ["What has her life been?", "그 애가 살아온 인생이 어땠겠니?"], ["Her surname even is not her true one.”", "성姓조차 진짜가 아니잖아.\""]], [["“She is Captain Vye’s granddaughter, and her father merely took her mother’s name.", "\"그분은 바이 선장의 손녀이고, 그녀의 아버지가 그저 어머니 쪽 성을 따랐을 뿐입니다."], ["And she is a lady by instinct.”", "그리고 그녀는 천성이 숙녀예요.\""]], [["“They call him ‘captain,’ but anybody is captain.”", "\"사람들이 그자를 '선장'이라 부르지만, 누구든 선장이 될 수 있지.\""]], [["“He was in the Royal Navy!”", "\"그분은 영국 해군에 계셨어요!\""]], [["“No doubt he has been to sea in some tub or other.", "\"틀림없이 무슨 통짝배인지 뭔지를 타고 바다에 나갔겠지."], ["Why doesn’t he look after her?", "그자는 어째서 손녀를 돌보지 않는 게냐?"], ["No lady would rove about the heath at all hours of the day and night as she does.", "숙녀라면 그 애처럼 밤낮 가리지 않고 광야를 쏘다니지 않는다."], ["But that’s not all of it.", "그러나 그게 전부가 아니다."], ["There was something queer between her and Thomasin’s husband at one time—I am as sure of it as that I stand here.”", "한때 그 애와 토머신의 남편 사이에 묘한 일이 있었어—내가 여기 서 있는 것만큼이나 확실하다.\""]], [["“Eustacia has told me.", "\"유스테이시아가 제게 말해줬어요."], ["He did pay her a little attention a year ago; but there’s no harm in that.", "그가 일 년 전에 그녀에게 약간의 관심을 보이긴 했지만, 거기에 해로울 건 없습니다."], ["I like her all the better.”", "저는 그녀가 더욱 좋아질 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Clym,”", "\"클림,\""], ["said his mother with firmness, “I have no proofs against her, unfortunately.", "그의 어머니가 단호하게 말했다, \"안타깝게도 그 애를 거스를 증거는 내게 없다."], ["But if she makes you a good wife, there has never been a bad one.”", "하지만 그 애가 네게 좋은 아내가 된다면, 나쁜 아내란 세상에 한 번도 없었던 셈이 될 거다.\""]], [["“Believe me, you are almost exasperating,”", "\"정말이지 어머니는 사람을 거의 격분시키시는군요.\""], ["said Yeobright vehemently.", "예오브라이트가 격하게 말했다."], ["“And this very day I had intended to arrange a meeting between you.", "\"바로 오늘 두 분이 만나도록 자리를 마련하려 했어요."], ["But you give me no peace; you try to thwart my wishes in everything.”", "그런데 어머니는 제게 한 점의 평안도 주지 않으시고, 매사에 제 바람을 가로막으려 하시는군요.\""]], [["“I hate the thought of any son of mine marrying badly!", "\"내 자식이 잘못된 혼인을 한다는 생각이 너무나 싫구나!"], ["I wish I had never lived to see this; it is too much for me—it is more than I dreamt!”", "이런 꼴을 보려고 이렇게 살아오지는 않았다. 내겐 너무 벅차—내가 꿈꾼 것 이상이야!\""], ["She turned to the window.", "그녀가 창문 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Her breath was coming quickly, and her lips were pale, parted, and trembling.", "숨이 가빠지고, 입술은 창백하게 벌어진 채 떨리고 있었다."]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["said Clym, “whatever you do, you will always be dear to me—that you know.", "클림이 말했다, \"어머니가 무얼 하시든, 제게는 늘 소중한 분이실 거예요—그건 아시잖아요."], ["But one thing I have a right to say, which is, that at my age I am old enough to know what is best for me.”", "하지만 한 가지 제가 말할 권리가 있는 게 있으니, 제 나이쯤 되면 무엇이 제게 최선인지 알 만큼은 충분히 나이를 먹었다는 거예요.\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright remained for some time silent and shaken, as if she could say no more.", "예오브라이트 부인은 한동안 침묵한 채 흔들렸으니, 마치 더는 할 말이 없는 듯했다."], ["Then she replied, “Best?", "그러더니 그녀가 대답했다, \"최선이라고?"], ["Is it best for you to injure your prospects for such a voluptuous, idle woman as that?", "저런 음탕하고 게으른 여자 때문에 네 앞날을 망치는 게 네게 최선이란 말이냐?"], ["Don’t you see that by the very fact of your choosing her you prove that you do not know what is best for you?", "바로 그 애를 택했다는 사실 자체로 네가 무엇이 네게 최선인지 모른다는 걸 입증하는 게 보이지 않니?"], ["You give up your whole thought—you set your whole soul—to please a woman.”", "너는 한 여자를 기쁘게 하려고 네 모든 생각을 내던지고—네 온 영혼을 바치고 있다.\""]], [["“I do.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["And that woman is you.”", "그리고 그 여자가 바로 어머니예요.\""]], [["“How can you treat me so flippantly!”", "\"어찌 나를 그리도 경박하게 대할 수 있단 말이냐!\""], ["said his mother, turning again to him with a tearful look.", "그의 어머니가 눈물 어린 표정으로 다시 그를 향하며 말했다."], ["“You are unnatural, Clym, and I did not expect it.”", "\"넌 비정상이다, 클림, 나는 그러리라곤 예상하지 못했어.\""]], [["“Very likely,”", "\"그러시겠지요.\""], ["said he cheerlessly.", "그가 풀죽은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“You did not know the measure you were going to mete me, and therefore did not know the measure that would be returned to you again.”", "\"어머니는 제게 베푸시려던 잣대를 모르셨고, 그러니 그 잣대가 어머니께 되돌아오리라는 것도 모르셨던 거예요.\""]], [["“You answer me; you think only of her.", "\"넌 내게 말대답을 하는구나. 넌 그 애 생각뿐이야."], ["You stick to her in all things.”", "매사에 그 애 편을 들고.\""]], [["“That proves her to be worthy.", "\"그건 그녀가 그럴 만한 사람임을 입증하는 거예요."], ["I have never yet supported what is bad.", "저는 여태껏 나쁜 것을 편든 적이 없습니다."], ["And I do not care only for her.", "그리고 저는 그녀만 위하는 게 아니에요."], ["I care for you and for myself, and for anything that is good.", "저는 어머니를, 그리고 저 자신을, 또 좋은 것이라면 무엇이든 위합니다."], ["When a woman once dislikes another she is merciless!”", "여자가 일단 다른 여자를 싫어하면 그토록 무자비한 법이지요!\""]], [["“O Clym!", "\"오 클림!"], ["please don’t go setting down as my fault what is your obstinate wrongheadedness.", "네 고집스러운 외고집을 부디 내 잘못으로 돌리지 말아다오."], ["If you wished to connect yourself with an unworthy person why did you come home here to do it?", "가치 없는 사람과 맺어지고 싶었다면 어째서 굳이 그러려고 이 집에 돌아온 게냐?"], ["Why didn’t you do it in Paris?—it is more the fashion there.", "어째서 파리에서 그러지 않았어?—거기서는 그게 더 흔한 풍습이잖니."], ["You have come only to distress me, a lonely woman, and shorten my days!", "너는 그저 나를, 외로운 여자를 괴롭히고 내 수명을 줄이려고 돌아온 게다!"], ["I wish that you would bestow your presence where you bestow your love!”", "네 사랑을 바치는 곳에 네 존재도 바쳤으면 좋겠구나!\""]], [["Clym said huskily, “You are my mother.", "클림이 쉰 목소리로 말했다, \"어머니는 제 어머니십니다."], ["I will say no more—beyond this, that I beg your pardon for having thought this my home.", "더는 말하지 않겠어요—다만 이것만은, 이곳을 제 집이라 여겼던 것을 용서해주십사 빌 뿐입니다."], ["I will no longer inflict myself upon you; I’ll go.”", "더는 어머니께 저를 떠안기지 않겠어요. 떠나겠습니다.\""], ["And he went out with tears in his eyes.", "그러고는 눈에 눈물을 머금은 채 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["It was a sunny afternoon at the beginning of summer, and the moist hollows of the heath had passed from their brown to their green stage.", "초여름이 시작될 무렵의 햇살 가득한 오후였고, 광야의 축축한 우묵한 곳들은 갈색에서 초록 단계로 옮아가 있었다."], ["Yeobright walked to the edge of the basin which extended down from Mistover and Rainbarrow.", "예오브라이트는 미스토버와 레인배로에서부터 뻗어 내려온 분지의 가장자리까지 걸어갔다."]], [["By this time he was calm, and he looked over the landscape.", "이 무렵 그는 차분해져서 풍경을 둘러보았다."], ["In the minor valleys, between the hillocks which diversified the contour of the vale, the fresh young ferns were luxuriantly growing up, ultimately to reach a height of five or six feet.", "골짜기의 윤곽을 다채롭게 하는 자그마한 둔덕들 사이의 작은 골짜기들에는, 싱싱한 어린 고사리들이 무성하게 자라 올라 마침내 대여섯 자 높이에 이를 참이었다."], ["He descended a little way, flung himself down in a spot where a path emerged from one of the small hollows, and waited.", "그는 조금 내려가, 작은 우묵한 곳 하나에서 오솔길이 나오는 자리에 몸을 던지듯 누워 기다렸다."], ["Hither it was that he had promised Eustacia to bring his mother this afternoon, that they might meet and be friends.", "바로 이곳으로 그는 오늘 오후 어머니를 모셔 와 유스테이시아와 만나게 하여 두 사람이 화해하도록 하겠노라고 약속했었다."], ["His attempt had utterly failed.", "그의 시도는 완전히 실패하고 말았다."]], [["He was in a nest of vivid green.", "그는 선명한 초록의 둥지 속에 있었다."], ["The ferny vegetation round him, though so abundant, was quite uniform—it was a grove of machine-made foliage, a world of green triangles with saw-edges, and not a single flower.", "그를 둘러싼 고사리 식생은 그토록 무성하면서도 아주 한결같았으니—기계로 찍어낸 듯한 잎사귀들의 숲이요, 톱니 모양의 가장자리를 지닌 초록 삼각형들의 세계였고, 단 한 송이의 꽃도 없었다."], ["The air was warm with a vaporous warmth, and the stillness was unbroken.", "공기는 수증기 같은 온기로 따스했고, 정적은 깨지지 않았다."], ["Lizards, grasshoppers, and ants were the only living things to be beheld.", "도마뱀과 메뚜기와 개미만이 눈에 띄는 유일한 생물이었다."], ["The scene seemed to belong to the ancient world of the carboniferous period, when the forms of plants were few, and of the fern kind; when there was neither bud nor blossom, nothing but a monotonous extent of leafage, amid which no bird sang.", "그 광경은 식물의 형태가 몇 안 되고 고사리류뿐이던, 봉오리도 꽃도 없이 그저 단조롭게 펼쳐진 잎사귀밖에 없어 그 가운데서 새 한 마리 노래하지 않던 석탄기의 태고 세계에 속한 듯이 보였다."]], [["When he had reclined for some considerable time, gloomily pondering, he discerned above the ferns a drawn bonnet of white silk approaching from the left, and Yeobright knew directly that it covered the head of her he loved.", "상당한 시간 동안 몸을 기댄 채 침울하게 생각에 잠겨 있던 그는, 고사리 위로 흰 비단 보닛이 왼편에서 다가오는 것을 알아보았고, 예오브라이트는 그것이 자신이 사랑하는 이의 머리를 감싸고 있음을 곧장 알았다."], ["His heart awoke from its apathy to a warm excitement, and, jumping to his feet, he said aloud, “I knew she was sure to come.”", "그의 마음은 무기력에서 깨어나 따스한 흥분으로 들떴고, 벌떡 일어서며 그는 소리 내어 말했다, \"그녀가 틀림없이 오리라는 걸 알고 있었어.\""]], [["She vanished in a hollow for a few moments, and then her whole form unfolded itself from the brake.", "그녀는 잠시 우묵한 곳으로 사라졌다가, 이윽고 덤불에서 온몸을 펼치듯 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["“Only you here?”", "\"여기 당신뿐이에요?\""], ["she exclaimed, with a disappointed air, whose hollowness was proved by her rising redness and her half-guilty low laugh.", "그녀가 실망한 듯한 기색으로 외쳤으나, 그 기색의 공허함은 점점 붉어지는 얼굴과 절반쯤 죄지은 듯한 나직한 웃음으로 드러났다."], ["“Where is Mrs. Yeobright?”", "\"예오브라이트 부인은 어디 계세요?\""]], [["“She has not come,”", "\"안 오셨소.\""], ["he replied in a subdued tone.", "그가 가라앉은 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“I wish I had known that you would be here alone,” she said seriously, “and that we were going to have such an idle, pleasant time as this.", "\"당신이 여기 혼자 있으리라는 걸, 그리고 우리가 이렇게 한가롭고 즐거운 시간을 갖게 되리라는 걸 미리 알았더라면 좋았을 텐데.\""], ["Pleasure not known beforehand is half wasted; to anticipate it is to double it.", "그녀가 진지하게 말했다. \"미리 알지 못한 즐거움은 절반은 허비되는 거예요."], ["I have not thought once today of having you all to myself this afternoon, and the actual moment of a thing is so soon gone.”", "즐거움을 기대하는 것은 그걸 두 배로 만드는 거니까요. 오늘 저는 이 오후에 당신을 온전히 제 차지로 두게 되리라곤 한 번도 생각하지 못했고, 어떤 일이든 실제 그 순간은 너무도 빨리 지나가버려요.\""]], [["“It is indeed.”", "\"정말 그렇소.\""]], [["“Poor Clym!”", "\"가엾은 클림!\""], ["she continued, looking tenderly into his face.", "그녀가 그의 얼굴을 다정하게 들여다보며 말을 이었다."], ["“You are sad.", "\"당신은 슬프군요."], ["Something has happened at your home.", "댁에서 무슨 일이 있었어요."], ["Never mind what is—let us only look at what seems.”", "무엇이 있는지는 신경 쓰지 마요—그저 무엇이 있어 보이는지만 봐요.\""]], [["“But, darling, what shall we do?”", "\"하지만 여보, 우리 어떻게 해야 하오?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Still go on as we do now—just live on from meeting to meeting, never minding about another day.", "\"그냥 지금처럼 계속해요—그저 만남에서 만남으로 살아가며, 다른 날에 대해서는 마음 쓰지 마요."], ["You, I know, are always thinking of that—I can see you are.", "당신이 늘 그걸 생각하고 있다는 걸 저는 알아요—보면 알아요."], ["But you must not—will you, dear Clym?”", "하지만 그래선 안 돼요—그러지 않을 거죠, 사랑하는 클림?\""]], [["“You are just like all women.", "\"당신은 꼭 모든 여인 같구려."], ["They are ever content to build their lives on any incidental position that offers itself; whilst men would fain make a globe to suit them.", "그들은 어쩌다 주어지는 처지 위에 자기 삶을 세우는 것에 늘 만족하지만, 사내들은 자기에게 맞는 세계를 기꺼이 만들어내려 하지요."], ["Listen to this, Eustacia.", "이 말을 들어보오, 유스테이시아."], ["There is a subject I have determined to put off no longer.", "더는 미루지 않기로 작정한 문제가 하나 있소."], ["Your sentiment on the wisdom of _Carpe diem_ does not impress me today.", "'카르페 디엠'의 지혜에 관한 당신의 정서는 오늘 내게 와닿지 않소."], ["Our present mode of life must shortly be brought to an end.”", "우리의 현재 삶의 방식은 머지않아 끝나야만 하오.\""]], [["“It is your mother!”", "\"당신 어머니 때문이군요!\""]], [["“It is.", "\"그렇소."], ["I love you none the less in telling you; it is only right you should know.”", "이걸 말한다고 해서 당신을 덜 사랑하는 게 아니오. 다만 당신이 마땅히 알아야 하기 때문이오.\""]], [["“I have feared my bliss,”", "\"제 환희가 두려웠어요.\""], ["she said, with the merest motion of her lips.", "그녀가 입술을 거의 움직이지 않은 채 말했다."], ["“It has been too intense and consuming.”", "\"그것은 너무 강렬하고 소진시키는 것이었어요.\""]], [["“There is hope yet.", "\"아직 희망은 있소."], ["There are forty years of work in me yet, and why should you despair?", "내게는 아직 사십 년의 일이 남아 있는데, 어찌 절망해야 하겠소?"], ["I am only at an awkward turning.", "나는 그저 어색한 길목에 서 있을 뿐이오."], ["I wish people wouldn’t be so ready to think that there is no progress without uniformity.”", "한결같음 없이는 진보도 없다고 사람들이 그토록 쉽게 단정하지 않았으면 좋겠소.\""]], [["“Ah—your mind runs off to the philosophical side of it.", "\"아—당신 마음은 그 일의 철학적인 측면으로 달아나는군요."], ["Well, these sad and hopeless obstacles are welcome in one sense, for they enable us to look with indifference upon the cruel satires that Fate loves to indulge in.", "음, 이 슬프고 가망 없는 장애물들은 어떤 의미에서는 반가운 것이에요, 운명이 즐겨 부리는 잔인한 풍자들을 무심하게 바라볼 수 있게 해주니까요."], ["I have heard of people, who, upon coming suddenly into happiness, have died from anxiety lest they should not live to enjoy it.", "들은 적이 있어요, 갑자기 행복에 들어선 사람들이 그걸 누릴 만큼 오래 살지 못할까 봐 불안에 못 이겨 죽었다는 이야기를요."], ["I felt myself in that whimsical state of uneasiness lately; but I shall be spared it now.", "저도 최근에 그런 변덕스러운 불안의 상태에 빠져 있었는데, 이제는 그걸 면하게 되겠네요."], ["Let us walk on.”", "자, 걸어요.\""]], [["Clym took the hand which was already bared for him—it was a favourite way with them to walk bare hand in bare hand—and led her through the ferns.", "클림은 이미 그를 위해 드러나 있던 손을 잡았으니—맨손과 맨손을 맞잡고 걷는 것이 그들이 좋아하던 방식이었다—그리고 고사리 사이로 그녀를 이끌었다."], ["They formed a very comely picture of love at full flush, as they walked along the valley that late afternoon, the sun sloping down on their right, and throwing their thin spectral shadows, tall as poplar trees, far out across the furze and fern.", "그날 저물녘 골짜기를 따라 걸어가는 그들은 한껏 만개한 사랑의 더없이 어여쁜 그림을 이루었으니, 해는 그들의 오른편으로 비스듬히 기울며 미루나무처럼 큰 그들의 가냘프고 유령 같은 그림자를 가시금작화와 고사리 너머 멀리까지 드리웠다."], ["Eustacia went with her head thrown back fancifully, a certain glad and voluptuous air of triumph pervading her eyes at having won by her own unaided self a man who was her perfect complement in attainment, appearance, and age.", "유스테이시아는 짐짓 머리를 뒤로 젖힌 채 걸었으니, 성취와 외모와 나이에서 자신을 완벽하게 보완해주는 사내를 오로지 자기 혼자 힘으로 얻어냈다는, 어떤 기쁘고도 관능적인 승리의 기운이 그 눈에 두루 어려 있었다."], ["On the young man’s part, the paleness of face which he had brought with him from Paris, and the incipient marks of time and thought, were less perceptible than when he returned, the healthful and energetic sturdiness which was his by nature having partially recovered its original proportions.", "젊은 사내 쪽으로 말하자면, 파리에서 가져왔던 얼굴의 창백함과 세월과 사색이 남긴 초기의 흔적들은, 본디 그의 것이었던 건강하고 활기찬 다부짐이 원래의 비율을 어느 정도 되찾으면서, 돌아왔을 때보다는 덜 두드러졌다."], ["They wandered onward till they reached the nether margin of the heath, where it became marshy and merged in moorland.", "그들은 광야의 가장 낮은 가장자리에 이를 때까지 거닐었으니, 그곳에서 땅은 질척해지며 황무지로 녹아들었다."]], [["“I must part from you here, Clym,”", "\"여기서 당신과 헤어져야 해요, 클림.\""], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이시아가 말했다."]], [["They stood still and prepared to bid each other farewell.", "그들은 멈춰 서서 작별 인사를 나눌 채비를 했다."], ["Everything before them was on a perfect level.", "그들 앞의 모든 것은 완벽하게 평평했다."], ["The sun, resting on the horizon line, streamed across the ground from between copper-coloured and lilac clouds, stretched out in flats beneath a sky of pale soft green.", "지평선에 걸린 해는 구릿빛과 연보랏빛 구름 사이로 땅을 가로질러 빛을 흘려보냈고, 그 구름들은 옅고 부드러운 초록빛 하늘 아래 평평하게 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["All dark objects on the earth that lay towards the sun were overspread by a purple haze, against which groups of wailing gnats shone out, rising upwards and dancing about like sparks of fire.", "해를 향해 누운 땅 위의 온갖 어두운 것들은 자줏빛 아지랑이에 뒤덮였고, 그것을 배경으로 구슬피 우는 각다귀 떼가 빛을 내며 위로 솟아올라 불티처럼 춤추듯 맴돌았다."]], [["“O!", "\"오!"], ["this leaving you is too hard to bear!”", "당신과 헤어지는 건 견디기에 너무 힘겨워요!\""], ["exclaimed Eustacia in a sudden whisper of anguish.", "유스테이시아가 별안간 고뇌에 찬 속삭임으로 외쳤다."], ["“Your mother will influence you too much; I shall not be judged fairly, it will get afloat that I am not a good girl, and the witch story will be added to make me blacker!”", "\"당신 어머니가 당신을 너무 많이 움직이실 거예요. 저는 공정하게 판단받지 못할 거고, 제가 착한 처녀가 아니라는 소문이 퍼질 거고, 거기에 마녀 이야기까지 더해져 저를 더 새카맣게 만들 거예요!\""]], [["“They cannot.", "\"그럴 수 없소."], ["Nobody dares to speak disrespectfully of you or of me.”", "누구도 감히 당신이나 나에 대해 함부로 말하지 못하오.\""]], [["“Oh how I wish I was sure of never losing you—that you could not be able to desert me anyhow!”", "\"오, 제가 당신을 결코 잃지 않으리라고—당신이 어떻게도 저를 저버릴 수 없으리라고 확신할 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까요!\""]], [["Clym stood silent a moment.", "클림은 잠시 말없이 서 있었다."], ["His feelings were high, the moment was passionate, and he cut the knot.", "그의 감정은 고조되어 있었고, 그 순간은 격정적이었으며, 그는 매듭을 끊어버렸다."]], [["“You shall be sure of me, darling,”", "\"당신은 나를 확신해도 좋소, 여보.\""], ["he said, folding her in his arms.", "그가 그녀를 품에 안으며 말했다."], ["“We will be married at once.”", "\"우리 당장 혼인합시다.\""]], [["“O Clym!”", "\"오, 클림!\""]], [["“Do you agree to it?”", "\"동의하오?\""], ["“If—if we can.”", "\"만약—만약 할 수 있다면요.\""]], [["“We certainly can, both being of full age.", "\"우리는 분명 할 수 있소, 둘 다 성년이니."], ["And I have not followed my occupation all these years without having accumulated money; and if you will agree to live in a tiny cottage somewhere on the heath, until I take a house in Budmouth for the school, we can do it at a very little expense.”", "그리고 나는 이 여러 해 동안 일을 하면서 돈을 모아두지 않은 것도 아니오. 학교를 차리려고 부드머스에 집을 얻을 때까지 당신이 광야 어딘가 자그마한 오두막에서 살기로 동의해준다면, 우리는 아주 적은 비용으로 해낼 수 있소.\""]], [["“How long shall we have to live in the tiny cottage, Clym?”", "\"그 자그마한 오두막에서 우리는 얼마나 오래 살아야 해요, 클림?\""]], [["“About six months.", "\"여섯 달쯤이오."], ["At the end of that time I shall have finished my reading—yes, we will do it, and this heart-aching will be over.", "그쯤 되면 나는 공부를 마칠 거요—그래요, 우리 그렇게 합시다, 그러면 이 마음앓이도 끝날 거요."], ["We shall, of course, live in absolute seclusion, and our married life will only begin to outward view when we take the house in Budmouth, where I have already addressed a letter on the matter.", "물론 우리는 완전한 은둔 속에 살 테고, 우리 부부생활은 부드머스에 집을 얻고 나서야 비로소 겉보기로도 시작될 텐데, 그 일에 관해서는 이미 편지를 부쳐두었소."], ["Would your grandfather allow you?”", "당신 할아버지가 허락하실까?\""]], [["“I think he would—on the understanding that it should not last longer than six months.”", "\"허락하실 것 같아요—여섯 달보다 더 길어지지 않는다는 조건이라면요.\""]], [["“I will guarantee that, if no misfortune happens.”", "\"불행한 일만 일어나지 않는다면 그건 내가 보장하리다.\""]], [["“If no misfortune happens,”", "\"불행한 일만 일어나지 않는다면요.\""], ["she repeated slowly.", "그녀가 천천히 되뇌었다."]], [["“Which is not likely.", "\"그럴 리야 없소."], ["Dearest, fix the exact day.”", "가장 사랑하는 이여, 정확한 날을 정합시다.\""]], [["And then they consulted on the question, and the day was chosen.", "그러고는 그들은 그 문제를 의논했고, 날이 정해졌다."], ["It was to be a fortnight from that time.", "그날로부터 두 주 뒤로 하기로 했다."]], [["This was the end of their talk, and Eustacia left him.", "이것으로 그들의 이야기는 끝났고, 유스테이시아는 그를 떠났다."], ["Clym watched her as she retired towards the sun.", "클림은 그녀가 해를 향해 물러가는 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["The luminous rays wrapped her up with her increasing distance, and the rustle of her dress over the sprouting sedge and grass died away.", "그녀가 멀어질수록 빛나는 햇살이 그녀를 감싸 안았고, 돋아나는 사초와 풀 위로 스치는 그녀의 옷자락 소리가 잦아들었다."], ["As he watched, the dead flat of the scenery overpowered him, though he was fully alive to the beauty of that untarnished early summer green which was worn for the nonce by the poorest blade.", "그가 지켜보는 동안, 그 풍경의 죽은 듯한 평평함이 그를 압도했으니, 비록 그가 그 흠 없는 초여름 초록—가장 보잘것없는 풀잎조차 잠시나마 걸치고 있는 그 초록의 아름다움에는 온전히 깨어 있었음에도 그러했다."], ["There was something in its oppressive horizontality which too much reminded him of the arena of life; it gave him a sense of bare equality with, and no superiority to, a single living thing under the sun.", "그 짓누르는 듯한 수평성에는 너무도 삶의 무대를 떠올리게 하는 무언가가 있었으니, 그것은 그에게 해 아래의 그 어떤 살아 있는 것보다도 우월하지 않고 그저 헐벗은 채 대등할 뿐이라는 느낌을 안겨주었다."]], [["Eustacia was now no longer the goddess but the woman to him, a being to fight for, support, help, be maligned for.", "이제 유스테이시아는 그에게 더는 여신이 아니라 한 여인이었으니, 그를 위해 싸우고, 부양하고, 돕고, 그로 인해 욕먹어야 할 존재였다."], ["Now that he had reached a cooler moment he would have preferred a less hasty marriage; but the card was laid, and he determined to abide by the game.", "더 냉정해진 순간에 이르고 보니 그는 좀 덜 성급한 혼인을 택했으면 했으나, 패가 던져졌으니 그는 이 승부를 따르기로 마음먹었다."], ["Whether Eustacia was to add one other to the list of those who love too hotly to love long and well, the forthcoming event was certainly a ready way of proving.", "유스테이시아가 오래도록 잘 사랑하기에는 너무 뜨겁게 사랑하는 이들의 명단에 또 하나의 이름을 보태게 될지, 다가올 사건이야말로 분명 그것을 가려낼 손쉬운 방법이었다."]], [["VI.", "VI."], ["Yeobright Goes, and the Breach Is Complete", "예오브라이트가 떠나고, 균열이 완성되다"]], [["All that evening smart sounds denoting an active packing up came from Yeobright’s room to the ears of his mother downstairs.", "그날 저녁 내내 부지런히 짐을 꾸리는 것을 알리는 분주한 소리들이 예오브라이트의 방에서 아래층의 어머니 귀로 흘러 내려왔다."]], [["Next morning he departed from the house and again proceeded across the heath.", "이튿날 아침 그는 집을 떠나 다시 광야를 가로질러 나아갔다."], ["A long day’s march was before him, his object being to secure a dwelling to which he might take Eustacia when she became his wife.", "긴 하루의 행군이 그 앞에 놓여 있었으니, 그의 목적은 유스테이시아가 아내가 되었을 때 데려갈 거처를 마련하는 것이었다."], ["Such a house, small, secluded, and with its windows boarded up, he had casually observed a month earlier, about two miles beyond the village of East Egdon, and six miles distant altogether; and thither he directed his steps today.", "그런 집—작고 외지며 창에 널빤지가 덧대어진 집을 그는 한 달쯤 전에 이스트 에그던 마을 너머 두 마일쯤 떨어진, 통틀어 여섯 마일 거리의 곳에서 우연히 봐두었고, 오늘 그곳으로 발걸음을 향했다."]], [["The weather was far different from that of the evening before.", "날씨는 전날 저녁과는 사뭇 달랐다."], ["The yellow and vapoury sunset which had wrapped up Eustacia from his parting gaze had presaged change.", "작별의 시선에서 유스테이시아를 감싸 안았던 노랗고 수증기 어린 일몰은 변화를 예고했었다."], ["It was one of those not infrequent days of an English June which are as wet and boisterous as November.", "십일월처럼 비 내리고 거센, 영국 유월의 드물지 않은 그런 날들 가운데 하루였다."], ["The cold clouds hastened on in a body, as if painted on a moving slide.", "차가운 구름들이 마치 움직이는 슬라이드에 그려진 듯 한 덩어리로 서둘러 지나갔다."], ["Vapours from other continents arrived upon the wind, which curled and parted round him as he walked on.", "다른 대륙에서 온 수증기들이 바람을 타고 닿아, 그가 걷는 동안 그를 둘러싸고 휘돌다 갈라졌다."]], [["At length Clym reached the margin of a fir and beech plantation that had been enclosed from heath-land in the year of his birth.", "마침내 클림은 그가 태어난 해에 광야에서 떼어내 둘러친 전나무와 너도밤나무 농원의 가장자리에 이르렀다."], ["Here the trees, laden heavily with their new and humid leaves, were now suffering more damage than during the highest winds of winter, when the boughs are especially disencumbered to do battle with the storm.", "이곳의 나무들은 새로 돋은 축축한 잎을 무겁게 인 채, 가지들이 폭풍과 싸우려 한껏 헐벗는 한겨울의 거센 바람 때보다도 지금 더 큰 피해를 입고 있었다."], ["The wet young beeches were undergoing amputations, bruises, cripplings, and harsh lacerations, from which the wasting sap would bleed for many a day to come, and which would leave scars visible till the day of their burning.", "비에 젖은 어린 너도밤나무들은 절단과 타박상과 불구가 됨과 모진 찢김을 겪고 있었으니, 그로부터 흘러나오는 수액은 앞으로 여러 날 피처럼 흐를 테고, 불태워지는 날까지도 눈에 보일 흉터를 남길 터였다."], ["Each stem was wrenched at the root, where it moved like a bone in its socket, and at every onset of the gale convulsive sounds came from the branches, as if pain were felt.", "줄기마다 뿌리께가 비틀려, 마치 관절 속의 뼈처럼 움직였고, 돌풍이 들이칠 때마다 가지들에서 경련하는 듯한 소리가 났으니, 마치 고통을 느끼는 듯했다."], ["In a neighbouring brake a finch was trying to sing; but the wind blew under his feathers till they stood on end, twisted round his little tail, and made him give up his song.", "이웃한 덤불에서 되새 한 마리가 노래하려 애쓰고 있었으나, 바람이 깃털 밑으로 불어 들어 깃이 곤두서고 작은 꽁지가 비틀리게 하더니 끝내 그 노래를 포기하게 만들었다."]], [["Yet a few yards to Yeobright’s left, on the open heath, how ineffectively gnashed the storm!", "그러나 예오브라이트의 왼편으로 몇 자 떨어진, 탁 트인 광야에서는 폭풍이 얼마나 무력하게 이를 갈았던가!"], ["Those gusts which tore the trees merely waved the furze and heather in a light caress.", "나무들을 찢어발기던 그 돌풍은 가시금작화와 히스를 그저 가벼운 애무로 흔들 뿐이었다."], ["Egdon was made for such times as these.", "에그던은 바로 이런 시절을 위해 지어진 곳이었다."]], [["Yeobright reached the empty house about midday.", "예오브라이트는 정오쯤 빈집에 이르렀다."], ["It was almost as lonely as that of Eustacia’s grandfather, but the fact that it stood near a heath was disguised by a belt of firs which almost enclosed the premises.", "그 집은 유스테이시아의 할아버지 집만큼이나 외로웠으나, 광야 가까이 서 있다는 사실은 그 부지를 거의 에워싼 전나무 띠에 가려져 있었다."], ["He journeyed on about a mile further to the village in which the owner lived, and, returning with him to the house, arrangements were completed, and the man undertook that one room at least should be ready for occupation the next day.", "그는 주인이 사는 마을까지 일 마일쯤 더 가서, 그와 함께 집으로 돌아와 합의를 매듭지었고, 그 사내는 적어도 방 하나는 이튿날 들어가 살 수 있도록 마련해두겠노라 약속했다."], ["Clym’s intention was to live there alone until Eustacia should join him on their wedding-day.", "클림의 뜻은 혼인날 유스테이시아가 합류할 때까지 그곳에서 혼자 사는 것이었다."]], [["Then he turned to pursue his way homeward through the drizzle that had so greatly transformed the scene.", "그러고 나서 그는 풍경을 그토록 크게 바꿔놓은 이슬비를 뚫고 집으로 돌아가는 길에 올랐다."], ["The ferns, among which he had lain in comfort yesterday, were dripping moisture from every frond, wetting his legs through as he brushed past; and the fur of the rabbits leaping before him was clotted into dark locks by the same watery surrounding.", "어제 편히 누워 있던 고사리들은 잎사귀마다 물기를 떨구고 있어, 그가 스쳐 지날 때 다리를 흠뻑 적셨고, 그 앞에서 깡충거리며 달아나는 토끼들의 털은 똑같은 물기 어린 둘레로 인해 거뭇한 가닥으로 엉겨 있었다."]], [["He reached home damp and weary enough after his ten-mile walk.", "십 마일을 걸은 끝에 그는 충분히 젖고 지친 채로 집에 이르렀다."], ["It had hardly been a propitious beginning, but he had chosen his course, and would show no swerving.", "그것은 결코 길조의 시작이라 할 수 없었으나, 그는 자기 길을 택했으니 한 치의 흔들림도 보이지 않으리라 다짐했다."], ["The evening and the following morning were spent in concluding arrangements for his departure.", "저녁과 이튿날 아침은 떠날 채비를 마무리하는 데 쓰였다."], ["To stay at home a minute longer than necessary after having once come to his determination would be, he felt, only to give new pain to his mother by some word, look, or deed.", "일단 결심에 이른 마당에 필요 이상으로 단 일 분이라도 집에 더 머무는 것은, 그가 느끼기에 어떤 말이나 표정이나 행동으로 어머니에게 새로운 고통을 안기는 일에 지나지 않을 터였다."]], [["He had hired a conveyance and sent off his goods by two o’clock that day.", "그는 운송 수단을 빌려 그날 두 시까지 짐을 부쳐 보냈다."], ["The next step was to get some furniture, which, after serving for temporary use in the cottage, would be available for the house at Budmouth when increased by goods of a better description.", "다음 차례는 가구 몇 점을 마련하는 일이었는데, 그것들은 오두막에서 임시로 쓰인 뒤 더 나은 종류의 물건들이 더해지면 부드머스의 집에서도 쓸 수 있을 터였다."], ["A mart extensive enough for the purpose existed at Anglebury, some miles beyond the spot chosen for his residence, and there he resolved to pass the coming night.", "그 목적에 충분히 넉넉한 시장이 그가 거처로 정한 곳에서 몇 마일 너머인 앵글베리에 있었으니, 그는 다가오는 밤을 그곳에서 보내기로 작정했다."]], [["It now only remained to wish his mother good-bye.", "이제 남은 것은 어머니에게 작별을 고하는 일뿐이었다."], ["She was sitting by the window as usual when he came downstairs.", "그가 아래층으로 내려왔을 때 그녀는 여느 때처럼 창가에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Mother, I am going to leave you,”", "\"어머니, 떠나려고 합니다.\""], ["he said, holding out his hand.", "그가 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“I thought you were, by your packing,” replied Mrs.", "\"네 짐 꾸리는 걸 보고 그러려는 줄 알았다.\""], ["Yeobright in a voice from which every particle of emotion was painfully excluded.", "예오브라이트 부인이, 감정의 입자가 하나도 남지 않도록 고통스럽게 배제된 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["“And you will part friends with me?”", "\"그래도 저와는 벗으로서 헤어져주시겠지요?\""]], [["“Certainly, Clym.”", "\"물론이지, 클림.\""]], [["“I am going to be married on the twenty-fifth.”", "\"이십오 일에 혼인할 겁니다.\""]], [["“I thought you were going to be married.”", "\"혼인하려는 줄 알고 있었다.\""]], [["“And then—and then you must come and see us.", "\"그러고 나서—그러고 나서 어머니가 오셔서 저희를 봐주셔야 해요."], ["You will understand me better after that, and our situation will not be so wretched as it is now.”", "그러고 나면 저를 더 잘 이해하시게 될 거고, 우리 사정도 지금처럼 비참하지는 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“I do not think it likely I shall come to see you.”", "\"내가 너를 보러 갈 것 같지는 않구나.\""]], [["“Then it will not be my fault or Eustacia’s, Mother.", "\"그러면 그건 제 잘못도, 유스테이시아의 잘못도 아닐 거예요, 어머니."], ["Good-bye!”", "안녕히 계세요!\""]], [["He kissed her cheek, and departed in great misery, which was several hours in lessening itself to a controllable level.", "그는 어머니의 뺨에 입맞추고는 큰 비참함을 안은 채 떠났으니, 그 비참함이 감당할 만한 수준으로 누그러지기까지는 여러 시간이 걸렸다."], ["The position had been such that nothing more could be said without, in the first place, breaking down a barrier; and that was not to be done.", "처지가 그러하여, 우선 하나의 장벽을 허물지 않고서는 더는 아무 말도 할 수 없었고, 그것은 할 수 없는 일이었다."]], [["No sooner had Yeobright gone from his mother’s house than her face changed its rigid aspect for one of blank despair.", "예오브라이트가 어머니의 집을 떠나기가 무섭게 그녀의 얼굴은 굳어 있던 기색을 거두고 막막한 절망의 얼굴로 바뀌었다."], ["After a while she wept, and her tears brought some relief.", "한참 뒤 그녀는 울었고, 눈물이 얼마간 위안을 가져다주었다."], ["During the rest of the day she did nothing but walk up and down the garden path in a state bordering on stupefaction.", "그날의 나머지 동안 그녀는 멍한 상태에 가까운 채로 정원 길을 오르내리기만 했다."], ["Night came, and with it but little rest.", "밤이 왔으나, 함께 온 안식은 거의 없었다."], ["The next day, with an instinct to do something which should reduce prostration to mournfulness, she went to her son’s room, and with her own hands arranged it in order, for an imaginary time when he should return again.", "이튿날, 무너진 기력을 슬픔 정도로나마 추슬러보려는 본능에 이끌려 그녀는 아들의 방으로 가서, 그가 다시 돌아올 어떤 가상의 때를 위해 제 손으로 방을 정돈했다."], ["She gave some attention to her flowers, but it was perfunctorily bestowed, for they no longer charmed her.", "그녀는 꽃들에 약간의 손길을 주었으나, 그것은 건성으로 베푼 것이었으니, 꽃들이 더는 그녀를 매혹하지 못했기 때문이다."]], [["It was a great relief when, early in the afternoon, Thomasin paid her an unexpected visit.", "그날 오후 이른 시각에 토머신이 뜻밖에 그녀를 찾아왔을 때 그것은 큰 위안이었다."], ["This was not the first meeting between the relatives since Thomasin’s marriage; and past blunders having been in a rough way rectified, they could always greet each other with pleasure and ease.", "이것은 토머신의 혼인 이후 두 친척 사이의 첫 만남은 아니었으니, 지난날의 실수들이 거칠게나마 바로잡혔던 터라 그들은 언제나 기쁨과 편안함으로 서로를 맞이할 수 있었다."]], [["The oblique band of sunlight which followed her through the door became the young wife well.", "그녀를 따라 문으로 들어온 비스듬한 햇살 띠는 그 젊은 아내에게 잘 어울렸다."], ["It illuminated her as her presence illuminated the heath.", "그것은 그녀의 존재가 광야를 밝히듯 그녀를 환히 비추었다."], ["In her movements, in her gaze, she reminded the beholder of the feathered creatures who lived around her home.", "그녀의 움직임에서, 그녀의 눈길에서, 그녀는 보는 이로 하여금 제 집 둘레에 사는 깃털 달린 생물들을 떠올리게 했다."], ["All similes and allegories concerning her began and ended with birds.", "그녀에 관한 모든 비유와 우의는 새로 시작해 새로 끝났다."], ["There was as much variety in her motions as in their flight.", "그녀의 움직임에는 새들의 비행만큼이나 다채로움이 있었다."], ["When she was musing she was a kestrel, which hangs in the air by an invisible motion of its wings.", "생각에 잠겼을 때 그녀는 보이지 않는 날갯짓으로 공중에 멈춰 있는 황조롱이였다."], ["When she was in a high wind her light body was blown against trees and banks like a heron’s.", "거센 바람 속에 있을 때면 그녀의 가벼운 몸은 왜가리처럼 나무와 둑에 떠밀렸다."], ["When she was frightened she darted noiselessly like a kingfisher.", "겁먹었을 때면 물총새처럼 소리 없이 쏜살같이 내달았다."], ["When she was serene she skimmed like a swallow, and that is how she was moving now.", "평온할 때면 제비처럼 미끄러지듯 날았으니, 지금 그녀가 움직이는 모습이 바로 그러했다."]], [["“You are looking very blithe, upon my word, Tamsie,”", "\"정말이지 무척 명랑해 보이는구나, 탬지.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright, with a sad smile.", "예오브라이트 부인이 슬픈 미소를 띠고 말했다."], ["“How is Damon?”", "\"데이먼은 어떻게 지내니?\""]], [["“He is very well.”", "\"아주 잘 지내요.\""]], [["“Is he kind to you, Thomasin?”", "\"네게 다정하게 대하니, 토머신?\""], ["And Mrs. Yeobright observed her narrowly.", "그러면서 예오브라이트 부인은 그녀를 유심히 살폈다."]], [["“Pretty fairly.”", "\"제법 괜찮게요.\""]], [["“Is that honestly said?”", "\"그게 솔직한 대답이냐?\""]], [["“Yes, Aunt.", "\"네, 이모."], ["I would tell you if he were unkind.”", "그이가 다정하지 않다면 이모께 말씀드릴 거예요.\""], ["She added, blushing, and with hesitation, “He—I don’t know if I ought to complain to you about this, but I am not quite sure what to do.", "그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며, 머뭇거리는 어조로 덧붙였다, \"그이가—이걸 이모께 하소연해도 되는 건지 모르겠지만, 어찌해야 할지 잘 모르겠어요."], ["I want some money, you know, Aunt—some to buy little things for myself—and he doesn’t give me any.", "저는 돈이 좀 필요한데, 그러니까, 이모, 제 자잘한 것들을 사려고요—그런데 그이가 한 푼도 주질 않아요."], ["I don’t like to ask him; and yet, perhaps, he doesn’t give it me because he doesn’t know.", "달라고 하기는 싫고, 그래도 어쩌면 그이가 안 주는 건 몰라서일지도 몰라요."], ["Ought I to mention it to him, Aunt?”", "제가 그이에게 말을 꺼내야 할까요, 이모?\""]], [["“Of course you ought.", "\"당연히 그래야지."], ["Have you never said a word on the matter?”", "그 일에 대해 한마디도 한 적이 없니?\""]], [["“You see, I had some of my own,”", "\"있잖아요, 제게 제 돈이 좀 있었거든요.\""], ["said Thomasin evasively, “and I have not wanted any of his until lately.", "토머신이 둘러대듯 말했다, \"그래서 최근까지는 그이의 돈이 필요하지 않았어요."], ["I did just say something about it last week; but he seems—not to remember.”", "지난주에 그 얘기를 살짝 꺼내기는 했는데, 그이는—기억하지 못하는 것 같아요.\""]], [["“He must be made to remember.", "\"기억하게 만들어야 한다."], ["You are aware that I have a little box full of spade-guineas, which your uncle put into my hands to divide between yourself and Clym whenever I chose.", "너도 알다시피 내게는 스페이드 기니가 가득 든 작은 상자가 하나 있는데, 네 큰아버지가 내 손에 맡기면서 내가 원하는 때에 너와 클림에게 나눠주라고 하셨다."], ["Perhaps the time has come when it should be done.", "어쩌면 그렇게 할 때가 온 모양이구나."], ["They can be turned into sovereigns at any moment.”", "그 돈은 언제든 소브린으로 바꿀 수 있다.\""]], [["“I think I should like to have my share—that is, if you don’t mind.”", "\"제 몫을 받고 싶어요—이모만 괜찮으시다면요.\""]], [["“You shall, if necessary.", "\"필요하다면 받아야지."], ["But it is only proper that you should first tell your husband distinctly that you are without any, and see what he will do.”", "하지만 우선 네 남편에게 네게 한 푼도 없다고 분명히 말하고 그가 어찌하는지 보는 것이 옳은 일이다.\""]], [["“Very well, I will....", "\"알겠어요, 그럴게요……"], ["Aunt, I have heard about Clym.", "이모, 저 클림 일을 들었어요."], ["I know you are in trouble about him, and that’s why I have come.”", "이모가 그 일로 괴로워하시는 걸 알아요, 그래서 온 거예요.\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright turned away, and her features worked in her attempt to conceal her feelings.", "예오브라이트 부인은 얼굴을 돌렸고, 감정을 감추려는 시도 속에 그녀의 이목구비가 일그러졌다."], ["Then she ceased to make any attempt, and said, weeping, “O Thomasin, do you think he hates me?", "그러더니 그녀는 더는 어떤 시도도 하지 않은 채, 울면서 말했다, \"오 토머신, 그 애가 나를 미워하는 걸까?"], ["How can he bear to grieve me so, when I have lived only for him through all these years?”", "이 모든 세월 동안 오로지 그 애만을 위해 살았는데, 어찌 그 애가 나를 이토록 슬프게 할 수 있단 말이냐?\""]], [["“Hate you—no,”", "\"미워하다니요—아니에요.\""], ["said Thomasin soothingly.", "토머신이 달래듯 말했다."], ["“It is only that he loves her too well.", "\"그저 그 사람을 너무 깊이 사랑하는 것뿐이에요."], ["Look at it quietly—do.", "차분히 보세요—그러세요."], ["It is not so very bad of him.", "그가 그렇게 아주 나쁜 건 아니에요."], ["Do you know, I thought it not the worst match he could have made.", "아세요, 저는 그가 맺을 수 있는 최악의 인연은 아니라고 생각했어요."], ["Miss Vye’s family is a good one on her mother’s side; and her father was a romantic wanderer—a sort of Greek Ulysses.”", "바이 양의 집안은 어머니 쪽으로는 좋은 가문이고, 그녀의 아버지는 낭만적인 방랑자였어요—일종의 그리스의 오디세우스 같은 사람이었지요.\""]], [["“It is no use, Thomasin; it is no use.", "\"소용없다, 토머신, 소용없어."], ["Your intention is good; but I will not trouble you to argue.", "네 뜻은 좋다만, 너를 수고롭게 따지게 하지는 않으마."], ["I have gone through the whole that can be said on either side times, and many times.", "양쪽에서 할 수 있는 말은 전부 몇 번이고, 또 몇 번이고 곱씹어보았다."], ["Clym and I have not parted in anger; we have parted in a worse way.", "클림과 나는 화가 나서 헤어진 게 아니다. 우리는 그보다 더 나쁜 식으로 헤어졌어."], ["It is not a passionate quarrel that would have broken my heart; it is the steady opposition and persistence in going wrong that he has shown.", "내 마음을 무너뜨렸을 건 격정적인 다툼이 아니라, 그 애가 보인 그 한결같은 반항과, 그릇된 길로 끝까지 밀고 나가는 고집이다."], ["O Thomasin, he was so good as a little boy—so tender and kind!”", "오 토머신, 그 애는 어린 사내아이일 때 그토록 착했단다—그토록 다정하고 상냥했어!\""]], [["“He was, I know.”", "\"그랬지요, 알아요.\""]], [["“I did not think one whom I called mine would grow up to treat me like this.", "\"내가 내 자식이라 부르던 아이가 자라서 나를 이렇게 대하게 될 줄은 생각도 못 했다."], ["He spoke to me as if I opposed him to injure him.", "마치 내가 자기를 해치려고 반대하는 것처럼 내게 말하더구나."], ["As though I could wish him ill!”", "내가 그 애가 잘못되기를 바라기라도 한다는 듯이!\""]], [["“There are worse women in the world than Eustacia Vye.”", "\"세상에는 유스테이시아 바이보다 더 나쁜 여자들도 있어요.\""]], [["“There are too many better that’s the agony of it.", "\"더 나은 여자들이 너무 많지—그게 바로 고통이다."], ["It was she, Thomasin, and she only, who led your husband to act as he did—I would swear it!”", "네 남편이 그렇게 행동하도록 이끈 건 그 애였다, 토머신, 오직 그 애뿐이었어—나는 맹세할 수 있다!\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["said Thomasin eagerly.", "토머신이 다급하게 말했다."], ["“It was before he knew me that he thought of her, and it was nothing but a mere flirtation.”", "\"그가 그녀를 마음에 둔 건 저를 알기도 전이었고, 그건 그저 한낱 가벼운 희롱에 지나지 않았어요.\""]], [["“Very well; we will let it be so.", "\"좋다, 그렇다고 해두자."], ["There is little use in unravelling that now.", "이제 와서 그걸 풀어헤쳐봐야 별 소용이 없지."], ["Sons must be blind if they will.", "아들들이란 자기가 그러고자 하면 눈멀기 마련이니까."], ["Why is it that a woman can see from a distance what a man cannot see close?", "어찌하여 여자는 멀리서도 사내가 코앞에서도 못 보는 걸 보는 걸까?"], ["Clym must do as he will—he is nothing more to me.", "클림은 제멋대로 하라지—그 애는 내게 더는 아무것도 아니다."], ["And this is maternity—to give one’s best years and best love to ensure the fate of being despised!”", "그리고 이것이 모성이라는 거다—최고의 세월과 최고의 사랑을 다 바쳐 멸시당할 운명을 보장받는 일 말이다!\""]], [["“You are too unyielding.", "\"이모는 너무 완고하세요."], ["Think how many mothers there are whose sons have brought them to public shame by real crimes before you feel so deeply a case like this.”", "이 정도 일을 그토록 깊이 느끼시기 전에, 진짜 범죄로 아들이 어머니를 세상의 수치 속에 빠뜨린 어머니들이 얼마나 많은지 생각해보세요.\""]], [["“Thomasin, don’t lecture me—I can’t have it.", "\"토머신, 내게 설교하지 마라—그건 견딜 수 없구나."], ["It is the excess above what we expect that makes the force of the blow, and that may not be greater in their case than in mine—they may have foreseen the worst....", "타격의 힘을 만드는 건 우리가 기대한 것을 넘어서는 그 초과분인데, 그 초과분이 그들의 경우라고 해서 내 경우보다 더 크지는 않을 게다—그들은 최악을 미리 내다보았을지도 모르니까……"], ["I am wrongly made, Thomasin,”", "나는 잘못 만들어진 사람이다, 토머신.\""], ["she added, with a mournful smile.", "그녀가 애통한 미소를 띠고 덧붙였다."], ["“Some widows can guard against the wounds their children give them by turning their hearts to another husband and beginning life again.", "\"어떤 과부들은 마음을 다른 남편에게로 돌려 삶을 다시 시작함으로써 자식이 안기는 상처를 막아낼 수 있지."], ["But I always was a poor, weak, one-idea’d creature—I had not the compass of heart nor the enterprise for that.", "하지만 나는 늘 가엾고 나약한, 한 가지 생각밖에 모르는 사람이었어—그럴 만한 마음의 그릇도, 그럴 만한 진취성도 없었다."], ["Just as forlorn and stupefied as I was when my husband’s spirit flew away I have sat ever since—never attempting to mend matters at all.", "남편의 혼이 날아가버렸을 때처럼 처량하고 멍한 채로 나는 그 후로 줄곧 주저앉아 있었지—사정을 고쳐보려는 시도조차 한 번 하지 않은 채로 말이다."], ["I was comparatively a young woman then, and I might have had another family by this time, and have been comforted by them for the failure of this one son.”", "그때 나는 비교적 젊은 여자였으니, 지금쯤이면 또 다른 가족을 두었을 수도 있었고, 이 한 아들의 실패에 대해 그들에게서 위로를 받았을 수도 있었을 거다.\""]], [["“It is more noble in you that you did not.”", "\"그러지 않으신 게 더 고결한 거예요.\""]], [["“The more noble, the less wise.”", "\"고결할수록, 어리석은 거지.\""]], [["“Forget it, and be soothed, dear Aunt.", "\"잊어버리시고 마음을 가라앉히세요, 이모."], ["And I shall not leave you alone for long.", "그리고 저는 이모를 오래 혼자 두지 않을 거예요."], ["I shall come and see you every day.”", "매일 와서 뵐게요.\""]], [["And for one week Thomasin literally fulfilled her word.", "그리고 한 주 동안 토머신은 말 그대로 약속을 지켰다."], ["She endeavoured to make light of the wedding; and brought news of the preparations, and that she was invited to be present.", "그녀는 혼사를 대수롭지 않게 넘기려 애썼고, 준비 상황에 관한 소식과, 자신이 참석하도록 초대받았다는 소식을 전해주었다."], ["The next week she was rather unwell, and did not appear.", "그다음 주에 그녀는 다소 몸이 안 좋아 모습을 보이지 않았다."], ["Nothing had as yet been done about the guineas, for Thomasin feared to address her husband again on the subject, and Mrs. Yeobright had insisted upon this.", "기니에 관해서는 아직 아무것도 진행된 것이 없었으니, 토머신이 그 문제로 다시 남편에게 말 꺼내기를 두려워했고, 예오브라이트 부인이 그렇게 하도록 고집했기 때문이었다."]], [["One day just before this time Wildeve was standing at the door of the Quiet Woman.", "이 무렵 직전의 어느 날 와일디브가 콰이엇 우먼의 문가에 서 있었다."], ["In addition to the upward path through the heath to Rainbarrow and Mistover, there was a road which branched from the highway a short distance below the inn, and ascended to Mistover by a circuitous and easy incline.", "광야를 가로질러 레인배로와 미스토버로 올라가는 길 말고도, 여관에서 조금 아래쪽 큰길에서 갈라져 나와 빙 둘러 완만하게 경사를 이루며 미스토버로 오르는 길이 하나 있었다."], ["This was the only route on that side for vehicles to the captain’s retreat.", "그쪽에서 선장의 은거지로 마차가 다닐 수 있는 길은 이것뿐이었다."], ["A light cart from the nearest town descended the road, and the lad who was driving pulled up in front of the inn for something to drink.", "가장 가까운 읍에서 온 가벼운 짐수레가 그 길을 내려왔고, 수레를 몰던 젊은이가 마실 것을 청하려 여관 앞에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“You come from Mistover?”", "\"미스토버에서 오는 길인가?\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["They are taking in good things up there.", "저 위에 좋은 물건들을 들이고 있던데요."], ["Going to be a wedding.”", "혼사가 있을 모양이에요.\""], ["And the driver buried his face in his mug.", "그러고는 마부가 머그잔에 얼굴을 파묻었다."]], [["Wildeve had not received an inkling of the fact before, and a sudden expression of pain overspread his face.", "와일디브는 그전까지 그 사실에 대해 한 톨의 낌새도 받지 못했던 터라, 별안간 고통의 표정이 그의 얼굴에 번졌다."], ["He turned for a moment into the passage to hide it.", "그는 그것을 감추려고 잠시 복도 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Then he came back again.", "그러고는 다시 돌아왔다."]], [["“Do you mean Miss Vye?”", "\"바이 양 말인가?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“How is it—that she can be married so soon?”", "\"어찌하여—그녀가 그렇게 빨리 혼인할 수 있단 말이지?\""]], [["“By the will of God and a ready young man, I suppose.”", "\"하느님의 뜻이고 채비된 젊은 사내가 있어서겠지요.\""]], [["“You don’t mean Mr. Yeobright?”", "\"예오브라이트 씨를 말하는 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"맞아요."], ["He has been creeping about with her all the spring.”", "그 사람이 봄내 그녀와 슬금슬금 만나고 다녔잖아요.\""]], [["“I suppose—she was immensely taken with him?”", "\"그러면—그녀가 그에게 단단히 빠졌단 말인가?\""]], [["“She is crazy about him, so their general servant of all work tells me.", "\"그녀가 그 사람한테 푹 빠졌다고, 그 댁 막일하는 하녀가 그러더라고요."], ["And that lad Charley that looks after the horse is all in a daze about it.", "그리고 말을 돌보는 그 찰리라는 녀석도 그 일로 얼이 다 빠져 있어요."], ["The stun-poll has got fond-like of her.”", "그 멍청이가 그녀한테 마음을 둔 거지요.\""]], [["“Is she lively—is she glad?", "\"그녀가 들떠 있던가—기뻐하던가?"], ["Going to be married so soon—well!”", "그렇게 빨리 혼인하다니—원!\""]], [["“It isn’t so very soon.”", "\"그렇게 아주 빠른 건 아니에요.\""]], [["“No; not so very soon.”", "\"그래, 그렇게 아주 빠른 건 아니지.\""]], [["Wildeve went indoors to the empty room, a curious heartache within him.", "와일디브는 묘한 가슴앓이를 품은 채 빈방으로 들어갔다."], ["He rested his elbow upon the mantelpiece and his face upon his hand.", "그는 팔꿈치를 벽난로 선반에 괴고 얼굴을 손에 묻었다."], ["When Thomasin entered the room he did not tell her of what he had heard.", "토머신이 방에 들어왔을 때 그는 자신이 들은 것을 그녀에게 말하지 않았다."], ["The old longing for Eustacia had reappeared in his soul—and it was mainly because he had discovered that it was another man’s intention to possess her.", "유스테이시아를 향한 옛 갈망이 그의 영혼 속에 되살아났으니—그것은 주로 다른 사내가 그녀를 차지하려 한다는 것을 알아챘기 때문이었다."]], [["To be yearning for the difficult, to be weary of that offered; to care for the remote, to dislike the near; it was Wildeve’s nature always.", "어려운 것을 갈망하고, 손에 주어진 것에는 싫증 내며, 멀리 있는 것에 마음 쓰고, 가까이 있는 것을 싫어하는 것—그것이 언제나 와일디브의 천성이었다."], ["This is the true mark of the man of sentiment.", "이것이야말로 감상가의 참된 표지다."], ["Though Wildeve’s fevered feeling had not been elaborated to real poetical compass, it was of the standard sort.", "비록 와일디브의 들뜬 감정이 진정한 시적 경지로까지 다듬어진 것은 아니었으나, 그것은 그 표준적인 부류에 속하는 것이었다."], ["His might have been called the Rousseau of Egdon.", "그를 두고 에그던의 루소라 불러도 좋았으리라."]], [["VII.", "VII."], ["The Morning and the Evening of a Day", "어느 하루의 아침과 저녁"]], [["The wedding morning came.", "혼인날 아침이 밝았다."], ["Nobody would have imagined from appearances that Blooms-End had any interest in Mistover that day.", "그날 블룸스엔드가 미스토버에 무슨 관심이라도 두고 있으리라고는 겉모습으로는 누구도 상상하지 못했을 것이다."], ["A solemn stillness prevailed around the house of Clym’s mother, and there was no more animation indoors.", "클림의 어머니 집 둘레에는 엄숙한 정적이 감돌았고, 집 안에도 더 이상의 생기는 없었다."], ["Mrs. Yeobright, who had declined to attend the ceremony, sat by the breakfast table in the old room which communicated immediately with the porch, her eyes listlessly directed towards the open door.", "예식 참석을 마다했던 예오브라이트 부인은, 현관과 곧장 통하는 옛 방의 아침상 곁에 앉아, 눈을 멍하니 열린 문 쪽으로 향하고 있었다."], ["It was the room in which, six months earlier, the merry Christmas party had met, to which Eustacia came secretly and as a stranger.", "그것은 여섯 달 전 흥겨운 크리스마스 모임이 열렸던 방이요, 유스테이시아가 남몰래 낯선 이로 가장하고 찾아왔던 그 방이었다."], ["The only living thing that entered now was a sparrow; and seeing no movements to cause alarm, he hopped boldly round the room, endeavoured to go out by the window, and fluttered among the pot-flowers.", "지금 그 방에 들어온 유일한 생물은 참새 한 마리였는데, 놀랄 만한 움직임이 없는 것을 보고는 대담하게 방 안을 통통 뛰어다니다가 창문으로 나가려 애쓰며 화분 꽃들 사이에서 파닥거렸다."], ["This roused the lonely sitter, who got up, released the bird, and went to the door.", "이것이 외로이 앉아 있던 이를 일깨웠으니, 그녀는 일어나 새를 놓아주고는 문가로 갔다."], ["She was expecting Thomasin, who had written the night before to state that the time had come when she would wish to have the money and that she would if possible call this day.", "그녀는 토머신을 기다리고 있었는데, 토머신이 전날 밤 편지를 보내, 이제 그 돈을 받고 싶은 때가 왔으며 될 수 있으면 오늘 들르겠다고 알려왔던 것이다."]], [["Yet Thomasin occupied Mrs. Yeobright’s thoughts but slightly as she looked up the valley of the heath, alive with butterflies, and with grasshoppers whose husky noises on every side formed a whispered chorus.", "그러나 토머신은, 나비들이 가득하고 사방에서 메뚜기들이 내는 쉰 듯한 소리가 속삭이는 합창을 이루는 광야의 골짜기를 올려다보는 동안, 예오브라이트 부인의 마음을 조금밖에 차지하지 못했다."], ["A domestic drama, for which the preparations were now being made a mile or two off, was but little less vividly present to her eyes than if enacted before her.", "일이 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에서 지금 준비가 진행되고 있는 한 편의 가정사가, 마치 그녀의 눈앞에서 펼쳐지는 것이나 다름없을 만큼 생생하게 그녀의 눈에 어렸다."], ["She tried to dismiss the vision, and walked about the garden plot; but her eyes ever and anon sought out the direction of the parish church to which Mistover belonged, and her excited fancy clove the hills which divided the building from her eyes.", "그녀는 그 환영을 떨쳐내려 애쓰며 정원 뙈기를 거닐었으나, 그 눈은 이따금 미스토버가 속한 교구 교회 쪽을 자꾸만 찾았고, 흥분한 그녀의 상상은 그 건물을 그녀의 눈에서 갈라놓고 있는 언덕들을 가르고 들어갔다."], ["The morning wore away.", "아침이 흘러갔다."], ["Eleven o’clock struck—could it be that the wedding was then in progress? It must be so.", "열한 시를 알리는 종이 울렸다—그렇다면 혼례가 그때 진행되고 있다는 것인가?"], ["She went on imagining the scene at the church, which he had by this time approached with his bride.", "분명 그러할 터였다. 그녀는 이 무렵이면 그가 신부를 데리고 다다랐을 교회의 광경을 계속 떠올렸다."], ["She pictured the little group of children by the gate as the pony carriage drove up in which, as Thomasin had learnt, they were going to perform the short journey.", "토머신에게서 들었듯이 그 짧은 여정을 치르기 위해 타고 갈 조랑말 마차가 다가올 때 문가에 모여 있을 자그마한 아이들 무리를 그녀는 그려보았다."], ["Then she saw them enter and proceed to the chancel and kneel; and the service seemed to go on.", "그러고는 그들이 들어와 성단소로 나아가 무릎 꿇는 것을 보았고, 예식이 진행되는 듯했다."]], [["She covered her face with her hands.", "그녀는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다."], ["“O, it is a mistake!”", "\"오, 이건 잘못된 거야!\""], ["she groaned.", "그녀가 신음했다."], ["“And he will rue it some day, and think of me!”", "\"그리고 그 애는 언젠가 이를 뉘우치며 나를 떠올릴 거야!\""]], [["While she remained thus, overcome by her forebodings, the old clock indoors whizzed forth twelve strokes.", "그녀가 그렇게 불길한 예감에 사로잡힌 채 머물러 있는 동안, 집 안의 낡은 시계가 윙 소리를 내며 열두 번을 쳤다."], ["Soon after, faint sounds floated to her ear from afar over the hills.", "곧이어 언덕 너머 먼 곳에서 희미한 소리들이 그녀의 귀로 흘러왔다."], ["The breeze came from that quarter, and it had brought with it the notes of distant bells, gaily starting off in a peal: one, two, three, four, five.", "산들바람이 그쪽에서 불어와, 멀리서 한바탕 흥겹게 울려 퍼지기 시작한 종소리를 실어 왔다. 하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯."], ["The ringers at East Egdon were announcing the nuptials of Eustacia and her son.", "이스트 에그던의 종지기들이 유스테이시아와 그녀의 아들의 혼례를 알리고 있었다."]], [["“Then it is over,”", "\"그러면 끝난 거구나.\""], ["she murmured.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Well, well!", "\"그래, 그래!"], ["and life too will be over soon.", "삶도 곧 끝나겠지."], ["And why should I go on scalding my face like this?", "그런데 어찌하여 나는 이렇게 계속 얼굴이 따갑도록 울고 있는 걸까?"], ["Cry about one thing in life, cry about all; one thread runs through the whole piece.", "삶의 한 가지 일로 울면, 모든 일로 우는 셈이지. 한 가닥 실이 천 전체를 꿰뚫고 흐르니까."], ["And yet we say, ‘a time to laugh!’”", "그런데도 우리는 '웃을 때가 있다'고 말하지!\""]], [["Towards evening Wildeve came.", "저녁 무렵 와일디브가 왔다."], ["Since Thomasin’s marriage Mrs. Yeobright had shown him that grim friendliness which at last arises in all such cases of undesired affinity.", "토머신의 혼인 이후 예오브라이트 부인은 그에게, 원치 않는 인척 관계의 이런 모든 경우에 끝내 생겨나는 그 무뚝뚝한 우호를 보여왔다."], ["The vision of what ought to have been is thrown aside in sheer weariness, and browbeaten human endeavour listlessly makes the best of the fact that is.", "마땅히 그러했어야 할 것에 대한 환상은 순전한 지침 속에 내던져지고, 기죽은 인간의 노력은 무기력하게 있는 그대로의 사실을 최선으로 받아들인다."], ["Wildeve, to do him justice, had behaved very courteously to his wife’s aunt; and it was with no surprise that she saw him enter now.", "와일디브는, 공평하게 말하자면, 아내의 이모에게 매우 정중하게 처신해왔으니, 그녀가 지금 그가 들어서는 것을 보고도 놀라지 않은 것은 그래서였다."]], [["“Thomasin has not been able to come, as she promised to do,”", "\"토머신이 약속한 대로 올 수 없게 됐습니다.\""], ["he replied to her inquiry, which had been anxious, for she knew that her niece was badly in want of money.", "그녀의 물음에 그가 대답했는데, 그 물음은 근심에 찬 것이었으니, 그녀는 조카가 돈이 몹시 궁하다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["“The captain came down last night and personally pressed her to join them today.", "\"선장이 어젯밤 내려와서 직접 오늘 함께하자고 그녀를 졸랐어요."], ["So, not to be unpleasant, she determined to go.", "그래서 모나게 굴지 않으려고 그녀가 가기로 작정했지요."], ["They fetched her in the pony-chaise, and are going to bring her back.”", "조랑말 마차로 그녀를 데려갔고, 다시 데려다줄 겁니다.\""]], [["“Then it is done,”", "\"그러면 일이 다 끝났군요.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "예오브라이트 부인이 말했다."], ["“Have they gone to their new home?”", "\"그들은 새 보금자리로 갔나요?\""]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"모르겠습니다."], ["I have had no news from Mistover since Thomasin left to go.”", "토머신이 떠난 뒤로 미스토버에서는 아무 소식도 받지 못했어요.\""]], [["“You did not go with her?”", "\"자네는 그 애와 함께 가지 않았나요?\""], ["said she, as if there might be good reasons why.", "그녀가, 마치 그럴 만한 까닭이라도 있다는 듯이 말했다."]], [["“I could not,”", "\"갈 수 없었어요.\""], ["said Wildeve, reddening slightly.", "와일디브가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“We could not both leave the house; it was rather a busy morning, on account of Anglebury Great Market.", "\"우리 둘 다 집을 비울 수는 없었지요. 앵글베리 큰 장날이라 꽤 분주한 아침이었거든요."], ["I believe you have something to give to Thomasin?", "토머신에게 주실 게 있다고 들었는데요?"], ["If you like, I will take it.”", "괜찮으시면 제가 가져가겠습니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright hesitated, and wondered if Wildeve knew what the something was.", "예오브라이트 부인은 망설이며, 와일디브가 그 무언가가 무엇인지 알고 있는지 의아해했다."], ["“Did she tell you of this?”", "\"그 애가 자네에게 이 얘길 했나요?\""], ["she inquired.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Not particularly.", "\"딱히 그런 건 아닙니다."], ["She casually dropped a remark about having arranged to fetch some article or other.”", "무슨 물건인가를 가져오기로 했다고 지나가는 말로 한마디 흘리더군요.\""]], [["“It is hardly necessary to send it.", "\"굳이 보낼 필요는 없어요."], ["She can have it whenever she chooses to come.”", "그 애가 오고 싶을 때 언제든 가져갈 수 있으니까요.\""]], [["“That won’t be yet.", "\"그건 당분간 안 될 겁니다."], ["In the present state of her health she must not go on walking so much as she has done.”", "지금 그 사람 건강 상태로는 그동안 해온 것처럼 그렇게 많이 걸어 다녀선 안 되거든요.\""], ["He added, with a faint twang of sarcasm, “What wonderful thing is it that I cannot be trusted to take?”", "그가, 그에게 흔한 희미한 빈정거림의 가락을 띠고 덧붙였다. \"제가 미덥지 못해서 맡길 수 없는 그 대단한 물건이란 게 뭡니까?\""]], [["“Nothing worth troubling you with.”", "\"자네를 수고롭게 할 만한 건 아니에요.\""]], [["“One would think you doubted my honesty,”", "\"제 정직함을 의심하시는 것 같군요.\""], ["he said, with a laugh, though his colour rose in a quick resentfulness frequent with him.", "그가 웃으며 말했으나, 그에게 잦은 빠른 분개로 얼굴빛이 달아올랐다."]], [["“You need think no such thing,” said she drily.", "\"그런 생각은 할 필요 없어요.\""], ["“It is simply that I, in common with the rest of the world, feel that there are certain things which had better be done by certain people than by others.”", "그녀가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"그저 나도 세상 사람들과 마찬가지로, 어떤 일들은 다른 이보다 특정한 이가 하는 편이 낫다고 느끼는 것뿐이에요.\""]], [["“As you like, as you like,”", "\"좋으실 대로, 좋으실 대로 하세요.\""], ["said Wildeve laconically.", "와일디브가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."], ["“It is not worth arguing about.", "\"다툴 만한 일도 아니지요."], ["Well, I think I must turn homeward again, as the inn must not be left long in charge of the lad and the maid only.”", "음, 이제 그만 집으로 돌아가야겠습니다, 여관을 머슴애와 하녀에게만 오래 맡겨둘 수는 없으니까요.\""]], [["He went his way, his farewell being scarcely so courteous as his greeting.", "그는 제 갈 길을 갔으니, 그 작별은 인사 때만큼 정중하지도 않았다."], ["But Mrs. Yeobright knew him thoroughly by this time, and took little notice of his manner, good or bad.", "그러나 예오브라이트 부인은 이 무렵이면 그를 속속들이 알고 있었으므로, 좋든 나쁘든 그의 태도에 거의 개의치 않았다."]], [["When Wildeve was gone Mrs. Yeobright stood and considered what would be the best course to adopt with regard to the guineas, which she had not liked to entrust to Wildeve.", "와일디브가 떠나자 예오브라이트 부인은 서서, 와일디브에게 맡기고 싶지 않았던 그 기니에 관해 어떤 길을 택하는 것이 가장 좋을지 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["It was hardly credible that Thomasin had told him to ask for them, when the necessity for them had arisen from the difficulty of obtaining money at his hands.", "토머신이 그에게 그것을 청해 오라고 시켰으리라고는 좀처럼 믿기 어려웠으니, 애초에 그것이 필요해진 까닭이 바로 그의 손에서 돈을 얻어내기 어려웠기 때문이었다."], ["At the same time Thomasin really wanted them, and might be unable to come to Blooms-End for another week at least.", "동시에 토머신은 정말로 그것이 필요했고, 적어도 또 한 주는 블룸스엔드에 오지 못할지도 몰랐다."], ["To take or send the money to her at the inn would be impolite, since Wildeve would pretty surely be present, or would discover the transaction; and if, as her aunt suspected, he treated her less kindly than she deserved to be treated, he might then get the whole sum out of her gentle hands.", "여관으로 돈을 가져가거나 보내는 것은 사려 깊지 못한 일일 터였으니, 와일디브가 거의 틀림없이 자리에 있거나 그 거래를 알아챌 것이고, 만약 그녀의 이모가 의심하는 대로 그가 그녀를 받아 마땅한 만큼 다정하게 대하지 않는다면, 그는 그녀의 온순한 손에서 그 전액을 빼앗아 갈지도 몰랐다."], ["But on this particular evening Thomasin was at Mistover, and anything might be conveyed to her there without the knowledge of her husband.", "그러나 바로 이날 저녁 토머신은 미스토버에 있었으니, 그곳에서라면 남편 모르게 무엇이든 그녀에게 전할 수 있었다."], ["Upon the whole the opportunity was worth taking advantage of.", "대체로 이 기회는 활용할 만한 가치가 있었다."]], [["Her son, too, was there, and was now married.", "게다가 그녀의 아들도 거기 있었고, 이제 혼인한 몸이었다."], ["There could be no more proper moment to render him his share of the money than the present.", "그에게 그의 몫의 돈을 건넬 만한 때로 지금보다 더 적절한 순간은 없을 터였다."], ["And the chance that would be afforded her, by sending him this gift, of showing how far she was from bearing him ill-will, cheered the sad mother’s heart.", "그리고 그에게 이 선물을 보냄으로써 자신이 그에게 악감정을 품기는커녕 얼마나 그러지 않는지를 보여줄 기회가 주어진다는 것이 그 슬픈 어머니의 마음에 위안을 주었다."]], [["She went upstairs and took from a locked drawer a little box, out of which she poured a hoard of broad unworn guineas that had lain there many a year.", "그녀는 위층으로 올라가 잠긴 서랍에서 작은 상자를 꺼냈고, 거기서 여러 해 동안 거기 놓여 있던, 넓적하고 한 번도 닳지 않은 기니 무더기를 쏟아냈다."], ["There were a hundred in all, and she divided them into two heaps, fifty in each.", "모두 백 개였고, 그녀는 그것을 두 무더기로, 각각 쉰 개씩 나누었다."], ["Tying up these in small canvas bags, she went down to the garden and called to Christian Cantle, who was loitering about in hope of a supper which was not really owed him.", "이것들을 작은 캔버스 자루에 묶고 나서 그녀는 정원으로 내려가, 정작 받을 자격도 없는 저녁밥을 바라며 어슬렁거리고 있던 크리스천 캔틀을 불렀다."], ["Mrs. Yeobright gave him the moneybags, charged him to go to Mistover, and on no account to deliver them into any one’s hands save her son’s and Thomasin’s.", "예오브라이트 부인은 그에게 돈 자루를 건네며, 미스토버로 가서 어떤 경우에도 그것을 자기 아들과 토머신 말고는 누구의 손에도 넘기지 말라고 단단히 일렀다."], ["On further thought she deemed it advisable to tell Christian precisely what the two bags contained, that he might be fully impressed with their importance.", "더 생각한 끝에 그녀는, 그가 그 중요성을 충분히 새기도록 두 자루에 무엇이 들었는지를 크리스천에게 정확히 일러두는 것이 좋겠다고 판단했다."], ["Christian pocketed the moneybags, promised the greatest carefulness, and set out on his way.", "크리스천은 돈 자루를 주머니에 넣고 더없이 조심하겠노라 약속한 뒤 길을 나섰다."]], [["“You need not hurry,”", "\"서두를 것 없다.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "예오브라이트 부인이 말했다."], ["“It will be better not to get there till after dusk, and then nobody will notice you.", "\"해가 진 뒤에 도착하는 편이 나을 게다, 그러면 아무도 너를 눈여겨보지 않을 테니."], ["Come back here to supper, if it is not too late.”", "너무 늦지 않거든 여기로 돌아와 저녁을 먹으렴.\""]], [["It was nearly nine o’clock when he began to ascend the vale towards Mistover; but the long days of summer being at their climax, the first obscurity of evening had only just begun to tan the landscape.", "그가 미스토버 쪽 골짜기를 오르기 시작한 것은 거의 아홉 시 무렵이었으나, 여름의 긴 낮이 절정에 이른 터라 저녁의 첫 어스름이 이제 막 풍경을 거뭇하게 그을리기 시작했을 뿐이었다."], ["At this point of his journey Christian heard voices, and found that they proceeded from a company of men and women who were traversing a hollow ahead of him, the tops only of their heads being visible.", "여정의 이 지점에서 크리스천은 사람들 목소리를 들었고, 그것이 자기 앞쪽의 우묵한 곳을 가로질러 가는 한 무리의 남녀에게서 나는 것임을 알았으니, 그들의 머리 꼭대기만이 보일 뿐이었다."]], [["He paused and thought of the money he carried.", "그는 멈춰 서서 자기가 지닌 돈을 떠올렸다."], ["It was almost too early even for Christian seriously to fear robbery; nevertheless he took a precaution which ever since his boyhood he had adopted whenever he carried more than two or three shillings upon his person—a precaution somewhat like that of the owner of the Pitt Diamond when filled with similar misgivings.", "크리스천이라 해도 강도질을 진지하게 두려워하기에는 거의 너무 이른 때였으나, 그럼에도 그는 소년 시절 이래로 두세 실링 이상을 몸에 지닐 때마다 채택해온 한 가지 예방책을 취했으니—그것은 똑같은 우려에 사로잡힌 피트 다이아몬드 주인의 것과 다소 비슷한 예방책이었다."], ["He took off his boots, untied the guineas, and emptied the contents of one little bag into the right boot, and of the other into the left, spreading them as flatly as possible over the bottom of each, which was really a spacious coffer by no means limited to the size of the foot.", "그는 부츠를 벗고 기니 자루를 풀어, 한쪽 작은 자루의 내용물을 오른쪽 부츠에, 다른 쪽 것을 왼쪽 부츠에 쏟아붓고는, 결코 발 크기에 한정되지 않은 실로 널찍한 함이라 할 만한 각 부츠의 바닥에 될 수 있는 한 평평하게 펼쳤다."], ["Pulling them on again and lacing them to the very top, he proceeded on his way, more easy in his head than under his soles.", "다시 부츠를 신고 맨 위까지 끈을 졸라맨 뒤, 그는 발바닥 밑보다는 머릿속이 더 편안해진 채로 제 갈 길을 나아갔다."]], [["His path converged towards that of the noisy company, and on coming nearer he found to his relief that they were several Egdon people whom he knew very well, while with them walked Fairway, of Blooms-End.", "그의 길은 그 시끌벅적한 무리의 길과 차츰 가까워졌고, 더 가까이 다가가면서 그는 안도하게도 그들이 자신이 아주 잘 아는 에그던 사람들 여럿이며, 그들과 함께 블룸스엔드의 페어웨이가 걷고 있음을 알았다."]], [["“What!", "\"아니!"], ["Christian going too?”", "크리스천도 가는 길인가?\""], ["said Fairway as soon as he recognized the newcomer.", "페어웨이가 새로 온 이를 알아보자마자 말했다."], ["“You’ve got no young woman nor wife to your name to gie a gown-piece to, I’m sure.”", "\"자네야 가운 감을 사줄 처녀도, 마누라도 없을 게 분명한데.\""]], [["“What d’ye mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 말이오?\""], ["said Christian.", "크리스천이 말했다."]], [["“Why, the raffle.", "\"아니, 추첨 말이야."], ["The one we go to every year.", "우리가 해마다 가는 그거."], ["Going to the raffle as well as ourselves?”", "우리처럼 추첨하러 가는 거 아닌가?\""]], [["“Never knew a word o’t.", "\"그런 건 한 마디도 들은 적 없소."], ["Is it like cudgel playing or other sportful forms of bloodshed?", "그게 몽둥이 시합이나, 뭐 피 보는 다른 놀이 같은 거요?"], ["I don’t want to go, thank you, Mister Fairway, and no offence.”", "가고 싶지 않소, 고맙소만, 페어웨이 씨, 기분 상하진 마시오.\""]], [["“Christian don’t know the fun o’t, and ’twould be a fine sight for him,”", "\"크리스천은 그게 무슨 재미인지 모르니, 저 친구한테는 좋은 구경거리겠네.\""], ["said a buxom woman.", "한 통통한 여인이 말했다."], ["“There’s no danger at all, Christian.", "\"위험이라곤 전혀 없어, 크리스천."], ["Every man puts in a shilling apiece, and one wins a gown-piece for his wife or sweetheart if he’s got one.”", "다들 한 사람당 일 실링씩 내고, 마누라나 정인이 있으면 그중 하나가 그이한테 줄 가운 감을 따는 거지.\""]], [["“Well, as that’s not my fortune there’s no meaning in it to me.", "\"음, 그건 내 팔자가 아니니 내겐 아무 의미도 없소."], ["But I should like to see the fun, if there’s nothing of the black art in it, and if a man may look on without cost or getting into any dangerous wrangle?”", "하지만 거기에 흑마술 같은 건 없고, 돈 안 들이고 위험한 시비에 휘말리지 않고 구경만 할 수 있다면, 그 재미는 보고 싶긴 하오.\""]], [["“There will be no uproar at all,”", "\"소란이라곤 전혀 없을 거야.\""], ["said Timothy.", "티머시가 말했다."], ["“Sure, Christian, if you’d like to come we’ll see there’s no harm done.”", "\"정말이지, 크리스천, 자네가 오고 싶다면 우리가 아무 일도 안 생기게 봐줌세.\""]], [["“And no ba’dy gaieties, I suppose?", "\"그리고 음탕한 흥청거림 같은 것도 없겠지요?"], ["You see, neighbours, if so, it would be setting father a bad example, as he is so light moral’d. But a gown-piece for a shilling, and no black art—’tis worth looking in to see, and it wouldn’t hinder me half an hour.", "보시오, 이웃들, 만약 그런 게 있다면 아버지께 나쁜 본을 보이는 셈이 될 거요, 그 어른이 워낙 마음이 가벼우신 분이니 말이오."], ["Yes, I’ll come, if you’ll step a little way towards Mistover with me afterwards, supposing night should have closed in, and nobody else is going that way?”", "하지만 일 실링에 가운 감이고, 흑마술도 없다면—들여다볼 만한 가치가 있고, 반시간도 안 걸릴 테지."], ["One or two promised; and Christian, diverging from his direct path, turned round to the right with his companions towards the Quiet Woman.", "그래, 가겠소, 끝나고 밤이 깊었는데 그쪽으로 가는 사람이 달리 없다면, 미스토버 쪽으로 조금만 데려다준다면 말이오.\""]], [["When they entered the large common room of the inn they found assembled there about ten men from among the neighbouring population, and the group was increased by the new contingent to double that number.", "한둘이 약속했고, 크리스천은 곧장 가던 길에서 벗어나 일행과 함께 오른쪽으로 돌아 콰이엇 우먼 쪽으로 향했다. 그들이 여관의 널따란 공동방에 들어섰을 때 그곳에는 인근 주민들 가운데 열 명쯤이 모여 있었고, 새로 합류한 무리로 그 수는 두 배로 불어났다."], ["Most of them were sitting round the room in seats divided by wooden elbows like those of crude cathedral stalls, which were carved with the initials of many an illustrious drunkard of former times who had passed his days and his nights between them, and now lay as an alcoholic cinder in the nearest churchyard.", "그들 대부분은, 거친 성당 좌석 같은 나무 팔걸이로 칸막이가 된 자리에 빙 둘러앉아 있었는데, 그 자리에는 지난 시절 한자리에서 낮과 밤을 보내다가 이제는 알코올에 절은 한 줌의 재가 되어 가장 가까운 묘지에 누워 있는 수많은 이름난 술꾼들의 이름 머리글자가 새겨져 있었다."], ["Among the cups on the long table before the sitters lay an open parcel of light drapery—the gown-piece, as it was called—which was to be raffled for.", "긴 탁자 위 술잔들 사이에는\n도박에 참가한 이들 앞에는 얇은 천이 든 꾸러미가 펼쳐져 있었으니—이른바 옷감이라 불리는 것으로—제비뽑기의 상품이었다."], ["Wildeve was standing with his back to the fireplace smoking a cigar; and the promoter of the raffle, a packman from a distant town, was expatiating upon the value of the fabric as material for a summer dress.", "와일디브는 벽난로를 등진 채 서서 시가를 피우고 있었고, 먼 고장에서 온 행상인이자 이 제비뽑기를 주관하는 사내는 여름 드레스 옷감으로 이만한 게 없다며 그 천의 값어치를 늘어놓고 있었다."]], [["“Now, gentlemen,”", "\"자, 신사 여러분.\""], ["he continued, as the newcomers drew up to the table, “there’s five have entered, and we want four more to make up the number.", "새로 들어온 이들이 탁자로 다가서자 그가 말을 이었다. \"다섯 분이 들어오셨고, 수를 채우려면 네 분이 더 있어야 합니다."], ["I think, by the faces of those gentlemen who have just come in, that they are shrewd enough to take advantage of this rare opportunity of beautifying their ladies at a very trifling expense.”", "방금 들어오신 신사분들의 얼굴을 보아하니, 푼돈으로 부인을 단장시킬 이 흔치 않은 기회를 놓치지 않으실 만큼 영리한 분들이로군요.\""]], [["Fairway, Sam, and another placed their shillings on the table, and the man turned to Christian.", "페어웨이와 샘, 그리고 또 한 사람이 탁자 위에 1실링씩을 올려놓았고, 사내는 크리스천을 향했다."]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"아닙니다, 나리.\""], ["said Christian, drawing back, with a quick gaze of misgiving.", "크리스천이 의심스러운 눈빛으로 얼른 훑어보며 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["“I am only a poor chap come to look on, an it please ye, sir.", "\"저는 그저 구경이나 하러 온 가난뱅이일 뿐입니다요, 괜찮으시다면요, 나리."], ["I don’t so much as know how you do it.", "어떻게 하는 건지조차 모릅니다요."], ["If so be I was sure of getting it I would put down the shilling; but I couldn’t otherwise.”", "만일 따게 될 게 확실하다면야 1실링을 내겠지만, 그렇지 않고서야 못합니다요.\""]], [["“I think you might almost be sure,”", "\"거의 확실하다고 봐도 될 겁니다.\""], ["said the pedlar.", "행상인이 말했다."], ["“In fact, now I look into your face, even if I can’t say you are sure to win, I can say that I never saw anything look more like winning in my life.”", "\"실은, 이렇게 얼굴을 들여다보니, 따리라고 장담할 순 없어도, 평생 이만큼 딸 것처럼 생긴 얼굴은 본 적이 없다고는 말할 수 있겠습니다.\""]], [["“You’ll anyhow have the same chance as the rest of us,”", "\"어쨌든 우리 나머지하고 똑같은 가망은 있는 거잖나.\""], ["said Sam.", "샘이 말했다."]], [["“And the extra luck of being the last comer,”", "\"게다가 맨 마지막에 온 운까지 더해지고 말이지.\""], ["said another.", "또 한 사람이 말했다."]], [["“And I was born wi’ a caul, and perhaps can be no more ruined than drowned?”", "\"게다가 난 양막을 쓰고 태어났으니, 물에 빠져 죽는 것 말고는 망할 일이 없는지도 모르지?\""], ["Christian added, beginning to give way.", "크리스천이 마음이 흔들리기 시작하며 덧붙였다."]], [["Ultimately Christian laid down his shilling, the raffle began, and the dice went round.", "마침내 크리스천은 1실링을 내려놓았고, 제비뽑기가 시작되어 주사위가 차례로 돌았다."], ["When it came to Christian’s turn he took the box with a trembling hand, shook it fearfully, and threw a pair-royal.", "크리스천의 차례가 오자 그는 떨리는 손으로 주사위 통을 들어 겁먹은 듯 흔들고는 트리플을 던졌다."], ["Three of the others had thrown common low pairs, and all the rest mere points.", "다른 셋은 그저 그런 낮은 페어를 던졌고, 나머지는 죄다 끗수뿐이었다."]], [["“The gentleman looked like winning, as I said,”", "\"제가 말씀드린 대로 이 신사분이 딸 상이었지요.\""], ["observed the chapman blandly.", "행상인이 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“Take it, sir; the article is yours.”", "\"가져가십시오, 나리. 그 물건은 당신 것입니다.\""]], [["“Haw-haw-haw!”", "\"하―하―하!\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“I’m damned if this isn’t the quarest start that ever I knowed!”", "\"이렇게 별난 일은 내 살다 살다 처음 보겠구먼!\""]], [["“Mine?”", "\"제 거라고요?\""], ["asked Christian, with a vacant stare from his target eyes.", "크리스천이 과녁 같은 눈으로 멍하니 바라보며 물었다."], ["“I—I haven’t got neither maid, wife, nor widder belonging to me at all, and I’m afeard it will make me laughed at to ha’e it, Master Traveller.", "\"저―저는 처녀든 아내든 과부든 제게 딸린 여자라곤 하나도 없는데요, 이걸 가지면 사람들한테 웃음거리가 될까 봐 무섭습니다요, 행상인 나리."], ["What with being curious to join in I never thought of that!", "끼고 싶은 마음에 그만 그 생각을 못 했습니다요!"], ["What shall I do wi’ a woman’s clothes in _my_ bedroom, and not lose my decency!”", "제 침실에 여자 옷을 두고서 어떻게 체면을 잃지 않겠습니까요!\""]], [["“Keep ’em, to be sure,”", "\"가지고 있게, 그럼.\""], ["said Fairway, “if it is only for luck.", "페어웨이가 말했다. \"운이라 치고서라도 말이지."], ["Perhaps ’twill tempt some woman that thy poor carcase had no power over when standing empty-handed.”", "자네 그 가련한 몰골이 맨손으로 서 있을 땐 꼬드기지 못했던 여자라도, 어쩌면 이걸로 꾀어낼지 누가 알겠나.\""]], [["“Keep it, certainly,”", "\"가지게, 당연히.\""], ["said Wildeve, who had idly watched the scene from a distance.", "멀찍이서 그 장면을 무심히 지켜보던 와일디브가 말했다."]], [["The table was then cleared of the articles, and the men began to drink.", "이윽고 탁자 위의 물건들이 치워지고, 사내들은 술을 마시기 시작했다."]], [["“Well, to be sure!”", "\"이런, 정말이지!\""], ["said Christian, half to himself.", "크리스천이 반쯤 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."], ["“To think I should have been born so lucky as this, and not have found it out until now!", "\"내가 이렇게 운 좋게 태어났는데 이제껏 그걸 몰랐다니!"], ["What curious creatures these dice be—powerful rulers of us all, and yet at my command!", "이 주사위라는 게 어찌나 묘한 물건인지―우리 모두를 쥐고 흔드는 막강한 지배자인데도, 내 명령을 따르다니!"], ["I am sure I never need be afeared of anything after this.”", "이젠 정말이지 무서울 게 하나도 없겠다.\""], ["He handled the dice fondly one by one.", "그는 주사위를 하나씩 애지중지 만지작거렸다."], ["“Why, sir,”", "\"그런데, 나리.\""], ["he said in a confidential whisper to Wildeve, who was near his left hand, “if I could only use this power that’s in me of multiplying money I might do some good to a near relation of yours, seeing what I’ve got about me of hers—eh?”", "그가 왼쪽 가까이에 있는 와일디브에게 은밀히 속삭였다. \"내 안에 깃든 이 돈 불리는 능력을 쓸 수만 있다면, 나리하고 가까운 친척분께 좋은 일을 해드릴 수도 있을 텐데요, 제가 그분 것을 몸에 지니고 있는 걸 생각하면 말입니다요―예?\""], ["He tapped one of his money-laden boots upon the floor.", "그는 돈이 가득 든 부츠 한 짝으로 바닥을 톡톡 쳤다."]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 소린가?\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 물었다."]], [["“That’s a secret.", "\"그건 비밀입죠."], ["Well, I must be going now.”", "자, 이제 가봐야겠습니다요.\""], ["He looked anxiously towards Fairway.", "그는 불안한 듯 페어웨이 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["“Where are you going?”", "\"어디로 가는가?\""], ["Wildeve asked.", "와일디브가 물었다."]], [["“To Mistover Knap.", "\"미스토버 냅으로요."], ["I have to see Mrs. Thomasin there—that’s all.”", "거기서 토머신 부인을 뵈어야 합니다요―그게 다입니다요.\""]], [["“I am going there, too, to fetch Mrs. Wildeve.", "\"나도 그리로 가는 길이네, 와일디브 부인을 데리러."], ["We can walk together.”", "같이 걸어가지.\""]], [["Wildeve became lost in thought, and a look of inward illumination came into his eyes.", "와일디브는 깊은 생각에 잠겼고, 그의 눈에 안에서 무언가 밝아지는 빛이 어렸다."], ["It was money for his wife that Mrs. Yeobright could not trust him with.", "요브라이트 부인이 그에게 맡기지 못한 것이 아내에게 줄 돈이었던 것이다."], ["“Yet she could trust this fellow,” he said to himself.", "\"그런데도 이 작자한테는 맡길 수 있단 말이지.\""], ["“Why doesn’t that which belongs to the wife belong to the husband too?”", "그가 속으로 중얼거렸다. \"아내 것이 어찌하여 남편 것이기도 하지 않단 말인가?\""], ["He called to the pot-boy to bring him his hat, and said, “Now, Christian, I am ready.”", "그는 술 따르는 소년에게 모자를 가져오라 부른 뒤 말했다. \"자, 크리스천, 준비됐네.\""]], [["“Mr. Wildeve,”", "\"와일디브 나리.\""], ["said Christian timidly, as he turned to leave the room, “would you mind lending me them wonderful little things that carry my luck inside ’em, that I might practise a bit by myself, you know?”", "크리스천이 방을 나서려 돌아서며 머뭇머뭇 말했다. \"제 운을 그 안에 품고 다니는 그 신통한 작은 물건들을 제게 빌려주시지 않겠습니까요, 혼자서 좀 연습해보게 말입니다요, 아시잖습니까요?\""], ["He looked wistfully at the dice and box lying on the mantlepiece.", "그는 벽난로 선반 위에 놓인 주사위와 통을 못내 그리운 듯 바라보았다."]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론.\""], ["said Wildeve carelessly.", "와일디브가 무심히 말했다."], ["“They were only cut out by some lad with his knife, and are worth nothing.”", "\"어차피 어느 녀석이 칼로 깎은 것일 뿐, 값나갈 것도 없네.\""], ["And Christian went back and privately pocketed them.", "그러자 크리스천은 되돌아가 그것을 슬쩍 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["Wildeve opened the door and looked out.", "와일디브는 문을 열고 밖을 내다보았다."], ["The night was warm and cloudy.", "밤은 따뜻하고 흐렸다."], ["“By Gad!", "\"이런!"], ["’tis dark,”", "캄캄하군.\""], ["he continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“But I suppose we shall find our way.”", "\"그래도 길은 찾겠지.\""]], [["“If we should lose the path it might be awkward,”", "\"길을 잃기라도 하면 곤란할 텐데요.\""], ["said Christian.", "크리스천이 말했다."], ["“A lantern is the only shield that will make it safe for us.”", "\"등불만이 우리를 안전하게 지켜줄 유일한 방패입니다요.\""]], [["“Let’s have a lantern by all means.”", "\"무슨 일이 있어도 등불은 챙기세.\""], ["The stable lantern was fetched and lighted.", "마구간 등불을 가져와 불을 붙였다."], ["Christian took up his gownpiece, and the two set out to ascend the hill.", "크리스천은 옷감을 집어 들었고, 두 사람은 언덕을 오르러 길을 나섰다."]], [["Within the room the men fell into chat till their attention was for a moment drawn to the chimney-corner.", "방 안에서는 사내들이 한담을 나누다, 잠시 그들의 주의가 굴뚝 모퉁이로 쏠렸다."], ["This was large, and, in addition to its proper recess, contained within its jambs, like many on Egdon, a receding seat, so that a person might sit there absolutely unobserved, provided there was no fire to light him up, as was the case now and throughout the summer.", "그 모퉁이는 넓었고, 본래의 우묵한 공간 외에도, 에그던의 많은 집들이 그렇듯 양옆 기둥 안쪽으로 뒤로 물러난 자리가 있어서, 불이 켜져 사람을 비추지만 않으면 누구든 거기 앉아 전혀 눈에 띄지 않을 수 있었으니, 지금처럼, 또 여름 내내 그러했다."], ["From the niche a single object protruded into the light from the candles on the table.", "그 우묵한 자리에서 단 하나의 물건이 탁자 위 촛불의 빛 속으로 비죽 나와 있었다."], ["It was a clay pipe, and its colour was reddish.", "그것은 진흙 파이프였고, 그 빛깔은 불그스름했다."], ["The men had been attracted to this object by a voice behind the pipe asking for a light.", "사내들이 이 물건에 주의를 끌린 것은 파이프 뒤에서 불을 청하는 목소리 때문이었다."]], [["“Upon my life, it fairly startled me when the man spoke!”", "\"맹세코, 그자가 말을 하니 정말 깜짝 놀랐네!\""], ["said Fairway, handing a candle.", "페어웨이가 양초를 건네며 말했다."], ["“Oh—’tis the reddleman!", "\"오―레들맨이로군!"], ["You’ve kept a quiet tongue, young man.”", "어지간히도 조용히 입 다물고 있었구먼, 젊은이.\""]], [["“Yes, I had nothing to say,”", "\"예, 할 말이 없었으니까요.\""], ["observed Venn.", "벤이 말했다."], ["In a few minutes he arose and wished the company good night.", "그는 몇 분 뒤 일어나 일행에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸다."]], [["Meanwhile Wildeve and Christian had plunged into the heath.", "그러는 사이 와일디브와 크리스천은 황야 속으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["It was a stagnant, warm, and misty night, full of all the heavy perfumes of new vegetation not yet dried by hot sun, and among these particularly the scent of the fern.", "답답하고, 따뜻하고, 안개 자욱한 밤이었으니, 뜨거운 햇볕에 아직 마르지 않은 새 초목의 온갖 짙은 향기가 가득했고, 그 가운데서도 특히 고사리 내음이 그러했다."], ["The lantern, dangling from Christian’s hand, brushed the feathery fronds in passing by, disturbing moths and other winged insects, which flew out and alighted upon its horny panes.", "크리스천의 손에 매달려 흔들리는 등불은 지나가며 깃털 같은 고사리 잎을 스쳐, 나방과 그 밖의 날벌레들을 휘저어 날아오르게 했고, 그것들은 날아올라 등불의 뿔로 만든 유리판에 내려앉았다."]], [["“So you have money to carry to Mrs. Wildeve?”", "\"그러니까 자네가 와일디브 부인에게 돈을 가져다주는 거로군?\""], ["said Christian’s companion, after a silence.", "침묵이 흐른 뒤 크리스천의 동행이 말했다."], ["“Don’t you think it very odd that it shouldn’t be given to me?”", "\"그게 내게 주어지지 않은 게 참 이상하다고 생각하지 않나?\""]], [["“As man and wife be one flesh, ’twould have been all the same, I should think,”", "\"남편과 아내는 한 몸이니, 어느 쪽이든 매한가지였을 거라고 봅니다요.\""], ["said Christian.", "크리스천이 말했다."], ["“But my strict documents was, to give the money into Mrs. Wildeve’s hand—and ’tis well to do things right.”", "\"하지만 제가 받은 엄한 지시는, 그 돈을 와일디브 부인 손에 직접 쥐여드리라는 거였습니다요―일은 제대로 하는 게 좋으니까요.\""]], [["“No doubt,”", "\"하긴.\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."], ["Any person who had known the circumstances might have perceived that Wildeve was mortified by the discovery that the matter in transit was money, and not, as he had supposed when at Blooms-End, some fancy nick-nack which only interested the two women themselves. Mrs.", "사정을 아는 사람이라면 누구든, 운반 중인 것이 돈이라는 사실에―블룸스엔드에서 그가 짐작했던 것처럼 두 여인끼리만 관심을 둘 어떤 자질구레한 장신구가 아니라는 사실에―와일디브가 굴욕감을 느끼고 있음을 알아챘으리라."], ["Yeobright’s refusal implied that his honour was not considered to be of sufficiently good quality to make him a safer bearer of his wife’s property.", "요브라이트 부인의 거절은 그의 명예가 아내의 재산을 더 안전히 맡길 만큼 충분히 좋은 품질로 여겨지지 않는다는 뜻이었다."]], [["“How very warm it is tonight, Christian!”", "\"오늘 밤은 어찌 이리 더운가, 크리스천!\""], ["he said, panting, when they were nearly under Rainbarrow.", "그들이 레인배로 거의 아래에 이르렀을 때 그가 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["“Let us sit down for a few minutes, for Heaven’s sake.”", "\"잠깐만 앉아 쉬세, 제발 좀.\""]], [["Wildeve flung himself down on the soft ferns; and Christian, placing the lantern and parcel on the ground, perched himself in a cramped position hard by, his knees almost touching his chin.", "와일디브는 부드러운 고사리 위에 몸을 던졌고, 크리스천은 등불과 꾸러미를 바닥에 내려놓은 뒤 바로 곁에 무릎이 거의 턱에 닿도록 옹송그린 자세로 자리를 잡았다."], ["He presently thrust one hand into his coat-pocket and began shaking it about.", "그는 곧 한 손을 외투 주머니에 찔러 넣고 안에서 무언가를 달그락거리기 시작했다."]], [["“What are you rattling in there?”", "\"거기서 뭘 그리 달그락거리나?\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."]], [["“Only the dice, sir,”", "\"그저 주사위입니다요, 나리.\""], ["said Christian, quickly withdrawing his hand.", "크리스천이 얼른 손을 빼며 말했다."], ["“What magical machines these little things be, Mr. Wildeve!", "\"이 작은 물건들은 어찌나 신통한 기계인지요, 와일디브 나리!"], ["’Tis a game I should never get tired of.", "아무리 해도 질리지 않을 놀이입니다요."], ["Would you mind my taking ’em out and looking at ’em for a minute, to see how they are made?", "잠깐 꺼내서 어떻게 만들어졌는지 들여다봐도 괜찮을까요?"], ["I didn’t like to look close before the other men, for fear they should think it bad manners in me.”", "아까는 다른 사람들 앞에서 가까이 들여다보기가 꺼려졌습니다요, 무례하다 여길까 봐서요.\""], ["Christian took them out and examined them in the hollow of his hand by the lantern light.", "크리스천은 그것을 꺼내 등불 빛에 손바닥을 오목하게 모아 살펴보았다."], ["“That these little things should carry such luck, and such charm, and such a spell, and such power in ’em, passes all I ever heard or zeed,”", "\"이 작은 것들이 이런 운과, 이런 마력과, 이런 주술과, 이런 힘을 품고 있다니, 제가 여태 듣거나 본 그 무엇보다도 놀랍습니다요.\""], ["he went on, with a fascinated gaze at the dice, which, as is frequently the case in country places, were made of wood, the points being burnt upon each face with the end of a wire.", "그는 주사위를 홀린 듯 바라보며 말을 이었으니, 그 주사위는 시골에서 흔히 그렇듯 나무로 만든 것이어서, 면마다 끗수가 철사 끝으로 지져 새겨져 있었다."]], [["“They are a great deal in a small compass, You think?”", "\"작은 데에 많은 걸 담고 있다고, 그런 뜻인가?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"예."], ["Do ye suppose they really be the devil’s playthings, Mr.", "정말로 이게 악마의 노리개라고 보십니까요, 와일디브 나리?"], ["Wildeve? If so, ’tis no good sign that I be such a lucky man.”", "만일 그렇다면, 제가 이렇게 운 좋은 사내라는 건 좋은 징조가 아니겠지요.\""]], [["“You ought to win some money, now that you’ve got them.", "\"이걸 손에 넣었으니 이제 돈을 좀 따야지."], ["Any woman would marry you then.", "그러면 어느 여자든 자네와 혼인할 걸세."], ["Now is your time, Christian, and I would recommend you not to let it slip.", "지금이 자네 때야, 크리스천, 놓치지 말라고 권하고 싶네."], ["Some men are born to luck, some are not.", "어떤 사내는 운을 타고나고, 어떤 사내는 못 타고나지."], ["I belong to the latter class.”", "나는 후자에 속하고.\""]], [["“Did you ever know anybody who was born to it besides myself?”", "\"저 말고 그런 운을 타고난 사람을 아십니까요?\""]], [["“O yes.", "\"오, 알지."], ["I once heard of an Italian, who sat down at a gaming table with only a louis, (that’s a foreign sovereign), in his pocket.", "언젠가 어느 이탈리아 사람 이야기를 들었네, 주머니에 루이(그게 외국 소브린이지) 한 닢만 들고 도박판에 앉았다더군."], ["He played on for twenty-four hours, and won ten thousand pounds, stripping the bank he had played against.", "스물네 시간을 내리 노름해서 만 파운드를 따고는, 상대한 물주를 알거지로 만들었다네."], ["Then there was another man who had lost a thousand pounds, and went to the broker’s next day to sell stock, that he might pay the debt.", "그리고 또 어떤 사내는 천 파운드를 잃고서 빚을 갚으려고 이튿날 증권 중개인에게 주식을 팔러 갔다네."], ["The man to whom he owed the money went with him in a hackney-coach; and to pass the time they tossed who should pay the fare.", "돈을 빌려준 사람이 그와 함께 삯마차에 탔는데, 시간이나 때울 겸 누가 삯을 낼지 던지기로 정했지."], ["The ruined man won, and the other was tempted to continue the game, and they played all the way.", "망한 사내가 이겼고, 다른 쪽이 노름을 계속하자고 꾀어서 가는 내내 던졌다네."], ["When the coachman stopped he was told to drive home again: the whole thousand pounds had been won back by the man who was going to sell.”", "마부가 멈추자 다시 집으로 가자고 했다지. 주식을 팔러 가던 사내가 천 파운드를 통째로 도로 따냈으니 말이야.\""]], [["“Ha—ha—splendid!”", "\"하―하―멋지군요!\""], ["exclaimed Christian.", "크리스천이 외쳤다."], ["“Go on—go on!”", "\"더―더 해주십쇼!\""]], [["“Then there was a man of London, who was only a waiter at White’s clubhouse.", "\"그리고 런던에 한 사내가 있었으니, 화이트 클럽하우스의 한낱 웨이터였지."], ["He began playing first half-crown stakes, and then higher and higher, till he became very rich, got an appointment in India, and rose to be Governor of Madras.", "처음엔 반 크라운씩 걸고 시작해서 점점 더 높이, 더 높이 걸다가, 아주 부자가 되어 인도에 자리를 얻고, 마드라스 총독까지 올랐다네."], ["His daughter married a member of Parliament, and the Bishop of Carlisle stood godfather to one of the children.”", "그 딸은 국회의원과 혼인했고, 칼라일 주교가 그 자식 중 하나의 대부가 되어주었지.\""]], [["“Wonderful!", "\"굉장합니다요!"], ["wonderful!”", "굉장해요!\""]], [["“And once there was a young man in America who gambled till he had lost his last dollar.", "\"또 한번은 미국에 한 젊은이가 있었는데, 노름을 하다가 마지막 한 달러까지 잃었다네."], ["He staked his watch and chain, and lost as before; staked his umbrella, lost again; staked his hat, lost again; staked his coat and stood in his shirt-sleeves, lost again.", "회중시계와 줄을 걸고 또 잃고, 우산을 걸고 또 잃고, 모자를 걸고 또 잃고, 외투를 걸고 셔츠 바람으로 남고도 또 잃었지."], ["Began taking off his breeches, and then a looker-on gave him a trifle for his pluck.", "바지를 벗기 시작하니, 구경하던 자가 그 배짱이 가상하다며 푼돈을 쥐여주었다네."], ["With this he won.", "그걸로 그가 이겼지."], ["Won back his coat, won back his hat, won back his umbrella, his watch, his money, and went out of the door a rich man.”", "외투를 도로 따고, 모자를 도로 따고, 우산을, 시계를, 돈을 도로 따서, 부자가 되어 문을 나섰다네.\""]], [["“Oh, ’tis too good—it takes away my breath! Mr.", "\"오, 너무 좋아서―숨이 막힙니다요!"], ["Wildeve, I think I will try another shilling with you, as I am one of that sort; no danger can come o’t, and you can afford to lose.”", "와일디브 나리, 저도 그런 부류이니, 나리하고 1실링 더 해봐야겠습니다요. 위험할 것도 없고, 나리는 잃어도 괜찮으시잖습니까요.\""]], [["“Very well,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said Wildeve, rising.", "와일디브가 일어서며 말했다."], ["Searching about with the lantern, he found a large flat stone, which he placed between himself and Christian, and sat down again.", "등불을 들고 이리저리 더듬어 그는 크고 평평한 돌을 찾아내 자신과 크리스천 사이에 놓고 다시 앉았다."], ["The lantern was opened to give more light, and its rays directed upon the stone.", "더 밝게 하려고 등불을 열어 그 빛을 돌 위로 향하게 했다."], ["Christian put down a shilling, Wildeve another, and each threw.", "크리스천이 1실링을 내려놓고, 와일디브가 또 1실링을 내려놓고, 각자 던졌다."], ["Christian won.", "크리스천이 이겼다."], ["They played for two, Christian won again.", "둘을 걸고 했더니, 크리스천이 또 이겼다."]], [["“Let us try four,”", "\"넷으로 해보세.\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."], ["They played for four.", "그들은 넷을 걸고 했다."], ["This time the stakes were won by Wildeve.", "이번엔 와일디브가 판돈을 땄다."]], [["“Ah, those little accidents will, of course, sometimes happen, to the luckiest man,”", "\"아, 저런 사소한 사고야 물론 더러 일어나는 법이지, 아무리 운 좋은 사내한테도 말이야.\""], ["he observed.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“And now I have no more money!”", "\"이제 제겐 돈이 더 없습니다요!\""], ["explained Christian excitedly.", "크리스천이 들떠서 설명했다."], ["“And yet, if I could go on, I should get it back again, and more.", "\"그런데도, 계속할 수만 있다면 도로 따고 또 더 딸 텐데요."], ["I wish this was mine.”", "이게 내 것이라면.\""], ["He struck his boot upon the ground, so that the guineas chinked within.", "그는 부츠로 바닥을 쳤고, 그러자 안에서 기니가 짤랑거렸다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라!"], ["you have not put Mrs. Wildeve’s money there?”", "자네 와일디브 부인의 돈을 거기 넣어둔 건가?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"예."], ["’Tis for safety.", "안전하게 두려고요."], ["Is it any harm to raffle with a married lady’s money when, if I win, I shall only keep my winnings, and give her her own all the same; and if t’other man wins, her money will go to the lawful owner?”", "결혼한 부인의 돈으로 제비뽑기를 하는 게 무슨 해가 되겠습니까요, 제가 따더라도 딴 것만 챙기고 그분 몫은 그대로 돌려드릴 거고, 다른 분이 따더라도 그분 돈은 정당한 임자에게 돌아갈 텐데 말입니다요?\""]], [["“None at all.”", "\"전혀 안 되지.\""]], [["Wildeve had been brooding ever since they started on the mean estimation in which he was held by his wife’s friends; and it cut his heart severely.", "와일디브는 길을 나선 뒤로 줄곧, 아내의 친지들이 자신을 하찮게 여긴다는 사실을 곱씹어왔으니, 그것이 그의 심장을 모질게 도려냈다."], ["As the minutes passed he had gradually drifted into a revengeful intention without knowing the precise moment of forming it.", "시간이 흐를수록 그는 정확히 어느 순간에 그런 생각이 들었는지도 모른 채 차츰 복수심에 빠져들어 갔다."], ["This was to teach Mrs. Yeobright a lesson, as he considered it to be; in other words, to show her if he could that her niece’s husband was the proper guardian of her niece’s money.", "그것은 요브라이트 부인에게―그가 보기엔―따끔한 교훈을 주려는 것이었으니, 다시 말해, 할 수만 있다면 그녀의 조카딸 남편이야말로 조카딸 돈의 마땅한 보호자임을 보여주려는 것이었다."]], [["“Well, here goes!”", "\"자, 간다!\""], ["said Christian, beginning to unlace one boot.", "크리스천이 부츠 한 짝의 끈을 풀기 시작하며 말했다."], ["“I shall dream of it nights and nights, I suppose; but I shall always swear my flesh don’t crawl when I think o’t!”", "\"밤이고 밤이고 이 일을 꿈에 볼 테지만, 생각해도 살갗이 오싹하지 않노라고 늘 맹세하겠습니다요!\""]], [["He thrust his hand into the boot and withdrew one of poor Thomasin’s precious guineas, piping hot.", "그는 부츠 안에 손을 넣어 가련한 토머신의 귀한 기니 한 닢을, 뜨끈뜨끈한 것을 꺼냈다."], ["Wildeve had already placed a sovereign on the stone.", "와일디브는 이미 소브린 한 닢을 돌 위에 올려놓은 터였다."], ["The game was then resumed.", "그리하여 노름이 다시 시작되었다."], ["Wildeve won first, and Christian ventured another, winning himself this time.", "와일디브가 먼저 이겼고, 크리스천이 또 한 닢을 걸어 이번엔 자신이 이겼다."], ["The game fluctuated, but the average was in Wildeve’s favour.", "승부는 엎치락뒤치락했으나, 평균적으로는 와일디브에게 유리했다."], ["Both men became so absorbed in the game that they took no heed of anything but the pigmy objects immediately beneath their eyes, the flat stone, the open lantern, the dice, and the few illuminated fern-leaves which lay under the light, were the whole world to them.", "두 사내 모두 노름에 어찌나 빠져들었는지, 바로 눈앞의 자그마한 것들 외엔 아무것에도 신경 쓰지 않았으니, 평평한 돌, 열린 등불, 주사위, 그리고 그 빛 아래 깔린 환히 비치는 몇 잎의 고사리가 그들에겐 온 세상이었다."]], [["At length Christian lost rapidly; and presently, to his horror, the whole fifty guineas belonging to Thomasin had been handed over to his adversary.", "마침내 크리스천은 빠르게 잃었고, 이내, 소스라치게도, 토머신의 것인 오십 기니가 통째로 그의 상대에게 넘어가버렸다."]], [["“I don’t care—I don’t care!”", "\"상관없어―상관없어!\""], ["he moaned, and desperately set about untying his left boot to get at the other fifty.", "그가 신음하며, 나머지 오십 기니를 꺼내려 절박하게 왼쪽 부츠 끈을 풀기 시작했다."], ["“The devil will toss me into the flames on his three-pronged fork for this night’s work, I know!", "\"이 밤에 벌인 일로 악마가 그 세 갈래 갈퀴로 나를 불길 속에 처넣을 테지, 알고말고!"], ["But perhaps I shall win yet, and then I’ll get a wife to sit up with me o’ nights and I won’t be afeard, I won’t!", "하지만 어쩌면 그래도 딸지도 몰라, 그러면 밤마다 나하고 같이 앉아 지새워줄 아내를 얻을 거고, 그럼 무섭지 않을 거야, 안 무서워!"], ["Here’s another for’ee, my man!”", "자, 또 한 닢 가시오, 나리!\""], ["He slapped another guinea down upon the stone, and the dice-box was rattled again.", "그는 또 한 닢의 기니를 돌 위에 탁 내려놓았고, 주사위 통이 다시 달그락거렸다."]], [["Time passed on.", "시간이 흘러갔다."], ["Wildeve began to be as excited as Christian himself.", "와일디브도 크리스천 못지않게 흥분하기 시작했다."], ["When commencing the game his intention had been nothing further than a bitter practical joke on Mrs. Yeobright.", "노름을 시작할 때만 해도 그의 의도는 요브라이트 부인을 향한 쓰디쓴 짓궂은 장난, 그 이상이 아니었다."], ["To win the money, fairly or otherwise, and to hand it contemptuously to Thomasin in her aunt’s presence, had been the dim outline of his purpose.", "정당하게든 아니든 돈을 따서, 그 이모 앞에서 토머신에게 경멸하듯 건네주는 것이 그가 품은 목적의 흐릿한 윤곽이었다."], ["But men are drawn from their intentions even in the course of carrying them out, and it was extremely doubtful, by the time the twentieth guinea had been reached, whether Wildeve was conscious of any other intention than that of winning for his own personal benefit.", "그러나 사람은 의도를 실행에 옮기는 와중에도 그 의도에서 멀어지는 법이어서, 스무 번째 기니에 이르렀을 무렵엔 와일디브가 그저 자기 잇속을 챙기려 딴다는 의도 외에 다른 의도를 의식하고 있는지조차 몹시 의심스러웠다."], ["Moreover, he was now no longer gambling for his wife’s money, but for Yeobright’s; though of this fact Christian, in his apprehensiveness, did not inform him till afterwards.", "게다가 그가 이제 거는 것은 아내의 돈이 아니라 요브라이트의 돈이었으나, 이 사실을 크리스천은 겁에 질린 나머지 나중에야 그에게 알렸다."]], [["It was nearly eleven o’clock, when, with almost a shriek, Christian placed Yeobright’s last gleaming guinea upon the stone.", "거의 열한 시가 되었을 때, 크리스천은 비명에 가까운 소리를 내며 요브라이트의 마지막 번득이는 기니를 돌 위에 올려놓았다."], ["In thirty seconds it had gone the way of its companions.", "삼십 초 만에 그것은 동무들이 간 길을 따라가버렸다."]], [["Christian turned and flung himself on the ferns in a convulsion of remorse, “O, what shall I do with my wretched self?”", "크리스천은 몸을 돌려 후회의 발작에 휩싸여 고사리 위로 몸을 던졌다. \"오, 이 비참한 놈을 어찌해야 한단 말인가?\""], ["he groaned.", "그가 신음했다."], ["“What shall I do?", "\"어찌해야 하나?"], ["Will any good Heaven hae mercy upon my wicked soul?”", "자비로우신 하늘이 이 죄 많은 영혼에 자비를 베풀어주실까?\""]], [["“Do?", "\"어쩌긴?"], ["Live on just the same.”", "그냥 똑같이 살아가는 거지.\""]], [["“I won’t live on just the same!", "\"똑같이 살지 않을 테요!"], ["I’ll die!", "죽어버릴 테요!"], ["I say you are a—a——”", "당신은―당신은―\""]], [["“A man sharper than my neighbour.”", "\"이웃보다 약빠른 사내지.\""]], [["“Yes, a man sharper than my neighbour; a regular sharper!”", "\"그래요, 이웃보다 약빠른 사내, 영락없는 사기꾼이로구먼!\""]], [["“Poor chips-in-porridge, you are very unmannerly.”", "\"가련한 죽 속의 부스러기 같으니, 자네 참 무례하군.\""]], [["“I don’t know about that!", "\"그건 모르겠소!"], ["And I say you be unmannerly!", "그리고 당신이야말로 무례하다고 하겠소!"], ["You’ve got money that isn’t your own.", "당신은 당신 것도 아닌 돈을 가졌어."], ["Half the guineas are poor Mr. Clym’s.”", "그 기니의 절반은 가련한 클림 도련님 거란 말이오.\""]], [["“How’s that?”", "\"그건 또 무슨 소린가?\""]], [["“Because I had to gie fifty of ’em to him.", "\"그분께 오십 닢을 드려야 했으니까요."], ["Mrs. Yeobright said so.”", "요브라이트 부인이 그렇게 말씀하셨소.\""]], [["“Oh?...", "\"오?…"], ["Well, ’twould have been more graceful of her to have given them to his wife Eustacia.", "글쎄, 그렇다면 그의 아내 유스테이샤에게 주는 편이 더 점잖았을 텐데."], ["But they are in my hands now.”", "어쨌든 이젠 내 손에 있네.\""]], [["Christian pulled on his boots, and with heavy breathings, which could be heard to some distance, dragged his limbs together, arose, and tottered away out of sight.", "크리스천은 부츠를 신고서, 멀찍이까지 들릴 만큼 거친 숨을 몰아쉬며 사지를 끌어모아 일어나, 비척비척 시야 밖으로 사라졌다."], ["Wildeve set about shutting the lantern to return to the house, for he deemed it too late to go to Mistover to meet his wife, who was to be driven home in the captain’s four-wheel.", "와일디브는 집으로 돌아가려 등불을 닫기 시작했으니, 미스토버로 가서 아내를 맞기엔 너무 늦었다고 여겼던 것이고, 그녀는 선장의 네 바퀴 마차로 집까지 실려 갈 참이었다."], ["While he was closing the little horn door a figure rose from behind a neighbouring bush and came forward into the lantern light. It was the reddleman approaching.", "그가 작은 뿔판 문을 닫고 있을 때, 가까운 덤불 뒤에서 한 형상이 일어나 등불 빛 속으로 나섰다."], ["VIII. A New Force Disturbs the Current", "다가오는 것은 레들맨이었다."]], [["Wildeve stared.", "와일디브가 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["Venn looked coolly towards Wildeve, and, without a word being spoken, he deliberately sat himself down where Christian had been seated, thrust his hand into his pocket, drew out a sovereign, and laid it on the stone.", "벤은 와일디브 쪽을 차갑게 바라보더니, 한마디도 없이 크리스천이 앉았던 자리에 천천히 앉아, 주머니에 손을 넣어 소브린 한 닢을 꺼내 돌 위에 올려놓았다."]], [["“You have been watching us from behind that bush?”", "\"저 덤불 뒤에서 우리를 지켜보고 있었나?\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."]], [["The reddleman nodded.", "레들맨은 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“Down with your stake,”", "\"판돈을 거시오.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Or haven’t you pluck enough to go on?”", "\"아니면 계속할 배짱이 없는 거요?\""]], [["Now, gambling is a species of amusement which is much more easily begun with full pockets than left off with the same; and though Wildeve in a cooler temper might have prudently declined this invitation, the excitement of his recent success carried him completely away.", "그런데 도박이란 주머니가 두둑할 때 시작하기는 쉬워도 그 두둑한 채로 손 떼기는 훨씬 어려운 종류의 소일거리여서, 와일디브가 좀 더 냉정한 마음이었다면 신중히 이 권유를 물렸을지 몰라도, 방금 거둔 성공의 흥분이 그를 완전히 휩쓸어갔다."], ["He placed one of the guineas on a slab beside the reddleman’s sovereign.", "그는 레들맨의 소브린 곁에, 평평한 돌 위에 기니 한 닢을 올려놓았다."], ["“Mine is a guinea,”", "\"내 건 기니일세.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“A guinea that’s not your own,”", "\"당신 것도 아닌 기니지.\""], ["said Venn sarcastically.", "벤이 빈정대며 말했다."]], [["“It is my own,”", "\"내 것이네.\""], ["answered Wildeve haughtily.", "와일디브가 거만하게 답했다."], ["“It is my wife’s, and what is hers is mine.”", "\"아내 것이고, 아내 것은 곧 내 것이니까.\""]], [["“Very well; let’s make a beginning.”", "\"좋소. 그럼 시작해봅시다.\""], ["He shook the box, and threw eight, ten, and nine; the three casts amounted to twenty-seven.", "그는 통을 흔들어 여덟, 열, 아홉을 던졌고, 세 번 던진 합이 스물일곱이었다."]], [["This encouraged Wildeve.", "이에 와일디브가 고무되었다."], ["He took the box; and his three casts amounted to forty-five.", "그가 통을 들었고, 세 번 던진 합이 마흔다섯이었다."]], [["Down went another of the reddleman’s sovereigns against his first one which Wildeve laid.", "레들맨의 또 다른 소브린 한 닢이, 와일디브가 올려둔 그의 첫 닢을 마주하여 놓였다."], ["This time Wildeve threw fifty-one points, but no pair.", "이번에 와일디브는 끗수로 쉰하나를 던졌으나 페어는 없었다."], ["The reddleman looked grim, threw a raffle of aces, and pocketed the stakes.", "레들맨은 무뚝뚝한 얼굴로 에이스만 가득한 패를 던지고는 판돈을 챙겼다."]], [["“Here you are again,”", "\"또 시작이로군.\""], ["said Wildeve contemptuously.", "와일디브가 경멸하듯 말했다."], ["“Double the stakes.”", "\"판돈을 두 배로 하지.\""], ["He laid two of Thomasin’s guineas, and the reddleman his two pounds.", "그는 토머신의 기니 두 닢을 올려놓았고, 레들맨은 2파운드를 올려놓았다."], ["Venn won again.", "또 벤이 이겼다."], ["New stakes were laid on the stone, and the gamblers proceeded as before.", "새 판돈이 돌 위에 놓였고, 노름꾼들은 전과 같이 계속했다."]], [["Wildeve was a nervous and excitable man, and the game was beginning to tell upon his temper.", "와일디브는 신경이 곤두서고 쉬이 흥분하는 사내였으니, 노름이 그의 성미를 건드리기 시작했다."], ["He writhed, fumed, shifted his seat, and the beating of his heart was almost audible.", "그는 몸을 뒤틀고, 씩씩거리고, 자리를 옮겼으며, 심장 뛰는 소리가 거의 들릴 지경이었다."], ["Venn sat with lips impassively closed and eyes reduced to a pair of unimportant twinkles; he scarcely appeared to breathe.", "벤은 입술을 무심히 다물고 눈을 하찮은 한 쌍의 반짝임으로 좁힌 채 앉아 있었으니, 숨을 쉬는 기색조차 거의 없었다."], ["He might have been an Arab, or an automaton; he would have been like a red sandstone statue but for the motion of his arm with the dice-box.", "그는 아랍인이거나 자동인형 같았으니, 주사위 통을 든 팔의 움직임만 아니었다면 붉은 사암으로 만든 조상 같았으리라."]], [["The game fluctuated, now in favour of one, now in favour of the other, without any great advantage on the side of either.", "승부는 엎치락뒤치락하며, 어느 한쪽이 크게 유리하다 할 것도 없이 이쪽으로 갔다 저쪽으로 갔다 했다."], ["Nearly twenty minutes were passed thus.", "그렇게 거의 이십 분이 지났다."], ["The light of the candle had by this time attracted heath-flies, moths, and other winged creatures of night, which floated round the lantern, flew into the flame, or beat about the faces of the two players.", "그새 촛불의 빛이 황야의 파리며 나방이며 그 밖의 밤 날벌레들을 끌어모았으니, 그것들은 등불 둘레를 떠다니다, 불꽃 속으로 날아들거나, 두 노름꾼의 얼굴 언저리에서 푸덕거렸다."]], [["But neither of the men paid much attention to these things, their eyes being concentrated upon the little flat stone, which to them was an arena vast and important as a battlefield.", "그러나 두 사내는 그런 것엔 별 주의를 두지 않았으니, 그들의 눈은 그 자그마한 평평한 돌에 쏠려 있었고, 그것은 그들에게 전장만큼이나 광대하고 중대한 격투장이었다."], ["By this time a change had come over the game; the reddleman won continually.", "이 무렵 노름에 변화가 찾아왔으니, 레들맨이 내리 이겼다."], ["At length sixty guineas—Thomasin’s fifty, and ten of Clym’s—had passed into his hands.", "마침내 육십 기니―토머신의 오십, 그리고 클림의 십―가 그의 손으로 넘어갔다."], ["Wildeve was reckless, frantic, exasperated.", "와일디브는 무모하고, 광란하고, 격분했다."]], [["“‘Won back his coat,’”", "\"'외투를 도로 따고,'\""], ["said Venn slily.", "벤이 능청맞게 말했다."]], [["Another throw, and the money went the same way.", "또 한 번 던졌고, 돈은 같은 길을 갔다."]], [["“‘Won back his hat,’”", "\"'모자를 도로 따고,'\""], ["continued Venn.", "벤이 이어 말했다."]], [["“Oh, oh!”", "\"오, 오!\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."]], [["“‘Won back his watch, won back his money, and went out of the door a rich man,’”", "\"'시계를 도로 따고, 돈을 도로 따서, 부자가 되어 문을 나섰다,'\""], ["added Venn sentence by sentence, as stake after stake passed over to him.", "벤이 판돈이 자기 쪽으로 넘어올 때마다 한 구절씩 덧붙였다."]], [["“Five more!”", "\"다섯 닢 더!\""], ["shouted Wildeve, dashing down the money.", "와일디브가 돈을 내동댕이치며 외쳤다."], ["“And three casts be hanged—one shall decide.”", "\"세 번 던지기는 집어치워―한 번으로 결판내자고.\""]], [["The red automaton opposite lapsed into silence, nodded, and followed his example.", "맞은편의 붉은 자동인형은 침묵에 잠겨 고개를 끄덕이고는 그를 따랐다."], ["Wildeve rattled the box, and threw a pair of sixes and five points.", "와일디브가 통을 달그락거려 육 한 쌍에 끗수 다섯을 던졌다."], ["He clapped his hands; “I have done it this time—hurrah!”", "그는 손뼉을 쳤다. \"이번엔 해냈군―만세!\""]], [["“There are two playing, and only one has thrown,”", "\"노름하는 사람이 둘인데, 던진 건 하나뿐이오.\""], ["said the reddleman, quietly bringing down the box.", "레들맨이 조용히 통을 내려놓으며 말했다."], ["The eyes of each were then so intently converged upon the stone that one could fancy their beams were visible, like rays in a fog. Venn lifted the box, and behold a triplet of sixes was disclosed.", "그러자 둘의 눈이 어찌나 골똘히 돌 위로 모였던지, 그 시선의 빛줄기가 안개 속의 광선처럼 눈에 보이는 듯했다."]], [["Wildeve was full of fury.", "벤이 통을 들어올리자, 보라, 육 세 개가 드러났다."], ["While the reddleman was grasping the stakes Wildeve seized the dice and hurled them, box and all, into the darkness, uttering a fearful imprecation.", "와일디브는 분노로 가득 찼다. 레들맨이 판돈을 그러쥐는 사이, 와일디브는 주사위를 움켜쥐고 통째로 어둠 속으로 내던지며 끔찍한 저주를 내뱉었다."], ["Then he arose and began stamping up and down like a madman.", "그러고는 일어나 미친 사람처럼 위아래로 쿵쿵거리며 걷기 시작했다."]], [["“It is all over, then?”", "\"그럼 다 끝난 거요?\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."]], [["“No, no!”", "\"아냐, 아냐!\""], ["cried Wildeve.", "와일디브가 외쳤다."], ["“I mean to have another chance yet.", "\"난 한 번 더 기회를 가질 작정이오."], ["I must!”", "그래야 해!\""]], [["“But, my good man, what have you done with the dice?”", "\"한데, 이보쇼, 주사위를 어찌해버린 거요?\""]], [["“I threw them away—it was a momentary irritation.", "\"내던졌소―한순간의 화풀이였소."], ["What a fool I am!", "내가 바보 천치지!"], ["Here—come and help me to look for them—we must find them again.”", "자―와서 찾는 걸 도와주시오―다시 찾아야 하오.\""]], [["Wildeve snatched up the lantern and began anxiously prowling among the furze and fern.", "와일디브는 등불을 낚아채 가시금작화와 고사리 사이를 안달하며 헤집기 시작했다."]], [["“You are not likely to find them there,”", "\"거기선 찾기 어려울 거요.\""], ["said Venn, following.", "벤이 뒤따르며 말했다."], ["“What did you do such a crazy thing as that for?", "\"대체 무슨 미친 짓을 한 거요? 여기 통은 있소."], ["Here’s the box. The dice can’t be far off.”", "주사위도 멀리 가진 않았을 거요.\""]], [["Wildeve turned the light eagerly upon the spot where Venn had found the box, and mauled the herbage right and left.", "와일디브는 벤이 통을 찾은 자리에 등불을 들이대고는 풀숲을 이리저리 마구 헤집었다."], ["In the course of a few minutes one of the dice was found.", "몇 분이 지나 주사위 하나를 찾았다."], ["They searched on for some time, but no other was to be seen.", "그들은 한동안 더 뒤졌으나, 다른 하나는 보이지 않았다."]], [["“Never mind,”", "\"괜찮소.\""], ["said Wildeve; “let’s play with one.”", "와일디브가 말했다. \"하나로 합시다.\""]], [["“Agreed,”", "\"좋소.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."]], [["Down they sat again, and recommenced with single guinea stakes; and the play went on smartly.", "그들은 다시 앉아 기니 한 닢씩을 걸고 다시 시작했고, 노름은 재빠르게 진행되었다."], ["But Fortune had unmistakably fallen in love with the reddleman tonight.", "그러나 운명은 오늘 밤 분명코 레들맨과 사랑에 빠진 터였다."], ["He won steadily, till he was the owner of fourteen more of the gold pieces.", "그는 꾸준히 이겨 금화를 열네 닢 더 손에 넣었다."], ["Seventy-nine of the hundred guineas were his, Wildeve possessing only twenty-one.", "백 기니 중 일흔아홉이 그의 것이었고, 와일디브에겐 고작 스물하나가 남았다."], ["The aspect of the two opponents was now singular.", "두 적수의 모습은 이제 기묘했다."], ["Apart from motions, a complete diorama of the fluctuations of the game went on in their eyes.", "몸짓을 제쳐두면, 노름의 부침을 고스란히 담은 디오라마가 그들의 눈 속에서 펼쳐지고 있었다."], ["A diminutive candle-flame was mirrored in each pupil, and it would have been possible to distinguish therein between the moods of hope and the moods of abandonment, even as regards the reddleman, though his facial muscles betrayed nothing at all.", "자그마한 촛불의 불꽃이 저마다의 눈동자에 비쳤으니, 그 안에서 희망의 기색과 체념의 기색을 분간하는 것이 가능했고, 얼굴 근육은 아무것도 드러내지 않았으나 레들맨조차 그러했다."], ["Wildeve played on with the recklessness of despair.", "와일디브는 절망의 무모함으로 노름을 이어갔다."]], [["“What’s that?”", "\"저게 뭐야?\""], ["he suddenly exclaimed, hearing a rustle; and they both looked up.", "그가 부스럭 소리를 듣고 갑자기 외쳤고, 둘 다 고개를 들었다."]], [["They were surrounded by dusky forms between four and five feet high, standing a few paces beyond the rays of the lantern.", "그들은 키 4피트에서 5피트쯤 되는 거무스름한 형상들에 둘러싸여 있었으니, 등불 빛이 닿는 곳에서 몇 걸음 떨어진 데 서 있었다."], ["A moment’s inspection revealed that the encircling figures were heath-croppers, their heads being all towards the players, at whom they gazed intently.", "잠시 살펴보니 둘레를 에워싼 형상들은 황야의 조랑말들로, 머리를 죄다 노름꾼들 쪽으로 향한 채 그들을 골똘히 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“Hoosh!”", "\"훠이!\""], ["said Wildeve, and the whole forty or fifty animals at once turned and galloped away.", "와일디브가 외치자, 마흔이나 쉰 마리쯤 되는 짐승 떼가 일제히 돌아서서 내달려 갔다."], ["Play was again resumed.", "노름이 다시 재개되었다."]], [["Ten minutes passed away.", "십 분이 지났다."], ["Then a large death’s head moth advanced from the obscure outer air, wheeled twice round the lantern, flew straight at the candle, and extinguished it by the force of the blow.", "그때 커다란 박각시나방 한 마리가 흐릿한 바깥 공기 속에서 나아와 등불 둘레를 두 바퀴 돌더니, 촛불을 향해 곧장 날아가, 부딪치는 힘으로 그것을 꺼뜨렸다."], ["Wildeve had just thrown, but had not lifted the box to see what he had cast; and now it was impossible.", "와일디브는 막 던진 참이었으나, 통을 들어 자신이 던진 것을 보지 못한 터였으니, 이제 그건 불가능했다."]], [["“What the infernal!”", "\"이런 빌어먹을!\""], ["he shrieked.", "그가 비명을 질렀다."], ["“Now, what shall we do?", "\"이제 어쩌지?"], ["Perhaps I have thrown six—have you any matches?”", "어쩌면 내가 육을 던졌을지도 몰라―성냥 있소?\""]], [["“None,”", "\"없소.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."]], [["“Christian had some—I wonder where he is.", "\"크리스천이 좀 가지고 있었는데―그 작자 어디 있나 몰라."], ["Christian!”", "크리스천!\""]], [["But there was no reply to Wildeve’s shout, save a mournful whining from the herons which were nesting lower down the vale.", "그러나 와일디브의 외침에 대한 답이라곤, 골짜기 아래쪽에 둥지를 튼 왜가리들의 구슬픈 울음소리뿐이었다."], ["Both men looked blankly round without rising.", "두 사내는 일어서지 않은 채 멍하니 둘레를 둘러보았다."], ["As their eyes grew accustomed to the darkness they perceived faint greenish points of light among the grass and fern.", "눈이 어둠에 익숙해지자, 풀과 고사리 사이로 희미한 푸르스름한 빛점들이 보였다."], ["These lights dotted the hillside like stars of a low magnitude.", "그 빛들은 작은 등급의 별들처럼 언덕 비탈에 점점이 박혀 있었다."]], [["“Ah—glowworms,”", "\"아―반딧불이로군.\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."], ["“Wait a minute.", "\"잠깐만."], ["We can continue the game.”", "노름을 계속할 수 있겠어.\""]], [["Venn sat still, and his companion went hither and thither till he had gathered thirteen glowworms—as many as he could find in a space of four or five minutes—upon a fox-glove leaf which he pulled for the purpose.", "벤은 가만히 앉아 있었고, 그의 동행은 이리저리 다니며, 사오 분 사이에 찾을 수 있는 만큼―열세 마리의 반딧불이를―그 일을 위해 뜯어온 디기탈리스 잎 위에 모았다."], ["The reddleman vented a low humorous laugh when he saw his adversary return with these.", "레들맨은 적수가 이것들을 들고 돌아오는 것을 보고 나직이 익살스러운 웃음을 흘렸다."], ["“Determined to go on, then?”", "\"그래도 계속하기로 마음먹은 거요?\""], ["he said drily.", "그가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."]], [["“I always am!” said Wildeve angrily.", "\"난 늘 그렇소!\""], ["And shaking the glowworms from the leaf he ranged them with a trembling hand in a circle on the stone, leaving a space in the middle for the descent of the dice-box, over which the thirteen tiny lamps threw a pale phosphoric shine.", "와일디브가 성을 내며 말했다. 그러고는 잎에서 반딧불이를 털어내, 떨리는 손으로 돌 위에 둥글게 늘어놓고, 가운데엔 주사위 통이 내려올 자리를 남겨두었으니, 그 위로 열세 개의 작은 등불이 창백한 인광을 던졌다."], ["The game was again renewed.", "노름이 다시 새로 시작되었다."], ["It happened to be that season of the year at which glowworms put forth their greatest brilliancy, and the light they yielded was more than ample for the purpose, since it is possible on such nights to read the handwriting of a letter by the light of two or three.", "마침 그 무렵은 반딧불이가 가장 찬란히 빛을 내는 철이어서, 그것들이 내는 빛은 이 일에 차고도 넘쳤으니, 그런 밤이면 두세 마리의 빛으로도 편지의 글씨를 읽을 수 있을 정도였다."]], [["The incongruity between the men’s deeds and their environment was great.", "두 사내의 행위와 그들이 처한 환경 사이의 부조화는 컸다."], ["Amid the soft juicy vegetation of the hollow in which they sat, the motionless and the uninhabited solitude, intruded the chink of guineas, the rattle of dice, the exclamations of the reckless players.", "그들이 앉은 오목한 자리의 보드랍고 물기 머금은 초목 가운데, 움직임 없고 인적 없는 적막 속으로, 기니의 짤랑임, 주사위의 달그락거림, 무모한 노름꾼들의 외침이 끼어들었다."], ["Wildeve had lifted the box as soon as the lights were obtained, and the solitary die proclaimed that the game was still against him.", "와일디브는 빛이 갖춰지자마자 통을 들어올렸고, 외톨이 주사위는 노름이 여전히 그에게 불리함을 알렸다."]], [["“I won’t play any more—you’ve been tampering with the dice,”", "\"더는 안 하겠소―당신이 주사위에 손을 댄 거야.\""], ["he shouted.", "그가 소리쳤다."]], [["“How—when they were your own?”", "\"어떻게―당신 것이었는데?\""], ["said the reddleman.", "레들맨이 말했다."]], [["“We’ll change the game: the lowest point shall win the stake—it may cut off my ill luck.", "\"방식을 바꿉시다. 가장 낮은 끗수가 판돈을 가져가는 걸로―그러면 내 불운이 끊길지도 모르지."], ["Do you refuse?”", "거절하겠소?\""]], [["“No—go on,”", "\"아니―계속합시다.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."]], [["“O, there they are again—damn them!”", "\"오, 저것들 또 왔군―빌어먹을 것들!\""], ["cried Wildeve, looking up.", "와일디브가 위를 올려다보며 외쳤다."], ["The heath-croppers had returned noiselessly, and were looking on with erect heads just as before, their timid eyes fixed upon the scene, as if they were wondering what mankind and candlelight could have to do in these haunts at this untoward hour.", "황야의 조랑말들이 소리 없이 돌아와, 전과 똑같이 머리를 곧추세우고 지켜보고 있었으니, 그 겁먹은 눈을 그 장면에 고정한 채, 이 어울리지 않는 시각에 인간과 촛불이 이런 후미진 곳에 무슨 볼일이 있는지 의아해하는 듯했다."]], [["“What a plague those creatures are—staring at me so!”", "\"저 짐승들 참 골칫거리야―날 저렇게 빤히 쳐다보다니!\""], ["he said, and flung a stone, which scattered them; when the game was continued as before.", "그가 말하며 돌을 던지자 그것들이 흩어졌고, 노름은 전과 같이 이어졌다."]], [["Wildeve had now ten guineas left; and each laid five.", "이제 와일디브에겐 열 기니가 남았고, 각자 다섯 닢을 걸었다."], ["Wildeve threw three points; Venn two, and raked in the coins.", "와일디브는 끗수 셋을 던졌고, 벤은 둘을 던져 동전을 긁어모았다."], ["The other seized the die, and clenched his teeth upon it in sheer rage, as if he would bite it in pieces.", "다른 쪽은 주사위를 움켜쥐고 이로 깨물 듯 격분하여, 산산조각으로 깨물어버릴 것처럼 어금니를 악물었다."], ["“Never give in—here are my last five!”", "\"절대 안 굽혀―여기 내 마지막 다섯 닢이오!\""], ["he cried, throwing them down.", "그가 그것들을 내던지며 외쳤다."], ["“Hang the glowworms—they are going out.", "\"빌어먹을 반딧불이―꺼져가는군."], ["Why don’t you burn, you little fools?", "왜 안 빛나는 거야, 이 멍청한 것들아?"], ["Stir them up with a thorn.”", "가시로 좀 쑤셔서 깨워.\""]], [["He probed the glowworms with a bit of stick, and rolled them over, till the bright side of their tails was upwards.", "그는 막대기 조각으로 반딧불이를 쑤셔, 꽁무니의 밝은 쪽이 위로 가도록 굴렸다."]], [["“There’s light enough.", "\"빛은 충분하오."], ["Throw on,”", "던지시오.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."]], [["Wildeve brought down the box within the shining circle and looked eagerly.", "와일디브는 빛나는 원 안으로 통을 내려놓고 간절히 들여다보았다."], ["He had thrown ace.", "그는 에이스를 던졌다."], ["“Well done!—I said it would turn, and it has turned.”", "\"잘됐다!―운이 돌 거라 했더니, 돌았어.\""], ["Venn said nothing; but his hand shook slightly.", "벤은 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 그의 손이 살짝 떨렸다."]], [["He threw ace also.", "그도 에이스를 던졌다."]], [["“O!”", "\"오!\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."], ["“Curse me!”", "\"젠장!\""]], [["The die smacked the stone a second time.", "주사위가 돌을 두 번째로 탁 쳤다."], ["It was ace again.", "또 에이스였다."], ["Venn looked gloomy, threw—the die was seen to be lying in two pieces, the cleft sides uppermost.", "벤은 침울한 얼굴로 던졌고―주사위는 두 조각으로 갈라져, 갈라진 면이 위로 향한 채 놓여 있었다."]], [["“I’ve thrown nothing at all,”", "\"난 아무것도 던지지 못한 거요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Serves me right—I split the die with my teeth.", "\"내가 당해도 싸지―이로 주사위를 쪼개버렸으니."], ["Here—take your money.", "자―당신 돈 가져가시오."], ["Blank is less than one.”", "무는 하나보다 적으니까.\""]], [["“I don’t wish it.”", "\"난 그걸 바라지 않소.\""]], [["“Take it, I say—you’ve won it!”", "\"가져가라니까―당신이 딴 거요!\""], ["And Wildeve threw the stakes against the reddleman’s chest.", "그러고는 와일디브가 판돈을 레들맨의 가슴팍에 던졌다."], ["Venn gathered them up, arose, and withdrew from the hollow, Wildeve sitting stupefied.", "벤은 그것을 그러모아 일어나 오목한 자리에서 물러났고, 와일디브는 망연자실하여 앉아 있었다."]], [["When he had come to himself he also arose, and, with the extinguished lantern in his hand, went towards the highroad.", "정신이 들자 그도 일어나, 꺼진 등불을 손에 든 채 큰길 쪽으로 갔다."], ["On reaching it he stood still.", "큰길에 이르자 그는 가만히 멈춰 섰다."], ["The silence of night pervaded the whole heath except in one direction; and that was towards Mistover.", "밤의 정적이 황야 전체에 깔려 있었으니, 한 방향만이 예외였고, 그것은 미스토버 쪽이었다."], ["There he could hear the noise of light wheels, and presently saw two carriagelamps descending the hill.", "거기서 그는 가벼운 바퀴 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 이내 마차 등불 두 개가 언덕을 내려오는 것을 보았다."], ["Wildeve screened himself under a bush and waited.", "와일디브는 덤불 아래 몸을 숨기고 기다렸다."]], [["The vehicle came on and passed before him.", "마차가 다가와 그의 앞을 지나갔다."], ["It was a hired carriage, and behind the coachman were two persons whom he knew well.", "그것은 삯마차였고, 마부 뒤에는 그가 잘 아는 두 사람이 앉아 있었다."], ["There sat Eustacia and Yeobright, the arm of the latter being round her waist.", "거기 유스테이샤와 요브라이트가 앉아 있었으니, 후자의 팔이 그녀의 허리를 두르고 있었다."], ["They turned the sharp corner at the bottom towards the temporary home which Clym had hired and furnished, about five miles to the eastward.", "그들은 밑자락의 급커브를 돌아, 클림이 빌려 가구를 들여놓은 임시 거처 쪽으로, 동쪽으로 5마일쯤 되는 곳으로 향했다."], ["Wildeve forgot the loss of the money at the sight of his lost love, whose preciousness in his eyes was increasing in geometrical progression with each new incident that reminded him of their hopeless division.", "와일디브는 잃어버린 연인의 모습에 돈 잃은 일을 잊었으니, 그녀의 귀함은 그의 눈에, 그들의 가망 없는 갈라섬을 일깨우는 새로운 일이 생길 때마다 기하급수적으로 더해갔다."], ["Brimming with the subtilized misery that he was capable of feeling, he followed the opposite way towards the inn.", "그가 느낄 수 있는 정련된 비참함으로 가득 차서, 그는 반대 방향으로, 여인숙 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["About the same moment that Wildeve stepped into the highway Venn also had reached it at a point a hundred yards further on; and he, hearing the same wheels, likewise waited till the carriage should come up.", "와일디브가 큰길로 들어선 것과 거의 같은 순간에, 벤도 백 야드쯤 더 떨어진 지점에서 큰길에 다다랐고, 그 역시 같은 바퀴 소리를 듣고 마차가 다가올 때까지 마찬가지로 기다렸다."], ["When he saw who sat therein he seemed to be disappointed.", "그 안에 누가 앉아 있는지 보고는 실망한 듯했다."], ["Reflecting a minute or two, during which interval the carriage rolled on, he crossed the road, and took a short cut through the furze and heath to a point where the turnpike road bent round in ascending a hill.", "일이 분 곰곰이 생각하는 사이 마차가 굴러갔고, 그는 길을 건너 가시금작화와 황야를 가로질러 지름길로, 통행세 길이 언덕을 오르며 굽이도는 지점으로 갔다."], ["He was now again in front of the carriage, which presently came up at a walking pace.", "이제 그는 다시 마차 앞에 있었으니, 마차는 이내 걷는 걸음으로 다가왔다."], ["Venn stepped forward and showed himself.", "벤은 앞으로 나서서 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["Eustacia started when the lamp shone upon him, and Clym’s arm was involuntarily withdrawn from her waist.", "등불이 그를 비추자 유스테이샤가 흠칫했고, 클림의 팔이 저도 모르게 그녀의 허리에서 거두어졌다."], ["He said, “What, Diggory?", "그가 말했다. \"아니, 디고리?"], ["You are having a lonely walk.”", "외로이 걷고 있군.\""]], [["“Yes—I beg your pardon for stopping you,”", "\"예―멈춰 세워 죄송합니다.\" 벤이 말했다."], ["said Venn. “But I am waiting about for Mrs.", "\"하지만 저는 와일디브 부인을 기다리고 있습니다."], ["Wildeve: I have something to give her from Mrs.", "요브라이트 부인이 전하라는 게 있어서요."], ["Yeobright. Can you tell me if she’s gone home from the party yet?”", "부인이 잔치에서 벌써 집으로 가셨는지 아십니까?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니."], ["But she will be leaving soon.", "하지만 곧 떠날 걸세."], ["You may possibly meet her at the corner.”", "모퉁이에서 만날 수도 있겠군.\""]], [["Venn made a farewell obeisance, and walked back to his former position, where the byroad from Mistover joined the highway.", "벤은 작별 인사로 고개를 숙이고, 미스토버에서 오는 샛길이 큰길과 만나는 본래의 자리로 되돌아갔다."], ["Here he remained fixed for nearly half an hour, and then another pair of lights came down the hill.", "여기서 그는 거의 반 시간을 붙박여 있었고, 그러자 또 한 쌍의 등불이 언덕을 내려왔다."], ["It was the old-fashioned wheeled nondescript belonging to the captain, and Thomasin sat in it alone, driven by Charley.", "그것은 선장 소유의 구식 바퀴 달린 정체 모를 탈것이었고, 토머신이 홀로 그 안에 앉아 찰리가 모는 대로 있었다."]], [["The reddleman came up as they slowly turned the corner.", "레들맨은 그들이 천천히 모퉁이를 돌 때 다가갔다."], ["“I beg pardon for stopping you, Mrs.", "\"멈춰 세워 죄송합니다, 와일디브 부인.\""], ["Wildeve,” he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“But I have something to give you privately from Mrs. Yeobright.”", "\"하지만 요브라이트 부인이 부인께 따로 전하라는 게 있습니다.\""], ["He handed a small parcel; it consisted of the hundred guineas he had just won, roughly twisted up in a piece of paper.", "그는 작은 꾸러미를 건넸으니, 방금 그가 딴 백 기니가 종이 한 장에 대충 비틀어 싸인 것이었다."]], [["Thomasin recovered from her surprise, and took the packet.", "토머신은 놀라움에서 정신을 차리고 그 꾸러미를 받았다."], ["“That’s all, ma’am—I wish you good night,”", "\"그게 다입니다, 마님―안녕히 가십시오.\""], ["he said, and vanished from her view.", "그가 말하고는 그녀의 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["Thus Venn, in his anxiety to rectify matters, had placed in Thomasin’s hands not only the fifty guineas which rightly belonged to her, but also the fifty intended for her cousin Clym.", "이리하여 벤은 일을 바로잡으려는 안달 속에, 토머신의 손에 정당히 그녀의 것인 오십 기니뿐만 아니라 그녀의 사촌 클림에게 갈 오십 기니까지 쥐여준 것이었다."], ["His mistake had been based upon Wildeve’s words at the opening of the game, when he indignantly denied that the guinea was not his own.", "그의 착오는 노름 첫머리에 와일디브가 그 기니가 자기 것이 아니라는 말을 분개하며 부인했던 데에서 비롯한 것이었다."], ["It had not been comprehended by the reddleman that at halfway through the performance the game was continued with the money of another person; and it was an error which afterwards helped to cause more misfortune than treble the loss in money value could have done.", "노름이 절반쯤 진행되었을 무렵부터는 다른 사람의 돈으로 이어졌다는 사실을 레들맨이 헤아리지 못했으니, 그것은 훗날 돈의 값어치로 친 손실의 세 곱절보다도 더 큰 불행을 불러오는 데 한몫한 잘못이었다."]], [["The night was now somewhat advanced; and Venn plunged deeper into the heath, till he came to a ravine where his van was standing—a spot not more than two hundred yards from the site of the gambling bout.", "밤은 이제 어지간히 깊었고, 벤은 황야 속으로 더 깊이 파고들어, 그의 짐마차가 서 있는 협곡에 다다랐으니―노름판이 벌어진 자리에서 이백 야드도 채 안 떨어진 곳이었다."], ["He entered this movable home of his, lit his lantern, and, before closing his door for the night, stood reflecting on the circumstances of the preceding hours.", "그는 이 움직이는 보금자리로 들어가 등불을 켰고, 밤을 위해 문을 닫기 전에 서서 지난 몇 시간의 정황을 곱씹었다."], ["While he stood the dawn grew visible in the northeast quarter of the heavens, which, the clouds having cleared off, was bright with a soft sheen at this midsummer time, though it was only between one and two o’clock.", "그가 서 있는 동안 하늘 북동쪽 모퉁이로 새벽이 보이기 시작했으니, 구름이 걷힌 그곳은 이 한여름철에 부드러운 광채로 환했고, 그래도 이제 겨우 한 시에서 두 시 사이였다."], ["Venn, thoroughly weary, then shut his door and flung himself down to sleep.", "그러자 벤은 몹시 지쳐서 문을 닫고 몸을 던져 잠을 청했다."]], [["I.", "I."], ["The Rencounter by the Pool", "연못가의 우연한 만남"]], [["The July sun shone over Egdon and fired its crimson heather to scarlet.", "칠월의 태양이 에그던 위로 내리쬐어 진홍빛 히스를 새빨갛게 불태웠다."], ["It was the one season of the year, and the one weather of the season, in which the heath was gorgeous.", "한 해 가운데 단 한 철, 그 한 철 가운데 단 한 가지 날씨, 그때라야 황야는 화려했다."], ["This flowering period represented the second or noontide division in the cycle of those superficial changes which alone were possible here; it followed the green or young-fern period, representing the morn, and preceded the brown period, when the heathbells and ferns would wear the russet tinges of evening; to be in turn displaced by the dark hue of the winter period, representing night.", "이 개화기는 이곳에서만 가능한 표면적 변화들의 순환 가운데 두 번째, 곧 정오에 해당하는 구간을 나타냈으니, 그것은 아침을 나타내는 푸른 어린 고사리 시기에 뒤이어 왔으며, 히스꽃과 고사리가 저녁의 적갈색 빛깔을 두르게 되는 갈색 시기에 앞섰고, 그 갈색 시기는 다시 밤을 나타내는 겨울 시기의 짙은 색조에 자리를 내주게 될 것이었다."]], [["Clym and Eustacia, in their little house at Alderworth, beyond East Egdon, were living on with a monotony which was delightful to them.", "이스트 에그던 너머 올더워스의 작은 집에서 클림과 유스테이샤는 단조로운 나날을 이어가고 있었는데, 그 단조로움이 두 사람에게는 즐거웠다."], ["The heath and changes of weather were quite blotted out from their eyes for the present.", "황야도 날씨의 변화도 지금만큼은 그들의 눈에서 완전히 지워져 있었다."], ["They were enclosed in a sort of luminous mist, which hid from them surroundings of any inharmonious colour, and gave to all things the character of light.", "그들은 일종의 환한 안개에 둘러싸여 있었으니, 그 안개는 조화롭지 못한 색채의 주변 풍경을 그들에게서 가려주고 모든 사물에 빛의 성격을 부여했다."], ["When it rained they were charmed, because they could remain indoors together all day with such a show of reason; when it was fine they were charmed, because they could sit together on the hills.", "비가 내리면 그들은 매료되었는데, 그토록 그럴듯한 명분을 내세워 온종일 둘이서 집 안에 머물 수 있었기 때문이요, 날이 맑으면 또 매료되었는데, 둘이서 언덕에 앉아 있을 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["They were like those double stars which revolve round and round each other, and from a distance appear to be one.", "그들은 서로의 둘레를 돌고 또 도는 이중성과 같았으니, 멀리서 보면 하나로 보이는 별들이었다."], ["The absolute solitude in which they lived intensified their reciprocal thoughts; yet some might have said that it had the disadvantage of consuming their mutual affections at a fearfully prodigal rate.", "그들이 살아가는 절대적인 고독은 서로를 향한 생각을 한층 짙게 했다. 그러나 어떤 이는 그 고독이 서로를 향한 애정을 무서울 만큼 헤프게 소진시킨다는 약점을 지녔다고 말했을지도 모른다."], ["Yeobright did not fear for his own part; but recollection of Eustacia’s old speech about the evanescence of love, now apparently forgotten by her, sometimes caused him to ask himself a question; and he recoiled at the thought that the quality of finiteness was not foreign to Eden.", "요브라이트 자신은 두려워하지 않았으나, 사랑의 덧없음에 관해 유스테이샤가 예전에 했던 말—이제는 그녀가 잊은 듯한 그 말—을 떠올릴 때면 이따금 스스로에게 한 가지 물음을 던졌으며, 유한함이라는 성질이 에덴에도 낯선 것이 아니라는 생각에 그는 움찔하여 물러섰다."]], [["When three or four weeks had been passed thus, Yeobright resumed his reading in earnest.", "이렇게 서너 주가 지나자 요브라이트는 다시 본격적으로 책 읽기에 몰두했다."], ["To make up for lost time he studied indefatigably, for he wished to enter his new profession with the least possible delay.", "잃어버린 시간을 만회하려고 그는 지칠 줄 모르고 공부했으니, 새로운 직업에 가능한 한 지체 없이 발을 들이고 싶었기 때문이다."]], [["Now, Eustacia’s dream had always been that, once married to Clym, she would have the power of inducing him to return to Paris.", "그런데 유스테이샤의 꿈은 언제나 이러했으니, 일단 클림과 결혼하기만 하면 그를 구슬려 파리로 돌아가게 할 힘이 자기에게 생기리라는 것이었다."], ["He had carefully withheld all promise to do so; but would he be proof against her coaxing and argument?", "그는 그렇게 하겠다는 약속이라면 무엇이든 조심스럽게 보류해왔으나, 그녀의 어르고 달램과 설득에 끝내 버틸 수 있을 것인가?"], ["She had calculated to such a degree on the probability of success that she had represented Paris, and not Budmouth, to her grandfather as in all likelihood their future home.", "그녀는 성공 가능성을 어찌나 굳게 헤아렸던지, 할아버지에게 장차 살 곳을 알릴 때 버드머스가 아니라 파리라고, 그것이 십중팔구 그들의 보금자리가 되리라고 말해두었던 터였다."], ["Her hopes were bound up in this dream.", "그녀의 희망은 이 꿈에 송두리째 매여 있었다."], ["In the quiet days since their marriage, when Yeobright had been poring over her lips, her eyes, and the lines of her face, she had mused and mused on the subject, even while in the act of returning his gaze; and now the sight of the books, indicating a future which was antagonistic to her dream, struck her with a positively painful jar.", "결혼 이후 조용한 나날 동안, 요브라이트가 그녀의 입술과 눈과 얼굴의 선을 들여다보며 골몰해 있을 때, 그녀는 그의 시선을 마주 되돌려주는 바로 그 순간에도 이 문제를 두고 거듭거듭 골똘히 생각에 잠겼으며, 이제 자기 꿈과 적대되는 미래를 가리키는 책들을 보자 그것은 그녀에게 그야말로 고통스러운 충격으로 다가왔다."], ["She was hoping for the time when, as the mistress of some pretty establishment, however small, near a Parisian Boulevard, she would be passing her days on the skirts at least of the gay world, and catching stray wafts from those town pleasures she was so well fitted to enjoy.", "그녀는 파리의 어느 큰길 가까이에, 아무리 작더라도 어여쁜 살림집의 안주인이 되어, 적어도 화려한 사교계의 언저리에서나마 나날을 보내며, 자기가 그토록 누리기에 알맞은 그 도회의 즐거움에서 흘러나오는 향기를 이따금이라도 붙잡을 그 시절을 고대하고 있었다."], ["Yet Yeobright was as firm in the contrary intention as if the tendency of marriage were rather to develop the fantasies of young philanthropy than to sweep them away.", "그러나 요브라이트는 정반대의 결심을 굳건히 지켰으니, 마치 결혼이라는 것이 젊은 박애의 환상을 쓸어내기보다 도리어 그것을 키워내는 쪽으로 기우는 것이기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["Her anxiety reached a high pitch; but there was something in Clym’s undeviating manner which made her hesitate before sounding him on the subject.", "그녀의 불안은 한껏 높아졌으나, 클림의 한결같은 태도에는 그 문제를 두고 그를 떠보기 전에 망설이게 만드는 무언가가 있었다."], ["At this point in their experience, however, an incident helped her.", "그렇지만 두 사람이 겪는 일 가운데 이 대목에서 한 가지 사건이 그녀를 거들었다."], ["It occurred one evening about six weeks after their union, and arose entirely out of the unconscious misapplication of Venn of the fifty guineas intended for Yeobright.", "그 일은 두 사람이 맺어진 지 여섯 주쯤 지난 어느 저녁에 일어났으며, 요브라이트에게 가야 할 오십 기니를 벤이 자기도 모르게 엉뚱한 데로 돌린 데서 전적으로 비롯되었다."]], [["A day or two after the receipt of the money Thomasin had sent a note to her aunt to thank her.", "돈을 받은 지 하루 이틀 지나 토머신은 고모에게 고마움을 전하는 쪽지를 보냈다."], ["She had been surprised at the largeness of the amount; but as no sum had ever been mentioned she set that down to her late uncle’s generosity.", "그녀는 액수가 큰 데 놀랐으나, 정해진 금액이 언급된 적은 한 번도 없었던 터라 그것을 돌아가신 큰아버지의 너그러움 덕분으로 돌렸다."], ["She had been strictly charged by her aunt to say nothing to her husband of this gift; and Wildeve, as was natural enough, had not brought himself to mention to his wife a single particular of the midnight scene in the heath.", "고모는 그녀에게 이 선물에 관해 남편에게 한마디도 하지 말라고 단단히 일러두었으며, 와일디브는 또 와일디브대로—지극히 자연스러운 일이지만—황야에서 벌어진 한밤의 장면을 단 한 가지 시시콜콜한 것조차 아내에게 입에 올릴 마음이 들지 않았다."], ["Christian’s terror, in like manner, had tied his tongue on the share he took in that proceeding; and hoping that by some means or other the money had gone to its proper destination, he simply asserted as much, without giving details.", "마찬가지로 크리스천도 그 일에서 자기가 맡은 몫에 관해서는 두려움 때문에 혀가 묶였으며, 어떻게든 돈이 제 갈 곳으로 갔으리라 바라면서 자세한 내막은 밝히지 않은 채 그저 그렇게 되었다고만 우겨댔다."]], [["Therefore, when a week or two had passed away, Mrs. Yeobright began to wonder why she never heard from her son of the receipt of the present; and to add gloom to her perplexity came the possibility that resentment might be the cause of his silence.", "그리하여 한두 주가 지나자 요브라이트 부인은 어째서 아들에게서 선물을 받았다는 기별이 도무지 없는지 의아해하기 시작했으며, 그 당혹스러움에 더욱 어두운 그늘을 드리운 것은 그의 침묵의 원인이 어쩌면 원망일지도 모른다는 가능성이었다."], ["She could hardly believe as much, but why did he not write?", "그녀는 차마 그렇게는 믿기 어려웠으나, 그렇다면 어찌하여 그는 편지를 쓰지 않는가?"], ["She questioned Christian, and the confusion in his answers would at once have led her to believe that something was wrong, had not one-half of his story been corroborated by Thomasin’s note.", "그녀가 크리스천에게 캐묻자, 그의 대답에 깃든 혼란은 곧장 무언가 잘못되었다고 그녀가 믿게 만들 만했으나, 그의 이야기 절반이 토머신의 쪽지로 뒷받침된 까닭에 그렇게까지는 가지 않았다."]], [["Mrs. Yeobright was in this state of uncertainty when she was informed one morning that her son’s wife was visiting her grandfather at Mistover.", "요브라이트 부인이 이런 미심쩍은 상태에 있던 어느 아침, 그녀는 아들의 아내가 미스토버에서 할아버지를 찾아뵙고 있다는 소식을 들었다."], ["She determined to walk up the hill, see Eustacia, and ascertain from her daughter-in-law’s lips whether the family guineas, which were to Mrs. Yeobright what family jewels are to wealthier dowagers, had miscarried or not.", "그녀는 언덕을 걸어 올라가 유스테이샤를 만나, 며느리의 입을 통해 집안의 기니가—그 기니는 요브라이트 부인에게 더 부유한 미망인들에게 집안의 보석이 갖는 것과 같은 의미였다—잘못 전해졌는지 아닌지를 알아내기로 마음먹었다."]], [["When Christian learnt where she was going his concern reached its height.", "크리스천은 그녀가 어디로 가는지 알게 되자 걱정이 극에 달했다."], ["At the moment of her departure he could prevaricate no longer, and, confessing to the gambling, told her the truth as far as he knew it—that the guineas had been won by Wildeve.", "그녀가 떠나려는 순간 그는 더는 둘러댈 수 없어, 노름에 손댄 일을 실토하고는 자기가 아는 한도 내에서 사실을 털어놓았으니—그 기니는 와일디브가 따 갔다는 것이었다."]], [["“What, is he going to keep them?”", "\"뭐라고, 그자가 그걸 가지려 한다는 말이냐?\""], ["Mrs. Yeobright cried.", "요브라이트 부인이 소리쳤다."]], [["“I hope and trust not!”", "\"부디 그러지 않기를 바랍니다요!\""], ["moaned Christian.", "크리스천이 끙끙거렸다."], ["“He’s a good man, and perhaps will do right things.", "\"그분은 좋은 분이니, 어쩌면 옳은 일을 하실 거예요."], ["He said you ought to have gied Mr. Clym’s share to Eustacia, and that’s perhaps what he’ll do himself.”", "클림 도련님 몫은 마님이 유스테이샤 아씨에게 주셨어야 했다고 그러시더니, 어쩌면 자기가 직접 그렇게 하실지도 모르지요.\""]], [["To Mrs. Yeobright, as soon as she could calmly reflect, there was much likelihood in this, for she could hardly believe that Wildeve would really appropriate money belonging to her son.", "요브라이트 부인은 차분히 곱씹어볼 수 있게 되자, 이 말에 그럴듯한 구석이 많다고 느꼈으니, 와일디브가 아들의 돈을 정말로 제 것으로 가로채리라고는 차마 믿기 어려웠기 때문이다."], ["The intermediate course of giving it to Eustacia was the sort of thing to please Wildeve’s fancy.", "그것을 유스테이샤에게 넘겨준다는 그 중간의 행로야말로 와일디브의 변덕을 흡족하게 할 만한 종류의 일이었다."], ["But it filled the mother with anger none the less.", "그러나 그렇다고 해서 어머니의 가슴이 분노로 가득 차지 않은 것은 아니었다."], ["That Wildeve should have got command of the guineas after all, and should rearrange the disposal of them, placing Clym’s share in Clym’s wife’s hands, because she had been his own sweetheart, and might be so still, was as irritating a pain as any that Mrs. Yeobright had ever borne.", "결국 와일디브가 그 기니를 손에 넣고는, 클림의 몫을 클림의 아내 손에—그녀가 한때 자기 연인이었고 어쩌면 지금도 그러할지 모른다는 이유로—쥐여주며 그 처분을 제멋대로 다시 정하려 한다는 것은, 요브라이트 부인이 여태껏 견뎌낸 그 어떤 것 못지않게 속을 긁는 고통이었다."]], [["She instantly dismissed the wretched Christian from her employ for his conduct in the affair; but, feeling quite helpless and unable to do without him, told him afterwards that he might stay a little longer if he chose.", "그녀는 그 일에서 보인 처신을 두고 가련한 크리스천을 당장 일자리에서 내쫓았으나, 그가 없으면 도무지 어찌할 도리가 없겠다 싶어 속수무책인 심정이 들자, 나중에는 그가 원한다면 조금 더 머물러도 좋다고 일렀다."], ["Then she hastened off to Eustacia, moved by a much less promising emotion towards her daughter-in-law than she had felt half an hour earlier, when planning her journey.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 유스테이샤를 향해 서둘러 떠났는데, 며느리를 향한 마음은 반 시간 전 길 떠날 채비를 하던 때 품었던 것보다 훨씬 덜 너그러운 감정에 떠밀려 있었다."], ["At that time it was to inquire in a friendly spirit if there had been any accidental loss; now it was to ask plainly if Wildeve had privately given her money which had been intended as a sacred gift to Clym.", "그때만 해도 우연히 잃어버린 것은 없는지 다정한 마음으로 물어보려는 것이었으나, 이제는 클림에게 바칠 신성한 선물로 정해졌던 돈을 와일디브가 그녀에게 몰래 건넨 것이 아닌지 대놓고 따져 물으려는 것이었다."]], [["She started at two o’clock, and her meeting with Eustacia was hastened by the appearance of the young lady beside the pool and bank which bordered her grandfather’s premises, where she stood surveying the scene, and perhaps thinking of the romantic enactments it had witnessed in past days.", "그녀는 두 시에 길을 나섰고, 유스테이샤와의 만남은 그 젊은 아가씨가 할아버지 집터를 두른 연못과 둑가에 나타나 앞당겨졌으니, 그녀는 거기 서서 풍경을 바라보며 어쩌면 지난날 그곳이 지켜본 낭만적인 장면들을 떠올리고 있었는지도 모른다."], ["When Mrs. Yeobright approached, Eustacia surveyed her with the calm stare of a stranger.", "요브라이트 부인이 다가가자 유스테이샤는 낯선 사람을 보듯 차분히 빤히 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["The mother-in-law was the first to speak.", "먼저 입을 연 것은 시어머니였다."], ["“I was coming to see you,”", "\"너를 보러 오는 길이었다.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"어머나!\""], ["said Eustacia with surprise, for Mrs. Yeobright, much to the girl’s mortification, had refused to be present at the wedding.", "유스테이샤가 놀라며 말했는데, 요브라이트 부인이 결혼식에 참석하기를 거절했던 일이 그 아가씨에게는 여간 모욕스러운 것이 아니었던 까닭이다."], ["“I did not at all expect you.”", "\"전혀 뜻밖이네요.\""]], [["“I was coming on business only,”", "\"긴히 볼일이 있어 왔을 뿐이다.\""], ["said the visitor, more coldly than at first.", "손님이 처음보다 더 차갑게 말했다."], ["“Will you excuse my asking this—Have you received a gift from Thomasin’s husband?”", "\"이걸 묻는 걸 양해해다오—토머신의 남편한테서 선물을 받은 일이 있느냐?\""]], [["“A gift?”", "\"선물요?\""]], [["“I mean money!”", "\"돈 말이다!\""]], [["“What—I myself?”", "\"뭐라고요—제가 말씀이세요?\""]], [["“Well, I meant yourself, privately—though I was not going to put it in that way.”", "\"그래, 너 말이다, 몰래 받은 것 말이야—이런 식으로 말할 생각은 아니었다만.\""]], [["“Money from Mr. Wildeve?", "\"와일디브 씨한테서 돈을요?"], ["No—never!", "아뇨—천만에요!"], ["Madam, what do you mean by that?”", "부인, 그게 무슨 말씀이세요?\""], ["Eustacia fired up all too quickly, for her own consciousness of the old attachment between herself and Wildeve led her to jump to the conclusion that Mrs. Yeobright also knew of it, and might have come to accuse her of receiving dishonourable presents from him now.", "유스테이샤는 너무도 성급히 발끈했으니, 자기와 와일디브 사이의 옛 정을 스스로 의식하고 있던 탓에 요브라이트 부인 역시 그것을 알고 있어, 이제 그에게서 떳떳지 못한 선물을 받았다고 자기를 나무라러 온 것이라고 지레 결론으로 치달았기 때문이다."]], [["“I simply ask the question,”", "\"그저 물어보는 것뿐이다.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright.", "요브라이트 부인이 말했다."], ["“I have been——”", "\"나는 그동안——\""]], [["“You ought to have better opinions of me—I feared you were against me from the first!”", "\"저를 좀 더 좋게 봐주셔야 했어요—처음부터 저를 못마땅하게 여기시는 게 아닐까 두려웠어요!\""], ["exclaimed Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 외쳤다."]], [["“No.", "\"아니다."], ["I was simply for Clym,”", "나는 그저 클림 편이었을 뿐이야.\""], ["replied Mrs. Yeobright, with too much emphasis in her earnestness.", "요브라이트 부인이 대답했는데, 그 진지함에는 힘이 지나치게 실려 있었다."], ["“It is the instinct of everyone to look after their own.”", "\"제 자식을 챙기는 건 누구에게나 있는 본능이지.\""]], [["“How can you imply that he required guarding against me?”", "\"제게서 그 애를 지켜야 했다는 듯이 어찌 그런 암시를 하실 수 있으세요?\""], ["cried Eustacia, passionate tears in her eyes.", "유스테이샤가 눈에 격정의 눈물을 띤 채 외쳤다."], ["“I have not injured him by marrying him!", "\"저는 그 사람과 결혼해서 그 사람을 해친 일이 없어요!"], ["What sin have I done that you should think so ill of me?", "제가 무슨 죄를 지었기에 저를 그토록 나쁘게 여기시는 거예요?"], ["You had no right to speak against me to him when I have never wronged you.”", "저는 부인께 잘못한 적이 없는데, 부인께서는 그 사람한테 저를 헐뜯어 말할 권리가 없으셨어요.\""]], [["“I only did what was fair under the circumstances,”", "\"나는 그저 상황에 비추어 마땅한 일을 했을 뿐이다.\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright more softly.", "요브라이트 부인이 한결 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“I would rather not have gone into this question at present, but you compel me.", "\"지금 이 문제를 끄집어내고 싶지는 않았다만, 네가 나를 떠미는구나."], ["I am not ashamed to tell you the honest truth.", "나는 정직한 진실을 네게 말하기가 부끄럽지 않다."], ["I was firmly convinced that he ought not to marry you—therefore I tried to dissuade him by all the means in my power.", "나는 그 애가 너와 결혼해서는 안 된다고 굳게 믿었어—그래서 내 힘이 닿는 온갖 방법으로 그 애를 말리려 했지."], ["But it is done now, and I have no idea of complaining any more.", "하지만 이제 다 된 일이고, 더는 불평할 생각이 없어."], ["I am ready to welcome you.”", "나는 너를 기꺼이 맞아들일 준비가 되어 있다.\""]], [["“Ah, yes, it is very well to see things in that business point of view,”", "\"아, 그래요, 일을 그렇게 거래의 관점에서 보시면야 더할 나위 없겠죠.\""], ["murmured Eustacia with a smothered fire of feeling.", "유스테이샤가 짓눌린 감정의 불길을 품고 중얼거렸다."], ["“But why should you think there is anything between me and Mr. Wildeve?", "\"하지만 저와 와일디브 씨 사이에 무슨 일이 있다고 어째서 생각하시는 거예요?"], ["I have a spirit as well as you.", "저도 부인 못지않게 자존심이 있어요."], ["I am indignant; and so would any woman be.", "저는 분개하고 있고, 어떤 여자라도 그럴 거예요."], ["It was a condescension in me to be Clym’s wife, and not a manœuvre, let me remind you; and therefore I will not be treated as a schemer whom it becomes necessary to bear with because she has crept into the family.”", "클림의 아내가 된 건 제 쪽에서 몸을 낮춘 거지 술책이 아니었다는 걸 일러두겠어요. 그러니 저는 집안에 슬그머니 기어들었으니 어쩔 수 없이 참아주어야 하는 모사꾼 취급을 받지는 않겠어요.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["said Mrs. Yeobright, vainly endeavouring to control her anger.", "요브라이트 부인이 부질없이 분노를 억누르려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["“I have never heard anything to show that my son’s lineage is not as good as the Vyes’—perhaps better.", "\"내 아들의 혈통이 바이 집안만 못하다는 말은 여태 들어본 적이 없다—어쩌면 더 나을 수도 있지."], ["It is amusing to hear you talk of condescension.”", "네가 몸을 낮췄다고 말하는 걸 들으니 우습구나.\""]], [["“It was condescension, nevertheless,”", "\"그래도 몸을 낮춘 거예요.\""], ["said Eustacia vehemently.", "유스테이샤가 격하게 말했다."], ["“And if I had known then what I know now, that I should be living in this wild heath a month after my marriage, I—I should have thought twice before agreeing.”", "\"그리고 지금 아는 걸 그때 알았더라면, 곧 결혼한 지 한 달 만에 이런 황량한 황야에서 살게 되리라는 걸 알았더라면, 저는—저는 승낙하기 전에 두 번은 생각했을 거예요.\""]], [["“It would be better not to say that; it might not sound truthful.", "\"그런 말은 안 하는 편이 좋겠구나. 진실하게 들리지 않을 수도 있으니."], ["I am not aware that any deception was used on his part—I know there was not—whatever might have been the case on the other side.”", "그 애 쪽에서 무슨 속임수가 쓰였다는 건 나는 알지 못한다—없었던 걸 알아—다른 쪽에서야 어떠했든 간에 말이다.\""]], [["“This is too exasperating!”", "\"이건 너무 화가 나는 일이에요!\""], ["answered the younger woman huskily, her face crimsoning, and her eyes darting light.", "젊은 여자가 목이 잠긴 채 대답했는데, 얼굴은 새빨개지고 두 눈에서는 빛이 쏟아졌다."], ["“How can you dare to speak to me like that?", "\"어떻게 감히 제게 그런 식으로 말씀하실 수 있어요?"], ["I insist upon repeating to you that had I known that my life would from my marriage up to this time have been as it is, I should have said _No_.", "다시 한번 분명히 말씀드릴게요, 결혼한 뒤로 지금까지 제 삶이 이러하리라는 걸 알았더라면 저는 싫다고 했을 거예요."], ["I don’t complain.", "저는 불평하는 게 아니에요."], ["I have never uttered a sound of such a thing to him; but it is true.", "그 사람한테는 그런 말을 한마디도 입 밖에 낸 적이 없어요. 하지만 사실이 그래요."], ["I hope therefore that in the future you will be silent on my eagerness.", "그러니 앞으로는 제가 결혼을 간절히 바랐던 일에 대해 입을 다물어주시길 바라요."], ["If you injure me now you injure yourself.”", "지금 저를 해치시면 부인 자신을 해치시는 거예요.\""]], [["“Injure you?", "\"너를 해친다고?"], ["Do you think I am an evil-disposed person?”", "내가 못된 마음을 품은 사람이라고 생각하느냐?\""]], [["“You injured me before my marriage, and you have now suspected me of secretly favouring another man for money!”", "\"결혼 전에도 저를 해치셨고, 이제는 제가 돈 때문에 다른 남자한테 몰래 마음을 두었다고 의심하셨잖아요!\""]], [["“I could not help what I thought.", "\"내가 생각한 걸 어쩔 수는 없었다."], ["But I have never spoken of you outside my house.”", "하지만 내 집 밖에서는 네 이야기를 입에 올린 적이 없어.\""]], [["“You spoke of me within it, to Clym, and you could not do worse.”", "\"집 안에서는 클림한테 제 이야기를 하셨고, 그보다 더한 짓은 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“I did my duty.” “And I’ll do mine.”", "\"나는 내 도리를 다했을 뿐이다.\""]], [["“A part of which will possibly be to set him against his mother.", "\"그럼 저도 제 도리를 다하겠어요.\" \"그 도리의 일부는 아마 그 애를 제 어미한테 등 돌리게 하는 일이겠지."], ["It is always so.", "늘 그런 법이니까."], ["But why should I not bear it as others have borne it before me!”", "하지만 나보다 앞서 다른 이들이 견뎌온 것을 내가 어찌 못 견디겠느냐!\""]], [["“I understand you,”", "\"무슨 뜻인지 알겠어요.\""], ["said Eustacia, breathless with emotion.", "유스테이샤가 감정에 겨워 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["“You think me capable of every bad thing.", "\"부인께서는 제가 온갖 못된 짓을 다 할 수 있는 사람이라고 여기시는군요."], ["Who can be worse than a wife who encourages a lover, and poisons her husband’s mind against his relative?", "정부情夫를 부추기고 남편의 마음에 그 친척을 두고 독을 풀어 넣는 아내보다 더 못된 사람이 어디 있겠어요?"], ["Yet that is now the character given to me.", "그런데 그게 지금 제게 씌워진 인물됨이로군요."], ["Will you not come and drag him out of my hands?”", "그 사람을 제 손아귀에서 끌어내가지 않으시겠어요?\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright gave back heat for heat.", "요브라이트 부인은 열기에 열기로 되받았다."]], [["“Don’t rage at me, madam!", "\"내게 화를 내지 마라, 얘야!"], ["It ill becomes your beauty, and I am not worth the injury you may do it on my account, I assure you.", "그건 네 아름다움에 어울리지 않거니와, 나 때문에 네가 그 아름다움을 해칠 만한 값어치가 내게는 없으니 말이다, 정말이지."], ["I am only a poor old woman who has lost a son.”", "나는 그저 아들을 잃은 가련한 늙은이일 뿐이야.\""]], [["“If you had treated me honourably you would have had him still.”", "\"저를 떳떳하게 대해주셨더라면 그 사람을 여전히 곁에 두셨을 거예요.\""], ["Eustacia said, while scalding tears trickled from her eyes.", "유스테이샤가 말하는 동안 데일 듯이 뜨거운 눈물이 두 눈에서 주르륵 흘러내렸다."], ["“You have brought yourself to folly; you have caused a division which can never be healed!”", "\"부인 스스로 어리석은 짓에 빠지신 거예요. 결코 아물 수 없는 균열을 일으키신 거라고요!\""]], [["“I have done nothing.", "\"나는 아무것도 한 게 없다."], ["This audacity from a young woman is more than I can bear.”", "젊은 여자가 이리 뻔뻔하게 구는 걸 나는 견딜 수가 없구나.\""]], [["“It was asked for; you have suspected me, and you have made me speak of my husband in a way I would not have done.", "\"자초하신 일이에요. 저를 의심하셨고, 제가 하지 않았을 방식으로 제 남편 얘기를 하게 만드셨잖아요."], ["You will let him know that I have spoken thus, and it will cause misery between us.", "제가 이렇게 말했다는 걸 그 사람한테 알리실 테고, 그러면 우리 사이에 불행이 생길 거예요."], ["Will you go away from me?", "제게서 물러가시겠어요?"], ["You are no friend!”", "부인은 제 편이 아니에요!\""]], [["“I will go when I have spoken a word.", "\"한마디만 하고 가마."], ["If anyone says I have come here to question you without good grounds for it, that person speaks untruly.", "누구든 내가 정당한 까닭 없이 너를 따지러 여기 왔다고 말한다면, 그 사람은 거짓을 말하는 게다."], ["If anyone says that I attempted to stop your marriage by any but honest means, that person, too, does not speak the truth.", "누구든 내가 정직하지 못한 수단으로 네 결혼을 막으려 했다고 말한다면, 그 사람 역시 진실을 말하는 게 아니야."], ["I have fallen on an evil time; God has been unjust to me in letting you insult me!", "나는 사나운 때를 만난 게다. 네가 나를 모욕하도록 내버려두시다니 하느님이 내게 부당하셨어!"], ["Probably my son’s happiness does not lie on this side of the grave, for he is a foolish man who neglects the advice of his parent.", "아마 내 아들의 행복은 무덤 이쪽에는 없는가 보다. 부모의 충고를 저버리는 자는 어리석은 자이니 말이다."], ["You, Eustacia, stand on the edge of a precipice without knowing it.", "유스테이샤, 너는 그런 줄도 모르고 벼랑 끝에 서 있는 게야."], ["Only show my son one-half the temper you have shown me today—and you may before long—and you will find that though he is as gentle as a child with you now, he can be as hard as steel!”", "오늘 내게 보인 성미의 절반만이라도 내 아들에게 보여보아라—머지않아 그럴지도 모르지—그러면 알게 될 게다, 지금은 그 애가 네게 어린아이처럼 순하다 해도 강철처럼 단단해질 수 있다는 걸!\""]], [["The excited mother then withdrew, and Eustacia, panting, stood looking into the pool.", "흥분한 어머니는 그러고 나서 물러갔고, 유스테이샤는 숨을 헐떡이며 서서 연못을 들여다보았다."]], [["II.", "II."], ["He Is Set upon by Adversities but He Sings a Song", "그는 역경에 시달리나 노래를 부른다"]], [["The result of that unpropitious interview was that Eustacia, instead of passing the afternoon with her grandfather, hastily returned home to Clym, where she arrived three hours earlier than she had been expected.", "그 불길한 만남의 결과로, 유스테이샤는 오후를 할아버지와 보내는 대신 황급히 클림에게로 돌아왔으니, 예상보다 세 시간이나 일찍 집에 닿았다."], ["She came indoors with her face flushed, and her eyes still showing traces of her recent excitement.", "그녀는 얼굴이 상기되고 두 눈에는 방금 전 격앙의 흔적이 아직 남은 채 집 안으로 들어섰다."], ["Yeobright looked up astonished; he had never seen her in any way approaching to that state before.", "요브라이트는 놀라 올려다보았는데, 그녀가 그 비슷한 상태에 이른 모습은 여태껏 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."], ["She passed him by, and would have gone upstairs unnoticed, but Clym was so concerned that he immediately followed her.", "그녀는 그를 지나쳐 눈에 띄지 않게 위층으로 올라가려 했으나, 클림이 어찌나 마음이 쓰였던지 곧장 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“What is the matter, Eustacia?”", "\"무슨 일이오, 유스테이샤?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["She was standing on the hearthrug in the bedroom, looking upon the floor, her hands clasped in front of her, her bonnet yet unremoved.", "그녀는 침실의 난롯가 깔개 위에 서서 바닥을 내려다보며, 두 손을 앞으로 맞잡은 채, 모자도 아직 벗지 않은 채로 있었다."], ["For a moment she did not answer; and then she replied in a low voice—", "한순간 그녀는 대답하지 않았고, 그러고 나서 낮은 목소리로 답했다—"]], [["“I have seen your mother; and I will never see her again!”", "\"당신 어머니를 만났어요. 그리고 다시는 그분을 만나지 않겠어요!\""]], [["A weight fell like a stone upon Clym.", "돌덩이 같은 무게가 클림을 짓눌렀다."], ["That same morning, when Eustacia had arranged to go and see her grandfather, Clym had expressed a wish that she would drive down to Blooms-End and inquire for her mother-in-law, or adopt any other means she might think fit to bring about a reconciliation.", "바로 그날 아침, 유스테이샤가 할아버지를 뵈러 가기로 했을 때, 클림은 그녀가 블룸스엔드로 마차를 몰고 내려가 시어머니의 안부를 묻거나, 화해를 이끌어낼 만하다고 여겨지는 다른 어떤 방법이든 취해주었으면 하는 바람을 내비쳤던 터였다."], ["She had set out gaily; and he had hoped for much.", "그녀는 즐거이 길을 나섰고, 그는 많은 것을 기대했었다."]], [["“Why is this?”", "\"왜 이러는 거요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“I cannot tell—I cannot remember.", "\"말 못 하겠어요—기억도 안 나요."], ["I met your mother.", "당신 어머니를 만났어요."], ["And I will never meet her again.”", "그리고 다시는 그분을 만나지 않겠어요.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["“What do I know about Mr. Wildeve now?", "\"제가 이제 와일디브 씨에 대해 뭘 안다고요?"], ["I won’t have wicked opinions passed on me by anybody. O!", "누구한테도 저를 두고 못된 험담을 듣지 않겠어요."], ["it was too humiliating to be asked if I had received any money from him, or encouraged him, or something of the sort—I don’t exactly know what!”", "오! 그한테서 돈을 받았느냐, 그를 부추겼느냐, 뭐 그런 비슷한 걸 묻다니 너무 굴욕적이었어요—정확히 뭔지도 모르겠어요!\""]], [["“How could she have asked you that?”", "\"어떻게 어머니가 당신한테 그런 걸 물을 수 있었지?\""]], [["“She did.”", "\"그러셨어요.\""]], [["“Then there must have been some meaning in it.", "\"그렇다면 거기엔 무슨 까닭이 있었을 거요."], ["What did my mother say besides?”", "어머니가 그 밖에 또 뭐라고 하셨소?\""]], [["“I don’t know what she said, except in so far as this, that we both said words which can never be forgiven!”", "\"무슨 말씀을 하셨는지 모르겠어요, 다만 이것만은요, 우리 둘 다 결코 용서받을 수 없는 말들을 했다는 것!\""]], [["“Oh, there must be some misapprehension.", "\"오, 거기엔 무슨 오해가 있었던 게 분명하오."], ["Whose fault was it that her meaning was not made clear?”", "어머니 말씀의 뜻이 분명히 전해지지 않은 게 누구 잘못이었소?\""]], [["“I would rather not say.", "\"말하고 싶지 않아요."], ["It may have been the fault of the circumstances, which were awkward at the very least.", "상황 탓이었을지도 모르죠, 적어도 어색하기 짝이 없었으니까요."], ["O Clym—I cannot help expressing it—this is an unpleasant position that you have placed me in.", "오 클림—말하지 않을 수가 없네요—이건 당신이 저를 몰아넣은 달갑잖은 처지예요."], ["But you must improve it—yes, say you will—for I hate it all now!", "하지만 당신은 이걸 나아지게 해야 해요—그래요, 그러겠다고 말해줘요—이제 이게 다 싫으니까요!"], ["Yes, take me to Paris, and go on with your old occupation, Clym!", "그래요, 저를 파리로 데려가서 예전 일을 다시 하세요, 클림!"], ["I don’t mind how humbly we live there at first, if it can only be Paris, and not Egdon Heath.”", "처음엔 거기서 아무리 초라하게 살아도 상관없어요, 그게 에그던 황야가 아니라 파리이기만 하다면요.\""]], [["“But I have quite given up that idea,”", "\"하지만 나는 그 생각을 아주 접었소.\""], ["said Yeobright, with surprise.", "요브라이트가 놀라며 말했다."], ["“Surely I never led you to expect such a thing?”", "\"내가 당신한테 그런 걸 기대하게 만든 적은 결코 없을 텐데?\""]], [["“I own it.", "\"인정해요."], ["Yet there are thoughts which cannot be kept out of mind, and that one was mine.", "그래도 마음에서 떨쳐낼 수 없는 생각이라는 게 있고, 그게 제 생각이었어요."], ["Must I not have a voice in the matter, now I am your wife and the sharer of your doom?”", "이제 제가 당신 아내이고 당신 운명을 함께 짊어진 사람인데, 이 문제에 목소리를 낼 수 없는 건가요?\""]], [["“Well, there are things which are placed beyond the pale of discussion; and I thought this was specially so, and by mutual agreement.”", "\"글쎄, 논의의 울타리 밖에 놓인 일들이라는 게 있는 법이오. 나는 이것이 특히 그러하다고, 그것도 서로 합의한 일이라고 여겼소.\""]], [["“Clym, I am unhappy at what I hear,”", "\"클림, 그 말을 들으니 비참해요.\""], ["she said in a low voice; and her eyes drooped, and she turned away.", "그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했고, 두 눈을 떨구더니 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["This indication of an unexpected mine of hope in Eustacia’s bosom disconcerted her husband.", "유스테이샤의 가슴속에 뜻밖의 희망의 광맥이 묻혀 있었다는 이 징후는 그녀의 남편을 당황하게 했다."], ["It was the first time that he had confronted the fact of the indirectness of a woman’s movement towards her desire.", "여자가 욕망을 향해 에둘러 움직인다는 사실과 마주한 것은 이번이 처음이었다."], ["But his intention was unshaken, though he loved Eustacia well.", "그러나 그는 유스테이샤를 깊이 사랑했음에도 그 결심만큼은 흔들리지 않았다."], ["All the effect that her remark had upon him was a resolve to chain himself more closely than ever to his books, so as to be the sooner enabled to appeal to substantial results from another course in arguing against her whim.", "그녀의 말이 그에게 미친 영향이라고는, 그녀의 변덕에 맞서 다른 길에서 거둔 실질적인 성과에 호소할 수 있도록 한시바삐 자신을 책에 더욱 단단히 옭아매겠다는 결심뿐이었다."]], [["Next day the mystery of the guineas was explained.", "이튿날 기니의 수수께끼가 풀렸다."], ["Thomasin paid them a hurried visit, and Clym’s share was delivered up to him by her own hands.", "토머신이 황급히 다녀갔고, 클림의 몫은 그녀가 직접 그의 손에 건네주었다."], ["Eustacia was not present at the time.", "그때 유스테이샤는 자리에 없었다."]], [["“Then this is what my mother meant,”", "\"그러니까 이게 어머니가 뜻하신 거였군.\""], ["exclaimed Clym.", "클림이 외쳤다."], ["“Thomasin, do you know that they have had a bitter quarrel?”", "\"토머신, 그분들이 모진 다툼을 벌인 걸 알아?\""]], [["There was a little more reticence now than formerly in Thomasin’s manner towards her cousin.", "토머신이 사촌을 대하는 태도에는 이제 예전보다 다소 조심스러움이 더해져 있었다."], ["It is the effect of marriage to engender in several directions some of the reserve it annihilates in one.", "결혼이란 한 방향에서 없애버린 신중함을 여러 방향에서 새로 자아내는 효과가 있는 법이다. \"고모님이 제게 말씀해주셨어요.\""], ["“Your mother told me,”", "그녀가 조용히 말했다."], ["she said quietly. “She came back to my house after seeing Eustacia.”", "\"유스테이샤를 만나신 뒤에 우리 집으로 다시 오셨거든요.\""]], [["“The worst thing I dreaded has come to pass.", "\"내가 가장 두려워하던 일이 벌어지고 말았군."], ["Was Mother much disturbed when she came to you, Thomasin?”", "너한테 오셨을 때 어머니가 많이 심란해하셨니, 토머신?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Very much indeed?”", "\"아주 많이?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["Clym leant his elbow upon the post of the garden gate, and covered his eyes with his hand.", "클림은 정원 문기둥에 팔꿈치를 기대고 손으로 두 눈을 가렸다."]], [["“Don’t trouble about it, Clym.", "\"너무 마음 쓰지 마세요, 클림."], ["They may get to be friends.”", "두 분이 친구가 되실 수도 있어요.\""]], [["He shook his head.", "그는 고개를 저었다."], ["“Not two people with inflammable natures like theirs.", "\"저 두 분처럼 불같은 성정을 지닌 사람들끼리는 안 돼."], ["Well, what must be will be.”", "뭐, 될 일은 되겠지.\""]], [["“One thing is cheerful in it—the guineas are not lost.”", "\"그래도 한 가지 다행스러운 게 있어요—기니는 잃지 않았잖아요.\""]], [["“I would rather have lost them twice over than have had this happen.”", "\"이런 일이 벌어지느니 차라리 그 돈을 두 번 잃는 편이 나았을 텐데.\""]], [["Amid these jarring events Yeobright felt one thing to be indispensable—that he should speedily make some show of progress in his scholastic plans.", "이렇게 어긋나는 일들 가운데서 요브라이트는 한 가지가 꼭 필요하다고 느꼈으니—자신의 학업 계획에서 어떤 식으로든 어서 진전을 보여야 한다는 것이었다."], ["With this view he read far into the small hours during many nights.", "그런 생각으로 그는 여러 밤 동안 새벽녘까지 책에 깊이 파고들었다."]], [["One morning, after a severer strain than usual, he awoke with a strange sensation in his eyes.", "여느 때보다 더 혹독하게 무리한 어느 아침, 그는 두 눈에 야릇한 느낌이 들며 잠에서 깨었다."], ["The sun was shining directly upon the window-blind, and at his first glance thitherward a sharp pain obliged him to close his eyelids quickly.", "햇빛이 창문 가리개에 곧장 내리쬐고 있었고, 그쪽으로 첫 눈길을 던지자 날카로운 통증에 그는 어쩔 수 없이 얼른 눈꺼풀을 감았다."], ["At every new attempt to look about him the same morbid sensibility to light was manifested, and excoriating tears ran down his cheeks.", "주위를 둘러보려고 새로 시도할 때마다 빛에 대한 똑같은 병적인 예민함이 드러났고, 살이 벗겨질 듯 따가운 눈물이 두 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."], ["He was obliged to tie a bandage over his brow while dressing; and during the day it could not be abandoned.", "그는 옷을 입는 동안 이마에 붕대를 매어야 했으며, 낮 동안에도 그것을 떼어낼 수 없었다."], ["Eustacia was thoroughly alarmed.", "유스테이샤는 몹시 놀랐다."], ["On finding that the case was no better the next morning they decided to send to Anglebury for a surgeon.", "이튿날 아침에도 차도가 없자 두 사람은 앵글베리로 사람을 보내 의사를 부르기로 했다."]], [["Towards evening he arrived, and pronounced the disease to be acute inflammation induced by Clym’s night studies, continued in spite of a cold previously caught, which had weakened his eyes for the time.", "저녁 무렵 그가 도착하여, 이 병은 클림의 밤샘 공부로 인해 생긴 급성 염증이라고 진단했으니, 그 공부가 앞서 걸린 감기에도 아랑곳없이 이어진 탓에 한동안 그의 눈이 약해져 있었던 것이다."], ["Fretting with impatience at this interruption to a task he was so anxious to hasten, Clym was transformed into an invalid.", "그토록 서두르고 싶던 일이 중단된 데 안달하며 애를 태우던 클림은 환자 신세로 바뀌었다."], ["He was shut up in a room from which all light was excluded, and his condition would have been one of absolute misery had not Eustacia read to him by the glimmer of a shaded lamp.", "그는 빛이 일절 들지 않는 방에 갇혀 지냈고, 만일 유스테이샤가 갓 씌운 등불의 희미한 빛에 의지해 책을 읽어주지 않았더라면 그의 처지는 그야말로 비참했을 것이다."], ["He hoped that the worst would soon be over; but at the surgeon’s third visit he learnt to his dismay that although he might venture out of doors with shaded eyes in the course of a month, all thought of pursuing his work, or of reading print of any description, would have to be given up for a long time to come.", "그는 최악의 고비가 곧 지나가기를 바랐으나, 의사의 세 번째 방문 때 낙담스럽게도 알게 된바, 한 달쯤 지나면 눈을 가린 채 바깥출입은 해볼 수 있겠으나, 자기 일을 계속하거나 어떤 종류의 인쇄물이든 읽으려는 생각은 앞으로 오랫동안 단념해야 하리라는 것이었다."]], [["One week and another week wore on, and nothing seemed to lighten the gloom of the young couple.", "한 주 또 한 주가 흘러갔으나, 그 무엇도 젊은 부부의 우울을 덜어주는 것 같지 않았다."], ["Dreadful imaginings occurred to Eustacia, but she carefully refrained from uttering them to her husband.", "끔찍한 상상들이 유스테이샤에게 떠올랐으나, 그녀는 그것을 남편에게 입에 올리지 않으려 조심스레 삼갔다."], ["Suppose he should become blind, or, at all events, never recover sufficient strength of sight to engage in an occupation which would be congenial to her feelings, and conduce to her removal from this lonely dwelling among the hills?", "만일 그가 눈이 멀게 된다면, 아니 적어도 그녀의 마음에 맞고 이 언덕 사이 외딴집에서 그녀를 데리고 나가는 데 도움이 될 직업에 종사할 만큼 시력을 다시 회복하지 못한다면?"], ["That dream of beautiful Paris was not likely to cohere into substance in the presence of this misfortune.", "아름다운 파리의 그 꿈은 이 불행 앞에서 실체로 엉겨붙을 가망이 없었다."], ["As day after day passed by, and he got no better, her mind ran more and more in this mournful groove, and she would go away from him into the garden and weep despairing tears.", "하루하루 지나도 그가 나아지지 않자, 그녀의 마음은 점점 더 이 구슬픈 홈을 따라 굴러갔고, 그녀는 그에게서 벗어나 정원으로 나가 절망의 눈물을 흘리곤 했다."]], [["Yeobright thought he would send for his mother; and then he thought he would not.", "요브라이트는 어머니를 부를까 생각했다가, 이내 부르지 않기로 생각을 고쳐먹었다."], ["Knowledge of his state could only make her the more unhappy; and the seclusion of their life was such that she would hardly be likely to learn the news except through a special messenger.", "자기 처지를 안다 한들 어머니를 더 불행하게 만들 뿐이요, 두 사람의 삶이 어찌나 외진지라 특별히 심부름꾼을 보내지 않고서는 어머니가 그 소식을 알 일도 좀처럼 없을 터였다."], ["Endeavouring to take the trouble as philosophically as possible, he waited on till the third week had arrived, when he went into the open air for the first time since the attack.", "이 고난을 되도록 의연하게 받아들이려 애쓰며, 그는 셋째 주가 올 때까지 기다렸고, 그제야 발병 이후 처음으로 바깥바람을 쐬러 나갔다."], ["The surgeon visited him again at this stage, and Clym urged him to express a distinct opinion.", "의사는 이 무렵 그를 다시 찾아왔고, 클림은 분명한 소견을 말해달라고 그를 다그쳤다."], ["The young man learnt with added surprise that the date at which he might expect to resume his labours was as uncertain as ever, his eyes being in that peculiar state which, though affording him sight enough for walking about, would not admit of their being strained upon any definite object without incurring the risk of reproducing ophthalmia in its acute form.", "젊은이는 한층 더 놀라며 알게 되었으니, 그가 다시 일을 시작하리라 기대할 수 있는 시점은 여전히 막연하기만 했는데, 그의 두 눈이 그 묘한 상태에 있었던 까닭이라, 걸어 다닐 만큼의 시력은 주어졌으나 어떤 일정한 대상에 눈을 무리하게 쓰면 급성 형태의 안염이 재발할 위험을 무릅쓰지 않고서는 안 되는 것이었다."]], [["Clym was very grave at the intelligence, but not despairing.", "클림은 그 소식에 매우 침울했으나 절망하지는 않았다."], ["A quiet firmness, and even cheerfulness, took possession of him.", "조용한 결연함이, 심지어 명랑함마저 그를 사로잡았다."], ["He was not to be blind; that was enough.", "그는 눈멀지는 않을 터였으니, 그것으로 충분했다."], ["To be doomed to behold the world through smoked glass for an indefinite period was bad enough, and fatal to any kind of advance; but Yeobright was an absolute stoic in the face of mishaps which only affected his social standing; and, apart from Eustacia, the humblest walk of life would satisfy him if it could be made to work in with some form of his culture scheme.", "기약 없는 세월 동안 그을린 유리를 통해 세상을 바라보아야 할 운명이란 어지간히 고약한 일이요, 어떤 종류의 진척에도 치명적이었으나, 요브라이트는 그저 자신의 사회적 지위에만 영향을 미치는 불운 앞에서는 완전한 금욕주의자였으며, 유스테이샤만 아니라면 아무리 미천한 삶의 길이라도 어떤 형태로든 자신의 교화 계획과 맞물리게만 할 수 있다면 그를 만족시킬 터였다."], ["To keep a cottage night-school was one such form; and his affliction did not master his spirit as it might otherwise have done.", "오두막 야학을 여는 것도 그런 형태 가운데 하나였으니, 그의 고난은 본디 그러했을 만큼 그의 기개를 짓누르지는 못했다."]], [["He walked through the warm sun westward into those tracts of Egdon with which he was best acquainted, being those lying nearer to his old home.", "그는 따스한 햇볕을 가르며 서쪽으로, 자기에게 가장 익숙한 에그던의 그 일대로 걸어갔으니, 옛 집에서 더 가까운 곳에 자리한 지역이었다."], ["He saw before him in one of the valleys the gleaming of whetted iron, and advancing, dimly perceived that the shine came from the tool of a man who was cutting furze.", "그는 골짜기 가운데 한 곳에서 날 선 쇠붙이가 번쩍이는 것을 앞에 두고 보았고, 다가가서야 그 번쩍임이 가시금작화를 베고 있는 한 남자의 연장에서 나온 것임을 어렴풋이 알아차렸다."], ["The worker recognized Clym, and Yeobright learnt from the voice that the speaker was Humphrey.", "일하던 사람은 클림을 알아보았고, 요브라이트는 그 목소리로 말하는 이가 험프리임을 알았다."]], [["Humphrey expressed his sorrow at Clym’s condition, and added, “Now, if yours was low-class work like mine, you could go on with it just the same.”", "험프리는 클림의 처지에 안타까움을 표하고는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그런데 도련님 일이 저 같은 막일이라면, 그냥 똑같이 계속하실 수 있을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Yes, I could,” said Yeobright musingly.", "\"그래, 그럴 수 있겠지.\""], ["“How much do you get for cutting these faggots?”", "요브라이트가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"이 단을 베면 얼마나 받나?\""]], [["“Half-a-crown a hundred, and in these long days I can live very well on the wages.”", "\"백 단에 반 크라운이요, 이렇게 해가 긴 날엔 그 삯으로도 아주 잘 살 수 있지요.\""]], [["During the whole of Yeobright’s walk home to Alderworth he was lost in reflections which were not of an unpleasant kind.", "요브라이트가 올더워스로 걸어 돌아오는 내내 그는 결코 언짢지 않은 상념에 잠겨 있었다."], ["On his coming up to the house Eustacia spoke to him from the open window, and he went across to her.", "집에 다다르자 유스테이샤가 열린 창에서 그에게 말을 건넸고, 그는 그녀에게로 건너갔다."]], [["“Darling,”", "\"여보,\""], ["he said, “I am much happier.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 한결 행복하오."], ["And if my mother were reconciled to me and to you I should, I think, be happy quite.”", "그리고 어머니가 나와 당신과 화해하신다면, 나는 아마 더없이 행복할 것 같소.\""]], [["“I fear that will never be,”", "\"그건 결코 이루어지지 않을 거예요.\""], ["she said, looking afar with her beautiful stormy eyes.", "그녀가 아름다운 폭풍 같은 눈으로 먼 곳을 바라보며 말했다."], ["“How _can_ you say ‘I am happier,’ and nothing changed?”", "\"어떻게 아무것도 달라진 게 없는데 '나는 한결 행복하오' 하고 말할 수 있어요?\""]], [["“It arises from my having at last discovered something I can do, and get a living at, in this time of misfortune.”", "\"이 불운한 때에 내가 할 수 있는, 그리고 그것으로 먹고살 수 있는 무언가를 마침내 찾아낸 데서 비롯된 거요.\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"뭔데요?\""]], [["“I am going to be a furze- and turf-cutter.”", "\"나는 가시금작화와 뗏장을 베는 사람이 될 거요.\""]], [["“No, Clym!”", "\"안 돼요, 클림!\""], ["she said, the slight hopefulness previously apparent in her face going off again, and leaving her worse than before.", "그녀가 말했고, 조금 전 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 희미한 희망의 빛은 다시 사라져 이전보다 더 나쁜 상태로 그녀를 남겨놓았다."]], [["“Surely I shall.", "\"분명 그렇게 할 거요."], ["Is it not very unwise in us to go on spending the little money we’ve got when I can keep down expenditures by an honest occupation?", "정직한 일로 지출을 줄일 수 있는데도 얼마 안 되는 우리 돈을 계속 써대는 게 매우 어리석은 짓이 아니겠소?"], ["The outdoor exercise will do me good, and who knows but that in a few months I shall be able to go on with my reading again?”", "바깥일을 하면 내게도 좋을 테고, 몇 달 뒤엔 어쩌면 다시 책 읽기를 계속할 수 있게 될지 누가 알겠소?\""]], [["“But my grandfather offers to assist us, if we require assistance.”", "\"하지만 할아버지가 우리를 도와주겠다고 하시잖아요, 도움이 필요하다면요.\""]], [["“We don’t require it.", "\"우린 그게 필요 없소."], ["If I go furze-cutting we shall be fairly well off.”", "내가 가시금작화를 베면 우린 그런대로 넉넉하게 지낼 거요.\""]], [["“In comparison with slaves, and the Israelites in Egypt, and such people!”", "\"노예들이나, 이집트의 이스라엘 사람들이나, 그런 사람들에 비하면 말이죠!\""], ["A bitter tear rolled down Eustacia’s face, which he did not see.", "쓰디쓴 눈물 한 방울이 유스테이샤의 얼굴을 타고 흘렀으나, 그는 그것을 보지 못했다."], ["There had been nonchalance in his tone, showing her that he felt no absolute grief at a consummation which to her was a positive horror.", "그의 어조에는 무심함이 있었으니, 그녀에게는 그야말로 끔찍한 그 귀결에 그가 절대적인 슬픔을 느끼지 않는다는 것을 그녀에게 일러주는 것이었다."]], [["The very next day Yeobright went to Humphrey’s cottage, and borrowed of him leggings, gloves, a whetstone, and a hook, to use till he should be able to purchase some for himself.", "바로 그 이튿날 요브라이트는 험프리의 오두막으로 가서, 자기 것을 살 수 있게 될 때까지 쓰려고 각반과 장갑과 숫돌과 낫을 빌렸다."], ["Then he sallied forth with his new fellow-labourer and old acquaintance, and selecting a spot where the furze grew thickest he struck the first blow in his adopted calling.", "그러고는 새 동료 일꾼이자 오랜 지인과 함께 길을 나서, 가시금작화가 가장 빽빽이 자란 자리를 골라, 자기가 택한 생업에서 첫 일격을 내리쳤다."], ["His sight, like the wings in Rasselas, though useless to him for his grand purpose, sufficed for this strait, and he found that when a little practice should have hardened his palms against blistering he would be able to work with ease.", "그의 시력은, 「라셀라스」에 나오는 날개처럼 그 원대한 목적에는 쓸모가 없었으나 이 고비에는 충분했으니, 그는 알게 되었다, 손바닥이 물집에 단련되어 좀 더 단단해지면 수월하게 일할 수 있으리라는 것을."]], [["Day after day he rose with the sun, buckled on his leggings, and went off to the rendezvous with Humphrey.", "날이면 날마다 그는 해와 함께 일어나 각반을 졸라매고 험프리와 만나기로 한 자리로 나갔다."], ["His custom was to work from four o’clock in the morning till noon; then, when the heat of the day was at its highest, to go home and sleep for an hour or two; afterwards coming out again and working till dusk at nine.", "그의 일과는 새벽 네 시부터 정오까지 일하고, 그런 다음 하루 중 가장 더운 때가 되면 집으로 가 한두 시간 잠을 자고, 그 뒤에 다시 나와 저물녘 아홉 시까지 일하는 것이었다."]], [["This man from Paris was now so disguised by his leather accoutrements, and by the goggles he was obliged to wear over his eyes, that his closest friend might have passed by without recognizing him.", "파리에서 온 이 남자는 이제 가죽 차림새와 눈 위에 어쩔 수 없이 써야 하는 보안경으로 어찌나 변장한 꼴이 되었던지, 가장 가까운 벗이라도 알아보지 못한 채 곁을 지나칠 만했다."], ["He was a brown spot in the midst of an expanse of olive-green gorse, and nothing more.", "그는 올리브빛 도는 초록색 가시금작화가 펼쳐진 가운데 자리한 갈색 점 하나일 뿐, 그 이상도 아니었다."], ["Though frequently depressed in spirit when not actually at work, owing to thoughts of Eustacia’s position and his mother’s estrangement, when in the full swing of labour he was cheerfully disposed and calm.", "실제로 일하고 있지 않을 때면 유스테이샤의 처지와 어머니와의 불화를 생각하느라 종종 기분이 가라앉았으나, 일이 한창 신명나게 돌아갈 때면 그는 명랑하고 차분했다."], ["His daily life was of a curious microscopic sort, his whole world being limited to a circuit of a few feet from his person.", "그의 일상은 기이하게도 미시적인 종류였으니, 그의 온 세계가 제 몸에서 몇 자 떨어진 둘레에 한정되어 있었다."], ["His familiars were creeping and winged things, and they seemed to enroll him in their band.", "그와 친숙한 것들이라곤 기어다니고 날아다니는 것들이었고, 그것들은 그를 제 무리에 끼워 넣은 듯했다."], ["Bees hummed around his ears with an intimate air, and tugged at the heath and furze-flowers at his side in such numbers as to weigh them down to the sod.", "벌들이 다정한 기색으로 그의 귓가에서 윙윙거리며 옆에서 히스꽃과 가시금작화꽃을 어찌나 떼 지어 잡아당기던지 꽃들을 흙바닥까지 짓누를 지경이었다."], ["The strange amber-coloured butterflies which Egdon produced, and which were never seen elsewhere, quivered in the breath of his lips, alighted upon his bowed back, and sported with the glittering point of his hook as he flourished it up and down.", "에그던이 길러냈고 다른 어디서도 본 적 없는 그 기이한 호박빛 나비들은 그의 입김에 파르르 떨다가 그의 굽은 등에 내려앉기도 하고, 그가 낫을 위아래로 휘두를 때 그 번쩍이는 끝과 어울려 장난을 쳤다."], ["Tribes of emerald-green grasshoppers leaped over his feet, falling awkwardly on their backs, heads, or hips, like unskilful acrobats, as chance might rule; or engaged themselves in noisy flirtations under the fern-fronds with silent ones of homely hue.", "에메랄드빛 초록 메뚜기 무리가 그의 발 위로 뛰어넘다가, 서투른 곡예사처럼 운수에 따라 등이며 머리며 엉덩이로 어설피 나자빠지기도 하고, 혹은 고사리 잎사귀 아래서 수수한 빛깔의 말 없는 메뚜기들과 요란한 시시덕거림에 빠져들기도 했다."], ["Huge flies, ignorant of larders and wire-netting, and quite in a savage state, buzzed about him without knowing that he was a man.", "식료품 저장실도 철망도 모르는, 그야말로 야생 상태 그대로인 커다란 파리들이 그가 사람인 줄도 모른 채 그의 둘레에서 윙윙거렸다."], ["In and out of the fern-dells snakes glided in their most brilliant blue and yellow guise, it being the season immediately following the shedding of their old skins, when their colours are brightest.", "뱀들은 고사리 골짜기를 들고나며 가장 찬란한 파랑과 노랑의 자태로 미끄러져 다녔으니, 묵은 허물을 갓 벗어 그 빛깔이 가장 선명한 철에 바로 뒤이은 때였기 때문이다."], ["Litters of young rabbits came out from their forms to sun themselves upon hillocks, the hot beams blazing through the delicate tissue of each thin-fleshed ear, and firing it to a blood-red transparency in which the veins could be seen.", "어린 토끼 무리가 보금자리에서 나와 둔덕에서 햇볕을 쬐었는데, 뜨거운 햇살이 저마다 얇은 살의 귀에 깃든 섬세한 조직을 꿰뚫어, 핏줄이 들여다보일 만큼 핏빛으로 투명하게 그것을 불태웠다."], ["None of them feared him.", "그것들 가운데 어느 하나도 그를 두려워하지 않았다."]], [["The monotony of his occupation soothed him, and was in itself a pleasure.", "그의 일이 지닌 단조로움은 그를 달래주었고, 그 자체로 하나의 즐거움이었다."], ["A forced limitation of effort offered a justification of homely courses to an unambitious man, whose conscience would hardly have allowed him to remain in such obscurity while his powers were unimpeded.", "노력에 강제로 한계가 지워진 덕에, 야심 없는 한 남자에게 수수한 길을 가는 일이 정당화되었으니, 그렇지 않았다면 그의 양심은 자기 능력이 막힘없는데도 그토록 무명 속에 머물러 있도록 좀처럼 허락하지 않았을 것이다."], ["Hence Yeobright sometimes sang to himself, and when obliged to accompany Humphrey in search of brambles for faggot-bonds he would amuse his companion with sketches of Parisian life and character, and so while away the time.", "그리하여 요브라이트는 이따금 혼자 노래를 불렀고, 단을 묶을 끈으로 쓸 가시덤불을 찾으러 험프리를 따라가야 할 때면 파리의 생활과 인물상을 그려 보이며 동료를 즐겁게 했으니, 그렇게 시간을 보냈다."]], [["On one of these warm afternoons Eustacia walked out alone in the direction of Yeobright’s place of work.", "이렇게 따스한 어느 오후, 유스테이샤는 요브라이트가 일하는 곳을 향해 홀로 걸어 나갔다."], ["He was busily chopping away at the furze, a long row of faggots which stretched downward from his position representing the labour of the day.", "그는 가시금작화를 부지런히 베어내는 중이었고, 그가 있는 자리에서 아래로 길게 늘어선 단의 행렬이 그날 하루의 노동을 나타내고 있었다."], ["He did not observe her approach, and she stood close to him, and heard his undercurrent of song. It shocked her.", "그는 그녀가 다가오는 것을 알아채지 못했고, 그녀는 그의 바로 곁에 서서 그가 낮게 흥얼거리는 노랫가락을 들었다."], ["To see him there, a poor afflicted man, earning money by the sweat of his brow, had at first moved her to tears; but to hear him sing and not at all rebel against an occupation which, however satisfactory to himself, was degrading to her, as an educated lady-wife, wounded her through.", "그것이 그녀에게 충격을 주었다. 그가 거기서, 가련하게도 병든 남자가 이마에 땀을 흘려 돈벌이를 하는 것을 보았을 때 처음에는 눈물이 솟았으나, 그가 노래를 부르며, 그 자신에게야 흡족할지 몰라도 교육받은 숙녀 아내인 그녀에게는 품위를 떨어뜨리는 일에 조금도 반항하지 않는 것을 듣자, 그것이 그녀의 가슴을 후벼 팠다."], ["Unconscious of her presence, he still went on singing:—", "그녀가 곁에 있음을 알아채지 못한 채 그는 계속 노래를 불렀다—"]], [["“Le point du jour A nos bosquets rend toute leur parure; Flore est plus belle à son retour; L’oiseau reprend doux chant d’amour; Tout célèbre dans la nature Le point du jour.", "\"날이 밝아오니 우리 숲에 온갖 단장을 되돌려주네. 플로라는 돌아와 더욱 어여쁘고, 새는 다시 사랑의 다정한 노래를 부르네. 자연 만물이 기리어 노래하네 밝아오는 날을."]], [["“Le point du jour Cause parfois, cause douleur extrême; Que l’espace des nuits est court Pour le berger brûlant d’amour, Forcé de quitter ce qu’il aime Au point du jour.”", "날이 밝아오니 때로는 시름을, 때로는 모진 아픔을 자아내네. 밤의 자락이 어찌 이리 짧은가, 사랑에 불타는 양치기에게는, 날이 밝아오면 사랑하는 이를 떠나야만 하는 그에게는.\""]], [["It was bitterly plain to Eustacia that he did not care much about social failure; and the proud fair woman bowed her head and wept in sick despair at thought of the blasting effect upon her own life of that mood and condition in him.", "유스테이샤에게는 그가 사회적 실패 따위는 그다지 개의치 않는다는 것이 쓰라리도록 분명했고, 자존심 강한 그 아름다운 여인은 고개를 숙이고 병든 절망 속에서 울었으니, 그의 그러한 기분과 처지가 제 삶에 미칠 황폐한 영향을 생각한 까닭이었다."], ["Then she came forward.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["“I would starve rather than do it!” she exclaimed vehemently.", "\"나라면 그런 짓을 하느니 차라리 굶어 죽겠어요!\""], ["“And you can sing!", "그녀가 격하게 외쳤다."], ["I will go and live with my grandfather again!”", "\"그런데 당신은 노래를 부르다니! 저는 다시 할아버지한테 가서 살겠어요!\""]], [["“Eustacia!", "\"유스테이샤!"], ["I did not see you, though I noticed something moving,”", "무언가 움직이는 건 알아챘지만 당신인 줄은 몰랐소.\""], ["he said gently.", "그가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["He came forward, pulled off his huge leather glove, and took her hand.", "그는 앞으로 나서더니 커다란 가죽 장갑을 벗고 그녀의 손을 잡았다."], ["“Why do you speak in such a strange way?", "\"어째서 그런 야릇한 식으로 말하는 거요?"], ["It is only a little old song which struck my fancy when I was in Paris, and now just applies to my life with you.", "그저 파리에 있을 때 내 마음을 사로잡았던 자그마한 옛 노래일 뿐이고, 지금 마침 당신과 함께하는 내 삶에 들어맞아서 그렇소."], ["Has your love for me all died, then, because my appearance is no longer that of a fine gentleman?”", "그럼 내 모습이 더는 훌륭한 신사 같지 않다고 해서 나를 향한 당신의 사랑이 죄다 죽어버린 거요?\""]], [["“Dearest, you must not question me unpleasantly, or it may make me not love you.”", "\"여보, 저를 언짢게 캐묻지 마세요, 안 그러면 당신을 사랑하지 않게 될지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“Do you believe it possible that I would run the risk of doing that?”", "\"내가 그렇게 만들 위험을 무릅쓸 거라고 정말 믿는 거요?\""]], [["“Well, you follow out your own ideas, and won’t give in to mine when I wish you to leave off this shameful labour.", "\"글쎄요, 당신은 당신 생각만 따르고, 제가 이 부끄러운 막일을 그만두기를 바랄 때조차 제 생각엔 굽혀주지 않잖아요."], ["Is there anything you dislike in me that you act so contrarily to my wishes?", "제게 무슨 못마땅한 점이라도 있어서 제 바람과 그토록 어긋나게 구는 건가요?"], ["I am your wife, and why will you not listen?", "저는 당신 아내인데, 어째서 귀 기울이지 않으세요?"], ["Yes, I am your wife indeed!”", "그래요, 저는 정말이지 당신 아내라고요!\""]], [["“I know what that tone means.”", "\"그 말투가 무슨 뜻인지 알겠소.\""]], [["“What tone?”", "\"무슨 말투요?\""]], [["“The tone in which you said, ‘Your wife indeed.’ It meant, ‘Your wife, worse luck.’”", "\"당신이 '정말이지 당신 아내'라고 말한 그 말투 말이오. 그건 '당신 아내라니, 더 박복한 일이지'라는 뜻이었소.\""]], [["“It is hard in you to probe me with that remark.", "\"그런 말로 저를 들쑤시다니 당신도 모질군요."], ["A woman may have reason, though she is not without heart, and if I felt ‘worse luck,’ it was no ignoble feeling—it was only too natural.", "여자에게도 가슴이 없지 않으면서 나름의 이유가 있을 수 있는 거예요. 그리고 제가 '더 박복하다'고 느꼈다면, 그건 비천한 감정이 아니었어요—너무도 자연스러운 것이었을 뿐이죠."], ["There, you see that at any rate I do not attempt untruths.", "자, 보세요, 어쨌든 저는 거짓을 꾸미려 들지 않잖아요."], ["Do you remember how, before we were married, I warned you that I had not good wifely qualities?”", "우리가 결혼하기 전에, 제게 좋은 아내 될 자질이 없다고 미리 일러둔 걸 기억하세요?\""]], [["“You mock me to say that now.", "\"이제 와 그 말을 하다니 나를 조롱하는 거요."], ["On that point at least the only noble course would be to hold your tongue, for you are still queen of me, Eustacia, though I may no longer be king of you.”", "적어도 그 점에 있어서는 입을 다무는 게 유일하게 고결한 길일 거요, 유스테이샤, 당신은 여전히 내 여왕이니 말이오, 내가 더는 당신의 왕이 아닐지라도.\""]], [["“You are my husband.", "\"당신은 제 남편이에요."], ["Does not that content you?”", "그것으로 만족하지 못하세요?\""]], [["“Not unless you are my wife without regret.”", "\"당신이 후회 없는 내 아내가 아니라면 만족 못 하오.\""]], [["“I cannot answer you.", "\"당신께 대답할 수 없어요."], ["I remember saying that I should be a serious matter on your hands.”", "제가 당신 손에 짐스러운 존재가 되리라고 말했던 건 기억해요.\""]], [["“Yes, I saw that.”", "\"그래, 나도 그걸 알아챘소.\""]], [["“Then you were too quick to see!", "\"그럼 당신은 너무 빨리 알아챈 거예요!"], ["No true lover would have seen any such thing; you are too severe upon me, Clym—I won’t like your speaking so at all.”", "참된 연인이라면 그런 걸 알아채지 않았을 거예요. 당신은 제게 너무 가혹해요, 클림—당신이 그렇게 말하는 게 전혀 마음에 안 들어요.\""]], [["“Well, I married you in spite of it, and don’t regret doing so.", "\"글쎄, 나는 그런데도 당신과 결혼했고, 그렇게 한 걸 후회하지 않소."], ["How cold you seem this afternoon!", "오늘 오후엔 당신이 어쩜 이리 차가워 보이는지!"], ["and yet I used to think there never was a warmer heart than yours.”", "게다가 나는 당신 가슴보다 더 따뜻한 가슴은 없으리라 여겼었는데.\""]], [["“Yes, I fear we are cooling—I see it as well as you,”", "\"그래요, 우리가 식어가는 게 아닌가 싶어요—당신만큼 저도 알겠어요.\""], ["she sighed mournfully.", "그녀가 구슬프게 한숨지었다."], ["“And how madly we loved two months ago!", "\"두 달 전엔 우리가 얼마나 미친 듯이 사랑했는지!"], ["You were never tired of contemplating me, nor I of contemplating you.", "당신은 저를 바라보는 데 결코 싫증 내지 않았고, 저도 당신을 바라보는 데 그랬어요."], ["Who could have thought then that by this time my eyes would not seem so very bright to yours, nor your lips so very sweet to mine?", "그때 누가 생각이나 했겠어요, 이맘때면 제 눈이 당신 눈에 그리 밝아 보이지 않고, 당신 입술도 제 입술에 그리 달콤하지 않으리라는 걸요?"], ["Two months—is it possible?", "두 달—그게 있을 수 있는 일인가요?"], ["Yes, ’tis too true!”", "그래요, 너무도 사실이에요!\""]], [["“You sigh, dear, as if you were sorry for it; and that’s a hopeful sign.”", "\"당신은 한숨을 쉬는구려, 여보, 마치 그걸 애석해하듯이. 그건 희망적인 징조요.\""]], [["“No.", "\"아뇨."], ["I don’t sigh for that.", "그것 때문에 한숨짓는 게 아니에요."], ["There are other things for me to sigh for, or any other woman in my place.”", "제게는, 아니 제 처지에 놓인 어떤 여자에게라도, 한숨지을 다른 것들이 있어요.\""]], [["“That your chances in life are ruined by marrying in haste an unfortunate man?”", "\"불운한 남자와 서둘러 결혼하는 바람에 인생의 기회를 망쳐버린 것 말이오?\""]], [["“Why will you force me, Clym, to say bitter things?", "\"어째서 저더러 쓰라린 말을 하게 만드시나요, 클림?"], ["I deserve pity as much as you.", "저도 당신만큼 동정받을 만해요."], ["As much?—I think I deserve it more. For you can sing!", "당신만큼이라고요?—제가 더 동정받을 만하다고 생각해요."], ["It would be a strange hour which should catch me singing under such a cloud as this!", "당신은 노래라도 부를 수 있으니까요! 이런 먹구름 아래서 제가 노래 부르는 걸 들킨다면 참 별난 일일 거예요!"], ["Believe me, sweet, I could weep to a degree that would astonish and confound such an elastic mind as yours.", "정말이지, 여보, 저는 당신처럼 탄력 있는 마음이라면 놀라 어쩔 줄 모를 만큼 울 수도 있어요."], ["Even had you felt careless about your own affliction, you might have refrained from singing out of sheer pity for mine.", "설령 당신이 자기 자신의 고난에는 무심했다 하더라도, 순전히 제 고난을 가엾이 여겨서라도 노래만은 삼갈 수 있었을 거예요. 맙소사!"], ["God! if I were a man in such a position I would curse rather than sing.”", "제가 이런 처지에 놓인 남자라면 노래를 부르느니 차라리 저주를 퍼붓겠어요.\""]], [["Yeobright placed his hand upon her arm.", "요브라이트는 그녀의 팔에 손을 얹었다."], ["“Now, don’t you suppose, my inexperienced girl, that I cannot rebel, in high Promethean fashion, against the gods and fate as well as you.", "\"자, 내 풋내기 아가씨, 나라고 당신만큼 프로메테우스답게 드높이 신들과 운명에 맞서 반항할 수 없으리라고는 생각지 마시오."], ["I have felt more steam and smoke of that sort than you have ever heard of.", "나는 당신이 들어본 그 어떤 것보다 더 많은 그런 종류의 김과 연기를 느껴왔소."], ["But the more I see of life the more do I perceive that there is nothing particularly great in its greatest walks, and therefore nothing particularly small in mine of furze-cutting.", "하지만 삶을 보면 볼수록, 그 가장 높은 길에도 딱히 위대할 게 없으며, 따라서 가시금작화를 베는 내 길에도 딱히 하찮을 게 없다는 걸 알게 되오."], ["If I feel that the greatest blessings vouchsafed to us are not very valuable, how can I feel it to be any great hardship when they are taken away?", "우리에게 허락된 가장 큰 축복조차 그리 값진 게 아니라고 느낀다면, 그것이 거두어진들 어찌 그게 대단한 고난이라 느낄 수 있겠소? 그래서 나는 시간을 보내려고 노래를 부르는 거요."], ["So I sing to pass the time. Have you indeed lost all tenderness for me, that you begrudge me a few cheerful moments?”", "당신은 정말로 나를 향한 다정함을 죄다 잃어버린 거요, 그래서 내게 명랑한 순간 몇 가지를 누리는 것조차 못마땅해하는 거요?\""]], [["“I have still some tenderness left for you.”", "\"당신을 향한 다정함이 아직 좀 남아 있어요.\""]], [["“Your words have no longer their old flavour.", "\"당신 말엔 더는 옛날의 향취가 없소."], ["And so love dies with good fortune!”", "그러니 사랑은 행운과 더불어 죽는구려!\""]], [["“I cannot listen to this, Clym—it will end bitterly,”", "\"이런 말 못 듣겠어요, 클림—쓰라리게 끝날 거예요.\""], ["she said in a broken voice.", "그녀가 갈라진 목소리로 말했다."], ["“I will go home.”", "\"집으로 가겠어요.\""]], [["III.", "III."], ["She Goes Out to Battle against Depression", "그녀는 우울에 맞서 싸우러 나간다"]], [["A few days later, before the month of August had expired, Eustacia and Yeobright sat together at their early dinner.", "며칠 뒤, 팔월이 채 다 가기 전에, 유스테이샤와 요브라이트는 이른 저녁식사를 함께하며 마주 앉아 있었다."]], [["Eustacia’s manner had become of late almost apathetic.", "유스테이샤의 태도는 근래 거의 무기력해져 있었다."], ["There was a forlorn look about her beautiful eyes which, whether she deserved it or not, would have excited pity in the breast of anyone who had known her during the full flush of her love for Clym.", "그녀의 아름다운 두 눈에는 처량한 빛이 어려 있었으니, 그녀가 그럴 만한 까닭이 있든 없든, 클림을 향한 사랑이 한껏 무르익었던 시절의 그녀를 알았던 이라면 누구든 가슴에 연민이 일었을 것이다."], ["The feelings of husband and wife varied, in some measure, inversely with their positions.", "남편과 아내의 심정은 어느 정도 그들의 처지와는 반대로 엇갈렸다."], ["Clym, the afflicted man, was cheerful; and he even tried to comfort her, who had never felt a moment of physical suffering in her whole life.", "병든 남자인 클림은 명랑했고, 평생 단 한 순간도 육체의 고통을 느껴본 적 없는 그녀를 도리어 위로하려 애쓰기까지 했다."]], [["“Come, brighten up, dearest; we shall be all right again.", "\"자, 기운 내요, 여보. 우린 다시 다 괜찮아질 거요."], ["Some day perhaps I shall see as well as ever.", "언젠가 어쩌면 나도 예전처럼 잘 보게 될 거요."], ["And I solemnly promise that I’ll leave off cutting furze as soon as I have the power to do anything better.", "그리고 나는 엄숙히 약속하오, 무언가 더 나은 일을 할 힘이 생기는 대로 가시금작화 베는 일은 그만두겠다고."], ["You cannot seriously wish me to stay idling at home all day?”", "설마 내가 하루 종일 집에서 빈둥거리며 지내기를 진심으로 바라는 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["“But it is so dreadful—a furze-cutter!", "\"하지만 너무 끔찍하잖아요—가시금작화 베는 사람이라니!"], ["and you a man who have lived about the world, and speak French, and German, and who are fit for what is so much better than this.”", "게다가 당신은 세상을 두루 살아왔고, 프랑스어도 독일어도 할 줄 알며, 이것보다 훨씬 더 나은 일에 어울리는 사람인데요.\""]], [["“I suppose when you first saw me and heard about me I was wrapped in a sort of golden halo to your eyes—a man who knew glorious things, and had mixed in brilliant scenes—in short, an adorable, delightful, distracting hero?”", "\"당신이 나를 처음 보고 내 이야기를 들었을 때, 내가 당신 눈에는 일종의 금빛 후광에 감싸인 것처럼 보였나 보군—영광스러운 것들을 알고, 화려한 무대에 섞여본 남자—요컨대 흠모할 만하고, 황홀하고, 정신을 쏙 빼놓는 영웅으로 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요.\""], ["she said, sobbing.", "그녀가 흐느끼며 말했다."]], [["“And now I am a poor fellow in brown leather.”", "\"그런데 이제 나는 갈색 가죽을 걸친 가련한 사내가 되었군.\""]], [["“Don’t taunt me.", "\"저를 빈정대지 마세요."], ["But enough of this.", "하지만 이런 얘긴 그만하죠."], ["I will not be depressed any more.", "저는 더는 우울해하지 않겠어요."], ["I am going from home this afternoon, unless you greatly object.", "당신이 크게 반대하지 않는다면, 오늘 오후 집을 나가 있겠어요."], ["There is to be a village picnic—a gipsying, they call it—at East Egdon, and I shall go.”", "이스트 에그던에서 마을 소풍이—집시 놀이라고들 하는 게—열린대요, 저는 거기 갈래요.\""]], [["“To dance?”", "\"춤추러?\""]], [["“Why not?", "\"왜 안 되나요?"], ["You can sing.”", "당신은 노래를 부르잖아요.\""]], [["“Well, well, as you will.", "\"그래, 그래, 당신 좋을 대로 하오."], ["Must I come to fetch you?”", "내가 데리러 가야겠소?\""]], [["“If you return soon enough from your work.", "\"당신 일에서 충분히 일찍 돌아온다면요."], ["But do not inconvenience yourself about it.", "하지만 그것 때문에 무리하진 마세요."], ["I know the way home, and the heath has no terror for me.”", "저는 집에 오는 길을 알고, 황야가 제겐 조금도 무섭지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“And can you cling to gaiety so eagerly as to walk all the way to a village festival in search of it?”", "\"그래, 당신은 그 흥겨움에 그토록 간절히 매달려, 그걸 찾아 마을 잔치까지 줄곧 걸어갈 만하단 말이오?\""]], [["“Now, you don’t like my going alone!", "\"이런, 제가 혼자 가는 게 싫으신 거군요!"], ["Clym, you are not jealous?”", "클림, 질투하시는 건 아니죠?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니오."], ["But I would come with you if it could give you any pleasure; though, as things stand, perhaps you have too much of me already.", "하지만 당신에게 조금이라도 즐거움이 된다면 함께 가겠소. 비록 지금 형편상 당신은 이미 나를 너무 많이 곁에 두고 있는지도 모르지만 말이오."], ["Still, I somehow wish that you did not want to go.", "그래도 어쩐지 나는 당신이 가고 싶어 하지 않았으면 싶소."], ["Yes, perhaps I am jealous; and who could be jealous with more reason than I, a half-blind man, over such a woman as you?”", "그래, 어쩌면 나는 질투하는 거요. 그런데 반쯤 눈먼 남자가 당신 같은 여자를 두고 질투하는 것보다 더 그럴 만한 이유를 가진 사람이 어디 있겠소?\""]], [["“Don’t think like it.", "\"그렇게 생각지 마세요."], ["Let me go, and don’t take all my spirits away!”", "저를 보내주시고, 제 기운을 죄다 앗아가지 마세요!\""]], [["“I would rather lose all my own, my sweet wife.", "\"차라리 내 기운을 죄다 잃는 편이 낫겠소, 내 사랑하는 아내여."], ["Go and do whatever you like.", "가서 당신 하고 싶은 대로 하오."], ["Who can forbid your indulgence in any whim?", "누가 당신이 어떤 변덕에 빠지는 것을 막을 수 있겠소?"], ["You have all my heart yet, I believe; and because you bear with me, who am in truth a drag upon you, I owe you thanks.", "당신은 아직 내 마음을 온통 가지고 있다고 나는 믿소. 그리고 사실 당신에게 짐이 되는 나를 당신이 견뎌주니, 나는 당신에게 고마운 빚을 지고 있소."], ["Yes, go alone and shine.", "그래, 혼자 가서 빛나구려."], ["As for me, I will stick to my doom. At that kind of meeting people would shun me.", "나로 말하면, 나는 내 운명을 붙들고 있겠소."], ["My hook and gloves are like the St.", "그런 모임에서 사람들은 나를 피할 테니."], ["Lazarus rattle of the leper, warning the world to get out of the way of a sight that would sadden them.”", "내 낫과 장갑은 나병 환자의 성 나사로 딸랑이와 같아서, 보는 이를 슬프게 할 광경을 비키라고 세상에 경고하는 거요.\""], ["He kissed her, put on his leggings, and went out.", "그는 그녀에게 입을 맞추고 각반을 차고는 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["When he was gone she rested her head upon her hands and said to herself, “Two wasted lives—his and mine.", "그가 떠나자 그녀는 두 손에 머리를 기대고 혼잣말을 했다. \"허비된 두 인생—그의 것과 내 것."], ["And I am come to this!", "그런데 내가 이 지경에 이르다니!"], ["Will it drive me out of my mind?”", "이러다 내가 미쳐버리는 건 아닐까?\""]], [["She cast about for any possible course which offered the least improvement on the existing state of things, and could find none.", "그녀는 지금의 상태에서 조금이라도 나아질 만한 길이 있을까 두루 궁리했으나, 아무것도 찾을 수 없었다."], ["She imagined how all those Budmouth ones who should learn what had become of her would say, “Look at the girl for whom nobody was good enough!”", "자기가 어찌 되었는지 알게 될 버드머스의 그 사람들이 \"아무도 성에 차지 않던 그 계집애 좀 봐!\" 하고 말하는 광경을 그녀는 그려보았다."], ["To Eustacia the situation seemed such a mockery of her hopes that death appeared the only door of relief if the satire of Heaven should go much further.", "유스테이샤에게는 이 처지가 자기 희망에 대한 너무도 지독한 조롱처럼 여겨져, 만일 하늘의 풍자가 더 멀리까지 나아간다면 죽음만이 유일한 구원의 문으로 보였다."]], [["Suddenly she aroused herself and exclaimed, “But I’ll shake it off.", "문득 그녀는 정신을 차리고 외쳤다. \"하지만 난 이걸 떨쳐내겠어."], ["Yes, I _will_ shake it off!", "그래, 난 기어이 떨쳐낼 거야!"], ["No one shall know my suffering.", "아무도 내 고통을 알지 못하게 하겠어."], ["I’ll be bitterly merry, and ironically gay, and I’ll laugh in derision.", "쓰라리도록 즐거워하고, 비꼬듯 명랑하게 굴며, 비웃듯 깔깔거리겠어."], ["And I’ll begin by going to this dance on the green.”", "그리고 우선 풀밭의 이 춤판에 가는 것부터 시작하겠어.\""]], [["She ascended to her bedroom and dressed herself with scrupulous care.", "그녀는 침실로 올라가 더없이 세심하게 차림새를 갖췄다."], ["To an onlooker her beauty would have made her feelings almost seem reasonable.", "곁에서 보는 이라면 그녀의 아름다움 덕에 그녀의 심정이 거의 당연한 것처럼 여겨졌을 것이다."], ["The gloomy corner into which accident as much as indiscretion had brought this woman might have led even a moderate partisan to feel that she had cogent reasons for asking the Supreme Power by what right a being of such exquisite finish had been placed in circumstances calculated to make of her charms a curse rather than a blessing.", "무분별 못지않게 우연이 이 여인을 몰아넣은 그 음울한 구석은, 어지간히 미온적인 편들기를 하는 사람조차도 이렇게 느끼게 했을 법하니, 곧 그녀가 지고至高한 권능에 대고 도대체 무슨 권리로 이토록 정교하게 빚어진 존재를, 제 매력이 축복이 아니라 도리어 저주가 되도록 꾸며진 상황 속에 놓았느냐고 따져 물을 만한 절절한 이유가 그녀에게 있다고 말이다."]], [["It was five in the afternoon when she came out from the house ready for her walk.", "그녀가 외출 채비를 마치고 집에서 나선 것은 오후 다섯 시였다."], ["There was material enough in the picture for twenty new conquests.", "그 그림 속에는 새로운 정복을 스무 번은 더 이뤄낼 만한 밑감이 담겨 있었다."], ["The rebellious sadness that was rather too apparent when she sat indoors without a bonnet was cloaked and softened by her outdoor attire, which always had a sort of nebulousness about it, devoid of harsh edges anywhere; so that her face looked from its environment as from a cloud, with no noticeable lines of demarcation between flesh and clothes.", "모자도 쓰지 않고 집 안에 앉아 있을 때면 다소 지나치게 도드라지던 반항기 어린 슬픔은, 그녀의 나들이옷에 가려 누그러져 있었으니, 그 옷차림에는 늘 어딘가 흐릿한 데가 있어 어디에도 모진 윤곽이 없었다. 그리하여 그녀의 얼굴은 마치 구름에서 내다보듯 그 옷자락 속에서 내다보였고, 살과 옷 사이에 이렇다 할 경계선이 보이지 않았다."], ["The heat of the day had scarcely declined as yet, and she went along the sunny hills at a leisurely pace, there being ample time for her idle expedition.", "한낮의 열기는 아직 거의 누그러지지 않았고, 그녀는 한가로이 빈둥거릴 그 나들이에 시간이 넉넉했던 터라, 햇볕 드는 언덕을 따라 느릿한 걸음으로 걸어갔다."], ["Tall ferns buried her in their leafage whenever her path lay through them, which now formed miniature forests, though not one stem of them would remain to bud the next year.", "길이 키 큰 고사리 사이로 날 때면 그것들의 잎사귀가 그녀를 파묻었으니, 그것들은 이제 자그마한 숲을 이루고 있었으나, 그 줄기 가운데 단 하나도 이듬해에 새싹을 틔우려 남아 있지는 않을 터였다."]], [["The site chosen for the village festivity was one of the lawnlike oases which were occasionally, yet not often, met with on the plateaux of the heath district.", "마을 잔치를 위해 고른 자리는 잔디밭 같은 오아시스 가운데 하나였으니, 황야 지대의 고원에서 이따금, 그러나 흔치는 않게 마주치는 곳이었다."], ["The brakes of furze and fern terminated abruptly round the margin, and the grass was unbroken.", "가시금작화와 고사리 덤불이 그 가장자리를 빙 둘러 뚝 끊겼고, 풀밭은 끊긴 데 없이 이어져 있었다."], ["A green cattletrack skirted the spot, without, however, emerging from the screen of fern, and this path Eustacia followed, in order to reconnoitre the group before joining it.", "푸른 가축길 하나가 그곳을 따라 났으나, 고사리 장막에서 벗어나지는 않았는데, 유스테이샤는 무리에 끼어들기 전에 정찰해보려고 이 길을 따라갔다."], ["The lusty notes of the East Egdon band had directed her unerringly, and she now beheld the musicians themselves, sitting in a blue wagon with red wheels scrubbed as bright as new, and arched with sticks, to which boughs and flowers were tied.", "이스트 에그던 악단의 우렁찬 가락이 그녀를 한 치도 어긋남 없이 인도했고, 이제 그녀는 악사들의 모습을 마주 보게 되었으니, 그들은 새것처럼 반짝반짝 닦은 붉은 바퀴가 달린 파란 짐마차에 앉아 있었고, 그 마차는 나뭇가지와 꽃을 동여맨 막대들로 아치를 이루고 있었다."], ["In front of this was the grand central dance of fifteen or twenty couples, flanked by minor dances of inferior individuals whose gyrations were not always in strict keeping with the tune.", "그 앞에서는 열다섯에서 스무 쌍이 이루는 웅장한 한가운데 춤판이 벌어졌고, 그 가락에 늘 엄격히 맞지는 않게 빙글빙글 도는, 한층 못한 이들의 자잘한 춤판들이 그 양옆에 늘어서 있었다."]], [["The young men wore blue and white rosettes, and with a flush on their faces footed it to the girls, who, with the excitement and the exercise, blushed deeper than the pink of their numerous ribbons.", "젊은 남자들은 파란색과 흰색 장식꽃을 달고, 얼굴에 홍조를 띤 채 처녀들에게 발맞춰 춤추었으니, 처녀들은 흥분과 운동으로 저들이 단 무수한 리본의 분홍빛보다 더 짙게 볼을 붉혔다."], ["Fair ones with long curls, fair ones with short curls, fair ones with lovelocks, fair ones with braids, flew round and round; and a beholder might well have wondered how such a prepossessing set of young women of like size, age, and disposition, could have been collected together where there were only one or two villages to choose from.", "긴 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨린 고운 처녀들, 짧은 곱슬머리의 고운 처녀들, 애교머리를 늘어뜨린 고운 처녀들, 땋은 머리를 한 고운 처녀들이 빙글빙글 돌고 또 돌았고, 보는 이는 이처럼 비슷한 키와 나이와 기질을 지닌 호감 가는 젊은 여인들이, 고를 만한 마을이라곤 한둘밖에 없는 곳에서 어찌 이리 한데 모일 수 있었는지 자못 의아해할 만했다."], ["In the background was one happy man dancing by himself, with closed eyes, totally oblivious of all the rest.", "뒤편에서는 행복한 사내 하나가 눈을 감고 나머지 모든 것은 까맣게 잊은 채 혼자 춤추고 있었다."], ["A fire was burning under a pollard thorn a few paces off, over which three kettles hung in a row.", "몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에서는 잘라낸 가시나무 아래 불이 타고 있었고, 그 위로 주전자 세 개가 한 줄로 걸려 있었다."], ["Hard by was a table where elderly dames prepared tea, but Eustacia looked among them in vain for the cattle-dealer’s wife who had suggested that she should come, and had promised to obtain a courteous welcome for her.", "바로 곁에는 나이 든 부인들이 차를 마련하는 탁자가 있었으나, 유스테이샤가 그들 사이에서 아무리 찾아보아도, 자기더러 오라고 권하며 정중한 환대를 받게 해주겠다고 약속했던 그 가축상의 아내는 보이지 않았다."], ["This unexpected absence of the only local resident whom Eustacia knew considerably damaged her scheme for an afternoon of reckless gaiety.", "유스테이샤가 아는 단 한 사람인 그 고장 주민이 뜻밖에 자리에 없자, 마음껏 흥겨운 오후를 보내려던 그녀의 계획은 적잖이 어그러졌다."], ["Joining in became a matter of difficulty, notwithstanding that, were she to advance, cheerful dames would come forward with cups of tea and make much of her as a stranger of superior grace and knowledge to themselves.", "무리에 끼어드는 일이 곤란해졌으니, 그녀가 나서기만 한다면 명랑한 부인들이 찻잔을 들고 다가와, 자기들보다 우아함과 식견이 빼어난 낯선 손님이라며 떠받들 터인데도 그러했다."], ["Having watched the company through the figures of two dances, she decided to walk a little further, to a cottage where she might get some refreshment, and then return homeward in the shady time of evening.", "두 차례 춤이 펼쳐지는 동안 무리를 지켜본 그녀는, 조금 더 멀리 어느 오두막까지 걸어가 요기를 좀 하고, 그늘이 지는 저녁때에 집으로 돌아가기로 마음먹었다."]], [["This she did, and by the time that she retraced her steps towards the scene of the gipsying, which it was necessary to repass on her way to Alderworth, the sun was going down.", "그녀는 그렇게 했고, 올더워스로 가는 길에 다시 지나쳐야 하는 집시 놀이판 쪽으로 발길을 되돌릴 무렵에는 해가 기울고 있었다."], ["The air was now so still that she could hear the band afar off, and it seemed to be playing with more spirit, if that were possible, than when she had come away.", "공기가 이제 어찌나 고요했던지 그녀는 멀리서 들려오는 악단 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 그 소리는 그녀가 자리를 떴을 때보다, 그럴 수 있다면, 한층 신명나게 연주되는 듯했다."], ["On reaching the hill the sun had quite disappeared; but this made little difference either to Eustacia or to the revellers, for a round yellow moon was rising before her, though its rays had not yet outmastered those from the west.", "언덕에 다다랐을 때 해는 완전히 자취를 감췄으나, 그것은 유스테이샤에게도 흥청거리는 이들에게도 별다른 차이가 없었으니, 둥근 노란 달이 그녀 앞에서 떠오르고 있었기 때문이다, 비록 그 빛살이 아직 서녘에서 오는 빛살을 압도하지는 못했으나."], ["The dance was going on just the same, but strangers had arrived and formed a ring around the figure, so that Eustacia could stand among these without a chance of being recognized.", "춤은 여전히 똑같이 펼쳐지고 있었으나, 낯선 이들이 와서 춤판 둘레에 고리를 이룬 터라, 유스테이샤는 알아볼 만한 일 없이 그들 사이에 끼어 설 수 있었다."]], [["A whole village-full of sensuous emotion, scattered abroad all the year long, surged here in a focus for an hour.", "한 해 내내 사방으로 흩어져 있던 한 마을 가득한 관능의 정념이, 여기 한 시간 동안 한 초점으로 솟구쳐 올랐다."], ["The forty hearts of those waving couples were beating as they had not done since, twelve months before, they had come together in similar jollity.", "물결치듯 춤추는 그 쌍들의 마흔 개 심장은, 열두 달 전 비슷한 흥겨움 속에 함께 모인 이래 뛰어보지 못한 만큼 세차게 뛰고 있었다."], ["For the time paganism was revived in their hearts, the pride of life was all in all, and they adored none other than themselves.", "한동안 그들의 가슴속에 이교의 정신이 되살아났고, 삶의 자긍심이 전부였으며, 그들은 자기 자신 말고는 아무것도 떠받들지 않았다."]], [["How many of those impassioned but temporary embraces were destined to become perpetual was possibly the wonder of some of those who indulged in them, as well as of Eustacia who looked on.", "그 격정에 사로잡혔으되 한순간일 뿐인 포옹들 가운데 얼마만큼이 영원한 것이 될 운명인지는, 어쩌면 그것에 빠져든 이들 가운데 몇몇뿐 아니라 곁에서 지켜보는 유스테이샤에게도 궁금한 일이었으리라."], ["She began to envy those pirouetters, to hunger for the hope and happiness which the fascination of the dance seemed to engender within them.", "그녀는 그 빙글빙글 도는 이들을 부러워하기 시작했고, 춤의 매혹이 그들 안에 자아내는 듯한 그 희망과 행복에 굶주리기 시작했다."], ["Desperately fond of dancing herself, one of Eustacia’s expectations of Paris had been the opportunity it might afford her of indulgence in this favourite pastime.", "본디 춤이라면 사족을 못 쓸 만큼 좋아했던 유스테이샤가 파리에 걸었던 기대 가운데 하나는, 이 가장 좋아하는 소일거리에 마음껏 빠져들 기회를 거기서 얻으리라는 것이었다."], ["Unhappily, that expectation was now extinct within her for ever.", "불행히도 이제 그 기대는 그녀 안에서 영영 꺼져버린 터였다."]], [["Whilst she abstractedly watched them spinning and fluctuating in the increasing moonlight she suddenly heard her name whispered by a voice over her shoulder.", "그녀가 짙어가는 달빛 속에서 빙글빙글 돌며 물결치는 그들을 넋 놓고 바라보던 중에, 문득 어깨 너머에서 어떤 목소리가 자기 이름을 속삭이는 것을 들었다."], ["Turning in surprise, she beheld at her elbow one whose presence instantly caused her to flush to the temples.", "놀라 돌아서니, 그녀는 팔꿈치께에서 한 사람을 보았는데, 그가 거기 있다는 것만으로도 그녀는 대번에 관자놀이까지 붉어졌다."]], [["It was Wildeve.", "와일디브였다."], ["Till this moment he had not met her eye since the morning of his marriage, when she had been loitering in the church, and had startled him by lifting her veil and coming forward to sign the register as witness.", "이 순간까지 그는 자기 결혼식 날 아침 이래 그녀와 눈이 마주친 적이 없었으니, 그날 그녀는 교회 안을 서성이다가 면사포를 들어 올리고 증인으로서 혼인 명부에 서명하러 앞으로 나서서 그를 깜짝 놀라게 했었다."], ["Yet why the sight of him should have instigated that sudden rush of blood she could not tell.", "그러나 어째서 그를 보자 그토록 갑작스레 피가 솟구쳤는지 그녀로서는 알 수 없었다."]], [["Before she could speak he whispered, “Do you like dancing as much as ever?”", "그녀가 입을 열기도 전에 그가 속삭였다. \"여전히 그렇게나 춤을 좋아하시오?\""]], [["“I think I do,”", "\"그런 것 같아요.\""], ["she replied in a low voice.", "그녀가 낮은 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["“Will you dance with me?”", "\"나와 춤추시겠소?\""]], [["“It would be a great change for me; but will it not seem strange?”", "\"제겐 큰 변화가 되겠죠. 하지만 이상해 보이지 않을까요?\""]], [["“What strangeness can there be in relations dancing together?”", "\"친척끼리 함께 춤추는 데 이상할 게 뭐가 있겠소?\""]], [["“Ah—yes, relations.", "\"아—그래요, 친척이죠."], ["Perhaps none.”", "어쩌면 아무것도 없겠네요.\""]], [["“Still, if you don’t like to be seen, pull down your veil; though there is not much risk of being known by this light.", "\"그래도 남의 눈에 띄는 게 싫다면 면사포를 내리시오. 이런 빛 속에서야 알아볼 위험이 크지 않겠지만 말이오."], ["Lots of strangers are here.”", "낯선 이들이 잔뜩 와 있소.\""]], [["She did as he suggested; and the act was a tacit acknowledgment that she accepted his offer.", "그녀는 그가 일러준 대로 했고, 그 행동은 그의 청을 받아들였다는 무언의 인정이었다."]], [["Wildeve gave her his arm and took her down on the outside of the ring to the bottom of the dance, which they entered.", "와일디브는 그녀에게 팔을 내주고 고리 바깥쪽을 따라 그녀를 춤판 맨 아래로 데려갔으며, 두 사람은 거기서 춤판에 들어섰다."], ["In two minutes more they were involved in the figure and began working their way upwards to the top.", "다시 이 분쯤 지나자 그들은 춤의 대형에 휘말려 위쪽 맨 윗자리를 향해 차근차근 나아가기 시작했다."], ["Till they had advanced halfway thither Eustacia wished more than once that she had not yielded to his request; from the middle to the top she felt that, since she had come out to seek pleasure, she was only doing a natural thing to obtain it.", "그리로 절반쯤 나아가는 동안 유스테이샤는 그의 청에 굴복하지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을 하고 한 번 이상 바랐으나, 중간에서 맨 위에 이르는 동안에는 즐거움을 찾아 나선 이상 그것을 얻으려 하는 것은 그저 자연스러운 일을 하는 것일 뿐이라고 느꼈다."], ["Fairly launched into the ceaseless glides and whirls which their new position as top couple opened up to them, Eustacia’s pulses began to move too quickly for long rumination of any kind.", "맨 윗자리 짝이라는 새로운 위치가 그들에게 열어준 끊임없는 미끄러짐과 휘돎 속으로 거침없이 뛰어드니, 유스테이샤의 맥박은 어떤 종류의 긴 사색에 잠기기에는 너무 빨리 뛰기 시작했다."]], [["Through the length of five-and-twenty couples they threaded their giddy way, and a new vitality entered her form.", "스물다섯 쌍의 줄을 누비며 그들은 어지러운 길을 헤쳐갔고, 새로운 생기가 그녀의 몸속으로 들어왔다."], ["The pale ray of evening lent a fascination to the experience.", "저물녘의 창백한 빛살이 그 경험에 매혹을 더했다."], ["There is a certain degree and tone of light which tends to disturb the equilibrium of the senses, and to promote dangerously the tenderer moods; added to movement, it drives the emotions to rankness, the reason becoming sleepy and unperceiving in inverse proportion; and this light fell now upon these two from the disc of the moon.", "감각의 균형을 흐트러뜨리고 한결 부드러운 기분을 위태로울 만큼 부추기는, 어떤 정도와 가락의 빛이라는 것이 있으니, 거기에 움직임이 더해지면 그것은 감정을 무성하게 내몰고, 이성은 그에 반비례하여 졸음에 겨워 분별하지 못하게 되는데, 지금 이 빛이 달의 원반에서 이 두 사람 위로 내려앉았다."], ["All the dancing girls felt the symptoms, but Eustacia most of all.", "춤추는 처녀들 모두가 그 증상을 느꼈으나, 그 누구보다 유스테이샤가 가장 강하게 느꼈다."], ["The grass under their feet became trodden away, and the hard, beaten surface of the sod, when viewed aslant towards the moonlight, shone like a polished table.", "그들의 발밑에서 풀은 짓밟혀 사라졌고, 단단히 다져진 흙바닥은 달빛 쪽으로 비스듬히 바라보면 잘 닦인 식탁처럼 빛났다."], ["The air became quite still, the flag above the wagon which held the musicians clung to the pole, and the players appeared only in outline against the sky; except when the circular mouths of the trombone, ophicleide, and French horn gleamed out like huge eyes from the shade of their figures.", "공기는 아주 고요해졌고, 악사들을 태운 짐마차 위의 깃발은 깃대에 달라붙었으며, 연주자들은 하늘을 배경으로 윤곽으로만 보였으니, 다만 트롬본과 오피클라이드와 프렌치 호른의 둥근 입이 그들 형체의 그늘에서 거대한 눈처럼 번쩍일 때만은 예외였다."], ["The pretty dresses of the maids lost their subtler day colours and showed more or less of a misty white.", "처녀들의 어여쁜 옷들은 한낮의 그 미묘한 빛깔을 잃고 어느 정도 뿌연 흰빛을 띠었다."], ["Eustacia floated round and round on Wildeve’s arm, her face rapt and statuesque; her soul had passed away from and forgotten her features, which were left empty and quiescent, as they always are when feeling goes beyond their register.", "유스테이샤는 와일디브의 팔에 의지해 빙글빙글 돌고 또 돌았고, 그녀의 얼굴은 넋을 잃은 듯 조각처럼 굳어 있었으니, 그녀의 영혼이 그 이목구비에서 빠져나가 그것을 잊어버려, 감정이 그것들의 표현 범위를 넘어설 때면 늘 그러하듯 그것들은 텅 빈 채 잠잠히 남겨졌다."]], [["How near she was to Wildeve!", "그녀가 와일디브와 얼마나 가까이 있었던가!"], ["it was terrible to think of.", "생각만 해도 끔찍한 일이었다."], ["She could feel his breathing, and he, of course, could feel hers.", "그녀는 그의 숨결을 느낄 수 있었고, 그 또한 물론 그녀의 숨결을 느낄 수 있었다."], ["How badly she had treated him!", "그녀가 그를 얼마나 모질게 대했던가!"], ["yet, here they were treading one measure.", "그런데도 여기서 그들은 하나의 박자를 밟고 있었다."], ["The enchantment of the dance surprised her.", "춤의 마법이 그녀를 놀라게 했다."], ["A clear line of difference divided like a tangible fence her experience within this maze of motion from her experience without it.", "또렷한 차이의 선 하나가 손에 잡힐 듯한 울타리처럼, 이 움직임의 미로 안에서의 경험과 그 바깥에서의 경험을 갈라놓았다."], ["Her beginning to dance had been like a change of atmosphere; outside, she had been steeped in arctic frigidity by comparison with the tropical sensations here.", "그녀가 춤추기 시작한 것은 마치 대기가 바뀌는 것과 같았으니, 바깥에서 그녀는 여기 이 열대의 감각에 견주면 북극의 매서운 추위에 푹 잠겨 있었던 것이다."], ["She had entered the dance from the troubled hours of her late life as one might enter a brilliant chamber after a night walk in a wood.", "그녀는 근래의 그 시름겨운 시간들로부터 이 춤 속으로 들어왔으니, 마치 숲속에서 밤길을 걷다가 휘황한 방으로 들어서는 것과 같았다."], ["Wildeve by himself would have been merely an agitation; Wildeve added to the dance, and the moonlight, and the secrecy, began to be a delight.", "와일디브 혼자였다면 그저 마음의 동요에 지나지 않았을 것이나, 춤과 달빛과 은밀함에 더해진 와일디브는 하나의 기쁨이 되기 시작했다."], ["Whether his personality supplied the greater part of this sweetly compounded feeling, or whether the dance and the scene weighed the more therein, was a nice point upon which Eustacia herself was entirely in a cloud.", "이 달콤하게 뒤섞인 감정의 더 큰 몫을 그의 사람됨이 빚어냈는지, 아니면 춤과 그 정경이 거기서 더 큰 무게를 차지했는지는, 유스테이샤 자신도 도무지 알 수 없는 미묘한 문제였다."]], [["People began to say “Who are they?”", "사람들이 \"저들이 누구지?\""], ["but no invidious inquiries were made.", "하고 말하기 시작했으나, 시샘 어린 캐물음은 일지 않았다."], ["Had Eustacia mingled with the other girls in their ordinary daily walks the case would have been different: here she was not inconvenienced by excessive inspection, for all were wrought to their brightest grace by the occasion.", "유스테이샤가 다른 처녀들과 어울려 평소의 나날을 걸었더라면 사정은 달랐을 테지만, 여기서는 지나친 눈길에 시달리지 않았으니, 모두가 이 흥겨운 자리에 한껏 우아하게 매만져진 까닭이었다."], ["Like the planet Mercury surrounded by the lustre of sunset, her permanent brilliancy passed without much notice in the temporary glory of the situation.", "해 질 녘의 광채에 둘러싸인 수성水星이라는 행성처럼, 그녀의 변함없는 광휘는 그 순간의 일시적인 영광 속에서 그다지 눈에 띄지 않은 채 지나갔다."]], [["As for Wildeve, his feelings are easy to guess.", "와일디브로 말하면, 그의 심정은 어렵잖게 짐작할 수 있다."], ["Obstacles were a ripening sun to his love, and he was at this moment in a delirium of exquisite misery.", "장애물은 그의 사랑에 무르익게 하는 햇볕이었고, 그는 이 순간 더없이 절묘한 비참함의 광란 속에 있었다."], ["To clasp as his for five minutes what was another man’s through all the rest of the year was a kind of thing he of all men could appreciate.", "한 해 내내 다른 남자의 것인 그 여자를 단 오 분이나마 제 것으로 끌어안는다는 것은, 다른 누구보다도 그가 진가를 알아볼 만한 종류의 일이었다."], ["He had long since begun to sigh again for Eustacia; indeed, it may be asserted that signing the marriage register with Thomasin was the natural signal to his heart to return to its first quarters, and that the extra complication of Eustacia’s marriage was the one addition required to make that return compulsory.", "그는 이미 오래전부터 유스테이샤를 향해 다시 한숨짓기 시작한 터였으니, 실로 토머신과 함께 혼인 명부에 서명한 것이야말로 그의 마음이 첫 거처로 되돌아가게 만든 자연스러운 신호였으며, 유스테이샤의 결혼이라는 부가된 복잡함이야말로 그 되돌아감을 거역할 수 없게 만드는 데 필요한 단 하나의 덤이었다고 단언할 수 있다."]], [["Thus, for different reasons, what was to the rest an exhilarating movement was to these two a riding upon the whirlwind.", "이렇게, 서로 다른 까닭에서, 나머지 사람들에게는 신명나는 율동이던 것이 이 두 사람에게는 회오리바람을 올라타는 일이었다."], ["The dance had come like an irresistible attack upon whatever sense of social order there was in their minds, to drive them back into old paths which were now doubly irregular.", "춤은 그들 마음속에 남아 있던 사회적 질서 감각이 무엇이든 그것을 향한 거역할 수 없는 공격처럼 들이닥쳐, 이제는 갑절로 어긋난 옛길로 그들을 도로 몰아넣었다."], ["Through three dances in succession they spun their way; and then, fatigued with the incessant motion, Eustacia turned to quit the circle in which she had already remained too long.", "잇따라 세 차례 춤을 추며 그들은 길을 빙빙 돌았고, 그런 다음 쉼 없는 움직임에 지친 유스테이샤는 이미 너무 오래 머물러 있던 그 고리를 떠나려고 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Wildeve led her to a grassy mound a few yards distant, where she sat down, her partner standing beside her.", "와일디브는 그녀를 몇 자 떨어진 풀 둔덕으로 이끌었고, 거기 그녀가 앉자 그녀의 짝은 그 곁에 섰다."], ["From the time that he addressed her at the beginning of the dance till now they had not exchanged a word.", "춤이 시작될 무렵 그가 그녀에게 말을 건 때부터 지금까지 그들은 한마디도 주고받지 않았다."]], [["“The dance and the walking have tired you?”", "\"춤추고 걷느라 지치셨소?\""], ["he said tenderly.", "그가 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“No; not greatly.”", "\"아뇨, 그리 많이는요.\""]], [["“It is strange that we should have met here of all places, after missing each other so long.”", "\"하필 모든 곳 가운데 여기서, 그토록 오래 서로 못 본 끝에 우리가 만났다니 이상한 일이오.\""]], [["“We have missed because we tried to miss, I suppose.”", "\"우리가 서로 못 본 건 안 보려고 애썼기 때문이겠죠, 아마도.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["But you began that proceeding—by breaking a promise.”", "하지만 그 일을 시작한 건 당신이오—약속을 깨뜨려서 말이오.\""]], [["“It is scarcely worth while to talk of that now.", "\"이제 와 그 얘긴 할 만한 가치도 없어요."], ["We have formed other ties since then—you no less than I.”", "우리는 그 뒤로 다른 인연을 맺었으니까요—저 못지않게 당신도요.\""]], [["“I am sorry to hear that your husband is ill.”", "\"당신 남편이 편찮다니 안되었소.\""]], [["“He is not ill—only incapacitated.”", "\"편찮은 게 아니에요—그저 일을 못 하게 됐을 뿐이죠.\""]], [["“Yes—that is what I mean.", "\"그래요—내 말이 그 말이오."], ["I sincerely sympathize with you in your trouble.", "당신이 겪는 곤경에 진심으로 마음 아파하오."], ["Fate has treated you cruelly.”", "운명이 당신을 모질게 대했구려.\""]], [["She was silent awhile.", "그녀는 한동안 말이 없었다."], ["“Have you heard that he has chosen to work as a furze-cutter?”", "\"그가 가시금작화 베는 일을 하기로 했다는 걸 들으셨어요?\""], ["she said in a low, mournful voice.", "그녀가 낮고 구슬픈 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“It has been mentioned to me,”", "\"그런 얘기가 제게 전해지긴 했소.\""], ["answered Wildeve hesitatingly.", "와일디브가 머뭇거리며 대답했다."], ["“But I hardly believed it.”", "\"하지만 도무지 믿기지 않았소.\""]], [["“It is true.", "\"사실이에요."], ["What do you think of me as a furze-cutter’s wife?”", "가시금작화 베는 사내의 아내가 된 저를 어떻게 생각하세요?\""]], [["“I think the same as ever of you, Eustacia.", "\"나는 당신을 한결같이 생각하오, 유스테이샤."], ["Nothing of that sort can degrade you—you ennoble the occupation of your husband.”", "그런 종류의 일이 당신의 품위를 떨어뜨릴 수는 없소—당신은 남편의 일을 도리어 고귀하게 만드는 거요.\""]], [["“I wish I could feel it.”", "\"저도 그렇게 느낄 수 있다면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“Is there any chance of Mr. Yeobright getting better?”", "\"요브라이트 씨가 나아질 가망은 좀 있소?\""]], [["“He thinks so.", "\"그는 그렇게 생각해요."], ["I doubt it.”", "저는 미심쩍지만요.\""]], [["“I was quite surprised to hear that he had taken a cottage.", "\"그가 오두막을 얻었다는 말을 듣고 나는 자못 놀랐소."], ["I thought, in common with other people, that he would have taken you off to a home in Paris immediately after you had married him.", "나는 다른 사람들처럼, 그가 당신과 결혼하자마자 곧장 당신을 파리의 보금자리로 데려가리라 여겼었소."], ["‘What a gay, bright future she has before her!’ I thought.", "'그녀 앞에는 얼마나 흥겹고 빛나는 미래가 펼쳐져 있는가!'"], ["He will, I suppose, return there with you, if his sight gets strong again?”", "하고 나는 생각했소. 그의 시력이 다시 튼튼해지면, 그는 아마 당신과 함께 그리로 돌아가겠지요?\""]], [["Observing that she did not reply he regarded her more closely.", "그녀가 대답하지 않는 것을 보고 그는 그녀를 더 가까이 살폈다."], ["She was almost weeping.", "그녀는 거의 울고 있었다."], ["Images of a future never to be enjoyed, the revived sense of her bitter disappointment, the picture of the neighbour’s suspended ridicule which was raised by Wildeve’s words, had been too much for proud Eustacia’s equanimity.", "결코 누리지 못할 미래의 영상들, 쓰라린 환멸이 되살아난 느낌, 와일디브의 말이 불러일으킨 이웃들의 잠시 미뤄둔 비웃음의 그림—이 모든 것이 자존심 강한 유스테이샤의 평정에는 너무 벅찼던 것이다."]], [["Wildeve could hardly control his own too forward feelings when he saw her silent perturbation.", "와일디브는 그녀의 말없는 동요를 보자 너무 앞서 나가는 제 감정을 좀처럼 다스리기 어려웠다."], ["But he affected not to notice this, and she soon recovered her calmness.", "그러나 그는 이를 알아채지 못한 체했고, 그녀는 이내 평정을 되찾았다."]], [["“You do not intend to walk home by yourself?”", "\"혼자 걸어서 집에 갈 생각은 아니겠지요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“O yes,” said Eustacia.", "\"아뇨, 그럴 거예요.\""], ["“What could hurt me on this heath, who have nothing?”", "유스테이샤가 말했다. \"가진 것 하나 없는 제가 이 황야에서 무엇에 해를 입겠어요?\""]], [["“By diverging a little I can make my way home the same as yours.", "\"조금만 길을 돌면 나도 당신과 같은 길로 집에 갈 수 있소."], ["I shall be glad to keep you company as far as Throope Corner.”", "스루프 모퉁이까지라도 기꺼이 동무가 되어드리겠소.\""], ["Seeing that Eustacia sat on in hesitation he added, “Perhaps you think it unwise to be seen in the same road with me after the events of last summer?”", "유스테이샤가 망설이며 그대로 앉아 있는 것을 보고 그가 덧붙였다. \"지난여름의 일들이 있고 나서 나와 같은 길에 있는 걸 남에게 보이는 게 현명치 못하다고 여기는 거요?\""]], [["“Indeed I think no such thing,”", "\"정말이지 그런 건 전혀 생각지 않아요.\""], ["she said haughtily.", "그녀가 도도하게 말했다."], ["“I shall accept whose company I choose, for all that may be said by the miserable inhabitants of Egdon.”", "\"에그던의 그 가련한 주민들이 무어라 떠들든, 저는 제가 고른 사람의 동무를 받아들이겠어요.\""]], [["“Then let us walk on—if you are ready.", "\"그럼 걸어갑시다—채비가 되었다면 말이오."], ["Our nearest way is towards that holly bush with the dark shadow that you see down there.”", "우리의 가장 가까운 길은 저 아래 보이는, 그늘이 짙게 드리운 저 호랑가시나무 덤불 쪽이오.\""]], [["Eustacia arose, and walked beside him in the direction signified, brushing her way over the damping heath and fern, and followed by the strains of the merrymakers, who still kept up the dance.", "유스테이샤는 일어나 그가 가리킨 방향으로 그의 곁에서 걸었으니, 이슬에 젖어가는 히스와 고사리를 헤치고 길을 내며, 여전히 춤을 이어가는 흥겨운 이들의 가락을 등 뒤로 들으며 걸었다."], ["The moon had now waxed bright and silvery, but the heath was proof against such illumination, and there was to be observed the striking scene of a dark, rayless tract of country under an atmosphere charged from its zenith to its extremities with whitest light.", "달은 이제 환하고 은빛으로 차올라 있었으나, 황야는 그러한 빛 비춤에도 끄떡없었으니, 그리하여 진기한 정경이 펼쳐졌다, 천정에서 끝자락까지 더없이 흰 빛으로 가득 찬 대기 아래, 어둡고 빛살 하나 들지 않는 한 자락 들판이라는 정경이."], ["To an eye above them their two faces would have appeared amid the expanse like two pearls on a table of ebony.", "그들 위에서 내려다보는 눈이 있었다면, 그 두 사람의 얼굴은 그 너른 들판 가운데 흑단黑檀 탁자 위에 놓인 두 알의 진주처럼 보였을 것이다."]], [["On this account the irregularities of the path were not visible, and Wildeve occasionally stumbled; whilst Eustacia found it necessary to perform some graceful feats of balancing whenever a small tuft of heather or root of furze protruded itself through the grass of the narrow track and entangled her feet.", "이러한 까닭에 길의 울퉁불퉁함은 눈에 보이지 않았고, 와일디브는 이따금 발이 걸려 비틀거렸으며, 한편 유스테이샤는 작은 히스 덤불이나 가시금작화 뿌리가 그 좁은 길의 풀 사이로 불쑥 솟아나 발을 휘감을 때마다 우아하게 균형을 잡는 묘기를 부려야 했다."], ["At these junctures in her progress a hand was invariably stretched forward to steady her, holding her firmly until smooth ground was again reached, when the hand was again withdrawn to a respectful distance.", "그렇게 길을 가는 고비고비마다 그녀를 붙들어주려 한 손이 어김없이 앞으로 내밀어져, 평탄한 땅에 다시 이를 때까지 그녀를 단단히 붙잡았고, 그러고 나면 그 손은 다시 정중한 거리로 거두어졌다."]], [["They performed the journey for the most part in silence, and drew near to Throope Corner, a few hundred yards from which a short path branched away to Eustacia’s house.", "그들은 길의 대부분을 말없이 갔고, 스루프 모퉁이에 가까워졌으니, 거기서 몇백 야드 떨어진 곳에서 짧은 샛길 하나가 유스테이샤의 집으로 갈라져 나갔다."], ["By degrees they discerned coming towards them a pair of human figures, apparently of the male sex.", "차츰 그들은 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 한 쌍의 사람 형상을 알아보았는데, 보아하니 남자들인 듯했다."]], [["When they came a little nearer Eustacia broke the silence by saying, “One of those men is my husband.", "그들이 조금 더 가까이 왔을 때 유스테이샤가 침묵을 깨고 말했다. \"저 사람들 중 하나는 제 남편이에요."], ["He promised to come to meet me.”", "저를 마중 나오겠다고 약속했거든요.\""]], [["“And the other is my greatest enemy,”", "\"그리고 다른 하나는 내 가장 큰 원수요.\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."]], [["“It looks like Diggory Venn.”", "\"디고리 벤 같네요.\""]], [["“That is the man.”", "\"바로 그 사람이오.\""]], [["“It is an awkward meeting,”", "\"거북한 만남이네요.\""], ["said she; “but such is my fortune.", "그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 그게 제 팔자죠."], ["He knows too much about me, unless he could know more, and so prove to himself that what he now knows counts for nothing.", "그는 저에 대해 너무 많이 알아요, 더 많이 알아서 지금 자기가 아는 게 아무것도 아니라는 걸 스스로 증명해 보일 수 없는 한 말이죠."], ["Well, let it be—you must deliver me up to them.”", "뭐, 될 대로 되라죠—당신은 저를 저들에게 넘겨주셔야 해요.\""]], [["“You will think twice before you direct me to do that.", "\"내게 그렇게 하라고 시키기 전에 두 번은 생각하실 거요."], ["Here is a man who has not forgotten an item in our meetings at Rainbarrow—he is in company with your husband.", "저기 우리가 레인배로에서 만났던 일을 단 한 가지도 잊지 않은 사내가 있소—그가 당신 남편과 함께 있단 말이오."], ["Which of them, seeing us together here, will believe that our meeting and dancing at the gipsy party was by chance?”", "여기서 우리가 함께 있는 걸 보고서, 우리가 집시 잔치에서 만나 춤춘 게 우연이었다고 저들 중 누가 믿겠소?\""]], [["“Very well,”", "\"좋아요.\""], ["she whispered gloomily.", "그녀가 음울하게 속삭였다."], ["“Leave me before they come up.”", "\"저들이 올라오기 전에 저를 떠나세요.\""]], [["Wildeve bade her a tender farewell, and plunged across the fern and furze, Eustacia slowly walking on.", "와일디브는 그녀에게 다정한 작별을 고하고 고사리와 가시금작화를 가로질러 뛰어들었고, 유스테이샤는 천천히 계속 걸었다."], ["In two or three minutes she met her husband and his companion.", "이삼 분 뒤 그녀는 남편과 그 동행을 만났다."]], [["“My journey ends here for tonight, reddleman,”", "\"오늘 밤 내 여정은 여기서 끝나오, 레들맨.\""], ["said Yeobright as soon as he perceived her.", "요브라이트가 그녀를 알아보자마자 말했다."], ["“I turn back with this lady.", "\"나는 이 사람과 함께 돌아가겠소."], ["Good night.”", "잘 가시오.\""]], [["“Good night, Mr. Yeobright,”", "\"안녕히 가세요, 요브라이트 씨.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."], ["“I hope to see you better soon.”", "\"곧 더 나아지신 모습을 뵙기 바랍니다.\""]], [["The moonlight shone directly upon Venn’s face as he spoke, and revealed all its lines to Eustacia.", "벤이 말할 때 달빛이 그의 얼굴에 곧장 비쳐 그 모든 주름살을 유스테이샤에게 드러냈다."], ["He was looking suspiciously at her.", "그는 미심쩍은 눈으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다."], ["That Venn’s keen eye had discerned what Yeobright’s feeble vision had not—a man in the act of withdrawing from Eustacia’s side—was within the limits of the probable.", "그\n역시 빛에 익숙한 눈으로 베인이 알아본 것을 요브라이트의 약한 시력은 보지 못했던 것이니 — 유스테이시아 곁에서 막 물러나는 한 남자의 모습이었다 — 그것은 충분히 있을 법한 일의 범위 안에 있었다."]], [["If Eustacia had been able to follow the reddleman she would soon have found striking confirmation of her thought.", "만약 유스테이시아가 레들맨을 뒤따라갈 수 있었다면 자신의 생각을 뒷받침하는 놀라운 증거를 곧 발견했으리라."], ["No sooner had Clym given her his arm and led her off the scene than the reddleman turned back from the beaten track towards East Egdon, whither he had been strolling merely to accompany Clym in his walk, Diggory’s van being again in the neighbourhood.", "클림이 그녀에게 팔을 내주어 그 자리에서 데리고 나가자마자 레들맨은 이스트 에그던으로 향하던 다져진 길에서 발길을 돌렸는데, 그가 그쪽으로 어슬렁거리며 걸었던 것은 단지 클림의 산책에 동행하기 위해서였을 뿐, 디고리의 마차가 다시 그 근방에 와 있었기 때문이다."], ["Stretching out his long legs, he crossed the pathless portion of the heath somewhat in the direction which Wildeve had taken.", "그는 긴 다리를 쭉 뻗어 와일드브가 갔던 방향과 어느 정도 일치하는 쪽으로 황야의 길 없는 구간을 가로질렀다."], ["Only a man accustomed to nocturnal rambles could at this hour have descended those shaggy slopes with Venn’s velocity without falling headlong into a pit, or snapping off his leg by jamming his foot into some rabbit burrow.", "이 시각에 베인만한 속도로 그 텁수룩한 비탈을 내려가면서도 구덩이에 곤두박질치거나 토끼 굴에 발이 끼어 다리가 부러지지 않을 수 있는 사람은 밤마다 떠돌아다니는 데 익숙한 자뿐이었으리라."], ["But Venn went on without much inconvenience to himself, and the course of his scamper was towards the Quiet Woman Inn.", "그러나 베인은 별다른 불편 없이 나아갔고, 그가 내달리는 진로는 콰이엇 우먼 여관을 향하고 있었다."], ["This place he reached in about half an hour, and he was well aware that no person who had been near Throope Corner when he started could have got down here before him.", "그는 반 시간쯤 만에 그곳에 다다랐으며, 자신이 출발할 때 스루프 코너 부근에 있던 어떤 사람도 자기보다 먼저 여기 내려올 수는 없었으리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["The lonely inn was not yet closed, though scarcely an individual was there, the business done being chiefly with travellers who passed the inn on long journeys, and these had now gone on their way.", "외딴 여관은 아직 문을 닫지 않았으나 안에 있는 사람이라곤 거의 없었으니, 거기서 이루어지는 영업이란 주로 먼 여정에 그 여관을 지나가는 나그네들을 상대로 하는 것인데, 그들은 이제 제 갈 길을 떠난 뒤였다."], ["Venn went to the public room, called for a mug of ale, and inquired of the maid in an indifferent tone if Mr. Wildeve was at home.", "베인은 공동 객실로 들어가 에일 한 잔을 청하고는 무심한 어조로 하녀에게 와일드브 씨가 집에 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["Thomasin sat in an inner room and heard Venn’s voice.", "토머신은 안쪽 방에 앉아 있다가 베인의 목소리를 들었다."], ["When customers were present she seldom showed herself, owing to her inherent dislike for the business; but perceiving that no one else was there tonight she came out.", "손님이 있을 때면 그녀는 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않았으니, 이 일에 대한 타고난 거부감 때문이었는데, 오늘 밤은 거기에 다른 사람이 아무도 없음을 알아채고는 나왔다."]], [["“He is not at home yet, Diggory,”", "\"그이는 아직 안 들어왔어요, 디고리.\""], ["she said pleasantly.", "그녀가 상냥하게 말했다."], ["“But I expected him sooner.", "\"하지만 더 일찍 올 줄 알았는데."], ["He has been to East Egdon to buy a horse.”", "이스트 에그던에 말을 사러 갔거든요.\""]], [["“Did he wear a light wideawake?”", "\"밝은 색 챙 넓은 모자를 쓰고 있었나요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Then I saw him at Throope Corner, leading one home,” said Venn drily.", "\"그렇다면 스루프 코너에서 말 한 마리를 집으로 끌고 가는 그를 봤어요.\""], ["“A beauty, with a white face and a mane as black as night.", "베인이 무덤덤하게 말했다. \"얼굴은 희고 갈기는 밤처럼 까만 멋진 놈이더군요."], ["He will soon be here, no doubt.”", "곧 여기 오겠지요, 틀림없이.\""], ["Rising and looking for a moment at the pure, sweet face of Thomasin, over which a shadow of sadness had passed since the time when he had last seen her, he ventured to add, “Mr.", "그는 자리에서 일어나 토머신의 맑고 다정한 얼굴을 잠시 바라보았는데, 마지막으로 본 이래로 그 얼굴 위에는 슬픔의 그림자가 드리워 있었으니, 그는 한마디 덧붙이는 모험을 감행했다."], ["Wildeve seems to be often away at this time.”", "\"와일드브 씨가 요즘 자주 집을 비우는 것 같네요.\""]], [["“O yes,”", "\"아, 그럼요.\""], ["cried Thomasin in what was intended to be a tone of gaiety.", "토머신이 명랑한 어조로 들리도록 한 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Husbands will play the truant, you know.", "\"남편이란 게 다 잘 빠져나가잖아요."], ["I wish you could tell me of some secret plan that would help me to keep him home at my will in the evenings.”", "저녁마다 그이를 제 마음대로 집에 붙잡아둘 수 있는 무슨 비책이라도 일러주시면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“I will consider if I know of one,”", "\"아는 게 있는지 생각해보지요.\""], ["replied Venn in that same light tone which meant no lightness.", "베인은 가벼움이 전혀 담기지 않은 그 똑같이 가벼운 어조로 대답했다."], ["And then he bowed in a manner of his own invention and moved to go.", "그러고는 자기 나름으로 고안한 식으로 절을 하고 가려고 몸을 움직였다."], ["Thomasin offered him her hand; and without a sigh, though with food for many, the reddleman went out.", "토머신이 그에게 손을 내밀었고, 레들맨은 한숨 한 번 내쉬지 않았으나, 한숨 여러 번 쉴 거리를 안은 채 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["When Wildeve returned, a quarter of an hour later Thomasin said simply, and in the abashed manner usual with her now, “Where is the horse, Damon?”", "십오 분쯤 뒤에 와일드브가 돌아오자 토머신은 요즘 그녀에게 버릇이 된 그 머뭇거리는 태도로 그저 간단하게 말했다. \"말은 어디 있어요, 데이먼?\""]], [["“O, I have not bought it, after all.", "\"아, 결국 안 샀어."], ["The man asks too much.”", "그자가 너무 비싸게 부르더라고.\""]], [["“But somebody saw you at Throope Corner leading it home—a beauty, with a white face and a mane as black as night.”", "\"하지만 누가 당신이 스루프 코너에서 그걸 끌고 오는 걸 봤다던데요 — 얼굴은 희고 갈기는 밤처럼 까만 멋진 놈을요.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Wildeve, fixing his eyes upon her; “who told you that?”", "와일드브가 그녀에게 눈길을 고정하며 말했다. \"누가 그런 소릴 하던가?\""]], [["“Venn the reddleman.”", "\"레들맨 베인이요.\""]], [["The expression of Wildeve’s face became curiously condensed.", "와일드브의 얼굴 표정이 묘하게 응축되었다."], ["“That is a mistake—it must have been someone else,”", "\"그건 잘못 본 거야 — 다른 누군가였겠지.\""], ["he said slowly and testily, for he perceived that Venn’s countermoves had begun again.", "그는 느리고 신경질적으로 말했으니, 베인의 반격이 다시 시작되었음을 알아챘기 때문이다."]], [["IV. Rough Coercion Is Employed", "거친 강압이 동원되다"]], [["Those words of Thomasin, which seemed so little, but meant so much, remained in the ears of Diggory Venn: “Help me to keep him home in the evenings.”", "토머신의 그 말은 별것 아닌 듯하면서도 많은 것을 담고 있었으니, 디고리 베인의 귀에 그대로 남았다. \"저녁마다 그이를 집에 붙잡아두게 도와주세요.\""]], [["On this occasion Venn had arrived on Egdon Heath only to cross to the other side—he had no further connection with the interests of the Yeobright family, and he had a business of his own to attend to.", "이번에 베인이 에그던 황야에 온 것은 그저 반대편으로 건너가기 위해서였을 뿐 — 그는 요브라이트 가문의 이해관계와 더 이상 아무 연관이 없었고, 따로 처리해야 할 자기 일이 있었다."], ["Yet he suddenly began to feel himself drifting into the old track of manœuvring on Thomasin’s account.", "그러나 그는 문득 토머신을 위해 책략을 부리던 옛 궤도로 자신이 떠밀려 들어가고 있음을 느끼기 시작했다."]], [["He sat in his van and considered.", "그는 마차 안에 앉아 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["From Thomasin’s words and manner he had plainly gathered that Wildeve neglected her.", "토머신의 말과 태도로 보아 와일드브가 그녀를 등한시하고 있음을 그는 분명히 짐작할 수 있었다."], ["For whom could he neglect her if not for Eustacia?", "유스테이시아가 아니라면 그가 대체 누구 때문에 그녀를 등한시하겠는가?"], ["Yet it was scarcely credible that things had come to such a head as to indicate that Eustacia systematically encouraged him.", "하지만 유스테이시아가 조직적으로 그를 부추기고 있음을 가리킬 만큼 일이 그렇게까지 무르익었다는 것은 좀처럼 믿기 어려웠다."], ["Venn resolved to reconnoitre somewhat carefully the lonely road which led along the vale from Wildeve’s dwelling to Clym’s house at Alderworth.", "베인은 와일드브의 거처에서 골짜기를 따라 올더워스의 클림 집으로 이어지는 그 외딴 길을 어느 정도 조심스럽게 정찰하기로 마음먹었다."]], [["At this time, as has been seen, Wildeve was quite innocent of any predetermined act of intrigue, and except at the dance on the green he had not once met Eustacia since her marriage.", "이 무렵, 앞서 보았듯이 와일드브는 미리 작정한 어떤 음모적 행위와도 전혀 무관했고, 잔디밭 춤판에서를 제외하면 그녀가 결혼한 이래 유스테이시아를 한 번도 만난 적이 없었다."], ["But that the spirit of intrigue was in him had been shown by a recent romantic habit of his—a habit of going out after dark and strolling towards Alderworth, there looking at the moon and stars, looking at Eustacia’s house, and walking back at leisure.", "그러나 음모의 기운이 그의 안에 있었다는 것은 그가 최근에 들인 낭만적인 버릇 — 날이 어두워진 뒤에 나가서 올더워스 쪽으로 어슬렁거리며 거기서 달과 별을 바라보고, 유스테이시아의 집을 바라보고, 느긋하게 걸어 돌아오는 버릇으로 드러난 바 있다."]], [["Accordingly, when watching on the night after the festival, the reddleman saw him ascend by the little path, lean over the front gate of Clym’s garden, sigh, and turn to go back again.", "따라서 축제 다음 날 밤 망을 보던 레들맨은 그가 작은 오솔길을 따라 올라가 클림네 정원 앞 대문에 기대어 한숨을 쉬고는 다시 돌아가려고 몸을 돌리는 것을 보았다."], ["It was plain that Wildeve’s intrigue was rather ideal than real.", "와일드브의 음모란 실제적이라기보다는 관념적인 것임이 분명했다."], ["Venn retreated before him down the hill to a place where the path was merely a deep groove between the heather; here he mysteriously bent over the ground for a few minutes, and retired.", "베인은 그보다 앞서 언덕을 내려가 오솔길이 그저 히스 사이로 깊이 파인 고랑에 지나지 않는 곳까지 물러났는데, 여기서 그는 몇 분간 알 수 없는 모양새로 땅 위로 몸을 굽혔다가 자리를 떴다."], ["When Wildeve came on to that spot his ankle was caught by something, and he fell headlong.", "와일드브가 그 지점에 이르렀을 때 그의 발목이 무언가에 걸려 그는 곤두박질쳤다."]], [["As soon as he had recovered the power of respiration he sat up and listened.", "숨 쉴 힘을 되찾자마자 그는 일어나 앉아 귀를 기울였다."], ["There was not a sound in the gloom beyond the spiritless stir of the summer wind.", "어둠 속에는 여름 바람의 맥없는 일렁임 말고는 아무 소리도 없었다."], ["Feeling about for the obstacle which had flung him down, he discovered that two tufts of heath had been tied together across the path, forming a loop, which to a traveller was certain overthrow.", "자신을 넘어뜨린 장애물을 손으로 더듬어 찾던 그는 히스 두 다발이 길을 가로질러 서로 묶여 고리를 이루고 있음을 발견했으니, 그것은 나그네에게 영락없는 넘어짐을 안기는 것이었다."], ["Wildeve pulled off the string that bound them, and went on with tolerable quickness.", "와일드브는 그것들을 묶은 끈을 잡아 뜯어내고는 제법 빠르게 길을 갔다."], ["On reaching home he found the cord to be of a reddish colour.", "집에 다다라 보니 그 끈은 불그스름한 색이었다."], ["It was just what he had expected.", "그가 예상했던 바로 그것이었다."]], [["Although his weaknesses were not specially those akin to physical fear, this species of coup-de-Jarnac from one he knew too well troubled the mind of Wildeve.", "그의 약점이 특별히 육체적 공포에 가까운 부류는 아니었으나, 너무도 잘 아는 자에게서 받은 이런 종류의 기습은 와일드브의 마음을 어지럽혔다."], ["But his movements were unaltered thereby.", "그러나 그의 행보는 그로 인해 달라지지 않았다."], ["A night or two later he again went along the vale to Alderworth, taking the precaution of keeping out of any path.", "하루 이틀 뒤에 그는 다시 골짜기를 따라 올더워스로 갔으니, 어떤 오솔길에도 들어서지 않는 조심성을 발휘했다."], ["The sense that he was watched, that craft was employed to circumvent his errant tastes, added piquancy to a journey so entirely sentimental, so long as the danger was of no fearful sort.", "누가 자신을 감시하고 있다는 것, 자신의 일탈적 취향을 가로막으려고 책략이 동원되고 있다는 느낌은 — 위험이 무서운 종류가 아닌 한 — 이토록 전적으로 감상적인 여정에 짜릿함을 더해주었다."], ["He imagined that Venn and Mrs. Yeobright were in league, and felt that there was a certain legitimacy in combating such a coalition.", "그는 베인과 요브라이트 부인이 한패라고 짐작했고, 그런 연합에 맞서 싸우는 데에는 어떤 정당성이 있다고 느꼈다."]], [["The heath tonight appeared to be totally deserted; and Wildeve, after looking over Eustacia’s garden gate for some little time, with a cigar in his mouth, was tempted by the fascination that emotional smuggling had for his nature to advance towards the window, which was not quite closed, the blind being only partly drawn down.", "오늘 밤 황야는 완전히 인적이 끊긴 듯했고, 와일드브는 입에 시가를 문 채 잠시 유스테이시아의 정원 대문 너머를 들여다본 뒤, 감정의 밀수가 그의 천성에 안기는 매혹에 이끌려 창문 쪽으로 다가갔는데, 그 창문은 완전히 닫혀 있지 않았고 블라인드도 일부만 내려져 있었다."], ["He could see into the room, and Eustacia was sitting there alone.", "그는 방 안을 들여다볼 수 있었고, 유스테이시아는 거기 혼자 앉아 있었다."], ["Wildeve contemplated her for a minute, and then retreating into the heath beat the ferns lightly, whereupon moths flew out alarmed.", "와일드브는 잠시 그녀를 바라보다가 히스 속으로 물러나 양치류를 가볍게 두드렸더니, 그러자 나방들이 놀라 날아올랐다."], ["Securing one, he returned to the window, and holding the moth to the chink, opened his hand.", "한 마리를 붙잡은 그는 창문으로 돌아와 나방을 틈새에 대고 손을 폈다."], ["The moth made towards the candle upon Eustacia’s table, hovered round it two or three times, and flew into the flame.", "나방은 유스테이시아의 탁자 위 촛불을 향해 가더니 두세 번 그 둘레를 맴돌다가 불꽃 속으로 날아들었다."]], [["Eustacia started up.", "유스테이시아가 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["This had been a well-known signal in old times when Wildeve had used to come secretly wooing to Mistover.", "이것은 옛 시절 와일드브가 미스토버로 몰래 구애하러 오곤 하던 때의 잘 알려진 신호였다."], ["She at once knew that Wildeve was outside, but before she could consider what to do her husband came in from upstairs.", "그녀는 와일드브가 밖에 있음을 즉시 알아챘으나, 어떻게 해야 할지 미처 생각하기도 전에 남편이 위층에서 내려왔다."], ["Eustacia’s face burnt crimson at the unexpected collision of incidents, and filled it with an animation that it too frequently lacked.", "뜻하지 않은 사건들의 충돌에 유스테이시아의 얼굴이 진홍빛으로 타올랐으니, 너무도 자주 결여되어 있던 생기로 그 얼굴이 채워졌다."]], [["“You have a very high colour, dearest,”", "\"안색이 아주 좋구려, 여보.\""], ["said Yeobright, when he came close enough to see it.", "요브라이트가 그것을 볼 수 있을 만큼 가까이 다가오며 말했다."], ["“Your appearance would be no worse if it were always so.”", "\"늘 그렇다면 모습이 더 나빠지진 않을 텐데.\""]], [["“I am warm,”", "\"더워서요.\""], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이시아가 말했다."], ["“I think I will go into the air for a few minutes.”", "\"잠깐 바람 좀 쐬고 와야겠어요.\""]], [["“Shall I go with you?”", "\"같이 갈까?\""]], [["“O no.", "\"아, 아니에요."], ["I am only going to the gate.”", "대문까지만 갈 거예요.\""]], [["She arose, but before she had time to get out of the room a loud rapping began upon the front door.", "그녀는 일어섰으나 방을 나설 새도 없이 현관문에 요란한 두드림이 시작되었다."]], [["“I’ll go—I’ll go,”", "\"제가 갈게요 — 제가 갈게요.\""], ["said Eustacia in an unusually quick tone for her; and she glanced eagerly towards the window whence the moth had flown; but nothing appeared there.", "유스테이시아가 그녀답지 않게 빠른 어조로 말하고는 나방이 날아든 창문 쪽을 간절히 흘끗 보았으나, 거기엔 아무것도 나타나지 않았다."]], [["“You had better not at this time of the evening,”", "\"이 저녁 시간엔 자네가 안 나가는 게 낫겠어.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["Clym stepped before her into the passage, and Eustacia waited, her somnolent manner covering her inner heat and agitation.", "클림이 그녀보다 앞서 복도로 나섰고, 유스테이시아는 기다렸는데, 그 졸린 듯한 태도가 속의 열기와 동요를 가리고 있었다."]], [["She listened, and Clym opened the door.", "그녀는 귀를 기울였고, 클림이 문을 열었다."], ["No words were uttered outside, and presently he closed it and came back, saying, “Nobody was there.", "밖에서는 아무 말도 들리지 않았고, 이윽고 그가 문을 닫고 돌아오며 말했다. \"아무도 없는데."], ["I wonder what that could have meant?”", "대체 무슨 영문이었을까?\""]], [["He was left to wonder during the rest of the evening, for no explanation offered itself, and Eustacia said nothing, the additional fact that she knew of only adding more mystery to the performance.", "그는 그날 저녁 내내 영문을 모른 채 남겨졌으니, 아무런 해명도 떠오르지 않았고, 유스테이시아는 아무 말도 하지 않았던바, 자신만이 아는 추가적인 사실이 그 일에 더 큰 수수께끼만 보탤 뿐이었다."]], [["Meanwhile a little drama had been acted outside which saved Eustacia from all possibility of compromising herself that evening at least.", "한편 바깥에서는 작은 한 막의 연극이 펼쳐졌으니, 적어도 그날 저녁만큼은 유스테이시아가 자신의 입장을 위태롭게 할 모든 가능성에서 벗어나게 해준 것이었다."], ["Whilst Wildeve had been preparing his moth-signal another person had come behind him up to the gate.", "와일드브가 나방 신호를 준비하고 있는 동안 또 다른 사람이 그의 뒤를 따라 대문까지 올라와 있었다."], ["This man, who carried a gun in his hand, looked on for a moment at the other’s operation by the window, walked up to the house, knocked at the door, and then vanished round the corner and over the hedge.", "손에 총을 든 이 남자는 창가에서 벌어지는 상대방의 작업을 잠시 지켜보다가 집 쪽으로 걸어가 문을 두드리고는 모퉁이를 돌아 산울타리를 넘어 사라졌다."]], [["“Damn him!”", "\"빌어먹을 놈!\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일드브가 말했다."], ["“He has been watching me again.”", "\"또 나를 감시하고 있었구나.\""]], [["As his signal had been rendered futile by this uproarious rapping Wildeve withdrew, passed out at the gate, and walked quickly down the path without thinking of anything except getting away unnoticed.", "이 요란한 두드림 때문에 자신의 신호가 헛것이 되자 와일드브는 물러나 대문 밖으로 나갔고, 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 빠져나가는 것 말고는 아무 생각 없이 오솔길을 따라 빠르게 걸어 내려갔다."], ["Halfway down the hill the path ran near a knot of stunted hollies, which in the general darkness of the scene stood as the pupil in a black eye.", "언덕을 반쯤 내려갔을 때 오솔길은 키 작은 호랑가시나무 한 무더기 가까이로 지나갔는데, 그것은 전체적으로 어두운 풍경 속에서 검은 눈동자 속의 동공처럼 서 있었다."], ["When Wildeve reached this point a report startled his ear, and a few spent gunshots fell among the leaves around him.", "와일드브가 이 지점에 이르렀을 때 한 발의 총성이 그의 귀를 놀라게 했고, 다 타버린 산탄 몇 알이 그의 주위 나뭇잎들 사이로 떨어졌다."]], [["There was no doubt that he himself was the cause of that gun’s discharge; and he rushed into the clump of hollies, beating the bushes furiously with his stick; but nobody was there.", "그 총이 발사된 원인이 바로 자기 자신이라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었으니, 그는 호랑가시나무 무더기 속으로 뛰어들어 지팡이로 미친 듯이 덤불을 후려쳤으나 거기엔 아무도 없었다."], ["This attack was a more serious matter than the last, and it was some time before Wildeve recovered his equanimity.", "이 공격은 지난번보다 더 심각한 일이었고, 와일드브가 평정을 되찾기까지는 얼마간 시간이 걸렸다."], ["A new and most unpleasant system of menace had begun, and the intent appeared to be to do him grievous bodily harm.", "새롭고 더없이 불쾌한 위협의 방식이 시작되었으며, 그 의도는 그에게 중대한 신체적 위해를 가하려는 것으로 보였다."], ["Wildeve had looked upon Venn’s first attempt as a species of horseplay, which the reddleman had indulged in for want of knowing better; but now the boundary line was passed which divides the annoying from the perilous.", "와일드브는 베인의 첫 시도를 일종의 거친 장난, 곧 레들맨이 더 나은 방법을 몰라 부린 짓쯤으로 여겼으나, 이제는 성가신 것과 위태로운 것을 가르는 경계선이 넘어선 것이었다."]], [["Had Wildeve known how thoroughly in earnest Venn had become he might have been still more alarmed.", "베인이 얼마나 철저히 진지해졌는지를 와일드브가 알았더라면 그는 더욱 경악했으리라."], ["The reddleman had been almost exasperated by the sight of Wildeve outside Clym’s house, and he was prepared to go to any lengths short of absolutely shooting him, to terrify the young innkeeper out of his recalcitrant impulses.", "레들맨은 클림의 집 밖에 있는 와일드브의 모습을 보고 거의 격분한 상태였으며, 그 젊은 여관 주인의 반항적인 충동을 겁주어 몰아내기 위해서라면 실제로 그를 쏘아 죽이는 것을 제외하고는 어떤 극단까지라도 갈 각오가 되어 있었다."], ["The doubtful legitimacy of such rough coercion did not disturb the mind of Venn.", "그런 거친 강압의 미심쩍은 정당성은 베인의 마음을 어지럽히지 않았다."], ["It troubles few such minds in such cases, and sometimes this is not to be regretted.", "그런 경우에 그런 마음을 어지럽히는 일은 드물며, 때로는 그것이 유감스러운 일도 아니다."], ["From the impeachment of Strafford to Farmer Lynch’s short way with the scamps of Virginia there have been many triumphs of justice which are mockeries of law.", "스트래퍼드의 탄핵에서부터 버지니아의 무뢰배들을 다룬 린치 농부의 간단명료한 방식에 이르기까지, 법을 조롱하는 정의의 승리는 수없이 많았다."]], [["About half a mile below Clym’s secluded dwelling lay a hamlet where lived one of the two constables who preserved the peace in the parish of Alderworth, and Wildeve went straight to the constable’s cottage.", "클림의 외딴 거처에서 반 마일쯤 아래에 한 작은 마을이 있었는데, 올더워스 교구의 치안을 지키는 두 치안관 중 하나가 거기 살았으니, 와일드브는 곧장 그 치안관의 오두막으로 갔다."], ["Almost the first thing that he saw on opening the door was the constable’s truncheon hanging to a nail, as if to assure him that here were the means to his purpose.", "문을 열자마자 그가 거의 처음으로 본 것은 못에 걸려 있는 치안관의 곤봉이었는데, 마치 여기에 자기 목적을 이룰 수단이 있노라고 그를 안심시키려는 듯했다."], ["On inquiry, however, of the constable’s wife he learnt that the constable was not at home.", "그러나 치안관의 아내에게 물어보니 치안관은 집에 없다는 것이었다."], ["Wildeve said he would wait.", "와일드브는 기다리겠다고 말했다."]], [["The minutes ticked on, and the constable did not arrive.", "시간이 째깍째깍 흘러갔으나 치안관은 오지 않았다."], ["Wildeve cooled down from his state of high indignation to a restless dissatisfaction with himself, the scene, the constable’s wife, and the whole set of circumstances.", "와일드브는 격렬한 분노의 상태에서 가라앉아 자기 자신과 그 상황과 치안관의 아내와 모든 정황에 대한 안절부절못하는 불만으로 식어갔다."], ["He arose and left the house.", "그는 일어나 그 집을 떠났다."], ["Altogether, the experience of that evening had had a cooling, not to say a chilling, effect on misdirected tenderness, and Wildeve was in no mood to ramble again to Alderworth after nightfall in hope of a stray glance from Eustacia.", "무릇 그날 저녁의 경험은 잘못 향한 애정에 식히는, 아니 얼리는 효과를 미쳤다고 해도 좋을 것이었고, 와일드브는 유스테이시아의 우연한 눈길 한 번을 바라며 날 저문 뒤에 다시 올더워스로 어슬렁거릴 기분이 전혀 아니었다."]], [["Thus far the reddleman had been tolerably successful in his rude contrivances for keeping down Wildeve’s inclination to rove in the evening.", "여기까지 레들맨은 와일드브가 저녁에 쏘다니려는 성향을 억누르려는 자신의 투박한 책략에서 제법 성공을 거두었다."], ["He had nipped in the bud the possible meeting between Eustacia and her old lover this very night.", "그는 바로 이날 밤 유스테이시아와 그녀의 옛 연인 사이에 있을 수 있는 만남을 싹부터 잘라버렸다."], ["But he had not anticipated that the tendency of his action would be to divert Wildeve’s movement rather than to stop it.", "그러나 그는 자신의 행동이 가져올 경향이 와일드브의 움직임을 멈추기보다는 오히려 다른 데로 돌려놓는 것이리라고는 예상하지 못했다."], ["The gambling with the guineas had not conduced to make him a welcome guest to Clym; but to call upon his wife’s relative was natural, and he was determined to see Eustacia.", "기니 금화를 걸고 한 노름이 그를 클림에게 반가운 손님으로 만들어주진 못했으나, 아내의 친척을 찾아가는 것은 자연스러운 일이었고, 그는 유스테이시아를 보기로 작정했다."], ["It was necessary to choose some less untoward hour than ten o’clock at night.", "밤 열 시 같은 험상궂은 시각보다는 좀 덜 거북한 시간을 골라야 했다."], ["“Since it is unsafe to go in the evening,”", "\"저녁에 가는 게 안전하지 않으니,\""], ["he said, “I’ll go by day.”", "그가 말했다. \"낮에 가야겠다.\""]], [["Meanwhile Venn had left the heath and gone to call upon Mrs. Yeobright, with whom he had been on friendly terms since she had learnt what a providential countermove he had made towards the restitution of the family guineas.", "한편 베인은 황야를 떠나 요브라이트 부인을 찾아갔으니, 그가 집안의 기니 금화를 되찾는 쪽으로 얼마나 섭리적인 반격을 가했는지를 부인이 알게 된 이래로 둘은 우호적인 사이였다."], ["She wondered at the lateness of his call, but had no objection to see him.", "부인은 그가 늦은 시각에 찾아온 것을 의아해했으나 그를 만나는 데에는 이의가 없었다."]], [["He gave her a full account of Clym’s affliction, and of the state in which he was living; then, referring to Thomasin, touched gently upon the apparent sadness of her days.", "그는 부인에게 클림의 고통과 그가 살아가는 형편에 대해 소상히 이야기했고, 이어 토머신을 언급하며 그 나날의 역력한 슬픔을 부드럽게 건드렸다."], ["“Now, ma’am, depend upon it,”", "\"자, 마님, 제 말 믿으세요.\""], ["he said, “you couldn’t do a better thing for either of ’em than to make yourself at home in their houses, even if there should be a little rebuff at first.”", "그가 말했다. \"두 사람 누구에게든 마님이 그들 집에 스스럼없이 드나드는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없습니다, 처음엔 좀 퇴짜를 맞는다 해도 말이죠.\""]], [["“Both she and my son disobeyed me in marrying; therefore I have no interest in their households.", "\"그 애도 내 아들도 결혼하면서 나를 거역했네. 그러니 나는 그들의 살림에 아무 관심이 없어."], ["Their troubles are of their own making.”", "그들의 곤경은 제 손으로 만든 거야.\""], ["Mrs. Yeobright tried to speak severely; but the account of her son’s state had moved her more than she cared to show.", "요브라이트 부인은 매정하게 말하려 했으나, 아들의 형편에 관한 이야기는 그녀가 내색하고 싶은 것 이상으로 마음을 움직였다."]], [["“Your visits would make Wildeve walk straighter than he is inclined to do, and might prevent unhappiness down the heath.”", "\"마님이 찾아가시면 와일드브가 지금 마음먹은 것보다 더 똑바로 처신하게 될 거고, 저 아래 황야에서 불행이 생기는 걸 막을 수도 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 소린가?\""]], [["“I saw something tonight out there which I didn’t like at all.", "\"오늘 밤 저기서 영 마음에 들지 않는 무언가를 봤습니다."], ["I wish your son’s house and Mr. Wildeve’s were a hundred miles apart instead of four or five.”", "댁의 아드님 집과 와일드브 씨 집이 사오 마일이 아니라 백 마일쯤 떨어져 있으면 좋겠다는 생각이 듭니다.\""]], [["“Then there _was_ an understanding between him and Clym’s wife when he made a fool of Thomasin!”", "\"그렇다면 그자가 토머신을 우롱하던 그때에 그와 클림의 아내 사이에 정말로 무슨 내통이 있었구나!\""]], [["“We’ll hope there’s no understanding now.”", "\"지금은 내통이 없기를 바라야지요.\""]], [["“And our hope will probably be very vain.", "\"그 바람도 아마 한낱 헛것이겠지."], ["O Clym!", "아, 클림!"], ["O Thomasin!”", "아, 토머신!\""]], [["“There’s no harm done yet.", "\"아직 잘못된 일은 없습니다."], ["In fact, I’ve persuaded Wildeve to mind his own business.”", "사실 제가 와일드브더러 제 일에나 신경 쓰라고 설득해두었어요.\""]], [["“How?”", "\"어떻게?\""]], [["“O, not by talking—by a plan of mine called the silent system.”", "\"아, 말로 한 게 아니라 — 침묵의 방식이라는 제 나름의 계책으로요.\""]], [["“I hope you’ll succeed.”", "\"부디 성공하길 바라네.\""]], [["“I shall if you help me by calling and making friends with your son.", "\"마님이 아드님을 찾아가 가까워지는 걸로 저를 도와주신다면 성공할 겁니다."], ["You’ll have a chance then of using your eyes.”", "그러면 마님 눈으로 직접 살펴보실 기회도 생기고요.\""]], [["“Well, since it has come to this,” said Mrs.", "\"그래, 일이 이쯤 되었으니,\""], ["Yeobright sadly, “I will own to you, reddleman, that I thought of going.", "요브라이트 부인이 슬프게 말했다. \"자네에게 털어놓겠네, 레들맨, 나도 갈 생각을 하고 있었어."], ["I should be much happier if we were reconciled.", "화해할 수 있다면 훨씬 행복할 게야."], ["The marriage is unalterable, my life may be cut short, and I should wish to die in peace.", "그 결혼은 돌이킬 수 없고, 내 목숨은 길지 않을지도 모르니, 평안히 죽고 싶구나."], ["He is my only son; and since sons are made of such stuff I am not sorry I have no other.", "그 애는 내 하나뿐인 아들이고, 아들이란 게 그런 재목으로 빚어지는 것이라면 다른 아들이 없는 게 안타깝지도 않아."], ["As for Thomasin, I never expected much from her; and she has not disappointed me.", "토머신으로 말하자면 애초에 그 애에게 큰 기대를 하지 않았으니, 그 애도 나를 실망시키지 않았지."], ["But I forgave her long ago; and I forgive him now.", "하지만 그 애는 오래전에 용서했고, 이제 그자도 용서하겠네."], ["I’ll go.”", "가겠어.\""]], [["At this very time of the reddleman’s conversation with Mrs. Yeobright at Blooms-End another conversation on the same subject was languidly proceeding at Alderworth.", "레들맨이 블룸스엔드에서 요브라이트 부인과 이야기를 나누던 바로 그 시각에, 올더워스에서는 같은 주제에 관한 또 하나의 대화가 나른하게 이어지고 있었다."]], [["All the day Clym had borne himself as if his mind were too full of its own matter to allow him to care about outward things, and his words now showed what had occupied his thoughts.", "온종일 클림은 마치 마음이 제 일로 가득 차서 바깥 것에는 신경 쓸 겨를이 없는 사람처럼 처신했고, 이제 그의 말은 무엇이 그의 생각을 차지하고 있었는지를 드러냈다."], ["It was just after the mysterious knocking that he began the theme.", "그가 그 화제를 꺼낸 것은 바로 그 알 수 없는 두드림이 있은 직후였다."], ["“Since I have been away today, Eustacia, I have considered that something must be done to heal up this ghastly breach between my dear mother and myself.", "\"유스테이시아, 오늘 나가 있는 동안 생각해봤는데, 나와 어머니 사이의 이 끔찍한 균열을 메우기 위해 뭔가 해야겠어."], ["It troubles me.”", "그게 나를 괴롭혀.\""]], [["“What do you propose to do?”", "\"어떻게 하실 작정인데요?\""], ["said Eustacia abstractedly, for she could not clear away from her the excitement caused by Wildeve’s recent manœuvre for an interview.", "유스테이시아가 멍하니 말했으니, 와일드브가 최근에 면담을 노려 부린 책략이 일으킨 흥분을 떨쳐낼 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["“You seem to take a very mild interest in what I propose, little or much,”", "\"내가 무슨 제안을 하든 당신은 거기에 영 시들한 관심밖에 없어 보이는군.\""], ["said Clym, with tolerable warmth.", "클림이 제법 격해진 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“You mistake me,”", "\"오해하시는 거예요.\""], ["she answered, reviving at his reproach.", "그녀가 그의 책망에 정신을 차리며 대답했다."], ["“I am only thinking.”", "\"그냥 생각에 잠겨 있었을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“What of?”", "\"무슨 생각?\""]], [["“Partly of that moth whose skeleton is getting burnt up in the wick of the candle,”", "\"한편으론 저 나방 생각요, 골격이 촛불 심지에서 타들어가고 있는 저 나방요.\""], ["she said slowly.", "그녀가 느릿느릿 말했다."], ["“But you know I always take an interest in what you say.”", "\"하지만 당신이 하는 말엔 늘 관심이 있다는 거 아시잖아요.\""]], [["“Very well, dear.", "\"좋아, 여보."], ["Then I think I must go and call upon her.”", "그럼 내가 가서 어머니를 찾아뵈어야겠어.\""], ["...He went on with tender feeling: “It is a thing I am not at all too proud to do, and only a fear that I might irritate her has kept me away so long.", "…그가 다정한 심정으로 말을 이었다. \"그건 내가 자존심 때문에 못 할 일은 전혀 아니고, 다만 어머니를 자극할까 두려운 마음에 이렇게 오래 멀리해온 것뿐이야."], ["But I must do something.", "하지만 뭔가는 해야겠어."], ["It is wrong in me to allow this sort of thing to go on.”", "이런 식의 일을 계속 내버려두는 건 내 잘못이지.\""]], [["“What have you to blame yourself about?”", "\"무엇 때문에 자책하시는 거예요?\""]], [["“She is getting old, and her life is lonely, and I am her only son.”", "\"어머니는 늙어가고, 그 삶은 외로우며, 나는 어머니의 하나뿐인 아들이잖아.\""]], [["“She has Thomasin.”", "\"토머신이 있잖아요.\""]], [["“Thomasin is not her daughter; and if she were that would not excuse me.", "\"토머신은 어머니의 딸이 아니야. 설령 딸이라 해도 그것이 나를 면죄해주진 못하지."], ["But this is beside the point.", "하지만 그건 요점에서 벗어난 얘기야."], ["I have made up my mind to go to her, and all I wish to ask you is whether you will do your best to help me—that is, forget the past; and if she shows her willingness to be reconciled, meet her halfway by welcoming her to our house, or by accepting a welcome to hers?”", "나는 어머니를 찾아뵙기로 마음을 굳혔고, 당신에게 부탁하고 싶은 건 오직 하나, 나를 도우려고 최선을 다해주겠느냐는 거야 — 그러니까 과거를 잊고, 만약 어머니가 화해할 뜻을 보이거든 우리 집에 어머니를 맞아들이든 어머니 집의 환대를 받아들이든 해서 중간에서 손을 마주잡아주겠어?\""]], [["At first Eustacia closed her lips as if she would rather do anything on the whole globe than what he suggested.", "처음에 유스테이시아는 마치 그가 제안한 일보다는 차라리 이 세상 무슨 일이라도 하겠다는 듯이 입술을 꾹 다물었다. 그러나 입가의 선이 생각에 잠겨 누그러졌는데, 누그러질 수 있을 만큼까지는 아니었으나, 그녀는 말했다."], ["But the lines of her mouth softened with thought, though not so far as they might have softened, and she said, “I will put nothing in your way; but after what has passed it is asking too much that I go and make advances.”", "\"당신 가는 길을 막지는 않겠어요. 하지만 그간 일어난 일을 생각하면 제가 가서 먼저 손을 내밀라는 건 지나친 요구예요.\""]], [["“You never distinctly told me what did pass between you.”", "\"당신과 어머니 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지 분명하게 말해준 적이 한 번도 없잖아.\""]], [["“I could not do it then, nor can I now.", "\"그때도 그럴 수 없었고, 지금도 그럴 수 없어요."], ["Sometimes more bitterness is sown in five minutes than can be got rid of in a whole life; and that may be the case here.”", "때로는 단 오 분 동안에 한평생을 다 써도 떨쳐낼 수 없는 쓰라림이 뿌려지기도 하는데, 여기가 그런 경우일지도 모르죠.\""], ["She paused a few moments, and added, “If you had never returned to your native place, Clym, what a blessing it would have been for you!...", "그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. \"당신이 고향에 돌아오지만 않았더라면, 클림, 당신에게 얼마나 큰 복이었을까요!…"], ["It has altered the destinies of——”", "그 일이 운명을 바꿔놓았어요, 사람들의——\""]], [["“Three people.”", "\"세 사람의 운명을.\""]], [["“Five,”", "'다섯 사람의.'"], ["Eustacia thought; but she kept that in.", "유스테이시아는 그렇게 생각했으나 그것은 속에 묻어두었다."]], [["V. The Journey across the Heath", "황야를 가로지르는 여정"]], [["Thursday, the thirty-first of August, was one of a series of days during which snug houses were stifling, and when cool draughts were treats; when cracks appeared in clayey gardens, and were called “earthquakes” by apprehensive children; when loose spokes were discovered in the wheels of carts and carriages; and when stinging insects haunted the air, the earth, and every drop of water that was to be found.", "팔월 삼십일일 목요일은, 아늑한 집들이 숨막히고 시원한 바람결이 진미가 되는, 점토질 정원에 갈라진 틈이 생겨나 겁 많은 아이들이 그것을 \"지진\"이라 부르는, 짐수레와 마차 바퀴에서 헐거워진 살이 발견되는, 쏘아대는 벌레들이 공중과 땅과 찾을 수 있는 물 한 방울 한 방울마다 들끓는 그런 날들 가운데 하나였다."]], [["In Mrs. Yeobright’s garden large-leaved plants of a tender kind flagged by ten o’clock in the morning; rhubarb bent downward at eleven; and even stiff cabbages were limp by noon.", "요브라이트 부인의 정원에서는 잎이 큰 연약한 식물들이 아침 열 시면 시들었고, 대황은 열한 시면 아래로 휘었으며, 뻣뻣한 양배추마저도 정오면 축 늘어졌다."]], [["It was about eleven o’clock on this day that Mrs. Yeobright started across the heath towards her son’s house, to do her best in getting reconciled with him and Eustacia, in conformity with her words to the reddleman.", "요브라이트 부인이 레들맨에게 한 말에 따라 아들과 유스테이시아와 화해해보려고 최선을 다하고자 황야를 가로질러 아들의 집을 향해 출발한 것은 이날 열한 시쯤이었다."], ["She had hoped to be well advanced in her walk before the heat of the day was at its highest, but after setting out she found that this was not to be done.", "부인은 하루의 더위가 가장 심해지기 전에 걸음을 상당히 진척시키기를 바랐으나, 막상 나서고 보니 그렇게 될 수 없음을 알았다."], ["The sun had branded the whole heath with its mark, even the purple heath-flowers having put on a brownness under the dry blazes of the few preceding days.", "해는 황야 전체에 자신의 낙인을 찍어놓았으니, 보랏빛 히스 꽃마저도 앞서 며칠간의 메마른 불볕 아래 갈색을 띠고 있었다."], ["Every valley was filled with air like that of a kiln, and the clean quartz sand of the winter water-courses, which formed summer paths, had undergone a species of incineration since the drought had set in.", "골짜기마다 가마 속 같은 공기가 들어차 있었고, 여름철 오솔길을 이루던 겨울 물길의 깨끗한 석영 모래는 가뭄이 시작된 이래 일종의 소성燒成을 겪은 터였다."]], [["In cool, fresh weather Mrs. Yeobright would have found no inconvenience in walking to Alderworth, but the present torrid attack made the journey a heavy undertaking for a woman past middle age; and at the end of the third mile she wished that she had hired Fairway to drive her a portion at least of the distance.", "서늘하고 상쾌한 날씨였다면 요브라이트 부인은 올더워스까지 걷는 데 아무런 불편이 없었으련만, 지금의 이 작열하는 공격은 중년을 넘긴 여인에게 그 여정을 힘겨운 과업으로 만들었으니, 삼 마일째가 끝날 무렵 그녀는 적어도 거리의 일부라도 페어웨이를 시켜 마차로 데려다 달라고 할 것을 그랬다고 후회했다."], ["But from the point at which she had arrived it was as easy to reach Clym’s house as to get home again.", "그러나 그녀가 다다른 지점에서는 다시 집으로 돌아가는 것이나 클림의 집에 이르는 것이나 매한가지로 수월했다."], ["So she went on, the air around her pulsating silently, and oppressing the earth with lassitude.", "그래서 그녀는 계속 나아갔으니, 주위의 공기는 소리 없이 고동치며 땅을 나른함으로 짓누르고 있었다."], ["She looked at the sky overhead, and saw that the sapphirine hue of the zenith in spring and early summer had been replaced by a metallic violet.", "그녀는 머리 위 하늘을 올려다보았고, 봄과 초여름이면 천정天頂을 물들이던 사파이어빛 색조가 금속성 보랏빛으로 대체되어 있음을 보았다."]], [["Occasionally she came to a spot where independent worlds of ephemerons were passing their time in mad carousal, some in the air, some on the hot ground and vegetation, some in the tepid and stringy water of a nearly dried pool.", "이따금 그녀는 하루살이들의 독립된 세계가 미친 듯한 잔치로 시간을 보내고 있는 곳에 다다랐으니, 어떤 것들은 공중에서, 어떤 것들은 뜨거운 땅과 초목 위에서, 또 어떤 것들은 거의 말라붙은 웅덩이의 미적지근하고 끈적한 물속에서 그러했다."], ["All the shallower ponds had decreased to a vaporous mud amid which the maggoty shapes of innumerable obscure creatures could be indistinctly seen, heaving and wallowing with enjoyment.", "더 얕은 못들은 모두 김 서린 진흙으로 졸아들어 있었고, 그 속에서는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 이름 모를 생물들의 구더기 같은 형체들이 즐거이 들썩이며 뒹구는 것이 어렴풋이 보였다."], ["Being a woman not disinclined to philosophize she sometimes sat down under her umbrella to rest and to watch their happiness, for a certain hopefulness as to the result of her visit gave ease to her mind, and between important thoughts left it free to dwell on any infinitesimal matter which caught her eyes.", "사색하기를 싫어하지 않는 여인이었던지라 그녀는 이따금 우산 아래 앉아 쉬면서 그들의 행복을 지켜보았으니, 방문의 결과에 대한 어떤 희망이 그녀의 마음에 편안함을 주어, 중대한 생각들 사이에서 마음이 눈에 들어오는 자질구레한 일이라면 무엇에든 자유로이 머물 수 있게 한 것이었다."]], [["Mrs. Yeobright had never before been to her son’s house, and its exact position was unknown to her.", "요브라이트 부인은 전에 아들의 집에 가본 적이 한 번도 없었던지라 그 정확한 위치를 알지 못했다."], ["She tried one ascending path and another, and found that they led her astray.", "그녀는 오르막길을 이리저리 시험해보았으나 그것들이 자신을 엉뚱한 데로 이끈다는 것을 알았다."], ["Retracing her steps, she came again to an open level, where she perceived at a distance a man at work.", "발걸음을 되짚어 그녀는 다시 탁 트인 평지에 이르렀는데, 거기서 멀찍이 한 남자가 일하고 있는 것을 알아보았다."], ["She went towards him and inquired the way.", "그녀는 그에게로 가서 길을 물었다."]], [["The labourer pointed out the direction, and added, “Do you see that furze-cutter, ma’am, going up that footpath yond?”", "그 일꾼은 방향을 가리켜주고는 덧붙였다. \"마님, 저기 저 오솔길을 올라가는 가시금작화 베는 사람이 보이십니까?\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright strained her eyes, and at last said that she did perceive him.", "요브라이트 부인은 눈을 잔뜩 부릅뜨고 보다가 마침내 그가 보인다고 말했다."]], [["“Well, if you follow him you can make no mistake.", "\"그럼, 그 사람을 따라가시면 틀림없을 겁니다."], ["He’s going to the same place, ma’am.”", "같은 데로 가는 길이거든요, 마님.\""]], [["She followed the figure indicated.", "그녀는 가리켜진 그 형체를 따라갔다."], ["He appeared of a russet hue, not more distinguishable from the scene around him than the green caterpillar from the leaf it feeds on.", "그는 적갈색을 띠어, 그 둘레의 풍경과 구별되는 정도가 잎을 갉아먹는 초록 애벌레가 그 잎에서 구별되는 정도보다 더하지 않았다."], ["His progress when actually walking was more rapid than Mrs. Yeobright’s; but she was enabled to keep at an equable distance from him by his habit of stopping whenever he came to a brake of brambles, where he paused awhile.", "실제로 걸을 때 그의 속도는 요브라이트 부인보다 빨랐으나, 그가 가시덤불 수풀에 이를 때마다 멈춰 서서 한동안 머무는 버릇 덕분에 그녀는 그와 일정한 거리를 유지할 수 있었다."], ["On coming in her turn to each of these spots she found half a dozen long limp brambles which he had cut from the bush during his halt and laid out straight beside the path.", "그런 곳에 차례로 다다를 때마다 그녀는 그가 멈춘 동안 덤불에서 베어내 오솔길 옆에 곧게 펴놓은 길고 늘어진 가시덩굴 대여섯 가닥을 발견했다."], ["They were evidently intended for furze-faggot bonds which he meant to collect on his return.", "그것들은 그가 돌아오는 길에 거두어가려는 가시금작화 단 묶음 끈으로 쓸 작정인 것이 분명했다."]], [["The silent being who thus occupied himself seemed to be of no more account in life than an insect.", "이렇게 일에 몰두한 그 말 없는 존재는 한 마리 곤충보다 더 대단할 것이 없어 보였다."], ["He appeared as a mere parasite of the heath, fretting its surface in his daily labour as a moth frets a garment, entirely engrossed with its products, having no knowledge of anything in the world but fern, furze, heath, lichens, and moss.", "그는 황야의 한낱 기생충처럼 보였으니, 나방이 옷가지를 갉듯 날마다의 노동으로 그 표면을 갉아대며, 그 산물에 온통 정신이 팔려, 양치류와 가시금작화와 히스와 지의류와 이끼 말고는 이 세상 어떤 것도 알지 못했다."]], [["The furze-cutter was so absorbed in the business of his journey that he never turned his head; and his leather-legged and gauntleted form at length became to her as nothing more than a moving handpost to show her the way.", "가시금작화 베는 이는 제 여정의 일에 너무도 몰두해 한 번도 고개를 돌리지 않았고, 가죽 각반을 차고 토시를 낀 그의 형체는 마침내 그녀에게는 길을 일러주는 움직이는 이정표 외에 아무것도 아닌 것이 되었다."], ["Suddenly she was attracted to his individuality by observing peculiarities in his walk.", "그러다 문득 그녀는 그의 걸음걸이에서 어떤 특이한 점을 알아채고는 그 사람됨에 마음이 끌렸다."], ["It was a gait she had seen somewhere before; and the gait revealed the man to her, as the gait of Ahimaaz in the distant plain made him known to the watchman of the king.", "그것은 어디선가 전에 본 적 있는 걸음걸이였으니, 그 걸음걸이가 마치 먼 들판에서의 아히마아스의 걸음걸이가 그를 임금의 파수꾼에게 알려주었듯 그 사람을 그녀에게 알려주었다."], ["“His walk is exactly as my husband’s used to be,”", "\"저 걸음걸이는 내 남편이 걷던 것과 똑같구나.\""], ["she said; and then the thought burst upon her that the furze-cutter was her son.", "그녀가 말했고, 그러자 저 가시금작화 베는 이가 제 아들이라는 생각이 그녀를 덮쳐왔다."]], [["She was scarcely able to familiarize herself with this strange reality.", "그녀는 이 기이한 현실에 좀처럼 자신을 익숙하게 만들 수 없었다."], ["She had been told that Clym was in the habit of cutting furze, but she had supposed that he occupied himself with the labour only at odd times, by way of useful pastime; yet she now beheld him as a furze-cutter and nothing more—wearing the regulation dress of the craft, and thinking the regulation thoughts, to judge by his motions.", "클림이 가시금작화를 베는 습관이 있다는 말은 들었으나, 그녀는 그가 그 노동에 매달리는 것이 어쩌다 한 번씩, 유익한 소일거리 삼아 하는 것이려니 짐작했었다. 그런데 이제 그녀는 그가 가시금작화 베는 이, 그 이상도 이하도 아닌 모습으로 — 그 일의 정해진 복장을 걸치고, 그 몸짓으로 미루어 보건대 정해진 생각을 하면서 있는 것을 보고 있었다."], ["Planning a dozen hasty schemes for at once preserving him and Eustacia from this mode of life, she throbbingly followed the way, and saw him enter his own door.", "그와 유스테이시아를 이런 삶의 방식에서 당장 구해낼 열두 가지 황급한 방책을 궁리하며 그녀는 가슴을 두근거리며 길을 따라갔고, 그가 제 집 문으로 들어가는 것을 보았다."]], [["At one side of Clym’s house was a knoll, and on the top of the knoll a clump of fir trees so highly thrust up into the sky that their foliage from a distance appeared as a black spot in the air above the crown of the hill.", "클림의 집 한쪽에는 작은 둔덕이 있었고, 그 둔덕 꼭대기에는 전나무 한 무더기가 하늘 높이 솟구쳐 있어서 멀리서 보면 그 잎이 언덕 마루 위 공중의 검은 점처럼 보였다."], ["On reaching this place Mrs. Yeobright felt distressingly agitated, weary, and unwell.", "이곳에 다다랐을 때 요브라이트 부인은 괴로울 만큼 동요하고 지치고 몸이 좋지 않음을 느꼈다."], ["She ascended, and sat down under their shade to recover herself, and to consider how best to break the ground with Eustacia, so as not to irritate a woman underneath whose apparent indolence lurked passions even stronger and more active than her own.", "그녀는 둔덕을 올라가 그 그늘 아래 앉아 기운을 차리고, 유스테이시아에게 어떻게 말문을 떼는 것이 가장 좋을지를 — 겉으로 드러난 나태함 아래 자기 못지않게, 아니 더 강하고 활발한 격정이 도사리고 있는 그 여인을 자극하지 않도록 — 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["The trees beneath which she sat were singularly battered, rude, and wild, and for a few minutes Mrs. Yeobright dismissed thoughts of her own storm-broken and exhausted state to contemplate theirs.", "그녀가 그 아래 앉은 나무들은 유난히 두들겨 맞고 거칠고 사나운 모습이었으니, 잠시 요브라이트 부인은 폭풍에 꺾이고 기진한 제 자신의 상태에 대한 생각을 떨치고 그것들의 상태를 응시했다."], ["Not a bough in the nine trees which composed the group but was splintered, lopped, and distorted by the fierce weather that there held them at its mercy whenever it prevailed.", "그 무리를 이루는 아홉 그루 나무에서 그 어느 가지 하나도 사나운 날씨에 쪼개지고 잘리고 뒤틀리지 않은 것이 없었으니, 그 날씨는 기승을 부릴 때마다 거기서 그것들을 제멋대로 다루었다."], ["Some were blasted and split as if by lightning, black stains as from fire marking their sides, while the ground at their feet was strewn with dead fir-needles and heaps of cones blown down in the gales of past years.", "어떤 것들은 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 시들고 갈라졌으며, 불에 그은 듯한 검은 자국이 옆구리에 나 있었고, 그 발치 땅바닥에는 죽은 솔잎과 지난 세월의 강풍에 떨어진 솔방울 더미가 흩어져 있었다."], ["The place was called the Devil’s Bellows, and it was only necessary to come there on a March or November night to discover the forcible reasons for that name.", "그곳은 악마의 풀무라 불렸는데, 그 이름의 강력한 까닭을 알아내려면 삼월이나 십일월의 어느 밤에 거기 와보기만 하면 되었다."], ["On the present heated afternoon, when no perceptible wind was blowing, the trees kept up a perpetual moan which one could hardly believe to be caused by the air.", "눈에 띄는 바람이라곤 불지 않는 이 무더운 오후에도 나무들은 끊임없는 신음을 이어갔으니, 그것이 공기로 인한 것이라고는 좀처럼 믿기 어려웠다."]], [["Here she sat for twenty minutes or more ere she could summon resolution to go down to the door, her courage being lowered to zero by her physical lassitude.", "그녀는 문으로 내려갈 결심을 그러모을 수 있기까지 여기서 이십 분 남짓 앉아 있었으니, 육체적 나른함에 그 용기가 영점까지 떨어진 것이었다."], ["To any other person than a mother it might have seemed a little humiliating that she, the elder of the two women, should be the first to make advances.", "어머니가 아닌 다른 어떤 사람에게는, 두 여인 중 손위인 자신이 먼저 손을 내미는 것이 다소 굴욕적으로 여겨졌을지도 모른다."], ["But Mrs. Yeobright had well considered all that, and she only thought how best to make her visit appear to Eustacia not abject but wise.", "그러나 요브라이트 부인은 그 모든 것을 충분히 헤아린 터였고, 그녀는 다만 자신의 방문이 유스테이시아에게 비굴한 것이 아니라 현명한 것으로 비치게 할 가장 좋은 방법만을 생각했다."]], [["From her elevated position the exhausted woman could perceive the roof of the house below, and the garden and the whole enclosure of the little domicile.", "그 높은 자리에서 지친 여인은 아래쪽 집의 지붕과 정원, 그리고 그 작은 거처의 울안 전체를 살펴볼 수 있었다."], ["And now, at the moment of rising, she saw a second man approaching the gate.", "그런데 막 일어서려는 순간, 그녀는 또 한 남자가 대문으로 다가오는 것을 보았다."], ["His manner was peculiar, hesitating, and not that of a person come on business or by invitation.", "그의 태도는 묘하고 머뭇거리는 것이었으며, 볼일이 있어서나 초대를 받아 온 사람의 태도가 아니었다."], ["He surveyed the house with interest, and then walked round and scanned the outer boundary of the garden, as one might have done had it been the birthplace of Shakespeare, the prison of Mary Stuart, or the Château of Hougomont.", "그는 흥미로운 듯 집을 둘러보고는 빙 돌아가 정원의 바깥 경계를 살폈는데, 마치 그곳이 셰익스피어의 출생지나 메리 스튜어트의 감옥이나 우구몽의 성관城館이라도 되는 양 했다."], ["After passing round and again reaching the gate he went in. Mrs.", "빙 돌고 나서 다시 대문에 이른 그는 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Yeobright was vexed at this, having reckoned on finding her son and his wife by themselves; but a moment’s thought showed her that the presence of an acquaintance would take off the awkwardness of her first appearance in the house, by confining the talk to general matters until she had begun to feel comfortable with them.", "요브라이트 부인은 이에 언짢았으니, 아들과 며느리만 있으리라고 셈해두었기 때문이다. 그러나 한순간 생각해보니, 아는 사람이 있으면 자신이 익숙해질 때까지 대화가 일반적인 사안에 한정됨으로써 그 집에 처음 나타나는 어색함이 가시리라는 것을 알았다."], ["She came down the hill to the gate, and looked into the hot garden.", "그녀는 언덕을 내려가 대문에 이르렀고, 뜨거운 정원 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["There lay the cat asleep on the bare gravel of the path, as if beds, rugs, and carpets were unendurable.", "거기 오솔길의 맨 자갈 위에 고양이가 잠들어 있었으니, 마치 잠자리며 깔개며 양탄자가 견딜 수 없다는 듯했다."], ["The leaves of the hollyhocks hung like half-closed umbrellas, the sap almost simmered in the stems, and foliage with a smooth surface glared like metallic mirrors.", "접시꽃 잎은 반쯤 접힌 우산처럼 늘어졌고, 줄기 속에서는 수액이 거의 끓어오를 지경이었으며, 매끄러운 표면의 잎사귀는 금속 거울처럼 번쩍였다."], ["A small apple tree, of the sort called Ratheripe, grew just inside the gate, the only one which throve in the garden, by reason of the lightness of the soil; and among the fallen apples on the ground beneath were wasps rolling drunk with the juice, or creeping about the little caves in each fruit which they had eaten out before stupefied by its sweetness.", "대문 바로 안쪽에는 래더라이프라 불리는 종류의 작은 사과나무 한 그루가 자라고 있었는데, 흙이 가벼운 덕분에 그 정원에서 잘 자란 유일한 나무였다. 그 아래 땅에 떨어진 사과들 사이로는 과즙에 취해 뒹구는 말벌들이 있었고, 그 단맛에 마취되기 전에 열매마다 파먹어놓은 작은 굴 속을 기어 다니는 것들도 있었다."], ["By the door lay Clym’s furze-hook and the last handful of faggot-bonds she had seen him gather; they had plainly been thrown down there as he entered the house.", "문 옆에는 클림의 가시금작화 낫과, 그가 거두는 것을 그녀가 본 마지막 한 줌의 단 묶음 끈이 놓여 있었으니, 그가 집에 들어가면서 거기에 던져놓은 것이 분명했다."]], [["VI. A Conjuncture, and Its Result upon the Pedestrian", "우연한 마주침, 그리고 그것이 보행자에게 미친 결과"]], [["Wildeve, as has been stated, was determined to visit Eustacia boldly, by day, and on the easy terms of a relation, since the reddleman had spied out and spoilt his walks to her by night.", "앞서 말했듯이 와일드브는, 레들맨이 자신이 밤에 그녀에게 가는 길을 염탐해내어 망쳐놓았으므로, 대놓고 낮에, 친척이라는 편한 명분으로 유스테이시아를 찾아가기로 작정한 터였다."], ["The spell that she had thrown over him in the moonlight dance made it impossible for a man having no strong puritanic force within him to keep away altogether.", "달빛 춤판에서 그녀가 그에게 걸어놓은 마법은, 내면에 강한 청교도적 힘을 갖추지 못한 남자로서는 도저히 아주 멀리할 수 없게 만들었다."], ["He merely calculated on meeting her and her husband in an ordinary manner, chatting a little while, and leaving again.", "그는 그저 그녀와 그녀의 남편을 평범하게 만나 잠시 담소를 나누고 다시 떠나는 것만을 계산했다."], ["Every outward sign was to be conventional; but the one great fact would be there to satisfy him—he would see her.", "겉으로 드러나는 모든 표시는 관습에 맞을 것이로되, 그를 만족시킬 단 하나의 큰 사실이 거기 있을 터였으니 — 그는 그녀를 보게 되리라는 것이었다."], ["He did not even desire Clym’s absence, since it was just possible that Eustacia might resent any situation which could compromise her dignity as a wife, whatever the state of her heart towards him.", "그는 클림이 없기를 바라지조차 않았으니, 그녀의 마음이 그에게 어떠하든 간에 아내로서의 위엄을 위태롭게 할 수 있는 어떤 상황이라도 유스테이시아가 못마땅해할 가능성이 충분히 있었기 때문이다."], ["Women were often so.", "여자들은 흔히 그러했다."]], [["He went accordingly; and it happened that the time of his arrival coincided with that of Mrs. Yeobright’s pause on the hill near the house.", "그리하여 그는 갔고, 그가 도착한 시각이 요브라이트 부인이 집 근처 언덕에서 멈춰 있던 시각과 우연히 겹쳤다."], ["When he had looked round the premises in the manner she had noticed he went and knocked at the door.", "그가 부인이 본 그런 식으로 집 안팎을 둘러본 뒤 그는 가서 문을 두드렸다."], ["There was a few minutes’ interval, and then the key turned in the lock, the door opened, and Eustacia herself confronted him.", "몇 분의 간격이 있은 다음 자물쇠에서 열쇠가 돌아가고 문이 열렸으며, 유스테이시아가 직접 그를 마주했다."]], [["Nobody could have imagined from her bearing now that here stood the woman who had joined with him in the impassioned dance of the week before, unless indeed he could have penetrated below the surface and gauged the real depth of that still stream.", "지금 그녀의 태도만 보아서는, 만약 누군가 그 표면 아래를 꿰뚫어보고 그 고요한 물줄기의 진짜 깊이를 가늠할 수 있는 사람이 아니고서야, 여기 서 있는 이가 지난주에 그와 더불어 그 격정적인 춤을 추었던 바로 그 여인이라고는 도저히 상상할 수 없었으리라."]], [["“I hope you reached home safely?”", "\"무사히 집에 도착하셨기를 바랍니다.\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일드브가 말했다."]], [["“O yes,”", "\"아, 그럼요.\""], ["she carelessly returned.", "그녀가 무심하게 대답했다."]], [["“And were you not tired the next day?", "\"그리고 이튿날 피곤하지 않으셨나요?"], ["I feared you might be.”", "그러셨을까 봐 걱정했습니다.\""]], [["“I was rather.", "\"좀 피곤하긴 했어요."], ["You need not speak low—nobody will over-hear us.", "목소리를 낮추실 필요 없어요 — 우리 말을 엿들을 사람이 아무도 없으니까요."], ["My small servant is gone on an errand to the village.”", "제 어린 하녀는 마을에 심부름 갔거든요.\""]], [["“Then Clym is not at home?”", "\"그럼 클림은 집에 없나요?\""]], [["“Yes, he is.”", "\"아니요, 있어요.\""]], [["“O!", "\"아!"], ["I thought that perhaps you had locked the door because you were alone and were afraid of tramps.”", "혹시 혼자 계셔서 떠돌이들이 무서워 문을 잠그신 줄 알았습니다.\""]], [["“No—here is my husband.”", "\"아니에요 — 여기 제 남편이 있어요.\""]], [["They had been standing in the entry.", "그들은 현관에 서 있던 참이었다."], ["Closing the front door and turning the key, as before, she threw open the door of the adjoining room and asked him to walk in.", "그녀는 현관문을 닫고 아까처럼 열쇠를 돌린 뒤, 이웃한 방의 문을 열어젖히고는 그에게 들어오라고 청했다."], ["Wildeve entered, the room appearing to be empty; but as soon as he had advanced a few steps he started.", "와일드브가 들어서니 방은 비어 있는 듯했다. 그러나 몇 걸음 나아가자마자 그는 흠칫했다."], ["On the hearthrug lay Clym asleep.", "난로 앞 깔개 위에 클림이 잠들어 있었던 것이다."], ["Beside him were the leggings, thick boots, leather gloves, and sleeve-waistcoat in which he worked.", "그 곁에는 그가 일할 때 차는 각반과 두꺼운 장화, 가죽 장갑, 소매 달린 조끼가 놓여 있었다."]], [["“You may go in; you will not disturb him,”", "\"들어오셔도 돼요. 그이를 깨우진 않을 거예요.\""], ["she said, following behind.", "그녀가 뒤따라오며 말했다."], ["“My reason for fastening the door is that he may not be intruded upon by any chance comer while lying here, if I should be in the garden or upstairs.”", "\"제가 문을 잠근 까닭은, 제가 정원에 있거나 위층에 있을 때 그이가 여기 누워 있는 동안 어쩌다 들른 사람에게 방해받지 않게 하려는 거예요.\""]], [["“Why is he sleeping there?”", "\"왜 저기서 자고 있죠?\""], ["said Wildeve in low tones.", "와일드브가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“He is very weary.", "\"몹시 지쳤거든요."], ["He went out at half-past four this morning, and has been working ever since.", "오늘 아침 네 시 반에 나가서 그때부터 줄곧 일했어요."], ["He cuts furze because it is the only thing he can do that does not put any strain upon his poor eyes.”", "가시금작화를 베는 건 그 가엾은 눈에 아무 부담도 주지 않으면서 할 수 있는 유일한 일이라서요.\""], ["The contrast between the sleeper’s appearance and Wildeve’s at this moment was painfully apparent to Eustacia, Wildeve being elegantly dressed in a new summer suit and light hat; and she continued: “Ah!", "잠든 이의 모습과 이 순간 와일드브의 모습 사이의 대조가 유스테이시아에게는 아프도록 또렷했으니, 와일드브는 새 여름 양복에 가벼운 모자를 쓰고 우아하게 차려입고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["you don’t know how differently he appeared when I first met him, though it is such a little while ago.", "\"아! 처음 만났을 때 그이가 얼마나 다른 모습이었는지 당신은 모를 거예요, 그리 오래전 일도 아닌데 말이죠."], ["His hands were as white and soft as mine; and look at them now, how rough and brown they are!", "그이 손은 제 손처럼 희고 부드러웠어요. 그런데 지금 저것 좀 보세요, 얼마나 거칠고 갈색이 되었는지!"], ["His complexion is by nature fair, and that rusty look he has now, all of a colour with his leather clothes, is caused by the burning of the sun.”", "그이 안색은 본래 희멀건데, 지금 저 가죽옷과 한가지 색으로 녹슨 듯한 저 빛깔은 햇볕에 타서 생긴 거예요.\""]], [["“Why does he go out at all!”", "\"대체 왜 나가는 겁니까!\""], ["Wildeve whispered.", "와일드브가 속삭였다."]], [["“Because he hates to be idle; though what he earns doesn’t add much to our exchequer.", "\"빈둥거리는 걸 질색하니까요. 벌어들이는 게 우리 살림에 보태지는 것도 별로 없지만요."], ["However, he says that when people are living upon their capital they must keep down current expenses by turning a penny where they can.”", "그래도 그이 말로는, 가진 밑천을 까먹고 사는 사람들은 벌 수 있는 데서 한 푼이라도 벌어 당장의 지출을 줄여야 한다는 거예요.\""]], [["“The fates have not been kind to you, Eustacia Yeobright.”", "\"운명의 여신들이 당신에게 친절하지 못했군요, 유스테이시아 요브라이트.\""]], [["“I have nothing to thank them for.”", "\"그들에게 고마워할 게 아무것도 없어요.\""]], [["“Nor has he—except for their one great gift to him.”", "\"그이도 마찬가지죠 — 그들이 그이에게 준 단 하나의 큰 선물만 빼면요.\""]], [["“What’s that?”", "\"그게 뭔데요?\""]], [["Wildeve looked her in the eyes.", "와일드브가 그녀의 눈을 들여다보았다."]], [["Eustacia blushed for the first time that day.", "유스테이시아가 그날 처음으로 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["“Well, I am a questionable gift,”", "\"글쎄요, 저야 미심쩍은 선물이죠.\""], ["she said quietly.", "그녀가 조용히 말했다."], ["“I thought you meant the gift of content—which he has, and I have not.”", "\"저는 만족이라는 선물을 말씀하시는 줄 알았어요 — 그이는 가졌고 저는 갖지 못한 그것 말이에요.\""]], [["“I can understand content in such a case—though how the outward situation can attract him puzzles me.”", "\"그런 경우라면 만족도 이해할 수 있죠 — 다만 어떻게 저 겉으로 드러난 처지가 그이를 끌어당기는지는 저로선 알 수 없는 노릇이지만요.\""]], [["“That’s because you don’t know him.", "\"그건 당신이 그이를 몰라서 그래요."], ["He’s an enthusiast about ideas, and careless about outward things.", "그이는 관념에 열광하고 바깥 것에는 무심하거든요."], ["He often reminds me of the Apostle Paul.”", "종종 저는 그이를 보면 사도 바울이 떠올라요.\""]], [["“I am glad to hear that he’s so grand in character as that.”", "\"그이가 그토록 위대한 인품을 지녔다니 반가운 말이로군요.\""]], [["“Yes; but the worst of it is that though Paul was excellent as a man in the Bible he would hardly have done in real life.”", "\"그래요. 하지만 곤란한 건, 바울이 성경 속 인물로는 더할 나위 없었을지언정 실제 삶에서는 어림도 없었으리라는 거예요.\""]], [["Their voices had instinctively dropped lower, though at first they had taken no particular care to avoid awakening Clym.", "그들의 목소리는 본능적으로 한층 낮아졌는데, 처음에는 클림을 깨우지 않으려고 딱히 조심하지도 않았던 터였다."], ["“Well, if that means that your marriage is a misfortune to you, you know who is to blame,”", "\"그게, 당신의 결혼이 당신에게 불행이라는 뜻이라면, 누구 탓인지는 당신도 알겠지요.\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일드브가 말했다."]], [["“The marriage is no misfortune in itself,”", "\"결혼 자체는 불행이 아니에요.\""], ["she retorted with some little petulance.", "그녀가 약간 발끈하며 받아쳤다."], ["“It is simply the accident which has happened since that has been the cause of my ruin.", "\"그저 그 뒤에 일어난 우연이 제 파멸의 원인이 된 거죠."], ["I have certainly got thistles for figs in a worldly sense, but how could I tell what time would bring forth?”", "세속적인 의미에서 무화과 대신 엉겅퀴를 얻은 건 분명하지만, 세월이 무엇을 가져올지 제가 어떻게 알 수 있었겠어요?\""]], [["“Sometimes, Eustacia, I think it is a judgment upon you.", "\"가끔은, 유스테이시아, 그게 당신에게 내린 심판이라는 생각이 들어요."], ["You rightly belonged to me, you know; and I had no idea of losing you.”", "당신은 응당 내 사람이었잖아요, 알다시피. 그리고 나는 당신을 잃을 생각이 전혀 없었고요.\""]], [["“No, it was not my fault!", "\"아니에요, 그건 제 잘못이 아니었어요!"], ["Two could not belong to you; and remember that, before I was aware, you turned aside to another woman.", "두 사람이 당신 사람일 수는 없었고, 게다가 기억하세요, 제가 미처 알기도 전에 당신이 먼저 다른 여자에게로 돌아섰다는 걸요."], ["It was cruel levity in you to do that.", "그렇게 한 건 당신의 잔인한 변덕이었어요."], ["I never dreamt of playing such a game on my side till you began it on yours.”", "당신이 당신 쪽에서 그걸 시작하기 전에는 저도 제 쪽에서 그런 장난을 칠 생각은 꿈에도 없었어요.\""]], [["“I meant nothing by it,”", "\"별 뜻 없이 한 거였어요.\""], ["replied Wildeve.", "와일드브가 대답했다."], ["“It was a mere interlude.", "\"그저 막간극이었을 뿐이죠."], ["Men are given to the trick of having a passing fancy for somebody else in the midst of a permanent love, which reasserts itself afterwards just as before.", "남자들이란 변치 않는 사랑 한가운데서 누군가에게 잠깐 마음이 끌리는 버릇이 있는데, 그 사랑은 나중에 다시 예전 그대로 고개를 드는 거예요."], ["On account of your rebellious manner to me I was tempted to go further than I should have done; and when you still would keep playing the same tantalizing part I went further still, and married her.”", "당신이 내게 반항하는 태도를 보이니 나는 그러지 말았어야 할 정도로 더 나아가고 싶은 유혹을 받았고, 당신이 여전히 그 애타게 하는 역할을 계속하니 나는 더더욱 나아가서 그녀와 결혼한 거예요.\""], ["Turning and looking again at the unconscious form of Clym, he murmured, “I am afraid that you don’t value your prize, Clym....", "그는 몸을 돌려 의식 없는 클림의 형체를 다시 바라보며 중얼거렸다. \"당신은 자기가 얻은 보물의 가치를 모르는 것 같소, 클림.…"], ["He ought to be happier than I in one thing at least.", "적어도 한 가지 점에서는 그가 나보다 행복할 거요."], ["He may know what it is to come down in the world, and to be afflicted with a great personal calamity; but he probably doesn’t know what it is to lose the woman he loved.”", "그는 세상에서 영락하는 것이 어떤 것인지, 큰 개인적 재앙에 시달리는 것이 어떤 것인지는 알지 모르나, 사랑하던 여자를 잃는 것이 어떤 것인지는 아마 모를 테니.\""]], [["“He is not ungrateful for winning her,”", "\"그이는 그녀를 얻은 것에 고마움을 모르는 사람이 아니에요.\""], ["whispered Eustacia, “and in that respect he is a good man.", "유스테이시아가 속삭였다. \"그 점에서 그이는 좋은 사람이죠."], ["Many women would go far for such a husband.", "많은 여자들이 그런 남편을 위해서라면 멀리까지 갈 거예요."], ["But do I desire unreasonably much in wanting what is called life—music, poetry, passion, war, and all the beating and pulsing that are going on in the great arteries of the world?", "하지만 제가 이른바 생⏤음악과 시와 열정과 전쟁, 그리고 이 세상의 큰 동맥 속을 흐르며 고동치고 약동하는 그 모든 것⏤을 바란다고 해서 분에 넘치게 너무 많은 걸 바라는 걸까요?"], ["That was the shape of my youthful dream; but I did not get it.", "그게 제 젊은 날 꿈의 모습이었는데, 저는 그걸 얻지 못했어요."], ["Yet I thought I saw the way to it in my Clym.”", "그래도 저는 제 클림에게서 거기로 가는 길을 본 것 같았어요.\""]], [["“And you only married him on that account?”", "\"그래서 그것 때문에만 그이와 결혼한 거요?\""]], [["“There you mistake me.", "\"거기서 당신은 저를 오해하고 있어요."], ["I married him because I loved him, but I won’t say that I didn’t love him partly because I thought I saw a promise of that life in him.”", "저는 그이를 사랑해서 결혼했어요. 다만 그이에게서 그런 삶의 약속을 본 것 같았기 때문에 부분적으로 그이를 사랑한 게 아니라고는 말하지 않겠어요.\""]], [["“You have dropped into your old mournful key.”", "\"당신, 옛날의 그 구슬픈 가락으로 빠져들었군요.\""]], [["“But I am not going to be depressed,”", "\"하지만 저는 가라앉지 않을 거예요.\""], ["she cried perversely.", "그녀가 짐짓 외쳤다."], ["“I began a new system by going to that dance, and I mean to stick to it.", "\"저는 그 춤판에 가는 걸로 새로운 방식을 시작했고, 그걸 고수할 작정이에요."], ["Clym can sing merrily; why should not I?”", "클림도 즐겁게 노래할 수 있는데, 저라고 왜 못 하겠어요?\""]], [["Wildeve looked thoughtfully at her.", "와일드브가 생각에 잠긴 듯 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["“It is easier to say you will sing than to do it; though if I could I would encourage you in your attempt.", "\"노래하겠다고 말하기는 노래하기보다 쉽죠. 그래도 할 수만 있다면 저는 당신의 시도를 북돋아 드리고 싶어요."], ["But as life means nothing to me, without one thing which is now impossible, you will forgive me for not being able to encourage you.”", "하지만 이제는 불가능해진 단 한 가지가 없으면 제게 삶이란 아무 의미도 없으니, 당신을 북돋아 드릴 수 없는 저를 용서해주세요.\""]], [["“Damon, what is the matter with you, that you speak like that?”", "\"데이먼, 무슨 일이 있길래 그렇게 말씀하시는 거예요?\""], ["she asked, raising her deep shady eyes to his.", "그녀가 그늘진 깊은 눈을 들어 그를 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“That’s a thing I shall never tell plainly; and perhaps if I try to tell you in riddles you will not care to guess them.”", "\"그건 제가 결코 대놓고 말하지 않을 거예요. 어쩌면 수수께끼로 말해보려 해도 당신은 그걸 풀어보려 하지 않으실 테죠.\""]], [["Eustacia remained silent for a minute, and she said, “We are in a strange relationship today.", "유스테이시아는 잠시 잠자코 있다가 말했다. \"오늘 우리는 묘한 관계에 있네요."], ["You mince matters to an uncommon nicety.", "당신은 유난스레 까다롭게 말을 에두르고 계세요."], ["You mean, Damon, that you still love me.", "데이먼, 당신은 여전히 저를 사랑한다는 뜻이군요."], ["Well, that gives me sorrow, for I am not made so entirely happy by my marriage that I am willing to spurn you for the information, as I ought to do.", "글쎄요, 그건 제게 슬픔을 안겨주는데, 저는 그 말을 빌미로 응당 그래야 하듯 당신을 매몰차게 내칠 만큼 결혼으로 그렇게 온전히 행복하지가 못하니까요."], ["But we have said too much about this.", "하지만 우리는 이 얘기를 너무 많이 했어요."], ["Do you mean to wait until my husband is awake?”", "제 남편이 깰 때까지 기다리실 작정이세요?\""]], [["“I thought to speak to him; but it is unnecessary, Eustacia, if I offend you by not forgetting you, you are right to mention it; but do not talk of spurning.”", "\"그이와 이야기할 생각이었는데, 그럴 필요는 없겠어요, 유스테이시아. 당신을 잊지 못하는 것으로 제가 당신을 언짢게 했다면, 그걸 입에 올리는 건 당신이 옳아요. 하지만 매몰차게 내친다는 말은 하지 마세요.\""]], [["She did not reply, and they stood looking musingly at Clym as he slept on in that profound sleep which is the result of physical labour carried on in circumstances that wake no nervous fear.", "그녀는 대답하지 않았고, 두 사람은 클림이 그 깊은 잠 속에서 계속 자고 있는 것을 생각에 잠긴 듯 바라보며 서 있었으니, 그것은 어떤 신경질적인 두려움도 일으키지 않는 환경에서 행해진 육체노동의 결과인 그런 잠이었다."]], [["“God, how I envy him that sweet sleep!”", "\"하느님, 저 달콤한 잠이 얼마나 부러운지!\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일드브가 말했다."], ["“I have not slept like that since I was a boy—years and years ago.”", "\"나는 소년이던 시절 이후로 저렇게 자본 적이 없어요⏤아주 오래전, 까마득한 옛날 이후로요.\""]], [["While they thus watched him a click at the gate was audible, and a knock came to the door.", "그들이 이렇게 그를 지켜보고 있을 때 대문에서 찰칵하는 소리가 들리더니 문에 두드림이 왔다."], ["Eustacia went to a window and looked out.", "유스테이시아가 창가로 가서 밖을 내다보았다."]], [["Her countenance changed.", "그녀의 안색이 변했다."], ["First she became crimson, and then the red subsided till it even partially left her lips.", "처음에는 진홍빛이 되었다가, 이윽고 그 붉은 기가 가라앉으며 입술에서마저 부분적으로 빠져나갔다."]], [["“Shall I go away?”", "\"제가 가는 게 좋겠어요?\""], ["said Wildeve, standing up.", "와일드브가 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“I hardly know.”", "\"잘 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“Who is it?”", "\"누군데요?\""]], [["“Mrs. Yeobright.", "\"요브라이트 부인이에요."], ["O, what she said to me that day!", "아, 그날 그분이 제게 한 말이라니!"], ["I cannot understand this visit—what does she mean?", "이 방문을 도무지 이해할 수 없네요⏤대체 무슨 속셈일까요?"], ["And she suspects that past time of ours.”", "게다가 그분은 우리의 그 지난 시절을 의심하고 있어요.\""]], [["“I am in your hands.", "\"저는 당신 손에 달렸어요."], ["If you think she had better not see me here I’ll go into the next room.”", "그분이 저를 여기서 보지 않는 게 낫겠다 싶으면 옆방으로 들어가지요.\""]], [["“Well, yes—go.”", "\"그래요, 네⏤들어가세요.\""]], [["Wildeve at once withdrew; but before he had been half a minute in the adjoining apartment Eustacia came after him.", "와일드브는 즉시 물러갔다. 그러나 그가 옆방에 들어간 지 반 분도 채 되지 않아 유스테이시아가 그를 뒤따라왔다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["she said, “we won’t have any of this.", "그녀가 말했다. \"이런 짓은 하지 않겠어요."], ["If she comes in she must see you—and think if she likes there’s something wrong!", "그분이 들어오면 당신을 봐야 할 거고⏤그러고 싶다면 뭔가 잘못됐다고 생각하라죠!"], ["But how can I open the door to her, when she dislikes me—wishes to see not me, but her son?", "하지만 그분이 저를 싫어하는데⏤보고 싶은 건 제가 아니라 아들인데, 제가 어떻게 그분에게 문을 열어드리겠어요?"], ["I won’t open the door!”", "문을 열지 않겠어요!\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright knocked again more loudly.", "요브라이트 부인이 더 크게 다시 두드렸다."]], [["“Her knocking will, in all likelihood, awaken him,”", "\"저 두드림이 십중팔구 그이를 깨울 거예요.\""], ["continued Eustacia, “and then he will let her in himself.", "유스테이시아가 말을 이었다. \"그러면 그이가 직접 어머니를 들이겠죠."], ["Ah—listen.”", "아⏤들어보세요.\""]], [["They could hear Clym moving in the other room, as if disturbed by the knocking, and he uttered the word “Mother.”", "그들은 클림이 두드림에 잠을 설친 듯 다른 방에서 뒤척이는 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 그가 \"어머니\"라는 말을 내뱉었다."]], [["“Yes—he is awake—he will go to the door,”", "\"네⏤그이가 깼어요⏤문으로 갈 거예요.\""], ["she said, with a breath of relief.", "그녀가 안도의 숨을 내쉬며 말했다."], ["“Come this way.", "\"이쪽으로 오세요."], ["I have a bad name with her, and you must not be seen.", "그분에게 저는 평판이 나쁘니, 당신이 들켜서는 안 돼요."], ["Thus I am obliged to act by stealth, not because I do ill, but because others are pleased to say so.”", "이렇게 저는 몰래 행동해야만 하는데, 제가 못된 짓을 해서가 아니라 남들이 그렇다고 말하기를 즐기기 때문이에요.\""]], [["By this time she had taken him to the back door, which was open, disclosing a path leading down the garden.", "이때쯤 그녀는 그를 뒷문으로 데려갔는데, 그 문은 열려 있어 정원으로 내려가는 오솔길이 드러나 있었다."], ["“Now, one word, Damon,”", "\"자, 한마디만 해요, 데이먼.\""], ["she remarked as he stepped forth.", "그가 밖으로 나서자 그녀가 말했다."], ["“This is your first visit here; let it be your last.", "\"이게 당신의 이곳 첫 방문이니, 마지막 방문이 되게 하세요."], ["We have been hot lovers in our time, but it won’t do now.", "우리도 한때는 뜨거운 연인이었지만, 이제는 그래선 안 돼요."], ["Good-bye.”", "잘 가요.\""]], [["“Good-bye,”", "\"잘 있어요.\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일드브가 말했다."], ["“I have had all I came for, and I am satisfied.”", "\"여기 온 목적은 다 이뤘으니, 저는 만족합니다.\""]], [["“What was it?”", "\"그게 뭐였는데요?\""]], [["“A sight of you.", "\"당신을 한 번 보는 것."], ["Upon my eternal honour I came for no more.”", "제 영원한 명예를 걸고, 그 이상을 바라고 온 게 아니에요.\""]], [["Wildeve kissed his hand to the beautiful girl he addressed, and passed into the garden, where she watched him down the path, over the stile at the end, and into the ferns outside, which brushed his hips as he went along till he became lost in their thickets.", "와일드브는 자기가 말을 건넨 그 아름다운 여인에게 손에 입맞춤을 보내고는 정원으로 들어섰고, 그녀는 그가 오솔길을 내려가 그 끝의 디딤단을 넘어 바깥의 양치류 속으로 들어가는 것을 지켜보았는데, 그 양치류는 그가 걸어가는 동안 그의 허리께를 스치다가 마침내 그가 그 덤불 속으로 사라져 보이지 않게 되었다."], ["When he had quite gone she slowly turned, and directed her attention to the interior of the house.", "그가 완전히 가버리자 그녀는 천천히 몸을 돌려 집 안쪽으로 주의를 돌렸다."]], [["But it was possible that her presence might not be desired by Clym and his mother at this moment of their first meeting, or that it would be superfluous.", "그러나 클림과 그 어머니가 처음 만나는 이 순간에 자신이 거기 있는 것을 두 사람이 바라지 않을 수도, 아니면 그것이 군더더기일 수도 있었다."], ["At all events, she was in no hurry to meet Mrs. Yeobright.", "어쨌든 그녀는 요브라이트 부인을 만나는 데 서두를 마음이 전혀 없었다."], ["She resolved to wait till Clym came to look for her, and glided back into the garden.", "그녀는 클림이 자기를 찾으러 올 때까지 기다리기로 작정하고 정원으로 슬그머니 돌아갔다."], ["Here she idly occupied herself for a few minutes, till finding no notice was taken of her she retraced her steps through the house to the front, where she listened for voices in the parlour.", "여기서 그녀는 몇 분간 하릴없이 시간을 보냈는데, 자신에게 아무 기별이 없는 것을 알고는 집을 가로질러 앞쪽으로 발걸음을 되짚어가서 거실의 목소리에 귀를 기울였다."], ["But hearing none she opened the door and went in.", "그러나 아무 소리도 들리지 않자 그녀는 문을 열고 들어갔다."], ["To her astonishment Clym lay precisely as Wildeve and herself had left him, his sleep apparently unbroken.", "놀랍게도 클림은 와일드브와 그녀가 두고 떠났던 바로 그 모습 그대로 누워 있었으니, 그 잠은 깨지지 않은 듯했다."], ["He had been disturbed and made to dream and murmur by the knocking, but he had not awakened.", "그는 두드림에 잠을 설치고 꿈을 꾸며 중얼거리긴 했으나 잠에서 깨지는 않았던 것이다."], ["Eustacia hastened to the door, and in spite of her reluctance to open it to a woman who had spoken of her so bitterly, she unfastened it and looked out.", "유스테이시아는 서둘러 문으로 갔고, 자신을 그토록 모질게 말한 여인에게 문을 열어주기가 꺼려졌음에도 빗장을 풀고 밖을 내다보았다."], ["Nobody was to be seen.", "아무도 보이지 않았다."], ["There, by the scraper, lay Clym’s hook and the handful of faggot-bonds he had brought home; in front of her were the empty path, the garden gate standing slightly ajar; and, beyond, the great valley of purple heath thrilling silently in the sun.", "거기 흙털개 옆에는 클림의 낫과 그가 집으로 가져온 한 줌의 단 묶음 끈이 놓여 있었고, 그녀 앞에는 텅 빈 오솔길과 살짝 열린 채 서 있는 정원 대문이 있었으며, 그 너머로는 보랏빛 히스의 큰 골짜기가 햇볕 속에서 소리 없이 전율하고 있었다."], ["Mrs. Yeobright was gone.", "요브라이트 부인은 가버린 것이었다."]], [["Clym’s mother was at this time following a path which lay hidden from Eustacia by a shoulder of the hill.", "클림의 어머니는 이때 언덕의 한 자락에 가려 유스테이시아에게는 보이지 않는 오솔길을 따라가고 있었다."], ["Her walk thither from the garden gate had been hasty and determined, as of a woman who was now no less anxious to escape from the scene than she had previously been to enter it.", "정원 대문에서부터 거기까지의 그녀의 걸음은 황급하고 단호했으니, 앞서 그 장면 안으로 들어가려 했던 것 못지않게 이제는 거기서 벗어나려고 애타는 여인의 걸음이었다."], ["Her eyes were fixed on the ground; within her two sights were graven—that of Clym’s hook and brambles at the door, and that of a woman’s face at a window.", "그녀의 눈은 땅에 박혀 있었고, 그녀 안에는 두 가지 광경이 새겨져 있었으니 — 문 옆에 놓인 클림의 낫과 가시덩굴, 그리고 창가에 비친 한 여인의 얼굴이었다."], ["Her lips trembled, becoming unnaturally thin as she murmured, “’Tis too much—Clym, how can he bear to do it!", "그녀의 입술은 떨렸고, 부자연스레 얇아지면서 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"너무하구나⏤클림, 그 애가 어떻게 저럴 수가!"], ["He is at home; and yet he lets her shut the door against me!”", "집에 있으면서도 그 애가 그 여자더러 내게 문을 닫게 내버려두다니!\""]], [["In her anxiety to get out of the direct view of the house she had diverged from the straightest path homeward, and while looking about to regain it she came upon a little boy gathering whortleberries in a hollow.", "집을 정면으로 보는 시야에서 벗어나려는 조바심에 그녀는 집으로 가는 가장 곧은 길에서 벗어났고, 그 길을 다시 찾으려고 둘러보다가 움푹한 곳에서 월귤을 따고 있는 어린 사내아이와 마주쳤다."], ["The boy was Johnny Nunsuch, who had been Eustacia’s stoker at the bonfire, and, with the tendency of a minute body to gravitate towards a greater, he began hovering round Mrs. Yeobright as soon as she appeared, and trotted on beside her without perceptible consciousness of his act.", "그 아이는 모닥불 때 유스테이시아의 불 때는 일꾼 노릇을 했던 조니 넌서치였는데, 작은 몸뚱이가 더 큰 것에 끌려가는 경향대로, 그는 요브라이트 부인이 나타나자마자 그녀 둘레를 맴돌기 시작하더니 제 행동을 뚜렷이 의식하지도 못한 채 그녀 곁에서 종종걸음을 쳤다."]], [["Mrs. Yeobright spoke to him as one in a mesmeric sleep.", "요브라이트 부인은 최면에 걸린 잠 속에 있는 사람처럼 그에게 말했다."], ["“’Tis a long way home, my child, and we shall not get there till evening.”", "\"집까지는 먼 길이란다, 얘야, 저녁이 되어야 거기 닿겠구나.\""]], [["“I shall,”", "\"저는 닿을 거예요.\""], ["said her small companion.", "그녀의 작은 동행이 말했다."], ["“I am going to play marnels afore supper, and we go to supper at six o’clock, because Father comes home.", "\"저는 저녁 먹기 전에 구슬치기를 할 거고, 우리는 여섯 시에 저녁을 먹어요, 아버지가 집에 오시니까요."], ["Does your father come home at six too?”", "아주머니 아버지도 여섯 시에 오세요?\""]], [["“No, he never comes; nor my son either, nor anybody.”", "\"아니, 그이는 절대 안 오신단다. 내 아들도 안 오고, 아무도 안 와.\""]], [["“What have made you so down?", "\"왜 그렇게 시무룩하세요?"], ["Have you seen a ooser?”", "우서를 보셨어요?\""]], [["“I have seen what’s worse—a woman’s face looking at me through a windowpane.”", "\"그보다 더한 걸 봤단다⏤창유리 너머로 나를 바라보는 한 여자의 얼굴을.\""]], [["“Is that a bad sight?”", "\"그게 나쁜 광경이에요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["It is always a bad sight to see a woman looking out at a weary wayfarer and not letting her in.”", "지친 나그네를 바라보면서도 들여보내 주지 않는 여자를 보는 건 언제나 나쁜 광경이지.\""]], [["“Once when I went to Throope Great Pond to catch effets I seed myself looking up at myself, and I was frightened and jumped back like anything.”", "\"한번은 영원을 잡으러 스루프 큰 못에 갔을 때, 저 자신이 저를 올려다보는 걸 봤는데, 깜짝 놀라서 막 뒤로 펄쩍 뛰었어요.\""]], [["...“If they had only shown signs of meeting my advances halfway how well it might have been done!", "…\"그들이 내 손길에 중간에서라도 응할 기미만 보였더라면 얼마나 잘됐을까!"], ["But there is no chance.", "하지만 가망이 없구나."], ["Shut out!", "문전박대라니!"], ["She must have set him against me.", "그 여자가 그 애를 나에게 등 돌리게 한 게 분명해."], ["Can there be beautiful bodies without hearts inside? I think so.", "속에 마음 없는 아름다운 몸뚱이가 있을 수 있을까?"], ["I would not have done it against a neighbour’s cat on such a fiery day as this!”", "그럴 수 있을 것 같구나. 이렇게 불볕 같은 날에는 이웃집 고양이한테도 그런 짓은 안 했을 텐데!\""]], [["“What is it you say?”", "\"무슨 말씀이세요?\""]], [["“Never again—never!", "\"두 번 다시⏤절대로!"], ["Not even if they send for me!”", "그들이 나를 부르러 보낸다 해도 안 가!\""]], [["“You must be a very curious woman to talk like that.”", "\"그렇게 말씀하시는 걸 보니 아주 별난 아주머니인가 봐요.\""]], [["“O no, not at all,”", "\"아, 아니야, 전혀 그렇지 않단다.\""], ["she said, returning to the boy’s prattle.", "그녀가 아이의 재잘거림으로 돌아오며 말했다."], ["“Most people who grow up and have children talk as I do.", "\"자라서 자식을 둔 사람들은 대개 나처럼 말한단다."], ["When you grow up your mother will talk as I do too.”", "너도 자라면 네 어머니가 나처럼 말할 거야.\""]], [["“I hope she won’t; because ’tis very bad to talk nonsense.”", "\"안 그러셨으면 좋겠어요, 헛소리하는 건 아주 나쁜 거니까요.\""]], [["“Yes, child; it is nonsense, I suppose.", "\"그래, 얘야, 헛소리지, 아마."], ["Are you not nearly spent with the heat?”", "너도 더위에 거의 다 지치지 않았니?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["But not so much as you be.”", "하지만 아주머니만큼은 아니에요.\""]], [["“How do you know?”", "\"그걸 어떻게 아니?\""]], [["“Your face is white and wet, and your head is hanging-down-like.”", "\"아주머니 얼굴이 하얗고 축축하고, 머리가 축 늘어진 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Ah, I am exhausted from inside.”", "\"아, 나는 속에서부터 기진했단다.\""]], [["“Why do you, every time you take a step, go like this?”", "\"왜 한 걸음 뗄 때마다 이렇게 하세요?\""], ["The child in speaking gave to his motion the jerk and limp of an invalid.", "아이가 말하면서 제 몸짓에 병자의 절뚝거림과 휘청거림을 흉내 냈다."]], [["“Because I have a burden which is more than I can bear.”", "\"내가 감당할 수 없을 만큼 무거운 짐을 졌기 때문이란다.\""]], [["The little boy remained silently pondering, and they tottered on side by side until more than a quarter of an hour had elapsed, when Mrs.", "어린 사내아이는 잠자코 골똘히 생각에 잠겼고, 두 사람은 십오 분 남짓이 지나도록 나란히 비틀거리며 나아갔는데, 그때 기운이 눈에 띄게 더 빠진 요브라이트 부인이 그에게 말했다."], ["Yeobright, whose weakness plainly increased, said to him, “I must sit down here to rest.”", "\"여기 좀 앉아서 쉬어야겠다.\""]], [["When she had seated herself he looked long in her face and said, “How funny you draw your breath—like a lamb when you drive him till he’s nearly done for.", "그녀가 자리에 앉자 아이는 그녀의 얼굴을 오래 들여다보고는 말했다. \"숨을 참 우습게 쉬세요⏤다 죽게 될 때까지 몰아대는 새끼 양처럼요."], ["Do you always draw your breath like that?”", "늘 그렇게 숨을 쉬세요?\""]], [["“Not always.”", "\"늘 그렇진 않단다.\""], ["Her voice was now so low as to be scarcely above a whisper.", "그녀의 목소리는 이제 너무 낮아 속삭임보다 겨우 조금 높을 뿐이었다."]], [["“You will go to sleep there, I suppose, won’t you?", "\"거기서 주무실 거죠, 그렇죠?"], ["You have shut your eyes already.”", "벌써 눈을 감으셨잖아요.\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니."], ["I shall not sleep much till—another day, and then I hope to have a long, long one—very long.", "나는 잠을 많이 못 잘 거야, 다른 어느 날까지는⏤그러면 그때는 길고 긴, 아주 긴 잠을 자게 되길 바란단다."], ["Now can you tell me if Rimsmoor Pond is dry this summer?”", "그런데 너 림스무어 못이 올여름에 말랐는지 말해줄 수 있겠니?\""]], [["“Rimsmoor Pond is, but Oker’s Pool isn’t, because he is deep, and is never dry—’tis just over there.”", "\"림스무어 못은 말랐는데, 오커스 풀은 안 말랐어요, 거긴 깊어서 절대 안 마르거든요⏤바로 저기예요.\""]], [["“Is the water clear?”", "\"물은 맑니?\""]], [["“Yes, middling—except where the heath-croppers walk into it.”", "\"네, 그럭저럭요⏤히스 뜯어먹는 망아지들이 들어가는 데만 빼면요.\""]], [["“Then, take this, and go as fast as you can, and dip me up the clearest you can find.", "\"그럼 이걸 받아서 되도록 빨리 가서, 네가 찾을 수 있는 가장 맑은 물을 떠다 주렴."], ["I am very faint.”", "나는 몹시 기운이 없구나.\""]], [["She drew from the small willow reticule that she carried in her hand an old-fashioned china teacup without a handle; it was one of half a dozen of the same sort lying in the reticule, which she had preserved ever since her childhood, and had brought with her today as a small present for Clym and Eustacia.", "그녀는 손에 들고 있던 작은 버들고리 손가방에서 손잡이 없는 구식 도자기 찻잔을 꺼냈다. 그것은 손가방 안에 들어 있는 같은 종류의 잔 대여섯 개 중 하나로, 그녀가 어린 시절부터 줄곧 간직해온 것이었으며, 오늘 클림과 유스테이시아에게 줄 자그마한 선물로 가져온 것이었다."]], [["The boy started on his errand, and soon came back with the water, such as it was.", "아이는 심부름을 떠났고, 곧 물을 떠서 돌아왔는데, 그저 그런 물이었다."], ["Mrs. Yeobright attempted to drink, but it was so warm as to give her nausea, and she threw it away.", "요브라이트 부인은 마셔보려 했으나 물이 너무 미지근해 구역질이 나서 그것을 쏟아버렸다."], ["Afterwards she still remained sitting, with her eyes closed.", "그 뒤로도 그녀는 눈을 감은 채 계속 앉아 있었다."]], [["The boy waited, played near her, caught several of the little brown butterflies which abounded, and then said as he waited again, “I like going on better than biding still.", "아이는 기다리며 그녀 곁에서 놀았고, 지천으로 널린 작은 갈색 나비를 몇 마리 잡고는 다시 기다리다가 말했다. \"저는 가만히 있는 것보다 계속 가는 게 더 좋아요."], ["Will you soon start again?”", "곧 다시 출발하실 거예요?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"모르겠다.\""]], [["“I wish I might go on by myself,”", "\"저 혼자 가도 됐으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["he resumed, fearing, apparently, that he was to be pressed into some unpleasant service.", "그가 무언가 달갑잖은 일에 끌려 들어갈까 봐 두려운 듯 말을 이었다."], ["“Do you want me any more, please?”", "\"제가 더 필요하세요, 아주머니?\""]], [["Mrs. Yeobright made no reply.", "요브라이트 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“What shall I tell Mother?”", "\"엄마한테 뭐라고 말할까요?\""], ["the boy continued.", "아이가 계속 말했다."]], [["“Tell her you have seen a broken-hearted woman cast off by her son.”", "\"아들에게 버림받은 가슴 찢어진 여자를 봤다고 말하렴.\""]], [["Before quite leaving her he threw upon her face a wistful glance, as if he had misgivings on the generosity of forsaking her thus.", "그녀를 아주 떠나기 전에 아이는 그녀의 얼굴에 그리운 듯한 눈길을 던졌으니, 마치 그녀를 이렇게 버려두는 것이 인심 좋은 일인지 마음에 걸리는 듯했다."], ["He gazed into her face in a vague, wondering manner, like that of one examining some strange old manuscript the key to whose characters is undiscoverable.", "그는 막연하고 의아한 태도로 그녀의 얼굴을 빤히 들여다보았는데, 마치 그 글자를 풀 열쇠를 도무지 찾을 수 없는 어떤 기이한 옛 사본을 살피는 사람의 태도 같았다."], ["He was not so young as to be absolutely without a sense that sympathy was demanded, he was not old enough to be free from the terror felt in childhood at beholding misery in adult quarters hitherto deemed impregnable; and whether she were in a position to cause trouble or to suffer from it, whether she and her affliction were something to pity or something to fear, it was beyond him to decide.", "그는 동정이 요구된다는 느낌이 전혀 없을 만큼 어리지는 않았고, 그때껏 난공불락으로 여겨온 어른들 영역에서 비참함을 목격할 때 어린 시절에 느끼는 그 공포에서 벗어날 만큼 나이가 들지도 않았다. 그리고 그녀가 곤경을 일으킬 처지인지 그것에 시달리는 처지인지, 그녀와 그녀의 고통이 가엾어해야 할 무엇인지 두려워해야 할 무엇인지를 가려내는 것은 그의 능력 밖이었다."], ["He lowered his eyes and went on without another word.", "그는 눈을 내리깔고 더는 한마디도 없이 가던 길을 갔다."], ["Before he had gone half a mile he had forgotten all about her, except that she was a woman who had sat down to rest.", "반 마일도 채 가기 전에 그는 그녀가 쉬려고 주저앉은 한 여자였다는 것 말고는 그녀에 관한 모든 것을 잊어버렸다."]], [["Mrs. Yeobright’s exertions, physical and emotional, had well-nigh prostrated her; but she continued to creep along in short stages with long breaks between.", "요브라이트 부인의 육체적, 정서적 노고는 그녀를 거의 쓰러뜨릴 지경이었으나, 그녀는 짧게짧게 나아가며 그 사이사이 길게 쉬어가는 식으로 계속 기어가듯 갔다."], ["The sun had now got far to the west of south and stood directly in her face, like some merciless incendiary, brand in hand, waiting to consume her.", "해는 이제 남쪽에서 서쪽으로 멀찍이 기울어 그녀의 얼굴을 정면으로 비추고 있었으니, 마치 손에 횃불을 든 어떤 무자비한 방화범이 그녀를 태워버리려고 기다리는 듯했다."], ["With the departure of the boy all visible animation disappeared from the landscape, though the intermittent husky notes of the male grasshoppers from every tuft of furze were enough to show that amid the prostration of the larger animal species an unseen insect world was busy in all the fullness of life.", "아이가 떠나자 풍경에서 눈에 보이는 모든 생기가 사라졌으나, 가시금작화 더미마다에서 띄엄띄엄 들려오는 수컷 메뚜기들의 쉰 듯한 울음소리만으로도, 더 큰 동물 종들이 기진해 쓰러진 가운데 눈에 보이지 않는 곤충의 세계가 생명의 충만함 속에서 분주하다는 것을 충분히 알 수 있었다."]], [["In two hours she reached a slope about three-fourths the whole distance from Alderworth to her own home, where a little patch of shepherd’s-thyme intruded upon the path; and she sat down upon the perfumed mat it formed there.", "두 시간 만에 그녀는 올더워스에서 자기 집까지 전체 거리의 사분의 삼쯤 되는 한 비탈에 다다랐는데, 거기에는 자그마한 백리향 무더기가 오솔길로 비집고 들어와 있었으니, 그녀는 그것이 이룬 향기로운 깔개 위에 앉았다."], ["In front of her a colony of ants had established a thoroughfare across the way, where they toiled a never-ending and heavy-laden throng.", "그녀 앞에는 개미 떼가 길을 가로질러 한길을 닦아놓고는 거기서 끝없이 무거운 짐을 진 무리를 이루어 고된 일을 하고 있었다."], ["To look down upon them was like observing a city street from the top of a tower.", "그것들을 내려다보는 것은 탑 꼭대기에서 도시의 거리를 굽어보는 것과 같았다."], ["She remembered that this bustle of ants had been in progress for years at the same spot—doubtless those of the old times were the ancestors of these which walked there now.", "그녀는 이 개미들의 부산함이 바로 이 자리에서 여러 해 동안 이어져왔음을 떠올렸으니⏤필시 옛 시절의 그것들이 지금 거기 걸어 다니는 이것들의 조상이리라."], ["She leant back to obtain more thorough rest, and the soft eastern portion of the sky was as great a relief to her eyes as the thyme was to her head.", "그녀는 더 온전히 쉬려고 몸을 뒤로 기댔는데, 하늘의 부드러운 동쪽 부분은 백리향이 그녀의 머리에 그러했던 것만큼이나 그녀의 눈에 큰 안식이었다."], ["While she looked a heron arose on that side of the sky and flew on with his face towards the sun.", "그녀가 바라보고 있자니 그쪽 하늘에서 왜가리 한 마리가 솟아올라 해를 향해 얼굴을 두고 날아갔다."], ["He had come dripping wet from some pool in the valleys, and as he flew the edges and lining of his wings, his thighs and his breast were so caught by the bright sunbeams that he appeared as if formed of burnished silver.", "그것은 골짜기 어느 못에서 흠뻑 젖은 채로 날아온 것이었는데, 날아가는 동안 그 날개의 가장자리와 안쪽, 그 넓적다리와 가슴이 밝은 햇살에 어찌나 사로잡혔는지, 마치 윤이 나는 은으로 빚어진 것처럼 보였다."], ["Up in the zenith where he was seemed a free and happy place, away from all contact with the earthly ball to which she was pinioned; and she wished that she could arise uncrushed from its surface and fly as he flew then.", "그가 떠 있는 저 높은 천정은, 그녀가 결박당해 있는 이 지상의 공에서 멀리 떨어진, 자유롭고 행복한 곳으로 보였다. 그녀는 자기도 짓눌리지 않고 그 표면에서 일어나 그가 지금 나는 것처럼 날아오를 수 있기를 바랐다."]], [["But, being a mother, it was inevitable that she should soon cease to ruminate upon her own condition.", "그러나 어머니인지라, 그녀가 곧 제 자신의 처지에 대한 생각을 멈추는 것은 불가피한 일이었다."], ["Had the track of her next thought been marked by a streak in the air, like the path of a meteor, it would have shown a direction contrary to the heron’s, and have descended to the eastward upon the roof of Clym’s house.", "만약 그녀의 다음 생각의 자취가 유성의 길처럼 공중에 한 줄기 선으로 그어졌더라면, 그것은 왜가리와는 반대되는 방향을 가리키며 동쪽으로 내려가 클림의 집 지붕 위에 내려앉았으리라."]], [["VII. The Tragic Meeting of Two Old Friends", "두 옛 벗의 비극적인 만남"]], [["He in the meantime had aroused himself from sleep, sat up, and looked around.", "그동안 그는 잠에서 깨어나 일어나 앉아 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Eustacia was sitting in a chair hard by him, and though she held a book in her hand she had not looked into it for some time.", "유스테이시아가 그의 바로 곁 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 손에 책을 들고 있었으나 한동안 그것을 들여다보지 않은 채였다."]], [["“Well, indeed!”", "\"이것 참!\""], ["said Clym, brushing his eyes with his hands.", "클림이 손으로 눈을 비비며 말했다."], ["“How soundly I have slept!", "\"내가 정말 깊이 잤군!"], ["I have had such a tremendous dream, too—one I shall never forget.”", "게다가 어찌나 엄청난 꿈을 꾸었는지⏤결코 못 잊을 그런 꿈을 말이야.\""]], [["“I thought you had been dreaming,”", "\"꿈을 꾸시는 것 같더라고요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["It was about my mother.", "어머니에 관한 꿈이었어."], ["I dreamt that I took you to her house to make up differences, and when we got there we couldn’t get in, though she kept on crying to us for help.", "꿈에서 내가 당신을 어머니 집에 데려가 불화를 풀려고 했는데, 막상 거기 닿으니 들어갈 수가 없는 거야, 어머니가 우리한테 도와달라고 계속 울부짖는데도 말이지."], ["However, dreams are dreams.", "하지만 꿈은 꿈일 뿐이지."], ["What o’clock is it, Eustacia?”", "몇 시야, 유스테이시아?\""]], [["“Half-past two.”", "\"두 시 반이에요.\""]], [["“So late, is it?", "\"그렇게 늦었어?"], ["I didn’t mean to stay so long.", "이렇게 오래 있을 생각은 아니었는데."], ["By the time I have had something to eat it will be after three.”", "뭐 좀 먹고 나면 세 시가 넘겠구나.\""]], [["“Ann is not come back from the village, and I thought I would let you sleep on till she returned.”", "\"앤이 마을에서 아직 안 돌아와서, 그 애가 올 때까지 당신을 자게 두는 게 좋겠다 싶었어요.\""]], [["Clym went to the window and looked out.", "클림은 창가로 가서 밖을 내다보았다."], ["Presently he said, musingly, “Week after week passes, and yet Mother does not come.", "이윽고 그가 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다. \"주가 가고 또 주가 가는데도 어머니는 오시지 않는구나."], ["I thought I should have heard something from her long before this.”", "진작에 어머니한테서 무슨 기별이 있을 줄 알았는데.\""]], [["Misgiving, regret, fear, resolution, ran their swift course of expression in Eustacia’s dark eyes.", "불안, 회한, 두려움, 결심이 유스테이시아의 검은 눈 속에서 잇따라 빠르게 표정으로 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["She was face to face with a monstrous difficulty, and she resolved to get free of it by postponement.", "그녀는 끔찍한 난관과 정면으로 마주하고 있었고, 미루기로 거기서 벗어나기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“I must certainly go to Blooms-End soon,”", "\"머잖아 블룸스엔드에 꼭 가봐야겠어.\""], ["he continued, “and I think I had better go alone.”", "그가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 혼자 가는 게 낫겠다 싶어.\""], ["He picked up his leggings and gloves, threw them down again, and added, “As dinner will be so late today I will not go back to the heath, but work in the garden till the evening, and then, when it will be cooler, I will walk to Blooms-End.", "그는 각반과 장갑을 집어 들었다가 도로 내던지고는 덧붙였다. \"오늘은 저녁이 이렇게 늦어질 테니 황야로 다시 나가진 않고 저녁까지 정원에서 일하다가, 좀 더 선선해지면 블룸스엔드까지 걸어가야겠어."], ["I am quite sure that if I make a little advance Mother will be willing to forget all.", "내가 조금만 먼저 다가가면 어머니는 기꺼이 모든 걸 잊어주실 거라고 확신해."], ["It will be rather late before I can get home, as I shall not be able to do the distance either way in less than an hour and a half.", "집에 돌아오기까지는 좀 늦어지겠지, 어느 쪽으로든 한 시간 반 안에는 그 거리를 갈 수 없을 테니까."], ["But you will not mind for one evening, dear?", "하지만 당신, 하룻저녁쯤은 괜찮지, 여보?"], ["What are you thinking of to make you look so abstracted?”", "그렇게 넋이 나간 표정을 짓는 걸 보니 무슨 생각을 하는 거야?\""]], [["“I cannot tell you,”", "\"말씀드릴 수 없어요.\""], ["she said heavily.", "그녀가 무겁게 말했다."], ["“I wish we didn’t live here, Clym.", "\"우리가 여기 살지 않았으면 좋겠어요, 클림."], ["The world seems all wrong in this place.”", "이곳에서는 세상이 온통 잘못된 것만 같아요.\""]], [["“Well—if we make it so.", "\"글쎄⏤우리가 그렇게 만든다면 그렇겠지."], ["I wonder if Thomasin has been to Blooms-End lately. I hope so.", "토머신이 요즘 블룸스엔드에 다녀갔는지 모르겠네."], ["But probably not, as she is, I believe, expecting to be confined in a month or so.", "다녀갔으면 좋으련만. 하지만 아마 아닐 거야, 내가 알기로 그 애는 한 달쯤 뒤에 해산할 예정이니까."], ["I wish I had thought of that before.", "진작 그 생각을 했어야 했는데."], ["Poor Mother must indeed be very lonely.”", "가엾은 어머니는 정말 무척 외로우실 거야.\""]], [["“I don’t like you going tonight.”", "\"오늘 밤 당신이 가는 게 싫어요.\""]], [["“Why not tonight?”", "\"왜 오늘 밤은 안 되지?\""]], [["“Something may be said which will terribly injure me.”", "\"저를 끔찍하게 해칠 무슨 말이 나올지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“My mother is not vindictive,”", "\"우리 어머니는 앙심을 품는 분이 아니야.\""], ["said Clym, his colour faintly rising.", "클림이 얼굴빛이 희미하게 붉어지며 말했다."]], [["“But I wish you would not go,”", "\"하지만 당신이 안 갔으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["Eustacia repeated in a low tone.", "유스테이시아가 낮은 목소리로 거듭 말했다."], ["“If you agree not to go tonight I promise to go by myself to her house tomorrow, and make it up with her, and wait till you fetch me.”", "\"오늘 밤은 안 가기로 해주시면, 제가 내일 혼자 그분 댁에 가서 화해하고, 당신이 데리러 올 때까지 기다리겠다고 약속할게요.\""]], [["“Why do you want to do that at this particular time, when at every previous time that I have proposed it you have refused?”", "\"전에 내가 그러자고 할 때마다 매번 마다하더니, 어째서 하필 이 시점에 그러고 싶어하는 거야?\""]], [["“I cannot explain further than that I should like to see her alone before you go,”", "\"당신이 가기 전에 제가 그분을 혼자 뵙고 싶다는 것 말고는 더 설명할 수가 없어요.\""], ["she answered, with an impatient move of her head, and looking at him with an anxiety more frequently seen upon those of a sanguine temperament than upon such as herself.", "그녀가 머리를 초조하게 한 번 움직이며 대답했는데, 그녀 같은 사람보다는 낙천적인 기질의 사람들에게서 더 자주 보이는 그런 불안을 띠고 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Well, it is very odd that just when I had decided to go myself you should want to do what I proposed long ago.", "\"참 이상하군, 막 내가 직접 가기로 정한 바로 그때에 당신은 내가 오래전에 제안했던 걸 하고 싶어하다니."], ["If I wait for you to go tomorrow another day will be lost; and I know I shall be unable to rest another night without having been.", "내일 당신이 가기를 기다리면 하루를 또 잃게 되고, 나는 다녀오지 않고서는 또 하룻밤을 도무지 편히 쉴 수 없을 게 분명해."], ["I want to get this settled, and will.", "나는 이 일을 매듭짓고 싶고, 그럴 거야."], ["You must visit her afterwards—it will be all the same.”", "당신은 나중에 그분을 찾아뵈면 돼⏤매한가지일 테니.\""]], [["“I could even go with you now?”", "\"지금 당신과 같이 가도 될까요?\""]], [["“You could scarcely walk there and back without a longer rest than I shall take.", "\"내가 쉴 것보다 더 오래 쉬지 않고서는 당신은 거기까지 갔다 오기 힘들 거야."], ["No, not tonight, Eustacia.”", "아니, 오늘 밤은 안 돼, 유스테이시아.\""]], [["“Let it be as you say, then,”", "\"그럼 당신 말씀대로 하죠.\""], ["she replied in the quiet way of one who, though willing to ward off evil consequences by a mild effort, would let events fall out as they might sooner than wrestle hard to direct them.", "그녀는, 비록 온건한 노력으로 나쁜 결과를 막으려는 마음은 있으나, 그것들을 기어이 비틀어 이끌려고 안간힘을 쓰느니 차라리 일이 되어가는 대로 내버려두려는 사람의 그 조용한 투로 대답했다."]], [["Clym then went into the garden; and a thoughtful languor stole over Eustacia for the remainder of the afternoon, which her husband attributed to the heat of the weather.", "이윽고 클림은 정원으로 나갔고, 오후의 남은 시간 내내 생각에 잠긴 나른함이 유스테이시아에게 스며들었는데, 남편은 그것을 날씨의 더위 탓으로 돌렸다."]], [["In the evening he set out on the journey.", "저녁이 되자 그는 그 여정에 나섰다."], ["Although the heat of summer was yet intense the days had considerably shortened, and before he had advanced a mile on his way all the heath purples, browns, and greens had merged in a uniform dress without airiness or graduation, and broken only by touches of white where the little heaps of clean quartz sand showed the entrance to a rabbit burrow, or where the white flints of a footpath lay like a thread over the slopes.", "여름의 더위는 아직 맹렬했으나 낮은 상당히 짧아져 있었고, 그가 길을 일 마일도 채 나아가기 전에 히스의 모든 보랏빛, 갈색, 초록빛은 산뜻함도 단계도 없는 한결같은 옷으로 녹아들어, 깨끗한 석영 모래의 작은 무더기들이 토끼 굴 입구를 드러내는 곳이나 오솔길의 흰 부싯돌들이 비탈 위로 실처럼 놓인 곳에서 흰빛으로 점점이 끊길 뿐이었다."], ["In almost every one of the isolated and stunted thorns which grew here and there a nighthawk revealed his presence by whirring like the clack of a mill as long as he could hold his breath, then stopping, flapping his wings, wheeling round the bush, alighting, and after a silent interval of listening beginning to whirr again.", "여기저기 자라난 외따로 떨어진 키 작은 가시나무들 거의 하나하나마다에서 쏙독새 한 마리가 숨을 참을 수 있는 한 물방앗간 덜컹이는 소리처럼 윙윙거리다가 멈추고, 날개를 퍼덕이고, 덤불 둘레를 빙 돌고, 내려앉고, 잠시 말없이 귀 기울인 뒤 다시 윙윙거리기 시작하는 것으로 제 존재를 드러냈다."], ["At each brushing of Clym’s feet white millermoths flew into the air just high enough to catch upon their dusty wings the mellowed light from the west, which now shone across the depressions and levels of the ground without falling thereon to light them up.", "클림의 발이 스칠 때마다 흰 가루나방들이 공중으로, 서쪽에서 비쳐오는 누그러진 빛을 그 가루투성이 날개로 받을 만큼만 딱 그만큼 높이 날아올랐는데, 그 빛은 이제 땅의 움푹한 곳과 평평한 곳을 가로질러 비치되 그 위에 내려앉아 그것들을 밝히지는 않았다."]], [["Yeobright walked on amid this quiet scene with a hope that all would soon be well.", "요브라이트는 모든 일이 곧 잘되리라는 희망을 안고 이 고요한 풍경 속을 걸어갔다."], ["Three miles on he came to a spot where a soft perfume was wafted across his path, and he stood still for a moment to inhale the familiar scent.", "삼 마일을 가서 그는 부드러운 향기가 길을 가로질러 풍겨오는 곳에 다다랐고, 그 낯익은 냄새를 들이마시려고 잠시 멈춰 섰다."], ["It was the place at which, four hours earlier, his mother had sat down exhausted on the knoll covered with shepherd’s-thyme.", "그곳은 네 시간 전에 그의 어머니가 백리향으로 뒤덮인 둔덕에 기진해 주저앉았던 자리였다."], ["While he stood a sound between a breathing and a moan suddenly reached his ears.", "그가 서 있는 동안 숨소리와 신음 사이쯤 되는 어떤 소리가 문득 그의 귀에 닿았다."]], [["He looked to where the sound came from; but nothing appeared there save the verge of the hillock stretching against the sky in an unbroken line.", "그는 그 소리가 나는 쪽을 보았으나, 거기에는 끊어지지 않은 선으로 하늘을 향해 뻗어 있는 둔덕의 가장자리 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["He moved a few steps in that direction, and now he perceived a recumbent figure almost close to his feet.", "그가 그쪽으로 몇 걸음 옮기자, 이제 그는 거의 제 발치에 누워 있는 한 형체를 알아보았다."]], [["Among the different possibilities as to the person’s individuality there did not for a moment occur to Yeobright that it might be one of his own family.", "그 사람이 누구일지에 관한 갖가지 가능성 가운데, 그것이 제 가족 중 하나일지 모른다는 생각은 요브라이트에게 한순간도 떠오르지 않았다."], ["Sometimes furze-cutters had been known to sleep out of doors at these times, to save a long journey homeward and back again; but Clym remembered the moan and looked closer, and saw that the form was feminine; and a distress came over him like cold air from a cave.", "이따금 가시금작화 베는 이들이 집까지 멀리 갔다가 다시 오는 수고를 덜려고 이런 때 한데서 자기도 한다는 것은 알려진 바였다. 그러나 클림은 그 신음을 떠올리고는 더 가까이 들여다보았고, 그 형체가 여자임을 알았다."], ["But he was not absolutely certain that the woman was his mother till he stooped and beheld her face, pallid, and with closed eyes.", "그러자 동굴에서 새어 나오는 찬 공기 같은 비통함이 그를 덮쳐왔다. 그러나 그는 몸을 굽혀 창백하고 눈을 감은 그 얼굴을 보고 나서야 비로소 그 여인이 제 어머니임을 완전히 확신했다."]], [["His breath went, as it were, out of his body and the cry of anguish which would have escaped him died upon his lips.", "숨이, 말하자면, 그의 몸에서 빠져나갔고, 그에게서 터져 나왔을 고뇌의 외침은 입술에서 사그라졌다."], ["During the momentary interval that elapsed before he became conscious that something must be done all sense of time and place left him, and it seemed as if he and his mother were as when he was a child with her many years ago on this heath at hours similar to the present.", "무언가 조처를 취해야 한다는 것을 의식하기까지의 그 찰나의 간격 동안 시간과 장소에 대한 모든 감각이 그에게서 떠났으니, 마치 그와\n그의 어머니는 여러 해 전 이 황야에서, 지금과 비슷한 시각에 그가 아이로서 함께 있을 때와 똑같았다."], ["Then he awoke to activity; and bending yet lower he found that she still breathed, and that her breath though feeble was regular, except when disturbed by an occasional gasp.", "그러자 그는 정신이 번쩍 들어 활동에 나섰고, 몸을 더 낮게 굽혀보니 어머니가 아직 숨을 쉬고 있으며, 그 숨은 약하긴 해도 이따금 헐떡임에 흐트러질 뿐 규칙적이라는 것을 알았다."]], [["“O, what is it!", "\"아, 이게 어찌 된 일이에요!"], ["Mother, are you very ill—you are not dying?”", "어머니, 많이 편찮으세요—돌아가시는 건 아니죠?\""], ["he cried, pressing his lips to her face.", "그가 입술을 어머니의 얼굴에 대며 외쳤다."], ["“I am your Clym.", "\"저 클림이에요."], ["How did you come here?", "어떻게 여기 오셨어요?"], ["What does it all mean?”", "이게 다 무슨 영문이에요?\""]], [["At that moment the chasm in their lives which his love for Eustacia had caused was not remembered by Yeobright, and to him the present joined continuously with that friendly past that had been their experience before the division.", "그 순간 유스테이샤에 대한 그의 사랑이 빚어낸 두 사람 삶 사이의 균열은 요브라이트의 기억에 떠오르지 않았으니, 그에게 현재는 갈라서기 전 두 사람이 함께 겪었던 그 다정한 과거와 끊김 없이 이어져 있었다."]], [["She moved her lips, appeared to know him, but could not speak; and then Clym strove to consider how best to move her, as it would be necessary to get her away from the spot before the dews were intense.", "어머니가 입술을 움직였고, 그를 알아보는 듯했으나 말은 하지 못했고, 그러자 클림은 어머니를 어떻게 옮기는 것이 가장 좋을지 헤아리려 애썼으니, 이슬이 짙어지기 전에 어머니를 이 자리에서 데려가야 할 것이었기 때문이다."], ["He was able-bodied, and his mother was thin.", "그는 몸이 튼튼했고, 어머니는 야위었다."], ["He clasped his arms round her, lifted her a little, and said, “Does that hurt you?”", "그는 두 팔로 어머니를 감싸 안고 조금 들어올리며 말했다. \"이러면 아프세요?\""]], [["She shook her head, and he lifted her up; then, at a slow pace, went onward with his load.", "어머니가 고개를 저었고, 그는 어머니를 들어올렸으며, 그러고는 느린 걸음으로 짐을 안고 나아갔다."], ["The air was now completely cool; but whenever he passed over a sandy patch of ground uncarpeted with vegetation there was reflected from its surface into his face the heat which it had imbibed during the day.", "이제 공기는 완전히 서늘했으나, 풀이 깔리지 않은 모래밭을 지날 때마다 그 표면에서 낮 동안 빨아들인 열기가 그의 얼굴로 반사되었다."], ["At the beginning of his undertaking he had thought but little of the distance which yet would have to be traversed before Blooms-End could be reached; but though he had slept that afternoon he soon began to feel the weight of his burden.", "일을 시작할 때만 해도 블룸스엔드에 닿기까지 가로질러야 할 거리에 대해서는 거의 생각하지 않았으나, 그날 오후에 잠을 자긴 했어도 곧 그는 짐의 무게를 느끼기 시작했다."], ["Thus he proceeded, like Æneas with his father; the bats circling round his head, nightjars flapping their wings within a yard of his face, and not a human being within call.", "그렇게 그는 아버지를 업은 아이네이아스처럼 나아갔으니, 박쥐들이 머리 주위를 맴돌고, 쏙독새들이 그의 얼굴에서 한 자도 안 되는 거리에서 날개를 퍼덕였으며, 부를 만한 사람이라곤 하나도 없었다."]], [["While he was yet nearly a mile from the house his mother exhibited signs of restlessness under the constraint of being borne along, as if his arms were irksome to her.", "집까지 아직 일 마일 가까이 남았을 즈음 어머니가 실려 가는 답답함에 안절부절못하는 기색을 보였으니, 마치 그의 두 팔이 거추장스럽다는 듯했다."], ["He lowered her upon his knees and looked around.", "그는 어머니를 무릎 위로 내려놓고 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["The point they had now reached, though far from any road, was not more than a mile from the Blooms-End cottages occupied by Fairway, Sam, Humphrey, and the Cantles.", "이제 두 사람이 다다른 지점은 어떤 길에서도 멀리 떨어져 있긴 했으나, 페어웨이와 샘, 험프리, 캔틀 일가가 사는 블룸스엔드의 오두막들에서 일 마일이 채 안 되는 곳이었다."], ["Moreover, fifty yards off stood a hut, built of clods and covered with thin turves, but now entirely disused.", "게다가 오십 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에 흙덩이로 짓고 얇은 뗏장으로 덮은 헛간이 하나 서 있었는데, 지금은 아주 버려진 채였다."], ["The simple outline of the lonely shed was visible, and thither he determined to direct his steps.", "외딴 헛간의 단순한 윤곽이 보였고, 그는 그쪽으로 발걸음을 향하기로 마음먹었다."], ["As soon as he arrived he laid her down carefully by the entrance, and then ran and cut with his pocketknife an armful of the dryest fern.", "도착하자마자 그는 어머니를 입구 옆에 조심스레 눕히고는, 달려가 주머니칼로 가장 마른 고사리를 한 아름 베었다."], ["Spreading this within the shed, which was entirely open on one side, he placed his mother thereon; then he ran with all his might towards the dwelling of Fairway.", "한쪽이 완전히 트인 헛간 안에 그것을 깔고 어머니를 그 위에 눕히고는, 페어웨이의 집을 향해 있는 힘껏 달렸다."]], [["Nearly a quarter of an hour had passed, disturbed only by the broken breathing of the sufferer, when moving figures began to animate the line between heath and sky.", "병자의 끊긴 숨소리만이 정적을 깨뜨리는 가운데 십오 분 가까이 흘렀을 즈음, 움직이는 형체들이 황야와 하늘 사이의 경계선을 살아 움직이게 하기 시작했다."], ["In a few moments Clym arrived with Fairway, Humphrey, and Susan Nunsuch; Olly Dowden, who had chanced to be at Fairway’s, Christian and Grandfer Cantle following helter-skelter behind.", "잠시 후 클림이 페어웨이, 험프리, 수전 넌서치와 함께 도착했고, 마침 페어웨이의 집에 와 있던 올리 다우든, 그리고 크리스천과 캔틀 영감이 뒤에서 허둥지둥 따라왔다."], ["They had brought a lantern and matches, water, a pillow, and a few other articles which had occurred to their minds in the hurry of the moment.", "그들은 등불과 성냥, 물, 베개, 그밖에 그 황급한 순간에 머리에 떠오른 몇 가지 물건을 가져왔다."], ["Sam had been despatched back again for brandy, and a boy brought Fairway’s pony, upon which he rode off to the nearest medical man, with directions to call at Wildeve’s on his way, and inform Thomasin that her aunt was unwell.", "샘은 브랜디를 가지러 다시 보내졌고, 한 소년이 페어웨이의 조랑말을 끌고 와서 그는 그것을 타고 가장 가까운 의원에게 달려갔는데, 가는 길에 와일디브의 집에 들러 토머신에게 숙모가 편찮다는 것을 알리라는 당부를 받았다."]], [["Sam and the brandy soon arrived, and it was administered by the light of the lantern; after which she became sufficiently conscious to signify by signs that something was wrong with her foot.", "샘과 브랜디가 곧 도착해 등불 빛 아래서 브랜디를 먹이니, 그 후 어머니는 발에 무언가 잘못되었다는 것을 손짓으로 알릴 만큼 의식을 차렸다."], ["Olly Dowden at length understood her meaning, and examined the foot indicated.", "올리 다우든이 마침내 그 뜻을 알아채고 가리킨 발을 살펴보았다."], ["It was swollen and red.", "발은 부어올라 붉었다."], ["Even as they watched the red began to assume a more livid colour, in the midst of which appeared a scarlet speck, smaller than a pea, and it was found to consist of a drop of blood, which rose above the smooth flesh of her ankle in a hemisphere.", "그들이 지켜보는 사이에도 붉은빛은 더 검푸른 색을 띠기 시작했고, 그 한가운데 완두콩보다 작은 진홍색 점 하나가 나타났는데, 살펴보니 그것은 핏방울 한 점으로, 발목의 매끄러운 살 위로 반구처럼 돋아 있었다."]], [["“I know what it is,”", "\"뭔지 알겠어요,\""], ["cried Sam.", "샘이 외쳤다."], ["“She has been stung by an adder!”", "\"독사에게 물리셨어요!\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said Clym instantly.", "클림이 곧바로 말했다."], ["“I remember when I was a child seeing just such a bite.", "\"어릴 적에 꼭 이런 물린 자국을 봤던 게 기억나."], ["O, my poor mother!”", "아, 가엾은 어머니!\""]], [["“It was my father who was bit,”", "\"우리 아버지가 물리셨었지,\""], ["said Sam.", "샘이 말했다."], ["“And there’s only one way to cure it.", "\"그리고 그걸 낫게 하는 길은 하나뿐이에요."], ["You must rub the place with the fat of other adders, and the only way to get that is by frying them.", "그 자리를 다른 독사들의 기름으로 문질러야 하는데, 그걸 얻는 유일한 방법은 그것들을 튀기는 거예요."], ["That’s what they did for him.”", "우리 아버지한테도 그렇게 했죠.\""]], [["“’Tis an old remedy,”", "\"그건 옛날 처방이라,\""], ["said Clym distrustfully, “and I have doubts about it.", "클림이 미심쩍게 말했다. \"나는 그게 미덥지가 않아."], ["But we can do nothing else till the doctor comes.”", "하지만 의사가 올 때까지는 달리 할 수 있는 게 없지.\""]], [["“’Tis a sure cure,”", "\"틀림없이 듣는 처방이에요,\""], ["said Olly Dowden, with emphasis.", "올리 다우든이 힘주어 말했다."], ["“I’ve used it when I used to go out nursing.”", "\"내가 병구완하러 다니던 시절에 써봤어요.\""]], [["“Then we must pray for daylight, to catch them,”", "\"그렇다면 날이 밝기를 빌어야겠군, 그것들을 잡으려면 말이야,\""], ["said Clym gloomily.", "클림이 침울하게 말했다."]], [["“I will see what I can do,”", "\"내가 어떻게든 해보지요,\""], ["said Sam.", "샘이 말했다."]], [["He took a green hazel which he had used as a walking stick, split it at the end, inserted a small pebble, and with the lantern in his hand went out into the heath.", "그는 지팡이로 쓰던 푸른 개암나무 가지를 가져다 끝을 쪼개고 작은 조약돌을 끼워 넣은 다음, 손에 등불을 들고 황야로 나갔다."], ["Clym had by this time lit a small fire, and despatched Susan Nunsuch for a frying pan.", "이때쯤 클림은 작은 불을 피워놓고 수전 넌서치를 보내 프라이팬을 가져오게 했다."], ["Before she had returned Sam came in with three adders, one briskly coiling and uncoiling in the cleft of the stick, and the other two hanging dead across it.", "그녀가 돌아오기도 전에 샘이 독사 세 마리를 들고 들어왔는데, 한 마리는 막대기 갈라진 틈에서 활발하게 똬리를 틀었다 풀었다 했고, 나머지 두 마리는 죽은 채 그 위에 걸쳐져 있었다."]], [["“I have only been able to get one alive and fresh as he ought to be,”", "\"마땅히 그래야 하는 만큼 산 채로 싱싱하게 잡은 건 한 마리뿐이에요,\""], ["said Sam.", "샘이 말했다."], ["“These limp ones are two I killed today at work; but as they don’t die till the sun goes down they can’t be very stale meat.”", "\"이 흐물흐물한 두 마리는 오늘 일하다가 잡은 건데, 해가 질 때까지는 죽지 않으니 그렇게 상한 고기는 아닐 거예요.\""]], [["The live adder regarded the assembled group with a sinister look in its small black eye, and the beautiful brown and jet pattern on its back seemed to intensify with indignation.", "산 독사는 작고 검은 눈에 음흉한 빛을 띤 채 모여 선 무리를 바라보았고, 등에 새겨진 아름다운 갈색과 검정 무늬는 분노로 한층 짙어지는 듯했다."], ["Mrs. Yeobright saw the creature, and the creature saw her—she quivered throughout, and averted her eyes.", "요브라이트 부인이 그 짐승을 보았고, 짐승도 그녀를 보았으니—그녀는 온몸을 떨며 눈길을 돌렸다."]], [["“Look at that,”", "\"저것 좀 봐,\""], ["murmured Christian Cantle.", "크리스천 캔틀이 중얼거렸다."], ["“Neighbours, how do we know but that something of the old serpent in God’s garden, that gied the apple to the young woman with no clothes, lives on in adders and snakes still?", "\"여보게들, 옷도 안 걸친 젊은 여자한테 사과를 건넨, 하느님의 동산에 있던 그 옛 뱀의 무언가가 아직도 독사와 뱀들 속에 살아 있지 않다고 어찌 장담하겠나?"], ["Look at his eye—for all the world like a villainous sort of black currant.", "그 눈을 좀 봐—영락없이 무슨 사악한 검은 까치밥나무 열매 같잖아."], ["’Tis to be hoped he can’t ill-wish us!", "우리한테 악담을 못 걸기를 바라야지!"], ["There’s folks in heath who’ve been overlooked already.", "황야엔 벌써 흘린 눈길을 받은 사람들도 있다고."], ["I will never kill another adder as long as I live.”", "나는 살아 있는 한 다시는 독사를 죽이지 않을 테야.\""]], [["“Well, ’tis right to be afeard of things, if folks can’t help it,”", "\"하긴, 어쩔 수 없다면 이런저런 걸 무서워하는 것도 옳지,\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle.", "캔틀 영감이 말했다."], ["“’Twould have saved me many a brave danger in my time.”", "\"그랬으면 내 평생 숱한 용감한 위험을 피할 수 있었을 텐데.\""]], [["“I fancy I heard something outside the shed,”", "\"헛간 밖에서 무슨 소리가 들린 것 같아,\""], ["said Christian.", "크리스천이 말했다."], ["“I wish troubles would come in the daytime, for then a man could show his courage, and hardly beg for mercy of the most broomstick old woman he should see, if he was a brave man, and able to run out of her sight!”", "\"재난이 낮에 닥쳐주면 좋겠어, 그래야 사내가 용기를 보일 수도 있고, 용감한 사내라서 그 여편네 눈에 안 띄게 달아날 수만 있다면, 아무리 빗자루 같은 늙은 마녀를 만나도 자비를 구걸하지 않아도 될 테니!\""]], [["“Even such an ignorant fellow as I should know better than do that,”", "\"나처럼 무지한 작자도 그것보단 잘 알겠다,\""], ["said Sam.", "샘이 말했다."]], [["“Well, there’s calamities where we least expect it, whether or no.", "\"하긴, 어찌 됐든 가장 안 그럴 것 같은 데서 재앙이 닥치는 법이지."], ["Neighbours, if Mrs. Yeobright were to die, d’ye think we should be took up and tried for the manslaughter of a woman?”", "여보게들, 요브라이트 부인이 죽기라도 하면 우리가 한 여자를 죽인 과실치사로 붙들려 재판받게 될까?\""]], [["“No, they couldn’t bring it in as that,”", "\"아니, 그렇게 몰아갈 수는 없어,\""], ["said Sam, “unless they could prove we had been poachers at some time of our lives.", "샘이 말했다. \"우리가 살면서 언젠가 밀렵꾼이었다고 입증할 수 있다면 모를까."], ["But she’ll fetch round.”", "하지만 부인은 회복하실 거야.\""]], [["“Now, if I had been stung by ten adders I should hardly have lost a day’s work for’t,”", "\"이봐, 내가 독사 열 마리한테 물렸어도 그 때문에 하루치 일을 잃지는 않았을 거야,\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle.", "캔틀 영감이 말했다."], ["“Such is my spirit when I am on my mettle.", "\"내가 기운이 펄펄할 때면 그런 사람이지."], ["But perhaps ’tis natural in a man trained for war.", "하긴 전쟁을 위해 훈련받은 사내한테는 자연스러운 건지도 모르지."], ["Yes, I’ve gone through a good deal; but nothing ever came amiss to me after I joined the Locals in four.”", "그래, 나는 별의별 일을 다 겪었어. 하지만 사 년에 의용군에 들어간 뒤로는 무엇 하나 나한테 거슬리는 게 없었지.\""], ["He shook his head and smiled at a mental picture of himself in uniform.", "그는 고개를 저으며 제복을 입은 자신의 모습을 마음속에 그리고는 미소 지었다."], ["“I was always first in the most galliantest scrapes in my younger days!”", "\"젊은 시절엔 가장 늠름한 곤경에서 늘 내가 앞장섰단 말이야!\""]], [["“I suppose that was because they always used to put the biggest fool afore,”", "\"그건 늘 가장 멍청한 놈을 앞세웠기 때문이겠지,\""], ["said Fairway from the fire, beside which he knelt, blowing it with his breath.", "불가에 무릎 꿇고 입김으로 불을 살리던 페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["“D’ye think so, Timothy?”", "\"그렇게 생각하나, 티머시?\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, coming forward to Fairway’s side with sudden depression in his face.", "캔틀 영감이 얼굴에 갑작스러운 침울함을 띠고 페어웨이 곁으로 다가오며 말했다."], ["“Then a man may feel for years that he is good solid company, and be wrong about himself after all?”", "\"그렇다면 사내가 여러 해 동안 자기가 든든하고 알찬 동무라고 느껴왔는데, 결국 제 자신에 대해 틀렸던 걸 수도 있는 건가?\""]], [["“Never mind that question, Grandfer.", "\"그딴 문제는 신경 쓰지 마쇼, 영감."], ["Stir your stumps and get some more sticks.", "다리나 놀려서 장작이나 좀 더 가져오쇼."], ["’Tis very nonsense of an old man to prattle so when life and death’s in mangling.”", "생사가 갈리는 판에 늙은이가 그렇게 떠벌리는 건 영 같잖은 짓이오.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래,\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, with melancholy conviction.", "캔틀 영감이 우울한 확신을 담아 말했다."], ["“Well, this is a bad night altogether for them that have done well in their time; and if I were ever such a dab at the hautboy or tenor viol, I shouldn’t have the heart to play tunes upon ’em now.”", "\"하긴, 한창때 잘나가던 이들한테는 오늘 밤이 통째로 고약한 밤이지. 내가 아무리 오보에나 테너 비올을 잘 다뤘던들, 지금 그것들로 가락을 켤 마음은 안 났을 테야.\""]], [["Susan now arrived with the frying pan, when the live adder was killed and the heads of the three taken off.", "이제 수전이 프라이팬을 들고 도착하자, 산 독사를 죽이고 세 마리의 머리를 모두 떼어냈다."], ["The remainders, being cut into lengths and split open, were tossed into the pan, which began hissing and crackling over the fire.", "나머지 몸통은 토막 내어 갈라 펼친 채 팬에 던져 넣으니, 팬이 불 위에서 지글지글 타닥타닥 소리를 내기 시작했다."], ["Soon a rill of clear oil trickled from the carcases, whereupon Clym dipped the corner of his handkerchief into the liquid and anointed the wound.", "곧 사체에서 맑은 기름이 가느다란 줄기로 흘러나왔고, 그러자 클림은 손수건 모서리를 그 액체에 적셔 상처에 발랐다."]], [["VIII.", "VIII."], ["Eustacia Hears of Good Fortune, and Beholds Evil", "유스테이샤가 행운의 소식을 듣고, 악을 목도하다"]], [["In the meantime Eustacia, left alone in her cottage at Alderworth, had become considerably depressed by the posture of affairs.", "그동안 올더워스의 오두막에 홀로 남겨진 유스테이샤는 사태의 형국에 적잖이 침울해져 있었다."], ["The consequences which might result from Clym’s discovery that his mother had been turned from his door that day were likely to be disagreeable, and this was a quality in events which she hated as much as the dreadful.", "어머니가 그날 자기 집 문 앞에서 쫓겨났다는 사실을 클림이 알게 됨으로써 빚어질 수 있는 결과들은 불쾌할 공산이 컸으니, 이는 그녀가 끔찍한 것 못지않게 싫어하는, 사건이 지닌 성질이었다."]], [["To be left to pass the evening by herself was irksome to her at any time, and this evening it was more irksome than usual by reason of the excitements of the past hours.", "홀로 저녁 시간을 보내도록 남겨지는 것은 언제든 그녀에게 거추장스러운 일이었으나, 이날 저녁은 지난 몇 시간의 흥분 때문에 여느 때보다 더 거추장스러웠다."], ["The two visits had stirred her into restlessness.", "두 차례의 방문이 그녀를 안절부절못하게 휘저어 놓았던 것이다."], ["She was not wrought to any great pitch of uneasiness by the probability of appearing in an ill light in the discussion between Clym and his mother, but she was wrought to vexation, and her slumbering activities were quickened to the extent of wishing that she had opened the door.", "클림과 그 어머니 사이의 언쟁에서 자기가 나쁜 처지에 놓이게 될 개연성에 대해 그녀가 크게 불안에 휩싸인 것은 아니었으나, 짜증이 일도록 휘저어진 것은 사실이었으니, 잠들어 있던 활동성이 되살아나 차라리 문을 열어줄걸 하고 바라는 지경에 이르렀다."], ["She had certainly believed that Clym was awake, and the excuse would be an honest one as far as it went; but nothing could save her from censure in refusing to answer at the first knock.", "그녀는 분명 클림이 깨어 있다고 믿었었고, 그 변명은 그 한도 안에서는 정직한 것이었으나, 첫 노크에 응답하기를 거부한 일에 대해서는 그 무엇도 그녀를 비난에서 구해줄 수 없었다."], ["Yet, instead of blaming herself for the issue she laid the fault upon the shoulders of some indistinct, colossal Prince of the World, who had framed her situation and ruled her lot.", "그러나 그녀는 그 결과에 대해 자신을 탓하는 대신, 자기 처지를 짜고 자기 운명을 지배해온, 어떤 흐릿하고 거대한 이 세상의 군주의 어깨에 그 잘못을 떠넘겼다."]], [["At this time of the year it was pleasanter to walk by night than by day, and when Clym had been absent about an hour she suddenly resolved to go out in the direction of Blooms-End, on the chance of meeting him on his return.", "해마다 이맘때면 낮보다 밤에 걷는 것이 더 쾌적했고, 클림이 떠난 지 한 시간쯤 되었을 무렵 그녀는 돌아오는 그를 만날 수 있을까 하여 불현듯 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 나가보기로 마음먹었다."], ["When she reached the garden gate she heard wheels approaching, and looking round beheld her grandfather coming up in his car.", "정원 문에 다다랐을 때 바퀴 소리가 다가오는 것이 들렸고, 둘러보니 할아버지가 마차를 몰고 올라오고 있었다."]], [["“I can’t stay a minute, thank ye,”", "\"고맙다만 잠시도 못 머문다,\""], ["he answered to her greeting.", "그가 그녀의 인사에 답했다."], ["“I am driving to East Egdon; but I came round here just to tell you the news.", "\"이스트에그던으로 가는 길이야. 다만 소식이나 전하려고 이리 둘러 왔지."], ["Perhaps you have heard—about Mr. Wildeve’s fortune?”", "어쩌면 들었을지도 모르겠다—와일디브 씨의 재산에 대해서 말이다.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아뇨,\""], ["said Eustacia blankly.", "유스테이샤가 멍하니 말했다."]], [["“Well, he has come into a fortune of eleven thousand pounds—uncle died in Canada, just after hearing that all his family, whom he was sending home, had gone to the bottom in the Cassiopeia; so Wildeve has come into everything, without in the least expecting it.”", "\"글쎄, 그 사람이 만천 파운드의 재산을 물려받게 됐단다—캐나다에 있던 삼촌이 죽었는데, 본국으로 보내려던 식구들이 죄다 카시오페이아호와 함께 가라앉았다는 소식을 듣자마자 죽었다지. 그래서 와일디브가 전혀 기대도 안 했는데 모든 걸 다 물려받게 된 거야.\""]], [["Eustacia stood motionless awhile.", "유스테이샤는 잠시 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다."], ["“How long has he known of this?”", "\"그가 이 일을 안 지 얼마나 됐어요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Well, it was known to him this morning early, for I knew it at ten o’clock, when Charley came back.", "\"글쎄, 오늘 아침 일찍 알았을 게다, 내가 열 시에 알았으니 말이다, 찰리가 돌아왔을 때였지."], ["Now, he is what I call a lucky man.", "자, 그가 바로 내가 말하는 운 좋은 사내야."], ["What a fool you were, Eustacia!”", "너야말로 어리석었지, 유스테이샤!\""]], [["“In what way?”", "\"어떤 점에서요?\""], ["she said, lifting her eyes in apparent calmness.", "그녀가 짐짓 침착하게 눈을 들며 말했다."]], [["“Why, in not sticking to him when you had him.”", "\"아니, 잡았을 때 붙들어두지 않았잖니.\""]], [["“Had him, indeed!”", "\"잡긴 뭘 잡아요!\""]], [["“I did not know there had ever been anything between you till lately; and, faith, I should have been hot and strong against it if I had known; but since it seems that there was some sniffing between ye, why the deuce didn’t you stick to him?”", "\"나는 최근까지도 너희 사이에 뭔가 있었던 줄 몰랐다. 그래, 알았더라면 펄펄 뛰며 격하게 반대했겠지. 하지만 너희 사이에 얼마간 냄새가 났던 듯하니, 그렇다면 도대체 왜 그를 붙들어두지 않았느냐?\""]], [["Eustacia made no reply, but she looked as if she could say as much upon that subject as he if she chose.", "유스테이샤는 아무런 대꾸도 하지 않았으나, 마음만 먹으면 그 일에 대해 자기도 그만큼 할 말이 있다는 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["“And how is your poor purblind husband?”", "\"그래, 네 가엾은 반쯤 눈먼 남편은 좀 어떠냐?\""], ["continued the old man.", "노인이 말을 이었다."], ["“Not a bad fellow either, as far as he goes.”", "\"그 친구도 그 나름으로는 나쁜 사람은 아니지.\""]], [["“He is quite well.”", "\"아주 잘 지내요.\""]], [["“It is a good thing for his cousin what-d’ye-call-her?", "\"그 사촌, 그 뭐라더라? 그 여자한테는 잘된 일이지."], ["By George, you ought to have been in that galley, my girl!", "이런, 네가 그 배에 탔어야 하는 건데, 얘야!"], ["Now I must drive on.", "자, 이제 가봐야겠다."], ["Do you want any assistance?", "도움이 필요하냐?"], ["What’s mine is yours, you know.”", "내 것이 곧 네 것이라는 거, 알지.\""]], [["“Thank you, Grandfather, we are not in want at present,”", "\"고맙지만 할아버지, 지금은 부족한 게 없어요,\""], ["she said coldly.", "그녀가 차갑게 말했다."], ["“Clym cuts furze, but he does it mostly as a useful pastime, because he can do nothing else.”", "\"클림은 가시금작화를 베고 있지만, 거의 유익한 소일거리 삼아 하는 거예요, 달리 할 수 있는 게 없으니까요.\""]], [["“He is paid for his pastime, isn’t he?", "\"그 소일거리로 삯도 받지 않느냐?"], ["Three shillings a hundred, I heard.”", "백 단에 삼 실링이라고 들었다만.\""]], [["“Clym has money,”", "\"클림은 돈이 있어요,\""], ["she said, colouring, “but he likes to earn a little.”", "그녀가 낯을 붉히며 말했다. \"다만 조금이라도 벌고 싶어 하는 거예요.\""]], [["“Very well; good night.”", "\"좋아, 잘 자거라.\""], ["And the captain drove on.", "그러고는 선장은 마차를 몰고 떠났다."]], [["When her grandfather was gone Eustacia went on her way mechanically; but her thoughts were no longer concerning her mother-in-law and Clym.", "할아버지가 가버리자 유스테이샤는 기계적으로 가던 길을 걸었으나, 그녀의 생각은 더 이상 시어머니와 클림에 관한 것이 아니었다."], ["Wildeve, notwithstanding his complaints against his fate, had been seized upon by destiny and placed in the sunshine once more.", "와일디브는 제 운명에 대한 온갖 불평에도 불구하고 운명의 손에 붙들려 다시금 햇살 속에 놓인 것이었다."], ["Eleven thousand pounds!", "만천 파운드!"], ["From every Egdon point of view he was a rich man.", "모든 에그던식 관점에서 그는 부자였다."], ["In Eustacia’s eyes, too, it was an ample sum—one sufficient to supply those wants of hers which had been stigmatized by Clym in his more austere moods as vain and luxurious.", "유스테이샤의 눈에도 그것은 넉넉한 액수였으니—클림이 더 엄격한 기분일 때면 헛되고 사치스럽다고 낙인찍던 그녀의 그 욕구들을 채워주기에 충분한 금액이었다."], ["Though she was no lover of money she loved what money could bring; and the new accessories she imagined around him clothed Wildeve with a great deal of interest.", "그녀가 돈을 사랑하는 사람은 아니었으나 돈이 가져다줄 수 있는 것은 사랑했고, 이제 그를 둘러싸고 있으리라 상상되는 그 새로운 부속물들이 와일디브를 더없는 흥미로 휘감았다."], ["She recollected now how quietly well-dressed he had been that morning—he had probably put on his newest suit, regardless of damage by briars and thorns.", "그녀는 이제 그가 그날 아침 얼마나 조용히 잘 차려입고 있었던가를 떠올렸으니—그는 아마 찔레와 가시에 상하든 말든 아랑곳 않고 가장 새 옷을 차려입었던 것이다."], ["And then she thought of his manner towards herself.", "그러고는 그가 자기를 대하던 태도를 생각했다."]], [["“O I see it, I see it,”", "\"아, 알겠어, 알겠어,\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“How much he wishes he had me now, that he might give me all I desire!”", "\"그가 지금 나를 가졌으면 하고 얼마나 바라겠어, 내가 원하는 걸 죄다 줄 수 있을 테니까!\""]], [["In recalling the details of his glances and words—at the time scarcely regarded—it became plain to her how greatly they had been dictated by his knowledge of this new event.", "당시에는 거의 눈여겨보지 않았던 그의 눈길과 말의 세세한 점들을 되짚어보니, 그것들이 얼마나 크게 이 새로운 사건에 대한 그의 앎에 좌우되었던가가 그녀에게 분명해졌다."], ["“Had he been a man to bear a jilt ill-will he would have told me of his good fortune in crowing tones; instead of doing that he mentioned not a word, in deference to my misfortunes, and merely implied that he loved me still, as one superior to him.”", "\"버림받은 데 앙심을 품을 사내였다면 의기양양한 어조로 제 행운을 내게 떠벌렸겠지. 그러기는커녕 그는 내 불운을 헤아려 한 마디도 입에 올리지 않았고, 그저 자기보다 우월한 사람을 대하듯 여전히 나를 사랑한다고만 넌지시 비쳤을 뿐이야.\""]], [["Wildeve’s silence that day on what had happened to him was just the kind of behaviour calculated to make an impression on such a woman.", "자기에게 일어난 일에 대해 그날 와일디브가 보인 침묵은 바로 그런 여자에게 깊은 인상을 남기도록 계산된 종류의 처신이었다."], ["Those delicate touches of good taste were, in fact, one of the strong points in his demeanour towards the other sex.", "그 섬세한 고상함의 손길이야말로 실은 이성을 대하는 그의 태도에서 강점 가운데 하나였다."], ["The peculiarity of Wildeve was that, while at one time passionate, upbraiding, and resentful towards a woman, at another he would treat her with such unparalleled grace as to make previous neglect appear as no discourtesy, injury as no insult, interference as a delicate attention, and the ruin of her honour as excess of chivalry.", "와일디브의 특이한 점은, 어떤 때는 한 여자에게 열정적이고 비난조이며 원망에 차 있다가도, 또 어떤 때는 전에 없이 우아하게 대함으로써, 앞서의 무관심을 무례가 아닌 것처럼, 해를 끼친 것을 모욕이 아닌 것처럼, 간섭을 섬세한 배려처럼, 그녀의 명예를 망친 것을 지나친 기사도처럼 보이게 만든다는 데 있었다."], ["This man, whose admiration today Eustacia had disregarded, whose good wishes she had scarcely taken the trouble to accept, whom she had shown out of the house by the back door, was the possessor of eleven thousand pounds—a man of fair professional education, and one who had served his articles with a civil engineer.", "그날 유스테이샤가 그 흠모를 무시했고, 그 호의를 애써 받아들이려 들지도 않았으며, 뒷문으로 집에서 내보냈던 이 사내가 바로 만천 파운드의 소유자였으니—제법 전문적인 교육을 받았고, 토목 기사 밑에서 견습 기간을 마친 사람이었다."]], [["So intent was Eustacia upon Wildeve’s fortunes that she forgot how much closer to her own course were those of Clym; and instead of walking on to meet him at once she sat down upon a stone.", "와일디브의 처지에 너무나 몰두한 나머지 유스테이샤는 클림의 처지가 자기 행로에 얼마나 더 가까이 닿아 있는가를 잊어버렸고, 곧장 걸어가 그를 만나는 대신 돌 위에 주저앉았다."], ["She was disturbed in her reverie by a voice behind, and turning her head beheld the old lover and fortunate inheritor of wealth immediately beside her.", "등 뒤의 목소리에 몽상이 흐트러져 고개를 돌리니, 그 옛 연인이자 행운으로 부를 물려받은 이가 바로 그녀 곁에 와 있었다."]], [["She remained sitting, though the fluctuation in her look might have told any man who knew her so well as Wildeve that she was thinking of him.", "그녀는 앉은 채로 있었으나, 그 표정의 일렁임은 와일디브만큼 그녀를 잘 아는 사내라면 누구든 그녀가 그를 생각하고 있었음을 알아챌 만한 것이었다."]], [["“How did you come here?”", "\"어떻게 여기 왔어요?\""], ["she said in her clear low tone.", "그녀가 맑고 낮은 어조로 말했다."], ["“I thought you were at home.”", "\"집에 있는 줄 알았는데.\""]], [["“I went on to the village after leaving your garden; and now I have come back again—that’s all.", "\"당신 정원을 떠난 뒤 마을로 갔다가, 이제 다시 돌아오는 길이오—그게 다요."], ["Which way are you walking, may I ask?”", "어느 쪽으로 걷고 있는지 물어봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["She waved her hand in the direction of Blooms-End.", "그녀는 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 손을 저어 보였다."], ["“I am going to meet my husband.", "\"남편을 만나러 가는 길이에요."], ["I think I may possibly have got into trouble whilst you were with me today.”", "오늘 당신이 나와 함께 있는 동안 아마 곤란한 일에 빠졌을지도 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“How could that be?”", "\"어쩌다 그럴 수가 있소?\""]], [["“By not letting in Mrs. Yeobright.”", "\"요브라이트 부인을 들이지 않았으니까요.\""]], [["“I hope that visit of mine did you no harm.”", "\"그 방문이 당신에게 해를 끼치지 않았기를 바라오.\""]], [["“None.", "\"전혀요."], ["It was not your fault,”", "당신 탓은 아니에요,\""], ["she said quietly.", "그녀가 조용히 말했다."]], [["By this time she had risen; and they involuntarily sauntered on together, without speaking, for two or three minutes; when Eustacia broke silence by saying, “I assume I must congratulate you.”", "이때쯤 그녀는 일어서 있었고, 두 사람은 저도 모르게 아무 말 없이 이삼 분 동안 함께 거닐었는데, 유스테이샤가 침묵을 깨고 말했다. \"축하 인사를 드려야 할 것 같네요.\""]], [["“On what?", "\"무엇에 대해?"], ["O yes; on my eleven thousand pounds, you mean.", "아 그렇지, 내 만천 파운드 말이군."], ["Well, since I didn’t get something else, I must be content with getting that.”", "글쎄, 다른 무언가를 얻지 못했으니, 그거라도 얻은 걸로 만족해야겠지.\""]], [["“You seem very indifferent about it.", "\"그 일에 대해 무척 무심해 보이네요."], ["Why didn’t you tell me today when you came?”", "오늘 왔을 때 왜 내게 말해주지 않았어요?\""], ["she said in the tone of a neglected person.", "그녀가 무시당한 사람의 어조로 말했다."], ["“I heard of it quite by accident.”", "\"나는 순전히 우연히 들었다고요.\""]], [["“I did mean to tell you,”", "\"말해줄 생각이긴 했소,\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."], ["“But I—well, I will speak frankly—I did not like to mention it when I saw, Eustacia, that your star was not high.", "\"하지만 나는—그래, 솔직히 말하리다—유스테이샤, 당신의 별이 높이 떠 있지 않은 걸 보고는 그 얘길 꺼내고 싶지 않았소."], ["The sight of a man lying wearied out with hard work, as your husband lay, made me feel that to brag of my own fortune to you would be greatly out of place.", "당신 남편이 그러고 누워 있던 것처럼, 한 사내가 고된 일에 지쳐 쓰러져 누워 있는 모습을 보니, 당신에게 내 행운을 자랑한다는 게 영 격에 맞지 않게 느껴졌소."], ["Yet, as you stood there beside him, I could not help feeling too that in many respects he was a richer man than I.”", "그러면서도, 당신이 그 곁에 서 있는 모습을 보니, 여러모로 그가 나보다 더 부유한 사내라는 느낌이 들지 않을 수 없었소.\""]], [["At this Eustacia said, with slumbering mischievousness, “What, would you exchange with him—your fortune for me?”", "이에 유스테이샤가 잠들어 있던 짓궂음을 담아 말했다. \"어머, 그럼 그와 바꾸겠어요—당신 재산을 주고 나를 가지겠어요?\""]], [["“I certainly would,”", "\"물론 그러겠소,\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."]], [["“As we are imagining what is impossible and absurd, suppose we change the subject?”", "\"우리가 불가능하고 터무니없는 걸 상상하고 있으니, 이쯤에서 화제를 바꾸는 게 어때요?\""]], [["“Very well; and I will tell you of my plans for the future, if you care to hear them.", "\"좋소. 듣고 싶다면 앞날에 대한 내 계획을 말해주겠소."], ["I shall permanently invest nine thousand pounds, keep one thousand as ready money, and with the remaining thousand travel for a year or so.”", "구천 파운드는 영구히 투자하고, 천 파운드는 당장 쓸 돈으로 두고, 남은 천 파운드로 일 년 남짓 여행을 다닐 작정이오.\""]], [["“Travel?", "\"여행이요?"], ["What a bright idea!", "정말 멋진 생각이네요!"], ["Where will you go to?”", "어디로 갈 건데요?\""]], [["“From here to Paris, where I shall pass the winter and spring.", "\"여기서 파리로 가서 거기서 겨울과 봄을 보낼 거요."], ["Then I shall go to Italy, Greece, Egypt, and Palestine, before the hot weather comes on.", "그런 다음 더위가 닥치기 전에 이탈리아, 그리스, 이집트, 팔레스타인으로 갈 거요."], ["In the summer I shall go to America; and then, by a plan not yet settled, I shall go to Australia and round to India.", "여름엔 아메리카로 가고, 그다음엔 아직 정해지지 않은 계획대로 오스트레일리아로 갔다가 인도로 돌아올 거요."], ["By that time I shall have begun to have had enough of it.", "그쯤이면 슬슬 진력이 나기 시작하겠지."], ["Then I shall probably come back to Paris again, and there I shall stay as long as I can afford to.”", "그러면 아마 다시 파리로 돌아와서, 형편이 닿는 한 거기 오래 머물 거요.\""]], [["“Back to Paris again,”", "\"다시 파리로,\""], ["she murmured in a voice that was nearly a sigh.", "그녀가 거의 한숨에 가까운 목소리로 중얼거렸다."], ["She had never once told Wildeve of the Parisian desires which Clym’s description had sown in her; yet here was he involuntarily in a position to gratify them.", "클림의 묘사가 그녀 안에 심어놓은 그 파리에 대한 갈망을 그녀는 와일디브에게 단 한 번도 말한 적이 없었으나, 여기 그가 저도 모르게 그 갈망을 채워줄 수 있는 처지에 있었던 것이다."], ["“You think a good deal of Paris?”", "\"파리를 무척 높이 치는군요?\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["In my opinion it is the central beauty-spot of the world.”", "내 생각에 그곳은 세상의 중심이 되는 아름다운 곳이오.\""]], [["“And in mine!", "\"내 생각에도요!"], ["And Thomasin will go with you?”", "그럼 토머신도 같이 가나요?\""]], [["“Yes, if she cares to.", "\"그럴 마음이 있다면 그렇겠지."], ["She may prefer to stay at home.”", "집에 남는 편을 택할지도 모르고.\""]], [["“So you will be going about, and I shall be staying here!”", "\"그럼 당신은 여기저기 돌아다니고, 나는 여기 머물러 있게 되겠네요!\""]], [["“I suppose you will.", "\"그렇게 되겠지."], ["But we know whose fault that is.”", "하지만 그게 누구 탓인지는 우리가 알잖소.\""]], [["“I am not blaming you,”", "\"당신을 탓하는 건 아니에요,\""], ["she said quickly.", "그녀가 얼른 말했다."]], [["“Oh, I thought you were.", "\"오, 탓하는 줄 알았소."], ["If ever you _should_ be inclined to blame me, think of a certain evening by Rainbarrow, when you promised to meet me and did not.", "혹여라도 나를 탓하고 싶어지거든, 레인배로 곁의 어느 저녁을 떠올려보시오, 나를 만나기로 약속해놓고 나오지 않았던 그날을."], ["You sent me a letter; and my heart ached to read that as I hope yours never will.", "당신은 내게 편지를 보냈고, 나는 그걸 읽으며 가슴이 아렸으니, 당신은 부디 그런 일이 없기를 바라오."], ["That was one point of divergence.", "그게 한 갈림길이었지."], ["I then did something in haste....", "그러고 나서 나는 성급하게 무언가를 했고……"], ["But she is a good woman, and I will say no more.”", "하지만 그 여자는 착한 여인이니, 더는 말하지 않으리다.\""]], [["“I know that the blame was on my side that time,”", "\"그때는 잘못이 내 쪽에 있었다는 거 알아요,\""], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 말했다."], ["“But it had not always been so.", "\"하지만 늘 그랬던 건 아니에요."], ["However, it is my misfortune to be too sudden in feeling.", "하지만 너무 갑작스레 감정에 휩쓸리는 게 내 불운이지요."], ["O, Damon, don’t reproach me any more—I can’t bear that.”", "아, 데이먼, 더 이상 나를 책망하지 말아요—나는 그걸 견딜 수가 없어요.\""]], [["They went on silently for a distance of two or three miles, when Eustacia said suddenly, “Haven’t you come out of your way, Mr.", "두 사람은 이삼 마일 거리를 말없이 걸었는데, 유스테이샤가 불쑥 말했다."], ["Wildeve?”", "\"당신 가던 길에서 벗어난 거 아니에요, 와일디브 씨?\""]], [["“My way is anywhere tonight.", "\"오늘 밤 내 길은 어디든 다 길이오."], ["I will go with you as far as the hill on which we can see Blooms-End, as it is getting late for you to be alone.”", "당신 혼자 다니기엔 늦은 시각이니, 블룸스엔드가 보이는 언덕까지 함께 가리다.\""]], [["“Don’t trouble.", "\"수고하지 말아요."], ["I am not obliged to be out at all.", "나는 꼭 나와 있어야 할 까닭도 없어요."], ["I think I would rather you did not accompany me further.", "더는 동행하지 않는 편이 나을 것 같아요."], ["This sort of thing would have an odd look if known.”", "이런 일은 알려지면 묘하게 보일 테니까요.\""]], [["“Very well, I will leave you.”", "\"좋소, 그럼 당신을 두고 가리다.\""], ["He took her hand unexpectedly, and kissed it—for the first time since her marriage.", "그가 뜻밖에 그녀의 손을 잡더니 입을 맞추었다—그녀가 결혼한 이래 처음이었다."], ["“What light is that on the hill?”", "\"저 언덕 위의 저 불빛은 뭐요?\""], ["he added, as it were to hide the caress.", "그가 마치 그 입맞춤을 감추려는 듯 덧붙였다."]], [["She looked, and saw a flickering firelight proceeding from the open side of a hovel a little way before them.", "그녀가 바라보니, 조금 앞쪽 오두막의 트인 한쪽에서 깜빡이는 불빛이 새어 나오고 있었다."], ["The hovel, which she had hitherto always found empty, seemed to be inhabited now.", "여태껏 늘 비어 있던 그 오두막에 이제 누군가 들어와 있는 듯했다."]], [["“Since you have come so far,”", "\"기왕 이렇게 멀리까지 왔으니,\""], ["said Eustacia, “will you see me safely past that hut?", "유스테이샤가 말했다. \"저 헛간을 무사히 지날 때까지만 데려다줄래요?"], ["I thought I should have met Clym somewhere about here, but as he doesn’t appear I will hasten on and get to Blooms-End before he leaves.”", "이 근처 어디선가 클림을 만날 줄 알았는데, 나타나질 않으니 서둘러 가서 그가 떠나기 전에 블룸스엔드에 닿아야겠어요.\""]], [["They advanced to the turf-shed, and when they got near it the firelight and the lantern inside showed distinctly enough the form of a woman reclining on a bed of fern, a group of heath men and women standing around her.", "두 사람이 뗏장 헛간으로 다가가니, 가까이 갔을 때 안쪽의 불빛과 등불이 고사리 자리에 비스듬히 누운 한 여인의 모습과 그 주위에 둘러선 황야의 남녀 무리를 또렷이 비춰 보였다."], ["Eustacia did not recognize Mrs. Yeobright in the reclining figure, nor Clym as one of the standers-by till she came close.", "유스테이샤는 가까이 다가가서야 비로소 누운 형체가 요브라이트 부인임을, 그리고 둘러선 이들 가운데 한 사람이 클림임을 알아보았다."], ["Then she quickly pressed her hand up on Wildeve’s arm and signified to him to come back from the open side of the shed into the shadow.", "그러자 그녀는 황급히 와일디브의 팔을 손으로 눌러, 헛간의 트인 쪽에서 그늘 속으로 물러나자고 신호했다."]], [["“It is my husband and his mother,”", "\"내 남편하고 그 어머니예요,\""], ["she whispered in an agitated voice.", "그녀가 동요한 목소리로 속삭였다."], ["“What can it mean?", "\"이게 무슨 일이지?"], ["Will you step forward and tell me?”", "앞으로 나가서 알아봐 줄래요?\""]], [["Wildeve left her side and went to the back wall of the hut.", "와일디브가 그녀 곁을 떠나 오두막 뒷벽으로 갔다."], ["Presently Eustacia perceived that he was beckoning to her, and she advanced and joined him.", "이윽고 유스테이샤는 그가 자기를 손짓해 부르는 것을 알아채고는 다가가 그에게 합류했다."]], [["“It is a serious case,”", "\"심각한 상황이오,\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."]], [["From their position they could hear what was proceeding inside.", "그들이 선 자리에서는 안에서 오가는 말이 들렸다."]], [["“I cannot think where she could have been going,”", "\"어머니가 어디로 가시던 길이었는지 도무지 짐작이 안 갑니다,\""], ["said Clym to someone.", "클림이 누군가에게 말했다."], ["“She had evidently walked a long way, but even when she was able to speak just now she would not tell me where.", "\"분명 먼 길을 걸어오셨는데, 조금 전 말을 할 수 있었을 때조차 어디였는지 말씀해주지 않으셨어요."], ["What do you really think of her?”", "어머니를 정말 어떻게 보십니까?\""]], [["“There is a great deal to fear,”", "\"두려워해야 할 점이 많습니다,\""], ["was gravely answered, in a voice which Eustacia recognized as that of the only surgeon in the district.", "진중하게 답하는 목소리가 들렸는데, 유스테이샤는 그것이 이 지역의 유일한 외과의의 목소리임을 알아챘다."], ["“She has suffered somewhat from the bite of the adder; but it is exhaustion which has overpowered her.", "\"독사에게 물린 것으로도 다소 고통을 겪으셨습니다만, 그분을 쓰러뜨린 것은 탈진입니다."], ["My impression is that her walk must have been exceptionally long.”", "제 인상으로는 그분의 걸음이 유달리 길었던 게 틀림없습니다.\""]], [["“I used to tell her not to overwalk herself this weather,”", "\"이런 날씨엔 무리하게 걷지 마시라고 늘 말씀드렸는데,\""], ["said Clym, with distress.", "클림이 비통하게 말했다."], ["“Do you think we did well in using the adder’s fat?”", "\"독사 기름을 쓴 게 잘한 일이었을까요?\""]], [["“Well, it is a very ancient remedy—the old remedy of the viper-catchers, I believe,”", "\"글쎄요, 그건 아주 오래된 처방입니다—제가 알기로 독사잡이들의 옛 처방이지요,\""], ["replied the doctor.", "의사가 답했다."], ["“It is mentioned as an infallible ointment by Hoffman, Mead, and I think the Abbé Fontana.", "\"호프만과 미드, 그리고 제 생각엔 폰타나 신부도 틀림없이 듣는 연고라고 언급했습니다."], ["Undoubtedly it was as good a thing as you could do; though I question if some other oils would not have been equally efficacious.”", "분명 할 수 있는 것 가운데 그만한 게 없었지요. 다른 기름들도 똑같이 효험이 있지 않았을까 싶긴 합니다만.\""]], [["“Come here, come here!”", "\"이리 와요, 이리 와!\""], ["was then rapidly said in anxious female tones, and Clym and the doctor could be heard rushing forward from the back part of the shed to where Mrs. Yeobright lay.", "그때 다급한 여자의 어조로 빠르게 외치는 소리가 났고, 클림과 의사가 헛간 뒤편에서 요브라이트 부인이 누운 곳으로 달려가는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Oh, what is it?”", "\"아, 무슨 일이지?\""], ["whispered Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 속삭였다."]], [["“’Twas Thomasin who spoke,”", "\"토머신이 말한 거요,\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일디브가 말했다."], ["“Then they have fetched her.", "\"그럼 저들이 그녀를 데려왔군."], ["I wonder if I had better go in—yet it might do harm.”", "내가 들어가는 게 나을지—그래도 해가 될지도 모르겠소.\""]], [["For a long time there was utter silence among the group within; and it was broken at last by Clym saying, in an agonized voice, “O Doctor, what does it mean?”", "오랫동안 안의 무리 사이에는 완전한 정적이 감돌았고, 마침내 클림이 고통에 찬 목소리로 말함으로써 그 정적이 깨졌다. \"오 선생님, 이게 무슨 뜻입니까?\""]], [["The doctor did not reply at once; ultimately he said, “She is sinking fast.", "의사는 곧바로 대답하지 않았으나, 끝내 이렇게 말했다. \"급격히 가라앉고 계십니다."], ["Her heart was previously affected, and physical exhaustion has dealt the finishing blow.”", "심장이 전부터 약했던 데다, 육체의 탈진이 마지막 일격을 가한 겁니다.\""]], [["Then there was a weeping of women, then waiting, then hushed exclamations, then a strange gasping sound, then a painful stillness.", "그러자 여자들의 울음소리가 나더니, 기다림이 이어지고, 이어 숨죽인 탄식, 이어 기이한 헐떡임 소리, 이어 괴로운 정적이 따랐다."]], [["“It is all over,”", "\"다 끝났습니다,\""], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했다."]], [["Further back in the hut the cotters whispered, “Mrs.", "오두막 더 안쪽에서 농막 사람들이 속삭였다."], ["Yeobright is dead.”", "\"요브라이트 부인이 돌아가셨어.\""]], [["Almost at the same moment the two watchers observed the form of a small old-fashioned child entering at the open side of the shed.", "거의 같은 순간 두 사람은 자그마하고 구식 차림을 한 아이 하나가 헛간의 트인 쪽으로 들어서는 모습을 보았다."], ["Susan Nunsuch, whose boy it was, went forward to the opening and silently beckoned to him to go back.", "그 아이의 어미인 수전 넌서치가 입구로 나아가 말없이 손짓하여 돌아가라고 일렀다."]], [["“I’ve got something to tell ’ee, Mother,”", "\"엄마한테 할 말이 있어요,\""], ["he cried in a shrill tone.", "아이가 새된 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“That woman asleep there walked along with me today; and she said I was to say that I had seed her, and she was a broken-hearted woman and cast off by her son, and then I came on home.”", "\"저기 잠든 저 아주머니가 오늘 나하고 같이 걸었는데, 자기를 봤다고 말하랬어요, 자기는 가슴이 찢어진 여자고 아들한테 버림받았다고요, 그러고 나서 나는 집으로 왔어요.\""]], [["A confused sob as from a man was heard within, upon which Eustacia gasped faintly, “That’s Clym—I must go to him—yet dare I do it?", "안에서 어떤 사내가 흐느끼는 듯한 어수선한 오열 소리가 들렸고, 그 소리에 유스테이샤가 희미하게 숨을 들이켜며 말했다. \"저건 클림이야—그에게 가야 해—그래도 감히 그럴 수 있을까?"], ["No—come away!”", "안 돼—가요, 어서!\""]], [["When they had withdrawn from the neighbourhood of the shed she said huskily, “I am to blame for this.", "헛간 근처에서 물러난 뒤 그녀가 잠긴 목소리로 말했다. \"이건 내 탓이에요."], ["There is evil in store for me.”", "나에게 닥칠 악이 도사리고 있어요.\""]], [["“Was she not admitted to your house after all?”", "\"그분이 결국 당신 집에 들지 못했단 말이오?\""], ["Wildeve inquired.", "와일디브가 물었다."]], [["“No, and that’s where it all lies!", "\"네, 바로 거기에 모든 게 걸려 있어요!"], ["Oh, what shall I do!", "오, 나는 어쩌면 좋아!"], ["I shall not intrude upon them—I shall go straight home.", "저들에게 끼어들지 않겠어요—곧장 집으로 갈래요."], ["Damon, good-bye!", "데이먼, 잘 가요!"], ["I cannot speak to you any more now.”", "이제 더는 당신과 말할 수가 없어요.\""]], [["They parted company; and when Eustacia had reached the next hill she looked back.", "두 사람은 헤어졌고, 유스테이샤가 다음 언덕에 다다라 뒤돌아보았다."], ["A melancholy procession was wending its way by the light of the lantern from the hut towards Blooms-End.", "등불 빛에 의지한 한 가닥 애처로운 행렬이 오두막에서 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 길을 더듬어 가고 있었다."], ["Wildeve was nowhere to be seen.", "와일디브의 모습은 어디에도 보이지 않았다."]], [["I. “Wherefore Is Light Given to Him That Is in Misery”", "어느 날 저녁, 요브라이트 부인의 장례식이 끝나고 삼 주쯤 지났을 때, 은빛 달의 얼굴이 한 다발의 빛줄기를 올더워스에 있는 클림의 집 마룻바닥으로 곧장 쏘아 보낼 무렵, 한 여인이 집 안에서 밖으로 나왔다."], ["One evening, about three weeks after the funeral of Mrs. Yeobright, when the silver face of the moon sent a bundle of beams directly upon the floor of Clym’s house at Alderworth, a woman came forth from within.", "그녀는 잠시나마 숨을 돌리려는 듯 정원 문에 기대었다."], ["She reclined over the garden gate as if to refresh herself awhile. The pale lunar touches which make beauties of hags lent divinity to this face, already beautiful.", "노파마저 미인으로 만들어버리는 창백한 달빛의 손길이, 이미 아름다운 이 얼굴에 신성을 더했다."]], [["She had not long been there when a man came up the road and with some hesitation said to her, “How is he tonight, ma’am, if you please?”", "그곳에 나온 지 얼마 되지 않아 한 사내가 길을 올라오더니 다소 머뭇거리며 그녀에게 말했다. \"오늘 밤은 좀 어떠신지요, 마님, 괜찮으시다면 여쭙고 싶습니다만?\""]], [["“He is better, though still very unwell, Humphrey,”", "\"좀 나아졌어요, 험프리, 그래도 여전히 많이 편찮으시고요.\""], ["replied Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 대답했다."]], [["“Is he light-headed, ma’am?”", "\"정신이 오락가락하시나요, 마님?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["He is quite sensible now.”", "지금은 아주 또렷하세요.\""]], [["“Do he rave about his mother just the same, poor fellow?”", "\"여전히 어머님 얘기로 헛소리를 하시나요, 가엾으셔라?\""], ["continued Humphrey.", "험프리가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Just as much, though not quite so wildly,”", "\"여전히 그러시는데, 그래도 그렇게 심하지는 않아요.\""], ["she said in a low voice.", "그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“It was very unfortunate, ma’am, that the boy Johnny should ever ha’ told him his mother’s dying words, about her being broken-hearted and cast off by her son.", "\"정말 안된 일이지요, 마님, 그 어린 조니 녀석이 그 양반한테 어머님 임종 때 하신 말씀을 다 일러바쳤다니 말입니다, 아드님한테 버림받아 가슴이 찢어지셨다는 그 말씀을요."], ["’Twas enough to upset any man alive.”", "살아 있는 사람이라면 누구라도 무너뜨릴 만한 얘기지요.\""]], [["Eustacia made no reply beyond that of a slight catch in her breath, as of one who fain would speak but could not; and Humphrey, declining her invitation to come in, went away.", "유스테이샤는 무어라 말하고 싶지만 그러지 못하는 사람처럼 살짝 숨이 막히는 소리를 낼 뿐 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으며, 들어오라는 그녀의 권유를 마다하고 험프리는 자리를 떴다."]], [["Eustacia turned, entered the house, and ascended to the front bedroom, where a shaded light was burning.", "유스테이샤는 몸을 돌려 집 안으로 들어가 갓을 씌운 등불이 타고 있는 앞쪽 침실로 올라갔다."], ["In the bed lay Clym, pale, haggard, wide awake, tossing to one side and to the other, his eyes lit by a hot light, as if the fire in their pupils were burning up their substance.", "침대에는 창백하고 초췌한 클림이 말똥말똥 깨어 이리저리 몸을 뒤척이고 있었으며, 그의 두 눈은 마치 동공 속의 불꽃이 그 실체를 다 태워버리기라도 하는 듯 뜨거운 빛으로 번득였다."]], [["“Is it you, Eustacia?”", "\"당신이오, 유스테이샤?\""], ["he said as she sat down.", "그녀가 앉자 그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Clym.", "\"네, 클림."], ["I have been down to the gate.", "문간에 내려갔다 왔어요."], ["The moon is shining beautifully, and there is not a leaf stirring.”", "달이 아름답게 비치고 있고 나뭇잎 하나 흔들리지 않아요.\""]], [["“Shining, is it?", "\"비친다고?"], ["What’s the moon to a man like me?", "나 같은 사람한테 달이 다 무슨 소용이오?"], ["Let it shine—let anything be, so that I never see another day!...", "비추든 말든—무엇이든 다 있으라지, 내가 또 하루를 보지 않게만 된다면!…"], ["Eustacia, I don’t know where to look—my thoughts go through me like swords.", "유스테이샤, 어디를 봐야 할지 모르겠소—생각들이 칼처럼 나를 꿰뚫고 지나가오."], ["O, if any man wants to make himself immortal by painting a picture of wretchedness, let him come here!”", "오, 비참함을 그린 그림으로 불멸을 얻고 싶은 자가 있거든 여기로 오라지!\""]], [["“Why do you say so?”", "\"왜 그런 말을 하세요?\""]], [["“I cannot help feeling that I did my best to kill her.”", "\"내가 어머니를 죽이려고 최선을 다했다는 느낌을 떨칠 수가 없소.\""]], [["“No, Clym.”", "\"아니에요, 클림.\""]], [["“Yes, it was so; it is useless to excuse me!", "\"아니, 그랬소. 나를 변명해봐야 소용없소!"], ["My conduct to her was too hideous—I made no advances; and she could not bring herself to forgive me.", "어머니께 한 내 행동은 너무도 끔찍했소—나는 먼저 다가가지 않았고, 어머니는 차마 나를 용서하지 못하셨소."], ["Now she is dead!", "그런데 이제 돌아가셨소!"], ["If I had only shown myself willing to make it up with her sooner, and we had been friends, and then she had died, it wouldn’t be so hard to bear.", "좀 더 일찍 화해할 뜻을 내비치기만 했더라도, 우리가 친구처럼 지냈더라면, 그러고 나서 어머니가 돌아가셨더라면, 이토록 견디기 힘들지는 않았을 텐데."], ["But I never went near her house, so she never came near mine, and didn’t know how welcome she would have been—that’s what troubles me.", "그런데 나는 한 번도 어머니 집 근처에 가지 않았고, 그래서 어머니도 내 집 근처에 오지 않으셨고, 와도 얼마나 반겼을지 모르신 채 돌아가셨소—그게 나를 괴롭히는 거요."], ["She did not know I was going to her house that very night, for she was too insensible to understand me.", "바로 그날 밤 내가 어머니 집으로 가려 했다는 걸 어머니는 모르셨소, 너무 정신을 잃으셔서 내 말을 알아듣지 못하셨으니까."], ["If she had only come to see me!", "어머니가 나를 보러 오기만 하셨더라면!"], ["I longed that she would.", "나는 그러시기를 간절히 바랐소."], ["But it was not to be.”", "하지만 그렇게 될 운명이 아니었소.\""]], [["There escaped from Eustacia one of those shivering sighs which used to shake her like a pestilent blast.", "유스테이샤에게서 예전에 그녀를 역병 같은 돌풍처럼 뒤흔들곤 하던 그 떨리는 한숨이 새어 나왔다."], ["She had not yet told.", "그녀는 아직 말하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["But Yeobright was too deeply absorbed in the ramblings incidental to his remorseful state to notice her.", "하지만 요브라이트는 회한에 사로잡힌 자신의 상태에서 비롯된 두서없는 넋두리에 너무도 깊이 빠져 있어 그녀를 알아채지 못했다."], ["During his illness he had been continually talking thus.", "앓는 동안 그는 줄곧 이렇게 떠들어댔다."], ["Despair had been added to his original grief by the unfortunate disclosure of the boy who had received the last words of Mrs. Yeobright—words too bitterly uttered in an hour of misapprehension.", "오해의 시간에 너무도 쓰라리게 내뱉은, 요브라이트 부인의 마지막 말을 전해 들은 소년의 불운한 폭로 때문에 그의 본래의 슬픔에 절망이 더해졌던 것이다."], ["Then his distress had overwhelmed him, and he longed for death as a field labourer longs for the shade.", "그때 비탄이 그를 압도했고, 그는 들일하는 일꾼이 그늘을 갈망하듯 죽음을 갈망했다."], ["It was the pitiful sight of a man standing in the very focus of sorrow.", "슬픔의 한가운데 초점에 서 있는 한 사내의 가련한 광경이었다."], ["He continually bewailed his tardy journey to his mother’s house, because it was an error which could never be rectified, and insisted that he must have been horribly perverted by some fiend not to have thought before that it was his duty to go to her, since she did not come to him.", "그는 어머니의 집으로 너무 늦게 떠난 것을 끊임없이 한탄했으니, 그것은 결코 바로잡을 수 없는 잘못이었기 때문이요, 어머니가 자기에게 오지 않으니 자기가 어머니에게 가는 것이 도리임을 진작 생각하지 못하다니 어떤 악마에게 무섭게 홀린 게 틀림없다고 우겼다."], ["He would ask Eustacia to agree with him in his self-condemnation; and when she, seared inwardly by a secret she dared not tell, declared that she could not give an opinion, he would say, “That’s because you didn’t know my mother’s nature.", "그는 유스테이샤에게 자신을 향한 자책에 동의해달라고 청하곤 했으며, 차마 말할 수 없는 비밀에 속이 타들어가던 그녀가 자기는 의견을 말할 수 없다고 하면, 그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"그건 당신이 우리 어머니의 성품을 몰라서 그러오."], ["She was always ready to forgive if asked to do so; but I seemed to her to be as an obstinate child, and that made her unyielding.", "어머니는 부탁만 하면 언제든 용서할 분이셨소. 하지만 어머니가 보시기에 나는 고집 센 아이 같았고, 그래서 어머니가 굽히지 않으셨던 거요."], ["Yet not unyielding—she was proud and reserved, no more....", "그렇다고 완고한 분은 아니셨소—자존심이 강하고 말수가 적으셨을 뿐, 그 이상은 아니었소…."], ["Yes, I can understand why she held out against me so long.", "그래, 어머니가 왜 그토록 오래 나를 거부하셨는지 이해할 수 있소."], ["She was waiting for me.", "어머니는 나를 기다리고 계셨던 거요."], ["I dare say she said a hundred times in her sorrow, ‘What a return he makes for all the sacrifices I have made for him!’ I never went to her!", "슬픔 속에서 백 번도 더 이렇게 말씀하셨을 거요. '내가 그 애를 위해 바친 그 모든 희생에 그 애는 이렇게 보답하는구나!'"], ["When I set out to visit her it was too late.", "나는 어머니께 한 번도 가지 않았소! 어머니를 뵈러 나섰을 때는 이미 너무 늦었소."], ["To think of that is nearly intolerable!”", "그걸 생각하면 거의 견딜 수가 없소!\""]], [["Sometimes his condition had been one of utter remorse, unsoftened by a single tear of pure sorrow: and then he writhed as he lay, fevered far more by thought than by physical ills.", "때로 그의 상태는, 순수한 슬픔의 눈물 한 방울로도 누그러지지 않는 완전한 회한에 빠진 것이었으니, 그럴 때면 그는 누운 채 몸부림쳤으며, 육체의 병보다 생각으로 인해 훨씬 더 심하게 달아올랐다."], ["“If I could only get one assurance that she did not die in a belief that I was resentful,”", "\"어머니가 내가 원망한다는 믿음 속에서 돌아가신 게 아니라는 확신 하나만 얻을 수 있다면,\""], ["he said one day when in this mood, “it would be better to think of than a hope of heaven.", "어느 날 이런 기분에 잠겨 그가 말했다. \"그건 천국의 희망보다도 더 생각하기 좋은 일일 거요."], ["But that I cannot do.”", "하지만 나는 그걸 할 수가 없소.\""]], [["“You give yourself up too much to this wearying despair,”", "\"당신은 이 지치게 하는 절망에 너무 빠져들고 있어요.\""], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 말했다."], ["“Other men’s mothers have died.”", "\"다른 사람들의 어머니도 돌아가셨잖아요.\""]], [["“That doesn’t make the loss of mine less.", "\"그렇다고 내 어머니를 잃은 게 덜한 일이 되는 건 아니오."], ["Yet it is less the loss than the circumstances of the loss.", "하지만 이건 잃음 자체보다도 그 잃음의 정황이 문제요."], ["I sinned against her, and on that account there is no light for me.”", "나는 어머니께 죄를 지었고, 그래서 내게는 빛이 없소.\""]], [["“She sinned against you, I think.”", "\"제 생각엔 어머님이 당신께 죄를 지으신 거예요.\""]], [["“No, she did not.", "\"아니, 그렇지 않소."], ["I committed the guilt; and may the whole burden be upon my head!”", "죄를 지은 건 나요, 그 모든 짐을 내 머리 위에 지울지어다!\""]], [["“I think you might consider twice before you say that,”", "\"그 말씀 하시기 전에 두 번은 생각해보셔야 할 것 같아요.\""], ["Eustacia replied.", "유스테이샤가 대답했다."], ["“Single men have, no doubt, a right to curse themselves as much as they please; but men with wives involve two in the doom they pray down.”", "\"독신 남자야 마음껏 자기 자신을 저주할 권리가 있겠지요, 의심할 바 없이. 하지만 아내가 있는 남자는 자기가 빌어 내리는 그 천벌에 둘을 끌어들이는 거예요.\""]], [["“I am in too sorry a state to understand what you are refining on,”", "\"당신이 무슨 궤변을 늘어놓는지 알아들을 만큼 내 처지가 한가하지가 않소.\""], ["said the wretched man.", "그 가련한 사내가 말했다."], ["“Day and night shout at me, ‘You have helped to kill her.’ But in loathing myself I may, I own, be unjust to you, my poor wife.", "\"낮이고 밤이고 내게 소리치오, '너는 어머니를 죽이는 데 한몫했다.' 하지만 나 자신을 혐오하다 보면, 인정하건대, 가엾은 내 아내인 당신에게 부당하게 굴 수도 있겠지."], ["Forgive me for it, Eustacia, for I scarcely know what I do.”", "그건 용서해주오, 유스테이샤, 나는 내가 무얼 하는지도 거의 모르오.\""]], [["Eustacia was always anxious to avoid the sight of her husband in such a state as this, which had become as dreadful to her as the trial scene was to Judas Iscariot.", "유스테이샤는 이런 상태에 빠진 남편을 보는 것을 늘 피하고 싶어했으니, 그것은 가룟 유다에게 심판의 장면이 그러했던 것만큼이나 그녀에게 끔찍한 일이 되어 있었다."], ["It brought before her eyes the spectre of a worn-out woman knocking at a door which she would not open; and she shrank from contemplating it.", "그것은 열어주지 않을 문을 두드리는, 기진맥진한 여인의 유령을 그녀의 눈앞에 불러왔으며, 그녀는 그것을 떠올리기만 해도 몸을 움츠렸다."], ["Yet it was better for Yeobright himself when he spoke openly of his sharp regret, for in silence he endured infinitely more, and would sometimes remain so long in a tense, brooding mood, consuming himself by the gnawing of his thought, that it was imperatively necessary to make him talk aloud, that his grief might in some degree expend itself in the effort.", "그렇지만 요브라이트 자신에게는 그가 자신의 날카로운 회한을 거리낌없이 말하는 편이 더 나았으니, 침묵 속에서는 그가 한없이 더 많은 것을 견뎌야 했기 때문이요, 때로는 너무도 오랫동안 팽팽하고 침울한 기분에 잠겨 자기 생각을 갉아먹으며 자신을 소진시켰던 까닭에, 그의 슬픔이 그 노력 속에서 어느 정도라도 발산되도록 그를 소리 내어 말하게 만드는 것이 반드시 필요했던 것이다."]], [["Eustacia had not been long indoors after her look at the moonlight when a soft footstep came up to the house, and Thomasin was announced by the woman downstairs.", "달빛을 바라본 뒤 유스테이샤가 집 안에 들어온 지 얼마 되지 않아 부드러운 발소리가 집으로 다가왔고, 아래층의 여자가 토머신이 왔음을 알렸다."]], [["“Ah, Thomasin!", "\"아, 토머신!"], ["Thank you for coming tonight,”", "오늘 밤 와줘서 고마워.\""], ["said Clym when she entered the room.", "토머신이 방에 들어서자 클림이 말했다."], ["“Here am I, you see.", "\"보다시피 내 꼴이 이렇소."], ["Such a wretched spectacle am I, that I shrink from being seen by a single friend, and almost from you.”", "워낙 비참한 몰골이라 친구 하나에게도 보이기가 움츠러들고, 당신한테조차 거의 그렇소.\""]], [["“You must not shrink from me, dear Clym,”", "\"나한테 움츠러들면 안 돼요, 클림.\""], ["said Thomasin earnestly, in that sweet voice of hers which came to a sufferer like fresh air into a Black Hole.", "토머신이 진심을 담아, 흑굴 속으로 흘러드는 신선한 공기처럼 고통받는 이에게 다가오는 그 다정한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Nothing in you can ever shock me or drive me away.", "\"당신의 그 무엇도 결코 나를 놀라게 하거나 내쫓을 수 없어요."], ["I have been here before, but you don’t remember it.”", "전에도 여기 왔었는데 기억 못 하시는 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes, I do; I am not delirious, Thomasin, nor have I been so at all.", "\"아니, 기억하오. 나는 정신 착란이 아니오, 토머신, 한 번도 그런 적 없었소."], ["Don’t you believe that if they say so.", "누가 그렇게 말하더라도 믿지 마시오."], ["I am only in great misery at what I have done, and that, with the weakness, makes me seem mad.", "나는 그저 내가 저지른 일 때문에 큰 비탄에 잠겨 있을 뿐인데, 그게 이 쇠약함과 더해져서 내가 미친 사람처럼 보이게 하는 거요."], ["But it has not upset my reason.", "하지만 그게 내 이성을 흩뜨려놓지는 않았소."], ["Do you think I should remember all about my mother’s death if I were out of my mind?", "내가 정신이 나갔다면 어머니의 죽음에 관한 모든 걸 다 기억할 것 같소?"], ["No such good luck.", "그런 다행은 없소."], ["Two months and a half, Thomasin, the last of her life, did my poor mother live alone, distracted and mourning because of me; yet she was unvisited by me, though I was living only six miles off.", "두 달 반 동안, 토머신, 생애 마지막 두 달 반을 우리 가엾은 어머니는 홀로, 나 때문에 미칠 듯이 슬퍼하며 사셨소. 그런데도 겨우 육 마일 떨어진 곳에 살던 나는 한 번도 찾아가지 않았소."], ["Two months and a half—seventy-five days did the sun rise and set upon her in that deserted state which a dog didn’t deserve!", "두 달 반—이레레 닷새 동안 해가 떴다 졌는데, 그 버림받은 처지는 개도 받을 만한 게 아니었소!"], ["Poor people who had nothing in common with her would have cared for her, and visited her had they known her sickness and loneliness; but I, who should have been all to her, stayed away like a cur.", "어머니와 아무런 인연도 없는 가난한 사람들이라도 어머니의 병과 외로움을 알았더라면 보살피고 찾아갔을 거요. 그런데 어머니께 모든 것이 되어드려야 했던 나는 똥개처럼 멀찍이 떨어져 있었소."], ["If there is any justice in God let Him kill me now.", "하느님께 정의가 있다면 지금 나를 죽이소서."], ["He has nearly blinded me, but that is not enough.", "그분은 내 눈을 거의 멀게 하셨소, 하지만 그것으로는 부족하오."], ["If He would only strike me with more pain I would believe in Him forever!”", "그분이 내게 더 큰 고통을 내리시기만 한다면 나는 영원히 그분을 믿으리다!\""]], [["“Hush, hush!", "\"쉿, 쉿!"], ["O, pray, Clym, don’t, don’t say it!”", "오, 제발, 클림, 그러지, 그런 말 하지 마요!\""], ["implored Thomasin, affrighted into sobs and tears; while Eustacia, at the other side of the room, though her pale face remained calm, writhed in her chair.", "토머신이 흐느낌과 눈물에 사로잡혀 애원했으며, 방 저편의 유스테이샤는 창백한 얼굴은 평온함을 유지했으나 의자에 앉은 채 몸부림쳤다."], ["Clym went on without heeding his cousin.", "클림은 사촌은 아랑곳하지 않고 말을 이어갔다."]], [["“But I am not worth receiving further proof even of Heaven’s reprobation.", "\"하지만 나는 하늘의 단죄를 더 입증받을 자격조차 없소."], ["Do you think, Thomasin, that she knew me—that she did not die in that horrid mistaken notion about my not forgiving her, which I can’t tell you how she acquired?", "토머신, 당신 생각엔 어머니가 나를 알아보셨을 것 같소—내가 어머니를 용서하지 않는다는 그 끔찍한 잘못된 생각, 어떻게 그런 생각을 품으셨는지 도무지 말할 수 없는 그 생각 속에서 돌아가신 게 아닐 것 같소?"], ["If you could only assure me of that!", "그것만이라도 확신시켜준다면!"], ["Do you think so, Eustacia?", "그렇게 생각하오, 유스테이샤?"], ["Do speak to me.”", "어디 내게 말 좀 해보오.\""]], [["“I think I can assure you that she knew better at last,”", "\"제 생각엔 어머님이 마지막엔 더 잘 아셨다고 장담할 수 있어요.\""], ["said Thomasin.", "토머신이 말했다."], ["The pallid Eustacia said nothing.", "창백한 유스테이샤는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Why didn’t she come to my house?", "\"어머니는 왜 내 집에 오지 않으셨을까?"], ["I would have taken her in and showed her how I loved her in spite of all.", "나는 어머니를 받아들이고, 그 모든 일에도 불구하고 내가 얼마나 어머니를 사랑하는지 보여드렸을 텐데."], ["But she never came; and I didn’t go to her, and she died on the heath like an animal kicked out, nobody to help her till it was too late.", "그런데 어머니는 끝내 오지 않으셨고, 나도 어머니께 가지 않았고, 어머니는 발길질당해 쫓겨난 짐승처럼 황야에서 돌아가셨소, 너무 늦을 때까지 도와줄 이 하나 없이."], ["If you could have seen her, Thomasin, as I saw her—a poor dying woman, lying in the dark upon the bare ground, moaning, nobody near, believing she was utterly deserted by all the world, it would have moved you to anguish, it would have moved a brute.", "토머신, 당신이 내가 본 대로 어머니를 보았더라면—맨땅 위 어둠 속에 누워 신음하는, 곁에 아무도 없이, 온 세상에게서 완전히 버림받았다고 믿는, 죽어가는 가엾은 여인을 보았더라면, 당신은 비통함에 사로잡혔을 거요, 짐승이라도 마음이 움직였을 거요."], ["And this poor woman my mother!", "그런데 이 가엾은 여인이 내 어머니라니!"], ["No wonder she said to the child, ‘You have seen a broken-hearted woman.’ What a state she must have been brought to, to say that!", "어머니가 그 아이에게 '너는 가슴이 찢어진 여인을 보았다'라고 하신 것도 무리가 아니지. 그런 말을 하기까지 어머니가 어떤 지경에 이르셨겠소!"], ["and who can have done it but I?", "그렇게 만든 게 나 말고 누구겠소?"], ["It is too dreadful to think of, and I wish I could be punished more heavily than I am.", "너무 끔찍해서 생각조차 할 수 없으니, 지금보다 더 무겁게 벌받을 수 있다면 좋겠소."], ["How long was I what they called out of my senses?”", "내가 소위 정신이 나가 있었다는 게 얼마나 됐소?\""]], [["“A week, I think.”", "\"일주일쯤이었던 것 같아요.\""]], [["“And then I became calm.”", "\"그러고 나서 내가 차분해졌고.\""]], [["“Yes, for four days.”", "\"네, 나흘 동안요.\""]], [["“And now I have left off being calm.”", "\"그러더니 이젠 더는 차분하지 못하게 됐군.\""]], [["“But try to be quiet—please do, and you will soon be strong.", "\"하지만 조용히 있으려고 애써봐요—제발 그래요, 그러면 곧 기운을 차릴 거예요."], ["If you could remove that impression from your mind—”", "그 생각만 마음에서 떨쳐낼 수 있다면—\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["he said impatiently.", "그가 조바심을 내며 말했다."], ["“But I don’t want to get strong.", "\"하지만 나는 기운 차리고 싶지 않소."], ["What’s the use of my getting well?", "내가 낫는 게 다 무슨 소용이오?"], ["It would be better for me if I die, and it would certainly be better for Eustacia.", "죽는 편이 내겐 더 나을 거고, 유스테이샤한테도 분명 더 나을 거요."], ["Is Eustacia there?”", "유스테이샤 거기 있소?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“It would be better for you, Eustacia, if I were to die?”", "\"내가 죽으면 당신한테 더 나을 거요, 유스테이샤?\""]], [["“Don’t press such a question, dear Clym.”", "\"그런 걸 캐묻지 말아요, 클림.\""]], [["“Well, it really is but a shadowy supposition; for unfortunately I am going to live.", "\"뭐, 사실 그저 허망한 가정일 뿐이지, 안타깝게도 나는 살아갈 테니까."], ["I feel myself getting better.", "내가 차도가 있는 게 느껴지오."], ["Thomasin, how long are you going to stay at the inn, now that all this money has come to your husband?”", "토머신, 이번에 그 많은 돈이 당신 남편한테 들어왔으니, 여관에는 얼마나 더 머물 셈이오?\""]], [["“Another month or two, probably; until my illness is over.", "\"아마 한두 달 더요, 제 병이 나을 때까지요."], ["We cannot get off till then.", "그전엔 떠날 수가 없어요."], ["I think it will be a month or more.”", "한 달이나 그 이상 걸릴 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"그래, 그래."], ["Of course.", "그렇겠지."], ["Ah, Cousin Tamsie, you will get over your trouble—one little month will take you through it, and bring something to console you; but I shall never get over mine, and no consolation will come!”", "아, 사촌 탬지, 당신은 슬픔을 이겨낼 거요—한 달이면 그걸 헤쳐나가 위안이 될 무언가를 얻게 되겠지. 하지만 나는 결코 내 슬픔을 이겨내지 못할 거고, 어떤 위안도 오지 않을 거요!\""]], [["“Clym, you are unjust to yourself.", "\"클림, 당신은 스스로에게 부당해요."], ["Depend upon it, Aunt thought kindly of you.", "정말이지 숙모님은 당신을 다정하게 생각하셨어요."], ["I know that, if she had lived, you would have been reconciled with her.”", "살아 계셨더라면 당신과 화해하셨을 거예요, 저는 알아요.\""]], [["“But she didn’t come to see me, though I asked her, before I married, if she would come.", "\"하지만 어머니는 나를 보러 오지 않으셨소, 내가 결혼하기 전에 와주시겠냐고 청했는데도 말이오."], ["Had she come, or had I gone there, she would never have died saying, ‘I am a broken-hearted woman, cast off by my son.’ My door has always been open to her—a welcome here has always awaited her.", "어머니가 오셨거나 내가 거기 갔더라면, 어머니가 '나는 가슴이 찢어진 여인이다, 아들에게 버림받았다'라고 하시며 돌아가시지는 않았을 거요. 어머니께 내 문은 늘 열려 있었소—여기서는 늘 환대가 어머니를 기다리고 있었소."], ["But that she never came to see.”", "그런데 어머니는 끝내 그것을 보러 오지 않으셨소.\""]], [["“You had better not talk any more now, Clym,”", "\"이제 그만 말씀하시는 게 좋겠어요, 클림.\""], ["said Eustacia faintly from the other part of the room, for the scene was growing intolerable to her.", "방 저편에서 유스테이샤가 희미하게 말했으니, 그 장면이 그녀로서는 견딜 수 없어지고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Let me talk to you instead for the little time I shall be here,”", "\"내가 여기 머무는 짧은 동안엔 대신 제가 얘기할게요.\""], ["Thomasin said soothingly.", "토머신이 달래듯 말했다."], ["“Consider what a one-sided way you have of looking at the matter, Clym.", "\"당신이 이 일을 얼마나 한쪽으로 치우치게 보고 있는지 생각해봐요, 클림."], ["When she said that to the little boy you had not found her and taken her into your arms; and it might have been uttered in a moment of bitterness.", "숙모님이 그 어린 소년에게 그 말을 하셨을 때 당신은 아직 숙모님을 찾아내 품에 안아드리지 못했어요. 그건 비통함의 한순간에 내뱉은 말일지도 몰라요."], ["It was rather like Aunt to say things in haste.", "숙모님은 좀 다급하게 말씀을 하시는 편이었어요."], ["She sometimes used to speak so to me.", "제게도 가끔 그렇게 말씀하시곤 했어요."], ["Though she did not come I am convinced that she thought of coming to see you.", "비록 오시지는 않았어도, 저는 숙모님이 당신을 보러 올 생각을 하셨다고 확신해요."], ["Do you suppose a man’s mother could live two or three months without one forgiving thought?", "어머니가 두세 달을 단 한 번의 용서하는 마음도 없이 사실 수 있다고 생각하세요?"], ["She forgave me; and why should she not have forgiven you?”", "숙모님은 저를 용서하셨어요, 그러니 당신을 용서하지 않으셨을 리가요?\""]], [["“You laboured to win her round; I did nothing.", "\"당신은 어머니의 마음을 돌리려고 애썼지만, 나는 아무것도 하지 않았소."], ["I, who was going to teach people the higher secrets of happiness, did not know how to keep out of that gross misery which the most untaught are wise enough to avoid.”", "사람들에게 행복의 더 높은 비밀을 가르치려던 내가, 가장 못 배운 자들도 피할 만큼은 지혜로운 그 천박한 비참함에서 벗어나는 법조차 몰랐던 거요.\""]], [["“How did you get here tonight, Thomasin?”", "\"오늘 밤은 어떻게 여기 왔어요, 토머신?\""], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 말했다."]], [["“Damon set me down at the end of the lane.", "\"데이먼이 길 끝에서 내려줬어요."], ["He has driven into East Egdon on business, and he will come and pick me up by-and-by.”", "일이 있어 이스트 에그던으로 마차를 몰고 갔는데, 이따가 와서 저를 태워 갈 거예요.\""]], [["Accordingly they soon after heard the noise of wheels.", "과연 그 직후에 그들은 바퀴 소리를 들었다."], ["Wildeve had come, and was waiting outside with his horse and gig.", "와일디브가 와서 말과 이륜마차를 데리고 밖에서 기다리고 있었다."]], [["“Send out and tell him I will be down in two minutes,”", "\"제가 이 분 안에 내려가겠다고 사람을 내보내 전해주세요.\""], ["said Thomasin.", "토머신이 말했다."]], [["“I will run down myself,”", "\"제가 직접 내려갈게요.\""], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 말했다."]], [["She went down.", "그녀가 내려갔다."], ["Wildeve had alighted, and was standing before the horse’s head when Eustacia opened the door.", "와일디브는 마차에서 내려 말 머리 앞에 서 있었는데, 유스테이샤가 문을 열었다."], ["He did not turn for a moment, thinking the comer Thomasin.", "그는 잠시 돌아보지 않았으니, 온 사람이 토머신이라고 여겼던 것이다."], ["Then he looked, startled ever so little, and said one word: “Well?”", "그러더니 그가 돌아보며 아주 조금 놀란 기색으로 한마디를 했다. \"어떻게 됐소?\""]], [["“I have not yet told him,”", "\"아직 그이한테 말 안 했어요.\""], ["she replied in a whisper.", "그녀가 속삭이듯 대답했다."]], [["“Then don’t do so till he is well—it will be fatal.", "\"그럼 그가 나을 때까지는 하지 마시오—치명적일 거요."], ["You are ill yourself.”", "당신도 편치 않아 보이오.\""]], [["“I am wretched....", "\"비참해요…."], ["O Damon,”", "오, 데이먼.\""], ["she said, bursting into tears, “I—I can’t tell you how unhappy I am!", "그녀가 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"내가—내가 얼마나 불행한지 당신한테 말로 다 할 수가 없어요!"], ["I can hardly bear this.", "이걸 도저히 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["I can tell nobody of my trouble—nobody knows of it but you.”", "내 괴로움을 아무한테도 말할 수 없어요—그걸 아는 사람은 당신밖에 없어요.\""]], [["“Poor girl!”", "\"가엾은 사람!\""], ["said Wildeve, visibly affected at her distress, and at last led on so far as to take her hand.", "와일디브가 그녀의 비탄에 눈에 띄게 마음이 움직여 말했으며, 마침내 그녀의 손을 잡기에 이를 만큼 이끌려 들어갔다."], ["“It is hard, when you have done nothing to deserve it, that you should have got involved in such a web as this.", "\"당신이 그럴 만한 짓을 아무것도 하지 않았는데 이런 거미줄 같은 일에 얽혀들다니 가혹한 일이오."], ["You were not made for these sad scenes.", "당신은 이런 슬픈 장면에 어울리게 태어난 사람이 아니오."], ["I am to blame most.", "누구보다 내 잘못이 크오."], ["If I could only have saved you from it all!”", "이 모든 일에서 당신을 구해낼 수만 있었더라면!\""]], [["“But, Damon, please pray tell me what I must do?", "\"하지만, 데이먼, 제발 제가 어떻게 해야 할지 말해줘요."], ["To sit by him hour after hour, and hear him reproach himself as being the cause of her death, and to know that I am the sinner, if any human being is at all, drives me into cold despair.", "그이 곁에 몇 시간이고 앉아 그이가 어머니의 죽음의 원인이 자기라고 자책하는 걸 듣고 있노라면, 그리고 사람이 조금이라도 죄인이라면 그게 바로 나라는 걸 알고 있노라면, 차디찬 절망 속으로 내몰려요."], ["I don’t know what to do.", "어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["Should I tell him or should I not tell him?", "그이한테 말해야 할까요, 말하지 말아야 할까요?"], ["I always am asking myself that.", "늘 스스로에게 그걸 묻고 있어요."], ["O, I want to tell him; and yet I am afraid.", "오, 말하고 싶어요, 그런데도 두려워요."], ["If he finds it out he must surely kill me, for nothing else will be in proportion to his feelings now.", "그이가 알게 되면 분명 나를 죽일 거예요, 지금 그이의 감정에 걸맞은 건 다른 그 무엇도 아닐 테니까요."], ["‘Beware the fury of a patient man’ sounds day by day in my ears as I watch him.”", "'참는 사람의 분노를 조심하라'라는 말이, 그이를 지켜보는 내 귀에 날마다 울려요.\""]], [["“Well, wait till he is better, and trust to chance.", "\"음, 그가 나아질 때까지 기다렸다가 운에 맡기시오."], ["And when you tell, you must only tell part—for his own sake.”", "그리고 말할 때는 일부만 말해야 하오—그를 위해서라도.\""]], [["“Which part should I keep back?”", "\"어느 부분을 숨겨야 하죠?\""]], [["Wildeve paused.", "와일디브가 잠시 머뭇거렸다."], ["“That I was in the house at the time,”", "\"그때 내가 그 집 안에 있었다는 것 말이오.\""], ["he said in a low tone.", "그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Yes; it must be concealed, seeing what has been whispered.", "\"그래요, 그건 숨겨야겠지요, 사람들이 수군거린 걸 생각하면."], ["How much easier are hasty actions than speeches that will excuse them!”", "성급한 행동이 그것을 변명할 말보다 얼마나 더 쉬운지!\""]], [["“If he were only to die—”", "\"그가 죽기만 한다면—\""], ["Wildeve murmured.", "와일디브가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Do not think of it!", "\"그런 생각 마요!"], ["I would not buy hope of immunity by so cowardly a desire even if I hated him.", "설령 내가 그이를 미워한다 해도, 그토록 비겁한 바람으로 화를 면할 희망을 사들이지는 않을 거예요."], ["Now I am going up to him again.", "이제 그이한테 다시 올라갈게요."], ["Thomasin bade me tell you she would be down in a few minutes.", "토머신이 몇 분 안에 내려간다고 전해달라고 했어요."], ["Good-bye.”", "안녕히.\""]], [["She returned, and Thomasin soon appeared.", "그녀가 돌아갔고, 곧 토머신이 나타났다."], ["When she was seated in the gig with her husband, and the horse was turning to go off, Wildeve lifted his eyes to the bedroom windows.", "그녀가 남편과 함께 이륜마차에 앉고 말이 돌아서 떠나려 할 때, 와일디브가 침실 창문 쪽으로 눈을 들었다."], ["Looking from one of them he could discern a pale, tragic face watching him drive away.", "그중 한 창에서 자기가 마차를 몰고 떠나는 것을 지켜보는 창백하고 비극적인 얼굴을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["It was Eustacia’s.", "유스테이샤의 얼굴이었다."]], [["II. A Lurid Light Breaks in upon a Darkened Understanding\nClym’s grief became mitigated by wearing itself out.", "클림의 슬픔은 스스로 소진되어가며 누그러졌다."], ["His strength returned, and a month after the visit of Thomasin he might have been seen walking about the garden.", "기운이 돌아왔고, 토머신이 다녀간 지 한 달 후에는 그가 정원을 거니는 모습을 볼 수 있게 되었다."], ["Endurance and despair, equanimity and gloom, the tints of health and the pallor of death, mingled weirdly in his face.", "인내와 절망, 평정과 우수, 건강의 빛깔과 죽음의 창백함이 그의 얼굴 위에 기이하게 뒤섞여 있었다."], ["He was now unnaturally silent upon all of the past that related to his mother; and though Eustacia knew that he was thinking of it none the less, she was only too glad to escape the topic ever to bring it up anew.", "그는 이제 어머니와 관련된 과거의 모든 일에 대해 부자연스러우리만치 입을 다물었으며, 유스테이샤는 그가 여전히 그것을 생각하고 있음을 알면서도 그 화제에서 벗어날 수 있어 너무도 다행스러운 나머지 그것을 새삼 다시 꺼내지 않았다."], ["When his mind had been weaker his heart had led him to speak out; but reason having now somewhat recovered itself he sank into taciturnity.", "정신이 더 약했을 때는 마음이 그를 털어놓게 했으나, 이제 이성이 다소 회복되자 그는 과묵 속으로 가라앉았다."]], [["One evening when he was thus standing in the garden, abstractedly spudding up a weed with his stick, a bony figure turned the corner of the house and came up to him.", "어느 날 저녁 그가 이렇게 정원에 서서, 지팡이로 잡초를 멍하니 파헤치고 있을 때, 뼈만 앙상한 한 형체가 집 모퉁이를 돌아 그에게 다가왔다."]], [["“Christian, isn’t it?”", "\"크리스천, 자넨가?\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."], ["“I am glad you have found me out.", "\"나를 찾아와줘서 반갑네."], ["I shall soon want you to go to Blooms-End and assist me in putting the house in order.", "곧 자네한테 블룸스엔드에 가서 집 정리를 도와달라고 부탁할 참이었네."], ["I suppose it is all locked up as I left it?”", "내가 떠날 때 그대로 다 잠가둔 채겠지?\""]], [["“Yes, Mister Clym.”", "\"네, 클림 도련님.\""]], [["“Have you dug up the potatoes and other roots?”", "\"감자랑 다른 뿌리들은 캐냈고?\""]], [["“Yes, without a drop o’ rain, thank God. But I was coming to tell ’ee of something else which is quite different from what we have lately had in the family.", "\"네, 비 한 방울 안 맞히고요, 다행스럽게도요."], ["I am sent by the rich gentleman at the Woman, that we used to call the landlord, to tell ’ee that Mrs.", "그런데 그것 말고 다른 걸 말씀드리러 온 거예요, 요새 우리 집안에 있었던 일하고는 영 다른 일이지요."], ["Wildeve is doing well of a girl, which was born punctually at one o’clock at noon, or a few minutes more or less; and ’tis said that expecting of this increase is what have kept ’em there since they came into their money.”", "우리가 여관 주인이라고 부르던 우먼의 그 부자 양반이 절 보내서 전하라고 했는데요, 와일디브 부인이 딸을 무사히 낳으셨대요, 정오 한 시 정각에, 아니면 몇 분 더 되거나 덜 되거나 해서 태어났다는데요. 그분들이 돈이 들어온 뒤로 거기 머물렀던 게 바로 이 식구 늘 일을 기다려서라는 얘기예요.\""]], [["“And she is getting on well, you say?”", "\"그래서 부인은 잘 회복하고 계신다는 거지?\""]], [["“Yes, sir. Only Mr.", "\"네, 도련님."], ["Wildeve is twanky because ’tisn’t a boy—that’s what they say in the kitchen, but I was not supposed to notice that.”", "다만 와일디브 씨가 아들이 아니라서 좀 시무룩하시다지요—부엌에서들 하는 말이긴 한데, 제가 그걸 알아챘다고는 안 하기로 했어요.\""]], [["“Christian, now listen to me.”", "\"크리스천, 이제 내 말 들어보게.\""]], [["“Yes, sure, Mr. Yeobright.”", "\"네, 그럼요, 요브라이트 도련님.\""]], [["“Did you see my mother the day before she died?”", "\"우리 어머니 돌아가시기 전날 우리 어머니를 뵈었나?\""]], [["“No, I did not.”", "\"아뇨, 못 뵈었어요.\""]], [["Yeobright’s face expressed disappointment.", "요브라이트의 얼굴에 실망이 드러났다."]], [["“But I zeed her the morning of the same day she died.”", "\"하지만 돌아가시던 바로 그날 아침엔 뵈었어요.\""]], [["Clym’s look lighted up.", "클림의 표정이 환해졌다."], ["“That’s nearer still to my meaning,”", "\"그게 내 말뜻에 더 가깝군.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I know ’twas the same day; for she said, ‘I be going to see him, Christian; so I shall not want any vegetables brought in for dinner.’”", "\"네, 같은 날이었던 게 분명해요, 마님이 '나는 그 애를 보러 갈 참이다, 크리스천, 그러니 저녁 찬거리는 들여올 것 없다'라고 하셨거든요.\""]], [["“See whom?”", "\"누구를 본단 거지?\""]], [["“See you.", "\"도련님을요."], ["She was going to your house, you understand.”", "도련님 댁으로 가시는 길이었던 거예요, 아시겠지요.\""]], [["Yeobright regarded Christian with intense surprise.", "요브라이트는 강렬한 놀라움으로 크리스천을 바라보았다."], ["“Why did you never mention this?”", "\"왜 이걸 한 번도 얘기하지 않았나?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Are you sure it was my house she was coming to?”", "\"어머니가 오시려던 곳이 내 집이었다는 게 확실해?\""]], [["“O yes.", "\"오, 그럼요."], ["I didn’t mention it because I’ve never zeed you lately.", "요새 도련님을 못 뵈어서 말씀을 못 드린 거예요."], ["And as she didn’t get there it was all nought, and nothing to tell.”", "그리고 마님이 거기 닿지도 못하셨으니 다 헛일이 됐고, 말씀드릴 것도 없었던 거고요.\""]], [["“And I have been wondering why she should have walked in the heath on that hot day!", "\"그래서 나는 어머니가 왜 그 더운 날 황야를 걸으셨을까 의아해하고 있었는데!"], ["Well, did she say what she was coming for?", "그래, 어머니가 무엇 때문에 오신다고 하셨나?"], ["It is a thing, Christian, I am very anxious to know.”", "크리스천, 그건 내가 꼭 알고 싶은 일이네.\""]], [["“Yes, Mister Clym.", "\"네, 클림 도련님."], ["She didn’t say it to me, though I think she did to one here and there.”", "저한텐 말씀 안 하셨는데, 여기저기 한두 사람한텐 하신 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Do you know one person to whom she spoke of it?”", "\"어머니가 그 얘기를 하신 사람을 한 명이라도 아나?\""]], [["“There is one man, please, sir, but I hope you won’t mention my name to him, as I have seen him in strange places, particular in dreams.", "\"한 사람 있어요, 도련님, 그런데 제 이름은 그 사람한테 말하지 말아주셨으면 해요, 제가 그 사람을 이상한 곳에서, 특히 꿈속에서 봤거든요."], ["One night last summer he glared at me like Famine and Sword, and it made me feel so low that I didn’t comb out my few hairs for two days.", "지난여름 어느 날 밤엔 그가 기근과 칼처럼 저를 노려봐서 어찌나 기가 죽었던지 이틀이나 제 몇 가닥 안 되는 머리도 안 빗었지 뭐예요."], ["He was standing, as it might be, Mister Yeobright, in the middle of the path to Mistover, and your mother came up, looking as pale—”", "그가, 말하자면, 요브라이트 도련님, 미스토버로 가는 길 한복판에 서 있었는데, 마님이 다가오셨고, 어찌나 창백해 보이시던지—\""]], [["“Yes, when was that?”", "\"그래, 그게 언제였나?\""]], [["“Last summer, in my dream.”", "\"지난여름요, 제 꿈속에서요.\""]], [["“Pooh!", "\"쳇!"], ["Who’s the man?”", "그 사람이 누군가?\""]], [["“Diggory, the reddleman.", "\"디고리요, 그 레들맨이요."], ["He called upon her and sat with her the evening before she set out to see you.", "마님이 도련님을 보러 떠나기 전날 저녁에 그가 찾아와 마님과 함께 앉아 있었어요."], ["I hadn’t gone home from work when he came up to the gate.”", "그가 문간으로 올라올 때 저는 아직 일에서 돌아오지 않았었고요.\""]], [["“I must see Venn—I wish I had known it before,”", "\"벤을 만나봐야겠군—진작 알았더라면 좋았을 것을.\""], ["said Clym anxiously.", "클림이 초조하게 말했다."], ["“I wonder why he has not come to tell me?”", "\"그 사람은 왜 나한테 말하러 오지 않았을까?\""]], [["“He went out of Egdon Heath the next day, so would not be likely to know you wanted him.”", "\"그 사람이 이튿날 에그던 황야를 떠났거든요, 그러니 도련님이 자길 찾는다는 걸 알 리가 없었지요.\""]], [["“Christian,”", "\"크리스천,\""], ["said Clym, “you must go and find Venn.", "클림이 말했다. \"자네가 가서 벤을 찾아주게."], ["I am otherwise engaged, or I would go myself.", "나는 달리 일이 있으니, 아니면 내가 직접 가겠네만."], ["Find him at once, and tell him I want to speak to him.”", "당장 찾아내서 내가 얘기하고 싶어한다고 전해주게.\""]], [["“I am a good hand at hunting up folk by day,”", "\"낮에 사람 찾아내는 일이라면 제가 솜씨가 좋지요.\""], ["said Christian, looking dubiously round at the declining light; “but as to night-time, never is such a bad hand as I, Mister Yeobright.”", "크리스천이 기우는 햇빛을 미심쩍게 둘러보며 말했다. \"그런데 밤이 되면, 저만큼 서툰 사람도 없답니다, 요브라이트 도련님.\""]], [["“Search the heath when you will, so that you bring him soon.", "\"언제든 황야를 뒤지게나, 그를 곧 데려오기만 한다면."], ["Bring him tomorrow, if you can.”", "할 수 있거든 내일 데려오게.\""]], [["Christian then departed.", "그리하여 크리스천은 떠났다."], ["The morrow came, but no Venn.", "이튿날이 왔지만 벤은 오지 않았다."], ["In the evening Christian arrived, looking very weary.", "저녁에 크리스천이 몹시 지친 모습으로 돌아왔다."], ["He had been searching all day, and had heard nothing of the reddleman.", "그는 온종일 찾아다녔으나 레들맨에 관해서는 아무것도 듣지 못했다."]], [["“Inquire as much as you can tomorrow without neglecting your work,”", "\"일을 소홀히 하지 않는 선에서 내일 되도록 많이 수소문해보게.\""], ["said Yeobright.", "요브라이트가 말했다."], ["“Don’t come again till you have found him.”", "\"그를 찾기 전엔 다시 오지 말게.\""]], [["The next day Yeobright set out for the old house at Blooms-End, which, with the garden, was now his own.", "이튿날 요브라이트는 블룸스엔드의 옛집으로 떠났으니, 그 집은 정원과 함께 이제 그의 소유가 되어 있었다."], ["His severe illness had hindered all preparations for his removal thither; but it had become necessary that he should go and overlook its contents, as administrator to his mother’s little property; for which purpose he decided to pass the next night on the premises.", "그의 중병이 그곳으로 이사할 모든 준비를 가로막았으나, 어머니의 작은 재산의 관리인으로서 그가 가서 그 안의 물건들을 살펴보아야 할 필요가 생겼던 것이며, 그 목적을 위해 그는 다음 날 밤을 그 집에서 보내기로 했다."]], [["He journeyed onward, not quickly or decisively, but in the slow walk of one who has been awakened from a stupefying sleep.", "그는 빠르거나 단호한 걸음이 아니라, 정신이 멍한 잠에서 깨어난 사람의 느린 걸음으로 길을 나아갔다."], ["It was early afternoon when he reached the valley.", "그가 골짜기에 다다른 것은 이른 오후였다."], ["The expression of the place, the tone of the hour, were precisely those of many such occasions in days gone by; and these antecedent similarities fostered the illusion that she, who was there no longer, would come out to welcome him.", "그곳의 표정도, 그 시각의 색조도, 지나간 날들의 그러한 수많은 순간들과 정확히 똑같았으며, 이 앞선 유사함들이, 이제 그곳에 더는 없는 그녀가 밖으로 나와 그를 맞아줄 것 같은 환상을 키웠다."], ["The garden gate was locked and the shutters were closed, just as he himself had left them on the evening after the funeral.", "정원 문은 잠겨 있었고 덧창들은 닫혀 있었으니, 장례식 다음 날 저녁 그 자신이 떠날 때 그대로였다."], ["He unlocked the gate, and found that a spider had already constructed a large web, tying the door to the lintel, on the supposition that it was never to be opened again.", "그가 문을 따 보니, 거미 한 마리가 다시는 그 문이 열리지 않으리라 짐작하고서 문을 문틀에 묶어두는 커다란 거미줄을 벌써 쳐놓았다."], ["When he had entered the house and flung back the shutters he set about his task of overhauling the cupboards and closets, burning papers, and considering how best to arrange the place for Eustacia’s reception, until such time as he might be in a position to carry out his long-delayed scheme, should that time ever arrive.", "집 안에 들어가 덧창을 다시 젖힌 그는, 찬장과 벽장을 샅샅이 뒤지고 서류를 태우며, 그가 오래 미뤄둔 계획을 실행할 처지가 될 때까지—그런 때가 과연 온다면—유스테이샤를 맞이하기에 가장 좋도록 어떻게 집을 꾸밀지 궁리하는 일에 착수했다."]], [["As he surveyed the rooms he felt strongly disinclined for the alterations which would have to be made in the time-honoured furnishing of his parents and grandparents, to suit Eustacia’s modern ideas.", "여러 방을 둘러보면서 그는, 유스테이샤의 현대적인 취향에 맞추려고 부모와 조부모의 유서 깊은 가구들에 가해야 할 변경에 강한 거부감을 느꼈다."], ["The gaunt oak-cased clock, with the picture of the Ascension on the door panel and the Miraculous Draught of Fishes on the base; his grandmother’s corner cupboard with the glass door, through which the spotted china was visible; the dumb-waiter; the wooden tea trays; the hanging fountain with the brass tap—whither would these venerable articles have to be banished?", "문짝 판에 승천 그림이, 받침대에 기적의 고기잡이 그림이 그려진 그 앙상한 오크 상자 시계며, 점박이 자기가 들여다보이는 유리문이 달린 할머니의 모퉁이 찬장이며, 덤웨이터며, 나무 찻쟁반들이며, 놋쇠 꼭지가 달린 매달린 물통—이 유서 깊은 물건들을 대체 어디로 추방해야 한단 말인가?"]], [["He noticed that the flowers in the window had died for want of water, and he placed them out upon the ledge, that they might be taken away.", "그는 창가의 꽃들이 물이 없어 시들어버린 것을 알아챘고, 가져가버릴 수 있도록 그것들을 선반 위로 내놓았다."], ["While thus engaged he heard footsteps on the gravel without, and somebody knocked at the door.", "이렇게 분주히 일하는 동안 그는 밖의 자갈길에서 발소리를 들었고, 누군가 문을 두드렸다."]], [["Yeobright opened it, and Venn was standing before him.", "요브라이트가 문을 열자 벤이 그 앞에 서 있었다."]], [["“Good morning,”", "\"안녕하세요.\""], ["said the reddleman.", "레들맨이 말했다."], ["“Is Mrs. Yeobright at home?”", "\"요브라이트 부인 계신가요?\""]], [["Yeobright looked upon the ground.", "요브라이트는 땅바닥을 내려다보았다."], ["“Then you have not seen Christian or any of the Egdon folks?”", "\"그럼 크리스천이나 에그던 사람 누구도 못 만났소?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“No. I have only just returned after a long stay away.", "\"못 만났습니다."], ["I called here the day before I left.”", "오래 떠나 있다가 막 돌아온 참이라서요. 떠나기 전날 여기 들렀었지요.\""]], [["“And you have heard nothing?”", "\"그러면 아무것도 못 들었소?\""]], [["“Nothing.”", "\"아무것도요.\""]], [["“My mother is—dead.”", "\"우리 어머니가—돌아가셨소.\""]], [["“Dead!”", "\"돌아가셨다고요!\""], ["said Venn mechanically.", "벤이 기계적으로 말했다."]], [["“Her home now is where I shouldn’t mind having mine.”", "\"이제 어머니의 집은, 나도 내 집을 거기 두어도 좋겠다 싶은 그곳이오.\""]], [["Venn regarded him, and then said, “If I didn’t see your face I could never believe your words.", "벤이 그를 바라보더니 말했다. \"도련님 얼굴을 보지 않았다면 그 말씀을 도무지 믿을 수 없었을 겁니다."], ["Have you been ill?”", "앓으셨나요?\""]], [["“I had an illness.”", "\"한 차례 앓았소.\""]], [["“Well, the change!", "\"그런데 이런 변고가!"], ["When I parted from her a month ago everything seemed to say that she was going to begin a new life.”", "제가 한 달 전 마님과 헤어질 땐 모든 게 마님이 새 삶을 시작하시려는 것처럼 보였는데요.\""]], [["“And what seemed came true.”", "\"그리고 그렇게 보이던 것이 사실이 되었소.\""]], [["“You say right, no doubt.", "\"옳은 말씀이십니다, 틀림없이요."], ["Trouble has taught you a deeper vein of talk than mine.", "슬픔이 도련님께 저보다 더 깊은 말솜씨를 가르쳤군요."], ["All I meant was regarding her life here.", "제가 말씀드린 건 그저 마님이 이곳에서 사실 삶에 관한 거였습니다."], ["She has died too soon.”", "마님은 너무 일찍 돌아가셨어요.\""]], [["“Perhaps through my living too long.", "\"어쩌면 내가 너무 오래 살아서일지도 모르지."], ["I have had a bitter experience on that score this last month, Diggory.", "지난 한 달 동안 나는 그 점에 관해 쓰라린 경험을 했소, 디고리."], ["But come in; I have been wanting to see you.”", "하지만 들어오시오. 당신을 만나고 싶었소.\""]], [["He conducted the reddleman into the large room where the dancing had taken place the previous Christmas, and they sat down in the settle together.", "그는 레들맨을 데리고, 지난 크리스마스에 춤판이 벌어졌던 그 큰 방으로 들어가, 함께 긴 의자에 앉았다."], ["“There’s the cold fireplace, you see,”", "\"저 차가운 벽난로가 보이오.\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."], ["“When that half-burnt log and those cinders were alight she was alive!", "\"저 반쯤 탄 장작과 저 잿더미에 불이 붙어 있었을 때 어머니는 살아 계셨소!"], ["Little has been changed here yet.", "여긴 아직 바뀐 게 거의 없소."], ["I can do nothing.", "나는 아무것도 할 수가 없소."], ["My life creeps like a snail.”", "내 삶은 달팽이처럼 기어가오.\""]], [["“How came she to die?”", "\"마님이 어쩌다 돌아가셨습니까?\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."]], [["Yeobright gave him some particulars of her illness and death, and continued: “After this no kind of pain will ever seem more than an indisposition to me.", "요브라이트는 그에게 어머니의 병과 죽음에 관한 몇 가지 사정을 들려준 뒤 말을 이었다. \"이 일을 겪고 나니 이제 그 어떤 고통도 내겐 가벼운 병치레 이상으로 여겨지지 않을 거요."], ["I began saying that I wanted to ask you something, but I stray from subjects like a drunken man.", "내가 뭔가 물어보고 싶다고 말을 꺼내놓고는 술 취한 사람처럼 화제를 벗어나고 있군."], ["I am anxious to know what my mother said to you when she last saw you.", "어머니가 당신을 마지막으로 보셨을 때 무슨 말씀을 하셨는지 꼭 알고 싶소."], ["You talked with her a long time, I think?”", "어머니와 한참 이야기를 나눴겠지?\""]], [["“I talked with her more than half an hour.”", "\"반 시간도 더 이야기를 나눴습니다.\""]], [["“About me?”", "\"나에 관해서?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["And it must have been on account of what we said that she was on the heath.", "그리고 마님이 황야에 나와 계셨던 건 우리가 나눈 그 얘기 때문이었던 게 분명합니다."], ["Without question she was coming to see you.”", "의심할 바 없이 마님은 도련님을 보러 오시던 길이었어요.\""]], [["“But why should she come to see me if she felt so bitterly against me?", "\"하지만 나를 그토록 모질게 여기셨다면 왜 나를 보러 오셨겠소?"], ["There’s the mystery.”", "거기에 수수께끼가 있소.\""]], [["“Yet I know she quite forgave ’ee.”", "\"그래도 마님이 도련님을 완전히 용서하셨다는 건 제가 압니다.\""]], [["“But, Diggory—would a woman, who had quite forgiven her son, say, when she felt herself ill on the way to his house, that she was broken-hearted because of his ill-usage?", "\"하지만, 디고리—아들을 완전히 용서한 여인이, 그 아들의 집으로 가는 길에 몸이 아파지자, 아들의 학대 때문에 가슴이 찢어졌다고 말하겠소?"], ["Never!”", "결코 아니지!\""]], [["“What I know is that she didn’t blame you at all.", "\"제가 아는 건 마님이 도련님을 전혀 탓하지 않으셨다는 겁니다."], ["She blamed herself for what had happened, and only herself.", "마님은 일어난 일을 두고 자기 자신을, 오직 자기 자신만을 탓하셨어요."], ["I had it from her own lips.”", "마님 입으로 직접 들은 얘기예요.\""]], [["“You had it from her lips that I had _not_ ill-treated her; and at the same time another had it from her lips that I _had_ ill-treated her?", "\"내가 어머니를 학대하지 않았다는 걸 당신은 어머니 입으로 들었고, 동시에 다른 사람은 내가 어머니를 학대했다는 걸 어머니 입으로 들었다?"], ["My mother was no impulsive woman who changed her opinion every hour without reason.", "우리 어머니는 까닭 없이 매시간 의견을 바꾸는 충동적인 분이 아니셨소."], ["How can it be, Venn, that she should have told such different stories in close succession?”", "어떻게, 벤, 어머니가 그렇게 짧은 간격을 두고 그토록 다른 얘기를 하셨을 수 있단 말이오?\""]], [["“I cannot say.", "\"저도 모르겠습니다."], ["It is certainly odd, when she had forgiven you, and had forgiven your wife, and was going to see ye on purpose to make friends.”", "도련님을 용서하시고, 도련님 부인까지 용서하시고, 화해하려고 일부러 도련님을 보러 가시던 길이었으니, 분명 이상한 일이긴 합니다.\""]], [["“If there was one thing wanting to bewilder me it was this incomprehensible thing!...", "\"나를 어리둥절하게 만들 한 가지가 더 필요했다면 바로 이 도무지 알 수 없는 일이었소!…"], ["Diggory, if we, who remain alive, were only allowed to hold conversation with the dead—just once, a bare minute, even through a screen of iron bars, as with persons in prison—what we might learn!", "디고리, 만약 우리, 살아남은 자들이 죽은 자들과 대화를 나누는 것이 허락된다면—단 한 번, 단 일 분만이라도, 감옥에 있는 사람과 그러하듯 쇠창살 너머로라도—우리가 무엇을 알아낼 수 있겠소!"], ["How many who now ride smiling would hide their heads!", "지금 웃으며 활보하는 자들 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 고개를 숨기겠소!"], ["And this mystery—I should then be at the bottom of it at once.", "그리고 이 수수께끼—나는 단번에 그 밑바닥까지 닿을 텐데."], ["But the grave has forever shut her in; and how shall it be found out now?”", "하지만 무덤이 어머니를 영원히 가두어버렸으니, 이제 어찌 그것을 밝혀낸단 말이오?\""]], [["No reply was returned by his companion, since none could be given; and when Venn left, a few minutes later, Clym had passed from the dullness of sorrow to the fluctuation of carking incertitude.", "그의 동행은 아무런 대답도 하지 않았으니, 아무런 대답도 할 수 없었기 때문이며, 몇 분 후 벤이 떠났을 때 클림은 슬픔의 무딘 상태에서 마음을 갉아먹는 불확실함의 동요로 넘어가 있었다."]], [["He continued in the same state all the afternoon.", "그는 오후 내내 그 상태로 머물렀다."], ["A bed was made up for him in the same house by a neighbour, that he might not have to return again the next day; and when he retired to rest in the deserted place it was only to remain awake hour after hour thinking the same thoughts.", "이웃 사람이 그가 다음 날 다시 돌아오지 않아도 되도록 같은 집에 잠자리를 마련해주었으며, 그 적막한 곳에서 그가 잠자리에 들었을 때, 그는 똑같은 생각을 곱씹으며 시간이 가도록 깨어 있을 뿐이었다."], ["How to discover a solution to this riddle of death seemed a query of more importance than highest problems of the living.", "이 죽음의 수수께끼에 대한 해답을 어떻게 찾아낼 것인가가, 살아 있는 자들의 가장 높은 문제들보다 더 중대한 물음으로 여겨졌다."], ["There was housed in his memory a vivid picture of the face of a little boy as he entered the hovel where Clym’s mother lay.", "그의 기억 속에는, 클림의 어머니가 누워 있던 오두막으로 들어설 때의 어린 소년의 얼굴이 또렷한 그림으로 깃들어 있었다."], ["The round eyes, eager gaze, the piping voice which enunciated the words, had operated like stilettos on his brain.", "그 둥근 눈과 간절한 눈빛과, 그 말들을 또박또박 내뱉던 가느다란 목소리가, 그의 뇌리에 단검처럼 박혀들었다."]], [["A visit to the boy suggested itself as a means of gleaning new particulars; though it might be quite unproductive.", "그 소년을 찾아가는 것이, 비록 아무 소득이 없을지라도, 새로운 사정을 얻어낼 방편으로 떠올랐다."], ["To probe a child’s mind after the lapse of six weeks, not for facts which the child had seen and understood, but to get at those which were in their nature beyond him, did not promise much; yet when every obvious channel is blocked we grope towards the small and obscure.", "여섯 주가 지난 뒤에 아이의 마음을, 아이가 보고 이해한 사실들을 캐내려는 게 아니라 본질상 아이의 능력 너머에 있는 것들을 얻어내려고 더듬는 일이 큰 성과를 기약하지는 못했으나, 뻔한 길목이 모두 막혀 있을 때 우리는 작고 희미한 것을 향해 더듬어 나아가는 법이다."], ["There was nothing else left to do; after that he would allow the enigma to drop into the abyss of undiscoverable things.", "달리 할 일은 아무것도 남아 있지 않았으니, 그러고 나면 그는 이 수수께끼가 알아낼 수 없는 것들의 심연 속으로 떨어지도록 내버려두려 했다."]], [["It was about daybreak when he had reached this decision, and he at once arose.", "그가 이 결심에 이른 것은 동틀 무렵이었고, 그는 곧바로 일어났다."], ["He locked up the house and went out into the green patch which merged in heather further on.", "집을 잠그고 나서 그는, 좀 더 가면 히스 들판으로 이어지는 푸른 풀밭으로 나섰다."], ["In front of the white garden-palings the path branched into three like a broad arrow.", "흰 정원 울타리 앞에서 길은 넓은 화살촉처럼 세 갈래로 갈라졌다."], ["The road to the right led to the Quiet Woman and its neighbourhood; the middle track led to Mistover Knap; the left-hand track led over the hill to another part of Mistover, where the child lived.", "오른쪽 길은 콰이어트 우먼과 그 인근으로 이어졌고, 가운데 길은 미스토버 냅으로 이어졌으며, 왼쪽 길은 언덕을 넘어 그 아이가 사는 미스토버의 다른 지역으로 이어졌다."], ["On inclining into the latter path Yeobright felt a creeping chilliness, familiar enough to most people, and probably caused by the unsunned morning air.", "마지막 길로 접어들면서 요브라이트는 대부분의 사람들에게 익히 친숙한, 그리고 아마도 햇볕이 들지 않은 아침 공기 탓이었을, 스멀스멀 기어드는 한기를 느꼈다."], ["In after days he thought of it as a thing of singular significance.", "훗날 그는 그것을 유난히 의미심장한 일로 떠올렸다."]], [["When Yeobright reached the cottage of Susan Nunsuch, the mother of the boy he sought, he found that the inmates were not yet astir.", "요브라이트가 그가 찾던 소년의 어머니인 수전 넌서치의 오두막에 다다랐을 때, 안의 식구들은 아직 일어나지 않은 모양이었다."], ["But in upland hamlets the transition from a-bed to abroad is surprisingly swift and easy.", "하지만 고지대의 작은 마을에서는 잠자리에서 바깥으로의 전환이 놀라우리만치 빠르고도 수월하다."], ["There no dense partition of yawns and toilets divides humanity by night from humanity by day.", "거기서는 하품과 몸단장의 빽빽한 칸막이가 밤의 인류와 낮의 인류를 갈라놓지 않는다."], ["Yeobright tapped at the upper windowsill, which he could reach with his walking stick; and in three or four minutes the woman came down.", "요브라이트는 지팡이로 닿을 수 있는 위층 창턱을 두드렸고, 서너 분 만에 여자가 내려왔다."]], [["It was not till this moment that Clym recollected her to be the person who had behaved so barbarously to Eustacia.", "클림이 그녀가 유스테이샤에게 그토록 야만적으로 군 바로 그 사람임을 떠올린 것은 이 순간에 이르러서였다."], ["It partly explained the insuavity with which the woman greeted him.", "그 사실이 여자가 그를 맞이하는 무뚝뚝함을 어느 정도 설명해주었다."], ["Moreover, the boy had been ailing again; and Susan now, as ever since the night when he had been pressed into Eustacia’s service at the bonfire, attributed his indispositions to Eustacia’s influence as a witch.", "게다가 소년이 또 앓고 있었으며, 수전은 모닥불 자리에서 그가 유스테이샤의 시중에 떠밀려 들었던 그날 밤 이후로 늘 그래왔듯이, 아이의 병치레를 마녀로서의 유스테이샤의 영향 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["It was one of those sentiments which lurk like moles underneath the visible surface of manners, and may have been kept alive by Eustacia’s entreaty to the captain, at the time that he had intended to prosecute Susan for the pricking in church, to let the matter drop; which he accordingly had done.", "그것은 예의범절의 보이는 표면 아래 두더지처럼 숨어 있는 그런 감정 중 하나였으며, 대장이 교회에서의 바늘 찌르기 일로 수전을 고발하려던 무렵 유스테이샤가 그 일을 그냥 덮어달라고 간청해 그가 그에 따라 그렇게 해주었던 까닭에 명맥을 유지해온 것일 수도 있었다."]], [["Yeobright overcame his repugnance, for Susan had at least borne his mother no ill-will.", "요브라이트는 거부감을 누그러뜨렸으니, 수전은 적어도 그의 어머니에게는 아무런 악감정도 품지 않았기 때문이다."], ["He asked kindly for the boy; but her manner did not improve.", "그는 다정하게 소년의 안부를 물었으나, 그녀의 태도는 나아지지 않았다."]], [["“I wish to see him,”", "\"그 애를 보고 싶소.\""], ["continued Yeobright, with some hesitation, “to ask him if he remembers anything more of his walk with my mother than what he has previously told.”", "요브라이트가 다소 머뭇거리며 말을 이었다. \"그 애가 우리 어머니와 걸었던 일에 대해, 전에 얘기한 것 말고 더 기억하는 게 있는지 물어보려고요.\""]], [["She regarded him in a peculiar and criticizing manner.", "그녀가 묘하고도 따지는 듯한 태도로 그를 바라보았다."], ["To anybody but a half-blind man it would have said, “You want another of the knocks which have already laid you so low.”", "반쯤 눈먼 사람이 아니고서야 누구라도 그것이 이렇게 말하는 것임을 알았으리라. \"당신은 이미 당신을 그토록 쓰러뜨린 매질을 한 번 더 맞고 싶은 게로군.\""]], [["She called the boy downstairs, asked Clym to sit down on a stool, and continued, “Now, Johnny, tell Mr.", "그녀가 소년을 아래층으로 불러 클림에게 걸상에 앉으라 하고는 말을 이었다."], ["Yeobright anything you can call to mind.”", "\"자, 조니, 요브라이트 씨한테 네가 떠올릴 수 있는 건 뭐든 다 말씀드려라.\""]], [["“You have not forgotten how you walked with the poor lady on that hot day?”", "\"네가 그 더운 날 그 가엾은 부인과 함께 걸었던 거 잊지 않았지?\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"네.\""], ["said the boy.", "소년이 말했다."]], [["“And what she said to you?”", "\"그리고 그분이 너한테 뭐라고 하셨는지도?\""]], [["The boy repeated the exact words he had used on entering the hut.", "소년은 오두막에 들어설 때 자기가 했던 바로 그 말들을 되풀이했다."], ["Yeobright rested his elbow on the table and shaded his face with his hand; and the mother looked as if she wondered how a man could want more of what had stung him so deeply.", "요브라이트는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 얼굴을 가렸으며, 어머니는 한 사내가 그토록 깊이 자기를 찔러놓은 일을 어떻게 더 듣고 싶어할 수 있는지 의아해하는 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["“She was going to Alderworth when you first met her?”", "\"네가 그분을 처음 만났을 때 그분은 올더워스로 가시던 길이었니?\""]], [["“No; she was coming away.”", "\"아뇨, 떠나오시던 길이었어요.\""]], [["“That can’t be.”", "\"그럴 리가 없어.\""]], [["“Yes; she walked along with me.", "\"맞아요, 그분이 저랑 같이 걸으셨어요."], ["I was coming away, too.”", "저도 떠나오던 길이었거든요.\""]], [["“Then where did you first see her?”", "\"그럼 그분을 어디서 처음 봤니?\""]], [["“At your house.”", "\"도련님 댁에서요.\""]], [["“Attend, and speak the truth!”", "\"잘 듣고, 사실대로 말해라!\""], ["said Clym sternly.", "클림이 엄하게 말했다."]], [["“Yes, sir; at your house was where I seed her first.”", "\"네, 도련님, 도련님 댁에서 그분을 처음 봤어요.\""]], [["Clym started up, and Susan smiled in an expectant way which did not embellish her face; it seemed to mean, “Something sinister is coming!”", "클림이 벌떡 일어났고, 수전은 그 얼굴을 돋보이게 하지 못하는, 무언가를 기대하는 듯한 미소를 지었으니, 그것은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"불길한 일이 다가오고 있어!\""]], [["“What did she do at my house?”", "\"그분이 내 집에서 무얼 하셨니?\""]], [["“She went and sat under the trees at the Devil’s Bellows.”", "\"악마의 풀무 옆 나무들 아래로 가서 앉으셨어요.\""]], [["“Good God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["this is all news to me!”", "이건 죄다 처음 듣는 얘기로군!\""]], [["“You never told me this before?”", "\"넌 나한테 이걸 한 번도 얘기 안 했잖니?\""], ["said Susan.", "수전이 말했다."]], [["“No, Mother; because I didn’t like to tell ’ee I had been so far.", "\"네, 엄마, 제가 그렇게 멀리 갔었다고 말하기가 싫어서요."], ["I was picking blackhearts, and went further than I meant.”", "검은딸기를 따다가 생각보다 멀리 갔던 거예요.\""]], [["“What did she do then?”", "\"그러고 나서 그분이 무얼 하셨니?\""], ["said Yeobright.", "요브라이트가 말했다."]], [["“Looked at a man who came up and went into your house.”", "\"한 남자가 올라와서 도련님 댁으로 들어가는 걸 보셨어요.\""]], [["“That was myself—a furze-cutter, with brambles in his hand.”", "\"그게 나였다—가시덤불을 손에 든 가시금작화 베는 사람이었지.\""]], [["“No; ’twas not you.", "\"아뇨, 도련님이 아니었어요."], ["’Twas a gentleman.", "신사였어요."], ["You had gone in afore.”", "도련님은 앞서 들어가셨고요.\""]], [["“Who was he?”", "\"그게 누구였지?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"몰라요.\""]], [["“Now tell me what happened next.”", "\"이제 그다음에 무슨 일이 있었는지 말해보렴.\""]], [["“The poor lady went and knocked at your door, and the lady with black hair looked out of the side window at her.”", "\"그 가엾은 부인이 가서 도련님 댁 문을 두드리셨는데, 검은 머리의 부인이 옆 창문으로 그분을 내다봤어요.\""]], [["The boy’s mother turned to Clym and said, “This is something you didn’t expect?”", "소년의 어머니가 클림을 돌아보며 말했다. \"이건 당신이 예상 못 한 일이지요?\""]], [["Yeobright took no more notice of her than if he had been of stone.", "요브라이트는 돌멩이라도 된 듯 그녀를 더는 거들떠보지 않았다."], ["“Go on, go on,”", "\"계속해라, 계속해.\""], ["he said hoarsely to the boy.", "그가 소년에게 쉰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“And when she saw the young lady look out of the window the old lady knocked again; and when nobody came she took up the furze-hook and looked at it, and put it down again, and then she looked at the faggot-bonds; and then she went away, and walked across to me, and blowed her breath very hard, like this.", "\"그리고 그 젊은 부인이 창문으로 내다보는 걸 보고서 그 나이 든 부인이 또 문을 두드리셨어요. 그래도 아무도 안 나오니까 가시금작화 낫을 집어 들어 들여다보고는 도로 내려놓으시고, 그러고는 나뭇단 묶음을 보시더니, 가버리셨고, 저한테 걸어오셔서, 이렇게, 숨을 아주 가쁘게 내쉬셨어요."], ["We walked on together, she and I, and I talked to her and she talked to me a bit, but not much, because she couldn’t blow her breath.”", "우리는, 그분이랑 저랑, 같이 걸어갔고, 제가 그분한테 얘기하고 그분도 저한테 얘기를 좀 하셨는데, 많이는 못 하셨어요, 숨을 쉴 수가 없으셔서요.\""]], [["“O!”", "\"오!\""], ["murmured Clym, in a low tone, and bowed his head.", "클림이 낮은 목소리로 중얼거리며 고개를 떨구었다."], ["“Let’s have more,”", "\"더 들어보자.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“She couldn’t talk much, and she couldn’t walk; and her face was, O so queer!”", "\"많이 얘기도 못 하시고, 걷지도 못하셨어요. 그리고 얼굴이, 오, 어찌나 이상하던지요!\""]], [["“How was her face?”", "\"얼굴이 어땠는데?\""]], [["“Like yours is now.”", "\"지금 도련님 얼굴 같았어요.\""]], [["The woman looked at Yeobright, and beheld him colourless, in a cold sweat.", "여자가 요브라이트를 바라보니, 그는 핏기 하나 없이 식은땀에 젖어 있었다."], ["“Isn’t there meaning in it?”", "\"여기에 뜻이 있지 않겠어요?\""], ["she said stealthily.", "그녀가 슬그머니 말했다."], ["“What do you think of her now?”", "\"이제 그 여자를 어떻게 생각하세요?\""]], [["“Silence!”", "\"조용히 하시오!\""], ["said Clym fiercely.", "클림이 사납게 말했다."], ["And, turning to the boy, “And then you left her to die?”", "그러고는 소년을 돌아보며 \"그러고 나서 너는 그분을 죽도록 내버려두었니?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아뇨.\""], ["said the woman, quickly and angrily.", "여자가 재빠르고 성난 목소리로 말했다."], ["“He did not leave her to die!", "\"이 애는 그분을 죽도록 내버려두지 않았어요!"], ["She sent him away.", "그분이 이 애를 돌려보내신 거예요."], ["Whoever says he forsook her says what’s not true.”", "이 애가 그분을 버렸다고 하는 자는 사실이 아닌 말을 하는 거예요.\""]], [["“Trouble no more about that,”", "\"그건 더 신경 쓰지 마시오.\""], ["answered Clym, with a quivering mouth.", "클림이 떨리는 입으로 대답했다."], ["“What he did is a trifle in comparison with what he saw.", "\"이 애가 한 일은 이 애가 본 것에 비하면 사소하오."], ["Door kept shut, did you say?", "문이 닫혀 있었다고 했지?"], ["Kept shut, she looking out of window?", "닫혀 있었고, 그 여자가 창문으로 내다보고 있었다고?"], ["Good heart of God!—what does it mean?”", "자비로우신 하느님!—이게 무슨 뜻이란 말이오?\""]], [["The child shrank away from the gaze of his questioner.", "아이가 자기를 캐묻는 사람의 시선에서 움츠러들었다."]], [["“He said so,”", "\"이 애가 그렇게 말했어요.\""], ["answered the mother, “and Johnny’s a God-fearing boy and tells no lies.”", "어머니가 대답했다. \"그리고 조니는 하느님을 두려워하는 아이라 거짓말은 안 해요.\""]], [["“‘Cast off by my son!’ No, by my best life, dear mother, it is not so!", "\"'아들에게 버림받았다!' 아니요, 내 가장 소중한 목숨을 걸고, 어머니, 그렇지 않습니다!"], ["But by your son’s, your son’s—May all murderesses get the torment they deserve!”", "하지만 당신의 아들에게는, 당신의 아들에게는—모든 살인하는 여자들이 마땅히 받아야 할 형벌을 받을지어다!\""]], [["With these words Yeobright went forth from the little dwelling.", "이 말과 함께 요브라이트는 그 작은 거처에서 밖으로 나왔다."], ["The pupils of his eyes, fixed steadfastly on blankness, were vaguely lit with an icy shine; his mouth had passed into the phase more or less imaginatively rendered in studies of Oedipus.", "텅 빈 허공에 한결같이 못 박힌 그의 두 눈동자는 얼음 같은 광채로 어렴풋이 빛났으며, 그의 입은 오이디푸스 연구에서 다소간 상상적으로 표현된 그 모습으로 옮아가 있었다."], ["The strangest deeds were possible to his mood.", "그의 기분이라면 가장 기이한 행위들도 가능했다."], ["But they were not possible to his situation. Instead of there being before him the pale face of Eustacia, and a masculine shape unknown, there was only the imperturbable countenance of the heath, which, having defied the cataclysmal onsets of centuries, reduced to insignificance by its seamed and antique features the wildest turmoil of a single man.", "하지만 그것들은 그의 처지에서는 불가능했다."], ["III. Eustacia Dresses Herself on a Black Morning", "그의 앞에는 유스테이샤의 창백한 얼굴과 알 수 없는 남자의 형체가 있는 것이 아니라, 그저 황야의 흔들림 없는 표정만이 있었으니, 그 황야는 수백 년에 걸친 격변의 습격을 막아내며, 주름지고 태고와도 같은 그 모습으로 한 인간의 그 가장 사나운 혼란을 하찮은 것으로 깎아내렸다."]], [["A consciousness of a vast impassivity in all which lay around him took possession even of Yeobright in his wild walk towards Alderworth.", "자기를 에워싼 모든 것 속에 깃든 광대한 무감각함에 대한 자각이, 올더워스를 향한 그 미친 듯한 걸음 속에서 요브라이트마저 사로잡았다."], ["He had once before felt in his own person this overpowering of the fervid by the inanimate; but then it had tended to enervate a passion far sweeter than that which at present pervaded him.", "그는 전에 한 번 무생물이 열정적인 것을 짓누르는 이 느낌을 제 몸으로 느낀 적이 있었으나, 그때 그것은 지금 그를 가득 채운 것보다 훨씬 더 달콤한 열정을 무디게 하는 쪽으로 작용했다."], ["It was once when he stood parting from Eustacia in the moist still levels beyond the hills.", "그것은 언젠가 그가 언덕 너머 축축하고 고요한 평지에서 유스테이샤와 헤어지며 서 있을 때였다."], ["But dismissing all this he went onward home, and came to the front of his house.", "하지만 이 모든 것을 떨치고 그는 집으로 나아가, 자기 집 앞에 다다랐다."], ["The blinds of Eustacia’s bedroom were still closely drawn, for she was no early riser.", "유스테이샤의 침실 블라인드는 여전히 꼭 닫혀 있었으니, 그녀는 일찍 일어나는 사람이 아니었던 것이다."], ["All the life visible was in the shape of a solitary thrush cracking a small snail upon the door-stone for his breakfast, and his tapping seemed a loud noise in the general silence which prevailed; but on going to the door Clym found it unfastened, the young girl who attended upon Eustacia being astir in the back part of the premises.", "눈에 보이는 살아 있는 것이라곤, 문간 돌 위에서 작은 달팽이를 깨뜨려 아침으로 삼고 있는 외로운 개똥지빠귀 한 마리뿐이었고, 사방에 감도는 적막 속에서 그 톡톡거리는 소리가 큰 소음처럼 들렸다. 하지만 문으로 가보니 클림은 그것이 잠겨 있지 않음을 알았으니, 유스테이샤의 시중을 드는 어린 소녀가 집 뒤편에서 부산을 떨고 있었던 것이다."], ["Yeobright entered and went straight to his wife’s room.", "요브라이트는 안으로 들어가 곧장 아내의 방으로 향했다."]], [["The noise of his arrival must have aroused her, for when he opened the door she was standing before the looking glass in her nightdress, the ends of her hair gathered into one hand, with which she was coiling the whole mass round her head, previous to beginning toilette operations.", "그가 도착한 소리에 그녀가 깨어났던 게 분명하니, 그가 문을 열었을 때 그녀는 잠옷 차림으로 거울 앞에 서서, 한 손에 머리끝을 모아 쥐고는 단장을 시작하기에 앞서 그 머리채 전부를 머리 둘레로 둘둘 감고 있었다."], ["She was not a woman given to speaking first at a meeting, and she allowed Clym to walk across in silence, without turning her head.", "그녀는 마주칠 때 먼저 말을 거는 여자가 아니었기에, 고개를 돌리지 않은 채 클림이 말없이 걸어 들어오게 두었다."], ["He came behind her, and she saw his face in the glass.", "그가 그녀의 뒤로 다가왔고, 그녀는 거울 속에서 그의 얼굴을 보았다."], ["It was ashy, haggard, and terrible.", "그것은 잿빛에 초췌하고 끔찍했다."], ["Instead of starting towards him in sorrowful surprise, as even Eustacia, undemonstrative wife as she was, would have done in days before she burdened herself with a secret, she remained motionless, looking at him in the glass.", "비밀로 자신을 옭아매기 전이었다면, 감정을 잘 드러내지 않는 아내인 유스테이샤조차 그러했을 법한, 슬픈 놀라움에 차서 그를 향해 몸을 돌리는 대신, 그녀는 미동도 없이 거울 속의 그를 바라보았다."], ["And while she looked the carmine flush with which warmth and sound sleep had suffused her cheeks and neck dissolved from view, and the deathlike pallor in his face flew across into hers.", "그리고 그녀가 바라보는 동안, 온기와 깊은 잠으로 그녀의 두 뺨과 목에 번져 있던 진홍빛 홍조가 시야에서 사라지고, 그의 얼굴에 어린 죽음 같은 창백함이 그녀의 얼굴로 옮아 날아들었다."], ["He was close enough to see this, and the sight instigated his tongue.", "그는 이것을 볼 만큼 가까이 있었고, 그 광경이 그의 혀를 부추겼다."]], [["“You know what is the matter,”", "\"당신은 무슨 일인지 알고 있군.\""], ["he said huskily.", "그가 쉰 목소리로 말했다."], ["“I see it in your face.”", "\"당신 얼굴에서 그게 보이오.\""]], [["Her hand relinquished the rope of hair and dropped to her side, and the pile of tresses, no longer supported, fell from the crown of her head about her shoulders and over the white nightgown.", "그녀의 손이 머리채를 놓아 옆구리로 떨어졌고, 더는 받쳐지지 않은 머리타래 더미가 머리 꼭대기에서 흘러내려 그녀의 어깨와 흰 잠옷 위로 쏟아졌다."], ["She made no reply.", "그녀는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Speak to me,”", "\"내게 말해.\""], ["said Yeobright peremptorily.", "요브라이트가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["The blanching process did not cease in her, and her lips now became as white as her face.", "그녀에게서 핏기 가시는 과정이 멈추지 않았고, 이제 그녀의 입술도 얼굴만큼이나 하얘졌다."], ["She turned to him and said, “Yes, Clym, I’ll speak to you.", "그녀가 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"그래요, 클림, 당신한테 말할게요."], ["Why do you return so early?", "왜 이렇게 일찍 돌아왔어요?"], ["Can I do anything for you?”", "제가 뭐 해드릴 게 있나요?\""]], [["“Yes, you can listen to me.", "\"그래, 당신은 내 말을 들을 수 있지."], ["It seems that my wife is not very well?”", "보아하니 내 아내가 별로 편치 않은가 보군?\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“Your face, my dear; your face.", "\"당신 얼굴 말이오, 여보, 당신 얼굴."], ["Or perhaps it is the pale morning light which takes your colour away?", "아니면 어쩌면 창백한 아침 빛이 당신의 핏기를 앗아가는 건가?"], ["Now I am going to reveal a secret to you.", "자, 이제 당신한테 비밀을 하나 밝히려 하오."], ["Ha-ha!”", "하하!\""]], [["“O, that is ghastly!”", "\"오, 그건 소름 끼쳐요!\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐가?\""]], [["“Your laugh.”", "\"당신 웃음이요.\""]], [["“There’s reason for ghastliness.", "\"소름 끼칠 만한 까닭이 있지."], ["Eustacia, you have held my happiness in the hollow of your hand, and like a devil you have dashed it down!”", "유스테이샤, 당신은 내 행복을 손바닥에 쥐고 있었으면서, 악마처럼 그것을 내동댕이쳐버렸어!\""]], [["She started back from the dressing-table, retreated a few steps from him, and looked him in the face.", "그녀가 화장대에서 뒷걸음질 치며 그에게서 몇 걸음 물러나 그의 얼굴을 정면으로 바라보았다."], ["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you think to frighten me,”", "당신은 나를 겁주려는 거군요.\""], ["she said, with a slight laugh.", "그녀가 살짝 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Is it worth while?", "\"그게 그럴 만한 가치가 있어요?"], ["I am undefended, and alone.”", "나는 무방비에, 혼자예요.\""]], [["“How extraordinary!”", "\"참으로 별일이군!\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻이에요?\""]], [["“As there is ample time I will tell you, though you know well enough.", "\"시간이 넉넉하니 말해주지, 당신도 충분히 알고 있겠지만."], ["I mean that it is extraordinary that you should be alone in my absence.", "내 말은, 내가 없는 동안 당신이 혼자였다는 게 별일이라는 거요."], ["Tell me, now, where is he who was with you on the afternoon of the thirty-first of August?", "자, 어디 말해보지, 팔월 삼십일일 오후에 당신과 함께 있던 자는 어디 있소?"], ["Under the bed?", "침대 밑에?"], ["Up the chimney?”", "굴뚝 위에?\""]], [["A shudder overcame her and shook the light fabric of her nightdress throughout.", "전율이 그녀를 덮쳐 그녀의 잠옷의 얇은 천을 온통 흔들었다."], ["“I do not remember dates so exactly,” she said.", "\"저는 날짜를 그렇게 정확히 기억하지 못해요.\""], ["“I cannot recollect that anybody was with me besides yourself.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신 말고 누가 저와 함께 있었던 건 기억나지 않아요.\""]], [["“The day I mean,”", "\"내가 말하는 그날은,\""], ["said Yeobright, his voice growing louder and harsher, “was the day you shut the door against my mother and killed her.", "요브라이트의 목소리가 점점 커지고 거칠어지며 말했다. \"당신이 우리 어머니를 문전박대해 죽인 날이오."], ["O, it is too much—too bad!”", "오, 너무하군—너무 끔찍해!\""], ["He leant over the footpiece of the bedstead for a few moments, with his back towards her; then rising again—“Tell me, tell me!", "그는 잠시 그녀에게 등을 돌린 채 침대 발치에 기댔다가, 다시 몸을 일으키며—\"말해, 말해!"], ["tell me—do you hear?”", "말하란 말이야—듣고 있어?\""], ["he cried, rushing up to her and seizing her by the loose folds of her sleeve.", "그가 외치며 그녀에게 달려들어 헐렁한 소매 자락을 움켜쥐었다."]], [["The superstratum of timidity which often overlies those who are daring and defiant at heart had been passed through, and the mettlesome substance of the woman was reached.", "마음속으로는 대담하고 반항적인 이들을 흔히 덮고 있는 그 소심함의 표층이 뚫리고, 이 여자의 기개 넘치는 본바탕에 가닿았다."], ["The red blood inundated her face, previously so pale.", "앞서 그토록 창백했던 그녀의 얼굴로 붉은 피가 밀려들었다."]], [["“What are you going to do?”", "\"뭘 하려는 거예요?\""], ["she said in a low voice, regarding him with a proud smile.", "그녀가 낮은 목소리로, 당당한 미소를 띠고 그를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“You will not alarm me by holding on so; but it would be a pity to tear my sleeve.”", "\"그렇게 붙잡는다고 나를 겁줄 수는 없어요. 그래도 내 소매를 찢는 건 아까운 일이겠네요.\""]], [["Instead of letting go he drew her closer to him.", "그는 놓아주기는커녕 그녀를 자기 쪽으로 더 바싹 끌어당겼다."], ["“Tell me the particulars of—my mother’s death,”", "\"우리 어머니의—죽음의 자초지종을 말해.\""], ["he said in a hard, panting whisper; “or—I’ll—I’ll—”", "그가 모질고도 헐떡이는 속삭임으로 말했다. \"안 그러면—내가—내가—\""]], [["“Clym,”", "\"클림,\""], ["she answered slowly, “do you think you dare do anything to me that I dare not bear?", "그녀가 천천히 대답했다. \"당신은 내가 견디지 못할 짓을 나한테 감히 할 수 있을 것 같아요?"], ["But before you strike me listen.", "하지만 나를 치기 전에 들어요."], ["You will get nothing from me by a blow, even though it should kill me, as it probably will.", "나를 친다고, 설령 그게 나를 죽인다고 해도—아마 그렇게 되겠지만—당신은 나한테서 아무것도 얻지 못할 거예요."], ["But perhaps you do not wish me to speak—killing may be all you mean?”", "하지만 어쩌면 당신은 내가 말하길 바라지 않는지도 모르죠—죽이는 게 당신이 바라는 전부일지도 모르니까요?\""]], [["“Kill you!", "\"당신을 죽인다고!"], ["Do you expect it?”", "그걸 기대하나?\""]], [["“I do.”", "\"기대해요.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["“No less degree of rage against me will match your previous grief for her.”", "\"나를 향한 어떤 격분도, 그만큼 덜한 정도로는 어머니를 잃은 당신의 앞선 슬픔에 걸맞지 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Phew—I shall not kill you,”", "\"흥—나는 당신을 죽이지 않겠어.\""], ["he said contemptuously, as if under a sudden change of purpose.", "그가 갑자기 마음을 바꾼 듯 경멸하듯 말했다."], ["“I did think of it; but—I shall not.", "\"그러려고 생각은 했지만—하지 않겠어."], ["That would be making a martyr of you, and sending you to where she is; and I would keep you away from her till the universe come to an end, if I could.”", "그건 당신을 순교자로 만들어 어머니가 계신 곳으로 보내는 일일 테고, 나는 할 수만 있다면 우주가 끝장날 때까지 당신을 어머니에게서 떼어놓겠어.\""]], [["“I almost wish you would kill me,”", "\"차라리 당신이 나를 죽여주면 좋겠어요.\""], ["said she with gloomy bitterness.", "그녀가 침울한 비통함으로 말했다."], ["“It is with no strong desire, I assure you, that I play the part I have lately played on earth.", "\"장담하건대, 제가 요사이 이 땅에서 맡아온 역할을 무슨 강한 욕망에서 하고 있는 게 아니에요."], ["You are no blessing, my husband.”", "당신은 내게 축복이 아니에요, 내 남편.\""]], [["“You shut the door—you looked out of the window upon her—you had a man in the house with you—you sent her away to die.", "\"당신은 문을 닫았고—창문으로 어머니를 내다봤고—집 안에 한 남자를 들이고 있었고—어머니를 돌려보내 죽게 했어."], ["The inhumanity—the treachery—I will not touch you—stand away from me—and confess every word!”", "그 비인간성—그 배신—나는 당신한테 손대지 않겠어—내게서 떨어져—그러고는 한마디도 빠짐없이 자백해!\""]], [["“Never!", "\"절대로!"], ["I’ll hold my tongue like the very death that I don’t mind meeting, even though I can clear myself of half you believe by speaking.", "나는 그 죽음처럼 입을 다물겠어요, 비록 말함으로써 당신이 믿는 것의 절반은 결백을 밝힐 수 있다 해도, 나는 그 죽음을 만나는 것조차 두렵지 않아요."], ["Yes. I will!", "그래요, 그러겠어요!"], ["Who of any dignity would take the trouble to clear cobwebs from a wild man’s mind after such language as this?", "이런 말을 듣고서, 자존심 있는 사람이라면 누가 거친 사내의 마음에서 거미줄을 걷어내는 수고를 하겠어요?"], ["No; let him go on, and think his narrow thoughts, and run his head into the mire.", "아니요, 그가 계속하라죠, 자기 옹졸한 생각이나 하고, 진창에 머리나 처박으라죠."], ["I have other cares.”", "나한텐 다른 근심이 있으니까요.\""]], [["“’Tis too much—but I must spare you.”", "\"너무하군—하지만 당신을 봐줘야겠어.\""]], [["“Poor charity.”", "\"가엾은 자비로군요.\""]], [["“By my wretched soul you sting me, Eustacia!", "\"이 비참한 내 영혼을 걸고, 당신은 나를 찌르는군, 유스테이샤!"], ["I can keep it up, and hotly too.", "나는 계속할 수 있어, 그것도 격렬하게."], ["Now, then, madam, tell me his name!”", "자, 그럼, 부인, 그자의 이름을 말해!\""]], [["“Never, I am resolved.”", "\"절대로, 나는 결심했어요.\""]], [["“How often does he write to you?", "\"그자가 당신한테 얼마나 자주 편지를 쓰지?"], ["Where does he put his letters—when does he meet you?", "어디다 그 편지를 두지—언제 당신을 만나지?"], ["Ah, his letters!", "아, 그자의 편지들!"], ["Do you tell me his name?”", "그자의 이름을 말해줄 텐가?\""]], [["“I do not.”", "\"안 해요.\""]], [["“Then I’ll find it myself.”", "\"그럼 내가 직접 찾아내지.\""], ["His eyes had fallen upon a small desk that stood near, on which she was accustomed to write her letters.", "그의 눈이 그녀가 편지를 쓰곤 하던, 근처에 놓인 작은 책상에 가닿았다. 그가 그리로 갔다."], ["He went to it. It was locked.", "책상은 잠겨 있었다."]], [["“Unlock this!”", "\"이걸 열어!\""]], [["“You have no right to say it.", "\"당신한텐 그렇게 말할 권리가 없어요."], ["That’s mine.”", "그건 내 거예요.\""]], [["Without another word he seized the desk and dashed it to the floor.", "그는 더 말하지 않고 책상을 움켜쥐어 바닥에 내동댕이쳤다."], ["The hinge burst open, and a number of letters tumbled out.", "경첩이 떨어져 나가며 여러 통의 편지가 쏟아져 나왔다."]], [["“Stay!”", "\"멈춰요!\""], ["said Eustacia, stepping before him with more excitement than she had hitherto shown.", "유스테이샤가 지금까지 보였던 것보다 더한 흥분에 차서 그의 앞을 가로막으며 말했다."]], [["“Come, come!", "\"이리, 이리!"], ["stand away!", "비켜!"], ["I must see them.”", "내가 봐야겠어.\""]], [["She looked at the letters as they lay, checked her feeling and moved indifferently aside; when he gathered them up, and examined them.", "그녀는 놓여 있는 편지들을 바라보다가 감정을 억누르고 무심한 듯 옆으로 비켜섰으며, 그러자 그가 편지들을 그러모아 살펴보았다."]], [["By no stretch of meaning could any but a harmless construction be placed upon a single one of the letters themselves.", "편지 그 자체로는, 어떻게 의미를 늘여 잡아도 단 한 통에조차 해 될 것 없는 해석 말고는 달리 붙일 수가 없었다."], ["The solitary exception was an empty envelope directed to her, and the handwriting was Wildeve’s.", "단 하나의 예외는 그녀 앞으로 부쳐진 빈 봉투였는데, 필적은 와일디브의 것이었다."], ["Yeobright held it up.", "요브라이트가 그것을 들어 올렸다."], ["Eustacia was doggedly silent.", "유스테이샤는 완강하게 입을 다물었다."]], [["“Can you read, madam?", "\"글을 읽을 줄 아오, 부인?"], ["Look at this envelope.", "이 봉투를 보시오."], ["Doubtless we shall find more soon, and what was inside them.", "분명 우리는 곧 더 많은 걸, 그 안에 들어 있던 걸 찾아내게 되겠지."], ["I shall no doubt be gratified by learning in good time what a well-finished and full-blown adept in a certain trade my lady is.”", "나는 우리 마님께서 어떤 장사에서 얼마나 완성도 높고 만개한 명수名手이신지를 머지않아 알게 되어 틀림없이 흡족하겠군.\""]], [["“Do you say it to me—do you?”", "\"나한테 그런 말을 해요—그러는 거예요?\""], ["she gasped.", "그녀가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."]], [["He searched further, but found nothing more.", "그는 더 뒤졌으나 더는 아무것도 찾지 못했다."], ["“What was in this letter?”", "\"이 편지엔 뭐가 들어 있었지?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Ask the writer.", "\"쓴 사람한테 물어봐요."], ["Am I your hound that you should talk to me in this way?”", "내가 당신 사냥개라서 나한테 이런 식으로 말하는 거예요?\""]], [["“Do you brave me?", "\"나한테 맞서는 거요?"], ["do you stand me out, mistress?", "나한테 버티는 거요, 마님?"], ["Answer.", "대답해."], ["Don’t look at me with those eyes if you would bewitch me again!", "또다시 나를 홀리려거든 그 눈으로 나를 보지 마!"], ["Sooner than that I die.", "그러느니 차라리 내가 죽겠어."], ["You refuse to answer?”", "대답하기를 거부하나?\""]], [["“I wouldn’t tell you after this, if I were as innocent as the sweetest babe in heaven!”", "\"이런 일이 있고서는, 내가 천국에서 가장 사랑스러운 갓난아기만큼 결백하다 해도, 당신한테는 말하지 않겠어요!\""]], [["“Which you are not.”", "\"당신은 그렇지 않은데도 말이지.\""]], [["“Certainly I am not absolutely,”", "\"전적으로 결백한 건 아니죠, 분명히.\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“I have not done what you suppose; but if to have done no harm at all is the only innocence recognized, I am beyond forgiveness.", "\"당신이 짐작하는 짓은 하지 않았어요. 하지만 아무런 해도 끼치지 않은 것만이 인정받는 결백이라면, 나는 용서받을 길이 없겠죠."], ["But I require no help from your conscience.”", "하지만 나는 당신 양심의 도움 따윈 필요 없어요.\""]], [["“You can resist, and resist again!", "\"당신은 버티고, 또 버틸 수 있군!"], ["Instead of hating you I could, I think, mourn for and pity you, if you were contrite, and would confess all.", "당신이 뉘우치고 모든 걸 자백하기만 한다면, 나는 당신을 미워하기는커녕 슬퍼하고 가엾이 여길 수도 있을 것 같소."], ["Forgive you I never can.", "하지만 용서는 결코 할 수 없어."], ["I don’t speak of your lover—I will give you the benefit of the doubt in that matter, for it only affects me personally.", "당신의 정부情夫 얘기를 하는 게 아니오—그 문제는 나 개인에게만 관계되는 일이니 의심의 이득은 당신에게 주리다."], ["But the other—had you half-killed _me_, had it been that you wilfully took the sight away from these feeble eyes of mine, I could have forgiven you.", "하지만 다른 문제는—당신이 나를 반쯤 죽였다 해도, 당신이 일부러 이 약한 두 눈에서 시력을 앗아갔다 해도, 나는 당신을 용서할 수 있었을 거요."], ["But _that’s_ too much for nature!”", "하지만 그건 사람의 본성으론 너무하단 말이오!\""]], [["“Say no more.", "\"더 말하지 마요."], ["I will do without your pity.", "나는 당신의 동정 없이도 살아갈게요."], ["But I would have saved you from uttering what you will regret.”", "하지만 나는 당신이 후회할 말을 내뱉지 않도록 막아주고 싶었어요.\""]], [["“I am going away now.", "\"나는 이제 떠나겠어."], ["I shall leave you.”", "당신을 두고 가겠어.\""]], [["“You need not go, as I am going myself.", "\"당신이 갈 필요 없어요, 내가 직접 갈 테니까요."], ["You will keep just as far away from me by staying here.”", "여기 머물든 떠나든 당신은 나한테서 꼭 그만큼 멀리 떨어져 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Call her to mind—think of her—what goodness there was in her—it showed in every line of her face!", "\"어머니를 떠올려봐—생각해봐—어머니 안에 얼마나 많은 선함이 있었는지를—그게 얼굴 한 줄 한 줄마다 드러났었지!"], ["Most women, even when but slightly annoyed, show a flicker of evil in some curl of the mouth or some corner of the cheek; but as for her, never in her angriest moments was there anything malicious in her look.", "대부분의 여자는 살짝만 짜증이 나도 입가의 어떤 비틀림이나 뺨의 어떤 구석에서 악의의 깜박임을 드러내는데, 어머니로 말하자면, 가장 화나신 순간에도 그 눈빛엔 악의라곤 결코 없었어."], ["She was angered quickly, but she forgave just as readily, and underneath her pride there was the meekness of a child.", "어머니는 금세 화를 내셨지만 그만큼 선뜻 용서하셨고, 그 자존심 밑에는 아이 같은 온순함이 있었지."], ["What came of it?—what cared you?", "그런데 결과가 어찌 되었소?—당신이 무얼 마음에 두었소?"], ["You hated her just as she was learning to love you. O!", "어머니가 막 당신을 사랑하려던 참에 당신은 어머니를 미워했어."], ["couldn’t you see what was best for you, but must bring a curse upon me, and agony and death upon her, by doing that cruel deed!", "오! 당신은 무엇이 당신한테 최선인지를 보지 못하고, 기어이 그 잔인한 짓을 저질러 나한테는 저주를, 어머니한테는 고통과 죽음을 안겨주어야 했단 말이오!"], ["What was the fellow’s name who was keeping you company and causing you to add cruelty to her to your wrong to me?", "당신과 한패가 되어 당신이 나한테 한 잘못에 어머니한테까지 잔인하게 굴도록 부추긴 그 작자의 이름이 뭐였소?"], ["Was it Wildeve?", "와일디브였소?"], ["Was it poor Thomasin’s husband?", "가엾은 토머신의 남편이었소?"], ["Heaven, what wickedness!", "맙소사, 이 무슨 사악함이오!"], ["Lost your voice, have you?", "목소리를 잃었소?"], ["It is natural after detection of that most noble trick....", "그 더없이 고결한 농간이 들통난 뒤이니 당연하겠지…."], ["Eustacia, didn’t any tender thought of your own mother lead you to think of being gentle to mine at such a time of weariness?", "유스테이샤, 당신 자신의 어머니에 대한 다정한 생각이 그토록 지친 때에 우리 어머니한테 부드럽게 대하도록 당신을 이끌지 못했소?"], ["Did not one grain of pity enter your heart as she turned away?", "어머니가 돌아서실 때 한 톨의 연민도 당신 마음에 들어오지 않았소?"], ["Think what a vast opportunity was then lost of beginning a forgiving and honest course.", "그때 용서하고 정직한 길로 들어설 그 얼마나 큰 기회를 놓쳤는지 생각해보시오."], ["Why did not you kick him out, and let her in, and say I’ll be an honest wife and a noble woman from this hour?", "왜 그자를 걷어차 내쫓고 어머니를 들이며, 이 순간부터 정직한 아내요 고결한 여자가 되겠다고 말하지 않았소?"], ["Had I told you to go and quench eternally our last flickering chance of happiness here you could have done no worse.", "내가 당신한테 우리의 마지막 깜박이는 행복의 기회를 여기서 영원히 꺼버리라고 시켰다 해도 당신은 이보다 더 나쁘게 할 수 없었을 거요."], ["Well, she’s asleep now; and have you a hundred gallants, neither they nor you can insult her any more.”", "자, 어머니는 이제 잠들어 계시니, 당신한테 백 명의 정부가 있다 한들 그들도 당신도 더는 어머니를 모욕할 수 없소.\""]], [["“You exaggerate fearfully,”", "\"당신은 끔찍하게 과장하고 있어요.\""], ["she said in a faint, weary voice; “but I cannot enter into my defence—it is not worth doing.", "그녀가 희미하고 지친 목소리로 말했다. \"하지만 나는 변명에 나설 수가 없어요—그럴 가치가 없으니까요."], ["You are nothing to me in future, and the past side of the story may as well remain untold.", "당신은 앞으로 내게 아무것도 아니고, 그 이야기의 지나간 쪽은 끝내 말하지 않은 채로 두는 게 차라리 낫겠어요."], ["I have lost all through you, but I have not complained.", "나는 당신 때문에 모든 걸 잃었지만 불평하지 않았어요."], ["Your blunders and misfortunes may have been a sorrow to you, but they have been a wrong to me.", "당신의 실책과 불운은 당신한테는 슬픔이었을지 몰라도 나한테는 부당함이었어요."], ["All persons of refinement have been scared away from me since I sank into the mire of marriage.", "내가 결혼이라는 진창에 빠진 뒤로 교양 있는 사람들은 죄다 나한테서 겁먹고 달아나버렸어요."], ["Is this your cherishing—to put me into a hut like this, and keep me like the wife of a hind?", "이게 당신의 보살핌인가요—나를 이런 오두막에 처박아 농사꾼의 아내처럼 가두는 게?"], ["You deceived me—not by words, but by appearances, which are less seen through than words.", "당신은 나를 속였어요—말로가 아니라 겉모습으로, 말보다 덜 꿰뚫어 보이는 겉모습으로요."], ["But the place will serve as well as any other—as somewhere to pass from—into my grave.”", "하지만 이곳도 다른 어떤 곳 못지않게 쓸모가 있겠죠—거쳐 가는 어딘가로—내 무덤으로 가기 위한.\""], ["Her words were smothered in her throat, and her head drooped down.", "그녀의 말이 목구멍에서 짓눌리고, 그녀의 고개가 떨구어졌다."]], [["“I don’t know what you mean by that.", "\"그게 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠군."], ["Am I the cause of your sin?”", "내가 당신 죄의 원인이란 말이오?\""], ["(Eustacia made a trembling motion towards him.) “What, you can begin to shed tears and offer me your hand?", "(유스테이샤가 그를 향해 떨리는 동작으로 다가섰다.) \"뭐, 당신이 눈물을 흘리며 나한테 손을 내밀려는 거요? 맙소사!"], ["Good God!", "당신이?"], ["can you? No, not I.", "안 돼, 나는 안 받아."], ["I’ll not commit the fault of taking that.”", "그 손을 잡는 잘못은 저지르지 않겠어.\""], ["(The hand she had offered dropped nervelessly, but the tears continued flowing.) “Well, yes, I’ll take it, if only for the sake of my own foolish kisses that were wasted there before I knew what I cherished.", "(그녀가 내밀었던 손이 맥없이 떨어졌으나, 눈물은 계속 흘렀다.) \"음, 그래, 잡지, 단지 내가 무얼 소중히 여기는지도 모른 채 저기서 허비해버린 그 어리석은 입맞춤들을 봐서라도."], ["How bewitched I was!", "내가 얼마나 홀려 있었던가!"], ["How could there be any good in a woman that everybody spoke ill of?”", "모두가 험담하던 여자한테 어찌 선함이 있을 수 있었겠소?\""]], [["“O, O, O!”", "\"오, 오, 오!\""], ["she cried, breaking down at last; and, shaking with sobs which choked her, she sank upon her knees.", "그녀가 마침내 무너지며 부르짖었으며, 목이 메도록 흐느낌에 떨면서 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉았다."], ["“O, will you have done!", "\"오, 이제 그만해줘요!"], ["O, you are too relentless—there’s a limit to the cruelty of savages!", "오, 당신은 너무 무자비해요—야만인의 잔인함에도 한계가 있어요!"], ["I have held out long—but you crush me down.", "나는 오래 버텼어요—그런데 당신이 나를 짓밟아 눕히는군요."], ["I beg for mercy—I cannot bear this any longer—it is inhuman to go further with this!", "자비를 빌어요—더는 이걸 견딜 수가 없어요—이걸 더 끌고 가는 건 사람의 짓이 아니에요!"], ["If I had—killed your—mother with my own hand—I should not deserve such a scourging to the bone as this.", "내가 당신의—어머니를—내 손으로—죽였다 해도—이렇게 뼛속까지 후려치는 매질을 받을 만하진 않을 거예요. 오, 오!"], ["O, O! God have mercy upon a miserable woman!...", "하느님, 이 비참한 여자에게 자비를 베푸소서!…"], ["You have beaten me in this game—I beg you to stay your hand in pity!...", "당신은 이 시합에서 나를 이겼어요—제발 가엾이 여겨 손을 멈춰줘요!…"], ["I confess that I—wilfully did not undo the door the first time she knocked—but—I should have unfastened it the second—if I had not thought you had gone to do it yourself.", "고백할게요, 내가—그분이 처음 문을 두드렸을 때 일부러 문을 열지 않았다는 걸—하지만—두 번째엔 열었을 거예요—당신이 직접 열러 가신 줄 알지 않았더라면요."], ["When I found you had not I opened it, but she was gone.", "당신이 안 그랬다는 걸 알았을 때 내가 문을 열었지만, 그분은 이미 가버리셨어요."], ["That’s the extent of my crime—towards _her_.", "그게 내 죄의 전부예요—그분한테 지은."], ["Best natures commit bad faults sometimes, don’t they?—I think they do.", "더없이 선한 본성도 때로는 나쁜 잘못을 저지르잖아요, 안 그래요?—그렇다고 생각해요."], ["Now I will leave you—for ever and ever!”", "이제 나는 당신을 떠나겠어요—영원히, 영원히!\""]], [["“Tell all, and I _will_ pity you.", "\"전부 말하시오, 그러면 내가 당신을 가엾이 여기리다."], ["Was the man in the house with you Wildeve?”", "당신과 함께 집 안에 있던 남자가 와일디브였소?\""]], [["“I cannot tell,”", "\"말 못 해요.\""], ["she said desperately through her sobbing.", "그녀가 흐느낌 사이로 절박하게 말했다."], ["“Don’t insist further—I cannot tell.", "\"더 다그치지 마요—말 못 해요."], ["I am going from this house.", "나는 이 집을 떠날 거예요."], ["We cannot both stay here.”", "우리 둘 다 여기 머물 수는 없어요.\""]], [["“You need not go—I will go.", "\"당신은 갈 필요 없소—내가 가겠어."], ["You can stay here.”", "당신은 여기 있어도 돼.\""]], [["“No, I will dress, and then I will go.”", "\"아뇨, 옷을 차려입고서 내가 가겠어요.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디로?\""]], [["“Where I came from, or _else_where.”", "\"내가 온 곳으로, 아니면 다른 어딘가로요.\""]], [["She hastily dressed herself, Yeobright moodily walking up and down the room the whole of the time.", "그녀는 서둘러 옷을 차려입었고, 요브라이트는 그동안 내내 침울하게 방안을 서성였다."], ["At last all her things were on.", "마침내 그녀의 차림이 다 갖춰졌다."], ["Her little hands quivered so violently as she held them to her chin to fasten her bonnet that she could not tie the strings, and after a few moments she relinquished the attempt.", "보닛을 매려고 턱에 갖다 댄 그녀의 작은 두 손이 너무도 격렬하게 떨려 끈을 묶을 수가 없었고, 몇 순간이 지나자 그녀는 그 시도를 단념했다."], ["Seeing this he moved forward and said, “Let me tie them.”", "이를 본 그가 다가서며 말했다. \"내가 묶어주지.\""]], [["She assented in silence, and lifted her chin.", "그녀가 말없이 응하고는 턱을 들어올렸다."], ["For once at least in her life she was totally oblivious of the charm of her attitude.", "그녀의 일생에서 적어도 한 번은, 그녀가 자기 자세의 매력을 까맣게 잊고 있었다."], ["But he was not, and he turned his eyes aside, that he might not be tempted to softness.", "하지만 그는 그렇지 않았기에, 부드러움에 빠져들 유혹을 받지 않으려고 눈을 옆으로 돌렸다."]], [["The strings were tied; she turned from him.", "끈이 묶였고, 그녀가 그에게서 돌아섰다."], ["“Do you still prefer going away yourself to my leaving you?”", "\"내가 당신을 떠나기보다는 여전히 당신이 직접 떠나기를 바라오?\""], ["he inquired again.", "그가 다시 물었다."]], [["“I do.”", "\"바라요.\""]], [["“Very well—let it be.", "\"좋소—그렇게 하지."], ["And when you will confess to the man I may pity you.”", "그리고 당신이 그 남자에게 자백하거든 그때는 당신을 가엾이 여길지도 모르오.\""]], [["She flung her shawl about her and went downstairs, leaving him standing in the room.", "그녀가 숄을 두르고 아래층으로 내려갔으며, 그는 방안에 선 채로 남겨졌다."]], [["Eustacia had not long been gone when there came a knock at the door of the bedroom; and Yeobright said, “Well?”", "유스테이샤가 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않아 침실 문에서 노크 소리가 났고, 요브라이트가 말했다. \"무슨 일인가?\""]], [["It was the servant; and she replied, “Somebody from Mrs.", "하녀였고, 그녀가 대답했다."], ["Wildeve’s have called to tell ’ee that the mis’ess and the baby are getting on wonderful well, and the baby’s name is to be Eustacia Clementine.”", "\"와일디브 부인 댁에서 누가 와서 전하라고 했는데요, 마님과 아기가 놀라우리만치 잘 지내고 있고, 아기 이름은 유스테이샤 클레먼타인이 될 거래요.\""], ["And the girl retired.", "그러고는 소녀가 물러갔다."]], [["“What a mockery!” said Clym.", "\"이런 조롱이 다 있나!\""], ["“This unhappy marriage of mine to be perpetuated in that child’s name!”", "클림이 말했다. \"내 이 불행한 결혼이 그 아이의 이름으로 영원히 이어지다니!\""]], [["IV. The Ministrations of a Half-forgotten One\nEustacia’s journey was at first as vague in direction as that of thistledown on the wind.", "유스테이샤의 여정은 처음엔 바람에 날리는 엉겅퀴 솜털만큼이나 방향이 모호했다."], ["She did not know what to do.", "그녀는 무엇을 해야 할지 몰랐다."], ["She wished it had been night instead of morning, that she might at least have borne her misery without the possibility of being seen.", "그녀는 차라리 아침이 아니라 밤이었더라면 좋았으리라 바랐으니, 그러면 적어도 남에게 보일 가능성 없이 자기 비참함을 견딜 수 있었을 것이다."], ["Tracing mile after mile along between the dying ferns and the wet white spiders’ webs, she at length turned her steps towards her grandfather’s house.", "죽어가는 양치류와 젖은 흰 거미줄 사이로 한 마일 또 한 마일을 더듬어 가며, 그녀는 마침내 할아버지의 집으로 발길을 돌렸다."], ["She found the front door closed and locked.", "앞문이 닫혀 잠겨 있는 것을 그녀는 발견했다."], ["Mechanically she went round to the end where the stable was, and on looking in at the stable door she saw Charley standing within.", "기계적으로 그녀는 마구간이 있는 끝 쪽으로 돌아갔고, 마구간 문 안을 들여다보니 찰리가 안에 서 있는 것이 보였다."]], [["“Captain Vye is not at home?”", "\"바이 대장님은 댁에 안 계시니?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“No, ma’am,”", "\"네, 마님.\""], ["said the lad in a flutter of feeling; “he’s gone to Weatherbury, and won’t be home till night.", "소년이 마음의 동요에 휩싸여 말했다. \"웨더베리에 가셨는데, 밤이 되어야 돌아오실 거예요."], ["And the servant is gone home for a holiday.", "그리고 하인은 휴가로 집에 갔고요."], ["So the house is locked up.”", "그래서 집은 잠겨 있어요.\""]], [["Eustacia’s face was not visible to Charley as she stood at the doorway, her back being to the sky, and the stable but indifferently lighted; but the wildness of her manner arrested his attention.", "유스테이샤가 문간에 서 있어 등 뒤로 하늘을 두고 있었고 마구간도 어슴푸레하게밖에 밝지 않았던 까닭에, 그녀의 얼굴은 찰리에게 보이지 않았으나, 그녀의 거친 태도가 그의 주의를 사로잡았다."], ["She turned and walked away across the enclosure to the gate, and was hidden by the bank.", "그녀는 돌아서서 울안을 가로질러 문 쪽으로 걸어가더니 둑에 가려 보이지 않게 되었다."]], [["When she had disappeared Charley, with misgiving in his eyes, slowly came from the stable door, and going to another point in the bank he looked over.", "그녀가 사라지자 찰리는 눈에 불안을 담고서 천천히 마구간 문에서 나와, 둑의 다른 지점으로 가서 너머를 살펴보았다."], ["Eustacia was leaning against it on the outside, her face covered with her hands, and her head pressing the dewy heather which bearded the bank’s outer side.", "유스테이샤가 바깥쪽에서 둑에 기대어 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채, 둑의 바깥쪽 면에 수염처럼 돋은 이슬 젖은 히스에 머리를 짓누르고 있었다."], ["She appeared to be utterly indifferent to the circumstance that her bonnet, hair, and garments were becoming wet and disarranged by the moisture of her cold, harsh pillow.", "그녀는 자기 보닛과 머리와 옷가지가 그 차갑고 거친 베개의 습기에 젖어 헝클어지고 있다는 사정에 완전히 무심한 듯 보였다."], ["Clearly something was wrong.", "분명 무언가 잘못된 것이었다."]], [["Charley had always regarded Eustacia as Eustacia had regarded Clym when she first beheld him—as a romantic and sweet vision, scarcely incarnate.", "찰리는 늘 유스테이샤를, 유스테이샤가 클림을 처음 보았을 때 그를 여겼던 것과 똑같이 여겨왔으니—거의 육신을 입지 않은, 낭만적이고 감미로운 환영으로 여겼던 것이다."], ["He had been so shut off from her by the dignity of her look and the pride of her speech, except at that one blissful interval when he was allowed to hold her hand, that he had hardly deemed her a woman, wingless and earthly, subject to household conditions and domestic jars.", "그녀의 손을 잡도록 허락받았던 그 단 한 번의 더없이 행복한 시간을 빼고는, 그녀의 눈빛이 지닌 위엄과 말투의 자존심에 가로막혀 그녀와 너무도 단절되어 있었던 까닭에, 그는 그녀를 살림살이의 조건과 가정의 불화에 매인, 날개도 없고 지상의 것인 여자라고는 거의 여기지 않았다."], ["The inner details of her life he had only conjectured.", "그녀의 삶의 내밀한 속사정은 그저 짐작만 했을 뿐이었다."], ["She had been a lovely wonder, predestined to an orbit in which the whole of his own was but a point; and this sight of her leaning like a helpless, despairing creature against a wild wet bank filled him with an amazed horror.", "그녀는 사랑스러운 경이驚異였으며, 그 자신의 삶 전부가 한낱 점에 지나지 않는 어떤 궤도를 돌도록 예정된 존재였으니, 거칠고 젖은 둑에 무력하고 절망한 피조물처럼 기대어 있는 그녀의 이 모습이 그를 아연한 공포로 가득 채웠다."], ["He could no longer remain where he was.", "그는 더는 있던 자리에 머물러 있을 수 없었다."], ["Leaping over, he came up, touched her with his finger, and said tenderly, “You are poorly, ma’am.", "뛰어넘어 다가가서, 그가 손가락으로 그녀를 건드리며 부드럽게 말했다. \"편찮으신가 봐요, 마님."], ["What can I do?”", "제가 뭘 해드릴까요?\""]], [["Eustacia started up, and said, “Ah, Charley—you have followed me.", "유스테이샤가 흠칫 일어나며 말했다. \"아, 찰리—네가 나를 따라왔구나."], ["You did not think when I left home in the summer that I should come back like this!”", "내가 여름에 집을 떠날 때 이런 꼴로 돌아오리라고는 너도 생각 못 했겠지!\""]], [["“I did not, dear ma’am.", "\"못 했어요, 마님."], ["Can I help you now?”", "지금 제가 도와드릴 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“I am afraid not.", "\"안 될 것 같구나."], ["I wish I could get into the house.", "집 안으로 들어갈 수 있으면 좋겠는데."], ["I feel giddy—that’s all.”", "어지러워서—그뿐이야.\""]], [["“Lean on my arm, ma’am, till we get to the porch, and I will try to open the door.”", "\"제 팔을 잡으세요, 마님, 현관까지 가게요, 그러면 제가 문을 열어보겠어요.\""]], [["He supported her to the porch, and there depositing her on a seat hastened to the back, climbed to a window by the help of a ladder, and descending inside opened the door.", "그는 그녀를 부축해 현관까지 데려가, 그곳 자리에 그녀를 앉히고는 서둘러 뒤편으로 가서 사다리를 디뎌 창문으로 올라가, 안으로 내려가 문을 열었다."], ["Next he assisted her into the room, where there was an old-fashioned horsehair settee as large as a donkey wagon.", "다음으로 그는 그녀를 부축해 방으로 들여보냈는데, 그곳에는 당나귀 짐수레만큼이나 큼직한 옛날식 말총 긴 의자가 있었다."], ["She lay down here, and Charley covered her with a cloak he found in the hall.", "그녀는 거기 누웠고, 찰리는 현관에서 찾아낸 외투로 그녀를 덮어주었다."]], [["“Shall I get you something to eat and drink?”", "\"뭐 먹고 마실 걸 가져다드릴까요?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“If you please, Charley.", "\"그래주렴, 찰리."], ["But I suppose there is no fire?”", "그런데 불이 없겠지?\""]], [["“I can light it, ma’am.”", "\"제가 피울 수 있어요, 마님.\""]], [["He vanished, and she heard a splitting of wood and a blowing of bellows; and presently he returned, saying, “I have lighted a fire in the kitchen, and now I’ll light one here.”", "그가 사라지자 그녀는 장작 쪼개는 소리와 풀무 부는 소리를 들었고, 이윽고 그가 돌아와 말했다. \"부엌에 불을 피웠고, 이제 여기도 피울게요.\""]], [["He lit the fire, Eustacia dreamily observing him from her couch.", "그가 불을 피웠고, 유스테이샤는 자기 의자에서 몽롱하게 그를 바라보았다."], ["When it was blazing up he said, “Shall I wheel you round in front of it, ma’am, as the morning is chilly?”", "불이 활활 타오르자 그가 말했다. \"아침이 쌀쌀하니, 제가 마님을 불 앞으로 돌려 모실까요?\""]], [["“Yes, if you like.”", "\"그래, 네가 그러고 싶으면.\""]], [["“Shall I go and bring the victuals now?”", "\"이제 가서 음식을 가져올까요?\""]], [["“Yes, do,”", "\"그래, 그러렴.\""], ["she murmured languidly.", "그녀가 나른하게 중얼거렸다."]], [["When he had gone, and the dull sounds occasionally reached her ears of his movements in the kitchen, she forgot where she was, and had for a moment to consider by an effort what the sounds meant.", "그가 가고 난 뒤, 부엌에서 그가 움직이는 둔탁한 소리가 이따금 그녀의 귀에 닿았으나, 그녀는 자기가 어디에 있는지 잊어, 잠시 동안 애써 그 소리가 무얼 뜻하는지 헤아려야 했다."], ["After an interval which seemed short to her whose thoughts were elsewhere, he came in with a tray on which steamed tea and toast, though it was nearly lunch-time.", "마음이 딴 데 가 있는 그녀에게는 짧게 느껴진 한참 후에, 그가 김이 모락모락 나는 차와 토스트를 얹은 쟁반을 들고 들어왔으니, 점심때가 다 되어 있었다."]], [["“Place it on the table,”", "\"탁자에 놓아두렴.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I shall be ready soon.”", "\"곧 준비할게.\""]], [["He did so, and retired to the door; when, however, he perceived that she did not move he came back a few steps.", "그가 그렇게 하고는 문 쪽으로 물러났으나, 그녀가 움직이지 않는 것을 알아채고는 몇 걸음 되돌아왔다."]], [["“Let me hold it to you, if you don’t wish to get up,”", "\"일어나기 싫으시면, 제가 들고 있을게요.\""], ["said Charley.", "찰리가 말했다."], ["He brought the tray to the front of the couch, where he knelt down, adding, “I will hold it for you.”", "그는 쟁반을 긴 의자 앞으로 가져와 거기 무릎을 꿇으며 덧붙였다. \"제가 마님 대신 들고 있을게요.\""]], [["Eustacia sat up and poured out a cup of tea.", "유스테이샤가 일어나 앉아 찻잔에 차를 따랐다."], ["“You are very kind to me, Charley,”", "\"넌 나한테 참 친절하구나, 찰리.\""], ["she murmured as she sipped.", "그녀가 차를 홀짝이며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Well, I ought to be,”", "\"음, 마땅히 그래야지요.\""], ["said he diffidently, taking great trouble not to rest his eyes upon her, though this was their only natural position, Eustacia being immediately before him.", "그가 수줍게, 유스테이샤가 바로 앞에 있었으니 그게 두 눈의 유일하게 자연스러운 자리였는데도, 그녀에게 눈길을 두지 않으려고 무진 애를 쓰며 말했다."], ["“You have been kind to me.”", "\"마님은 저한테 친절하셨잖아요.\""]], [["“How have I?”", "\"내가 어떻게 그랬는데?\""], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 말했다."]], [["“You let me hold your hand when you were a maiden at home.”", "\"마님이 처녀로 집에 계실 때 제가 마님 손을 잡게 해주셨잖아요.\""]], [["“Ah, so I did.", "\"아, 그랬지."], ["Why did I do that?", "내가 왜 그랬을까?"], ["My mind is lost—it had to do with the mumming, had it not?”", "정신이 다 나가버렸네—그 가면극하고 관련된 거였지, 아니었니?\""]], [["“Yes, you wanted to go in my place.”", "\"네, 마님이 제 대신 들어가고 싶어 하셨어요.\""]], [["“I remember.", "\"기억나."], ["I do indeed remember—too well!”", "정말이지 기억나—너무도 또렷하게!\""]], [["She again became utterly downcast; and Charley, seeing that she was not going to eat or drink any more, took away the tray.", "그녀는 다시 완전히 풀이 죽어버렸고, 찰리는 그녀가 더는 먹지도 마시지도 않으리라는 것을 보고서 쟁반을 치웠다."]], [["Afterwards he occasionally came in to see if the fire was burning, to ask her if she wanted anything, to tell her that the wind had shifted from south to west, to ask her if she would like him to gather her some blackberries; to all which inquiries she replied in the negative or with indifference.", "그 후 그는 이따금 들어와서 불이 잘 타고 있는지 살피고, 그녀에게 무언가 필요한 게 있는지 묻고, 바람이 남쪽에서 서쪽으로 바뀌었다고 알려주고, 검은딸기를 좀 따다 드릴까 물었으나, 이 모든 물음에 그녀는 아니라고 하거나 무심하게 대꾸했다."]], [["She remained on the settee some time longer, when she aroused herself and went upstairs.", "그녀는 그 긴 의자에 한참을 더 머물러 있다가, 정신을 차려 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["The room in which she had formerly slept still remained much as she had left it, and the recollection that this forced upon her of her own greatly changed and infinitely worsened situation again set on her face the undetermined and formless misery which it had worn on her first arrival.", "예전에 그녀가 자던 방은 그녀가 떠날 때와 거의 그대로였으며, 이것이 그녀에게 강제로 떠올리게 한, 자신이 처한 크게 달라지고 한없이 나빠진 상황에 대한 회상이, 처음 도착했을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 어렸던 그 막연하고 형체 없는 비참함을 그녀의 얼굴에 다시 새겨놓았다."], ["She peeped into her grandfather’s room, through which the fresh autumn air was blowing from the open window.", "그녀는 할아버지의 방을 들여다보았는데, 열린 창문으로 신선한 가을 공기가 그 방을 가로질러 불어들고 있었다."], ["Her eye was arrested by what was a familiar sight enough, though it broke upon her now with a new significance.", "그녀의 눈길이, 익히 친숙한 광경이긴 했으나"]], [["It was a brace of pistols, hanging near the head of her grandfather’s bed, which he always kept there loaded, as a precaution against possible burglars, the house being very lonely.", "이제 그녀에게 새로운 의미를 띠고 다가왔다. 그것은 한 쌍의 권총으로, 할아버지의 침대 머리맡에 걸려 있었으며, 집이 워낙 외진 탓에 침입자가 들지도 모른다는 대비책으로 늘 장전해둔 채 그곳에 두던 것이었다."], ["Eustacia regarded them long, as if they were the page of a book in which she read a new and a strange matter.", "유스테이샤는 마치 새롭고 낯선 사연이 적힌 책장이라도 들여다보듯 그것을 오랫동안 바라보았다."], ["Quickly, like one afraid of herself, she returned downstairs and stood in deep thought.", "이윽고 제 자신이 두렵기라도 한 사람처럼 황급히 아래층으로 내려와 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 섰다."]], [["“If I could only do it!”", "\"내가 그걸 할 수만 있다면!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“It would be doing much good to myself and all connected with me, and no harm to a single one.”", "\"나 자신에게도, 나와 얽힌 모든 사람에게도 크게 이로울 것이요, 어느 누구에게도 해가 되지 않을 텐데.\""]], [["The idea seemed to gather force within her, and she remained in a fixed attitude nearly ten minutes, when a certain finality was expressed in her gaze, and no longer the blankness of indecision.", "그 생각은 그녀 안에서 힘을 키워가는 듯했고, 그녀는 거의 십 분 가까이 한자리에 붙박인 채 서 있었는데, 마침내 그녀의 눈빛에는 더 이상 결단을 못 내리는 멍한 기색이 아니라 어떤 확고한 결말이 어려 있었다."]], [["She turned and went up the second time—softly and stealthily now—and entered her grandfather’s room, her eyes at once seeking the head of the bed.", "그녀는 돌아서서 두 번째로 위층에 올라갔다—이번에는 살그머니 몰래—할아버지의 방으로 들어서며 즉시 침대 머리맡으로 눈길을 보냈다."], ["The pistols were gone.", "권총은 사라지고 없었다."]], [["The instant quashing of her purpose by their absence affected her brain as a sudden vacuum affects the body—she nearly fainted.", "그것이 없어 자신의 의도가 단숨에 좌절되자 마치 갑작스러운 진공이 몸에 미치듯 그녀의 머릿속도 영향을 받아—그녀는 거의 정신을 잃을 뻔했다."], ["Who had done this?", "누가 이런 짓을 했단 말인가?"], ["There was only one person on the premises besides herself.", "이 집 안에 그녀 말고 사람은 단 한 명뿐이었다."], ["Eustacia involuntarily turned to the open window which overlooked the garden as far as the bank that bounded it.", "유스테이샤는 자기도 모르게 정원을, 정원을 두른 둑까지 내려다보이는 열린 창문 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["On the summit of the latter stood Charley, sufficiently elevated by its height to see into the room.", "그 둑 꼭대기에 찰리가 서 있었는데, 둑의 높이 덕분에 방 안이 들여다보일 만큼 충분히 올라가 있었다."], ["His gaze was directed eagerly and solicitously upon her.", "그의 시선은 간절하고 근심 어린 빛으로 그녀에게 쏠려 있었다."]], [["She went downstairs to the door and beckoned to him.", "그녀는 아래층 문가로 내려가 그에게 오라고 손짓했다."]], [["“You have taken them away?”", "\"네가 그것들을 치웠지?\""]], [["“Yes, ma’am.”", "\"예, 아씨.\""]], [["“Why did you do it?”", "\"왜 그랬느냐?\""]], [["“I saw you looking at them too long.”", "\"아씨가 그걸 너무 오래 들여다보시는 걸 봤어요.\""]], [["“What has that to do with it?”", "\"그게 무슨 상관이란 말이냐?\""]], [["“You have been heart-broken all the morning, as if you did not want to live.”", "\"아침 내내 아씨는 가슴이 미어지신 듯, 살고 싶지 않으신 것처럼 보였어요.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“And I could not bear to leave them in your way.", "\"그래서 그걸 아씨 손 닿는 곳에 두고 차마 떠날 수가 없었어요."], ["There was meaning in your look at them.”", "아씨가 그걸 보시는 눈빛에 어떤 뜻이 담겨 있었거든요.\""]], [["“Where are they now?”", "\"지금 그것들은 어디 있느냐?\""]], [["“Locked up.”", "\"잠가뒀어요.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디에?\""]], [["“In the stable.”", "\"마구간에요.\""]], [["“Give them to me.”", "\"내게 다오.\""]], [["“No, ma’am.”", "\"안 돼요, 아씨.\""]], [["“You refuse?”", "\"거절하는 게냐?\""]], [["“I do.", "\"그래요."], ["I care too much for you to give ’em up.”", "아씨를 너무 아끼니까 내드릴 수 없어요.\""]], [["She turned aside, her face for the first time softening from the stony immobility of the earlier day, and the corners of her mouth resuming something of that delicacy of cut which was always lost in her moments of despair.", "그녀는 고개를 돌렸고, 그녀의 얼굴은 그날 아침의 돌처럼 굳은 무표정에서 처음으로 누그러졌으며, 절망의 순간이면 늘 사라지고 말던 그 섬세한 윤곽이 입꼬리에 다시금 어렸다."], ["At last she confronted him again.", "마침내 그녀는 다시 그를 마주했다."]], [["“Why should I not die if I wish?”", "\"내가 죽고 싶다면 죽지 못할 게 뭐란 말이냐?\""], ["she said tremulously.", "그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“I have made a bad bargain with life, and I am weary of it—weary.", "\"나는 삶과 잘못된 거래를 맺었고, 이제 그것에 지쳤어—지쳤다고."], ["And now you have hindered my escape.", "그런데 네가 내 탈출을 막은 거야."], ["O, why did you, Charley!", "오, 찰리, 너는 어쩌자고!"], ["What makes death painful except the thought of others’ grief?—and that is absent in my case, for not a sigh would follow me!”", "남들이 슬퍼하리라는 생각 말고 죽음을 고통스럽게 하는 게 무엇이겠느냐?—그런데 내 경우엔 그것조차 없으니, 내 뒤를 따라올 한숨 하나 없을 테니까!\""]], [["“Ah, it is trouble that has done this!", "\"아아, 이 모든 게 그 고통 탓이에요!"], ["I wish in my very soul that he who brought it about might die and rot, even if ’tis transportation to say it!”", "이걸 입에 담는 게 유형감에 처할 일이라 해도, 이 일을 꾸민 그자가 죽어 썩어버리기를 영혼 깊이 바라요!\""]], [["“Charley, no more of that.", "\"찰리, 그런 말은 그만하거라."], ["What do you mean to do about this you have seen?”", "네가 본 이 일을 어찌할 셈이냐?\""]], [["“Keep it close as night, if you promise not to think of it again.”", "\"아씨가 다시는 그런 생각 안 하겠다 약속하시면, 밤처럼 비밀로 묻어두겠어요.\""]], [["“You need not fear.", "\"걱정할 것 없다."], ["The moment has passed.", "그 순간은 지나갔으니."], ["I promise.”", "약속하마.\""], ["She then went away, entered the house, and lay down.", "그러고는 그녀는 자리를 떠나 집 안으로 들어가 자리에 누웠다."]], [["Later in the afternoon her grandfather returned.", "오후 늦게 그녀의 할아버지가 돌아왔다."], ["He was about to question her categorically, but on looking at her he withheld his words.", "그는 그녀에게 조목조목 캐물으려 했으나, 그녀를 바라보고는 말을 거두었다."]], [["“Yes, it is too bad to talk of,”", "\"네, 입에 담기엔 너무 끔찍한 일이에요.\""], ["she slowly returned in answer to his glance.", "그녀는 그의 눈길에 대답하듯 천천히 받았다."], ["“Can my old room be got ready for me tonight, Grandfather?", "\"할아버지, 오늘 밤 제 옛 방을 다시 쓸 수 있게 준비해주실 수 있어요?"], ["I shall want to occupy it again.”", "다시 그 방을 쓰고 싶어요.\""]], [["He did not ask what it all meant, or why she had left her husband, but ordered the room to be prepared.", "그는 이 모든 게 무슨 영문인지, 그녀가 왜 남편을 떠났는지 묻지 않고, 방을 준비하도록 일렀다."]], [["V.", "V."], ["An Old Move Inadvertently Repeated", "무심코 되풀이된 옛 수"]], [["Charley’s attentions to his former mistress were unbounded.", "옛 주인을 향한 찰리의 정성은 끝이 없었다."], ["The only solace to his own trouble lay in his attempts to relieve hers.", "그 자신의 괴로움을 달랠 유일한 위안은 그녀의 괴로움을 덜어주려는 노력에 있었다."], ["Hour after hour he considered her wants; he thought of her presence there with a sort of gratitude, and, while uttering imprecations on the cause of her unhappiness, in some measure blessed the result.", "시간이 가고 또 가도 그는 그녀에게 필요한 것을 헤아렸으며, 그녀가 그곳에 있다는 사실을 일종의 감사하는 마음으로 떠올렸고, 그녀의 불행을 일으킨 원인을 저주하면서도 그 결과만큼은 어느 정도 축복했다."], ["Perhaps she would always remain there, he thought, and then he would be as happy as he had been before.", "어쩌면 그녀가 늘 이곳에 머물지도 모른다고, 그러면 자기도 예전처럼 행복해지리라고 그는 생각했다."], ["His dread was lest she should think fit to return to Alderworth, and in that dread his eyes, with all the inquisitiveness of affection, frequently sought her face when she was not observing him, as he would have watched the head of a stockdove to learn if it contemplated flight.", "그가 두려워한 것은 그녀가 올더워스로 돌아가는 게 마땅하다고 여기는 일이었으며, 그 두려움 속에서 그의 눈은, 애정 특유의 온갖 살핌을 담은 채, 그녀가 자기를 보지 않을 때면 자주 그녀의 얼굴을 좇았으니, 마치 산비둘기가 날아오를 궁리를 하는지 알아보려 그 머리를 지켜보듯 했다."], ["Having once really succoured her, and possibly preserved her from the rashest of acts, he mentally assumed in addition a guardian’s responsibility for her welfare.", "한 번 정말로 그녀를 구해냈고, 어쩌면 가장 무모한 짓에서 그녀를 지켜냈기에, 그는 마음속으로 그녀의 안녕에 대한 보호자의 책임까지 덧붙여 떠맡았다."]], [["For this reason he busily endeavoured to provide her with pleasant distractions, bringing home curious objects which he found in the heath, such as white trumpet-shaped mosses, redheaded lichens, stone arrowheads used by the old tribes on Egdon, and faceted crystals from the hollows of flints.", "이런 까닭에 그는 그녀에게 즐거운 소일거리를 마련해주려 부지런히 애썼으니, 황야에서 찾아낸 진귀한 물건들—나팔 모양의 흰 이끼며, 머리가 붉은 지의류며, 옛적 에그던의 부족들이 쓰던 돌화살촉이며, 부싯돌 구멍에서 나온 결정면이 진 수정 따위를 집으로 가져왔다."], ["These he deposited on the premises in such positions that she should see them as if by accident.", "이것들을 그는 그녀가 우연인 듯 보게끔 집 안 곳곳 적당한 자리에 놓아두었다."]], [["A week passed, Eustacia never going out of the house.", "한 주가 지나도록 유스테이샤는 한 번도 집 밖으로 나가지 않았다."], ["Then she walked into the enclosed plot and looked through her grandfather’s spyglass, as she had been in the habit of doing before her marriage.", "그러던 어느 날 그녀는 울타리를 두른 땅으로 걸어 나가 할아버지의 망원경으로 내다보았으니, 결혼 전에 늘 하던 버릇 그대로였다."], ["One day she saw, at a place where the highroad crossed the distant valley, a heavily laden wagon passing along.", "어느 날 그녀는 큰길이 멀리 골짜기를 가로지르는 지점에서 짐을 잔뜩 실은 마차가 지나가는 것을 보았다."], ["It was piled with household furniture.", "마차에는 살림살이가 수북이 쌓여 있었다."], ["She looked again and again, and recognized it to be her own.", "그녀는 보고 또 보다가, 그것이 자기 것임을 알아보았다."], ["In the evening her grandfather came indoors with a rumour that Yeobright had removed that day from Alderworth to the old house at Blooms-End.", "저녁이 되자 할아버지가 집 안으로 들어오며, 요브라이트가 그날 올더워스에서 블룸스엔드의 옛집으로 이사했다는 소문을 전했다."]], [["On another occasion when reconnoitring thus she beheld two female figures walking in the vale.", "또 한 번은 그렇게 망원경으로 살피다가 골짜기를 걸어가는 두 여인의 모습을 보았다."], ["The day was fine and clear; and the persons not being more than half a mile off she could see their every detail with the telescope.", "날은 맑고 화창했으며, 그들이 반 마일도 채 떨어지지 않은 터라 그녀는 망원경으로 그들의 세세한 모습까지 다 볼 수 있었다."], ["The woman walking in front carried a white bundle in her arms, from one end of which hung a long appendage of drapery; and when the walkers turned, so that the sun fell more directly upon them, Eustacia could see that the object was a baby.", "앞서 걷는 여자는 흰 보따리를 두 팔에 안고 있었는데, 그 한쪽 끝에서는 길게 늘어진 천자락이 드리워져 있었으며, 두 사람이 몸을 돌려 햇빛이 더 곧장 그들 위로 떨어지자 유스테이샤는 그 물건이 아기임을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["She called Charley, and asked him if he knew who they were, though she well guessed.", "그녀는 이미 짐작은 했으면서도 찰리를 불러, 저들이 누구인지 아느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Mrs. Wildeve and the nurse-girl,”", "\"와일디브 부인하고 보모 아이예요.\""], ["said Charley.", "찰리가 말했다."]], [["“The nurse is carrying the baby?”", "\"보모가 아기를 안고 있는 게냐?\""], ["said Eustacia.", "유스테이샤가 말했다."]], [["“No, ’tis Mrs. Wildeve carrying that,”", "\"아뇨, 그건 와일디브 부인이 안고 있는 거고요.\""], ["he answered, “and the nurse walks behind carrying nothing.”", "그가 대답했다. \"보모는 뒤에서 아무것도 안 들고 걷고 있어요.\""]], [["The lad was in good spirits that day, for the Fifth of November had again come round, and he was planning yet another scheme to divert her from her too absorbing thoughts.", "그날 그 소년은 기분이 좋았으니, 십일월 오일이 다시 돌아온 까닭에, 그녀를 너무 골똘한 생각에서 돌려놓을 또 다른 계획을 짜고 있던 것이었다."], ["For two successive years his mistress had seemed to take pleasure in lighting a bonfire on the bank overlooking the valley; but this year she had apparently quite forgotten the day and the customary deed.", "두 해 잇따라 그의 주인 아씨는 골짜기가 내려다보이는 둑에 모닥불을 피우는 것을 즐기는 듯했으나, 올해는 그 날도 그 으레 하던 일도 까맣게 잊은 모양이었다."], ["He was careful not to remind her, and went on with his secret preparations for a cheerful surprise, the more zealously that he had been absent last time and unable to assist.", "그는 그녀에게 일깨우지 않으려 조심하면서, 즐거운 깜짝 선물을 마련하려는 은밀한 채비를 이어갔으니, 지난번에는 자기가 자리를 비워 거들지 못했던 터라 더욱 열심이었다."], ["At every vacant minute he hastened to gather furze-stumps, thorn-tree roots, and other solid materials from the adjacent slopes, hiding them from cursory view.", "짬이 날 때마다 그는 부지런히 가까운 비탈에서 가시금작화 그루터기며, 가시나무 뿌리며, 그 밖의 단단한 땔감을 그러모아, 얼핏 봐서는 보이지 않게 감추어 두었다."]], [["The evening came, and Eustacia was still seemingly unconscious of the anniversary.", "저녁이 되었으나 유스테이샤는 여전히 그 기일을 까맣게 잊은 듯했다."], ["She had gone indoors after her survey through the glass, and had not been visible since.", "그녀는 망원경으로 둘러본 뒤 집 안으로 들어갔고, 그 후로는 모습을 보이지 않았다."], ["As soon as it was quite dark Charley began to build the bonfire, choosing precisely that spot on the bank which Eustacia had chosen at previous times.", "사방이 완전히 어두워지자마자 찰리는 모닥불을 쌓기 시작했으니, 바로 유스테이샤가 예전에 골랐던 그 둑 위의 자리를 똑같이 골랐다."]], [["When all the surrounding bonfires had burst into existence Charley kindled his, and arranged its fuel so that it should not require tending for some time.", "주위의 모닥불이 죄다 살아나 타오르자 찰리도 제 불을 지폈으며, 한동안 손볼 일이 없도록 땔감을 잘 쌓아두었다."], ["He then went back to the house, and lingered round the door and windows till she should by some means or other learn of his achievement and come out to witness it.", "그러고는 집으로 돌아가, 그녀가 어떻게든 자기가 해낸 일을 알아채고 나와서 보아주기를 바라며 문과 창문 언저리를 서성였다."], ["But the shutters were closed, the door remained shut, and no heed whatever seemed to be taken of his performance.", "그러나 덧창은 닫혀 있었고, 문도 닫힌 채였으며, 그가 한 일에는 아무런 관심도 두지 않는 듯했다."], ["Not liking to call her he went back and replenished the fire, continuing to do this for more than half an hour.", "그녀를 부르기는 내키지 않아 그는 다시 가서 불을 돋우었으며, 반 시간 넘게 그 일을 거듭했다."], ["It was not till his stock of fuel had greatly diminished that he went to the back door and sent in to beg that Mrs. Yeobright would open the window-shutters and see the sight outside.", "땔감이 거의 다 떨어지고 나서야 그는 뒷문으로 가서, 요브라이트 부인이 창의 덧창을 열고 바깥 광경을 보아주십사 청하는 말을 안으로 들여보냈다."]], [["Eustacia, who had been sitting listlessly in the parlour, started up at the intelligence and flung open the shutters.", "응접실에서 맥없이 앉아 있던 유스테이샤는 그 기별에 벌떡 일어나 덧창을 활짝 열어젖혔다."], ["Facing her on the bank blazed the fire, which at once sent a ruddy glare into the room where she was, and overpowered the candles.", "둑 위에서 그녀를 마주 보며 불이 타오르고 있었으니, 그것은 즉시 그녀가 있는 방 안으로 불그레한 불빛을 던져 넣어 촛불을 압도했다."]], [["“Well done, Charley!”", "\"잘했다, 찰리!\""], ["said Captain Vye from the chimney-corner.", "굴뚝 모퉁이에서 바이 선장이 말했다."], ["“But I hope it is not my wood that he’s burning....", "\"허나 그놈이 태우는 게 내 장작이 아니길 바라네……"], ["Ah, it was this time last year that I met with that man Venn, bringing home Thomasin Yeobright—to be sure it was!", "아, 그러고 보니 작년 이맘때였지, 그 베너라는 사내가 토머신 요브라이트를 집으로 데려오는 걸 만난 게—정말 그랬지!"], ["Well, who would have thought that girl’s troubles would have ended so well?", "그래, 그 처녀의 풍파가 그리 잘 끝날 줄 누가 알았겠나?"], ["What a snipe you were in that matter, Eustacia!", "그 일에서 너는 어찌 그리 멍청했던지, 유스테이샤!"], ["Has your husband written to you yet?”", "네 남편한테서 이제 편지는 왔느냐?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아뇨.\""], ["said Eustacia, looking vaguely through the window at the fire, which just then so much engaged her mind that she did not resent her grandfather’s blunt opinion.", "유스테이샤가 창 너머로 불을 멍하니 바라보며 말했으니, 그 불이 마침 그녀의 마음을 너무도 사로잡은 터라 그녀는 할아버지의 무뚝뚝한 평에도 발끈하지 않았다."], ["She could see Charley’s form on the bank, shovelling and stirring the fire; and there flashed upon her imagination some other form which that fire might call up.", "그녀는 둑 위에서 삽질을 하며 불을 쑤석이는 찰리의 모습을 볼 수 있었으며, 그 불이 불러낼지도 모를 또 다른 어떤 모습이 문득 그녀의 상상 속에 번뜩였다."]], [["She left the room, put on her garden bonnet and cloak, and went out.", "그녀는 방을 나와 정원용 보닛과 망토를 걸치고 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Reaching the bank, she looked over with a wild curiosity and misgiving, when Charley said to her, with a pleased sense of himself, “I made it o’ purpose for you, ma’am.”", "둑에 이르러 그녀가 격한 호기심과 불안을 안고 너머를 살피자, 찰리가 자기 자신이 흐뭇한 듯 그녀에게 말했다. \"아씨를 위해 일부러 피운 거예요.\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙구나.\""], ["she said hastily.", "그녀가 황급히 말했다."], ["“But I wish you to put it out now.”", "\"허나 이제 그걸 꺼주었으면 한다.\""]], [["“It will soon burn down,”", "\"곧 사그라들 거예요.\""], ["said Charley, rather disappointed.", "찰리가 다소 실망한 듯 말했다."], ["“Is it not a pity to knock it out?”", "\"꺼버리기엔 아깝지 않아요?\""]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"모르겠다.\""], ["she musingly answered.", "그녀가 골똘히 대답했다."]], [["They stood in silence, broken only by the crackling of the flames, till Charley, perceiving that she did not want to talk to him, moved reluctantly away.", "두 사람은 불꽃이 타닥거리는 소리 말고는 아무것도 깨뜨리지 않는 침묵 속에 서 있었으나, 이윽고 그녀가 자기와 이야기하고 싶어하지 않음을 알아챈 찰리가 마지못해 자리를 떴다."]], [["Eustacia remained within the bank looking at the fire, intending to go indoors, yet lingering still.", "유스테이샤는 집 안으로 들어가려 마음먹으면서도 여전히 머뭇거리며, 둑 안쪽에 남아 불을 바라보았다."], ["Had she not by her situation been inclined to hold in indifference all things honoured of the gods and of men she would probably have come away.", "그녀가 처한 처지로 말미암아 신들과 인간들이 떠받드는 온갖 것을 무심히 여기는 마음이 들지 않았더라면, 아마 그녀는 그 자리를 떠났으리라."], ["But her state was so hopeless that she could play with it.", "그러나 그녀의 처지는 너무도 가망 없어 그것을 가지고 놀 수도 있을 정도였다."], ["To have lost is less disturbing than to wonder if we may possibly have won; and Eustacia could now, like other people at such a stage, take a standing-point outside herself, observe herself as a disinterested spectator, and think what a sport for Heaven this woman Eustacia was.", "잃어버린 것은, 어쩌면 이겼을지도 모른다고 의아해하는 것보다 덜 어지러운 법이니, 이제 유스테이샤도 그런 처지에 놓인 다른 이들처럼 자기 바깥에 발판을 두고, 짐짓 무관심한 구경꾼처럼 자기를 바라보며, 이 유스테이샤라는 여인이 하늘에게는 얼마나 좋은 노리개인가 생각할 수 있었다."]], [["While she stood she heard a sound.", "그렇게 서 있는데 어떤 소리가 들렸다."], ["It was the splash of a stone in the pond.", "연못에 돌이 떨어지며 첨벙이는 소리였다."]], [["Had Eustacia received the stone full in the bosom her heart could not have given a more decided thump.", "유스테이샤가 그 돌을 가슴 한복판에 정통으로 맞았더라도 그녀의 심장이 그보다 더 확연히 쿵 뛰지는 못했으리라."], ["She had thought of the possibility of such a signal in answer to that which had been unwittingly given by Charley; but she had not expected it yet.", "그녀는 찰리가 무심코 보낸 신호에 대한 응답으로 그런 신호가 올 가능성을 생각해두기는 했으나, 아직은 오리라 예상하지 못했다."], ["How prompt Wildeve was!", "와일디브는 어찌 그리 재빠른가!"], ["Yet how could he think her capable of deliberately wishing to renew their assignations now?", "허나 이제 와서 자기가 작정하고 밀회를 다시 잇기를 바랄 수 있는 여자라고 그가 어찌 여길 수 있단 말인가?"], ["An impulse to leave the spot, a desire to stay, struggled within her; and the desire held its own.", "그 자리를 떠나려는 충동과 머무르려는 욕망이 그녀 안에서 다투었으며, 욕망이 우세를 지켰다."], ["More than that it did not do, for she refrained even from ascending the bank and looking over.", "그 이상은 하지 않았으니, 둑에 올라 너머를 살피는 것조차 삼간 것이었다."], ["She remained motionless, not disturbing a muscle of her face or raising her eyes; for were she to turn up her face the fire on the bank would shine upon it, and Wildeve might be looking down.", "그녀는 미동도 없이, 얼굴 근육 하나 까딱하지 않고 눈도 들지 않은 채 머물렀으니, 얼굴을 들었다가는 둑의 불빛이 그 위로 비칠 것이요, 와일디브가 내려다보고 있을지도 몰랐기 때문이다."]], [["There was a second splash into the pond.", "연못으로 두 번째 첨벙이는 소리가 났다."]], [["Why did he stay so long without advancing and looking over?", "그는 어찌하여 앞으로 나와 너머를 살피지도 않고 그리 오래 머물러 있는 걸까?"], ["Curiosity had its way—she ascended one or two of the earth-steps in the bank and glanced out.", "호기심이 끝내 이겨—그녀는 둑의 흙계단을 한두 칸 올라가 밖을 흘끗 내다보았다."]], [["Wildeve was before her.", "와일디브가 그녀 앞에 있었다."], ["He had come forward after throwing the last pebble, and the fire now shone into each of their faces from the bank stretching breast-high between them.", "마지막 조약돌을 던진 뒤 그가 앞으로 나온 것이었으며, 이제 불빛이, 두 사람 사이에 가슴 높이까지 뻗어 있는 둑 너머로, 둘 각자의 얼굴을 비추었다."]], [["“I did not light it!”", "\"내가 피운 게 아니에요!\""], ["cried Eustacia quickly.", "유스테이샤가 황급히 외쳤다."], ["“It was lit without my knowledge.", "\"내가 모르는 새 피워진 거예요."], ["Don’t, don’t come over to me!”", "그러지, 그러지 말고 내게로 넘어오지 말아요!\""]], [["“Why have you been living here all these days without telling me?", "\"왜 이 여러 날을 내게 알리지도 않고 여기서 지냈소?"], ["You have left your home.", "당신은 집을 떠났구려."], ["I fear I am something to blame in this?”", "이 일에 내 탓도 좀 있는 게 아닐까 두렵소만?\""]], [["“I did not let in his mother; that’s how it is!”", "\"내가 그이의 어머니를 들이지 않았어요. 그게 사연이에요!\""]], [["“You do not deserve what you have got, Eustacia; you are in great misery; I see it in your eyes, your mouth, and all over you.", "\"당신은 지금 받고 있는 일을 받을 사람이 아니오, 유스테이샤. 당신은 크나큰 고통 속에 있소."], ["My poor, poor girl!”", "당신 눈에서, 입에서, 온몸에서 그게 보이오."], ["He stepped over the bank.", "가엾고 가엾은 사람!\""], ["“You are beyond everything unhappy!”", "그가 둑을 넘었다. \"당신은 무엇보다도 불행하구려!\""]], [["“No, no; not exactly—”", "\"아뇨, 아뇨, 꼭 그런 건—\""]], [["“It has been pushed too far—it is killing you—I do think it!”", "\"너무 멀리 밀어붙여졌어—그게 당신을 죽이고 있소—정말로 그런 것 같소!\""]], [["Her usually quiet breathing had grown quicker with his words.", "평소엔 잔잔하던 그녀의 숨결이 그의 말에 가빠졌다. \"나는—나는—\""], ["“I—I—” she began, and then burst into quivering sobs, shaken to the very heart by the unexpected voice of pity—a sentiment whose existence in relation to herself she had almost forgotten.", "그녀가 입을 떼더니, 이내 떨리는 흐느낌을 터뜨렸으니, 뜻밖의 연민의 목소리에—자기 자신과 관련해서는 그런 감정이 존재한다는 것조차 거의 잊고 있던 그 감정에—가슴 깊은 데까지 흔들린 것이었다."]], [["This outbreak of weeping took Eustacia herself so much by surprise that she could not leave off, and she turned aside from him in some shame, though turning hid nothing from him.", "이 울음의 터짐은 유스테이샤 자신마저 너무도 놀라게 한 터라 그녀는 그칠 수가 없었고, 다소 부끄러워 그에게서 옆으로 고개를 돌렸으나, 돌린다고 그에게 가려지는 것은 없었다."], ["She sobbed on desperately; then the outpour lessened, and she became quieter.", "그녀는 절망적으로 흐느껴 울었으며, 이윽고 쏟아짐이 잦아들더니 차츰 잠잠해졌다."], ["Wildeve had resisted the impulse to clasp her, and stood without speaking.", "와일디브는 그녀를 끌어안고 싶은 충동을 억누른 채, 아무 말 없이 서 있었다."]], [["“Are you not ashamed of me, who used never to be a crying animal?”", "\"한 번도 울보였던 적이 없는 나 같은 게, 부끄럽지 않아요?\""], ["she asked in a weak whisper as she wiped her eyes.", "그녀가 눈을 닦으며 가냘픈 속삭임으로 물었다."], ["“Why didn’t you go away?", "\"왜 가지 않았어요?"], ["I wish you had not seen quite all that; it reveals too much by half.”", "이 모든 걸 다 보지 않았으면 좋았을 것을. 너무 많은 걸 드러내고 마니까요.\""]], [["“You might have wished it, because it makes me as sad as you,”", "\"그리 바랐을지도 모르겠소, 그게 나를 당신만큼이나 슬프게 하니까.\""], ["he said with emotion and deference.", "그가 감정을 담아 조심스럽게 말했다."], ["“As for revealing—the word is impossible between us two.”", "\"드러낸다는 것으로 말하자면—우리 둘 사이에 그런 말은 가당치 않소.\""]], [["“I did not send for you—don’t forget it, Damon; I am in pain, but I did not send for you!", "\"내가 당신을 부른 게 아니에요—그걸 잊지 말아요, 데이먼. 나는 괴롭지만, 당신을 부른 게 아니에요!"], ["As a wife, at least, I’ve been straight.”", "적어도 아내로서, 나는 떳떳했어요.\""]], [["“Never mind—I came.", "\"괘념치 마오—내가 온 거니까."], ["O, Eustacia, forgive me for the harm I have done you in these two past years!", "오, 유스테이샤, 지난 두 해 동안 내가 당신에게 끼친 해를 용서해주오!"], ["I see more and more that I have been your ruin.”", "내가 당신을 망쳐온 거라는 걸 점점 더 알겠소.\""]], [["“Not you.", "\"당신이 아니에요."], ["This place I live in.”", "내가 사는 이곳이에요.\""]], [["“Ah, your generosity may naturally make you say that.", "\"아아, 당신의 너그러움이 그리 말하게 하는 것도 당연하겠지."], ["But I am the culprit.", "허나 죄인은 나요."], ["I should either have done more or nothing at all.”", "나는 더 했어야 했거나, 아니면 아예 아무것도 하지 말았어야 했소.\""]], [["“In what way?”", "\"어떻게요?\""]], [["“I ought never to have hunted you out, or, having done it, I ought to have persisted in retaining you.", "\"나는 결코 당신을 찾아 나서지 말았어야 했거나, 아니면 그렇게 한 이상 끝까지 당신을 붙들었어야 했소."], ["But of course I have no right to talk of that now.", "허나 물론 이제 와 그런 말을 할 자격이 내겐 없지."], ["I will only ask this—can I do anything for you?", "이것만 묻겠소—내가 당신을 위해 무어라도 할 수 있겠소?"], ["Is there anything on the face of the earth that a man can do to make you happier than you are at present?", "지금보다 당신을 더 행복하게 해주려 사내가 이 땅 위에서 할 수 있는 일이 무어라도 있다면?"], ["If there is, I will do it.", "있다면, 내가 하리다."], ["You may command me, Eustacia, to the limit of my influence; and don’t forget that I am richer now.", "명하시오, 유스테이샤, 내 힘이 미치는 한껏. 그리고 내가 이제 더 부유해졌다는 걸 잊지 마오."], ["Surely something can be done to save you from this!", "분명 당신을 이 처지에서 구할 무언가가 있을 거요!"], ["Such a rare plant in such a wild place it grieves me to see.", "이런 거친 곳에 이런 진귀한 화초라니, 보는 것만으로도 가슴이 아프오."], ["Do you want anything bought?", "사주었으면 하는 게 있소?"], ["Do you want to go anywhere?", "가고 싶은 데가 있소?"], ["Do you want to escape the place altogether?", "이곳을 아주 벗어나고 싶소?"], ["Only say it, and I’ll do anything to put an end to those tears, which but for me would never have been at all.”", "말만 하오, 그러면 나 때문이 아니었다면 애초에 흐르지도 않았을 그 눈물을 그치게 하기 위해 무엇이든 하리다.\""]], [["“We are each married to another person,”", "\"우리는 각자 다른 사람과 혼인한 몸이에요.\""], ["she said faintly; “and assistance from you would have an evil sound—after—after—”", "그녀가 힘없이 말했다. \"그리고 당신에게서 도움을 받으면 좋지 않은 소문이 날 거예요—나중에—나중에—\""]], [["“Well, there’s no preventing slanderers from having their fill at any time; but you need not be afraid.", "\"음, 험담꾼들이 언제든 실컷 떠드는 걸 막을 길이야 없지만, 당신은 두려워할 것 없소."], ["Whatever I may feel I promise you on my word of honour never to speak to you about—or act upon—until you say I may.", "내가 무엇을 느끼든, 당신이 좋다고 할 때까지는 결코 그것을 당신에게 입에 올리지도—행동에 옮기지도—않겠다고 명예를 걸고 약속하리다."], ["I know my duty to Thomasin quite as well as I know my duty to you as a woman unfairly treated.", "부당한 대접을 받은 한 여인으로서 당신에 대한 내 도리를 아는 만큼이나, 토머신에 대한 내 도리도 잘 알고 있소."], ["What shall I assist you in?”", "무엇을 도와드리면 되겠소?\""]], [["“In getting away from here.”", "\"여기서 벗어나는 거예요.\""]], [["“Where do you wish to go to?”", "\"어디로 가고 싶소?\""]], [["“I have a place in my mind.", "\"마음에 둔 곳이 있어요."], ["If you could help me as far as Budmouth I can do all the rest.", "버드머스까지만 도와주면 나머지는 다 내가 할 수 있어요."], ["Steamers sail from there across the Channel, and so I can get to Paris, where I want to be.", "거기서 증기선이 해협을 건너 떠나니, 그걸 타면 내가 가고 싶은 파리로 갈 수 있어요."], ["Yes,”", "그래요.\""], ["she pleaded earnestly, “help me to get to Budmouth harbour without my grandfather’s or my husband’s knowledge, and I can do all the rest.”", "그녀가 간곡히 청했다. \"할아버지나 남편이 모르게 버드머스 항구까지만 가게 도와줘요, 그러면 나머지는 다 내가 할게요.\""]], [["“Will it be safe to leave you there alone?”", "\"거기 당신 혼자 두는 게 안전하겠소?\""]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"네, 네."], ["I know Budmouth well.”", "나는 버드머스를 잘 알아요.\""]], [["“Shall I go with you?", "\"내가 함께 가리까?"], ["I am rich now.”", "나는 이제 부유하오.\""]], [["She was silent.", "그녀는 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“Say yes, sweet!”", "\"좋다고 하오, 내 사랑!\""]], [["She was silent still.", "그녀는 여전히 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“Well, let me know when you wish to go.", "\"그럼, 떠나고 싶을 때 알려주오."], ["We shall be at our present house till December; after that we remove to Casterbridge.", "우리는 십이월까지 지금 집에 있을 거요. 그 후에는 캐스터브리지로 옮기오."], ["Command me in anything till that time.”", "그때까지는 무엇이든 내게 명하시오.\""]], [["“I will think of this,”", "\"이 일은 생각해볼게요.\""], ["she said hurriedly.", "그녀가 황급히 말했다."], ["“Whether I can honestly make use of you as a friend, or must close with you as a lover—that is what I must ask myself.", "\"당신을 떳떳이 친구로 쓸 수 있을지, 아니면 연인으로 받아들여야 할지—그게 내가 나 자신에게 물어야 할 거예요."], ["If I wish to go and decide to accept your company I will signal to you some evening at eight o’clock punctually, and this will mean that you are to be ready with a horse and trap at twelve o’clock the same night to drive me to Budmouth harbour in time for the morning boat.”", "떠나기로 마음먹고 당신의 동행을 받아들이기로 하면, 어느 날 저녁 정각 여덟 시에 신호를 보낼게요, 그러면 그건 당신이 같은 날 밤 열두 시에 말과 이륜마차를 채비하고 나를 버드머스 항구로 데려가 아침 배 시간에 대게 하라는 뜻이에요.\""]], [["“I will look out every night at eight, and no signal shall escape me.”", "\"매일 밤 여덟 시에 살피겠소, 어떤 신호도 놓치지 않으리다.\""]], [["“Now please go away.", "\"이제 제발 가요."], ["If I decide on this escape I can only meet you once more unless—I cannot go without you.", "이 탈출을 결심한다면 당신을 다시 만날 수 있는 건 한 번뿐이에요, 그렇지 않으면—나는 당신 없이는 갈 수 없으니."], ["Go—I cannot bear it longer.", "가요—더는 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["Go—go!”", "가요—가요!\""]], [["Wildeve slowly went up the steps and descended into the darkness on the other side; and as he walked he glanced back, till the bank blotted out her form from his further view.", "와일디브는 천천히 계단을 올라 반대편 어둠 속으로 내려갔으며, 걸으면서 자꾸 뒤를 돌아보았으나, 이윽고 둑이 그의 시야에서 그녀의 모습을 지워버렸다."]], [["VI.", "VI."], ["Thomasin Argues with Her Cousin, and He Writes a Letter", "토머신이 사촌과 다투고, 그는 편지를 쓰다"]], [["Yeobright was at this time at Blooms-End, hoping that Eustacia would return to him.", "이 무렵 요브라이트는 블룸스엔드에 있으면서, 유스테이샤가 자기에게 돌아오기를 바라고 있었다."], ["The removal of furniture had been accomplished only that day, though Clym had lived in the old house for more than a week.", "클림은 그 옛집에서 한 주가 넘게 지내왔으나, 가구를 옮기는 일은 바로 그날에야 끝마쳤다."], ["He had spent the time in working about the premises, sweeping leaves from the garden paths, cutting dead stalks from the flower beds, and nailing up creepers which had been displaced by the autumn winds.", "그는 그 시간을 집 안팎을 손보는 일로 보냈으니, 정원길의 낙엽을 쓸고, 화단의 시든 줄기를 자르고, 가을바람에 떨어진 덩굴식물을 못질해 붙였다."], ["He took no particular pleasure in these deeds, but they formed a screen between himself and despair.", "이런 일들에서 그가 별다른 즐거움을 느낀 것은 아니었으나, 그것들은 그 자신과 절망 사이의 가림막을 이루어주었다."], ["Moreover, it had become a religion with him to preserve in good condition all that had lapsed from his mother’s hands to his own.", "게다가 어머니의 손에서 자기 손으로 넘어온 모든 것을 좋은 상태로 간수하는 일은 그에게 일종의 신앙이 되어 있었다."]], [["During these operations he was constantly on the watch for Eustacia.", "이런 일들을 하는 내내 그는 끊임없이 유스테이샤를 살피고 있었다."], ["That there should be no mistake about her knowing where to find him he had ordered a notice board to be affixed to the garden gate at Alderworth, signifying in white letters whither he had removed.", "그녀가 자기를 어디서 찾아야 할지 헷갈리지 않도록, 그는 올더워스의 정원 대문에 안내판을 붙여 자기가 어디로 옮겨 갔는지 흰 글씨로 알려두게 했다."], ["When a leaf floated to the earth he turned his head, thinking it might be her foot-fall.", "나뭇잎 하나가 땅으로 떠내려가면 그는 그녀의 발소리인가 싶어 고개를 돌렸다."], ["A bird searching for worms in the mould of the flower-beds sounded like her hand on the latch of the gate; and at dusk, when soft, strange ventriloquisms came from holes in the ground, hollow stalks, curled dead leaves, and other crannies wherein breezes, worms, and insects can work their will, he fancied that they were Eustacia, standing without and breathing wishes of reconciliation.", "화단의 부드러운 흙에서 벌레를 찾는 새 한 마리가 그녀가 대문 빗장에 손을 대는 소리처럼 들렸으며, 땅거미가 질 무렵 땅의 구멍이며, 속이 빈 줄기며, 말려 죽은 잎이며, 산들바람과 벌레와 곤충이 제멋대로 굴 수 있는 그 밖의 틈새에서 부드럽고 기묘한 복화술 같은 소리가 들려오면, 그는 그것이 밖에 서서 화해의 소망을 내쉬는 유스테이샤라고 상상했다."]], [["Up to this hour he had persevered in his resolve not to invite her back.", "이 순간까지 그는 그녀를 돌아오라 청하지 않으리라는 결심을 굳건히 지켜왔다."], ["At the same time the severity with which he had treated her lulled the sharpness of his regret for his mother, and awoke some of his old solicitude for his mother’s supplanter.", "한편으로 그가 그녀를 다룬 그 매정함은 어머니를 향한 회한의 날카로움을 누그러뜨렸고, 어머니의 자리를 빼앗은 여인을 향한 옛 애틋함을 얼마간 되살려놓았다."], ["Harsh feelings produce harsh usage, and this by reaction quenches the sentiments that gave it birth.", "모진 감정은 모진 처사를 낳고, 그것은 다시 반작용으로 그것을 낳은 정서를 꺼뜨리는 법이다."], ["The more he reflected the more he softened.", "곰곰이 헤아릴수록 그는 더 누그러졌다."], ["But to look upon his wife as innocence in distress was impossible, though he could ask himself whether he had given her quite time enough—if he had not come a little too suddenly upon her on that sombre morning.", "그러나 아내를 곤경에 빠진 결백으로 여기는 것은 불가능했으니, 다만 자기가 그녀에게 충분한 시간을 준 것이었는지—그 음울한 아침에 너무 갑작스레 그녀에게 들이닥친 것은 아니었는지 스스로 물어볼 수는 있었다."]], [["Now that the first flush of his anger had paled he was disinclined to ascribe to her more than an indiscreet friendship with Wildeve, for there had not appeared in her manner the signs of dishonour.", "처음의 끓어오르던 분노가 가신 지금, 그는 그녀에게 와일디브와의 경솔한 우정 이상의 것을 돌리고 싶지 않았으니, 그녀의 태도에 부정의 기색은 나타나지 않았던 까닭이다."], ["And this once admitted, an absolutely dark interpretation of her act towards his mother was no longer forced upon him.", "그리고 이것을 일단 인정하고 나니, 어머니를 향한 그녀의 행동에 대한 더없이 어두운 해석이 더는 억지로 들이밀어지지 않았다."]], [["On the evening of the fifth November his thoughts of Eustacia were intense.", "십일월 오일 저녁, 유스테이샤를 향한 그의 생각은 격렬했다."], ["Echoes from those past times when they had exchanged tender words all the day long came like the diffused murmur of a seashore left miles behind.", "온종일 다정한 말을 주고받던 그 지난 시절의 메아리가 몇 마일이나 뒤에 두고 온 바닷가의 어렴풋한 웅성거림처럼 밀려왔다."], ["“Surely,”", "\"분명,\""], ["he said, “she might have brought herself to communicate with me before now, and confess honestly what Wildeve was to her.”", "그가 말했다. \"그녀도 지금쯤은 나에게 연락을 해와, 와일디브가 자기에게 어떤 사람이었는지 솔직히 털어놓을 만했건만.\""]], [["Instead of remaining at home that night he determined to go and see Thomasin and her husband.", "그날 밤 집에 머무는 대신 그는 토머신과 그 남편을 보러 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["If he found opportunity he would allude to the cause of the separation between Eustacia and himself, keeping silence, however, on the fact that there was a third person in his house when his mother was turned away.", "기회가 닿으면 유스테이샤와 자기 사이의 결별 원인을 넌지시 언급하되, 어머니가 쫓겨났을 때 집 안에 제삼자가 있었다는 사실에 대해서는 입을 다물 작정이었다."], ["If it proved that Wildeve was innocently there he would doubtless openly mention it. If he were there with unjust intentions Wildeve, being a man of quick feeling, might possibly say something to reveal the extent to which Eustacia was compromised.", "와일디브가 결백하게 거기 있었던 것으로 드러난다면 그는 분명 그 사실을 거리낌 없이 말할 것이요, 만일 부당한 속셈을 품고 거기 있었던 거라면, 감정이 빨리 동하는 사내인 와일디브가 유스테이샤가 얼마나 곤란한 처지에 놓였는지 드러내는 무언가를 어쩌면 발설할지도 몰랐다."]], [["But on reaching his cousin’s house he found that only Thomasin was at home, Wildeve being at that time on his way towards the bonfire innocently lit by Charley at Mistover.", "그러나 사촌의 집에 이르고 보니 토머신만 집에 있었으니, 와일디브는 그때 찰리가 미스토버에서 무심코 피운 모닥불 쪽으로 가는 길이었다."], ["Thomasin then, as always, was glad to see Clym, and took him to inspect the sleeping baby, carefully screening the candlelight from the infant’s eyes with her hand.", "그때나 늘 그러하듯 토머신은 클림을 보고 반가워하며, 그를 데려가 잠든 아기를 들여다보게 했는데, 손으로 촛불을 가려 아기의 눈에 빛이 들지 않도록 조심스레 막았다."]], [["“Tamsin, have you heard that Eustacia is not with me now?”", "\"탐신, 유스테이샤가 지금 나와 함께 있지 않다는 걸 들었느냐?\""], ["he said when they had sat down again.", "두 사람이 다시 자리에 앉았을 때 그가 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아뇨.\""], ["said Thomasin, alarmed.", "토머신이 놀라 말했다."]], [["“And not that I have left Alderworth?”", "\"내가 올더워스를 떠났다는 것도?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아뇨."], ["I never hear tidings from Alderworth unless you bring them.", "오라버니가 가져오지 않는 한 올더워스 소식은 도통 듣지를 못해요."], ["What is the matter?”", "무슨 일이에요?\""]], [["Clym in a disturbed voice related to her his visit to Susan Nunsuch’s boy, the revelation he had made, and what had resulted from his charging Eustacia with having wilfully and heartlessly done the deed.", "클림은 불안한 목소리로 그녀에게 자기가 수전 넌서치의 아들을 찾아갔던 일과, 그 아이가 털어놓은 사실과, 유스테이샤가 고의로 매정하게 그 짓을 했다고 자기가 그녀를 추궁하여 빚어진 결과를 들려주었다."], ["He suppressed all mention of Wildeve’s presence with her.", "그녀와 함께 와일디브가 있었다는 이야기는 일절 입에 담지 않았다."]], [["“All this, and I not knowing it!”", "\"이 모든 일을, 나는 까맣게 모르고 있었다니!\""], ["murmured Thomasin in an awestruck tone, “Terrible!", "토머신이 두려움에 질린 어조로 중얼거렸다. \"끔찍해요!"], ["What could have made her—O, Eustacia!", "무엇이 그녀를 그리—오, 유스테이샤!"], ["And when you found it out you went in hot haste to her?", "그리고 그걸 알아내고는 오라버니가 한달음에 그녀에게 달려갔다고요?"], ["Were you too cruel?—or is she really so wicked as she seems?”", "너무 모질지는 않으셨어요?—아니면 그녀가 정말 보이는 그대로 그리 사악한가요?\""]], [["“Can a man be too cruel to his mother’s enemy?”", "\"제 어머니의 원수에게 사내가 너무 모질 수가 있겠느냐?\""]], [["“I can fancy so.”", "\"그럴 수 있을 것도 같아요.\""]], [["“Very well, then—I’ll admit that he can.", "\"좋다, 그럼—그럴 수 있다고 인정하마."], ["But now what is to be done?”", "헌데 이제 어찌해야 하느냐?\""]], [["“Make it up again—if a quarrel so deadly can ever be made up.", "\"다시 화해하세요—이리 끔찍한 다툼도 화해될 수 있다면요."], ["I almost wish you had not told me.", "차라리 이 얘기를 안 들었으면 싶을 정도예요."], ["But do try to be reconciled.", "허나 부디 화해하려 애써보세요."], ["There are ways, after all, if you both wish to.”", "두 분 다 원하기만 한다면, 어쨌든 길은 있는 법이니까요.\""]], [["“I don’t know that we do both wish to make it up,”", "\"우리 둘 다 화해를 원하는지 모르겠구나.\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."], ["“If she had wished it, would she not have sent to me by this time?”", "\"그녀가 원했다면, 지금쯤 나에게 기별을 보내지 않았겠느냐?\""]], [["“You seem to wish to, and yet you have not sent to her.”", "\"오라버니는 원하는 것 같으면서도, 그녀에게 기별을 보내지 않으셨잖아요.\""]], [["“True; but I have been tossed to and fro in doubt if I ought, after such strong provocation.", "\"그렇지. 허나 나는 그만한 도발을 당하고도 마땅히 그래야 하는지, 의심 속에서 이리저리 마음이 흔들려왔다."], ["To see me now, Thomasin, gives you no idea of what I have been; of what depths I have descended to in these few last days.", "지금의 나를 보고는, 탐신, 너는 내가 어떤 꼴이었는지, 이 며칠 사이 내가 어떤 나락까지 떨어졌는지 짐작도 못 할 게다."], ["O, it was a bitter shame to shut out my mother like that!", "오, 그런 식으로 내 어머니를 문전박대한 것은 쓰라린 치욕이었어!"], ["Can I ever forget it, or even agree to see her again?”", "내가 그걸 잊을 수 있겠느냐, 아니 그녀를 다시 보는 데 응할 수나 있겠느냐?\""]], [["“She might not have known that anything serious would come of it, and perhaps she did not mean to keep Aunt out altogether.”", "\"그녀는 그 일로 심각한 무슨 일이 생기리라곤 몰랐을지도 몰라요, 어쩌면 큰이모님을 아주 막아버릴 생각은 아니었을지도 모르고요.\""]], [["“She says herself that she did not.", "\"그녀 스스로도 그럴 생각은 아니었다고 하더구나."], ["But the fact remains that keep her out she did.”", "허나 막아버린 건 사실로 남아 있지 않으냐.\""]], [["“Believe her sorry, and send for her.”", "\"그녀가 뉘우치고 있다 믿고, 사람을 보내 불러오세요.\""]], [["“How if she will not come?”", "\"그녀가 오지 않으려 하면 어쩌고?\""]], [["“It will prove her guilty, by showing that it is her habit to nourish enmity.", "\"그건 그녀가 적의를 품는 게 버릇임을 보여주는 셈이니, 죄가 있음을 증명하는 거겠죠."], ["But I do not think that for a moment.”", "허나 저는 한순간도 그렇게는 생각지 않아요.\""]], [["“I will do this.", "\"이렇게 하마."], ["I will wait for a day or two longer—not longer than two days certainly; and if she does not send to me in that time I will indeed send to her.", "하루나 이틀쯤 더 기다리겠다—분명 이틀을 넘기지는 않으마. 그래도 그 안에 그녀가 나에게 기별을 보내지 않으면, 그땐 정말 내가 그녀에게 기별을 보내마."], ["I thought to have seen Wildeve here tonight.", "오늘 밤 여기서 와일디브를 볼 줄 알았는데."], ["Is he from home?”", "그는 집을 비웠느냐?\""]], [["Thomasin blushed a little. “No,”", "토머신이 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["she said.", "\"아뇨.\""], ["“He is merely gone out for a walk.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그냥 산책하러 나갔어요.\""]], [["“Why didn’t he take you with him?", "\"왜 너를 데려가지 않았지?"], ["The evening is fine.", "저녁 날씨가 좋은데."], ["You want fresh air as well as he.”", "너도 그 사람만큼이나 바람을 쐬어야지.\""]], [["“Oh, I don’t care for going anywhere; besides, there is baby.”", "\"오, 저는 어디 가는 거 별로 안 좋아해요, 게다가 아기도 있고요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"그래, 그래."], ["Well, I have been thinking whether I should not consult your husband about this as well as you,”", "그런데 나는 이 일을 너만이 아니라 네 남편한테도 의논해야 하는 게 아닐까 생각해왔다.\""], ["said Clym steadily.", "클림이 차분히 말했다."]], [["“I fancy I would not,”", "\"안 하시는 게 좋을 것 같아요.\""], ["she quickly answered.", "그녀가 얼른 대답했다."], ["“It can do no good.”", "\"도움이 안 될 거예요.\""]], [["Her cousin looked her in the face.", "사촌은 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."], ["No doubt Thomasin was ignorant that her husband had any share in the events of that tragic afternoon; but her countenance seemed to signify that she concealed some suspicion or thought of the reputed tender relations between Wildeve and Eustacia in days gone by.", "토머신이 그 비극적인 오후의 일에 제 남편이 한몫 끼어 있었다는 것을 모르고 있는 것은 틀림없었으나, 그녀의 표정은 지난날 와일디브와 유스테이샤 사이에 다정한 관계가 있었다는 소문에 대한 어떤 의심이나 생각을 감추고 있음을 드러내는 듯했다."]], [["Clym, however, could make nothing of it, and he rose to depart, more in doubt than when he came.", "그러나 클림은 그것에서 아무것도 알아낼 수 없었고, 그는 올 때보다 더 큰 의심을 안은 채 떠나려 일어섰다."]], [["“You will write to her in a day or two?”", "\"하루 이틀 안에 그녀에게 편지 쓰실 거죠?\""], ["said the young woman earnestly.", "젊은 여인이 간곡히 말했다."], ["“I do so hope the wretched separation may come to an end.”", "\"이 비참한 결별이 끝나기를 정말이지 간절히 바라요.\""]], [["“I will,”", "\"그러마.\""], ["said Clym; “I don’t rejoice in my present state at all.”", "클림이 말했다. \"나도 지금의 처지가 조금도 기쁘지 않으니까.\""]], [["And he left her and climbed over the hill to Blooms-End.", "그러고는 그녀와 헤어져 언덕을 넘어 블룸스엔드로 갔다."], ["Before going to bed he sat down and wrote the following letter:—", "잠자리에 들기 전 그는 자리에 앉아 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다."]], [["MY DEAR EUSTACIA,—I must obey my heart without consulting my reason too closely.", "사랑하는 유스테이샤,—나는 내 이성에 너무 가까이 묻지 않고 내 마음을 따라야겠소."], ["Will you come back to me?", "내게 돌아와주겠소?"], ["Do so, and the past shall never be mentioned.", "그래주오, 그러면 지난 일은 결코 입에 올리지 않으리다."], ["I was too severe; but O, Eustacia, the provocation!", "내가 너무 모질었소. 허나 오, 유스테이샤, 그 도발이라니!"], ["You don’t know, you never will know, what those words of anger cost me which you drew down upon yourself.", "당신이 스스로 불러들인 그 분노의 말들이 내게 얼마만 한 대가를 치르게 했는지 당신은 모르오, 결코 알지 못하리다."], ["All that an honest man can promise you I promise now, which is that from me you shall never suffer anything on this score again.", "정직한 사내가 약속할 수 있는 모든 것을 나는 이제 약속하니, 곧 이 일로 당신이 나로 인해 다시는 어떤 고통도 겪지 않으리라는 것이오."], ["After all the vows we have made, Eustacia, I think we had better pass the remainder of our lives in trying to keep them.", "우리가 맺은 그 모든 맹세를 두고 보건대, 유스테이샤, 우리는 남은 생을 그것을 지키려 애쓰며 보내는 편이 낫겠다 싶소."], ["Come to me, then, even if you reproach me.", "그러니 내게 오시오, 당신이 나를 책망한다 해도."], ["I have thought of your sufferings that morning on which I parted from you; I know they were genuine, and they are as much as you ought to bear.", "내가 당신과 헤어진 그 아침의 당신의 고통을 나는 떠올려왔소. 그것이 진심이었음을 나는 알며, 그것만으로도 당신이 감당해야 할 몫은 다한 것이오."], ["Our love must still continue.", "우리의 사랑은 여전히 이어져야 하오."], ["Such hearts as ours would never have been given us but to be concerned with each other.", "우리 같은 마음은 서로에게 매이라고가 아니고서야 우리에게 주어졌을 리 없소."], ["I could not ask you back at first, Eustacia, for I was unable to persuade myself that he who was with you was not there as a lover.", "처음에는 당신을 돌아오라 청할 수 없었소, 유스테이샤, 당신과 함께 있던 그 사내가 연인으로 거기 있던 게 아니라고 내 자신을 납득시킬 수 없었던 까닭이오."], ["But if you will come and explain distracting appearances I do not question that you can show your honesty to me.", "허나 당신이 와서 그 어지러운 정황을 설명해준다면, 당신이 내게 자신의 결백을 보일 수 있으리라는 걸 나는 의심치 않소."], ["Why have you not come before?", "어찌하여 진작 오지 않았소?"], ["Do you think I will not listen to you?", "내가 당신 말을 듣지 않으리라 여기오?"], ["Surely not, when you remember the kisses and vows we exchanged under the summer moon.", "여름 달빛 아래 우리가 주고받은 입맞춤과 맹세를 떠올린다면, 분명 그럴 리 없을 텐데."], ["Return then, and you shall be warmly welcomed.", "그러니 돌아오시오, 그러면 따뜻이 맞으리다."], ["I can no longer think of you to your prejudice—I am but too much absorbed in justifying you.—Your husband as ever,", "더는 당신을 해롭게 생각할 수 없소—나는 당신을 두둔하는 데에 너무도 골몰해 있으니.—언제나 당신의 남편,"]], [["“There,”", "클림."], ["he said, as he laid it in his desk, “that’s a good thing done.", "\"됐다.\" 그것을 책상에 넣으며 그가 말했다."], ["If she does not come before tomorrow night I will send it to her.”", "\"잘한 일이지. 내일 밤까지 그녀가 오지 않으면 이걸 그녀에게 보내야겠다.\""]], [["Meanwhile, at the house he had just left Thomasin sat sighing uneasily.", "한편, 그가 막 떠나온 집에서 토머신은 마음이 편치 않아 한숨을 짓고 앉아 있었다."], ["Fidelity to her husband had that evening induced her to conceal all suspicion that Wildeve’s interest in Eustacia had not ended with his marriage.", "그날 저녁 남편에 대한 정절로 인해, 그녀는 와일디브의 유스테이샤를 향한 관심이 결혼과 더불어 끝나지 않았으리라는 모든 의심을 감추었던 것이다."], ["But she knew nothing positive; and though Clym was her well-beloved cousin there was one nearer to her still.", "그러나 그녀는 확실히 아는 것이 없었고, 클림이 그녀가 끔찍이 아끼는 사촌이긴 했으나, 그녀에게는 그보다 더 가까운 이가 있었다."]], [["When, a little later, Wildeve returned from his walk to Mistover, Thomasin said, “Damon, where have you been?", "조금 뒤 와일디브가 미스토버 산책에서 돌아오자, 토머신이 말했다. \"데이먼, 어디 갔었어요?"], ["I was getting quite frightened, and thought you had fallen into the river.", "무척 겁이 나서, 당신이 강에 빠진 줄 알았다고요."], ["I dislike being in the house by myself.”", "나는 혼자 집에 있는 게 싫어요.\""]], [["“Frightened?”", "\"겁이 났다고?\""], ["he said, touching her cheek as if she were some domestic animal.", "그가 마치 그녀를 무슨 집짐승이라도 되는 양 그 뺨을 어루만지며 말했다."], ["“Why, I thought nothing could frighten you.", "\"원, 나는 무엇도 당신을 겁먹게 하지 못하는 줄 알았는데."], ["It is that you are getting proud, I am sure, and don’t like living here since we have risen above our business.", "분명 당신이 콧대가 높아져서, 우리가 장사 밑천에서 벗어나 형편이 나아진 뒤로 이런 데서 사는 게 마음에 안 드는 게지."], ["Well, it is a tedious matter, this getting a new house; but I couldn’t have set about it sooner, unless our ten thousand pounds had been a hundred thousand, when we could have afforded to despise caution.”", "음, 새집을 구하는 건 따분한 일이오. 허나 우리 만 파운드가 십만 파운드였다면 모를까, 신중을 무시할 만큼 여유가 있는 게 아니고서야 그보다 더 일찍 손댈 수는 없었소.\""]], [["“No—I don’t mind waiting—I would rather stay here twelve months longer than run any risk with baby.", "\"아뇨—나는 기다리는 거 상관없어요—아기에게 무슨 위험이라도 무릅쓰느니 차라리 여기서 열두 달을 더 머무는 게 나아요."], ["But I don’t like your vanishing so in the evenings.", "허나 당신이 저녁마다 그렇게 사라지는 게 싫어요."], ["There’s something on your mind—I know there is, Damon.", "무언가 마음에 걸리는 게 있죠—분명 있어요, 데이먼."], ["You go about so gloomily, and look at the heath as if it were somebody’s gaol instead of a nice wild place to walk in.”", "당신은 그리 음울하게 돌아다니고, 황야를 마치 거닐기 좋은 멋진 들판이 아니라 누군가의 감옥인 양 바라보잖아요.\""]], [["He looked towards her with pitying surprise.", "그가 가엾다는 듯 놀란 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["“What, do you like Egdon Heath?”", "\"뭐라, 당신은 에그던 황야가 좋소?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I like what I was born near to; I admire its grim old face.”", "\"나는 내가 가까이서 나고 자란 것을 좋아해요. 그 험상궂은 옛 얼굴이 좋아요.\""]], [["“Pooh, my dear.", "\"쳇, 여보."], ["You don’t know what you like.”", "당신은 자기가 뭘 좋아하는지도 몰라.\""]], [["“I am sure I do.", "\"분명히 알아요."], ["There’s only one thing unpleasant about Egdon.”", "에그던에 대해 마음에 안 드는 건 딱 하나뿐이에요.\""]], [["“What’s that?”", "\"그게 뭔데?\""]], [["“You never take me with you when you walk there.", "\"당신이 거길 거닐 때 한 번도 나를 데려가지 않는 거요."], ["Why do you wander so much in it yourself if you so dislike it?”", "그토록 싫다면서 어째서 당신은 거길 그리 혼자 헤매고 다니는 거예요?\""]], [["The inquiry, though a simple one, was plainly disconcerting, and he sat down before replying.", "그 물음은 단순한 것이었으나 분명 당황스러운 데가 있어, 그는 대답하기 전에 자리에 앉았다."], ["“I don’t think you often see me there.", "\"당신이 거기서 나를 자주 보는 것 같진 않은데."], ["Give an instance.”", "한 번 예를 들어보오.\""]], [["“I will,”", "\"그러죠.\""], ["she answered triumphantly.", "그녀가 의기양양하게 대답했다."], ["“When you went out this evening I thought that as baby was asleep I would see where you were going to so mysteriously without telling me.", "\"오늘 저녁 당신이 나갔을 때, 아기가 잠들었으니 당신이 나한테 말도 없이 그렇게 알 수 없는 데로 어딜 가는지 봐야겠다 싶었어요."], ["So I ran out and followed behind you.", "그래서 뛰어나가 당신 뒤를 따라갔죠."], ["You stopped at the place where the road forks, looked round at the bonfires, and then said, ‘Damn it, I’ll go!’ And you went quickly up the left-hand road.", "당신은 길이 갈라지는 곳에 멈춰 서서 모닥불들을 둘러보더니, '제기랄, 가야겠다!' 하고는 왼쪽 길로 빠르게 올라갔어요."], ["Then I stood and watched you.”", "그래서 나는 서서 당신을 지켜봤죠.\""]], [["Wildeve frowned, afterwards saying, with a forced smile, “Well, what wonderful discovery did you make?”", "와일디브가 얼굴을 찌푸렸다가, 이내 억지웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"음, 그래서 무슨 놀라운 발견을 했소?\""]], [["“There—now you are angry, and we won’t talk of this any more.”", "\"거봐요—이제 당신은 화를 내잖아요, 그러니 이 얘기는 더 안 할래요.\""], ["She went across to him, sat on a footstool, and looked up in his face.", "그녀는 그에게로 건너가, 발받침에 앉아 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다."]], [["“Nonsense!”", "\"실없는 소리!\""], ["he said, “that’s how you always back out.", "그가 말했다. \"당신은 늘 그런 식으로 발을 빼지."], ["We will go on with it now we have begun.", "일단 시작했으니 이제 계속합시다."], ["What did you next see?", "그다음에 뭘 봤소?"], ["I particularly want to know.”", "나는 그게 특히 알고 싶군.\""]], [["“Don’t be like that, Damon!”", "\"그러지 말아요, 데이먼!\""], ["she murmured.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."], ["“I didn’t see anything.", "\"아무것도 못 봤어요."], ["You vanished out of sight, and then I looked round at the bonfires and came in.”", "당신이 시야에서 사라져서, 나는 모닥불들을 둘러보고 들어왔어요.\""]], [["“Perhaps this is not the only time you have dogged my steps.", "\"어쩌면 당신이 내 뒤를 밟은 게 이번이 처음이 아닐지도 모르지."], ["Are you trying to find out something bad about me?”", "나에 대해 뭔가 나쁜 걸 캐내려는 거요?\""]], [["“Not at all!", "\"전혀 아니에요!"], ["I have never done such a thing before, and I shouldn’t have done it now if words had not sometimes been dropped about you.”", "전엔 한 번도 그런 적 없고, 당신에 대해 이런저런 말이 가끔 흘러나오지 않았더라면 지금도 안 했을 거예요.\""]], [["“What _do_ you mean?”", "\"대체 무슨 소리요?\""], ["he impatiently asked.", "그가 참지 못하고 물었다."]], [["“They say—they say you used to go to Alderworth in the evenings, and it puts into my mind what I have heard about—”", "\"사람들이 그러는데—당신이 저녁마다 올더워스에 가곤 했다더라고요, 그래서 내가 들은 어떤 얘기가 떠올라서—\""]], [["Wildeve turned angrily and stood up in front of her.", "와일디브가 성을 내며 돌아서 그녀 앞에 우뚝 섰다."], ["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["he said, flourishing his hand in the air, “just out with it, madam!", "그가 손을 허공에 휘저으며 말했다. \"어디 다 털어놔보시오, 부인!"], ["I demand to know what remarks you have heard.”", "당신이 무슨 말을 들었는지 내 알아야겠소.\""]], [["“Well, I heard that you used to be very fond of Eustacia—nothing more than that, though dropped in a bit-by-bit way.", "\"음, 당신이 예전에 유스테이샤를 무척 좋아했다는 얘기를 들었어요—그게 다예요, 그것도 한 토막씩 흘리는 식으로요."], ["You ought not to be angry!”", "화내지 마세요!\""]], [["He observed that her eyes were brimming with tears.", "그는 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 것을 보았다."], ["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["he said, “there is nothing new in that, and of course I don’t mean to be rough towards you, so you need not cry.", "그가 말했다. \"그거야 새삼스러울 게 없는 얘기고, 물론 나는 당신에게 거칠게 굴 생각은 없으니, 울 것 없소."], ["Now, don’t let us speak of the subject any more.”", "자, 이 얘기는 더 하지 맙시다.\""]], [["And no more was said, Thomasin being glad enough of a reason for not mentioning Clym’s visit to her that evening, and his story.", "그리고 더는 말이 없었으니, 토머신으로서는 그날 저녁 클림이 자기를 찾아온 일과 그가 한 이야기를 입에 올리지 않을 구실이 생긴 것이 적이 다행이었다."]], [["VII.", "VII."], ["The Night of the Sixth of November", "십일월 엿샛날 밤"]], [["Having resolved on flight Eustacia at times seemed anxious that something should happen to thwart her own intention.", "탈출을 결심하고 나자 유스테이샤는 때때로 무슨 일인가 일어나 제 의도가 어그러지기를 바라는 듯했다."], ["The only event that could really change her position was the appearance of Clym.", "그녀의 처지를 정녕 바꿔놓을 수 있는 단 하나의 사건은 클림이 나타나는 것이었다."], ["The glory which had encircled him as her lover was departed now; yet some good simple quality of his would occasionally return to her memory and stir a momentary throb of hope that he would again present himself before her.", "연인으로서 그를 감싸던 그 영광은 이제 가셨으나, 그래도 그의 어떤 선량하고 소박한 자질이 이따금 그녀의 기억으로 되돌아와, 그가 다시 제 앞에 나타나주리라는 한순간의 희망의 떨림을 자아내곤 했다."], ["But calmly considered it was not likely that such a severance as now existed would ever close up—she would have to live on as a painful object, isolated, and out of place.", "그러나 차분히 헤아려보면, 지금처럼 벌어진 단절이 다시 메워지리라고는 도무지 보이지 않았으니—그녀는 고립되고 어울리지 않는, 고통스러운 존재로 살아가야 할 터였다."], ["She had used to think of the heath alone as an uncongenial spot to be in; she felt it now of the whole world.", "예전에는 황야만을 머물기에 마뜩잖은 곳으로 여겼으나, 이제는 온 세상을 그렇게 느꼈다."]], [["Towards evening on the sixth her determination to go away again revived.", "엿샛날 저녁 무렵, 다시 떠나리라는 그녀의 결심이 되살아났다."], ["About four o’clock she packed up anew the few small articles she had brought in her flight from Alderworth, and also some belonging to her which had been left here; the whole formed a bundle not too large to be carried in her hand for a distance of a mile or two.", "네 시쯤 그녀는 올더워스에서 도망쳐 나올 때 가져왔던 몇 안 되는 자잘한 물건들과, 이곳에 두고 갔던 자기 소지품 몇 가지를 새로 꾸렸으니, 모두 합쳐 한두 마일쯤은 손에 들고 갈 만한 그리 크지 않은 보따리가 되었다."], ["The scene without grew darker; mud-coloured clouds bellied downwards from the sky like vast hammocks slung across it, and with the increase of night a stormy wind arose; but as yet there was no rain.", "바깥 풍경은 점점 어두워졌으며, 진흙빛 구름이 마치 하늘을 가로질러 매단 거대한 그물침대처럼 하늘에서 아래로 불룩이 처져 내렸고, 밤이 깊어감에 따라 사나운 바람이 일었으나, 아직 비는 내리지 않았다."]], [["Eustacia could not rest indoors, having nothing more to do, and she wandered to and fro on the hill, not far from the house she was soon to leave.", "유스테이샤는 더 할 일도 없는 터라 집 안에 가만히 있을 수가 없어, 곧 떠나게 될 그 집에서 멀지 않은 언덕을 이리저리 거닐었다."], ["In these desultory ramblings she passed the cottage of Susan Nunsuch, a little lower down than her grandfather’s.", "이렇게 정처 없이 헤매던 중 그녀는 할아버지의 집보다 조금 더 아래쪽에 있는 수전 넌서치의 오두막을 지나게 되었다."], ["The door was ajar, and a riband of bright firelight fell over the ground without.", "문이 빠끔히 열려 있어, 밝은 불빛의 띠가 바깥 땅 위로 드리워져 있었다."], ["As Eustacia crossed the firebeams she appeared for an instant as distinct as a figure in a phantasmagoria—a creature of light surrounded by an area of darkness; the moment passed, and she was absorbed in night again.", "유스테이샤가 그 불빛 줄기를 가로지르자 그녀는 한순간 환등 속의 형상처럼 또렷이—어둠의 영역에 둘러싸인 빛의 피조물처럼—드러났으나, 그 순간이 지나자 그녀는 다시 밤 속으로 빨려들었다."]], [["A woman who was sitting inside the cottage had seen and recognized her in that momentary irradiation.", "오두막 안에 앉아 있던 한 여인이 그 한순간의 빛 속에서 그녀를 보고 알아보았다."], ["This was Susan herself, occupied in preparing a posset for her little boy, who, often ailing, was now seriously unwell.", "그것은 수전 자신이었으니, 자주 골골거리다가 이제는 심히 앓아누운 어린 아들에게 줄 따뜻한 우유죽을 만들고 있던 참이었다."], ["Susan dropped the spoon, shook her fist at the vanished figure, and then proceeded with her work in a musing, absent way.", "수전은 숟가락을 떨어뜨리고, 사라진 형상을 향해 주먹을 흔들고는, 골똘히 넋이 나간 듯한 태도로 다시 제 일을 이어갔다."]], [["At eight o’clock, the hour at which Eustacia had promised to signal Wildeve if ever she signalled at all, she looked around the premises to learn if the coast was clear, went to the furze-rick, and pulled thence a long-stemmed bough of that fuel.", "여덟 시, 곧 유스테이샤가 신호를 보낼 일이 있다면 와일디브에게 신호를 보내기로 약속한 그 시각에, 그녀는 집 안팎을 둘러보아 인적이 없는지 살핀 뒤, 가시금작화 더미로 가서 그 땔감의 긴 줄기가 달린 가지 하나를 뽑아냈다."], ["This she carried to the corner of the bank, and, glancing behind to see if the shutters were all closed, she struck a light, and kindled the furze.", "이것을 그녀는 둑 모퉁이로 가져가, 덧창이 다 닫혀 있는지 뒤를 흘끗 돌아본 다음, 불을 켜 가시금작화에 불을 붙였다."], ["When it was thoroughly ablaze Eustacia took it by the stem and waved it in the air above her head till it had burned itself out.", "그것이 완전히 활활 타오르자 유스테이샤는 줄기를 잡고 그것이 다 타버릴 때까지 머리 위 허공에 휘저었다."]], [["She was gratified, if gratification were possible to such a mood, by seeing a similar light in the vicinity of Wildeve’s residence a minute or two later.", "이런 심정에 만족이라는 게 가능하다면, 그녀는 일이 분쯤 뒤 와일디브의 거처 근처에서 비슷한 불빛이 보이는 것에 만족했다."], ["Having agreed to keep watch at this hour every night, in case she should require assistance, this promptness proved how strictly he had held to his word.", "도움이 필요할 경우를 대비해 이 시각에 매일 밤 망을 보기로 약속해두었던바, 이 신속함은 그가 얼마나 철저히 제 약속을 지켰는지를 증명해주었다."], ["Four hours after the present time, that is, at midnight, he was to be ready to drive her to Budmouth, as prearranged.", "지금부터 네 시간 뒤, 곧 자정에 그는 미리 정해둔 대로 그녀를 버드머스로 데려갈 채비를 하고 있어야 했다."]], [["Eustacia returned to the house.", "유스테이샤는 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["Supper having been got over she retired early, and sat in her bedroom waiting for the time to go by.", "저녁 식사가 끝나자 그녀는 일찌감치 물러나, 시간이 가기를 기다리며 침실에 앉아 있었다."], ["The night being dark and threatening, Captain Vye had not strolled out to gossip in any cottage or to call at the inn, as was sometimes his custom on these long autumn nights; and he sat sipping grog alone downstairs.", "밤이 어둡고 험상궂은 터라 바이 선장은 이 긴 가을밤이면 더러 그러하듯 어느 오두막에 어슬렁어슬렁 잡담을 하러 나가거나 주막에 들르지도 않고, 아래층에서 홀로 그로그주를 홀짝이며 앉아 있었다."], ["About ten o’clock there was a knock at the door.", "열 시쯤 문 두드리는 소리가 났다."], ["When the servant opened it the rays of the candle fell upon the form of Fairway.", "하인이 문을 열자 촛불이 페어웨이의 모습을 비추었다."]], [["“I was a-forced to go to Lower Mistover tonight,”", "\"오늘 밤 어쩔 수 없이 로어 미스토버에 가야 했는데,\""], ["he said, “and Mr.", "그가 말했다."], ["Yeobright asked me to leave this here on my way; but, faith, I put it in the lining of my hat, and thought no more about it till I got back and was hasping my gate before going to bed.", "\"요브라이트 씨가 가는 길에 이걸 여기 두고 가달라더군요. 헌데, 원, 그걸 모자 안감에 끼워놓고는, 돌아와서 자기 전에 대문을 걸려다가 다시 떠올릴 때까지 까맣게 잊고 있었지 뭐요."], ["So I have run back with it at once.”", "그래서 곧장 들고 되돌아온 거요.\""]], [["He handed in a letter and went his way.", "그는 편지 한 통을 건네고는 제 갈 길을 갔다."], ["The girl brought it to the captain, who found that it was directed to Eustacia.", "하녀가 그것을 선장에게 가져갔는데, 선장은 그것이 유스테이샤에게 보내진 것임을 알았다."], ["He turned it over and over, and fancied that the writing was her husband’s, though he could not be sure.", "그는 그것을 이리저리 뒤집어보며, 확신할 수는 없었으나 글씨가 그녀 남편의 것이리라 짐작했다."], ["However, he decided to let her have it at once if possible, and took it upstairs for that purpose; but on reaching the door of her room and looking in at the keyhole he found there was no light within, the fact being that Eustacia, without undressing, had flung herself upon the bed, to rest and gather a little strength for her coming journey.", "어쨌든 그는 되도록 빨리 그녀에게 전해주기로 마음먹고 그러려고 그것을 위층으로 가져갔으나, 그녀의 방문에 이르러 열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보니 안에 불빛이 없었으니, 실은 유스테이샤가 옷도 벗지 않은 채 다가올 여정에 대비해 잠깐 쉬며 기운을 좀 차리려고 침대에 몸을 던진 것이었다."], ["Her grandfather concluded from what he saw that he ought not to disturb her; and descending again to the parlour he placed the letter on the mantelpiece to give it to her in the morning.", "할아버지는 본 것으로 미루어 그녀를 깨우지 않는 편이 낫겠다 판단하고, 다시 응접실로 내려와 아침에 그녀에게 주려고 편지를 벽난로 선반 위에 놓아두었다."]], [["At eleven o’clock he went to bed himself, smoked for some time in his bedroom, put out his light at half-past eleven, and then, as was his invariable custom, pulled up the blind before getting into bed, that he might see which way the wind blew on opening his eyes in the morning, his bedroom window commanding a view of the flagstaff and vane.", "열한 시에 그는 자신도 잠자리에 들어, 침실에서 한동안 담배를 피우다가 열한 시 반에 불을 끄고는, 늘 변함없이 하는 버릇대로, 침대에 들기 전에 차양을 걷어 올렸으니, 아침에 눈을 뜰 때 바람이 어느 쪽으로 부는지 볼 수 있도록 함이었고, 그의 침실 창은 깃대와 풍향계가 내다보이는 자리였다."], ["Just as he had lain down he was surprised to observe the white pole of the staff flash into existence like a streak of phosphorus drawn downwards across the shade of night without.", "막 자리에 누웠을 때, 그는 깃대의 흰 기둥이 마치 바깥 밤의 그늘을 가로질러 아래로 그은 인광의 줄무늬처럼 번뜩 나타나는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["Only one explanation met this—a light had been suddenly thrown upon the pole from the direction of the house.", "이를 설명할 길은 단 하나뿐이었으니—집 쪽에서 그 기둥에 갑자기 불빛이 던져진 것이었다."], ["As everybody had retired to rest the old man felt it necessary to get out of bed, open the window softly, and look to the right and left.", "모두가 잠자리에 든 터라 노인은 침대에서 나와 살그머니 창을 열고 좌우를 살펴볼 필요를 느꼈다."], ["Eustacia’s bedroom was lighted up, and it was the shine from her window which had lighted the pole.", "유스테이샤의 침실에 불이 켜져 있었고, 기둥을 비춘 것은 그녀의 창에서 나온 빛이었다."], ["Wondering what had aroused her, he remained undecided at the window, and was thinking of fetching the letter to slip it under her door, when he heard a slight brushing of garments on the partition dividing his room from the passage.", "무엇이 그녀를 깨웠을까 의아해하며 그는 창가에서 망설이고 있었고, 편지를 가져다 그녀의 방문 밑으로 밀어 넣을까 생각하던 차에, 그의 방과 복도를 가르는 칸막이벽 너머에서 옷자락이 가볍게 스치는 소리를 들었다."]], [["The captain concluded that Eustacia, feeling wakeful, had gone for a book, and would have dismissed the matter as unimportant if he had not also heard her distinctly weeping as she passed.", "선장은 유스테이샤가 잠이 깨어 책을 가지러 갔으려니 짐작했고, 그녀가 지나가며 또렷이 우는 소리를 듣지 않았더라면 대수롭지 않은 일로 여기고 넘겼을 것이다."]], [["“She is thinking of that husband of hers,”", "\"그 남편 생각을 하고 있구나.\""], ["he said to himself.", "그가 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“Ah, the silly goose!", "\"아아, 어리석은 것!"], ["she had no business to marry him.", "그자와 혼인할 일이 아니었거늘."], ["I wonder if that letter is really his?”", "저 편지가 정말 그자 것일까?\""]], [["He arose, threw his boat-cloak round him, opened the door, and said, “Eustacia!”", "그는 일어나 보트클로크를 둘러 걸치고 문을 열며 말했다. \"유스테이샤!\""], ["There was no answer.", "대답이 없었다."], ["“Eustacia!”", "\"유스테이샤!\""], ["he repeated louder, “there is a letter on the mantelpiece for you.”", "그가 더 크게 되풀이했다. \"벽난로 선반 위에 네게 온 편지가 있다.\""]], [["But no response was made to this statement save an imaginary one from the wind, which seemed to gnaw at the corners of the house, and the stroke of a few drops of rain upon the windows.", "그러나 이 말에 돌아온 대답이라곤, 집 모퉁이를 갉아대는 듯한 바람의 가상의 응답과, 창문에 떨어지는 몇 방울 빗방울의 두드림뿐이었다."]], [["He went on to the landing, and stood waiting nearly five minutes.", "그는 층계참으로 나가, 거의 오 분을 기다리며 서 있었다."], ["Still she did not return.", "그래도 그녀는 돌아오지 않았다."], ["He went back for a light, and prepared to follow her; but first he looked into her bedroom.", "그는 불을 가지러 되돌아갔다가 그녀를 따라가려 채비했으나, 우선 그녀의 침실을 들여다보았다."], ["There, on the outside of the quilt, was the impression of her form, showing that the bed had not been opened; and, what was more significant, she had not taken her candlestick downstairs.", "거기, 누비이불 위에 그녀의 몸이 누웠던 자국이 남아 있어, 침대를 펴지 않았음을 보여주었으며, 더욱 의미심장하게도 그녀는 촛대를 아래층으로 들고 가지 않았다."], ["He was now thoroughly alarmed; and hastily putting on his clothes he descended to the front door, which he himself had bolted and locked.", "이제 그는 완전히 질겁하여, 황급히 옷을 챙겨 입고 자기가 손수 빗장을 지르고 잠가둔 현관문으로 내려갔다."], ["It was now unfastened.", "그것은 이제 잠겨 있지 않았다."], ["There was no longer any doubt that Eustacia had left the house at this midnight hour; and whither could she have gone?", "유스테이샤가 이 한밤중에 집을 나섰다는 데에는 더 이상 의심의 여지가 없었으니, 대체 그녀는 어디로 갔단 말인가?"], ["To follow her was almost impossible.", "그녀를 뒤쫓기란 거의 불가능했다."], ["Had the dwelling stood in an ordinary road, two persons setting out, one in each direction, might have made sure of overtaking her; but it was a hopeless task to seek for anybody on a heath in the dark, the practicable directions for flight across it from any point being as numerous as the meridians radiating from the pole.", "만일 집이 평범한 길가에 있었다면, 두 사람이 각기 한 방향씩 나서서 그녀를 따라잡을 수 있었으련만, 어둠 속 황야에서 누군가를 찾는다는 것은 가망 없는 일이었으니, 어느 지점에서든 황야를 가로질러 도망칠 수 있는 길은 극점에서 뻗어 나가는 자오선만큼이나 수두룩한 까닭이었다."], ["Perplexed what to do, he looked into the parlour, and was vexed to find that the letter still lay there untouched.", "어찌할 바를 몰라 그는 응접실을 들여다보았다가, 편지가 여전히 손도 대지 않은 채 거기 놓여 있는 것을 보고 부아가 치밀었다."]], [["At half-past eleven, finding that the house was silent, Eustacia had lighted her candle, put on some warm outer wrappings, taken her bag in her hand, and, extinguishing the light again, descended the staircase.", "열한 시 반, 집 안이 조용해진 것을 알고 유스테이샤는 촛불을 켜고, 따뜻한 겉옷을 두르고, 손에 가방을 든 다음, 다시 불을 끄고 계단을 내려왔다."], ["When she got into the outer air she found that it had begun to rain, and as she stood pausing at the door it increased, threatening to come on heavily.", "바깥 공기 속으로 나서자 그녀는 비가 내리기 시작한 것을 알았고, 문가에 멈춰 서 있는 동안 비는 점점 거세져, 세차게 쏟아질 기세였다."], ["But having committed herself to this line of action there was no retreating for bad weather.", "그러나 이 행로에 몸을 맡긴 이상 궂은 날씨 때문에 물러설 수는 없었다."], ["Even the receipt of Clym’s letter would not have stopped her now.", "클림의 편지를 받았다 해도 이제는 그녀를 멈추게 하지 못했을 것이다."], ["The gloom of the night was funereal; all nature seemed clothed in crape.", "밤의 음울함은 장례식 같았으니, 온 자연이 상장을 두른 듯했다."], ["The spiky points of the fir trees behind the house rose into the sky like the turrets and pinnacles of an abbey.", "집 뒤편 전나무들의 뾰족한 꼭대기는 마치 수도원의 작은 탑과 첨탑처럼 하늘로 솟아 있었다."], ["Nothing below the horizon was visible save a light which was still burning in the cottage of Susan Nunsuch.", "지평선 아래로는 아무것도 보이지 않았으니, 다만 수전 넌서치의 오두막에서 여전히 타고 있는 불빛 하나뿐이었다."]], [["Eustacia opened her umbrella and went out from the enclosure by the steps over the bank, after which she was beyond all danger of being perceived.", "유스테이샤는 우산을 펴고 둑 위 계단을 통해 울안에서 나갔으며, 그 후로는 누군가에게 들킬 위험에서 완전히 벗어났다."], ["Skirting the pool, she followed the path towards Rainbarrow, occasionally stumbling over twisted furze roots, tufts of rushes, or oozing lumps of fleshy fungi, which at this season lay scattered about the heath like the rotten liver and lungs of some colossal animal.", "연못을 끼고 돌아, 그녀는 레인배로 쪽 오솔길을 따라갔으니, 이따금 뒤틀린 가시금작화 뿌리며, 골풀 다발이며, 진물이 흐르는 물컹한 균류 덩어리에 걸려 비틀거렸는데, 그 균류는 이 철이면 마치 어떤 거대한 짐승의 썩은 간과 허파처럼 황야 곳곳에 흩어져 있었다."], ["The moon and stars were closed up by cloud and rain to the degree of extinction.", "달과 별은 구름과 비에 막혀 아주 꺼져버린 지경이었다."], ["It was a night which led the traveller’s thoughts instinctively to dwell on nocturnal scenes of disaster in the chronicles of the world, on all that is terrible and dark in history and legend—the last plague of Egypt, the destruction of Sennacherib’s host, the agony in Gethsemane.", "그것은 나그네의 생각을 절로 세상의 연대기에 적힌 밤의 재앙 장면들로, 역사와 전설 속의 온갖 끔찍하고 어두운 것들로—이집트의 마지막 재앙으로, 산헤립 군대의 멸망으로, 겟세마네의 고뇌로—이끄는 밤이었다."]], [["Eustacia at length reached Rainbarrow, and stood still there to think.", "유스테이샤는 마침내 레인배로에 이르러, 생각에 잠기려 그곳에 멈춰 섰다."], ["Never was harmony more perfect than that between the chaos of her mind and the chaos of the world without.", "그녀의 마음의 혼돈과 바깥 세상의 혼돈 사이만큼 완벽한 조화는 일찍이 없었다."], ["A sudden recollection had flashed on her this moment—she had not money enough for undertaking a long journey.", "한 가지 기억이 이 순간 그녀에게 번뜩 떠올랐으니—긴 여정에 나서기에는 돈이 충분하지 않다는 것이었다."], ["Amid the fluctuating sentiments of the day her unpractical mind had not dwelt on the necessity of being well-provided, and now that she thoroughly realized the conditions she sighed bitterly and ceased to stand erect, gradually crouching down under the umbrella as if she were drawn into the Barrow by a hand from beneath.", "그날의 변덕스러운 감정들 한가운데서 그녀의 비현실적인 머리는 채비를 잘 갖춰야 한다는 필요를 헤아리지 못했고, 이제 그 사정을 절절히 깨닫자 그녀는 쓰라리게 한숨지으며 더는 똑바로 서 있지 못하고, 마치 아래에서 뻗어 나온 손에 봉분 속으로 끌려들기라도 하듯 우산 아래 차츰 몸을 웅크렸다."], ["Could it be that she was to remain a captive still?", "자기가 여전히 포로로 남아야 한단 말인가?"], ["Money—she had never felt its value before.", "돈—그녀는 한 번도 그 값어치를 느껴본 적이 없었다."], ["Even to efface herself from the country means were required.", "이 고장에서 자취를 지우는 데조차 수단이 필요했다."], ["To ask Wildeve for pecuniary aid without allowing him to accompany her was impossible to a woman with a shadow of pride left in her; to fly as his mistress—and she knew that he loved her—was of the nature of humiliation.", "자기와 동행하는 것을 허락하지 않으면서 와일디브에게 금전적 도움을 청하기란, 자존심의 그림자라도 남은 여인에게는 불가능한 일이었으며, 그의 정부로 도망친다는 것은—그가 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 그녀는 알았으나—굴욕에 다름 아니었다."]], [["Anyone who had stood by now would have pitied her, not so much on account of her exposure to weather, and isolation from all of humanity except the mouldered remains inside the tumulus; but for that other form of misery which was denoted by the slightly rocking movement that her feelings imparted to her person.", "지금 곁에 서 있던 누군가가 있었다면 그녀를 가엾이 여겼으리라, 비록 그것이 그녀가 비바람에 내몰린 탓이나, 봉분 속 삭아버린 유해 말고는 온 인류에서 고립된 탓이라기보다는, 그녀의 감정이 그 몸에 실어주는 그 살짝 흔들리는 움직임으로 드러나던 그 다른 형태의 비참함 탓이었다."], ["Extreme unhappiness weighed visibly upon her.", "극도의 불행이 그녀를 눈에 띄게 짓누르고 있었다."], ["Between the drippings of the rain from her umbrella to her mantle, from her mantle to the heather, from the heather to the earth, very similar sounds could be heard coming from her lips; and the tearfulness of the outer scene was repeated upon her face.", "우산에서 망토로, 망토에서 히스로, 히스에서 땅으로 비가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 사이사이, 그것과 아주 흡사한 소리가 그녀의 입술에서도 흘러나왔으며, 바깥 풍경의 눈물겨움이 그녀의 얼굴 위에서 되풀이되었다."], ["The wings of her soul were broken by the cruel obstructiveness of all about her; and even had she seen herself in a promising way of getting to Budmouth, entering a steamer, and sailing to some opposite port, she would have been but little more buoyant, so fearfully malignant were other things.", "그녀의 영혼의 날개는 주위 온갖 것들의 모진 가로막음에 부러져버렸으며, 설령 자기가 버드머스로 가서 증기선에 올라 어느 맞은편 항구로 떠나는 가망 있는 길에 들어선 모습을 보았다 한들, 다른 것들이 너무도 무섭도록 악의에 차 있던 터라 그녀는 그다지 더 들떴을 리 없었다."], ["She uttered words aloud.", "그녀는 소리 내어 말을 내뱉었다."], ["When a woman in such a situation, neither old, deaf, crazed, nor whimsical, takes upon herself to sob and soliloquize aloud there is something grievous the matter.", "늙지도, 귀먹지도, 미치지도, 변덕스럽지도 않은 여인이 이런 처지에서 소리 내어 흐느끼고 혼잣말을 하기에 이른다면, 무언가 비통한 일이 있는 것이다."]], [["“Can I go, can I go?” she moaned.", "\"갈 수 있을까, 갈 수 있을까?\""], ["“He’s not _great_ enough for me to give myself to—he does not suffice for my desire!...", "그녀가 신음했다. \"그이는 내가 나를 바칠 만큼 위대하지 못해—그이는 내 욕망을 채워주지 못해!……"], ["If he had been a Saul or a Bonaparte—ah!", "그이가 사울이나 보나파르트 같은 사람이었더라면—아아!"], ["But to break my marriage vow for him—it is too poor a luxury!...", "허나 그런 사람을 위해 내 혼인 서약을 깨다니—그건 너무도 초라한 호사야!……"], ["And I have no money to go alone!", "게다가 나는 혼자 떠날 돈도 없어!"], ["And if I could, what comfort to me?", "또 설령 떠난들, 그게 나에게 무슨 위안이 될까?"], ["I must drag on next year, as I have dragged on this year, and the year after that as before.", "나는 올해를 끌어왔듯 내년에도 끌어가야 할 테고, 그다음 해도 그전과 같겠지."], ["How I have tried and tried to be a splendid woman, and how destiny has been against me!...", "내가 얼마나 찬란한 여인이 되려 애쓰고 또 애썼는데, 운명은 또 얼마나 나를 거슬러왔는지!……"], ["I do not deserve my lot!”", "나는 이런 신세를 당할 까닭이 없어!\""], ["she cried in a frenzy of bitter revolt.", "그녀가 쓰라린 반항의 광기에 휩싸여 외쳤다."], ["“O, the cruelty of putting me into this ill-conceived world!", "\"오, 나를 이 잘못 빚어진 세상에 들여놓다니 이 무슨 잔혹함인가!"], ["I was capable of much; but I have been injured and blighted and crushed by things beyond my control!", "나는 많은 것을 할 수 있는 사람이었건만, 내 힘으로 어쩔 수 없는 것들에 다치고 시들고 짓밟혀버렸어!"], ["O, how hard it is of Heaven to devise such tortures for me, who have done no harm to Heaven at all!”", "오, 하늘에는 아무 해도 끼친 적 없는 나에게 이런 고문을 궁리해내다니, 하늘은 어찌 이리도 모진가!\""]], [["The distant light which Eustacia had cursorily observed in leaving the house came, as she had divined, from the cottage window of Susan Nunsuch.", "유스테이샤가 집을 나설 때 얼핏 보았던 그 멀리의 불빛은, 그녀가 짐작한 대로 수전 넌서치의 오두막 창에서 나온 것이었다. 그러나 유스테이샤가 짐작하지 못한 것은 그 순간 안에 있는 여인이 하고 있던 일이었다."], ["What Eustacia did not divine was the occupation of the woman within at that moment.", "그날 저녁 좀 더 일찍, 앓는 아이가 \"엄마, 나 정말 너무 괴로워!\""], ["Susan’s sight of her passing figure earlier in the evening, not five minutes after the sick boy’s exclamation, “Mother, I do feel so bad!” persuaded the matron that an evil influence was certainly exercised by Eustacia’s propinquity.", "하고 외친 지 채 오 분도 안 되어 그녀가 지나가는 모습을 본 것이, 그 부인으로 하여금 유스테이샤가 가까이 있음으로써 분명 어떤 악한 기운이 미치고 있다고 확신케 했다."]], [["On this account Susan did not go to bed as soon as the evening’s work was over, as she would have done at ordinary times.", "이런 까닭에 수전은 평소 같으면 저녁 일이 끝나자마자 곧장 잠자리에 들었을 것을 그러지 않았다."], ["To counteract the malign spell which she imagined poor Eustacia to be working, the boy’s mother busied herself with a ghastly invention of superstition, calculated to bring powerlessness, atrophy, and annihilation on any human being against whom it was directed.", "가엾은 유스테이샤가 부린다고 그녀가 상상한 그 악한 주술을 막으려고, 아이의 어머니는 어떤 섬뜩한 미신의 술수에 몰두했으니, 그것은 그 대상이 된 어떤 인간에게든 무력함과 쇠약과 파멸을 가져온다고 여겨지는 것이었다."], ["It was a practice well known on Egdon at that date, and one that is not quite extinct at the present day.", "그것은 그 시절 에그던에서 익히 알려진 풍습이었으며, 오늘날까지도 아주 사라지지는 않은 것이었다."]], [["She passed with her candle into an inner room, where, among other utensils, were two large brown pans, containing together perhaps a hundredweight of liquid honey, the produce of the bees during the foregoing summer.", "그녀는 촛불을 들고 안쪽 방으로 들어갔으니, 그곳에는 다른 살림 도구들 가운데 큼직한 갈색 항아리 두 개가 있었는데, 둘을 합치면 어쩌면 백웨이트쯤 되는 묽은 꿀이 담겨 있었으며, 지난여름 벌들이 거둔 산물이었다."], ["On a shelf over the pans was a smooth and solid yellow mass of a hemispherical form, consisting of beeswax from the same take of honey.", "항아리 위 선반에는 매끈하고 단단한 노란 덩어리가 반구 모양으로 놓여 있었으니, 그 꿀을 거둘 때 함께 나온 밀랍이었다."], ["Susan took down the lump, and cutting off several thin slices, heaped them in an iron ladle, with which she returned to the living-room, and placed the vessel in the hot ashes of the fireplace.", "수전은 그 덩어리를 내려, 얇게 몇 조각을 잘라 쇠국자에 수북이 담아 거실로 돌아와서는, 그 그릇을 벽난로의 뜨거운 잿더미 속에 놓았다."], ["As soon as the wax had softened to the plasticity of dough she kneaded the pieces together.", "밀랍이 반죽처럼 말랑말랑해지자 그녀는 그 조각들을 한데 이겨 붙였다."], ["And now her face became more intent.", "그러자 그녀의 얼굴은 한층 골똘해졌다."], ["She began moulding the wax; and it was evident from her manner of manipulation that she was endeavouring to give it some preconceived form.", "그녀는 밀랍을 빚기 시작했으며, 그 다루는 손놀림으로 미루어 거기에 미리 마음에 둔 어떤 형상을 부여하려 애쓰고 있음이 분명했다."], ["The form was human.", "그 형상은 사람이었다."]], [["By warming and kneading, cutting and twisting, dismembering and re-joining the incipient image she had in about a quarter of an hour produced a shape which tolerably well resembled a woman, and was about six inches high.", "데우고 이기고, 자르고 비틀고, 떼었다 다시 붙이고 하며 만들다 만 그 형상을 가지고 그녀는 십오 분쯤 만에 제법 여인과 비슷한, 높이 육 인치쯤 되는 모양을 빚어냈다."], ["She laid it on the table to get cold and hard.", "그녀는 그것을 식어 단단해지도록 탁자 위에 놓았다."], ["Meanwhile she took the candle and went upstairs to where the little boy was lying.", "그러는 사이 그녀는 촛불을 들고 어린 아들이 누워 있는 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["“Did you notice, my dear, what Mrs. Eustacia wore this afternoon besides the dark dress?”", "\"얘야, 오늘 오후에 유스테이샤 부인이 그 검은 옷 말고 또 무얼 걸치고 있었는지 봤느냐?\""]], [["“A red ribbon round her neck.”", "\"목에 빨간 리본을 둘렀어요.\""]], [["“Anything else?”", "\"또 다른 건?\""]], [["“No—except sandal-shoes.”", "\"아뇨—샌들 신발 말고는요.\""]], [["“A red ribbon and sandal-shoes,”", "\"빨간 리본에 샌들 신발이라.\""], ["she said to herself.", "그녀가 혼자 중얼거렸다."]], [["Mrs. Nunsuch went and searched till she found a fragment of the narrowest red ribbon, which she took downstairs and tied round the neck of the image.", "넌서치 부인은 가서 뒤지다가 가장 가느다란 빨간 리본 한 조각을 찾아내, 그것을 아래층으로 가져가 형상의 목에 둘러 묶었다."], ["Then fetching ink and a quilt from the rickety bureau by the window, she blackened the feet of the image to the extent presumably covered by shoes; and on the instep of each foot marked cross-lines in the shape taken by the sandalstrings of those days.", "그런 다음 창가의 삐걱대는 책상에서 잉크와 깃펜을 가져와, 신발에 가려졌으리라 짐작되는 만큼 형상의 발을 검게 칠했으며, 양 발등에는 그 시절 샌들 끈이 이루던 모양대로 가로지른 선들을 그어 표시했다."], ["Finally she tied a bit of black thread round the upper part of the head, in faint resemblance to a snood worn for confining the hair.", "마지막으로 그녀는 머리 윗부분에 검은 실 한 가닥을 둘러 묶었으니, 머리카락을 여미려 두르던 머리띠를 어렴풋이 본뜬 것이었다."]], [["Susan held the object at arm’s length and contemplated it with a satisfaction in which there was no smile.", "수전은 그것을 팔 끝까지 뻗어 들고서, 웃음기라곤 없는 만족스러운 눈으로 그것을 바라보았다."], ["To anybody acquainted with the inhabitants of Egdon Heath the image would have suggested Eustacia Yeobright.", "에그던 황야의 주민들을 아는 이라면 누구든 그 형상에서 유스테이샤 요브라이트를 떠올렸을 것이다."]], [["From her workbasket in the window-seat the woman took a paper of pins, of the old long and yellow sort, whose heads were disposed to come off at their first usage.", "여인은 창가 자리의 반짇고리에서 핀 한 쌈을 꺼냈으니, 옛날식의 길고 누런 종류로, 처음 쓸 때부터 머리가 잘 빠지는 것들이었다."], ["These she began to thrust into the image in all directions, with apparently excruciating energy.", "이것들을 그녀는 형상에 사방으로 찔러 넣기 시작했으니, 보아하니 살을 에는 듯한 기세였다."], ["Probably as many as fifty were thus inserted, some into the head of the wax model, some into the shoulders, some into the trunk, some upwards through the soles of the feet, till the figure was completely permeated with pins.", "아마도 쉰 개쯤을 그렇게 찔러 넣었을 터인데, 더러는 밀랍 모형의 머리에, 더러는 어깨에, 더러는 몸통에, 더러는 발바닥을 뚫고 위쪽으로 박아, 마침내 그 형상은 온통 핀으로 빈틈없이 뒤덮였다."]], [["She turned to the fire.", "그녀는 불 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["It had been of turf; and though the high heap of ashes which turf fires produce was somewhat dark and dead on the outside, upon raking it abroad with the shovel the inside of the mass showed a glow of red heat.", "그것은 토탄 불이었는데, 토탄 불이 만들어내는 그 높이 쌓인 잿더미가 겉은 다소 거뭇하고 사위어 있었으나, 부삽으로 그것을 헤쳐놓자 그 속살은 시뻘건 열기로 이글거리고 있었다."], ["She took a few pieces of fresh turf from the chimney-corner and built them together over the glow, upon which the fire brightened.", "그녀는 굴뚝 모퉁이에서 새 토탄 몇 덩이를 가져와 그 불기 위에 쌓아 올렸고, 그러자 불이 환해졌다."], ["Seizing with the tongs the image that she had made of Eustacia, she held it in the heat, and watched it as it began to waste slowly away.", "부젓가락으로 자기가 만든 유스테이샤의 형상을 집어, 그녀는 그것을 그 열기 속에 들이대고, 그것이 서서히 사위어가기 시작하는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["And while she stood thus engaged there came from between her lips a murmur of words.", "그리고 그렇게 골몰해 서 있는 동안 그녀의 입술 사이로 나직이 웅얼거리는 말이 새어 나왔다."]], [["It was a strange jargon—the Lord’s Prayer repeated backwards—the incantation usual in proceedings for obtaining unhallowed assistance against an enemy.", "그것은 기이한 주문이었으니—거꾸로 외는 주기도문이었으며—원수를 거스를 부정한 도움을 얻으려는 일에서 으레 쓰이는 주술이었다."], ["Susan uttered the lugubrious discourse three times slowly, and when it was completed the image had considerably diminished.", "수전은 그 음울한 사설을 세 번 천천히 읊조렸고, 그것이 끝났을 때 형상은 상당히 줄어들어 있었다."], ["As the wax dropped into the fire a long flame arose from the spot, and curling its tongue round the figure ate still further into its substance.", "밀랍이 불 속으로 떨어지자 그 자리에서 긴 불꽃이 일어, 혀를 형상에 감으며 그 몸을 한층 더 파먹어 들어갔다."], ["A pin occasionally dropped with the wax, and the embers heated it red as it lay.", "이따금 핀 하나가 밀랍과 함께 떨어지면, 잉걸불이 그것이 놓인 채로 시뻘겋게 달구었다."]], [["VIII.", "VIII."], ["Rain, Darkness, and Anxious Wanderers", "비와, 어둠과, 애타게 헤매는 이들"]], [["While the effigy of Eustacia was melting to nothing, and the fair woman herself was standing on Rainbarrow, her soul in an abyss of desolation seldom plumbed by one so young, Yeobright sat lonely at Blooms-End.", "유스테이샤의 형상이 녹아 사라져가고, 그 아름다운 여인 자신은 레인배로에 서서, 그토록 젊은 사람으로서는 좀처럼 가늠하기 힘든 황폐의 심연에 영혼을 담그고 있을 무렵, 요브라이트는 블룸스엔드에 홀로 앉아 있었다."], ["He had fulfilled his word to Thomasin by sending off Fairway with the letter to his wife, and now waited with increased impatience for some sound or signal of her return.", "그는 페어웨이를 시켜 아내에게 편지를 보냄으로써 토머신에게 한 약속을 이행했으며, 이제는 그녀가 돌아오리라는 어떤 기척이나 신호를 더한층 초조하게 기다리고 있었다."], ["Were Eustacia still at Mistover the very least he expected was that she would send him back a reply tonight by the same hand; though, to leave all to her inclination, he had cautioned Fairway not to ask for an answer.", "유스테이샤가 아직 미스토버에 있다면, 그가 바란 최소한의 것은 그녀가 오늘 밤 같은 사람 편에 답장을 보내주리라는 것이었으나, 모든 것을 그녀의 마음에 맡기고자 그는 페어웨이에게 답장을 청하지 말라고 일러두었다."], ["If one were handed to him he was to bring it immediately; if not, he was to go straight home without troubling to come round to Blooms-End again that night.", "답장이 그에게 건네지면 즉시 가져오되, 그렇지 않으면 그날 밤 다시 블룸스엔드로 돌아오는 수고를 할 것 없이 곧장 집으로 가라고 했다."]], [["But secretly Clym had a more pleasing hope.", "그러나 클림은 속으로 더 흐뭇한 희망을 품고 있었다."], ["Eustacia might possibly decline to use her pen—it was rather her way to work silently—and surprise him by appearing at his door.", "유스테이샤가 어쩌면 펜을 들기를 마다할지도 모르고—말없이 일을 처리하는 것이 차라리 그녀다운 방식이었으니—제 집 문간에 나타나 그를 놀라게 할지도 몰랐다."], ["How fully her mind was made up to do otherwise he did not know.", "그녀의 마음이 얼마나 굳게 그와는 다른 쪽으로 정해져 있는지 그는 알지 못했다."]], [["To Clym’s regret it began to rain and blow hard as the evening advanced.", "클림으로서는 안타깝게도 저녁이 깊어가면서 비가 내리고 바람이 거세지기 시작했다."], ["The wind rasped and scraped at the corners of the house, and filliped the eavesdroppings like peas against the panes.", "바람이 집 모퉁이를 긁고 할퀴며, 처마에서 떨어지는 물방울을 콩알처럼 유리창에 튀겼다."], ["He walked restlessly about the untenanted rooms, stopping strange noises in windows and doors by jamming splinters of wood into the casements and crevices, and pressing together the leadwork of the quarries where it had become loosened from the glass.", "그는 빈방들을 안절부절못하고 거닐며, 나뭇조각을 창틀과 틈새에 끼워 넣고, 유리에서 헐거워진 격자창의 납틀을 서로 눌러 붙임으로써 창과 문에서 나는 기이한 소리들을 멎게 했다."], ["It was one of those nights when cracks in the walls of old churches widen, when ancient stains on the ceilings of decayed manor houses are renewed and enlarged from the size of a man’s hand to an area of many feet.", "오래된 교회 벽의 갈라진 틈이 더 벌어지고, 쇠락한 장원 저택 천장의 해묵은 얼룩이 사람 손바닥만 한 크기에서 여러 피트 넓이로 새로 번지고 커지는, 그런 밤들 중 하나였다."], ["The little gate in the palings before his dwelling continually opened and clicked together again, but when he looked out eagerly nobody was there; it was as if invisible shapes of the dead were passing in on their way to visit him.", "집 앞 말뚝 울타리의 작은 대문이 끊임없이 열렸다가 다시 딸깍 닫혔으나, 그가 애타게 내다보면 거기엔 아무도 없었으니, 마치 죽은 이들의 보이지 않는 형상들이 그를 찾아오는 길에 들어서고 있는 듯했다."]], [["Between ten and eleven o’clock, finding that neither Fairway nor anybody else came to him, he retired to rest, and despite his anxieties soon fell asleep.", "열 시와 열한 시 사이, 페어웨이도 그 누구도 자기에게 오지 않는 것을 알고 그는 잠자리에 들었으며, 온갖 근심에도 이내 잠이 들었다."], ["His sleep, however, was not very sound, by reason of the expectancy he had given way to, and he was easily awakened by a knocking which began at the door about an hour after.", "그러나 그가 빠져들었던 그 기대 탓에 그의 잠은 그리 깊지 못했고, 한 시간쯤 뒤 문에서 시작된 두드림에 그는 쉽게 잠이 깼다."], ["Clym arose and looked out of the window.", "클림은 일어나 창밖을 내다보았다."], ["Rain was still falling heavily, the whole expanse of heath before him emitting a subdued hiss under the downpour.", "비는 여전히 세차게 내리고 있었고, 그 앞에 펼쳐진 황야 전체가 쏟아지는 빗줄기 아래 나직이 쉭쉭거리는 소리를 내고 있었다."], ["It was too dark to see anything at all.", "너무 어두워 아무것도 보이지 않았다."]], [["“Who’s there?”", "\"거기 누구요?\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."]], [["Light footsteps shifted their position in the porch, and he could just distinguish in a plaintive female voice the words, “O Clym, come down and let me in!”", "가벼운 발소리가 현관에서 자리를 옮겼고, 그는 애처로운 여인의 목소리로 \"오, 클림, 내려와 나를 들여보내줘요!\" 하는 말을 겨우 알아들을 수 있었다."]], [["He flushed hot with agitation.", "그는 동요로 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐다."], ["“Surely it is Eustacia!”", "\"분명 유스테이샤야!\""], ["he murmured.", "그가 중얼거렸다."], ["If so, she had indeed come to him unawares.", "그렇다면 그녀가 정말 뜻밖에 그에게 온 것이었다."]], [["He hastily got a light, dressed himself, and went down.", "그는 급히 불을 켜고 옷을 챙겨 입고는 내려갔다."], ["On his flinging open the door the rays of the candle fell upon a woman closely wrapped up, who at once came forward.", "문을 활짝 열어젖히자 촛불이 몸을 꽁꽁 싸맨 한 여인을 비추었으니, 그녀가 즉시 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["“Thomasin!”", "\"토머신!\""], ["he exclaimed in an indescribable tone of disappointment.", "그가 형용할 수 없는 실망의 어조로 외쳤다."], ["“It is Thomasin, and on such a night as this!", "\"토머신이로구나, 그것도 이런 밤에!"], ["O, where is Eustacia?”", "오, 유스테이샤는 어디 있는 거지?\""]], [["Thomasin it was, wet, frightened, and panting.", "토머신이었으니, 흠뻑 젖고 겁에 질려 헐떡이고 있었다."]], [["“Eustacia?", "\"유스테이샤요?"], ["I don’t know, Clym; but I can think,”", "나도 몰라요, 클림. 하지만 짐작은 가요.\""], ["she said with much perturbation.", "그녀가 몹시 동요하며 말했다."], ["“Let me come in and rest—I will explain this.", "\"안에 들어가 좀 쉬게 해줘요—내가 설명할게요."], ["There is a great trouble brewing—my husband and Eustacia!”", "큰일이 벌어지고 있어요—내 남편하고 유스테이샤가!\""]], [["“What, what?”", "\"뭐라, 뭐라고?\""]], [["“I think my husband is going to leave me or do something dreadful—I don’t know what—Clym, will you go and see?", "\"내 남편이 나를 떠나려는 것 같아요, 아니면 무슨 끔찍한 짓을 하려는 건지—뭔지 모르겠어요—클림, 가서 좀 봐주겠어요? 나를 도와줄 사람은 오라버니밖에 없어요."], ["I have nobody to help me but you; Eustacia has not yet come home?” “No.”", "유스테이샤는 아직 집에 안 왔죠?\""]], [["She went on breathlessly: “Then they are going to run off together!", "\"안 왔다.\" 그녀가 숨가쁘게 이어갔다."], ["He came indoors tonight about eight o’clock and said in an off-hand way, ‘Tamsie, I have just found that I must go a journey.’ ‘When?’ I said.", "\"그럼 둘이서 함께 달아나려는 거예요! 그이가 오늘 밤 여덟 시쯤 집에 들어와서는 아무렇지 않은 듯 말하더라고요, '탐시, 방금 알았는데 나 어디 좀 다녀와야겠소.'"], ["‘Tonight,’ he said.", "'언제요?' 내가 물었죠."], ["‘Where?’ I asked him.", "'오늘 밤이오.' 그가 말했어요."], ["‘I cannot tell you at present,’ he said; ‘I shall be back again tomorrow.’ He then went and busied himself in looking up his things, and took no notice of me at all.", "'어디로요?' 내가 물었죠."], ["I expected to see him start, but he did not, and then it came to be ten o’clock, when he said, ‘You had better go to bed.’ I didn’t know what to do, and I went to bed.", "'지금은 말해줄 수 없소.' 그가 말했어요."], ["I believe he thought I fell asleep, for half an hour after that he came up and unlocked the oak chest we keep money in when we have much in the house and took out a roll of something which I believe was banknotes, though I was not aware that he had ’em there.", "'내일 다시 돌아올 거요.' 그러고는 가서 제 물건들을 챙기느라 부산을 떨며, 나는 거들떠보지도 않았어요."], ["These he must have got from the bank when he went there the other day.", "나는 그이가 떠나는 걸 보려니 했는데, 떠나질 않더니, 열 시가 되자 '당신은 이만 자는 게 좋겠소' 하더군요."], ["What does he want banknotes for, if he is only going off for a day?", "나는 어찌할 바를 몰라 잠자리에 들었어요. 그이는 내가 잠든 줄 알았나봐요, 그러고 반 시간쯤 뒤에 올라와서는 집에 돈이 많을 때 넣어두는 떡갈나무 궤짝을 열고 무언가 둘둘 만 것을 꺼냈는데, 지폐 뭉치였던 것 같아요, 거기 그런 게 있는 줄도 몰랐는데 말이에요."], ["When he had gone down I thought of Eustacia, and how he had met her the night before—I know he did meet her, Clym, for I followed him part of the way; but I did not like to tell you when you called, and so make you think ill of him, as I did not think it was so serious.", "그건 분명 며칠 전 은행에 갔을 때 거기서 찾아온 거겠죠. 하루만 다녀올 거라면서 지폐는 뭣 하러 필요한 걸까요?"], ["Then I could not stay in bed; I got up and dressed myself, and when I heard him out in the stable I thought I would come and tell you.", "그이가 내려간 뒤 나는 유스테이샤 생각이 났어요, 그이가 어젯밤 그녀를 만난 것도요—분명히 만났어요, 클림, 내가 가는 길 일부를 따라가봤거든요. 하지만 오라버니가 찾아왔을 때는 차마 말할 수가 없었어요, 그래서 오라버니가 그이를 나쁘게 여기게 하고 싶지 않았던 거예요, 그렇게 심각한 일인 줄은 몰랐으니까요."], ["So I came downstairs without any noise and slipped out.”", "그러고 나니 도저히 침대에 있을 수가 없어서, 일어나 옷을 챙겨 입고, 그이가 마구간에 나가 있는 소리가 들리기에 와서 오라버니한테 알려야겠다 싶었어요. 그래서 소리 없이 아래층으로 내려와 빠져나온 거예요.\""]], [["“Then he was not absolutely gone when you left?”", "\"그럼 네가 떠나올 때 그는 아직 완전히 가버린 건 아니었구나?\""]], [["“No.", "\"네."], ["Will you, dear Cousin Clym, go and try to persuade him not to go?", "사랑하는 클림 오라버니, 가서 그이가 떠나지 않도록 설득해보겠어요?"], ["He takes no notice of what I say, and puts me off with the story of his going on a journey, and will be home tomorrow, and all that; but I don’t believe it.", "그이는 내가 하는 말은 들은 척도 안 하고, 여행을 다녀온다, 내일 돌아온다, 하는 따위의 얘기로 나를 따돌리는데, 나는 그 말을 믿지 않아요."], ["I think you could influence him.”", "오라버니라면 그이를 움직일 수 있을 것 같아요.\""]], [["“I’ll go,”", "\"가마.\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."], ["“O, Eustacia!”", "\"오, 유스테이샤!\""]], [["Thomasin carried in her arms a large bundle; and having by this time seated herself she began to unroll it, when a baby appeared as the kernel to the husks—dry, warm, and unconscious of travel or rough weather.", "토머신은 두 팔에 커다란 보따리를 안고 있었는데, 이때쯤 자리에 앉아 그것을 풀기 시작하자, 겉껍질 속의 알맹이처럼 아기가 나타났으니—보송보송하고 따뜻하며, 여정도 궂은 날씨도 모르는 채였다."], ["Thomasin briefly kissed the baby, and then found time to begin crying as she said, “I brought baby, for I was afraid what might happen to her.", "토머신은 아기에게 가볍게 입을 맞추고는, 그제야 짬을 내어 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"이 애한테 무슨 일이 생길까 두려워 데려왔어요."], ["I suppose it will be her death, but I couldn’t leave her with Rachel!”", "아마 이 애 목숨을 앗는 일이 되겠지만, 레이철한테 맡기고 올 수가 없었어요!\""]], [["Clym hastily put together the logs on the hearth, raked abroad the embers, which were scarcely yet extinct, and blew up a flame with the bellows.", "클림은 급히 화덕에 장작을 모아 쌓고, 아직 채 꺼지지 않은 잉걸불을 헤쳐 놓고는, 풀무로 불을 일으켰다."]], [["“Dry yourself,”", "\"몸을 말리거라.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I’ll go and get some more wood.”", "\"내가 가서 장작을 좀 더 가져오마.\""]], [["“No, no—don’t stay for that.", "\"아뇨, 아뇨—그것 때문에 지체하지 말아요."], ["I’ll make up the fire.", "불은 내가 지필게요."], ["Will you go at once—please will you?”", "곧장 가주겠어요—제발 가주겠어요?\""]], [["Yeobright ran upstairs to finish dressing himself.", "요브라이트는 옷차림을 마무리하려 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다."], ["While he was gone another rapping came to the door.", "그가 간 사이 또 한 번 문 두드리는 소리가 났다."], ["This time there was no delusion that it might be Eustacia’s—the footsteps just preceding it had been heavy and slow.", "이번에는 그것이 유스테이샤의 것일지 모른다는 헛된 기대는 없었으니—그에 앞서 들린 발소리가 묵직하고 느렸던 까닭이다."], ["Yeobright thinking it might possibly be Fairway with a note in answer, descended again and opened the door.", "요브라이트는 어쩌면 페어웨이가 답장을 가지고 온 것일지도 모른다 생각하며 다시 내려가 문을 열었다."]], [["“Captain Vye?”", "\"바이 선장님?\""], ["he said to a dripping figure.", "그가 물에 흠뻑 젖은 형상에게 말했다."]], [["“Is my granddaughter here?”", "\"내 손녀가 여기 있는가?\""], ["said the captain.", "선장이 말했다. \"아뇨.\""]], [["“No.” “Then where is she?”", "\"그럼 그 애는 어디 있나?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“But you ought to know—you are her husband.”", "\"하지만 자네가 알아야지—자네가 그 애 남편이 아닌가.\""]], [["“Only in name apparently,”", "\"이름만 그런 모양이지요.\""], ["said Clym with rising excitement.", "클림이 점점 격해지며 말했다."], ["“I believe she means to elope tonight with Wildeve.", "\"그녀는 오늘 밤 와일디브와 달아날 작정인 것 같습니다."], ["I am just going to look to it.”", "저는 막 그걸 살피러 가려던 참이고요.\""]], [["“Well, she has left my house; she left about half an hour ago.", "\"음, 그 애는 내 집을 떠났네. 반 시간쯤 전에 나갔어."], ["Who’s sitting there?”", "거기 앉아 있는 건 누군가?\""]], [["“My cousin Thomasin.”", "\"제 사촌 토머신입니다.\""]], [["The captain bowed in a preoccupied way to her.", "선장이 정신이 딴 데 팔린 듯 그녀에게 인사를 했다."], ["“I only hope it is no worse than an elopement,”", "\"그저 사랑의 도피보다 더 나쁜 일이나 아니길 바랄 뿐이네.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Worse?", "\"더 나쁘다니요?"], ["What’s worse than the worst a wife can do?”", "아내가 저지를 수 있는 가장 나쁜 일보다 더 나쁜 게 무어란 말입니까?\""]], [["“Well, I have been told a strange tale.", "\"음, 내 기이한 이야기를 하나 들었네."], ["Before starting in search of her I called up Charley, my stable lad.", "그 애를 찾아 나서기 전에 내 마구간지기 소년 찰리를 불러 세웠지."], ["I missed my pistols the other day.”", "며칠 전 내 권총들이 없어진 걸 알았거든.\""]], [["“Pistols?”", "\"권총이요?\""]], [["“He said at the time that he took them down to clean.", "\"그때 그 애는 닦으려고 그걸 내렸다고 하더군."], ["He has now owned that he took them because he saw Eustacia looking curiously at them; and she afterwards owned to him that she was thinking of taking her life, but bound him to secrecy, and promised never to think of such a thing again.", "헌데 이제 와 털어놓기를, 유스테이샤가 그걸 묘하게 바라보는 걸 봐서 가져갔다는 거야. 그리고 그 애는 나중에 그에게 제 목숨을 끊을 생각을 하고 있었다고 실토했다지, 헌데 그에게 비밀을 지키라 다짐을 두고, 다시는 그런 생각을 않겠다 약속했다는군."], ["I hardly suppose she will ever have bravado enough to use one of them; but it shows what has been lurking in her mind; and people who think of that sort of thing once think of it again.”", "그 애가 그중 하나를 쓸 만큼 객기를 부리리라고는 도무지 생각지 않네만, 그건 그 애 마음속에 무엇이 도사려왔는지를 보여주지. 그리고 한 번 그런 걸 생각한 사람은 또 생각하는 법이거든.\""]], [["“Where are the pistols?”", "\"권총들은 어디 있습니까?\""]], [["“Safely locked up.", "\"안전하게 잠가뒀네."], ["O no, she won’t touch them again.", "오 아니, 그 애는 다시는 거기 손대지 않을 거야."], ["But there are more ways of letting out life than through a bullet-hole.", "하지만 총알구멍 말고도 목숨을 내보낼 길은 더 있지."], ["What did you quarrel about so bitterly with her to drive her to all this?", "대체 무엇 때문에 그 애와 그리 모질게 다투어 그 애를 이 지경까지 몰아넣은 건가?"], ["You must have treated her badly indeed.", "자네가 그 애를 정말 못되게 다룬 게 틀림없어."], ["Well, I was always against the marriage, and I was right.”", "음, 나는 늘 이 혼인을 반대했고, 내가 옳았던 거야.\""]], [["“Are you going with me?”", "\"저와 함께 가시겠습니까?\""], ["said Yeobright, paying no attention to the captain’s latter remark.", "요브라이트가 선장의 뒷말에는 아랑곳하지 않고 말했다."], ["“If so I can tell you what we quarrelled about as we walk along.”", "\"그러시면 가는 길에 우리가 무엇 때문에 다투었는지 말씀드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“Where to?”", "\"어디로 가는가?\""]], [["“To Wildeve’s—that was her destination, depend upon it.”", "\"와일드브 집으로—거기가 그 사람 목적지요, 틀림없이.\""]], [["Thomasin here broke in, still weeping: “He said he was only going on a sudden short journey; but if so why did he want so much money?", "토마신이 여기서 여전히 흐느끼며 끼어들었다. \"그이는 그저 갑작스럽게 잠깐 다녀올 데가 있다고 했어요."], ["O, Clym, what do you think will happen?", "하지만 정말 그렇다면 왜 그렇게 많은 돈이 필요했겠어요?"], ["I am afraid that you, my poor baby, will soon have no father left to you!”", "아, 클림, 무슨 일이 벌어질 것 같아요? 가엾은 우리 아기, 너는 곧 아버지마저 잃게 될까 봐 무서워요!\""]], [["“I am off now,”", "\"이제 가야겠어요.\""], ["said Yeobright, stepping into the porch.", "요브라이트가 현관으로 들어서며 말했다."]], [["“I would fain go with ’ee,”", "\"나도 자네와 함께 가고 싶네만.\""], ["said the old man doubtfully.", "노인이 미심쩍게 말했다."], ["“But I begin to be afraid that my legs will hardly carry me there such a night as this.", "\"이런 밤에 내 다리가 거기까지 나를 데려다줄 수 있을지 슬슬 두렵네."], ["I am not so young as I was.", "예전만큼 젊지가 못하니."], ["If they are interrupted in their flight she will be sure to come back to me, and I ought to be at the house to receive her.", "만약 도망치다가 누가 가로막으면 그 애는 분명 내게로 돌아올 테고, 그러면 나는 집에서 그 애를 맞이해야 하지."], ["But be it as ’twill I can’t walk to the Quiet Woman, and that’s an end on’t.", "하지만 어찌 됐든 나는 콰이엇 우먼까지 걸어갈 수 없으니, 그걸로 끝일세."], ["I’ll go straight home.”", "곧장 집으로 가겠네.\""]], [["“It will perhaps be best,” said Clym.", "\"그게 아마 가장 나을 겁니다.\""], ["“Thomasin, dry yourself, and be as comfortable as you can.”", "클림이 말했다. \"토마신, 몸을 좀 말리고 되도록 편히 있어요.\""]], [["With this he closed the door upon her, and left the house in company with Captain Vye, who parted from him outside the gate, taking the middle path, which led to Mistover.", "이 말과 함께 그는 그녀 앞에서 문을 닫고는, 바이 선장과 함께 집을 나섰는데, 선장은 대문 밖에서 그와 갈라져 미스토버로 이어지는 가운뎃길로 접어들었다."], ["Clym crossed by the right-hand track towards the inn.", "클림은 오른쪽 샛길을 가로질러 여관 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["Thomasin, being left alone, took off some of her wet garments, carried the baby upstairs to Clym’s bed, and then came down to the sitting-room again, where she made a larger fire, and began drying herself.", "홀로 남겨진 토마신은 젖은 옷가지를 몇 벌 벗고, 아기를 위층 클림의 침대로 안고 올라갔다가, 다시 거실로 내려와 불을 더 크게 지피고는 몸을 말리기 시작했다."], ["The fire soon flared up the chimney, giving the room an appearance of comfort that was doubled by contrast with the drumming of the storm without, which snapped at the windowpanes and breathed into the chimney strange low utterances that seemed to be the prologue to some tragedy.", "불은 곧 굴뚝 위로 활활 타올라 방 안에 안락한 기운을 자아냈는데, 그것은 바깥에서 두드려대는 폭풍과의 대비로 곱절이 되었으니, 폭풍은 창유리를 물어뜯고 굴뚝 속으로 기이한 낮은 소리를 불어넣었으며, 그 소리는 어떤 비극의 서막인 듯했다."]], [["But the least part of Thomasin was in the house, for her heart being at ease about the little girl upstairs she was mentally following Clym on his journey.", "하지만 토마신의 마음은 거의 집 안에 있지 않았으니, 위층 어린 딸에 대한 걱정이 가라앉자 그녀는 마음속으로 길 떠난 클림을 뒤따르고 있었다."], ["Having indulged in this imaginary peregrination for some considerable interval, she became impressed with a sense of the intolerable slowness of time.", "이 상상의 여정에 상당한 시간 동안 빠져 있다가, 그녀는 시간이 견딜 수 없을 만큼 더디 흐른다는 느낌에 사로잡혔다. 그래도 그녀는 그대로 앉아 있었다."], ["But she sat on. The moment then came when she could scarcely sit longer, and it was like a satire on her patience to remember that Clym could hardly have reached the inn as yet.", "그러다 더는 앉아 있을 수 없는 순간이 닥쳤고, 클림이 아직 여관에 닿았을 리 만무하다는 것을 떠올리니 그것은 마치 그녀의 인내심을 비웃는 풍자 같았다."], ["At last she went to the baby’s bedside.", "마침내 그녀는 아기의 머리맡으로 갔다."], ["The child was sleeping soundly; but her imagination of possibly disastrous events at her home, the predominance within her of the unseen over the seen, agitated her beyond endurance.", "아이는 곤히 잠들어 있었다. 하지만 집에서 어쩌면 끔찍한 일이 벌어졌을지 모른다는 상상, 보이는 것보다 보이지 않는 것이 그녀 안에서 우세를 점하는 형국이 견딜 수 없을 만큼 그녀를 흔들어놓았다."], ["She could not refrain from going down and opening the door.", "그녀는 아래층으로 내려가 문을 여는 것을 참을 수 없었다."], ["The rain still continued, the candlelight falling upon the nearest drops and making glistening darts of them as they descended across the throng of invisible ones behind.", "비는 여전히 쏟아지고 있었고, 촛불은 가장 가까운 빗방울들 위로 떨어져 그것들이 뒤편에 무리 지은 보이지 않는 빗방울들을 가로질러 내려올 때 반짝이는 화살처럼 만들어냈다."], ["To plunge into that medium was to plunge into water slightly diluted with air.", "그 매질 속으로 뛰어든다는 것은 공기로 살짝 묽어진 물속으로 뛰어드는 것이나 다름없었다."], ["But the difficulty of returning to her house at this moment made her all the more desirous of doing so—anything was better than suspense.", "하지만 지금 이 순간에 집으로 돌아가기가 어렵다는 점이 도리어 그녀로 하여금 더욱 그렇게 하고 싶게 만들었으니—그 무엇이든 마음 졸이는 것보다는 나았다."], ["“I have come here well enough,”", "\"여기까지 그럭저럭 잘 왔잖아.\""], ["she said, “and why shouldn’t I go back again?", "그녀가 말했다. \"그러니 다시 못 돌아갈 게 뭐람?"], ["It is a mistake for me to be away.”", "내가 집을 비운 건 잘못이야.\""]], [["She hastily fetched the infant, wrapped it up, cloaked herself as before, and shoveling the ashes over the fire, to prevent accidents, went into the open air.", "그녀는 서둘러 아기를 데려와 감싸고, 전처럼 망토를 두른 다음, 사고를 막으려고 불 위에 재를 끼얹고는 바깥으로 나섰다."], ["Pausing first to put the door key in its old place behind the shutter, she resolutely turned her face to the confronting pile of firmamental darkness beyond the palings, and stepped into its midst.", "우선 멈춰 서서 문 열쇠를 덧문 뒤 본래 자리에 둔 뒤, 그녀는 단호히 울타리 너머에 마주 선 창공의 어둠 덩어리를 향해 얼굴을 돌리고 그 한복판으로 발을 내디뎠다."], ["But Thomasin’s imagination being so actively engaged elsewhere, the night and the weather had for her no terror beyond that of their actual discomfort and difficulty.", "하지만 토마신의 상상은 다른 데 그토록 활발히 매여 있었던 터라, 밤과 날씨는 그녀에게 실제의 불편함과 어려움 이상의 두려움은 주지 못했다."]], [["She was soon ascending Blooms-End valley and traversing the undulations on the side of the hill.", "이윽고 그녀는 블룸스엔드 골짜기를 오르며 언덕 비탈의 굴곡을 가로지르고 있었다."], ["The noise of the wind over the heath was shrill, and as if it whistled for joy at finding a night so congenial as this.", "황야 위로 부는 바람 소리는 날카로웠고, 마치 이토록 마음에 드는 밤을 만난 기쁨에 휘파람을 부는 듯했다."], ["Sometimes the path led her to hollows between thickets of tall and dripping bracken, dead, though not yet prostrate, which enclosed her like a pool.", "이따금 길은 그녀를 키 큰 고사리 덤불 사이 우묵한 곳으로 이끌었는데, 물방울이 뚝뚝 듣는 그 고사리들은 죽었으나 아직 쓰러지지 않은 채 그녀를 못처럼 에워쌌다."], ["When they were more than usually tall she lifted the baby to the top of her head, that it might be out of the reach of their drenching fronds.", "고사리가 평소보다 더 키 큰 곳에서는 그녀가 아기를 머리끝까지 들어 올려 흠뻑 젖은 잎사귀가 닿지 않게 했다."], ["On higher ground, where the wind was brisk and sustained, the rain flew in a level flight without sensible descent, so that it was beyond all power to imagine the remoteness of the point at which it left the bosoms of the clouds.", "바람이 거세고 끊임없는 더 높은 지대에서는 비가 느낄 만큼 떨어지는 기색도 없이 수평으로 날아들어, 그것이 구름의 품을 떠난 지점이 얼마나 먼지 가늠하기란 도무지 불가능했다."], ["Here self-defence was impossible, and individual drops stuck into her like the arrows into Saint Sebastian.", "여기서는 방어란 불가능했고, 빗방울 하나하나가 마치 성 세바스티아누스에게 박힌 화살처럼 그녀에게 박혔다."], ["She was enabled to avoid puddles by the nebulous paleness which signified their presence, though beside anything less dark than the heath they themselves would have appeared as blackness.", "그녀는 웅덩이의 존재를 알려주는 흐릿한 창백함 덕에 웅덩이를 피할 수 있었는데, 황야보다 덜 어두운 무언가 곁에 있었다면 그 웅덩이들 자체가 검은 빛깔로 보였을 것이었다."]], [["Yet in spite of all this Thomasin was not sorry that she had started.", "그러나 이 모든 것에도 토마신은 길을 나선 것을 후회하지 않았다."], ["To her there were not, as to Eustacia, demons in the air, and malice in every bush and bough.", "그녀에게는, 유스테이샤에게와 달리, 대기 속에 악령이 있다거나 모든 덤불과 가지마다 악의가 깃들어 있다거나 하지 않았다."], ["The drops which lashed her face were not scorpions, but prosy rain; Egdon in the mass was no monster whatever, but impersonal open ground.", "그녀의 얼굴을 후려치는 빗방울은 전갈이 아니라 그저 무미건조한 비였고, 에그던 전체도 결코 괴물 따위가 아니라 비인격적인 트인 땅이었다."], ["Her fears of the place were rational, her dislikes of its worst moods reasonable.", "이 장소에 대한 그녀의 두려움은 이치에 맞았고, 그 최악의 변덕에 대한 그녀의 혐오는 합당했다."], ["At this time it was in her view a windy, wet place, in which a person might experience much discomfort, lose the path without care, and possibly catch cold.", "지금 그녀가 보기에 이곳은 사람이 적잖이 불편을 겪고, 조심하지 않으면 길을 잃고, 어쩌면 감기에 걸릴 수도 있는, 바람 불고 비 내리는 장소였다."]], [["If the path is well known the difficulty at such times of keeping therein is not altogether great, from its familiar feel to the feet; but once lost it is irrecoverable.", "길을 잘 알면 이런 때에도 그 길을 벗어나지 않기란 그리 어렵지 않으니, 발에 닿는 익숙한 감촉 덕분이다. 하지만 일단 길을 잃으면 되찾을 길이 없다."], ["Owing to her baby, who somewhat impeded Thomasin’s view forward and distracted her mind, she did at last lose the track.", "토마신의 앞쪽 시야를 다소 가리고 정신을 흩뜨리는 아기 탓에, 그녀는 끝내 길을 잃고 말았다."], ["This mishap occurred when she was descending an open slope about two-thirds home.", "이 낭패는 그녀가 집까지 삼분의 이쯤 와서 트인 비탈을 내려가던 중에 일어났다."], ["Instead of attempting, by wandering hither and thither, the hopeless task of finding such a mere thread, she went straight on, trusting for guidance to her general knowledge of the contours, which was scarcely surpassed by Clym’s or by that of the heath-croppers themselves.", "이리저리 헤매며 한낱 실오라기 같은 길을 찾으려는 가망 없는 일을 시도하는 대신, 그녀는 곧장 앞으로 나아가며 지형의 윤곽에 관한 전반적인 지식에 의지해 길을 가늠했는데, 그 지식은 클림이나 황야의 풀 뜯는 사람들의 것에도 거의 뒤지지 않았다."]], [["At length Thomasin reached a hollow and began to discern through the rain a faint blotted radiance, which presently assumed the oblong form of an open door.", "마침내 토마신은 우묵한 곳에 이르러 빗속으로 희미하게 번진 빛을 분간하기 시작했는데, 그 빛은 이내 열린 문의 길쭉한 형태를 띠었다."], ["She knew that no house stood hereabouts, and was soon aware of the nature of the door by its height above the ground.", "그녀는 이 근방에 집이 없다는 것을 알았고, 그 문이 땅 위로 솟은 높이를 보고 곧 그것의 정체를 알아챘다."]], [["“Why, it is Diggory Venn’s van, surely!”", "\"아니, 이건 분명 디고리 벤의 마차잖아!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["A certain secluded spot near Rainbarrow was, she knew, often Venn’s chosen centre when staying in this neighbourhood; and she guessed at once that she had stumbled upon this mysterious retreat.", "레인배로 인근의 어느 외진 곳이, 그녀가 알기로, 벤이 이 일대에 머물 때 곧잘 택하는 거점이었다. 그래서 그녀는 자기가 이 수수께끼 같은 은신처에 우연히 닿았음을 단번에 짐작했다."], ["The question arose in her mind whether or not she should ask him to guide her into the path.", "그를 불러 길로 안내해달라고 청할 것인가 말 것인가 하는 물음이 그녀 마음에 일었다."], ["In her anxiety to reach home she decided that she would appeal to him, notwithstanding the strangeness of appearing before his eyes at this place and season.", "집에 닿고 싶은 조바심에, 그녀는 이런 장소와 계절에 그의 눈앞에 나타나는 일이 아무리 기이하다 한들 그에게 도움을 청하기로 마음을 정했다."], ["But when, in pursuance of this resolve, Thomasin reached the van and looked in she found it to be untenanted; though there was no doubt that it was the reddleman’s.", "하지만 이 결심을 좇아 토마신이 마차에 이르러 안을 들여다보니, 그것은 비어 있었다. 그것이 레들먼의 것임에는 의심의 여지가 없었지만 말이다."], ["The fire was burning in the stove, the lantern hung from the nail.", "난로에는 불이 타고 있었고, 등불은 못에 걸려 있었다."], ["Round the doorway the floor was merely sprinkled with rain, and not saturated, which told her that the door had not long been opened.", "문간 둘레의 바닥에는 비가 흩뿌려졌을 뿐 흠뻑 젖지는 않았는데, 이는 문이 열린 지 오래되지 않았음을 그녀에게 일러주었다."]], [["While she stood uncertainly looking in Thomasin heard a footstep advancing from the darkness behind her, and turning, beheld the well-known form in corduroy, lurid from head to foot, the lantern beams falling upon him through an intervening gauze of raindrops.", "머뭇거리며 안을 들여다보고 서 있는데 토마신은 뒤편 어둠 속에서 다가오는 발소리를 들었고, 돌아보니 머리부터 발끝까지 시뻘건, 코듀로이 차림의 낯익은 형상이 보였으며, 등불 빛이 가로놓인 빗방울의 망사를 뚫고 그에게 떨어지고 있었다."]], [["“I thought you went down the slope,”", "\"비탈 아래로 내려간 줄 알았는데.\""], ["he said, without noticing her face.", "그가 그녀의 얼굴은 알아보지 못한 채 말했다."], ["“How do you come back here again?”", "\"어쩌다 다시 여기로 돌아왔소?\""]], [["“Diggory?”", "\"디고리?\""], ["said Thomasin faintly.", "토마신이 가냘프게 말했다."]], [["“Who are you?”", "\"당신은 누구요?\""], ["said Venn, still unperceiving.", "벤이 여전히 알아채지 못하고 말했다."], ["“And why were you crying so just now?”", "\"그리고 방금은 왜 그리 울었소?\""]], [["“O, Diggory!", "\"아, 디고리!"], ["don’t you know me?”", "나를 못 알아보겠어요?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“But of course you don’t, wrapped up like this.", "\"하긴 이렇게 둘둘 감싸고 있으니 그럴 만도 하죠."], ["What do you mean?", "무슨 말이에요?"], ["I have not been crying here, and I have not been here before.”", "나는 여기서 운 적도 없고, 전에 여기 온 적도 없는걸요.\""]], [["Venn then came nearer till he could see the illuminated side of her form.", "그러자 벤이 더 가까이 다가와 그녀 형상의 불빛 비치는 쪽을 볼 수 있게 되었다."]], [["“Mrs. Wildeve!”", "\"와일드브 부인!\""], ["he exclaimed, starting.", "그가 흠칫 놀라며 외쳤다."], ["“What a time for us to meet!", "\"우리가 만날 때가 따로 있지!"], ["And the baby too!", "게다가 아기까지!"], ["What dreadful thing can have brought you out on such a night as this?”", "도대체 무슨 끔찍한 일이 있었기에 이런 밤에 밖으로 나오셨소?\""]], [["She could not immediately answer; and without asking her permission he hopped into his van, took her by the arm, and drew her up after him.", "그녀는 곧바로 대답할 수 없었다. 그러자 그는 허락을 구하지도 않고 자기 마차로 훌쩍 올라타 그녀의 팔을 잡고 뒤따라 끌어 올렸다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["he continued when they stood within.", "두 사람이 안에 서자 그가 이어 물었다."]], [["“I have lost my way coming from Blooms-End, and I am in a great hurry to get home.", "\"블룸스엔드에서 오는 길에 길을 잃었어요. 어서 집에 가야 해요."], ["Please show me as quickly as you can!", "되도록 빨리 길을 가르쳐주세요!"], ["It is so silly of me not to know Egdon better, and I cannot think how I came to lose the path.", "에그던을 더 잘 알지 못하는 게 어찌나 어리석은지, 어쩌다 길을 잃었는지 도무지 모르겠어요."], ["Show me quickly, Diggory, please.”", "어서 가르쳐주세요, 디고리, 제발요.\""]], [["“Yes, of course.", "\"그럼요, 물론이죠."], ["I will go with ’ee.", "함께 가드리리다."], ["But you came to me before this, Mrs. Wildeve?”", "그런데 부인, 이보다 먼저 제게 오시지 않았소?\""]], [["“I only came this minute.”", "\"방금 막 왔는걸요.\""]], [["“That’s strange.", "\"이상한 일이군요."], ["I was lying down here asleep about five minutes ago, with the door shut to keep out the weather, when the brushing of a woman’s clothes over the heath-bushes just outside woke me up, for I don’t sleep heavy, and at the same time I heard a sobbing or crying from the same woman.", "한 오 분 전쯤에 나는 날씨를 막으려고 문을 닫아두고 여기 누워 잠들어 있었는데, 바로 바깥 황야 덤불 위를 스치는 여인의 옷자락 소리에 잠이 깼소. 나는 잠귀가 밝아서 말이오."], ["I opened my door and held out my lantern, and just as far as the light would reach I saw a woman; she turned her head when the light sheened on her, and then hurried on downhill.", "그와 동시에 같은 여인이 흐느끼거나 우는 소리를 들었지요. 문을 열고 등불을 내미니, 빛이 닿는 데까지 어떤 여인이 보였소."], ["I hung up the lantern, and was curious enough to pull on my things and dog her a few steps, but I could see nothing of her any more.", "빛이 비치자 그 여인은 고개를 돌리더니, 비탈 아래로 서둘러 가버렸지. 나는 등불을 다시 걸고는, 호기심이 일어 옷을 챙겨 입고 몇 걸음 뒤쫓았지만, 그 여인은 더 이상 어디서도 보이지 않았소."], ["That was where I had been when you came up; and when I saw you I thought you were the same one.”", "부인이 올라왔을 때 내가 가 있던 데가 바로 거기였고, 부인을 보고 나는 같은 여인인 줄 알았던 거요.\""]], [["“Perhaps it was one of the heathfolk going home?”", "\"어쩌면 집으로 가던 황야 사람 가운데 하나였을지도 모르죠?\""]], [["“No, it couldn’t be.", "\"아니, 그럴 리 없소."], ["’Tis too late.", "너무 늦은 시각이오."], ["The noise of her gown over the he’th was of a whistling sort that nothing but silk will make.”", "황야 위를 스치던 그 가운 소리는 비단이 아니고서는 낼 수 없는 휘파람 같은 소리였소.\""]], [["“It wasn’t I, then.", "\"그럼 나는 아니었네요."], ["My dress is not silk, you see....", "보다시피 내 옷은 비단이 아니니까요……"], ["Are we anywhere in a line between Mistover and the inn?”", "우리가 지금 미스토버와 여관 사이 어디쯤에 있는 건가요?\""]], [["“Well, yes; not far out.”", "\"글쎄요, 그렇소. 그리 벗어나지는 않았소.\""]], [["“Ah, I wonder if it was she!", "\"아, 그게 그 여자였을까!"], ["Diggory, I must go at once!”", "디고리, 당장 가야겠어요!\""]], [["She jumped down from the van before he was aware, when Venn unhooked the lantern and leaped down after her.", "그가 미처 알아채기도 전에 그녀가 마차에서 뛰어내리자, 벤은 등불을 떼어 들고 그녀를 따라 뛰어내렸다."], ["“I’ll take the baby, ma’am,”", "\"아기는 제가 안겠소, 부인.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You must be tired out by the weight.”", "\"무게에 지쳐 녹초가 되셨겠소.\""]], [["Thomasin hesitated a moment, and then delivered the baby into Venn’s hands.", "토마신은 잠시 망설이다가 아기를 벤의 손에 건넸다. \"꽉 쥐지 마세요, 디고리.\""], ["“Don’t squeeze her, Diggory,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “or hurt her little arm; and keep the cloak close over her like this, so that the rain may not drop in her face.”", "\"작은 팔도 다치게 하지 말고요. 망토는 이렇게 바짝 덮어서 빗물이 얼굴에 떨어지지 않게 해주세요.\""]], [["“I will,”", "\"그러리다.\""], ["said Venn earnestly.", "벤이 진지하게 말했다."], ["“As if I could hurt anything belonging to you!”", "\"내가 어찌 부인에게 딸린 무엇 하나라도 다치게 하겠소!\""]], [["“I only meant accidentally,”", "\"그저 실수로 그럴까 봐 그런 거예요.\""], ["said Thomasin.", "토마신이 말했다."]], [["“The baby is dry enough, but you are pretty wet,”", "\"아기는 충분히 말라 있지만, 부인은 흠뻑 젖으셨소.\""], ["said the reddleman when, in closing the door of his cart to padlock it, he noticed on the floor a ring of water drops where her cloak had hung from her.", "레들먼은 자기 수레의 문을 닫아 자물쇠를 채우다가, 바닥에 그녀의 망토가 드리워졌던 자리로 동그랗게 떨어진 물방울 자국을 보고 말했다."]], [["Thomasin followed him as he wound right and left to avoid the larger bushes, stopping occasionally and covering the lantern, while he looked over his shoulder to gain some idea of the position of Rainbarrow above them, which it was necessary to keep directly behind their backs to preserve a proper course.", "토마신은 그가 더 큰 덤불을 피해 오른쪽 왼쪽으로 굽이굽이 나아가는 것을 뒤따랐는데, 그는 이따금 멈춰 서서 등불을 가리고는, 어깨너머로 머리 위 레인배로의 위치를 가늠하려 돌아보았으니, 제대로 된 방향을 유지하려면 그것을 정확히 등 뒤에 두어야 했다."]], [["“You are sure the rain does not fall upon baby?”", "\"비가 아기한테 안 떨어지는 거 확실해요?\""]], [["“Quite sure.", "\"아주 확실하오."], ["May I ask how old he is, ma’am?”", "한데 부인, 이 녀석은 몇 살이오?\""]], [["“He!” said Thomasin reproachfully.", "\"이 녀석이라뇨!\""], ["“Anybody can see better than that in a moment.", "토마신이 나무라듯 말했다. \"누구든 보면 한눈에 알 텐데."], ["She is nearly two months old.", "얘는 태어난 지 두 달이 다 됐어요."], ["How far is it now to the inn?”", "여관까지는 이제 얼마나 남았죠?\""]], [["“A little over a quarter of a mile.”", "\"사분의 일 마일 좀 넘게 남았소.\""]], [["“Will you walk a little faster?”", "\"좀 더 빨리 걸어줄래요?\""]], [["“I was afraid you could not keep up.”", "\"부인이 못 따라오실까 봐 그랬소.\""]], [["“I am very anxious to get there.", "\"거기 닿는 게 너무도 조급해요."], ["Ah, there is a light from the window!”", "아, 저기 창문에서 불빛이 비치네요!\""]], [["“’Tis not from the window.", "\"창문에서 나는 게 아니오."], ["That’s a gig-lamp, to the best of my belief.”", "내가 보기엔 마차 등불이오.\""]], [["“O!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Thomasin in despair.", "토마신이 절망하여 말했다."], ["“I wish I had been there sooner—give me the baby, Diggory—you can go back now.”", "\"더 일찍 거기 닿았더라면—아기를 주세요, 디고리—이제 돌아가셔도 돼요.\""]], [["“I must go all the way,”", "\"끝까지 가야 하오.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."], ["“There is a quag between us and that light, and you will walk into it up to your neck unless I take you round.”", "\"우리와 저 불빛 사이에 늪이 있어서, 내가 부인을 돌아가게 하지 않으면 목까지 빠지게 될 거요.\""]], [["“But the light is at the inn, and there is no quag in front of that.”", "\"하지만 불빛은 여관에 있고, 여관 앞에는 늪이 없잖아요.\""]], [["“No, the light is below the inn some two or three hundred yards.”", "\"아니오, 그 불빛은 여관에서 이삼백 야드쯤 아래에 있소.\""]], [["“Never mind,”", "\"상관없어요.\""], ["said Thomasin hurriedly.", "토마신이 서둘러 말했다."], ["“Go towards the light, and not towards the inn.”", "\"여관 쪽이 아니라 불빛 쪽으로 가요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"알겠소.\""], ["answered Venn, swerving round in obedience; and, after a pause, “I wish you would tell me what this great trouble is.", "벤이 그 말에 따라 방향을 틀며 답했고, 잠시 사이를 두었다가 말했다. \"이 큰 걱정거리가 무엇인지 제게 말씀해주시면 좋겠소."], ["I think you have proved that I can be trusted.”", "부인은 내가 믿을 만한 사람이라는 걸 증명받으신 셈이잖소.\""]], [["“There are some things that cannot be—cannot be told to—”", "\"세상엔 말할 수 없는—말할 수 없는 일이 있어요—\""], ["And then her heart rose into her throat, and she could say no more.", "그러고는 가슴이 목구멍까지 치밀어 올라 그녀는 더 말을 잇지 못했다."]], [["IX.", "IX."], ["Sights and Sounds Draw the Wanderers Together", "광경과 소리가 방랑자들을 한데로 이끌고"]], [["Having seen Eustacia’s signal from the hill at eight o’clock, Wildeve immediately prepared to assist her in her flight, and, as he hoped, accompany her.", "여덟 시에 언덕에서 유스테이샤의 신호를 보았던 와일드브는 곧바로 그녀의 도주를 돕고, 또 바라건대 그녀와 동행할 채비를 했다."], ["He was somewhat perturbed, and his manner of informing Thomasin that he was going on a journey was in itself sufficient to rouse her suspicions.", "그는 다소 마음이 어지러웠고, 길을 떠난다고 토마신에게 알린 그 태도만으로도 그녀의 의심을 사기에 충분했다."], ["When she had gone to bed he collected the few articles he would require, and went upstairs to the money-chest, whence he took a tolerably bountiful sum in notes, which had been advanced to him on the property he was so soon to have in possession, to defray expenses incidental to the removal.", "그녀가 잠자리에 들자 그는 필요한 몇 가지 물건을 챙긴 뒤, 위층의 돈궤로 가서 적잖이 넉넉한 금액의 지폐를 꺼냈는데, 그 돈은 그가 곧 손에 넣게 될 재산을 담보로 그에게 미리 내어준 것으로, 이주에 따르는 비용을 치르기 위한 것이었다."]], [["He then went to the stable and coach-house to assure himself that the horse, gig, and harness were in a fit condition for a long drive.", "그런 다음 그는 마구간과 마차 보관소로 가서 말과 이륜마차와 마구가 먼 길을 가기에 적당한 상태인지 확인했다."], ["Nearly half an hour was spent thus, and on returning to the house Wildeve had no thought of Thomasin being anywhere but in bed.", "거의 반 시간이 그렇게 흘렀고, 집으로 돌아온 와일드브는 토마신이 잠자리 말고 다른 어디에 있으리라고는 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["He had told the stable lad not to stay up, leading the boy to understand that his departure would be at three or four in the morning; for this, though an exceptional hour, was less strange than midnight, the time actually agreed on, the packet from Budmouth sailing between one and two.", "그는 마구간 소년에게 깨어 있지 말라고 일러두었는데, 자기 출발이 새벽 서너 시쯤이리라고 소년이 알아듣게끔 했으니, 그 시각이 비록 예사롭지는 않으나, 실제로 약속한 시각인 자정보다는 덜 이상했기 때문이요, 버드머스에서 오는 배는 한 시와 두 시 사이에 출항하기로 되어 있었다."]], [["At last all was quiet, and he had nothing to do but to wait.", "마침내 사방이 조용해졌고, 그는 기다리는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었다."], ["By no effort could he shake off the oppression of spirits which he had experienced ever since his last meeting with Eustacia, but he hoped there was that in his situation which money could cure.", "유스테이샤와 마지막으로 만난 이래로 줄곧 겪어온 마음의 짓눌림을 그는 아무리 애써도 떨쳐낼 수 없었으나, 자기 처지에는 돈으로 고칠 수 있는 무언가가 있으리라 바랐다."], ["He had persuaded himself that to act not ungenerously towards his gentle wife by settling on her the half of his property, and with chivalrous devotion towards another and greater woman by sharing her fate, was possible.", "그는 자기 재산의 절반을 온화한 아내에게 떼어줌으로써 그녀에게 야박하지 않게 굴고, 또 다른 더 위대한 여인과 운명을 함께함으로써 그녀에게 기사도적 헌신을 다하는 것이 가능하다고 스스로를 설득해두었다."], ["And though he meant to adhere to Eustacia’s instructions to the letter, to deposit her where she wished and to leave her, should that be her will, the spell that she had cast over him intensified, and his heart was beating fast in the anticipated futility of such commands in the face of a mutual wish that they should throw in their lot together.", "그리고 비록 그는 유스테이샤가 원하는 곳에 그녀를 내려놓고, 그녀의 뜻이 그렇다면 그녀를 떠나라는 그녀의 지시를 한 자도 어김없이 따르려 했으나, 그녀가 그에게 건 마력은 점점 강해졌고, 둘이 운명을 함께하기를 바라는 서로의 마음 앞에서 그런 지시가 부질없으리라는 예감에 그의 심장은 빠르게 뛰고 있었다."]], [["He would not allow himself to dwell long upon these conjectures, maxims, and hopes, and at twenty minutes to twelve he again went softly to the stable, harnessed the horse, and lit the lamps; whence, taking the horse by the head, he led him with the covered car out of the yard to a spot by the roadside some quarter of a mile below the inn.", "그는 이런 추측과 격언과 희망에 오래 머무는 것을 스스로 허락하지 않았으니, 열두 시 이십 분 전에 그는 다시 살그머니 마구간으로 가서 말에 마구를 채우고 등을 켰으며, 그런 다음 말의 머리를 잡고 덮개 씌운 수레와 함께 마당을 빠져나가 여관에서 사분의 일 마일쯤 아래 길가의 한 지점으로 끌고 갔다."]], [["Here Wildeve waited, slightly sheltered from the driving rain by a high bank that had been cast up at this place.", "여기서 와일드브는, 이곳에 쌓아 올려진 높은 둑에 몰아치는 비를 조금 피하며 기다렸다."], ["Along the surface of the road where lit by the lamps the loosened gravel and small stones scudded and clicked together before the wind, which, leaving them in heaps, plunged into the heath and boomed across the bushes into darkness.", "등불에 비친 길 위에서는 풀린 자갈과 작은 돌멩이들이 바람 앞에서 후드득 굴러가며 서로 부딪쳐 딸각거렸고, 바람은 그것들을 무더기로 남겨둔 채 황야 속으로 뛰어들어 덤불을 가로질러 어둠 속으로 우르릉거렸다."], ["Only one sound rose above this din of weather, and that was the roaring of a ten-hatch weir to the southward, from a river in the meads which formed the boundary of the heath in this direction.", "이 날씨의 소란 위로 솟아오르는 소리는 단 하나뿐이었으니, 그것은 남쪽으로 열 개의 수문을 둔 둑에서 나는 굉음이었고, 그 둑은 이 방향에서 황야의 경계를 이루는 풀밭의 강에 있었다."]], [["He lingered on in perfect stillness till he began to fancy that the midnight hour must have struck.", "그는 완벽한 정적 속에서 머뭇거리며 머물다가, 어느덧 자정이 쳤을 게 틀림없다는 생각이 들기 시작했다."], ["A very strong doubt had arisen in his mind if Eustacia would venture down the hill in such weather; yet knowing her nature he felt that she might.", "유스테이샤가 이런 날씨에 과연 언덕을 내려올까 하는 의구심이 그의 마음에 강하게 일었으나, 그녀의 성정을 아는 터라 그는 그녀가 그럴 수도 있겠다고 느꼈다."], ["“Poor thing!", "\"가엾은 것!"], ["’tis like her ill-luck,”", "그 애의 불운다운 일이지.\""], ["he murmured.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["At length he turned to the lamp and looked at his watch.", "마침내 그는 등불 쪽으로 돌아서서 시계를 보았다."], ["To his surprise it was nearly a quarter past midnight.", "놀랍게도 자정에서 십오 분쯤 지나 있었다."], ["He now wished that he had driven up the circuitous road to Mistover, a plan not adopted because of the enormous length of the route in proportion to that of the pedestrian’s path down the open hillside, and the consequent increase of labour for the horse.", "이제 그는 미스토버로 빙 도는 길로 마차를 몰고 올라갔더라면 좋았을 것이라 여겼는데, 그 길을 택하지 않은 까닭은 트인 비탈을 걸어 내려오는 보행로에 비해 그 노선이 엄청나게 긴 데다, 그만큼 말의 수고가 늘어나기 때문이었다."]], [["At this moment a footstep approached; but the light of the lamps being in a different direction the comer was not visible.", "이 순간 발소리가 다가왔다. 하지만 등불은 다른 방향을 비추고 있던 터라 오는 사람은 보이지 않았다."], ["The step paused, then came on again.", "발소리가 멈추었다가 다시 다가왔다."]], [["“Eustacia?”", "\"유스테이샤?\""], ["said Wildeve.", "와일드브가 말했다."]], [["The person came forward, and the light fell upon the form of Clym, glistening with wet, whom Wildeve immediately recognized; but Wildeve, who stood behind the lamp, was not at once recognized by Yeobright.", "그 사람이 앞으로 나오자 빛이 클림의 형상 위로 떨어졌는데, 그는 흠뻑 젖어 번들거리고 있었고, 와일드브는 그를 즉시 알아보았다. 하지만 등불 뒤에 서 있던 와일드브를 요브라이트는 곧바로 알아보지 못했다."]], [["He stopped as if in doubt whether this waiting vehicle could have anything to do with the flight of his wife or not.", "그는 이 기다리는 마차가 자기 아내의 도주와 무슨 관계가 있는지 없는지 의심하듯 멈춰 섰다."], ["The sight of Yeobright at once banished Wildeve’s sober feelings, who saw him again as the deadly rival from whom Eustacia was to be kept at all hazards.", "요브라이트의 모습은 와일드브의 차분한 감정을 단숨에 몰아냈으니, 그는 클림을 또다시 그 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 유스테이샤에게서 떼어놓아야 할 철천지원수로 보았다."], ["Hence Wildeve did not speak, in the hope that Clym would pass by without particular inquiry.", "그리하여 와일드브는, 클림이 별다른 캐물음 없이 지나쳐 가기를 바라며 입을 열지 않았다."]], [["While they both hung thus in hesitation a dull sound became audible above the storm and wind.", "둘이 그렇게 머뭇거리며 멈춰 있는 동안 폭풍과 바람 위로 둔탁한 소리가 들려왔다."], ["Its origin was unmistakable—it was the fall of a body into the stream in the adjoining mead, apparently at a point near the weir.", "그 소리의 정체는 틀림없었으니—이웃한 풀밭의 개울로 무언가가 떨어진 소리였고, 둑 가까운 지점인 듯했다."]], [["Both started.", "둘 다 흠칫했다."], ["“Good God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["can it be she?”", "그 애일 수가 있나?\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."]], [["“Why should it be she?”", "\"왜 그게 그 애겠소?\""], ["said Wildeve, in his alarm forgetting that he had hitherto screened himself.", "와일드브가 당황한 나머지, 자신이 여태 몸을 숨기고 있었다는 것을 잊고 말했다."]], [["“Ah!—that’s you, you traitor, is it?”", "\"아!—당신이군, 이 배신자, 그렇지?\""], ["cried Yeobright.", "요브라이트가 외쳤다."], ["“Why should it be she?", "\"왜 그게 그 애냐고?"], ["Because last week she would have put an end to her life if she had been able.", "지난주에 그 사람은 할 수만 있었다면 제 목숨을 끊으려 했으니까."], ["She ought to have been watched!", "누군가 지켜봤어야 했어!"], ["Take one of the lamps and come with me.”", "등불 하나 들고 나를 따라와.\""]], [["Yeobright seized the one on his side and hastened on; Wildeve did not wait to unfasten the other, but followed at once along the meadow track to the weir, a little in the rear of Clym.", "요브라이트는 자기 쪽에 있던 등불을 움켜쥐고 서둘러 나아갔다. 와일드브는 다른 등불을 풀러 떼낼 새도 없이 곧장 클림보다 조금 뒤처져 풀밭 길을 따라 둑으로 뒤따랐다."]], [["Shadwater Weir had at its foot a large circular pool, fifty feet in diameter, into which the water flowed through ten huge hatches, raised and lowered by a winch and cogs in the ordinary manner.", "섀드워터 둑은 그 아래에 지름 오십 피트의 커다란 원형 못을 두고 있었는데, 그 못으로 물이 열 개의 거대한 수문을 통해 흘러들었고, 수문은 여느 방식대로 권양기와 톱니바퀴로 올리고 내렸다."], ["The sides of the pool were of masonry, to prevent the water from washing away the bank; but the force of the stream in winter was sometimes such as to undermine the retaining wall and precipitate it into the hole.", "못의 가장자리는 물이 둑을 씻어내지 못하도록 석조로 되어 있었으나, 겨울철 물살의 힘이 때로 워낙 거세어 그 옹벽 밑을 파고들어 구멍 속으로 무너뜨리기도 했다."], ["Clym reached the hatches, the framework of which was shaken to its foundations by the velocity of the current.", "클림은 수문에 이르렀는데, 그 수문틀은 물살의 빠르기에 기초까지 흔들리고 있었다."], ["Nothing but the froth of the waves could be discerned in the pool below.", "아래 못에서는 물결의 거품 말고는 아무것도 분간할 수 없었다."], ["He got upon the plank bridge over the race, and holding to the rail, that the wind might not blow him off, crossed to the other side of the river.", "그는 방수로 위에 놓인 널다리에 올라서서, 바람에 떠밀려 떨어지지 않도록 난간을 붙잡고 강 건너편으로 건너갔다."], ["There he leant over the wall and lowered the lamp, only to behold the vortex formed at the curl of the returning current.", "거기서 그는 벽 너머로 몸을 기울여 등불을 내렸으나, 되돌아 흐르는 물살이 휘말리는 곳에 생긴 소용돌이만 보일 뿐이었다."]], [["Wildeve meanwhile had arrived on the former side, and the light from Yeobright’s lamp shed a flecked and agitated radiance across the weir pool, revealing to the ex-engineer the tumbling courses of the currents from the hatches above.", "그사이 와일드브는 먼젓번 쪽에 다다랐고, 요브라이트의 등불에서 나온 빛이 얼룩지고 일렁이는 광채를 둑 못 위로 던지며, 위쪽 수문에서 쏟아져 내려 뒤엉키는 물줄기를 그 옛 기사技士에게 드러내 보였다."], ["Across this gashed and puckered mirror a dark body was slowly borne by one of the backward currents.", "이 베이고 주름진 거울 위로 검은 물체 하나가 뒤로 흐르는 물살 가운데 하나에 실려 천천히 떠밀려 왔다."]], [["“O, my darling!”", "\"오, 내 사랑!\""], ["exclaimed Wildeve in an agonized voice; and, without showing sufficient presence of mind even to throw off his greatcoat, he leaped into the boiling caldron.", "와일드브가 고통에 찬 목소리로 외쳤다. 그러고는 외투를 벗어 던질 분별력조차 보이지 못한 채, 부글거리는 가마솥 속으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["Yeobright could now also discern the floating body, though but indistinctly; and imagining from Wildeve’s plunge that there was life to be saved he was about to leap after.", "이제 요브라이트도 떠 있는 물체를 분간할 수 있었으나, 흐릿하게나마였다. 와일드브가 뛰어든 것을 보고 구할 목숨이 있는 줄로 여겨 그도 막 뒤따라 뛰어들려 했다."], ["Bethinking himself of a wiser plan, he placed the lamp against a post to make it stand upright, and running round to the lower part of the pool, where there was no wall, he sprang in and boldly waded upwards towards the deeper portion.", "더 현명한 방도를 떠올린 그는 등불이 똑바로 서 있도록 기둥에 기대 세워놓고, 벽이 없는 못의 아래쪽으로 돌아 뛰어가, 그 안으로 뛰어들어 대담하게 더 깊은 쪽을 향해 거슬러 헤쳐 나아갔다."], ["Here he was taken off his legs, and in swimming was carried round into the centre of the basin, where he perceived Wildeve struggling.", "여기서 그는 발이 땅에서 떨어졌고, 헤엄을 치다 보니 못의 한복판으로 휩쓸려 들어갔는데, 거기서 그는 와일드브가 허우적거리는 것을 알아보았다."]], [["While these hasty actions were in progress here, Venn and Thomasin had been toiling through the lower corner of the heath in the direction of the light.", "이곳에서 이 다급한 일들이 벌어지는 동안, 벤과 토마신은 그 불빛 방향으로 황야의 아래쪽 모퉁이를 헤치며 애써 나아가고 있었다."], ["They had not been near enough to the river to hear the plunge, but they saw the removal of the carriage lamp, and watched its motion into the mead.", "그들은 첨벙 소리를 들을 만큼 강에 가까이 있지는 못했으나, 마차 등불이 옮겨지는 것을 보았고, 그것이 풀밭으로 움직여 가는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["As soon as they reached the car and horse Venn guessed that something new was amiss, and hastened to follow in the course of the moving light.", "수레와 말에 다다르자마자 벤은 무언가 새로운 일이 어긋났음을 짐작하고, 움직이는 불빛의 행로를 서둘러 뒤따랐다."], ["Venn walked faster than Thomasin, and came to the weir alone.", "벤은 토마신보다 빨리 걸었던 터라, 혼자서 둑에 다다랐다."]], [["The lamp placed against the post by Clym still shone across the water, and the reddleman observed something floating motionless.", "클림이 기둥에 기대 세워둔 등불은 여전히 물 위로 빛을 비추고 있었고, 레들먼은 무언가가 미동도 없이 떠 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Being encumbered with the infant, he ran back to meet Thomasin.", "아기를 안고 있던 터라 그는 토마신을 맞으러 도로 뛰어갔다."]], [["“Take the baby, please, Mrs. Wildeve,”", "\"아기를 받으세요, 와일드브 부인.\""], ["he said hastily.", "그가 다급히 말했다."], ["“Run home with her, call the stable lad, and make him send down to me any men who may be living near.", "\"아기를 데리고 집으로 뛰어가, 마구간 소년을 불러서, 근처에 사는 남자들을 누구든 내게로 내려보내게 하시오."], ["Somebody has fallen into the weir.”", "누군가 둑에 빠졌소.\""]], [["Thomasin took the child and ran.", "토마신은 아이를 받아 안고 뛰었다."], ["When she came to the covered car the horse, though fresh from the stable, was standing perfectly still, as if conscious of misfortune.", "그녀가 덮개 씌운 수레에 이르렀을 때, 말은 마구간에서 갓 나온 참인데도, 마치 불행을 의식하기라도 한 듯 완벽히 가만히 서 있었다."], ["She saw for the first time whose it was.", "그녀는 그것이 누구의 것인지 그제야 처음으로 알아보았다."], ["She nearly fainted, and would have been unable to proceed another step but that the necessity of preserving the little girl from harm nerved her to an amazing self-control.", "그녀는 거의 졸도할 뻔했고, 어린 딸을 화에서 지켜야 한다는 절박함이 그녀에게 놀라운 자제력을 불어넣지 않았더라면 한 발짝도 더 나아갈 수 없었을 것이다."], ["In this agony of suspense she entered the house, put the baby in a place of safety, woke the lad and the female domestic, and ran out to give the alarm at the nearest cottage.", "이 마음 졸이는 고통 속에서 그녀는 집으로 들어가 아기를 안전한 곳에 누이고, 소년과 하녀를 깨운 다음, 가장 가까운 오두막으로 달려가 알렸다."]], [["Diggory, having returned to the brink of the pool, observed that the small upper hatches or floats were withdrawn.", "못 가장자리로 돌아온 디고리는, 위쪽의 작은 수문, 즉 부표들이 빠져 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He found one of these lying upon the grass, and taking it under one arm, and with his lantern in his hand, entered at the bottom of the pool as Clym had done.", "그는 그중 하나가 풀밭에 놓여 있는 것을 발견하고, 그것을 한쪽 팔에 끼고 다른 손에 등불을 든 채, 클림이 그랬던 것처럼 못의 아래쪽으로 들어갔다."], ["As soon as he began to be in deep water he flung himself across the hatch; thus supported he was able to keep afloat as long as he chose, holding the lantern aloft with his disengaged hand.", "깊은 물에 잠기기 시작하자마자 그는 그 수문 위로 몸을 던졌다. 그리하여 받쳐진 그는 빈 손으로 등불을 높이 치켜든 채 원하는 만큼 떠 있을 수 있었다."], ["Propelled by his feet, he steered round and round the pool, ascending each time by one of the back streams and descending in the middle of the current.", "발로 밀어내며 그는 못을 빙빙 돌았는데, 매번 뒤로 흐르는 물줄기 가운데 하나를 타고 올라갔다가 물살 한복판으로 내려왔다."]], [["At first he could see nothing.", "처음에 그는 아무것도 볼 수 없었다."], ["Then amidst the glistening of the whirlpools and the white clots of foam he distinguished a woman’s bonnet floating alone.", "그러다 소용돌이의 번득임과 하얀 거품 덩어리들 가운데서 그는 여인의 보닛이 홀로 떠 있는 것을 분간했다."], ["His search was now under the left wall, when something came to the surface almost close beside him.", "이제 그의 수색은 왼쪽 벽 아래에서 이루어지고 있었는데, 무언가가 거의 그의 바로 곁에서 수면으로 떠올랐다."], ["It was not, as he had expected, a woman, but a man.", "그것은 그가 예상했던 것과 달리 여인이 아니라 남자였다."], ["The reddleman put the ring of the lantern between his teeth, seized the floating man by the collar, and, holding on to the hatch with his remaining arm, struck out into the strongest race, by which the unconscious man, the hatch, and himself were carried down the stream.", "레들먼은 등불의 고리를 이로 문 채, 떠 있는 남자의 멱살을 움켜쥐고, 남은 한 팔로 수문을 붙들면서, 가장 거센 물줄기 속으로 헤쳐 나아갔는데, 그 물줄기에 의식 잃은 남자와 수문과 그 자신이 함께 하류로 떠밀려 갔다."], ["As soon as Venn found his feet dragging over the pebbles of the shallower part below he secured his footing and waded towards the brink.", "발이 아래쪽 더 얕은 곳의 자갈에 끌리는 것을 벤이 느끼자마자 그는 발 디딜 자리를 단단히 잡고 가장자리를 향해 헤쳐 나아갔다."], ["There, where the water stood at about the height of his waist, he flung away the hatch, and attempted to drag forth the man.", "거기, 물이 그의 허리 높이쯤 차오르는 곳에서, 그는 수문을 던져버리고 남자를 끌어내려 했다."], ["This was a matter of great difficulty, and he found as the reason that the legs of the unfortunate stranger were tightly embraced by the arms of another man, who had hitherto been entirely beneath the surface.", "이것은 무척 어려운 일이었는데, 그 까닭은 그 불운한 낯선 남자의 다리를, 여태 완전히 수면 아래 잠겨 있던 또 다른 남자의 팔이 단단히 끌어안고 있기 때문임을 그는 알게 되었다."]], [["At this moment his heart bounded to hear footsteps running towards him, and two men, roused by Thomasin, appeared at the brink above.", "이 순간 그를 향해 달려오는 발소리에 그의 심장이 뛰었으니, 토마신에게 깨워진 두 남자가 위쪽 가장자리에 나타났다."], ["They ran to where Venn was, and helped him in lifting out the apparently drowned persons, separating them, and laying them out upon the grass.", "그들은 벤이 있는 곳으로 달려와, 익사한 듯한 사람들을 들어 올려 떼어놓고 풀밭에 눕히는 것을 도왔다."], ["Venn turned the light upon their faces.", "벤은 그들의 얼굴에 불빛을 비추었다."], ["The one who had been uppermost was Yeobright; he who had been completely submerged was Wildeve.", "위에 있던 사람은 요브라이트였고, 완전히 물에 잠겨 있던 사람은 와일드브였다."]], [["“Now we must search the hole again,”", "\"이제 구멍을 다시 뒤져야 하오.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."], ["“A woman is in there somewhere.", "\"그 안 어딘가에 여자가 있소."], ["Get a pole.”", "장대를 가져오시오.\""]], [["One of the men went to the footbridge and tore off the handrail.", "남자들 중 하나가 널다리로 가서 난간을 뜯어냈다."], ["The reddleman and the two others then entered the water together from below as before, and with their united force probed the pool forwards to where it sloped down to its central depth.", "그러자 레들먼과 다른 두 사람은 다 함께 아래쪽에서 전처럼 물속으로 들어가, 합친 힘으로 못이 그 한복판의 깊은 곳으로 비스듬히 떨어지는 데까지 앞으로 더듬어 나아갔다."], ["Venn was not mistaken in supposing that any person who had sunk for the last time would be washed down to this point, for when they had examined to about halfway across something impeded their thrust.", "마지막으로 가라앉은 사람이라면 누구든 이 지점으로 떠밀려 내려가리라는 벤의 짐작은 틀리지 않았으니, 그들이 절반쯤 가로질러 살폈을 때 무언가가 그들의 찔러대는 장대를 가로막았다."]], [["“Pull it forward,”", "\"앞으로 끌어당기시오.\""], ["said Venn, and they raked it in with the pole till it was close to their feet.", "벤이 말했고, 그들은 그것이 발치에 가까이 올 때까지 장대로 긁어당겼다."]], [["Venn vanished under the stream, and came up with an armful of wet drapery enclosing a woman’s cold form, which was all that remained of the desperate Eustacia.", "벤이 물속으로 사라졌다가, 여인의 차가운 형체를 감싼 젖은 옷자락을 한 아름 안고 떠올랐는데, 그것이 절망에 빠졌던 유스테이샤에게 남은 전부였다."]], [["When they reached the bank there stood Thomasin, in a stress of grief, bending over the two unconscious ones who already lay there.", "그들이 둑에 다다랐을 때 거기에는 토마신이, 슬픔에 짓눌린 채, 이미 그곳에 누인 의식 없는 두 사람 위로 몸을 숙이고 서 있었다."], ["The horse and cart were brought to the nearest point in the road, and it was the work of a few minutes only to place the three in the vehicle.", "말과 수레가 길의 가장 가까운 지점으로 끌려왔고, 셋을 마차에 싣는 데는 몇 분밖에 걸리지 않았다."], ["Venn led on the horse, supporting Thomasin upon his arm, and the two men followed, till they reached the inn.", "벤은 토마신을 자기 팔로 부축하며 말을 끌고 앞장섰고, 두 남자가 뒤따라 여관에 다다랐다."]], [["The woman who had been shaken out of her sleep by Thomasin had hastily dressed herself and lighted a fire, the other servant being left to snore on in peace at the back of the house.", "토마신에게 잠을 깨운 여인은 황급히 옷을 챙겨 입고 불을 지폈는데, 다른 하인은 집 뒤편에서 편히 코를 골도록 그대로 두었다."], ["The insensible forms of Eustacia, Clym, and Wildeve were then brought in and laid on the carpet, with their feet to the fire, when such restorative processes as could be thought of were adopted at once, the stableman being in the meantime sent for a doctor.", "그런 다음 유스테이샤와 클림과 와일드브의 정신 잃은 몸이 안으로 옮겨져, 발을 불 쪽으로 두고 양탄자 위에 눕혀졌고, 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 소생술이 즉시 시도되었으며, 그동안 마구간지기는 의사를 부르러 보내졌다."], ["But there seemed to be not a whiff of life in either of the bodies.", "하지만 그 어느 몸에도 한 가닥 생기조차 없어 보였다."], ["Then Thomasin, whose stupor of grief had been thrust off awhile by frantic action, applied a bottle of hartshorn to Clym’s nostrils, having tried it in vain upon the other two.", "그때 토마신이, 슬픔에 멍한 상태가 미친 듯한 행동에 잠시 밀려나 있던 터라, 사슴뿔 정精 한 병을 클림의 콧구멍에 갖다 댔는데, 다른 둘에게는 헛되이 써본 뒤였다."], ["He sighed.", "그가 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“Clym’s alive!”", "\"클림이 살아 있어요!\""], ["she exclaimed.", "그녀가 외쳤다."]], [["He soon breathed distinctly, and again and again did she attempt to revive her husband by the same means; but Wildeve gave no sign.", "그는 곧 또렷이 숨을 쉬었고, 그녀는 같은 방법으로 남편을 되살려보려 거듭거듭 애썼으나, 와일드브는 아무런 기척이 없었다."], ["There was too much reason to think that he and Eustacia both were for ever beyond the reach of stimulating perfumes.", "그와 유스테이샤 둘 다 자극적인 향기가 미칠 수 없는 곳으로 영영 떠났다고 여길 근거가 너무도 많았다."], ["Their exertions did not relax till the doctor arrived, when one by one, the senseless three were taken upstairs and put into warm beds.", "그들의 노력은 의사가 도착하고서야 누그러졌고, 그제야 정신 잃은 셋이 하나씩 위층으로 옮겨져 따뜻한 침대에 뉘어졌다."]], [["Venn soon felt himself relieved from further attendance, and went to the door, scarcely able yet to realize the strange catastrophe that had befallen the family in which he took so great an interest.", "벤은 곧 더 거들 일이 없게 되었음을 느끼고 문가로 갔는데, 자기가 그토록 깊은 관심을 두던 가족에게 닥친 이 기이한 참변을 아직 좀처럼 실감하지 못했다. 토마신은 분명 이 갑작스럽고 압도적인 사건의 본질에 무너지고 말 것이었다."], ["Thomasin surely would be broken down by the sudden and overwhelming nature of this event. No firm and sensible Mrs.", "이제 그 온화한 처녀를 이 시련 속에서 지탱해줄 굳세고 분별 있는 요브라이트 부인은 살아 있지 않았다."], ["Yeobright lived now to support the gentle girl through the ordeal; and, whatever an unimpassioned spectator might think of her loss of such a husband as Wildeve, there could be no doubt that for the moment she was distracted and horrified by the blow.", "그리고 와일드브 같은 남편을 잃은 것을 두고 무심한 구경꾼이야 어떻게 생각하든, 그녀가 지금 이 순간 그 충격에 넋이 나가고 소름이 끼쳐 있다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["As for himself, not being privileged to go to her and comfort her, he saw no reason for waiting longer in a house where he remained only as a stranger.", "그 자신으로 말하자면, 그녀에게로 가서 위로할 처지가 못 되는 터라, 그저 낯선 사람으로 머물 뿐인 집에서 더 기다릴 까닭이 없다고 여겼다."]], [["He returned across the heath to his van.", "그는 황야를 가로질러 자기 마차로 돌아왔다."], ["The fire was not yet out, and everything remained as he had left it.", "불은 아직 꺼지지 않았고, 모든 것이 그가 두고 간 그대로였다."], ["Venn now bethought himself of his clothes, which were saturated with water to the weight of lead.", "이제 벤은 납만큼이나 무겁게 물에 절은 자기 옷이 떠올랐다."], ["He changed them, spread them before the fire, and lay down to sleep.", "그는 옷을 갈아입고 그것을 불 앞에 펼쳐 놓은 다음, 잠을 자려 누웠다."], ["But it was more than he could do to rest here while excited by a vivid imagination of the turmoil they were in at the house he had quitted, and, blaming himself for coming away, he dressed in another suit, locked up the door, and again hastened across to the inn.", "하지만 떠나온 그 집의 소란을 생생히 떠올리며 마음이 들떠 있는 동안 여기서 쉬기란 그가 할 수 있는 일이 못 되었으니, 떠나온 것을 자책하며 그는 다른 옷 한 벌로 갈아입고 문을 잠근 뒤, 다시 황야를 가로질러 여관으로 서둘러 갔다."], ["Rain was still falling heavily when he entered the kitchen.", "그가 부엌에 들어섰을 때 비는 여전히 세차게 내리고 있었다."], ["A bright fire was shining from the hearth, and two women were bustling about, one of whom was Olly Dowden.", "화덕에서는 환한 불이 빛나고 있었고, 두 여인이 부산히 오가고 있었는데, 그중 하나는 올리 다우든이었다."]], [["“Well, how is it going on now?”", "\"그래, 지금은 어찌 돼가오?\""], ["said Venn in a whisper.", "벤이 속삭이듯 물었다."]], [["“Mr. Yeobright is better; but Mrs.", "\"요브라이트 씨는 좋아지셨어요."], ["Yeobright and Mr. Wildeve are dead and cold.", "하지만 요브라이트 부인과 와일드브 씨는 차갑게 죽었답니다."], ["The doctor says they were quite gone before they were out of the water.”", "의사 말로는 물에서 나오기도 전에 완전히 숨이 끊겼다네요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I thought as much when I hauled ’em up.", "끌어 올릴 때 나도 그런 줄 짐작했소."], ["And Mrs. Wildeve?”", "그럼 와일드브 부인은?\""]], [["“She is as well as can be expected.", "\"기대할 만큼은 괜찮으세요."], ["The doctor had her put between blankets, for she was almost as wet as they that had been in the river, poor young thing.", "의사가 담요 사이에 누이게 했는데, 그분도 강에 빠졌던 이들 못지않게 흠뻑 젖어 있었거든요, 가엾은 젊은 분."], ["You don’t seem very dry, reddleman.”", "레들먼, 당신도 그리 말라 보이지 않네요.\""]], [["“Oh, ’tis not much.", "\"오, 별것 아니오."], ["I have changed my things.", "나는 옷을 갈아입었소."], ["This is only a little dampness I’ve got coming through the rain again.”", "이건 다시 빗속을 뚫고 오느라 생긴 약간의 축축함일 뿐이오.\""]], [["“Stand by the fire.", "\"불가에 서세요."], ["Mis’ess says you be to have whatever you want, and she was sorry when she was told that you’d gone away.”", "마님께서 당신이 원하는 건 무엇이든 드리라 하셨고, 당신이 가버렸다는 말을 듣고는 안타까워하셨답니다.\""]], [["Venn drew near to the fireplace, and looked into the flames in an absent mood.", "벤은 화덕 가까이 다가가, 멍한 기분으로 불꽃 속을 들여다보았다."], ["The steam came from his leggings and ascended the chimney with the smoke, while he thought of those who were upstairs.", "그의 각반에서 김이 피어올라 연기와 함께 굴뚝으로 솟아오르는 동안, 그는 위층에 있는 이들을 생각했다."], ["Two were corpses, one had barely escaped the jaws of death, another was sick and a widow.", "둘은 주검이었고, 하나는 가까스로 죽음의 아가리를 벗어났으며, 또 하나는 병들고 과부가 된 여인이었다."], ["The last occasion on which he had lingered by that fireplace was when the raffle was in progress; when Wildeve was alive and well; Thomasin active and smiling in the next room; Yeobright and Eustacia just made husband and wife, and Mrs. Yeobright living at Blooms-End.", "그가 그 화덕가에 마지막으로 머물렀던 때는 제비뽑기가 한창이던 무렵으로, 그때 와일드브는 살아서 멀쩡했고, 토마신은 옆방에서 활기차게 미소 짓고 있었으며, 요브라이트와 유스테이샤는 갓 부부가 된 참이었고, 요브라이트 부인은 블룸스엔드에 살아 있었다."], ["It had seemed at that time that the then position of affairs was good for at least twenty years to come.", "그때만 해도 당시의 형편은 적어도 앞으로 이십 년은 거뜬할 것처럼 보였다."], ["Yet, of all the circle, he himself was the only one whose situation had not materially changed.", "그러나 그 무리 가운데, 처지가 본질적으로 달라지지 않은 이는 그 자신뿐이었다."]], [["While he ruminated a footstep descended the stairs.", "그가 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨 있는데 발소리가 계단을 내려왔다."], ["It was the nurse, who brought in her hand a rolled mass of wet paper.", "그것은 간병하는 여인이었는데, 손에 둘둘 만 젖은 종이 뭉치를 들고 있었다."], ["The woman was so engrossed with her occupation that she hardly saw Venn.", "그 여인은 자기 일에 어찌나 몰두해 있던지 벤을 거의 보지도 못했다."], ["She took from a cupboard some pieces of twine, which she strained across the fireplace, tying the end of each piece to the firedog, previously pulled forward for the purpose, and, unrolling the wet papers, she began pinning them one by one to the strings in a manner of clothes on a line.", "그녀는 찬장에서 노끈 몇 가닥을 꺼내 화덕 위로 팽팽히 걸쳤는데, 그 일을 위해 미리 앞으로 끌어낸 장작 받침쇠에 각 가닥의 끝을 묶고는, 젖은 종이를 펼쳐 빨랫줄에 빨래를 널듯 한 장씩 그 줄에 핀으로 꽂기 시작했다."]], [["“What be they?”", "\"그게 뭐요?\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."]], [["“Poor master’s banknotes,”", "\"가엾은 주인님 지폐예요.\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 답했다."], ["“They were found in his pocket when they undressed him.”", "\"옷을 벗길 때 주머니에서 나왔답니다.\""]], [["“Then he was not coming back again for some time?”", "\"그럼 그분은 한동안 다시 돌아오지 않을 작정이었나 보군?\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."]], [["“That we shall never know,”", "\"그건 우리가 영영 알 수 없을 거예요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["Venn was loth to depart, for all on earth that interested him lay under this roof.", "벤은 차마 떠나기가 싫었으니, 세상에서 그의 관심을 끄는 모든 것이 이 지붕 아래 있었기 때문이다."], ["As nobody in the house had any more sleep that night, except the two who slept for ever, there was no reason why he should not remain.", "그 집에서는 그날 밤 더 이상 아무도 잠을 자지 못했으니, 영영 잠든 두 사람만 빼고 말이며, 그러니 그가 머물지 못할 까닭이 없었다."], ["So he retired into the niche of the fireplace where he had used to sit, and there he continued, watching the steam from the double row of banknotes as they waved backwards and forwards in the draught of the chimney till their flaccidity was changed to dry crispness throughout.", "그리하여 그는 늘 앉곤 하던 화덕가 우묵한 자리로 물러나, 거기서 두 줄로 늘어선 지폐가 굴뚝의 외풍에 앞뒤로 나부끼다가 그 흐물흐물함이 속속들이 바싹한 빳빳함으로 바뀔 때까지 그것들에서 피어오르는 김을 지켜보며 계속 있었다."], ["Then the woman came and unpinned them, and, folding them together, carried the handful upstairs.", "그러자 여인이 와서 핀을 빼고는, 그것들을 한데 접어 한 움큼 위층으로 가져갔다."], ["Presently the doctor appeared from above with the look of a man who could do no more, and, pulling on his gloves, went out of the house, the trotting of his horse soon dying away upon the road.", "이윽고 의사가 위층에서 더는 손쓸 도리가 없는 사람의 표정으로 나타나, 장갑을 끼며 집을 나섰고, 그의 말이 종종걸음 치는 소리는 이내 길 위에서 잦아들었다."]], [["At four o’clock there was a gentle knock at the door.", "네 시에 문에서 가벼운 노크 소리가 났다."], ["It was from Charley, who had been sent by Captain Vye to inquire if anything had been heard of Eustacia.", "그것은 찰리였는데, 유스테이샤에 관해 무슨 소식이라도 들었는지 알아보라고 바이 선장이 보낸 것이었다."], ["The girl who admitted him looked in his face as if she did not know what answer to return, and showed him in to where Venn was seated, saying to the reddleman, “Will you tell him, please?”", "그를 들인 처녀는 무슨 대답을 해야 할지 모르겠다는 듯 그의 얼굴을 바라보다가, 벤이 앉아 있는 데로 안내하며 레들먼에게 말했다. \"당신이 말해줄래요?\""]], [["Venn told.", "벤이 말했다."], ["Charley’s only utterance was a feeble, indistinct sound.", "찰리의 입에서 나온 것이라고는 힘없고 또렷잖은 소리뿐이었다."], ["He stood quite still; then he burst out spasmodically, “I shall see her once more?”", "그는 아주 가만히 서 있다가, 발작하듯 터뜨렸다. \"한 번만 더 그분을 볼 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“I dare say you may see her,”", "\"아마 볼 수 있을 거다.\""], ["said Diggory gravely.", "디고리가 무겁게 말했다."], ["“But hadn’t you better run and tell Captain Vye?”", "\"하지만 어서 달려가 바이 선장께 알리는 게 낫지 않겠니?\""]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"네, 네."], ["Only I do hope I shall see her just once again.”", "다만 한 번만, 단 한 번만 더 그분을 뵙고 싶어요.\""]], [["“You shall,”", "\"그러게 될 거다.\""], ["said a low voice behind; and starting round they beheld by the dim light, a thin, pallid, almost spectral form, wrapped in a blanket, and looking like Lazarus coming from the tomb.", "등 뒤에서 낮은 목소리가 말했고, 흠칫 돌아보니 그들은 희미한 빛 속에서, 담요에 둘둘 감싸인 채 무덤에서 나온 나사로 같은, 야위고 창백한, 거의 유령 같은 형상을 보았다."]], [["It was Yeobright.", "그것은 요브라이트였다."], ["Neither Venn nor Charley spoke, and Clym continued, “You shall see her.", "벤도 찰리도 입을 열지 않았고, 클림이 말을 이었다."], ["There will be time enough to tell the captain when it gets daylight.", "\"그 사람을 보게 될 거다. 날이 밝으면 선장께 알릴 시간은 충분해."], ["You would like to see her too—would you not, Diggory?", "자네도 그 사람을 보고 싶겠지—그렇지 않은가, 디고리?"], ["She looks very beautiful now.”", "지금 그 사람은 무척 아름다워 보인다네.\""]], [["Venn assented by rising to his feet, and with Charley he followed Clym to the foot of the staircase, where he took off his boots; Charley did the same.", "벤은 자리에서 일어나는 것으로 응했고, 찰리와 함께 클림을 따라 계단 아래로 갔는데, 거기서 그는 장화를 벗었고 찰리도 그렇게 했다."], ["They followed Yeobright upstairs to the landing, where there was a candle burning, which Yeobright took in his hand, and with it led the way into an adjoining room.", "그들은 요브라이트를 따라 위층 층계참으로 올라갔으며, 거기에는 촛불 하나가 타고 있어 요브라이트가 그것을 손에 들고 앞장서 옆방으로 이끌었다."], ["Here he went to the bedside and folded back the sheet.", "거기서 그는 침대맡으로 가서 시트를 젖혔다."]], [["They stood silently looking upon Eustacia, who, as she lay there still in death, eclipsed all her living phases.", "그들은 유스테이샤를 말없이 내려다보며 서 있었는데, 죽음 속에 그렇게 누운 그녀는 살아생전의 그 모든 모습을 무색하게 했다."], ["Pallor did not include all the quality of her complexion, which seemed more than whiteness; it was almost light.", "창백함이라는 말로는 그녀 안색의 온갖 결을 다 담을 수 없었으니, 그것은 하양 이상으로 보였고, 거의 빛에 가까웠다."], ["The expression of her finely carved mouth was pleasant, as if a sense of dignity had just compelled her to leave off speaking.", "정교하게 새겨진 그녀의 입가에 어린 표정은 흐뭇해 보였는데, 마치 위엄의 감각이 방금 그녀에게 말을 멈추게 한 듯했다."], ["Eternal rigidity had seized upon it in a momentary transition between fervour and resignation.", "영원한 굳음이 열정과 체념 사이의 순간적인 전환의 와중에 그것을 사로잡았던 것이다."], ["Her black hair was looser now than either of them had ever seen it before, and surrounded her brow like a forest.", "그녀의 검은 머리카락은 이제 그 둘 중 누구도 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 풀어헤쳐져, 숲처럼 그녀의 이마를 에워싸고 있었다."], ["The stateliness of look which had been almost too marked for a dweller in a country domicile had at last found an artistically happy background.", "시골 거처에 사는 이에게는 거의 지나치다 싶었던 그 위풍당당한 모습이 마침내 예술적으로 흡족한 배경을 찾은 것이었다."]], [["Nobody spoke, till at length Clym covered her and turned aside.", "아무도 입을 열지 않다가, 마침내 클림이 그녀를 덮고 돌아섰다."], ["“Now come here,”", "\"이제 이리 와.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["They went to a recess in the same room, and there, on a smaller bed, lay another figure—Wildeve.", "그들은 같은 방의 한쪽 후미진 곳으로 갔는데, 거기 더 작은 침대 위에 또 다른 형상이 누워 있었으니—와일드브였다."], ["Less repose was visible in his face than in Eustacia’s, but the same luminous youthfulness overspread it, and the least sympathetic observer would have felt at sight of him now that he was born for a higher destiny than this.", "그의 얼굴에는 유스테이샤보다 평온함이 덜 보였으나, 똑같은 환한 젊음이 그 위에 어려 있어, 아무리 무덤덤한 관찰자라도 지금 그를 보면 그가 이런 것보다 더 높은 운명을 타고났음을 느꼈을 것이다."], ["The only sign upon him of his recent struggle for life was in his fingertips, which were worn and sacrificed in his dying endeavours to obtain a hold on the face of the weir-wall.", "그가 최근에 목숨을 부지하려 사투를 벌인 흔적이라고는 그의 손끝에 남은 것뿐이었으니, 그 손끝은 둑 벽면을 붙잡으려 죽어가며 안간힘을 쓰는 통에 닳고 망가져 있었다."]], [["Yeobright’s manner had been so quiet, he had uttered so few syllables since his reappearance, that Venn imagined him resigned.", "요브라이트의 태도가 어찌나 차분했던지, 다시 나타난 이래 어찌나 몇 마디 하지 않았던지, 벤은 그가 체념했으려니 여겼다."], ["It was only when they had left the room and stood upon the landing that the true state of his mind was apparent.", "그의 마음의 참된 상태가 드러난 것은 그들이 방을 나와 층계참에 섰을 때뿐이었다."], ["Here he said, with a wild smile, inclining his head towards the chamber in which Eustacia lay, “She is the second woman I have killed this year.", "여기서 그는 유스테이샤가 누운 방 쪽으로 고개를 기울이며 광기 어린 미소를 띤 채 말했다. \"저 사람이 올해 내가 죽인 두 번째 여자야."], ["I was a great cause of my mother’s death, and I am the chief cause of hers.”", "나는 어머니의 죽음에 큰 원인이 되었고, 저 사람의 죽음에는 으뜸가는 원인이지.\""]], [["“How?”", "\"어째서요?\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."]], [["“I spoke cruel words to her, and she left my house.", "\"나는 어머니에게 모진 말을 했고, 어머니는 내 집을 떠났어."], ["I did not invite her back till it was too late.", "너무 늦어서야 나는 어머니더러 돌아오라고 청했지."], ["It is I who ought to have drowned myself.", "물에 빠져 죽었어야 할 사람은 바로 나야."], ["It would have been a charity to the living had the river overwhelmed me and borne her up.", "강이 나를 집어삼키고 저 사람을 떠받쳐주었더라면 산 사람들에게는 자비였을 텐데."], ["But I cannot die.", "그러나 나는 죽을 수가 없어."], ["Those who ought to have lived lie dead; and here am I alive!”", "살았어야 할 이들은 죽어 누웠는데, 여기 나는 살아 있으니!\""]], [["“But you can’t charge yourself with crimes in that way,”", "\"하지만 그런 식으로 스스로에게 죄를 씌울 수는 없소.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."], ["“You may as well say that the parents be the cause of a murder by the child, for without the parents the child would never have been begot.”", "\"그건 마치 부모가 없었다면 자식이 결코 잉태되지 않았으리라는 이유로, 자식이 저지른 살인의 원인이 부모라고 말하는 것이나 마찬가지요.\""]], [["“Yes, Venn, that is very true; but you don’t know all the circumstances.", "\"그래, 벤, 그건 아주 옳은 말이야. 하지만 자네는 모든 정황을 알지 못해."], ["If it had pleased God to put an end to me it would have been a good thing for all.", "하느님께서 나를 끝내주시는 게 마땅하셨다면 모두에게 좋은 일이었을 거야."], ["But I am getting used to the horror of my existence.", "그러나 나는 내 존재의 끔찍함에 차차 익숙해지고 있어."], ["They say that a time comes when men laugh at misery through long acquaintance with it.", "사람이 비참함과 오래 함께 지내다 보면 그것을 비웃게 되는 때가 온다고들 하지."], ["Surely that time will soon come to me!”", "분명 내게도 곧 그런 때가 오겠지!\""]], [["“Your aim has always been good,”", "\"자네의 뜻은 늘 선했네.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."], ["“Why should you say such desperate things?”", "\"어찌하여 그토록 자포자기한 말을 하는가?\""]], [["“No, they are not desperate.", "\"아니, 자포자기한 게 아니야."], ["They are only hopeless; and my great regret is that for what I have done no man or law can punish me!”", "그저 가망 없을 뿐이지."], ["I. The Inevitable Movement Onward", "그리고 내 가장 큰 한은, 내가 저지른 일을 두고 그 어떤 사람도, 그 어떤 법도 나를 벌할 수 없다는 거야!\""]], [["The story of the deaths of Eustacia and Wildeve was told throughout Egdon, and far beyond, for many weeks and months.", "유스테이시아와 와일디브의 죽음에 관한 이야기는 여러 주, 여러 달에 걸쳐 에그던 전역으로, 그 너머 먼 곳까지 퍼져나갔다."], ["All the known incidents of their love were enlarged, distorted, touched up, and modified, till the original reality bore but a slight resemblance to the counterfeit presentation by surrounding tongues.", "두 사람의 사랑에 얽힌 알려진 일화들은 모조리 부풀려지고, 일그러지고, 덧칠되고, 변형되어, 마침내 원래의 실상은 주변 입들이 빚어낸 위조된 형상과 좀처럼 닮은 구석이 없게 되었다."], ["Yet, upon the whole, neither the man nor the woman lost dignity by sudden death. Misfortune had struck them gracefully, cutting off their erratic histories with a catastrophic dash, instead of, as with many, attenuating each life to an uninteresting meagreness, through long years of wrinkles, neglect, and decay.", "그럼에도 전체적으로 보면 그 사내도 그 여인도 급작스러운 죽음으로 위엄을 잃지는 않았으니, 불행이 그들을 우아하게 후려쳐, 많은 이들처럼 오랜 세월의 주름과 무관심과 쇠락을 거치며 한 생애를 시들하고 보잘것없는 것으로 깎아내리는 대신, 그들의 종잡을 수 없던 내력을 파국의 일격으로 단숨에 끊어버렸기 때문이다."]], [["On those most nearly concerned the effect was somewhat different.", "가장 가까이 얽힌 이들에게는 그 영향이 다소 달랐다."], ["Strangers who had heard of many such cases now merely heard of one more; but immediately where a blow falls no previous imaginings amount to appreciable preparation for it. The very suddenness of her bereavement dulled, to some extent, Thomasin’s feelings; yet irrationally enough, a consciousness that the husband she had lost ought to have been a better man did not lessen her mourning at all.", "그런 사건을 숱하게 들어온 외인들에게는 이제 한 건이 더 추가되었을 뿐이나, 타격이 떨어지는 바로 그 자리에서는 어떤 사전의 상상도 그것에 대한 이렇다 할 대비가 되지 못하는 법이니, 사별의 그 급작스러움 자체가 토머신의 감정을 어느 정도 무디게 했는데, 그러면서도 어이없게도, 잃어버린 남편이 더 나은 사내였어야 마땅하다는 의식이 그녀의 애도를 조금도 덜어주지 못했다."], ["On the contrary, this fact seemed at first to set off the dead husband in his young wife’s eyes, and to be the necessary cloud to the rainbow.", "오히려 이 사실은 처음에는 젊은 아내의 눈에 죽은 남편을 한층 돋보이게 하는 듯했고, 무지개에 꼭 필요한 구름처럼 보였다."], ["But the horrors of the unknown had passed.", "그러나 미지의 것이 안기던 공포는 지나갔다."], ["Vague misgivings about her future as a deserted wife were at an end.", "버림받은 아내로서 자신의 앞날에 대한 막연한 불안도 끝이 났다."], ["The worst had once been matter of trembling conjecture; it was now matter of reason only, a limited badness.", "한때는 떨리는 추측의 대상이던 최악의 일이 이제는 한낱 이성의 문제, 한정된 나쁨에 불과했다."], ["Her chief interest, the little Eustacia, still remained.", "그녀의 으뜸가는 관심사인 어린 유스테이시아는 여전히 곁에 남아 있었다."], ["There was humility in her grief, no defiance in her attitude; and when this is the case a shaken spirit is apt to be stilled.", "그녀의 슬픔에는 겸허함이 있었고, 그 태도에는 반항이 없었으니—이럴 때 흔들린 정신은 가라앉기 마련이다."]], [["Could Thomasin’s mournfulness now and Eustacia’s serenity during life have been reduced to common measure, they would have touched the same mark nearly.", "지금의 토머신의 비탄과 살아 있을 적 유스테이시아의 평온을 공통된 척도로 환산할 수 있었다면, 둘은 거의 같은 지점에 가닿았으리라."], ["But Thomasin’s former brightness made shadow of that which in a sombre atmosphere was light itself.", "그러나 토머신의 예전의 밝음은, 음울한 분위기에서라면 그 자체로 빛이었을 것을 그림자로 만들어버렸다."]], [["The spring came and calmed her; the summer came and soothed her; the autumn arrived, and she began to be comforted, for her little girl was strong and happy, growing in size and knowledge every day.", "봄이 와서 그녀를 진정시켰고, 여름이 와서 그녀를 어루만졌으며, 가을이 닥쳐오자 그녀는 위안을 받기 시작했으니, 어린 딸이 튼튼하고 행복하게, 날마다 몸집도 지식도 자라고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Outward events flattered Thomasin not a little.", "바깥의 사정도 토머신을 적잖이 다독였다."], ["Wildeve had died intestate, and she and the child were his only relatives.", "와일디브는 유언 없이 죽었고, 그녀와 아이가 그의 유일한 혈육이었다."], ["When administration had been granted, all the debts paid, and the residue of her husband’s uncle’s property had come into her hands, it was found that the sum waiting to be invested for her own and the child’s benefit was little less than ten thousand pounds.", "유산 관리가 인가되고, 모든 빚이 청산되고, 남편의 숙부가 남긴 재산의 잔여분이 그녀의 손에 들어오자, 그녀 자신과 아이의 이익을 위해 투자될 수 있는 금액이 일만 파운드에 조금 못 미친다는 사실이 드러났다."]], [["Where should she live? The obvious place was Blooms-End.", "그녀는 어디서 살아야 할 것인가?"], ["The old rooms, it is true, were not much higher than the between-decks of a frigate, necessitating a sinking in the floor under the new clock-case she brought from the inn, and the removal of the handsome brass knobs on its head, before there was height for it to stand; but, such as the rooms were, there were plenty of them, and the place was endeared to her by every early recollection.", "두말할 것 없이 블룸스엔드였다. 옛 방들이 프리깃함의 갑판 사이만큼이나 천장이 낮은 것은 사실이라, 여관에서 가져온 새 괘종시계를 세우려면 그 아래 바닥을 파내고 시계 머리에 달린 멋진 놋쇠 손잡이들을 떼어내야 비로소 세울 만한 높이가 나왔지만, 방들이야 어떻든 수가 넉넉했고, 그곳은 어린 시절의 온갖 추억으로 그녀에게 정겨운 곳이었다."], ["Clym very gladly admitted her as a tenant, confining his own existence to two rooms at the top of the back staircase, where he lived on quietly, shut off from Thomasin and the three servants she had thought fit to indulge in now that she was a mistress of money, going his own ways, and thinking his own thoughts.", "클림은 기꺼이 그녀를 세입자로 받아들였고, 자신의 거처는 뒤쪽 계단 꼭대기의 두 방으로 한정하여, 이제 돈을 쥔 안주인이 되어 거느려도 좋겠다 여긴 토머신과 세 하인으로부터 떨어져, 자기만의 길을 걷고 자기만의 생각을 하며 조용히 지냈다."]], [["His sorrows had made some change in his outward appearance; and yet the alteration was chiefly within.", "슬픔은 그의 겉모습에도 얼마간 변화를 가져왔으나, 그 달라짐은 주로 속에 있었다."], ["It might have been said that he had a wrinkled mind.", "그의 정신에 주름이 졌다고 말할 수도 있었으리라."], ["He had no enemies, and he could get nobody to reproach him, which was why he so bitterly reproached himself.", "그에게는 적이 없었고, 그를 책망해줄 자를 아무도 찾을 수 없었으니, 그래서 그토록 모질게 자신을 책망했다."]], [["He did sometimes think he had been ill-used by fortune, so far as to say that to be born is a palpable dilemma, and that instead of men aiming to advance in life with glory they should calculate how to retreat out of it without shame.", "그는 자신이 운명에게 부당하게 다루어졌다고 이따금 생각하기는 했으니, 태어난다는 것이 명백한 진퇴양난이며, 인간은 영광과 함께 인생에서 전진하려 할 것이 아니라 수치 없이 그곳에서 후퇴할 방도를 궁리해야 한다고까지 말할 정도였다."], ["But that he and his had been sarcastically and pitilessly handled in having such irons thrust into their souls he did not maintain long. It is usually so, except with the sternest of men.", "그러나 자신과 자신의 사람들이 영혼에 그런 쇠꼬챙이를 박히는 일을 당함으로써 빈정거림과 무자비함의 대상이 되었다는 주장은 오래 고수하지 못했다."], ["Human beings, in their generous endeavour to construct a hypothesis that shall not degrade a First Cause, have always hesitated to conceive a dominant power of lower moral quality than their own; and, even while they sit down and weep by the waters of Babylon, invent excuses for the oppression which prompts their tears.", "가장 냉혹한 부류의 인간을 제외하면 대개 그러한데, 인간이란 존재는 첫째 원인을 격하하지 않는 가설을 세우려는 그 후한 노력 속에서, 자신들보다 도덕적 질이 낮은 지배적 권능을 상상하기를 늘 주저해왔으며, 바빌론 강가에 주저앉아 울면서도 자신들의 눈물을 자아내는 그 억압에 변명을 지어내는 것이다."], ["Thus, though words of solace were vainly uttered in his presence, he found relief in a direction of his own choosing when left to himself.", "그리하여 그의 면전에서 위로의 말이 부질없이 발화되긴 했어도, 그는 혼자 남겨졌을 때 스스로 택한 방향에서 위안을 찾았다."], ["For a man of his habits the house and the hundred and twenty pounds a year which he had inherited from his mother were enough to supply all worldly needs.", "그 같은 습성을 지닌 사내에게는 어머니에게서 물려받은 집과 연 백이십 파운드면 세속의 온갖 필요를 채우기에 충분했다."], ["Resources do not depend upon gross amounts, but upon the proportion of spendings to takings.", "살림이란 총액에 달린 것이 아니라 수입에 대한 지출의 비율에 달린 것이다."]], [["He frequently walked the heath alone, when the past seized upon him with its shadowy hand, and held him there to listen to its tale.", "그는 자주 혼자 황야를 걸었으니, 그럴 때면 과거가 그림자 같은 손으로 그를 붙들어 거기 세워두고 제 이야기를 듣게 했다."], ["His imagination would then people the spot with its ancient inhabitants—forgotten Celtic tribes trod their tracks about him, and he could almost live among them, look in their faces, and see them standing beside the barrows which swelled around, untouched and perfect as at the time of their erection.", "그러면 그의 상상은 그 자리를 그 옛 거주자들로 채웠으니—잊힌 켈트 부족들이 그를 둘러싸고 제 자취를 밟고 다녔고, 그는 거의 그들 사이에서 살며, 그들의 얼굴을 들여다보고, 사방으로 부풀어 오른 봉분 곁에 서 있는 그들을, 세워졌던 그 시절처럼 손상되지 않고 온전한 모습으로 볼 수 있었다."], ["Those of the dyed barbarians who had chosen the cultivable tracts were, in comparison with those who had left their marks here, as writers on paper beside writers on parchment.", "물들인 야만인들 가운데 경작지를 택했던 자들은, 여기 자취를 남긴 자들에 견주면, 양피지에 글을 쓴 자들 옆에 선 종이에 글을 쓴 자들과 같았다."], ["Their records had perished long ago by the plough, while the works of these remained.", "전자의 기록은 오래전 쟁기에 스러졌으나 후자의 작업은 남아 있었다."], ["Yet they all had lived and died unconscious of the different fates awaiting their relics.", "그럼에도 그들은 모두 제 유물을 기다리는 상이한 운명을 알지 못한 채 살다 죽었다."], ["It reminded him that unforeseen factors operate in the evolution of immortality.", "그것은 그에게 불멸의 진화에는 예측 못 할 요인들이 작용한다는 사실을 일깨워주었다."]], [["Winter again came round, with its winds, frosts, tame robins, and sparkling starlight.", "다시 겨울이 돌아왔으니, 그 바람과 서리와 길든 울새와 반짝이는 별빛과 함께였다."], ["The year previous Thomasin had hardly been conscious of the season’s advance; this year she laid her heart open to external influences of every kind.", "지난해에 토머신은 계절의 흐름을 거의 의식하지 못했으나, 올해는 온갖 종류의 외부 영향에 마음을 활짝 열었다."], ["The life of this sweet cousin, her baby, and her servants, came to Clym’s senses only in the form of sounds through a wood partition as he sat over books of exceptionally large type; but his ear became at last so accustomed to these slight noises from the other part of the house that he almost could witness the scenes they signified.", "이 다정한 사촌과 그녀의 갓난아기와 하인들의 삶은, 유달리 큰 활자의 책을 들여다보며 앉은 클림의 감각에는 나무 칸막이 너머로 들려오는 소리의 형태로만 가닿았지만, 그의 귀는 마침내 집의 다른 편에서 나는 이 사소한 소음들에 어찌나 익숙해졌던지, 그 소리가 가리키는 장면들을 거의 목격할 수 있을 지경이었다."], ["A faint beat of half-seconds conjured up Thomasin rocking the cradle, a wavering hum meant that she was singing the baby to sleep, a crunching of sand as between millstones raised the picture of Humphrey’s, Fairway’s, or Sam’s heavy feet crossing the stone floor of the kitchen; a light boyish step, and a gay tune in a high key, betokened a visit from Grandfer Cantle; a sudden break-off in the Grandfer’s utterances implied the application to his lips of a mug of small beer, a bustling and slamming of doors meant starting to go to market; for Thomasin, in spite of her added scope of gentility, led a ludicrously narrow life, to the end that she might save every possible pound for her little daughter.", "반초의 희미한 박자는 토머신이 요람을 흔드는 모습을 떠올리게 했고, 일렁이는 흥얼거림은 그녀가 아기를 재우며 노래하고 있다는 뜻이었으며, 맷돌 사이에서 나듯 모래가 바스러지는 소리는 험프리나 페어웨이나 샘의 무거운 발이 부엌의 돌바닥을 가로지르는 그림을 불러일으켰고, 소년 같은 가벼운 발걸음과 높은 음역의 흥겨운 곡조는 캔틀 영감이 찾아온 것을 알렸으며, 영감의 말이 별안간 뚝 끊기는 것은 약한 맥주 잔이 그의 입술에 갖다 대어졌다는 뜻이었고, 부산하게 문이 여닫히는 소리는 장에 나서려는 채비를 뜻했으니—토머신은 점잖음의 여지가 늘어났음에도, 어린 딸을 위해 가능한 모든 파운드를 아끼겠다는 일념으로, 우스꽝스러우리만치 빠듯한 삶을 살았다."]], [["One summer day Clym was in the garden, immediately outside the parlour window, which was as usual open.", "어느 여름날 클림은 거실 창문 바로 바깥의 정원에 있었는데, 창문은 여느 때처럼 열려 있었다."], ["He was looking at the pot-flowers on the sill; they had been revived and restored by Thomasin to the state in which his mother had left them.", "그는 창턱의 화분 꽃들을 바라보고 있었으니, 토머신이 그것들을 되살리고 손질하여 그의 어머니가 남겨두었던 상태로 돌려놓은 터였다."], ["He heard a slight scream from Thomasin, who was sitting inside the room.", "방 안에 앉아 있던 토머신의 작은 비명 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“O, how you frightened me!”", "\"어머, 사람 놀라게 하긴!\""], ["she said to someone who had entered.", "그녀가 들어온 누군가에게 말했다."], ["“I thought you were the ghost of yourself.”", "\"당신 유령인 줄 알았잖아요.\""]], [["Clym was curious enough to advance a little further and look in at the window.", "클림은 호기심이 일어 조금 더 다가가 창문 안을 들여다보았다."], ["To his astonishment there stood within the room Diggory Venn, no longer a reddleman, but exhibiting the strangely altered hues of an ordinary Christian countenance, white shirt-front, light flowered waistcoat, blue-spotted neckerchief, and bottle-green coat.", "놀랍게도 방 안에는 디고리 벤이 서 있었는데, 이제는 더 이상 레들맨이 아니라, 평범한 기독교인의 이상하게 달라진 안색—새하얀 셔츠 앞섶, 꽃무늬가 든 밝은 조끼, 푸른 점박이 목수건, 병녹색 외투—을 드러내고 있었다."], ["Nothing in this appearance was at all singular but the fact of its great difference from what he had formerly been.", "이 행색에 조금이라도 기이한 데가 있다면 그것은 그가 예전의 모습과 엄청나게 달라졌다는 사실뿐이었다."], ["Red, and all approach to red, was carefully excluded from every article of clothes upon him; for what is there that persons just out of harness dread so much as reminders of the trade which has enriched them?", "붉은색은, 붉은색에 가까운 그 무엇도, 그의 몸에 걸친 모든 의복에서 세심하게 배제되어 있었으니, 막 일에서 손을 뗀 사람들이 자신을 부유하게 만들어준 그 생업을 상기시키는 것만큼 질색하는 것이 또 있겠는가?"]], [["Yeobright went round to the door and entered.", "여브라이트는 문 쪽으로 돌아가 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["“I was so alarmed!”", "\"어찌나 놀랐는지!\""], ["said Thomasin, smiling from one to the other.", "토머신이 두 사람을 번갈아 보며 미소 지었다."], ["“I couldn’t believe that he had got white of his own accord!", "\"이분이 제 힘으로 하얘졌다는 게 도무지 믿기지 않았어요!"], ["It seemed supernatural.”", "무슨 초자연적인 일 같았다니까요.\""]], [["“I gave up dealing in reddle last Christmas,”", "\"지난 크리스마스에 레들 장사를 접었지요.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."], ["“It was a profitable trade, and I found that by that time I had made enough to take the dairy of fifty cows that my father had in his lifetime.", "\"수지맞는 일이긴 했고, 그때쯤 되니 아버지가 생전에 치던 젖소 쉰 마리짜리 낙농장을 차릴 만큼은 벌었더군요."], ["I always thought of getting to that place again if I changed at all, and now I am there.”", "무슨 변화를 줄 작정이라면 그 자리로 다시 돌아가고 싶다고 늘 생각해왔는데, 이제 거기 가 있는 겁니다.\""]], [["“How did you manage to become white, Diggory?”", "\"어떻게 하얘진 거예요, 디고리?\""], ["Thomasin asked.", "토머신이 물었다."]], [["“I turned so by degrees, ma’am.”", "\"차차 그리됐습죠, 마님.\""]], [["“You look much better than ever you did before.”", "\"전보다 훨씬 보기 좋아요.\""]], [["Venn appeared confused; and Thomasin, seeing how inadvertently she had spoken to a man who might possibly have tender feelings for her still, blushed a little.", "벤은 당황한 기색이었고, 토머신은 어쩌면 아직도 자신에게 애틋한 마음을 품고 있을지 모를 사내에게 자기가 얼마나 경솔하게 말했는지 깨닫고는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["Clym saw nothing of this, and added good-humouredly—", "클림은 이런 낌새를 전혀 알아채지 못한 채 유쾌하게 덧붙였다—"]], [["“What shall we have to frighten Thomasin’s baby with, now you have become a human being again?”", "\"이제 자네가 다시 사람이 되었으니, 토머신네 아기를 무엇으로 겁줘서 달랜담?\""]], [["“Sit down, Diggory,”", "\"앉으세요, 디고리.\""], ["said Thomasin, “and stay to tea.”", "토머신이 말했다. \"차나 한잔 들고 가요.\""]], [["Venn moved as if he would retire to the kitchen, when Thomasin said with pleasant pertness as she went on with some sewing, “Of course you must sit down here.", "벤이 부엌으로 물러가려는 듯 몸을 움직이자, 토머신은 바느질을 계속하며 정답고도 야무진 투로 말했다. \"당연히 여기 앉으셔야죠."], ["And where does your fifty-cow dairy lie, Mr. Venn?”", "그런데 그 젖소 쉰 마리짜리 낙농장은 어디 있어요, 벤 씨?\""]], [["“At Stickleford—about two miles to the right of Alderworth, ma’am, where the meads begin.", "\"스티클포드입니다—올더워스에서 오른쪽으로 한 이 마일 가서, 풀밭이 시작되는 데지요, 마님."], ["I have thought that if Mr. Yeobright would like to pay me a visit sometimes he shouldn’t stay away for want of asking.", "여브라이트 씨가 가끔 저를 찾아주고 싶으시다면, 청하지 않았다고 못 오시는 일은 없도록 했으면 합니다."], ["I’ll not bide to tea this afternoon, thank’ee, for I’ve got something on hand that must be settled.", "오늘 오후엔 차를 들고 갈 수가 없겠습니다, 고맙습니다만, 매듭지어야 할 일이 하나 있어서요."], ["’Tis Maypole-day tomorrow, and the Shadwater folk have clubbed with a few of your neighbours here to have a pole just outside your palings in the heath, as it is a nice green place.”", "내일이 메이폴 날인데, 섀드워터 사람들이 여기 이웃 몇 분과 한패가 되어, 댁의 울타리 바로 바깥 황야에다 기둥을 세우기로 했답니다, 거기가 푸르고 좋은 자리라서요.\""], ["Venn waved his elbow towards the patch in front of the house.", "벤은 팔꿈치를 집 앞의 빈터 쪽으로 휘둘렀다."], ["“I have been talking to Fairway about it,”", "\"페어웨이하고 그 얘길 나눴는데,\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["he continued, “and I said to him that before we put up the pole it would be as well to ask Mrs. Wildeve.”", "\"기둥을 세우기 전에 와일디브 부인께 여쭤보는 게 좋겠다고 그에게 일러두었지요.\""]], [["“I can say nothing against it,”", "\"반대할 이유가 없네요.\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“Our property does not reach an inch further than the white palings.”", "\"우리 땅은 저 흰 울타리에서 한 치도 더 나가지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“But you might not like to see a lot of folk going crazy round a stick, under your very nose?”", "\"하지만 코앞에서 한 무리의 사람들이 막대기를 둘러싸고 미쳐 날뛰는 걸 보는 게 달갑지 않으실 수도 있잖습니까?\""]], [["“I shall have no objection at all.”", "\"전혀 거리낄 것 없어요.\""]], [["Venn soon after went away, and in the evening Yeobright strolled as far as Fairway’s cottage.", "벤은 곧 자리를 떴고, 저녁에 여브라이트는 페어웨이의 오두막까지 산책 삼아 걸어갔다."], ["It was a lovely May sunset, and the birch trees which grew on this margin of the vast Egdon wilderness had put on their new leaves, delicate as butterflies’ wings, and diaphanous as amber.", "사랑스러운 5월의 일몰이었고, 광막한 에그던 황무지의 이 가장자리에 자란 자작나무들은 나비 날개처럼 섬세하고 호박처럼 투명한 새잎을 내밀고 있었다."], ["Beside Fairway’s dwelling was an open space recessed from the road, and here were now collected all the young people from within a radius of a couple of miles.", "페어웨이의 거처 옆에는 길에서 움푹 들어간 빈터가 있었고, 거기에는 이제 반경 이 마일 안의 젊은이들이 죄다 모여 있었다."], ["The pole lay with one end supported on a trestle, and women were engaged in wreathing it from the top downwards with wild-flowers.", "기둥은 한쪽 끝을 받침대에 괴어 누인 채였고, 여인들은 그것을 꼭대기에서부터 들꽃으로 휘감는 일에 매달려 있었다."], ["The instincts of merry England lingered on here with exceptional vitality, and the symbolic customs which tradition has attached to each season of the year were yet a reality on Egdon.", "즐거운 잉글랜드의 본능이 유달리 왕성한 생기로 이곳에 깃들어 있었고, 한 해의 각 계절에 전통이 결부시켜온 상징적 풍습들이 에그던에서는 아직 현실이었다."], ["Indeed, the impulses of all such outlandish hamlets are pagan still—in these spots homage to nature, self-adoration, frantic gaieties, fragments of Teutonic rites to divinities whose names are forgotten, seem in some way or other to have survived mediæval doctrine.", "사실 이런 외진 촌락들의 충동은 여전히 이교적인 것이니—이런 곳에서는 자연에 대한 경배, 자기 숭배, 광란의 흥겨움, 이름조차 잊힌 신들에게 바치던 게르만 제례의 단편들이, 어떤 식으로든 중세의 교리를 뚫고 살아남은 듯했다."]], [["Yeobright did not interrupt the preparations, and went home again.", "여브라이트는 준비에 끼어들지 않고 다시 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["The next morning, when Thomasin withdrew the curtains of her bedroom window, there stood the Maypole in the middle of the green, its top cutting into the sky.", "이튿날 아침 토머신이 침실 창문의 커튼을 걷자, 빈터 한가운데에 메이폴이 서서 그 꼭대기가 하늘을 가르고 있었다."], ["It had sprung up in the night, or rather early morning, like Jack’s bean-stalk.", "그것은 잭의 콩나무처럼 밤사이에, 아니 이른 아침에 불쑥 솟아난 것이었다."], ["She opened the casement to get a better view of the garlands and posies that adorned it.", "그녀는 그것을 장식한 화환과 꽃다발을 더 잘 보려고 여닫이창을 열었다."], ["The sweet perfume of the flowers had already spread into the surrounding air, which, being free from every taint, conducted to her lips a full measure of the fragrance received from the spire of blossom in its midst.", "꽃들의 달콤한 향기가 이미 둘레의 대기로 퍼져 있었는데, 그 대기는 어떤 오염도 없이 깨끗하여, 그 한가운데 솟은 꽃의 첨탑에서 받아낸 향기를 가득 그녀의 입술로 실어다 주었다."], ["At the top of the pole were crossed hoops decked with small flowers; beneath these came a milk-white zone of Maybloom; then a zone of bluebells, then of cowslips, then of lilacs, then of ragged-robins, daffodils, and so on, till the lowest stage was reached.", "기둥 꼭대기에는 작은 꽃들로 장식된 십자 모양의 테가 얹혀 있었고, 그 아래로는 우윳빛 산사꽃 띠가, 그다음에는 블루벨 띠가, 그다음에는 카우슬립이, 그다음에는 라일락이, 그다음에는 동자꽃과 수선화 따위가, 가장 아래 단에 이르기까지 차례로 둘러져 있었다."], ["Thomasin noticed all these, and was delighted that the May revel was to be so near.", "토머신은 이 모든 것을 알아보았고, 5월의 흥겨운 잔치가 이토록 가까이서 벌어진다는 데 기뻤다."]], [["When afternoon came people began to gather on the green, and Yeobright was interested enough to look out upon them from the open window of his room.", "오후가 되자 사람들이 빈터에 모여들기 시작했고, 여브라이트도 자기 방의 열린 창문으로 그들을 내다볼 만큼은 흥미를 느꼈다."], ["Soon after this Thomasin walked out from the door immediately below and turned her eyes up to her cousin’s face.", "그러고 나서 곧 토머신이 바로 아래 문에서 걸어 나와 사촌의 얼굴 쪽으로 눈을 들었다."], ["She was dressed more gaily than Yeobright had ever seen her dressed since the time of Wildeve’s death, eighteen months before; since the day of her marriage even she had not exhibited herself to such advantage.", "그녀는 십팔 개월 전 와일디브가 죽은 이래로 여브라이트가 본 그 어느 때보다도 화사하게 차려입었으니, 결혼하던 날조차 그녀는 이만큼 자신을 돋보이게 드러낸 적이 없었다."]], [["“How pretty you look today, Thomasin!” he said.", "\"오늘 참 예뻐 보이는구나, 토머신!\""], ["“Is it because of the Maypole?”", "그가 말했다. \"메이폴 때문이니?\""]], [["“Not altogether.”", "\"꼭 그것만은 아니에요.\""], ["And then she blushed and dropped her eyes, which he did not specially observe, though her manner seemed to him to be rather peculiar, considering that she was only addressing himself.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 눈을 내리떴는데, 그는 이를 특별히 눈여겨보지 않았으나, 그녀가 자기에게만 말을 건네고 있을 뿐인 것치고는 그 태도가 어딘지 묘하게 여겨졌다."], ["Could it be possible that she had put on her summer clothes to please him?", "그녀가 자기를 기쁘게 하려고 여름옷을 차려입은 것이 가당키나 한 일일까?"]], [["He recalled her conduct towards him throughout the last few weeks, when they had often been working together in the garden, just as they had formerly done when they were boy and girl under his mother’s eye.", "그는 지난 몇 주 동안 그녀가 그를 대한 태도를 떠올렸으니, 그 무렵 두 사람은 어머니의 눈길 아래 소년 소녀였던 옛날처럼 종종 함께 정원에서 일하곤 했다."], ["What if her interest in him were not so entirely that of a relative as it had formerly been?", "그녀가 그에게 품은 관심이 예전처럼 그저 친족의 그것만은 아니라면 어쩐단 말인가?"], ["To Yeobright any possibility of this sort was a serious matter; and he almost felt troubled at the thought of it.", "여브라이트에게 이런 종류의 가능성은 무엇이든 심각한 문제였고, 그는 그 생각만으로도 거의 괴로움을 느꼈다."], ["Every pulse of loverlike feeling which had not been stilled during Eustacia’s lifetime had gone into the grave with her.", "유스테이시아가 살아 있는 동안 잠재워지지 않았던 연인다운 감정의 고동은 죄다 그녀와 함께 무덤으로 들어가버렸다."], ["His passion for her had occurred too far on in his manhood to leave fuel enough on hand for another fire of that sort, as may happen with more boyish loves.", "그녀를 향한 그의 정열은 사내로서 그의 생애에서 너무 늦은 지점에 닥쳐온 탓에, 더 소년다운 사랑에서라면 그럴 수도 있겠지만, 그런 종류의 또 다른 불길을 지필 만한 땔감을 손에 남겨두지 못했다."], ["Even supposing him capable of loving again, that love would be a plant of slow and laboured growth, and in the end only small and sickly, like an autumn-hatched bird.", "설령 그가 다시 사랑할 수 있다 한들, 그 사랑은 더디고 힘겹게 자라는 식물일 것이고, 끝내는 가을에 부화한 새처럼 자그맣고 병약한 것에 그칠 터였다."]], [["He was so distressed by this new complexity that when the enthusiastic brass band arrived and struck up, which it did about five o’clock, with apparently wind enough among its members to blow down his house, he withdrew from his rooms by the back door, went down the garden, through the gate in the hedge, and away out of sight.", "이 새로운 복잡함에 어찌나 괴로웠던지, 그 열성적인 금관악대가 다섯 시쯤 도착하여 연주를 시작했을 때, 단원들 사이에 그의 집을 불어 날려버릴 만큼 바람이 그득한 듯한 그 소리가 울려 퍼지자, 그는 뒷문으로 자기 방을 빠져나가, 정원을 내려가, 산울타리의 쪽문을 지나, 보이지 않는 곳으로 멀리 가버렸다."], ["He could not bear to remain in the presence of enjoyment today, though he had tried hard.", "오늘만큼은 흥겨움의 한복판에 머물러 있는 것을 견딜 수 없었으니, 무던히 애를 썼는데도 그러했다."], ["Nothing was seen of him for four hours.", "네 시간 동안 그의 모습은 보이지 않았다."], ["When he came back by the same path it was dusk, and the dews were coating every green thing.", "그가 같은 길로 돌아왔을 때는 땅거미가 졌고, 이슬이 모든 푸른 것을 적시고 있었다."], ["The boisterous music had ceased; but, entering the premises as he did from behind, he could not see if the May party had all gone till he had passed through Thomasin’s division of the house to the front door.", "떠들썩한 음악은 그쳐 있었으나, 그가 그렇게 뒤편으로 부지에 들어선 탓에, 토머신 쪽 집을 가로질러 앞문에 이를 때까지는 메이폴 잔치꾼들이 다 갔는지 알 수 없었다."], ["Thomasin was standing within the porch alone.", "토머신은 현관 안에 혼자 서 있었다."]], [["She looked at him reproachfully.", "그녀가 그를 나무라듯 바라보았다."], ["“You went away just when it began, Clym,”", "\"막 시작하려니까 가버리셨잖아요, 클림.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["I felt I could not join in.", "끼어들 수가 없겠더구나."], ["You went out with them, of course?”", "너야 물론 그들과 함께 나갔겠지?\""]], [["“No, I did not.”", "\"아뇨, 안 나갔어요.\""]], [["“You appeared to be dressed on purpose.”", "\"일부러 차려입은 것 같던데.\""]], [["“Yes, but I could not go out alone; so many people were there.", "\"그렇긴 한데, 혼자서는 나갈 수가 없었어요, 사람이 그리 많은데."], ["One is there now.”", "지금도 한 사람 나와 있네요.\""]], [["Yeobright strained his eyes across the dark-green patch beyond the paling, and near the black form of the Maypole he discerned a shadowy figure, sauntering idly up and down.", "여브라이트는 울타리 너머 짙푸른 빈터로 눈을 부릅떴고, 메이폴의 검은 형체 가까이에서 그림자 같은 형상 하나가 하릴없이 오르내리며 거니는 것을 분간해냈다."], ["“Who is it?”", "\"누구냐?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Venn,”", "\"벤 씨예요.\""], ["said Thomasin.", "토머신이 말했다."]], [["“You might have asked him to come in, I think, Tamsie.", "\"안으로 들어오라고 청하지 그랬니, 탬시."], ["He has been very kind to you first and last.”", "그 사람은 처음부터 끝까지 너한테 무척 친절했잖니.\""]], [["“I will now,”", "\"지금 할게요.\""], ["she said; and, acting on the impulse, went through the wicket to where Venn stood under the Maypole.", "그녀가 말하고는, 충동에 이끌려, 쪽문을 지나 메이폴 아래 벤이 서 있는 곳으로 갔다."]], [["“It is Mr. Venn, I think?”", "\"벤 씨, 맞죠?\""], ["she inquired.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["Venn started as if he had not seen her—artful man that he was—and said, “Yes.”", "벤은 그녀를 못 본 척—교활한 사내답게—흠칫하더니 말했다. \"네.\""]], [["“Will you come in?”", "\"들어오시겠어요?\""]], [["“I am afraid that I—”", "\"아무래도 저는—\""]], [["“I have seen you dancing this evening, and you had the very best of the girls for your partners.", "\"오늘 저녁 춤추시는 걸 봤는데, 처녀들 중에 제일 좋은 짝들을 골라 추시던걸요."], ["Is it that you won’t come in because you wish to stand here, and think over the past hours of enjoyment?”", "들어오지 않으시려는 게, 여기 서서 지나간 즐거운 시간들을 곱씹어보고 싶어서인가요?\""]], [["“Well, that’s partly it,”", "\"뭐, 그것도 한몫합죠.\""], ["said Mr. Venn, with ostentatious sentiment.", "벤이 짐짓 감상에 젖은 투로 말했다."], ["“But the main reason why I am biding here like this is that I want to wait till the moon rises.”", "\"하지만 제가 이렇게 여기 머무는 주된 까닭은, 달이 떠오를 때까지 기다리고 싶어서랍니다.\""]], [["“To see how pretty the Maypole looks in the moonlight?”", "\"달빛 속에서 메이폴이 얼마나 예쁜지 보려고요?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아닙니다."], ["To look for a glove that was dropped by one of the maidens.”", "처녀들 중 하나가 떨어뜨린 장갑을 찾으려고요.\""]], [["Thomasin was speechless with surprise.", "토머신은 놀라 말문이 막혔다."], ["That a man who had to walk some four or five miles to his home should wait here for such a reason pointed to only one conclusion—the man must be amazingly interested in that glove’s owner.", "집까지 사오 마일은 걸어야 하는 사내가 그런 이유로 여기서 기다린다는 것은 단 하나의 결론을 가리켰으니—그 사내는 그 장갑 임자에게 놀랍도록 마음이 가 있는 게 틀림없었다."]], [["“Were you dancing with her, Diggory?”", "\"그 처녀와 춤추셨어요, 디고리?\""], ["she asked, in a voice which revealed that he had made himself considerably more interesting to her by this disclosure.", "이 고백으로 그가 자기에게 훨씬 더 흥미로운 존재가 되었음을 드러내는 목소리로 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["he sighed.", "그가 한숨지었다."]], [["“And you will not come in, then?”", "\"그럼 들어오지 않으시겠다는 거죠?\""]], [["“Not tonight, thank you, ma’am.”", "\"오늘 밤은 안 되겠습니다, 고맙습니다, 마님.\""]], [["“Shall I lend you a lantern to look for the young person’s glove, Mr. Venn?”", "\"그 젊은 처녀의 장갑을 찾으시게 등불을 빌려드릴까요, 벤 씨?\""]], [["“O no; it is not necessary, Mrs. Wildeve, thank you.", "\"아, 아닙니다, 그럴 것 없습니다, 와일디브 부인, 고맙습니다만."], ["The moon will rise in a few minutes.”", "몇 분이면 달이 뜰 테니까요.\""]], [["Thomasin went back to the porch.", "토머신은 현관으로 돌아갔다."], ["“Is he coming in?”", "\"들어온다더냐?\""], ["said Clym, who had been waiting where she had left him.", "그녀가 두고 간 자리에서 기다리던 클림이 말했다."]], [["“He would rather not tonight,”", "\"오늘 밤은 안 들어오는 게 낫겠대요.\""], ["she said, and then passed by him into the house; whereupon Clym too retired to his own rooms.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하고는 그를 지나쳐 집 안으로 들어갔고, 그러자 클림도 자기 방으로 물러갔다."]], [["When Clym was gone Thomasin crept upstairs in the dark, and, just listening by the cot, to assure herself that the child was asleep, she went to the window, gently lifted the corner of the white curtain, and looked out.", "클림이 가버리자 토머신은 어둠 속에서 살금살금 위층으로 올라가, 요람 곁에서 잠깐 귀를 기울여 아이가 잠든 것을 확인하고는, 창가로 가서 흰 커튼 자락을 살며시 들어 올려 밖을 내다보았다."], ["Venn was still there.", "벤은 아직도 거기 있었다."], ["She watched the growth of the faint radiance appearing in the sky by the eastern hill, till presently the edge of the moon burst upwards and flooded the valley with light.", "그녀는 동쪽 언덕 옆 하늘에 나타난 희미한 빛이 자라나는 것을 지켜보았고, 이윽고 달의 가장자리가 위로 솟구쳐 골짜기를 빛으로 가득 채웠다."], ["Diggory’s form was now distinct on the green; he was moving about in a bowed attitude, evidently scanning the grass for the precious missing article, walking in zigzags right and left till he should have passed over every foot of the ground.", "디고리의 형체가 이제 빈터에 또렷이 드러났으니, 그는 허리를 굽힌 자세로 움직이며, 분명 그 귀한 잃어버린 물건을 찾으려 풀밭을 살피고 있었고, 땅의 한 자 한 자를 빠짐없이 훑을 때까지 좌우로 갈지자를 그리며 걷고 있었다."]], [["“How very ridiculous!”", "\"참 우스꽝스럽기도 하지!\""], ["Thomasin murmured to herself, in a tone which was intended to be satirical.", "토머신이 빈정거리려는 어조로 혼자 중얼거렸다."], ["“To think that a man should be so silly as to go mooning about like that for a girl’s glove!", "\"사내라는 사람이 계집애 장갑 하나 때문에 저렇게 얼빠진 모양으로 어슬렁거리다니, 어쩜 저리 미련할까!"], ["A respectable dairyman, too, and a man of money as he is now.", "더구나 어엿한 낙농가에다, 이제는 돈도 있는 사람이!"], ["What a pity!”", "안됐기도 하지!\""]], [["At last Venn appeared to find it; whereupon he stood up and raised it to his lips. Then placing it in his breastpocket—the nearest receptacle to a man’s heart permitted by modern raiment—he ascended the valley in a mathematically direct line towards his distant home in the meadows.", "마침내 벤이 그것을 찾은 듯했으니, 그러자 그는 몸을 일으켜 그것을 입술로 가져갔다."], ["II. Thomasin Walks in a Green Place by the Roman Road", "그러고는 그것을 가슴 주머니—현대의 의복이 허락하는, 사람의 심장에 가장 가까운 보관처—에 넣고서, 풀밭 너머 멀리 있는 자기 집을 향해 수학적으로 곧은 일직선을 그리며 골짜기를 거슬러 올라갔다."]], [["Clym saw little of Thomasin for several days after this; and when they met she was more silent than usual.", "이 일이 있은 뒤 며칠 동안 클림은 토머신을 거의 보지 못했고, 마주칠 때면 그녀는 여느 때보다 말이 적었다."], ["At length he asked her what she was thinking of so intently.", "마침내 그는 그녀에게 무엇을 그리 골똘히 생각하느냐고 물었다."]], [["“I am thoroughly perplexed,”", "\"정말 갈피를 못 잡겠어요.\""], ["she said candidly.", "그녀가 솔직하게 말했다."], ["“I cannot for my life think who it is that Diggory Venn is so much in love with.", "\"디고리 벤이 그토록 사랑에 빠진 상대가 누군지, 아무리 해도 짐작이 안 가요."], ["None of the girls at the Maypole were good enough for him, and yet she must have been there.”", "메이폴에 있던 처녀들 중에 그 사람한테 어울릴 만한 애는 하나도 없었는데, 그래도 그 자리에 분명 있었을 거 아녜요.\""]], [["Clym tried to imagine Venn’s choice for a moment; but ceasing to be interested in the question he went on again with his gardening.", "클림은 잠깐 벤이 골랐을 상대를 떠올려보려 했으나, 그 문제에 흥미가 시들해지자 다시 정원 일을 계속했다."]], [["No clearing up of the mystery was granted her for some time.", "한동안 그녀에게 그 수수께끼가 풀리지 않았다."], ["But one afternoon Thomasin was upstairs getting ready for a walk, when she had occasion to come to the landing and call “Rachel.”", "그러나 어느 오후 토머신은 위층에서 산책 채비를 하다가, 마침 층계참으로 나와 \"레이철\" 하고 부를 일이 생겼다."], ["Rachel was a girl about thirteen, who carried the baby out for airings; and she came upstairs at the call.", "레이철은 열세 살쯤 된 계집아이로, 아기를 데리고 나가 바람을 쐬어주는 일을 했는데, 그 부름에 위층으로 올라왔다."]], [["“Have you seen one of my last new gloves about the house, Rachel?”", "\"내 새 장갑 한 짝을 집 안에서 본 적 있니, 레이철?\""], ["inquired Thomasin.", "토머신이 물었다."], ["“It is the fellow to this one.”", "\"이거랑 짝이 되는 거 말이야.\""]], [["Rachel did not reply.", "레이철은 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“Why don’t you answer?”", "\"왜 대답을 안 해?\""], ["said her mistress.", "안주인이 말했다."]], [["“I think it is lost, ma’am.”", "\"잃어버린 것 같아요, 마님.\""]], [["“Lost?", "\"잃어버려?"], ["Who lost it?", "누가 잃어버렸는데?"], ["I have never worn them but once.”", "난 그걸 딱 한 번밖에 안 꼈는데.\""]], [["Rachel appeared as one dreadfully troubled, and at last began to cry.", "레이철은 몹시 곤란해하는 사람처럼 굴더니, 끝내 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“Please, ma’am, on the day of the Maypole I had none to wear, and I seed yours on the table, and I thought I would borrow ’em.", "\"저, 마님, 메이폴 날에 제가 낄 장갑이 없었는데, 탁자 위에서 마님 것을 보고는 빌려 끼면 되겠다 싶었어요."], ["I did not mean to hurt ’em at all, but one of them got lost.", "상하게 할 마음은 조금도 없었는데, 그중 한 짝을 잃어버리고 말았어요."], ["Somebody gave me some money to buy another pair for you, but I have not been able to go anywhere to get ’em.”", "어떤 분이 마님께 새 한 켤레를 사드리라고 저한테 돈을 주셨는데, 어디 가서 살 짬을 못 냈어요.\""]], [["“Who’s somebody?”", "\"어떤 분이라니?\""]], [["“Mr. Venn.”", "\"벤 씨요.\""]], [["“Did he know it was my glove?”", "\"그가 그게 내 장갑인 줄 알았어?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["I told him.”", "제가 말씀드렸거든요.\""]], [["Thomasin was so surprised by the explanation that she quite forgot to lecture the girl, who glided silently away.", "토머신은 그 설명에 어찌나 놀랐던지 계집아이를 나무라는 것마저 깜빡 잊었고, 아이는 소리 없이 슬그머니 물러갔다."], ["Thomasin did not move further than to turn her eyes upon the grass-plat where the Maypole had stood.", "토머신은 메이폴이 서 있던 풀밭 쪽으로 눈을 돌리는 것 말고는 더 움직이지 않았다."], ["She remained thinking, then said to herself that she would not go out that afternoon, but would work hard at the baby’s unfinished lovely plaid frock, cut on the cross in the newest fashion.", "그녀는 생각에 잠긴 채 있다가, 그날 오후엔 나가지 않고 아기의 미완성 격자무늬 고운 원피스—최신 유행대로 비스듬히 재단한 것—를 부지런히 짓겠노라고 혼잣말을 했다."], ["How she managed to work hard, and yet do no more than she had done at the end of two hours, would have been a mystery to anyone not aware that the recent incident was of a kind likely to divert her industry from a manual to a mental channel.", "그녀가 어떻게 부지런히 일하면서도 두 시간이 다 가도록 한 일이 그것뿐이었는지는, 방금의 그 사건이 그녀의 부지런함을 손의 일에서 마음의 일로 돌려놓기 십상인 종류였음을 모르는 이에게는 수수께끼였으리라."]], [["Next day she went her ways as usual, and continued her custom of walking in the heath with no other companion than little Eustacia, now of the age when it is a matter of doubt with such characters whether they are intended to walk through the world on their hands or on their feet; so that they get into painful complications by trying both.", "이튿날 그녀는 여느 때처럼 제 일을 보았고, 어린 유스테이시아 말고는 동행 없이 황야를 거니는 버릇을 이어갔으니, 아이는 이제 그런 부류의 존재가 손으로 세상을 헤쳐나가게 되어 있는지 발로 헤쳐나가게 되어 있는지가 의심스러운 나이라, 양쪽을 다 시도하다가 고통스러운 곤경에 빠지곤 했다."], ["It was very pleasant to Thomasin, when she had carried the child to some lonely place, to give her a little private practice on the green turf and shepherd’s-thyme, which formed a soft mat to fall headlong upon them when equilibrium was lost.", "토머신에게는, 아이를 외딴 곳으로 데려가, 균형을 잃었을 때 곤두박질해도 부드러운 깔개가 되어주는 푸른 잔디와 백리향 위에서 살짝 남몰래 연습을 시켜주는 것이 무척이나 즐거운 일이었다."]], [["Once, when engaged in this system of training, and stooping to remove bits of stick, fern-stalks, and other such fragments from the child’s path, that the journey might not be brought to an untimely end by some insuperable barrier a quarter of an inch high, she was alarmed by discovering that a man on horseback was almost close beside her, the soft natural carpet having muffled the horse’s tread.", "한번은 이런 식의 훈련에 골몰하여, 사 분의 일 인치 높이의 넘을 수 없는 장벽 따위에 아이의 여정이 때 이르게 끝나버리는 일이 없도록, 아이가 갈 길에서 나뭇가지 토막이며 고사리 줄기며 그 밖의 그런 부스러기를 치우려 허리를 굽히고 있을 때, 말을 탄 사내 하나가 자기 바로 곁에 거의 다 와 있는 것을 발견하고 그녀는 깜짝 놀랐으니, 부드러운 천연의 양탄자가 말발굽 소리를 죽여버린 탓이었다."], ["The rider, who was Venn, waved his hat in the air and bowed gallantly.", "벤이었던 그 기수는 공중에 모자를 흔들고 정중하게 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“Diggory, give me my glove,”", "\"디고리, 내 장갑 줘요.\""], ["said Thomasin, whose manner it was under any circumstances to plunge into the midst of a subject which engrossed her.", "어떤 처지에서든 자기 마음을 사로잡은 주제의 한복판으로 곧장 뛰어드는 것이 토머신의 방식이었다."]], [["Venn immediately dismounted, put his hand in his breastpocket, and handed the glove.", "벤은 즉시 말에서 내려, 가슴 주머니에 손을 넣더니, 장갑을 건넸다."]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고마워요."], ["It was very good of you to take care of it.”", "잘 간직해주시다니 참 친절도 하셔라.\""]], [["“It is very good of you to say so.”", "\"그리 말씀해주시니 부인이야말로 참 친절하십니다.\""]], [["“O no.", "\"아, 아녜요."], ["I was quite glad to find you had it.", "그걸 당신이 가지고 있어서 정말 기뻤어요."], ["Everybody gets so indifferent that I was surprised to know you thought of me.”", "다들 어찌나 무심해지는지, 당신이 나를 떠올려줬다는 게 놀라웠는걸요.\""]], [["“If you had remembered what I was once you wouldn’t have been surprised.”", "\"제가 한때 어떤 사람이었는지 기억하셨다면 놀라지 않으셨을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Ah, no,”", "\"아, 그렇지요.\""], ["she said quickly.", "그녀가 얼른 말했다."], ["“But men of your character are mostly so independent.”", "\"하지만 당신 같은 성품의 남자들은 대개 너무나 자립적이잖아요.\""]], [["“What is my character?”", "\"제 성품이 어떤데요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“I don’t exactly know,”", "\"정확히는 모르겠어요.\""], ["said Thomasin simply, “except it is to cover up your feelings under a practical manner, and only to show them when you are alone.”", "토머신이 솔직하게 말했다. \"다만 자기 감정을 실속 있는 태도 아래 감춰두고, 혼자 있을 때만 그걸 드러낸다는 것뿐이지요.\""]], [["“Ah, how do you know that?”", "\"아, 그건 또 어찌 아십니까?\""], ["said Venn strategically.", "벤이 슬쩍 떠보듯 말했다."]], [["“Because,”", "\"왜냐면,\""], ["said she, stopping to put the little girl, who had managed to get herself upside down, right end up again, “because I do.”", "그녀가 거꾸로 뒤집힌 꼴이 되어버린 어린 딸을 바로 세워주려고 멈추며 말했다. \"그냥 아니까요.\""]], [["“You mustn’t judge by folks in general,”", "\"세상 사람들 일반으로 헤아리시면 안 됩니다.\""], ["said Venn.", "벤이 말했다."], ["“Still I don’t know much what feelings are nowadays.", "\"하긴 저도 요새는 감정이라는 게 뭔지 잘 모르겠습니다."], ["I have got so mixed up with business of one sort and t’other that my soft sentiments are gone off in vapour like.", "이런저런 사업에 어찌나 뒤엉켜 지냈던지, 제 무른 마음씨는 죄다 김처럼 날아가버렸어요."], ["Yes, I am given up body and soul to the making of money.", "그래요, 저는 몸도 마음도 돈벌이에 내맡겨버렸습니다."], ["Money is all my dream.”", "돈이 제 꿈의 전부랍니다.\""]], [["“O Diggory, how wicked!”", "\"어머 디고리, 어쩜 그리 못됐어요!\""], ["said Thomasin reproachfully, and looking at him in exact balance between taking his words seriously and judging them as said to tease her.", "토머신이 나무라듯 말하면서, 그 말을 진지하게 받아들일지 자기를 놀리려는 말로 여길지 딱 중간에서 저울질하며 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Yes, ’tis rather a rum course,”", "\"네, 좀 괴상한 노릇이긴 합죠.\""], ["said Venn, in the bland tone of one comfortably resigned to sins he could no longer overcome.", "더는 이겨낼 수 없는 죄에 편안히 체념한 사람의 그 무던한 어조로 벤이 말했다."]], [["“You, who used to be so nice!”", "\"그렇게나 착하던 분이!\""]], [["“Well, that’s an argument I rather like, because what a man has once been he may be again.”", "\"뭐, 그건 제가 꽤나 좋아하는 논리인데요, 사람이 한때 어떠했다면 다시 그리 될 수도 있는 법이니까요.\""], ["Thomasin blushed.", "토머신이 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["“Except that it is rather harder now,”", "\"다만 지금은 좀 더 어렵게 됐다는 게 문제지만요.\""], ["Venn continued.", "벤이 말을 이었다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Because you be richer than you were at that time.”", "\"부인이 그때보다 더 부자가 되셨으니까요.\""]], [["“O no—not much.", "\"아, 아녜요—그리 대단치 않아요."], ["I have made it nearly all over to the baby, as it was my duty to do, except just enough to live on.”", "마땅히 해야 할 도리라, 딱 먹고살 만큼만 빼고는 거의 다 아기 앞으로 돌려놨거든요.\""]], [["“I am rather glad of that,”", "\"그 말씀 들으니 좀 기쁘군요.\""], ["said Venn softly, and regarding her from the corner of his eye, “for it makes it easier for us to be friendly.”", "벤이 부드럽게 말하며, 곁눈으로 그녀를 보았다. \"그래야 우리가 가깝게 지내기 더 수월하니 말입니다.\""]], [["Thomasin blushed again, and, when a few more words had been said of a not unpleasing kind, Venn mounted his horse and rode on.", "토머신은 다시 얼굴을 붉혔고, 싫지 않은 종류의 말이 몇 마디 더 오간 뒤, 벤은 말에 올라 떠나갔다."]], [["This conversation had passed in a hollow of the heath near the old Roman road, a place much frequented by Thomasin.", "이 대화는 옛 로마 가도 가까운 황야의 우묵한 곳에서 오갔는데, 토머신이 자주 다니던 곳이었다."], ["And it might have been observed that she did not in future walk that way less often from having met Venn there now.", "그리고 그곳에서 이제 벤을 만났다 하여 그녀가 이후로 그 길을 덜 다니지는 않았음을 누구든 눈여겨보았을 법하다."], ["Whether or not Venn abstained from riding thither because he had met Thomasin in the same place might easily have been guessed from her proceedings about two months later in the same year.", "벤이 같은 곳에서 토머신을 만났다 하여 그곳으로 말을 달리는 것을 삼갔는지 아닌지는, 그해 두어 달쯤 뒤 같은 자리에서 보인 그녀의 행적으로 능히 짐작할 수 있었으리라."]], [["III. The Serious Discourse of Clym with His Cousin\nThroughout this period Yeobright had more or less pondered on his duty to his cousin Thomasin.", "이 기간 내내 여브라이트는 사촌 토머신에 대한 자신의 도리를 다소간 곱씹어왔다."], ["He could not help feeling that it would be a pitiful waste of sweet material if the tender-natured thing should be doomed from this early stage of her life onwards to dribble away her winsome qualities on lonely gorse and fern.", "이 마음씨 고운 사람이 생애의 이 이른 단계에서부터 줄곧 외로운 가시금작화와 고사리에다 제 사랑스러운 자질을 한 방울씩 흘려보내도록 운명 지어진다면, 그것은 달콤한 재료의 가련한 낭비이리라는 느낌을 그는 떨칠 수 없었다."], ["But he felt this as an economist merely, and not as a lover.", "그러나 그가 이를 느낀 것은 한낱 경제가經濟家로서일 뿐, 연인으로서는 아니었다."], ["His passion for Eustacia had been a sort of conserve of his whole life, and he had nothing more of that supreme quality left to bestow.", "유스테이시아를 향한 그의 정열은 말하자면 그의 온 생애가 졸여진 것이었기에, 그에게는 그 지고한 것을 다시 내어줄 만큼이 더는 남아 있지 않았다."], ["So far the obvious thing was not to entertain any idea of marriage with Thomasin, even to oblige her.", "거기까지만 보면 두말할 것 없이 토머신과의 결혼이라는 생각은, 설령 그녀의 청을 들어주기 위해서라도, 품지 않는 것이 도리였다."]], [["But this was not all.", "그러나 그것이 전부가 아니었다."], ["Years ago there had been in his mother’s mind a great fancy about Thomasin and himself.", "여러 해 전 그의 어머니의 마음속에는 토머신과 그 자신에 관한 큰 공상이 있었다."], ["It had not positively amounted to a desire, but it had always been a favourite dream.", "그것이 단호히 소망에까지 이른 것은 아니었으나, 늘 마음에 드는 꿈이긴 했다."], ["That they should be man and wife in good time, if the happiness of neither were endangered thereby, was the fancy in question.", "어느 쪽의 행복도 그로 인해 위태로워지지 않는다면, 때가 무르익었을 때 둘이 부부가 되어야 한다는 것, 그것이 문제의 그 공상이었다."], ["So that what course save one was there now left for any son who reverenced his mother’s memory as Yeobright did?", "그러니 여브라이트만큼 어머니의 기억을 경외하는 아들에게 이제 하나 말고 무슨 길이 남아 있단 말인가?"], ["It is an unfortunate fact that any particular whim of parents, which might have been dispersed by half an hour’s conversation during their lives, becomes sublimated by their deaths into a fiat the most absolute, with such results to conscientious children as those parents, had they lived, would have been the first to decry.", "부모의 어떤 특정한 변덕이란, 그들이 살아 있을 적 반 시간의 대화로 흩어버릴 수도 있었을 것이건만, 그들의 죽음으로 더없이 절대적인 명령으로 승화되어, 양심적인 자식들에게는 그런 결과를 안기니, 정작 그 부모가 살아 있었다면 누구보다 먼저 그것을 못마땅해했을 일이라는 것은 불행한 사실이다."]], [["Had only Yeobright’s own future been involved he would have proposed to Thomasin with a ready heart.", "여브라이트 자신의 앞날만 걸린 일이었다면 그는 기꺼운 마음으로 토머신에게 청혼했으리라."], ["He had nothing to lose by carrying out a dead mother’s hope.", "죽은 어머니의 바람을 이룬다 하여 그가 잃을 것은 없었다."], ["But he dreaded to contemplate Thomasin wedded to the mere corpse of a lover that he now felt himself to be.", "그러나 토머신이 이제 그 자신이 느끼기에 한낱 연인의 시신에 불과한 사내에게 시집가는 것을 떠올리는 것은 두려웠다."], ["He had but three activities alive in him.", "그의 안에 살아 있는 활동은 셋뿐이었다."], ["One was his almost daily walk to the little graveyard wherein his mother lay; another, his just as frequent visits by night to the more distant enclosure which numbered his Eustacia among its dead; the third was self-preparation for a vocation which alone seemed likely to satisfy his cravings—that of an itinerant preacher of the eleventh commandment.", "하나는 어머니가 누워 있는 작은 묘지로 거의 날마다 걷는 것이었고, 또 하나는 유스테이시아를 그 망자들 가운데 헤아리는 더 멀리 떨어진 묘역으로 밤마다 그만큼 잦게 찾아가는 것이었으며, 셋째는 자신의 갈망을 채워줄 성싶은 유일한 천직—열한 번째 계명을 전하는 떠돌이 설교자—을 위해 스스로를 채비시키는 것이었다."], ["It was difficult to believe that Thomasin would be cheered by a husband with such tendencies as these.", "토머신이 이런 성향을 지닌 남편에게서 기쁨을 얻으리라고는 믿기 어려웠다."]], [["Yet he resolved to ask her, and let her decide for herself.", "그래도 그는 그녀에게 청하여, 스스로 결정하게 하기로 마음먹었다."], ["It was even with a pleasant sense of doing his duty that he went downstairs to her one evening for this purpose, when the sun was printing on the valley the same long shadow of the housetop that he had seen lying there times out of number while his mother lived.", "도리를 다한다는 흐뭇한 느낌마저 안고 어느 저녁 그는 이 일을 위해 그녀에게로 아래층으로 내려갔으니, 그때 해가 골짜기에 지붕마루의 그 긴 그림자를, 어머니가 살아 있을 적 셀 수 없이 여러 번 거기 드리워져 있던 것을 보았던 바로 그 그림자를 찍어내고 있었다."]], [["Thomasin was not in her room, and he found her in the front garden.", "토머신은 자기 방에 없었고, 그는 앞뜰에서 그녀를 찾아냈다."], ["“I have long been wanting, Thomasin,”", "\"오래전부터 말하고 싶었던 게 있었다, 토머신.\""], ["he began, “to say something about a matter that concerns both our futures.”", "그가 입을 열었다. \"우리 둘의 앞날에 다 관련된 일이야.\""]], [["“And you are going to say it now?”", "\"그걸 지금 말씀하시려는 거예요?\""], ["she remarked quickly, colouring as she met his gaze.", "그의 시선과 마주치며 얼굴을 붉힌 채 그녀가 얼른 말했다."], ["“Do stop a minute, Clym, and let me speak first, for oddly enough, I have been wanting to say something to you.”", "\"잠깐만요, 클림, 저부터 말하게 해주세요, 이상하게도 저 역시 오라버니께 드릴 말씀이 있었거든요.\""]], [["“By all means say on, Tamsie.”", "\"얼마든지 말하렴, 탬시.\""]], [["“I suppose nobody can overhear us?”", "\"아무도 우리 얘길 못 엿듣겠죠?\""], ["she went on, casting her eyes around and lowering her voice.", "그녀가 사방으로 눈길을 돌리고 목소리를 낮추며 말을 이었다."], ["“Well, first you will promise me this—that you won’t be angry and call me anything harsh if you disagree with what I propose?”", "\"자, 우선 이걸 약속해주세요—제가 드리는 제안이 마음에 안 들더라도 화내거나 모진 말로 저를 나무라지 않겠다고요.\""]], [["Yeobright promised, and she continued: “What I want is your advice, for you are my relation—I mean, a sort of guardian to me—aren’t you, Clym?”", "여브라이트가 약속하자 그녀가 계속했다. \"제가 바라는 건 오라버니의 조언이에요, 오라버니는 제 친족이니까요—그러니까, 일종의 후견인이잖아요—그렇죠, 클림?\""]], [["“Well, yes, I suppose I am; a sort of guardian.", "\"음, 그래, 그렇지, 일종의 후견인이지."], ["In fact, I am, of course,”", "사실, 물론 그렇고말고.\""], ["he said, altogether perplexed as to her drift.", "그녀의 속내가 무언지 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채 그가 말했다."]], [["“I am thinking of marrying,”", "\"저 결혼할까 생각 중이에요.\""], ["she then observed blandly.", "그러고서 그녀가 무던하게 말했다."], ["“But I shall not marry unless you assure me that you approve of such a step.", "\"하지만 오라버니가 그런 발걸음을 찬성한다고 다짐해주지 않으면 안 할 거예요."], ["Why don’t you speak?”", "왜 말씀이 없으세요?\""]], [["“I was taken rather by surprise.", "\"좀 놀라서 그랬다."], ["But, nevertheless, I am very glad to hear such news.", "하지만 그래도 그런 소식 들으니 무척 기쁘구나."], ["I shall approve, of course, dear Tamsie.", "물론 찬성하지, 우리 탬시."], ["Who can it be?", "누굴까?"], ["I am quite at a loss to guess.", "도무지 짐작이 안 가는걸."], ["No I am not—’tis the old doctor!—not that I mean to call him old, for he is not very old after all.", "아니다—그 늙은 의사 선생이구나!—늙었다고 부를 생각은 아니다, 따지고 보면 그리 늙은 양반도 아니니까."], ["Ah—I noticed when he attended you last time!”", "아—지난번에 그가 너를 돌볼 때 내가 눈치챘지!\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아녜요, 아녜요.\""], ["she said hastily.", "그녀가 황급히 말했다."], ["“’Tis Mr. Venn.”", "\"벤 씨예요.\""]], [["Clym’s face suddenly became grave.", "클림의 얼굴이 별안간 굳어졌다."]], [["“There, now, you don’t like him, and I wish I hadn’t mentioned him!”", "\"거 봐요, 오라버닌 그분을 싫어하잖아요, 괜히 그 사람 얘길 꺼냈나 봐요!\""], ["she exclaimed almost petulantly.", "그녀가 거의 토라진 투로 소리쳤다."], ["“And I shouldn’t have done it, either, only he keeps on bothering me so till I don’t know what to do!”", "\"사실 꺼내지 말았어야 했는데, 그분이 어찌나 자꾸 졸라대는지 어쩔 줄을 모르겠어서요!\""]], [["Clym looked at the heath.", "클림은 황야를 바라보았다."], ["“I like Venn well enough,”", "\"벤은 나도 어지간히 좋아한다.\""], ["he answered at last.", "마침내 그가 대답했다."], ["“He is a very honest and at the same time astute man.", "\"정직하면서도 동시에 빈틈없는 사람이지."], ["He is clever too, as is proved by his having got you to favour him.", "영리하기도 하고, 너로 하여금 자기에게 마음 두게 만든 것만 봐도 그게 증명되잖니."], ["But really, Thomasin, he is not quite—”", "하지만 정말로, 토머신, 그는 영 그게—\""]], [["“Gentleman enough for me?", "\"제게 어울릴 만한 신사가 못 된다고요?"], ["That is just what I feel.", "저도 꼭 그게 마음에 걸려요."], ["I am sorry now that I asked you, and I won’t think any more of him.", "이제 여쭤본 게 후회되고, 더는 그분 생각 안 할래요."], ["At the same time I must marry him if I marry anybody—that I _will_ say!”", "하지만 그래도 누구하고든 결혼한다면 저는 그분과 결혼해야 해요—이건 꼭 말하겠어요!\""]], [["“I don’t see that,”", "\"그건 잘 모르겠구나.\""], ["said Clym, carefully concealing every clue to his own interrupted intention, which she plainly had not guessed.", "그녀가 전혀 눈치채지 못한, 가로막혀버린 자기 본래의 의중에 대한 단서를 일일이 조심스레 감추며 클림이 말했다."], ["“You might marry a professional man, or somebody of that sort, by going into the town to live and forming acquaintances there.”", "\"읍내에 나가 살면서 거기서 사람들과 사귀면, 전문직에 종사하는 사람이나 그런 부류의 누군가와 결혼할 수도 있을 텐데.\""]], [["“I am not fit for town life—so very rural and silly as I always have been.", "\"전 읍내 생활엔 안 맞아요—제가 늘 이렇게나 촌스럽고 어리석은데."], ["Do not you yourself notice my countrified ways?”", "오라버니도 제 촌티 나는 태도를 알아채지 않으셨어요?\""]], [["“Well, when I came home from Paris I did, a little; but I don’t now.”", "\"음, 파리에서 돌아왔을 땐 좀 그랬지, 하지만 지금은 아니야.\""]], [["“That’s because you have got countrified too.", "\"그건 오라버니도 촌사람이 다 되어서 그래요."], ["O, I couldn’t live in a street for the world!", "아, 저는 죽었다 깨어나도 거리에선 못 살아요!"], ["Egdon is a ridiculous old place; but I have got used to it, and I couldn’t be happy anywhere else at all.”", "에그던은 우스꽝스러운 낡은 곳이지만, 저는 거기 익숙해졌고, 다른 어디서도 도무지 행복할 수가 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“Neither could I,”", "\"나도 그래.\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."]], [["“Then how could you say that I should marry some town man?", "\"그럼 어떻게 제가 어디 읍내 사람과 결혼해야 한다고 말씀하실 수 있어요?"], ["I am sure, say what you will, that I must marry Diggory, if I marry at all.", "분명히 말하지만, 뭐라 하시든, 제가 결혼한다면 디고리와 해야 해요."], ["He has been kinder to me than anybody else, and has helped me in many ways that I don’t know of!”", "그분은 누구보다 제게 친절했고, 제가 알지도 못하는 여러 방식으로 저를 도와주셨다고요!\""], ["Thomasin almost pouted now.", "토머신은 이제 거의 입을 삐죽였다."]], [["“Yes, he has,”", "\"그래, 그랬지.\""], ["said Clym in a neutral tone.", "클림이 덤덤한 어조로 말했다."], ["“Well, I wish with all my heart that I could say, marry him.", "\"음, 진심으로 마음 같아선 그와 결혼하라고 말하고 싶다."], ["But I cannot forget what my mother thought on that matter, and it goes rather against me not to respect her opinion.", "하지만 어머니가 그 문제에 대해 어떻게 생각하셨는지 잊을 수가 없고, 그 뜻을 존중하지 않는다는 게 영 마음에 걸리는구나."], ["There is too much reason why we should do the little we can to respect it now.”", "이제 와 우리가 할 수 있는 작은 일이나마 해서 그 뜻을 존중해야 할 이유가 너무도 많지 않니.\""]], [["“Very well, then,”", "\"그럼 좋아요.\""], ["sighed Thomasin.", "토머신이 한숨지었다."], ["“I will say no more.”", "\"더는 아무 말 안 할게요.\""]], [["“But you are not bound to obey my wishes.", "\"하지만 네가 내 뜻에 따라야 할 의무는 없어."], ["I merely say what I think.”", "난 그저 내 생각을 말할 뿐이야.\""]], [["“O no—I don’t want to be rebellious in that way,”", "\"아, 아녜요—그런 식으로 거역하고 싶진 않아요.\""], ["she said sadly.", "그녀가 서글프게 말했다."], ["“I had no business to think of him—I ought to have thought of my family.", "\"그분을 생각해선 안 되는 거였어요—제 집안을 생각했어야지요."], ["What dreadfully bad impulses there are in me!”", "제 안에 얼마나 끔찍하게 못된 충동이 있는지!\""], ["Her lips trembled, and she turned away to hide a tear.", "그녀의 입술이 떨렸고, 그녀는 눈물을 감추려 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["Clym, though vexed at what seemed her unaccountable taste, was in a measure relieved to find that at any rate the marriage question in relation to himself was shelved.", "클림은 도무지 알 수 없는 그녀의 취향에 언짢기는 했어도, 어쨌든 자기와 관련된 결혼 문제만은 보류되었음을 알고 어느 정도 안도했다."], ["Through several succeeding days he saw her at different times from the window of his room moping disconsolately about the garden.", "그 뒤로 며칠에 걸쳐 그는 여러 차례 자기 방 창문으로, 그녀가 풀이 죽어 정원을 침울하게 서성이는 것을 보았다."], ["He was half angry with her for choosing Venn; then he was grieved at having put himself in the way of Venn’s happiness, who was, after all, as honest and persevering a young fellow as any on Egdon, since he had turned over a new leaf.", "그는 그녀가 벤을 택한 것에 반쯤은 화가 났고, 그러다가는 결국 마음을 고쳐먹은 뒤로는 에그던의 어느 젊은이 못지않게 정직하고 끈기 있는 그 사내의 행복에 자기가 끼어든 꼴이 된 것에 마음이 쓰였다."], ["In short, Clym did not know what to do.", "요컨대 클림은 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었다."]], [["When next they met she said abruptly, “He is much more respectable now than he was then!”", "다음번에 마주쳤을 때 그녀가 불쑥 말했다. \"그분은 그때보다 지금 훨씬 더 어엿하다고요!\""]], [["“Who?", "\"누가?"], ["O yes—Diggory Venn.”", "아 그래—디고리 벤 말이구나.\""]], [["“Aunt only objected because he was a reddleman.”", "\"고모님은 그분이 레들맨이라서 반대하셨을 뿐이잖아요.\""]], [["“Well, Thomasin, perhaps I don’t know all the particulars of my mother’s wish.", "\"음, 토머신, 어쩌면 내가 어머니 바람의 자초지종을 다 아는 게 아닐지도 모르지."], ["So you had better use your own discretion.”", "그러니 네 판단대로 하는 게 좋겠다.\""]], [["“You will always feel that I slighted your mother’s memory.”", "\"오라버닌 제가 어머니의 기억을 업신여겼다고 두고두고 여기실 거예요.\""]], [["“No, I will not.", "\"아니, 그러지 않아."], ["I shall think you are convinced that, had she seen Diggory in his present position, she would have considered him a fitting husband for you.", "어머니가 지금 처지의 디고리를 보셨다면 그를 네게 어울리는 남편으로 여기셨으리라고 네가 확신한 거라고 생각할 거야."], ["Now, that’s my real feeling.", "자, 그게 내 솔직한 마음이다."], ["Don’t consult me any more, but do as you like, Thomasin.", "더는 내게 묻지 말고, 네 뜻대로 하렴, 토머신."], ["I shall be content.”", "난 만족할 테니까.\""]], [["It is to be supposed that Thomasin was convinced; for a few days after this, when Clym strayed into a part of the heath that he had not lately visited, Humphrey, who was at work there, said to him, “I am glad to see that Mrs.", "토머신이 확신을 얻었던 모양이니, 이 일이 있은 며칠 뒤 클림이 근래 가보지 않았던 황야의 한 구역으로 발길이 닿았을 때, 거기서 일하던 험프리가 그에게 말했다."], ["Wildeve and Venn have made it up again, seemingly.”", "\"와일디브 부인하고 벤이 보아하니 다시 좋게 된 것 같아서 다행입니다.\""]], [["“Have they?”", "\"그런가?\""], ["said Clym abstractedly.", "클림이 멍하니 말했다."]], [["“Yes; and he do contrive to stumble upon her whenever she walks out on fine days with the chiel.", "\"네, 그리고 부인이 좋은 날 아이를 데리고 산책 나올 때마다 그가 용케 마주치곤 합죠."], ["But, Mr. Yeobright, I can’t help feeling that your cousin ought to have married you.", "하지만, 여브라이트 씨, 저는 자꾸만, 사촌 누이께서 댁과 결혼했어야 했다는 느낌이 들어 어쩔 수가 없어요."], ["’Tis a pity to make two chimleycorners where there need be only one.", "굴뚝가가 하나면 될 것을 둘로 만드는 건 애석한 노릇이지요."], ["You could get her away from him now, ’tis my belief, if you were only to set about it.”", "댁이 마음만 먹으신다면 지금이라도 그 여자를 그자한테서 떼어올 수 있다는 게 제 생각입니다.\""]], [["“How can I have the conscience to marry after having driven two women to their deaths?", "\"여자를 둘이나 죽음으로 몰아넣은 사람이 무슨 염치로 결혼을 한단 말인가?"], ["Don’t think such a thing, Humphrey.", "그런 생각일랑 말게, 험프리."], ["After my experience I should consider it too much of a burlesque to go to church and take a wife.", "내가 겪은 일을 생각하면, 교회에 가서 아내를 맞는다는 건 너무도 우스꽝스러운 익살이라 여겨질 걸세."], ["In the words of Job, ‘I have made a covenant with mine eyes; when then should I think upon a maid?’”", "욥의 말마따나, '내가 내 눈과 약속하였나니, 어찌 처녀에게 주목하랴.'\""]], [["“No, Mr. Clym, don’t fancy that about driving two women to their deaths.", "\"아닙니다, 클림 도련님, 여자 둘을 죽음으로 몰았다느니 그런 생각은 하지 마십쇼."], ["You shouldn’t say it.”", "그런 말씀 하시면 안 됩니다.\""]], [["“Well, we’ll leave that out,”", "\"음, 그건 빼고 얘기하세.\""], ["said Yeobright.", "여브라이트가 말했다."], ["“But anyhow God has set a mark upon me which wouldn’t look well in a love-making scene.", "\"하지만 어쨌든 하느님이 내게 표를 찍어두셨는데, 그게 구애의 장면에선 영 보기 좋지 않을 걸세."], ["I have two ideas in my head, and no others.", "내 머릿속엔 두 가지 생각뿐이고 다른 건 없네."], ["I am going to keep a night-school; and I am going to turn preacher.", "야학을 열 작정이고, 또 설교자가 될 작정이야."], ["What have you got to say to that, Humphrey?”", "거기에 대해 자넨 뭐라 하겠나, 험프리?\""]], [["“I’ll come and hear ’ee with all my heart.”", "\"진심으로 나가서 도련님 말씀을 듣겠습니다요.\""]], [["“Thanks.", "\"고맙네."], ["’Tis all I wish.”", "내가 바라는 건 그게 전부야.\""]], [["As Clym descended into the valley Thomasin came down by the other path, and met him at the gate.", "클림이 골짜기로 내려가는데 토머신이 다른 길로 내려와 문에서 그와 마주쳤다."], ["“What do you think I have to tell you, Clym?”", "\"제가 무슨 말씀을 드리려는지 아세요, 클림?\""], ["she said, looking archly over her shoulder at him.", "어깨 너머로 그를 짓궂게 바라보며 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I can guess,”", "\"짐작할 수 있지.\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["She scrutinized his face.", "그녀가 그의 얼굴을 찬찬히 살폈다."], ["“Yes, you guess right.", "\"네, 옳게 짐작하셨어요."], ["It is going to be after all.", "결국 그렇게 되려나 봐요."], ["He thinks I may as well make up my mind, and I have got to think so too.", "그분 말이 저도 이제 마음을 정하는 게 좋겠다고 하시고, 저도 그래야겠다는 생각이 들었거든요."], ["It is to be on the twenty-fifth of next month, if you don’t object.”", "오라버니만 반대 안 하시면, 다음 달 이십오일에 하기로 했어요.\""]], [["“Do what you think right, dear.", "\"옳다고 여기는 대로 하렴, 얘야."], ["I am only too glad that you see your way clear to happiness again.", "네가 다시 행복으로 가는 길을 환히 본다니 더없이 기쁠 뿐이다."], ["My sex owes you every amends for the treatment you received in days gone by.”*", "지난날 네가 받은 대접에 대해 내 성性은 네게 온갖 보상을 해야 마땅하지.\"*"]], [["[*] The writer may state here that the original conception of the story did not design a marriage between Thomasin and Venn.", "[*] 글쓴이는 여기서, 이 이야기의 본래 구상이 토머신과 벤의 결혼을 의도하지 않았음을 밝혀둘 수 있겠다."], ["He was to have retained his isolated and weird character to the last, and to have disappeared mysteriously from the heath, nobody knowing whither—Thomasin remaining a widow. But certain circumstances of serial publication led to a change of intent.", "그는 끝까지 고립되고 기괴한 성격을 간직한 채, 누구도 어디로 갔는지 모르게 황야에서 신비롭게 사라질 것이었고—토머신은 과부로 남을 것이었다."], ["Readers can therefore choose between the endings, and those with an austere artistic code can assume the more consistent conclusion to be the true one.", "그러나 연재 출판의 어떤 사정으로 의도가 바뀌게 되었다."]], [["IV. Cheerfulness Again Asserts Itself at Blooms-End, and Clym Finds His Vocation", "따라서 독자는 두 가지 결말 사이에서 고를 수 있으며, 엄정한 예술적 규율을 지닌 이들은 더 일관된 결론을 참된 것으로 여겨도 좋다."]], [["Anybody who had passed through Blooms-End about eleven o’clock on the morning fixed for the wedding would have found that, while Yeobright’s house was comparatively quiet, sounds denoting great activity came from the dwelling of his nearest neighbour, Timothy Fairway.", "결혼식이 정해진 날 오전 열한 시쯤 블룸스엔드를 지나간 사람이라면, 여브라이트의 집은 비교적 조용한 반면, 그의 가장 가까운 이웃인 티머시 페어웨이의 거처에서는 굉장한 부산함을 알리는 소리가 흘러나오는 것을 알아챘으리라."], ["It was chiefly a noise of feet, briskly crunching hither and thither over the sanded floor within.", "그것은 주로 발소리였으니, 안쪽 모래 깔린 바닥을 이리저리 활기차게 밟아대는 소리였다."], ["One man only was visible outside, and he seemed to be later at an appointment than he had intended to be, for he hastened up to the door, lifted the latch, and walked in without ceremony.", "바깥에 보이는 사람은 단 하나였는데, 그는 작정했던 것보다 약속에 늦은 모양이었으니, 서둘러 문으로 다가가, 빗장을 들어 올리고, 인사도 없이 들어섰기 때문이다."]], [["The scene within was not quite the customary one.", "안의 광경은 그리 흔한 것은 아니었다."], ["Standing about the room was the little knot of men who formed the chief part of the Egdon coterie, there being present Fairway himself, Grandfer Cantle, Humphrey, Christian, and one or two turf-cutters.", "방 여기저기에는 에그던 패거리의 주축을 이루는 사내들이 옹기종기 모여 서 있었으니, 페어웨이 본인과 캔틀 영감과 험프리와 크리스천과 한둘쯤 되는 잔디꾼이 자리해 있었다."], ["It was a warm day, and the men were as a matter of course in their shirtsleeves, except Christian, who had always a nervous fear of parting with a scrap of his clothing when in anybody’s house but his own.", "따뜻한 날이라 사내들은 으레 그렇듯 셔츠 바람이었는데, 다만 크리스천만은 예외였으니, 그는 제 집이 아닌 남의 집에서는 옷가지 한 조각이라도 벗는 것에 늘 신경질적인 두려움을 느꼈다."], ["Across the stout oak table in the middle of the room was thrown a mass of striped linen, which Grandfer Cantle held down on one side, and Humphrey on the other, while Fairway rubbed its surface with a yellow lump, his face being damp and creased with the effort of the labour.", "방 한가운데 튼튼한 떡갈나무 탁자 위에는 줄무늬 아마포 한 무더기가 던져져 있었고, 캔틀 영감이 한쪽을, 험프리가 다른 쪽을 눌러 잡은 가운데, 페어웨이가 노란 덩어리로 그 표면을 문지르고 있었으니, 그 일의 고됨에 얼굴이 땀에 젖고 잔뜩 찌푸려져 있었다."]], [["“Waxing a bed-tick, souls?”", "\"이불 천에 밀랍 먹이는 건가, 양반들?\""], ["said the newcomer.", "새로 온 이가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Sam,”", "\"그래, 샘.\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle, as a man too busy to waste words.", "말을 낭비하기엔 너무 바쁜 사람처럼 캔틀 영감이 말했다."], ["“Shall I stretch this corner a shade tighter, Timothy?”", "\"이 귀퉁이를 좀 더 팽팽히 당길까, 티머시?\""]], [["Fairway replied, and the waxing went on with unabated vigour.", "페어웨이가 대답했고, 밀랍 먹이기는 줄지 않는 기세로 이어졌다."], ["“’Tis going to be a good bed, by the look o’t,”", "\"보아하니 좋은 이불이 되겠는걸.\""], ["continued Sam, after an interval of silence.", "한동안 잠잠하다가 샘이 말을 이었다."], ["“Who may it be for?”", "\"누구 줄 건데?\""]], [["“’Tis a present for the new folks that’s going to set up housekeeping,”", "\"이제 새살림 차릴 새 식구들한테 줄 선물이지.\""], ["said Christian, who stood helpless and overcome by the majesty of the proceedings.", "이 절차의 위엄에 짓눌려 어쩔 줄 모르고 서 있던 크리스천이 말했다."]], [["“Ah, to be sure; and a valuable one, ’a b’lieve.”", "\"아, 그렇고말고, 값나가는 선물이렷다.\""]], [["“Beds be dear to fokes that don’t keep geese, bain’t they, Mister Fairway?”", "\"이불은 거위를 안 치는 사람들한테는 비싼 거 아닌가요, 페어웨이 양반?\""], ["said Christian, as to an omniscient being.", "전지전능한 존재에게 묻듯 크리스천이 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇지.\""], ["said the furze-dealer, standing up, giving his forehead a thorough mopping, and handing the beeswax to Humphrey, who succeeded at the rubbing forthwith.", "가시금작화 장수가 몸을 일으켜, 이마를 말끔히 훔치고는, 벌집 밀랍을 험프리에게 건네자, 그가 곧장 문지르는 일을 이어받았다."], ["“Not that this couple be in want of one, but ’twas well to show ’em a bit of friendliness at this great racketing vagary of their lives.", "\"이 두 사람이 이불이 없어서 그러는 건 아니지만, 한평생에 이런 큰 법석이자 야단인 때에 정을 좀 보이는 게 좋지."], ["I set up both my own daughters in one when they was married, and there have been feathers enough for another in the house the last twelve months.", "내 두 딸도 시집갈 때 하나씩 장만해 살림을 차려줬고, 지난 열두 달 동안 또 하나 만들 만큼 깃털이 집에 쌓였단 말이야."], ["Now then, neighbours, I think we have laid on enough wax.", "자, 이웃들, 밀랍은 이만하면 충분히 먹인 것 같네."], ["Grandfer Cantle, you turn the tick the right way outwards, and then I’ll begin to shake in the feathers.”", "캔틀 영감, 천을 바로 뒤집어 겉이 밖으로 나오게 하시게, 그럼 내가 깃털을 털어 넣기 시작하겠네.\""]], [["When the bed was in proper trim Fairway and Christian brought forward vast paper bags, stuffed to the full, but light as balloons, and began to turn the contents of each into the receptacle just prepared.", "이불이 제대로 채비되자 페어웨이와 크리스천이 가득 채워졌으되 풍선처럼 가벼운 커다란 종이 봉지들을 들고 와, 방금 마련한 자루에다 봉지마다의 내용물을 쏟아붓기 시작했다."], ["As bag after bag was emptied, airy tufts of down and feathers floated about the room in increasing quantity till, through a mishap of Christian’s, who shook the contents of one bag outside the tick, the atmosphere of the room became dense with gigantic flakes, which descended upon the workers like a windless snowstorm.", "봉지가 하나씩 비워질 때마다 솜털과 깃털이 점점 더 많이 방 안을 둥둥 떠다녔고, 마침내 크리스천이 봉지 하나의 내용물을 천 바깥에 쏟아버린 실수로 방 안 공기가 거대한 송이들로 자욱해져, 바람 없는 눈보라처럼 일꾼들 위로 내려앉았다."]], [["“I never saw such a clumsy chap as you, Christian,”", "\"자네처럼 굼뜬 녀석은 본 적이 없네, 크리스천.\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle severely.", "캔틀 영감이 매섭게 말했다."], ["“You might have been the son of a man that’s never been outside Blooms-End in his life for all the wit you have.", "\"지닌 재간으로 치면 자넨 평생 블룸스엔드 밖으로 한 발짝도 나가본 적 없는 사내의 아들 같다니까."], ["Really all the soldiering and smartness in the world in the father seems to count for nothing in forming the nater of the son.", "정말이지 애비가 군대 다니며 닦은 그 온갖 영민함이 아들놈 천성 만드는 데는 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없는 모양이야."], ["As far as that chief Christian is concerned I might as well have stayed at home and seed nothing, like all the rest of ye here.", "저 잘난 크리스천을 두고 보면, 나도 여기 자네들 나머지 모두처럼 집에 들어앉아 아무것도 못 본 거나 다름없을 뻔했어."], ["Though, as far as myself is concerned, a dashing spirit has counted for sommat, to be sure!”", "하긴 나로 말하자면, 패기 넘치는 기상이 한몫했고말고, 암!\""]], [["“Don’t ye let me down so, Father; I feel no bigger than a ninepin after it.", "\"그렇게 깎아내리진 마세요, 아버지, 그러시면 제가 볼링 핀만큼도 못한 것 같단 말예요."], ["I’ve made but a bruckle hit, I’m afeard.”", "변변찮게 어설픈 솜씨밖에 못 부린 게 아닌가 싶어 겁이 나요.\""]], [["“Come, come.", "\"이런, 이런."], ["Never pitch yerself in such a low key as that, Christian; you should try more,”", "그렇게까지 풀죽은 가락은 잡지 말게나, 크리스천, 좀 더 애를 써봐.\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, you should try more,”", "\"그래, 좀 더 애를 써야지.\""], ["echoed the Grandfer with insistence, as if he had been the first to make the suggestion.", "마치 자기가 먼저 그 말을 꺼낸 양 우기듯이 영감이 따라 말했다."], ["“In common conscience every man ought either to marry or go for a soldier.", "\"양심상 사내라면 마땅히 장가를 들든지 군인이 되든지 해야 하는 법이야."], ["’Tis a scandal to the nation to do neither one nor t’other.", "둘 다 안 하는 건 나라에 망신이지. 난 둘 다 했어, 하느님 덕분에!"], ["I did both, thank God! Neither to raise men nor to lay ’em low—that shows a poor do-nothing spirit indeed.”", "사람을 일으키지도 눕히지도 못한다는 건 정말이지 보잘것없는 무위無爲의 기상을 드러내는 것이렷다.\""]], [["“I never had the nerve to stand fire,”", "\"전 한 번도 포화 앞에 설 배짱이 없었어요.\""], ["faltered Christian.", "크리스천이 더듬거렸다."], ["“But as to marrying, I own I’ve asked here and there, though without much fruit from it.", "\"하지만 장가에 관해서라면, 여기저기 청해본 건 사실이에요, 별 소득은 없었지만요."], ["Yes, there’s some house or other that might have had a man for a master—such as he is—that’s now ruled by a woman alone.", "그래요, 어딘가엔 사내를 주인으로—이 꼴이나마—맞을 수도 있었을 집이 한둘 있었는데, 지금은 여자 혼자 다스리고 있지요."], ["Still it might have been awkward if I had found her; for, d’ye see, neighbours, there’d have been nobody left at home to keep down Father’s spirits to the decent pitch that becomes a old man.”", "그래도 제가 그 여자를 찾아냈다면 곤란했을지도 몰라요, 왜냐면, 보세요, 이웃들, 그랬으면 집에 남아서 늙은이한테 어울리는 점잖은 가락으로 아버지 기운을 눌러둘 사람이 아무도 없었을 테니까요.\""]], [["“And you’ve your work cut out to do that, my son,”", "\"그래, 그 일이라면 자네가 할 일이 태산이지, 이 아들놈아.\""], ["said Grandfer Cantle smartly.", "캔틀 영감이 잽싸게 말했다."], ["“I wish that the dread of infirmities was not so strong in me!—I’d start the very first thing tomorrow to see the world over again!", "\"노쇠가 두렵다는 게 내 안에 이리 강하지만 않다면!—내일 당장 제일 먼저 길을 떠나 세상을 다시 한 바퀴 돌아볼 텐데!"], ["But seventy-one, though nothing at home, is a high figure for a rover....", "하지만 일흔하나면, 집에선 아무것도 아니라도, 떠돌이로는 높은 나이지……"], ["Ay, seventy-one, last Candlemasday.", "그래, 일흔하나, 지난 성촉절에 말이야."], ["Gad, I’d sooner have it in guineas than in years!”", "제기랄, 이게 나이가 아니라 기니였으면 좋겠구먼!\""], ["And the old man sighed.", "그러고서 노인이 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“Don’t you be mournful, Grandfer,”", "\"서글퍼하지 마세요, 영감님.\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“Empt some more feathers into the bed-tick, and keep up yer heart.", "\"깃털이나 좀 더 이불 천에 부어 넣고, 기운 차리세요."], ["Though rather lean in the stalks you be a green-leaved old man still.", "줄기야 좀 빼빼하셔도 영감님은 아직 푸른 잎 단 노인이에요."], ["There’s time enough left to ye yet to fill whole chronicles.”", "온 연대기를 채울 만한 시간이 아직 영감님께 충분히 남았답니다.\""]], [["“Begad, I’ll go to ’em, Timothy—to the married pair!”", "\"그래, 가야지, 티머시—신혼부부한테로 말이야!\""], ["said Granfer Cantle in an encouraged voice, and starting round briskly.", "캔틀 영감이 기운을 차린 목소리로 말하며, 기운차게 빙그르르 돌았다."], ["“I’ll go to ’em tonight and sing a wedding song, hey?", "\"오늘 밤 그들한테 가서 혼례 노래를 한 곡 불러야겠어, 어떤가?"], ["’Tis like me to do so, you know; and they’d see it as such.", "그게 나다운 일이지, 알겠나, 그들도 그리 여길 거야."], ["My ‘Down in Cupid’s Gardens’ was well liked in four; still, I’ve got others as good, and even better.", "내가 부르던 '큐피드 동산에서'는 네 군데서 인기가 좋았지, 그래도 그만큼 좋은, 아니 더 나은 노래도 있어."], ["What do you say to my", "이건 어떤가,"]], [["She cal′-led to′ her love′ From the lat′-tice a-bove, ′O come in′ from the fog-gy fog′-gy dew′.", "윗창에서 그녀 임을 부르네\n'오 들어오소 안개 자욱 이슬 속에서'"]], [["’Twould please ’em well at such a time!", "그런 때엔 그들도 무척 좋아할 거야!"], ["Really, now I come to think of it, I haven’t turned my tongue in my head to the shape of a real good song since Old Midsummer night, when we had the ‘Barley Mow’ at the Woman; and ’tis a pity to neglect your strong point where there’s few that have the compass for such things!”", "정말이지, 가만 생각해보니, 우리가 여인숙에서 '보릿단' 부르던 그 한여름 밤 이후로는 입을 놀려 제대로 좋은 노래 가락을 빚어본 적이 없구먼, 그런 노래 부를 만한 음역을 갖춘 이가 드문데 자기 장기를 묵히는 건 애석한 노릇이지!\""]], [["“So ’tis, so ’tis,”", "\"그렇고말고, 그렇고말고.\""], ["said Fairway.", "페어웨이가 말했다."], ["“Now gie the bed a shake down.", "\"자, 이불을 한 번 흔들어 다지세."], ["We’ve put in seventy pounds of best feathers, and I think that’s as many as the tick will fairly hold.", "제일 좋은 깃털을 일흔 파운드나 넣었으니, 천이 너끈히 담을 만큼은 다 됐을 거야."], ["A bit and a drap wouldn’t be amiss now, I reckon.", "이제 요기 좀 하고 한 모금 적시는 것도 나쁘지 않겠는걸."], ["Christian, maul down the victuals from corner-cupboard if canst reach, man, and I’ll draw a drap o’ sommat to wet it with.”", "크리스천, 손이 닿거든 모퉁이 찬장에서 먹을 것 좀 끄집어 내리게, 이 사람아, 그동안 난 목 축일 걸 한 잔 따라보겠네.\""]], [["They sat down to a lunch in the midst of their work, feathers around, above, and below them; the original owners of which occasionally came to the open door and cackled begrudgingly at sight of such a quantity of their old clothes.", "그들은 일하던 한복판에서 점심을 들려고 자리에 앉았으니, 깃털이 그들 둘레와 위와 아래에 가득했고, 그 본래 임자들은 이따금 열린 문간에 와서 제 묵은 옷가지가 그렇게 잔뜩 쌓인 것을 보고는 아까운 듯 꽥꽥 울어댔다."]], [["“Upon my soul I shall be chokt,”", "\"내 영혼을 걸고 말하건대 이러다 목이 막히겠네.\""], ["said Fairway when, having extracted a feather from his mouth, he found several others floating on the mug as it was handed round.", "입에서 깃털 하나를 빼낸 페어웨이가, 잔이 돌려질 때 거기 또 몇 개가 둥둥 떠 있는 것을 보고 말했다."]], [["“I’ve swallered several; and one had a tolerable quill,”", "\"난 벌써 몇 개나 삼켰는걸, 하난 깃대가 제법 굵더라고.\""], ["said Sam placidly from the corner.", "구석에서 샘이 태평하게 말했다."]], [["“Hullo—what’s that—wheels I hear coming?”", "\"이런—저게 뭐야—바퀴 소리가 들리는데?\""], ["Grandfer Cantle exclaimed, jumping up and hastening to the door.", "캔틀 영감이 벌떡 일어나 문으로 달려가며 소리쳤다."], ["“Why, ’tis they back again—I didn’t expect ’em yet this half-hour.", "\"아니, 저들이 벌써 돌아오네—이렇게 빨리 올 줄은 몰랐는데, 반 시간은 더 걸릴 줄 알았건만."], ["To be sure, how quick marrying can be done when you are in the mind for’t!”", "하긴, 마음만 먹으면 혼례라는 게 어찌나 빨리 끝나는지!\""]], [["“O yes, it can soon be _done_,”", "\"오 그래, 끝내는 거야 금세 되지.\""], ["said Fairway, as if something should be added to make the statement complete.", "그 말을 온전히 완성하려면 무언가 덧붙여야 한다는 듯이 페어웨이가 말했다."]], [["He arose and followed the Grandfer, and the rest also went to the door.", "그가 일어나 영감을 뒤따랐고, 나머지도 문으로 갔다."], ["In a moment an open fly was driven past, in which sat Venn and Mrs. Venn, Yeobright, and a grand relative of Venn’s who had come from Budmouth for the occasion.", "곧이어 덮개 없는 마차 한 대가 지나갔는데, 거기에는 벤과 벤 부인과 여브라이트와, 이 일을 위해 버드머스에서 온 벤의 한 점잖은 친척이 타고 있었다."], ["The fly had been hired at the nearest town, regardless of distance and cost, there being nothing on Egdon Heath, in Venn’s opinion, dignified enough for such an event when such a woman as Thomasin was the bride; and the church was too remote for a walking bridal-party.", "마차는 거리도 비용도 아랑곳없이 가장 가까운 읍내에서 빌려온 것이었으니, 벤이 보기에 에그던 황야에는 토머신 같은 여인이 신부일 때 그런 행사에 걸맞을 만큼 위엄 있는 것이 아무것도 없었고, 교회는 걸어서 가는 혼례 행렬을 꾸리기엔 너무 멀었기 때문이다."]], [["As the fly passed the group which had run out from the homestead they shouted “Hurrah!”", "마차가 농가에서 뛰쳐나온 무리 곁을 지날 때 그들은 \"만세!\""], ["and waved their hands; feathers and down floating from their hair, their sleeves, and the folds of their garments at every motion, and Grandfer Cantle’s seals dancing merrily in the sunlight as he twirled himself about.", "하고 외치며 손을 흔들었으니, 움직일 때마다 깃털과 솜털이 그들의 머리에서, 소매에서, 옷자락에서 떠다녔고, 캔틀 영감이 몸을 빙빙 돌리는 동안 그의 인장들이 햇빛 속에서 흥겹게 춤추었다."], ["The driver of the fly turned a supercilious gaze upon them; he even treated the wedded pair themselves with something like condescension; for in what other state than heathen could people, rich or poor, exist who were doomed to abide in such a world’s end as Egdon?", "마차 마부는 그들에게 거만한 시선을 던졌으니, 신혼부부 본인들마저도 다소 깔보는 듯이 대했는데, 에그던 같은 세상의 끝에 머물도록 운명 지어진 사람들이라면, 부자든 가난뱅이든, 이교도 말고 다른 무슨 상태로 존재할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Thomasin showed no such superiority to the group at the door, fluttering her hand as quickly as a bird’s wing towards them, and asking Diggory, with tears in her eyes, if they ought not to alight and speak to these kind neighbours.", "토머신은 문간의 무리에게 그런 우월감 따위 전혀 내비치지 않은 채, 새 날개처럼 잽싸게 그들을 향해 손을 펄럭이며, 눈에 눈물이 어린 채로, 내려서 이 친절한 이웃들에게 인사를 건네야 하지 않겠느냐고 디고리에게 물었다."], ["Venn, however, suggested that, as they were all coming to the house in the evening, this was hardly necessary.", "그러나 벤은, 저녁에 다들 집으로 올 터이니 그럴 것까진 없겠다고 일러주었다."]], [["After this excitement the saluting party returned to their occupation, and the stuffing and sewing were soon afterwards finished, when Fairway harnessed a horse, wrapped up the cumbrous present, and drove off with it in the cart to Venn’s house at Stickleford.", "이 소동이 지난 뒤 인사를 건넨 무리는 다시 제 일로 돌아갔고, 곧이어 속 채우기와 바느질이 마무리되자, 페어웨이가 말 한 마리에 마구를 채워, 그 거추장스러운 선물을 싸서, 수레에 싣고 스티클포드의 벤네 집으로 몰고 갔다."]], [["Yeobright, having filled the office at the wedding service which naturally fell to his hands, and afterwards returned to the house with the husband and wife, was indisposed to take part in the feasting and dancing that wound up the evening.", "여브라이트는 자연히 자기 손에 떨어진 혼례 예식에서의 소임을 다하고, 그 뒤 부부와 함께 집으로 돌아왔으나, 저녁을 마무리 짓는 잔치와 춤판에 끼어들 마음은 없었다."], ["Thomasin was disappointed.", "토머신은 실망했다."]], [["“I wish I could be there without dashing your spirits,”", "\"네 흥을 깨지 않고 거기 함께할 수 있다면 좋으련만.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“But I might be too much like the skull at the banquet.”", "\"하지만 난 잔치판의 해골 꼴이 되기 십상이겠지.\" \"아녜요, 아녜요.\""]], [["“No, no.” “Well, dear, apart from that, if you would excuse me, I should be glad.", "\"음, 얘야, 그건 그렇다 치고, 양해해준다면, 빠지는 게 좋겠다."], ["I know it seems unkind; but, dear Thomasin, I fear I should not be happy in the company—there, that’s the truth of it.", "매정하게 보이는 줄은 안다, 하지만 사랑하는 토머신, 그 자리에서 내가 행복하지 못할까 봐 두렵구나—그래, 그게 솔직한 마음이야."], ["I shall always be coming to see you at your new home, you know, so that my absence now will not matter.”", "알다시피 앞으로도 네 새집으로 너를 보러 늘 갈 테니, 지금 내가 빠진다 해도 대수롭지 않을 거다.\""]], [["“Then I give in.", "\"그럼 제가 졌어요."], ["Do whatever will be most comfortable to yourself.”", "오라버니 마음 가장 편한 대로 하세요.\""]], [["Clym retired to his lodging at the housetop much relieved, and occupied himself during the afternoon in noting down the heads of a sermon, with which he intended to initiate all that really seemed practicable of the scheme that had originally brought him hither, and that he had so long kept in view under various modifications, and through evil and good report.", "클림은 한결 마음이 놓여 지붕마루의 제 거처로 물러갔고, 오후 동안 설교 한 편의 요지를 적어 내려가는 일에 매달렸으니, 그 설교로써 그는 애초에 자기를 이리로 데려왔고 그토록 오랫동안 여러 변형을 거치며 좋은 평판과 나쁜 평판을 두루 겪으면서도 줄곧 마음에 품어온 그 계획 가운데 정말로 실행할 만해 보이는 모든 것을 시작할 작정이었다."], ["He had tested and weighed his convictions again and again, and saw no reason to alter them, though he had considerably lessened his plan.", "그는 자기 신념을 거듭거듭 시험하고 가늠해보았으나 그것을 바꿀 까닭을 찾지 못했으니, 다만 계획은 상당히 줄여놓은 터였다."], ["His eyesight, by long humouring in his native air, had grown stronger, but not sufficiently strong to warrant his attempting his extensive educational project.", "그의 시력은 고향의 공기 속에서 오래 다독여진 덕에 더 강해지긴 했으나, 그 방대한 교육 사업을 시도해도 좋을 만큼 충분히 강하지는 않았다."], ["Yet he did not repine—there was still more than enough of an unambitious sort to tax all his energies and occupy all his hours.", "그래도 그는 한탄하지 않았으니—그의 온 정력을 쏟고 온 시간을 채울 만한, 야심 없는 종류의 일은 여전히 차고 넘쳤다."]], [["Evening drew on, and sounds of life and movement in the lower part of the domicile became more pronounced, the gate in the palings clicking incessantly.", "저녁이 깊어갔고, 집 아래편에서 들려오는 생기와 움직임의 소리가 더 또렷해졌으니, 울타리의 쪽문이 쉴 새 없이 찰칵거렸다."], ["The party was to be an early one, and all the guests were assembled long before it was dark.", "잔치는 일찍 여는 것으로 되어 있어, 손님들은 어두워지기 한참 전에 죄다 모였다."], ["Yeobright went down the back staircase and into the heath by another path than that in front, intending to walk in the open air till the party was over, when he would return to wish Thomasin and her husband good-bye as they departed.", "여브라이트는 뒤쪽 계단으로 내려가, 앞쪽이 아닌 다른 길로 황야에 들어섰으니, 잔치가 끝날 때까지 바깥 공기 속을 거닐다가, 토머신과 그 남편이 떠날 때 돌아와 작별 인사를 건넬 작정이었다."], ["His steps were insensibly bent towards Mistover by the path that he had followed on that terrible morning when he learnt the strange news from Susan’s boy.", "그의 발걸음은 어느새 미스토버 쪽으로, 수전의 아들에게서 그 기이한 소식을 들었던 그 끔찍한 아침에 따라갔던 그 길로 향했다."]], [["He did not turn aside to the cottage, but pushed on to an eminence, whence he could see over the whole quarter that had once been Eustacia’s home.", "그는 오두막 쪽으로 빠지지 않고, 한때 유스테이시아의 집이었던 그 일대 전체를 굽어볼 수 있는 높직한 데로 밀고 올라갔다."], ["While he stood observing the darkening scene somebody came up.", "어두워져가는 풍경을 바라보며 서 있는데 누군가 다가왔다."], ["Clym, seeing him but dimly, would have let him pass silently, had not the pedestrian, who was Charley, recognized the young man and spoken to him.", "그를 어렴풋이밖에 보지 못한 클림은 그냥 말없이 지나치게 두었을 터인데, 그 보행자가, 곧 찰리였는데, 청년을 알아보고 그에게 말을 걸어왔다."]], [["“Charley, I have not seen you for a length of time,”", "\"찰리, 오랫동안 못 봤구나.\""], ["said Yeobright.", "여브라이트가 말했다."], ["“Do you often walk this way?”", "\"이 길로 자주 다니니?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아뇨.\""], ["the lad replied.", "청년이 대답했다."], ["“I don’t often come outside the bank.”", "\"둑 바깥으로는 잘 안 나옵니다.\""]], [["“You were not at the Maypole.”", "\"메이폴에는 안 왔더구나.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Charley, in the same listless tone.", "찰리가 같은 맥없는 어조로 말했다."], ["“I don’t care for that sort of thing now.”", "\"이젠 그런 거에 마음이 안 가요.\""]], [["“You rather liked Miss Eustacia, didn’t you?”", "\"넌 유스테이시아 아씨를 꽤 좋아했지, 그렇지?\""], ["Yeobright gently asked.", "여브라이트가 부드럽게 물었다."], ["Eustacia had frequently told him of Charley’s romantic attachment.", "유스테이시아는 찰리의 그 낭만적인 연모를 그에게 자주 이야기했었다."]], [["“Yes, very much.", "\"네, 무척이나요."], ["Ah, I wish—”", "아, 저는 바라기를—\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"뭘?\""]], [["“I wish, Mr. Yeobright, you could give me something to keep that once belonged to her—if you don’t mind.”", "\"저는 바랍니다, 여브라이트 씨, 한때 아씨 것이었던 걸 간직하게 뭐든 제게 주실 수 있다면—괜찮으시다면요.\""]], [["“I shall be very happy to.", "\"기꺼이 그러마."], ["It will give me very great pleasure, Charley.", "그러면 나도 무척 기쁘겠다, 찰리."], ["Let me think what I have of hers that you would like.", "네가 가지고 싶어할 만한 아씨 물건이 뭐가 있는지 어디 생각해보자."], ["But come with me to the house, and I’ll see.”", "그러니 나와 함께 집으로 가자, 가서 찾아보마.\""]], [["They walked towards Blooms-End together.", "두 사람은 함께 블룸스엔드 쪽으로 걸었다."], ["When they reached the front it was dark, and the shutters were closed, so that nothing of the interior could be seen.", "앞쪽에 이르렀을 때는 어두웠고, 덧창이 닫혀 있어 안의 모습은 전혀 보이지 않았다."]], [["“Come round this way,”", "\"이리 돌아가자.\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."], ["“My entrance is at the back for the present.”", "\"당분간 내 출입구는 뒤편이거든.\""]], [["The two went round and ascended the crooked stair in darkness till Clym’s sitting-room on the upper floor was reached, where he lit a candle, Charley entering gently behind.", "둘은 빙 돌아가, 어둠 속에서 구불구불한 계단을 올라 위층의 클림의 거실에 이르렀고, 거기서 그가 초에 불을 붙이는 동안 찰리가 살며시 뒤따라 들어왔다."], ["Yeobright searched his desk, and taking out a sheet of tissue-paper unfolded from it two or three undulating locks of raven hair, which fell over the paper like black streams.", "여브라이트는 책상을 뒤져, 박엽지 한 장을 꺼내, 거기서 칠흑같이 검은 머리채 두세 가닥을 펼쳐냈으니, 그것들이 검은 물줄기처럼 종이 위로 흘러내렸다."], ["From these he selected one, wrapped it up, and gave it to the lad, whose eyes had filled with tears.", "그중 하나를 골라, 싸서, 청년에게 건넸으니, 청년의 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁했다."], ["He kissed the packet, put it in his pocket, and said in a voice of emotion, “O, Mr.", "그는 그 꾸러미에 입을 맞추고, 주머니에 넣고는, 북받치는 목소리로 말했다."], ["Clym, how good you are to me!”", "\"오, 클림 도련님, 어쩜 제게 이리 잘해주십니까!\""]], [["“I will go a little way with you,”", "\"가는 길을 조금 함께 가주마.\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."], ["And amid the noise of merriment from below they descended.", "그리고 아래층에서 들려오는 흥겨운 소란 속에 둘은 내려갔다."], ["Their path to the front led them close to a little side window, whence the rays of candles streamed across the shrubs.", "앞쪽으로 가는 길은 그들을 작은 옆창 가까이로 데려갔으니, 거기서 촛불의 빛줄기가 관목들을 가로질러 새어 나오고 있었다."], ["The window, being screened from general observation by the bushes, had been left unblinded, so that a person in this private nook could see all that was going on within the room which contained the wedding guests, except in so far as vision was hindered by the green antiquity of the panes.", "그 창문은 덤불에 가려 사람들의 눈에 띄지 않았기에 가리개를 치지 않은 채였고, 그래서 이 은밀한 구석에 있는 사람은, 유리창의 푸르스름한 고풍스러움에 시야가 가로막히는 만큼을 빼고는, 혼례 손님들이 든 방 안에서 벌어지는 일을 죄다 볼 수 있었다."]], [["“Charley, what are they doing?”", "\"찰리, 저들이 뭘 하고 있느냐?\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."], ["“My sight is weaker again tonight, and the glass of this window is not good.”", "\"오늘 밤은 내 눈이 다시 약해진 데다, 이 창유리도 좋질 않구나.\""]], [["Charley wiped his own eyes, which were rather blurred with moisture, and stepped closer to the casement.", "찰리는 습기로 다소 흐려진 제 눈을 닦고, 여닫이창에 더 바싹 다가섰다."], ["“Mr. Venn is asking Christian Cantle to sing,”", "\"벤 씨가 크리스천 캔틀더러 노래를 하라고 청하는데요,\""], ["he replied, “and Christian is moving about in his chair as if he were much frightened at the question, and his father has struck up a stave instead of him.”", "그가 대답했다. \"크리스천은 그 청에 잔뜩 겁먹은 듯이 의자에서 들썩들썩하고, 그래서 그 아버지가 대신 한 가락 뽑기 시작했어요.\""]], [["“Yes, I can hear the old man’s voice,” said Clym.", "\"그래, 노인 목소리가 들리는구나.\""], ["“So there’s to be no dancing, I suppose.", "클림이 말했다. \"그럼 춤은 없는 모양이지."], ["And is Thomasin in the room?", "토머신은 방에 있느냐?"], ["I see something moving in front of the candles that resembles her shape, I think.”", "촛불 앞에서 그 애 모습 같은 게 움직이는 것 같은데.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["She do seem happy.", "행복해 보이는걸요."], ["She is red in the face, and laughing at something Fairway has said to her.", "얼굴이 붉고, 페어웨이가 한 말에 웃고 계세요."], ["O my!”", "어이쿠!\""]], [["“What noise was that?”", "\"무슨 소리였느냐?\""], ["said Clym.", "클림이 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Venn is so tall that he knocked his head against the beam in gieing a skip as he passed under.", "\"벤 씨가 어찌나 키가 큰지, 들보 밑을 지나며 깡충 뛰다가 머리를 들보에 쾅 부딪쳤어요."], ["Mrs. Venn has run up quite frightened and now she’s put her hand to his head to feel if there’s a lump.", "벤 부인이 잔뜩 놀라 달려가서, 지금 혹이 났나 만져보려고 그 머리에 손을 대고 있고요."], ["And now they be all laughing again as if nothing had happened.”", "이제 다들 아무 일 없었던 듯 다시 웃네요.\""]], [["“Do any of them seem to care about my not being there?”", "\"내가 거기 없는 걸 누가 마음 쓰는 것 같으냐?\""], ["Clym asked.", "클림이 물었다."]], [["“No, not a bit in the world.", "\"아뇨, 눈곱만큼도요."], ["Now they are all holding up their glasses and drinking somebody’s health.”", "이제 다들 잔을 치켜들고 누군가의 건강을 위해 마시고 있어요.\""]], [["“I wonder if it is mine?”", "\"그게 내 건강일까?\""]], [["“No, ’tis Mr. and Mrs. Venn’s, because he is making a hearty sort of speech. There—now Mrs. Venn has got up, and is going away to put on her things, I think.”", "\"아뇨, 벤 씨 부부 거예요, 그분이 흥겨운 투로 한 자락 연설을 하고 계시거든요. 저것 봐요—이제 벤 부인이 일어나서, 채비를 하러 자리를 뜨시는 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Well, they haven’t concerned themselves about me, and it is quite right they should not.", "\"그래, 저들이 나에 대해 마음 쓰지 않았구나, 그게 마땅하지."], ["It is all as it should be, and Thomasin at least is happy.", "다 마땅한 대로 되었고, 적어도 토머신은 행복하니."], ["We will not stay any longer now, as they will soon be coming out to go home.”", "이제 더는 머물지 말자, 곧 다들 집에 가려고 나올 테니까.\""]], [["He accompanied the lad into the heath on his way home, and, returning alone to the house a quarter of an hour later, found Venn and Thomasin ready to start, all the guests having departed in his absence.", "그는 청년을 황야까지 바래다 돌아가는 길에 함께해주고, 십오 분 뒤 혼자 집으로 돌아오니, 그가 없는 사이 손님들은 죄다 떠났고 벤과 토머신이 출발 채비를 마치고 있었다."], ["The wedded pair took their seats in the four-wheeled dogcart which Venn’s head milker and handy man had driven from Stickleford to fetch them in; little Eustacia and the nurse were packed securely upon the open flap behind; and the milker, on an ancient overstepping pony, whose shoes clashed like cymbals at every tread, rode in the rear, in the manner of a body-servant of the last century.", "신혼부부는 벤네 우두머리 젖꾼이자 잡일꾼이 그들을 데리러 스티클포드에서 몰고 온 네 바퀴 이륜마차에 자리를 잡았고, 어린 유스테이시아와 유모는 뒤쪽 열린 받침판에 단단히 실렸으며, 발굽이 디딜 때마다 심벌즈처럼 부딪치는 편자를 단 늙고 발 헛디디는 조랑말에 올라탄 젖꾼이, 지난 세기의 시종처럼 뒤를 따라 달렸다."]], [["“Now we leave you in absolute possession of your own house again,”", "\"이제 다시 오라버니 집을 오롯이 오라버니께 맡기고 갈게요.\""], ["said Thomasin as she bent down to wish her cousin good night.", "사촌에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하려고 몸을 숙이며 토머신이 말했다."], ["“It will be rather lonely for you, Clym, after the hubbub we have been making.”", "\"우리가 떨던 이 법석 끝이라 오라버닌 좀 쓸쓸하시겠어요, 클림.\""]], [["“O, that’s no inconvenience,”", "\"아, 그런 거야 거리낄 게 못 되지.\""], ["said Clym, smiling rather sadly.", "다소 서글프게 미소 지으며 클림이 말했다."], ["And then the party drove off and vanished in the night shades, and Yeobright entered the house.", "그러고 나서 일행은 마차를 몰아 떠나 밤 그늘 속으로 사라졌고, 여브라이트는 집으로 들어섰다."], ["The ticking of the clock was the only sound that greeted him, for not a soul remained; Christian, who acted as cook, valet, and gardener to Clym, sleeping at his father’s house.", "그를 맞아준 것은 시계의 똑딱거림뿐이었으니, 한 사람의 혼도 남지 않았기 때문이라, 클림의 요리사이자 시중꾼이자 정원사 노릇을 하는 크리스천은 제 아버지 집에서 잤던 것이다."], ["Yeobright sat down in one of the vacant chairs, and remained in thought a long time.", "여브라이트는 빈 의자 가운데 하나에 앉아, 오래도록 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["His mother’s old chair was opposite; it had been sat in that evening by those who had scarcely remembered that it ever was hers.", "어머니의 낡은 의자가 맞은편에 있었으니, 그날 저녁 그것이 한때 어머니의 것이었음을 거의 기억하지 못하는 이들이 거기 앉아 있었다."], ["But to Clym she was almost a presence there, now as always.", "그러나 클림에게 어머니는 거기, 지금도 늘 그렇듯, 거의 하나의 현존이었다."], ["Whatever she was in other people’s memories, in his she was the sublime saint whose radiance even his tenderness for Eustacia could not obscure.", "다른 이들의 기억 속에서 어머니가 어떤 존재였든, 그의 기억 속에서 어머니는, 유스테이시아를 향한 그의 애틋함조차 흐리지 못한 광휘를 지닌 숭고한 성인聖人이었다."], ["But his heart was heavy, that Mother had _not_ crowned him in the day of his espousals and in the day of the gladness of his heart.", "그러나 그의 마음은 무거웠으니, 어머니가 그의 혼례날에, 그의 마음이 기쁘던 날에 그에게 면류관을 씌워주지 않았기 때문이다."], ["And events had borne out the accuracy of her judgment, and proved the devotedness of her care.", "그리고 사건들은 어머니 판단의 정확함을 입증했고, 그 보살핌의 헌신을 증명했다."], ["He should have heeded her for Eustacia’s sake even more than for his own.", "그는 자기 자신을 위해서보다도 유스테이시아를 위해서 더더욱 어머니의 말씀을 새겼어야 했다."], ["“It was all my fault,”", "\"다 내 탓이었어.\""], ["he whispered.", "그가 속삭였다."], ["“O, my mother, my mother!", "\"오, 어머니, 어머니!"], ["would to God that I could live my life again, and endure for you what you endured for me!”", "제발 다시 한번 제 삶을 살아, 어머니가 저를 위해 견디신 것을 제가 어머니를 위해 견딜 수 있다면!\""]], [["On the Sunday after this wedding an unusual sight was to be seen on Rainbarrow.", "이 혼례가 있은 다음 일요일, 레인배로에서 진귀한 광경을 볼 수 있었다."], ["From a distance there simply appeared to be a motionless figure standing on the top of the tumulus, just as Eustacia had stood on that lonely summit some two years and a half before.", "멀리서 보면 그저 봉분 꼭대기에 미동도 않는 형상 하나가 서 있는 듯했으니, 한 이 년 반 전 유스테이시아가 그 외로운 정상에 서 있던 모습 그대로였다."], ["But now it was fine warm weather, with only a summer breeze blowing, and early afternoon instead of dull twilight.", "그러나 이번에는 화창하고 따뜻한 날씨에, 여름 산들바람만 불고 있었고, 칙칙한 황혼이 아니라 이른 오후였다."], ["Those who ascended to the immediate neighbourhood of the Barrow perceived that the erect form in the centre, piercing the sky, was not really alone.", "봉분 바로 가까이까지 올라간 이들은, 하늘을 찌르는 그 한가운데의 꼿꼿한 형상이 실은 혼자가 아님을 알아챘다."], ["Round him upon the slopes of the Barrow a number of heathmen and women were reclining or sitting at their ease.", "그를 둘러싸고 봉분 비탈에는 여러 황야 사내와 여인이 편히 기대거나 앉아 있었다."], ["They listened to the words of the man in their midst, who was preaching, while they abstractedly pulled heather, stripped ferns, or tossed pebbles down the slope.", "그들은 가운데 선 사내의 말에 귀를 기울이고 있었으니, 그는 설교를 하고 있었고, 그들은 그러는 동안 멍하니 히스를 뜯거나, 고사리를 훑어 벗기거나, 조약돌을 비탈 아래로 던지고 있었다."], ["This was the first of a series of moral lectures or Sermons on the Mount, which were to be delivered from the same place every Sunday afternoon as long as the fine weather lasted.", "이것이 일련의 도덕 강론, 곧 산상수훈의 첫 번째였으니, 화창한 날씨가 이어지는 한 매주 일요일 오후 같은 자리에서 베풀어질 것이었다."]], [["The commanding elevation of Rainbarrow had been chosen for two reasons: first, that it occupied a central position among the remote cottages around; secondly, that the preacher thereon could be seen from all adjacent points as soon as he arrived at his post, the view of him being thus a convenient signal to those stragglers who wished to draw near.", "레인배로의 위풍당당한 높이가 택해진 데에는 두 가지 까닭이 있었으니, 첫째는 그것이 둘레의 외딴 오두막들 가운데 중심을 차지하고 있다는 것이었고, 둘째는 그 위의 설교자가 제 자리에 이르자마자 인접한 모든 지점에서 보일 수 있어, 그가 보이는 것 자체가 가까이 다가오고자 하는 떠돌이들에게 편리한 신호가 된다는 것이었다."], ["The speaker was bareheaded, and the breeze at each waft gently lifted and lowered his hair, somewhat too thin for a man of his years, these still numbering less than thirty-three.", "말하는 이는 맨머리였고, 산들바람이 한 줄기씩 불 때마다 그의 머리칼을, 그 나이의 사내치고는 다소 지나치게 성긴 머리칼을 부드럽게 들어 올렸다 내렸으니, 그 나이는 아직 서른셋이 못 되었다."], ["He wore a shade over his eyes, and his face was pensive and lined; but, though these bodily features were marked with decay there was no defect in the tones of his voice, which were rich, musical, and stirring.", "그는 눈에 차양을 쓰고 있었고, 얼굴은 수심에 잠겨 주름져 있었으나, 이런 육신의 특징들에 쇠락의 흔적이 어려 있긴 해도, 그 목소리의 가락에는 아무런 결함이 없었으니, 풍부하고, 음악적이고, 마음을 울리는 것이었다."], ["He stated that his discourses to people were to be sometimes secular, and sometimes religious, but never dogmatic; and that his texts would be taken from all kinds of books.", "그는 사람들에게 베푸는 자신의 강론이 때로는 세속적인 것이고, 때로는\n종교적이되 결코 독단적이지 않았으며, 그의 설교 본문은 온갖 종류의 책에서 따왔다는 것이다."], ["This afternoon the words were as follows:—", "이날 오후의 말씀은 다음과 같았다.—"]], [["“‘And the king rose up to meet her, and bowed himself unto her, and sat down on his throne, and caused a seat to be set for the king’s mother; and she sat on his right hand.", "\"'왕이 일어나 어머니를 맞이하여 몸을 굽혀 절하고는 자기 보좌에 앉으며 왕의 어머니를 위하여 자리를 베풀게 하니, 어머니가 그의 오른편에 앉으니라."], ["Then she said, I desire one small petition of thee; I pray thee say me not nay.", "그러고는 어머니가 이르되, 내가 그대에게 한 가지 작은 청이 있으니 청컨대 거절하지 마소서."], ["And the king said unto her, Ask, on, my mother: for I will not say thee nay.’”", "왕이 어머니에게 이르되, 청하소서, 어머니여, 내가 거절하지 아니하리이다.'\""]], [["Yeobright had, in fact, found his vocation in the career of an itinerant open-air preacher and lecturer on morally unimpeachable subjects; and from this day he laboured incessantly in that office, speaking not only in simple language on Rainbarrow and in the hamlets round, but in a more cultivated strain elsewhere—from the steps and porticoes of town halls, from market-crosses, from conduits, on esplanades and on wharves, from the parapets of bridges, in barns and outhouses, and all other such places in the neighbouring Wessex towns and villages.", "요브라이트는 사실 도덕적으로 흠잡을 데 없는 주제를 다루는 떠돌이 야외 설교자이자 강연자라는 직업에서 자신의 소명을 찾았으니, 이날부터 그는 그 직무에 쉼 없이 힘써 레인배로와 그 주변 촌락에서 평이한 언어로 설교하였을 뿐 아니라 다른 곳에서는 한층 세련된 어조로—읍사무소의 계단과 주랑에서, 시장 십자가에서, 수도전에서, 해안 산책로와 부두에서, 다리의 난간에서, 헛간과 곳간에서, 그 밖에 인근 웨섹스 읍과 마을의 온갖 그러한 장소에서 말하였다."], ["He left alone creeds and systems of philosophy, finding enough and more than enough to occupy his tongue in the opinions and actions common to all good men.", "그는 교리와 철학 체계는 내버려두었으니, 모든 선한 사람에게 공통된 견해와 행동만으로도 그의 혀를 충분히, 아니 차고 넘치도록 바쁘게 할 거리를 찾은 까닭이다."], ["Some believed him, and some believed not; some said that his words were commonplace, others complained of his want of theological doctrine; while others again remarked that it was well enough for a man to take to preaching who could not see to do anything else.", "어떤 이는 그를 믿었고 어떤 이는 믿지 않았으며, 어떤 이는 그의 말이 평범하다 하였고 또 다른 이는 신학적 교리의 결여를 탓하였으며, 한편 또 어떤 이들은 달리 할 수 있는 일을 찾지 못하는 사람이 설교에 나서는 것도 그런대로 괜찮은 일이라고 평하였다."], ["But everywhere he was kindly received, for the story of his life had become generally known.", "그러나 어디서나 그는 따뜻이 맞아들여졌으니, 그의 생애에 얽힌 이야기가 두루 알려진 까닭이었다."]]];
